{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "I feel good, sometimes, I don’t (Ayy, don’t)", "vault_lines": ["I feel good, sometimes, I don't (Ayy, don't)"], "meaning": "Drake, bouncing back from the last line discussing his beef, treats it with an indifference. He shows that his beef doesn’t affect his regular life, and that just like other humans, he feels good and sometimes he doesn’t.\n\nAlso, Drake is known for making songs that encapsulate a wide range of emotions from happy, to chill, to sad. He often transitions from happy to sad in one line, as he does in this line from “Karaoke” off of his 2010 project Thank Me Later:\n\nI know they say the first love is the sweetest, but that first cut is the deepest", "annotation_id": "14018517", "url": "https://genius.com/14018517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "I hold back, sometimes, I won’t, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I hold back, sometimes, I won't, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake has had beef with many artists during the course of his career,  most notably with Philadelphia rapper Meek Mill.  Meek attacked Drake on Twitter for not writing his own music. Drake held back and never really responded to these allegations, but did state this to his friend Hitman Holla:\n\nI signed up for greatness, this comes with it.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/meekmill/status/623700698509758464?lang=en\n\nAnother recent example is the awfully one-sided beef between XXXTENTACION and Drizzy. X had accused Drake of stealing the flow from his hit single “Look At Me!” and continued to rant for months, but eventually stopped. Once again, Drake did not respond to any of his remarks.\n\nThough Drake has “held back” at times he has also responded to certain disses, like Pusha T’s “Exodus 23:1,” when he stated this at a concert:\n\nIf you was doing 16s when I was 16, and your shit still flopped, and you switched teams, don’t talk to me my nigga.", "annotation_id": "13797336", "url": "https://genius.com/13797336", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "Yes, I see the things that they wishin’ on me", "vault_lines": ["And they wishin' and wishin'", "And wishin' and wishin', they wishin' on me", "Yes, I see the things that they wishin' on me"], "meaning": "Drake has a long history of conflicts. Chris Brown, who allegedly fought Drake in a bar, has publicly defended Meek Mill over legal issues. Meek Mill and Drake had been beefing since 2015, when Meek alleged that Drake didn’t write his own bars.\n\nThese lines are likely referencing the ongoing dispute between Drake and Joe Budden. On the other Scary Hours song, “Diplomatic Immunity,” Drake throws subliminal shots at Joe when he raps:\n\nShit is complex like short niggas ‘round tall ladies\nI gotta watch who I’m talkin’ to like it’s all-ages\nI’ve seen buddin' careers turn to sit around and talk about other careers, judgin' their peers\nKnowledge from niggas who did not contribute to none of this here\n\nThe above verse is explained in detail here.", "annotation_id": "13570953", "url": "https://genius.com/13570953", "state": "accepted", "votes": 413, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "She say, “Do you love me?” I tell her, ”Only partly I only love my bed and my mama, I’m sorry”", "vault_lines": ["She say, \"Do you love me?\" I tell her, \"Only partly", "I only love my bed and my mama, I'm sorry\"", "And you know me"], "meaning": "Drake has a close relationship with his mom, Sandi Graham. He frequently mentions her in his songs, and even dedicated his 2015 track, “You & The 6” to her.\n\nDrake previously mentioned not getting much sleep on 2016’s “9,” 2017’s “Gyalchester,” and “Do Not Disturb.\" He also spoke about his lack of rest in a toast during his 30th birthday. The limited rest time he gets makes him appreciate his bed even more.\n\nTwo days following the release of “God’s Plan,“ Drake posted a reference to the lyric on his Instagram. Ten days later, he posted the lyric again in an Instagram post dedicated to his mom on her 58th birthday.", "annotation_id": "13565245", "url": "https://genius.com/13565245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1227, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "I don’t wanna die for them to miss me", "vault_lines": ["I don't wanna die for them to miss me"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing how the general public continuously praises artists after they pass away. Artists like Van Gogh were only appreciated as cultural icons posthumously, while successful musical artists such as Prince and David Bowie have garnered a ton of social media attention and memorial tributes after they died in 2016.\n\nDrake wants to earn respect and accolades while he’s alive, as he rapped on “Portland\":\n\nFuck being rich when I’m forty, man, I’m tryna make it now\n\nHe also talked about the phenomenon of posthumous fame in songs like “Gyalchester”:\n\nBury me now and I only get bigger\nThat’s word to my nigga\n\nand “Legend”:\n\nOh my God, oh my God\nIf I die, I’m a legend\nOh my God, oh my God\nIf I die, I’m a legend", "annotation_id": "13564877", "url": "https://genius.com/13564877", "state": "accepted", "votes": 150, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "Fifty Dub, I even got it tatted on me", "vault_lines": ["Fifty Dub, I even got it tatted on me"], "meaning": "Fifty Dub is short for Fifty World, where dub is slang for “w”. This is a reference to Drake’s friend and a member of his entourage, Fifty a.k.a Fif, who was murdered in September 2017 in Toronto. To commemorate his late friend, Drake tatted Fif’s face on himself in tribute.\n\nIn March of 2018, Drake showcased a chain dedicated to his late friend with the the caption, “Rest in Peace Fif…50 WORLD RWAY”", "annotation_id": "13561819", "url": "https://genius.com/13561819", "state": "accepted", "votes": 263, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "Hope I got some brothers that outlive me They gon’ tell the story, shit was different with me", "vault_lines": ["Hope I got some brothers that outlive me", "They gon' tell the story, shit was different with me"], "meaning": "Drake may be referring to his late OVO affiliate, Fif, who was gunned down in Toronto in September 2017. Drake later commemorated him with a tattoo of his face on his forearm a few days after his passing.\n\n\n\nThis may also refer to frequent collaborator, Lil Wayne, whom Drake considers a big brother. He has had a frequent history of almost deadly seizures heightened by his use of the narcotic, lean.\n\nSince God is a central theme of the song, Drake could also be comparing the 12 Apostles and Jesus Christ, to his OVO crew members and himself. As Jesus Christ’s most devout disciples, the 12 Apostles were tasked with spreading the gospel of Jesus after his death.\n\nDrake has not shied away from deifying himself using the alias ‘6 God’ in past tracks such as 6 God and 10 Bands:\n\nI’m the real 6 God, boy\n\nIf his OVO crew members outlive him, they can tell the story of his rise to fame, much like the Apostles did for Jesus Christ post-mortem. If he happens to die and somebody outlives him in his crew, they can tell you who Drake was behind closed doors, much better than what outside sources write up. That’s where “They gon' tell the story, shit was different with me” comes from.", "annotation_id": "13561781", "url": "https://genius.com/13561781", "state": "accepted", "votes": 144, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "I make sure that north-side eat", "vault_lines": ["I make sure that north-side eat", "I make sure that north-side eat, yeah"], "meaning": "“North-side” is a reference to Toronto, Canada where Drake was born. Drake constantly shouts out the city in his music, an example being the 2016 album Views, which contains a great deal of references to Toronto. He may also be referencing the north-side of Toronto where​ he grew up near the Weston Road and Jane Street area.\n\nOn the other hand, Drake has also become the global ambassador for the Toronto Raptors, a basketball franchise, representing them at events, games and other festivities.\n\n\n\nDrake also recently opened a restaurant in Toronto called Pick6ix with his OVO partner Chubbs.", "annotation_id": "13560791", "url": "https://genius.com/13560791", "state": "accepted", "votes": 129, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "Eighty-one, they’ll bring the crashers to the party", "vault_lines": ["Eighty-one, they'll bring the crashers to the party"], "meaning": "This line references Drake and OVO’s ties to the notorious Hells Angels Motorcycle Club (also referred to as A.H., HAMC or 81). The Hells Angels is considered an organized crime syndicate and one of North America’s largest criminal enterprises, with over 6,000 members involved in organized drug trafficking, extortion, prostitution and gang warfare.\n\nThe bar itself refers to the Hells Angels as 81, with 8 representing “H” and 1 representing “A” in the English alphabet. The “crashers” refer to HAMC enforcers, who commonly sport HAMC apparel with the term “Party Crasher” displayed.\n\n\n\nDrake’s connections to the HAMC stem from his security team, comprised of many notable Hells Angels' members within the Toronto area. Mike and Shane are the most recognizable examples.\n\n\n\nPictured: Drake (left) sitting in the Hells Angels T-shirt.", "annotation_id": "13560777", "url": "https://genius.com/13560777", "state": "accepted", "votes": 166, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "I been movin’ calm, don’t start no trouble with me Tryna keep it peaceful is a struggle for me", "vault_lines": ["I been movin' calm, don't start no trouble with me", "Tryna keep it peaceful is a struggle for me"], "meaning": "Although Drake squashed his major beef​, he’s no stranger to conflict.\n\nMost notably, he beefed with Philadelphia rapper Meek Mill in 2015 over claims that he didn’t write his own songs. Drake responded to these allegations with his diss tracks “Charged Up” and “Back to Back,” after which Meek Mill eventually responded with “Wanna Know.” After Drake’s OVO Fest, where he performed “Back to Back” and showed a slideshow of fan-made memes that dissed Meek Mill, Drake was widely considered the victor.\n\nDrake rapped a similar bar during his verse on Lil Wayne’s “Family Feud”:\n\nBut this isn’t all about a callin' truce\nI’m still dishin' out verbal abuse\nThat shit could get re-introduced\nIf somebody got somethin' they urgin' to prove, nigga", "annotation_id": "13560738", "url": "https://genius.com/13560738", "state": "accepted", "votes": 197, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "I can’t do this on my own, ayy (No, ayy) Someone watchin’ this shit close (Yep, close)", "vault_lines": ["I can't do this on my own, ayy (No, ayy)", "Someone watchin' this shit close (Yep, close)"], "meaning": "Drake knows that whatever God has planned for him, is for a greater good. As he crosses paths with other people and obstacles get put in his way, Drake can trust that the outcome will benefit him in the end.\n\nSometimes Drake seems to find God’s tests for him foolish:\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/180176189103673344\n\nThis concept is echoed in “Sacrifices,” featured on his 2017 release More Life, where he states in the chorus:\n\nSomeone watchin' over us, so shout goes out to Him", "annotation_id": "13560360", "url": "https://genius.com/13560360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "God’s plan, God’s plan", "vault_lines": ["God's plan, God's plan"], "meaning": "Drake frequently calls himself “6 God” in his music;  “the 6” is a nickname for Toronto (and its six boroughs), where Drake is from, so calling himself “6 God” is his way of expressing his influence on the Toronto scene.\n\nHowever, this isn’t the first time Drake’s given it up to a religious God rather than himself. On the If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late cut “Jungle,” he raps:\n\nThese days, I’m lettin' God handle all things above me\n\nAnd on “Sneakin’,” his 2016 collaboration with 21 Savage, he raps an even more closely related bar:\n\nThis is all God’s doing, man, you can’t plan it\n\nThis line portrays the effects of Judaism in Drake’s life as he believes that God has had a plan for him from the beginning. Drake here implies that it was God’s Plan for him to be successful.", "annotation_id": "13560004", "url": "https://genius.com/13560004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 236, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "I’ve been me since Scarlett Road, ayy (Road, ayy)", "vault_lines": ["I've been me since Scarlett Road, ayy (Road, ayy)"], "meaning": "Scarlett Road is a main road through Toronto, Ontario.\n\nhttps://www.google.co.uk/maps/place/Scarlett+Rd,+Toronto,+ON,+Canada/@43.683987,-79.5115265,13z/data=!4m2!3m1!1s0x882b36d65ac03c93:0x451f4be4eba4ddbb?sa=X&hl=en-GB\n\nDrake is saying that the fame never changed him as a person. He’s still the same person as he was when he lived on Weston Road.\n\nHe referenced Scarlett Road on his track “You & the 6”, saying:\n\nLet’s just call this shit even, we got some things to believe in\nDo you remember back to Weston Road, Scarlett Road?", "annotation_id": "13559852", "url": "https://genius.com/13559852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "I go hard on Southside G (Yeah, wait)", "vault_lines": ["I go hard on Southside G (Yeah, wait)"], "meaning": "Southside may be a reference to Memphis, Tennessee.\n\n\n\nShortly after this song’s release, Drizzy proceeded to feature on Memphis rapper BlocBoy JB’s hit song “Look Alive” where he represented Memphis with the opening line:\n\n901 Shelby Drive, look alive, look alive (‘live)\n\nDrake’s father’s side of the family is all based in Memphis. He even shot the “Worst Behavior” music video in Memphis, featuring his father and cousins.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/CccnAvfLPvE\n\nDrake may also be vaguely paying homage to G-Unit’s song “Straight Outta Southside”:\n\nYeah, nigga I’m straight outta Southside\n\nThis could also be a reference to the Atlanta producer, Southside. Drake tends to go hard on any Southside produced track, such as 2016’s “Grammys” or 2015’s “Digital Dash”.\n\nLastly, Drake could also be referring to the southside of the Scarborough neighborhood Galloway, which he references in a few of his songs.", "annotation_id": "13559527", "url": "https://genius.com/13559527", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "Might go down a G-O-D (Yeah, wait)", "vault_lines": ["Might go down a G-O-D (Yeah, wait)", "Might go down as G-O-D (Yeah, wait, yeah)"], "meaning": "Drake seems fixated on the idea that death will immortalize him. He mentioned the same idea in the first verse of this song, but it’s clearly been on his mind for a while—he memorably rapped about it on 2015’s “Legend”:\n\nIf I die, I’m a legend", "annotation_id": "13559520", "url": "https://genius.com/13559520", "state": "accepted", "votes": 232, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "And you know me Turn the O2 into the O3, dog", "vault_lines": ["And you know me", "Turn the O2 into the O3, dog"], "meaning": "The O2 is a reference to an entertainment complex in southeast London, The O2, which has a capacity of 20,000. Behind Manchester Arena, The O2 is the second-largest indoor venue in the United Kingdom in terms of capacity.\n\nDrake performed at The O2 in late March 2017 during his ‘The Boy Meets World Tour’ and offered to refund the entire crowd’s ticket fee after Travis Scott fell off the stage, therefore turning it into the O3 (0-free).\n\nhttps://twitter.com/JmeBBK/status/965020068244574208\n\nDrake could also be saying that so many people come to the O2 to see him that it becomes the O3.\n\n\n\nIn preparation for doing 7 dates in London, the O2 Arena actually changed the 2 to 3\n\nhttps://twitter.com/OfficialGTHH/status/1112350093271814145\n\nDrake later confirmed that the O2 Arena sign changed to O3.", "annotation_id": "13559083", "url": "https://genius.com/13559083", "state": "accepted", "votes": 377, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "Without 40, Oli, there’d be no me ’Magine if I never met the broskies", "vault_lines": ["Without 40, Oli, there'd be no me", "'Magine if I never met the broskies"], "meaning": "Noah “40” Shebib is Toronto native, OVO family member, and Drake’s main musical partner as a producer and engineer. 40’s production has become synonymous with a changing trend in hip-hop.\n\nOliver North is the nickname for Oliver El-Khatib, Drake’s manager, half of October Firm, and OVO co-founder.\n\nWithout them, he wouldn’t be the artist he is today. But, with his friends, ​Drake made an OVO Institution, or a “firm” as Drake preferred to call it during the More Life era.", "annotation_id": "13559017", "url": "https://genius.com/13559017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 313, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "I finessed down Weston Road (Ayy, ’nessed)", "vault_lines": ["I finessed down Weston Road (Ayy, 'nessed)"], "meaning": "Weston Road is a street in the Toronto area where Drake grew up. He even titled a song “Weston Road Flows” on his 2016 album Views, and on “Still Here\" he name-dropped the particular area where he grew up, the corner of Jane and Weston:\n\nHow did I finesse all of this shit from Jane and Weston?\n\nhttps://www.google.com/maps/place/1364-1356+Weston+Rd,+York,+ON+M6M+4R8,+Canada/@43.6906446,-79.501465,17z/data=!3m1!4b1!4m5!3m4!1s0x882b313377453079:0xec1e1d7901037616!8m2!3d43.6906407!4d-79.4992763", "annotation_id": "13558999", "url": "https://genius.com/13558999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "line": "Don’t pull up at 6 a.m. to cuddle with me You know how I like it when you lovin’ on me", "vault_lines": ["And you know me", "Don't pull up at 6 a.m. to cuddle with me", "You know how I like it when you lovin' on me"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t want to be a booty call in the middle of the night—he’s worth the whole night.  Aubrey has echoed similar sentiments on his 2007 mixtape deep cut, “Don’t You Have A Man\":\n\nAll my other girls all up on that subtle shit\nThey don’t wanna cut it, they want a rapper to cuddle with\n\n“6” also has correlations to Toronto, Ontario, Canada, where Drake grew up and holds ties to. The nickname “The 6ix” comes from the six boroughs that make up Toronto as well as its area code, 416.", "annotation_id": "13549995", "url": "https://genius.com/13549995", "state": "accepted", "votes": 200, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"God’s Plan\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>God’s PlanDrake10.1M", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 19, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-gods-plan-lyrics", "genius_title": "God’s Plan"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "God’s Plan (Demo)*", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-gods-plan-demo-lyrics", "genius_title": "God’s Plan (Demo)*"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "line": "Ever since I left the city—", "vault_lines": ["Ever since I left the city, you", "'Cause ever since I left the city, you", "Ever since I left the city, you, you, you", "Ever since I left the city—"], "meaning": "By ending with this line from the top of the first two verses, it seems like Drake has an infinite list of things that’ve changed with this girl since he blew up.\n\nThe longing for the old times with his girl before he was famous and she got interested in other guys/traveling the world/etc doesn’t stop at the end of the song. It’s almost as if the song is meant to be played on repeat, which in fact it was, for 12 minutes, when Drake premiered it on the the second episode of the OVO Sound show on Beats1 radio on July 25, 2015 (starts around 1:09:35).", "annotation_id": "7819563", "url": "https://genius.com/7819563", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "line": "Why you never alone? Why you always touchin’ road? Used to always stay at home Be a good girl, you was in the zone Yeah, you should just be yourself Right now, you’re someone else", "vault_lines": ["You used to, you used to", "Why you never alone?", "Why you always touchin' road?", "Used to always stay at home", "Be a good girl, you was in the zone", "Yeah, you should just be yourself", "Right now, you're someone else"], "meaning": "“Touch road” is Jamaican slang commonly used in Toronto to mean going out. Drake’s girl seems to be doing this more often than he remembers—he remembers her as a stay-at-home girl who he could get in the zone with whenever they got the chance.\n\nThe girl he once knew is gone, and even though he knows it is this new place that has changed her, he has a difficult time accepting that and pleads she should be herself.", "annotation_id": "7651636", "url": "https://genius.com/7651636", "state": "accepted", "votes": 150, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "line": "[Produced by Nineteen85]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Hotline Bling” samples the drum machine loop and jazzy Hammond organ from the Timmy Thomas soul song “Why Can’t We Live Together.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cFU-FJzPE80\n\n“Why Can’t We Live Together” was previously covered by Sade on her 1984 debut album. If Drake doesn’t already know about this connection, he would probably be very pleased:\n\n\n\nLots of people think it samples D.R.A.M.’s “Cha Cha”, which is why D.R.A.M. feels jacked:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/ONLY1DRAM/status/656311192198082561\n\nWhen the song premiered on Beats 1 Radio, the service called out the connection on Twitter:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Beats1/status/625082033002319873", "annotation_id": "7503151", "url": "https://genius.com/7503151", "state": "accepted", "votes": 124, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "line": "Doing things I taught you, gettin’ nasty for someone else You don’t need no one else You don’t need nobody else, no", "vault_lines": ["Doing things I taught you, gettin' nasty for someone else", "You don't need no one else", "You don't need nobody else, no"], "meaning": "Drake has tricks up his sleeve to hit all the right spots, so it’s no surprise his girl learned a thing or two from him.\n\n\n\nThis line is reminiscent of Kanye’s skit at the end of “Blame Game”, where Yeezy taught his girl how to get sexy for another man.", "annotation_id": "7466202", "url": "https://genius.com/7466202", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "line": "These days, all I do is Wonder if you’re bendin’ over backwards for someone else Wonder if you’re rollin’ up a Backwoods for someone else", "vault_lines": ["These days, all I do is", "Wonder if you're bendin' over backwards for someone else", "Wonder if you're rollin' up a Backwoods for someone else", "Right now, you're someone else"], "meaning": "Filled with regret, Drizzy’s got an ex on his mind. Is she sleeping with someone else? Is she rolling blunts for someone else?\n\n“Backwoods” are a cheap brand of cigar. While it’s common practice to empty out the tobacco and re-fill it with weed to create a blunt, it also sounds like Drake says “backwards.” Is this girl pleasing someone else in the bedroom? He used a similar line on “Shot For Me”.\n\n\n\n“Bending over backwards” has a double meaning as an idiom for putting a lot of effort into helping someone. As well as the more literal sexual meaning. So this can be read as the narrator not just missing the sexual side of the relationship but the emotional closeness, with them putting a lot of effort into making someone else happy, but not him anymore.", "annotation_id": "7447246", "url": "https://genius.com/7447246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 222, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "line": "Ever since I left the city, you, you, you You and me, we just don’t get along You make me feel like I did you wrong Goin’ places where you don’t belong Ever since I left the city, you You got exactly what you asked for Runnin’ out of pages in your passport Hangin’ with some girls I’ve never seen before", "vault_lines": ["Ever since I left the city, you", "Everybody knows and I feel left out", "'Cause ever since I left the city, you", "Hangin' with some girls I never seen before", "Ever since I left the city, you, you, you", "You and me, we just don't get along", "You make me feel like I did you wrong", "Goin' places where you don't belong", "You got exactly what you asked for", "Runnin' out of pages in your passport", "Hangin' with some girls I've never seen before", "Ever since I left the city—"], "meaning": "In 2009, it was reported Drake was seeing fashion designer, Nebby. Yet once he blew up and left Toronto, it seems like the relationship ended and Drake doesn’t know why.\n\n\n\nDid she want to leave with him and he said no? It’s possible, her Tumblr and Instagram see her roaming the world, running out of pages in her passport. Nowadays a lot of “Instagram models” get flown out to exotic countries by wealthy men. Could she be doing the same thing? Sure seems like it.", "annotation_id": "7446623", "url": "https://genius.com/7446623", "state": "accepted", "votes": 148, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "line": "And I know when that hotline bling That can only mean one thing I know when that hotline bling That can only mean one thing", "vault_lines": ["And I know when that hotline bling", "That can only mean one thing", "I know when that hotline bling"], "meaning": "Drake knows that at this time of night, there’s only one person who’ll call him. He also knows that the only thing she’ll want is to come over and have sex. By describing his cell phone as a hotline, he’s referencing phone sex hotlines that people call “for a good time.”\n\nNotably, Drake is using “bling” as the sound of his phone ringing. Drake’s mentor Lil Wayne originally coined the ideophone “bling bling” as a sound that could adequately express the flash of jewelry. Also worth noting that rings are jewelry, cellphone ring = bling. Wow!\n\nSlate wrote about Drake’s love-hate relationship with communications technology and even said “he’s claimed the laurels of the 21st-century bard of telephony.”", "annotation_id": "7446347", "url": "https://genius.com/7446347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 457, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "line": "Ever since I left the city, you Got a reputation for yourself now Everybody knows and I feel left out Girl, you got me down, you got me stressed out ’Cause ever since I left the city, you Started wearin’ less and goin’ out more Glasses of champagne out on the dance floor Hangin’ with some girls I never seen before", "vault_lines": ["Ever since I left the city, you", "Got a reputation for yourself now", "Everybody knows and I feel left out", "Girl, you got me down, you got me stressed out", "'Cause ever since I left the city, you", "Started wearin' less and goin' out more", "Glasses of champagne out on the dance floor", "Hangin' with some girls I never seen before", "Ever since I left the city, you, you, you", "Hangin' with some girls I've never seen before", "Ever since I left the city—"], "meaning": "Similar to the way he did on “Club Paradise,” Drake reflects on how the girls he used to kick it with in Toronto have changed. Drizzy presumably acted as a mentor, and without his guidance, these girls have gone down a route of selling themselves short to attain money and fame.\n\nThe subject seems to be his old flame from ‘09, Nebby. Her Instagram showcases her wild adventures around the world whilst at times wearing very little clothing. Drake is no stranger to dedicating entire tracks to women, as both Cece and Bria have served as muses for his songs with the similar sentiment of long distance and regret.\n\nThey say that all your old girls got somebody new", "annotation_id": "7446113", "url": "https://genius.com/7446113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 231, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "line": "You used to call me on my You used to, you used to Yeah", "vault_lines": ["You used to call me on my", "You used to, you used to", "You used to call me on my cell phone", "Call me on my cell phone"], "meaning": "Before his rise to fame, Drake was just an average guy living in the suburban part of Toronto. Back then he had a girl that adored him, but his stardom slowly tarnished their relationship as she no longer gives him any attention.\n\nThis girl is most likely his former Canadian flame Nebby whom he has mentioned in songs such as “Look What You’ve Done” and “Best I Ever Had.” Regardless of what she needed, Drake would drop everything when she’d call, especially if it was at night.\n\nDrake stops and starts in the intro, perhaps choking up with sadness at the loss he’s experiencing. Also, in the third line of the chorus, Drake omits the “you used to…” anacrusis, turning the nostalgic, regretful tone into a more demanding one, with an imperative.", "annotation_id": "7446097", "url": "https://genius.com/7446097", "state": "accepted", "votes": 530, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/1929f986016020edb588a245b7509a82.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hotline Bling\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hotline BlingDrake7.4M", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 9, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-hotline-bling-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hotline Bling"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hotline Bling (Remix) [Mixed]", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-hotline-bling-remix-mixed-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hotline Bling (Remix) [Mixed]"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes Countin’ money, you know how it goes", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Countin' money, you know how it goes"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "8098827", "url": "https://genius.com/8098827", "state": "verified", "votes": -4, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I’m way up, I stay up, I’m two up, I’m three up I had to get back to you, woah", "vault_lines": ["I'm way up, I stay up, I'm two up, I'm three up", "I had to get back to you, woah"], "meaning": "what drake is saying is that he is always above everybody. He says “I’m way up I stay up” so he’s to good that his fans support him and he’s the best.", "annotation_id": "7633535", "url": "https://genius.com/7633535", "state": "verified", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "[Part II]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The song sampled here is a 1980 instrumental called “Tinted Glass” by Network. The group was one of many formed by prolific Dutch stock-music composer Jan Stoeckart, who recorded over 1300 songs for commercial libraries.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=knR8rsGW0iU", "annotation_id": "7473969", "url": "https://genius.com/7473969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 133, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "...Shell out, all if yuh eye nuh big Always haffi look out, yuh no know if yuh head book out", "vault_lines": ["...Shell out, all if yuh eye nuh big", "Always haffi look out, yuh no know if yuh head book out"], "meaning": "This line means you always have to be vigilant cause you’ll never know if a hit is on your head or someone is trying to kill you", "annotation_id": "6566376", "url": "https://genius.com/6566376", "state": "verified", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "[Outro: Popcaan & Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Popcaan is a popular Jamaican dancehall artist and leader of Unruly Gang, a brand that’s similar to Drake’s OVO. Drake has been a fan of Popcaan for a while, in 2012 he tweeted lyrics to Popcaan’s breakout hit “Only Man She Wants.”\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/167086100236599296", "annotation_id": "6345572", "url": "https://genius.com/6345572", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Man, that was when Ethan was pushin’ a Subaru hatchback", "vault_lines": ["Man, that was when Ethan was pushin' a Subaru hatchback"], "meaning": "These last 8 lines of the first verse is Drake reminiscing on a time long ago, before he blew up and became a face in the rap industry. To convey how different it was, Drake is referring to when his friend Ethan was driving around in a Subaru hatchback, a lame-ass car compared to the whips he has now. Not to say Drake himself didn’t have humble beginnings", "annotation_id": "5428225", "url": "https://genius.com/5428225", "state": "verified", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Had a job sellin’ Girbaud jeans", "vault_lines": ["Had a job sellin' Girbaud jeans"], "meaning": "“Girbraud jeans” -> “Girbaud jeans”", "annotation_id": "4988296", "url": "https://genius.com/4988296", "state": "verified", "votes": 12, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I’m turnin’ into a nigga that thinks about money and women Like 24/7, that’s where my life took me That’s just how shit happened to go And you know Runnin’ through the 6 with my, with my", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "You know how that shit go", "Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "Yeah, but you know how that shit go", "I'm turnin' into a nigga that thinks about money and women", "Like 24/7, that's where my life took me", "That's just how shit happened to go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "And you know how that shit go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "Conventional grammar structures usually prescribe the use of “that” in relation to objects, not people. Drake’s choice of the word “that” instead of the word “who” could indicate that he considers his constant preoccupation with “money and women” a negative trait, perhaps even something subhuman.", "annotation_id": "4981981", "url": "https://genius.com/4981981", "state": "verified", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Yuh can jus suck yuh mada!! With no apology,", "vault_lines": ["Yuh can jus suck yuh mada!!"], "meaning": "‘'Suck yuh mada/mother’‘ is a highly offensive curse phrase in Jamaica. It suggests you perform oral sex on your own mother. Since it mentions the person being dissed and their mother, it’s essentially like two insults in one.\n\nSuck yuh madda is commonly used in Jamaica and can accurately ‘translate’ to “fuck off” in English slang. In this context, Popcaan’s saying that if you don’t appreciate OVO/Chromatic, you can go fuck yourself.", "annotation_id": "4964946", "url": "https://genius.com/4964946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "This is that nasty flow Top boy in this shit, I’m so international", "vault_lines": ["This is that nasty flow", "Top boy in this shit, I'm so international"], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/Drake/status/520066319773745153", "annotation_id": "4944172", "url": "https://genius.com/4944172", "state": "verified", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "And if a boy nuh like OVO or Chromatic", "vault_lines": ["And if a boy nuh like OVO or Chromatic"], "meaning": "Chromatic is a 5-man crew of Jamaican Sound System DJs. They have a tight connection with Drake and the OVO crew; the audio at the beginning of this song is from a trip the OVO crew took to Jamaica to hang out with Chromatic, Popcaan, and others. Chromatic is also mentioned in Drake’s thank you letter for the album.", "annotation_id": "4939229", "url": "https://genius.com/4939229", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Man, that was when Ethan was pushin’ a Subaru hatchback", "vault_lines": ["Man, that was when Ethan was pushin' a Subaru hatchback"], "meaning": "Subaru Hatchback is likely referring to the ever popular WRX wagon. The impreza WRX, having hit the U.S. and Canada Market by storm in 2002, was offered in a sedan and hatchback model. The vehicles were capable of 200 + horsepower stock on a flat-four boxer engine. Being rather affordable and offering substantial “bang for your buck”, Ethan clearly made a very wise choice for a whip…", "annotation_id": "4937349", "url": "https://genius.com/4937349", "state": "verified", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I’m turnin’ into a nigga that thinks about money and women Like 24/7, that’s where my life took me That’s just how shit happened to go And you know Runnin’ through the 6 with my, with my", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "You know how that shit go", "Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "Yeah, but you know how that shit go", "I'm turnin' into a nigga that thinks about money and women", "Like 24/7, that's where my life took me", "That's just how shit happened to go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "And you know how that shit go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "Drake has talked upon the topics of money and women ever since his So Far Gone days. He even had hearts and dollar bills used on the cover of the mixtape.", "annotation_id": "4936666", "url": "https://genius.com/4936666", "state": "verified", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Man, that was when Ethan was pushin’ a Subaru hatchback", "vault_lines": ["Man, that was when Ethan was pushin' a Subaru hatchback"], "meaning": "Homeboy was driving a beater compared to what they can afford now which is a reoccurring theme of referencing how he started from the bottom because most think drizzy started out rich before fame.", "annotation_id": "4936082", "url": "https://genius.com/4936082", "state": "verified", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Man, that was when Ethan was pushin’ a Subaru hatchback", "vault_lines": ["Man, that was when Ethan was pushin' a Subaru hatchback"], "meaning": "Ethan is a friend who’s been with Drake since he started from the bottom.\n\nSubaru is a Japanese automobile brand. In Canada, Subaru’s are the 14th most popular car brand; the Impreza, which can come as a hatchback, is its most popular model. Drizzy can afford better cars now.", "annotation_id": "4936081", "url": "https://genius.com/4936081", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "With my woes", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "I’m gonna offer a rebuttal to this lyric. So far those who I’ve discussed the song with believe that “woes” is his New Orleans homage to whoadie, but I believe it’s less a reference to Weezy as it is simply his woes. Drake has always been known for his emotional output on his songs, wearing his heart on his sleeve, his trouble with current bitches and past relationships, so he’s running thoo the 6 with his troubles, the blues that illuminate his life. It’s a handsomely ambiguous lyric.", "annotation_id": "4935379", "url": "https://genius.com/4935379", "state": "verified", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "With my woes", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/OVO40/status/566096453186363393\n\nDrake was really out in his city grinding with his crew before he made it to the level of stardom he is at now.\n\nIn this case “woes” translates to his friends. It is short for whoadie, a Southern slang term for “friend”. Fellow Toronto rapper Devontée has said that “woe” also stands for “working on excellence”.\n\nDrake and his squad knew exactly what their goals in life were, and aimed to achieve them. Obviously they achieved a great level of success since Drake’s newest project sold a little over 500k in a week.", "annotation_id": "4935378", "url": "https://genius.com/4935378", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes Countin’ money, you know how it goes", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Countin' money, you know how it goes"], "meaning": "According to Noisey Interview of Devontée (OVO Sound affiliate) WOES= working on excellence\n\n\n\nExcerpt from Interview: http://noisey.vice.com/en_ca/blog/devontee-woe-interview-2014\n\nWhat is Woe?\nWoe is my crew. It stands for “working on excellence.” It’s just my whole brand and my whole movement and my way of life for everyone. I want everyone to work on excellence. So, all my friends are my Woes and I feel anybody working on excellence in life is a Woe in life as well.", "annotation_id": "4932510", "url": "https://genius.com/4932510", "state": "verified", "votes": 143, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I ain’t rockin’ my jewelry, that’s on purpose", "vault_lines": ["I ain't rockin' my jewelry, that's on purpose"], "meaning": "This is a reference to when Drake was robbed at gunpoint in Toronto in 2009.  He references this incident on “The Resistance” on his debut album ‘Thank Me Later.’ https://genius.com/18187/Drake-the-resistance/Maybe-it-was-the-fast-paced-switch-up-or-the-two-guns-in-my-face-durin-the-stick-up-maybe-cause-a-girl-i-thought-i-trusted-was-who-set-the-whole-shit-up\n\nDrake has confirmed that it is a true story in a radio interview. http://youtu.be/0dnRRScx-Gw?t=2m56s", "annotation_id": "4923131", "url": "https://genius.com/4923131", "state": "verified", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I’ve always been me, I guess I know myself Shakiness, man, I don’t have no time for that", "vault_lines": ["I've always been me, I guess I know myself", "Shakiness, man, I don't have no time for that"], "meaning": "Quintessential aphorism, Drake has always maintained his strength of Character despite his rise to fame.\n\n“Shakiness” is modern Jamaican slang used to refer to instability or uncertainty in a situation. In this context he just doesn’t have time for fuckery.", "annotation_id": "4917407", "url": "https://genius.com/4917407", "state": "verified", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I know a nigga named Johnny Bling He put me on to the finer things", "vault_lines": ["I know a nigga named Johnny Bling", "He put me on to the finer things"], "meaning": "Most likely a reference to Johnny Dang, aka TV Johnny, aka “The King of Bling,” a legendary jeweler from Houston, Texas. Here is a picture of Drake and Johnny. Also pictured is another famous Johnny…", "annotation_id": "4917099", "url": "https://genius.com/4917099", "state": "verified", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I’m way up, I stay up, I’m two up, I’m three up I had to get back to you, woah", "vault_lines": ["I'm way up, I stay up, I'm two up, I'm three up", "I had to get back to you, woah"], "meaning": "I’m two up should be “I’m too up”. He then says “three up” to refer to the double meaning too/two, saying he’s even more than “too up”.", "annotation_id": "4912112", "url": "https://genius.com/4912112", "state": "verified", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I know a nigga named Johnny Bling He put me on to the finer things", "vault_lines": ["I know a nigga named Johnny Bling", "He put me on to the finer things"], "meaning": "Most likely referring to the famous jeweler TV Johnny. Well know for having many famous clients. http://www.tvjohnny.net", "annotation_id": "4905029", "url": "https://genius.com/4905029", "state": "verified", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Man, I’m talkin’ way before hashtags I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes (Yeah!)", "vault_lines": ["Man, I'm talkin' way before hashtags", "I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "This isn’t the first time Drake is saying he’s been at it before hashtags and Twitter names https://genius.com/3271781/Drake-0-to-100-the-catch-up/", "annotation_id": "4904731", "url": "https://genius.com/4904731", "state": "verified", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "You know how that shit go", "Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "Yeah, but you know how that shit go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "And you know how that shit go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "https://www.thefader.com/2015/02/13/what-does-drake-really-mean-when-he-talks-about-his-woe\n\nDrake talks about what his woes mean here.", "annotation_id": "4904157", "url": "https://genius.com/4904157", "state": "verified", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "My city too turned up, I’ll take the fine for that This been where you find me at That’s been where you find me at", "vault_lines": ["My city too turned up, I'll take the fine for that", "This been where you find me at", "That's been where you find me at"], "meaning": "He’s also saying that he’ll take the credit for the hype surrounding his city.", "annotation_id": "4899681", "url": "https://genius.com/4899681", "state": "verified", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "it supposed to be “no sleepin in the streets” niko said this to drake in the “jungle” video\nhttp://youtu.be/0lKH5dMNcq0?t=13m1s", "annotation_id": "4899261", "url": "https://genius.com/4899261", "state": "verified", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "You know how that shit go", "Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "Yeah, but you know how that shit go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "And you know how that shit go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "The hook does not say “You know how that shit go” He is saying “You know how that should go” Listen to it carefully. I also have the clean version of the Album it does not get edited out because he’s not saying “shit”. Also for more clarification Noah “40” Shebib tweets the Line on his Twitter https://twitter.com/OVO40/status/566096453186363393\nOnly In the 5th Line in the Verse 2 he says “You know how that shit go”\n\nAlso Woes DOES NOT! mean working on Excellence its a south slang word Woadie Meaning “my Friend” “My Nigga” for example. So he took a short form of it and said woes. Running through the 6 with my working on excellence makes 0 sense.", "annotation_id": "4898338", "url": "https://genius.com/4898338", "state": "verified", "votes": 644, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Sleeping in this case refers to not knowing what is going on, not actual sleeping. He’s saying that you don’t really know what goes on in these streets.", "annotation_id": "4897823", "url": "https://genius.com/4897823", "state": "verified", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Man, I’m talkin’ way before hashtags I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes (Yeah!)", "vault_lines": ["Man, I'm talkin' way before hashtags", "I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "Drake is really conscious of his life before stardom and everything that he went through to get to where he is now. Unlike other artists who try to rewrite their own history (being classy mafia leaders in the NY projects), Drake accepts and relishes all that he did to get where he is today. A lot of fans really like the honest approach to life that is unique in Drake’s music", "annotation_id": "4896884", "url": "https://genius.com/4896884", "state": "verified", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I’ve always been me, I guess I know myself Shakiness, man, I don’t have no time for that", "vault_lines": ["I've always been me, I guess I know myself", "Shakiness, man, I don't have no time for that"], "meaning": "Drake hasn’t change who he is for anyone, he’s always maintained his strength of character despite his rise to fame. As he said back on “Say What’s Real”:\n\nDon’t listen to anybody that knew me because to have known me would mean there’s a new me and if you think I changed in the slightest could’ve fooled me\n\n“Shakiness” is modern Jamaican slang that refers to instability or uncertainty in a situation.", "annotation_id": "4895453", "url": "https://genius.com/4895453", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "*Machine gun shots*", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The gunshots refer to the intro of the albums previous song “Energy,” where the DJ tells us gunshots mean the song will be rewound. This is why we hear the song’s hook when these shots finish.\n\nThis also relates to Popcaan’s earlier statement in the outro that “if you diss you will get execute”. Shots will pop off if you speak ill of the OVO crew.", "annotation_id": "4895362", "url": "https://genius.com/4895362", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Hol’ it yute, hol’ it, hol’ it, hol’ it, hol’ it, hol’ it No sleepin’ in the streets! Shaky warrior", "vault_lines": ["Hol' it yute, hol' it, hol' it, hol' it, hol' it, hol' it", "No sleepin' in the streets!"], "meaning": "This part of the intro is taken from a clip of the OVO crew in Jamaica. The person is speaking in Patois. “Hol it” means pay attention. He grabs the attention of a man passed out on the corner and tells him he can’t sleep in the streets. Check out the 3:38 mark:\n\nhttps://vimeo.com/116727998\n\n“Shaky Warrior” means someone is acting suspect. It’s also a song by Jamaican artist Popcaan, who appears on the outro of this track.", "annotation_id": "4894270", "url": "https://genius.com/4894270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 207, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "This is that nasty flow Top boy in this shit, I’m so international", "vault_lines": ["This is that nasty flow", "Top boy in this shit, I'm so international"], "meaning": "Top Boy was a British television drama that aired from 2011 to 2013. Drake, who was once on a TV Drama himself, is a pretty big fan of the show.\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/520066319773745153\n\nThe TV series was shown not only in Britain but also the United States, making it international like Drake, who is Canadian, but is quite popular all over the world.", "annotation_id": "4892756", "url": "https://genius.com/4892756", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Had a job sellin’ Girbaud jeans", "vault_lines": ["Had a job sellin' Girbaud jeans"], "meaning": "Drake reminisces about the days when his hustle was a more traditional job; selling jeans made by Marithé + François Girbaud, an international clothing company based in France that was very popular throughout the 1990’s.", "annotation_id": "4892742", "url": "https://genius.com/4892742", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Boi-1da, what’s poppin’? Yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A quick shoutout to Toronto-bred producer Boi-1da who is Drake’s good friend and OVO label-mate. He also produced this track along with SkySense & Vinylz.\n\n\n\nBoi-1da is pictured left.", "annotation_id": "4892670", "url": "https://genius.com/4892670", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "My nigga Jibba, he whip it, I ride in the passenger", "vault_lines": ["My nigga Jibba, he whip it, I ride in the passenger"], "meaning": "Much like his other homie Ethan, Drake and Jibba (shown in the Jungle premier video) drive or whip it around Toronto until the brakes fall off.", "annotation_id": "4890971", "url": "https://genius.com/4890971", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "...Shell out, all if yuh eye nuh big Always haffi look out, yuh no know if yuh head book out", "vault_lines": ["...Shell out, all if yuh eye nuh big", "Always haffi look out, yuh no know if yuh head book out"], "meaning": "Popcaan’s singing the intro to his song “Ghetto” off his debut album Where We Come From:\n\nAlways haffi look out\nYuh no know fi yuh head book out\n\nPopcaan is advising you to keep your head on a swivel. “Book out” can mean to that your head is sticking out in the light due to your popularity or that people have put money on your head like they would put money on the books or records.", "annotation_id": "4889471", "url": "https://genius.com/4889471", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Pray the real live forever, man Pray the fakes get exposed", "vault_lines": ["Pray the real live forever, man", "Pray the fakes get exposed"], "meaning": "Drake expresses a similar sentiment to A$AP Rocky’s banger “Multiply” with a strike at the many rappers who have lied about their past experiences. Drake explains on “You & The 6”:\n\nCause I’m not acting tough\nOr making stories up ‘bout where I’m actually from\n\n\n\nThis lyric became rather ironic when Quentin Miller’s reference track was leaked during the Meek Mill vs Drake beef.", "annotation_id": "4889377", "url": "https://genius.com/4889377", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I know a nigga named Johnny Bling He put me on to the finer things", "vault_lines": ["I know a nigga named Johnny Bling", "He put me on to the finer things"], "meaning": "A reference to a childhood friend, Jonny Bling. His love of gold, diamonds, etc. rubbed off on Drake at an early age.\n\nSome have suggested that this is a reference to Johnny “Bling” Dang, aka TV Johnny, a legendary jeweler from Houston, Texas, although Dang has never been known as “Johnny Bling.”\n\n\n\n Dang is in the middle, with another famous Johnny and OVO affiliate on the right.", "annotation_id": "4889239", "url": "https://genius.com/4889239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Man, I’m talkin’ way before hashtags I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes (Yeah!)", "vault_lines": ["Man, I'm talkin' way before hashtags", "I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "All the events Drake discusses take place in an era before the world was consumed by smartphones and hashtags (pre-2007). He made a similar reference on “0 to 100/The Catch Up”:\n\nMe and Noel been at it before Twitter names", "annotation_id": "4889039", "url": "https://genius.com/4889039", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes Countin’ money, you know how it goes", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "Countin' money, you know how it goes"], "meaning": "416 and 647 are two of three area codes for the city of Toronto. Referring to Toronto as “the 6” is something Drake invented and was not a widely used metonym before he tried to popularize it. Torontonians did not react positively.\n\n“Woes” is an abbreviation of the word “Woadie,” which is New Orleans slang for people from the same ward (which sounds like “WHOA-dee” when pronounced with a New Orleans accent). Drake’s home city of Toronto is divided into 44 wards, each with its own city councillor so even though the slang is southern, it still makes sense for the 6.\n\nFellow Toronto rapper and OVO affiliate Devontée has said that “woe” also stands for “working on excellence,” although Drake has never confirmed it.", "annotation_id": "4888996", "url": "https://genius.com/4888996", "state": "accepted", "votes": 460, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "My city too turned up, I’ll take the fine for that This been where you find me at That’s been where you find me at", "vault_lines": ["My city too turned up, I'll take the fine for that", "This been where you find me at", "That's been where you find me at"], "meaning": "Drake cracks a joke about the the NBA fining him $25,000 for allegedly recruiting Kevin Durant to the Toronto Raptors. During the 2014 OVO Fest, Drake, the Raptors' “Global Ambassador,” suggested Durant come play for his hometown team. The team had the option of forcing Drake to step down from his Ambassador position or take the fine, so the team took the fine.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ECk4v7pSsZs", "annotation_id": "4888846", "url": "https://genius.com/4888846", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Niggas want my spot and don’t deserve it I don’t like how serious they take themselves", "vault_lines": ["Niggas want my spot and don't deserve it", "I don't like how serious they take themselves"], "meaning": "After dropping hit after hit, Drake’s solidified his position as king of the rap game. He knows people are coming to replace him but he feels that none of them are worthy of his spot.\n\n\nIf I ain't the greatest then I'm headed for it― “0 to 100/The Catch Up” by Drake", "annotation_id": "4888823", "url": "https://genius.com/4888823", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I’m way up, I stay up, I’m two up, I’m three up I had to get back to you, woah", "vault_lines": ["I'm way up, I stay up, I'm two up, I'm three up", "I had to get back to you, woah"], "meaning": "While people often try to get “one up” on others, Drake is three up on his competition. This could be referring to the number of studio albums Drake had released to that date.\n\n\n\nThe phrase “way up, stay up” may come from a Chi Ching Ching song by that title. Popcaan, who is on this song’s outro, is featured on that track.", "annotation_id": "4888709", "url": "https://genius.com/4888709", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Yeah, this that Oliver, 40, Niko shit man 15 Fort York shit, y’know?", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, this that Oliver, 40, Niko shit man", "15 Fort York shit, y'know?"], "meaning": "15 Fort York Blvd is the condo in Toronto where Drake’s longtime friend and producer 40 used to live. It’s also where Drake’s first projects were put together.\n\n\n\nOliver El-Khatib is the creative director and brand manager of OVO, while Niko is Drake’s right hand man and responsible for OVO’s merchandise. Niko and Drake have been friends since Drake was on Degrassi. They both helped Drake find OVO.", "annotation_id": "4888303", "url": "https://genius.com/4888303", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I might declare it a holiday as soon as Baka get back on the road Yeah, but you know how that shit go They so irrational, they don’t wanna patch it up They wanna mash it up, woah", "vault_lines": ["You know how that shit go", "Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "I might declare it a holiday as soon as Baka get back on the road", "Yeah, but you know how that shit go", "They so irrational, they don't wanna patch it up", "They wanna mash it up, woah", "I had to get back to you, woah", "And you know how that shit go"], "meaning": "Baka is Drake’s friend who reps Scarborough, Toronto’s eastern region. He was charged for Human Trafficking in June 2014 and served an 11 month sentence.\n\nBaka tweeted “#knowyourself” in May 2014, so this song may be paying homage to him, or Drake may have been cooking this tape since that time.\nhttps://twitter.com/BakaAKAnotnice/status/472184374822060033\n\nIn April 2015, Baka was released from jail. While there was no indication that Drake actually did declare that day a holiday, there were 11 prayer hand emojis in the Instagram post announcing his friend’s return.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/1MTDldjQPk/?taken-by=champagnepapi", "annotation_id": "4888232", "url": "https://genius.com/4888232", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I ain’t rockin’ my jewelry, that’s on purpose", "vault_lines": ["I ain't rockin' my jewelry, that's on purpose"], "meaning": "Drake isn’t showing off his jewelry because his wealth speaks for itself. He doesn’t need to brag, but does anyway – note the pun on “rockin'” (jewelry is diamond-encrusted), that’s on purpose.\n\nHe’s grown considerably since he “Started From the Bottom”:\n\nI wear every single chain, even when I’m in the house cause we…\n\nIt should also be noted Drake was once robbed at gunpoint in Toronto in 2009. He refers to the incident on “The Resistance,” from his debut album Thank Me Later.", "annotation_id": "4888203", "url": "https://genius.com/4888203", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Don’t fuck with them niggas, they too irrational, woah", "vault_lines": ["Don't fuck with them niggas, they too irrational, woah"], "meaning": "This lyric, and culminating hook is about Drake and his long time friends, particularly those from Scarborough. He only messes with his close friends, sticking to his “no new friends” policy from 2013.", "annotation_id": "4888187", "url": "https://genius.com/4888187", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I’ve always been me, I guess I know myself Shakiness, man, I don’t have no time for that", "vault_lines": ["I've always been me, I guess I know myself", "Shakiness, man, I don't have no time for that"], "meaning": "Drake hasn’t change who he is for anyone. This can be referenced back to his “Say What’s Real” line “don’t listen to anybody that knew me because to have known me would mean there’s a new me and if you think I changed in the slightest could’ve fooled me”", "annotation_id": "4888185", "url": "https://genius.com/4888185", "state": "verified", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "Reps up is in here Got P Reign and Chubby and TJ and Winnie and woah Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "vault_lines": ["You know how that shit go", "Reps up is in here", "Got P Reign and Chubby and TJ and Winnie and woah", "Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "Yeah, but you know how that shit go", "And you know how that shit go"], "meaning": "Reps Up Entertainment is the Toronto record label Drake’s homeboy and OVO affiliate P Reign is signed.\n\nChubby or Chubbs is Drake’s bodyguard. He literally plays Drake’s right hand man in the “Hold On We’re Going Home” video as he is mostly Drake’s right side for the video.\n\nWinnie is a member of Reps Up Records and is a close affiliate of Drake’s. He appears in the “Energy” music video alongside Drake as he raps the line:\n\nY’all don’t wanna see Win Win, 50 or Whoa\n\n\n\nPictured left", "annotation_id": "4888162", "url": "https://genius.com/4888162", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I’m turnin’ into a nigga that thinks about money and women Like 24/7, that’s where my life took me That’s just how shit happened to go And you know Runnin’ through the 6 with my, with my", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "You know how that shit go", "Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "Yeah, but you know how that shit go", "I'm turnin' into a nigga that thinks about money and women", "Like 24/7, that's where my life took me", "That's just how shit happened to go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "And you know how that shit go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "Its interesting that Drake is only just now admitting to thinking about money and women, since both have been subjects in Drake’s music since his So Far Gone days. He even had hearts and dollar bills on the cover of the mixtape.", "annotation_id": "4887973", "url": "https://genius.com/4887973", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go", "vault_lines": ["I was runnin' through the 6 with my woes", "You know how that shit go", "Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "Yeah, but you know how that shit go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my, with my", "I was running through the 6 with my woes", "And you know how that shit go", "Runnin' through the 6 with my woes"], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/OVO40/status/566096453186363393\n\nDrake was really out in his city (Toronto’s area codes are 416 and 647) grinding with his crew before he made it to the level of stardom he is at now. Toronto is also divided into six boroughs (like New York City) and include Old Toronto, York, North York, East York, Etobicoke and Scarborough.\n\n“Woes” is an abbreviation of the word “Woadie,” which is New Orleans slang for people from the same ward (which sounds like “WHOA-dee” when pronounced with a New Orleans accent). Drake’s home city of Toronto is divided into 44 wards, each with its own city councillor so even though the slang is southern, it still makes sense for the 6.\n\nFellow Toronto rapper and OVO affiliate Devontée has said that “woe” also stands for “working on excellence,” although Drake has never confirmed it.", "annotation_id": "4887951", "url": "https://genius.com/4887951", "state": "accepted", "votes": 431, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I want that Ferrari, then I swerve I want that Bugatti, just to hurt", "vault_lines": ["I want that Ferrari, then I swerve", "I want that Bugatti, just to hurt"], "meaning": "Drake wants to buy a Ferrari and Bugatti just make an impression on his haters. Since Drizzy’s worth about $75 million, he has more than enough money to blow on these lavish vehicles, which cost up to $3 million and $2.7 million respectively.", "annotation_id": "4887895", "url": "https://genius.com/4887895", "state": "accepted", "votes": 178, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "line": "I had a yellow TechnoMarine Then Kanye dropped, it was polos and backpacks", "vault_lines": ["I had a yellow TechnoMarine", "Then Kanye dropped, it was polos and backpacks"], "meaning": "TechnoMarine is a brand of watch that was popular with rappers in the early 2000s. Kanye refers to them as outdated on “Last Call.”\n\nKanye went onto popularize the Ralph Lauren Polo and backpack “prep-rapper” look when he (and The College Dropout) blew up in 2004.\n\nOn his Chicago tour stop of the Aubrey & the Three Migos Tour promoting his fifth album Scorpion, Drake performed this lyric as “Kanye flopped” rather than “Kanye dropped.” Kanye and Drake both released albums in June 2018: ye and Scorpion respectively. Both albums debuted at number 1 on the Billboard 200, but Kanye bowed with a paltry 208,000 album-equivalent units compared to Drake’s 732,000. Prior to this, Drake had been taking shots at Kanye over what he perceived to be a betrayal since immediately after the release of Pusha-T’s DAYTONA, notably on “Duppy Freestyle” and “Behind Barz.”\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EdvqKuzc4r8", "annotation_id": "4887766", "url": "https://genius.com/4887766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 167, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b19e6eca082dd81ae04da590082ac31e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Know Yourself\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Know YourselfDrake5.4M", "status": "done", "targets": 55, "clicked": 55, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-know-yourself-lyrics", "genius_title": "Know Yourself"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "After a while, girl, they all seem the same", "vault_lines": ["After a while, girl, they all seem the same"], "meaning": "After a while of luxurious and carefree lifestyle choices with these women, they all seem to come off the same to him. It is rumoured that Drake was listening to ‘All Girls Are The Same’ by fellow singer Juice WRLD when coming up with this line from his pen. Clever, Aubrey. Clever indeed.", "annotation_id": "40639752", "url": "https://genius.com/40639752", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "[Interlude: Drake] You not gon’ come? I guess I’m ’bout to just kick it here then", "vault_lines": ["You not gon' come?", "I guess I'm 'bout to just kick it here then"], "meaning": "To Aubrey’s dismay, it appears his lover does not show any intent on appearing at his location. He then decides to kick back on his own, spending this solemn and somber night by himself, with nothing but cups of the rosé and his own shadow.", "annotation_id": "40639733", "url": "https://genius.com/40639733", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "I think I’m addicted to naked pictures", "vault_lines": ["I think I'm addicted to naked pictures"], "meaning": "It appears Aubrey has an addiction of taking nude snapshots! Cheeky, cheeky Aubrey!", "annotation_id": "40639707", "url": "https://genius.com/40639707", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "The woman that I would try Is happy with a good guy", "vault_lines": ["The woman that I would try", "Is happy with a good guy"], "meaning": "The woman that Aubrey Drake Graham is trying to date is happily with another man.", "annotation_id": "40598256", "url": "https://genius.com/40598256", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Just throw up while I hold your hair back", "vault_lines": ["Just throw up while I hold your hair back"], "meaning": "Hey Geniuses! This lyric is about Drake’s willingness to hold back this lucky woman (or man)’s hair as they regurgitate in a toilet. Drake has always been known for his virtues of kindness and compassion when taking care of his fellow partymates.", "annotation_id": "40118220", "url": "https://genius.com/40118220", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "I’ve been talkin’ crazy, girl, I’m lucky that you picked up", "vault_lines": ["I've been talkin' crazy, girl, I'm lucky that you picked up", "Lucky that you stayed on"], "meaning": "Drake is acknowledging that he has been talking crazy, likely due to intoxication, and is in his feelings about this girl answering his drunk call.", "annotation_id": "39161896", "url": "https://genius.com/39161896", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "All my people been here I see all of her friends here", "vault_lines": ["All my people been here", "I see all of her friends here"], "meaning": "Despite Drizzy admitting that all his people (friends and acquaintances) are there, yet his ex lover’s friends stand out to him. This showcases that he is not over her, and even surrounding himself with large groups of people- cannot make him forget his past relationship.", "annotation_id": "31350403", "url": "https://genius.com/31350403", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Guess she don’t have the time to kick it no more", "vault_lines": ["Guess she don't have the time to kick it no more"], "meaning": "Damnn", "annotation_id": "30364092", "url": "https://genius.com/30364092", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Too many drinks have been given to me I got some women that’s livin’ off me Paid for their flights and hotels, I’m ashamed", "vault_lines": ["Too many drinks have been given to me", "I got some women that's livin' off me", "Paid for their flights and hotels, I'm ashamed"], "meaning": "The flow and cadence of these and the next 3 lines were interpolated by SZA while featuring on Travis Scott song “TELEKINESIS”:\n\nI still wanna be with you, trust me, I know that’s insane\nI’d rather fuck on you than fuck on lames\nI did some shit in Berlin, I’m OD\nWe both ain’t shit and it’s workin' for me\nWorkin' for me, yeah", "annotation_id": "30210385", "url": "https://genius.com/30210385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "I’ve had sex four times this week, I’ll explain Havin’ a hard time adjustin’ to fame Sprite in there mixed up", "vault_lines": ["I've had sex four times this week, I'll explain", "Havin' a hard time adjustin' to fame", "Sprite in there mixed up"], "meaning": "As a coping mechanism for the stress of his new found fame, Drake is drinking Dirty Sprite a.k.a ‘Lean’ as well as having, what he believes, to be an excessive amount of sex", "annotation_id": "29436043", "url": "https://genius.com/29436043", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "And sittin’ talkin’ ’bout bitches that we almost had", "vault_lines": ["And sittin' talkin' 'bout bitches that we almost had"], "meaning": "This song is about drake being in love with an ex girlfriend but then here he talks about being addicted to sitting and talking about women he almost had. The reason is because any woman that he gets can’t compare to the ex girlfriend that he is still in love with. He is trying to move past it, but all attempts fail and so he starts to romanticize girls that he almost had and wondering where those could’ve gone. In reality this is normal behavior for someone going through a tough break up as he tries to forget his ex.", "annotation_id": "28316730", "url": "https://genius.com/28316730", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Lucky that you stayed on I need someone to put this weight on Fuck, I’m sorry", "vault_lines": ["Lucky that you stayed on", "I need someone to put this weight on"], "meaning": "He could be “apologizing” for several things at this point.\n\n\nFor one, he could be apologizing to himself or some higher figure for becoming a mess and for all of the sins he’s recently committed (such as the heavy indulgence in lust/sex).\nHe could be apologizing to the girl’s (possible) new guy for the disrespect dished to him throughout this song (“Fuck that nigga…”).\nHe could be repenting to this girl for messing up and dialing her up with all these issues…\n\n\n\nIt could also be any combination of all three.\n\nThis closure is how Drake ultimately reacts to the “shame” he mentions he has earlier in the verse. Whether this is sincere or not…up for some debate (considering he is severely drunk at this point).", "annotation_id": "1530557", "url": "https://genius.com/1530557", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "[Intro: Ericka Lee]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The woman whose phone calls are featured in this song, Ericka Lee, sued Drake over his use of her voice:\n\nAccording to Billboard, Lee filed a lawsuit in California federal court yesterday [February 2, 2012]. She claims that Drake texted her messages like, “U basically made that song” and “It’s shit without you.” While Drake has reportedly offered her a percentage of the publishing money, Lee seeks credit as a co-writer of the song and is seeking other damages.\n\nThe case was settled the following year out of court.\n\nDrake later went on to mention her on “Redemption” in 2016, saying:\n\nErika sued me and opened a business", "annotation_id": "570742", "url": "https://genius.com/570742", "state": "accepted", "votes": 417, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Yeah, I hope no one heard that ‘Cause if they did, we gon’ be in some trouble, yeah If they did, we gon’ be in some trouble, trouble, oh", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I just walked in", "Yeah, I hope no one heard that", "'Cause if they did, we gon' be in some trouble, yeah", "If they did, we gon' be in some trouble, trouble, oh"], "meaning": "He hopes nobody heard this white girl say the word because there may be an altercation over its usage. The ending is supposed to illustrate that he feels more alone at this party of distanced, hostile people than he would if he were actually alone, and it’s made him even more depressed than he already was about missing his ex.", "annotation_id": "438896", "url": "https://genius.com/438896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 207, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Her white friend said, “You niggas crazy,” I hope no one heard that", "vault_lines": ["Her white friend said, \"You niggas crazy,\" I hope no one heard that", "Yeah, I hope no one heard that"], "meaning": "The word “nigga” is considered a reclamation word, heavily rooted in black culture and speech, so it’s noteworthy for Drake to mention her being white because it is considered taboo for non-black people to use the word (especially white people), even if it is said in jest.", "annotation_id": "304571", "url": "https://genius.com/304571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 185, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "I’m just sayin’ you could do better Tell me, have you heard that lately? I’m just sayin’ you could do better And I’ll start hatin’ only if you make me", "vault_lines": ["I'm just sayin' you could do better", "Tell me, have you heard that lately?", "And I'll start hatin' only if you make me"], "meaning": "He actually implying that he himself is better, only because he is drunk, he is not saying her boyfriend is a bad boyfriend, as earlier stated in the lyrics, he is  implying that her current guy isn’t bad, just that he’s better.\n\nThe song continues with sections in which Drake tries to coerce his unwilling ex over the phone.  His efforts ultimately go unrewarded.", "annotation_id": "294817", "url": "https://genius.com/294817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 216, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Are you drunk right now?", "vault_lines": ["Are you drunk right now?", "I need you right now, are you down to listen to me?"], "meaning": "Indeed he is, and she can tell because such blunt honesty rarely comes from someone sober.\n\nThis is a sample from a convo with Ericka Lee. Lee sued Drake for using her voice without her permission and excluding her from royalties, and they settled out of court.", "annotation_id": "235095", "url": "https://genius.com/235095", "state": "accepted", "votes": 243, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Bet that you know them, I won’t say no names", "vault_lines": ["Bet that you know them, I won't say no names"], "meaning": "He has been messing with girls within her circle, so instead of breaking up friendships, their names will remain anonymous.\n\nAlso, this is  a statement directed to the listener as if he doesn’t want to expose the girls who are living off of him to the rest of the world and embarrass them.", "annotation_id": "232966", "url": "https://genius.com/232966", "state": "accepted", "votes": 100, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "But I’ve been drinkin’ so much That I’ma call her anyway and say", "vault_lines": ["But I've been drinkin' so much", "That I'ma call her anyway and say", "I've been drinkin' so much", "That I'ma call you anyway and say"], "meaning": "Here we see an unusually aggressive side of Drake as he claims to be better than any new guy in his ex’s life.\n\nThis may be a reference to Rihanna and her on-again-off-again relationship with Chris Brown—which ended on a bad note after a violent dispute. Though Drake and Rih were never official, it was rumored for years that they were dating.", "annotation_id": "232956", "url": "https://genius.com/232956", "state": "accepted", "votes": 319, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Flight’s in the morning What you doin’ that’s so important?", "vault_lines": ["Flight's in the morning", "What you doin' that's so important?"], "meaning": "Fun Fact: Drake tweeted this line a few weeks before dropping the song:\n\nhttp://twitter.com/#!/drakkardnoir/status/76950821367062529\n\nHe’s saying he feels hurt because he’s only here for tonight, he’s leaving in the morning, but the girl still isn’t interested in seeing him and she is acting like she is on the road all the time too.", "annotation_id": "229835", "url": "https://genius.com/229835", "state": "accepted", "votes": 167, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Cups of the rosé", "vault_lines": ["Cups of the rosé", "Uh, cups of the XO"], "meaning": "Rosé is a wine (and can come in a champagne or sparkling variety). Rosé Imperial is made by Moet.\n\n\n\nSince champagne is usually for celebrating, this shows a turn of events that Drake wasn’t planning for – drunken emotions.", "annotation_id": "229816", "url": "https://genius.com/229816", "state": "accepted", "votes": 163, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "I don’t think I’m conscious of makin’ monsters Outta the women I sponsor ’til it all goes bad But, shit, it’s all good", "vault_lines": ["I don't think I'm conscious of makin' monsters", "Outta the women I sponsor 'til it all goes bad", "But, shit, it's all good"], "meaning": "The more Drizzy sponsors, or pays, for a woman’s wants, the more blinded the women become by the money.  They turn into fiends who are only out for Drake’s wallet, or he could be suggesting that he makes monsters out of the women he’s been with in his mind after the relationship goes bad to justify his actions or its failure.\n\nThese four lines really sum up Drake’s complicated relationship with women. Referring to women as bitches in one couplet and then realizing his own role in turning  them into “bitches” in the next.\n\nHe keeps making the same mistakes with the same type of girls he throws money at and he’s starting to realize it.\n\nThis could also relate to how he portrays his girls in his music: he can never seem to write songs about a healthy relationship, but he’s not aware of this until it’s too late and he’s out of the relationship. He makes a “monster” out of them when he writes a song, but he can never do that until it “all goes bad”.", "annotation_id": "229495", "url": "https://genius.com/229495", "state": "accepted", "votes": 146, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "I say fuck that nigga that you think you found And since you pick up, I know he’s not around, oh, oh", "vault_lines": ["I say fuck that nigga that you think you found", "And since you pick up, I know he's not around, oh, oh", "And since you pick up, I know he's not around"], "meaning": "If she picked up a call from her ex, her current boyfriend must not be around. Otherwise, she’s gonna have some explaining to do!\n\nThere are few other notable things about these lines:\n\n\nDrake uses the term “think” as a way of making her doubt the dude is good for her.\n“Found” denotes she merely was looking for someone to replace the need for Drake.\nHer answering the phone gives him a reason to believe he still has a part of her.", "annotation_id": "229467", "url": "https://genius.com/229467", "state": "accepted", "votes": 368, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "line": "Uh, cups of the XO", "vault_lines": ["Cups of the rosé", "Uh, cups of the XO"], "meaning": "XO Cafe Patron, though XO is a designation for “Extra Old” and could be a variety of liquors. It could be an allusion to Hennessy Cognac XO, too.\n\n\n\nIt’s notable that from the beginning of the 1st verse to now, Drizzy goes from sipping champagne (associated with celebration) to sipping XO, which is not only harder in alcohol content, but more associated with a different type of partying.\n\nAlso, XO references fellow Torontonian The Weeknd, the R&B singer whose label is called “XO” (he often sings about drinking lean from a styrofoam cup). Drake does frequently refer to lean in songs (cf. “I’m On One”).", "annotation_id": "229411", "url": "https://genius.com/229411", "state": "accepted", "votes": 256, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/a0822204644f5abd6f1c96f200bc643c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Marvins Room\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Marvins RoomDrake3.7M", "status": "done", "targets": 24, "clicked": 24, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-marvins-room-lyrics", "genius_title": "Marvins Room"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "[Part I: 0 to 100]", "vault_lines": ["0 to 100 / The Catch Up Lyrics"], "meaning": "This song was allegedly supposed to be Diddy’s but Frank Dukes shared it with Boi-1da and Drake ended up making the song. In an interview with Noisey, Frank Dukes stated:\n\nI just kinda wrote it and gave it to Boi-1da, who was in session at the time in Miami, this was with Diddy. This was like the whole controversy of the incident. From what I understand he made the beat on the spot, played it for Diddy and Diddy passed on it, he said it wasn’t life changing enough and that was that.”\n\nAfterwards Drake crafted the song and the superfluous beef with Diddy and Drake began. Be sure to check out the full analysis made by TMZ.", "annotation_id": "4961787", "url": "https://genius.com/4961787", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "[Produced by Boi-1da, Frank Dukes, Noah “40” Shebib, & Nineteen85]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/Boi1da/status/473262859418222592\n\nBoi-1da mentioning that there are no samples is slightly misleading – the beat did contain a sample of “Vibez”, a composition played by Frank Dukes, that will be a part of the upcoming 4th volume of his Kingsway Music Library.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9_Rm2TEf-9k", "annotation_id": "3274596", "url": "https://genius.com/3274596", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Maybe I’m searchin’ for the problems askin’ what was said and who was involved Too focused on people’s feedback and provin’ ’em wrong", "vault_lines": ["Maybe I'm searchin' for the problems askin' what was said and who was involved", "Too focused on people's feedback and provin' 'em wrong"], "meaning": "Like he says in “Think Good,” Drake is constantly exceeding expectation.\n\nWhile some stereotype Drake as a soft rapper due to tracks like “I Get Lonely Too”, he spits a killer verse on another track.", "annotation_id": "3272685", "url": "https://genius.com/3272685", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "The other night Lavish Lee told me that I’m all these people listen to She said they love me unconditional", "vault_lines": ["The other night Lavish Lee told me that I'm all these people listen to", "She said they love me unconditional"], "meaning": "Lavish Lee is the best friend of Melissa Shay who has had an, shall we say, interesting alleged relationship with Drake over the past few months.\n\n\n\nLavish wants Drake to understand the power that Drizzy has over his fans. Unconditional love by definition is love without boundaries, and as such Drake has all the power in this relationship. Lavish urges him to use it for good and give back to his fans.", "annotation_id": "3272333", "url": "https://genius.com/3272333", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Whole squad on that real shit Whole squad on that real shit Whole squad on that real shit, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Real quick, whole squad on that real shit", "Whole squad on that real shit", "Whole squad on that real shit, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake’s only squads are OVO (and TOPSZN lowkey) and YMCMB.", "annotation_id": "3272181", "url": "https://genius.com/3272181", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "[Part II: The Catch Up]", "vault_lines": ["0 to 100 / The Catch Up Lyrics", "Could it be the way that I'll catch up"], "meaning": "“The Catch Up” transitions from a vicious banger to a slowed down, thoughtful cut, which includes Drizzy’s usual venting over a beautifully chill, melodic instrumental courtesy of Noah “40” Shebib.\n\nThere were many notable beat switches on Drake songs around the time of this single’s release, including “Pound Cake”, “2 On/Thotful”, and “Tuscan Leather”.", "annotation_id": "3272075", "url": "https://genius.com/3272075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Fuck all that “Drake, you gotta chill” shit I be on my Lil Mouse drill shit", "vault_lines": ["Fuck bein' on some chill shit", "Fuck all that \"Drake, you gotta chill\" shit", "I be on my Lil Mouse drill shit"], "meaning": "Drake had caught criticism for acting hard in his raps leading up to this song, for example when he threatened to catch a body on “Headlines.” Rather than listen to his critics, Drake plans to start acting even more ignorant in his raps, modeling his rhymes after the crime-ridden raps of Chicago drill artist Lil Mouse.\n\n\n\n(Lil Mouse got his song remixed by Lil Wayne.)", "annotation_id": "3272064", "url": "https://genius.com/3272064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "I’m just here for the bucks and the billis, nigga Don’t make me kill one of the GOATs for it (Arf!)", "vault_lines": ["I'm just here for the bucks and the billis, nigga", "Don't make me kill one of the GOATs for it (Arf!)"], "meaning": "These lines continue the shots between Drizzy and Jay Z. In Hova’s verse on 50 Cent’s “I Get Money (Forbes 1, 2, 3 Remix)”, he says:\n\nI’m already the GOAT, next stop is a billi\n\nDrake feels like he has what it takes to surpass Hova’s half a billion so he can be the one who’s truly swimming in the money.\n\nNote the play on “buck,” “billi’s” and “goat\"—a billy goat is a male goat, and a buck is a male animal, normally referring to a goat.", "annotation_id": "3272003", "url": "https://genius.com/3272003", "state": "accepted", "votes": 125, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Oh, Lord, got a whole lot to show for it I mean, we could really get it, we could go for it", "vault_lines": ["Oh, Lord, got a whole lot to show for it", "I mean, we could really get it, we could go for it"], "meaning": "All those late nights in the studio have paid off tremendously for Drake. Initially, he received buzz from his So Far Gone mixtape; since then, he has blossomed into one of the leading figures in rap today.\n\nAfter the success of his platinum debut album, he followed it with his Grammy winning sophmore, Take Care. His singles are just as successful. He broke the record for most #1 Rap singles on the Billboard Charts, as well as creating a way of life with the term “YOLO”. So as you can see Drake does have a whole lot to show for his success.", "annotation_id": "3271985", "url": "https://genius.com/3271985", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "I’ll do it for you niggas and do it for myself", "vault_lines": ["I'll do it for you niggas and do it for myself"], "meaning": "Not only is Drake killing the rap game himself, he has also been the “go-to guy” for up-and-coming artists trying to make a name for themselves. Among the artists that Drake has helped catapult towards the mainstream through featured verses or cosigns: The Weeknd, Future, A$AP Rocky, Migos, and ILoveMakonnen.\n\n\n\nNow with his own record label, Drake has begun to breed his own roster of talented artists, most notably Majid Jordan, featured on the smash hit single “Hold On We’re Going Home”, and OB O'Brien.", "annotation_id": "3271968", "url": "https://genius.com/3271968", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "If I ain’t the greatest, then I’m headed for it Yeah, that mean I’m way up (Way up)", "vault_lines": ["She ain't ready for it;", "if I ain't the greatest, then I'm headed for it", "Yeah, that mean I'm way up (Way up)"], "meaning": "Reminiscent to Jay Z’s line on “Hola Hovita,” Drake won’t hesitate to take the top spot from anyone as he feels he’s on his way to being a legend.\n\nDrake has definitely shown why he deserves the title of the G.O.A.T. With Grammy Awards and chart-topping hits to his name, Drake has both the critical and commercial success needed to be considered one of the best ever.", "annotation_id": "3271961", "url": "https://genius.com/3271961", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "So now I’m all in Niko basement puttin’ work in on the phones Either that or drive to Money Mart to make the pickups", "vault_lines": ["So now I'm all in Niko basement puttin' work in on the phones", "Either that or drive to Money Mart to make the pickups"], "meaning": "One of the ways Drake made money in between Degrassi and his rap career was shady phone scams with his friends Niko and Haffa. He briefly mentions these scams again on the track “Star67.”\n\nMoney Marts are Canadian financial centers that allow discrete money transfer services that might not fly at a bank, making it an ideal location to pick up the money they were making from these phone scams.", "annotation_id": "3271943", "url": "https://genius.com/3271943", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Yeah, I mean you already wrote for us Damn, nigga, what’s one more quote for us?", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I mean you already wrote for us", "Damn, nigga, what's one more quote for us?"], "meaning": "In the past, it’s been rumored that Drake has ghostwritten for other rappers—particularly Lil Wayne:\n\nYou know, we’re all great artists, great minds, and we all just contribute to each other. There’s been times when Wayne has helped me out, and I’m sure I’ve influenced him to do or say things on tracks. We help each other out; that’s part of being two artists who respect each other’s creativity.\n\nWhile that interview response seems like a deflection, this line may confirm those ghostwriting rumors!\n\n\n\nFor Us is also a homophone for Forrest from the previous line", "annotation_id": "3271922", "url": "https://genius.com/3271922", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "OVO, man, we really with the shits, boy Yeah, really with the shits,", "vault_lines": ["360 with the wrist, boy", "OVO, man, we really with the shits, boy", "Yeah, really with the shits,"], "meaning": "“With the shits” is slang in the Chicago drill scene. It essentially means “for the same cause,” therefore Drake’s OVO crew is very tight-knit.\n\n\n\nThe use of drill slang sets up the reference to drill rapper Lil Mouse later in the verse.", "annotation_id": "3271910", "url": "https://genius.com/3271910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "They tell him that he talkin’ crazy but he doesn’t care Bein’ humble don’t work as well as bein’ aware", "vault_lines": ["They tell him that he talkin' crazy but he doesn't care", "Bein' humble don't work as well as bein' aware"], "meaning": "Drake is known to stunt in his rhymes. This draws a lot of criticism from those that feel he needs to be more humble, but Drake feels honesty about how good he is is better than false modesty.", "annotation_id": "3271903", "url": "https://genius.com/3271903", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Been cookin’ with the sauce, Chef Curry with the pot, boy 360 with the wrist, boy Ayy, who the fuck them niggas is, boy?", "vault_lines": ["Been cookin' with the sauce, Chef Curry with the pot, boy", "360 with the wrist, boy", "Ayy, who the fuck them niggas is, boy?"], "meaning": "Drake previously mentioned Stephen Curry, now he mentions his wife, Ayesha Curry, who has a cooking channel on YouTube. Although Ayesha whips up sauces, desserts, and other culinary specialties, it’s likely that Drake is using her to paint a picture of himself whipping his wrist while cooking crack.\n\nAyesha would later release a remix to this lyric:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YjU7BYcJwIw\n\nNote that “360 with the wrist” continues the basketball reference, specifically referring to James Harden’s “cook and stir the pot” dance that he stole from Lil B.", "annotation_id": "3271879", "url": "https://genius.com/3271879", "state": "accepted", "votes": 107, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Oh, Lord, know yourself, know your worth, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Oh, Lord, know yourself, know your worth, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake urges listeners to have self-confidence and avoid selling themselves short in life. “Know yourself” is a motto for Drake’s crew: Baka says it at the end of “From Time,” Drake mentions it in “Jodeci Freestyle,” and even used it as a song title.\n\nThis line is one of sixteen Drake quotes to be printed on Sprite cans in June 2015.", "annotation_id": "3271875", "url": "https://genius.com/3271875", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "They say the shoe can always fit, no matter whose foot it’s on These days feel like I’m squeezin’ in ’em Whoever wore ‘em before just wasn’t thinkin’ big enough, I’m ’bout to leave ’em with ’em ‘Cause if I run the game in these, man, the seams are splittin’ No pun intended, but they smellin’ defeat in the air Headed where nobody took it, who meetin’ me there?", "vault_lines": ["if I ain't the greatest, then I'm headed for it", "They say the shoe can always fit, no matter whose foot it's on", "These days feel like I'm squeezin' in 'em", "Whoever wore 'em before just wasn't thinkin' big enough, I'm 'bout to leave 'em with 'em", "'Cause if I run the game in these, man, the seams are splittin'", "No pun intended, but they smellin' defeat in the air", "Headed where nobody took it, who meetin' me there?"], "meaning": "In this metaphor, the shoes represent the rap game and Drake’s feet represent his influence. The previous runners of the rap game (one of which was feuding with Drake around the same time…) didn’t do enough to expand the genre, which is why the metaphorical shoes feel so tight on Drake when he tries to take it to new levels.\n\nThis makes Drake consider taking the shoes off and leaving them in the past with their previous owners, since it’s difficult to run the game when your sneakers don’t fit. If that’s the case, and Drake decides to do his running barefoot, his competition would smell both da feet and defeat when they walked past him.", "annotation_id": "3271864", "url": "https://genius.com/3271864", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Yeah, been on the move like the lease is up And I can’t even name one person that’s keepin’ up Damn, fuck how it was in the past tense Ask yourself, how do we match up now? ‘Cause I’m only twenty-seven and I’m only gettin’ better If I haven’t passed you yet, watch me catch up now, for real", "vault_lines": ["if I ain't the greatest, then I'm headed for it", "I was ready... fuck that, I been ready", "I'll do it for you niggas and do it for myself", "Yeah, been on the move like the lease is up", "And I can't even name one person that's keepin' up", "Damn, fuck how it was in the past tense", "Ask yourself, how do we match up now?", "'Cause I'm only twenty-seven and I'm only gettin' better", "If I haven't passed you yet, watch me catch up now, for real"], "meaning": "When the lease to your apartment is up, you have to move to a new place. Similarly, Drake has outgrown his old life and is moving on to better things, leaving his stagnant fans and competition behind him.\n\nIn recognition of this progress, Drake demands that the old legends evaluate their current work against his recent projects – meaning that Jay can’t hide behind Reasonable Doubt and instead must compare MCHG to NWTS (an unfavorable comparison.)  Drake is 27, sitting on more than 27 mill, and catching up with the few rappers he hasn’t passed at a speed that explains both halves of the song’s title.", "annotation_id": "3271862", "url": "https://genius.com/3271862", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "But when they need a favor, who do they call?", "vault_lines": ["But when they need a favor, who do they call?"], "meaning": "Drake’s peers aren’t functioning at the same level as he is. They just stay in the same place. not bothering to improve, while Drake continues to progress. When they need to get something done, instead of trying to move forward themselves they simply take advantage of Drake’s popularity.\n\nSimilar sentiments are expressed on Drake’s smooth track “The Motion.”", "annotation_id": "3271860", "url": "https://genius.com/3271860", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Could it be the way that I’ll catch up Could it be the way that I’ll catch up Could it be the way that I’ll catch up Could it be the way that I’ll catch up Could it be the way that I’ll catch up Could it be the way that I’ll catch up Could it be the way that I’ll catch up Could it be the way that I’ll catch up Could it be the way that I’ll catch up", "vault_lines": ["Could it be the way that I'll catch up"], "meaning": "This is the first official collaboration between James Blake and Drake, although Blake did perform at OVO Fest and remix “Come Thru” in 2013.\n\n\n\nNote: This outro isn’t included on the iTunes or Spotify version of this track.", "annotation_id": "3271848", "url": "https://genius.com/3271848", "state": "accepted", "votes": 162, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Imagine how it feel to watch another nigga at the top You know that if you wasn’t you, you would be dissin’ you, dawg Damn, okay, from that perspective I see what you’re talkin’ ’bout, no way to soften that ’Cause me, I was tryna find out where I lost ’em at But maybe I ain’t lose ’em at all Maybe I keep movin’ forward and they’re just stagnant, they ain’t movin’ at all", "vault_lines": ["Imagine how it feel to watch another nigga at the top", "You know that if you wasn't you, you would be dissin' you, dawg", "Damn, okay, from that perspective", "I see what you're talkin' 'bout, no way to soften that", "'Cause me, I was tryna find out where I lost 'em at", "But maybe I ain't lose 'em at all", "Maybe I keep movin' forward and they're just stagnant, they ain't movin' at all"], "meaning": "Drake was name-dropped by Kendrick Lamar on “Control” prior to this song. The call-out was more based in competitive spirit than animosity, but Kendrick did claim that\n\nI’m tryna murder you niggas / Trying to make sure your core fans never heard of you niggas / They don’t wanna hear not one more noun or verb from you niggas\n\nDrake understands that Kendrick was riding high after the success of his good kid, m.A.A.d. city album, so he doesn’t blame him for saying something bold. But the line did make him feel as if he was losing fans until Lavish Lee told him otherwise. After his conversation with Lavish, he realized that his fans weren’t deserting him: they were just stuck in the past and couldn’t keep up with his progress. His sales confirm his thesis.", "annotation_id": "3271842", "url": "https://genius.com/3271842", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Since my dad used to tell me he was comin’ to the house to get me He ain’t show up Valuable lesson, man, I had to grow up That’s why I never ask for help", "vault_lines": ["Since my dad used to tell me he was comin' to the house to get me", "He ain't show up", "Valuable lesson, man, I had to grow up", "That's why I never ask for help"], "meaning": "As Drake vented on “Look What You’ve Done,” his parents were divorced growing up, and his father’s promises to visit him in Toronto often fell through. As a result, Drake was forced to grow up on his own without asking for help. That explains why he refuses to take handouts as an adult.", "annotation_id": "3271837", "url": "https://genius.com/3271837", "state": "accepted", "votes": 95, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Oh, Lord, who else soundin’ like this? They ain’t made me what I am, they just found me like this I was ready... fuck that, I been ready", "vault_lines": ["Oh, Lord, who else soundin' like this?", "They ain't made me what I am, they just found me like this", "I was ready... fuck that, I been ready"], "meaning": "Drake didn’t change his sound once he was discovered to become more famous; his distinct and creative sound is a major reason he was discovered in the first place. As Complex wrote in their retrospective review of So Far Gone,\n\n[Drake] established a consistent sound, a place where to enter Drake’s world, you have to breathe his and 40’s oxygen – a foggy, atmospheric sensibility that embraced minimalism and ambiguity.", "annotation_id": "3271830", "url": "https://genius.com/3271830", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Oh, Lord, I’m the rookie and the vet", "vault_lines": ["Oh, Lord, I'm the rookie and the vet"], "meaning": "As he showed in his verse on “No Lie,” where he rapped “young as an intern / but money like I built the shit,” Drake gets great satisfaction from the success he’s reached at a young age.\n\nDrake was only 28 when this song dropped. At the time, he was considered as a member of the “new school” of hip-hop. However he also plays the veteran, since he’d been in the game for 8 years when this song released and has broken records that took his older peers much longer to set.\n\nThis is also one of the 5 Drake lyrics Sprite printed on cans as part of the “Obey Your Verse” campaign.", "annotation_id": "3271821", "url": "https://genius.com/3271821", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Man, it’s 2008, I’m tryna paint the picture Comeback Season in the works and now I’m thinkin’ bigger", "vault_lines": ["Man, it's 2008, I'm tryna paint the picture", "Comeback Season in the works and now I'm thinkin' bigger"], "meaning": "In 2008, Drake was still unsure if he should stick with rapping or return to an acting career. But when his mixtape Comeback Season started building him a buzz, he decided to commit to a rap career. He eventually came up with an even bigger project—So Far Gone.", "annotation_id": "3271815", "url": "https://genius.com/3271815", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "They be on that rap-to-pay-the-bills shit And I don’t feel that shit, not even a little bit", "vault_lines": ["Fuck all that rap-to-pay-your-bills shit", "They be on that rap-to-pay-the-bills shit", "And I don't feel that shit, not even a little bit"], "meaning": "In hip-hop circles, there’s a stigma against relying on rap as a primary source of income. It’s why coke rappers Jay Z and Pusha T bragged that “without rap I was crazy straight” and “I ain’t spent 1 rap dollar in 3 years.”\n\nDrake skipped the coke and went for the Sprite, so he can’t brag about the spoils from his drug dealing days, but he does make money from a variety of non-rap outlets—sponsorships with Jordan and advertising with American Airlines just to name a few.", "annotation_id": "3271813", "url": "https://genius.com/3271813", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "How you so high, but still so down to Earth, nigga? Niggas wanna do it, we can do it on they turf, nigga", "vault_lines": ["How you so high, but still so down to Earth, nigga?", "Niggas wanna do it, we can do it on they turf, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake may be a worldwide sensation, but he still keeps his ear to the ground. He’s willing to battle lesser known rappers on their own turf if they come at him and understands the importance of staying grounded. In 2015, hosted a Houston charity event, bringing hip-hop’s best together to benefit urban youth academies. When asked about Drake’s involvement, Houston artist Onehunnidt said:\n\nI appreciate Drake for putting it together, bringing people together. There are some artists who’ve been making money in the H for years who haven’t done anything for the community event wise. They’ve been in it for themselves.", "annotation_id": "3271805", "url": "https://genius.com/3271805", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Feel like we paid the refs off, man, we fixed the game", "vault_lines": ["Feel like we paid the refs off, man, we fixed the game"], "meaning": "Seeing as Tim Donaghy, the referee who admitted to fixing games, claimed the refs fixed the Nets beating Drake’s Raptors in the 2014 playoffs, it would only be right that Drake “fixes the game” to ensure his OVO crew’s success.", "annotation_id": "3271804", "url": "https://genius.com/3271804", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "My actions been louder than my words, nigga", "vault_lines": ["My actions been louder than my words, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake exemplifies the idiom “actions speak louder than words\"—instead of yammering about his future success, he’s actually successful. According to RapStats, the idiom has been lyrically popular in the 21st century, peaking in 2002.\n\n\n\nThe phrase originates in 1 John 3:18: “Let us not love with word or speech but with actions and in truth.”", "annotation_id": "3271793", "url": "https://genius.com/3271793", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Listen up, boy, you’re better off eavesdroppin’ We already got spring 2015 poppin’ PND droppin’, Reps-up P droppin’ Majid Jordan droppin’, OB droppin’, not to mention me droppin’", "vault_lines": ["Listen up, boy, you're better off eavesdroppin'", "We already got spring 2015 poppin'", "PND droppin', Reps-up P droppin'", "Majid Jordan droppin', OB droppin', not to mention me droppin'"], "meaning": "PARTYNEXTDOOR dropped PARTYNEXTDOOR TWO in July 2014, his friend P. Reign of the Reps Up Gang dropped the Dear America EP in August 2014,  Majid Jordan of “Hold On We’re Going Home” fame dropped A Place Like This EP, and Drake associate OB O'Brien dropped the hot “Energy” beat in February 2015.\n\n\n\nAt the time, it seemed Drake was referencing his upcoming album Views From The 6, but it turned out that he released the surprise mixtape If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late instead. And his prediction came true: the project remained in the top 5 of the Billboard 200 well into the spring despite its February release date.", "annotation_id": "3271786", "url": "https://genius.com/3271786", "state": "accepted", "votes": 146, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Me and Noel been at it before Twitter names", "vault_lines": ["Me and Noel been at it before Twitter names"], "meaning": "Noel is Noel Cadastre, Drake’s assistant engineer. They’ve been putting in work since before Twitter was founded in 2006. Notice how he purposely uses the word “at:” the @ symbol is used in front of Twitter names.\n\n\n\nCheck out Drake’s twitter @Drake, and Noel Cadastre’s twitter @ovonoel!", "annotation_id": "3271781", "url": "https://genius.com/3271781", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Yeah, the 6 ain’t friendly, but that’s where I lay up (Lay up)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, the 6 ain't friendly, but that's where I lay up (Lay up)"], "meaning": "“The 6” is a nickname for Toronto, derived from the 416 and 647 area codes in the city. Drake credits fellow Toronto artist Jimmy Johnson as the creator of the term.\n\n\n\nDrake made frequent references to “the 6” around the time of this song’s release—from a trio of songs on If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late (“6 God”, “6 Man”, “You & The 6”) to his studio album Views From the 6.", "annotation_id": "3271778", "url": "https://genius.com/3271778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "All up on TV, I thought it’d make me richer Wasn’t payin’ me enough, I needed somethin’ quicker", "vault_lines": ["All up on TV, I thought it'd make me richer", "Wasn't payin' me enough, I needed somethin' quicker"], "meaning": "Before he was rapping, you may recall seeing Drizzy on Degrassi: The Next Generation (look for the one in the wheelchair.)\n\n\n\nWhile it’s en vogue to criticize Drake for complaining about the amount of money he made for a popular TV show, Drake has stated that Degrassi doesn’t pay as much as you’d think.  As he told Complex,\n\nA season of Canadian television is under a teacher’s salary, I’ll tell you that much. It’s definitely not something to go fucking get.\n\nContrast that with the fact that Drake now grosses over $500,000 in sales per show and was sitting on about $35M when this song dropped, and it seems he made the right decision.", "annotation_id": "3271773", "url": "https://genius.com/3271773", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Fuck bein’ on some chill shit We go zero to a hundred, nigga, real quick", "vault_lines": ["Fuck bein' on some chill shit", "We go zero to a hundred, nigga, real quick", "I go zero to a hundred, nigga, real quick", "Zero to a hundred, nigga, real quick", "Real quick, real fuckin' quick, nigga"], "meaning": "Instead of relaxing after releasing projects,  Drake stays on his grind and can snap on a new track at a moment’s notice. When this track dropped in June 2014, the world still had “Started From the Bottom” fever, but Drake decided to put out another hit rather than rest on his laurels.\n\n“0 to 100” represents working your way up to the top by playing off the 0 to 100 km/hr test used to judge the pickup speed of cars.\n\n\n\n“0 to 100” is also a reference to the temper of an individual. At one point they are calm, cool, and collected but their temper is flared and reaches to the highest point of madness.", "annotation_id": "3271767", "url": "https://genius.com/3271767", "state": "accepted", "votes": 265, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "I should prolly sign to Hit-Boy ’cause I got all the hits, boy (Ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["I should prolly sign to Hit-Boy 'cause I got all the hits, boy (Ha-ha)"], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/Hit_Boy/status/472948747974873088\n\nEven though Drake is running his own label, (OVO), he’d be a good fit in Hit-Boy’s label (HS87), since they’re both known for making hits.\n\nCheck out “Trophies” – their first collaboration.", "annotation_id": "3271765", "url": "https://genius.com/3271765", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "Shoutout to the bitches out here holdin’ down the set All up in my phone, lookin’ at pictures from the other night She gon’ be upset if she keep scrollin’ to the left, dawg She gon’ see some shit that she don’t wanna see She ain’t ready for it;", "vault_lines": ["Shoutout to the bitches out here holdin' down the set", "All up in my phone, lookin' at pictures from the other night", "She gon' be upset if she keep scrollin' to the left, dawg", "She gon' see some shit that she don't wanna see", "She ain't ready for it;"], "meaning": "As the old saying goes: “if you look for something hard enough, you’ll find it.” Rather than enjoying Drizzy’s company, this woman scans through his phone, looking for pictures that may alert her to the fact that she isn’t the only one for him.\n\nSend me your pictures, baby, you know I’d never leak it\n\nSending nude photographs has become commonplace in the 21st Century. According to a study conducted by Pew in 2013, 6% of adults admit to having sent a photograph with 15% having admitted to receiving one. In an Elle interview back in 2011, Drake responded that “of course” he receives nude photographs.\n\n\n\nNote , as well, the craftsmanship: Drake skips the rhyme in the third bar (“night”) and then doubles down in the fourth (“upset, ”“left”).", "annotation_id": "3271764", "url": "https://genius.com/3271764", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "I got 40 in the studio, every night, late night Gotta watch that shit, don’t want to make him sicker, that’s my nigga", "vault_lines": ["I got 40 in the studio, every night, late night", "Gotta watch that shit, don't want to make him sicker, that's my nigga"], "meaning": "Noah “40” Shebib is Drake’s longtime friend and producer. He worked hard to create Drake’s sound in the early days, and continued to work with Drake as he rose through the ranks.\n\nSince Drake has said “I really don’t know if I could keep making music without 40,” Drake has to be careful not to overwork him. Shebib was diagnosed with multiple sclerosis in 2005 at the age of 22, and opened up about the affliction in an interview with CNN:\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90OdMnZdxiA\n\n40 also got his nickname because he worked so tirelessly. Originally, the nickname was “40 Days & 40 Nights”, but was shortened to “40” over time.", "annotation_id": "3271763", "url": "https://genius.com/3271763", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "I run this shit, they like, ”Go Forrest Run Forrest, run Forrest, go Forrest”", "vault_lines": ["I run this shit, they like, \"Go Forrest", "Run Forrest, run Forrest, go Forrest\""], "meaning": "Drake is currently running the rap game. As such, he compares himself to the titular character of Forrest Gump who runs across America on foot while hundreds of people run with him.\n\nShortly after dropping this track, Drake posted this gem on his Instagram:\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/oufzXTjQCQ/", "annotation_id": "3271761", "url": "https://genius.com/3271761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 111, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "line": "This shit a mothafuckin’ lay-up I been Steph Curry with the shot", "vault_lines": ["This shit a mothafuckin' lay-up", "I been Steph Curry with the shot"], "meaning": "Stephen Curry is a professional basketball player for the Golden State Warriors. He’s known for his exceptional shooting ability – in fact, President Barack Obama and many others have crowned him the best shooter they’ve ever seen. Drake considers himself to be a sharpshooter because he hasn’t missed on tracks.", "annotation_id": "3271757", "url": "https://genius.com/3271757", "state": "accepted", "votes": 273, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/e4e1f1e42a17873dce151a2cac6b6562.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"0 to 100 / The Catch Up\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>0 to 100 / The Catch UpDrake3.9M", "status": "done", "targets": 40, "clicked": 40, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-0-to-100-the-catch-up-lyrics", "genius_title": "0 to 100 / The Catch Up"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "You love her, then you gotta give the world to her Is that a world tour or your girl’s tour? I know that you gotta be a thug for her This ain’t what she meant when she told you to open up more", "vault_lines": ["You love her, then you gotta give the world to her", "Is that a world tour or your girl's tour?", "I know that you gotta be a thug for her", "This ain't what she meant when she told you to open up more"], "meaning": "In her song “Buy A Heart” from The Pinkprint, Nicki expressed both her desire for Meek and her fear of jumping into a relationship with him. She wasn’t sure if he was emotionally ready, and wanted him to “open up” to her.\n\nDrake pokes fun at this, once again implying Nicki wears the pants both in their relationship and their business deals – he is merely the “opening act” on her Pinkprint Tour. Even then, he’s only one of many—Rae Sremmurd, Tinashe and DeJ Loaf appear on the tour as well. He’s opening up more alright, because he’s only the opening act.\n\n\n\nMeek and Nicki confirmed their break up in January 2017. While Nicki was mature and respectful, Meek took a parting shot at her fashion sense.", "annotation_id": "7466334", "url": "https://genius.com/7466334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 776, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "I got the fest in five days and it’s my shit Soon as a nigga hit the stage, they gon’ [Chorus] They gon’ ask if I can play this shit back to back Yeah, they want it back to back They gon’ ask if I can play this shit back to back", "vault_lines": ["Back To Back Lyrics", "I got the drink in me, going back to back", "Yeah, going back to back", "Yeah, I'm going back to back", "I got the fest in five days and it's my shit", "Soon as a nigga hit the stage, they gon'", "They gon' ask if I can play this shit back to back", "Yeah, they want it back to back"], "meaning": "A reference to Drake’s 6th annual OVO Fest, which is held in Toronto every year, and took place August 1st through August 3rd.\n\nUnlike Meek Mill, who got booed at a Toronto concert, Drake got a warm welcome from his fans at OVO Fest. The first song that Drake performed was indeed “Back To Back,” complete with memes making fun of Meek playing in the background.\n\nhttp://twitter.com/thefader/status/628371934363783168/photo/1", "annotation_id": "7466327", "url": "https://genius.com/7466327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 220, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "Please, somebody stop me I’m talkin’ boasy and gwanin wassy", "vault_lines": ["Please, somebody stop me", "I'm talkin' boasy and gwanin wassy"], "meaning": "“Boasy” and “gwanin wassy” are slang terms originally from Jamaica, but picked up by Toronto. There’s a vast population of West Indians in Toronto, see a list for specialized Toronto/OVO slang here.\n\nThe term “boasy” originated from the Reggae scene and means confident or showing off. It stems from “boasty.”\n\n“Gwanin” and “wassy” mean “going on,” and “crazy” respectively, Drake’s “going crazy.”", "annotation_id": "7466272", "url": "https://genius.com/7466272", "state": "accepted", "votes": 247, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "Woah—very important and very pretentious When I look back, I might be mad that I gave this attention Yeah, but it’s weighin’ heavy on my conscience", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, but it's weighin' heavy on my conscience", "Woah—very important and very pretentious", "When I look back, I might be mad that I gave this attention"], "meaning": "Drake calls himself out for the Jordan reference above, then goes against his usual stance of not replying to disses. He’s been involved in back and fourths over the years with the likes of Pusha T and Common, but never directly responded. On “Successful,” from his debut mixtape So Far Gone, he rapped:\n\nDiss me, you’ll never hear a reply for it\n\nIn time, a wiser, older Drake may look back at his beef with Meek and laugh at himself for even participating in it.", "annotation_id": "7466231", "url": "https://genius.com/7466231", "state": "accepted", "votes": 345, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "I been puttin’ on a show, it was a sell-out event Oh, you need better seatin’", "vault_lines": ["I been puttin' on a show, it was a sell-out event", "Oh, you need better seatin'"], "meaning": "Drake compares his career to a performance that sells more tickets than Meek can even dream of. While Drake takes centre stage, Meek can’t even afford seats close enough to be in his immediate vicinity. Drake is on another level.\n\nDrake’s become tougher since transitioning away from Cash Money. In “Energy,” he said his acting days were over, but Drake’s still putting on a show.", "annotation_id": "7466217", "url": "https://genius.com/7466217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 141, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "I don’t wanna hear about this ever again Not even when she tell him that they better as friends Not even when you saying, “Drizzy, tell ’em again!”", "vault_lines": ["I don't wanna hear about this ever again", "Not even when she tell him that they better as friends", "Not even when you saying, \"Drizzy, tell 'em again!\""], "meaning": "After hitting Meek with a 1-2 punch, Drake feels like he’s landed a knock out blow and doesn’t want to hear about Meek or this ghostwriting business again. Drake even feels that when Nicki inevitably breaks up with Meek, he won’t speak on it even if Meek tries to go at him again for attention. Nicki broke up with Meek in January 2017:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/nickiminaj/status/817011839401922566\n\nDrake has not yet commented. Shortly before Nicki’s post, Drake announced he would stop performing the song and claimed a New Years Eve party in Las Vegas was the last time he’d do so.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f1wfqxx9CC4", "annotation_id": "7466168", "url": "https://genius.com/7466168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 340, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "Please, check ’em for a wire or a earpiece Please, please do not let these niggas near me", "vault_lines": ["Please, check 'em for a wire or a earpiece", "Please, please do not let these niggas near me"], "meaning": "A wire and earpiece are parts of electronic listening devices that criminal investigators use to try to record incriminating audio against their suspects.\n\nDrake is wondering if Meek could possibly be a snitch, wearing a wire around Drake to get evidence of a ghostwriter and use that to gain more fame. Just like Drake said on his previous diss, “Charged Up”:\n\nNiggas is snitching on us without no interrogation\n\n\n\nThis might also allude to a line in the song “R.I.C.O” where Meek notices informants are tapping into his studio session hoping to hear him self-incriminate.\n\nI can hear echos from feds on this beat from informants\nI think they recording\nShhh, listen, you hear that?", "annotation_id": "7466162", "url": "https://genius.com/7466162", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "This for y’all that think that I don’t write enough They just mad ’cause I got the Midas touch", "vault_lines": ["This for y'all that think that I don't write enough", "They just mad 'cause I got the Midas touch"], "meaning": "According to the myth, Midas was the king of Phrygia, a Greek polis in Asia Minor. After he did a favor to one of the Gods, he was promised to have one wish granted, and he asked for a golden touch: everything he touches would turn to gold. While at first he was happy with his new gift, he came to suffer for it. Eventually he turned his own daughter into a golden statue.\n\n\n\nThis may be a reference to this line from “Charged Up” – “I see you niggas having trouble going gold.” While Meek can barely go gold, Drake produces certified “gold material” (500,000+ units sold) without even trying.", "annotation_id": "7466123", "url": "https://genius.com/7466123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 327, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "I didn’t wanna do it, gave me every reason The point I’m tryin’ to make is I don’t ever need ’em Seen what you’d do for fame, what would you do for freedom?", "vault_lines": ["I didn't wanna do it, gave me every reason", "The point I'm tryin' to make is I don't ever need 'em", "Seen what you'd do for fame, what would you do for freedom?"], "meaning": "Meek has been in jail twice, so he knows what it means to be desperate for freedom. Drake compares his desperation for freedom and fame. If Meek snitched on Drake to promote his own music, how far will he go to stay out of jail?\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/625960152018087937\n\nDrake doesn’t respect people who diss him to become famous, and normally just ignores them. Occasionally he has to put people in their place, as he explained on “You & The 6”:\n\nI really hate wasting your time to check a clone or two, momma\nIt’s just they cloning me, momma\nThem niggas wannabes, momma\nIts like – I’m the one they wanna be, momma", "annotation_id": "7466118", "url": "https://genius.com/7466118", "state": "accepted", "votes": 196, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "Oh man Oh man, oh man Not again", "vault_lines": ["Oh man, oh man"], "meaning": "This track dropped very early on the 29th of July 2015, making it the second song Drake dropped aimed at Meek Mill in just four days after this tweet, among others, sparked a feud.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623700698509758464\n\nThe first song, “Charged Up,” premiered on OVOSOUND Radio on the 25th of July, along with the singles “Right Hand” and “Hotline Bling.”", "annotation_id": "7466117", "url": "https://genius.com/7466117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 372, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "Please, think before you come for the great one Please, who’s a real nigga and who ain’t one?", "vault_lines": ["Please, think before you come for the great one", "Please, who's a real nigga and who ain't one?"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t think Meek is real. While Drake now has two songs firing shots, Meek has none, and continues to use social media to diss Drake.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/DJA_Tron/status/626317089016410112\n\n“The great one” refers to Wayne Gretzky, arguably the best hockey player of all time. Since Drake is Canadian, you can imagine that he grew up watching the legend play.", "annotation_id": "7466110", "url": "https://genius.com/7466110", "state": "accepted", "votes": 182, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "Yeah, and fuck, you left The Boy no options I wanna see my niggas go insane", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and fuck, you left The Boy no options", "I wanna see my niggas go insane"], "meaning": "“The Boy” is a nickname Drake gave himself, along with his Instagram handle name, Champagne Papi.\n\nAfter Meek Mill called him out on Twitter for using a ghostwriter and Funkmaster Flex dropped the “10 Bands” reference track, Drake felt like he had no option but to release a diss record. “Charged Up” was released on July 25th, while this record dropped 4 days later on the 29th.", "annotation_id": "7466084", "url": "https://genius.com/7466084", "state": "accepted", "votes": 213, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "And shout to all my boss bitches wifin’ niggas Make sure you hit him with the prenup Then tell that man to ease up", "vault_lines": ["And shout to all my boss bitches wifin' niggas", "Make sure you hit him with the prenup", "Then tell that man to ease up"], "meaning": "Drake (unofficially) shouts out label-mate Nicki Minaj and recommends she protect her assets as the primary breadwinner in the relationship. Considering Meek’s worth $3 million and Nicki’s worth $60 million, he has a point.\n\nDrizzy offers the same advice to Nicki as Kanye did to the world in “Gold Digger”:\n\nIf you ain’t no punk, holla, “We want prenup!”\n“We want prenup!” (Yeah!)\n\nIt should also be noted, Drizzy is imitating Nicki’s trademark voice and lyrical style with “wifin' niggas.” Drake satirically raps from her perspective, suggesting Meek is a joke, and shining light on how much lower on the rap food chain Meek is than both Drake and Nicki.\n\nMeek and Nicki broke up in early January 2017.", "annotation_id": "7466081", "url": "https://genius.com/7466081", "state": "accepted", "votes": 670, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "I mean woah, can’t fool the city, man, they know what’s up Second floor at Tootsies, gettin’ shoulder rubs", "vault_lines": ["I mean woah, can't fool the city, man, they know what's up", "Second floor at Tootsies, gettin' shoulder rubs"], "meaning": "Shortly after Drake dropped “Charged Up,” Toronto’s City Councillor Norm Kelly jumped into the situation and tweeted Meek saying he “wasn’t welcome in Toronto anymore.”\n\nTootsies could be a reference to the famous strip club in Miami, where according to SPIN Magazine, rappers and athletes are staples on the second floor, with a shoulder rub costing around $20.\n\nSimilarly, it could be a reference to Ms. Tootsies, the famous soul food spot in Meek’s home town, Philadelphia. The second floor is for guests of high status. Drake mocks Meek with the “shoulder rub” line because Drake feels like he’s treated better than Meek, even in his hometown.", "annotation_id": "7466078", "url": "https://genius.com/7466078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 224, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "I took a break from Views, now it’s back to that, nigga (Six)", "vault_lines": ["Back To Back Lyrics", "I took a break from Views, now it's back to that, nigga (Six)"], "meaning": "Drake took time out of the studio to “do some charity” and deal with Meek, but with this track he thinks there will be no reason for him to go back to the beef, and he can get back to his album Views, which was released in April of the following year.", "annotation_id": "7466066", "url": "https://genius.com/7466066", "state": "accepted", "votes": 502, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "I waited four days, nigga, where y’all at? I drove here in the Wraith, playin’ AR-AB", "vault_lines": ["I waited four days, nigga, where y'all at?", "I drove here in the Wraith, playin' AR-AB"], "meaning": "Drake’s first record aimed at Meek, “Charged Up,” was released on the 25th of July, while this song was released on four days later on the 29th. Meek didn’t issue a response on wax until July 30th.\n\nDrake does indeed own a Rolls Royce Wraith. While King Ab, also known as AR-AB, is a popular underground rapper from Meek’s hometown Philadelphia who has also been beefing with Meek in 2015.  It’s rumoured AR-AB just signed with OVO, as per King Ab’s tweet below.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/AssaultRifleAB/status/626349607166038016\n\nAR-AB also joined in on the beef by dissing Meek Mill in his own “Back to Back” freestyle.", "annotation_id": "7466049", "url": "https://genius.com/7466049", "state": "accepted", "votes": 225, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "You gon’ make me step out of my fuckin’ frame You gon’ make me buy bottles for Charlamagne You gon’ make me go out of my fuckin’ way", "vault_lines": ["You gon' make me step out of my fuckin' frame", "You gon' make me buy bottles for Charlamagne", "You gon' make me go out of my fuckin' way"], "meaning": "The enemy of your enemy is your friend, so Drake decided to align with Power 105.1’s Charlamagne Tha God – a known Drake “hater” and a sworn enemy of rival radio station Hot 97’s Funkmaster Flex. Flex supported Meek Mill during the beef, dropping the reference tracks of alleged ghostwriter Quentin Miller, as well as premiering Meek Mill’s diss “Wanna Know.”\n\nIn response to the spreading beef, Charlemagne named Flex Donkey of the Day, delivering a eulogy to Flex’s credibility that trended worldwide. Shortly after, Charlemagne also gave Meek Mill donkey of the day honors.\n\nOh, and the 6 God actually bought Charlamagne 6 bottles.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/5uWHk5TNbS/", "annotation_id": "7466035", "url": "https://genius.com/7466035", "state": "accepted", "votes": 510, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "Yeah, trigger fingers turn to Twitter fingers Yeah, you gettin’ bodied by a singin’ nigga I’m not the type of nigga that’ll type to niggas", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, trigger fingers turn to Twitter fingers", "Yeah, you gettin' bodied by a singin' nigga", "I'm not the type of nigga that'll type to niggas", "Yeah, I'm going back to back"], "meaning": "This song is in response to a series of tweets Meek Mill sent Drake’s way accusing him of using a ghost writer for his verse on his song “R.I.C.O.” While Drake has since fired back on wax with “Charged Up,” Meek continues to Tweet about the situation. Not the response you would expect from a man who’s “trigger finger itchin'.”\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/625101719836434433\n\nIn similar fashion to “Charged Up,” Drake channels Eminem’s 8 Mile vibe and flips a negative about him into a positive. What’s worse than getting dissed by a rapper? Getting dissed by a singer. Moreover, he uses the popular internet meme – “Drake the type of nigga that_____.” to flip a joke about himself on its head and use it against Meek.", "annotation_id": "7466020", "url": "https://genius.com/7466020", "state": "accepted", "votes": 916, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "I’m not sure what it was that really made y’all mad But I guess this is what I gotta do to make y’all rap", "vault_lines": ["I'm not sure what it was that really made y'all mad", "But I guess this is what I gotta do to make y'all rap"], "meaning": "And now Drake digs into the beef’s genesis. Meek Mill, frustrated that Drake didn’t promote Dreams Worth More Than Money, said Drake didn’t write his verse on “R.I.C.O,” and lashed out in a twitter rant. Here, Drake brushes off the ghostwriting accusations once more, and claims he doesn’t really see the reason Meek is going at him.\n\nThis is the second track Drake has dropped addressing the beef (hence “Back to Back”), but Meek still doesn’t have anything other than twitter to back his statements. He was supposed to release a response track on July 27th, but he once again took to social media instead, posting  a 15 second audio of – screams.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/626304322981105664", "annotation_id": "7466019", "url": "https://genius.com/7466019", "state": "accepted", "votes": 188, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "Yeah I learned the game from William Wesley, you can never check me", "vault_lines": ["I learned the game from William Wesley, you can never check me"], "meaning": "William “Worldwide Wes” Wesley is considered one of the most influential businessmen in sports, especially basketball.  “Check me” is a basketball term that means to “challenge me.” By saying “you can never check me,” Drake continues the basketball analogy by belittling Meek.\n\nThis is the second time Drake has personally mentioned Wes in a song. In his verse on Mary J. Blige’s “The One,” he says:\n\nWes put me on the marquee flight life\nPlus I go hard for the woman that I might wife\n\nAnd of course, Jay Z shouted out Wes on Drake’s “Pound Cake/Paris Morton Music 2”:\n\nShout out to World Wide Wes\nEverywhere we go, we leave a worldwide mess", "annotation_id": "7466014", "url": "https://genius.com/7466014", "state": "accepted", "votes": 308, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "Back to back for the niggas that didn’t get the message Back to back, like I’m on the cover of Lethal Weapon Back to back, like I’m Jordan ’96, ’97", "vault_lines": ["Back To Back Lyrics", "Yeah, I'm going back to back", "Back to back for the niggas that didn't get the message", "Back to back, like I'm on the cover of Lethal Weapon", "Back to back, like I'm Jordan '96, '97"], "meaning": "Meek stayed silent in the four days after Drake released “Charged Up,” so this freestyle is the second of back-to-back volleys from Drake’s side of the beef.\n\nMel Gibson and Danny Glover stand back to back in the poster for Lethal Weapon 3, and Michael Jordan delivered consecutive titles in 1996 and 1997.\n\n\n\nInterestingly, Jordan won a third consecutive title in 1998, and along with the championship ring Drake posted on instagram, it was enough to start rumors of a track called “Three Peat.”", "annotation_id": "7466012", "url": "https://genius.com/7466012", "state": "accepted", "votes": 386, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "line": "I did another one, I did another one You still ain’t did shit about the other one", "vault_lines": ["I did another one, I did another one", "You still ain't did shit about the other one"], "meaning": "This song marks Drake’s second track aimed at Meek Mill. When this song was released, Meek had only responded on Twitter. It wasn’t until the day after this song was released did he drop “Wanna Know.”\n\nThe song cover on Soundcloud shows Joe Carter playing for Drake’s hometown team, the Toronto Blue Jays, after he hit the home run that won the 1993 World Series against Meek’s hometown team, the Philadelphia Phillies. The Blue Jays had won the year before in 1992, thus winning “back to back” titles.\n\nDrizzy really thought this one out: the same day this song was released, the Blue Jays and Phillies were scheduled to play. Poetically, the Blue Jays defeated the Phillies 8-2.\n\nCarter has since responded, shouting out Drizzy on Twitter to personally thank him for using his picture as cover art.\n\n\n\nDrake, the king of memes, channels DJ Khaled’s classic “Another One” meme.", "annotation_id": "7466009", "url": "https://genius.com/7466009", "state": "accepted", "votes": 379, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e3dba170124f70a8ed995e7cf3d42eb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Back To Back\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Back To BackDrake5.4M", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 22, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-back-to-back-lyrics", "genius_title": "Back To Back"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "You love her, then you gotta give the world to her Is that a world tour or your girl’s tour? I know that you gotta be a thug for her This ain’t what she meant when she told you to open up more", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In her song “Buy A Heart” from The Pinkprint, Nicki expressed both her desire for Meek and her fear of jumping into a relationship with him. She wasn’t sure if he was emotionally ready, and wanted him to “open up” to her.\n\nDrake pokes fun at this, once again implying Nicki wears the pants both in their relationship and their business deals – he is merely the “opening act” on her Pinkprint Tour. Even then, he’s only one of many—Rae Sremmurd, Tinashe and DeJ Loaf appear on the tour as well. He’s opening up more alright, because he’s only the opening act.\n\n\n\nMeek and Nicki confirmed their break up in January 2017. While Nicki was mature and respectful, Meek took a parting shot at her fashion sense.", "annotation_id": "7466334", "url": "https://genius.com/7466334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 776, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "I got the fest in five days and it’s my shit Soon as a nigga hit the stage, they gon’ [Chorus] They gon’ ask if I can play this shit back to back Yeah, they want it back to back They gon’ ask if I can play this shit back to back", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A reference to Drake’s 6th annual OVO Fest, which is held in Toronto every year, and took place August 1st through August 3rd.\n\nUnlike Meek Mill, who got booed at a Toronto concert, Drake got a warm welcome from his fans at OVO Fest. The first song that Drake performed was indeed “Back To Back,” complete with memes making fun of Meek playing in the background.\n\nhttp://twitter.com/thefader/status/628371934363783168/photo/1", "annotation_id": "7466327", "url": "https://genius.com/7466327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 220, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "Please, somebody stop me I’m talkin’ boasy and gwanin wassy", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Boasy” and “gwanin wassy” are slang terms originally from Jamaica, but picked up by Toronto. There’s a vast population of West Indians in Toronto, see a list for specialized Toronto/OVO slang here.\n\nThe term “boasy” originated from the Reggae scene and means confident or showing off. It stems from “boasty.”\n\n“Gwanin” and “wassy” mean “going on,” and “crazy” respectively, Drake’s “going crazy.”", "annotation_id": "7466272", "url": "https://genius.com/7466272", "state": "accepted", "votes": 247, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "Woah—very important and very pretentious When I look back, I might be mad that I gave this attention Yeah, but it’s weighin’ heavy on my conscience", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake calls himself out for the Jordan reference above, then goes against his usual stance of not replying to disses. He’s been involved in back and fourths over the years with the likes of Pusha T and Common, but never directly responded. On “Successful,” from his debut mixtape So Far Gone, he rapped:\n\nDiss me, you’ll never hear a reply for it\n\nIn time, a wiser, older Drake may look back at his beef with Meek and laugh at himself for even participating in it.", "annotation_id": "7466231", "url": "https://genius.com/7466231", "state": "accepted", "votes": 345, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "I been puttin’ on a show, it was a sell-out event Oh, you need better seatin’", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake compares his career to a performance that sells more tickets than Meek can even dream of. While Drake takes centre stage, Meek can’t even afford seats close enough to be in his immediate vicinity. Drake is on another level.\n\nDrake’s become tougher since transitioning away from Cash Money. In “Energy,” he said his acting days were over, but Drake’s still putting on a show.", "annotation_id": "7466217", "url": "https://genius.com/7466217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 141, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "I don’t wanna hear about this ever again Not even when she tell him that they better as friends Not even when you saying, “Drizzy, tell ’em again!”", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "After hitting Meek with a 1-2 punch, Drake feels like he’s landed a knock out blow and doesn’t want to hear about Meek or this ghostwriting business again. Drake even feels that when Nicki inevitably breaks up with Meek, he won’t speak on it even if Meek tries to go at him again for attention. Nicki broke up with Meek in January 2017:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/nickiminaj/status/817011839401922566\n\nDrake has not yet commented. Shortly before Nicki’s post, Drake announced he would stop performing the song and claimed a New Years Eve party in Las Vegas was the last time he’d do so.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f1wfqxx9CC4", "annotation_id": "7466168", "url": "https://genius.com/7466168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 340, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "Please, check ’em for a wire or a earpiece Please, please do not let these niggas near me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A wire and earpiece are parts of electronic listening devices that criminal investigators use to try to record incriminating audio against their suspects.\n\nDrake is wondering if Meek could possibly be a snitch, wearing a wire around Drake to get evidence of a ghostwriter and use that to gain more fame. Just like Drake said on his previous diss, “Charged Up”:\n\nNiggas is snitching on us without no interrogation\n\n\n\nThis might also allude to a line in the song “R.I.C.O” where Meek notices informants are tapping into his studio session hoping to hear him self-incriminate.\n\nI can hear echos from feds on this beat from informants\nI think they recording\nShhh, listen, you hear that?", "annotation_id": "7466162", "url": "https://genius.com/7466162", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "This for y’all that think that I don’t write enough They just mad ’cause I got the Midas touch", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "According to the myth, Midas was the king of Phrygia, a Greek polis in Asia Minor. After he did a favor to one of the Gods, he was promised to have one wish granted, and he asked for a golden touch: everything he touches would turn to gold. While at first he was happy with his new gift, he came to suffer for it. Eventually he turned his own daughter into a golden statue.\n\n\n\nThis may be a reference to this line from “Charged Up” – “I see you niggas having trouble going gold.” While Meek can barely go gold, Drake produces certified “gold material” (500,000+ units sold) without even trying.", "annotation_id": "7466123", "url": "https://genius.com/7466123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 327, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "I didn’t wanna do it, gave me every reason The point I’m tryin’ to make is I don’t ever need ’em Seen what you’d do for fame, what would you do for freedom?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Meek has been in jail twice, so he knows what it means to be desperate for freedom. Drake compares his desperation for freedom and fame. If Meek snitched on Drake to promote his own music, how far will he go to stay out of jail?\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/625960152018087937\n\nDrake doesn’t respect people who diss him to become famous, and normally just ignores them. Occasionally he has to put people in their place, as he explained on “You & The 6”:\n\nI really hate wasting your time to check a clone or two, momma\nIt’s just they cloning me, momma\nThem niggas wannabes, momma\nIts like – I’m the one they wanna be, momma", "annotation_id": "7466118", "url": "https://genius.com/7466118", "state": "accepted", "votes": 196, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "Oh man Oh man, oh man Not again", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This track dropped very early on the 29th of July 2015, making it the second song Drake dropped aimed at Meek Mill in just four days after this tweet, among others, sparked a feud.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623700698509758464\n\nThe first song, “Charged Up,” premiered on OVOSOUND Radio on the 25th of July, along with the singles “Right Hand” and “Hotline Bling.”", "annotation_id": "7466117", "url": "https://genius.com/7466117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 372, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "Please, think before you come for the great one Please, who’s a real nigga and who ain’t one?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t think Meek is real. While Drake now has two songs firing shots, Meek has none, and continues to use social media to diss Drake.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/DJA_Tron/status/626317089016410112\n\n“The great one” refers to Wayne Gretzky, arguably the best hockey player of all time. Since Drake is Canadian, you can imagine that he grew up watching the legend play.", "annotation_id": "7466110", "url": "https://genius.com/7466110", "state": "accepted", "votes": 182, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "Yeah, and fuck, you left The Boy no options I wanna see my niggas go insane", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“The Boy” is a nickname Drake gave himself, along with his Instagram handle name, Champagne Papi.\n\nAfter Meek Mill called him out on Twitter for using a ghostwriter and Funkmaster Flex dropped the “10 Bands” reference track, Drake felt like he had no option but to release a diss record. “Charged Up” was released on July 25th, while this record dropped 4 days later on the 29th.", "annotation_id": "7466084", "url": "https://genius.com/7466084", "state": "accepted", "votes": 213, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "And shout to all my boss bitches wifin’ niggas Make sure you hit him with the prenup Then tell that man to ease up", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake (unofficially) shouts out label-mate Nicki Minaj and recommends she protect her assets as the primary breadwinner in the relationship. Considering Meek’s worth $3 million and Nicki’s worth $60 million, he has a point.\n\nDrizzy offers the same advice to Nicki as Kanye did to the world in “Gold Digger”:\n\nIf you ain’t no punk, holla, “We want prenup!”\n“We want prenup!” (Yeah!)\n\nIt should also be noted, Drizzy is imitating Nicki’s trademark voice and lyrical style with “wifin' niggas.” Drake satirically raps from her perspective, suggesting Meek is a joke, and shining light on how much lower on the rap food chain Meek is than both Drake and Nicki.\n\nMeek and Nicki broke up in early January 2017.", "annotation_id": "7466081", "url": "https://genius.com/7466081", "state": "accepted", "votes": 670, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "I mean woah, can’t fool the city, man, they know what’s up Second floor at Tootsies, gettin’ shoulder rubs", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Shortly after Drake dropped “Charged Up,” Toronto’s City Councillor Norm Kelly jumped into the situation and tweeted Meek saying he “wasn’t welcome in Toronto anymore.”\n\nTootsies could be a reference to the famous strip club in Miami, where according to SPIN Magazine, rappers and athletes are staples on the second floor, with a shoulder rub costing around $20.\n\nSimilarly, it could be a reference to Ms. Tootsies, the famous soul food spot in Meek’s home town, Philadelphia. The second floor is for guests of high status. Drake mocks Meek with the “shoulder rub” line because Drake feels like he’s treated better than Meek, even in his hometown.", "annotation_id": "7466078", "url": "https://genius.com/7466078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 224, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "I took a break from Views, now it’s back to that, nigga (Six)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake took time out of the studio to “do some charity” and deal with Meek, but with this track he thinks there will be no reason for him to go back to the beef, and he can get back to his album Views, which was released in April of the following year.", "annotation_id": "7466066", "url": "https://genius.com/7466066", "state": "accepted", "votes": 502, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "I waited four days, nigga, where y’all at? I drove here in the Wraith, playin’ AR-AB", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s first record aimed at Meek, “Charged Up,” was released on the 25th of July, while this song was released on four days later on the 29th. Meek didn’t issue a response on wax until July 30th.\n\nDrake does indeed own a Rolls Royce Wraith. While King Ab, also known as AR-AB, is a popular underground rapper from Meek’s hometown Philadelphia who has also been beefing with Meek in 2015.  It’s rumoured AR-AB just signed with OVO, as per King Ab’s tweet below.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/AssaultRifleAB/status/626349607166038016\n\nAR-AB also joined in on the beef by dissing Meek Mill in his own “Back to Back” freestyle.", "annotation_id": "7466049", "url": "https://genius.com/7466049", "state": "accepted", "votes": 225, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "You gon’ make me step out of my fuckin’ frame You gon’ make me buy bottles for Charlamagne You gon’ make me go out of my fuckin’ way", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The enemy of your enemy is your friend, so Drake decided to align with Power 105.1’s Charlamagne Tha God – a known Drake “hater” and a sworn enemy of rival radio station Hot 97’s Funkmaster Flex. Flex supported Meek Mill during the beef, dropping the reference tracks of alleged ghostwriter Quentin Miller, as well as premiering Meek Mill’s diss “Wanna Know.”\n\nIn response to the spreading beef, Charlemagne named Flex Donkey of the Day, delivering a eulogy to Flex’s credibility that trended worldwide. Shortly after, Charlemagne also gave Meek Mill donkey of the day honors.\n\nOh, and the 6 God actually bought Charlamagne 6 bottles.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/5uWHk5TNbS/", "annotation_id": "7466035", "url": "https://genius.com/7466035", "state": "accepted", "votes": 510, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "Yeah, trigger fingers turn to Twitter fingers Yeah, you gettin’ bodied by a singin’ nigga I’m not the type of nigga that’ll type to niggas", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song is in response to a series of tweets Meek Mill sent Drake’s way accusing him of using a ghost writer for his verse on his song “R.I.C.O.” While Drake has since fired back on wax with “Charged Up,” Meek continues to Tweet about the situation. Not the response you would expect from a man who’s “trigger finger itchin'.”\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/625101719836434433\n\nIn similar fashion to “Charged Up,” Drake channels Eminem’s 8 Mile vibe and flips a negative about him into a positive. What’s worse than getting dissed by a rapper? Getting dissed by a singer. Moreover, he uses the popular internet meme – “Drake the type of nigga that_____.” to flip a joke about himself on its head and use it against Meek.", "annotation_id": "7466020", "url": "https://genius.com/7466020", "state": "accepted", "votes": 916, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "I’m not sure what it was that really made y’all mad But I guess this is what I gotta do to make y’all rap", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "And now Drake digs into the beef’s genesis. Meek Mill, frustrated that Drake didn’t promote Dreams Worth More Than Money, said Drake didn’t write his verse on “R.I.C.O,” and lashed out in a twitter rant. Here, Drake brushes off the ghostwriting accusations once more, and claims he doesn’t really see the reason Meek is going at him.\n\nThis is the second track Drake has dropped addressing the beef (hence “Back to Back”), but Meek still doesn’t have anything other than twitter to back his statements. He was supposed to release a response track on July 27th, but he once again took to social media instead, posting  a 15 second audio of – screams.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/626304322981105664", "annotation_id": "7466019", "url": "https://genius.com/7466019", "state": "accepted", "votes": 188, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "Yeah I learned the game from William Wesley, you can never check me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "William “Worldwide Wes” Wesley is considered one of the most influential businessmen in sports, especially basketball.  “Check me” is a basketball term that means to “challenge me.” By saying “you can never check me,” Drake continues the basketball analogy by belittling Meek.\n\nThis is the second time Drake has personally mentioned Wes in a song. In his verse on Mary J. Blige’s “The One,” he says:\n\nWes put me on the marquee flight life\nPlus I go hard for the woman that I might wife\n\nAnd of course, Jay Z shouted out Wes on Drake’s “Pound Cake/Paris Morton Music 2”:\n\nShout out to World Wide Wes\nEverywhere we go, we leave a worldwide mess", "annotation_id": "7466014", "url": "https://genius.com/7466014", "state": "accepted", "votes": 308, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "Back to back for the niggas that didn’t get the message Back to back, like I’m on the cover of Lethal Weapon Back to back, like I’m Jordan ’96, ’97", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Meek stayed silent in the four days after Drake released “Charged Up,” so this freestyle is the second of back-to-back volleys from Drake’s side of the beef.\n\nMel Gibson and Danny Glover stand back to back in the poster for Lethal Weapon 3, and Michael Jordan delivered consecutive titles in 1996 and 1997.\n\n\n\nInterestingly, Jordan won a third consecutive title in 1998, and along with the championship ring Drake posted on instagram, it was enough to start rumors of a track called “Three Peat.”", "annotation_id": "7466012", "url": "https://genius.com/7466012", "state": "accepted", "votes": 386, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "line": "I did another one, I did another one You still ain’t did shit about the other one", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song marks Drake’s second track aimed at Meek Mill. When this song was released, Meek had only responded on Twitter. It wasn’t until the day after this song was released did he drop “Wanna Know.”\n\nThe song cover on Soundcloud shows Joe Carter playing for Drake’s hometown team, the Toronto Blue Jays, after he hit the home run that won the 1993 World Series against Meek’s hometown team, the Philadelphia Phillies. The Blue Jays had won the year before in 1992, thus winning “back to back” titles.\n\nDrizzy really thought this one out: the same day this song was released, the Blue Jays and Phillies were scheduled to play. Poetically, the Blue Jays defeated the Phillies 8-2.\n\nCarter has since responded, shouting out Drizzy on Twitter to personally thank him for using his picture as cover art.\n\n\n\nDrake, the king of memes, channels DJ Khaled’s classic “Another One” meme.", "annotation_id": "7466009", "url": "https://genius.com/7466009", "state": "accepted", "votes": 379, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Back to Back", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 22, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-back-to-back-lyrics", "genius_title": "Back To Back"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Wish a nigga would like a tree in this bitch", "vault_lines": ["Wish a nigga would like a tree in this bitch"], "meaning": "Wish a nigga would (wood) like a tree in this bitch", "annotation_id": "37584634", "url": "https://genius.com/37584634", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "E ’bout it every day, every day, every day", "vault_lines": ["And we 'bout it every day, every day, every day"], "meaning": "This lyric is in reference to Hamish Dhillion who is actually “about eating” everyday rather than being “about it”. As Hamish is a diehard drake fan", "annotation_id": "35406478", "url": "https://genius.com/35406478", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Can’t see ’em ’cause the money in the way, real nigga, what’s up?", "vault_lines": ["Can't see 'em 'cause the money in the way, real nigga, what's up?", "Real nigga, what's up?"], "meaning": "This is a likely reference to Lil Wayne’s line in his and Birdman’s song “Stuntin' Like My Money”\n\nYeah, bitch I’m paid, that’s all I gotta say, can’t see you lil niggas, the money in the way", "annotation_id": "30725669", "url": "https://genius.com/30725669", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Seven grams in the blunt", "vault_lines": ["Seven grams in the blunt"], "meaning": "Wayne is referring to a quad of weed which weighs about 7 grams – enough for a large blunt.", "annotation_id": "30223295", "url": "https://genius.com/30223295", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "I tongue-kiss her other tongue", "vault_lines": ["I tongue-kiss her other tongue"], "meaning": "Her clit", "annotation_id": "29335062", "url": "https://genius.com/29335062", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "I’m twisted, doorknob", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake compares himself to a doorknob through this metaphor. he’s twisted the same way you turn a doorknob…in the case that he’s not thinking straight, “I’m fucked up, tore down” emphasizing the types of thoughts/actions he could be going through", "annotation_id": "26006713", "url": "https://genius.com/26006713", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Got a drum on the gun, Energizer Bunny", "vault_lines": ["Got a drum on the gun, Energizer Bunny"], "meaning": "A drum is a magazine that can attach to a gun to allow many more bullets to be fired before reloading. The Energizer Bunny is the mascot for Energizer, the battery company, who carries around a bass drum.", "annotation_id": "16368920", "url": "https://genius.com/16368920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Skeet, skeet, skeet, water gun", "vault_lines": ["Skeet, skeet, skeet, water gun"], "meaning": "“Skeeting” is slang for ejaculating, most famously used by Lil' Jon and the East Side Boyz in their song “Get Low”.\n\nTil all skeet skeet motherfuckers, all skeet skeet god damn!\nTil all skeet skeet motherfuckers, all skeet skeet god damn!\n\nWayne compares his ejaculate to water coming out of a squirt gun.", "annotation_id": "11262839", "url": "https://genius.com/11262839", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "I’m the fuckin’ man, y’all don’t get it, do ya?", "vault_lines": ["I'm the fuckin' man, y'all don't get it, do ya?"], "meaning": "If you didn’t know it by now, Drake’s rise to the top of the rap game has been a swift move. His relatively short career has spawned major success, such as getting rich off a mixtape and his debut album topping Billboard. Still, after so much success he’s still slept on by hip hop heads who say Drake’s too soft.\n\nIf you wrote off Drake after hearing Thank Me Later, chances are you no longer carry that same attitude. “The Motto” is the bonus track, meaning you’ve heard what many call Drake’s best work. Drake is now the man.", "annotation_id": "8457323", "url": "https://genius.com/8457323", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Go Uptown, New York City, bitch", "vault_lines": ["Go Uptown, New York City, bitch"], "meaning": "Uptown NYC is a very fashion-minded neighborhood with little tourism. Being a fashion connoisseur himself, Drake must spend a lot of time in the Uptown area.\n\nThere is also a large Hispanic population in Upper Manhattan, specifically in Washington Heights or the upper-western side of Manhattan, which ties in with the next line about Spanish girls loving him.\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to Drizzy’s song “Uptown” off of So Far Gone.", "annotation_id": "726383", "url": "https://genius.com/726383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Type of money, everybody acting like they knew ya", "vault_lines": ["Type of money, everybody acting like they knew ya"], "meaning": "Drake’s making sufficient sums of money for fake people to come out of the woodwork and act like they knew him back when he was up-and-coming.\n\nHe’s previously mentioned how people try to use him – such as in the “Say What’s Real” freestyle:\n\nAnd don’t listen to anybody that knew me\nCause to have known me would\nMean that there’s a new me\nAnd if you think I changed in the slightest\nCould of fooled me", "annotation_id": "725549", "url": "https://genius.com/725549", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Clubbin’ hard, fuckin’ women, there ain’t much to do", "vault_lines": ["Clubbin' hard, fuckin' women, there ain't much to do"], "meaning": "He’s so bored with this lifestyle that the activites most men his age love to do are just what he does to pass the time.\n\n\n\nThis is expressed better by his OVOXO colleague Abel Tesfaye of The Weeknd, who is self-destructively addicted to this lifestyle.\n\nAlso, these are the things Uncle Luke (see below) describe that rappers like to do in Miami, instead of trying to help the community.", "annotation_id": "505731", "url": "https://genius.com/505731", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Tell Uncle Luke I’m out in Miami too", "vault_lines": ["Tell Uncle Luke I'm out in Miami too"], "meaning": "Southern rap pioneer, former 2 Live Crew impresario and former Miami mayoral candidate Luther “Luke” Campbell recently put a few hip-hop personalities on blast for living in Miami while not giving back to the community. If they don’t change their ways by January 2012, Luke threatens to have their “Miami cards” revoked. Luke lost the election.\n\nThe open letter mentions “Lil Wayne, DJ Khaled, Puff Daddy, and all their boys” – Drake apparently feels like he was worthy of inclusion by name as well.", "annotation_id": "490906", "url": "https://genius.com/490906", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "She know even if I’m fucking with her, I don’t really need her", "vault_lines": ["She know even if I'm fucking with her, I don't really need her"], "meaning": "This line is reminiscent to a Jay Z lyric on the song “Big Pimpin'”:\n\nYou know I – thug em, fuck em, love em, leave em\nCause I don’t fuckin' need em", "annotation_id": "446517", "url": "https://genius.com/446517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Nigga, money talks, and Mr. Ed, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, money talks, and Mr. Ed, yeah"], "meaning": "You’re hearing it straight from the horse’s mouth:\n\n\nMr. Ed is the titular talking horse of a 1960s sitcom.\n“Money talks” is a popular expression meaning that anyone can talk big, but if you can prove it with your bank account, then you’re really “talking.”", "annotation_id": "430137", "url": "https://genius.com/430137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "I’m so Young Money", "vault_lines": ["I'm so Young Money"], "meaning": "Perfunctory shout-out to his label.\n\nHere, Wayne is also referencing the clever wordplay in his choice of his label’s name. Young Money is a clever flip of the term “old money,” which refers to established upper-class families who have been able to maintain their wealth and social standing over multiple generations. Young money on the other hand would describe someone who has gone from rags to riches and also denotes Wayne’s ability to live the lap of luxury at such a young age because of his immense success.", "annotation_id": "429856", "url": "https://genius.com/429856", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Gettin’ paid, we’ll holler whenever that stop", "vault_lines": ["Gettin' paid, we'll holler whenever that stop"], "meaning": "YMCMB has been making a lot of money lately (See: Tha Carter IV). Take Care sold 631,000 its first week, and as of December 2011 he’s sold over one million copies. Certified platinum!\n\n\n\nAin’t that right, Aubrey?\n\nAlso, he’s continuing his conversation with Mac Dre, saying he’ll talk to Mac whenever he stops getting money, i.e. when he’s dead.", "annotation_id": "426254", "url": "https://genius.com/426254", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Wrist bling, got a condo up on Biscayne", "vault_lines": ["Wrist bling, got a condo up on Biscayne"], "meaning": "For once, a rapper isn’t lying about his wealth.  Drake recently bought two condos in the Marquis building in Miami, which is on Biscayne Boulevard downtown.\n\nDrake later sold the luxury condos to Miami Heat guard, Mario Chalmers. The units sold less than 24 hours after hitting the market and Drake turned a $500,000 profit on the sale.", "annotation_id": "425975", "url": "https://genius.com/425975", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "It’s Eastside, we in this bitch", "vault_lines": ["It's Eastside, we in this bitch"], "meaning": "Wayne is from the east side of New Orleans, along with a substantial portion of Cash Money, including Mack Maine and Taz. Drake is from Forest Hill in Toronto, which does not really count (though it’s broadly in eastern Canada).\n\nThis is also an extension of the “One…” and “Seven…” lines above (notice how Weezy pauses after the numbers). Put them together with eastside and you get the 17th Ward in New Orleans where Weezy grew up", "annotation_id": "425968", "url": "https://genius.com/425968", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "I’m callin’ niggas out like the umpire", "vault_lines": ["I'm callin' niggas out like the umpire"], "meaning": "In baseball and tennis, umpires call players out.\n\n\n\nSimilarly, Wayne’s not afraid to name names or get rid of anyone in his way.\n\nWeezy’s not the first to say this: Black Thought said it in “Web 20/20,” and the most intellectually gifted member of the Wu-Tang Clan said something similar.", "annotation_id": "425964", "url": "https://genius.com/425964", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "One time, fuck one time", "vault_lines": ["One time, fuck one time"], "meaning": "This speaks to Wayne’s tendency to to go big or go home. He doesn’t want to have sex with a girl one time when he can hit it multiple times, and he wants to live his awesome life more than once – fuck YOLO.\n\n“One time” is also slang for the police, so this line is also a variation of “fuck the police”. Wayne also says “fuck one time” in reference to the po-po on his track “30 Minutes To New Orleans”.", "annotation_id": "425942", "url": "https://genius.com/425942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Like we sittin’ on the bench, nigga, we don’t really play", "vault_lines": ["Like we sittin' on the bench, nigga, we don't really play"], "meaning": "This can mean three things:\n\n\nDrake’s too cool to play the (dating/pick-up) game, so he stays on the bench and watches all the other players (i.e. playas) hustling around. In the end, all the women come to him, ‘cause he’s so laid back. This carries on from his verse’s main themes.\nDrake’s serious about his YOLO lifestyle (which consists entirely of the totally unserious pursuits of clubbing and fucking) and he doesn’t play around about it–in other words, he sits on the bench.\nThe habit top clubs have of hiring famous stars to sit on their benches (happens frequently in Miami and Vegas). The stars don’t get called to play but if they did they would kill it.\n\n\n\nIn other words, he doesn’t play around, he gets things done. He takes his music and everything he does seriously.\n\n\n\nChildish Gambino said it first in “I Be On That”:\n\nYes I’m Sittin on the bench, cuz I don’t play no games\nI just spit that Belleview, watch them go insane", "annotation_id": "425933", "url": "https://genius.com/425933", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "‘Cause the pimpin’ ice cold, all these bitches wanna chill", "vault_lines": ["'Cause the pimpin' ice cold, all these bitches wanna chill"], "meaning": "As Ludacris said in “Pimpin All Over the World”,\n\nI went up north and thought it was way too cold,\nUntil I went to Canada and saw some beautiful hoes.\n\nThere is wordplay on “ice cold” and “wanna chill”.", "annotation_id": "425931", "url": "https://genius.com/425931", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Me, Franny, and Mally Mall at the crib-o", "vault_lines": ["Me, Franny, and Mally Mall at the crib-o"], "meaning": "Drake had the album finished and ready to ship, but during his birthday weekend in Vegas he recorded “The Motto” at DJ Franzen (Franny) & Mally Mall’s studio, hence the shout-out. He loved the song so much he decided to add it to his album as a bonus track.\n\n“Mally Mall” is of course a homage/plagiarism of rap legend Marley Marl, who used to team up with Freddie Foxx.\n\nMally Mall, by the way, who is now a rapper associated with Tyga, was formerly a Las Vegas entrepreneur of sorts (there’s suspciously little information about his pre-music life available online), but he managed to make enough money prior to rapping to build his own studio in his home, where Drake recorded “The Motto.” In this video, Mally Mall discusses recording with Drake.", "annotation_id": "425923", "url": "https://genius.com/425923", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Tell Tune light one, pass it like a relay", "vault_lines": ["Tell Tune light one, pass it like a relay"], "meaning": "Drake tells Wayne to pass him the blunt like relay racers do with batons.  Considering Wayne’s status as a parolee, encouraging his weed smoking may not be the best idea.", "annotation_id": "425898", "url": "https://genius.com/425898", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Them Spanish girls love me like I’m Aventura", "vault_lines": ["Them Spanish girls love me like I'm Aventura"], "meaning": "Aventura is a largely-beloved Bachata group, formed in the Bronx, New York during the ‘90s by four Dominican-American musicians, two of whom (Lenny and Max) were brothers, while the other two (Anthony “Romeo” and Henry) were cousins.\n\n\n\nThe group disbanded in 2011, with their lead singer Romeo Santos experiencing a particularly successful solo career ever since: he would go on to collaborate with Drake himself in 2014, on the track “Odio”.\n\nAventura is also a neighborhood in the northeastern Miami-Dade County, Florida, which notably hosts one of the biggest malls in the world, which is quite popular with the city’s Spanish-speaking community.", "annotation_id": "425882", "url": "https://genius.com/425882", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Talk stupid, off with your head", "vault_lines": ["Talk stupid, off with your head"], "meaning": "Say anything he doesn’t like and he’ll decapitate you, like Robespierre of France, who ordered the guillotine for thousands of his countrymen.\n\n\n\nThe line alludes to Alice’s Adventure in Wonderland where the Queen of Hearts is talking nonsense – here’s an excerpt,\n\n“No, no!” said the Queen. “Sentence first – verdict afterwards.”\n\n“Stuff and nonsense!” said Alice loudly. “The idea of having the sentence first!”\n\n“Hold your tongue!” said the Queen, turning purple.\n\n“I won’t!” said Alice.\n\n“Off with her head!” the Queen shouted at the top of her voice. Nobody moved.", "annotation_id": "425806", "url": "https://genius.com/425806", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "That’s my MO, add a B to that shit", "vault_lines": ["That's my MO, add a B to that shit"], "meaning": "“M.O.” is an abbreviation for the Latin term modus operandi (1), or “Mode of Operation”–simply put, how you live your life. His modus operandi is to add a B to anything to make it better (music, food, sex etc.).\n\nWayne’s M.O. is Money Over Bitches (as his chest tattoo states).\n\n\n\nM.O.B. is also slang for the Bloods, with whom Wayne is openly affiliated. The Bloods are a gang, or a mob.", "annotation_id": "425788", "url": "https://genius.com/425788", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Shout goes out to Niko, J, and Chubbs, shout to Gibbo", "vault_lines": ["Shout goes out to Niko, J, and Chubbs, shout to Gibbo"], "meaning": "All members of his OVOXO crew.", "annotation_id": "425737", "url": "https://genius.com/425737", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Twenty-five, sitting on twenty-five mil’, uh", "vault_lines": ["Twenty-five, sitting on twenty-five mil', uh"], "meaning": "A shout-out to of The Game’s “American Dream”\n\nWhen you’re 29, sitting on 29 mil\n\nDrake turned 25 on October 24th, 2011 and claimed to have $25 million in the bank at the time. Doing the math, this isn’t unreasonable, considering his $10 million in 2010 combined with his $11 million in 2011. That’s already $21 million as of August 2011, and it doesn’t include any 2009 data, Degrassi money, or bar mitzvah money. It’s also possible that some of the $20.5 million he made in 2012 is included in that $25 million figure if he signed the contracts before writing the lyrics.", "annotation_id": "425721", "url": "https://genius.com/425721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Almost drowned in her pussy, so I swam to her butt", "vault_lines": ["Almost drowned in her pussy, so I swam to her butt"], "meaning": "The line itself is pretty explanatory, but could this be the weirdest reference to Finding Nemo ever? (in which a boat is mistakenly labeled as a “butt,” and Nemo swims towards it)", "annotation_id": "425684", "url": "https://genius.com/425684", "state": "accepted", "votes": 168, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "YMCMB, you niggas more YMCA", "vault_lines": ["YMCMB, you niggas more YMCA"], "meaning": "Young Money Cash Money Billionaires, the alternate name of the Young Money rap crew. Drake and his team are YMCMB while everyone else in the rap game is closer to the Village People, the disco singing group who were “originally created to target disco’s gay audience by featuring popular gay fantasy personas”.  Their biggest hit was “YMCA”, a not-very-subtle ode to the “men’s” part of the Young Men’s Christian Association franchises", "annotation_id": "425483", "url": "https://genius.com/425483", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Still gettin’ brain from a thang, ain’t shit changed", "vault_lines": ["Still gettin' brain from a thang, ain't shit changed"], "meaning": "Drake may be famous, but he keeps hood girls around for services (getting head).\n\nReference to a Jay-Z line from “Watch Me”\n\nStill getting brain and it’s plain ain’t a thang gon' change", "annotation_id": "425481", "url": "https://genius.com/425481", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "You only live once, that’s the motto, nigga, YOLO", "vault_lines": ["You only live once, that's the motto, nigga, YOLO"], "meaning": "YOLO is the name of a popular restaurant in Miami. Since Drake mentions Miami in this song, it makes sense he’d draw inspiration from a place he’s been.\nAnother restaurant, Planet Hollywood in Las Vegas, is also called YOLO. The slogan is “You only live once so…live the good life!”\n\nThe phrase You Only Live Once went on to become quite the fixture within pop culture.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, Mac Dre rapped a similar line on “Life’s a Bitch”:\n\nLife is short you only live once and ain’t no telling when your leaving", "annotation_id": "425404", "url": "https://genius.com/425404", "state": "accepted", "votes": 155, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "Oh my God, Becky, look at her butt, Tunechi", "vault_lines": ["Oh my God, Becky, look at her butt, Tunechi"], "meaning": "This line is an interpolation of the opening lines from Sir Mix-A-Lot’s “Baby Got Back.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X53ZSxkQ3Ho\n\nThis is why Sir Mix-a-Lot was given a writing credit in the metadata.", "annotation_id": "425371", "url": "https://genius.com/425371", "state": "accepted", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "I’m in the building and I’m feeling myself Rest in peace Mac Dre, I’ma do it for the Bay, okay", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the building and I'm feeling myself", "Rest in peace Mac Dre, I'ma do it for the Bay, okay"], "meaning": "Drake pays homage to legendary Bay Area rapper Mac Dre by reciting the “I’m in the buildin' and I’m feelin' myself” line from the chorus of his July 2004 single “Feelin' Myself.” He was killed in a drive-by shooting in Kansas City, Missouri on November 1, 2004.\n\nIn Northern California’s Bay Area (San Francisco/Oakland/Vallejo), they’re known for “thizzing” – more commonly called “rolling” – on the Ecstasy pill.  When you’re on the E pill your senses are way more (ahem) sensitive, hence “feeling myself” –sexual overtones definitely intended. Mac Dre popularized the “thizz” subculture as part of the Bay Area’s Hyphy Movement in the early 2000s. You can watch the “Thizzelle Dance” here:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BbdpEPoN9Kw\n\nTrivia: This song was released on November 1, 2011: exactly seven years after the death of Mac Dre.\n\nAnother reason for the Mac Dre shoutout comes in the chorus when Drake introduces us to the acronym ‘YOLO’: You only live once is a lyric from the Mac Dre song “Life’s a Bitch”. All of this ties in to the fact that Mac Dre died at the age of 34 (which he eerily predicted on the track):\n\nLife is short, you only live once and ain’t no telling when you’re leaving\nSo I'ma do it all, have a ball while I’m still living and breathing\nLife’s a bitch and then you die.\n\nThis line also interpolates Bay Area rapper Keak da Sneak’s “Super Hyphy”:\n\nI’m in the buildin' and I’m feelin' like yee!\nRIP to the Mac D.R.E.", "annotation_id": "425311", "url": "https://genius.com/425311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "line": "I mean maybe she won’t, then again, maybe she will", "vault_lines": ["I mean maybe she won't, then again, maybe she will"], "meaning": "This is an interpolation of a line from “She Will,” a song from Lil Wayne’s album Tha Carter IV.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_7nYuyfkjCk&t=1m42s", "annotation_id": "425251", "url": "https://genius.com/425251", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/011b9f00d72d0d3841f6bcf9cbc4e83c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"The Motto\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>The MottoDrake3.9M", "status": "done", "targets": 37, "clicked": 37, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-the-motto-lyrics", "genius_title": "The Motto"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "Oh my God, Becky Look at her butt", "vault_lines": ["Oh my God, Becky", "Look at her butt"], "meaning": "Referring to Nicki Minaj’s infamous song “Anaconda”.\n\nOh my God, look at her butt", "annotation_id": "32227050", "url": "https://genius.com/32227050", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "Them Spanish girls love me like I’m Aventura", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Aventura is a popular Dominican bachata group featuring lead singer Romeo Santos, who Drake worked with in 2014 on the song ‘Odio’.", "annotation_id": "22455046", "url": "https://genius.com/22455046", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "I’m calling niggas out like the umpire", "vault_lines": ["I'm calling niggas out like the umpire"], "meaning": "In baseball and tennis, umpires call players out.\n\n\n\nSimilarly, Wayne’s not afraid to name names or get rid of anyone in his way.\n\nWeezy’s not the first to say this: Black Thought said it in “Web 20/20,” and the most intellectually gifted member of the Wu-Tang Clan said something similar.", "annotation_id": "8459630", "url": "https://genius.com/8459630", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "[Produced by T-Minus]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "According to the liner notes, the track was produced by T-Minus, and the bass part was performed by Noah “40” Shebib. There are no samples credited, but there are two interpolations, both occurring at the end of a verse:\n\n\nOn Drake’s verse, he interpolates a line from “She Will” by Lil Wayne (which featured Drake and was also produced by T-Minus)\nOn Wayne’s verse, he interpolates a line from “Baby Got Back” by Sir Mix-a-Lot", "annotation_id": "8459609", "url": "https://genius.com/8459609", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "Tell Uncle Luke, “I’m out in Miami, too” Clubbin’ hard, fuckin’ women, there ain’t much to do", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Southern rap pioneer, former 2 Live Crew impresario and former Miami mayoral candidate Luther “Luke” Campbell recently put a few hip-hop personalities on blast for living in Miami while not giving back to the community. If they don’t change their ways by January 2012, Luke threatens to have their “Miami cards” revoked. The Miami card is a free pass to all of Miami’s city-owned attractions. It became irrelevant however as Luke lost the election.\n\nThe open letter mentions “Lil Wayne, DJ Khaled, Puff Daddy, and all their boys”. Apparently Drake feels like he was worthy of inclusion by name as well.", "annotation_id": "8457536", "url": "https://genius.com/8457536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "Go Uptown, New York City, bitch", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Uptown NYC is a very fashion-minded neighborhood with little tourism. Being a fashion connoisseur himself, Drake must spend a lot of time in the Uptown area.\n\nThere is also a large Hispanic population in Upper Manhattan, specifically in Washington Heights or the upper-western side of Manhattan, which ties in with the next line about Spanish girls loving him.\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to Drizzy’s song “Uptown” off of So Far Gone.", "annotation_id": "8457341", "url": "https://genius.com/8457341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "Type of money, everybody actin’ like they knew ya", "vault_lines": ["Type of money, everybody actin' like they knew ya"], "meaning": "Drake’s making sufficient sums of money for fake people to come out of the woodwork and act like they knew him back when he was up-and-coming.\n\nHe’s previously mentioned how people try to use himin the “Say What’s Real” freestyle:\n\nAnd don’t listen to anybody that knew me\nCause to have known me would\nMean that there’s a new me\nAnd if you think I changed in the slightest\nCould of fooled me", "annotation_id": "8457304", "url": "https://genius.com/8457304", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "I’m the fuckin’ man, y’all don’t get it, do ya?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s rise to the top of the rap game has been a swift move. His relatively short career has spawned major success, such as getting rich off a mixtape and his debut album topping Billboard. Still after so much success he’s still slept on by hip hop heads who say Drake’s too soft.", "annotation_id": "8457295", "url": "https://genius.com/8457295", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "Real L.A. nigga, chucks with the gun drawn", "vault_lines": ["Real L.A. nigga, chucks with the gun drawn"], "meaning": "Chuck Taylor All Stars are commonly worn among gangsters in California, who are widely understood to not take any chances and therefore avoid using a holster for their weapons.", "annotation_id": "3370915", "url": "https://genius.com/3370915", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "Shout goes out to Niko, J and Chubbs, shouts to Gibbo", "vault_lines": ["Shout goes out to Niko, J and Chubbs, shouts to Gibbo"], "meaning": "Niko, J, and Chubbs are all part of Drake’s crew.\n\nNiko’s Drake’s good friend. Niko recommended a good barber to Drake when he was still just Jimmy on Degrassi. and the two have been BFFs ever since. In his lengthy Thank Me Later thank you’s, Drake calls Niko his “closest confidant.” He continued, “our friendship is what keeps me alive and focused, I always thought I was an only child buy my mother lied!! Haaa.” Aww. And when he’s not with Drake? He’s the father to a very cute daughter and apparently a decent fly fisherman. http://www.thefader.com/2013/10/03/fader-explains-drakes-boys/\n\nChubbs is Drake’s muscle. As he put it to Complex in 2011: “Everybody wants to poke and jab at Drake because they don’t feel like he will throw back. But nobody around here is going to let something happen to him at any time, especially me. I’m not ever going to let nothing happen to him. If it’s going to happen it’s going to happen to me first.” Maybe that’s why he’s the only OVO member to stay by Drake’s side during the Miami Vice style shoot-out at the end of the “Hold On We’re Going Home” video. He’s also Drake’s regular basketball buddy.\n\nRead more: http://www.thefader.com/2013/10/03", "annotation_id": "2463318", "url": "https://genius.com/2463318", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "Drawing on your face, you’re a clown, jack in the box", "vault_lines": ["Drawing on your face, you're a clown, jack in the box"], "meaning": "Another shot at Gucci Mane\n\n\nOnly clowns have drawings on their faces, so if you got  drawings/tattoos on your face then you’re a clown.\nif he pulls a gun to Gucci Mane’s face, he’s gonna shut up and pussy out, in other words, get back in his box, like a Jack in the box", "annotation_id": "448557", "url": "https://genius.com/448557", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "You funny little nigga, Duval", "vault_lines": ["You funny little nigga, Duval"], "meaning": "Tyga makes a reference to to Lil' Duval, a famous black comedian who has starred in films such as Meet the Blacks.", "annotation_id": "434343", "url": "https://genius.com/434343", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "line": "Matt Forte got the bitch running back", "vault_lines": ["Matt Forte got the bitch running back"], "meaning": "Matt Forte is the running back for the Chicago Bears.Tyga has bitches running back to him because he’s not gonna go to them. Running as in constantly texting and calling him to be “their’s” again.", "annotation_id": "431677", "url": "https://genius.com/431677", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Motto (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 13, "clicked": 13, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-the-motto-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "The Motto (Remix)"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "[Outro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This spoken word outro is present only on the physical copy of the album, not the digital version on streaming services.", "annotation_id": "23910054", "url": "https://genius.com/23910054", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "Streets not safe but I never run away Even when I’m away", "vault_lines": ["Streets not safe but I never run away", "Even when I'm away"], "meaning": "In spite of troubles on the ground, Drizzy stays loyal to his girl and to his city, no matter how rough-and-tumble things get. Even though he’s often on the road, Drake can be counted on to stay in touch—and expects the same in return.", "annotation_id": "9072419", "url": "https://genius.com/9072419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "Got a pretty girl and she love me long time Fine like a wine, she love me long time Oh, yeah, very long time Back up, back up, back up, and whine it Back up, back up, and whine it Girl, just back up, back up, back up, and whine, girl Oh, yes, steady and fine Back, up, back up and whine it, mm", "vault_lines": ["Got a pretty girl and she love me long time", "Fine like a wine, she love me long time", "Oh, yeah, very long time", "Back up, back up, back up, and whine it", "Back up, back up, and whine it", "Girl, just back up, back up, back up, and whine, girl", "Oh, yes, steady and fine", "Back, up, back up and whine it, mm", "Where, where, where, where (Oh, yeah, very long time)"], "meaning": "Wining is a dance of Caribbean descent (also part of Wizkid’s Nigerian heritage) in which one slowly winds their waist in a circular motion. The pace of rotation varies, depending on the music and the beats per minute.\n\n\n\nWizkid and Kyla are playing the characters of two people who enjoy dancing together.\n\nThe phrase “love me long time” is thought to have originated in Stanley Kubrick’s 1987 film Full Metal Jacket about the Vietnam War.\nIn it, there’s a scene in which a Vietnamese prostitute, trying to solicit sex from American soldiers, promises “me love you long time.” The phrase has since been used in 2 Live Crew’s “Me So Horny” (another phrase uttered in the scene) and Fergie’s “London Bridge.”\n\nIn this part of the song, Wizkid says, “Back up, back up, back up and wine am,” not “wine it,” because “wine am” is Nigerian pidgin English and the direct translation for “wine it” in normal English.", "annotation_id": "8940841", "url": "https://genius.com/8940841", "state": "accepted", "votes": 204, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "You know you gotta stick by me Soon as you see the text, reply me I don’t wanna spend time fighting We’ve got no time, and that’s why", "vault_lines": ["You know you gotta stick by me", "Soon as you see the text, reply me", "I don't wanna spend time fighting", "We've got no time, and that's why", "You know that I don't play"], "meaning": "Drake has expressed his dislike for people who don’t reply to his texts before on Riz’s April 2012 song, “Waiting Up”:\n\nYeah, I wonder if I should call you\nBut then again you never call me\nAnd I’m just looking at my phone girl\nAnd the last three messages are all me", "annotation_id": "8940742", "url": "https://genius.com/8940742", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "Oti, oti There’s never much love when we go OT I pray to make it back in one piece I pray, I pray", "vault_lines": ["There's never much love when we go OT", "I pray to make it back in one piece"], "meaning": "In Yoruba (one of the Nigerian languages that Wizkid speaks), there’s some great wordplay going on that English listeners will miss.  See, “oti” means “no,” but “ọtí” (that’s oti with a short o and a rising i) means “liquor.”\n\nBack in English, “OT” is an acronym for “overtime” or “out of town.” Drake has used it before, on ILoveMakonnen’s “Tuesday”:\n\nAlways workin' OT, overtime and outta town\n\nDrake claims whenever he and his girl venture out of the city, they’re greeted with nothing but the threat of violence. He wants one more euphoric dance before they have to return to the dangerous streets.", "annotation_id": "8940646", "url": "https://genius.com/8940646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 420, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "Strength and guidance All that I’m wishing for my friends", "vault_lines": ["All that I'm wishing for my friends"], "meaning": "“Strength and guidance” is a popular phrase among members of the Jamaican Rastafari community and the wider Jamaican diaspora worldwide.\n\nAdhering to the Caribbean nature of the song, Drake uses the phrase to explain what he hopes his day-one friends may find, and perhaps what he hopes for as well. Under the latter reading, he may be praying for strength and guidance as it relates to the girl he’s with, since they appear to be getting into some rough situations.", "annotation_id": "8940024", "url": "https://genius.com/8940024", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "That’s why I need a one dance Got a Hennessy in my hand One more time ’fore I go Higher powers taking a hold on me I need a one dance Got a Hennessy in my hand One more time ’fore I go Higher powers taking a hold on me", "vault_lines": ["That's why I need a one dance", "Got a Hennessy in my hand", "One more time 'fore I go", "Higher powers taking a hold on me", "I need a one dance"], "meaning": "Drake needs one more dance with his lady before he leaves. These “higher powers” he’s talking about seem to be his desire to dance more when he’s drunk.\n\nDrake famously busted a move in his video for “Hotline Bling.” Critics called his dancing “awkward” and “interesting,” but the vid sparked one of the biggest internet memes of 2015.\n\nToronto has a large Caribbean population, and the West Indian influence can be seen in the city’s food, music, and culture. It’s these flavors that Drake used to spice up Views, showcasing the city’s love of reggae and dancehall as seen in Rihanna’s “Work” music video.\n\n\n\nHennessy is a French cognac and a staple within hip-hop culture. Drake has referenced the brand before on “HYFR.”", "annotation_id": "8939988", "url": "https://genius.com/8939988", "state": "accepted", "votes": 369, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "Nobody makes it from my ends I had to bust up the silence", "vault_lines": ["Nobody makes it from my ends", "I had to bust up the silence"], "meaning": "“Ends” is a slang term for an area where you live, or have grown up in; often used in London (UK) and Toronto. Drake has used the term before when giving a nod to Skepta’s “That’s Not Me” on his song “Used To”:\n\nShout out to the G’s from the ends\nWe don’t love no girls from the ends\n\nThe term originates from Jamaican patois as both Toronto and London have a rather large Caribbean diaspora.\n\nDrake grew up in many different parts of Toronto and spent his early years on Weston Road, a place known for its working class. Here, he might be reaffirming that he defied odds and rose from very little, as he did in his 2013 hit single, “Started From The Bottom.” However, he could also be referring to his hometown’s relatively small music scene, and how he’s the only artist to reach a certain level of international success.", "annotation_id": "8939522", "url": "https://genius.com/8939522", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "[Refrain: Kyla] Baby, I like your style", "vault_lines": ["Baby, I like your style"], "meaning": "This line was sampled from British singer Kyla’s 2008 hit “Do You Mind.” When she was first contacted by Drake’s team, she initially thought she was being pranked.\n\n“Baby I like your style” is a common Nigerian pick-up line. Many Naija artists have used this phrase, including:\n\n\n\nDavido on “One of a Kind,”\n\n\nCapital FEMI on “Tonight,”\n\n\nWizkid on “Drop”", "annotation_id": "8939318", "url": "https://genius.com/8939318", "state": "accepted", "votes": 378, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "line": "Grips on your waist, front way, back way You know that I don’t play", "vault_lines": ["Grips on your waist, front way, back way", "You know that I don't play"], "meaning": "Drake’s got his hands on his girl’s waist as they grind to dancehall music. The dance he describes is the Caribbean “wine/whine,” which involves hip gyration.\n\n“Front way, back way” is a common dancehall phrase that calls for a switch in positions during the wine: the woman moves from facing the man to turning around, so that her partner can grind on her from behind.\n\nIt has been used in numerous dancehall songs, including Beenie Man’s “Wine Gal,” Sean Paul’s “Like Glue,” and RDX’s “Bend Over”.", "annotation_id": "8939156", "url": "https://genius.com/8939156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 185, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0af93f8023b7f0885a4f1455b4099e8d.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"One Dance\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>One DanceDrake6.4M", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 10, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-one-dance-lyrics", "genius_title": "One Dance"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "Next soundbwoy is not my friend", "vault_lines": ["Next soundbwoy is not my friend"], "meaning": "A “Soundbwoy” is a caribbean term for a DJ ,specifically one from the Dancehall scene. Drake believes that other DJ’s are not on his side. Given that this version was premiered at an event with Wiz Khalifa (someone who DJ’s under the name DJ DaddyKat)\n\n\n\n in attendance, who was dissed by the Unruly gang during the performance, this could be a Drake Barb aimed at Wiz as well as anyone who refuses to play this record.", "annotation_id": "10041481", "url": "https://genius.com/10041481", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "Grips on your waist", "vault_lines": ["Grips on your waist"], "meaning": "Influenced by the carribbean vibe, drake uses the term ‘'grips on your waist’‘ to show that he is performing a dance with the female which involves being close and grinding on each other.This dance is known in the caribbean as 'whining’\n\nhttps://s3.amazonaws.com/filepicker-images-rapgenius/fVFh8aOqS9SgV6gQO2cb_CcBggh4WEAA5UXC.jpg%3Alarge", "annotation_id": "9984372", "url": "https://genius.com/9984372", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "Wicked, wicked, wicked", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Reference to Popcaan’s slang wicked wicked wicked, as shown in his song Wicked Man Thing", "annotation_id": "9943558", "url": "https://genius.com/9943558", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "Drizzle got the machine in his hand", "vault_lines": ["Drizzle got the machine in his hand"], "meaning": "He is holding a gun in his hand", "annotation_id": "9943557", "url": "https://genius.com/9943557?", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "What sound can test? I don’t know Watch the whole dance get turned over", "vault_lines": ["Watch the whole dance get turned over", "Watch the whole dance get turned over, say", "What sound can test? I don't know"], "meaning": "Premiering a dub of one of the most popular dancehall styled songs of 2016, Drake shows his support of Popcaan and the Unruly Crew and knows that no dub could be better than his. He also recognizes that once the song begins, everyone in the crowd will turn up even more.\n\nIntroducing a dubbed version of a song is often looked upon with high regard. A dubbed song usually utilizes the original artist, editing lyrics to support a certain crew. It is often very difficult to acquire a dubbed version of a song, because some artists don’t feel comfortable changing their original lyrics.", "annotation_id": "9942910", "url": "https://genius.com/9942910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "Unruly me say", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake likely refers to Unruly Entertainment, which is a company founded by Popcaan. It is also home to Unruly clothing and a brand of rolling papers that is commonly referred to as ‘Unruly Rizla’.\n\n“Unruly” is considered to be Popcaan’s signature motto. In an interview with Boomshots:, he said:\n\nYou know say we Godly, but we unruly same way.Cause you done know we nah left the street. The street is a part of me yunno.", "annotation_id": "9942646", "url": "https://genius.com/9942646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "Oti, oti, there’s never much love when we go OT", "vault_lines": ["Oti, oti, there's never much love when we go OT"], "meaning": "“oti” means “no” in Yoruba, one of the Nigerian languages that Wizkid speaks. However, “ọtí” (with a short o and a rising i) means “liquor.”\n\nOT is an acronym for “overtime,” “out of town,” or “on tour”.", "annotation_id": "9942442", "url": "https://genius.com/9942442", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "Poppy got that Hennessy in his hand", "vault_lines": ["Poppy got that Hennessy in his hand"], "meaning": "Drake could be refering to is himself as Papi since his Instagram name is champagnepapi, but the name is most likely a shoutout to Popcaan who is also known as Poppy for short.\n\nHennessy is a cognac and a staple within hip-hop culture. Drake has referenced the brand before on HYFR.", "annotation_id": "9942418", "url": "https://genius.com/9942418?", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "[Intro: Kyla] Baby I like your style", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This line was sampled from British singer Kyla’s 2008 hit “Do You Mind.” When she was first contacted by Drake’s team, she initially thought she was being pranked.\n\n“Baby I like your style” is a common Nigerian pick-up line. Many Naija artists, including Davido on “One of a Kind,” Capital FEMI on “Tonight,” and Wizkid on “Drop,” have used this phrase.", "annotation_id": "9942398", "url": "https://genius.com/9942398", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "This sound just done the place", "vault_lines": ["This sound just done the place"], "meaning": "In this context Drake is using the word “done” similarly to how he did on Hype only instead of meaning to “finish” or “cease” the place, he’s saying that this song kills it in the club", "annotation_id": "9942156", "url": "https://genius.com/9942156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "line": "Don’t know why they still try me Bun Vegas, he cyaan reply me", "vault_lines": ["Don't know why they still try me", "Bun Vegas, he cyaan reply me"], "meaning": "This line is in reference to Mr. Vegas' previous statements and concerns with Drake’s crediting manner on the song “Controlla” from his album Views.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VI-6eE_XKnE", "annotation_id": "9624884", "url": "https://genius.com/9624884", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "One Dance (Dub)", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 11, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-one-dance-dub-lyrics", "genius_title": "One Dance (Dub)"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "There’s nowhere to hide, there’s nowhere to hide, you know what I mean", "vault_lines": ["There's nowhere to hide, there's nowhere to hide, you know what I mean"], "meaning": "After the whole UMG lawsuit that Drake pursued post the beef, it became public that a company called Nowhere to Hide linked to an individual named Kojo Asamoah. Kojo and his company has been subpoenaed in the UMG lawsuit. Kojo is allegedly an associate of Kendrick Lamar and is accused of helping inflate Lamar’s streaming numbers during the beef by using streaming bots and paying for playlist placements (payola).", "annotation_id": "40975871", "url": "https://genius.com/40975871", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You a dog and you know it, you just play sweet", "vault_lines": ["You a dog and you know it, you just play sweet"], "meaning": "In the wake of recent developments involving Drake’s ongoing lawsuit against Universal Music Group (UMG), Drake appears to directly address Kendrick Lamar, telling him to know his place and play his role, implying he was controlled or used as a pawn throughout their feud. According to Drake, the ultimate goal of this manipulation was to dethrone him from the top spot in the music industry.\n\nRegarding this bar, Drake paints Kendrick as the label “dog,” who does as he is told. Given that Kendrick only started responding actively in the feud after securing a new deal with UMG, and considering Drake’s lawsuit against UMG alleging the label manipulated Kendrick’s releases to undermine him, this interpretation makes sense.\n\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/ccdff1188864e7fbe1e518ef5142e1bc3ab02dba13670fa4d42649b13ef6a101%2FxV96VLHXQ32oVx2hFbci_536226459_17920869078120981_7939428418253457050_n.webp\n A fictional portrayal of Lucian Grainge (CEO of UMG) holding a Kendrick Lamar puppet\n\nThe whole idea that Kendrick was being pushed or used from behind the scenes is widely believed to have inspired the Pinocchio character that Drake introduced during his Iceman album rollout series. Many fans interpret Pinocchio, a well-known symbol of lying and dishonesty, as representing Kendrick’s manipulated role in the feud.", "annotation_id": "37441161", "url": "https://genius.com/37441161", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Drop, drop, drop, drop", "vault_lines": ["Drop a fifty bag, 29 for the thot", "I get active when it's war time, I ain't even really let my dick drop, nigga", "What the fuck I heard Rick drop, nigga? Talkin' somethin' 'bout a nose job, nigga", "Drop, drop, drop, drop", "Even when you do drop, they gon' say you should've modeled 'cause it's mid again", "Drop a fifty bag for the mob in the spot"], "meaning": "This is Drake’s way of taunting Kendrick to drop his response. What makes it more interesting is that Drake actually hinted at this before, Particularly on the leaked version of “Push Ups,” where there’s a line there where Drake suggests Kendrick has been sitting on an unreleased diss track for four years:\n\nYou had a song for four years, drop that shit and shut your mouth", "annotation_id": "33182933", "url": "https://genius.com/33182933", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Uh, I was really, really tryna keep it—", "vault_lines": ["Uh, I was really, really tryna keep it PG", "I was really, really tryna keep it PG", "Uh, I was really, really tryna keep it—"], "meaning": "Drake’s ending to the chorus here differs from that used on “Push Ups” and the earlier in the track:\n\nUh, I was really, really tryna keep it PG\n\nDrake omits the “PG” here. PG is reference to Parental Guidance, a content rating given to a film that is suitable for anyone over the age of about 8 years, but also pgLang, a company specialized in music and visual media production founded by Kendrick Lamar and Dave Free in 2020.\n\nNotably, this is the last line of Part One of “Family Matters”. While Part One consisted of mainly shots at Kendrick, Part Two largely contains shots at other artists such as Rick Ross; The Weeknd; A$AP Rocky; Future; and Metro Boomin, with much fewer bars aimed at Kendrick compared to Part One and Part Three. Drake omitting “PG” for gun shots may be him saying he is not just trying to just aim at Kendrick anymore but is coming to destroy all of his rivals, as he does in Part Two.", "annotation_id": "31945342", "url": "https://genius.com/31945342", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Anthony set up the plays, Kojo be chargin’ you double for nothin’", "vault_lines": ["Anthony set up the plays, Kojo be chargin' you double for nothin'"], "meaning": "Drake is taking a shot at Kendrick Lamar’s business dealings and possibly the structure of his record label, Top Dawg Entertainment (TDE).\n\nThe line suggests that Anthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith, the founder of TDE, is orchestrating the strategic moves or decisions for Kendrick.\n\nInitially, it was thought that the “Kojo” mentioned referred to Duval “Kojo” Bankole Timothy, the songwriter and producer involved in Kendrick Lamar’s 2022 album Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers. It was believed that Drake was simply accusing him of charging Kendrick too much without providing real value. However, in light of the Drake’s recent UMG lawsuit, it appears a different individual named Kojo Asamoah is involved. Kojo Asamoah has been subpoenaed for allegedly using streaming bots and payola schemes to artificially inflate Kendrick Lamar’s streaming numbers during his beef with Drake. The implication from Drake’s is that despite these manipulative strategies, it will all be in vain.", "annotation_id": "31777688", "url": "https://genius.com/31777688", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Anything to take the spotlight off the fact the boss is a drugged-out lil’ punk sissy from the Northside", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Here he is continuing the shot at The Weeknd  after saying Abel, run your fuckin' bread, need to buy some more chains for some more guys Drake is saying The Weeknd will do anything to take attention of the fact he is a “ drugged-out lil' punk sissy from the Northside”", "annotation_id": "31634494", "url": "https://genius.com/31634494", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "[Verse]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake proceeds to flow in a very melodic singy-songy tone throughout the whole third verse, a verse that is specifically aimed at Lamar, The flow is pretty unorthodox to be utilized in a diss track. This could be fueled by Lamar earlier jabs on “Euphoria,”:\n\nI like Drake with the melodies, I don’t like Drake when he act tough", "annotation_id": "31726134", "url": "https://genius.com/31726134", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Oh shit, just follow me, right? ‘Cause nothin’ you sayin’ could bother me, right?", "vault_lines": ["Oh shit, just follow me, right? 'Cause nothin' you sayin' could bother me, right?"], "meaning": "Drake’s no stranger to hate, being one of the biggest artists out there. Over the years, he’s built up “thick skin” to handle all the slander and diss tracks that come his way. But it wasn’t always smooth—he’s had beefs with other rappers like Meek Mill, Pusha T, and Kanye West. By now, though, he’s heard it all.\n\nWhen it comes to Kendrick Lamar’s shots on tracks like  “Like That,” “Euphoria,” and “6:16 in LA.” Drake’s basically saying they don’t bother him. In fact, he flips the script and suggests Kendrick isn’t as credible as people think. Drake really digs into this on  “The Heart Part 6” with lines like:\n\n\nThe ones that you’re getting your stories from, they all clowns\n\n\nYou gotta learn to fact-check things and be less impatient\n\nJust let me know when we’re gettin' to the facts\nEverything in my shit is facts\nI’m waitin' on you to return the favor, like", "annotation_id": "31704080", "url": "https://genius.com/31704080", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "The picture you painted ain’t what it seem, you’re dead", "vault_lines": ["You're dead, you're dead", "The picture you painted ain't what it seem, you're dead"], "meaning": "Throughout the whole feud, Drake been painting Lamar as a “hypocrite” who doesn’t practice what he preaches. Drake wants the public to see  Kendrick not as the wise, virtuous, thorn crown wearing, messiah his fans hold him to be but as the rapper who hits women, cheats on his wife/fiance with white girls, and leaves the hood just like every other celebrity.  This angle is very palpable throughout the track:\n\n\nDon’t even go back to your hood and plant no money trees\n\nYou the Black messiah wifin' up a mixed queen\n\nThey hired a crisis management team to clean up the fact that you beat on your queen", "annotation_id": "31681692", "url": "https://genius.com/31681692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You’re dead You’re dead, you’re dead", "vault_lines": ["You're dead, you're dead", "The picture you painted ain't what it seem, you're dead"], "meaning": "Drake is basically doing a victory lap to conclude this ferocious diss-track, while pointing out to Kendrick that he can’t escape the barrage of disses he threw his way. Little did he anticipate, the back and forth will drag on further; Kendrick would release “meet the grahams” a response song, that dropped just an hour after the release of Drake’s “Family Matters.”\n\nThis could also be a playful mockery of Kendrick’s lines from “Rigamortus”:\n\nDon’t ask for your favorite rapper\n(He dead), yes, sir (Amen), church\n(He dead), I killed him (Amen), bitch", "annotation_id": "31681594", "url": "https://genius.com/31681594", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Murder scene on your man tonight, then come to the vigil with the candlelight", "vault_lines": ["Murder scene on your man tonight, then come to the vigil with the candlelight"], "meaning": "Over the head word play on “Man tonight” (Mennonites)- Mennonites are a religious group known for their commitment to peace, community, and simplicity, might participate in candlelight vigils as a form of peaceful protest, solidarity, or remembrance.", "annotation_id": "31679134", "url": "https://genius.com/31679134", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Bitches gotta drive two hours ’fore you pay ’em just to give a blowjob, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Bitches gotta drive two hours 'fore you pay 'em just to give a blowjob, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake doubles down on whomever he’s referring to in the previous bar (potentially Ross).\n\nDrake’s poking fun at how far out and distant this person’s home is that inviting women and even prostitutes is a journey in and of itself because it’s in the middle of nowhere.\n\nIf Drake refers to Ross, his Fayetteville mansion is around 30 – 45 minutes from Atlanta, depending on traffic.", "annotation_id": "31678452", "url": "https://genius.com/31678452", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Drop a fifty bag for the mob in the spot Drop a fifty bag, 29 for the thot", "vault_lines": ["Drop a fifty bag, 29 for the thot", "Drop, drop, drop, drop", "Drop a fifty bag for the mob in the spot"], "meaning": "“29” refers to Area 29, a Houston adult venue notorious for A-list celebrities, exclusivity, and, honorary Houstonian, Drake himself. The Canadian rapper has already made references to the club on “Mob Ties” and “Fair Trade“:\n\nI’m outside, 29, G5, Seaside\n\nIt is also a callback to his earlier diss track “Push Ups,” which features Drake calling for Kendrick to drop and give push ups. Drake is playing around with the definitions on the tracks, first drop and give push ups, second to drop actual music, and now just dropping money.\n\nNo stranger to the venue, Drake has been spotted posing with Area 29 dancers, possibly the “thot” reference. He reportedly attended the venue with Travis Scott the same night as the 2021 Astroworld stampede, with Drake dropping one million dollars over the course of the evening, leaving club dancers “counting dollar bills left on the floor, thanking the singer.”", "annotation_id": "31651179", "url": "https://genius.com/31651179", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Let me get a used Ferrari for a rapper, take the nigga on a horse ride", "vault_lines": ["Let me get a used Ferrari for a rapper, take the nigga on a horse ride"], "meaning": "The lyric uses the image of a “used Ferrari” and a “horse ride” to create a double entendre by playing on the Ferrari brand’s iconic prancing horse logo. Here, the “horse ride” isn’t a literal ride on a horse but a metaphorical ride in the Ferrari.", "annotation_id": "31651150", "url": "https://genius.com/31651150", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Realest shooter in your gang, that’s P’s brother, y’all ain’t getting shit shot, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Realest shooter in your gang, that's P's brother, y'all ain't getting shit shot, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake references P’s (Preme/P-Reign) brother who is a Gilla (Reps Up). Drake is implying no one from XO is a real shooter and the one shooter they have wouldn’t shoot due to his ties as an OVO-affiliate.\n\n\n\nThe Weeknd alludes to this on “Price on My Head”\n\nNiggas on your side, they would ride for me too\n\nAt the 1:34 mark in the accompanying music video, you see Gilla (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o_v2r8rdGM8&t=94s)", "annotation_id": "31649270", "url": "https://genius.com/31649270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "I’m goin’ on vacation now, hope next time y’all plan it right", "vault_lines": ["I'm goin' on vacation now, hope next time y'all plan it right"], "meaning": "He’s gonna take a break and next time those that he’s beefing with respond, they need to have a better strategy.\n\nHe had previously mentioned that he wasn’t gonna continue with this beef in the demo version of his diss track “Push Ups”:\n\nI ain’t even rappin' after this, I’m way too busy", "annotation_id": "31641207", "url": "https://genius.com/31641207", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Our sons should go play at the park, two lightskin kids, that shit would be cute Unless you don’t want to be seen with anyone that isn’t Blacker than you", "vault_lines": ["Our sons should go play at the park, two lightskin kids, that shit would be cute", "Unless you don't want to be seen with anyone that isn't Blacker than you"], "meaning": "Drake has received criticism from Kendrick for being mixed race, however here Drake is mentioning that both he and Kendrick’s son have the a lighter complexion, further alluding to Kendrick’s fiancé being biracial to point to the hypocrisy of him calling into question Drake’s blackness.\n\nFurther, this could also be a reference to the lyrics of Kendrick Lamar’s song “Blacker The Berry” which he later directly references in the next two lines.", "annotation_id": "31633880", "url": "https://genius.com/31633880", "state": "pending", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You don’t even be at home, dog, you a souvenir-out-the-gift-shop nigga", "vault_lines": ["You don't even be at home, dog, you a souvenir-out-the-gift-shop nigga"], "meaning": "Drake is criticizing The Weeknd for leaving their hometown of Toronto and relocating to Los Angeles, likening him to a souvenir that tourists often purchase when visiting cities. This comparison implies that The Weeknd has abandoned his roots and sold out to fit in with the glamorous LA lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "31633557", "url": "https://genius.com/31633557", "state": "pending", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "If Drake shooters doing TikToks, nigga", "vault_lines": ["If Drake shooters doing TikToks, nigga"], "meaning": "Reference to these lines off The Weeknd’s verse on track “All To Myself”:\n\nThey shooters makin’ TikToks\nGot us laughin’ in the Lambo (Yeah)\n\nAbel’s lines allude to Drake’s former security guard and OVO affiliate Baka Not Nice making TikToks, poking fun at his legitimacy as a shooter.\n\n\nDrake and Baka", "annotation_id": "31633547", "url": "https://genius.com/31633547", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "[Part II]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The music video for Part Two features black and white footage of a hearse, a metaphor for the Drake murdering the careers of Kendrick Lamar, The Weeknd, A$AP Rocky, Future, Metro Boomin, and Rick Ross via the shots he takes at these artists throughout Part Two and “Family Matters” as a whole.\n\nAdditionally, the hearse in the video is a Maybach, which is a direct shot at Rick Ross and his label Maybach Music Group.\n\n\n\nThe Maybach hearse featured in the “Family Matters” music video for Part Two", "annotation_id": "31639725", "url": "https://genius.com/31639725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "I ain’t even know you rapped still ‘cause they only talkin’ ’bout your ’fit again Probably gotta have a kid again ‘fore you think of droppin’ any shit again Even when you do drop, they gon’ say you should’ve modeled ’cause it’s mid again", "vault_lines": ["Drop, drop, drop, drop", "Rakim talkin' shit again", "I ain't even know you rapped still 'cause they only talkin' 'bout your 'fit again", "Probably gotta have a kid again 'fore you think of droppin' any shit again", "Even when you do drop, they gon' say you should've modeled 'cause it's mid again"], "meaning": "Drake criticizes A$AP Rocky for his sparse release of music (at this time, his most recent album was released in 2018) while joking that Rocky is more famous for his involvement in the fashion industry than he is for his rap career. He also takes a jab at Rocky’s artistry, asserting that his music won’t be well received by the fans when it is released.", "annotation_id": "31638550", "url": "https://genius.com/31638550", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Just like how Metro nigga slimed him for his main squeeze", "vault_lines": ["Just like how Metro nigga slimed him for his main squeeze"], "meaning": "Drake claims that one of Metro Boomin’s friends had sex with Metro’s girlfriend, Chelsea Cotton.\n\nMetro later refuted this claim on X:", "annotation_id": "31633511", "url": "https://genius.com/31633511", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "[Verse]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "For most of the verse, Drake deliberately uses the n-word at the end of every line in order to mock Kendrick Lamar after Kendrick pleaded for him to stop saying it in his 2024 track “euphoria,” where he rapped:\n\nI even hate when you say the word “nigga,” but that’s just me, I guess\nSome shit just cringeworthy, it ain’t even gotta be deep, I guess\n[…]\nWe don’t wanna hear you say “nigga” no more\nWe don’t wanna hear you say “nigga” no more\nStop", "annotation_id": "31637010", "url": "https://genius.com/31637010", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Uh, I was really, really tryna keep it PG I was really, really tryna keep it PG", "vault_lines": ["Uh, I was really, really tryna keep it PG", "I was really, really tryna keep it PG", "Uh, I was really, really tryna keep it—"], "meaning": "Drake uses the same lines he used at the end of “Push Ups”, executing a double entendre. Parental Guidance, or “PG,” is a content rating given to a film that is suitable for anyone over the age of around 8 years-old. Drake wanted to keep his image clean and didn’t want things to get nasty – but now it’s Rated R.\n\n“PG” is also a reference to pgLang, a company specialized in music and visual media production founded by Kendrick Lamar and Dave Free in 2020. This could mean that Drake was trying keep the disses focused at Kendrick, but ended up having to reply to other artists such as Future, The Weeknd, and Rick Ross due to the shots they’ve been taking at Drake.", "annotation_id": "31636519", "url": "https://genius.com/31636519", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Michael was prayin’ his features would change so people believe that he’s actually white", "vault_lines": ["Michael was prayin' his features would change so people believe that he's actually white"], "meaning": "The question of who is Michael Jackson and who is the Prince of Hip Hop has been a fierce debate throughout the back and forth between Kendrick and Drake.\n\nIn this bar, Drake runs on from the previous, continuing to rap about Michael Jackson, but also addressing another point of contention in this beef: skin color.\n\nIn Kendrick’s track “Euphoria” he raps:\n\nHow many more Black features ‘til you finally feel that you’re Black enough?\n\nThis bar and the following are a direct rebuttal from Drake. Michael Jackson had a skin condition named Vitiligo, in which patches of skin become bleached white. Due to the self-consciousness MJ would have likely felt, he wore fair-colored makeup and presumably used skin-bleaching medicine to hide his blotches.\n\nIn the next bar, Drake runs with the theme of white and claims that Kendrick appeals to a white fan base by featuring on songs with pop artists like Maroon 5, Taylor Swift, and Lonely Island, who all have large white fan-bases.\n\n\n\n A comparison of Michael Jackson’s look 20 years apart", "annotation_id": "31635576", "url": "https://genius.com/31635576", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "They hired a crisis management team to clean up the fact that you beat on your queen", "vault_lines": ["They hired a crisis management team to clean up the fact that you beat on your queen"], "meaning": "Drake claims that Kendrick Lamar physically abused his fiancée, Whitney Alford, and had to hire a crisis management team to prevent the story from spreading and damaging Kendrick’s reputation.\n\nWhile no concrete evidence supports this claim, accusations of Kendrick physically abusing women have surfaced before, prompting crisis management interventions. Media Take Out reported that on May 3rd, 2014, security at The Hard Rock Hotel in Las Vegas was contacted by a young white woman who was found beaten and bloodied. The woman identified Kendrick, who had performed at the hotel that day, as the assailant. Media Take Out claims they published a story about the incident, but were first contacted by Top Dawg Entertainment (Kendrick’s label at the time), requesting the story’s removal, and later by a crisis management team that successfully pressured them to comply. The crisis management firm allegedly informed Media Take Out that they had the video of the woman’s claims removed from YouTube and wiped from the internet. However, a video of the security guard, confirmed to have worked at the hotel, claiming Kendrick beat the woman can be seen below:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/N8FSxZv0vf0?si=Julzp8CQzRgL-kuo\n\nInterestingly, the alleged incident on May 3rd coincides with the day “Family Matters” was released. The other headliner at the show was DJ Whoo Kid, which suggests that the use of the “Whoo Kid” producer tag on “Push Ups” might have been an indirect hint from Drake about his knowledge of the incident and his intention to bring it to light.", "annotation_id": "31634942", "url": "https://genius.com/31634942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You Stunna and Wayne, like father, like son", "vault_lines": ["you Stunna and Wayne, like father, like son"], "meaning": "Referring to Birdman and Lil Wayne who have referred to each other as father and son publicly on multiple occasions. One of Birdman’s aliases is Stunna. Birdman & Lil Wayne also have a album titled Like Father, Like Son.\n\n\n\nCover art for Like Father, Like Son.\n\nThis also refers to Lil Wayne and Birdman’s feud in 2015, where it was reported that Birdman had been cheating Lil Wayne out of millions of dollars and Lil Wayne filed a $51 million lawsuit against Birdman and Cash Money Records. Drake similarly claimed that Anthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith, Kendrick’s former label boss, had been taking a large portion of Kendrick’s earnings on previous diss track “Push Ups”:\n\nExtortion baby, whole career, you been shook up\n’Cause Top told you, “Drop and give me fifty,” like some push-ups, huh\n\nDrake is saying that Anthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith is robbing Kendrick Lamar of his money just like he did to his father, a topic Kendrick discussed on “DUCKWORTH.”.", "annotation_id": "31634919", "url": "https://genius.com/31634919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "We get that you like to put gin in your juice", "vault_lines": ["We get that you like to put gin in your juice", "We get that you think that you Bishop in Juice"], "meaning": "Reference to west coast legend Snoop Dogg’s song “Gin & Juice”\n\nKendrick Lamar is from the west coast and Snoop Dogg is one of his major idols who even handed him the torch to carry on the west.\n\nAlso Drake had used Snoop Dogg’s voice in artificial intelligence form in his diss track “Taylor Made Freestyle”", "annotation_id": "31634897", "url": "https://genius.com/31634897", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "I get active when it’s war time, I ain’t even really let my dick drop, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I get active when it's war time, I ain't even really let my dick drop, nigga", "Drop, drop, drop, drop"], "meaning": "Drake is asserting his readiness and willingness to engage in conflict or warfare. He’s suggesting that he doesn’t shy away from confrontation and is prepared to take action when necessary. The second part of the lyric is a metaphorical way of saying that he hasn’t even fully unleashed his arsenal of disses or attacks yet.", "annotation_id": "31634873", "url": "https://genius.com/31634873", "state": "pending", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Tears runnin’ down my cheek, laughin’ at you pussies dyin’, it’s a war cry", "vault_lines": ["Tears runnin' down my cheek, laughin' at you pussies dyin', it's a war cry"], "meaning": "Drake describes tears running down his cheek, but instead of showing sadness or remorse, he’s laughing at the demise of his rivals.\n\n\n\nThis also alludes to how gang members get tear drops tattooed on their faces representing a person they have killed. This is Drake claiming he’s killed the careers of the plethora of artists he’s sent shots at on this track and throughout the feud.\n\n\nLil Wayne’s teardrop tattoos.", "annotation_id": "31634840", "url": "https://genius.com/31634840", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "On some Bobby shit, I wanna know what Whitney need", "vault_lines": ["On some Bobby shit, I wanna know what Whitney need"], "meaning": "Drake is using Bobby Brown and Whitney Houston as a metaphor and wordplay to allude to an alleged rift between Kendrick and the mother of his children Whitney.", "annotation_id": "31634823", "url": "https://genius.com/31634823", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "House sittin’ on some land, but it’s out where no one even really know y’all niggas", "vault_lines": ["House sittin' on some land, but it's out where no one even really know y'all niggas"], "meaning": "It is a bit unclear here, but Drake might be referring to Rick Ross, as he’s recently been building new houses in far-out locations.\nRick Ross' mansion is in Fayetteville, GA (30 minutes from Atlanta). Drake is saying it’s pretty much in the middle of nowhere even though it’s huge (the largest house in Georgia).\n\nThis could also be a reference to Kendrick saying this on euphoria\n\nYeah, I’m out the way, yeah, I’m low, okay\nYeah, the island right here’s remote, okay\n\nDrake is implying no one knows who they are in those places.", "annotation_id": "31634751", "url": "https://genius.com/31634751", "state": "pending", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "This shit gotta be over by now for anyone out here that’s calling it, right?", "vault_lines": ["This shit gotta be over by now for anyone out here that's calling it, right?"], "meaning": "Drake thinks that people watching this battle would call it as a win for Drake at this point.\n\nOn his track Red Button, Drake teased the idea that pushing the button, AKA revealing his cards, everything would change forever.\n\nIf I press this red button, dog, everybody Heaven Gated\nPress this red button, dog, and everything forever changes\n\nDrake adds the “right” both as a rhetorical question, and a qualifier for an unbiased referee of the rap to call it here, aka somebody that is calling it “right”.\n\nThis lyric is also a callback to Champagne Poetry on Drake’s September 2021 album Certified Lover Boy when he says\n\nThe city’s on fire and people are in denial […] I’m calling this shit from now", "annotation_id": "31634744", "url": "https://genius.com/31634744", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "‘Cause all these rappers wavin’ white flags while the whole fuckin’ club sing to it", "vault_lines": ["'Cause all these rappers wavin' white flags while the whole fuckin' club sing to it"], "meaning": "Drake is likely addressing the perception of his race within the hip-hop industry. The line suggests that other rappers are surrendering or giving up, metaphorically represented by waving white flags, while Drake continues to succeed and maintain his dominance in the industry. The mention of “white flags” could also be a play on words, alluding to the color white and possibly implying that some people perceive Drake as being “white” due to his mixed-race background, despite his success in a predominantly Black genre.", "annotation_id": "31634717", "url": "https://genius.com/31634717", "state": "pending", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You know who really bang a set? My nigga YG", "vault_lines": ["You know who really bang a set? My nigga YG", "You know who really bang a set? My nigga Chuck T", "You know who even bang a set out there is CB"], "meaning": "YG is a rapper from Compton, the same place as Kendrick Lamar. “Bang a set” means to represent for your gang. While YG is a member of the Compton-based Westside Tree Top Piru Blood gang, Kendrick never affiliated with the Bloods or Crips. Drizzy is essentially mocking Kendrick’s lack of respect and street credibility in Compton.\n\nDrake and YG were friends and worked together on the songs “Who Do You Love?” and “Why You Always Hatin?”. However, many interpreted these lines on YG’s track “WEIRD” as a negative response to this shoutout:\n\nWhen it’s smoke, don’t ever mention my name (Mention my name)\nYou gon’ have me jeapoardizin’ my fame\nJeapoardizin’ the gang, jeapoardizin’ where you hang, nigga\n\nHowever, YG denies this is a Drake diss.\n\nIn June 2024, YG performed at Kendrick’s The Pop Out: Ken & Friends concert, in which Lamar played his Drake diss tracks for the first time. This was widely seen as YG siding with Kendrick over Drake in the feud, despite Drake shouting out YG here.\n\nYG (left) & Kendrick (right)", "annotation_id": "31634699", "url": "https://genius.com/31634699", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "I’m losin’ perspective on beef, Boi-1da send beat and I’ll kill you for fun", "vault_lines": ["I'm losin' perspective on beef, Boi-1da send beat and I'll kill you for fun"], "meaning": "Drake suggests that he’s losing sight of all the threads and timelines of this multi-person beef, and at this point he’s responding just for fun.\n\nBoi-1da is a producer and long time collaborator of Drake. As Drake alludes to in the line, he produced “Push Ups”, “Family Matters”, and presumably “Taylor Made Freestyle”.\n\nBoi-1da will send a beat, and Drake will kill rappers on it just for the fun of it at this point.\n\nAdditionally, Drake subliminally continues his narrative of a 20-v-1, by saying he’s losing perspective of things, as in, in his line of sight or scope, he’s not able to keep track of everything going on.\n\nWhat the fuck is this, a twenty-v-one, nigga?\n\nInitially on “push ups” Drake mentioned that Kendrick is “in a scope” right now. The culmination of all these rappers piling on would make it hard for drake to have “perspective” of the entirety of his beef with his scope set on a few key rappers.\n\nLike your label, boy, you in a scope right now", "annotation_id": "31634653", "url": "https://genius.com/31634653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "‘Cause you gotta pay for sayin’ my name, guess now niggas understand the price", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you gotta pay for sayin' my name, guess now niggas understand the price"], "meaning": "After Drake dropped For All The Dogs, he was allegedly supposed to take a break from rap.\n\nAfter dropping For All The Dogs Scary Hours Edition, he mentioned on “Stories About My Brother”, that he walked back in the studio, unable to leave the rap game alone.\n\nI walked in the studio, Noel said, “I thought you quit”\nI said “Nah, I’m quittin' again ‘cause I started again”\nGotta wait on that\nThis like the storm before the calm, we’ll get to the vacation later\n\nThis time, Drake expects to go on vacation without anyone name dropping him since they know the true price after the back and forth beef that has occurred in the last 8 weeks. Drake has responded to every notable rapper that has called on his name, and suggests he will continue to do so from this point on.", "annotation_id": "31634646", "url": "https://genius.com/31634646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "West Coast niggas do fades, right? Come get this ass whoopin’, I’m handin’ ’em out", "vault_lines": ["West Coast niggas do fades, right? Come get this ass whoopin', I'm handin' 'em out"], "meaning": "A fade is a haircut that is common amongst men from the West Coast. Fade is also another word for a fight.\n\nDrake is implying since Kendrick has sported fade hairstyles, he’s ready to fight him and catch an “ass whoopin'.”\n\nThis is Drake’s response to the line on “euphoria” where Kendrick says he’s giving out beatings:\n\n[…] I’m Terence Crawford, yeah, I’m whoopin' feet\n\n“Whooping feet” is West Coast slang, and in saying this Drake is implying they could go toe-to-toe with the beatings/beef.", "annotation_id": "31634642", "url": "https://genius.com/31634642", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Put a nigga in the bars, let a nigga rot, kind of like your old job, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Put a nigga in the bars, let a nigga rot, kind of like your old job, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake is now dissing Rick Ross, a.k.a. putting him in his “bars” and letting him rot.\nRick Ross was a correctional officer before becoming a rapper, so he used to “put niggas in bars” (jail cells) and let them rot.", "annotation_id": "31634641", "url": "https://genius.com/31634641", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You tell me what I shouldn’t say, but fuck it, my nigga, it’s already done", "vault_lines": ["You tell me what I shouldn't say, but fuck it, my nigga, it's already done"], "meaning": "On Kendrick Lamar’s diss track “euphoria”, he tells Drake to not use the N-word:\n\nWe don’t wanna hear you say “nigga” no more\n\nDrake defies Kendrick’s orders by using it in this line and throughout the song, also intentionally making it the first word he says on this track.\n\nAdditionally, Kendrick warned Drake to not escalate their beef on “euphoria”:\n\nIf you take it there, I’m takin’ it further\nPsst, that’s something you don’t wanna do\n\nHowever, on “Push-Ups” Drake name-drops Kendrick’s fiancée Whitney, which was released prior to “euphoria”. Drake may be saying he has already taken it far by involving family, and there is no going back now.", "annotation_id": "31634639", "url": "https://genius.com/31634639", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "These niggas had a plan and they finally found a way to rope you into it", "vault_lines": ["These niggas had a plan and they finally found a way to rope you into it"], "meaning": "Reference to Metro Boomin and Future’s alleged plan to takedown Drake on albums WE DON’T TRUST YOU and WE STILL DON’T TRUST YOU.\n\nOn WE DON’T TRUST YOU, it is highly suspected that Future took shots at Drake on title track “We Don’t Trust You”; and recruited Kendrick Lamar for track “Like That” where K Dot took direct shots at Drake.\n\nJust 3 weeks later on WE STILL DON’T TRUST YOU, Metro and Future recruited The Weeknd for track “All to Myself”, where he also takes shots at Drake. Finally, A$AP Rocky also featured on the track “Show of Hands” where he also throws shades at Drake.", "annotation_id": "31634625", "url": "https://genius.com/31634625", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Ask Fring if this a good idea the next time you cuddled in that bed again She’ll even tell you leave the boy alone ’fore you get your head split again", "vault_lines": ["Ask Fring if this a good idea the next time you cuddled in that bed again", "She'll even tell you leave the boy alone 'fore you get your head split again"], "meaning": "Drake is telling A$AP Rocky to confer with his girl Rihanna before dissing Drake again, claiming even his own girlfriend will not support him in doing so, as she knows full well that Drake will destroy him again.\n\n“Fring” was Drake’s nickname for Rihanna while they were dating. The nickname supposedly originates from legendary Breaking Bad antagonist Gustavo Fring, who was Rihanna’s favourite character in the critically-acclaimed television series. Aaron Paul, who plays Jesse Pinkman in Breaking Bad, stated Rihanna and Drake were both ecstatic when meeting him, emphasising how much they love the show. Aaron Paul also said that Rihanna put Drake onto Breaking Bad, supporting the theory that this is where the nickname originated from. Drake also named a restaurant in Toronto “Frings”.\n\n\n Pictured: Aaron Paul photographed separately with Drake and Rihanna\n\nDrake saying Rocky will get his “head split again” may be reference to him sustaining a head injury while crashing a razor scooter in 2019. To hide the injury, Rocky was often famously seen wearing a babushka.\n\n\n Pictured: Rocky wearing a babushka.", "annotation_id": "31634611", "url": "https://genius.com/31634611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 148, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "I get off the plane and nothing has changed, I head to Delilah with all of my ice", "vault_lines": ["I get off the plane and nothing has changed, I head to Delilah with all of my ice", "Head to Delilah with all of my ice, head to Delilah with all of my ice"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake is emphasizing that he faces no problems when in Los Angeles or more specifically in Compton. This is a direct response to Kendrick’s line in euphoria which states,\n\nExtortion my middle name as soon as you jump off of that plane, bitch\n\nThis is in conjunction with this line from his original diss on “Like That” which reads,\n\nGot 2TEEZ with me, I’m snatchin' chains and burnin' tattoos, it’s up\n\nDrake is asserting that he is safe with his jewelery even in Kendrick’s hometown.", "annotation_id": "31634581", "url": "https://genius.com/31634581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Smokin’ Fenty ’bout it, should’ve put you on the first one, tryna get it in", "vault_lines": ["Smokin' Fenty 'bout it, should've put you on the first one, tryna get it in"], "meaning": "Fenty Beauty in relation to ASAP ROCKY’S wife Rihanna, but Fenty is also the street name for the drug Fentanyl.\n\nDrake is refering to ASAP ROCKY saying “Call up Pluto, Metro, should’ve put me on the first one” on the song “Show of hands”. ROCKY is unhappy because Metro and Future didn’t include him on their first release “we don’t trust you”. He was only able to take shots at Drake on their second release “we still don’t trust you” which had less impact.", "annotation_id": "31634566", "url": "https://genius.com/31634566", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Head to Delilah with all of my ice, head to Delilah with all of my ice", "vault_lines": ["I get off the plane and nothing has changed, I head to Delilah with all of my ice", "Head to Delilah with all of my ice, head to Delilah with all of my ice"], "meaning": "Flipping Kendrick’s mention of being at New Ho King with a blammy, Drake is saying that he heads to Delilah in LA with all of his ice, not fearing that he will get robbed.\n\nDelilah is an upscale restaurant and nightclub in LA that Drake visits. We know that because in “Champagne Poetry” from the album Certified Lover Boy, he mentioned the place.\n\n“I used to hide my pain in Delilah behind the bar ’til my niggas carried me out”", "annotation_id": "31634485", "url": "https://genius.com/31634485", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "[Part I]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Family Matters” was first teased during Drake’s “Push Ups”, his first response to the various shots taken by artists like Kendrick Lamar among others. The outro of that track featured a beat switch to the same one heard on part I, as well as the following chorus:\n\nMmm, mmm, yeah\nDrop, drop, drop, drop\nDrop a fifty bag for the mob in the spot\nDrop a fifty bag, twenty-nine for the thot\nUh, I was really, really tryna keep it PG\n\nIn the music video for “Part One”, Drake symbolically destroys Kendrick’s legacy by crushing the van featured on the cover of Kendrick’s 2012 breakthrough album, good kid, m.A.A.d city:\n\nThe good kid, m.A.A.d city van in the “Family Matters” music video", "annotation_id": "31634427", "url": "https://genius.com/31634427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Rakim talkin’ shit again Gassed ‘cause you hit my BM first, nigga, do the math, who I was hittin’ then?", "vault_lines": ["Rakim talkin' shit again", "Gassed 'cause you hit my BM first, nigga, do the math, who I was hittin' then?"], "meaning": "Drake responds to A$AP Rocky (whose real name is Rakim Athelaston Mayers) after Rocky dissed Drake on the Future collaboration, “Show of Hands.”\n\nNiggas in they feelings over women, what, you hurt or somethin’?\nI smash before you birthed, son, Flacko hit it first, son\n\nDrake acknowledges that what Rocky said about him having sex with Sophie Brussaux, Drake’s baby mother or “BM,” before Drake met her is true, yet Drake says at that time he was hitting someone else, presumably Rihanna, who is now Rocky’s current baby momma.", "annotation_id": "31634394", "url": "https://genius.com/31634394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 280, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You the Black messiah wifin’ up a mixed queen", "vault_lines": ["You the Black messiah wifin' up a mixed queen"], "meaning": "Drake is suggesting Kendrick’s not living in line with how he presents himself. Kendrick strongly identifies with his race through his music, and he’s been mocking Drake for “not being black,” but his own wife is mixed, just like Drake.", "annotation_id": "31634374", "url": "https://genius.com/31634374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "We get that you think that you Bishop in Juice", "vault_lines": ["We get that you like to put gin in your juice", "We get that you think that you Bishop in Juice"], "meaning": "“Bishop” is a fictional character from the movie Juice, played by Tupac Shakur. Drake is calling out Kendrick for trying to emulate the fearsome persona of Bishop, seeking power and respect in a manner that Drake finds excessive or unfounded. Kendrick has been very vocal about the profound influence Tupac has had on both his music and personal philosophy, even featuring a fictional interview with him on track “Mortal Man”, the Outro to his 2015 album To Pimp a Butterfly. Additionally, K Dot was quoted in an interview with Variety saying:\n\n“Tupac inspired me just the simple way of how he put his words together, You really felt them, you felt the passion behind them. They meant something, not only to him, but when you heard it, you actually got a full experience rather than just words. You actually felt his words. That’s something that I was inspired to put in my music.”\n\nBy Drake saying Kendrick thinks he’s like Pac, he may be implying this is merely a delusion, with Drizzy believing Kendrick is not up to par with his idol and should stop trying to emulate his style.\n\n\n\nTupac and Kendrick\n\nAdditionally, Bishop murdered his friend Raheem in Juice, and attempted to kill his other friend Q. This may be Drake mocking Kendrick for these bars on “euphoria”:\n\nI pray they my real friends, if not, I’m YNW Melly\n\nKendrick likened himself to YNW Melly in those bars, who was charged with the double murder of two fellow rappers closely affiliated with his music collective, Young Nigga World. Again, this may be Drake clowning Kendrick for thinking he can murder him like Juice and Melly did to their friends, claiming Dot’s all bark no bite.\n\nBishop in Juice\n\n\n\n\n\nThis line gains additional significance in the context of Drake using an AI-generated Tupac voice on his diss-track “Taylor Made Freestyle”, just to antagonize Kendrick.", "annotation_id": "31634340", "url": "https://genius.com/31634340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Say you hate the girls I fuck, but what you really mean? I been with Black and white and everything that’s in between", "vault_lines": ["Say you hate the girls I fuck, but what you really mean?", "I been with Black and white and everything that's in between"], "meaning": "This is a direct response to Kendrick Lamar’s critique in “Euphoria”:\n\n We hate the bitches you fuck ‘cause they confuse themself with real women\n\nDrake counters by flaunting his diverse romantic history, which encompasses women of all races and backgrounds. In doing so, he dismisses Kendrick’s moralizing tone and hypocrisy, a point he further addresses in the following bar.", "annotation_id": "31634320", "url": "https://genius.com/31634320", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "What the fuck I heard Rick drop, nigga? Talkin’ somethin’ ’bout a nose job, nigga", "vault_lines": ["What the fuck I heard Rick drop, nigga? Talkin' somethin' 'bout a nose job, nigga", "Drop, drop, drop, drop"], "meaning": "Drake is mocking Rick Ross’s diss track “Champagne Moments” and implying it wasn’t listenable or worth it’s own response diss track.\n\nDrake is also reiterating that the nose job accusations are false and he didn’t get plastic surgery.", "annotation_id": "31634299", "url": "https://genius.com/31634299", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Pluto shit make me sick to my stomach, we ain’t never really been through it", "vault_lines": ["Pluto shit make me sick to my stomach, we ain't never really been through it"], "meaning": "Drake and Future (Pluto) were very close before Future’s collaboration album with Metro Boomin dropped. They made many songs together, including a collaboration album (What A Time To Be Alive) and Future’s first and second number one (“Way 2 Sexy” and “WAIT FOR U” respectively). Drake feels sick because his close friend has switched up on him.\n\n\n\nDrake and Future together", "annotation_id": "31634260", "url": "https://genius.com/31634260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 172, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "I heard that one of ’em little kids might be Dave Free Don’t make it Dave Free’s", "vault_lines": ["I heard that one of 'em little kids might be Dave Free", "Don't make it Dave Free's"], "meaning": "Drake claims that one of Kendrick Lamar’s two children’s biological father may actually be Dave Free. Dave Free, a childhood friend and right-hand man of Kendrick, also cofounded creative company and label pgLang with him. Drake implies that Dave slept with and impregnated Kendrick’s fiancé Whitney.\n\nKendrick Lamar and Dave Free", "annotation_id": "31634256", "url": "https://genius.com/31634256", "state": "accepted", "votes": 111, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "If you had a set, they’d give your ass a DP But you civilian gang, in real life, you PC", "vault_lines": ["If you had a set, they'd give your ass a DP", "But you civilian gang, in real life, you PC"], "meaning": "“Set” refers to being gang-affiliated. “DP” stands for disciplinary punishment, a form of punishment within gangs for members who break the rules. Despite growing up in the gang-infested Compton, Kendrick Lamar never officially affiliated with the Bloods or Crips, but associates with the Westside Pirus by virtue of his friends. During a January 2011 interview with HipHopDX, Kendrick spoke about his affiliation with gangs:\n\nMy whole family is Crips and Pirus […] I always had that right there, but out of respect for my pops, I never jumped into the gang life that was around me.\n\nDrake implies that if Kendrick were in a gang, they would discipline him for his perceived weak and indecisive behavior. Essentially, Drizzy is questioning Kendrick’s street credibility in the city he so proudly represents.\n\nDrake might also be dissing Rick Ross, whose past as a correctional officer contradicts the codes of organized crime. This insinuates that if Rozay were truly involved in gang life as he claims, his gang would have punished him for breaking the rules.\n\nIn reality, Drake suggests that both Kendrick and Ross are merely civilians who would need protective custody (PC) if exposed to real danger, highlighting a lack of true street authenticity.\n\nKendrick repping Piru’s in his adolescence", "annotation_id": "31634245", "url": "https://genius.com/31634245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You know who even bang a set out there is CB", "vault_lines": ["You know who really bang a set? My nigga YG", "You know who even bang a set out there is CB"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Chris Brown being involved in or associated with gang activity, specifically with the Bloods gang.\n\nIn the music video for “No Guidance,” Chris' choice of attire, specifically wearing a red bandana-printed shirt and a red bandana on his head, likely serves as a visual nod to his affiliation with the Bloods.", "annotation_id": "31634226", "url": "https://genius.com/31634226", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Your back is up against the curb, you diggin’ for dirt, should be diggin’ for proof", "vault_lines": ["Your back is up against the curb, you diggin' for dirt, should be diggin' for proof"], "meaning": "In Kendrick’s first diss “Euphoria” he raps about how he has some inside knowledge on Drake.\n\nI know some shit about niggas that make Gunna Wunna look like a saint\n\nHowever Drake believes that Kendrick is bluffing, and Kendrick needs to show proof.\n\nAdditionally, Kendrick having his back to the curb is a play on one having their back to the wall. Kendrick is really short (1.65 Metres).", "annotation_id": "31634194", "url": "https://genius.com/31634194", "state": "pending", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Where is your uncle at? ’Cause I wanna talk to the man of the house", "vault_lines": ["Where is your uncle at? 'Cause I wanna talk to the man of the house"], "meaning": "This bar could be a reference to Kendrick’s track “Auntie Diaries”, off his most recent album Mr Morale and the Big Steppers. In this song Kendrick tells the story of how his aunt transitioned into becoming his uncle. Drake is mocking Kendrick, saying that his transgender uncle is more masculine than Kendrick.", "annotation_id": "31634064", "url": "https://genius.com/31634064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 336, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "And wait, you say your brother Jermaine, but you wanted him to stay out of the light", "vault_lines": ["And wait, you say your brother Jermaine, but you wanted him to stay out of the light"], "meaning": "“Jermaine” is J. Cole’s first name, who was originally part of the ongoing feud, and released “7 Minute Drill”, a response track to Kendrick’s antagonization on “Like that”:\n\nMotherfuck the big three, nigga, it’s just big me\n\nJ. Cole would infamously exit the beef and even ended up apologizing to Lamar on stage.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/DQUTk2i-5OQ\n\nDrake could be stating that Kendrick tried to get J. Cole to stay out of the beef behind the scenes, as in good faith – treating him like a brother. However it is more likely that he is insinuating that Kendrick could have been doing this out of bad faith to keep J. Cole in the shadows out of selfishness. This feud was a testament to the public’s colossal appetite for rivalries and the exposure it guaranteed to all participants, which in hindsight Cole was robbed of.\n\nIt is worth noting that there has been some reports that alleges that ScHoolboy Q, a close associate of Kendrick, had a conversation with J. Cole at the Dreamville festival and advised him to exit the beef.\n\nKeeping up with theme of the mutual references of Michael Jackson and Prince throughout the lyrical battle, Michael Jackson had a brother named Jermaine. Similar to Michael, Jermaine was a singer and a member of the Jackson 5. In 1991, Jermaine Jackson released a song called “Word to the Badd!!”, The song accused Michael of changing his appearance, abandoning his family, and forgetting his roots. This was probably in response to Jermaine’s feeling black-balled in the music industry by his younger brother.\n\n\n\nThis might also be hinting at Kendrick’s colorism since J. Cole is light-skinned and bi-racial, much like Drake. This ties in with Drake’s lines earlier in the verse where he pointed out Lamar disingenuous/fake stance as a representative of the culture/genre:\n\nOur sons should go play at the park, two lightskin kids, that shit would be cute\nUnless you don’t want to be seen with anyone that isn’t Blacker than you\nWe get it, we got it\nThe blacker the berry, the sweeter the juice\nWe get that you like to put gin in your juice\nWe get that you think that you Bishop in Juice\n\nThe tension between Kendrick and J. Cole goes back a long way and was marked by subtle jabs and competitive verses. For example, Kendrick’s verse on Big Sean’s track “Control” in 2013, where he called out various rappers including J. Cole, A verse that contributed to the competitive nature of their relationship. During one particular XXL photoshoot, J. Cole was running late and Kendrick asked the magazine company if he could replace him in the freshman class, Cole ended up keeping his spot. There were even talks of a collaboration album between the two that was never actualized. It’s possible Kendrick wasn’t exactly vying for J. Cole to outshine him, or help him through Kendrick’s popularizing shine coming up.", "annotation_id": "31634048", "url": "https://genius.com/31634048", "state": "accepted", "votes": 146, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "I’ve emptied the clip over friendlier jabs You mentioned my seed, now deal with his dad I gotta go bad, I gotta go bad", "vault_lines": ["I've emptied the clip over friendlier jabs", "You mentioned my seed, now deal with his dad", "I gotta go bad, I gotta go bad"], "meaning": "Throughout this beef, Kendrick and Drake took jabs at each other by comparing themselves to pop icons Prince and Michael Jackson. Moreover, Drake constantly compared himself to Michael Jackson throughout his career, most notably on his 2023 single “First Person Shooter:”\n\nNiggas talkin' ‘bout when this gon’ be repeated\nWhat the fuck, bro? I’m one away from Michael\nNigga, beat it, nigga, beat it, what?\n\nIn response, Kendrick continuously uses Michael Jackson throughout his disses to refer to Drake, seen and “6:16 in LA,” and “Like That.”\n\nMichael Jackson has a song titled “Bad,” detailing his transformation into an edgy, more dangerous persona.\n\n\n\nSimilar to Michael on that song, Drake decides to go bad on this song in response to Kendrick mentioning his son, Adonis, on “euphoria,”:\n\nThat’s ho shit, I got a son to raise, but I can see you don’t know nothin' ‘bout that\n\nDrake is also referencing he already had beefs where he went all out, over more trivial reasons; like when the Meek Mill beef was instigated when Meek took to twitter and accused Drake of “not writing his own raps”, but involving his kid is where Drake drew the line.", "annotation_id": "31634047", "url": "https://genius.com/31634047", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You wanna take up for Pharrell? Then come get his legacy out of my house", "vault_lines": ["You wanna take up for Pharrell? Then come get his legacy out of my house"], "meaning": "Reference to these lines off “euphoria”, where Kendrick criticises Drake for taking shots at Pharrell:\n\nI don’t like you poppin’ shit at Pharrell, for him, I inherit the beef\n\nDrake refers to the jewellery he flexed in the official music video for “Jumbotron Shit Poppin,” which was noticed by fans to be designed and previously owned by Pharrell.\n\n\nDrake boasting $3 million worth of jewellery previously owned by Pharrell Williams in the “Jumbotron Shit Poppin” music video\n\n\nDrake also featured Pharrell’s jewellery collection in the “Family Matters” music video in congruence with these lyrics, with Drake’s ex-accountant and now personal assistant Juju flexing the collection in the “New Ho King” restaurant, which Kendrick previously mentioned on “euphoria”.\n\n\nDrake’s personal assistant Juju flexing Pharrell’s jewellery collection in the “Family Matters” music video\n\n\nDrake refers to the jewellery collection as Pharrell’s legacy, and if Kendrick truly wants to inherit Drake’s beef with Pharrell, he challenges Kendrick to pull up to Drake’s house and reclaim Pharrell’s collection.", "annotation_id": "31634036", "url": "https://genius.com/31634036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Kendrick just opened his mouth, someone go hand him a Grammy right now", "vault_lines": ["Kendrick just opened his mouth, someone go hand him a Grammy right now"], "meaning": "Drake references how Kendrick Lamar is accepted by mainstream critics and media. Kendrick is somewhat infamous within Hip Hop circles as he has received 17 Grammys, the third most for any rapper, whereas Drake has only won five. Drake and others believe that Kendrick doesn’t deserve these awards, and the people in charge of the Grammys hand out the awards anytime Kendrick releases an album or song.\n\n\n\nThroughout almost the entirety of his career, Drake has been very vocal about how he believes Grammy’s don’t matter. Drizzy even put them on blast during his acceptance speech when he won Best Rap Song for “God’s Plan” at the 2019 Grammys, and was famously cut off by the academy mid-speech:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=giWhfDn8ltk\n\nDrake reiterated this sentiment on his Instagram story in 2024, stating the Grammy’s “doesn’t dictate shit in our world”:", "annotation_id": "31634022", "url": "https://genius.com/31634022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 253, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "We already know it’s a twenty-v-one, we already know why you went number one It’s clearly because of The Boy, the honorable thing is to give me the loot", "vault_lines": ["We already know it's a twenty-v-one, we already know why you went number one", "It's clearly because of The Boy, the honorable thing is to give me the loot"], "meaning": "Kendrick Lamar initiated the beef with Drake on track “Like That”, taking direct shots at Drake:\n\nMotherfuck the big three, nigga, it’s just big me\nNigga, bum\nWhat? I’m really like that\nAnd your best work is a light pack\nNigga, Prince outlived Mike Jack'\nNigga, bum\n‘Fore all your dogs gettin’ buried\nThat’s a K with all these nines, he gon' see Pet Sematary (Yeah)\n\n“Like That” debuted at number-one on the Billboard Hot 100. Drake claims the track only went number-one because it was dissing him, suggesting his prominent name attracts engagement. Drake asks for the ‘loot’ from using his name to increase sales for the track.\n\n“Like That” led to the “20 v 1”, where others got inspired to voice their aggravation at Drake, such as Rick Ross; A$AP Rocky and others.", "annotation_id": "31634020", "url": "https://genius.com/31634020", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "A cease and desist is for hoes, can’t listen to lies that come out of your mouth You called the Tupac estate and begged ’em to sue me and get that shit down", "vault_lines": ["A cease and desist is for hoes, can't listen to lies that come out of your mouth", "You called the Tupac estate and begged 'em to sue me and get that shit down"], "meaning": "This is in direct response to Kendrick Lamar’s track “euphoria” released four days prior, where Lamar claims:\n\nTry cease and desist on the “Like That” record?\nHo, what? You ain’t like that record?\n\nKendrick Lamar claims that Drake sent a cease and desist letter—a warning letter threatening legal action if said thing isn’t removed—to take down Future & Metro Boomin’s viral track, “Like That”, which Kendrick Lamar appeared as a hidden feature, dissing and sending strong shots at Drake.\n\nDrake denies this claim and paints Kendrick Lamar as a hypocritical liar, accusing him of having Tupac’s Estate file a cease and desist for Drake’s diss track, “Taylor Made Freestyle”, which features Artifical Intelligence-assisted vocals that replicated the voices of the legendary 2Pac and Snoop Dogg; which in the end, was taken down as it does  infringe on Tupac and his estate.", "annotation_id": "31634017", "url": "https://genius.com/31634017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Your daddy got robbed by Top,", "vault_lines": ["Your daddy got robbed by Top,"], "meaning": "Drake interestingly brings up the story of Kendrick Lamar’s father, Kenny Duckworth, and Anthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith, when his father was working at a Kentucky Fried Chicken and Tiffith was planning to rob the location while Kendrick was a lot younger.\n\nKendrick had already brought up this information on the outro to his 2017 album, “DAMN.”, with the song, respectively titled “DUCKWORTH.”\n\n\nCrooked cops told Anthony he should kick it\nHe brushed ‘em off and walked back to the Kentucky Fried Chicken\n\nSee, at this chicken spot, there was a light-skinned nigga that talked a lot\n\n‘Cross the street from the projects, Anthony planned to rob it\n\nWith a curly top and a gap in his teeth, he worked the window\nHis name was Ducky\n\n\nDUCKWORTH. played as an intro shows what Kendrick predicts his life without his father would have been, although in DUCKWORTH, a coincidence happens where Kendrick’s father gave Anthony extra food and befriended Anthony, which in return stopped him from robbing the KFC he worked at.\n\nThe idea of “DAMN.” is that you can execute two parallel stories, by moving the album in reverse, and in DUCKWORTH, decides whether what happens, and the reality of DUCKWORTH comes from having it as the outro.\n\nDrake contradicts this, stating that the opposite happened, where the robbery really did happen instead of him resolving to a peaceful solution as Kendrick said on DUCKWORTH.\n\nDrake also compares the real robbery of his father to Kendrick’s contracts with Top Dawg Entertainment, as he rapped about earlier in “Push Ups.”\n\nPull your contract ‘cause we gotta see the split\nThe way you doin’ splits, bitch, your pants might rip\nYou better do that motherfuckin' show inside the bity\nMaroon 5 need a verse, you better make it witty\nThen we need a verse for the Swifties\nTop say drop, you better drop and give ‘em fifty", "annotation_id": "31634005", "url": "https://genius.com/31634005", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Nigga said, “Uh, uh” Almost started reachin’ for my waist", "vault_lines": ["Almost started reachin' for my waist"], "meaning": "Drake pulls out some beautiful vocals and quickly sings out an ad lib, while mocking famous artist The Weeknd.", "annotation_id": "31634000", "url": "https://genius.com/31634000", "state": "pending", "votes": 161, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Oh shit, it’s all makin’ sense, maybe I’m Prince and you actually Mike", "vault_lines": ["Oh shit, it's all makin' sense, maybe I'm Prince and you actually Mike"], "meaning": "This is a response to the diss made on ‘Like That’, where Kendrick referred to himself as Prince, saying he outlived Mike (Michael Jackson). This essentially was Kendrick saying he will not be outperformed by Drake, relating to Prince’s more extensive catalogue over Mike’s:\n\nNigga, Prince outlived Mike Jack'\n\nDrake spun this and referred to himself as Prince, meaning he’ll outperform and outlive Kendrick through this diss war and in rap.", "annotation_id": "31633951", "url": "https://genius.com/31633951", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "When you put your hands on your girl, is it self-defense ’cause she bigger than you?", "vault_lines": ["When you put your hands on your girl, is it self-defense 'cause she bigger than you?", "Hands on your knees, hands on your knees"], "meaning": "Drake claims Kendrick Lamar physically abuses his fiancée, Whitney Alford, and sarcastically questions if Kendrick justifies his alleged physical abuse as self-defense as she is physically larger than him. Drake is mocking both Kendrick’s physical stature, masculinity, and highlights the gravity of domestic violence, insinuating that there’s no valid excuse for such behavior.\n\nKendrick and Whitney\n\nWhile no concrete evidence supports this claim, accusations of Kendrick physically abusing women have surfaced before, prompting crisis management interventions. Media Take Out reported that on May 3rd, 2014, security at The Hard Rock Hotel in Las Vegas was contacted by a young white woman who was found beaten and bloodied. The woman identified Kendrick, who had performed at the hotel that day, as the assailant. Media Take Out claims they published a story about the incident, but were first contacted by Top Dawg Entertainment (Kendrick’s label at the time), requesting the story’s removal, and later by a crisis management team that successfully pressured them to comply. The crisis management firm allegedly informed Media Take Out that they had the video of the woman’s claims removed from YouTube and wiped from the internet. However, a video of the security guard, confirmed to have worked at the hotel, claiming Kendrick beat the woman can be seen below:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/N8FSxZv0vf0?si=Julzp8CQzRgL-kuo\n\nInterestingly, the alleged incident on May 3rd coincides with the day “Family Matters” was released. The other headliner at the show was DJ Whoo Kid, which suggests that the use of the “Whoo Kid” producer tag on “Push Ups” might have been an indirect hint from Drake about his knowledge of the incident and his intention to bring it to light.", "annotation_id": "31633919", "url": "https://genius.com/31633919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Why did you move to New York? Is it ‘cause you livin’ that bachelor life? Proposed in 2015, but don’t wanna make her your actual wife I’m guessin’ this wedding ain’t happenin’, right? ’Cause we know the girls that you actually like", "vault_lines": ["We get that you like to put gin in your juice", "Why did you move to New York? Is it 'cause you livin' that bachelor life?", "Proposed in 2015, but don't wanna make her your actual wife", "I'm guessin' this wedding ain't happenin', right?", "'Cause we know the girls that you actually like", "You did her dirty all your life, you tryna make peace", "Don't make it Dave Free's"], "meaning": "Kendrick proposed to his longtime partner Whitney Alford in 2015, but they have not yet married despite having two kids together. Drake implies that this delay might be due to a lack of fidelity between them, resulting in Lamar moving to New York.", "annotation_id": "31633912", "url": "https://genius.com/31633912", "state": "accepted", "votes": 160, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Top would make you do features for change, get on pop records and rap for the whites", "vault_lines": ["Top would make you do features for change, get on pop records and rap for the whites"], "meaning": "This is reference to Anthony “Top Dawg Tiffith, Kendrick Lamar’s ex label boss, supposedly forcing Kendrick to feature on white pop artist’s songs in order to appeal to the mainstream and make a quick cheque. Drake previously critiqued Lamar for this on these lines from previous diss track “Push Ups”:\n\nMaroon 5 need a verse, you better make it witty\nThen we need a verse for the Swifties\n\nThose lines were reference to Kendrick’s features on “Bad Blood (Remix)” and “Don’t Wanna Know” by mainstream pop acts Taylor Swift and Maroon 5 respectively.\n\n\n\nKendrick Lamar and Taylor Swift in the music video for “Bad Blood (Remix.)”", "annotation_id": "31633903", "url": "https://genius.com/31633903", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Your darkest secrets are comin’ to light It’s all on your face like what happened to Mike", "vault_lines": ["Your darkest secrets are comin' to light", "It's all on your face like what happened to Mike"], "meaning": "Michael Jackson suffered from vitiligo, an auto-immune disorder that presents itself with white patches on the skin. In this line, Drake is saying that Kendrick’s secrets from his past are finally being revealed. Thus, the dark is coming to light, similar to Michael Jackson’s face.", "annotation_id": "31633895", "url": "https://genius.com/31633895", "state": "accepted", "votes": 107, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You know who really bang a set? My nigga Chuck T", "vault_lines": ["You know who really bang a set? My nigga YG", "You know who really bang a set? My nigga Chuck T"], "meaning": "The Game, also known as Chuck Taylor (Chuck T), is a West Coast rapper affiliated with the Compton Cedar Block Piru.\n\n\n\nThe Game pictured with members of the Cedar Block Piru\n\n\nThis is seen as a big jab at Kendrick Lamar since him and Game have a long standing respect for each other and there are multiple instances where K-Dot appears on a Game’s track. Such as “The City” which features a very early Kendrick Lamar, along with “See No Evil” and “On Me”.\n\nTherefore, it is considerably disrespectful to say Game actually rides with Drake, considering Game and K-Dot have such a long history. In this interview he talks about that history along with saying anybody who battles Kendrick will lose.\nhttps://youtu.be/TCO72w20VMI?si=RvV6OF1c50U8m08i\n\nThe Game and Drake also had a massive collaboration with the song “100”, and they are known to be relatively close, as Drake has mentioned game previously in YG’s track “Who Do You Love?”\n\nAnd man shout my nigga Game he just rolled through\nEatin’ crab out in Malibu at Nobu\n\n\n\nAmid the height of the beef, Game took a stance by showing support for Drake on Instagram, sharing a snippet of Drake’s 2015 hit Energy. Fans saw this as a betrayal, considering that he and other West Coast legends had once passed the torch to Kendrick at House of Blues—an event Drake had already referenced in “Taylor Made Freestyle”:\n\nNephew, what the fuck you really ‘bout to do?\nWe passed you the torch at the House of Blues", "annotation_id": "31633892", "url": "https://genius.com/31633892", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "The blacker the berry, the sweeter the juice", "vault_lines": ["The blacker the berry, the sweeter the juice"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the track “The Blacker The Berry” by Kendrick Lamar.\n\nRecently, Kendrick Lamar was criticising the use of the n-word by Drake on Euphoria because Drake is mixed race.\n\nDrake is responding by calling out this selectivism, he is being rejected for his words by Kendrick despite being black.\n\nIts also worth mentioning that this could be a reference to the opening line of Tupac’s “Keep ya Head Up”", "annotation_id": "31633886", "url": "https://genius.com/31633886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You right about “Fuck the big three,” it’s only Big D, and there’s video proof", "vault_lines": ["You right about \"Fuck the big three,\" it's only Big D, and there's video proof"], "meaning": "This is a response to Kendrick Lamar’s line on the track “Like That” which took the beef to a new level when he rapped:\n\nMotherfuck the big three, nigga, it’s just big me\n\nDrake seemingly agrees with Kendrick’s opinion, but instead spins the line and places himself in Kendrick’s spot by claiming “Big D” is on top. The line also evidently sneak disses J. Cole by placing him below his level, much like he did with Kendrick.\n\nWhen Drake raps, “it’s only Big D, and there’s video proof,” he is referring to a video that he took of himself masturbating that leaked onto the internet in February 2024. The internet was taken by storm due to the size of Drake’s genitalia and sparked a meme that had people referring to long objects as a “Drake” or imitating Drake’s actions in the leaked video as a dance.", "annotation_id": "31633853", "url": "https://genius.com/31633853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 350, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "K-Dot shit is only hittin’ hard when Baby Keem put his pen to it", "vault_lines": ["K-Dot shit is only hittin' hard when Baby Keem put his pen to it"], "meaning": "Drake claims that Kendrick Lamar‘s cousin and prominent rapper Baby Keem writes for Kendrick, and the only time Kendrick drops heat is when Keem has put pen to the sheets.\n\nBaby Keem does have multiple writing credits on Kendrick songs, which does somewhat support Drake’s claim. For example, Keem is credited as a writer on Lamar’s track “N95”, but does not have a real feature appearance on it, only providing a few ad-libs. It is entirely possible Keem was credited purely for his contribution on the ad-libs, but Drake suggests otherwise.\n\nHowever, it would likely be infeasible for Keem to have written some of Kendrick Lamar’s best work, as he was only thirteen when “good kid, m.A.A.d city” was released, which has recieved widespread critical acclaim and is considered one of Lamar’s strongest bodies of work.\n\nBaby Keem and Kendrick Lamar", "annotation_id": "31633841", "url": "https://genius.com/31633841", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "[Part III]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This serves as the third beat switch in the song, a call back to Kendrick Lamar’s line on “Like That”:\n\nFuck sneak dissin’, first-person shooter, I hope they came with three switches\n\nThe video accompanying the third part of the song is filled with references to various aspects of the beef. First, Drake can be seen eating at New Ho King, accompanied by Chubbs, the head of his security team, and another member of his entourage. Specifically, they appear to be eating fried rice with dip sauce, the exact meal mentioned by Lamar in “euphoria”:\n\nI be at New Ho King eatin' fried rice with a dip sauce and a blammy, crodie\n\n\n\nDrake’s meal at New Ho King\n\nDrake is also wearing a FUBU outfit, another reference to a line in “euphoria”:\n\nTommy Hilfiger stood out, but FUBU never had been your collection\n\nDrake rocking a FUBU hoodie\n\nDrake’s ex-accountant and now personal assistant Juju can be seen wearing pieces from Pharrell’s famous jewellry collection, which Drake purchased at auction in 2022, and memorably threatened to melt down in his verse on Travis Scott track “MELTDOWN”. Kendrick also came to Pharrell’s defence on “euphoria”:\n\nI don’t like you poppin’ shit at Pharrell, for him, I inherit the beef\n\nJuju flexing Pharrell’s jewellery collection\n\nWe also see the famous crown ring designed and worn by Tupac Shakur, which Drake purchased for over one million USD at auction last year. Kendrick stated his frustration with Drake owning the ring on “euphoria”:\n\nSomebody had told me that you got a ring, on God, I’m ready to double the wage\nI’d rather do that than let a Canadian nigga make Pac turn in his grave\n\n\n\nTupac’s ring\n\nNext, we see two cakes reading “Happy Co-Parenting” and “Happy Divorce” respectively, an allusion to what Drake alledges is the estranged relationship between Kendrick and his fiancee, Whitney Alford.\n\nCake’s referring to Kendrick’s estranged relationship\n\nWe later see images of a Michael Jackson figurine and a glove. Jackson has been a point of reference for Drake throughout his career, and he has compared himself to Jackson on numerous occasions, specifically regarding their shared ability to make hits, like in his second verse on “First Person Shooter”, where he raps:\n\nWhat the fuck, bro? I’m one away from Michael\nNigga, beat it, nigga, beat it, what?\n\nJackson and his legacy have also figured prominently in the Drake-Lamar feud, being mentioned in multiple songs by both.\n\n\n\nIn the final section of the video, we see Drake having a discussion with a man in a red bandana. The man has been identified as Andrew Curnew, Drake’s neighbor on the Bridle Path, and the owner of the property where this part of the video was filmed. Curnew faced charges for possession of guns and drugs, and domestic assault in 2005, that he maintains were wrongful. Curnew is also alleged to have links to, or to have been a former member of the Hell’s Angels biker gang.\n\nDrake and Andrew Curnew\n\nFinally, we see Drake wearing a diamond ring on a chain around his neck. The ring has been alleged to be a replica of Whitney Alford’s engagement ring.\n\n\n\nAn alleged replica of Whitney’s engagement ring", "annotation_id": "31633826", "url": "https://genius.com/31633826", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Ross callin’ me the white boy and the shit kind of got a ring to it", "vault_lines": ["Ross callin' me the white boy and the shit kind of got a ring to it"], "meaning": "Rick Ross’s recent smear campaign at Drake has revolved around calling Drake a “white boy” and calling him out for his appropriation of black culture, being mixed, and being a mama’s boy.", "annotation_id": "31633813", "url": "https://genius.com/31633813", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Nigga, what? (6ix)", "vault_lines": ["You tell me what I shouldn't say, but fuck it, my nigga, it's already done", "What the fuck I heard Rick drop, nigga? Talkin' somethin' 'bout a nose job, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake said this because on Kendrick’s track ‘euphoria’ Kendrick said he doesn’t want Drake to say “Nigga” because Drake uses black culture as an outfit. Also on his recent diss track ‘6:16 in LA’ the cover is a sextuple entendre which is why drake says nigga 6 times in this adlib", "annotation_id": "31633811", "url": "https://genius.com/31633811", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Me and Savage had the hoes drippin’ wet at shows, almost had to swim to it", "vault_lines": ["Me and Savage had the hoes drippin' wet at shows, almost had to swim to it"], "meaning": "This could be a sly shot at Ye (Kanye West), his line of WET tank tops have been advertised with shots of women dripping in sweat running on videos.\n\nFurthermore Kim Kardashian (Kanye West’s ex-wife) attended Drake’s concert in 2023 during Drake and Savage’s tour and was caught on video fawning over him. Drake is implying that she was sexually aroused and he considered sleeping with here, in a subtle dig at Kanye West.\n\nDrake and 21 Savage released their album ‘Her Loss’ back in 2022, where they shortly went on a joint tour after it’s release.\n\nDrake could just be stating this for the rap community to understand that despite 21 Savage’s closeness to Metro Boomin' and others who are dissing Drake, he is still tight with Savage.\n\nThis could also be an attempt by Drake to defend the album from slander by Metro Boomin'. Leland (Metro Boomin') was also mad that Heroes and Villains didn’t get nominated for Best Rap Album of the Year at the Grammies over Her Loss.", "annotation_id": "31633808", "url": "https://genius.com/31633808", "state": "pending", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Body after fuckin’ body and you know Rick readin’ my Miranda rights", "vault_lines": ["Body after fuckin' body and you know Rick readin' my Miranda rights"], "meaning": "Drake is “murdering” the 20 rappers he is battling one by one, and jokes that Rick Ross will read him his miranda rights due to Ross’s past life as a correctional officer.\n\nRick Ross working as a correctional officer", "annotation_id": "31633783", "url": "https://genius.com/31633783", "state": "accepted", "votes": 159, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Two separate albums dissin’, I just did a Kim to it, nigga, skim through it", "vault_lines": ["Two separate albums dissin', I just did a Kim to it, nigga, skim through it"], "meaning": "This line references the two collaborative projects released by Future and Metro Boomin. The first project WE DON’T TRUST YOU released on March 22nd, 2024 and its sequel project titled WE STILL DONT TRUST YOU released on April 12th, 2024. Both projects featured various artists taking shots at Drake including Kendrick Lamar, A$AP Rocky, The Weeknd and Rick Ross. Most notably, the duo featured Kendrick Lamar on “Like that”, a track that would reignite the Drake–Kendrick Lamar feud.\n\n\n\nThe second part of the line refers to Kim Kardashian’s clothing brand Skims. This can be seen as a diss aimed at Kanye West, Kim’s ex-husband, referring to the incident where the reality TV star was criticized for listening to Kanye West’s album, Donda, on mute.\n\n\n\nOn April 24, 2024, Just ten days shy of Drake’s releasing “Family Matters”, Kanye featured on a remix of “Like That,” where he took shots at Drake.\n\nDrake is stating that he did not pay much attention to the jabs thrown his way on the two projects, and that he just merely “skimmed” through them.", "annotation_id": "31633776", "url": "https://genius.com/31633776", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Abel, run your fuckin’ bread, need to buy some more chains for some more guys", "vault_lines": ["Abel, run your fuckin' bread, need to buy some more chains for some more guys"], "meaning": "Reference to The Weeknd’s, whose real name is Abel, manager CashXO spending Abel’s money on luxury items for men. Drake previously criticised The Weeknd and CashXO for this on these lines off “Push Ups”:\n\nCash blowin’ Abel bread, out here trickin’ (Out here trickin’)\nShit we do for bitches, he doin’ for niggas (What the fuck?)\nJets, whips, chains, wicked, wicked, wicked (Wicked, wicked)", "annotation_id": "31633743", "url": "https://genius.com/31633743", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "​good kid, m.A.A.d city van, we’ll pop the latch and let the door slide", "vault_lines": ["​good kid, m.A.A.d city van, we'll pop the latch and let the door slide"], "meaning": "Reference to the van from the cover of Kendrick Lamar’s 2012 album good kid, m.A.A.d city (Deluxe):\n\n\ngood kid, m.A.A.d city (Deluxe) album cover.\n\nThe music video accompanying Part One of “Family Matters” shows the same van being destroyed in a car crusher:\n\nThe good kid, m.A.A.d city van in the “Family Matters” music video\n\nPopping the latch and letting the door slide is a reference to committing a drive-by shooting from the van, by sliding open the door to shoot out of. Now Drake owns the van, he’s saying he’s ready to ride out on Kendrick in it.", "annotation_id": "31633720", "url": "https://genius.com/31633720", "state": "accepted", "votes": 156, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Weeknd music gettin’ played in all the spots where boys got a little more pride That’s why all your friends dippin’ to Atlanta, payin’ just to find a tour guide", "vault_lines": ["Weeknd music gettin' played in all the spots where boys got a little more pride", "That's why all your friends dippin' to Atlanta, payin' just to find a tour guide"], "meaning": "Atlanta is a notably popular LGBTQ+ friendly city, referred to as a “gay capital” or a “sanctuary” to the LGBTQ+ community. In this context, “paying just to find a tour guide” could refer to paying to have sex and party with men who are well-acquainted with the gay scene in Atlanta.", "annotation_id": "31633719", "url": "https://genius.com/31633719", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Leland Wayne, he a fuckin’ lame, so I know he had to be an influence", "vault_lines": ["Leland Wayne, he a fuckin' lame, so I know he had to be an influence"], "meaning": "Drake is dissing Metro Boomin, whose real name is Leland Wayne, and implying that he had a negative influence on Future’s opinion of Drake, leading to the dissension between the longtime collaborators.\n\nDrake and Metro Boomin’s feud began in December of 2023, after a since deleted tweet from Metro criticizing Drake and 21 Savage’s Her Loss for, in his opinion, undeservingly winning awards over his studio album Heroes and Villains.\n\nDrake responded with a video calling out, among many, the “tweeters and deleters,” which was presumably aimed at Metro, and the two have continued to exchange jabs since.\n\nWith Future and Metro Boomin teaming up for their first and second collaborative albums, WE DON’T TRUST YOU and WE STILL DON’T TRUST YOU, respectively, in the midst of Metro’s beef with Drake, it’s seemingly safe for Drake to assume that Metro has been some kind of influence on Drake’s relationship  with his friend and collaborator, Future.", "annotation_id": "31633718", "url": "https://genius.com/31633718", "state": "accepted", "votes": 205, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Must’ve snorted up a snowball", "vault_lines": ["Must've snorted up a snowball"], "meaning": "These lines are all continuing on with Ross — snorting up a snowball is an obvious coke reference, and Ross’s whole persona is based on being a big cocaine pusher. As for the house—In Champagne Moments, Ross mocks Drake for bragging about how much more money he makes, when Ross owns one of the biggest private homes in America with  (house cost $50 mil, where the fuck you want me to stay?). So Drake is talking about how that house is big but it’s in the middle of nowhere, and girls have to travel all the way out to give him blowjobs.\nFinally, the 400ms/lowball reference is another response to Ross and all his jabs about money.", "annotation_id": "31633679", "url": "https://genius.com/31633679", "state": "pending", "votes": -49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Which one of my so-called niggas Which one of my so-called niggas Which one of my so-called niggas need a shell from the clip? Always knew I had to smoke y’all niggas", "vault_lines": ["Which one of my so-called niggas", "Which one of my so-called niggas need a shell from the clip?", "Always knew I had to smoke y'all niggas"], "meaning": "Drake takes shots at a plethora of artists on this track, and he was once acquaintances with all of them. In 2012, Drake took Kendrick Lamar and A$AP Rocky on his Club Paradise Tour at a time they were relatively unknown compared to Drake. In 2010, Drake was one of the first artists to co-sign The Weeknd, and featured him extensively on his 2011 sophomore album Take Care. Future and Rick Ross both have an abundance of collaborations with Drake, with these collaborations birthing some of their biggest songs, such as “WAIT FOR U” and “Aston Martin Music” respectively. Drake may be referring to these artists as his “so called niggas” as they never really fucked with Drake, but only collaborated with him to gain fame. Drake knew they would turn on him once they got a boost in popularity, and he would have to “smoke” them.\n\nA$AP Rocky, Kendrick and Drake on the 2012 Club Paradise Tour\n\nThis may also be a reference to the alleged moles in Drake’s camp, which Kendrick declared on on previous diss “6:16 in LA”:\n\nAre you finally ready to play have-you-ever? Let’s see\nHave you ever thought that OVO is workin’ for me?\nFake bully, I hate bullies, you must be a terrible person\nEveryone inside your team is whispering that you deserve it", "annotation_id": "31633674", "url": "https://genius.com/31633674", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Let me stop playin’ around, let me take this shit serious, like", "vault_lines": ["Let me stop playin' around, let me take this shit serious, like"], "meaning": "Drake originally warned Kendrick Lamar and many other artists who dissed him on Future and Metro Boomin’s WE DON’T TRUST YOU on his track “Push Ups”:\n\nThis ain’t even everything I know, don’t wake the demon up\nThis ain’t even everything I know, don’t wake the demon up\n\nDrake then later released “Taylor Made Freestyle” on Instagram with the Tupac and Snoop Dogg AI, and went to post multiple different memes on Instagram.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/i/status/1786529700665237576\n\nDrake says he’ll “stop playing around”, suggesting he is now going to take this beef more serious than he was before, possibly alluding to how he’s close to pressing the red button, in reference to his track “Red Button” from For All The Dogs Scary Hours Edition.\n\nTickin' time bomb and they beggin' me to detonate it\nIf I press this red button, dog, everybody Heaven Gated\nPress this red button, dog, and everything forever changes", "annotation_id": "31633653", "url": "https://genius.com/31633653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Ozempic got a side effect of jealousy and doctor never told y’all niggas", "vault_lines": ["Ozempic got a side effect of jealousy and doctor never told y'all niggas"], "meaning": "Ozempic is a weight loss drug often abused by people to lose weight. Rick Ross used to weigh over 400lbs, but lost it all through gastric bypass and ozempic usage. Ozempic has many side effects. Drake is joking about jealousy being one.\n\nRick got involved in the rap beef between Kendrick and Drake, with the track “Champagne Moments”.\n\nRick Ross' potential jealousy stems from remarks like:\n\nYou a worker wantin' to chart, don’t make me laugh\n\nHe tested Drake’s wealth and power, talking down on his deal despite Drake claiming he signed a ‘400M’ deal, which is somehow a low bar for him.\n\nThat deal would alone would be worth over double Rick Ross' net worth at $150m. So the audacity to diminish Drake’s wealth makes Rick Ross' diss look cheap and jealous.\n\n\n\n\nIn Kendrick Lamar’s cover for his response released 57 minutes later, “meet the grahams”, an Ozempic box prescribed to what seems to be “Aubrey Graham” can be seen in the bottom right of the photo, seemingly showing that Drake is a hypocrite and uses the drug himself.\n\nDon’t cut them corners like your daddy did, fuck what Ozempic did", "annotation_id": "31633648", "url": "https://genius.com/31633648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 139, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "’Cause my last record deal was four hundred M’s, these days, that’s a low ball, nigga", "vault_lines": ["'Cause my last record deal was four hundred M's, these days, that's a low ball, nigga"], "meaning": "Reference to Drake’s 2021 deal with Universal records reportedly being worth $400 million.", "annotation_id": "31633634", "url": "https://genius.com/31633634", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Ayy Who’s next on the list?", "vault_lines": ["Who's next on the list?"], "meaning": "Drake has made it clear he feels it is his vs the industry with the quote “what the fuck is this, a 20v1 nigga?”, and he is addressing multiple targets with this diss track", "annotation_id": "31633628", "url": "https://genius.com/31633628", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Still mad about that one ho, we ain’t even fuck, I just lip-locked with her", "vault_lines": ["Still mad about that one ho, we ain't even fuck, I just lip-locked with her"], "meaning": "The Weeknd dated supermodel Bella Hadid on and off from 2015-2019. In 2017, rumours emerged that Drake was dating Bella after she and The Weeknd split in late 2016. Drake suggests that The Weeknd is still mad at Drizzy for dating Bella, and is the reason Abel took shots at him on “All to Myself”.\n\nThe Weeknd and Bella\n\nAlternatively, this line may be alluding to Drake and Future’s alleged beef over a girl known as Just Tokyo. Future took shots at Drake on “We Don’t Trust You”, claiming Drake was pillow talking to a girl about Future. Drake again suggests Future only took shots at him because Future’s mad that Drake kissed Tokyo.\n\nJust Tokyo backstage at Future’s Rolling Loud set", "annotation_id": "31633589", "url": "https://genius.com/31633589", "state": "accepted", "votes": 182, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Can’t listen to the stick talk in falsetto, save it for a hip-hop nigga", "vault_lines": ["Can't listen to the stick talk in falsetto, save it for a hip-hop nigga"], "meaning": "Drake is not a fan of being dissed by The Weeknd in his high note falsetto voice, like he did on the song “All To Myself”", "annotation_id": "31633558", "url": "https://genius.com/31633558", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "He always said I overlooked him, I was starin’ straight", "vault_lines": ["He always said I overlooked him, I was starin' straight"], "meaning": "To overlook someone means to fail to notice them but it also means to look over them from a height advantage. Drake is riffing on the height difference between him and Kendrick Lamar: Drake stands at 6'2\", Kendrick at a reported 5'5\".\n\nDrake brought Kendrick on his first major tour (the 2012 Club Paradise run) and featured him on Take Care. He was the bigger artist who extended a hand to a promising newcomer. In Drake’s telling, that dynamic never changed, Kendrick has always been someone Drake looks down at, whether in stature or status.", "annotation_id": "31633528", "url": "https://genius.com/31633528", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "’Cause if your GM is your BM secret BD Then this is all makin’ plenty fuckin’ sense to me", "vault_lines": ["'Cause if your GM is your BM secret BD", "Then this is all makin' plenty fuckin' sense to me"], "meaning": "Drake insinuates that Kendrick Lamar’s GM (general manager), Dave Free, had an affair and impregnated Kendrick’s BM (baby mama), Whitney Alford, and is secretly the BD (baby daddy) of Kendrick’s son.\n\nDrake stated that K Dot walked out on Whitney and moved to New York later in the track. Drake notes that this makes sense considering Whitney’s affair.", "annotation_id": "31633521", "url": "https://genius.com/31633521", "state": "accepted", "votes": 154, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "All that puppy love was over in y’all late teens", "vault_lines": ["All that puppy love was over in y'all late teens"], "meaning": "Kendrick Lamar and his fiancée, Whitney Alford, met in high school. Drake insinuates that Lamar’s love with Whitney has faded, and are only staying together to preserve Kendrick’s image.\n\nAdditionally, Kendrick signed to Top Dawg Entertainment in 2005, when he was 17 years old. This is a double meaning that when they were in their late teens, “puppy love” ended once he signed with Top “Dawg”.\n\nKendrick and Whitney in High School", "annotation_id": "31633513", "url": "https://genius.com/31633513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "You better have some paperwork or that shit fake tea Can’t be rappin’ ‘bout no rattin’ that we can’t read", "vault_lines": ["You better have some paperwork or that shit fake tea", "Can't bе rappin' 'bout no rattin' that we can't read"], "meaning": "On “euphoria”, Kendrick Lamar used Gunna’s reputation as a “snitch” in the YSL RICO case to imply that Drake has snitched in the past:\n\nI know some shit about niggas that make Gunna Wunna look like a saint\n\nK Dot also accused Drake of cease and desisting “Like That” on “euphoria”:\n\nTry cease and desist on the “Like That” record?\nHo, what? You ain’t like that record?\n\nAdditionally, on “6:16 in LA” claimed members of Drake’s entourage have been ratting on him and leaking information to Kendrick:\n\nHave you ever thought that OVO is workin’ for me?\n\nHowever, Drake insists Lamar must come with evidence or he is lying.", "annotation_id": "31633508", "url": "https://genius.com/31633508", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "I know you like to keep it short, so let me paraphrase", "vault_lines": ["I know you like to keep it short, so let me paraphrase"], "meaning": "Drake once again goes for Kendricks short height of 5'5, saying he likes to “keep it short, so let me paraphrase.”\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: also word play for ‘pair a phrase’ as he ties this to the last bar", "annotation_id": "31633494", "url": "https://genius.com/31633494", "state": "pending", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Knew it was smoke when Abel hit us with the serenade", "vault_lines": ["Knew it was smoke when Abel hit us with the serenade"], "meaning": "The Weeknd took shots at Drake on “All to Myself”, which were delivered in a melodic, serenade-like tone:\n\nThey could never diss my brothers, baby (Future)\nWhen they got leaks in they operation\nI thank God that I never signed my life away\nAnd we never do the big talk (No, no, no, no, no)\nThey shooters makin' TikToks\n\nDrake sarcastically states he knew the beef was serious when he heard Abel’s diss, mockingly suggesting his melodic delivery is not suited for sending shots in a beef.", "annotation_id": "31633485", "url": "https://genius.com/31633485", "state": "accepted", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "And hit vanilla cream to help out with your self-esteem", "vault_lines": ["And hit vanilla cream to help out with your self-esteem"], "meaning": "This is reference to Kendrick Lamar sleeping with white women, which Drake refers to as vanilla cream. Kendrick discussed this on track “Worldwide Steppers”:\n\nThe first time I fucked a white bitch\nI was sixteen at the Palisades\n\nThis may also be reference to Kendrick literally hitting his fiancé, Whitney, who Drake refers to as vanilla cream as she is lightskin. Drake explicitly claims that Kendrick hit Whitney later on this track:\n\nThey hired a crisis management team to clean up the fact that you beat on your queen\n\nDrake also claims that Whitney slept with and had a child with Kendrick’s manager and childhood friend Dave Free later in this song:\n\nI heard that one of them little kids might be Dave Free\nDon’t make it Dave Free’s\n‘Cause if your GM is your BM secret BD\nThen this is all makin’ plenty fuckin' sense to me\n\nExperiencing infidelity can cause tremendous damage to one’s self esteem. Drake may be claiming that Kendrick hit Whitney in an attempt to bolster his self-esteem and seek vengeance for this supposed betrayal.\n\n\n Pictured: Whitney and Kendrick", "annotation_id": "31633482", "url": "https://genius.com/31633482", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "These bars go over Kenny head no matter what I say", "vault_lines": ["These bars go over Kenny head no matter what I say"], "meaning": "Double entendre here that Kendrick will not get Drake’s lyrics because 1. he doesn’t understand their meaning and 2. Drake’s words will literally go “over his head” since Drake (6'0\") is seven inches taller than Kendrick (5'5\").", "annotation_id": "31633472", "url": "https://genius.com/31633472", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Your man a lil’ K, we call that shit a mini Drac’", "vault_lines": ["Your man a lil' K, we call that shit a mini Drac'"], "meaning": "Draco Pistols are firearms inspired by the design of the AK-47. Drake is specifically referring to Dracos' two alternative smaller variants (Mini and Micro) to again allude to Kendrick’s short stature.\n\n\n\nBy calling him a “mini Dra[ke],” Drake reminds Kenny of the opportunities that he gave him while coming up in the industry, such as a spot on his 2011 sophomore album Take Care and an opening act on his 2012 Club Paradise Tour.\n\nThis line is likely a callback and response to Kenny’s similar line on his April 30 track “Euphoria”:\n\nThe very first time I shot me a Drac', the homie had told me to aim it this way\nI didn’t point down enough, today, I’ll show you I learned from those mistakes\n\nAs well as to Kendrick’s March 22 feature on “Like That”:\n\nThat’s a K with all these nines, he gon' see Pet Sematary (Yeah)\nNigga, bum", "annotation_id": "31633454", "url": "https://genius.com/31633454", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Always rappin’ like you ’bout to get the slaves freed You just actin’ like an activist, it’s make-believe", "vault_lines": ["Always rappin' like you 'bout to get the slaves freed", "You just actin' like an activist, it's make-believe"], "meaning": "Drake alleges that Kendrick’s signature subject matter of Black empowerment, most noticeably woven into his 2015 opus To Pimp a Butterfly, is more of a shallow aesthetic than anything.\n\n\n\n Cover art for To Pimp a Butterfly.\n\n\nThe album has been dubbed by many as a masterful display of the African American experience due to how it tackled themes of colorism, class strife and police brutality at the height of the Black Lives Matter movement.\n\nLamar notably attended a Black Lives Matter protest in his home city of Compton in June 2020: https://x.com/retOne/status/1269771320788545536", "annotation_id": "31633447", "url": "https://genius.com/31633447", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Ayy, let that shorty breathe Shake that ass, bitch, hands on your knees Hands on your knees, hands on your knees Shake that ass for Drake, now shake that ass for free Yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, let that shorty breathe", "Shake that ass, bitch, hands on your knees", "Hands on your knees, hands on your knees", "Shake that ass for Drake, now shake that ass for free"], "meaning": "The first line, “Ayy, let that shorty breathe” is a shot at Kendrick Lamar and his fiancée, Whitney Alford. With Drake alleging Kendrick is abusing her and needs to let her go.\n\nFor the following lines, Drake interpolates the chorus from his track “Rich Baby Daddy” that features Sexyy Red and SZA, where Sexyy Red raps:\n\nBend that ass over (Baow), let that coochie breathe (Yeah)\nShake that ass, bitch, hands on your knees (Ho)\nHands on your knees (Ho), hands on your knees (Ow)\nShake that ass for Drake (Yup), now, shake that ass for me\nBend that ass over (Baow), let that coochie breathe (Yeah)\nShake that ass, bitch, hands on your knees\nHands on your knees, hands on your knees\nShake that ass for Drake (Yeah), now, shake that ass for me\n\nThese lines are also seemingly a response to Kendrick Lamar’s diss that was directed towards Drake and Sexyy Redd on the track “euphoria,” where he rapped:\n\nWhen I see you stand by Sexyy Red, I believe you see two bad bitches\nI believe you don’t like women, it’s real competition, you might pop ass with ‘em\n\nThe final line, “Shake that ass for Drake, now shake that ass for free” can be interpreted as another jab at Whitney Alford. With the allegation that one of Kendrick’s children may actually belong to Dave Free, his close friend and former president of Top Dawg Entertainment, Drake is telling Whitney to “shake that ass” on Free and himself, suggesting she is not loyal to Kendrick.", "annotation_id": "31633443", "url": "https://genius.com/31633443", "state": "accepted", "votes": 180, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Out here beggin’ for attention, nigga, say please", "vault_lines": ["Out here beggin' for attention, nigga, say please"], "meaning": "Drake is calling out Kendrick Lamar for his aggressive demeanor and provocation within the song, “Like That”.\n\nDrake points out that Kendrick Lamar, particularly his dissing of other artists (including him), are a plea for attention, like how he gatekeeps his thoughts in “euphoria”.\n\nI can double down on that line, but spare you this time, that’s random acts of kindness\n\nDrake is positioning himself as someone who doesn’t need to resort to such tactics to maintain his status or relevance.\n\nDrake also suggests that Kendrick Lamar is desperate for his attention as he dropped “6:16 in LA”, another diss targeted towards Drake, just 3 days after “euphoria” making it three songs in which Kendrick Lamar has gone after Drake.\n\nIt may also be a continuation of the previous line about Metro. The beef between Drake and him is believed to have started after Metro tweeted about being more deserving of Grammies for his album after Drake won several.\n\nDrake suggests that Metro only receives any attention if he (Drake) decides to talk about him.", "annotation_id": "31633430", "url": "https://genius.com/31633430", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Your baby mama captions always screamin’, “Save me” You did her dirty all your life, you tryna make peace", "vault_lines": ["Your baby mama captions always screamin', \"Save me\"", "You did her dirty all your life, you tryna make peace"], "meaning": "Kendrick Lamar’s baby mama and fiancée, Whitney Alford, has several Instagram posts with captions detailing her hardships:\n\nOne of Whitney’s Instagram captions\n\nDrake blames Kendrick “doing her dirty” by being unfaithful as the reason for Whitney’s mental suffering. Kendrick’s infidelity and attempts to make peace with Whitney were discussed extensively on his 2022 album Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers.", "annotation_id": "31633423", "url": "https://genius.com/31633423", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Why you never hold your son and tell him, ”Say cheese”? We could’ve left the kids out of this, don’t blame me", "vault_lines": ["Why you never hold your son and tell him, \"Say cheese\"?", "We could've left the kids out of this, don't blame me"], "meaning": "Although limited, Kendrick Lamar has kept a distance from his son in every public picture the two have been in together. Later in the track, Drake proclaims one of Kendrick’s children’s biological father is Dave Free, Kendrick’s right hand man. Drake suggests this is the reason K Dot does not hold his son in photos, as his son is not actually his.\n\nKendrick holding his daughter in the cover art for Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers\n\n\nWhile Drake claims he was willing to leave the children out of the feud, he states all bets were off the table when Kendrick mentioned his son, Adonis, and criticised Drake’s parenting abilities on “euphoria”:\n\nThat’s ho shit, I got a son to raise, but I can see you don’t know nothin’ ’bout that", "annotation_id": "31633416", "url": "https://genius.com/31633416", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Don’t even go back to your hood and plant no money trees", "vault_lines": ["Don't even go back to your hood and plant no money trees"], "meaning": "Drake references the famous Kendrick track “Money Trees” while also stating that Kendrick has never actually given out any type of financial support to Compton (a place that has been integral to his life and discography themes). Despite this, Kendrick has personally donated hundreds of thousands of dollars to support sports programs, after-school programs, and music programs in the Compton Unified School District to help keep Compton students off the streets and in the classroom. Not to mention being given the key to the city by the Mayor of Compton in 2016.", "annotation_id": "31633390", "url": "https://genius.com/31633390", "state": "accepted", "votes": 206, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "I mean it’s true a nigga slimed me for my AP", "vault_lines": ["I mean it's truе a nigga slimed me for my AP"], "meaning": "In 2009, Drake was robbed.\n\nHe and his date had finished eating at a restaurant and had walked to Drake’s SUV on Beatrice Street. Moments after they got to the vehicle, two armed men approached Drake and demanded his money and jewelry. The then 23-year-old rapper complied, giving the perpetrators $2,000 in cash, an Audemars Piguet watch, and a diamond chain Lil Wayne had gifted him.", "annotation_id": "31633385", "url": "https://genius.com/31633385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "And, nigga, Cole losin’ sleep on this, it ain’t me", "vault_lines": ["And, nigga, Cole losin' sleep on this, it ain't me"], "meaning": "Kendrick Lamar’s game-shaking March 2024 verse on Future and Metro Boomin’s “Like That” had shots for both Drake and J. Cole:\n\nSay, it’s a lot of goofies with a check\n[…] Niggas clickin' up, but cannot be legit\n[…] Fuck sneak dissin', first-person shooter, I hope they came with three switches\n[…] Motherfuck the big three, nigga, it’s just big me\nNigga, bum\n\nCole spontaneously dropped his project Might Delete Later two weeks later with the Kendrick-dedicated “7 Minute Drill”:\n\nHe still doin' shows, but fell off like The Simpsons\nYour first shit was classic, your last shit was tragic\nYour second shit put niggas to sleep, but they gassed it\n[…] He averagin' one hard verse like every thirty months or somethin'\n[…] Your arms might be too short to box with the god\n\nJust days later, Cole apologized to Kendrick at Dreamville Fest about the diss record:\n\nThat shit disrupts my peace\n[…] I haven’t been sleeping right the last few days\n[…] The past two days felt terrible\nIt let me know how good I’ve been sleeping for the past 10 years\n\nThis is also a direct response to Kendrick Lamar’s words on “6:16 in LA”:\n\nknow you can’t sleep, these images trouble you\nKnow the wires in your circle should puzzle you", "annotation_id": "31633364", "url": "https://genius.com/31633364", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "line": "Maybe in this song, you shouldn’t start by saying Nigga, I said it, I know that you mad", "vault_lines": ["Maybe in this song, you shouldn't start by saying", "Nigga, I said it, I know that you mad"], "meaning": "Drake starts this diss track with his mother’s voice. Sandi tries to warn him from using profanity throughout the diss; however, it is obvious Drake set it up so that he can start the song with “nigga.”\n\nOn “euphoria”, Kendrick made it clear that he does not want to hear Drake using the word, nor does he consider him “black enough” to use it:\n\nWe don’t wanna hear you say “nigga” no more\nWe don’t wanna hear you say “nigga” no more\nStop\n\nUnlike Kendrick’s slow start on his disses, Drake immediately goes in and makes his intentions clear. Drake would use the word 37 times throughout the track.", "annotation_id": "31633329", "url": "https://genius.com/31633329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 388, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/64df40485baa8d02820789fc543273db.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Family Matters\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Family MattersDrake5.3M", "status": "done", "targets": 110, "clicked": 110, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-family-matters-lyrics", "genius_title": "Family Matters"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Now she engaged to be married, what’s the rush on commitment?", "vault_lines": ["Now she engaged to be married, what's the rush on commitment?"], "meaning": "potential triple entendre\n\n\nEngaged to be married (engagement, marriage)\nEngaged in marriage (actively doing, marriage)\nEngaged, rush (actively doing, rushing to commitment)", "annotation_id": "41766777", "url": "https://genius.com/41766777", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "You don’t even know what you want from love anymore I search for something I’m missing and disappear when I’m bored But girl, what qualities was I looking for before? Who you settling for? Who better for you than the boy, huh?", "vault_lines": ["You don't even know what you want from love anymore", "I search for something I'm missing and disappear when I'm bored", "But girl, what qualities was I looking for before?", "Who you settling for? Who better for you than the boy, huh?"], "meaning": "Even though he is giving mixed signals like maybe giving love then disappering once he gets bored, he still believes he possess all the qualities the girl is looking for", "annotation_id": "38969605", "url": "https://genius.com/38969605", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "My mother is sixty-six and her favorite line to hit me with is “Who the fuck wants to be seventy and alone?”", "vault_lines": ["My mother is sixty-six and her favorite line to hit me with is", "\"Who the fuck wants to be seventy and alone?\""], "meaning": "Mama drake is worried about him,she urges him to not do risky thing so he dies and she lives alone consequently", "annotation_id": "35889388", "url": "https://genius.com/35889388", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Or Beverly Center Macy’s where I discovered Bria", "vault_lines": ["Or Beverly Center Macy's where I discovered Bria"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to Bria Myles, his ex-girlfriend. He dedicated an entire song to her, “Bria’s Interlude”, from his 2009 mixtape “So Far Gone”.\n\nShe disputed his account in a tweet where she says they actually met at Bloomingdale’s.\nhttps://twitter.com/RealBriaMyles/status/379751781439270912?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E379751781439270912&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.empireboobookitty.com%2F2017%2F01%2Fbria-myles-drake-before-surgery-birthday%2F", "annotation_id": "17153868", "url": "https://genius.com/17153868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "[Verse 3: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "It might be easy to miss, but the second verse marks a turning point in the song.\n\nIn the first verse, the beat moves forward in time and Drake talks about the current problems he has with a girl.\n\nIn this second verse, though, the beat’s harmonizing goes backwards (like in Tuscan Leather). This is because Drake is looking backwards at the troubles he had with girls before he was famous. (Although nothing was the same, I guess some things don’t change, eh?)\n\n\n\nThis forward/backward conflict happens all over the album. In NWTS, when Drake stares into his past his past stares back at him. Those two Drakes are in constant dialog, and this is just one example of their conversation.", "annotation_id": "3003012", "url": "https://genius.com/3003012", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "[Chorus: Jhené Aiko]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jhene stated that when she originally got the beat she made a completly different song in an interview with Brittany Lewis:\n\n\nBL: Let’s talk “Beautiful Ruins.” I know that when Drake initially sent you the “From Time” track you had written a completely different song and decided to redo it and send it to him. Are you planning on doing a CDQ version of “Beautiful Ruins?”\n\nJA: Yeah, “Beautiful Ruins” is going to be on Souled Out. I recorded it, put it together. I sang it to a guitar. Steve Wyreman, he plays the guitar in Cocaine 80s, so that’s how we start. I don’t know if we are going to build on the beat, but right now it’s an acoustic song. I’m excited for people to hear that.", "annotation_id": "2479536", "url": "https://genius.com/2479536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "I look around the peers that surround me, these niggas tripping", "vault_lines": ["I look around the peers that surround me, these niggas tripping"], "meaning": "Back in March of 2013 rumors surfaced that Drake and his Toronto peer The Weeknd had a falling out because The Weeknd decided not to sign to Drake’s own label. In July of 2013 Drake posted pics of himself and the Weeknd in the studio working together to shut down the rumors.", "annotation_id": "2241798", "url": "https://genius.com/2241798", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Remember our talk in the parking lot at the Ritz", "vault_lines": ["Remember our talk in the parking lot at the Ritz"], "meaning": "Drake and his girl are trying to fix the problems in front of a very luxurious hotel. This could indicate that they are talking while Drake is touring, which could stress the relationship even more.\n\n\n\nWouldn’t read in to this line too much, but the Ritz Carlton is next door to the Hooters on Peachtree.", "annotation_id": "2240474", "url": "https://genius.com/2240474", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "You a flower child, beautiful child, I’m in your zone Looking like you came from the seventies on your own", "vault_lines": ["You a flower child, beautiful child, I'm in your zone", "Looking like you came from the seventies on your own"], "meaning": "“Flower child” is a synonym for “hippie” or one who holds an ideology similar to that of a hippie (e.g. someone who deeply values peace and the environment). In the ‘70s there were a lot of hippies\n\n\n\nShe has a very classic beauty which is rare in the type of girls Drake is used to dealing with.", "annotation_id": "2216610", "url": "https://genius.com/2216610", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Well, fuck it, we had a couple Coronas We might have rolled a white paper, just something to hold us We even talked about you and our couple of moments He said we should hash it out like a couple of grown-ups", "vault_lines": ["Well, fuck it, we had a couple Coronas", "We might have rolled a white paper, just something to hold us", "We even talked about you and our couple of moments", "He said we should hash it out like a couple of grown-ups"], "meaning": "Drake and his dad pop open Corona’s and smoke a little to get relaxed to vent and talk about relationships.\n\nThe extended weed metaphor is a well thought out couple of lines. Drake rolls white papers into joints and smokes them with his dad.  He then says they should hash it out like a couple of grown-ups. Hash has two meanings: it is a product of cannabis, and it can also be used to mean talk it out it in this situation.\n\n\n\nDrake hits his dad up for relationship advice, just before asking his mom", "annotation_id": "2216183", "url": "https://genius.com/2216183", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Learning the true consequences of my selfish decisions When you find out how I’m living, I just hope I’m forgiven", "vault_lines": ["Learning the true consequences of my selfish decisions", "When you find out how I'm living, I just hope I'm forgiven"], "meaning": "Drake has to deal with the regret and result of being selfish, losing out on another “piece to complete him.” However, he hopes that his exes can see how he’s living so they can understand why he made those decisions.\n\nThis also refers to the fact that most girls think that Drake only has a few main girls. When they find out he’s having multiple partners in a week they feel some type of way. The line from Marvin’s Room “I’ve had sex 4 times this week let me explain”. He has dealt with the themes expressed in this verse before.", "annotation_id": "2216134", "url": "https://genius.com/2216134", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Now it’s therapeutic blowing money in the Galleria", "vault_lines": ["Now it's therapeutic blowing money in the Galleria"], "meaning": "The Galleria is the largest mall in Houston, and is frequented by celebrities due to the upscale selection and clientele.\n\nRetail therapy is done with the purpose of improving one’s mood or disposition, so it only makes sense for Drake to make his “comfort buys” at the most exclusive mall Houston has to offer.", "annotation_id": "2215916", "url": "https://genius.com/2215916", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Landmarks of the muses that inspired the music When I could tell it was sincere without tryna prove it", "vault_lines": ["Landmarks of the muses that inspired the music", "When I could tell it was sincere without tryna prove it"], "meaning": "This refers to the line above where he discovered Bria Myles.\nMuses, in Greek and Roman mythology refer to the nine daughters of Zeus and goddesses the arts and science.\n\n\n\nSo the place where he met Bria, a muse of beauty and art that inspired him musically (“Bria’s Interlude”), is now a landmark serving to honor her.", "annotation_id": "2215702", "url": "https://genius.com/2215702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Started realizing a couple places I could take it I want to get back to when I was that kid in the basement I want to take it deeper than money, pussy, vacation And influence a generation that’s lacking in patience", "vault_lines": ["Started realizing a couple places I could take it", "I want to get back to when I was that kid in the basement", "I want to take it deeper than money, pussy, vacation", "And influence a generation that's lacking in patience"], "meaning": "Drake realizes that with the large scope his art has, he can take it anywhere he chooses. He’d rather talk about/think like the younger Drake who lived in the basement (as seen on Degrassi Unscripted: Drake) than focus on money, pussy, and vacation. He hopes to be a good influence on his listeners\n\n\n\nThe artwork of Nothing Was the Same is the perfect example of Drake’s attitude towards music. He has stated that he wanted to give his fans a true piece of art, instead of a standard rap album cover. The artwork is an oil painting by Kadir Nelson, who also did the artwork for Michael Jackson’s posthumous album, Michael.\n\nThe line I want to take it deeper than money, pussy, vacation is one of the many lines Drake includes throughout Nothing Was the Same that subtly tells the listener that he’s growing apart from Lil Wayne.\n\nRecently, Lil Wayne has taken to rapping, as Drake says, mainly about money, girls and vacation – constantly mentioning 2 of them in the same line. Drake does not want to end up making music as shallow and disposable as his mentor. The reader should also note that Lil Wayne does not feature on any of the songs on Nothing Was the Same.\n\nRather, Drake references Lupe Fiasco’s Strange Fruition\n\nMan, they so impatient with the stations that they have", "annotation_id": "2214873", "url": "https://genius.com/2214873", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Been Baka aka Not Nice from time, G Been a East Side ting Scarborough ting from time, G Been have up di ting dem from time, G So I don’t know what’s wrong with these Little wasteman out here, eh? Y’all need to know yourself", "vault_lines": ["Been Baka aka Not Nice from time, G", "Been a East Side ting", "Scarborough ting from time, G", "Been have up di ting dem from time, G", "So I don't know what's wrong with these", "Little wasteman out here, eh?", "Y'all need to know yourself"], "meaning": "Baka Not Nice is a friend of Drake, representing Scarborough since he could remember. But he’s sick of the wannabes saying they have been ‘bout it and saying they have been there since the start when they haven’t. They need to check themselves.\n\n\n\n“Wasteman” is a Jamaican slang term used to refer to people who are a waste of anyone’s time", "annotation_id": "2214699", "url": "https://genius.com/2214699", "state": "accepted", "votes": 184, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "It seem like you don’t want this love anymore I’m acting out in the open, it’s hard for you to ignore", "vault_lines": ["It seem like you don't want this love anymore", "I'm acting out in the open, it's hard for you to ignore"], "meaning": "The “acting” here may be acting out in the partying sense, or simply making the relationship public via his music, a bit like Kid Cudi on “Erase Me”:\n\nI’m in the magazines, on the TV\nNo matter where you go, you might see me", "annotation_id": "2214560", "url": "https://genius.com/2214560", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "The one that I needed was Courtney from Hooters on Peachtree I’ve always been feeling like she was the piece to complete me", "vault_lines": ["The one that I needed was Courtney from Hooters on Peachtree", "I've always been feeling like she was the piece to complete me"], "meaning": "You mean this Hooters? It didn’t take long until people managed to track down and find pictures of Courtney Janell who had our boy Drake floating on cloud nine at one time.", "annotation_id": "2214509", "url": "https://genius.com/2214509", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "I love me I love me enough for the both of us", "vault_lines": ["I love me enough for the both of us"], "meaning": "I love myself enough to know what love actually is … I’m not concerned with you loving me or not…. that has nothing to do with the love I have for you. I am so full of love that i can love you and me… at the same damn time .", "annotation_id": "2214363", "url": "https://genius.com/2214363", "state": "verified", "votes": 1354, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "What’s up? Been a minute since we kicked it, you’ve been caught up", "vault_lines": ["Been a minute since we kicked it, you've been caught up"], "meaning": "hello …. we haven’t seen each other in a while … you’ve probably been hanging out with woman who couldn’t possibly understand you like i can .", "annotation_id": "2214359", "url": "https://genius.com/2214359", "state": "verified", "votes": 895, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Then we got to talking ’bout something we disagreed on Then she start telling me how I’ll never be as big as Trey Songz Boy, was she wrong", "vault_lines": ["Then we got to talking 'bout something we disagreed on", "Then she start telling me how I'll never be as big as Trey Songz", "Boy, was she wrong"], "meaning": "Trey Songz‘ last album has sold 238,400 copies domestically so far, while Take Care has topped two million", "annotation_id": "2213618", "url": "https://genius.com/2213618", "state": "accepted", "votes": 174, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Or further back than that, before I had the Houston leverage", "vault_lines": ["Or further back than that, before I had the Houston leverage"], "meaning": "H-Town has been a big part of Drake’s rise as a rapper.  It’s where he first met Lil Wayne, and Texas rap legend Bun B has been like an uncle to the Canadian\n\nDrake refers to his ties with Houston in many of his songs like “No New Friends”: “H-town my second home like I’m James Harden”", "annotation_id": "2213585", "url": "https://genius.com/2213585", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "I’ve been dealing with my dad, speaking of lack of patience Just me and my old man getting back to basics We’ve been talking ’bout the future and time that we wasted When he put that bottle down, girl, that nigga’s amazing", "vault_lines": ["I've been dealing with my dad, speaking of lack of patience", "Just me and my old man getting back to basics", "We've been talking 'bout the future and time that we wasted", "When he put that bottle down, girl, that nigga's amazing"], "meaning": "Drake’s dad says the hip-hop star was destined to be a mega talent even BEFORE the rapper was born … for one very specific reason – him\n\n\n\nDrizzy has been thinking a lot about his relationship with his father lately:\n\n“There have been dark times,” Drake admitted. “At the end of the day, my dad knows I love him and that he’s a great friend of mine, but as far as being a father goes, I look forward to being a better father for the children that I have. But at the end of the day, he is a great man and I love him very much.”\n\nThose “dark times” could mean that his father is an alcoholic, but when he’s not drinking he’s a great person\n\nClever wordplay with “time that we wasted” and “put that bottle down”", "annotation_id": "2213509", "url": "https://genius.com/2213509", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "So what are you What are you, what are you so afraid of? Darling, you You give, but you cannot take love", "vault_lines": ["So what are you", "What are you, what are you so afraid of?", "You give, but you cannot take love"], "meaning": "Drake’s biggest weakness. So big, its the fuel for all his albums, mixtapes, label deals, popularity, and lyrical content.\n\nShe asks him about this fear and reminds him that love is a mutual commitment.", "annotation_id": "2213466", "url": "https://genius.com/2213466", "state": "accepted", "votes": 202, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "When I got Summer a Michael Kors with my momma’s debit A weak attempt at flexing, I’ll never forget it", "vault_lines": ["When I got Summer a Michael Kors with my momma's debit", "A weak attempt at flexing, I'll never forget it"], "meaning": "People have wondered if this Summer is Summer P. Walker—now ex-wife of DJ Drama. Drake shouts her out again in “I’m the Plug.”\n\n\n\nMichael Kors is a fashion designer. Products with the name Kors on them sell for hundreds or even thousands of dollars, so buying one for a girl you like might impress her. Using your moms debit card to do buy it however isn’t a smart move.\n\nDecisions like this may be why Drake and his mother were always fighting", "annotation_id": "2213298", "url": "https://genius.com/2213298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Yeah, I needed to hear that shit, I hate when you’re submissive Passive aggressive when we’re texting, I feel the distance", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I needed to hear that shit, I hate when you're submissive", "Passive aggressive when we're texting, I feel the distance"], "meaning": "Drake appreciates the direct message of the song’s hook, as opposed to the constant game-playing and one-upmanship of electronic communication.", "annotation_id": "2213143", "url": "https://genius.com/2213143", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "line": "Uh, thinking ’bout Texas, back when Porscha used to work at Treasures", "vault_lines": ["Uh, thinking 'bout Texas, back when Porscha used to work at Treasures"], "meaning": "Treasures is a popular strip club in Houston.  And we all know about strippers named “Porscha”", "annotation_id": "2213031", "url": "https://genius.com/2213031", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"From Time\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>From TimeDrake4.1M", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 26, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-from-time-lyrics", "genius_title": "From Time"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "From Time (Demo)", "line": "I love me I love me enough for the both of us", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jhené Aiko explained the line in a verified annotation on the official track:\n\nI love myself enough to know what love actually is … I’m not concerned with you loving me or not…. that has nothing to do with the love I have for you. I am so full of love that i can love you and me… at the same damn time .", "annotation_id": "18212810", "url": "https://genius.com/18212810", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "From Time (Demo)", "line": "What’s up? Been a minute since we kicked it, you’ve been caught up", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jhené Aiko explained the line in a verified annotation on the official track:\n\nhello …. we haven’t seen each other in a while … you’ve probably been hanging out with woman who couldn’t possibly understand you like i can .", "annotation_id": "18212758", "url": "https://genius.com/18212758", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "From Time (Demo)", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 2, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-from-time-demo-lyrics", "genius_title": "From Time (Demo)"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "I’m sorry for the people I’ve pushed out", "vault_lines": ["I'm sorry for the people I've pushed out"], "meaning": "In the line before he mentions getting rich ain’t an issue.\n\nBut, then he brings up he has trust issues. He doesn’t only mean emotionally trusting someone, he means establishing a financial trust for people in his family and close to him. So for the people he has pushed out of the trust, he has cutting them out of his life financially and emotionally.", "annotation_id": "35795352", "url": "https://genius.com/35795352", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "And my dick so hard it make the metal detector go off", "vault_lines": ["And my dick so hard it make the metal detector go off"], "meaning": "He is so hard (like metal) so it makes the metal detector go off", "annotation_id": "35668022", "url": "https://genius.com/35668022", "state": "pending", "votes": -10, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Not complicated, it’s simple", "vault_lines": ["Not complicated, it's simple"], "meaning": "Referring to what he needs to get done it’s straightforward. Also a play on to the type of watches he prefers, a complication just makes the watch more difficult than it has to be compared to a simple plain jane. In the same verse it’s stated he needs to just take his “time”.", "annotation_id": "35344007", "url": "https://genius.com/35344007", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Look, just understand that I’m on a roll like Cottonelle I was made for all of this shit", "vault_lines": ["Look, just understand that I'm on a roll like Cottonelle", "I was made for all of this shit", "I'm getting paid for all of this shit"], "meaning": "“Cottonelle” is an American brand of toilet paper (rolls) produced by Kimberly-Clark. The company has made several different toilet paper types and are currently sold in the United States and Australia under the Kleenex brand. Cottonelle is marketed as “Andrex” in the UK and Republic of Ireland, “Baby Soft” in South Africa, and marketed in Italy as “Scottonelle.” \"", "annotation_id": "25429379", "url": "https://genius.com/25429379", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "From the A to Toronto, we let the metal go off", "vault_lines": ["From the A to Toronto, we let the metal go off"], "meaning": "That’s a reference to the fact that even if they come from different places- Drake is from Toronto, Canada, while 2 Chainz is from Atlanta- they can still coexist and make great music. That saying was later used by Tee Grizzley and Lil Yatchy on “From the D to the A”", "annotation_id": "18771195", "url": "https://genius.com/18771195", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "[Produced by KeY Wane]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The drums and snares used by Key Wane here brings the beat to a start. With kicks and horns used later, it brings it to near perfection. But then he really sent this home with the track’s 2 samples:\n\n\n“My Man” is used as a vocal sample, slowed down with a slightly deeper tone that fits into the beat very well. You can catch it in the back starting at 0:39 and it continues throughout this track. The exact sample is said by singer Abbey Lincoln at 0:19 all the way back in 1957:\n\n\n\nhttp://youtu.be/5AdEUekAoIM?t=17s\n\n\nThe second sample is more recent than the last. It is called “Ol' Time Killin'” from Kardinal Offishall. The exact sample starts at the beginning in the original, but in this track Wane brings it in at 4:34.\n\n\n\nWatch Key Wane break down the beat himself here:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RytwTdntLGA", "annotation_id": "2626429", "url": "https://genius.com/2626429", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Okay, made a million off a dinner fork, watch me switch it up", "vault_lines": ["Okay, made a million off a dinner fork, watch me switch it up"], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5236769626001", "annotation_id": "2264128", "url": "https://genius.com/2264128", "state": "verified", "votes": 231, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "[Verse 4: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse was removed from the album version of the song, due to the production on this section having an uncleared Kardinal Offishall sample.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dMW6EW5hDcI", "annotation_id": "2213803", "url": "https://genius.com/2213803", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "I learn from what I’ve been through", "vault_lines": ["I learn from what I've been through"], "meaning": "i learn lessons everyday… one of them is never shit where you eat.", "annotation_id": "2107523", "url": "https://genius.com/2107523", "state": "verified", "votes": 549, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "[Verse 3: Big Sean]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/Royceda59/status/362826216677457920\n\nBig Sean originally wanted the Key Wane beat for his own album, “Hall of Fame.” Sean explained what went down:\n\n“I had done a verse to [Key Wayne’s beat] and didn’t know [Drake] did a song to it,” Sean said. “I sent him the verse and [Drake’s] like, ‘You bodied this shit.’ Then we had the song forever. He was like, ‘I already did my verse and 2 Chainz did his verse, but we’ve gotta get the right chorus,’ and that took a while. But everything happened at the right time.”", "annotation_id": "2054984", "url": "https://genius.com/2054984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 95, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Smoking on that kush all in our section Like it’s legalized", "vault_lines": ["Smoking on that kush all in our section"], "meaning": "They’re still at the Diamonds in Miami smoking Kush. Drake knows that marijuana legalization has not passed yet in Florida, but he takes the risk of a small fine and smokes at the club anyways like it is legal.\n\nKush is an expensive, high quality strain of weed that is characterized by its shallow buds. Among other things, it helps Drake shape the lines in his head.", "annotation_id": "2054251", "url": "https://genius.com/2054251", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "You need that work, I got that work, got bitches in my condo", "vault_lines": ["You need that work, I got that work, got bitches in my condo", "Got everything, I got everything"], "meaning": "Chainz has apparently learned a thing or two from his Down South predecessors in flossing, the Big Tymers:", "annotation_id": "2053698", "url": "https://genius.com/2053698", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Y’all don’t fuck with us, then we don’t fuck with y’all It’s no different over on this side", "vault_lines": ["Goddamn, ain't no wishing over on this side", "Y'all don't fuck with us, then we don't fuck with y'all", "It's no different over on this side"], "meaning": "Possible reference to what Lil Wayne said during an explosive Miami Heat courtside beef in 2010. After Weezy was rooting for the Lakers, he told Dwyane Wade:\n\nY'all don’t fuck with me, so I don’t fuck with y'all\n\nAnd Wade allegedly responded, “we don’t”.\n\nDon’t get this confused with Drake not being cool with the Miami Heat, because he is actually very cool with them.", "annotation_id": "2053640", "url": "https://genius.com/2053640", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "I plead the fifth, drink a fifth, load the nine", "vault_lines": ["I plead the fifth, drink a fifth, load the nine"], "meaning": "To plead the fifth amendment means to refuse to answer a question so as to avoid incriminating yourself.\n\nA fifth is a standard sized bottle of heavy liquor – such as a fifth of vodka.\n\n\n\nLoading the 9 refers to a 9 millimeter handgun.\n\nNumbers and measurements are a common theme through these lines, which reinforces the line about careful he is. This care is contrasted with drinking a shit-ton(also a measurement) of alcohol and operating a weapon.", "annotation_id": "2053569", "url": "https://genius.com/2053569", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Daddy was in jail, we was talking through the window Like a motherfucking drive-thru", "vault_lines": ["Daddy was in jail, we was talking through the window", "Like a motherfucking drive-thru"], "meaning": "While Drake’s dad was spending jail time he was only allowed to talk to him through a window, just like a drive-through at a fast food restaurant.\n\n\n\nDrake has spoken on his dad’s incarceration before:\n\n“My dad went to jail for, like, a year on – I don’t even know what he was in jail for – an assault charge or a drug charge or something.\n\nHe also spoke about this also in an interview for Rolling Stone.", "annotation_id": "2053519", "url": "https://genius.com/2053519", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Smoke a half, need a zip", "vault_lines": ["Leave you split, in the half, smoke a half, need a zip"], "meaning": "Sean is known to be a big time smoker, and he is not afraid to let others know. He has written songs about it such as ‘High’, and ‘RWT’.\n\nA half ounce, or 14 grams of weed wasn’t enough. Sean needs the whole zip (ounce).", "annotation_id": "2053517", "url": "https://genius.com/2053517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Givenchy, nigga, God bless you", "vault_lines": ["Givenchy, nigga, God bless you"], "meaning": "To his broke antagonists, Givenchy, a luxury clothing brand, sounds like nothing more than a sneeze. If you’re broke, there’s no need to know about it since you can’t even afford it.\n\n\n\nThe way 2 Chainz delivers the word “Givenchy” in this line is meant to sound like a sneeze.\n\nAlso you can buy a Givenchy shirt with the overprint: “God bless Givenchy Paris”.", "annotation_id": "2053512", "url": "https://genius.com/2053512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 167, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "I’m the type to have a bullet-proof condom And still gotta pull out", "vault_lines": ["I'm the type to have a bullet-proof condom", "And still gotta pull out"], "meaning": "Sean’s mentor/friend Kanye mentioned his own “bullet proof condoms” back on Watch the Throne. Of course, Kanye’s condoms aren’t so bullet proof anymore.\n\n\n\nBig Sean is also referring to the trust issues that he mentions in the previous line. Even if he had a bullet-proof condom, he would still pull out because he wouldn’t trust the condom’s integrity. Perhaps this is hinting at the idea that he has too much to lose if he got a girl pregnant.\n\nAdditionally, this line may also refer to his perception of the strength of his sperm, and can be seen as him flaunting his virility. A bullet-proof condom would not be enough to stop a girl from getting pregnant because he has strong swimmers.", "annotation_id": "2053482", "url": "https://genius.com/2053482", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "I’m the light-skinned Keith Sweat, I’ma make it last forever", "vault_lines": ["I'm the light-skinned Keith Sweat, I'ma make it last forever"], "meaning": "This picture of Keith Sweat lets you know just how high Drake is setting the bar for himself.\n\n\n\nSweat is a New Jack Swing crooner and Drake makes a reference to his song “Make It Last Forever,” though the Sweat song was about sex lasting a while and here Drake wants his success to last\n\nSweat’s being name-dropped all over the place these days, including having 2 tracks named after him:\n\n\n\n“Fuckin' Problems” by A$AP Rocky\n\n“OK: by Mac Miller\n\n“Keith Sweat” by Chuck Inglish\n\n“Keith Sweat” by Lil B\nJ Cole’s verse on “Diamonds” by French Montana\n\n\n\nand that’s just in the past year!", "annotation_id": "2053463", "url": "https://genius.com/2053463", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "’Cause if we judging off your advances Then I just got paid like eight advances, goddamn", "vault_lines": ["Got everything, I got everything", "'Cause if we judging off your advances", "Then I just got paid like eight advances, goddamn", "Like I got trust issues"], "meaning": "Most likely a reference to the advance check he received from signing his record label OVO Sound to Warner Music Group. Reportedly, the deal is worth $10 million dollars, something Drake rapped about on “Us”:\n\nNew deal on the table look like bout like 10 milla\nShout my lawyer man he a contract killa", "annotation_id": "2053423", "url": "https://genius.com/2053423", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Ho, shut the fuck up!", "vault_lines": ["Ho, shut the fuck up!"], "meaning": "Sean brings the track to an absolute hype-line stop before going through with his verse, no better way to make an entrance.\n\nAccording to the artist himself, he came up with this opening line playing around.\n\nI’m having fun and that very first line — that’s what you feel like saying to some people. I don’t know how many times a day I roll around feeling like, ‘H—, shut the f—– up!’ That’s just real.", "annotation_id": "2053396", "url": "https://genius.com/2053396", "state": "accepted", "votes": 258, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "I swear to God I got 99 problems but a bitch ain’t one I got 99 problems, getting rich ain’t one", "vault_lines": ["Got everything, I got everything", "I swear to God I got 99 problems but a bitch ain't one", "I got 99 problems, getting rich ain't one"], "meaning": "This is a reference to JAY-Z’s 2003 hip hop anthem, “99 Problems,” the song itself borrows lyrics from Ice-T 1993 “99 Problems,” the chorus from both songs goes:\n\nIf you’re havin' girl problems, I feel bad for you, son\nI got 99 problems, but a bitch ain’t one\n\nSean doesn’t have a problem making money either, he’s been working all goddamn year, and it’s definitely paying off. He is already worth 2 million dollars, and the 25-year-old rapper also has a highly anticipated album coming out later this summer called Hall of Fame, which will only add to his fortune.\n\nIt’s also noteworthy that Sean is signed to Roc Nation, Jay Z’s entertainment company that houses a record label.", "annotation_id": "2053385", "url": "https://genius.com/2053385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "My new girl is on Glee and shit Probably making more money than me and shit", "vault_lines": ["My new girl is on Glee and shit", "Probably making more money than me and shit", "Oh me, oh me, oh my"], "meaning": "At the time of this song’s release, Big Sean was dating the famous actress, Naya Rivera who played Santana Lopez on Glee. Given Glee’s success, it’s entirely possible that Rivera could outweigh Sean’s stacks. Also note the reference to another popular Fox show, New Girl.\n\nNaya Rivera then flipped this line after their breakup to accuse Sean of stealing a Rolex in a tweet but quickly deleted it.", "annotation_id": "2053360", "url": "https://genius.com/2053360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 164, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Oh me, oh me, oh my", "vault_lines": ["Oh me, oh me, oh my"], "meaning": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O-W6IEGaNtw\n\nBoth this line and the beat change during this verse echo the 2001 song “Ol' Time Killin'” from Canadian rapper Kardinal Offishall, also born and raised in Toronto. Coincidence? We think not.\n\nFun fact: At around 1:54 of “Ol' Time Killin,” you’ll hear a sample of “Dust a Sandboy” which should sound very familiar if you’re a Kanye West fan. Coincidence? Yes, probably.", "annotation_id": "2053321", "url": "https://genius.com/2053321", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "J’s on, pinky ring,", "vault_lines": ["J's on, pinky ring,"], "meaning": "He’s got his jewelry, notably his pinky ring, on, as usual.\n\n\n\n2 Chainz is known to toss thousands for his sneakers. This time, he is wearing his J’s (Jordan’s) – despite his Adidas endorsement. It isn’t common to see 2 Chainz wearing Jordans – he speaks about his sneaker preference in this interview.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7CAycRSR7P4", "annotation_id": "2053211", "url": "https://genius.com/2053211", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "I think I done fucked too many women from the 305", "vault_lines": ["I think I done fucked too many women from the 305"], "meaning": "305 is the area code for Miami, the home of the Young Money Cash Money Billionaires imprint of Cash Money Records, to which he is signed", "annotation_id": "2053206", "url": "https://genius.com/2053206", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "And my latest shit is like a greatest hits", "vault_lines": ["And my latest shit is like a greatest hits"], "meaning": "This is a fair assessment.  As of late, Drake seems to have been dropping some really amazing tracks: “Started From The Bottom”, “Versace”, “5AM in Toronto” and “The Motion”.\n\n\n\nKanye once made a similar boast:\n\nRight when magazines wrote Kanye West off\nI dropped my new shit, it sound like a ‘best of’", "annotation_id": "2053194", "url": "https://genius.com/2053194", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "In the same league, but we don’t ball the same", "vault_lines": ["In the same league, but we don't ball the same"], "meaning": "Chainz uses a basketball metaphor with “league” and “ball”. You may be trying to do the same thing as Chainz, but you’re not nearly as successful. He’s on that upper echelon.\n\n2 Chainz knows about balling first hand, he actually played high school ball for North Clayton, and went on to play division 1 college ball for Alabama State.\n\nIn the clip below, he is #21 and the announcer calls him by his real name, which is Tauheed Epps.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c6rKEhod24o", "annotation_id": "2053190", "url": "https://genius.com/2053190", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "I even fucked the girl that used to babysit But that was years later on some crazy shit", "vault_lines": ["I even fucked the girl that used to babysit", "But that was years later on some crazy shit"], "meaning": "Shortly after this song’s release, Drake sat down with Chelsea Handler on her late night show Chelsea Lately, and he told a story about an older woman who he was infatuated with when he was about 15. Drake did say “years later” from when he was 6 years old, so this could be what he is referring to", "annotation_id": "2053178", "url": "https://genius.com/2053178", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "Damon Wayans, homie don’t play that shit", "vault_lines": ["Damon Wayans, homie don't play that shit"], "meaning": "Referring to the previous line, Drake hates the rapper’s new shit, he’s saying that he literally won’t play his music.\n\nDrizzy continues the In Living Color references here.  “Homie don’t play that” is the catchphrase of Homie D. Clown, also played by Wayans.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_QhuBIkPXn0#t=1m01s\n\nDrake also used a similar line on Rick Ross’s “Made Men”", "annotation_id": "2053173", "url": "https://genius.com/2053173", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "I touched down in ’86, knew I was the man by the age of six", "vault_lines": ["I touched down in '86, knew I was the man by the age of six"], "meaning": "Drake was born on October 24, 1986 at St. Michael’s hospital in Toronto, Canada.", "annotation_id": "2053143", "url": "https://genius.com/2053143", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "line": "[Intro: Aziz Ansari] I’m really stepping up my game These bitches gotta start paying me for this Can’t get no more free Randy", "vault_lines": ["I'm really stepping up my game", "These bitches gotta start paying me for this", "Can't get no more free Randy", "Oh me, oh me, oh my"], "meaning": "This is a line from Raaaaaaaandy, Aziz Ansari’s character in the Judd Apatow comedy Funny People:\n\nI’m really stepping up my game. These bitches gotta start paying me for this. Can’t get no more free Randy. I just fucked that crowd in the ass. Yeah.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QYduKTRtXHE\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/statuses/362790543727661056\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/362825049243254784\n\nAs to why he put it in the song? Maybe to emphasize that he just happened to be able to make money off of his widely popular persona, much like Raaaaaaaaaaaaaaandy.\n\nAlternatively, this could be a reference to Drake’s music: he’s just released a series of free tracks and now that his album is about to come out, folks gotta pay for that music.\n\nThis sample along with everything after Big Sean’s verse was removed from the album version of the song, possibly stemming from clearance issues.", "annotation_id": "2053137", "url": "https://genius.com/2053137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/6557cf1d8660623b66f6a6017d75370c.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"All Me\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>All MeDrake4.2M", "status": "done", "targets": 32, "clicked": 32, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-all-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "All Me"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "[Chorus: Timbaland]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Didn’t Method Man sing this?", "annotation_id": "40375880", "url": "https://genius.com/40375880", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Like I didn’t study the game to the letter", "vault_lines": ["Like I didn't study the game to the letter"], "meaning": "The letter being T", "annotation_id": "37644001", "url": "https://genius.com/37644001", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Like I should alert niggas when I’m ‘bout to drop somethin’ crazy", "vault_lines": ["Like I should alert niggas when I'm 'bout to drop somethin' crazy"], "meaning": "Maybe it’s a coincidence…maybe it’s not, but Drake’s next album “If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late” was released February 13, 2015 without a prior announcement.", "annotation_id": "28669509", "url": "https://genius.com/28669509", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Cake, cake-cake, cake-cake, uh", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This and the line prior are also a nod to frequent collaborator Rihanna’s track, Birthday Cake.", "annotation_id": "27799875", "url": "https://genius.com/27799875", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Get ’em a red nose, they clown cakes", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A clown is an entertainer that is known to dress up is numerous costumes. Most typical clown costumes comes with a red nose.\n.\n\n\n\nClowns typically have the connotation of being humorous and should not be taken seriously. Here Jay-Z is saying that others are just clowns in the industry and are not real threats to him. He is saying that he does not take them seriously and they might as well wear red noses because he thinks of them as a clown", "annotation_id": "22862101", "url": "https://genius.com/22862101", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Overly focused, it’s far from the time to rest now", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "in this verse, Drake may be talking about how he dont got time for any breaks and needs one pretty bad. Or he could be talking about how he is grinding in the music industry. Most think he just stays focused and never gives up hope and never ever gives up.", "annotation_id": "22725172", "url": "https://genius.com/22725172", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "My eyes bloodshot, but my jet don’t lag", "vault_lines": ["My eyes bloodshot, but my jet don't lag"], "meaning": "In commercial aviation, a red-eye flight is a flight scheduled to depart at night and arrive the next morning. The term “red-eye” derives from the symptom of having red eyes, which can be caused by fatigue, HOV has red eyes (bloodshot) but its not because of a flight….   (its cause hes been smoking that shit!)\n\nthis is a crazy play on words.", "annotation_id": "19710061", "url": "https://genius.com/19710061", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "[Verse 3: JAY-Z]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the first part of this verse , Jay Z uses an epistrophe to accentuate the ubiquity of “cakes” which represent the huge amount of money that Jay Z has earned since entering the rap game.", "annotation_id": "9030736", "url": "https://genius.com/9030736", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Shout out to World Wide Wes Everywhere we go, we leave a worldwide mess", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is me, Wes and Suh. I call him “Uncle Wes” though rather than his more famous moniker used by Jay-Z here.", "annotation_id": "2247556", "url": "https://genius.com/2247556", "state": "verified", "votes": 226, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Only real music is gonna last All that other bullshit is here today and gone tomorrow", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the original track, Jimmy Smith actually says\n\n“Jazz is the only real music that’s gonna last…”\n\nFor this song, it is edited out to endorse Drake as someone that is making music that will last.\n\nThis was originally sampled by Count Bass D back in 2002.", "annotation_id": "2227654", "url": "https://genius.com/2227654", "state": "accepted", "votes": 151, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "[Part I: Pound Cake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The production on this portion of the track is highly minimalistic, yet intricate in every way imaginable. The 94 bpm instrumental emphasizes beautifully placed samples; making for one hell of an outro song.\n\nThe main vocal sample comes from pop sensation Ellie Goulding’s “Don’t Say a Word.” The sample:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZYB1GV4KIFE\n\nhttps://twitter.com/elliegoulding/status/382095637145456640\n\nAccompanied by smooth chords and a clean but powerful combination of a booming kick and crisp snare, this sample really carries the beat and the song in general.\n\nOn the hook, there is a scratched sample of what appears to be Timbaland reciting the hook from Wu-Tang Clan’s classic song “C.R.E.A.M.”. This sample brings an old-school element to a new-school beat, furthering Drake’s well-thought-out musical style.\n\nBurnett and Evans discuss their role in Pound Cake’s production here http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tQYj2IiTgWk", "annotation_id": "2217141", "url": "https://genius.com/2217141", "state": "accepted", "votes": 160, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "You know, years ago they had the A&R men to tell you what to play, how to play it and you know, whether it’s disco and rock But, uh, we just went in the studio and we did it", "vault_lines": ["And we just laid back and did it"], "meaning": "The A&R men (Artists and Repertoire) are middlemen between the record label and the artists. They oversee talent scouting, album production, etc.\n\nMany record labels can be very controlling about what they want an artist to sound like, but this is rarely the case for a well-established rapper like Drizzy.\n\nThey’re happy that they could just go in the studio and do their thing without being told what to do.", "annotation_id": "2216243", "url": "https://genius.com/2216243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "[Intro: Jimmy Smith]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The intro is sampled from Jimmy Smith. The track is called “Jimmy Smith Rap” and is from his Off the Top album released in 1982.\n\nChristian Rich is a producer/DJ duo who did the sampling.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/christianrich/statuses/379667176732778497\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn 2014, Drake was sued by Jimmy’s estate for $300,000 for lying about getting permission to use the sample on the song. In June 2017, Drake won the lawsuit due to the fact that the content Drake used was transformative, and there was no liability for copyright infringement.", "annotation_id": "2216206", "url": "https://genius.com/2216206", "state": "accepted", "votes": 269, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "I’ve done made more millionaires than the lotto did Dame made millions, Biggs made millions Ye made millions, Just made millions Lyor made millions, Cam made millions", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Just” = Just Blaze. As a team member of The Combat Jack Show, I’m just happy that my dude got a BIG shout out from Jay Z. No major annotation, just proud of my dude is all. Ok. Bye now.", "annotation_id": "2215969", "url": "https://genius.com/2215969", "state": "verified", "votes": 186, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "And did that shit ‘cause it’s somethin’ to do Yeah, I guess that’s just who I became, dawg", "vault_lines": ["And did that shit 'cause it's somethin' to do", "Yeah, I guess that's just who I became, dawg"], "meaning": "When you have as much money as Drake does, sometimes you tend to spend money just to spend it, without any reasonable cause.\nDrake’s ‘Crew Love’ contains similar lyrics:\n\n“And really, I think I like who I’m becoming\nThere’s times where I might do it just to do it like it’s nothing”\n\nDrake seems more positive on ‘Crew Love’, admitting he’s liking himself better (at the time ‘Take Care’ dropped, that is), whereas now he is reminiscent of his past-self and contemplating how nothing was the same", "annotation_id": "2215487", "url": "https://genius.com/2215487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "I text her from time to time, she a mom now I guess sometimes life forces us to calm down I told her she could live with me if she need to, I got a compound But I think she’s straight", "vault_lines": ["But I think she's straight", "I text her from time to time, she a mom now", "I guess sometimes life forces us to calm down", "I told her she could live with me if she need to, I got a compound"], "meaning": "This girl Drake used to be with has a kid now, so her new life responsibilities mean she has to calm down.\n\nThe girl Drake talks about is probably Paris Morton, who recently gave birth to a son named Carter.\n\nDrake cares so much about her, he’s willing to put her up (and he’s got space), but she doesn’t need him to.\n\n\n\nHe’s got a habit for texting those old flames.\n\nThis might also be a reference to his song “From Time”, which contains a similar line about texting:\n\nPassive aggressive when we’re textin', I feel the distance\n\nDue to the similarity of lines, Drake could also be referring to Courtney. The song placement almost could be a timeframe showing the time that’s past, to the time that she now has a baby.", "annotation_id": "2215132", "url": "https://genius.com/2215132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Couldn’t believe when he called me You never know what could happen to you", "vault_lines": ["Couldn't believe when he called me", "You never know what could happen to you"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne literally called Drake up while he was listening to his mixtape. Next thing he knew, he was flying to Houston to meet him, and nothing was the same.\n\nIt seems so far-fetched, you never think it could happen, but it could happen to anyone out here trying to live their dreams.", "annotation_id": "2215108", "url": "https://genius.com/2215108", "state": "accepted", "votes": 121, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "These are usually just some thoughts that I would share with myself But I thought ”Fuck it”, it’s worth it to share ’em With someone else other than Paris for once", "vault_lines": ["These are usually just some thoughts that I would share with myself", "But I thought \"Fuck it\", it's worth it to share 'em", "With someone else other than Paris for once"], "meaning": "Drake normally keeps his thoughts to himself and Paris. That’s Paris, as mentioned before in “Paris Morton Music.”\n\n\n\nHe’s saying “YOLO” and decides to spill it all out on this track.", "annotation_id": "2215096", "url": "https://genius.com/2215096", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Gucci airbag just in case we crash", "vault_lines": ["Gucci airbag just in case we crash"], "meaning": "Hov doesn’t deal with regular airbags, he got his cars equipped with Gucci airbags. Seems like Jay’s insurance is going to have to cough up a lot money if he does crash.\n\nThe Gucci bag might be the bag they have with them in case they do not go home after the club, but crash at your place. All because ‘last night was mad trill’.\n\nIn addition, “Gucci air bag” could refer to a buffer in case he loses a bunch of money (stock crash)–he still has a lot of material things that he could sell to get back the money.", "annotation_id": "2214915", "url": "https://genius.com/2214915", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Look, fuck all that “Happy to be here” shit that y’all want me on", "vault_lines": ["Look, fuck all that \"Happy to be here\" shit that y'all want me on"], "meaning": "Using the common chitchat of talk shows, he’s saying he’s outgrown the obligation to feel thankful for his position, and expects people to start respecting his success to be his own. He earned every penny and every fan through his hard work and talent, in other words. He dedicated a whole track on the album just to drive the point home.\n\nNo More free Randy", "annotation_id": "2214654", "url": "https://genius.com/2214654", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "[Part II: Paris Morton Music 2]", "vault_lines": ["Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2 Lyrics"], "meaning": "Sequel to Paris Morton Music.\n\nThe switch from Pound Cake to Paris Morton Music 2 comes at the 4:10 mark of the song.\n\nAccording to the description of this video:\n\nOh and in case you were wondering, Boi-1da, Matthew Burnett and Jordan Evans did not produce the “Paris Morton Music 2” at the end of “Pound Cake.” Jordan says that was Detail.", "annotation_id": "2214632", "url": "https://genius.com/2214632", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "You know it’s real when you are who you think you are", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Through the ride of fame and all its deceptions, it takes everything to maintain a clear mind, and if you’re still sane and devoid of delusion after all of it, then it doesn’t get any realer.\n\nSimilar to what he said on “The Ride”: “You know it’s real when your latest nights are your greatest nights.” You know your life is real when you’ve become what you imagined. Drake is a fan of the “law of attraction”: the fact that he is exactly who thought he was gonna be in the past really amazes him.\n\nOf course, you also know it’s real when you tat your name on it!", "annotation_id": "2214605", "url": "https://genius.com/2214605", "state": "accepted", "votes": 125, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "’Cause she supported since Hot Beats Right before Wayne came and got me", "vault_lines": ["'Cause she supported since Hot Beats", "Right before Wayne came and got me"], "meaning": "Hot Beats is a recording studio in Atlanta, GA where Drizzy had his humble beginnings, before So Far Gone and his record deal. The girl’s been there since, not just for the ride.\n\nWhen I was in Hot Beats in Atlanta steady giving Daps\nFetching niggas drinks but I never got to rap\n\nLil Wayne was the one who brought Drake into the rap scene. He discovered Drake’s youtube mixtapes and offered him a record deal along on YMCM. The girl that he’s with has stuck with him since before he was famous.", "annotation_id": "2214164", "url": "https://genius.com/2214164", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "El Gran Santo on the mantle", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "El Gran Santo (The Great Saint) either refers to Chango/Santa Barbara as in the previous line or Santa Muerte. Many who swear by Santa Muerte’s protection refer to her as the great saint or El Gran Santo.\n\n\n\nDrug traffickers have adopted this saint as the protector of the trade. Santa Muerte is typically worshiped in private and has caught a negative reputation because the only private shrines in the media are the one in the raided homes of drug traffickers. It may be an allusion for Jay Z in his drug dealing days.\n\nAs seen in Breaking Bad:", "annotation_id": "2214036", "url": "https://genius.com/2214036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "International Hov, that’s my handle", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jay’s never been too concerned with social media, so he plays off the mention here to say, if you really want to know what his (Twitter, IG) handle is, it’s international. He’s everywhere.\n\n(His actual handle is @S_C_)\n\nA “handle” is also a term used in NASDAQ: it’s the whole-dollar price that is actually offered/acquired for a stock trade. This is about big business and all kinds of currency: dollar, yen, etc. If Jay Z was a stock, his handle would have to be International Hov. He’s getting money everywhere!\n\nInternational Hov is one of Jay’s many monikers as he’s made clear on several songs before:\n\n“Don’t You Know?”\n“Do You Wanna Ride?”\n“Beware”\n\nThis is one of many self-proclaimed titles, check out all of them here!", "annotation_id": "2214024", "url": "https://genius.com/2214024", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Out of the back room Where I was rapping with Jas over beats that I shouldn’t have In the hopes for the glory", "vault_lines": ["out of the back room", "Where I was rapping with Jas over beats that I shouldn't have", "In the hopes for the glory"], "meaning": "Drake was rapping with Jas Prince over “A Milli” beat before Wayne. Later Jas played Drake’s freestyle (off his mixtape) for Lil Wayne and that’s what got Drake the famous Wayne phone call. Drake got that call from Lil Wayne while he was at the barber shop getting his hair cut. He was caught completely off guard by the call.", "annotation_id": "2214013", "url": "https://genius.com/2214013", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Nothing was the same, dawg", "vault_lines": ["Nothing was the same, dawg"], "meaning": "It’s traditional for Drake to end his albums with a line that contains the title to the album. He first did this on  Thank Me Later’s final track, “Thank Me Now”:\n\nIt’s okay, you could thank me now\n\nHe did it again on Take Care’s final track, “The Ride”:\n\nTake care, nigga", "annotation_id": "2213994", "url": "https://genius.com/2213994", "state": "accepted", "votes": 235, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Niggas is frontin’, that’s upside-down cake", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "When baking an upside-down cake, you put stuff on the bottom that you can’t see until it’s turned out.\n\nHov is using this as a metaphor for “frontin'” because there’s a facade they put up that nobody can see behind until push comes to shove (the cake is revealed).\n\n\n\nThis can also be wordplay over the “front in”; when you bake the upside down cake, you have to put the front or top of the cake inside the pan.", "annotation_id": "2213797", "url": "https://genius.com/2213797", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "They shoulda never let you ’round cake", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Round cake / AROUND cake. Those clowns shouldn’t be around his kind of money.\n\nDoubles as a reference to a type of cake that most of us are familiar with; cakes that are round in shape.\n\n\n\nOn Watch the Throne, Jay references The Chappelle Show by saying “fuck your awards like Eddie Murphy’s couch”\n\nHere it sounds as though Jay might be slightly referencing the same episode, where Rick James yells “they never shoulda gave you niggas money!” …“they shoulda never let you round cake”\n\nJay does feel as though many of these rappers are ignorant and don’t know how to handle themselves around money.", "annotation_id": "2213758", "url": "https://genius.com/2213758", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Yeah Uh, after hours at Il Mulino Or Sotto Sotto, just talkin’ women and vino", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake spends his time at high-class restaurants in Toronto sipping wine (“vino” is the Italian translation), like Il Mulino in Upper Forest Hill or Sotto Sotto Trattoria located in the posh Yorkville neighborhood.\n\nDrake and his good friends stop by Sotto Sotto when in the city and discuss exactly what you’d expect him to over great food.\n\nUnfortunately for Drake, on Christmas Day 2014 Sotto Sotto burned down. Guess he’ll have to go somewhere else to talk women and vino…", "annotation_id": "2213577", "url": "https://genius.com/2213577", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "I just might learn to speak Mandarin Japanese for the yen that I’m handlin’", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "It looks like Jiggaman is having a “pivot” of his own! With over 882 million speakers, Mandarin Chinese is the most popular language in the world. Learning to speak this language would definitely be a good idea, from a business perspective.\n\n\n\nAs a polished international businessman, Jay knows the importance of the yen (official currency of Japan) for his many business ventures.\n\nMultimillionaires and billionaires invest their money into economies of other countries in case the primary country their money is invested in fails. Jay has so much money that he is investing some into the Japanese economy.", "annotation_id": "2213443", "url": "https://genius.com/2213443", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "My classmates, they went on to be chartered accountants Or work with their parents But thinkin’ back on how they treated me My high school reunion might be worth an appearance", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is thinking about his peers from school (he went to Forest Hill CI). They are all doing their own thing now, likely working a normal job 9-5. A “chartered accountant.” It is the Canadian equivalent of the Certified Public Accountant (CPA) in the US.\n\nHe doesn’t doubt though that he crosses their mind sometimes. He alludes to the fact that some of them may have treated him badly, but now that he’s made it, they probably regret it.\n\nHe’s so big time that he probably shouldn’t even waste his time going to his high school reunion, but he is considering going just to belittle them with his presence.\n\n\n\nForest Hill Collegiate Institute – Drake’s high school", "annotation_id": "2213405", "url": "https://genius.com/2213405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 220, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "The homie said, “Hov, it ain’t many of us” I told ’em, “Less is more, nigga, it’s plenty of us”", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The new Jean-Michel knows his concepts. Less is more for minimalist design, is about arranging components to create an impression of extreme simplicity.\n\n\n\nThere are only a few people at the top, but Hov has enough people from his own circle there with him.\n\nHov might be implying that since there are less of them, there will be more money, as they split it up between each other. So the fewer the people, the more the money…“it’s plenty of us”.\n\nThis also implies he doesn’t need a big entourage. At this point, he pretty much just needs Be and Blue.", "annotation_id": "2213401", "url": "https://genius.com/2213401", "state": "accepted", "votes": 127, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "A pair of Jordan 3's, tryna chase this cash", "vault_lines": ["A pair of Jordan 3's, tryna chase this cash"], "meaning": "Jay Z is a big fan of the Air Jordan III and rocks them on the regular. He’s running in them, and literally putting in work:\n\n\n\nJordan 3s were originally released in ‘88, which coincides with this line from The Takeover. Wearing the Jordan 3s is symbolic to the longevity of Jay’s paper chase!", "annotation_id": "2213385", "url": "https://genius.com/2213385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Case y’all didn’t know, I speak Spanish too, uh", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "We know, Hovito.\n\nThis may be a nod to Beyoncé’s Spanish version of “Irreplaceable”. In other words, he’s saying, “My wife isn’t the only one who speaks Spanish. I can speak it too!”", "annotation_id": "2213381", "url": "https://genius.com/2213381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "My saint’s Changó, light a candle", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jay is either referring to Catholicism or Santeria in this line and the following one.\n\nSanteria is a combination of Catholicism and Yoruba and is most likely the religion that is being referred to in these lines. Changó/Shango is the greatest saint in the Yoruba religion. Shango’s Santeria representation is Santa Barbara. The Santa Barbara statue is who one would pray to in an altar for Shango. Jay apparently has one on his mantle.\n\n\n\nCatholics light candle wicks to pray to former saints, but Hov lights sparklers on the French luxury champagne Moët et Chandon (Sean Don), all in the name of celebration.\n\n\n\nFun Fact: Shango/Santa Barbara Feast Day is on December 4th, Jay-Z’s birthday. Since Shango is the Yoruba god of fire, maybe he’s lighting Jay’s birthday candles.", "annotation_id": "2213375", "url": "https://genius.com/2213375", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "With the ink I could murder, word to my nigga Irv", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake kills his rhymes with his pen as he writes them.\nUsing an Inc/Ink homophone, saying how he now can make an impact businesswise.\nShout out to rap producer Irv Gotti, who founded Murda Inc. the home of acts like Ja Rule, Ashanti and Lloyd.", "annotation_id": "2213351", "url": "https://genius.com/2213351", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Look at my neck, I got a karat cake, uh", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jay plays off carrot cake with the unit of measure of the purity of gold alloy, “karats”. He’s certainly boasting many karats:", "annotation_id": "2213311", "url": "https://genius.com/2213311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Took a while, got the jokers out of the deck now", "vault_lines": ["out of the back room"], "meaning": "“You and your whole crew are like a deck a 54\nSo it’s obvious y'all gone steady be rolling with some jokers”\n\n\nBarry Bonds Freestyle\n\n\n\nSince the debate about who’s best is heating up, Drake has at least eliminated the jokers who shouldn’t even be in the running. He feels like he’s\n now holding the whole game.\n\nYou usually remove the Jokers from a new deck of cards, because they’re useless when playing most card games.", "annotation_id": "2213277", "url": "https://genius.com/2213277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "I’m the big homie, they still be tryna lil bro me, dog", "vault_lines": ["I'm the big homie, they still be tryna lil bro me, dog"], "meaning": "Drake has accomplished a lot of things even though he’s only on his third album. He’s the rapper with the most #1 singles and two double-Platinum albums. Yet some people still treat him like he’s a pup, probably because, despite his success, he’s only 26.\n\nThat’s the first time I’ve ever said that before. On this album I’m extremely aware of where I’m at and nobody can tell me differently. I’m the big homie too. As much as I do remain humble and stay cordial, don’t think I don’t know. I know what’s going on out here, I’m 26 – I’m out here in the streets with the people.\n\nBut Drake did admit that he offered this line in response to Hov’s first bar:\n\n“I think Jay still gets his first line off there. He always takes his opportunity to remind me that he is the true Big Homie. Sometimes I’ll set out and have something different in mind for Jay… but we always just end up rapping, I love rapping with that guy so much it’s tough to go in any different direction.", "annotation_id": "2213269", "url": "https://genius.com/2213269", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Cash rules everything around me, frikki-frikki-frik C.R.E.A.M.; get the-get the-get the-get the Get the-get the-get the-get the money, dollar-dollar D-d-d-d-dollar-dollar, dollar-dollar bill, y’all (Y’all, y’all)", "vault_lines": ["Get the-get the-get the-get the money, dollar-dollar"], "meaning": "This chorus interpolates the chorus from the classic song “C.R.E.A.M.” by the Wu-Tang-Clan.\n\n\n\nThe chorus stresses the importance of obtaining money, as opposed to spending it. It also originally showed youth that selling drugs was not a good way to make money, and that hard work and creativity would make more money, a goal Drake sympathizes with.\n\nThis is the second time that Drake pays homage to the Wu-Tang Clan on this album. He did it before with the song “Wu-Tang Forever.”", "annotation_id": "2213148", "url": "https://genius.com/2213148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 373, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Yes, still Roc La Familia", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Roc-A-Fella Records is a record label founded by Jay Z, Damon “Dame” Dash, and Kareem “Biggs” Burke in 1995.\n\nDespite the creation of Roc Nation in 2008, Jay remains true to his humble beginnings. La familia is still Roc.\n\nJay-Z is also saying “still rock the family”, basically stating that he is still rocking his family, supporting and representing them. He says part of this line in a foreign language because as you know, he speaks Spanish too…", "annotation_id": "2213118", "url": "https://genius.com/2213118", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Tables turn, bridges burn, you live and learn", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake utilizes two famous idioms to make his point:\n\n“The tables have turned”\n\nThere are numerous variations of this idiom but it mainly deals with reversed positions between adversaries, a turn of events that has reversed one’s plans for better or worse. This idiom is mainly used to refer to adversaries.\n\n“Burn bridges”\n\nThis idiom is usually phrased in a way such as “don’t burn bridges”, usually to warn against ruining relationships between friends or loved ones. Falling out with a loved one can burn a bridge connecting the two and makes it impossible to go back to how things were before. Unlike the first idiom, this one deals with those who are close.\n\nUsing those two phrases, Drake uses their contrasting moods and tones to emphasize a important life lesson:\n\n\n\nYou learn as much from your dark moments (falling out with friends and family) as you do from your highs (victories against obstacles, enemies, adversaries, etc). Either way, experiences are just part of life.\n\nThis probably has a real-life corollary in the infamous nightclub incident between Drake and Chris Brown. Tables in the club were turned there, burning all bridges between the pair.\n\nDrake back in the day – see for yourself how the tables have turned https://vimeo.com/68754435", "annotation_id": "2213050", "url": "https://genius.com/2213050", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "And do it major like the niggas who paved the way for us", "vault_lines": ["And do it major like the niggas who paved the way for us"], "meaning": "Drake is likely referring to Kanye on “We Major” where Ye says:\n\nCan I talk my shit again?\nCan I talk my shit again?!\n\nAfter all, Ye did pave the way for Drizzy. It was Kanye’s 808s and Heartbreak album that opened the world up to the rapper/singer sound that Drake implements in his music to this day.\n\nDrake also has the ability to be successful in hip hop without acting like he was a “street guy.” Drake commented on how Kanye “…made it OK to not necessarily be the most street dude.”", "annotation_id": "2213044", "url": "https://genius.com/2213044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "I’ve done made more millionaires than the lotto did Dame made millions, Biggs made millions Ye made millions, Just made millions Lyor made millions, Cam made millions", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jay’s introduced many people to the life of fame and fortune, though they haven’t always returned the favor. Even claiming he’s made more people millionaires than the lotto does.\n\n\n\nJust to clarify:\nDame Dash – one of the Roc-A-Fella founders.\nKareem “Biggs” Burke – one of the Roc-A-Fella founders.\nKanye West – Roc-A-Fella artist\nJust Blaze – Roc-A-Fella producer\nLyor Cohen – signed Roc-A-Fella Records during his Def Jam days.\nCam'ron – Former Roc-A-Fella artist", "annotation_id": "2213004", "url": "https://genius.com/2213004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 233, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "The all-black Maybach, but I’m not a racist Inside’s whiter than Katy Perry’s face is", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jay has an all-black Maybach, but don’t think he’s racist – he made sure the interior of the car is white. Possibly a reference to the nasty history of Maybach: “the black Maybach, I’m back inside the boat”?\n\nKaty got called a racist as well for her whitest face, when she dressed up as a Geisha. She also defended herself as not a racist to GQ.\n\n\n\nMight be the same Maybach from the “Otis” music video, which was later sold at auction.\n\n\n\nJay also mentioned the car on Rick Ross’s “Maybach Music.” Hov really loves his black Maybachs.", "annotation_id": "2212999", "url": "https://genius.com/2212999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "I’m holdin’ all the cards and niggas wanna play chess now", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake got rid of all the other irrelevant rappers (the joker cards are useless in most card games), and he’s holding all the other cards so rappers literally can’t make a move and can’t win.\n\nThey would rather try to change the game and play chess so they might have a chance at winning.\n\n\n\nPerhaps Pusha T responded to this with a diss?\n“We playing on a higher game of chess”", "annotation_id": "2212952", "url": "https://genius.com/2212952", "state": "accepted", "votes": 143, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "I’m authentic, real name, no gimmicks", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s full name is Aubrey Drake Graham, making his rap moniker technically his real name.\n\nDrake is also often honest about his relationships and personal life, most notably talking about lovers on Marvin’s Room,  talking about his Uncle and talking about his grandmother.\n\nA reference to Obie Trice, who rapped the phrase, “real name, no gimmicks.”\n\nThis line comes from the late D12 rapper Proof. As stated on the the annotation on Obie’s track “Rap Name”, the late mayor of Detroit (and best friend of Eminem) Proof first spoke it to Obie:\n\nObie Trice went by the name of Obie 1 (like Obi Wan Kenobi), but when he first met Proof, who was about to introduce him at the Hip Hop Shop, Proof asked him, “What’s your name? Your real name, no gimmicks.” He was then introduced as Obie Trice, and has kept his real name as his rap name ever since.", "annotation_id": "2212949", "url": "https://genius.com/2212949", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Uh, last night was mad trill I’m fresh out of Advil—Jesus, grab the wheel", "vault_lines": ["out of the back room", "Uh, last night was mad trill", "I'm fresh out of Advil—Jesus, grab the wheel"], "meaning": "This is a reference to these Kanye lines from “No Church in the Wild”:\n\nSunglasses and Advil\nLast night was mad real\n\nHere Jay depicts himself after a night of drinking and partying, so he needs the Advil to help him focus. As he slowly starts to feel the effects of his hangover creep back, he relies on Jesus (or religion) to take the wheel and ultimately save his life. This is interesting considering he decided to borrow a line from a song called “No Church in the Wild,” so maybe Jay wants to get back in touch with his religion. He made a similar line about driving and God on his most recent album.\n\n\n\nThis is an allusion to the phrase “Jesus, take the wheel,” meaning, Jesus, take control of my life, referenced by country singer Carrie Underwood and comedian Kevin Hart.\n\nThis is kind of a sequel to “Empire State of Mind”’s “Jesus can’t save you, life starts when the church ends,” where, now that “life” (partying) is over, Jay’s ready to go back to church.", "annotation_id": "2212945", "url": "https://genius.com/2212945", "state": "accepted", "votes": 171, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Beans’a tell you if he wasn’t in his feelings", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The relationship between Jay and former Roc-A-Fella records signee Beanie Sigel famously soured.\n\nJay insist that Beans made millions with the Roc, which is something Sigel might concede if he didn’t hold hard feelings against Hov. “Feelings” also reference Bean’s hit single “Feel It in the Air”.\n\nIt’s a point Hova has made before, mentioning it on “Monster” and dedicating the song “Why I Love” to the topic.\n\n\n\nBeans shared similar sentiments in an interview with XXL:\n\n\nWhatever I felt this dude Jay did wrong to me, it can’t outweigh the one thing he did do for me – he gave me an opportunity.\n\nA lot of people don’t get opportunities. That outweighs everything. I got caught in the moment and put my feelings out there. I should have never done that.”", "annotation_id": "2212942", "url": "https://genius.com/2212942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 174, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Yellow diamonds in my Jesús", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jay rocks a Jesus piece covered in yellow diamonds.\n\nYellow continues the color string began with the “I’m not a racist” line; after reaching out to Hispanics, the next line refers to Mandarin and Japanese (stereotypically connected to the color Yellow).\n\n\n\nA similar rhyme and pronunciation of Jesús was used in his previous Drake collaboration, 2009’s “Off That”:\n\nBlack diamonds in my Jesus piece, my God", "annotation_id": "2212938", "url": "https://genius.com/2212938", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Cake, cake-cake, cake-cake, cake Five hundred million, I got a pound cake", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A reference to both Rihanna’s “Birthday Cake” and Lupe Fiasco’s cake in “Light Blue”\n\nCake = money. Jay makes cake wherever he goes, whatever he does, as denoted by his repetition. He’s also noted to have a net worth of $500 million.\n\n\n\nJay starts a trend here of referring to actual types of cakes with this line about pound cakes, which is a double entendre referencing Jay’s past dealing drugs (pound = weight). In New York City, 500 dollars is a pound. Throwin' dice, “5” is a pound cake. Jay keeps the street slang but takes it up to the level he’s at.", "annotation_id": "2212930", "url": "https://genius.com/2212930", "state": "accepted", "votes": 186, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "I swear this guy Michael Rapino’s boosting my ego", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Michael Rapino is the CEO of Live Nation. Drake’s concerts have been doing incredibly well, Club Paradise Tour was the most successful hip-hop tour of 2012, grossing over $42 million worldwide. Rapino must be all in his ear pushing for more tours.", "annotation_id": "2212928", "url": "https://genius.com/2212928", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "The contract like ’91 Dan Marino", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "On Tuesday, August 20th, 1991, Dan Marino signed what was then the largest contract in NFL history. His deal with Dolphins was worth $23 million dollars over five years.\n\nDrake is earning even more than Marino (on a per year basis). And at the rate he’s going, he might spend $23 million at Miami’s King of Diamonds strip club.", "annotation_id": "2212900", "url": "https://genius.com/2212900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "line": "Uh, I had Benzes ’fore you had braces", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "As always, Jay’s ahead of new rappers. Even having a Mercedes Benz before they could afford braces.\n\nMany people have braces in their teens. This is a reference to the age difference between Jay and Drake. Jay was a millionaire before Drake was at the age to get braces. When this song was released, Jay Z was 43 to Drake’s 26, so this line is conceivably true.\n\n\n\nThere’s also a difference here of grills: Jay’s are on his car, while y'all’s are on your faces. The grill of Jay’s luxurious Benz is the first thing you see when he pulls up, like the braces are the first thing you see when you open your mouth.", "annotation_id": "2212869", "url": "https://genius.com/2212869", "state": "accepted", "votes": 162, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b55953177013ca20466401280f855a08.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2Drake3.9M", "status": "done", "targets": 55, "clicked": 55, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-pound-cake-paris-morton-music-2-lyrics", "genius_title": "Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "Gotta be real with it, ayy, yup", "vault_lines": ["Gotta be real with it, ayy, yup"], "meaning": "In the Music video, this line is restated as “I just gotta be real with it” As it was purposely cut off in streaming platforms.", "annotation_id": "38493242", "url": "https://genius.com/38493242", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, he bad", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a sample from Magnolia Shorty’s 2010 track, “Smoking Gun,” which in turn sampled Jadakiss' 2009 track of the same name. Magnolia’s version exemplifies an authentic New Orleans sound, known as Bounce, and the sample here also serves as homage to Magnolia Shorty, Drake’s former labelmate at Cash Money Records, who was murdered in 2010.\n\n\n\nMagnolia, right, standing with Cash Money founder and fellow New Orleans rapper, Birdman.\n\n“Trade” is New Orleans slang for men who’re handsome and have money.", "annotation_id": "15207694", "url": "https://genius.com/15207694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "Look, the new me is really still the real me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake may be repudiating critics' claims that he has “sold out”–both as an artist and as a father/partner–in his pursuit of commercial success.\n\nFollowing the controversial revelations elucidated in Pusha-T’s “The Story of Adidon,” Drake utilizes this first line to affirm his belief that his decisions are true to himself.", "annotation_id": "15085692", "url": "https://genius.com/15085692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "Skate and Smoke and Rap Now let me see you", "vault_lines": ["Skate and Smoke and Rap", "Now let me see you"], "meaning": "A sample from Lil Wayne’s 2012 “S.A.S.A.R.A.F.,” this lyric was initially not included in “In My Feelings” when Scorpion dropped. Previously, there was four seconds of silence between the chorus and the breakdown, before being updated on streaming services.\n\nProducer BlaqNmilD added this sample after Drake mentioned that it would’ve been nice to have gotten Wayne on the song.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/mAr9bX_38ms?t=100", "annotation_id": "15034493", "url": "https://genius.com/15034493", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "Two bad bitches and we kissin’ in the Wraith Kissin’-kissin’ in the Wraith, kiss-kissin’ in the Wraith", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A Wraith is a British luxury vehicle manufactured by Rolls-Royce Motor Cars. It starts at $320,000.\n\nSince the 2013 model released, many big-name rappers have been seen driving Wraiths, including Drake, who owns one in black.\nhttp://thenewswheel.com/wp-content/themes/patterns/timthumb.php?src=http://thenewswheel.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/12/Drakes-Rolls-Royce-Wraith.png&q=90&w=660&zc=1\n\nIn 2016, Birdman, Drake’s former boss, released a pointed video calling out rappers who buy Wraiths—saying the car is for ladies, in response to Rick Ross' claims of being the first with one.", "annotation_id": "14998712", "url": "https://genius.com/14998712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "I need that black card and the code to the safe Code to the safe, code-code to the safe-safe", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The American Express Centurion Card, informally known as the Black Card, is an invite only charge card.\n\n\n\n American Express only issues these cards to members after they have met a variety of criteria. The $7,500 initiation fee has been enough to make the Black Card a symbol of wealth and prosperity, as it offers members features such as 24/7 concierge service and an unlimited spend limit.", "annotation_id": "14981411", "url": "https://genius.com/14981411", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "(BlaqNmilD, you a genius, you diggin’ me?)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the producer tag for BlaqNmilD, who co-produced this song with TrapMoneyBenny. He also produced “Nice for What,” the other song on the Scorpion album influenced by New Orleans bounce—which is BlaqNmilD’s speciality.", "annotation_id": "14890642", "url": "https://genius.com/14890642", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "Kiki, do you love me? Are you riding? Say you’ll never ever leave from beside me ’Cause I want ya, and I need ya And I’m down for you always KB, do you love me? Are you riding? Say you’ll never ever leave from beside me ’Cause I want ya, and I need ya And I’m down for you always", "vault_lines": ["'Resha, do you love me? Are you riding?", "'Cause I want you, and I need you", "'Cause I want ya, and I–"], "meaning": "While some fans speculated that “Kiki” was singer Keshia Chanté, a credible source told Genius “Kiki” is actually model and social media personality K'yanna Barber (“KB”).\n\nDrake would go on to confirm this in July 2023, in a podcast with Bobbi Altoff:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/YrdQI3nqh7A?t=1987\n\nAlthough Barber has not directly acknowledged that she’s the Kiki in these lyrics, she tweeted about them, liked a tweet where a fan speculated they were referring to her, and even quoted the “KB” lyric in a tweet:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/4daloveofKJ/status/1016160146676781056", "annotation_id": "14882003", "url": "https://genius.com/14882003", "state": "accepted", "votes": 662, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "Bring that ass, bring that ass, bring that ass back B-bring that ass, bring that ass, bring that ass back Shawty say the nigga that she with can’t hit But shawty, I’ma hit it, hit it like I can’t miss", "vault_lines": ["Bring that ass, bring that ass, bring that ass back", "B-bring that ass, bring that ass, bring that ass back", "Shawty say the nigga that she with can't hit", "But shawty, I'ma hit it, hit it like I can't miss", "Clap that ass, clap-clap that ass", "Bring that ass back"], "meaning": "This is a sample of Lil Wayne’s 2008 hit “Lollipop.” It was his most commercially successful song, winning him a Grammy, and it went 4x Platinum.\n\nDrake sampled another of his label mates, Nicki Minaj, on “That’s How You Feel,” which appears two songs prior to this one on the Scorpion tracklist.", "annotation_id": "14881972", "url": "https://genius.com/14881972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "I buy you Champagne but you love some Henny", "vault_lines": ["I buy you Champagne but you love some Henny"], "meaning": "Hennessy, or Henny, is a cognac from France. It’s very popular in the African American community and countless rappers have mentioned it in their music (Drake rapped about it in 2016’s “One Dance” and 2012’s “HYFR”). Drake offers champagne, which is a symbol of sophistication and class, but Kiki and KB are more down to earth and from the block, so they prefer Henny.\n\n\n\nDrake also goes under the alias of “champagne papi,” as per his Instagram handle. Though he gives her himself (the champagne) she still refuses and prefers something else.", "annotation_id": "14877723", "url": "https://genius.com/14877723", "state": "accepted", "votes": 100, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "Trap, TrapMoneyBenny", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "TrapMoneyBenny is a producer from Cleveland, Ohio who has worked with artists like Chief Keef, 21 Savage, and Gucci Mane. This track marks Drake and Benny’s first collaboration.\n\nIn an interview with The Fader published six days after “In My Feelings” dropped, Benny spoke about working on the track:\n\nI did kind of like the “meat and potatoes” of the beat: the melody, and most of the drums. 40 and BlaqNmilD are the other producers on it, and did a hell of a job with it. They really kind of flipped it and went crazy. They did the samples and gave it that super New Orleans bounce feel.", "annotation_id": "14877418", "url": "https://genius.com/14877418", "state": "accepted", "votes": 695, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "’Resha, do you love me? Are you riding? Say you’ll never ever leave from beside me ’Cause I want ya, and I need ya And I’m down for you always JT, do you love me? Are you riding? Say you’ll never ever leave from beside me ’Cause I want ya, and I need ya And I’m down for you always", "vault_lines": ["'Resha, do you love me? Are you riding?", "'Cause I want you, and I need you", "'Cause I want ya, and I–"], "meaning": "JT and Yung Miami, whose real name is Caresha, make up the Quality Control-signed duo City Girls. They’re featured in the segue following this chorus.", "annotation_id": "14876695", "url": "https://genius.com/14876695", "state": "accepted", "votes": 312, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "[Skit: Zazie Beetz] You know what, I don’t even care, I need a photo with Drake Because my Instagram is weak as fuck I’m just being real, my shit, look—", "vault_lines": ["You know what, I don't even care, I need a photo with Drake", "Because my Instagram is weak as fuck", "I'm just being real, my shit, look—"], "meaning": "This is a scene from Atlanta Season 2, Episode 7: “Champagne Papi” (named after Drake’s Instagram handle).\n\nIn the episode, Zazie Beetz’s character Van and her friends attend a New Years Eve party at Drake’s house. Though Van is hoping to get a picture with Drake (potentially to make Earn jealous), Drake ends up being out of town and on tour.\n\nSoon after the episode aired on FX, Drake posted to his Instagram story making it clear that he had watched and enjoyed the episode.\n\nhttps://pbs.twimg.com/media/Da_J_HUVwAADn_W.jpg:large", "annotation_id": "14876588", "url": "https://genius.com/14876588", "state": "accepted", "votes": 393, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "line": "From the block like you Jenny", "vault_lines": ["From the block like you Jenny"], "meaning": "“Jenny from the Block” is Jennifer Lopez‘ nickname, and also the title of a song she dropped in 2002 featuring The Lox. The song is about Lopez never forgetting her roots in the Bronx despite her fame and fortune. In the same way Drake states that his girl has been 100 with him since day one, and has not let his fame change her.\n\nIn late 2016, after Drake and J-Lo posted some snuggly photos online, it was rumored that they were more than friendly. While Drake never publicly addressed the rumor, he seemed to imply they’d fallen out of favor on his 2017 track, “Free Smoke”:\n\nI drunk text J-Lo\nOld number, so it bounce back\n\nAnd his January 2018 single “Diplomatic Immunity”:\n\n2017 I lost a J. Lo\n\nDrake also sampled J-Lo on “Teenage Fever” from his 2017 “playlist” More Life.", "annotation_id": "14876322", "url": "https://genius.com/14876322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/d07c7aca495a624d5d4e0eb11cccd577.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"In My Feelings\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>In My FeelingsDrake8M", "status": "done", "targets": 14, "clicked": 14, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-in-my-feelings-lyrics", "genius_title": "In My Feelings"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/df15007a623347465eca1126b922afe7.300x300x1.jpg\" alt=\"Fake Love\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fake LoveDrake3.8M", "line": "And more chune for your headtop So watch how you speak on my name, you know?", "vault_lines": ["And more chune for your headtop", "So watch how you speak on my name, you know?"], "meaning": "This segement is sampled from Drake’s acceptance speech after winning the “Favorite Artist Award” at the 2016 American Music Awards show.\n\nDrake states in Jamaican Patois that he has more tunes (“chune”) coming for your head (“headtop”) and warns people to show him respect.\n\nThis statement was said as a promotion and eventually became a slogan for the then upcoming More Life playlist.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/AMAs/status/800532136876871680\n\nThe sample can only be heard on the digital version of “Fake Love.” It can also be heard on “Free Smoke” and “Jorja Interlude.”", "annotation_id": "11709315", "url": "https://genius.com/11709315", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/df15007a623347465eca1126b922afe7.300x300x1.jpg\" alt=\"Fake Love\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fake LoveDrake3.8M", "line": "Just when shit look out of reach I reach back like one, three Like one, three, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Just when shit look out of reach", "I reach back like one, three", "Like one, three, yeah"], "meaning": "Odell Beckham Jr. is an American football wide receiver for the New York Giants who wears the number 13. He became popular after his catch in a November 2014 game against the Dallas Cowboys—the ball looked like it was too far behind him to catch, but at the last moment he lunged backwards and grabbed it above his head with one hand.\n\nLike Odell’s catch, Drizzy’s willing to stretch to accomplish an important goal. Drake and Odell have been friendly in the past, and Odell even spent a good amount of time at the OVO Compound in Calabasas over the summer of 2016.", "annotation_id": "10710329", "url": "https://genius.com/10710329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 281, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/df15007a623347465eca1126b922afe7.300x300x1.jpg\" alt=\"Fake Love\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fake LoveDrake3.8M", "line": "That’s when they smile in my face Whole time they wanna take my place Whole time they wanna take my place Whole time they wanna take my place Yeah, I know they wanna take my place I can tell that love is fake I don’t trust a word you say How you wanna clique up after your mistakes? Look you in the face, and it’s just not the same", "vault_lines": ["That's when they smile in my face", "Whole time they wanna take my place", "Yeah, I know they wanna take my place", "I can tell that love is fake", "I don't trust a word you say", "How you wanna clique up after your mistakes?", "Look you in the face, and it's just not the same", "I came up, you changed up", "I caught that whole play", "They look up to me", "Straight up to my face, straight up to my face"], "meaning": "Drake interpolates lyrics from the chorus of the 1972 R&B song “Back Stabbers” by The O'Jays:\n\n(They smilin' in your face)\nAll the time they want to take your place", "annotation_id": "10710258", "url": "https://genius.com/10710258", "state": "accepted", "votes": 207, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/df15007a623347465eca1126b922afe7.300x300x1.jpg\" alt=\"Fake Love\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fake LoveDrake3.8M", "line": "I’ve been down so long, it look like up to me They look up to me I got fake people showin’ fake love to me Straight up to my face, straight up to my face I’ve been down so long, it look like up to me They look up to me I got fake people showin’ fake love to me Straight up to my face, straight up to my face", "vault_lines": ["I've been down so long, it look like up to me", "Yeah, straight up to my face, tryna play it safe", "They look up to me", "I got fake people showin' fake love to me", "Straight up to my face, straight up to my face"], "meaning": "Drake juxtaposes being down (depressed) with being physically down (lower than others). Even while he’s depressed, there are those who “look up” to him, idolizing his artistry.\n\nThe first line is taken from the title of several pieces such as The Doors' song “Been Down So Long” and Richard Fariña’s novel Been Down So Long It Looks Like Up to Me, but 20th century blues singer Furry Lewis was its originator with his song “I Will Turn Your Money Green”:\n\nI been down so long, it seem like up to me\nI been down so long, it seem like up to me\nWoman I love, she done quit, poor me", "annotation_id": "10710162", "url": "https://genius.com/10710162", "state": "accepted", "votes": 306, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/df15007a623347465eca1126b922afe7.300x300x1.jpg\" alt=\"Fake Love\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fake LoveDrake3.8M", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 4, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-fake-love-lyrics", "genius_title": "Fake Love"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/539dfa9f85a8848474c27c1aca9fb486.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hold On, We’re Going Home\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hold On, We’re Going HomeDrake5.7M", "line": "[Produced by Nineteen85, Majid Jordan & Noah “40” Shebib]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song started as a beat by Nineteen85. Then Majid—half of the OVO-signed duo Majid Jordan—laid some vocals over it while Jordan sped it up to infuse the track with more energy. They handed it over to 40 and Drake, and the rest is history. As 40 told the FADER:\n\nMajid Jordan captures a moment that Drake and I have always been chasing.\n\nThis is a 100BPM song with a four-on-the-floor rhythm with a clap on every second beat for most of the track. The main arrangement is drums, synths, bass, vocals and backing vocals. It’s been described by SPIN as “synth-soaked R&B” meeting “slow-motion deep house”. The song uses a lot of reverb to give it a spacious feeling. During the final hook white noise cuts and hi-hats are added in to highlight the grand finalé.\n\nGrammy nominated producer Chilly Gonzales broke down the keys used in the beat:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1ysNZRt3hoE", "annotation_id": "2835527", "url": "https://genius.com/2835527", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/539dfa9f85a8848474c27c1aca9fb486.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hold On, We’re Going Home\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hold On, We’re Going HomeDrake5.7M", "line": "It’s hard to do these things alone", "vault_lines": ["It's hard to do these things alone"], "meaning": "Drake has vented about being/sleeping alone before.\n\nDrake’s had many relationships not work out and like he implies on “Find Your Love”, he’s sick of failed relationships and being alone. He’s also apparently looking to settle down in a way, as he refers to on “Girls Love Beyonce” in the second verse.\n\nDrizzy doesn’t like being like this:", "annotation_id": "2110549", "url": "https://genius.com/2110549", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/539dfa9f85a8848474c27c1aca9fb486.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hold On, We’re Going Home\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hold On, We’re Going HomeDrake5.7M", "line": "I got my eyes on you You’re everything that I see I want your heart, love and emotion endlessly", "vault_lines": ["Gave you everything I loved", "I got my eyes on you", "You're everything that I see", "I want your heart, love and emotion endlessly", "I want your hot love and emotion endlessly"], "meaning": "When one is truly in love, it’s often said they only have eyes for that one person. And with love comes infatuation, so much so that you just can’t get enough.", "annotation_id": "2089541", "url": "https://genius.com/2089541", "state": "accepted", "votes": 174, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/539dfa9f85a8848474c27c1aca9fb486.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hold On, We’re Going Home\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hold On, We’re Going HomeDrake5.7M", "line": "I can’t get over you You left your mark on me I want your hot love and emotion endlessly", "vault_lines": ["I want your heart, love and emotion endlessly", "I can't get over you", "You left your mark on me", "I want your hot love and emotion endlessly"], "meaning": "Drake is known for talking about women that leave an impression or “mark” on him in his songs. In the “Find Your Love” music video, Drake makes the whole theme of the video about a woman who left her “mark” on him.\n\nhttp://youtu.be/Xyv4Bjja8yc\n\nLater, he did the same with his first big radio single, “Best I Ever Had” from his EP/Mixtape So Far Gone\n\nAnd from “Take Care”\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zzP29emgpg&feature=share&list=UUQznUf1SjfDqx65hX3zRDiA", "annotation_id": "2088672", "url": "https://genius.com/2088672", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/539dfa9f85a8848474c27c1aca9fb486.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hold On, We’re Going Home\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hold On, We’re Going HomeDrake5.7M", "line": "’Cause you’re a good girl and you know it I know exactly who you could be", "vault_lines": ["I know exactly who you could be", "'Cause you're a good girl and you know it"], "meaning": "Deep down, this girl is good and Drake can see who she really is. He wants to bring that out of her.\n\nIn what is assuredly not a coincidence, Drake has had past real-life dealings with a certain good girl gone bad.\n\nDrake’s interest helping his romantic interests—like helping his girl realize her full potential—comes up a lot in his work. Whether he’s worrying about a girl being lonely in a new city or feels bad for the 26-and-older girls who can’t maintain friendships—Drake stays concerned. He’s also known to use the romantic interests in his songs as a way to talk to himself—something he mentioned in his interview with Elliot Wilson.", "annotation_id": "2071883", "url": "https://genius.com/2071883", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/539dfa9f85a8848474c27c1aca9fb486.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hold On, We’re Going Home\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hold On, We’re Going HomeDrake5.7M", "line": "Just hold on, we’re going home Just hold on, we’re going home", "vault_lines": ["Hold On, We’re Going Home Lyrics", "Just hold on, we're going home", "So just hold on, we're going home (Going home)", "Oh, just hold on, we're going home (Going home)"], "meaning": "Drake, in an interview/Q&A with Elliott Wilson, explained that he didn’t have time for romance at this point in his life. He’s talking to himself and his romantic interest when he says—\"just hold on,“ asking for her patience. He continued:\n\nI’m so dedicated to this (music), I’m so commited to this room, that I can’t really offer somebody the nurturing and the dedication and the loyalty and the consistency that it takes to maintain a relationship.\n\nExplaination ends at 13:32\nhttp://youtu.be/zv0S7zMzAJA?t=11m32s\n\nAnd Drake’s home is pretty ballin'. I mean 7 bedrooms, 9 bathrooms and 7500 square feet!? Check it out!\n\nDrake told MTV that this song was meant “to be played at weddings in ten years.  Or [to be something that] people that are away from their families in the Army could listen to.”", "annotation_id": "2071674", "url": "https://genius.com/2071674", "state": "accepted", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/539dfa9f85a8848474c27c1aca9fb486.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hold On, We’re Going Home\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hold On, We’re Going HomeDrake5.7M", "line": "’Cause you’re a good girl and you know it You act so different around me", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you're a good girl and you know it", "You act so different around me"], "meaning": "Drake, in an interview/Q&A with Elliott Wilson, explained in detail what this line means to him.\n\nI’ve seen potential in women that they (my friends) have told me were no good….But maybe I see something in them, maybe OUR relationship is exciting.\n\nExplanation ends at 13:32\nhttp://youtu.be/zv0S7zMzAJA?t=12m55s\n\nAlternatively, this “good girl” might feel she has to act a certain way around Drake, but he still sees her for what she really is, good. No need to play “bad”, he likes her as she is.", "annotation_id": "2071670", "url": "https://genius.com/2071670", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/539dfa9f85a8848474c27c1aca9fb486.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Hold On, We’re Going Home\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Hold On, We’re Going HomeDrake5.7M", "status": "done", "targets": 7, "clicked": 7, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-hold-on-were-going-home-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hold On, We’re Going Home"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "For your birthday, your man got a table at Hibachi Last time I ate there, Wayne was doin’ numbers off the cup like Yahtzee", "vault_lines": ["For your birthday, your boyfriend got a party bus", "For your birthday, your man got a table at Hibachi", "Last time I ate there, Wayne was doin' numbers off the cup like Yahtzee"], "meaning": "Drake is using a double meaning here while shouting out Lil Wayne.\n\n“Off the cup like Yahtzee” references the cup used to shake the dice in Yahtzee, where rolling the right numbers is the whole point of the game. Drake then flips “doing numbers” into the meaning that Lil Wayne was seeing major success commercially.\n\nAt the same time, “the cup” also points to Wayne’s long-known connection with lean, often seen sipping from a double cup (which is often associated with sipping lean) throughout his career. So when Drake says Wayne was “doing numbers off the cup,” he’s tying together the Yahtzee reference, Wayne’s cup imagery, and the idea that Wayne was turning that image into hits, sales, and cultural impact.", "annotation_id": "39610725", "url": "https://genius.com/39610725", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Come to the 6 and I’m like the actin’ sheriff, deputy", "vault_lines": ["Come to the 6 and I'm like the actin' sheriff, deputy"], "meaning": "“The 6” being Toronto.\n\nDrake is using a metaphor likening himself to law enforcement in Toronto", "annotation_id": "30722461", "url": "https://genius.com/30722461", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "I really can’t remember it properly", "vault_lines": ["I really can't remember it properly"], "meaning": "The couple of previous lines are all not remembered properly because of how long ago all the stories took place\n\nHence Drake not remembering all the details about wayne doing numbers at the table or the process of getting on the song “versace”", "annotation_id": "30607285", "url": "https://genius.com/30607285", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Long way from Sette Mezzo", "vault_lines": ["Long way from Sette Mezzo"], "meaning": "Sette Mezzo is an upper east side based Italian restaurant out of New York.\n\nImplying that Drake seems to be a long way out from New York during his time recording the track.", "annotation_id": "30607255", "url": "https://genius.com/30607255", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Whippin’ the Vespa off of six tequilas", "vault_lines": ["Whippin' the Vespa off of six tequilas"], "meaning": "A Vespa is an Italian luxury brand of scooters and mopeds manufactured by Piaggio. The name means wasp in Italian.\n\nSo, Drake clearly flexes the fact that he is somewhere in Italy, drinking a lot of tequila and whippin' the Vespa.", "annotation_id": "29750776", "url": "https://genius.com/29750776", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "I wouldn’t trade my life for none of y’alls, it’s an embargo", "vault_lines": ["I wouldn't trade my life for none of y'alls, it's an embargo"], "meaning": "An embargo is an official ban on trade or other commercial activity with a particular country.\n\nGiven Drake’s success and wealth, his life is a lot better than most and he wouldn’t want to live anyone else’s life.", "annotation_id": "29447055", "url": "https://genius.com/29447055", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "My favorite two words from you white boys is, “Sign here” And then comes the sound of glass clinkin’ from a wine cheers Swear I’m pocket checkin’ y’all for five years", "vault_lines": ["I'm here for the moral support", "My favorite two words from you white boys is, \"Sign here\"", "And then comes the sound of glass clinkin' from a wine cheers", "Swear I'm pocket checkin' y'all for five years"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to the momentus occasion when a rapper signs a deal — either their first, or an extension. With most deals in the rap industry, the label signing the rapper is usually white.\n\nRegardless, these deals result in a large sum of money being given to the rapper and a celebration is held afterwards. In this case, Drizzy is sharing wine with his current partners who just re-signed him.\n\nAdditionally, Drake talks about pocket checking. This is defined as asking people if they have money to “borrow” which usually is never returned.. He’s talking about taking his label’s money.", "annotation_id": "28617832", "url": "https://genius.com/28617832", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Paris Hilton was steady duckin’ the paparazzi", "vault_lines": ["And Paris Hilton was steady duckin' the paparazzi"], "meaning": "Paris Hilton signed to Cash Money Records in May 2013, making her Drake’s labelmate at the time.", "annotation_id": "28225282", "url": "https://genius.com/28225282", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "That’s how motherfuckers in CMB play it", "vault_lines": ["That's how motherfuckers in CMB play it"], "meaning": "This might be a reference for Hot Boys‘ song (Alongside with Birdman himself), “Neighborhood Superstar”:\n\nAsk them motherfuckers how the CMB play it\nTanqueray and Alize it, take the llello and weigh it", "annotation_id": "27455978", "url": "https://genius.com/27455978", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Me and Smiggz on the loose in the city, you know how the rest go", "vault_lines": ["Me and Smiggz on the loose in the city, you know how the rest go"], "meaning": "Smiggz most likely refers to Smiley, a Canadian-based rapper from Toronto who is currently signed to “OVO Sound”, Drake’s Label.\nSmiley used the nickname “Smiggz” too on his songs “Topic” and “Moving Different”.", "annotation_id": "27415235", "url": "https://genius.com/27415235", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Shout out TVGUCCI", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake shout outs his friend and Reps Up affiliate\nTVGUCCI.", "annotation_id": "27260464", "url": "https://genius.com/27260464", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Meet Tommaso and Ernesto", "vault_lines": ["Meet Tommaso and Ernesto"], "meaning": "Ernsto most likely refers to Ernesto Bertarelli, the billionaire owner of the yacht VAVA II that Drake was partying on in Summer of 22 and which he name drops in the Intro of the song.\nTommaso most likely refers to Tommaso Buti, an Italian business man.\nYou can see his name on the tagged list alongside with Drake on the second slide of this post (this video was taken on the Vava).\n\n\n\nTommaso Buti and Ermesto Bertarelli together on December 2021", "annotation_id": "27245322", "url": "https://genius.com/27245322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "We goin’ from the Vava to Cinquante-Cinq, then back to the Vava", "vault_lines": ["We goin' from the Vava to Cinquante-Cinq, then back to the Vava"], "meaning": "Vava refers to the Yacht VAVA II, a 8.5 billion $ yacht owned by Suisse-Italian billionaire Ernesto Bertarelli.\n\n\n\nCinquante-Cinq, the French word for Fifty-Five, is a reference to a legendary St. Tropez Beach Club called Club 55, which is one of the hottest celebrity clubs in France.\n\n\n\nDrake is basically describing his time in Europe partying with Ernesto, whom he refers to in the first lines of his first verse, hopping from luxury Yachts to luxury club and back. Only members of the high society circles Drake frequents would understand this instantly.", "annotation_id": "27245290", "url": "https://genius.com/27245290", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "This shit designed for divine ears", "vault_lines": ["This shit designed for divine ears"], "meaning": "these lines tie into the top 3 which cannot be annotated but its a triple entendre.. its “sky cleareth” which leads into the next line of divine ears. (bible refrences)\n\n1st – he’s serious that he has his heads in the clouds, that he’s above the competition\n\n2nd – he says “serious” to sound like “cirrus”, which are a type of cloud, it is a cloud that shows up after a sky of thicker clouds starts to clear up\n\n3rd – he has his head in the clouds on the album cover of Nothing Was The Same, which came out 9 years ago", "annotation_id": "27135678", "url": "https://genius.com/27135678", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Treat me like a newborn, Lucian not droppin’ me", "vault_lines": ["Treat me like a newborn, Lucian not droppin' me"], "meaning": "This line is a metaphor for how Lucian [Grainge], CEO of Universal Music Group, would never drop Drake from their record label. In May of 2022 Drake signed a newly negotiated contract with Universal Music Group for a whopping estimated $400 million dollars.", "annotation_id": "27128271", "url": "https://genius.com/27128271", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "They should’ve been in the fountain based on what y’all wishin’ me", "vault_lines": ["They should've been in the fountain based on what y'all wishin' me"], "meaning": "Many people are wishing nothing good for Drake. He often addresses the problem of successful people that every one just wants to see them fall. Especially him, as he is unarguably the most successful rapper/artist for nearly two decades.\n\nHe did that in other songs as well:\n\nNo body praying for you when you‘re winning\n\nThe Remorse of the Album Certified Lover Boy\n\nThe line is a wordplay. Those people, wishing him bad, should be down in a fountain. As this is a tradition. People throwing coins on the bottom of a fountain, wishing things.", "annotation_id": "27095319", "url": "https://genius.com/27095319", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "EmRata here fresh off divorce And I’m tryna look in her eyes, maybe express my remorse", "vault_lines": ["EmRata here fresh off divorce", "And I'm tryna look in her eyes, maybe express my remorse"], "meaning": "Emily O'Hara Ratajkowski (born June 7, 1991) is an English-born American model and actress. Born in London to American parents and raised in Encinitas, California, Ratajkowski began acting as a child before she gained a recurring role on the Nickelodeon series iCarly (2009–2010). Her modeling debut was on the cover of the March 2012 issue of the erotic magazine treats!, which led to her appearing in two music videos: Robin Thicke’s “Blurred Lines” and Maroon 5’s “Love Somebody”. (Wikipedia)\n\nhttps://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcTp0-d3ZjoddPxMvySgF50X3CnVfhsgXsAZDA&usqp=CAU\n\nDrake plays with her Instagram (@emrata) which is very famous.\n\nShe was recently divorced from her husband and Drake is implying that he could maybe fake some remorse, while he is probably happy that she is now again free on the dating market.", "annotation_id": "27094833", "url": "https://genius.com/27094833", "state": "pending", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "She could be givin’ me head and somehow you not toppin’ me", "vault_lines": ["She could be givin' me head and somehow you not toppin' me"], "meaning": "Another word for “giving head” is “top.”", "annotation_id": "27090136", "url": "https://genius.com/27090136", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "You niggas are too concerned with makin’ sure y’all outfits gon’ match in Paris", "vault_lines": ["You niggas are too concerned with makin' sure y'all outfits gon' match in Paris"], "meaning": "“Match in Paris” also sounds like “match in pairs.”\n\nAnything thats in a pair is a match.", "annotation_id": "27090123", "url": "https://genius.com/27090123", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "I’m really on a roll like hamachi", "vault_lines": ["I'm really on a roll like hamachi"], "meaning": "Hamachi is Japanese yellowtail, commonly served at sushi restaurants. Drake is continuing to flaunt his vast knowledge of Japanese cuisine", "annotation_id": "27079758", "url": "https://genius.com/27079758", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "The lyrics begin to reveal themselves over time periods Promise you’ll get that shit when the sky clears", "vault_lines": ["The lyrics begin to reveal themselves over time periods", "Promise you'll get that shit when the sky clears"], "meaning": "Over the past few years, Drake’s albums have gotten mixed reviews when they initially dropped. However, after a few months or years go by, people began to see them for what they truly were.\n\nWhen the sky clears basically means when the dust settles. Once a good amount of time passes, you’ll understand what Drake was really talking in his songs. Also tying together the previous line of having his head in the clouds.", "annotation_id": "27077348", "url": "https://genius.com/27077348", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "If she want a rebound with me, I’m down to go get her some boards", "vault_lines": ["If she want a rebound with me, I'm down to go get her some boards"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the homonym “rebound”. The first meaning is rebound sex, which is when someone who’s just come out of a romantic relationship hooks up with someone new, in this case Emily Ratajkowski, as referenced above, is fresh out of a divorce and may be looking for a new sexual partner, potentially Drake. The second is referencing basketball rebounds, which is where a player retrieves the ball after a missed shot. Rebounds are also known as “boards” in basketball, so when Drake says “he’s down to go get her some boards”, it means he’s down to get her on the rebound and have sex with her.", "annotation_id": "27074303", "url": "https://genius.com/27074303", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Contract Lord of the Rings, think it’s a script for the movie", "vault_lines": ["Contract Lord of the Rings, think it's a script for the movie"], "meaning": "Lord of the Rings movies are notorious for being long with the final movie of the trilogy clocking in at 3 hours and 20 minutes.\n\nDrake likens the scripts to his contracts, as they are also quite thick.", "annotation_id": "27074101", "url": "https://genius.com/27074101", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Might be the only teacher that gets paid enough", "vault_lines": ["Might be the only teacher that gets paid enough"], "meaning": "Teachers notoriously do not make enough for what their job entails. This has created unions, strikes, and a teacher shortage in the US.\n\nThe average starting salary for a teacher is $49,933. In 2021 Drake made an estimated $50 million, or 1,000 times that.", "annotation_id": "27074081", "url": "https://genius.com/27074081", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Sidebar, Serena, your husband a groupie He claim we don’t got a problem but No, boo, it is like you comin’ for sushi We might pop up on ’em at will like Suzuki", "vault_lines": ["Drake, you got it", "Sidebar, Serena, your husband a groupie", "He claim we don't got a problem but", "No, boo, it is like you comin' for sushi", "We might pop up on 'em at will like Suzuki"], "meaning": "Drake airs out Serena William’s husband Alexis Ohanian, an American entrepreneur and co-founder of Reddit, claiming he is someone who  seeks to achieve status by having sex with celebrities known as a “groupie”. Drake and Serena were reportedly romantically involved in 2015 according to TMZ Sports where photos surfaced of them spotted kissing in a restaurant before Ohanian was in the picture.\n\n\n\nDrake asserts they do indeed have a problem despite what Ohanian may say whilst playing on words. “No, boo” (a slang for someone you love) is likened to the famous Japanese restaurant that serves sushi, Nobu.\n\n\n\nDrake threatens his opposition (or possibly Ohanian) claiming he will meet them in person on purpose. This is a double entendre referring to the stunt of a wheelie performed on motorcycles such as Suzuki’s where the front wheel is popped up in the air.\n\n\n\n“At will” is likely another layer to this scheme as  Will Smith played Serena William’s father – Richard Williams, in the 2021 American sports biographical ‘King Richard’.", "annotation_id": "27068428", "url": "https://genius.com/27068428", "state": "pending", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Matter of time before I go net a Bey like apiarist", "vault_lines": ["Matter of time before I go net a Bey like apiarist"], "meaning": "Firstly, “net a b” refers to Drake’s yearning to reach a billion dollars. This goal is something he’s rapped about on multiple occasions, with his bars on Hype,\n\nI’ma have B’s when I’m 70\n\nand Lil Wayne’s Family Feud remix serving as examples:\n\nWe gon' have to break the billi' curse\n\nIt’s worth noting that Bey’s (a shortened nickname for Beyoncé) husband, Jay-Z, has already reached the billion dollar mark.\n\nFinally, apiarist is a synonym for a beekeeper, which gives us another way of interpreting the “net a bee” wordplay.", "annotation_id": "27068383", "url": "https://genius.com/27068383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "I send the label bills, bills, bills like the other two women standing next to Bey, that shit was just— Independent women is lovin’ the new appearance", "vault_lines": ["I send the label bills, bills, bills like the other two women standing next to Bey, that shit was just—", "Independent women is lovin' the new appearance", "Just like that (Shine, shine)"], "meaning": "A reference to the American girl group Destiny’s Child’s comprised of Beyoncé, “and the other two women,” Kelly Rowland, and Michelle Williams.\n\nIt also references their 1999 song Bills, Bills, Bills alongside 2001’s Independent Women, Pt. 1 & Pt. 2.\n\nhttps://imagesvc.meredithcorp.io/v3/mm/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fstatic.onecms.io%2Fwp-content%2Fuploads%2Fsites%2F6%2F2016%2F04%2Fdestinys-child.jpg&q=60", "annotation_id": "27068362", "url": "https://genius.com/27068362", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Niggas see the 6ix God pass, they high-fivin’ me Need to be high-sixin’ me", "vault_lines": ["Niggas see the 6ix God pass, they high-fivin' me", "Need to be high-sixin' me"], "meaning": "Drake has a constant theme of dubbing himself as the “6 God,” which influenced him to produce his October 2014 track “6 God.” The “6” is a reference to Toronto’s area codes, 416 and 647. Drake, however, sees himself as a high priority, hence, the number 6 comes after 5, which also affirms his wealth.", "annotation_id": "27068001", "url": "https://genius.com/27068001", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Me, Spider-Man, and Leonardo, I’m back tomorrow", "vault_lines": ["Me, Spider-Man, and Leonardo, I'm back tomorrow"], "meaning": "Tobey Maguire (the first actor to play Spider-Man) is good friends with Leonardo DiCaprio. Together, they are part of a group who are known for their partying lifestyle.\n\nTobey Maguire and Leonardo DiCaprio partying in St. Tropez\n\nDrake, Tobey and Leo were together at Ernesto Bertarelli’s party in St. Tropez this summer.\n\nhttps://pagesix.com/2022/07/23/billionaires-a-listers-and-leonardo-dicaprio-hit-st-tropez/\n\nTobey pictured with Honestly Nevermind merch:\nhttps://pbs.twimg.com/media/FYja1O4WIAEO9p0?format=jpg&name=900x900\n\nBeyond the celebrities in real life, this is also a reference to the movie “The Great Gatsby”, which stars both Leonardo and Tobey. The movie showcases the extravagant lifestyles and parties of ridiculously wealthy socialites – Drake says that he fits right in and keeps coming back.\n\nFun fact, Leonardo was planned to play Spiderman back in the early 90s\n\nLeonardo is also a reference to the character from Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles, a connection between he and Spider-Man who are both action characters.", "annotation_id": "27063663", "url": "https://genius.com/27063663", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Life insurance policies, you niggas ’bout to need the gecko", "vault_lines": ["Life insurance policies, you niggas 'bout to need the gecko"], "meaning": "A reference to the Gecko mascot of Geico Insurance, also referenced on songs like Gold Digger and Knock Off", "annotation_id": "27061598", "url": "https://genius.com/27061598", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "I’m goin’ Pink Floyd, you niggas cannot rock with me", "vault_lines": ["I'm goin' Pink Floyd, you niggas cannot rock with me"], "meaning": "Literally a play on the fact that Pink Floyd is a rock band.", "annotation_id": "27060271", "url": "https://genius.com/27060271", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Feel like an AMBER Alert the way that I can take her to the mall and she find Tiffany", "vault_lines": ["Feel like an AMBER Alert the way that I can take her to the mall and she find Tiffany"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Tiffany Amber Thiessen, the actress from Saved By The Bell.\nThis could also be a very creative play on words by Drake. An AMBER alert is an emergency response system that disseminates information about a missing person, usually a child. Drake mentions the mall and uses the name Tiffany specifically to refer to the luxury store Tiffany & Co..", "annotation_id": "27060256", "url": "https://genius.com/27060256", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "I got my head in the clouds, I’m serious", "vault_lines": ["I got my head in the clouds, I'm serious"], "meaning": "This is a clever double entendre. Drake seriously had his head in the clouds on his third studio album Nothing Was The Same. Cirrus is a genus of high cloud that appear delicate and wispy.", "annotation_id": "27060105", "url": "https://genius.com/27060105", "state": "pending", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Eight karats like vegetarians, nigga, the earrings are droopy", "vault_lines": ["Eight karats like vegetarians, nigga, the earrings are droopy"], "meaning": "The gemstones on Drake’s earrings are so large, they droop.\n\n\n\nWhile a carat is a unit of measurement to weigh gemstones, he makes a double entendre with “eight carats” and “ate carrots,” like vegetarians.\n\nThis bar also describes rabbits, which are vegetarian, love carrots, and have droopy ears.", "annotation_id": "27059748", "url": "https://genius.com/27059748", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Kawasaki, sushi, saké, the money grow on trees like shiitake They tried to get spicy with me, so I wonder how they gon’ stop me", "vault_lines": ["Kawasaki, sushi, saké, the money grow on trees like shiitake", "They tried to get spicy with me, so I wonder how they gon' stop me"], "meaning": "Drake describes his money as “grow[ing] on trees like shiitake”. Drake has used the term money trees in The Resistance in 2010, and again on 6PM in New York. In the ladder, it is clear he is referencing Kendrick Lamar’s song, Money Trees.\n\nIn this particular instance, it is unclear if Drake describing his money as growing on trees is somehow referencing or throwing a Jab at Kendrick, however, with the line that comes next, this line may just be the latest installment in Drake & Kendrick’s never-ending subliminal war.", "annotation_id": "27058750", "url": "https://genius.com/27058750", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "First got to America, niggas wouldn’t check for me No chance the kid’ll make it here like vasectomy They underestimated my trajectory", "vault_lines": ["First got to America, niggas wouldn't check for me", "No chance the kid'll make it here like vasectomy", "They underestimated my trajectory"], "meaning": "Drake likens his early days in the American hip-hop scene as bleak, with people paying him little attention. His uphill struggle to make it as a young artist in the American hip-hop community is compared to a vasectomy, which is a medical procedure that eliminates the supply of sperm to semen and almost certainly prevents fertilization during sex.\n\nDrake thinks of his prospects in the American hip-hop community in these medical terms—i.e., no one fancied his chances of growing as an artist like how this type of birth control minimizes the chances of pregnancy. Faced with metaphorical challenging odds, Drake drives home the point that no one in the U.S. market ever expected the career path he’s had to date when he released Room for Improvement and Comeback Season in 2006 and 2007, respectively.", "annotation_id": "27057557", "url": "https://genius.com/27057557", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "I’m like a cup holder the way that these dimes stick to me", "vault_lines": ["I'm like a cup holder the way that these dimes stick to me"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to the cup holder in a car where most people keep loose change typically for parking meters. In the heat, this change sticks together and to the cup holder typically due to soda or something else from the cup holder.\n\nDimes also refers to girls who are “10s” that “stick” to him by staying around waiting for him to come back around to them.", "annotation_id": "27056180", "url": "https://genius.com/27056180", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Quavo might’ve sent me a song that he called “Versace”", "vault_lines": ["Quavo might've sent me a song that he called \"Versace\""], "meaning": "Drake recounts how he got involved in Migos‘ 2013 song “Versace,” which became their first smash hit single after Drizzy’s remix. The song also put the trio’s signature triplet flows on the map. In a 2017 interview with Ebro Darden, Quavo explained how this collaboration came about:\n\n[In a video], Drama was trying to tell him [Drake] that Rich Homie Quan is the new thing […] [Drake] was like ‘Nah man, have you heard Migos though?’ When I seen that, I knew he wanted to work with us but we never met him ‘til we got to Birthday Bash. And ever since then, I sent him two or three records. He wanted to get on ‘Pronto’ or 'Versace’ and I just sent him ‘Versace.’\"", "annotation_id": "27054963", "url": "https://genius.com/27054963", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Niggas so ignorant in our hood, they be like, “Why the fuck you makin’ techno?”", "vault_lines": ["Niggas so ignorant in our hood, they bе like, \"Why the fuck you makin' techno?\""], "meaning": "This is Drake’s second reply to the backlash he received on social media, and apparently in his hood, regarding his 7th studio album Honestly, Nevermind which he released earlier this year. A vast majority of the album featured house and Jersey club styled instrumentals, which was a far cry from the trap beats and bars many rap fans wanted.\n\nThe album was co-produced by renowned DJ Black Coffee, and was an homage to the late Virgil Abloh whose DJ sets were heavily influenced by house music.", "annotation_id": "27054673", "url": "https://genius.com/27054673", "state": "pending", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "I had the chopper to a wedding out in Monaco or Monte Carlo I’m losin’ track of where we all go", "vault_lines": ["I had the chopper to a wedding out in Monaco or Monte Carlo", "I'm losin' track of where we all go"], "meaning": "Drake with the A Tier double entendre here. Monaco aka “The Billionaire’s Playground” is known to be the wealthiest country in the world. Every 1 in 3 people who live there are millionaires. Very fitting for Drake. It also borders the French Riviera, calling back to the title of the song\n\nThe small French country is equally know for its rich history in motorsport. Dating back to 1929, the narrow streets of Monte Carlo get converted to a race TRACK a couple times a year for the most talented drivers in the world to maneuver around. Ever heard of 7 time Formula 1 Champion Lewis Hamilton? Not only has he won 3 races there for the Mercedes F1 team, he also lives there as well.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/ifiwasperfect/status/998815006547480576\n\nhttps://twitter.com/ifiwasperfect/status/1131554313472499714", "annotation_id": "27053642", "url": "https://genius.com/27053642", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Yeah I’m in the Missoni room at the Byblos The boat was rockin’ too much on some Aaliyah shit", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the Missoni room at the Byblos", "The boat was rockin' too much on some Aaliyah shit"], "meaning": "The “Missoni Home” is a 2000 sqft suite at the Byblos hotel in Saint-Tropez, France. This suite features 2 bedrooms, 4 bathrooms, and a private pool. As the name suggests, this room is styled by Italian fashion house Missoni and features their well known colorful striped style.\n\n\n\n\n\nFurthermore, Missoni has been one of Drake’s favourite clothing brands since as early as 2011, expressing a desire to collaborate with the brand in an interview with GQ.\n\nSaint-Tropez is a coastal town and thus a popular destination for docking yachts. Despite Drake chartering a $50 million super-yacht for a recent excursion around Europe, it appears the rocking motion of the boat is too much for him so he decided to stay on land, calling back to Aaliyah’s hit song “Rock the Boat.”", "annotation_id": "27053542", "url": "https://genius.com/27053542", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Swedish jail cell smellin’ like some Carby Musk", "vault_lines": ["Swedish jail cell smellin' like some Carby Musk"], "meaning": "Drake could be alluding to his July 2022 trip to Sweden where he was rumored to be detained by the Swedish police as he shared a now-deleted photo of a handout on Instagram titled “Information for Those Suspected of a Crime and Subsequently Detained.” Despite the post, the rumor, and the trending hashtag #FreeDrake on Twitter, Drizzy’s team later told The Hollywood Reporter that the rapper was not arrested.\n\nDrake also plugs his own luxury scented candle Carby Musk, from his Better World Fragrance House.", "annotation_id": "27053449", "url": "https://genius.com/27053449", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Big Benjamins like the Pittsburgh Steelers Drake, you got it Robert Kraft sent the jet for us, that shit was patriotic You would think we live in Baltimore, the way they ravin’ ’bout the latest product", "vault_lines": ["Big Benjamins like the Pittsburgh Steelers", "Drake, you got it", "Robert Kraft sent the jet for us, that shit was patriotic", "You would think we live in Baltimore, the way they ravin' 'bout the latest product"], "meaning": "Drake uses a sequence of NFL-themed references and wordplay. He first connects the face of the $100 bill in the US, Benjamin Franklin, to Pittsburgh Steelers’s quarterback Ben Roethlisberger, whose nickname is “Big Ben.”\n\nDrizzy then namedrops Robert Kraft, who is an American billionaire with a net worth of approximately $10.6B as of the song’s release. Drake also forms a double entendre as Kraft owns the New England Patriots. Drake finally references the Baltimore Ravens by playing on “ravin'.”\n\n\n\nDrizzy and Kraft attended Michael Rubin’s July 4th Party together in 2022.", "annotation_id": "27052938", "url": "https://genius.com/27052938", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "line": "Fifty-nine bags on the 767, this is heavy cargo Yeah Fifty-nine bags on the—, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Fifty-nine bags on the 767, this is heavy cargo", "Fifty-nine bags on the—, ayy"], "meaning": "Drizzy boasts of his wealth and his Boeing 767 private plan named Air Drake, which was redesigned in 2020 and seemingly as a nod to the cover art of his September 2013 album, Nothing Was the Same. Drake has mentioned the plane on several tracks before: on his May 2020 track “Landed,” he spits:\n\nBoardin' Air Drake, then we takin' off in it, yeah\n\nOn Migos‘ June 2021 song “Having Our Way,” Drizzy raps:\n\nTold myself that I would get through this verse, and I’m not gonna mention the plane\nBut look at the plane", "annotation_id": "27052793", "url": "https://genius.com/27052793", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Middle of the Ocean", "status": "done", "targets": 44, "clicked": 44, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-middle-of-the-ocean-lyrics", "genius_title": "Middle of the Ocean"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "One truck in front of me, one behind me to follow", "vault_lines": ["One truck in front of me, one behind me to follow"], "meaning": "Drake rarely travels anywhere without security, especially by road. He tends to have multiple groups of bodyguards at his disposal in case anything bad occurs. He suggests that he’d normally have a truck both in front AND behind him to cover him from any possible attacks, acts of terrorism, etc.\n\nDrake’s typical security movement can be seen here", "annotation_id": "23398187", "url": "https://genius.com/23398187", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "G-Wagen for my bitch, now, girl, go live it up", "vault_lines": ["G-Wagen for my bitch, now, girl, go live it up"], "meaning": "The Mercedes-Benz G-Class, sometimes called G-Wagen (short for Geländewagen, “terrain vehicle”), is a mid-size four-wheel drive luxury SUV manufactured by Magna Steyr (formerly Steyr-Daimler-Puch) in Austria and sold by Mercedes-Benz. In certain markets, it has been sold under the Puch name as Puch G. The G-Class starts at around $132,000. Though most features are standard, a wide range of optional trim packages add everything from extra-cost paint (as much as $6,500), AMG styling bits, massaging seats, softer leather, and more can easily add $20,000 to that.\n\nMore info on the G-Wagen can be found here\n\n\n\nRoss suggests that he bought his girl a G-Wagen; a subtle flex to how wealthy he is.", "annotation_id": "23398169", "url": "https://genius.com/23398169", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Three-sixty up front, it all comes full circle", "vault_lines": ["Three-sixty up front, it all comes full circle"], "meaning": "Many artists who start out are baited into signing a predatory “360 deal,” an exclusive arrangement with a record label, where the label provides a range of platforms, outlets and services while taking a cut of everything the artist touches.\n\n\n\nBuilding off the “purple heart” line, Drake is battle-hardened in the music industry now—and “three-sixty up front” may be giving listeners insight into how he structures his fees for guest verses ($360k upfront, the other half on completion, so approx. $750k for a verse… fudging the numbers to make the poetics work).\n\nIn essence, his life has come “full circle” by avoiding the predatory deals at first, controlling what he creates and setting his own exorbitant prices for verses.", "annotation_id": "22563617", "url": "https://genius.com/22563617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Courtroom soundin’ like I’m in an opera", "vault_lines": ["Courtroom soundin' like I'm in an opera"], "meaning": "To “sing” in a courtroom or trial means that one is providing a judge with incriminating information about another individual or group, or as some would put it, “snitching”. In this line Ross suggests that an individual in the courtroom that he is in is taking part in said act.", "annotation_id": "22393352", "url": "https://genius.com/22393352", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Everybody that survive got survivor’s guilt", "vault_lines": ["Everybody that survive got survivor's guilt"], "meaning": "Survivor’s guilt is a phenomenon where a person feels as if they have done something wrong by surviving a traumatic experience when others did not.\n\nDrake is thinking about his own career and various points where he could not have made it, perhaps he didn’t sell an album as well as he wanted or deliver the project that fans were expecting. With this, he’s solidified himself as one of the most successful entertainers of all time, and many have speculated that Drake’s “too big to fail,” which has affected the criticism surrounding his projects, and personal life. Drake’s calling himself a “survivor” because he’s managed to push past controversies and low points in his career surprisingly well, and seemingly feels guilty for it at the same time.", "annotation_id": "22393042", "url": "https://genius.com/22393042", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "“The number’s all good, just pay me, I’m at the Rhino”", "vault_lines": ["\"The number's all good, just pay me, I'm at the Rhino\""], "meaning": "The Rhino is in reference to Las Vegas, Nevada based gentleman’s club Spearmint Rhino. Spearmint Rhino is located on the Las Vegas strip and is less than a mile from the Wynn Las Vegas. Drake signed a residency deal with the Wynn in 2019.", "annotation_id": "22372157", "url": "https://genius.com/22372157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Matthew Maddox called in the pit boss, double checkin’", "vault_lines": ["Matthew Maddox called in the pit boss, double checkin'"], "meaning": "Matthew Maddox is the CEO of Wynn Resorts, which notably includes a large hotel and casino in Las Vegas.  When you’re winning a lot of money gambling, the owner will oftentimes call in the pit boss to look over your shoulder and make sure you’re not cheating in any way.  Drake uses the symbol of a pit boss watching his moves to emphasize the degree to which he’s winning.", "annotation_id": "22370106", "url": "https://genius.com/22370106", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "I sent her the child support, she sent me the heart emoji", "vault_lines": ["I sent her the child support, she sent me the heart emoji"], "meaning": "On his May 2018 single, “I’m Upset,” Drake denounced paying child support and made a connection to his dad not paying child support when Drake himself was still a child:\n\nCan’t go fifty-fifty with no ho\nEvery month\nI’m supposed to pay her bills and get her what she want\nI still got like seven years of doin' what I want\nMy dad still got child support from 1991 (One, one, one)\n\nThese lines came at an interesting time as the song was released the day after Drake released his Pusha T diss track, “Duppy Freestyle.” Four days later, Pusha released his response, “The Story of Adidon,” where he exposed Drake for “hiding” Adonis, his son, from the public eye.\n\nNowadays, with Drake three years into fatherhood at the time of this song’s release, his perspective has child support has changed. This line shows that he and Adonis' mother, Sophie Brussaux, are in a good place and have a good co-parenting relationship with each other.", "annotation_id": "22366478", "url": "https://genius.com/22366478", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Spinnin’ vinyl, Teddy P, or is it Lionel?", "vault_lines": ["Spinnin' vinyl, Teddy P, or is it Lionel?"], "meaning": "Teddy Pendergrass was a popular artist in the 80s and 90s, known for hits like July 1980’s “Love T.K.O.”. On the other hand, Lionel Richie is another 80s ballad powerhouse, known for hits like October 1983’s “Hello” and “All Night Long (All Night),” and is one of the best selling artists of all time.\n\nThe line is a callback to a line Ross said on Kanye West’s November 2010 song, “Devil in a New Dress”:\n\nSpinnin' Teddy Pendergrass vinyl as my J burns", "annotation_id": "22365612", "url": "https://genius.com/22365612", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Yeah, make a set sail in Croatia to get the leverage", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, make a set sail in Croatia to get the leverage"], "meaning": "Croatia is a country that’s located in the northwestern part of the Balkan Peninsula. It’s a popular destination for many celebrities during the summer due to its beautiful shores and islands.", "annotation_id": "22364394", "url": "https://genius.com/22364394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Patty Mahomes, ’bout to fall short a couple hunnid", "vault_lines": ["Patty Mahomes, 'bout to fall short a couple hunnid"], "meaning": "Patrick Mahomes is the starting NFL quarterback for the Kansas City Chiefs. The young QB has proven to be one of the most talented players in his league. In the 2018 NFL season—his first season as a starter—Mahomes broke several passing records and won the NFL MVP award, the first Chief player to do so.\n\nDuring the 2019 season, Mahomes took the Chiefs to Super Bowl LIV, the team’s first appearance since 1970, and won against the San Francisco 49ers. Following the win, the Chiefs and Mahomes agreed to a ten-year contract extension worth $477 million with another $26 million in potential bonuses. At the time, this made Mahomes the first athlete with a half-billion-dollar contract and the first NFL player to be the highest-paid athlete in the sports world.\n\nDrake playfully notes here that his contract may be worth more than Mahomes', seeing that Drake is one of the most commercially successful artists in the world. However, he could also be making a nod to Mahomes' loss against the Tampa Bay Buccaneers in Super Bowl LV during the 2020 season, as he likely lost incentive money because of it. The event took place a few weeks prior to this song’s release.\n\nMahomes jokingly reacted to the name-drop via Twitter a few hours after the song’s release:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/PatrickMahomes/status/1367808727450062853", "annotation_id": "22364189", "url": "https://genius.com/22364189", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Livin’ in the 6ix, eight weeks, sun blazin’ After that, the killies just go into hibernation", "vault_lines": ["Livin' in the 6ix, eight weeks, sun blazin'", "After that, the killies just go into hibernation"], "meaning": "“The 6” is a nickname for Toronto, which comes from the 416 and 647 area codes in the city. Also, at one point, Toronto was broken up into six areas, Old Toronto, Scarborough, East York, North York, Etobicoke, and York, giving the nickname more significance.\n\nIn the summer, the crime rate tends to go up. This is due to the increase in temperatures, which makes people uncomfortable, causing them to be more aggressive and violent. Toronto is no different—during the summer of 2018, the city had 109 shootings alone.", "annotation_id": "22363626", "url": "https://genius.com/22363626", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Me and Chubbs drive by, shorty heart racin’", "vault_lines": ["Me and Chubbs drive by, shorty heart racin'"], "meaning": "Nessel “Chubbs” Beezer is one of Drake’s closest friends and his OVO head of security. Drake frequently name-drops him in his music—for example, on his June 2016 song, “4PM in Calabasas,” he raps:\n\nCameras pointing every time I look up\nThat’s why I gotta duck behind Chubb’s shoulder just to hit the kush up", "annotation_id": "22363578", "url": "https://genius.com/22363578", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Ross sittin’ on two hunnid thirty-five acres", "vault_lines": ["Ross sittin' on two hunnid thirty-five acres"], "meaning": "Drake gives a nod to Rick Ross' 235-acre mansion in Georgia. The house is listed as the largest residence in the state of Georgia and boasts a lavish interior, including a bowling alley and swimming pool. The mansion was showcased for a January 2020 edition of Complex Closets:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/JfQ22aiYddU", "annotation_id": "22363526", "url": "https://genius.com/22363526", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Dockin’ jet skis in the Florida Keys", "vault_lines": ["Dockin' jet skis in the Florida Keys"], "meaning": "Florida Keys are a coral key island chain located in southern Florida. Drake partially recorded his June 2018 album Scorpion and songs such as May 2020’s “From Florida With Love” in Miami.\n\nIn the music video of Drizzy’s August 2020 song, “Laugh Now Cry Later,” he rides a jet ski at Nike World Headquarters in Beaverton, Oregon.", "annotation_id": "22363224", "url": "https://genius.com/22363224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Class photographs, Sandi had me on my Urkel", "vault_lines": ["Class photographs, Sandi had me on my Urkel"], "meaning": "Drake makes reference to his childhood class photographs in which he sported rather large glasses that he likens to the Steve Urkel character from the tv series Family Matters. The Urkel character was played by Jaleel White and was renown for his high-water pants, suspenders and forenamed large glasses. In 2014, Drake parodied the Urkel character on Saturday Night Live in this skit by incorporating his style and apparel with the imagery of Lil Wayne. Drake and White met in 2017 for the first time after Drake had parodied the Urkel character on Saturday Night Live. Sandi refers to Drakes mother, Sandra Graham.", "annotation_id": "22362367", "url": "https://genius.com/22362367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Damn, rest in peace Dolla Bill", "vault_lines": ["Damn, rest in peace Dolla Bill"], "meaning": "On September 15, 2020, Drake posted a photo on his Instagram account captioned,\n\nKnown you for so many years and since day one you came around with a smile and positive words for me and your main priority was ALWAYS your family and the guys…you will be missed dearly brother RIP DOLLA BILL @repsupdolla\n\nDolla Bill worked for the graphic design company Dreamwork in Toronto, Canada and collaborated with Drake and the OVO Sound crew throughout the past decade. Chubbs and Baka Not Nice also paid tribute to Dolla Bill upon his passing.", "annotation_id": "22362268", "url": "https://genius.com/22362268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Yeah, heart just turned purple", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, heart just turned purple"], "meaning": "A Purple Heart is a military medal awarded to those wounded or killed while serving – in this context however, Drake means to say he is in his element. This line is also follow-up to his lines in his October 2016 song “Sneakin”, where he raps:\n\nFaceTime with my shawty on tour,\nAnd she textin' purple hearts ‘cause she know that we at war, yuh", "annotation_id": "22362258", "url": "https://genius.com/22362258", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "And that’s facts, Hamdan Mohammed like my third cousin (Facts) Mansoor Mohammed like my real brother (Facts) Dubai embrace me like a Emirati (Facts)", "vault_lines": ["And that's facts, Hamdan Mohammed like my third cousin (Facts)", "Mansoor Mohammed like my real brother (Facts)", "Dubai embrace me like a Emirati (Facts)"], "meaning": "Sheikh Hamdan bin Mohammed Al Maktoum and Sheikh Mansoor bin Mohammed Al Maktoum are the Crown Princes of the Emirate of Dubai.\n\nHere, Drake expresses a special sense of kinship with Dubai and its leadership, comparing himself to an Emirati or native of the United Arab Emirates. During his first visit to Dubai in 2015, Drake connected with the royal family of Dubai. He went on to post the following photo with Sheikh Mansoor bin Mohammed  on his Instagram page, with the caption:\n\nFinally found my long lost brother", "annotation_id": "22362068", "url": "https://genius.com/22362068", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Big Boi got me tryna buy the Braves", "vault_lines": ["Big Boi got me tryna buy the Braves"], "meaning": "Rick Ross speaks on his desire to buy a sports franchise and in particular the MLB’s Atlanta Braves. Ross purchased the former Atlanta home of boxer Evander Holyfield in 2014. Ross has previously spoken on his desire to purchase a sports franchise- in 2014 he discussed minority ownership of the Miami Dolphins with the Dolphins brass and in 2019 he spoke on his desire to own an NFL team with former NBA great Dwyane Wade.", "annotation_id": "22362011", "url": "https://genius.com/22362011", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "We want everything galore, not just Lira Galore", "vault_lines": ["We want everything galore, not just Lira Galore"], "meaning": "Drake references model and influencer Lira Mercer a.k.a. Lira Galore. Galore previously dated rapper Rick Ross and the two were engaged in 2015. The engagement ended up only lasting for two months before the couple split. Galore appeared on the OWN talk show Iyanla, Fix My Life in 2016 to speak on the couple’s split. The full episode can be viewed here. Drake also has history with Galore and retired her jersey in July of 2017 at the Houston gentlemen’s club Dreams. Drake desires things in abundance, like the Galore surname.", "annotation_id": "22361886", "url": "https://genius.com/22361886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Godfather with a garden full of snakes", "vault_lines": ["Godfather with a garden full of snakes"], "meaning": "A reference to the Garden of Eden, where the Devil, incarnated as a snake, persuaded Eve to bite an apple from the tree that was forbidden by God.\n\n\n\nRozay uses this religious imagery to portray himself as the Godfather as in the criminal organisation (like the movies) surrounded by traitors. This is particularly relevant in The Godfather II when Fredo Corleone betrays the family.", "annotation_id": "22361515", "url": "https://genius.com/22361515", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "All my Rolls-Royces got a different body (Facts) Mansory, kitted out with every option (Facts)", "vault_lines": ["All my Rolls-Royces got a different body (Facts)", "Mansory, kitted out with every option (Facts)"], "meaning": "Rolls-Royce is a popular British brand of luxury cars. In November 2019, Drake bought a custom-made, one of a kind Rolls-Royce Phantom for a reported $700,000.\n\nThe custom changes include a Bushukan yellow finish with a signature two-tone paint scheme, courtesy of Mansory—a luxury car modification company. Aside from that, the Rolls-Royce’s signature Spirit of Ecstasy bonnet ornament was replaced with a gold owl, which has two large diamonds that make up the owl’s eyes, that has Drake’s full name—Aubrey Drake Graham—engraved in it.\n\nThe brain-child behind the design was none other than Drake’s friend and vehicle designer, Tony Bet, who unveiled the car on his Instagram.", "annotation_id": "22361452", "url": "https://genius.com/22361452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Air Canada Centre nigga when I die Y’all gon’ have to fly in and do your fake cry First couple rows, you gon’ see the real guys", "vault_lines": ["Air Canada Centre nigga when I die", "Y'all gon' have to fly in and do your fake cry", "First couple rows, you gon' see the real guys"], "meaning": "Air Canada Centre is home to the Toronto Raptors. Drake’s history with the team is deep—he became their global ambassador in 2013, and has raked in a lot of revenue for them, so his idea of being commemorated by the Raptors after death is not unlikely.\n\nDrake explored themes of false admiration from his peers on October 2016’s “Fake Love,” and memorably denounced fake people mourning him on The Game’s June 2015 track, “100”:\n\nY'all better not come to my studio with that fake shit\nY'all better not come to my funeral with that fake shit\n\nHaving his funeral in a sports arena would follow late legends such as Michael Jackson, Nipsey Hussle, and Kobe Bryant, who each had their memorial services at Staples Center in downtown Los Angeles.\n\n\n\nDrake on sidelines during the 2019 NBA playoffs", "annotation_id": "22361272", "url": "https://genius.com/22361272", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Of course Pull up to the front in a fleet of Suburbans Flooded French immersion with the Secret Service Shit is so obvious, it defeats the purpose", "vault_lines": ["Pull up to the front in a fleet of Suburbans", "Flooded French immersion with the Secret Service", "Shit is so obvious, it defeats the purpose"], "meaning": "French immersion is a form of bilingual education where kids who don’t speak French as a first language will receive their learning in French through completion of the standard curriculum classes in French. Drake’s son, Adonis Graham, attends Toronto French School, a private French immersion school.  He’s half-French since his mother, Sophie Brussaux, is from France, which makes this an ideal school for him.\n\nThe United States Secret Service is a federal law enforcement agency under Homeland Security, elected to protect U.S. politicians and their families. They transport U.S. presidents in motorcades, using Surburban vehicles, to ensure their safety. Drake travels Adonis to school in a similar fashion, while also joking that the large motorcade defeats the purpose of having a “secret” service—meaning his security—since it won’t be much of a secret anymore.\n\n\n\n\nA Presidential Motorcade", "annotation_id": "22361244", "url": "https://genius.com/22361244", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "We all grateful for Weezy, but no one more than me", "vault_lines": ["We all grateful for Weezy, but no one more than me"], "meaning": "Weezy, or Lil Wayne, is an American rapper hailing from New Orleans. His indisputable impact on music credits his reputation of often being cited as one of the greatest rappers of all time. He has been consistently mentioned numerous times as a core influencer for many artists, such as Kendrick Lamar, Lil Uzi Vert, and of course Drake.\n\nKnown for founding Young Money Entertainment, Lil Wayne signed Drake to his label in 2009. Under the label, eight of Drake’s albums landed a number one spot on the Billboard 200 chart.\n\n\n\nDrake previously addressed his connection with Weezy on his 2015 track “6PM in New York”:\n\nLil Wayne could not have found him a better successor", "annotation_id": "22361185", "url": "https://genius.com/22361185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Real life, the whole fam’ goons like Ralo", "vault_lines": ["Real life, the whole fam' goons like Ralo"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out the Atlanta rapper Ralo, whose social media handle is @ralofamgoon. On Ralo’s January 2017’s Young Thug, Lil Uzi Vert and Lil Yachty assisted “Young Nigga,” Yachty raps:\n\nRalo fam goon, don’t nobody go harder\n\n\n\nRalo’s fam goon merch hoody", "annotation_id": "22361043", "url": "https://genius.com/22361043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Yeah, dropped him off at school, big day for my lil’ man Recess hits, daddy prolly made another M School bell rings and I’m out there to get him again", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, dropped him off at school, big day for my lil' man", "Recess hits, daddy prolly made another M", "School bell rings and I'm out there to get him again"], "meaning": "On September 9, 2020, Adonis Graham, Drake’s son, started attending school. Drake and Adonis' mother, Sophie Brussaux both shared pictures on Instagram to commemorate their child’s first day of school.\n\n\n\nSince Drake is a superstar, he might get asked quite frequently whether he knows certain artists or not, using Beyoncé and Nicki Minaj as examples.", "annotation_id": "22361033", "url": "https://genius.com/22361033", "state": "accepted", "votes": 121, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "How I’m so famous, gotta live where they hide the hills?", "vault_lines": ["How I'm so famous, gotta live where they hide the hills?"], "meaning": "Drake is alluding to the exclusive, gated California neighborhood Hidden Hills. Business Insider reported on his 2012 purchase of a 7.7 million dollar house in Hidden Hills. Like other celebrities who live there, such as Miley Cyrus, Kylie Jenner, and The Weeknd, Drake now values the perks that come with esoteric living.\n\nDrake references the same property in his 2016 song Summer Sixteen:\n\nNow I got a house in LA, now I got a bigger pool than Ye.\nAnd look man, Ye’s pool is nice, mine’s just bigger’s what I’m saying", "annotation_id": "22360779", "url": "https://genius.com/22360779", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Foot Locker, twenty deep, a nigga spend a dub", "vault_lines": ["Foot Locker, twenty deep, a nigga spend a dub"], "meaning": "Foot Locker is a popular American retail store that sells shoes and sports clothing. A “dub” is usually slang for $20 worth of weed but in Ross' case, he means a lot of money.\n\n\n\n\nA Foot Locker store", "annotation_id": "22360756", "url": "https://genius.com/22360756", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Tryin’, tryin’, tryin’, tryin’", "vault_lines": ["Tryin', tryin', tryin', tryin'", "I been tryin', tryin', tryin', tryin'", "But none of my brothers could caption that line (I been tryin', tryin', tryin', tryin')"], "meaning": "The intro, break, and outro of “Lemon Pepper Freestyle” sample the live version of Danish duo Quadron’s March 2010 track, “Pressure”:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ILPNhmg3geM&t=101s", "annotation_id": "22360680", "url": "https://genius.com/22360680", "state": "accepted", "votes": 241, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Ushered a generation in, these are where my confessions live", "vault_lines": ["Ushered a generation in, these are where my confessions live"], "meaning": "Drizzy and his OVO Sound label have inspired a new generation of musicians in both hip-hop and R&B over the last decade. Artists such as The Weeknd, PARTYNEXTDOOR, and Popcaan all started at OVO.\n\nDrake uses clever wordplay to compare the success and influence of his music to that of Usher’s 2004 fourth studio album Confessions, which is one of the most influential and critically acclaimed R&B albums of our time, and quite literally “ushered” in a new generation of musicians, including Drake himself.", "annotation_id": "22360662", "url": "https://genius.com/22360662", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Practicin’ social distance with all these snitch niggas", "vault_lines": ["Practicin' social distance with all these snitch niggas"], "meaning": "Social distancing is a health practice that involves standing six feet, or about two arms length, away from another person.\n\nThis strategy became widely used during the COVID-19 pandemic as one way to mitigate the spread of the deadly virus from person to person.\n\nRick Ross reapplies this to his life, staying away from  “snitches,” which are people who tattletale.", "annotation_id": "22360598", "url": "https://genius.com/22360598", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "For real, and my city love me like DeMar DeRozan", "vault_lines": ["For real, and my city love me like DeMar DeRozan"], "meaning": "DeMar DeRozan is a professional basketball player in the NBA. He currently plays for the San Antonio Spurs, but spent the first nine years of his career on the Toronto Raptors, the team of Drake’s hometown.\n\n\n\nDeRozan was a beloved player by the Raptors fanbase, co-leading the team with fellow star Kyle Lowry for most of the 2010s. The duo lead the Raptors five playoff runs and multiple Eastern Conference Regular Season titles, but consistently ran into problems in the NBA Playoffs, getting knocked out by LeBron James‘ Cleveland Cavaliers in DeRozan’s last three years with the team. On July 18, 2018, following the team’s third straight playoff exit at the hands of James, DeRozan was traded out of San Antonio.\n\nIn the weeks after the trade, the city of Toronto and DeRozan expressed their mutual love for each other in many forms. DeRozan took to Instagram just days after the trade to express what his time in the city meant to him:\n\nWords could never express what you’ve meant to me. I was just a 19 year old kid from Compton when we first met, but you took me in and embraced me as one of your own. I am so grateful for the Love and Passion that you’ve given me over the past 9 years. All I ever wanted to do was duplicate it 10x over just to show my appreciation. Thank you Toronto, thank you Canada. #Comp10 #ProveEm.\n\nDrake himself even took to social media to express his admiration for DeRozan after the trade:\n\nTo my brother @demar_derozan I want to say 10 million thank you’s on behalf of YOUR city. You are a fixture in Toronto forever and you gave everything you had[…] Through your leadership we had the most exciting years in franchise history. I am grateful to have witnessed your combination of skill, persistence, and loyalty from the same seats every night. Thank you for being an incredible captain and an even better friend\n\nDeRozan’s trade to San Antonio gave the Raptors future hall of famer Kawhi Leonard, which culminated in the first-ever championship for the franchise. DeRozan commented on the finals victory and his trade, saying\n\nI was happy. Good for them. Happy for them. I spent a lot of time with a few of them guys this offseason. It was good for them. I spent nine years of my basketball career there. Even at times when I see pictures seeing my jersey in the crowd, you are there some way somehow. You got to be happy with that.", "annotation_id": "22360449", "url": "https://genius.com/22360449", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "(M-M-M-Maybach Music)", "vault_lines": ["Biggest, you feel me? (M-M-M-Maybach Music)"], "meaning": "This is the tag for Rick Ross’s record label Maybach Music Group. It is often prominently featured in the mix of songs by or featuring Ross, and also appears on songs by artists who are signed to the label.\n\nThe woman heard saying the tag is Jessica Gomes, an Australian model. She told the story behind the tag’s origin in a 2012 feature with DJcityTV:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4vOExWDh9KQ", "annotation_id": "22360427", "url": "https://genius.com/22360427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "line": "Death Row, that’s for these niggas, I’ma hit ’em up Makaveli, it’s all eyes on me Pinkie rings, it’s still M.O.B.", "vault_lines": ["Death Row, that's for these niggas, I'ma hit 'em up", "Makaveli, it's all eyes on me", "Pinkie rings, it's still M.O.B."], "meaning": "Rick Ross pays homage to Death Row Records signee, 2Pac, referencing some of his most influential work in the past: from Shakur’s June 1996 diss track, “Hit ’Em Up,” to his February 1996 album, All Eyez On Me, and his November 1996 posthumous album, The Don Killuminati: The 7 Day Theory, which was released under his stage name, Makaveli and is commonly shortened to Makaveli. Both projects peaked at #1 on the Billboard 200 chart.\n\n\n\nRoss continues the Tupac references with “M.O.B.”, which Pac tattooed on the back of his right arm. Standing for “Money Over Bitches,” “M.O.B.” is also one of 2Pac’s most frequent phrases used in his music, for example, on “Blasphemy,” the fifth track off of Makaveli, he spits the following lines:\n\nNow, rule one: get your cash on, M.O.B.\nThat’s Money Over Bitches, ‘cause they breed envy\n\nMoreover, “pinkie rings” are often associated with organized crime and affiliation, which could be an allusion to 2Pac’s gangsta rap style and his gang-member-involved drive-by murder.", "annotation_id": "22360372", "url": "https://genius.com/22360372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 120, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 36, "clicked": 36, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-lemon-pepper-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Lemon Pepper Freestyle"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "They’ll be mournin’ you like 8 a.m. (R.I.P.)", "vault_lines": ["They’ll be mournin' you like 8 a.m. (R.I.P.)"], "meaning": "This is a alliteration comparing the “mourning” of his enemy’s death and 8AM, the time in the “morning”.", "annotation_id": "20360065", "url": "https://genius.com/20360065", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Now they wanna know me since I hit the top, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Now they wanna know me since I hit the top, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake is putting light on the fact that people want to be friends with him and know who he is now that he is a celebrity. This isn’t the first time Drake has been vocal on his songs about this either. On his 2014 track “How About Now”, Drake raps:\n\nYeah, you ain’t really fuck with me way back then, girl\nBut how ‘bout now?\n'Cause I’m up right now, and you stuck right now", "annotation_id": "15355364", "url": "https://genius.com/15355364", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "I like all the profit, man, I hardly do percents (I don’t do that shit)", "vault_lines": ["I like all the profit, man, I hardly do percents (I don't do that shit)"], "meaning": "Drake enjoys profiting from his work as a musician and only gives a percentage of his profit to certain individuals who are close to him.\n\nDrake has touched on this subject before with this line from the song, “6PM in New York”, from his 2015 mixtape, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late:\n\nI’m managed by my friends that I grew up with\nI’d rather give that 15% to people I fuck with\n\nDrake is managed by his close friend Oliver El-Khatib. Another one of Drake’s best friends, Noah “40” Shebib, has been an executive producer for several of Drake’s largely successful projects such as Views and Nothing Was the Same.\n\nThese are individuals Drake does not mind sharing a percentage of his profit with because they are his life-long friends and have helped him become a record-breaking artist.", "annotation_id": "15182701", "url": "https://genius.com/15182701", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "I don’t have no time for no misunderstandings again", "vault_lines": ["I don't have no time for no misunderstandings again"], "meaning": "Drake is probably referring to when Puff Daddy allegedly punched him outside a Miami nightclub for stealing the beat used on “0 to 100 / The Catch Up”. The Bad Boy label owner later referred to the incident as ‘a misunderstanding’.", "annotation_id": "14986789", "url": "https://genius.com/14986789", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Like I went blind, dog, you gotta hand it to me (Gotta gimme that shit, dog)", "vault_lines": ["Like I went blind, dog, you gotta hand it to me"], "meaning": "A blind person will sometimes need assistance, such as being handed an object because they cannot see its immediate location. “Dog” may be a reference to guide dogs, also sometimes utilized by blind people. These lines reference the assistance that Drake has gotten from his friends as noted in the previous lines. “You gotta hand it to me” also indicates that Drake deserves recognition for orchestrating these accomplishments.", "annotation_id": "14960727", "url": "https://genius.com/14960727", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "I don’t know nobody else that’s doin’ this", "vault_lines": ["I don't know nobody else that's doin' this"], "meaning": "Drizzy is one of a select few in the rap game that can consistently put up huge numbers with almost everything they release.\n\nDrake is somewhat of a phenomena, dominating the Billboard Hot 100 three times in 2018 alone with “God’s Plan”, “Nice For What”, and most recently with “In My Feelings”. He was also able to reach 749k album sales in the first week of Scorpion’s release.", "annotation_id": "14928734", "url": "https://genius.com/14928734", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Pullin’ back the curtain by myself, take a look, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Pullin' back the curtain by myself, take a look, ayy"], "meaning": "To “pull back the curtain” is to unveil some hidden meaning or truth. This imagery calls to mind magicians, who are normally unwilling to reveal the secrets to their tricks.\n\n\n\nDrake is giving us all the behind-the-scenes details of his life on Scorpion; from his child, to his love life, to his standing in the game—Drizzy hides nothing as he is not afraid to acknowledge these details.", "annotation_id": "14899601", "url": "https://genius.com/14899601", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "My head is spinnin’, from smokin’ the chicken, the bass is kickin’ My head is spinnin’, from smokin’ the chicken, the bass is kickin’ My head is spinnin’, from smokin’ the chicken, the bass is kickin’ My head is spinnin’", "vault_lines": ["My head is spinnin', from smokin' the chicken, the bass is kickin'", "My head is spinnin'", "From smokin' the chicken, the bass is kickin'"], "meaning": "The obscure vocal sample for the chorus originates from Lil Sko’s verse in DJ Squeeky’s 1993 release, “Drink A Yak.”\n\nSqueeky sampled Sko’s verse for the chorus in Mack Daddy Ju’s 1995 release, “My Head Is Spinnin,” which is the version that has been sampled for “Nonstop”:\nhttps://youtu.be/k7CPCRb0SsA?t=260&si=0cTYzUiM-v3nMAVP\n\n  \n\n\nhttps://youtu.be/wRoc_Il8QfA?si=zPm-3n-ndoJ_eypM&t=2\n\n  \n\n\nDrake’s insertion of the non-stopping Rolex line accentuates the repetitive nature of the chorus. He is essentially giving a shout out to the Dirty South while incorporating the repeating chorus style to complement the “Nonstop” theme in this song.", "annotation_id": "14895527", "url": "https://genius.com/14895527", "state": "accepted", "votes": 111, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "I’m a wig splitta, I’m a tall figure", "vault_lines": ["I'm a wig splitta, I'm a tall figure"], "meaning": "A wig split is the act of inflicting someone with head trauma, usually a headshot.\n\nAs a tall figure, he is metaphorically looking down on the rest of the game. This may also be a literal reference to his stature, as Drake has referenced his height directly in other songs such as “Sneakin'”:\n\n6'1\", man, a nigga need some leg room", "annotation_id": "14891217", "url": "https://genius.com/14891217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Money for revenge, man, that’s hardly an expense", "vault_lines": ["Money for revenge, man, that’s hardly an expense"], "meaning": "During his beef with Pusha-T, Pusha-T claimed that Drake offered $100,000 for any dirt on him.\n\n\n\nIn this line, Drake addresses the claim, implying that money spent on vengeance is worth it, while also pointing out that the $100k price is nothing compared to the amount of money he makes, as suggested by the rest of the verse.\n\nThis line, along with a few others that had been released after “The Story of Adidon” dropped, indicate Drake did in fact capitalize on his offer to gain hurtful information on Pusha-T and Kanye. According to Rap-A-Lot Records CEO and friend J. Prince, Drake had been working on a career ending diss for both rappers before being asked not to release it. A week after Scorpion was released, Drake appeared on London’s Link Up TV, where he rapped in his freestyle, “I know so much shit i can’t expose, I keep it inside and laugh on my own.” One might say Drake believes he already has his revenge on Push and Kanye by having this alleged information in his back-pocket.", "annotation_id": "14891095", "url": "https://genius.com/14891095", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Bodies start to drop, ayy (Hit the floor)", "vault_lines": ["Bodies start to drop, ayy (Hit the floor)"], "meaning": "Drake is possibly making reference to the song “Bodies” from the heavy metal rock band Drowning Pool, mimicking the iconic line, “Let the bodies hit the floor”.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/04F4xlWSFh0", "annotation_id": "14890125", "url": "https://genius.com/14890125", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "I’m a bill printer, I’m a gravedigger", "vault_lines": ["I'm a bill printer, I'm a gravedigger"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake brags about his great wealth, describing it as so much that he might as well be printing bills.\n\nDrake also doesn’t literally mean he digs graves. Rather, it is a statement that because of his influence, Drake has the ability to end a career or a life. He makes a similar association in the song “Mob Ties”:\n\nStandin' over coffin with a hammer and a nail\n\nDrake could also be referencing his beef with Pusha T as he sent a mock bill totaling $100k to G.O.O.D Music for “career reviving” after “Duppy Freesyle.”", "annotation_id": "14888475", "url": "https://genius.com/14888475", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Either hand is the upper hand (Oh, yeah, shit) Got a bubba on my other hand (Woah, yeah, shit, yeah) This shit ain’t no hundred bands (Nah, nah, nah, nah)", "vault_lines": ["Either hand is the upper hand (Oh, yeah, shit)", "Got a bubba on my other hand (Woah, yeah, shit, yeah)", "This shit ain't no hundred bands (Nah, nah, nah, nah)"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his Richard Mille (RM 38-01) – Bubba Watson Edition. A Bubba is an extremely limited and prestigious timepiece that can cost anywhere over $200,000.\n\nMetaphorically, he relates his timepiece to his “upper hand” daily situations and events.\n\n\n\nWith the use of “Either hand is the upper hand” Drake emphasizes that whatever situation he is faced with, he will always come out on top. A similar mention of being ambidextrous from earlier in this track follows this same notion:\n\nI just took it left like I’m ambidex'\nBitch, I move through London with the Euro step", "annotation_id": "14883092", "url": "https://genius.com/14883092", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Palace look like Buckingham", "vault_lines": ["Palace look like Buckingham"], "meaning": "Drake’s mansion is still under construction, but it is clearly shaping up to be a massive house. It is located in the Bridle Path, Toronto’s wealthiest neighbourhood (previous residents have included Prince).\n\nDrake extends his references to London by comparing his mansion to Buckingham Palace, the iconic residence of the British monarch constructed in the 18th century.\n\n\n\nDrake’s incomplete Toronto mansion", "annotation_id": "14881354", "url": "https://genius.com/14881354", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "(Tay Keith, fuck these niggas up)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the producer tag of Tay Keith, producer of this track. This tag is said by Lil Juice, rapper from Clarksville, Tennessee.\n\nTay Keith worked with rappers such as BlocBoy JB, Yo Gotti, Blac Youngsta, Moneybagg Yo and more. He rose to fame for producing BlocBoy JB’s platinum certified hit song “Look Alive” featuring Drake. This is his second time working with Drake after “Look Alive.”", "annotation_id": "14880873", "url": "https://genius.com/14880873", "state": "accepted", "votes": 183, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "This the flow that got the block hot, shit got super hot, ayy", "vault_lines": ["This the flow that got the block hot, shit got super hot", "This the flow that got the block hot, shit got super hot, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake uses a similar flow and style on this song to his verse on his collaboration with Memphis artist BlocBoy JB, “Look Alive”. BlocBoy’s fanbase and exposure grew significantly after his collaboration with Drake. With Drake’s father being from Memphis, he has strong ties with artists from there and frequently collaborates with them. This song is also produced by Memphis producer, Tay Keith, who produced “Look Alive” as well.\n\nThis is also a reference to Drake’s mentor and Cash Money President Lil Wayne’s 1999 album Tha Block is Hot. It was Wayne’s debut album and his first platinum album as a soloist. It is free from profanity due to his mother Jacita’s wishes except for the song “Fuck the World.”", "annotation_id": "14879479", "url": "https://genius.com/14879479", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "I’m a bar spitta, I’m a hard hitta Yeah, I’m light-skinned, but I’m still a dark nigga", "vault_lines": ["I'm a bar spitta, I’m a hard hitta", "Yeah, I’m light-skinned, but I'm still a dark nigga"], "meaning": "Men of “light-skinned\" complexion are often stereotyped as being ‘softer’ or less masculine than those of a darker tone. Throughout his career, Drake has been an easy target for this rhetoric due to his music style, message, and stature in pop culture.\n\nDrake cleverly counters this stereotype by stating that although he isn’t dark physically, he is dark in a sense that he holds less-than-ambient personality traits, perhaps displayed in his numerous beefs, most recently with Pusha T.\n\nMore specifically, this may be a direct response to the series of coloristic shots aimed at Drake throughout Pusha’s infamous diss track.\n\nConfused, always felt you weren’t Black enough\nAfraid to grow it ‘cause your 'fro wouldn’t nap enough\n\n\n\nThis is also a response to Kanye’s “No Mistakes”, in which he addressed Drake by referring to him as “light skin”:\n\nToo bold to type you, too rich to fight you\nCalm down, you light skin!", "annotation_id": "14876822", "url": "https://genius.com/14876822", "state": "accepted", "votes": 145, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "This a Rollie, not a stopwatch, shit don’t ever stop", "vault_lines": ["This a Rollie, not a stopwatch, shit don't ever stop"], "meaning": "“Rollie” is common slang for a Rolex timepiece, while a stopwatch is a very simple timepiece that measures time when activated. The Oyster Rolex models are equipped with self-winding mechanical movement systems which allows for perpetual time keeping in the same way Drake hasn’t stopped and continues to put music out that make the charts.\n\nOn Rolexes, as with other high-end mechanical watches, the second hand “sweeps” or “glides”, as opposed to cheaper quartz watches which tick, stopping and starting at each second. Drake’s work ethic is akin to that of a Rolex which never stops.\n\nThe Canadian rapper has embraced Rolexes as a symbol of success, as he owns more than several.\n\n\n\nDrake is pictured above wearing a Rolex Day-Date, also known as a Rolex President.", "annotation_id": "14876794", "url": "https://genius.com/14876794", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Al Haymon checks off of all of my events", "vault_lines": ["Al Haymon checks off of all of my events"], "meaning": "Alan “Al” Haymon is an American boxing adviser/manager and is the adviser to Floyd Mayweather, Jr. He negotiates contracts for Mayweather as well as numerous other athletes and has subsequently amassed a vast wealth throughout his career. None of Haymon’s fighters have third-party promoters, therefore he is paid to coordinate events for his athletes and subsequently receives a hefty paycheck. Drizzy feels similarly seeing as it is estimated he charges $350k-$600k for a show.", "annotation_id": "14876642", "url": "https://genius.com/14876642", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Bills so big, I call ’em Williams, for real", "vault_lines": ["Bills so big, I call 'em Williams, for real"], "meaning": "Drake’s estimated net-worth is $100 million as of the release of Scorpion. He states that due to his immense wealth, he refers to his bills as Williams because they are so expensive. The name William is commonly shortened to Bill.\n\nFurthermore, this is a continuation of the previous line’s British Royalty theme. Prince William is the Duke of Cambridge, second in line for the British throne behind his father, Charles, the Prince of Wales.", "annotation_id": "14876628", "url": "https://genius.com/14876628", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "And you know it’s King Slime Drizzy, damn", "vault_lines": ["And you know it's King Slime Drizzy, damn"], "meaning": "Drizzy shouts out his collaborator and former tourmate Young Thug who often refers to himself as “Slime.” Slime is a motif that’s prevalent in Thugger’s music and social media persona, he even named a mixtape series after the phrase.\n\nThe meaning of the word slime is similar to “gangsta”, “grimy”, or “repulsive”, depending on the context. He explains the word’s meaning around the 25:00 mark:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/ip8dztJp840?t=25m", "annotation_id": "14876604", "url": "https://genius.com/14876604", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "I just took it left like I’m ambidex’ Bitch, I move through London with the Eurostep (Two) Got a sneaker deal and I ain’t break a sweat Catch me ’cause I’m gone (Outta there, I’m gone) How I go from 6 to 23 like I’m LeBron?", "vault_lines": ["Got a sneaker deal and I ain't break a sweat", "Catch me 'cause I'm gone (Outta there, I’m gone)", "How I go from 6 to 23 like I'm LeBron?", "I just took it left like I'm ambidex'", "Bitch, I move through London with the Eurostep (Two)"], "meaning": "By taking the ball left, as an ambidextrous ball player, Drake is saying that he has no weak side.\n\nDrake continues his basketball metaphor with the “Euro step,” an offensive move where the player takes a deliberate step in one direction, followed by a quick step in the other direction to move past his defender.\n\n\n\nDrake cleverly creates a play on words associating the Euro step with London, the capital of the England, a country in Europe (though on its way out of the European Union). Drake spent two days in London the week after he released “Nice For What” and has been known to party there.\n\nDuring his tenure with the Miami Heat, LeBron wore the number 6 jersey, as the number 23 was retired in honor of Michael Jordan. Upon returning to the Cleveland Cavaliers, James switched back to the iconic number 23.\n\n\n\nWhile placing himself at the level of LeBron James, arguably the greatest basketball player of his time, Drake is referencing his hometown Toronto, “The 6” and indicates how far he’s come in his career to become the greatest in the game and acquire his own Jordans sneaker deal in “23”; the final connection comparing himself with basketball stardom.", "annotation_id": "14876432", "url": "https://genius.com/14876432", "state": "accepted", "votes": 312, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Pinky ring ’til I get a wedding ring (Woah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Pinky ring 'til I get a wedding ring (Woah, yeah)"], "meaning": "In 2017, a source told Hollywood Life Drake’s plans for marriage once he finds the right girl:\n\nDrake does want to settle down and have kids one day, and he’s confident that the right woman will come along at the right time. Until then, Drake’s not forcing anything, and he’s making the most of the lifestyle that comes along with being Drake.\n\nDrake may be referencing Rihanna–nearly two years ago, Drake stood up on the 2016 MTV Video Music Awards During 2016, Drake and Rihanna also collaborated on “Too Good” and engaged in other supposed love affairs.\n\nSince then, in an interview with Vogue, Rihanna has explained that they are no longer friends. Although he is not currently involved with any women, he will continue to flaunt and live his life.", "annotation_id": "14876331", "url": "https://genius.com/14876331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "She just said I’m bae, I hit the thizzle dance (Mac Dre shit)", "vault_lines": ["She just said I'm bae, I hit the thizzle dance (Mac Dre shit)"], "meaning": "The Thizzle dance is the legendary song from Bay Area rap icon Mac Dre. The namesake song is enormously popular among locals and the 6 god himself:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TkG_J7XP6eg\n\nAlso, note the double entendre. The girl Drake is referring to calls him “bae,” which is slang for—among other things— “babe” or “before all else”, while Mac Dre is from the San Francisco Bay Area.", "annotation_id": "14876232", "url": "https://genius.com/14876232", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Future took the business and ran it for me I let Ollie take the owl, told him brand it for me I get 2 million a pop and that’s standard for me", "vault_lines": ["Future took the business and ran it for me", "I let Ollie take the owl, told him brand it for me", "I get 2 million a pop and that’s standard for me", "And I hardly take offense"], "meaning": "Future The Prince, Drake’s DJ, was brought in to manage the business-end of his career since 2013.\n\nOliver El-Khatib, who runs OVO, spoke with GQ in 2016 about the company and how he helped make OVO into what it is. He is also credited with coining the phrase “October’s Very Own.”\n\nThis may refer to the $4 million 2017 Snapchat New Year’s Eve performance that featured Drake as a headlining act.", "annotation_id": "14876228", "url": "https://genius.com/14876228", "state": "accepted", "votes": 151, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "line": "Look, I just flipped a switch (Flipped, flipped)", "vault_lines": ["Look, I just flipped a switch (Flipped, flipped)"], "meaning": "This was one of the many lines first revealed by Drake on June 22, 2018 through  promotional billboards that were placed in Toronto for Scorpion.\n\n\n\nDrake “flipping the switch” most likely refers to his aggressive side, as it was speculated that Drake would be “emotionless” on Side A of the album.", "annotation_id": "14876217", "url": "https://genius.com/14876217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nonstop", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 26, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-nonstop-lyrics", "genius_title": "Nonstop"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "[Produced by DJ Dahi]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The instrumental to “Worst Behavior” is considered by some (me) to be one of the best beats of 2013. A neck-snapping kick drum and an 808 snare compliment what sounds like a stick hitting wood being pitched up and down, while auto-tuned melisma gives an effect similar to that of a Future song. Occasionally the beat gets suspended in time, stuttering over a tiny fraction for up to a quaver and often extending the effect of the kick drum.\n\nAlso worth noting that while the natural key would appear to be F sharp minor, the bass never actually hits that note, instead going from a VI-V cadence to the third and fourth above. This gives the bass line an uneasy lingering feel.\n\n\n\nDahi broke down how he made the beat for “Worst Behavior in this documentary:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AaXoFEjvpbM&t=8m22s", "annotation_id": "3262507", "url": "https://genius.com/3262507", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Always hated the boy, but now the boy is the man Motherfucker, I done growed up", "vault_lines": ["Always hated the boy, but now the boy is the man", "Motherfucker, I done growed up"], "meaning": "Drake is likely talking about himself here. He’s often referred to himself as “The Boy” and he even does it in this album multiple times, including this song like he does here.\n\nIn “Furthest Thing”, he states\n\nYours truly, the boy\n\nIn “From Time”, Drizzy again states:\n\nWho you settling for? Who better for you than the boy, huh?\n\n\n\nHe’s reiterating the issue in these bars: people always hated him for being himself, but now he doesn’t have to care because he’s undeniably running the game right now.", "annotation_id": "3044274", "url": "https://genius.com/3044274", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Remember? Remember? Motherfucker Remember? Hold up, hold my phone They used to never want to hear us Remember? Motherfucker never loved us Remember? Motherfucker Remember? Worst behavior", "vault_lines": ["Motherfucker never loved us", "On my worst behavior, no?", "They used to never want to hear us", "Remember? Motherfucker never loved us", "I'm on my worst behavior", "Fucker never loved us", "Motherfuckers never loved us", "Man, motherfuckers never loved us", "Hold up, hold my phone", "Mothafuckers never loved us", "Nigga never loved us"], "meaning": "Remember when Drake was just a budding Canadian rapper who many people thought would never make it? Well, those days are long gone and Drizzy is now a Grammy award-winning artist who also happens to be running the rap game. And to all the people who doubted him, he’s going to rub it in, hence be on his “worst behavior”.\n\n\n\nThen she start tellin' me how I’ll never be as big as Trey Songz, Boy was she wrong\n\n\nLyrics taken from “From Time” by Drake.", "annotation_id": "2217451", "url": "https://genius.com/2217451", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Hold up, hold my phone Motherfuckers never loved us Fucker never loved us Now you want to roll one Motherfucker never loved us So everywhere we go now, full cup", "vault_lines": ["Motherfucker never loved us", "Remember? Motherfucker never loved us", "Fucker never loved us", "Motherfuckers never loved us", "Man, motherfuckers never loved us", "Hold up, hold my phone", "Now you want to roll one", "So everywhere we go now, full cup", "Mothafuckers never loved us", "Nigga never loved us"], "meaning": "“Hold my phone” is likely a reference to Drake’s infamous Hot 97 freestyle where he rapped off his Blackberry and never lived it down.\n\n\n\n“Hold my phone” could also indicate Drake is about to fight someone and doesn’t want his phone to get messed up, similar to a girl asking someone to hold her earrings before throwing down. He knew they were never rooting for him so he’s not about to be friendly and roll one (smoke weed) with them now", "annotation_id": "2216453", "url": "https://genius.com/2216453", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Drake, you know ain’t nothin’ changed With these funny style niggas, we done put on in the game", "vault_lines": ["You know me? You know me?", "You ain't know, now you know now", "Same old pimp, Drake, you know ain't nothin' changed", "With these funny style niggas, we done put on in the game"], "meaning": "Drake is borrowing the first half of this line from Mase in “Mo' Money, Mo' Problems”, but changes it up for the second half of the line.\n\nDrake feels he’s the only rapper who’s putting out music with a new sound and style right now. He knows that other rappers' music sounds tired, he is the only one whose sound is evolving\n\n\n\nDrake’s addressing the rappers (perhaps Kendrick Lamar?) that he feels he helped get exposure and now are acting funny.", "annotation_id": "2216177", "url": "https://genius.com/2216177", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "You ain’t know, now you know now", "vault_lines": ["You know me? You know me?", "You ain't know, now you know now", "Same old pimp, Drake, you know ain't nothin' changed"], "meaning": "Rephrasing The Notorious B.I.G.’s line on “Juicy”:\n\nAnd if you don’t know, now you know, nigga", "annotation_id": "2214259", "url": "https://genius.com/2214259", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "I’m with my whole set, tennis matches at the crib I swear I could beat Serena when she playin’ with her left", "vault_lines": ["I'm with my whole set, tennis matches at the crib", "I swear I could beat Serena when she playin' with her left"], "meaning": "“Whole set” refers to his OVO crew. Drake plays off the tennis reference (set) to say they’re all playing tennis at his house – i.e. his house is big enough to have a tennis court.\n\n\n\nDrake fancies himself the rap game Serena Williams who is the number one ranked women’s tennis player. Drake actually did play Serena, and beat her, while she was using her left hand\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to:\nhttps://genius.com/138567", "annotation_id": "2213491", "url": "https://genius.com/2213491", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Bar mitzvah money like my last name Mordecai", "vault_lines": ["Bar mitzvah money like my last name Mordecai"], "meaning": "Mordecai is a prophet from The Book of Esther in the Hebrew Bible. Haman the Agagite made a decree that all should prostrate themselves before Haman, but Mordecai refused to do so.\n\n\n\nIt’s only customary to get large sums of money at one’s Bar Mitzvah. Drake’s jewish, so just ask Drake himself.\n\n\n\nDrake’s got that Talmud money.", "annotation_id": "2213469", "url": "https://genius.com/2213469", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Who else making rap albums, doing numbers like it’s pop?", "vault_lines": ["Who else making rap albums, doing numbers like it's pop?"], "meaning": "Drake is questioning the other rappers in the game and wondering how many of them are as successful in record sales as him. Especially since his albums are selling more like pop albums, which is the best selling genre. We already know he’s “got the most number ones ever.”\n\nMany people think he says “Pac” here. Even if he is referencing 2Pac, he’d be doing pretty well: including his posthumous released work, nine of Pac’s albums went platinum, and All Eyez On Me alone went 9x platinum", "annotation_id": "2213373", "url": "https://genius.com/2213373", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "I just ask for some blessings at my grandmother’s grave", "vault_lines": ["I just ask for some blessings at my grandmother's grave"], "meaning": "Drake’s grandma, Evelyn Sher or “Bubby”, passed on Thanksgiving in 2012. He was very close to her, as she partially raised him. He’s even included her voice in his music.", "annotation_id": "2213358", "url": "https://genius.com/2213358", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Still at it, scrub Js with a toothbrush", "vault_lines": ["Still at it, scrub Js with a toothbrush"], "meaning": "This is a good way to keep your kicks clean. This method Drake has been at for awhile.\n\n\n\nHe keeps it real, trying to keep his kicks in good shape, versus just buying new ones as soon as there’s a scuff. This symbolizes that nothing has changed since he became famous.", "annotation_id": "2213287", "url": "https://genius.com/2213287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 120, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Who’s hot, who not? Tell me, who rock, who sell out in stores? You tell me who flop, who copped the new drop Whose jewels got rocks", "vault_lines": ["You know me? You know me?", "You owe me, you owe me", "Who's hot, who not?", "Tell me, who rock, who sell out in stores?", "You tell me who flop, who copped the new drop", "Whose jewels got rocks"], "meaning": "Drake borrows these lines directly from Mase’s verse on “Mo' Money Mo' Problems”.\n\nConsidering how wealthy Drake is (Forbes' #6 top hip Hop earner in 2012) he’s got something in common with ‘96 Ma$e – more money, more problems.", "annotation_id": "2213284", "url": "https://genius.com/2213284", "state": "accepted", "votes": 200, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "This ain’t the son you raised who used to take the Acura", "vault_lines": ["This ain't the son you raised who used to take the Acura"], "meaning": "Nod to “Underground Kings” where he reminisces driving the very unlavish Acura:\n\nhttp://rapgenius.com/439285\n\nHe’s changed a lot since then:", "annotation_id": "2213280", "url": "https://genius.com/2213280", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "5 a.m. then go and shoot Degrassi up on Morningside", "vault_lines": ["5 a.m. then go and shoot Degrassi up on Morningside"], "meaning": "They filmed (“shot”) Degrassi at Centennial College which is on Morningside Avenue in the Scarborough part of town in Toronto.\n\nLikely a reference to “5AM in Toronto” – a double entendre for the song (about his struggle, lifestyle and relationships) and his call time for the show. He would often sleep in his show’s dressing room after being out all night\n\n\n\nDrake’s character was also literally shot on Degrassi.", "annotation_id": "2213235", "url": "https://genius.com/2213235", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Open the mail, starin’ at the check Enough to make you throw up, man, it’s gross what I net", "vault_lines": ["Then it's back to L.A., open the mail, starin' at the check", "Enough to make you throw up, man, it's gross what I net"], "meaning": "In financial terms, “gross” means the total amount, and “net” means total take away loss/expenses (basically, how much you actually earn). Saying “it’s gross what I net” suggests that he’s not losing any money to taxes—his net income is his gross pay. This could in fact be true if he sends his money to offshore banks in tax-free zones.\n\nDrake also changes the meaning of “gross” to mean nasty, enough to make you throw up—i.e. it’s sick how much money he really makes. He makes so much money he doesn’t even know how much he makes.\n\n\n\nThis could could also be a play on basketball:\n\nEnough to make you throw up (basketballs), it’s gross what he nets.", "annotation_id": "2213200", "url": "https://genius.com/2213200", "state": "accepted", "votes": 95, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "line": "Bitch, you better have my money when I come for that shit like O.D.B", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, you better have my money when I come for that shit like O.D.B"], "meaning": "Reference to Ol' Dirty Bastard, a deceased rapper who was also a Wu-Tang Clan member notorious for his craziness, from his song “Got Your Money”.\n\nHey, Dirty, baby I got your money\nDon’t you worry, I said hey! Baby, I got your money", "annotation_id": "2213195", "url": "https://genius.com/2213195", "state": "accepted", "votes": 181, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Worst Behavior", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-worst-behavior-lyrics", "genius_title": "Worst Behavior"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "And you showin’ off, but it’s alright And you showin’ off, but it’s alright (Alright)", "vault_lines": ["And you showin' off, but it's alright"], "meaning": "“Showin' off” in these lines is likely what inspired the original title of the song being “Showin' Off” instead of “Nice for What.” After the video of Lauryn Hill’s son playing the song spread across the internet, it was swiftly removed by UMG for violating the copyright of a song titled “Showin' Off.” Of course, Drizzy later changed his mind on what he wanted to call the song.", "annotation_id": "14638193", "url": "https://genius.com/14638193", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "Work at 8 A.M., finish ’round five", "vault_lines": ["Work at 8 A.M., finish 'round five"], "meaning": "Following the theme of female empowerment, the woman referred to in the song breaks the female stereotype of being a caretaker rather than a worker in society. This woman supports herself, working an 8-5 job that usually requires an education and routine dedication.", "annotation_id": "14304602", "url": "https://genius.com/14304602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "Gotta hit the club like you hit them, hit them, hit them angles", "vault_lines": ["Gotta hit the club like you hit them mothafuckin' angles", "Gotta hit the club like you hit them, hit them, hit them angles"], "meaning": "To “hit them angles” refers to taking pictures, typically for social media, in a way that is most flattering to the subject. Many people, women, in particular, obsess over their social media photos, making sure they are “perfect.” Recent studies show that social media is directly contributing to many people having low self-esteem.\n\nThese lines are consistent with the song’s message that women should be less self-conscious and enjoy life.", "annotation_id": "14298427", "url": "https://genius.com/14298427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "Hoes talk down, you don’t see ’em outside Yeah, they don’t really be the same offline", "vault_lines": ["Hoes talk down, you don't see 'em outside", "Yeah, they don't really be the same offline"], "meaning": "Online and over social media, people have no problem “talking down” or talking badly about another person, but those same people act differently in person or “offline”.  Face to face interaction often causes people to be less aggressive than online.", "annotation_id": "14296661", "url": "https://genius.com/14296661", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "I made (Watch the breakdown)", "vault_lines": ["I made (Watch the breakdown)"], "meaning": "Letitia Wright, who says “watch the breakdown” here, came to the front of the public eye in early 2018 with her roles in blockbusters Black Panther and Black Mirror.\n\nOne of her earliest credits was the crime drama Top Boy. Drake bought the rights to the show in 2017, and Letitia only had good things to say about it:\n\nI love Drake, he’s amazing. But my character would have to be someone positive. I can’t go back on my word and play a character that at the time  I didn’t really find three-dimensional. I hope they do a good job with it. I’m sure they will.\n\nLetitia also appears in the “Nice For What” video:", "annotation_id": "14292042", "url": "https://genius.com/14292042", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "You ain’t stressin’ off no lover in the past tense You already had them", "vault_lines": ["You ain't stressin' off no lover in the past tense", "You already had them"], "meaning": "Drake’s playing on the phrase “past tense”—“past” meaning gone by in time, and “tense” meaning a stressful situation. He also intentionally uses the past tense of the word “has” to describe her nonchalant attitude towards the relationships she used to have.", "annotation_id": "14284927", "url": "https://genius.com/14284927", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "I understand, you got a hunnid bands You got a baby Benz, you got some bad friends", "vault_lines": ["I understand, you got a hunnid bands", "You got a baby Benz, you got some bad friends"], "meaning": "A band is $1000 in notes, tied with a rubber band. So a “hunnid bands” is a hundred thousand dollars.\n\nA “Baby Benz” is a Mercedes C-Class or CLA, which are the entry models in the Mercedes-Benz line up. They start from around $35,000 CAD.\n\n\n\nThese lyrics follow the song’s theme of female empowerment. Drake knows this woman is self-made, and also understands women sometimes do not receive as much merit for their accomplishments. Men are typically portrayed as the breadwinners in a relationship, but Drake knows how hard this woman had to work in order to get to where she is now.", "annotation_id": "14272584", "url": "https://genius.com/14272584", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "You really pipin’ up on these niggas You gotta be nice for what to these niggas?", "vault_lines": ["You really pipin' up on these niggas", "You gotta be nice for what to these niggas?"], "meaning": "Here Drake takes the perspective of a woman in our current society and comments on the hypocrisy they face.  Especially in the rap/hip-hop industry, women are often objectified.  Drake questions this practice, asking why women are held to such a high standard of respecting men but not vice-versa.  Why would a woman go out of her way to respect a man when she does not get respected or treated well in return?\n\nAdditionally, this woman is financially stable and has no need to rely on a man to support her, furthering her independence.", "annotation_id": "14272472", "url": "https://genius.com/14272472", "state": "accepted", "votes": 267, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "I’ve been peepin’ what you bringin’ to the table Workin’ hard, girl, everything paid for First-last, phone bill, car note, cable", "vault_lines": ["I've been peepin' what you bringin' to the table", "Workin' hard, girl, everything paid for", "First-last, phone bill, car note, cable"], "meaning": "“Bringing something to the table” is an idiom that refers to the skills and abilities that you can provide—not only does this woman have those skills, she’s also literally putting food on the table.\n\nOn “Redemption,” the seventh track on Drake’s 2016 album Views, he raps about longing for an independent woman like the one he describes here:\n\nWhy do I want an independent woman\nTo feel like she needs me?—I lost my way", "annotation_id": "14264934", "url": "https://genius.com/14264934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "Gotta make that jump, gotta make that, gotta, gotta make that Gotta make that jump, gotta make that, gotta, gotta make that Gotta, gotta, gotta g-g-gotta, g-g-gotta, gotta Gotta, g-g-gotta, gotta, gotta make that jump, jump (Let’s go) Bend it over, lift it up, bend it over, lift it up Make that jump, jump Bend it over, lift it up, bend it over, lift it up Make that jump, jump Bend it over, over, over, over, over, lift it up Make that jump, jump Bend it over, lift it up (Make that jump, jump) Bend it over, lift it up (Make that jump, jump)", "vault_lines": ["Gotta make that jump, gotta make that, gotta, gotta make that", "Gotta, gotta, gotta g-g-gotta, g-g-gotta, gotta", "Gotta, g-g-gotta, gotta, gotta make that jump, jump (Let's go)", "Bend it over, lift it up, bend it over, lift it up", "Make that jump, jump", "Bend it over, over, over, over, over, lift it up", "Bend it over, lift it up (Make that jump, jump)"], "meaning": "This part of the track interpolates 5th Ward Webbie’s 2009 N.O. bounce and party classic, “Bend It Ova”—another piece of New Orleans bounce glaring its headlights: commands.\n\nThis is where the bounce artists play conductor and instruct the dancers on what to do. This section of repetitive commands is present in most bounce songs. Drake made sure to keep the essence of true New Orleans bounce.", "annotation_id": "14264325", "url": "https://genius.com/14264325", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "I wanna know who mothafuckin’ representin’ in here tonight Hold on, hold on", "vault_lines": ["I wanna know who mothafuckin' representin' in here tonight", "Hold on, hold on"], "meaning": "Big Freedia, “The Queen Diva,” opens the track with a call out. Freedia’s a legend in the bounce genre and is largely responsible for getting it on the map. Now, as New Orleans bounce re-popularizes with tracks like “Nice For What” and N.E.R.D.’s “Lemon,” it’s only appropriate that Freedia’s attached to the big name projects.\n\nIn New Orleans bounce, artists constantly call out their neighborhood or ask the crowd to call it out themselves. In a January 2018 Vox interview, Big Freedia spoke about bounce music:\n\nYou got to represent the people. That’s the people who gon' talk about the songs. That’s the people who gon' play the songs. That’s the people who gon' shake they ass. That’s the people who gon' feed the music where it needs to be fed.", "annotation_id": "14264267", "url": "https://genius.com/14264267", "state": "accepted", "votes": 164, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "I keep lettin’ you back in (You back in) How can I explain myself?", "vault_lines": ["I keep lettin' you back in (You back in)", "How can I explain myself?"], "meaning": "This is a sample of the bridge from Lauryn Hill’s iconic 1998 song “Ex-Factor​”, originally from Gladys Knight & The Pips “The Way We Were.”\n\nAlthough Lauryn took a hiatus from music after the release of her acclaimed debut in 1998, she made guest appearances, including one at a Drake show in 2014. Drake also sampled Hill’s “Doo Wop (That Thing)” on his 2014 tribute to Johnny Manziel, “Draft Day.”\n\nDrake, who signed to Lil Wayne’s label in 2009, also gives a shoutout to Weezy’s home state of Louisiana, by conflating his Lil Weezyana nickname—it’s also where bounce music originated. The song has a strong bounce influence and features additional vocals by an icon of the genre, Big Freedia from New Orleans.", "annotation_id": "14262777", "url": "https://genius.com/14262777", "state": "accepted", "votes": 298, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "High school pics, you was even bad then", "vault_lines": ["High school pics, you was even bad then"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the stereotype that high school years are punctuated by awkward phases often immortalized through bad photos. However, “bad” is also a slang term used to describe good-looking women; therefore Drake says this woman is so attractive that he suggests even her high school photos were good looking.\n\nConsidering Drake’s highly publicized beef with Kendrick Lamar that peaked in 2024 in which Drake was accused of liking underage girls, lines like this may not have aged the best.", "annotation_id": "14262718", "url": "https://genius.com/14262718", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "Everybody get your mothafuckin’ roll on I know shorty and she doesn’t want no slow song", "vault_lines": ["Everybody get your mothafuckin' roll on", "I know shorty and she doesn't want no slow song"], "meaning": "This is a shout-out to Big Tymers and their 2000 hit “Get Your Roll On.” The New Orleans-based group featured Mannie Fresh and Cash Money Records co-founder Birdman (then known as “Baby”).\n\nhttps://youtube.com/watch?v=hjVe_PTODcM\n\nDrake may also be continuing to pay homage to the bounce culture given the upbeat nature of bounce songs.", "annotation_id": "14262585", "url": "https://genius.com/14262585", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Nice For What", "line": "With your phone out, gotta hit them angles With your phone out, snappin’ like you Fabo", "vault_lines": ["With your phone out, gotta hit them angles", "With your phone out, snappin' like you Fabo", "Gotta hit the club like you hit them mothafuckin' angles", "Gotta hit the club like you hit them, hit them, hit them angles"], "meaning": "Fabo is a rapper known as the former frontman for snap music group D4L.\n\n“Snap” music is a sub-genre of hip-hop that started in Atlanta, Georgia in the late 1990s and lasted until the mid-2000s. D4L played a major role in popularizing the movement, along with snap dancing, called “snapping,” which Fabo can be seen doing in the music video for “Laffy Taffy,” the group’s biggest hit.\n\n\n\n“Snapping” also means taking photos, in this instance, likely for social media website Snapchat. Drake is aware that some people put a lot of effort into taking the perfect selfie by capturing their best angle.", "annotation_id": "14262287", "url": "https://genius.com/14262287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 209, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
