{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got rap niggas that I gotta act like I like But my actin’ days are over, fuck them niggas for life, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I got rap niggas that I gotta act like I like", "But my actin' days are over, fuck them niggas for life, yeah"], "meaning": "Apparently, Drake performed the song in New Zealand and replaced “rap niggas” to “r&b niggas”. It could direct to Chris Brown due to an interview he had with Tyga, saying that he gets paid by talking about him and that he don’t mess with him.", "annotation_id": "4948222", "url": "https://genius.com/4948222", "state": "verified", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "Yeah, way, way, way up (6ix) Turn it all up Yeah, look", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, way, way, way up (6ix)", "Turn it all up"], "meaning": "Way up as in hes Way up, like he states in “Blessings” furthermore, hes at the top right now, cant get shot down.", "annotation_id": "4933158", "url": "https://genius.com/4933158", "state": "verified", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got rap niggas that I gotta act like I like But my actin’ days are over, fuck them niggas for life, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I got rap niggas that I gotta act like I like", "But my actin' days are over, fuck them niggas for life, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake’s referring to one of the best songs in the history of rap “Hit em up” by 2pac..\n\n“https://genius.com/13542/2pac-hit-em-up/We-bust-on-bad-boys-niggas-fucked-for-life\n\nDrake is clearly sending a message to P.diddy after their recent brawl", "annotation_id": "4932943", "url": "https://genius.com/4932943", "state": "verified", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I hear everybody talkin’ ‘bout what they gon’ be I got high hopes for you niggas, we gon’ see", "vault_lines": ["I hear everybody talkin' 'bout what they gon' be", "I got high hopes for you niggas, we gon' see"], "meaning": "This is a jab to rappers he feels are not on his level. Rappers who claim they are going to take his place, be more successful or become one of the best in the game.\n\nDrake then sarcastically disses them by saying he has high hopes for them..meaning he really doubts their claims of one day becoming great because he is the only one who runs this game right now.", "annotation_id": "4919202", "url": "https://genius.com/4919202", "state": "verified", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "Yeah, way, way, way up (6ix) Turn it all up Yeah, look", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, way, way, way up (6ix)", "Turn it all up"], "meaning": "Way Up as in Canada is way up North and He wants Boi1da and OB to turn the volume, bass, and etc. on his headphones up", "annotation_id": "4911960", "url": "https://genius.com/4911960", "state": "verified", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Directly after this hook you can hear a vocal sample from Eazy-E’s “Eazy Duz It.” A similar sample can be heard in Logic’s “Under Pressure.”\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kdf_GIV9Svo", "annotation_id": "4897963", "url": "https://genius.com/4897963", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "[Produced by Boi-1da & OB O’Brien]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song samples the “Knowledge Me” instrumental as well as the “Do it” hook from “I Need a Hot Girl.”\n\nOB O'Brien is Drake’s OVO crew member, he assists Boi-1da with the production of this song.\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/y_laUhDQCR/?modal=true", "annotation_id": "4895742", "url": "https://genius.com/4895742", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "Nah, fuck all of you niggas, I ain’t finished Y’all don’t wanna hear me say it’s a go Y’all don’t wanna see Win Win, 50 or Whoa", "vault_lines": ["Nah, fuck all of you niggas, I ain't finished", "Y'all don't wanna hear me say it's a go", "Y'all don't wanna see Win Win, 50 or Whoa"], "meaning": "Drake isn’t bothered by the hate, that only drives him to be more successful. They can hate on Drake all they want, but they can’t stop him.\n\nWin Win, Fifty, and Whoa are all members of P. Reign’s Reps Up Gang, as well as close friends of Drake. They can be seen alongside Drizzy in the “Energy” music video.", "annotation_id": "4893694", "url": "https://genius.com/4893694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "Lickwood means “rewind” and gunshot means “forward” You requested it, so we rewind (6ix)", "vault_lines": ["Lickwood means \"rewind\" and gunshot means \"forward\"", "You requested it, so we rewind (6ix)"], "meaning": "The voice in this sample is believed to belong to Jamaican sound system DJ Jah Walton, and was likely recorded at a sound clash contest. It has been extensively sampled since appearing prominently in Cutty Ranks' “The Stopper” remix in 1991.\n\n\n\nIn sound clash culture, “lick wood” is to hit the wall enthusiastically, while “forward” is the crowds way of expressing approval for a DJ’s song selection, usually through loud noise (such as horns, noisemakers, and gunshots). A particularly enthusiastic forward might earn a rewind (repeat) of the song. After this sample, we hear the gunshots, and the song goes forward.\n\nJust like a DJ playing the beat back and forth, Drake’s switchin' up his style for his critics. Common Drake criticisms include his tired subject matter and lack of authenticity.", "annotation_id": "4888667", "url": "https://genius.com/4888667", "state": "accepted", "votes": 199, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "Tryna take the wave from a nigga So tired of savin’ all these niggas, mayne", "vault_lines": ["Tryna take the wave from a nigga", "So tired of savin' all these niggas, mayne", "They tryna take the wave from a nigga"], "meaning": "Drake suffers from the trappings of success. Detractors attempting to take their own slice of his energy, and hangers-on trying to get a leg-up in the industry via association.\n\nThe way his relationship with Birdman has played out is a direct confirmation of this theory. Although Birdman is wildly successful on his own, the way he has kept Drake locked in to a contract could be construed as an attempt to profit at the expense of his career.", "annotation_id": "4888331", "url": "https://genius.com/4888331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I’m tryna tell ya I got enemies, got a lot of enemies Every time I see ‘em, somethin’ wrong with their memory", "vault_lines": ["I'm tryna tell ya I got enemies, got a lot of enemies", "Every time I see 'em, somethin' wrong with their memory", "I got enemies, got a lot of enemies"], "meaning": "A common gripe in hip hop’s modern landscape is who’s beefing with who behind the scenes. A known enemy of Drake, Tyga, wrote the single “Senile” with Lil Wayne and Nicki Minaj, which attacked this very issue – enemies who act as though they don’t remember you or your beef when they see you in public.", "annotation_id": "4888287", "url": "https://genius.com/4888287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got niggas that’ll still try fuckin’ me over", "vault_lines": ["I got niggas that'll still try fuckin' me over"], "meaning": "This may refer to Cash Money Records. Drake, along with Lil Wayne, has been embroiled in controversy since December 2014 when Lil Wayne tweeted that Birdman was not paying royalties to Young Money’s roster. Wayne later filed a suit seeking damages of $51 million, and further claimed that Birdman had “mismanaged” Drake and Nicki Minaj.\n\nIn more general terms, Drake explains this best on “Used To”:\n\nThey never told me when you get the crown\nIt’s gon' take some getting used to\nNew friends all in their old feelings now\nThey don’t love you like they used to man", "annotation_id": "4888136", "url": "https://genius.com/4888136", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got money in the courts ’til all my niggas are free", "vault_lines": ["I got money in the courts 'til all my niggas are free"], "meaning": "While Drake owns an undisclosed stake of the Toronto Raptors basketball franchise, he also claims that if anyone from his crew gets locked up, he’ll happily bail them out.\n\n\n\nHe’s alluded to this topic before with YG on “Who Do You Love”:\n\nBut I’m big on the west like I’m big in the south\nSo we gon' pay some people off, we gon' figure it out", "annotation_id": "4888125", "url": "https://genius.com/4888125", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got real shit poppin’ with my family too I got niggas that can never leave Canada too (6ix)", "vault_lines": ["I got real shit poppin' with my family too", "I got niggas that can never leave Canada too (6ix)"], "meaning": "Drake has touched on tensions with family in previous songs. The issue was brought up in Take Care’s poignant “Look At What’ve Done” and Nothing Was The Same’s reflective “Too Much.”\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/zBwG16uqW1/?modal=true\n\nMany of Drake’s friends can’t leave Canada due to their criminal record. One of those is P Reign, who can’t cross the USA border due to drug charges dating back to 2004.", "annotation_id": "4888114", "url": "https://genius.com/4888114", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got two mortgages, thirty million in total", "vault_lines": ["I got two mortgages, thirty million in total"], "meaning": "Owing $30 million dollars on property could really drain your energy, but when you’re throwing parties like this, we’re sure it’s worth every penny. Drake bought a home in Calabasas for $7.7 million in mid 2012.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/37XCFVDQP2/\n\nCould thirty million hurt Drizzy like it did MC Hammer?\n\nAnd Hammer went broke so you know I’m more focused / I lost 30 mil, so I spent another 30\nCause unlike Hammer, thirty million can’t hurt me", "annotation_id": "4888110", "url": "https://genius.com/4888110", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I hear everybody talkin’ ‘bout what they gon’ be I got high hopes for you niggas, we gon’ see", "vault_lines": ["I hear everybody talkin' 'bout what they gon' be", "I got high hopes for you niggas, we gon' see"], "meaning": "Drake has been incredibly involved in a lot of upcoming rappers in this new era. He has already started his own successful label and signed some of the hottest artists in today’s industry.\n\nAs he rapped on “0 to 100/The Catch Up”:\n\nWe already got spring 2015 poppin'\nPND droppin', Reps-up P droppin'\nMajid Jordan droppin', OB droppin', not to mention me droppin'", "annotation_id": "4888036", "url": "https://genius.com/4888036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "’Bout to call your ass a Uber, I got somewhere to be", "vault_lines": ["'Bout to call your ass a Uber, I got somewhere to be"], "meaning": "“Uber” is an app-based transportation network and taxi company. The app allows the user to find an on-demand driver within minutes, providing an easier and more convenient experience than a traditional taxi service. Drake’s girl has overstayed her welcome, and it seems Champagne Papi has more important things to attend to.\n\nIn Drizzy’s defense, although you wouldn’t “call” an Uber, in this context the word is a synonym of “get/fetch.”", "annotation_id": "4887894", "url": "https://genius.com/4887894", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got girls in real life tryna fuck up my day Fuck goin’ online, that ain’t part of my day", "vault_lines": ["Fuck goin' online, that ain't part of my day", "I got girls in real life tryna fuck up my day"], "meaning": "While his “haters” are posting nonsense about him online, Drake’s got hundreds of (presumably) attractive girls chasing after him. What better way to silence the trolls than with scenes like this.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/katiraaax0/status/647826750521667587?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\nIn early 2015, Drizzy was accused of trying to “creep” on pornstar Mia Khalifa via Instagram. The story went viral. Seems like everyone’s out to get him – offline, and online!", "annotation_id": "4887800", "url": "https://genius.com/4887800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got enemies, got a lot of enemies Got a lot of people tryna drain me of my energy They tryna take the wave from a nigga Fuckin’ with the kid and pray for your nigga", "vault_lines": ["Got a lot of people tryna drain me of this energy", "I'm tryna tell ya I got enemies, got a lot of enemies", "Tryna take the wave from a nigga", "I got enemies, got a lot of enemies", "Got a lot of people tryna drain me of my energy", "They tryna take the wave from a nigga", "Fuckin' with the kid and pray for your nigga"], "meaning": "Drake’s copped plenty of hate throughout his career. He’s shared feuds with rappers like Pusha T, Common, Chris Brown, Ludacris, Kendrick Lamar, Jay Z, Tyga, Diddy and Meek Mill.\n\nSince his career began in 2009, being a “Drake hater” has become a meme throughout hip-hop and popular culture. Even essays have been penned on the subject.\n\nUnfortunately for the haters, Drake is resilient – with a multitude of platinum-selling singles and albums, a Grammy Award, and a loyal and ever-growing fan base around the world, he always seems to get the last laugh.", "annotation_id": "4887799", "url": "https://genius.com/4887799", "state": "accepted", "votes": 269, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I hear fairy tales ‘bout how they gon’ run up on me Well, run up when you see me, then, and we gon’ see", "vault_lines": ["I hear fairy tales 'bout how they gon' run up on me", "Well, run up when you see me, then, and we gon' see"], "meaning": "Apart from Diddy already running up on Drake, he seems to think there are still plenty of fake thugs in the game.\n\n\n\nThis line is similar to one during his feud with Common.", "annotation_id": "4887784", "url": "https://genius.com/4887784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got rap niggas that I gotta act like I like But my actin’ days are over, fuck them niggas for life, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I got rap niggas that I gotta act like I like", "But my actin' days are over, fuck them niggas for life, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake’s forced to associate with people that he doesn’t feel positive about. This is something he has mentioned before on “Legend.”\n\nBefore rap stardom, Drake was indeed a screen actor, best known for his role on Degrassi: The Next Generation.\n\n\n\nThis could also allude to Drake’s recent issues with label-mate Tyga and his involvement with Lil Wayne’s situation with Birdman, due contractual obligations with his label Cash Money Records.", "annotation_id": "4887747", "url": "https://genius.com/4887747", "state": "accepted", "votes": 121, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "Ex-girl, she the female version of me I got strippers in my life, but they virgins to me", "vault_lines": ["Ex-girl, she the female version of me", "I got strippers in my life, but they virgins to me"], "meaning": "It’s no secret that Drake has frequented strip clubs; in fact, one of his exes, Maliah Michel, is an exotic dancer.\n\n\n\nHe’s no stranger to writing about them, either, as he’s talked about a stripper least once on every project since So Far Gone in 2009:\n\n\n“Houstatlantavegas” (So Far Gone)\n“Miss Me” (Thank Me Later)\n“The Real Her” (Take Care)\n“305 to My City” (Nothing Was the Same)\n\n\n\nNevertheless, even though strippers have pasts that some people would frown upon, Drake doesn’t focus on that and treats them with respect.", "annotation_id": "4887711", "url": "https://genius.com/4887711", "state": "accepted", "votes": 122, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "line": "I got real ones livin’ past Kennedy Road I got real ones with me everywhere that I go", "vault_lines": ["I got real ones livin' past Kennedy Road", "I got real ones with me everywhere that I go"], "meaning": "Kennedy Road is a major street that runs north-south through Scarborough – a region in east Toronto. Drake mentions  Scarborough as the place where most of his friends lived, where he met girls and where Degrassi was filmed.", "annotation_id": "4887669", "url": "https://genius.com/4887669", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Energy", "status": "done", "targets": 33, "clicked": 33, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-energy-lyrics", "genius_title": "Energy"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "I, I, I, I, I", "vault_lines": ["Okay (o—), you like it (You like it), when I get (When I get)", "I do it how you say you want it", "I thank God you came (You came)", "And that's why I need", "How many more days could I wait?", "I made plans with you (With you)", "And I won't let 'em fall through", "I think I'd lie for you (Woop)", "I think I'd die for you"], "meaning": "“I,I,I,I,I” kinda rhymes with “yiy yiy yiy…” calling back to his “yiy” which changed in the first line", "annotation_id": "39581020", "url": "https://genius.com/39581020", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The hook in “Controlla” sounds very similar to the the hook of Erykah Badu’s song “Out My Mind, Just In Time.” For instance:\n\nAnd I’d lie for you\nI’d cry for you…\n…Do what you want me to\n\nListen to it below:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/78-E1NnGxQo?t=37", "annotation_id": "9109599", "url": "https://genius.com/9109599", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "[Break: Beenie Man]", "vault_lines": ["Drake from Canada, Beenie Man from Jamaica"], "meaning": "Beenie Man is a Jamaican dancehall legend—he’s often referred to as  “the King of Dancehall.”\n\nHe told Billboard that he’s protective of dancehall’s local roots, especially now that its sound has entered the mainstream—Rihanna and Drake’s dancehall-infused collaboration “Work” hit #1.\n\nDancehall is in the mainstream now…Dancehall is a culture that everyone wants to be a part of. Nobody wants to be part of a culture with no meaning in it. Right now, everybody tries to kick it with the dancehall. It’s our music. And it’s not going nowhere. We make dancehall so we can feel good about ourselves….I have to protect the culture. Even when I go on tour, I don’t do other songs, just dancehall music. I introduce the world to what dancehall is ‘cause I’m the king of it, and I have to work to keep it up. Jamaica needs trendsetters.", "annotation_id": "9107073", "url": "https://genius.com/9107073", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "My yiy just changed (Just changed) You just buzzed the front gate, I Thank God you came (You came)", "vault_lines": ["My yiy just changed (Just changed)", "You just buzzed the front gate", "I thank God you came (You came)"], "meaning": "“My yiy just changed” is an expression which stems from a 2012 collaborative mixtape by Popcaan and DJ Creep Chromatic. ‘Yiy change’ can refer to the physical change of one’s eyes getting red from smoking, or a more metaphorical change of mindset.\n\nPhysically, the pupils in the eye can dilate a lot if you take drugs, become sexually aroused, or basically do anything that gets your adrenaline pumping. Drake (and his libido) perks up at the sound the woman ringing the front gate. He also used the term on his “Sweeterman (Remix).”\n\nMetaphorically, Drake usually doesn’t get emotionally attached, but he’s drawn to this “angel,” and feels their connection growing stronger. Perhaps this is how he felt as he grew closer to Rihanna as they collaborated for their hit single “Work.”\n\n\n\nWhile there is an aura of buzz around the two, Rihanna spoke with Ellen telling her that she wouldn’t have the time to commit to one person.\n\nI’m single right now and it’s not that I’m like, ‘Oh, pro-single, yes, I hate men!’ I just think I have so much on my plate that I can’t even find the time to entertain a steady relationship or anything serious. Or even a text.", "annotation_id": "9093665", "url": "https://genius.com/9093665", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "And that’s why I need All the energy that you bring to me My last girl would tear me apart But she’d never wanna split a ting with me", "vault_lines": ["And that's why I need", "All the energy that you bring to me", "My last girl would tear me apart", "But she'd never wanna split a ting with me", "I made plans with you (With you)"], "meaning": "Drake has revealed in past songs that he has a lot of enemies and they drain him. On “Energy,” Drake raps:\n\nI got enemies, got a lot of enemies\nGot a lot of people tryna drain me of my energy\n\nA successful man needs a strong woman behind him, and Drake needs the love of a good woman to help him restore this lost energy.\n\nDrake’s last girl would split him apart, but she wouldn’t split a “ting” with him. The word carries a couple different slang meanings: Ting is a very popular Jamaican soda. Not wanting to share a simple thing such as a drink exemplifies the ex girl’s selfishness.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oCjeDvJJYjI\n\nOther slang meanings suggest that perhaps she wouldn’t share a joint with him, or split her stash, which is so not chill. Or else she refused to split a ‘bad girl’ with him (see ‘ting’ usage later in this song), i.e. have a threesome, which is quite popular among rappers these days.\n\nWhatever the ting was, the overall meaning shows through: Drake’s last girl wasn’t as generous with herself materially, emotionally, or sexually as his new flame.", "annotation_id": "9088584", "url": "https://genius.com/9088584", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "They don’t wanna see me Smiling back when they pree", "vault_lines": ["They don't wanna see me find your lovin'", "They don't wanna see me", "Smiling back when they pree"], "meaning": "“Pree” is Jamaican Patois for taking special notice of someone or something, to the point of lurking.\n\nIn one way, these lines speak to the fact that Drake is always being chased by women everywhere he goes. Even though they’re supposedly ‘obsessed with him,’ they don’t want to see him smiling back at some other woman, and don’t respond well when they find out he’s in a committed relationship.\n\nHowever, in another way, Drake is also calling these jealous girls out. Drake knows that they get catty and root against the women he dates. He could be saying they don’t want to see him smiling back at them, continuing to be happy in the face of their disapproval.\n\n“They don’t wanna see me” seems to be inspired by DJ Khaled, who characterizes “they” as people who try to bring negative energy into your life when it’s going well (i.e. haters):\n\nhttps://youtu.be/zHqujeBeCDA?t=52\n\n“Jheeze!” is London slang, started by rapper Giggs. It means to be happy about something. He has used it in various songs like “Talkin' da Hardest”:\n\nIf you’re talking the hardest\nGiggs better pop up in your thoughts as an artist (Jheeze)", "annotation_id": "9072970", "url": "https://genius.com/9072970", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "But you can’t just diss and come tell man sorry You can’t listen to me talk and go tell my story, nah (Nah) It don’t work like that when you love somebody", "vault_lines": ["Aggressive (Aggressive), tell you to (Tell you to), yeah", "But you can't just diss and come tell man sorry", "You can't listen to me talk and go tell my story, nah (Nah)", "It don't work like that when you love somebody", "But when it comes to you, you"], "meaning": "Drake uses the Jamaican slang “tell man sorry” to express that although he’d do anything to please this girl, he’ll not tolerate disloyalty.\n\nIf she really wants to be with him, she won’t sell him out, gossiping about him to others/the public and then crawling back saying sorry, the fame went to her head. The vulnerability and secrets that people confide in their significant others is a sacred thing, a trust issue, and Drake has been burned by women betraying him on this front before.", "annotation_id": "9070961", "url": "https://genius.com/9070961", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "Music a drop! Gyal a whine up dem bumpa Dis is di summa summa controlla Drake from Canada, Beenie Man from Jamaica Da one yah a murda—, Zagga!", "vault_lines": ["Gyal a whine up dem bumpa", "Dis is di summa summa controlla", "Drake from Canada, Beenie Man from Jamaica", "Da one yah a murda—, Zagga!"], "meaning": "While shouting out Drake and himself, Beenie describes what happens in Jamaica when a song drops in a club: the girls start whining.\n\nWhining is a dance of Caribbean descent in which one slowly winds their waist in a circular motion. The pace of rotation varies, depending on the music and the beats per minute.\n\n\n\nFinally, he points out that the people want to hear “murda.” “Murda” is a slang in Jamaican/Caribbean dialect for killing a track. Zagga is a casual greeting like Hello.", "annotation_id": "9070852", "url": "https://genius.com/9070852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "Gyal a tear off mi garments An’ a bawl fi come inna mi apartment (Woy!) Di gyal dem waan di length an’ strength Action speak louder dan argument, well", "vault_lines": ["Gyal a tear off mi garments", "An' a bawl fi come inna mi apartment (Woy!)", "Di gyal dem waan di length an' strength", "Action speak louder dan argument, well"], "meaning": "This a sample from Beenie Man’s 1995 song “Tear Off Mi Garment.”\n\nIn these lyrics, Beenie describes a situation where a woman wants to get right into sex. He uses the saying “actions speak louder than words” to express how much this girl is ready to go. She’s not just saying it,  she starts to get physical fast and tears off his clothes.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GAHyaJ0eIA8", "annotation_id": "9070684", "url": "https://genius.com/9070684", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "Them girls, they just wanna take my money They don’t want me to give you nothing They don’t want you to have nothing They don’t wanna see me find your lovin’", "vault_lines": ["Them girls, they just wanna take my money", "They don't want me to give you nothing", "They don't want you to have nothing", "They don't wanna see me find your lovin'", "They don't wanna see me"], "meaning": "“Them girls” seem to be the “waste ting shorty” types from above who aren’t honest when they’re with him. He sees through them. They would never support Drake when he’s with another girl, even if she obviously makes him happy, because that means they can’t get their chance with him. The final line is probably a reference to Drake’s single, “Find Your Love.”\n\nDrake defies them girls who want to be the object of his affection for mercenary reasons. He already has this girl, and he will take care of her because they have something special together, and not because she flatters him to get a piece of his fame.\n\nDrake highlights the abusive nature of “them girls,” portraying them as jealous gold diggers. You don’t want a gold digger—just ask Kanye. However, according to CNN, some girls see logic in marrying for money:\n\nBank accounts—and debts—do matter. And acknowledging that doesn’t make me a gold digger akin to Anna Nicole Smith — it makes me smart.\n\nAccording to Forbes, they may not have the worst thought process:\n\nIt’s time to start treating your life like reality, not a fairy tale. We live in a world that runs on dollars and good sense. The GDI can prevent a future that no smart girl believes will happen to her. Were not saying become Anna Nicole and go after some old codger just for his wallet (unless that’s what you’re into). We’re saying find a man to enjoy, to have fun with, be intimate with. Just remember to take economics into account. It’s a move toward financial security.", "annotation_id": "9070679", "url": "https://genius.com/9070679", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "I think I’d lie for you (Woop) I think I’d die for you Jodeci “Cry for You” Do things when you want me to Like controlla, controlla (Woop), yeah Like controlla, controlla (Woop), yeah", "vault_lines": ["Jodeci \"Cry for You\"", "Do things when you want me to", "Like controlla, controlla (Woop), yeah", "Aggressive (Aggressive), tell you to (Tell you to), yeah", "Knowing I'd lie for you (Woop)", "Thinking I'd die for you", "Like controlla, controlla, uh", "I think I'd lie for you (Woop)", "I think I'd die for you"], "meaning": "In line with the Jamaican dialect which appears throughout the track, ‘controller’ becomes ‘controlla.’ Think of ‘controlla’ like a game controller. You use the controller and what ever is desired is done. Basically, Drake’s giving this woman full autonomy in their relationship—she can pick the time and the place, and he’ll satisfy all her requests.\n\nAlternatively, ‘controlla’ could be meant to sound like control her, suggesting what Drake talks about in the second verse—this woman wants him to take the dominant role, both in bed and in other aspects of their partnership.\n\nAt first glance, these sentiments could appear to be purely sexual, however, Drizzy emphasizes his long term commitment to this relationship by paying homage to Jodeci’s passion-filled song “Cry For You.” The track itself paints the vivid image of a man laying out every emotion and devoting his whole life to attain a woman’s heart.\n\n\n\nAside from sampling them on his track “How About Now,” Drake’s shown love for Jodeci on many occasions. His father, Dennis Graham, spoke on how influential their music was during the early stages of his son’s life in Drizzy’s track “Jodeci Freestyle” featuring J. Cole:\n\nThe music that you and I used to listen to ahh.. was just..\nAbsolutely phenomenal, because we went through it all\nWe went through errythang..\nI remember you loved Jodeci\nI mean like studied, you even made me a CD…", "annotation_id": "9070665", "url": "https://genius.com/9070665", "state": "accepted", "votes": 168, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "Okay (o—), you like it (You like it), when I get (When I get) Aggressive (Aggressive), tell you to (Tell you to), yeah “Go slower (Slower), go faster (Faster)” Like controlla, controlla, yeah Like controlla, controlla, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Like controlla, controlla (Woop), yeah", "Okay (o—), you like it (You like it), when I get (When I get)", "Aggressive (Aggressive), tell you to (Tell you to), yeah", "\"Go slower (Slower), go faster (Faster)\"", "Like controlla, controlla, uh", "But when it comes to you, you"], "meaning": "This girl likes to be dominated in the bed. Drake talks as if he has a controller where he “pushes her buttons” and his girl acts on his command. “Waste ting” is Patois for a useless thing. Drake isn’t going to get funky in the sheets with a girl unless she’s honest about what she wants, and tells him just how to please her. Obviously, it’s something he’s into as well, so it all works out!\n\nThis once again could be about Rihanna, who has stated publicly that she likes a dominant guy in the bedroom. In an interview with Elle, she said, “Any man that tells me what to do is sexy!” and in Rolling Stone:\n\nBeing submissive in the bedroom is really fun. You get to be a little lady, to have somebody be macho and in charge of your shit. That’s fun to me…I like to be spanked. Being tied up is fun. I like to keep it spontaneous. Sometimes whips and chains can be overly planned – you gotta stop, get the whip from the drawer downstairs. I’d rather have him use his hands.\n\nOn May 18, 2016, Drake appeared on The Ellen Show and was asked about his relationship with Rihanna. Ellen confirmed that RiRi and Drizzy were “just friends, only friends” to which Drake unconvincingly responded, “yes.”\n\nIn the below video, Ellen brings up a photo of Drake and Rihanna grinding onstage, to which Drake coyly responds, “that’s friendship at its finest.”\n\nhttp://ellentube.com/videos/0-nw6hexsb/", "annotation_id": "9070645", "url": "https://genius.com/9070645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Controlla", "line": "My old flex is my new flex now, and we’re workin’ on it, yeah", "vault_lines": ["My old flex is my new flex now, and we're workin' on it, yeah"], "meaning": "Flex in this case is slang for a girl. His old girl is now his new girl because he’s back with her trying to work on their relationship to fix the problem that ended it in the first place.\n\nThis line is probably about Rihanna, as “we’re workin' on it” could easily be a reference to the Rihanna’s song “Work,” on which Drake is featured.", "annotation_id": "9070560", "url": "https://genius.com/9070560", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
