{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "I be yellin’ out, “Money over everything,” money on my mind Mind, mind-mind Tell ’em I apologize, it happened over time Time, time-time", "vault_lines": ["I be yellin' out, \"Money over everything,\" money on my mind", "Tell her I apologize, happened over time", "Tell 'em I apologize, it happened over time"], "meaning": "Everybody knows this. This part is from the first and third lines of Verse 2 for Headlines.", "annotation_id": "38860720", "url": "https://genius.com/38860720", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "They know, they know, they know They know, they know, they know They know, they know, they know Yeah, they know, yeah That the real is on the rise Fuck them other guys I even gave ’em a chance to decide Now it’s something they know They know, they know, they know (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["That's just something they know", "They know, they know, they know", "That the real is on the rise", "Fuck them other guys", "I even gave 'em a chance to decide", "Now it's something they know"], "meaning": "The Greekend and Thrake insinuate that they’re aware of the problems explained in the EP.\n\nThe “other guys,” as Audrey explains, seemingly refer to his beef with Jean Poppa NBC, and Lil Dwaka back in 2012, when his label turned them against Drake to get him to leave the label.", "annotation_id": "38774997", "url": "https://genius.com/38774997", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "You gon’ make someone around me catch a body like that", "vault_lines": ["You gon' make someone around me catch a body like that", "You gon' hype me up and make me catch a body like that"], "meaning": "Drake is saying here that he will bring his OVO goons/accomplices and they are intimidating", "annotation_id": "37681890", "url": "https://genius.com/37681890", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "They know, they know, they know They know, they know, they know They know, they know, they know Yeah, they know, yeah", "vault_lines": ["That's just something they know", "They know, they know, they know", "Now it's something they know", "You know that they ain't even got it like that", "When they get my shit and play it, I ain't even gotta say it, they know"], "meaning": "This line could refer to his notoriety. By saying ‘they know’ with no further explanation shows that everyone shares the same common knowledge.", "annotation_id": "36951775", "url": "https://genius.com/36951775", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "Tuck my napkin in my shirt ‘cause I’m just mobbin’ like that", "vault_lines": ["Tuck my napkin in my shirt 'cause I'm just mobbin' like that"], "meaning": "“Mobbin’” refers to being connected with a criminal organization, particularly the Mafia. By mentioning the practice of mob bosses tucking napkins into their shirts while dining, Drake aligns himself with the confidence and authority associated with these powerful figures. This imagery underscores his control in the rap game, suggesting that he navigates his life with the same dominance as a mobster.\n\nDrake later continues this theme of mob influence on his 2018 track “Mob Ties.”\n\n\nTony Soprano and Ralph Cifaretto from The Sopranos with their napkins tucked in, embodying the classic mobster dining style.", "annotation_id": "32745193", "url": "https://genius.com/32745193", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "When they get my shit and play it, I ain’t even gotta say it, they know", "vault_lines": ["Those my brothers, I ain't even gotta say it", "They know, they know, they know", "When they get my shit and play it, I ain't even gotta say it, they know"], "meaning": "Drake is saying when they play his music he doesn’t need to tell them but they know it’s fire", "annotation_id": "31700094", "url": "https://genius.com/31700094", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "That new shit that you got is overdue", "vault_lines": ["That new shit that you got is overdue"], "meaning": "subtle wordplay using the word “overdue” which implies something is “late” to juxtapose how someone’s new sht is really his old sht", "annotation_id": "27650698", "url": "https://genius.com/27650698", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "Drinkin’ every night because we drink to my accomplishments Faded way too long, I’m floatin’ in and out of consciousness", "vault_lines": ["Drinkin' every night because we drink to my accomplishments", "Faded way too long, I'm floatin' in and out of consciousness"], "meaning": "People will often drink when they have something to celebrate for, and Drake is no different. It only took him around 3 years to have the most #1 rap hits, while other rap artists who have been in the game for many years are not even close to reaching him.\n\n\n\nAt this point, he hasn’t left the charts once during those 3 years, and if he’s drinking for every hit he makes, it’s no surprise that he will always be in the state of unconsciousness.", "annotation_id": "12872030", "url": "https://genius.com/12872030", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "And they sayin’ I’m back, I’d agree with that I just take my time with all this shit, I still believe in that", "vault_lines": ["And they sayin' I'm back, I'd agree with that", "I just take my time with all this shit, I still believe in that"], "meaning": "After the release of Thank Me Later, critics started saying that Drake had begun to fall off.\n\nDrake himself admitted that he wasn’t happy with his debut album. However, on this record, Drake admits that he feels better about what he crafted, especially since he spent more time:\n\nTo be 100 percent honest … I wasn’t necessarily happy with ‘Thank Me Later.’ People loved it (but) I just knew what I was capable of with a little more time,“ he said. \"I’m very confident in ‘Take Care.’ I definitely made the exact album that I wanted. Will it appear that way to the world? I’m not sure, because it’s definitely different. It’s not 15 ‘I’m on Ones.’ It’s not ‘She Will.’ I’m very happy with this album. More so than I’ve ever been with a project.", "annotation_id": "12709286", "url": "https://genius.com/12709286", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "I had someone tell me I fell off, ooh, I needed that", "vault_lines": ["I had someone tell me I fell off, ooh, I needed that"], "meaning": "Upon the release of his  classic debut mixtape, So Far Gone, Drake had lots of critical acclaim and was hailed by many as the next big artist. However, when his first album Thank Me Later was released, people started to believe his music had lost its quality. Contemporary reviews were lukewarm to say the least.\n\nEven publications from Drake’s stomping ground were harsh. A review from NOW Toronto claimed\n\nAfter a year of anticipating what should’ve been a game-changer, Drake turns in a merely passable album and comes across as the rap music version of Justin Bieber. Drake’s hooks are flimsy and irritating. His long-winded emo choruses gnaw at your brain.\n\nHowever, Drake has always treated haters as motivators.\n\nOn his now classic song “Forever,” he says:\n\nStarted off local, but thanks to all the haters\nI know G-IV pilots on a first name basis\n\nWith a strong resolve to prove doubters wrong, Drake uses someone telling him he fell off as a reason to put more effort into his music.", "annotation_id": "12528148", "url": "https://genius.com/12528148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "I guess it really is just me, myself, and all my millions", "vault_lines": ["I guess it really is just me, myself, and all my millions"], "meaning": "A play on the phrase “me, myself, and I” to include his millions of wanna-be clones as mentioned in earlier lines.\n\n Also, millions refers to his millions of fans and millions of dollars as being the only things he has in this life besides himself.", "annotation_id": "11934150", "url": "https://genius.com/11934150", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "’Cause I live for this, it isn’t just a hobby like that", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I live for this, it isn't just a hobby like that"], "meaning": "For Drake, his music is his life. Not only does he value his career, but he genuinely feels that his role is to make music. As a massive act in music, Drake is a common topic in tabloid gossip, but his discography and catalog still overshadow his presence anywhere else.\n\nOn “5AM in Toronto,” he raps:\n\nA lot of niggas PR stuntin' like that’s the movement\nAnd I’m the only nigga still known for the music", "annotation_id": "11791749", "url": "https://genius.com/11791749", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "I might be too strung out on compliments Overdosed on confidence Started not to give a fuck and stopped fearin’ the consequence", "vault_lines": ["I might be too strung out on compliments", "Started not to give a fuck and stopped fearin' the consequence"], "meaning": "On his first studio album Thank Me Later, Drake spends a lot of time addressing his haters and his doubters. At the time, he was a massive commodity in hip-hop, and before he signed to Cash Money Records, there was huge debate surrounding his validity and skill level as everyone wanted a piece of him.\n\nWith this single from his sophomore effort, Drake acknowledges that he does place a lot of consideration on the opinions of others, but at the same time, he has gained a lot of confidence in himself.", "annotation_id": "11791717", "url": "https://genius.com/11791717", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "And Drizzy got the money, so Drizzy gon’ pay it Those my brothers, I ain’t even gotta say it That’s just something they know", "vault_lines": ["And Drizzy got the money, so Drizzy gon' pay it", "Those my brothers, I ain't even gotta say it", "That's just something they know", "They know, they know, they know", "Now it's something they know", "You know that they ain't even got it like that", "When they get my shit and play it, I ain't even gotta say it, they know"], "meaning": "As he boasts in his song,“The Motto”, he’s:\n\n25, sitting on 25 mill\n\nHe has enough money to spend on virtually anything, so whatever he and his friends want, he’ll pay.\n\nNot only does he have it to spend, he has an understanding with his friends that he will cover them whenever they need it.", "annotation_id": "11382421", "url": "https://genius.com/11382421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "And they want to see me pick back up, well, where’d I leave it at?", "vault_lines": ["And they want to see me pick back up, well, where'd I leave it at?"], "meaning": "In the previous year, Drake released his debut studio album, which was met with critical acclaim and went platinum within a year. It included hit songs such as “Over” and top 10 single “Find Your Love”. So, he is understandably confused. What exactly is he coming back from?", "annotation_id": "11382320", "url": "https://genius.com/11382320", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "[Outro Poem]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I heard once that they would rather hear about memories than enemies\nRather hear what was or what will be than what is\nRather hear how you got it over how much it cost you\nRather hear about findin' yourself than how you lost you\nRather you make this an open letter\nAbout family, and struggle, and it takin' forever\nAbout hearts that you’ve broken, and ties that you’ve severed\nNo doubt in my mind, that’ll make them feel better\n\nNote that this poem isn’t in the video, the iTunes single version, or the free version, you would only hear this if you had bought the album as a CD or as the full iTunes album.", "annotation_id": "4341929", "url": "https://genius.com/4341929", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "[Verse 1]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "For a complete breakdown into the smallest details, check here.\n\nProduction-wise, the rising horns provides a scene of a grand entrance. Boi-1da is at it again with his drum patterns driving the song with the main melody making this song very easy to listen to and follow. This is a martial beat and have heavy staccato strings, buoyant, bouncy staccato synth line and Casio snares.", "annotation_id": "2837285", "url": "https://genius.com/2837285", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "I’m like, “Why I gotta be all that?” But still, I can’t deny the fact that it’s true Listening to you expressin’ all them feelings", "vault_lines": ["I'm like, \"Why I gotta be all that?\"", "But still, I can't deny the fact that it's true", "Listening to you expressin' all them feelings"], "meaning": "He is displeased by the fact that so much is expected of him, but he realizes that these expectations are reality. He’s also assuring his fans and critics that he is aware of their wishes and criticism.\n\nThis bar is reminiscent of a few of his lines from “9am in Dallas”:\n\nScared for the first time, everything just clicked\nWhat if I don’t really do the numbers they predict?\nConsidering the fact that I’m the one that they just picked\nTo write a chapter in history, this shit has got me sick", "annotation_id": "325798", "url": "https://genius.com/325798", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "You gon’ hype me up and make me catch a body like that", "vault_lines": ["You gon' make someone around me catch a body like that", "You gon' hype me up and make me catch a body like that"], "meaning": "You going to hype him up and kill somebody…\n\nBut Drake literally killing someone?  C'mon…not the same Drake that made Marvin’s Room.\n\n\n\nIn rap, being “gassed up” or hyped up by your crew to take on a fellow rapper lyrically is a common occurrence. Drake may be the king of subliminal disses, but eventually, when cornered, he attacks.", "annotation_id": "317886", "url": "https://genius.com/317886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "Tell her I apologize, happened over time", "vault_lines": ["Tell her I apologize, happened over time", "Tell 'em I apologize, it happened over time"], "meaning": "He’s saying he apologizes to her, but over time he’s gotten richer and richer and his fame has grown, that is why he’s the way he is.\n\nThis can allude to how he’s been singing more on his tracks, which a lot of his fans are asking, why? Thus, he’s saying “I’m sorry, over time I got like this”.\n\nBefore the fame, Drake was a very warm, loving young man that had admiration and respect for almost everybody (artists, women, friends, fam). But the betrayal he has received from these people throughout his career, being used, set up and robbed, hated on, etc. stole that warmth he so easily gave. His heart’s turning cold, trust is gone. Here, he is apologizing to this woman for not being able to show the affection and care he has been known for and she is expecting.", "annotation_id": "311811", "url": "https://genius.com/311811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "I know I exaggerated things, now I got it like that", "vault_lines": ["I know I exaggerated things, now I got it like that"], "meaning": "Back in the day, Drake exaggerated his wealth and possessions, going so far as to lease a Rolls Royce Phantom (actual car below) while still living with his mother.\n\n\n\nI’m the greatest, man, I said that before I knew I was…", "annotation_id": "302414", "url": "https://genius.com/302414", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "’Cause one of us goes in and we all go through it (Ayy)", "vault_lines": ["'Cause one of us goes in and we all go through it (Ayy)"], "meaning": "If you mess with one of them, you mess with them all…\n\n\n\nAlso an allusion to Drake’s classic “I’m Goin' In”, featuring his rap family.\n\nDrake helps out his homies. One of Drake’s homies from the OVO set went through a rather large court case, in which Drake spent a lot of money to help with the proceedings. He alluded to this in the intro of Take Care (see the 2nd verse of “Over My Dead Body”).", "annotation_id": "301773", "url": "https://genius.com/301773", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "She says, “They miss the old Drake,” girl, don’t tempt me", "vault_lines": ["She says, \"They miss the old Drake,\" girl, don't tempt me"], "meaning": "In the song “9am in Dallas”, Drake says:\n\nPeople say that old Drake we started to miss it\nBut they need to be a little more specific\n\nSince Drake has recently been making songs about him doing lean and has started singing more, his fans are questioning what happened to the “real Drake”. He says that this has just happened overtime as he’s developed as a rapper. Everyone wants to see Drake return to his mixtape glory…\n\nAlso a double meaning, as girls think he has changed with the fame (a topic discussed in Marvin’s Room).\n\nOr he could also be talking about this…", "annotation_id": "301768", "url": "https://genius.com/301768", "state": "accepted", "votes": 165, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "line": "Soap-opera rappers, all these niggas sound like all my children", "vault_lines": ["Soap-opera rappers, all these niggas sound like all my children"], "meaning": "Double entendre:\n\n\nThe drama of the rap game today (cf. Consequence/GOOD music beef) reminds Drake of All My Children, the American soap opera running since the 70’s\nRappers all aspire to be like him, as if they were his children.\n\n\n\nDrake acted in the teenage soap opera Degrassi: The Next Generation, so he might be using this line ironically.", "annotation_id": "301766", "url": "https://genius.com/301766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Headlines", "status": "done", "targets": 27, "clicked": 27, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-headlines-lyrics", "genius_title": "Headlines"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "[Produced by Mike Zombie]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This beat samples Bruce Sanfilippo and Mathew Grassow’s “Ambessence Piano & Drones 1”.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HnpxgrdN8mw\n\nDrake’s “Started from the Bottom” is a triumph of minimalism. The beat’s barely there, the lyrics are pared down and shouty—it’s like Drake’s own version of mid-2000s ringtone rap. Leading the way is OVO Sound signee Mike Zombie, whose production here loops up a short, new age-y piano line (that really sounds like someone starting up Windows on a PC) over sprightly 808 programming and a bassline that picks out a weird notes under the fore.\n\n\n\nLil John & The Eastside Boyz’s “Who U Wit” where you can hear the sample that has the same bass, clap and drum loop.", "annotation_id": "2559147", "url": "https://genius.com/2559147", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "[Chorus]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This hook was apparently inspired by none other than Future, who talks about his impact on the song at 4:10 of this video.", "annotation_id": "1659904", "url": "https://genius.com/1659904", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "Boys tell stories ’bout the man", "vault_lines": ["Boys tell stories 'bout the man"], "meaning": "Establish yourself as the man, and your buzz will never fade.\n\nDrake tweeted this line 4 months before the song dropped:\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/262328930051174400\n\nThis can also be seen to have a double meaning, referring to how your experiences as a boy “at the bottom” inform and direct what you are when you become a man.", "annotation_id": "1469128", "url": "https://genius.com/1469128", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "[Bridge]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A lot of people have fake friends and don’t even realize it. Drake’s saying you should associate yourself with people who are your real friends, as opposed to superficial or, in his case, industry friends.\n\n\n\nDrake gave a speech towards the end of last year about choosing fake versus real friends.", "annotation_id": "1467733", "url": "https://genius.com/1467733", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "Nigga, I just think it’s funny how it goes", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, I just think it's funny how it goes"], "meaning": "Now Drake lets his uncle borrow the whip, a huge step up from when his dad had to pay for his cigarettes with dollars and some change.", "annotation_id": "1466306", "url": "https://genius.com/1466306", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "Say I never struggled, wasn’t hungry, yeah, I doubt it", "vault_lines": ["Say I never struggled, wasn't hungry, yeah, I doubt it"], "meaning": "Drake has always been known for growing up in a suburban neighborhood in Toronto, Canada as an actor in his teen years on the hit show Degrassi: The Next Generation.\n\n\n\nIn 2009 Cormega was one of the first people to call out Drake for being rich, essentially saying he has never struggled…\n\nHe’s rich man. I don’t like when these spoiled rich kids just get into rap because it’s something they can do.\n\nThis misconception has been greatly exaggerated by his critics. Growing up in half-of-a-house (duplex) in Forest Hill, Toronto with a single mother who was a teacher is not rich by any standards. And getting a cut of money from a minor Canadian TV show is not going to make anyone a baller. At the very least, it’s no different or less luxurious than the upbringing of plenty of heralded MC’s from New York such as Mike D, Rakim, Chuck D and De La Soul who have grown up in similar semi-urban/suburban enclaves.", "annotation_id": "1466206", "url": "https://genius.com/1466206", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "Fuck a fake friend, where your real friends at?", "vault_lines": ["Fuck a fake friend, where your real friends at?"], "meaning": "The bridge is given piercing strength by the way several lines like this one begin with a string of alliteration, the repetition of consonant sounds on each line\n\nNo new niggas, nigga we don’t feel that\nFuck a fake friend, where you real friends at\n\nBeing a successful rapper makes honest connections with new people seem blurry. Everyone seems to only want your money and fame, not you.\n\n\n\nSome recent developments made it a little ironic to show a picture of OVOXO. XO is The Weeknd’s crew, and this bridge is (most likely) a subliminal diss towards The Weeknd. There’s animosity between the two right now because The Weeknd refused to sign with Drake’s label OVO after Drake helped put him on.\n\nAccording to Songfacts, Drake told Billboard magazine:\n\nI’ll be out trying to get a sandwich or something and the guy will say to me, ‘I’d give you a free drink with that, but you know, no new friends. I swear I’m not sitting around going, 'What’s the new meme going to be?’ But I do spend a lot of time when I’m writing, especially lately, trying to make something for people to live by. I’m trying to make anthems that are empowering to people, to find phrases that I haven’t heard before. I’m not just going to sit here and be like, ‘Fu–in’ bitches, getting money!'", "annotation_id": "1466153", "url": "https://genius.com/1466153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "Nigga, we just want the credit where it’s due", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, we just want the credit where it's due"], "meaning": "Throughout Drake’s early career, he was considered soft and was heavily underestimated. Now that he’s dropped some huge releases, he feels he deserves credit for making it.\n\n\n\nThis is also a potential shot at the The Weeknd, whose career Drake helped kickstart. However, The Weeknd dropped his debut album without any acknowledgements to Drake or OVO. The Weeknd also ended up signing to a different record label than OVO. Drake tweeted “Just OVO now” and removed all XO material from his clothing website.", "annotation_id": "1466077", "url": "https://genius.com/1466077", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "Workin’ all night, traffic on the way home", "vault_lines": ["Workin' all night, traffic on the way home"], "meaning": "All the nights he spent making music with producer, Noah “40” Shebib, in Apartment 1503\n\n\n\nAnother famous rapper got inspiration for his first single from a car accident after a long studio night.\n\nIn the context of the official video to this song, Drake worked at a Shoppers drug mart as a night manager", "annotation_id": "1466059", "url": "https://genius.com/1466059", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "And my uncle callin’ me, like, ”Where you at? I gave you the keys, told you bring it right back”", "vault_lines": ["And my uncle callin' me, like, \"Where you at?", "I gave you the keys, told you bring it right back”"], "meaning": "Drake has mentioned before that his uncle used let him whip the drop top Lexus, back before getting signed.\n\n\n\nCome to think of it, a certain rapper has recently brought up memories of himself having a family member calling about the whip.\n\nWhile Drake has two famous uncles, Larry Graham and Teenie Hodges, this is most likely a reference to Drizzy’s Uncle Steve, who helped raise him:\n\nWithout this man there is no me…Uncle Steve\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/TXW472DQMj/\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fjXK6KkdDS4", "annotation_id": "1466050", "url": "https://genius.com/1466050", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "No new niggas, nigga, we don’t feel that", "vault_lines": ["No new niggas, nigga, we don't feel that"], "meaning": "“No new friends” has been a Drake motto of late as well as DJ Khaled’s first single from Suffering From Success in which Drizzy is prominently featured.\n\n\n\nAs a way to complain about being famous, Drake has had a longstanding hate of new niggas. As evidenced by his 2010 single, “Over”.", "annotation_id": "1466039", "url": "https://genius.com/1466039", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "Nigga, I could turn your boy into the man", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, I could turn your boy into the man"], "meaning": "Pause.\n\n\n\nHe’s the shit and as soon as he mentions someone’s name people are gonna pay more attention to that individual, aka The Man.\n\nDrake has helped buffer the careers of several famous artists, including The Weeknd, ASAP Rocky, and Kendrick Lamar by including them in his Club Paradise Tour\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/262799502908461056", "annotation_id": "1466028", "url": "https://genius.com/1466028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "Now I’m on the road, half a million for a show, and we—", "vault_lines": ["Now I'm on the road, half a million for a show, and we—"], "meaning": "Drake’s Club Paradise Tour was the most successful hip-hop tour of 2012, grossing over $42 million worldwide – for every show, Drizzy nets $500,000 of that.\n\nWhen it comes to money (or anything else), Drake never lies on a track.\n\n\n\nAlso, now that he’s “on the road”, flying private jets and living it up in tour buses, he doesn’t need to borrow his Uncle’s car anymore and be home by a certain time.", "annotation_id": "1466019", "url": "https://genius.com/1466019", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started from the Bottom", "line": "Started from the bottom, now we’re here Started from the bottom, now my whole team fuckin’ here Started from the bottom, now we’re here Started from the bottom, now the whole team here, nigga Started from the bottom, now we’re here Started from the bottom, now my whole team here, nigga Started from the bottom, now we’re here Started from the bottom, now the whole team fuckin’ here", "vault_lines": ["Started from the bottom, now we're here", "Started from the bottom, now my whole team fuckin' here", "Started from the bottom, now the whole team here, nigga", "Started from the bottom, now my whole team here, nigga", "Started from the bottom, now the whole team fuckin' here"], "meaning": "Drake is adopting the classic story of a rapper who “started from the bottom” and has made it against all odds. He gets a lot of criticism from people saying he already had money prior to rapping, due to his acting career. That isn’t the case, however, as Drake only made roughly $50,000 a year. He had to take care of his extremely sick mom and his grandmother and since his father was in and out of prison there was no help.\n\n\nPeople like to build their own story about my life. I don’t know if it makes them feel better, or if it makes it okay for them to not like me, but the last thing I grew up as was rich.\n\nI had rich friends, but they weren’t giving me their money. I’m just not the type of guy to go ‘No, no, no, I’m not rich.’ People can say whatever they want about me, though. If they really want to learn them, the facts are out there. But I guess it’s easier on their hearts if I didn’t have to struggle, and makes it easier to not like me. It is what it is.\n- Drake on EVR radio", "annotation_id": "1466000", "url": "https://genius.com/1466000", "state": "accepted", "votes": 313, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
