{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "She creamin’ on me, I’m her crush", "vault_lines": ["She leanin' on me, I'm her crutch", "She creamin' on me, I'm her crush"], "meaning": "Drake refers to the soda brand called “Crush”, which one of its protucts is named “Crush Cream Soda”. This specific product is sold only in Canada, Drake’s homeland.\n\n\n\nThe additional and quite literal meaning is that her vagina is becoming creamy and wet because she is very attracted to Drake.", "annotation_id": "27060746", "url": "https://genius.com/27060746", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "You’re misreadin’ me, I’m too geeked, tryna turn you up", "vault_lines": ["I like it, but I'm too geeked, tryna turn you up", "You're misreadin' me, I'm too geeked, tryna turn you up", "But I turn you up"], "meaning": "“Geeked” is a slang term to refer to one who’s happy and excited after consuming several drugs. Drake is suggesting that this women is misreading him because he is struggling to articulate his intentions due to being waved after taking all those drugs.", "annotation_id": "27475305", "url": "https://genius.com/27475305", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "You’re actin’ like a bride-to-be Behind closed doors, slimin’ me Friends are all advisin’ me Sayin’ I could die tryna turn you up", "vault_lines": ["You're misreadin' me, I'm too geeked, tryna turn you up", "You're actin' like a bride-to-be", "Behind closed doors, slimin' me", "Friends are all advisin' me", "Sayin' I could die tryna turn you up", "But I turn you up"], "meaning": "Drake’s love interest is pretending to be in love with him for show, but when no one’s around she’s manipulating him and deceiving him to the point where even his friends think he should let it go.\n\nHis close people are worried that he’s going to drive himself to the point of no return for a woman who doesn’t even love him.", "annotation_id": "27240422", "url": "https://genius.com/27240422", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "Baby M, you know I got a heart of gold You pawned that shit and hit the road, baby", "vault_lines": ["I think, think that I", "And I hope that you feel the same", "Baby M, you know I got a heart of gold", "You pawned that shit and hit the road, baby"], "meaning": "Pawn shops are a place people go to make quick cash on valuable items. The woman Drake is referencing in this song pawned his heart of “gold”\nAKA she used Drake to climb the latter or to benefit herself. She took her profit and “hit the road”.", "annotation_id": "27189504", "url": "https://genius.com/27189504", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "He said he rap, he ain’t signed, that ain’t a good sign", "vault_lines": ["He said he rap, he ain't signed, that ain't a good sign"], "meaning": "Drake is saying rappers who are not signed to a record label shows they’re unsuccessful and not a really a good rapper.\nEspecially new and upcoming rappers with a buzz.\nAlso, could be talking about independent rappers who claim being independent is better than being signed to a label but however, these rappers miss out on the opportunity of being signed to a record label with a team, finance and connections, thus leading to a successful rap career.", "annotation_id": "27162506", "url": "https://genius.com/27162506", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "I gotta stop goin’ Lenci’, Rollie, first week", "vault_lines": ["I gotta stop goin' Van Cleef, condo, third week", "I gotta stop goin' Lenci', Rollie, first week"], "meaning": "Lenci' and Rollie are nicknames for the Luxury Brands Balenciaga and Rolex.\n\nDrake continues to mention expensive luxury brands (alongside Van Cleef) that he supposedly bought to the girl he talks about, while reminding himself that it is too soon and that he needs to relax and stop being so geeked up and buy her expensive stuff.\n\nDrake and his Rolex\n\nDrake wearing Balenciaga tracksuit\n\nIt is also possible that Drake compares his spendings on luxury brands to his average album sales by official week. His 1st week sales are upwards of 500k.", "annotation_id": "27117382", "url": "https://genius.com/27117382", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "Shoulda been a wham, bam, thank you ma’am", "vault_lines": ["Shoulda been a wham, bam, thank you ma'am"], "meaning": "A “wham bam thank you ma’am” is slang for a quickie — a fast-paced sexual experience with someone you barely know.\n\nDrake is saying he should’ve just hooked up with her for one night and  moved on.", "annotation_id": "27093195", "url": "https://genius.com/27093195", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "Know I gotta turn my bitch up, turn my bih, T-T Turn my bih, T-T, turn my bih", "vault_lines": ["Gotta turn my bitch up, turn my bitch up", "Turn my bitch up, turn my bitch up", "Turn my bitch up, turn my—", "Know I gotta turn my bitch up, turn my bih, T-T", "Turn my bih, T-T, turn my bih", "Gotta turn my bitch up, turn my— (Ayy)", "But I turn you up"], "meaning": "“T-T” stands for “too turnt.” Drake also used this acronym in his song “Time Flies” from Dark Lane Demo Tapes:\n\n“I’m outside in an AMG\nRight outside, T-T, Too turnt, baby girl, you know me”\n\n“Turn my bitch up” means he wants to lavish his girl with expensive items and put her on to a more lavish lifestyle. This is why in the next few lines he says he needs to stop buying her condos, Rollies, and expensive jewelry from Van Cleef.", "annotation_id": "27093077", "url": "https://genius.com/27093077", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "She leanin’ on me, I’m her crutch", "vault_lines": ["She leanin' on me, I'm her crutch", "She creamin' on me, I'm her crush"], "meaning": "Drake expresses that she is dependent on him, just like a person that can’t walk without a crutch.", "annotation_id": "27075387", "url": "https://genius.com/27075387", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "She askin’ why haven’t I nut I didn’t know we in a rush Enjoyin’ the moment, so hush", "vault_lines": ["She askin' why haven't I nut", "I didn't know we in a rush", "Enjoyin' the moment, so hush"], "meaning": "Women tend to mistake a man not ejaculating within a certain time as if the man is not enjoying the sex the way she expects him to enjoy it. Drake later confirms he does enjoy it by saying he’s “Enjoying the moment”. Time is money and Drake’s time is valuable even though he is aware of this, he still takes time in order to experience the pleasures of life.", "annotation_id": "27075374", "url": "https://genius.com/27075374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "There’s three sides to this story, girl The one you subtweet The one your group chat gets to read The one you come and tell to me I understand it finally", "vault_lines": ["There's three sides to this story, girl", "The one you subtweet", "The one your group chat gets to read", "The one you come and tell to me", "I understand it finally"], "meaning": "The woman Drake is dealing with tends to tell different stories depending on the audience when something happens in her life that involves Drake. She does this in order to paint a different picture than what the reality actually is. In this way she can maintain her current status and the view she wants this specific audience to have. And due to his past experiences, Drake now understands this, finally. This also resonates beautifully with the way Drake says: “You were lost until me” and “It’s my fault”. Taking accountability for the lifestyle and standards he introduced her to.\n\nWhen he says “three sides to the story”, he is probably referring to her version, his version and indeed the true version.", "annotation_id": "27075338", "url": "https://genius.com/27075338", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "The smoke got me talkin’ in Dutch", "vault_lines": ["The smoke got me talkin' in Dutch"], "meaning": "Drake’s so high during this conversation with this woman, his statements sound unintelligible — the same way the language of Dutch sounds to an individual who doesn’t speak it.\n\nAlso, double entendre at play — Dutch Masters is a popular cigar and cigarillo brand used to roll blunts.", "annotation_id": "27054912", "url": "https://genius.com/27054912", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "I gotta stop goin’ Van Cleef", "vault_lines": ["I gotta stop goin' Van Cleef, condo, third week", "I gotta stop goin' Lenci', Rollie, first week"], "meaning": "Van Cleef is a luxury jewelry brand, that Drake seems to mention a lot in his songs. In the song, BackOutsideBoyz, he says,\n\n“Breakin' a Brink’s truck, my right wrist Van Cleef”\n\nShared on his story:\n\n\n\nDrake is a huge fan of jewelry/diamonds.", "annotation_id": "27062638", "url": "https://genius.com/27062638", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "It’s my fault for once, I drain accounts to make you love me", "vault_lines": ["It's my fault, it's my fault, for once I take accountability", "It's my fault for once, I drain accounts to make you love me", "It's my fault for once, it's one-sided loyalty", "It's my fault for once, I got the Wagon, G", "It's my fault for once, that's how you make it seem", "It's my fault, it's my fault"], "meaning": "Drake trying to say he will do anything for a girl try make her love him even by wasting all of his savings on his bank account for her.  Hence “drain accounts”", "annotation_id": "27061659", "url": "https://genius.com/27061659", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "You were lost until me I didn’t get no finder’s fee", "vault_lines": ["You were lost until me", "I didn't get no finder's fee", "You were lost until me, mm-mm, mhm-mm"], "meaning": "A finder’s fee is compensation paid in a business transaction to a third-party for bringing the deal, typically in the sale of real estate or a business, to the purchasing party.\n\nDrake is saying he was not fairly compensated for giving this girl direction and now she’s run off on her own stuff.", "annotation_id": "27055992", "url": "https://genius.com/27055992", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": ".30 on my waist ‘Cause a lotta broke niggas ’round the way ridin’ ’round town And they lookin’ for my face", "vault_lines": ["'Cause a lotta broke niggas 'round the way ridin' 'round town", "And they lookin' for my face"], "meaning": "21 is referring to the semi-automatic pistol Glock .30, which he has rapped about on numerous occasions. On his verse on the XXL Freshmen 2016 Cypher he raps:\n\nRollie on my bitch (What?), 30 on my waist (What?)\n30 to your face (Yeah), 60 to your face (Yeah)\n\nOn Drake’s September 2021 track “Knife Talk”:\n\nMy finger itchy, the Glock like to leave hickeys\n\nAnd on his October 2020 collaboration with Metro Boomin, “Glock in My Lap,” Savage spits the following line:\n\nGlock in my lap, everywhere I’m strapped\nMost these rappers cap, I ain’t givin' dap", "annotation_id": "27052426", "url": "https://genius.com/27052426", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "All weapons formed against me gotta jam", "vault_lines": ["All weapons formed against me gotta jam"], "meaning": "This line seems to be twisting a few words from a scripture in the Old Testament of the Bible, Isaiah 54:17, which states:\n\n17 No weapon that is formed against thee shall prosper; and every tongue that shall rise against thee in judgment thou shalt condemn. This is the heritage of the servants of the Lord, and their righteousness is of me, saith the Lord.", "annotation_id": "27052368", "url": "https://genius.com/27052368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "line": "A pill in Ibiza, so what?", "vault_lines": ["A pill in Ibiza, so what?"], "meaning": "This is a nod to Mike Posner’s 2016 hit “I Took A Pill In Ibiza,” where he sings:\n\nI took a pill in Ibiza to show Avicii I was cool\nAnd when I finally got sober, felt 10 years older\nBut fuck it, it was something to do\n\nhttps://youtu.be/foE1mO2yM04", "annotation_id": "27052247", "url": "https://genius.com/27052247", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hours In Silence", "status": "done", "targets": 40, "clicked": 40, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-21-savage-hours-in-silence-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hours In Silence"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "They actin’ like it’s so aggressive, but you just never known affection I don’t wanna diss you anymore, this really got me second-guessin’", "vault_lines": ["They actin' like it's so aggressive, but you just never known affection", "I don't wanna diss you anymore, this really got me second-guessin'"], "meaning": "Drake asserts that he isn’t jarred by the accusations Kendrick has made against him in “Not Like Us.” Rather, he feeds into the narrative that Kendrick was molested as a youngster, which is introduced and denied on Kendrick’s track “Mother I Sober”.\n\nFamily ties, they accused my cousin, “Did he touch you, Kendrick?”\nNever lied, but no one believed me when I said “He didn’t”\n\nEven though Kendrick explicitly stated he wasn’t molested, His mother didn’t believe his denials, Drake is adopting Lamar’s mother stance casting doubt on Kendrick’s personal backstory. Here he implies that Kendrick has some skeletons in his closet that compromise his judgement. Disingenuously, he expresses empathy towards Lamar, deciding that he has already suffered enough trauma – to the point that Drake is second-guessing whether he should diss him again.", "annotation_id": "31671573", "url": "https://genius.com/31671573", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "But that’s not the lesson, clearly, there’s a deeper message Deep cuts that never healed, and now they got infected", "vault_lines": ["But that's not the lesson, clearly, there's a deeper message", "Deep cuts that never healed, and now they got infected"], "meaning": "But that’s not the lesson, clearly, there’s a deeper message\nDeep cuts that never healed, and now they got infected\n\nHere, Drake attempts to change the subject matter from addressing the allegations being made by people and his opponent, to aggressively chipping away at Kendrick’s armor. Both sides have vulnerabilities and rumors that the other is trying to infiltrate and pick apart.\n\nDeep cuts that never healed, and now they got infected\n\nDrake continues to reference themes from Kendrick’s album, Mr. Morale & the Big Steppers. Earlier he misquoted the message of Mother I Sober by saying “That’s that one record where you say you got molested.”\n\nBut that’s not the lesson, clearly, there is a deeper message\n\nDrake asserts that the lesson is not his innocence or stance on sexual assault and the allegations being made, but rather the challenges and rumours in Kendrick’s life that makes him a worse person than Drake. The following points that Drake makes are not backed by evidence and have several flaws.", "annotation_id": "31666708", "url": "https://genius.com/31666708", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You thought you left D flat, D major", "vault_lines": ["You thought you left D flat, D major"], "meaning": "“D” here refers to Drake himself. Drake earlier used “D” on “Family Matters” to refer to himself in third person:\n\nYou right about “Fuck the big three,” it’s only Big D and there’s video proof\n\nDrake is alluding to the fact that the general perception after “meet the grahams” and “Not Like Us”, released not even 24 hours apart, was that he conceded a big defeat. Drake is clear he isn’t wavering yet and will reign supreme despite the general perception.\n\n“D Major” is a triple entendre :\n\n\nDrake is continuing his use of pitch scale wordplay in retaliation to Kendrick’s “A minor” line on “Not Like Us”.\nDrake recites the line to sound like he is also saying, “D made ya”, claiming that Kendrick’s success is thanks to what Drake has done for him. In February of 2012 Drake invited Kendrick to open on the Club Paradise Tour when he was on the come up. This helped Kendrick garner attention during the early phase of his career.\n\nDrake also could be talking about his people thinking his penis is flaccid in response to the diss tracks, but it’s actually erect. As he said in “Family Matters”:\n\nYou right about “Fuck the big three,” it’s only Big D, and there’s video proof\"", "annotation_id": "31658881", "url": "https://genius.com/31658881", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I am a war general seasoned in preparation My jacket is covered in medals, honor and decoration", "vault_lines": ["I am a war gеneral seasoned in prеparation", "My jacket is covered in medals, honor and decoration"], "meaning": "Here Drake references both the fact that he is coming into this beef well prepared, and that he also has a proven record of victories in previous contests with other rappers.\n\nThe preparation alludes to how Drake has purportedly planned traps that Kendrick has fallen into, most notably that someone in his camp has fed Kendrick false information about an 11-year-old daughter brought up on Lamar’s “meet the grahams.”\n\nDear baby girl\nI’m sorry that your father not active inside your world\n\nThe medals Drake references symbolise previous battles he has won, the most-well known one being his 2015 beef with Meek Mill, that most onlookers acknowledge as a win for Drake. The canadian rapper is wearing these medals proudly, indicating to Kendrick that he thinks he knows exactly how to come out on top in a battle between the two.", "annotation_id": "31655498", "url": "https://genius.com/31655498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I don’t understand why these people praise ya Soundin’ like you send him commissary when he need some paper", "vault_lines": ["I don't understand why these people praise ya", "Soundin' like you send him commissary when he need some paper"], "meaning": "A commissary, in this case, is a store or restaurant that sells food or personal supplies to imprisoned persons. It also refers to the currency used in exchange for these goods. Drake entertains the likelihood that Kendrick would put money on R. Kelly’s books in order for him to spend it in a commissary,", "annotation_id": "31655056", "url": "https://genius.com/31655056", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "The ones that you’re getting your stories from, they all clowns", "vault_lines": ["The ones that you're getting your stories from, they all clowns"], "meaning": "Drake is calling out those who have been feeding Kendrick Lamar information about him and his camp. This could refer to people within Drake’s own circle or external sources. He refers to them as “clowns,” implying that the individuals spreading this inside info are not credible, or perhaps were fed misleading intel themselves. This idea is supported later in the track when Drake raps, “We plotted for a week, and then we fed you the information,” suggesting that he and his camp may have intentionally planted false narratives to expose leaks or mislead Kendrick.\n\nKendrick has already boasted on “6:16 in LA” about having access to OVO’s camp, suggesting an inside connection:\n\nHave you ever thought that OVO is workin' for me?\n\nAfter the beef, there was speculation surrounding hip-hop journalist Elliott Wilson (who was once close to Drake and had multiple interviews with him,) with rumors suggesting that Drake believed Wilson may have been the one passing along information to Kendrick. Supposedly, in response to Wilson asking about “Family Matters,” Drake sent a rat emoji—hinting that he thought he was feeding the other camp info. Drake also told him that he know he is in Venice beach, Florida, supposedly the same place where Kendrick was recording his responses during the beef.\n\n\n\nElliot sharing his text messages with Drake \n\n\n\n\nIn an interview with DJ Vlad, Elliott would address these accusations and, interestingly, said that Kendrick had also jokingly called him a rat as a joke after the fact.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/Ly5FWLps3PM", "annotation_id": "31654345", "url": "https://genius.com/31654345", "state": "accepted", "votes": 173, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Album droppin’ soon, no wonder you turn to a clout chaser ‘stead of doin’ hard labor", "vault_lines": ["Album droppin' soon, no wonder you turn to a clout chaser 'stead of doin' hard labor"], "meaning": "There have been rumours circulating that Kendrick Lamar is planning to drop an album in 2024. Drake claims Kendrick only engaged in this beef as a publicity stunt to promote his new album. Kendrick did instigate this beef back in March 2024 on “Like That”, taking shots at Drake and J. Cole:\n\nMotherfuck the big three, nigga, it’s just big me\nNigga, bum", "annotation_id": "31653833", "url": "https://genius.com/31653833", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Stop buyin’ views and bot comments,", "vault_lines": ["Stop buyin' views and bot comments,"], "meaning": "Drake is insinuating that Kendrick and his camp are cheating their way to victory by buying viewership and deploying bot accounts. It is no secret that Kendrick’s earlier diss tracks gained substantially more views and recognition by the masses; for example, on video-streaming platform YouTube, Kendrick’s “Not Like Us” reached 12.3 million views in just under 13 hours while Drake’s “Family Matters” reached 11.5 million views in approximately 33.5 hours. The botting allegation has been mutual – on “6:16 in LA,” Lamar claimed that Drake utilized paid engagement to promote his tracks:\n\nYou’re playin' nerdy with Zack Bia and Twitter bots\n\nAccusations of Drake utilizing paid botting stem all the way back to the release of his 2016 album Views, which performed with absurdly high, record-breaking numbers.", "annotation_id": "31653057", "url": "https://genius.com/31653057", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You mentionin’ A minor, but niggas gotta B sharp and tell the fans, “Who was it?”", "vault_lines": ["You mentionin' A minor, but niggas gotta B sharp and tell the fans, \"Who was it?\""], "meaning": "Drake gives a response to a play on words line that Kendrick made in Not Like Us meant to accuse Drake of being inappropriate with underage girls.\n\nTryna strike a chord and it’s probably A-Minor\n\n\n\nDrake rebuts the diss by asking Kendrick to “be sharp”, and show proof of any wrongdoings.\n\nThere is no B# note in the chromatic scale, nor is there a B# chord. In written music it is standard practice to refer to B# as just C, though in some special cases you may say “B#”. This is due to enharmonic equivalence; when two notes are the same but written differently.\n\nIt should also be noted that Drake plays the notes for the chords he mentions on a piano in the background. However, when he says “A minor”, he actually plays a G sharp, which is either a mistake or a direct rebuttal to the “A-minor” quote above, because he doesn’t ‘strike’ it.", "annotation_id": "31653990", "url": "https://genius.com/31653990", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "But you a piece of shit, so this shit really no coincidence", "vault_lines": ["But you a piece of shit, so this shit really no coincidence"], "meaning": "This bar concludes the two claims Drake threw Lamar’s way. He first claimed that Kendrick got molested by his cousin when he was young referencing Kendrick’s “Mother I Sober”, a track off Lamar’s release Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers where he takes it’s listeners on a journey through his experiences with personal traumas and sexuality:\n\nFamily ties, they accused my cousin, “Did he touch you, Kendrick?”\nNever lied, but no one believed me when I said “He didn’t”\n\nThe second claim was regarding the faithfulness of  Kendrick’s fiancé, which he questions on this very song once again:\n\nLike if Dave really fucked your girl and got her pregnant, talk about breedin' resentment\n\nDrake is basically saying that Kendrick is a piece of shit and had it coming and deserves the hardships he allegedly suffered in a sense.", "annotation_id": "31653957", "url": "https://genius.com/31653957", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Yeah, maybe when you meet your maker", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, maybe when you meet your maker"], "meaning": "“Meeting one’s maker\" is often used as a euphemism for dying or getting killed (thus meeting one’s god, or creator of life). This would tie back to Drake’s previous line\n\nI’m a fucking hitmaker, dog, not a peacemaker\n\nwhich suggests a few possible things:\n\n\nDrake believes he is the reason why Kendrick is relevant, i.e., he’s the “maker” of Kendrick’s success, so Kendrick will have to keep “meeting” him to keep that success and relevancy (i.e. make Drake the topic of his music).\nBecause of the previous lines being about user engagement and streaming revenue, Drake may be suggesting that Kendrick doesn’t have many real, non-bot fans, and Kendrick can only make the same amount of money as him once he passes away. It’s common for artists to gain a lot of new listeners, sales, and post-mortem releases following their deaths. One notable example is the musician Prince who Kendrick has likened himself to in previous songs.\nDrake suggests that the beef will be lifelong and Kendrick won’t encounter Drake until the afterlife, at which time peace will be made.", "annotation_id": "31653573", "url": "https://genius.com/31653573", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Yeah, bullets that I’m stuffin’ in each chamber, your ass in extreme danger", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, bullets that I'm stuffin' in each chamber, your ass in extreme danger"], "meaning": "The ‘bullets’ may be the ammunition of additional tracks that include more disparaging information aimed at Kendrick, putting his reputation in danger.", "annotation_id": "31653518", "url": "https://genius.com/31653518", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I’m your baby mama’s screen saver", "vault_lines": ["I'm your baby mama's screen saver"], "meaning": "The “baby mama” in question is Whitney Alford, Lamar’s high school sweetheart.\n\nInterestingly, Drake uses “baby mama” as opposed to the more accurate label of “fiancée.” He may be implying, here, that some separation has occurred between Lamar and Alford. This would not be out of the realm of possibility; Drake also suggested some breakup of sorts in the music video for FAMILY MATTERS, with two cakes saying “Happy Co-Parenting” and “Happy Divorce” on display.", "annotation_id": "31653439", "url": "https://genius.com/31653439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You haven’t seen the kids in six months, the distance is wild", "vault_lines": ["You haven't seen the kids in six months, the distance is wild"], "meaning": "Drake is trying to frame Kendrick as more of an absent father/deadbeat father than he is by claiming that Kendrick has not seen his kids in six months and is too busy living out a bachelor life in New York while his fiancee of 9 years is being unfaithful and having kids with Kendrick’s long-time friend and even co-founder of his solo enterprise, pgLang.", "annotation_id": "31653349", "url": "https://genius.com/31653349", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You waited for this moment, overcome with the desperation", "vault_lines": ["You waited for this moment, overcome with the desperation"], "meaning": "Drake and Kendrick Lamar have engaged in a feud for over a decade, exchanging subtle disses. The tension peaked when Kendrick Lamar featured on Future and Metro Boomin’s track “Like That,” with lyrics throwing shots at Drake:\n\nAh, yeah, huh, yeah, get up with me\nFuck sneak dissin', first-person shooter, I hope they came with three switches\n\nDrake is insinuating that Kendrick Lamar is currently desperate to put out anything he can to discredit him, as he’s already dropped multiple tracks back to back.", "annotation_id": "31653344", "url": "https://genius.com/31653344", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Nigga, I’ll see you when I see you like Fantasia", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, I'll see you when I see you like Fantasia"], "meaning": "Drake references American R&B singer and actress Fantasia Barrino’s 2007 song “When I See U”.", "annotation_id": "31653157", "url": "https://genius.com/31653157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You know, but Just let me know when we’re gettin’ to the facts Everything in my shit is facts (Prove it) I’m waitin’ on you to return the favor, like", "vault_lines": ["Just let me see ya prove it", "It is what it is", "Just let me know when we're gettin' to the facts", "Everything in my shit is facts (Prove it)", "I'm waitin' on you to return the favor, like"], "meaning": "Drake claims that Kendrick Lamar’s diss tracks in the feud have been full of lies, while Drake’s have been nothing but facts.\n\nOne of Kendrick’s most notable allegations was that Drake was hiding an abandoned 11-year-old daughter. On diss track “meet the grahams”, Kendrick rapped:\n\nDear baby girl\nI’m sorry that your father not active inside your world\n[…]\nShould be teachin' you time tables or watchin' Frozen with you\nOr at your eleventh birthday singin' poems with you\n\nHowever, earlier in this track Drake claimed he intentionally leaked this false information to Kendrick to make a fool out of him, and essentially to waste his time creating a diss track that is predicated on false information:\n\nWe plotted for a week and then we fed you the information\nA daughter that’s eleven years old, I bet he takes it\n\nAnother prominent theme in Kendrick’s diss tracks was claiming that Drake is a pedophile. For example, on “Not Like Us” Kendrick explicitly says:\n\nCertified Lover Boy, certified pedophiles\n\nHowever, earlier in this track Drake denies these allegations and claims they are a simply a projection of Kendrick’s description of sexual abuse as heard on “Mother I Sober”.\n\nSome of Drake’s most notable disses against Kendrick are that he physically abused his fiancé Whitney, and that one of his children’s biological fathers is actually Dave Free, Kendrick’s childhood friend and manager. Drake claims these allegations are true. However, no concrete evidence has been provided for any of these allegations by either sides. And without receipts from either side, this is simply a game of he said, she said.", "annotation_id": "31653147", "url": "https://genius.com/31653147", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I’m not gonna lie, this shit was some, some good exercise, like It’s good to get out, get the pen workin’", "vault_lines": ["I'm not gonna lie, this shit was some, some good exercise, like", "It's good to get out, get the pen workin'"], "meaning": "One of the most ironic parts about this beef between Drake and Kendrick Lamar is that Drake was planning on taking a significant break from music, as he revealed on the 3rd episode of his Sirius XM radio show Table For One in the minutes before the release of For All The Dogs in October.\n\nAfter Kendrick started the beef with his verse on “Like That”, Drake has now become involved in a career-defining rap beef against one of his biggest competitors that will largely affect his legacy when his career comes to a close.\n\nDrake also mentions this beef is “good exercise”. Notably, he didn’t deny any of the claims Kendrick made about his abs being fake on “euphoria” on this track, so this may be a tongue-in-cheek resignation on his part.\n\nYeah, my first one like my last one, it’s a classic, you don’t have one\nLet your core audience stomach that, then tell ‘em where you get your abs from", "annotation_id": "31653145", "url": "https://genius.com/31653145", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You may as well keep the paper Shit you ’bout to need for later I give a fuck about your streamin’ data", "vault_lines": ["you may as well keep the paper", "Shit you 'bout to need for later", "I give a fuck about your streamin' data"], "meaning": "Drake is calling out other artists or individuals who artificially inflate their popularity online by purchasing views and bot comments on platforms like YouTube, Instagram, and X (formerly Twitter). Drake suggests that instead of wasting money on fake engagement, they might as well keep their money (the ‘paper’) for future needs.\n\nDrake then dismisses the significance of their streaming data, indicating that he’s not impressed by their supposed success achieved through artificial means.", "annotation_id": "31653131", "url": "https://genius.com/31653131", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You could drop a hundred more records, I’ll see you later", "vault_lines": ["You could drop a hundred more records, I'll see you later"], "meaning": "Drake implies that no matter how many more tracks Kendrick drops, it won’t make him feel any  bit of concern or threat.\n\nOn “Not Like Us”, Kendrick Lamar says that there is even more to the situation about Drake than what he’s revealed, confirming that there are more tracks that he will drop:\n\nRabbit hole is still deep, I can go further, I promise", "annotation_id": "31653114", "url": "https://genius.com/31653114", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You know, at least your fans are gettin’ some raps out of you I’m happy I could motivate you Bring you back to the game, like", "vault_lines": ["You know, at least your fans are gettin' some raps out of you", "I'm happy I could motivate you", "Bring you back to the game, like"], "meaning": "Drake seems to be boasting that he is the reason Kendrick Lamar made a return to mainstream music, while also telling Kendrick’s fans to thank him for it.\n\nThis comes after Kendrick released his 2017 album, DAMN., and went radio silent for the following five years. After only producing the soundtrack for the movie Black Panther in 2018, it was thought he was retiring from music. But then in 2021, on a website named oklama.com, he wrote the following message which was taken as a retirement speech and new album confirmation by fans:\n\nAs I produce my final TDE album, I feel joy to have been a part of such a cultural imprint after 17 years. The Struggles. The Success. And most importantly, the Brotherhood. May the Most High continue to use Top Dawg as a vessel for candid creators. As I continue to pursue my life’s calling.\n\nFollowing this message, he once again fell silent until in 2022, when a fan on X (previously named Twitter) called him out by saying he was “officially retired.” Kendrick then replied to this tweet with a link to the oklama website, which displayed the upcoming release date for his thought-to-be final album, Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kendricklamar/status/1516114615477825537?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E1516114615477825537%7Ctwgr%5E7725fa9884a389c81de22b04c5446d2c515c7191%7Ctwcon%5Es1_&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fbleumag.com%2Fmusic%2Fis-kendrick-lamar-retired%2F\n\nAfter the album’s release, he didn’t put out any music of his own, instead opting to do features on songs. This is where the track feature “Like That” comes in when Kendrick seemingly reignited the beef between him, Drake, and J. Cole. Kendrick has released four singles since then as he and Drake are engaged in a back-and-forth with diss tracks aimed at one another.", "annotation_id": "31653105", "url": "https://genius.com/31653105", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I never been with no one underage, but now I understand why this the angle that you really mess with Just for clarity, I feel disgusted, I’m too respected If I was fucking young girls, I promise I’d have been arrested I’m way too famous for this shit you just suggested", "vault_lines": ["This Epstein angle was the shit I expected", "Just for clarity, I feel disgusted, I'm too respected", "If I was fucking young girls, I promise I'd have been arrested", "I'm way too famous for this shit you just suggested"], "meaning": "Drake puts his guard up and denies Kendrick’s allegations of him being a pedophile, and continues his claims that Kendrick Lamar was molested as a child and therefore obsessed seeing how eager Kendrick is trying to push the allegations onto Drake with the songs, “​meet the grahams”, “Not Like Us”.\n\nDrake then explains he feels he is too popular and famous to mess around with underaged girls, as he has a lot to lose in such a foolish situation, despite several known celebrities such as Harvey Weinstein, Jeffrey Epstein, Bill Cosby, R. Kelly, Roman Polanski, and most recently, P. Diddy being referred to as sex offenders with multiple accusations each of sex trafficking and sexual assault.", "annotation_id": "31653104", "url": "https://genius.com/31653104", "state": "accepted", "votes": 216, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I would like that one, that w—, that-that would be some shit I could dance to if you wasn’t Triplin’ down on some whole other bullshit, but", "vault_lines": ["I would like that one, that w—, that-that would be some shit I could dance to if you wasn't", "Triplin' down on some whole other bullshit, but"], "meaning": "Fans praised Kendrick’s most recent diss, “Not Like Us”, released a day prior to this track, for being more danceable than the previous disses Kendrick directed towards Drake.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Imakemyownshit/status/1786923275789971808\n\nIce Cube’s son, O'Shea Jackson Jr., also shared this sentiment, garnering 87k+ likes at the time of this writing.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/OsheaJacksonJr/status/1786907901065626086\n\nThe statement about “triplin' down” refers to Kendrick releasing 3 diss tracks towards Drake in rapid succession over the previous 2 days, “6:16 in LA”, “meet the grahams”, and “Not Like Us”\n\nDrake states that unlike the fans celebrating it, he cannot dance to Kendrick’s latest track because of the “bullshit” i.e. false accusations (according to Drake) on the song.", "annotation_id": "31653100", "url": "https://genius.com/31653100", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "And Whitney, you can hit me if you need a favor And when I say I hit ya back, it’s a lot safer Huh, I promise", "vault_lines": ["And Whitney, you can hit me if you need a favor", "And when I say I hit ya back, it's a lot safer"], "meaning": "Whitney Alford is Kendrick Lamar’s fiancée. Judging by Drake’s tone and how personal this battle has become, this “favor” likely has sexual connotations, and could be viewed as an opportunity for Whitney to get revenge for the alleged domestic abuse from Kendrick.\n\nDrake plays on two homonyms of “hit.“ One describes physical abuse and the other is slang for sending some sort of message—Drake insinuates that Kendrick tends to enact the former, while Drake only intends to use the latter if Whitney chooses to accept his offer.\n\nDrake claims that Kendrick domestically abused Whitney on his previous diss track “Family Matters”:\n\nWhen you put your hands on your girl, is it self-defense ’cause she bigger than you?\n[…] They hired a crisis management team to clean up the fact that you beat on your queen\n\nDrake also implies that if he has sex with Whitney, he will wear protection, unlike Dave Free. Drake alleges that Dave, Kendrick’s manager and childhood friend, fathered one of her children both on this track as well as on the aforementioned “Family Matters”:\n\nI heard that one of ‘em little kids might be Dave Free\nDon’t make it Dave Free’s\n'Cause if your GM is your BM secret BD\nThen this is all makin’ plenty fuckin' sense to me\n[…] Shake that ass for Drake, now shake that ass for free", "annotation_id": "31653098", "url": "https://genius.com/31653098", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I’m a fucking hitmaker, dog, not a peacemaker", "vault_lines": ["I'm a fucking hitmaker, dog, not a peacemaker"], "meaning": "Drake calls back to his claim on “Push Ups”:\n\nI’m the hitmaker y'all depend on\n\nThis could also be interpreted as a slight toward Kendrick Lamar who consistently portrays himself as a positive artist creating uplifting music that resonates deeply with his audience, and had previously portrayed himself as a peacemaker on “Rich Spirit”:\n\nAyy, peacemaker, but I’m not naive, brother\n\nHowever, this time Drake is taking advantage of the homophone and referring to calling hits on people, making it clear that the beef has elevated into something serious.\n\nIt wouldn’t be the first time that Drake has alluded to contract killing with street lingo:\n\nDrop, drop, drop, drop\nDrop a fifty bag for the mob in the spot\nDrop a fifty bag, twenty-nine for the thot\nUh, I was really, really tryna keep it PG\n  Drake on “Push Ups”\n\n\nIt’s worth noting that Kendrick directly wished death upon Drake on “meet the grahams”:\n\nMm-mm, your son’s a sick man with sick thoughts, I think niggas like him should die\nHim and Weinstein should get fucked up in a cell for the rest they life\n[…] Fuck a rap battle, he should die so all of these women can live with a purpose\n\nThis might also be a response to Lamar’s verse on “euphoria”:\n\nI make music that electrify ‘em, you make music that pacify 'em", "annotation_id": "31653096", "url": "https://genius.com/31653096", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I don’t wanna fight with a woman beater, it feeds your nature", "vault_lines": ["I don't wanna fight with a woman beater, it feeds your nature"], "meaning": "This links back to Drake’s previous diss track, “Family Matters”, where he alleged that Kendrick abused his fiancée:\n\nThey hired a crisis management team to clean up the fact that you beat on your queen\n\nDrake implies that by beefing with Kendrick, he is feeding his supposed aggressive “nature”, which would increase the likelihood of that person taking out their anger in the form of assault or, as Drake alleges, domestic abuse.", "annotation_id": "31653075", "url": "https://genius.com/31653075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "My Montreal connects stand up, not fall down", "vault_lines": ["My Montreal connects stand up, not fall down"], "meaning": "Drake suggests that his connections – whether shooters or intelligence – can be mobilized or stand down at any moment’s notice.\n\nHe makes this commanding image more explicit a few bars later in this song:\n\nI am a war general, seasoned in preparation\n\nSpecifically, Canada has significant Hell’s Angels presence. At the end of the music video for Drake’s diss, “Family Matters,” he meets with an alleged Hells Angels member, Andrew Curnew, who is Drake’s neighbor.\n\n\n\nAndre Curnew making an appearance in the music video for Drake’s diss, “Family Matters”", "annotation_id": "31653064", "url": "https://genius.com/31653064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You would be a worthy competitor if I was really a predator And you weren’t fuckin’ lying to every blogger and editor, but (Prove it) It is what it is", "vault_lines": ["You would be a worthy competitor if I was really a predator", "And you weren't fuckin' lying to every blogger and editor, but (Prove it)", "It is what it is"], "meaning": "Drake was accused in having connections with younger women, as stated by Kendrick Lamar on “Not Like Us”.\n\nSay, Drake, I hear you like ‘em young\n\nBack in 2018, there was a lot of discussion about Drake and actress Millie Bobby Brown’s friendship, which sparked interest due to their age gap. This all started when Millie, just 14 at the time, met Drake at his concert in Brisbane and posted about it on Instagram, calling him “fantastic” and “a great friend.” Their close bond raised questions with some questioning the appropriateness of their relationship given Drake’s way older age and reputation.\n\n\n\nAlso, back in 2010, there was a video circulating around where Drake started kissing a 17-year-old in one of his concerts and after finding out her age he said:\n\nSeventeen? Why you look like that? You thick, look at all this. I don’t know if I should feel guilty or not, but I had fun. I like the way your breasts feel against my chest.", "annotation_id": "31653058", "url": "https://genius.com/31653058", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "You definitely got this shit burnt the fuck out though, like You got ten more records to drop The one before the last one, we finessed you into tellin’ a story that doesn’t even exist And then, you go and drop the West Coast one to try and cover that up", "vault_lines": ["You definitely got this shit burnt the fuck out though, like", "You got ten more records to drop", "And then, you go and drop the West Coast one to try and cover that up"], "meaning": "In Kendrick’s diss “Not Like Us” Kendrick raps\n\nHow many stocks do I really have in stock? Ayy\nOne, two, three, four, five plus five, ayy\n\nDrake replies by saying that he doesn’t care, as nothing Kendrick says is true, and that the OVO rat was actually working with Drake to feed Kendrick misinformation. Drake claims Kendrick dropped “Not Like Us” to hide the mistake he made with claiming Drake had a hidden daughter on his track “Meet the Grahams.”", "annotation_id": "31653051", "url": "https://genius.com/31653051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Like if Dave really fucked your girl and got her pregnant, talk about breedin’ resentment I’m not sure how to ease the sentiment, this shit’s too intimate I’m praying you recover from both incidents", "vault_lines": ["Like if Dave really fucked your girl and got her pregnant, talk about breedin' resentment", "I'm not sure how to ease the sentiment, this shit's too intimate", "I'm praying you recover from both incidents"], "meaning": "Drake again references Dave Free, Kendrick Lamar’s manager and childhood friend, allegedly sleeping with Whitney (Kendrick’s fiancé) and being the biological father of one of his children. Drake previously discussed this on “Family Matters”:\n\nI heard that one of ’em little kids might be Dave Free\n\nDrake plays on the homonym “breeding”, insinuating that when Dave Free bred (impregnated) Whitney, this bred (created) resentment inside Kendrick, potentially for Whitney, Dave and even his own son.\n\nAs Drake states, if true, this is an extremely intimate subject for Kendrick. Suffering infidelity can cause post-traumatic stress, particularly if the betrayal was executed by one of your closest friends, and especially if they are the biological father of your child. This further plays into Drake’s counter-narrative that Kendrick continuously accuses him of being a pedophile as a projection of Kendrick’s own sexual trauma. Drake discusses this in more detail earlier in this track:\n\n“Mother, I—, mother, I—, mother—”\nAhh, wait a second, that’s that one record where you say you got molested\nAw, fuck me, I just made the whole connection\nThis about to get so depressin’\nThis is trauma from your own confessions\nThis when your father leave you home alone with no protection, so neglected\nThat’s why these pedophile raps is shit you so obsessed with, it’s so excessive", "annotation_id": "31652990", "url": "https://genius.com/31652990", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "My mom came over today, and I was like, ”Mother, I— Mother, I—, mother—,” ah, wait a second That’s that one record where you say you got molested Aw, fuck me, I just made the whole connection This about to get so depressin’ This is trauma from your own confessions This when your father leave you home alone with no protection, so neglected That’s why these pedophile raps and shit you so obsessed with, it’s so excessive", "vault_lines": ["This Epstein angle was the shit I expected", "My mom came over today, and I was like, \"Mother, I—", "Mother, I—, mother—,\" ah, wait a second", "That's that one record where you say you got molested", "Aw, fuck me, I just made the whole connection", "This about to get so depressin'", "This is trauma from your own confessions", "This when your father leave you home alone with no protection, so neglected", "That's why these pedophile raps and shit you so obsessed with, it's so excessive", "I give a fuck about your streamin' data", "You definitely got this shit burnt the fuck out though, like"], "meaning": "Drake references “Mother I Sober” by Kendrick Lamar, the 17th track of Lamar’s 2022 release Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers where he delves into his sexual traumas:\n\nFamily ties, they accused my cousin, “Did he touch you, Kendrick?”\nNever lied, but no one believed me when I said “He didn’t”\n\nAs that quote implies, it should be noted that Kendrick makes it explicitly clear in the song that he wasn’t molested. Regardless, Drake figures that Lamar is projecting this trauma when he accuses Drake of being a pedophile in his May 2024 diss tracks, “meet the grahams” and “Not Like Us.” On the latter, he straight up says:\n\nCertified Lover Boy? Certified pedophiles\n\nDrake refusing to say “sober” may be him implying that the track was so boring that he can’t remember the title or subtly playing into Lamar’s allegations that Drake is a drug addict. On the aforementioned “meet the grahams,” Kendrick rapped:\n\nYou got gamblin' problems, drinkin' problems, pill-poppin' and spendin' problems", "annotation_id": "31652978", "url": "https://genius.com/31652978", "state": "accepted", "votes": 343, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "If you still bumpin’ R. Kelly, you could thank the Savior Said if they deleted his music, then your music is goin’ too, a hypocrite", "vault_lines": ["If you still bumpin' R. Kelly, you could thank the Savior", "Said if they deleted his music, then your music is goin' too, a hypocrite"], "meaning": "Drake references Spotify’s launch of its Hate Content & Hateful Conduct policy in 2018. This policy stated that the platform “may remove” hateful music and that “when an artist or creator does something that is especially harmful or hateful, it may affect the ways [they] work[ed] with or support[ed] that artist or creator.” The policy’s implementation led to multiple artists being removed from playlists, with the most notable examples being XXXTENTACION and R. Kelly. At the time, the latter was a known child pornographer and faced further allegations of abuse, which were explored in-depth for the 2019 documentary series, Surviving R. Kelly. The series and subsequent legal proceedings revealed that Kelly was responsible for multiple instances of sexual abuse and sex trafficking.\n\nThe policy was met with significant criticism from fans and the music community, leading Spotify to retract it within a month. Top Dawg, the CEO of Kendrick’s then-label, Top Dawg Entertainment, was among the vocal critics. In a Billboard interview, Top recounted:\n\n[I] had a conversation [with Spotify], and I expressed [that] I don’t think it’s right for artists to be censored, especially in our culture. […] How come they didn’t pick out any other [artists] from any other genres or […] cultures? There [are] so many other artists [with] different things going on, and they could’ve picked anybody. But it seems to me that they’re constantly picking on hip-hop culture. My whole thing with them was, we gotta fix this situation, and if it can’t be fixed, then there’s gonna be a real problem; we’re gonna have to start pulling our music from the site.\n\nFollowing this interview, many publications misreported that Top spoke as Kendrick’s representative and not as TDE’s CEO. Years before in 2015, Kendrick clarified that Top Dawg’s opinions were personal and not representative of his views.\n\nDrake recirculates this misconception to his benefit to address Kendrick’s allegations in his 2024 disses, where Kendrick called Drake a sexual predator. Drake argues that Kendrick’s criticism is hypocritical if Kendrick has supported another alleged sexual predator. Drake may also be referencing Kendrick’s 2015 track “The Blacker the Berry” where he repeatedly labeled himself a hypocrite.\n\nDrake also references Kendrick’s 2022 Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers track “Savior” to imply Kendrick hails himself as a “savior”, although Kendrick’s lyrics in the song reject that notion. While this could be perceived as Drake misrepresenting Kendrick’s lyrics again, the context suggests otherwise. This track is Drake’s response to Kendrick’s “meet the grahams”, which saw him claim he made MM&TBS to help future generations “cope”; this sentiment appears at odds with the album’s narrative where he sheds his savior complex.\n\nIt is noteworthy that Kelly has writing credits on multiple Drake songs, including 2010’s “Unforgettable”, 2017’s “Since Way Back” and 2021’s “TSU”. The former is due to Drake sampling Aaliyah, whom Drake has long been obsessed with. Kelly notably wrote a considerable amount of her music and engaged in an unethical relationship with her, which included them getting married when she and he were 15 and 27, respectively.\n\nAll this said, Kendrick has not been unequivocally anti-abuser. In 2017, Kendrick promoted the music of XXXTENTACION, who admitted to being a domestic abuser and had numerous troubling abuse allegations. On his previously mentioned 2022 album MM&TBS, Kendrick referenced R. Kelly twice, discussing the separation of art from artist and empathetically raising questions about childhood trauma and the cyclical nature of abuse (“I think about Robert Kelly/If he weren’t molested, I wonder if life’d fail him”). Additionally, rapper Kodak Black, who was controversially interwoven into Kendrick’s MM&TBS, has consistently demonstrated misogynistic viewpoints during his career and has faced legal issues related to sexual misconduct. Kendrick explored the complexity of abuse and how it can shape the behavior of figures like Kodak in MM&TBS’s “Mother I Sober”:\n\nEvery other brother has been compromised\nI know the secrets, every other rapper sexually abused\nI see ‘em daily buryin’ they pain in chains and tattoos\nSo listen close before you start to pass judgment on how he move\nLearn how he cope, whenever his uncle had to walk him from school\nHis anger grows deep in misogyny", "annotation_id": "31652971", "url": "https://genius.com/31652971", "state": "accepted", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I’ll slit your throat with the razor And do Rick Ross Air like that one flight from Malaysia", "vault_lines": ["I'll slit your throat with the razor", "And do Rick Ross Air like that one flight from Malaysia"], "meaning": "Drake directly threatens to murder Rick Ross, whose plane emergency crash landed on May 3, 2024. Ross jokingly blamed Drake for the incident when he tweeted:\nhttps://x.com/RickRoss/status/1786559840585720248\n\n\n\n Photo taken of Ross' Gulfstream G-5 after the emergency landing \nDrake refers to Malaysia Airlines flight 370 and its Internationally-covered disappearance. The plane departed from Malaysia on March 8, 2014 before vanishing above the Indian Ocean. Drake plays on the name of Rick Ross' private jet, as cutting open his throat would cut off his oxygen.", "annotation_id": "31652948", "url": "https://genius.com/31652948", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Drake is not a name that you gon’ see on no sex offender list, Eazy-Duz-It", "vault_lines": ["Drake is not a name that you gon' see on no sex offender list, Eazy-Duz-It", "It is what it is"], "meaning": "Drake is very explicitly saying he is NOT a sex offender, despite what other people may believe. He follows up using the phrase “Easy does it” which is a reference to the Eazy E song “Eazy-Duz-It,“ which was originally used as a sample for the song ”Like That,“. He is saying that you will not see Drake on a sex offender list but you will however see Eric Wright aka Eazy W on the FBI.gov sex offender list, hence the phrase “Eazy Duz It”", "annotation_id": "31652942", "url": "https://genius.com/31652942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Only fuckin’ with Whitneys, not Millie Bobby Browns, I’d never look twice at no teenager", "vault_lines": ["Only fuckin' with Whitneys, not Millie Bobby Browns, I'd never look twice at no teenager"], "meaning": "Drake jokes that he can’t possibly be a pedophile like Lamar alleges he is since he’s only sexually interested in Lamar’s fiancé, Whitney Alford, as opposed to actress Millie Bobby Brown.\n\n\n\nMillie Bobby Brown (Left) and Drake (Right.)\n\nDrake came under fire for his controversially close relationship with the actress in 2018 when she said this about Drake in an Emmy Awards interview:\n\nI love him. I met him in Australia and he’s honestly so fantastic and a great friend and a great role model […] We just texted the other day and he was like ‘I miss you so much,’ and I was like ‘I miss you more!‘ […] [He gives me advice] about boys, he helps me. He’s great. He’s wonderful. I love him. […] [The advice] stays in the text messages.\n\nBrown was 14 years old at the time of the interview, prompting many to accuse Drake of grooming her. Drake laughed off these allegations on his “Another Late Night” from his 2023 release For All The Dogs:\n\nWeirdos in my comments talkin' ‘bout some Millie Bobby, look\nBring them jokes up to the gang, we get to really flockin’\n\nIt should be noted that a controversial video of Drake kissing and caressing a 17-year-old girl onstage during a May 2010 concert muddies the validity of his claim that he “never looks twice” at a teenager. When the attendee revealed her age, Drake said:\n\nI can’t go to jail yet, man. You’re 17, why do you look like that? You thick. Look at all this. […] I don’t know if I should feel guilty or not, but I had fun. I like the way your breasts feel against my chest.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fp5b9dW1nrA", "annotation_id": "31652934", "url": "https://genius.com/31652934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 123, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "I got your fucking lines tapped, I swear that I’m dialed in", "vault_lines": ["I got your fucking lines tapped, I swear that I'm dialed in"], "meaning": "“Lines tapped” refers to phone conversations being listened to by unauthorized parties. This is a response to Kendrick’s claims that he has moles in OVO, suggesting that Drake also has sources in Kendrick’s camp. “Dialed in” is a way to say you’re dedicating all your focus on a single task – in this case, Drake is focused on the beef. It’s also a play on words as “dial” is what you do to make a phone call. It could also mean that he was “in” on the call, implying he was setting Kendrick Lamar up with a staged OVO rat, to lead him on with false information.\n\nKendrick Lamar ended up using this alleged misinformation in “6:16 IN LA”, saying that Drake had people who ratted out information, using the information against him\n\n\n“Have you ever thought that OVO is workin' for me?”\n\n“Everyone inside your team is whispering that you deserve it, can’t Toosie Slide up out of this one”\n\n“It’s time that you look around on who’s around you, before you figure that you’re not alone, ask what Mike would do”\n\n\nand on “meet the grahams”, mentioning he has another child, following the cover art on from the mole that included alleged items from Drake’s personal collection.\n\nDrake has also been seen using the phrase “dialed in” for all of his posts last year leading up to his album, For All The Dogs.", "annotation_id": "31652893", "url": "https://genius.com/31652893", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "What about the bones we dug up in that excavation? And why isn’t Whitney denyin’ all of the allegations? Why is she followin’ Dave Free and not Mr. Morale?", "vault_lines": ["What about the bones we dug up in that excavation?", "And why isn't Whitney denyin' all of the allegations?", "Why is she followin' Dave Free and not Mr. Morale?", "It is what it is"], "meaning": "This is a follow-on from “Family Matters”:\n\nProposed in 2015, but don’t wanna make her your actual wife\nI’m guessin' this wedding ain’t happenin', right?\n[…]\nI heard that one of ‘em little kids might be Dave Free\n\nThese rumors include Kendrick’s fiance, Whitney Alford, and Dave Free, Kendrick’s long-time collaborator and right-hand man. Drake alleges several times in the track that one of Kendrick’s kids is actually Free’s.\n\nAs Drake says, Alford is not following Kendrick on Instagram (and never has), while she is following Dave Free:\n\n\n\nKendrick warned Drake not to bring up his family on “euphoria”, and it has been thought that the release of “meet the grahams” and “Not Like Us” were a direct response to when he did:\n\nDon’t speak on the family, crodie\nIt can get deep in the family, crodie\nTalk about me and my family, crodie?\nSomeone gon' bleed in your family, crodie\n\nEven so, Drake is questioning why neither Kendrick or Whitney are rebutting or addressing these claims; Kendrick does not dispute the issue on any of the above tracks.", "annotation_id": "31652882", "url": "https://genius.com/31652882", "state": "accepted", "votes": 111, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "“Touch My Body” by Mariah Carey play, you probably start reflectin’", "vault_lines": ["\"Touch My Body\" by Mariah Carey play, you probably start reflectin'"], "meaning": "Reference to Mariah Carey’s number-one hit “Touch My Body”.\n\nKendrick’s song “Mother I Sober” details his mother’s experience with sexual abuse, and effects this trauma can have on a whole bloodline and the black community at whole.\n\nDrake is therefore saying that “Touch My Body” triggers Kendrick’s indirect trauma of sexual abuse and is likely to start reminiscing, as he did on “Mother I Sober”", "annotation_id": "31652869", "url": "https://genius.com/31652869", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "[Intro: Drake, Aretha Franklin] Now let me see ya prove it (Never miss) Just let me see ya prove it", "vault_lines": ["Now let me see ya prove it (Never miss)", "Just let me see ya prove it"], "meaning": "Boi-1da samples Aretha Franklin’s “Prove It” to reflect Kendrick Lamar’s lack of concrete evidence in this feud:\nhttps://youtu.be/vrg1K1SykTA?si=PgE1sGkn5Z3lyV0w&t=98\n\nThe song recounts Aretha Franklin’s heartbreak; she begs her lover to “prove” that when he leaves, she’ll be okay. Notably, she sings:\n\nProve it to my starving heart\nProve it now before we part\n\nThis may link back to the title of this track.", "annotation_id": "31652848", "url": "https://genius.com/31652848", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "The Pulitzer Prize winner is definitely spiralin’", "vault_lines": ["The Pulitzer Prize winner is definitely spiralin'"], "meaning": "Kendrick Lamar won the 2018 Pulitzer Prize in Music for his 2017 album DAMN. It’s regarded as one of the most prestigious awards in journalism, literature, and musical composition in the United States.\n\n\n\nRick Ross also mentioned Kendrick’s achievement on his Drake diss track “Champagne Moments”:\n\nPulitzer Prize winner switchin' up like dyed denim\n\nThis line is a direct reference to Kendrick’s bar on “euphoria”:\n\nThe famous actor we once knew is lookin' paranoid and now is spiralin'\n\nThe line might be Drake pointing out a hypocrisy in Kendrick and a feeling of bias that Drake might have felt he received for releasing two diss tracks. In terms of mental health, “spiraling” refers to overwhelming and catastrophic thinking, in which people imagine worst-case possibilities. For example, one minute someone is concerned about a cough, and the next they’re convinced they’ve contracted a terrible sickness. Likewise, Kendrick insinuated that Drake was having a mental spiral because of the anxiety resulting from the silence Kendrick had been giving him. This was after Drake released two diss tracks, “Push Ups” and “Taylor Made Freestyle” without any response from Kendrick until 11 days later when “euphoria” was released.\n\nIn a twist of events, Kendrick went on to release four diss tracks within five days; “euphoria” on the 30th of April 2024, “6:16 in LA” three days later, and on the next day he released “meet the grahams” within an hour of Drake’s “Family Matters” to which he followed up with “Not Like Us” in less than 24 hours, making it four Kendrick tracks against one from Drake. Therefore, Drake points out that according to Kendrick’s definition of “spiraling” he too began spiraling just as he claimed Drake did.", "annotation_id": "31652844", "url": "https://genius.com/31652844", "state": "accepted", "votes": 95, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "First, I was a rat, so where’s the proof of the trial then? Where’s the paperwork or the cabinet it’s filed in? 1090 Jake woulda took all the walls down The streets woulda had me hidin’ out in a small town", "vault_lines": ["First, I was a rat, so where's the proof of the trial then?", "Where's the paperwork or the cabinet it's filed in?", "1090 Jake woulda took all the walls down", "The streets woulda had me hidin' out in a small town"], "meaning": "Here. Drake denies claims that he’s “ratted” or testified to authorities in his past.\n\nThere is a half-baked legitimacy to these claims. In 2009, Drake was robbed outside of New Ho King, a small Chinese restaurant and late-night staple in Toronto. His cooperation with the police led to the arrest of a fellow Toronto rapper by the name of Sizzlac. With this situation rippling into the Toronto rap scene, it led many of Drake’s fans and peers to question his street credibility.\n\nKendrick rehashed the conversation around these events by name dropping New Ho King and seemingly mocking Drake for being robbed in his diss track, “euphoria.”\n\nI be at New Ho King eatin' fried rice with a dip sauce and a blammy, crodie\n\nDrake goes on to mention a well known  media personality, “1090 Jake.” Jake is notorious for researching court paperwork of popular rappers and street figures and uploading YouTube videos exposing them of dishonesty, cooperation with law enforcement, and criminal misconduct. Drake asserts that if he ever snitched, then his name would be found in court documents and an individual like 1090 Jake would’ve exposed him for it.", "annotation_id": "31652840", "url": "https://genius.com/31652840", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Dave leavin’ heart emojis underneath pics of the child", "vault_lines": ["Dave leavin' heart emojis underneath pics of the child"], "meaning": "Kendrick’s childhood friend and manager Dave Free commented a heart emoji on an Instagram post with Kendrick’s fiance Whitney Alford and the couple’s children. This comment was also featured on the cover art.\n\n\n\nDrake previously claimed one of Kendrick’s children’s real dad was Dave Free on “Family Matters”:\n\nI heard that one of ’em little kids might be Dave Free", "annotation_id": "31652832", "url": "https://genius.com/31652832", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "Speakin’ of anything with a child, let’s get to that now This Epstein angle was the shit I expected TikTok videos you collected and dissected Instead of bein’ on some diss-direct shit You rather fucking grab your pen and misdirect shit", "vault_lines": ["Speakin' of anything with a child, let's get to that now", "This Epstein angle was the shit I expected", "TikTok videos you collected and dissected", "Instead of bein' on some diss-direct shit", "You rather fucking grab your pen and misdirect shit"], "meaning": "Drake likens the pedophile allegations to a level of Jeffrey Epstein, an American financier and sex offender who in 2019 got arrested for sex trafficking of minors in Florida and New York.\n\nThe “Epstein” angle levied on him by Kendrick Lamar is something Drake expected, mentioned, and even goaded Lamar into doing beforehand, on “Taylor Made Freestyle”:\n\nFuck this Canadian lightskin, Dot\n[…]\nTalk about him likin' young girls, that’s a gift from me\nHeard it on the Budden Podcast, it’s gotta be true\n\nKendrick went on to make the claims of Drake and his OVO team being child predators and sex traffickers on the diss track titled “Not Like Us”:\n\nSay, Drake, I hear you like ‘em young\n[…]\nCertified Lover Boy? Certified pedophiles\n[…]\nWhy you trollin’ like a bitch? Ain’t you tired?\nTryna strike a chord and it’s probably A minor\n\nHe additionally claims that Kendrick had gotten allegations from TikTok. What Drake might be referring to here is the following line from Kendrick’s diss, “meet the grahams”:\n\nI been in this industry twelve years, I'ma tell y'all one lil' secret\nIt’s some weird shit goin' on and some of these artists be here to police it\nThey be streamlinin' victims all inside of they home and callin' ‘em tender\nThen leak videos of themselves to further push their agendas\n\nWhat Kendrick is referring to here are allegations made by TikToker @eileensgarden, claiming Drake and his team were running a “private tinder”.\n\nIt should be noted that on his song “Chicago Freestyle” from his May 2020 album Dark Lane Demo Tapes, Drake admits outright that his friend Chubbs does go out to find him women:\n\nAll good, Chubbs will look around\nFind one that seem my type\nThat my dawg and he know what I like\nHe done found me plenty in my life", "annotation_id": "31652831", "url": "https://genius.com/31652831", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "THE HEART PART 6", "line": "We plotted for a week, and then we fed you the information A daughter that’s eleven years old, I bet he takes it We thought about givin’ a fake name or a destination But you so thirsty, you not concerned with investigation Instead you in that Venice studio, it’s a celebration You gotta learn to fact-check things and be less impatient Your fans are rejoicin’ thinkin’ this is my expiration Even the picture you used, the jokes and the medication The Maybach glove and the drug he uses for less inflation Master manipulator, you bit on the speculation You dumb and reactive, nigga, I’m petty with dedication", "vault_lines": ["We plotted for a week, and then we fed you the information", "A daughter that's eleven years old, I bet he takes it", "We thought about givin' a fake name or a destination", "But you so thirsty, you not concerned with investigation", "Instead you in that Venice studio, it's a celebration", "You gotta learn to fact-check things and be less impatient", "Your fans are rejoicin' thinkin' this is my expiration", "Even the picture you used, the jokes and the medication", "The Maybach glove and the drug he uses for less inflation", "Master manipulator, you bit on the speculation", "You dumb and reactive, nigga, I'm petty with dedication", "This about to get so depressin'", "It is what it is"], "meaning": "Kendrick Lamar’s May 2024 diss track “meet the grahams” saw him seemingly expose the fact that Drake has an abandoned 11-year-old daughter:\n\nDear baby girl\nI’m sorry that your father not active inside your world\n[…]\nShould be teachin' you time tables or watchin' Frozen with you\nOr at your eleventh birthday singin' poems with you\n\nThe original cover art for the track displayed a hodgepodge of items allegedly stolen from Dennis Graham, Drake’s father. Included in the cover art was the medical drug Ozempic, which helps with weight loss or “lessening inflation,” and a black glove with a Maybach logo on it. Kendrick mentioned the drug by name on “meet the grahams:”\n\nDon’t cut them corners like your daddy did, fuck what Ozempic did\n\n\n\nThe cover art for “meet the grahams.”\n\nHowever, Drake claims he intentionally leaked this false information to Lamar to make a fool out of him. It should be noted that Drake’s immediate reaction to “meet the grahams” was shown on an Instagram Story where he wrote:\n\nnahhhh hold on can someone find my hidden daughter pls and send her to me… these guys are in shambles 🤣🤣🤣\n\nDrake would double down on the denial moments later according to his affiliate DJ Akademiks, who claimed that Drake told him this:\n\nIf you or anybody can find proof that I have an 11-year-old daughter, I’ll quit rapping and I’ll come work for you.\n\nNow revealing his plan, Drake proudly wears the title of “master manipulator,” an insult Kendrick levied at him on April 2024’s “euphoria:”\n\nKnow you a master manipulator and habitual liar too", "annotation_id": "31652790", "url": "https://genius.com/31652790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 205, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
