{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Y’all don’t know the stamina it takes to run up the stats", "vault_lines": ["Y'all don't know the stamina it takes to run up the stats"], "meaning": "Drake is stating that those guys that want his first place have no idea how much stamina it takes to have his place and also statistics of streams and Billboard Hot 100 entries.\nhttps://www.billboard.com/music/chart-beat/drake-first-artist-400-hot-100-hits-1236256941/\n\nDrake even after releasing an album and then get on tour he would preview a new song.\nhttps://americansongwriter.com/drake-previews-new-song-with-up-and-coming-producer-bnyx/\n\nPeople always said he does not rest or go on vacation after dropping. He mentions this on Iceman too. Mostly because they believe if he does, artists will have the attention to push their music for billboard entries and also to fulfil their record labels obligations. Which often isn’t true as Drake has carved a niche that follows his music religiously as we saw even during his silence in 2024/2025 his old music made re-entries into Billboard 200 albums.", "annotation_id": "40412218", "url": "https://genius.com/40412218", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Fuck you artists and producers I don’t wanna hear excuses of your plans for future truces", "vault_lines": ["Fuck you artists and producers", "I don't wanna hear excuses of your plans for future truces"], "meaning": "Drake is making it clear to all the producers and artists who remained quiet when he was under attack in 2024.\n\nThose who were directly involved and those who chose to remain shut about it. He is making it know to them in advance that he does not want to hear anything from them justifying themselves. They should know where they stand with Drake without having to talk to Drake.", "annotation_id": "40353627", "url": "https://genius.com/40353627", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Even when under attack", "vault_lines": ["Even when under attack"], "meaning": "Drake is suggesting he running it back, breaking records and making new music even when he is under attack.\n\nHe pushes beyond boundaries during his peak and during his lows when he is under attack, he pushes beyond.\n\nIn 10/24/2025 (Drake’s Birthday) he shared diamond display pictures on his Instagram profile so as his squad for having 15 songs he contributed to going diamond.\n\nSide note: His album (Take Care) is legible for RIAA Diamond certification too.\n\nhttps://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/drake-diamond-riaa-39th-birthday-1236097599/", "annotation_id": "40353594", "url": "https://genius.com/40353594", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "They say, “What we doin’, Ice?” We runnin’ it back What has Iceman ever done other than that?", "vault_lines": ["And we back at it, back at it", "They say, \"What we doin', Ice?\" We runnin' it back", "What has Iceman ever done other than that?"], "meaning": "“running it back” is a term often used in sports, it means to come back next season and try again.\n\nFor people who were wondering what Drake’s next move was after the 2024 beef, Drake reassures them, he is running it back with more music, and another bid to run the summer.", "annotation_id": "40264805", "url": "https://genius.com/40264805", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I got shots about to fly at some of y’all heads again Man, shots about to fly Even though some of my friends still talk about you guys like they leanin’ towards your side", "vault_lines": ["I got shots about to fly at some of y'all heads again", "Man, shots about to fly", "Even though some of my friends still talk about you guys like they leanin' towards your side"], "meaning": "Drake implies that he still has more disses (shots) ready to be sent to more people, and that he isn’t finished with them after the release of ICEMAN, Maid Of Honour and Habibti. By using the word again, he is reminding everyone about the feud in 2024.\n\nAdditionally, Drake talks about how some people that are mutuals or are from his extended circles end up playing both sides. The friends in question are the people who almost seem to talk good about those that Drake has a problem with, and a preference to the others.", "annotation_id": "40116946", "url": "https://genius.com/40116946", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "You just do what you should’ve done since day one, you stick with your day ones", "vault_lines": ["You just do what you should've done since day one, you stick with your day ones"], "meaning": "In the aftermath of his 2024–2025 feud with Kendrick Lamar (and broader industry tensions), Drake reflects on misplaced loyalty and the value of his inner circle.\n\nRather than continuing to platform and elevate other high-profile artists — many of whom he’s given features, co-signs, or career boosts to (e.g., early support for Kendrick, The Weeknd, ASAP Rocky, Rick Ross, and others outside his core OVO crew) — Drake concludes he should’ve stuck to the principle he’s known for since the beginning: “stick with your day ones.”\n\n“Day ones” refers to his original, loyal Toronto/OVO affiliates who were with him before the fame and massive success. This is Drake doubling down on tightening his circle amid perceived betrayals and fake alliances exposed during the beef. It’s a moment of strategic regret and self-correction: focus on building his own empire with proven loyalists instead of spreading influence too widely.", "annotation_id": "39996625", "url": "https://genius.com/39996625", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Made a livin’ off of oh-no-he-didn’ts, bitch, yes I did", "vault_lines": ["Made a livin' off of oh-no-he-didn'ts, bitch, yes I did"], "meaning": "The “Ohno Hadid disease” refers to Lyme disease, which members of the Hadid family (including Bella and Yolanda) have famously battled. It is a tick-borne bacterial infection that causes a wide range of flu-like, musculoskeletal, and neurological symptoms.\n\nThis he later connects with J Cole’s bone to pick bar.", "annotation_id": "39944794", "url": "https://genius.com/39944794", "state": "pending", "votes": -6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Mistakin’ my sons for brothers, but it won’t happen again You better check your tone, I’m not one of y’all lil’ friends", "vault_lines": ["Mistakin' my sons for brothers, but it won't happen again", "You better check your tone, I'm not one of y'all lil' friends"], "meaning": "Drake regrets treating his peers as friends/brothers and is establishing that they are beneath him and have never been on his level.\n\nTo build on the “son” scheme, Drake invokes a black proverb “I’m not one of your little friends,” which parents use to shut down backtalk and demand respect from their children.", "annotation_id": "39932050", "url": "https://genius.com/39932050", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "They deep-seated resentment is what’s fillin’ up they seats", "vault_lines": ["They deep-seated resentment is what's fillin' up they seats"], "meaning": "The phrase “deep-seated” refers to a long-standing feeling that is deeply ingrained and difficult to change. Drake is saying that his adversaries and their fanbases are entirely driven by this resentment towards him.\n\nDrake ties the concept of a “deep-seated” emotion into literal arena/stadium seats at his adversaries’ concerts. He is saying that the massive crowd turnouts (seats) for his rivals aren’t a celebration of their own artistry, but a congregation fueled by a mutual desire to see him fall.\n\nThis references events such as Kendrick Lamar’s “Pop Out”, which was widely described by critics and fans as a collective movement to “dance on Drake’s grave.”", "annotation_id": "39931975", "url": "https://genius.com/39931975", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Two hours, Gstaad for some peace and serenity Then it’s back to Zurich for the OVO supremacy and the hotel amenities", "vault_lines": ["Two hours, Gstaad for some peace and serenity", "Then it's back to Zurich for the OVO supremacy and the hotel amenities"], "meaning": "The sequence “Gstaad for some peace and serenity, then it’s back to Zurich for the OVO supremacy” maps out Drake’s exact rhythm in Switzerland. Gstaad is one of the most exclusive ski resorts in the world, a village in the Swiss Alps where billionaires and royalty go to disappear. Two hours there is enough to decompress before re-entering the arena.\n\n\n\nZurich is Switzerland’s financial capital, the most important banking city in Europe and one of the wealthiest cities on the planet. Drake choosing it as the base for “OVO supremacy” is deliberate: this is where the real business happens, where money moves quietly and names carry weight. The two-hour styling session for peace and serenity before returning to Zurich for OVO supremacy captures the rhythm of his life: brief moments of calm before re-entering the arena of business dominance.\n\n\n\n“OVO supremacy” also carries a double meaning. OVO is Drake’s record label, clothing brand and lifestyle company, founded in 2012 under the name October’s Very Own. Asserting OVO supremacy in Zurich, one of the wealthiest cities on the planet, is Drake placing his brand at the pinnacle of both music and business at the same time.\n\n“Hotel amenities” is the final flex. When you operate at that level, the hotel isn’t just a room. It’s an extension of the brand, a statement about how you move. Drake isn’t mentioning room service. He’s talking about a standard of life that most people will never access.", "annotation_id": "39921683", "url": "https://genius.com/39921683", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "What does somebody do after doin’ everything you could’ve done for everyone?", "vault_lines": ["What does somebody do after doin' everything you could've done for everyone?"], "meaning": "Drake is likely echoing a similar emotional question from “Jungle”, where he asks:\n\nWhat am I supposed to do after we done everything that we done?\n\nIn both lyrics, he reflects on the emptiness and isolation that can come after giving everything to the people around him. On “Firm Friends,” the line shifts from a failed relationship to loyalty and friendship, questioning what is left for someone to do once they have already sacrificed and provided so much for everyone else.", "annotation_id": "39910507", "url": "https://genius.com/39910507", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I think karma’s takin’ too long, an eye for both of your eyes That’ll show you love is blind for real", "vault_lines": ["I think karma's takin' too long, an eye for both of your eyes", "That'll show you love is blind for real"], "meaning": "Karma usually defined as “what goes around comes around” is moving to slow for Drake in reference to the people that turned out to be fake friends. And a well known commandment in the mosaic law in the Bible is an eye for and eye. Meaning what you do to me I’ll do to you but Drake says he will take both eyes although his opponents only took one blinding them in the process .", "annotation_id": "39903849", "url": "https://genius.com/39903849", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Your friends never had to decide if they wanna stay The strength of your bond has never been tested in any way", "vault_lines": ["They'll never know they friends' decision, friends never had to decide", "Your friends never had to decide if they wanna stay", "The strength of your bond has never been tested in any way", "They'll never know they friends' decisions, friends never had to decide"], "meaning": "Once again Drake is emphasizing that most people have never been in a position where they’re friends loyalty was tested but he has.", "annotation_id": "39903800", "url": "https://genius.com/39903800", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "It explains why most people die not knowin’ who would’ve stuck by they side It doesn’t even cross they mind, they just go along for the ride They’ll never know they friends’ decision, friends never had to decide", "vault_lines": ["It explains why most people die not knowin' who would've stuck by they side", "It doesn't even cross they mind, they just go along for the ride", "They'll never know they friends' decision, friends never had to decide", "Your friends never had to decide if they wanna stay", "They'll never know they friends' decisions, friends never had to decide"], "meaning": "Drake is contrasting himself with most people who will never know who would’ve stuck by their side if the world turned on them. Whereas he does know because he’s been through that situation .", "annotation_id": "39903770", "url": "https://genius.com/39903770", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I don’t want no “Drake, my apologies, I’m trippin’, you’re top three” My group chat was bumpin’ those oppy guys, but not me", "vault_lines": ["I don't want no \"Drake, my apologies, I'm trippin', you're top three\"", "My group chat was bumpin' those oppy guys, but not me"], "meaning": "Here, Drake is addressing fans and/or friends he believes will try to downplay/backtrack on how they listened to and supported Kendrick Lamar and “Not Like Us” over the last two years.", "annotation_id": "39890546", "url": "https://genius.com/39890546", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Like beggin’ to get out a ticket, now they wanna cop pleas", "vault_lines": ["Like beggin' to get out a ticket, now they wanna cop pleas"], "meaning": "When begging to get out of a ticket, a driver might say “cop please” as the lyric suggests.\n\nThis serves as a double entendre, as the lyric could also be interpreted as saying to “cop” (meaning buy) a “plea” (deal), which allows a defendant to plead guilty or “no contest” in exchange for a guaranteed, usually lighter sentence or reduced charges in the context of America’s legal system.\n\nDrake likens buying (negotiating) a plea deal to how he believes the fans, and his friends, who listened to/supported Kendrick Lamar and “Not Like Us” will now try to listen to and support Drake after he released his new albums.", "annotation_id": "39890406", "url": "https://genius.com/39890406", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "From mass distribution to leadin’ my own version of the revolution Private arbitration leads to resolution", "vault_lines": ["From mass distribution to leadin' my own version of the revolution", "Private arbitration leads to resolution"], "meaning": "Drake reflects on his career and the progress he has made, hence his use of “mass distribution.” The phrase can refer to how far his music, image, and influence have spread globally, especially after becoming one of the biggest commercial artists in hip-hop (and in general)\n\n\nDrake's Diamond Certifications\n\n\n\n\nWhen he says he went from “mass distribution” to “leadin’ my own version of the revolution,” the line can be interpreted as Drake positioning himself against the industry system. This connects to his legal battle with UMG, where he accused the label, along with platforms such as Spotify, of helping to inflate the streams of Kendrick Lamar’s song “Not Like Us.” which is a disstrack towards drake. In that sense, Drake frames himself as challenging the same corporate machine that helped build his career.\n\nThis also ties into the larger narrative throughout ICEMAN, including songs like “Whisper My Name” and “Make Them Pay”, where Drake suggests the industry is working against him while backing Kendrick’s commercial success. Its also worth noting that the release of  ICEMAN and  the 2 other albums dropped has been speculated to have fulfilled his UMG deal, tying back into the theme of “revolution”.\n\nThis could also be read as a subtle diss toward Kendrick Lamar, who often speaks about social issues, Black struggle, and revolution in his music. Since Drake has previously mocked Kendrick’s introspective and activist toned lyrics, such as on “Family Matters”, he may be mocking kendrick in a sense. See A 2017 genius article on a history of kendrick and drake’s subliminal war, this is something happening for a while now.\n\nIt is also worth noting that during Kendrick’s Super Bowl performance, the phrase “the revolution is televised” appeared, creating another possible comparison between the two. Drake’s line may be responding to that imagery by presenting his own version of a revolution.\n\n\nKendrick Lamar’s Super Bowl LIX halftime show\n\n\n\n\nAlthough this may be a stretch, i thought it was worth noting :)\n\nFinally the line “private arbitration leads to resolution” refers to conflicts being settled privately, outside of public court battles. Drake may be implying that while the public focuses on controversy, lawsuits, and media spectacle, the real resolutions are happening quietly behind closed doors.", "annotation_id": "39877858", "url": "https://genius.com/39877858", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Reportin’ live from the scene where I wasn’t supposed to be revived by the EMTs, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Reportin' live from the scene where I wasn't supposed to be revived by the EMTs, yeah"], "meaning": "After just returning from a serious showdown between him and Kendrick, which critics say Kendrick lamar won , Drake continues the theme of ICEMAN here. He implies that despite the beef, which many say was the end of drakes career, The Boy still prevails\n\nFurthermore, an EMT, which stands for “Emergency Medical Technician,” is a job typically associated with performing life-saving procedures during emergencies. News stations are also commonly found reporting outside these emergency scenes. Drake flips this idea by portraying himself as the one reporting live from the scene where many claimed his career had died and could not be revived.\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: On Drake’s Iceman Zine there was a photo of a news broadcast photo taken by Barbara Alper that said\n\n “Despite the first casualties, it seems to be part of the game.”\n\nDrake is REPORTING from the CASUALTIE he wasn’t supposed to return from. but did", "annotation_id": "39877114", "url": "https://genius.com/39877114", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I did not break up a happy home, baby, you pulled up on me Tell ’em why you really hate me and what year did she date me", "vault_lines": ["I did not break up a happy home, baby, you pulled up on me", "Tell 'em why you really hate me and what year did she date me"], "meaning": "This is Drake leaning into the oldest flex in rap beef: I had her first.\n\n“What year did she date me” is aimed at A$AP Rocky, with Drake turning his past with Rihanna into the punchline. He is basically saying Rocky already knows why he hates him, and it has less to do with a “happy home” than jealousy over Rihanna’s history with Drake.\n\n“Pulled up on me” makes the diss more pointed, because Drake frames Rihanna as someone who came to him willingly. The whole bar plays like Drake taunting Rocky with the timeline: before the family, before the public relationship, there was already a Drake/Rihanna chapter.\n\n\n\nDrake and Rihanna were publicly linked through the 2009-2016 era, while Rihanna and Rocky became public years later in 2020.", "annotation_id": "39870156", "url": "https://genius.com/39870156", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Like it was my first Chanel, I could’ve posted receipts That would’ve made niggas read ’em and weep", "vault_lines": ["Like it was my first Chanel, I could've posted receipts", "That would've made niggas read 'em and weep"], "meaning": "Similar to his bar on “Make Them Pay”:\n\nI rack up a tab in Chanel ‘cause I do buy everything like I’m Middle Eastern\n\nDrake flaunts his wealth by spending extravagantly on high-end clothing and accessories for himself and those around him.\n\n“Posted receipts” is a double entendre. Drake refers to both posting physical Chanel purchase receipts online, and posting “receipts” in the form of screenshots or evidence used to expose somebody, ultimately making the exposed “read ‘em and weep”\n\nDrake’s love for Chanel runs deep, having released “CRYING IN CHANEL” off 2025’s $ome $exy $ongs 4 U.", "annotation_id": "39869442", "url": "https://genius.com/39869442", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Shout out Ref1", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Ref1” is the professional nickname for Ben Dorado, a French art director and graphic designer who serves as Drake’s creative director. He is the one behind the artwork and rollouts for several Drake projects, including 2020’s Dark Lane Demo Tapes, 2022’s Honestly, Nevermind and ICEMAN.\n\nDrake also name-drops Ref1 on the previous song, “Don’t Worry”:\n\nRef1, he so drunk, he just played Nicki then some Cardi, I’m sorry", "annotation_id": "39868113", "url": "https://genius.com/39868113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "But I get it, you all wanna be the main guy", "vault_lines": ["But I get it, you all wanna be the main guy", "You all wanna be the guy"], "meaning": "in this line Drake expresses understanding for all the people who betrayed him during the beef. In a way he outlines their motive to be ambition and how he was on the receiveing end of so much hate solely because he is the guy at the top.\n\nIt is similar to the line from the 2015 song – “ O to 100”\n\n“Imagine how I feel to watch another nigga at the top\nYou know that if it wasn’t you, you would be dissin' you, dawg”", "annotation_id": "39866956", "url": "https://genius.com/39866956", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I’ve been on the longest run, I don’t know how to walk away It’s baby steps for you boys, y’all walkin’ before y’all run", "vault_lines": ["I've been on the longest run, I don't know how to walk away", "It's baby steps for you boys, y'all walkin' before y'all run"], "meaning": "Drake is playing on words walk/run/steps.\n\nHe has been on the longest (run) streak of being on top of the music industry, his first hit record was Best I Ever Had (2009) so he is addicted and he doesnt know how to stop making music and disappear (walk away), his competitors on the other hand are barely getting started (baby steps) and they have to be patient and make a lot of hit records before they can even say they are on top of the rap game/on a hot streak (walk before they run).", "annotation_id": "39855589", "url": "https://genius.com/39855589", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I’m a product of where I’m from, one sound, one band The orders make they way through the proper chain of command And if everything goes to plan I will be makin’ the name change from The Boy to The Man But y’all just wouldn’t understand, damn", "vault_lines": ["I'm a product of where I'm from, one sound, one band", "The orders make they way through the proper chain of command", "And if everything goes to plan", "I will be makin' the name change from The Boy to The Man", "But y'all just wouldn't understand, damn"], "meaning": "“The Boy” is one of Drake’s many nicknames—for instance, he used it on his October 2023 track, “8am in Charlotte”:\n\nBreakin' news, they tried to kill him, but the boy prevails\n\nIn alignment with his album title, ICEMAN, the line symbolizes both Drake’s physical aging and the evolution of his identity. However, it’s also a call back to to Kendrick Lamar’s line on his April 2024 diss track, “euphoria”:\n\nI’m knowin' they call you The Boy, but where is the man? ‘Cause I ain’t seen him yet", "annotation_id": "39834198", "url": "https://genius.com/39834198", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I’ve seen GOATs act like sheeps, coworkers turn to enemies", "vault_lines": ["I've seen GOATs act like sheeps, coworkers turn to enemies"], "meaning": "This is a subtle shots sent towards J. Cole on Drake’s ICEMAN.\n\nIn the rap scene, J. Cole is considered by many as one of the Greatest Of All Time, a GOAT. Drake likens Cole to a sheep, an animal that is known for their flock mentality and running from what frightens them, claiming Cole acted sheepish in backing away from Drake and Kendrick’s beef\n\nDrake and J. Cole had supported each other for years prior to their falling out, combining on tracks from as early as J. Cole’s “In The Morning”, up until Drake’s “Evil Ways”.", "annotation_id": "39833849", "url": "https://genius.com/39833849", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Trust me, the owl’s glistenin’ more than it’s ever been And trust me, I’m the highest that I’ve ever been The greatest that they’ve ever seen", "vault_lines": ["Trust me, the owl's glistenin' more than it's ever been", "And trust me, I'm the highest that I've ever been", "The greatest that they've ever seen"], "meaning": "“The owl” is the face of Drake’s branding and his label, October’s Very Own. In nature, owls perch themselves high above their surroundings, scanning below for prey. Drake compares himself to the owl to emphasize that he is already a shining star, “glistening” at the top of the rap game, while continuing to progress and get better.\n\nDrake called himself “the greatest” in a similar fashion on the 2009 star-studded classic, “Forever”:\n\nLast name Ever, first name Greatest", "annotation_id": "39833786", "url": "https://genius.com/39833786", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I’m not the villain or the victim, I am the author My right hand guides the pen along the page until the chapter’s done", "vault_lines": ["I'm not the villain or the victim, I am the author", "My right hand guides the pen along the page until the chapter's done"], "meaning": "Drake claiming to be “the author” reflects a recurring theme throughout his music in which he tries to reclaim control over the narratives people generate about his fame, relationships, public feuds and public perception. Rather than allowing himself to be portrayed at either end of the good/bad — victim/villain spectrum, he chooses to be the one controlling his own story.\n\nDrake extends the writing metaphors, comparing his life to a book he is writing. This can be seen as every new album release/era of Drake is a chapter in the book of his life. He is writing his own story through his music.", "annotation_id": "39833540", "url": "https://genius.com/39833540", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "And we back at it, back at it", "vault_lines": ["And we back at it, back at it", "They say, \"What we doin', Ice?\" We runnin' it back"], "meaning": "“Firm Friends” marks the fourth collaboration between Drake and Conductor Williams. They first linked up on Drake’s October 2023 single, “8AM In Charlotte,” a month later on “Stories About My Brother” and on January 2025’s “Fighting Irish Freestyle.”", "annotation_id": "39829323", "url": "https://genius.com/39829323", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Skeletons in your closet, well, trust this ain’t the bone to pick", "vault_lines": ["Skeletons in your closet, well, trust this ain't the bone to pick"], "meaning": "Drake appears to be flipping J. Cole’s “Two Six” bar “Caught a lot of bodies, so my closet, it got skeletons Don’t believe me? Call your favorite rapper for the evidence”.\n\nCole raps that his closet has “skeletons” because he has “caught a lot of bodies,” meaning he has lyrically killed other rappers. Drake turns that imagery back on Cole with “this ain’t the bone to pick.” Since the skeletons in Cole’s closet represent dead rappers, Drake is saying he is not one of those “bones” Cole can claim. The phrase also plays on “bone to pick,” meaning a conflict or issue with someone, suggesting Cole chose the wrong opponent or wrong battle.", "annotation_id": "39828948", "url": "https://genius.com/39828948", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I know niggas goin’ crazy as I cop all the items you niggas put up for sale And start a museum in my house called “Try Someone Else”", "vault_lines": ["I know niggas goin' crazy as I cop all the items you niggas put up for sale", "And start a museum in my house called \"Try Someone Else\""], "meaning": "Drake often buys jewelry and souvenirs that other popular artists have owned in the past. For example, in 2023, he bought a gold, ruby, and diamond crown ring that 2Pac wore in one of his final public appearances for $1 million at a Sotheby’s auction.\n\n\n\nAlso, Drake is seen wearing Michael Jackson’s signature Swarovski crystal-studded glove in the album cover for ICEMAN—he reportedly bought it for $120,000.", "annotation_id": "39819384", "url": "https://genius.com/39819384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Right now, me and Conductor, we got more fuckin’ chemistry than MIT", "vault_lines": ["Right now, me and Conductor, we got more fuckin' chemistry than MIT"], "meaning": "Conductor Williams is the producer behind this track. Their chemistry is well documented as Conductor previously produced “8AM In Charlotte” and “Stories About My Brother” from For All The Dogs.\n\nMIT — the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, is one of the world’s best institutions for chemistry and science, making it the gold standard for the comparison Drake is making.\n\nFor Drake to boast about their chemistry, he might be hinting that they have worked extensively lately which might be supportive of the rumors that an EP exclusively produced by Conductor will be dropping soon.\n\nBack in 2025, Conductor himself confirmed “there’s more” music with Drake, though he added “I don’t know if y'all are ever going to hear it.”", "annotation_id": "39819137", "url": "https://genius.com/39819137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Beat the game so many times, this shit has got abusive", "vault_lines": ["Beat the game so many times, this shit has got abusive"], "meaning": "Drake’s run is definitely something to behold, he has stood as an era-defining artist for the better of two decades, so much that the relationship between him and the music industry has taken on the dynamics of an abusive relationship.\n\nThis is reminiscent of his lyrics off “8am in Charlotte”:\n\nThings get kinky after fifteen years of dominance", "annotation_id": "39817107", "url": "https://genius.com/39817107", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Strong opinions from the weak for four hundred-somethin’ weeks", "vault_lines": ["Strong opinions from the weak for four hundred-somethin' weeks"], "meaning": "Four hundred-something weeks is roughly  about eightyears, which takes us back onto the period spanning from the Scorpion era in 2018 to ICEMAN in 2026. Almost a decade of sustained commentary from observers and media personalities who never missed an opportunity to shit on him/his musical output. It’s interesting that both Scorpion and ICEMAN arrived as comeback releases after beefs that left Drake on the wrong end of public perception.\n\nAlso note the contrast between “strong opinions” and “weak” and how “weak” is a homophone for “week.”", "annotation_id": "39816913", "url": "https://genius.com/39816913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "They try to have all of my precious memories Layin’ in my girl’s lap like she Jackie Onassis and I’m John Kennedy But I am not Jonathan Kennedy Rest in peace, but I really can’t bloody up the triple-button Giovanni suit because I’m OCD", "vault_lines": ["They try to have all of my precious memories", "Layin' in my girl's lap like she Jackie Onassis and I'm John Kennedy", "But I am not Jonathan Kennedy", "Rest in peace, but I really can't bloody up the triple-button Giovanni suit because I'm OCD"], "meaning": "Jacqueline Lee “Jackie” Kennedy Onassis was the first lady of the United States from 1961 to 1963, as the wife of John F. Kennedy, the 35th president of the United States.\n\nDrake refers to JFK’s 1963 assassination in Dallas, Texas. During the chaos, the President’s body fell into his wife’s lap in the limousine, with First Lady Jackie Kennedy desperately holding parts of his exposed skull and brain in her hands and lap in an attempt to keep him together.\n\n\n\nDrake wants to avoid this situation because his Obsessive Compulsive Disorder (OCD) means he cannot stand the thought of staining his suit from Giovanni Clothes Inc., a legendary, family-owned bespoke tailoring atelier based on St-Laurent Boulevard in Montreal, Canada.", "annotation_id": "39811463", "url": "https://genius.com/39811463", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "What would they do to me if they ever caught me ridin’ dolo? Would they take my chain and my watch or would they take my photo?", "vault_lines": ["What would they do to me if they ever caught me ridin' dolo?", "Would they take my chain and my watch or would they take my photo?"], "meaning": "Drake is posing a genuine question about his vulnerability, what actually happens if he’s caught outside alone with no security or entourage? Does someone approach him for a photo, starstruck? Or does someone try to take the chain off his neck?\n\nThe speculation isn’t hypothetical. In May 2009, Drake and a date had just left a Toronto restaurant when two armed men approached his SUV and robbed him at gunpoint, taking $2,000 in cash, an Audemars Piguet watch, and a diamond chain Lil Wayne had gifted him. Drake was convinced it was an inside job, telling GQ: “I knew it was a setup, because I had on a sweater and a jacket but when they banged on the car window with a gun and opened the door, the first thing he said was, ‘Yo, run that chain.’”\n\nHe addressed the incident on “From Florida With Love”, describing it as something that changed how he moves:\n\nWeezy played that shit for me and Kobe on the bus\nWent and got a chain for me, I had to give it up (Yeah)\nNiggas had they pistols loaded pointed at my truck, ayy\nAnd you know that lesson stuck (Right)\nFrom that day I never touched the road without a (Plugg)\nAyy, from that day I never saw the point in talkin' tough (Nah)\nHasn’t happened since, I guess you niggas know what’s up (Yup)t up", "annotation_id": "39797790", "url": "https://genius.com/39797790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "You think I didn’t see that you stayed silent That’s like you endorsing it all, may as well stand behind him I stood beside you, thought there was some good inside you Situations like these, they force you to notice the difference", "vault_lines": ["You think I didn't see that you stayed silent", "That's like you endorsing it all, may as well stand behind him", "I stood beside you, thought there was some good inside you", "Situations like these, they force you to notice the difference"], "meaning": "Feeling betrayed, not so much by the people who openly sided with Kendrick Lamar, but by the ones who said nothing during the 2024 feud when he was getting dismantled publicly.\n\nThe people in his crosshairs are those whose silence hit differently because of how close they were. These were people Drake had stood beside both professionally and personally, so their silence amounts to the same thing as standing with the other side.\n\nJ. Cole is the most loaded case. Cole initially released “7 Minute Drill” as a Kendrick Lamar diss response, only to publicly apologize and withdraw from the beef entirely, a move widely mocked as spineless and one that Drake felt as a direct abandonment at the worst possible moment.\n\nDJ Khaled, one of Drake’s closest and most visible collaborators, went conspicuously quiet throughout, notable for a man whose entire brand is built on being the loudest person in the room.", "annotation_id": "39796526", "url": "https://genius.com/39796526", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "It’s so many opportunities, but we still here by ourselves It’s been nearly fourteen years, he ain’t work with no one else", "vault_lines": ["It's so many opportunities, but we still here by ourselves", "It's been nearly fourteen years, he ain't work with no one else"], "meaning": "Noel Cadastre and Drake first began working together on Drake’s debut studio album Thank Me Later in 2010. Since then Noel has been credited on over a 100 of Drake’s songs, including hits such as God’s Plan and Hotline Bling.", "annotation_id": "39795725", "url": "https://genius.com/39795725", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Look at me and Neeks for instance We know we live different lives and we’ve been on our separate missions Look at how time flies, twenty years passed, he’s still the same guy", "vault_lines": ["And we back at it, back at it", "Look at how time flies, twenty yеars passed, he's still the samе guy", "Look at me and Neeks for instance", "We know we live different lives and we've been on our separate missions"], "meaning": "Neeks is the nickname of Drake’s long-time friend Nicholas Carino. He’s a co-founder of OVO Sound—he serves as the brand’s marketing manager and is responsible for running OVO’s day-to-day operations. He has worked with Drake going back to his So Far Gone days, where he managed day-to-day logistics, travel schedules, and on-the-road demands.", "annotation_id": "39795695", "url": "https://genius.com/39795695", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "And y’all are talkin’ ‘bout inflatin’ the numbers on DSPs And me havin’ better perspective on life doin’ DMT", "vault_lines": ["And y'all are talkin' 'bout inflatin' the numbers on DSPs", "And me havin' better perspective on life doin' DMT"], "meaning": "Drake is responding to lines and insinuations by Kendrick Lamar on his song ‘Meet the Grahams’ where he raps:\n\n“Therapy’s a lovely start, but I suggest some ayahuasca, strip the ego from the bottom”.\n\nWhile DMT (dimethyltryptamine) and ayahuasca are not the same, they possess many of the same psychedelic traits and characteristics, one closely resembling the other.", "annotation_id": "39779608", "url": "https://genius.com/39779608", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "(Con-Con—)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Denzel “Conductor” Williams is a producer from Kansas City, Missouri. He is best known for his work with various artists from the Griselda label. This is one of Williams’ producer tags — “Conductor, we have a problem” — however, in this case it is chopped up. The original sound comes from a 2015 meme.\n\nDrake and Conductor first collaborated on “8am in Charlotte” off of “Drake’s For All The Dogs.\n\nBack in 2024, It was also rumored that Drake might be releasing an album solely produced by Conductor Williams. These rumors gained some more momentum after the release of ICEMAN.", "annotation_id": "39778067", "url": "https://genius.com/39778067", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "My numbers are humblin’ facts", "vault_lines": ["My numbers are humblin' facts"], "meaning": "Drake is the most streamed artist on Spotify, and has been one of the most famous rappers at the 2010s. Also a subtle reminder to other rappers to “be humble” and “sit down”.", "annotation_id": "39778065", "url": "https://genius.com/39778065", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "CTE from tryin’ me and still they want another hit", "vault_lines": ["CTE from tryin' me and still they want another hit"], "meaning": "CTE is a neurodegenerative disease that is usually found in people that play contact sports and is caused from repetitive blows to the head.", "annotation_id": "39776711", "url": "https://genius.com/39776711", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "My son is takin’ corner kicks in front of Luis Enrique at PSG", "vault_lines": ["My son is takin' corner kicks in front of Luis Enrique at PSG"], "meaning": "Luis Enrique is the current manager of Paris Saint-Germain. Drake is flexing that his son is taking corner kicks in front of one of Europe’s elite coaches, at one of the world’s biggest clubs.\n\n\n\nAdonis — Drake’s 8-year-old son with French artist Sophie Brussaux, is enrolled in the PSG Academy, splitting his time between Paris with his mother and Toronto with Drake.\n\nhttps://s.yimg.com/ny/api/res/1.2/gjPKaRBn0S7CFrTifAeZyg--/YXBwaWQ9aGlnaGxhbmRlcjt3PTk2MDtoPTY0MDtjZj13ZWJw/https://media.zenfs.com/en/people_218/177c1f9b20df51cb2670dc221e2e16bd Sophie and Adonis pictured with PSG superstar Kylian Mbappe\n\nSoccer is clearly his sport of choice, and Drake has been showing up for it. He was spotted on the sidelines with Sophie as Adonis warmed up on the field in June 2024, and again going viral in October 2025 after footage of him casually joining a basketball layup in the schoolyard of Adonis’s Toronto elementary school circulated online.", "annotation_id": "39776644", "url": "https://genius.com/39776644", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Fuck that, I’m in the stu’ with Noel, the sun went down, we couldn’t tell", "vault_lines": ["Fuck that, I'm in the stu' with Noel, the sun went down, we couldn't tell"], "meaning": "Noel Cadastre, often referred to as OVO Noel, is a key sound engineer, producer, and integral member of Drake’s OVO (October’s Very Own) crew.", "annotation_id": "39776640", "url": "https://genius.com/39776640", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "I am so far gone that a thank-me-later is useless now", "vault_lines": ["I am so far gone that a thank-me-later is useless now"], "meaning": "Drake pays homage to his previous albums So Far Gone and Thank Me Later, suggesting he’s too far removed to be thanked.", "annotation_id": "39776637", "url": "https://genius.com/39776637", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Damn, I got enemies, got a lot of enemies Lot of niggas tryna drain me of my energy", "vault_lines": ["Damn, I got enemies, got a lot of enemies", "Lot of niggas tryna drain me of my energy"], "meaning": "Drake interpolates his 2015 track “Energy”:\n\nI got enemies, got a lot of enemies\nGot a lot of people tryna drain me of my energy", "annotation_id": "39776548", "url": "https://genius.com/39776548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Firm Friends", "line": "Nigga, KYS ASAP, that’s some shit that you could do for me", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, KYS ASAP, that's some shit that you could do for me"], "meaning": "KYS ASAP = Kill-Your-Self-As-Soon-As-Possible.\n\nGiven Drake’s well-documented tension with A$AP Rocky over Rihanna, the placement feels like a subliminal tucked inside an insult. Rather than naming Rocky outright, Drake potentially buried the callout inside the acronym itself.\n\nDrake already addresses Rocky explicitly on “Burning Bridges,” the eighth track on the album.", "annotation_id": "39775849", "url": "https://genius.com/39775849", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
