{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Special", "line": "And pass me, classy, something like Drew Lord", "vault_lines": ["And pass me, classy, something like Drew Lord"], "meaning": "Drake makes a basketball reference about Eagle Rock coach Drew Lord. He wants to be passed, like the basketball move of giving a teammate the ball, like how Lord was temporarily on leeway because of a basketball feud between two coaches and was being passed over by a substitute.\n\n\n\nDrake wants people to pass over him and not be noticed, which is cohesive to the songs message.", "annotation_id": "1076088", "url": "https://genius.com/1076088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Special", "line": "And since Drake done took it from curls to waves Its been kind of like a whirlwind phase", "vault_lines": ["And since Drake done took it from curls to waves", "Its been kind of like a whirlwind phase"], "meaning": "We all remember Drakes curly hair in Degrassi right?\n\n\n\nHis hair isn’t the only thing that has changed. Since then, he’s acquired money, fame, and is surrounded by beautiful women. You could almost say that nothing was the same.", "annotation_id": "220637", "url": "https://genius.com/220637", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Special", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 21, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-special-lyrics", "genius_title": "Special"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "You niggas move too cold", "vault_lines": ["You niggas move too cold"], "meaning": "He’s saying “you niggas move Tuco”. Not “too cold”. He’s saying y’all niggas move crazy, with no reasoning- and you guys don’t make sense.\n\nIt’s a ‘Breaking Bad’ reference about the character Tuco Salamanca (he also appears in Better Call Saul) from the show(s).\n\nTuco was a psychopathic cartel kingpin, who was always snorting crystal meth. He’s best know known for random drug induced rage-filled outbursts (Kanye diss). He’s always snorting meth off of Bowie knives, frequently yelling and beating his friends and others to death for absolutely no reason.\n\nIf you pay attention to the Spanish references, he’s making throughout the whole first verse, it makes perfect sense. Tuco was also known to wear the infamous steel toe Mexican cowboy boots, (which drizzy refers to in another line in this sequence, in a separate double entendre “…and I got the flows,  niggas steal(steel) like boot toes,)…", "annotation_id": "29622157", "url": "https://genius.com/29622157", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "There’s a mad shortage of people givin’ me kudos", "vault_lines": ["Thеre's a mad shortage of peoplе givin' me kudos"], "meaning": "when Drake says “There’s a mad shortage of people givin’ me kudos,” he’s essentially stating that he feels there’s a lack of recognition or appreciation for his work, achievements, or efforts from others. Given Drake’s stature and success, this could be seen as an expression of frustration about the constant scrutiny celebrities face, where their achievements might sometimes be overshadowed by criticism or they might feel underappreciated despite their contributions.", "annotation_id": "29417496", "url": "https://genius.com/29417496", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "I wish everyone could tell me exactly what they need from me", "vault_lines": ["I wish everyone could tell me exactly what they need from me"], "meaning": "Drake wishes that people in his life were more direct about their needs or intentions, so he could better meet their expectations or avoid misunderstandings.", "annotation_id": "29417486", "url": "https://genius.com/29417486", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Yeah (Gold Squad)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is repping the Berry Academy of Technology’s gold squad cheerleaders, a Charlotte-area high school cheerleaders group which he samples for the beat of the song.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q2BY9AeO4a4", "annotation_id": "27043725", "url": "https://genius.com/27043725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Only tee you’re getting from me is one for the walk of shame", "vault_lines": ["Only tee you're getting from me is one for the walk of shame"], "meaning": "This line plays off of the previous “tryna get the earl grey on me”. The “earl grey” being a tea which is a pun for T which is slang for truth. Drake used the previous line to set up this one, once again using the T homophone but this time referencing Tee-shirts.\n\nHe is simply saying the only T you are getting from me is the one I give you to go home in.\n\nThis use of a clothing tee is also followed up on with the next line saying “heart on my sleeve” as well as a few lines later, Drake raps “have somebody put you on a Gildan” which is a Canadian owned clothing company mostly supplying Tee’s as clothing blanks for people to print on.", "annotation_id": "26312843", "url": "https://genius.com/26312843", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "[Outro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In 2011, at the beginning of Drakes hiatus, he featured on DJ Khaled’s ‘I’m On One’. At the end of his verse he rapped the lyrics:\n\n‘I’m just feeling like the throne is for the taking, watch me take it’.\n\nIn this present moment, it is quite clear Drake took the throne and that his hiatus is yet to slow down. Drake now ends his verse on ‘7AM on Bridle Path’ with the lyrics:\n\n‘they used to tell me back in the day “Boy, you’re going places”,\nNever understood where I could really take it-‘.\n\nEnding the verse with the same cadence used on ‘I’m On One’, without saying ‘watch me take it’, allures to the fact that Drake knows himself he took the throne.", "annotation_id": "25908511", "url": "https://genius.com/25908511", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Maybe I’m gettin’ too stoned", "vault_lines": ["Maybe I'm gettin' too stoned"], "meaning": "According to a 2017 Vice article, “It’s no secret that Drake sucks at smoking weed.” He allegedly got so stoned in Amsterdam that he was incapable of performing (although Drake claimed it was due to food poisoning).\n\nDrake smoked onstage for the first time with famed MJ aficionado Wiz Khalifa in 2014. He’s also said in a Jimmy Fallon interview that he doesn’t like performing while high, so that appears to be more of a one-off.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VEJvCBigYyI\n\nDrake has also had a cannabis company in the works for some time now. In 2019, Drake partnered with Canopy Growth to launch his own cannabis brand. The future of his More Life Growth Company is now somewhat uncertain; in June 2021, it was announced that Drake’s business relationship with Canopy Growth had dissolved.", "annotation_id": "23928313", "url": "https://genius.com/23928313", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "My losing nights are still a vibe That’s the things I accept", "vault_lines": ["My losing nights are still a vibe", "That's the things I accept"], "meaning": "This line continues the metaphor from the previous lines regarding betting large amounts of money at a casino. It is also a double entendre. “losing nights” are “still a vibe” in two ways for Drake.\n\nWhen he is at the casino betting money and ends the night in the negative, he was still having a lot of fun with his buddies hanging out playing a fun game. He is so rich that throwing down, say, $300K is a fucking game to him. This plays to a line from Drake’s song “Omertà”:\n\n“Nevada gaming commission in a frenzy\nHow much money can this casino lend me?\nRippin' markers up over shots of the Henny”\n\nHere Drake implies that he is in a position where he can use the casino’s money to play, and take Hennessey shots (that the casino is most likely also paying for).\n\nThe second meaning to this double entendre refers to Drake’s attitude towards life in general. Everyone, no matter how successful, or unsuccessful, takes L’s and W’s. Drake expresses that even when he takes an L, the night is “still a vibe”. Some hardcore mental health advice right there. Take a page out his book yo, and you might just be living better afterwards.", "annotation_id": "23920883", "url": "https://genius.com/23920883", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Heart is on my sleeve and my body is in the hall of fame High fashion and my life is full of climaxes", "vault_lines": ["Heart is on my sleeve and my body is in the hall of fame", "High fashion and my life is full of climaxes"], "meaning": "Following describing a “walk of shame,” Drake’s making a phonetic play on the common euphemism of wearing his “heart on” his “sleeve;” suggesting that “the artisan must leave” the metaphorical “hall of fame” containing either musician’s “body” of work.\n\nWhilst an artisan is often defined as someone exceptionally skilled with their hands; it’s more specifically associated with people who create hand-woven clothing/goods for a living. This is a thinly veiled compliment/insult directed toward Kanye and his role within the fashion industry.\n\nFurthering the idea of a “walk of shame,” it’s also possible to discern the word “shun” (“fashion”) here, building upon the idea that Drake is ensuring Kanye’s exodus, subliminally responding to Pusha T’s historical diss “Exodus 23:1” in a reciprocally covert manner.\n\nThat’s why your people not believers, they all leavin' ya\n\nDrake’s strangely specific use of the word “climaxes” makes this link even clearer, going as far as describing the exodus of Kanye’s followers later on in the track.\n\n“Climax” is often considered to be synonymous with orgasm (a word that would have been easier to fit within this rhyme scheme) and Drake’s suggesting that he “fashions” things in a sexual/musical context in his role as an artisan.\n\nIntense moments of change are considered to be “climaxes” and Drake’s claiming that he copes with them more stoically than his competitors – either that or that “[he] fortunes” (“high-fashion”) and that his life is “full of” high points.", "annotation_id": "23895313", "url": "https://genius.com/23895313", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "You niggas hot to them little kids, you ain’t famous to me", "vault_lines": ["You niggas hot to them little kids, you ain't famous to me"], "meaning": "Drake’s making a play on words oriented around the process of ignition here, with it being possible to discern “gas” (“niggas”) and “lit” (“little”) surrounding the term “hot.”\n\n“Gas,” “hot” and then “lit,” are presented in an order that chronologically describes the process of lighting a fire; likely referencing Kanye West’s highly publicised act of self-immolition prior to the release of DONDA.\n\nConsidering how Kanye garners media coverage in all manner of public spectacles like these, Drake’s alleging him as being “infamous” phonetically; i.e. “ain’t-famous.”", "annotation_id": "23894300", "url": "https://genius.com/23894300", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "In the LaFerrari, my emotions racin’", "vault_lines": ["In the LaFerrari, my emotions racin'"], "meaning": "Drake used to be the owner of a Ferrari LaFerrari. The LaFerrari was the first hybrid hypercar produced by the Maranello, Italy-based manufacturer. The car has a V12 engine that produces 950 horsepower. Drake’s LaFerrari was painted in Giallo Modena, a shade of yellow, and had a Nero (black) Leather interior. In July 2019, Drake sold his LaFerrari, but he clearly has fond memories of the car, made clear by referencing it in his music. In mid-2020, Drake’s LaFerrari was purchased by Colombian singer J Balvin.", "annotation_id": "23893147", "url": "https://genius.com/23893147", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Far as the Drake era, man, we in the golden ages", "vault_lines": ["Far as the Drake era, man, we in the golden ages"], "meaning": "Drake is directly responding to Charlamagne Tha God, who stated on his “Brilliant Idiots” podcast that he was not looking forward to Drake’s CLB album and that he believes the “Drake Era” has been over for about 2-3 years and that radio hadn’t caught up to current music trends.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/eta2yv_z4ew?t=2869\n\nCharlamagne doubled down on his comments on The Breakfast Club,  claiming that Drake now needs a Lil Baby feature more than Lil Baby needs Drake.  On another episode of Brilliant Idiots, Charlamagne stated that “Toosie Slide,” the lead single from Dark Lane Demo Tapes, was “wack” and beneath Drake.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/lgoMv8b4et4?t=6211\n\nThese comments came amidst, DLDT becoming the first commercial release by Drake, since his debut album, not to debut at number one on Billboard. On “The Remorse,” Drake raps about the difficulty trying to stay focused and ignore the naysayers like Charlamagne who say his run is over. Although Drake admits “they even had my ass convinced a couple times.”", "annotation_id": "23891297", "url": "https://genius.com/23891297", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Somehow I’m the greatest in the game, to my own amazement", "vault_lines": ["Somehow I'm the greatest in the game, to my own amazement"], "meaning": "Drake has been a mainstay in the rap game for over a decade, maintaining relevance and consistently coming out with hits. It’s no surprise given his career that Drake was the first artist to hit 50 billion Spotify streams, that he’s the most-streamed artist in Spotify history, or that Certified Lover Boy broke the all-time record for single-day streams. Among his plethora of records includes the most top-10 singles in Billboard chart history.", "annotation_id": "23889276", "url": "https://genius.com/23889276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "If we talkin’ top three, then you been slidin’ to third like stolen bases", "vault_lines": ["If we talkin' top three, then you been slidin' to third like stolen bases"], "meaning": "In baseball, when you steal base (and especially third base specifically), you are likely to drive the final leap into a jump and then slide towards the base. The metaphorical meaning is that Kanye is on a decline, but since he never was as great as Drake is, he couldn’t have started from higher than 2nd place, and he’s only gotten worse since, hence sliding down.\n\nHere’s the all-time stolen base leader, Rickey Henderson – sliding – stealing a third base:", "annotation_id": "23886269", "url": "https://genius.com/23886269", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "See, they gettin’ loose with the lyin’ like open cages", "vault_lines": ["See, they gettin' loose with the lyin' like open cages"], "meaning": "Wordplay: “line” vs. “lion” on the loose once it’s let out of a cage.", "annotation_id": "23885573", "url": "https://genius.com/23885573", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "See the watch collection and assume I got time ’Cause of the ruby rose two-tone", "vault_lines": ["See the watch collection and assume I got time", "'Cause of the ruby rose two-tone"], "meaning": "Drake has a vast watch collection, so it’s easy to assume he’s talking about a two-tone watch; a type of watch made out of two different materials, and thus in different colors. This line may also be a double entendre, referencing actress Ruby Rose, who played a prison inmate in the popular Netflix series Orange Is The New Black, where she was quite literally “doing time.”\n\nUpon Certified Lover Boy’s release, rapper Rubi Rose, believed the line to be areference to her, which Drake has not publicly confirmed or denied. Thus, by name dropping Rubi, this line is potentially a triple entendre, as she has an Eritrean mother and a Black American and Japanese father; her skin color is quite literally “two-tone.” This wouldn’t be a stretch as Drake has shown love to Rubi before, snapping a photo with her in 2018 and singing along to her 2019 song “Big Mouth” on his Instagram story in January 2020.", "annotation_id": "23885091", "url": "https://genius.com/23885091", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "The first second they speak to me, I’m not with all the secrecy Secretly beefin’ me behind closed doors", "vault_lines": ["The first second they speak to me, I'm not with all the secrecy", "Secretly beefin' me behind closed doors"], "meaning": "Drake believed that he had squashed his beef with Kanye when he was invited to his Wyoming sessions. Despite this, Kanye proceeded to do things that Drake felt were shady, especially related to Pusha T’s “The Story of Addidon” and Ye’s “Lift Yourself”. Drizzy feels like he was deceived because the conflict continued between the two rappers while he thought that they had made up.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0-tAK7yxZ_c&ab_channel=TAlmighty", "annotation_id": "23885043", "url": "https://genius.com/23885043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Why you over there tryna impress the nation?", "vault_lines": ["Why you over there tryna impress the nation?"], "meaning": "Refers to when Kanye was running for President in 2020, trying to impress the nation for votes.", "annotation_id": "23884870", "url": "https://genius.com/23884870", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "And my wishes come true without blowin’ eyelashes", "vault_lines": ["And my wishes come true without blowin' eyelashes"], "meaning": "when someone’s eyelash gets stuck people say a wish and blow it off there’s a couple traditions to this one stems from the belief that the devil tries to collect as much human hair as possible to gain power over the humans. So, when you let go of a stray lash, you don’t allow the devil to gain power over you.\n\nAnother one is people believed that when you lose an eyelash, you need to place it on the back of your hand. Then blow the eyelash if it falls over smoothly, his or her wish just might come true. According to these folklores, if the stray lash gets stuck anywhere, apparently, the wish won’t come true.\n\nDrake was stating he doesn’t need all these folklores for his fame & success he manifested his own destiny", "annotation_id": "23884632", "url": "https://genius.com/23884632", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Lettin’ me take the rap for that Casper the Ghost shit While you findin’ all of the loopholes", "vault_lines": ["Lettin' me take the rap for that Casper the Ghost shit", "While you findin' all of the loopholes"], "meaning": "In these lines, Drake continues to address his tumultuous relationship with Kanye West.\n\nDrake has helped write songs for Kanye over the years, notably receiving writing credits on Ye’s 2016 song “30 Hours” and 2018’s “Yikes,” yet nobody seems to have paid attention. Meanwhile, Drake has been critisized for his use of ghostwriters, most notably by Meek Mill during their 2015 feud.\n\nDrake feels that, despite Kanye using writers, nobody is calling him out due to the “loopholes,” whatever they are, that he uses to hide them.\n\nWhen it comes to ghostwriters, the main difference between Drake and Kanye is that, while Drake tried to hide his use of ghostwriters, such as Quentin Miller during the recording of his 2015 album If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, Kanye openly admits to collaborating with others during his creative process.\n\nFurther, the inclusion of “ghost” imagery is likely in reference to Kanye’s 2021 track “Ok Ok,” in which Ye referenced jabs made by Drake on his 2018 songs “Behind Barz” and “Duppy Freestyle”:\n\nSee me in person, I look like a ghost (Grr)\nSee me in person, I look like a ghost (Bow)", "annotation_id": "23884187", "url": "https://genius.com/23884187", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Hundred thousand on the line", "vault_lines": ["Hundred thousand on the line"], "meaning": "Following bars describing “passin' time” and “fishbowl wineglasses,” Drake’s incorporating some phonetic wordplay; referencing using an hourglass to observe the “passin' of time.”\n\nWith “sand” and “hour” being audible within this bar, it fits in with the context of those coming prior.\n\nHe’s also remarking upon the subliminal nature of the shots he’s sending, likening them to “underhand throws on the line.”\n\nThis is applicable in a sporting sense, considering he’s hanging with LeBron James.\n\nDrake could be dismissing Kanye leaking his home address “online” as something trivial here too, suggesting that it was his “hundredth house” at stake – i.e. “on the line.”", "annotation_id": "23883455", "url": "https://genius.com/23883455", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "They sing them shits like folk songs Kumbaya, boom-ba-ye", "vault_lines": ["They sing them shits like folk songs"], "meaning": "“Boom-ba-ye” is meant as a shot (boom) at Kanye West while at the same time saying bye to him (bye Ye).\n\nDrake alludes to “bomaye” which means “kill him” in the Bantu language. “Ali, bomaye” was chanted by Muhammed Ali’s fans in Zaire at his infamous 1974 boxing match, the so-called “Rumble in the Jungle,” demanding him to kill his opponent George Foreman.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/KXh7RimHE54?t=23\n\nThis line is Drake’s way of saying he’s surpassed Kanye in popularity and that Kanye’s time has passed. Drake is comparing his song’s impact to folk songs like the African-American spiritual “Kumbaya.”", "annotation_id": "23883218", "url": "https://genius.com/23883218", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "They used to tell me back in the day, “Boy, you’re going places” Never understood where I could really take it", "vault_lines": ["They used to tell me back in the day, \"Boy, you're going places\"", "Never understood where I could really take it"], "meaning": "Drake’s describing those that claimed to “back” him prior to his ascent to fame, suggesting they didn’t truly believe his aspirations would garner such grandeur.\n\nNiggas textin' “Bro,” but we are not of no close relation\n\nHe’s also making use of some phonetic word-play here, suggesting “they used to tail” (“tell”) him “back in the day” with the thought: “Boy, you’re going places.” The physical link between “back” and “tail” justifies this to some extent.\n\nReflecting upon “back in the day,”  Drake’s ironically stating that he’s always been accustomed to living for the moment; “never understanding” existing beyond the present.", "annotation_id": "23883029", "url": "https://genius.com/23883029", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "I was down to give these niggas credit, but hold the payment", "vault_lines": ["I was down to give these niggas credit, but hold the payment"], "meaning": "These bars are most likely aimed at Kanye and Pusha T.\n\nEarly in Drake’s career, he expressed admiration for the G.O.O.D. Music rappers, citing them as two of his biggest inspirations. In a 2009 interview with Peter Rosenberg, he cited Kanye’s Graduation and the Clipse’s Lord Willin' as two of his favourite albums.\n\nWhile Drake had no problem paying his dues to those who inspired him, after being publicly disrespected by Kanye and Pusha multiple times, he is no longer prepared to give them “credit,” using the monetary definition of the word as a metaphor to describe his disappointment.", "annotation_id": "23881179", "url": "https://genius.com/23881179", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Niggas textin’ “Bro,” but we are not of no close relation", "vault_lines": ["Niggas textin' \"Bro,\" but we are not of no close relation"], "meaning": "This could be a reference to a 2018 incident between Kanye and Drake; Kanye publicly called out Drake on Twitter, claiming Drake had been ducking his texts and then asked for a clearance request to sample one of his songs. In a long series of tweets, Kanye repeatedly referred to Drake as “bro.”\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1073319978579955712?s=20\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1073320449088675841?s=20\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1073321268529766401?s=20", "annotation_id": "23878097", "url": "https://genius.com/23878097", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Up in ’Bron crib, fishbowl wineglasses That’s how we passin’ time", "vault_lines": ["Up in 'Bron crib, fishbowl wineglasses", "That's how we passin' time"], "meaning": "Here, Drake flexes his well-known friendship with LeBron James.\n\nDrake’s 2009 hit “Forever” was a single from the soundtrack to LeBron’s More than a Game documentary. More recently, Drake has attended Lebron’s son Bronny’s high school basketball games and is currently producing a movie with LeBron.", "annotation_id": "23878029", "url": "https://genius.com/23878029", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Look at the total wages", "vault_lines": ["Look at the total wages", "And look at the heroes fallin' from grace in their older ages"], "meaning": "According to RIAA, Drake ranks 4th in all time album sales, only trailing Eminem, 2Pac and Jay-Z. Notably, he has sold more than Kanye West, who he disses in this song.", "annotation_id": "23877823", "url": "https://genius.com/23877823", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Toss around Rollies with the frozen faces", "vault_lines": ["Toss around Rollies with the frozen faces"], "meaning": "“Rollies” is a nickname for Rolex watches. Drake says his Rolex watches have “frozen faces” because they have been “iced out” with diamonds. While Rolex makes some watches with diamonds, they are often customized by jewelers to add even more diamonds to them.", "annotation_id": "23877725", "url": "https://genius.com/23877725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Clouds is hanging over you now, ’cause I’m reigning supreme", "vault_lines": ["Clouds is hanging over you now, 'cause I'm reigning supreme"], "meaning": "Drake is using wordplay here using “reigning” as a homophone for “rain” to send a triple message.\n\n1) He is “reigning supreme” (i.e., winning) in his beef with Kanye West.\n\n2) His disses are ‘raining’ down on Kanye. This follows along with Drake being above Kanye, as in clouds (Drake) are hanging over Kanye.\n\n3) Drake is “reigning supreme” over the rap game as a whole.", "annotation_id": "23877494", "url": "https://genius.com/23877494", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "That’s why your people not believers, they all leavin’ ya", "vault_lines": ["That's why your people not believers, they all leavin' ya"], "meaning": "This appears to be a reference to the Sunday Service  Choir who were group of “believers” who sang gospel songs with Kanye at Sunday Service events. Now much of the choir has left Kanye and they have also sued him.  He’s accused of allegedly mistreating and not paying nearly 1,000 performers and employees of his Sunday Service events.\n\n\n\nhttps://thejasminebrand.com/2021/01/30/kanye-west-sued-by-sunday-service-choir-crew-in-class-action-could-face-up-to-30-million-in-damages/", "annotation_id": "23876945", "url": "https://genius.com/23876945", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Calacatta marble for my tombstone", "vault_lines": ["Calacatta marble for my tombstone"], "meaning": "Originating from Italy, Calacatta marble is a highly sought-after and expensive white marble.\n\n\n\nDrake desires for his grave to be a famous and historical place to visit. Naturally, making it out of expensive, eye-catching material is one way to do that.", "annotation_id": "23876716", "url": "https://genius.com/23876716", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Mind’s runnin’ wild with the speculation", "vault_lines": ["Mind's runnin' wild with the speculation"], "meaning": "As the previous line refers to Kanye’s presidential run, here Drake points out ‘Ye’s mind is full of speculations, as evident in Kanye’s South Carolina rally and the podcast episode with Joe Rogan where Kanye theorized several plans he had for the future of the U.S., proving 'Ye’s mind was “running wild”… and not grounded much in reality.", "annotation_id": "23875907", "url": "https://genius.com/23875907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "And I got the flows niggas steal like boot toes", "vault_lines": ["And I got the flows niggas steal like boot toes"], "meaning": "Drake claims that other rappers keep stealing his flows, with some wordplay on “…steal/steel like boot toes”, referencing boots with steel toes.\n\nYou might see these a lot in old Western movies, but steel toes are still popular today in work boots as well.", "annotation_id": "23875750", "url": "https://genius.com/23875750", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Why the fuck we peacemakin’, doin’ the explanations If we just gon’ be right back in that bitch without hesitation", "vault_lines": ["Why the fuck we peacemakin', doin' the explanations", "If we just gon' be right back in that bitch without hesitation"], "meaning": "This line is a hint to the history of beef between Drake and artists like Kanye. The two previously had beef but then made up in time for ‘Ye to slot in on Drake’s mixtape More Life, but since then they’ve just gone right back to being enemies. What’s the point of making up, Drake questions, if they’re just going to beef again?", "annotation_id": "23875563", "url": "https://genius.com/23875563", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Throwin’ parties in Miami, they lovin’ us mucho", "vault_lines": ["Throwin' parties in Miami, they lovin' us mucho"], "meaning": "A reference to his song: “From Florida With Love“ from his fifth mixtape: “Dark Lane Demo Tapes“, released in May 1st, 2020.\n\nDrake is well-known for throwing parties in Miami; he also shot the “God’s Plan“ music video at multiple locations in the city:\n\n- Miami Senior High School, where he made a $25,000 donation, he even laced students with fresh uniforms.\n\n- University of Miami, where he donated $50,000 to cover tuition for a student named Destiny James.\n\n- Sabor Tropical Supermarket; Drake decided that he would be paying for everything the customers wanted.\n\n- The Lotus House of Miami, where he donated $50,000 and gave out Target gift cards.\n\n\n\nDuring the Scorpion era, Drake also threw a couple of parties in Miami.\n\n“Mucho“ means “a lot” in Spanish.", "annotation_id": "23875121", "url": "https://genius.com/23875121", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "You boys ‘bout to will me to defeat like we rollerbladin’", "vault_lines": ["You boys 'bout to will me to defeat like we rollerbladin'"], "meaning": "In Jamaican and UK Grime culture, to “wheel” a song is to bring it back to the start, and play it again. Rewinds are normally performed by selectors in response to crowd demand.\n\nDrake makes the double entendre here of endlessly replaying his music, while sarcastically using the homophone of “defeat / da feet” like in rollerblading, where wheels are strapped to the feet.", "annotation_id": "23875053", "url": "https://genius.com/23875053", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Papi chulo, grippin’ culo", "vault_lines": ["Papi chulo, grippin' culo"], "meaning": "The Spanish phrase “papi chulo” translates to “pimp daddy” in English. Drake uses “papi” in his Instagram handle, which is @champagnepapi.\n\nThis may be a reference to Skepta and Octavian’s 2020 track “Papi Chulo,” as Drake has previously shown love to Skepta. Notably, Drake was sampled on Skepta’s 2015 track “Shutdown,” and Skepta was featured on Drake’s 2017 track “Skepta Interlude.”\n\nFurther, Drake is gripping “culo,” which means “ass” in Spanish.", "annotation_id": "23875044", "url": "https://genius.com/23875044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "And look at the heroes fallin’ from grace in their older ages", "vault_lines": ["Look at the total wages", "And look at the heroes fallin' from grace in their older ages"], "meaning": "A clear shot at Kanye West, who engaged Drake in a beef the week after Drake rapped about him and Pusha T on Trippie Redd’s song “Betrayed” before Donda’s release.", "annotation_id": "23874936", "url": "https://genius.com/23874936", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Could at least keep it a buck like Antetokounmpo", "vault_lines": ["Could at least keep it a buck like Antetokounmpo"], "meaning": "A “buck” is worth a dollar or 100 cents, so to “keep it a buck” means to keep it 100 or to be sincere. “Buck” also doubles as a reference to the Milwaukee Bucks, where NBA superstar Giannis Antetokounmpo plays.\n\nAs the Toronto Raptors‘ global ambassador, Drake is involved with the NBA. In a February 2020 match-up between Toronto and Milwaukee, Giannis told Drake to “shut the f-ck up” as he was talking trash from the sideline. In recalling this, Drake asks for people to be direct with him rather than talking behind his back.\n\nDrake and Giannis have a good relationship off the court and they teamed up for a Certified Lover Boy T-shirt that says “I NEED A FREAK,” paying homage to Giannis' nickname, “Greek Freak.”\n\n\n\nThis line was inspired by a fan tweet following the Bucks winning the NBA championship in July 2021. Shortly after the song’s release, ESPN reposted the tweet on Instagram, where Drake commented:\n\nOnly did it cause of this tweet\n\nConsidering the song is centered around Drake’s strained relationship with Kanye West, it’s worth noting that Kanye name-dropped Giannis on his August 2021 track, “Junya”:\n\nLet me be honest (Mmh, mmh)\nI won with the bucks, boy\nLet me Giannis (Mmh, mmh)\nI won with the bucks, boy (Mm, mm)", "annotation_id": "23874602", "url": "https://genius.com/23874602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "At the table prayin’ banker shows a natural nine It never happens half the time", "vault_lines": ["At the table prayin' banker shows a natural nine", "It never happens half the time"], "meaning": "In baccarat, a card game commonly found at casinos, a natural is a two-card hand totaling 8 or 9, for either the player or the banker. Natural 9 beats natural 8.", "annotation_id": "23874512", "url": "https://genius.com/23874512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "They tried to label me mean, I say what I mean People that could’ve stayed on the team They played in-between", "vault_lines": ["They tried to label me mean, I say what I mean", "People that could've stayed on the team"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing Kanye West’s instagram posts in August of 2021, where he shared a screenshot of an iMessage groupchat which appears to include Pusha T, Drake, and Virgil Abloh.\n\n\n\nThe message Kanye wrote appears to label Drake as a mean bully. The people who could’ve stayed on the team is referring to Virgil, who was originally very close with Kanye as a fashion collaborator. Drake has called out Kanye before for his relationship with Virgil on Pusha T diss track “Duppy Freestyle”, rapping:\n\nI could never have a Virgil in my circle\nAnd hold him back ‘cause he makes me nervous", "annotation_id": "23874506", "url": "https://genius.com/23874506", "state": "pending", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "I could give a fuck ’bout who designing your sneakers and tees", "vault_lines": ["I could give a fuck 'bout who designing your sneakers and tees"], "meaning": "Drake continues to bash Kanye West, this time emphasizing how he does not care for West’s Yeezy brand. Yeezy works with Adidas on sneakers, a brand that Drake has repeatedly insulted in songs as he reps his deal with Nike. For instance, he showed his preference for the iconic Nike check over Adidas' three stripes on “SICKO MODE”:\n\nJesus Christ (Yeah), checks over stripes (Yeah)\nThat’s what I like (Yeah), that’s what we like (Yeah)\n\nAlso, Yeezy began its partnership with GAP for clothing in 2021, which led to Drake sending a subliminal to Kanye on the single “Laugh Now, Cry Later”:\n\nDistance between us is not like a store, this isn’t a closeable gap, ayy", "annotation_id": "23874449", "url": "https://genius.com/23874449", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "You know the fourth level of jealousy is called medea Isn’t that an ironic revelation?", "vault_lines": ["You know the fourth level of jealousy is called medea", "Isn't that an ironic revelation?"], "meaning": "Psychologically, there are four stages of jealousy: identification, confrontative, redirecting, and the fourth, Medea, which is the deepest and strongest.", "annotation_id": "23874447", "url": "https://genius.com/23874447", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Don’t move like a puto", "vault_lines": ["Don't move like a puto"], "meaning": "“Puto” is a derogatory term in the Spanish language.\nIt is most commonly used to describe a coward person, but it is also used to negatively describe a homosexual person in the Hispanic community.\n\nHere, Drake is telling the person who he is referring to in the song, to not “move like a coward”.", "annotation_id": "23874385", "url": "https://genius.com/23874385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Had to pull my niggas out the mud like I’m trainin’ Marines", "vault_lines": ["Had to pull my niggas out the mud like I'm trainin' Marines"], "meaning": "Here, Drake refers to the notorious “Crucible” that Marine recruits go through before they become Marines. The event is described as:\n\nThe Crucible takes place over 54-hours and includes food and sleep deprivation and over 45 miles of marching.\n\nDuring the Crucible, recruits need to crawl through obstacles and tunnels containing mud ; when Drake says “out the mud”, he means he’s taking his people out of the hood into a better life, where “the mud” refers to any low situation in life like living in the projects.", "annotation_id": "23874324", "url": "https://genius.com/23874324", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "But let me digress on behalf of the association I’ll play it cool with you, then mark a nigga like Copenhagen", "vault_lines": ["But let me digress on behalf of the association", "I'll play it cool with you, then mark a nigga like Copenhagen"], "meaning": "Drake is using wordplay to emphasize him targeting his enemy. He’s saying “Then mark” to target and at the same time referencing Denmark as it sounds similar to “then mark.” And afterwards saying “like Copenhagen” which is the capital of Denmark.", "annotation_id": "23874319", "url": "https://genius.com/23874319", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Have somebody put you on a Gildan, you play with my seed", "vault_lines": ["Have somebody put you on a Gildan, you play with my seed"], "meaning": "Gildan is an American-Canadian manufacturer of branded clothing, most notably associated wiht their T-shirts.\n\n\n\nIt is common practice for individuals to place their dead friends or family on t-shirts in memoriam of their life.\n\n\n\nDrake is threatening that anyone who attempts to “play” with his “seed” (his son) will end up dead and therefore appear on a t-shirt.", "annotation_id": "23874291", "url": "https://genius.com/23874291", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Man, fuck a “Respectfully,” I just want my respect", "vault_lines": ["Man, fuck a \"Respectfully,\" I just want my respect"], "meaning": "This line references social media personality Justin Laboy who hosts his own late-night internet talk show called Respectfully Justin, and uses the term “Respectfully” as his slogan. He rose to fame in mid-to-late 2020 through his “Demon Time” Instagram Live sessions in the midst’s of the COVID-19 pandemic. Justin has been in his fair share of controversy, most notably allegedly getting jumped by the Migos in March 2021.\n\nJustin has shown his admiration for Drake on social media but it hasn’t been reciprocated by Drizzy. In June 2021, Drake mocked Justin’s personalised #Respectfully chain, and later called “cap” on Justin teasing an upcoming interview with him on Respectfully Justin. The reason to this diss may relate to how he was heavily involved with Kanye for the release of Donda, and Drizzy doesn’t abide with him getting comfortable with this opposition.\n\nWhen a set of Drake fans bombarded Kanye’s childhood home in August 28th 2021, one of the cards also read:\n\nFUCK JUSTIN LABOY", "annotation_id": "23874286", "url": "https://genius.com/23874286", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Know the owl stay on me", "vault_lines": ["Know the owl stay on me"], "meaning": "This line refers to OVO or October’s Very Own, Drake’s record label. “OVO” is incorporated into the logo as the eyes and beak of an owl, so whenever Drake wears OVO apparel, he’s repping his label with the owl.", "annotation_id": "23874270", "url": "https://genius.com/23874270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Or switch it to the one I call “R.I.P. Nipsey” ’Cause I swear to God the bezel got sixty of them blue stones", "vault_lines": ["Or switch it to the one I call \"R.I.P. Nipsey\"", "'Cause I swear to God the bezel got sixty of them blue stones"], "meaning": "Drake compares the diamonds in one of his watches to the gang affiliation of the artist Nipsey Hussle, who was murdered in March of 2019. A native of Crenshaw in South Central Los Angeles, Nipsey was a member of the Rollin' 60s Neighborhood Crips, a gang set that primarily operates between Slauson and Florence Avenue.\n\nThe mention of blue stones also refers to the blue clothing often worn by Crip-affiliated gangs, distinct from the red traditionally worn by rival Blood gangs.\nRegardless of color, Nipsey’s death was a unifying force in the Crenshaw community that led to Blood and Crip peace talks.\n\nDrake was featured on Nipsey’s 2009 track “Killer.” After Nipsey’s sudden death, Drake posted the following message to his Instagram:\n\nFuck. My whole energy is just at a low right now hearing this. We just linked for the first time in years and said we were gonna do a new song this summer cause it had been too long. You were having the best run and I was so happy watching from distance fam nobody ever talks down on your name you were a real one to your people and to the rest of us. I’m only doing this here cause I want the world to know I saw you as a man of respect and a don. Rest easy my g.", "annotation_id": "23874264", "url": "https://genius.com/23874264", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "With the ho ratio, I’m like David Caruso", "vault_lines": ["With the ho ratio, I'm like David Caruso"], "meaning": "David Caruso is a retired American actor famous for playing the role of Lieutenant Horatio Caine in the TV series CSI: Miami.\n\nIn this line Drake makes a wordplay on the name of the character and the amount of success he is getting with girls in Miami.", "annotation_id": "23874224", "url": "https://genius.com/23874224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "I’m made north of the border like Vito Rizzuto", "vault_lines": ["I'm made north of the border like Vito Rizzuto"], "meaning": "Vito Rizzuto, known as “Montreal’s Teflon Don”, was an infamous crime boss who  was alleged to be the leader of the Sicilian Mafia in Canada. He oversaw a drug empire that imported hashish, cocaine and heroin into Canada. He was eventually extradited to the United States and imprisoned there for crimes including racketeering and conspiracy to commit murder.\n\nDrake uses a double entendre comparing his Canadian heritage to Vito Rizzuto’s status as a made man. With the term bing made to describe Drake’s birth as well as the process in which Italian mobsters go through to become official parts of the life. This process is called “Being Made” and is done under the authority of the boss of the family, often proposed by captains or “Capos” in the organization.\n\n\n\nVito Rizzuto", "annotation_id": "23874222", "url": "https://genius.com/23874222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Tryna get the Earl Grey on me", "vault_lines": ["Tryna get the Earl Grey on me"], "meaning": "They’re trying to get the “tea”, which is slang for “gossip”. Earl Grey is a common blend of tea. Interesting to note, there is also a Canadian connection here since the grandson of the inventor of the tea, Albert Grey (also referred to by his title of Earl Grey), donated the trophy annually presented to the champions of the Canadian Football League in Drake’s native Canada, while he was Governor General there in the early 20th century (the trophy is also colloquially referred to as “Earl Grey’s mug” or “cup”).", "annotation_id": "23874170", "url": "https://genius.com/23874170", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "I been doin’ this since T-Pain was pourin’ us Nuvo", "vault_lines": ["I been doin' this since T-Pain was pourin' us Nuvo"], "meaning": "This is a reference to a line by T-Pain from Jamie Foxx’s 2009 track “Blame It”:\n\nI'ma take a shot of Nuvo, shawty then you know\nIt’s going down, we can go and kick it like judo\n\nNuvo L'esprit De Paris is a sparkling liqueur, made in Paris, France, which was popular in 2009.\n\nNotably, 2009 was the same year that Lil Wayne signed Drake to his label, Young Money.", "annotation_id": "23874169", "url": "https://genius.com/23874169", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "You tell ’em I run the country, they’ll say, “True, though”", "vault_lines": ["You tell 'em I run the country, they'll say, \"True, though\""], "meaning": "“True, though” sounds like “Trudeau,” a play on the last name of Canada’s 23rd Prime Minister, the Right Honourable Justin Trudeau who literally runs the country (and whose dad also served as PM).\n\nIn May 2021, Trudeau did an interview on The Brandon Gonez Show (which Drake has endorsed) admitting that while Drake is not on his personal playlist, his kids love him and that’s how he hears his music:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qjpFe1fdhxU&t=387s", "annotation_id": "23874150", "url": "https://genius.com/23874150", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "line": "Give that address to your driver, make it your destination ’Stead of just a post out of desperation", "vault_lines": ["Give that address to your driver, make it your destination", "'Stead of just a post out of desperation"], "meaning": "Following up on the previous lines, Drake takes another direct shot at Kanye West, who posted a picture with the address of Drake’s Toronto home and deleted it minutes after, further escalating the beef between them.\n\n\n\nDrake quickly replied to the tweet on his IG stories by laughing at what was happening while taking a ride around Toronto.\n\nDrake says that Kanye should directly come and meet him up at his house to discuss their differences rather than going for desperate attempts to get clout through social media.", "annotation_id": "23873962", "url": "https://genius.com/23873962", "state": "accepted", "votes": 169, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "7am On Bridle Path", "status": "done", "targets": 56, "clicked": 56, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-7am-on-bridle-path-lyrics", "genius_title": "7am On Bridle Path"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "We move the operation to Cali soon as the snow drop", "vault_lines": ["We move the operation to Cali soon as the snow drop"], "meaning": "These lines and the proceeding lines are subliminals to Kendrick Lamar. These lines are likely what made Kendrick drop Control in the coming months.", "annotation_id": "39885003", "url": "https://genius.com/39885003", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "And show up at the show, the afterparty, and the hotel", "vault_lines": ["And show up at the show, the afterparty, and the hotel"], "meaning": "A  reference to the Jodeci album The Show, The After Party, The Hotel", "annotation_id": "25741259", "url": "https://genius.com/25741259", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Either I’m gettin’ bigger or you’re just gettin’ smaller or it’s both, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Either I'm gettin' bigger or you're just gettin' smaller or it's both, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake discusses his burgeoning rap career and his ascent to the top of the rap game. Drake’s career truly began to blossom after signing with Young Money and releasing his 2011 album Take Care, which thrust him into the mainstream spotlight.\n\nDrake later discusses his placement in rap and his relative size in comparison to others on his June 2016 track, “4PM in Calabassas”:\n\nI got a lot to lose ‘cause in every situation I’m the bigger artist\nAlways gotta play it smarter", "annotation_id": "17726587", "url": "https://genius.com/17726587", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "I should run a clinic for niggas that think that they winning On some coach shit, 50 Ms for a three-month road trip", "vault_lines": ["I should run a clinic for niggas that think that they winning", "On some coach shit, 50 Ms for a three-month road trip"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to his Club Paradise tour, which featured Kendrick Lamar and A$AP Rocky. The successful tour brought in approximately $43.4 million and consisted of 67 different shows, while lasting for almost 4 months.\n\nIn addition, featured artist J. Cole actually served as the tour opener on the second leg of the tour.", "annotation_id": "17726429", "url": "https://genius.com/17726429", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "[Verse 3: J. Cole]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In an interview with Vibe Magazine, Cole explained that he recorded the verse while he was doing his radio run for his sophomore LP Born Sinner in Philadelphia.\n\nBacklash that he received from a controversial line in the middle of this verse prompted Cole and Drake to apologize.", "annotation_id": "5202426", "url": "https://genius.com/5202426", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "(Fuck you mean you ain’t got no condoms)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Continuing on from the previous line where Cole stated “The way that I’m respondin', she know that we black”, he’s implying in a hushed tone that black guys don’t really mess around when it comes to having sex.\n\nThis could be to do with the amount of hoes that they’ve had unprotected sex with or it could be to do with the fact that if they make a woman pregnant, child support could be an issue", "annotation_id": "2014722", "url": "https://genius.com/2014722", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Flow dumber than your projections", "vault_lines": ["Flow dumber than your projections"], "meaning": "He’s saying that his flow is stupid good. It’s kind of an oxymoron just like the name of one of his supporting cast DJ Dummy.\n\nHis first album also which was projected at 50,000-60,000 when it actually sold 219,000 the first week. Stores were selling out to say the least. Stupid projection. Retweet the pic below.\n\nAlso, the projected sales numbers for Born Sinner were originally 150k first week;  he ended up doing about 300k.", "annotation_id": "1922197", "url": "https://genius.com/1922197", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "But that’s… that’s where all this... that’s where the feeling is That’s where all that shit comes from… man I mean like The music that you and I used to listen to ahh.. was just..", "vault_lines": ["But that’s… that’s where all this... that’s where the feeling is", "That’s where all that shit comes from… man I mean like", "The music that you and I used to listen to ahh.. was just.."], "meaning": "Drake puts his dad’s voice on the track like he did for his grandmother on “Look what you’ve done” to describe how they have that everlasting bond and he’s still the same person.\n\n\n\nDrake and Dennis used to listen to music driving from Toronto to Memphis and back when Dennis would spend time with him. This is why Dennis is saying they went through EVERYTHING. They had time on their hands to create a shared musical history.\n\n“I’d never fly to Memphis; my dad would always pick me up in Toronto and we’d drive, and it was like 21 hours,” says Drake. “On that drive he would just play me the greatest music, and then bring me to Beale Street and drive around the city, and I started to understand what it was about Memphis.”", "annotation_id": "1903969", "url": "https://genius.com/1903969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Felt naked ’cause I laid out all my glory and my shame", "vault_lines": ["Felt naked 'cause I laid out all my glory and my shame"], "meaning": "While Cole has always been one to wear his heart on his sleeve, he recently grabbed headlines with his song “Let Nas Down”, in which he attempts to convey his sadness over the news that his greatest glory – his most commercially successful single “Work Out” -, also lead to his greatest shame – his idol, Nas, openly expressing disappointment in the young artist’s making of that song.", "annotation_id": "1898733", "url": "https://genius.com/1898733", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "I’m artistic, you ****** ** ******** ********", "vault_lines": ["I'm good in every town, I'ma be there doing shows where you at", "I know I'm a sure shot, middle finger poking you in your sore spot", "I'm artistic, you ****** ** ******** ********"], "meaning": "Cole is an artistic rapper.\n\nSome people are “autistic”, which is a developmental disorder that, amongst other things, causes sensory issues and difficulties with social interaction – but it is not a form of learning disability.\n\nThis line was not accepted well by autism community, it went so far that people already started with petitions.\n\nCole was in trouble already early 2013 for his “faggot line” in “Villuminati” from his album Born Sinner.\n\nHe later formally apologised to the Autistic community through his website https://twitter.com/JColeNC/status/359098376404484097\n\nOn his OVO blog, Drake also issued an apology.\n\nIndividuals with autism have brilliant and creative minds, and their gifts should not be disparaged or discounted.\n\nDrake also decided to remove the line from the song.\n\nhttp://www.usatoday.com/story/life/music/2013/07/22/j-cole-apologizes-for-autism-lyric/2574459/", "annotation_id": "1897384", "url": "https://genius.com/1897384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 121, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Your money is just a little Barney’s Co-Op for you to be tryna show out", "vault_lines": ["Your money is just a little Barney’s Co-Op for you to be tryna show out"], "meaning": "Barneys Co Op is a series of smaller department stores in the Barney chain so Drake is possibly saying that your money is too small in relation to his big spending to be trying to show off.", "annotation_id": "1897351", "url": "https://genius.com/1897351", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Heard a couple niggas hatin’ but them fuck niggas weak Count a hundred thousand dollars like it sucks to be me Ain’t that what you wanted? Stuntin’ on you niggas Came in this game never frontin’ on you niggas", "vault_lines": ["Heard a couple niggas hatin' but them fuck niggas weak", "Count a hundred thousand dollars like it sucks to be me", "Ain't that what you wanted? Stuntin' on you niggas", "Came in this game never frontin' on you niggas"], "meaning": "Cole based his entire rap career off of raw honesty and always kept his fans best interest at heart. Now that he’s released two studio albums, three successful mixtapes, and released countless other projects including Friday Night Lights and Any Given Sunday, Cole has added a bit of materialism into his music, because it seems that’s what some people want to hear. However Cole understands he can’t please everybody. Haters gon' Hate.\n\n\n\nThis line conveys the same message as a line from Cole’s song “Rich Niggaz”:\n\nNiggas can’t front on the flows you got\nBut every fucking verse how much dough you got", "annotation_id": "1897286", "url": "https://genius.com/1897286", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "[Interlude: Dennis Graham]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Interesting enough, Drake chooses to feature his father, Dennis Graham, who once worked as a drummer for the legendary Jerry Lee Lewis.", "annotation_id": "1897283", "url": "https://genius.com/1897283", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Caught fire just to have niggas ignore me and my flame ’Bout to burn down the house, they tryna put me in the rain, no", "vault_lines": ["Caught fire just to have niggas ignore me and my flame", "'Bout to burn down the house, they tryna put me in the rain, no"], "meaning": "After releasing numerous free mixtapes, Cole finally got signed by Jay-Z. Even with that flame, he was virtually unknown in the rap game. He best described his feelings towards this issue in “Looking For Trouble” saying:\n\nIronic you been sleeping on the one that you been dreamin' ’bout\n\nFast forward to today, he is currently one of the biggest rappers around and his flame has gotten so big that it could burn a house down. The industry, however, still hasn’t stop trying to shun him out, and they are now pushing him in the rain to put out his flame.", "annotation_id": "1897276", "url": "https://genius.com/1897276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Fuckin’ hoes like Jodeci’s back", "vault_lines": ["The lights hit women screaming like Jodeci's back, nigga", "I hit the lobby, women's screaming like Jodeci's back, nigga", "You bitches screamin' like Jodeci's back", "Fuckin' hoes like Jodeci's back", "Bitches screamin' like Jodeci's back"], "meaning": "Jodeci – being the R&B powerhouse that they were – always had thousands of screaming female fans at their shows who wanted to get a piece of them, quite similar to Cole and Drake.", "annotation_id": "1897260", "url": "https://genius.com/1897260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "I know I’m a sure shot, middle finger poking you in your sore spot", "vault_lines": ["I know I'm a sure shot, middle finger poking you in your sore spot", "I'm artistic, you ****** ** ******** ********"], "meaning": "Drake knows that if he is on a track it will surely top charts and other musicians know this as well.\n\nHe can throw his middle finger up all he wants because, these musicians need Drake in this era of rap or any other category of music that is.\n\nSubsequently, if you make a fake handgun,\n\n\n\nYou can not only use your pointer finger as the shooter but your “middle finger” to poke.\n\nHe’s also adding insult to injury – Drake flips the bird at these rappers whose egos he’s already bruised", "annotation_id": "1897242", "url": "https://genius.com/1897242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "No need for pause when I tell you my balls bigger than yours, Cole", "vault_lines": ["No need for pause when I tell you my balls bigger than yours, Cole"], "meaning": "The “pause” is a modern trope used after someone says a phrase that would be perceived as a homosexual statement. By Cole excluding the pause, he’s not talking about literally comparing ball sizes, but is talking figuratively about how he has much more nerve and is a much bigger man.", "annotation_id": "1897227", "url": "https://genius.com/1897227", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Bars sound like I’m under oath nigga", "vault_lines": ["Bars sound like I'm under oath nigga"], "meaning": "Someone who is under oath in a court of law is confined to tell nothing but the truth, so you know Drake only rhymes about real shit.\n\n\n\nHe’s made similar statements on “No Lie,” and “Fo' Real.”", "annotation_id": "1897204", "url": "https://genius.com/1897204", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "I’ll hang you with it after I teach you the ropes nigga", "vault_lines": ["I'll hang you with it after I teach you the ropes nigga"], "meaning": "Drizzy plays with his words pretty cleverly. In essence, this is just the previous line re-phrased. Drake is trying to get through to whoever he may be talking about that just because he taught you the rope of the game, doesn’t mean he can’t kill your game with the same rope.\n\nSounds like something I heard from my mom many times,\n\n\n\nBefore people start saying he’s dissing The Weeknd, take a look:\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JgHx9JgD6Bc", "annotation_id": "1897200", "url": "https://genius.com/1897200", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Gave you heart and soul, stories of my pain", "vault_lines": ["Gave you heart and soul, stories of my pain"], "meaning": "J. Cole just put out his emotions on his records, telling his fans stories of his childhood and come up.\n\nMany of J. Cole’s fans were attracted to his music because of its' relateable lyrics. This is exemplified in J. Cole’s song about impregnating a woman as a teenager, Lost Ones.", "annotation_id": "1897147", "url": "https://genius.com/1897147", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Roof top, hoes turnt up Lookin’ for your bitch, bet she won’t turn up Wonder where she is, fuck could she be? She’s a ho, she’s a slut, she’s a freak", "vault_lines": ["Roof top, hoes turnt up", "Lookin' for your bitch, bet she won't turn up", "Wonder where she is, fuck could she be?", "She's a ho, she's a slut, she's a freak"], "meaning": "It’s a play on words. Cole is on the rooftop partying and if you’re looking for you girl, you won’t find her because she’s partying with Cole.\n\nGood luck finding your girl in a place that probably looks something like this.\n\n\n\nThe last line is a reference to the song Crush on You by Lil Kim, in which The Notorious B.I.G. performs the hook that goes:\n\nListenin to what your girlfriends mention\nHe’s a slut, he’s a ho, he’s a freak", "annotation_id": "1897113", "url": "https://genius.com/1897113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Pay yourself and owe yourself", "vault_lines": ["Pay yourself and owe yourself"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing a term used a lot in the business world. The first rule to investing is paying yourself which means to set aside money usually deposited into your 401k.\n\nEssentially what Drizzy is saying is he’s created a loop in which he saves his money but then continues to owe himself or in other terms make more money.\n\n\n\nDrake’s worth $150 million USD.", "annotation_id": "1897101", "url": "https://genius.com/1897101", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Nigga woulda thought Def Poetry back (Cole)", "vault_lines": ["Nigga woulda thought Def Poetry back (Cole)"], "meaning": "Def Poetry, was an HBO television series produced by hip-hop music entrepreneur Russell Simmons. The series had performances by spoken word poets, as well as up-and-coming ones. Well-known actors and musicians would often surprise the audience by showing up to recite their own original poems. The show was hosted by Mos Def.", "annotation_id": "1896948", "url": "https://genius.com/1896948", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Fuck your list you lame niggas and doubters I’m undoubtedly the hottest and that’s just me bein’ modest", "vault_lines": ["Fuck your list you lame niggas and doubters", "I'm undoubtedly the hottest and that's just me bein' modest", "I'm artistic, you ****** ** ******** ********"], "meaning": "Cole is referring to his absence from the Hottest MC List by MTV. Many people argued that Cole should have been on the list especially considering how well his debut album performed commercially.\n\nCheck out the interview where Cole responds to being left off the list:\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sRg78Q5j_wI\n\nCole lets us know that even when he talks about him being the best, he is still being modest, that’s how good he really is.", "annotation_id": "1896939", "url": "https://genius.com/1896939", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Before you come to my city just know yourself Know where you at", "vault_lines": ["Before you come to my city just know yourself", "Know where you at"], "meaning": "Although Drake can mean this:\n\n“Before you come to my city just know yourself” – Know who you are as a person so you won’t get lost (within the street life).\n“Know where you at” – Know which area to be in and which area to not be in.\n\nHe can also be indicating that,\n\nIf anyone plans to come to the city in which he reps “Toronto” they should know who Drake is, as he is running that town and has built the map for it in the music industry. It would be kind of hard to break his career and and take shots at him as he is one of the most successful rappers, he even won a Grammy- Add that to the resume.\n\nIn “5AM in Toronto”, Drizzy says:\n\nI got a gold trophy from the committee for validation", "annotation_id": "1896936", "url": "https://genius.com/1896936", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Who gives a poker face when you jokers showed me your cards?", "vault_lines": ["Who gives a poker face when you jokers showed me your cards?"], "meaning": "Cards reference. In poker you are supposed to have poker face which is usually a blank expression or a misleading one that way people don’t know what you have in your hand. But since people already showed Cole what he got, why does he have to hide his smile if he already know his hand is better than there? This a rap game reference he knows his music is better than most people’s out right now.\n\n\n\nFurthermore, in cards the jokers are taken out of the deck because they generally have no value in a normal game. Therefore, why would he care about anything you say when you’re worthless. This is also wordplay on the card analogy by using the term Jokers.", "annotation_id": "1896781", "url": "https://genius.com/1896781", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "IRS all in my books getting they Matlock on", "vault_lines": ["IRS all in my books getting they Matlock on"], "meaning": "Drake makes a lot of money, so the Internal Revenue System is investigating him like Matlock. Matlock was a TV show about legal issues who’s main character was lawyer Ben Matlock.", "annotation_id": "1896755", "url": "https://genius.com/1896755", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "The lights hit women screaming like Jodeci’s back, nigga", "vault_lines": ["The lights hit women screaming like Jodeci's back, nigga", "I hit the lobby, women's screaming like Jodeci's back, nigga", "Fuckin' hoes like Jodeci's back", "Bitches screamin' like Jodeci's back"], "meaning": "Jodeci is an American R&B band, which has been on hiatus since 1998. The group was extremely popular among the female audience, so if it were to return, a lot of women just might get overly excited.", "annotation_id": "1896744", "url": "https://genius.com/1896744", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "It’s like every time I plot a return I seem to shift the game", "vault_lines": ["It's like every time I plot a return I seem to shift the game"], "meaning": "Anytime Drake comes out with new music, there is always a lot of hype behind it. And his music usually sets precedent for a long time after.\n\n\n\nAlso, the line before this and the line after this also share a common factor which is a reference to the keyboard. He has the CAPS LOCK on, he is gonna RETURN to SHIFT the game, and he is talking different keys.", "annotation_id": "1896740", "url": "https://genius.com/1896740", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Oh well, bitches paint OVO on their toenails", "vault_lines": ["Oh well, bitches paint OVO on their toenails"], "meaning": "OVO is Drake’s own label.\n\n\n\nBy painting the name of the label on their toenails, these girls show that they are fans of Drake’s music and the music, his label produces.", "annotation_id": "1896734", "url": "https://genius.com/1896734", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "I’m good in every town, I’ma be there doing shows where you at", "vault_lines": ["Know where you at", "I'm good in every town, I'ma be there doing shows where you at", "I'm artistic, you ****** ** ******** ********"], "meaning": "Drake has just launched his “Would You Like A Tour?” tour with Miguel and Future. This tour has 42 dates, so Drake will most likely end up performing near you.\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/346108571630583808", "annotation_id": "1896676", "url": "https://genius.com/1896676", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Young Mike playin’ against the Monstars", "vault_lines": ["No false gods, young Mike playin' against the Monstars"], "meaning": "This still flows with the “takin' on the giants” concept. In the animated basketball movie Space Jam – which has a truly amazing soundtrack – the Monstars want to enslave all Looney Tunes characters to save an entertainment park. Of course, the Looney Tunes characters don’t agree and they challenge the weak and little Monstars to a basketball match. Later, the Monstars drain the powers from a bunch of professional basketball players and get huge and seem unbeatable. The Looney Tunes call in Michael Jordan to help them and they eventually win the match.", "annotation_id": "1896502", "url": "https://genius.com/1896502", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "You bit the hand, now starve, it’s not a joke nigga", "vault_lines": ["You bit the hand, now starve, it's not a joke nigga"], "meaning": "A play on the phrase, “don’t bite the hand that feeds you”.\n\nSince Drake’s ‘hand’ has already been ‘bit’, meaning he’s already been betrayed, his friend-turned-foe is not ‘fed’ anymore, so he’s obviously starving! YOU NEED DRAKE FOR THE RADIO HITS!\n\nSome believe this is a shot at former friend/labelmate The Weeknd.", "annotation_id": "1896401", "url": "https://genius.com/1896401", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Oh stop, please stop arguin’ ’bout who’s the best MC I think everyone would agree, they know that you’re not", "vault_lines": ["Oh stop, please stop arguin' 'bout who's the best MC", "I think everyone would agree, they know that you're not"], "meaning": "No matter how much [insert generic rapper] disagrees with the lists being made for the top MCs in the game, it is ultimately of no use, since [generic rapper] has no chance of ever getting on that list.\n\nDrake expressed a similar sentiment on 5 AM in Toronto", "annotation_id": "1896376", "url": "https://genius.com/1896376", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "This makes twice now, I doubled what you expected, yet Your covers keep perplexin’ me Maybe it’s too complex for me", "vault_lines": ["This makes twice now, I doubled what you expected, yet", "Your covers keep perplexin' me", "Maybe it's too complex for me"], "meaning": "I DOUBLEd what you EX-pected is a play on the Magazine XXL. Cole hasn’t yet been on the cover solely, even though he has achieved so much in Hip Hop.\n\n\n\nComplex is another magazine that J Cole hasn’t covered. He says it’s COMPLEX because he doesn’t understand why.\n\n\n\nCole is also referring to his albums sales. His debut album was projected to sell less than 100k, but ended up selling more than 200 thousand. His sophomore album was projected to sell 150k and he’s on track to sell about 300k.", "annotation_id": "1896367", "url": "https://genius.com/1896367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "See I can still talk keys without pitchin’ cane", "vault_lines": ["See I can still talk keys without pitchin' cane"], "meaning": "While there are a lot of rappers talking about selling kilos, i.e,‘ keys ’ of cocaine, Drake can still make money off of music that doesn’t involve drug-dealing references. This could be a shot at Pusha T, an artist known for rapping about his drug-dealing past. The two have history so it’s definitely possible.\n\nThis is also a play off the two lines before where he references different keys on a keyboard (caps lock, return, shift.)\n\nThese keys could also refer to the musical ones. The second half of this line includes the term “pitch,” which is a musical term used in reference to placing sounds on a frequency-related scale.", "annotation_id": "1896351", "url": "https://genius.com/1896351", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "I Comedy Central roast niggas and turn ’em to ghost niggas", "vault_lines": ["I Comedy Central roast niggas and turn 'em to ghost niggas"], "meaning": "The Comedy Central Roasts are a series of celebrity roast specials which air on the Comedy Central cable network. A group of celebs take shots and diss the celeb being roasted.\n\nDrake is doing the same and raps all his enemies into oblivion.", "annotation_id": "1896341", "url": "https://genius.com/1896341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Thoughts stream like I’m Spotifyin’", "vault_lines": ["Thoughts stream like I'm Spotifyin'"], "meaning": "The next big thing after CDs & MP3s seem to be streaming services. The Scandinavian based Spotify is one of the most successful services in terms of streaming music over the internet.\n\nJ. Cole’s mind metaphorically works the same: A constant wirelessly transmitted stream of data/information, generating new ideas and ill concepts for new songs.", "annotation_id": "1896308", "url": "https://genius.com/1896308", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Today I’m out in Philly, my fans waitin’ for 12 hours Just to get their dollar signed", "vault_lines": ["Today I'm out in Philly, my fans waitin' for 12 hours", "Just to get their dollar signed"], "meaning": "When Drake & J. Cole recorded the song on June 21st, Jermaine was in Philadelphia at the current stop of his Dollar & A Dream Tour.\n\n\n\nSince no tickets were sold but the entrance fee was $1, his fans waited for 12 hours straight, to get to the show and to get their dollar autographed.\n\nOn a side note, J. Cole has used this returning concept of only having “a dollar and a dream” in several songs throughout his entire career:\nDolla and A Dream\nDolla and a Dream II\nDollar And A Dream III", "annotation_id": "1896264", "url": "https://genius.com/1896264", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "David and Goliath, takin’ on the giants", "vault_lines": ["David and Goliath, takin' on the giants"], "meaning": "Cole is referring to one of the most known biblical stories about the war between the Philistine and Israeli armies. The Philistines had a giant, measuring just over nine feet tall, named Goliath. One day, a young boy named David was sent by his father to retrieve news about his older brothers. He ended up killing Goliath that very day with a slingshot and rocks.\n\nHere’s a video that fully explains the entire story:\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MYxjDsRKOuw\n\nThis is also the start of him referencing his battle with Kanye, where Cole is clearly the underdog in the sales category.", "annotation_id": "1896243", "url": "https://genius.com/1896243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "To these too cool for school niggas I’m Columbine", "vault_lines": ["To these too cool for school niggas I'm Columbine"], "meaning": "It was considered to be cool for rappers to state that they dropped out of school. This begins the shots at Kanye West, who named his first LP “The College Dropout” (which is in Cole’s top 10 favorite albums.) Jermaine, unlike Ye, didn’t drop out of school and obtained a major in communications from St. John’s University.\n\nMetaphorically he will murder the dumbed down rappers with his words, almost like how Eric Harris and Dylan Klebold literally murdered 12 students during the infamous Columbine High School massacre in 1999.", "annotation_id": "1896228", "url": "https://genius.com/1896228", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Which is really just Slim Shady, I’m silly, my pen crazy", "vault_lines": ["Which is really just Slim Shady, I'm silly, my pen crazy"], "meaning": "Going on with the classic albums, a reference to Eminem’s classic major debut album “The Slim Shady LP”, which was executive produced by his mentor Dr. Dre.\n\n\n\nThis is also alluding to Eminem ghostwriting for Dre on  2001, also known as The Chronic 2001 to be specific.", "annotation_id": "1896197", "url": "https://genius.com/1896197", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Or that Chronic shit, Dre", "vault_lines": ["or that Chronic shit, Dre"], "meaning": "Continuing with his enumeration of classic albums:\nThe West Coast G-Funk classic, Dr. Dre’s “The Chronic”, released in 1992.", "annotation_id": "1896195", "url": "https://genius.com/1896195", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Tappin’ into ’94 Nas", "vault_lines": ["Tappin' into '94 Nas"], "meaning": "In 1994, Nas released, what many consider the best album in hip-hop history, “Illmatic.”\n\n\n\nThis album happens to be one of Cole’s favorites:\n\nWhen this album dropped I had to be nine, so I give myself a pass for missing out on this at first. It wasn’t until my cousin forced me to listen to ‘I gave you power’ off of It Was Written that I realized Nas was one of the greatest, and I had some homework to do! Illmatic is one of those albums that demonstrates the highest level of lyricism possible!", "annotation_id": "1896178", "url": "https://genius.com/1896178", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Or that ’96 Jay,", "vault_lines": ["Or that '96 Jay,"], "meaning": "In 1996, Jay-Z released his classic album Reasonable Doubt.", "annotation_id": "1896160", "url": "https://genius.com/1896160", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "All this capital it’s like I left the caps lock on", "vault_lines": ["All this capital it's like I left the caps lock on"], "meaning": "Drizzy is obtaining so much Capital, as in money or currency, that it appears he has left his Capital Lock button on his keyboard on, this is a play on words.\n\n\n\nThis line (along with the next two) sound similar to these lines at the end of Wale’s “Beautiful Bliss”:\n\nCapital I’m raising like I’m through punctuating\nOr shift keys or an I placement cause\nShifted keys gets your capital raised up", "annotation_id": "1896154", "url": "https://genius.com/1896154", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "I see straight through them like fish tanks with no fish in them", "vault_lines": ["I see straight through them like fish tanks with no fish in them"], "meaning": "He can read them, he knows they aren’t truly winning as he could see right through clear empty fish tanks.\n\nOn Ryan Leslie’s song, “Swiss Francs”, he said this similar line:\n\nI can see right through these haters: fish tanks", "annotation_id": "1896150", "url": "https://genius.com/1896150", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Your new shit sound like you do covers Of all of my old shit, oh shit", "vault_lines": ["Your new shit sound like you do covers", "Of all of my old shit, oh shit"], "meaning": "Drake connects this line to the line above through his usage of the word “cover.” While Drake is on magazine covers, other rappers cover his old songs, sounding “like Drake featuring Drake.”\n\nThis line could be adding fuel to the fire of the ongoing Chris Brown vs. Drake beef. Last year, Drake released a song featuring Aaliyah’s vocals called “Enough Said”. Chris Brown proceeded to also use these vocals on his new song “Don’t Think They Know”, something which, due to Drizzy’s love for the deceased songstress, must have struck a nerve and prompted this line to happen.\n\n#SHOTS FIRED", "annotation_id": "1895967", "url": "https://genius.com/1895967", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "26 on my third GQ cover", "vault_lines": ["26 on my third GQ cover"], "meaning": "Despite his relatively young age in the rap game, Drake is able to boast being on three covers of the GQ magazine. His first cover describes his rise to fame in the rap game and his second delves into his fashion sense and exposure to designer clothing.\n\nHis third and most recent cover delves into his difficult upbringing and further describes his rise to fame and prosperity.", "annotation_id": "1895961", "url": "https://genius.com/1895961", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "line": "Drizzy still got some ’06 in him", "vault_lines": ["Drizzy still got some '06 in him"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to his February 2006 project Room For Improvement, where he was noticeably more interested in rapping. In his 2011 album Take Care, he embraced his softer side more and decided to sing more, which was a succesful choice.", "annotation_id": "1895945", "url": "https://genius.com/1895945", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jodeci Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 50, "clicked": 50, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-jodeci-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Jodeci Freestyle"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "My competition, it’s beyond offensive", "vault_lines": ["My competition, it's beyond offensive"], "meaning": "Continuing from the previous line. Could be a shot at Kendrick. For not always showing out.", "annotation_id": "39898319", "url": "https://genius.com/39898319", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "I know catchin’ flies with honey is still sticky", "vault_lines": ["I know catchin' flies with honey is still sticky"], "meaning": "Drake is flipping the classic saying “you catch more flies with honey than with vinegar,” which means being nice or charming gets you further than being harsh.\n\nBy adding that using honey “is still sticky,” Drake uses a double entendre to point out that literally, catching flies with honey can make a mess, but also figuratively, even when you are nice and polite, things can still go awry. He’s acknowledging that choosing kindness comes with its own kind of “stickiness.”", "annotation_id": "37620901", "url": "https://genius.com/37620901", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "I could only speak what I know of", "vault_lines": ["I could only speak what I know of"], "meaning": "This might be a reach but this could also be interpreted as “I could only speak without Noah,” Noah “40” being a well known collaborator Drizzy tends to get his beats from and engineering from.", "annotation_id": "35634651", "url": "https://genius.com/35634651", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "I know when someone lyin’", "vault_lines": ["I know when someone lyin'", "I know what we're both thinkin' even when you're quiet"], "meaning": ".", "annotation_id": "35527608", "url": "https://genius.com/35527608", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "I notice people standin’ for nothin’ and gettin’ tired", "vault_lines": ["I notice people standin' for nothin' and gettin' tired"], "meaning": "A shot at people who live superficially, going through the motions chasing clout or money with no real purpose or values.", "annotation_id": "35429795", "url": "https://genius.com/35429795", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Opinions started to burn when tables started to turn", "vault_lines": ["Opinions started to burn when tables started to turn"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that people push their beliefs to the side when the going gets tough.", "annotation_id": "35429781", "url": "https://genius.com/35429781", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Please never label niggas who lay down for a livin’", "vault_lines": ["Please never label niggas who lay down for a livin'"], "meaning": "This is possibly a subtle dig at Kendrick, who gets criticism for dropping music and disappearing from the scene for a minute.", "annotation_id": "33548589", "url": "https://genius.com/33548589", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "How they go from not wantin’ me at all To wantin’ to see me lose it all?", "vault_lines": ["How they go from not wantin' me at all", "To wantin' to see me lose it all?"], "meaning": "Drake has faced a quite amount of backlash of treatment for his fame. All around coz of how he grew up. Even all of his online videos, comment section is filled with people picking apart what they think about Drake. So naturally, in the day and age of Cancel Culture, people want to feel powerful by their influence to be able to stop a celebrity’s success come to an end in their accord. He mentions it in his song, “Fear”. ‘They build you up so you can be the biggest in the game. And when you get there you realise it ain’t the same!’. People are just waiting with bated eyes to see Drake fall off more so he has seen more success and isn’t from United States literally. So its an abrasive take. This is cemented by the Back & Forth with Kendrick in May 2024.", "annotation_id": "32411197", "url": "https://genius.com/32411197", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Too overly ambitious, too late to fix it Too late for condolences when it’s over with I need to start sayin’ shit when I notice it Be open with people I need some closure with Be honest with myself and take ownershi", "vault_lines": ["Too overly ambitious, too late to fix it", "Too late for condolences when it's over with", "I need to start sayin' shit when I notice it", "Be open with people I need some closure with", "Be honest with myself and take ownershi", "Maybe I share it with too many people"], "meaning": "In this lines Drake reflects on the importance of communication and honesty in relationships. He acknowledges that he’s been too focused on his career and hasn’t prioritized his relationships with his friends.\n\nToo overly ambitious, too late to fix it expresses regret for prioritizing success over personal connections.\n\nToo late for condolences when it’s over with suggests that expressing remorse after a relationship ends may be meaningless.\n\nI need to start sayin' shit when I notice it  emphasizes the importance of speaking up about his feelings in the moment.\n\nBe open with people I need some closure with highlights his desire to have honest conversations to find resolution in struggling relationships.\n\nBy acknowledging these shortcomings, Drake conveys a sense of wanting to learn and grow from his experiences.", "annotation_id": "32037215", "url": "https://genius.com/32037215", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "All these number ones and we still not the ones No hard feelings, but I’ll still get you spun", "vault_lines": ["All these number ones and we still not the ones", "No hard feelings, but I'll still get you spun"], "meaning": "Commercial success vs. critical acclaim:  On one hand, it could be seen as a reference to Drake’s immense commercial success, particularly with the global smash hit “One Dance” from his album Views in 2016. “One Dance” topped the Billboard Hot 100 chart and broke streaming records. However, Views received mixed reviews from critics, with some praising its catchy melodies and introspective lyrics, while others found it bloated and lacking in focus.", "annotation_id": "32036942", "url": "https://genius.com/32036942", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "But you’re mindful of it all when your mind full of it all", "vault_lines": ["But you're mindful of it all when your mind full of it all"], "meaning": "In the lyric “But you’re mindful of it all when your mind full of it all,” Drake seems to be expressing a paradoxical situation. It suggests that despite being consumed by thoughts and emotions, there’s still an awareness or mindfulness present. The line conveys the idea of being overwhelmed by everything that’s happening or on one’s mind, yet maintaining a sense of consciousness or awareness about those feelings and circumstances. It touches on the complexity of experiencing intense emotions while trying to stay present and aware amidst the chaos.", "annotation_id": "30443009", "url": "https://genius.com/30443009", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Things get dark", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the second demo version of this song, this line was changed to replace the original, ‘Half a Percocet’ to ‘Things get dark’", "annotation_id": "28968574", "url": "https://genius.com/28968574", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "And never placin’ second And even better knowin’ you’re first but then takin’ second Inspirin’ and never takin’ credit", "vault_lines": ["And never placin' second", "And even better knowin' you're first but then takin' second", "Inspirin' and never takin' credit"], "meaning": "This references a line in the song “6PM in New York” from Drake’s 2015 album, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late:\n\nBein' number two is just being the first to lose\n\nDrake may feel strongly about being at the top, however with this album being released in 2017, it is clear that he has had time to reflect on this approach. Instead, Drake realises that as much as being at the top of the rap game, taking joy in the success of others is just as satisfactory – by redefining success so that it does not entail a slap on the back for himself, but rather the satisfaction of knowing deep inside that he can accomplish an important goal by empowering others to do their best.\n\nDrake has taken many artists under his wing throughout the years, helping boost their fame such as when he jumped on a remix of the song “Wanna Know” by British rapper Dave.", "annotation_id": "16752581", "url": "https://genius.com/16752581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "America’s most wanted, man, I’m still on the run", "vault_lines": ["America's most wanted, man, I'm still on the run"], "meaning": "Drake is describing how his fandom in America keeps him on the road constantly.\n\nAmerica’s Most Wanted is a tv show about dangerous criminals who were, at the time, living on the run. To emphasize how much time he spends touring, he references two recent summer concert tours–Lil Wayne’s 2013 America’s Most Wanted Tour and Jay-Z and Beyonce’s 2014 On the Run Tour.", "annotation_id": "13329567", "url": "https://genius.com/13329567", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "When did all the things I mean From the bottom of my heart start to lose meaning? Maybe I share it with too many people", "vault_lines": ["Did I lose you?", "When did all the things I mean", "From the bottom of my heart start to lose meaning?", "Maybe I share it with too many people"], "meaning": "Here, Drake confesses that his rise to prominence is not only starting to affect his fanbase and treatment from his peers, but it has also affected the way he makes his music. The intimacy Drake shared with his listeners during his earlier projects has slowly started to fade as he has become more successful.\n\nConclusively, his appeal to a wider audience has caused him to lose touch with his early listeners and now even when he says something he really means or feels, people may not believe him because they can’t tell what version of him they’re listening to.", "annotation_id": "13104567", "url": "https://genius.com/13104567", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Winnin’ is problematic People like you more when you workin’ towards somethin’ Not when you have it", "vault_lines": ["People like you more when you workin' towards somethin'", "Not when you have it"], "meaning": "Coming off the chorus Drake starts looking at the reasons why some of his previous pillars of support have started to fall. Examples of this can be seen in all forms of competition whether it be sports, academics & even politics. Drake starts to shows us that it’s no different in Rap.\n\nIf we were to look directly at the example of LeBron James, who tweeted this line in January, he was loved during his early stints in Cleveland from 2003-2009 but faced massive criticism when he won the ring that eluded him in Miami. His dominance in the East since has only draw more criticism. Drake shows no one likes it when you’re always winning", "annotation_id": "13104507", "url": "https://genius.com/13104507", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Two middle fingers as I make a exit, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Two middle fingers as I make a exit, yeah"], "meaning": "This could be referring to a football game in November 2013, when Ohio State’s Marcus Hall was ejected along with 2 other players, one from Michigan and one from Ohio State. As Marcus Hall left the game, he gave the Michigan fans 2 middle fingers.\n\n\n\nDrake referenced Ohio State Buckeyes in his Take Care album when he said:\n\nI see all this money through my Ohio State Buckeyes", "annotation_id": "11907033", "url": "https://genius.com/11907033", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "And puttin’ your trust in someone with the risk of financially sinkin’", "vault_lines": ["And puttin' your trust in someone with the risk of financially sinkin'"], "meaning": "Probably referring to OVO Sound, a Toronto-Based record label, founded by Drake, Noah “40” Shebib, and Oliver “Oli North” El-Khatib.\n\n\n40 is Drake’s longtime friend and producer.\nOli is Drake’s longtime friend, Manager (along with Future the Prince), and OVO brainchild. Oliver oversees the growth of OVO’s clothing line.\n\n\n\nDrake likely provided a good amount of capital to get the label’s line going when it first began, placing his financial well-being in the hand’s of Oliver. The line opened its flagship store in downtown Toronto at the end of 2014.\n\nAnd let’s not forget OVO’s annual OVO Fest, which began in 2010 as a one-time-only show with the hopes of becoming an annual event. OVO Fest is no longer just a concert, hosting an educational seminar as well as a community basketball tournament. OVO Fest probably has a good amount of sponsors these days, but I’m sure Drake’s wallet was heavily relied on in the Fest’s first few years.\n\nIn a 2015 interview with Fader, Oliver stated:\n\n If there is any idea that this is some massive corporate entity that’s worth billions of dollars, then that’s a misconception,” he says, laughing. “There’s a few of us putting our blood, sweat, and tears into this 24/7. It’s a lifestyle. It’s not a job that we all go home from. It’s something that we’ve put our own time and money and equity into, and we’re building it with hammers and nails.  \n\n\n\nThe same article/interview by Fader ends with a quote from 40:\n\nDrake has to stay touring and keep the machine running so that we can help the city. I want to start setting up more speaking engagements, community programs with music programming,” 40 explains. “If he just stops everything he’s doing, what happens next?  \n\n\n\nThis annotation should be linked with the line above it", "annotation_id": "11691908", "url": "https://genius.com/11691908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Why is my struggle different than others’? Only child that’s takin’ care of his mother As health worsens and bills double That’s not respectable all of a sudden? I don’t get a pat on the back for the come up?", "vault_lines": ["Why is my struggle different than others'?", "Only child that’s takin' care of his mother", "As health worsens and bills double", "That’s not respectable all of a sudden?", "I don’t get a pat on the back for the come up?"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake is addressing the criticism that he faced for saying that he “Started from The Bottom”.\n\nWhen Drake was younger his mom became sick and was unable to work so he had to work hard to take care of her. Since his father didn’t live with them, and he was the only child, he held most of the burden. As explained in “Look What You’ve Done”, Drake reveals the devastation of learning about his mom’s illness:\n\nI see painkillers on the kitchen counter\nI hate to see it all hurt so bad\n\nMost criticism concerning Drake’s struggles come from his history being an actor on the TV show Degrassi. Drake didn’t lie about his past, but shows a new perspective: all the money he got from the show all went to his mother, as shown in “Star67”:\n\nI was on TV makin' fifty racks a year\nAfter helpin' mama out, the shit would disappear", "annotation_id": "11689097", "url": "https://genius.com/11689097", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Trust this little light of mine is gonna shine positively", "vault_lines": ["Trust this little light of mine is gonna shine positively"], "meaning": "By making a reference to the gospel song “This Little Light of Mine”, Drake shows that he wants to have a positive attitude amidst hard times.\n\nDrake could also be nodding to Kanye West, as Kanye recited similar lyrics on “Hey Mama”.\n\nhttps://genius.com/34081\n\nKanye was also featured on this album, making it more likely that Drake was shouting out his mentor.", "annotation_id": "11646897", "url": "https://genius.com/11646897", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "How you forget to fill up with gas on the road to riches?", "vault_lines": ["How you forget to fill up with gas on the road to riches?"], "meaning": "Playing off the previous line, as in the “Ignition (Remix)” R. Kelly spends most of the song metaphorically comparing driving a car to having sex (itself a very old trope). Drake instead uses the car metaphor to discuss his drive (no pun intended) to succeed.\n\nDrake has to prepare and put a lot of work and effort into his music to be successful, which he metaphorically likens to remembering to fill up your car with gas before taking it for a drive.\n\nThe specific term “road to riches” has been previously used by Kanye West on “All Falls Down”, and earlier by The Notorious B.I.G. on the chorus to “Real Niggas” (which was later used by Jay-Z for his own “Real Niggaz” and Nas on his song “N.I.G.G.E.R.”)", "annotation_id": "11598275", "url": "https://genius.com/11598275", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "I’m in it for the glory, not the honor mention", "vault_lines": ["I'm in it for the glory, not the honor mention"], "meaning": "This relates to Drake’s earlier line about receiving his high school diploma – similarly he’s not seeking any particular award or distinction (like being on the honour roll for a semester), but rather aiming for having an overall successful career.", "annotation_id": "11598263", "url": "https://genius.com/11598263", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Ego strokin’, picture postin’ Claimin’ that you’d do it for motivational purposes only But you just had to show me See, I know, ’cause I study you closely", "vault_lines": ["What do you see when you see me?", "Ego strokin', picture postin'", "Claimin' that you'd do it for motivational purposes only", "But you just had to show me", "See, I know, 'cause I study you closely"], "meaning": "Meek is known for boasting about his glamorous lifestyle and rise to fame. He used to have the humble brag—“Only For Motivational Purposes ” in his Instagram bio. Drake used this opportunity to exploit him, slowly stalking his prey, watching every move and taking note of Meek’s weaknesses. That’s how he’s managed to stay on top throughout their entire beef.", "annotation_id": "11570238", "url": "https://genius.com/11570238", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "All you did was write the book on garbage-ass Rollies", "vault_lines": ["All you did was write the book on garbage-ass Rollies"], "meaning": "Drake throws some subliminal shade at Meek Mill and his passion for Rolex watches, as expressed on several of his tracks. For example, on “Dreams and Nightmares (Intro)”,\n\nIt was something ‘bout that Rollie When it first touched my wrist\n\n\n\nHe’s mentioned his Rollie in countless other tracks like “Wanna Know”, “Litty”, “Classic”, “Rich Porter”, “Stand Up”… too many to list.\n\nHe also did a track with Ace Hood called “Before the Rollie”!\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ec_n2LkmH2E", "annotation_id": "11564192", "url": "https://genius.com/11564192", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "This is the 6’s year Six hundreds instead of six, OVO You know we’ll be rockin’ our asses out To my big brother Drizzy You know what I’m sayin’? More Life, 6 to the world, gang shit", "vault_lines": ["This is the 6’s year", "Six hundreds instead of six, OVO", "You know we’ll be rockin' our asses out", "To my big brother Drizzy", "You know what I’m sayin'?", "More Life, 6 to the world, gang shit"], "meaning": "600Breezy is affiliate with the Black Disciples gang, most notably the Chicago-based “600” set. Drake is from the “6” (aka Toronto),  so “6” applies to both of them.\n\nIn late 2015, Drake was spotted with 600Breezy and was allegedly doing a certain part of the Black Disciples handshake, sparking rumors that Drake had alleged ties to gang ties as well. However, the following year, Breezy confirmed that it was not even a gang handshake and that people were just blowing the picture out of proportion.", "annotation_id": "11553252", "url": "https://genius.com/11553252", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "And rival neighborhoods linkin’", "vault_lines": ["And icy Heineken drinkin', and rival neighborhoods linkin'"], "meaning": "Over the past year, Drake has made a serious push to unite the Toronto music scene. After Mo-G, Tory Lanez, and other Toronto artists trying to take him down, Drake is urging to band together for the city. Drake and The Weeknd (shouted out earlier in this song) have been together frequently including The Weeknd’s “Reminder” music video.\n\n\n\nCoincidentally, The Weeknd has background vocals in Drake’s 2011 track, “Club Paradise,” where Drake states “[I] need credentials for every one of these Toronto kids. I promised they’d see it with me, we just trying to live.” Drake has always brought a lot of people with him and delivered opportunity to those around him in his city. One of Drake’s primary themes has been to “link” the rival neighborhoods and come together with positivity.", "annotation_id": "11547560", "url": "https://genius.com/11547560", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Not tryna be fourth and inches, I’m tryna go the distance", "vault_lines": ["Not tryna be fourth and inches, I'm tryna go the distance"], "meaning": "In American football, a team gets four “downs,” or plays, to move the ball 10 yards. If they make the yardage, the downs reset, but if they don’t, the other team gets the ball.\n\n“Fourth and inches” means it’s the fourth down and there’s a small distance remaining to achieve the first down. “Going the distance” means not only achieving the first down, but running down the field and scoring the touchdown on the same play. Drake wants to get right to his goals without taking small steps to get there.", "annotation_id": "11540055", "url": "https://genius.com/11540055", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "I don’t care what society thinks. They’re nothing anyway. They’re no better than me. Out there you just have to fit into a pattern that somebody’s already laid out for you. Life we live, you have to set your own patterns, your own ideals. You have to handle the whole job yourself (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is taken from the opening dialogue of the  1966 Biker documentary Satan’s Choice. The person speaking is John ‘Tiny’ Taylor who was the leader of the biker gang “Satan’s Choice.”\n\nHe speaks against the rest of the society that has their life already set out for them. However, he lives a life in which  he has had to set out his own path and form his own morals and ideals.\n\nThe Director of the documentary, Donald Shebib, is the father of Drake’s most prominent producer Noah “40” Shebib.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2WJF862bsRo", "annotation_id": "11534881", "url": "https://genius.com/11534881", "state": "accepted", "votes": 156, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Went and got diplomas and we still goin’ dumb", "vault_lines": ["Went and got diplomas and we still goin' dumb"], "meaning": "Drake dropped out of school in 2002 at age 15. He then went on to appear on the show Degrassi: The Next Generation to support his family. After that, he went to start his new rapping career with the help of his uncle. Ten years later, after his Take Care album release, he graduated from high school with the help of his teacher.\nhttps://twitter.com/drake/status/258731694687920130?lang=en", "annotation_id": "11534811", "url": "https://genius.com/11534811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Did I lose you? Did I? Did I? Did I lose you? Did I? Did I? Did I lose you?", "vault_lines": ["Did I lose you?", "Sometimes I gotta just make sure that I didn't lose you"], "meaning": "Two months before this song was released, LeBron James tweeted the chorus.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/KingJames/status/826338938616942592\n\nThis could be a parallel between both LeBron and Drake’s careers, as they wonder whether or not they’ve lost the support of fans and peers. LeBron has taken tons of public criticism despite his many achievements, on and off the court; people are “bored with his greatness”.\n\nDrake’s overwhelming success has led many to claim that he’s reached his peak, saying that all of the topics he’s discussed are bland, and that he has nothing more to rap about.", "annotation_id": "11532143", "url": "https://genius.com/11532143", "state": "accepted", "votes": 165, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Oli North pull in, like, 10 million a season", "vault_lines": ["Oli North pull in, like, 10 million a season"], "meaning": "Oliver North is the nickname for Oliver El-Khatib,  Drake’s manager, half of October Firm, and OVO co-founder. As the manager of a multiplatinum superstar, Oliver is likely making a considerable income from management fees and the various OVO ventures.\n\nHe also runs & manages OVO flagships stores which would make him a considerable income. Every season, the OVO store would release new streetwear. Drake is claiming that they’re making $10 million in only one season. Oliver claims working on the OVO store is a hard job in a Hypebeast article:\n\nThere’s a few of us putting our blood, sweat, and tears into this 24/7. It’s a lifestyle. It’s not a job that we all go home from. It’s something that we’ve put our own time and money and equity into, and we’re building it with hammers and nails.", "annotation_id": "11531907", "url": "https://genius.com/11531907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "No snow tires, the Range slip-slide like Trick Daddy and Trina", "vault_lines": ["No snow tires, the Range slip-slide like Trick Daddy and Trina"], "meaning": "Trick Daddy and Trina are collaborating rappers from Miami. Both artists were originally signed to the Slip-N-Slide record label. Drake refers to his Range Rover “slipping and sliding” as he has no snow tires.", "annotation_id": "11531683", "url": "https://genius.com/11531683", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "The city gets stronger when everybody is speakin’ Not when everybody out here beefin’", "vault_lines": ["The city gets stronger when everybody is speakin'", "Not when everybody out here beefin'"], "meaning": "Drake wants to unite Toronto instead of having internal conflicts. He’s beefed with fellow Toronto artists before, including Tory Lanez, though Tory has said that their beef was blown out of proportion. Fans even speculated that he and The Weeknd had beef, but Drake shot down those rumors in a 2013 interview with Elliot Wilson.\n\nNot long before More Life dropped, Toronto rapper Mo-G called out Drake for failing to pay him for his work as an OVO ghostwriter. This was amplified by the fact that Drizzy has admittedly co-opted elements of Mo-G’s style, most notably the “Ginobili” dance move, which Drake shouted out on “Summer Sixteen”:\n\nMo-G with the dance moves\nAve Boy with the dance moves", "annotation_id": "11531673", "url": "https://genius.com/11531673", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "If we not on the charts, my XO niggas eatin’ Fifty-two consecutive weekends, shout out to Weeknd", "vault_lines": ["If we not on the charts, my XO niggas eatin'", "Fifty-two consecutive weekends, shout out to Weeknd"], "meaning": "XO is the label created by Drake’s current friend and hit-maker The Weeknd.\n\nBoth Drake and The Weeknd have their songs reach the top of the Top 100s. They both have had number 1 songs with Drake’s “One Dance” and The Weeknd’s “Can’t Feel My Face,” “Starboy,” and “The Hills.”\n\nAlso, the year only has 52 weekends, which means that Drake has never left the charts. In fact, Drake hasn’t left the charts since 2009.", "annotation_id": "11531589", "url": "https://genius.com/11531589", "state": "accepted", "votes": 216, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Queen Street visions that nobody believed in", "vault_lines": ["Queen Street visions that nobody believed in"], "meaning": "Queen Street is a major street located in downtown Toronto, the baseline for the original east-west avenues of Toronto’s grid pattern of major streets. It is filled with centers for Canadian broadcasting, music, fashion, performance, and the visual arts.\n\n\n\nDrake’s surprise pop-up shop in April 2016 was on Queen St.", "annotation_id": "11531530", "url": "https://genius.com/11531530", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "This the remix to “Ignition,” hot and fresh out the kitchen", "vault_lines": ["This the remix to \"Ignition,\" hot and fresh out the kitchen"], "meaning": "Drake interpolates lyrics from R. Kelly’s 2002 hit “Ignition” (Remix).", "annotation_id": "11531529", "url": "https://genius.com/11531529", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Grateful like Jerry, Bob and Mickey", "vault_lines": ["Grateful like Jerry, Bob and Mickey"], "meaning": "This is a reference to psychedelic rock band Grateful Dead and three of its members: Jerry Garcia, Bob Weir, and Mickey Hart.", "annotation_id": "11531374", "url": "https://genius.com/11531374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lose You", "line": "Jealous ones still envy and niggas turn king cobra", "vault_lines": ["Jealous ones still envy and niggas turn king cobra"], "meaning": "This is a callback to his feud with Joe Budden. On his diss track “4pm In Calabasas”, he said:\n\nwhole demeanor just spells envy\n\nAs Budden broke down on his podcast, I’ll Name This Podcast Later, this was a reference to Fat Joe’s “Jealous One’s Envy,” and is now a tribute to his following project, “Jealous Ones Still Envy”. Drake is making sure that everyone knows who won their beef and that Budden is still envious of Drake’s success.", "annotation_id": "11531159", "url": "https://genius.com/11531159", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
