{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Video Girl", "line": "Uh, yea, get in my Slick Rick mode N’a mean?, I’m a tell ya a Story real quick", "vault_lines": ["Uh, yea, get in my Slick Rick mode", "I'm in a bad position", "N'a mean?, I'm a tell ya a Story real quick"], "meaning": "Slick Rick is a Golden Age rapper who is known for being one of hip-hop’s greatest story tellers.  In fact, his best song is called “Children’s Story,” and it is a veritable hip-hop classic.  Slick Rick embraced this praise, with his fourth studio album being titled The Art of Storytelling.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HjNTu8jdukA\n\nDrake is about to drop a story – hopefully on the level of the great Slick Rick.", "annotation_id": "3138363", "url": "https://genius.com/3138363", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Video Girl", "line": "She all up in the closet so much Got a hunch thats shes feelin other women", "vault_lines": ["Chillin on the linen, she all up in the closet so much", "Got a hunch thats shes feelin other women"], "meaning": "This girl spends a lot of time in her closet looking for clothes, which causes Drake to feel like she is “in the closet” and is yet to come out and reveal her homosexuality.", "annotation_id": "222424", "url": "https://genius.com/222424", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Video Girl", "line": "Crib was 2 mil, plus I just bought a couch for ten grand", "vault_lines": ["Crib was 2 mil, plus I just bought a couch for ten grand"], "meaning": "Drake is stunting hard here. This song dropped in 2006, years before Drizzy saw any type of mainstream success, so claiming to have bought a $2 million dollar house is quite the exaggeration.", "annotation_id": "158558", "url": "https://genius.com/158558", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Video Girl", "line": "But you ain’t thandie newton, and this ain’t crash here", "vault_lines": ["But you ain't thandie newton, and this ain't crash here"], "meaning": "This line goes with the 2 lines before it. In the movie Crash, Thandie Newton’s character gets patted down and molested by a police officer played by Matt Dillon.", "annotation_id": "158554", "url": "https://genius.com/158554", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Video Girl", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 6, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-video-girl-lyrics", "genius_title": "Video Girl"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Windows of opportunity, let me go through the doors", "vault_lines": ["**** windows of opportunity, let me go through the doors"], "meaning": "“Window(s) of opportunity” is a saying that refers to a short, limited period of time during which a specific action or decision can be taken to achieve success or benefit.\n\nHere, Drake isn’t going to wait for the opportunity to present itself since the “window” is relatively short or quick to disappear. Instead, he’ll go through the doors -metaphorically speaking- to signify that he won’t stop until he’s reached his goals, and become even more succesful. So many opportunities are presenting themselves to Drake because he’s putting in the work to reach his pinnacle.\n\nAlso, a door is wider than a window so the chances of reaching those opportunities are bigger and a lot more attainable.", "annotation_id": "36252322", "url": "https://genius.com/36252322", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "I remain unfazed, trust, worse has been done", "vault_lines": ["I remain unfazed, trust, worse has been done"], "meaning": "This line can be interpreted as a dialog as Drake reassures us he’s being transparent, relieving the moments of the beef between him and Pusha T, complementing the previous line as it resulted in Drake being exposed for hiding his kid from the world.\n\nTo recap, he tells us worse has happened; that he lived through worse and now remains unfazed when faced upon easier situations.", "annotation_id": "35776820", "url": "https://genius.com/35776820", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "I been hot since the birth of my son", "vault_lines": ["I been hot since the birth of my son"], "meaning": "Drake is alluding to the aftermath of the start of his paternity journey, mentioning his son, Adonis Mahbed Graham, who was born on October 11, 2017. After previous speculations of Drake having a kid, he officially announced his paternity through his 2018 album, Scorpion. Being “hot” serves as a meaning to Drake being on alert; attentive.\n\nTo recap, Drake confirms ever since Adonis was born, he’s been on alert and presumably ready for any confrontation or chances to dismiss his credibility or taint his reputation.", "annotation_id": "35776749", "url": "https://genius.com/35776749", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Career is going great, but now the rest of me’s fading slowly", "vault_lines": ["Career is going great, but now the rest of me's fading slowly"], "meaning": "Drake may be alluding to the concept of an existential crisis. Despite the success he is achieved Drake still is fighting his own personal battles.\n\nThis can be a reflection on how that peak of fame and success that Drake has reached takes a tole on one’s well being.", "annotation_id": "30406105", "url": "https://genius.com/30406105", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "And if the last negotiation had you feeling out of pocket Well, this is the perfect time that I empty them shits for sure You owe that shit to the boy, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I love you, I love you, I love you", "I need you, I need you, I need you", "That shit is wearin' me out", "And if the last negotiation had you feeling out of pocket", "Well, this is the perfect time that I empty them shits for sure", "You owe that shit to the boy, yeah"], "meaning": "After reflecting on the shit he’s been going through on the second half of the song, Drake decides to transform into a giant climax for the end of the song.", "annotation_id": "30406047", "url": "https://genius.com/30406047", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Built this house for us all, pain in my back still", "vault_lines": ["Built this house for us all, pain in my back still"], "meaning": "Building a house requires laborious tasks that often have an emphasis on being hunched over or bending at the back, which notoriously cause back pain.\n\nDrake claims that through years of hard work and perserverance, he’s been able to put his friends and families into high-class positions that they otherwise couldn’t do themselves.", "annotation_id": "30121640", "url": "https://genius.com/30121640", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "And nothin’ tell the truth like the eyes will", "vault_lines": ["And nothin' tell the truth like the eyes will"], "meaning": "A person’s eyes are able to reveal their level of confidence, assertiveness — and even levels of honesty. Hence, Drake’s statement that “nothin' tell the truth like the eyes will”.", "annotation_id": "30121619", "url": "https://genius.com/30121619", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Seen too many brothers get twenty-five from the boys I’d rather see all of ’em get twenty-five from the Lord And if the last negotiation made you pay me twenty-five Well, this is the perfect time to give me twenty-five more", "vault_lines": ["What you mean to me", "Seen too many brothers get twenty-five from the boys", "I'd rather see all of 'em get twenty-five from the Lord", "And if the last negotiation made you pay me twenty-five", "Well, this is the perfect time to give me twenty-five more", "And if the last negotiation had you feeling out of pocket"], "meaning": "25 from the boys means 25 years in jail from the police. Being in rap, a lot of rappers haven’t been making legal money for a long time it’s only bound for their actions catch up to them.\n\n25 from the Lord means 25 more years of life from God. Drake is saying rather than 25 years in jail, it’d be better to receive 25 more years of life from Our Father, Who art in heaven.\n\nThe last 25, Drake explains how good his negotiating skills are to get paid 25 (whatever dollar amount), that he should be receiving double, or 25 more", "annotation_id": "29070806", "url": "https://genius.com/29070806", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "I’d have to be dead for them to say that you took it from me", "vault_lines": ["I'd have to be dead for them to say that you took it from me", "What you mean to me"], "meaning": "As Drake says, “Over My Dead Body”.", "annotation_id": "26095741", "url": "https://genius.com/26095741", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Champagne poetry, these are the effortless flows, supposedly Something else is controlling me", "vault_lines": ["Champagne poetry, these are the effortless flows, supposedly", "Something else is controlling me"], "meaning": "Drake revisits his infamous ghostwriting rumors with this line. The rumor was first popularized by Meek Mill causing a beef between the two artists back in 2015. These lines however don’t refer to Meek since the two squashed their beef & collaborated on Going Bad back in 2018.\n\nThese lines actually refer to Pusha T bringing these allegations back to light in his album Daytona. Pusha sent subliminal hints throughout the album, even naming the 6th song, “What Would Meek Do”.\n\nThe final song on the album, Infared is where the allegations came to light", "annotation_id": "25638729", "url": "https://genius.com/25638729", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "No fair what Drizzy made on the second leg of the tour", "vault_lines": ["No fair what Drizzy made on the second leg of the tour"], "meaning": "In early 2019 Drake went on his “Assassination Vacation” tour in Europe. The tour ended up grossing over $33 Million, in 23 shows. The tour before that in North America with Migos (2018) grossed over $79 Million.", "annotation_id": "24387272", "url": "https://genius.com/24387272", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "CJ grab racks out the bag and throw it to me", "vault_lines": ["CJ grab racks out the bag and throw it to me"], "meaning": "CJ = CJ Gibson, Drake’s tour manager and “body man”.\n\nIn this line he’s stating that CJ holds the bag with money, and whenever Drake needs some, he throws him thousands of dollars.\n\n\n\nFlyer from 2019 party thrown in CJ’s hometown of  Jackson, Mississippi", "annotation_id": "24367250", "url": "https://genius.com/24367250", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Under her pictures live some of the greatest quotes from me", "vault_lines": ["Under her pictures live some of the greatest quotes from me"], "meaning": "Drake is commenting on how popular it is for females to use his lyrics as captions on Instagram.", "annotation_id": "24367242", "url": "https://genius.com/24367242", "state": "pending", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Ollie got the first edition parked up roadside", "vault_lines": ["Ollie got the first еdition parked up roadside"], "meaning": "Ollie is Oliver El-Khatib, OVO co-founder and Drake’s manager.\n\nDrizzy is saying in this line that Oliver gets the newest and most exclusive cars very early.", "annotation_id": "24367148", "url": "https://genius.com/24367148", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Man, fuck a valuation, show me personal funds", "vault_lines": ["Man, fuck a valuation, show me personal funds"], "meaning": "This line is a slight dig at Kanye, who Drake has been engaged in a simmering “beef” with over the past few years. At various times Ye has claimed to be a worth as much as $6 billion. When Forbes took a look at his financials in 2020, they placed his net worth closer to $1.3 billion with the majority of that based on his ownership stake in YEEZY.\n\nDrake is commenting on the valuation of a company vs money actually inside a personal bank account.\n\nEdit: Ye & Drake have since reconciled with a joint concert – The Larry Hoover concert in Los Angeles on December 9, 2021", "annotation_id": "24367032", "url": "https://genius.com/24367032", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Under me, I see all the people that claim they over me", "vault_lines": ["Under me, I see all the people that claim they over me"], "meaning": "This line has two potential meanings. More literally, in the spirit of the album title, Certified Lover Boy, Drake may be alluding to old romantic partners who claim they’ve moved on from their relationship with Drake but are still under him—literally, as they’re still sleeping with him.\n\nHe could also target this line at the numerous nemeses and rivals that surround him in the rap game. The industry is competitive by nature and many rappers have attempted to prove their position above Drake publicly whether through songs or interviews. Here, Drake humbles them by reminding them that they are still yet to reach him… if they can do it at all.\n\nThe evidence seems to be clear. Drake holds an outstanding and undisputed career studded with prestigious achievements. He was ranked at No. 1 on Billboard’s Top Artists of the 2010s chart, thanks to his decade-long dominance on the Billboard Hot 100 songs chart and the Billboard 200 albums tally.\n\nhttps://thumbor.forbes.com/thumbor/960x0/https%3A%2F%2Fspecials-images.forbesimg.com%2Fimageserve%2F686541504%2F960x0.jpg%3Ffit%3Dscale", "annotation_id": "23939222", "url": "https://genius.com/23939222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Future sign the contracts, he don’t even show it to me", "vault_lines": ["Future sign the contracts, he don't even show it to me"], "meaning": "Not to be confused with rapper Future who is a frequent collaborator on multiple Drake songs. In this verse Drake is referring to Future the Prince – Drizzy’s DJ and OVO member.\n\nOn Nonstop, Drake references Future:\n\nFuture took the business and ran it for me\nI let Ollie take the owl, told him brand it for me", "annotation_id": "23900093", "url": "https://genius.com/23900093", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "I used to hide my pain in Delilah behind the bar ’til my niggas carried me out", "vault_lines": ["I used to hide my pain in Delilah behind the bar 'til my niggas carried me out"], "meaning": "Delilah is an upscale restaurant and nightclub in LA where Drake frequently visits.", "annotation_id": "23899706", "url": "https://genius.com/23899706", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Trust in my brothers is as strong as I know it should be", "vault_lines": ["Trust in my brothers is as strong as I know it should be"], "meaning": "“Know it should be” sounds very similar to the name of Noah Shebib, otherwise known as 40, Drake’s longtime friend and producer.", "annotation_id": "23894672", "url": "https://genius.com/23894672", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "You niggas gassed up, you couldn’t pay the", "vault_lines": ["You niggas gassed up, you couldn't pay the"], "meaning": "It’s implied here that Drake is going to rhyme “gas bill,” but he cleverly leaves the line open to interpretation, letting the sample talk here.", "annotation_id": "23879566", "url": "https://genius.com/23879566", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "How the **** do we manage to win everything but awards?", "vault_lines": ["How the **** do we manage to win everything but awards?"], "meaning": "Drake’s won a number of awards over the course of his career, so it’s possible he’s specifically referring to his performance at the Grammys. Despite the repeated huge commercial success of his albums, he tends to win less Grammys than other similarly popular artists in the zeitgeist. Drake has just four total Grammy wins, whereas, for example, Kendrick Lamar has 13 wins, Kanye West has 22 wins, and JAY-Z has 23 wins. As of Certified Lover Boy’s release, Drake’s latest Grammy win was Best Rap Song for “God’s Plan” in 2019.\n\nDrake most likely censors the expletive here as a callback to a line earlier in the verse where he raps:\n\nAnd I always censor myself ‘cause no matter what, they reporting on me", "annotation_id": "23876933", "url": "https://genius.com/23876933", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "I love you, I love you, I love you Until I, until I I love you, I love you, I love you Until I find a Say the only words I know that you’ll", "vault_lines": ["I love you, I love you, I love you", "I need you, I need you, I need you", "Say the only words I know that you'll"], "meaning": "The introduction of “Champagne Poetry” samples the intro from Masego’s 2017 song “Navajo”, which, in turn, samples The Singers Unlimited’s cover of  “Michelle” by The Beatles, released in their 1972 album A Capella.\n\nThe Beatles original track was released in December 1965 on their sixth studio album, Rubber Soul.", "annotation_id": "23876635", "url": "https://genius.com/23876635", "state": "accepted", "votes": 228, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "The city’s on fire and people are in denial Charges being laid, but we’ll see what they do at trial I’m calling this shit from now Sweetheart deals that the judges been handing down", "vault_lines": ["The city's on fire and people are in denial", "Charges being laid, but we'll see what they do at trial", "I'm calling this shit from now", "Sweetheart deals that the judges been handing down"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing the protests and riots that have occurred across the world in recent years due to people unhappy with policing efforts and denying what’s actually happened. In recent years, specifically, many people have been unhappy when someone is brought to justice because the judges let them off with a “slap on the wrist\".", "annotation_id": "23876242", "url": "https://genius.com/23876242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "I know I tend to talk about how I got a fortune on me But with that comes the politics the city been forcing on me Man, I can’t even R.I.P. and show my remorse to the homie Know I carry the guilt of the city’s misfortunes on me", "vault_lines": ["I know I tend to talk about how I got a fortune on me", "But with that comes the politics the city been forcing on me", "Man, I can't even R.I.P. and show my remorse to the homie", "Know I carry the guilt of the city's misfortunes on me", "I even got the cleanin' staff plotting extortion on me"], "meaning": "Because of Drake’s massive influence and superstardom, the City of Toronto and its government officials often pressure Drake to align his actions with their desired politics. Everything from how he displays his wealth to who he associates with and how he mourns is publicly scrutinized.\n\nFor example, Drake was criticized by the Toronto Star for being “tone deaf” after he released his video for “Toosie Slide” dancing in his mansion during the COVID-19 pandemic, in which many people were left unemployed.\n\nIn 2017, Drake’s friend Anthony “Fif” Soares was gunned down in Toronto. Toronto police publicly criticized Drake for not using his public platform to help solve the murder.  That same year Drake was also chided for associating with Toronto rapper Pressa, who was out on bail on serious charges.\n\nA year later, Drake was publicly criticized by Toronto’s Chief of Police after being photographed wearing a “Hells Angels” hoodie.", "annotation_id": "23875371", "url": "https://genius.com/23875371", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "And if money’s all I need in my grave, then bury me now", "vault_lines": ["I need you, I need you, I need you", "And if money's all I need in my grave, then bury me now"], "meaning": "In this line Drake references his Rick Ross-assisted June 2019 single “Money In The Grave” ;\n\nWhen I die, put my money in the grave\n\nDrake might be boasting about how much money he has; enough to last him a lifetime. He’s already achieved his goal of getting rich, so if that was the ultimate goal, he’s ready to be buried already.", "annotation_id": "23874339", "url": "https://genius.com/23874339", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "line": "Co-parent of the year, we figured out a rapport", "vault_lines": ["Co-parent of the year, we figured out a rapport"], "meaning": "This is a direct response to all those who doubted Drake’s love for his son after Pusha T, who exposed Drake for hiding his child with Sophie Brussaux, an artist and former porn star.\n\nOn “The Story of Adidon,” Pusha rapped:\n\nSophie knows better as your baby mother\nCleaned her up for IG, but the stench is on her\nA baby’s involved, it’s deeper than rap\nWe talkin' character, let me keep with the facts\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\n\nDrake responds by claiming that he has a harmonious relationship with the mother of his son which benefits his son and his present, making him ‘Co-parent of the year’.\n\nDrake has figured out a way to take care of his child without it seeming like he is being ‘hidden’.\nNotable in his song ‘Emotionless’ where he claims:\n\nI wasn’t hidin' my kid from the world\nI was hidin' the world from my kid\n\nWe see a contrast in Drake’s action with him having walked on stage hand-in-hand with Adonis as he accepted the award for Artist of the Decade.", "annotation_id": "23874214", "url": "https://genius.com/23874214", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Champagne Poetry", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 26, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-champagne-poetry-lyrics", "genius_title": "Champagne Poetry"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I’m sleeping with death on my mind ’Cause the shots that I called in my time", "vault_lines": ["I'm sleeping with death on my mind", "'Cause the shots that I called in my time"], "meaning": "double entendre at min.\n\nsleeping bad because of the hits he has called on others. Drake has big paper and a hit would be nothing to drop for someone like him.\n\nhe is sleeping bad because he is a boss in the music game and has called shots(made decisions) that some people may not like and want to kill him physically, emotionally, career wise etc\n\nBig Bars from Drake", "annotation_id": "40935432", "url": "https://genius.com/40935432", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I’m a god", "vault_lines": ["'Cause she can't even see I'm a god"], "meaning": "The correct bar is “I’m a guy”.", "annotation_id": "40245035", "url": "https://genius.com/40245035", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "And karma has been on my side But I also just heard that she’s blind", "vault_lines": ["I tried and tried and tried", "And karma has been on my side", "But I also just heard that she's blind"], "meaning": "Karma is helping him win, but if karma is also “blind,” then it isn’t rewarding people based on who’s good or bad. Drake could be suggesting that his success isn’t necessarily because he deserves it—he’s just benefiting from fate.\nIt plays on the idea that Lady Justice, often depicted wearing a blindfold, judges everyone equally. Drake blends karma and justice into one concept, suggesting the universe is ruling in his favor.", "annotation_id": "40102121", "url": "https://genius.com/40102121", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "Bobby from 6 want bro And he under my arm like I’m ticklish", "vault_lines": ["Bobby from 6 want bro", "And he under my arm like I'm ticklish"], "meaning": "The correct lyric is “Bobby from 6-1  bro.” Drake is referencing Smiley, who is a gang member from Pelham/61/B.O.B, something reflected in his Instagram handle, @smiley61st being his close friend.\n\nThe name Bobby is a stylized version of the nickname Drake gave Smiley last year “Bob Jack”.\n\nWhen Drake says he’s “under my arm like I’m ticklish,” it’s a double entendre. On one level, it plays on the physical location of being under someone’s arm. On another, it highlights how closely Smiley is aligned with Drake, emphasizing their strong personal and professional relationship.\n\nDespite frequent criticism of Smiley’s unconventional vocal style, Drake has consistently supported him throughout his career. He has collaborated with Smiley on multiple occasions and continues to give him major looks, most recently appearing alongside him on “2 Mazza.”", "annotation_id": "40062343", "url": "https://genius.com/40062343", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "If I free up, my bro gotta live with it ’Cause we know that that man isn’t innocent If they sayin’ he did it, he did the shit But he still might come home in a little bit", "vault_lines": ["'Cause the shots that I called in my time", "If I free up, my bro gotta live with it", "'Cause we know that that man isn't innocent", "The worst that I felt in a while", "If they sayin' he did it, he did the shit", "But he still might come home in a little bit", "'Cause I know that they'll never consider it", "They gotta do it in increments"], "meaning": "This is referring to Drakes friend Bakanotnice who has been criticised for his legal cases referred to famously by Kendrick Lamar in Not like us. Drake is acknowledging his friend may have participated in wrongdoing and if he is guilty he will bare the consequences however he may come home quickly regardless.\n\nhttps://www.complex.com/music/a/backwoodsaltar/what-is-baka-not-nice-weird-case-kendrick-lamar-drake-diss", "annotation_id": "40001815", "url": "https://genius.com/40001815", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "And they’ll act like I lost my appeal But they’ll pay me for changin’ they mind", "vault_lines": ["I tried and tried and tried", "When they pay me for wastin' my time", "And they'll act like I lost my appeal", "But they'll pay me for changin' they mind", "And act like they fuckin' invisible"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that UMG has been treating him like he’s lost his place at the top, but once he proves otherwise, they’ll come back offering him a new contract.", "annotation_id": "39994293", "url": "https://genius.com/39994293", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "They’ll frame it as people retired But we know what’s a truth and a lie", "vault_lines": ["They'll frame it as people retired", "But we know what's a truth and a lie"], "meaning": "Once the person/people referenced in the previous line are fired, there will be an attempt to make the public narrative be that their exit was a personal choice.", "annotation_id": "39966483", "url": "https://genius.com/39966483", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "The lawsuit I got is fried, ’til the R switched place with the I", "vault_lines": ["'Til the R switched place with the I", "The lawsuit I got is fried, 'til the R switched place with the I"], "meaning": "When you switch the letters “R” and “I” in “fried”, you get “fired”. Based on the following line, this suggests that even though the legal situation seemingly has not been going well for him, someone will be getting fired/replaced for how the situation has gone down.", "annotation_id": "39966386", "url": "https://genius.com/39966386", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "Freeze the world, freeze the world Freeze the world, freeze the world Freeze the—", "vault_lines": ["And the whole world started to chime", "Freeze the world, freeze the world"], "meaning": "Iconic snippet that was heavily used during the ICEMAN rollout and marketing campaigns.\n\nhttps://genius.com/Drake-freeze-the-world-lyrics\n\nPlaying onto the lyrics, the CN Tower was frozen during Episode Four of iceman, to signify “freezing the world.”", "annotation_id": "39944627", "url": "https://genius.com/39944627", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "We don’t even want what they givin’ us Them shits don’t even have no significance", "vault_lines": ["We don't even want what they givin' us", "Them shits don't even have no significance"], "meaning": "Drake dismisses the Grammys as meaningless to him and his camp.\n\nThis line continues his long-running skepticism of the Recording Academy, despite his commercial success and multiple wins. He makes the same point on “First Person Shooter”:\n\nI told Jimmy Jam I use a Grammy as a door stop", "annotation_id": "39914401", "url": "https://genius.com/39914401", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "And they’ll act like we signin’ a deal When they pay me for wastin’ my time", "vault_lines": ["And they'll act like we signin' a deal", "When they pay me for wastin' my time", "And they'll act like I lost my appeal", "But they'll pay me for changin' they mind", "And act like they fuckin' invisible"], "meaning": "This line is a jab at Universal Music Group (UMG)Chairman & CEO: Sir Lucian Grainge. Drake’s deal ended with Cash Money(also distributed by UMG) after his last album and Drake accuses Lucian of negotiating a new deal between him and UMG in bad faith. UMG is also the parent company of Aftermath/Interscope and PGlang (Kendrick Lamar’s record label).\n\nDrake filed a federal defamation lawsuit against his own record label, Universal Music Group (UMG), for distributing and promoting Kendrick Lamar’s hit diss track “Not Like Us”. He accused the label of amplifying a track that contained false, highly damaging pedophilia allegations and encouraged violent retribution. Drake argued that UMG knew these claims were false but aggressively promoted the song—including during Lamar’s Super Bowl and Grammy performances—to the detriment of Drake’s brand and safety.\n\nThese allegations greatly diminished the value of Drake’s OVO label negotiations with UMG for the benefit of cost savings to Sir Lucian Grainge.", "annotation_id": "39889948", "url": "https://genius.com/39889948", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I’ve taken an eye for an eye I’ve sent a boy up to the sky", "vault_lines": ["I've sent a boy up to the sky", "I've taken an eye for an eye"], "meaning": "“An eye for an eye\" is Drake saying he’s already retaliated for whatever was done to him. He has already gotten revenge and returned the favor.\n\nThe phrase comes from a biblical principle that punishment should match the crime. It appears three times in the Old Testament as a standard for judicial fairness, though Jesus later rejects that principle in the New Testament.\n\n“Sent a boy up to the sky” is a dark metaphor for ending someone’s career and leaving them finished.", "annotation_id": "39889794", "url": "https://genius.com/39889794", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "If a Rolex I want ’bout to drop", "vault_lines": ["If a Rolex I want 'bout to drop"], "meaning": "Drake has a double entendre in this line; If a new model of Rolex is about to come out, he makes it his, the first one to ever own that Rolex in that make and model.\n\nWhile Reserve is a reference to Reservations which are home to Indigenous(Native) People of Canada. There are no “Native American’s” in Canada, only Indigenous people, the first people of the land.", "annotation_id": "39889696", "url": "https://genius.com/39889696", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "Ayo, Gucci get back in the ride ’Cause that’s definitely not the guy Mistaken identity mixed with adrenaline, almost just kissed you goodbye", "vault_lines": ["Ayo, Gucci get back in the ride", "'Cause that's definitely not the guy", "Mistaken identity mixed with adrenaline, almost just kissed you goodbye"], "meaning": "Riding with TVGUCCI, an OVO-affiliated artist Drake previously shouted out on “Middle of the Ocean” – “Shout out TVGUCCI, my cousin is spooky” – Drake recounts a real near-miss where someone outside the car is almost mistaken for an opp, a misread that almost turned fatal.", "annotation_id": "39844990", "url": "https://genius.com/39844990", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "Pressure been getting applied and I know I been bad with replies", "vault_lines": ["I tried and tried and tried", "Pressure been getting applied and I know I been bad with replies"], "meaning": "On this line, Drake addresses the immense professional and personal pressure he has faced, while offering a rare, vulnerable apology for being slow to reply to those in his inner circle.\n\nThe bar works on two levels.\n\n“Pressure been getting applied” is a nod to the non-stop warfare Drake has endured since the 2024 rap beef. From Kendrick Lamar’s onslaught to A$AP Rocky’s shots on Don’t Be Dumb, Drake has been under unprecedented industry scrutiny. Instead of denying it, he subtly acknowledges that the pressure has been heavy and constant, forcing him into a defensive, hyper-focused state.\n\n“I know I been bad with replies” references a well-documented personal habit: isolating himself and cutting off communication during high-stress periods. Drake has used his music to address this before, such as on 2018’s “Emotionless” where he rapped about being unable to keep up with messages.\n\nThe irony is that while his close circle gets left on read, random fans occasionally get the opposite treatment. In October 2025, a high school senior named Seth messaged Drake on Instagram asking for a senior quote. Instead of ghosting him, Drake replied with a full custom 6-bar verse in the DMs:\n\nDid there. Been that. Thanks for all the knowledge\nSeth move like liquid baby but Seth kept it solid\n5 deep in the whip plotting on them dollas\nMe I’m sitting backseat stoned in the middle like a Olive\nPraying all the broskis can dodge life’s problems\nProblem is more kids tryna go viral than go to college…\n\n\n\nBy admitting he’s usually bad with replies, Drake makes those rare moments of direct fan interaction feel even more deliberate and legendary.", "annotation_id": "39836586", "url": "https://genius.com/39836586", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "[Outro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The outro was played as the outro for the ICEMAN EPISODE 3, on September 4, 2025. Fans theorized it would be a entire song but ended up being the outro of the album.", "annotation_id": "39833158", "url": "https://genius.com/39833158", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I’m glad that you think that I’m soft I’m glad that you think that I’m shy", "vault_lines": ["I'm glad that you think that I'm soft", "I'm glad that you think that I'm shy"], "meaning": "Drake is leaning towards the online/media image he has adopted for the past 15 years as the funny-emotional-singing rapper and says that his good reputation benefits him in a variety of ways. These lines are followed by him bragging about almost getting someone killed and “sending someone up to the sky” puts the contrast in perspective.\n\nThis could also reference back to his “8 out of 10” line from Scorpion (2018) “As luck would have it I’ve settled into my role as the good guy” where he bragged about his good reputation benefiting him after the Pusha-T beef.", "annotation_id": "39832410", "url": "https://genius.com/39832410", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "Not scared of no suit or no tie ’Cause shit, victory always been mine", "vault_lines": ["Not scared of no suit or no tie", "'Cause shit, victory always been mine"], "meaning": "In the context of his ongoing lawsuit against UMG, alleging they sided against him during the beef, Drake’s already confident of the outcome. The suit and tie is the uniform of the opposition (whether that’s the lawyers or the executives he’s taking to court.)\n\n“Suit & tie” is impossible to separate from Justin Timberlake’s 2013 record of the same name, which features JAY-Z. Given that JAY has been a consistent behind-the-scenes presence every time Drake finds himself under fire.", "annotation_id": "39819779", "url": "https://genius.com/39819779", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "My bros wanna mob on the stage But I tell them boys, “Nah, have some discipline”", "vault_lines": ["My bros wanna mob on the stage", "But I tell them boys, \"Nah, have some discipline\""], "meaning": "Drake is pulling rank on his own crew telling them to fall back, stay composed, not let the moment override the bigger picture.\n\nThe line carries real weight given what happened at his Wireless Festival headline set in London in July 2025. Toronto rapper and Drake affiliate Top5 was stabbed following Drake’s performance at the festival, with the Metropolitan Police confirming he was beaten and stabbed by a group of individuals near the festival grounds shortly after Drake closed out his three-day takeover. The incident added to a growing list of controversies surrounding Top5, who had previously been charged as the alleged triggerman in a 2021 fatal shooting, charges later dropped, and was arrested again on firearms charges in January 2025.\n\n\n\nThe line could also be a subtle nod to Kanye West’s infamous 2015 BRIT Awards performance, where his stage invasion was widely criticized as chaotic and self-indulgent, a cautionary tale of what happens when there’s no discipline backstage.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/_ABk7TmjnVk?si=hDEANl8f4e7Tgtwf\n\nWhen you’re running with people who attract that level of chaos, discipline isn’t optional, it’s survival. Drake telling his bros to hold back isn’t a sign of weakness. It’s the only way to keep the show going.", "annotation_id": "39817809", "url": "https://genius.com/39817809", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I tried and tried and tried ’Til the R switched place with the I", "vault_lines": ["I tried and tried and tried", "'Til the R switched place with the I", "The lawsuit I got is fried, 'til the R switched place with the I"], "meaning": "It’s a wordplay on the word “tried.”\n\nIf the R and I switch places in tried, it becomes tired.\n\nSo the lyric means:\n\n“I kept trying so much that I became tired/exhausted.”", "annotation_id": "39812149", "url": "https://genius.com/39812149", "state": "pending", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I act like the money’s unlimited", "vault_lines": ["I act like the money's unlimited"], "meaning": "Refers to the ICEMAN rollout, one of the most expensive and elaborate album campaigns in hip-hop history.\n\nPhysical promotion included a 1.2 million pound ice sculpture installed at 81 Bond Street in downtown Toronto, with the album’s release date hidden inside.\n\n\n\nStreamer Kishka eventually retrieved it and was rewarded $50,000 by Drake’s team.\n\nEpisode 4 of the ICEMAN livestream series used 75 synchronized media servers generating 2.57 million lumens to make the CN Tower appear to freeze from bottom to top while new music played.\n\n\n\nDrake told Complex that a traditional album rollout felt redundant, saying he wanted “the perfect mix of risk and reward.”\n\nThe “act like” matters here: the rollout was not reckless, but calculated confidence. Every dollar spent made the same point: nobody else was doing this, few could afford to think this big, and even fewer would bet that heavily on themselves.", "annotation_id": "39809699", "url": "https://genius.com/39809699", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "That bitch might just switch on a dime", "vault_lines": ["That bitch might just switch on a dime"], "meaning": "A dime has two sides, a tail and heads, which goes into his next line about her not seeing “the guy” which is the heads in this scenario. She’s looking at the tail, the wrong side of the dime.", "annotation_id": "39807707", "url": "https://genius.com/39807707", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "Niggas gon’ see me at birds", "vault_lines": ["Niggas gon' see me at birds"], "meaning": "Drake might be talking about people seeing him at The Bird Streets which is a neighborhood in west Hollywood Hills or the bar/club called The Bird Streets.", "annotation_id": "39803390", "url": "https://genius.com/39803390", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "What happened to Drake with the innocence? I don’t think we’ll be seein’ him again", "vault_lines": ["What happened to Drake from 2009", "What happened to Drake with the innocence?", "I don't think we'll be seein' him again"], "meaning": "In this lyric, Drake is talking to the fans who think the “new Drake” will never compare to the older Drake from albums like Views or Take Care. A lot of fans expect his newer music to sound and feel the same as those older albums because that’s the version of Drake they are most used to. Back then, Drake was seen as more “innocent” since he wasn’t nearly as famous or successful as he is now.\nToday, Drake openly lives a luxurious lifestyle living in a penthouse and gifting the finest woman you have ever seen a $100K purse. Because of his fame, age, and everything he has experienced throughout his music career, he can’t really be as relatable as he once was, which can be seen on Churchill Downs when he says, “I’m gettin' so rich, my music’s not even relatable”. He’s making the point that the old Drake is never going to be the same as the new Drake, and that’s something fans just have to accept.", "annotation_id": "39792333", "url": "https://genius.com/39792333", "state": "pending", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "And bro just touched down in the States Took a jetski from Windsor to Michigan", "vault_lines": ["And bro just touched down in the States", "Took a jetski from Windsor to Michigan"], "meaning": "Windsor and Detroit are roughly 1 mile apart at their closest points across the Detroit River, making the jetski trip geographically plausible and relatively short.", "annotation_id": "39790887", "url": "https://genius.com/39790887", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I’ll never forget that July The worst that I felt in a while", "vault_lines": ["I'll never forget that July", "The worst that I felt in a while"], "meaning": "Mid-2024 was arguably the lowest point of the entire post-beef for Drake. The abandonment from people he considered close was accumulating, and unlike the heat of the actual beef, July was the quiet aftermath.\n\nAlso July 4, 2024 was the day Kendrick Lamar released the music video for “Not Like Us,” a diss to Drake where he accused him of being a “certified pedophile,” which increased the song’s popularity and continued its dominance at the forefront of pop culture.\n\nThe way Drake pronounces “that July” is a play on “that you lied,” which could be alluding to either the people in his inner circle performing loyalty they didn’t mean or his opponents lying on his name. This homophone is reminscent of “Slime You Out”:\n\nJuly, that’s when I found out you lied", "annotation_id": "39779176", "url": "https://genius.com/39779176", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "These niggas recoupin’ on 2012 They gotta do it in increments", "vault_lines": ["These niggas recoupin' on 2012", "They gotta do it in increments"], "meaning": "A direct response to Kendrick Lamar’s diss track, “euphoria“:\n\n“Yeah, my first one like my last one, it’s a classic, you don’t have one.”\n\nDrake appears to mock Kendrick for constantly leaning on the legacy of Good Kid, M.A.A.D City, released in 2012, implying that he is still “recouping” off the acclaim and reputation of that era “over a decade later“.", "annotation_id": "39778405", "url": "https://genius.com/39778405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "This album, I’m never submittin’ it ’Cause I know that they’ll never consider it This might be the one year I won ’Cause I know how they like to position it", "vault_lines": ["I reserve it like I'm aboriginal", "This album, I'm never submittin' it", "'Cause I know that they'll never consider it", "This might be the one year I won", "'Cause I know how they like to position it"], "meaning": "Drake references his “ongoing issues with the Grammy Awards” and suggests that ICEMAN will not be submitted for consideration. This carries added weight given his legal dispute with Universal Music Group.\n\nHe has repeatedly criticized the Grammys as “disconnected from cultural impact”, accused them of favoring certain artists, and mocked that favoritism.\n\n\n\nLike the Oscars, the Grammys are widely seen as requiring a well-funded campaign backed by the artist’s label or studio. Given Drake’s current relationship with UMG, he does not think submitting ICEMAN for consideration would be worthwhile.", "annotation_id": "39778202", "url": "https://genius.com/39778202", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "Fifty millimeter president For all the times shit got political", "vault_lines": ["For all the times shit got political"], "meaning": "50mm also refers to guns that shoot a 50mm bullet- he reserved for the times shit gets political. A Rolex President is a watch, and he says political too, another reference to Rolex.", "annotation_id": "39778140", "url": "https://genius.com/39778140", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "What happened to Drake from 2009 When all of the moments was intimate?", "vault_lines": ["What happened to Drake from 2009", "When all of the moments was intimate?", "What happened to Drake with the innocence?"], "meaning": "Drake directly acknowledges the change in his demeanor and lyrical content since the days of “So Far Gone,” blaming the lack of intimacy and sincerity in his current persona and recent releases on the personal betrayals and hardships he’s been exposed to in the time since then. The “Iceman” persona that serves as the running theme of the album is being deliberately exposed in this and the following lines as a coping mechanism and not innately authentic, acting as a surprisingly self-aware moment on an otherwise facade-and-bravado laden album.", "annotation_id": "39778127", "url": "https://genius.com/39778127", "state": "pending", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "How do they say they got classics If it’s shit that you never revisited? And if that’s the shit you revisited Then why am I boss of the business?", "vault_lines": ["I tried and tried and tried", "How do they say they got classics", "If it's shit that you never revisited?", "And if that's the shit you revisited", "Then why am I boss of the business?"], "meaning": "A  rebuttal to Kendrick Lamar’s  “euphoria”:\n\nYeah, my first one like my last one\nit’s a classic, you don’t have one\n\nA classic, by definition, is music that stands the test of time, records people return to long after they dropped. Drake is pointing out that the streaming data tells a very specific story about whose catalog people are actually revisiting. Drake currently holds six solo albums that have each independently surpassed 5 billion streams. By contrast, Kendrick’s catalog, while critically revered, has only two solo albums that approach that threshold.\n\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/929e2ac800ab41b1eeb0f34db144f87d5c2a5ff1354b0bd1d4cc53f7d16b1a80%2Fzba8vt3wx8h\n\nDrake has also surpassed 130 billion total streams on Spotify (more than double the next closest rapper.)", "annotation_id": "39778102", "url": "https://genius.com/39778102", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "The pleasure Don’t walk away You’ve got your love locked up for me, oh For me, yeah, yeah You’ve got your love locked up for me, oh", "vault_lines": ["You've got your love locked up for me, oh"], "meaning": "After presenting himself as emotionally cold and unreachable under the album’s ‘'ICEMAN’‘ persona, this track introduces themes of longing and emotional vulnerability, implying that beneath the hardened exterior is unresolved loneliness.\n\nThe outro is performed by @makeyouknowlove rather than Drake himself, which gives the ending a detached, almost cinematic feeling. Instead of directly speaking to the listener, Drake closes ‘'ICEMAN’‘ by letting another voice carry the album’s final emotional moment.\n\nDrake previewed this during the ‘'ICEMAN’‘ livestreams. During those sessions, he teased this outro sampling ’‘Wahdon’‘, the 1979 classic by the Lebanese singer Fairuz.", "annotation_id": "39777884", "url": "https://genius.com/39777884", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "They got wind of the fact I was hit And the whole world started to chime", "vault_lines": ["I tried and tried and tried", "They got wind of the fact I was hit", "And the whole world started to chime", "Freeze the world, freeze the world"], "meaning": "Drake is lamenting on the world reacting to the scathing diss track “Not Like Us” and commenting on him. Wind / Chime is the wordplay here.", "annotation_id": "39777794", "url": "https://genius.com/39777794", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "Niggas been talkin’ on digital Knowin’ how shit could get physical", "vault_lines": ["Niggas been talkin' on digital", "Knowin' how shit could get physical"], "meaning": "Drake calls out social media gossip and warns that things could turn ugly if it goes too far.\n\nIt’s also a double entendre: he plays on “digital” and “physical,” referring to both online and real-world conflict, as well as digital and physical music.", "annotation_id": "39777495", "url": "https://genius.com/39777495", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I’m goin’, goin’ back to Cali", "vault_lines": ["I'm goin', goin' back to Cali"], "meaning": "This is a territorial provocation since California is the state to Kendrick Lamar’s home turf, Compton. The whole West Coast happened to rally behind him throughout the beef.\n\nThis echoes Drake earlier bars during the beef on “Family Matters”:\n\nHead to Delilah with all of my ice, head to Delilah with all of my ice\n\nDelilah is an upscale restaurant and nightclub in L.A. that Drake frequently visits.\n\nThis is also a callback to The Notorious B.I.G.’s track “Going Back to Cali,” which was one of Biggie’s last released singles after his murder in Los Angeles in March 1997.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TiBKJlCijL4\n\nThe East Coast legend kept returning to California despite the smoldering East-West coast beef with 2Pac and Death Row Records, and California is ultimately where his life ended.\n\n\n\nThe Notorious B.I.G. at the 11th Annual Soul Train Music Awards that was held at the Shrine Auditorium in Los Angeles, California, two days prior to his murder", "annotation_id": "39776929", "url": "https://genius.com/39776929", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I still haven’t lost any sleep", "vault_lines": ["I still haven't lost any sleep"], "meaning": "This line refers to Drake’s “Family Matters,” where he says J. Cole, not Drake, is the one losing sleep over the feud with Kendrick Lamar:\n\nAnd, nigga, Cole losin' sleep on this, it ain’t me\n\nBut, on August 30, 2024, about three months after the last diss track dropped, Drake released “Circadian Rhythm,” a song about his broken sleep schedule:\n\nRap game like my sleepin' pattern ‘cause how the fuck I’m still up?", "annotation_id": "39776842", "url": "https://genius.com/39776842", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "Iceman, baby, why are you so cold?", "vault_lines": ["Iceman, baby, why are you so cold?"], "meaning": "ICEMAN is not just the album title. It is also an alias Drake has used throughout the rollout, and many people have referred to him that way.\n\nThis works as a double entendre. “Cool” refers to temperature, which ties to ice man, but it also means suave or composed, describing Drake’s presence and how he moves.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lcyg0pXStHg\n\n“Ice” can also mean a flashy, diamond-covered watch, and Drake is known for a watch collection worth millions.", "annotation_id": "39776826", "url": "https://genius.com/39776826", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I reserve it like I’m aboriginal", "vault_lines": ["I reserve the shit like I'm indigenous", "I reserve it like I'm aboriginal"], "meaning": "Another reference to indigenous reservations, this time for the Aboriginal people of Australia.", "annotation_id": "39776786", "url": "https://genius.com/39776786", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "line": "I reserve the shit like I’m indigenous", "vault_lines": ["I reserve the shit like I'm indigenous", "I reserve it like I'm aboriginal"], "meaning": "This is a reference to “reservations” for Native Americans, a place where the indigenous people of the United States and Canada were pushed to.", "annotation_id": "39776766", "url": "https://genius.com/39776766", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Know", "status": "done", "targets": 39, "clicked": 39, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-make-them-know-lyrics", "genius_title": "Make Them Know"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "I said see you at the top and they misunderstood me", "vault_lines": ["I said see you at the top and they misunderstood me"], "meaning": "A comma can change the sentence. “see, you at the top” conveys a feeling that they made it, leading to complacency. Drake wanted to race, “they” wanted to stop", "annotation_id": "41173407", "url": "https://genius.com/41173407", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "Said he put some money on my head, I guess we gon’ see I won’t put no money on his head, my niggas owe me", "vault_lines": ["Said he put some money on my head, I guess we gon' see", "I won't put no money on his head, my niggas owe me"], "meaning": "Here Drake is calling a literal or metaphorical bounty placed on his head, by his rivals in the rap industry or even by criminal elements, that signals peril to his person and integrity. By breaking down “we gon' see,” he’s acknowledging the danger and also indicating assurance that he can outwit anyone who’d bet on their victory over him. This humble-brag nods to his status (“put money on my head” only makes sense if you’re worth something) while also showing that he’s unfazed by external attempts to take him down\n\nRather than escalating violence with an offer of his own, Drake flips the equation on its head: he doesn’t need to buy someone off to “take care of business” because his entourage is already owing him. This line is proof of the power of relationships over money—his inner circle’s respect and loyalty are more (and surer) than any amount of cash can buy. It’s a brag of self-assured leadership: Drake doesn’t need to pay for security when he’s already got invincible loyalty", "annotation_id": "35601447", "url": "https://genius.com/35601447", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "And saved a couple bitches too like I’m from Cape Town", "vault_lines": ["And I'm too about it", "And saved a couple bitches too like I'm from Cape Town"], "meaning": "Travis is making a play on words off the South African city, Cape Town, and the fact that superheroes are typically portrayed wearing a cape, such as Superman or Batman. He is implying, like a hero, he “saved” a couple bitches", "annotation_id": "26040837", "url": "https://genius.com/26040837", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "That butterfly emoji hold me down and all my babies them", "vault_lines": ["That butterfly emoji hold me down and all my babies them"], "meaning": "In this line Travis Scott is talking about Kylie Jenner, the couple has both matching butterfly tattoos and also Kylie confirmed with a 90-second video in his Instagram that they are waiting for the second baby.\n\nhttps://pbs.twimg.com/media/E-Y4RWcXEBYOwcF?format=jpg&name=large\nLots of references from both using butterflies", "annotation_id": "23944548", "url": "https://genius.com/23944548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "And they want the tea on me, I swear these bitches nosey", "vault_lines": ["And they want the tea on me, I swear these bitches nosey"], "meaning": "Being one of the most coveted rapper celebrities on today’s hip-hop scene, lots of people are interested in attaining news surrounding Drake’s music, personal life and whereabouts. In other words, spreading «tea» about him.\n\n•  «Tea» — a slang term for gossip.\n\nAlthough the Toronto native is known for not letting paparazzis and media bother him too much (unlike his adidas-loving contemporary), being the superstar that he is, Aubrey has before fallen victim to people getting too intrusive, which might be what this line is referring to as well.", "annotation_id": "23894184", "url": "https://genius.com/23894184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "Mama used to be on disability but gave me this ability And now she walkin’ with her head high and her back straight", "vault_lines": ["Mama used to be on disability but gave me this ability", "And now she walkin' with her head high and her back straight"], "meaning": "Drake has spoken publicly before about how his mother was very sick when he was growing up. Sandi Graham reportedly suffered osteoporosis and joint pain, though this is unconfirmed. Drake himself had this to say in a 2011 Complex interview:\n\nMaybe I haven’t talked enough about it, but I didn’t grow up happy. I wasn’t in a happy home. My mother was very sick. We were very poor, like broke.\n\nThe second line, however, may confirm the disease affected the joints as his mother can now walk with “her back straight”. Then again, these may only be metaphorical statements that his mother is more confident than ever. These bars continue a long history of Drake hyping up his mom in his lyrics. The 2015 song You & The 6 is a whole track dedicated to his mother’s advice and care.\n\n\n\nDrake’s mother has been very vocal in her praise for her son during the lead up to Certified Lover Boy’s release. She wrote him several letters and poems that Drake shared on Instagram.", "annotation_id": "23884526", "url": "https://genius.com/23884526", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "Baby, if you want me, can’t be turnin’ up with everybody, nah Can’t be fuckin’ on just anybody, yeah I got feelings for you, that’s the thing about it, yeah You know that it’s somethin’ when I sing about it, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Baby, if you want me, can't be turnin' up with everybody, nah", "Can't be fuckin' on just anybody, yeah", "I got feelings for you, that's the thing about it, yeah", "You know that it's somethin' when I sing about it, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake wants loyalty from his significant other. In order for them to be in a relationship with each other, she has to stay committed to him.\n\nThe last two lines are likely a nod to Drake’s June 2018 smash hit, “In My Feelings.” On the song, he asks certain women whether they love him or not. He notes here that he isn’t afraid to make a song like “IMF” for the woman in question.", "annotation_id": "23882330", "url": "https://genius.com/23882330", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "I’ve been losin’ friends and findin’ peace But honestly, that sound like a fair trade to me If I ever heard one and I’m still here Outside, Frontline, Southside I’ve been losin’ friends and findin’ peace Honestly, that sound like a fair trade to me", "vault_lines": ["I've been losin' friends and findin' peace", "But honestly, that sound like a fair trade to me", "If I ever heard one and I'm still here", "Honestly, that sound like a fair trade to me"], "meaning": "Drake “losing friends” means he’s cutting out the fake people in his life and as a result, he’s finding peace from it. This has been a sentiment Drake has stuck to throughout his career; on his February 2013 song, “Started From The Bottom,” he raps:\n\nNo new niggas, nigga, we don’t feel that\nFuck a fake friend, where your real friends at?\nWe don’t like to do too much explainin'\nStory stayed the same, I never changed it", "annotation_id": "23882088", "url": "https://genius.com/23882088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 161, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "And I show my face in a case so you know it’s me", "vault_lines": ["And I show my face in a case so you know it's me"], "meaning": "Possibly another shot at Kanye West. In a June 2021 court deposition Kanye famously donned a full-face hood and head covering adorned with Jesus Christ’s image and insisted the attorneys didn’t deserve to see his face. Kanye has since worn a full-face covering since that time.", "annotation_id": "23879434", "url": "https://genius.com/23879434", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "Publicly she at the crib again, company", "vault_lines": ["Publicly she at the crib again, company"], "meaning": "This line references Drake and Travis' first official collaboration from 2015 on the mixtape If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, Company.\n\nOn Company, the two artists offer a stark contrast in terms of subject matter and ultimately portray both sides of a man in a relationship: Drake croons about his emotional needs, while Travis details his sexual and physical desires. The first half features vibe-setting production from WondaGurl and TM88, before transitioning to Travis' dark, melodic synths, which allows him to delve into his hedonistic lifestyle.\n\nThis song mirrors Company in the sense where Drake talks about losing friends and relationships over a sanguine beat before a notable beat switch which focuses on Travis examining his Nightcrawler lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "23878738", "url": "https://genius.com/23878738", "state": "pending", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "And the dirt that they threw on my name Turned to soil and I grew up out it", "vault_lines": ["And the dirt that they threw on my name", "Turned to soil and I grew up out it"], "meaning": "Many rappers have attempted to damage Drake’s reputation in his career, all the ‘threw dirt’ such as speaking bad on his name or just antics to damage his name.\n\nThis is notable with Pusha T claiming Drizzy doesn’t write his own songs in the song, “Infrared” from his 2018 album, DAYTONA produced by Ye:\n\nHow could you ever right these wrongs\nWhen you don’t even write your songs?\n\nWhich did not reflect well on him in the Rap industry as many started doubting his accolades by claiming he had a ghostwriter. This rumor was started on Twitter with Meek Mill claiming Drake has a Ghostwriter:\n\nStop comparing drake to me too…. He don’t write his own raps! That’s why he ain’t tweet my album because we found out! 😁\n\nDrake feels after all the ‘Dirt’ that has been thrown on his name he turned it into something good (soil), meaning whatever they said against him and meant it as bad turned out to be for his own benefit.\n\nIt could also be a reference to Lil Wayne’s famous line in ‘No love’:\n\nThrow dirt on me and grow a wildflower", "annotation_id": "23874601", "url": "https://genius.com/23874601", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "Feelin’ young, but they treat me like the OG", "vault_lines": ["Feelin' young, but they treat me like the OG"], "meaning": "Drake is a hip-hop veteran. At the release of this song, he would be fifteen years into his career, with his first project, Room for Improvement being released back in 2006.\n\nDrake has reflected on his veteran status throughout the years; for instance, on his June 2014 song, “0 to 100 / The Catch Up,” he raps:\n\nOh, Lord, I’m the rookie and the vet", "annotation_id": "23872383", "url": "https://genius.com/23872383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "I’m outside, 29, G5, Seaside", "vault_lines": ["I'm outside, 29, G5, Seaside"], "meaning": "It is very likely that 29, G5, and Seaside are three locations since Area 29, G5 Primetime and Seaside Lounge are all stripclubs and nightclubs in Houston, Texas.\n\nIt would make a lot of sense with Travis Scott, a Houston native and advocat, being the feature on this song and all three names being said in sequence with being outside, during these fearful unprecedented times of COVID-19 where being in crowded places is looked at as a risk.\n\nAnother interpretation is that “29” refers to Ward 29, a section in Toronto, Canada. As a child, Drake grew up on Weston Road in Toronto before moving to Forest Hill a few years later. Both locations are located within Ward 29.\n\nA G5, otherwise known as a Gulfstream G500, is a private jet aircraft with two Rolls-Royce BR710 engines. While Drake doesn’t have a G5 jet, he has access to a private $185 million Boeing 767 Jet, which he nicknamed “Air Drake.” He got it as a promotional gift from the Canadian cargo carrier, Cargojet, back in June 2019.", "annotation_id": "23853697", "url": "https://genius.com/23853697", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "Uno, dos, tres, in a race, they can’t hold me", "vault_lines": ["Uno, dos, tres, in a race, they can't hold me"], "meaning": "This line is likely a reference to Drake’s March 2021 release Scary Hours 2 from which all three songs debuted at the top 3 spots on the Billboard Hot 100 simultaneously.\n\nThe songs debuted on the Hot 100 in the tracklist’s order, being:\n#1 – “What’s Next”\n#2 – “Wants and Needs” featuring Lil Baby\n#3 – “Lemon Pepper Freestyle” featuring Rick Ross\n\nHe made history being “the first artist ever to debut three songs in the top three simultaneously.”", "annotation_id": "23100310", "url": "https://genius.com/23100310", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "I don’t understand why you blame me Just take me as I am, it’s the same me We should be (Yeah) Senseless sight to see Yeah Senseless sight to see Ayy", "vault_lines": ["I don't understand why you blame me", "Just take me as I am, it's the same me", "We should be (Yeah)", "Senseless sight to see", "We should be, senseless sight to see"], "meaning": "The intro samples the second verse and the chorus of Canadian R&B singer and songwriter Charlotte Day Wilson’s September 2019 song, “Mountains.” Notably, Wilson hails from Drake’s hometown, Toronto, and is the cousin of OVO founder, Oliver El-Khatib.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/ldAaDsJ9AtE?t=118", "annotation_id": "23082845", "url": "https://genius.com/23082845", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "line": "Look, don’t invite me over if you throw another pity party, uh Lookin’ back, it’s hard to tell you where I started", "vault_lines": ["Look, don't invite me over if you throw another pity party, uh", "Lookin' back, it's hard to tell you where I started"], "meaning": "Drake reflects on his humble beginnings. From growing up poor in Weston Road, Toronto to taking care of his sick mother while only making a measly $50,000 per year from acting, Drake has come a long way since.\n\nSome of his achievements include having a net worth of over $180 million, being the highest-certified digital singles artist in history, winning four Grammys from 42 nominations and breaking several Billboard records.\n\nWith Drake now sitting at the top, he notes it’s hard for him to see the bottom or even remember what the bottom was like, given how long he’s been successful.", "annotation_id": "23082734", "url": "https://genius.com/23082734", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fair Trade", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-fair-trade-lyrics", "genius_title": "Fair Trade"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "And spit true feelings until our tooth fillings come flyin’ up out of our mouths, now rewind it", "vault_lines": ["And spit true feelings until our tooth fillings come flyin' up out of our mouths, now rewind it"], "meaning": "Tooth fillings are generally associated with growing older and having dental problems. This is Eminem acknowledging that he is an older person than he used to be, however, he says that he will still rap with the same talent and ferocity he used to – so much that his age will not be able to keep up with it. The tooth fillings flying out of his mouth could also represent him casting away drugs and the dental problems that came with them, rewinding to the days before his addiction.", "annotation_id": "40245592", "url": "https://genius.com/40245592", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Payback, motherfucker, for the way that you doubted me, how’s it taste? When I slap the taste outta your mouths with a bass so loud that it shakes the place", "vault_lines": ["Payback, motherfucker, for the way that you doubted me, how's it taste?", "When I slap the taste outta your mouths with a bass so loud that it shakes the place"], "meaning": "When he describes payback as a “taste”, saying immediately that he will “slap the taste” out of their mouth implies that he will come back to the top so fast that they will not even have time to realise how misplaced their doubts in him were. In addition, the consistent “ow” sounds create powerful assonance in “doubt”, “how”, “out”, “mouths” and loud.", "annotation_id": "40245585", "url": "https://genius.com/40245585", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "There they go, packin’ stadiums as Shady spits his flow", "vault_lines": ["There they go, packin' stadiums as Shady spits his flow"], "meaning": "Em is tellin than he is being amazing as he is spitting his lyrics, people have gathered whole stadiums", "annotation_id": "39963766", "url": "https://genius.com/39963766", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "If I had one guess, then I guess I’m just New Orleans", "vault_lines": ["If I had one guess, then I guess I'm just New Orleans"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is famously proud of his hometown of New Orleans, Louisiana. Throughout his career, Wayne has referenced the area numerous times and frequently boasts his allegiance. A month later, he proudly reinforced his Louisiana ties on his October 2009 freestyle “Wasted”:\n\nNew Orleans nigga, I get Super-dome\nLoyal to the game, the game been good to me\n\nWayne spoke about the strong rap presence in New Orleans and its influence on him via a interview with Fader:\n\nWhat influenced us was the early New Orleans artists. We’re all just following that fabric, that tradition, and the culture now and it’s easy to follow when it’s natural.", "annotation_id": "18483984", "url": "https://genius.com/18483984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Chasin’ the stardom will turn you to a maniac", "vault_lines": ["Chasin' the stardom will turn you to a maniac"], "meaning": "In the Cambridge Dictionary, the definition of ”maniac” is: ”a person who behaves in an uncontrolled way, not worrying about risks or danger”. Someone obsessive or rather insane is interpreted as a Maniac.\n\nIn the article “Kanye West ’suffered nervous breakdown triggered by the anniversary of his mother’s death’ before being hospitalized last week” published by Joanna Crawley and Shaym Dodge on MailOnline, 30 November 2016, it says:\n\nThe 39-year-old rapper- who has always been a slave to his work- has reportedly been asking his wife Kim if he can have music equipment at his bedside and see his Yeezy fashion designs […] Kim told him he must focus on getting better.\n\nThe song ”Forever” was released 2009, which was less than a month before Kanye West interrupted Taylor Swift’s VMA award, acceptance speech. The media questioned his behavior and made it seem as if he was a maniac.", "annotation_id": "14796869", "url": "https://genius.com/14796869", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "I’m like Nevada in the middle of the summer", "vault_lines": ["I'm like Nevada in the middle of the summer"], "meaning": "Much of the state of Nevada is in the desert. Between June and August, temperatures can go above 100 degrees Fahrenheit. In other words, Wayne is HOT!", "annotation_id": "13479851", "url": "https://genius.com/13479851", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Who’da thought a countrywide tour’d be the outcome?", "vault_lines": ["Who'da thought a countrywide tour'd be the outcome?"], "meaning": "In August 2009, several months after the release of his classic tape So Far Gone, Drake went on the America’s Most Wanted Tour with Young Money, which lasted approximately one month, touring across the United States.", "annotation_id": "12983340", "url": "https://genius.com/12983340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "[Verse 2: Kanye West]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "According to Mr. West himself stated in the post-VMA rant that was leaked online, after hearing Eminem’s verse, he went back and spent two days rewriting his verse to make sure it was perfect for the song. Moreover, he went on to say that it’s for this reason that Taylor Swift “didn’t deserve to win over Beyoncé.”", "annotation_id": "2003507", "url": "https://genius.com/2003507", "state": "accepted", "votes": 231, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "So I don’t plan on stoppin’ at all", "vault_lines": ["So I don't plan on stoppin' at all"], "meaning": "With all the fame and glory Drizzy has, why would he want to ever leave this lifestyle? The lifestyle may be catching up to him however and he may be “too young to be feeling this old”. Apparently, Drake wants to retire by age 30", "annotation_id": "844207", "url": "https://genius.com/844207", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "You can see The Hip-Hop group Slaughterhouse behind Em in the video. This started the speculation that Slaughterhouse was signing to Shady which proved to be true.", "annotation_id": "552710", "url": "https://genius.com/552710", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Pistol on my side, you don’t wanna hear that thing talk", "vault_lines": ["Pistol on my side, you don't wanna hear that thing talk"], "meaning": "He’s probably referring to his tattoo of a pistol he has on his neck…\n\n\n\nIn a live performance on the Grammys he actually said, “Pistol on my neck, you don’t wanna hear that thing talk.”\n\nLil Wayne would later interpolate this on the chorus of Swizz Beatz' 2018 track, “Pistol on My Side.”", "annotation_id": "329350", "url": "https://genius.com/329350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Ayy, ever-ever, Mr. West is in the buildin’ Ain’t no question who about to kill ’em", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, ever-ever, Mr. West is in the buildin'", "Ain't no question who about to kill 'em"], "meaning": "“Ever-ever” is a reference to the hook of “Diamonds from Sierra Leone”.\n\nNote he also used a similar opening in his verse on “Swagga Like Us” by T.I.:\n\n“Mr. West is in the buildin'\nSwagger on a hundred thousand trillion”\n\nHe is implying that he is about to “kill” the other rappers on this song (or just in general).\n\nAlso note he used a similar opening in his verse on Chris Brown’s Down (which coincidentally was followed on Brown’s Exclusive by another song called Forever). “Mr West is in the building, Children, Ladies”.\n\nInterestingly, these introductory lines were not present in the song’s original audio release.", "annotation_id": "221510", "url": "https://genius.com/221510", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "[Verse 4: Eminem]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "If you wrote Relapse off, this was the moment you realized Shady might be back for real….\n\nThe YouTube channel Highlighted would showcase the rhyme scheme from this verse:\nhttps://youtu.be/qZpysyylDhA\nFun Fact: 50 Cent stated that Eminem was very upset with how his verse came out on the finished product. Em is a perfectionist in the studio.  He had his verse mixed exactly as he wanted it, and apparently didn’t know it was gonna be on a song with Kanye West and Lil Wayne. When he sent it to the producer, the producer went back in and mixed it his way, pissing Em off.\n\nEminem has since suggested quite the opposite sentiment in his 2018 track “Fall”, with the following lyrics suggesting that he did in fact still steal the show on this song and finds some satisfaction in his performance\n\nThe fans waited for this moment like that feature\nWhen I stole the show (ha), sorry if I took Forever (haha)", "annotation_id": "162542", "url": "https://genius.com/162542", "state": "accepted", "votes": 514, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "You would think I ran the world like Michelle’s husband", "vault_lines": ["You would think I ran the world like Michelle's husband"], "meaning": "“Michelle” is referring to Michelle Obama, who was the First Lady of the US from 2009 to 2017. Barack Obama, her husband, “ran the world” as President of the US.", "annotation_id": "62166", "url": "https://genius.com/62166", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Life is such a fuckin’ roller coaster, then it drops But what should I scream for? This is my theme park", "vault_lines": ["Life is such a fuckin' roller coaster, then it drops", "But what should I scream for? This is my theme park"], "meaning": "Life is full of ups and downs and then it ‘drops’ – then you die. Similar  to the ‘life’s a bitch then you die’ phrase. But he shouldn’t fear death because he owns the world (his life – his theme park)\n\n This line extends the earlier reference to Space Jam, in which alien antagonists are sent to Earth to enslave NBA players.  The aliens are working for Moron Mountain, an extraterrestrial theme park.", "annotation_id": "40296", "url": "https://genius.com/40296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 143, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Back into the game and they know rap will never be the same as befo’", "vault_lines": ["Back into the game and they know rap will never be the same as befo'"], "meaning": "Perhaps an allusion to his changed state of mind, more fully revealed on the song “25 To Life” from the Recovery album\n\nIt’s also a reference to how Em is constantly changing the rap game. He was out of the game for awhile, but now he’s back into it, and it’ll be better than it was before. Possibly a reference to how Relapse bombed critically, but he’s learned his lesson and he’ll be better than that now.", "annotation_id": "37016", "url": "https://genius.com/37016", "state": "accepted", "votes": 150, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Last name Ever, first name Greatest", "vault_lines": ["Last name Ever, first name Greatest"], "meaning": "Despite never releasing a studio album when this song was recorded, Drake is calling himself the GOAT. Ego much?\n\nFun fact: Em came out on stage at one of Drake’s concerts to perform his verse on this track. After Em spit his verse, Drake called him the “greatest ever”.", "annotation_id": "32343", "url": "https://genius.com/32343", "state": "accepted", "votes": 323, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "I used to want this thing forever, y’all can have it back", "vault_lines": ["I used to want this thing forever, y'all can have it back"], "meaning": "Summary of this verse: he’s tired of the limelight..\n\n\n\nKANYE: We don’t believe you, you need more people!", "annotation_id": "26851", "url": "https://genius.com/26851", "state": "accepted", "votes": 231, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Everybody got a deal, I did it without one", "vault_lines": ["Everybody got a deal, I did it without one"], "meaning": "The mixtape that made Drake’s reputation as a rapper, So Far Gone, was released without backing from a major record label", "annotation_id": "26586", "url": "https://genius.com/26586", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "But understand, nothin’ was done for me", "vault_lines": ["But understand, nothin' was done for me"], "meaning": "No labels signed Drake, so he had to promote himself entirely on his own with mixtapes and tours… and it worked: he had a bigger fanbase unsigned than almost all signed underground rappers.\n\nYes, it is true that “nothing was done for me” is an exaggeration. Drake’s uncle was in a band called Sly and the Family Stone and helped secure Drake a management team (connected to Jay-Z and others) way before Drake had a record deal, and he did make a little money on the side for his work with Degrassi. But for everyone claiming that he knows nothing about struggle, I direct you to this annotation.", "annotation_id": "5395", "url": "https://genius.com/5395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 236, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "He ain’t had him a buzz like this since the last time that he overdosed", "vault_lines": ["He ain't had him a buzz like this since the last time that he overdosed"], "meaning": "Quintuple entendre people!  The buzz he’s talkin about was:\n\n\nhis buzz cut when he first blew up.\nthe amount of publicity he had when he almost overdosed\nthe actual buzz the fans are producing for him.\nthe buzz of the defibrilator used to revive him following an OD.\nThe high one gets when using drugs or alcohol is also called a “buzz”\n\n\n\nReference to Em’s drug problems in the period between Encore (2004) and Relapse (2009)\nThis line could also refer to when he attempted suicide back in 1997 by overdosing on Tylenol shortly after the release of Infinite.\n\nA buzz for the average person is the result of a couple drinks or a puff of a joint–even a cigarette for some.\n\nThis line suggests that a buzz for Eminem is an overdose (in parallel to over-performing). Hence: Eminem has an outrageously high drug tolerance (as opposed to other rappers who get a buzz from less good performance).", "annotation_id": "5389", "url": "https://genius.com/5389", "state": "accepted", "votes": 255, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "And tellin’ ’ery girl she the one for me And I ain’t even plannin’ to call", "vault_lines": ["And tellin' 'ery girl she the one for me", "And I ain't even plannin' to call"], "meaning": "Drake flatters girls with empty lies; this is also the theme of his hit “Best I Ever Had”", "annotation_id": "5387", "url": "https://genius.com/5387", "state": "accepted", "votes": 154, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "All the way in Hollywood and I can’t even act", "vault_lines": ["All the way in Hollywood and I can't even act"], "meaning": "So far Kanye’s only had one serious acting role, in hip hop thriller State Property 2. Surely there’s more to come\n\n\n\nThere is a double meaning here. On a deeper level, Kanye always acts crazy/can´t act normal in public! (cf. Swiftgate)\n\n‘Ye was also in the movie Love Guru\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LzKxSIcHdT4\n\nAnd recently made a cameo appearance in Anchorman 2", "annotation_id": "5384", "url": "https://genius.com/5384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "I’m Hannibal Lecter, so just in case you’re thinkin’ of savin’ face You ain’t gonna have no face to save by the time I’m through with this place, so, Drake", "vault_lines": ["I'm Hannibal Lecter, so just in case you're thinkin' of savin' face", "You ain't gonna have no face to save by the time I'm through with this place, so, Drake"], "meaning": "Em compares himself to Hannibal Lecter, the deranged cannibal from The Silence of the Lambs. (Em’s depicted himself like this before: his appearance in the “You Don’t Know” video was patterned after Hannibal.)\n\n\n\nTo “save face” is an idiom that means to avoid humiliation. Now, one of Hannibal the Cannibal’s more famous deeds was to cut off a cop’s face (which he used as a disguise). Exaggerated violence like this has always been a hallmark of Slim Shady. Since Slim is Hannibal, his point is that you literally won’t even have a face you can save, as he’s gonna cut it off.\n\nAnother possible reference is one to the T.V. series “The A-Team”. The main character, Hannibal, saves “Faceman” or Face’s life multiple times.", "annotation_id": "5365", "url": "https://genius.com/5365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 382, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "You dealin’ with a few true villains who stand inside of the booth, truth-spillin’", "vault_lines": ["You dealin' with a few true villains who stand inside of the booth, truth-spillin'"], "meaning": "He and the other rappers on this track are willing to tell you exactly what they think about life and fame. The “few” rappers in the video are: Eminem & Slaughterhouse.", "annotation_id": "5362", "url": "https://genius.com/5362", "state": "accepted", "votes": 157, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Let the king talk, check the price and pay attention Lil Wayne, that’s what they gotta say or mention", "vault_lines": ["Let the king talk, check the price and pay attention", "Lil Wayne, that's what they gotta say or mention"], "meaning": "Possibly a reference to the pretty penny that Wayne supposedly charges people to perform on their songs (and the requirement that the host artist mentions his name in the track?)\n\nThis song is in the More Than a Game soundtrack, which was a movie about Lebron James – AKA The King (also a reference of Wayne being the King of rap)\n\n“Say or Mention” is contract languange. If you’re required to promote something it’ll be by “saying or mentioning”  like, Lil' Wayne on your radio station.", "annotation_id": "5361", "url": "https://genius.com/5361", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "The passion in the flame is ignited, you can’t put it out once we light it", "vault_lines": ["The passion in the flame is ignited, you can't put it out once we light it"], "meaning": "In keeping with the theme of rejuvenation in this verse, Eminem is back and hungry again. His passion for rap has once again been ignited and no one can put it out.\n\nAlso, it’s referencing the Olympic torch, which can’t be put out once it is lit until the end of each Olympic Games. Like athletes in the Olympics, Em is the best of the best.", "annotation_id": "5360", "url": "https://genius.com/5360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "His cup just runneth over, oh no", "vault_lines": ["his cup just runneth over, oh no"], "meaning": "A reference to Psalms 23:5 (my Haftorah portion), which is usually quoted to mean “I have more than enough wealth”\n\nAlso, Em is putting so many lyrics into his section of the song with his fast flow that the song almost can’t contain his words\n\n(This is also a recycled line he used from the 8 Mile track, “Rabbit Run”.)", "annotation_id": "5358", "url": "https://genius.com/5358", "state": "accepted", "votes": 133, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "He’s wonderin’ if he should spit this slow Fuck no, go for broke,", "vault_lines": ["he's wonderin' if he should spit this slow", "Fuck no, go for broke,"], "meaning": "Slowing down one’s flow is normally something rappers do in order to make themselves more commercially viable.  This is because, prior to Rap Genius, listeners had trouble understanding rapidfire lyrics\n\nBut Eminem doesn’t care about sales: he’s sold enough albums, more than any other rapper by far. This verse is all about his pride, and he wants to highlight his technical proficiency with a ridiculously fast flow\n\nThis may also be a subliminal diss to Wayne (mostly), Drake (less likely) and Kanye (really not) because they all spit their verses slow and he picks up the pace after he asks this question. therefore making them look like bozos\n\nGo for broke also means to risk everything. He knows a slow verse would sell more, but he basically can do whatever he wants. This case, go against the expectations (and overcome them)\n\nWith reference to the theory that ‘slow = sales’, Em may be making a bid to prove his transcendence amidst the game. As was stated Eminem has sold more albums than any other rapper so obviously fast rapping does not mean less sales, but maybe that’s de to Em’s supreme gift for enunciation and narrative clarity. In fact, Em’s work here is a 101 course on the construction of a good verse; it should build, starting off slow and picking up energy as it progresses. While Em starts off fast here, wait til you see how frenetic things have become by the end!\n\nEminem increases his words per minute after this line from 223 Wpm to 288 Wpm, as a response to his  previous line.", "annotation_id": "5356", "url": "https://genius.com/5356", "state": "accepted", "votes": 258, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "He can make them look like bozos,", "vault_lines": ["He can make them look like bozos,"], "meaning": "Eminem wants to remind people that he’s one of the most skilled rappers of all time, especially at battling.\n\nMoreover, Eminem might be implying that he makes nincompoops famous by battling them and thereby drawing the public’s attention (Bozo was a relatively famous clown).\n\n\n\nOne may say that the “clown theme” could imply a diss to the Insane Clown Posse – but Proof squashed the beef for them back in 2005.", "annotation_id": "5355", "url": "https://genius.com/5355", "state": "accepted", "votes": 192, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "They pull they cameras out and, goddamn, he snap", "vault_lines": ["They pull they cameras out and, goddamn, he snap"], "meaning": "Kanye has had a couple paparazzi camera smash lash-outs.\n\nCameras “snap” pictures; Paparazzi cause Kanye to “snap” (lose his temper).\n\n\n\nKanye has been known to speak his mind on live TV heeding no thought to any ramifications, sparking rumors regarding his mental health. Two particular instances were his controversial comments about George Bush’s lack of respect for Black people and his iconic tirade at the VMA’s.", "annotation_id": "5344", "url": "https://genius.com/5344", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Nuts they go, Macadamian, they go so ballistic, woah", "vault_lines": ["Nuts they go, Macadamian, they go so ballistic, woah"], "meaning": "Macadamian is a type of nut. Eminem drives the stadium crowd “nuts”\n\nMacadamian nut:\n\n\n\n Also, I don’t think it is a coincidence that the Macadamia nut is a very pale/white colored nut…", "annotation_id": "5342", "url": "https://genius.com/5342", "state": "accepted", "votes": 350, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "They been waiting patiently for Pinocchio to poke his nose", "vault_lines": ["They been waiting patiently for Pinocchio to poke his nose"], "meaning": "Refers to the remix of Mariah Carey’s “Obsessed” (which is about Eminem) in which Gucci Mane accuses Eminem of lying about having had sex with Mariah. Mane says:\n\nSo like Pinocchio, his nose gon' grow\n\n\n\nWhat Eminem means exactly by this line, is that the public, and other rappers probably, is “waiting patiently” for Eminem to lie (When ). They want something to call him out on and tear him down with. In the context of Gucci’s accusation, Eminem’s saying: “You keep waiting patiently for my nose to grow—you do that—it never well.”\n\nThe “waiting patiently” line is a subtle allusion to the 50 Cent song “Patiently Waiting” on which Em (predictably) out-rapped 50.", "annotation_id": "5341", "url": "https://genius.com/5341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 217, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "’Member she had that bad hip like a fanny pack?", "vault_lines": ["'Member she had that bad hip like a fanny pack?"], "meaning": "This line is a triple entendre:\n\n\nA touching reminiscence as Kanye remembers his grandma’s frailty and her hip problems.\nFanny packs are “unhip” (unfashionable).\nFanny packs make your hip look bad (see below).", "annotation_id": "5334", "url": "https://genius.com/5334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Trade the Grammy plaques just to have my granny back", "vault_lines": ["Trade the Grammy plaques just to have my granny back"], "meaning": "It is sweet that Kanye would trade all his success to have his grandmother (who died in the mid-2000s and was the subject of “Roses”)\n\nOne wonders why he doesn’t talk instead about his mother’s death, which sent him into a deep depression for a time. Perhaps he just needed time to grieve properly, as he did mention her several years later on Clique.", "annotation_id": "5328", "url": "https://genius.com/5328", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "You would think these niggas know me when they really doesn’t Like they was down with the old me, no you fuckin’ wasn’t", "vault_lines": ["You would think these niggas know me when they really doesn't", "Like they was down with the old me, no you fuckin' wasn't"], "meaning": "Rappers often talk about how people come out of the woodwork to pretend that they’ve been friends with the rapper all along once the rapper finally makes money\n\nThis comes up in Eminem’s “Marshall Mathers”:\n\nFamily fighting and fussing\nOver who wants to invite me to supper\nAll the sudden, I got 90 some cousins\nA half-brother and sister who never seen me\nOr even bothered to call me until they saw me on TV\n- Eminem –  “Marshall Mathers”\n\nAnd Jay-Z talks about this on “8 Miles and Running”:\n\n“Got their Jay-Z pom-poms and their Hov uniforms\nClaimin' they been runnin'\nAnd tellin' everybody like Martin Lawrence\n‘Bout how hot my rap performance was before I was who I was\nClaimin’ that they threw it up before I threw it up\nYou what? Where was you before I blew this up?”\n- Jay-Z – 8 Miles and Running", "annotation_id": "5327", "url": "https://genius.com/5327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Now it’s super bad chicks givin’ me McLovin", "vault_lines": ["Now it's super bad chicks givin' me McLovin"], "meaning": "McLovin is the name on the fake ID of Fogell, the dorkiest of the 3 teens trying to get laid in the movie Superbad, and the only one to succeed. Now that Kanye has money all the bad bitches (Gold Diggers) want to fuck.\n\nMoreover, this a use of wordplay, in reference to the popular fast food restaurant, McDonalds, known for their chicken nuggets.", "annotation_id": "5326", "url": "https://genius.com/5326", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "I stuck my dick inside this life until that bitch came", "vault_lines": ["I stuck my dick inside this life until that bitch came"], "meaning": "A subtle allusion to the “life’s a bitch” meme in rap started by the Nas track “Life´s a Bitch”. He is comparing the music business with satisfying a woman. He tried in the music business (stuck my dick) until he succeeded (bitch came). Came=cum.\n\nKanye has, through his success, taken such control over his own destiny that life is lazily enjoying herself in his bed.", "annotation_id": "5325", "url": "https://genius.com/5325", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "I used to have hood dreams, big fame, big chains", "vault_lines": ["I used to have hood dreams, big fame, big chains"], "meaning": "Hood dreams are the hopes people in the ghetto have of maybe getting lucky and becoming rich and famous.  Chains are necklaces.\n\nWe know he was able to accomplish this, especially when he released a track titled “Chain Heavy”.\n\n\n\nHood dreams is a possible play on the more common “hoop dreams”, this being a song for a movie about LeBron James I think this was intentional by Mr. West.", "annotation_id": "5324", "url": "https://genius.com/5324", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "If you ain’t been a part of it, at least you got to witness, bitches", "vault_lines": ["If you ain't been a part of it, at least you got to witness, bitches"], "meaning": "This song was used in “More Than a Game”, a documentary about LeBron James\n\nDrake is comparing himself to LeBron, who will go down as one of, if not the best basketball player ever. Drake, still new to the rap game, is setting equally high expectations for himself\n\nA Nike marketing campaign for LeBron is centered on the idea that we’re all witnesses to LeBron’s greatness:", "annotation_id": "5313", "url": "https://genius.com/5313", "state": "accepted", "votes": 127, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Um, you’re such a fuckin’ loser He ain’t even go to class, Bueller", "vault_lines": ["Um, you're such a fuckin' loser", "He ain't even go to class, Bueller"], "meaning": "Kanye is doing an impression of all the people who used to disdain him before he became successful\n\n“Bueller” is a reference to Ferris Bueller’s Day Off in which the title character skips school for a day to go to downtown Chicago, just as Kanye used to skip classes before dropping out of college entirely (a subject addressed in his debut album The College Dropout). In an oft-referenced scene in the film, a teacher (played by monotone character actor Ben Stein) repeats Bueller’s name on the attendance while the class stares at his empty seat.", "annotation_id": "5266", "url": "https://genius.com/5266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Y’all seen my story, my glory I had raped the game young, you could call it statutory", "vault_lines": ["Y'all seen my story, my glory", "I had raped the game young, you could call it statutory"], "meaning": "Yes, we’ve seen your glory Ye, we loved it…\n\nIf you sleep with a young girl, it’s statutory rape.  So his domination of the rap game at an early age could be called statutory rape.\n\nWhile clever, there is a problem with this line: Kanye’s debut album College Dropout came out when he was 27 – although he may have raped the production game when he was young, which is still the music business.\n\nHowever, this song was inspired by LeBron James, who went straight to the NBA out of high school and immediately dominated, raping the game (basketball) at a young enough age comparable to statutory.", "annotation_id": "5264", "url": "https://genius.com/5264", "state": "accepted", "votes": 178, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Just so I can make it rain all spring", "vault_lines": ["Just so I can make it rain all spring"], "meaning": "“Making it rain” (memorialized in a bomb ass song in its own right) is throwing dollar bills in the air in a strip club, a display of wealth common among athletes and rappers\n\nKanye plays with the standard meaning of the phrase, based on the line above about basketball teams: in basketball, a good shooter “makes it rain” and NBA teams want to shoot particularly well in the Spring, which is playoffs season (and also rainy season, weatherwise)\n\nHe pronounces it like “off spring” to continue the sexual metaphor since it would liken making it rain to orgasming during intercourse. The resulting phenomenon would be the birth of offspring or children.", "annotation_id": "5263", "url": "https://genius.com/5263", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "And went hard all fall like the ball teams", "vault_lines": ["And went hard all fall like the ball teams"], "meaning": "MLB season starts in late March/early April and ends in late October/early November.\nNFL season starts in late August/early September and ends in late January/early February\nNBA season starts in late October/early November and ends in late May/early June.\n\nThe “Big Three” (baseball, football, and basketball) are all played during the “fall” (the one season when all organizations play.)\n\n\n\nAnd so does hockey, but that’s not really a “ball team”, is it? “And went hard” is a continuation on the intercourse metaphor in the previous line.", "annotation_id": "5262", "url": "https://genius.com/5262", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Labels want my name beside a X, like Malcolm", "vault_lines": ["Labels want my name beside a X, like Malcolm"], "meaning": "Drake was highly coveted by every rap record label: they wanted him to sign his name on a contract (right next to the “X”, just like 1960s black civil right leader Malcolm X)\n\n\n\n“X” is used to substitute for “&” in naming collaborating artists.\nEg : \"Used to This – Future x Drake”", "annotation_id": "5258", "url": "https://genius.com/5258", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Dropped the mixtape, that shit sounded like a album", "vault_lines": ["Dropped the mixtape, that shit sounded like a album"], "meaning": "While Drake has dropped many mixtapes, this is probably a reference to So Far Gone, his 3rd mixtape which generated enough buzz to make Drake the most coveted free agent in the rap industry.", "annotation_id": "5257", "url": "https://genius.com/5257", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "If I was at the club, you know I balled, chemo", "vault_lines": ["If I was at the club, you know I balled, chemo"], "meaning": "Drake uses “balled” (past tense, slang for getting or having excess wealth) to function as a pun: “bald,” which is a side-effect of “chemo[therapy]”.", "annotation_id": "5256", "url": "https://genius.com/5256", "state": "accepted", "votes": 157, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Swimmin’ in the money, come and find me, Nemo", "vault_lines": ["Swimmin' in the money, come and find me, Nemo"], "meaning": "In Finding Nemo, Nemo is a fish who goes missing, and his father has to look for him.\n\nThis line can be taken to mean that Drake is literally swimming in money so you can’t see him\n\n\n\nMight also be worthy to note that “Nemo” is a syllable reverse of “Money”. It’s another pun as an elision (shorthand for a full word/phrase) as in “Need mo' money!”", "annotation_id": "5255", "url": "https://genius.com/5255", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "In your city, faded off the brown, Nino", "vault_lines": ["In your city, faded off the brown, Nino"], "meaning": "In Ontario when you buy liquor from the LCBO it comes in a brown bag that fits just the bottle. You see people drinking from the brown bag with booze in it and they are “faded off the brown.” Drake could also be drinking a brown liquor like whiskey.\n\nDrake also plays on the name Nino Brown—the drug lord played by Wesley Snipes in New Jack City.", "annotation_id": "5253", "url": "https://genius.com/5253", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Started off local, but thanks to all the haters I know G-IV pilots on a first name basis", "vault_lines": ["Started off local, but thanks to all the haters", "I know G-IV pilots on a first name basis"], "meaning": "A common theme in rap: the rapper is motivated to succeed by criticism. The haters have inspired Drake, and now he’s rich enough that he’s a regular flier on G-IV jets\n\nCf. Jay-Z’s “Hate” for another example of feeding off hate\n\nThis line also inspired Far East Movement’s “Like A G6”, as this article shows.\n\nA G6 is not a Gatorade flavor. It’s not a car, convertible, four-door. It’s not a watch… But Drake, Drake talks about having G-IV pilots on deck, so we said, ‘What’s flyer than a G-IV?’ Of course, it would be a G6.", "annotation_id": "5252", "url": "https://genius.com/5252", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Like a sprained ankle, boy, I ain’t nothin’ to play with", "vault_lines": ["Like a sprained ankle, boy, I ain't nothin' to play with"], "meaning": "You shouldn’t play basketball with a sprained ankle, and you shouldn’t “play” (try to mess) with Drake.\n\nA noted bit of irony is that before this song was released, Drake hurt his knee in a game of basketball, then further injured it on stage; after this, he had to get surgery.", "annotation_id": "5251", "url": "https://genius.com/5251", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "When a nigga blow up, they gon’ build statues for me Old money, Benjamin Button, “What?” nothin’", "vault_lines": ["When a nigga blow up, they gon' build statues for me", "Old money, Benjamin Button, \"What?\" nothin'"], "meaning": "Kanye’s saying people are going to build statues of him just like they do for people from wealthy families (“old money”).\n\nKanye, who is actually young money but appears to be old money, is age-confused like Benjamin Button, who looked like an old man but was actually young.\n\nAlso: Note the Benjamins = $$$$$ diction.", "annotation_id": "5237", "url": "https://genius.com/5237", "state": "accepted", "votes": 141, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Forever", "line": "Okay, hello, it’s the Martian, Space Jam Jordans", "vault_lines": ["Okay, hello, it's the Martian, Space Jam Jordans"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne once again refers to himself as a Martian (see “Phone Home”), as an explanation for the surrealism of his lyrics\n\n“Space Jam” (a movie starring Michael Jordan) is a movie about aliens who compete against MJ and the Looney Tunes in a basketball game.\n\n\n\nHe’s also playing on the fact that Marvin the Martian is a character in Space Jam while simultaneously bragging that he wears Air Jordan XI’s, the sneakers that came out to coincide with MJ’s movie:\n\n\n\nThe reference to Jordan in this line could also be a clever continuation of Drake’s referencing Lebron James. Drake may be Lebron (the young hotshot superstar) but Lil Wayne is reminding everyone that he is Jordan, the undisputed, seasoned pro. Wayne’s whole verse is basically reiterates that idea over and over in lines like “let the king talk” and “this is my theme park” for example. Just my thought.", "annotation_id": "5232", "url": "https://genius.com/5232", "state": "accepted", "votes": 208, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
