{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "I said “Nah, I’m quittin’ again ’cause I started again” Gotta wait on that", "vault_lines": ["I said \"Nah, I'm quittin' again 'cause I started again\"", "Gotta wait on that"], "meaning": "???", "annotation_id": "40936683", "url": "https://genius.com/40936683", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Get your ass twist right here, he a cane rower", "vault_lines": ["Get your ass twist right here, he a cane rower"], "meaning": "Drake evokes different black protective hairstyles to metaphorically describe his brother’s willingness to intimidate or murder for him.\n\nCanerows is a Caribbean term for the tightly-braided protective hairstyle cornrows. Twists are a more relaxed, looser style that also achieve protection.\n\nDrake is saying that his brother will put pressure on his opponents in the same way that a hairstylist will bind hair tightly in a protective style.", "annotation_id": "39919691", "url": "https://genius.com/39919691", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "’Cause I’ll backhand you boys with my writing hand", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I'll backhand you boys with my writing hand"], "meaning": "“Backhand” is a double entendre:\n\n\nA backhand compliment; in simplified terms, a compliment characterized by a sense of irony and/or falsity.\nA backhand slap; a slap likely aimed at one’s face performed with a quick movement and using the back of one’s hand.", "annotation_id": "37312192", "url": "https://genius.com/37312192", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Neeks", "vault_lines": ["I remember Bloor Street with Neeks"], "meaning": "Neeks is in reference to one of Drake’s close friend, Niko Carino aka OVONIKO", "annotation_id": "32900161", "url": "https://genius.com/32900161", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Cliquein’ up with haters that was thinkin’ of signin’ to us", "vault_lines": ["Cliquein' up with haters that was thinkin' of signin' to us"], "meaning": "The Weeknd was considering signing to OVO in the early 2010s, after appearing on Drake’s Take Care. (He also ghost-wrote some tracks.) Ultimately, he decided not to sign with Drake, which started a long beef between the Toronto artists.\n\nCould Drake have already been aware of the events that would unfold in 2024, when The Weeknd teamed up with Future, Kendrick Lamar, etc.?", "annotation_id": "31693618", "url": "https://genius.com/31693618", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Niggas want smoke with us, I promise, it’s game over", "vault_lines": ["Niggas want smoke with us, I promise, it's game over"], "meaning": "Drake threatens that if anyone wants to start an altercation with him and his “brother” they won’t come out to play another game.", "annotation_id": "31278607", "url": "https://genius.com/31278607", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Takin’ off the sneakers ‘cause you tired of tyin’ ’em up", "vault_lines": ["Takin' off the sneakers 'cause you tired of tyin' 'em up"], "meaning": "Kobe Bryant bars below could be a reference to Kanye West because of his track from Donda “24\"", "annotation_id": "30136417", "url": "https://genius.com/30136417", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "(Conductor)", "vault_lines": ["I mean, enough about me (Conductor)", "Man, either way, nigga, beware of the dog, deep in my character flaws (Conductor)"], "meaning": "Denzel “Conductor” Williams is a producer known for his work with record label Griselda, notably Westside Gunn and Mach-Hommy.\n\nThis producer tag is one of Williams' producer tags, which originates from a YouTube video uploaded in late 2015.\n\nWilliams has previously worked with Drake before on the song “8am in Charlotte”.", "annotation_id": "30132583", "url": "https://genius.com/30132583", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Humble back in 2012, now I give arrogant bars", "vault_lines": ["Humble back in 2012, now I give arrogant bars"], "meaning": "Drake is known for his arrogant lines. He often talkes about how much money he has and how luxurious his way of life is.\n\nIn songs like “Middle of the Ocean” on Her Loss, he talked a lot about his luxurious life.\n\nBack in 2012, he wasn’t necessarily as big in the game as he is now, yet he was very big. Him not being as big in the game leads to him being more humble.", "annotation_id": "30132024", "url": "https://genius.com/30132024", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "I should charge for interviews, they want me to share more", "vault_lines": ["I should charge for interviews, they want me to share more"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that he should charge the people interviewing him since they are reaching for information that Drake doesn’t want to share.\n\nAt least people should have to pay him to get information out of him.", "annotation_id": "30132011", "url": "https://genius.com/30132011", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "A ticket just to check in with the boy, like the airport", "vault_lines": ["A ticket just to check in with the boy, like the airport"], "meaning": "In the airport, you need to show a validated ticket or boarding pass to be able to board the plane.", "annotation_id": "30131997", "url": "https://genius.com/30131997", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "And niggas bound to slip eventually like they sent permission", "vault_lines": ["And niggas bound to slip eventually like they sent permission"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake plays on the idea of permission slips children are often required to send in to teachers before field trips, therefore insinuating the childlike nature of haters and their lack of independence.", "annotation_id": "30121130", "url": "https://genius.com/30121130", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "My brother carry bundles and extensions like hair stores", "vault_lines": ["My brother carry bundles and extensions like hair stores"], "meaning": "A bundle is a quantity of things wrapped or tied together. Drake is probably referring to carrying cash.\nExtension refers to extended magazines, which is a firearm attachment.\nBundles and extensions also refer to hair, used to add on to a person’s natural hair.", "annotation_id": "30110358", "url": "https://genius.com/30110358", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Your last album was buns, you niggas Hawaiian as fuck", "vault_lines": ["Your last album was buns, you niggas Hawaiian as fuck"], "meaning": "“Buns” in this bar means “bad” or “poor quality.” Drake refers to the album as “buns” and references Hawaiian Rolls, a type of sweet dinner roll.\n\n\n\nUsing the above information, “Hawaiian” in this case can also be interpreted as “sweet,” meaning Drake is calling “you niggas” sweet.", "annotation_id": "30109744", "url": "https://genius.com/30109744", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "I walked in the studio, Noel said, “I thought you quit”", "vault_lines": ["I walked in the studio, Noel said, \"I thought you quit\""], "meaning": "After Drake released For All The Dogs, which marked his third album release in just a span of a year, he talked about going on a hiatus from music for his physical health. The surprise announcement of Scary Hours 3 came as a shocker to fans and this bar shows that even his producers were surprised to see him in the studio soon again.", "annotation_id": "30106546", "url": "https://genius.com/30106546", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "My brother put some holes in your top, you a Air Force", "vault_lines": ["My brother a flame thrower", "My brother put some holes in your top, you a Air Force"], "meaning": "Drake’s affiliate shoots someone in the head, leaving bullet holes in his “top.”\n\nDrake also makes a play on the iconic Nike Air Force 1 shoe line that features holes above the toe box for breathability.", "annotation_id": "30104016", "url": "https://genius.com/30104016", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "My brother make your ace disappear like a sleight of hand", "vault_lines": ["My brother a flame thrower", "My brother make your ace disappear like a sleight of hand"], "meaning": "Here, “ace” is referring to a person “of first or high rank or quality”, meaning someone’s go-to guy.\n\n“Sleight of hand” refers to fine motor skills used by magicians to entertain or manipulate the audience. It is closely associated with card tricks. The Ace (A) is one of 13 numbered cards in a card deck, appearing over four different shapes.", "annotation_id": "30103921", "url": "https://genius.com/30103921", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Got me a ranch in the H, fuck all the carryin’ cause", "vault_lines": ["Got me a ranch in the H, fuck all the carryin' cause"], "meaning": "Drake, during his second show in Houston on the It’s All A Blur Tour, announced that he found a place to live in Houston.\n\nThe H as it is affectionately known, is home to frequent collaborator and close friend, Travis Scott, as well as other rappers.\n\nHouston has become notoriously famous for it’s women and strip clubs that rappers and NBA players alike have shown a great attraction towards. He rapped about this on TSU (standing for Texas Southern University) on his 2023 album, Certified Lover Boy. So, by buying a house in Houston, he’s better suited to living a life in which he enjoys and finds comfort in outside of his home in the 6.", "annotation_id": "30103172", "url": "https://genius.com/30103172", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Secret admirers too embarrassed, they hide in the cut", "vault_lines": ["Secret admirers too embarrassed, they hide in the cut"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing a line on 8am in Charlotte, released on his previous album, For All The Dogs, where he raps;\n\nYou niggas obsessed with me, and it’s not on no hetero vibe\n\nDrake hints that people who are secret admirers of him are likely homosexual.", "annotation_id": "30103105", "url": "https://genius.com/30103105", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Blood is thicker than water, nigga, his iron is up And if you keep eyein’ me down, trust me, his iron is up", "vault_lines": ["Blood is thicker than water, nigga, his iron is up", "And if you keep eyein' me down, trust me, his iron is up"], "meaning": "“Blood is thicker than water” is an English proverb that means family bonds are stronger than other relationships, tying back into the theme of the song, it’s message and content.\n\nJust as iron is essential in the production of steel, used to manufacture weaponary, it is also essential to human life. Therefore, by blood being thicker than water, iron (which 70% of the body’s iron content is found in blood), increases.\n\nFurthermore, Drake raps on people who give him funny looks, eyeing him down. As a result, his iron which is a common reference for a gun, is now in his grip, ready to be shot if needed.", "annotation_id": "30103087", "url": "https://genius.com/30103087", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "And then you niggas whine to J. Prince like some grape growers", "vault_lines": ["And then you niggas whine to J. Prince like some grape growers"], "meaning": "James Prince or J Prince, is an American music executive and promoter. He is the CEO and co-founder of the Houston based record label Rap-A-Lot and is affiliated with artists such as Drake and Lil Wayne.\n\nDrake raps on Prince’s ability to recognise young rap talent and how young rappers seeking fame “whine” to him that they should be the next up-and-coming talent. He also writes how rappers run to J Prince to get their beef squashed with Drake.\n\n“Whine” also acts as a homonym for the word wine, which is the product of grape fermentation.\n\nAnother smart play on the word ‘whine’ acts as a way of saying how J Prince has his own brand of wine.", "annotation_id": "30103035", "url": "https://genius.com/30103035", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "But let me tell you ’bout my brother, though Let me tell you ’bout my brother, though, for real Let me tell you ’bout my brother Man, let me tell you ’bout my brother, though [Verse 2] My brother a tool toter, my brother a tombstoner Diamonds and violence made us bond, we grew closer Brother a clip loader, my brother a 6ix soldier That nigga’ll stick it to you for real, he a lint roller My brother an aimbotter, I bought him a Range Rover My brother a flame thrower", "vault_lines": ["But let me tell you 'bout my brother, though", "Let me tell you 'bout my brother, though, for real", "Let me tell you 'bout my brother", "Man, let me tell you 'bout my brother, though", "My brother a tool toter, my brother a tombstoner", "Diamonds and violence made us bond, we grew closer", "Brother a clip loader, my brother a 6ix soldier", "That nigga'll stick it to you for real, he a lint roller", "My brother an aimbotter, I bought him a Range Rover", "My brother a flame thrower", "Let me tell you 'bout my brother, though"], "meaning": "Despite no offical reports having been released about a blood brother to Drake, he’s commonly referred to many people close to him as his “brother”. Whether it be frequent collaborators 21 Savage and J. Cole, or fellow Toronto native and producer / engineer, 40, Drake holds many of his friends close to his heart in a way that he would a brother.\n\nHowever, Drake was most likely talking about Chubbs in this case, his head of security. He’s been referenced multitudes of times regarding “street shit”. As his “Capo”, Chubbs is Drake’s ear to the streets.", "annotation_id": "30102918", "url": "https://genius.com/30102918", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Same story every time, they heckle in repetition I’m top of the mountain, these niggas still down at base camp, they plannin’ they expedition", "vault_lines": ["Same story every time, they heckle in repetition", "I'm top of the mountain, these niggas still down at base camp, they plannin' they expedition"], "meaning": "The heckler in reference Drake is taking shots at in this line and the previous one is Joe Budden, who wrote a scathing review on Drake’s For All The Dogs. Budden is a former musician who has arguably gained a greater amount of fame from his self-titled podcast, rather than his rapping ability.\n\nDrake additionally writes that similar people to Joe also take shots at him from lower positions, all of which are unsuccessful.", "annotation_id": "30102816", "url": "https://genius.com/30102816", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Talkin’ loose, then hit me up after on some “Please, Drake listen, listen”", "vault_lines": ["Talkin' loose, then hit me up after on some \"Please, Drake listen, listen\""], "meaning": "This is a reference to artists who have beefed with Drake in the past, and then years later seek a feature or to be featured on a song by him, to which Drake declines and makes fun at.", "annotation_id": "30102793", "url": "https://genius.com/30102793", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Done a lot of postgame talkin’, but this one different", "vault_lines": ["Done a lot of postgame talkin', but this one different"], "meaning": "Drake raps;\n\nPostgame talkin'\n\nThis refers to a sports player being interviewed prior to a successful team or individual performance after a game.\n\nThis drop, Scary Hours III, also acts as a deluxe for his latest release, For All The Dogs, thus making it a post-album interview. The deluxe also help in getting certain things off of his chest after FATD wasn’t recieved as successfully as Drake hoped it would be.", "annotation_id": "30102780", "url": "https://genius.com/30102780", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "This like the storm before the calm, we’ll get to the vacation later", "vault_lines": ["This like the storm before the calm, we'll get to the vacation later"], "meaning": "Typically, the phrase goes, “the calm before the storm”, however, Drake’s writing alludes to the idea that these violent tracks are similar to that of a thunderstorm before the weather settles and relaxes, like his raps.", "annotation_id": "30102749", "url": "https://genius.com/30102749", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Boy, I’m on top of the cake, just like some caramel sauce", "vault_lines": ["Boy, I'm on top of the cake, just like some caramel sauce"], "meaning": "In cake recipes, caramel sauce is often drizzled on top of the dessert to add a deliciously sweet flavor.\n\n\n\nSticky cake with caramel sauce topping\n\n“Cake,” in this bar, refers to a slang term for money, where “on top of” means “in control of.” Drake is not literally on top of a cake, however, he has his finances safeguarded and secure. Drizzy previously referred to money as cake on 2022’s “Rich Flex”:\n\nShoutout to the 6ix, R.I.P to 8\nSwear this shit is gettin' ate, I’m on ten for the cake", "annotation_id": "30102616", "url": "https://genius.com/30102616", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "You niggas do your shootin’ on the web, you Spider-Man My brother robbin’ it down on you juniors like Iron Man", "vault_lines": ["You niggas do your shootin' on the web, you Spider-Man", "My brother robbin' it down on you juniors like Iron Man"], "meaning": "Drake mentions two of the most popular superheroes from Marvel comics, Spider-Man and Iron Man. The two crimefighters were created in the 1960s, having since expanded into media like films, video games, television, and books outside of comic books.\n\n\n\nSpider-Man’s powers alongside wall crawling, super strength, and agility, allow him to shoot webs from his wrists. Drake plays off of this ability, dissing his enemies who only “shoot on the web,” where “web” means the World Wide Web, a nod to his opposition only appearing threatening on the internet, and never in real life.\n\nDrake is accentuating his brother’s prowess in robberies, to the point where he can hand off his expertise to those who are younger and less experienced. The phrase “Robbin' it down to you juniors,” is an allusion to Robert Downey Jr., the actor synonymous with Iron Man after playing the character in Marvel’s cinematic universe for over ten years.", "annotation_id": "30102439", "url": "https://genius.com/30102439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "It’s me, him, and a white ting, we movin’ like Paramore", "vault_lines": ["It's me, him, and a white ting, we movin' like Paramore"], "meaning": "Drake makes reference to the successful American rock band Paramore.\n\n\n\nThe slang term “ting”, which has Jamaican and Caribbean origins, is used here to refer to a hot girl.", "annotation_id": "30101807", "url": "https://genius.com/30101807", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "CN Towerin’ over you niggas, I’m giant as fuck", "vault_lines": ["CN Towerin' over you niggas, I'm giant as fuck"], "meaning": "Drake references the CN Tower in Toronto, Canada, known as one of the 7 Wonders of the Modern World. Drake calls himself a giant, likening himself to the building that was awarded the World’s Tallest Building and Free-standing Structure in 1996.\n\nThe cover to Drake’s 2016 album, Views, features a photo of Drake sitting atop the CN Tower.", "annotation_id": "30101488", "url": "https://genius.com/30101488", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "I got two Virgil Benzes, one that I’m whippin’ right now And the other one, I’m keepin’ in mint condition", "vault_lines": ["I got two Virgil Benzes, one that I'm whippin' right now", "And the other one, I'm keepin' in mint condition"], "meaning": "Drake refers to his exclusive Mercedes Maybach S-Class Edition 100, with a starting price of $230,000, said to be modified by Virgil Abloh himself.\n\nDrake also featured the Project Maybach concept car designed by Virgil in the music video for “Sticky.” The luxury off-roader is a 1 of 1 design.", "annotation_id": "30101411", "url": "https://genius.com/30101411", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Y’all the type to catch a charge, head to the deposition And act like the rapper named after the sex position", "vault_lines": ["Y'all the type to catch a charge, head to the deposition", "And act like the rapper named after the sex position"], "meaning": "After being indicted on charges such as racketeering, armed robbery, and drug trafficking, 6ix9ine had the potential to face a life sentence in prison. Later pleading guilty and cooperating with prosecutors by giving up information on his Nine Trey associates and other crimes he witnessed, 6ix9ine managed to get a sentence of only two years in prison.\n\nDrake likens whomever he is talking down on to rapper 6ix9ine, stating that they will cooperate and snitch to get out of potential prison sentences.", "annotation_id": "30101184", "url": "https://genius.com/30101184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "That one day you wake up and tell ’em “Enough is enough” That’s how you gon’ find out you not Kobe Bryant to us Man, you not Kobe Bryant to us, at all", "vault_lines": ["That one day you wake up and tell 'em \"Enough is enough\"", "That's how you gon' find out you not Kobe Bryant to us", "Man, you not Kobe Bryant to us, at all"], "meaning": "Kobe Bryant was an American professional basketball player, often regarded as one of the greatest to play the sport. Following Kobe’s untimely death, he received the utmost praise from fans, critics, and other players, hence why Drake uses him as a metaphor for success following leaving one’s career.\n\nThis may be a reference to Joe Budden who he mentioned in the first verse as following Budden’s critical review of For All The Dogs, Drake responded to him in an Instagram comment in which he described his music career failed while also stating that no one respected or cared that he retired:\n\nYou retired and we never hung up your jersey we don’t even remember your number. We know you for doing this… you withdrew from rap not cause you accomplished all you need to it’s cause it wasn’t working for you.\n\nDrake states that following Budden’s retirement, he wasn’t treated with the respect he may have expected due to his rapping skills which he criticized Drake for.", "annotation_id": "30101181", "url": "https://genius.com/30101181", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "Imagine us gettin’ our validation from an ex-musician searchin’ for recognition", "vault_lines": ["Imagine us gettin' our validation from an ex-musician searchin' for recognition"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the ex-rapper turned podcaster Joe Budden’s critical review of Drake’s For All The Dogs.\n\nDrake responded through an Instagram comment, stating that Budden shouldn’t be critiquing others on something he’s failed at.", "annotation_id": "30101097", "url": "https://genius.com/30101097", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "line": "It’s like he playin’ EDM, that nigga a chain smoker", "vault_lines": ["It's like he playin' EDM, that nigga a chain smoker"], "meaning": "Drake makes a wordplay reference to the successful Electronic Dance Music (EDM) and New York City-originating duo, The Chainsmokers, which consists of members Andrew Taggart and Alex Pall.\n\nhttps://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcSbtZZezto9EVhINXz5fr9OKtTPw-_DOFb1PUz3ycUQoUfqO5oDCcJFzU9n&s=10\n\nDuring an interview with Billboard (October 2023), Alex Pall explained how the duo’s name originated:\n\nWe actually met each other while we were both taking a smoke break outside so it was serendipitous that we ended up adopting ‘The Chainsmokers’ as our name.\n\nCommonly, the term “chain smoker” refers to a person who usually smokes a lot of cigarettes, one after another.", "annotation_id": "30101040", "url": "https://genius.com/30101040", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stories About My Brother", "status": "done", "targets": 35, "clicked": 35, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-stories-about-my-brother-lyrics", "genius_title": "Stories About My Brother"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "You could thank me now, uh, go ’head", "vault_lines": ["You could thank me now, uh, go 'head", "It's uh-okay, you could thank me now", "Y-You could thank me now (Thank me now), uh, go 'head"], "meaning": "Drake leaves a pause in between the first stanza and go ‘head cleaverly leaving a space for you to meet his wish to thank him", "annotation_id": "40096964", "url": "https://genius.com/40096964", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "Uh, shallow nigga but deep enough to have gone swimming", "vault_lines": ["Uh, shallow nigga but deep enough to have gone swimming"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION:\n\nA shallow person is someone that lacks depth in intellect, knowledge, or emotions. To say that he’s deep enough to have gone swimming implies that he’s grown and has taken the time to reflect on his shallow ways, maybe to even improve beyond them.", "annotation_id": "28463177", "url": "https://genius.com/28463177", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "At this point me is who I am trying to save myself from", "vault_lines": ["At this point me is who I am trying to save myself from"], "meaning": "Drake could be saying a direct correlation to the internal everyday struggle in life that everyone faces “me vs me.” it is implied simply that you are at a constant war with the bad parts of yourself. Drake is taking it a step further by saying he has noticed that the bad parts of himself have taken over so severely to the point where he has no choice, but to save himself from what he knows will come if he gives into himself due to the new found fame, status, money and power.", "annotation_id": "39349826", "url": "https://genius.com/39349826", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "Lately I’ve been drinking like there’s a message in a bottle", "vault_lines": ["Lately I've been drinking like there's a message in a bottle"], "meaning": "Drinking as if he has something to search for at the bottom of the bottle. Drinking is used in a positive, celebratory manner, rather than a negative, alcholic  one.", "annotation_id": "37165584", "url": "https://genius.com/37165584", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "How long they choose to love you will never be your decision", "vault_lines": ["How long they choose to love you will never be your decision"], "meaning": "oop", "annotation_id": "21701497", "url": "https://genius.com/21701497", "state": "pending", "votes": -7, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "I rep the ATF in case you need to be reminded", "vault_lines": ["I rep the ATF in case you need to be reminded"], "meaning": "ATF stands for All Things Fresh, Drake’s old clique and label. He released his first two mixtapes, 2006’s Room for Improvement and 2007’s Comeback Season under ATF. It was also the name of a fan-made website that posted and promoted Drake’s music before he became famous that later shut down in 2011.\n\nATF eventually turned into October’s Very Own or OVO, Drake’s official record label he founded in 2012.", "annotation_id": "18559555", "url": "https://genius.com/18559555", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "Man, the good girls went silent on me They got a boyfriend, or left for college on me And all the bad ones I used to hit are friends now That make me wish I had a little less mileage on me", "vault_lines": ["Man, the good girls went silent on me", "They got a boyfriend, or left for college on me", "And all the bad ones I used to hit are friends now", "That make me wish I had a little less mileage on me"], "meaning": "“Mileage” is slang for sexual encounters, which Drake notes he has had a lot of. Later on Chris Brown’s June 2019 track, “No Guidance,” Drake uses the term again to talk about his romantic interest’s lack of sexual experience:\n\nYou just took it off the lot, no mileage", "annotation_id": "18559408", "url": "https://genius.com/18559408", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "I’m feeling like Nas, who am I to disagree?", "vault_lines": ["I'm feeling like Nas, who am I to disagree?"], "meaning": "On the chorus of Nas‘ October 1996 song, “Street Dreams,” he rapped:\n\nStreet dreams are made of these\nShorties on they knees, for niggas with big cheese\nWho am I to disagree?\n\nInterestingly, Nas was actually interpolating the chorus of Eurythmics‘ January 1983 song, “Sweet Dreams (Are Made of This)”:\n\nSweet dreams are made of this\nWho am I to disagree?\nI’ve traveled the world and the seven seas\nEverybody’s lookin' for something", "annotation_id": "18559304", "url": "https://genius.com/18559304", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "Fuck that old shit, I’m on new things OVO Clique, Red Wing Boot Gang", "vault_lines": ["Fuck that old shit, I'm on new things", "OVO Clique, Red Wing Boot Gang"], "meaning": "October’s Very Own, also known as OVO, is Drake’s group and record label, which he officially later founded in 2012 and Red Wing Shoes is an American brand that sells boots.\n\nAround the time of this song’s release, Drake was frequently seen wearing their boots and referred his group as the ‘Red Wing Boot Gang.’ He also tweeted this line in December 2009.\n\n\n\nDrake wearing a pair of Red Wing boots", "annotation_id": "18559044", "url": "https://genius.com/18559044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "It’s December 31st and we in Miami just meditating You got your resolutions, we just got reservations", "vault_lines": ["It's December 31st and we in Miami just meditating", "You got your resolutions, we just got reservations"], "meaning": "A New Year’s resolution is a personal goal that someone makes to better their lives for the new year. While people are making resolutions, Drake’s on the road touring and meditates before every show.\n\nDrake actually performed in Miami in 2010 during his Away from Home Tour. He performed at the James L. Knight Center for two nights, September 20th and 21st before moving on to Tampa on the 24th for the next showing.", "annotation_id": "18558530", "url": "https://genius.com/18558530", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "I guess a hit doesn’t add up to a career for you niggas I must have been hard to watch, what a year for you niggas", "vault_lines": ["I guess a hit doesn't add up to a career for you niggas", "I must have been hard to watch, what a year for you niggas"], "meaning": "A similar sentiment later appears on Nicki Minaj’s December 2010 song, “Moment 4 Life,” where Drake raps:\n\nThis supposed to be y'all year? We ain’t get the memo", "annotation_id": "18558500", "url": "https://genius.com/18558500", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "Alohas to women with no ties to men That I know well, that way there are no lies", "vault_lines": ["That I know well, that way there are no lies", "Alohas to women with no ties to men"], "meaning": "Drake continues speaking Hawaiian and says “hello” to the women that have never been with a member of his crew – that way she won’t have to lie to him about how serious the relationship was when she’s with him.", "annotation_id": "1485738", "url": "https://genius.com/1485738", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "They hear about your cons, but focus on your pros And love you for who you are from the bottom of they soul", "vault_lines": ["They hear about your cons, but focus on your pros", "And love you for who you are from the bottom of they soul"], "meaning": "Drake has love for those that ignore the bad shit getting plastered around by the media and focus on his good attributes.\n\nPros can also be read as “prose”, meaning people are focusing on Drake’s wordplay and pen game.", "annotation_id": "1268304", "url": "https://genius.com/1268304", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "It’s uh-okay, you could thank me now", "vault_lines": ["You could thank me now, uh, go 'head", "It's uh-okay, you could thank me now", "Y-You could thank me now (Thank me now), uh, go 'head", "It's a- okay (Okay)"], "meaning": "This started off the trend of Drake ending his albums with a line that contains the title to the album. He ends “The Ride” off of Take Care with “take care, nigga” and “Paris Morton Music 2” off of Nothing Was The Same with “nothing was the same, dawg”.", "annotation_id": "712627", "url": "https://genius.com/712627", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "They tell me I’m the hottest, guess we finally get to see", "vault_lines": ["They tell me I'm the hottest, guess we finally get to see"], "meaning": "Drake certainly had a buzz, but you can’t claim to be the “hottest in the game” until you drop a hot album. Thank Me Later debuted at #1 on Billboard and eventually went platinum, and Drake has been just about the hottest in the game ever since.", "annotation_id": "82392", "url": "https://genius.com/82392", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "Damn, I swear sports and music are so synonymous Cause we want to be them, and they want to be us, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Damn, I swear sports and music are so synonymous", "Cause we want to be them, and they want to be us, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake seems to be trying to explain why so many basketball players (Allen Iverson, Chris Webber, Kobe Bryant, Tony Parker, Ron Artest, Rasheed Wallace and Shaquille O'Neal) have all put out rap songs.\n\nMeanwhile, several rappers – The Game, Master P, Cam'ron etc. – moonlight as ballers\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kEp-N6pbxvI&t=26m38s", "annotation_id": "18332", "url": "https://genius.com/18332", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "And I know life is just a game in which the cards are facing down", "vault_lines": ["And I know life is just a game in which the cards are facing down"], "meaning": "Games with imperfect information (which a card game where other player’s cards are face down is) are very difficult to figure out mathematically, so you pretty much have to do the best you can with the information available to you. Drake compares this to his life, since he is pretty much figuring things out as he goes.", "annotation_id": "18011", "url": "https://genius.com/18011", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "I’m the black sheep but Chris Farley wears the crown", "vault_lines": ["I'm the black sheep but Chris Farley wears the crown"], "meaning": "A “black sheep” is a common expression for someone who doesn’t quite fit in with their peers. It was also the title of a comedy film starring Chris Farley (released when Drake was 9 years old!)", "annotation_id": "18010", "url": "https://genius.com/18010", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "And that’s around the time That your idols become your rivals You make friends with Mike But got to A.I. him for your survival", "vault_lines": ["And that's around the time", "That your idols become your rivals", "You make friends with Mike", "But got to A.I. him for your survival"], "meaning": "Growing up, it was Drake’s dream to befriend and hopefully work with some of the legends in the game. But hip-hop is so competitive that there’s no time for making friends, you have to try and knock off the legends if you want to earn your keep.\n\nThe same goes in basketball, where up-and-coming stars are constantly clashing with veterans hoping to overthrow them and cement their own legacy. Drake uses the example of Allen Iverson growing up his whole life admiring Michael Jordan, but scoring on him with a vicious crossover when it finally came time to play against him.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y3X274lz3wY\n\nIf Drake is AI, the best example of MJ would have to be Jay-Z. Jay started off as Drake’s mentor, even landing a guest verse on his debut album, but when Drake started feeling like the throne was for the taking he began taking shots at Jay to try and elevate his own status.", "annotation_id": "17992", "url": "https://genius.com/17992", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "I can relate to kids going straight to the league When they recognize that you got what it takes to succeed", "vault_lines": ["I can relate to kids going straight to the league", "When they recognize that you got what it takes to succeed"], "meaning": "Many of the best high school basketball players have to choose between college and going straight to the NBA. It’s a much-debated issue, and was dramatized in Spike Lee’s film He Got Game. Examples of players who went “straight to the league” are LeBron James, Kobe Bryant, Kevin Garnett, Dwight Howard, and Tracy McGrady.\n\n\n\nDrake feels like he can relate because he skipped college in favor of going directly into a career in entertainment, so he experienced fame and success before he could really develop emotionally.", "annotation_id": "17991", "url": "https://genius.com/17991", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "And we just want to party, Patron straight up", "vault_lines": ["And we just want to party, Patron straight up"], "meaning": "Patron is a high end brand of Tequila, and drinking it straight up means without any ice or mix.\n\n“Straight up OG” is a song by the rapper Patron who pronounces his stage name “Pat-Ron”.", "annotation_id": "17987", "url": "https://genius.com/17987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "line": "Uh, Mahalo from the hardest act to follow", "vault_lines": ["Uh, Mahalo from the hardest act to follow"], "meaning": "“Mahalo” means “thank you” in Hawaiian. Drake starts the outro of his debut album by thanking the listener.", "annotation_id": "17978", "url": "https://genius.com/17978", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Thank Me Now", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 22, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-thank-me-now-lyrics", "genius_title": "Thank Me Now"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "Even when I come back, I don’t know if it’s me that’s gonna be coming back", "vault_lines": ["Even when I come back, I don't know if it's me that's gonna be coming back", "I know you love me"], "meaning": "Throughout the song, Drake speaks on his past towards the beginning of his rap career and how he felt.\n\nComeback Season is Drake’s second official mixtape released on September 1, 2007.", "annotation_id": "40210364", "url": "https://genius.com/40210364", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "I tried to kill them boys with kindness, but they wouldn’t die", "vault_lines": ["I tried to kill them boys with kindness, but they wouldn't die"], "meaning": "Drake’s bar “I tried to kill them boys with kindness, but they wouldn’t die” takes a common cliché and twists it into something darker and ironic. On the surface, he’s saying that he tried to deal with his critics and enemies in a mature way, showing them respect instead of hostility. The expectation is that by being kind, he’d silence the negativity or win them over.\n\nBut the punch comes in the second half — even when he chose the high road, they didn’t stop coming at him. By flipping the phrase literally, Drake turns “kindness” into a failed weapon, highlighting both his frustration and the futility of trying to please everyone. Beneath that, it also reflects his usual stance in the industry: he often avoids direct beef, positioning himself as the bigger person, but this bar carries an undertone that if kindness doesn’t work, harsher measures might. It’s a slick mix of wit, self-awareness, and a subtle warning, all wrapped in one quick line.", "annotation_id": "36618077", "url": "https://genius.com/36618077", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "I mean, even with his salary, you can’t put no prices on that There is no salary cap, there is no payin’ him back, for real", "vault_lines": ["I mean, even with his salary, you can't put no prices on that", "There is no salary cap, there is no payin' him back, for real"], "meaning": "In the lines above Drake goes off on a small tangent about his friend’s contributions and loyalty while addressing them by name far before he made it to where he is now .\n\nTruly a measure of real brotherhood in the “salary cap line” meaning no amount of money can make for the actions that were shown.", "annotation_id": "31082357", "url": "https://genius.com/31082357", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "All these IOU’s, it’s hard for me to be keeping track", "vault_lines": ["All these IOU's, it's hard for me to be keeping track"], "meaning": "An IOU is a written promise that you will pay some money back that you have borrowed. (Abbreviation for “I owe you”)\n\nDrake is most likely linking this line with all of his past handouts that he’s given to people which is why the number could be hard to keep track.", "annotation_id": "31082325", "url": "https://genius.com/31082325", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "To my dogs in the game who wasn’t pick of the litter", "vault_lines": ["To my dogs in the game who wasn't pick of the litter"], "meaning": "Drake is known to acknowledge fellow artists in this case OVO artists who were not initially chosen as top picks.", "annotation_id": "30563574", "url": "https://genius.com/30563574", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "I cracked the door for us all, put my foot inside", "vault_lines": ["I cracked the door for us all, put my foot inside"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to providing opportunities so everyone close to him can step into the same realm of success.", "annotation_id": "30563533", "url": "https://genius.com/30563533", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "So now I kill ’em all for acting like they couldn’t die (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["So now I kill 'em all for acting like they couldn't die (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Double entrada from the line above", "annotation_id": "30563527", "url": "https://genius.com/30563527", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "Look, gotta hold my head high with two dry eyes", "vault_lines": ["Look, gotta hold my head high with two dry eyes"], "meaning": "This is a double entendre.\nFirst, it talks about being proud and ready to face obstacles by wiping away the tears caused by one’s pain.\nSecond, being high on marijuana, drys your eyes out and makes them red.", "annotation_id": "24165136", "url": "https://genius.com/24165136", "state": "pending", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "But I still don’t know where I get the calm from", "vault_lines": ["But I still don't know where I get the calm from"], "meaning": "This could be a callback to his classic track ‘The Calm’ from his 2009 mixtape So Far Gone.\n\nIn ‘The Calm’, Drake raps about all of the things causing him stress on his rise to stardom, but notes that for whatever reason he’s calm and confident in his future, despite being “the furthest thing from calm”. This theme is reflected all throughout Certified Lover Boy as well, and although Drake is now at a different place in life and a hugely successful artist, he still has many things to stress over (females, money, his son) but he somehow finds a way to stay calm and overcome.", "annotation_id": "24032178", "url": "https://genius.com/24032178", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "From the lemon faced radio host that love to be bitter", "vault_lines": ["From the lemon faced radio host that love to be bitter"], "meaning": "Drake is possibly referencing The Breakfast Club host Charlamagne Tha God, who mentioned Drake’s era being over on December 2020 on his Brilliant Idiots podcast:\n\nDrake has given us so much music that I don’t know if he has another gear. […] I don’t think we’re in a Drake era, I don’t think we’ve been in a Drake era for the past three or four years.\n\nFollowing Drake’s record of debuting at the top 3 on the top 100 hits following the release of his Scary Hours 2, Charlamagne was quick to accuse Drake of cheating his way to this record:\n\nI asked a question a few months ago and the question was simply: Are we still in the Drake era? When you’re number 1, 2 and 3 on the charts, I think that answers your question. When it comes to streaming and radio, he’s still your guy. […] Aubrey Graham is a demon who doesn’t play fair. He cheated. I can’t prove it. I have nothing to back this up. This might be pure hate. I’m not standing on nothing with this.\n\nCharlamagne and Drake’s relationship has always been a love-hate relationship. However, Drake does not appreciate Charlamagne’s bitterness and distrust in his talent, or his ability to be consistent with his music. Drake’s touched on these points before on Scorpion’s “Sandra’s Rose,” where he said:\n\nLike Charlamagne, I see the light and see the darkest patches\n\nIt’s also important to note the clever play on words employed by Drake, contrasting sour and bitter.  Although he mocked Charlamagne’s appearance and compared his head shape to a lemon, known to be sour in taste, Drake sees the influencer’s actions to be completely bitter—to be filled with harmful feeling. Nevertheless, both are considered to be unpleasant tastes.", "annotation_id": "23928808", "url": "https://genius.com/23928808", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "I’m taking heads off, Future out here like Irving Azoff", "vault_lines": ["I'm taking heads off, Future out here like Irving Azoff"], "meaning": "Irving Azoff is chairman of Full Stop Management, a record label that recognizes and signs recording artists. Examples on these artists include Travis Scott, Nicki Minaj, Harry Styles and Bon Jovi.\n\nDrake compares Irving’s ability to recognize and sign talents to that of his manager Future the Prince, who manages OVO sound. OVO sound is Drake’s record label,  managed by his lifelong friends Oliver El-Khatib and Future the Prince. The label has signed several notable artists, including PARTYNEXTDOOR, dvsn, Roy Woods and Majid Jordan.\n\nThis is not the first time Drake has shown his appreciation to Future’s contribution towards his success. On his 2018’s “Nonstop,” Drake said:\n\nFuture took the business and ran it for me\nI let Ollie take the owl, told him brand it for me", "annotation_id": "23927035", "url": "https://genius.com/23927035", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "Please don’t google my net worth, the numbers are way off I mean, if you comparin’ it to what I really made off with I’m more like Bernie with all the earnings that they came off Trust me it was nothin’ light, holmes They tried to give me a slice of the pie, and I took the knife home", "vault_lines": ["Please don't google my net worth, the numbers are way off", "I mean, if you comparin' it to what I really made off with", "I'm more like Bernie with all the earnings that they came off", "Trust me it was nothin' light, holmes", "They tried to give me a slice of the pie, and I took the knife home", "I know you love me"], "meaning": "As Drake raps on Having Our Way:\n\nBillionaires talk to me different when they see my paystub from Lucian Grainge\n\nDrake lets us know he’s made far more money than any public estimation can calculate. He compares his wealth to that of billionaire fraudster Bernie Madoff, using “made off” to allude to his surname. It’s not uncommon for net worth calculations to be inaccurate. However, it’s unusual for any musical artist to have the wealth Drake seemingly has. With the prominence of 360-deals and labels having ownership of master recordings, artists usually only make off with a small slice of profit from the music industry with little room for negotiation and leverage — a practise Drake has experienced himself under Birdman and Cash Money Records.\n\nSince 2019’s Care Package, it seems Drake’s projects have been released under his own OVO Sound under exclusive license to Republic Records. It’s safe to assume, after finishing his deal with Cash Money, Drake and his team leveraged a deal with Republic and UMG where Drake was able to cut himself a big slice of profit and, as he alludes to later, ownership of his master recordings.\n\nAt this point in Drake’s career, he wields the knife in any business deal.", "annotation_id": "23876941", "url": "https://genius.com/23876941", "state": "pending", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "Dropped out of school ’cause nobody was teaching that The Best Western is where a nigga was sleeping at", "vault_lines": ["Dropped out of school 'cause nobody was teaching that", "The Best Western is where a nigga was sleeping at"], "meaning": "Both lines seem to take aim at the school system, and Drake starts by reminding the audience that he had famously “dropped out of school#Early_life)” at the age 15. He never saw value in what he was being taught, and instead chose to pursue his acting career.\n\nThe second line seems to reference both the Best Western hotel & resort chain, as well as Ontario’s Western University; a post-secondary institution popular among graduates from Drake’s hometown Toronto.\n\nSimilar to his views towards high school, Drake makes it clear that he sees little value in a college degree, as he feels the “best” Western is in fact the hotel as opposed to the distinguished university.", "annotation_id": "23876113", "url": "https://genius.com/23876113", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "Daemen College booking me to pull up and speak the facts", "vault_lines": ["Daemen College booking me to pull up and speak the facts"], "meaning": "Drake has performed at Daemen College, New York on Springfest 2009. He returned to the state on his Summer Sixteen tour and gave a shoutout to the college during the August 12 concert.\n\nDrake during the Springfest 2009 at Daemen College, New York:\n\n\n\nDaemen College has honored the return of Drake in the state in this post:\n\n      \n\n\n \n  View this post on Instagram\n\n \n   \n\n  \n\n   \n\n   \nA post shared by Daemen College (@daemencollege)", "annotation_id": "23875400", "url": "https://genius.com/23875400", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "Y’all music gets watered down, when you love ’em back And you know what I’m on, blowing past ya, owning masters Said you’re like a father figure to me, but now daddy’s not around so I’m a bastard Oh, of course, the only villain that show remorse when I owe you more Soft spot for all the ones that came before, I can’t ignore it", "vault_lines": ["I know you love me", "Y'all music gets watered down, when you love 'em back", "And you know what I'm on, blowing past ya, owning masters", "Said you're like a father figure to me, but now daddy's not around so I'm a bastard", "Oh, of course, the only villain that show remorse when I owe you more", "Soft spot for all the ones that came before, I can't ignore it"], "meaning": "Drake’s final verse on the album concludes with more shots at Kanye West and the deterioration of his music. Drake continues to emphasize his lead against West in the rap game, as he did previously on the single “Laugh Now, Cry Later”:\n\nDistance between us is not like a store, this isn’t a closeable gap, ayy\n\nDrake also mocks West’s recent rants about him not owning his masters to his music. He then references how the two once had a close relationship, which fell off once Kanye tricked Drake with the release of “Lift Yourself”. Drake claims he showed remorse in their beef by not directly retaliating to Pusha T’s  diss track “The Story of Adidon”, perhaps due to the fact that Pusha and Kanye came before Drake in the hip hop scene.", "annotation_id": "23874599", "url": "https://genius.com/23874599", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "Hard to pay 40, pay Noel, pay Niko back", "vault_lines": ["Hard to pay 40, pay Noel, pay Niko back"], "meaning": "“40”, “Noel” and “Niko” are just some of the producers, music engineers and friends that comprise Drake’s Toronto based record label OVO Sound. Two of which feature within multiple production credits on Certified Lover Boy.\n\nDrake has been known to feature the names of his musical production partners on his tracks in the past.\n\nYeah, this that Oliver, 40, Niko shit man\n\nDrake – Know Yourself", "annotation_id": "23874423", "url": "https://genius.com/23874423", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "People recognize me from the TV, but I’m done with that", "vault_lines": ["People recognize me from the TV, but I'm done with that"], "meaning": "Drake started his career in the spotlight in 2001, when he played the role of Jimmy Brooks in the Canadian teen drama show Degrassi: The Next Generation, at the age of 15.", "annotation_id": "23874295", "url": "https://genius.com/23874295", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "Look, me and Lil Sandra bagging CDs in the bubble wrap", "vault_lines": ["Look, me and Lil Sandra bagging CDs in the bubble wrap"], "meaning": "Opening the introspective track, Drake reflects on his humble beginnings in Toronto, packaging CDs of his early mixtapes with his mother, Sandra Graham.\n\nDrake independently released his debut mixtape, Room for Improvement, in 2006, while still starring in the series Degrassi: The Next Generation.", "annotation_id": "23874292", "url": "https://genius.com/23874292", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Remorse", "line": "Can’t picture bein’ a hubby, finger too stubby to fit a ring on Unless Kawhi wanna run it back", "vault_lines": ["Can't picture bein' a hubby, finger too stubby to fit a ring on", "Unless Kawhi wanna run it back"], "meaning": "Professional basketball player Kawhi Leonard was traded to the Toronto Raptors after seven seasons with the San Antonio Spurs in 2018. The following year, in June, he led the Raptors to their first NBA Championship.\n\nIn an October 2022 interview with Howard Stern, he advances in his future plans. Drake finds difficulty in starting a family:\n\nI think that eventually once all this is said and done for us and that addiction of work and success and forward movement is over, I feel like we’re all gonna need somethin' real, hopefully, it’s not too late. But then like right now, I feel like I get into this habit of datin' like […] four, five women to make one woman ‘cause I hate these two things about this girl and I like what this girl does for a living. I honestly, hopefully, I find somebody. Biggest thing is that I need to be inspired […] I don’t know how to find that with the porn question in there. I just need all those things.\n\nGiven his low motivation of seeking a wife, in a December 2019 interview with Rap Radar, he decides to place his routine first above a partner:\n\nI love […] my space, I love my work and I love my routine. And for me to break that for somebody, it would have to be a really special person that fits into that puzzle and that is supportive of the things I’ve been doing. Has to be somebody who has taste in music. It has to be somebody […] that I get along with so much to the point when we’re separate, I’m feeling I can’t function without their presence. I have come across it a few times. I’ve yet to be able to hold onto it, for whatever reason. I just really […] I’m the captain of the ship and I look behind me and I see a lot of people on board. Full steam ahead, that’s just how I gotta keep rockin' right now. And hopefully, I can find somebody that can just stand beside me by the wheel and help me steer, while we keep the journey going, apposed to me havin' to pull over and stop ‘cause that person’s gettin’ seasick.\n\nAs opposed to buying a wedding ring, Drake would preferably see Kawhi return to Drake’s home city, leading the Raptors to victory so that he can wear an NBA Championship ring, awarded to the winners of the NBA Finals.\n\nDespite the custom rings made for the NBA players, Drake proposed a custom-made ring from the Raptor’s first Championship.\n\n\n\nPictured: Drake showing his Raptors ring.", "annotation_id": "23874255", "url": "https://genius.com/23874255", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
