{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Just", "vault_lines": ["I just want to get paid and cut", "She wanna go Nobu Malibu just for the highlight", "I just got the groove back just like Angela Bassett", "Time just moves by too slow"], "meaning": "Judge*", "annotation_id": "37249255", "url": "https://genius.com/37249255", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Three up Baby M outta you know Need your back around, we was too close Choices that you made to reach new goals I know you got regret about a few of those Three up Baby M outta you know Need you back around so we can do growth", "vault_lines": ["And I know your friends too", "Three up Baby M outta you know", "Need your back around, we was too close", "Choices that you made to reach new goals", "I know you got regret about a few of those", "Need you back around so we can do growth", "I need you back around, we was too close", "I know we got regret about a few of those", "I need you back around"], "meaning": "Three up is a slang term meaning, “to free someone from jail or prison”.\n\nBaby M or Mirna Habib, is one of Drake’s recurring muses in his lyrics about certain women in his life. Most notably, she had a vocal feature on For All The Dogs with the song being, Calling For You featuring 21 Savage. Some speculate that her part in the song was her ranting about a failed vacation with basketball star Dillon Brooks, -who at the time- was beefing with Drake.\n\nIn the same album but on a different song, Another Late Night with Lil Yachty, Drake raps about Brooks saying:\n\n…Shorty rinsin’ Dillon Brooks, can’t believe this nigga talkin’ damn.\n\nAlluding to how Habib was displeased on her vacation gone wrong with Brooks. Drake has referred to Mirna Habib as Baby M many times in past songs:\n\n\nBaby M, you know I got a heart of gold. You pawned that shit and hit the road.\n*Hours In Silence\n\nFree Baby M out the shackles and chains.\n*All The Parties\n\n… I tell Baby M what she did to the kid, then eyeliner like emo.\n*On The Radar Freestyle\n\n\nIn October of 2022, Mirna Habib was charged with aggravated identity theft in a U.S. court and was sentenced to four months in prison. Allegedly, Drake paid for the court and legal fees, and was even in disguise at the court hearings in full prosthetics and make up.\n\nSome fans also speculate that this song and many other Drake songs have been about Baby M and how he spent a lot of money on her despite still being with Dillon Brooks which Drake views as a deception to their own friendship. Since Mirna was unsatisfied with how Brooks treated her, Drake feels like he wasted his time and energy on building even a friendship with her in the first place.", "annotation_id": "37247945", "url": "https://genius.com/37247945", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Long live F-Dot", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Fernando “FDot” Johnson was a British drill rapper who passed away on January 26, 2023, after being ambused and stabbed nearby his house at the age of 23.\n\n\n\nFDot was a member of the 12Anti gang, a group Central Cee was known to have had a close relationship with. The group even celebrated along when Central Cee’s mixtape 23 debuted at number one on the UK Albums Chart.", "annotation_id": "36983180", "url": "https://genius.com/36983180", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "We don’t got a G check DJ’s cuz opps don’t get played at clubs", "vault_lines": ["We don't got a G check DJ's cuz opps don't get played at clubs"], "meaning": "“G checking” (short for gang checking) someone means to testing or challenge that they are as tough and gangster as they present themself to be. This is often seen as highly disrespectful. Because the DJ’s don’t play music of Cench’s opps (opponents/rivals/enemies) at the club, the DJ’s do not need to be G checked.", "annotation_id": "36982391", "url": "https://genius.com/36982391", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Lambo parked in the landing strip", "vault_lines": ["Lambo parked in the landing strip"], "meaning": "Cench takes this line from his song BAND4BAND with Lil Baby. A lambo is slang for the luxury Italian automobile manufacturer Lamborghini. Cench is able to drive his Lambo right up to the landing strip/runway and park it there, prior to his flight likely on a private jet.", "annotation_id": "36982355", "url": "https://genius.com/36982355", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "I’m in Beverly Hills with a Hollywood actress They keep laughin’ when I say bottle of water Gonna say I got a weird accent", "vault_lines": ["I'm in Beverly Hills with a Hollywood actress", "They keep laughin' when I say bottle of water", "Gonna say I got a weird accent"], "meaning": "The city of Beverly Hills is known for its affluent lifestyle, luxury homes, upscale shopping and dining, and celebrities and wealthy residents.\n\nCench is from the Shepherd’s Bush neighborhood in West London.\nThe Hollywood actress he is with comments on his funny British accent and finds it funny how he says the words “bottle of water”.\n\nCench used a similar line in his song St. Patrick’s\n\n“These American girls keep laughin' any time I say, \"Bottle of water”\n\nhttps://genius.com/34321921", "annotation_id": "36982281", "url": "https://genius.com/36982281", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Sneaky link, no strings attached", "vault_lines": ["Sneaky link, no strings attached"], "meaning": "A “sneaky link” is slang for a casual, secret sexual or romantic relationship that is not known to others. There are “no strings attached” in this relationship, because both partners agree they don’t have expectations of support or exclusivity from the other.", "annotation_id": "36982256", "url": "https://genius.com/36982256", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "I paid for the crib, alright, no interest rate I ain’t payin’ it monthly", "vault_lines": ["I paid for the crib, alright, no interest rate", "I ain't payin' it monthly", "No rest for the wicked"], "meaning": "When a person pays cash for a home (a crib), they own it outright, immediately. Most people are not wealthy enough to do this, and instead purchase the home through a mortgage, that contains monthly payments and an interest rate.\nCench is wealthy enough to have bought the home outright and does not have a mortgage with an interest rate and monthly payments.", "annotation_id": "36982220", "url": "https://genius.com/36982220", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "She wanna go Nobu Malibu just for the highlight She ain’t even hungry", "vault_lines": ["She wanna go Nobu Malibu just for the highlight", "She ain't even hungry"], "meaning": "Nobu Malibu is a high end restaraunt in  Malibu California, that offers an exceptional blend of exquisite Japanese cuisine and a scenic oceanfront ambiance. Nobu was founded by the iconic Chef Nobuyuki Matsuhisa, who partnered with Robert De Niro to create the brand.\n\nThis woman wants to go to the restaurant not because she is hungry, but to feature on social media that she was able to eat there, specifically on Instagram where you can “highlight” on your profile previous stories you have posted.", "annotation_id": "36982170", "url": "https://genius.com/36982170", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "They don’t give me credit but I’d rather take the cash shit", "vault_lines": ["They don't give me credit but I'd rather take the cash shit"], "meaning": "Drake as always be talked down on, despite proving over  and over again that he can actually rap ,people have always try to water than his rapping performance calling him a pop star I even labeling him a culture vulture which was over the internet during 2024 be with Kendrick Lamar and 20 V1\n\nDrake is saying that you’re already knows that they will never give him his flowers so he’s going for the cash.", "annotation_id": "36981387", "url": "https://genius.com/36981387", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Why you pay for law school if you keep skippin’ classes?", "vault_lines": ["Why you pay for law school if you keep skippin' classes?"], "meaning": "Drake has expressed disappointment from students failure to stick to their studies, he has made similar  sentence in a song\"Heat of the moment\" where he rapped\"all these high school kids are so sick of book and learning they don’t read anymore ,they don’t even read anymore,\"\n\nYou’re so mess such reference in his 2015 song\"Jungle\" off “if you’re reading this is too late mixtape” where he rapped\"these days the new girls they get me nervous they go to school and do bottle service they can’t decide they keep switching majors\".\n\nDrake understands the need to be educated which made him go back to school even after dropping out earlier before purshing his musical career", "annotation_id": "36981338", "url": "https://genius.com/36981338", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "I ain’t blowin’ out candles being romantic Baby - I’m blowin’ your back out", "vault_lines": ["I ain't blowin' out candles being romantic", "Baby - I'm blowin' your back out"], "meaning": "Rather than creating a romantic evening with candles, Cench is planning on having rough sex with the woman. He contrasts blowing out the romantic candles with blowing the woman’s back out, a euphemism for vigorous and intense sex, in which the person may have trouble with walking or back pain as a result, similar to when someone “blows their back out” with a back injury.", "annotation_id": "36980862", "url": "https://genius.com/36980862", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Hella petite like Bella Hadid so I handle that delicately, she fragile", "vault_lines": ["Hella petite like Bella Hadid so I handle that delicately, she fragile"], "meaning": "Isabella Khair Hadid is a famous American model. Keeping with the model theme from the previous line, Cench compares the body of the woman he is with to Hadid’s slender frame, which he views as fragile. As a result, he is being careful to treat her gently.", "annotation_id": "36980844", "url": "https://genius.com/36980844", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Now fucking on UK’s next top model that’s our generation, Naomi Campbell", "vault_lines": ["Now fucking on UK's next top model that's our generation, Naomi Campbell"], "meaning": "Naomi Campbell is a British supermodel who was one of the most prominent models of her generation.\n\nCench is having sex with the current top supermodels, the Naomi Campbell’s of this generation.", "annotation_id": "36980818", "url": "https://genius.com/36980818", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Fuckin’ in the Trump Tower, tryna get me cancelled", "vault_lines": ["Fuckin' in the Trump Tower, tryna gеt me cancelled", "Fuckin' in the Trump Tower, tryna get me cancelled"], "meaning": "Trump Tower is a 58-story, 663-foot-tall skyscraper at 721–725 Fifth Avenue in the Midtown Manhattan neighborhood of New York City, famously owned by Donald Trump. It contains both luxury hotel rooms and apartments. Drake and this woman are having sex in the tower, but Drake fears he may get “cancelled” for staying there because of Donald Trump’s controversial and unpopular reputation.", "annotation_id": "36980788", "url": "https://genius.com/36980788", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "I just got the groove back just like Angela Bassett", "vault_lines": ["I just got the groove back just like Angela Bassett"], "meaning": "How Stella Got Her Groove Back is a 1998 American romantic comedy-drama film which stars Angela Bassett.\n\nIn the context of the song, Drake is back on the top of his game and has his groove back.", "annotation_id": "36980755", "url": "https://genius.com/36980755", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "No rest for the wicked", "vault_lines": ["I paid for the crib, alright, no interest rate", "No rest for the wicked"], "meaning": "“No rest for the wicked” is a common self-deprecating english idiom. It suggests that difficulties or consequences arise as a result of the person’s own choices to not conduct their life in the most moral way.\n\nThe phrase originates from the biblical verse Isaiah 57:21: “There is no peace, says my God, for the wicked”\n\nCench is saying that his hectic life and inability to rest is a result of the life he has chosen for himself.", "annotation_id": "36980674", "url": "https://genius.com/36980674", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "line": "Before I give you my Insta password I’ll give you the pin to my Amex", "vault_lines": ["Before I give you my Insta password I'll give you the pin to my Amex"], "meaning": "Central Cee describes how he would rather allow the woman to spend his money on his American Express card than look through his instagram account messages. He used this lyric in his 2023 hit song “Sprinter”.", "annotation_id": "36980652", "url": "https://genius.com/36980652", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trump Tower", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 20, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-central-cee-trump-tower-lyrics", "genius_title": "Trump Tower"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "When they told me take an R&B nigga on the road And I told ’em no and drew for Kendrick and Rocky", "vault_lines": ["When they told me take an R&B nigga on the road", "And I told 'em no and drew for Kendrick and Rocky"], "meaning": "Urged by promoters to bring an R&B supporting act on his 2012 Club Paradise Tour as a safer promo play, Drake declined and instead brought in two of the hottest newcomers then, Kendrick Lamar and A$AP Rocky.\n\n\n\nDrake on stage with Kendrick Lamar and A$AP Rocky during the tour", "annotation_id": "19638666", "url": "https://genius.com/19638666", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Architects takin’ dimensions, they redoin’ the entrance Yeah, redoin’ the entrance", "vault_lines": ["Architects takin' dimensions, they redoin' the entrance", "Yeah, redoin' the entrance"], "meaning": "In early 2016, Drake’s architects presented plans to the Toronto City Council of Drake’s brand new 21,000 square-foot-mega-mansion which includes a basketball court, spa, indoor pool and jersey museum.\n\nApparently, the house is so large that it violates a number of zoning restrictions on building height and driveway width. This forced Drake’s people into a meeting to discuss the size limits of the house, to obtain a number of zoning bylaw exemptions in order to build it as planned.\n\nAfter the meeting with the City Council, it was determined that the new mansion is too big for the city’s richest neighbourhood.\n\nIn order to have his dream home built, they must take more dimensions and edit the size of the driveway, which could be considered the entrance, and the building height.\n\nThe architect referenced here is Ferris Rafauli and his team, the same architects behind the design of the rapper’s exclusive space inside the Air Canada Centre, Sher Club.", "annotation_id": "9280772", "url": "https://genius.com/9280772", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Green, white, and red on my body ’cause I’m dipped in Ferrari", "vault_lines": ["Green, white, and red on my body 'cause I'm dipped in Ferrari"], "meaning": "Green, white, and red are the colors of the Italian flag. Ferrari, Drake’s favourite vehicle at the moment, is an Italian-made sports car.\n\nDipped is slang for “dressed in,” so Drake could also be implying he’s wearing Gucci, an Italian brand that has a “red, green and white” theme.", "annotation_id": "9280746", "url": "https://genius.com/9280746", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "These guys move so greezy nowadays", "vault_lines": ["These guys move so greezy nowadays"], "meaning": "Drake’s commenting that a lot of the other rappers in the game; both up and comers and established rappers, will do anything to disparage Drizzy’s career in order to assert themselves in this game.\n\n“Greezy” is popular slang meaning slick/sly, someone not to be trusted.", "annotation_id": "9280340", "url": "https://genius.com/9280340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "The rest of you boys, I blow Keeks right in your face", "vault_lines": ["The rest of you boys, I blow Keeks right in your face"], "meaning": "Keysh—short for Keysha and slang for Kush—is one of the many nicknames for weed.\n\nBlowing smoke in someone’s face is viewed as a very disrespectful gesture. Drake calling the individuals “boys” instead of men drives home the fact that there’s little to no respect left.", "annotation_id": "9280279", "url": "https://genius.com/9280279", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Y’all don’t hear no songs They hit my phone like, “You did that” They even hit my line like, “Where you been at?” It’s always on some shit like", "vault_lines": ["Y'all don't hear no songs", "They hit my phone like, \"You did that\"", "They even hit my line like, \"Where you been at?\"", "It's always on some shit like"], "meaning": "Drake has to deal with fake friends, people only associating with him to reap the benefits of his success. He claims they pretend to listen to his music and blame him for not keeping in touch. Drake has discussed this many times on record, particularly on “Started From The Bottom”:\n\nNo new niggas, nigga, we don’t feel that\nFuck a fake friend, where you real friends at?\n\n\n\nHe also touched on this deterioration in old relationships more recently on his hit single “Hotline Bling,” outlining the estrangement between him and a past girlfriend.", "annotation_id": "9280266", "url": "https://genius.com/9280266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "I tell you my life and y’all don’t believe me when I say it Save my stories for down the line I’m too ahead of the curve every time", "vault_lines": ["I tell you my life and y'all don't believe me when I say it", "Save my stories for down the line", "I'm too ahead of the curve every time"], "meaning": "Tracks like “Started From The Bottom” induced a torrent of surprise on the internet, when people claimed he didn’t actually start from the bottom. During “Star67” he rapped:\n\nI was on TV makin' fifty racks a year\nAfter helpin' mama out the shit would disappear\nI am not a man, I can’t do this on my own\nSo I started askin' them if they would put me on\n\nDrake utilizes the metaphor of being “too ahead of the curve every time”; he’s too advanced and progressed for people to even comprehend, leading to people being unable to believe him.", "annotation_id": "9280086", "url": "https://genius.com/9280086", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Just total the hits and see what you find, you SWV ’Cause you weak and I’m always, always on your mind", "vault_lines": ["Just total the hits and see what you find, you SWV", "'Cause you weak and I'm always, always on your mind"], "meaning": "In 2015 Drake had his 100th Billboard 100 hit, and in 2016 he finally achieved his first number one with “One Dance.”\n\nTotal and SWV were 90s female R&B groups and rivals. Drake currently has the most number one hits of any hip-hop/R&B artists and just beat out Justin Bieber for a number one spot on Spotify by being the most streamed artist in the platform’s history.\n\nThe two lines categorize “weak” rappers who don’t have the potential to grow or stay consistent, because they keep focusing on Drake and his greatness instead of trying to build something amazing of their own.\n\nTotal was signed by Bad Boy Records (Puff Daddy’s label), but SWV lasted longer as a group. Subliminal maybe? This also a reference to SWV’s hit songs “Weak” and “You’re Always On My Mind.”", "annotation_id": "9280016", "url": "https://genius.com/9280016", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Don’t push me ’cause I’m way too uneasy nowadays", "vault_lines": ["Don't push me 'cause I'm way too uneasy nowadays"], "meaning": "A reference to a section of the hook in Puff Daddy’s “Can’t Nobody Hold Me Down” which,  goes:\n\nDon’t push us, cause we’re close to the, edge\n\nThe original line (and sample from the Puff Daddy song) came from a famous Grandmaster Flash & The Furious Five song called “The Message”:\n\nDon’t push me ’cause I’m close to the edge\nI’m trying not to lose my head\nIt’s like a jungle sometimes\nIt makes me wonder how I keep from going under", "annotation_id": "9279648", "url": "https://genius.com/9279648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "The higher I get, the less they accept me", "vault_lines": ["The higher I get, the less they accept me"], "meaning": "The more successful Drake gets, the more criticism he receives. Views went platinum in just one week, and is his most successful album of 2016, but Drake still receives criticism from the media and people within the music industry.\n\nHe spoke on how people in the music industry are appreciating him less because he’s beginning to surpass them during an interview with Zane Lowe:\n\nI’ve always felt like an outsider naturally. […] When you start doing well, people will make you feel like an outsider", "annotation_id": "9279644", "url": "https://genius.com/9279644", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Bad breed, bad bwoy Uh-huh, yeah That’s right, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Bad breed, bad bwoy"], "meaning": "In a clearer translation:\n\nBad breed, bad boy\n\n“Bad breed, bad bwoy” is representation of Jamaican Patois, a mix of English and West African languages. Drake may have picked up a few words from Popcaan considering the two have collaborated a bit in the past.\n\nDrizzy may be saying that he’s a bigger than Puffy who owns Bad Boy Records, and also anyone else who thinks they’re big and bad in the game.", "annotation_id": "9279623", "url": "https://genius.com/9279623", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "And you know me as a Cris’ bottle sender, check picker-upper", "vault_lines": ["And you know me as a Cris' bottle sender, check picker-upper"], "meaning": "Drake boasts that he’s the one in the club sending bottles of Cristal to people. Cristal is an expensive champagne produced in France by Louis Roederer.\n\nThis may also be an allusion to Drake’s 2012 fight with Chris Brown in a NYC nightclub. In 2012, Drake and Brown fought when the two got into a fight after Drake allegedly sent Brown a bottle with a disparaging note about Rihanna. While they squashed the beef, it resurfaced in 2015.\n\nDrake is also a noted bottle sender, lacing Charlamagne Tha God with 6 bottles of Dom Perignon after he mentioned sending bottles to the radio personality in “Back to Back.”", "annotation_id": "9279599", "url": "https://genius.com/9279599", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "“When can I get a favor?” or, “Where my bitch at?” Like I’m about to tell you where she been at Costa Careyes, I got her kidnapped She ain’t sorry and I ain’t sorry, it’s too late for sorry", "vault_lines": ["They even hit my line like, \"Where you been at?\"", "\"When can I get a favor?\" or, \"Where my bitch at?\"", "Like I'm about to tell you where she been at", "Costa Careyes, I got her kidnapped", "She ain't sorry and I ain't sorry, it's too late for sorry"], "meaning": "Costa Careyes is a popular holiday destination in Mexico. Drake is “kidnapping” these women; taking them to locations their ex’s can’t find or can’t afford to go.\n\nThis may be connected to his earlier reference to Joe Budden, after he was spotted with Budden’s ex Tahiry Jose on vacation.\n\n\n\nIt may also be a reference to Justin Bieber’s ex-girlfriend Hailey Baldwin, with whom Drake was spotted “wearing the same kind of necklace” after their alleged date in June 2016. Bieber’s massive comeback single was “Sorry,” Drake may be using that to show he is cold blooded when it comes to women.\n\nPossibly a reference to Serena Williams, whom Drake was rumoured to be dating. During the video portion of Beyoncé’s “Sorry,” Williams appeared with the words “I ain’t sorry” appearing on the screen.", "annotation_id": "9279571", "url": "https://genius.com/9279571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Got the rose pink tinted lenses, it’s a Wednesday", "vault_lines": ["Got the rose pink tinted lenses, it's a Wednesday"], "meaning": "“Rose colored glasses” refers to a person seeing life through positive eyes. The pink tinted glasses are his gold frame vintage Cartier’s. Even on a Wednesday Drake is having fun, despite being in the middle of the working week. On “Headlines” he rapped:\n\nDrinking every night because we drink to my accomplishments\n\nThis maybe a reference to a quote from the movie Mean Girls:\n\nOn Wednesdays we wear pink!", "annotation_id": "9279562", "url": "https://genius.com/9279562", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "He be like, “Drake, will you please stop smoking la-la?” Chubbs, why try? I’m a thug, I’ma die high", "vault_lines": ["He be like, \"Drake, will you please stop smoking la-la?\"", "Chubbs, why try? I'm a thug, I'ma die high"], "meaning": "Drake references Puff Daddy and Ma$e’s song “Can’t Nobody Hold Me Down,” which this song also samples. Specifically, the line refers to one of Mase’s verses:\n\n(Mase can you please stop smoking lah lah?)\nPuff why try? I’m a thug, I'mma die high\n\nHowever, Drake switches out Mase & Puff for himself and Chubbs, one of his OVO crew members.\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake has borrowed Ma$e bars. On “Worst Behavior,” Drake re-purposed a portion of Ma$e’s verse on The Notorious B.I.G.’s “Mo' Money Mo' Problems,” which also features Puff.\n\nWho’s hot, who not? Tell me who rock, who sell out in stores?\nYou tell me who flop, who copped the new drop, whose jewels got rocks", "annotation_id": "9279506", "url": "https://genius.com/9279506", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "A-ha-ha-ha, ha", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is laughing at all the enemies he’s made and possibly the use of words to refer to his hit single, “One Dance.”\n\nHowever, these lines mirror Diddy’s laugh from his collaboration with Mase on “Can’t Nobody Hold Me Down.” (itself borrowed from the Melle Mel’s laugh on “The Message.”) Probably because of how rich Drizzy and Diddy are, both having over $60 million dollars from rap.\n\nIn addition, Drake also makes a direct reference to Puff’s song in the previous lyrics:\n\nHe be like, “Drake, will you please stop smoking la la?”\nChubbs, why try, I’m a thug, I'mma die high", "annotation_id": "9279404", "url": "https://genius.com/9279404", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "My summer diet is just Rosé and calamari", "vault_lines": ["My summer diet is just Rosé and calamari"], "meaning": "Rosé (a sweet red wine) from France, Italy (rosato) and other European countries is considered a summer wine, and calamari (fried squid) is a traditional Mediterranean dish—Drake spends his summers dining on Europe’s finest.", "annotation_id": "9279387", "url": "https://genius.com/9279387", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Kinda like when you niggas drop on some again and again shit And you still never quite get it", "vault_lines": ["It's always on some shit like", "Kinda like when you niggas drop on some again and again shit", "And you still never quite get it"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Meek Mill’s continued attempts at re-igniting his feud with Drake, the most recent being “All The Way Up (Remix)”:\n\nI was on the block when you was a sneakerhead\nN— it’s a check up in that box, ain’t no shoe inside\nN— ain’t no Nike, ain’t no Yeezy, no Jordan V\n\nSince “Back to Back,” Meek has also dissed Drake in “War Pain,” and “Trap Vibes.”", "annotation_id": "9279353", "url": "https://genius.com/9279353", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "I roll big body, wide body, Calabasas road winder Sun shinin’, waxed tires", "vault_lines": ["I roll big body, wide body, Calabasas road winder", "Sun shinin', waxed tires"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to the Mercedes Maybach Pullman, which has quite the wide body, he rapped about the car on “30 for 30 Freestyle”:\n\nI just got me the Mercedes Pullman\nYou niggas never heard of it, you gotta hit up Google\n\n\n\nDrake purchased a mansion in Hidden Hills for $7.7 Million in Calabasas, Cali right next to Kanye and whips a big body Benz down Calabasas road.", "annotation_id": "9279315", "url": "https://genius.com/9279315", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Y’all shook up, I’m here on the cookup", "vault_lines": ["Y'all shook up, I'm here on the cookup"], "meaning": "The multiple shots at multiple targets in this song have Drake’s competition shook.\n\n“Cook up” is a term used to describe a strong work ethic, or to create something (be it music, drugs, food etc). In little over a month’s timespan, he has released his fourth studio LP, Views,, and featured on DJ Khaled’s “For Free”, Gucci Mane’s “Back on Road”, and YG’s “Why You Always Hatin?”.  He’s cookin' up on the regular.\n\nDrake also rapped a similar line on “Come Thru”:\n\nYeah they know I got the hook-up, they just wait on me to cook up\nBaby I heat up the stove, you do the dishes ya know?", "annotation_id": "9279300", "url": "https://genius.com/9279300", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "[Outro] Yeah, and we can’t stop Make you dance to this, uh I’ma make you One Dance to this", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I make you dance to this", "Yeah, and we can't stop", "Make you dance to this, uh", "I'ma make you One Dance to this"], "meaning": "“One Dance” is a historic moment in Drake’s career as it was the song that propelled him to have his first #1 hit as a solo artist in the US, UK and Canada. On his Instagram, he touched on this accomplishment while “Hotline Bling” was sitting at number 2 on the chart:\n\nNo accolades really matter to me other than the fact that I have never had a billboard number one. If I get my first number one during the month of October it will be the biggest moment of my career to date (in my mind).\n\nThis is also could be another reference to Puff Daddy as “can’t stop, won’t stop” and “I'mma make you dance to this” were both Puffy lyrics. He was also caught on camera dancing to “One Dance” at a performance.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ms3zOP9W7xE", "annotation_id": "9279279", "url": "https://genius.com/9279279", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Meantime, Drizzy over there, tryna make you, make you dance to this Yeah, I make you dance to this", "vault_lines": ["Meantime, Drizzy over there, tryna make you, make you dance to this", "Yeah, I make you dance to this", "Make you dance to this, uh", "I'ma make you One Dance to this"], "meaning": "Drake’s singles like “Hotline Bling” and “One Dance” have been massive cross-over pop hits, being played on dancefloors worldwide.\n\nDrizzy himself is not afraid to hit the dance floor  either as his moves in the “Hotline Bling” music video inspired countless memes on the internet.\n\n\n\nDuring this part of the song, Drake uses the flow that Rakim used in the song, “Eric B. is President”, when he rapped:\n\nAnd you know that I’m the soloist\nSo Eric B make ‘em clap to this\n\nThis might also be a reference to Puff Daddy’s song “Diddy” where he rapped:\n\nLet the party people dance to this", "annotation_id": "9279274", "url": "https://genius.com/9279274", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "I got a lot to lose ’cause in every situation I’m the bigger artist Always gotta play it smarter", "vault_lines": ["I got a lot to lose 'cause in every situation I'm the bigger artist", "Always gotta play it smarter"], "meaning": "Drake believes he’s the biggest artist of the moment and has the most to lose when engaging with another artist. Jay Z said something similar on “Streets Is Watching”:\n\nLook, if I shoot you, I’m brainless\nBut if you shoot me, then you’re famous\nWhat’s a nigga to do?\n\nWhen Meek Mill came out accusing Drake of using ghostwriters, Drake played it smart. He produced two subsequent diss tracks (“Charged Up” and “Back To Back”), with the second being after four days of silence from Meek, which he mentions. It was a smash hit and was nominated for a Grammy.", "annotation_id": "9279270", "url": "https://genius.com/9279270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "All she wanna do is get high and listen to PARTY She complain, I tell the driver to drop her at Barneys", "vault_lines": ["All she wanna do is get high and listen to PARTY", "She complain, I tell the driver to drop her at Barneys"], "meaning": "Drake’s girl wants to indulge in drugs while listening to OVO signee PARTYNEXTDOOR. If she starts complaining then he has the driver drop her off to shop at Barneys New York, to remind her she can’t find this kind of luxury with anyone else. As he said on “Child’s Play”:\n\nDon’t make me give you back to the hood\n\n\n\nhttps://twitter.com/PARTYOMO/status/739231578576945152", "annotation_id": "9279260", "url": "https://genius.com/9279260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Can’t nobody hold me down Especially not right now", "vault_lines": ["Can't nobody hold me down", "Especially not right now"], "meaning": "This is an interpolation of Puff Daddy’s iconic track “Can’t Nobody Hold Me Down (Bad Boy Remix).”\n\nOn December 9th, 2014, Drake and Puffy were involved in an altercation where Puff literally tried to “hold him down” with an alleged assault. The dispute was over the “0 to 100” beat which Puffy was apparently promised from Boi-1da. Sources reported that Drake was hospitalized after the incident, but despite the OGs pressing him nothing can stop him, and going platinum in a week confirms that.", "annotation_id": "9279258", "url": "https://genius.com/9279258", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Pistol by my bed, I’m sleep, but I’m awake For that one night when niggas try reach inside my safe", "vault_lines": ["Pistol by my bed, I'm sleep, but I'm awake", "For that one night when niggas try reach inside my safe"], "meaning": "Creeping paranoia has been a theme in his most recent releases, notably IYRTITL.\n\nIn “No Tellin'”:\n\nYeah, she invite me to the telly, keep the blade with me\nWhen I go to check a bitch, ain’t no tellin'\n\nAnd on “Wu-Tang Forever”:\n\nParanoid, always rolling with my mothafuckin' boys\nBut you gotta understand when it’s yours\nThey don’t really leave your ass with a mothafuckin' choice, man\n\nIn 2016 he was still paranoid on “Weston Road Flows”:\n\nI got a price on my head but there’s a risk to collect it\nI might be here as a vessel to teach people the lesson\n\nWith these factors in mind, it’s not unlikely that Drake would at least keep a weapon close for whoever he believes is out to get him both on wax and in real life.", "annotation_id": "9279244", "url": "https://genius.com/9279244", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "I thought we looked out for one another Saw us all as brothers in the struggle, too blessed to be humble I guess it’s different in the city I come from All the sudden I got people showing how much they truly resent me They whole demeanor just spells envy, they tryna tempt me", "vault_lines": ["All the sudden I got people showing how much they truly resent me", "They whole demeanor just spells envy, they tryna tempt me", "I thought we looked out for one another", "Saw us all as brothers in the struggle, too blessed to be humble", "I guess it's different in the city I come from"], "meaning": "Drake spoke at length during his interview with Zane Lowe, expressing his frustration and confusion to why upcoming artists, especially those from his city of Toronto, choose to bad mouth him in the media, hoping to get the spotlight onto themselves.\n\nNotable cases include Tory Lanez, who threw shade at Drake during his Breakfast Club Interview. The likes of Meek Mill and Mo-G have called out Drake for not writing his own raps and not crediting his collaborators fairly.\n\nThey whole demeanor just spells envy\n\nThis specific line is a subliminal diss towards Joe Budden, who stated on his podcast that Drake sounded “uninspired” on Views.\n\nDrake believes this hatred is fuelled by pure jealousy, and that the people targeting him should focus on growing their careers instead of derailing his. As he said on “Views”:\n\nLook, they’d rather run up on me than towards their goals", "annotation_id": "9279231", "url": "https://genius.com/9279231", "state": "accepted", "votes": 152, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Certain shit is just too wild to reconcile Take that, take that No love in they heart so they fake that DiCaprio level the way they play that Damn, nigga, what is that?", "vault_lines": ["Certain shit is just too wild to reconcile", "Take that, take that", "No love in they heart so they fake that", "DiCaprio level the way they play that", "Damn, nigga, what is that?"], "meaning": "In these lines, Drake takes direct shots at Puff Daddy by using his iconic catchphrase “Take that, take that.”\n\nAfter the “0 to 100” beat incident, Puff went on to say he loves Drake and he is his favorite current artist in multiple interviews. Drake may be perceiving that as fake love and compares his ability to act that fake to that of Oscar winning actor, Leonardo DiCaprio, who is often considered one of the greatest actors of his generation.\n\nIt’s also a possible subliminal reference to Joe Budden. “No love in they heart” links with Budden’s album All Love Lost.", "annotation_id": "9279228", "url": "https://genius.com/9279228", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "See Kris Jenner, I beep twice and I wave", "vault_lines": ["See Kris Jenner, I beep twice and I wave"], "meaning": "In his interview with Zane Lowe, Drake shared that he lives down the street from Kanye West. It’s likely that he would see Kris Jenner (Kanye’s mother-in-law) as she is also another Calabasas resident which is likely the reason he mentions that he would see her and wave.\n\nKanye lives at her house along with Kim Kardashian, North and Saint. Even though they recently moved out, they have been living there for a year and a half.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/_tHCRCnGqR/", "annotation_id": "9279221", "url": "https://genius.com/9279221", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Cameras pointing every time I look up That’s why I gotta duck behind Chubb’s shoulder just to hit the kush up Sponsorship dollars is sky high", "vault_lines": ["Cameras pointing every time I look up", "That's why I gotta duck behind Chubb's shoulder just to hit the kush up", "Sponsorship dollars is sky high"], "meaning": "Drake’s most notable sponsorship deals are Apple Music and Nike’s famed brand Jordan.  The latter is reportedly worth $10 million to the rapper.\n\n\n\nSince his status as a celebrity comes with little to no privacy, Drake needs to hide behind his bodyguard and one of his closest friends, Chubb, when he smokes weed because he doesn’t want to lose any of his sponsorship deals due to a bad image.", "annotation_id": "9279210", "url": "https://genius.com/9279210", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Look, now you got me started I’m the black sheep, rest in peace to Chris Farley", "vault_lines": ["Look, now you got me started", "I'm the black sheep, rest in peace to Chris Farley"], "meaning": "A “black sheep” is a common expression for someone who doesn’t fit in with their peers, often times they are even seen as beneath them. Perhaps this is why so many rappers want beef with Drake.\n\nBlack Sheep is also the name of the film starring actor Chris Farley. Sadly, Chris passed away from a drug overdose at the age 33 in 1997.\n\n\n\nDrake had a similar line on “Thank Me Now”:\n\nI’m the black sheep but Chris Farley wears the crown", "annotation_id": "9279204", "url": "https://genius.com/9279204", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "“Mike never tried to rap like Pac, Pac never tried to sing like Mike” Those my dad’s words to me when I asked him how to make it in life And I always said my mother gave the greatest advice, yeah", "vault_lines": ["\"Mike never tried to rap like Pac, Pac never tried to sing like Mike\"", "Those my dad's words to me when I asked him how to make it in life", "And I always said my mother gave the greatest advice, yeah"], "meaning": "2Pac and Michael Jackson are both iconic artists, but both operated in different lanes. Although Jackson dabbled in hip-hop, Drake makes the point that his father Dennis Graham told him to combine the two elements and present something the world had never seen before. In an interview, Drake said that his father gave him the idea to rap and sing, and that he initially thought it was “the worst idea.”\n\nIn an effort to “shock the world,” Dennis Graham told his son that he needed to both rap and sing.\n\nHis mother told him on “You & The 6” “Don’t ever take advice,” although it appears he took his father’s, as Drake is arguably a star in both genres, and he told Zane Lowe that Views was his “greatest vocal performance.” He even mentions Michael on “Hype”:\n\nThat boy light as Michael Jackson\nBut off verses, he been blackin'", "annotation_id": "9279199", "url": "https://genius.com/9279199", "state": "accepted", "votes": 179, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Yeah All you self-promoters are janky", "vault_lines": ["All you self-promoters are janky"], "meaning": "“Janky” means unreliable or undesirable. Hip-hop has a tradition of “braggadocio” rap, and Drake is no stranger to this. On tracks like “Legend” and “0 to 100” he claims:\n\nIf I ain’t the greatest then I’m headed for it\nThat mean I’m way up\n\nDrake appears to shun other rappers who take a similar route. If he considers himself the best, then everyone else must be lying.\n\nThis is also a reference to the 2009 Ice Cube movie, Janky Promoters.", "annotation_id": "9279152", "url": "https://genius.com/9279152", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "We established like the Yankees This whole fuckin’ game thankless", "vault_lines": ["We established like the Yankees", "This whole fuckin' game thankless"], "meaning": "Drake believes his label is as prominent as the New York Yankees, an American baseball team that were founded over a century ago (in 1901). The franchise holds two of the most important MLB team records with 18 division titles and 27 World Series championships.\n\nDrake’s OVO Sound record label was founded in 2012 and  has since cultivated its own unique sound that has heavily influenced modern day hip-hop and R&B. This is powered by their talented roster of artists, such as PartyNextDoor, Roy Woods, and Majid Jordan.\n\nAdditionally, thanks to Drake, Toronto’s OVO, and more broadly the Canadian rap scene, is now as established in mainstream hip-hop as any American (“Yankee”) rap label.", "annotation_id": "9279093", "url": "https://genius.com/9279093", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Look at me now, they look at me like the golden child", "vault_lines": ["Look at me now, they look at me like the golden child"], "meaning": "In April of 2016, Joe Budden released a podcast with his thoughts on Drake’s Views. After calling himself one of Drizzy’s biggest fans he went on to criticize the album by calling Drake the “golden child” of rap.\n\nI miss the Drake that starts the waves, not hops on other waves. And I’ll say it again: I miss the Drake that pushes the fuckin’ agenda, not let the agenda push him.\n\nBudden responded to this line via his Twitter, and references Drake’s earlier lines about Diddy.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/JoeBudden/status/739237492113674246?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\nGolden “child” also plays off the previous lines, where he was speaking about his parents and their respective advice. “Look At Me Now” may also be a Chris Brown reference.", "annotation_id": "9279086", "url": "https://genius.com/9279086", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "I’m not attending when I do a show and get a ticket Good business can clean millions, I got the vision", "vault_lines": ["I'm not attending when I do a show and get a ticket", "Good business can clean millions, I got the vision"], "meaning": "Drake would rather do business and tour, then go to award shows and fraternize with folks he doesn’t really like. He played golf while the 2016 Billboard Awards were on and chose not to perform at the 2016 Grammys.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/696496980990668801?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\nA ticket refers to a million dollars: Drake makes millions of dollars from his shows. His 2013-2015 Would You Like a Tour? raked in over $46 million dollars.", "annotation_id": "9278969", "url": "https://genius.com/9278969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "We movin’ militant but somehow you the one tankin’ No limit to where I can take it", "vault_lines": ["We movin' militant but somehow you the one tankin'", "No limit to where I can take it"], "meaning": "While Drake and his crew make decisions with military precision, his opposition are “tanking,” another term for losing.\n\nDrake touches on the movement of his crew and the aggressive nature of the rap game in his remix of Wiz Kid’s track “Ojuelegba”:\n\nLook, it’s gon' be a long long time ‘fore we stop\nBoy better know, they better know who make the scene pop\nAll I ever needed was a chance to get the team hot\nOnly thing I fear is a headshot or a screenshot\n\nThis is also a nod to the No Limit record label’s logo, which pictures an army tank.", "annotation_id": "9278968", "url": "https://genius.com/9278968", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "I been had it since “No Scrubs” and “No Pigeons” Even back when I wasn’t as poppin’", "vault_lines": ["I been had it since \"No Scrubs\" and \"No Pigeons\"", "Even back when I wasn't as poppin'"], "meaning": "“No Pigeons” is Sporty Thievz’s second and final single, released on their 1999 album Street Cinema. It was a rebuttal of TLC’s hit single, “No Scrubs.”\n\nThis may be a reference to his SNL skit “Bar Mitzvah In 1999.” Drake’s acting career didn’t take off until 2001, and in 1999 he was just 13 years old.\n\nIn “All Me,\" Drake expressed a similar sentiment:\n\nI touched down in ‘86, knew I was the man by the age of 6\nI even fucked the girl that used to babysit\nBut that was years later on some crazy shit", "annotation_id": "9278938", "url": "https://genius.com/9278938", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "line": "Even had the OG’s tryna press me, ha-ha-ha, ha No way out ’cause I’m already in it", "vault_lines": ["Even had the OG's tryna press me, ha-ha-ha, ha", "No way out 'cause I'm already in it"], "meaning": "This appears to be a direct shot at Puff Daddy, with whom Drake had a dispute in 2014, allegedly over the beat to “0 To 100 / The Catch Up.” Diddy is certainly an OG in the game, and the way Drake says “ha ha ha ha” appears to mock the way Diddy said it during “Can’t Nobody Hold Me Down,” which sampled Grandmaster Flash’s “The Message”:\n\nDon’t push us, cause we’re close to the, edge\nWe’re tryin, not to lose our heads, a-hah hah hah hah\n\nWhile it was reported in 2015 that the two had squashed their beef, this seems to confirm Drake still has an issue.\n\nHe may also be referring to Jadakiss, who said on Uncommon Sense that rappers who use ghostwriters (specifically Drake) can’t be in the “top 5” conversation. Slaughterhouse made a similar statement on Ebro:\n\nIt don’t stop the music from being great, it don’t stop you from being successful, I don’t lose respect for you personally, but just in my comic book hero, rapper baseball card collection, I can’t put you [in that conversation].\n\nNo Way Out is also the name of Diddy’s debut album.", "annotation_id": "9278925", "url": "https://genius.com/9278925", "state": "accepted", "votes": 161, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4PM in Calabasas", "status": "done", "targets": 39, "clicked": 39, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-4pm-in-calabasas-lyrics", "genius_title": "4PM in Calabasas"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "line": "Hi, haters, I’m back off of hiatus", "vault_lines": ["And \"Hi, haters, I'm back off of hiatus"], "meaning": "This line interpolates the first verse off Grammy Family by Consequence, Kanye West, John Legend and DJ Khaled.", "annotation_id": "40456707", "url": "https://genius.com/40456707", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "line": "When you hollerin’ at labels and they silencing you back ’Cause you fail to thoroughly discuss some violence in ya track Well, gunshot for the young yacht owner", "vault_lines": ["When you hollerin' at labels and they silencing you back", "'Cause you fail to thoroughly discuss some violence in ya track", "Well, gunshot for the young yacht owner"], "meaning": "This is Drake pointing at industry gatekeeping, specifically how “marketability” is already filtering his voice before it even reaches an audience.\n\nDrake is framing labels as reactive censors here: they don’t just judge skill, they respond to perceived risk in content. “Silencing you back” suggests rejection is happening after engagement, meaning the material is being actively evaluated and then shut down for not aligning with commercial or editorial comfort around violence in rap narratives.\n\nIn the context of The Comeback Season, this fits early Drake’s positioning as someone trying to enter rap spaces where authenticity is often measured through explicit street content. He’s highlighting a mismatch between his storytelling approach and what gatekeepers expect from “valid” rap output.\n\n“Gunshot for the young yacht owner” reads like a contrast mechanism: wealth aspiration (“yacht owner”) colliding with violent expectation (“gunshot”)—basically compressing two incompatible identities the industry tries to force into a single track identity. It’s not just imagery, it’s commentary on how narrow the accepted spectrum is.\n\nOverall, the bar is less about violence itself and more about how external validation systems decide which versions of an artist are allowed to exist publicly.", "annotation_id": "39646174", "url": "https://genius.com/39646174", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "line": "That it’s hard to find a woman when you talented and black", "vault_lines": ["That it's hard to find a woman when you talented and black"], "meaning": "Truth", "annotation_id": "35714689", "url": "https://genius.com/35714689", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "line": "Shola Ama, I told her I’ma", "vault_lines": ["Shola Ama, I told her I'ma"], "meaning": "Shola Ama is a singer from London, England who has been associated with Craig David (from the line before) throughout her career.\n\nThis may be one of Drake’s first instances of lusting over beautiful women through his lyrics.", "annotation_id": "20438080", "url": "https://genius.com/20438080", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "line": "On my Craig David shit the Artful Dodger", "vault_lines": ["Racin' through back streets on my Craig David shit the Artful Dodger"], "meaning": "Craig David is a singer from England who rose to fame after being featured on the song Rewind. by Artful Dodger.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NP93bl1VFBM", "annotation_id": "20438067", "url": "https://genius.com/20438067", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "line": "Mr., Big Dreams,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "At the time of his Comeback Season mixtape, Drake was an independent artist, without having a recording contract with a major label company. However, that didn’t stop him from having big dreams about his career in music. Indeed, his dreams would later become true with his 2009 mixtape So Far Gone, which earned him a recording contract with Lil Wayne’s Young Money Entertainment label, and allowed him to achieve international recognition.", "annotation_id": "7637620", "url": "https://genius.com/7637620", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "line": "From meetin’ Trey in Atlanta to doin’ a cameo in his video Which made everything right with X (Songz)", "vault_lines": ["At which life progressed from meetin' Trey in Atlanta to doin' a cameo in his video", "Which made everything right with X (Songz)"], "meaning": "Drake made a cameo in Trey’s video for the hit record “Wonder Woman”. (Around 1:15)\n\nThis carries over to the other line where he mentions “which made everything right with X”. Little X directed the Wonder Woman video.\n\nDrake also mentions Little X in The Presentation where he raps:\n\nI’m keepin' it cleaner than a Little X direction\n\n\nThis is in reference to Little X’s style of shooting. It is also not a coincidence that Little X is Canadian", "annotation_id": "439566", "url": "https://genius.com/439566", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "line": "Surrounded by Filipinos, I think of the worst case", "vault_lines": ["Surrounded by Filipinos, I think of the worst case"], "meaning": "He is referring to “Worst Case Scenario Survival Handbook”, which is a travel handbook for the Philippines\n\nSome members of his crew are of Filipino descent", "annotation_id": "141099", "url": "https://genius.com/141099", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "line": "I’ve been Urkel for some years, it’s better bein’ Jaleel (Heh-heh)", "vault_lines": ["I've been Urkel for some years, it's better bein' Jaleel (Heh-heh)"], "meaning": "Jaleel White is the actor who played Steve Urkel on the hit 90’s sitcom Family Matters\n\nOn TV, Urkel was the epitome of a nerd while Jaleel in real life was as smooth as they come..\n\n\n\nAlso can be a metaphor about him playing the character Jimmy on Degrassi and how he enjoys being himself.", "annotation_id": "70132", "url": "https://genius.com/70132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Closer to My Dreams", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 9, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-closer-to-my-dreams-lyrics", "genius_title": "Closer to My Dreams"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "But if y’all goin’ to war I’m there for sure Wouldn’t miss it", "vault_lines": ["But if y'all goin' to war I'm there for sure"], "meaning": "From the inception of his career, Drake has never shied away for going to war with other rappers, and has been caught up in a plethora of feuds. Ironically, early in Drizzy’s career on 2009 track “Successful”, he claimed to never respond to disses:\n\nDiss me, you’ll never hear a reply for it\n\nSome of Drake’s most notable battles were with Meek Mill in 2015, and years of subliminal disses escalating in 2018 with Pusha T and 2024 with Kendrick Lamar.\n\n\nDrake, Pusha T, and Kendrick Lamar pictured separately.", "annotation_id": "31818725", "url": "https://genius.com/31818725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "My niggas really made somethin’ from nothin’ Dodge sentences like they don’t wanna discuss it, change subject", "vault_lines": ["My niggas really made somethin' from nothin'", "Dodge sentences like they don't wanna discuss it, change subject"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the homonym “sentences” and uses a simile to depict how his brother’s dodge prison sentences like how a person who “don’t want to discuss it” would dodge speaking actual grammatical sentences and instead opt for silence.\n\nDrake’s entourage has been known to get in trouble with the law, for example when Drake’s good friend and bodyguard “Chubbs” violently assaulted record producer Detail. However, as Drake claimed, Chubbs managed to dodge a prison sentence for this.", "annotation_id": "27499290", "url": "https://genius.com/27499290", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Tom Ford assistant texted asking if I want classic or double breasted", "vault_lines": ["Tom Ford assistant texted asking if I want classic or double breasted"], "meaning": "Tom Ford is a leading American fashion designer who, in addition to his own namesake label, previously served as creative director for Gucci and YSL.\n\nDrake has a close affinity to the brand, wearing various different styles and even flexing a custom Tom Ford suit in the video for I’m Upset.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/tomford/status/1007695774401130497?lang=en\n\nHe references this penchant for the label, describing how the shop assistant is getting in touch to confirm what style he would prefer for his next piece.\n\nIn the context of the preceding bar about connections, Drizzy may in fact be referring to Tom’s personal assistant rather than a typical retail worker.", "annotation_id": "19194913", "url": "https://genius.com/19194913", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "I flex with broad shoulders When I walk through the halls they move over", "vault_lines": ["I flex with broad shoulders", "When I walk through the halls they move over"], "meaning": "In recent years, Drake has gone through quite the transformation putting on considerable muscle and crafting a strong physique.\n\nHe illustrates his physical size with a hyperbole describing how due to the considerable size of his shoulders, others are literally forced to move aside in order to get past him in the hallway.", "annotation_id": "19194788", "url": "https://genius.com/19194788", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Or hold the wheel of the Fuji white Rover", "vault_lines": ["Or hold the wheel of the Fuji white Rover"], "meaning": "Drizzy references Land Rover’s luxury SUV, the Range Rover. Popular amongst celebrities, he owns one with a ‘Fuji White’ paint job, which can be seen briefly in the music video for Chicago Freestyle.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/0jz0GAFNNIo?t=155\n\n\n\nWith the lightest version of the vehicle weighing in at well over 4000lbs, it would require a tight grip on the steering wheel to control.\n\nDrake plays on his opps insecurities comparing this grip to how hard they must work to “hold onto” or maintain the interest of the opposite sex.", "annotation_id": "19194588", "url": "https://genius.com/19194588", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "I took a lot of my brothers from B and E licks or the pot rotation To ridin’ shotgun in a Picasso painting", "vault_lines": ["I took a lot of my brothers from B and E licks or the pot rotation", "To ridin' shotgun in a Picasso painting"], "meaning": "Drake shares his success with his entourage who used to spend their time selling crack and breaking and entering houses.\n\nNot only did Drake steer them away from the life of crime, but he’s been able to give them jobs managing his career. The late Pablo Picasso is a famous painter whose artwork has been critically acclaimed by many. Drake compares his career to a Picasso painting, which at the end, will not only have him in the picture but also his crew.", "annotation_id": "16985812", "url": "https://genius.com/16985812", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "I speak truth and get to shatterin’ dreams So it’s best to tiptoe, niggas better know, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Tiptoe, niggas better know", "I speak truth and get to shatterin' dreams", "So it's best to tiptoe, niggas better know, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake has been known to “keep it a hunnid”. Even when other rappers come at him, he comes back with facts to stay true to Hip-Hop. As he stated in verse one, his haters need to “think twice” when it comes to the stories they tell about him because the omniscient 6God probably has the drop on them. Drake sends another warning to tread with caution, or “tip-toe”, because we know he’ll keep coming with the fire!", "annotation_id": "15156640", "url": "https://genius.com/15156640", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Make ’em think twice ’bout the stories that they tell No one knows when the plan is hatchin’ I’ma let ’em walk on eggshells Tiptoe, niggas better know", "vault_lines": ["Make 'em think twice 'bout the stories that they tell", "No one knows when the plan is hatchin'", "I'ma let 'em walk on eggshells", "Tiptoe, niggas better know"], "meaning": "Drake sees himself as one of the only rappers who is true to the game. He sends a warning shot to anyone that comes at him. If you go at Drake you better make sure you know what you’re doing because Drake isn’t afraid to set it straight.\n\nDrake is also three moves ahead of everyone in the game so he no one knows what he has up his sleeve next. “Walking on eggshells” is an idiom for “be careful”. Drake’s haters have to tread with caution, or “tip-toe”, because Drake is liable to pop off out of nowhere and we all should know this by now.", "annotation_id": "15156567", "url": "https://genius.com/15156567", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "El chico", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Following the previous line touching on Drizzy’s squad October Firm as the new chain of command, he asserts himself as “El Chico”, which is Spanish for “The boy”.\n\nDrake has long referenced himself as ‘the boy’ or ‘the kid’, a common thing in rap to assert dominance as the main guy doing it. The Spanish twist aims at likening Drizzy’s position in the rap game to South American dictators that would naturally use similar notions, such as “El Jefe”.", "annotation_id": "15096282", "url": "https://genius.com/15096282", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "That’s these guys Us guys don’t wear New Balance or Levi’s", "vault_lines": ["Us guys don't wear New Balance or Levi's"], "meaning": "Drake ridicules his opponents while asserting his wealth simultaneously to exhibit how he refuses to wear apparel that is considered “cheap” or even “moderately priced.” The first mentioned brand is New Balance, a footwear company that is known for manufacturing running sneakers that can typically sell anywhere from $60 to $180. Levi’s produces denim-based products at a fairly moderate price: their jeans go for between $50 to $80.\n\nDespite this, Drake has been seen wearing some of Levi’s products, especially their jean jackets from time to time.", "annotation_id": "15014065", "url": "https://genius.com/15014065", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Goldman Sachs, they wanna hold my racks", "vault_lines": ["Goldman Sachs, they wanna hold my racks"], "meaning": "Goldman Sachs is one of the premier private wealth management firms with the typical client having $50M. It’s rumored that the firm manages JAY-Z’s fortune.\n\nDavid Solomon, the new CEO of Goldman Sachs, is a party monster DJ who likes to kick it with Hip-Hop royalty.", "annotation_id": "14990791", "url": "https://genius.com/14990791", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Grippin’ the Callaway club in my hand Hittin’ balls off of the balcony into the sand Tryna see where they land", "vault_lines": ["Grippin' the Callaway club in my hand", "Hittin' balls off of the balcony into the sand", "Tryna see where they land"], "meaning": "Callaway is a golf brand started by Ely Callaway when he bought shares to a then-titled golf brand, Hickory Stick in 1984, later changing the name to “Callaway Golf Company” in 1986. The brand is perhaps best known for its Big Bertha line of clubs introduced in 1991. However, their association with Annika Sorenstam—arguably the greatest women’s golfer of all time—and Phil Mickelson, who was recently ranked 10th best golfer of all time, have also helped establish the brand as a leader in golf equipment.\n\n\n\nDrake brags about his golf prowess, saying he’s hitting it so far he has to work to see where it lands. This may also be a nod to Drake’s wealth, as having a balcony on the beach, especially in Key West following the previous line can cost a fair amount.", "annotation_id": "14982381", "url": "https://genius.com/14982381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Shout out Offset, blessings on the new ting.", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This freestyle comes only three days after Offset along with wife, Cardi B welcomed their first child together, Kulture Kiari Cephus.\n\nCardi first confirmed her pregnancy and marriage with Offset during a live performance of “Be Careful” on Saturday Night Live. A day after the birth, she posted a photo via her Instagram announcing that she had given birth.\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BlGE833BJzK/\n\nKulture Kiari Cephus 07/10/18🎀🌸 @offsetyrn\n\nAlthough this is their first baby together, Offset actually has three children with other unidentified women; Kody, Jordan, and Kalea Marie.", "annotation_id": "14981636", "url": "https://genius.com/14981636", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Shout out Boi-1da too", "vault_lines": ["Shout out Boi-1da too"], "meaning": "A quick shoutout to Toronto-bred producer Boi-1da whom is Drakes good friend and OVO label mate. He’s also the proucer for this freestyle. Drake and Boi-1da have been making hits for almost a decade, including hits like “Best I Ever Had,” “Forever,” “Headlines,” and “0 to 100 / The Catch Up.”", "annotation_id": "14980970", "url": "https://genius.com/14980970", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Y’all keep the awards, I’ll take the rewards", "vault_lines": ["Y'all keep the awards, I'll take the rewards"], "meaning": "Drake could be referring to the G.O.O.D. Music head Kanye West who has been beefing with Drake over the past couple of months. Kanye West has won much more awards than Drake: he holds an astonishing 21 Grammy Awards in comparison to Drake’s 3.  However, Drake has garnered many streaming sales and other forms of revenue such as selling a total of 2,277,000 first week album sales with his last three albums. On top of that, his 2018 album, Scorpion broke the one-week streaming record with over 1 billion streams.\n\nThis may also be a shot at Kendrick Lamar who has been subliminally engaged in a war with Drake for the past 5 years and has won 12 Grammy Awards. On “The Heart Part 4”, the Compton MC rapped:\n\nI’ll let y'all worry about a list, I’m on some other shit\nA difference between accomplishments and astonishments", "annotation_id": "14976036", "url": "https://genius.com/14976036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "If y’all goin’ for fun, I’ll stay and record", "vault_lines": ["If y'all goin' for fun, I'll stay and record"], "meaning": "Drake has rapped about foregoing fun to record before, notably on Nothing Was The Same’s “Furthest Thing”:\n\nY'all niggas party too much, man, I just chill and record\n\nand Thank Me Later’s “Light Up”:\n\n‘Cause while all of my closest friends out partyin’\nI’m just here makin' all the music that they party to", "annotation_id": "14975644", "url": "https://genius.com/14975644", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Key West retirement plan", "vault_lines": ["Key West retirement plan"], "meaning": "Key West is an island and city in the Straits of Florida.\n\nDrake cleverly delivers this line to sound similar to “Keep West’s retirement plan”. During Drake’s beef with Kanye West and Pusha T, Drake allegedly made another diss song that was ready as a reply to  The Story of Adidon. This reply would have had lyrics that would severely harm Kanye West’s career. When Kanye was made aware of some of the contents of the diss song, he contacted Drake’s friend, mentor and CEO of Rap-A-Lot records, James Prince, to plead with Drake not to release the diss song and end the beef. But in this line Drake is alluding to him keeping the diss song on the shelf in case the beef would escalate or be reignited in the future. He is insinuating that the information he would expose about Kanye West in the diss song would be so compromising that Kanye West would have to retire after its release because of what it would do to his public image.\n\nDrake mentioned something similar in his Behind Barz freestyle:\n\nI got so much tings in the stash\nFold up anyone, dun that clash", "annotation_id": "14975257", "url": "https://genius.com/14975257", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "I’m living next to Michael, Bobby, Ralph, Johnny My neighbors soundin’ like New Edition", "vault_lines": ["I'm living next to Michael, Bobby, Ralph, Johnny", "My neighbors soundin' like New Edition"], "meaning": "Bobby Brown, Michael Bivins, Ralph Tresvant and Johnny Gill were four of the members of the R&B group New Edition. Bobby and Johnny were not in the group concurrently during their intial run, as Bobby was voted out of the group in 1985 and replaced by Johnny in 1987. However, all six member reunited in 1996 for Home Again.\n\n\n\nDrake uses the members of New Edition as a play on the names of his white, rich neighbors.", "annotation_id": "14975107", "url": "https://genius.com/14975107", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "100 mill’ hands free like Ronaldinho", "vault_lines": ["100 mill' hands free like Ronaldinho"], "meaning": "Ronaldinho is a famous football player who played for the likes of Paris Saint-Germain, FC Barcelona and AC Milan. Throughout his illustrious career, he managed to earn upwards to $100m, a feat that Drake has also accomplished.\n\nOutfield football players cannot use their hands to touch the ball during play unless a player is taking a throw-in. Drake claims his financial ascent was also “hands free.”", "annotation_id": "14975040", "url": "https://genius.com/14975040", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fire in the Booth", "line": "Hedge fund investors talk to Adel like he the expert", "vault_lines": ["Hedge fund investors talk to Adel like he the expert"], "meaning": "Adel Nur, better known as Future The Prince, is Drake’s tour DJ and manager.", "annotation_id": "14975001", "url": "https://genius.com/14975001", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
