{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I fell in love with the pen, started fuckin’ the ink (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["I fell in love with the pen, started fuckin' the ink (Huh)"], "meaning": "When he says he’s in “love with the pen” he could be talking about how he (Rick Ross) loves PENis", "annotation_id": "25310566", "url": "https://genius.com/25310566", "state": "pending", "votes": 205, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I know that showin’ emotion don’t ever mean I’m a pussy", "vault_lines": ["I know that showin' emotion don't ever mean I'm a pussy"], "meaning": "Being one of the early pioneers in melodic/sentimental rap and known for liking strippers for their personality, Drake has been the consistent subject of debate when discussing ‘soft rappers’.\n\nIn Drake’s defence against his emotional persona, he quickly follows up with a bar for his critics, and proceeds to rap one of the most memorable lines within the track…", "annotation_id": "25015343", "url": "https://genius.com/25015343", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Went and got a yacht, I’m talkin’ Carnival Cruise", "vault_lines": ["Went and got a yacht, I'm talkin' Carnival Cruise"], "meaning": "Ross is saying that the yacht that he purchased is as big as a cruise ship. He is of course using hyperbole, but the point is to convey that his boat is extremely big because he has a lot of money.", "annotation_id": "24425771", "url": "https://genius.com/24425771", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Know that I don’t make music for niggas who don’t get pussy So those are the ones I count on to diss me or overlook me", "vault_lines": ["Know that I don't make music for niggas who don't get pussy", "So those are the ones I count on to diss me or overlook me"], "meaning": "Around this time Drake was heavily criticized from certain communities for talking about “soft” subject matter such as women in a vulnerable way (as exemplified with the “Drake the type of nigga to…” memes).\n\nDrake reverses this criticism onto his critics claiming that the people who diss him are people who can’t get any women, and thus can not relate to his songs about relationships.", "annotation_id": "22778496", "url": "https://genius.com/22778496", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "In this bitch, all drinks on the house like Snoopy", "vault_lines": ["In this bitch, all drinks on the house like Snoopy"], "meaning": "Wordplay from Drake comparing how all of his drinks are complimentary wherever he goes (“on the house”) to how Snoopy from the Charlie Brown cartoons famously sleeps on top of his dog house rather than sleeping inside of it.", "annotation_id": "22778485", "url": "https://genius.com/22778485", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "The hustle’s an art, I painted what I would think", "vault_lines": ["The hustle's an art, I painted what I would think"], "meaning": "Regarding the previous line, Ross’ “fell in love with the pen”, line can also mention how his love for the hustle of the game and dedication placed him where is today in his career. He sees his past achievements in both his personal and professional life as a canvas and describes his life experiences behind the mic using lyrics as his muse.", "annotation_id": "22189201", "url": "https://genius.com/22189201", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Mink coats make your woman wanna fornicate Rozay and Drake, I’m getting cake, nothin’ short of great (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Mink coats make your woman wanna fornicate", "Rozay and Drake, I'm getting cake, nothin' short of great (Huh)"], "meaning": "“Cake” is slang for money and fornication is the act of two unmarried people who have sexual intercourse. On Drake’s September 2011 track, “Free Spirit,” Ross also mentions fornication:\n\nI fondle the money, fornicate with a fortune", "annotation_id": "18559731", "url": "https://genius.com/18559731", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Huh You know I love this YOLO, you only live once", "vault_lines": ["You know I love this", "YOLO, you only live once"], "meaning": "Drake used the term YOLO more prominently on the Take Care hit single, “The Motto”:\n\nYou only live once: that’s the motto, nigga, YOLO\n\nDrake and Rick Ross also had plans to release a joint mixtape titled YOLO but they were eventually scrapped. Drake told MTV News in June 2012 that he was considering changing the mixtape’s title to something else, confirming there was something in the works:\n\n[YOLO] has become so … it’s like an epidemic. So I hope by the time we finish it, it would still hold weight and substance […] We might have to call it something different now ‘cause that’s just so attached to 'The Motto.’", "annotation_id": "18559653", "url": "https://genius.com/18559653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "(M-M-Maybach Music)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Maybach Music Group is a record label imprint founded by rapper Rick Ross. Some of the most notable rappers from Maybach Music include Rick Ross himself, Meek Mill, and French Montana.", "annotation_id": "14365057", "url": "https://genius.com/14365057", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Young-ass nigga, lifelong dreams", "vault_lines": ["Young-ass nigga, lifelong dreams"], "meaning": "Drake may be referring to his days as Jimmy Brooks on Degrassi: The Next Generation.  During his time as Jimmy Brooks, he wrote 12 pages worth of lyrics in a purple spiral notebook titled “10 Mack Commandments”.\n\n\n\nA page directly from Drake’s Degrassi notebook, showing a list of songs and signatures of his real name.\n\nAt the commencement of Drake’s “Degrassi”-era, his mother was still the sole income of his household. His “lifelong dreams” as a 15-year-old may have been to become wealthy enough to support himself and his mother. Drake also discusses this topic on “10 Bands” of 2015’s “If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late”:\n\nI was payin' mama’s rent when was I turning 17", "annotation_id": "14364645", "url": "https://genius.com/14364645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "[Intro: Choir & Rick Ross]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "While it may sound like a classic sample-based Just Blaze production, no samples were technically used. In an interview with Stereogum, Drake had this to say about the production:\n\n…Just Blaze did the beat. It was so amazing because it was a gospel sample and the gospel group didn’t want to clear the sample, so we had a choir come in and re-sing the sample. The new beat versus the old beat, the new beat is so much better…\n\nHere is a very rare video of the choir session that was arranged for the production of this track:\n\nhttp://www.dailymotion.com/video/x17ujbj_lord-knows-session_music", "annotation_id": "2498527", "url": "https://genius.com/2498527", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Lookin’ for the right way to do the wrong things (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Lookin' for the right way to do the wrong things (Huh)"], "meaning": "Doing ‘the wrong things’ would be things like partying with strippers, doing drugs, drinking heavily, smoking weed, etc. (Things society would be against).\n\n\n\nDrake’s therefore trying to figure out the ‘right way’ to approach these habits, so as not to fall victim to them.", "annotation_id": "516221", "url": "https://genius.com/516221", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "It’s your worst nightmare, it’s my first night here", "vault_lines": ["It's your worst nightmare, it's my first night here"], "meaning": "Drake kicks off this hard hitting track by stating that he is the worst nightmare for his fellow rappers. This is due to the fact that he is young, fresh, original and aiming to reach the top of the rap game.\n\nStating that it’s his ‘first night here’ implies that Drizzy hasn’t been around long (this is his sophomore album after all) but is already a nightmare to the opposition – ‘here’ being the rap scene.", "annotation_id": "516209", "url": "https://genius.com/516209", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Lord knows, Lord knows, I’m heavy, I got my weight up", "vault_lines": ["Lord knows, Lord knows, I'm heavy, I got my weight up"], "meaning": "Could refer to a couple of things:\n\n\nDrake has got his money up.\nGaining weight in boxing is to fight in a different weight class, so he’s moving up in the sense that he’s competing with a different class of rappers as he is consistently ranked high on MTV’s Hottest MC’s Lists.\nPerhaps Drake is alluding to increased the meaning in his lyrics since we last saw him on Thank Me Later.\nDrake has literally gotten bigger by working out.", "annotation_id": "490847", "url": "https://genius.com/490847", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Haffa and Jordan my only role models", "vault_lines": ["But Weezy and Stunna my only role models", "Haffa and Jordan my only role models"], "meaning": "Two of his closest friends. Jordan is a member of OVO and is usually seen with the crew.\n\nThere was speculation on this line for years, but it’s been solved since his IG post .", "annotation_id": "478254", "url": "https://genius.com/478254", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Talking strippers and models that try to gain attention Even a couple pornstars that I’m ashamed to mention", "vault_lines": ["Even a couple pornstars that I'm ashamed to mention", "Talking strippers and models that try to gain attention"], "meaning": "A few strippers and models have tried to have sex with Drake, just for the mediatic boost of being in a relationship with a celebrity like him.\n\nMost famously, artist and former pornstar Sophie Brussaux, who claims to have a child by Drake, is at the center of his beef with Pusha-T, who has attacked Drake for what Push sees as his failure to adequately step up as a father.\n\n2021 Update: Sophie Brussaux it turns out WAS pregnant with Drake’s son. She in fact did have a brief stint as a “soft-core” porn actress with some solo videos.\n\n\n\nDrake and Sophie went on a now infamous date in Amsterdam in 2017.\n\n\n\nIn the past, Drake has also received negative attention for allegedly thirstily messaging pornstar Mia Khalifa on Instagram.", "annotation_id": "470405", "url": "https://genius.com/470405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "And this girl right here, who knows what she knows?", "vault_lines": ["And this girl right here, who knows what she knows?"], "meaning": "Drakes met a girl who comes off as sketchy… Not knowing whether this girl is a gold digger or not, as he’s dealt with too many of them since becoming famous. (As he mentions later)\n\nDrake doesn’t trust these types of women, from previous experience, as detailed on his song, “The Resistance”, with:\n\nMaybe cause a girl I thought I trusted was who set the whole shit up\n\nReferring to being robbed and a trusted girl setting it up.", "annotation_id": "451240", "url": "https://genius.com/451240", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Destined for greatness, but got a place in Jamaica (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["Destined for greatness, but got a place in Jamaica (Woo)"], "meaning": "It’s a play on how he’s “destined” for greatness but already owns a home in Jamaica; as well as the idea that that is his “destination” (as in actual physical destination).\n\nRoss' intention with this line appears to be to say that even though he’s rich (and going to get richer), he still owns a house in a country with a significant population of people in poverty (Jamaica).\n\nLike another line in this same verse; this is an example of sophisticated ignorance.\n\nAny future changes to this annotation should take a leaf out of TV Tropes' book and use the Rule Of Cautious Editing Judgment.", "annotation_id": "450132", "url": "https://genius.com/450132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Roberson, boost the rate up, it’s time that somebody paid up", "vault_lines": ["Roberson, boost the rate up, it's time that somebody paid up"], "meaning": "Gee Roberson is one of Drake’s managers from the famous management and production company, Hip Hop Since 1978.", "annotation_id": "449778", "url": "https://genius.com/449778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Mixtape legend, underground kings", "vault_lines": ["Mixtape legend, underground kings"], "meaning": "Earlier in the album, Drake dealt with the underground king theme at length.  As for “mixtape legend”, he mentioned that too on the same song\n\nUnderground kings is a reference in part to UGK, an influential and popular Houston rap duo featuring Bun B (who Drake collaborated with on Uptown and Put It Down) and the late Pimp C. Drizzy has spoken on their influence on him time and time again (in part possibly why Drake is on Put It Down).\n\n\n\nAs for ‘mixtape legend’ – “So Far Gone” is what made Drake not only famous, but rich.", "annotation_id": "440847", "url": "https://genius.com/440847", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Trill nigga, for real", "vault_lines": ["Trill nigga, for real"], "meaning": "Trill, a term coined by Houston rappers, is a combination of “true” and “real”.", "annotation_id": "437913", "url": "https://genius.com/437913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I wonder if they’d ever survive in this era In a time where it’s recreation To pull all your skeletons out the closet like Halloween decorations", "vault_lines": ["I wonder if they'd ever survive in this era", "In a time where it's recreation", "To pull all your skeletons out the closet like Halloween decorations"], "meaning": "Well speaking of greats from the past, Jay’s still doing alright, and even six-feet under Biggie Smalls and Pac are as popular as they ever were. Then there’s also the small matter of one Marshall Mathers.\n\nDrake elaborated on this idea in a recent Billboard interview:\n\n\n I genuinely feel like the first one that really launched a career in this time period. I say it on my album, “They take the greats from the past and compare us/But I wonder if they’d ever survive in this era.” And that shit to me [is] everything. Not even the leaks-I’m just talking about how malicious and intrusive the Internet is, and how essential and necessary the Internet is in our lives, in our careers. Twitter is an opinion with no consequences because you can be anybody, you can hide from anybody, you don’t have to see anybody – but you can reach anybody. And that shit is crazy\n\n These sites that are driven on tearing artists down daily… I remember back in the day – there were like two or three individuals on the radio, and one publication that would attack rappers and artists. And if you didn’t make that, you were good. There wasn’t really that much else to fuck you over. Lord only knows what people got away with – I can’t imagine. I commend any artist thriving in this generation, because it’s difficult. You have to have thick skin and just care about your craft, and know that there’s a thousand positive people for every one negative person\n\n It’s crazy how fast it’s all moving. Artists from the past, they had a different grind. It was more pavement, hitting the streets. Maybe it was harder work, I’ll never know. But for this generation it’s very hard work, upkeep, to be successful", "annotation_id": "437456", "url": "https://genius.com/437456", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "A lot of niggas came up off of a style that I made up But if all I hear is me, then who should I be afraid of?", "vault_lines": ["A lot of niggas came up off of a style that I made up", "But if all I hear is me, then who should I be afraid of?"], "meaning": "Drake’s style is heavily influenced by R&B. He includes plenty of singing and emotion in his songs (as heard in 2009’s So Far Gone). Although they may have jacked his style, he’s confident that they can’t compete with him.\n\nIt also means if competitors in the game are “coppin” a style he’s generated then he has nothing to fear. He’s supplying the style that they are “jocking” so all he’s seeing & hearing his himself basically being funneled through other artist, why would he then be worried?\n\nA dope punchline on the surface even though if you look deeper this argument is not entirely true since it´s widely recognised that Kanye West with 808s & Heartbreaks made that style up. That album is more of singing/R&B from a rapper and deals with the same themes as Drakes album – Love, loneliness, heartbreak and being introspective on that level. Drake has also citied 808s & Heartbreaks as his biggest influence.", "annotation_id": "435875", "url": "https://genius.com/435875", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I never hear the disses they try and point out to me But it’s whatever if somebody won’t make it a thing", "vault_lines": ["I never hear the disses they try and point out to me", "But it's whatever if somebody won't make it a thing"], "meaning": "Drake, from a November 2011 Village Voice article:\n\n To be honest with you, I coined the phrase ‘Dis me, and you’ll never hear a reply for it,’ so for me to go in would be silly.  But at the same time, I’m a competitive rapper, so I mean, nobody could ever take a direct shot at me, say my name or do a full song about me, and I sit idly by and take it. Unless it was wack, then I’d just be like, ‘You sound stupid,’ but if it was somebody with bars? I’m going back for sure\n\nWell, let’s see if he stands by those words in his beef with Common…", "annotation_id": "435782", "url": "https://genius.com/435782", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I’m going trigger happy just to see my niggas happy", "vault_lines": ["I'm going trigger happy just to see my niggas happy"], "meaning": "Continuing the metaphor of “killing” the rap game. Drake is gonna kill everything to make sure his homies are living good too.\n\nWe hope you didn’t forget what his name stood for:\n\nDo\nRight\nAnd\nKill\nEverything", "annotation_id": "435468", "url": "https://genius.com/435468", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "It’s the red bottom boss (Huh), came to buy the bar (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["It's the red bottom boss (Huh), came to buy the bar (Huh)"], "meaning": "Rick Ross wears Louboutins, which are known for their signature red-lacquered soles. This isn’t the first time Ricky’s worn red bottoms", "annotation_id": "435077", "url": "https://genius.com/435077", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Every day, them hammers bang, whippin’ yay like Anna Mae", "vault_lines": ["Every day, them hammers bang, whippin' yay like Anna Mae"], "meaning": "Ross is whipping up the crack in the kitchen. This may be a reference to Tina Turner (born Anna Mae Bullock) and the famous whipping (abuse) she suffered at the hands of Ike Turner.\n\nIt could also be from the movie Which Way Is Up? starring Richard Pryor. In the movie there is a scene in which he and his wife Anne Mae use a whip during sex.", "annotation_id": "434968", "url": "https://genius.com/434968", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Only fat nigga in the sauna with Jews", "vault_lines": ["Only fat nigga in the sauna with Jews"], "meaning": "Ross mentions here that he is rich enough to be conducting inside business deals with the real power players – it is a meme (if troubling) idea that Jews are rich and control Hollywood\n\nRoss is likely referring to the cultural institution of Jewish bathhouses in Miami—(the city with the second-most Jews per capita in the United States)—most famously the Russian Turkish Bath on Collins Ave in Miami Beach.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, Drake is half-Jewish, and Rozay is based in Miami, which is home to lots of retired Jewish people. Also possibly being some clever wordplay – ‘a pig in a sauna with jews’.\n\nThis is a perfect example of sophisticated ignorance.", "annotation_id": "434888", "url": "https://genius.com/434888", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "But Weezy and Stunna my only role models", "vault_lines": ["But Weezy and Stunna my only role models", "Haffa and Jordan my only role models"], "meaning": "Young Money Entertainment CEO is Lil Wayne aka Weezy\n\n\n\nYoung Money is an imprint of  Cash Money Records, which is founded by the hand rubber extraordinaire , Birdman a.k.a. Stunna\n\n\n\nAll joking aside, Birdman’s label has an extraordinary discography, from New Orleans classics beloved in the city but relatively unknown elsewhere to international smashes", "annotation_id": "434754", "url": "https://genius.com/434754", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I’m hearing all of the jokes, I know that they tryna push me", "vault_lines": ["I'm hearing all of the jokes, I know that they tryna push me"], "meaning": "Drake hears all the jokes about him being emo and soft because of his lyrics. He knows haters are trying to push him to throw shots back, but he makes music for his fans to love and not the haters\n\nThe use of the word “push” here may not be accidental – rapper Pusha T had some lyrics that many regarded as a diss towards the Canadian wonder, though the Clipse member has denied it.  The lyrics in question mention “Rappers on their sophomores, acting like they boss lords” and “The talk don’t match the leather, the swag don’t match the sweaters”", "annotation_id": "434631", "url": "https://genius.com/434631", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Bought a white Ghost, now shit is gettin’ spooky Very, very scary like shit you see in the movies", "vault_lines": ["Bought a white Ghost, now shit is gettin' spooky", "Very, very scary like shit you see in the movies"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the meaning of “ghost” to describe the Rolls-Royce Ghost he bought himself.  This line is, um, “reminiscent” of this Jay-Z classic\n\nThis plays into the scariness theme of the previous couplet", "annotation_id": "434613", "url": "https://genius.com/434613", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "YM, I remain that forever In the same place my brother Wayne at forever", "vault_lines": ["YM, I remain that forever", "In the same place my brother Wayne at forever"], "meaning": "Lots of talk recently that Drake might leave Young Money especially after OVOXO came about. Drake is letting everyone that they are misinformed\n\n\n\nDrizzy recently expanded on his relationship with Weezy in an interview with Billboard:\n\nCan you talk a bit about your relationship with Lil Wayne?\n\n That’s my brother. Over music, over everything. He’s responsible for everything that’s going on around me. But of course, it’s amazing. There’s a constant competitive vibe because that’s what rap is and we both love the same things in rap. In the back of my mind I’m always like, “I got to rap better than Wayne.” And I can’t say I know, but I feel like I put enough good music out there that he also feels like he’s … got to rap better than everybody. We play off each other, we make each other better. He said something in an interview that was so true that I respected him for it so much. They tried to corner him with question like, “Drake’s put out so much beastly music, do you feel like you created a monster?” And he’s like, ‘No, that’s my artist.“ And he’s right. I’m his soldier. That’s all I ever want to be for Wayne", "annotation_id": "434592", "url": "https://genius.com/434592", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Get some shake, a brick in the press And chef it like Mrs. Fields, they makin’ the cookie stretch", "vault_lines": ["Get some shake, a brick in the press", "And chef it like Mrs. Fields, they makin' the cookie stretch"], "meaning": "Mrs. Fields!:\n\n\n\n‘Shake’ and ‘brick’ are slang words in the drug world.\n\nDealers (that want to buy gifts for their daughters) have to stretch their product to make more money, similar to how cooks working for Mrs. Fields have to stretch the cookie dough. (Dough is also slang for money)", "annotation_id": "434492", "url": "https://genius.com/434492", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I’m a descendant of either Marley or Hendrix I haven’t figured it out ’cause my story is far from finished", "vault_lines": ["I'm a descendant of either Marley or Hendrix", "I haven't figured it out 'cause my story is far from finished"], "meaning": "Bob Marley and Jimi Hendrix were respectively visionary reggae and psychedelic rock musicians.  Drake has long talked of his admiration of Hendrix. Not to mention, in his second mainstream hit, “Over”, he makes sure to add his allusion to Marley’s own “Trenchtown Rock”: “One thing ‘bout music: when it hits, you feel no pain.”\n\n“My story is far from finished” also refers to the fact that both of the aforementioned legends are now deceased (cut down in their prime, even), but Drake does not see this same fate befalling him.\n\n\n\nSidenote: Like Drake, both Bob Marley and Jimi Hendrix were mixed race.", "annotation_id": "434486", "url": "https://genius.com/434486", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lord Knows", "status": "done", "targets": 46, "clicked": 46, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-lord-knows-lyrics", "genius_title": "Lord Knows"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I’m battlin’ the fact that the album ain’t even drop and already they asses complainin’", "vault_lines": ["I'm battlin' the fact that the album ain't even drop and already they asses complainin'"], "meaning": "People were complaining about and say other stuff about ICEMAN before it even dropped is what Drake is referring too", "annotation_id": "41025256", "url": "https://genius.com/41025256", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "Niggas want me all bent out of shape, rattled, and shaken", "vault_lines": ["Niggas want me all bent out of shape, rattled, and shaken"], "meaning": "For being so popular for so long Drake realizes people might have grown to resent his success. Since his first hit Best I Ever Had (2009) Drake has been on the top of the rap scene delivering popular music/hit records year after year.\n\nThe line could also be him reflecting back to what happened in 2024 during his beef with Kendrick Lamar where Drake might have realized how many people were waiting for him to slip up and been praying on his downfall “bent out of shape”", "annotation_id": "39868378", "url": "https://genius.com/39868378", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I have to father my mother and treat my son’s grandfather like my older brother", "vault_lines": ["I have to father my mother and treat my son's grandfather like my older brother"], "meaning": "This speaks to what it meant growing up as the man of the house after his father left. Sandi Graham raised Drake as a single mother in Toronto’s Forest Hill neighborhood while simultaneously dealing with serious health issues throughout his childhood. Drake had to step up into a protective role for her long before he was old enough to be expected to.\n\n\n\n“His son’s grandfather” would be his father Dennis Graham, whose relationship with him has swung between abandonment and eventual reconciliation. He frames Dennis more as an older brother rather than a father figure; not as an insult but an honest description of the only dynamic that actually works between them at this point. It could also be a nod to Dennis himself – who at 71, is still very much that flashy, outgoing guy living, moving more like a peer than a father-figure.", "annotation_id": "39829607", "url": "https://genius.com/39829607", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "While I turned a blind eye ’cause it’s more than painful, yeah", "vault_lines": ["While I turned a blind eye 'cause it's more than painful, yeah"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the previous line where Drake references Dora the Explorer. Dora often asks the audience if they can spot an object/character on screen, turning a “blind eye” to what’s around her.", "annotation_id": "39813581", "url": "https://genius.com/39813581", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "And this time, ask me to dig deeper, I’ll gladly explain it", "vault_lines": ["And this time, ask me to dig deeper, I'll gladly explain it"], "meaning": "Throughout the song, Drake repeatedly returns to the phrase “dig deep.” Early in the verse, he expresses frustration with people telling him to “dig deep” or explain himself further, rejecting the pressure to confront emotions and experiences he would rather keep buried. This is also a reference to the theme of death and being buried highlighted throughout the album, having to literally dig deep before lowering a coffin into a grave.\n\nAs the song progresses, Drake begins unpacking the emotional weight he has been carrying throughout the verse – family struggles, paranoia, failed relationships, pressure from fans, aging, and the burden of fame. By the final line, his attitude toward the phrase has completely shifted. Instead of resisting vulnerability, he now embraces it, showing a willingness to openly express his thoughts and emotions.\n\nAs the closing line of the album’s intro, this moment sets the tone for the rest of the project, where Drake appears far more candid and emotionally transparent, openly addressing his struggles and critics without hesitation.", "annotation_id": "39809211", "url": "https://genius.com/39809211", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I’m looking at all my niggas, they say, “Bro, we love you” But all my words really the shit that they be goin’ up to", "vault_lines": ["But all my words really the shit that they be goin' up to", "I'm looking at all my niggas, they say, \"Bro, we love you\""], "meaning": "Drake is recalling a moment where his crew affirmed their love to him. But Drake can’t help turning it over in his mind. Are they drawn to him, or to his words? The man and the music are two different things, and even the people closest to him might relate to him more through his lyrics than through who he actually is. This is a feeling he has wrestled with before on “Fake Love.”", "annotation_id": "39808771", "url": "https://genius.com/39808771", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "The skies are grey in Toronto, they not a golden color", "vault_lines": ["The skies are grey in Toronto, they not a golden color"], "meaning": "Toronto is famously overcast, The grey skies are the city’s aesthetic. Nicknamed the Golden State, California cultural identity is tied to it’s weather and sunshine. Drake could be drawing a contrast between his grey Toronto roots and Kendrick Lamar’s sun-soaked Compton upbringing.\n\nKendrick has spoken openly about being raised in a two-parent household in Compton. Drake’s story is the inverse, his father Dennis Graham left when he was five, leaving Sandi Graham to raise him alone in Toronto. The grey skies aren’t just meteorological, Drake is making sure nobody forgets which one of them did it the harder way.", "annotation_id": "39808663", "url": "https://genius.com/39808663", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "My dad got cancer right now, we battlin’ stages Trust me when I say there’s plenty things that I’d rather be facin’ For real", "vault_lines": ["My dad got cancer right now, we battlin' stages", "Trust me when I say there's plenty things that I'd rather be facin'"], "meaning": "Shortly after ICEMAN’s release, Drake’s father, Dennis Graham, confirmed to TMZ that he does not have cancer currently, but did in the past:\n\nNo, that was a while back. I’m okay now […] it was lung cancer at first, but, ultimately, I got a message that it disappeared, so… here I am.\n\nCancer is medically classified in stages one through four, measuring how far the disease has progressed. Drake maps that same idea onto his relationship with his father — that went from abandonment, to slow and complicated reconciliation.", "annotation_id": "39787620", "url": "https://genius.com/39787620", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "Either I’m too numb to this shit or the city is changin’", "vault_lines": ["Either I'm too numb to this shit or the city is changin'"], "meaning": "“Numb” ties back into the theme of the album, ICEMAN. Drake’s emotional coldness fits directly with his “Iceman” persona, where he no longer reacts emotionally to things that once affected him. Rather than responding emotionally, Drake presents himself as frozen over, distant, and harder to affect.\n\nThe line leaves open two possibilities. Either Toronto and the people around him have changed, or he has changed internally to the point where he barely recognises the difference anymore.", "annotation_id": "39783585", "url": "https://genius.com/39783585", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I think I also put the “man” in “manifestation”", "vault_lines": ["I think I also put the \"man\" in \"manifestation\""], "meaning": "Drake continues the wordplay from his previous “manipulation” line, flipping man to manifestation — the idea of turning desires or ambitions into reality.\n\nThe bar works as a flex about influence and success. Drake suggests that he not only manipulates situations, but he also manifests outcomes through his status as a leader in the rap game.\n\nAdditionally, by inserting “man” into both words, Drake frames himself as the reason behind everything happening around him.\n\nFinally, “the man” ties into the long-running theme of “The Boy” and his evolution to “The Man”, which he addresses on a later track, Firm Friends:\n\nAnd if everything goes to plan\nI will be makin' the name change from The Boy to The Man", "annotation_id": "39783519", "url": "https://genius.com/39783519", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I put the “man” in “manipulation” when I pay your rent and that is an obligation to our attachment Then I sprinkle in a little Mercedes and fashion", "vault_lines": ["Then I sprinkle in a little Mercedes and fashion", "I think I also put the \"man\" in \"manifestation\""], "meaning": "The first bar sees Drake admit to using his role as a male provider with power to influence his relationship and its surroundings.\n\nThe wordplay turns “man” into both masculinity and control, suggesting he knows the authority it gives him. He further frames financial support (rent) as the base of his relationship with this person, turning it into something that builds emotional dependence by claiming that she couldn’t afford to be with him.\n\nThe high-end gifts of luxury cars and clothes suggest that Drake can manipulate their relationship by providing luxury items as well as the necessities. The notion that he can “sprinkle it in” makes this behaviour mundane and effortless.\n\nUltimately, Drake doesn’t shy away from intertwining romance and money, acknowledging himself as the person who controls any relationship he’s in.", "annotation_id": "39783381", "url": "https://genius.com/39783381", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "This album better have some big features Well, sorry to burst your bubble, but I’m all alone for my mental And I’ve been tryna end so many people, I almost forgot the intro", "vault_lines": ["This album better have some big features", "Well, sorry to burst your bubble, but I'm all alone for my mental", "And I've been tryna end so many people, I almost forgot the intro"], "meaning": "Drake uses “end” to represent him attacking rivals like Kendrick Lamar in the aftermath of the 2024 rap wars. He then states he “almost forgot the intro”—a literal reference to this being the album’s opening track, but also acts as a darker metaphor for how his mental exhaustion and obsession with retaliation have caused him to lose focus on the music itself.\n\nHe ties that idea in with saying he’s “all alone” for his peace and mind, which is his reasoning for no big features on ICEMAN. However, he still included his good friends, 21 Savage on “B’s On the Table” and Future on “Ran To Atlanta.”", "annotation_id": "39783265", "url": "https://genius.com/39783265", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "Too many wire transfers bound to have a good girl’s morals tangled", "vault_lines": ["Too many wire transfers bound to have a good girl's morals tangled"], "meaning": "Word play on too many wires being tangled – but also commentary on how money compromises morals", "annotation_id": "39782990", "url": "https://genius.com/39782990", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I know for sure that my parents, they look at me and see an overcomer I’m looking back at them and these days, I see an older couple", "vault_lines": ["I know for sure that my parents, they look at me and see an overcomer", "I'm looking back at them and these days, I see an older couple"], "meaning": "Drake sees his own life as something he had to survive and exceed, and he believes his parents recognize that journey in him. They watched him endure continuous obstacles to get here; from a child raised in a blended family with a distant father, to a teen TV actor who reinvented himself into a global superstar, to a man who has weathered public scandals, rumors, and rap beefs that would have buried most people permanently.", "annotation_id": "39782460", "url": "https://genius.com/39782460", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I’m an only child, no one could’ve made another", "vault_lines": ["I'm an only child, no one could've made another"], "meaning": "A major part of Drake’s persona is that he was raised by his single mother, Sandi Graham – hence, his father was not around to make a biological full brother for him. (He does have several half-siblings who were never part of his household growing up.)\n\nDrake uses this as a metaphor for his conviction that he is a singular talent and the only person in the world like him.", "annotation_id": "39781807", "url": "https://genius.com/39781807", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "Sometimes I only see myself in my therapist glasses But I’m not taking it serious ’cause she’s very attractive", "vault_lines": ["Sometimes I only see myself in my therapist glasses", "But I'm not taking it serious 'cause she's very attractive"], "meaning": "A reference to the character Dr. Jennifer Melfi from The Sopranos.\n\n\n\nThroughout the series, she is the therapist of the main character Tony Soprano, who ends up falling in and out of love with her throughout their sessions, even dreaming of sleeping with her, which distracts him from the actual therapy.\n\nIn this line, Drake puts himself in Tony’s shoes, with Tony’s main reasons for therapy in the series being his newfound depression and trying to overcome his panic attacks. Drake likely relates to Tony’s depressive feelings following his loss in the Kendrick Lamar beef, which he has said throughout the album was one of the hardest times of his life.\n\nIt’s also worth mentioning that the song “Janice STFU” is also on the album, with it being speculated that this is (in part) a reference to Janice Soprano: the sister of Tony Soprano. Throughout the series, Janice is known for being short-tempered and loud-mouthed, often clashing with Tony and those around her.", "annotation_id": "39780637", "url": "https://genius.com/39780637", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "The rent-free penthouse and the Van Cleef floral bangles And plenty more examples that had you goin’ Dora the Explorer outside", "vault_lines": ["The rent-free penthouse and the Van Cleef floral bangles", "And plenty more examples that had you goin' Dora the Explorer outside"], "meaning": "The expensive lifestyle and gifts he provided made his girl feel comfortable enough to go out and act reckless/adventurous like Dora while he “turned a blind eye” on her unfaithfulness", "annotation_id": "39779847", "url": "https://genius.com/39779847", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "Feel like 40 won’t even listen to my words when he knows I’m in a load of trouble I’m in the cut just loadin’ rebuttals And I got a bunch of hits on my hand, see the swollen knuckles? I try to tell him it’s working out for me now, he say, ”Then show your muscles Prove to me that you’re still as strong when it’s only us two They know you thorough with bread, but there’s some shit you gotta pony up to” Basically he’s sayin’ I got growin’ up to do, I gotta dig deep I really hate those two words, they never bring peace", "vault_lines": ["But all my words really the shit that they be goin' up to", "Feel like 40 won't even listen to my words when he knows I'm in a load of trouble", "I'm in the cut just loadin' rebuttals", "And I got a bunch of hits on my hand, see the swollen knuckles?", "I try to tell him it's working out for me now, he say, \"Then show your muscles", "Prove to me that you're still as strong when it's only us two", "They know you thorough with bread, but there's some shit you gotta pony up to\"", "Basically he's sayin' I got growin' up to do, I gotta dig deep", "I really hate those two words, they never bring peace", "When I dig deep, they say dig deeper", "I'm still processing that shit, it got me so distracted", "I know for sure that my parents, they look at me and see an overcomer", "I see my only uncle lookin' like that shit that I ain't ownin' up to"], "meaning": "Drake reflects back to a private conversation he had with Noah “40” Shebib, his longtime friend, producer and co-founder of OVO Sound.\n\n40 asked Drake to look past his “eye for an eye” mindset following the 2024 beefs, but Drake justifies his preparation for attacks—“loadin' rebuttals”—and uses a clever play on words by comparing his catalog of musical “hits” to the physical “swollen knuckles” of a street fighter.\n\n“Thorough with bread” sounds like “Thoroughbred,” a breed of horse used for racing. 40 uses these words—along with some gym metaphors—to plead to Drake, asking him to take accountability for his actions and emphasizes that his wealth cannot solve his internal issues.\n\nBy asking 40 asking him to finally mature and “dig deep” into his own psyche, it forces Drake to confront his own flaws, insecurities, and past mistakes, which brings him turmoil rather than the peace he is searching for.", "annotation_id": "39779086", "url": "https://genius.com/39779086", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "Shout out to the real fans that knew what I had in the basement", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to the real fans that knew what I had in the basement"], "meaning": "Drake shoutouts his OG fans who trusted in what he had in store, a nod to the “basement” era, which artists usually refer to when talking about their beginning. The basement is also where things are stored, making the metaphor work for holding value as well. This is reminiscent of his bars from “Gimme a Hug,” where he promised his fans that he had their backs:\n\nI appreciate the fans rocking with me, this is really just a small token\nReally fuckin' with a visionary", "annotation_id": "39778414", "url": "https://genius.com/39778414", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "Fuck it, I’ll battle the label Fuck it, I’ll battle the majors, I’ll battle the stations ’til my ass is back in rotation", "vault_lines": ["Fuck it, I'll battle the label", "Fuck it, I'll battle the majors, I'll battle the stations 'til my ass is back in rotation"], "meaning": "Since the Kendrick Lamar beef, Drake has accused record labels and radio stations of defamation and secretly blacklisting his music. He thinks the industry is not giving him the radio play he deserves as the biggest rap artist because they want to build a narrative with Drake as the villain.", "annotation_id": "39778212", "url": "https://genius.com/39778212", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "It’ll take more than six pallbearers to carry that casket", "vault_lines": ["It'll take more than six pallbearers to carry that casket"], "meaning": "Mentioning (Pallbearers) people who actually carry the caskets. And a play on Paul Bearer a hall of famer and former WWE manager for the Undertaker. As he is seen with an urn and casket matches.\n\nAs well as saying 6, mentioning drakes style of writing.", "annotation_id": "39776784", "url": "https://genius.com/39776784", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "What died back in 2024 was a big piece So it’s like, this shit is me, but it isn’t me Y’all keep on asking me what it did to me, that’s what it did to me When I dig deep, they say dig deeper Tell us how it felt to meet the grim reaper", "vault_lines": ["What died back in 2024 was a big piece", "So it's like, this shit is me, but it isn't me", "Y'all keep on asking me what it did to me, that's what it did to me", "When I dig deep, they say dig deeper", "Tell us how it felt to meet the grim reaper"], "meaning": "Drake tackles the experiences he had going while engaging his publicized 2024 feud with Kendrick Lamar.\n\nAddressing the people asking how the feud affected him, Drake deflected the shots Lamar directed to him, saying that the latter’s claims were untrue of his character.\n\nHe also labelled Lamar as ‘the grim reaper’, akin to the artists' reputation as The Boogeyman.", "annotation_id": "39776689", "url": "https://genius.com/39776689", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I’m at the Bulgari in Turkey with my bitch and we ain’t come here for no big teeth, nah I came here to turn a new leaf and maybe finally get some sleep But all I can think about is the mountain to climb and the conversations surrounding my music like, “Did twin peak?”", "vault_lines": ["I'm at the Bulgari in Turkey with my bitch and we ain't come here for no big teeth, nah", "I came here to turn a new leaf and maybe finally get some sleep"], "meaning": "While the internet was busy writing his obituary, Drake was staying at the ultra-luxury resort, Bvlgari Resort Bodrum, on Turkiye’s coast.\n\n\n\nBvlgari might be a pun on “Bulgaria”, the country bordering Turkiye. The Bulgaria/Turkiye border is well known for being brutal to pass, as it is the entrance to EU territories–a desperate migrant might be willing to climb over mountains to get there.\n\nTurkey has become one of the world’s premier destinations for cosmetic dental tourism, with thousands flying in annually for veneers/crowns and whitening procedures. It became so popular that the term “Turkey teeth” became a shorthand for the blindingly white, oversized veneers of affluent idiots and Nouveau riche. By reminding us that’s not why he’s there, Drake is showing us he understands good taste (especially following the “BBL Drizzy” jibes during the beef, which alleged he had tacky plastic surgery).\n\nHe went there to exhale after one of the most grueling chapters of his career, but even there, his mind won’t quiet thinking about the discourse. The mountain to climb (symbolizing music hierarchy) and the noise surrounding it are inseparable (much like a mountain with two summits.) Drake will talk more about the racial dimensions of the mountain of rap in “Make Them Remember”, in which he compares the summit of rap to his light skin and Jewish heritage – the twin peaks could therefore be his two races.\n\nhttps://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcTx786XSWDRNE_GbyKedmNxpRAKUfho9HcZ4w&s\n\n“Twin Peak” is also a reference to David Lynch’s 1990 landmark series, which is basically a show about duality. Twin Peaks is also known for its score, which even won a Grammy in 1991.", "annotation_id": "39776419", "url": "https://genius.com/39776419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "Random intrusive thought, but what happened to Taz’s Angels? So many people that’s not around from that generation", "vault_lines": ["Random intrusive thought, but what happened to Taz's Angels?", "So many people that's not around from that generation"], "meaning": "Founded in 2011, Taz’s Angels was a group of models managed by Michael “Taz” Williams, who built a glamorous social media empire out of a Miami mansion. In 2015, their Miami mansion was raided by the FBI as part of a human trafficking investigation. The FBI suspected involvement in underage prostitution and sex trafficking.\n\n\n\nAnthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith — CEO of TDE, Kendrick Lamar’s former label — has long been linked to former Taz Angel Ashley Martelle, with rumors of an abusive relationship circulating online for years.\n\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/f9e9d47448a94fdbc290c3f0c12de85cfad44a1293592346464003a8cb7c72dc%2F4fhj960io2p Ashley posting to Twitter and Instagram in relation to the abusive relationship", "annotation_id": "39775984", "url": "https://genius.com/39775984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I don’t do psychedelics because I’m too scared of unpacking", "vault_lines": ["I don't do psychedelics because I'm too scared of unpacking"], "meaning": "Psychedelics are known for making people confront their emotions, so Drake is scared of his true emotions coming out.\n\nThis may also be a reference to Kendrick’s line on “Meet the Grahams”: “Therapy’s a lovely start, but I suggest some Ayahuasca, strip the ego from the bottom…”", "annotation_id": "39775951", "url": "https://genius.com/39775951", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "Been so sure of my words that I haven’t used a pencil", "vault_lines": ["Been so sure of my words that I haven't used a pencil"], "meaning": "A pencil comes with an eraser to second-guess and walk things back. Drake hasn’t needed one. Everything he’s put down he’s stood behind. Pen only, permanent ink, exactly like a man who knows what he wants to say before he says it.\n\nTrivia: Drake revealed in his 2010 documentary Better Than Good Enough that he doesn’t actually write his raps on paper at all, he used to type them out on his BlackBerry,", "annotation_id": "39775800", "url": "https://genius.com/39775800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "line": "I’m feeling like BTS ’cause it took the whole career for me to be so discovered", "vault_lines": ["I'm feeling like BTS 'cause it took the whole career for me to be so discovered"], "meaning": "Drake namedrops BTS, drawing a parallel between his own rise and theirs.\n\nBTS' rise to stardom and fame came under initial hardships of finding their place in the music industry. Just as BTS operated largely behind the scenes before the world caught on (also note the play on BTS/Behind the scenes,) Drake suggests his truest self has always been there to find—it just took people long enough to look. On what may be his most introspective album, he frames this moment as a kind of late discovery after one of the longest runs in modern rap, his audience is finally seeing him more fully. That reading lands harder given that his Spotify currently sits at an all-time high of 92.3M monthly listeners.\n\nMoreover, Drake slanted his pronunciation of ‘career’ and ‘so’, making them sound like ‘(South) Korea’ and ‘Seoul’, respectively, referring to BTS' home country and city.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBTS members V and j-hope quickly responded to this lyric with an Instagram story on V’s Instagram account.", "annotation_id": "39775752", "url": "https://genius.com/39775752", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Cry", "status": "done", "targets": 27, "clicked": 27, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-make-them-cry-lyrics", "genius_title": "Make Them Cry"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "And Drake just stand for Do Right And Kill Everything", "vault_lines": ["And Drake just stand for Do Right And Kill Everything"], "meaning": "Drake uses his middle name as an acronym to shed light on what he does on a daily basis. A frequent idiom is applied here in a different format, in which people may say “that’s my middle name” to signify that they are synonymous with the best qualities of the said attribute. For instance, Michael Jordan may say, “Basketball is my middle name,” because he is renowned worldwide as one of the greatest basketball players of all time.\n\nDrake uses a similar idea here with his middle name, with “Do Right And Kill Everything” being his defined slogan of what it is he lives by.", "annotation_id": "21295817", "url": "https://genius.com/21295817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Make ’em run and tell they friends like a marathon", "vault_lines": ["Make 'em run and tell they friends like a marathon"], "meaning": "This line is a reference to the Story of the Battle of Marathon (where the sporting event got its name). Following the battle, a messenger had to run from Marathon to Athens to deliver the message of the Greek victory.\n\nLil' Wayne makes the girls he’s with want to go run and tell their friends about him, just like the messenger did.\n\nFurthermore, in modern society when someone runs a marathon for sport, they tend to share it as a personal update in conversation and on social media", "annotation_id": "21234334", "url": "https://genius.com/21234334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Soo-woo if you bangin’, motherfucker", "vault_lines": ["Soo-woo if you bangin', motherfucker"], "meaning": "“Soo-woo” is a call used by members of the Bloods to express affiliation and to greet each other. It’s been claimed that Wayne had ties to the Bloods since he was a teenager, during his Hot Boyz days. While he hasn’t validated that claim, he has used the call on numerous songs, including June 2008’s “Lollipop (Remix)”:\n\nI do it for Bloods' sake, soo-woo, think it’s voodoo\n\nAnd June 2008’s “Mr Carter”:\n\nNow all my Bloods scream “Soo-woo” and “Da-da-doe”", "annotation_id": "18428690", "url": "https://genius.com/18428690", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "I’m stickin’ to the script like lint on denim", "vault_lines": ["I'm stickin' to the script like lint on denim"], "meaning": "Lint is the tiny bits of fabric fibers that are usually stuck to clothing. Wayne plays on the word “sticking\" as “sticking to the script” means to be legit. This correlates with the last two lines above which talk about Wayne’s prison stint at Rikers Island.", "annotation_id": "18428499", "url": "https://genius.com/18428499", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Damn, I’ll be gone ’til November But fuck it, I ain’t trippin’, I know Drizzy gon’ kill ’em", "vault_lines": ["Damn, I'll be gone 'til November", "But fuck it, I ain't trippin', I know Drizzy gon' kill 'em"], "meaning": "On October 22, 2009, Lil Wayne pleaded guilty to attempted criminal possession of a weapon, which stems from a 2007 incident when police found .40 caliber pistol in his possession. After two postponements, on March 8, 2010, Wayne was sentenced to one year in prison, which he served at Rikers Island. On November 4, 2010, after serving eight months of his one-year sentence, Wayne was released.\n\nIn April 2010, during Lil Wayne’s incarceration, his friends created a website called Weezy Thanx You, which published letters written by Wayne while he was in prison. The first letter was titled “Gone ‘til November,” in which Wayne was said he thought about his children every day, spent his time working out regularly, and read the Bible on a daily basis.", "annotation_id": "18428349", "url": "https://genius.com/18428349", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Voice baritone, haters carry on", "vault_lines": ["Voice baritone, haters carry on"], "meaning": "A baritone voice is the most common male voice and is the second-lowest adult male singing voice. It falls in between the higher tenor and lower bass registers. An example of a male singer with a baritone voice is a male opera singer.", "annotation_id": "18428298", "url": "https://genius.com/18428298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "It’s only me and her ’cause the Bugatti a coupe", "vault_lines": ["It's only me and her 'cause the Bugatti a coupe"], "meaning": "A “coupe” is a passenger car with a fixed roof and Bugatti is a French supercar manufacturer. In 2010, Lil Wayne became the first rapper to buy the Bugatti Veyron, which cost him $1.8 million.\n\nThe Veyron is one of the fastest cars in the world, clocking a top speed of 254.04 MPH and is one of the rarest with Wayne’s Bugatti being one of 450 units manufactured as of 2018.", "annotation_id": "18428183", "url": "https://genius.com/18428183", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "’Cause to her I’m just a rapper and soon she’ll have met another", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne would later reuse this line on Drake’s November 2011 song, “The Real Her.” The song, along with Wayne, notably features André 3000 and is on Drake’s follow-up album to Thank Me Later, Take Care.", "annotation_id": "18428072", "url": "https://genius.com/18428072", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "No, I’m not that thuggish, not that ruggish but I do pack Bone", "vault_lines": ["No, I'm not that thuggish, not that ruggish but I do pack Bone"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Bone Thugs-N-Harmony’s April 1994 song, “Thuggish Ruggish Bone.\" However, “bone,” in this context, refers to Wayne’s genitalia.\n\nThis may also be another line paying homage to New Orleans rapper Mystikal, who rapped a nearly identical line on his June 1994 song, “Y’all Ain’t Ready Yet”:\n\nI’m not that Thug and I’m not that Ruggish but I do pack Bone", "annotation_id": "18421497", "url": "https://genius.com/18421497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Weezy F. Baby and the F is for Front Do’ ’Cause that’s where I brang it", "vault_lines": ["Weezy F. Baby and the F is for Front Do'", "'Cause that's where I brang it"], "meaning": "‘Weezy F Baby’ is one of Wayne’s various monikers that he has coined throughout his rap career. He’s been using the name as far back as 2004—on “Da Drought,” he raps:\n\nWeezy F Baby, F ya ladies so quick so fast\n\nIn February 2018, Genius broke down the numerous times Wayne has used the nickname:\n\nhttps://www.dailymotion.com/video/x6f74nr", "annotation_id": "18421417", "url": "https://genius.com/18421417", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "I ain’t lyin’, I shoot, you don’t need signs for proof", "vault_lines": ["I ain't lyin', I shoot, you don't need signs for proof"], "meaning": "In October 2009, Lil Wayne pleaded guilty to attempted criminal possession of a weapon and in March 2010, he was sentenced to one year in prison, which he served at Rikers Island.", "annotation_id": "18421271", "url": "https://genius.com/18421271", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "And when I’m in the booth, bitch, the lion is loose", "vault_lines": ["And when I'm in the booth, bitch, the lion is loose", "I'm gone, I'm gone, when I'm gone"], "meaning": "Young Lion is one of Lil Wayne’s nicknames. He also called himself Young Lion on his June 2007 song, “Zoo”:\n\nSee I’m the young lion and he the young bull\n\nDrake also mentioned the nickname, as well as his own, Young Angel, on Wayne’s November 2008 song, “Stuntin”:\n\nYoung Angel, Young Lion, I’m done tryin'\n\nAnd again on his February 2009 song, “Ignant Shit”:\n\nYoung Angel and Young Lion, you know what it is\n\nThis line also concludes the “lying/lion” wordplay Wayne used in the last two lines.", "annotation_id": "18421195", "url": "https://genius.com/18421195", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Turn you to a vegetable like you lyin’ in soup", "vault_lines": ["Turn you to a vegetable like you lyin' in soup"], "meaning": "When someone in a “vegetable” state, they are no longer able to function physically or mentally. On Gudda Gudda’s 2009 song, “Demolition Freestyle Part 2,” Wayne similarily raps:\n\nTell your boyfriend I’ll turn him to a vegetable", "annotation_id": "18421159", "url": "https://genius.com/18421159", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Young Money’s Jerry Sloan, I turn every stone", "vault_lines": ["Young Money's Jerry Sloan, I turn every stone"], "meaning": "Jerry Sloan was a long-time NBA coach for the Utah Jazz who, aside from being a Hall Of Fame player, is known for coaching one team longer than anyone in NBA history. He coached the Jazz for 23 seasons until he resigned in 2011. He was the fifth coach in NBA history to reach 1,000 wins and is one of two coaches in NBA history to earn 1,000 wins with one team.\n\nOn the other hand, Young Money is Lil Wayne’s record label that he found in 2005.", "annotation_id": "18420867", "url": "https://genius.com/18420867", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Beat the pussy up, call me Larry Holmes", "vault_lines": ["Beat the pussy up, call me Larry Holmes"], "meaning": "Larry Holmes is a former Ameican professional heavyweight boxer. He is regarded as one of the greatest heavyweights of all time—he held the WBC heavyweight title from 1978 to 1983, the lineal heavyweight titles from 1980 to 1985, and the IBF heavyweight title from 1983 to 1985. He retired in 2002 with a record of 69 wins and 6 losses and was inducted into the International Boxing Hall of Fame in 2008.", "annotation_id": "18418886", "url": "https://genius.com/18418886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Uh, I’m a love machine And I won’t work for nobody but you", "vault_lines": ["Uh, I'm a love machine", "And I won't work for nobody but you"], "meaning": "These lines are taken from The Miracles‘ 1975 hit, “Love Machine” where the lead vocalist Billy Griffin said the same thing. The overall song is about a lover being compared to an electronic device.\n\nThe song is The Miracles' best selling single—it peaked at number one on the Billboard Hot 100, sold over 4.5 million copies, and generated over $15 million in revenue.", "annotation_id": "18415518", "url": "https://genius.com/18415518", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "James Bond cologne, honey I put on", "vault_lines": ["James Bond cologne, honey I put on"], "meaning": "A February 2019 article by GQ Magazine broke down the type of fragrance fictional spy, James Bond wore since it’s never explicitly explained in the novels and movies. According to the article:\n\nBond never endorses a specific Floris fragrance, but he clearly approves of the brand. That’s no accident. [The author of James Bond novels, Ian Fleming] wore Floris No. 89 Eau de Toilette. This is arguably, therefore, the closest you will get to a ‘Bond fragrance’.", "annotation_id": "18415378", "url": "https://genius.com/18415378", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Yes, I am Weezy, but I ain’t asthmatic", "vault_lines": ["Yes, I am Weezy, but I ain't asthmatic"], "meaning": "“Weezy” is a nickname Lil Wayne has been using for a long time and notes he’s not wheezing, a primary symptom of asthma. While it’s not known whether Wayne has asthma or not, a 2009 Rolling Stone article noted that Wayne used an asthma inhaler while recording a song.", "annotation_id": "18415239", "url": "https://genius.com/18415239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Man, I swear my bitches do it ’til they suck the brown off Ew, that’s nasty", "vault_lines": ["Man, I swear my bitches do it 'til they suck the brown off"], "meaning": "This may be a nod to the Family Guy and The Cleveland Show character, Cleveland Brown. “That’s nasty” is one of the signature phrases he says from time to time on the shows.", "annotation_id": "18415115", "url": "https://genius.com/18415115", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Gettin’ money every day, I’m a groundhog", "vault_lines": ["Gettin' money every day, I'm a groundhog"], "meaning": "This is an allusion to the critically acclaimed 1993 American comedy film, Groundhog Day, which stars Bill Murray as Phil Connors. In the movie, Phil is caught in a time-loop, reliving the same day over and over repeatedly. Wayne uses the same concept for getting money repeatedly every day.\n\nNotably, there’s also a scene in the movie where Phil steals a bag of money from an unguarded armored car.", "annotation_id": "18415053", "url": "https://genius.com/18415053", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "’Bout to scoop ya girl up like a ground ball", "vault_lines": ["'Bout to scoop ya girl up like a ground ball"], "meaning": "In baseball, a “ground-ball play” is when the ball is hit on the ground and rolls or bounces into the mitt of a member of the opposing team. Wayne uses this analogy figurately for taking someone’s girl.", "annotation_id": "18415027", "url": "https://genius.com/18415027", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Kickin’ bitches out the condo like Pam", "vault_lines": ["Kickin' bitches out the condo like Pam"], "meaning": "Pam is a character from the popular 90s sitcom, Martin. Pam was Gina’s (Martin’s girlfriend) best friend. She often visited their apartment and in some cases, got kicked out. In the same way, Wayne kick girls out of his house after sex.", "annotation_id": "18413761", "url": "https://genius.com/18413761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Ooh, shit, motherfucker, God damn", "vault_lines": ["Ooh, shit, motherfucker, God damn"], "meaning": "Wayne may be paying homage to New Orleans rapper Mystikal, who opened his verse on Snoop Dogg’s 1999’s song, “Ghetto Symphony,” with the same exact line.", "annotation_id": "18413682", "url": "https://genius.com/18413682", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Yeah, girl, it’s on You know what it is when I finally make it home I just hope that you miss me a little when I’m gone Yeah, I hope that you miss me a little when I’m gone And you just tell me what you down for, anything you down for I know things have changed, know I used to be around more But you should miss me a little when I’m gone I just hope that you miss me a little when I’m gone, gone, gone", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, girl, it's on", "You know what it is when I finally make it home", "I just hope that you miss me a little when I'm gone", "Yeah, I hope that you miss me a little when I'm gone", "And you just tell me what you down for, anything you down for", "I know things have changed, know I used to be around more", "But you should miss me a little when I'm gone", "I just hope that you miss me a little when I'm gone, gone, gone", "And I won't work for nobody but you", "I'm gone, I'm gone, when I'm gone", "I just hope that you miss me, miss me", "Miss me, miss me", "Miss me a little when I'm gone", "Miss me a little when I'm gone, gone, gone"], "meaning": "In an April 2010 interview with MTV News, Drake explained these lines:\n\n[These lines] about being away from what you love and hoping that when you’re gone, doing you, somebody out there misses you.", "annotation_id": "18409402", "url": "https://genius.com/18409402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "I love Nicki Minaj, I told her I’d admit it I hope one day we get married just to say we fuckin’ did it And girl, I’m fuckin’ serious, I’m wit’ it if you wit’ it ‘Cause yo’ verses turn me on and yo’ pants are mighty fitted", "vault_lines": ["I love Nicki Minaj, I told her I'd admit it", "I hope one day we get married just to say we fuckin' did it", "And girl, I'm fuckin' serious, I'm wit' it if you wit' it", "'Cause yo' verses turn me on and yo' pants are mighty fitted", "I just hope that you miss me, miss me"], "meaning": "In August 2010, Drake and Nicki Minaj both announced on Twitter that they were married:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/nickiminaj/status/22298785237?lang=en\n\nWhile this was likely a publicity stunt, Drake revealed two months earlier in a June 2010 interview with MTV News that he always had a crush on her:\n\nI’ve always really, actually, really had a crush on her, always really loved her, and she’s always just looked at me as, like, her little brother.\n\nHe later rapped about marrying Nicki again on her December 2010 song, “Moment 4 Life”:\n\nFuck it, me and Nicki Nick gettin' married today", "annotation_id": "18409281", "url": "https://genius.com/18409281", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "I’m just feelin’ sorry for whoever gotta sweep up Yeah, bills everywhere, trill everything", "vault_lines": ["I'm just feelin' sorry for whoever gotta sweep up", "Yeah, bills everywhere, trill everything"], "meaning": "Drake depicts the scene of a strip club after a big night with money everywhere on the floor.\n\nThe word “trill” is a blend of the words true and real. Bun B—who made an appearance in the song earlier—notably has five albums with the word “trill” in it: 2005’s Trill, 2008’s II Trill, 2010’s Trill OG, 2013’s Trill OG: The Epilogue and 2018’s Return of the Trill.", "annotation_id": "18409165", "url": "https://genius.com/18409165", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "That’s why me and Lil Jaz ’bout to spaz, can you keep up?", "vault_lines": ["That's why me and Lil Jaz 'bout to spaz, can you keep up?"], "meaning": "“Lil Jaz” is Jas Prince, son of Houston rap executive James Prince. He was the one who introduced Lil Wayne to Drake—he told The Fader in 2015:\n\nI was really cool with Lil Wayne, and I brought Drake to his attention […] I put on ‘Replacement Girl’ and look over and see that he’s bopping his head, and I’m like Okay! […] I played Wayne another song called ‘Brand New,’ and I’m like, ‘He sings, too.’ Wayne is like, ‘Hold up, man, this dude is really fucking dope.’ He’s like, ‘Where is he at?’ I told him in Canada, and Wayne was like, ‘Let’s fly him here right now.’ We flew him into Houston. Drake and I had been talking a lot—I was really blowing him up, we was always texting—but that was my first time actually meeting him. When I brought him to Wayne, Wayne was nonchalant. […] Eventually Wayne was like, ‘I’m about to go to Atlanta tonight, why don’t y'all ride with me on the bus?’ […] When we got there, we went to the studio. That’s when Drake first recorded the original ‘Forever’ and ‘Stunt Hard.’ A couple of weeks later that got leaked, and that was the beginning of Drake.", "annotation_id": "18409036", "url": "https://genius.com/18409036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Call up King of Diamonds and tell Chyna it’d be worth the flight I’ll be at my table, stackin’ dollars to the perfect height Work somethin’, twerk somethin’, basis", "vault_lines": ["Call up King of Diamonds and tell Chyna it'd be worth the flight", "I'll be at my table, stackin' dollars to the perfect height", "Work somethin', twerk somethin', basis"], "meaning": "King of Diamonds is a popular strip club and Blac Chyna is a former stripper. She began stripping at the King of Diamonds in Miami in 2010 to help pay for her business classes at Johnson and Wales University before transiting to modeling shortly afterward.\n\n\n\nThe line “Work somethin', twerk somethin', basis” is taken from UGK’s November 2001 song, “Take It Off,” where Pimp C raps:\n\nIt’s onna, work somethin, twerk somethin basis", "annotation_id": "18408934", "url": "https://genius.com/18408934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Yeah, I’m in the city of the purple Sprite", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm in the city of the purple Sprite"], "meaning": "“Purple Sprite” is lean—a mixture of promethazine codeine and soda. Lean originated in Houston in the 1960s when blues musicians would mix Robitussin with alcohol. Houston legends like DJ Screw and Pimp C all contributed to the popularity of lean, as well as Three 6 Mafia’s February 2000 hit “Sippin’ on Some Syrup,” which was a song dedicated to lean.\n\n“Miss Me” was also notably recorded in Houston. In a June 2010 interview with Complex, producer Noah “40” Shebib said:\n\nWe did it in January or February. We started working on that in Houston.", "annotation_id": "18408539", "url": "https://genius.com/18408539", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Neeks got the weed, Hush got a gun CJ got my credit cards and a lot of ones", "vault_lines": ["Neeks got the weed, Hush got a gun", "CJ got my credit cards and a lot of ones"], "meaning": "Neeks (Niko)—one of Drake’s longtime friends from high school—and Hush are both members of Drake’s OVO crew and CJ is his road manager. Drake gave Niko along with other OVO members a shoutout on his November 2011 song, “The Motto”:\n\nShout goes out to Niko, J and Chubbs, shouts to Gibbo", "annotation_id": "18408422", "url": "https://genius.com/18408422", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "I will have a model wife, yo’ bitch is as hot as ice", "vault_lines": ["I will have a model wife, yo' bitch is as hot as ice"], "meaning": "Models are a common topic in Drake’s music—on his September 2007 song, “Closer,” he raps:\n\nTo be the one and only nigga that you ain’t gon' see\nIn the club with a model spillin' drinks on me\n\nOn his November 2011 song, “Lord Knows,” he also raps:\n\nTalking strippers and models that try to gain attention\n\n“Hot” means attractive. Drake plays on “hot” by making a comparison to ice—which is cold—essentially meaning this other girl is unattractive.", "annotation_id": "18386896", "url": "https://genius.com/18386896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Drake, you got ’em, right? Yeah, I got ’em, Bun", "vault_lines": ["Drake, you got 'em, right? Yeah, I got 'em, Bun"], "meaning": "“Miss Me” was originally rapper Bun B’s song—titled “All Night Long”—but after Lil Wayne hopped on the song, Drake asked if he could have the record, to which Bun B agreed.  In a June 2009 interview with Complex, Bun B explained:\n\nIt was recorded for my project […] It was the ’All Night Long’ hook because it wasn’t called ’Miss Me’ when we recorded it. Then, Wayne heard it and wanted to get on it. So we ended up putting Wayne on the record, and Drake asked me if he could have the record back with Wayne on it. It doesn’t bother me because we still make music. It wasn’t shady business or anything. All the verses were 32 bars [so they took mine off.] […] [The part where I ask, ‘Drake, you got ’em right?’] was already in the song because we wrote it together for my album so that was already in his verse. He wrote it like that for me to say that line right there. Then, when he did the verse with Wayne, they just kept me in there.", "annotation_id": "18386750", "url": "https://genius.com/18386750", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "I’m ‘bout my paper like a motherfuckin’ scratch-and-win", "vault_lines": ["I'm 'bout my paper like a motherfuckin' scratch-and-win"], "meaning": "Scratch-and-win cards are cards that people can scratch off numbers and potentially win large amounts of money.\n\nDrake’s certainly focused on his money—as of 2019, he is worth about $150 million, according to Forbes. He also told them in 2013:\n\nEvery year, we just want to get more prepared and better at touring and better at things that make money.", "annotation_id": "18386608", "url": "https://genius.com/18386608", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Uh, yeah, forever in our hearts JJ, we love you boy—H-Town!", "vault_lines": ["Uh, yeah, forever in our hearts", "JJ, we love you boy—H-Town!"], "meaning": "“JJ” aka Shootamac is the name of one of Drake’s acquaintances who was shot and killed in Houston, TX in 2010. JJ is also the younger brother to Houston hip hop artist Onehunnidt", "annotation_id": "2214750", "url": "https://genius.com/2214750", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "I said, “Tell me what’s really goin’ on” Drizzy back up in this thing, I’m ready, what’s hannenin’?", "vault_lines": ["I said, \"Tell me what's really goin' on\"", "Drizzy back up in this thing, I'm ready, what's hannenin'?"], "meaning": "Drake is interpolating lyrics from the first verse of Soulja Boy’s August 2007 song, “What’s Hannenin'”:\n\nTell me what’s really going on\nSoulja Boy up in this thing, I’m ready\nWhat’s hannenin'?\n\nThese aren’t Soulja’s original lyrics however—he interpolated those lines from Shawty Lo’s chorus on D4L’s November 2005 song, “I’m Da Man”:\n\nI don’t know what y'all been told\nShawty Lo back on the map, what’s up\nThat’s what’s hannenin'\n\nDrake’s interpolation re-entered the public consciousness in January 2019 when Soulja Boy appeared on Power 105.1 FM’s The Breakfast Club and cited it as evidence of Drake copying his flow.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/Kp5A_1UAJog?t=2950", "annotation_id": "45828", "url": "https://genius.com/45828", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Someone tell Maliah I’m on fire, she should work tonight", "vault_lines": ["Someone tell Maliah I'm on fire, she should work tonight"], "meaning": "Maliah Michel is an American adult performer, hip-hop model, and stripper from Houston. At the time, she and Drake were dating.\n\nDrake first met her at a Houston-based strip club and flew her to Jamaica a few months later to star in the official video for “Find Your Love” as the lead dancer. “I’m on fire” comes from the fact that the URL of Maliah’s MySpace account is maliahsonfire.\n\nDrake also gave her a shoutout on his February 2009 song, “November 18th”:\n\nJust met a girl, say she from the H-Town", "annotation_id": "17704", "url": "https://genius.com/17704", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Gotta do it one time for Haiti, what up, Zo?", "vault_lines": ["Gotta do it one time for Haiti, what up, Zo?"], "meaning": "Wayne is giving a shoutout to the country of Haiti, which was devastated by an earthquake in January 2010 – much like Alonzo “Zo” Mourning who put in a lot of volunteer work to raise money for Haiti in the wake of the disaster.\n\n\n\nWayne shouts out Zoe Pound, a Haitian street gang.", "annotation_id": "17697", "url": "https://genius.com/17697", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "It’s Blood gang, slime, but I parlay with Snoop", "vault_lines": ["It's Blood gang, slime, but I parlay with Snoop"], "meaning": "“Parlay” means to hang out—Lil Wayne is a Blood while Snoop Dogg is a Crip. Although the Bloods and Crips are rivals, Wayne hangs out with Snoop regardless. They have also collaborated on four different occasions: OutKast’s August 2006 song, “Hollywood Divorce,” The Game’s November 2006 song, “It’s Okay (One Blood) [Remix],” David Banner’s July 2007 song, “Speaker,” and Wayne’s September 2018 song, “Dope Niggaz.”\n\n“Slime” is an acronym for “street life intelligence, money [is] everything.” It originated from Harlem, New York.", "annotation_id": "17614", "url": "https://genius.com/17614", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "line": "Gone for surgery but now I’m back again", "vault_lines": ["Gone for surgery but now I'm back again", "I'm gone, I'm gone, when I'm gone"], "meaning": "On July 31, 2009, Drake tore his ACL while performing “Best I Ever Had” with Lil Wayne in Camden, New Jersey. Subsequently, he had surgery on his knee in August.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/H7Jw9pbN-eQ?t=30", "annotation_id": "17593", "url": "https://genius.com/17593", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Miss Me", "status": "done", "targets": 39, "clicked": 39, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-miss-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "Miss Me"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "For a label meetin’, thinkin’ ’bout more cake than a funnel I remember", "vault_lines": ["for a label meetin', thinkin' 'bout more cake than a funnel"], "meaning": "Drake is thinking about cake, which in this context, means money, as he recalls heading to an important label meeting early in his career.\n\nDrake is also referring to funnel cake, which is a type of cake that originated in the US.", "annotation_id": "40936758", "url": "https://genius.com/40936758", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Crib I got now make me think of when I had to put some money on a mortgage", "vault_lines": ["Crib I got now make me think of when I had to put some money on a mortgage"], "meaning": "Drake here refers to when he was broke, continuing the theme of coming up from nothing as seen throughout the song. This theme is starkly contrasted by the theme of him being extremely rich now. Crib is another word for house, so the house he has now makes him remember when he was struggling to pay the mortgage on a house.", "annotation_id": "38560840", "url": "https://genius.com/38560840", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "[Chorus]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Like a broke couple we on one accord, for sure”\n\n“We on one accord, for sure” is a homophonic sentence to “We don’ wan’ a car for sure” meaning they can’t afford a car because they’re a broke couple", "annotation_id": "36249589", "url": "https://genius.com/36249589", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I was in the club gettin’ lost in the crowd Wasn’t doin’ what I wanted to, I’d do what was allowed", "vault_lines": ["I was in the club gettin' lost in the crowd", "Wasn't doin' what I wanted to, I'd do what was allowed"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to having a lack of power, basically feeling powerless in certain situations. He realized fame wasn’t going to give him the freedom he desired so turned to the “gangster” way for power.", "annotation_id": "32173267", "url": "https://genius.com/32173267", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "This shit don’t come on vinyl, I’m still record-breakin’", "vault_lines": ["This shit don't come on vinyl, I'm still record-breakin'"], "meaning": "Vinyl (or Vinyl Records) is the common name for phonograph records made with polyvinyl chloride.\nDrake didn’t release an official vinyl record for his latest projects (including For All The Dogs), and using this fact to make a wordplay on 2 meanings of the word “record”, when claiming his albums are breaking streaming and selling records without even releasing a vinyl record of his album, which would have increased streams and sales.", "annotation_id": "30165151", "url": "https://genius.com/30165151", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "We in love with the same girl, beefin’ like a cow I would pick them shits up, nigga wouldn’t even smile Nigga better crack a smile ’fore we do a nigga foul", "vault_lines": [", we in love with the same girl, beefin' like a cow", "I would pick them shits up, nigga wouldn't even smile", "Nigga better crack a smile 'fore we do a nigga foul"], "meaning": "Kai Bent Lee is who Drake is referring to. At some point before they were friends they were dealing with the same girl. Kai owns a clothing brand called Strawberry which means he was most likely and still is doing Drakes merch. They knew about each other and they didn’t like each other but they didn’t let that get in the way of business and now they’re good friends. Also noted if you go to Kai Instagram he almost never smiles in any of his pictures so that is probably just Drake poking fun at him which is why he says “crack a smile\"", "annotation_id": "29885601", "url": "https://genius.com/29885601", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I was livin’ on a cloud", "vault_lines": ["I was livin' on a cloud", "I was on a Greyhound way before the jet"], "meaning": "A possible reference to Drake’s  third studio album, Nothing Was The Same, which its album cover is literally Drake’s head in the clouds.", "annotation_id": "29834380", "url": "https://genius.com/29834380", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "‘Member Reesie stayin’ at the house, then she left me for my dawg like she never took the vow", "vault_lines": ["'Member Reesie stayin' at the house, then she left me for my dawg like she never took the vow"], "meaning": "Here Drake is recalling what seems to be another heartbreak he suffered before the success. Arguably the most consistent theme throughout his music.\n\nI’m sure by “took the vow” he’s referring to their profession of love to each other. Reese clearly broke their promise and did him dirty by getting with his friend", "annotation_id": "29834369", "url": "https://genius.com/29834369", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "To keep it real, I wasn’t really gangster ’til now", "vault_lines": ["To keep it real, I wasn't really gangster 'til now"], "meaning": "Drake admits that he’s never really been a gangster until now. Essentially saying the money and fame made him change", "annotation_id": "29834354", "url": "https://genius.com/29834354", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Start adoptin’ all my brothers’ beefs like a orphan I was still on roads when it’s pourin’ and stormin’ This shit is not rewardin’, I do not feel important", "vault_lines": ["Start adoptin' all my brothers' beefs like a orphan", "I was still on roads when it's pourin' and stormin'", "This shit is not rewardin', I do not feel important", "I was livin' on a cloud"], "meaning": "On his way to fame and success Drake didn’t have an easy or fun ride. He talks about his bros and how he had their back, their enemies or beefs he took like they were his own. Before the money Drake didn’t like not seeing immediate results", "annotation_id": "29834314", "url": "https://genius.com/29834314", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Now these niggas don’t remember", "vault_lines": ["Now these niggas don't remember"], "meaning": "Drake is poking fun at/calling out his haters with this line. With all his notoriety and success now, people seem to “forget” where he came from and how he started", "annotation_id": "29832802", "url": "https://genius.com/29832802", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I was tryna get a portion", "vault_lines": ["TT was headed to the league, I was sleepin' up at Metalworks tryna plant a seed", "I was tryna get a portion", "I was in the cold, windows froze, I was sleepin' in the whip tryna get a fucking grip"], "meaning": "Tryna get a portion, as in getting paid, also interpreted as “Get a Porsche in”, since Porsches are considered high class cars.", "annotation_id": "29827434", "url": "https://genius.com/29827434", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "This don’t feel like home anymore It’s just wall, doors, and floors That only I can afford Remember when it used to mean more, mean more I got money in the top drawer That ain’t much, but we not poor You’re mine and I’m yours", "vault_lines": ["This don't feel like home anymore", "It's just wall, doors, and floors", "That only I can afford", "Remember when it used to mean more, mean more", "I got money in the top drawer", "That ain't much, but we not poor", "You're mine and I'm yours"], "meaning": "In simpler terms, Drake is rapping about how he’s used to being wealthy. So wealthy in fact, that his estate -well worth over $100 million dollars- known as “The Embassy”, doesn’t feel like his home anymore.\n\nBefore obtaining status, he “remembers when it used to mean more” to him because he was constantly grinding and trying to make it big. Much like how this whole entire song is about him reminiscing on his past. Now that’s he’s achieved astronomical success, material things -like his own house, the walls, doors, floors, etc.- aren’t intangible anymore because he can afford them.", "annotation_id": "29783856", "url": "https://genius.com/29783856", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Try and find an inch of the city I ain’t stepped in If it’s war, I’ll lay my life down for the set then", "vault_lines": ["Try and find an inch of the city I ain't stepped in", "If it's war, I'll lay my life down for the set then"], "meaning": "In these lines, Drake alludes to the fact that he’s been to mostly everywhere there is to go in his hometown of Toronto. He’ll do so by risking his own life, even if it means dying for his “set” (gang or family).", "annotation_id": "29765601", "url": "https://genius.com/29765601", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Who fillin’ up the piggy bank? Who bringin’ home the bacon?", "vault_lines": ["Who fillin' up the piggy bank? Who bringin' home the bacon?"], "meaning": "Drake speaks a double entender about filling up the piggy bank and bringing home the bacon meaning he’s the breadwinner.", "annotation_id": "29758395", "url": "https://genius.com/29758395", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I know Neeks remember, I know Jay remember,", "vault_lines": ["I know Neeks remember, I know Jay remember,"], "meaning": "Neeks is also known as Niko. Niko met Drake back in the Degrassi days, and the two have been great friends ever since. Drake calls Niko his “closest confidant” in the Thank Me Later album credits.\n\n Neeks got the weed, Hush got a gun, CJ got my credit card and a lot of ones.", "annotation_id": "29746215", "url": "https://genius.com/29746215", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Like a broke couple we on one accord, for sure", "vault_lines": ["Like a broke couple we on one accord, for sure"], "meaning": "Referencing how a broke couple would share the same car. Ironically, sometimes it just may be an Honda Accord. On one accord would mean they compromise with each other. Also could be heard as “Own one Accord”\n\nAnother possible word play might be with the way that this line sounds like “We don’t wanna court, for sure”, alluding to the likelihood a couple with financial problems would end up breaking up.", "annotation_id": "29742582", "url": "https://genius.com/29742582", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I wanna see some scratch from the shit that I’m recordin’", "vault_lines": ["I wanna see some scratch from the shit that I'm recordin'"], "meaning": "Drake delivers a play on words, expressing his desire to receive some scratch, as in money, from the songs he is recording.\n\nScratching is also a technique used by DJ’s or turntablist to move a vinyl record back and forth on a turntable to produce percussive or rhythmic sound.", "annotation_id": "29741643", "url": "https://genius.com/29741643", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "My cousin Ryan fronted me a pound and I couldn’t even flip it, had to bring it back around", "vault_lines": ["My cousin Ryan fronted me a pound and I couldn't even flip it, had to bring it back around"], "meaning": "Drake reminisces on the time his cousin, Ryan Silverstein (AKA “OVO Ryan”), gave him a pound of presumably weed on consignment. He wasn’t able to sell it so he had to return said pound back to him.\n\n\n\nRyan has also been a long standing member of the OVO crew, and has made several cameos in Drake’s videos, most notably appearing in the skit in the “Started From The Bottom” video\n\nhttps://youtu.be/RubBzkZzpUA?si=CeOL4uEeS4YAOrDw&t=95", "annotation_id": "29741543", "url": "https://genius.com/29741543", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I remember fightin’ over Monica with Jason I remember only knowin’ Andre and Jason I remember buyin’ all the iceberg from Jason That’s three different Jasons, my life like The Matrix", "vault_lines": ["I remember fightin' over Monica with Jason", "I remember only knowin' Andre and Jason", "I remember buyin' all the iceberg from Jason", "That's three different Jasons, my life like The Matrix"], "meaning": "Drake likens his life to the The Matrix, a film depicting intertwined and alternate realities.\n\nJason Lock is the commander of Zion’s defense system in The Matrix. The recurrent mention of Jason represent Drake’s interactions with authority figures throughout his career, symbolizing negotiations within the industry. Drake is alluding to the film’s tension between Morpheus and Jason over Niobe, Morpheus’ former captain of his hovercraft: the Nebuchadnezzar, paralleling his own relationship conflicts with Jason concerning the unknown Monica.\n\nEach Jason symbolizes distinct phases in Drake’s life, representing unique ‘realities’ through which he has navigated, analogous to the three disparate realities explored in The Matrix: Paradise, Nightmare, and Modern.\n\nDrake is subtly likening himself here with the Merovingian, a character known for his ability to navigate, survive, and prosper through these different iterations of the simulated reality. In the same way, Drake is highlighting his own aptitude in artfully navigating through the ‘systems’ of the music industry. He has experienced distinct phases of his career, each presenting its own trials and triumphs, yet has managed to adapt, ensuring his sustained success and influence through various changes, challenges, and proverbial ‘reboots’ in the industry.\n\nDrake could also be saying that his life feels like a simulation and doesn’t feel real to him.\n\nWhen Drake mentions he got his iceberg from Jason he is referring to the owner, Jason Arasheben, the owner of the jewelry company, “Jason of Beverly Hills.”\n\nIceberg is also a luxury fashion brand that became notably synonymous with American hip-hop culture in the late ‘90s and early 2000s, thanks in part to endorsements from prominent artists like Jay-Z.", "annotation_id": "29741530", "url": "https://genius.com/29741530", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I remember pullin’ up at TAO, seein’ Quincy Jones, nigga talkin’ to me like he proud", "vault_lines": ["I remember pullin' up at TAO, seein' Quincy Jones, nigga talkin' to me like he proud"], "meaning": "TAO is a restaurant and nightclub chain, which is known for its celebrity clientele and its high-energy atmosphere.\n\n\nQuincy Jones is an American record producer, singer, songwriter, arranger, composer, and film and television producer. He is considered one of the most influential figures in the history of music.", "annotation_id": "29741502", "url": "https://genius.com/29741502", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Chubbs’ll make them boys remember", "vault_lines": ["Chubbs'll make them boys remember"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake shouts out his longtime friend and bodyguard Chubbs.\n\nDrake has mentioned Chubbs several times on his tracks previously. On War Drake raps,\n\nFeds wanna tap up man and wire up man like Chubbs did Detail", "annotation_id": "29737242", "url": "https://genius.com/29737242", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "(I-I-I—)", "vault_lines": ["I know Neeks remember, I know Jay remember,", "Fuck a spread, we was splittin' chicken tenders (I-I-I—)", "TT was headed to the league, I was sleepin' up at Metalworks tryna plant a seed", "Rollin' through the city, gettin' beats, always peepin' out the streets 'cause I was beefin' with the bleeks", "I was tryna get a portion", "I was still on roads when it's pourin' and stormin'", "This shit is not rewardin', I do not feel important", "I wanna see some scratch from the shit that I'm recordin'", "In Memphis smokin' Blacks, tryna act like I enjoy them (I-I-I—)", "I was in the cold, windows froze, I was sleepin' in the whip tryna get a fucking grip", "Crib I got now make me think of when I had to put some money on a mortgage", "That only I can afford", "I got money in the top drawer", "I remember pullin' up at TAO, seein' Quincy Jones, nigga talkin' to me like he proud", "I remember Mike designed the owl, then he sued the fucking guys, and he duckin' until now", "My cousin Ryan fronted me a pound and I couldn't even flip it, had to bring it back around", "I remember printin' up our T-shirts with Kai", "I would pick them shits up, nigga wouldn't even smile", "To keep it real, I wasn't really gangster 'til now", "I was livin' on a cloud", ", I was quiet as a mouse", "I was in the club gettin' lost in the crowd", "Wasn't doin' what I wanted to, I'd do what was allowed", "I remember fightin' over Monica with Jason", "I remember only knowin' Andre and Jason", "I remember buyin' all the iceberg from Jason", "Fuck 'em, I remember", "How could I forget? How could I forget?", "I was on a Greyhound way before the jet", "Way before they had a fuckin' DM, I was whippin' in the Ac' like that shit was European", "Buffalo, New York was like the furthest I could get", "Try and find an inch of the city I ain't stepped in", "And I put that shit on Mazin and that nigga was my best friend", "And I put that shit on Johnny Bling, that nigga was my best friend", "And I put that shit on Richard, that nigga was my best friend", "I remember drivin' Holland Tunnel"], "meaning": "The tag of popular producer group Working on Dying. The group-member that produced this song is BNYX.\nIt’s not the first time that the group’s producer tag is on a Drake’s track, since Oogie Mane and FaxOnly, which are group members, produced Drake’s 2018 single, “I’m Upset” and Drake’s 2021 track “Pipe Down”, respectively.", "annotation_id": "29734157", "url": "https://genius.com/29734157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I remember printin’ up our T-shirts with Kai", "vault_lines": ["I remember printin' up our T-shirts with Kai"], "meaning": "Potentially Referring to his close friend Kai Bent Lee, who runs a successful clothing brand. Kai is often seen in Drakes circle and a valued member of the team.", "annotation_id": "29733964", "url": "https://genius.com/29733964", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "And I put that shit on Johnny Bling, that nigga was my best friend", "vault_lines": ["And I put that shit on Mazin and that nigga was my best friend", "And I put that shit on Johnny Bling, that nigga was my best friend", "And I put that shit on Richard, that nigga was my best friend"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out his long-time friend, Sean “Johnny Bling” Allen.\n\n\n\nSean influenced Drake early in his life/career as mentioned on “Know Yourself”:\nhttps://genius.com/4889239", "annotation_id": "29733537", "url": "https://genius.com/29733537", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Way before they had a fuckin’ DM, I was whippin’ in the Ac’ like that shit was European", "vault_lines": ["I was on a Greyhound way before the jet", "Way before they had a fuckin' DM, I was whippin' in the Ac' like that shit was European"], "meaning": "Drizzy continues to recall times in the past when he had less fortune, driving his Acura.\nAlthough, Acura’s are known to be more common and affordable vehicles, known for their reliability. Drake assures listeners he was driving this car as if it was a luxury European car like what he is more used to today.\n\nDrake has mentioned his vehicle of the past before on “Worst Behavior,” as well:\nhttps://genius.com/2213280", "annotation_id": "29733497", "url": "https://genius.com/29733497", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I was in the cold, windows froze, I was sleepin’ in the whip tryna get a fucking grip I remember Didn’t have no hoes tryna go, all these rappers lil’ broed me, fuck, fuck Fuck ’em, I remember", "vault_lines": ["I was tryna get a portion", "I was in the cold, windows froze, I was sleepin' in the whip tryna get a fucking grip", "Didn't have no hoes tryna go, all these rappers lil' broed me, fuck, fuck", "Fuck 'em, I remember"], "meaning": "Drake ruminates on past times when he was much less fortunate, mentioning sleeping in his car and being “lil bro’d” by rappers who were ahead of him at the time.\n\nHe reiterates the fact that he has not forgotten these times and continues to hold it against those who doubted him.\n\nThese lyrics are very reminiscent of  “Worst Behavior”:\n\nThey used to never want to hear us\nRemember? Motherfucker never loved us\nRemember? Motherfucker\nRemember? Motherfucker never loved us", "annotation_id": "29733479", "url": "https://genius.com/29733479", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I’m tryna keep it humble, I’m tryna keep it gracious Who give a fuck Michelle Obama put you on her playlist? Then we never hear from you again like you was taken", "vault_lines": ["I'm tryna keep it humble, I'm tryna keep it gracious", "Who give a fuck Michelle Obama put you on her playlist?", "Then we never hear from you again like you was taken"], "meaning": "Drake takes aim at common adversary Kendrick Lamar, possibly as a response to the original version of “ELEMENT.”, which leaked on September 7th, where K-Dot calls him out:\n\nDrake, your Meek Mill beef might get you gassed up\nBut I’m a whole ‘nother beast, I’d really fuck you up\n\nFirst referencing his hit song HUMBLE., Drake then laments the fact that Kendrick was included on Michelle Obama’s Spotify playlist inspired by the first season of her podcast while Drake himself was left out. Kendrick also made an appearance on the former FLOTUS' 2020 workout playlist while Drake was once again left out.\n\nDrake dismisses the praise for Kendrick, and calls him out for rarely dropping music and staying out of the limelight, echoing his statements from a July 6th concert in Chicago:\n\nI look around at all these faces. I know it’s summertime, I gotta give you shit! I don’t know about these guys that go away for three, four, five years and wanna chill out and all that shit. That’s not me.\n\nDrake dropped 3 albums (Scorpion, Dark Lane Demo Tapes, & Certified Lover Boy) during the 5 year hiatus between DAMN. and Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers, and has dropped 3 more since (Honestly, Nevermind, Her Loss, & For All The Dogs).", "annotation_id": "29732246", "url": "https://genius.com/29732246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "And I put that shit on Mazin and that nigga was my best friend", "vault_lines": ["And I put that shit on Mazin and that nigga was my best friend", "And I put that shit on Johnny Bling, that nigga was my best friend", "And I put that shit on Richard, that nigga was my best friend"], "meaning": "Mazin , One of his oldest associates , who he has referred to as his best friend multiple times.", "annotation_id": "29731676", "url": "https://genius.com/29731676", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Whole time, Courtne Smith was keepin’ me in check", "vault_lines": ["Whole time, Courtne Smith was keepin' me in check"], "meaning": "Courtne Smith is a Canadian-American entrepreneur, who began her career as Drake’s personal assistant. She worked in Drake’s management team for over a decade. Smith and Drake grew up together in Toronto, Ontario and were best friends since they were kids.", "annotation_id": "29731561", "url": "https://genius.com/29731561", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "3 AM at Y2, runnin’ off endorphins", "vault_lines": ["3 AM at Y2, runnin' off endorphins"], "meaning": "Wah Too, located in Toronto’s downtown core, is a seafood restaurant. The restaurant is/was known as a spot to eat after the club.", "annotation_id": "29731500", "url": "https://genius.com/29731500", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "‘Member I was sleepin’ in the basement, watchin’ Tigger in the basement, I was tryna get a placement", "vault_lines": ["I was tryna get a portion", "I was in the cold, windows froze, I was sleepin' in the whip tryna get a fucking grip"], "meaning": "“Rap City: Tha Basement” was the retitled name that was given to the 1989 music TV show – Rap City.\nThe program showcased hip hop music videos, and features interviews with and freestyles from popular rappers.\n\nThe host of the “Tha Basement” version was Darian “Big Tigger” Morgan.\n\nDrake was back home dreaming of getting placed on the show so his career can take off.", "annotation_id": "29731455", "url": "https://genius.com/29731455", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I remember drivin’ Holland Tunnel", "vault_lines": ["I remember drivin' Holland Tunnel"], "meaning": "The Holland Tunnel is a tunnel under the Hudson River that connects Hudson Square & Lower Manhattan in New York City to Jersey City, New Jersey.", "annotation_id": "29731424", "url": "https://genius.com/29731424", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I was on a Greyhound way before the jet Buffalo, New York was like the furthest I could get", "vault_lines": ["I was tryna get a portion", "I was livin' on a cloud", "I was on a Greyhound way before the jet", "Buffalo, New York was like the furthest I could get"], "meaning": "Greyhound is a bus service that is known to be used by travelers on a budget. Drake is saying that he used to use Greyhound before he could afford to fly everywhere.\n\nDrake was likely taking the Greyhound from Toronto Union Station to Buffalo, New York, to enter the United States.", "annotation_id": "29729698", "url": "https://genius.com/29729698", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "At the Walden Galleria tryna make the money stretch", "vault_lines": ["At the Walden Galleria tryna make the money stretch"], "meaning": "Before the boom in Canada’s commerce industry, shopping options in Toronto were heavily limited. During the 90s and early 2000s, it was common to commute from Toronto across the border into Buffalo, NY’s Walden Galleria to shop for clothes and shoes.", "annotation_id": "29729618", "url": "https://genius.com/29729618", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Rollin’ through the city, gettin’ beats, always peepin’ out the streets ‘cause I was beefin’ with the bleeks", "vault_lines": ["Rollin' through the city, gettin' beats, always peepin' out the streets 'cause I was beefin' with the bleeks"], "meaning": "In 2009, Drake was set up and robbed at gunpoint while on a date. The robbers were from Toronto’s Bleeker neighborhood, and a beef ignited involving BLKZ members as a result. This conflict allegedly involved slain Toronto rapper Sizzlac and Rolexx Homi as well.\n\nDrake already rapped about this robbery on his songs “The Resistance” and “From Florida With Love”:\n\n\nMaybe it was the fast paced switch up\nOr the two guns in my face during the stick up\nMaybe ‘cause a girl I thought I trusted\nWas who set the whole shit up\n- The Resistance\n\nWent and got a chain for me, I had to give it up (Yeah)\nNiggas had they pistols loaded pointed at my truck, ayy\nAnd you know that lesson stuck (Right)\nFrom that day I never touched the road without a (Plugg)\nAyy, from that day I never saw the point in talkin' tough (Nah)\nHasn’t happened since, I guess you niggas know what’s up (Yup)\n- From Florida With Love", "annotation_id": "29729429", "url": "https://genius.com/29729429", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "B-Town with Candy, I’m tryna be her boyfriend", "vault_lines": ["B-Town with Candy, I'm tryna be her boyfriend"], "meaning": "Brampton, or The B-Town, is a city in the Greater Toronto Area that is a little over an hour North-West from downtown Toronto.\n\nDrake is reminiscing on trips from Toronto to Brampton for the girl he was trying to date at the time, Candy.", "annotation_id": "29729232", "url": "https://genius.com/29729232", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Student Price Card and a Yorkdale connect", "vault_lines": ["Student Price Card and a Yorkdale connect"], "meaning": "The Student Price Card, also known as the SPC Card, is a student loyalty discount program in Canada offering discounts and deals on items such as fashion, food, shoes, and more.\n\nYorkdale Shopping Centre is one of the biggest and most popular malls in Toronto, where students often use their SPC card for discounts.", "annotation_id": "29729190", "url": "https://genius.com/29729190", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "I remember Mike designed the owl, then he sued the fucking guys, and he duckin’ until now", "vault_lines": ["I remember Mike designed the owl, then he sued the fucking guys, and he duckin' until now"], "meaning": "Michael Raphael is a jewelry designer who designed Drake a diamond-studded platinum necklace.\nRaphael sued Drake on 2013 for consulting, travel, and shopping expenses and for the fact that Drake illegally copied the OVO owl chain that he for him.", "annotation_id": "29729167", "url": "https://genius.com/29729167", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "TT was headed to the league, I was sleepin’ up at Metalworks tryna plant a seed", "vault_lines": ["TT was headed to the league, I was sleepin' up at Metalworks tryna plant a seed", "I was tryna get a portion"], "meaning": "While Tristan Thompson was headed to the NBA, Drake was putting in late nights at Metalworks Studios  trying to get his big break.", "annotation_id": "29729136", "url": "https://genius.com/29729136", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Days in the East with the bros, watchin’ Tristan and Devoe while they runnin’ up the score", "vault_lines": ["Days in the East with the bros, watchin' Tristan and Devoe while they runnin' up the score"], "meaning": "Tristan Thompson and Devoe Joseph were high school basketball phenoms in Canada.\n\nDrake also references the time he spent on Toronto’s Eastside, which he has previously mentioned multiple times throughout his career on tracks such as “The Motion”, “Days In The East” and “BackOutsideBoyz”.", "annotation_id": "29729126", "url": "https://genius.com/29729126", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Dr. Dre’ll send a nigga home, that took patience", "vault_lines": ["Dr. Dre'll send a nigga home, that took patience"], "meaning": "There’s a wordplay with the words patience and patients, that sounds similarly. Patients are medical doctors' clients, making it double entendre with Dr. Dre’s name.\n\n19-year old Drake worked for Dr. Dre as a songwriter in 2005, although no usable songs materialized from this opportunity.\nLil Wayne also announced that both Drake and himself had written for Detox, an unreleased Dr Dre album, on the song Ransom, a Drake and Wayne collaboration from 2008.\nhttps://genius.com/29749322", "annotation_id": "29726715", "url": "https://genius.com/29726715", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Maurice François Girbaud what I’m sportin’", "vault_lines": ["Maurice François Girbaud what I'm sportin'"], "meaning": "Drake is wearing French clothing company Marithé et François Girbaud, which was very popular in the 90’s.\n\nDrake has name-dropped the brand before when reminiscing the past on his song “Know Yourself”:\n\nHad a job sellin' Girbaud jeans", "annotation_id": "29725843", "url": "https://genius.com/29725843", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Who the CEO of Universal? They mistaken ‘Cause Google sayin’ Lucian, but that just doesn’t make sense", "vault_lines": ["Who the CEO of Universal? They mistaken", "'Cause Google sayin' Lucian, but that just doesn't make sense"], "meaning": "Sir Lucian Grainge is the Chairman and CEO of Universal Music Group. This isn’t the first time that Drake name-drops him — on Rick Ross‘ April 2012 song, “Stay Schemin’,” Drizzy raps:\n\nTell Lucian I said ‘fuck it,’ I’m tearin' holes in my budget\n\nWhen asked about the line at the Universal Grammy afterparty in January 2020, Lucian said:\n\nIf ever Drake or whenever Drake calls up and says he needs more money for his project, I give it to him […] because he’s the greatest\n\nChampagne Papi also mentions Lucian on Migos' June 2021 song “Having Our Way”:\n\nShit done changed, billionaires talk to me different when they see my paystub from Lucian Grainge", "annotation_id": "29725365", "url": "https://genius.com/29725365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Even got on 106 & Park with Replacement", "vault_lines": ["Even got on 106 & Park with Replacement"], "meaning": "Drake’s 2007 single “Replacement Girl” from his mixtape Comeback Season was played on the TV show “106 & Park” around the time of its release.\nhttps://mixtapes.tv/drakes-first-time-bets-106-park/", "annotation_id": "29725319", "url": "https://genius.com/29725319", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "line": "Four GRAMMY’s to my name, a hundred nominations Esperanza Spalding was gettin’ all the praises", "vault_lines": ["Four GRAMMY's to my name, a hundred nominations", "Esperanza Spalding was gettin' all the praises"], "meaning": "As of the song’s release, Drake has five Grammy wins and fifty-one nominations. Among the losses, Drizzy was nominated for Best New Artist at the 53rd Annual Grammy Awards in 2011 but lost to American jazz artist Esperanza Spalding. Afterwards, Kanye West took to Twitter:\n\nDamn… I can’t find the words to explain how I feel about the best new artist award…Don’t wanna say the wrong thing.\n\nOn Big Sean’s January 2015 Ye-assisted song “Blessings,” Drake raps:\n\nI could give two fucks ‘bout where the Grammys go\nI just gave out Grammys on my Instagram\n\nDrake also withdrew two nominations from the 2022 Grammys and criticized Grammys for nominating his July 2015 hit “Hotline Bling” as a rap song.", "annotation_id": "29725292", "url": "https://genius.com/29725292", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Away From Home", "status": "done", "targets": 46, "clicked": 46, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-away-from-home-lyrics", "genius_title": "Away From Home"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "I get more satisfaction outta goin’ at your head And seein’ all of you die And I seen a lot of you die", "vault_lines": ["I get more satisfaction outta goin' at your head", "And seein' all of you die", "And I seen a lot of you die"], "meaning": "Here, Drake is not literally talking about killing people, but rather sending diss tracks and shots at competition using his music.\n\nHe has done this many times before to artists including Meek Mill on the 2015 diss tracks Charged Up and Back To Back.\n\nThere are also many subliminal shots on More Life, including on “Portland”, “Can’t Have Everything”, “Lose You”, “Ice Melts” and “Do Not Disturb.”", "annotation_id": "11698957", "url": "https://genius.com/11698957", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Is it the strength of your feelings Overthrowing your pain? You’ll see new heights you’ll be reaching And is it today that you will find your new release? And in your wake, ripple your sweet fate", "vault_lines": ["Is it the strength of your feelings", "You'll see new heights you’ll be reaching", "And is it today that you will find your new release?", "And in your wake, ripple your sweet fate"], "meaning": "These lyrics are sampled from Australian band  Hiatus Kaiyote’s 2015 song “Building a Ladder.”\n\nWhen More Life dropped, Hiatus Kaiyote tweeted about the sample:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/HiatusKaiyote/status/843240422289956865\n\nNai Palm also commented on it in a Billboard interview:\n\nHe’s been a fan of our sh– for a while. It’s kind of beautiful.\n\nDrake also showed love by uploading an Instagram video of him jamming to the song in a limo:\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BR1x8uaDUKI/", "annotation_id": "11574460", "url": "https://genius.com/11574460", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Lot of niggas goin’ bad on me Please, one at a time", "vault_lines": ["Lot of niggas goin' bad on me", "Please, one at a time"], "meaning": "A lot of people within the industry that have been friends with Drake have started to turn on him. In a 2017 interview with DJ Semtex, Drake said that he has realized that it’s part of his career to get turned on and he still doesn’t know why.\n\nThe list of people that have had words to say about Drake aren’t exactly low-profile: Meek Mill, JAY Z, Kid Cudi, Tory Lanez, Joe Budden, and Diddy.", "annotation_id": "11545729", "url": "https://genius.com/11545729", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "I start my day slow Silk pajamas when I wake though", "vault_lines": ["I start my day slow", "Silk pajamas when I wake though"], "meaning": "Silk pajamas are typically reserved for the rich and famous. Given Drake’s net worth of $60 million, it’s quite possible that the 6 God himself enjoys sleeping in the finest of silks.\n\nIn the Views photobook, you can see Drake in silk pajamas.", "annotation_id": "11544556", "url": "https://genius.com/11544556", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "I’m the troublemaker in the neighborhood Far as troublemakin’ goes, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I'm the troublemaker in the neighborhood", "Far as troublemakin' goes, yeah"], "meaning": "Parties in the Hollywood Hills have been increasing with new stars making their way out west. While Drizzy’s off in the Hidden Hills, even he admits that he’s the biggest troublemaker in his area, going as far as buying his neighbor’s house after they complained about him.\n\nHe also referenced this purchase on “Where Ya At” with Future.", "annotation_id": "11544326", "url": "https://genius.com/11544326", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Women I like was ignorin’ me Now they like, “Aren’t you adorable?” I know the question rhetorical", "vault_lines": ["Women I like was ignorin' me", "Now they like, \"Aren't you adorable?\"", "I know the question rhetorical"], "meaning": "Comparing his life now to those tour days with Lil Wayne, the women have come full circle. The same women that were all over Weezy, are the same ones loving Drake.\n\nThey call him “adorable” and he knows he doesn’t have to respond because they’ll still be after him anyways.", "annotation_id": "11544298", "url": "https://genius.com/11544298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Miraval to the face though", "vault_lines": ["Miraval to the face though"], "meaning": "Château Miraval is a French vineyard that Drake seems to be sampling wines from.\n\nThe vineyard is famous for two reasons—one, their Pink Floyd rosè (pictured below) is widely heralded as one of the best rosès in the world,\n and two—Brad Pitt and Angelina Jolie famously bought the vineyard in 2008 and got married on-site in 2012. Brangelina didn’t last, but Miraval is still a big time ting, ay!", "annotation_id": "11543437", "url": "https://genius.com/11543437", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Me and Gibbo was about that Eatin’ Applebee’s and Outback", "vault_lines": ["Me and Gibbo was about that", "Eatin' Applebee's and Outback"], "meaning": "Drake maybe referring to his close friend/road manager CJ Gibson.  ‘Gibbo’ has been there for Drake since the beginning of his career touring and dining at Grill & Bar restaurants like Applebee’s & Outback.", "annotation_id": "11543038", "url": "https://genius.com/11543038", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Southwest, no first class", "vault_lines": ["Southwest, no first class"], "meaning": "Unlike many other airlines, Southwest does not have first class seating. Instead, it competes in the airline industry by offering cheaper fares nationwide. When Drake was an up-and-coming rapper, he flew Southwest to cut costs so he could perform shows in different cities.", "annotation_id": "11537401", "url": "https://genius.com/11537401", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Hilton rooms, gotta double up Writin’ our name on a double cup", "vault_lines": ["Hilton rooms, gotta double up", "Writin’ our name on a double cup"], "meaning": "Drake reminisces on his pre-fame tour life. He used to have to share a Hilton Hotel room to cut costs, but he’s so wealthy now that it’s no longer a concern.\n\n“Double cup” refers to lean—it’s served in two styrofoam cups to the keep the drink cold.", "annotation_id": "11536646", "url": "https://genius.com/11536646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Y’all keep playin’ with your nose, yeah You get high and do the most, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Y'all keep playin' with your nose, yeah", "You get high and do the most, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake pokes fun at people who waste their time on drugs instead of putting it into their music. “Playing with your nose” alludes to snorting cocaine, a stimulant that boosts self-esteem and confidence.\n\n\n\nThis couplet, and the following lyrics, may also be a shot at Kid Cudi, who Drake threw shots at earlier in the song. Cudi has a history of drug problems. It’s also worth noting that Drake also repeats “kid” three times in this verse.", "annotation_id": "11535350", "url": "https://genius.com/11535350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Niggas moves so waste Please come outside the house and show yourself So I can say it to your face", "vault_lines": ["Miraval to the face though", "Niggas moves so waste", "Please come outside the house and show yourself", "So I can say it to your face"], "meaning": "This line may be a subliminal diss directed at Kid Cudi. After Drake dissed his mental health on “Two Birds, One Stone,” Cudi directly tweeted Drake, requesting that he “say it to his face”:", "annotation_id": "11535138", "url": "https://genius.com/11535138", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "I wanna move to Dubai So I don’t never have to kick it with none of you guys", "vault_lines": ["I wanna move to Dubai", "So I don't never have to kick it with none of you guys"], "meaning": "Drake has made multiple visits to  Dubai in 2015/2016 and has been welcomed by Dubai’s A list like HH Shiekh Mansoor, son of vice president and prime minister of the United Arab Emirates and Mohammed K AL Habtoor, CEO of Al Habtoor Group, one of the biggest business tycoons in the region.", "annotation_id": "11533824", "url": "https://genius.com/11533824", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "[Outro: Baka Not Nice] Baka Yeah, we outchea Ya dun know, eh? It’s a OVO ting, eh? Ya dun know, eh? It’s a East Side ting, eh? Ya dun know, eh? More life", "vault_lines": ["More life, more everything", "Ya dun know, eh?", "It's a OVO ting, eh?", "It's a East Side ting, eh?"], "meaning": "Baka is Drake’s long-time friend.  His criminal history includes offenses such as human trafficking and assault. Despite this, Drake has shouted him out multiple times, most notably on “Know Yourself” and “No Shopping.”\n\nBaka is free now, which Drake celebrated in an Instagram post:\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/1MTDldjQPk/\n\nWhile Baka’s exact role within OVO is unknown, he has performed similar outros on other Drake songs, including “From Time” from 2013’s Nothing Was the Same.\n\nOn the opening lines of the following track, “No Long Talk,” Drake informs the listener that these lines were Baka’s.\n\n‘Ya dun know’ is a slang term derivative of Jamaican Patois meaning ‘you already know’.", "annotation_id": "11532577", "url": "https://genius.com/11532577", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "But we all so spoiled now More life, more everything", "vault_lines": ["But we all so spoiled now", "More life, more everything"], "meaning": "Drake’s life has been one big come up since he burst into the scene during the 2009’s. His success resulted in many improvements and benefits to his life and career. Some of his spoils included:\n\n\nAn $8 million dollar mansion\nA vast collection of exotic cars\nA net worth of over $100 million\n4 RIAA-certified platinum albums\n10 Billion Spotify plays\n400 consecutive weeks on the Billboard Top 100\nHis own record label, OVO Sound\n\n\n\nThe line could also be a reference to a Seinfeld episode in which a flight attendant has a conversation with Seinfeld.", "annotation_id": "11532238", "url": "https://genius.com/11532238", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Boi-1da got the bounce back", "vault_lines": ["Boi-1da got the bounce back"], "meaning": "Boi-1da produced this song along with Allen Ritter. Drake and Boi-1da have been churning out bangers for almost a decade, including hits like “Best I Ever Had,” “Forever,” “Headlines,” and “0 to 100 / The Catch Up.”\n\n“Got the bounce back” probably refers to Boi-1a’s drums—they slap!", "annotation_id": "11532185", "url": "https://genius.com/11532185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "We ain’t even have a tour bus Girls wouldn’t even think of recordin’ me I fall asleep in sororities I had some different priorities Weezy had all the authority", "vault_lines": ["We ain’t even have a tour bus", "Girls wouldn't even think of recordin’ me", "I fall asleep in sororities", "I had some different priorities", "Weezy had all the authority"], "meaning": "Drake reminisces about the “I Am Music Tour,” which he hopped on right after meeting Lil Wayne in 2008.\n\nThe tour took place after Wayne dropped his classic Tha Carter III album; so he most certainly had all the authority and got with all the women. Drake, who hadn’t broken through the mainstream yet, was largely overlooked at the time. He often recalls sleeping on the floor of the tour bus.\n\nNow, Drake wouldn’t dare to fall asleep in a sorority for fears that someone would film his sexual encounters and use them against him.", "annotation_id": "11531977", "url": "https://genius.com/11531977", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "House party up the road, yeah I’m not Kid ’n Play This kid doesn’t play about the flow, yeah", "vault_lines": ["House party up the road, yeah", "I'm not Kid 'n Play", "This kid doesn't play about the flow, yeah"], "meaning": "This is a nod to the 1990 classic film House Party starring Kid ‘n Play.\n\n\n\nThis could be a jab at up and coming Florida rapper XXXTENTACION. After X heard “KMT,” he accused Drake of stealing his flow from “Look At Me,” but Drake denied it in an interview with DJ Semtex.\n\nX heard the interview and tweeted:", "annotation_id": "11531854", "url": "https://genius.com/11531854", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "And more chune for your headtop So watch how you speak on my name, you know?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a soundbite from Drake’s speech during the 2016 American Music Awards where he won “Best Rap/Hip Hop Artist” nominee. During the build up to the release of More Life, it became a slogan for the project. It can also be heard at the end of the Spotify trailer for More Life:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Spotify/status/842489889203482624\n\n“Chune” is the Patois pronunciation of “tune,” while “head top” means “crown of the head.”", "annotation_id": "11531740", "url": "https://genius.com/11531740", "state": "accepted", "votes": 231, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "I took the team plane from Oracle Mama never used to cook much Used to chef KD Now me and Chef, KD Bet on shots for twenty G’s", "vault_lines": ["I took the team plane from Oracle", "Mama never used to cook much", "Used to chef KD", "Now me and Chef, KD", "Bet on shots for twenty G's"], "meaning": "Drake juxtaposes his humble beginnings of eating Kraft Dinner’s microwaveable macaroni and cheese with his new reality, which includes rides on the Golden State Warriors team plane (Oracle Arena is their home court) and $20,000 bets on jump shot competitions with professional basketball players Kevin Durant (KD) and Steph Curry (Chef, as in Chef Curry). Drake’s friends with them, and even trash talked them during a November 2016 game.\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake’s referred to the Golden State Warriors NBA team. He’s name-dropped the team or its players on a number of tracks, including “0-100/The Catch Up” and “Summer Sixteen.”\n\nFollowing the “0-100/The Catch Up” name drop, Steph Curry made a “Chef Curry” video with his wife Ayesha Curry:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YjU7BYcJwIw\n\nIn 2020, both Warriors coach Steve Kerr and Steph Curry confirmed Drake’s lyrics about taking the team plane.", "annotation_id": "11531645", "url": "https://genius.com/11531645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "I brought the game to its knees I make too much these days to ever say, “Poor me”", "vault_lines": ["I brought the game to its knees", "I make too much these days to ever say, \"Poor me\""], "meaning": "A common self-pity saying, “poor me,” doesn’t refer to a person’s wealth, but something unfortunate happening to them. Although it is commonly used sarcastically, Drake can’t even say it that way, as his net worth $60 million invalidates both meanings of the phrase.\n\nThis could also be a play on ‘pour’ and ‘poor,’ to show that Drake’s success allows him to pour himself his own drinks instead of asking for some from other people.", "annotation_id": "11531440", "url": "https://genius.com/11531440", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Yeah, I couldn’t get a bill paid You couldn’t buy the real thing I was stayin’ up at yo’ place Tryin’ to figure out the whole thing I saw people doin’ things Almost gave up on the music thing", "vault_lines": ["I saw people doin' things", "Almost gave up on the music thing", "Yeah, I couldn't get a bill paid", "You couldn't buy the real thing", "I was stayin’ up at yo' place", "Tryin' to figure out the whole thing"], "meaning": "Drake reminisces on life before fame. He wasn’t born into the life of a superstar; he had to work for the position he’s in today. “Started From the Bottom” explored his childhood in detail:\n\n I done kept it real from the jump\nLivin' at my momma’s house, we’d argue every month\nNigga, I was tryna get it on my own\nWorkin' all night, traffic on the way home\nAnd my uncle callin' me, like, “Where you at?\nI gave you the keys, told you bring it right back.”\n\nDrake also mentioned his humble beginnings at the first Paris show of his European “Boy Meets World” tour:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/brad_wolff/status/841033973727952898\n\nDiscussing the obstacles one had to overcome to earn their current status is a common theme in hip-hop, especially for Drake, as he’s shown on tracks like “All Me” and “How ‘Bout Now?”", "annotation_id": "11531428", "url": "https://genius.com/11531428", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Used to get paid for shows in front-door money Five, ten, twenties, hand sanitize after you count that", "vault_lines": ["Used to get paid for shows in front-door money", "Five, ten, twenties, hand sanitize after you count that"], "meaning": "Up and coming artists usually start off booking shows in small venues where they get paid commission from front door ticket sales. Patrons of these venues pay with small bills that get passed around by concert goers, cashiers—the excessive handling warrants some antibacterial action.\n\nThis is a stark contrast to the arenas Drake is currently selling out around the world and the large corporate checks he receives in return.", "annotation_id": "11531170", "url": "https://genius.com/11531170", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Must have never had your phone tapped All that yappin’ on the phone shit You must really love the road life All that never comin’ home shit", "vault_lines": ["Must have never had your phone tapped", "All that yappin' on the phone shit", "You must really love the road life", "All that never comin' home shit", "Weezy had all the authority"], "meaning": "This subliminal diss seems to be aimed at Tory Lanez. On Tory’s album, I Told You, there are skits throughout where Tory is talking on the phone. If this is in fact aimed at Lanez, which it seems to be, then he may be implying that Tory is a fake gangster, because real gangsters don’t do business on the phone as it is easy for the police to track. Telephone tapping is when somebody, typically the government, secretly listens in on phone or internet conversations.\n\nThe last two lines reference Tory’s non-stop touring (like for real look at these dates, he’s doing a show pretty much every night in Summer 2017). They are both from Toronto, so Drake may be implying that Lanez is staying on tour as a way to avoid Drake. Drake later disses Tory in other tracks on the “playlist” such as “Do Not Disturb.”", "annotation_id": "11531167", "url": "https://genius.com/11531167", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "I drunk text J-Lo Old number, so it bounce back", "vault_lines": ["I drunk text J-Lo", "Old number, so it bounce back"], "meaning": "Drake posted an Instagram photo of himself with Jennifer Lopez in December 2016, confirming they were romantically involved.\n\nThough he humblebrags about how casually he drunk texts supermodels and pop stars, the text message “bounced back” because she hasn’t kept him updated with her new number, indicating the end of the relationship.", "annotation_id": "11531149", "url": "https://genius.com/11531149", "state": "accepted", "votes": 210, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "How you let the kid fightin’ Ghost-writin’ rumors turn you to a ghost? Oh, you niggas got jokes", "vault_lines": ["How you let the kid fightin'", "Ghost-writin' rumors turn you to a ghost?", "Oh, you niggas got jokes"], "meaning": "“The Kid” is one of Drake’s many monikers. In 2015, Meek Mill accused him of using ghost-writers—the accusation later erupted into a full fledged beef.\n\nThe intensity of Drake’s “Back to Back,” combined with a lackluster response from Meek Mill, quickly turned public opinion against the Philadelphia rapper. In Drake’s eyes, the feud forever labeled Meek as a lesser rapper, turning him into a “ghost,” while Drake managed to ride the criticism and grew in popularity.", "annotation_id": "11530989", "url": "https://genius.com/11530989", "state": "accepted", "votes": 220, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Hidden Hills where I post", "vault_lines": ["Hidden Hills where I post", "Hidden Hills where I post, yeah"], "meaning": "Hidden Hills is a gated neighborhood in Los Angeles, California. Drake’s “YOLO Estate” is located there—it’s worth $20 million, and its amenities include a 28-seat movie theater, tennis and volleyball courts, a grotto equipped with flatscreen TVs, and a massive pool with an 80-foot water slide.\n\n\n\nThe Hidden Hills estate is located near Calabasas, where many stars live, including Drake’s longtime collaborator Kanye West.", "annotation_id": "11530927", "url": "https://genius.com/11530927", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "I didn’t listen to Hov on that old song When he told me, “Pay it no mind”", "vault_lines": ["I didn't listen to Hov on that old song", "When he told me, \"Pay it no mind\""], "meaning": "On their 2010 collab “Light Up,” Hov gave Drake direct advice on how to survive in the rap game:\n\nDrake, here’s how they gon' come at you\nWith silly rap feuds, trying to distract you\nIn disguise, in the form of a favor\nThe Barzini meet, watch for the traitors\n\nThis is also a reference to one of Jay Z’s “old” lines on his 2001 classic track “Heart of the City (Ain’t No Love),” on which he advises his detractors to just ignore him and his success:\n\nYoungins ice-grilling me; oh, you not feeling me?\nFine, it cost you nothing; pay me no mind\n\nDrake expresses here that he has ignored both pieces of Jigga’s advice, instead choosing to engage in “silly rap feuds,” such as his 2015 beef with Meek Mill and even a possible subliminal beef with Hov himself.", "annotation_id": "11530896", "url": "https://genius.com/11530896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 192, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Free smoke, free smoke, ayy Free smoke, free smoke, ayy Free smoke, free smoke, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Free smoke, free smoke, ayy", "Free smoke, free smoke"], "meaning": "A “free smoke” is a dare—Drake’s warning anyone thinking of testing him that if they do, he’ll crush them. The term likely originates from “smoke,” which is slang for killing someone with a gun, although in this case, it’s a metaphor for lyrical murder.\n\nDrake’s arrogance isn’t unwarranted—he’s one of the most popular artists of his generation and continues to fight off would be contenders such as Joe Budden, Pusha T, and most notably Meek Mill. Meek became the victim of Drake’s Grammy-nominated diss track “Back to Back” in July 2015, which only benefited Drake and increased his popularity.\n\nDrake used “free smoke” in this context before on his 2016 remix of Dave’s “Wanna Know”:\n\nFree smoke, anybody want it they can get it\nNo joke, call up landlord, nigga that’s bro", "annotation_id": "11529631", "url": "https://genius.com/11529631", "state": "accepted", "votes": 211, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "line": "Dom Rosé toast", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Dom Perignon Rosé is a brand of champagne that can cost over $400 a bottle.\n\n\n\nDrake’s preference for Rosé is an ongoing theme in his lyrics, as mentioned in “Marvin’s Room,” “Comeback Season,” and “Pop Rosé,” a 2008 collaboration with Trey Songz. On 2016’s “4pm in Calabasas,” he even said:\n\nMy summer diet is just Rosé and calamari", "annotation_id": "11528388", "url": "https://genius.com/11528388", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Smoke", "status": "done", "targets": 30, "clicked": 30, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-free-smoke-lyrics", "genius_title": "Free Smoke"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shut It Down", "line": "TMZ, Concrete Loop You shut that, shut that down, down Ice cream conversations, they all want the scoop You shut that, shut that down, down", "vault_lines": ["You shut that, shut that down, down", "Ice cream conversations, they all want the scoop", "You shut that thing down, you shut it down", "You shut it down, you shut it down", "You shut it down", "You shut it down, down, down, down, down", "Lay you down, down, down"], "meaning": "TMZ and Concrete Loop are both urban entertainment blog sites that were created in November 2005. However, Concrete Loop later shut down in 2014, with the owner saying “it was no longer [their] passion.”\n\nIce Cream Convos is another urban entertainment blog site and “the scoop” means gossip.", "annotation_id": "18479855", "url": "https://genius.com/18479855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shut It Down", "line": "Hey lady, step into my heart And let me put you in them Louboutins", "vault_lines": ["Hey lady, step into my heart", "And let me put you in them Louboutins", "Let me lay you down, down, down"], "meaning": "Christian Louboutin is a high-fashion French brand. They are most famous for their trademark red bottom sneakers.\n\nThe-Dream uses clever imagery here as one literally “stepping into a heart” would leave the bottom of their shoes red—hence the Louboutins mention—but in this context, it means to start a relationship.", "annotation_id": "18479754", "url": "https://genius.com/18479754", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shut It Down", "line": "Ki-Killa, Killa", "vault_lines": ["They ain't got nothing on you, Ki-Killa, Killa"], "meaning": "“Radio Killa” is a nickname The-Dream has used a lot throughout his career and is the name of his record label he created in 2007. In a verified annotation, he explained the origin of the name:\n\nI needed something that made people say “Radio killer? You ain’t killing shit! You’re not on the radio.” So, I needed to be there. I needed to be in the corner to fight my way out to that place to earn the name. So I felt like that was the best thing. That’s what you dream about. Your records being on the radio. And you literally killing it.\n\nHe also used the name on July 2007’s “Shawty Is Da Shit,” April 2008’s “I Luv Your Girl,” December 2008’s “Rockin’ That Shit,” and March 2009’s “My Love.”", "annotation_id": "18479711", "url": "https://genius.com/18479711", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shut It Down", "line": "Shut it down (Ayy), down (Ayy), down (Ayy) You would shut it down (Ayy), down (Ayy), down (Ayy) You’d be the baddest girl around (Ayy), ’round (Ayy), ’round (Ayy) And they’d notice, they’d notice You would shut it down (Ayy), down (Ayy), down (Ayy) You would shut it down (Ayy), down (Ayy), down (Ayy) You’d be the baddest girl around (Ayy), ’round (Ayy), ’round (Ayy) And they’d notice, they’d notice You would shut it—", "vault_lines": ["Shut it down (Ayy), down (Ayy), down (Ayy)", "You would shut it down (Ayy), down (Ayy), down (Ayy)", "You'd be the baddest girl around (Ayy), 'round (Ayy), 'round (Ayy)", "And they'd notice, they'd notice", "You would shut it—", "Uh, girl, you the greatest", "And together, we shut it down (Ayy), down (Ayy), down (Ayy)", "They'd know you shut it down", "You shut that, shut that down, down", "You shut that thing down, you shut it down", "Down (Ayy), down (Ayy), down (Ayy)", "You shut it down, you shut it down", "You shut it, oh, oh", "You shut it down", "You shut it down, down, down, down, down", "Lay you down, down, down", "You still the baddest girl around, 'round, 'round", "They'd notice, they'd notice"], "meaning": "“Bad” means to be attractive. Drake also talked about “bad” girls on his September 2011 song, “Free Spirit”:\n\nTell a bad bitch “Girl, let’s go hang”\n\nHis October 2011 song, “Make Me Proud”:\n\nEverything’s adding up, you’ve been through hell and back\nThat’s why you’re bad as fuck and you—\n\nAnd his November 2011 song, “Practice”:\n\nYou working with some ass yeah, you bad yeah", "annotation_id": "18479639", "url": "https://genius.com/18479639", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shut It Down", "line": "[Verse 3: Drake & The-Dream]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In this part of the song, Drake loops a part of the demo/mixtape version of the song and raps on  it.", "annotation_id": "252655", "url": "https://genius.com/252655", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shut It Down", "line": "I could bring her through and shut ’em down, Onyx", "vault_lines": ["I could bring her through and shut 'em down, Onyx"], "meaning": "Onyx is a hip hop group who had a song and album called “Shut ‘em Down” in 1998.\n\nDrake has expressed regret over using this line, telling AllHipHop:\n\nWell, that [punchline] flow has been killed by so many rappers. And, I never want to use that flow again in life. [Laughs] I wanted to take if off my album, because I was like, \"I shut em down. Onyx.“ I hate the fact that that rhyme is still in there. … [Lines like that are] all punchlines. Then a bunch of rappers started doing it and using the most terrible references in the world. I don’t want to offend somebody. I hate that rappers picked that flow up. I wish they had left that for people that know how to use it. [They go like,] “It’s a parade! MACY'S!”\nAt that point, you never want to use it.\n\nThis shot sparked a minor beef with Ludacris,.", "annotation_id": "64863", "url": "https://genius.com/64863", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shut It Down", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 6, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-shut-it-down-lyrics", "genius_title": "Shut It Down"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Can’t show us where the cash is, me, I don’t judge I’m just going off what the math is, numbers inflated", "vault_lines": ["Can't show us where the cash is, me, I don't judge", "I'm just going off what the math is, numbers inflated"], "meaning": "Drake only acknowledges the facts when it comes to his money—reportedly, he is worth $101 million.\n\nJudge Mathis is a reality court show lead by retired Superior Court Judge Greg Mathis as he presides over small claims court proceedings. In a court of law, it’s the facts that matter.", "annotation_id": "10739038", "url": "https://genius.com/10739038", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Girl, I love you, but I don’t miss ya", "vault_lines": ["Girl, I love you, but I don't miss ya"], "meaning": "Drake says that although he still loves and cherishes this girl, he is in not regretting or missing her as he doesn’t have much time to think about her.\n\nDrake could be speaking about Rih. Some rumors circulated saying that they had stopped “dating” or “seeing each other”.", "annotation_id": "10716298", "url": "https://genius.com/10716298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Count it, it’s all there, and we all square Quick money, I’m in and out", "vault_lines": ["Count it, it's all there, and we all square", "Quick money, I'm in and out"], "meaning": "A “square deal” is one in which all parties have reached an equitable agreement, such as when a loan is repaid. Drake asserts that this is a square deal; all the money is there.\n\nRegardless, Drake has no time for small talk. All he cares about as soon as he enters the room is his money; greetings and colloquies are secondary to him collecting his paper.\n\nDrake has always been unapologetically money-minded. He wants “No New Friends,” and his lyrics on “Versace” makes this abundantly clear:\n\nFuck all your feelings, ‘cause business is business\nIt’s strictly financial", "annotation_id": "10715989", "url": "https://genius.com/10715989", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "I’m down 200 in Vegas but winning life on a daily basis", "vault_lines": ["I'm down 200 in Vegas but winning life on a daily basis"], "meaning": "At Drake’s level of success, it doesn’t really matter to him if he gambles away money—the wealth he has amassed over the course of his career is more than enough to support his lifestyle.\n\nReportedly, Drake is worth $101 million, a stat that makes sense given every album he has released has at least gone platinum and he constantly sells out arenas all over the world. Even beyond the money, many of the artists he looked up to when he was up-and-coming have now become his peers with whom he is collaborating.", "annotation_id": "10714976", "url": "https://genius.com/10714976", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Mixin’ liquor got us both twisted, words get so vicious You just stare at me while you roll Swishers", "vault_lines": ["Mixin' liquor got us both twisted, words get so vicious", "You just stare at me while you roll Swishers"], "meaning": "“Twisted” refers to being drunk and high at the same time. “Swishers” is a reference to the brand Swishers Sweets cigars.\n\nThese cigars are relatively cheap and therefore considered to be one of the cheapest ways to roll a good blunt by emptying out the bad quality tobacco and only using the shell.", "annotation_id": "10712122", "url": "https://genius.com/10712122", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "And no matter what year it is, I’m a 06er", "vault_lines": ["And no matter what year it is, I'm a 06er"], "meaning": "A play on the fact that Drake comes from Toronto, Canada—Toronto is made up of 6 former cities/boroughs.\n\nDrake claims he is an old player in the rap game, no matter if it is 2010 or 2016. He calls himself the “6 God” on the album If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, short for 416 and 647, Toronto’s area codes.\n\nFurthermore “06er” could also be a reference to his 2006 debut mixtape, Room For Improvement which kickstarted his rap career.", "annotation_id": "10712089", "url": "https://genius.com/10712089", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Got two of my niggas off with a “not guilty”", "vault_lines": ["Got two of my niggas off with a \"not guilty\""], "meaning": "Two members of Drake’s crew have been involved in legal battles—Baka aka Not Nice was found not guilty of assault in May 2015, and Chubbs was sued by producer Detail over an alleged assault in 2014. No settlement has yet been announced by either party in that case. Drake implies he had a say in Baka’s not guilty verdict.", "annotation_id": "10711886", "url": "https://genius.com/10711886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "It seems like nobody wants to stay in my good graces I’m like a real estate agent, putting you all in your places Look what happens soon as you talk to me crazy Is you crazy?", "vault_lines": ["It seems like nobody wants to stay in my good graces", "I'm like a real estate agent, putting you all in your places", "Look what happens soon as you talk to me crazy"], "meaning": "Putting someone in their place is an idiom, meaning to give someone a reality check. Drake has sought to give a number of rappers a wake up call, including Meek Mill, Common, Pusha T, and Tory Lanez, among others.\n\nDrake compares himself to a real estate agent, someone who helps you buy and sell a house—real estate agents literally put people into places.\n\nThe last line targets Kid Cudi. Right after he called Drake out, Cudi checked himself into rehab for depression and suicidal urges. Drake is asking whether it’s a coincidence, in a real low but hard hitting jab.", "annotation_id": "10711269", "url": "https://genius.com/10711269", "state": "accepted", "votes": 140, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Used to carry a lot of dead weight like a pallbearer People too scared to tell the truth, so it’s all dares", "vault_lines": ["Used to carry a lot of dead weight like a pallbearer", "People too scared to tell the truth, so it's all dares"], "meaning": "A pallbearer helps carry a coffin. Drake’s dead weight may be related to the demons he spoke of earlier, or all of his grudges.\n\nDrake likens his rap beefs to a game of truth or dare. His enemies are afraid of the truth, likely meaning his rampant success, and instead dare each other to diss him.", "annotation_id": "10711038", "url": "https://genius.com/10711038", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Rap like I know I’m the greatest then give you tropical flavors", "vault_lines": ["Rap like I know I'm the greatest then give you tropical flavors"], "meaning": "Drake drew inspiration from dancehall—a style of music originating on the Caribbean island of Jamaica—for his songs “One Dance,” “Fake Love,” and “Controlla.”", "annotation_id": "10710979", "url": "https://genius.com/10710979", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "More time with family and friends, more life More time to get it right", "vault_lines": ["More time with family and friends, more life", "More time to get it right"], "meaning": "“More life” is a Jamaican phrase meaning to “wish someone well.” Drake has titled his upcoming project More Life.\n\nFamily was an important theme of Drake’s 2016 album Views. On the first track, “Keep The Family Close,” he dives into his trust issues with those closest to him. 2015 and 2016 have been busy years for Drake—he released multiple projects and embarked on the epic Summer Sixteen Tour, which has included 60 shows as of October 2016. He might just want some time to himself.", "annotation_id": "10710462", "url": "https://genius.com/10710462", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "It’s only me, but I’m seeing four shadows in the light My demons visit me every night", "vault_lines": ["It's only me, but I'm seeing four shadows in the light", "My demons visit me every night"], "meaning": "Drake uses a homophone on “four shadows/foreshadows,” implying he can see his future.\n\nWhile Drake feels haunted by his demons, whatever they may be, he’s confident his future is bright and his demons (shadows) are behind him. Drake used similar lyrics on his song “9”:\n\nYou know 40 wants peace\nBut I’m down to cut ties for it\nAnd I can’t sleep these days unless I take one\nIf they don’t have a story these days\nThey’ll make one\n\nDrake may also be alluding to his Summer Sixteen Tour—performing on stage under multiple stage lights casts multiple shadows over him in varying directions.", "annotation_id": "10710443", "url": "https://genius.com/10710443", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "To the most high, I’m forever indebted I know I gotta pay somethin’, I know that day’s comin’ I put it all in the music Because if I don’t say it here, then I won’t say nothin’", "vault_lines": ["To the most high, I'm forever indebted", "I know I gotta pay somethin', I know that day's comin'", "I put it all in the music", "Because if I don't say it here, then I won't say nothin'"], "meaning": "Drake thanks God for his recent successes—\"the most high\" means God. While others use social media to air each other out, Drake promises to use his music to address any issues he has with other rappers—something he does in this song with Kid Cudi and Pusha T.\n\nHere Drake is saying because God has blessed him to get to the position where hes at now, he’s aware that he is going to have to go through some struggle or challenge to pay for his success.", "annotation_id": "10710367", "url": "https://genius.com/10710367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "My dad used to use a soap bar ‘til it’s thinnin’ out But, shit, look at Dennis now, all Stacy Adams and linen’d out More blessings for Sandi and him, more life My parents never got it right But God bless ’em both, I think we all alike We all wide awake late at night, thinking on what to change If we do get to do it twice in another life", "vault_lines": ["Count it, it's all there, and we all square", "My dad used to use a soap bar 'til it's thinnin' out", "But, shit, look at Dennis now, all Stacy Adams and linen'd out", "More blessings for Sandi and him, more life", "My parents never got it right", "But God bless 'em both, I think we all alike", "More time to get it right", "We all wide awake late at night, thinking on what to change", "If we do get to do it twice in another life", "I never worked to get it"], "meaning": "Drake uses the term “more life” to wish his parents Dennis and Sandy well. Even though Drake dropped this song to celebrate his 30th birthday, his family still feels the effect of the divorce his parents had when he was 5.\n\nAfter going through tough times, Dennis has flourished—he dropped an R&B song, and can now afford Stacy Adams, a menswear brand.", "annotation_id": "10710351", "url": "https://genius.com/10710351", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Ever since the blue basement, I found God and I lost patience Between rocks and hard places of all places", "vault_lines": ["Ever since the blue basement, I found God and I lost patience", "Between rocks and hard places of all places"], "meaning": "During the recording of his debut EP So Far Gone, Drake lived in a blue basement with limited finances. The idiom “between a rock and a hard place” means to face a difficult decision—Drizzy is likely recalling when he quit acting to focus on music.\n\nDrake’s earnings as an actor were so low that he was close to getting a real job—i.e. he lost patience—but gambled on music instead. In a 2010 interview with Heeb Magazine, Drake expressed:\n\nI was coming to terms with the fact that I might have to work at a restaurant or something just to keep things going. The money from [Degrassi] was very small. And it was dwindling.\n\nAlthough Drake isn’t particularly religious, he’s frequently cited God as the reason for his success in numerous songs and interviews.", "annotation_id": "10710225", "url": "https://genius.com/10710225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Please welcome the October fall baby Vaughan Road Academy, star player—my mind’s not all there", "vault_lines": ["Please welcome the October fall baby", "Vaughan Road Academy, star player—my mind's not all there"], "meaning": "“Two Birds, One Stone” was released during Drake’s special birthday episode of OVO Sound Radio. His birthday is October 24, making him a Fall season baby.\n\nBefore finding fame on Degrassi, Drake attended high school at Vaughan Road Academy in Toronto, ON. On December 8th, 2016, it was announced the school is scheduled to be closed down.", "annotation_id": "10710179", "url": "https://genius.com/10710179", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Spotted everywhere, like Dalmatian Cops snoop around now, ’cause all of my dogs famous", "vault_lines": ["Spotted everywhere, like Dalmatian", "Cops snoop around now, 'cause all of my dogs famous"], "meaning": "Dalmatian is a breed of dog known for being covered in spots. Drake pops up in different cities, undeterred by threats.\n\n\n\nHe plays on the name of rapper Snoop Dogg to claim cops hang around his crew, waiting for one of them to commit a felony, purely because they’re famous. He may also be referencing the canine character Snoopy from the cartoon TV show Peanuts, who was also spotted.", "annotation_id": "10710166", "url": "https://genius.com/10710166", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Two birds, one stone, my aim is amazin’ I need to start losing my shit on you niggas that’s hatin’ Too reserved, like I called ahead for me and my lady", "vault_lines": ["Two birds, one stone, my aim is amazin'", "I need to start losing my shit on you niggas that's hatin'", "Too reserved, like I called ahead for me and my lady"], "meaning": "To “kill two birds with one stone” is to accomplish multiple things with one action. In this context, Drake is talking about addressing multiple beefs throughout this song.\n\nThe disses are left intentionally ambiguous so that they seem to be directed at more than one of his opponents at once. To “lose your shit” means to lose your cool or your mind. Drake may mean he needs to start openly using names in his disses to show who is and isn’t on his side.\n\nThere’s also a play on word with “too/two reserved” which could refer to one reservation for him and one for his lady.", "annotation_id": "10710148", "url": "https://genius.com/10710148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Scared to go to sleep now ’Cause being awake is what all my dreams were like Back when the bar that I had set for myself was out of sight Tell me how I went and did chin-ups on this shit when I can’t see it", "vault_lines": ["Scared to go to sleep now", "'Cause being awake is what all my dreams were like", "Back when the bar that I had set for myself was out of sight", "Tell me how I went and did chin-ups on this shit when I can't see it", "My numbers out of this world"], "meaning": "Before he was famous, Drake dreamed of becoming rich and successful. He’s since thrived, so much so that he imagines the high bar he set for himself as a physical pull-up bar. His chin-ups on the bar represent him rising above his goals.\n\nIn 2016, Drake spent time working out to define his body— chin-ups were, likely, part of his regime.\n\n\n\nDrake’s inability to sleep, revealed in 2016’s “9,” is likely due to the demons he’s mentioned.\n\nThe “chin-ups” part could also be him saying even the high bar he set for himself when starting up was unbelievably out of reach, but how he reached and even surpassed it is even more unbelievable.", "annotation_id": "10710142", "url": "https://genius.com/10710142", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "My numbers out of this world No wonder they got me feeling so alienated", "vault_lines": ["My numbers out of this world", "No wonder they got me feeling so alienated"], "meaning": "Drake’s numbers are incredibly impressive. Views has set all kinds of records, and is the highest selling album of 2016. As of this song’s release, his single “One Dance” is now the most streamed Spotify song of all time.\n\nHe argues this success has isolated him from his peers, and is the reason why he has been attacked by fellow rappers. His numbers are so “out of this world” that other rappers treat him as an alien rather than a peer.", "annotation_id": "10710135", "url": "https://genius.com/10710135", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "They all look at me, like, “What have you done for me lately?” “I like your older shit but wasn’t in love with the latest.” Aw, baby, stop debatin’, I’m just a creative", "vault_lines": ["They all look at me, like, \"What have you done for me lately?\"", "\"I like your older shit but wasn't in love with the latest.\"", "Aw, baby, stop debatin', I'm just a creative"], "meaning": "Drake may be referring to the reception of his 2016 album, VIEWS, and his subsequent beef with Joe Budden. Despite going platinum and getting over a billion streams, with a 69 on Metacritic, VIEWS had the lowest average critical rating of any Drake album. After the album dropped, longtime Drake supporter Joe Budden criticized VIEWS and Drake on his show I’ll Name This Podcast Later, saying:\n\nI think that that kid on that album that I heard sounds real fuckin’ uninspired…40 continues to progress. Drake you do not. I said the other day on this podcast I miss the Drake that starts the waves, not hops on other waves.\n\n“Awe baby” is also something Joe Budden often says on his podcast.\n\n“What Have You Done For Me Lately” is the name of a 1986 Janet Jackson song. The phrase was later used in a famous skit on Eddie Murphy’s 1987 stand-up comedy performance Raw.", "annotation_id": "10710109", "url": "https://genius.com/10710109", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Gave back to the city and never said it if I didn’t live it But still, they try and tell you I’m not the realest Like I’m some privileged kid that never sat through a prison visit Or like it was just handed to me tied with a ribbon I never worked to get it", "vault_lines": ["Gave back to the city and never said it if I didn't live it", "But still, they try and tell you I'm not the realest", "Like I'm some privileged kid that never sat through a prison visit", "Or like it was just handed to me tied with a ribbon", "I never worked to get it"], "meaning": "Drake was criticized after his track “Started From The Bottom” dropped, with some claiming he never actually started from the bottom. He also copped heat from Meek Mill over Quentin Miller’s role in his classic hook from “Know Yourself.” Meek rapped:\n\nI just wanna know, if you ain’t write that running through the six shit\nTell us who the fuck was Quentin running through the six with?\n\nDrake was not a privileged child—revealed during 2015’s “Star67,” the money he earned from his role on Degrassi went towards supporting his mother. Drake’s father was also incarcerated “two separate times.”\n\nDrake’s charity work is often overlooked. He claims he is giving back to his city of Toronto but not seeking gratitude for it. NPR certainly thinks so, estimating that Drake has bestowed “$3 billion worth of free advertising” upon the city.", "annotation_id": "10710055", "url": "https://genius.com/10710055", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "But really it’s you with all the drug dealer stories That’s gotta stop, though You made a couple chops and now you think you Chapo If you ask me though, you ain’t lining the trunk with kilos You bagging weed watching Pacino with all your niggas Like, “This what we need to be on,” but you never went live You middle-man in this shit, boy, you was never them guys I can tell, ’cause I look most of you dead in your eyes And you’ll be tryna sell that story for the rest of your lives", "vault_lines": ["I need to start losing my shit on you niggas that's hatin'", "But really it's you with all the drug dealer stories", "That's gotta stop, though", "You made a couple chops and now you think you Chapo", "If you ask me though, you ain't lining the trunk with kilos", "You bagging weed watching Pacino with all your niggas", "Like, \"This what we need to be on,\" but you never went live", "You middle-man in this shit, boy, you was never them guys", "I can tell, 'cause I look most of you dead in your eyes", "And you'll be tryna sell that story for the rest of your lives", "\"I like your older shit but wasn't in love with the latest.\"", "You were the man on the moon"], "meaning": "Drake calls out Pusha T, adding another barb to their long-lasting subliminal war. Pusha threw shots at Drake a few weeks prior to “Two Birds, One Stone” on “H.G.T.V. Freestyle”:\n\nIt’s too far gone when the realest ain’t real\nI walk amongst the clouds so your ceilings ain’t real\nThese niggas Call of Duty cause their killings ain’t real\nWith a questionable pen so the feelin' ain’t real\n\nPusha’s music career is built on his drug dealing past, a lifestyle which he alludes to in most songs he releases. Here, Drake insinuates that this is an exaggeration designed to sell records and that Pusha was merely bagging up weed while watching the classic gangsta flick Scarface for inspiration.\n\nGiven Pusha’s history with Clipse—their former manager Tony “Geezy” Gonzalez was charged with running  $10 million drug ring in 2009—it’s ironic that Drake (of all people) is the one to question the authenticity of his story.\n\nA “chop” in this scenario is a small-time deal. Doing a “couple chops” gave Pusha the mentality that he’s a big time drug lord like El Chapo, who Pusha compared himself to on “Retribution.”\n\nIn an interview with Complex Push responded to these lines:\n\nI heard something. I would never, ever attribute that [song] to myself. My past is cemented. My past happened. Like slavery happened. The Holocaust happened. You can’t ever question anything that has actually happened … Listen. I’m not speaking to him at all. I’m telling you, the real of it is: It ain’t real if it’s about me.\n\nThese lines could also be directed at Jay Z or Tory Lanez. Jay recently rapped about his drug dealing past on Pusha’s “Drug Dealers Anonymous,” while Tory questioned Drake’s privileged upbringing on his “Uber Everywhere (Remix).”\n\nThe ambiguous nature of these lines ties into theme of killing two birds with one stone, as it can be applicable to anyone who fits the mold. Pusha would later respond with “Infrared” which was released the year after this track.", "annotation_id": "10710053", "url": "https://genius.com/10710053", "state": "accepted", "votes": 201, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Go figure, cold nigga, stay in school, man Fuck the rap game, it’s all lies and it’s all filthy Two percent of us rich and the rest of these niggas all milk it", "vault_lines": ["Go figure, cold nigga, stay in school, man", "Fuck the rap game, it's all lies and it's all filthy", "Two percent of us rich and the rest of these niggas all milk it"], "meaning": "The expression “milking it,” short for “milking it for all it’s worth,” means to take more than you deserve or to be generally unrealistic about what you’re due. Two percent is a type of low fat milk.\n\nDrake takes a shot at rappers who inflate their wealth and success via their music and videos, notably those who wear fake jewelry and rap about things they can’t afford. They’re “milking” what little income they make in order to appear more successful and influential than they are.", "annotation_id": "10710048", "url": "https://genius.com/10710048", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Could feel my hand getting tired from holding the grudges", "vault_lines": ["Could feel my hand getting tired from holding the grudges"], "meaning": "Drake has clashed with a plethora of other artists over the years, including Meek Mill, Joe Budden, Diddy, Pusha T, Tyga, Common, and Chris Brown.\n\nAfter years of conflict, Drake seems willing to either let them go and move on with his life and career.", "annotation_id": "10710039", "url": "https://genius.com/10710039", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Free C5, how the fuck we got the boss waiting?", "vault_lines": ["Free C5, how the fuck we got the boss waiting?"], "meaning": "Drake’s label boss and mentor Lil Wayne has been in a highly publicized, multi-year battle for the release of his album Tha Carter V.\n\nA few weeks before this song’s release, Drake used the phrase “Free C5” in the caption of an Instagram post thanking Lil Wayne for all he’s done.", "annotation_id": "10710022", "url": "https://genius.com/10710022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 148, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "You were the man on the moon Now you just go through your phases Life of the angry and famous", "vault_lines": ["You were the man on the moon", "Now you just go through your phases", "Life of the angry and famous"], "meaning": "Man On The Moon is an album by Kid Cudi.\nThe album is said to have had a major impact on the new generation of rappers, including Drake.\n\nA month before the release of this song, Cudi caused a stir by calling both Kanye West and Drake “clowns” who don’t write their own music.\n\n\n\nDrake shot back during a show, saying:\n\nBoy, you getting way too high, you need to Cud-it\n\nAdditionally, “go through your phases” is a play on lunar phases, which ties into Cudi’s Man On the Moon, and in addition may refer to his fluctuating mental health. The word “lunatic” originally meant a person whose sanity is affected by the moon’s cycles.\n\nCudi’s manager responded within a day, while Cudi himself took to Twitter a week after to fire back at Drake:", "annotation_id": "10710012", "url": "https://genius.com/10710012", "state": "accepted", "votes": 234, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "line": "Pin-ups of Meagan Good and Pam Grier Soul sisters inspired my old scriptures Now that feeling’s gone like them old pictures", "vault_lines": ["Pin-ups of Meagan Good and Pam Grier", "Soul sisters inspired my old scriptures", "Now that feeling's gone like them old pictures"], "meaning": "Meagan Good and Pam Grier are both actresses. Drake appears to have used their posters as inspiration during his early musical endeavors. He references his early track “Scriptures” to confirm the time frame. His hunger for success that came with those early days is now gone.\n\nIn an interview with Nardwuar, Drake said:\n\nI love Pam Grier. I still lust after Pam Grier to this day. I tried to find her, I tried following her on Twitter and Instagram but I don’t think it’s the real her. She was one of my first muses […] She shaped what my idea of a woman was as far as curves, shape, and body, and Pam Grier’s what I fell in love with as a young guy.\n\nMeagan Good spoke about acting alongside Drake during Anchorman 2 on VladTV.", "annotation_id": "10709975", "url": "https://genius.com/10709975", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Two Birds, One Stone", "status": "done", "targets": 28, "clicked": 28, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-two-birds-one-stone-lyrics", "genius_title": "Two Birds, One Stone"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "I’ll tell ’em link up at the valet at the Hazy", "vault_lines": ["I'll tell 'em link up at the valet at the Hazy"], "meaning": "The “Hazy” is the nickname for The Hazleton Hotel, a five-star hotel in Toronto’s Yorkdale neighborhood.", "annotation_id": "41369155", "url": "https://genius.com/41369155", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Jewels look like I found a motherfuckin’ treasure map And ain’t told no one where the fuck it’s at", "vault_lines": ["Jewels look like I found a motherfuckin' treasure map", "And ain't told no one where the fuck it's at", "I ain't got no time"], "meaning": "The money and riches Drake has is been accumulated liked the jewels. He is keeping the success secret, like not telling anyone where he found the treasure map.", "annotation_id": "40571820", "url": "https://genius.com/40571820", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "[Verse 3: Lil Wayne]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The Wayne verse on this version has a different mix than the OG with the released cut using noticeably less Auto-Tune.", "annotation_id": "39439666", "url": "https://genius.com/39439666", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Fuck my nigga Terry for a new Blackberry", "vault_lines": ["Fuck my nigga Terry for a new Blackberry"], "meaning": "terry seems too small to be worth an iphone 6, but a blackberry will do", "annotation_id": "29706146", "url": "https://genius.com/29706146", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Way more gully gully than buddy buddy Never needed your acceptance, never needed nothin’", "vault_lines": ["Way more gully gully than buddy buddy", "Never needed your acceptance, never needed nothin'"], "meaning": "This diss could be a possible jab at famous hip-hop enthusiast, Joe Budden. Drake has gone after Budden multiple times including in his songs  4pm In Calabasas  &  No Shopping  with French Montana where Drake says\n\nPump, pump, pump it up (Skrrt)\nShe got a good head on her, but I pump it up (Skrrt)\nI’m not a one hit wonder, they know all my stuff (Haan)\nYou let me turn into the nigga that you almost was”\n\nBudden fully explained the beef to Hot 97’s Ebro Darden back in 2016.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/5G_EwfZ85Wk", "annotation_id": "25290424", "url": "https://genius.com/25290424", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Make sure the plane got a phone now So when we ’bout to land I can call to tell the woes I’m home now", "vault_lines": ["Make sure the plane got a phone now", "So when we 'bout to land I can call to tell the woes I'm home now"], "meaning": "In an interview with Noisey, Toronto rapper and OVO Sound affiliate Devontee defines the meaning behind his and Drake’s ‘woes’:\n\n“Woe is my crew. It stands for ‘working on excellence.’ It’s just my whole brand and my whole movement and my way of life for everyone. I want everyone to work on excellence. So, all my friends are my Woes and I feel anybody working on excellence in life is a Woe in life as well.”\n\nWhen Drake returns to his home in the six (Toronto), he’s able to call his crew to get together.", "annotation_id": "20086446", "url": "https://genius.com/20086446", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "[Produced by WondaGurl]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In an episode of The Creator Class' ORIGINALS series, WondaGurl unveiled the sample (Marcello Giombini’s “Synthotango”) and how she sent this beat to Drake:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/J7unBlyNGeg?t=137", "annotation_id": "8338874", "url": "https://genius.com/8338874", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Y’all don’t understand, I’m the one they want to hear from Y’all don’t understand that it’s me or nothin’", "vault_lines": ["Y'all don't understand, I'm the one they want to hear from", "Y'all don't understand that it's me or nothin'"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the beef between Drake and Diddy. Originally, Diddy wanted to use the beat that Boi-1da had sent him. However, Drake quickly hopped on the beat, and released 0 to 100/The Catch Up, which infuriated Diddy, causing him to punch Drake.\n\n\n\nDrake responds here by saying that the people would rather hear from him than anyone else, being that he is a legend.\n\nAnd just like every single other thing in my life, you can have her when I’m finished", "annotation_id": "4920497", "url": "https://genius.com/4920497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Shaq postin’ up on niggas that I used to have posters of", "vault_lines": ["Shaq postin' up on niggas that I used to have posters of"], "meaning": "This line might refer to the retired basketball player Shaquille O'Neal, who was known for his dominant plays in the post.\n\nDrake is a big fan of basketball and is currently working as the Global Ambassador of his hometown team in the NBA: Toronto Raptors.\n\n(e.g., Shaq with some post moves)\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MxZQw-dEydU\n\nHe could also be referring to the fact that the same artists that have influenced his drive for music (e.g., Kanye West and Jay-Z) are currently the same individuals he is up against in Hip-Hop, and possibly will overcome towards the latter of his career.", "annotation_id": "4898112", "url": "https://genius.com/4898112", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Real quick, man, you couldn’t have hated that Let’s be real, nigga, you couldn’t have made it that, woo!", "vault_lines": ["Real quick, man, you couldn't have hated that", "Let's be real, nigga, you couldn't have made it that, woo!"], "meaning": "It is rumored that Diddy smacked Drake over the song “0 to 100.” In the chorus he says, “0 to 100 nigga real quick,” because Diddy gave the instrumental to Drake to write something for him to rap over, but instead he put it out for himself.\n\nHere, Drake refers to the controversy saying that Diddy couldn’t have hated the move he made because he couldn’t have made such an amazing record on his own.", "annotation_id": "4891897", "url": "https://genius.com/4891897", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Yeah, I’m fuckin’ glowin’ up", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm fuckin' glowin' up"], "meaning": "Drake is not only glowin' up in the rap industry, he’s outshining everyone else around him. Recently, Drake has been dominating the game and with the recent release of If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, Drake is further emphasizing his dominance.\n\nThis is also more than likely a reference to the Glo Up Challenge on Twitter where you post pictures of your past and compare them to the current.\n\nYeah, you ain’t really fuck with me way back then, girl but How Bout Now?\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to Chief Keef and his Glo Gang.", "annotation_id": "4888712", "url": "https://genius.com/4888712", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Although this song appeared on both Lil Wayne’s Sorry 4 The Wait 2 and Drake’s If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, this verse was only featured on the latter.", "annotation_id": "4888426", "url": "https://genius.com/4888426", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "I be with the bands Like a nigga went to Jackson State or Grambling Young Nick Cannon with the snare drum, dancin’ Watch the way I handle it, uh", "vault_lines": ["I be with the bands", "Like a nigga went to Jackson State or Grambling", "Young Nick Cannon with the snare drum, dancin'", "Watch the way I handle it, uh", "With the bands like I must've went to Clark, went to Hampton", "I ain't playin' with it"], "meaning": "We were not really feeling this line…AT ALL", "annotation_id": "4768262", "url": "https://genius.com/4768262", "state": "verified", "votes": -854, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Trap pumpin’ all night like Chevron", "vault_lines": ["Trap pumpin' all night like Chevron"], "meaning": "Chevron is an energy corporation that owns gas stations. The “trap” can refer to Wayne’s trap music, or his trap house. Regardless, the trap is pumping like the gas pumped at a Chevron gas station – 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.", "annotation_id": "4767972", "url": "https://genius.com/4767972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Boy, do I smell beef? Mmmm, pheromones", "vault_lines": ["Boy, do I smell beef? Mmmm, pheromones"], "meaning": "Beef is the common term for a fight or feud and a lot of animals are attracted to each other by chemical releases called pheromones. Weezy is saying that rather than run from it, he loves a good fight; anyone who wants to beef with him had better beware.", "annotation_id": "4761331", "url": "https://genius.com/4761331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Promethazine over pinot", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Wayne prefers promethazine, an ingredient in lean, which he was strongly addicted to, rather than wine – pinot is a grape used to make wine.", "annotation_id": "4761277", "url": "https://genius.com/4761277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Goin’ at a nigga throat like a herringbone", "vault_lines": ["Goin' at a nigga throat like a herringbone"], "meaning": "Herringbone is a weaving pattern in fabric, typically seen in neckties (or chains). Blood would splatter all over the herringbone tie if the wearer’s throat was slashed. Manners, Wayne.", "annotation_id": "4761061", "url": "https://genius.com/4761061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "I got, mind control over Deebo", "vault_lines": ["I got, mind control over Deebo"], "meaning": "A reference to the classic film, Friday.\n\nhttp://m.youtube.com/watch?v=Kw31fE_AWsI", "annotation_id": "4760645", "url": "https://genius.com/4760645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Shout out to the G’s from the ends We don’t love no girls from the ends", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to the G's from the ends", "We don't love no girls from the ends"], "meaning": "In a nod to Skepta’s 2014 banger “That’s Not Me”, Drake informs the listener that even neighborhood girls don’t get love from him or his crew.\n\n“Ends” is a London slang term for “neighborhood”, as referenced in Skepta’s track. This can also apply to Drake because in Toronto they refer to different parts of the city as the “East End and West End” of the city.\n\nDrake credits Skepta as an inspiration in the album notes for If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, and has praised the artist in a series of recent Instagram posts.", "annotation_id": "4760266", "url": "https://genius.com/4760266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "When you get to where the fuck I’m at You gotta remind ’em about where you been About all of the money that done came and went About the two cents I ain’t never spent When they say you’re too famous to pack a gat I gotta remind ’em about where I’m from", "vault_lines": ["And ain't told no one where the fuck it's at", "They never told me when you get the crown", "Yeah, when you get to where I'm at", "You gotta remind 'em where the fuck you at", "When you get to where the fuck I'm at", "You gotta remind 'em about where you been", "About all of the money that done came and went", "About the two cents I ain't never spent", "When they say you're too famous to pack a gat", "I gotta remind 'em about where I'm from"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is from Hollygrove, New Orleans, where the crime rate is high. Due to the high crime rate, it’s a no-brainer that he knows how to handle a gat (slang for gun).\n\nNow, Wayne is sitting on $140 million in net worth. He looks at his current state of living, and compares it to when he was growing up.\n\n\n\nThe idiom two cents is used to describe what someone has to put in with their wisdom. Wayne uses the idiom to say that he has never told somebody how to do their job, so they should not tell him how to do his! This could be in reference to the Cash Money beef.", "annotation_id": "4760032", "url": "https://genius.com/4760032", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "They gon’ say your name on them airwaves They gon’ hit you up right after like it’s only rap", "vault_lines": ["They gon' say your name on them airwaves", "They gon' hit you up right after like it's only rap"], "meaning": "This could be a shot at Kendrick Lamar, who called out Drake, along with many other rappers,  in his blown up “Control” verse. In the track, Kendrick mentioned that in the end, it’s all just rap.\n\nCould also  be a subliminal at Chris Brown who said during an interview with Sway:\n\nIt’s rap man, it’s hip-hop. I say whatever I want, I’m not a rapper, so you gonna be stupid rapping back to a singer, but I'ma keep coming\n\nhttps://youtu.be/vQdL6XROV90?t=1m5s\n\nDrake and Chris Brown have been beefing for a while.", "annotation_id": "4759707", "url": "https://genius.com/4759707", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Only see the truth when I’m staring in the mirror Lookin’ at myself like, there it is there Yeah, like there it is there man, whoo!", "vault_lines": ["Only see the truth when I'm staring in the mirror", "Lookin' at myself like, there it is there", "Yeah, like there it is there man, whoo!"], "meaning": "Drizzy alludes to the popular song named “Whoomp! (There It Is)” by Tag Team.\n\nLooking for the truth in a mirror is a waste of time with Drake; it’s standing there right in front of him!", "annotation_id": "4759149", "url": "https://genius.com/4759149", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "And when my bloods start shootin’ that’s B-roll, bitch", "vault_lines": ["And when my bloods start shootin' that's B-roll, bitch"], "meaning": "B-roll is a production term used in film theatres hence the reference to “shooting” both in the sense of making a movie (or making it big) or defending your clique by firing back.\n\nB is also a blood type.", "annotation_id": "4758844", "url": "https://genius.com/4758844", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "I ain’t tryna chance it,", "vault_lines": ["I ain't tryna chance it,", "I ain't playin' with it"], "meaning": "As Drake said in “Draft Day”:\n\nAnd if I left this shit to chance I would’ve picked a name like Chance the Rapper", "annotation_id": "4758824", "url": "https://genius.com/4758824", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Yeah—sound, sound, sound Yeah, real 6 side shit Sickos, ah man", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, real 6 side shit"], "meaning": "Drizzy sets the tone by shouting out his label OVO Sound and his home town, Toronto (416).\n\nComing from the 6 side", "annotation_id": "4758795", "url": "https://genius.com/4758795", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "I was only five, but still remember the drought in ’87", "vault_lines": ["I was only five, but still remember the drought in '87"], "meaning": "In 1987 the United States suffered a terrible drought.\n\nHowever Lil Wayne is probably referring a series of mix tapes he released earlier during his career called Da Drought. Although he was only five in 1987 he had already seen things that left an impression in his life forever and he incorporated these memories in his music. He has always been a young visionary!", "annotation_id": "4756799", "url": "https://genius.com/4756799", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "Suck a nigga dick for a iPhone 6", "vault_lines": ["Suck a nigga dick for a iPhone 6"], "meaning": "A reference to the classic line in DJ Jimi’s “Where They At”\n\nI said BITCH! stop talking that shit\nAnd suck a nigga dick for an outfit\n\nWeezy previously (and famously) referred to this line on French Montana’s “Pop That”\n\nBitch! Stop talkin' that shit\nAnd suck a nigga dick for some Trukfit", "annotation_id": "4756536", "url": "https://genius.com/4756536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "You can get buried for a ounce of Katy Perry", "vault_lines": ["You can get buried for a ounce of Katy Perry"], "meaning": "Is Wayne really talking about RiFF RAFF’s girl, when he is on the intro and outro?\n\nAnyway, an ounce of Katy Perry refers to an ounce of cocaine, as Katy Perry is a white girl, and “white” is a commonly used term when talking about cocaine.", "annotation_id": "4756420", "url": "https://genius.com/4756420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "I ain’t felt the pressure in a little while It’s gonna take some getting used to (What you say?) Floatin’ all through the city with the windows down Puttin’ on like I used to (What you say?) They never told me when you get the crown It’s gon’ take some getting used to (What you say?) New friends all in their old feelings now They don’t love you like they used to, man", "vault_lines": ["I be with the bands", "I ain't playin' with it", "I ain't felt the pressure in a little while", "It's gonna take some getting used to (What you say?)", "Floatin' all through the city with the windows down", "Puttin' on like I used to (What you say?)", "They never told me when you get the crown", "It's gon' take some getting used to (What you say?)", "New friends all in their old feelings now", "They don't love you like they used to, man", "Yeah, they don't love you like they used to (What you say?)", "In the city with the- windows down", "Like I used to (What you say?)", "Take some to getting used to (What you say?)", "Man, they don't love you like they used to, man"], "meaning": "Unlike The Throne, Drake feels like no one’s pressuring him musically. Since every song really is Drake featuring Drake – he can afford to forget about Jay Z & Kendrick Lamar’s lyrical jabs.\n\nKeeping the same “No New Friends” policy keeps Drake fleek – those “do you remember me?” acquaintances just get shrugged off.\n\nShe says they miss the old Drake, girl don’t tempt me", "annotation_id": "4756367", "url": "https://genius.com/4756367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "I’m gon hit ’em with the wham once again I’ma always end up as a man in the end, dog It’s just apparent every year", "vault_lines": ["I'm gon hit 'em with the wham once again", "I'ma always end up as a man in the end, dog", "It's just apparent every year"], "meaning": "To wham someone is to verbally assault them. Drizzy’s coming for the number one spot in 2015, just like he did in 2014, and 2013.\n\nDuring an interview with GQ Magazine, Kanye West had this to say about Drake’s success in 2013:\n\n“[Drake]. He got last summer. And I’d never given it up till last summer.” Now he’s thinking about taking it back. “It’s a real question for me. Do I want to?”\n\nAdditionally, “gon hi'em with the wham once again” could allude to Drake deploying the vegetarian ham replacement ‘Wham\" as a weapon. This effectively captures Drake’s defining characteristics (can go hard but is obviously a softy deep down, hence the meat replacement..)", "annotation_id": "4756352", "url": "https://genius.com/4756352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "line": "I be with the bands Like a nigga went to Jackson State or Grambling Young Nick Cannon with the snare drum, dancin’ Watch the way I handle it, uh", "vault_lines": ["I be with the bands", "Like a nigga went to Jackson State or Grambling", "Young Nick Cannon with the snare drum, dancin'", "Watch the way I handle it, uh", "With the bands like I must've went to Clark, went to Hampton", "I ain't playin' with it"], "meaning": "On “Believe Me”, Drake made a similar connection between his money and rock bands Nirvana and Coldplay. In this line he takes a new approach: college marching bands.\n\nJackson State, Grambling, Clark-Atlanta and Hampton University are all HBCUs with renowned marching bands. Jackson State even used Drake’s “Trophies” in one of their performances.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L8Db1WJ5-58\n\nTopping off this set of lines is another college band reference: the 2002 film, Drumline, where Nick Cannon plays the role of a talented drummer from Harlem who was recruited to play for the fictional Atlanta A&T University.", "annotation_id": "4756244", "url": "https://genius.com/4756244", "state": "accepted", "votes": 206, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used To", "status": "done", "targets": 31, "clicked": 31, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-used-to-lyrics", "genius_title": "Used To"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I Do (Remix)", "line": "Please put away the cameras Cause we just in the club getting MC hammered", "vault_lines": ["Unh, Please put away the cameras", "Cause we just in the club getting MC Hammered"], "meaning": "They’re gettin' drunk (or hammered) and they are dancing and going crazy (wordplay here as MC Hammer is known for his dance moves) so Drizzy doesn’t want anyone to tape it because he knows it will be put into the news/tabloids the next day if it is.", "annotation_id": "583737", "url": "https://genius.com/583737", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
