{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Drop, drop, drop, drop", "vault_lines": ["'Cause Top told you, \"Drop and give me fifty,\" like some push-ups, huh", "Top say drop, you better drop and give 'em fifty", "Top say drop, your little midget ass better fuckin'", "Ayy, better drop and give me fifty, ayy", "Drop and give me fifty, drop and give me fifty, ayy", "This for all the top dogs, drop and give me fifty, drop, drop", "Drop and give me fifty, all you fuck niggas teamin' up", "Drop a fifty bag, twenty-nine for the thot"], "meaning": "This line has a double meanning. “Drop” as in go down and “Drop” as in release a song.", "annotation_id": "34540313", "url": "https://genius.com/34540313", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Niggas really got me out here rappin’ what I’m livin’", "vault_lines": ["Niggas really got me out here talkin' like I'm 50, ayy", "Niggas really got me out here rappin' what I'm livin'"], "meaning": "Due to the multitude of rappers dissing Drake, they’ve got him to respond, coming back with the harsh reality. From giving Future his first number-one, The Weeknd running from Toronto, Kendrick being outsold and more, Drizzy claims he is simply rapping about the life he is living and coming with the facts.", "annotation_id": "32060900", "url": "https://genius.com/32060900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "[Outro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "On May 4, 2024, Drake would release “Family Matters” in response to Kendrick Lamar’s “euphoria” and “6:16 in LA”.\n\nUnknown at the time “Push Ups” released, the outro to “Push Ups” would serve as the first part of “Family Matters”.", "annotation_id": "31633318", "url": "https://genius.com/31633318", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Drop a fifty bag for the mob in the spot Drop a fifty bag, twenty-nine for the thot", "vault_lines": ["Drop a fifty bag, twenty-nine for the thot"], "meaning": "In the last two lines before the beat cuts in this melodic outro, Drake seemingly reasserts his wealth and willingness to drop fifty thousand dollars on his crew at Area 29, one of Houston’s adult venues known for A-list celebrities, exclusivity, and, honorary Houstonian, Drake himself, last seen at the club in January 2024, in a video that surfaced on X showing Drake posing with one of the club’s dancers. He has also directly shared photos of himself at Area 29 on Instagram, notably in December 2023, where he is pictured with stacks of cash, throwing money to the crowd.\n\nThe Canadian rapper has also referenced the club in “Mob Ties” and “Fair Trade.\" Drake would then later reuse the outro lines in the chorus in his follow-up diss track “Family Matters.”", "annotation_id": "31619594", "url": "https://genius.com/31619594", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "This for all the top dogs, drop and give me fifty, drop, drop", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, better drop and give me fifty, ayy", "Drop and give me fifty, drop and give me fifty, ayy", "This for all the top dogs, drop and give me fifty, drop, drop"], "meaning": "While Drake takes clear shots at Kendrick Lamar throughout the track, he makes it clear that he’s also out for Anthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith and all affiliates of Top Dawg Entertainment (TDE). He implies that other artists under the label have also been subject to the supposed 50% split.\n\n\n\n Top Dawgs From Left to Right:\nKendrick Lamar, SZA, Schoolboy Q, Ab-Soul, Isaiah Rashad, Jay Rock\n\n\nDrake also plays on the title of his eighth full-length studio album, For All the Dogs (2023), whilst using a phrase that he’s employed throughout his catalog:\n\nFor example, on “9” (2016), Drake starts the track with:\n\nYeah, for the dogs dem, you know\n\nAnd more recently on “Bahamas Promises” off the aforementioned project:\n\nDogs, man\nYeah, For All The Dogs\n[…] Dogs, man\nYeah, For All The Dogs", "annotation_id": "31558696", "url": "https://genius.com/31558696", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Nigga callin’ Top to see if Top wanna peace it up “Top, wanna peace it up? Top, wanna peace it up?” Nah, pussy, now you on your own when you speakin’ up", "vault_lines": ["Nigga callin' Top to see if Top wanna peace it up", "\"Top, wanna peace it up? Top, wanna peace it up?\"", "Nah, pussy, now you on your own when you speakin' up"], "meaning": "In this bar Drake implies that Kendrick Lamar will have to call Anthony Tiffith, who is currently CEO of Top Dawg Entertainment and a long time prolific producer who has worked with Kendrick Lamar since 2004, to see if he is permitted to diss Drake or if Anthony would like him to make peace with Drake.\n\nWith this, Drake implies that Kendrick is not in control of his own artistry and needs permission  from Anthony to publish a diss track addressing Drake’s 2024 song “Push Ups”, or any music for that matter.\n\nHe then implies that Kendrick is not going to have any support from his label or fellow label signees when he is responding to Drake, and that he does not have Anthony’s permission to release a diss track directed at Drake in response to “Push Ups”.", "annotation_id": "31527202", "url": "https://genius.com/31527202", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Drizzy Chip ’n Dale, probably got your bitch Chanel", "vault_lines": ["Drizzy Chip 'n Dale, probably got your bitch Chanel"], "meaning": "Chip ‘n’ Dale first appeared in the cartoon Private Pluto in 1943. They were in 23 animated short films.\n\nThe two animated chipmunks were known for outsmarting Donald Duck, and Pluto the dog. Which is also Future’s nickname, Drake referred to him as in “What Would Pluto Do”\n\nIn this bar Drake compares himself to Chip n' Dale, outsmarting and getting into trouble with “Pluto”, or Future.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, a girl known as Just Tokyo, who Drake and Future are supposedly beefing over, posted a gifted pair of Chanel shoes on her Instagram the day after attending a show of Drake’s. As the rest of the line is a shot at Future, this is likely another jab at him.\n\nJust Tokyo repping Future’s merch", "annotation_id": "31485481", "url": "https://genius.com/31485481", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Champagne trippin’, he is not fuckin’ easin’ up", "vault_lines": ["Champagne trippin', he is not fuckin' easin' up"], "meaning": "Drake proclaims that he isn’t stopping anytime soon, as he continues to go after rappers and celebrities who he has beef with. With the line, he uses his nickname, Champagne Papi, to create a double entendre. “Champagne trippin” can be seen as someone who has had too much to drink, but in this case he is referring to his moniker.\n\nThe rapper’s affinity for champagne has been well-documented for a while, most notably with his release of his own champagne, Mod Sélection. The drink has been commonly associated with the Toronto native for the majority of his career, including mentions of it throughout his discography, including the song “Champagne Poetry,” the first track off of his September 2021 album, Certified Lover Boy.\n\nOn Birdman’s September 2009 hit single “Money to Blow,” with Lil Wayne and Drake, Champagne Papi raps:\n\nI am on a 24-hour champagne diet\nSpilling while I’m sipping—I encourage you to try it\nI’m probably just saying that ‘cause I don’t have to buy it\nThe club owner supply it; boy, I’m on that fly shit", "annotation_id": "31442511", "url": "https://genius.com/31442511", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Niggas really got me out here talkin’ like I’m 50, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Niggas really got me out here talkin' like I'm 50, ayy", "Niggas really got me out here rappin' what I'm livin'"], "meaning": "Drake likens himself to hip hop icon 50 Cent, who is notorious for instigating various beefs throughout his career, one of which had him famously survive being shot 9 times. 50 flexed this reputation on his 2003 hit “Many Men (Wish Death)” from his debut album Get Rich Or Die Tryin' where he rapped:\n\nMany men wish death upon me\nBlood in my eye, dawg, and I can’t see\nI’m tryin' to be what I’m destined to be\nAnd niggas tryin' to take my life away\n\nDrake feels that he, like 50, has made quite a list of enemies over the years, hence why he insists that he feels “teamed up” against in a lopsided beef.\n\n50 Cent notably reacted to the leaked diss with a predictably hostile Instagram caption on April 15th, 2024:\n\nAll you 🥷🏾’s got smoked by a light skinned Ni66a. 😳🤭LOL YALL BETTER GET HIGH AS A MOTHER FVCKER AND COME UP WITH SOMETHING!", "annotation_id": "31439994", "url": "https://genius.com/31439994", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "I know my picture on the wall when y’all cook up", "vault_lines": ["I know my picture on the wall when y'all cook up"], "meaning": "Drake takes shots at Metro Boomin, Future, and the plethora of artists that have dissed him on WE DON’T TRUST YOU and WE STILL DON’T TRUST YOU. He lives in their head rent-free, especially when it comes time for them to make music. Drake suggests that they keep a picture of his face to remind them of their motivation.\n\nDrake is also talking about the plaques that he’s helped artists obtain that they cherish and hang on their walls. Metro and Future have multiple platinum tracks with Drake, many of which coming off of the 2015 mixtape What a Time To Be Alive. The same can be said about Rick Ross with songs like “Money in the Grave” and A$AP Rocky with his biggest song “Fuckin' Problems” (a track that Kendrick Lamar is also apart of).", "annotation_id": "31434692", "url": "https://genius.com/31434692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Y’all nigga manager was Chubbs lil’ blunt runner", "vault_lines": ["Y'all nigga manager was Chubbs lil' blunt runner"], "meaning": "This is a shot at The Weeknd’s manager, CashXO. Drake is saying that Cash was a blunt runner for the head of Drake’s security, Chubbs.\n\nA blunt runner is essentially a personal assistant who would get weed, liquor, women, etc. for a rapper and his entourage.", "annotation_id": "31433438", "url": "https://genius.com/31433438", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "You done rolled deep to this, it’s not fuckin’ deep enough", "vault_lines": ["You done rolled deep to this, it's not fuckin' deep enough"], "meaning": "Another reference to how many people seem to be teaming up against Drake at the time of this song’s recording. Further continuing the “20-v-1” line.\n\nDrake is noting that his opponent (Whether that be Kendrick, Metro Boomin', Future, or any of them) might have brought a lot of help to come at Drake, but even still, they didn’t bring enough to make a difference.", "annotation_id": "31433233", "url": "https://genius.com/31433233", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Beggin’ Kai Cenat, boy, you not fuckin’ beatin’ us", "vault_lines": ["Beggin' Kai Cenat, boy, you not fuckin' beatin' us"], "meaning": "This is a shot at Kendrick and his record label Interscope Records. Interscope supposedly begged Kai Cenat to stream with Kendrick as you can see in this video. Many rappers and artists go on stream with Kai for publicity before a drop, as 21 Savage did a stream with Kai Cenat prior to his release of american dream.", "annotation_id": "31432879", "url": "https://genius.com/31432879", "state": "accepted", "votes": 160, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "I’ll be rockin’ every fuckin’ chain I own next visit, ayy", "vault_lines": ["I'll be rockin' every fuckin' chain I own next visit, ayy"], "meaning": "On “Like That”, in which Kendrick Lamar’s verse is full of shots at Drake, K Dot said:\n\nGot 2TEEZ with me, I’m snatchin’ chains and burnin’ tattoos, it’s up\n\nDrake responds by saying he is not scared of 2TEEZ or Kendrick snatching his chains by claiming he will wear every one he owns next time he sees Kendrick, showcasing his confidence that Kendrick is lying.", "annotation_id": "31432621", "url": "https://genius.com/31432621", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Nigga, shout out to the hooper that be bustin’ out the griddy We know why you mad, nigga, I ain’t even trippin’ All that lil’ heartbroken Twitter shit for bitches", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, shout out to the hooper that be bustin' out the griddy", "We know why you mad, nigga, I ain't even trippin'", "All that lil' heartbroken Twitter shit for bitches"], "meaning": "Drake talks about known Memphis NBA Player “Ja Morant”, known for doing a dance named “The Griddy” and known for being suspended multiple times for gun showcases and more.\n\nJa Morant responded to Metro Boomin’s tweet regarding his and Future’s new album, WE STILL DON’T TRUST YOU, which was full of subliminals at Drake, supporting the album:\n\n\n\nThe reason Morant may have sided with Metro is that Drake was rumoured to have gone on a date with Ja’s ex girlfriend, Brooklyn Nicole. However, Drake makes fun of Ja for being heartbroken and running his mouth on Twitter.", "annotation_id": "31432504", "url": "https://genius.com/31432504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "What top five you smokin’ on, Kendrick?", "vault_lines": ["What top five you smokin' on, Kendrick?"], "meaning": "This was taken from an infamous Twitch stream by DJ Akademiks, showing his admiration for Drake before listening to his 2021 album Certified Lover Boy:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OHXmY51y-R4\n\nAkademiks was talking about Kendrick Lamar’s feature on Baby Keem’s 2021 smash hit “family ties,” where Kendrick rapped:\n\nSmokin' on top fives\nMotherfuck that album, fuck that single\nBurn that hard drive\n\nBoth Drake and Akademiks don’t think Kendrick is “smoking on” anyone.", "annotation_id": "31431372", "url": "https://genius.com/31431372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 226, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "[Interlude: DJ Akademiks]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "DJ Akademiks is a podcaster and internet personality best known for his King Akademiks YouTube channel and his Off The Record Podcast.\n\n\n\nAs a man who often publicly displays his love for Drake, it only makes sense for Drizzy to sample him like this.\n\nOn the day that this song was leaked, Akademiks went live to talk about the song and once again claim Drake’s superiority over Kendrick Lamar:\n\nHe (Drake) don’t hate on niggas, you know why, cause he’s the biggest. You know what Kendrick does? He stays in a rat hole for four years working on his contract with TDE or pgLang, pop out, and the first thing he gotta say is “Yo, fuck the big three!”", "annotation_id": "31431318", "url": "https://genius.com/31431318", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "(Whoo Kid)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the producer tag of DJ Whoo Kid. The tag, voiced by himself, was first heard on the 1999 Mixtape, “90 Minute Shootout Pt. 1.”\n\nThe Haitian-American producer is known for his broadcast personality and high-profile mixtapes with 50 Cent, which are full of diss tracks. As Whoo Kid did not contribute to the production on “Push Ups”, Drake is setting the tone for this war via this sample. He is letting the people know he’s ready to take on the plethora of artists dissing him, similar to how 50 did on “How To Rob”, and is going to replicate 50’s brutal, fearless approach to diss records. Drake even likened himself to 50 later on this track:\n\nNiggas really got me out here talkin’ like I’m 50, ayy\n\nDJ Whoo Kid and 50\n\nIn Drake’s May 2024 Kendrick Lamar diss track “Family Matters”, Drizzy claimed one of Kendrick’s kid’s biological father is Dave Free, Lamar’s right hand man. Drake may have sampled this producer tag to foreshadow this future diss, as we learnt who Kendrick’s kid’s dad really is.", "annotation_id": "31431267", "url": "https://genius.com/31431267", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "I might take your latest girl and cuff her like I’m Ricky Can’t believe he jumpin’ in, this nigga turnin’ fifty Every song that made it on the chart, he got from Drizzy Spend that lil’ check you got and stay up out my business", "vault_lines": ["Get more love in the city that you from, nigga", "I might take your latest girl and cuff her like I'm Ricky", "Can't believe he jumpin' in, this nigga turnin' fifty", "Every song that made it on the chart, he got from Drizzy", "Spend that lil' check you got and stay up out my business"], "meaning": "This is a jab towards Rick Ross, who unfollowed Drake on IG soon after Metro Boomin and Future dropped We Don’t Trust You, which he appears on. Other than that, Rick Ross posted an IG story of him listening to Kendrick’s diss towards Drake in “Like That”.\n\nRick Ross responded to the diss several hours later with “Champagne Moments” where he clarifies the reason for unfollowing Drake, which was that Drake allegedly sent a cease and desist to French Montana over a Metro Boomin' produced collab on “Mac & Cheese 5”.\n\nDrake making it clear that he could make anyone’s girl his and ‘cuff them' is a nod to Rick Ross’s past career as a correctional officer in 1995.\n\nDrake has also personally invited Cristina Mackey (Rick Ross’s ex-girlfriend) to the Sunrise, Florida stop of It’s All a Blur – Big as the What tour. She posted VIP tickets on her IG after Rick Ross unfollowed him.\n\nThe Canadian rapper shares his shock over Rick Ross’s decision to be involved with the beef because of his relatively mature age (48 years old) and his chart success from Drake’s contributions on “Aston Martin Music”, “Stay Schemin'”, “Diced Pineapples” and “Gold Roses” as well as many more where they are both featured artists.", "annotation_id": "31431239", "url": "https://genius.com/31431239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 140, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Claim the 6ix and you boys ain’t even come from it", "vault_lines": ["Claim the 6ix and you boys ain't even come from it"], "meaning": "Drake is likely referring to either Iranian-born Amir Esmailian, or Lebanon-born Wassim Slaiby, who are the co-managers of The Weeknd. Despite claiming the city of Toronto, operating within it, and signing Canadian artists, Drake, as the self-professed 6ix God, implies that the entire XO team has no influence or hold over the city of Toronto, and he dismisses these boys simply as blunt runners for his security team.", "annotation_id": "31431047", "url": "https://genius.com/31431047", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "I just got ’em done, boy, don’t make me have to chip a nail", "vault_lines": ["I just got 'em done, boy, don't make me have to chip a nail"], "meaning": "Drake has been known to paint his nails. He’s saying that he just got them done, but now he’s about to get his hands dirty.\n\nDrake’s pink nail polish\n\nAlso could be a reaction to Pusha-T, who recently responded to a tweet claiming that no one had laid a finger on him in reference to his ongoing beef with Drake. Not even a pinky nail.", "annotation_id": "31431006", "url": "https://genius.com/31431006", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Uh, I was really, really tryna keep it PG", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake finishes the track with a double entendre. Parental Guidance, or “PG,” is a content rating given to a film that is suitable for anyone over the age of around 8 years-old. Drake wanted to keep his image clean and didn’t want things to get nasty – but now it’s Rated R.\n\n“PG” is also a reference to pgLang, a company specialized in music and visual media production founded by Kendrick Lamar and Dave Free in 2020. This could mean that Drake was trying keep the disses focused at Kendrick, but ended up having to reply to other artists such as Future, The Weeknd, and Rick Ross due to the shots they’ve been taking at Drake.", "annotation_id": "31430876", "url": "https://genius.com/31430876", "state": "accepted", "votes": 177, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Yeah, I’m the 6ix God, I’m the frontrunner", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm the 6ix God, I'm the frontrunner"], "meaning": "“6” refers to Drake’s hometown of Toronto, as “6” is short for Toronto area codes “416” and “647”, and also refers to Toronto’s six boroughs, being East York, Etobicoke, North York, Scarborough, Toronto, and York.\n\nThese lyrics are also a reference to Drake’s titular 2014 hit “6 God,” where he rapped:\n\nI’m the real 6 god, boy (Whoa)\n\nConsidering the following lines are shots at The Weeknd and his entourage, this is also likely aimed with him in mind. The Weeknd also originates from Toronto, however Drake asserts his dominance and claims he is top boy in the city, mocking The Weeknd for having to take second place.\n\nThe Weeknd and Drake", "annotation_id": "31430534", "url": "https://genius.com/31430534", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Like your label, boy, you in a scope right now", "vault_lines": ["Like your label, boy, you in a scope right now"], "meaning": "Wordplay between ‘in a scope’ and ‘Interscope’. Interscope Records is the label that Kendrick Lamar has been signed to since 2012. Drake is using this double entendre to describe how he is taking shots at Kendrick.\n\nIt’s valuable to note other rappers have used this double entendre previously–for example Eminem uses it in ‘Killshot’:\n\nSay you got me in a scope, but you grazed me\nI say one call to Interscope and you’re Swayze\"", "annotation_id": "31429552", "url": "https://genius.com/31429552", "state": "accepted", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Big difference ’tween Mike then and Mike now", "vault_lines": ["Big difference 'tween Mike then and Mike now"], "meaning": "With “First Person Shooter”, Drake tied Michael Jackson’s record as the male solo artist with the most No. 1s on the Billboard Hot 100 chart.\nIn the same song, Drake compares himself to Michael Jackson with the following lyrics:\n\nNiggas talkin' ‘bout when this gon’ be repeated\nWhat the fuck, bro? I’m one away from Michael\nNigga, beat it, nigga, beat it, what?\n\nIn this line, he is drawing the same comparison, but points out that Michael Jackson back then was peaceful, and did not diss nor involve himself in beef, but the new Mike (or in this case Drake) is ready for war.\n\nThis is also a reference to a line on “Like That” where Kendrick raps:\n\nYour best work is a light pack\nNigga, Prince outlived Mike Jack’, nigga, bum\n\nDrake is stating that like Michael Jackson has now passed, Kendrick’s career is past its prime.\n\nIt’s worth mentioning that “Mike now” could also be referring to Mike Tyson.", "annotation_id": "31430378", "url": "https://genius.com/31430378", "state": "accepted", "votes": 129, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "This ain’t even everything I know, don’t wake the demon up This ain’t even everything I know, don’t wake the demon up", "vault_lines": ["This ain't even everything I know, don't wake the demon up"], "meaning": "Drake uses this song as a warning shot towards everyone mentioned, claiming he has more information that he can unveil but has decided not to yet. This is similar to J. Cole’s warning shot to Kendrick Lamar on “7 Minute Drill” before he apologized.\n\nThis is merely a warning shot to back niggas down\n\nDrake took a similar stance with Pusha T in 2018 when they had their own back and forth. Drake stated in an interview that he had a response track made for Pusha T but did not release it due to it not being a track he wanted to be remembered by due to the information in the track.", "annotation_id": "31430199", "url": "https://genius.com/31430199", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Hugs and kisses, man, don’t tell me ’bout no switches", "vault_lines": ["Hugs and kisses, man, don't tell me 'bout no switches"], "meaning": "Hugs and kisses refers to The Weeknd’s label, XO. Drake is saying that Abel is too soft to be talking about violence and guns on a song as his label’s name is often interpreted as “hugs and kisses” rather than “ecstacy and oxycontin.”\n\nDrake is also referencing Kendrick’s diss on “Like That”:\n\nFuck sneak dissin', first-person shooter, I hope they came with three switches\n\nDrake responds to the diss by calling out Kendrick’s bluff, insinuating that Kendrick doesn’t have any more disses in him, a provocation that aims to elicit a response. Whilst using Kendrick’s bar about “switches,” Drake could also be making a double entendre about Abel “switching” up on Drake, having once been a close collaborator of his and even almost an OVO signee.\n\nThis corroborates Drake’s mindset about the diss, as on April 11, 2024 — two days before the leaking of this song — Drake replied to DJ Akademiks asking him if he had any diss ready:\n\nI’m ready and waiting, I’m tired of all these bluffin' podcasts callin' this nigga, he gotta show me smoke for me and only me and I’m right there", "annotation_id": "31430140", "url": "https://genius.com/31430140", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "This the bark with the bite, nigga, what’s up?", "vault_lines": ["This the bark with the bite, nigga, what's up?"], "meaning": "The phrase “all bark and no bite” is synonymous with cowardice and inaction. Drake makes it clear he’s ready for conflict. This is likely a response to Kendrick Lamar’s verse on Metro Boomin and Future’s “Like That”:\n\nOkay, let’s get it up, it’s time for him to prove that he’s a problem\n\nIt’s also likely a play on Drake’s 2023 studio release For All The Dogs, an album that Kenny subtly referenced multiple times on his verse:\n\nSay, it’s a lot of goofies with a check\n[…] Fuck sneak dissin', first-person shooter, I hope they came with three switches\n[…] ‘Fore all your dogs gettin’ buried\nThat’s a K with all these nines, he gon' see Pet Sematary (Yeah)\nNigga, bum", "annotation_id": "31430129", "url": "https://genius.com/31430129", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Pull your contract ’cause we gotta see the split The way you doin’ splits, bitch, your pants might rip", "vault_lines": ["Pull your contract 'cause we gotta see the split", "The way you doin' splits, bitch, your pants might rip"], "meaning": "In 2022, it was rumoured Drake signed to Universal Music Group for $500 million whilst being able to keep all his masters. Drake has seemingly confirmed this record deal in his song “Major Distribution”:\n\nFive-hundred million, just for Aubrey\n\nDrake states that other artists do not have his same freedom and privileges, making him curious to see their contracts to see how much their splits are.\n\nDrake also very cleverly plays on the homonym “split”, eliciting multiple meanings. Firstly, it refers back to Drake claiming Kendrick Lamar had to split 50% of his earnings with his previous label, Top Dawg Entertainment. This also refers to Kendrick splitting from Top Dawg in 2022, “pull[ing] his contract” from the label.\n\nFurthermore, on Drake’s May 2024 diss track “Family Matters”, he claimed Kendrick split from his fiancée, Whitney Alford. Kendrick would have to split 50% of his earnings with Whitney if they did separate, just like he supposedly did with Top Dawg.\n\nFinally, the splits is a gymnastics move which if performed too many times, can often cause your pants to rip, playing on the number of different “splits” Kendrick is figuratively performing.\n\nKendrick and Anthony Tiffith, founder of TDE", "annotation_id": "31430105", "url": "https://genius.com/31430105", "state": "accepted", "votes": 145, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "You ain’t in no big three, SZA got you wiped down Travis got you wiped down, Savage got you wiped down", "vault_lines": ["You ain't in no big three, SZA got you wiped down", "Travis got you wiped down, Savage got you wiped down"], "meaning": "On Drake’s 2023 J. Cole-assisted collaborative track “First Person Shooter,” J. Cole asked who the “G.O.A.T.” (greatest of all time) of the music industry is, between him, Drake, and Kendrick Lamar:\n\nIs it K-Dot? Is it Aubrey? Or me?\nWe the big three like we started a league, but right now, I feel like Muhammad Ali\n\nKendrick Lamar, however, in his feature on the controversial 2024 hit “Like That,” clapped back at both Cole and Drake, mocking For All The Dogs for being “the worst Drake album ever” (the album “First Person Shooter” was on):\n\nAh, yeah, huh, yeah, get up with me\nFuck sneak dissin', first-person shooter, I hope they came with three switches\nI crash out like, “Fuck rap,” diss Melle Mel if I had to\n[…]\nMotherfuck the big three, nigga, it’s just big me\nNigga, bum\n[…]\n‘Fore all your dogs gettin’ buried\nThat’s a K with all these nines, he gon' see Pet Sematary (Yeah)\n\nDrake states that fellow artists SZA, Travis Scott, and 21 Savage have all “wiped out” Kendrick (slang for destroying or passing something), likely alluding to Billboard placements.\n\nAs of the day that this song leaked (April 13, 2024), Kendrick’s most recent album, Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers, sold only 295,500 album-equivalent units, which has been surpassed by SZA’s SOS (2022)\n which sold 365,000 units, Travis Scott’s UTOPIA (2023) which sold 496,000 units, and Drake’s and 21 Savage’s collaborative Her Loss (2022) which sold 404,000 units.\n\nDrake may also be referring to the fact that the artists mentioned above have all made a #1 hit with him, including 2023’s “Slime You Out” with SZA, 2018’s “SICKO MODE” with Travis Scott, and 2022’s “Jimmy Cooks” with 21 Savage. It’s also worth noting that Drake and 21 also have a #2 hit titled “Rich Flex.”\n\nAdditionally, on April 13, 2024, SZA responded to her being name-dropped in the song with the iconic Nene Leakes meme, “Now, why am I in it?”\n\nThis may also be a subtle shot at J. Cole with Drake leaving him out of the big three conversation, which initially revolved around Cole, Kendrick and Drake. Two weeks after “Like That”, Cole dropped “7 Minute Drill” in response. However, Cole would go on to retract the track not even 48 hours later during his performance at Dreamville Festival 2024, and even removed “7 Minute Drill” from streaming services a week later. Drake may have seen this as such a weak move that Cole does not warrant a place in the Big Three anymore.", "annotation_id": "31430101", "url": "https://genius.com/31430101", "state": "accepted", "votes": 288, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Numbers-wise, I’m out of here, you not fuckin’ creepin’ up Money-wise, I’m out of here, you not fuckin’ sneakin’ up", "vault_lines": ["Numbers-wise, I'm out of here, you not fuckin' creepin' up", "Money-wise, I'm out of here, you not fuckin' sneakin' up"], "meaning": "Drake responds to Kendrick Lamar’s “Like That” diss:\n\nD-O-T, the money, power, respect\nThe last one is better\n\nDrake states that he has much more power (numbers) and money than Kendrick Lamar, and it isn’t even close. Drake is reportedly worth upwards of 200 million dollars, whereas Kendrick Lamar is valued at 75 million dollars, less than half of Drake’s valuation.\n\nNumbers wise — at the time of the leaking of the song — Drake accumulated more than 80 million monthly Spotify listeners compared to Kendrick’s 50 million. Additionally, Drake’s It’s All a Blur tour beat Kendrick’s Big Steppers Tour for the highest grossing hip hop tour of all time, further corroborating Drake’s boast.\n\nThis is also a shot at The Weeknd, who has a hit song “Creepin'” with Metro Boomin' and 21 Savage.", "annotation_id": "31429992", "url": "https://genius.com/31429992", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Rolling Loud stage, y’all were turnt, that was slick as hell Shit’ll probably change if your BM start to kiss and tell", "vault_lines": ["Rolling Loud stage, y'all were turnt, that was slick as hell", "Shit'll probably change if your BM start to kiss and tell"], "meaning": "Drake references the preview Metro Boomin and Future showed at Rolling Loud of their track ‘Like That’. The version previewed was an early version of the track, not featuring the Kendrick verse dissing Drake.\n\nOn the original demo of the song, there’s a slight change in the lines:\n\nRolling Loud stage, y'all were turnt, that was slick as hell\nLucky that your dawg lil' BM doesn’t kiss and tell\n\n“Kiss and Tell” is also a reference to Ciara’s 2015 hit. Ciara was famously Future’s ex-fiancé, and the two had a child as well. However, their engagement was called off after Ciara suspected Future was cheating on her.\n\nDrake references how Ciara was one of his Baby Mamas.", "annotation_id": "31429969", "url": "https://genius.com/31429969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 101, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "I’m at the top of the mountain, so you tight now Just to have this talk with your ass, I had to hike down", "vault_lines": ["I'm at the top of the mountain, so you tight now", "Just to have this talk with your ass, I had to hike down"], "meaning": "With 13 number one hits, Drake is easily hip hop’s biggest artist, hence being at the top. Drizzy’s stature makes rappers jealous therefore being ‘tight’ with Drake.\n\nFrom there Drake uses a double entendre to first say that he has to ‘hike down’ the mountain to diss these rappers, but also making fun of Kendrick Lamar’s 5'5 height.", "annotation_id": "31429949", "url": "https://genius.com/31429949", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "I be with some bodyguards like Whitney", "vault_lines": ["I be with some bodyguards like Whitney"], "meaning": "To flex his security, Drake likens himself to pop icon and actress Whitney Houston, who starred in 1992 romance film The Bodyguard. This is in response to how Kendrick Lamar invoked his bodyguard on “Like That” when he rapped:\n\nGot 2TEEZ with me, I’m snatchin' chains and burnin' tattoos, it’s up\n\nThis could also be a jab at Whitney Alford, the wife of Kendrick Lamar. She’s featured on the cover of his 2022 album Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers, guarded by Lamar and his concealed gun:\n\n\n\n The cover art of Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers featuring Kendrick Lamar, his wife Whitney Alford, and their children.\n\nConsidering that the album, mocked earlier in the verse, sees Lamar delve into his struggles with infidelity, this line may simultaneously be a veiled allegation of unfaithfulness against Lamar’s wife.\n\nThis wouldn’t be the first time Drake’s mentioned someone’s wife in a diss. On May 2018’s “Duppy Freestyle,” he made a joke out of Virgina Williams, the wife of his then-target Pusha T:\n\nI told you keep playin' with my name\nAnd I'ma let it ring on you like Virginia Williams\n\nAdditionally, on May 2024’s diss track “Family Matters”, Drake claimed Kendrick physically abused Whitney. Drake may be foreshadowing this diss here, claiming Whitney needs bodyguards to protect herself from Kendrick.", "annotation_id": "31429858", "url": "https://genius.com/31429858", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Jets, whips, chains, wicked, wicked, wicked (Wicked, wicked)", "vault_lines": ["Jets, whips, chains, wicked, wicked, wicked (Wicked, wicked)"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Future’s 2016 song “Wicked,” produced by Metro Boomin. Specifically, Drake repeats the word “wicked” similar to Future’s flow on the chorus of that song:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3gt-rxxs-RQ&t=14", "annotation_id": "31429845", "url": "https://genius.com/31429845", "state": "accepted", "votes": 122, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Your last one bricked, you really not on shit They make excuses for you ’cause they hate to see me lit", "vault_lines": ["Your last one bricked, you really not on shit", "They make excuses for you 'cause they hate to see me lit"], "meaning": "Drake goes after Kendrick Lamar’s album Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers stating that it did not do well in the long run. At the time of this song’s release, Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers was Kendrick’s latest album and was met with mixed feedback from people as they felt the album was not as good as his previous work. Before apologizing to Kendrick Lamar, J. Cole attacked Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers on his song “7 Minute Drill”.\n\nyour last shit was tragic\n\nThis caused a high amount of controversy on social media with a high amount of mixed opinions on the album. According to the numbers, Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers is Kendrick Lamar’s shortest charting album on the Billboard 200 “only” being on the charts for 70 weeks before departing.", "annotation_id": "31429844", "url": "https://genius.com/31429844", "state": "accepted", "votes": 121, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "You won’t ever take no chain off of us How the fuck you big steppin’ with a size-seven men’s on?", "vault_lines": ["You won't ever take no chain off of us", "How the fuck you big steppin' with a size-seven men's on?"], "meaning": "Drake directly responds to Kendrick Lamar, who threatened to snatch his chain on “Like That” from WE DON’T TRUST YOU by Future & Metro Boomin when he rapped:\n\nGot 2TEEZ with me, I’m snatchin' chains and burnin' tattoos, it’s up\n\nDrake makes a punchline out of Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers, the title of Lamar’s 2022 release that he slanders later on. Drake jokes that Lamar can’t be a “big stepper” with such a presumably small shoe size due to his short height of roughly 5'5.\n\n The height difference between Drake, who is roughly 6'0\", and Kendrick Lamar.\n\nThe song’s official cover art is in direct reference to this joke, it being a shoe tag displaying a US mens size 7:\n\n\n\nOn an Instagram post by media network Complex discussing the diss, Los Angeles sneaker boutique RIF LA came to Lamar’s defense in the comment section, clarifying that his actual shoe size is 8.5-9 mens:\n\nLol I’ve given Kdot dozens of free shoes and he’s a size 8.5-9 mens. One time he squeezed into a 9.5 😂 Facts only\n\nDrake doubles down on the short jokes later by calling Kendrick “pipsqueak” and a “little midget.”  This is similar to how J. Cole attacked Kendrick on “7 Minute Drill,” his now retracted diss track wherein he too used Kendrick’s short stature to demean him:\n\nGet a nigga stretched if I feel the disrespect, uh\nYour arms might be too short to box with the god", "annotation_id": "31429831", "url": "https://genius.com/31429831", "state": "accepted", "votes": 197, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "And when you boys got rich, you had to run from it Cash blowin’ Abel bread, out here trickin’ (Out here trickin’) Shit we do for bitches, he doin’ for niggas (What the fuck?)", "vault_lines": ["And when you boys got rich, you had to run from it", "Cash blowin' Abel bread, out here trickin' (Out here trickin')", "Shit we do for bitches, he doin' for niggas (What the fuck?)"], "meaning": "The first line is directed towards The Weeknd, who has resided in New York and Los Angeles since he became successful, most recently buying a $70M house in Bel Air, Los Angeles, unlike Drake who has stayed put in Toronto all these years.\n\nDrake then goes after CashXO who is The Weeknd’s manager, stating that he is giving out high amounts of The Weeknd’s money to men in the form of “tricking”, which describes when rich people fly women out and buy them extravagant gifts in exchange for sex.\n\nDrake also mentions the high amount of features The Weeknd gave Future & Metro Boomin’s WE DON’T TRUST YOU and WE STILL DON’T TRUST YOU. Across the two albums, The Weeknd is featured on “Young Metro”, “We Still Don’t Trust You”, “All to Myself” and “Always Be My Fault”. Drake states with the amount of features The Weeknd has given to Future and Metro Boomin, he’s generating a high amount of money for CashXO, Future and Metro Boomin as the tracks will likely gain high attraction due to his featuring.", "annotation_id": "31429808", "url": "https://genius.com/31429808", "state": "accepted", "votes": 142, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "You pussies can’t get booked outside America for nan’ I’m out in Tokyo because I’m big in Japan I’m the hitmaker y’all depend on Backstage in my city, it was friendzone", "vault_lines": ["You pussies can't get booked outside America for nan'", "I'm out in Tokyo because I'm big in Japan", "I'm the hitmaker y'all depend on", "Backstage in my city, it was friendzone"], "meaning": "Drake claims the people that are against him are only known in America whilst he is global, with his name reaching to Japan. At the time of this song, Drake had seventy-seven top ten songs and sixteen top ten albums, with thirteen being number one songs and thirteen being number one albums on the Billboard Hot 100 & Billboard 200 charts.\n\nOn the original demo of the song, there’s a slight change in the lines:\n\nI’m the hitmaker y'all depend on\nTough on record; in my city, it was friend zone\n\nDrake says that people rely on him and without him many other artists would not be getting the same level of attention or streams as they are getting now. However, Drake states that behind closed doors some people who throw shots at him are friendly to him despite the beef they portray to the public.\n\nThis line also references Alphaville’s 80s hit “Big in Japan.”\n\nHowever, this may also be alluding to a girl known as Just Tokyo, who is rumoured to be the girl Drake and Future are beefing over.\n\nIn September 2023, she was at Drake’s It’s All A Blur Tour in Miami, spotted backstage. Months later in March 2024, she posted a picture of herself backstage at Future’s Rolling Loud show, and has been linked romantically to Future for years. Just Tokyo is Japanese, so by Drake saying he’s “big in Japan”, he may be insinuating that he slept with her.\n\nJapan is actually Drake’s worst market commercially, and he has never performed in Japan. Therefore, this lends credence to the fact Drake is talking about Just Tokyo, and is another one of Drake’s famous subliminals.\n\nJust Tokyo repping Future’s merch at Rolling Loud", "annotation_id": "31429780", "url": "https://genius.com/31429780", "state": "accepted", "votes": 186, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Extortion baby, whole career, you been shook up ’Cause Top told you, “Drop and give me fifty,” like some push-ups, huh", "vault_lines": ["Extortion baby, whole career, you been shook up", "'Cause Top told you, \"Drop and give me fifty,\" like some push-ups, huh", "Top say drop, you better drop and give 'em fifty", "Ayy, better drop and give me fifty, ayy", "Drop and give me fifty, drop and give me fifty, ayy", "Drop and give me fifty, all you fuck niggas teamin' up"], "meaning": "“Drop and give me fifty” is a double entendre. Firstly, it references a viral 2023 video of rapper Kendrick Lamar doing “prison push-ups” outside. Secondly, it refers to Kendrick “dropping” music and having to give 50 percent of his earnings to his former label, Top Dawg Entertainment.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/yNWDQpYBxic?feature=shared&t=4\n\nWhile the specifics of Lamar’s previous deal with the label aren’t public, he signed his record deal in 2004 at the age of 16, thanks in part to his father’s good will with label CEO Anthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith. Though Lamar has mythologized their story as a divine coincidence, Drake alleges that Lamar left out the less flattering detail of how Lamar’s deal made him split half the earnings of his music with Tiffith. It should be noted that Lamar left his label on good terms in 2022 after dropping five studio albums and various mixtapes.\n\nNote how Drake punctuates the phrase with a “huh” adlib the first time he says it. This interpolates that of “Silent Hill” from Lamar’s 2022 release Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers, a project that Drake mocks later.\n\nThe phrase is also an interpolation of Mike Jones’ 2007 record, “Drop & Gimme 50.” The way Drake repeats the line in the chorus and the similar tempo of the song’s beat are similar to Jones' song.", "annotation_id": "31429750", "url": "https://genius.com/31429750", "state": "accepted", "votes": 206, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "What the fuck is this, a twenty-v-one, nigga?", "vault_lines": ["What the fuck is this, a twenty-v-one, nigga?"], "meaning": "Drake notes how lopsided his beef with Kendrick Lamar has become, as the decade-long conflict has grown to involve Metro Boomin, Future, A$AP Rocky, and The Weeknd, all big names who took shots at Drake with Lamar on Future & Metro’s 2024 WE DON’T TRUST YOU duology. Shots also came from Rick Ross, addressed later in the song.\n\nAfter “Push Ups” leaked on April 13, 2024, Drake posted an Instagram story of the iconic scene from the 2003 film Kill Bill where character Beatrix Kiddo, played by Uma Thurman, fends off an army of 88 men on her own, likely in reference to the “twenty-v-one” sentiment:", "annotation_id": "31429690", "url": "https://genius.com/31429690", "state": "accepted", "votes": 185, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "I could never be nobody number-one fan Your first number one, I had to put it in your hand", "vault_lines": ["I could never be nobody number-one fan", "Your first number one, I had to put it in your hand"], "meaning": "Drake responds to Future, who seemed to go after Drake on “We Don’t Trust You,” the title track of Future’s 2024 collab album with Metro Boomin where he rapped:\n\nYou a nigga number-one fan, dog\nSneak dissin', I don’t understand, dog\nPillowtalkin', actin' like a fed, dog\nI don’t need another fake friend, dog\nCan’t be ‘bout a ho, 'cause we sharin’, dog\nIn your feelings, nigga, why you playin', dog?\n\nDrake claps back by insisting that he was the one who granted Future his first song to chart number one, which is true: Drake’s “Way 2 Sexy” from his 2021 project Certified Lover Boy was the first song featuring Future to top the Billboard 100.\n\nThis also invokes Drake’s reputation as one of the most commercially influential feature artists in popular music history, as he’s helped elevate 33 artists to their highest charting position in the 2010’s and given six artists their first song to chart #1. In the same vein, Drake later insists that he was the sole reason Rick Ross charted in the prime of his career:\n\nCan’t believe he jumpin' in, this nigga turnin' fifty\nEvery song that made it on the chart, he got from Drizzy", "annotation_id": "31429652", "url": "https://genius.com/31429652", "state": "accepted", "votes": 275, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "And you gon’ feel the aftermath of what I write down", "vault_lines": ["And you gon' feel the aftermath of what I write down"], "meaning": "Continuing from the previous label reference, Drake plays on Kendrick being signed to Dr. Dre’s Aftermath, stating that Kendrick will deal with the consequences of his pen.", "annotation_id": "31429636", "url": "https://genius.com/31429636", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "[Verse 1]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "These verses leaked in two versions; the final CDQ version transcribed below and the original demo version. While both versions of both verses are the same for the most part, there are slight differences. Namely, the filler words for the following bars differ:\n\nI’m the hit maker y'all depend on\nTough on record; in my city, it was friend zone\n[…]\nRolling Loud stage, y'all were turnt, that was slick as hell\nLucky that your dawg lil' BM doesn’t kiss and tell\n[…]\nEvery song that made it on the chart, he got from Drizzy\nWorry ‘bout whatever going on with you and (Puff)\n[…]\nI know why you mad, nigga, I ain’t even trippin’\nI ain’t even rapping after this, I’m way too busy\n\nThe full lyrics page for the original demo version of the song can be found here.", "annotation_id": "31429622", "url": "https://genius.com/31429622", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Get more love in the city that you from, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Get more love in the city that you from, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake claims that he is appreciated and respected more by the people of Compton, California – Kendrick Lamar’s hometown – more than Kendrick himself is, making the implication that Drake’s influence is far-reaching and has reached such an extent that Kendrick is rendered second in importance amongst the people around him.\n\nKendrick has discussed in various songs his belief that he is a leader in Compton, and that he has been entrusted with the responsibility of serving that role in his community. One core theme of his 2015 album To Pimp A Butterfly, in particular, was his struggle to live up to his community’s expectations. This was especially conveyed within his internal diatribe on the song “u” through his display of vicious self-hatred for not being able to satisfy everyone in Compton, not even the people he cares about, while he is blessed with copious amounts of fame, success, and appreciation on a global level:\n\nWhere was your antennas?\nWhere was the influence you speak of?\nYou preached in front of one-hunnid-thousand but never reached her\nI fuckin' tell you fuckin' failure—you ain’t no leader!\nI never liked you, forever despise you—I don’t need ya!\n\nThis sentiment was further touched upon in Kendrick’s 2022 single “The Heart Part 5”, during which he expresses fondness for  Compton and conveys his desire to be appreciated by those who remain there, as well as future generations, for what he has done as a musician to inspire and benefit them:\n\n(I want, I want, I want, I want)\nBut I want you to want me too (I want, I want, I want, I want)\nI want the hood to want me back (I want, I want, I want, I want)\nI want the hood\nLook what I done for you (Look what I done for you)\nLook what I done for you\n\nDrake has, in the past, visited Compton. In 2015, he and the Game, another rapper from the city who mentored Kendrick prior to him gaining mainstream recognition in the rap industry, filmed a music video there for their song “100”. Interestingly, the Game is seen wearing a Toronto Blue Jays jersey in the video. Given that the Game has been a long-time ally of Kendrick, the fact that he had once given Drake appreciation and went as far as to wear merchandise representing Drake’s hometown baseball team, this could lend further credence to the claim that Drake’s influence has spread as far as Compton.", "annotation_id": "31429555", "url": "https://genius.com/31429555", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "What’s a prince to a king? He a son, nigga", "vault_lines": ["What's a prince to a king? He a son, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake references his and Kendrick Lamar’s prior comparisons to two megastars, being Michael Jackson and Prince.\n\nDrake had previously compared himself to Michael Jackson on “First Person Shooter,” where he raps:\n\nWhat the fuck, bro? I’m one away from Michael\nNigga, beat it, nigga, beat it, what?\n\nIn a similar fashion, Kendrick likened himself to Prince on “Mask Off (Remix)”:\n\nShe said she broke down when Prince died (It’s alright)\nBitch, my hair down, Prince live through me (I am Prince)\n\nDrake then acknowledges Kendrick Lamar’s bar from Future and Metro Boomin’s track, “Like That” from just three weeks prior, where Kendrick raps:\n\nYour best work is a light pack\nNigga, Prince outlived Mike Jack’, nigga, bum\n\nKendrick is saying that Prince—who passed away in 2016—outlived Michael Jackson—who passed away in 2009—implying that Drake will not live as long as him. Additionally—despite Michael’s much more broader mainstream appeal—many critics saw Prince to be the much more subversive, artistic, and risk-taking artist of the duo which Kendrick uses to compare his and Drakes music.\n\nDrake claps back at this comment by referring to himself as a “king\"—as Michael Jackson’s nickname was “The King of Pop”—and Kendrick Lamar as a “prince,” given Lamar’s prior comparisons to Prince. Drake points out that a King—being the father of a prince—has more power than his son, essentially calling Kendrick his bitch.\n\nMichael Jackson also has a son named Prince Jackson.", "annotation_id": "31429512", "url": "https://genius.com/31429512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 319, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "Metro, shut your ho ass up and make some drums, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Metro, shut your ho ass up and make some drums, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake goes after Metro Boomin after tension between the two have been rising for months. At the time of this song, Drake’s last produced record by Metro Boomin was “More M’s” with 21 Savage, which released over one and a half years prior. On the track, Drake reportedly dissed Metro Boomin in his verse on the track with multiple subliminals.\n\nNiggas ain’t got love for the boy, so they fake it\nCrack a couple jokes to some bitches on some snake shit\n\nOver the following months, Drake and Metro Boomin made more subliminals towards each other. On December 3, 2023, Metro Boomin made a tweet attacking Her Loss for winning awards over HEROES & VILLAINS despite HEROES & VILLAINS having higher streaming numbers in which Drake responded the same day with an Instagram story and by unfollowing Metro Boomin on Instagram. On January 15, 2024, a few days after 21 Savage released american dream, Drake posted a song off the album to his Instagram story but covered Metro Boomin’s name. On March 22, 2024, Future & Metro Boomin released their first collaborative album WE DON’T TRUST YOU which had many subliminal disses towards Drake but the main diss being “Like That” featuring Kendrick Lamar in which Kendrick Lamar took multiple shots at Drake.\n\nSay, it’s a lot of goofies with a check\n[…]\nFuck sneak dissin', first-person shooter, I hope they came with three switches\n[…]\nMotherfuck the big three, nigga, it’s just big me\n[…]\n‘Fore all your dogs gettin’ buried\nThat’s a K with all these nines, he gon' see Pet Sematary\n\nIn the days after “Push Ups” leaked, Drake trolled Metro with a series of Instagram stories. One had Metro’s face superimposed onto a clip of the 2002 film Drumline, another showed a marching band playing outside a venue where Metro had promoted WE STILL DON’T TRUST YOU, and a third showcased a Merengue remix (though, with Mexican Huapango dancers) of this line.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/XXL/status/1779996874293883375\n\nhttps://x.com/nfr_podcast/status/1780231596592787477\n\nhttps://x.com/raptalksk/status/1780223093543178253", "annotation_id": "31429507", "url": "https://genius.com/31429507", "state": "accepted", "votes": 233, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "You better do that motherfuckin’ show inside the bity Maroon 5 need a verse, you better make it witty Then we need a verse for the Swifties", "vault_lines": ["You better do that motherfuckin' show inside the bity", "Maroon 5 need a verse, you better make it witty", "Then we need a verse for the Swifties"], "meaning": "Drake clowns Kendrick Lamar for catering to the mainstream by making reference to his features on “Bad Blood (Remix)” and “Don’t Wanna Know” by mainstream pop acts Taylor Swift and Maroon 5 respectively. “Swifties” is the nickname of the former’s fanbase.\n\n\n\nKendrick Lamar and Taylor Swift in the music video for “Bad Blood (Remix.)”\n\nFrom Drake’s perspective, this is important to point out since Kendrick is widely considered to be the savior of “real” hip hop while Drake’s music has been criticized for prioritizing mainstream appeal over quality. Drake implies that Lamar is just as prone to musical inauthenticity as any other artist signed to a record label, saying that Kendrick will submit to the wishes of “top,” shorthand for the CEO of Kendrick’s former record label Anthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith, when demanded.\n\nExemplifying this, Drake suggests Kendrick’s label would force him to perform “in the bity” or cities with a high population of Blood gang members despite Kendrick’s documented indifference to the gang affiliations that pervaded his youth.\n\nThis jab applies to Future as well, as he collaborated with Swift on “End Game” and Maroon 5 on “Cold.”\n\nDrake praised Swift’s status in the music industry a while back on “Red Button” from his 2023 release For All The Dogs Scary Hours Edition when he rapped:\n\nTaylor Swift the only nigga that I ever rated\nOnly one could make me drop the album just a little later\n\nIronically, Drake would formally release “Push Ups” on April 19, 2024, the same day Swift released THE TORTURED POETS DEPARTMENT, her eleventh studio album. Even more ironically, Drake and Swift were featured on a November 2016 commercial for Apple Music where Drake sang “Bad Blood:”\nhttps://youtu.be/86D7AGm5sHk?si=xKirtBP_cG_jOSUg&t=13", "annotation_id": "31429450", "url": "https://genius.com/31429450", "state": "accepted", "votes": 218, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "line": "And that fuckin’ song y’all got did not start the beef with us This shit been brewin’ in a pot, now I’m heatin’ up I don’t care what Cole think, that Dot shit was weak as fuck", "vault_lines": ["And that fuckin' song y'all got did not start the beef with us", "This shit been brewin' in a pot, now I'm heatin' up", "I don't care what Cole think, that Dot shit was weak as fuck"], "meaning": "Drake clarifies that the shots thrown at him and J. Cole by “Dot” or Kendrick Lamar on “Like That” were not the impetus of their beef. This gives credence to the history of subliminal shots between Drake and Lamar tracing all the way back to 2013 where Kendrick famously declared war against Drake and the rest of his peers by name on “Control:”\n\nI’m usually homeboys with the same niggas I’m rhymin' with\nBut this is hip-hop, and them niggas should know what time it is\nAnd that goes for Jermaine Cole, […] Drake […]\nI got love for you all, but I’m tryna murder you niggas\n\nDrake insists that tension between him and Lamar has been brewing since then. That tension would pressure J. Cole to drop “7 Minute Drill,” a diss track in response to Lamar where he controversially insisted that Lamar’s 2015 sophomore album To Pimp a Butterfly was overrated:\n\nYour first shit was classic, your last shit was tragic\nYour second shit put niggas to sleep but they gassed it\n\nCole would go on to retract these lines not even 48 hours later during his performance at Dreamville Festival 2024, admitting that he was denying the greatness of Lamar’s discography:\n\nI want to say tonight, in the midst of me […] tryna find a lil angle and downplay this nigga’s fucking catalog and his greatness, how many people think Kendrick Lamar is one of the greatest motherfuckers to ever touch a microphone? Dreamville, y'all love Kendrick Lamar, correct? As do I.\n\nAfter then indicting the diss track as “the lamest shit [he’s] ever did in [his] fucking life,” Cole deleted it from streaming platforms a week later. Despite this, Drake insists that Cole was right to call To Pimp a Butterfly a weak record.", "annotation_id": "31429414", "url": "https://genius.com/31429414", "state": "accepted", "votes": 187, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Push Ups", "status": "done", "targets": 49, "clicked": 49, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-push-ups-lyrics", "genius_title": "Push Ups"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Drake", "line": "I was, born to do it, born to make bomb music I flow tight like I was born Jewish Well, actually I was born Jewish I guess at this point you could say I was born foolish", "vault_lines": ["I was, born to do it, born to make bomb music", "I flow tight like I was born Jewish", "Well, actually I was born Jewish", "I guess at this point you could say I was born foolish"], "meaning": "This is a reference to a Jay line off the Kanye-produced “This Can’t Be Life.”\n\nhttps://genius.com/33286\n\nDrake is Jewish and Jews are stereo typically a frugal people. Though by the way he has been spending his money he believes he might as well be born “foolish” for blowing so much cash.", "annotation_id": "347617", "url": "https://genius.com/347617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Drake", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 1, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-still-drake-lyrics", "genius_title": "Still Drake"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "“Fuck it”, like ”YOLO”", "vault_lines": ["That's just me on my solo like \"Fuck it\", like \"YOLO\""], "meaning": "Drake drops the term, YOLO, a phrase he popularised on his 2011 hit single, The Motto featuring Lil Wayne. The acronym stands for You Only Live Once and basically means that one should not be afraid to go for their goals or do the things they want to do, because we only have one life to do it.", "annotation_id": "36643168", "url": "https://genius.com/36643168", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "You don’t have to prove shit to no one except yourself", "vault_lines": ["You don't have to prove shit to no one except yourself"], "meaning": "This line can also be interpreted as\n‘ You don’t have to prove shit to know and accept yourself’ or ‘You don’t have to prove shit to noone, accept yourself’", "annotation_id": "36643096", "url": "https://genius.com/36643096", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Get hype on tracks and jump in front of a bullet you wasn’t meant for ’Cause you don’t really wanna hear me vent more Hot temper, scary outcome Here’s a reason for niggas that’s hatin’ without one That always let they mouth run Bench players talkin’ like starters, I hate it", "vault_lines": ["Get hype on tracks and jump in front of a bullet you wasn't meant for", "'Cause you don't really wanna hear me vent more", "Hot temper, scary outcome", "Here's a reason for niggas that's hatin' without one", "That always let they mouth run", "Bench players talkin' like starters, I hate it"], "meaning": "These lines along with the last two lines are likely a response to one of Drake’s rap rivals Pusha T, who threw shots at Drake on a freestyle of Drake’s “Dreams Money Can Buy” titled Don’t Fuck With Me (2011) plus more shots at Drake and his mentor Lil Wayne on Exodus 23:1 (2012).\n\nPusha T shots at Drake on Don’t Fuck With Me:\n\nRappers on their sophomores, acting like they boss lords.\nFame such a funny thing for sure.\nWhen niggas start believing all them encores.\nI’m just the one to send you off, bonjour\n\nPusha T shots at Lil Wayne and Drake on Exodus 23:1 :\n\nContract all fucked up\nI guess that means you all fucked up\nYou signed to one nigga that signed to another nigga\nThat’s signed to three niggas, now that’s bad luck\nDamn that shit even the odds now\nYou better off selling this hard now\nYou call it living out your dreams\nYou can’t fly without your wings\n\n Pusha T’s 2011 freestyle was released one month before Drake’s sophomore album Take Care hit stores.\n\nLil Wayne responded to the 2012 Exodus 23:1 diss with the track titled Goulish. The two rappers have been at odds since 2006.\n\nDrake and Pusha T would finally go head to head with diss tracks directed at one another 6 years after the release of this song in 2018.  Drake’s Duppy Freestyle and Pusha T’s The Story of Adidon.", "annotation_id": "31653471", "url": "https://genius.com/31653471", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Born a perfectionist, guess that makes me a bit obsessive", "vault_lines": ["Born a perfectionist, guess that makes me a bit obsessive"], "meaning": "Here’s an example: This track is exactly 6 minutes and 6 seconds long. Coincidence or intentional?", "annotation_id": "19189127", "url": "https://genius.com/19189127", "state": "pending", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Started from the bottom, now we here, nigga, we made it", "vault_lines": ["We keep it thorough, nigga", "Started from the bottom, now we here, nigga, we made it"], "meaning": "This is a reference to “Started From the Bottom,” the first single from Drake’s 2013 album Nothing Was the Same, which “Furthest Thing” also appears on. This line also interpolates “Started From the Bottom”’s chorus:\n\nStarted from the bottom, now we’re here\nStarted from the bottom, now my whole team fuckin' here\n\nDrake has received criticism for claiming to have once been living with very little money because of his early involvement with the television program Degrassi: The Next Generation. However, the rapper debunked claims that he didn’t start from the bottom in an April 13, 2013 interview with East Village Radio:\n\nPeople like to build their own story about my life. I don’t know if it makes them feel better, or if it makes it okay for them to not like me, but the last thing I grew up as was rich. I had rich friends, but they weren’t giving me their money. I’m just not the type of guy to go ‘No, no, no, I’m not rich.’ People can say whatever they want about me, though. If they really want to learn them, the facts are out there. But I guess it’s easier on their hearts if I didn’t have to struggle, and makes it easier to not like me. It is what it is.", "annotation_id": "19121985", "url": "https://genius.com/19121985", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "And I wouldn’t change a thing if you paid me Now real nigga, wassup? Ayy, yeah, ayy", "vault_lines": ["And I wouldn't change a thing if you paid me", "Now real nigga, wassup?"], "meaning": "This is a nod to Drake’s November 2011 hit, “The Motto,” which also features Young Money rapper and Drake’s mentor, Lil Wayne. On the song, Drake ends his verse with:\n\nI can almost guarantee she know the deal, real nigga, what’s up?\n\nHe also says a similar line on the chorus:\n\nCan’t see ‘em 'cause the money in the way, real nigga, what’s up?", "annotation_id": "17482091", "url": "https://genius.com/17482091", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "I sip the Pora", "vault_lines": ["I sip the Pora"], "meaning": "As in “Barbaresco Pora Riserva” which is an expensive Italian wine", "annotation_id": "2229234", "url": "https://genius.com/2229234", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "[Produced by Noah “40” Shebib]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The production for this track is remarkable; as Drake’s longtime friend/producer 40 sampled Whitney Houston’s “I Have Nothing” not once, not twice, but three times for this intro track. Not only did he flip it various times, he literally flipped it; reversing Whitney Houston’s vocals. Although each verse gets its own beat, and they all sound different, they mesh together perfectly to create a powerful anthem that Drake could rap on for an hour.\n\nThe breakdown to this production all lies within the sample. Take a listen to this (apologies for the lower pitch; Soundcloud won’t let us play a snippet of “Tuscan Leather” at the original pitch) to further understand the concept behind the sampling mechanism used by 40. In this audio, you will hear two excerpts from “Tuscan Leather,” followed by the same excerpts reversed, and finally, the sampled portion from the original Whitney Houston track.\n\nhttps://soundcloud.com/producergenius/breakdown-of-drakes-tuscan-2", "annotation_id": "2223670", "url": "https://genius.com/2223670", "state": "accepted", "votes": 183, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "If there’s hell below, I’ll see you when you get there Are you enjoyin’ yourselves? If we may, we would just like to close off with somethin’ a bit inspirational Hopefully something a bit relevant as to us all are having the same fears, shedding similar tears, and of course dying in so many years It don’t mean that we can’t have a good life So we’d like to just maybe close out with something, some food for thought, for all of us", "vault_lines": ["you know you see the necklace", "Past the present when you have to mention", "If there's hell below, I'll see you when you get there", "Are you enjoyin' yourselves?", "If we may, we would just like to close off with somethin' a bit inspirational", "It don't mean that we can't have a good life", "So we'd like to just maybe close out with something, some food for thought, for all of us"], "meaning": "This is a quote from soul legend Curtis Mayfield, from a 1987 performance in Montreux.  Watch the whole gig here\n\n\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_embedded&v=zGOeH4LWp-U#t=3453\n\nBut Drake could be digging deeper here. What if this is the last song? When it says “ If we may we’d just like to close off with something a bit inspirational…” What if the album was made like this intentionally. On the way you see that really, “All Me” begins it with him basically saying “All I’ve accomplished I did it with my team.” And working his ways to Pound Cake, The Language, and Started From the Bottom. Drake could be saying “Hey, we are going to start the album from the bottom, the last song will be the first.” And so on to end with an amazing song produced by 40 flipped three times and Drake catching tempos, while also coming full circle with his image, artistry and impact he may leave on hip hop. Alas, we may never truly know.", "annotation_id": "2215465", "url": "https://genius.com/2215465", "state": "accepted", "votes": 189, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Owl chains like credentials,", "vault_lines": ["Owl chains like credentials,"], "meaning": "Drake knows more than a little bit about academic credentials – his mother is a teacher, after all\n\n\n\nThe “owl chain” is a reference to the logo of Drake’s label OVO sound\n\n\n\nNote that this line also sounds like “I don’t change like credentials.”", "annotation_id": "2214888", "url": "https://genius.com/2214888", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Rap like this for all of my borough niggas", "vault_lines": ["Rap like this for all of my borough niggas"], "meaning": "“The borough” is short for Scarborough, a part of Toronto. Scarborough is known as a hard, gritty neighborhood particularly susceptible to crime.  Drizzy recently raised a bunch of money for the neighborhood’s Boys and Girls Club with a charity volleyball game", "annotation_id": "2214701", "url": "https://genius.com/2214701", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "I could go a hour on this beat, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I could go a hour on this beat, nigga"], "meaning": "He can definitely do that. He sometimes switches his flow many times in just one track. Here’s a few lines where he talks about his ability to change up his flows:\n\n“Where to Now”\n\nI switch flows much as my girl switch purses\"\n\n“AFT Radio Freestyle\n\nAight check, flow switch, Flow switch\n\nAlso, word is that this song was originally 12 minutes long, which means that Drake could definitely keep the flows coming for a whole hour.", "annotation_id": "2214640", "url": "https://genius.com/2214640", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Tell me who dissin’, I got some things that’ll hit the culprit Them strep throat flows, them shits to stop all of the talkin’", "vault_lines": ["Tell me who dissin', I got some things that'll hit the culprit", "Them strep throat flows, them shits to stop all of the talkin'"], "meaning": "Give Drake the name of the person still talking smack, and he’ll hit him with straight BARS.\n\nA number of different rappers have beefed with Drizzy over the past few years, including Common, Pusha T, and Chris Brown.\n\nStrep throat is a sore throat with a fever that makes it harder for the person talking to speak. Drake’s flow will shut the person up as if they had strep throat.", "annotation_id": "2214304", "url": "https://genius.com/2214304", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "I just set the bar, niggas fall under it like a limbo", "vault_lines": ["I just set the bar, niggas fall under it like a limbo"], "meaning": "Setting the bar is a commonly used idiom, meaning to set the standard. It’s easy for Drake to set the standard and his competition are under the bar he is setting like they are playing a game of limbo\n\n\n\nConsidering the Memento reference just before, “limbo” might also have a double meaning: the dream space, or “limbo” in the movie Inception (both movies are directed by Christopher Nolan), is where you believe dreams are reality. These other rappers are dreaming if they think they can match Drizzy.", "annotation_id": "2213986", "url": "https://genius.com/2213986", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "How much time is this nigga spendin’ on the intro?", "vault_lines": ["How much time is this nigga spendin' on the intro?"], "meaning": "6 minutes and 6 seconds Drizzy. He did state earlier in the song that he was a perfectionist.\n\nOr, taken more metaphorically, his career so far is still in the intro because he’s just getting started.", "annotation_id": "2213888", "url": "https://genius.com/2213888", "state": "accepted", "votes": 189, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "The family all that matters, I’m just out here with my kinfolk", "vault_lines": ["The family all that matters, I'm just out here with my kinfolk"], "meaning": "Drake starts a slew of 90’s TV show references. Here he plays off the name of Family Matters, which starred Jaleel White as Steve Urkel.", "annotation_id": "2213883", "url": "https://genius.com/2213883", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "I’m tired of hearin’ ‘bout who you checkin’ for now Just give it time, we’ll see who’s still around a decade from now That’s real", "vault_lines": ["I'm tired of hearin' 'bout who you checkin' for now", "Just give it time, we'll see who's still around a decade from now"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing the idea of “standing the test of time”\n\nHe doubts these other rappers you think are hot (are “checkin' for”) will even be around/relevant in 10 years. He feels like he will be the last one standing after the competition is weeded out. Like he said on 5AM in Toronto:\n\nSome niggas been here for a couple, never been here again\n\nDrake also says in Over My Dead Body, the opening track from Take Care:\n\nFeel like I’ve been here before, huh?\nI still got 10 years to go, huh?\n\n2 years on he still thinks he has 10 years, so maybe he’ll always want another decade at the top.", "annotation_id": "2213878", "url": "https://genius.com/2213878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "I’m honest, I make mistakes, I’d be the second to admit it Think that’s why I need her in my life, to check me when I’m trippin’", "vault_lines": ["I'm honest, I make mistakes, I'd be the second to admit it", "Think that's why I need her in my life, to check me when I'm trippin'"], "meaning": "Minaj was the first to admit that she and Drizzy had become disconnected (during a Hot 97 interview), but she also hinted that whatever he’s doing right now in terms of moving away from YMCMB is a mistake.", "annotation_id": "2213815", "url": "https://genius.com/2213815", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "All these nineties fantasies on my mind The difference is that with mine, they all come true in due time", "vault_lines": ["All these nineties fantasies on my mind", "The difference is that with mine, they all come true in due time"], "meaning": "Having dinner with the ex-child actress Tatyana brought back some fantasies he had while watching her on TV. 20 or so years ago Drake stood no chance of meeting her, he’s now taking her out and doesn’t have work in the morning.\n\nWith all the Fresh Prince and Family Matters shout-outs, as well as era-appropriate fashion choices and an obsession with the Wu-Tang Clan, it seems like everything is coming up 90s for Drake\n\n\n\nHis 2013 OVO Fest lineup featured 90s staples Mase and TLC", "annotation_id": "2213517", "url": "https://genius.com/2213517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Off everything my pen wrote, we went from Bundy to Winslow", "vault_lines": ["Off everything my pen wrote, we went from Bundy to Winslow"], "meaning": "On the strengths of his raps, Drake went from the working class income of the Bundy family–of the sitcom, Married… with Children–to the upper middle class life of the Winslow family–of Family Matters. Both families are from Chicago.\n\n\n\nThe Winslows balled pretty hard.", "annotation_id": "2213480", "url": "https://genius.com/2213480", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "How this nigga workin’ like he got a fuckin’ twin though?", "vault_lines": ["How this nigga workin' like he got a fuckin' twin though?"], "meaning": "Not only does he release new albums and is featured in many songs with various artists, but most of the time he’s always on the road, (on tour): international performances, tv appearances, charity events, meet & greets, radio stations, etc. Drake has been releasing so much new stuff, other rappers would’ve thought he had a twin! Which he doesn’t.\n\n\n\nOr does he…\n\nThe line is similar to a line on Drake’s The Calm:\n\nTo make everybody happy I think I would need a clone\n\nDrizzy stepped his game up and is keeping everybody happy, but he doesn’t even need a clone anymore.\n\nAlso, Drake is making another Christopher Nolan-directed movie reference. In his movie The Prestige, Christian Bale is a tormented magician whose (spoiler) most famous trick, The Transported Man, is accomplished by cloning himself.", "annotation_id": "2213475", "url": "https://genius.com/2213475", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Yeah, Tom Ford Tuscan Leather smellin’ like a brick", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, Tom Ford Tuscan Leather smellin' like a brick"], "meaning": "Tom Ford Tuscan Leather is a pricy cologne that sells from anywhere from $210-520.\n\n\n\nThis cologne contains a mixture of saffron, raspberry, thyme, olibanum, and jasmine and is topped off with hints of leather, black suede, and amberwood. However, despite the ingredients, there have been multiple reports of the cologne smelling like cocaine\n\nIn an interview with Bridget Foley, Tom Ford revealed that some of his older customers would literally snort coke right in front of him:\n\nI’m in a totally different place. I don’t drink, I don’t smoke. I’m not the guy who’s designing clothes for someone who is doing lines of coke off the table, like I was when I was at Gucci and Saint Laurent.\n\nPerhaps this was the inspiration behind the scent?", "annotation_id": "2213419", "url": "https://genius.com/2213419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Yeah, I’m just as famous as my mentor But that’s still the boss, don’t get sent for", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm just as famous as my mentor", "But that's still the boss, don't get sent for"], "meaning": "At 30, Lil Wayne has already made a massive influence on a young generation of rappers. He famously discovered and mentored Drake and the rest is history. Several GQ covers, 3 albums, and a Grammy later, Drake is undeniably as big as Wayne.\n\nDespite that, Drizzy remains loyal to the man who launched him to stardom\n\n\n\n“Get sent for” references a mafia term when you are called on by your boss to be executed for disloyalty, snitching, or screwing up to a point only redeemable by death.", "annotation_id": "2213410", "url": "https://genius.com/2213410", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Not even talkin’ to Nicki, communication is breakin’ I dropped the ball on some personal shit, I need to embrace it", "vault_lines": ["Not even talkin' to Nicki, communication is breakin'", "I dropped the ball on some personal shit, I need to embrace it"], "meaning": "We’ve always known Drake and fellow label mate Nicki Minaj have had a special connection. They were together on Nicki’s track “Moment 4 Life”, where they, (in the music video), literally got married. He once rapped:\n\nI love Nicki Minaj\nI told her I’d admit it\nI hope one day we get married just to say we fuckin did it\n\n\n\nSince they’ve both become famous they haven’t really talked as much. Nicki recently went on Hot 97 and talked about how Drake and her have become too busy to have a real connection recently.", "annotation_id": "2213395", "url": "https://genius.com/2213395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 144, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "This for shorty up on Glengrove who love when I catch my tempo", "vault_lines": ["This for shorty up on Glengrove who love when I catch my tempo"], "meaning": "Glengrove Avenue is northwest of Forest Hill, hence “up” on Glengrove. Drake had to go a few blocks north of his hood to visit his girl. She especially loves it when he catches his tempo, which can drastically effect the mood of the track.", "annotation_id": "2213374", "url": "https://genius.com/2213374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "And listen to Cappadonna (Talk to ’em)", "vault_lines": ["and listen to Cappadonna (Talk to 'em)"], "meaning": "It’d make sense for Drake to listen to Cappadonna, a close affiliate of the famed Wu-Tang Clan. He named a song after them, and even used a sample of “C.R.E.A.M.” in “Pound Cake/Paris Morton Music 2.”", "annotation_id": "2213366", "url": "https://genius.com/2213366", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Past the present when you have to mention This is nothin’ for the radio, but they’ll still play it though ’Cause it’s that new Drizzy Drake, that’s just the way it go Heavy airplay all day with no chorus We keep it thorough, nigga", "vault_lines": ["We keep it thorough, nigga", "Past the present when you have to mention", "This is nothin' for the radio, but they'll still play it though", "'Cause it's that new Drizzy Drake, that's just the way it go", "Heavy airplay all day with no chorus"], "meaning": "Literally, this song has NO HOOKS, so it’s not your regular “radio song”, but Drizzy knows they’re going to play it anyway because it’s that good. And he also feels it’s ahead of everything else out right now (present).\n\n\n\nDrake references a Prodigy song “Keep It Thoro” off of his debut album H.N.I.C.\n\nHeavy airplay all day with no chorus\nWe keep it thoro nigga", "annotation_id": "2213350", "url": "https://genius.com/2213350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Lately I’ve been feelin’ like Guy Pearce in Memento", "vault_lines": ["Lately I've been feelin' like Guy Pearce in Memento"], "meaning": "Guy Pearce starred in the 2000 film Memento as the character Leonard Shelby. In the film, Shelby has a form of amnesia in which he can’t store new memories.\n\nHow does this relate? Drake has a no new everything mentality. He’s the same him, but nothing in the present will ever change him as a person.\n\nMemento is also presented in rough backwards chronological order, and Drake may be using this line to say that he feels he is moving backwards.", "annotation_id": "2213319", "url": "https://genius.com/2213319", "state": "accepted", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Life is soundin’ crazy, 40 on Martin Scorsese", "vault_lines": ["Life is soundin' crazy, 40 on Martin Scorsese"], "meaning": "Noah “40” Shebib is Drake’s best friend and produces most of Drake’s songs. Like Martin Scorsese, 40 is reaching another plateau\n\n\n\nIt’s also worth noting that Martin Scorsese’s initials, MS, are also an abbreviation of the disease Multiple sclerosis, which 40 was diagnosed with in 2005, although other main characters from some of Scorsese’s movies are quite crazy in one way or another, (e.g. Taxi Driver and The Aviator).", "annotation_id": "2213296", "url": "https://genius.com/2213296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Degenerates, but even Ellen love our shit", "vault_lines": ["Degenerates, but even Ellen love our shit"], "meaning": "Play on Ellen Degeneres‘ name, who is a talk show host and comedian. Drake has performed a few times on her show.\n\n\n\nOther than being a play off her name, Drake refers to him and his crew as degenerates which are impure or corrupt people. Regardless of their status, Ellen still likes them, so why shouldn’t others?", "annotation_id": "2213260", "url": "https://genius.com/2213260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Like ayy, B, I got your CD, you get an E for effort I piece letters together and get to talkin’ reckless", "vault_lines": ["Like ayy, B, I got your CD, you get an E for effort", "I piece letters together and get to talkin' reckless"], "meaning": "Yes, yes he does:\n\nDrake plays off the adage “A for Effort” to let the other rappers know he doesn’t like their new shit.\n\nHe does this by using the first 6 letters of the alphabet: “Aye, B I got your CD, you get an E for effort”.\n\nSee this blog post for a list of our favorite alphabetical rhymes.", "annotation_id": "2213241", "url": "https://genius.com/2213241", "state": "accepted", "votes": 260, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Sittin’ Gucci Row like they say up at UNLV Young rebel, Young Money, nothin’ you could tell me", "vault_lines": ["Sittin' Gucci Row like they say up at UNLV", "Young rebel, Young Money, nothin' you could tell me"], "meaning": "The UNLV (the University of Nevada, Las Vegas) Rebels basketball arena features courtside seating, known as “Gucci Row”\n\n\n\nYoung Money is a reference to the record label that Drake is signed to.\n\nDrake might be name dropping UNLV because of former Rebel Anthony Bennett, who was the 1st overall pick in the 2013 NBA Draft and like Drake is from Toronto.\n\nAdditionally, U.N.L.V. was a rap group formerly signed to Cash Money Records, the label Young Money was birthed out of, in its early years.", "annotation_id": "2213226", "url": "https://genius.com/2213226", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "The fresh prince, just had dinner with Tatyana, no lie", "vault_lines": ["The fresh prince, just had dinner with Tatyana, no lie"], "meaning": "This line is riddled with three pop culture references in it:\n\n\n\nThe Fresh Prince of Bel-Air was a popular tv show back in the 1990’s, led by actor Will Smith. Drake has previously mentioned The Fresh Prince in his single, “5 AM In Toronto”:\n\nAnything happen to pop then I got you like Uncle Phil\n\nTatyana refers to Tatyana Ali, who played Ashley on The Fresh Prince. Drake had dinner with her on this night. Drake was rumored to be in a relationship with the actress.\n“No lie” refers to Drake’s feature last year on the popular “No Lie”.\n\n\n\n\n\nDrake has also had casual dinners with Will Smith, who played the Fresh Prince\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q0E2XxN0iCA", "annotation_id": "2213191", "url": "https://genius.com/2213191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Comin’ off the last record I’m gettin’ twenty million off the record Just to off these records, nigga, that’s a record", "vault_lines": ["Comin' off the last record", "I'm gettin' twenty million off the record", "Just to off these records, nigga, that's a record"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his last album Take Care which just hit the  2 million mark in sales. With a unit price of $10 dollars an album, that’s about $20 million dollars total. As he said in “Versace,”\n\nI think I’m sellin' a million first week, man I guess I’m a optimist\n\n\n\n“Off the record” can also mean he’s getting $20 million that’s not on the record, i.e. not in the public eye. Might be connected to the “contract like ‘91 Dan Marino” line in “Pound Cake”, as Dan Marino got $23 million in his contract (close to the $20 million Drake references).\n\nAnd if the contract is a touring contract – plausible given that the line after the Dan Marino line name checks Michael Rapino, the Live Nation CEO – then “just to off these records” could refer to breaking tour revenue records as opposed to album sale records.", "annotation_id": "2213187", "url": "https://genius.com/2213187", "state": "accepted", "votes": 101, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "Prince Akeem, they throw flowers at my feet, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Prince Akeem, they throw flowers at my feet, nigga"], "meaning": "Prince Akeem is the main character in Coming to America, a prince from Zamunda played by Eddie Murphy.\n\nIn the film, Prince Akeem goes to the US in search of a bride, and when he arrives, they throw flowers at his feet.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_detailpage&v=lj2tIDXpKCk#t=106\n\nAlso relates to Dwight Howard reference one line prior to this. Price Hakeem/Akeem helped the Rockets recruit Dwight Howard to the Houston Rockets. Hakeem the Dream Olajuwon was the great Rockets center in the 80’s and 90’s. Note Prince “H\"akeem won two championships in 1994-95", "annotation_id": "2213079", "url": "https://genius.com/2213079", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "line": "I reached the point where don’t shit matter to me, nigga I reached heights that Dwight Howard couldn’t reach, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I reached the point where don't shit matter to me, nigga", "I reached heights that Dwight Howard couldn't reach, nigga"], "meaning": "NBA superstar Dwight Howard is 6 foot 10 inches tall. Drizzy is making implications on both his success and inebriation, pointing out that he’s accomplished more than most athletes and rappers. Drake’s net worth was estimated to be $73 million in 2013.\n\nIn addition to his monetary gains, Drake also reached heights in terms of music. Nothing Was the Same sold 658,000 copies in the first week and charted multiple hits on the Billboard Hot 100. During a June 2013 interview with GQ Magazine, Drake explained his ambitions to exceed as an artist by reaching unrivaled peaks:\n\nI feel this great responsibility to see how far can we take it. How out of reach can I set that bar for whoever comes after? While I’m here, I’m gonna keep pushing that bar higher and higher up…\n\nMoreover, this might also be a shot at Howard’s inability to conquer an NBA title with the 2012–2013 Los Angeles Lakers.", "annotation_id": "2213049", "url": "https://genius.com/2213049", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuscan Leather", "status": "done", "targets": 36, "clicked": 36, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-tuscan-leather-lyrics", "genius_title": "Tuscan Leather"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "You prolly heard a lot about the boy, well, true, true, haha", "vault_lines": ["You prolly heard a lot about the boy, well, true, true, haha"], "meaning": "The boy is drake’s nickname, and he is saying that all the good things we heard about him is true.", "annotation_id": "37796866", "url": "https://genius.com/37796866", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Breakin’ news, they tried to kill him, but the boy prevails", "vault_lines": ["Breakin' news, they tried to kill him, but the boy prevails"], "meaning": "In this bar he is referencing himself, as many people call him “the boy” a nickname he denoted to himself. In Kendrick Lamar’s disstrack he dissed Drake by saying that he’s “the boy” but Kendrick is “the man” and telling him to grow up. He is saying in this line that many people tried to bring him down and ruin him but he still rose to the point he is today.", "annotation_id": "36419282", "url": "https://genius.com/36419282", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Preachin’ to the dogs ‘bout wantin’ more for themselves", "vault_lines": ["Preachin' to the dogs 'bout wantin' more for themselves"], "meaning": "Drake makes it undoubtably clear in this bar that he wants his ‘dogs’ to succeed. He implies that his dogs do not already want “more for themselves”, revealing deep frustration – not just with their lack of ambition, but with the emotional burden he carries trying to uplift those who won’t uplift themselves.", "annotation_id": "35533723", "url": "https://genius.com/35533723", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I’m used to seein’ tears drop over enormous meals", "vault_lines": ["I'm used to seein' tears drop over enormous meals"], "meaning": "Whilst Drake usually publicizes his wealth by showing off his belongings. In this bar, Drake reveals the darker side of his life despite his wealth.\n\nWith the metaphor of the “tears drop”, Drake illustrates the sorrow in what we can infer is his familial relationships as the setting is within meal times. More specifically, Drake juxtaposes the luxury and grief, suggesting emotional pain even in moments that are supposed to signify success or comfort. Ultimately revealing emotional emptiness behind his grand wealth.", "annotation_id": "35533625", "url": "https://genius.com/35533625", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "The restaurant clears out, faint echoes of Lauryn Hill", "vault_lines": ["The restaurant clears out, faint echoes of Lauryn Hill"], "meaning": "Lauryn Hill appeared in the 1998 film Restaurant along with Oscar-winner Adrien Brody. Drake is simply giving Ms. Lauryn Hill a shoutout, while also giving some play on words.", "annotation_id": "34991700", "url": "https://genius.com/34991700", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Like, don’t even worry about it, like You can hit me back whenever, or Or don’t, you know?", "vault_lines": ["Like, don't even worry about it, like", "You can hit me back whenever, or", "Or don't, you know?"], "meaning": "The interlude is addressing the last line in verse 2.\n\n“I’m givin' hits to niggas on some, don’t even mention it”\n\nThe interlude further emphasize his nonchalant approach to communication, indicating that the recipient of his help shouldn’t feel obligated to respond promptly or at all. It conveys a sense of casual support and a lack of expectation in return for the assistance given.", "annotation_id": "30035258", "url": "https://genius.com/30035258", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Walk in Chanel, they like, “How the fuck you need more Chanel?”", "vault_lines": ["Walk in Chanel, they like, \"How the fuck you need more Chanel?\""], "meaning": "Drake boasts his wealth again by implying that he got so many items of the French luxury fashion house, Chanel, that the workers at the Chanel shop are surprised by the fact that he came to purchase more Chanel items.", "annotation_id": "30008908", "url": "https://genius.com/30008908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Niggas steady cryin’ to my daddy, well, boo-hoo", "vault_lines": ["Niggas steady cryin' to my daddy, well, boo-hoo"], "meaning": "Here, Drake could be referring to how his father got a call from an unknown caller in July of this year, supposedly telling him about how his son could be a freemason and part of the Illuminati.", "annotation_id": "29985200", "url": "https://genius.com/29985200", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I got these cats tuckin’ tails on fourth-quarter sales", "vault_lines": ["I got these cats tuckin' tails on fourth-quarter sales"], "meaning": "Drake uses vivid imagery to describe a scene he’s witnessing. He metaphorically portrays cats (people) who are feeling defeated or disappointed in their fourth-quarter sales, which can represent their financial performance or overall success. The mention of “tuckin' tails” refers to a common animal behavior where cats or dogs lower their tails when they feel scared or submissive. By using this phrase, Drake emphasizes the feeling of defeat or insecurity these individuals are experiencing.", "annotation_id": "29796256", "url": "https://genius.com/29796256", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Helicopters, cop lights, and news crews", "vault_lines": ["Helicopters, cop lights, and news crews"], "meaning": "“Helicopters, cop lights, and news crews”\n\nFollowing the line in reference to DJ Screw, we can assume that this is targeted at the lifestyle of drug use which lead to DJ Screw’s eventual overdose on Lean.\n\n(Lean, for those unaware, is a combination of prescription grade cough syrup, soda, and candy. It’s a highly addictive sedative.)\n\nHelicopters and cop lights points out the criminal side of “the rap game” which is all too common and emphasizes previous lines about murder and deceit. Furthermore, DJ Screw who is mentioned in the previous line is native to Houston. Houston among other things is notorious for its popularization of lean. This leads into the portion about news crews which may be in reference to the large amount of coverage the DJ Screw overdose brought to the lean epidemic which is still an issue today.", "annotation_id": "29758504", "url": "https://genius.com/29758504", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Fire top from a bitch that work at corporate sales", "vault_lines": ["Fire top from a bitch that work at corporate sales"], "meaning": "Fire top is good head but also a reference to the competitive nature of working in corporate sales, climbing the ranks to the top or getting fired/firing people", "annotation_id": "29749147", "url": "https://genius.com/29749147", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Ayy, niggas lyin’ for a livin’, I couldn’t relate", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, niggas lyin' for a livin', I couldn't relate"], "meaning": "Lyin’ is a double entendre.\n\n\nLyin for a livin ( new rappers rapping bout shoes, cars, and women when in reality they lack in those things. )\nLyin as in lying in a bed. Taking about how these newer rappers don’t put it as much heart or time into their songs, therefore, they’re laying down with time in their life to spare. Meanwhile, Drake cannot because he puts in an immense amount of work everyday to a point where he can’t lay down because he has stuff to do.\n\n\n\nAnd that’s the why Drake talks about how he couldn’t relate to these fake artists and how little time they put into music.\n\nIronic that Drake has Honestly, Nevermind in his discography though…", "annotation_id": "29738332", "url": "https://genius.com/29738332", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Could tell I’m gettin’ under your skin like a orange peel", "vault_lines": ["Could tell I'm gettin' under your skin like a orange peel"], "meaning": "Getting under one’s skin means to annoy someone. Orange peel stings when put on open wounds due to the citric acid in them. Orange peel is also the skin of the orange.\n\nThis means that he gets under someone’s skin and stings them\n\nDrake also plays on the previous line where he says Jordan Peele, with “under your skin” being a reference to the plot of Jordan Peele’s ‘Get Out’, where some white characters seek to be transferred into black character’s bodies – under their skin.", "annotation_id": "29735076", "url": "https://genius.com/29735076", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "We all gotta lay in the bed we make, but that couldn’t be Drake", "vault_lines": ["We all gotta lay in the bed we make, but that couldn't be Drake"], "meaning": "When you say that someone has made their bed and now lie in it, you suggest that because they chose to do something, they must now accept the negative consequences of their actions.\nBut this doesn’t apply to my majestic, light skin,  certified lover boy, king Drake as his decisions are well calculated. Drake could also be talking about how his maids make his bed and thats why is couldn’t be him.\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: He could also be referencing his Bobbi Althoff interview here too", "annotation_id": "29728925", "url": "https://genius.com/29728925", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "You young boys take some of that money and set it aside Not havin’ enough to pay your tax is a federal crime", "vault_lines": ["You young boys take some of that money and set it aside", "Not havin' enough to pay your tax is a federal crime"], "meaning": "When people earn money, they are in the obligation to pay a tax that is usually a percentage of this profit.\nHere, Drake is stating that a lot of people say that they earn more money than what they really make, so they may not have enough “real”  money to pay the tax percentage of that fake income they claim to have, making that a Federal Crime.\n\nThis is also a reference and diss towards the rapper NBA Youngboy, who was reported to have had missed tax fillings and subsequently paid the IRS $10,000,000 in taxes.", "annotation_id": "29728336", "url": "https://genius.com/29728336", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "It’s weighin’ heavy on my moral scale", "vault_lines": ["It's weighin' heavy on my moral scale"], "meaning": "This phrase is an example of Drake’s lyrical dexterity. The double entendre. Weight is a measure taken by scale, however, Drake is referring to his moral compass which is being tested by his affiliations with street guys.", "annotation_id": "29728213", "url": "https://genius.com/29728213", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "In God’s hands", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A sample of Polyphonic Music Library’s track – A Faithful Spirit (Featuring Nichol Eskridge)\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zEuVAfJ9h8E", "annotation_id": "29726381", "url": "https://genius.com/29726381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "The money speakin’ for itself, I call it fortune-tell", "vault_lines": ["The money speakin' for itself, I call it fortune-tell"], "meaning": "What Drake is saying is basically he doesn’t need anyone to tell his fortune (to tell someone’s fortune is basically to read their future) because he has so much money (double entendre on fortune).\n\nThis much money allows him to make his own future", "annotation_id": "29723557", "url": "https://genius.com/29723557", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Not sayin’ I’m the best at what I do I’m just sayin’ that it’s me versus whoever wanna lose", "vault_lines": ["Not sayin' I'm the best at what I do", "I'm just sayin' that it's me versus whoever wanna lose"], "meaning": "Drake states he’s not the “best” at what he does, but will come out victorious against any other artist that wants to contest otherwise. The bar is paradoxical, distinguishing him as a winner regardless of the competition.", "annotation_id": "29723499", "url": "https://genius.com/29723499", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "’Cause your words don’t match your actions like a foreign film", "vault_lines": ["'Cause your words don't match your actions like a foreign film"], "meaning": "This line refers to English-Dubbed movies of “foreign” production. Many movies, TV shows, and commercials that are originally made in Korean, Japanese, Spanish and more, often pay actors to voice-over the entirety of the film or episode. This leads to rushed or delayed subtitles, making the word-to-movement ratio off.\n\nhttps://media1.giphy.com/media/xUPN3ate3Edrf0gNP2/giphy.webp?cid=6c09b9520rlnaka1ni4ha6hl71szio1m24bgr4izjbxgohrp&ep=v1_internal_gif_by_id&rid=giphy.webp&ct=g", "annotation_id": "29723257", "url": "https://genius.com/29723257", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "The numbers goin’ up, someone pull up the line graph", "vault_lines": ["The numbers goin' up, someone pull up the line graph"], "meaning": "A line graph, also known as a line chart, is a type of data visualization used to represent data points over a continuous interval or time period. It is particularly useful for showing trends and changes in data over time. Drake is stating that his “numbers are goin’ up” to scrutinize this data and uncover the reality, one might opt for a comprehensive line chart that incorporates all the accessible information. Such a chart serves to underscore or enhance the listener’s understanding of the significance of his achievements.", "annotation_id": "29721211", "url": "https://genius.com/29721211", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Thank God, another USB to put in the safe", "vault_lines": ["Thank God, another USB to put in the safe"], "meaning": "Double entendre\n\nUSB: File storage of the footage he has  on camera from the previous line\n\nUSB: 1 Billion US dollars (1 US Billion)\nDrake is alluding to his enormous wealth and possible billionaire status", "annotation_id": "29718460", "url": "https://genius.com/29718460", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "You forced a lot of fake love when real ones stood in your face", "vault_lines": ["You forced a lot of fake love when real ones stood in your face"], "meaning": "This is a reference and diss signed at Drake’s longtime nemesis Kanye who forged fake friendships with politicians and fashion houses to progress career wise, while at the same time watching his label (GOOD Music) disband and the artists who stood beside him most distance themselves.\n\nDrake has seen a number of rivals and adversaries talk tough with him, but as soon as people with more street credibility come into the picture, they become meek and apologetic.\n\nThis is also a broadside at people he once thought were loyal to him, but were looking out for themselves that whole time.", "annotation_id": "29717534", "url": "https://genius.com/29717534", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "The days are goin’ by, it’s like I’m livin’ in time-lapse", "vault_lines": ["The days are goin' by, it's like I'm livin' in time-lapse"], "meaning": "Drake reflects on the passage of time by comparing it to a time-lapse video. A time-lapse video is a technique in photography or videography, where a series of frames are captured at regular intervals and then played back at a faster speed, creating a condensed visual representation of the passing of time.\n\nBy saying,\n\n“it’s like I’m living in time lapse,”\n\nDrake is expressing how quickly time seems to be flying by. The days are passing by so swiftly that he feels as though he is witnessing his life in an accelerated manner, similar to how a time-lapse video captures and compresses time. This line conveys a sense of astonishment or perhaps even slight disbelief at the rapid pace at which life is progressing.", "annotation_id": "29716587", "url": "https://genius.com/29716587", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Diss me so long ago, we making your memories fly", "vault_lines": ["Diss me so long ago, we making your memories fly"], "meaning": "Even though you may have dissed Drake a long time ago he alludes to refreshing his oppositions memory by putting a bullet through their head. Making memories fly can be seen as a reference to making the oppositions brain fly out their skull due to bullets.", "annotation_id": "29716576", "url": "https://genius.com/29716576", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Handle beef so quiet, you think that I’m lettin’ it slide Next thing you know, we tip-toein’ past enemy lines", "vault_lines": ["Handle beef so quiet, you think that I'm lettin' it slide", "Next thing you know, we tip-toein' past enemy lines"], "meaning": "In trojan horse style, Drake states that what publicly appears to be him making his peace with beef is a front to sneak into enemy territory.\n\nFor years, Drake has eluded to having inside information on Kanye West that would derail him.\nIn an unfortunate irony, Pusha T managed to have inside information on Drake’s son’s existence during their very historic beef.", "annotation_id": "29716496", "url": "https://genius.com/29716496", "state": "pending", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I leave for tour and my niggas fuckin’ go to jail", "vault_lines": ["I leave for tour and my niggas fuckin' go to jail"], "meaning": "As of the release of this song, Drake is currently on the “It’s All a Blur” tour. During this tour fellow Toronto artist Tory Lanez was sentenced to 10 years in prison in respect to the shooting of Megan thee Stallion.\n\nDrake referenced the shooting and subsequent case on his project, ‘Her Loss’, where In the song “Circo Loco,” Drake raps:\n\nThis bitch lie ’bout getting shots but she still a stallion.\n\nMeg responded on twitter to the lines, claiming the Toronto rapper was using her name for attention.\n\nhttps://variety.com/2022/music/news/megan-thee-stallion-slams-drake-song-shooting-1235423257/", "annotation_id": "29716479", "url": "https://genius.com/29716479", "state": "pending", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "You tryna rob me, and it’s gon’ feel like you sittin’ at your favorite restaurant ‘cause, nigga, that’s where you dyin’ at", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Dine at,” something one often does at their favorite restaurant, is a homophone to “dyin' at,” implying you’ll get killed for attempting to rob Drake.", "annotation_id": "29716307", "url": "https://genius.com/29716307", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Formal is the dress code, dawg, so many checks owed", "vault_lines": ["Formal is the dress code, dawg, so many checks owed"], "meaning": "Drake uses double entendre. Continuing with the Eastern Europe theme established in the previous line, Drake flexes his financial success by the fact that he has owes “so many checks”- a homonym with ‘Czechs’, i.e. people originating from Czech Republic.\n\n\n\nIt is important to note that Czechoslovakia doesn’t exist anymore, and split into the Czech Republic and Slovakia in 1993. This connects with the previous line of linking vs breaking up with 21 Savage in a geopolitical sense, ala Yugoslavia.\n\nLastly, Drake is so amazed at his own rap skills, managing to rhyme “Yugoslavian” and “Czechoslovakian” in his lines, that he closes the verse with a surprised “What the fuck?”", "annotation_id": "29716146", "url": "https://genius.com/29716146", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Where I go, you go, brother, we Yugoslavian", "vault_lines": ["Where I go, you go, brother, we Yugoslavian"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the former Balkan nation Yugoslavia, which split into six republics, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia, Macedonia, Montenegro, Serbia, and Slovenia, in 1992. The country split up due to cultural and religious divisions leading to a war which started the separation.\n\n\n\nThe region is known for such tumultuous history and conflict that the phrase ‘Balkanization’ was coined in the early 19th century in the fallout of the Ottoman Empire. The Yugoslavian state established in 1918 was an attempt at unity and peace in the region.\n\nHowever, these days the political idea of Balkan unity is one of major contention and has connotations of conflict, ethnonationalism, and deep ties to land and heritage. This parallels 21 Savage’s own tenuous connection to his European heritage, as Drake evokes images of sticking with his “brother” through thick and thin.", "annotation_id": "29716004", "url": "https://genius.com/29716004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I swear that y’all turned me into the villain, I couldn’t escape", "vault_lines": ["I swear that y'all turned me into the villain, I couldn't escape"], "meaning": "Here Drake is making reference that he was obliged to turn bad, or into a villain by the other people even if he didn’t wanted to. He makes a similar bar in his CLB song “The Remorse” where hen sings:\n\n“I tried to kill them boys with kindness, but they wouldn’t die\nSo now I kill ‘em all for acting like they couldn’t die”\n Saying how he tried to do the things in the “good” way before not having another option that to become the “villain”.", "annotation_id": "29715599", "url": "https://genius.com/29715599", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Thank God, at the crib, dippin’ my foot in the lake", "vault_lines": ["Thank God, at the crib, dippin' my foot in the lake"], "meaning": "Part of Drake’s 50,000 square foot property, nicknamed ‘The Embassy,’ is his massive pool that is similar in size to that of a small lake.", "annotation_id": "29715337", "url": "https://genius.com/29715337", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I’ll prolly hold a grudge against you guys ’til I’m seventy-five", "vault_lines": ["I'll prolly hold a grudge against you guys 'til I'm seventy-five"], "meaning": "Drake could be talking about how Pusha T and Kanye West as Kanye gave Pusha the information for infamous diss track “The Story Of Adidon”, exposing Adonis,Drakes son.\n\nIn a 2018 interview, Drake stated he has no intent to forgive both of them either again.", "annotation_id": "29714799", "url": "https://genius.com/29714799", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I got you on camera bowin’ down, but the footage is safe", "vault_lines": ["I got you on camera bowin' down, but the footage is safe"], "meaning": "Drake is probably saying he knows a lot of secret or things that has happened backstage or behind doors about other celebrities but choose to keep to himself.\nUnlike them who would say it in public for attention.\nThis might also be  a reference to Pusha calling him out about hiding his a kid\n\nDrake rapped a similar message on his 2018’s track, “Behind Barz”:\n\nI know so much shit that I cannot expose\nI keep it inside and I laugh on my own\nGot all the tea and I’m hottin' it up\nI need a shade mansion, a room’s not enough", "annotation_id": "29714537", "url": "https://genius.com/29714537", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Pick any one of the Who’s Whos, I got .22s for new crews", "vault_lines": ["Pick any one of the Who's Whos, I got .22s for new crews"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that he is so good that he is willing to take on anyone, even the biggest and best in the music industry.\n\nThe “two 2’s” could be a reference to two .22 caliber pistols.", "annotation_id": "29714266", "url": "https://genius.com/29714266", "state": "pending", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "That’s why you got deserted by your niggas like puddin’ and cake", "vault_lines": ["That's why you got deserted by your niggas like puddin' and cake"], "meaning": "This line is a continuation of the previous. Here, Drake uses another double entendre. Said person who was practicing “fake love” finally is faced with the consequences of his actions (practice). In turn made everyone leave him (desert). In the second half of the same line, he uses a wordplay on “desert” which means to abandon (a person, idea or organization) to the word dessert. A homophone, which is common with Drake when he’s rapping. Pudding and cake are both popular desserts.", "annotation_id": "29714082", "url": "https://genius.com/29714082", "state": "pending", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Things get kinky after fifteen years of dominance That October sky is lookin’ ominous The money is autonomous", "vault_lines": ["Things get kinky after fifteen years of dominance", "That October sky is lookin' ominous", "The money is autonomous"], "meaning": "Kinky and dominance are terms associated with sadomasochism. Dominants control submissives in fetish play.\n\nThe interpretation is a reference to Drake’s longevity in the music industry and his continued dominance of the charts since releasing “So Far Gone” in 2009.\n\n“October sky” is a further reference to Drake’s successes, pointing to his branding of October’s Very Own. From clothing to a record label and more, Drake’s success for 15 years and counting is not understated.", "annotation_id": "29714034", "url": "https://genius.com/29714034", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I feel like if Mike switched out the glove for the pen, like", "vault_lines": ["I feel like if Mike switched out the glove for the pen, like"], "meaning": "Drake references the late great “King of Pop”, Michael Jackson, who famously adopted wearing a glove after the onset of his vitiligo.\n\nTurning the former line into a double entendre, Drake references Mike Tyson using the word enticing as a homophone to the great boxer’s surname.\n\nIn either case, Drake is equating his talent and success in Hip-Hop and music to the talent and success both Michael and Mike showcased in their careers, suggesting that they would have been just as successful in Drake’s position.", "annotation_id": "29713975", "url": "https://genius.com/29713975", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "You niggas obsessed with me, and it’s not on no hetero vibe", "vault_lines": ["You niggas obsessed with me, and it's not on no hetero vibe"], "meaning": "People obsession with Drake has made him question their sexual orientation. He no longer believes they’re acting as a heterosexual person would and sees them as a member of a gray area between the hetero(sexual) and homo(sexual) continuum. Albeit, leaning more towards the homosexual spectrum\n\nThis line is also a subliminal shot at “Charlamagne tha God” on a recently deleted instagram post Drakes states his ongoing feud with the radio host. Calling the offbrand Morris Chestnut as the radio host’s criticism of the song “Slime You Out” doesn’t sit well with the rapper.", "annotation_id": "29713966", "url": "https://genius.com/29713966", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "And Jeremiah the watchdog, you niggas know what time it is", "vault_lines": ["And Jeremiah the watchdog, you niggas know what time it is", "It is what it is, I guess"], "meaning": "Drake makes a wordplay with “watchdog” and “know what time it is” by referring to reading time on a watch.\n\nJeremiah is Drake’s Bodyguard. Jeremiah even got his own t-shirt on drakerelated.com.", "annotation_id": "29713573", "url": "https://genius.com/29713573", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I’m givin’ hits to niggas on some, don’t even mention it", "vault_lines": ["I'm givin' hits to niggas on some, don't even mention it"], "meaning": "Due to how highly acclaimed and well situated Drake has become in the music industry, any guest appearance he makes on a track is bound to become a hit. For this, some may refer to it as the “Drake effect.” As deemed by J. Cole on “The Secret Recipe” a track from Lil Yachty’s EP.\n\n“He FaceTime to ask for a feature and saw the face of death\nI’m on your song, your streams goin' up, not quite the Drake effect”\n\nSince it’s become so habitual for Drake, he doesn’t see it as no big deal to help aspiring artists. Mentioning it wouldn’t be necessary as it is a act of generosity from him.", "annotation_id": "29713900", "url": "https://genius.com/29713900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Shout’ to Oliver North, he out in Rome doin’ Toronto shit", "vault_lines": ["Shout' to Oliver North, he out in Rome doin' Toronto shit"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his long-time friend and co-founder of the “OVO Sound” label, Oliver el-Khatib, sometimes referred to as “Oliver North”.", "annotation_id": "29713857", "url": "https://genius.com/29713857", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "That’s what’s really brackin’ like this verse in parentheses", "vault_lines": ["That's what's really brackin' like this verse in parentheses"], "meaning": "Another display of Drake’s immeasurable wordplay. Here Drake asks the listener “what’s brackin” which is an alternative of “what’s crackin.” A phrase in slang used as a greeting or confrontation that means “what’s going on with you.”\n\nHowever, Drake substitutes the ‘c’ in “crackin” with a ‘b’, this practice of switching c’s with b’s is common with blood (gang) members.\n\nWhich allows Drake to set up for yet another double entendre, where “brackin” is referring to the aforementioned and also brackets. As parenthesis are essentially round brackets.", "annotation_id": "29713854", "url": "https://genius.com/29713854", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Been talkin’ to Adel like he majored in finance", "vault_lines": ["Been talkin' to Adel like he majored in finance"], "meaning": "Drake shout-outs close business partner Adel “Future Prince” Nur.\nAdel is known for being the executive producer of the HBO hit-series ‘Euphoria’ as well as Drake’s own Netflix show ‘Top Boy’.\n\n\n\nThe phrase is also a play on words that compares Adel to a Dell computer, a well-respected brand known for its computer products and financial services.", "annotation_id": "29713818", "url": "https://genius.com/29713818", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I’m preachin’ to the dawgs about cleanin’ they images", "vault_lines": ["I'm preachin' to the dawgs about cleanin' they images"], "meaning": "T.D. Jakes is a preacher, senior pastor, and author.\n\n\n\nDrake draws comparisons to Jakes; preaching to those around him to clean up their image, as he recounts in an earlier line:\n\nPreachin' to the dogs ‘bout wantin’ more for themselves\nIt’s weighin' heavy on my moral scale", "annotation_id": "29713808", "url": "https://genius.com/29713808", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Three hunnid acres, PGA on the premises", "vault_lines": ["Three hunnid acres, PGA on the premises"], "meaning": "Here, Drake emphasizes the massive size of his land by saying that it is large enough to contain a PGA-standard (Professional Golfers' Association (of America)) golf course, which are around 300 acres in size, such as the 365-acre Augusta National Golf Club, which is the annual venue for the Masters.", "annotation_id": "29713789", "url": "https://genius.com/29713789", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Conspiracy theories start floatin’ ’round like the Kennedy guy", "vault_lines": ["Conspiracy theories start floatin' 'round like the Kennedy guy"], "meaning": "Drake uses a triple meaning in this bar.\n\nFirstly, he continues on from his previous bar on “making memories fly”, which refers to eliminating his opponents by shooting them in the head, which is how U.S. President John F. Kennedy was assassinated.\n\nSecondly, he also could be alluding to the discreet nature in which Drake eliminates his opponents, leaving no trace of his involvement, causing “conspiracy theories” to form surrounding who really eliminated them.\n\nThirdly, he could be referring to John F. Kennedy Jr., President Kennedy’s son, who passed away after crashing in a light aircraft over the Atlantic Ocean, hence the words “floatin' ‘round like the Kennedy guy.”", "annotation_id": "29713788", "url": "https://genius.com/29713788", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Long-kiss goodnight, PDA for my nemesis", "vault_lines": ["Long-kiss goodnight, PDA for my nemesis"], "meaning": "Drake refers to The Notorious B.I.G.’s March 1997 track, “Long Kiss Goodnight”, in discussing his demeanor towards his enemies. In mentioning giving his enemies a “long kiss goodnight”, an euphemism for death, Drake implies that he intends on killing his enemies in a discrete manner, namely in their sleep when they would be unable to notice or expect anything to happen.\n\nOn the eponymous track, Biggie holds a similar sentiment as Drake, which is conveyed through its chorus:\n\nTime, time for you to die\nAs I kiss yo' ass goodnight\n\nIn addition, on Drake’s 2021 song “Race My Mind”, he also notably utilizes a sample of Biggie’s December 1999 track “Hope You Niggas Sleep” to convey his discontent:\n\n‘Cause I remember last time you wouldn’t make love to me\nThen you fell fast asleep (I hope you niggas sleep)", "annotation_id": "29713759", "url": "https://genius.com/29713759", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Shania Twain, notepad, I’m makin’ it line-dance", "vault_lines": ["Shania Twain, notepad, I'm makin' it line-dance"], "meaning": "Drake refers to his song-writing as making his notepad “line dance”, referencing Shania Twain, who has a popular country line dancing song, titled “Any Man of Mine”", "annotation_id": "29713757", "url": "https://genius.com/29713757", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Hoes waitin’ on Cench in the lobby, that boy a lobbyist", "vault_lines": ["Hoes waitin' on Cench in the lobby, that boy a lobbyist"], "meaning": "Central Cee, also known as Cench is a UK rapper and Drake collaborator, who was the opening act of many shows throughout Drake and 21 Savage’s 2023 It’s All a Blur Tour.\n\nCench and Drake finally teamed up for a highly-anticipated “On The Radar Freestyle” in July, 2023.", "annotation_id": "29713738", "url": "https://genius.com/29713738", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I’m in and out of Houston Hobby so much, I’m a hobbyist", "vault_lines": ["I'm in and out of Houston Hobby so much, I'm a hobbyist"], "meaning": "Houston Hobby is a call to William P. Hobby Airport in Houston, Texas.\n\nDrake refers to himself as a “hobbyist,” stating that he takes so many flights, he can consider it a hobby of his. He does own his own jet, after all.", "annotation_id": "29713736", "url": "https://genius.com/29713736", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Diamonds do the silly dance, I raise up the wine glass", "vault_lines": ["Diamonds do the silly dance, I raise up the wine glass"], "meaning": "Drake’s diamonds are so genuine that they seem to be dancing. This unique dazzling effect caused by the glistening minerals is just one way of conveying to viewers/listeners that ones diamonds are pure and carved from the ground. This could also be a shout to his collaborative track with Future “Diamonds Dancing” from their hit collab 2015 mixtape “What a Time to Be Alive”\n\nRaising wine glass is a common act used when celebrating an accomplishment. Usually performed to commemorate someone or something by a toast.\n\nDespite the metal detector being alarmed when Drake goes through security checks, his high profile and stature allows him to be allowed a bypass- let off the hook.", "annotation_id": "29713707", "url": "https://genius.com/29713707", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Mob ties, I swear we like a bitch with fine sisters and fine cousins, the family all bad", "vault_lines": ["Mob ties, I swear we like a bitch with fine sisters and fine cousins, the family all bad"], "meaning": "Drake is making a shout out to James Prince, CEO of Rap-a-Lot records and “Mob Ties” affiliated group in Houston. This is one of many shoutouts Drake has made over the years. He shouted them out even more directly on “Circo Loco”:\n\nLinking with the opps, bitch, I did that shit for J. Prince\nBitch, I did it for the Mob ties\n\nEven naming a song on his album Scorpion,  “Mob Ties”.\n\n\n\nDrake sees his Mob Ties as family, in which consists of all “bad” people, as in failing to conform to standards of moral virtue. The line compares this to a family in which everyone is attractive, where “bad” is being used as slang to describe attractiveness.", "annotation_id": "29713667", "url": "https://genius.com/29713667", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Chinchilla ushanka, we skiin’ out in Courchevel", "vault_lines": ["Chinchilla ushanka, we skiin' out in Courchevel"], "meaning": "Chinchilla fur is expensive and luxurious, and ushanka hats are typically made of fur from animals like sable or mink.\n\n\nCourchevel is a high-end ski resort in the French Alps.\n\n\nThe phrase is a reference to the luxury lifestyle Drake has obtained, while it suggests that Drake is wearing a chinchilla ushanka hat while skiing in Courchevel. This is a very expensive and exclusive lifestyle, and it is a symbol of wealth and status.", "annotation_id": "29713660", "url": "https://genius.com/29713660", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "And now it’s silence in the Lamb’ like the horror film Things get quiet after me statin’ the obvious", "vault_lines": ["And now it's silence in the Lamb' like the horror film", "Things get quiet after me statin' the obvious"], "meaning": "Drake calls on the 1991 horror film The Silence of the Lambs while also bringing attention to the silence in his Lamborghini after having the last say in the discussion between him and his girl.", "annotation_id": "29713643", "url": "https://genius.com/29713643", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Savage got a green card straight out of the consulate", "vault_lines": ["Savage got a green card straight out of the consulate"], "meaning": "Drake refers to 21 Savage’s immigrant status first revealed when he was arrested by U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) in February 2019. According to ICE’s documents, he is a UK citizen from birth who overstayed his tourist visa in 2005 and subsequently cannot leave the US until he obtains permanent US residency through a green card.\n\nSavage rapped about his obtainment of a green card in an August 2023 song “By Chance” with UK rapper Giggs:\n\nWhen I get my Green Card, I’m goin' straight to Brixton", "annotation_id": "29713631", "url": "https://genius.com/29713631", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "(Conductor)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Denzel “Conductor” Williams is a producer from Kansas City, Missouri. He is best known for his work with artists from the Griselda label, namely Westside Gunn and Mach-Hommy. This is the first-ever collaboration between Williams and Drake.\n\nThis is one of Williams' producer tags, this one, in particular is usually chopped-up. The full tag is “Conductor, we have a problem!” and comes from this 2015 meme.", "annotation_id": "29713629", "url": "https://genius.com/29713629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "R.I.P. to the DJ from Houston, we loose screws", "vault_lines": ["R.I.P. to the DJ from Houston, we loose screws"], "meaning": "DJ Screw, a popular music figure from Houston who passed away on November 16, 2000, was known for creating the “Chopped & Screwed” style remixes we still see on albums today.\n\n“He was a true artist,” said record executive Russell Washington. “He cared about the minutest of details. The scratches had to be just right. He told me that every song on [a] tape told a story.”", "annotation_id": "29713613", "url": "https://genius.com/29713613", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "I say, “We gotta talk about us,” I feel like Jordan Peele", "vault_lines": ["I say, \"We gotta talk about us,\" I feel like Jordan Peele"], "meaning": "An allusion to the 2019  retrospective film Us, written and directed by Jordan Peele.", "annotation_id": "29713608", "url": "https://genius.com/29713608", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8am in Charlotte", "line": "Knowin’ they gon’ sell another citizen ’caine, they think they Orson Welles", "vault_lines": ["Knowin' they gon' sell another citizen 'caine, they think they Orson Welles"], "meaning": "An oronym is employed as Drake details his aforementioned friends being imprisoned for selling ‘caine (cocaine), while referencing the 1941 film Citizen Kane starring Orson Welles.", "annotation_id": "29713575", "url": "https://genius.com/29713575", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
