{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Man of the Year", "line": "She just tryna make a four fit", "vault_lines": ["Her ass big, she just tryna make a four fit"], "meaning": "Drake makes a homophone, previous to this line he saids “or did you forfeit” which sounds exactly like four fit", "annotation_id": "26736132", "url": "https://genius.com/26736132", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Man of the Year", "line": "Plus I got a city that I Carey like Mariah", "vault_lines": ["Plus I got a city that I Carey like Mariah"], "meaning": "Mariah’s last name is Carey. “Mariah Carey”\n\n\n\nDrake is from Toronto. He is basically stating how he is carrying his city into being more popular.", "annotation_id": "23829437", "url": "https://genius.com/23829437", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Man of the Year", "line": "Two times on Sunday", "vault_lines": ["Y-M, bitch, everyday, two times on Sunday"], "meaning": "This dates from an old-time saying referring to entertainment acts or church attendance. You would go to church twice on Sundays or some theatrical performances would have two performances on Sundays. Many broadway shows and churches still typically have two Sunday shows/masses, though the schedule during the week or on Saturdays may be the same.\n\nThis phrase is used very rarely in modern vernacular.", "annotation_id": "10156834", "url": "https://genius.com/10156834", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Man of the Year", "line": "They threatened by my presence, ’cause I make ’em feel old", "vault_lines": ["They threatened by my presence, 'cause I make 'em feel old"], "meaning": "Old rappers fear Drake because they know how hard it is to maintain relevancy in the game for an extended period. When they see an up-and-comer like Drake starting to get recognized, they begin to fear their days on top are numbered.", "annotation_id": "1161906", "url": "https://genius.com/1161906", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Man of the Year", "line": "Spitta!", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Spitta is the nickname of former Cash Money artist Curren$y", "annotation_id": "1051153", "url": "https://genius.com/1051153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Man of the Year", "line": "Just remember where I live at, fifty thousand’s going gold", "vault_lines": ["Just remember where I live at, fifty thousand's going gold"], "meaning": "Prior to May 1st, 2008, in order for a record to be certified Gold in Canada (where Drake comes from) it needed to sell 50,000 copies. (In America the requirement is 500,000 for Gold certification, due to it’s much larger population). As of May 1st 2008, the requirement for Gold Certification in Canada has been lowered to 40,000.", "annotation_id": "83094", "url": "https://genius.com/83094", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Man of the Year", "line": "These Skee-Lo rappers wishin’ they could be tall", "vault_lines": ["These Skee-Lo rappers wishin' they could be tall"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to the the mid-90’s rapper Skee-Lo’s song, “I Wish”. One line in the chorus of the song “I Wish” says, “I wish I was a little bit taller, I wish I was a baller…”", "annotation_id": "83083", "url": "https://genius.com/83083", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Man of the Year", "status": "done", "targets": 7, "clicked": 7, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-man-of-the-year-lyrics", "genius_title": "Man of the Year"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "A lot of people just hit me up when my name is mentioned Shout out to KD, we relate, we get the same attention It’s rainin’ money, Oklahoma City Thunder The most successful rapper 35 and under", "vault_lines": ["A lot of people just hit me up when my name is mentioned", "Shout out to KD, we relate, we get the same attention", "It's rainin' money, Oklahoma City Thunder", "The most successful rapper 35 and under"], "meaning": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YeYj1549IB8\n\nDrake’s my favorite rapper. When you get a shout out in a song you look around and see if anyone else heard it. You feel, “somebody recognized me.”\n\nMe and a lot of other people were wondering if Drake was coming back with some new music. When I have a game, he texts me or Snapchats me and we talk about stuff. I’m sure when he got in the booth, he was feeling it a little bit and put it in there. It went together nice. I guess he just wanted me to hear it as I was going through the album. Once I heard it, I was like, “That was dope.”\n\nWe talk and going through that stuff, him and Meek Mill—it was entertainment for me because I’m such a huge rap fan—they both came with it. I think Drake set it off and went on another level. Meek came back with some jabs as well, so it’s always fun to be a part of that.", "annotation_id": "10080174", "url": "https://genius.com/10080174", "state": "verified", "votes": 287, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Told my momma that I found a lady in the east", "vault_lines": ["Told my momma that I found a lady in the east"], "meaning": "The “east” Drake is referring to is likely eastern Toronto, or Scarborough. Drake has mentioned that part of town on songs like “Energy” and “Worst Behavior”, and it’s where his homie Baka is from.\n\nDrake dropped a song called “Days In The East” in 2014 that is widely believed to be about Rihanna. Perhaps that’s who he’s referring to here as well.", "annotation_id": "9130528", "url": "https://genius.com/9130528", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "I gave niggas like you a reason for celebration", "vault_lines": ["I gave niggas like you a reason for celebration"], "meaning": "Before Drake was done doing favours for people, he would collaborate with many artists such as The Weeknd, Kendrick Lamar, Future, YG, Migos, among others, particularly early on in their career. These collaborations would usually catapult the artist to worldwide attention.\n\n\n\nTying into the previous line, Drake fires a shot at the collaborators he fell out of favour with, like Meek Mill, stating that, firstly, they are not as successful alone as they were when working with him and, lastly, he was the reason for their crowning moment.", "annotation_id": "9081647", "url": "https://genius.com/9081647", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "You get the message over and over like it was urgent And then act like you ain’t heard it when you see me in person The only thing I did to end up here was put the work in And did it with a purpose", "vault_lines": ["You get the message over and over like it was urgent", "And then act like you ain't heard it when you see me in person", "The only thing I did to end up here was put the work in", "And did it with a purpose", "Get high when I want"], "meaning": "Drake points out how pretentious some other rappers in the game are. He states that their music’s platinum status doesn’t mean much, because they get it mainly off of streams. He’s sure they’re aware of that, but they act like it’s a big deal anyway, just to show off.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/260460813373026304\n\nHowever, when Drake mentions their platinum status in a face-to-face conversation, they don’t want to brag about it because they know it isn’t that much of an achievement.\n\nDrake on the other hand has multiple platinum albums that he earned off of sales. He states he’s where he’s at now due to his work ethic and passion to be the best in the rap game and put Toronto on the map.", "annotation_id": "9077761", "url": "https://genius.com/9077761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Then he would leave us at the house and go out on a mission We probably would’ve got in less trouble just goin’ with him Feel like I never say much but, man, there’s a lot to know", "vault_lines": ["Feel like I never say much but, man, there's a lot to know", "Then he would leave us at the house and go out on a mission", "We probably would've got in less trouble just goin' with him"], "meaning": "Renny’s older brother would hang out with Renny and Drake sometimes, but other times he would go somewhere to do something dangerous, or illegal (a ‘mission’ is often the term used within gangs when a member goes out to perform an assigned task by the OG or ranking members). His “protective-older-brother instinct” didn’t want to get the little ones involved in such things, but Renny and Drake got themselves in trouble anyway.\n\nDrake has a reputation of rapping about his memories of growing up in Toronto, but he tell us that there are still many more stories than the ones he’s told already.", "annotation_id": "9077694", "url": "https://genius.com/9077694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "He was wavy, doin’ mixtapes out of your basement He let us hit the weed on occasion for entertainment", "vault_lines": ["He was wavy, doin' mixtapes out of your basement", "He let us hit the weed on occasion for entertainment"], "meaning": "Renny’s older brother was like any local rapper trying to get big from mixtapes.\n\nHe’d let Renny and Drake smoke because he thought it was funny watching them get high.", "annotation_id": "9075503", "url": "https://genius.com/9075503", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Used to have secret handshakes to confirm my friendships Nowadays, they just shakin’ my hand to hide the tension", "vault_lines": ["Used to have secret handshakes to confirm my friendships", "Nowadays, they just shakin' my hand to hide the tension"], "meaning": "While secret handshakes are a general indication that people are genuinely friends, shaking hands is a formality between two strangers when they first met.\n\nDrake states that people he once was cool with are now faking it in public and aren’t close to him anymore. What once used to be something reserved for two people with an established friendship is now done as front to cover up the underlying contempt.\n\nThis could be about subliminal diss to Kendrick Lamar. In a 2013 interview with Angie Martinez, when asked about Kendrick’s “Control” verse, Drizzy said that when he saw him days later at the MTV VMAs, Kendrick just shook his hand and pretended like nothing happened.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/r68BzLfbOyg?t=2m26s", "annotation_id": "9073927", "url": "https://genius.com/9073927", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Big Apple had the white Hummer parked right in front of Fluid And we be walkin’ in that bitch like we already knew it", "vault_lines": ["Big Apple had the white Hummer parked right in front of Fluid", "And we be walkin' in that bitch like we already knew it"], "meaning": "According to Toronto Life, “Big Apple” is better known as Swagg Boss, a Toronto street mogul who has dipped in retail, clothing and media, and gave Drake advice as he was coming up. Fluid Lounge was a nightclub in Toronto at 217 Richmond Street West.\n\nAlthough Drake was coming up at the time, he walked inside of the nightclub with confidence of a mainstream star like he’s now.", "annotation_id": "9072772", "url": "https://genius.com/9072772", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "But shit ain’t always how it seems when it’s sewn together Yeah, I let that last line breathe, it take a second to get it", "vault_lines": ["But shit ain’t always how it seems when it’s sewn together", "Yeah, I let that last line breathe, it take a second to get it", "Get high when I want"], "meaning": "Drake cleverly uses the homophone seem/seam (which is what you get when you sew two things together), to refer to how even though things may appear to be set a certain way, that isn’t always the case.\n\nThe line “when its so together” also sounds like “when its sewed together” which changes the meaning of the line. Although Drake’s winning in this rap game, he’s still struggling in other areas, specifically his love life.", "annotation_id": "9071712", "url": "https://genius.com/9071712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "I’m assumin’ everybody’s 35 and under That’s when I plan to retire, man, it’s already funded", "vault_lines": ["I'm assumin' everybody's 35 and under", "That's when I plan to retire, man, it's already funded"], "meaning": "The previous line makes it sound like Drake (29) is only claiming to be the most successful rapper (alive) under the age of 35, which would mean he believes himself to be more successful than the likes of Kendrick Lamar (28), J. Cole (31) and even his mentor Lil Wayne (33). This still leaves out the usual contenders for “most successful” like Jay Z (46), Kanye West (38), and Eminem (43), to name a few.\n\nHe’s claiming that really, he considers everyone to be under 35 for all intents and purposes (maybe believing 35 to be an expiration date for rappers); thus broadening his claim to ‘most successful in the game.’\n\nDrake has been thinking about retiring at 35 for a while now. In a 2012 interview with i-D he said:\n\nThere are artists who are 35 and up that still make rap and that still works for them. I don’t know if I want to be that guy. A lot of my music is about being young and figuring it all out. I don’t know if I can still make my brand of music when I am up there. Maybe I’ll be just acting or whatever it is. I plan to make as much music as I can until a story from Drake doesn’t impact you as it used to.\n\n35 is also Kevin Durant’s jersey number—tying this line to the previous reference.", "annotation_id": "9071125", "url": "https://genius.com/9071125", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "You platinum like wrappers On Hershey’s boy, that shit is worthless", "vault_lines": ["You platinum like wrappers", "On Hershey's boy, that shit is worthless"], "meaning": "Drake plays on “wrappers” and “rappers,” claiming they aren’t actually going platinum— their achievements are worthless.\n\nHershey kisses are wrapped in a lightweight aluminum foil, which resembles the color of platinum.\n\n\n\nDrake could be alluding to the fact that over the past few months many rappers (including Drake himself, and rival Kendrick Lamar) have gone platinum due to the new rules the RIAA issued.  Drake hints that these rappers aren’t going platinum off of sales, but they’re actually going platinum off of streams, which is a more attainable achievement.\n\nIt’s also possible he’s alluding to Meek’s platinum hit “All Eyes On You,” which features Nicki Minaj, “Hershey’s” is a near homophone of “her shit.”", "annotation_id": "9071117", "url": "https://genius.com/9071117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "You don’t be scorin’ perfect You don’t be workin’ like how we be workin’", "vault_lines": ["You don't be scorin' perfect", "You don't be workin' like how we be workin'"], "meaning": "Drake has always been a perfectionist. He’s meticulous with his craft, and his team has a strong work ethic when it comes to producing music that is top notch and widely praised by the public.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/356682981760438272\n\nDrake rapped similar lines in his song “The Motion:”\n\nSeem like everybody calling cause they want me on their song\nIt’s like every time I touch it I could never do no wrong", "annotation_id": "9071036", "url": "https://genius.com/9071036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "I used to hit the corner store to get to Tahiti Treat Now the talk at the corner store is I’m TBE The best ever, don’t ever question, you know better", "vault_lines": ["I used to hit the corner store to get to Tahiti Treat", "Now the talk at the corner store is I'm TBE", "The best ever, don't ever question, you know better"], "meaning": "Back in the day, Drake used get the fruity soda Tahiti Treat (now called Tahitian Treat) from his local corner store. Now with Drake being arguably the biggest rapper today, people in those same stores discuss if he’s the best ever.\n\n\n\nDrake’s immense success has drawn comparisons to Jay Z, who is considered by many to be the greatest rapper ever. Drake himself has even compared himself to Hov, more recently with the song “Summer Sixteen”, where he spits:\n\nI used to wanna be on Roc-A-Fella then I turned into Jay\n\nFurthermore, Drake has even been consigned by many rappers such as Kanye, who in an interview with Zane Lowe said that Drizzy was “the hottest rapper in the game.”", "annotation_id": "9070841", "url": "https://genius.com/9070841", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "You treat me like I’m born yesterday, you forgot my birthday", "vault_lines": ["You treat me like I'm born yesterday, you forgot my birthday"], "meaning": "Treating someone like they were born yesterday means that you perceive them as dumb and naive. Drake suggests that because he is relatively young (29), people still view him as inexperienced in the industry, despite his many accomplishments stating otherwise.\n\n\n\nForgetting Drake’s birthday, which is October 24th, also hints at his relationship with his ex-friends. They pretend to know him, but in reality they couldn’t care less.", "annotation_id": "9070617", "url": "https://genius.com/9070617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Weston Road flows, my confidence level gettin’ settled Don’t get hyped for the moment then start to backpedal Don’t let your newfound fame fool you Or cloud up your judgement to talk loosely, I really do this", "vault_lines": ["Weston Road Flows, I did this shit for my nigga Renny", "Weston Road flows, my confidence level gettin' settled", "Don't get hyped for the moment then start to backpedal", "Don't let your newfound fame fool you", "Or cloud up your judgement to talk loosely, I really do this"], "meaning": "In his interview with Zane Lowe, Drake mentions always having felt like an outsider. However, it now seems he’s finally getting the confidence a man of his talent and stature should have.\n\nThis appears to be a shot at Tory Lanez, who dissed Drake a few times but when confronted replied: “I don’t want any problems with that man.” Drake’s telling him not to get cocky and then go back on what he said.\n\n\n\nNote the wordplay, “Hype” was the song before “Weston Road Flows,” hence he said doesn’t start to “backpedal.”", "annotation_id": "9070555", "url": "https://genius.com/9070555", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Yeah, I brought your wifey out to Saint Martin She violated, I sent her back where it all started How quick they are to forget about their bachelor apartment Leave it to niggas like you to show em’ light in the darkness", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I brought your wifey out to Saint Martin", "She violated, I sent her back where it all started", "How quick they are to forget about their bachelor apartment", "Leave it to niggas like you to show em' light in the darkness"], "meaning": "This is a response to the lines Meek Mill said on “I’m the Plug” (Freestyle)“:\n\nYou took her to an island, I fucked her on a couch\nI fucked her in the studio, she never seen my house\nShe told me that you’re lame, I told her clean you out\nI got that pussy same day she made you eat her out, woo!\n\nWhen Drake was in Saint Martin filming the video for P Reign’s “DnF,” he hung out with Lira Galore, who is Rick Ross' ex-fiance and was also in the video. At the time of the shoot, Meek Mill was also rumored to be dating Lira, and although he denied he had sex with her, he seemed to confirm it in “I’m The Plug (Freestyle).”\n\nDrake confirms that he in fact flew Lira to Saint Martin, but once she was there, she didn’t care about Meek anymore. She realized that the actual lame was Meek and not Drake, therefore Meek “showed her the light in the darkness.” Alternatively, she could be saying the same thing she said to Meek just to please Drake, since she’s got a reputation to be a gold digger due to be linked to Drake and Meek before being with Rozay.", "annotation_id": "9070506", "url": "https://genius.com/9070506", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Creepin’ like Chilli without the tender, love, and care No tender love and care, no love and affection", "vault_lines": ["Creepin' like Chilli without the tender, love, and care", "No tender love and care, no love and affection"], "meaning": "Drake references 90’s R&B trio TLC—specifically he mentions member Rozonda “Chilli” Thomas and the group’s 1994 single, “Creep.”. TLC also stands for tender, love, and care.\n\n\n\nIn 2010 Drake covered TLC’s “I Get Lonely, Too.”", "annotation_id": "9070504", "url": "https://genius.com/9070504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Y’all was so afraid to lay claim to it Too busy face screwin’ on waste movements", "vault_lines": ["Y'all was so afraid to lay claim to it", "Too busy face screwin' on waste movements"], "meaning": "In the mid-2000s, there was a Toronto movement where people started calling the city the “Screwface Capital” because people from Toronto would hate on everything from their own city—from sports to music.\n\nThis could be another shot at Tory Lanez since he has been showing dislike for Drake quite a few times. Drake states that instead of focusing on his career and keep Toronto on the map, Tory trashes Drake’s music and brings negativity back to the city.", "annotation_id": "9070489", "url": "https://genius.com/9070489", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Drinkin’ Hpnotiq with Glenn Lewis, I been through it", "vault_lines": ["Drinkin' Hpnotiq with Glenn Lewis, I been through it"], "meaning": "Going back in time, Drake recounts a tender moment when he drank the blended alcoholic drink Hpnotiq with R&B singer Glenn Lewis, who is also from Toronto, Canada.", "annotation_id": "9070433", "url": "https://genius.com/9070433", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "First take Drake, you know I rarely have to take two it And they still take to it", "vault_lines": ["First take Drake, you know I rarely have to take two it", "And they still take to it"], "meaning": "When recording in the booth, artists tend to mess up their lines and need many tries to get the right take—but not Drake.\n\nIn a 2013 profile in GQ, Drake’s right-hand man 40 said:\n\nWe can do fifteen takes, and I swear, we almost always go back to the first one. That’s the one in which the emotion and flow and original thought is captured.\n\nThis line could also be a nod to being like one of the greats, Jay Z, who is also known for doing single takes and even once rapped:\n\nOne take Hov, I’m real at this rapping\n\nDrake could also be shouting out the ESPN show First Take, which he was a guest on in 2013.", "annotation_id": "9070412", "url": "https://genius.com/9070412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "But money can’t buy happiness, Jellee talkin’ truthful But I’m happiest when I can buy what I want Get high when I want", "vault_lines": ["But money can't buy happiness, Jellee talkin' truthful", "But I'm happiest when I can buy what I want", "Get high when I want"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Canadian rapper Jelleestone’s “Money (Part 1)”. In the song “Jellee” (short for Jelleestone) raps:\n\nMoney can’t buy me happiness\nBut I’m happiest when I can buy what I want, anytime that I want\nGet high when I want\n\nDrake acknowledges that even though the old saying “money can’t buy happiness” might be true, he feels like he is happier when he can buy whatever he wants and get high whenever he feels like it—without needing to worry about his economic situation. He’s spoken on the power of money in songs like “Dreams Money Can Buy.”", "annotation_id": "9070389", "url": "https://genius.com/9070389", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Cancun, when I got signed, they upgraded the suite Don Julio in the freezer that they gave us for free I get you all you can eat, just have some patience with me", "vault_lines": ["Cancun, when I got signed, they upgraded the suite", "Don Julio in the freezer that they gave us for free", "I get you all you can eat, just have some patience with me", "Get high when I want"], "meaning": "In 2009, Drake signed with Cash Money Records and received an advance of $2 million. After signing, Drake’s life was never the same again. He got high-profile suites at hotels and free bottles of Don Julio, a tequila which price ranges from $100-400.\n\n\n\nDrake’s willing to share his profits with his loved ones by pleasing their cravings. However, he needs to see if you’re really down with him. Being part of Drake’s entourage is not an easy task, so he wants to see if he can trust you first so he can spend on you later.", "annotation_id": "9070323", "url": "https://genius.com/9070323", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Look in the mirror, I’m closer than I really appear", "vault_lines": ["Look in the mirror, I'm closer than I really appear"], "meaning": "Passenger side car mirrors in the US and Canada (and also India) are required to display this notice that neighboring vehicles are closer to your car than the mirror may show. This optical illusion happens because side mirrors are constructed to be slightly convex in order to broaden the driver’s field of view. So you can see more, but it all looks a bit farther away than it really is, which makes this another kind of trade off.\n\n\n\nDrake plays with the phrase, alluding to these big OG rappers that think he’s still behind them. Some people feel that Drake still has ten years to go, but looking at what he’s already achieved, he’s a lot closer than they think.", "annotation_id": "9070281", "url": "https://genius.com/9070281", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "You was ridin’ TTC metro, I had the place boomin’", "vault_lines": ["You was ridin' TTC metro, I had the place boomin'"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out Metro Boomin, who executive produced his tape with Future, What a Time to be Alive, and produced “Child’s Play.” He connects both “metro” and “boomin”; at the same time he mentions his illustrious rap career towards the “New Toronto.” While they were taking the TTC (which stands for Toronto Transit Commission), Drake was opening the door for the Canadian rap scene.\n\nAccording to The FADER, Metro Boomin actually got the first part of his name from the MetroLink—the subway system in his hometown of St. Louis'.", "annotation_id": "9070270", "url": "https://genius.com/9070270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "40 the only one that know how I deal with the pressure A couple nights where it started to feel like the feelings fadin’ A lot of problems that can’t be fixed with a conversation", "vault_lines": ["40 the only one that know how I deal with the pressure", "A couple nights where it started to feel like the feelings fadin'", "A lot of problems that can't be fixed with a conversation"], "meaning": "Noah “40” Shebib is Drake’s producer and one of his closest friends. As Drake has said in “You & The 6,” he uses alcohol to deal with his problems. With a company to run, music to create, and personal situations, the toll Drake must be feeling on his mental health is something that can’t be fixed with a simple conversation.\n\nDrake has rapped similar lines in his song “Successful”:\n\nAnd 40, that was all before I showed up\nAnd brought a thousand dollars worth of drinks and got poured up", "annotation_id": "9070233", "url": "https://genius.com/9070233", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "I got a price on my head but there’s a risk to collect it I might be here as a vessel to teach people a lesson Feel like they wanted me dead but couldn’t pull it together Yeah, but here we are and it’s a new semester", "vault_lines": ["I got a price on my head but there's a risk to collect it", "I might be here as a vessel to teach people a lesson", "Feel like they wanted me dead but couldn't pull it together", "Yeah, but here we are and it's a new semester", "And they still take to it"], "meaning": "In 2016, Drake was arguably the most popular figure in hip-hop, which made him a target for potential usurpers. However, taking on such a colossal figure comes with its risks, and few if any are willing to take them. Just ask Meek Mill what happens when you instigate a feud with Drake and lose.\n\n\n\nA similar sentiment was expressed on “5AM in Toronto”:\n\nI could load every gun with bullets that fire backwards\nYou probably wouldn’t lose a single rapper\n\n“Pull it” refers to pulling a trigger, and no-one has yet been able to do that to Drake, despite their conspiracies.", "annotation_id": "9070221", "url": "https://genius.com/9070221", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "You wouldn’t tell me you loved me, started seein’ Monique Last time I heard from Monique, T-Minus was makin’ beats", "vault_lines": ["You wouldn't tell me you loved me, started seein' Monique", "Last time I heard from Monique, T-Minus was makin' beats"], "meaning": "In a very Drake move, when one woman wouldn’t profess her love to him, he moved on to another woman named Monique. Ironically, the last time he heard from Monique was when producer T-Minus was popular, around 2013.\n\nT-Minus was everywhere in the early 2010s, and he even produced a couple of hits for Drake, including “The Motto” and “Make Me Proud”—two records on Take Care. Despite his success, T-Minus' credits have dropped since 2013 even though he’s still signed with Drake’s OVO Sound record label.\n\n\n\nComplex brought this line up to T-Minus in 2019 and after laughing about it, he said:\n\n\nYou know what, a lot of people still think that I retired. I never actually “retired.” I was just taking a break from the music for a bit and… I shouldn’t even say taking a break from the music itself, just taking a break from the business. I just needed to chill and just come from an organic place. From like, the roots. You know what I mean? Like, how it all kind of started?\n\nSo I was just making beats. And naturally, I was still getting placements, because I had beats floating around. Like I had the record with Big Sean on Dark Sky Paradise, I had cut a record with Bieber, on Journals. I had a record with Sia. I just had stuff just popping out, all over the place. You know what I mean? But this is just while I was breaking. I don’t know, I guess there’s still this illusion that I retired. But no, I wasn’t in a retirement. I was more kind of like how an artist would make an album, and then chill for like, two years and then come back out. But mine was just more of a three year stunt, you know what I mean? I was gone for a bit. But I never truly left.", "annotation_id": "9070213", "url": "https://genius.com/9070213", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Your best day is my worst day, I get green like Earth Day", "vault_lines": ["Your best day is my worst day, I get green like Earth Day"], "meaning": "Even on his worst days, Drake is still more successful than most of the rap game.\n\nHe couldn’t care less as he continues getting his money (green) like it was Earth Day. Drake’s known for being one of the biggest rappers in the game, with a net worth of $60 million.\n\n\n\nInterestingly, Earth Day 2016 was on April 22, 2016, a week before Views dropped on April 29, 2016.", "annotation_id": "9070183", "url": "https://genius.com/9070183", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Feel like the difference between us really startin’ to show I’m lookin at they first week numbers like, “What are those?” I mean, you boys not even coming close", "vault_lines": ["Feel like the difference between us really startin' to show", "I'm lookin at they first week numbers like, \"What are those?\"", "I mean, you boys not even coming close"], "meaning": "Even not counting streams, Views still sold over 850,000 copies in its first week, becoming Drake’s biggest sales week ever and one of the largest in hip-hop history.\n\nAlthough he didn’t always shatter records, Drake has consistently put up huge first week numbers. Complex even tracked all of his first week sales in this infographic:\n\n\n\nDrake also jokingly references the “What are those?” meme.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FixfVJ9gOIQ", "annotation_id": "9070178", "url": "https://genius.com/9070178", "state": "accepted", "votes": 160, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "You number one and I’m Eddie Murphy, we tradin’ places", "vault_lines": ["You number one and I'm Eddie Murphy, we tradin' places"], "meaning": "Trading Places is a 1983 comedy starring Eddie Murphy. In the movie, Murphy plays a character who begins his life as a poor street hustler who trades places with an investor and becomes economically prosperous in the position of a managing director.\n\nDrake makes this reference to explain how he’s trading places with whoever is number 1 in the rap game at the moment.", "annotation_id": "9070176", "url": "https://genius.com/9070176", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Been flowin’ stupid since Vince Carter Was on some through the legs arm in the hoop shit", "vault_lines": ["Been flowin' stupid since Vince Carter", "Was on some through the legs arm in the hoop shit"], "meaning": "During the 2000 NBA All-Star Dunk Contest, Toronto Raptors shooting guard Vince Carter threw down a series of dunks, including a stunning arm-in-rim dunk and a through-the-legs slam. Drake was only 13 years old at the time, but he was already nice at rapping by then.\n\nThrough the legs:\n\n\n\nArm in the hoop:\n\n\n\nCarter was still in the league when this song came out in 2016, playing for the Memphis Grizzlies.\n\nInterestingly enough, Drake would later join the Toronto Raptors in honoring Vince Carter by retiring his jersey in 2024, celebrating the legacy of the player who put Canadian basketball on the map.", "annotation_id": "9070153", "url": "https://genius.com/9070153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "line": "Weston Road Flows, I did this shit for my nigga Renny Back when we couldn’t buy pizza ’cause we were down to pennies Feel like we go back a century, run in the store for Centuries Then bring it back to your brother, man, we had to respect it", "vault_lines": ["Weston Road Flows, I did this shit for my nigga Renny", "Back when we couldn't buy pizza 'cause we were down to pennies", "Feel like we go back a century, run in the store for Centuries", "Then bring it back to your brother, man, we had to respect it"], "meaning": "In an interview with Complex, Drake described his childhood:\n\nHere’s the thing, I grew up on Weston Road. That’s near the west end of the city. It’s not the nicest area in the world. I grew up there. […] My mother was very sick. We were very poor, like broke. The only money I had coming in was off of Canadian TV, which isn’t that much money when you break it down.\n\nRenny was Drake’s childhood friend who pursued a dangerous lifestyle growing up in Toronto, whom he mentioned in “You & The 6.” It seems that Drake and Renny looked up to Renny’s brother so much that they would buy him Centuries—which is a slang for Century Sam, a cigar brand used to wrap weed blunts—at his request.", "annotation_id": "9070142", "url": "https://genius.com/9070142", "state": "accepted", "votes": 129, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Weston Road Flows", "status": "done", "targets": 32, "clicked": 32, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-weston-road-flows-lyrics", "genius_title": "Weston Road Flows"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Reny", "vault_lines": ["Hangin' with Aaron Bell and Reny"], "meaning": "A drake freidn who we can find too at weston road flows\nhttps://genius.com/9070142", "annotation_id": "39921203", "url": "https://genius.com/39921203", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Like that girl from your gym who trains you I know you wanna arrange it, you told me she’s free Thursday And I’m sure that she’s an angel, but she don’t want this life", "vault_lines": ["Like that girl from your gym who trains you", "I know you wanna arrange it, you told me she's free Thursday", "And I'm sure that she's an angel, but she don't want this life"], "meaning": "In this verse, Drake addresses his mother’s tendency to disapprove of his relationships and suggest alternative partners she considers more suitable.\n“That girl from your gym who trains you” is widely believed to reference a real person, not a metaphor.\n\nA gym trainer who claims to train Drake’s mother has stated that this lyric refers directly to her and that the verse was inspired by that dynamic.\n\nDrake ultimately frames the woman as someone outside his celebrity lifestyle — ideal in theory, but incompatible with the realities of fame and timing.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8QgZa3XwPIg", "annotation_id": "38504397", "url": "https://genius.com/38504397", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Shit that can only be created if you go through it", "vault_lines": ["Shit that can only be created if you go through it"], "meaning": "The popular idea that you can only perfectly manifest what you have experienced is very common in music; from drug dealers knowing how to rap about drugs to heartbroken people knowing how to sing their heart out.", "annotation_id": "35542221", "url": "https://genius.com/35542221", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "I’m content with this story, who are we not to forgive him?", "vault_lines": ["I'm content with this story, who are we not to forgive him?"], "meaning": "Drake gets back down to Earth and realizes his father, who may have not been present his whole life, is a human being who can make mistakes.\nAnd given his father’s plead for forgiveness, Drake becomes mature enough to forgive him and realize that he is a human too.\n\nA song very similar to this line (yet unrelated to Drake) is “I Love You, I Hate You” by Little Simz", "annotation_id": "35542208", "url": "https://genius.com/35542208", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Know I don’t call enough, momma I just been working with so little time for personal, momma, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I don't pretend with this, momma", "Know I don't call enough, momma", "I just been working with so little time for personal, momma, yeah"], "meaning": "He knows he needs to call his mom more, he’s just so busy", "annotation_id": "34506669", "url": "https://genius.com/34506669", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "I mean I kill ’em every time they do a song with me, momma", "vault_lines": ["I mean I kill 'em every time they do a song with me, momma", "I sing a hook they sing along with me, momma"], "meaning": "Every time he has a feature or is featured on a song, he’s saying he’s the better part of the song", "annotation_id": "34506658", "url": "https://genius.com/34506658", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Retired teacher, but your words still got me evolvin’", "vault_lines": ["Retired teacher, but your words still got me evolvin'"], "meaning": "His mom used to be a teacher, yet Drake’s still learning from her", "annotation_id": "34506654", "url": "https://genius.com/34506654", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Ain’t been returning the texts, so she been reading the press She got Google Alerts, them shits go straight to her phone She worry ’bout me from home, you know she raised me alone", "vault_lines": ["You got the sweetest heart", "Ain't been returning the texts, so she been reading the press", "She got Google Alerts, them shits go straight to her phone", "She worry 'bout me from home, you know she raised me alone"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t have to time to text back, so his mom reads about him from the internet, making sure she’s notified whenever there’s a new alert. This also touches on the subject that she was a single mom.", "annotation_id": "34506622", "url": "https://genius.com/34506622", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "She said, “I heard you back with you know who” I told her, “girl, I’m always back with you know who,” yeah", "vault_lines": ["She said, \"I heard you back with you know who\"", "I told her, \"girl, I'm always back with you know who,\" yeah"], "meaning": "his mom heard that he’s back with his ex , & he confirmed this for her, saying they’re always back together", "annotation_id": "34506617", "url": "https://genius.com/34506617", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "That nigga Memphis for real, girl, he love you to death", "vault_lines": ["That nigga Memphis for real, girl, he love you to death"], "meaning": "Although Drake is from Toronto, his dad was originally from Memphis Tennessee. After the divorce Drake got to experience his dad’s perspective in Memphis when he was moving back and forth which is why he salutes him.\n\n[2nd half]\n\nEven after the divorce Drake can tell that his father will always have some love for Drake’s mother despite the terms that they are currently on.", "annotation_id": "30899411", "url": "https://genius.com/30899411", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Call him after we get off the phone and show him some love", "vault_lines": ["Call him after we get off the phone and show him some love"], "meaning": "Although Drake’s Dad Dennis Graham has been absent throughout Drake’s life, Drake obviously still has a place inside of him with some love for his pops even throughout the trauma he had to endure.", "annotation_id": "30899375", "url": "https://genius.com/30899375", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Yeah! Having conversations with momma, we start talkin’ ’bout dad", "vault_lines": ["Having conversations with momma, we start talkin' 'bout dad"], "meaning": "After talking about his mom in the first verse, Drake does an 180 and opens up on the dad topic.", "annotation_id": "30899323", "url": "https://genius.com/30899323", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Shit could’ve gone south for me, he looked out for me, ma He never let me do drugs He let me shoot a gun one summer, but out there everyone does", "vault_lines": ["Shit could've gone south for me, he looked out for me, ma", "He never let me do drugs", "He let me shoot a gun one summer, but out there everyone does"], "meaning": "Drake’s dad may not have had a strong presence in his life when he was growing up, but he made sure to steer him down the right path when he could. He also wasn’t the most conventional and protective father, as he was letting Drake shoot guns as a child, but when he was around, he made sure that Drake stayed out of trouble. This could have been the difference that allowed Drake to remain off the streets and pursue music.", "annotation_id": "28316474", "url": "https://genius.com/28316474", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "“Don’t ever take advice,” that was great advice", "vault_lines": ["\"Don't ever take advice,\" that was great advice"], "meaning": "This line is an example of an Oxymoron. The concept of not taking advice is in itself a piece of advice, therefore contradicting itself. Drake  acknowledges this by saying “that was great advice”", "annotation_id": "24670682", "url": "https://genius.com/24670682", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "I got no friends in this, momma", "vault_lines": ["I got no friends in this, momma"], "meaning": "Ever since Kendrick took aim at Drake on his infamous Control verse, Drake has had no shortage of beefs.\n\nBy 2021, Drake had beefed with Meek Mill, Pusha T, Kanye West, Kendrick Lamar, and plenty more.\n\nWhen you’re the king of hip hop, you’re gonna get aimed at by everyone else in the game. Hip hop is similar to Game of Thrones in that way. Drake feels like he’s in some deep shit.\n\nHe’s getting assassinated left and right.\n\nHe is the King of the North, after all.", "annotation_id": "22256341", "url": "https://genius.com/22256341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "I pull the knife out my back and I cut they throat with it, momma I’m Game of Thrones with it, momma I’m Home Alone with it, momma", "vault_lines": ["I pull the knife out my back and I cut they throat with it, momma", "I'm Game of Thrones with it, momma", "I'm Home Alone with it, momma", "I'm not condoning it, momma"], "meaning": "Drake expresses how vengeful he is to people who he’s been betrayed or threatened by.\n\nThe “Game of Thrones” television series is notorious for it’s unflinching violence and the constant plot twists. Drake compares his position in the rap game to what they do in the Game of Thrones. While “Home Alone” is a popular film about a young boy who uses violent & creative tactics to fend off burglars during Christmas time. Drake compares his tactics to the tactics of the young boy.\n\nDropping this album unexpectedly was just one of his many strategies to continue his take over.", "annotation_id": "4889099", "url": "https://genius.com/4889099", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Yeah, this is a crazy life But you and the six raised me right", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, this is a crazy life", "But you and the six raised me right", "You and the six raised me right, that shit saved my life"], "meaning": "Drake was born in Toronto, Ontario, Canada in 1986, and although his father has been absent, his mother – and his city – raised him well.\n\nNote that his recurring use of “The 6” refers to Toronto’s two area codes, the 416 and the 647. Drake seems to have created this brand himself.\n\n\n\nDrizzy is Jewish Canadian, and he attended a Jewish Day School and even had a Bar Mitzvah, probably due to his mother’s influence.", "annotation_id": "4889089", "url": "https://genius.com/4889089", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Let’s just call this shit even, we got some things to believe in", "vault_lines": ["Let's just call this shit even, we got some things to believe in"], "meaning": "Drake at this point is wrapping up listing the accomplishments he has achieved. Though he’ll never be able to get rid of the emotional loss of not always having his father around when growing up, at this point he is gonna let bygones be bygones and look to their future together.", "annotation_id": "4888827", "url": "https://genius.com/4888827", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "I just- I- I can’t be out here being vulnerable, momma", "vault_lines": ["I just- I- I can't be out here being vulnerable, momma"], "meaning": "Where Drake is right now leaves little room for him to be vulnerable in his music, but there is a slight bit of irony since he’s put this on the biggest project of the year and he adds the stutter for effect to show he’s hiding that vulnerability. Lately, Drake’s musical persona switched up with releases like “0-100/The Catch Up” and “6 God,” which might have left some of his day one fans, and possibly even his momma, behind leaving them to wonder what happened to that old Drake.", "annotation_id": "4888748", "url": "https://genius.com/4888748", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "’Cause I’m not acting tough Or making stories up ’bout where I’m actually from, yeah", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I'm not acting tough", "Or making stories up 'bout where I'm actually from, yeah"], "meaning": "Growing up Jewish was hard for Drake while being black, yet he stays true to himself instead of pretending to be hard and tough or that he’s from a different area to sell his music.\n\nThis line can be read as a diss towards Tyga, considering Drake’s other shots at him on this album, combined with allegations that Tyga did not actually grow up in Compton.\n\nNote the parallels that Drake draws to the bar a few lines up:\n\nHe made me listen to his music, old music, soul music // Shit that can be created only if you go through it\n\nIt certainly seems like Drake is implying that Tyga, and others throughout the music industry, are not making soul music because they didn’t go through it.", "annotation_id": "4888721", "url": "https://genius.com/4888721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "You know he dropping a single, he saying this is his window", "vault_lines": ["You know he dropping a single, he saying this is his window"], "meaning": "Drake’s Dad, Dennis Graham, dropped a single called “Kinda Crazy”.\n\nHe feels that this is “his window” because he’s always referred to as Drake’s father, and someone who is very successful in the music industry. He wants some of the action and to make a name for himself.\n\nNot to mention he’s addressing the controversy over whether Drake might feel that his father is trying to eat off his fame. Despite not directly addressing the issue, Drake mentioning the subject makes him seem somewhat accepting. Not to mention the single will get EVEN MORE attention now that Drake acknowledged it on his surprise album.", "annotation_id": "4888560", "url": "https://genius.com/4888560", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "I used to get teased for being black And now I’m here and I’m not black enough", "vault_lines": ["I used to get teased for being black", "And now I'm here and I'm not black enough"], "meaning": "For for Vibe’s Winter 2014 issue–also called “The Race Issue”, Drake spoke to as a child having to deal with kids, who did not understand that he could share black and Jewish identity.“The racism I experienced was being Jewish,“ \"Jewish kids didn’t understand how I could be black and Jewish…It was just stupid, annoying rich kids that were close-minded and mean.\"\n\nHe might no longer be a kid, but for his entire rap career he has been criticized for not being “hard” enough and fought being called “soft.” But at this point most rappers no matter where they come from would do anything for a co-sign for call from his phone.", "annotation_id": "4888457", "url": "https://genius.com/4888457", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "And fuck it, look how we’re living", "vault_lines": ["But he just want our forgiveness, and fuck it, look how we're living"], "meaning": "Drake’s rapped before about how he feels guilty for living lavish while his father’s living hand to mouth:\n\nHoping Western Union doing currency exchange\nCause my dad called and got me feeling guilty and ashamed\nLike, how I had a Rolls and I went and got a Range\nAnd he paying for his cigarettes with dollars and some change\nDamn, and I could only feel his pain\nCause in Memphis Tennessee there’s only so much to attain\n\nWith the amount of money and the lavish lifestyle Drake has, it would be selfish of him to not forgive his father. Forgiveness wouldn’t hurt him any. Drake questions how he and his mom could reject someone who admits his wrongs and isn’t asking for money, but only for forgiveness.", "annotation_id": "4888442", "url": "https://genius.com/4888442", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "And look I hate it when you hate on all my girlfriends And assistants always convinced that there’s someone better", "vault_lines": ["And look I hate it when you hate on all my girlfriends", "And assistants always convinced that there's someone better"], "meaning": "Back on “Club Paradise”, Drake’s mom took a far different stance on his women, actually making the effort to check up on them.\n\nI wish she’d stop checking up on women I can’t stand / ‘Cause I got new girls I could use her opinion on\n\nDrake, however, has also changed, no longer searching for new girls, but instead hoping to get approval on the one(s) in his life.", "annotation_id": "4888423", "url": "https://genius.com/4888423", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "But I just roll with it, momma, rolling stone with it, momma Gotta be careful around Rolling Stone Or anyone that’s tryna throw stones at me, momma I’m not condoning it, momma They will not tear nothing down,", "vault_lines": ["I'm Home Alone with it, momma", "It's just they cloning me, momma", "But I just roll with it, momma, rolling stone with it, momma", "Gotta be careful around Rolling Stone", "Or anyone that's tryna throw stones at me, momma", "I'm not condoning it, momma", "They will not tear nothing down,"], "meaning": "Drake’s saying he’s a music legend, like the Rolling Stones.\n\n\n\nBut he’s still bitter about an interview with Rolling Stone which he claims contained fabricated or misrepresented quotes dissing rappers Kanye and Jay-Z.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/433971541622001664\n\nHe’s not endorsing that negativity and won’t let press vultures destroy while he builds and builds more.\n\nA rolling stone is also defined as; “a person who is unwilling to settle for long in one place”. Drake keeps it moving.", "annotation_id": "4888402", "url": "https://genius.com/4888402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "He made me listen to his music, old music, soul music", "vault_lines": ["He made me listen to his music, old music, soul music"], "meaning": "Drake’s father, a professional jazz and soul drummer, had a profound musical influence on him.\n\nDrake said to GQ in 2012:\n\nWhen my dad drove me to Memphis one time from Toronto, it was a 20-something hour drive. I brought my Doggystyle cassette. I asked him, “Dad, can I please play this? Mom won’t let me play it in the house.” He was like, “Yeah. You can play it for 20 minutes. And then we listen to an hour of my music…It really was pivotal for me because I started listening to the melodies and the emotion in this music that he was playing me. It grabbed me maybe even a little more than that Doggystyle cassette did. I would say that I’m more moved by melody, even though I love to rap.", "annotation_id": "4888368", "url": "https://genius.com/4888368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "At least I been to a prison, at least I know what it’s like I used to rap on the phone with one of his friends doing life And now I got me a Grammy, that could be part of the reason", "vault_lines": ["At least I been to a prison, at least I know what it's like", "I used to rap on the phone with one of his friends doing life", "And now I got me a Grammy, that could be part of the reason"], "meaning": "Drake has said that when his father was in prison, he would rap over the phone to his dad’s cellmate, who also wrote raps.\n\n\n\nDrake won the Grammy for Best Rap Album for Take Care at the 2013 Grammys. He thinks his father’s support from prison may have helped him get there.\n\nIn this interview, mostly discussing the release of his third studio album, Drake mentions rapping on the phone with his dad’s cell mate, a homeless rapper that went by the name Poverty.", "annotation_id": "4888275", "url": "https://genius.com/4888275", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "line": "Do you remember back to Weston Road, Scarlett Road?", "vault_lines": ["Do you remember back to Weston Road, Scarlett Road?"], "meaning": "Weston is one of the most dangerous areas in Toronto, earning itself the title “Toronto’s Murder Capital.” Drake is originally from Weston, but he later moved to the more affluent area of Forest Hill, also in Toronto.\n\n\n\nHe has mentioned Eglinton Road before.", "annotation_id": "4887985", "url": "https://genius.com/4887985", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You & The 6", "status": "done", "targets": 28, "clicked": 28, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-you-and-the-6-lyrics", "genius_title": "You & The 6"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "But, who am I to complain now? I’m still around, they know Yeah", "vault_lines": ["But, who am I to complain now? I'm still around, they know"], "meaning": "At the end of all of his introspection and musing, Drake realizes he shouldn’t be complaining much. Compared to artists he mentions in the previous lines (Keyshia Cole, Young Dro), Drake is having significantly greater, lasting success in the industry, and everyone is aware of it. “They know” also serves as a callback to “Headlines” from Take Care.", "annotation_id": "7850274", "url": "https://genius.com/7850274", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "We just supposed to get the pie, then split it in two? Supposed to forget your mistakes, but not forget about you?", "vault_lines": ["We ain't in it, we just ghostride", "We just supposed to get the pie, then split it in two?", "Supposed to forget your mistakes, but not forget about you?"], "meaning": "Though he’s had his own share of influences, Drake’s coverage of more introspective topics through a melodic rap style has been immensely successful commercially and has paved the way for artists to mimic this approach while gaining respect from the hip-hop community and attention from the pop fans. He wants to be compensated accordingly for his efforts instead of evenly sharing the “pie” of success with similar artists who may not have done as much to advance the style.\n\nAt the same time, Drake released this tape in the midst of his feud with his Cash Money label: Drake is supposed to make the money and share it with members of the label who didn’t do anything? Forget about how they’ve wronged him and think of them when the money comes?", "annotation_id": "7840399", "url": "https://genius.com/7840399", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "My plan was always to make the product jump off the shelf And treat the money like secrets, keep the shit to ourself", "vault_lines": ["My plan was always to make the product jump off the shelf", "And treat the money like secrets, keep the shit to ourself"], "meaning": "Drake’s overall goal is to sell the most records as possible. “Jump off the shelf” alludes to the fact that his music will be a must buy. You won’t have to reach up to put it in your cart, consider it done.\n\n\n\nAnd when the loot comes in, he is going to make sure it stays within OVO because remember, “No New Friends”.", "annotation_id": "7836673", "url": "https://genius.com/7836673", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "These kids’ll hit your noodle, then take a girl to the movies", "vault_lines": ["These kids'll hit your noodle, then take a girl to the movies"], "meaning": "He is referring to the disregard for human life, where the kids can shoot someone in the head (their noodle), then just go to the movies as if nothing happened.\n\nThere might also be wordplay here: “noodle… movie” = “dinner and a movie”.", "annotation_id": "7835723", "url": "https://genius.com/7835723", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "They been droppin’ out on both sides We ain’t in it, we just ghostride", "vault_lines": ["They been droppin' out on both sides", "We ain't in it, we just ghostride"], "meaning": "“Ghost riding the whip” refers to the act of putting an automatic car in drive and hanging out on or near the car while it’s still running. The car appears to be driving itself, birthing the name “ghost ride” for the trend.\n\n\n\nAs violence escalates in Toronto, kids are dropping out of school and getting killed. Drake and his crew are not involved, they’re just aware of what’s happening.\n\nIn fact, a shooting occurred at one of his own shows in Toronto where two people were shot dead. Drake said “I am plagued and pained by the violence that continues to escalate in our city” in a statement following the incident.", "annotation_id": "7835703", "url": "https://genius.com/7835703", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "And women that you seen on TV look better in person And either they wanna fuck you Or convince you that they care and see where it goes from there, but These ain’t the girls from Brampton, this ain’t that local action", "vault_lines": ["And women that you seen on TV look better in person", "And either they wanna fuck you", "Or convince you that they care and see where it goes from there, but", "These ain't the girls from Brampton, this ain't that local action"], "meaning": "Some good straight-up bragging from Drizzy here: not only is he famous enough to be bumping shoulders with people you see on TV, but he wants you to know that they actually look better when you’re hanging out with them than they do on screen.\n\nBut Aubrey “No New Friends” Graham seems skeptical of these women, who as he says in the next line “ain’t the girls from Brampton.”\n\nBrampton is located just outside of Toronto, on the south side of the GTA (Greater Toronto Area).", "annotation_id": "7835581", "url": "https://genius.com/7835581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "I got a club in the Raptors arena Championship celebrations durin’ regular season", "vault_lines": ["I got a club in the Raptors arena", "Championship celebrations durin' regular season"], "meaning": "Drake has his own exclusive club in the Scotiabank Arena, his hometown Toronto Raptors' arena, called the “Sher Club.”\n\nWhile championships are normally celebrated after the regular season, Drake stays winning throughout the year.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/2NHD00jQI9/", "annotation_id": "7835485", "url": "https://genius.com/7835485", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "And your absence is very concernin’ It’s like you went on vacation with no plan of returnin’", "vault_lines": ["And your absence is very concernin'", "It's like you went on vacation with no plan of returnin'"], "meaning": "Another jab at Meek Mill, who recently got himself into some beef with Drake when he accused him on Twitter of not writing his own lyrics. Drake responded to Meek’s tweets with two well-received tracks, “Charged Up” and “Back to Back”.\n\nMeek fired back with some more tweets and a diss track, “Wanna Know”, that received generally negative reviews. There has been no more sound from Meek Mill since.", "annotation_id": "7835444", "url": "https://genius.com/7835444", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "I’m tired of awkward exchanges and niggas’ crooked ways Tired of champagne toasts with people that look away", "vault_lines": ["I'm tired of awkward exchanges and niggas' crooked ways", "Tired of champagne toasts with people that look away"], "meaning": "Drake is sick of all the snakes and actors who pretend to like him for his money or after he does something for them. As Drake says on “Started from the Bottom”:\n\nFuck a fake friend, where your real friends at?\n\nSomeone who toasts you but won’t look you in the eye doesn’t actually appreciate your success—they’re envious of it.\n\n A clear shot at his Cash Money problems.", "annotation_id": "7835436", "url": "https://genius.com/7835436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Back in the city, shit is gettin’ brutal", "vault_lines": ["Back in the city, shit is gettin' brutal"], "meaning": "Drizzy has seen Toronto evolve throughout his childhood there. He’s realized it’s getting more and more dangerous and violent as time goes on.\n\nIn his short film, “Jungle,” he describes the energy there as dark: https://vimeo.com/119416353", "annotation_id": "7835422", "url": "https://genius.com/7835422", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "I mean, I’ll say hats off for a solid effort But we didn’t flinch for a second, we got our shit together (6) Yeah, not here to fight wars But niggas wanna talk high scores", "vault_lines": ["But niggas wanna talk high scores", "I mean, I'll say hats off for a solid effort", "But we didn't flinch for a second, we got our shit together (6)", "Yeah, not here to fight wars"], "meaning": "Drake, sportsmanlike, congratulates Meek Mill, Funkmaster Flex and co. for attempting to destroy his career. Meek released just one diss track, “Wanna Know” to Drake’s “Charged Up”, “Back to Back” and OVO Fest onslaught.\n\nBut he’s comfortable at the top of the rap game, above a fray of childish bickering that reminds him of kids fighting over high scores at the arcade", "annotation_id": "7835412", "url": "https://genius.com/7835412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "The hate is just bringin’ me and my people closer, actually What happened to the things you niggas said was supposed to happen? Are we just supposed to ignore the fact that it never happened?", "vault_lines": ["The hate is just bringin' me and my people closer, actually", "What happened to the things you niggas said was supposed to happen?", "Are we just supposed to ignore the fact that it never happened?"], "meaning": "Drake has been the most successful rap artist of recent years and because of this has made plenty of enemies. Many thought that the ghostwriting allegations earlier in the year would be his demise, but if anything it’s given him a boost.\n\nFunk Master Flex claimed he had more ghostwritten tracks, and that he had access to a Meek Mill diss that he would play before anyone else… but it didn’t happen. Tyga said Drake would catch the fade from Chris Brown… but it didn’t happen.", "annotation_id": "7835352", "url": "https://genius.com/7835352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Aw, nigga, that shit gotta go platinum", "vault_lines": ["Aw, nigga, that shit gotta go platinum"], "meaning": "Drake’s such a sales phenomenon that even his overreaction to another artist’s diss will go platinum. (Indeed, as of September 2015, “Back to Back,” his reply to Meek Mill, has sold 253,000 copies in the United States.)", "annotation_id": "7835342", "url": "https://genius.com/7835342", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Four thousand square feet just isn’t as spacious You loved me back in the basement, guess it is what we make it", "vault_lines": ["We ain't in it, we just ghostride", "Four thousand square feet just isn't as spacious", "You loved me back in the basement, guess it is what we make it"], "meaning": "Drake’s no longer satisfied with 4,000 square feet (which in Toronto might go for like $2.5 million)—just as he’s elevated his rap game into a different “space,” he’s literally outgrown the house he grew up in.\n\nBut he says he’s not ashamed of his roots—in fact, he made the most of them, and we loved him for it. Here he is giving a tour of his childhood home. Started from the bottom? You decide:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7OisZAqxeEI", "annotation_id": "7835335", "url": "https://genius.com/7835335", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Luckily, I’m great at avoidin’ distraction Used to give no reaction, now I’m overreacting", "vault_lines": ["Luckily, I'm great at avoidin' distraction", "Used to give no reaction, now I'm overreacting"], "meaning": "Drake affirms that he is good at avoiding distraction and he’s got a point.\n\nIt started with “Drake the type of…”, a meme series joking about Drizzy being so emotional in his songs. Later came the shots to him.\n\nDrizzy didn’t used to respond to those disses directly, but now it seems like he’s really overreacting. This year, unlike Papi said in the past, he replied to Tyga and to Meek Mill twice, on Charged Up & Back to Back.", "annotation_id": "7835331", "url": "https://genius.com/7835331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Drastically changin’, thank you for all your patience I’m just in a different space and I choose to embrace it", "vault_lines": ["Drastically changin', thank you for all your patience", "I'm just in a different space and I choose to embrace it"], "meaning": "Drake has come a long way since 2008. At this time in his life you can almost understand the kind of pressure he must be under to release the highest quality music possible. The leader is supposed to set the example and, as 40 said on Twitter, Drake is arguably the king of the moment:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/OVO40/status/645318125722931200\n\nHe’s in a different space now—no longer the rapper that his fans fell in love with on “Comeback Season” and So Far Gone. He started to switch his style on Nothing Was The Same and he rarely returns back to the “old Drake.”", "annotation_id": "7835293", "url": "https://genius.com/7835293", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Rookie season, I would’ve never thought this was comin’ They knees give out and they passin’ to you all of the sudden Now you the one gettin’ buckets They put they arm around you, now you becomin’ the crutches Kids got on your number ’cause you the one they look up to", "vault_lines": ["Rookie season, I would've never thought this was comin'", "They knees give out and they passin' to you all of the sudden", "Now you the one gettin' buckets", "They put they arm around you, now you becomin' the crutches", "Kids got on your number 'cause you the one they look up to"], "meaning": "When So Far Gone was released in 2009, Drake had no idea he’d blow up into a worldwide sensation by 2015 and surpass his elders. In keeping with the song’s 30 for 30 sports theme, Drake alludes to the same thing happening in basketball: veterans mentor rookies until injuries and retirement force the rookies onto center stage and into fans' hearts.\n\n\n\nDrizzy loves thinking about his career in terms of basketball. Here he is on “Thank Me Now”:\n\nAnd that’s around the time that your idols become your rivals / You make friends with Mike but got to A.I. him for your survival\n\nBut maybe first he should work on his jump shot…", "annotation_id": "7835289", "url": "https://genius.com/7835289", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "When the higher-ups have all come together as a collective With conspiracies to end my run and send me a message 40, did you get the message? ’Cause I just checked my phone and I didn’t get it", "vault_lines": ["When the higher-ups have all come together as a collective", "With conspiracies to end my run and send me a message", "40, did you get the message?", "'Cause I just checked my phone and I didn't get it"], "meaning": "Usually, “to send a message” is an idiom meaning to do something that vividly gets a point across. (Like industry higher-ups making moves to undermine Drake.)\n\nHere, Drake takes the phrase literally, asking his producer and confidant Noah “40” Shebib if he got a text from the people that wanted to knock them off the top spot of the rap game.\n\n40 took to Twitter to answer the question:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/OVO40/status/645735673182441472", "annotation_id": "7835265", "url": "https://genius.com/7835265", "state": "accepted", "votes": 155, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Peyton and Eli when niggas called me they brother The season start and I don’t wanna see you end up with nothin’ Y’all throw the word “family” around too much in discussion", "vault_lines": ["Peyton and Eli when niggas called me they brother", "The season start and I don't wanna see you end up with nothin'", "Y'all throw the word \"family\" around too much in discussion", "And either they wanna fuck you"], "meaning": "Like NFL quarterbacks Peyton and Eli Manning, Drake might be brotherly with other rappers, but he’s still going to compete with them—and win—when it counts.\n\n\n\nOVO Sound was unofficially founded back in 2006, but became a legitimate label in 2012 when Noah “40” Shebib came on board. Drake considers the OVO crew his family and he roots for their success, just as they supported him.", "annotation_id": "7835222", "url": "https://genius.com/7835222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "And I just came from dinner where I ate some well-done seared scallops that were to die for But I got bigger fish to fry I’m talkin’ bigger shit than you and I Kids are losin’ lives, got me scared of losin’ mine And if I hold my tongue about it, I get crucified", "vault_lines": ["And I just came from dinner where I ate some well-done seared scallops that were to die for", "But I got bigger fish to fry", "I'm talkin' bigger shit than you and I", "Kids are losin' lives, got me scared of losin' mine", "And if I hold my tongue about it, I get crucified", "'Cause I just checked my phone and I didn't get it"], "meaning": "Drake uses some seafood wordplay here, and claims that he has “bigger fish to fry” (i.e., more important things to worry about) than either the fancy scallops he just ate or his beef with Meek. In particular, he’s worried about the death of black youths.\n\nThis is a nationwide issue, but Drake is specifically talking about the two youths who were shot dead after his after-party in August 2015. Drake took a while to address the shooting, as that’s what his lawyers advised, but a combination of criticism (like the “#DrakeNoticeJamal” Twitter trend) and guilt led him to finally speak out.", "annotation_id": "7835217", "url": "https://genius.com/7835217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "The pen is workin’ if you niggas need some ghost lines I thought you wanted yours like I want mine", "vault_lines": ["The pen is workin' if you niggas need some ghost lines", "I thought you wanted yours like I want mine"], "meaning": "This line is simultaneously self-aggrandizing and self-deprecating. Drake offers to write for his competition like he’s done before, saying that it’s the only way they’ll get lines as good as what he himself aspires to. But “like I want mine” also acknowledges, in a joking way, that he’d only do this because he allegedly likes having his own lines ghost-written.\n\nMeek Mill sparked controversy 2 months before the release of WATTBA when he accused Drake of using a ghostwriter. This caused Drake to write two tracks, “Charged Up” and “Back to Back,” in response.", "annotation_id": "7835185", "url": "https://genius.com/7835185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 173, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Papi champú, young Pablito de seis dios 6 G-O-D, I think I Was destined for this shit when I was ’round Keyshia Cole and T.I. And Young Dro was poppin’ off with “Ain’t I” Way before niggas had they hands out like they doin’ macarena", "vault_lines": ["Papi champú, young Pablito de seis dios", "Was destined for this shit when I was 'round Keyshia Cole and T.I.", "And Young Dro was poppin' off with \"Ain't I\"", "Way before niggas had they hands out like they doin' macarena"], "meaning": "Papi Shampoo is a Dominican slang term for a womanizer that dresses well, and a play on Drake’s nickname, “Champagne Papi.” He also calls himself the young 6 God in Spanish, while making an allusion to notorious Colombian drug lord Pablo Escobar.\n\nDrake knew from the start that he would be running the game. (Even his mother knew…) If he had to estimate the time when he first realized it, it would be when T.I., Keyshia Cole, and Young Dro were blowing up. (“Ain’t I” is a 2009 song that Young Dro and T.I. were both featured on, and it also mentions the Macarena.) This was before he was Champagne Papi or the 6 God and everyone wanted to borrow money from him.\n\nFinally, “Macarena” was a popular dance song released in the US in 1995 where one of the moves is to literally stick your hands out. Although Drake would have only been 9 years old at the time the song became popular, he’s insinuating his success was a long time coming, even before people started trying to freeload off of him.", "annotation_id": "7835154", "url": "https://genius.com/7835154", "state": "accepted", "votes": 153, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "I just listened to “Closer to My Dreams” Wide-eyed and uneducated at nineteen I can’t rap like that, all young and naïve Not after all the shit I seen and the things I believe", "vault_lines": ["I just listened to \"Closer to My Dreams\"", "Wide-eyed and uneducated at nineteen", "I can't rap like that, all young and naïve", "Not after all the shit I seen and the things I believe"], "meaning": "“Closer To My Dreams” is track #4 on Drake’s 2008 mixtape Comeback Season. 40 even layers the piano notes and chorus from “Closer” under the beat during these lines.\n\nListening to “Closer” you hear a young Drake that finally realizes his talent and how big he could be. These days, Drake is too jaded to rap like a newcomer, but he still looks fondly on older songs like “Closer.”\n\n\n\nAnd Drake was “uneducated” at 19: He dropped out of high school at age 16 to pursue acting.", "annotation_id": "7835146", "url": "https://genius.com/7835146", "state": "accepted", "votes": 133, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "PARTY just dipped off in a white Porsche", "vault_lines": ["PARTY just dipped off in a white Porsche"], "meaning": "PARTYNEXTDOOR is an OVO Sound artist who’s seen commercial success since signing with the label in 2013. He actually owns a white Porsche.", "annotation_id": "7835138", "url": "https://genius.com/7835138", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Shit is purely for sport, I need a 30 for 30 Banners are ready case we need to retire your jersey, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Shit is purely for sport, I need a 30 for 30", "Banners are ready case we need to retire your jersey, yeah"], "meaning": "30 for 30 is an ESPN sports documentary series. Drake feels his career is important enough to have a documentary made about him.\n\nAnd not just any documentary, but a sports documentary: he’s lyrically slaughtering rappers “purely for sport.” He’s not killing to eat, or in this case, he’s not battling in order to increase his fame or sales; he’s already making money hand over fist. He’s basically just doing it because he can. The “30 for 30” allusion just reminds us that he’s damn good at it, too!\n\nNormally, retiring someone’s jersey would be a good thing, but Drake’s clowning his opposition here: they’re washed up and need to retire.", "annotation_id": "7835137", "url": "https://genius.com/7835137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "I guess you just makin’ moves on your own time But just know it’ll be January in no time", "vault_lines": ["I guess you just makin' moves on your own time", "But just know it'll be January in no time"], "meaning": "Compared to Drake, most other rappers take their time releasing music and making career moves. And if that’s how you operate, “in no time” it’ll be a new year, and you’ll still be in the same place, doing the same shit.\n\nIn particular, this is a reference to how long it took Meek Mill to come back with a response to “Charged Up” and “Back to Back”. As he said in an interview with The FADER:\n\nThis is a discussion about music, and no one’s putting forth any music? You guys are gonna leave this for me to do?", "annotation_id": "7835113", "url": "https://genius.com/7835113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "I just got me the Mercedes Pullman You niggas never heard of it, you gotta hit up Google, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I just got me the Mercedes Pullman", "You niggas never heard of it, you gotta hit up Google, yeah"], "meaning": "Or just hit up Genius… The Mercedes Pullman is an expansion of the classic S600, ubiquitous in luxurious rap.\n\nAfter discontinuing the Maybach in 2013, Mercedes plans to bring back the Pullman in 2016—a full 4 inches taller and 4 feet longer than the already bonkers S600. It’s such an exclusive piece of craftsmanship, mere mortals are unlikely to be aware of its existence.\n\n\n\nDrake told listeners to Google it and people actually did. Searches for Mercedes Pullman surged the month What a Time To Be Alive released.\n\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/36758f3f56e902d6e437b97661fb243bc0249349837aca6f0a49a675e8d4ce61%2Fegghj74puc5", "annotation_id": "7835109", "url": "https://genius.com/7835109", "state": "accepted", "votes": 178, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Wrote this shit on a bumpy flight on a summer night Flyin’ over Chattanooga, out here tryna spread the movement", "vault_lines": ["Wrote this shit on a bumpy flight on a summer night", "Flyin' over Chattanooga, out here tryna spread the movement"], "meaning": "Drake is trying to spread the OVO Sound awareness. When collaborating with Future in Atlanta, he has to fly from Toronto. On the way his plane flies over Tennessee, which includes a fly-over of Chattanooga, TN, a mid-sized city about two hours away from Atlanta.", "annotation_id": "7835088", "url": "https://genius.com/7835088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Paternity testin’ for women that I never slept with I’m legally obligated if they request it So much legal action like I’m Michael Jackson", "vault_lines": ["Paternity testin' for women that I never slept with", "I'm legally obligated if they request it", "So much legal action like I'm Michael Jackson"], "meaning": "Aubrey relates the crazy attention he gets from women and their lawyers to the mountain of media and court proceedings that Michael Jackson faced after allegations of child sexual abuse in the 1990s.\n\nMJ also had to deal with women that claimed he was the father of their child—which became the basis of “Billie Jean.”\n\n\n\nThese lines (and the surrounding bars) were first heard when Drake made an appearance at J. Cole’s concert for his Forest Hills Drive tour.\n\nBeing legally obligated to take a test varies widely depending on what state Drake has been mischievous in, and whether a judge orders him to or not. It is generally a civil matter.", "annotation_id": "7834865", "url": "https://genius.com/7834865", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "line": "Never thought I’d be talkin’ from this perspective But I’m not really sure what else you expected", "vault_lines": ["Never thought I'd be talkin' from this perspective", "But I'm not really sure what else you expected"], "meaning": "Drake never thought he’d speak as a Canadian symbol and one of the most celebrated rappers in the game.\n\nBut did we expect any less? Drake fans know he’s versatile, playing here on a style he developed in his past mixtape, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, and on earlier introspective tracks like “The Resistance”, “The Ride”, and “The Catch Up.”", "annotation_id": "7834864", "url": "https://genius.com/7834864", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 for 30 Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 30, "clicked": 30, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-30-for-30-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "30 for 30 Freestyle"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "Lebanon Dons in this bitch.", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Oliver & 40 are both Lebanese Canadian\n\nNoah Shebib, better known as “40”, is Drake’s producer and co-founder of OVO (alongside Drake and Oliver El-Khatib). He was born in Canada but is of Lebanese descent\n\nOliver El-Khatib, on the other hand, is Drake’s co-manager and co-founder of OVO (alongside Drake and 40) Oliver was also born in Canada but is of Lebanese descent. El-Khatib is credited to be the genius behind Drake’s global branding existence.", "annotation_id": "34340082", "url": "https://genius.com/34340082", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "Paige, I hope you don’t mind, I hope you don’t mind me, uhm havin’ a little fun you know?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "He minded.", "annotation_id": "30506854", "url": "https://genius.com/30506854", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
