{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "Celebrate at Bymark, fuck ’em at the SoHo", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The Bymark is a high-end restaurant in Toronto, and the SoHo Hotel is down the street.", "annotation_id": "23507952", "url": "https://genius.com/23507952", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "Took over my city and I never asked permission to;", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Since the start of his career, Drake has always paid respect to his hometown of Toronto, aka the 6, and is credited for bringing international fame to the city. He did this through interviews and songs like “The City is Mine”.\n\nDrake continues to emphasise that he has control over Toronto, implying that he was the only notable rapper who came from the 6 and so did not have to compete with others to become the “6 God”.", "annotation_id": "10194040", "url": "https://genius.com/10194040", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "We got, we got the real life entourage", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Reference to the famous HBO TV Series “Entourage”. A recurring theme in Entourage is the strength of male camaraderie and its importance over work, and that’s one of the reasons the show was so well received. It also featured guest appearances by a lot of rappers. Drake compares these guys to his own Crew since they’re also all very good friends from the same city, and judging by what we’ve seen through the years he might just be right..\nDrake of course would be the “Vincent Chase” to the OVO Crew.", "annotation_id": "3886757", "url": "https://genius.com/3886757", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "That’s where you find all the exclusives at", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Since the beginning of Drake’s jump to stardom all of his songs and his breakthrough mixtape So Far Gone were released on their blog, and until this day his exclusive songs and also releases from OVO affiliated artists such as P Reign, Jimmy Johnson are posted on the website.", "annotation_id": "3886751", "url": "https://genius.com/3886751", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "[Outro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Here Drake shouts out almost of his OVO Crew, who at the time were just a bunch of unknown names (this song was released in 2009) but now has become more than just his mostly Toronto-based crew. It’s a brand, it’s a movement, and its grown immensely since it started. In 2012, Drake, Noah “40” Shebib and Oliver El-Khatib founded “OVOSound”, a record label currently distributed by Warner Bros. Records.", "annotation_id": "3886732", "url": "https://genius.com/3886732", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "John B.", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jonny Roxx, Drake’s personal trainer. Pictured below with Johnny Manziel, who is also an OVO affiliate, and Drake.", "annotation_id": "3886718", "url": "https://genius.com/3886718", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "And man, my DJ shoulda won that Nelissa and Wristpect, Man the city owe us one back", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This line refers to his Dj at the time Dj Wristpect losing at the Toronto Stylus Awards when he was deserving of winning that year.", "annotation_id": "191823", "url": "https://genius.com/191823", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "October’s Very Own", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "http://octobersveryown.blogspot.ie/ (aka OVO) is a record label founded by Drake, his producer 40 and Oliver El-Khatib.\n\nMembers of the record label include PARTYNEXTDOOR, Roy Woods, Majid Jordan and dvsn. Furthermore, OVO has in-house producers such as 40, Nineteen85 and Boi-1da.\n\nHere is more info on the entire crew.", "annotation_id": "85855", "url": "https://genius.com/85855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "Niko just came and scooped me from the airport", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Niko, Drake’s good friend (producer) picked him up from the Toronto airport.\n\nNiko is one of Drake’s oldest and closest friends from Toronto, Niko introduced the rapper to his own barber when the former “Degrassi” star needed a fresh look. He’s now responsible for OVO’s Merchandising.", "annotation_id": "84649", "url": "https://genius.com/84649", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "Freeko Suave, Oliver, the Parisian Gangster", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Oliver El-Khatib is one of OVO Sound' founders alongside Drake and 40, and is also Drake’s Manager, producer (he’s credited in The Resistance and creative Director.", "annotation_id": "49350", "url": "https://genius.com/49350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "line": "Responsible for everything you listen to", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake had three hit singles the year that this freestyle was released (“Forever”, “Best I Ever Had”, and “Successful”)\n\nIt’s all the radio would play! Drake Drake Drake!", "annotation_id": "49346", "url": "https://genius.com/49346", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Still", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 20, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-im-still-fly-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "I’m Still Fly (Freestyle)"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "I never really had no one like you, man, this all new shit", "vault_lines": ["I never really had no one like you, man, this all new shit"], "meaning": "Drake is talking about how the person he’s addressing gave him a kind of support and care he wasn’t used to growing up. With all the chaos at home, having someone who calmed him down and made his world feel bigger was something new for him. He’s basically saying this was the first time he had someone who truly changed his perspective and made a real difference in his life. – king", "annotation_id": "36836569", "url": "https://genius.com/36836569", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "Had all this fightin’ going on at the crib You would calm me down when I lose it", "vault_lines": ["Had all this fightin' going on at the crib", "You would calm me down when I lose it"], "meaning": "He says “Had all this fightin’ going on at the crib” which places constant conflict inside a place that should feel safe.\n\nHe follows with “You would calm me down when I lose it” and credits his uncle as the emotional anchor. He guides him back from anger or panic. This presents her as steady and protective while the house stays unstable.", "annotation_id": "36344739", "url": "https://genius.com/36344739", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "Made the world I know bigger Changed the way that I viewed it", "vault_lines": ["Made the world I know bigger", "Changed the way that I viewed it"], "meaning": "He credits his mother for widening his limits. “The world I know” shows he once felt confined. Her influence opened new places, ideas, or emotions.\n\nShe did more than add experiences. She reshaped how he sees those experiences. The change is internal, showing growth guided by her presence.\n\nMaternal guidance shifts him from narrow sight to broader understanding.", "annotation_id": "36344678", "url": "https://genius.com/36344678", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "Then Wayne calls up my phone Conversation wasn’t that long Gets me a flight to Houston in the morning Oh, it’s my time, yeah, it’s on He’s thinkin’ of signin’ me, I come home", "vault_lines": ["Then Wayne calls up my phone", "Conversation wasn't that long", "Gets me a flight to Houston in the morning", "Oh, it's my time, yeah, it's on", "He's thinkin' of signin' me, I come home"], "meaning": "In a 2010 interview with Katie Couric, Drake revealed that before stardom he used to upload music on the social networking site Myspace, where he was discovered by longtime friend Jas Prince, who introduced Drake’s music to Lil Wayne. In 2008, Drake was called while in a barbershop by Lil Wayne, who invited Drake to Houston to record music and tour a week or two prior to the release of Wayne’s Tha Carter III album. However, Drake was yet to be signed by Lil Wayne until June of the following year.", "annotation_id": "15393369", "url": "https://genius.com/15393369", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "And your back hurt, and your neck hurt and you smokin’ heavy And I sit next to you, and I lecture you because those are deadly", "vault_lines": ["And your back hurt, and your neck hurt and you smokin' heavy", "And I sit next to you, and I lecture you because those are deadly"], "meaning": "Drake has previously mentioned his mother’s health issues in numerous interviews, which had caused a teenage Drake to be the primary breadwinner of his family since his father was absent. To numb her back and neck pain, Drake’s mother chose to smoke, to which Drake tries to educate his mother that this practice would only add on to their problems.", "annotation_id": "15393158", "url": "https://genius.com/15393158", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "Just a young kid in a drop-top Lexus hopin’ that I don’t get arrested", "vault_lines": ["Just a young kid in a drop-top Lexus hopin' that I don't get arrested"], "meaning": "When Drake was a teenager, his maternal uncle Steve owned a Lexus which would be frequently lended to Drake to drive around Toronto. However, since Drake is of a darker skin complexion he could have been a victim of racial profiling by police officers, or that maybe the thrill of finally being able to drive a nice car caused a young Drake to drive recklessly. Either way, Drake didn’t want to end up in jail.\n\nHe has referenced this uncle requesting his car keys from Drake on his 2013 hit “Started From the Bottom”.", "annotation_id": "15393134", "url": "https://genius.com/15393134", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "[Intro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5237570168001", "annotation_id": "1625818", "url": "https://genius.com/1625818", "state": "verified", "votes": 122, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "Just another kid that’s going through life so worried that I won’t be accepted But I could do anything You said that, and you meant that", "vault_lines": ["Just another kid that's going through life so worried that I won't be accepted", "But I could do anything", "You said that, and you meant that"], "meaning": "Drake has mentioned his uncle in several songs including “Successful” and “Say What’s Real” and it is clear that he was an integral part of Drake’s central support system.\n\nJust coming from Degrassi, Drake was also worried this would be a stigma hindering him of acceptance into the hip-hop community.", "annotation_id": "459791", "url": "https://genius.com/459791", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "[Outro: Drake’s Grandmother] Hi Aubrey I’m here sitting with my son having a little talk And he knew, knows how to get in touch with you now To let you know how grateful I am for your help in keeping me in this In this comfortable place All I can say, Aubrey, is I remember the good times we had together And the times I used to look after you And I still have wonderful feeling about that So God bless you And I hope I’ll see you", "vault_lines": ["And get to make good on my promise", "Now I'm more in touch with the music", "You said that, and you meant that", "I'm here sitting with my son having a little talk", "And he knew, knows how to get in touch with you now", "To let you know how grateful I am for your help in keeping me in this", "In this comfortable place", "All I can say, Aubrey, is I remember the good times we had together", "And the times I used to look after you", "And I still have wonderful feeling about that", "So God bless you", "And I hope I'll see you"], "meaning": "The song ends with Drake’s grandmother in a voicemail wishing him the best and expressing how thankful she is for him. In 2010, she was placed into a nursing home as discussed in “The Resistance”.\n\nAs previously discussed in other songs, Drake was partially raised by his family in Memphis, Tennessee including his father Dennis Graham and his uncle Larry Graham. Drake is also very close with his grandmother, Evelyn Sher, whom he affectionately refers to as “Bubbe” which is grandmother in Yiddish. Many Jews refer to their grandmothers as their “Bubbe” and have been for centuries.\n\n\n\nDrake’s grandmother sadly passed away on Thanksgiving in 2012.", "annotation_id": "459788", "url": "https://genius.com/459788", "state": "accepted", "votes": 407, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "[Verse 2]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse is dedicated to his uncle Steve, who he’s had a close relationship with throughout his rise to fame. He is also mentioned in “The Winner”:\n\nThanks to Uncle Steve, my hero who always saved us\nI could never give back everything you gave us", "annotation_id": "452658", "url": "https://genius.com/452658", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "We make a mixtape with seventeen songs And almost get a Grammy off of that thing They love your son, man, that boy gone", "vault_lines": ["We make a mixtape with seventeen songs", "And almost get a Grammy off of that thing", "They love your son, man, that boy gone"], "meaning": "That would be So Far Gone, which he has mentioned on this record before.  Drake still has hurt feelings over his Grammy loss.  In an interview with Billboard he stated:\n\n\n But the one [Grammy defeat] that really got to me was when I lost to “D.O.A.” for “Best I Ever Had.” That one to me was, like, really? Not to say that Jay-Z isn’t the most incredible rapper and that “D.O.A.” wasn’t a good song. I just felt like they had an option to give me a Grammy for a mixtape, and they just didn’t do it [laughs]. Because it goes against the grain of everything that is traditional\n\n I don’t measure my success anymore by the Grammys. I can’t because I’ll just end up crushed. And, not to be offensive, I just feel it’s political. Would I love to win a Grammy? Sure. Will I win one after that comment? Probably not\n\n\nDrake would win the Grammy for Take Care.", "annotation_id": "441121", "url": "https://genius.com/441121", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "But maybe I wouldn’t have worked this hard if you were healthy and it weren’t so bad, uh Maybe I should walk up the street And try and get a job at the bank ’Cause leave it up to me, J, and Neeks We’ll probably end up robbin’ a bank", "vault_lines": ["But maybe I wouldn't have worked this hard if you were healthy and it weren't so bad, uh", "Maybe I should walk up the street", "And try and get a job at the bank", "'Cause leave it up to me, J, and Neeks", "We'll probably end up robbin' a bank", "'Cause you deserve it"], "meaning": "Neeks (Niko), who Drake has shouted out in song before, is the rapper’s “closest confidant”.  One reader theorizes that the “J” here is someone named Julian Lloyd, though we have no confirmation of that\n\nNiko is left, below:\n\n\n\nThe J is for Jordan, a long time friend of Drake’s.\nPictured here on the left:\nhttp://instagram.com/p/NruwwRDQIK/", "annotation_id": "441113", "url": "https://genius.com/441113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "And then you ash it, and we argue about spendin’ money on bullshit And you tell me I’m just like my father, my one button, you push it Now it’s fuck you, I hate you, I’ll move out in a heartbeat And I leave out and you call me and you tell me that you sorry And you love me and I love you and your heart hurts, mine does too And it’s just words and they cut deep, but it’s our world, it’s just us two", "vault_lines": ["It's like '09 in your basement and I'm in love with Nebby", "And it's just words and they cut deep, but it's our world, it's just us two", "It's like '06 in your backyard, I'm in love with Jade", "And then you ash it, and we argue about spendin' money on bullshit", "You said that, and you meant that", "And you tell me I'm just like my father, my one button, you push it", "Now it's fuck you, I hate you, I'll move out in a heartbeat", "And I hope I'll see you", "And I leave out and you call me and you tell me that you sorry", "And you love me and I love you and your heart hurts, mine does too"], "meaning": "Before Drake had all this money there was  tension in the house about how he spent it — like the time he leased a Rolls Royce before he could afford an apartment.\n\nDrake’s parents divorced when he was five, and he was subsequently raised by his mother, though he spent summers with his dad. Drake doesn’t want to be like his dad so being compared to him, especially by his mother, makes him angry.\n\nDrake has acknowledged the similarity before:\n\nDamn, where I get it from? These niggas always wonder who\nThen they meet my pop and tell him, “Drake is just a younger you”", "annotation_id": "441107", "url": "https://genius.com/441107", "state": "accepted", "votes": 127, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "It’s like ’06 in your backyard, I’m in love with Jade And I’m still in love ’cause when it’s that real is when it doesn’t fade", "vault_lines": ["It's like '09 in your basement and I'm in love with Nebby", "It's like '06 in your backyard, I'm in love with Jade", "And I'm still in love 'cause when it's that real is when it doesn't fade"], "meaning": "The “Jade” here is likely Jade Lee, Drake’s ex who he spoke about in “Karaoke.”\n\nThe “you” in this verse is Drake’s uncle Steve who he previously mentioned in the song “The Winner.”", "annotation_id": "438701", "url": "https://genius.com/438701", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "And my father living in Memphis now, he can’t come this way Over some minor charges and child support that just wasn’t paid, damn Boohoo, sad story, Black American dad story", "vault_lines": ["And my father living in Memphis now, he can't come this way", "Over some minor charges and child support that just wasn't paid, damn", "Boohoo, sad story, Black American dad story"], "meaning": "“The Personal Responsibility and Work Opportunity Act” revoked the passports of anyone who owed more than $5,000 in child support. This is probably why Drake’s dad can’t come to Canada\n\nDrake is discussing the “typical black American dad” story. i.e. African American fathers who leave their children and avoid responsibility.\n\nDrake sounds pretty sarcastic when he says “boo-ho sad story” as this is a very common problem so is viewed as not particularly bad, yet in reality it is a problem that is very painful for everyone involved and despite it is a typical story it is still one that affects him", "annotation_id": "436271", "url": "https://genius.com/436271", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "It’s like ’09 in your basement and I’m in love with Nebby And I still love her, but it fell through because I wasn’t ready", "vault_lines": ["It's like '09 in your basement and I'm in love with Nebby", "And I still love her, but it fell through because I wasn't ready", "It's like '06 in your backyard, I'm in love with Jade"], "meaning": "Nebby is allegedly a Toronto ex he loved and lost\n\n\n\nThe “you” in this verse is Drizzy’s beloved mother, Sandi Graham\n\n\n\nA nice compilation of Drake interviews from said basement: https://vimeo.com/68754435\n\nDrake also grew up in the basement of the small home his mother owned, and wrote a lot of his old music in this basement.", "annotation_id": "436062", "url": "https://genius.com/436062", "state": "accepted", "votes": 155, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "You took me places, you spent that They said no, we went back Checks bounced, but we bounced back I put all the money in your accounts back And I thank you, I don’t know where I’d really be without that It worked out, man, you deserve it", "vault_lines": ["It all worked out, girl, we should've known", "'Cause you deserve it", "You said that, and you meant that", "You took me places, you spent that", "They said no, we went back", "Checks bounced, but we bounced back", "I put all the money in your accounts back", "And I thank you, I don't know where I'd really be without that", "It worked out, man, you deserve it", "And I leave out and you call me and you tell me that you sorry"], "meaning": "Uncle Steve spent money on Drake, even when he didn’t really have it, and kept a good attitude despite the hard times. Drake paid him back, and more, after his success. He thanks his uncle for being in his life, when he needed him most.\n\nDrake’s uncle passed away in the summer of 2011, while Drake was in the middle of a concert tour.\n\n\n\nDrake and Uncle Steve together at the Grammys", "annotation_id": "435907", "url": "https://genius.com/435907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 156, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "Is this shit real? Should I pinch you? After all the things that we been through, I got you, yeah Look what you’ve done, look what you’ve done Look what you’ve done for me now, yeah You knew that I was gon’ be somethin’ When you’re stressed out and you need somethin’, I got you, yeah Look what you’ve done, look what you’ve done Look what you’ve, look what you’ve", "vault_lines": ["Is this shit real? Should I pinch you?", "After all the things that we been through, I got you, yeah", "Look what you've done, look what you've done", "Look what you've done for me now, yeah", "You knew that I was gon' be somethin'", "When you're stressed out and you need somethin', I got you, yeah", "Look what you've, look what you've", "You said that, and you meant that", "And the times I used to look after you", "And I leave out and you call me and you tell me that you sorry"], "meaning": "Drakes got money now to take care of his mom and uncle to pay them back for all the dedication they gave to him when he was broke. In 2015 his approximate net worth was 76 million dollars.\n\n“Look what you’ve done!” is often used to point out someone’s mistakes. So on one hand, Drake highlights the faults of these authority figures in his life. But he pauses and finishes the line with “what you’ve done for me,” pointing out through their faults they have had beneficial impacts on his life.\n\nThe “I got you” line was taken from the song “If U Scared, Say U Scared” by R&B group Playa. The song is also sampled for “Look What You’ve Done.”\n\nDrake’s mother describes how she always knew her son was going to be successful.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/8zjLZPpRpWg?t=4m45s", "annotation_id": "435844", "url": "https://genius.com/435844", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "You get the operation you dreamed of And I finally send you to Rome And get to make good on my promise It all worked out, girl, we should’ve known ’Cause you deserve it", "vault_lines": ["You get the operation you dreamed of", "And I finally send you to Rome", "And get to make good on my promise", "It all worked out, girl, we should've known", "'Cause you deserve it", "It worked out, man, you deserve it"], "meaning": "In summer 2010, Drake was finally able to afford an apparently long-needed operation for his mother.\n\nOn the track “Must Hate Money” off of the 2007 mixtape Comeback Season, Drake says,\n\nMy momma tryna go to Rome and I just wanna take her.\n\nIt looks like he finally got the chance to.", "annotation_id": "435517", "url": "https://genius.com/435517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 122, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "Told you I think I’m done acting Now I’m more in touch with the music You said, either way, I’d be a star, I could go so far Talked to me, then you got to me Then you’d toss the keys and loan me your car, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Told you I think I'm done acting", "Now I'm more in touch with the music", "You said, either way, I'd be a star, I could go so far", "Talked to me, then you got to me", "Then you'd toss the keys and loan me your car, yeah", "You said that, and you meant that"], "meaning": "Drake is an actor turned rapper.  He had a fairly large role in the TV series Degrassi before beginning his music career\n\nDrake’s uncle may have literally loaned his nephew a car, or his uncle’s encouragement and support were the “key” to his success\n\nDrake talks about his Uncle lending him his car at least once.", "annotation_id": "435313", "url": "https://genius.com/435313", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "Know that I’m your sister’s kid, but it still don’t explain the love that you have for me I remember sneaking in your pool after school dances Damn, your house felt like the Hamptons For all of my summer romances", "vault_lines": ["Know that I'm your sister's kid, but it still don't explain the love that you have for me", "I remember sneaking in your pool after school dances", "Damn, your house felt like the Hamptons", "For all of my summer romances"], "meaning": "Drake explains that he knows his uncle has gone way above and beyond mere familial obligation in taking care of him.\n“The Hamptons” is a community on the far east end of Long Island that has some of the most expensive houses in the world.", "annotation_id": "435304", "url": "https://genius.com/435304", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "line": "I see painkillers on the kitchen counter, I hate to see it all hurt so bad", "vault_lines": ["I see painkillers on the kitchen counter, I hate to see it all hurt so bad"], "meaning": "Drake has, reasonably enough, not been public about the specifics of his mother’s illness.  However, it was bad enough at one point that he cancelled a whole European tour in order to be with her during some surgery she was receiving.", "annotation_id": "435278", "url": "https://genius.com/435278", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Look What You’ve Done", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 22, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-look-what-youve-done-lyrics", "genius_title": "Look What You’ve Done"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Fuck teasin’ time, if I cum inside you, it’s breedin’ time", "vault_lines": ["Fuck teasin' time, if I cum inside you, it's breedin' time"], "meaning": "fr", "annotation_id": "41293629", "url": "https://genius.com/41293629", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "I did everything that I could for my hood, so how do they say I don’t care for the mandem?", "vault_lines": ["I did everything that I could for my hood, so how do they say I don't care for the mandem?"], "meaning": "Cench hits back at claims that he abandoned his roots after finding global success. Growing up in Shepherd’s Bush, West London, his early life revolved around riding for his ends and trying to elevate his circle. Here, he expresses frustration at the lack of gratitude from people back home, pointing out that he has done everything in his power to financially and socially support the mandem since blowing up.", "annotation_id": "40022972", "url": "https://genius.com/40022972", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "What goes up must come down so when you’re in the air, prepare for the landin’", "vault_lines": ["What goes up must come down so when you're in the air, prepare for the landin'"], "meaning": "Central Cee here is saying that success must come to an end by flipping the classic proverb “What goes up, must come down”. But Cench advises that when a person is in the air, meaning his highest point of success, they should prepare for the landing so they won’t end up in a worse position than they were before.", "annotation_id": "40022923", "url": "https://genius.com/40022923", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Some madness and badness, combination", "vault_lines": ["Some madness and badness, combination"], "meaning": "Drake’s Jamaican pronunciation of ‘combination’ would go viral, generating endless memes upon the freestyle release. He has dabbled before with the Jamaican Patois accent, particularly from around 2016 onwards with projects like Views and More Life.", "annotation_id": "39083605", "url": "https://genius.com/39083605", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "I tell Baby M what she did to the kid,", "vault_lines": ["I tell Baby M what she did to the kid,"], "meaning": "Baby M refers to Mirna Habib, an instagram model who drake has rumours of dating.\ndrake has highlighted how annoyed he is at baby m for abandoning drakes kid, attempting to make baby m feel bad for leaving him and adonis.\ndrake really does not like baby m, and has multiple other disses throughout album “For All The Dogs”", "annotation_id": "38704779", "url": "https://genius.com/38704779", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Why you swearing to God? You don’t even believe in Christ", "vault_lines": ["Why you swearing to God? You don't even believe in Christ"], "meaning": "The term god come from Christianity and means Christ. Central Cee is referring that the girl who is he talking to in this bar is a Muslim which means she does belive in Allah not Christ so when she say to Central Cee “swaer to god” she could be lying since she doesn’t believe in Christ.", "annotation_id": "37333256", "url": "https://genius.com/37333256", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "He mention my name ’cause he wanted some fame and he ain’t established (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["He mention my name 'cause he wanted some fame and he ain't established (Huh)"], "meaning": "Central Cee was involved in a minor beef with rapper Aitch earlier in January 2023, and this may be referring to that.", "annotation_id": "37298629", "url": "https://genius.com/37298629", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Twenty-four inch on the rims on the Benz, I’m doing donuts and skidding on tarmac (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Twenty-four inch on the rims on the Benz, I'm doing donuts and skidding on tarmac (Huh)"], "meaning": "Mercedes-Benz cars do not come with 24-inch wheels, which are an aftermarket modification.\n“Donuts” here refers to the circular drifting one can do, typically with a muscle or sports car, for fun.", "annotation_id": "37298619", "url": "https://genius.com/37298619", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Pass me a glass of the vino", "vault_lines": ["Pass me a glass of the vino"], "meaning": "The repetition of “zero” in the previous line is a homophone, and can be interpreted as “rosé” repeatedly.\nRosé is a type of wine, which ties into this line, where he says “pass me a glass of the vino”. Vino is Italian for wine.", "annotation_id": "37298584", "url": "https://genius.com/37298584", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "It used to be 3.5s, now I can sell out the O2 arena twice", "vault_lines": ["It used to be 3.5s, now I can sell out the O2 arena twice"], "meaning": "A 3.5 typically refers to 3.5 grams of cannabis. In this line, Central Cee is referencing how he used to deal small amounts of cannabis on the street or that he used to smoke these 3.5s himself.\n\nThis directly contrasts with the latter part of the bar, where he claims he can now sell out the O2 Arena twice. The O2 Arena in London is one of the most iconic and largest live music venues, with a capacity of around 20,000 people. Selling it out twice shows not only his massive popularity, but also the growing demand for his performances.\n\n\n\nOverall, the line serves as a rags-to-riches narrative, showing how far he’s come in such a short amount of time.", "annotation_id": "35826010", "url": "https://genius.com/35826010", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "The numbers are good with me as long as it’s comma then zero, zero, zero, zero, zero, zero, zero", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is saying that he will be fine to sign deals as long as they are in the millions. Only six zeroes would have been needed for this but it’s possible that either he added the seventh by accident or to fill the rest of space in the bar.", "annotation_id": "35786677", "url": "https://genius.com/35786677", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "So much for all these heroes", "vault_lines": ["So much for all these heroes"], "meaning": "One of Drake’s common themes in his career is his realization that his heroes and idols of the past turned out to be disappointments to him as he got bigger in the music game. This is also a new double entendre off of the gyro line. A popular street food sandwich found in delicatessens across America is the Submarine sandwich known in New York City as a “hero”. Notably, this was recorded in New York. Here Drake is saying since he is making the gyros which is another popular street food sandwich, then we should forget about eating heroes.", "annotation_id": "35786654", "url": "https://genius.com/35786654", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "I ain’t the man of your dreams, now you wish that you stayed with your previous guy", "vault_lines": ["I ain't the man of your dreams, now you wish that you stayed with your previous guy"], "meaning": "When a girl got to know the real side of Cee, she want to change him and is know regretting that she left her boyfriend for a celebrity crush.", "annotation_id": "35698652", "url": "https://genius.com/35698652", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Told you I needed time, but really the truth is I don’t believe in time", "vault_lines": ["Told you I needed time, but really the truth is I don't believe in time"], "meaning": "Sometimes when someone confess love to us, and we don’t want to say no straight from the shoulder, we say that we need more time. Drizzy told a girl he need more time, but deep inside, he know that waiting won’t change anything.", "annotation_id": "35698619", "url": "https://genius.com/35698619", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "J-A-U-M, how it looks when man put you in a jam like typos", "vault_lines": ["J-A-U-M, how it looks when man put you in a jam like typos"], "meaning": "JAUM is a typographical error you might make if someone puts U in a JAM.", "annotation_id": "31121019", "url": "https://genius.com/31121019", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Sick of these high-rise buildings, flip the script, now I’m livin’ on farmland", "vault_lines": ["Sick of these high-rise buildings, flip the script, now I'm livin' on farmland"], "meaning": "This isn’t Cench’s first time making a reference to his desire to escape the urban environment he grew up in for a more peaceful rural place on “Let Go,” he rapped:\n\nMove out of London town then move to a rural village\n\n“Flip the Script”, is a throwback to Crooks. \n\nThis song is unreleased (part of the Wild West era) and was leaked in June 2023.\n\nOn the song, when Cench mentions “The Script” as the life that he was predisposed to, like continuing to sell drugs or getting a 9-5, but he took risks and worked for years to achieve success, flipping it by doing so.\n\nIn this line, Cench maintains the same intentions, but he’s referring to his current life, which is in the spotlight, “flipping” it into a more peaceful life away from the city.", "annotation_id": "30713125", "url": "https://genius.com/30713125", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "What’s makin’ the haters jealous? I’m thinking the AP’s eighteen karats", "vault_lines": ["What's makin' the haters jealous? I'm thinking the AP's eighteen karats"], "meaning": "Central Cee makes a reference to his limited, 18 karats, white gold Audemars Piget, which he frequently boasts about in his songs, one instance of which can be seen on “Sprinter”:\n\nAP baby blue, paper’s pink, I’d probably hate me too\n\n\n\n Pictured: Central Cee’s Audemars Piget", "annotation_id": "30263344", "url": "https://genius.com/30263344", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Knew that I made it when I had drivers holding my last name up in arrivals", "vault_lines": ["Knew that I made it when I had drivers holding my last name up in arrivals"], "meaning": "In the “Arrivals” section of an airport or a train station, you’ll often find chauffeurs holding up signs for their reserved clients so they can drive them to their first destination. Once Drake realized there were more of them holding up his last name, he knew he must be living lavish. This is also a play on words, knowing that he “made it” (to his destination).", "annotation_id": "29554396", "url": "https://genius.com/29554396", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Pressin’ against me, thinkin’ ’bout entry, know you could tell it’s a decent size You wanna know the real me, girl, say you wanna see deep inside", "vault_lines": ["Pressin' against me, thinkin' 'bout entry, know you could tell it's a decent size", "You wanna know the real me, girl, say you wanna see deep inside"], "meaning": "This is a double entendre like many bars in this verse\n\nThe first is:\n\nThe woman thats leaning against Drake, she can feel that he is above average in package and wants to feel him inside of her.\n\nThe second:\n\nShe feels a “bond” with Drake in the moment, but Drake is telling her, “You need to go deeper than this moment if you want to know the real me.”", "annotation_id": "29367298", "url": "https://genius.com/29367298", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Big fish in this ting, no armbands like a backstroke how I swim in that shark tank", "vault_lines": ["Big fish in this ting, no armbands like a backstroke how I swim in that shark tank"], "meaning": "In this lyric, Central Cee portrays himself as a significant player (“big fish”) in a challenging environment (“ting”). He navigates confidently and skillfully, likening it to swimming in a shark tank without needing any assistance or support (“no armbands like a backstroke”).", "annotation_id": "29160856", "url": "https://genius.com/29160856", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Drop me in the jungle, tree-to-tree, start swingin’ like Tarzan", "vault_lines": ["Drop me in the jungle, tree-to-tree, start swingin' like Tarzan"], "meaning": "Central Cee uses a cleaver Tarzan wordplay. Stating that if he were to be dropped in the jungle (goes back to the hood) then he would be able to adjust to the street life by swinging tree-to-tree (323, which is commonly used to describe an 8mm bullet). 323 is the diameter of the ammunition.", "annotation_id": "29114149", "url": "https://genius.com/29114149", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Crocodile tears, she ain’t even cryin’", "vault_lines": ["Crocodile tears, she ain't even cryin'"], "meaning": "The line “Crocodile tears, she ain’t even crying” is a figurative expression often used to describe insincere or fake displays of emotion. It implies that the person who is seemingly shedding tears is not actually experiencing genuine sadness or remorse.\n\nThe term “crocodile tears” originates from the ancient belief that crocodiles would weep while consuming their prey. In reality, crocodiles produce tears as a natural response to feeding, and the tears have no emotional basis.\n\nWhen someone uses this expression, they are suggesting that the person in question is putting on a show of emotion without truly feeling it. It can be used in various contexts, such as when someone is pretending to be upset to gain sympathy or manipulate a situation. Essentially, it calls out the lack of authenticity in their display of emotion.", "annotation_id": "29113960", "url": "https://genius.com/29113960", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "On Morningside, got no stick in the ride", "vault_lines": ["On Morningside, got no stick in the ride"], "meaning": "Morningside, also known as Seven Oaks, is a suburban neighborhood in Toronto.\n\nBeing a native of Toronto, Drake feels safe riding around in his own city, so he doesn’t take a stick, a term for a firearm, with him in the car. Having no stick in the ride can also refer to the car not being driven with a stick shift, rather using an automatic shift.", "annotation_id": "29106068", "url": "https://genius.com/29106068", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "So much for idols, KMT with the eye roll", "vault_lines": ["So much for idols, KMT with the eye roll"], "meaning": "“KMT” is an abbreviation for kissing my teeth, a British gesture similar in meaning to sarcastically roll one’s eyes, often to express that one is fed up with a conversation, person, or topic. Thus, it can be said that Drake is no longer impressed by the current calibre of talent he’s competing in the hip-hop genre, whether that new or old.\n\nNotably, Drake released a collaboration with the UK artist Giggs in 2017, titled “KMT.”\n\nThe mention of “KMT” may also piggyback from the previous subliminal diss towards XXXTENTACTION, as X claimed that Drake stole his flow on the song “KMT.”", "annotation_id": "29104741", "url": "https://genius.com/29104741", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "6 G-O-D and my broski 2C", "vault_lines": ["6 G-O-D and my broski 2C"], "meaning": "“6 G-O-D” serves as one of Drake’s well-known monikers, with the “6” constituting to Toronto’s six boroughs, which are East York, Etobicoke, North York, Scarborough, Toronto, and York, whiles also representing Toronto’s area codes 416 and 647.\n\nLastly, “2C” serves as one of Central Cee’s many monikers—representing the two initials of his rap name.", "annotation_id": "29102431", "url": "https://genius.com/29102431", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Alright, you know the verse gon’ hit when you hear that adlib", "vault_lines": ["Alright, you know the verse gon' hit when you hear that adlib"], "meaning": "“Alright” is Central Cees signature ad-lib. Meaning, if you hear his ad-lib, you know the next verse is gonna be good because he’s on the track.", "annotation_id": "29101504", "url": "https://genius.com/29101504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Feel like I been here over a thousand years like since the medieval times And I’m still just a cutthroat king even though this is recent times", "vault_lines": ["Feel like I been here over a thousand years like since the medieval times", "And I'm still just a cutthroat king even though this is recent times"], "meaning": "Drake has been in the spotlight since his teenage years as an actor on the popular Canadian teen drama series Degrassi: The Next Generation. Drake then achieved break-out success with his 2009 mixtape So Far Gone and debut studio album Thank Me Later, released under Young Money the following year. Suffice to say, Drake has been one of the biggest names in music for nearly 15 years, yet, despite his tenure, he still strives for greatness, never letting his foot off the gas.", "annotation_id": "29100576", "url": "https://genius.com/29100576", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "If I cum inside, then I won’t leave ’til I see her take the tablet (Nah)", "vault_lines": ["If I cum inside, then I won't leave 'til I see her take the tablet (Nah)"], "meaning": "Cench won’t leave the scene after unprotected intercourse until he sees his partner take the Plan B tablet.\n\nTherefore preventing an unwanted pregnancy.", "annotation_id": "29099271", "url": "https://genius.com/29099271", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Two hundred mill’ on the table, I hope I’m able to get this agreement signed", "vault_lines": ["Two hundred mill' on the table, I hope I'm able to get this agreement signed"], "meaning": "Drake refers to his newly signed joint venture record deal between Republic Records and his very own OVO Sound in 2020. The deal was described as “Lebron sized” which suggests the deal is between $190 million and $300 million.\n\nHowever, on his November 2022, 21 Savage-accompanied, “Major Distribution,” he suggested he signed for higher as he rapped:\n\nBad Bunny numbers, it’s a robbery\nFive-hundred million, just for Aubrey\n\nThis could also be a reference to a table at a casino as Drake is known to love gambling and isn’t afraid to make large bets. If his luck runs out at the casino, he might need a freshly signed agreement to arrange for some additional funds.", "annotation_id": "29099258", "url": "https://genius.com/29099258", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "If Lita’s there, then the mash is there with grains in there like silos", "vault_lines": ["If Lita's there, then the mash is there with grains in there like silos"], "meaning": "In this play on words from Drake, he shouts out Lita, who he’s previously mentioned on his song “On BS” with 21 Savage, where he raps:\n\nI got Lita in this bitch and he might spank it on a lame\n\nA “mash,” is a slang term for a gun in the UK, where Cench is from, and “grains” is UK slang for “bullets” or “hollows.” Thus, Drake implies that if Lita’s there, he will most likely have a gun and bullets.\n\nSilos are structures used in agriculture to store grains in bulk, which is a comparison to how loaded the guns are.", "annotation_id": "29099242", "url": "https://genius.com/29099242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "You know I’m like 6'2\" in the right shoe, love, but you love on some medium guys", "vault_lines": ["You know I'm like 6'2\" in the right shoe, lovе, but you love on some medium guys"], "meaning": "In modern-day, studies show that women prefer men who are over 6ft tall. Nevertheless, despite the studies, the threshold has become a meme throughout social media, pushing men to appear as tall as possible hence why Drake suggests he is past that threshold in certain shoes which makes people perceive him as taller than he is.\n\nDrake also introduces a double entendre as he suggests he’s bigger than other men. This could be taken metaphorically or literally as most men are under six foot, however, Drake is also one of the highest selling rappers of all time.", "annotation_id": "29099198", "url": "https://genius.com/29099198", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Rap boy on a footballer’s wage, I’m on the same pay as the players at Dallas", "vault_lines": ["Rap boy on a footballer's wage, I'm on the same pay as the players at Dallas"], "meaning": "Central Cee suggests his wage is equivalent to a professional footballer who earns millions of pounds in a year. This isn’t Cench’s first time making a reference to the wage of a footballer as in his February 2023, “Me & You,” he rapped:\n\nI got paper, now they say that I’m important\nRap boy on a footballer’s wage, I’m on the same pay as the players at Dortmund\nPrivate plane, that’s normal\n\nThe Dallas Cowboys are an American football team based in Dallas, Texas and play within the Eastern Conference of the NFC.", "annotation_id": "29099196", "url": "https://genius.com/29099196", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "I can lean out the window and horn it, I’m on the same pay as the players at Charlotte", "vault_lines": ["I can lean out the window and horn it, I'm on the same pay as the players at Charlotte"], "meaning": "Cench with the witty worldplay.\n\nWhile he’s leaning out the window of his ride, he’ll “horn it”, or shoot his gun. But, he’s also bragging about his wealth. Stating that he’s on the same pay as the players on the Charlotte Hornets, a NBA team.", "annotation_id": "29099138", "url": "https://genius.com/29099138", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "I’m tryna be under your breasts like peas and rice, you never get treated nice", "vault_lines": ["I'm tryna be under your breasts like peas and rice, you never get treated nice"], "meaning": "This is a clever play on words by Drake, using a double-entendre on “breast.”\n\nMuch like chicken breast, which is commonly served with sides such as peas and rice, Drake is trying to be under this so woman’s actual breasts, or titties in a sexual context.\n\nHe also nods at the fact that she doesn’t even get treated “nice,” which he offers to change through providing her with sexual satisfaction.", "annotation_id": "29099004", "url": "https://genius.com/29099004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Cench might need vaccine when he see how this vid’ start going viral", "vault_lines": ["Cench might need vaccine when he see how this vid' start going viral"], "meaning": "Through some slick wordplay, Drake emphasises how viral their freestyle will become.\n\nCOVID 19, shortened by Drake to “vid,” is a viral disease that sent the world into a global pandemic  in 2020. During the pandemic, various companies produced vaccines to lower the risk of contracting the disease, and, if you did contract the virus, you wouldn’t feel the effects as much as you would have without a vaccine.\n\nDrake says that Central Cee, who’s nickname is Cench, may need a vaccine when he sees how the video, or “vid,” goes viral. This play on words shows that Drake’s pen is not to be played with.", "annotation_id": "29098979", "url": "https://genius.com/29098979", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Seen you around at Sotto, enough man, baby, you love on some eediat guys", "vault_lines": ["Seen you around at Sotto, enough man, baby, you love on some eediat guys"], "meaning": "Drake refers to Sotto Sotto, an upscale Italian restaurant located in Yorkville, Toronto, Canada.\n\nIn addition to being spotted multiple times at Sotto Sotto, Drake also references this restaurant in many of his other songs, including 5AM in Toronto, where he raps:\n\nStashin' money like hoarders off multi-platinum recordings\nEatin' like I’m seated at Swish, Sotto, and Joso’s\nNothing Was The Same, this shit for Ezee and Coco", "annotation_id": "29098960", "url": "https://genius.com/29098960", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "The morning rain clouds up my window, I swear I feel like Dido", "vault_lines": ["The morning rain clouds up my window, I swear I feel like Dido"], "meaning": "Drake describes the cloudy weather in his location with a reference to Dido’s song “Thank You,” released in April 1998, in which she sings:\n\nMy tea’s gone cold, I’m wondering why\nI got out of bed at all\nThe morning rain clouds up my window\nAnd I can’t see at all", "annotation_id": "29098901", "url": "https://genius.com/29098901", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "What you sayin’ wallahi for? You ain’t been on your din, you’ve been eating swine, nah", "vault_lines": ["What you sayin' wallahi for? You ain't been on your din, you've been eating swine, nah"], "meaning": "Through its beliefs and practices, Islam prohibits people from eating pork (swine). Therefore, muslims that eat swine are committing a sin, and are not that religious. In Islam, people pray to Allah, their one and only God. Knowing that, when people swear, they use the term “Wallahi.” It is broken down to “W,” which is “and” in arabic, and “Allah.” In arabic, “Wallahi” means Allah be my witness. In English, “Wallahi” translates to “I swear to God.”\n\nIn a series of verses that reflect his disgust with her dishonesty, Central confronts her use of “Wallahi.” Seeing her commit a sin (eating swine), he does not think she’s in a position to be looking for help from God, nor lying straight to his face.", "annotation_id": "29098896", "url": "https://genius.com/29098896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Email YBeeez for the feature, look", "vault_lines": ["Email YBeeez for the feature, look"], "meaning": "Central Cee references his manager Bello, also known as YBeez.", "annotation_id": "29098858", "url": "https://genius.com/29098858", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Fool you once, that’s fine, but you look dumb if you let them repeat it twice", "vault_lines": ["Fool you once, that's fine, but you look dumb if you let them repeat it twice"], "meaning": "As the proverb goes, “Fool me once, shame on you. Fool me twice, shame on me.” Continuing the discussion of her mistreatment by lesser men than himself, Drake implies that if this girl lets herself get played or lied to more than once, there is no one to blame but herself at that point, making her look dumb since she let herself go through that a second time.", "annotation_id": "29098808", "url": "https://genius.com/29098808", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Tryna round up three hundred Spartans, we goin’ to war but we ain’t in Athens", "vault_lines": ["Tryna round up three hundred Spartans, we goin' to war but we ain't in Athens"], "meaning": "The Battle of Thermopylae (480 BCE) is a historical event in ancient Greece where a King Leonidas led 300 Spartans to defend Thermopylae.", "annotation_id": "29098800", "url": "https://genius.com/29098800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "’Cah we know some demon guys with jealous and evil eyes You know that’s how Jesus died, you know that’s how Julius Caesar died I bet they were decent guys, I swear they remind me of me sometimes", "vault_lines": ["'Cah we know some demon guys with jealous and evil eyes", "You know that's how Jesus died, you know that's how Julius Caesar died", "I bet they were decent guys, I swear they remind me of me sometimes"], "meaning": "Drake refers to those who possess malicious and envious traits, often looking upon others with ill intent, drawing comparison to the circumstances surrounding the death of Jesus Christ, who was betrayed by Judas Iscariot.\n\nThe implication is that jealousy and evil intentions played a role in the crucifixion of Jesus, as told in the Christian religious narrative.\n\nDrake also makes reference to the assassination of Julius Caesar, the Roman statesman and military leader, who was murdered by a group of conspirators, some of whom were motivated by feelings of envy and the desire to protect the Roman Republic from what they saw as his growing power.", "annotation_id": "29098789", "url": "https://genius.com/29098789", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Seen you from the stage in the one hundreds, these must be eagle eyes", "vault_lines": ["Seen you from the stage in the one hundreds, these must be eagle eyes"], "meaning": "This is a deeply layered bar.\n\nFirstly, Drake highlights that the woman in question is seated in one of the cheapest seats in the venue at one of his concerts, likely because her boyfriend can’t afford decent seats. Notably, Drake was on tour with 21 Savage at the time of this freestyle’s release.\n\nOne interpretation of this lyric is that Drake is comparing the girl’s vision and ability to spot him on the stage to that of an eagle, a species which has instinctive clear vision compared to humans.\n\nFurthermore, the phrase “eagle eyes” means to have a careful or close watch on someone or something. Thus, Drake could be inferring that the woman in question is so beautiful that he was able to spot her in the audience due to his impeccable eyesight and knack for spotting beauty", "annotation_id": "29098778", "url": "https://genius.com/29098778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Realistic, the shit might N15 like I’m going through Tottenham High Road", "vault_lines": ["Realistic, the shit might N15 like I'm going through Tottenham High Road"], "meaning": "Drake references Tottenham High Road, located in N15, which is a postcode district in North London incorporating the areas of South Tottenham district: South Tottenham, Harringay (part), West Green, Seven Sisters, Stamford Hill (part) and is covered by the Hackney and Haringey local authorities.\n\n\n\nThis line is also a continuation of the previous line, with Drake negotiating twenty percent of the business but here he states realistically it might end fifteen percent. “End fifteen” sounds very similar to N15.", "annotation_id": "29098747", "url": "https://genius.com/29098747", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "If Chubbs has on a Chanel side bag, you don’t wanna see him reach inside", "vault_lines": ["If Chubbs has on a Chanel side bag, you don't wanna see him reach inside"], "meaning": "Drake mentions his fellow long time friend, Nessel “Chubbs” Beezer. Chubbs is the OVO crew’s heavy artillery and is known to inflict pain upon those who disrespect the 6 God.\n\nIf Chubbs has on a Chanel side bag, he probably has a gun or some form of firearm inside it. Drake is simply saying, don’t make Chubbs reach for it.", "annotation_id": "29098726", "url": "https://genius.com/29098726", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Then I line her like emo", "vault_lines": ["then I line her like emo"], "meaning": "Here drake shares how a woman whom he had relations with try to steal his Rolex watch. These watches price at a minimum of $15,000. He emphasizes that she tried to buy Valentino (an Italian luxury fashion house) using the proceeds from his watch.\n\nTo get back at her Drake claims that he will “line her”, which basically means set her up.\n\nHe says this using his nifty wordplay saying, “I line her like emo”, which doubles as “eyeliner like emo”.\n\nEmo refers to type of person who is overly emotional. This type of person usually listens to punk/rock, used dye in their hair, relates to a lot of dark colors and energy, but most importantly wears eyeliner.", "annotation_id": "29098724", "url": "https://genius.com/29098724", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "If I take flicks with the guys, I gotta put emojis over like three faces ’cause the feds can’t see those eyes People I shouldn’t be beside When I was an actor, they would go Stratford just to sell food on Bieber’s side", "vault_lines": ["People I shouldn't be beside", "When I was an actor, they would go Stratford just to sell food on Bieber's side"], "meaning": "Drake contrasts his youth, spent as an actor on the teen drama series Degrassi: The Next Generation, with that of his friends, who sold drugs (also known as “food” in UK slang) in Stratford, Ontario, a small town where Drake’s friend and musical collaborator Justin Bieber grew up. Stratford is approximately 1 hr 30 mins away from Drake’s hometown of Toronto", "annotation_id": "29098686", "url": "https://genius.com/29098686", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "She didn’t rock my clothing line when we had one shirt to go with the jeans though Now man like me and Clint can afford those whips like Gran Torino", "vault_lines": ["She didn't rock my clothing line when we had one shirt to go with the jeans though", "Now man like me and Clint can afford those whips like Gran Torino"], "meaning": "Despite people initially not wanting to wear his merchandise when he first started making music, Drake has since come along way, with his OVO clothing brand and Nike “Nocta” collaboration now being highly popular.\n\nSimilarly, Drake draws a comparison between himself and Clint419, the British fashion designer who founded the popular streetwear brand Cortiez, which Drake has been seen wearing.\n\nGran Torino is a 2008 film directed by and starring Clint Eastwood, in which Eastwood has an immaculately maintained 1972 Ford Torino. Much like Eastwood, who is worth approximately $370 million, Drake and Clint can now afford expensive vehicles.", "annotation_id": "29098679", "url": "https://genius.com/29098679", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "If it’s beef, I’ll get that shaved for bread and grease like I’m making giros, or I’m making gyros", "vault_lines": ["If it's beef, I'll get that shaved for bread and grease like I'm making giros, or I'm making gyros"], "meaning": "It is a common mistake for people to mispronounce the Greek wrap “gyro” (pronounced yee-roh), instead pronouncing it like\n“Giro.” Drake intentionally messes up the pronunciation for the purpose of the rhyme, but quickly saves himself in the following bar.\n\nDrake states that if he gets into “beef,” another word for a confrontation, he won’t be bothered due to his “bread” and “grease,“ both slang for money, and in addition to beef, they are all ingredients usually found in gyros.\n\nFurthermore, Drake’s pronunciation of gyros sounds like Euros, the official currency of most European countries, which Drake is undoubtedly making lots of.", "annotation_id": "29098600", "url": "https://genius.com/29098600", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "line": "Come a long way, I was sellin’ them opium on my lonely, but I’m not Ken Carson", "vault_lines": ["Come a long way, I was sellin' them opium on my lonely, but I'm not Ken Carson"], "meaning": "Central Cee subtly discusses his early life by using a double entendre to reference Playboi Carti’s Opium label signees, Ken Carson and Destroy Lonely.\n\nAccording to a quote he gave for an article in The Guardian, Cee was dealing drugs by the age of 13:\n\nAt 13, he started making music with little outside influence beyond 2Pac’s Ghetto Gospel on MP3. He did regular jobs in the meantime, by which he mostly means dealing drugs. “When you’re coming from where I’m coming from, everybody from all walks of life, nine times out of 10, they had to do that. It’s like learning to ride a bike.\"", "annotation_id": "29098474", "url": "https://genius.com/29098474", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On The Radar Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 50, "clicked": 50, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-central-cee-on-the-radar-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "On The Radar Freestyle"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Watchin’ Hov’s show, embarrassed to pull my camera out", "vault_lines": ["Watchin' Hov's show, embarrassed to pull my camera out", "And my mother embarrassed to pull my Phantom out"], "meaning": "Transitioning to being a star, drake finds it difficult to no longer be a fanboy and has to start assuming the role rather than videotaping other stars. this refrences his ealier bar “I can’t live and hold the camera someone gotta tape this”", "annotation_id": "34245565", "url": "https://genius.com/34245565", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "And Jordan sweat suits are your flight clothes And you still make it even when they say your flight closed", "vault_lines": ["When Stefano Pilati suits are your night clothes", "And Jordan sweat suits are your flight clothes", "And you still make it even when they say your flight closed"], "meaning": "This is when drake had officially reached stardom, he was a known celebrity and could do things that normal people cant like show up late to a flight and still make it. this doesnt happen to him now of course because he has his own private jet", "annotation_id": "34245528", "url": "https://genius.com/34245528", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "And make hits like a bitch that’s married, I ain’t miss", "vault_lines": ["And make hits like a bitch that's married, I ain't miss"], "meaning": "Drake is playing on the idea that when a woman gets married, her title changes from “Miss” to “Mrs.” By this, he cleverly implies that he never “misses” when he releases a song.", "annotation_id": "33960380", "url": "https://genius.com/33960380", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Feedback", "vault_lines": ["So they stick around kicking out feedback"], "meaning": "The “cue to go” here sounds like “cuticle” which is continued in the line “kicking out feedback” – “kicking our feet back”.\n\nSimilar to a pedicure session, Drake is relaxed while he drops these hard bars.", "annotation_id": "33398820", "url": "https://genius.com/33398820", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Cue to go", "vault_lines": ["To give these bitch niggas the cue to go"], "meaning": "The “cue to go” here sounds like “cuticle” which is continued in the next line “kicking out feedback” – “kicking our feet back”.\n\nSimilar to a pedicure session, Drake is relaxed while he drops these hard bars.", "annotation_id": "33398805", "url": "https://genius.com/33398805", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Life was so full, now this shit just being lipo’d", "vault_lines": ["Life was so full, now this shit just being lipo'd"], "meaning": "Liposuction surgery is when body fat is removed to make the patient slimmer.\n\nDrake is expressing that before being famous his life was more enjoyable", "annotation_id": "30054433", "url": "https://genius.com/30054433", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "24 hours from greatness, I’m that close", "vault_lines": ["24 hours from greatness, I'm that close"], "meaning": "This was the last song Drake recorded on this mixtape before its release, because he had just received the Kanye west beat and decided to hop on it almost 24 hours before he released the mixtape", "annotation_id": "30054402", "url": "https://genius.com/30054402", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "And my mother embarrassed to pull my Phantom out So I park about five houses down", "vault_lines": ["And my mother embarrassed to pull my Phantom out", "So I park about five houses down"], "meaning": "Drake’s mother was very strict about how Drake spent his money and so when leased himself a Rolls Royce Phantom, she didn’t want to be seen with it. So she made him park down the block", "annotation_id": "30054383", "url": "https://genius.com/30054383", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "And everybody talks and everybody listen And somehow the truth just always comes up missing", "vault_lines": ["And everybody talks and everybody listen", "And somehow the truth just always comes up missing"], "meaning": "data:image/png;base64,", "annotation_id": "29801331", "url": "https://genius.com/29801331", "state": "pending", "votes": -7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Drug dealers live vicariously through me", "vault_lines": ["Drug dealers live vicariously through me"], "meaning": "In conjunction with the ‘23' on the previous bar, this alludes to how Michael Jordan often says how he lives vicariously through others, whether it be his family, other athletes or his teams.", "annotation_id": "27140268", "url": "https://genius.com/27140268", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Boy, and to my city I’m the 2-3", "vault_lines": ["Boy, and to my city I'm the 2-3"], "meaning": "Drake is comparing himself to being the Michael Jordan of Toronto. Jordan is the most well-known basketball player of all time and wore number 23 while on the Chicago Bulls. In his later music, Drake refers to Toronto as the 6 and he is known as the 6 god.", "annotation_id": "20214694", "url": "https://genius.com/20214694", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Future said, “’Cause this ’Ye shit, you better kill”", "vault_lines": ["Future said, \"'Cause this 'Ye shit, you better kill\""], "meaning": "The beat of “Say you Will”, produced and recorded  initially by Kanye West off 808s & Heartbreak is used by Drake here. On the project, Drake used other artist’s beats for example Jay-Z and Kanye West ( as seen on this track). This track was essentially a test of Drake’s skills. What his manager is saying, Future the Prince, is that this is Drake’s shot to rap over a legendary and highly popular instrumental and his shot to prove himself in Hip Hop.", "annotation_id": "18557007", "url": "https://genius.com/18557007", "state": "pending", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Why do I feel so alone?", "vault_lines": ["Why do I feel so alone?"], "meaning": "Drake begins the track with a question, mirroring Kanye on “Say You Will” when he sings:\n\nWhy would she make calls out the blue?\n\nLike Ye, Drake is questioning his relationships and struggling to make them work.", "annotation_id": "16491208", "url": "https://genius.com/16491208", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Los Angeles Cabanas or Atlanta South", "vault_lines": ["Los Angeles Cabanas or Atlanta South"], "meaning": "A cabana is “a small hut built with a thatched roof.” You probably wouldn’t find any in Los Angeles, but they’re perfect for California weather on the shore.\n\n\n\nAtlanta, on the other hand, is not so luxurious. It is the sixth most dangerous city in the US, serving as a perfect trafficking hub specifically for drugs imported from Mexico.\n\n\n\nDrizzy is familiar with both the lifestyle of a rockstar on one coast, as well as that of a poor and young black male hungry to make a name for himself on the other.", "annotation_id": "4203078", "url": "https://genius.com/4203078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Oh, they did, Po, at least they tried to", "vault_lines": ["Oh, they did, Po, at least they tried to"], "meaning": "Cortez Bryant, or Tez Poe, is Drizzy’s friend and manager who has been there from the start. He also manages Weezy and Nicki.\n\nHe was obviously a big part of Drake’s signing process, but here it’s news to him how much Sylvia Rhone and Universal secretly wanted him.", "annotation_id": "4202988", "url": "https://genius.com/4202988", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "But slip up and shoot the wrong fucking video And they think they can market you however they decide to", "vault_lines": ["But slip up and shoot the wrong fucking video", "And they think they can market you however they decide to"], "meaning": "A reference to the Replacement girl video with Trey Songz (circa 2007). Drake had the typical over-sized clothes with girls dancing in the background type video. Even his flow was reflective of popular rappers at the time. His team, from that video alone, figured they can market him like any other rapper at the time due to how that video turned out.", "annotation_id": "137228", "url": "https://genius.com/137228", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "So she wonder where my mind is, accounts in the minus But yet I’m rolling round the fuckin’ city like your highness", "vault_lines": ["So she wonder where my mind is, accounts in the minus", "But yet I'm rolling round the fuckin' city like your highness"], "meaning": "Although Drake is on the cusp of super-stardom, he’s spending his show money and such on those Stefano Pilati suits and (presumably leasing) a Phantom, which results in him actually not having any money in his account. Despite this, you gotta floss like you got it.\n\nTypical rap meme of “Your favorite artist doesn’t have all that money he claims to have”\n\nAlso a play on words with “minus” meaning negative, and “highness” meaning positive – a math reference.", "annotation_id": "58107", "url": "https://genius.com/58107", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "I quit school and it’s not because I’m lazy I’m just not the social type and campus life is crazy", "vault_lines": ["I quit school and it's not because I'm lazy", "I'm just not the social type and campus life is crazy"], "meaning": "Drake attended Forest Hill Collegiate Institute where he began acting. He later quit once he got a recurring role on “Degrassi.” However, even though he says “campus,” Forest hill is a high school.\n\nHighschool is a social battleground in term so, if you don’t belong in the social highschool life than you tend to feel it’s a little crazy.\n\nIn future reference a University campus is just more students than a high school, so if you didn’t enjoy the social highschool life, you ain’t gonna enjoy the mass of socialites that be in College/University.", "annotation_id": "50458", "url": "https://genius.com/50458", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "Lost some of my hottest verses down in Cabo So if you find a Blackberry with the side scroll Sell that mothafucka to any rapper that I know", "vault_lines": ["Lost some of my hottest verses down in Cabo", "So if you find a Blackberry with the side scroll", "Sell that mothafucka to any rapper that I know"], "meaning": "Drake lost his old Blackberry in Mexico, on which he writes all his verses", "annotation_id": "33899", "url": "https://genius.com/33899", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "And promoters try to get me out to they club And say I have fun but I can’t imagine how ’Cause I just seen my ex-girl, standing with my next girl Standing with the girl that I’m fuckin’ right now And shit could get weird unless they all down And so I stay clear, we from a small town", "vault_lines": ["And promoters try to get me out to they club", "And say I have fun but I can't imagine how", "'Cause I just seen my ex-girl, standing with my next girl", "Standing with the girl that I'm fuckin' right now", "And shit could get weird unless they all down", "And so I stay clear, we from a small town", "And I don't have the heart"], "meaning": "Because of his fame, he can never have a good time at the clubs because his relationships with different women always seem to cause him trouble. Imagine that scene… that wouldn’t be a good thing for Drake, especially if their menses are synchronized. That’s a lot of hormones!\n\nThe only way this wouldn’t be awkward is if they were down for a 4-way and a little daisy chain action.", "annotation_id": "33895", "url": "https://genius.com/33895", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "It’s hard to think of them polite flows When Stefano Pilati suits are your night clothes", "vault_lines": ["It's hard to think of them polite flows", "When Stefano Pilati suits are your night clothes"], "meaning": "Drake finds it difficult to stay polite in his music as his lifestyle changes and his economical status grows.\n\nHe reflects this by namedropping Stefano Pilati, the head designer at the time of the French fashion brand, Yves Saint Laurent.", "annotation_id": "33709", "url": "https://genius.com/33709", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say What’s Real", "line": "I’ve always been something that these labels can’t buy Especially if they tryin’ to take a piece of my soul And Sylvia be tellin’ Tez, “Damn, Drake fly” And he just be like, ”Silly motherfucker, I know That was your bad, how could you pass up on ’em?", "vault_lines": ["I've always been something that these labels can't buy", "Especially if they tryin' to take a piece of my soul", "And Sylvia be tellin' Tez, \"Damn, Drake fly\"", "And he just be like, \"Silly motherfucker, I know", "That was your bad, how could you pass up on 'em?", "He just take them records and he gas up on 'em"], "meaning": "This is regarding Drake’s beef with Sylvia Rhone, President of Motown Records, who Drake accuses of threatening him with legal action if he signed with a different label. Now Drake is saying they made a big mistake by mistreating him and allowing him to leave because he’s become one of the most popular hip-hop artists in the world\n\nAlso notice Soul/Motown pun", "annotation_id": "26757", "url": "https://genius.com/26757", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
