{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "’Cause I’m the one to paint a vivid picture, no HD", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I'm the one to paint a vivid picture, no HD"], "meaning": "Drake is saying no HD is required to see his vivid vision.\n\nThis came at a time when High-Definition Television (or HDTV) was becoming more standard for TV stations, cable/satellite channels, and physical media (Blu-ray, HD DVD).\n\n\n\nHDTV describes a television or video system which provides a substantially higher image resolution than the previous generation of technologies.", "annotation_id": "33980272", "url": "https://genius.com/33980272", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "Ha, ain’t that a female dog?", "vault_lines": ["Ha, ain't that a female dog?"], "meaning": "Weezy interpolates the phrase “Ain’t that a bitch?”\n\nThe term bitch comes from the Old English word bicce or bicge, meaning “female dog”, which dates to around 1000 CE. It may have derived from the earlier Old Norse word bikkja, also meaning “female dog”", "annotation_id": "16655141", "url": "https://genius.com/16655141", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "[Interlude]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "While this interlude was included in the original release of So Far Gone, it was removed from the song on the shortened EP version of the project. However, when So Far Gone was later re-released in full for its 10th anniversary, the interlude was re-added back. The interlude was also part of a leaked demo for “Successful” that includes another Drake verse.", "annotation_id": "16535385", "url": "https://genius.com/16535385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "I’ve always treated my city like some shoulder pads", "vault_lines": ["I've always treated my city like some shoulder pads"], "meaning": "Shoulder pads are a type of fabric-covered padding used in men’s and women’s clothing to give the wearer the illusion of having broader and less sloping shoulders.\nDrake here is trying to say that his singing and the city which he represents (which happens to be Toronto) is trying to support each other as he’s trying to do something big in industry and Toronto is getting all the fame as when they’ll know where he’s from.\nAlso Drake loves to wear shoulder pads and he’s seen sporting it many times.\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/Bp3G6onAyF0/?utm_source=ig_web_copy_link", "annotation_id": "16507534", "url": "https://genius.com/16507534", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "Tired of hearin’ bullshit, bring on the cow shit", "vault_lines": ["Tired of hearin' bullshit, bring on the cow shit"], "meaning": "“Bullshit” means “nonsense”. Here, Wayne says that he’s tired of hearing other artist’s useless bars and verses and he’s ready for some “cow shit”.\nAccording to Hindus, “cow” is often referred to as “god”. So he says he’s waiting for some songs which he can respect and which are extraordinary.", "annotation_id": "16507513", "url": "https://genius.com/16507513", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "And I’ma fuck the world, but this is just foreplay", "vault_lines": ["And I'ma fuck the world, but this is just foreplay"], "meaning": "Here, Lil Wayne says that he’s ready to bring in some fire beats and songs in the coming future and this is just a teased start.\nHe implied the same line using sexual reference saying that just like “foreplay” is a sexual activity that precedes the sexual intercourse is the same way his song for now precedes the upcoming new songs.\nAlso this can be a very slight reference to the fact that he’s introducing Drake to the real music industry via this mixtape and says that this is just a teaser (foreplay) and more is yet to come under his guidance.", "annotation_id": "16507497", "url": "https://genius.com/16507497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "And when I leave, I always come right back here", "vault_lines": ["And when I leave, I always come right back here"], "meaning": "Drake has never been shy of his love for Toronto. In an industry where people relocate to hubs like Los Angeles, Drake reminds everyone that Toronto is his only home.\n\nOn his first project Room For Improvement Drake released a song titled “City is Mine”. In the hook he explains it simply for listeners:\n\nYo, the city is mine (which one?)\nT-O-R-O-N-T-O\nD-R-A-K-E that’s me\nYou know how the story goes", "annotation_id": "13639691", "url": "https://genius.com/13639691", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "I’m on the other side, but it is a short gate", "vault_lines": ["I'm on the other side, but it is a short gate"], "meaning": "This line may have a double meaning:\n\nFirst, Lil Wayne’s mind is elevated, as it’s on whole other level than the one of common mortals. So, the other side is a place where only selected influential people lounge. They have an artistic vision and purpose.\n\nSecond, by other side he means the world of wealth and fame, in which he is a permanent citizen. The other side is a place were all celebrities and rich people are. By short gate he means that the entrance of this world is accessible by few people, who have vision and determination, just like Lil Wayne.", "annotation_id": "6609963", "url": "https://genius.com/6609963", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "Damn, my reality just set in", "vault_lines": ["Damn, my reality just set in"], "meaning": "This line sums up this verse and where Drake was during that time of his life, basically doing everything to make it in the rap game, but at this point he realizes  that maybe he’s not doing the right things, like spending money on drinks and leasing cars. But, in this case the ends did justify the means and everything worked out just fine for him so..", "annotation_id": "4114976", "url": "https://genius.com/4114976", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "I do a lot of things hopin’ I never have to fit in So tryna keep up with my progress, it’s like a dead-end", "vault_lines": ["I do a lot of things hopin' I never have to fit in", "So tryna keep up with my progress, it's like a dead-end"], "meaning": "Here Drake kind of contradicts himself, because he admits to having leased a Phantom in the previous line, something he needed to feed his ego but also of course to blend into the rap game.\n\nBut, he could also be talking about how he was already creating his own lane on rap, so that he wouldn’t have to “fit” in by any standards.", "annotation_id": "4114137", "url": "https://genius.com/4114137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "I suppose", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In an MTV interview, Trey Songz said:\n\nConceptually for me, the deepest part of the record was when I say, ‘I suppose.’ I supposed that’s what success is about. That’s what [we’re] led to believe.\"", "annotation_id": "807667", "url": "https://genius.com/807667", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "Nickels for my thoughts,", "vault_lines": ["Nickels for my thoughts,"], "meaning": "Its taken from the Old English saying “penny for your thoughts” which is simply asking people to volunteer their opinions on an issue being discussed or “put in their two cents.”\n\n\n\nHis opinions must be worth every penny if he’s using nickels for his thoughts…", "annotation_id": "535609", "url": "https://genius.com/535609", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "And my mother tried to run away from home But I left something in the car and so I caught her in the driveway", "vault_lines": ["And my mother tried to run away from home", "But I left something in the car and so I caught her in the driveway"], "meaning": "Drake explained this lyric in a 2009 interview with MTV News:\n\n\nWhen I was going through the creative process for So Far Gone, I was actually at a pretty dark place in my life. It was a frustrating time for my family, because my grandmother—who is now in her mid- to late 90s—was just losing it. It was hard for my mother to watch. And it was just, it was really at a point where it was like, ‘Is this rap thing going to work?’ Like, ‘Is this my choice? Is this what I am committing to?’ Money was an issue. Degrassi had ended years ago, and we were just all kind of trying to figure stuff out.\n\nSo my mother was going through a rough time. And yeah, man, one night she just broke down and tried to actually run away from the house, tried to leave it all behind. It was just a lot going on, and she never would have really left. She would’ve always come back, but it was just a frustrating moment for her. So I had forgot something in my car, and I came outside and I saw bags sitting by her car. I saw her out there, and she just started crying right away when she saw me. Anyway, I don’t wanna get too emotional, but I had to hug her. My father doesn’t stay with us, so I had to be security for her and just let her know that everything is going to be all right.", "annotation_id": "23152", "url": "https://genius.com/23152", "state": "accepted", "votes": 161, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "How come I can’t Miss a woman like I can’t miss court dates", "vault_lines": ["Miss a woman like I can't miss court dates"], "meaning": "This line was written during Lil Wayne’s legal troubles in New York, where he was sentenced to one year on Rikers Island for weapons possession. One of Wayne’s themes is how low women are on his list of priorities – money, success, and now court dates are all higher", "annotation_id": "19324", "url": "https://genius.com/19324", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "Haven’t met a smell that’s stinkier than our shit And that’s word to Toronto So high up, I got birds in the condo", "vault_lines": ["Haven't met a smell that's stinkier than our shit", "And that's word to Toronto", "So high up, I got birds in the condo"], "meaning": "He is so high (stoned AND literal altitude) that he has “birds” (UK slang for woman) in his apartment.\n\nAlso the more obvious, being high up he has literal birds (the flying  ones) in his condo.\n\nBirds is referred to kilos of cocaine as his stepfather Birdman used to say in his songs.\n\nBirds also ties back to “owl shit”, as Drake (who is from Toronto) owns a record label, OVO Sound. Their logo is an owl.", "annotation_id": "19013", "url": "https://genius.com/19013", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "line": "Diss me, you’ll never hear a reply for it", "vault_lines": ["Diss me, you'll never hear a reply for it"], "meaning": "Unlike his rapping peers (e.g. Jay Z), Drake doesn’t respond to disses.\n\nThis claim is backed up by the Pusha T/YMCMB feud back in 2012. Pusha T was dissing Drake, but it was Lil Wayne that clapped back, and all he got from Drake was a few subliminals in “Lord Knows”.\n\nThat being said, Drake almost certainly replied to Common’s disses on Sweet in Rick Ross' Stay Schemin', and may or may not have replied to Kendrick Lamar’s Control verse on either The Language or Future’s Shit Remix.\n\nIn 2015 Drake replied to another diss, this time set off by a set of tweets from Meek Mill. Rather than just tweeting back or staying quiet, Drake hit Meek with the one-two punch of “Charged Up” and “Back to Back.”", "annotation_id": "18015", "url": "https://genius.com/18015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Successful", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-successful-lyrics", "genius_title": "Successful"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deceiving", "line": "I’m Uncle Phil I switch wifey like every season", "vault_lines": ["I'm Uncle Phil I switch wifey like every season"], "meaning": "After season 3 of the popular 90’s sitcom ‘the Fresh Prince of Bel Air’ the actress that played aunt Viv, Janet Hubert, was controversially replaced by actress Daphne Reid after many of the cast members (most notably Will Smith) reportedly did not get along well with the former.\n\nDrake attributes himself to Uncle Phil in a braggadocious way–he changes girls ‘like every season’\n\nFun Fact: In 2004 during an episode of Degrassi Unscripted Drake showcased (for maybe the first time on television) his rapping ability and used a similar bar. The verse was as follows:\n\n“I’ve been a mess since,every girl I left went,\nand got a new man,\nbut im the new version of fresh prince.\nAnd the Benz got bump for real, I switch wifey every season like Uncle Phil.”", "annotation_id": "20213559", "url": "https://genius.com/20213559", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deceiving", "line": "And I don’t wanna diss niggas when I write no more", "vault_lines": ["Cause I don't really wanna fight no more", "And I don't wanna diss niggas when I write no more"], "meaning": "He wants to settle down and stop subliminally dissing niggas in his songs. Ironically, this is a track off Comeback Season which released in 2008, and it appears he’s failed to take his own advice, as the track he just jumped on with the one and only “Rozay” has him taking shots at Common.", "annotation_id": "538151", "url": "https://genius.com/538151", "state": "accepted", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
