{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Drake, here’s how they gon’ come at you", "vault_lines": ["Drake, here's how they gon' come at you"], "meaning": "Drake has been in multiple many beefs, excluding those he hasn’t given energy, but early in his career jayz warned him. He’s since been in rap beefs with rappers like meek mill, pusha t, Kendrick Lamar and more. Despite it all OVO continues to reach new heights\n\nhttps://www.billboard.com/lists/drake-kendrick-lamar-beef-timeline/nov-2011-kendrick-lamar-featured-on-drakes-take-care-album/\n\nhttps://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/drakes-march-14-and-pusha-ts-the-story-of-adidon-a-complicated-relationship-696198/\n\nhttps://www.bet.com/photo-gallery/ejp1hc/a-timeline-of-the-meek-mill-vs-drake-drama/0h48ct", "annotation_id": "36278183", "url": "https://genius.com/36278183", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "They must be talkin’ to themselves, Hov: hands free", "vault_lines": ["They must be talkin' to themselves, Hov: hands free"], "meaning": "Drake is likely talking about HandsFreeLink. The Bluetooth feature standard on most Honda/Acura models.", "annotation_id": "34042948", "url": "https://genius.com/34042948", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "And since no good deed go unpunished I’m not as cool with niggas as I once was", "vault_lines": ["And since no good deed go unpunished", "I'm not as cool with niggas as I once was", "I once was cool as the Fonz was"], "meaning": "The phrase “no good deed goes unpunished” suggests that even when someone tries to do something good or helpful, they may end up suffering consequences for their actions. It’s a cynical or sometimes humorously ironic view on the idea that good intentions don’t always lead to positive outcomes. What Jay Z is saying here is, since by doing good for others you end up paying the price beyond money, he is no longer open to helping people around him as he used to long before he got here. This line can be connect with what he said in the song Blueprint 2 where he said “When the Twin Towers dropped, I was the first in line\nDonatin' proceeds off every ticket sold, When I was out on the road, that’s how you judge Hov, no?” in this scenerio the good deed is donating proceeds and the punishment is everyone talking about everything else but this.", "annotation_id": "32923579", "url": "https://genius.com/32923579", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Top down in the winter, that’s what winners do", "vault_lines": ["Top down in the winter, that's what winners do"], "meaning": "Someone who wins at life is able to spend the winters in nice warm areas and able to ride his convertible in months when most people don’ take their convertible out.", "annotation_id": "32851127", "url": "https://genius.com/32851127", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Before one of these haters load a couple shells and shoot", "vault_lines": ["Before one of these haters load a couple shells and shoot"], "meaning": "Before his competition makes a diss track calling out Drake.", "annotation_id": "32429346", "url": "https://genius.com/32429346", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "I got these new rappers nervous: prom night, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I got these new rappers nervous: prom night, nigga"], "meaning": "A couple in Prom Night before they meet, they are often nervous not sure how the night will go. So he alludes these rappers are nervous of Drake’s presence in the game.", "annotation_id": "32429340", "url": "https://genius.com/32429340", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Y’all can miss me with the money talk", "vault_lines": ["Y'all can miss me with the money talk"], "meaning": "This might be a clever reference to the song. Miss me, which is literally the next track on the album. This being track ten while Miss Me is track eleven on the album: ‘Thank Me Later.’\n\n\n\nIt’s also worth noting that later. In Drake’s career, he would have a song called: ‘Dreams that Money Can’t Buy.’", "annotation_id": "31985688", "url": "https://genius.com/31985688", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Sorry, mama, I promised it wouldn’t change me But I would have went insane had I remained the same me", "vault_lines": ["Sorry, mama, I promised it wouldn't change me", "But I would have went insane had I remained the same me"], "meaning": "HOV", "annotation_id": "30147933", "url": "https://genius.com/30147933", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Yeah, and I’m just fillin’ up this daily planner Gettin’ busy ’cause I’m a star: no spangled banner", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and I'm just fillin' up this daily planner", "Gettin' busy 'cause I'm a star: no spangled banner"], "meaning": "His daily planner is full, because he is so busy working. Drake also refers to the Star Spangled Banner, which is the national anthem of the USA", "annotation_id": "26676315", "url": "https://genius.com/26676315", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "I grow tired of these fuckin’ grown man liars Storytellers, they ain’t even need a campfire", "vault_lines": ["I grow tired of these fuckin' grown man liars", "Storytellers, they ain't even need a campfire"], "meaning": "A witty and funny line comparing naysayers to storytelling at a campfire. The naysayers,who can be critics and other jealous rappers, tell lies about Drake and make up stories. People, especially kids, gather around campfires to tell and listen to stories. So Drake is also grown men by comparing them to children.", "annotation_id": "26676267", "url": "https://genius.com/26676267", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "‘Cause while all of my closest friends out partyin’ I’m just here makin’ all the music that they party to", "vault_lines": ["I'm the man of the hour", "'Cause while all of my closest friends out partyin'", "I'm just here makin' all the music that they party to"], "meaning": "Drake shows how busy (and maybe lonely) he is. He makes the music (on his own) that his friends party to. Most people party at night, so Drake also implies that he works late night shifts regularly. Working late night is a theme Drake likes. One example from the song “6 Man”:\n\n\nYoung, but I'm makin' millions to work the night shift― Drake – 6 Man", "annotation_id": "26676228", "url": "https://genius.com/26676228", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "They always tell me: “Nobody’s workin’ as hard as you” And even though I laugh it off, man, it’s probably true", "vault_lines": ["They always tell me: \"Nobody's workin' as hard as you\"", "And even though I laugh it off, man, it's probably true"], "meaning": "Drake stresses his work ethic and humility at the same time.", "annotation_id": "26676211", "url": "https://genius.com/26676211", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Rolexes, chauffers and low fades", "vault_lines": ["Rolexes, chauffers and low fades"], "meaning": "Drake lists several things which have to do with wealth and appearance: Luxury watches and drivers mean wealth. Low fades are a type of haircut, meaning Drake also takes care of his looks.", "annotation_id": "26676138", "url": "https://genius.com/26676138", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "The smart money’s on Hov’, fuck what the dummies talk, uhh", "vault_lines": ["The smart money's on Hov', fuck what the dummies talk, uhh"], "meaning": "“Smart money” refers to cash invested or wagered by those considered experienced, well informed, “in-the-know,” or all three. It is the collective force of big money that can move markets. The term, “smart money” comes from gamblers that had a deep knowledge of the sport they were betting on or insider knowledge that the public was unable to tap into.", "annotation_id": "25376708", "url": "https://genius.com/25376708", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "[Verse 1: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "According to Songfacts, Drake told in an interview on MTV2’s Sucker Free about this verse :\n\nMy verse was way harder on “Light Up” before. I had done a song, and nothing was supposed to happen with it, and it leaked. The bars from the song were so hard, it was a song with Big Sean [‘Made It’]. There were some bars on the song that fit better with }Light Up\" and made so much more sense. And the last night before mastering, I had to go in and switch the first 12 bars. On “Light Up,” I lost my footing a bit, and Jay came in and killed me.", "annotation_id": "19821056", "url": "https://genius.com/19821056", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "And I just say I wrote it for your girlfriends: Kelsey Grammer", "vault_lines": ["And I just say I wrote it for your girlfriends: Kelsey Grammer"], "meaning": "Kelsey Grammar is an actor/writer/director/producer who is best known for his iconic portrayal of Dr. Crane on Cheers and Frasier. In this line, Drake references the show Girlfriends which ran from 2000-2008 and was produced by Grammer.", "annotation_id": "18967614", "url": "https://genius.com/18967614", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "I keep thinkin’, how young can you die from old age?", "vault_lines": ["I keep thinkin', how young can you die from old age?"], "meaning": "What Drake’s saying or trying to say is that he’s making old money", "annotation_id": "18935981", "url": "https://genius.com/18935981", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Welcome to Hollywood, don’t let this town ruin you And if you pillow-talkin’ with the women that are screwin’ you Just know that she gon’ tell another nigga When she through with you", "vault_lines": ["Welcome to Hollywood, don't let this town ruin you", "And if you pillow-talkin' with the women that are screwin' you", "Just know that she gon' tell another nigga", "When she through with you"], "meaning": "This song came out in 2010, yet ironically enough, Drake’s long time producer and friend Noah “40” Shebib allegedly was the one who leaked information about Drake’s child in 2018.\n\nAccording to Pusha T on an October 17th, 2018 interview on Joe Budden’s podcast, he said that a woman claiming to have slept with 40 had leaked this information. Hence, the pillow talking.\n\nDrake’s “secret” child ended up being the premise of Pusha T’s “The Story of Adidon” diss that took shots at Drake’s fatherhood, family, and 40. This diss track was a heavy blow to Drake – prompting him not to respond with another track.\n\nThese lines are another eerie future predicting example in this 2010 song where Jay-Z later warns Drake about rap feuds.", "annotation_id": "15597281", "url": "https://genius.com/15597281", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "I once was cool as the Fonz was", "vault_lines": ["I'm not as cool with niggas as I once was", "I once was cool as the Fonz was"], "meaning": "Before his success, Hov claims that he was nice and compares himself as Fonzie, the famous fictional character from Happy Days for that. Fonzie says in every episode of the series that he’s “cool”.", "annotation_id": "11598084", "url": "https://genius.com/11598084", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Let’s celebrate with a toast and get lost in tonight And make it all light up (Ay Guru tell, tell homegirl to open that Ace right there...) Wait until the sun goes down We gon’ make this bitch light up Even when the sun goes down I’ma make it go", "vault_lines": ["Let's celebrate with a toast and get lost in tonight", "And make it all light up", "Wait until the sun goes down", "We gon' make this bitch light up", "Even when the sun goes down I'ma make it go"], "meaning": "Note the parallel between the sun and light: once the natural light source goes away, Drake and his friends will physically “light up” marijuana or metaphorically “light up” the club by getting everyone going in order to celebrate their success.", "annotation_id": "1328783", "url": "https://genius.com/1328783", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "I just run the town, I don’t do too much joggin’", "vault_lines": ["I don't do too much bloggin'", "I just run the town, I don't do too much joggin'"], "meaning": "While Jay doesn’t get much exercise, he still runs New York City and the rap game. He’s also implying that he does this with little effort.\n\n\n\nPotential allusion to Jay’s “Run This Town”\n\nJay’s love of the homophone comes to light, as he pronounces “jogging” similar to “jargon”, a word that usually describes annoying, elitist forms of speech, particularly with relation to the blogosphere.", "annotation_id": "731715", "url": "https://genius.com/731715", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Uh, I’ve been up for four days, gettin’ money both ways Dirty and clean,", "vault_lines": ["Uh, I've been up for four days, gettin' money both ways"], "meaning": "Although Drake has made it clear that he “avoided the coke game and went with Sprite instead”, this is just him stunting and presenting himself as tough as he occasionally does on a track. The meaning being that he can get money through legitimate means such as selling records and touring, as well as through selling drugs and hustling.", "annotation_id": "204721", "url": "https://genius.com/204721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "I done seen it all, done it all That’s why none of these dum-dums could done him off", "vault_lines": ["I done seen it all, done it all", "That's why none of these dum-dums could done him off"], "meaning": "Jay has hustled, rapped, and managed. He’s played every role from the ground up and never got tripped up, so there’s no way he’s going to let a few idiots put an end to his reign.\n\nThis could also be seen as another Godfather reference, as “done” sounds very similar to “Don”, the name of the head of the family in the Sicilian mafia. So Jay is saying he’s like Don Corleone, and none of the “dum Dons” can “Don” him off, despite attempts from other families in the movie to assassinate Don Corleone.", "annotation_id": "198485", "url": "https://genius.com/198485", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "With silly rap feuds, tryin’ to distract you", "vault_lines": ["With silly rap feuds, tryin' to distract you"], "meaning": "This line became prophetic just 4 years later, when Jay and Drake got into their own lyrical altercations over fondue, lacrosse, and fine art. The subliminals have continued into 2017, with Jay calling Drake a “runner” and not a “boss” on 2017’s “Shining.”\n\nDrake certainly didn’t take Jay’s advice though. He’s been engaged in high-profile spats with Common, Diddy, Chris Brown, Ludacris, DMX, Pusha T, Kendrick Lamar, and since 2015 Joe Budden, Meek Mill, and Our 2015 Song Picks.\n\nOn 2017’s “Free Smoke” Drake referenced these bars and offered an explanation as to why he didn’t heed the warning in this track:\n\nI didn’t listen to Hov on that old song\nWhen he told me pay it no mind\nI get more satisfaction outta goin' at your head\nAnd seein' all of you die\nAnd I seen a lot of you die\n\nJay also utilises the near homophone “distract/diss track”.", "annotation_id": "43629", "url": "https://genius.com/43629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Fuck niggas! Bitches, too! All I got is this money—this’ll do", "vault_lines": ["Fuck niggas! Bitches, too!", "All I got is this money—this'll do"], "meaning": "Generally, rappers will say “fuck bitches, get money” because they only feel that their male friends are loyal. But Jay has been screwed over by both genders, so he is essentially saying “fuck people, get money”, which explains why he often feels lonely/depressed.\n\nAlso a possible reference to “Get Money” by Junior M.A.F.I.A. featuring Biggie, where the hook goes “fuck bitches, get money / fuck niggas, get money”", "annotation_id": "17812", "url": "https://genius.com/17812", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "But these bright lights turned me to a monster", "vault_lines": ["But these bright lights turned me to a monster"], "meaning": "Jay continues the light imagery in this verse, and once again claims that the bright lights in the big city are responsible for changing his personality. Looks like Jay might be borrowing Kanye’s technique of using lights as symbols for fame, e.g. “Flashing Lights,” “All of the Lights,” “Street Lights.”\n\nThis line is likely also a reference to Kanye’s song “Monster” from the same year, that also featured Hov talking about turning into a monster.", "annotation_id": "17811", "url": "https://genius.com/17811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Con Edison flow, I’m connected to a higher power Bright lights’ll make your whole city light up A trillion-watt light bulb when I’m in the night club", "vault_lines": ["Con Edison flow, I'm connected to a higher power", "Bright lights'll make your whole city light up", "A trillion-watt light bulb when I'm in the night club"], "meaning": "Consolidated Edison is a giant energy company; Jay’s source of energy is much greater than your average rapper, and he uses this power to metaphorically “light up” every city and night club he performs at.\n\n\n\nThomas Edison, along with Hiram Maxin and Joesph Swan, perfected the light bulb, an invention by Humphrey Davy.\n\nLight bulbs are typically fixed to the ceiling, hence “connected to a higher power”. A light bulb usually uses 60 watts of power, so Jay’s exaggeration shows just how powerful and bright he is.", "annotation_id": "17806", "url": "https://genius.com/17806", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "And to these niggas I’m like Windows 7 You let them tell it, they swear that they invented you", "vault_lines": ["And to these niggas I'm like Windows 7", "You let them tell it, they swear that they invented you"], "meaning": "The theme of the marketing campaign for Microsoft’s newest operating system, “Windows 7”, is that it was created by the customers. The commercials feature customers saying, “Windows 7 was my idea.”\n\n\n\nJay-Z claims that people have used some revisionist history in his career as well, claiming credit for his success. This is likely aimed at former associates such as Jaz-O, Dehaven Damon Dash, who both played a major role in Jay’s career and have since fallen out.\n\nJay addressed this earlier on his career on “Lost One” saying:\n\nI heard motherfuckers saying they made Hov\nmade Hov say, “Ok so, make another Hov.”", "annotation_id": "17730", "url": "https://genius.com/17730", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "In disguise, in the form of a favor The Barzini meeting, watch for the traitors, uhh", "vault_lines": ["In disguise, in the form of a favor", "The Barzini meeting, watch for the traitors, uhh"], "meaning": "In the classic film The Godfather, the eponymous character warns his son Michael that whoever tries to set up a meeting with a character called Don Barzini has turned against Michael and that the meeting will be designed to kill him.\n\n\n\nMichael learns that Salvatore Tessio is the one who betrayed him when he comes up to Michael, claiming that he has set up the meeting and promising to sit in on it to ensure his safety. So what Jay is trying to pass on to Drake is that oftentimes beef doesn’t arise from a deep hatred or a violent diss track, but rather from a close friend that turns on you and begins misleading you.", "annotation_id": "17725", "url": "https://genius.com/17725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "I don’t do too much bloggin’", "vault_lines": ["I don't do too much bloggin'", "I just run the town, I don't do too much joggin'"], "meaning": "An anomaly in this social-media-driven-age, Jay doesn’t write his thoughts on the internet. He’s been notoriously quiet about his private life in the media for his entire career. The rare exceptions to this are the [very] rare tweets on his Twitter page.", "annotation_id": "17722", "url": "https://genius.com/17722", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "I just landed in that G450 Caught the Mayweather fight ’cause the satellite was crispy, uhh", "vault_lines": ["I just landed in that G450", "Caught the Mayweather fight 'cause the satellite was crispy, uhh"], "meaning": "While we at RG dislike turning what seems to be a straightforward line about watching boxing matches from the comfort of your private jet into an elaborate diss, it actually isn’t unreasonable to see this line and the follow bars as jabs at Jay’s frenemy 50 Cent.\n\nFirst off, Jay literally says “50” before going on to mention Floyd Mayweather, a close friend of 50’s. Next, the main point of this verse is Jay warning Drake to avoid “silly rap feuds”, which makes sense considering he and Fif have been scuffling over trivial shit for years. Finally, 50 called Jay out the last time he collaborated with Drake for working with other artists but not helping out those signed to his label. So what better way to get back at him than by taking him down during another collaboration with Drake?", "annotation_id": "17720", "url": "https://genius.com/17720", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "Oww, hoes turn they heads like owls I’m the man of the hour Triple entendre, don’t even ask me how", "vault_lines": ["Oww, hoes turn they heads like owls", "I'm the man of the hour", "Triple entendre, don't even ask me how"], "meaning": "The first bar has four possible meanings that Jay may have intended to count towards his “triple entendre”.\n\n\n“Oww” is a way to call attention to pain. Girls turn their heads so quickly as Jay walks past they suffer pain.\nOwls can turn their heads 270 degrees, so Jay implies women watch him wherever he goes.\nDrake’s OVO Sound crew uses an owl as a logo.\n\nJay may be setting up an extended metaphor based around being in the sky, or higher than his competition. This begins with “owl” and ends with the line “caught the Mayweather fight cause the satellite was crispy”.\n\n\n\n“Man of the hour” merely means Jay is the centre of attention, hence the following bars, “trillion watt lightbulb when I’m in the nightclub”.\n\nIt’s not certain that these interpretations are enough to be a triple entendre.  An English professor once went through some of Jay’s most famous lines that are considered multi-entendres, and came to the conclusion that only about 10/50 of Hov’s best “entendres” actually qualified as double-entendres.\n\nTrue double entendres rely upon more than just dual references; they require two parallel levels of meaning, one explicit and one coded. Traditionally, that coded meaning was usually of a sexual nature. Jay-Z uses a lot of puns—where the humor derives from a similar sounding words and/or that word’s multiple meanings. Jay-Z also uses a lot of similes, and metaphors. Excellent lines like “I was moving birds like an Oriole fitted” and “I’m on the block like I’m 8 feet tall” do convey the sense of two distinct images, but as mentioned before, the meaning is clear, not coded.", "annotation_id": "17718", "url": "https://genius.com/17718", "state": "accepted", "votes": 260, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "The shit feel like when Fredro Starr Was in Sunset Park stuntin’ hard in his yellow goose - yeah!", "vault_lines": ["The shit feel like when Fredro Starr", "Was in Sunset Park stuntin' hard in his yellow goose - yeah!"], "meaning": "Fellow rapper Fredro Starr of Onyx starred in the film Sunset Park (1996), where in some scenes he did some “stunting” (showing off) in a yellow jacket.\n\n\n\n“Goose” refers to a jacket filled with goose down.\n\n\n\nFredro responded and accepted the shoutout via Twitter. Later, in 2013, he sampled this piece in the track “The Truth.”\n\nhttp://twitter.com/Fredro_Starr/status/14879073247", "annotation_id": "17712", "url": "https://genius.com/17712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "line": "I could use a glass of cold Spades", "vault_lines": ["I could use a glass of cold Spades"], "meaning": "Armand de Brignac champagne is usually called “Ace of Spades” because of the spade featured on the bottle. If Drake really wants a glass he can just hit up the other dude on the track, since Jay owns the company.", "annotation_id": "17708", "url": "https://genius.com/17708", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Light Up", "status": "done", "targets": 38, "clicked": 38, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-light-up-lyrics", "genius_title": "Light Up"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "If you my bitch, stop postin’ other rappers on the ’gram ‘Cause I don’t appreciate you dick-ridin’ me and them", "vault_lines": ["If you my bitch, stop postin' other rappers on the 'gram", "'Cause I don't appreciate you dick-ridin' me and them"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "39373032", "url": "https://genius.com/39373032", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "You know I had to do one, I gotta get one, yuh Life’s crazy, it’s hard for me to trust It’s hard for me to trust ’Cause even if they not down, they act down ’cause they know what’s up", "vault_lines": ["You know I had to do one, I gotta get one, yuh", "Life's crazy, it's hard for me to trust", "It's hard for me to trust", "'Cause even if they not down, they act down 'cause they know what's up"], "meaning": "This is Drake voicing his caution about collaborations. Every rapper wants the Drake stimulus package because it guarantees exposure, but that doesn’t mean they genuinely rock with him on a personal level.\n\nHowever, Drake still offers the benefit of the doubt, he’ll do the track (“I had to do one”), even if he’s not fully sure of the intentions behind the relationship. This leak likely came from one of Drake and Kodak’s first sessions in the studio together, and the lyrics reflects the vibe Drake was on at the time.", "annotation_id": "37074277", "url": "https://genius.com/37074277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "All my brothers eyes blood red like they on somethin’", "vault_lines": ["All my brothers eyes blood red like they on somethin'"], "meaning": "Somebody’s eyes being red is often linking to “being on something”/getting high, due to it lowering blood pressure and dilating blood vessels and capillaries.\n\nSeeing red is also a metaphor for suddenly becoming very angry.", "annotation_id": "37055631", "url": "https://genius.com/37055631", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "I’m from that Pompano, yeah, Ugly Corner, 1800 Block Golden Acres Projects baby, man, I’m a sunshine boy", "vault_lines": ["I'm from that Pompano, yeah, Ugly Corner, 1800 Block", "Golden Acres Projects baby, man, I'm a sunshine boy"], "meaning": "The 1800 block is on Martin Luther King Jr Boulevard in the Golden Acres neighborhood of Pompano Beach, Florida. This is the birthplace and childhood neighborhood of Kodak Black.\n\n\n\n“Ugly Corner” is a nickname for that specific neighborhood intersection in Pompano Beach, Florida. The name likely refers to the rough and dangerous nature of that area.", "annotation_id": "37045684", "url": "https://genius.com/37045684", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "And the car black matted on some woke shit", "vault_lines": ["And the car black matted on some woke shit"], "meaning": "The “woke” movement refers to an awareness and education about social and political issues. It originally emerged as a call within Black communities for consciousness about racism and social injustice, urging people to stay “woke” or aware. Over time, this term became strongly associated with hip-hop artists who often used their lyrics to highlight these issues.\n\n\n\nDrake plays on the car’s stylish matte black look as if having this finish is a seal of being socially aware and conscious, owing to the fact that the “woke” movement originated within the Black communities.\n\n“Black matted” can also be interpreted as a homonym for the “Black Lives Matter” movement.", "annotation_id": "37003141", "url": "https://genius.com/37003141", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "Tag a nigga toe on some eenie-minie-mo shit", "vault_lines": ["Tag a nigga toe on some eenie-minie-mo shit"], "meaning": "In forensic context, “toe tagging” is the practice of placing an identification tag on the toe of a deceased person for identification purposes.\n\n\n\nHowever, here it plays off the popular children’s counting rhyme “Eeny, meeny, miny, moe” which goes:\n\nEeny, meeny, miny, moe, catch a tiger by the toe; if it hollers, let it go\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PchSYAbm3sg", "annotation_id": "37003076", "url": "https://genius.com/37003076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "And my girl, Hawi, she was Ethiopian", "vault_lines": ["And my girl, Hawi, she was Ethiopian"], "meaning": "The name Hawi in Ethiopian means hope, and it is a fairly common Ethiopian female name.\n\nDrake mentions having had a girlfriend named Hawi when he was still on the come up, who he describes as Ethiopian. He happens to have referred to an Ethiopian goddess in PARTYNEXTDOOR’s 2013 track “Over Here”:\n\nGo to Jungle, pick up my Ethiopian goddess\n\nThere has been speculation that this Ethiopian girl named Hawi is a real person he reminisces about from his past. Drake tweeted a birthday wish to a user named @FreeDaRealest in 2013, believed by some fans to be Hawi:\n\nhttps://x.com/Drake/status/341787978877775873?lang=en\n\nThere happens to be an Instagram account with the same username of the account he tweeted. The girl from the said account is named Hawi and has pictures circulating online that features her with Drake.\n\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/29c2c9210716d0d76e5dd006b8cd9d5bda1ef9d1ddd22a6f6c577e1c26c244ad%2Fahm59p9wguw", "annotation_id": "37002497", "url": "https://genius.com/37002497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "Like Johnny punch me in the mouth, he got my teeth bust", "vault_lines": ["Like Johnny punch me in the mouth, he got my teeth bust"], "meaning": "This refers to Johnny Dang, a well-known celebrity jeweler famous for custom diamond-encrusted dental grills. He is the one who installed or “busted down” Kodak’s teeth with diamonds.", "annotation_id": "37002342", "url": "https://genius.com/37002342", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "Then the skies parted and I saw a openin’ Tried doin’ drugs, it ain’t work, I was focusin’", "vault_lines": ["Then the skies parted and I saw a openin'", "Tried doin' drugs, it ain't work, I was focusin'"], "meaning": "“Doing drugs” can be interpreted in multiple ways. On one level, it could mean that Drake tried using drugs to cope with this stressful bout in his life, but found that it didn’t help him, so he chose to stay focused instead, or he might be even saying that stimulants brought him clarity and focus rather than the typical expected “high” people seek to escape reality. Alternatively, it might also allude to trying his hand at dealing drugs as a way/opening to improve his financial situation, but realizing it wasn’t the right path for him.\n\nThis latter interpretation aligns with his lyrics from “Away From Home,” where he reflects on a time he tried to get into the drug-dealing business but wasn’t built for it:\n\nMy cousin Ryan fronted me a pound and I couldn’t even flip it, had to bring it back around.", "annotation_id": "37000141", "url": "https://genius.com/37000141", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "We ain’t strippin’ when we grippin’ on a pole, bitch", "vault_lines": ["We ain't strippin' when we grippin' on a pole, bitch"], "meaning": "“Pole” is slang for a gun, contrasting with the common association of a pole with stripping or pole dancing.", "annotation_id": "36999957", "url": "https://genius.com/36999957", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "Feed her ass McDonald’s on some E-I-E-I-O shit", "vault_lines": ["Feed her ass McDonald's on some E-I-E-I-O shit"], "meaning": "McDonald’s is a popular fast food chain that is synonymous with accessible and cheap food options. Drake is basically saying he won’t spend extravagantly on his female companions, just the bare minimum, contrasting with what they might expect from him.\n\n“E-I-E-I-O” is a nod to the popular nursery rhyme “Old MacDonald Had a Farm.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_6HzoUcx3eo", "annotation_id": "36999648", "url": "https://genius.com/36999648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "You know how we rockin’ in this bitch, it’s like Stallone comin’", "vault_lines": ["You know how we rockin' in this bitch, it's like Stallone comin'"], "meaning": "A nod to action movie star Sylvester Stallone, who is best known for creating and starring in the Rocky film franchise.", "annotation_id": "36999591", "url": "https://genius.com/36999591", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "Thinkin’ far back, I was on some broke shit Whole time all that money sittin’ in my notes, bitch", "vault_lines": ["Thinkin' far back, I was on some broke shit", "Whole time all that money sittin' in my notes, bitch"], "meaning": "Drake comes from humble beginnings, growing up on Weston Road (a working class area in western Toronto, Canada) with his mom who was an English teacher.\n\n\n\nWeston Road\n\nDrake reflects back on his upbringing of being “broke”, not realizing that the lyrics that he would write would eventually turn into billions of dollars worth of music.\n\nEven lyrics Drake didn’t release were worth money. In 2019, Drake’s childhood notebooks were found in the garbage outside his grandpa’s furniture store before being auctioned off for $35,000.\n\n\n\nDrake’s Notebook\n\nAs an adult in the earlier part of his career, Drake wrote his lyrics in his BlackBerry. He lost one of his BlackBerrys in the ocean in Mexico. In his song “Say What’s Real” from his 2009 mixtape So Far Gone, Drake recognizes the value of his notes/lyrics, saying:\n\nLost some of my hottest verses down in Cabo\nSo if you find a Blackberry with the side scroll\nSell that mothafucker to any rapper that I know", "annotation_id": "36994837", "url": "https://genius.com/36994837", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "She wanna get married, all I did was get her a little lamb", "vault_lines": ["She wanna get married, all I did was get her a little lamb"], "meaning": "This double entendre references gifting a Lamborghini (shortened to “Lamb”) to a woman as opposed to getting married/gifting a ring.\n\nKodak dodges a woman’s desire for marriage (a large commitment) with a “little lamb” (an expensive gift to the average person, but trivial to someone like Kodak, who is reportedly worth $5 million).\n\nThe line can also be read as:\n\nShe wanna get [Mary], all I did was get her a [Little Lamb]\n\nA reference to the nursery rhyme “Mary Had a Little Lamb”.", "annotation_id": "36994641", "url": "https://genius.com/36994641", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "I never got to graduate or do a homecomin’", "vault_lines": ["I never got to graduate or do a homecomin'"], "meaning": "Drake dropped out of high school at age 15 years old to star as Jimmy Brooks in Degrassi: The Next Generation, which allowed him to support his family while pursuing his acting and music career. While working as an actor on Degrassi, he released his first mixtape, Room For Improvement.\n\nHe would eventually graduate high school ten years later in 2012 at 25 years old, but still missed out on high school milestone events like homecoming.", "annotation_id": "36994545", "url": "https://genius.com/36994545", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "line": "Never dreamt of seven-hundred million when I wrote shit", "vault_lines": ["Never dreamt of seven-hundrеd million when I wrote shit"], "meaning": "Drake has been in the music industry for over 15 years and during the time has earned a significant amount of wealth which he never expected to gain at the beginning of his career.\n\nDrake started writing rap music whilst he was an actor for Degrassi in 2001. He self-published his first mixtape Room for Improvement in 2006 but it wouldn’t be until the release of his third mixtape So Far Gone in 2009 that Drake would see an international level of fame, even landing him a signing to Lil Wayne’s Young Money Entertainment label. After that Drake has gone on to drop many hit records and albums, with him having twelve Billboard Hot 100 numbers 1s and thirteen Billboard 200 numbers 1s as of November 2023.\n\nDrake’s claims in this lyric he has made $700 million off music which is likely true as Drake reportedly made $500 million off one record deal alone in 2022. Drake has seemingly confirmed this record deal in his song “Major Distribution” stating:\n\nFive-hundred million, just for Aubrey", "annotation_id": "29498076", "url": "https://genius.com/29498076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skies Parted", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-skies-parted-lyrics", "genius_title": "Skies Parted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Hype Williams, Big Pimpin’, yeah Just like the old times", "vault_lines": ["Hype Williams, Big Pimpin', yeah", "Just like the old times"], "meaning": "Drake reminisces with the old times here, citing two infamous parts of hip-hop history.\n\nHe references legendary music video director Hype Williams, who directed many of Busta Rhymes and Missy Elliott’s classic music videos, and Jay-Z’s classic 1999 single, “Big Pimpin'” featuring UGK.", "annotation_id": "28657591", "url": "https://genius.com/28657591", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Same niggas from the old days Lotta sides on the same side", "vault_lines": ["Same niggas from the old days", "Lotta sides on the same side"], "meaning": "Drake’s no new friends policy has yet to hurt his stature in the rap game. With his Young Money record deal, he’s involved with both Lil Wayne and Nicki Minaj, from New Orleans and Queens respectively. That relationship has existed since early 2009.\n\nHis OVO Sound family is made up almost exclusively of Toronto-based artists, although they are all based in different parts of the city.", "annotation_id": "9088353", "url": "https://genius.com/9088353", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Yeah, yeah Yeah, yeah Jheeze, yeah Right, look, look", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Jheeze!” is London slang, started by rapper Giggs. It means to be happy about something. Drake used it earlier in the album version of “Controlla”:\n\nThey don’t wanna see me\nSmiling back when they pree\nJheeze!", "annotation_id": "9076165", "url": "https://genius.com/9076165", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "And the whole city rate me And I’m back inside The Matrix", "vault_lines": ["And the whole city rate me", "And I'm back inside The Matrix"], "meaning": "Much of Views is focused on the unity and strength of Toronto, and during his Zane Lowe interview, Drake constantly repeated his devotion to the city he grew up in.\n\n“Rate” is also slang for liking something, hence you “rate” it. Drake scored the key to the city of Toronto in 2016, so clearly his city is on his side.\n\nDrake mentions The Matrix on “6 Man” too:\n\nI am in the Matrix and I just took the blue pill", "annotation_id": "9075287", "url": "https://genius.com/9075287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Cosign, cosign", "vault_lines": ["I'm cashin' out, fuck a cosign"], "meaning": "While Drake’s cosigned the likes of Makonnen (see “Tuesday”), Migos (see “Versace (Remix)”), and Fetty Wap (see “My Way (Remix)”), some artists haven’t appreciated Drizzy’s midas touch. When discussing Kodak Black on Twitter, Earl Sweatshirt had this to say:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/earlxsweat/status/658313846231207936?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\nSee a full list of Drake’s cosigns here.", "annotation_id": "9072972", "url": "https://genius.com/9072972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "I stand out, I don’t blend in When I say that I meant that I don’t want to talk to you has-beens", "vault_lines": ["I stand out, I don't blend in", "When I say that I meant that", "I don't want to talk to you has-beens"], "meaning": "Possibly a diss to Kanye West’s new artist Desiigner and his huge hit  “Panda.”\n\nEven GQ has claimed the New York artist sounds like Future, and the original artist is not afraid to air him out for it.\n\nJermaine Dupri posted some tweets claiming similarities exist in between the beats of Futures huge hit “Fuck Up Some Commas” and “Panda”:\nhttps://twitter.com/jermainedupri/status/723651969487917056\nhttps://twitter.com/jermainedupri/status/723662095087243268\n\nWith the current run he’s on, Future is far from being a has-been, and this may be a further shot at Desiigner, claiming his time has already come and gone, and that he is merely a one hit wonder.", "annotation_id": "9072702", "url": "https://genius.com/9072702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "I don’t want features from nan them I don’t want features or nothin’", "vault_lines": ["I don't want features from nan them", "I don't want features or nothin'"], "meaning": "Although Future has provided countless guest appearances over his career, the artists he chooses to collaborate with on his own projects are carefully chosen and usually repeat customers. His incredible 2015 album DS2 featured just one other artist, Drake. The two even made a full project together, titled What a Time to Be Alive.\n\nThe two will even hit the road together in the Summer of 2016.", "annotation_id": "9072530", "url": "https://genius.com/9072530", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Gonna peel off like a bandit", "vault_lines": ["Gonna peel off like a bandit"], "meaning": "“Peel off” means “to leave or depart in a hurry.”\nWhen a bandit, or criminal, creates a crime scene, they tend to leave quickly so they can avoid getting caught by the police.\n\nHis earlier allusion to his panoramic roofed vehicle paints the picture of an incredibly rich trap lord speeding off after inspecting his trap house.", "annotation_id": "9072500", "url": "https://genius.com/9072500", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "I’m cashin’ out, fuck a cosign", "vault_lines": ["I'm cashin' out, fuck a cosign"], "meaning": "Future doesn’t need a Grammy as confirmation for the popularity of his music. With all the big projects he releases and all the hits he is featured on, he earns millions.\n\n\n\nWhy would you care about awards if you can fuck up some commas, blow a bag everyday, and have rich sex?\n\nHis verse keeps in theme with Drake’s “keep the family close” direction of Views. Future isn’t concerned with high profile cosigns, or features from major artists. To “cash out” is to take your winnings and leave, although this seems unlikely in his case, with the amount of product he allegedly has lined up.", "annotation_id": "9072276", "url": "https://genius.com/9072276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Heard you never claimed the hood Heard the hood claimed you That can’t sit well Oh well, ship sailed Still mine, all mine", "vault_lines": ["Heard you never claimed the hood", "Heard the hood claimed you", "That can't sit well", "Oh well, ship sailed", "Still mine, all mine"], "meaning": "Views is ostensibly about Toronto, and although Drake was never a gang member as a youth, he did engage in illegal activities, and came up in a rough part of town.\n\nIf the “hood claims you,” it usually means death or destruction in some manner. Losing your life or career to the influence and actions of the place you grew up in. Drake was clear on “Still Here” that he’d risen above the harshness:\n\nHow did I finesse all this shit from Jane and Weston\n\nThe expression “the ship has sailed” means your chance for fame and fortune has passed.", "annotation_id": "9071896", "url": "https://genius.com/9071896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Get pissed off, start airin’ it Get a head start, ain’t friendly", "vault_lines": ["Get pissed off, start airin' it", "Get a head start, ain't friendly"], "meaning": "No Future beef has been more publicized than that between he and singer Ciara, the mother of his youngest son. Along with the tabloids, Future has been airing out the dirty laundry about their relationship on social media, chiefly concerning the rights of his paternity.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/1future/status/684085537339224065\n\nhttps://twitter.com/1future/status/684086685567684608\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFuture has also expressed his indifference to the singer’s fiancé, Seattle Seahawks quarterback Russell Wilson, who has been taking somewhat of a fatherly role in life of Future’s son.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/K6MufKZ6h4s?t=12m49s", "annotation_id": "9071602", "url": "https://genius.com/9071602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "You can’t even get on my guest list They want me to go to the Met Gala", "vault_lines": ["You can't even get on my guest list", "They want me to go to the Met Gala"], "meaning": "The Met Gala is a couture fashion event at the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York City, attended by many elite celebrities like Beyoncé and Kanye West. While Future attended the Met Gala again this year, he’d much rather spend his time on Percs and lean.\n\nIn this snapchat Futures shows himself chilling with The Weeknd at the after party.\n\n\n\nFuture wore an Ermenegildo Zegna tuxedo.", "annotation_id": "9071534", "url": "https://genius.com/9071534", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "I don’t know no one That could tell me what to do", "vault_lines": ["I don't know no one", "That could tell me what to do"], "meaning": "During Drake’s interview with Zane Lowe, he said:\n\nI just woke up one day and was like yo this is my time. This is my ting I run this you know. Nobody can really talk to me, I’m good at what I do.\n\nThis sentiment was echoed in “Views” when he raps:\n\nI get a blank page when I try to draw a comparison\n\nThe message is Drake is at the top of his game, and calling Views his highest level of vocal performance highlights the confidence he has in his own ability.\n\nConfident enough even to remove both Jay Z and Kanye West from his “Pop Style.”\n\n\n\nHis flow morphs yet again into something resembling UK rapper C Biz, this time his track “Money March.”", "annotation_id": "9071498", "url": "https://genius.com/9071498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "OVO, we a gold mine But I’m goin’ gold, no time Doing plat, plat only", "vault_lines": ["OVO, we a gold mine", "But I'm goin' gold, no time", "Doing plat, plat only"], "meaning": "OVO’s signature logo is gold and Drake’s Air Jordan Collaboration J’s feature gold aspects.\n\nHis last mixtape to only go Gold was So Far Gone in 2009, every other release since has gone Platinum. If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late hit platinum in March 2015, a month after release. As Drake predicted, Views went platinum within the first week.\n\n\n\nIt may be another shot at Meek Mill. On “Charged Up” he rapped:\n\nI see you niggas having trouble going gold\n\nImplying Meek was struggling for gold certification while Drake was steadily achieving platinum.\n\nIn it’s first week of sales, Views sold 851,000 traditional copies, with streaming bumping that number up to 1 million in the US and 1.2 million worldwide.\n\nAlso: All of Drake’s albums have been certified platinum, as have many of his singles.", "annotation_id": "9071150", "url": "https://genius.com/9071150", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Celebratin’ everyday ’Cause I’m really really fresh out the coke house Countin’ up every single day ’Bout to bring a whole ’nother whip out", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I'm really really fresh out the coke house", "Countin' up every single day", "'Bout to bring a whole 'nother whip out"], "meaning": "Future has made it in the rap industry, progressing from the underbelly of Atlanta to rap superstardom. “Coke house” is another term for trap house.\n\nNow his daily activities include celebrating, counting money, and driving a different car from the day before. He boasted about this on “Chosen One”:\n\nAnd I can have ten cars and I wouldn’t drive one", "annotation_id": "9070966", "url": "https://genius.com/9070966", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Swervin’ out the Panoramic I’m hangin’ out, they can’t stand me", "vault_lines": ["Swervin' out the Panoramic", "I'm hangin' out, they can't stand me", "I'm showin' out, they can't stand me", "I'm swervin' off, they can't stand me", "I'm rr off, can't stand me"], "meaning": "Future is in a luxury vehicle featuring a Panoramic roof (with a view)—Future’s ‘haters’ are envious of him and can’t stand his new found success.\n\nHe said something similar on “Freeband Taliban”:\n\nI’m super Future, I’m jammin the roof\nAnd panoramic the coupe", "annotation_id": "9070729", "url": "https://genius.com/9070729", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "They gon’ think I won a Grammy They gon’ think I won a Grammy", "vault_lines": ["They gon' think I won a Grammy"], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/702563965491085312\n\nFuture’s last projects have been met with a positive reception from critics, going on a streak with Monster, Beast Mode, 56 Nights, and DS2, and recently the joint project with Drake, What A Time To Be Alive.\n\nOn February 10th 2019, Future won his first Grammy for his feature on King’s Dead.", "annotation_id": "9070602", "url": "https://genius.com/9070602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "I wear the chain like a bowtie I wear the ring like a fo’-five Keep a fo’-five for the po’ guys Black tints, low profile", "vault_lines": ["I wear the chain like a bowtie", "I wear the ring like a fo'-five", "Keep a fo'-five for the po' guys", "Black tints, low profile"], "meaning": "Future wears his gold and diamond encrusted chains around his neck like a bowtie (an item he wore at the Met Gala), and keeps his “Big Rings” on like he’s strapped up everyday with the .45 caliber gun for the “po po” or police, just in case.\n\nOn “March Madness,” he confirmed this dress code:\n\nDirty muddy in a cup\n45 by my gut\n\nFuture is famous for wearing his “black tints,” or his shades, just about everywhere he goes. Even at night and indoors, and tinted windows which offer a sense of security and anonymity,\nFuture stated on ‘Everything Ours\":\n\nYou see this ice on me and all my windows tinted", "annotation_id": "9070565", "url": "https://genius.com/9070565", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "And I said that we would make it Aim squad with some traitors Knew my niggas from the basement This ain’t no met-along-the-way shit We done really put some days in", "vault_lines": ["Same niggas from the old days", "And I said that we would make it", "Aim squad with some traitors", "Knew my niggas from the basement", "This ain't no met-along-the-way shit", "We done really put some days in"], "meaning": "Drake used to make music out of a basement with his friend and producer Noah “40” Shebib.  Drizzy stays down with the people that have been true to him since day one, since they have also been there for him during bad times. As he says on “Look What You’ve Done”:\n\n“It’s like ’09 in your basement”\n\nIn his Zane Lowe interview, at about the 43 minute mark, Drake talks of the friendships he’s seen come and go in the industry:\n\nThese fly-by-night people that you just come across. They’re all not to be trusted, I don’t trust none of them… This is war man, we’re playing for keeps bro… Yeah I’ve seen people I’ve had the utmost respect for do foul shit. If they could have their way I’d be on the street corner with all the guys in this room and my family.\n\nDrake goes on to say he’s “ready” for anything, and the traitors will not penetrate his squad.", "annotation_id": "9070169", "url": "https://genius.com/9070169", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Boys better back off me Hall of fame, hall of fame Like I’m shirt off, like I’m shirt off Like I’m shirt off shorty Whole city goin’ crazy Whole city goin’ crazy Top five, no debatin’ Top five, top five, top five", "vault_lines": ["Boys better back off me", "Hall of fame, hall of fame", "Like I'm shirt off, like I'm shirt off", "Like I'm shirt off shorty", "Whole city goin' crazy", "Top five, no debatin'", "Top five, top five, top five"], "meaning": "Rapper Top5 is a Canadian artist that Drake has previously shouted out on his Instagram.  He has a track titled “Hall of Fame,” in which he starts:\n\nIm Top 5\nShirt off..\n\nHe also has a track entitled “Shirt Off Shawty.” This is another example of Drake paying homage to artists that he cosigns.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/wfv7ZnDQF1/\n\n“Top 5” is a common barbershop debate, questioning who the top 5 rappers of all time are. After his comparison to Jay Z earlier in the verse, and particularly during “Summer Sixteen” it seems as though Drake places himself firmly in the top 5.\n\nIt was announced in May 2016 that Drake was the 5th wealthiest hip-hop artist of the year, with a net worth of $60 million. Drake once said that being in Forbes top 5 was “pretty much my objective every year.”\n\nAlso, as of June 2016, Drake has had five songs in the Top 5 on the Billboard Hot 100.", "annotation_id": "9070147", "url": "https://genius.com/9070147", "state": "accepted", "votes": 119, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Hey, why you so excited? You know what I’m sayin’? What happened? Did you win the Grammy? God damn You actin’ like you fuckin’ won a trophy and shit This nigga turnt the fuck up", "vault_lines": ["Hey, why you so excited? You know what I'm sayin'?", "Did you win the Grammy? God damn", "You actin' like you fuckin' won a trophy and shit", "This nigga turnt the fuck up"], "meaning": "At the time of this song’s release, Drake had won one Grammy. Take Care earned him a trophy for Best Rap Album in 2012.\n\n\n\nFuture, on the other hand, has yet to win a Grammy, or pick up a nomination. Considering he released one of the most critically acclaimed rap albums of 2015, one would assume that he’d get a nod.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/702563965491085312", "annotation_id": "9070146", "url": "https://genius.com/9070146", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "We sittin’ right on the courtside I know the players on both side", "vault_lines": ["We sittin' right on the courtside", "I know the players on both side"], "meaning": "Part of Future’s lavish lifestyle includes sitting court side at NBA games. He is most commonly found watching the Atlanta Hawks, but spreads himself around generously.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/mcten/status/670402159289192449\n\nFuture insisted in 2012 that he could’ve been in the NBA himself.", "annotation_id": "9070074", "url": "https://genius.com/9070074", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "I want a Percocet and a gallon That Actavis Hi-Tech it don’t matter", "vault_lines": ["I want a Percocet and a gallon", "That Actavis Hi-Tech it don't matter"], "meaning": "Future’s drugs of choice include Percocets (Oxycodone) and Codeine, both are opiates. One gallon of Codeine amounts to 8 pints of syrup.\n\nI’m drinkin' on that lean by the gallon — “Upper Echelon”\n\nActavis & Hi-Tech are two of the most popular, sought after brands of codeine cough syrup.\n\n\nWhere your ass was at, dog, when I was drinking Hi-Tech? — “Where Ya At”\n\nWe got purple Actavis, I thought it was a drought — “Thought It Was A Drought”", "annotation_id": "9070060", "url": "https://genius.com/9070060", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Tell me how you really feel Tell me how you really feel I would ask you what’s the deal But y’all don’t even got a deal", "vault_lines": ["Tell me how you really feel", "I would ask you what's the deal", "But y'all don't even got a deal"], "meaning": "Known to be sensitive, Drake is not afraid to talk feelings and emotions.  These lyrics closely resemble lines from his hit from Take Care, “The Motto,” in which he sarcastically invited the opinions of his haters:\n\nThat’s how you feel, man? That’s really how you feel?\n\nThis may be a shot at Mo-G, an unsigned Toronto rapper who has recently been involved in beef with Drake.\n\nHis flow may be a nod to UK rapper C Biz, and his track “The Game’s Mine,” in which he employs a similar cadence.", "annotation_id": "9070036", "url": "https://genius.com/9070036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "Most niggas with a deal Couldn’t make a greatest hits Y’all a whole lot of things But you still ain’t this", "vault_lines": ["But you still ain't this", "Most niggas with a deal", "Couldn't make a greatest hits", "Y'all a whole lot of things"], "meaning": "At the time of the release of So Far Gone, Drake’s third mixtape, the rapper was unsigned hype and a protégé of Young Money’s Lil Wayne.\n\nSo Far Gone was released through Drake’s website October’s Very Own on February 13, 2009. It was a catalyst for the relationship between major record labels and the distribution of mixtapes. The mixtape spawned hits such as “Best I Ever Had” and “Successful.”\n\nDropped the mixtape, that shit sounded like an album\n\nEven without a deal, Drake had released 3 high quality mixtapes, and he argues he could’ve created a greatest hits compilation that would rival any other entertainer based on his unsigned work.\n\nIt may also be a reference to Skepta’s 2007 record Greatest Hits, an artist Drake has publicly cosigned.", "annotation_id": "9070034", "url": "https://genius.com/9070034", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "line": "I’m noddin’ off on a Xanax", "vault_lines": ["I'm noddin' off on a Xanax"], "meaning": "Xanax induces sleepiness, causing the user to start nodding off or floating in and out of consciousness.\n\nXanax is frequently mentioned in Future’s music:\n\n\nMolly and them xans got me aggravated\n— “I Serve The Base”\n\nStill nodding off cause a nigga too faded\n— “56 Nights”\n\nDown five Xanax and I pray I wake up and forget — “Just Like Bruddas”\n\n\nAnd the appropriately titled “Xanny Family.”", "annotation_id": "9070024", "url": "https://genius.com/9070024", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Grammys", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 26, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-grammys-lyrics", "genius_title": "Grammys"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "I never sleep, though I sleep with a baddie I’m movin’ deep ‘cause that’s just how it’s happenin’ (Ooh)", "vault_lines": ["I never sleep, though I sleep with a baddie", "I'm movin' deep 'cause that's just how it's happenin' (Ooh)"], "meaning": "Alluring to Kylie Jenner, his now-former partner. Also a reference to “Never Sleep,” his collaboration with NAV and Lil Baby that was released over three months before this song.", "annotation_id": "35911976", "url": "https://genius.com/35911976", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "Quarter-million dollars on her dental", "vault_lines": ["Quarter-million dollars on her dental"], "meaning": "21 spent $250,000 on veneers for his girl, who presumably needed to take better care of her teeth.\n\nNotably, 21 upgraded his gold grills for a set of veneers in 2021, setting him back over $75,000.", "annotation_id": "27486071", "url": "https://genius.com/27486071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "Who said it’s cheaper to keep her? I’d rather release her Body and give it away like a feature You know the procedure", "vault_lines": ["Who said it's cheaper to keep her?", "I'd rather release her", "Body and give it away like a feature", "You know the procedure"], "meaning": "“Cheaper to Keep Her” was a song by legendary soul singer Johnnie Taylor. In the song, Taylor advises men who are fed up with their wives and consider leaving them not to do it because they would have to pay alimony and child support and subsequently go broke. Therefore it would be cheaper to stay with the woman they already have.\n\nDrake is using the reference to this song to flex his wealth, saying that he would rather “release” the woman and let her find someone else and doesn’t care about any potential monetary downsides of separating.\n\nAlthough Drake has never been married, he does have a son with a woman he is separated from, Sophie Brusseaux, and of course, has to pay child support.", "annotation_id": "27388628", "url": "https://genius.com/27388628", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "Flaca, Flacacita", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Flacka” and “flackacita” roughly translate to “thin girl” or “little thin girl” in Spanish.\n\nDrake likely could be referencing this unnamed woman that he knows who lives in Turks and Caicos, a place where he vacations often. Drake follows her and she’s also been seen repping Nocta, his partner brand with Nike.", "annotation_id": "27208665", "url": "https://genius.com/27208665", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "Rockin’ the braids, not the twists Full puff, he can’t comb", "vault_lines": ["Rockin' the braids, not the twists", "Full puff, he can't comb"], "meaning": "Braids and Twists are two different common Afro-textured hairstyles.\n\nTravis mentions his hairstyle preference and choosing braids over twists. He also mentions that his hair is so puffed, so it is unable to comb it, while referencing to the famous rapper Sean Combs, who is also known as Puff Diddy.", "annotation_id": "27208592", "url": "https://genius.com/27208592", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "And you know I got a sex symbol that eat my kids up", "vault_lines": ["I'm tryna call a sex symbol to eat my kids up", "And you know I got a sex symbol that eat my kids up"], "meaning": "Changing the lyrics from the Chorus where Drake says he’s “tryna call a sex symbol to eat my kids up,” Travis switches that to saying he already has one.\n\nAgain, referring to his partner Kylie Jenner as he did in his verse.", "annotation_id": "27090077", "url": "https://genius.com/27090077", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "Not actin’ like Christians in here But Christian Dior in they homes, the bigger we get Gotta thank God for this, I got a lot that I give I be handin’ out gifts like the Christmas on twenty-fifth", "vault_lines": ["I like the money, for sure", "Not actin' like Christians in here", "But Christian Dior in they homes, the bigger we get", "Gotta thank God for this, I got a lot that I give", "I be handin' out gifts like the Christmas on twenty-fifth"], "meaning": "Some great religious wordplay from Trav here! Firstly, there’s wordplay in the previous line with “Puff” and “Comb”, referring to Sean “Diddy” Combs, aka Puff Daddy, who has a son called Christian, playing into the religious wordplay. Travis then plays on the homonym “Christian” again and says he’s not acting like a devoted Christian, who for example wouldn’t take illegal drugs or substances, which Travis is definitely guilty of. Travis then says the bigger he gets the more money is available to him, and therefore more of the luxury fashion brand Christian Dior he can afford, which is known to be expensive.\n\nTrav then suggests he is Christian by thanking god, and then ties in all the Christianity references by referencing Christmas, a Christian holiday that celebrates the birth of Jesus Christ.", "annotation_id": "27072726", "url": "https://genius.com/27072726", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "I’m reppin’ 4L and my twin, he so treacherous, introduce y’all to the leader", "vault_lines": ["I'm reppin' 4L and my twin, he so treacherous, introduce y'all to the leader"], "meaning": "A reference to another song on the album, “Treacherous Twins,” and to 21 Savage’s 4L Gang, which he has repped since the very start. When Drake says “introduce y'all to the leader,” he is talking about 21, whose verse starts after this very line.", "annotation_id": "27064671", "url": "https://genius.com/27064671", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "[Verse 2: 21 Savage]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the second half of this verse, 21 uses the same flow as the second half of his verse on DaBaby’s “STICKED UP”.", "annotation_id": "27061919", "url": "https://genius.com/27061919", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "Braided up at Matsuhisa", "vault_lines": ["Braided up at Matsuhisa"], "meaning": "Matsuhisa is a luxury Japanese restaurant chain founded by the famous Nobu Matsuhisa with 11 locations around the world.\n\n\nPictured: Drake having his hair braided at Matsuhisa in Beverly Hills, California.", "annotation_id": "27058831", "url": "https://genius.com/27058831", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "They say more money, more problems (Well) Bring on the problems (Well) Bring on the problems (Well) Bring on the motherfuckin’ problems They say more money, more problems Bring on the problems Bring on the problems Bring on the motherfuckin’ problems, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Bring on the motherfuckin'—", "Bring on the rings (Yeah)", "Breakin', breakin' the records, more money, more problems", "They say more money, more problems (Well)", "Bring on the problems (Well)", "Bring on the motherfuckin' problems", "Bring on the motherfuckin' problems, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to The Notorious B.I.G.’s March 1997 song, “Mo' Money, Mo' Problems” which emphasizes the hard responsibilities as well as how people will try to make you fail after your success while under the influence of money—a similar concept that Drake is trying to imply while demonstrating courage in his willingness to face them:\n\nI don’t know what they want from me\nIt’s like the more money we come across\nThe more problems we see\nI don’t know what they want from me\n\nhttps://youtu.be/gUhRKVIjJtw\n\nDrake has also made reference to the song on his November 2011 song, “The Ride”:\n\nThey say more money, more problems, my nigga, don’t believe it\nI mean, sure, there’s some bills and taxes I’m still evadin'", "annotation_id": "27057650", "url": "https://genius.com/27057650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "I’m off of the juice, never Canseco the mix", "vault_lines": ["I'm off of the juice, never Canseco the mix"], "meaning": "This line actually says “I’m off the juice, never Canseco the mix”. This line continues the major athlete theme he was using in the lines prior. Jose Canseco was a professional baseball player for the Oakland Athletics. He released a tell all book where he admitted to using steroids, also known as “juicing”. This line is a play on “juice” a common term in baseball referring to steroids.\n\nAdditionally this line is a double entendre. The second meaning being a metaphor for how Travis  makes his mixed alcoholic drinks. He is saying he’s off the juice and could never Canseco the mix meaning he won’t make the drink too strong.", "annotation_id": "27054990", "url": "https://genius.com/27054990", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "Chains on my neck, no Kente", "vault_lines": ["Chains on my neck, no Kente"], "meaning": "21 references Kunta Kinte, a fictional character based on the novel and adapted mini-series Roots. Kinte was a slave, and the chains 21 is referring to are the chains that were commonly used on the black people who were slaves. Instead, 21 rocks diamond-encrusted chains to honour his slave ancestors.", "annotation_id": "27053240", "url": "https://genius.com/27053240", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "And my Pateks, they came with a pole", "vault_lines": ["And my Pateks, they came with a pole"], "meaning": "21 owns several Patek Philippe watches. Some of the most expensive watches from the brand are displayed in glass cases where the watch is held up by a central column or “pole”.\n\n\n\nFurthermore, “pole” has a double meaning, also referring to a gun.\n\nAlso, Antoni Patek, the founder of Patek Phillipe, was a Polish immigrant, and people from Poland are referred to as “Poles.”\n\n\nPictured: 21 wearing a rose gold Patek Phillipe “Aquanaut.”", "annotation_id": "27053217", "url": "https://genius.com/27053217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "We the new Lucian Grainge", "vault_lines": ["We the new Lucian Grainge"], "meaning": "Lucian Grainge is considered “the most powerful man in music”. He is the chairman and CEO of Universal Music Group., the largest record label globally.", "annotation_id": "27052630", "url": "https://genius.com/27052630", "state": "pending", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "Just needed some time I settled my time, now they see the signs", "vault_lines": ["Just needed some time", "I settled my time, now they see the signs"], "meaning": "Following the tragedy that occurred at Travis Scott’s AstroWorld festival in November 2021, he had little to no presence across any of his social media pages and seized the promotion of his upcoming album UTOPIA. After months of taking time to recover, he has slowly been promoting his album again through billboards in California and Las Vegas.\n\n\n\nBillboards for his once discontinued CACTI Agave Seltzer and Cactus Farms weed strain have made billboard appearances as well.", "annotation_id": "27052549", "url": "https://genius.com/27052549", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "line": "Bring on the rings (Yeah) A MJ thing, I’m talkin’ a Brady thing", "vault_lines": ["Bring on the motherfuckin'—", "Bring on the rings (Yeah)", "A MJ thing, I'm talkin' a Brady thing", "Bring on the problems (Well)", "Bring on the motherfuckin' problems"], "meaning": "In North American sports, a championship ring is presented to members of the winning team each season. Michael Jordan and Tom Brady, recognized as two of the greatest of all time, have six and seven “rings,” respectively.\n\nTravis alludes to MJ and Brady’s success as a metaphor to describe the wealth and beauty of the woman he is with.", "annotation_id": "27052071", "url": "https://genius.com/27052071", "state": "pending", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pussy & Millions", "status": "done", "targets": 17, "clicked": 17, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-21-savage-pussy-and-millions-lyrics", "genius_title": "Pussy & Millions"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Yeah I know", "vault_lines": ["I know you're seein' someone that loves you", "I used to know you back when you was young"], "meaning": "On the intro, Drake’s “yeah, I get it, I get it” feels like he’s brushing her off. But now, at the end of the song, he’s realized everything he’s done understands the woman’s perspective.\n\nThis is a similar ending to “Come and See Me” with PARTYNEXTDOOR.", "annotation_id": "9096791", "url": "https://genius.com/9096791", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Damn, I’m not unrealistic with none of my women I tell them if they ain’t with it then let’s just forget it Relationships slowin’ me down, they slow down the vision Guess I’m not in a position to deal with commitment", "vault_lines": ["Damn, I'm not unrealistic with none of my women", "I tell them if they ain't with it then let's just forget it", "Relationships slowin' me down, they slow down the vision", "Guess I'm not in a position to deal with commitment"], "meaning": "Drake’s idea of a courtship seems vastly different to what this girl is used to. While it seems this girl wants a relationship, Drake explains he can’t afford to because it distracts him from his creative process. Word to Kanye.\n\nHe may be implying that he wants to see multiple women at once, but he could also be explaining he wants to remain monogamous without being tied down with something offical.\n\nDrake made a good point about relationships on “My Side”:\n\nWhy are we wasting our relationship on a relationship?", "annotation_id": "9084704", "url": "https://genius.com/9084704", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "I’m a walkin’ come-up, I’m a bank deposit", "vault_lines": ["I'm a walkin' come-up, I'm a bank deposit"], "meaning": "Drake feels like some women only see him as a means to an end. To these women, Drake is the opportunity that could come out of associating with him, he’s more of a cash machine than a human being.", "annotation_id": "9078863", "url": "https://genius.com/9078863", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Cliqued up with Jessica, Kim You know that I’m bound to see you And they both say that you’re over and done with me Aw yeah, yeah, yeah Aw yeah, yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["But you say you're over and done with me", "To say to you right now, yeah", "Cliqued up with Jessica, Kim", "You know that I'm bound to see you", "And they both say that you're over and done with me"], "meaning": "Jessica and Kim are friends of the girl Drake’s referring to. While we don’t know who they are (they could be made up names), he tells them how he feels about their friend, his former lover.\nUnfortunately for Drizzy they explain she’s over him.\n\nDrake’s had issues with friends of lovers before. In August 2015, TMZ reported that Serena Williams' friends had told her to be careful around him as he’s often seen with different women. Coincidently, the “she thinks I’m playing her but I’m really not” narrative is the one Drake’s alludes to throughout the song.", "annotation_id": "9078565", "url": "https://genius.com/9078565", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Syn had a baby and treated me different", "vault_lines": ["Syn had a baby and treated me different"], "meaning": "Texas Syn is a dancer/model acquaintance of Drake’s from Houston. Drake mentioned her in YG’s “Who Do You Love?”:\n\nI got a shorty name Texas Syn\nShe got a buddy named Young JB and now you know the deal\n\nIn an interview with Global Grind, Syn said she had her first child when she was 13 years old and also that Drake and her were more than just friends at the time. It could be possible that since Syn is a mother, she wanted a more serious relationship than what Drake was looking for.", "annotation_id": "9078296", "url": "https://genius.com/9078296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Certain people need to tell me they’re proud of me That mean a lot to me Not havin’ closure, it take a lot out of me This year for Christmas I just want apologies", "vault_lines": ["Certain people need to tell me they're proud of me", "That mean a lot to me", "Not havin' closure, it take a lot out of me", "This year for Christmas I just want apologies"], "meaning": "Closure could refer to romantic relationships or friendships. Either way, it frustrates Drake that he has some issues with people which haven’t been worked out yet.\n\n“Proud of me” may allude to Nicki Minaj, she features on Drake’s song “Make Me Proud.” In his Views interview with Zane Lowe, Drake said he doesn’t speak to her much since his beef with Meek Mill. This may be the closure he so desperately seeks.", "annotation_id": "9077817", "url": "https://genius.com/9077817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Master bedroom’s where we get it poppin’ Just ignore all the skeletons in my closet", "vault_lines": ["Master bedroom's where we get it poppin'", "Just ignore all the skeletons in my closet"], "meaning": "“Skeletons in your closet” means Drake has secrets that he may not be comfortable with sharing. These “secrets” are most likely other women he’s slept with.\n\nHis master bedroom makes up for it.\n\n\n\nThis is also another play on “poppin'” tying in with the previous couplet.", "annotation_id": "9073663", "url": "https://genius.com/9073663", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "West Palm girls are spoiled rotten Tiffany on you, you know you poppin’ I’ll kill somebody if they give you problems", "vault_lines": ["West Palm girls are spoiled rotten", "Tiffany on you, you know you poppin'", "I'll kill somebody if they give you problems"], "meaning": "“Spoil Rotten” means to do everything possible to satisfy someone’s desires.\n\nWest Palm Beach is a city in Florida known for its luxurious lifestyle and tourism. Due to the wealth of most people living there, it may be assumed that people are spoiled with fancy items in their lives such as Tiffany & Co. jewelry.\n\n“Poppin'” plays on “kill somebody\"—unlikely, but apparently Drake is willing to spray a few bullets in the name of love.", "annotation_id": "9072695", "url": "https://genius.com/9072695", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "But second chances, that ain’t how you livin’ Redemption on your mind, I’ll never be forgiven", "vault_lines": ["But second chances, that ain't how you livin'", "Redemption on your mind, I'll never be forgiven"], "meaning": "As Drake has grown more mature, he feels like he’s been able to start redeeming himself for all his wrongdoings.\n\nHe thinks he finally deserves a second chance, but a lot of times in life, second chances just don’t happen. He’ll have to live with the fact that he made a mistake that can never be fixed.\n\nDrake’s dislike for the number 2 fuels his realisation that even if this girl does give him a second chance, in his own mind, he cannot truly forgive himself. Paradoxically, he wants her forgiveness even more, because he can respect her dislike for second chances.", "annotation_id": "9072517", "url": "https://genius.com/9072517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "I know you’re seein’ someone that loves you And I don’t want you to see no one else I don’t want you here with no one else I don’t wanna do this with no one else", "vault_lines": ["I know you're seein' someone that loves you", "And I don't want you to see no one else", "I don't want you here with no one else", "I don't wanna do this with no one else", "You know that I'm bound to see you"], "meaning": "Similar to his feelings on “Marvin’s Room,” Drake knows that this girl may be happy with another guy, but he doesn’t really care. He wants her to be with him and no one else.\n\nDrake has a habit of wanting girls that are already tied down. He mentions the term “someone else” in a similar context on “Hotline Bling,” “Days In The East,” and “Doing It Wrong.”", "annotation_id": "9072486", "url": "https://genius.com/9072486", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Wonder if they wish it could’ve been different Wonder what they’d do put in my position I wonder, when my shit drop, do they listen?", "vault_lines": ["Wonder if they wish it could've been different", "Wonder what they'd do put in my position", "I wonder, when my shit drop, do they listen?"], "meaning": "After reminiscing about his previous exes, Drake wonders what they would’ve done if they would’ve put themselves on his shoes. It’s obvious that these girls had their reasons to do what they did to Drizzy, but have they ever thought about how he felt.\n\nKnown for name-dropping random exes in his songs, Drake wonders if they hear his side of the story and have any regrets about what could’ve been.\n\nThis song also samples “One Wish” by Ray J.", "annotation_id": "9072046", "url": "https://genius.com/9072046", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "I’m searchin’ for these words to say to you Please give me time Cause I’m searchin’ for these words to say to you Oh, please give me time Cause I’m searchin’ for these words to say to you right now Redemption’s on your mind when you think about me, yeah Redemption’s on your mind when you think about me, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Cause I'm searchin' for these words to say to you", "Oh, please give me time", "Cause I'm searchin' for these words to say to you right now", "Redemption's on your mind when you think about me, yeah", "Now redemption's on your mind when you think about me", "Redemption's on your mind when you think about me", "Redemption's on your mind when you (Think about me)", "Aw, please give me time", "Cause I'm searchin' for these words", "To say to you right now, yeah", "They say redemption's on your mind when you think about me", "I'm searchin' for these words to say to you", "Please give me time"], "meaning": "Drake’s searching for redemption. Given the lyrics in the first verse, the girl he’s talking to may have assumed he was playing her, but in actuality, he was giving her his all.\n\nWhile his intentions seem clear, he doesn’t know what to say to bring about the changes he desires. However, “redemption’s on your mind when you think about me” does allude to Drake feeling like this girl wants what he wants—a rekindled romance.", "annotation_id": "9071664", "url": "https://genius.com/9071664", "state": "accepted", "votes": 181, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Why would I say all these things to have you feelin’ a way? Why would I tell you I’m 30 away if I’m not on the way? Why do I settle for women that force me to pick up the pieces? Why do I want an independent woman To feel like she needs me?—I lost my way", "vault_lines": ["Cause I'm searchin' for these words to say to you", "Why would I say all these things to have you feelin' a way?", "Why would I tell you I'm 30 away if I'm not on the way?", "That mean a lot to me", "Why do I settle for women that force me to pick up the pieces?", "Why do I want an independent woman", "To feel like she needs me?—I lost my way", "I'm searchin' for these words to say to you"], "meaning": "Reflecting on a past relationship, Drake questions his girl, asking why would he open himself up to her and why would he make the effort to see her if his feelings weren’t genuine. Maybe she thought he was playing her?\n\nDrizzy is plagued by past relationships with women who lack devotion like his. This gives him conflicting emotions, he feels like he’s “lost [his] way.”\n\nHe rapped something similar on “Connect”:\n\nBut when it all falls apart, I’m always still down To pick a million tiny pieces off the ground", "annotation_id": "9071290", "url": "https://genius.com/9071290", "state": "accepted", "votes": 121, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "I used to know you back when you was young Your brother brought me through, you saw me That made me think of you so differently I miss the feeling of you missing me But you say you’re over and done with me Aw, yeah, yeah, yeah Aw, yeah, yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Redemption's on your mind when you think about me, yeah", "I know you're seein' someone that loves you", "I used to know you back when you was young", "Your brother brought me through, you saw me", "That made me think of you so differently", "I miss the feeling of you missing me", "But you say you're over and done with me", "To say to you right now, yeah", "Was for me and you, girl", "And they both say that you're over and done with me"], "meaning": "If Drake knew this girl when she was younger, she could be a Toronto native. Drake has mentioned his local former flames before in his music; Jade, Nebby, Alisha, and Catya are just some examples.\n\n\n\nWhen the girl’s brother brought Drake around to their house, he saw her for what seems is the first time in while and instantly began reminiscing.", "annotation_id": "9070673", "url": "https://genius.com/9070673", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Wonder if they’re second guessin’ their decisions I hate the number 2, that shit is unforgiving", "vault_lines": ["Wonder if they're second guessin' their decisions", "I hate the number 2, that shit is unforgiving"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t like being someone else’s second choice. He plays on “second guessin'” and “number 2\"—if they second guess themselves, (change their mind), Drake could become number 1. On “6PM in New York” he rapped:\n\nBeing number 2 is just being the first to lose\n\nThis doesn’t just affect Drake’s social life. In 2015, Drake was on track to acheive his first solo number one record with “Hotline Bling” until Adele released “Hello” and stole the spot.", "annotation_id": "9070643", "url": "https://genius.com/9070643", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Run your mouth, I’d rather listen to someone else I gave your nickname to someone else", "vault_lines": ["Run your mouth, I'd rather listen to someone else", "I gave your nickname to someone else"], "meaning": "Drake is starting to realize that the girl he’s with isn’t worth his time. He’d rather let this girl run her mouth all she wants as long as he don’t have to listen to her.\n\nWhen people are in a relationship, they tend to give each other cute nicknames to call each other. Drake giving her nickname to another is another way of saying “we’re over.”", "annotation_id": "9070523", "url": "https://genius.com/9070523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Who’s gonna save me when I need savin’? Since Take Care, I’ve been caretakin’", "vault_lines": ["Who's gonna save me when I need savin'?", "Since Take Care, I've been caretakin'"], "meaning": "Drake released his album Take Care on November 15, 2011 and ever since then, he has been taking care of everyone around him.\n\nHis massive success with Take Care gave him the ability to help those he cares about, but who has been looking out for him? Drake insinuates that he feels as though he’ll have no one to help him if he ever needs saving, even though he’s been providing for others for years.", "annotation_id": "9070494", "url": "https://genius.com/9070494", "state": "accepted", "votes": 164, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Erika sued me and opened a business", "vault_lines": ["Erika sued me and opened a business"], "meaning": "Erika Lee is the voice of the woman on the phone in “Marvin’s Room.” In 2012, she sued Drake for not giving her a co-writing credit or any royalties for the song.\n\nIt was reported:\n\nLee filed a lawsuit in California federal court yesterday. She claims that Drake texted her messages like, “U basically made that song” and “It’s shit without you.” While Drake has reportedly offered her a percentage of the publishing money, Lee seeks credit as a co-writer of the song and is seeking other damages.\n\n\n\nThe case was settled the following year out of court.", "annotation_id": "9070267", "url": "https://genius.com/9070267", "state": "accepted", "votes": 191, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Say to you—but eighty percent of it wasn’t true, yeah Say to you—a Mercedes coupe that you’re still drivin’ Was for me and you, girl Really gon’ spend the winter with this other nigga? Act like he’s really the one to get through it with you", "vault_lines": ["But you say you're over and done with me", "To say to you right now, yeah", "Say to you—but eighty percent of it wasn't true, yeah", "Say to you—a Mercedes coupe that you're still drivin'", "Was for me and you, girl", "Really gon' spend the winter with this other nigga?", "Act like he's really the one to get through it with you", "And they both say that you're over and done with me", "That mean a lot to me"], "meaning": "Drake admits that while he didn’t give her the whole truth, he feels like 80% is pretty reasonable. Apparently it wasn’t enough.\n\nHe goes onto question why this girl would want to spend cuffing season with another guy. As he sees it, he’s clearly the superior option.\n\nThis girl could also be the same Drake was talking about in PARTYNEXTDOOR’s “Recognize”:\n\nI gotta get you those snow tires for your Mercedes, I’m glad you reminded me baby", "annotation_id": "9070234", "url": "https://genius.com/9070234", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "line": "Sell my secrets and get top dollar Sell my secrets for a Range Rover Opportunity and temptation They would sell my secrets for a tropical vacation Sell my secrets back to me if I was payin’", "vault_lines": ["Was for me and you, girl", "Sell my secrets and get top dollar", "Sell my secrets for a Range Rover", "They would sell my secrets for a tropical vacation", "Sell my secrets back to me if I was payin'"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Drake’s recent song leaks. His secrets are worth so much money, exposing him could afford you a $85,000 Range Rover.\n\nOn January 31st, Drake dropped “Summer Sixteen” and just 15 minutes later, rival Meek Mill released “War Pain,” containing multiple references to Drake’s song. It was clear someone from Drake’s camp had sold him out.\n\nThis was further brought to light during a Pitchfork interview with “One Dance” collaborator Kyla, who commented:\n\nThey were very much like, “Let’s run with this version, there’s no time for recording it or anything like that. We’re getting hacked left, right, and center.”\n\nDrake further claims that his frenemies would even sell his secrets back to him, or in other words, blackmail him. This idea is reminiscent of Kanye West’s song “Real Friends,” where he rapped:\n\nI had a cousin that stole my laptop that I was fuckin' bitches on\nPaid that nigga 250 thousand just to get it from him", "annotation_id": "9070103", "url": "https://genius.com/9070103", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Redemption", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 20, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-redemption-lyrics", "genius_title": "Redemption"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "[Verse 2: Snoop Dogg (AI)]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The same day that this song was released on April 19 2024, Snoop Dogg posted a video on Instagram of him reacting to hearing about Drake using his voice in artificial intelligence form.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZkK7JE-fqlc\n\n8 days later Snoop Dogg posted the song on his Instagram story where he captioned it “90s HipHop Forever”.\nhttps://www.reddit.com/r/Drizzy/comments/1cehz0e/snoop_sharing_and_enjoying_taylor_made_freestyle/\n\nMonths later Kendrick Lamar would mention this in his song “Wacced Out Murals”:\n\nSnoop posted “Taylor Made,” I prayed it was the edibles, I couldn’t believe it, it was only right for me to let it go\n\nSnoop Dogg would then respond to this via a tweet where he jokingly admitted that it was the edibles.", "annotation_id": "33852246", "url": "https://genius.com/33852246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "The first one really only took me an hour or two The next one is really ’bout to bring out the coward in you", "vault_lines": ["The first one really only took me an hour or two", "The next one is really 'bout to bring out the coward in you"], "meaning": "Referring to his diss track “Push Ups” which only took him an hour or two to either write or record. His next diss track will be even more scathing and is gonna expose Kendrick Lamar as a coward.", "annotation_id": "31509846", "url": "https://genius.com/31509846", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Dot, you know that the D-O-G never fuckin’ doubted you", "vault_lines": ["Dot, you know that the D-O-G never fuckin' doubted you"], "meaning": "as AI Snoop says this, the real Snoop Dogg gave Kendrick Lamar “ The Torch ” in 2011 at one of Lamar’s concerts.\n\nand in Modern Terms, Snoop showed love to Lamar’s 2021 “Mr Morale And The Big Steppers”", "annotation_id": "31504460", "url": "https://genius.com/31504460", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Now’s a time to really make a power move ‘Cause right now it’s lookin’ like you writin’ out the game plan on how to lose", "vault_lines": ["Now's a time to really make a power move", "'Cause right now it's lookin' like you writin' out the game plan on how to lose"], "meaning": "In these lines, Drake channels Snoop Dogg to send a sharp message to Kendrick Lamar. He talks about making a “power move,” a bold strategic action that could significantly change the game in one’s favor.\n\nHe criticizes Kendrick by saying it looks like he’s planning how to lose. This suggests that Kendrick’s current strategies might lead him to failure rather than victory.\n\nBy using Snoop’s voice, Drake adds weight to his critique, highlighting the importance of strategic thinking in their rivalry.\n\nDrake insinuates to his idol’s eyes, the only history Kendrick is writing is a game plan on how to lose a beef.", "annotation_id": "31499122", "url": "https://genius.com/31499122", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Yeah, unc’, that’s the truth", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, unc', that's the truth"], "meaning": "“Unc” is a common nickname for Snoop Dogg.\n\n\n\nAt the start of AI Snoop’s verse, he raps:\n\nNephew, what the fuck you really ‘bout to do?", "annotation_id": "31498365", "url": "https://genius.com/31498365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Killuminati", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Reference to 2Pac’s “The Don Killuminati: The 7 Day Theory” and “The Don Killuminati: The 3 Day Theory”", "annotation_id": "31498244", "url": "https://genius.com/31498244", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "The rest of y’all are definitely involved, y’all gettin’ it too Soon as you get the courage to drop, I’m out on the loose, on the loose", "vault_lines": ["The rest of y'all are definitely involved, y'all gettin' it too", "Soon as you get the courage to drop, I'm out on the loose, on the loose"], "meaning": "Across two albums released by Future & Metro Boomin in late March 2024 and early April 2024, vocal performances by Future, The Weeknd, & Asap Rocky all featured jabs at Drake in some capacity. Rick Ross was also featured on WE DON’T TRUST YOU, on the track “Everyday Hustle”, but did not send direct shots at Drake on the song. However, he would later respond to shots sent at him on Drake’s diss record “Push Ups”:\n\nI might take your latest girl and cuff her like I’m Ricky\nCan’t believe he jumpin' in, this nigga turnin' fifty\nEvery song that made it on the chart, he got from Drizzy\nSpend that lil' check you got and stay up out my business\n\nRoss would respond with his own diss record aimed at Drake, released on the same day as “Push Ups”, entitled “Champagne Moments”.\n\nProducer Metro Boomin, who is partially responsible for platforming many of these artists, and their shots at Drake, on his successive albums WE DON’T TRUST YOU and WE STILL DON’T TRUST YOU, is also reportedly at odds with Drake, likely more so after Drake’s dismissal of him on the same diss track, “Push Ups,” where Drake raps:\n\nMetro, shut your ho ass up and make some drums\n\nBetween all of these artists currently “beefing” Drake, Drake closes out Taylor Made Freestyle by acknowledging that he’s ready to clash with all of them and bring the same energy he’s bringing to his current feud with Kendrick Lamar. While his current focus is on Kendrick, if any other artists are willing to drop works inciting conflict with Drake, he indicates he’d be “out on the loose,” likely meaning he would come at them with little restraint.", "annotation_id": "31493748", "url": "https://genius.com/31493748", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Hate all you corporate industry puppets, I’m not in the mood I love it when you niggas talk loose like I’m not in the room", "vault_lines": ["Hate all you corporate industry puppets, I'm not in the mood", "I love it when you niggas talk loose like I'm not in the room"], "meaning": "Drake creates distance between himself and other rappers with juxtaposing statements of “hating” them and “loving” their actions\n\nHe hates their “industry puppets” side by stating he is his own boss and can make moves of his own free will, unlike the strings attached to puppets who are voiced by the ventriloquist (Executives like Top Dawg to Kendrick for example) in complete control of them\n\nHe also argues against the idea of celebrities (specifically himself) can be “out of touch” and does not know what people are saying about him, as instead he is closely listening to everything said about him\n\nThis could also imply Drake has deep rooted connections by having an inside/undercover person relaying information back to him", "annotation_id": "31492498", "url": "https://genius.com/31492498", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Never shot nobody, never stabbed nobody Never did nothin’ violent to no one, it’s the homies that empower you", "vault_lines": ["Never shot nobody, never stabbed nobody", "Never did nothin' violent to no one, it's the homies that empower you"], "meaning": "Drake insinuates Kendrick is donning a false bravado via what he’s seen rather than what he’s experienced\nThis “non-criminal” persona is a frequently occurring stance Kendrick takes within his songs, such as:\n\nI’m just a good kid from Compton that want to rap\nWeighing my options, pick up a Bible or a strap?\nI never killed a man, never sold any crack\nAll I ever did was try to keep my city on the map\n\nThis is also a recurring theme within Kendrick’s universally praised song “The Art of Peer Pressure”, where he is persistently stating he’s a peaceful civilian, unless he’s with the “homies”\n\nBut I’m with the homies right now\nAnd we ain’t askin' for no favors\nRush a nigga quick, then laugh about it later,\nReally, I’m a peacemaker\nBut I’m with the homies right now\n\nDrake wishes to point out this hypocrisy, this is even implied within Kendrick’s appearance on Metro Boomin’s “Like that”\n\nGot 2TEEZ with me, I’m snatchin' chains and burnin' tattoos, it’s up\nLost too many soldiers not to play it safe\n\nDrake noticed how its only through Kendrick’s bodyguard or “homies” that he will be “snatchin' chains”, instead of Kendrick alone/himself", "annotation_id": "31492294", "url": "https://genius.com/31492294", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "That’s jail talk for real thugs, you gotta be you", "vault_lines": ["That's jail talk for real thugs, you gotta be you"], "meaning": "Drake (voicing as Kendrick’s idol) is playing with 2Pac’s iconic catchphrase and rap group “Thug Life” by using it to downplay Kendrick’s lyrics as inauthentic and advises Kendrick should instead aim to be himself, as Kendrick said on the song “Like That”:\n\nGot 2TEEZ with me, I’m snatchin' chains and burnin' tattoos\nIt’s up, lost too many soldiers not to play it safe\nIf he walk around with that stick, it ain’t Andre 3K\n\nDrake claims these threatening words are reserved for gangsters/thugs who have really faced imprisonment, unlike Kendrick Lamar who has never faced criminal charges or jail time.", "annotation_id": "31492025", "url": "https://genius.com/31492025", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Nephew, what the fuck you really ’bout to do? We passed you the torch at the House of Blues And now you gotta do some dirty work, you know how to move, right? Right?", "vault_lines": ["Nephew, what the fuck you really 'bout to do?", "We passed you the torch at the House of Blues", "And now you gotta do some dirty work, you know how to move, right? Right?", "Of what the fuck you 'bout to do"], "meaning": "Though not at the famed House of Blues, Kendrick was “passed the torch” of West Coast rap in 2011 by West Coast hip hop legends, The Game, Snoop Dogg, Kurupt, Daz, and Warren G during a show in Los Angeles, California. The gesture notably moved Lamar to tears:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/KYjyVjI2-z0?si=QXdYkLMRpKMf_TdW&t=113\n\nUsing Dogg’s likeness to taunt Lamar, Drake implies that such an honor surely means Lamar is capable of responding to Drake’s diss track, “Push Ups.”", "annotation_id": "31491801", "url": "https://genius.com/31491801", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "How to bark up the wrong tree and then get your head popped in a crowded room", "vault_lines": ["How to bark up the wrong tree and then get your head popped in a crowded room"], "meaning": "Drake continues to mock Kendrick Lamar for not responding promptly, making it clear that Kendrick has picked the wrong person to lyrically contend with.\nDrake cleverly uses the expression “barking up the wrong tree” continuing his references to his album “For All the Dogs”, as well as it being a play on the name of Snoop Dogg.\nOn his 2021 track: Wants and Needs, Drake used a similar analogy:\n\nBarkin' up the wrong tree, you know how the dogs get\n\nPreviously comparing his beef as a ‘20 v 1’ in “Push Ups”, Drake also recognises it as a ‘Crowded Room’, a reference to an Apple TV series detailed below (spoilers).\n\nHe comes across assured of his accuracy to target Kendrick, so much so that he can lyrically target and kill him off, even with all the others surrounding him who have dissed Drake in Future and Metro Boomin’s two recent projects.\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: The Crowded Room is an Apple TV series about Danny Sullivan who shoots his step-dad, but he’s acquitted by reason of insanity cause of his multiple personality disorder.\n\nDrake has been accused of having “multiple personalities” in his music, but in this diss specifically he is actually channeling 2 different personalities; 2Pac and Snoop.", "annotation_id": "31491735", "url": "https://genius.com/31491735", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "You supposed to be the boogeyman, go do what you do Unless this is a moment that you tell us this not really you", "vault_lines": ["You supposed to be the boogeyman, go do what you do", "Unless this is a moment that you tell us this not really you", "Nephew, what the fuck you really 'bout to do?", "Of what the fuck you 'bout to do"], "meaning": "Kendrick’s persona is that of a rap “boogeyman,” who isn’t afraid to call out or rap about anyone, dating back to his infamous “Control” verse (and even further back in a 2010 remix of Kanye West’s “Monster” where he called out people like Drake, Big Sean, J. Cole, and more).\n\nHere, Drake is essentially saying that if Kendrick was really that person, then he wouldn’t care about industry relationships with people like Taylor Swift (who he is friends with and has collaborated with previously) and would instead be the “boogyman” and would release a response to Drake’s “Push Ups,” even if it steps on the toes of Swift’s The Tortured Poets Department release.\n\nDrake ends the sentiment by noting that if Kendrick doesn’t do that, then maybe he isn’t the “real hip hop” boogeyman that everyone thought he was.", "annotation_id": "31491369", "url": "https://genius.com/31491369", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "I know you never been to jail, orange jumpsuits and shower shoes", "vault_lines": ["I know you never been to jail, orange jumpsuits and shower shoes"], "meaning": "Kendrick famously opens up his song “Poe Man’s Dreams (His Vice)”, with the following lines:\n\n“I used to want to see the penitentiary way after elementary\nThought it was cool to look the judge in the face when he sentenced me\nSince my uncles was institutionalized\nMy intuition had said I was suited for family ties”\n\nKendrick almost romanticizes a life of crime and criminal connections throughout this verse and many of his songs, yet he himself has not been imprisoned or convicted of anything similar to the likes the friends he raps about and his peers have.", "annotation_id": "31491353", "url": "https://genius.com/31491353", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "But still, you gotta show this fuckin’ owl who’s boss on the West", "vault_lines": ["But still, you gotta show this fuckin' owl who's boss on the West"], "meaning": "Drake’s label’s image for OVO Sound has famously been an owl and he has associated himself heavily with said imagery with his own personal jewelry and merchandise line since its inception in 2012.", "annotation_id": "31491342", "url": "https://genius.com/31491342", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "You better have a motherfuckin’ quintuple entendre on that shit Some shit I don’t even understand, like", "vault_lines": ["You better have a motherfuckin' quintuple entendre on that shit", "Some shit I don't even understand, like"], "meaning": "Kendrick is renowned for his complex lyrical layers, frequently employing double and triple entendres—phrases crafted to have multiple interpretations.\n\nAn entendre is a figure of speech where a word or phrase conveys two or more meanings simultaneously.\n\nIn the context of ongoing lyrical exchanges, Drake has commented on the prolonged silence from Kendrick in response to his lyrical challenges.\n\nDrake suggests that for Kendrick’s delayed response to be worth the wait, it should feature quintuple entendres, indicating a level of lyrical complexity that would surpass simple or even intricate wordplay, achieving a staggering fivefold ambiguity in meaning.\n\nDrake is saying this in an ironic/humorous tone as opposed to Kendrick’s status as a lyricist, and continuing the “lyrical gymnastics” line, he alleges Kendrick’s lines don’t really mean much to him (in a similar vein to the “lyrical miracle” insult).", "annotation_id": "31490911", "url": "https://genius.com/31490911", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Talk about him likin’ young girls, that’s a gift from me Heard it on the Budden Podcast, it’s gotta be true", "vault_lines": ["Talk about him likin' young girls, that's a gift from me", "Heard it on the Budden Podcast, it's gotta be true"], "meaning": "Drake jokes that the ghost of late hip hop legend 2Pac is pushing Kendrick to mention Drake’s alleged pedophilia in his response to Drake’s diss track “Push Ups.” This accusation most commonly stems from Drake’s controversialy close relationship with actress Millie Bobby Brown. Though she was 20-years-old when this song released, Brown mentioned that she and Drake texted often during a 2018 Emmy Awards interview:\n\nI love him. I met him in Australia and he’s honestly so fantastic and a great friend and a great role model […] We just texted the other day and he was like ‘I miss you so much,’ and I was like ‘I miss you more!‘ […] [He gives me advice] about boys, he helps me. He’s great. He’s wonderful. I love him. […] [The advice] stays in the text messages.\n\nBrown was age 14 at the time, leading many to accuse Drake of grooming the then teenaged star. Drake would dismiss these allegations on “Another Late Night” from his 2023 release For All The Dogs where he rapped:\n\nWeirdos in my comments talkin' ‘bout some Millie Bobby—look\nBring them jokes up to the gang, we get to really flockin’\n\nA year later, a controversial video of Drake kissing and caressing a 17-year-old girl onstage during a May 2010 concert resurfaced. When the attendee revealed her age, Drake said:\n\nI can’t go to jail yet, man. You’re 17, why do you look like that? You thick. Look at all this. […] I don’t know if I should feel guilty or not, but I had fun. I like the way your breasts feel against my chest.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/h-p1feEHJZM?feature=shared\n\nHere, Drake implicitly suggests that the allegations are false by joking that they originated on the Joe Budden Podcast, the hip hop podcast by the titular retired rapper that Drake beefed with in 2016.\n\nSince “Push Ups” released, many fans of Kendrick predicted that he’d go after Drake for this. In a sense, this is Drake attempting to preemptively neuter the shot before Kendrick can take it.", "annotation_id": "31490881", "url": "https://genius.com/31490881", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "I’m definitely ‘bout to come around the Lang gang and let my fuckin’ bowel move Shittin’ on you niggas from a whole different altitude", "vault_lines": ["I'm definitely 'bout to come around the Lang gang and let my fuckin' bowel move", "Shittin' on you niggas from a whole different altitude"], "meaning": "Here, Drake references pgLang, Kendrick Lamar’s creative label co-founded by himself and Dave Free, with Kendrick’s cousin Baby Keem a labelmate.\n\nDrake claims he’s on a higher level than the members of pgLang, and as such, indicates his intention to “shit on” them, or to impose his superiority over them.\n\nThis is not the first time that Drake mentions he’s on a higher level than his peers. In fact, he raps about it on his precursory diss record, “Push Ups”:\n\nI’m at the top of the mountain, so you tight now\nJust to have this talk with your ass, I had to hike down", "annotation_id": "31490876", "url": "https://genius.com/31490876", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "She got the whole pgLang on mute like that Beyoncé challenge, y’all boys quiet for the weekend, like", "vault_lines": ["She got the whole pgLang on mute like that Beyoncé challenge, y'all boys quiet for the weekend, like"], "meaning": "Beyonce’s song ‘Energy’ contains the lyric:\n\n“Look around, everybody on mute”\n\nDuring her Renaissance Tour, when the lyric would be played the entire crowd would go silent.\n\nDrake is comparing this to Kendrick’s response time to his diss track ‘Push Ups’. It seems that when Drake released his track Kendrick and team went on mute.\n\n\n\nIronically, this is likely also a reference to the incident where Kendrick came out as a surprise guest for one of Beyonce’s shows, but was inaudible due to his mic malfunctioning.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=axuIpzdpslM&ab_channel=HipHopDX", "annotation_id": "31490856", "url": "https://genius.com/31490856", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "K, you gotta fuck this nigga girl, he gotta get abused All that shit ‘bout burnin’ tattoos, he is not amused", "vault_lines": ["K, you gotta fuck this nigga girl, he gotta get abused", "All that shit 'bout burnin' tattoos, he is not amused"], "meaning": "Drake directly responds to Kendrick’s threat of burning tattoos on “Like That” by Future & Metro Boomin from their 2024 collab project WE DON’T TRUST YOU where Kendrick raps:\n\nI’m snatching chains and burning tattoos, it’s up\n\nDrake suggests he isn’t moved by the threat, prompting Kendrick to do something more disrespectful such as sleeping with Drake’s significant other. Pac claimed to do exactly this to The Notorious B.I.G. on his legendary 1996 diss track “Hit ‘Em Up”  where he rapped:\n\nYou claim to be a player, but I fucked your wife", "annotation_id": "31490853", "url": "https://genius.com/31490853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "They told me the spirit of Makaveli is alive In a nigga under 5'5\", so it’s gotta be you I would beef the whole fuckin’ game It was me and Snoop Dogg, had my fuckin’ shirt off in the House of Blues", "vault_lines": ["They told me the spirit of Makaveli is alive", "In a nigga under 5'5\", so it's gotta be you", "I would beef the whole fuckin' game", "It was me and Snoop Dogg, had my fuckin' shirt off in the House of Blues"], "meaning": "Drake implies that Kendrick, despite his seemingly universal consideration as the successor to Tupac, can’t possibly be that due to his height. This continues the trend of short jokes at Kendrick’s expense on “Push Ups” where Drake rapped:\n\nHow the fuck you big steppin' with a size-seven men’s on?\n[…]\nPipsqueak, pipe down\n[…]\nTop say drop, your little midget ass better fuckin' [drop]\n\nTupac, who also went by the stage name Makaveli, notably had a height of 5'9\" compared to Lamar’s 5'6\". Pac was involved in a myriad of beefs stemming from his conflict with the late Notorious B.I.G. In fact, Tupac’s final concert before his death featured him and rapper Snoop Dogg at the House of Blues in Los Angeles, California where the duo performed “2 of Amerikaz Most Wanted,” a song where the two flex their defenses against enemies:\n\nAffiliated with the hustlers, so we made it\nNo answers to questions, I’m tryin' to get up on it\nMy nigga, Dogg, and me, eternally the most wanted\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=76OveHWjZnY", "annotation_id": "31490839", "url": "https://genius.com/31490839", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Kendrick, we need ya, the West Coast savior Engraving your name in some hip-hop history If you deal with this viciously You seem a little nervous about all the publicity Fuck this Canadian lightskin, Dot We need a no-debated West Coast victory, man Call him a bitch for me", "vault_lines": ["Call him a bitch for me", "Kendrick, we need ya, the West Coast savior", "Engraving your name in some hip-hop history", "If you deal with this viciously", "You seem a little nervous about all the publicity", "Fuck this Canadian lightskin, Dot", "We need a no-debated West Coast victory, man"], "meaning": "Drake taunts Kendrick by using an artificial voice of the late hip hop legend Tupac “2pac” Shakur, an artist considered by many to be the greatest rapper ever, to beg Lamar to respond to Drake’s diss track “Push Ups.”\n\nThis is likely intended to mock how Kendrick used snippets from Tupac interviews for the outro of “Mortal Man” from his 2015 opus To Pimp a Butterfly to simulate a conversation with the late rapper:\nhttps://youtu.be/axwpgn3GRMs?si=lXDzbxN3WYIz-O26&t=396\n\nKendrick, like Tupac did, reps the United States' west coast of Los Angeles, California. Seeing as Kendrick obviously holds the rapper in high regard and is often considered his successor, it’s fitting for Drake to go about attacking Lamar this way.\n\nDrake also attempts to preemptively neuter some of the more predictable shots Lamar could take at him, such as Drake’s status as a biracial man from Canada being such a prominent figure in a predominantly African American genre. This was something rapper Pusha T famously clowned Drake for on “Story of Adidon” during their 2018 beef where he famously rapped:\n\nConfused, always felt you weren’t Black enough\nAfraid to grow it ‘cause your 'fro wouldn’t nap enough\n\nThis lack of “real” Blackness was something rapper Rick Ross clowned Drake for in his 2024 diss track “Champagne Moments” where he repeatedly called Drake out for being a “white boy.”", "annotation_id": "31490831", "url": "https://genius.com/31490831", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "World is watchin’ this chess game, but are you out of moves?", "vault_lines": ["World is watchin' this chess game, but are you out of moves?"], "meaning": "Drake and Kendrick Lamar’s beef has captivated the world after J. Cole seemingly dropped out of the fight.\n\nAfter Drake’s Push Ups was leaked on April 13th, 2024, Drakes second hit at Kendrick, this track, makes it abundantly clear that Drake is ready for a fight. He awaits Kendrick’s response, as stated later in the song.\n\nUsing a “chess game” as a metaphor also refers to the music video to Drake and J. Cole’s First Person Shooter, the track K. Dot originally responded to.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/CzrmkZYOOii/?igsh=dWJtaTJncWY0bXQx\n\nThe original inspiration for the chess match in the First Person Shooter video was taken after a photoshoot between soccer legends Lionel Messi and Cristiano Ronaldo for a Louis Vuitton ad. This photoshoot was posted just days before the two stars kicked off the 2022 FIFA World Cup. The two players are often talked about in tandem, with fierce arguments over who is better. Drake was most likely referencing these two GOATs and that debate as well.\n\nIt is also worth nothing that Lionel Messi is 5’7”, one inch taller than Kendrick Lamar. Ronaldo is two inches taller than Drake at 6’2”. Kendrick’s height is something Drake repeatedly made fun of in Push Ups.", "annotation_id": "31490827", "url": "https://genius.com/31490827", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "High up in the sky like I’m Howard Hughes", "vault_lines": ["High up in the sky like I'm Howard Hughes"], "meaning": "The line is an obvious play on the aviation career of business magnate Howard Hughes. However, it is also worth noting that Howard Hughes was, for many years, the richest man in the United States. Throughout this beef, Drake has constantly centered on his wealth as a theme, citing the disparity between his net worth and Kendrick’s.", "annotation_id": "31490811", "url": "https://genius.com/31490811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Dot, I know you’re in that NY apartment, you strugglin’ right now, I know it In the notepad doing lyrical gymnastics, my boy", "vault_lines": ["Dot, I know you're in that NY apartment, you strugglin' right now, I know it", "In the notepad doing lyrical gymnastics, my boy"], "meaning": "Kendrick Lamar bought a penthouse in Brooklyn, New York for $8.6 million.\n\n\n\nDrake references this to dissociate Kendrick with Compton and his West Coast roots and attach him to a high-end lavish lifestyle.\n\nHe attempts to paint Kenny as someone more worried about the complexity of his lines than quickly dropping a meaningful response.", "annotation_id": "31490767", "url": "https://genius.com/31490767", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "Yeah, shout out to Taylor Swift Biggest gangster in the music game right now You know, I moved my album when she dropped, I said that already", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, shout out to Taylor Swift", "Biggest gangster in the music game right now", "You know, I moved my album when she dropped, I said that already"], "meaning": "Taylor Swift is almost universally considered the most commercially successful music artist of the 2010s. This is why Drake calls her the “biggest gangster” in the music industry, even admitting to fearing her a bit on “Red Button”:\n\nTaylor Swift the only nigga that I ever rated\nOnly one could make me drop the album just a little later\nRest of y’all, I treat you like you never made it\n\n Taylor Swift and Drake.", "annotation_id": "31490711", "url": "https://genius.com/31490711", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "You know, she ‘bout to milli’ run through a Milly Rock on your head top, well", "vault_lines": ["You know, she 'bout to milli' run through a Milly Rock on your head top, well"], "meaning": "Referencing Taylor Swift’s newly-released double album THE TORTURED POETS DEPARTMENT, which features 31 songs in total and is projected to sell two MILLION units in its first week.", "annotation_id": "31490682", "url": "https://genius.com/31490682", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "You asked for the smoke, now it seem you too busy for the smoke I won’t lie, the people confused Now you ’bout to give this shit another week?", "vault_lines": ["You asked for the smoke, now it seem you too busy for the smoke", "I won't lie, the people confused", "Now you 'bout to give this shit another week?"], "meaning": "Drake insists that Kendrick is conflicted on how to reply to “Push Ups,” his response to Kendrick’s disses on “Like That” by Future & Metro Boomin from their 2024 collab album WE DON’T TRUST YOU.\n\nDrake dropped this track a week after “Push Ups” leaked online on April 13, 2024 to no immediate response from Lamar. After the track leaked, Drake would go on to try and bait Lamar into responding with daily social media antics, such as posting a photo of Lamar’s manager and asking him for a response and commenting under an Instagram post defending Kendrick’s silence reading “Use me as a ‘they have nothing to drop’ button.”\n\nIt should also be noted that Kendrick never made a direct response to anyone he challenged on his historic “Control” verse in 2013 despite getting some replies; Kendrick only replied through continuous subliminal shots over the years and a leaked alternate verse for his 2018 song “ELEMENT.” For this, Drake calls out K-Dot for “throwing rocks and hiding his hand:”\n\nNot this time, nigga, you followin' through", "annotation_id": "31490657", "url": "https://genius.com/31490657", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "line": "And fall back so homegirl can run her numbers up? I woulda refused Fuck these industry relationships, she not in your shoes", "vault_lines": ["And fall back so homegirl can run her numbers up? I woulda refused", "Fuck these industry relationships, she not in your shoes"], "meaning": "Drake alleges that Kendrick delayed the release of his response to “Push Ups” to avoid challenging the charting of THE TORTURED POET’S DEPARTMENT, an album by pop icon Taylor Swift that dropped on April 19, 2024. Drake previously teased Lamar for catering to Swift’s agenda on “Push Ups” when he rapped:\n\nWe need a verse for the Swifties\nTop say drop, you better drop and give ‘em fifty\n\nDrake officially released “Push Ups” to streaming services on the same day, showing that he doesn’t fear Swift’s charting dominance. Drake jokes that this effectively makes Swift a new version of Anthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith, the CEO of Kendrick’s former record label that decided the release dates for his albums. This dig made for the hook of “Push Ups” where Drake rapped:\n\nExtortion baby, whole career, you been shook up\n‘Cause Top told you, “Drop and give me fifty,” like some push-ups\n\nDrake makes a homonymic pun out of the term “tailor-made,” joking that Kendrick’s music release schedule is controlled by Taylor Swift due to her release dates for music dictating that of Lamar’s.\n\n\n\nTaylor Swift (Left) and Kendrick Lamar (Right) at the 2016 Grammy Awards.\n\nSoon after this song, Kendrick dropped a response, “euphoria”, and four days later, he dropped a second response called “6:16 in LA”. The latter track was produced by Jack Antonoff, serving as a response to this line: while Ant & Kendrick may be working together, a response doesn’t need to take a week.", "annotation_id": "31490608", "url": "https://genius.com/31490608", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Taylor Made Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 29, "clicked": 29, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-taylor-made-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Taylor Made Freestyle"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "YOU THE ONE", "status": "missing", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "I taught you everything you know, now you got student pride", "vault_lines": ["I taught you everything you know, now you got student pride"], "meaning": "This is a subtle diss aimed at Kendrick Lamar, suggesting that Kendrick owes his success and knowledge to Drake.\n\nThe phrase “now you got student pride” can be a reference to Lamar’s song ‘PRIDE’ whilst also suggesting  that Kendrick, despite his accomplishments, is still just a student compared to Drake. It downplays Kendrick’s success by attributing it to Drake’s influence rather than Kendrick’s own talent and hard work.\n\nIt is important to mention that Kendrick sent shots at Drake on the same day in ‘All the Stars’ featuring SZA", "annotation_id": "31822661", "url": "https://genius.com/31822661", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Tell ’em I don’t wanna link like a broken necklace", "vault_lines": ["Tell 'em I don't wanna link like a broken necklace"], "meaning": "Drake tells his opps (mentioned in the line before) that he doesn’t want to link.\n\nHe plays on a double meaning of the word link.\n\nTo link: slang, meaning to connect or join forces.\n\nA link in a necklace: a necklace is made up of multiple small pieces called links. These intertwine to create a chain. A broken necklace is therefore a necklace that is missing a link.", "annotation_id": "26252911", "url": "https://genius.com/26252911", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Advance the venue so we know the exits", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, advance the venue so we know the exits"], "meaning": "‘Advancing’  a venue means his security team goes to a place Drake is scheduled to go to beforehand. The security team then maps out a plan for his visit to ensure Drake’s safety. This involves knowing where all the exits of a building are.", "annotation_id": "26252898", "url": "https://genius.com/26252898", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "My security government tier", "vault_lines": ["Cover my ears, my security government tier"], "meaning": "Government officials usually have extremely good security. This ties in with the overall ‘diplomatic immunity’ theme of the song.", "annotation_id": "26252895", "url": "https://genius.com/26252895", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Forecastin’ tornadoes, brainstormin’ retaliation at dinner tables", "vault_lines": ["Forecastin' tornadoes, brainstormin' retaliation at dinner tables"], "meaning": "Drake is forecasting tornadoes (predicting bad news) for his opposition while he is brainstorming (thinking and planning) retaliation at dinner tables.\n\nNote the wordplay around; forecasting, tornadoes and brainstorming. All correlating to weather.", "annotation_id": "26252883", "url": "https://genius.com/26252883", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "I had to lay low, Hot Topic like your everyday clothes Closed off but I could never stay closed", "vault_lines": ["I had to lay low, Hot Topic like your everyday clothes", "Closed off but I could never stay closed"], "meaning": "After all the controversy surrounding Drake in 2017, he found it best to disappear for a while thus explaining his hiatus after 2017’s More Life.\n\nHot Topic is an American retail chain specializing in counterculture-related clothing and accessories, as well as licensed music that started in 2011.", "annotation_id": "13622495", "url": "https://genius.com/13622495", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Promise shit is gettin’ outta line like when you make a stencil Or gettin’ outta line like when you break a pencil", "vault_lines": ["Promise shit is gettin' outta line like when you make a stencil", "Or gettin' outta line like when you break a pencil"], "meaning": "Getting “out of line” means breaking the rules or being inappropriate. Drake’s playing on the phrase’s origins: literally drawing outside of the lines on a piece of paper.\n\nA stencil is a tool that allows the user to make perfectly straight lines to make letters or designs.  Stencils can be used over and over again to create the same design or “line.” Also, when you’re drawing with a pencil, if you accidentally break the graphite tip, you’re sure to mess up your lines.", "annotation_id": "13591365", "url": "https://genius.com/13591365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "I listen to heavy metal for meditation, no silence", "vault_lines": ["I listen to heavy metal for meditation, no silence"], "meaning": "On November 16, 2017, Odell Beckham Jr. shared this line on Instagram, reminding us how close he and Drake are (Drake sent him the song before it was released). The rapper has also sent his songs to Kevin Durant early.\n\n\n\nHeavy metal is known for its massive sound and shouldn’t be used for meditation.  Drake is saying that he’s nothing like a normal person.  He’s always lit and there’s never a dull moment.", "annotation_id": "13571908", "url": "https://genius.com/13571908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Diplomatic immunity, fuck ’em All that peace and that unity All that weak shit’ll ruin me, fuck ’em, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Diplomatic immunity, fuck 'em", "All that peace and that unity", "All that weak shit'll ruin me, fuck 'em, yeah"], "meaning": "A diplomat is someone who is appointed by a state to conduct negotiations between representatives of states. Essentially, a diplomat’s job is to bring peace and unity between countries, so they are granted “diplomatic immunity” which makes them exempt from some laws and ensures their safety.\n\nWith diss tracks like “Charged Up,” “Back to Back,” and “Two Birds, One Stone,” Drake is no stranger to rap beef, but on occasion he has proven himself capable of making peace. For example, he ended his beef with Ludacris at the 2017 Billboard Music Awards, and showed support for long-time rival Meek Mill on “Family Feud” by criticizing his jail sentence:\n\nOr too long like a sentence from a Philly judge\nFuck is the point in all the beefin' when we really blood?", "annotation_id": "13564779", "url": "https://genius.com/13564779", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Niggas stakin’ out the crib, it’s all gravy Protection that God gave me", "vault_lines": ["Niggas stakin' out the crib, it's all gravy", "Protection that God gave me"], "meaning": "Drake’s playing on “staking out,” as in surveilling, and steak as in the meat. You literally put gravy on meat, but “it’s all gravy” is also an idiom meaning “it’s all good”—Drake doesn’t mind people spying on him because he believes God’s looking out for him. The concept of God watching over him also ties into the other Scary Hours song, “God’s Plan.”", "annotation_id": "13564467", "url": "https://genius.com/13564467", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Booked a private room at Wally’s, waiter twistin’ the cork", "vault_lines": ["Booked a private room at Wally's, waiter twistin' the cork"], "meaning": "Wally’s is an upscale wine restaurant located in Beverly Hills, Los Angeles. “Twisting the cork” refers to the waiter uncorking a bottle of wine.", "annotation_id": "13564390", "url": "https://genius.com/13564390", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Girls huggin’ me then askin’ me, “What scent that is, boy?”", "vault_lines": ["Girls huggin' me then askin' me, \"What scent that is, boy?\""], "meaning": "Drake is most likely wearing Drakkar Noir, one of his favorite fragrances. “Drakkardnoir” was one of his old Twitter handles, and he even got a tattoo of the cologne on his left shoulder.", "annotation_id": "13564384", "url": "https://genius.com/13564384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Yeah, who am I? The do or die The one with the fewest lies and the truest ties", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, who am I? The do or die", "The one with the fewest lies and the truest ties"], "meaning": "“Who Am I” is a song by the rap group Do or Die from their 1998 album Headz or Tailz. Often heralded as the man to discover Drake and bring him to Lil Wayne, J Prince Jr. is the son of J Prince, who was the founder of Rap-A-Lot records, the label that Do or Die released Headz or Tailz under.\n\n\n\nJ Prince Jr. is known for using the phrase “Mob Ties,” which Drake could be referencing here. Drake’s “truest ties” are his OVO crew members, though, like Oliver El-Khatib, Noah Shebib, and Future The Prince. The core of his clique hasn’t changed for over a decade.", "annotation_id": "13563138", "url": "https://genius.com/13563138", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Motor City Casino, I’m at the cage with my old license They tell me “don’t worry, I got your money” like Osirus", "vault_lines": ["Motor City Casino, I'm at the cage with my old license", "They tell me \"don't worry, I got your money\" like Osirus"], "meaning": "The Motor City Casino Hotel is a popular resort complex located in Detroit’s downtown area.\n\n“The cage” is another name for casino cashier desks (where you turn your chips in for money), which are often protected with bars, giving them appearance of a cage. Usually, casino cashiers wouldn’t let people producing expired licenses to exchange their chips, but they let Drake do it because he’s famous.\n\n\n\nOsirus is an alias of Ol' Dirty Bastard, a member of the Wu-Tang Clan. “Got Your Money” is a song from ODB’s 1999 album Nigga Please. This isn’t the first time Drake referenced “Got Your Money” on one of his songs; on “Worst Behavior,” he rapped:\n\nBitch you better have my money when I come for this shit like O.D.B.", "annotation_id": "13559725", "url": "https://genius.com/13559725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "At award shows, cuttin’ through the tension, of course", "vault_lines": ["At award shows, cuttin' through the tension, of course"], "meaning": "At the 2017 Billboard Music Awards, Drake ended his beef with Ludacris on stage. It was also the first time he and Meek Mill were in the same building together since their conflict.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UAdrFOUyEaM\n\nAnother memorable awards show moment was at the 2016 MTV VMAs, where Drake presented Rihanna with the Vanguard Award. He fawned over her and leaned in for a kiss, but was met with a friendly hug in return.", "annotation_id": "13560082", "url": "https://genius.com/13560082", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Dirty 30, workin’ on me", "vault_lines": ["Dirty 30, workin' on me"], "meaning": "As of this song’s release, Drake was 31. Like many others in their “dirty 30s,” he seems to believe he’s in a transitional stage in his life where he has to practice self care. Drizzy would rather work on himself instead of giving his time and energy to others that may not have his best interest at heart like the women he references in the upcoming line.\n\nAlso, “Dirty” is likely in reference to Ol Dirty Bastard, continuing the Wu-Tang Clan allusions in the previous lines.", "annotation_id": "13560009", "url": "https://genius.com/13560009", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "All factual, I call the house “the embassy” The studio ”the chapel”, I hate to travel", "vault_lines": ["All factual, I call the house \"the embassy\"", "The studio \"the chapel\", I hate to travel"], "meaning": "When Scary Hours dropped, Drake was building a new mansion located in Toronto’s wealthy Bridle Path neighborhood. Designed by Ferris Rafauli, it includes a near NBA-sized basketball court and a pool in the basement.\n\nIn an Instagram post uploaded four days before Drake released this song, he referred to the new estate as an “embassy.” An embassy, sometimes called a “diplomatic mission,” is the headquarters of a country’s diplomatic affairs in another nation; Drake’s calling himself Toronto’s ambassador.", "annotation_id": "13559902", "url": "https://genius.com/13559902", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "‘Cause niggas started talkin’ to me like I’m slowin’ down Opinions over statistics, of course Gassed off journalistic Come at me and all you’ll get is the ballistic report", "vault_lines": ["'Cause niggas started talkin' to me like I'm slowin' down", "Opinions over statistics, of course", "Come at me and all you'll get is the ballistic report"], "meaning": "“Gassed” means overexcited or overhyped, which Drake feels music journalists tend to become about their own opinions. If they focused on the stats instead, they’d probably praise Drake more, considering in July 2017 he had had a song on the Billboard Hot 100 every week for the prior eight years and constantly breaks streaming records.\n\nA ballistic report is a forensic examination of bullets after they’re fired; Drake is warning the critics that if they take figurative shots at him, the only reporting will be on the literal shots he sends.\n\nDrake even takes a shot at Complex later on this song:\n\nShit is complex like short niggas around tall ladies", "annotation_id": "13559867", "url": "https://genius.com/13559867", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Billboard awards, I claimed 13 out in Vegas like Sureños", "vault_lines": ["Billboard awards, I claimed 13 out in Vegas like Sureños"], "meaning": "At the 2017 Billboard Music Awards back in May, Drake became the biggest single year winner ever. He feted a whopping 13 wins total, including top artist, top male artist, and many more.\n\nFollowing his victory, Drake took to Las Vegas' Bellagio Fountains for a groundbreaking performance of album cut “Gyalchester,” which can be seen on his Youtube VeVo.\n\n\n\nThe Sureños ‍, Sur 13 or Sureños X3 are groups of loosely affiliated gangs that pay tribute to the Mexican Mafia while in U.S. state and federal correctional facilities.\n\nThe number 13—has been affiliated with this gang and can have several meanings. The most common held meaning is it’s being the 13th letter of the alphabet “M” and stands for the Mexican Mafia.", "annotation_id": "13559814", "url": "https://genius.com/13559814", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Black excellence, but I guess when it comes to me it’s not the same though, all goodie That just pushed me to do the things we all couldn’t", "vault_lines": ["Black excellence, but I guess when it comes to me it's not the same though, all goodie", "That just pushed me to do the things we all couldn't", "I love, I love it"], "meaning": "Despite having reached an unprecedented amount of success in the rap game as well as enjoying cross-over appeal in pop music, Drake isn’t really celebrated for his success as a “black artist” like some of his contemporaries are.\n\nJAY-Z, Beyoncé, Kanye West and Kendrick Lamar are often explicitly celebrated as black artists, especially after the release of their respective albums 4:44, Lemonade, Yeezus, and To Pimp a Butterfly. Drake feels like he’s not placed in that conversation, perhaps on account of his ethnicity (he’s bi-racial).\n\nJAY-Z and Kanye famously rapped about “black excellence” on the song “Murder to Excellence” from their 2011 collaborative album Watch The Throne\n\nIt’s a celebration of black excellence, black tie, black Maybachs\nBlack excellence, opulence, decadence", "annotation_id": "13559777", "url": "https://genius.com/13559777", "state": "accepted", "votes": 125, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "I broke spirits that I never meant to My body isn’t much of a sacred temple, with vodka and wine And sleep at the opposite times", "vault_lines": ["I broke spirits that I never meant to", "My body isn't much of a sacred temple, with vodka and wine", "And sleep at the opposite times"], "meaning": "“Spirit” is courage, but it can also mean a distilled alcoholic beverage. Distillation is an additional process after fermentation that increases alcohol content by removing impurities. Vodka is a spirit because it’s distilled from starchy or sugary plants that were already fermented, but wine isn’t a spirit because it’s made from fermented grapes and not distilled.\n\n“The body is a temple” is a phrase that suggests you should treat your body with as much care and reverence as you would a holy place—and avoid abusing it with substances like alcohol that can have negative side effects. The concept comes from the Bible, specifically 1 Corinthians 6:19-20:\n\nDo you not know that your bodies are temples of the Holy Spirit, who is in you, whom you have received from God? You are not your own; you were bought at a price. Therefore honor God with your bodies.\n\nSleeping at opposite times has a term – nocturnal. Owls are nocturnal, which is also the logo for his label, October’s Very Own (OVO).", "annotation_id": "13559712", "url": "https://genius.com/13559712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "A Rotterdam trip had me on front page though", "vault_lines": ["A Rotterdam trip had me on front page though"], "meaning": "Rotterdam is a city in the Netherlands. During his trip to the Netherlands for the Boy Meets World Tour in 2017, Drizzy canceled a show three times, played at a young boys Bar Mitzvah, and reportedly impregnated a former porn star; all which courted controversy.\n\nThe phrase “A Rotterdam trip” is also a homophone for “A-Rod a damn trip.” J-Lo’s current boyfriend is Alex Rodriguez, otherwise known as A-Rod.", "annotation_id": "13559707", "url": "https://genius.com/13559707", "state": "accepted", "votes": 148, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Violatin’ the Treaty of Versailles Champagne is the alias, but no cease and desist in I I refuse to comply with regulations", "vault_lines": ["Violatin' the Treaty of Versailles", "Champagne is the alias, but no cease and desist in I", "I refuse to comply with regulations"], "meaning": "After his rise to power in the 1930s, Adolf Hitler repeatedly broke the Treaty of Versailles, which was an inciting incident for World War II. While it may not have been Drake’s intention, he’s peripherally aligning himself with the likes of Hitler.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UmedhkNkicY\n\nSigned in 1919 in the royal chateau of Versailles in France, the Treaty of Versailles marked the end of the World War I. It gave Germany total responsibility and debt for the war, as well as an obligation to yield won territory and reduce their army.\n\nFrance also included a chapter about “unfair competition” in order to protect Champagne and Cognac in the Treaty of Versailles. Germany had to respect “the right to any regional appellation in respect of wine or spirits produced in the State to which the region belongs.” This solidified Champagne as the sparkling wine produced from grapes grown in the geographic region of the same name with its name and production standards protected by French law.\n\nDrake’s Instagram handle is “@champagnepapi,” which he playfully states as a violation of the already invalid treaty. But this hasn’t gotten him in trouble. More generally, he paints himself as a nonconformist that doesn’t stop when facing obstacles.\n\nCoincidentally, Quentin Miller has a 2015 song called “Cease and Desist.” He is affiliated with Drake through his writing credits on Drake’s If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, released in the same year.", "annotation_id": "13559644", "url": "https://genius.com/13559644", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "I got multi-colored rings like the Olympics, of course", "vault_lines": ["I got multi-colored rings like the Olympics, of course"], "meaning": "The Olympics are international sporting events featuring summer and winter sports. The logo for the Olympics depicts multicolored rings intertwining that represent the participating continents of the world.\n\n\n\nDrake has a habit of boasting about his rings; he even created a song called “Big Rings” with Future in 2015.", "annotation_id": "13559634", "url": "https://genius.com/13559634", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "I’ll be in better company with my own reflection Mirror, mirror, on the ceiling of my suite in the Royal Palace The TV playin’ Al Jazeera", "vault_lines": ["I'll be in better company with my own reflection", "Mirror, mirror, on the ceiling of my suite in the Royal Palace", "The TV playin' Al Jazeera"], "meaning": "Drake feels more comfortable by himself, away from everyone else. Given his previous references to The UAE, it’s safe to say that’s where Drake is watching Al Jazeera, a state-funded broadcaster in Doha, Qatar.\n\nWhile “Mirror, Mirror” is probably a nod to Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs, ceiling mirrors are often used as sex props.", "annotation_id": "13559596", "url": "https://genius.com/13559596", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "2010 was when I lost my halo", "vault_lines": ["2010 was when I lost my halo"], "meaning": "In 2010 Drake dropped his official debut, Thank Me Later. The same year, they moved his grandmother, Evelyn Sher, into a nursing home. He referred to his grandmother as an angel on his 2009 song “The Winner,” which he later used as a freestyle during the 2010 Grammys:\n\nYeah, and in the name of Evelyn Sher\nI’ll forever forgive anybody that never was there\nFor me, no other woman could ever compare\nMy angel, I hope heaven’s prepared for whenever you there\n\nDrake also used to refer to himself as Young Angel around that time, on tracks like “Ignant Shit”. Losing his “halo” may mean dropping the Young Angel moniker.", "annotation_id": "13559593", "url": "https://genius.com/13559593", "state": "accepted", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Word to the flags that belong in the hood of my Benz Very presidential, I broke all the codes for zonin’ in my residential", "vault_lines": ["Word to the flags that belong in the hood of my Benz", "Very presidential, I broke all the codes for zonin' in my residential"], "meaning": "Presidential limos sport the United States flag on the driver side and the flag of the president on the passenger side. For security reasons, there is also a decoy car with flags on it to confuse attackers. Drake thinks he deserves similar flags to designate his importance, but on the hood of his Mercedes Benz instead—probably the Maybach Pullman he bought for $600,000 in 2015.\n\n\n\nDrake’s claiming he was able to avoid zoning laws when building his 21,000-square-foot Toronto mansion, similar to how presidents are able to use their power and influence to avoid some laws. It wasn’t quite that easy, though—Drake faced a zoning battle over his plans for the estate because the proposed building size was larger than regulations allowed. However, since then, he’s been given the green light.", "annotation_id": "13559552", "url": "https://genius.com/13559552", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Insha’Allah, I hope the mission keeps on gettin’ clearer Whenever, wherever, OVO will always be together like Shakira", "vault_lines": ["Insha'Allah, I hope the mission keeps on gettin' clearer", "Whenever, wherever, OVO will always be together like Shakira"], "meaning": "Insha'Allah (إن شاء الله) is Arabic for “if God wills it.” As Drizzy continues to work on music, he hopes that his purpose becomes clear. This is a continuation of the Arab theme of the previous line as Al Jazeera is an Arabic channel.\n\nIn the following line, Drake makes a nod to Shakira’s “Whenever, Wherever”, a song about how lucky Shakira is to have found her romantic partner. Drake lets everyone know that OVO will stick together through thick and thin.", "annotation_id": "13559495", "url": "https://genius.com/13559495", "state": "accepted", "votes": 229, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Catering is from Giorgio Baldi, Robyn’s favorite Shit is nice, but I prefer Madeo Calamari rings and tomato I got the sauce and now shorties keep claimin’ preggo", "vault_lines": ["Catering is from Giorgio Baldi, Robyn's favorite", "Shit is nice, but I prefer Madeo", "Calamari rings and tomato", "I got the sauce and now shorties keep claimin' preggo"], "meaning": "Giorgio Baldi and Madeo are both high-end Italian restaurants located in the Los Angeles area. Robyn Fenty is Rihanna’s real name. Giorgio Baldi really is one of RiRi’s favorite spots—in 2012, she told Vogue she eats there six times a week when she’s in town.\n\nCalamari rings cooked in a tomato sauce is a popular Italian dish, while Prego is a brand of tomato sauce—“sauce” is slang for style and confidence. In 2017, two women claimed Drake got them pregnant but were later exposed for lying about it to try and gain popularity on social media.\n\nDrake’s been dealing with similar accusations since 2015; on “30 for 30 Freestyle,” he rapped:\n\nPaternity testin' for women that I never slept with\nI’m legally obligated if they request it", "annotation_id": "13559468", "url": "https://genius.com/13559468", "state": "accepted", "votes": 129, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "2017 I lost a J. Lo", "vault_lines": ["2017 I lost a J. Lo"], "meaning": "In late 2016, Drake and Jennifer Lopez sparked rumors about a romantic relationship between them after they collaborated and posted suggestive pictures on social media:\n\n\n\nThey were seen together over the course of a few months, though in March, J. Lo admitted that the two were only “hanging out” in a sit down with Ellen. Drake also told People Magazine that it was “never very serious.”\n\nOn “Free Smoke,” Drake rapped:\n\nI drunk text J-Lo\nOld number, so it bounce back\n\nDrake also sampled J. Lo’s 1999 song “If You Had My Love” on “Teenage Fever,” another cut from his 2017 project More Life.", "annotation_id": "13559467", "url": "https://genius.com/13559467", "state": "accepted", "votes": 167, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Shit is complex like short niggas ’round tall ladies I gotta watch who I’m talkin’ to like it’s all-ages I’ve seen buddin’ careers turn to sit around and talk about other careers, judgin’ their peers Knowledge from niggas who did not contribute to none of this here", "vault_lines": ["Shit is complex like short niggas 'round tall ladies", "I gotta watch who I'm talkin' to like it's all-ages", "I've seen buddin' careers turn to sit around and talk about other careers, judgin' their peers", "Knowledge from niggas who did not contribute to none of this here"], "meaning": "Drake is likely referring to the Complex program Everyday Struggle. On the show, DJ Akademiks and Joe Budden discuss the state of hip-hop and other rappers' careers.\n\n“Budding career” is a play on “Budden’s career”; a “budding career” is a career that has just begun. Joe started off his career with the hit single “Pump It Up.” After the song’s buzz died down, Budden struggled to chart with another single and his career lost momentum.\n\nWhen Drake dropped “4PM in Calabasas” Budden gave him props for sounding “inspired” as he was not impressed with Drake’s previous album, Views.\n\nIn 2017, a year before this track’s release, Joe  admitted to being “aggressive” on Twitter. Here, however, Drake flips it around by saying that Joe is aggressive because he has a complex. A “Napoleon complex” is a theorized condition observed in people of short stature. It is characterized by overly-aggressive or domineering social behavior to compensate for their physical shortcomings.", "annotation_id": "13559455", "url": "https://genius.com/13559455", "state": "accepted", "votes": 360, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Tired of women that may tag me in pictures airin’ my dirty laundry", "vault_lines": ["Tired of women that may tag me in pictures airin' my dirty laundry"], "meaning": "Maytag is a home appliance company that specializes in washing machines. “Dirty laundry” is an idiom referring to one’s secrets being aired out by others.\n\nDrake may also be playing on the word “tired” by pronouncing it like Tide, a laundry detergent brand.\n\n“Dirty” is also a continuation of Drake’s homage to  Ol' Dirty Bastard in the previous lines.", "annotation_id": "13559441", "url": "https://genius.com/13559441", "state": "accepted", "votes": 100, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "I be walkin’ around the Six like I invented it, boy", "vault_lines": ["I be walkin' around the Six like I invented it, boy", "I love, I love it"], "meaning": "416 and 647 are two of three area codes for the city of Toronto. Referring to Toronto as “the 6” is something local artist Jimmy Prime invented after a brainstorming meeting with Drake.\n\nTorontonians did not react positively to the term—as The Toronto Star asserted, Drake “sent [their] city of many names tail-spinning into an identity crisis.”\n\nThe “6” nickname eventually evolved into a reference to Toronto’s six boroughs.\n\n\n\nDrake has made frequent references to “the 6” in his\n music—he named a trio of songs on If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late after it (“6 God,” “6 Man,” “You & The 6”) and was even planning on naming his album Views “Views from the 6” before its 2016 release.", "annotation_id": "13559436", "url": "https://genius.com/13559436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Wakin’ up at 6 p.m. like, “Where does the day go?”", "vault_lines": ["Wakin' up at 6 p.m. like, \"Where does the day go?\""], "meaning": "Drake may be alluding to his 2015 song “6PM in New York.” Drake was born in Toronto, Canada, which he nicknamed “the 6” due to the six boroughs of the city. He has mentioned “the 6” in many of his songs over the years and has popularized the term.", "annotation_id": "13559431", "url": "https://genius.com/13559431", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "I just pulled up on Solána show, the girl’s a natural I knew her way back when Hollywood was international Yeah, the furthest I’ve been, that’s word to my ends", "vault_lines": ["I just pulled up on Solána show, the girl's a natural", "I knew her way back when Hollywood was international", "Yeah, the furthest I've been, that's word to my ends"], "meaning": "Solána Imani Rowe is SZA’s real name. On August 23, 2017, SZA met up with Drake during the Toronto show on her CTRL Tour.\n\n\n\nDrake claims to have known SZA since before he became really famous. While Hollywood used to be an “international” destination for him and was the furthest place he’d travelled to, he makes a lot of money (ends) and lives in L.A. now", "annotation_id": "13559420", "url": "https://genius.com/13559420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 141, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "They try to compare us, but Like a job straight outta high school there’s no you and I", "vault_lines": ["They try to compare us, but", "Like a job straight outta high school there's no you and I"], "meaning": "“You and I” is a homophone of “U.N.I.,” as in “university”—people who start working right after high school haven’t gone to university yet.\n\nDrake went to the Forest Hill Collegiate Institute in Toronto, Canada, but was unable to graduate and eventually dropped out of the Vaughan Road Academy. He remained a high school dropout until he finally passed in his late 20s, which he declared in a tweet.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/258731694687920130\n\nAfter Drake dropped out of high school, he pursued an acting career, which gave him a job right out of high school. His first major role as a teen actor was in the Canadian television show Degrassi. He played the character Jimmy Brooks and appeared in 145 episodes.", "annotation_id": "13559411", "url": "https://genius.com/13559411", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "Like Sanders on the Detroit Lions Get a run around and I’ll bury you where they won’t find ya", "vault_lines": ["Like Sanders on the Detroit Lions", "Get a run around and I'll bury you where they won't find ya"], "meaning": "Barry Sanders was a Hall of Fame running back for the Detroit Lions. He was widely renown as one of the greatest running backs of all time and dominated the league while he played.\n\nSanders was the kind of running back that could own an entire defense by himself. His ability to break tackles and keep plays alive is rarely ever seen.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OFB3fVFs3jk\n\nBury in the second line can also be used as a homophone for “Barry”.", "annotation_id": "13559204", "url": "https://genius.com/13559204", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "line": "(I’m the Osiris of this shit)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a sample from the intro of Wu-Tang Clan’s 1997 song “Triumph”:\n\nI’m the Osiris of this shit\n\nOsiris is the Egyptian lord of the underworld and judge of the dead. ODB thought of himself as rap’s Osiris.\n\nDrake previously sampled Wu-Tang on various songs from his 2013 album Nothing Was the Same. The album was noted as a tribute to his influences in rap.\n\nThis sample also creates a larger buzz for fans hoping for a Take Care 2 project, as he returns to his 2012 production and uses similarity in his flow.\n\nDrake also rapped this line on “Show Me a Good Time” off his debut album Thank Me Later.", "annotation_id": "13084562", "url": "https://genius.com/13084562", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diplomatic Immunity", "status": "done", "targets": 38, "clicked": 38, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-diplomatic-immunity-lyrics", "genius_title": "Diplomatic Immunity"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "I don’t want to have to go to funerals", "vault_lines": ["I don't want to have to go to funerals"], "meaning": "Drake has been known not to want to go to funerals. It make him extremely upset. He once said “RIP to one of our family members…our brother… I still can’t even believe this morning was real. It was a honor to have shared years together and I will always keep your memory alive – Forever Fif,“. This was the funeral of OVO FIf who was shot down outside of his Toronto apartment complex.", "annotation_id": "22587948", "url": "https://genius.com/22587948", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Scary whenever I close my eyes at night Wakin’ up to public statements about my private life", "vault_lines": ["Scary whenever I close my eyes at night", "Wakin’ up to public statements about my private life"], "meaning": "With the celebrity status that Drake has, he understands that the paparazzi and media press are trying to find any juicy private information about him so they can expose it to the public.\n\nHe’s had to deal with embarrassing situations revealed to the public such as sliding in a porn star’s DM’s and getting accused of pregnating a woman.", "annotation_id": "12829826", "url": "https://genius.com/12829826", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "[Verse: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "On the last verse of More Life, Drake chooses to end the project the same way he has finished every other project—one last, “tell-all” introspective verse.\n\nExamples of this: “The Ride”,  “Paris Morton Music 2”, “30 for 30”, “Views”", "annotation_id": "12741103", "url": "https://genius.com/12741103", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Silence keeps cloudin’ me Hand on my heart And I, I wish time never mattered (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Silence keeps cloudin' me", "Hand on my heart", "And I, I wish time never mattered (Yeah)"], "meaning": "This is a sample of Snoh Aalegra’s song “Time,” which released on the same day as Drake’s track.\n\nhttps://youtube.com/watch?v=eNmVCcqLvH0&t=51", "annotation_id": "12127241", "url": "https://genius.com/12127241", "state": "accepted", "votes": 207, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "I want to see my dog, but his grandmother’s his surety He can’t even dip out to see me in an emergency", "vault_lines": ["I want to see my dog, but his grandmother's his surety", "He can't even dip out to see me in an emergency"], "meaning": "A surety is someone who is responsible for supervising an accused person while they are out on bail. A surety promises an amount of money to the court by signing a bond. If the accused person (Drake’s friend, who he is referring to as his “dog”) doesn’t follow one or more of his bail conditions, his surety (Drake’s friend’s grandmother in this case) is at risk of losing some or all of the money she promised to the court. Therefore, Drake’s friend is not willing to risk having his grandmother lose her money because he violated his bail conditions by going to see Drake.", "annotation_id": "11660978", "url": "https://genius.com/11660978", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Who knows where I end up when that shit gets old? Maybe it never gets old and that’s just how it goes", "vault_lines": ["Who knows where I end up when that shit gets old?", "Maybe it never gets old and that's just how it goes"], "meaning": "Firstly,  \n\nDrake seems to question himself about what will happen to him when he’ll be older, maybe when he’ll be above the 35 years old line he designed several times:\n\n\nWhenever it be about keeping a real friendship or a brotherhood when it comes to being in touch with people like Skepta, cited in 2015’s interview grant to the Fader.\n\n\n\nYou don’t get that too much in this thing that we’re in, honestly. You don’t [often] meet somebody and actually feel like, ‘OK, we might actually still talk when we’re 35, 40 years old.\n\n\nOr when the time to start watching back to your legacy has come, when the whole game definitely watch you as a Hall Of Famer with no discussion.\n\n\n\nI’m assumin' everybody’s 35 and under\nThat’s when I plan to retire, man, it’s already funded\n\nSecondly  \n\nHe looks much selfless about his legacy as if time won’t affects his work. Or if it has to do so, he won’t be bothered, because, like Pac once said, “ Life goes On ”, and that’s not the type of thing you can have a touch on.", "annotation_id": "11649352", "url": "https://genius.com/11649352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Yeah, ducked a lot of spiteful moves I was a angry yute while I was writin’ Views Saw a side of myself that I just never knew I’ll probably self-destruct if I ever lose, but I never do", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, ducked a lot of spiteful moves", "I was a angry yute while I was writin’ Views", "Saw a side of myself that I just never knew", "I’ll probably self-destruct if I ever lose, but I never do"], "meaning": "“Angry yute” is a Jamaican pronunciation of the phrase “angry youth,” which is used to describe a defiant adolescent that displays aggression. It is commonly used in areas with significant Caribbean immigrant populations, such as Toronto. Drake was in the midst of a rap beef with Meek Mill, and facing heavy speculation for using ghostwriters, while making Views. The drama affected how the rapper began to write music.\n\nIn an interview with DJ Semtex, Drake described his experience recording the album:\n\nTo be completely honest with you I was having trouble figuring myself out in rap at the time. I was a very defensive individual. You know, just coming off the situations that I had come off of. I was having trouble trying to make rap music where I could peel back the layers. Every time I would make a song, 40…would be like ‘man you sound like defensive or aggressive.’\n\nEventually, Drake overcame his struggles by including “music that made [him] happy” on the album. The process of writing Views helped him get past the spiteful attacks from Meek Mill and the rest of the industry.", "annotation_id": "11556082", "url": "https://genius.com/11556082", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Stylin’ though", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drizzy opens with a call back to Biggie’s classic track, “What’s Beef”:\n\nThat nigga from the Brook just be\nWilin' on you, just be stylin' on you\n\nDrake is a big fan of Biggie and recalling “What’s Beef” segues into the following lines that address his beef with Meek Mill.\n\n“Stylin, though,” is Drake’s way of saying he’s still flourishing financially and in the rap game.", "annotation_id": "11553120", "url": "https://genius.com/11553120", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Steady doin’ double shifts", "vault_lines": ["Steady doin' double shifts"], "meaning": "This could be a reference to Drake doing back to back tours: Summer Sixteen Tour (July 20th, 2016 – October 8th, 2016), followed by The Boy Meets World Tour (January 28th, 2017 – March 28th, 2017). Many artists take big breaks between tours, whereas Drake decided he wanted to put in the extra effort to please more of his fans.\n\nIt could also be alludes to his work ethic, having dropped 4 full projects in the space of 25 months:\n\n\n\nIf You’re Reading This, It’s Too Late (February, 2015)\n\nWhat a Time to Be Alive (September, 2015)\n\nViews (April, 2016)\n\nMore Life (March, 2017)", "annotation_id": "11546663", "url": "https://genius.com/11546663", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Thirty-seater plane for like ten of us", "vault_lines": ["Thirty-seater plane for like ten of us"], "meaning": "Drake is boasting about his ability to fly private here and not even need to fill up the entire plane. Before getting his own jumbo jet, he would fly on 30 seater planes, even if his entourage at that moment only had ten people.\n\nThe flex is even more impressive when you consider how expensive each private plane flight is (sometimes as much as $100,000).\n\nPopcaan made some short-videos when he boarded Drake’s plane on Boy Meets World Tour:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zdj0alaPPB8", "annotation_id": "11544376", "url": "https://genius.com/11544376", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "1da doin’ the beat and I open up like a double click", "vault_lines": ["1da doin’ the beat and I open up like a double click"], "meaning": "Boi-1da has been a long-time collaborator of Drake’s, going as far back as his February 2006 mixtape, Room For Improvement, where he produced two tracks. He has produced some of Drake’s most reflective songs; for example, November 2013’s “Pound Cake” and February 2015’s “6PM in New York.”\n\nNow, Drake is teaming up with Boi-1da on another introspective cut where he opens up to his listeners—much like when one double clicks on a folder to open its contents on a computer.", "annotation_id": "11541576", "url": "https://genius.com/11541576", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "I can never sleep ‘til mornin’ on all my quiet nights But you can rest assured that my mind is right", "vault_lines": ["I can never sleep 'til mornin' on all my quiet nights", "But you can rest assured that my mind is right"], "meaning": "Drake has mentioned his lack of sleep prior to More Life. On “9,” he raps:\n\nAnd I can’t sleep these days unless I take one\n\nIn the past, Drake has used these quiet hours to drop tracks like “Days In The East” or to reflect and write songs like “5AM in Toronto.”\n\nThere’s also a bit of a play on words when he says “rest assured.”", "annotation_id": "11541522", "url": "https://genius.com/11541522", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "I take a glass of Domina to go with me home", "vault_lines": ["I take a glass of Domina to go with me home"], "meaning": "Domina is a type of red wine commonly found in Germany. Drake is known for name-dropping different types of wine in his verses. The word “Domina” is Latin for “Lady,” so he could also be eluding to taking a lady home with him.", "annotation_id": "11540997", "url": "https://genius.com/11540997", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "All the things I know, I still been silent though", "vault_lines": ["All the things I know, I still been silent though"], "meaning": "In an interview with DJ Semtex, Drake acknowledged that other hip-hop artists use writers and reference tracks more extensively than he ever has. However, he declined to call people out by name, stating:\n\nIf I was like an evil spirit, if I had like a different agenda, I could sit here and tell you how the shit really works. I could sit here and tell you 10, 20 people that are worse than me…that literally take everything and it’s just a verbatim process. But I’m not like that.", "annotation_id": "11540472", "url": "https://genius.com/11540472", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "More blessings because I’m generous", "vault_lines": ["More blessings because I'm generous"], "meaning": "Drake feels like he’s getting more blessings because of his generosity which comes in the form of global exposure and physical gifts.\n\nRecently Drake has been appearing on songs with new artists and subsequently boosting their fame. This includes the likes of Fetty Wap, Dave, Giggs, Skepta, and 21 Savage.\n\nHe’s also given out expensive gifts lately—a red Ferrari to 21 and a $100,000 necklace to J-Lo. All of his acts of kindness have in turn boosted his good karma.", "annotation_id": "11540463", "url": "https://genius.com/11540463", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Last table left in Carbone, callin’ plays on the rotary phone", "vault_lines": ["Last table left in Carbone, callin' plays on the rotary phone"], "meaning": "Carbone is one of New York’s most acclaimed and tough-to-get-into restaurants. It’s been a dining destination for the Obama family on multiple occasions.\n\nEater offered some additional background on this line:\n\nDrake has, in fact, actually eaten at Carbone — both the one in New York and the one in Las Vegas. He celebrated his birthday with food from the restaurant two years in a row. For his 29th birthday in 2015, Drake hosted a party at the Carbone in Las Vegas at the Aria hotel. In 2016, the artist had his 30th birthday catered by Carbone. Dishes like spicy rigatoni, veal parm, and the Caesar alla ZZ were served. He and Rihanna were also spotted at the restaurant’s outpost in New York last August while they were dating.\n\nWith the line, “callin' plays on a rotary phone”, Drake compares his status as the ‘play-caller’ for his OVO team to sideline coaches and managers who use a rotary phone to call up to the supporting staff in a stadium to pick a play. The line serves as a set-up to Drake suggesting he needs to move his OVO Fest to the Dome.", "annotation_id": "11540322", "url": "https://genius.com/11540322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Last verse that I gotta do is always like surgery Always tryin’ to let go of anything that’ll burden me That’s the reason you can feel the tension and the urgency Last chance I get to make sure that you take it personally Take this shit to heart, it’s always executed perfectly", "vault_lines": ["Last verse that I gotta do is always like surgery", "Always tryin' to let go of anything that'll burden me", "That's the reason you can feel the tension and the urgency", "Last chance I get to make sure that you take it personally", "Take this shit to heart, it's always executed perfectly"], "meaning": "“Surgery” refers to taking his time to perfect this verse, but to also alleviate the burdens he mentions throughout the song.\n\nClosing an album with an introspective final track to give fans an intimate look at his psyche is one of Drake’s signatures. Examples of these tracks include, “Fear,” “Thank Me Now,” “The Ride,” “Pound Cake/Paris Morton Music 2,” “6PM in New York,” “30 for 30 Freestyle,” and “Views.”", "annotation_id": "11540207", "url": "https://genius.com/11540207", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "All the girls I know are there with E and Tho", "vault_lines": ["All the girls I know are there with E and Tho"], "meaning": "“E and Tho” is referring to DJ Eric and Big Tho, radio and strip club DJs from Houston.\n\nDrake previously mentioned Eric on 2015’s “Company”:\n\nGotta come through quick, quick\nOh you at the club cashin' out with Eric", "annotation_id": "11540197", "url": "https://genius.com/11540197", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "My life is centered ’round competition and currency", "vault_lines": ["My life is centered 'round competition and currency"], "meaning": "At the time of this songs release, Drake was at the top of the rap game and had competition everywhere; from his feud with Meek Mill, to his subliminal battle with Jay Z.\n\nAs for currency, with a net worth of about $90 million according to Forbes, he is looking to continue and make more money with More Life.", "annotation_id": "11533748", "url": "https://genius.com/11533748", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Get no sick days, I leave for like three months in six days Never stick around to see shit change", "vault_lines": ["Get no sick days, I leave for like three months in six days", "Never stick around to see shit change"], "meaning": "Drake’s busy schedule keeps him on the road and prevents him from seeing things change.\n\nFor example, last year, Drake co-headlined the Summer Sixteen Tour, alongside, Future. It began on July 20, 2016 in Austin, and concluded with its final show in Drake’s hometown, Toronto, on October 8, 2016. Just a few months later, Drake began his Boy Meets World Tour in January 28, 2017, in Amsterdam. The tour concluded on March 29, 2017. In the midst of the tour, he dropped More Life.\n\nIronically, after this song was released, Drake had to cancel one of his shows in Amsterdam due to food poisoning.", "annotation_id": "11533745", "url": "https://genius.com/11533745", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "There’s real ones around me, I want to make sure they learn from me", "vault_lines": ["There's real ones around me, I want to make sure they learn from me"], "meaning": "Drake pulls back and realizes bickering with other artists isn’t nearly as important as helping those who he loves. Drake, being one of the most accomplished artists of his generation, definitely has a lot of knowledge he could discharge.\n\nThe OVO/Reps Up crew that surrounds Drake is full of up-and-coming artists that he believes in like Roy Woods, dvsn, P Reign, and PARTYNEXTDOOR. Instead of worrying about artists who have rejected his tutelage, Drake wants to focus on the guys in his camp who deserve to succeed.", "annotation_id": "11533386", "url": "https://genius.com/11533386", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "I gotta start sleepin’ at the studio I don’t have no time to be no Romeo", "vault_lines": ["I gotta start sleepin' at the studio", "I don't have no time to be no Romeo"], "meaning": "Drake is known for sleeping at the studio, but maybe he hasn’t been doing it as much as he used to:\n\n\n\nRomeo is one of the principal characters in the archetypal romance Romeo & Juliet. His name is often synonymous with the “perfect” lover, since his love for Juliet was pure and honest.", "annotation_id": "11533183", "url": "https://genius.com/11533183", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Dissin’ but got pictures with me smilin’ though All the things you need, you still want problems though", "vault_lines": ["Dissin' but got pictures with me smilin' though", "All the things you need, you still want problems though"], "meaning": "Drake calls back to his beef with Meek Mill. Although they used to be friends, support each other’s music, and even go on tour together, Meek still came for Drake.\n\n\n\nIn the second line, Drake’s implying that Meek still needs money, fame, popularity, and girls, and that he doesn’t understand why Meek would want another “problem” when he’s already got so many already.", "annotation_id": "11533130", "url": "https://genius.com/11533130", "state": "accepted", "votes": 168, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "All the love I need is at the rodeo All the love I need is here at OVO, yeah", "vault_lines": ["All the love I need is at the rodeo", "All the love I need is here at OVO, yeah"], "meaning": "RodeoHouston is a famous event that takes place in Houston, Texas. Drake showed love for Houston as early as his 2009 mixtape So Far Gone, which had various Houston influences, including songs like “November 18th” and “Houstatlantavegas” that praised the city’s culture.\n\n\n\nThis may also be a shoutout to Travis Scott, since his debut album was titled Rodeo. Travis appears later in the More Life tracklist on “Portland,” and also featured on “Company” from Drake’s 2015 mixtape If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late.", "annotation_id": "11532873", "url": "https://genius.com/11532873", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Went from Club Palazzo in the Bridge to Club LIV To not even showin’ up at a club ‘less we doin’ biz’ I can’t even party while a nigga pursuin’ this", "vault_lines": ["Went from Club Palazzo in the Bridge to Club LIV", "To not even showin' up at a club 'less we doin' biz'", "I can't even party while a nigga pursuin' this"], "meaning": "Club Palazzo is located in Woodbrige aka “the bridge,” a suburb in Toronto primarily home to Italians.\n\nClub LIV is a famous club in Miami Beach. Drake has shouted out LIV on many of his songs like “305 to My City.”\n\nhttps://twitter.com/LIVmiami/status/843272270877548545", "annotation_id": "11532872", "url": "https://genius.com/11532872", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Remember when I bought Sealey the fake Chanel wallet She knew that shit was a fraud, but never told me about it Nowadays when we catchin’ up, we just laugh about it Can’t describe what my life is like when she asks about it", "vault_lines": ["Saw a side of myself that I just never knew", "Remember when I bought Sealey the fake Chanel wallet", "She knew that shit was a fraud, but never told me about it", "Nowadays when we catchin' up, we just laugh about it", "Can't describe what my life is like when she asks about it"], "meaning": "“Sealy” refers to Leanne Sealey—Drake’s spoken of her before on 2011’s “Club Paradise”:\n\nThey say that all your old girls got somebody new\nI said, “Damn, really? Even Rose Mary? Even Leanne Sealey?\n\nThis may be calling back to the album’s opener, “Free Smoke” where Drake raps:\n\nI couldn’t get a bill paid\nYou couldn’t buy the real thing", "annotation_id": "11532703", "url": "https://genius.com/11532703", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "More Life", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake ends his verse with the title of the playlist, More Life.\n\nDrake has a pattern of referencing the name of the projects on the last song of the project. He’s done this before on songs like “Thank Me Now” off Thank Me Later, “The Ride” off Take Care, and “Paris Morton Music” off Nothing Was The Same. Drake ends the last verse of the last song (on the regular version) with the title of each respective work (or close to it in the case of Thank Me Later).\n\nIn the case of the album Views, Drake ended the album with a song titled “Views,” once again alluding to the name of the album.", "annotation_id": "11532638", "url": "https://genius.com/11532638", "state": "accepted", "votes": 232, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Takin’ summer off, ’cause they tell me I need recovery Maybe gettin’ back to my regular life will humble me I’ll be back 2018 to give you the summary", "vault_lines": ["Takin' summer off, 'cause they tell me I need recovery", "Maybe gettin' back to my regular life will humble me", "I'll be back 2018 to give you the summary"], "meaning": "In the last few lines of More Life, Drake leaves listeners guessing, because he was rumored to have three releases in 2017.\n\nDrake began 2018 with a bang, dropping the Scary Hours EP that included “God’s Plan,” which broke numerous records, such as topping both Apple Music and Spotify’s single-day streams standings.\n\nJust 5 months later, Drake also released his fifth studio album, Scorpion. The project was 25 songs long, and included “God’s Plan” along with singles “Nice For What” and “I’m Upset.” Drake split the album in half with a rap side and an R&B side to please different sections of his fanbase.", "annotation_id": "11532467", "url": "https://genius.com/11532467", "state": "accepted", "votes": 402, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Yeah, used to be at SilverCity, Indigo Used to be in lunchroom, playin’ dominoes", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, used to be at SilverCity, Indigo", "Used to be in lunchroom, playin’ dominoes"], "meaning": "Drake’s reminiscing about his youth. SilverCity is a line of large Cineplex movie theaters, while Indigo is a popular Canadian bookstore chain. The stores are often positioned near each other in shopping plazas.\n\nThe specific locations Drake’s referring to are likely in Yonge-Eglinton, the neighborhood in Toronto just around the corner from where Drake grew up. There are similar locations, however, at Yorkdale Mall and in Richmond Hill.", "annotation_id": "11532359", "url": "https://genius.com/11532359", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Distractions will do you in, in the truest sense Especially people that want to lecture me And frame it like they just want the best for me Or they check for me, whatever splits it up So there’s more for them and there’s less for me They don’t know they got to be faster than me to get to me No one’s done it successfully", "vault_lines": ["Distractions will do you in, in the truest sense", "Especially people that want to lecture me", "And frame it like they just want the best for me", "Or they check for me, whatever splits it up", "So there's more for them and there's less for me", "They don't know they got to be faster than me to get to me", "No one's done it successfully"], "meaning": "Drake takes aim at people who pretend to be friends and family to him, all while trying to undermine him.\n\nThis might be a shot at Joe Budden, whose purported to be a longtime Drake fan, but criticized Drake’s last album, Views, on his podcast by calling it “uninspired.” Budden’s comments lead to a short-lived feud with Drake.\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake has talked about people shaving money off the top. He also mentioned it in his verse on The Game’s “100”:\n\nHad niggas tell me to my face how we were family\nAnd how they love me while they were skimmin' off the budget\nNow, when I see ‘em, they’re the ones that’s actin’ funny", "annotation_id": "11531948", "url": "https://genius.com/11531948", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Get lil’ updates, texts in my inboxes have been poppin’ Seasons go by like I’m binge-watchin’", "vault_lines": ["Get lil' updates, texts in my inboxes have been poppin'", "Seasons go by like I'm binge-watchin'"], "meaning": "Drake compares the updates he gets from friends to watching a whole “season” of a TV show at one time. Binge-watching refers to a person watching episodes of a TV show in succession. Because of this, the seasons pass by quickly, which Drake compares to his life.\n\nDrake is also using the line as a double entendre, referring to both the seasons in a TV show and the seasons in a year.", "annotation_id": "11531912", "url": "https://genius.com/11531912", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "7 a.m. in Germany, can’t believe that they heard of me", "vault_lines": ["7 a.m. in Germany, can't believe that they heard of me"], "meaning": "Drake toured Europe during the build-up and release of More Life. On March 9 & 10, Drake performed shows in Germany, where he stated he was “finishing the playlist in his hotel room,” suggesting this track was recorded on that date.\n\nDrake also has several “time in location” songs in his AM-PM Series, including “9 AM in Dallas,” “5 AM in Toronto,” “6 PM in New York,” and most recently, “4 PM in Calabasas.”", "annotation_id": "11531633", "url": "https://genius.com/11531633", "state": "accepted", "votes": 179, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "Might move our annual shit to the ’Dome I need forty-thousand people to see what I’m on", "vault_lines": ["Might move our annual shit to the 'Dome", "I need forty-thousand people to see what I'm on"], "meaning": "Drake’s debating moving his annual OVO Fest to Toronto’s Rogers Centre which was previously—and is still quite stubbornly by many Torontonians—called the SkyDome. It holds 50,000 people (40,000 when set-up for a music performance) and would let more fans make it to the show. It has been used for other artists such as Beyoncé.", "annotation_id": "11531590", "url": "https://genius.com/11531590", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "line": "If we do a song, it’s like takin’ my kids to work with me You overnight celebrity, you one day star Swear I told you that I’m in this bitch for eternity I am a reflection of all of your insecurities Behind closed doors, a lot of 6 God worshiping Done talk now, ‘cause there’s other shit that’s concernin’ me", "vault_lines": ["If we do a song, it's like takin' my kids to work with me", "You overnight celebrity, you one day star", "Swear I told you that I'm in this bitch for eternity", "I am a reflection of all of your insecurities", "Behind closed doors, a lot of 6 God worshiping", "Done talk now, 'cause there's other shit that's concernin' me"], "meaning": "This shot seems to be aimed at Tory Lanez.  Drake has “sonned” Tory Lanez musically, so if they were to do a song together, it would be just like taking his kid to work with him.\n\nDrake flips Lanez’s government name of Daystar Peterson—as well as the titles of his debut album I Told You and track “One Day”—into insults about the rapper’s lack of staying power and personal insecurities about success.\n\nThe two have been trading thinly-veiled jabs for years. Lanez said that Drake’s nickname for Toronto, “The 6,” was not cool, Drake used Lanez’s flow to call out new Toronto rappers on “Summer Sixteen,” and Lanez made fun of Drake’s OVO owl logo on “Drive You Crazy.”\n\n\n\nFurthermore, the following line:\n\nBehind closed doors, a lot of 6 God worshipping\n\nmay refer to Meek Mill publicly singing “Fake Love” in a club. Although the scene does not occur in a private area (behind closed doors), it still emphasizes how one of Drake’s worst enemies still showed him love:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z1EgWwd_KlE", "annotation_id": "11531245", "url": "https://genius.com/11531245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 235, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Do Not Disturb", "status": "done", "targets": 34, "clicked": 34, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-do-not-disturb-lyrics", "genius_title": "Do Not Disturb"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "[Part II]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This where shit gets crazy", "annotation_id": "39266220", "url": "https://genius.com/39266220", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Nigga", "vault_lines": ["Niggas usually send they verses back to me and they be terrible, just like a two-year-old", "Like a kid that act bad from January to November, nigga, it's just you and Cole", "Niggas so thirsty to put me in beef", "To them niggas that say they wan' off us, you better be talkin' 'bout workin' in cubicles", "Lot of niggas debatin' my número", "You niggas still takin' pictures on a Gulfstream", "Niggas talkin' 'bout when this gon' be repeated", "Nigga, beat it, nigga, beat it, what?"], "meaning": "This is how J. Cole reacted when Drake made the producer switch the beat.", "annotation_id": "36218237", "url": "https://genius.com/36218237", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Ayy, I’m ’bout to, I’m ’bout to I’m ’bout to, yeah Yeah", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, I'm 'bout to, I'm 'bout to", "I'm 'bout to, yeah", "I'm 'bout to click out on this shit", "I'm 'bout to click, woah"], "meaning": "nervous drizzy", "annotation_id": "35684152", "url": "https://genius.com/35684152", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Girl gave me some head because I need it", "vault_lines": ["Girl gave me some head because I need it"], "meaning": "Throughout the verse, Drake tries to flex that he is the master of everything.\n\nI told Jimmy Jam I use a GRAMMY as a door stop\n\nDrake flexes over his prestige showing that he doesn’t care about trophies since he got so many of them.\n\nMan, if your pub was up for sale, I buy the whole thing\nDrake flexes that he’s rich.\n\nIn this line there is a connection to the previous Drake lines in this verse\n\nMan, I pack ‘em in this phone like some sardines\nAnd they send me naked pictures, it’s the small things\n\nDrake flexes here that he’s got women that do whatever he wants.", "annotation_id": "32220324", "url": "https://genius.com/32220324", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Everybody steppers, well, fuck it, then everybody breakfast and I’m ’bout to clear up my plate (Huh, huh, huh)", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, I'm 'bout to, I'm 'bout to", "I'm 'bout to, yeah", "I'm 'bout to click, woah"], "meaning": "Everybody steppers\n\nCole suggests that everyone views themselves as steppers. Meaning everyone thinks they are the best.\n\nwell, fuck it, then everybody breakfast and I’m ‘bout to clear up my plate (Huh, huh, huh)\n\nCole uses here the breakfast metaphor to show his greatness over the other rappers who think they are steppers.\n\nThis line can also be seen as a sneak diss to Kendrick Lamar. “steppers” might refer to Kendrick Lamar’s latest album “Mr.Morale and The Big Steppers”.\n\nConnecting this analogy with the following lines in the song also supports the theory that Cole is trying to show in this line that he’s better than Kendrick.\n\nLove when they argue the hardest MC\nIs it K-Dot? Is it Aubrey? Or me?\nWe the big three like we started a league, but right now, I feel like Muhammad Ali\nHuh, yeah, yeah, huh-huh, yeah, Muhammad Ali\n…\nThe Spider-Man meme is me lookin' at Drake", "annotation_id": "32199750", "url": "https://genius.com/32199750", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "But the difference is it’s just two guys playin’ shit that they did in the studio", "vault_lines": ["But the difference is it's just two guys playin' shit that they did in the studio"], "meaning": "I interpreted this in two different ways.\n\n\nInstead of two teams versing like in the Super Bowl they are just two guys playing around listening to their music in the studio.\nThey aren’t preforming or trying to hard because it all comes naturally to them. Hence the playing stuff that they did in the studio. No big dramatics like the usual superbowl performances.", "annotation_id": "31369648", "url": "https://genius.com/31369648", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "[Verse 2: J. Cole]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Spited broke down the rhyme scheme to J. Cole’s verse in this video:\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OzZAYvQvycU", "annotation_id": "31328088", "url": "https://genius.com/31328088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Rhymin’ with me is the biggest mistake", "vault_lines": ["Rhymin' with me is the biggest mistake"], "meaning": "Cole is acknowledging his insane rhyme schemes in his lyrics. An example of his insane rhyme schemes can be seen on The Secret Recipe as he maintains a three-syllabic rhyme scheme throughout the whole verse.\nhttps://genius.com/Lil-yachty-and-j-cole-the-secret-recipe-lyrics", "annotation_id": "31233853", "url": "https://genius.com/31233853", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "And if I fuck with you, then after, I might eat it, what?", "vault_lines": ["And if I fuck with you, then after, I might eat it, what?", "Beat it, what? Beat it, what? Beat it, what? Beat it, what?"], "meaning": "This is a double entendre. “Fuck with you” can be interpreted as getting along with someone, but also literally having sex with someone.. which continues the sexual pattern that these 2 bars have started", "annotation_id": "31172732", "url": "https://genius.com/31172732", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Mystique", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Ever since NBA Youngboy dissed Cole a lot of people (on twitter reddit etc.) have reached so hard, creating more drama and theories since Cole hasn’t responded to anything regarding that diss.\n\nEven though he’s upset at people making these things up, at the same time he finds it humorous & intriguing so he’s letting it slide cause it’s not true.", "annotation_id": "31072715", "url": "https://genius.com/31072715", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "I’m lettin’ it rock ’cause I love the", "vault_lines": ["I'm lettin' it rock 'cause I love the"], "meaning": "“I’m lettin' it Rock ‘cause I love the Mystique” could be a reference to “The Rock Mystique” phenomenon that has surrounded the Slippery Rock University NCAA Division II football program since 1936, giving them a cult following.\n\nWith a debate as to who should be crowned 1936 college football national champion, one columnist made a facetious case for Slippery Rock. His column was picked up around the country, leading to a buzz of interest in the small school. Boston University subsequently scheduled the Slippery Rock State Teachers' College for a 1937 match in Fenway Park, which was the first time Slippery Rock traveled outside of the Western Pennsylvania tri-state region for a game.\n\nIn 1959, the University of Michigan began including Slippery Rock football results in their public address announcements. It began as a gag but turned into a lasting tradition. In 1979, Michigan hosted a Slippery Rock vs. Shippensburg regular season game, drawing a crowd of over 60,000 – an NCAA Division II football attendance record.\n\nSources: https://www.ncaa.com/news/football/article/2020-10-27/slippery-rock-footballs-history-michigan-explained\nhttps://rockathletics.com/news/2020/11/19/thursday-football-feature.aspx\nhttps://rockathletics.com/news/2004/3/25/FB_032504aab_650.aspx", "annotation_id": "30115429", "url": "https://genius.com/30115429", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "I really hate that you been sellin’ them some false dreams", "vault_lines": ["I really hate that you been sellin' them some false dreams"], "meaning": "When he says, “I really hate that you have been sellin' them some false dreams,” he means that he strongly dislikes the fact that this person has been misleading or deceiving the young individuals he cares about.\n\nDrake is likely protective of his younger friends or proteges and doesn’t appreciate someone leading them astray with unrealistic promises or unattainable goals. He’s calling out the person for not being honest or sincere in their interactions with these young individuals, emphasizing the importance of providing guidance and support based on reality rather than false hopes and unattainable dreams. This line reflects his concern for the well-being and future prospects of the people he’s looking out for.", "annotation_id": "29841151", "url": "https://genius.com/29841151", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Can’t trust everything that you saw on IG", "vault_lines": ["Can't trust everything that you saw on IG"], "meaning": "In this context, J. Cole criticizes individuals who believe everything they see on the networks (including false IG template news). Similarly, in an interview with Kevin hart, he admits that he no longer uses social media because it would disrupt his artistic process.J Cole is speaking the truth about self control and the addiction of social media. Social media is turning into a toxic and harmful substance for the younger generations\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3xicjEoJUj8&ab_channel=Peacock", "annotation_id": "29840125", "url": "https://genius.com/29840125", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "The GOAT with the golden pen, the top toucher", "vault_lines": ["Who the GOAT? Who the GOAT? Who the GOAT?", "The GOAT with the golden pen, the top toucher"], "meaning": "here, we can understand that j. cole is the one who delivers the best raps because he has the best pen. we can also say that since he delivers the best, he’s the one who touches us the most.", "annotation_id": "29840123", "url": "https://genius.com/29840123", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "With The Boy in the status, a stadium", "vault_lines": ["With The Boy in the status, a stadium"], "meaning": "The Boy is one of Drake’s aliases and when Drake is in town he sells out stadiums. On November 11, 2023, Drake announced a second tour titled, “It’s All A Blur Tour: Big As The What?” via social media. This second leg of the tour plans to bring along J. Cole for the ride and to hit some arenas not featured in the first leg of the tour which spans from January 18th — March 27th", "annotation_id": "29832477", "url": "https://genius.com/29832477", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Don’t even pay me back on none them favors, I don’t need it", "vault_lines": ["Don't even pay me back on none them favors, I don't need it"], "meaning": "This is directed to multiple rappers, could also be for Young Thug’s latest album “Business Is Business” as Drake posted “You owe me this time” after the album’s release.", "annotation_id": "29827464", "url": "https://genius.com/29827464", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "I click the trigger on the stick like a high beam", "vault_lines": ["I click the trigger on the stick like a high beam"], "meaning": "Drake creates a similar comparing the trigger of a gun (stick), to the control knob for the headlights in a car.  Usually, a person pushes the knob (forward or backward) to turn on the high beams.", "annotation_id": "29772476", "url": "https://genius.com/29772476", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Yeah, them boys had it locked, but I knew the code", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, them boys had it locked, but I knew the code"], "meaning": "Cole uses a metaphor to describe his entrance into the rap game. “them boys had it locked” refers to the negated access. “But I knew the code” references his knowledge to having access to the “locked” contents.", "annotation_id": "29771467", "url": "https://genius.com/29771467", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "I’m ’bout to click out on this shit I’m ’bout to click, woah I’m ’bout to click out on this shit I’m ’bout to click, woah I’m down to click out on you hoes and make a crime scene", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, I'm 'bout to, I'm 'bout to", "I'm 'bout to, yeah", "I'm 'bout to click out on this shit", "I'm 'bout to click, woah", "I'm down to click out on you hoes and make a crime scene"], "meaning": "This ties into the title of which is labeled first person shooter, which is a popular video genre where you have to click to shoot.\n\nDrake  is basically making it clear that he’s okay with shooting anyone against him. This could also be and more likely is him saying he is okay with firing shots at anyone in a dissing sense.", "annotation_id": "29763498", "url": "https://genius.com/29763498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Man, if your pub was up for sale, I buy the whole thing", "vault_lines": ["Man, if your pub was up for sale, I buy the whole thing"], "meaning": "Drake often flexes his wealth. In this example, he claims he’ll buy your pub, in which he is likely referring to as your “publishing”. Publishing rights refer to the actual music composition and are responsible for ensuring that songwriters and composers receive payment when their compositions are used commercially. Owning your publishing means you control all the rights to your music.\n\nAn example of how owning someone else’s publishing can be used as revenge can be seen with Michael Jackson and Eminem. Michael Jackson was so upset Eminem dissed him (on the song “Just lose it”)  he used his partnership company Sony/ATV Famous Music (the company that owned the rapper’s music) & bought the catalog for a staggering $370 million. That means Jackson continued to receive paid royalties on Eminem’s entire catalog until he died in 2009.\n\nAccording to Boss Hunting, Eminem bought the publishing rights back for his music in 2016.\n\nDrake can likely pull this move. Especially on rappers whose catalogs are not as extensive as his.", "annotation_id": "29762606", "url": "https://genius.com/29762606", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Man, I was Bentley wheel whippin’ when I was nineteen", "vault_lines": ["Man, I was Bentley wheel whippin' when I was nineteen"], "meaning": "Drake used to rent a Bentley for $5K/month when he was up-and-coming at 19 years old to “keep up appearances”\n\nhttps://www.complex.com/music/a/b-blanchet/drake-gifted-rolls-royce-phantom-used-to-rent-keep-up-appearances\n\nNow, he looks back and brags about how pushing a Bentley is old news, but at the time, this was major expense that ultimately lead to his success.", "annotation_id": "29755794", "url": "https://genius.com/29755794", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "The spot rusher, sprayed his whole shit up, the crop duster", "vault_lines": ["The spot rusher, sprayed his whole shit up, the crop duster"], "meaning": "A crop duster is an aircraft used for dusting or spraying large acreages with pesticides.\n\n\n\nThe line means he sprays someone with his gun the same way a crop duster sprays pesticides.", "annotation_id": "29742707", "url": "https://genius.com/29742707", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Will they ever give me flowers? Well, of course not They don’t wanna have that talk, ’cause it’s a sore spot They know The Boy, the one they gotta boycott", "vault_lines": ["Will they ever give me flowers? Well, of course not", "They don't wanna have that talk, 'cause it's a sore spot", "They know The Boy, the one they gotta boycott"], "meaning": "Drake is expressing how people are too caught up in the “G.O.A.T” debate to give him praise for what he’s been able to accomplish. He feels that no matter how much he proves and gives to the world, it will never be enough to satisfy them and solidify his position as the greatest.\nSaying it’s a ‘sore spot’ is saying that people tend to get butt-hurt when the numbers and accolades contradict them saying he’s not the greatest. They  don’t want it to be true.", "annotation_id": "29740119", "url": "https://genius.com/29740119", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Hate how the game got away from the bars, man, this shit like a prison escape", "vault_lines": ["Hate how the game got away from the bars, man, this shit like a prison escape"], "meaning": "Double play on “bars”. Cole is comparing how rappers these days getting away from bars and lyricism are like a prison escape because when you are in prison you are behind bars.", "annotation_id": "29736863", "url": "https://genius.com/29736863", "state": "pending", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "(Pew, pew-pew) First-person shooter mode, we turnin’ your song to a funeral", "vault_lines": ["First-person shooter mode, we turnin' your song to a funeral"], "meaning": "First-person shooter (FPS) is a video game subgenre that centers gun-based combat gameplay from the first person point of view, hence the term. Examples include Activision’s Call of Duty and iD Software’s Doom franchises.\n\n\n\n Gameplay from first-person shooter game Call of Duty: Warzone.\n\n\nCole uses this shoot-and-kill gameplay as a metaphor for how he and Drake approach featuring on songs; both artists have quite a reputation for outshining or “killing” the main act when featured. Their feature stands out so greatly, effectively “turnin' [the rest of the] song to a funeral.”\n\nNote the “pew, pew, pew” sound meant to mimic gunshots from Drake’s son, Adonis Graham.", "annotation_id": "29736705", "url": "https://genius.com/29736705", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Niggas so thirsty to put me in beef Dissectin’ my words and start lookin’ too deep I look at the tweets and start suckin’ my teeth", "vault_lines": ["Niggas so thirsty to put me in beef", "Dissectin' my words and start lookin' too deep", "I look at the tweets and start suckin' my teeth"], "meaning": "Given that Drake mentioned that this song was a fourth quarter addition, these lines may be addressing the conversation that arose from J. Cole’s verse on Lil Yachty’s song, “The Secret Recipe”. When the song dropped, there was debate over whether the following lines were addressed at NBA Youngboy:\n\n“Niggas makin' threats and I laugh, that’s ‘cause you ain’t a threat\nDon’t ask how I feel 'bout no rappers, shit, they okay, I guess\nIncomin’ call, press the button, the one that say accept\nHe FaceTime to ask for a feature and saw the face of death\nI’m on your song, your streams goin' up, not quite the Drake effect\nBut don’t complain, bitch, take what you get and cut the label check”\n\nUsing these lines to clarify his stance, he later mentions in his verse:\n\n“I still wanna get me a song with YB”\n\nThis indicates that he has no ill feelings towards the Baton Rouge rapper and all speculation on Cole’s stance are unfounded.", "annotation_id": "29736647", "url": "https://genius.com/29736647", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Still in this bitch gettin’ bigger, they waitin’ on the kid to come drop like a father to be", "vault_lines": ["Still in this bitch gettin' bigger, they waitin' on the kid to come drop like a father to be"], "meaning": "Cole is hinting on how people be waiting on his upcoming album “It’s a Boy” with “waitin' on the kid to come drop”. Back in 2021, Cole shared a post on Instagram teasing the titles of his upcoming albums.\n\n\n\nCole says a “bitch” to mean a pregnant lady (you can also tell by the fact that she’s getting bigger, just like a pregnant lady) “waiting on the kid to come drop” (her having the baby) and wraps it up by being the male figure that caused he to be pregnant (a father to be).", "annotation_id": "29736239", "url": "https://genius.com/29736239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "She call my number, leave her hangin’, she got dry-cleaned", "vault_lines": ["She call my number, leave her hangin', she got dry-cleaned"], "meaning": "When you take your clothes to the “cleaners” they clean your clothes without the use of water. That’s why it called dry cleaning. Once the clothes are cleaned & ironed, the clothes are plastic wrapped and put on “hangers” until they’re ready to be shipped out, or picked up.\n\nTo leave somebody hanging is to leave them without information or an answer / response etc…\n\nSo Drake is saying when a certain girl calls him, he leaves her hanging like how dry cleaners leave clothes on hangers when they’re done with them.", "annotation_id": "29734383", "url": "https://genius.com/29734383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "You niggas still takin’ pictures on a Gulfstream", "vault_lines": ["You niggas still takin' pictures on a Gulfstream"], "meaning": "Gulfstream is a private jet popularised in hip hop lyrcis.\n\nDrake is explaining how much money, compared to other rappers, he actually has.\n\nFurthermore, he owns and flies Boeing 767 which is primarily used for commercial use because of its size and really high $$$.", "annotation_id": "29734183", "url": "https://genius.com/29734183", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "The one that they call when they shit ain’t connectin’ no more, feel like I got a job in IT", "vault_lines": ["The one that they call when they shit ain't connectin' no more, feel like I got a job in IT"], "meaning": "It’s a sublime double entendre  from Cole here;\ncontinuing with the flow from the previous line, that ‘he’s feels like' Muhammad Ali (the Greatest hitter in the game) and he’s the one they call for help when “they shit (jabs) ain’t connectin' no more” like he “got  a Job (jab) in it”, while also implying that, he’s the one they call when they ‘call’ “ain’t connectin’ …” like he “got a job in IT” (IT support).", "annotation_id": "29732198", "url": "https://genius.com/29732198", "state": "pending", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "I told Jimmy Jam I use a Grammy as a door stop", "vault_lines": ["I told Jimmy Jam I use a Grammy as a door stop"], "meaning": "Jimmy Jam is an American producer and actor who is also the father of Drake’s alleged ex-girlfriend, the then 18-year-old Bella Haris, back in 2018.\n\nAlthough winning numerous GRAMMY Awards throughout his over a decade-year career, he disregards its appeal within the music industry and opts to use them as a doorstop due to their sudden irrelevancy claims by him which he further spoke on in a November 2020 Instagram post:\n\nThis is a great time for somebody to start something new that we can build up over time and pass on to the generations to come and just accept that what was once the highest form of recognition may no longer matter to the artists that exist now and the ones that come after.\n\nOn October 6, 2023—hours after the release of Drake’s highly-anticipated FOR ALL THE DOGS—he further mocked GRAMMY by posting a picture of him using a GRAMMY as a doorstop:", "annotation_id": "29730766", "url": "https://genius.com/29730766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "To them niggas that say they wan’ off us, you better be talkin’ ‘bout workin’ in cubicles", "vault_lines": ["To them niggas that say they wan' off us, you better be talkin' 'bout workin' in cubicles"], "meaning": "This is classic double entendre.\n\nFirstly, it referrences opponents trying to take them off the rap game. At the same time, “off us” doubles as “office,” with Cole alluding that that those trying to take over his position would rather be his employees, therefore sitting at “cubicles.”\n\nFurthermore, the music video intro features actor Brian Baumgartner who famously played the character Kevin Malone in the American sitcom/mockumentary series “The Office”.\n\n\nAlthough this could be seen as a reference to a recent beef with NBA Youngboy, in response to the latter being denied a feature, Cole cleared up any drama between the two in his verse of this song.", "annotation_id": "29725881", "url": "https://genius.com/29725881", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "I love a dinner with some fine women when they start debatin’ about who the GOAT I’m like go’n ’head, say it then, who the GOAT? Who the GOAT? Who the GOAT? Who the GOAT? Who you bitches really rootin’ for?", "vault_lines": ["I love a dinner with some fine women when they start debatin' about who the GOAT", "I'm like go'n 'head, say it then, who the GOAT?", "Who the GOAT? Who the GOAT? Who the GOAT?", "Who you bitches really rootin' for?", "I'm lettin' it rock 'cause I love the"], "meaning": "Drake asks these women who they think “the GOAT,” shorthand for the “Greatest [Rapper] of All-Time,” is. While what constitutes “the GOAT” of rap is highly contentious, there are three common reasons Drake is considered worthy of the title: Commercial success, versatility, and longevity.\n\nFrom a commercial standpoint, Drake is almost objectively the greatest. Digitally, he’s the most successful artist in music history with more than 188 million singles and 33.5 million albums sold. This, of course, easily makes him the highest streamed artist of the 2010’s and the most RIAA certified digital artist of all-time. With a net worth of $250 million, if you ever see a “richest rappers ever” list, chances are Drake is towards the top of it. In fact, he even seems to consider being the GOAT secondary to his wealth in “0 to 100” where he famously rapped:\n\nI’m just here for the bucks and the billis, nigga\nDon’t make me kill one of the GOATs for it\n\nDrake’s versatility, shown in how he can switch from the moody R&B rap of his 2011 project Take Care to 2022’s house-inspired Honestly, Nevermind, is why many listeners also laud him as “the most versatile artist of all time.”\n\nFinally, Drake certainly has the longevity of a GOAT.  From his 2010 debut album Thank Me Later going No. 1 on the Billboard 100, Drake would go on to drop eight No. 1 albums with a historic 299 charting hits across a little over a decade. He even boasts about (previously) being one slot away from having more No. 1 hits than the multi-decade charting Michael Jackson at the end of the song:\n\nWhat the fuck, bro? I’m one away from Michael", "annotation_id": "29729083", "url": "https://genius.com/29729083", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Lot of niggas debatin’ my número Not the three, not the two, I’m the U-N-O Yeah Numero U-N-O", "vault_lines": ["Lot of niggas debatin' my número", "Not the three, not the two, I'm the U-N-O"], "meaning": "“Uno” translates to the number “one” from Spanish to English. J. Cole notes that he always comes #1 in any debates regarding rap.\n\nSince the 2010s, it’s been unanimously decided that J. Cole, Drake, and Kendrick Lemar are the top three rappers in mainstream Hip-Hop. The lyric foreshadows what’s to come later in the track as in the track’s third verse (Cole’s second), he raps:\n\nLove when they argue the hardest MC\nIs it K-Dot? Is it Aubrey? Or me?\nWe the big three like we started a league, but right now, I feel like Muhammed Ali\n\nIn the lyric, Cole basically anoints himself as the greatest rapper of all time, noting that he’s the best and has no competition. This isn’t new as in Benny the Butcher’s January 2022, “Johnny P’s Caddy,” Cole rapped that he is the best rapper alive:\n\nReally, no MC equal, feel me?\nCole pen be lethal, crib like an old MTV show, uh\nOn God, the best rapper alive\n\nIt’s worth noting that all of Cole’s studio albums have debuted at the #1 spot of the Billboard 200 albums chart, hence why he labels himself as “#1.”", "annotation_id": "29729001", "url": "https://genius.com/29729001", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Me and Drizzy, this shit like the Super Bowl Man, this shit damn near big as the", "vault_lines": ["Big as the Super Bowl", "Me and Drizzy, this shit like the Super Bowl", "Man, this shit damn near big as the", "Big as the what? (Ah)"], "meaning": "The Super Bowl is an annual championship game of the National Football League—or “NFL” for short—which started in 1966. J. Cole compares him and Drake to the Super Bowl due to their renowned popularity status.\n\nAlthough Cole hasn’t performed at a Super Bowl before, on February 15, 2023, Drake performed at the  2023 Super Bowl Party, performing his January 2018 track “God’s Plan” and February 2013’s “Started from the Bottom,” and many more.", "annotation_id": "29727917", "url": "https://genius.com/29727917", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "My youngins richer than you rappers and they all stream", "vault_lines": ["My youngins richer than you rappers and they all stream"], "meaning": "Drake likely shouts of popular New York Twitch streamer Kai Cenat who is one of the most subbed streamers on the site. It is estimated that Kai rakes it up to $230,000 a month through his Twitch streams alone, making an estimated $3 million annually.\n\nDrake has previously endorsed Kai heavily, with Drake even urging Twitch to pay Kai $50 million after becoming the number 2 streamer. Kai also got a special shoutout from Drake during his 2023 Tour in Atlanta.\n\nDrake later confirmed it while calling Kai during his reaction stream to the album: https://youtu.be/NyXPwgzwdJg?si=tfKxnVcIebQSaCyq&t=1866", "annotation_id": "29726302", "url": "https://genius.com/29726302", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Just know if I diss you, I’d make sure you know that I hit you like I’m on your caller ID", "vault_lines": ["Just know if I diss you, I'd make sure you know that I hit you like I'm on your caller ID"], "meaning": "Whenever Cole addresses someone in a song, he leaves no room to question who it’s about. For instance, when he recounted an altercation he had with hip hop icon Puff Daddy on “l e t . g o . m y . h a n d” from his 2021 project The Off-Season, Cole mentions him by name:\n\nMy last scrap was with Puff Daddy, who would’ve thought it?\nI bought that nigga album in seventh grade and played it so much\nYou would’ve thought my favorite rapper was Puff\n\nThe two have since publicly reconciled. When Cole infamously critiqued the advocacy rhetoric of rapper Noname on “Snow on tha Bluff” back in June 2020, she immediately responded with “Song 33,” where she essentially dismissed Cole’s input. To that, Cole simply tweeted a link to her song. Later on, Noname would apologize for her response, saying her “ego got the best of [her.]”\n\nMost famously, “1985,” the final track of Cole’s 2018 project KOD, is widely understood to be a direct analysis of rapper Lil Pump. Pump had gone viral for randomly shouting “fuck J. Cole” on a leaked diss track from 2018. Cole, likely seeing this as a publicity stunt, replied by genuinely trying to level with Pump and warn him about the trajectory of his career. Cole would even try and spark more fruitful discourse by having a joint interview with Pump at The Sheltuh about both tracks.\n\nIn the same way that the caller ID in smartphones makes it explicitly clear who’s calling, Cole has gone out of his way to make it explicitly clear who his songs are about and even push for resolution.", "annotation_id": "29726084", "url": "https://genius.com/29726084", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "When I show up, it’s motion picture blockbuster", "vault_lines": ["When I show up, it's motion picture blockbuster"], "meaning": "Cole used a very similar rhyme scheme a while back on “Folgers Crystals” from Dreamville’s 2015 collaboration project Revenge of the Dreamers when he rapped:\n\nCock sucker, I’m a blockbuster\nMotion picture, flows consist of potent scriptures\n\nIn both lines, Cole asserts that his presence alone is gripping enough to be cinematic.", "annotation_id": "29726010", "url": "https://genius.com/29726010", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "It’s like we recruited your homies to be demon deacons, we got ’em attending your wake", "vault_lines": ["It's like we recruited your homies to be demon deacons, we got 'em attending your wake"], "meaning": "J. Cole continues the discussion around the status of him and Drake in rap. Wake Forest University is based in Winston-Salem, North Carolina, the home state of Cole. The university’s athletic teams are known as the Demon Deacons.", "annotation_id": "29725853", "url": "https://genius.com/29725853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Niggas usually send they verses back to me and they be terrible, just like a two-year-old", "vault_lines": ["Niggas usually send they verses back to me and they be terrible, just like a two-year-old"], "meaning": "The “terrible twos” is a stage in a child’s development in which a toddler regularly bounces between reliance on adults and a new desire for independence, which can cause frequent mood changes and temper tantrums.\n\nThe verses that the rappers send to Drake sound like a toddler made it and is below his status, not making him inclined to send in a feature.", "annotation_id": "29725805", "url": "https://genius.com/29725805", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "She got a Android, her messages is lime green", "vault_lines": ["She got a Android, her messages is lime green"], "meaning": "Sending a message from an iPhone to an Android displays green bubbles, and messages between iPhones display blue bubbles. The difference is shown below:\n\n\n\nHere’s a conversation between an iPhone user and an Android user:\n\n\n\nAlso note that this could be a reference to to the song “Texts Go Green” from his last solo album, Honestly, Nevermind.", "annotation_id": "29725740", "url": "https://genius.com/29725740", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "I’m namin’ the album The Fall Off, it’s pretty ironic ’cause it ain’t no fall off for me", "vault_lines": ["I'm namin' the album The Fall Off, it's pretty ironic 'cause it ain't no fall off for me"], "meaning": "The Fall Off is Cole’s highly anticipated final album. Should it be his next project, it’d be his eighth studio album following 2024’s Might Delete Later.\n\nWhile “falling off” is usually slang for losing once held high status, Cole’s project is titled as such to be the thematic conclusion to his very first mixtape, 2007’s The Come Up. In that context, The Fall Off is Cole making his discography come full circle and letting him end his career on his own terms.\n\n\n\n Cover art for ‘The Come Up.’\n\nCole teased The Fall Off a few months back on the “Adonis Interlude” from Creed III: The Soundtrack when he rapped:\n\nJust cop ‘The Fall Off’ and he’ll explain\nIs it the end of the chapter, happily ever after?", "annotation_id": "29725728", "url": "https://genius.com/29725728", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Not Russia, but apply pressure To your cranium, Cole’s automatic when aimin’ ’em", "vault_lines": ["Not Russia, but apply pressure", "To your cranium, Cole's automatic when aimin' 'em"], "meaning": "Cole’s rapping “applies pressure” the same way Russia has been figuratively applying pressure against Ukraine in their ongoing war. “Ukraine” is phonetically similar to “your cran(ium)” or head; his raps are as deadly as headshots, hence the song title.\n\n“a p p l y i n g . p r e s s u r e” is also the fourth track of Cole’s 2021 album, The Off-Season.", "annotation_id": "29725421", "url": "https://genius.com/29725421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Like a kid that act bad from January to November, nigga, it’s just you and Cole", "vault_lines": ["Like a kid that act bad from January to November, nigga, it's just you and Cole"], "meaning": "Drake makes a masterful double entendre, answering the rhetorical question in the previous line, he states that it’s “just him and J. Cole” who own the G.O.A.T. title, while also comparing it to a “bad” kid getting no presents, just coal for Christmas.", "annotation_id": "29725335", "url": "https://genius.com/29725335", "state": "accepted", "votes": 120, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Niggas talkin’ ‘bout when this gon’ be repeated What the fuck, bro? I’m one away from Michael Nigga, beat it, nigga, beat it, what?", "vault_lines": ["Niggas talkin' 'bout when this gon' be repeated", "What the fuck, bro? I'm one away from Michael", "Nigga, beat it, nigga, beat it, what?", "Beat it, what? Beat it, what? Beat it, what? Beat it, what?", "Beat it, what? Beat it, what? Beat it, ayy, beat it, what?"], "meaning": "On Billboard’s list of “Most No. 1 Songs on the Hot 100,” Drake–at the time of the song’s release–sat in the fifth place, with a total of twelve No. 1 hits. In the fourth place, with only one more No. 1 hit, sat the King of Pop himself, Michael Jackson. Of course, with “Beat It”, Drake references here to one of Michael’s most popular songs, which was also a Billboard No. 1 in 1983.\n\nIn Billboard’s prestigious top ten, Drake is the only rapper. Therefore, Drake doesn’t feel like his legacy is going to be repeated anytime soon, and he has the accolades to back it up.", "annotation_id": "29725317", "url": "https://genius.com/29725317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 156, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "The Spider-Man meme is me lookin’ at Drake", "vault_lines": ["The Spider-Man meme is me lookin' at Drake"], "meaning": "While discussing his status in the rap game, Cole references the Spider-Man meme where two Spider men point at each other. The meme originated from an episode of the Spider-Man cartoon in the 60s where a villain impersonates the superhero.\n\n\n\nIn the music video, the meme is recreated with Cole pointing at Drake wearing a Spiderman suit under a San Francisco Spiders jersey with former NHL player John Purves‘ name on the back.", "annotation_id": "29724878", "url": "https://genius.com/29724878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 172, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "Love when they argue the hardest MC Is it K-Dot? Is it Aubrey? Or me? We the big three like we started a league, but right now, I feel like Muhammad Ali Huh, yeah, yeah, huh-huh, yeah, Muhammad Ali", "vault_lines": ["Love when they argue the hardest MC", "Is it K-Dot? Is it Aubrey? Or me?", "We the big three like we started a league, but right now, I feel like Muhammad Ali", "Huh, yeah, yeah, huh-huh, yeah, Muhammad Ali"], "meaning": "Since the late 2010s, there’s been a common consensus amongst mainstream hip hop fans that Cole himself, Aubrey “Drake” Graham, and Kendrick “K-Dot” Lamar are the three greatest rappers alive.\n\n\n\n  Left to right: J. Cole, Drake, Kendrick Lamar.\n\nCole spoke on this notion a while back on “Heavens EP” from Dreamville’s 2022 collaboration project D-Day: A Gangsta Grillz Mixtape when he rapped:\n\nSome people say that I’m running third, they threw the bronze at me\nBehind Drake and Dot, yeah, them niggas is superstars to me\n\nWhile humble in that line, Cole insists that he’s the Muhammed Ali of the trio here. Since Ali is widely regarded as “the greatest fighter ever” with a winning record of 56-5 and 37 knockouts, it’s safe to say Cole sees himself as one-of-one.\n\n\n\nMuhammad Ali.\n\n“We the big three like we started a league” also doubles as a reference to BIG3, a literal American basketball league founded by hip hop icon Ice Cube.\n\nKendrick Lamar famously rejected this sentiment with a diss on March 2024’s “Like That” from WE DON’T TRUST YOU by Future & Metro Boomin where Lamar rapped:\n\nMotherfuck the big three, nigga, it’s just big me", "annotation_id": "29724734", "url": "https://genius.com/29724734", "state": "accepted", "votes": 265, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "I search one name, and end up seein’ twenty tings Nadine, Christine, Justine, Kathleen, Charlene, Pauline, Claudine Man, I pack ’em in this phone like some sardines And they send me naked pictures, it’s the small things", "vault_lines": ["I search one name, and end up seein' twenty tings", "Nadine, Christine, Justine, Kathleen, Charlene, Pauline, Claudine", "Man, I pack 'em in this phone like some sardines", "And they send me naked pictures, it's the small things"], "meaning": "Drake has these women’s numbers “packed like sardines” in his phone; they crowd his contacts list. He might be interpolating the iconic second verse of “What These Bitches Want” by DMX from his 1999 classic album …And Then There Was X. In the verse, DMX lists out his romantic history by first names:\n\nThere was Brenda, Latisha, Linda, Felicia\nDawn, LeShaun, Ines and Alicia\nTeresa, Monica, Sharon, Nicki\nLisa, Veronica, Karen, Vicky", "annotation_id": "29724724", "url": "https://genius.com/29724724", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "First Person Shooter", "line": "I still wanna get me a song with YB", "vault_lines": ["I still wanna get me a song with YB"], "meaning": "In May 2023, Louisiana rapper YoungBoy Never Broke Again released his track “Fuck The Industry Pt. 2,” which featured him dissing J. Cole for YoungBoy himself not attending a planned studio session:\n\nJ a ho, that nigga played it cold like he was gon' do a feature\nSo I texted his line a muscle sign, I swear it’s gon' be nice to meet you\nPussy niggas tryna close the door\nBitch, you ain’t heard? Back from Baton Rouge to that ‘Nolia, we cutthroat\nAnd we hate hoes\n\nCole and YoungBoy’s beef first started in March 2021, when Cole waited over eight hours for a planned studio session with YoungBoy and then later collaborated with YoungBoy’s biggest opposition—Chicago native Lil Durk—in May 2023, releasing their collaborative “All My Life.” However, YoungBoy didn’t consent to this, resulting in him disrespecting Cole in an December 2021 interview with DJ Akademiks, stating:\n\nI was going through something so what the fuck I'mma go in this man’s studio session for a meet up with him, knowing that I ain’t in a mood to talk or just to be in the mode to fuck around? […] What the fuck, I'ma go around this man if I know I ain’t in a mood to talk to nobody or sit there or just even be around somebody?", "annotation_id": "29724646", "url": "https://genius.com/29724646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
