{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "You know what I’m sayin’? Tell you right from wrong Who’s smart from who’s the fool Which utensil to use for which food, like", "vault_lines": ["You know what I'm sayin'? Tell you right from wrong", "Who's smart from who's the fool", "Which utensil to use for which food, like"], "meaning": "Drake keeps throwing jabs at those girls with an eternal daughter mindset. When you have children you have to teach them stuff like how to properly use cutlery or what behaviors are right vs which are wrong, so it’s pretty obvious that Drake is wondering if all these girls look for in a partner is just someone who’ll treat them like a parent treats their children.", "annotation_id": "35617355", "url": "https://genius.com/35617355", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Damn, these niggas got me so twisted How the fuck you so real, but play bitch on my line?", "vault_lines": ["Damn, these niggas got me so twisted", "How the fuck you so real, but play bitch on my line?"], "meaning": "He’s acting real to everyone else but is the complete opposite on her “line” which is her phone.", "annotation_id": "31369690", "url": "https://genius.com/31369690", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "[Verse 4: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In Drake’s second verse on Slime You Out, he creatively narrates the complexities of his relationship by referencing each month of the year. This choice draws inspiration from Neil Sedaka’s Calendar Girl, where Sedaka similarly divides the twelve months into three distinct verses.\n\nFor a direct look at Neil Sedaka’s Calendar Girl check here.", "annotation_id": "30496212", "url": "https://genius.com/30496212", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "June have you movin’ ice cold, goin’ back and forth with a married man", "vault_lines": ["June have you movin' ice cold, goin' back and forth with a married man"], "meaning": "Drake also refers to his partner “movin' ice cold” which means that she may not respond to his messages or calls. Drake also has mentioned “ice cold” as June is usually the start of summer in many countries, and that a high volume of the population of the world are putting their efforts into “cooling down”. June is commonly known as the “month of marriage” and Drake has  described the person in question as “goin' back and forth” with a married male, which suggests that this woman is a mistress. Drake has briefly touched upon the subject of marriage, which could foreshadow the fact that his current partner is disloyal and could cheat on him.", "annotation_id": "29929223", "url": "https://genius.com/29929223", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "I could have you on payment plan ’til you’re hundred and fifty", "vault_lines": ["I could have you on payment plan 'til you're hundred and fifty"], "meaning": "A payment plan refers to paying off any debt by means of consolidation into an organized payment schedule. The consumer pays back a fixed amount of money every month until the balance is cleared.\nDrake claims that he had spent such a big amount of money on “them bitches”, that any attemp of payment plan in order to return the money will last until they are 150 years old (which is impossible, of course).", "annotation_id": "29831341", "url": "https://genius.com/29831341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Trippin’ when that dick is barely third place", "vault_lines": ["Trippin' when that dick is barely third place"], "meaning": "This annotation may covertly allude to a subtle jab at TDE, notably due to SZA’s contentious history with the label. Several of SZA’s recent artistic endeavors have served as metaphoric commentaries on her turbulent affiliation with TDE. The suggestion here is that she might be insinuating that OVO and Dreamville hold the positions of primacy when juxtaposed with TDE.\n\nFurthermore, the collaborative effort between Drake and J. Cole in the track “First Person Shooter” on the same album provides additional evidence supporting the notion that the reference to “third place” could indeed pertain to TDE, especially considering Kendrick Lamar’s prior status as TDE’s flagship artist. Drake also takes multiple subliminals at Kendrick throughout the album such as in “Away From Home.” Even J Cole mentions “big three” on his verse.", "annotation_id": "29729728", "url": "https://genius.com/29729728", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "You lucky that I don’t take back what was given", "vault_lines": ["You lucky that I don't take back what was given"], "meaning": "When Drake ends things with a woman he’s given many expensive gifts to, he doesn’t take the gifts back.\n\nIn Pain 1993, he raps:\n\n“I put some ice on her hand\nI let her take an advance\nShit wasn’t goin’ as planned\nI put that shit in the van”\n\nDrake allows women to keep the gift as he is too wealthy to need them back.", "annotation_id": "29629777", "url": "https://genius.com/29629777", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Tryna build trust, showin’ me your DMs, how they tryna bag you", "vault_lines": ["Tryna build trust, showin' me your DMs, how they tryna bag you"], "meaning": "The girl that Drake is referring to has many men interested in her and “trying to bag her” through online direct messages (DMs), meaning she has an abundance of romantic options. She decides to show these messages to Drake, however, and “build trust” with him by proving that she is loyal and only wants Drake, as she obviously ignored the messages if she openly showed them to him. She also “builds trust” by presumably attempting to create an open relationship in which she is not hiding romantically-motivated messages from Drake.", "annotation_id": "29576325", "url": "https://genius.com/29576325", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "For the fantasy of gettin’ married, very scary", "vault_lines": ["For the fantasy of gettin' married, very scary"], "meaning": "Drake has been quite self-limited when it comes to marriage. He’s even put Nicki Minaj’s hand in marriage and produced various tracks with her. He’s also purchased a predicted $12,000,000 necklace, inspired by his November 2022 album Her Loss. The chain consists of forty-two engagement rings for times he thought about marriage. Jeweler Alex Moss further explained the background behind it:\n\nNew piece titled ‘Previous Engagements’ for all the times he thought about it but never did it. The necklace is made up of 42 engagement rings and 351.38 carats in diamonds.\n\nIn a August 2023 interview with American podcaster Bobbi Althoff, Drake exposes his side of marriage:\n\nYou asked me why I haven’t gotten married. The truth? I don’t think I can offer somebody what they’d be looking for, right now. Just consistency, my life. My work is my priority.", "annotation_id": "29575382", "url": "https://genius.com/29575382", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "I don’t know what’s wrong with you girls I feel like y’all don’t need love, you need somebody who could micromanage you", "vault_lines": ["I don't know what's wrong with you girls", "I feel like y'all don't need love, you need somebody who could micromanage you"], "meaning": "Drake contradicts his feature in Girls Need Love, which is a remix in Summer Walker’s debut mixtape, Last Day Of Summer. In ‘Girls Need Love’, He claims that he is able to satisfy the needs of a women, granting their wishes. He constantly emphasises the idea that guys always have it their way and agrees with the narrative that girls need their fair share of appreciation and care though sexual pleasure. This was the most popular track in the mixtape, receiving a total of more than a million views.\n\nInstead in this particular track, there’s a change in thought which is “girls’” problematic nature and how they are undeserving of love. He expresses that girls shouldn’t have someone that is capable of providing them with affection. He emphases on the idea of how indecisive they are by expressing how they should be micromanaged.\n\nBeing micromanaged is someone having control over the aspects of your life which brings forward the idea of inferiority and this is a common theme in rap music. Although the word “girls” is commonly used in Drake’s music and in the rap industry in general, it displays emphasis on the idea that women aren’t worthy of being called “women” as they portray themselves as childish, in need of control and can’t go on about their lives by their own means.", "annotation_id": "29563037", "url": "https://genius.com/29563037", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "How you niggas get so carried away?", "vault_lines": ["How you niggas get so carried away?", "You bitches really get carried away"], "meaning": "SZA interpolates Drake’s flow from his first verse for the final four bars of hers. She wonders how her partners get so lost in thinking about what others have that they don’t.", "annotation_id": "29560990", "url": "https://genius.com/29560990", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Whipped and chained you like American slaves", "vault_lines": ["Whipped and chained you like American slaves"], "meaning": "The transatlantic slave trade was the tragic institution that involved the transportation of more than ten million enslaved African people between the 15th and 19th centuries. Nearly all were brought to the North and South American Continents, many specifically to the United States of America.\n\n\n\nDrake questionably relates the way these people were often chained  and brutally whipped to his ability to provide a woman with fancy cars (whips) and expensive necklaces (chains).", "annotation_id": "29558631", "url": "https://genius.com/29558631", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Seven bodyguards just in case somebody really wanna try and crash through", "vault_lines": ["Seven bodyguards just in case somebody really wanna try and crash through"], "meaning": "Being one of the biggest music artists in the world, and the fact that he’s a multi millionaire, Drake is always with his security team when he’s out in public, although they are rarely in any of his paparazzi pictures.\n\nThe bar also plays on the previous line where he describes how his feelings must be guarded because of the type of women he sees.\n\n\n\nDrake’s head of security, Chubbs, also makes appearances in some of his lyrics.", "annotation_id": "29556013", "url": "https://genius.com/29556013", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Don’t know why I listen to you when I hear you talkin’ to me, it’s some half-truths", "vault_lines": ["Don't know why I listen to you when I hear you talkin' to me, it's some half-truths"], "meaning": "Drake feels he is wasting energy trying to consume what the other person is saying. Due to them only expressing half of the truth and not being completely real.\n\nA “half truth” is a form of deceptive speech that only conveys parts that are beneficial to the deciever, to lighten their act(s). While sugarcoating the bigger picture.\n\nNote: Half-truth’s is a common practice amongst kids to avoid getting in big trouble. This could relate back to the “kids choices” bar from earlier.", "annotation_id": "29555154", "url": "https://genius.com/29555154", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "July, that’s when I found out you lied", "vault_lines": ["Slimin' you out, slimin' you out, slimin' you out", "July, that's when I found out you lied"], "meaning": "Drake relationship in the past has always been faced with lack of commitment, trust lies, and betrayal\n\nDrake has made references about partners lying to him in his past relationships, either to make something out from him or to enjoy the fame that comes along with it\n\nIn songs like GET ALONG BETTER from CLB\nIn the lyrics reads\n\n“You said I was too invested\nAnd that’s what had forced you to go and pretend”\n\nIn His collaboration with Jessie Reyez\nDrake asked the girl he’s in a relationship with about her body count\nHe lyrics below is from the song\n\n“You ask how many girls I been with in my life\nI tell ya, \"I stopped keeping count when I was 25”\nI ask you the same thing, you tell me, “Seven guys”\nBut we both know that's a lie\"\n\nDrake has been faced with a lot of relationship issues and it speaks in his song", "annotation_id": "29555097", "url": "https://genius.com/29555097", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Act like you not used to Sheraton stays", "vault_lines": ["Act like you not used to Sheraton stays"], "meaning": "Sheraton Hotels and Resorts are relatively good quality locations. However, this bar is meant to be an insult. That’s because from Drake’s wealthy perspective, these hotels are subpar.\n\n\n\nDrake calls back to his 2017 track “Can’t Have Everything”:\n\nY'all niggas is arrogant, y'all sleep at the Sheraton\nAll that shit embarrassin'", "annotation_id": "29553668", "url": "https://genius.com/29553668", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Bitch, next time, I swear on my grandmother grave", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, next time, I swear on my grandmother grave"], "meaning": "Drake swears on his grandmother’s grave to emphasize how serious he is. He was very close with his “Bubbe,” Evelyn Sher, before she tragically passed away in 2012. She helped raise him alongside his mother and grandfather as a child.\n\n\nHe tweeted a touching goodbye to his grandmother after her death:\n\nhttps://x.com/Drake/status/271778632115044354?s=20\n\nThe two were very close. Drake gave context to their relationship when he sampled her voicemail on his 2011 track “Look What You’ve Done”:\n\nHi Aubrey\nI’m here sitting with my son having a little talk\nAnd he knew, knows how to get in touch with you now\nTo let you know how grateful I am for your help in keeping me in this\nIn this comfortable place\nAll I can say, Aubrey, is I remember the good times we had together\nAnd the times I used to look after you\nAnd I still have wonderful feeling about that\nSo God bless you\nAnd I hope I’ll see you\n\nHe would go on to open SherClub in Toronto in his grandparents' honor in 2015, saying:\n\nRest in peace to my grandparents Rueben and Evelyn Sher. My grandmother was the first person to ever play catch with me and my grandfather was the biggest sports fan in the world. I opened this club in the memory of both of you.", "annotation_id": "29553550", "url": "https://genius.com/29553550", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "If I don’t pay your rent, it end up like an old hairstyle, girl, it’s past due", "vault_lines": ["If I don't pay your rent, it end up like an old hairstyle, girl, it's past due"], "meaning": "The woman Drake is paying rent for won’t be paying it herself, so if he doesn’t pay it, it will become “Past-Due” (Past-‘do)\n\n“Past-‘do” being a synonym for an old hairstye", "annotation_id": "29553260", "url": "https://genius.com/29553260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "I met the nigga you thought could replace How were there even comparisons made?", "vault_lines": ["I met the nigga you thought could replace", "How were there even comparisons made?"], "meaning": "On “Jaded” Drake references how a woman from a past relationship has a new interest who also makes music.\n\n“I need to know how the new nigga you got does the same thing\nI do for a living but is way less wavy”\n\nAfter meeting this gentleman, Drake is wondering how the woman could see this new man as his replacement.", "annotation_id": "29553205", "url": "https://genius.com/29553205", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "September, we fallin’ off, but I’m still the man you tryna win over", "vault_lines": ["September, we fallin' off, but I'm still the man you tryna win over"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the homonyms of “fall.” Autumn (fall) in 2023 begins September 23. As deciduous trees' leaves begin to fall, their romantic relationship begins to fall apart.", "annotation_id": "29552892", "url": "https://genius.com/29552892", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "April, spring is here and just like a spring, you start to spiral May brings some warmer days, poolside, gettin’ very tan", "vault_lines": ["April, spring is here and just like a spring, you start to spiral", "May brings some warmer days, poolside, gettin' very tan"], "meaning": "Spring in 2023  began on March 20 and ended on June 20, including all of April and May.\n\nDrake uses a homonym of the season of spring. A spiral spring is a device made up of rigid and elastic material in the shape of a helix.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, Drake explains that this woman is experiencing a thought spiral, which is a series of compounding negative thoughts that seem to only grow in number and severity.", "annotation_id": "29552763", "url": "https://genius.com/29552763", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "December the gift-givin’ month and now you wanna rekindle our year", "vault_lines": ["December the gift-givin' month and now you wanna rekindle our year"], "meaning": "Hanukkah and Christmas are the major “gift giving” December holidays. Drake celebrates both: his mother is Jewish and his father is Catholic.\n\n\n\nThis woman wants to reconnect with Drake to take advantage of the potential gifts his wealth could buy her. He specifically uses “rekindle” to create an association with the warmth of an iconic Christmas fireplace.", "annotation_id": "29551586", "url": "https://genius.com/29551586", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Yeah January, you pretend to see life clearly, yearly", "vault_lines": ["January, you pretend to see life clearly, yearly"], "meaning": "New Year’s Resolutions are vows that people take at the beginning of every year to better themselves. Often, these promises are only kept for a few weeks or months before being abandoned. While she at first appears highly motivated to change, Drake can see right through her facade.\n\nDrake has had several public resolutions, for example:\n\n2011:\n\nI do pride myself on being a humble guy that is still connected to his fans and still every much with the people. But I just wanna get even better than that, as challenging as it is, I just want to be a better guy, a nicer guy. I really want people, when I leave this earth, to say that was a great man, that was a great guy, he was always kind. But that takes a lot because there’s a lot of sh*t that can frustrate you in this position but that’s one of my things man. I just want to live this year, not getting caught up in certain things, even when people frustrate me still shake everybody’s hand.\n\n2014 (SNL Skit):\nhttps://youtu.be/9lnkgrJtiyU?si=YksP51zHRMgo0mnX&t=42\n\n2019:\n\nLook, I’m gonna let you know, all that resolution stuff is great, all that mental talk to yourself is great, but what you gotta do on the first, you gotta get up, you gotta go actually do it. you know what I’m saying, that’s the difference. So, I linked with my man and we’re in here getting it done. You know what time it is, it’s January 1st. The first opportunity to do all those things you told yourself you were going to do. Go get it.\n\n2023:\n\nI just really want to continue down the path that I’m on. I feel extremely healthy. I feel I am in the best shape mentally, physically in my life. I’ve been spending so much time with my family and my son.", "annotation_id": "29551294", "url": "https://genius.com/29551294", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Get a nigga hit, I’ll make his ass see the light like a half-moon", "vault_lines": ["Get a nigga hit, I'll make his ass see the light like a half-moon"], "meaning": "It is often said that when one dies, they “see the light.” However, rather than an actual light, it’s described as an overwhelming sense of tranquility and well-being.\n\nHalf-moon describes the first and third quarter of the lunar cycle when the moon appears to be 50% lit.\n\n\n\nSomething also “sees the light of day” when it’s highly visible. Additionally, “to moon” means to expose one’s buttocks. Here, Drake likens a targeted assassination to a harmless juvenile prank.", "annotation_id": "29550906", "url": "https://genius.com/29550906", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "(6ix)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake represents Toronto, Canada (the 6ix) with his signature tag. It samples Biggie’s “Ten Crack Commandments.”\nhttps://youtu.be/duKb2s-4Qt8?si=Yzm4BQFInJvR30Y_", "annotation_id": "29550899", "url": "https://genius.com/29550899", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Got me wiggin’ on you like I’m Arrogant Tae", "vault_lines": ["Got me wiggin' on you like I'm Arrogant Tae"], "meaning": "To wig is to angrily scold. Drake plays on its homonym which refers to artificial hair.\n\nArrogant Tae is a hair and wig stylist best known for working with Drake-collaborator and friend Nicki Minaj. Tae took to Instagram to react to this bar:\n\nMANNNNN WAITTTTTT WHATTTTT😩😩😩😩😩😩😩😩😩😩😩 WOWWWWWW!!!! I GOT A MENTION FROM THE GOAT 😩🥹🙏🏾 NIGGA DRAKE JUST MENTIONED ME ‼️‼️‼️‼️‼️‼️‼️🗣️🗣️🗣️🗣️🗣️🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐🐐 @champagnepapi @sza 🔥🔥🔥🔥🔥🔥🔥🔥🔥🔥🔥 imagine waking up to a freakin mention from one of your favorite artist bro 😩🥹🥹🥹🥹🥹🥹🥹 WOW 🙏🏾🙏🏾🙏🏾🙏🏾\n\nFurthermore, in Drake’s Lil Durk-assisted, August 2020, “Laugh Now Cry Later,” Durk made a notable mention to Tae:\n\nWe in Atlanta, I buy her a wig, she tellin' me Tae is the best", "annotation_id": "29550861", "url": "https://genius.com/29550861", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "And my slime right here, she got some bars for y’all niggas So I’ma fall back and let SZA talk her shit for a minute", "vault_lines": ["And my slime right here, she got some bars for y'all niggas", "So I'ma fall back and let SZA talk her shit for a minute"], "meaning": "Drake refers to SZA as his “slime,” or friend, to further dispel any rumors of beef stemming from Drake’s feature on 2020’s “Mr. Right Now”:\n\nYeah, said she wanna fuck to some SZA, wait\n‘Cause I used to date SZA back in '08\nIf you cool with it, baby, she can still play\n\nSZA responded to the bar soon after:\nhttps://x.com/sza/status/1312942896576782337?s=20\n\nDuring an interview with Audacy and V-103 Atlanta’s Big Tigger in 2022, she rehashed this same idea:\n\nWe’re cool. And we’ve always been cool.\n[…] It’s never been weird. It didn’t come completely out of the blue when he let me know. I didn’t know it was a song with 21 or anything like that.\n\nThe two seem to be close friends. They were seen together at SZA’s CTRL tour in 2017:", "annotation_id": "29550793", "url": "https://genius.com/29550793", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "November got you moodboardin’ for next year and you’re single", "vault_lines": ["November got you moodboardin' for next year and you're single"], "meaning": "A moodboard, popularly known on platforms like Pinterest, is a visual representation or collage that captures and conveys a particular mood, theme, or concept. It’s a curated collection of images, photographs, graphics, and text that together create a visual narrative or inspiration board. Moodboards serve several creative and practical purposes including but not limited to: inspiration, visual communication, and aesthetic exploration.", "annotation_id": "29550736", "url": "https://genius.com/29550736", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "August, it was “baby” this, “baby” that like you had your tubes tied", "vault_lines": ["August, it was \"baby\" this, \"baby\" that like you had your tubes tied"], "meaning": "After finding out about her infidelities, Drake is unbothered by her attempts at love-bombing him.\n\nTubal litigation (getting one’s “tubes tied”) is surgical female sterilization. The procedure eliminates the ability for a woman to get pregnant and have children.", "annotation_id": "29550726", "url": "https://genius.com/29550726", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "All I really know is M bags like I drove through and ordered fast food", "vault_lines": ["All I really know is M bags like I drove through and ordered fast food"], "meaning": "Drake boasts that he’s used to “M bags” – bags of money – like bags of fast food from McDonald’s that are marked with the company’s signature  “M” logo:", "annotation_id": "29550541", "url": "https://genius.com/29550541", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "You tell these hoes you ain’t cuddlin’ But with me, you know you doin’ all that shit You tellin’ these hoes you ain’t trickin’ off But with me, you know I’m gon’ get it all", "vault_lines": ["You tell these hoes you ain't cuddlin'", "But with me, you know you doin' all that shit", "You tellin' these hoes you ain't trickin' off", "But with me, you know I'm gon' get it all"], "meaning": "SZA implies that he is cheating on his current girlfriend(s) with her and lying about it.\n\n“Trickin' off” is slang used for men that excessively spend money on (often multiple) women they aren’t in a relationship with.", "annotation_id": "29550439", "url": "https://genius.com/29550439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "February is the time that you put the evil eye and the pride aside", "vault_lines": ["February is the time that you put the evil eye and the pride aside"], "meaning": "“Evil Eye” is a concept deeply rooted in various cultures around the world. It refers to the belief in a malevolent gaze or stare that can bring harm or bad luck to the person, object, or even an idea that it is directed towards. This superstition often involves the idea that jealousy or envy can manifest as this harmful gaze.\n\nIt’s believed that wearing protective amulets or charms, like the Nazar in Turkey or the Hamsa in the Middle East, can ward off its negative effects.\n\nIn the context of lyrics, mentioning the “evil eye” can convey a sense of vulnerability or the need for protection from external negative influences.", "annotation_id": "29550421", "url": "https://genius.com/29550421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Shout to QC, pretty sure I made Pee M’s like it’s past noon", "vault_lines": ["Shout to QC, pretty sure I made Pee M's like it's past noon"], "meaning": "Pierre “Pee” Thomas is the president and CEO of Atlanta-based music label Quality Control, or QC for short. The label boasts huge names such as Migos, Lil Baby and Lil Yachty as well as many other musical acts.\n\nDrake remixed the Migos song “Versace\" in June 2013, giving the group their first smash hit and an important early cosign. Before Lil Baby had achieved mainstream stardom, Drake hopped on his May 2018 track “Yes Indeed” which produced Baby’s first top ten hit on the Billboard Hot 100. These early career accelerants benefited the artists massively and no doubt netted label-head Pee millions of dollars in the longterm.\n\nBy referencing Pierre’s nickname and the millions he’s earned, Drake is also making a play on P.M. (post-meridiem) time, which refers to the hours in a day that are past 12 o'clock noon.", "annotation_id": "29550060", "url": "https://genius.com/29550060", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Get a nigga hit for fifty racks, girl, the beef cost like it’s wagyu", "vault_lines": ["Get a nigga hit for fifty racks, girl, the beef cost like it's wagyu"], "meaning": "Drake notes that if he must, he’ll place a fifty thousand dollar bounty on his opposition’s head due to their past beef—a slang term used to refer to past animosity between two people or groups.\n\nHowever, Drake makes a clever play on words as he notes that his beef is often expensive because of how much money he spends to get his opposition killed, referring to Wagyu beef, one of the most expensive cuts of meat in the world, originating in Japan.\n\n\n\nPictured: A Wagyu beef diagram pointing out the factors which make the meat so expensive", "annotation_id": "29550008", "url": "https://genius.com/29550008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Ah-ah-ah, that’s as far as I got", "vault_lines": ["Ah-ah-ah, that's as far as I got"], "meaning": "Drake ends his rambling fourth verse in the same manner as André 3000’s end to his similarly long-winded verse on his September 2003 track “A Life in the Day of Benjamin André (Incomplete)”:\n\nAnd that’s as far as I got\n(music fades)\n\nThree Stacks spoke on the abrupt ending  in a 2012 interview with GQ Magazine:\n\nThere are actually songs on The Love Below that were not finished, but that’s how they are, that’s how it came out. Like the last track, “A Life in the Day of Benjamin André (Incomplete).” We were up like three days straight drinking Red Bull or whatever, finishing that album. I just knew I wanted to put that on there, but it wasn’t done, but it was enough.", "annotation_id": "29549986", "url": "https://genius.com/29549986", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "October is all about me ’cause your turn should’ve been over", "vault_lines": ["October is all about me 'cause your turn should've been over"], "meaning": "Drake’s birthday is October 24th, 1986. He clearly takes great pride in the month, as the OVO name that titles his ubiquitous record label and luxury clothing brand stands for “October’s Very Own.”\n\n\n\nDrake’s OVO logo.\n\nThe woman feels obligated to treat Drake nicely not only because it’s his birth month, but because she knows he usually would’ve given up on pursuing someone well before nine months of drama.\n\nIt’s worth noting that following the release of “Slime You Out,” Drake officially pushed back the release of FOR ALL THE DOGS from September 22, 2023, to October 6, 2023, noting that “it’s only right” for the project to be released in October.", "annotation_id": "29549942", "url": "https://genius.com/29549942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "March got you already second guessin’ titles", "vault_lines": ["March got you already second guessin' titles"], "meaning": "Their relationship doesn’t have a concrete label; she’s unsure if the two are just talking, dating, or doing something in between.\n\nThis bar may also be a subtle reference to the NCAAB National Championship title game that takes place during the annual March Madness tournament.\n\n\n\nDuke wins the 2015 National Title", "annotation_id": "29549933", "url": "https://genius.com/29549933", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "And you ain’t ‘bout the shit you rappin’ about And I can spin a ho, I’m airin’ it out I’m goin’ off like a sawed-off", "vault_lines": ["Slimin' you out, slimin' you out, slimin' you out", "And I ain't even cummin', I'm in and out", "And you ain't 'bout the shit you rappin' about", "And I can spin a ho, I'm airin' it out", "I'm goin' off like a sawed-off", "Slimin' you out, I'm slimin' you out, I'm slimin' you out"], "meaning": "SZA takes a direct shot at certain rappers who lie in their raps to make themselves seem more authentic and cool, whereas, in reality, they aren’t associated with anything they rap about.\n\nA sawed-off shotgun is an extremely deadly firearm due to its extreme power and short effective range. SZA makes a play on words as she notes that she’s gonna “spin” her partner’s mistress—a slang term used to refer to killing someone, linking back to the firearm. Furthermore, SZA also notes that she’ll “air” the mistress' business out—a term used to refer to one’s personal business being told to everyone. The term is also linked to shooting a firearm.\n\nHowever, SZA employs a double entendre as she sings, “I can spin a ho” which can refer to her exposing somebody’s secrets, playing into the fact that to air something out means to expose one’s business to the public.\n\n\n\nPictured: A Lupara sawed-off shotgun\n\nThis isn’t the first time SZA’s shared her homicidal tendencies regarding an unfaithful partner, as in her December 2022 chart-topper, “Kill Bill,” she sang:\n\nI might\nI might kill my ex, not the best idea\nHis new girlfriend’s next, how’d I get here?\nI might kill my ex, I still love him though", "annotation_id": "29549849", "url": "https://genius.com/29549849", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "All I really know is W’s and M’s, life lookin’ like a bathroom", "vault_lines": ["All I really know is W's and M's, life lookin' like a bathroom"], "meaning": "“W” is slang for win and “M” is slang for a million dollars. Drake is saying that he’s successful and rich.\n\nPublic restrooms typically have both a women’s and a men’s room.", "annotation_id": "29549692", "url": "https://genius.com/29549692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "Send wires on wires on wires like Idris", "vault_lines": ["Send wires on wires on wires like Idris"], "meaning": "Idris Elba is an English actor who starred as Russell “Stringer” Bell in the first three seasons of the HBO original series The Wire. The show is generally regarded as one of the best series ever created on television and his portrayal of Stringer is seen one of the seminal performances of Elba’s long acting career.\n\n\n\nIdris Elba as Stringer Bell\n\nThe show’s name, The Wire, comes from the overarching theme of surveillance in the series, whether it be CCTV cameras, informants or undercover officers placed in situations by the authorities, or wiretaps on the characters' forms of communication. Drake flips the meaning of this word for his bar, talking about sending his girl wire transfers of money, which is a direct electronic transfer of funds between bank accounts.", "annotation_id": "29549538", "url": "https://genius.com/29549538", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slime You Out", "line": "I’m slimin’ you for them kid choices you made", "vault_lines": ["I'm slimin' you for them kid choices you made", "Slimin' you out, slimin' you out, slimin' you out", "Slimin' you out, I'm slimin' you out, I'm slimin' you out"], "meaning": "Drake criticizes grown-ups who have childish tendencies such as being emotional or petty in certain situations, as though they need to be the center of attention, noting that he’ll “slime them out” — a slang term referring to using a person for your own purposes, sexual intercourse in Drake’s case, and then break up.\n\nFor SZA, it’s the opposite. She feels she is being “slimed out” by her partner, so she decides to end the relationship on her own.\n\nUndoubtedly, it is also a reference to the famous celebrity slimings on the Nickelodeon Kids' Choice Awards. One such sliming can be seen on the promotional cover for the song, which features Halle Berry getting slimed at the 25th Annual Kids' Choice Awards in March 2012:", "annotation_id": "29547668", "url": "https://genius.com/29547668", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
