{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Your famous girlfriend ass keep gettin’ thicker than the plot does", "vault_lines": ["Your famous girlfriend ass keep gettin' thicker than the plot does"], "meaning": "Clever word play relating this alleged gf as* to that of a plot “thickening”. Also could serve as a subtle triple entendre since the first girl he mentioned isn’t referred to as his girlfriend. Also, this girlfriend is the ‘famous’ one, meaning there’s more in this story line to come from just the intro, and so the plot thickens", "annotation_id": "36955486", "url": "https://genius.com/36955486", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Brand new girl and she still growin’ Brand new titties, stitches still showin’ Yeah, and she just prayin’ that it heals good I’m ‘bout to fuck and I’m just prayin’ that it feels good", "vault_lines": ["Brand new girl and she still growin'", "Brand new titties, stitches still showin'", "Yeah, and she just prayin' that it heals good", "I'm 'bout to fuck and I'm just prayin' that it feels good"], "meaning": "Drake is describing a young woman who is new to the nightlife scene “Brand new girl an she still growin”. The lyrics “brand new titties, stitches still showin’” suggests she recently got breast implants, acknowledging her transformation or reinvention. He’s hoping for a smooth recovery “prayin’ that it heals good”. Drakes perspective in the song reflects anticipation and desire to get physical and emotional.", "annotation_id": "31295470", "url": "https://genius.com/31295470", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "For four months out the year, it’s got you asking what’s good at home? What’s good at home? The same hoes are still at it, I should’ve known", "vault_lines": ["For four months out the year, it's got you asking what's good at home?", "What's good at home?", "The same hoes are still at it, I should've known"], "meaning": "For part of the year, Drake is out traveling around the world for tours, for making music, and for parties, but for the rest of the year when he is at his home town of Toronto, Canada, the same girls that were there was he wasn’t known are doing their same thing, signifying how successful Drizzy is and how he’s built his career into something that allows him to visit many other places.", "annotation_id": "34604420", "url": "https://genius.com/34604420", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "And even though they hate you They just keep on tellin’ you they feel you, nigga", "vault_lines": ["And even though they hate you", "They just keep on tellin' you they feel you, nigga"], "meaning": "Although people might hate Drake for things he’s done, those same people will stick around with him to use him for his money and media attention.", "annotation_id": "34604381", "url": "https://genius.com/34604381", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Tellin’ stories that nobody relate to", "vault_lines": ["Tellin' stories that nobody relate to"], "meaning": "Many of the stories that Drake has told throughout his music career have been experiences that he has been through trying to become a known rapper.", "annotation_id": "34604377", "url": "https://genius.com/34604377", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "You know it’s real when your latest nights are your greatest nights", "vault_lines": ["You know it's real when your latest nights are your greatest nights"], "meaning": "Making a real connection in the music industry is difficult for Drake because a lot of people try to him for clout and money, but when Drake spends the whole night with somebody and they have a good time the whole time, he knows that he met somebody real who likes to be around him.", "annotation_id": "34604374", "url": "https://genius.com/34604374", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "I hate when, I hate when people say they feel me, man I hate that shit It’ll be a long time before y’all feel me If ever", "vault_lines": ["I hate when, I hate when people say they feel me, man", "I hate that shit", "It'll be a long time before y'all feel me"], "meaning": "Drake’s thesis statement of the whole song; despising when someone says they feel him, because they could not understand his unusual feeling that nobody has experienced,", "annotation_id": "34258754", "url": "https://genius.com/34258754", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "That runway can be cold, especially after summer’s rolled on", "vault_lines": ["That runway can be cold, especially after summer's rolled on"], "meaning": "Following on from previous lines, Drake makes use of a double entendre. Alluding not only to the Toronto Pearson Airport runway, he also employs figurative language that a modelling runway will be ‘cold’ after summer months, which is when winter clothing is back in fashion, leading on from his mention of the need for a coat.\nA notable woman this could be in reference to is Bria Myles, known for her successful modelling career and whom he dated in 2008-2009, which he also dedicated a song to in his mixtape So Far Gone, titled “Bria’s Interlude”.", "annotation_id": "32379831", "url": "https://genius.com/32379831", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Walking through airport security with your hat down ‘Stead of gettin’ a pat-down, they just keep on sayin’ that they feel you, nigga, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Walking through airport security with your hat down", "'Stead of gettin' a pat-down, they just keep on sayin' that they feel you, nigga, yeah", "They just keep on tellin' you they feel you, nigga"], "meaning": "Note the wordplay – in a pat-down the security will have to ‘feel’ Drake. But also, they won’t bother patting down Drake because he’s so famous now; instead they’ll just compliment him.", "annotation_id": "29414073", "url": "https://genius.com/29414073", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Or get three SUVs for niggas dressed like refugees", "vault_lines": ["Or get three SUVs for niggas dressed like refugees"], "meaning": "Drake’s buying cars for his friends, who he jokes are dressed like refugees. This might refer to modern style trends (think Kanye’s Yeezus era fashion), as well as his friends' impoverishment absent Drake’s beneficence.\n\nThis might also be a reference to The Weeknd – his parents immigrated to Canada from Ethiopia in the 1980s, when the country suffered from a devastating famine which resulted in 1 million deaths, and there were Cold War clashes between the Soviet-imposed communist government and insurrectionist groups.", "annotation_id": "11953436", "url": "https://genius.com/11953436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "[Intro: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song, closes his second album and also puts end to a trilogy of songs that started with “The Calm” on his critically acclaimed mixtape — So Far Gone;   the second being “The Resistance” from his first album — Thank Me Later.\n\nAll three songs are similar in subject matter as they feature Drake venting about his life.\n\nHe tweeted this before Take Care dropped :\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/66324328530190337", "annotation_id": "3932163", "url": "https://genius.com/3932163", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Young money-maker, season-ticket holder", "vault_lines": ["Young money-maker, season-ticket holder"], "meaning": "Obvious pun on Drake being a young person who is making money and being signed to/belonging to the label Young Money\n\nThis also references Drake dominating the music industry for the 2011 Fall/Winter season (when Take Care was released), as well as having season seats courtside for the Raptors.", "annotation_id": "528205", "url": "https://genius.com/528205", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "And the girl you fuckin’ hates you and your friends faded off shots of What you ordered to forget about the game that you on top of", "vault_lines": ["And the girl you fuckin' hates you and your friends faded off shots of", "What you ordered to forget about the game that you on top of"], "meaning": "Drake does have a habit of buying shots for the bar, as Billboard reported that he did during an October 2011 “birthday party” at the TAO in Las Vegas\n\nThe reasoning is since Drake is the one with the money, who is exposed to all the top-shelf liquor. Since his crew doesn’t even have the money to go out and order drinks at the bar, it’s unlikely that they’d be familiar with all the liquor he’s buying. They’re just going with it", "annotation_id": "444283", "url": "https://genius.com/444283", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Yeah, you see a girl and you ask about her Bitches smiling at you, it must be happy hour", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, you see a girl and you ask about her", "Bitches smiling at you, it must be happy hour"], "meaning": "That seems to work for him.  Drake started dating video model Maliah Michel after mentioning her in a song.  As Maliah tells it:\n\n Drake had actually come to the club where I work at in Houston called Dreams. I think that’s where he first saw me. I didn’t even get to speak to him that night and like a week later everybody started telling me Drake shouted me out in a song. I thought that it was so cool because I hadn’t even talked to him before. I mean, obviously he saw something he liked [laughs].\n\nSo prior to Drake name dropping you on “Miss Me”, you guys never even shared a conversation?\n\n Yup, I was so flattered, too —especially with me being used to the strip club environment, where guys are always just… thirsty and on you. This guy didn’t even say anything but gave me props on a song.\n\nAs far as the Happy Hour reference, it’s that time from 4-8p or so after work when all drinks at the bar are half priced-or 2 for 1- and these women are all looking for a hand out, smiling and ready to get in a brother’s wallet.", "annotation_id": "444272", "url": "https://genius.com/444272", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "And deal with the questions about all your excessive needs And you do dinners at French Laundry in Napa Valley", "vault_lines": ["And deal with the questions about all your excessive needs", "And you do dinners at French Laundry in Napa Valley"], "meaning": "The French Laundry is a Napa Valley restaurant repeatedly named  “the best restaurant in the world” by Restaurant Magazine.  Its chef and owner is Thomas Keller.  According to Wikipedia, “[e]very day, the French Laundry serves two different nine-course tasting menus, none of which uses the same ingredient more than once”.  A menu from a day in 2005, below:\n\n\n\nThe restaurant:", "annotation_id": "444255", "url": "https://genius.com/444255", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "And so you up there every night, you swear you gettin’ close That champagne money was for gas and phone bills But shit, you ’bout to spend it on what matters most", "vault_lines": ["And so you up there every night, you swear you gettin' close", "That champagne money was for gas and phone bills", "But shit, you 'bout to spend it on what matters most"], "meaning": "40 explained the story behind this section of the song in a recent GQ interview:\n\n It was a crazy, crazy night. That was when we were living in an apartment building in Toronto, downtown, Apartment 1503 15 Fort York Boulevard. He says 1503, two couches and paintings, and he goes on to talk about that apartment where we did all that music, on the new album. He was distraught one night and showed up with $1,000 worth of champagne and I’m cussing at him because we’re all broke and trying to make this shit work! Meanwhile, he’s renting Phantoms and shit. It’s all documented. He shows up with all the liquor and he’s drinking and we’re trying to start working and he gets into a real argument with his uncle, and he went out on the balcony and started yelling at his uncle and I’d never seen him that distraught or emotionally beat up about something. He just came back in the room and said, I need to rap. Make me something. In 45 minutes, I made “The Calm” and he wrote those bars as I made the beat. Over the next five or six hours, that record unfolded in its entirety\n\nHe first mentions this on So Far Gone with this line from Successful:\n\nAnd 40 that was all before I showed up and brought a thousand dollars worth of drinks and got poured up", "annotation_id": "443980", "url": "https://genius.com/443980", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "I really don’t know much, but, shit, I know a secret They say more money, more problems, my nigga, don’t believe it I mean, sure, there’s some bills and taxes I’m still evadin’ But I blew six million on myself and I feel amazin’", "vault_lines": ["I really don't know much, but, shit, I know a secret", "They say more money, more problems, my nigga, don't believe it", "I mean, sure, there's some bills and taxes I'm still evadin'", "But I blew six million on myself and I feel amazin'"], "meaning": "Drizzy goes against hip-hop orthodoxy and denies the truth of Biggie’s great aphorism.  We hope that the $6 million spent figure is an exaggeration, as Drake’s 2010 earnings were projected to be $9 million.  Save some for a rainy day, Drizzy!  Then again, the $6 million figure could just be a rhetorical shout out to this guy:", "annotation_id": "442819", "url": "https://genius.com/442819", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "And steal your mother’s debit card so you maintain an image And ride around in overpriced rental cars that ain’t tinted", "vault_lines": ["And steal your mother's debit card so you maintain an image", "And ride around in overpriced rental cars that ain't tinted"], "meaning": "Drake, pre-rap stardom, tried to create the appearance of being a baller to people in his town by doing things such as leasing a Rolls Royce before he had an apartment of his own\n\nDrake leased his Rolls Royce without tint so that everyone could see him on these expensive vehicles. He did this to create an image that he was a baller.\n\nHe mentions the tint of the windows because you can’t tint the windows of a rental car.", "annotation_id": "442817", "url": "https://genius.com/442817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Apartment 1503, some couches and paintings Where you record with two others that want the same things Yeah, it start to feel better than home feels", "vault_lines": ["Apartment 1503, some couches and paintings", "Where you record with two others that want the same things", "Yeah, it start to feel better than home feels"], "meaning": "In a recent article, Drake remembers, as he mentions here, living part-time with producer Noah “40” Shebib in Shebib’s bedroom studio circa 2009:\n\n“I would be there every night and I hated going home,” [Drake] says. “I was deep in debt with my family. We were fighting every night. I had spent a lot of money at trying to succeed at music with these poppy songs like ‘Replacement Girl.’ Trying to be famous and trying to do it with a hit\"", "annotation_id": "440697", "url": "https://genius.com/440697", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "I’m out here messing over the lives of these niggas that couldn’t fuck with my freshman floater", "vault_lines": ["I'm out here messing over the lives of these niggas that couldn't fuck with my freshman floater"], "meaning": "Drake uses “floater” here to describe his debut, or freshman album, Thank Me Later.  A floater is a shot in basketball that mostly small guards use in the paint to get shots over larger defenders.  Drake’s saying that his freshman album can’t be touched by other musicians.", "annotation_id": "440484", "url": "https://genius.com/440484", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Take care, nigga", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Take Care = Goodbye (This is the last line of the 17 track album, not including the bonus tracks)\n\nDrake ends his album by saying the title of it, as he has done similarly in the past and future:\n- Nothing Was the Same finishes with those words on “Paris Morton Music 2”\n- Thank Me Later finishes with the song “Thank Me Now”\n- Views finishes with the song “VIEWS”", "annotation_id": "440481", "url": "https://genius.com/440481", "state": "accepted", "votes": 120, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "My sophomore, I was all for it, they all saw it My junior and senior will only get meaner", "vault_lines": ["My sophomore, I was all for it, they all saw it", "My junior and senior will only get meaner"], "meaning": "Drake is using the American convention for describing the four years of high school/university in order to describe his albums: sophomore for his second album, junior for his next (third) album, and senior for his fourth album.\n\nPeople were hyped about Take Care – Drake’s second album – knowing it would be amazing. So Drake is letting everyone know that his third and fourth albums will be even better.\n\nThese turned out to be his 2013 album Nothing Was the Same (3x platinum)  and 2016 album Views (4x platinum)", "annotation_id": "440289", "url": "https://genius.com/440289", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "I haven’t been inside Terminal 1 and 3 in so long I’m drivin’ right up to it now, make sure you got your coat on", "vault_lines": ["I haven't been inside Terminal 1 and 3 in so long", "I'm drivin' right up to it now, make sure you got your coat on"], "meaning": "Toronto Pearson International Airport currently has two operating terminals, Terminal 1 and Terminal 3.\n\n\n\nPearson International Airport Terminal 1\n\nDrake hasn’t been to either terminal in a long time because he has only been taking private flights,\n which don’t require him to enter through the terminals. In effect, he’s saying that he takes a lot of private flights.\n\nNote how he ties this back to the airport visualization from earlier in the song.", "annotation_id": "440148", "url": "https://genius.com/440148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "Problem children that all be reppin’ October’s Own", "vault_lines": ["Problem children that all be reppin' October's Own"], "meaning": "October’s Very Own, also known as OVO, is Drake’s overarching name for his crew, blog, business ventures, etc.", "annotation_id": "440046", "url": "https://genius.com/440046", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "My young niggas poppin’ M’s and sippin’ dirty Jones", "vault_lines": ["My young niggas poppin' M's and sippin' dirty Jones"], "meaning": "The Weeknd and the OVO crew are popping MDMA (blues, 30mg Roxies) and sipping purple drank, mixed with some Jones soda.\n\n\n\n“M’s” is short for Molly, which is the name of MDMA (ecstasy) in its purest form (C11H15NO2)", "annotation_id": "440011", "url": "https://genius.com/440011", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Ride", "line": "You niggas gettin’ older, I see no threat in Yoda", "vault_lines": ["You niggas gettin' older, I see no threat in Yoda"], "meaning": "Drake is only in his early/mid 20’s – but he doesn’t think age necessarily brings wisdom\n\nHe feels the older and more experienced rappers are not a problem despite their “power”. He likens this to how Jedi Master Yoda (from the Star Wars film series) is very old (900 years) but also very powerful – but still not an issue for Drizzy.\n\n\n\nIs this another possible diss to Jay-Z?\nSee\n1. Lil Wayne – It’s Good\n2. Drake – Dreams Money Can Buy", "annotation_id": "439980", "url": "https://genius.com/439980", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
