{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "You would prolly kill another man for the boy", "vault_lines": ["You would prolly fly out to Japan for the boy", "You would prolly kill another man for the boy", "You would prolly lie 'bout it on the stand for your boy"], "meaning": "Drake may be referencing Rihanna’s 2010 song “Man Down”. Since “Days in the East” is widely believed to be about Rihanna, the phrase “you would prolly kill another man for the boy” could be a subtle callback to “Man Down”, where Rihanna sings about killing a man.", "annotation_id": "39997487", "url": "https://genius.com/39997487", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "[Interlude: Drake & Rihanna]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse and song is dedicated to Rihanna almost entirely and Drake confirmed the rumors at his annual OVO Fest held in Toronto. While performing this song he had a picture of her on the screen with the number “6” repeated 3 times.\n\n\n\nMany people are speculating he’s implying she’s the devil for breaking his heart.\n\nDrake of course was quick to deny any controversy by tweeting :\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/497148916240896000", "annotation_id": "3878404", "url": "https://genius.com/3878404", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "(Why? Fring)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s nickname for Rihanna – He captioned “Fring. Love you. #WYLAT” on this photo.\nhttp://instagram.com/p/mCVBHEjQDp/\n\nIt’s a Breaking Bad reference, Gustavo Fring is a character on the show. Rihanna is a big fan of the show and got Drake to start watching it. Here is a interview with Aaron Paul talking about them:\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PlrrHNVSgqg", "annotation_id": "3023026", "url": "https://genius.com/3023026", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Gave you little help, you need more already Damn, you need more already?", "vault_lines": ["Gave you little help, you need more already", "Damn, you need more already?"], "meaning": "Drake has no issue giving family members a little monetary assistance.\n\nAnd if you end up needin' some extra help, then I could help\nYou know, back on your feet and shit\nTryna get my karma up, fuck the guilty and greedy shit\n\nIt seems like they run through the money and he is questioning the spending habits.", "annotation_id": "3022920", "url": "https://genius.com/3022920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Put the Bible to your right hand for the boy", "vault_lines": ["Put the Bible to your right hand for the boy"], "meaning": "Drake continues to reiterate how this girl is all the way down for him. She’d take it as far as committing perjury for him.\n\nPutting your hand to the Bible is apart of the ritual of taking an oath. And as previously mentioned:\n\nYou would probably lie ‘bout it on the stand for your boy\n\nPerjury – The offense of willfully telling an untruth in a court after having taken an oath or affirmation.\n\n\n\nUsually, it is customary to place one’s left hand on the bible and raise their right. He is reaffirming that not only will she lie on the stand like the previous line, but she will purposely put the wrong hand on the bible to commit perjury, not be under oath, and still lie.", "annotation_id": "3022878", "url": "https://genius.com/3022878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Said the city stress him out on the low I said, “I feel you, nigga, I already know” Yeah, already...", "vault_lines": ["Said the city stress him out on the low", "I said, \"I feel you, nigga, I already know\"", "You know you got..."], "meaning": "Drake trusts his boys to be wary of enemies or fake friends. Although he knows the verbal attacks are often when he’s not around, he’s got family to fill him in.\n\n\n\nChubbs is streetwise, but the interweaving of street shit and luxury in the industry requires a dual vigilance. Lowkey, Chubbs admits it’s stressful. Drake agrees, but without attentiveness, OVO could be caught slippin'.", "annotation_id": "3022257", "url": "https://genius.com/3022257", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Already, already, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake pronounces the word “already” with that classic H-Town drawl (awwwready). This popular staple in Texas urban vernacular is prominent in Houston rap and the city’s chopped and screwed style music. Drizzy is truly a connoisseur of the H-Town culture.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F1XeVfQeUZ8\n\nThe repetition of the word “already” could be an emphasis on how Drake has already reached ultimate success, even though he’s a part (leader) of the new school.", "annotation_id": "3021774", "url": "https://genius.com/3021774", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "’Fore you said “You’re mine” I was yours already", "vault_lines": ["'Fore you said \"You're mine\" I was yours already"], "meaning": "He’s yours, he’s yours, he’s yours that’s for sure, that’s for sure\n\n\n\nACCEPTED COMMENT: This is also a reference to Beyoncé’s “Mine”, a song which Drake featured on.\n\nI just wanna say, you’re mine, you’re mine\nI just wanna say, you’re mine, you’re mine", "annotation_id": "3021164", "url": "https://genius.com/3021164", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Studyin’ your body, that’s exams for the boy Take a flight here, pop a Xan for your boy", "vault_lines": ["Studyin' your body, that's exams for the boy", "Take a flight here, pop a Xan for your boy", "Long flight home, 'nother Xan for your boy"], "meaning": "Drake appreciates the beauty of a woman. He often makes references to the body of a women such as in “Deuces (Remix)” in which Drake directly correlates a woman’s body to curves in which is desire and lust cannot be tamed.\n\nDrake’s anxious behavior leads him to become a student of the woman’s body. Drake’s love for women and their nature leads to him not caring about what it costs to fly them out. When under the influence of the popular drug xanax, in which Drake has made reference to before in “Overdose”, Drake doesn’t make rational decisions he might often regret just because of his infamous lust for women\n\nExample from “Marvin’s Room”:\n\nPaid for their flights and hotels, I’m ashamed", "annotation_id": "3020922", "url": "https://genius.com/3020922", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Young nigga switchin’ up the program right now Tryna put the power in my own hands right now", "vault_lines": ["Young nigga switchin' up the program right now", "Tryna put the power in my own hands right now"], "meaning": "Drake switched from his more assertive and aggressive flow as presented on April 2014’s “Draft Day” to a softer poetic flow that reflects his ties to R&B. Furthermore, singer PARTYNEXTDOOR produced this beat, which further enhances the OVO R&B brand.\n\nThis release by Drake was also foreshadowed on “Draft Day”:\n\nThat boy singin' on every song when he know he can spit\nThat boy manifested it, that boy knew it was written\nThat boy did it on purpose", "annotation_id": "3020878", "url": "https://genius.com/3020878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Long flight home, ’nother Xan for your boy", "vault_lines": ["Take a flight here, pop a Xan for your boy", "Long flight home, 'nother Xan for your boy"], "meaning": "Zan is a nickname for Xanax is actually an anxiolytic of the benzodiazepine drug class. It is primarily used to treat severe anxiety like one who is afraid of heights or flying may experience on a plane. RiRi is on her way back home, so she popped another Xan for the flight home.", "annotation_id": "3020743", "url": "https://genius.com/3020743", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Damn, my lil’ nephew turned four already", "vault_lines": ["Damn, my lil' nephew turned four already"], "meaning": "Drake is so busy making music and living the life that the time passes by with him missing out on important events. His nephew turned four without him even noticing the time that went by…\n\nAs he says on the song “The Language”\n\n“Can’t even drive with the top off\nI been workin' so hard on the album, I missed the whole summer\n\nSaid nephew, named Ezee, below.  He’s @OVOniko’s son.\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/d5W2_Lr8TK/\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/mTRnq-DQMr/", "annotation_id": "3020462", "url": "https://genius.com/3020462", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Know I do this shit for Third Ward already Know I do this shit for H-Town already", "vault_lines": ["Know I do this shit for Third Ward already", "Know I do this shit for H-Town already"], "meaning": "Drizzy has a storied history with the H-town that dates back many years. Let’s take a quick glance at a history behind Drake’s relationship with Houston:\n\n\n2008: Drake meets mentor Lil Wayne on November 18th in Houston which would eventually inspire a song on his mixtape. Drake spoke on his meeting with Lil Wayne:\n\nI felt like I hit Houston and got my swag back. I was single, I was with Wayne and it was Houston, I was going nuts, sipping drank, smoking\n\n2009: Drake releases So Far Gone which includes a collaboration with Houston mogul Bun B, a tribute to DJ Screw, and a song entitled “Houstatlantavegas”.\nOpening for Bun at Warehouse Live in 2009, he played the infamous “Mo City Don Freestyle” by Z-Ro that had the Houston natives taking over his set.\n2010: Drake proclaims he will rep H Town till the day he dies while performing the “June 27th” inspired track in Houston.\n2013: Drake shows off his Astros tattoo on Instagram. His tribute for everything Houston has done for him. He also proclaims H- town to be his second home\n\n\n\n\nFrom this small timeline we can see the indelible impression Houston has left on Drake. It was the first place Drake met Lil Wayne, and thus the birthplace of Champagne Papi’s career. At least that’s one thing him and Beyonce have in common.", "annotation_id": "3020430", "url": "https://genius.com/3020430", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "I I threw my hands in the air and said show me somethin’, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I I threw my hands in the air and said show me somethin', yeah"], "meaning": "The line is sampled from Rihanna’s track “Stay,” which may confirm that this is about her.", "annotation_id": "3020330", "url": "https://genius.com/3020330", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Waking up in Amsterdam with the boy", "vault_lines": ["Waking up in Amsterdam with the boy"], "meaning": "Drake and Rihanna were recently spotted enjoying dinner in Amsterdam together, fueling speculation that they had rekindled their romance.", "annotation_id": "3020327", "url": "https://genius.com/3020327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Yeah, the other night Chubbs showed up at my house He had weed for me That my nigga ’til we gone, keep his ear to the streets for me", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, the other night Chubbs showed up at my house", "He had weed for me", "That my nigga 'til we gone, keep his ear to the streets for me"], "meaning": "Chubbs is an OVO crew member: Drake’s long time pal, bodyguard, and right-hand-man", "annotation_id": "3020243", "url": "https://genius.com/3020243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Remember one night I went to Erykah Badu house She made tea for me We talked about love and what life could really be for me She said, “When that shit is real, you just know”", "vault_lines": ["Remember one night I went to Erykah Badu house", "She made tea for me", "We talked about love and what life could really be for me", "She said, \"When that shit is real, you just know\"", "You know you got..."], "meaning": "Erykah Badu is always looking out for Drake. She even encouraged him to respond to Kendrick’s “Control” verse.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/fatbellybella/status/386357018992181248\n\nBadu has had her own series of high-profile relationships, so she would be in a good position to give Drake advice if he were to, say, be dating a famous pop star.\n\nErykah Badu even did a Vine about this particular line.\n\nhttps://vine.co/v/MgvOBZ6lDVu", "annotation_id": "3020237", "url": "https://genius.com/3020237", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Told you ‘bout givin’ him chances on chances on chances He’s not holdin’ you down, he’s holdin’ you back right now", "vault_lines": ["Told you 'bout givin' him chances on chances on chances", "She's not here right now, she's not here right now", "He's not holdin' you down, he's holdin' you back right now"], "meaning": "Many media outlets have speculated that these lines refer to Rihanna’s controversial relationship with Chris Brown. The two were supposedly back together towards the end of 2012 and the start of 2013\n\nThis is also a reference to “Practice” where he says:\n\nDon’t give them no more chances, oh girl they had their turn\n\nThis could be an indirect reference to DJ Khaled’s song ‘Hold You Down’ where Chris Brown features heavily and fittingly sings the chorus “I’ll Hold You Down” – this fits with the message below.", "annotation_id": "3020224", "url": "https://genius.com/3020224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Yeah, why you keep askin’ me about... (Why?) She’s not here right now, she’s not here right now", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, why you keep askin' me about... (Why?)", "Why you keep askin' me about... (Why?)", "She's not here right now, she's not here right now"], "meaning": "Drake never finishes the first line, leaving the listener to question who he’s talking about. Many believe it’s Rihanna, not only because the third line ends with “drama,” but because the two have a history together, as reported in an April 2014 MTV News article that states:\n\nthey’ve been spending plenty of time together in Europe\n\nIf the missing word isn’t Rihanna, however, Drake could be be reassuring Rihanna that she’s the only one for him.\n\n\n\nIt should also be noted that the response vocals at the end of these lines are sampled from the music group, The Kids at Widney High from the 2005  film, The Ringer.", "annotation_id": "2982684", "url": "https://genius.com/2982684", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "Spendin’ all my days on the east side, oh Forgettin’ who I was on the other side, oh", "vault_lines": ["Spendin' all my days on the east side, oh", "Forgettin' who I was on the other side, oh"], "meaning": "Drake spends a lot of time on the Eastside of Toronto, usually with his main man Chubbs, as he stated on his June 2013 track, “The Motion”:\n\nAnd my dog Chubby Chub, that’s my nigga from the way\nOn the Eastside of the city, that’s where everybody stay\n\nHe rapped about the difficulties of balancing his lifestyle with his relationships and family on his June 2010 track, “The Resistance”:\n\nI heard they just moved my grandmother to a nursing home\nAnd I be acting like I don’t know how to work a phone", "annotation_id": "2969718", "url": "https://genius.com/2969718", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "line": "I’m about to ride through the old hood right now", "vault_lines": ["I'm about to ride through the old hood right now"], "meaning": "Drake’s hometown is Toronto, Canada—a place he shouts out on many songs, such as March 2013’s “5 A.M in Toronto.” Drake is also a global ambassador for the Toronto Raptors.\n\n\n\nThe “old hood,” could also be Memphis, Tennessee, where Drake spent his summers with his dad.", "annotation_id": "2965502", "url": "https://genius.com/2965502", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Days in the East", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 21, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-days-in-the-east-lyrics", "genius_title": "Days in the East"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "So much, we gotta count the 20s up in a different room", "vault_lines": ["So much, we gotta count the 20s up in a different room"], "meaning": "Drake has so much money it won’t fit in one room. He has to count it in multiple places since.", "annotation_id": "40874821", "url": "https://genius.com/40874821", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "The things that we’ve done to protect the name are unsettling But no regrets, though, the name’ll echo Years later, none greater", "vault_lines": ["The things that we've done to protect the name are unsettling", "But no regrets, though, the name'll echo", "Years later, none greater"], "meaning": "The ‘name’ here alludes to Drake’s son, Adonis. His existence was brought into the spotlight during Drake’s notorious beef with Pusha T in 2018, notably on the track “Story of Adidon”:\n\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\nDeadbeat mothafucka playin' border patrol, ooh\nAdonis is your son\nAnd he deserves more than an Adidas press run, that’s real\n\n\n\n Drake and his son, Adonis\n\nDespite taking significant steps to keep his son out of the public eye, Drake remains confident that his (and his son’s) legacy will outshine any controversy.", "annotation_id": "35115246", "url": "https://genius.com/35115246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "They say that they happy, my man, that’s not how they seem to be", "vault_lines": ["They say that they happy, my man, that's not how they seem to be"], "meaning": "Given the context of Drake’s beef with Pusha T (who’s basically Pharrell Williams’s protégé), this line feels like a shot. Pharrell’s biggest solo hit is literally called “Happy,” so Drake might be flipping that title here to imply that the other camp isn’t as content as they act and are tired of watching him dominate the charts and stay on top of the game.\n\n\n\n  Pharrell Williams performing “Happy” at the 2014 Oscars", "annotation_id": "35115088", "url": "https://genius.com/35115088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "I plan to buy your most personal belongings when they up for auction", "vault_lines": ["I plan to buy your most personal belongings when they up for auction"], "meaning": "This line seems to be aimed at both Pusha T and Kanye West, who once held the upper hand in their feud with Drake. However, Drake is positioning himself as someone who will have the last laugh.\n\nThe idea of buying someone’s “most personal belongings” at an auction implies that when his rivals eventually fall from grace, Drake will be there to take a piece of their legacy. It shows that his grudge isn’t just temporary—it’s something he’s determined to take to the grave. It’s also a longevity flex, as in he will remain financially secure unlike others, in the long run.\n\nDrake has long been prolific in collecting memorabilia, often acquiring items tied to rivalries or influential figures. One notable example is a “mic signed by Pusha T,” which he referenced in “Duppy Freestyle”:\n\nI had a microphone of yours, but then the signature faded\nI think that pretty much resembles what’s been happenin' lately\n\nIn 2023, Drake would buy Pharrell Williams’ jewelry collection at an auction, acquiring chains, pendants, and other iconic pieces that Pharrell wore at the height of his career. He later showcased these in his “Jumbotron Shit Poppin” music video. https://i.insider.com/63c828bc2a1e600018b89282?width=700\n\nDrake literally manifested these lyrics with the Pharrell jewelry purchase especially since Pharrell himself is another one of Drake’s rivals. This move carried additional weight because Pharrell was once Pusha T’s biggest supporter and mentor in the industry, making the acquisition even more potent in the context of the  ongoing rivalry.", "annotation_id": "34415031", "url": "https://genius.com/34415031", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Man, truth be told, I think about it often", "vault_lines": ["Man, truth be told, I think about it often"], "meaning": "There has been some rumors circulating within the hip-hop forums that were sparked by a post made on the forum KanyeToThe (now deleted) by a user named “600notnice” who claimed to be the cousin of producer Vinylz and said that Drake’s diss track against Pusha T and Kanye West was titled “Truth Be Told”. The news would even inspire an anticipation thread for the diss track on the forum. The thread was shortly posted after Drake was seen leaving or arriving at his apartment in 15 Fort York shortly after Pusha T dropped “The Story of Adidon,” This is the same apartment where Drake used to live and recorded most of his hits.\n\n\n\n Drake leaving or arriving at his Fort York apt in his Rolls Royce shortly after Pusha released “The Story of Adidon”\n\nInstead of releasing a response track, Drake opted to focus on his music and career, keeping the animosity simmering rather than escalating it further, especially considering the personal stakes involved. On Drake’s interview with Rap Radar, he said that Vinylz produced the beat for his unreleased/shelved diss track.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/Xcta-ROw8JM?si=A_0ti1o-DHUZoeKa&t=90\n\nThe fact that the post in question pre-dates the track “Omertà” and that the track in itself includes multiple jabs thrown at Kanye and Pusha, might be supportive of this rumor, also the subtle mention of the contemplation is very reminiscent of other instances where Drake was hinting at retaliation vs the Kanye/Pusha T Camp:\n\n\nYeah, I was about to— Man, I thought about it\nIt’s unsettlin' to talk about it “Survival”\n\nI got a whole ‘nother level that I can tap into “8 Out of 10”", "annotation_id": "32983768", "url": "https://genius.com/32983768", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "To me, Benihana is pigeon food", "vault_lines": ["To me, Benihana is pigeon food"], "meaning": "Benihana is a restaurant chain know for their expensive Asian cuisine. Drake is comparing them to “pigeon food\" showing that with his expensive lifestyle, even a pricey restaurant means little to him.", "annotation_id": "17453196", "url": "https://genius.com/17453196", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Send ’em to Lagos or Turks and Caicos Barbados, Trinidad and Tobago", "vault_lines": ["Send 'em to Lagos or Turks and Caicos"], "meaning": "Not long after the release of this song, Drake took his own advice:\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/By5i79UgZqK/", "annotation_id": "17427282", "url": "https://genius.com/17427282", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "I don’t carry cash ’cause the money is digital It’s the American Expresser, the debt collector", "vault_lines": ["I don't carry cash 'cause the money is digital", "It's the American Expresser, the debt collector"], "meaning": "Drake rather keep his wealth in the bank than in a stack of bank notes. ‘Digital’ refers to cards, checks, and mobile payments.\n\nHe follows up with a double entendre in ‘American Expresser,’ which could be a play on American Express Credit Cards. Much like credit card companies, American gangsters would ‘debt collect’ money from local businesses or people in their interest.", "annotation_id": "17408512", "url": "https://genius.com/17408512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "I’m buyin’ the buildin’ of every door that closed on me", "vault_lines": ["I'm buyin' the buildin' of every door that closed on me"], "meaning": "‘Closed doors’ represent a missed opportunity, often due to doubt in potential or circumstances out of our control. Drake persevered from his humble beginnings to become one of the most prolific artists of the decade.\n\nDrake detailed these rejections on “Look What You’ve Done”:\n\nYou took me places, you spent that They said no, we went back\nChecks bounced but we bounced back I put all the money in your accounts back“\n\n‘Buyin the building’ can be literally or metaphorically seen as a rite of success and a message to all those who doubted him.", "annotation_id": "17405478", "url": "https://genius.com/17405478", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "I’m ’bout to call the bluff of anybody that fold on me", "vault_lines": ["I'm 'bout to call the bluff of anybody that fold on me"], "meaning": "Drake drops off a bit of clever poker wordplay to deliver a warning to those who cross him, promising that he will not allow traitors to lurk in his circle without being identified.\n\nAccording to Wikipedia:\n\nIn the card game of poker, a bluff is a bet or raise made with a hand which is not thought to be the best hand. To bluff is to make such a bet. The objective of a bluff is to induce a fold by at least one opponent who holds a better hand. The size and frequency of a bluff determines its profitability to the bluffer. By extension, the phrase “calling somebody’s bluff” is often used outside the context of poker to describe cases where one person “demand[s] that someone prove a claim” or prove that he or she “is not being deceptive.”\n\nOn the other hand, to fold is to discard one’s hand and forfeit interest in the current pot. No further bets are required by the folding player, but the player cannot win.", "annotation_id": "17402446", "url": "https://genius.com/17402446", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "The petty king, the overseer of many things", "vault_lines": ["The petty king, the overseer of many things"], "meaning": "Drake is well known for keeping his petty acts no secret. Most recently in his beef with Pusha-T, on his track “Duppy Freestyle”, he’s quoted as saying:\n\nTell ‘Ye we got a invoice comin’ to you\nConsiderin' that we just sold another 20 for you\n\nPusha-T then initially responds on Twitter by saying:\n\nSend the invoice for the extra 20…\n\nSoon after, Drake responds on Instagram with the following image:\n\n\n\nHe describes his professional services rendered as promotional assistance and career revival.\n\nBut Drake’s trolling doesn’t end there. Back in 2015 when he was beefing with Meek Mill, he put on a huge anti-Meek Mill set during his annual OVO Fest that included Meek Mill memes and even a “Free Meek Mill” t-shirt he wore.\n\n\n\nSo whether it be online or in real life, Drizzy is proving to really be the petty king.", "annotation_id": "17400441", "url": "https://genius.com/17400441", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Rippin’ markers up over shots of the Henny", "vault_lines": ["Rippin' markers up over shots of the Henny"], "meaning": "Markers are lines of credit that are issued by casinos to gamblers, typically reserved for high rollers with reputable sources of wealth or income.\n\nHenny is short for Hennessy, a popular brand of Cognac.\n\nIn this line, Drake describes the life of luxury he’s living, ripping up the debts he owes to Vegas Casinos while drinking shots of Hennessy.", "annotation_id": "17398001", "url": "https://genius.com/17398001", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Haunted houses I don’t know how to count in thousands, only millions", "vault_lines": ["I don't know how to count in thousands, only millions"], "meaning": "Drake is once again referencing his brand October’s Very Own and record label, OVO Sound. Haunted houses are common around Halloween, which is celebrated at the end of October.\n\nDrake also says that he only counts in millions, which is a reputable claim given that his net worth was estimated at $150 Million when this song was released in June of 2019.", "annotation_id": "17397965", "url": "https://genius.com/17397965", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Monopoly action", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Monopoly is the age-old board game that challenges players to conquer the economy. Drake is claiming that he has a monopoly on the music industry, with all of his projects charting at number one on the Billboard Top 200, except for his February 2009 So Far Gone mixtape.\n\nhttps://i.genius.com/79e5b144af2fbd2ecf66807567160e35a559590e?url=https%3A%2F%2Fshawetcanada.files.wordpress.com%2F2018%2F08%2Fdlkbtm2w4aalqp_1.jpg%3Fquality%3D80%26strip%3Dall%26w%3D720%26h%3D480%26crop%3D1\n\n In August 2018, Scotiabank Arena gifted Drake a customized Monopoly gameboard for his joint tour with Migos.", "annotation_id": "17397666", "url": "https://genius.com/17397666", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Ethics and values, mob traditions, old fashioned", "vault_lines": ["Ethics and values, mob traditions, old fashioned"], "meaning": "This line alludes to the song title, “Omertà,” which is a longstanding tradition in the Italian mafia that subscribes a code of silence about illegal activities and non-compliance with law enforcement. The Sicilian Mafia, or “Cosa Nostra” (Italian for “Our Thing”), that Drake shouts out later in the song still practice omertà.", "annotation_id": "17397652", "url": "https://genius.com/17397652", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "And some gunnas that’ll hit you outta nowhere like epiphany Really, that’s it to me Aside from the obvious, man, it changes in scenery Testin’ me gon’ have my niggas testin’ machinery", "vault_lines": ["And some gunnas that'll hit you outta nowhere like epiphany", "Really, that's it to me", "Aside from the obvious, man, it changes in scenery", "Testin' me gon' have my niggas testin' machinery"], "meaning": "An epiphany is a sudden realization that doesn’t seem to stem from a previous thought and feels like it came out of nowhere.\n\nDrake also talks about having his crew taking care of enemies, a sentiment previously mentioned on the 2018 track “Sandra’s Rose”:\n\nNiggas scared to come towards us, gotta run from us\nLouisville hush money for my young gunners", "annotation_id": "17397634", "url": "https://genius.com/17397634", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "West Hollywood, know my presence is menacing Cosa Nostra, shady dealings Racketeering, the syndicate got they hand in plenty things", "vault_lines": ["West Hollywood, know my presence is menacing", "Cosa Nostra, shady dealings", "Racketeering, the syndicate got they hand in plenty things"], "meaning": "Drake resides in Calabasas and has been seen in Hollywood before—in October and July 2018, he was seen at the Delilah Nightclub, located in West Hollywood.\n\nCosa Nostra is a mafia branch that operates in the United States and originates from Italy. The phrase translates to “our thing,” and is synonymous with “mafia.” It is also indicative of loyalty within a mob, which Drake explored on June 2018’s, “Mob Ties”:\n\nI fuck with the mob and I got ties (lotta ties, lotta ties)\nKnock you off to pay their tithes (do doo)\n\nThe reference to Cosa Nostra also ties in with the title of the track, “Omertà,” the mafia’s (formerly) unbreakable oath of silence.\n\nRacketeering is a fraudulent way of obtaining money and is often associated with a syndicate such as the mafia. On Meek Mill’s June 2015 track, “R.I.C.O.,” Drake mentions the term as well:\n\nOVO, East End, Reps Up, we might just get hit with the R.I.C.O", "annotation_id": "17397311", "url": "https://genius.com/17397311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "My enemies send each other the texts that they could never send me", "vault_lines": ["My enemies send each other the texts that they could never send me"], "meaning": "Even though Drake has made peace with a couple of his past “Enemies” (such as Meek Mill and Chris Brown), he still has his share of enemies like Pusha T that keep cursing him behind his back. This is nothing new for Drake.\n\n He also alluded to his enemies teaming up on his track “Hype”:\n\nMy enemies wanna be friends with my other enemies\nI don’t let it get to me.", "annotation_id": "17396444", "url": "https://genius.com/17396444", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "The mind controller, the Ayatollah I built a bridge to success and had visions of me ridin’ over", "vault_lines": ["The mind controller, the Ayatollah", "I built a bridge to success and had visions of me ridin' over"], "meaning": "An Ayatollah, literally “Sign of God” in Arabic, is a high-ranking clergyman who is well versed in the studies of Islam. They use their knowledge to interpret the Quran in the way they believe it was meant when revealed.\n\n\n\n Ali Khamenei, the current Grand Ayatollah as of 2019\n\nThe Grand Ayatollahs have the ability to make legislative decisions, like the Supreme Leader of Iran pictured above. More importantly, however, they are known for having widespread influence among followers of Islam’s Shi'a branch. Drake suggests that the influence of an Ayatollah is similar to that of someone who has the ability to control minds and mold them.\n\nHe also mentions visions of his rise to success, which can be considered prophetic, hence his allusions to religion and use of religious terminology.", "annotation_id": "17396342", "url": "https://genius.com/17396342", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Keepin’ my connections strictly physical Everyone that’s married is miserable I know that that is not a lifestyle I can give into", "vault_lines": ["Keepin' my connections strictly physical", "Everyone that's married is miserable", "I know that that is not a lifestyle I can give into"], "meaning": "This might partly be about Drake’s own experience with his parents' marriage, which didn’t last long. His father Dennis Graham, a musician, left his wife Sandi and his son when Aubrey was only five.\n\nDrake himself became a father in 2017, which he kept secret. He does not live with Sophie Brussaux and their son Adonis.\n\n\n\nDennis and Sandi Graham, Drake’s parents", "annotation_id": "17396341", "url": "https://genius.com/17396341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Step in the room and October gets a lot closer", "vault_lines": ["Step in the room and October gets a lot closer"], "meaning": "October’s Very Own or OVO is the name of Drake’s clique and record label he founded in 2012. It’s also the name of his clothing line, festival, and radio show. Additionally, Drake was born on October 24th, 1986.", "annotation_id": "17396301", "url": "https://genius.com/17396301", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Hailin’ all the way from the Mecca", "vault_lines": ["Hailin' all the way from the Mecca"], "meaning": "Mecca is a city in Saudi Arabia. It is regarded as the holiest site in Islam and is also the birthplace of the prophet Muhammad. Drake hails from Toronto, which was dubbed the “Mecca of the North” in a March 2015 article by the Toronto Sun.\n\nYoung Money founder, Lil Wayne, used a similar line on Nicki Minaj’s October 2014 track, “Only,” which featured Drake as well:\n\nAll my goons so overzealous\nI’m from Hollygrove, the holy Mecca\n\n\n\nMecca, Saudi Arabia", "annotation_id": "17396279", "url": "https://genius.com/17396279", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "This shit could last forever", "vault_lines": ["This shit could last forever"], "meaning": "Drake’s dominance in the music industry has yet to waiver over his illustrious career. He’s been in the realm of musical superstardom since the success of his February 2009 mixtape, So Far Gone. It seems as if every year since, he has continued to break sales and streaming records, sometimes even breaking his own records. He’s also won a plethora of musical awards, from Grammys to BBMAs. Even discrediting accusations and scathing diss tracks have been unable to slow Drake’s success. It seems to many that he will be a powerhouse for as long as he is around.\n\nHowever, before dropping his first album in June of 2010, Drake rapped about his desire for continued success on the aptly titled August 2009 posse cut, “Forever”:\n\nIt may not mean nothin' to y'all\nBut understand, nothin' was done for me\nSo I don’t plan on stoppin' at all\nI want this shit forever, man\n\nThis line is almost a continuation of those above, as he’s realizing that it’s now a legitimate possibility.", "annotation_id": "17396273", "url": "https://genius.com/17396273", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Separate vacations A far cry from when Drizzy had slept in the basement", "vault_lines": ["A far cry from when Drizzy had slept in the basement"], "meaning": "Drake previously discussed living in the cellar on his September 2013 track, “Come Thru”:\n\nWho used to sleep on the floor with you when you lived in the basement?\n\nOverall, Drake boasts about his rise to fame and wealth, which provided him with a privileged and luxurious lifestyle and the ability to take “separate vacations.” Drizzy’s “rags to riches” story is present throughout his discography, perhaps most notably on his February 2013 track, “Started from the Bottom”:\n\nStarted from the bottom, now we’re here", "annotation_id": "17396230", "url": "https://genius.com/17396230", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "I wish that I was playin’ in a sport where we were gettin’ rings I wouldn’t have space on either hand for anything", "vault_lines": ["I wish that I was playin' in a sport where we were gettin' rings", "I wouldn't have space on either hand for anything"], "meaning": "Drake parallels the rap game to a sport—he made a similar comparison on the August 2016 track, “You Know, You Know”:\n\nI made it but I’m still playin' like I’m bein' scouted til I’m out it\nGame time bitch, I hope you’re proud of us\n\nIn American sports, players on championship teams receive rings as a memento of their success. In September 2015, Drake ordered custom championship rings from Elliot Avianne to celebrate the release of his collaborative mixtape with Future, What a Time to Be Alive.\n\nDrake is also the global ambassador of the Toronto Raptors, who won the 2019 NBA Finals. He received a championship ring due to his ties with the franchise and spoke on the value of the Raptors' victory in an interview with the media:\n\nThe six in six. Kyle Lowry with a ring. Kawhi Leonard bringing a chip to the city. I want my chips with the dip, that’s all I know. I don’t want my chips plain. I want my chips with the dip.", "annotation_id": "17396211", "url": "https://genius.com/17396211", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "The only thing I see is custom owls from Tiffany", "vault_lines": ["The only thing I see is custom owls from Tiffany"], "meaning": "Tiffany & Co. is a  luxury jewelry company that also sells fragrances, watches and other personal accessories. Drizzy previously referenced his jewelry acquisition from the brand on his April 2016 track, “Hype”:\n\nSwear I just had an epiphany\nIt cost me 50 at Tiffany’s\n\nDrake also has a few OVO owl pendants. In April 2017, the Toronto rapper acquired a diamond-encrusted owl chain from Ben Baller and had another OVO piece made in September 2018, courtesy of Jason Arasheben of Jason of Beverly Hills.\n\n\n\nIt is also important to note that Tiffany & Co is the company tasked with designing the NBA’s Larry O'Brien trophy. The Toronto Raptors, Drake’s hometown team, famously won their first championship in franchise history during 2018-2019 NBA season, which will ultimately result in a new trophy for the team.", "annotation_id": "17396201", "url": "https://genius.com/17396201", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "I was never on the path to get into Cambridge I mean, I was good at doin’ math, but I’m better with language", "vault_lines": ["I was never on the path to get into Cambridge", "I mean, I was good at doin' math, but I'm better with language"], "meaning": "Drake must be referencing Cambridge University, a world-renowned institution in Cambridge, UK. Also, Cambridge in Massachusetts is home to two of the most prestigious and selective colleges in the world; Harvard University and Massachusetts Institute of Technology.\n\nOn the more obscure cut “A Milli Freestyle,” Drake rapped a similar line:\n\nDope boys know me cause I often speak they language, so well spoken man I should have went to Cambridge.\n\n“Doin' math” likely means to count money, in fact, Drake’s entrepreneurship made him one of the top 5 most prosperous rappers worldwide. Considering this song was released in celebration of the Toronto Raptors “better at language” may allude to his notorious courtside trash-talk, which was especially showcased during the 2019 season.", "annotation_id": "17396189", "url": "https://genius.com/17396189", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "The rise to the top of this mountain has been biblical", "vault_lines": ["The rise to the top of this mountain has been biblical"], "meaning": "Mountains appear often in biblical scripture; they display powerful imagery of successes and overcoming obstacles.\n\nThis line could particularly refer to the sacrifice of Isaac story which took place at the summit of Mount Moriah. Drake is no stranger to sacrifices, as he’s made many on his ascent to the top of the rap game.\n\nIt could also be a timely reference to the Toronto Raptors, of which Drake is the global ambassador. The Raptor’s 2018 pre-season trade of beloved franchise player DeMar DeRozan, along with Jakob Poeltl and a protected 2019 first round pick, in return for San Antonio Spurs players Kawhi Leonard and Danny Green, had a biblical flavour to it – DeRozan even describes himself as a sacrificial lamb. This trade was pivotal in bringing the Raptors their first NBA Championship in June 2019.", "annotation_id": "17396116", "url": "https://genius.com/17396116", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "A lot of pain, a lot of passion A lot of relaxin’ while other niggas is overreactin’ That’s how we continue down the path of Jordan and Jackson That’s some insight for y’all even if no one’s askin’", "vault_lines": ["A lot of pain, a lot of passion", "A lot of relaxin' while other niggas is overreactin'", "That's how we continue down the path of Jordan and Jackson", "That's some insight for y'all even if no one's askin'"], "meaning": "Here, Drake points to himself and LeBron as the spiritual successors to Michael Jackson and Michael Jordan, respectively (following in the footsteps of Biggie). Drake is arguably the biggest musical act on the planet and LeBron is widely regarded as the greatest active basketball player in the world and one of the best ever.\n\nMichael Jordan is one of the most successful NBA players of all time, winning six championships during his illustrious NBA career. The NBA describes him as “the greatest basketball player of all time.”\n\nMichael Jackson is called “The King of Pop,” and is considered one of the greatest musicians and performers of all time. In an untimely homage, Drake used a sample from an unfinished MJ song, to make him appear on his track “Don’t Matter to Me”, off of his 2018 album, Scorpion.\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake has drawn parallels between music and hoops. Perhaps most notably, Drake rapped on “Thank Me Now”:\n\nDamn, I swear sports and music are so synonymous\nCause we want to be them, and they want to be us", "annotation_id": "17396111", "url": "https://genius.com/17396111", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Death to a coward and a traitor, that’s just in my nature, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Death to a coward and a traitor, that's just in my nature, yeah"], "meaning": "In the italian mafia, the Omertà refers to the oath of silence that members of the family must respect even by putting their lives on the line. Thus the family inflicts the death sentence to a traitor or to a member dishonoring the group with their cowardliness, as payback.\n\nDrake naturally sees in the Omertà a fitting punishment for those who betray him or let him down. He has thorough the song consistently established a picture of himself as a sovereign of undisputable dominion by counting his deeds and accomplishments, and likens himself here to a mafia godfather taking care of his family affairs, thus making of the OVO team the mafia he commands.\n\nDrizzy often takes the role of a ruler to reflect his success and established position in the game, and he has already mentionned being the head of the mafia in his track “No Tellin'” out of his 2015 surprise mixtape If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late :\n\nV-Live, I order that Alfredo pasta\nThen eat in the kitchen like I’m in the mafia\nHouston, they get me though\n\nThis lyric may be alluding to Drake’s 2018 beef between former collaborator and friend Kanye West and his fellow G.O.O.D. Music member Pusha T.\n\nWithin his diss-response to Drake’s “Duppy Freestyle,” Pusha T revealed that Drake is a father, and apparently hiding his child from the world. Drake reportedly confided in Kanye while in Wyoming, where he collaborated on Ye’s Yikes and shared an early listen on the upcoming Scorpion. Interestingly, Pusha’s diss was also produced in Wyoming.\n\nDrake could thus refer to Pusha as the ‘coward’ and Kanye as the ‘traitor’ because the circumstances suggest that Kanye acted maliciously as an informant, although Pusha denies these claims.", "annotation_id": "17396107", "url": "https://genius.com/17396107", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Last year, niggas really feel like they rode on me Last year, niggas got hot ’cause they told on me", "vault_lines": ["Last year, niggas really feel like they rode on me", "Last year, niggas got hot 'cause they told on me"], "meaning": "In May 2018, Drake and Pusha T exchanged diss tracks, starting with Pusha T’s “Infrared.” Pusha claimed Quentin Miller was Drake’s ghostwriter, which led to Drake’s response, “Duppy Freestyle,” where he admitted to receiving some help on his verses. Pusha T would issue the last official diss track between the two with “The Story of Adidon,” even though Drake continues to throw subtle jabs in various songs, like here and on June 2018’s “8 Out of 10.”\n\nThe rap battle ultimately culminated when Pusha T revealed that Drake was “hiding [his] child.” The exposure Push received after the diss track was so great that Drake sent an invoice to Pusha’s label, G.O.O.D. Music, for $100,000, claiming he wanted compensation for reviving Pusha’s career.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BjOL4WcHt-E/?utm_source=ig_embed\n\nPusha T’s album, DAYTONA, went on to receive a nomination for Best Rap Album of 2018 at the 61st Grammy Awards, suggesting that the publicity from the feud was not the sole factor for Pusha’s success.", "annotation_id": "17396085", "url": "https://genius.com/17396085", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "The boy, he wild and peaceful, rest in peace Teena Marie", "vault_lines": ["The boy, he wild and peaceful, rest in peace Teena Marie"], "meaning": "Wild and Peaceful is the debut album from American singer Teena Marie, which released in 1979.\n\nShe was known as the “Ivory Queen Of Soul” and released 13 projects before passing away at the age of 54 in 2010.", "annotation_id": "17396084", "url": "https://genius.com/17396084", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Yeah, Loro Piana and Brioni, the one and only", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, Loro Piana and Brioni, the one and only"], "meaning": "Loro Piana and Brioni are two luxury Italian brands that have lots of heritage within Italian fashion. These brands that Drake brings up are just another example on this track of the luxurious life he lives, from, “popping champagne,” to name-dropping other luxury brands, like Tiffany.\n\nEarlier this year in April, Drake was seen wearing a custom Brioni jacket inscribed, “Brioni for Aubrey Graham,” the rapper’s birth name, on the inside of the jacket. The jacket was valued at $11,000.", "annotation_id": "17396083", "url": "https://genius.com/17396083", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "I’m bankin’ two million a show for the residency Nevada gaming commission in a frenzy How much money can this casino lend me?", "vault_lines": ["I'm bankin' two million a show for the residency", "Nevada gaming commission in a frenzy", "How much money can this casino lend me?"], "meaning": "A concert or musical residency is a series of concerts performed by an artist at a single venue rather than traveling to different places. They are a prominent aspect of Las Vegas life.\n\nOn January 10, 2019, during a performance at The Wynn Las Vegas’ XS Nightclub, Drake hinted that he would have a residency at XS, telling the crowd:\n\nVegas! When I came back, I thought it’d be back at T-Mobile Arena or some shit, but I’ll be back here at XS all year. You’ll have a lotta chances to see me.\n\nIt was reported by the Las Vegas Review-Journal that Drake earned $2 million for the show in which he made the announcement. Individuals close to the situation also stated that Drake will perform twelve shows over the course of two years. By Drake’s math, he will have earned $24 million from The Wynn alone by 2020’s end.\n\nDrake officially began his residency on May 4, 2019.", "annotation_id": "17396081", "url": "https://genius.com/17396081", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Bronny buyin’ up Brentwood like he still in Akron", "vault_lines": ["Bronny buyin' up Brentwood like he still in Akron"], "meaning": "LeBron James is a professional basketball player, who—at the time of this song’s release—plays for the Los Angeles Lakers. In July 2018, it was reported that James had purchased a $23 million mansion in Brentwood. In addition to this, LeBron also has another house in his hometown of Akron, Ohio. The star athlete has a net worth of $440 million.\n\nLeBron and Drake are close friends and have known each other for more than a decade. In the second episode of his HBO series, The Shop, James expanded on his friendship with Drake, and discussed his humble beginnings:\n\nWe’ve grown closer and closer every year, and I’m proud of who he is today. Not only as an artist, not only as a son, but as a father. And he’s one of the biggest stars that we have on this planet right now. […] When you grow up in the inner city and you don’t have much, all you do is have aspirations and dreams and things of being more than what you actually can be…", "annotation_id": "17396076", "url": "https://genius.com/17396076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "line": "Champagne popper, the love doctor", "vault_lines": ["Champagne popper, the love doctor"], "meaning": "Drake references his Instagram username and alter ego, Champagne Papi. He first used this username in 2011 and has not changed it since. Drake plays on “papi,” the common colloquialism for dad in Spanish, and “papa,” a similar term used in English for father. This is a double entendre as Drake is also deeming himself “the popper” of champagne.\n\nDrake is also well known for his attractiveness to women, which he boasts here by considering himself to be the “love doctor.” He frequently raps about his sexual prowess, such as on his 2016 collaboration with DJ Khaled, “For Free,” where he raps:\n\nYeah, I go on and on\nCan’t understand how I last so long\nI must have the superpowers\nLast 223 thousand hours\n\nDrake also caused controversy for giving dating advice to actress Millie Bobby Brown, who was merely 15 at the time of this song’s release. Coincidentally, one article even deems Drizzy a “teen love doctor.”", "annotation_id": "17396074", "url": "https://genius.com/17396074", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Omertà", "status": "done", "targets": 36, "clicked": 36, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-omerta-lyrics", "genius_title": "Omertà"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "S.T.R.E.S.S.", "line": "Like girls with Double D’s, I got a lot on my chest", "vault_lines": ["I got stress on my shoulders", "Like girls with Double D's, I got a lot on my chest"], "meaning": "A double D is a quite enormous bra size, so any girl with them would literally have a lot on her chest.\n\n\n\nDrake uses this simile to elaborate on what he metaphorically has on his chest – stress.", "annotation_id": "1062558", "url": "https://genius.com/1062558", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "S.T.R.E.S.S.", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 1, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-stress-lyrics", "genius_title": "S.T.R.E.S.S."}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Winner", "line": "Thanks to my mother for never givin’ up You deserve the world, now, gon’ live it up", "vault_lines": ["Thanks to my mother for never givin' up", "You deserve the world, now, gon' live it up"], "meaning": "Drake talks about his mother  struggling but did everything to keep her little boy happy. Now that he made it, he thanks her and shares his Millions with her. He mentions this theory in “Fireworks”:\n\nGot my mother in a place with some better decor, She searched the entire city, I let her explore", "annotation_id": "2694332", "url": "https://genius.com/2694332", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Winner", "line": "Yeah, and Polo isn’t at my session", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and Polo isn't at my session"], "meaning": "Polo is short for Polow Da Don, who is a famous Hip-hop/R&B producer.", "annotation_id": "90927", "url": "https://genius.com/90927", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Winner", "line": "SoHo, Cosmo, Balmain blow fish", "vault_lines": ["SoHo, Cosmo, Balmain blow fish"], "meaning": "The Soho Cosmo (short for Soho Cosmopolitan) and Bymark are very high end luxury hotels in Downtown Toronto, Drake’s native city.", "annotation_id": "90924", "url": "https://genius.com/90924", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Winner", "line": "Don’t cry for me, this ain’t motherfucking Mario", "vault_lines": ["Don't cry for me, this ain't motherfucking Mario"], "meaning": "He’s referring to R&B singer Mario’s song called “Crying out For Me”.", "annotation_id": "54230", "url": "https://genius.com/54230", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Winner", "line": "They say gon’ head, ball like Ms. Arie and I do", "vault_lines": ["They say gon' head, ball like Ms. Arie and I do"], "meaning": "Singer India Arie has a shaved head like Drake. A similar line was used by Lil Wayne in their song “Gonorrhea”:\n\nBal' like Solange, India Arie, Britney Spears", "annotation_id": "54225", "url": "https://genius.com/54225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Winner", "line": "But I, always got a Starbucks verse Bein’ brewed, too hot, please, please double-cup me", "vault_lines": ["But I, always got a Starbucks verse", "Bein' brewed, too hot, please, please double-cup me"], "meaning": "His verses are as hot (awesome) as Starbucks coffee. To “double cup” means that you get two coffee cups to absorb the heat so that you don’t burn your hands. In other words, Drake’s verses are so hot that, if they were a coffee, you would need two cups to handle them\n\nThis could also be a reference to Lil Wayne’s song, “Me and My Drank” where he says:\n\nWill somebody please-please double cup me\nAnd everybody please-please don’t judge me", "annotation_id": "44823", "url": "https://genius.com/44823", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Winner", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 6, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-the-winner-lyrics", "genius_title": "The Winner"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Time to shake these haters off like the skin of a locust", "vault_lines": ["Time to shake these haters off like the skin of a locust"], "meaning": "A locust is an insect that sheds and shakes off its outer layer as it grows. Bun B is comparing haters to something he can simply shake off and leave behind.", "annotation_id": "40763648", "url": "https://genius.com/40763648", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Yeah, wrong way down a one-way", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, wrong way down a one-way"], "meaning": "Drake uses driving down a one-way as a metaphor to explain how he feels like he is going against everyone’s expectations and ignoring the path expected for him to follow. Many people didn’t think a Canadian actor from Degrassi would become one of the biggest rap artists of all time.", "annotation_id": "40763580", "url": "https://genius.com/40763580", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Weezy in ya mouth, now, Weezy what you talkin’ ’bout?", "vault_lines": ["Weezy in ya mouth, now, Weezy what you talkin' 'bout?"], "meaning": "reference to Weezer", "annotation_id": "38667938", "url": "https://genius.com/38667938", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Guess I might, know what? Fuck it, yes I will", "vault_lines": ["Guess I might, know what? Fuck it, yes I will"], "meaning": "Perhaps a subtle foreshadowing of Lil Wayne and Drake’s 2011 hit, She Will", "annotation_id": "36979349", "url": "https://genius.com/36979349", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Loc-ness", "vault_lines": ["The gangsta recognize me for my loc-ness"], "meaning": "Referring to ‘loco’ meaning crazy, therefore he’s recognizable for his craziness.", "annotation_id": "29520460", "url": "https://genius.com/29520460", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "That’s why I never follow y’all suggestions I just always did my own thing", "vault_lines": ["That's why I never follow y'all suggestions", "I just always did my own thing"], "meaning": "Drake didn’t allow other people to change him, and through that he found success", "annotation_id": "22262359", "url": "https://genius.com/22262359", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "’Cause you the type to lose her, and I’m about to get her", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you the type to lose her, and I'm about to get her"], "meaning": "Drake can get any girl that you can’t", "annotation_id": "22262341", "url": "https://genius.com/22262341", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Shit been goin’ good, but good can turn to better", "vault_lines": ["Shit been goin' good, but good can turn to better"], "meaning": "Drake is always improving himself, although he is arguably always at the top of the game", "annotation_id": "22262335", "url": "https://genius.com/22262335", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I’ll erase you like I drew you, bitch", "vault_lines": [", I'll erase you like I drew you, bitch"], "meaning": "Wayne could destroy you/your career as if he made it", "annotation_id": "22262320", "url": "https://genius.com/22262320", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "And now I’m on that rock shit", "vault_lines": ["That I'm on, I'm on, I'm on, I'm on", "I'm on, I'm on, I'm on, I'm on", "And now I'm on that rock shit"], "meaning": "At this time, Lil Wayne tried to incorporate more elements of rock music into his sound.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lTurfn7vvCk\n\nIn 2010, he Wayne released his “rock” album Rebirth.", "annotation_id": "19125102", "url": "https://genius.com/19125102", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I drive two black cars, I named ’em Malcolm X and Martin Luther", "vault_lines": ["I drive two black cars, I named 'em Malcolm X and Martin Luther"], "meaning": "Drake boasts his affluence by drawing a comparison between his multiple cars and two of the most important figures of the civil rights movement, Martin Luther King, Jr., and Malcolm X. Both activists for desegregation and equal-race rights, King and Malcolm X are considered some of the most impactful figures of the civil rights struggle.\n\n\n\nLater in 2009, Drake would make another reference to Malcolm X on his hit single “Forever”:\n\nLabels want my name beside a X, like Malcolm\nEverybody got a deal, I did it without one", "annotation_id": "19092908", "url": "https://genius.com/19092908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "From the free-throw line and slam it down like I’m the great Mike", "vault_lines": ["From the free-throw line and slam it down like I'm the great Mike"], "meaning": "Michael Jordan is a retired NBA basketball player who is often considered one of, if not the, greatest athletes the sport has ever seen. Jordan, who rose to fame during the 1990’s, was known for his shooting skills. Bun B is comparing the way he’s taking control over the rap game to the way Jordan took control over basketball in his prime.", "annotation_id": "17772347", "url": "https://genius.com/17772347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake uses a melody very similar to the one he would use two years later on his sophomore album Take Care:\n\nAre you down, are you down? Yeah, you all the way down, every time\nAm I down, am I down? Yeah, I’m all the way down, We’ll be fine\nAre you down, are you down? Yeah, you all the way down, every time\nAm I down, am I down? Yeah, I’m all the way down, We’ll be fine", "annotation_id": "13350394", "url": "https://genius.com/13350394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Look at all these posers bite our swagger like a great white", "vault_lines": ["Look at all these posers bite our swagger like a great white"], "meaning": "In rap, “biting” is slang for imitating or copying another person’s flows or character. Much the way that a Great White Shark bites its prey, people around Bun and his crew often try to imitate his ways.", "annotation_id": "13350354", "url": "https://genius.com/13350354", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "They just lose to love, those are tennis games, lady", "vault_lines": ["They just lose to love, those are tennis games, lady"], "meaning": "Drake forms a double entendre with “lose to love”:\n\nThe most obvious meaning is the one that extends the previous line. Drake claims to not be a player, but he is in the game nonetheless. Therefore, some girls lose to love when they falsely assume that he is committed to them.\n\nThe second meaning is that a score of 0 in a game of tennis is referred to as “love,” and losing to love means that the losing player did not win a single rally in the tennis game.\n\nInterestingly, Drake was romantically linked to tennis star Serena Williams, and based on the way he felt on “Nothings Into Somethings” from his 2017 project More Life, it was Drake that lost to love as Serena moved on from him and got engaged to Reddit co-founder Alexis Ohanian.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RGGUzrUYXP0", "annotation_id": "11793381", "url": "https://genius.com/11793381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The hook was originally going to be shared with Lil Wayne, using the opening bar of his verse “I am the leather jacket, black glasses all american bad boy” followed by “I’m so uptown, i’m so uptown, i’m so uptown.. and motherfucker if you aint don’t go uptown” followed by Drake’s part.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fbkvTiQCLIQ", "annotation_id": "6748412", "url": "https://genius.com/6748412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "And when they call you the answer, you are hardly questioned", "vault_lines": ["And when they call you the answer, you are hardly questioned"], "meaning": "Nobody is questioning Drake because he is “the answer” to what they want.  He could do anything with anyone doubting him.\n\nThis isn’t necessarily true, as Drake constantly get skewered for his singing, sweater styles, softness, etc.\n\nBesides it’s not always easy being “the Answer.”\nJust ask Allen Iverson, who went from being a premier baller to facing some serious struggles.", "annotation_id": "2188583", "url": "https://genius.com/2188583", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Sippin’ on Pink Floyd and puffin’ Wayne Brady", "vault_lines": ["Sippin' on Pink Floyd and puffin' Wayne Brady"], "meaning": "Pink Floyd was an English rock band that started making music in 1965. Though in this context, Drake is likely referencing codeine – which is generally referred to as purple drank, but is often pink in color.\n\n\n\nWayne Brady is what people call marijuana that makes you “choke” – Brady appeared on a famous Chapelle skit where he asked “Is Wayne Brady gonna have to choke a bitch?”.", "annotation_id": "1592669", "url": "https://genius.com/1592669", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I see all this money through my Ohio State Buckeyes", "vault_lines": ["I see all this money through my Ohio State Buckeyes"], "meaning": "A Buckeye is the name of OSU’s sports teams, it also refers to Ohio natives. But here “buck eyes” refer to the eyes Drake uses to look at all of his bucks/money!", "annotation_id": "557267", "url": "https://genius.com/557267", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I palm the game like it’s a Spalding ball and take flight", "vault_lines": ["I palm the game like it's a Spalding ball and take flight"], "meaning": "To “palm” refers to the control that Bun B has in the rap game. Bun B compares this control to that of a Spalding basketball in which he “takes flight”. “Flight” is the traditional moniker used by basketball legend Michael Jordan who is referenced in the next line.", "annotation_id": "69015", "url": "https://genius.com/69015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I’m so uptown", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "An alternate version of this song excludes Waynes verese and uses part of it as the hook, which makes more sense to call it Uptown. Otherwise this song should be called It’s Okay (I’m On) or something.\n\nThe song is called uptown because they took a sample from Uptown Girl by Billy Joel.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hCuMWrfXG4E\n\nWhen he say’s “i’m so uptown”, he just saying he so hood or so from the hood, and that’s why the next line, “mutherfucka if you ain’t, don’t go uptown”, means if you ain’t from this hood, or you ain’t hood enough don’t go there. There meaning literally the hood, or even deeper don’t act hood if you aren’t hood.\n\nThe song is called uptown because they took a sample from Uptown Girl by Billy Joel.", "annotation_id": "65613", "url": "https://genius.com/65613", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "And yo’ frame makes me wanna bowl a strike, well alright", "vault_lines": ["And yo' frame makes me wanna bowl a strike, well alright"], "meaning": "“Frame” refers to a girl’s body/figure, and this girl’s frame is so fine that it makes Drake want to bowl a strike. A bowling pin could be said to resemble the frame of a curvy woman.\n\n\n\nThis is also a bowling reference, as frame is the word for each “turn,” and bowling a strike refers to knocking all of the pins over in one go. Drake is a notorious fan of bowling.", "annotation_id": "47155", "url": "https://genius.com/47155", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Soowoo, bitch, I do this shit", "vault_lines": ["Soowoo, bitch, I do this shit"], "meaning": "“Soowoo” is a gang call used by Bloods to alert another member of something.\n\nMany people call Lil Wayne a fake gangster, so here Wayne is affirming he is indeed a real member of the blood gang who is really about that life, hence the next line.", "annotation_id": "47154", "url": "https://genius.com/47154", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "And I keep that toaster, you can come and be my strudel, bitch", "vault_lines": ["And I keep that toaster, you can come and be my strudel, bitch"], "meaning": "Some nice wordplay by Weezy in this line.  It can be interpreted a few different ways:\n\n\nWeezy has that toaster (gun), and will have you leaking red like a toaster strudel.\nA chick can be his toaster strudel and when he comes, he’s gonna cover her in white frosting. Yuck.\n\n\n\nAt the end of it, both interpretations include a pun on toaster strudels (obviously). And if you don’t like toaster strudels, then you are a terrorist.", "annotation_id": "47153", "url": "https://genius.com/47153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Damn, whose line is it anyways?", "vault_lines": ["Damn, whose line is it anyways?"], "meaning": "Wayne Brady was a regular on the United States versions of the improvisational comedy show “Whose Line is it Anyway?”\n\n\n\nWayne Brady also refers to cocaine. Drake wants to know… whose line is it anyway??", "annotation_id": "47144", "url": "https://genius.com/47144", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Women don’t get saved ’round me, even on a Sunday", "vault_lines": ["Women don't get saved 'round me, even on a Sunday"], "meaning": "To “get saved,” which would happen in a church, would normally occur on Sunday.  Women don’t get saved by him because he isn’t a “Captain Save  A Hoe”.\n\nDrake would go on to contradict this belief in “The Real Her” when he rapped about wanting to save a stripper no matter what his friends say.", "annotation_id": "47142", "url": "https://genius.com/47142", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "When I’m awake, you always restin’", "vault_lines": ["When I'm awake, you always restin'"], "meaning": "Drake is infamous for his late-night, around the clock studio sessions. On the MTV Documentary ‘Better Than Good Enough’ Drake admitted to sleeping and eating terribly because of his music career. Drake is in the studio cooking up classics, while his competition are in their beds getting a good night’s rest.", "annotation_id": "47139", "url": "https://genius.com/47139", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Now I run the game, you stupid motha-suckas", "vault_lines": ["Now I run the game, you stupid motha-suckas"], "meaning": "Michael Blackson, a Ghanaian-American comedian, famously uses “motha-sucka” in his comedy. Since Season 8, he has been a cast member of the hip-hop sketch show Wild ‘N Out, hosted by Nick Cannon.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N8xCwEkp25M", "annotation_id": "45032", "url": "https://genius.com/45032", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Uh, hardly home but always reppin’", "vault_lines": ["Uh, hardly home but always reppin'"], "meaning": "Drake often laments the fact that he is away from his hometown of Toronto in his songs. He has many second homes, such as Atlanta, Houston, Miami, and Memphis, where he spent a lot of time as a kid because his dad lived there. However, despite his nomadic lifestyle, Drake always represents his city in his music. One of the most common colloquial nicknames for Toronto is “the 6”, due to the area code 416 and the amalgamation of Toronto from six boroughs in 1998.\n\nSimilar lines in Drake’s discography are:\n\n“Worst Behavior”\n\nYeah, back and forth across the border line\nHate to leave the city, but I’ve got to do the overtime\n\n“10 Bands”\n\nMan, I told my city I’d be gone till November then November came\nThen I came, right back on my worst behav'", "annotation_id": "44534", "url": "https://genius.com/44534", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Uptown", "status": "done", "targets": 29, "clicked": 29, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-uptown-lyrics", "genius_title": "Uptown"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "Now I’m that guy that know them strippers by their real names Rochelle, Jordan", "vault_lines": ["Now I'm that guy that know them strippers by their real names"], "meaning": "When a stripper joins a club they’re given or they choose a name mostly of non-human things to keep their identity a secret. It would take a lot to get to know a strippers real name and/or identity, so Drake has either been at the club so many times he knows the stripper by their real names or, as a link to the next bar “Thick bitches, they just talked me out of four grand” He has payed or bribed the strippers to get to know them personally or to carry out other actions.", "annotation_id": "38788245", "url": "https://genius.com/38788245", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "How’d a pile of kush become a mountain of truth? How’d a bottle of wine become the fountain of youth?", "vault_lines": ["How'd a pile of kush become a mountain of truth?", "How'd a bottle of wine become the fountain of youth?"], "meaning": "Drake uses the idea that when you are under the influence, you speak whats really on your mind without any filters. So when you smoke kush (weed), you speak the truth. Also some wordplay with how a pile turns into a mountain showing the influence of the weed. Drake also relates a bottle of wine to a fountain of youth. Wine is also mentioned in the bible many times, with Jesus famously giving his apostles wine as his blood, so if you drink God’s blood it could be similar to the powers of a fountain of youth.", "annotation_id": "37363174", "url": "https://genius.com/37363174", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "She thinks I’ve become a slave to the wealth But I’d never break the promises I made to myself", "vault_lines": ["She thinks I've become a slave to the wealth", "But I'd never break the promises I made to myself"], "meaning": "Money can come with sacrificing your morals and goals. It’s even worse when the people in your life notice it. Keeping the best parts of you along that journey is important.", "annotation_id": "36976670", "url": "https://genius.com/36976670", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "They say that all your old girls got somebody new I said, “Damn, really? Even Rosemary? Even Leann Sealy?”", "vault_lines": ["They say that all your old girls got somebody new", "I said, \"Damn, really? Even Rosemary? Even Leann Sealy?\""], "meaning": "Drake is taking an allegation of one of his ex’s having a new man, but he implies that he has many ex’s by asking which one.", "annotation_id": "35690067", "url": "https://genius.com/35690067", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "[Chorus]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The chorus features backing vocals from R&B singer The Weeknd, one of Drake’s longtime collaborators and frequent collaborator on the Take Care album.", "annotation_id": "35525752", "url": "https://genius.com/35525752", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "[Outro: Bob Marley]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This sample is only present on the original version of the song, it’s absent from the official version uploaded to streaming services on Care Package. Instead, the song ends with the same instrumental outro but without Marley’s vocals.", "annotation_id": "22887162", "url": "https://genius.com/22887162", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "”I swear, you don’t know this city anymore They might have loved you before But you’re out here doing your thing, they don’t know you”", "vault_lines": ["But you're out here doing your thing, they don't know you\"", "With \"I swear, you don't know this city anymore", "They might have loved you before"], "meaning": "Over the course of his rising stardom, Drake has mentioned quite a few times that he struggles to remain a favorite to people in his past from his hometown: like here or here.\n\nHere, Drake quotes his doubters, as they tell him he’s changed on his growing fame.\n\nBut, in reality, all he cares about is money and Toronto\n\nThese 3 lines are co-vocalized by The Weeknd", "annotation_id": "386147", "url": "https://genius.com/386147", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "And I would never make up names for myself Then change the names that I just gave to myself", "vault_lines": ["And I would never make up names for myself", "Then change the names that I just gave to myself"], "meaning": "Drake’s legal name is Aubrey Drake Graham. He would never make up a name like Puff Daddy, and then change it to P. Diddy… and then change it again to Diddy.\n\n\n\nDrake made a name for himself with the type of music he makes, along with signature sounds of 40’s beats.  He is saying here that he is not going to change the same thing that got him famous.  He’s not going to get famous for making music like “so far gone” and then change the style of his music or fall off.", "annotation_id": "379780", "url": "https://genius.com/379780", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "And long for that ignorant Young Money Miami Beach shit", "vault_lines": ["And long for that ignorant Young Money Miami Beach shit"], "meaning": "Drake feels more comfortable hanging out in Miami acting goonish than with the high-class folk.\n\nDrake’s label Young Money has its HQ in Miami. Drizzy followed them there and moved to M.I.A. in late 2010", "annotation_id": "357979", "url": "https://genius.com/357979", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "My mother is back to who she was years ago It’s like a new page me and her are beginning on", "vault_lines": ["My mother is back to who she was years ago", "It's like a new page me and her are beginning on"], "meaning": "Drake has often talked about his up-and-down relationship with his mother, including an incident where she tried to run away from home\n\nHis mother has gone through physical ailments as well, undergoing unspecified “emergency surgery” in summer 2010", "annotation_id": "357891", "url": "https://genius.com/357891", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "And I was told once, things will change By a nigga named Tip when my deal came", "vault_lines": ["And I was told once, things will change", "By a nigga named Tip when my deal came"], "meaning": "While the legendary Q-Tip has produced some of the tracks on Take Care, he is really talking about T.I.\n\nT.I., a.k.a. T.I.P. confirmed in his latest interview with Sway that he gave this advice to Drake right around the time his Young Money deal came (and appeared on his debut guesting on Fancy).\n\nThere is also word play with this line, for When you give a “tip” you need “change”.\n\nDrake has essentially made it in the rap game. Doing what he does so well, he reflects back on his path to the top. In 2009, T.I. told Drake “things will change” – Things have certainly changed in a good way for the young star. This is a prime example of Drake’s metaphoric stories that often relate to the past.\n\nFor further reading..", "annotation_id": "357684", "url": "https://genius.com/357684", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Club Paradise", "line": "I be with my nigga Chubbs, he in love with street shit No wonder why I feel awkward at this Fashion Week shit No wonder why I keep fucking up the double-cheek kiss", "vault_lines": ["I be with my nigga Chubbs, he in love with street shit", "No wonder why I feel awkward at this Fashion Week shit", "No wonder why I keep fucking up the double-cheek kiss"], "meaning": "When Drake goes out to all the fancy events he feels out of place, not least because his crew members are not used to the high life.\n\nChubbs is a member of Drake’s OVO crew, which recently visited Crooks N Castles, a street clothing line\n\n\n\nChubbs is Drake’s personal bodyguard.", "annotation_id": "357670", "url": "https://genius.com/357670", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
