{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "line": "Cars all black like the cover of Essence", "vault_lines": ["Cars all black like the cover of Essence"], "meaning": "Essence is a women’s magazine that focuses on black women. The covers usually have influential black women on it.\n\nDrake’s cars are all black, like how the magazines covers are too.", "annotation_id": "14310504", "url": "https://genius.com/14310504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "line": "October’s Very Own,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "October’s Very Own is a record label founded by Drake, 40 and Oliver.\n\nIt started off as a blog to promote Drake’s music and has since evolved into an actual record label under the name of “OVO Sound” with a distribution deal through Warner Bros and a growing roster of artists.", "annotation_id": "3285955", "url": "https://genius.com/3285955", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "line": "June 15th in this bitch, what’s up?", "vault_lines": ["Thank me later in this bitch, what's up?", "Free Weezy in this bitch, what's up?", "June 15th in this bitch, what's up?"], "meaning": "June 15th 2010 was the date Drakes debut album, Thank Me Later was released.", "annotation_id": "2595364", "url": "https://genius.com/2595364", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "line": "And have girls fall through like coins in the couch", "vault_lines": ["And have girls fall through like coins in the couch"], "meaning": "Drake utilizes a simile to compare his former girls who got lost, to coins that fall out of his pockets when sitting on the couch. It turns out that these women aren’t really lost and he runs into them later, similar to the day when you pick up the cushion and discover all the coins that have fallen out of peoples pockets.\n\nThere has been an influx in recent history of rappers using “couch” punchlines:\n\nBig Sean\nJ. Cole\nPusha T", "annotation_id": "72645", "url": "https://genius.com/72645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "line": "Ducking your questions, fuck your suggestions", "vault_lines": ["Ducking your questions, fuck your suggestions"], "meaning": "Drake has come along way from his mixtape days when he would pretend to take other rapper’s suggestions into consideration. Now, at this stage in his career, he can just say “Fuck you, your life and every one of your suggestions.”\n\nLike everybody passing through the studio\nIs in character as if we acting out a movie role\nTalking bullshit as if it was for you to know\nAnd I don’t have the heart to give these\nBitch ass niggas the cue to go\nSo they stick around kicking out feedback\nAnd I entertain it as if I need that\n\nSince his fame, everyone’s become a critic. Some people are just going to keep trying to change his music to where he can’t in good conscious call it his anymore. Music is about expression of his fear, anger, frustration, and his life in general. Drake’s music is his only so fuck your suggestions. He dont need them because he can do this all on his own.", "annotation_id": "67347", "url": "https://genius.com/67347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "line": "Throwing up in the huddle nigga, Willie Beamen But still throwing touchdown passes", "vault_lines": ["Throwing up in the huddle nigga, Willie Beamen", "But still throwing touchdown passes"], "meaning": "Jamie Foxx played the role of a football player named Willie Beamen, in the movie Any Given Sunday.  Foxx’s character vomits in the huddle in the movie. Despite the vomit-inducing stress that Drake experiences, he can still get the job done.\n\nDrake also makes a habit of creating punchlines out of movies that, though good, have not been relevant for years. Example 1 and Example 2.", "annotation_id": "31584", "url": "https://genius.com/31584", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "line": "Kush got the room smelling like teen spirit", "vault_lines": ["Kush got the room smelling like teen spirit"], "meaning": "Kush is a strand of marijuana, and “Smells Like Teen Spirit” is a seminal Nirvana song that defines the grunge music genre (which was obviously very popular with potheads)", "annotation_id": "31568", "url": "https://genius.com/31568", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "line": "These are my One St. Thomas flows", "vault_lines": ["These are my One St. Thomas flows"], "meaning": "1 St. Thomas is a high-scale condominium building in Toronto. These are the raps he has written while he overlooks downtown Toronto from his plush living arrangements and reflects on his life.", "annotation_id": "31561", "url": "https://genius.com/31561", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "line": "Me, my niggas and some Madonna hoes", "vault_lines": ["Me, my niggas and some Madonna hoes"], "meaning": "Madonna hoes is a reference to Madonna Catholic Secondary School in Toronto, where the girls were known for being sleezy.", "annotation_id": "31449", "url": "https://genius.com/31449", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9AM in Dallas", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 10, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-9am-in-dallas-lyrics", "genius_title": "9AM in Dallas"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "After this no one a threat to me", "vault_lines": ["After this no one a threat to me"], "meaning": "This track takes brutal aim at most of Drake’s enemies, although true to form, most of them are not mentioned by name, and can be considered subliminal shots.\n\nAfter his demolition of Meek Mill, he may genuinely feel he’s at the pinnacle of the rap game. Certainly many taste-makers place Drake at the top of their list, although Kendrick Lamar and J. Cole might question his reign. Kanye West said in 2015 he believed Drake to be the number one rapper in the game.", "annotation_id": "9076908", "url": "https://genius.com/9076908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "They cannot fuck with the legacy I don’t know what else is left for me", "vault_lines": ["They cannot fuck with the legacy", "I don't know what else is left for me"], "meaning": "On “Summer Sixteen” Drake rapped:\n\n“Oh it’s your time now” yeah, that’s what everybody say\nI used to wanna be on Roc-A-Fella then I turned into Jay\nNow I got a house in LA, Now I got a bigger pool than Ye\n\nA similar message to the first lines of the chorus, promoting his vast achievements in the field of hip hop. He’s technically only been at the forefront of the industry since 2009, but his influence is far reaching, and with a net worth of $78 million and a massive Apple deal, he may be feeling like there’s nothing left to achieve.\n\nIf he needs inspiration and want’s to genuinely turn into Jay Z, this list might give him some direction.", "annotation_id": "9076900", "url": "https://genius.com/9076900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I feed my family with this So don’t play with my money this summer, I’m serious", "vault_lines": ["I feed my family with this", "So don't play with my money this summer, I'm serious"], "meaning": "Unlike the summer of 2015 when Meek Mill accused him of using ghostwriters and it was discovered Cash Money weren’t paying Young Money royalties, Drake’s warning both parties not to mess with his money come summer sixteen.\n\nWhile Drake dedicated “Keep The Family Close” and “Look What You’ve Done” to his family, Drake’s “family” incorporates more than his blood. His crew is full of people from different stages of his life, and his desire to avoid new friends proves everyone around him has been there since day one.", "annotation_id": "9076889", "url": "https://genius.com/9076889", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "They love to talk Me, I’m just dunning the hype Me, I’m just dunning the hype Me I’m just done, done, done, done, done", "vault_lines": ["They love to talk", "Me, I'm just dunning the hype", "Me I'm just done, done, done, done, done"], "meaning": "“They” could refer to his enemies, who are quite vocal, or the fans and commentators, who constantly speak on Drake’s status in the game, and whether he can live up to the hype on VIEWS.\n\n“Dunnin'” or “doneing” is Jamaican patois for ending, or ceasing. This track, and album, serve to eliminate the lingering questions around Drake’s legacy, the “can he do it again?” narrative is cast aside. It’s also a dismissal of his various beef’s, with artists are wide ranging as Birdman, Meek Mill, Tory Lanez, Mo-G and even The Weeknd. During his Zane Lowe interview Drake basically went on record squashing most of his issues, with special regards to The Weeknd, saying they had no problems but they aren’t currently on speaking terms.", "annotation_id": "9076802", "url": "https://genius.com/9076802", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Done Look what I’ve done in my life", "vault_lines": ["Look what I've done in my life"], "meaning": "Drake’s accomplishments are too numerous to list here, but the dream he had in 2009’s “Successful” has come true.\n\nI want the money, money and the cars\nCars and the clothes, the hoes, I suppose\nI just wanna be, I just wanna be successful\n\nHe’s won a Grammy, had 10 top ten singles, sold 8 million records in the US, and forged ahead as one of the leaders of the new school of hip hop.\n\nHe’s also worth $78 million at the age of 29.", "annotation_id": "9073896", "url": "https://genius.com/9073896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Man don’t live of a di hype, huh? Real ting, ay", "vault_lines": ["Man don't live of a di hype, huh?"], "meaning": "In a more clear translation:\n\nMan don’t live off of the hype, huh\nReal thing, eh\n\nBaka, Drake’s friend, reminds people not to get caught up in what others are saying. It’s just gossip, talk, and fictional.\n\nThe hype surrounding this album has been immense, and with place-holder projects like If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late and What A Time To Be Alive achieving commercial and critical success, the hype for VIEWS grew further. Drake divorces himself from all the talk, and focuses on creating the best possible album.", "annotation_id": "9073226", "url": "https://genius.com/9073226", "state": "accepted", "votes": 119, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "They feeling a way and won’t hide it Niggas done being silent", "vault_lines": ["They feeling a way and won't hide it", "Niggas done being silent"], "meaning": "Drake seems to continue to talk about his Meek Mill beef in these lines.\n\nThe “they” in these rhymes could be the fans who no longer hide their disdain for Meek Mill. After his beef with Drake popped off, Meek went off on a fan at his show who showed up holding a Drake sign. Or the “they” can be Meek himself, who is no longer hiding or being silent about his dislike for Drake.\n\nDrake tried to get along with Meek in the past by collaborating on songs like “Amen” and “R.I.C.O.” but what was the point? In the end, Meek called Drake out for using ghostwriters just because Drake didn’t tweet about his album.", "annotation_id": "9072784", "url": "https://genius.com/9072784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "The chain too heavy to tuck it, I’m serious", "vault_lines": ["The chain too heavy to tuck it, I'm serious"], "meaning": "Drake may be referring to his large diamond encrusted OVO “O” chain he debuted on his Instagram two days before VIEWS was released. Drake was first seen wearing this chain in Snapchat video from an interview with Nardwuar.\n\n\n\nTucking one’s chain is a sign of fear, especially when in an unfriendly neighbourhood. On “War Pain” Meek Mill claimed:\n\nCatch you out in Brooklyn, get your chain tookin'\nMy Philly boys’ll creep up on you when you ain’t lookin'\n\nIt appears Drake isn’t too concerned about this threat, and even if he was, his chain is too chunky to fit in his shirt.", "annotation_id": "9072584", "url": "https://genius.com/9072584", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Her gram too poppin’ to fuck her", "vault_lines": ["Her gram too poppin' to fuck her"], "meaning": "Porn star Mia Khalifa once claimed Drake actually tried to slide into her DMs on Instagram with some “cringeworthy” lines. Since Drake’s advances became well known, it seems her gram was just too popping indeed.\n\n\n\nShe may try what PARTYNEXTDOOR did to Kehlani, posting a picture of them in bed together, exposing their sexual relationship.", "annotation_id": "9072578", "url": "https://genius.com/9072578", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Okay now we got some action Everything I said, it happened", "vault_lines": ["Okay now we got some action", "Everything I said, it happened"], "meaning": "In “We Made It” he said:\n\nIf I said it, I meant it there’s no way around it\n\nDrake is narrating his life; living out his words. After “Back To Back” dropped, he really did buy bottles for Charlamagne.\n\nDuring 2009 track “Successful” he rapped:\n\nI want the money, money and the cars\nCars and the clothes, the hoes, I suppose\nI just wanna be, I just wanna be successful\n\nUnrivaled success, and his early career predictions of “making it” came true and more.", "annotation_id": "9072194", "url": "https://genius.com/9072194", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I had to count it and count it again To make sure the money was right", "vault_lines": ["I had to count it and count it again", "To make sure the money was right"], "meaning": "Drake expressed a similar sentiment on Meek Mill’s “R.I.C.O.”:\n\nI’ve been counted out so many times, I couldn’t count it\nFunny how now my accountant is havin' trouble tryna count it\n\nIt’s a possible reference to the turmoil his record label is currently involved with. During Lil Wayne’s lawsuit, something called the 2009 Drake Letter Agreement emerged, stating that Cash Money hadn’t kept it’s promise of disclosing all of Drake’s monthly profits early in his career. During “Star67” he specifically addressed his problems with Cash Money:\n\nWalk up in my label like, where the check though?\nYeah, I said it, wouldn’t dap you with the left ho\n\nJust to ensure it’s all there, Drake count’s it more than once. He doesn’t trust the hands that feed him.", "annotation_id": "9071950", "url": "https://genius.com/9071950", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I hate a goofy especially They always dyin’ to mention me They gotta die out eventually", "vault_lines": ["I hate a rapper especially", "I hate a goofy especially", "They always dyin' to mention me", "They gotta die out eventually", "They gotta go, they gotta go"], "meaning": "A goofy is common Chicago slang often used to describe an ignorant person, but is sometimes mistaken as a playful term. Drake hates goofies—particularly those who try to mention him. Whether it’s a reference to the six or a subtle jab at Drake, he’s fed up.\n\nThis could be aimed at any numbers of rappers taking shots at Drake. It’s possible Mo-G is the target, as the full Chicago term is “goofy mog,” meaning someone has said something ignorant or stupid. This could also be aimed at Chicago native Vic Mensa, who took a shot a Drake on his song “Danger.”", "annotation_id": "9071629", "url": "https://genius.com/9071629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Swear I just had an epiphany It cost me 50 at Tiffany’s", "vault_lines": ["Swear I just had an epiphany", "It cost me 50 at Tiffany's"], "meaning": "Tiffany’s is an American Jewellery store and is rarely mentioned in rap music.\n\nWhile it appears Drake prefers to shop at Cartier, the $50,000 he spends at Tiffany’s on this occasion gives him an epiphany, or sudden clarity of thought. It’s the girls that don’t ask him for expensive products that he needs to focus his attention on. He ends the verse with: “my enemies want to be friends with my other enemies,” pointing to women turning on him when he faces a crisis or a battle.\n\n\n\nDuring Nicki Minaj’s verse on Yo Gotti’s “5 Star Chick” she raps:\n\nI just had a epiphany, I need to go to Tiffany’s\n\nDrake’s is the other way around, his epiphany was inside Tiffany’s.", "annotation_id": "9071126", "url": "https://genius.com/9071126", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I’ma have B’s when I’m 70", "vault_lines": ["I'ma have B's when I'm 70"], "meaning": "B’s refers to billions—his comparisons to Jay Z suggest that he’s on track to achieve this target.\n\nDrake is 29 and according to Forbes, earned $39.5 million in 2015.\n\nThis is also a double entendre because a mark or grade of 70% is equivalent to a B in the Canadian academic grading system.", "annotation_id": "9071029", "url": "https://genius.com/9071029", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I pull up in Lexus’ like it’s ’07 I just hit a lick, I got to hit a next one", "vault_lines": ["I pull up in Lexus' like it's '07", "I just hit a lick, I got to hit a next one"], "meaning": "On “Look What You’ve Done,” a track in part about his life during the late 2000’s, Drake spoke about cruising around in his uncle’s drop-top Lexus.\n\nOn the track “Star67” he detailed his version of “hit a lick,” which means to rob someone or a place of residence. His phone scam is equated to the way other rappers speak of their illegal activities.\n\nDrake has certainly upgraded his automotive game since then.", "annotation_id": "9070996", "url": "https://genius.com/9070996", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Bustin’ 1's out the plastic", "vault_lines": ["Bustin' 1's out the plastic"], "meaning": "In 2013, it was reported Drake brought a cardboard box full of 50,000 one dollar bills to “make it rain” in a Carolina strip club. He’s constantly referenced strippers in his music, most notably on 2015’s “Plastic Bag” with Future.\n\nI roll up with the bands on me\nThis a good night to dance on me", "annotation_id": "9070985", "url": "https://genius.com/9070985", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I hate a rapper especially They feel the same, but they hide it They just discuss it in private Don’t get along man, we tried it What’s the point in even tryin’?", "vault_lines": ["I hate a rapper especially", "They feel the same, but they hide it", "They just discuss it in private", "Don't get along man, we tried it", "What's the point in even tryin'?", "I hate a goofy especially", "What's the point of even tryin'?"], "meaning": "Many of Drake’s beefs, including his exchanges with Meek Mill and Kendrick Lamar, have been with rappers he originally got along with but ended up beefing with anyway.\n\nAlthough Drake’s beef with Meek Mill was open and direct, he’s been accused regularly of favouring subliminal shots over more overt methods. A history of his subliminal war with Kendrick Lamar proves he’s not below hiding his dislike for another artist, especially after Kendrick named him during his incendiary “Control” verse:\n\nAnd that goes for Jermaine Cole, Big K.R.I.T., Wale\nPusha T, Meek Millz, A$AP Rocky, Drake\nBig Sean, Jay Electron', Tyler, Mac Miller\nI got love for you all but I’m tryna murder you n—s", "annotation_id": "9070953", "url": "https://genius.com/9070953", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Shout out to Tiffany, Stephanie They used to always come check for me", "vault_lines": ["They always dyin' to mention me", "Shout out to Tiffany, Stephanie", "They used to always come check for me"], "meaning": "Drake has a younger step sister named Stephanie. It seems his sister and her friend, ironically named Tiffany, would come and make sure Drake didn’t spend too much cash in the jewelry store.\n\nIt may also be a shout to fellow Young Money artist Stephanie Acevedo, although Drake has yet to collaborate with her.\n\n\n\nInterestingly, on “Side to Side,” released after this song, Nicki Minaj raps about Tiffany and Stephanie:\n\nKissing me, copped the blue box that say Tiffany\nCurry with the shot, just tell ‘em to call me Stephanie", "annotation_id": "9070920", "url": "https://genius.com/9070920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I don’t take this shit for granted I do my own propaganda", "vault_lines": ["I don't take this shit for granted", "I do my own propaganda"], "meaning": "Hard work underpins the success he’s achieved. On tracks like “Unstoppable” and “Pop Style,” Drake explicitly states the time and effort he puts into his music.\n\nSee me putting in the hard work now, aye\nMomma doesn’t have to call work now, aye\nI decide when I start work now, aye\nProblems hit the gym, they all work out\n\n“Propaganda” (information spun in a way to promote a certain person, group, or view) is a reference to Drake’s own braggadocio lyrics and lack of modesty when it comes to his wealth.\n\nHis marketing campaigns are self-explanatory. The quality of his music and the messages housed within handles the promotion, with no extra spend required. Dropping If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late out of the blue didn’t hurt it’s success, with the album going platinum before it’s physical release.", "annotation_id": "9070907", "url": "https://genius.com/9070907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I don’t run out of material You shouldn’t speak on me, period You try to give ’em your side of the story They heard it, but they wasn’t hearin’ it", "vault_lines": ["I cannot give you the recipe", "I don't let it get to me", "I don't run out of material", "You shouldn't speak on me, period", "You try to give 'em your side of the story", "They heard it, but they wasn't hearin' it"], "meaning": "In the summer of 2015, Meek Mill accused Drake of not writing his own verses and even having someone write his feature on Meek’s single “R.I.C.O.”\n\nhttps://twitter.com/meekmill/status/623700698509758464\n\nDespite Funkmaster Flex claiming he had proof that Drake used ghostwriters, most fans didn’t seem too concerned about the issue. Drake responded to the situation with two diss tracks, “Charged Up” and “Back to Back,” the latter of which was nominated for a Grammy. Meanwhile, Meek released “Wanna Know” and other songs dissing Drake—but none of it seemed to matter since Drake was already considered the winner of the beef.\n\nIt may also be a sly shot at Tory Lanez. During Drake’s interview with Zane Lowe he said he lost respect for the rapper, after he came at him for no apparent reason.", "annotation_id": "9070881", "url": "https://genius.com/9070881", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "6 cold like Alaska", "vault_lines": ["6 cold like Alaska"], "meaning": "Drake compares Toronto’s climate to Alaska’s.  The average temperature in Toronto during the winter is 19-30 degrees fahrenheit, easily enough to induce snowfalls.\n\n\n\n“Cold” also refers to hard working conditions, intensity, and lack of empathy. During his Zane Lowe interview, Drake mentioned that VIEWS is based around seasons, and the changing weather has been prevalent in Drake’s music. During “6 Man” he raps:\n\nI’m here to fuck with n—’s souls, my heart is cold\nIt’s prolly cause I’m from the snow, with all my woes\n\nIronically, it was 6 degrees in Toronto the day VIEWS dropped.", "annotation_id": "9070877", "url": "https://genius.com/9070877", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Views already a classic", "vault_lines": ["Views already a classic"], "meaning": "The hype surrounding this record has been intense, with some commentators claiming the record had to be his best work.\n\nAfter the epic 2015 that Drake enjoyed, releasing both If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late and What A Time To Be Alive to commercial and critical success, not to mention his mega-hit “Hotline Bling,” the pressure was huge. With Drake’s track record, he probably felt the album was destined for classic status.\n\nIn May 2016, the album held a rating of 68 on *Metacritic,\" his lowest so far. However, the album went platinum in it’s first week of sale, and has sold around 1.5 million copies in the US alone as of May 2016.", "annotation_id": "9070851", "url": "https://genius.com/9070851", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I cannot give you the recipe You know the game is sold separately", "vault_lines": ["I cannot give you the recipe", "You know the game is sold separately"], "meaning": "Playing off the famous “game sold separately” by-line at the end of gaming advertisements, Drake’s telling his rivals he can’t give them the secrets to success—they need to go out and get it themselves.\n\nThis may be another subliminal shot in the cold war between Drake and Kendrick Lamar since Kendrick once had a song called “The Recipe.”\n\nFurthermore, in 2016 it emerged Drake may have ghostwritten for Dr. Dre, deepening his connection to the track “The Recipe” which featured the West Coast legend.", "annotation_id": "9070776", "url": "https://genius.com/9070776", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "That boy light as Michael Jackson But on verses, he been blackin’", "vault_lines": ["That boy light as Michael Jackson", "But on verses, he been blackin'"], "meaning": "Drake compares himself to Michael Jackson. While Kanye promised he’d never allow light-skin rappers to come back in style, Drake (light-skinned) has taken over the music business, just as Michael did.\n\nIn addition, Drake has a unique ability to sing hooks and deliver menacing verses. Just as MJ delivered light, high pitched hooks, Drake can do the same, but also black out on a verse when required.\n\nDrake has referred to himself in this context previously. During “All Me,” he referred to himself as the “light-skinned Keith Sweat.”", "annotation_id": "9070662", "url": "https://genius.com/9070662", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "My enemies wanna be friends with my other enemies I don’t let it get to me", "vault_lines": ["My enemies wanna be friends with my other enemies", "I don't let it get to me"], "meaning": "It’s no secret Drake’s got a lot of enemies. And unfortunately for Drizzy, most of them have teamed up against him.\n\n\nChris Brown, a noted Drake enemy, has publicly defended Meek Mill during his legal issues.\nChris Brown and Tyga are close friends and have recorded an album together. Drake and Tyga haven’t been friendly since 2015.\nTory Lanez has taken shots at Drake as well, and worked with Meek on his “Lord Knows” track.\nMeek Mill gave Jay Z the nod on “War Pain” when he referred to Jay’s: “wrong sport, boy, you know you’re as soft as the lacrosse team” Drake subliminal on DJ Khaled’s “They Don’t Love You No More.”\n\n\n\nIt’s also a possible counter to Meek’s line on “Trap Vibes (Summer Sixteen and Panda Remix)” on which he rapped:\n\nI’m so poppin' all my haters turn friends now\n\nBefore referencing Drake’s hit song with Future.\n\nIn his interview with Zane Lowe leading up to the release of VIEWS, Drake mentioned feeling like an outsider in the rap game:\n\nI’ve always felt like an outsider, naturally… The better you do at music, the more you feel like an outsider. When you start doing well, they’ll make you feel like an outsider. You can be an insider, and they’ll make you feel like an outsider.\"", "annotation_id": "9070374", "url": "https://genius.com/9070374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Last year I know you learned your lesson", "vault_lines": ["Last year I know you learned your lesson"], "meaning": "A year before the release of VIEWS, Drake dropped his “mixtape” If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, which was positively received and commercially successful.\n\n His single “Hotline Bling” and video was a hit, and he roasted Meek Mill with “Charged Up” and “Back to Back”. Challenging Drake when he is at the absolute peak of the rap game isn’t an intelligent move.", "annotation_id": "9070246", "url": "https://genius.com/9070246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "‘09 they was biddin’ on me But I’m Young Money, got it written on me", "vault_lines": ["But I'm Young Money, got it written on me", "'09 they was biddin' on me"], "meaning": "In Feb. 2009, Drake released his third mixtape So Far Gone, which became such a success many labels were competing to sign him. By June 2009. sources told Billboard Drake was in “one of the biggest bidding wars ever.” In the end, Drake signed to his mentor Lil Wayne’s label, YMCMB.\n\nDrake has YMCMB inked on his back, one of his first tattoos. Although the OVO owl on his back is larger and darker, right next to it in faint ink it reads:\n\nIf I die today, it’ll be a holiday\natf\novo\nymcmb\nreps up", "annotation_id": "9070227", "url": "https://genius.com/9070227", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Chasin’ women a distraction They wan’ be on TV right next to me You cannot be here right next to me Don’t you see RiRi right next to me?", "vault_lines": ["Chasin' women a distraction", "They wan' be on TV right next to me", "You cannot be here right next to me", "Don't you see RiRi right next to me?"], "meaning": "It seems Drake’s days of chasing women  (and creeping in DM’s) are done. He’d much rather be the envy of everyone in the rap game while Rihanna wines on him during music video shoots and live shows like the BRIT Awards.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SQWqksdE4gw&feature=youtu.be\n\nDespite Drake’s affection for RiRi, his relationship with her seems confusing at the best of times. He dedicated an entire verse to her on his 2010 track “Fireworks,” then claimed she used him as a pawn during an interview in 2010, but has continued to collaborate with her, most notably on “What’s My Name,” “Take Care,” “Work,” and “Too Good.”\n\nOutside of music, Drake was involved in an altercation with Chris Brown, believed to be Rihanna-related.", "annotation_id": "9070210", "url": "https://genius.com/9070210", "state": "accepted", "votes": 156, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Boss up, I’m the bigger homie But I’m one year older than my lil homie", "vault_lines": ["Boss up, I'm the bigger homie", "But I'm one year older than my lil homie"], "meaning": "During “Summer Sixteen,” Drake compared himself to Jay Z, an artist he’s referred to as “the big homie.” In that same interview, Drake called himself “the big homie too.”\n\nThe term “lil homie” is usually used to refer to a protégé, or in the rap game a younger artist who is under the mentorship of the “big homie.” At 29, Drake had achieved an incredible amount, enough to place him above many of his peers.\n\nOne such peer is Kendrick Lamar, who, coincidentally, is one year younger than Drake, and is known to use the term “lil' homie” regularly in his music. Meek Mill is also a year younger from Drake.", "annotation_id": "9070189", "url": "https://genius.com/9070189", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "Roy outta here like NASA", "vault_lines": ["Roy outta here like NASA"], "meaning": "Roy Woods is an up-and-coming Canadian rapper from Brampton, Ontario who is signed to Drake’s OVO Sound label.\n\nOn July 31, 2015, Woods released his debut EP Exis,  Latin for “you exit, you depart, you go out” or figuratively “you escape.” With the mention of NASA—whose mission of space travel requires exiting and escaping Earth—Drake makes a sly reference to the title of Woods' EP.", "annotation_id": "9070182", "url": "https://genius.com/9070182", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I feel like Juelz Santana Leg hangin’ out the Phantom", "vault_lines": ["I feel like Juelz Santana", "Leg hangin' out the Phantom"], "meaning": "Juelz Santana is a fellow rapper known for his collections of cars, especially his Rolls Royce Phantom:\n\nAll white phantom directing it through the hood\nReal soft leather my dash is cherry wood\n\nDuring the video for “Black Republicans,” Juelz' collaboration with Lil Wayne, he can be seen with his leg hanging out of a Phantom.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BFiFpV5mg18/?taken-by=thejuelzsantana&hl=en\n\nThis more contemporary picture of Drake and his dog “Diamond” next to a Rolls Royce Phantom was revealed as part of the promotion for the record.", "annotation_id": "9070164", "url": "https://genius.com/9070164", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "line": "I could GPS you if you need addressin’", "vault_lines": ["I could GPS you if you need addressin'"], "meaning": "Playing on the double meaning of “address,” Drake utilises his portable global positioning system (GPS) to locate anyone who has a problem with him, then pulls up and addresses them in person.\n\nThis may be another jab in Drake’s on-going beef with Toronto rapper Tory Lanez, who released the track “Real Addresses” in March 2016.", "annotation_id": "9070159", "url": "https://genius.com/9070159", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hype", "status": "done", "targets": 32, "clicked": 32, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-hype-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hype"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "To make everybody happy I think I would need a clone", "vault_lines": ["To make everybody happy I think I would need a clone"], "meaning": "Drake feels that he himself will never be able to make everyone around him happy with him because of the differing opinions and how much time this life of fame has taken from him communicating with his loved ones.", "annotation_id": "40944650", "url": "https://genius.com/40944650", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "The shit I write while staring out the window of a plane Is the single-handed reason I remain me, yeah", "vault_lines": ["The shit I write while staring out the window of a plane", "Is the single-handed reason I remain me, yeah"], "meaning": "You can only write singled-handed. His thoughts would consume them if he didnt put them in the music.", "annotation_id": "35821636", "url": "https://genius.com/35821636", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "And he paying for his cigarettes with dollars and some change", "vault_lines": ["And he paying for his cigarettes with dollars and some change"], "meaning": "Drake feels guilty that he is buying expensive cars while his dad is scraping together coins and dollar notes to buy some cigarettes.", "annotation_id": "30560872", "url": "https://genius.com/30560872", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Please leave me alone", "vault_lines": ["Please leave me alone"], "meaning": "Drake has gone too far  And he wants to go further\n\nIF YOU WANT TO GO FAR GO ALONE", "annotation_id": "23899121", "url": "https://genius.com/23899121", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Uh And life is so insane Look what I became trying to make a name All my first dates are interrupted by my fame Because every picture taken is a fan that you can gain", "vault_lines": ["And life is so insane", "Look what I became trying to make a name", "All my first dates are interrupted by my fame", "Because every picture taken is a fan that you can gain"], "meaning": "Here Drake states that he can’t have a peaceful time out on a date with women without fans noticing him and trying to get a pic, but he tries to take a pic with them because it is an opportunity to gain a fan.", "annotation_id": "23123170", "url": "https://genius.com/23123170", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "I’m sittin’ in a chair but in the future it’s a throne", "vault_lines": ["I'm sittin' in a chair but in the future it's a throne"], "meaning": "Drake uses this line to vividly demonstrate the traction and fame the young artist is steadily reaching, which implies in the future he will be a king/OG to the rap game", "annotation_id": "20221211", "url": "https://genius.com/20221211", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Like, how I had a Rolls and I went and got a Range", "vault_lines": ["Like, how I had a Rolls and I went and got a Range"], "meaning": "Drake is disappointed in himself that he finally got the car of his dreams, a Rolls Royce, and then decided to drive a different car, a Range Rover.", "annotation_id": "20214863", "url": "https://genius.com/20214863", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "40 Mama always say don’t ask permission Just ask forgiveness you know so uh, forgive me, yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["40 Mama always say don’t ask permission", "Just ask forgiveness you know so uh, forgive me, yeah, yeah"], "meaning": "Referring to the mother of Noah “40” Shebib, Drake’s long time producer and one of his closest friends.\n\nThis line plays on the phrase “better to beg for forgiveness than ask for permission”. It means that it’s better to push the issue than to ask if what you are doing is okay before doing so. This implies that those who ask if they should do something do not deserve to do so because they do not have enough passion to achieve what they want to on their own accord. By saying “forgive me” Drake is suggesting that he has already done what he wanted without the opinions of others stopping him.", "annotation_id": "3085379", "url": "https://genius.com/3085379", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "It’s a curse you gotta live with when you born to entertain", "vault_lines": ["It's a curse you gotta live with when you born to entertain"], "meaning": "Refers to the fact that his father, Dennis Graham was a former drummer for Jerry Lee Lewis. As well, his uncle Larry Graham is the legendary guitarist from 70’s funk band, Graham Central Station; and uncle Tennie Hodges was a  guitarist and songwriter for Al Green.", "annotation_id": "741551", "url": "https://genius.com/741551", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "I’m just so far gone", "vault_lines": ["I'm just so far gone"], "meaning": "Drake feels that he has drifted away from reality. As said in an interview with Complex, the title of the mixtape, So Far Gone“, came from a phone conversation that he had with one of his best friends Oliver, in which they were speaking disrespectfully about women. After the conversation, Oliver texted Drake, commenting that they were becoming just like men that their mothers divorced. With this situation in mind, Drake explains the title’s significance as: \"You’re just elevating past the bullshit and past all the shit that you used to be a part of and you’re not that proud of, you’re just so far gone. ”", "annotation_id": "63100", "url": "https://genius.com/63100", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Feelin’ so distant from everyone I’ve known", "vault_lines": ["Feelin' so distant from everyone I've known"], "meaning": "Drake wrote this song after a fight with his family over the phone. Remember the line in “Successful”…\n\nAnd 40 that was all before i showed up\nand brought a thousand dollars worth of drinks\nand got poured up\n\n…he is describing what happened after the fight, when he brough several bottles of alcohol to 40’s house–almost in tears–then they sat down and wrote “The Calm”\n\nThis line describes how distant he feels from his family since he became so successful. Drake had Don Perignon (expensive champagne) after seeing the suffering his mother was going threw and together worrying about what they would do for money as degrassi had ended for a few years now and Drake was running out of money because he gave out free music. Once Drake got there and started drinking his uncle called him and drake said he was screaming threw the phone at his uncle on the other end who he loved with all his heart. It is said the argument was over money and his mom tried to run away from home earlier that day. Everyone is becoming less close to Drake as he gets more successful.", "annotation_id": "54149", "url": "https://genius.com/54149", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Dedicated to my mom and I swear", "vault_lines": ["Dedicated to my mom and I swear"], "meaning": "This precise song was written the same night when Drake had an altercation with his mother; he discusses the argument in the first 8 bars of the 2nd verse in “Successful” (from his So Far Gone mixtape)\n\nAfter Aubrey had the verbal altercation with his mother, he later felt guilt, and decided that he wanted to make up to her what he felt he owed, the respect and love of a son.  A couple years later, he dedicated a song on Take Care “Look What You’ve Done” to her. This specific line has a lot of background to it.", "annotation_id": "22376", "url": "https://genius.com/22376", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "They love it when you smile, unaware that it’s a strain", "vault_lines": ["They love it when you smile, unaware that it's a strain"], "meaning": "Forcing smiles strains the zygomaticus-major\n\nOn a deeper note. “every picture taken..” relates back to him always taking time out of life to please his fans no matter the occasion, even dates. Realistically,  Drake may be stressing or carrying a heavy burden and may not always be in smiling mood. Therefore “..unaware that its a strain” meaning you smile through your emotions because ,“They love it when you smile..” which supports the follow up “..curse you gotta live with when you born to entertain”", "annotation_id": "10176", "url": "https://genius.com/10176", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Damn, and I can only feel his pain ’Cause in Memphis, Tennessee there’s only so much to attain", "vault_lines": ["Damn, and I can only feel his pain", "'Cause in Memphis, Tennessee there's only so much to attain"], "meaning": "He can’t even tell his dad “it’s your own fault you don’t have money” since Drake is aware that it’s just not easy – and certainly not for a black man – to stay afloat in a dead-end, discriminatory place like Memphis\n\nRemember that Drake is from Toronto; his dad being located in Memphis indicates that his dad didn’t help raise him (which maybe explains why Drake is so “in touch with his feelings”)", "annotation_id": "5422", "url": "https://genius.com/5422", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "It’s a weight that’s on my chest whoever spotting me is playing So I’m lifting all alone trying not to get a sprain", "vault_lines": ["It's a weight that's on my chest whoever spotting me is playing", "So I'm lifting all alone trying not to get a sprain"], "meaning": "When bench pressing, it is recommended to use a spotter (someone who helps you lift the weight when you can’t); Drake feels a “weight on his chest” (a metaphor for stress/responsibility) as if his spotter is careless", "annotation_id": "5419", "url": "https://genius.com/5419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Tryin’ to enjoy myself with Tez in Miami at the game I just wish he knew how much it really weighed like Dwayne", "vault_lines": ["Tryin' to enjoy myself with Tez in Miami at the game", "I just wish he knew how much it really weighed like Dwayne"], "meaning": "He’s making a play on words, using the phonetic sound of Dwyane’s last name as opposed to the grammatical spelling to say “weighed like Dwyane”.\n\nTez is “Cortez Bryant,” who is not only the CEO of Young Money Entertainment, but also is the manager to Lil' Wayne and Drake. Drake and Tez are at the Miami Heat game but unfortunately Drake has a lot of stuff weighing on his chest but Tez doesn’t care about Drake’s feelings. He’s more interested in the Heat game and Dwayne Wade. So drake says “I just wish he knew how much it really weighed like Dwayne.” , He wants Tez to focus on him and not the game.", "annotation_id": "5418", "url": "https://genius.com/5418", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "You can see it in my face or even read it on my palm Leader of the new school, it’s proven and it’s known", "vault_lines": ["You can see it in my face or even read it on my palm", "Leader of the new school, it's proven and it's known"], "meaning": "He feels that his destiny is manifest. Also a reference to the classic rap group Leaders of the New School, which featured a young Busta Rhymes\n\n\n\nRead it in my palm refers to the fact that fortune tellers can “see” your future in the palm of your hand.", "annotation_id": "5413", "url": "https://genius.com/5413", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "My word is bond", "vault_lines": ["my word is bond"], "meaning": "“Word is bond” means that you are bound by what you say: he always keeps his word\n\nHe is Canadian, and in Canada we are taught a bit of French at minimum. “Bon” in french (although he pronounces it like an english guy) means “Good”. He swears his word is good.", "annotation_id": "5410", "url": "https://genius.com/5410", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "I’m just in my zone I call this shit the calm Yeah, but I’m the furthest thing from calm", "vault_lines": ["I'm just in my zone", "I call this shit the calm", "Yeah, but I'm the furthest thing from calm", "I said I call this shit the calm", "But I’m the furthest thing from calm"], "meaning": "Even though he’s not calm, Drake calls his contemplative time “the calm”. Artists should sympathize: the process involves mania, which is a paradox – frenetic and tranquil at the same time", "annotation_id": "5409", "url": "https://genius.com/5409", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "It’s only been three years Look at how I’ve grown", "vault_lines": ["It's only been three years", "Look at how I've grown"], "meaning": "This song came out in 2009, 3 years after Drake’s first mixtape “Room for Improvement”. He’s also pointing out how he’s improved since then.", "annotation_id": "5408", "url": "https://genius.com/5408", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Places we get flown, parties that we’ve thrown I’ve done more for this city than these rappers that have blown", "vault_lines": ["Places we get flown, parties that we've thrown", "I've done more for this city than these rappers that have blown"], "meaning": "Drake represents Toronto.  By flying his Torontan thugs around and giving them a taste of the fast life, he’s helped his people. By throwing grand fetes and generally balling out he’s put his city on the map\n\nThis is indirectly a shot at Kardinall Offishall, another Toronto rapper with whom Drake has beefed in the past", "annotation_id": "5407", "url": "https://genius.com/5407", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Women borrow sweaters that I spray with my cologne And tell me don’t forget ’em and I promise that I won’t", "vault_lines": ["Women borrow sweaters that I spray with my cologne", "And tell me don't forget 'em and I promise that I won't"], "meaning": "A common theme for Drake: women want him to care about them. He wants to want to care, but he just can’t do it\n\nHe’s also referring to his common statement that his clothe smell like him and his women love his smell so they usually end up taking a sweater or something of the type to wear around when they  miss him and his smell", "annotation_id": "5403", "url": "https://genius.com/5403", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "When my diamond chain is on, still nothin’ set in stone", "vault_lines": ["When my diamond chain is on, still nothin' set in stone"], "meaning": "A clever double-usage of “set”. Rappers normally brag about how the diamonds are “set in the bezel” – a jeweler’s term\n\nHere, Drake uses the word to create a contrast with the possible ephemerality of his commercial success, which allowed him to buy “stones” (diamonds) in the first place", "annotation_id": "5402", "url": "https://genius.com/5402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "Drunk off champagne Screamin’ in the phone See my house is not a home Fuck is going on? Where did we go wrong? Where do we belong?", "vault_lines": ["Screamin' in the phone", "See my house is not a home", "Fuck is going on?", "Where did we go wrong?", "Where do we belong?"], "meaning": "Drake wrote this verse at 40’s after he had an altercation with his uncle on the phone.\n\n“My House Is Not A Home” references the Luther Vandross classic “A House Is Not A Home”.\n\n Interestingly enough, what Drake talks about in Successful about his mom and then says “and 40 that was all before I showed up and brought a thousand dollars worth of drinks and got poured up” Drake brought bottles of Don Perignon (expensive champagne) to 40’s apartment and they were gonna drink to clear Drake’s mind so they could work on some music. While Drake was there and after they were drinking his uncle called and Drake said in an interview he was standing on 40’s balcony screaming through the phone to a man on the other end in which he loved with all his heart.", "annotation_id": "5398", "url": "https://genius.com/5398", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "line": "October’s own", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "October’s Own – OVO – Octobers Very Own is a group of Drake’s friend acting as his marketing team. Everyones birthday is in Octover except for Drake’s engineer, 40. Other members include Oliver and Niko. They run a blog named after the team, called “October’s Very Own”.", "annotation_id": "5397", "url": "https://genius.com/5397", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Calm", "status": "done", "targets": 25, "clicked": 25, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-the-calm-lyrics", "genius_title": "The Calm"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "These days, I could probably pack it For like twenty nights if I go in there", "vault_lines": ["These days, I could probably pack it", "For like twenty nights if I go in there"], "meaning": "In contrast to his popularity when he first started out (previous line), Drake is so popular now that if he were to perform at “the warehouse” today, he would be able to “pack” the venue with a sell-out crowd, even if he were to perform there for 20 consecutive nights.", "annotation_id": "36833114", "url": "https://genius.com/36833114", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Moment I stop having fun with it, I’ll be done with it", "vault_lines": ["Moment I stop having fun with it, I'll be done with it"], "meaning": "When making music is no longer enjoyable for Drake that’s when he will retire from doing it. This is similar to Snoop Dogg’s lines at the end of his first and third verse in his song “Go Away”:\n\nI’m too young to retire, I’m havin' fun wit it\nSee when you learn what to do with it, I’ll be done with it, come wit it", "annotation_id": "33770947", "url": "https://genius.com/33770947", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "My uncle used to have all these things on his bucket list And now he’s actin’ like, “Oh, well, this is life, I guess” Nah, fuck that shit Listen, man, you can still do what you wanna do You gotta trust that shit", "vault_lines": ["Fuck that, is anyone comin'", "My uncle used to have all these things on his bucket list", "And now he's actin' like, \"Oh, well, this is life, I guess\"", "Nah, fuck that shit", "Listen, man, you can still do what you wanna do", "You gotta trust that shit"], "meaning": "It seems like he’s reflecting on his uncle’s attitude towards life. The uncle had a bucket list, a list of things he wanted to do before he died. However, over time, the uncle seems to have resigned himself to the idea that life may not allow him to fulfill all those desires, saying, “Oh, well, this is life, I guess.”\n\nDrake’s response, “Nah, fuck that shit,” suggests a more assertive and optimistic perspective. He’s urging his uncle (and perhaps others) not to give up on their dreams and desires. Drake is encouraging the idea that one should continue pursuing what they want in life and not accept defeat or resignation. The phrase “You gotta trust that shit” implies a sense of faith and confidence in the possibility of achieving one’s goals, even if the path may be challenging. Overall, the lyrics convey a message of resilience and determination.", "annotation_id": "30941090", "url": "https://genius.com/30941090", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Don’t think about it too much, too much, too much, too much There’s no need for us to rush it through Don’t think about it too much, too much, too much, too much This is more than just a new lust for you", "vault_lines": ["Don't think about it too much, too much, too much, too much", "There's no need for us to rush it through", "This is more than just a new lust for you", "Oh, don't think about it too much, too much, too much, too much", "There's no need for us to rush it through, oh", "This is more than just a new lust for you, oh, oh"], "meaning": "Over-thinking ends up perpetuating a problem rather than solving it. In a sense, just let it all work out…\n\nLike most troubled artists, Drake has a burning sense of anxiety–a perfectionist with the need to control everything. This is a reminder to himself to go with the flow.\n\nNotice at the start of the song you hear “em lets try it one more time”….goes to show you need to relax sometimes and go with the flow…trying to be too perfect is susceptible to creating mistakes.", "annotation_id": "2206647", "url": "https://genius.com/2206647", "state": "accepted", "votes": 316, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "’Cause I’m such a dreamer, a believer in a sense", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I'm such a dreamer, a believer in a sense"], "meaning": "These two lines bring to mind an old song from 1967, “Daydream Believer” by The Monkees.\n\nCheer up, sleepy Jean.\nOh, what can it mean.\nTo a daydream believer\nAnd a homecoming queen.\n\nA popular interpretation of the song is events after the marriage of a daydream believer and a homecoming queen. Their relationship is unconventional due to different social statuses, but their love gets them through hard times. Sampha could be referring to a relationship with a woman, or a “relationship” with the industry and one’s career.\n\nAnother point of reference might be The Dreamer/The Believer, the 2011 album by Common. Interesting that Sampha would drop the reference on Drake’s track, considering the beef war between Com and Drizzy.", "annotation_id": "2206399", "url": "https://genius.com/2206399", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Shit got me feeling pinned down Pick the pen up or put the pen down? I’m writing to you from a distance like a pen-pal But we’ve been down", "vault_lines": ["Shit got me feeling pinned down", "Pick the pen up or put the pen down?", "I'm writing to you from a distance like a pen-pal", "But we've been down"], "meaning": "Drake’s wordplay with homophones: pinned up/pen up/pen down/pen pal all to show how his family issues are making him feel trapped, so much so that he has writer’s block.\n\n\n\nHe’s literally writing to his family from far away (he’s on the road/on a track) but also from a different space (he feels very distant from them emotionally). The distance is confusing to him because this is his family, they already know each other.", "annotation_id": "2205601", "url": "https://genius.com/2205601", "state": "accepted", "votes": 158, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "[Chorus: Sampha]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake worked with Sampha on “The Motion” and he previously remixed “Wildfire” by SBTRKT. Sampha is not officially in SBTRKT, but he works with him regularly.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aW-v5tvgXsI\n\nThe hook was originally on Sampha’s song with the same title.", "annotation_id": "2205370", "url": "https://genius.com/2205370", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "I was birthed there in my first year Man, I know that place like I come from it", "vault_lines": ["I was birthed there in my first year", "Man, I know that place like I come from it"], "meaning": "Drake credits Houston for really jump starting his rap career, and considers it like a second home. From meeting Wayne for the first time, to recording most of So Far Gone there, Drake knows H-town like the back of his hand.", "annotation_id": "2205242", "url": "https://genius.com/2205242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Stuck in the house, need to get out more", "vault_lines": ["Stuck in the house, need to get out more"], "meaning": "Over the last year, Drake has been cooped up in the studio making NWTS. Guess it’s time to go out now that the album is done.\n\nBut Drizzy’s been here before: On “Light Up” from Thank Me Later, he bemoaned:\n\n‘Cause while all of my closest friends out partying\nI’m just here making all the music that they party to", "annotation_id": "2205222", "url": "https://genius.com/2205222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Done sayin’ I’m done playin’ Last time was on the outro", "vault_lines": ["Done sayin' I'm done playin'", "Last time was on the outro"], "meaning": "On the outro to Take Care, “The Ride”, Drizzy says:\n\nMy sophomore, I was all for it, they all saw it\nMy junior and senior will only get meaner\n\nShowing that he’s done messing around. Nothing is the same…\n\n\n\nThe track that serves as the album’s outro, Pound Cake, actually has a line were Drizzy actually says he isn’t playing. The last time he is indeed on the outro.", "annotation_id": "2205216", "url": "https://genius.com/2205216", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Fuck that, is anyone comin’ ’Fore I show up there and there’s no one there?”", "vault_lines": ["Fuck that, is anyone comin'", "'Fore I show up there and there's no one there?\""], "meaning": "One of Drake’s first live performances after releasing So Far Gone was at Warehouse. Drake’s worrying about no one showing up to his show.\n\n\n\n#Au contraire,\n…it was a sold out show. And the rest is history.", "annotation_id": "2205200", "url": "https://genius.com/2205200", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Money got my whole family going backwards No dinners, no holidays, no nothin’ There’s issues at hand that we’re not discussin’", "vault_lines": ["Money got my whole family going backwards", "No dinners, no holidays, no nothin'", "There's issues at hand that we're not discussin'"], "meaning": "Instead of the money Drake makes bringing his family closer, it’s actually creating distance between them.\n\nDrake has shed light on this subject before. Being extremely rich, he isn’t sure anymore whether or not his family is sincere or just hoping to profit financially.\n\n\n\nNotice the homophone with backwards and Backwoods in the previous line.", "annotation_id": "2205193", "url": "https://genius.com/2205193", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Like H-Town in the summertime, I keep it one-hunnid Met a lot of girls in my times there Word to Paul Wall, not one fronted", "vault_lines": ["Like H-Town in the summertime, I keep it one-hunnid", "Met a lot of girls in my times there", "Word to Paul Wall, not one fronted"], "meaning": "Drake always keeps it 100, meaning 100%; he spits the truth. The weather in Houston (H-Town) in the summertime also stays right around 100 degrees Fahrenheit\n\n\n\nDrake shouts out Houston legend Paul Wall, while referencing his song “Girl”. Focus on Drake’s usage of the term, “not one fronted.” Paul Wall is known for owning and distributing gold/silver/platinum “fronts” or “grills” for many people and artists globally and most specifically in the Houston area.\n\nThere is a subtle reference to Houston artist Onehunnidt in “H-Town in the summer time, I keep it 100”. Onehunnidt is the older brother to the deceased “JJ” that Drake mentioned on “Miss Me”. Onehunnidt released a project entitled “Keep It 100” in the summer of 2012.", "annotation_id": "2205184", "url": "https://genius.com/2205184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "And like a pot luck, you need to come with it, don’t run from it", "vault_lines": ["And like a pot luck, you need to come with it, don't run from it"], "meaning": "A potluck is a gathering where everybody brings their own dish and shares it.\n\n\n\nSimilar to how you would come with food to a potluck, you need to come with everything you have to go against Drake. These other rappers aren’t bringing anything to the table. He’s switching the game up and feels like he’s the only one really trying to be creative and improve their craft right now (“puttin' shots up”).", "annotation_id": "2205180", "url": "https://genius.com/2205180", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "And all my family from the M-Town that I’ve been ’round Started treating me like I’m “him” now Like we don’t know each other, we ain’t grow together We just friends now", "vault_lines": ["And all my family from the M-Town that I've been 'round", "Started treating me like I'm \"him\" now", "Like we don't know each other, we ain't grow together", "We just friends now"], "meaning": "Drake’s dad, Dennis Graham, is from Memphis. Drake used to spend his summers in Memphis with his pop’s family. After his success, his relationship with them changed.\n\n\n\n“Him” meaning Drizzy is “that nigga”, but also could be referring to his father. Drizzy has stated before that he’s very similar to him", "annotation_id": "2205178", "url": "https://genius.com/2205178", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Back rub from my main thing, I’ve been stressed out Talkin’ to her like back then they didn’t want me, I’m blessed now", "vault_lines": ["Back rub from my main thing, I've been stressed out", "Talkin' to her like back then they didn't want me, I'm blessed now"], "meaning": "I’m way up, I feel blessed\n\nChillin' with his girl, Drake tells her about his struggles. When he first came out no one thought he’d be anything, now he’s blessed and one of the biggest artists in the game. He’s got everything he needs\n\nThis shares the same message as Houston rapper Mike Jones‘ “Back Then”\n\nBack then hoes didn’t want me\nNow I’m hot, hoes all on me", "annotation_id": "2205168", "url": "https://genius.com/2205168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Backstage at Warehouse in ’09 like, ”Is Bun coming?", "vault_lines": ["Backstage at Warehouse in '09 like, \"Is Bun coming?"], "meaning": "Drake was wondering before his show at the Warehouse if Houston legend Bun B would actually show up to perform their song, “Uptown.”\n\nWhich, he did\n\nhttp://youtu.be/B2MXvIHttXI", "annotation_id": "2205163", "url": "https://genius.com/2205163", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Heard once that in dire times when you need a sign That’s when they appear Guess since my text message didn’t resonate, I’ll just say it here I hate the fact my mom cooped up in her apartment Telling herself that she’s too sick to get dressed up and go do shit Like that’s true shit", "vault_lines": ["You gotta trust that shit", "Heard once that in dire times when you need a sign", "That's when they appear", "Guess since my text message didn't resonate, I'll just say it here", "I hate the fact my mom cooped up in her apartment", "Telling herself that she's too sick to get dressed up and go do shit", "Like that's true shit"], "meaning": "A lot of people wait for a “sign” that they need to do a certain thing. Drake’s been texting his mom, but it doesn’t do anything so he’s gonna tell her on this song.\n\nHis mom hasn’t been getting out of her house lately using the excuse that she is sick, which Drizzy thinks is untrue. Although, Drake’s mom has been very sick before and to the extent Drake has cancelled shows to be with her.\n\n\n\nHe’s angry with his mom for giving in to her sickness. He knows she’s sick, but he also knows that she’s strong – strong enough to keep pushing on and living life.", "annotation_id": "2205136", "url": "https://genius.com/2205136", "state": "accepted", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "line": "Uh, someone go tell Noel to get the Backwoods", "vault_lines": ["Uh, someone go tell Noel to get the Backwoods"], "meaning": "Noel Cadastre is Drake’s assistant recording engineer. After Drake’s done recording this verse he wants to smoke out to relieve some stress on his mind.\n\n\n\nDrake wants Noel to get Backwoods Brand Cigars in particular so he can smoke.\n\n\n\nLike Swisher Sweets, Backwoods are mostly used for their wraps – hollowing them out and refilling with weed. This is most likely Drake’s plan.", "annotation_id": "2205126", "url": "https://genius.com/2205126", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Much", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 19, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-too-much-lyrics", "genius_title": "Too Much"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I’ma just come like tat-tat-tat I’ma just end that there, that’s that", "vault_lines": ["I'ma just come like tat-tat-tat", "I'ma just end that there, that's that"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t like verbal conflict and would rather to just shoot his enemies ….bottom line.", "annotation_id": "20443655", "url": "https://genius.com/20443655", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "All that bark but we know he’s a cat I don’t really like goin’ tit for tat", "vault_lines": ["All that bark but we know he's a cat", "I don't really like goin' tit for tat"], "meaning": "Drake knows that his enemies are all talk and no action but refuses to fall into feed into the back and forth.\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: He’s talking about Pusha T.", "annotation_id": "20443648", "url": "https://genius.com/20443648", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Can’t do love ’cause they get too attached Phone get broke and whip get scratched", "vault_lines": ["Can't do love 'cause they get too attached", "Phone get broke and whip get scratched"], "meaning": "Drake is not looking for love because of the dangers that come with emotional attachment.\nDrake just wants to live his life freely to do whatever he wishes without worrying about hurting anyone’s feelings.", "annotation_id": "20443636", "url": "https://genius.com/20443636", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I got so much tings in the stash", "vault_lines": ["I got so much tings in the stash"], "meaning": "Drake implies that he is sitting on a plethora of unreleased music that would defeat anyone that rises against him in a lyrical battle.", "annotation_id": "20443626", "url": "https://genius.com/20443626", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I just wanna make these songs for the set", "vault_lines": ["I just wanna make these songs for the set"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t want to be concerned with the drama that comes along with being a Hip Hop artiste and would rather just make songs for him and his friends to enjoy not really caring about the opinion of the outside world.", "annotation_id": "20443612", "url": "https://genius.com/20443612", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I don’t have time for the wasteman jokes Personal ting if I’m gettin’ up close", "vault_lines": ["I don't have time for the wasteman jokes", "Personal ting if I'm gettin' up close"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t plan on doing back and forth with wastemen ( A Jamaican slang term for an idiot/timewaster).\n\nDrake would rather a physical altercation rather than a verbal one if there is tension with him and an artiste.", "annotation_id": "20443588", "url": "https://genius.com/20443588", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Fold up anyone, dun that clash", "vault_lines": ["Fold up anyone, dun that clash"], "meaning": "Drake is suggesting he can compete and defeat any person in a ‘clash’, which is a majority-used UK term for a battle between two MC’s, though the term derives from Patois.\n\nDrake uses the Patois slang term ‘dun’ which in this context means to complete the clash.", "annotation_id": "14961712", "url": "https://genius.com/14961712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "They told me relax ‘cause they’re takin’ control Take all that shit up with P and his bro", "vault_lines": ["They told me relax 'cause they're takin' control", "Take all that shit up with P and his bro"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to Rap-a-Lot Records CEO James “J” Prince, who told Drake not to respond to Pusha T’s “The Story of Adidon” diss track.\nhttp://www.newsweek.com/james-j-prince-drake-pusha-t-957140\n\nDuring an interview Prince said he spoke with Drake and instructed him to let the rap dispute die.\n“I made an OG call to Drake this morning to tell him ‘I don’t want you to respond to this,’” he said.\n\nDrake is saying that for all those who wanted to hear his come back to Pusha’s diss should take it up with J Prince.", "annotation_id": "14956706", "url": "https://genius.com/14956706", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I just wanna load that and let that wet", "vault_lines": ["I just wanna load that and let that wet"], "meaning": "Someone getting “wet” is typical Hip-Hop slang (also used in UK Drill rap) referring to someone getting shot. So in this line Drake implies that he just wants to load up a gun and let that “wet” (shoot) his enemies.", "annotation_id": "14945952", "url": "https://genius.com/14945952", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Still can’t see them after it snows", "vault_lines": ["Still can't see them after it snows"], "meaning": "Drake adds a double meaning to the word “snow” relating it to the previous line referencing “road” which is slang in the UK for drugs and at the same time using it to refer to money.\n\nIn this line Drake is saying that Pusha T and Kayne West can retweet what they want on twitter and act like they used to be in the drug game to make music and make more money but Drake is going to pay no mind to them because with all the money he is making they are not even in the picture.\n\nDrake could also be referencing the simple lack of visibility when your driving in a snow storm and not being able to see.\n\nIn the song Yes Indeed by Lil Baby (feat. drake), Drake uses “snow” as a double meaning again stating:\n\n“I tell her, \"look up” ‘cause it’s snowin’ in Tootsie’s\"\n\n\n\nTootsie’s Cabaret is the #1 strip club in Miami, FL which is known to draw big name celebrities from around the world and is frequented by Drake.", "annotation_id": "14944055", "url": "https://genius.com/14944055", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Big Scorpion, out now", "vault_lines": ["Big Scorpion, out now"], "meaning": "Drake shouts his new album, Scorpion released little over a week before the release of this freestyle", "annotation_id": "14943435", "url": "https://genius.com/14943435", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "You’re droppin’ some shit but that shit was a bust", "vault_lines": ["You're droppin' some shit but that shit was a bust"], "meaning": "This is probably a subliminal shot at Kanye West, who’s upcoming album (which we now know is ye) was extremely hyped being the first studio album he had worked on since The Life of Pablo in 2016, however consisting of only 7 songs and lasting only 23 minutes, it seemed to underwhelm many.\n\nKanye also dropped ‘Lift Yourself’ as well as ‘Ye vs. the People’ however they quickly became memes and joke songs; unsurprisingly as it had lines such as:\n\nPoopy di-scoop\nScoop-diddy-whoop\nWhoop-di-scoop-di-poop\nPoop-di-scoopty\nScoopty-whoop\n\nAs Drake wanted the beat to the first song mentioned and Kanye apparently butchered it, he wasn’t left incredibly happy.", "annotation_id": "14943355", "url": "https://genius.com/14943355", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Your man is a goofy and he could get rushed", "vault_lines": ["Your man is a goofy and he could get rushed"], "meaning": "“Goofy” is a native Chicago term that relates to a foolish person. Drake calls out his opponents and claims that their associates are quite ignorant and could be eliminated with ease as “rushed” is UK slang for “attacked.”\n\nDrake has previously shown distaste to “goofies” on “Hype”:\n\nI hate a goofy especially\nThey always dyin' to mention me\nThey gotta die out eventually", "annotation_id": "14942900", "url": "https://genius.com/14942900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I wish you the best, let me know how it goes Wanna be free and I wanna let go", "vault_lines": ["I wish you the best, let me know how it goes", "Wanna be free and I wanna let go", "You know how the paigon chit-chat goes"], "meaning": "Since this freestyle appears to take aim at Kanye West, Drake could be riffing on the outro of Kanye’s song “Ghost Town” where 070 Shake sings:\n\nWoah, once again I am a child\nI let it all go, of everything that I know, yeah\nOf everything that I know, yeah\nAnd nothing hurts anymore, I feel kinda free\n\nDrake wants to make a clean break with his old friend. He’s got too much on his plate to get sucked into more drama.", "annotation_id": "14938215", "url": "https://genius.com/14938215", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "They do anything except road", "vault_lines": ["They do anything except road"], "meaning": "In UK slang, “doing road,” refers to living the street life such as fighting rivals yet is mainly selling or moving drugs.\n\nDrake questions the street credibility of his foes, most specifically, rapper, Pusha T. In Drake’s most recent diss track, “Duppy Freestyle”, he accuses Pusha of lying in reference to the amount of drugs he pushed\n\nMan, you might’ve sold to college kids for Nike and Mercedes\nBut you act like you sold drugs for Escobar in the ‘80s\n\nThis could also be a sub aimed at Kanye for not being able to finish his Saint Pablo Tour when he was hospitalized in late 2016.", "annotation_id": "14938111", "url": "https://genius.com/14938111", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "We seen man last night, they froze Wasn’t no cameras, wasn’t no pose", "vault_lines": ["We seen man last night, they froze", "Wasn't no cameras, wasn't no pose"], "meaning": "These lines seem like they were inspired by Big Shaq’s “Man’s Not Hot”:\n\nYou man thought I froze\nI see a peng girl, then I pose (chilin')\n\nHowever, Drake spins it to show that his crew can be intimidating.", "annotation_id": "14936141", "url": "https://genius.com/14936141", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Just like that one time at ’Chella Good thing man weren’t pullin’ out phones", "vault_lines": ["Just like that one time at 'Chella", "Good thing man weren't pullin' out phones", "Just like that one time at Nobu"], "meaning": "Drake has had a tumultuous relationship with the famous Coachella Music Festival.\n\nDrake headlined Coachella in 2015, performing songs from his mixtape If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late. This was also the set where he spat out a sloppy kiss from Madonna, spawning a bunch of memes.\n\n\n\nAfter a surprise appearance during Future’s set in 2017, Drake took to Instagram to accuse a Coachella Country Club of racial profiling. The club in question, named The Madison Club, is located only a few miles from the Coachella festival site. Drake posted an image of the club’s logo alongside the brutal caption, “the most offensive place I have ever stayed at in my life with staff who pick and choose who they are going to accommodate based on racial profiling.”\n\nDrake’s insinuating that not everything he does makes it into the media, he’s been in a few situations that he’s glad never surfaced.", "annotation_id": "14935644", "url": "https://genius.com/14935644", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Staying in big six-six with woes", "vault_lines": ["Staying in big six-six with woes"], "meaning": "Drake may be referring to the province of Ontario which contains his hometown of Toronto. Drake is well known for shouting out his hometown and was recently credited for bringing around $440 million dollars of revenue a year to Toronto.\n\nThe line also references his track “Know Yourself” where he raps:\n\nI was running through the six with my woes\n\nDrizzy could also be referring to his record label October’s Very Own. a company based in Toronto, Canada.", "annotation_id": "14934639", "url": "https://genius.com/14934639", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I got way too big off Views", "vault_lines": ["I got way too big off Views"], "meaning": "Drake’s 2016 album Views sold 1 million copies in its first week and shattered a number of streaming records, becoming his most successful album of his career at the time. The success of Views cemented Drake’s position as one of the biggest names in music.", "annotation_id": "14934233", "url": "https://genius.com/14934233", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Just like that one time at Nobu Good thing man weren’t pullin’ out phones", "vault_lines": ["Just like that one time at 'Chella", "Good thing man weren't pullin' out phones", "Just like that one time at Nobu"], "meaning": "Nobu is a chain of Japanese restaurants located all around the world (as well as London).\n\nDrake mentioned the restaurant on YG’s 2015 track “Who Do You Love” where he rapped:\n\nEatin' crab out in Malibu at Nobu\n\nNobu is famous for its high-quality food as well as its celebrity visitors. Because of this people aren’t allowed to pull out their phones and take photos of the celebrities who often visit.\n\n\n\nDrake with Anwar Hadid at Nobu Malibu in 2013", "annotation_id": "14934150", "url": "https://genius.com/14934150", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "You know how the paigon chit-chat goes", "vault_lines": ["I wish you the best, let me know how it goes", "You know how the paigon chit-chat goes"], "meaning": "‘Paigon’ is a London slang term, meaning ‘enemy’ or ‘fake friend’. The term is commonly used in UK Rap.\n\nUK artist Stormzy famously called the British Prime Minister, Theresa May, a paigon during his 2017 GQ acceptance speech.\n\n\n\nDrake is stating that he knows what his paigons (enemies) predictably discuss about him.", "annotation_id": "14933040", "url": "https://genius.com/14933040", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Yeah, Link Up TV, Behind Barz", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, Link Up TV, Behind Barz", "Yeah, Link Up TV"], "meaning": "Behind Barz is one of the most popular UK freestyle series. Having made its debut in 2009, the series has grown in significance ever since. A majority of emerging UK rappers begin their career with a Behind Barz freestyle, however established artists also routinely perform – Stormzy, Chip, Wretch 32, MoStack and J Hus, among others, have all appeared on the series.", "annotation_id": "14932519", "url": "https://genius.com/14932519", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Got no sense like Jizzle and shh Big and bad like leader and shh", "vault_lines": ["Got no sense like Jizzle and shh", "Big and bad like leader and shh"], "meaning": "Drake’s use of “shh” is a reference to the UK Drill adlib popularized by artists such as  Kwengface, Headie One & 86.\n\nThe adlib, often used to silence someone, is used to censor a line in order to not disclose incriminating information, while implying it at the same time.\n\nJizzle is a friend of Drake and has been mentioned previously on “Wanna Know (Remix)”.", "annotation_id": "14932423", "url": "https://genius.com/14932423", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Versace hotel and I’m takin’ the robes", "vault_lines": ["Versace hotel and I'm takin' the robes"], "meaning": "The Versace hotel, called Palazzo Versace, is located on the Gold Coast in Australia. Drake toured Australia in November 2017 so it is possible that he may have stayed in the hotel during his time down under and taken one of their famous bathrobes.\n\n\n\nWe know Drake loves Versace because of his 2013 remix of Migos' smash hit “Versace,” an ode to the titular designer clothing brand.", "annotation_id": "14932412", "url": "https://genius.com/14932412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Seen ‘em in person, I’m seein’ a ghost", "vault_lines": ["Seen 'em in person, I'm seein' a ghost"], "meaning": "This is likely a reference to the Kanye x Cudi album KIDS SEE GHOSTS.\n\n\n\nAlso, Drake’s diss track towards Pusha T was called “Duppy Freestyle”; a Duppy being a Jamaican term for ghost or spirit.", "annotation_id": "14932405", "url": "https://genius.com/14932405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Got all the tea and I’m hottin’ it up I need a shade mansion, a room’s not enough", "vault_lines": ["Got all the tea and I'm hottin' it up", "I need a shade mansion, a room's not enough"], "meaning": "“The tea” refers to a piece of sensitive and possibly highly sought-after information. Drake plays with the idea of literal tea, insinuating that he’s going to bring all of this drama to a boiling point.\n\nThe Shade Room is a media company based around celebrity gossip. A mansion house has several rooms, implying that Drake has enough dirt to keep The Shade Room busy for a while. This might also be alluding to what J. Prince said about Drake having a career-ending diss track that would hurt people at G.O.O.D. Music.", "annotation_id": "14932339", "url": "https://genius.com/14932339", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I dream about turnin’ these yutes into dust", "vault_lines": ["I dream about turnin' these yutes into dust"], "meaning": "Yute is Jamaican Patois, used commonly in the UK as slang for “youth.” Yutes is similar to saying “guys” but is a little more disrespectful since you’re basically calling someone a kid. Drake has used the term before on the More Life song, “No Long Talk.”", "annotation_id": "14932145", "url": "https://genius.com/14932145", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Throwin’ up six like man had flu", "vault_lines": ["Throwin' up six like man had flu"], "meaning": "Drake has been throwing up the “6” hand signal for years now, an ode to his native Toronto. The “6” hand even made it on the single artwork for “Summer Sixteen.”\n\n\n\nDrake makes a play on fact that throwing up and the near homophone of “six” “sick” are both associated with having the flu.", "annotation_id": "14931913", "url": "https://genius.com/14931913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "You know how the paigon chit-chat goes", "vault_lines": ["I wish you the best, let me know how it goes", "You know how the paigon chit-chat goes"], "meaning": "‘Paigon’ is a London slang term, meaning ‘enemy’ or ‘fake friend’. The term is commonly used in UK Rap.\n\nUK artist Stormzy famously called the British Prime Minister, Theresa May, a ‘paigon’ during his 2017 GQ acceptance speech.\n\n\n\nDrake is stating that he knows what his paigons (enemies) predictably discuss about him.", "annotation_id": "14931868", "url": "https://genius.com/14931868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I like Rs and Vs and Os I don’t really play no tic-tac-toe", "vault_lines": ["I like Rs and Vs and Os", "I don't really play no tic-tac-toe"], "meaning": "It is unclear what each of these letters represents, but “R” likely stands for OVO affiliate Preme’s collective Reps Up. “R” could also designates the Toronto Raptors, Drake’s hometown NBA team. He has served as the global ambassador of the franchise since 2013. “V’s and O’s” are likely a reference to the letters that constitute OVO, the acronym for his personal brand and label: October’s Very Own.\n\nDrake’s refusal to play tic-tac-toe could be a reference to the fact that he did not respond to Pusha T’s diss track “The Story of Adidon.” Tic-tac-toe is notable for its rapid back and forth gameplay.\n\nIt could also be a possible shot at The Weeknd, whose brand and label XO is made up of the letters used in the game tic-tac-toe.\n\n\n\nDrake rapped a similar line on “Summer Sixteen”:\n\nAll your exes know I like my O’s with a V in the middle\n\nDrake was linked to The Weeknd’s ex Bella Hadid in 2017 while Abel was dating Selena Gomez, and the following bars on the Scorpion track “Elevate” have been perceived as intended shots at Abel:\n\nI already hit her when you left her lonely\nShe is not the type that likes to take things slowly", "annotation_id": "14931826", "url": "https://genius.com/14931826", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Big up man like Rashid, Joey, Tadas, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Big up man like Rashid, Joey, Tadas, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake takes the opportunity to hail up three influential figures behind the scenes at Link Up TV: founder Rashid Kasirye, visual director/editor Tadas Vanagas, and A&R Joey Clipstar.", "annotation_id": "14931614", "url": "https://genius.com/14931614", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Man start dissin’ and doin’ reposts", "vault_lines": ["Man start dissin' and doin' reposts"], "meaning": "This could refer to when Pusha reposted a picture of Drake in blackface from something he’d done in 2007.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/PUSHA_T/status/1001625145230929920", "annotation_id": "14931554", "url": "https://genius.com/14931554", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "I know so much shit that I cannot expose I keep it inside and I laugh on my own", "vault_lines": ["I know so much shit that I cannot expose", "I keep it inside and I laugh on my own"], "meaning": "Following Pusha-T’s “The Story of Adidon,” a disstrack aimed at Drake, reports claimed that the Toronto rapper had a response that supposedly would have ended both Push and Kanye’s career.\n\nHowever, J. Prince, CEO of Rap-a-Lot Records, intervened, telling Drake not to release his response, and later gave a comment on the conflict when guesting on the radio show Sway in the Morning:\n\nAfter speaking with Kanye, I spoke with the brother, and he didn’t want this. I saw this going to a place that I feel would have ended his career if Drake would’ve put out this song that he had on him and definitely would’ve hurt families and we’re not in it for that.\n\nThese lines bear a similarity to the Scorpion track “Emotionless,” where Drizzy also mentioned it was best to simply say nothing:\n\nThey always ask, “Why let the story run if it’s false?”\nYou know a wise man once said nothin' at all", "annotation_id": "14931532", "url": "https://genius.com/14931532", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "They wanna link when they got no chunes They too worried ‘bout sellin’ out shoes I don’t give a fuck about jeans and crep Or goin’ to Milan or goin’ to the Met", "vault_lines": ["They wanna link when they got no chunes", "They too worried 'bout sellin' out shoes", "I don't give a fuck about jeans and crep", "Or goin' to Milan or goin' to the Met"], "meaning": "This is likely a shot at Kanye West. Kanye has been focused on a career in fashion with his Yeezy line in recent years, particularly his Yeezy sneakers (“crep”).\n\nEver since his rant during the Saint Pablo tour concerning Drake and DJ Khaled’s song “For Free” receiving too much airplay, it has seemed that Kanye envies Drake’s hit-making prowess.\n\nAs suggested on “Duppy Freestyle,” Drake believes that Kanye seeks to leverage off this. He only seeks to collaborate when he cannot make any hits himself (“chunes”). It is no secret that Kanye has come to Drake for his assistance in songwriting, happening most recently in May 2018 for Kanye’s ye album and in 2016 for songs such as “30 Hours” and “Father Stretch My Hands, Pt 1.”\n\nThese lines are also a continuation of the subliminals that Drake and Kanye traded with each other in mid 2018. Kanye took a shot at Drake on “No Mistakes,” which promoted a  response from Drake on “8 Out of 10.” He also addressed the situation between him and Kanye on “Survival.”", "annotation_id": "14931507", "url": "https://genius.com/14931507", "state": "accepted", "votes": 101, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Shoutout Giggs for settin’ the ting SN1, OVO", "vault_lines": ["Shoutout Giggs for settin' the ting"], "meaning": "Drake thanks Giggs for setting him up with the opportunity of doing the Link Up TV Behind Barz freestyle, who has been a regular on the channel.\n\nGiggs is a London native from Peckham, who Drake featured twice on his 2017 playlist More Life, on the tracks “No Long Talk” and “KMT.” Drake first shouted Giggs out on Dave’s “Wanna Know (Remix)” by referencing his 4th studio album, Landlord:\n\nCall up Landlord, nigga that’s bro\n\nOVO (October’s Very Own) is Drake’s record label and slogan. SN1 (Spare No. 1) is Giggs' record label and rap collective.", "annotation_id": "14931467", "url": "https://genius.com/14931467", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "We got ties in West End too", "vault_lines": ["We got ties in West End too"], "meaning": "Drake has known affiliates in South London with the likes of Giggs and Dave, but claims to also have friends on London’s more affluent West End.\n\nDrake used an almost identical line on the Giggs assisted track “No Long Talk”:\n\nBut we still got love for the West End", "annotation_id": "14931466", "url": "https://genius.com/14931466", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Been with Roxx through highs and lows", "vault_lines": ["Been with Roxx through highs and lows", "Been with Chubbs through highs and lows"], "meaning": "Jonny Roxx is Drake’s personal trainer. He stated in a 2017 interview that he has known Drake for over ten years.", "annotation_id": "14931463", "url": "https://genius.com/14931463", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Behind Barz (Link Up TV Freestyle)", "line": "Been with Chubbs through highs and lows", "vault_lines": ["Been with Roxx through highs and lows", "Been with Chubbs through highs and lows"], "meaning": "Chubbs, government name Nessel Beezer, is Drake’s bodyguard. He is a native of Scarborough, Ontario and has been affiliated with Drake since the early years of his career. Chubbs acts as Drake’s body guard or “Fixer” as Drake has referred to him in the past.\n\n\n\nChubbs and Drake pictured courtside\n\nDrake also mentioned Chubbs on his song “No Long Talk” from his 2017 More Life project:\n\nJust know man like Chubbs \nHe’s a fixer if I ever gotta fix tings", "annotation_id": "14931456", "url": "https://genius.com/14931456", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
