{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "It’s just me, 40, O, and Nik standing in a huddle", "vault_lines": ["It's just me, 40, O, and Nik standing in a huddle"], "meaning": "Drake is simply saying that; himself, Noah “40” Shebib, Oliver El-Khatib & Niko who are apart of OVO with him are the people standing by his side when times are tough.\n\nThe 4 of them strategize, move and execute together. In addition to that, Drake in a way is thanking them for staying true to him and staying loyal on his journey to the fame and getting signed", "annotation_id": "20783216", "url": "https://genius.com/20783216", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "No auto-tune, but you can feel the pain", "vault_lines": ["No auto-tune, but you can feel the pain"], "meaning": "A wordplay with the  commmom vocal effect nowadays  called “Auto-Tune” and one of the artists that became best known for using and popularizing this effect, T-Pain.\nOn the back, Drake uses this punhline to show that he doesn’t need any adjustments to his voice and still manages to pass the emotion (pain) perfectly.\n\n\n\nYou can find a plugin done to achieve the same effect as T-Pain.", "annotation_id": "19813030", "url": "https://genius.com/19813030", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "(Don’t take this the wrong way but, uh) I never cried when Pac died", "vault_lines": ["I never cried when Pac died"], "meaning": "A lot of people also sort of don’t understand the meaning of that line. It’s not necessarily that I don’t love West Coast Hip-Hop or that I don’t love Pac now that I’m 22, but that line was just said to show how new I am to Hip-Hop. And like I said, I was 9 when that happened, so it didn’t really affect me. But that song in general was just one of those things that took awhile to finish because it was an opportunity to say a lot of the things on my mind. That always feels good as a rapper, when you can get your thoughts out.\n\n\n\n\nI bought a piece of art when I was in LA, I went to an art showing. I was walking around looking at all the pieces, and I came to this one piece and my heart dropped. It was this light piece, and it said ‘Less Drake, More Tupac.’\n\nI’m staring at the wall, man, and at first it was just anger. I felt like ripping that shit down. Then I stood there for a second, and I was like, ‘That’s my name up there. I’m representative of this generation, and this guy loved Pac enough to make this piece, and that’s fucking amazing man.’ Because you know what? I hope some day someone will make that piece for me, and it’ll be like, ‘Less Whatever, More Drake.’\n\nBut just the fact that my name is up there at this art gallery as a piece of someone’s art,  and they felt like… It could have been anybody else. It’s evident that he doesn’t like my music, which is okay. I ended up buying the piece and putting it up in my crib too. It cost me like $6,000. I put it in my place in Miami, in the studio, just to remind me that I’m doing something right.", "annotation_id": "11227393", "url": "https://genius.com/11227393", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "But I probably will when Hov does And if my tears hold value then I would drop one For every single thing he showed us And I’ll be standing in a puddle", "vault_lines": ["But I probably will when Hov does", "And if my tears hold value then I would drop one", "For every single thing he showed us", "And I'll be standing in a puddle"], "meaning": "Jay Z is one of the titans of the rap game. He created the blueprint for up-and-coming rappers on how to make it from having nothing to sitting on rap’s Throne. Drake understands Hov’s place in rap history, and he holds him in high regard as an idol and mentor:\n\nIt’s one of the strongest relationships, if not the strongest [of with any rapper] … He’s just like an incredible mentor to me. I’ll forever be looking up [to him].\n\nWhen it comes time for Hov to pass on, Drake admits that he will cry so many tears that they could form a puddle around him. Each of his tears has a value equal to “one thing that Hov taught us.” A puddle = thousands upon thousands of tears, therefore meaning Hov has given Drake, and the rest of the rap game, thousands of jewels.", "annotation_id": "11227341", "url": "https://genius.com/11227341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "And him missing in my life is kind of messing with me I hope this isn’t one of those forever things", "vault_lines": ["And him missing in my life is kind of messing with me", "I hope this isn't one of those forever things"], "meaning": "Since Fear was made, Drake has settled disputes with his uncle. On his sopohomore album Take Care he raps about this:\n\nKnow that I’m your sister’s kid but\nIt still don’t explain the love that you have for me\n\n\n\nThis was after So Far Gone, but it’s nice to see that things have settled between the two.", "annotation_id": "1960169", "url": "https://genius.com/1960169", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "Don’t believe the lies, look me in my eyes", "vault_lines": ["Don't believe the lies, look me in my eyes"], "meaning": "Drake raps about how great his life is, but the truth is, it’s not as great as people think it is. The hook and song were written because rumors were started that Drake did not write his own lyrics in which he refers to as “lies”\n\nThis hook is also referring to the fact that drakes friends keep saying hes changed. When he sees people he use to know its awkward and the seem afraid since he seems so much higher than them now due to fame.\n\nWe can also see the implicit reference of Tupac :\n\n“Real eyes/realize/real lies” is a homophone triplet that is famously quoted by 2Pac.", "annotation_id": "55546", "url": "https://genius.com/55546", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "What up, Lil Bro?", "vault_lines": ["What up, Lil Bro?"], "meaning": "Little Brother is a rap group from North Carolina with whom he did a few songs with before his popularity sprouted up. Such as, “Don’t You Have a Man” and “Think Good Thoughts”. They broke up in 2010.", "annotation_id": "30156", "url": "https://genius.com/30156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "What up, Slum Ville?", "vault_lines": ["What up, Slum Ville?"], "meaning": "More “alterna-rapper” shout-outs (Slum Village) is a hip-Hop group from Detroit)\n\nDrake is on record as a big Slum Village fan, as he has performed “Climax (Girl Shit)”, a SV classic at a concert in LA in 2010. He has also done work with SV member Elzhi on “Think Good Thoughts”.", "annotation_id": "30155", "url": "https://genius.com/30155", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "Yeah, and my uncle ain’t even messaging me", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and my uncle ain't even messaging me"], "meaning": "Drake means that his uncle has stopped talking to him; he also played a big part in his life. On “Say What’s Real” Drake’s uncle gives him some sage advice (this was obviously before he stopped messaging him:\n\nI had a talk with my uncle and he agreed that\nMy privacy about the only thing I need back\n\nHe also talks about his uncle in Successful and The Calm. The person he was “screaming into the phone to” was his uncle. He had an altercation with him that day and things got out of hand..", "annotation_id": "26634", "url": "https://genius.com/26634", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear", "line": "And I been getting high just to balance out the lows", "vault_lines": ["And I been getting high just to balance out the lows"], "meaning": "Drake is depicting life as a series of “highs” and “lows”. He describes his “high” as “getting high”, i.e. smoking marijuana, and how that balances out the “low” moments in his life.", "annotation_id": "26483", "url": "https://genius.com/26483", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
