{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Punt", "vault_lines": ["We on the side and we punt"], "meaning": "kicking a football (american football)", "annotation_id": "36910516", "url": "https://genius.com/36910516", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I put a check up on my neck", "vault_lines": ["I can't be wasting time on the 'net, I just be running up a fuckin' check", "I put a check up on my neck, life check ya in the head", "I put a check up on my neck"], "meaning": "check refers to alot of money and yeat puts diamond chains on his neck", "annotation_id": "36910499", "url": "https://genius.com/36910499", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "My dawg in the can like a metal lid Three the kid, three the kid, three the kid", "vault_lines": ["My dawg in the can like a metal lid", "Three the kid, three the kid, three the kid"], "meaning": "“In the can” is slang for being in jail. Drake plays on the homonym “can” by metaphorically comparing his friend’s incarceration to a metal lid inside a literal can.\n\n“Free the kid” is a plea for his boy’s release, but Drizzy flips it further by playing on the homophones “free” and “three”, repeating free three times.", "annotation_id": "36283852", "url": "https://genius.com/36283852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "(BNYX)", "vault_lines": ["I called up my twizz' (BNYX)"], "meaning": "Benjamin Saint Fort (born April 23, 1995), professionally known as BNYX®, (pronounced Benny X), is a record producer from Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. He is known for his production for Drake, Yeat, Lil Uzi Vert, Travis Scott, and Lancey Foux, among others.\n\nBNYX® acquired his pseudonym as a result of taking his nickname, Benny, and attaching it to the letter X, which he describes stands for “extreme,” a quality that he strives to achieve when creating music.\n\nIn 2021, BNYX® signed to record label and production group, Working on Dying.\n\nI-I-I’m working on dying, BNYX", "annotation_id": "31027083", "url": "https://genius.com/31027083", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "All of my twizz’ got a gun (Blam) Couple of my twizz’ on the run (Phew)", "vault_lines": ["All of my twizz' got a gun (Blam)", "Couple of my twizz' on the run (Phew)", "Yeah, couple of my twizz' sell a bunch"], "meaning": "In this line; Yeat is referring to his “twizzies” (which are his homies), by saying how they all posses firearms and how they are on the run for possibly doing something wrong.", "annotation_id": "31027010", "url": "https://genius.com/31027010", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Luh geek", "vault_lines": ["And I'm poppin' these Percs just for fun (Luh geek", "And I'm poppin' these Percs just for fun (Luh geek)", "Couple of my twizz' sellin' Runtz (Luh geek)", "Couple of my twizz' cut 'em up (Luh geek)"], "meaning": "Luh Geeky is a Yeatanese rapper who was featured on Yeat’s song ‘Now’\nYou can hear him moaning very loudly in the background saying “I got bands now.”\n\nLuh Geek would translate into English as little high which Yeat would be after ‘poppin’ these percs' in the line this is the adlib to.\n\nYeat also has an unreleased song called “luh geeky” https://on.soundcloud.com/p4sYVnbBQp4M2cDz6", "annotation_id": "29875976", "url": "https://genius.com/29875976", "state": "pending", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "If you did the crime, bitch, why you cryin’?", "vault_lines": ["If you did the crime, bitch, why you cryin'?"], "meaning": "Taking to Kantian ethics, Yeat utilizes a detonological questioning of his lover, whether a female domesticated dog or a partner referred to as such, on why they are catalyzing moisture from their tear ducts over inconsequential affairs that apparently have broken the purview of the law", "annotation_id": "29870104", "url": "https://genius.com/29870104", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I signed for a couple milli’, you only touched a hundred thou’", "vault_lines": ["I signed for a couple milli', you only touched a hundred thou'"], "meaning": "“I signed for a couple milli'” means that Yeat has signed a record deal or contract with a music label, and as a result, he’s set to make several million dollars from this deal. This is a common theme in rap music where artists often sign lucrative contracts with major record labels.\n\nThe second part of the line, “you only touched a hundred thou',” is a way of saying that the person he’s addressing has only made or earned $100,000. In the context of the lyrics, Yeat is emphasizing the vast difference in their financial achievements. He’s saying that while he’s set to make millions through his music career, the other person has only managed to earn $100,000, which is significantly less. This is a common theme in hip-hop lyrics, where artists frequently boast about their wealth and success, often in a competitive or confrontational manner.\nAdditionally, Yeat references a lyric from a previous collaborator of his, Autumn!. Since 2021, the two had not been on good terms, and previously, Autumn! stated in one of the duo’s songs, Probably!:\n\nAin’t got a million, it’s more like a hundred thousand in my bank\".", "annotation_id": "29841095", "url": "https://genius.com/29841095", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I stay with that O like a tire I stay with that O like a bagel", "vault_lines": ["I stay with that O like a tire", "I stay with that O like a bagel", "I stay with that O like a lifesaver"], "meaning": "O here could mean multiple things, such as a zero in an amount of money. But the more likely references are to an ounce of illicit substances, or the other members of Drake’s OVO label that are referenced in the previous line.\n\nObviously a tied knot and a bagel are both in the shape of circles, or O’s.", "annotation_id": "29814060", "url": "https://genius.com/29814060", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I stay with that O like a lifesaver", "vault_lines": ["I stay with that O like a tire", "I stay with that O like a bagel", "I stay with that O like a lifesaver"], "meaning": "Lifesavers are an O shaped mint/candy, intentionally such that when choked on there is still a hole to breath through (that’s why their name is Lifesavers)", "annotation_id": "29773588", "url": "https://genius.com/29773588", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "My Glocky, it sit on the night table You light on the waist like a lightsaber", "vault_lines": ["My Glocky, it sit on the night table", "You light on the waist like a lightsaber"], "meaning": "Referring to his “glock,” Drake keeps his piece on his night table, juxtaposing his opp, who is “light on the waist” a double entendre meaning they are absent of a weapon, also a lightsaber being a “light” weapon holstered on the hip", "annotation_id": "29733109", "url": "https://genius.com/29733109", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Comin’ live from the one after five Comin’ live from the three, two time", "vault_lines": ["Look, comin' live from the", "Comin' live from the one after five", "Comin' live from the three, two time", "Three the kid, three the kid, three the kid"], "meaning": "One after five and two times three are both equal six. Drake says he is comin' live from the six, which is another name for Toronto, Drake’s hometown.\nThe reason Toronto is called the six is because of the six municipalities making up Metro Toronto before they were all joined in 1998. It is also derived from the main area code in Toronto, 416.", "annotation_id": "29726392", "url": "https://genius.com/29726392", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "In Seattle, it’s just me and Twizz’", "vault_lines": ["In Seattle, it's just me and Twizz'"], "meaning": "Drake calls back to when he stopped in Seattle during his “It’s All A Blur” Tour and brought him out for a shoutout.\n\nhttps://x.com/Kurrco/status/1695416005852279280?s=20", "annotation_id": "29725051", "url": "https://genius.com/29725051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "October’s Own, it’s a label", "vault_lines": ["October's Own, it's a label"], "meaning": "October’s Very Own, or OVO (officially known as OVO Sound), is Drake’s music label. OVO was founded by Drake, 40, and Oliver El-Khatib.\n\nThe phrase “October’s Own is a Label” is a reference to the line “YSL is a label”, which was widely used on social media during the RICO case against YSL, Young Thug, and Gunna.\nPolice used lyrics from Young Thug’s music to connect him to gang related crimes. Drake frequently alludes to his mob ties (even got a song named “Mob Ties”) and connection to the streets. This line is telling any feds watching his movements that OVO is a record label and not a criminal organization.", "annotation_id": "29725036", "url": "https://genius.com/29725036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "That ain’t gon’ fly, nope", "vault_lines": ["That ain't gon' fly, nope"], "meaning": "Drake mimics Yeat’s “low voice” that can be seen on some parts of this song.", "annotation_id": "29724957", "url": "https://genius.com/29724957", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Whole gang fuckin’ eastbound and they down just like Danny McBride", "vault_lines": ["Whole gang fuckin' eastbound and they down just like Danny McBride"], "meaning": "Danny McBride is the leading actor, co-creator and co-writer of the sports comedy television series “Eastbound & Down”.", "annotation_id": "29724869", "url": "https://genius.com/29724869", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Traveling forever in the dark Darkness into blackness There and back, it’s always black Oh-ah, it screeched across the sky Nothing to do but lie", "vault_lines": ["Traveling forever in the dark", "There and back, it's always black", "Oh-ah, it screeched across the sky", "Nothing to do but lie"], "meaning": "This track samples Azimuth’s 1977 song “The Tunnel”:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/LRFqc1lUxqQ?si=X0jIObk1dQjtZr7g&t=158", "annotation_id": "29724830", "url": "https://genius.com/29724830", "state": "accepted", "votes": 151, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I just got off the phone with my demon and he told me that I’m gon’ win", "vault_lines": ["I just got off the phone with my demon and he told me that I'm gon' win"], "meaning": "Yeat has mentioned many times that he talks with his demons, and that they have helped him succeed in the industry.\n\nIn his track, “Monëy so big”, Yeat raps:\n\nI got the devil on my phone, he told me, keep doin' this shit\nYeah, devil taught me how to get rich", "annotation_id": "29625877", "url": "https://genius.com/29625877", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Big body Lamb’ truck", "vault_lines": ["Big body Lamb' truck"], "meaning": "Yeat has said in previous songs that he rides in his Lamb' truck, a Lamborghini Urus. He sometimes calls his Urus (along with his Mercedes-Benz GLE) a “Tonka”. This term can be appointed to any of his big body vehicles.\n\nIn “Tonka 2”, Yeat raps about it, saying\n\nTonka, this a big truck, this a Lamb' truck\n\n\n\nHe commonly refers to his Tonka in many tracks as “Big body”, he says this because It’s a Urus. Unlike traditional Lamborghini’s, it is an SUV, with a big body. More examples where he refers to it being:\n\n\nRollin\nBig tonka\nDouble", "annotation_id": "29560142", "url": "https://genius.com/29560142", "state": "pending", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Couple of my twizz’ sellin’ Runtz (Luh geek)", "vault_lines": ["Couple of my twizz' sellin' Runtz (Luh geek)", "Couple of my twizz' cut 'em up (Luh geek)"], "meaning": "Some of Yeat’s friends (of which he refers to as twizzies) get income from selling Runtz, a stronger strand of Marijuana.\n\nYeat has previously sold drugs like weed and cocaine, and has made these friends from affiliation.\n\nWhile Yeat has moved on from selling drugs and has become a rapper, they haven’t.", "annotation_id": "29560078", "url": "https://genius.com/29560078", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IDGAF", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 20, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-idgaf-lyrics", "genius_title": "IDGAF"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Yachts on the 4th of July, G5 soarin’ the skies", "vault_lines": ["Yachts on the 4th of July, G5 soarin' the skies"], "meaning": "In this line, he’s talking about a lavish lifestyle of celebrating the 4th July on yachts in style.\n\nG5 refers to the Gulfstream V/5 jet, which is one of the most luxury private aircraft of this century", "annotation_id": "38451792", "url": "https://genius.com/38451792", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "What you got, time on your hands or time on your side?", "vault_lines": ["What you got, time on your hands or time on your side?"], "meaning": "Drake’s wordplay here is clever.\n\nHe talks about having a lot of “time on your hands” meaning having a lot of free time with nothing to do, and having “time on your side” which refers to having favorable conditions, or a good pace, allowing for unhurried progression towards a goal without being rushed.", "annotation_id": "37874990", "url": "https://genius.com/37874990", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "[Outro: Nai Palm]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I think he’s personifying life using this sample. Nai is life telling Drake she’ll offer more since he feels like there had to be more to it than what he’s now used to.", "annotation_id": "35769901", "url": "https://genius.com/35769901", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Love certain ones but never get attached to ’em", "vault_lines": ["Love certain ones but never get attached to 'em"], "meaning": "this could be a refrence to jorja smith which was one of drakes love intrests for a long time inwhich he spent and put alot of himself into yet it didnt work out. He might of been in love once but he had to let go of her for himself.", "annotation_id": "29073633", "url": "https://genius.com/29073633", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Give ’em nice things, but what’s that to ’em? Especially when another girl I flew in is flickin’ up in my bathroom And they recognize the bathroom", "vault_lines": ["Give 'em nice things, but what's that to 'em?", "Especially when another girl I flew in is flickin' up in my bathroom", "And they recognize the bathroom"], "meaning": "In 2014, Drake allegedly flew model Lira Galore to Toronto. She then took a photo self in the rapper’s bathroom.", "annotation_id": "22770708", "url": "https://genius.com/22770708", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "My peers are a talented group But even if you take all they statistics and carry the two Even if you rounded up the numbers and rounded the troops There’s still nothin’ they could really do", "vault_lines": ["And they recognize the bathroom", "My peers are a talented group", "But even if you take all they statistics and carry the two", "Even if you rounded up the numbers and rounded the troops", "There's still nothin' they could really do"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing the rap peers he’s always compared to, J. Cole & Kendrick Lamar. While Cole & Kendrick are talented, Drake says that their numbers put together couldn’t match his own numbers.", "annotation_id": "22481572", "url": "https://genius.com/22481572", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Only cryin’ I do is cryin’ from laughin’ ’bout it", "vault_lines": ["Only cryin' I do is cryin' from laughin' 'bout it"], "meaning": "https://images.app.goo.gl/dCsETKRkyyobHZgX7", "annotation_id": "20901310", "url": "https://genius.com/20901310", "state": "pending", "votes": -4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "I mean, you know I love a challenge, but challenged by who?", "vault_lines": ["I mean, you know I love a challenge, but challenged by who?"], "meaning": "Drake’s claim to the rap title has been nearly effortless, as he has become untouchable commercially and sees no clear successor to the throne. Even though his detractors may try to find another person to dethrone Drake, with albums like Scorpion dominating the charts, the contemporary artists of his era are highly unlikely to do so. On songs like “Diplomatic Immunity,” Drake touched on the subject of reportedly being surpassed by other rappers when he said:\n\n‘Cause niggas started talkin’ to me like I’m slowin' down\nOpinions over statistics, of course\nGassed off journalistic\nCome at me and all you’ll get is the ballistic report\n\nAs the name of the song implies, Drake wants to know if there are more rappers out there who can offer him a challenge or anyone who can help motivate him to bring his music to the next level.\n\nThis could also be a diss to Pusha T and Kanye West, who he has exchanged shots with prior to the release of this track. After Pusha took shots at Drake for his ghostwriting rumors on “Infrared,” a rap feud entailed, leading to diss tracks such as “Duppy Freestyle” and “The Story of Adidon.” The latter was a vicious song that attacked Drake’s insecurities and exposed Drake’s son to the world, but didn’t elicit a response from Drake. Many people declared Pusha the victor and claimed that he let the beef go, due to a lack of material.\n\nIn these lines, Drake refutes this theory and confirms that he hasn’t released another diss track because he doesn’t feel as though Pusha is worth his time. He has echoed this sentiment before, in “8 Out of 10,” when his sample of Plies implies a similar concept:\n\nI’m happy! Leave me alone!\nI just want some money, a lot of money\nI don’t get paid to argue with you\nNo! Who is you? You ain’t nobody!\nYou mad", "annotation_id": "15177922", "url": "https://genius.com/15177922", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Only problems I do are math problems with profit", "vault_lines": ["Only problems I do are math problems with profit"], "meaning": "The only problems present in Drake’s life are accounting calculations. According to Forbes Magazine in 2017, Drizzy had earned about $94 million and was the most-streamed artist on a global scale.", "annotation_id": "14914635", "url": "https://genius.com/14914635", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Only lackin’ I can do is my lack of responses", "vault_lines": ["Only holdin' up I do is my end of the bargain", "Only lackin' I can do is my lack of responses", "Only rest that I do is, \"Where the rest of my commas?\""], "meaning": "Although addressed many times throughout Scorpion, Drake has yet to release a response to Pusha-T’s “The Story of Adidon.” Pusha’s track was a reply to “Duppy Freestyle” which revealed Drake was hiding a son.\n\nIn fact, Drake has an unreleased reply aimed towards Pusha and Kanye West, but J. Prince urged to keep it unreleased as it was “overwhelming” and “career ending.”\n\nAfter speaking with Kanye, I spoke with the brother…and he didn’t want this. I saw this going to a place that I feel would have ended his career if Drake would’ve put out this song that he had on him. It definitely would’ve hurt families and we’re not in it for that. That’s not Drake’s character to tear a man down to that extent.", "annotation_id": "14896810", "url": "https://genius.com/14896810", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Only rest that I do is, “Where the rest of my commas?”", "vault_lines": ["Only holdin' up I do is my end of the bargain", "Only tryin' that I do is me tryin' the hardest", "Only lackin' I can do is my lack of responses", "Only rest that I do is, \"Where the rest of my commas?\""], "meaning": "Drake uses a double entendre and a play on words in this line.\n\nA comma (,) is a punctuation mark used to take rests or pauses in a sentence. Drake says while songwriting, jotting commas are the only “rests” he takes—implying he works “Nonstop”.\n\nA comma is also used in separating large values in order to simplify reading. For example, 1(,)000(,)000. This has turned the punctuation mark into a slang term for a large amount of money which is why Drake has to take a rest and ask where the rest of his money is.", "annotation_id": "14896673", "url": "https://genius.com/14896673", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "I would rather lose my leg than lose they respect", "vault_lines": ["I would rather lose my leg than lose they respect"], "meaning": "This bar relates to Drake’s relationship with Quentin Miller. In 2016, Q had his leg amputated after sustaining injuries in a car accident. A year earlier, Drake lost respect from many of his fans amidst rumors that Quentin ghostwrote several of his hits from If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late.\n\nIn this line, Drake acknowledges Q’s recent amputation and emphasizes how he would rather lose his leg than betray his fans and those close around him.", "annotation_id": "14893729", "url": "https://genius.com/14893729", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Houston women I wine-and-dine and take to the house My moral compass is janky, it breaks in the South", "vault_lines": ["Houston women I wine-and-dine and take to the house", "My moral compass is janky, it breaks in the South"], "meaning": "In May 2010, Drake began dating Maliah Michel who was at the time an exotic dancer at the Houston strip club Dreams. Michel is also born and bred in Houston, Texas.\n\nIn July of 2017, Drake retired the jerseys of Michel along with the jerseys of Lira Galore and Miracle Watts at Dreams. Like Michel, Galore and Watts were former exotic dancers at Dreams.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BW32WBbjUVm/?utm_source=ig_embed\n\nGalore appreciated Drake’s gesture on her Twitter saying:\n\nWow , made strip club history tonight. Got in , got out .. STILL relevant 🤷🏽‍♀️S/O to the BOY !! @Drake\"\n\nMichel was less appreciative of Drake’s gesture to retire her jersey as she posted a number of disparaging tweets directed at Drake on her Twitter. Amongst Michel’s tweets was the following:\n\nAlways telling me I’m not better than anyone else in the club selling ass instead of dancing. But nigga can’t stay out the club\"\n\nWatts did not comment on Drake’s gesture.\n\nWhile its unknown as to whether Drake has ever wined and dined Galore and/or Watts, its known that Drake previously dated Michel and has likely had other relationships with females from Houston that the public is unaware of.", "annotation_id": "14889311", "url": "https://genius.com/14889311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Too many Walt Disney characters, mouses and goofs", "vault_lines": ["Too many Walt Disney characters, mouses and goofs"], "meaning": "Drake states that the competition is full of rats and goofs (i.e. foolish/stupid people), similar to how Walt Disney (and his namesake company) famously have characters who are mice (Mickey Mouse, Minnie Mouse, etc.) and “goofs” (Goofy and his species)\n\n\n\nHe could also be alluding to Kanye, who has been known to call himself “Walt Disney”.", "annotation_id": "14883046", "url": "https://genius.com/14883046", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Is there more to life than goin’ on trips to Dubai?", "vault_lines": ["Is there more to life than digits and bankin' accounts?", "Is there more to life than sayin' I figured it out?", "Is there more? Yeah", "Is there more to life than goin' on trips to Dubai?", "Is there more to life than all of these corporate ties", "Is there more to life than just when I'm feelin' alive?"], "meaning": "This is not the first time Drake has mentioned Dubai\n\nIn Drake’s “Free Smoke”:\n\n“I wanna move to Dubai\nSo I don’t never have to kick it with none of you guys\n\nDubai is a city associated with a lavish lifestyle and in these lines, Drake is suggesting that after a while this lifestyle can get tiresome.", "annotation_id": "14879334", "url": "https://genius.com/14879334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "It’s too bad reality checks don’t cover the balances due", "vault_lines": ["It's too bad reality checks don't cover the balances due"], "meaning": "Drake utilizes wordplay between “checks and balances” as a subliminal diss towards Kanye West who was involved in a $53 million debt on account of his fashion endeavors. However, this may also callback to an invoice that Drake sent to G.O.O.D. Music based on his beef with Pusha T for “promotional assistance and career-reviving.”\n\nDrake’s notation of Kanye and his label’s “reality checks” goes to show how moments where one is brought back into the life of those around them doesn’t necessarily fix the damages made before this check.", "annotation_id": "14877375", "url": "https://genius.com/14877375", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Sweeter the berry, the blacker the juice", "vault_lines": ["Sweeter the berry, the blacker the juice"], "meaning": "This is an inversion of the classic phrase, “the blacker the berry, the sweeter the juice” from Wallace Thurman’s 1929 novel of the same name which explores themes of colorism. This was originally used in a rap by 2Pac in “Keep Ya Head Up”. It was also notably referenced as the title of a song by Kendrick Lamar.\n\nDrake is basically saying when the sex is better, the consequences are worse or the girl is likely to be a worse person. As “cherry” is a common slang term for vagina, he uses “berry” instead to fit the wordplay of the phrase.", "annotation_id": "14877074", "url": "https://genius.com/14877074", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "I’m in control of my destiny, never in doubt", "vault_lines": ["I'm in control of my destiny, never in doubt"], "meaning": "This could serve as a reference to Quentin Miller’s “Destiny (Freestyle)”. The song was a response to Drake and Pusha T’s feud where Quentin addresses his role in the beef. The song’s narrative is based on his current position in his life and music since Drake’s beef with Meek Mill and his leg amputation in 2016.", "annotation_id": "14876861", "url": "https://genius.com/14876861", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "In the house playin’ D’Angelo, “How Does It Feel?”", "vault_lines": ["In the house playin' D'Angelo, \"How Does It Feel?\""], "meaning": "Drake makes a reference to the 2000s track “Untitled (How Does It Feel)” by American R&B and soul artist, D'Angelo. Interestingly, in June 2018, Drizzy auctioned off his notebook containing handwritten lyrics from his debut mixtape Room For Improvement. Alongside the lyrics, the Toronto rapper had made a personal checklist of goals:\n\nYou Need: The Greatest Song Ever…Maxwell or D'Angelo Feature, André 3000 Produced Track, Indie Arie Feature, Usher Feature, Voyce Feature.", "annotation_id": "14876828", "url": "https://genius.com/14876828", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Still I rise, Maya Angelou vibes When life comin’ at you from all angles and sides", "vault_lines": ["When life comin' at you from all angles and sides", "Still I rise, Maya Angelou vibes"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to Maya Angelou’s poem “Still I Rise,” which is an empowering story that speaks of keeping hope, even when it seems that everything is hopeless:\n\nYou may trod me in the very dirt\nBut still, like dust, I’ll rise.\n\nDrake mentions his ability to overcome personal struggles and might be alluding to the controversies surrounding him—from his beef with Pusha T, to his blackface scandal, and that he’s “hiding a child.”", "annotation_id": "14876821", "url": "https://genius.com/14876821", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Yeah, soon as this album drop I’m out of the deal", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, soon as this album drop I'm out of the deal"], "meaning": "Drake’s album deal with Cash Money Records, Birdman’s Record Label Company, reportedly stipulated a 6 album deal, with a hefty 2 million dollar advance, when signed first in 2009. After the drop of If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, it seemed that the deal had been extended further.\n\n\n\nScorpion has purposefully been made a 2 disc album to account as 2 albums, which would finish his 9th and 10th albums under Cash Money and make him a free agent by ending his album stipulation.", "annotation_id": "14876580", "url": "https://genius.com/14876580", "state": "accepted", "votes": 166, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Is There More", "line": "Passion, instant Sweat beads, feel me (Feel me) Cupid’s shot me My heartbeat’s racing (My heartbeat’s racing) Tempt me, drive me (Tempt me) It feels so exciting Thought of highly (Highly) It’s yours entirely I’ll be, I’ll be more than a lover More than a woman More than a lover for you", "vault_lines": ["Sweat beads, feel me (Feel me)", "My heartbeat's racing (My heartbeat's racing)", "Tempt me, drive me (Tempt me)", "It feels so exciting", "Thought of highly (Highly)", "I'll be, I'll be more than a lover", "More than a woman", "More than a lover for you"], "meaning": "This is an interpolation of the first verse and part of the chorus from Aaliyah’s 2001 song “More Than a Woman.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pAbxS-6XsMY\n\nDrake has a long history of Aaliyah worship, from interpolating her on Young Money’s 2009 hit “Bedrock,” and sampling a song of hers on “Unforgettable” to namedropping her on the Take Care cut “We’ll Be Fine.” He even penned her a letter on the ninth anniversary of her death, and got her face tattooed on his back in 2011.\n\nHe also has a history with Hiatus Kaiyote frontwoman Nai Palm, who sings this outro—he sampled her song “Building a Ladder” on “Free Smoke,” the opener to his 2017 album More Life. It’s no stretch of the imagination that her ensuing enthusiasm about the homage may have led to this collaboration.", "annotation_id": "14876419", "url": "https://genius.com/14876419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
