{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "Dip your finger in the bag to get your buzz back", "vault_lines": ["Dip your finger in the bag to get your buzz back"], "meaning": "Cocaine is often kept in bags and provides a stimulating experience, sometimes referenced to as a “buzz.”", "annotation_id": "32290414", "url": "https://genius.com/32290414", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "Lost you to the game, I gotta hug that", "vault_lines": ["Lost you to the game, I gotta hold that", "Lost you to the game, I gotta face that", "Lost you to the game, and I see why", "Lost you to the game, I gotta hug that"], "meaning": "Asking myself constantly where the love at?\nWas never enough for you, I wasn’t all that.", "annotation_id": "32217969", "url": "https://genius.com/32217969", "state": "pending", "votes": -7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "[Intro: Dennis Graham]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Dennis Graham is Drake’s father and has released several R&B songs.", "annotation_id": "30672570", "url": "https://genius.com/30672570", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "You wrote me off and then you never wrote back", "vault_lines": ["You wrote me off and then you never wrote back"], "meaning": "This is the original lyric that Seahawks' quarterback Geno Smith used in a Week 1 win during the 2022-2023 NFL season, where he won the league’s Comeback Player of the Year Award.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/6aCWkxBrFjA", "annotation_id": "28658243", "url": "https://genius.com/28658243", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "I’m a fortune teller, listen to what I say", "vault_lines": ["I'm a fortune teller, listen to what I say"], "meaning": "“I’m a fortune teller” could have a few meanings.\n1. Drake is a good predictor of the future, either through intuition or speaking things into existence and making them happen\n2. He could also mean that his words and verses are worth a Fortune, hence “fortune teller”.", "annotation_id": "28257446", "url": "https://genius.com/28257446", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "I was here when you was asking where the love at", "vault_lines": ["I was here when you was asking where the love at"], "meaning": "This is Drizzy’s second use of the phrase “where the love at” on Views, which is repeated in From Florida With Love", "annotation_id": "28257440", "url": "https://genius.com/28257440", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "Like I’m tryna show the waves, this is no cap", "vault_lines": ["Like I'm tryna show the waves, this is no cap"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to not wearing a cap to show off his waves, as a play on words to show that he is not lying – as the phrase ‘no cap’ suggests", "annotation_id": "20703311", "url": "https://genius.com/20703311", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "Spoiled rotten, they could smell you ’fore you walked in", "vault_lines": ["Spoiled rotten, they could smell you 'fore you walked in"], "meaning": "There is a double meaning here – one of a positive connotation and one of a negative connotation.\n\nThe negative one, which is what is more associate with the phrase “spoiled rotten” is that she has a bad entitled attitude and everyone knows her by it when she walks in.\n\nThe positive connotation, which is less easily detectable, is that Drake has spoiled her with wealth, gifts and fame, that people also know her as a result of that. They can even smell the expensive signature fragrances that she wears and know that it is her before she enters a room.", "annotation_id": "20080381", "url": "https://genius.com/20080381", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "I had friends that only had half of they heart in", "vault_lines": ["I had friends that only had half of they heart in"], "meaning": "Firstly, it could mean that these people were not fully engaged or committed to the friendship and had ulterior motives.\n\nSecondly, when one is said to not have a heart, among many things, it means they are horrible people. Having half a heart would just be a step below that.", "annotation_id": "20080346", "url": "https://genius.com/20080346", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "This life is really something you get lost in", "vault_lines": ["This life is really something you get lost in"], "meaning": "As a huge A-list celebrity, Drake lives larger than life and it is easy for someone who is not used to it like the girl he speaks about to get lost in. She gets lost in the fame, the expensive gifts, popularity etc. At the same time, she’s too focused on it that she has lost herself and as a result Drake has lost her.", "annotation_id": "20079967", "url": "https://genius.com/20079967", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "When I visit my last place is the only time I’m in last place See me these days, I never got on a sad face", "vault_lines": ["When I visit my last place is the only time I'm in last place", "See me these days, I never got on a sad face", "See me these days, I"], "meaning": "Drake puts a twist on a line from Mike Stud’s July 2015 song, “These Days”:\n\nOnly time I’m second place is when I’m at my second place", "annotation_id": "19756123", "url": "https://genius.com/19756123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "Like I’m tryna show the waves, this is no cap", "vault_lines": ["Like I'm tryna show the waves, this is no cap"], "meaning": "Drake uses this line as a double entendre explaining that he enjoys showing off his waves instead of keeping a wave-cap on to keep them fresh.\n\n\n\nAn infamous selfie of Drake in a wave-cap (a.k.a. a “du-rag”)\n\nThe term “no cap” simply means someone isn’t lying. Drake then uses the line to say that he is making waves in music and that he isn’t lying about it.\n\nAn example of his trendiness in music is his song “Toosie Slide” that created a dance craze on TikTok, even before the song had released.", "annotation_id": "19719885", "url": "https://genius.com/19719885", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "This is not one of them stories that got three sides This is not one of them stories you could rewind", "vault_lines": ["This is not one of them stories that got three sides", "This is not one of them stories you could rewind"], "meaning": "Drake could be dropping a nod to two storytelling songs—Joe Budden’s December 2005 song, “Three Sides To A Story” and Nas‘ December 2001 song, “Rewind.”\n\nNas listed Drake as one of his top five current rappers in 2016 while Budden and Drake have had a long history of feuding before squashing their beef on Budden’s Instagram Live in April 2020.", "annotation_id": "19719252", "url": "https://genius.com/19719252", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "I did it by being myself with no dramatic acting I couldn’t sit around and wait, I had to have it happen", "vault_lines": ["I did it by being myself with no dramatic acting", "I couldn't sit around and wait, I had to have it happen"], "meaning": "Before finding fame as a rapper, Drake acted in the  Canadian television show, Degrassi: The Next Generation where he played Jimmy Brooks, a high school basketball star who’s wheelchair-bound after he was shot by another student. This ties in with the “couldn’t sit around” line.\n\n\n\nIn a 2013 interview with CBC News, Drake spoke about how he doesn’t have to put on a persona, splitting between his public and private life. He said:\n\nI get to wake up every day, and I don’t have to think about anything, I just get to go and be myself. I don’t have to remember to do this, or dress like this, or put on this accent. I just get to wake up and be myself.\n\nIn the same interview, when asked about why he chose a career in music over acting he said:\n\nI knew right away, and it had a lot to do with the process of acting. It’s just so dependent on so many other things other than yourself. It actually takes a long time to get on set and do what you love.", "annotation_id": "19717263", "url": "https://genius.com/19717263", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "I never thought I’d see awards in a glass case I could’ve ended up in your crib with a masked face", "vault_lines": ["I never thought I'd see awards in a glass case", "I could've ended up in your crib with a masked face"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake is talking about the display of his various awards in his Toronto mansion, which was first seen in the music video for his April 2020 hit, “Toosie Slide”:\n\n\n\nContinuing this, Drake then claims how his life could have played out entirely different, even to where he may have been a masked man breaking into homes.\n\nThis could also be a double entendre for how he and his woman could have quarantined together, as this song was released during the COVID-19 pandemic, which forced citizens around the world to stay at home to protect themselves from the virus.\n\nAlso, Drake was pictured in a ski mask in the artwork and music video for “Toosie Slide” and the artwork for Dark Lane Demo Tapes.", "annotation_id": "19717031", "url": "https://genius.com/19717031", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "Hey, Instagram This song I’ve been writing, oh, for a few months I don’t know, I, I started writing it about family Just came up now, so Anybody feel any way about it, I don’t give a fuck", "vault_lines": ["This song I've been writing, oh, for a few months", "I don't know, I, I started writing it about family", "I don't see it", "Just came up now, so", "Anybody feel any way about it, I don't give a fuck"], "meaning": "The intro, interlude, and outro on the track are taken from Drake’s Instagram Live session with his father, Dennis Graham, that occurred on March 21, 2020, amidst the outbreak of COVID-19 and international quarantine efforts used to deter its spread.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v5M3CGBG9Lw On the call, the two talked, listened to music, and shared a shot of Drake’s Virginia Black Whiskey.", "annotation_id": "19716910", "url": "https://genius.com/19716910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "We started this from the bottom, now you alone there", "vault_lines": ["We started this from the bottom, now you alone there"], "meaning": "Drake namedrops one of his biggest hits, February 2013’s “Started from the Bottom.” The song’s premise is Drake’s rise to fame from nothing, and this line shows how he remembers those he left behind on his journey to stardom.", "annotation_id": "19716907", "url": "https://genius.com/19716907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "I do it for the Grahams, not the ’Gram I do it for the fam, but still fuckin’ Uncle Sam", "vault_lines": ["I do it for the Grahams, not the 'Gram", "I do it for the fam, but still fuckin' Uncle Sam"], "meaning": "Drake uses a double entendre here to proclaim the two things that motivate him: the “Grahams” refers to his family, as Drake’s birth name is Aubrey Drake Graham, but the “grams” also refers to money. Drake then plays off the famous term, “do it for the ‘Gram,” an abbreviation of Instagram, but he’s emphasizing that he doesn’t do things for social media like many of his rap contemporaries.\n\n‘Gram could also refer to the four Grammy Awards that Drake has won as of this song’s release, but it’s used as a way to minimize their importance to him. Drizzy has repeatedly spoken about not caring for the Grammys and even abstained from attending the ceremony for many years. When he won his fourth Grammy in 2019 for Best Rap Song, he still subtly dissed the award in his acceptance speech.\n\nSecondly, Drake raps about Uncle Sam (U.S.), which is a personification of the United States. Drake is a dual citizen of the U.S. and Canada, so he is legally obligated to pay income taxes to the U.S. federal government. Taxes are commonly associated with Uncle Sam; however, on his and Future’s January 2020 single, “Life Is Good,” Drake admitted that he hadn’t paid his taxes:\n\nHaven’t done my taxes, I’m too turnt up", "annotation_id": "19716798", "url": "https://genius.com/19716798", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Losses", "line": "See me these days, I Lost you to the game, and I see why It was always you and I without the T-Y", "vault_lines": ["Lost you to the game, I gotta hold that", "I don't see it", "Lost you to the game, I gotta face that", "See me these days, I", "Lost you to the game, and I see why", "It was always you and I without the T-Y", "Lost you to the game, I gotta hug that"], "meaning": "“U.N.I.T.Y.” is Queen Latifah’s late 1993 hit single. Drake flips the song’s famous chorus to reflect on how his relationship with a girl became broken.\n\nU-N-I-T-Y, U-N-I-T-Y, that’s a unity\n\nThis is a triple entendre. “U-N-I without the T-Y” alludes to a lack of unity in their relationship. Also, “T-Y” is a common abbreviation of “thank you,” so Drake seems to be saying that the woman did not show gratitude for the things that he did for her.", "annotation_id": "19716718", "url": "https://genius.com/19716718", "state": "accepted", "votes": 193, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
