{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "Ohhh, ohhh", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "PND sample or backing vocals?", "annotation_id": "19705625", "url": "https://genius.com/19705625", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "Yeah, remember you was livin’ at The London for a month Service elevator up to 4201 We was still a secret, couldn’t come in through the front", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, remember you was livin' at The London for a month", "Service elevator up to 4201", "We was still a secret, couldn't come in through the front"], "meaning": "The London is a luxury hotel in New York City. Drake has spent time in the city before, even has titled a song about it, so it’s likely that he has stayed there for a night or two.\n\nDrake might take the service elevator, opposed to the main elevator, up to his girl’s floor to avoid being seen. The London has 54 floors so 4201 is likely referring to a room 01 on the 42nd floor.\n\n\n\nAround 2014 Drake was rumoured to be in a relationship with Rihanna. It’s possible he’s reminiscing about the times they had together.\n\nThe lyrics have been slightly altered from the leaked version.", "annotation_id": "9072951", "url": "https://genius.com/9072951", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "And on my way to make this dough A lot of niggas cut the check so they can take this flow A lot of niggas cut the check so they can take this flow", "vault_lines": ["And on my way to make this dough", "A lot of niggas cut the check so they can take this flow"], "meaning": "Of the entire song, Drake chooses to put his greatest emphasis on these lines indicating their importance.\n\nA 6ix God feature costs a lot. In fact, it’s rumoured to be upwards of $200,000.\n\nHe’s stated how he’s “done doing favours for people, cause it ain’t like I need the money I make off a feature,” and he’s certainly taking it to heart. In 2015 and 2016, the only time you’d see Drake on a feature was for a friend, like for Future, Rihanna and Big Sean.\n\nAdditionally, cutting a check to take someone’s flow refers to ghostwriting. Drake has ghostwritten for Dr. Dre, Diddy, and Alicia Keys, among others. This is likely a response to the ghostwriting allegations, saying he in fact does write his own stuff, and even writes for other people.\n\nLate last year there was a leak of this same song where the song cuts off at the 3rd verse. The snippet was named “Views from the 6” and started off the same way this song starts off. Drake knew about the leak so he changed it. “Take the flow” could be referring to this incident, and possible a reason he changed the song around.", "annotation_id": "9070860", "url": "https://genius.com/9070860", "state": "accepted", "votes": 286, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "I wanna know how much time you spent on them paragraphs Where you get at me All that grey in our conversation history, you Playin’ mind games, when you sayin’ things Playin’ mime games, we both doin’ the same thing", "vault_lines": ["I wanna know how much time you spent on them paragraphs", "Where you get at me", "All that grey in our conversation history, you", "Playin' mind games, when you sayin' things", "Playin' mime games, we both doin' the same thing", "Do things in our off time"], "meaning": "In the iPhone’s iMessage app, grey text bubbles display the messages you’ve received. This girl is clearly bombarding Drake with well-crafted messages to play “mind games” with him but isn’t getting many responses in return, hence all of the grey on Drake’s screen. “Grey in our conversation” also refers to the state of their relationship. It’s not black & white (straightforward), it’s a grey area (uncertain).\n\nThe “mime games” line is a pun. Drake refers to the mime’s trick of imitating exactly what the other subject does. They’re both playing mind games with each other.", "annotation_id": "9070842", "url": "https://genius.com/9070842", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "That’s for sure though, I made a career off reminiscin’ Time got a funny way of tickin’, things are so much different I’ll admit it, I’ve admitted to a lot of things", "vault_lines": ["You got a different vision", "That's for sure though, I made a career off reminiscin'", "Time got a funny way of tickin', things are so much different", "I'll admit it, I've admitted to a lot of things"], "meaning": "In his albums Take Care and Nothing Was The Same, and especially in songs like “Look What You’ve Done,” “From Time,” and “Furthest Thing,” Drake reminisces on past relationships and experiences.\n\nHis constant honesty throughout his career has made him admit various things and earned him a reputation of being soft. Among the things Drake has admitted, is his love for labelmate Nicki Minaj. As he said on his song “Miss Me”:\n\nI love Nicki Minaj, I told her I’d admit it\nI hope one day we get married just to say we fucking did it\nAnd girl I’m fucking serious, I’m with it if you with it\n\nNow Drake’s moved on to reminiscing about reminiscing.\n\nThis line is similar to the one he delivers in “6 God” where he constantly repeats:\n\nI’ll admit it, I’ll admit it\n\nDrake’s now become completely self aware to be able to admit all of his issues.", "annotation_id": "9070839", "url": "https://genius.com/9070839", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "LOLOL, I’m glad you find this shit amusin’", "vault_lines": ["LOLOL, I'm glad you find this shit amusin'"], "meaning": "“Lol” is a common phrase used to express laughter. It seems the girl Drake’s sending DM’s to isn’t taking him seriously. Drizzy sarcastically states that he’s glad she’s finding his efforts to rekindle their love amusing.\n\nThe way Drake delivers “LOLOL” also sounds like “Lol, oh well.”", "annotation_id": "9070821", "url": "https://genius.com/9070821", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "And my house is the definition Of alcohol and weed addiction", "vault_lines": ["And my house is the definition", "Of alcohol and weed addiction"], "meaning": "Drake’s house is never without marijuana or alcohol. This becomes a double entendre as they could be at his house to entertain his guests or help Drake deal with his inner issues. As he said in “You & The 6”:\n\nNever get sloppy drunk, but alcohol is problem solving", "annotation_id": "9070748", "url": "https://genius.com/9070748", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "Act like you know it, fuck them stories, fuck the shade they throwin’ Understand I got responsibilities to people that I need", "vault_lines": ["Act like you know it, fuck them stories, fuck the shade they throwin'", "Understand I got responsibilities to people that I need"], "meaning": "Drake’s telling this girl to not believe in the false allegations they say about him.\n\nHe faced a lot of controversy after Meek Mill accused him of not writing his own lyrics. After all the beef served in 2015, Drake was considered the winner. Drake’s girl doesn’t need to worry because it doesn’t matter what they say or what they do, nobody is on Drake’s level.", "annotation_id": "9070724", "url": "https://genius.com/9070724", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "We both slide on a late night Do things in our off time We both, yeah Made some mistakes, pon road Yeah, how’s that for real?", "vault_lines": ["Slide on a late night", "You like to slide on a late night", "We both slide on a late night", "Do things in our off time", "Made some mistakes, pon road", "Yeah, how's that for real?"], "meaning": "Drake admits that both he and this girl “slide” a.k.a pursue other people when they’re not seeing each other, or in their “off time.” Drake even admits this may have been a mistake.\n\n“Pon” means “on” in Patois, meaning they both made mistakes on the road.", "annotation_id": "9070397", "url": "https://genius.com/9070397", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "I tell ’em they belong to me, that goes on for forever And I think we just get closer when we not together", "vault_lines": ["I tell 'em they belong to me, that goes on for forever", "And I think we just get closer when we not together"], "meaning": "Playing on the saying “distance makes the heart grow fonder,” Drake is more comfortable with his exes when they’re distanced from him—Drizzy’s always felt possessive of all his past significant others.\n\nSince he’s attached to these girls, he tells them that it’s better to keep distance given that he’d mess up and lose them again if they got back together.", "annotation_id": "9070338", "url": "https://genius.com/9070338", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "Tryna give your ass the world You runnin’ your fingers through my curls You knew me when the kid had waves But that’s enough of that You could never say I came up and forgot about your ass And that’s some real shit", "vault_lines": ["That's that shit that I don't like", "Tryna give your ass the world", "You runnin' your fingers through my curls", "You knew me when the kid had waves", "But that's enough of that", "You could never say I came up and forgot about your ass", "And that's some real shit"], "meaning": "Drake shows his frustration with the girl he’s addressing. It seems like she’s blind to what he’s done for her. He’s always been giving her all she wants but he’s never had anything in return.\n\nShe seems to have known Drizzy for a while now since he mentions the waves he had on the cover of his 2007 mixtape Comeback Season, all the way to the hairstyle of curls he had between 2012 and 2013.\n\n\n\nDrake’s had enough of all the friendly flirting and wants a serious relationship. And he might have a real shot since even after all the success and fame he has received, he still hasn’t forgotten about her.\n\nThese lines were originally written by PARTYNEXTDOOR with intentions of appearing on Drake’s “Legend,” as heard on PND’s “Legend (Demo).”", "annotation_id": "9070282", "url": "https://genius.com/9070282", "state": "accepted", "votes": 162, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "Cuts too deep for a band-aid solution We too deep in this thing, you’ll never lose me", "vault_lines": ["Cuts too deep for a band-aid solution", "We too deep in this thing, you'll never lose me"], "meaning": "Playing on “deep,” Drake describes the magnitude of emotions he’s feeling.\n\nHis pain is heartfelt and stinging and can’t simply be healed by a band-aid. Yet, he feels too invested in the relationship to leave now. It’s a push and pull in the dance of lovers.\n\nCuts was an American sitcom that featured Marques Houston, mentioned in the previous line.", "annotation_id": "9070263", "url": "https://genius.com/9070263", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "You got a different vision You wanna walk around naked in the kitchen Without runnin’ into one of my niggas That’s not the way we livin’ Too much goin’ on, it’s just not realistic", "vault_lines": ["You got a different vision", "You wanna walk around naked in the kitchen", "Without runnin' into one of my niggas", "That's not the way we livin'", "Too much goin' on, it's just not realistic"], "meaning": "As we found out in the promo clip of Drake receiving his Grammy for Take Care, Drake’s YOLO Estate has enough rooms to accommodate all his entourage, so he does in fact keep his family close.\n\nThis girl wants an intimate relationship with Drake, to the point where the two of them can live alone together and she can be as comfortable as she likes. However, this isn’t possible because of Drake’s lifestyle. This portrays the tension between his music life and romantic life.\n\nDrake rapped something similar on his single “Trophies”:\n\n“She like ‘I heard all your niggas stay where you stay’ House so big I haven’t seen them boys in 2 days”\n\nDrizzy is also alluding to a statement by NY Giants receiver Odell Beckham, a close friend and house guest of Drake’s during the album creation:\n\n“He needs to chill out with that house he’s got out there, it’s just too much going on”", "annotation_id": "9070137", "url": "https://genius.com/9070137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "It’s like a lot of games bein’ played How’s it goin’ down? If it’s on ’til we gone, then I gots to know now Is you wit’ me or what? Yeah It’s like a lot of games are bein’ played How’s it goin’ down? If it’s on ’til we gone, then I gots to know now Is you wit’ me or what? Yeah", "vault_lines": ["It's like a lot of games bein' played", "How's it goin' down?", "If it's on 'til we gone, then I gots to know now", "Is you wit' me or what?", "It's like a lot of games are bein' played", "You like to slide on a late night", "What type of games are bein' played?"], "meaning": "This hook is taken directly from DMX’s hook on “How’s It Goin' Down” and it’s delivered in a similar flow. “How’s it Goin' Down” is also about an affair, so Drake may be alluding to some type of infidelity by the girl.\n\nDrake and his girl are having serious trust issues—he doesn’t see how their relationship is progressing and is doubtful his girl is as committed to the relationship as she should be. He needs to know if his girl will ride for him or if they should end the relationship without wasting anymore of each other’s time.\n\nDrake’s unsure where his relationship is going, he is falling out of touch with the girl and believes that their love is fading.\n\nLike he says in his song “Jungle”:\n\nAre you down? Are you down? Are you down?\nAre you down for the cause?\nAre you down? Are you down? Are you down?\nAre you down for the cause?\nYou still down? You still down? You still down?", "annotation_id": "9070090", "url": "https://genius.com/9070090", "state": "accepted", "votes": 200, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "[Verse 3: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Using the same format as “Summer Sixteen” and “0 to 100 / The Catch Up,” Noah “40” Shebib switches the beat once again.\n\nThe lyrics in verse 3 are eerily similar to those that were leaked on August of 2014, originally thought to be for a song titled “Views From the 6.” While there are distinct differences in the lyrics, the flow and subject matter is very similar.", "annotation_id": "9070083", "url": "https://genius.com/9070083", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "Slide on a late night You like to slide on a late night You sent a “Are you here?” text without an invite That’s that shit that I don’t like", "vault_lines": ["Slide on a late night", "You like to slide on a late night", "You sent a \"Are you here?\" text without an invite", "That's that shit that I don't like", "We both slide on a late night"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Chief Keef’s song “I Don’t Like.”\n\n\n\nDrake plays with the texting theme in the song, while pointing out that this woman only likes to meet with him after hours. She’s sliding into his messages late at night in hopes of sliding into his bed.", "annotation_id": "9070030", "url": "https://genius.com/9070030", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "Girl, I had your back when all you used to do was front", "vault_lines": ["Girl, I had your back when all you used to do was front"], "meaning": "Drake plays on “front” and “back” to explain how he remained completely loyal and faithful to his girl; however, she only played games and maintained a dishonest relationship.\n\nHe also plays off “couldn’t come in through the front” from the previous line.", "annotation_id": "9070027", "url": "https://genius.com/9070027", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "I know you heard that my girl is sponsored by Audemars That’s why she always correctin’ me when my time is off", "vault_lines": ["I know you heard that my girl is sponsored by Audemars", "That's why she always correctin' me when my time is off"], "meaning": "This is an implicit reference to the rumors about Drake and Serena Williams, who is sponsored by Audemars Piguet, a manufacturer of luxury watches.\n\n\n\nDrake plays with Serena’s sponsorship, saying that she’s always correcting him when his timing is off. We’re sure he does the same when her timing is off on the court.\n\nDrake also mentioned Serena in his 2013 hit “Worst Behavior”:\n\nI’m with my whole set, tennis matches at the crib\nI swear I could beat Serena when she playin' with her left\n\nDrizzy could be innocently talking about playing tennis with the legend, or “tennis match” could be a euphemism for the sexy-time that also happens “at [his] crib.”", "annotation_id": "9070022", "url": "https://genius.com/9070022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "On some DMX shit I group DM my exes", "vault_lines": ["On some DMX shit", "I group DM my exes"], "meaning": "Aubrey shouts out the collaborator whom he has sampled twice on VIEWS. The line, paired with DMX’s, may be a  reference to J. Cole’s “She Knows”:\n\nWhat these bitches want from a nigga?\nOn some DMX shit.\n\nNote the pairing of “DMX” and “DM my eXes.”\n\nMore over Drizzy is group DM-ing his exes can be an explanation of how busy he is with life and not finding the time to text each one individually.\n\nAlso, by grouping them he is sending them all the same message “I tell ‘em they belong to me”, which indicates he does not prefer any of them over the other and he loved them all the same way and equally.\n\nAn interesting choice for a name drop and sample subject in light of DMX showing his dislike for Drake on The Breakfast Club.\n\nIn 2016, DMX said he approves of Drake’s shout-out.", "annotation_id": "9070017", "url": "https://genius.com/9070017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 229, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "What these bitches want from a nigga?", "vault_lines": ["What these bitches want from a nigga?"], "meaning": "This line is a sample of the late rapper DMX’s song “What These Bitches Want,” from DMX’s 1999 album, …And Then There Was X.\n\nIn 2012, DMX called out Drake on a Power 105 interview, saying:\n\nI don’t like anything about Drake. I don’t like his fucking voice. I don’t like what he talks about. I don’t like his face. I don’t like the way he walks. Nothing…\n\nThe sample clearance and use may indicate that the beef between the two rappers has faded, and that Drake is giving props to another one of the hip hop legends. N.O.R.E. claimed he’s the reason Drake got in touch with DMX.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/noreaga/status/725929485841747969", "annotation_id": "9070015", "url": "https://genius.com/9070015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 218, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "Heard a lot about me ’fore we started off I know you heard that my pool parties like Mardi Gras", "vault_lines": ["Heard a lot about me 'fore we started off", "I know you heard that my pool parties like Mardi Gras"], "meaning": "Mardi Gras, also known as Fat Tuesday, is the day before the Christian holiday Ash Wednesday which signifies the begin of the Season of Lent. In Lent, Christians repent, and in doing so, usually cut down on partying, junk-food, etc. For this reason, Mardi Gras is one of the biggest days of partying of the year.\n\n\n\nDrake’s pool parties have always been the talk of the town. He hosted a foam pool party on Summer 2015 and the likes of Kanye West, The Game, and even Amber Rose were in attendance.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/37XCFVDQP2/", "annotation_id": "9069991", "url": "https://genius.com/9069991", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "3 dots, you thinkin’ of a reaction still While you’re typin’, make sure you tell me", "vault_lines": ["3 dots, you thinkin' of a reaction still", "While you're typin', make sure you tell me"], "meaning": "When typing an iMessage on an iPhone, three gray dots enclosed within an oval appear in the bottom corner of the conversation log, signifying that the other person is typing a message.\n\nDrake’s girl is taking too long to decide how to react, perhaps revealing a deceitful side to her.", "annotation_id": "9069984", "url": "https://genius.com/9069984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 140, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "You tell me that I’m confusin’ More immature than Marques Houston", "vault_lines": ["You tell me that I'm confusin'", "More immature than Marques Houston"], "meaning": "One of Drake’s ex’s compares his immaturity to Marques Houston, founding member of the R&B group “Immature,” later known as IMX.  Marques also played Roger in the popular 90’s show Sister, Sister.  Roger was always immature, as he constantly attempted to make either Tia & Tamara (twins), his girlfriend.\n\nThis girl could be alluding to Drake’s immaturity in the sense of him not being committed the way Roger was.  In the season four episode “When a Man Loves Two Women” in particular, he asks out both Tia and Tamera, along with several other girls.", "annotation_id": "9069973", "url": "https://genius.com/9069973", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "line": "You toyin’ with it like Happy Meal", "vault_lines": ["You toyin' with it like Happy Meal"], "meaning": "McDonald’s Happy Meal’s contain small, inexpensive toys for kids to play with. This girl is playing with Drake’s heart as if it were a cheap toy.\n\nIt’s clear that Drizzy doesn’t like being taken advantage of in the relationship, and he wants her to take him more seriously.", "annotation_id": "9069962", "url": "https://genius.com/9069962", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "U With Me?", "status": "done", "targets": 25, "clicked": 25, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-u-with-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "U With Me?"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, you feel the pressure, man, I know the pressure"], "meaning": "Yeah", "annotation_id": "41763702", "url": "https://genius.com/41763702", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "And I never bend my morals for the ticket sales Tippin’ scales, bars heavy like triple XL", "vault_lines": ["And I never bend my morals for the ticket sales", "Tippin' scales, bars heavy like triple XL"], "meaning": "Drake’s incredible loyalty to his friends is played out regularly on wax, notably earlier in this song. His dedication to his craft and his art hasn’t been queried, preferring to focus on his own personal growth and development rather than chase trends or attempt to get all his music played in strip clubs. His ticket sales haven’t suffered.\n\nThis seems to imply he could have focused more on his singing, catering to the huge fanbase that see’s him as a hybrid of rap and R&B. “Triple XL” means huge, and heavy bars imply he’s dropping lyrical gems on a regular basis. At least one artist refutes Drake’s lyrical ability, but his quotables speak for themselves. He’s got bars.", "annotation_id": "9081835", "url": "https://genius.com/9081835", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "My niggas still hit the club when it’s 20 below", "vault_lines": ["My niggas still hit the club when it's 20 below"], "meaning": "This section of the record correlates with winter, as outlined by Drake during his Zane Lowe interview:\n\nYou start to value your days a lot more when most people won’t go outside type of thing…It creates a different person. I thought it was important to make the album here during the winter.\n\nIn the promo shots for the record, Drake was certainly kitted out for a club-hop in the middle of winter.", "annotation_id": "9081760", "url": "https://genius.com/9081760", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Thoughts too deep to go work ’em out with a therapist", "vault_lines": ["Thoughts too deep to go work 'em out with a therapist"], "meaning": "Drake’s feelings are too deep and personal to divulge to a therapist—his therapy is the music.\n\nAlthough some criticise Drake for being simple, some of his lyrics are incredibly insightful.\n\n\nI live for the nights that I can’t remember\nWith the people that I won’t forget — “Show Me a Good Time”\n\nBetter late than never but never late is better —  “Fancy”\n\nTook a while, got the jokers out of the deck now / I’m holdin' all the cards and n—s wanna play chess now — “Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2”", "annotation_id": "9081722", "url": "https://genius.com/9081722", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Yeah, you feel the pressure, man, I know the pressure", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, you feel the pressure, man, I know the pressure"], "meaning": "A lot of the pressure surrounding this record was self-induced. Drake announced the project in July 2014, in a Billboard article, 644 days before its eventual release. While in that time he released two critically and commercially successful records: If You’re Reading  This It’s Too Late, and What a Time To Be Alive with Future, rival artists like Kendrick Lamar and Kanye West were releasing their albums to critical acclaim.\n\nThis is a far cry from the hook on “Used To,” in which he says:\n\nI ain’t felt the pressure in a little while\nIt’s gonna take some getting used to\n\nWith name checks as one of the best artists in the world from alumni like Kanye West and Jay Z, the pressure on Drake to deliver another stunning project is immense.", "annotation_id": "9081534", "url": "https://genius.com/9081534", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I’m possessed, you can see it under the contacts", "vault_lines": ["I'm possessed, you can see it under the contacts"], "meaning": "In a literal (but fictional) sense, when one is possessed, their eyes typically change in appearance. Drake’s worn glasses before and who’s to say he doesn’t wear contact lenses?\n\nIn a different sense of being possessed, Drake may be alluding to his current label situation, with news outlets speculating on whether Views is his final album on the label, or whether it’s been released purely under Young Money Entertainment. iTunes lists the album: “℗ 2016 Young Money Entertainment/Cash Money Records”", "annotation_id": "9075163", "url": "https://genius.com/9075163", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "My exes made some of my favorite music I dated women from my favorite movies Karma’s such a thing of beauty", "vault_lines": ["My exes made some of my favorite music", "I dated women from my favorite movies", "Karma's such a thing of beauty"], "meaning": "Drake isn’t just famous for his music, his dating history has been tabloid fodder too. His exes that “made some of his favorite music,” would include Rihanna, Rita Ora, and possibly Nicki Minaj; the ones from the movies would include Tyra Banks and Zoe Kravitz. The full list of Drake’s ex-girlfriends can be found here.\n\n\n\nHe may also be referring to the topics in his music. He uses his exes regularly for material when making music.\n\nThe “karma” refers to the way Drake has capitalised on all of the experiences these relationships have brought to create his art. Many of his songs speak about ex-girlfriends.\n\nThis is also a flip on the phrase “karma’s a bitch.”", "annotation_id": "9075151", "url": "https://genius.com/9075151", "state": "pending", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I worked at Jaydees Connections whenever Jason let me", "vault_lines": ["I worked at Jaydees Connections whenever Jason let me"], "meaning": "Jaydees Connections is a former clothing store on Eglinton Avenue, West York, Toronto.\n\nAlthough he’s been uncovered as a Popeye’s alumnus, his work at this establishment hasn’t been referenced before, however, he did mention in “Know Yourself” that he used to sell jeans.\n\nToronto Life lists the manager/owner of the business as Jason Hamilton.", "annotation_id": "9073445", "url": "https://genius.com/9073445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Kiddie’s Caribana, tryin’ not to catch a stray", "vault_lines": ["Kiddie's Caribana, tryin' not to catch a stray"], "meaning": "Caribana is an annual festival held in Toronto— Kiddie’s Caribana being the version for youngsters.\n\nCaribana has been under scrutiny in recent years due to gun violence. Drake contrasts the innocence and happiness associated with Kiddie’s Caribana with the fact that he still has to be on the lookout for stray bullets.\n\nDrake’s OVO Festival, is now billed as the “biggest event” at Caribana.", "annotation_id": "9073072", "url": "https://genius.com/9073072", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I might take a breather but I won’t ever leave you", "vault_lines": ["I might take a breather but I won't ever leave you"], "meaning": "“Taking a breather” is another way of saying taking a break. A reference back to the last line where he said, “I’m running a fever”.\n\nNotice how “running” and “breather” have a comparison to a person jogging taking a break because they’re fatigued. Drake may take some time out for himself, but he’ll always come back with new music.", "annotation_id": "9072999", "url": "https://genius.com/9072999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Ceesay’s, I was buyin’ fitteds everyday", "vault_lines": ["Ceesay’s, I was buyin' fitteds everyday"], "meaning": "Ceesay’s, short for Ceesay’s M&M Military Sporting Goods, is a local sporting goods store in Toronto, Canada.  This is the store where pre-famous Drake would purchase his fitted hats.", "annotation_id": "9072582", "url": "https://genius.com/9072582", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Lately I just feel so out of character The paranoia can start to turn into arrogance", "vault_lines": ["Lately I just feel so out of character", "The paranoia can start to turn into arrogance"], "meaning": "When you get rich, people around you change. On So Far Gone track “Say What’s Real; ” Drake says:\n\nWhy do I feel so alone?\nLike everybody passing through the studio\nIs in character as if we acting out a movie role\nTalking bullshit as if it was for you to know\n\nDuring “Wu-Tang Forever” he expressed his paranoia, explaining why he always rode with his crew. This paranoia leeks into the theme of the record, the changing of the seasons in Toronto. In his Zane Lowe interview he says:\n\nYou start to value your days a lot more when most people won’t go outside type of thing…It creates a different person. I thought it was important to make the album here during the winter.\n\nWhen you’re surrounded by your crew, hyping everything you do up, it’s almost impossible to avoid arrogance. Although for Drake to claim arrogance is out of character isn’t strictly true, even on his first album he was talking up his own abilities.", "annotation_id": "9071702", "url": "https://genius.com/9071702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "And like it’s goin’ in a trunk, I put it all behind us Where you tryna go? I got it, I’ll take us wherever", "vault_lines": ["And like it's goin' in a trunk, I put it all behind us", "Where you tryna go? I got it, I'll take us wherever"], "meaning": "The trunk is located at the rear of the car (usually). Putting their family debt behind them, with a net worth of $78 million, is now achievable.\n\nHis dedication to his mother can be seen all over his records. In March 2015 he was snapped taking her out to lunch, and she can be seen court-side with him on occasion.", "annotation_id": "9071652", "url": "https://genius.com/9071652", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "You could throw curve balls but I got the glove fitted They’ve been sayin’ it’s love, but it isn’t love, is it? Toast to the days when they wasn’t out to get me", "vault_lines": ["You could throw curve balls but I got the glove fitted", "They've been sayin' it's love, but it isn't love, is it?", "Toast to the days when they wasn't out to get me"], "meaning": "Drake addresses his rivals, comparing them to baseball pitches and himself a catcher. Rather than firing back, Drizzy catches their disses with ease, leaving him unscathed. This is another entry into his long list of sporting references.\n\n\n\nThis is also a possible shot at Tory Lanez, who sent out an ill-advised tweet saying that “the 6” wasn’t cool. Drake seemed to throw the shots right back on “Summer Sixteen,” referencing Lanez mixtape and song titles.\n\nIn February 2016, Lanez said “I’d never diss him, I’m a fan.” Despite this, Drake used his Zane Lowe interview to encourage the rapper to make good music and let it speak for himself:\n\nAsk me about anybody [in Toronto], I’m biggin’ ’em up. It’s our city, man. Why would you get up on a public platform and start being like ‘aaaaahh!’ Nah, man. And then what – just to come back and start backpedalling and begging it out?", "annotation_id": "9071615", "url": "https://genius.com/9071615", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "You don’t worry ‘bout fittin’ in when you custom made", "vault_lines": ["You don't worry 'bout fittin' in when you custom made"], "meaning": "Most of this track deals with Drake’s desire to be seen as separate from much of the rap game.\n\nI get a blank page when I try to draw a comparison\n\nFrom very early on in his career Drake didn’t fit in to the typical hip-hop culture as a half-Jewish Canadian who sang his own hooks, spilled his feelings on the tracks, and donned expensive sweaters. His style hasn’t always been embraced by the culture, but like his clothing, he’s a one of one.", "annotation_id": "9071528", "url": "https://genius.com/9071528", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Who you think runnin’ this show? You saw it in me at 20 years old", "vault_lines": ["Who you think runnin' this show?", "You saw it in me at 20 years old"], "meaning": "Drake was actually younger than 20 when he got his first break on the big screen. As an actor on Degrassi, Drake was just 15 years old.\n\nBy the time he was 20, he’d dropped the critically acclaimed Comeback Season, with track “Replacement Girl” becoming BET’s “New Joint of the Day,” a first for an unsigned Canadian artist.\n\nThis helped springboard his career.", "annotation_id": "9071512", "url": "https://genius.com/9071512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Runnin’ through the 6, thumbin’ through the contracts", "vault_lines": ["Runnin' through the 6, thumbin' through the contracts"], "meaning": "A callback to a meme line from “Know Yourself”:\n\nRunnin' through the six with my woes\nCountin' money you know how it goes\n\nThis line shows that he still doing both. In fact, he’s counting even more money, thanks to big endorsement deals from Nike and Sprite and a $19 million contract from Apple Music.", "annotation_id": "9071383", "url": "https://genius.com/9071383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I still got somethin’ left to prove since you left me room Payment plan for the family debt, we in the minus", "vault_lines": ["I still got somethin' left to prove since you left me room", "Payment plan for the family debt, we in the minus"], "meaning": "A throwback reference to his debut mixtape Room for Improvement, Drake preaches the message “always strive to better yourself,” and since his place in the game isn’t comparable, he’s focused on making it count.\n\nDuring “Star67” he detailed the problem:\n\nI was on TV makin' fifty racks a year\nAfter helpin' mama out the shit would disappear\n\nToronto, being an expensive city, only added to their money woes, and with his mom’s illness he may very well have been in debt.\n\n“We in the minus” means he isn’t currently in debt.", "annotation_id": "9071236", "url": "https://genius.com/9071236", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "They wanna pree the movements, gotta start to make ’em sooner", "vault_lines": ["They wanna pree the movements, gotta start to make 'em sooner"], "meaning": "“Pree” is Jamaican slang for “watch” or to take notice of something. In English, “pre” also means “before,” which may relate to Meek Mill’s track “War Pain,” in which Meek knew ahead of time some of the lyrics that would appear on Drake’s “Summer Sixteen.”", "annotation_id": "9071220", "url": "https://genius.com/9071220", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I got a pure soul, I don’t do the hate", "vault_lines": ["I got a pure soul, I don't do the hate"], "meaning": "Drake may have gone from ignoring disses sent his way to sending meme-worthy, Grammy nominated  disses himself, but he still maintains that business is strictly business and any shots he sends are nothing personal.", "annotation_id": "9071157", "url": "https://genius.com/9071157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "The lingo start to sound like we talkin’ in code", "vault_lines": ["The lingo start to sound like we talkin' in code"], "meaning": "Drake’s lingo mainly incorporates his record label, OVO, the label he is signed to, YMCMB, and “The 6”, his hometown. Some fans may not understand his new lingo, hence “talking in code.”\n\nThe OVO owl is also part of the Egyptian code of hieroglyphics. This adds to the “code talk”.", "annotation_id": "9071114", "url": "https://genius.com/9071114", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Numbers goin’ unlisted just to create some distance I might see you on and off but I’ll never switch ya", "vault_lines": ["Numbers goin' unlisted just to create some distance", "I might see you on and off but I'll never switch ya"], "meaning": "It’s no surprise Drake has an unlisted number. This way he can avoid the various enemies he’s mentioned throughout this song. His circle is tight, and recording this record in Toronto allowed him the distance to create without frenemies blowing up his phone.\n\nHe plays on the two settings a traditional light switch has (off and on) to re-affirm his dedication to those he truly deems friends. His sabbatical in Toronto may create the allusion that they’re not associated, but Drake wouldn’t trade the friendship of these people for anything.\n\nThis also refers to the math based wordplay from earlier with “20” “20” “40”. The number are not mentioned directly after each other thus they are “unlisted” and there is “distance” between them in both utterance and context.", "annotation_id": "9071100", "url": "https://genius.com/9071100", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "They think I had the silver spoon but they’ll get it soon", "vault_lines": ["They think I had the silver spoon but they'll get it soon"], "meaning": "After “Started From The Bottom” was released, a number of people cried foul that Drake didn’t in fact “start from the bottom.” Drake said of the record:\n\nI feel sometimes that people don’t have enough information about my beginnings and therefore they make up a life story for me that isn’t consistent with actual events. I did not buy my way into this spot and it was the furthest thing from easy to achieve. I am proud of every part of my past and I’m excited for this song to find a place in your life as well\n\nDuring an Uncut cover story, Drake explained that his mother was incredibly sick, and the money he was making from his TV appearances was barely enough to cover the bills. He confirmed this during “Too Much.”\n\nRegardless of how “well off” he was as a youth, his drive and desire to succeed is the takeaway from his career.", "annotation_id": "9071028", "url": "https://genius.com/9071028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Look, they’d rather run up on me than towards their goals", "vault_lines": ["Look, they'd rather run up on me than towards their goals"], "meaning": "With the success Drake has had from 2009 up to the release of Views,  many regard him as the hottest in the game. Rather than working towards their goal of being at the top or getting bread, other rappers seem more interested in taking shots at Drake online and in songs. Drake has proven several times that coming at him that way is not a great idea for your career.", "annotation_id": "9071018", "url": "https://genius.com/9071018", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "If I was you, I wouldn’t like me either", "vault_lines": ["If I was you, I wouldn't like me either"], "meaning": "During “0 to 100/ The Catch Up” Drake raps:\n\nImagine how it feel to watch another n— at the top\nYou know that if you wasn’t you, you would be dissin' you, dawg\n\nDrake is certifiably at the pinnacle of hip hop, and that places almost everyone else below him. Rap is competitive, so his competition lament his status in the game and wish it applied to them.", "annotation_id": "9071015", "url": "https://genius.com/9071015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 146, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "A lot of pent up aggression comin’ out of my section OVO on me while y’all niggas was playin’ dress-up", "vault_lines": ["A lot of pent up aggression comin' out of my section", "OVO on me while y'all niggas was playin' dress-up"], "meaning": "October’s Very Own, also known as OVO, is Drake’s music label and fashion brand. Young Toronto artists like Majid Jordan and Roy Wood$ are building lots of buzz thanks to the label, and the clothing line is coorporating with fashion brands like Jordan.\n\nNot everyone is happy with OVO, however. Mo-G, a Torontonian rapper who got a shout out on “Summer Sixteen”, burned OVO gear in his music video for “Wiggins” because he allegedly didn’t get paid from OVO.\nhttps://youtu.be/VsnfPsXTQOg?t=2m20s", "annotation_id": "9070982", "url": "https://genius.com/9070982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I never tag no one in, I’d rather get you myself", "vault_lines": ["No, no, no, no", "I never tag no one in, I'd rather get you myself"], "meaning": "He uses the term ‘tag’ which is used commonly in sports; if you’re tired, you can tag somebody in, and they can start playing for you. It’s an important term in wrestling, where the tag team format allows the wrestlers to call on their friends to help them in the fight.\n\nMany beefs have incorporated the entire crew, with every rapper going at the enemy regardless of his original target. 2Pac highlighted this on “Hit ‘Em Up”:\n\nI don’t know why I’m even on this track\nY'all n—s ain’t even on my level. I’m going to let my little homies ride on you bitch-made ass Bad Boy bitches\n\nOn “War Pain,” Meek enlisted Omelly for a verse. Drake has had no certified help in his dismantling of the Philly rapper, and his various beefs have all been fought solo.", "annotation_id": "9070932", "url": "https://genius.com/9070932", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I get a blank page when I try to draw a comparison I’m gettin’ straight to the point with it", "vault_lines": ["I get a blank page when I try to draw a comparison", "I'm gettin' straight to the point with it"], "meaning": "Complex named Drake as the best rapper in the world in 2015, and he’s constantly in the conversation over who is the best. Both Kanye and Jay Z have labelled him as an incredible, rare talent.\n\nSince no one is like him, if Drake were to literally try and draw a comparison, he wouldn’t be able to come up with anyone, and he’d just have a blank page.\n\nAlso note the wordplay: “drawing” (usually done with a pen or pencil) goes with next line “straight to the point.”", "annotation_id": "9070930", "url": "https://genius.com/9070930", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Now me and Kobe doin’ shots the night before the game Still drop 40 with liquor in my system", "vault_lines": ["Now me and Kobe doin' shots the night before the game", "Still drop 40 with liquor in my system"], "meaning": "Drake is taking “shots” of alcohol with Kobe the night before the Lakers game but also shooting hoops with him. Most shots are 40% alcohol—Drake can drop 40 points in a game even after the effects of alcohol.\n\nRunning practice with the Golden State Warriors must be helping Drake improve his basketball game.\n\n\n\nLeading up to this lyric, Drake mentions the number 20 twice, thus “40.”", "annotation_id": "9070803", "url": "https://genius.com/9070803", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "[Intro: The Winans] Question is, will I ever leave you? The answer is no, no, no-no-no, no, no No, no-no-no, no, no No, no, no, no Question is, will I ever leave you? The answer is no, no, no-no-no, no, no No, no-no-no, no, no No, no, no, no", "vault_lines": ["Question is, will I ever leave you?", "The answer is no, no, no-no-no, no, no", "No, no, no, no"], "meaning": "This is a sample from The Winans' “The Question Is.” They’re a Detroit gospel quartet active since the 1980’s.\n\nThis sample was also used in the beat of  “Summer Sixteen” which was featured on the 14th episode of OVO SOUND Radio.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XTGaZXsRgvo\n\nThis intro could also be a rhetorical question addressed to his city, Toronto. Will Drake ever leave Toronto? The answer is no.\n\nIn an interview with Nardwuar, Drake spoke about the use of the sample, saying that he and 40 had been listening to it for a long time trying to figure out where to put it:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/DOEDtqdbWAg?t=28m22s", "annotation_id": "9070541", "url": "https://genius.com/9070541", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Me and Niko used to plot on how to make a change", "vault_lines": ["Me and Niko used to plot on how to make a change"], "meaning": "Niko is one of Drake’s oldest and best friends. In his thank you’s on Thank Me Later, Drake wrote that Niko is his “closest confidant:\n\nOur friendship is what keeps me alive and focused, I always thought I was an only child but my mother lied!! Haaa.\n\n\n\nThe change Drake refers to may be simply in their own lives, becoming successful, or the entire industry:\n\nGive these niggas the look, the verse, and even the hook\nThat’s why every song sound like Drake featuring Drake", "annotation_id": "9070392", "url": "https://genius.com/9070392", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "And my life is on display like Truman", "vault_lines": ["And my life is on display like Truman"], "meaning": "The Truman Show is a movie about a man whose whole life is broadcast as a 24-hour reality TV show. However, he isn’t aware of the broadcast, and thinks his life is normal when in fact, it’s all staged.\n\nDrake is comparing his life to Truman’s, because he is so famous that much of his life ends up on the news—on display to the consuming people.", "annotation_id": "9070333", "url": "https://genius.com/9070333", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Need y’all to know that I never needed none of y’all niggas Fuck bein’ all buddy buddy with the opposition It’s like the front of the plane, nigga, it’s all business But I haven’t flown with y’all boys in a minute", "vault_lines": ["Need y'all to know that I never needed none of y'all niggas", "Fuck bein' all buddy buddy with the opposition", "It's like the front of the plane, nigga, it's all business", "But I haven't flown with y'all boys in a minute"], "meaning": "The “opposition” is likely Meek Mill, a rapper he’s had a torrid and enthralling beef with.\n\nThroughout Views and specifically this song, he’s expressed a desire to keep his friends close and his enemies at a distance. The production and guestlist on the record exemplifies this.\n\nAlthough Drake deals almost exclusively in subliminal disses, the distinction between friend and enemy is usually incredibly clear. His dealings with Kanye West and Jay Z showcase his “all business” attitude. After taking their submissions off “Pop Style” he told Zane Lowe:\n\nJay didn’t really do a verse… Really I just was trying to get Ye on it at first and Ye just sent it to me like that: ‘Yo, Jay kind of just did my first few lines for me, and he was just here and that’s how we flexed it. It was cool you know and he was all excited about it. I just ended up going with my version of the song. It didn’t play out how I wanted it to go\n\nThe front of the plane is business class, and the fact that Drake removed a full-blown Kanye feature from his record, something rappers would die for, proves his is in rarefied air.", "annotation_id": "9070310", "url": "https://genius.com/9070310", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I’d share more of my story but you wouldn’t believe it It’s farfetched like I threw that shit a hundred meters", "vault_lines": ["I'd share more of my story but you wouldn't believe it", "It's farfetched like I threw that shit a hundred meters"], "meaning": "The story of Drake’s come-up has been played out all over this record, from his basement beginnings on “Grammys” to his success finesse on “Still Here.” He alludes to an even grander narrative, possibly saved for his next project.\n\nIn yet another Drake sports reference, he explains his stories are just as far fetched as a Quarterback tossing a football 100 metres. If it were to travel that distance, it would have to be “fetched”, hence the wordplay on far-fetched.", "annotation_id": "9070289", "url": "https://genius.com/9070289", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "Niggas quick to double cross like both of us Christian Lamborghini got me feelin’ like I’m Christian Bale", "vault_lines": ["Niggas quick to double cross like both of us Christian", "Lamborghini got me feelin' like I'm Christian Bale"], "meaning": "Two Christians would have two crosses = double cross. This is why he refuses to have new friends, his paranoia creeping in.\n\nChristian Bale played Batman in the Dark Knight Trilogy, who drives a Lamborghini when he’s in the guise of Bruce Wayne. It’s claimed Drake has a lambo of his own.", "annotation_id": "9070224", "url": "https://genius.com/9070224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I dropped out right before I graduate Six credits left, my mama had her saddest day “It’s only up from here, I promise, you just gotta wait” And she took my word for it, that’s all I had to say", "vault_lines": ["I dropped out right before I graduate", "Six credits left, my mama had her saddest day", "“It's only up from here, I promise, you just gotta wait”", "And she took my word for it, that's all I had to say"], "meaning": "When Drake was 17, he dropped out of high school to pursue his acting career on the show Degrassi. His mom, a teacher, wasn’t impressed.\n\nOn the topic of his dropout, he said:\n\nI’m just advising you to follow your heart and if you really believe in something and you project that, I believe that it does come back to you, so, I aligned myself with great people, which I feel like is the key to success and here I am.\n\nAlthough unimpressed at the time, she’s probably quite happy with the outcome.", "annotation_id": "9070179", "url": "https://genius.com/9070179", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "And my wifey is a spice like I’m David Beckham", "vault_lines": ["And my wifey is a spice like I'm David Beckham"], "meaning": "Drake compares his girl to Victoria Beckham, wife of former soccer star David Beckham. In her heyday as a singer, she went by Posh Spice, of the British Pop group the Spice Girls.\n\n\n\n“Spice” is also UK slang for an attractive woman. As of April 2016, Drake is not married, although he did ask Nicki Minaj to marry him on his 2009 song “Miss Me.”", "annotation_id": "9070105", "url": "https://genius.com/9070105", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I keep it a hundred like I’m running a fever", "vault_lines": ["I keep it a hundred like I'm running a fever"], "meaning": "In his song “0 to 100 / The Catch Up,” Drake warns of how he is rising in the rap game and if he hasn’t passed you yet, he will. Since then, nearly two years have past and it seems he’s kept his word.\n\n“Keeping it at 100” refers to working 100% all the time, which is very true for Drake. In 2015, he released two projects: If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late and, in collaboration with Future, What a Time to Be Alive.\n\nAlso, if one’s body temperature surpasses 100°F, they are considered to have a fever. Working hard in the gym can cause a fever, but whether keeping it 100 in the rap game does is unknown. Perhaps Drake, like his frequent collaborater DJ Khaled, is suffering from success.", "annotation_id": "9070095", "url": "https://genius.com/9070095", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "line": "I’m a staple in the game, all my papers together", "vault_lines": ["I'm a staple in the game, all my papers together"], "meaning": "A “staple” is the most important element of something, in this case the rap game. Drake’s position at the top identifies him as a staple of the game. He plays another meaning of “staple,” to attach two pieces of paper together.\n\n“Paper” is another word for money, and the previous lines about putting his family debt behind him, not to mention his Apple deal, ensure his paper is all present and correct.", "annotation_id": "9070072", "url": "https://genius.com/9070072", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Views", "status": "done", "targets": 39, "clicked": 39, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-views-lyrics", "genius_title": "Views"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Does my hair look beautiful? (Yeah) Do my titties look right? Think I can knock a boy off right now?", "vault_lines": ["Does my hair look beautiful? (Yeah)", "Do my titties look right?", "Think I can knock a boy off right now?"], "meaning": "Sampled from Tiktok-er / YouTuber – Quenlin Blackwell\n @quenblackwell .\n\nQuenlin has been known to have had conversations with The Boy ( https://www.reddit.com/r/Drizzy/comments/1s0wicu/quen_blackwells_convo_with_drake_after_he_posted/ )", "annotation_id": "39871585", "url": "https://genius.com/39871585", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "You want Cench or your ex, which one?", "vault_lines": ["Which one? Which one?", "You want Cench or your ex, which one?", "You want friends or success, which one?", "Which one? Which one? Which one? Which—", "Ayy, you want Cench or your ex, which one?"], "meaning": "Cench is a nickname for Central Cee, and Which One is the name of the song ❤️", "annotation_id": "39855025", "url": "https://genius.com/39855025", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "You could cry out ya eye and curse", "vault_lines": ["You could cry out ya eye and curse"], "meaning": "The phrase “cry out ya eye” is pulled straight from Caribbean/Jamaican dialect it means to cry so hard and so long that you’ve got nothing left, completely cried out. Drake leans heavily into UK and dancehall language throughout the track, and this line is one of the most authentic moments of that register.\n\nThe context is crucial it comes right after “your last man broke your heart and it hurts.” Drake is acknowledging her pain without dwelling on it. Yeah, you could sit with it, cry yourself empty, curse his name or you could be here, right now, choosing something new.", "annotation_id": "39792332", "url": "https://genius.com/39792332", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Ayy, you want Cench or your ex, which one?", "vault_lines": ["Which one? Which one?", "You want Cench or your ex, which one?", "You want friends or success, which one?", "Which one? Which one? Which one? Which—", "Ayy, you want Cench or your ex, which one?"], "meaning": "The whole song is built around a single question, and this line is its sharpest form. The central tension crystallizes here: old loyalty versus new possibility, comfort versus ambition. Her ex is familiar, safe, and probably hurt her Cench is Central Cee, one of the hottest artists in the world, standing right in front of her on the dancefloor.\n\n\n\nDrake isn’t just name-dropping his collaborator for clout. By putting “Cench or your ex” in the same breath as “friends or success,” he elevates the choice from a romantic one into something bigger an ultimatum between the old and the new, comfort and ambition.", "annotation_id": "39792293", "url": "https://genius.com/39792293", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Your backside weighin’ you down, one ton", "vault_lines": ["Your backside weighin' you down, one ton"], "meaning": "Cench is alluding to this girl having a large bum, possibly a BBL.", "annotation_id": "39780831", "url": "https://genius.com/39780831", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "[Intro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Intro is said by 25-year-old Quenlin Blackwell. She is a internet star who blew up off Vine and carried that fame into TikTok, YouTube, and Instagram with her chaotic and funny personality, memes, and real life content. She’s modeled for big fashion brands (Victoria Secret runway, Chanel, Glossier, etc.). She has popped up at major events, and turned herself into a pop culture figure. People love her because she is loud, honest, and doesn’t try to be polished or fake. She has became friends with Billie Eilish, Doja Cat, Lil Yachty, PinkPantheress, SZA, Odessa A’zion, JT, & others. She has her own cooking show on YouTube called “Feeding Starving Celebrities” where she invites her friends to come cook with her! Some notable people that made an appearance are Duke Dennis, Larray, PinkPantheress, Kali Uchis, Bretman Rock, Gabbriette, Lil Yatchy & more! Recently on Rachel Sennot’s HBO Original “I LOVE LA,” playing herself!\n\nig: @quenblackwell\nyt: @QUENBLACKWELL", "annotation_id": "38459001", "url": "https://genius.com/38459001", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "A G’d up ting that I bring sticks ’round And I’ve got a good girl, good girl, snitch one", "vault_lines": ["Got a fairy ting and I got a witch one", "A G'd up ting that I bring sticks 'round", "And I've got a good girl, good girl, snitch one"], "meaning": "Playing forward with the previous line, Cench says he knows a “G’d up ting” (A girl familiar and presumably comfortable with gang activities) whom he feels comfortable bringing guns(sticks) around, as well as a “good girl” whom he thinks could “snitch”", "annotation_id": "36373176", "url": "https://genius.com/36373176", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Then wine your waist for the 6ix Side yute and", "vault_lines": ["Then wine your waist for the 6ix Side yute and", "Then wine your waist for the 6ix Side yute dem"], "meaning": "In Caribbean dancing, “to wine” refers to the movement of your hips in a circular motion. It is derived from the word “winding”.\n\n“Six-side” refers to the city of Toronto. The city is famously known as “The 6”, a nickname popularized by Drake. The name comes from the city’s six original municipalities and its area codes, 416  and 647.\n\n“Yute” is Jamaican slang for youth or young people.", "annotation_id": "36371132", "url": "https://genius.com/36371132", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "I’m in the jam, know the mandem, burst", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the jam, know the mandem, burst"], "meaning": "“Jam” = party or gathering. “Mandem” = your crew, the guys you roll with. “Burst” here means to arrive or show up hard, like making an entrance.\n\nBasically Drake’s saying:\n“I’m at the party, with my crew, making moves.”", "annotation_id": "36357125", "url": "https://genius.com/36357125", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Got a fairy ting and I got a witch one", "vault_lines": ["Got a fairy ting and I got a witch one"], "meaning": "Clever wordplay joining which and witch.\n\n“Fairy ting” = a sweet, dreamy, soft, maybe innocent girl.\n\n“Witch one” = a bold, mysterious, possibly chaotic or powerful girl (goth, emo)\n\nSo the line means:\n“I’ve got two types of girls — one’s all soft and light, the other’s dark and dangerous.”", "annotation_id": "36357075", "url": "https://genius.com/36357075", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Yeah, all the girls that’s here for the truth Come put two hands on the DJ booth Then wine your waist pon a real good yute dem", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, all the girls that's here for the truth", "Come put two hands on the DJ booth", "Then wine your waist pon a real good yute dem", "All the girls that's here for the truth", "Ayy, all the girls that's here for the truth", "Then wine your waist for the 6ix Side yute and", "Come put both hands on the DJ booth", "Then wine your waist for the 6ix Side yute dem"], "meaning": "“Ayy, all the girls that’s here for the truth, come put two hands on the DJ booth”\n\nThis is a call to the ladies. “Here for the truth” probably means they’re not fake, they’re real — they came to vibe, not front.\n\nPutting “two hands on the DJ booth” is like saying get ready to dance, or maybe literally lean forward — DJ booth becomes the party anchor.\n\n“Then wine your waist for the six-side yute”\nWine your waist” = classic Caribbean slang for winding/rotating your hips — a dance move, seductive or playful depending on the vibe.\n\n“Six-side yute” = Drake’s from the 6ix (Toronto).\n\nSo basically, he’s saying:\n“Ladies, if you’re real, come dance for the Toronto boy.”", "annotation_id": "36357005", "url": "https://genius.com/36357005", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Put a coin in the slot, ya just hit jackpot", "vault_lines": ["Put a coin in the slot, ya just hit jackpot"], "meaning": "Putting a coin the slot is a euphemism for sexual intercourse. Additionally, Central is saying this girl “hit the jackpot” and got lucky by getting to have sex with him. This is similar to how a person playing a casino game by putting a coin in an arcade slot can “hit a jackpot”.", "annotation_id": "36356202", "url": "https://genius.com/36356202", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "I’m at the Claridge’s in London, burst", "vault_lines": ["I'm at the Claridge's in London, burst"], "meaning": "Drake refers to spending time at the iconic luxury hotel Claridge’s, which is located in the Mayfair district of the West End of London.", "annotation_id": "36356034", "url": "https://genius.com/36356034", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "You want friends or success, which one?", "vault_lines": ["Which one? Which one?", "You want Cench or your ex, which one?", "You want friends or success, which one?", "Which one? Which one? Which one? Which—", "Ayy, you want Cench or your ex, which one?"], "meaning": "Drake frames friendship and material success as mutually exclusive, suggesting that at his level of fame, true friends are hard to find. There are a few potential reasons for this. Firstly, success often demands time and focus, leaving little room for personal relationships. Additionally, once someone reaches Drake’s status, people may approach them not out of genuine connection but because of what that success can offer, making relationships feel transactional rather than authentic.\n\nHowever, due to Drake’s recent circumstances, envy seems to be the driving idea behind this line. In 2024, Drake stood as the lone wolf in rap’s civil war; a massive feud involving Kendrick Lamar, Future, Metro Boomin, A$AP Rocky, The Weeknd, Rick Ross, and Kanye West going at Drake. Kanye even referred to it as the attempted “elimination of Drake.” On his 2024 track “No Face,” Drake suggested that many of these artists turned on him because they were envious of his success, spitting:\n\nI’m just so happy that niggas who envied and held that shit in got to finally show it\n\nThis line echoes the idea that at the highest level, friendships can fracture under the weight of envy, leaving him questioning whether one can truly have both success and loyal friends.", "annotation_id": "36351625", "url": "https://genius.com/36351625", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Too many gyal, now I gotta switch one", "vault_lines": ["Too many gyal, now I gotta switch one"], "meaning": "Cench flexes the volume of women available to him, interpolating a line from his 2023 Dave-assisted hit “Sprinter”:\n\nI got too many gyal, too many-many gyal, I got\nThey can last me the next two weeks, uh", "annotation_id": "36351091", "url": "https://genius.com/36351091", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Tell me what you want, what you really, really want", "vault_lines": ["Tell me what you want, what you really, really want"], "meaning": "This is an interpolation of the Spice Girls‘ 1996 smash-hit single “Wannabe.” On the track’s refrain, the girls go back and forth before revealing their requirements for their ideal romantic partners:\n\nYo, I’ll tell you what I want, what I really, really want\nSo tell me what you want, what you really, really want\n\nThe song was the lead single for their debut album Spice and is now regarded as one of the best records from a girl group of all time.", "annotation_id": "36349932", "url": "https://genius.com/36349932", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "These guys can’t make songs for the gyal dem Trust me, this how the single should sound", "vault_lines": ["These guys can't make songs for the gyal dem", "Trust me, this how the single should sound", "Play this for the gyal dem party, dun"], "meaning": "Artists these days can’t seem to make music that women can actually dance to. Take someone like Kendrick Lamar, he’s out here with all these double and triple entendres, instead of making jams. This is a callback to Drake’s bar “GIMME A HUG”:\n\nFunny how it’s only bitch niggas that are waiting on the boy’s obituary\n‘Cause if I die, it’s these niggas that become the sole beneficiary\nAnd what the fuck are they gon’ do with it?\nHave the girls up at 29 on stage twerkin' with a dictionary?", "annotation_id": "36349236", "url": "https://genius.com/36349236", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "I got X if you wanna take drugs Do you wanna have sex or do you wanna make love?", "vault_lines": ["I got X if you wanna take drugs", "Do you wanna have sex or do you wanna make love?"], "meaning": "This line cleverly interpolates two ideas from 50 Cent’s iconic anthem “In Da Club.”\n\nFirst, Central Cee echoes 50 Cent’s delivery in the following line:\n\n“I got X, if you wanna take drugs.”\n\nThis line serves as a reinterpretation of 50 Cent’s well-known hook in “In da Club” — “Look, mami, I got the X if you into takin' drugs.”\n\nHere, “I got X” is a direct nod to that same party/drug culture energy.\n\nThen in the next line:\n\n“You wanna have sex or do you wanna make love?”\n\nThe artist flips 50 Cent’s famous chorus line:\n\n“I’m into having sex, I ain’t into making love.”\n\nInstead of stating a clear preference like 50 Cent did, Central Cee gives options — drugs, sex, or love — leaving it open-ended and emotionally unclear.", "annotation_id": "36348898", "url": "https://genius.com/36348898", "state": "pending", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Turn the hotel to a twenty-V-one", "vault_lines": ["Turn the hotel to a twenty-V-one"], "meaning": "“Twenty-V-One” could refer to popular YouTube video’s, twenty women vs one man. The YouTube series was originally popularised by the UK YouTube group, Sidemen, where one guy goes on a one-by-one speed date with twenty women.\n\nThese lines paints a picture of Central Cee having twenty girls in his hotel, fighting for his attention or craving sexual pleasures. This bar could also possibly be a reference to Drake’s bars in “Push Ups” from April 2024 during the start of his rap feud with Kendrick Lamar:\n\nWhat the fuck is this, a twenty-v-one, nigga?", "annotation_id": "36348448", "url": "https://genius.com/36348448", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Girl, your body is tea, it’s pain, I’m sprung", "vault_lines": ["Girl, your body is tea, it's pain, I'm sprung"], "meaning": "In modern slang, “tea” refers to a woman’s attractive or desirable physique. The phrase “I’m sprung” means someone is infatuated or deeply obsessed, often due to physical attraction.\n\nThe full line plays on T-Pain’s 2005 hit single “I’m sprung”.", "annotation_id": "36348411", "url": "https://genius.com/36348411", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "Then work, work, work, work, work Yeah, work, work, work, work, work", "vault_lines": ["Then work, work, work, work, work", "Yeah, work, work, work, work, work"], "meaning": "Interpolation of the chorus of Rihanna’s 2016 hit single, “Work,” featuring Drake:\n\nJust get ready fi work, work, work, work, work, work\nHe said me haffi work, work, work, work, work, work\nHe see me do mi dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt\nAh so me put in work, work, work, work, work, work\nNer, ner, ner, ner, ner, ner\nWhen you ah guh learn, learn, learn, learn, learn, learn?\nBefore the tables turn, turn, turn, turn, turn, turn\n\nRepeated use of the term “work” after asking for sexual favors refers to putting in greater effort or a better performance in the bedroom to fulfill a sexual desire.", "annotation_id": "36347407", "url": "https://genius.com/36347407", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Which One", "line": "I got a chopstick for your wonton", "vault_lines": ["I got a chopstick for your wonton"], "meaning": "Cee uses a sexual metaphor linking the proper way of eating Wonton (with a chopstick) to an intimate act where the “chopstick” symbolizes his schlong and the “wonton” represents the female body, the buttocks to be exact.\n\n\n\n Wonton, a folded dumpling often eaten with chopsticks,", "annotation_id": "36347153", "url": "https://genius.com/36347153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
