{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "We can vacation wherever we want ’cause I don’t care where none of my opps is", "vault_lines": ["We can vacation wherever we want 'cause I don't care where none of my opps is", "I don't care about my opps (I don't care about my opps)"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION:\n\nDrake is responding to threats from Kendrick Lamar and other rappers on the recent Rap beef saying he will go wherever he wants whenever he wants because he’s not fearful of his opps (enemies) Drake might also be referring to the last bar of the second verse\n“I’m going on vacation now hope next time y'all plan it right”", "annotation_id": "32594624", "url": "https://genius.com/32594624", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Slaughter Gang", "vault_lines": ["Leavin' him flatline, Slaughter Gang, knife in the back, I'm on that time", "I'm Slaughter Gang OVO"], "meaning": "Slaughter Gang is a label created in 2017 by no one other than 21 Savage, this is heavily referenced in multiple songs between Drake and 21 Savage.", "annotation_id": "32735172", "url": "https://genius.com/32735172", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Seem like everybody want an opp now", "vault_lines": ["Goin' live'll get your ass popped now, seem like everybody want an opp now"], "meaning": "This is a new day of age with social media. A simple scroll through anyone’s social media comments or videos and you’ll find someone saying something negative. Before having a feud with someone was hard to get the public’s attention but now thousands too millions of people can support you now with socials.", "annotation_id": "32735100", "url": "https://genius.com/32735100", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "We can vacation wherever we want ’cause I don’t care where none of my opps is", "vault_lines": ["We can vacation wherever we want 'cause I don't care where none of my opps is", "I don't care about my opps (I don't care about my opps)"], "meaning": "This is most likely a response to “Not like us” when  Kendrick said “That Oakland show might just be yo last stop nigga.” In response drake saying that he can travel to anywhere he wants and there will be no danger because his “Opps” oppose none.\n\nThis line is also similar to a line on drakes 3rd diss “Family matters” when he says “ I get off the plane and nothing has changed.” meaning when he travels nothing has changed about the people or the vibe towards him because Kendrick’s tracks did no damage.", "annotation_id": "32711827", "url": "https://genius.com/32711827", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Heard you miss your dogs, now it’s long live who? Idiot", "vault_lines": ["Heard you miss your dogs, now it's long live who?"], "meaning": "Drake is mentioning A$AP Rocky‘s album Long.Live.A$AP. after previously talking about his Crew A$AP in the lines before.\n\nThe way Drake says “who” can also be heard as “hoo,” referencing an owl, a trademark of OVO. The Long.Live.A$AP reference becomes “Long Live OVO” when interpreted this way.", "annotation_id": "32665598", "url": "https://genius.com/32665598", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Bitch, I’m 21, bitch, I’m not 22", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, I'm 21, bitch, I'm not 22"], "meaning": "Drake mentions the ASAP Mog, 21 Savage and 22 Savage (who is now known as Young 22).\n\nThe A$AP Mog is a hip hop group that was formed in 2006 and has 22 members.\n\nIn October 2021, Ferg dropped a new single named MDMX along with dropping his moniker (A$AP) which sparked questions amongst fans if Ferg has left the Mob (which would make it 21 members instead of 22). As of now, it is not clear if Ferg has left or stayed.\n\nDrake is also alluding to 21’s street oriented\npersona saying he is just as real and tough as 21 is even though Drake had a more peaceful past than 21 did.\n\nHe’s also referring to 22 Savage, who is a parody of 21 Savage. It’s also worth noting 22 has a song called “Ain’t No 21” which Drake obviously flips the title of the song, comparing himself to someone who’s real (21) and not someone who is a parody (22).", "annotation_id": "32657438", "url": "https://genius.com/32657438", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "We put an AirTag on his car, he in Miami by Brickell", "vault_lines": ["We put an AirTag on his car, he in Miami by Brickell"], "meaning": "Brickell is a neighbourhood in Miami, Florida known for being an affluent and upscale area.", "annotation_id": "32651819", "url": "https://genius.com/32651819", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "The Mexicans love me, I’m out here just makin’ connections", "vault_lines": ["She textin' she love me, the Mexicans love me, I'm out here just makin' connections"], "meaning": "This is a reference to OVO affiliate, Nessel “Chubbs” Beezer’s, new label “PFL” or “Play for Legacy” and it’s first announced artist, Chino Pacas of Mexican origin.", "annotation_id": "32648613", "url": "https://genius.com/32648613", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Yeah, niggas doin’ all that woofin’, pussy", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, niggas doin' all that woofin', pussy"], "meaning": "This may be a reference to Drake’s line in ‘Push Ups\" where he says “That’s the bark with the bite nigga what’s up?” Implying that drake talks and does what he says he’ll do. But other rappers do all talk but no actions. 21 is backing up Drake in his infamous 20v1 against rappers like Kendrick Lamar, Rick Ross, Future and metro boomin, Drake is yet to prove himself in the beef though.", "annotation_id": "32646751", "url": "https://genius.com/32646751", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Clifford", "vault_lines": ["I wanna see red, stop sign, I wanna see red, Clifford"], "meaning": "Clifford the Big Red Dog is an American children’s book series about the adventures of a girl named Emily Elizabeth and her titular pet: a giant, red-furred dog named Clifford", "annotation_id": "32641219", "url": "https://genius.com/32641219", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Them niggas be lyin’, they niggas be dyin’ and they don’t never post shit", "vault_lines": ["Them niggas be lyin', they niggas be dyin' and they don't never post shit"], "meaning": "A possible subliminal diss at Kendrick Lamar, and is the reverse of Kendrick’s line on Euphoria\n\nI hate when a rapper talk about guns, then somebody die\nThey turn into nuns, then hop online, like “Pray for my city”", "annotation_id": "32636360", "url": "https://genius.com/32636360", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "She textin’ she love me,", "vault_lines": ["She textin' she love me, the Mexicans love me, I'm out here just makin' connections"], "meaning": "Homophone for “She Texan, she love me” which is a continuation of the previous line", "annotation_id": "32636358", "url": "https://genius.com/32636358", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Goin’ live’ll get your ass popped now,", "vault_lines": ["Goin' live'll get your ass popped now, seem like everybody want an opp now"], "meaning": "This line means “going live” sound double entendre here\n1) Going LIVE in social medias for threatening the rapper who gone you beef so 21 just stating that point.\n2) “Going (ALIVE) will get you ass popped now” because in the previous lines he was talking about guns and violence.", "annotation_id": "32633807", "url": "https://genius.com/32633807", "state": "pending", "votes": -4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Say hello to the four-nickel, I got a magazine, who got an issue?", "vault_lines": ["Say hello to the four-nickel, I got a magazine, who got an issue?"], "meaning": "This line has two primary levels.\n\nA “four-nickel” refers to a .45 pistol. As such, the reference to a magazine would immediately call this unit of  ammunition storage to mind.  It is unclear whether 21 Savage is referring to a spare, or secondary magazine. However, the intention is clear — anyone with an issue or problem will have to be on its receiving end.\n\nFurther, this line draws on “magazines” in the publication sense, which are released in issues.", "annotation_id": "32631128", "url": "https://genius.com/32631128", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "It’s all just a part of the game, we all gotta roll with protection", "vault_lines": ["It's all just a part of the game, we all gotta roll with protection"], "meaning": "Drake’s line highlights the harsh realities of the rap industry, where challenges and threats are inevitable. By referencing “protection” as firearms, he underscores the need to be armed and prepared to navigate dangers and competition.", "annotation_id": "32627674", "url": "https://genius.com/32627674", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Turn him to a hashtag, make him a “long live,” niggas ain’t my kind", "vault_lines": ["Turn him to a hashtag, make him a \"long live,\" niggas ain't my kind"], "meaning": "The often tag on social media is #LongLive in order to commemorate the individual who died. In this case 21 is claiming once he kills somebody people will honor the victim by making a hashtag (#).", "annotation_id": "32622229", "url": "https://genius.com/32622229", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "This world has some real things dividin’ them", "vault_lines": ["This world has some real things dividin' them"], "meaning": "This is Drake indirectly referencing the Israeli–Palestinian conflict which is currently ongoing and “dividing” people worldwide.\n\nHe follows this line up by mentioning\n\n“My right hand, he’s Muslim, I’ll die for him”\n\nPalestine’s population consists of approximately 99% Muslims, whilst Israel has a majority Jewish ethnic population.\n\nDrake himself is of Jewish descent through his mother Sandra Graham, so this can be seen as a shout to demonstrate his impartiality in the conflict, as he has close friends that are “Muslim”.\n\nIn 2023, Drake also signed a penned letter, with other artists, to President Joe Biden calling for a ceasefire in the Israeli-Palestinian war.", "annotation_id": "32620230", "url": "https://genius.com/32620230", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "[Chorus: Young Thug]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Parts of Young Thug’s vocals are taken from a previously unreleased track previewed by Thug in 2022:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/lxdzhTTXH64?si=6LD-pAPim9JYAqp_", "annotation_id": "32619713", "url": "https://genius.com/32619713", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "We don’t play patty cake, they just be lettin’ us in, we don’t do the inspection", "vault_lines": ["We don't play patty cake, they just be lettin' us in, we don't do the inspection"], "meaning": "Drake is building on his previous line about sitting across from his opps in the club. In this bar, he references the common practice of patting down patrons before they enter a club, which is often done to ensure no one is carrying weapons. The phrase “play patty cake” is a clever play on words, referencing the pat-down process in a playful way.\n\nDrake implies that he and his crew have enough influence or status to bypass this security check (“they just be lettin’ us in”), suggesting that they enter the club without inspection. This further implies that they could be armed and dangerous, ready for any confrontation if their rivals decide to test them.\n\nKodak also has a popular song called patty cake so don’t make no mistake who he’s referring to", "annotation_id": "32619073", "url": "https://genius.com/32619073", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Knockin’ off the namebrand niggas in your crew", "vault_lines": ["Knockin' off the namebrand niggas in your crew"], "meaning": "Drake is taking shots at A$AP Rocky.\n\nA$AP Ferg recently took ‘A$AP’ off his name on youtube. Fans are speculating that he might have parted ways with the A$AP mob.\n\nIt also supports the following lines, “Heard you miss your dogs, now it’s long live who? Idiot”; since his first studio album was “Long. Live. ASAP”.", "annotation_id": "32603478", "url": "https://genius.com/32603478", "state": "pending", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Ayy, I’m feelin’ like 2 Chainz, T.R.U.", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, I'm feelin' like 2 Chainz, T.R.U."], "meaning": "This is a reference to 2 Chainz' “T.R.U. REALigion” mixtape made back in 2011. It was brought back with 2 additional songs on its 10th anniversary on November 8, 2021.\n\nReviewing 2 Chainz' mixtape, Martin Spasov of XXL said “…when the subject is stuntin’ or ballin’, 2 Chainz excels…”\n\nThe lyrics of “IT’S UP” is Drake and 21 Savage expressing that they excel at stunting on their enemies.", "annotation_id": "32601244", "url": "https://genius.com/32601244", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Ref 1 leave the game, I’m sayin’ bye with him", "vault_lines": ["Ref 1 leave the game, I'm sayin' bye with him"], "meaning": "This bar is in reference to his engineer, Noel Cadastre, who often titles original recordings of Drake’s vocals with the tag of “Ref One,” which Drake is referencing here.\n\nDrake is suggesting that if Noel ever leaves the music “game”, he will retire aswell.\n\nDrake has mentioned taking breaks and retiring from music multiple times in the past, for example on “Weston Road Flows” he says\n\nI’m assumin' everybody’s 35 and under\nThat’s when I plan to retire, man, it’s already funded\n\nHe never actually followed through with this plan, as he was consistently releasing music after turning 36.\n\nIn February 2023, Drake hinted at the idea of a “Graceful Exit” on an interview with Lil Yachty.\n\nOn the date of release of “For All The Dogs”, Drake suggested that he was taking a break for “maybe a year”, from music to focus on his health.\n\nA month after this announcement he released “For All The Dogs Scary Hours Edition” with 6 additional songs, and continued to drop music throughout 2024 – most notably multiple diss tracks during his feud with Kendrick Lamar.", "annotation_id": "32594250", "url": "https://genius.com/32594250", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "The way that I’m used to playin’ the politics, swear I could win the election", "vault_lines": ["The way that I'm used to playin' the politics, swear I could win the election"], "meaning": "Drake draws a parallel between his strategic maneuvering in the music industry and the tactics used in political campaigns to secure an electoral success. This is especially relevant because of the upcoming 2024 presidential election.\n\nBeyond music, Drake has successfully ventured into various business endeavors, such as co-founding the OVO brand, which includes a record label, clothing line, and radio station, and having lucrative partnerships with household brands as Nike.\n\nThe theme of strategic maneuvering is consistent throughout Drake discography and seems like something he really takes great pride in. “Diplomatic Immunity,” is a song by Drake off his 2018 EP Scary Hours. The title itself references the world of politics, and throughout the track, Drake discusses his influence and the careful navigation required to sustain it.", "annotation_id": "32594238", "url": "https://genius.com/32594238", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "And Junior Boy rollin’ like Cinco de Mayo, he may hold a fifth in possession", "vault_lines": ["And Junior Boy rollin' like Cinco de Mayo, he may hold a fifth in possession"], "meaning": "Cinco de Mayo is an annual celebration held on May 5 to celebrate Mexico’s victory over the Second French Empire at the Battle of Puebla in 1862 led by General Ignacio Zaragoza.\n\nDrake sounds like he is shouting out J Prince Jr with the double entendre, firstly he may be alluding that Jr is moving around like a war general who is part of the May 5th celebrations and secondly, since in 2018 according to police records J Prince Jr was arrested and found to be in possession of a firearm, Drake does a play on words from May 5th, to suggesting that Jr may have had a FN Five-seven (fifth) in his possession.", "annotation_id": "32588783", "url": "https://genius.com/32588783", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "I told the promoter tonight, put me across from they section", "vault_lines": ["I told the promoter tonight, put me across from they section"], "meaning": "After the beef with Kendrick Lamar, Drake was spotted at KAMP Houston sitting across what seemed to be Kodak Black’s section. This may be a shot at Kodak after speculation that the two don’t get along.", "annotation_id": "32588553", "url": "https://genius.com/32588553", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "These niggas tryna send a message, well, leave that shit down at reception, ayy", "vault_lines": ["These niggas tryna send a message, well, leave that shit down at reception, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake uses metaphorical language to address critics in the music industry, potentially including Kendrick Lamar (Kdot), Kanye West (Ye), and The Weeknd. When he says, “These niggas tryna send a message, well, leave that shit down in the reception,” he implies that their criticisms are unimportant and should be ignored.", "annotation_id": "32585988", "url": "https://genius.com/32585988", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "My right hand, he’s Muslim, I’ll die for him", "vault_lines": ["My right hand, he's Muslim, I'll die for him"], "meaning": "Drake is from Toronto Canada, in 2021 9.6% of the inhabitants identified as muslim. So a lot of his friends are muslim.\n\nAlso 40 who is drake’s “right hand” might be Muslim he has Lebanese origins and he spoke on Muslim cases many times\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: This line is a tribute to Drake’s close friend and manager, Adel “Future the Prince” Nur. As Drake’s right-hand man, Future the Prince has been a crucial part of his career and personal life.", "annotation_id": "32585122", "url": "https://genius.com/32585122", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Nigga, what’s up? A hundred and forty-three Rolexes in my collection", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, what's up? A hundred and forty-three Rolexes in my collection"], "meaning": "Rolex is a popular Swiss luxury watchmaker.\n\nDrake is a huge fan of the company, as seen in 2023’s “Rich Baby Daddy,” where he rapped:\n\nRollie gang, patty gang, rich baby daddy gang\n\nAlthough all of Drake’s supposed 143 watches are not revealed to the public, Drake is known to have several of them. His most notable is his 42mm, 18-carat white gold Rolex Sky-Dweller “Meteorite” which goes for around $1 million.\n\n\n\n Drake with his Rolex Sky Dweller", "annotation_id": "32583182", "url": "https://genius.com/32583182", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Yeah, take a pic (Take a pic), Richard Millie, no stopwatch (Let’s go, uh-uh)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, take a pic (Take a pic), Richard Millie, no stopwatch (Let's go, uh-uh)"], "meaning": "Richard Millie is a Swiss luxury watchmaker founded in 2001. Richard Millies are one of the most expensive watches on the market.\n\nYoung Thug speaks on how his Richard Millie is an automatic watch and not a stop watch which needs to be worked manually.\n\nYoung Thug frequently mentioned the company in his music, an example being in 2021’s “Bad Boy” with Juice WRLD:\n\nNewborn baby, my Richard Mille nine months, ooh\n\nIn fact, Young Thug owns three Richard Millies. He owns the Richard Mille RM 11-01, Richard Mille RM 16-01, and the Richard Mille RM 055 Bubba Watson, all combined equals a whooping cost of $850k.\n\n\n\nYoung Thug with his Richard Mille RM 055 Bubba Watson", "annotation_id": "32583076", "url": "https://genius.com/32583076", "state": "pending", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "I just picked up somethin’ from Mercedes, it’s a one of one Black exterior the one that I want, red inside like that time of the month Blood money, that’s how I got this, blood money, that’s how I got this", "vault_lines": ["I just picked up somethin' from Mercedes, it's a one of one", "Black exterior the one that I want, red inside like that time of the month", "Blood money, that's how I got this, blood money, that's how I got this"], "meaning": "Drake compares his black Mercedes' red interior to menstruation, creating a vivid image. This metaphor not only highlights the striking contrast and luxury of the car’s design but also cleverly sets up the following lines.\n\nThe mention of “blood” in the context of menstruation smoothly transitions to “blood money,” which refers to money obtained through violent or illicit activities. By acknowledging that his wealth may have come through morally ambiguous or dangerous means, Drizzy juxtaposes the glamorous exterior of his success with the underlying harsh realities of how this wealth can be acquired.\n\nDrake flexing with a one-of-a-kind Vision Mercedes-Maybach 6 Cabriolet concept.", "annotation_id": "32582843", "url": "https://genius.com/32582843", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "I put the horns on the front of the ‘Bach, you know Drizzy gon’ roll like a Texan", "vault_lines": ["I put the horns on the front of the 'Bach, you know Drizzy gon' roll like a Texan"], "meaning": "In Texas culture, it is a style to mount cow horns on the front grille of your car. This line is a play on “front”/“back” since the horns are mounted on the front of the Mercedes-Maybach (pronounced like “back”).\n\n\n\nDrake pictured with his customized Mercedes Maybach with the mounted steer horns", "annotation_id": "32582768", "url": "https://genius.com/32582768", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "She keep tryna rip off my zipper, I moved to LA, I’m a Clipper", "vault_lines": ["She keep tryna rip off my zipper, I moved to LA, I'm a Clipper"], "meaning": "“Clipper” is slang for someone involved in the streets, often associated with using guns and violence. 21 asserts that his status as a clipper is so well-established and respected that he could move to Los Angeles and fit right in, drawing a parallel with the Los Angeles Clippers, a professional basketball team.\n\n21 and Thugger court side", "annotation_id": "32582751", "url": "https://genius.com/32582751", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "I’m at 48 with Ty and the vino, we drunk, it’s not even a question", "vault_lines": ["I'm at 48 with Ty and the vino, we drunk, it's not even a question"], "meaning": "“48” refers to Steak 48, a high-end steakhouse chain with locations in Houston, TX and Beverly Hills, CA.", "annotation_id": "32582729", "url": "https://genius.com/32582729", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "You niggas is overprotective with hoes, I’m already over the next one", "vault_lines": ["You niggas is overprotective with hoes, I'm already over the next one"], "meaning": "Drake reaffirms his ops seem to be lashing out against him over women which he has pointed out during his “20v1” beef with Kendrick, The Weeknd, Future, Metro Boomin, Rick Ross, & more. Several of these rappers have been rumored to be beefing with Drake over women.", "annotation_id": "32582654", "url": "https://genius.com/32582654", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "And just like the motherfuckin’ judge, the hammers come out for objections", "vault_lines": ["And just like the motherfuckin' judge, the hammers come out for objections"], "meaning": "Judges will often pull out a hammer (aka judicial gavel) during court cases to maintain order.\n\n\n\nHammer is also a commonly usedslang for gun. So pulling out the hammer for objections can also refer to pulling out a gun to use against anyone who is a nuisance.", "annotation_id": "32582625", "url": "https://genius.com/32582625", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "I’m Slaughter Gang OVO", "vault_lines": ["Leavin' him flatline, Slaughter Gang, knife in the back, I'm on that time", "I'm Slaughter Gang OVO"], "meaning": "Slaughter Gang is a record label founded in 2017 by 21 Savage. OVO Sound is a record label founded in 2012 by Drake and several others.\n\n21 Savage isn’t actually an OVO signee but is simply showing support to Drake—the two are frequent collaborators.", "annotation_id": "32582490", "url": "https://genius.com/32582490", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "I can’t sleep at night with Thug at Cobb County Corrections I think he did enough reflectin’, I think my brother learned his lesson", "vault_lines": ["I can't sleep at night with Thug at Cobb County Corrections", "I think he did enough reflectin', I think my brother learned his lesson"], "meaning": "On May 9, 2022, members of Young Stoner Life were arrested and indicted on 56 counts of suspected gang-related crimes and alleged violation of Georgia’s RICO Act. Young Thug was kept at Cobb County Corrections whilst court was going on. Drake is a friend and frequent collaborator with the label’s CEO, Young Thug. This song also features Young Thug using vocals from his unreleased song “Don’t Care About My Opps”.\n\nAt the time of this song’s release, Young Thug has yet to be released from Cobb County Corrections and any attempt at a bond has been denied. Drake has shown support for Young Thug during this case multiple times through music and Instagram stories.", "annotation_id": "32582287", "url": "https://genius.com/32582287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "No rap cap come out my mouth, made a couple songs, think he hot now Hit his ass up, he think he Pac now, broad daylight, he got shot down", "vault_lines": ["No rap cap come out my mouth, made a couple songs, think he hot now", "Hit his ass up, he think he Pac now, broad daylight, he got shot down"], "meaning": "“Hit ’Em Up” by 2Pac is one of the most iconic diss tracks in hip hop history.\n\nIn messages to hip hop media personality DJ Akademiks, 21 Savage’s manager Meezy denied rumors that these lyrics were sneak-dissing Kendrick Lamar.\n\nPac references have been a constant in hip hop since his 1996 death, and it’s not uncommon for rappers to have lines directed at other unnamed rappers or rival gang members which were written without a real person in mind. Regardless, fans were initially convinced by how well these lyrics aligned with the themes of Drake’s beef with Kendrick just a couple of months prior—Savage publicly supported Drake during the beef.\n\nPac was a big factor in the feud after Drake’s diss track “Taylor Made Freestyle,” which featured a 2Pac artificial intelligence voice filter. Kendrick proceeded to use this as an angle in his disses “euphoria” and “Not Like Us,” and has portrayed himself as a successor to Pac in the past, such as on the 2015 song “Mortal Man.”\n\nAdditionally, Savage’s claim—that the unnamed rapper he’s dissing only “made a couple songs” recently and now thinks he’s hot—fits with the common criticism of Kendrick (including by Drake during the beef) that he spends a long time between releases and that his output isn’t as prolific as many of his contemporaries. “Not Like Us” became a cultural phenomenon and made Kendrick the hottest he’s been in years, which detractors painted in a negative light, claiming he needed Drake’s clout. However, after this song’s release, Savage’s manager claimed on behalf of him that this was not a shot at Kendrick Lamar.", "annotation_id": "32582180", "url": "https://genius.com/32582180", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "line": "Chopper bullets make his ass hop ’round, bloodshed, ain’t no way to stop now", "vault_lines": ["Chopper bullets make his ass hop 'round, bloodshed, ain't no way to stop now"], "meaning": "Chopper is slang for automatic firearms such as Dracos, AK-47s, and Uzis.", "annotation_id": "32582131", "url": "https://genius.com/32582131", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Up", "status": "done", "targets": 39, "clicked": 39, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-young-thug-and-21-savage-its-up-lyrics", "genius_title": "It’s Up"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "I got it rollin’ in all kinda ways, lump sum and residual, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I got it rollin' in all kinda ways, lump sum and residual, yeah"], "meaning": "In accounting, “lump sum” means receiving cash all at once. “Residual” means earning over a long period of time.\n\nDrake’s label paid him a one-time deal (recently rumoured $400-500m), while also earning royalties on top of that initial amount. Money’s coming in both lump sum and residual.", "annotation_id": "40347529", "url": "https://genius.com/40347529", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "European, my vehicle, how much it hit me fo’?", "vault_lines": ["European, my vehicle, how much it hit me fo'?"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that his only driving Euro Cars and they expensive.", "annotation_id": "29434227", "url": "https://genius.com/29434227", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "Please do not speak to me like I’m that Drake from four years ago I’m at a higher place", "vault_lines": ["Please do not speak to me like I'm that Drake from four years ago", "I'm at a higher place"], "meaning": "Drake is comparing himself to 4 years ago from when IYRTITL was released. He has changed considerably not just in the music game, but also in life. His flow switched from that of Take Care and Thank me Later to IYRTITL and Drake has risen in wealth and fame, which is what he means that I’m at a higher place. It could also be a reference that alludes to God, who God is often seen as in a high place, or a place of power, which is where Drake sees himself. It could also allude to where he lives, as penthouses such as the one he owned in Toronto, are at the top floor.", "annotation_id": "21157834", "url": "https://genius.com/21157834", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "[Beat Switch]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "2:52 into the song, the beats changes from a ‘tough’ beat to a softer one, which is extremely reminiscent of Drake’s older track “0 to 100 / The Catch Up”.\n\nAlthough the track is not broken into two distinctly named parts, “No Tellin'” takes on a similar path that 0 to 100 did, in that the second half is filled with more thoughtful lyrics and it focuses on the possibilities of the future, not Drake’s past.\n\nThis is also not the first time that a beat switch has been used on the album; one was used on the preceding track, “Know Yourself”.", "annotation_id": "8594472", "url": "https://genius.com/8594472", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "I gotta keep watchin’ for oppers ’Cause anything’s possible, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I gotta keep watchin' for oppers", "'Cause anything's possible, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake is watching for Apples, cause his deal with the corporation was already made when he released that song, but wasn’t public. It’s a clue!\nAnything is possible, even being involved with a giant like Apple.", "annotation_id": "8002491", "url": "https://genius.com/8002491", "state": "verified", "votes": -6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "You pick the casket, I’ll put the nail through it I ain’t gotta do it, but fuck it, somebody gotta do it Hate if someone else did it, fuck, I may as well do it, damn!", "vault_lines": ["You pick the casket, I'll put the nail through it", "I ain't gotta do it, but fuck it, somebody gotta do it", "Hate if someone else did it, fuck, I may as well do it, damn!"], "meaning": "He’s Killing rappers, he doesn’t have too. But He does it for fun", "annotation_id": "7684673", "url": "https://genius.com/7684673", "state": "verified", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "Niggas think they can come take what I got Let’s be logical, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Niggas think they can come take what I got", "Let's be logical, yeah"], "meaning": "I’m pretty sure you (Alucard) are right connecting this line with Logic but i think you are going a little too deep. In February, Logic was on Power 106 where they did “Shots Fired,” and Logic had the option to take a shot on Drake, Chris Brown, or G-Eazy. He chose Drake but didn’t shoot any shots and actually praised him. I will link that video below.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zj8426ytEB8\n\nThe line right before this was “There’s no code of ethics out here, anyone will take shots at you, yeah”\n\nThat line is referring to “Shots Fired.”", "annotation_id": "7667061", "url": "https://genius.com/7667061", "state": "verified", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "I gotta keep watchin’ for oppers ’Cause anything’s possible, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I gotta keep watchin' for oppers", "'Cause anything's possible, yeah"], "meaning": "I gotta keep watching for ‘oddballs’, cuz anything’s possible yea.", "annotation_id": "7645821", "url": "https://genius.com/7645821", "state": "verified", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "I’m just lookin’ in the mirror like I’m really him Man, I’m really him, you just fillin’ in", "vault_lines": ["I'm just lookin' in the mirror like I'm really him", "Man, I'm really him, you just fillin' in"], "meaning": "Never mind the perception of him as soft/innocent. When Drake looks in the Mirror he know’s who he is.  People who don’t know him are making their own assumptions based on his perception.", "annotation_id": "7645790", "url": "https://genius.com/7645790", "state": "verified", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "Yeah, police comin’ ‘round lookin’ for some help On a case they gotta solve, we never help ’em", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, police comin' 'round lookin' for some help", "On a case they gotta solve, we never help 'em"], "meaning": "For me, this sounds like a shot at Hov. Recently its been known that Jay Z has been in talks with the FBI over a case that they “don’t have enough proof” for… This was mentioned by Damon Dash in his interview on the breakfast club\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XHX4hWurt8M\n\nI know it’s a stretch but who knows??? ….", "annotation_id": "7468767", "url": "https://genius.com/7468767", "state": "verified", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Tellin’", "line": "Okay, I had to switch the flow up on you niggas The shit was gettin’ too predictable, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Okay, I had to switch the flow up on you niggas", "The shit was gettin' too predictable, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that he wants his flow to always come off as appealing and ultimately different from all the other rappers. Hence, Nothing Was The Same.", "annotation_id": "6611183", "url": "https://genius.com/6611183", "state": "verified", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
