{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Yeah, you know me well, nigga, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, you know me well, nigga, yeah"], "meaning": "Most likely Drake means that the nigga knows him very well", "annotation_id": "38735830", "url": "https://genius.com/38735830", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "I just been playin’, I ain’t even notice I was winnin’", "vault_lines": ["I just been playin', I ain't even notice I was winnin'"], "meaning": "drake never found it that deep and won life by accident without being a try hard\n\n\nshows to take it slow and live life just normally and just chill and do what you want and let life draw your path as you walk along", "annotation_id": "37837999", "url": "https://genius.com/37837999", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "And this is the only sound you should fear", "vault_lines": ["And this is the only sound you should fear"], "meaning": "This line refers to the unique hip hop sound coming out of Toronto right now, popularized by Drizzy, The Weeknd, and producer Noah “40” Shebib.\n\n\n\nAs far as Drake is concerned, the only people that his competitors should be worried about are the upcoming artists from his home city.", "annotation_id": "37390109", "url": "https://genius.com/37390109", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Yeah, it’s whatever You know, feelin’ good, livin’ better I think maybe I was numb to it last year But you know I feel it now more than ever", "vault_lines": ["You know, feelin' good, livin' better", "I think maybe I was numb to it last year", "But you know I feel it now more than ever"], "meaning": "describes and proves that change is bound to happen. this is my favorite bar in the whole song", "annotation_id": "35235986", "url": "https://genius.com/35235986", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "And fuck you to the niggas that think it’s their time", "vault_lines": ["And fuck you to the niggas that think it's their time"], "meaning": "Drake is saying here that he is at the top of the game and no one can compete with him. He’s disregarding people that think they can challenge him in the rap game, as well as those who are critical of his work.", "annotation_id": "35224382", "url": "https://genius.com/35224382", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "I think I killed everybody in the game last year Man, fuck it, I was on, though", "vault_lines": ["Mmm-mmm, fuck it, I was wrong, though", "I think maybe I was numb to it last year", "I think I killed everybody in the game last year", "Man, fuck it, I was on, though"], "meaning": "Thank Me Later is the debut studio album by Drake. It was released on June 15, 2010, the year before Take Care came out. Following an anticipated release, Thank Me Later debuted at number one on the Billboard 200 with first week sales of 447,000 copies in the US, eventually selling 1.8 million copies there by August 2015. It also topped the Canadian Albums Chart and attained a platinum certification in Canada in its debut week.", "annotation_id": "34209930", "url": "https://genius.com/34209930", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Shots came, I don’t know where they was sent from", "vault_lines": ["Shots came, I don't know where they was sent from"], "meaning": "Drake has implied that he gets hate, but he’s got more to worry about than who is hating on him", "annotation_id": "33385646", "url": "https://genius.com/33385646", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "[Outro] Three in the morning, still going down", "vault_lines": ["Three in the morning, still going down"], "meaning": "Drake pays homage to Houston’s iconic rap scene by sampling DJ Screw’s 1995 track “Sailin' Da South.”\n\nThe slowed-down, atmospheric sound pioneered by DJ Screw has deeply influenced Drake’s music, resonating throughout his discography. This influence is perhaps most prominently showcased on 2009’s “November 18th,” where the title itself references the day Drake first visited Houston and became captivated by its culture. The song also samples DJ Screw, further cementing Drake’s connection to and respect for Houston’s musical legacy.", "annotation_id": "32662907", "url": "https://genius.com/32662907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "But jealousy is just love and hate at the same time, yeah", "vault_lines": ["But jealousy is just love and hate at the same time, yeah"], "meaning": "Drizzy captures the dual nature of jealousy with a profound and memorable phrase. Jealousy is an emotion that blends admiration, a form of love or appreciation for what someone else possesses, with resentment or envy, a form of hate which arises from not having it yourself.\n\nDrake, who has achieved immense success by typical capitalist society’s standards, amassing wealth, status, and the attention of women, understands that success often breeds envy. By acknowledging that jealousy is a mix of love and hate, he’s recognizing that people’s jealousy towards him is driven by a desire to attain the same level of success.\n\nDrake even acknowledged this perspective on 2016’s “Views”, where he candidly admitted:\n\nIf I was you, I wouldn’t like me either", "annotation_id": "32617755", "url": "https://genius.com/32617755", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Oh well, guess you lose some and win some Long as the outcome is income", "vault_lines": ["Oh well, guess you lose some and win some", "Long as the outcome is income"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that if you lose something it’s OK as long as you get money from it (common Drake W)", "annotation_id": "30722595", "url": "https://genius.com/30722595", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "And I don’t ever be trippin’ off of what ain’t mine", "vault_lines": ["And I don't ever be trippin' off of what ain't mine"], "meaning": "Real lyrics!!", "annotation_id": "29812927", "url": "https://genius.com/29812927", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "And shout out to the bitches there when it’s bedtime", "vault_lines": ["And shout out to the bitches there when it's bedtime"], "meaning": "In Drizzys early works, one of the main themes that comes up time and time again is his sex addiction and experiences with women. Drake is giving a shoutout to the women who are there to sexually comfort him before he goes to bed.", "annotation_id": "25254911", "url": "https://genius.com/25254911", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Don’t Michael Jordan still got his hoop earring in?", "vault_lines": ["Don't Michael Jordan still got his hoop earring in?"], "meaning": "Drake is comparing himself to MJ during the his prime – at the height of Jordan’s popularity, one of the things that he was very famous for, was his single hoop earring, which was a particularly fashion forward statement piece in the 1990s.\n\n\n\nDrake is calling himself the GOAT with the statement and asking why critics would think he’s lost his touch.", "annotation_id": "24416466", "url": "https://genius.com/24416466", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Like this was my comeback season back, back in the day", "vault_lines": ["Like this was my comeback season back, back in the day"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Drake’s 2007 mixtape Comeback Season. It pairs with the previous line about bandwagoners.", "annotation_id": "15139678", "url": "https://genius.com/15139678", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "But shout out to the niggas that’s doin’ dead time", "vault_lines": ["But shout out to the niggas that's doin' dead time"], "meaning": "Dead time is time in jail.", "annotation_id": "15139674", "url": "https://genius.com/15139674", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "My city love me like Mac Dre in the Bay", "vault_lines": ["My city love me like Mac Dre in the Bay"], "meaning": "Mac Dre is a legendary rapper representing the city of Vallejo in the northern region of the San Francisco Bay Area. From maintaining his status as an independent artist throughout his career and recording albums from a prison pay phone to being a pioneer of the Thizz Movement and personification of the Hyphy Movement prior to his passing in 2004, Mac Dre had a major impact on the substance and style of the Bay Area hip-hop culture—as Bay Area rapper G-Eazy discussed in a 2014 interview with HipHopDX:\n\nMac Dre means everything to Hip-Hop in the Bay. Mac Dre is more than a legend. He just had such a big like catalog of these mixtapes and albums. He had so much music. So it was like, when Mac Dre really, really, really caught on, which was around the time he died unfortunately, that was when everyone really got swept up into it. He was the biggest force in the Bay.\n\nDrake also paid respect to Dre on “The Motto,” which appears later in the Take Care tracklist—in 2016, Dre’s mom told Rolling Stone how these shout-outs inspired her to make a documentary on her son’s life:\n\nI met with Drake in San Francisco and we sat down and he talked with me about how Mac Dre impacted his life and his career, what he’s doing and the big impact that Mac Dre had when Drake was a young boy. So listening to that, it kind of opened up my eyes that maybe I do need to do this. If Andre was alive he would definitely be capitalizing and making as much money as he could.\n\nWhether it be Toronto City Councillors supporting Drake during the beef he had with Meek Mill, or Toronto radio stations playing him more than any other artist, Toronto always gives love back to Drake—just like Bay Area hip-hop fans supported Mac Dre.\n\n\n\nDrake with Toronto Politician Norm Kelly, a supporter of Drake during the Meek x Drake beef", "annotation_id": "12890602", "url": "https://genius.com/12890602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Feel like I’ve been here before, huh? I still got ten years to go, huh?", "vault_lines": ["Feel like I've been here before, huh?", "I still got ten years to go, huh?"], "meaning": "Drake’s no stranger when it comes to fame. By his second year of entering the mainstream, he already dropped several chart topping singles.\n\nDrake has logged over 430 weeks—-over 8 years!—on the billboard hot 100. Although his streak has ended recently, he still feels as if he “still got 10 years to go”.", "annotation_id": "12702981", "url": "https://genius.com/12702981", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake performed bits and pieces of this verse (and the last two lines of the first verse) prior to this song’s release on New Years Eve 2011 when he performed a variation of “Fancy” in L.A.", "annotation_id": "843017", "url": "https://genius.com/843017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "How I’m feeling, it doesn’t matter ’Cause you know I’m okay Instead, I ask myself, “Why do you worry?” When you know, you know I’m the same I know, I know you don’t love me, baby They’re trying to take you away from me Only over my dead body", "vault_lines": ["How I'm feeling, it doesn't matter", "'Cause you know I'm okay", "Instead, I ask myself, \"Why do you worry?\"", "Oh, I know you don't love me, baby", "When you know, you know I'm the same", "I know, I know you don't love me, baby", "They're trying to take you away from me", "Only over my dead body"], "meaning": "This hook draws on two different stories. On the one hand, it it is easily interpreted as one lover talking to another lover about the stability of their relationship. This story is drawn to into parallel with Drake’s position in the Hip-Hop industry. People “trying to take position in hip hop away from Drake” won’t ever be able to take his power away.\n\n“Over My Dead Body “is definitely a statement, an opening statement. It has this haunting voice, a woman’s voice on that sample, which is Chantal Kreviazuka, a Canadian singer, and she says, “They’re trying to take this away from me / Over my dead body.” The verses from that, originally I rapped them at a live show. And there’s a YouTube clip of it that some people have seen. You can actually locate those old verses. It’s just a statement, man. It’s saying I’m not going anywhere. You can’t pry it out of my hands right now.", "annotation_id": "655590", "url": "https://genius.com/655590", "state": "accepted", "votes": 190, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "And this is the only sound you should fear", "vault_lines": ["And this is the only sound you should fear"], "meaning": "This line refers to the unique hip hop sound coming out of Toronto right now, popularized by Drizzy, The Weeknd, and producer Noah “40” Shebib.\n\n\n\nAs far as Drake is concerned, the only people that his competitors should be worried about are the upcoming artists from his home city.", "annotation_id": "620288", "url": "https://genius.com/620288", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "And I met your baby moms last night We took a picture together, I hope she frames it", "vault_lines": ["And I met your baby moms last night", "We took a picture together, I hope she frames it"], "meaning": "Being famous, Drake meets a lot of women and they all want pictures usually they frame it because he’s Drake. But in this case he’s saying it so she could piss her man off who is probably a hater.\n\n\n\nWhen Drake performed “Fancy” last year at Dick Clark’s New Year’s Eve, he rapped these last seven lines as an extra verse.", "annotation_id": "475441", "url": "https://genius.com/475441", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Oh, I know you don’t love me, baby They’re trying to take you away from me Only over my dead body", "vault_lines": ["Oh, I know you don't love me, baby", "I know, I know you don't love me, baby", "They're trying to take you away from me", "Only over my dead body"], "meaning": "The chorus is meant to be a conversation with Drake and “fame.” The whole song tracks his experience with “fame” since Thank Me Later dropped.\n\nAt times, people said that he “fell off” or regressed, and the chorus demonstrates Drake’s uncertainty about his fame moving forward. (Drake is probably the most polarizing figure in the rap game.)\n\nHe eventually says that, although “fame” doesn’t love him, he will not let it go until he dies (over his dead body).", "annotation_id": "458456", "url": "https://genius.com/458456", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "You say I’m old news, well, who the new star? ‘Cause if I’m goin’ anywhere, it’s probably too far", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you know I'm okay", "You say I'm old news, well, who the new star?", "'Cause if I'm goin' anywhere, it's probably too far"], "meaning": "If Drake is over, who has taken his place? It’s hard to point to someone. J.Cole’s debut LP was highly anticipated but divided critics in a way that Thank Me Later didn’t.  Other than Cole, no other new rappers have come close to Drake’s sales or airplay.\n\nIn terms of popularity, Drake is holding his own with rap’s legends, guys like Eminem, Jay-Z, and Kanye West.\n\nAlso, an allusion to So Far Gone the mixtape that introduced Drake to the world.\n\nIt’s also something of an allusion, thematically, to these lines from Kanye’s All of the Lights.", "annotation_id": "446261", "url": "https://genius.com/446261", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "[Intro: Chantal Kreviazuk]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Chantal is a well-known Canadian singer.  She was extremely happy to work with Drizzy:\n\nI am thrilled because the creative process was beautiful. I feel privileged to have lent my voice and melodic take on his fancy footwork.\n\n\n\nThe reason why I am so excited about this song is because I just love this song. I love how Drake is all about Toronto and all about Canada. Amidst all of this stardom, there is such a humility.", "annotation_id": "438439", "url": "https://genius.com/438439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 221, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Yeah, don’t make me take your life apart, boy You and whoever the fuck gave you your start, boy", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, don't make me take your life apart, boy", "You and whoever the fuck gave you your start, boy", "Don't make me break your Kevin Hart, boy"], "meaning": "Drake will destroy you and whoever made you “famous”…\n\nSome feel that this is a subliminal directed at Big Sean and his mentor Kanye.  Sean has been very public about his belief that Drake borrowed his so-called “Supa Dupa flow” style. This belief is backed by Drakes use of the word “boy,” something that Sean is known for.\n\n\n\nDrake and Sean, however, both deny that there are any problems between them.", "annotation_id": "437361", "url": "https://genius.com/437361", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "And I was drinkin’ at the Palms last night And ended up losin’ everything that I came with, yeah", "vault_lines": ["And I was drinkin' at the Palms last night", "And ended up losin' everything that I came with, yeah"], "meaning": "He either means he lost all his money buying drinks, lost it at the tables (Palms is a Vegas casino owned by the Maloof brothers, owner of the Sacramento Kings), or lost his lady friend and/or his clothes – or possibly all three!", "annotation_id": "436236", "url": "https://genius.com/436236", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Man, these kids wear crowns over here and everything is alright", "vault_lines": ["Man, these kids wear crowns over here and everything is alright"], "meaning": "Drake is a big fan of Maurice Sendak’s 1963 classic children’s book Where the Wild Things Are, which was evident at the time as it was his account location on Twitter.\n\n\n\nMax, the main character of the story, is a child who wears a crown. By comparing himself to Max, Drake’s claiming to be a king, like he did again on “Lord Knows”.\n\n\n\nPerhaps unrelated, but there’s also a Canadian rock band called These Kids Wear Crowns.", "annotation_id": "435997", "url": "https://genius.com/435997", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Just performed at a bar mitzvah over in the States Used half of the money to beat my brother’s case Red wine over fed time", "vault_lines": ["Just performed at a bar mitzvah over in the States", "Used half of the money to beat my brother's case", "Red wine over fed time"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to Raynford “P. Reign” Humphrey, Toronto rapper and close friend, who just beat his court case against weapons charges back in May.\n\nDrake is Jewish himself and had a Bar Mitzvah. He earned 250k performing at former NBC exec Jeff Zucker’s son’s Bar Mitzvah.", "annotation_id": "435797", "url": "https://genius.com/435797", "state": "accepted", "votes": 101, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Are these people really discussin’ my career again? Askin’ if I’ll be goin’ platinum in a year again?", "vault_lines": ["Are these people really discussin' my career again?", "Askin' if I'll be goin' platinum in a year again?"], "meaning": "Drizzy here addresses the inevitable haters, who wonder if he was a one-album wonder – in hip-hop, not an unreasonable expectation.\n\nBut seeing as Take Care went platinum in 2 months, Drizzy doesn’t have to worry about being forgotten any time soon.", "annotation_id": "435766", "url": "https://genius.com/435766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "I mean you ain’t the only real nigga They got me on these white women like Seal, nigga Slave to the pussy, but I’m just playin’ the field, nigga, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, you know me well, nigga, yeah", "I mean you ain't the only real nigga", "They got me on these white women like Seal, nigga", "Slave to the pussy, but I'm just playin' the field, nigga, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake keeps going with the wordplay here:\n\nSeal Henry Olusegun Olumide Adeola Samuel, a black British singer who performs as Seal, was formerly married to German model Heidi Klum\n\n\n\nDrake plays on the historical image of the black buck and other Antebellum tropes to pun on the word “slave” and the term “field Negro”, which was best explained by Malcolm X in his famous speech:\n\nThe field negro, those were the masses. There was always more negros in the field as there were negros in the house. There negro in the field caught hell. He ate leftovers. In the house, they ate high up on the hog. The negro in the field didn’t get nothing but what was left in the insides of the hog. They call them chit'lins nowaday. In those days, they called them what they were, guts! That’s what you were, a guteater. And some of you are still guteaters. The field negro was beaten, from morning til night. He lived in a shack, in a hut. He wore cast-off clothes. He hated his master.", "annotation_id": "435754", "url": "https://genius.com/435754", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Shout out to Asian girls, let the lights dim some", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to Asian girls, let the lights dim some"], "meaning": "A nod here to Drake’s hometown of Toronto and its large Asian population – about 70% of Toronto’s minorities are of Asian ancestry.  The pun here is, of course, on the popular Chinese dumpling dish.\n\n\n\nDrake seems to be channeling his inner Childish Gambino.", "annotation_id": "435746", "url": "https://genius.com/435746", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Shout out to all my niggas livin’ tax-free Nowadays, it’s six figures when they tax me", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to all my niggas livin' tax-free", "Nowadays, it's six figures when they tax me"], "meaning": "Drizzy shouts out friends of his still caught up in the black market economy, while finding time to brag about his own wealth\n\nIf he’s getting taxed six figures even while living in a state with no state income tax, like his new home of Florida, he must be doing all right", "annotation_id": "435741", "url": "https://genius.com/435741", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "And I thought I found the girl of my dreams at the strip club Mmm-mmm, fuck it, I was wrong, though", "vault_lines": ["And I thought I found the girl of my dreams at the strip club", "Mmm-mmm, fuck it, I was wrong, though", "Man, fuck it, I was on, though"], "meaning": "These lines may refer to a woman named Maliah Michel, a dancer at Houston’s strip club Dreams who Drake allegedly started dating after shouting her out on his song “Miss Me.” Maliah also portrayed his love interest in the “Find Your Love” video.\n\nDrizzy seems to be with his inner T-Pain\n\n\n\nShoulda took advice from Kanye.", "annotation_id": "435737", "url": "https://genius.com/435737", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Second album, I’m back pavin’ the way The backpackers are back on the bandwagon", "vault_lines": ["Second album, I'm back pavin' the way", "The backpackers are back on the bandwagon"], "meaning": "He’s back with a new sound to pave the way for new artists again. And now his new movement and buzz has got the backpackers (self-proclaimed “real rap” connoisseurs) back digging his music like when he first came out and didn’t have the fame\n\nDrizzy has dealt with the backpackers' hate in song before, on these lines from his song “City is Mine”, from Room For Improvement.", "annotation_id": "435499", "url": "https://genius.com/435499", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "And I be hearin’ the shit you say through the grapevine", "vault_lines": ["And I be hearin' the shit you say through the grapevine"], "meaning": "Drake, being such a huge Marvin Gaye fan, is almost surely here making a reference to Gaye’s famous version of the song by Norman Whitfield and Barrett Strong, “I Heard It Through the Grapevine”\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hajBdDM2qdg\n\nAt the time, there were a few rappers, most notably Ludacris, who were subtly dissing Drake in various ways.", "annotation_id": "434895", "url": "https://genius.com/434895", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Oh, you wanna be a motherfuckin’ funny guy? Don’t make me break your Kevin Hart, boy", "vault_lines": ["Oh, you wanna be a motherfuckin' funny guy?", "Don't make me break your Kevin Hart, boy"], "meaning": "Kevin Hart is a famous funny guy that has hung out with Drizzy numerous times\n\n#wordplay\n\n\n\nPerhaps a response to Los’s shots at Drake on his track, “6 Foot 7 Foot (Freestyle).”", "annotation_id": "434600", "url": "https://genius.com/434600", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over My Dead Body", "line": "Man, all of your flows bore me, paint dryin’", "vault_lines": ["Man, all of your flows bore me, paint dryin'"], "meaning": "Drake uses the so-called Supa Dupa flow here in a pointed way – he criticizes people (and possibly Big Sean in the upcoming lines) who have stolen it on an album where he otherwise abandons the much criticized “hashtag rap” form. Watching paint dry is a pretty boring pastime..", "annotation_id": "434226", "url": "https://genius.com/434226", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
