{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Mad", "vault_lines": ["How you got a manager you mad", "It's because you niggas insecure, you ain't made men"], "meaning": "The lyric in actuality is “met after you got the win”.  Following the time old Drake adage, “no new friends”, Drake is highlighting his loyalty socially and monetarily to his original friends, accomplices, and business associates. Essentially, dont switch up when the money comes.  Keep your circle tight.", "annotation_id": "23868929", "url": "https://genius.com/23868929", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Snakes stay slitherin’", "vault_lines": [", snakes stay slitherin'"], "meaning": "A reference to Harry Potter. “Slytherin” is a “house” of Hogwarts in Harry Potter. A snake is seen on the Slytherin logo", "annotation_id": "23633218", "url": "https://genius.com/23633218", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Five hundred mil’ and I’ll fall back in the 6ix", "vault_lines": ["Five hundred mil' and I'll fall back in the 6ix"], "meaning": "https://www.forbes.com/sites/zackomalleygreenburg/2019/06/14/drake-net-worth-150-million-in-2019/?sh=3ec5665e1774 Fun fact, Drake’s estimated networth is 150 million dollars as reported by Forbes in 2019. Though on the other hand 2020 was his most successful year yet with outlets reporting his estimated annual income for the year to be around 49-90 million dollars for the year, so he’s well on track to become  a half billionaire.\n\nSide note, if we do the math for the maximum estimated income hes earned for 2020 at 90 million dollars hes earning $4,794.52 an hour. Thats some crazy shit Drizzy and if youre reading this donate a dollar to my poor ass.\n\n\n\n Drake with a rack or some shit.", "annotation_id": "22750811", "url": "https://genius.com/22750811", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Look, we can argue back and forth, but who’s the richer man? Isn’t that what matters in this world that we livin’ in?", "vault_lines": ["Look, we can argue back and forth, but who's the richer man?", "Isn't that what matters in this world that we livin' in?"], "meaning": "This is a subliminal message aimed towards Drake’s adversaries Kanye West and Pusha-T.\n\nDrake asserts that they can “argue back and forth” on diss tracks and discredit each other in interviews, but Drake makes it clear that he has the most money.\n\nAs of 2021, Drake is worth approximately $200 million, whereas Pusha-T is only worth $15 million, however, Kanye’s net worth is estimated in excess of $6.6 Billion.", "annotation_id": "21051298", "url": "https://genius.com/21051298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Certified lover man", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake maybe sneaked his possible new album name. Apparently he chose for a little change ‘Certified Lover Boy’.\n\nHe announced his album name in a Instagram post after the Laugh Now Cry Later release.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/CD2wdoZlWQ6/?igshid=1017x66da6ie9", "annotation_id": "20605379", "url": "https://genius.com/20605379", "state": "pending", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Thirty-three mil’, I’ll save that for the rain", "vault_lines": ["Thirty-three mil', I'll save that for the rain"], "meaning": "A slight nod to his 2011 hit “The Motto”, Drake drops a similar line in that song when he says “25 sitting on 25 mil' ”.\n\nNow at 33, his wealth has since grown immensely — no longer bragging about just having millions that match his age, he has enough that he could comfortably set aside 33 million as purely emergency or “rain check” money.", "annotation_id": "20030938", "url": "https://genius.com/20030938", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "But you never listen, so you niggas goin’ wood again", "vault_lines": ["But you never listen, so you niggas goin' wood again"], "meaning": "As defined by Urban Dictionary, “wood,” in the context of the music industry, is used to describe a record that has flopped — this is in comparison to Platinum or Gold records, materials far more valuable than wood.\n\nThis is also a double entendre, in which the people he is referring to are saying “would” again. In the next line, he says “talkin' ‘bout what niggas coulda been, shoulda been”, completing the classic line “could’ve, would’ve should’ve”, indicating the regret of the people who are weighed down by inaction.\n\nPotentially another reference to his 2011 hit “The Motto,” where Lil Wayne raps a similar line:\n\nWish a nigga wood like a tree in this bitch", "annotation_id": "20030926", "url": "https://genius.com/20030926", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Brought a few Ws to the 6ix", "vault_lines": ["Brought a few Ws to the 6ix"], "meaning": "“W” is an abbreviation for a win. Drake, being a Toronto native, has bought major buzz to the city. A June 2018 segment of Vice News Tonight reported that Drake’s influence and promotion of “the 6ix” added an estimated $440 million to the city’s $8.8 billion tourism in 2017.\n\nDrake’s also a global ambassador of the Toronto’s Raptors and has been one since 2013 when he and his brand OVO announced a partnership with the Raptors.", "annotation_id": "19175959", "url": "https://genius.com/19175959", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "[Outro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song’s original outro was sampled from a February 19, 2019 episode of Entertainment Report Podcast.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fQtbG-RZk2o\n\nThe outro was removed from the song once it was released on streaming services as part of Drake’s Dark Lane Demo Tapes.", "annotation_id": "19175940", "url": "https://genius.com/19175940", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Always move right so my watch is on the other hand", "vault_lines": ["Always move right so my watch is on the other hand"], "meaning": "Contrary to the line, Drake always wears his watch on his right hand which is his dominant arm. Most watches have a crown on the right-hand side, so to avoid damages it is conventionally worn on left-hand.\n\n\n\nThis line also subtly references his 2015 track “Right Hand” as well:\n\nYou’re my right hand, you’re my go to\nTold me everything about you\nThat’s a bold move\nI know that you’re out here\n\nSome fans have pointed out that by wearing his watch on the right-hand Drake pays homage to Canadian ice-hockey player, Tim Horton when he scored the winning goal in the Spengler Cup and tapped to his right hand and asked the crowd “What time is it?”\n\n\n\nTim Horton, playing for the Toronto Maple Leafs, sitting in the penalty box at Madison Square Garden", "annotation_id": "19174246", "url": "https://genius.com/19174246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Michael Jackson shit, but the palace is not for kids", "vault_lines": ["Michael Jackson shit, but the palace is not for kids"], "meaning": "Drake has an extravagant $100 million dollar mansion that’s located in his hometown of Toronto, Canada. Dubbed “The Embassy,” the 50,000-square-foot property houses an indoor swimming pool, an NBA regulation-size basketball court, a recording studio, among other things. Drake’s mansion can be seen in the jointed-music video for “When To Say When” and “Chicago Freestyle,” as well as the music video for his April 2020 song, “Toosie Slide.” Additionally, when describing the house, Drake told Architectural Digest:\n\nIt’s overwhelming high luxury […] That message is delivered through the size of the rooms and the materials and details of the floors and the ceilings. I wanted to make sure people can see the work I’ve put in over the years reflected from every vantage point.\n\nHe compares “The Embassy” to Michael Jackson’s Neverland Ranch estate that’s located in California. Jackson first purchased the property in 1988 for a disclosed amount of money. He often invited children to visit and stay at the Ranch—which would eventually result in the infamous child molestation accusations against Jackson, which claimed he assaulted children inside of the home.\n\n\n\nDrake’s dubbed “The Embassy”", "annotation_id": "19174178", "url": "https://genius.com/19174178", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Five hundred weeks, I’ll fill the charts with my pain", "vault_lines": ["Five hundred weeks, I'll fill the charts with my pain"], "meaning": "In 2017, Drake was asked by the paparazzo how it felt to have been on the Billboard Hot 100 for 431 weeks straight. Drake replied saying, “I need 500”.", "annotation_id": "19173969", "url": "https://genius.com/19173969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "6 God, prayin’ hands like I’m a religious man", "vault_lines": ["6 God, prayin' hands like I'm a religious man"], "meaning": "Drake references his 2014 track, “6 God,” off of his project If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, a title of which Drake soon after found himself being nicknamed. The song’s cover art includes praying hands alongside the 6:\n\n\n\nThe 6 denotes Toronto and Ontario, Canada, Drake’s hometown.\n\nDrake has since tattooed the iconic logo on his right forearm and further elaborated on it’s meaning to him in a 2011 interview with Tim Westwood:\n\nI always talk to the big guy upstairs every night […] [This inspired it]", "annotation_id": "19173932", "url": "https://genius.com/19173932", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Baby mama fluke, but I love her for who she is", "vault_lines": ["Baby mama fluke, but I love her for who she is"], "meaning": "In May 2018, it became public knowledge that Drake had a child when Pusha T dropped the track “The Story of Adidon” amidst the pair’s infamous feud. Drake’s son, Adonis Graham, was actually born in the previous calendar year, 2017, on October 11th.\n\nDrake hadn’t planned on having a child and was initially sceptical as to whether the kid was his. The mother of Drake’s son, French retired adult film star Sophie Brussaux, alleged that, upon discovering of her pregnancy, Drake sent her text messages requesting her to have an abortion. More information on Drake and Sophie’s relationship can be seen here.\n\nDespite the pregnancy not having been planned, Drake has come to appreciate Brussaux and is fond of her as a person.\n\nUpon the release of “When To Say When,” this line was met with criticism, with fans viewing the line as “disrespectful” towards Brussaux.", "annotation_id": "19173897", "url": "https://genius.com/19173897", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "(June, you’re a genius)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the producer tag of June James, an American hip-hop producer based out of Houston who has worked with the likes of Travis Scott and YoungBoy Never Broke Again. “When To Say When” marks James and Drake’s first collaborative effort.\n\nThe tag plays off of James' producer alias of “June, The Genius.”", "annotation_id": "19173858", "url": "https://genius.com/19173858", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "I watched Michael Rubin win a million off a couple hands", "vault_lines": ["I watched Michael Rubin win a million off a couple hands"], "meaning": "Michael Rubin is an American businessman, who, at the time of this track’s release, is known best for co-owning the Philadelphia 76ers and being on the executive chairman of sports apparel company Fanatics.\n\nDrake’s hometown and favorite NBA team, the Toronto Raptors, fought of the 76ers in an intense seven-game series in the 2019 Eastern Conference Semifinals. However, after that, Rubin and Drake did not seem to have much bad blood, as the two were seen gambling together in a Bahamian casino after the Raptors' Eastern Conference Finals win.", "annotation_id": "19173811", "url": "https://genius.com/19173811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Word to Sandra Graham, no one love you like your mother can", "vault_lines": ["Word to Sandra Graham, no one love you like your mother can"], "meaning": "Drake flips the famous saying about a parent’s unconditional love to express his gratitude to his single mother, Sandra Graham. He famously devoted his February 2015 track, “You & The 6” to his mother, thanking her for her kindness and for her guidance:\n\nYou and the six raised me right, that shit saved my life\n\nOn his January 2018 single, “God’s Plan,” Drake once again mentioned his love for his mother:\n\nShe say, “Do you love me?” I tell her, “Only partly\nI only love my bed and my mama, I’m sorry”", "annotation_id": "19173779", "url": "https://genius.com/19173779", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Ayy Thirty-three years, I gave that to the game", "vault_lines": ["Thirty-three years, I gave that to the game"], "meaning": "At the time of the release of “When To Say When,” Drake, who was born on October 24, 1986, was 33 years old.\n\nHe uses this line to indicate that he has given his life to the game, with all of the years spent on the Earth spent honing his craft in the rap game.", "annotation_id": "19173735", "url": "https://genius.com/19173735", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "line": "Baby, Wayne and Tez responsible for this shit", "vault_lines": ["Baby, Wayne and Tez responsible for this shit"], "meaning": "“Baby” is a nickname for Birdman, “Tez” is a nickname for Cortez Bryant, Drake’s former manager and Lil Wayne’s a close friend and frequent collaborator of Drake. He’s also signed with Young Money, Wayne’s record label.\n\nIn a December 2019 interview with Rap Radar, Drake discussed this specific line:\n\nWell you know, obviously my loyalty lies with the people that gave me the platform – my first shot. So that’s really where that line comes from. Despite how things may have ended up, I’m always so grateful for those three people selflessly giving me a chance.", "annotation_id": "19173720", "url": "https://genius.com/19173720", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "When To Say When", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 22, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-when-to-say-when-lyrics", "genius_title": "When To Say When"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Got us watchin’ our words like it’s wiretaps on the team", "vault_lines": ["Got us watchin' our words like it's wiretaps on the team"], "meaning": "Drake’s popularity has him at the center of the public attention. Now he needs to watch his words closely and has to assume anything wrong he says could lead to the public opinion to turn against him. This is alike to someone speaking carefully as their conversation is recorded", "annotation_id": "31157434", "url": "https://genius.com/31157434", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "But if I stay, then this shit is a bunch of corpses", "vault_lines": ["But if I stay, then this shit is a bunch of corpses"], "meaning": "If Drake stays in the rap game, he is so talented that he is going to metaphorically “kill” all of the other rappers careers, leaving a bunch of corpses.", "annotation_id": "27092513", "url": "https://genius.com/27092513", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Without me, rap is just a bunch of orphans", "vault_lines": ["Without me, rap is just a bunch of orphans"], "meaning": "Drake is sonning the rap game in this line. The term “son” somebody is to claim you have respect and/or authority over somebody like a father does to their son. Now, Drake is saying that if he stops rapping or never came into the game, then rap is without inspiration. The rap game can no longer be “sonned” like it is when Drake is here, it is “fatherless”.", "annotation_id": "20725901", "url": "https://genius.com/20725901", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "And me and my dread nigga from New Orleans Stashin’ money like hoarders off multi-platinum recordings", "vault_lines": ["And me and my dread nigga from New Orleans", "Stashin' money like hoarders off multi-platinum recordings"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is Drake’s mentor who originates from New Orleans and is known for his iconic dreadlocks. Their professional relationship began when Lil Wayne signed Drake to Young Money Entertainment in 2009, significantly boosting Drake’s career and helping to establish him as a major figure in the music industry.\n\nDrizzy emphasizes the massive financial success they have achieved together through their music careers. By comparing their wealth accumulation to hoarders, Drake underscores the substantial amounts of money they earn and save from their plethora of platinum recordings. Both artists have contributed significantly to the financial success of Young Money Cash Money (YMCMB) with their numerous hit records, essentially stashing money together as they dominate the charts. This reflects not only their individual successes but also their collaborative impact on the label’s prosperity.\n\nLil Wayne and Drake", "annotation_id": "19565428", "url": "https://genius.com/19565428", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Bitches lovin’ my drive, I never give it a break", "vault_lines": ["Bitches lovin' my drive, I never give it a break"], "meaning": "Drizzy uses a clever triple entendre to convey multiple meanings, showcasing his ambition, continuous hustle, and sexual prowess.\n\nFirstly, Drake’s relentless work ethic and determination are qualities he claims assist in attracting women. These traits are often found appealing because, from an evolutionary perspective, they can be seen as indicators of a good provider. This ties into humans' fundamental desire for survival and security.\n\nSecondly, Drake plays on the homonym “drive,” referring to driving a car, and the homophone “break,” referring to brakes on a car. By using the metaphor of driving with no brakes, he illustrates his constant and unstoppable movement towards his goals.\n\nThirdly, Drizzy plays on the homonym “drive” again, this time referring to his sex drive. By saying he never gives it a break, Drake hints at his high libido and constant sexual activity, which he suggests is adored by his partners.\n\nDrake grinding in the studio", "annotation_id": "18392901", "url": "https://genius.com/18392901", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Everything’s lookin’ gorgeous", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "When you make the type of money Drake makes and have the success he has, then yeah everything looks good. Literally everything Drizzy touches turns to gold.", "annotation_id": "17818826", "url": "https://genius.com/17818826", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Some niggas been here for a couple, never been here again", "vault_lines": ["Some niggas been here for a couple, never been here again"], "meaning": "Drake here is referring to older rappers who had a singular good run or album and are still hanging on to the relevance they gained from said album without producing top tier material ever again.\nThis specifically could be aimed at Common who he had beef with at the time this freestyle was originally dropped in 2013, since Common has not really put out much notable music since about 2008, but is still regarded with respect due to his contribution and rejuvenation of the soulful MC.", "annotation_id": "17818715", "url": "https://genius.com/17818715", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Damn, this shit could go on a tape", "vault_lines": ["Damn, this shit could go on a tape"], "meaning": "Drake dropped “5AM” purely for fans, not placing the track as a single or future album credit. Even on his throw-away song, though, he sounds like a legend. Just because Drake pushes it out for free does not mean that he slacks.\n\nThis also refers back to the girl. Drake’s sex with her is good enough to make it to tape, looking more like porn than your everyday couple. They look good.\n\n\n\nIn 2019, “5AM In Toronto” was officially released on streaming services as part of Drizzy’s Care Package, a compilation album of loose tracks that didn’t make it onto any of his projects.\n\n\nCare Package cover art", "annotation_id": "1565573", "url": "https://genius.com/1565573", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Yeah This on some old Tommy Campos Dice Raw shit, you know? For my niggas, though", "vault_lines": ["This on some old Tommy Campos Dice Raw shit, you know?", "For my niggas, though", "This shit for Karim, this shit for Jaevon"], "meaning": "Thomas Campos and Dice “The City Of Godz” Raw are members of the ALIFE New York family, and friends of Drake.\n\nThey go way back. Drake shouts them out on his album Thank Me Later’s liner notes.", "annotation_id": "1562648", "url": "https://genius.com/1562648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "So they never take shots, I got everybody on safety", "vault_lines": ["So they never take shots, I got everybody on safety"], "meaning": "Drake feels like he has the whole rap game shook to say something negative about him, and so they’re quiet and taking no shots like a gun on safety.\n\nThis track is released in the cultural context of a fierce public debate about gun control taking place in the U.S. in 2012-2013.\n\n\n\nBuilding off the preceding lines, artists within the industry won’t make the negative remarks about him publicly as they do in private due to their need for him.", "annotation_id": "1562193", "url": "https://genius.com/1562193", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "This shit for Ezee and Coco This shit for Karim, this shit for Jaevon This shit for Julius, Milly Mill, boy, we do this shit for real", "vault_lines": ["this shit for Ezee and Coco", "This shit for Karim, this shit for Jaevon", "This shit for Julius, Milly Mill, boy, we do this shit for real"], "meaning": "A shoutout to his OVO family. Ezee and Coco are Drake’s friend Nikko’s daughter and baby mama. “My friends are having babies…”", "annotation_id": "1562111", "url": "https://genius.com/1562111", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Nothing Was The Same,", "vault_lines": ["Nothing Was The Same,"], "meaning": "“Nothing Was The Same” is the title of Drizzy’s 3rd studio album, to be released sometime in 2013.\n\nHe revealed the album title to Ryan Seacrest on the red carpet at the 2013 Grammys.\n\nhttp://youtu.be/hx7TcxXmw8o\n\nFrom his Twitter account we learned that he’s wrote the album list, or at least some of it and therefore likely to have some of the songs already recorded.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/307392645603405824", "annotation_id": "1562002", "url": "https://genius.com/1562002", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Eatin’ like I’m seated at Swish, Sotto, and Joso’s", "vault_lines": ["Eatin' like I’m seated at Swish, Sotto, and Joso's"], "meaning": "Swish, Sotto, and Joso’s are three restaurants in Toronto. They specialize in Italian and seafood. They are frequented by celebrities. Many have tweeted about seeing Drake eating at these hometown restaurants.\n\nAlso, Joso’s is where Drake shot his album cover for Take Care.", "annotation_id": "1561951", "url": "https://genius.com/1561951", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Comin’ live from the screwface, livin’ out a suitcase", "vault_lines": ["Comin' live from the screwface, livin' out a suitcase"], "meaning": "“I’m from the T dot O, yeah, the Screw Face Capital”\n-Theology3\n\nDrake is shouting out his hometown Toronto in this line. The Tdot is infamously known as the Screw Face Capital for having one of the most unsupportive crowds for upcoming artists in North America.\n\nToronto MC Theology 3 first used this nickname back in 2000\n\nThe second half of the line is reffering to the tour lifestyle, where artists have to bring everything they need with them", "annotation_id": "1561944", "url": "https://genius.com/1561944", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Guess it’s funny how money can make change outta niggas", "vault_lines": ["Guess it's funny how money can make change outta niggas"], "meaning": "Wordplay on the word “change.” The literal meaning being a person changing, or change (coin), which would be a person making money. This alludes to the notion that once the money and fame hits people change.\n\nWhen Drake notes that money can “make change outta niggas” he is also implying that money turns people into a less person than what they were in the sense that relative to “money,” “change” is very small and minimal in value. It makes change out of them in the sense that they are reduced in overall value.\n\nIn addition, Drake has rapped a similar line on his debut EP So Far Gone on the song “Fear”:\n\nIt’s funny how money can change everything", "annotation_id": "1561939", "url": "https://genius.com/1561939", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Fuck them niggas this year,", "vault_lines": ["Fuck them niggas this year,"], "meaning": "This is directed towards the artists' who were ranked ahead of him on the MTV’s “Hottest MC’s” list, he feels they should not be ahead of him in any way, because he believes he is the hottest rapper out right now. Forget the other artists doing publicity stunts, this year Drake is going to put out some real music.", "annotation_id": "1561932", "url": "https://genius.com/1561932", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "All them boys in my will,", "vault_lines": ["All them boys in my will,", "all them boys is my Wills"], "meaning": "Drake’s always been big on family and the guys that came up with him. He and his boys are honest in their music, what they rap about is what they really do. He’s got his OVO boys for life and even has them named in his will.\nA will is a legal declaration by which a person names one or more persons to manage his/her estate and provides for the transfer of his property at death. Like Caesar, Drake is leaving it all behind to his empire.", "annotation_id": "1561924", "url": "https://genius.com/1561924", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "A couple albums dropped, those are still on the shelf I bet them shits would have popped if I was willin’ to help", "vault_lines": ["A couple albums dropped, those are still on the shelf", "I bet them shits would have popped if I was willin' to help"], "meaning": "Drake nods to other rappers' albums that have flopped in recent years. If Drake had been willing to give them a guest verse or two, they would’ve got more hype behind them and undoubtedly sold more units.\n\nThis could also be a shot at all the artists that have dissed him, like GOOD Music (notably Common) and Chris Brown, who had underwhelming album sales and both got into “beef” with Drake before their album release. It could also be a shot at DMX, whose album dropped to poor sales. Coincidentally, X dissed Drake before the album dropped.\n\n\n\nThis line is a reference to The Weeknd and how his release ‘The Trilogy’ is out but no one is buying it. If The Weeknd had stuck with the OVOXO movement his albums would probably have sold more than the 500,000 they have to date. The fact that the Weeknd was basically reissuing the exact same music on Trilogy, with only a few new additions, modifications, and remixes (as well as some subtractions; “What You Need” lost a crucial Aaliyah sample because of clearance issues) was supposedly because of pressure from Warner, the major label to which he signed. In the hands of OVO, the Weeknd could have pressed forward with new projects without such pressure. At the very least, if they had wanted to re-release the tapes, the group could’ve remixed/added a bunch of features from other OVO artists like Drizz.", "annotation_id": "1561920", "url": "https://genius.com/1561920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "For real, some nobody started feelin’ himself", "vault_lines": ["For real, some nobody started feelin' himself"], "meaning": "That would likely be The Weeknd.  The shots are for the Drake protege opting to sign with Universal rather than OVO after having hyped up the OVO+XO partnership in his Club Paradise tour\n\nThis could also be a reference to the YMCMB beef with Pusha-T. Pusha’s 2011 Fear God II mixtape had a track called “Feeling Myself”.\n\nIn addition to that, in his Take Care bonus track “The Ride” he mentioned some pretty self explanatory introduction words:\n\nI hate when people say they feel me man, I hate that shit. It’ll be a long time before yall feel me, if ever\n\nIt should be noted that at the 2:00 mark Drake says these lines weren’t aimed at The Weeknd, but we all know how that is… https://soundcloud.com/missinfotv/drake-on-evr-radio-part-3", "annotation_id": "1561911", "url": "https://genius.com/1561911", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Give these niggas the look, the verse, and even the hook", "vault_lines": ["Give these niggas the look, the verse, and even the hook"], "meaning": "Drake has been on numerous hits the past couple years. Due to his versatility as an artist he can sing the hook (Love Me – Lil Wayne), rap the verse (Pop That – French Montana), and on occasion write the song (Your Type – Jamie Foxx). Or all three.\n\nHe’s reinforcing why rappers need him more than they hate him. This seems to be referencing the line about how all rappers need him to be successful. Drake has been doing loads of features recently, boosting up many careers.\n\nDrake is also suggesting that he has laid the template for a successful rap song style & rap career, that is why the next line explains how it appears as if every rapper is mimicking his style, in order to remain commercially successful.", "annotation_id": "1561907", "url": "https://genius.com/1561907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "My weight up, I refused to wait up, I started a new race", "vault_lines": ["My weight up, I refused to wait up, I started a new race"], "meaning": "Drake is getting his weight up, physically getting stronger, and also getting richer.\n\nHe’s not going to sit around and wait for other artists to get to his level, he’s going to keep going.\n\nDrake is also claiming to have started a new style of music and therefore separating himself from other artists.\n\n“Don’t you know that I run this place and I’ve begun this race”. Saying this sets him up for the next line when he does mention Lupe.", "annotation_id": "1561899", "url": "https://genius.com/1561899", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Owl sweaters inside her luggage, you gotta love it", "vault_lines": ["Owl sweaters inside her luggage, you gotta love it"], "meaning": "The OVO merchandise is flying off the shelves.\n\n\n\nGirls going on holiday or just travelling round the world have his sweaters inside their suitcase.\n\nIn the previous line we speculated that he could be having secret meetings with Rihanna so his OVO sweater could be inside her suitcase as she jets around the world.\n\n\n\nCould also be a reference to this line from his song, The Calm.", "annotation_id": "1561896", "url": "https://genius.com/1561896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "It’s funny when you think a nigga blew up after Lupe", "vault_lines": ["It's funny when you think a nigga blew up after Lupe"], "meaning": "While Drake has the swagger and sales of a long-time artist, he did not come into fame until well after fellow artist Lupe Fiasco. Both dropped tapes in 2006, but only Lupe’s career took off instantly. Today, however, Drake is in a different league than Lupe, even with a younger career. Drake may be a post-Lupe, and thus young, rapper, but he’s a living legend.\n\n\n\nThis is NOT a shot, but the complete opposite. Drake has been a huge fan of Lupe, remixing Lupe’s Kick Push and calling him a legend. Drake is surprised that he was able to set trends after Lupe debuted in the game with Food & Liquor in 2006, hence the I started a new race in the previous line.\n\nAlso could be a reference that Drake was once an opening act for Lupe.", "annotation_id": "1561893", "url": "https://genius.com/1561893", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Why is it always me?", "vault_lines": ["Why is it always me?"], "meaning": "Probably a shoutout to his buddy Balotelli, former Manchester City striker, who he rapped about on “Enough Said”:\n\nWent from my nigga serving it by the tele to venues in Manchester\nJust swerving with Balotelli the fuck are you trying to tell me", "annotation_id": "1561878", "url": "https://genius.com/1561878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "’Cause I show love, never get the same outta niggas", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I show love, never get the same outta niggas"], "meaning": "Drake has shown love to other artists in the hip-hop community, but many have not reciprocated and shown any love back.\n\nMost notably, The Weeknd, who rejected a recording contract offered to him by Drake and YMCMB.\n\nDrake isn’t happy with this as he feels he’s done a lot to boost The Weeknd’s career such as:\n\n\nTweeting his lyrics\n\nFeaturing him on his highly successful album Take Care\n\nInviting him to perform at his OVO festival", "annotation_id": "1561864", "url": "https://genius.com/1561864", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "But I’m feelin’ good, Johnny got me pushin’ two plates", "vault_lines": ["But I'm feelin' good, Johnny got me pushin' two plates"], "meaning": "Drake is benching 225lbs (2 plates on each side):\n\n\n\n@OvOJonnyRoxx is Drake’s friend and personal trainer.", "annotation_id": "1561857", "url": "https://genius.com/1561857", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "All them boys is my Wills", "vault_lines": ["All them boys in my will,", "all them boys is my Wills"], "meaning": "This line completes the previous line as well as sets up the next line in a different way. The way Drake slightly slurs the word “wheels”/“Wills” presents two possible meanings. “Wheels” means his boys (friends, his “416 crew”) lead him in the right direction with music, business, and life.\n\n\n\nAlso, possibly indicating that by saying “Wills” he is indicating that if something were to happen to Drizzy, his boys would get all of his cash and belongings etc.\n\nAlso, “Will’s” as in Will Smith, who played The Fresh Prince of Bel Air.  This line plays on the entendre of the last line & sets up the next “Uncle Phil” line.", "annotation_id": "1561845", "url": "https://genius.com/1561845", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Niggas treat me like I’ve been here for 10", "vault_lines": ["Niggas treat me like I've been here for 10"], "meaning": "People in the music industry know Drake’s power with his hit making abilities and continued success. Because of this they treat him as if he’s been in the rap game for ten years.\n\nEven rap legend Jay-Z congratulated Drake’s 631,466 first week album sales for Take Care with a note that read:\n\n“Monster # comes in. Proud of your journey. Peace Hov”\n\n\n\nEveryone wants in on Drake’s money making music. A Drake feature will help your music career because of how popular he is, exposing you to his huge fan base.", "annotation_id": "1561837", "url": "https://genius.com/1561837", "state": "pending", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "A lot of niggas PR stuntin’ like that’s the movement And I’m the only nigga still known for the music, I swear", "vault_lines": ["A lot of niggas PR stuntin' like that's the movement", "And I'm the only nigga still known for the music, I swear"], "meaning": "A PR stunt is an act done simply to attract publicity. His rivals are only doing things to get more attention and fame, while he’s the only one who is honest in his songs.\n\nTwo specific rivals of Drake’s are Common and Chris Brown. On Stay Schemin', Drake addresses rappers who start beef just to sell records. Drake sees himself the only guy who’s proving himself with his music primarily.\n\nMeanwhile, other artists are using more creative approaches…\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R7Jkw6k54HA\n\nDrake clearly does not see his clothing line OVO Clothing as a  something getting in the way of his being known for his music, nor does he see his being a tabloid fixture for his dating habits as such. Or his making quotes of mind-bending shortsightedness. Or his beefs. Or his egotism-incited divisiveness. Or his being the-guy-who-Chris-Brown-fought-with. Or the fact that all of that is the only shit his music concerns itself with.", "annotation_id": "1561834", "url": "https://genius.com/1561834", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Weezy been on that edge, you niggas just need to chill If anything happen to Papi, might pop a nigga for real", "vault_lines": ["Weezy been on that edge, you niggas just need to chill", "If anything happen to Papi, might pop a nigga for real"], "meaning": "Wayne has had some trouble lately, calling out basketball players, getting kicked out of games, etc. So this isn’t his most stable of mindsets.\n\nDrake is warning everyone and reminding: get off his case, because if anything serious happens to Wayne, Drake might be driven to the edge.\n\nLil Wayne was arrested and incarcerated for illegal weapon possession, so it’s plausible.\n\n\n\nIn the mindstate Wayne has been in, he might lose it and shoot somebody if anything happens to “Papi” (Champagne Papi aka Drake himself).\n\nThis is a form of antanaclasis (a stylistic scheme of repeating a word in its different meanings) – first he said “pop” as in father, then “Papi” to refer to himself, then “pop” as in fire a gun.", "annotation_id": "1561831", "url": "https://genius.com/1561831", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "I could load every gun with bullets that fire backwards Probably wouldn’t lose a single rapper", "vault_lines": ["I could load every gun with bullets that fire backwards", "Probably wouldn't lose a single rapper"], "meaning": "A continuance of the gun metaphor from the previous line – Drake could give everyone a faulty gun that fires backwards (ie. into the shooter), and no one would be killed, because no one would dare try to shoot Drizzy. They need him too much.\n\n\n\nHe is also implying that other rappers are fake; even if every gun backfired and killed the shooter, no rappers would die because none of them shoot guns for real. Drake has been attacked by critics for putting on a “tough guy” or “gangster” persona at times ( Examples: “Headlines” and “Stay Schemin'”) while it is very apparent that he never has been involved in that lifestyle. Here he offloads some of that criticism to other rappers.", "annotation_id": "1561825", "url": "https://genius.com/1561825", "state": "accepted", "votes": 255, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Str8, Y pree?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Str8/Tr8” and “Y pree?” are popular slang in Jamaica and undoubtedly in Canada as well. The former is a stylized version of “straight”, often used as a term of affirmation and the latter is simply asking why people are watching the moves he makes so intently.\n\nVybz Kartel had a big part in popularising the latter phrase with “World Boss (Y Pree).”\n\n\n\nPree – To watch or study something or someone, like to stare at someone and watch their every move.", "annotation_id": "1561821", "url": "https://genius.com/1561821", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "I made Forbes list, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I made Forbes list, nigga"], "meaning": "It’s true\n\nDrake has been making Forbes top Hip-hop earners for the past couple years and his objective is the list every year. Speaking to Forbes, Drake said:\n\nEvery year, we just want to get more prepared and better at touring and better at things that make money. If I’m not on your list this year, I’d be gravely disappointed.", "annotation_id": "1561806", "url": "https://genius.com/1561806", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Fuck your list,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A shot at MTV’s hottest MC in the game list. Drake was ranked at #5 for 2013. Nobody was happy this year.\n\nKanye West, ASAP Rocky, Pusha T, J Cole and Danny Brown have all responded to their placings.\n\n\n\nThe other list that holds a lot of credence is the Forbes list. If you can make it on the Forbes list, there’s no speculation involved. Money is a fact. So if Drake can make it into Forbes he isn’t going to care about trivial top 10 rappers list.", "annotation_id": "1561805", "url": "https://genius.com/1561805", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "I mean, where you think she at when she ain’t with ya? Wildin’, doin’ shit that’s way out of your budget", "vault_lines": ["I mean, where you think she at when she ain't with ya?", "Wildin', doin' shit that's way out of your budget"], "meaning": "Drake is stealing your girl and treating her to things you could never afford to give her.\n\n\n\nPossible shot at Chris Brown who Drake’s had beef with for a while.\n\nRihanna is currently dating Chris again. However, she’s linked to Drake through music and friendship so when she’s not with Breezy, she could be jamming with Drizzy.\n\nAlso, Rihanna is the money maker in the relationship. According to Forbes, Riri made 53 million this last year and 28 mill the year before. That’s a lot more than Chris makes, so Drake could be poking fun at the fact that she’s really the provider in the relationship and she can do things not in Chris’s budget.\n\n\n\nWildin', doin shit that’s way out of your budget\n\nLike dropping 50k at a strip club..?\n\n\n\n\n\nCould also be another shot at Common, as some believed Serena Williams was the cause of the Drake/Common beef.", "annotation_id": "1561795", "url": "https://genius.com/1561795", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Sinatra lifestyle, I’m just bein’ frank with ya", "vault_lines": ["Sinatra lifestyle, I'm just bein' frank with ya"], "meaning": "Drake’s living the ultimate luxury lifestyle like Frank Sinatra used to enjoy. Sinatra was a huge influence on Drake and other R&B/hip-hop acts. Drake plays on the name Frank to be blunt, telling it like it is.\n\n\n\nThis may also be a reference to Common’s track Sweet where he disses Drake saying “You aint mothafucking Frank Sinatra”\n\nThis is also double reference to hooking up with Serena Williams (Common’s girl) and hooking up with Rihanna (Chris Brown’s girl).\n\nThe “Frank” could also be referring to Frank Ocean who allegedly got into a heated altercation with Chris Brown. This would be more evidence that this line is aimed at Breezy and Rihanna.", "annotation_id": "1561786", "url": "https://genius.com/1561786", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Bad press during the summer over allegations", "vault_lines": ["Bad press during the summer over allegations"], "meaning": "Drake allegedly got into altercation with Chris Brown in June, which included a bottle supposedly thrown by Drake at Chris Brown resulting in a cut on on Chris’s chin.", "annotation_id": "1561778", "url": "https://genius.com/1561778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "I’m on my King James shit, I’m tryna win here again", "vault_lines": ["I'm on my King James shit, I'm tryna win here again"], "meaning": "“King James” is the nickname of [Cleveland Cavaliers] forward LeBron James, who just received his first ring after taking much criticism, similar to Drake and his style of singing and rapping. Not only is Lebron looking for another championship, Drizzy himself is looking for more hardware in the form of a Grammy. This is not the first time Drake has mentioned Lebron, and it won’t be the last either.\n\nFun Fact: At the time this song was released the Miami Heat were on a franchise record 16 straight wins, and look to repeat as champions, (which they did).", "annotation_id": "1561766", "url": "https://genius.com/1561766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "The part I love most is they need me more than they hate me", "vault_lines": ["The part I love most is they need me more than they hate me"], "meaning": "According to the all insightful Drizzy Drake, most of hip-hop’s hits are Drake assisted. This pisses off artists because they supposedly can’t get a number 1 or a successful hit without him. They can’t do anything about it though, because they need that hit.\n\nDrake might have a point.\n\nIn 2012 alone, four of the eight singles Drake was featured on charted in the top five of Billboard’s Rap Songs Chart: “No Lie” with 2 Chainz (#1), “Pop That” with French Montana (#2), “Amen” with Meek Mill (#4), and “Fuckin' Problems” with A$AP Rocky (#2, also #10 on the Pop chart). (“Poetic Justice” and “Bitches Love Me” were officially released in 2013 but they also charted at #6 and #3, for your information.) Drake is dominating the charts right now and there is nothing we can do about it.\n\n\n\nOn the other hand, this may not be a diss to the artists that he collaborates with – which do need him, but never hated on him – but rather a diss to the hip-hop fans that keep on hating on him just because he represents hiphop in a different way, and even if they won’t admit it, they know his has made important contributions to hip-hop culture: singing his own hooks, introspective emotional point of view, being “real” rather than trying to pose like someone else, production influence from 40/Boi-1da/T-Minus, the fact that he’s a jewish light-skinned Canadian ex-teenage actor and its implications on what a rapper should look like.\n\ncf. Aristophones on Alcibiades: “They love, and hate, and cannot do without him.”", "annotation_id": "1561764", "url": "https://genius.com/1561764", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "I ain’t lyin’, my nigga, my time is money That’s why I ain’t got time for a nigga whose time is coming", "vault_lines": ["I ain't lyin', my nigga, my time is money", "That's why I ain't got time for a nigga whose time is coming"], "meaning": "Drake is making too much money to be wasting it beefing with rappers he believes careers are almost over.\n\nCould be shots at Weeknd who has recently started to separate from Drake, supposedly because he didn’t sign to OVO.\n\nCould also be a shot at Chris Brown. Chris’s time could be coming to an end as his record sales have declined despite having such a huge fan base.", "annotation_id": "1561761", "url": "https://genius.com/1561761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Anything happen to pop, then I got you like Uncle Phil", "vault_lines": ["Anything happen to pop, then I got you like Uncle Phil"], "meaning": "This refers to the Fresh Prince of Bel-Air. In the show, Will’s father left him and his mother while Will was young. Uncle Phil took care of Will like he was his own.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z7MKS4sM1KE\n\nSimilarly, Drake will look out for his homies in his “will,” leaving them his millions. If they lose their families or lives, he’s there to keep them afloat.", "annotation_id": "1561758", "url": "https://genius.com/1561758", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Niggas make threats, can’t hear ’em over the laughter Yeah, that’s ’cause I’m headed to the bank, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, that's 'cause I'm headed to the bank, nigga", "Niggas make threats, can't hear 'em over the laughter"], "meaning": "Since achieving unimaginable fame, the list of people taking shots at Drake is a long one. However, Drake doesn’t acknowledge them as he’s filthy rich and enjoying life.\n\nHe is referencing the phrase “laughing all the way to the bank,” as exemplified in the 50 Cent track “Straight to the Bank” where you can hear a “ha ha ha ha” throughout the song.\n\nPossible reference to Pusha T’s diss track Exodus 23:1. The production sampled The Notorious B.I.G. What’s Beef? – Drake creates a play on laughter – He can’t hear the shots/threats because he’s too busy laughing that he’s rich.", "annotation_id": "1561757", "url": "https://genius.com/1561757", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "I got a gold trophy from the committee for validation", "vault_lines": ["I got a gold trophy from the committee for validation"], "meaning": "The albums referenced in the previous line would have done better had he been involved with them. Want proof? Just ask the Grammy committee. Drake won Best Rap Album for Take Care at the Grammys. He’s got proof he’s winning.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hx7TcxXmw8o\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/300752101062103041\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAlso, Drake loved to talk about award shows, like the Grammys, earlier in his career and about how he hadn’t gotten to where he wanted to be.", "annotation_id": "1561749", "url": "https://genius.com/1561749", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "Uh, you underestimated greatly Most number ones ever, how long did it really take me?", "vault_lines": ["Uh, you underestimated greatly", "Most number ones ever, how long did it really take me?"], "meaning": "So Far Gone, Drake’s breakout mixtape dropped in 2009. In the four years that have followed Drake has had enormous success.\n\nDrake is the titleholder of both Billboard’s R&B/Hip-Hop chart and Rap chart. He recently surpassed first Diddy with his 11th No. 1 on Billboard’s Rap Songs chart and then Jay-Z for the most Number 1 Hip-Hop/R&B singles and hasn’t looked back since.\n\nBillboard’s most number-one Rap Songs\n(name, debut year – total)\n1. Drake, 2009 – 14\n2. Diddy, 1997 – 10\n3. Kanye West, 2005 – 9\n\nBillboard’s most number-one Hip-Hop/R&B singles\n1. Drake, 2009 – 10\n2. Jay-Z, 1995 – 9\n3. Lil Wayne, 1999 – 8\n\nTo answer his question…\n\nHis first charted number one song comes from the mixtape So Far Gone which was released on september 15, 2009 and the latest charted number one comes from his second studio album Take Care which was released on november 15, 2011.\n\nAnswer…2 years between the projects. Impressive!", "annotation_id": "1561747", "url": "https://genius.com/1561747", "state": "accepted", "votes": 169, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "5AM in Toronto", "line": "That’s why every song sound like Drake featurin’ Drake", "vault_lines": ["That's why every song sound like Drake featurin' Drake"], "meaning": "Even though he’s featured on others peoples songs, Drake tends to hog whatever song he’s on, getting on the hook and a verse so whatever it is it sounds like ‘Drake ft Drake’.  For example: No lie; 2 chainz ft Drake. Doesn’t actually make that much sense when you think about it…\n\nAlternately, some Drake songs feel like they have Drake as the featured artist because he’s singing on the choruses and rapping on the verses, i.e. Over off his “Thank Me Later” album.\n\nHe sings, raps, acts, and even cooks\n\n\n\nAlternately, and more likely, he is saying that even those who aren’t actually featuring Drake are copying his style and his flow.\n\nShout out to Jibbs\n\n\n\nAnother meaning could be that since Drake has a distinct sound in terms of lyricism and lyrical themes, whenever he writes a song for another artist (that he’s already featured on) it just sounds like two of him instead of the artist featuring him. Like in that Jamie Foxx song – Fall For Your Type. That’s just Drake written all over it.", "annotation_id": "1561746", "url": "https://genius.com/1561746", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
