{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "I pop out, get ghost on a bitch, she don’t know where I went (Where I go?)", "vault_lines": ["I pop out, get ghost on a bitch, she don't know where I went (Where I go?)"], "meaning": "In this line Future boast about leaving his romantic interest adruptly and suddenly. As to ghost is the act of  abruptly cutting off contact with someone without any explanation or reason", "annotation_id": "32111757", "url": "https://genius.com/32111757", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "Think we got too sexy for that metro housin’ (Way too sexy)", "vault_lines": ["Too sexy for this ice", "Too sexy for that jack, yeah, yeah", "Too sexy for your gang", "Too sexy for that cap", "Too sexy for my shirt", "Think we got too sexy for that metro housin' (Way too sexy)", "Too sexy for this cash", "Too sexy for this syrup", "I'm too sexy for Milan", "Too sexy for Milan", "Too sexy for your girl", "Too sexy for this world"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION:\n\nMetropolitan Toronto Housing Corporation was the former name of Toronto Community Housing and is still known colloquially as “metro housing”", "annotation_id": "23995543", "url": "https://genius.com/23995543", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "Section need more tings in here, I like it crowded, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Section need more tings in here, I like it crowded, ayy", "Yeah, I like it crowded"], "meaning": "Drake uses the Jamaican slang (not uncommon for Drizzy who’s used Jamican slang in his songs and album names , and Instagram posts .) ‘tings’  here to point out his club section needs more seats, because he likes it packed. ( photo source )", "annotation_id": "24340251", "url": "https://genius.com/24340251", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "My neck baguetty (Hoo) Red diamonds, spaghetti (Oh)", "vault_lines": ["My neck baguetty (Hoo)", "Red diamonds, spaghetti (Oh)"], "meaning": "Baguette is a type of precious stone cut, that has 14 facets ( a facet is one side of something that has many sides, so a 14 facet cut has 14 sides to the stone).  Red diamonds are very rare, making them one of the most expensive types of diamonds you can get – valued at a million dollars per carat.  Overall Young Thug is saying he has a baguette cut red diamond necklace, that is more than likely worth millions.", "annotation_id": "23995720", "url": "https://genius.com/23995720", "state": "pending", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "Pay attention to the detail, goin’ two-tone on a choker", "vault_lines": ["Pay attention to the detail, goin' two-tone on a choker"], "meaning": "Future is saying he is going to get/has a necklace (choker) that is two-tone.  Jewelers worldwide call any type of jewelry that contains two types of precious metals or two different types of colors ‘two-tone’.", "annotation_id": "23995587", "url": "https://genius.com/23995587", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "Pray for all my dogs, all my niggas behind the fence (Prayer)", "vault_lines": ["Pray for all my dogs, all my niggas behind the fence (Prayer)"], "meaning": "Here Future is talking about his people behind bars, in prison/jail with ‘fence’.  The Fence is what keeps the prisoners secured inside, as they lack the means to overcome the fence.  They usually feature   Concertina wire, which is installed in such a way that if a prisoner attempted to climb over it, they would cut themselves to the point they would require immediate professional medical attention, or even bleed to death there. The Fence is the violent reminder for prisoners that they are kept out of the world and are just animals to the system.", "annotation_id": "23995569", "url": "https://genius.com/23995569", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "And she popped a Tesla, now she goin’ electric", "vault_lines": ["And she popped a Tesla, now she goin' electric"], "meaning": "Double entendre referring to both the popular Tesla electric vehicle and the infamous Tesla logo-shaped ecstasy pills. Ecstasy’s effects include altered sensations, increased energy, empathy, as well as pleasure.", "annotation_id": "23877021", "url": "https://genius.com/23877021", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "Diamond popped out, almost swallowed sixty thousand (Sixty piece)", "vault_lines": ["Diamond popped out, almost swallowed sixty thousand (Sixty piece)"], "meaning": "In 2018 Drake had a pink diamond installed. The diamond came loose and he was almost down sixty thousand dollars for the error.", "annotation_id": "23874587", "url": "https://genius.com/23874587", "state": "pending", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "I’m too sexy for my shirt Too sexy for my shirt So sexy, it hurts I’m too sexy for Milan Too sexy for Milan New York or Japan", "vault_lines": ["I'm too sexy for my shirt", "Too sexy for this ice", "I'm too sexy for this chain", "Too sexy for your gang", "I'm too sexy for the trap", "Too sexy for that cap", "Too sexy for my shirt", "So sexy, it hurts", "Too sexy for this cash", "Too sexy for this syrup", "I'm too sexy for Milan", "Too sexy for Milan", "I'm too sexy (Yeah)", "New York or Japan", "I'm too sexy for this syrup", "Too sexy for your girl", "Too sexy for this world"], "meaning": "These lines are sampled directly from British group Right Said Fred’s chart-topping 1991 hit “I’m Too Sexy”:\n\nI’m too sexy for my shirt\nToo sexy for my shirt\nSo sexy it hurts\nAnd I’m too sexy for Milan\nToo sexy for Milan\nNew York or Japan\n\nInspired by the narcissism prevalent in 1990s London’s gym scene, the song was originally recorded as an rock song, however, after being rejected by multiple record companies, it was reworked a dance track. The song was a commercial success, notably topping the Billboard Hot 100.", "annotation_id": "23873946", "url": "https://genius.com/23873946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 159, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "Did a 360 windmill when I left the scene", "vault_lines": ["Did a 360 windmill when I left the scene"], "meaning": "In basketball, a “360 windmill” is a dunk that involves the player spinning their body in 360 degrees in the air while swinging their arm fully extended, like a windmill. Future references the difficult maneuver to assert his dominance in the rap game.\n\n\n\n\nVince Carter’s 360 windmill dunk at the 2000 NBA Slam Dunk Contest", "annotation_id": "23873940", "url": "https://genius.com/23873940", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "And I’m way too sexy to go unprotected (Way too sexy)", "vault_lines": ["I'm feelin' too sexy to accept requests (Way too sexy)", "And I'm way too sexy to go unprotected (Way too sexy)", "I'm too sexy for Milan", "I'm too sexy (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Drake feels that he is “too sexy” to have sex with a woman without the use of a condom or contraceptive.\n\nIn 2017, Drake had an unplanned child with French retired adult film star Sophie Brussaux. Brussaux alleged that, upon discovering of her pregnancy, Drake sent her text messages requesting her to have an abortion.\n\nFollowing this incident, Drake seems to have learnt his lesson, even posting an image of a Certified Lover Boy branded condom to his Instagram story the night of the album’s release.\n\nAn alternative interpretation of this line suggests that Drake feels that he is “too sexy” to go out in public without being accompanied by security or bodyguards.", "annotation_id": "23873896", "url": "https://genius.com/23873896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "line": "Yeah, woah, woah, woah I’m too sexy for this syrup Too sexy for your girl Too sexy for this world Too sexy for this ice Too sexy for that jack, yeah, yeah I’m too sexy for this chain Too sexy for your gang Too sexy for this fame, yeah, yeah I’m too sexy for the trap Too sexy for that cap Too sexy for that jack, yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I'm too sexy for my shirt", "Too sexy for this ice", "Too sexy for that jack, yeah, yeah", "I'm too sexy for this chain", "Too sexy for your gang", "Too sexy for this fame, yeah, yeah", "I'm too sexy for the trap", "Too sexy for that cap", "Too sexy for my shirt", "Too sexy for this cash", "Too sexy for this syrup", "Too sexy for these pills, I'm too sexy for this", "I'm too sexy for Milan", "Too sexy for Milan", "I'm too sexy (Yeah)", "I'm too sexy for this syrup", "Too sexy for your girl", "Too sexy for this world"], "meaning": "On the song’s chorus, Future pays homage to British band Right Said Fred, interpolating lyrics and cadences from their 1991 hit “I’m Too Sexy”:\n\nI’m too sexy for my shirt\nToo sexy for my shirt\nSo sexy it hurts\n\nInspired by the narcissism prevalent in London’s gym scene during the 1990s, “I’m Too Sexy” was written to poke fun at self-absorbed gym-goers. On “Way 2 Sexy,” Future retools Right Said Fred’s song as a modern male empowerment anthem.\n\nUpon its release, fans cited “Way 2 Sexy” as a male counterpart to Cardi B and Megan Thee Stallion’s 2020 song “WAP” due to its empowering lyrics.", "annotation_id": "23873887", "url": "https://genius.com/23873887", "state": "accepted", "votes": 193, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Way 2 Sexy", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-way-2-sexy-lyrics", "genius_title": "Way 2 Sexy"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "East egg huntin’, they gotta look for somethin’", "vault_lines": ["East egg huntin', they gotta look for somethin'"], "meaning": "Drake makes implications about parallels between the subject matter of his beef with Meek Mill and the main plot of The Great Gatsby.\n\nIn The Great Gatsby, “East Egg” is where “old money” resides; people who have accumulated great wealth and respect. The intimation is that Drake lives on the affluent side of town, and Meek channels his jealousy in to attempts to discredit him.\n\n“Easter eggs” are hidden gems found in music, or video’s, that expose new meanings. An Easter Egg is a desirable object, and Drake accuses Meek of searching through his music and his life for ways to discredit him and his career.", "annotation_id": "7875745", "url": "https://genius.com/7875745", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "[Sample] ”You know, I kept it real It ain’t too many real people out here And people be on some weak bitch-ass shit all day long Industry people… regular people… And then when you keep it real with people, they cross the line, take your kindness for weakness and that’s weak.”", "vault_lines": ["\"You know, I kept it real", "It ain’t too many real people out here", "And people be on some weak bitch-ass shit all day long", "Industry people… regular people…", "And then when you keep it real with people, they cross the line, take your kindness for weakness and that’s weak.\""], "meaning": "For this outro OVO in-house producer Noah “40” Shebib uses a highly distorted version of an interview Big Sean gave for the Power 105 Breakfast Club on February 24th 2015.\n\nSean gave this sampled response after being asked by Charlamagne Tha God to elaborate on a few lines from his song “Dark Sky”\n\nAnd I don’t owe nobody in the world no favors. I started from the basement, made it to the skyscrapers.\n\nThese words follow a similar theme as Drake’s hit track “Started From The Bottom” and reiterates the sentiment of regret that “Charged Up” addresses. Specifically it emphasizes where Drake raps that he is “done doing favors for people”.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/_-sFjJ7YBmY?t=22m2s\n\nWith the inclusion of this phrase, Drake is expressing disappointment, as he’s kept it 100 and real with Meek. Conversely, these ghost-writer allegations came mere days after the release of Meek’s Dreams Worth More Than Money album which featured Drake on the song R.I.C.O. This type of ‘switching up’ did not settle well with Drake; making it only ever more ironic that Meek Mill tweeted the following:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623853951822114816\n\nIt is also interesting to note that Funkmaster Flex claimed to have a reference track recorded by Quentin Miller for the song “Blessings” which was a single from Big Sean’s album Dark Sky Paradise.", "annotation_id": "7456066", "url": "https://genius.com/7456066", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "(Stop, stop, stop, stop…) Have you seen me? I’m doing so good Every time we meet: “Oh, I’m so sorry” (Fuck niggas gon’ be fuck niggas That’s why we never give a fuck when a fuck nigga switched up)", "vault_lines": ["I’m charged up, I’m charged up", "Fuck niggas gon' be fuck niggas", "That’s why we never give a fuck when a fuck nigga switched up", "Have you seen me? I’m doing so good", "Every time we meet: \"Oh, I’m so sorry\""], "meaning": "Every time 6 God is present – it’s all smiles, laughter, and positivity. The second he leaves,  people are apologetic because they realize they need him for their success.\n\nConsidering Drake was featured on both Meek’s major label albums, this is a valid point. Drizzy seems to be just waiting on Meek’s apology.", "annotation_id": "7446791", "url": "https://genius.com/7446791", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Rumor has it, there’s somethin’ that only I know Rumor has it, I steer the ship with my eyes closed", "vault_lines": ["Rumor has it, there’s somethin' that only I know", "Rumor has it, I steer the ship with my eyes closed"], "meaning": "Drake pokes fun at all the people who make false allegations against him, most notably Meek’s accusation that Drake doesn’t write his own raps. Drizzy highlights the unlikely chance that he could’ve kept this, along with other rumours about him, to himself, thus emphasizing the poor validity behind these claims.\n\nThe only thing evident: Drake’s ability to consistently stay relevant without having to force attention to himself.", "annotation_id": "7446467", "url": "https://genius.com/7446467", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "But rumor has it, hasn’t done you niggas any good", "vault_lines": ["But rumor has it, hasn’t done you niggas any good"], "meaning": "Drizzy parallels “rumor has it,” to show that even the rumors about him employing a ghost writer on “R.I.C.O” haven’t benefited anyone, including Meek and Funkmaster Flex.\n\nHis tone also implies the previous three rumours he mentions; having a ghost writer, running the rap game, and sleeping with Nicki Minaj, could be true or false – it’s up to the listener to decide.", "annotation_id": "7446108", "url": "https://genius.com/7446108", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "It’s sound, sound, sound ‘til the day I die We goin’ live to keep this shit alive", "vault_lines": ["It’s sound, sound, sound ‘til the day I die", "We goin' live to keep this shit alive"], "meaning": "OVO Sound, Drake’s label, has been gaining more attention as he continues to shout it out in his more recent tracks.\n\nIn order to keep OVO on top, Drake will continue to make music, party, and enjoy life. In his own words, he’ll follow the line:\n\nYoung, but I’m making millions to work the night shift", "annotation_id": "7446085", "url": "https://genius.com/7446085", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Stop, stop, stop… Fuck niggas gon’ be fuck niggas That’s why we never give a fuck when a fuck nigga switched up I’m so sorry Did you hope I was crying? It was rough without you It was rough without you", "vault_lines": ["I’m charged up, I’m charged up", "Fuck niggas gon' be fuck niggas", "That’s why we never give a fuck when a fuck nigga switched up", "Did you hope I was crying?", "It was rough without you"], "meaning": "Drake and his mom once discussed the perception that Drake’s a chronic crier. For a man whose career has been built upon a genre-unique self-awareness, he addresses one of the most prominent Drake memes in existence.\n\nHe references his own lyrics on Future’s “Sh!t.” The intro is not included on the Soundcloud version of the song.", "annotation_id": "7446079", "url": "https://genius.com/7446079", "state": "accepted", "votes": 254, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Must I remind you that Jimmy got 20 million on it?", "vault_lines": ["Must I remind you that Jimmy got 20 million on it?"], "meaning": "Drake started his career as an actor on Degrassi: The Next Generation as Jimmy Brooks when he was 15. Now 28, Drake’s signed a $19 million dollar deal with Apple Music, supposedly struck by former head of Interscope Records, Jimmy Iovine, to promote his brand.\n\nThis line carries the vibe of Eminem’s character Rabbit in the film 8 Mile, who during the final rap battle, rendered any insult worthless by making fun of himself. Sure, Drake was “wheelchair Jimmy,” but that guy’s worth $75 million now – can’t front on that!", "annotation_id": "7446051", "url": "https://genius.com/7446051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 139, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "I seen it all comin’, knew they would push a button", "vault_lines": ["I seen it all comin', knew they would push a button"], "meaning": "While Meek is desperately running around “East egg hunting” in an attempt to find imaginary eggs (insults for Drake), Drizzy sticks with his “No New Friends” policy which means not handing out features anymore. He’s previously addressed this on “The Motion.”\n\nWhen they need a favor from your man, they don’t leave you alone\n\nLately, Drake has provided several features for artists such as Fetty Wap, Big Sean, and Lil Wayne, clearly in favor of the fans. With a large net worth, it seems there’s no reason for Drake to collaborate for money anymore.\n\nMore specifically he’s talking about his feature on Meek Mill’s song “R.I.C.O”. This was the verse that Meek referenced in his infamous series of tweets that sparked the whole feud in the first place.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623703382675460096?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.billboard.com%2Farticles%2Fcolumns%2Fthe-juice%2F6641784%2Fmeek-mill-drake-timeline\n\nHe claimed that Drake didn’t write the verse on the song, and eventually Funkmaster Flex released an alleged reference track. Miller was credited for his contributions to the song.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/6237033826754\n\nHe also told the world in 2013 that he was ready for the day someone would call him out by name.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/SbmlqJqez1U?t=4m20s", "annotation_id": "7445962", "url": "https://genius.com/7445962", "state": "accepted", "votes": 164, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "I see you niggas havin’ trouble goin’ gold Turnin’ into some so-and-sos that no one knows But so it go", "vault_lines": ["I see you niggas havin' trouble goin' gold", "Turnin' into some so-and-sos that no one knows", "But so it go"], "meaning": "While Drake’s last album, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late went gold in 5 days and is currently certified platinum, those who’re “beefing” with him – namely Tyga & Meek Mill, are struggling to sell over 500,000 records.\n\nTyga’s The Gold Album: 18th Dynasty sold a devastating 2,200 copies in its first week, while Meek’s debut album Dreams and Nightmares took three years to go gold.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623328133673058304\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n“So it goes” is a Kurt Vonnegut technique used in his book Slaughterhouse-Five. Every time death or mortality occurs, he uses the phrase. Drake applies it to Meek’s career.", "annotation_id": "7445948", "url": "https://genius.com/7445948", "state": "accepted", "votes": 127, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Trust me, this information is vital I’m tryin’ to take the high road,", "vault_lines": ["Trust me, this information is vital", "I’m tryin' to take the high road,"], "meaning": "Instead of taking to social media to hit back at Meek Mill, Drake takes the more authentic route of letting the truth spill out over a beat instead.\n\nThe term “high road” is defined as taking the more superior approach (in terms of wisdom or morals) in a situation.\n\nHis stanky leg reference may be deemed as a reach by some, but Drake proves in this line the depth of research he has conducted in to Meek’s past, and warns just how expertly he is capable of exposing him.", "annotation_id": "7445921", "url": "https://genius.com/7445921", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Okay, the flow start to feel familiar, don’t it?", "vault_lines": ["Okay, the flow start to feel familiar, don’t it?"], "meaning": "Funkmaster Flex released the alleged reference track for “10 Bands,” showing Quentin Miller supposedly creating the flow on the track.\n\nDrake counters this, implying he created the flow that’s stayed popular for so long. After all, his songs spend most of his time being played on the radio. In 2015, he broke the record for the most songs on the Billboard charts at one time, with a staggering 21 out of 50.", "annotation_id": "7445904", "url": "https://genius.com/7445904", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "I’m charged up Oh no, we eatin’, niggas ain’t about to starve us Nah, niggas ain’t about to starve us I’m charged up", "vault_lines": ["I’m charged up, I’m charged up", "Oh no, we eatin', niggas ain’t about to starve us", "Nah, niggas ain't about to starve us", "Niggas ain’t about to starve us", "Nah, I’m charged up!"], "meaning": "“Eating” is a term commonly used to indicate wealth. With a net worth in 2015  of over $75 million, a recent $20 million deal with Apple Music, and chart topping music – Drake has no fears of ever going broke, sorry Meek!\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/624349813593784320\n\n“Charged up” was released in response to a series of tweets from Meek Mill, and then Funkmaster Flex, which allege that Drake did not deserve his status due to collaborating with other artists, most notably Quentin Miller.\n\nThis incident is unlikely to stop Drake’s momentum, and may have served as a motivating mechanism – as he released the songs “Cha Cha Remix” as well as “Right Hand” immediately following this track.", "annotation_id": "7445902", "url": "https://genius.com/7445902", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Rumor has it, I either fucked her or never could", "vault_lines": ["Rumor has it, I either fucked her or never could"], "meaning": "Drake slyly responds to the rumors of having sex with Meek Mill’s girlfriend, Nicki Minaj. This follows Meek’s tweet stating how he had originally taken a line on “R.I.C.O.” as Drake dissing him.\n\nNicki herself has been very clear about this before:\n\nI never fucked Wayne, I never fucked Drake", "annotation_id": "7445872", "url": "https://genius.com/7445872", "state": "accepted", "votes": 142, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "You boys are gettin’ into your feelings on me You should embrace it, this could be one of our realest moments", "vault_lines": ["You boys are gettin' into your feelings on me", "You should embrace it, this could be one of our realest moments"], "meaning": "Meek’s motivation for “exposing” Drake was allegedly because Drake didn’t tweet about Meek’s new album, Dreams Worth More Than Money. There was also an incident where Drake apparently didn’t turn up to a planned performance at Meek’s welcome home party, causing some animosity.\n\nDrake sons Meek here, calling him a boy (not a man), and, ironically, warns him not to get his “feelings” on Drake, a real man. They could’ve experienced brilliance together via their track “R.I.C.O.,” but instead all that came of it is beef. A beef that, according to Drake, will be one of the pairs “realest moments” – Meek has finally shown his true colors.", "annotation_id": "7445867", "url": "https://genius.com/7445867", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Cops are killin’ people with they arms up And your main focus is tryna harm us? And you think you ‘bout to starve us? Niggas ain’t about to starve us Nah, I’m charged up!", "vault_lines": ["I’m charged up, I’m charged up", "Oh no, we eatin', niggas ain’t about to starve us", "Nah, niggas ain't about to starve us", "Cops are killin' people with they arms up", "And your main focus is tryna harm us?", "And you think you ‘bout to starve us?", "Niggas ain’t about to starve us", "Nah, I’m charged up!"], "meaning": "These lyrics were met with a mixed response. Some listeners have labelled this a low-blow, since Meek was vocal about the death of Sandra Bland on Twitter the same day he “exposed” Drake, while others have agreed with Drizzy, and feel there are bigger things (like the #BlackLivesMatter movement) that hip-hop should be focused on in 2015.\n\nIn any case, the former seems unintentional. Both rappers have spoken on police brutality in their music this year. Drake addressed the topic on “6 PM In New York,” while Meek did the same on “Ice Cream Freestyle.”\n\nWhat’s more important is that in 2015 alone, over 600 lives have been taken by the police in the United States – and when taking into those unaccounted for, the number would rise to 928 persons per year. Rarely are police ever charged – only 41 officers were ever charged for manslaughter in the past 7 years.", "annotation_id": "7445855", "url": "https://genius.com/7445855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 166, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "No woman ever had me star-struck Or was able to tell me to get my bars up", "vault_lines": ["No woman ever had me star-struck", "Or was able to tell me to get my bars up"], "meaning": "Meek Mill and Nicki Minaj have been in a relationship for some time now.  Drake, a close affiliate to Nicki through Young Money, states that no woman has ever had superiority over him or been able to tell him to step his game up, whereas Meek has always been considered the worse and lesser known artist in his relationship with Nicki.", "annotation_id": "7445854", "url": "https://genius.com/7445854", "state": "accepted", "votes": 374, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Wow, I’m honored that you think this is staged I’m flattered, man, in fact, I’m amazed The perfect start to the summer, man, this shit is a doozy This shit a twist in the movie, but don’t jump to conclusions", "vault_lines": ["Wow, I’m honored that you think this is staged", "I’m flattered, man, in fact, I’m amazed", "The perfect start to the summer, man, this shit is a doozy", "This shit a twist in the movie, but don't jump to conclusions"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to Meek Mill’s tweets where he accuses Drizzy of using ghostwriters.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623700698509758464\n\nSince Drake’s popularity and sales are surpassing every other hip-hop artist, other artists are certain it must be staged – that he must be getting help from others somehow. This is the highest form of flattery.\n\nDrake has always been focused on dominating the summer, but this is an unexpected windfall of press and popularity. It’s a twist, because Meek and Drake have supported each other recently, and no other shots have been fired before this. It’s an unexpected event, but just because someone tweeted it, does not make it so.", "annotation_id": "7445851", "url": "https://genius.com/7445851", "state": "accepted", "votes": 216, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Come live all your dreams out at OVO We gon’ make sure you get your bread and you know the ropes", "vault_lines": ["Come live all your dreams out at OVO", "We gon' make sure you get your bread and you know the ropes"], "meaning": "Similar to Meek Mill’s on-going “Dreamchasers” theme, Drake wants any upcoming artist to know that OVO Sound – Drake’s label and posse will teach you the music industry and pay you well. You’d be able to “live all your dreams out” rather than merely chase them. You’d “get your bread” being with OVO, unlike being with Birdman and Cash Money Records, with whom many signees of the label have issues due to unpaid royalties.\n\nIt’s no coincidence Drake uses the term “bread” here – his alleged ghostwriter Quentin Miller worked at a bakery before being picked up by OVO as a song writer.", "annotation_id": "7445850", "url": "https://genius.com/7445850", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "I still got love for my idols I got me a deal with Apple and I still feel entitled", "vault_lines": ["I still got love for my idols", "I got me a deal with Apple and I still feel entitled"], "meaning": "Even if Drake is competing with his idols, who are owners of the streaming service Tidal, he still shows love for them. He recently did a cover and brought Kanye to the stage, did a tour with Lil Wayne, brought the legendary group Outkast to his annual OVO fest, also he has a track called “Wu Tang Forever” a tribute for the legendary group.\n\n“Feel entitled” sounds like “feelin' Tidal”; while Drake is still hungrier than ever, he was supposed to sign a contract with Tidal, however he turned it down to go with Apple for a $19,000,000 contract. Despite his choice of Apple’s streaming service, he still has love for his idols.", "annotation_id": "7445848", "url": "https://genius.com/7445848", "state": "accepted", "votes": 268, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "I did some charity today for the kids But I’m used to it ’cause all y’all charity cases All y’all stare in my face in hopes you could be the replacement Niggas is snitchin’ on us without no interrogation I stay silent ’cause we at war and I’m very patient Six God is watchin’, I just hope you’re prepared to face him I’m charged up, I’m charged up", "vault_lines": ["Niggas is snitchin' on us without no interrogation", "I stay silent 'cause we at war and I’m very patient", "Six God is watchin', I just hope you’re prepared to face him", "I’m charged up, I’m charged up", "Did you hope I was crying?", "Nah, I’m charged up!", "It was rough without you", "I did some charity today for the kids", "But I'm used to it 'cause all y'all charity cases", "All y’all stare in my face in hopes you could be the replacement"], "meaning": "When the drama surrounding Meek Mill’s allegations towards him first hit, Drake was busy attending a charity kickball game.  Drake labels Meek a charity case, implying that the reason he decided to accept the feature on “R.I.C.O.” was out of pity for him. In March, Drake also donated money to fund a recording studio for Stawberry Mansion High School in Philadelphia, which just happens to also be Meek Mills’s alma-mater.\n\nAlthough Meek stated that Drake had a ghostwritten verse, there was no evidence for this, and Drake decided to let it pass until now.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/PeterKimGlobal/status/624736431097933824", "annotation_id": "7445843", "url": "https://genius.com/7445843", "state": "accepted", "votes": 245, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "Need DJ Clue to drop a bomb on it None of them want this shit more than I want it", "vault_lines": ["Need DJ Clue to drop a bomb on it", "None of them want this shit more than I want it"], "meaning": "New York DJ Funkmaster Flex has been extremely vocal in his support of Meek Mill’s claim that Drake uses a ghost writer. Flex premiered the supposed ghost writer Quentin Miller’s reference track to “10 Bands” and claims to have many more.\n\nDrake is poking fun at Flex for gathering all this “evidence” of a ghost writer by referring to Flex as DJ Clue, a rival of Flex’s at Power 105. Drake even says these lines in almost the exact way that Kendrick Lamar did for his opening bar on the infamous Control verse, adding to insult that Flex doesn’t have a clue to what’s going on and Drake is always in control.\n\nAdditionally, Clue is also DJing Meek Mill’s album release party in New York. Unlike Flex, it seems Drake really did his research on this one.", "annotation_id": "7445840", "url": "https://genius.com/7445840", "state": "accepted", "votes": 139, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "I bought my niggas rollies off of Thank Me Later While these boys was stanky leggin’", "vault_lines": ["I bought my niggas rollies off of Thank Me Later", "While these boys was stanky leggin'"], "meaning": "Drake released his debut album Thank Me Later in 2010. The LP has sold over 1.5 million copies since release, making Drizzy so much money that he was able to buy himself and his posse a Rolex each.\n\n\n\nAt around the same time, Meek Mill was releasing his Flamers 3: The Wait is Over, which contained original lyrical musings from Meek affiliate and fellow Philly artist K Smith:\n\nRicky Bobby on ya ho, stanky leg up in her tummy\n\nOne of Meek’s favourite topics is his rollie collection.", "annotation_id": "7445838", "url": "https://genius.com/7445838", "state": "accepted", "votes": 123, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "line": "I get a ring and I bring it home like I’m Cory Joe", "vault_lines": ["I get a ring and I bring it home like I’m Cory Joe"], "meaning": "“Cory Joe” is Cory Joseph, a Toronto-born point guard who, after winning a championship (and getting a sweet ring) with the San Antonio Spurs in 2014, with whom he “learned the ropes” and “lived out his dream”, signed with his hometown Toronto Raptors in the 2015 offseason.\n\n\n\nFor Drake, his championship rings are the awards he gets for his music, which he proudly brings back home to Toronto. In an interview with The Fader, OVO producer Vinylz (who actually helped produce “R.I.C.O.”) said that they actually call themselves the Spurs.\n\nMe, Boi-1da, and all the OVO people — we call ourselves the Spurs. The Spurs are not a team that people look at, but they always win. And they’re still so humble. That’s how we all move.", "annotation_id": "7445837", "url": "https://genius.com/7445837", "state": "accepted", "votes": 120, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Charged Up", "status": "done", "targets": 24, "clicked": 24, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-charged-up-lyrics", "genius_title": "Charged Up"}
