{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "Came with coral reefer", "vault_lines": ["Water on my neck, these diamonds came with coral reefer"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "31545266", "url": "https://genius.com/31545266", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "Popped an Adderall, I feel like I can lift a tree up", "vault_lines": ["Popped an Adderall, I feel like I can lift a tree up"], "meaning": "Amphetamines can cause people who don’t have ADHD to get a “rush” high.\n\nSo for someone like Drake, him feeling that he can lift a tree up doesn’t sound too far off if he is using Adderall recreationally.", "annotation_id": "30722603", "url": "https://genius.com/30722603", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "My uncle’s sister know she raised a real one, ill one", "vault_lines": ["My uncle's sister know she raised a real one, ill one"], "meaning": "Drake’s mother can be considered his uncle’s sister in law. This can be an add-on reference to the previous line that is allegedly about XXXTentacion’s multiple Instagram Lives in which he talked negatively about Drake and his mom.\n\nDespite his suburban upbringing, Drake considers himself a “real one” or someone who is able fight back / defend themselves. Allegedly, Drake has done this by sending subliminal disses in various songs to XXXTentacion throughout the years to the point where his lyrics in question were brought into XXXTentacion’s murder trial.", "annotation_id": "28361037", "url": "https://genius.com/28361037", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "Damn, maybe I should do a twenty, maybe I should break that twenty, do a ten Maybe I should break that ten, do a five, then if it gets live, do a five again If he held his tongue on that live, he’d be alive again, damn", "vault_lines": ["Damn, maybe I should do a twenty, maybe I should break that twenty, do a ten", "Maybe I should break that ten, do a five, then if it gets live, do a five again", "If he held his tongue on that live, he'd be alive again, damn"], "meaning": "These lyrics serve as a double entendre, alluding to the reduction of Adderall’s dosages—which range from 5mg to 30mg—resulting in a lesser-effective high but a quicker withdrawal and the late rapper XXXTENTACION, who was shot and robbed shortly after exiting a motorcycle dealership on June 18th, 2018.\n\nSpeculation arose, especially on TikTok, as when you do the conversion of the numbers into Roman numerals it equates to ‘XXX’; twenty being ‘XX’ and ten being ‘X’.\n\nCoincidentally or not, in February 2023—five years after XXX’s death—Drake was ordered to appear in XXX’s murder trial, due to their long history of beef together and him seemingly portraying XXX’s exact murder scene in his May 2018 track “I’m Upset”:\n\nSMS, triple X\nThat’s the only time I ever shoot below the neck (Skrr)\nWhy you keep on shootin' if you know that nigga dead? (Skrr)\nThat’s the only kind of shit that gets you some respect\n\nXXX also previously sent shots at Drake’s mom for allowing Drake to steal his flow in a March 2017 Instagram live, which he further expanded on in a March 2017 interview with 1035 TheBeat, stating:\n\nDrake was s'posed to contact one of my managers, so he doesn’t do it, but, that same fucking week bro, […] he dropped a fucking video, previewed that shit on Instagram. […] I was on the phone with my dog, Chris, he was like, “yo, you gotta listen to this shit, this nigga Drake a fuck nigga.” […] So he plays this shit, then i hear “dun-dun-di-da-my-dun” and I’m like what the fuck? As soon as it started […] I was already gettin’ that. […] Like they done mash-up, they put his verse on my song and like the cadence is literally just at the same tempo, it’s not off-beat at all. If you listen to my song then you listen to bro song, like, you get it.", "annotation_id": "27840868", "url": "https://genius.com/27840868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "I jump on your song and make a label think they need ya, for real (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I jump on your song and make a label think they need ya, for real (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Drizzy brags about his influence, and how a feature from him grants an artist with a stamp of legitimacy. In a nutshell, having Drake on your song makes you a popular demand.\n\nOver the years there have been several examples of artists experiencing short-lived success following a co-sign from Drake, with two notable examples being iLoveMakonnen and BlocBoy JB.", "annotation_id": "27368236", "url": "https://genius.com/27368236", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "I jump on your song and make you sound like you the feature", "vault_lines": ["I jump on your song and make you sound like you the feature"], "meaning": "21 says he’s outperforms any artist who features him by outrapping them on the song and taking the shine.", "annotation_id": "27368216", "url": "https://genius.com/27368216", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "She wouldn’t wear no panties ’round me even if she could Gave out plenty spankings ’til they got it understood", "vault_lines": ["She wouldn't wear no panties 'round me even if she could", "Gave out plenty spankings 'til they got it understood"], "meaning": "21 Savage conveys the idea here that any girl he’s with understands that she shouldn’t wear underwear in his company, as he’s “Gave out plenty spankings” on their asses to let them know his preferences.\n\nAdditionally, this line carries a double entendre, with 21 Savage also speaks to his opposition when he mentions\n“[Giving] out plenty spankings”. A “spanking”, in this context, would be referring to a violent physical altercation in which 21 handily defeated his enemy. In this way, he’s implying that he “spanked” his opposition so many times that they now know not to mess with him.", "annotation_id": "27177595", "url": "https://genius.com/27177595", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "Y’all be goin’ in and out recessions The same way that I be goin’ in and out of Texas Or in and out my sessions, or in and out her best friends Or in and out these courtrooms, my lawyer like, “Objection”", "vault_lines": ["Y'all be goin' in and out rеcessions", "The same way that I be goin' in and out of Texas", "Or in and out my sessions, or in and out her best friends", "Or in and out these courtrooms, my lawyer like, \"Objection\""], "meaning": "A wordplay. The world, in times this was written, has been on the brink of ruin. 2022 was one of the worst financial situations and near-by-maybe-future-events have not been there for nearly every person living\nAs people are now getting in bad financial situations.\nDrake is kind of bragging, that this obviously not a problem for his wealth.\nHe says, people mostly of course, nearest most direct rivals are touching financial crisis and brings up, he is just flying to Texas as he mentions very often that he loves Houston and the women of Houston\n\nHe compares that with the times he visits, getting in and out of working, in and out(sexually) the best friend of his current partner and visits in court rooms. The last also a play on how influental his life is, as he is beeing accused of things many times and people have interests in you.", "annotation_id": "27114180", "url": "https://genius.com/27114180", "state": "pending", "votes": -8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "I’m just— what? In the cut, throwin’ Franklins on her frame I’m a— ayy, I’m a gentleman, I’m generous I blow a half a million on you hoes, I’m a feminist", "vault_lines": ["I'm just— what? In the cut, throwin' Franklins on her frame", "I'm a— ayy, I'm a gentleman, I'm generous", "I blow a half a million on you hoes, I'm a feminist"], "meaning": "“Franklins” refers to an $100 dollar bill, as they have a portrait of Benjamin Franklin on them. Likely at a strip club, where Drake is known to spend a lot, Drizzy is throwing $100 bills all over this stripper, dismissing any claims he’s misogynistic, as he emphasises that he is a feminist and cannot hate women if he’s blowing all this money on them. Drake stood up for women’s abortion rights later in the album on “Spin Bout U”, supporting the claim that he’s a feminist and wants equal rights for women.\n\nDrake allegedly spent $1 million in a strip club previously, meaning his half a million claim is almost certainly true.\n\n\n\nIn these lines, Drake also uses a similar flow to one he used on the hook of 2018 track “Going Bad” with Meek Mill:\n\nI just… what? I just… uh, put a Richard on the card", "annotation_id": "27069797", "url": "https://genius.com/27069797", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "It’s been thirty minutes, I don’t feel nothin’ Oh shit, wait a minute, think I’m startin’ to feel somethin’ Where you get this motherfuckin’ pill from?", "vault_lines": ["Oh, time to get exposed", "It's been thirty minutes, I don't feel nothin'", "Oh shit, wait a minute, think I'm startin' to feel somethin'", "Where you get this motherfuckin' pill from?"], "meaning": "Here, Drake calls back to the end of the previous verse where he talks about taking adderall.\n\n20mg is a common dose for Adderall and pills often have lines etched in making it easy to divide into 10 or 5 mg.", "annotation_id": "27066094", "url": "https://genius.com/27066094", "state": "pending", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "You ain’t been from ’round here, nigga, come get off your show Savage said you pussy and he hit it on the nose But that board is open, why you actin’ like it’s closed? I don’t know", "vault_lines": ["You ain't been from 'round here, nigga, come get off your show", "Savage said you pussy and he hit it on the nose", "But that board is opеn, why you actin' like it's closed?", "If you know it's tension, don't come 'round me like it's good"], "meaning": "Drake is taking a shot at Pusha T. Pusha T said during his interview on the Drink Champs podcast that he has been banned from Canada.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/i/status/1523029987716177921\n\nIn 2018, a few Drake fans rushed Pusha T’s stage which lead to violence. This incident is probably what got Pusha T banned.\n\nhttps://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/news/pusha-t-concert-drake-feud-rivalry-stage-rush-toronto-canada-beer-throw-video-a8644081.html\n\nDrake responds saying that he wasn’t banned, and agrees with his collaborator 21 Savage that Pusha T is a coward.\n\nDrake also does a callback to his popular song “Jimmy Cooks” featuring 21 Savage himself. With the line: “ Savage said you pussy and he hit it on the nose” – calling back to Jimmy Cooks where drake segways into 21’s verse with the line “if i let 21 say it, you a:” and savage famously finishes the line with an extended line “pussy”", "annotation_id": "27063530", "url": "https://genius.com/27063530", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "I come from the ghetto, so my trunk is in my hood", "vault_lines": ["I come from the ghetto, so my trunk is in my hood"], "meaning": "Here, 21 uses a play on words and double entendre to flex about coming from the hood and being able to own luxury cars.\n\nHis trunk (a suitcase containing something illegal) is in his hood, as in the neighbourhood, and it’s also in the hood (or bonnet) of his car.\n\nHis car trunk (storage space at the back) is in his hood (bonnet) at the front of the vehicle. This is because high-end cars are known to have a trunk in the hood, also known as “Frunk.”", "annotation_id": "27061834", "url": "https://genius.com/27061834", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "Fuck the nosebleeds, baby, come sit on this wood", "vault_lines": ["Fuck the nosebleeds, baby, come sit on this wood"], "meaning": "“Nosebleeds” commonly refer to the uppermost, highest, and farthest seats in an arena or stadium. The nosebleeds section gets its name from the reference to mountain climbers suffering from nosebleeds at high altitudes.\n\n\n\nIn this line, 21 states that he would detest purchasing tickets to seats at the nosebleeds section and that he would rather sit on the (hard)wood or courtside—its name comes from the flooring of NBA games being made of hardwood.\n\nLastly, 21 uses a double entendre in the line wherein he depicts a scenario with his female companion, ordering her to sit on his “wood,” which stems from UK slang and simply means an erected penis.", "annotation_id": "27053532", "url": "https://genius.com/27053532", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "She a supermodel, so she only eatin’ Caesar", "vault_lines": ["She a supermodel, so she only eatin' Caesar"], "meaning": "Caeser is well-known, low-calorie salad cooked with chicken, cherry tomatoes, garlic, parmesan, olive oil, and lettuce. It is a popular dish among people who want to have a good figure, including models.\n\n\n\nThese bars are also reference to Las Vegas Caesers Place, a well-known hotel, casino, and luxury shopping destination. The Forum Shops include approximately 160 specialty stores, fine restaurants, and attractions.\n\n\n\nAnother interpretation of these bars is that 21 is comparing himself to Julius Caesar, the ruler of the Roman Empire from 49 BC until his assassination in 44 BC, and the namesake of the aforementioned Caesars Palace. Thus, given 21’s status, the woman in question only wants to “eat” him, colloquially meaning to perform oral sex on him.\n\nFurthermore, the consonance of Caesar and the name of American R&B-singer SZA are similar. As such, this bar could allude to her 2017 song “Supermodel”. Notably, Drake revealed that he previously dated SZA on his 2020 collaboration with 21 Savage, “Mr. Right Now”:\n\nYeah, said she wanna fuck to some SZA, wait\n‘Cause I used to date SZA back in '08\nIf you cool with it, baby, she can still play\nWhile I jump inside that box and have a field day", "annotation_id": "27053185", "url": "https://genius.com/27053185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "I can’t right my wrongs, but I can still write these hooks", "vault_lines": ["I can't right my wrongs, but I can still write these hooks"], "meaning": "21 Savage interpolates Pusha-T’s ‘Infrared’ off of Daytona:\n\nHow could you ever right these wrongs\nWhen you don’t even write your songs?\n\nThis was a diss aimed at Drake amid the latter end of the ghostwriting rumors, prompting ‘Duppy Freestyle’ and the since legendary cemented ‘Story of Adidon’ diss tracks. 21 flips Push’s bars on its head to put emphasis on his rough history whilst simultaneously flexing his pen.", "annotation_id": "27052964", "url": "https://genius.com/27052964", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "line": "All my bitches Spanish, bodiqua", "vault_lines": ["All my bitches Spanish, bodiqua"], "meaning": "21 Savage uses the term “boricua” to refer to Puerto Rican women.\n\n“Boricua” is a term that Puerto Ricans use to identify themselves as a nation. “Boricua\" is to Puerto Rico as “American” is to the USA.", "annotation_id": "27052226", "url": "https://genius.com/27052226", "state": "pending", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "On BS", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-21-savage-on-bs-lyrics", "genius_title": "On BS"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Ocean deep in my swimmin’ pool, yeah (In my pool)", "vault_lines": ["Ocean deep in my swimmin' pool, yeah (In my pool)"], "meaning": "Scott boastfully compares the size of the pool at his residence to the ocean.", "annotation_id": "32192741", "url": "https://genius.com/32192741", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "I spent a fifty on the chain (Racks) You spent your last fifty (Ugh, cash)", "vault_lines": ["I spent a fifty on the chain (Racks)", "You spent your last fifty (Ugh, cash)"], "meaning": "Quavo juxtaposes the financial situations between himself and his rival, while also boasting about his expensive chains. In particular, Quavo describes his Eliante Yoda chain, which reportedly cost him upwards of $50,000.\n\nQuavo previously flexed his expensive jewelry on 2 Chainz’s June 2017 track, “Blue Cheese”:\n\n50,000 on me, I’m a walkin' lick\nShe fuck with the squad, she gon' grip the stick", "annotation_id": "17513980", "url": "https://genius.com/17513980", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "I got a 150,000 dollars for an afterparty And I gave it to the killas just to break it down", "vault_lines": ["I got a 150,000 dollars for an afterparty", "And I gave it to the killas just to break it down", "I got the keys to the streets (Keys)"], "meaning": "Drake is a hot commodity in the rap game and his presence isn’t cheap. He likely refers to his Summer Sixteen tour, which occurred after the release of the April 2016 album Views. It broke records for grossing 85 million dollars over the span of 54 shows, or approximately $1.5 million per show.", "annotation_id": "17513927", "url": "https://genius.com/17513927", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "I’m a magnet for bad bitches (Magnet) You got the goin’ out sad bitches (Sad)", "vault_lines": ["I'm a magnet for bad bitches (Magnet)", "You got the goin' out sad bitches (Sad)"], "meaning": "Quavo juxtaposes his sexual prowess with that of his competition, who only manages to get with women that weren’t able to hook up with the famous Atlanta rapper.\n\nQuavo and his rap group Migos frequently discuss naughty women in their music. They later dedicated a January 2018 track to them, titled “BBO (Bad Bitches Only),” where they rapped:\n\nBad bitches only, bad bitches on me (bad)\nBad bitches only, bad bitches on me (woo)", "annotation_id": "17513732", "url": "https://genius.com/17513732", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "I’m so high up I’m like, “How is niggas really gettin’ down?”", "vault_lines": ["I'm so high up I'm like, \"How is niggas really gettin' down?\""], "meaning": "Drake references both his intoxication level and placement in hip hop. Since entering the rap game, Drake’s had unprecedented success and record-breaking numbers, indicating his placement atop the hip hop hierarchy.\n\nIn addition, “high” could refer to his inebriation and the euphoric feeling he experiences after consuming marijuana.", "annotation_id": "17513632", "url": "https://genius.com/17513632", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Tryna show the dogs brighter days Got a torch, tryna light the way", "vault_lines": ["Tryna show the dogs brighter days", "Got a torch, tryna light the way"], "meaning": "Drake has done so much for his city Toronto, Canada. Researchers have concluded that with all the promotion Drake has done, he helped gross out over 3 billion dollars to his city. By doing this, he has helped his city become an icon, which has made it easier for other artists from the 6 to get on the spotlight.\n\nIn a 2017 interview, Drake spoke about how he felt that he wasn’t getting enough credit:\n\nSometimes I feel like the most hated, sometimes I still feel like a huge underdog, people just kinda almost are just too scared to give me support or credit, cos they don’t wanna be, cos that’s too like cliché", "annotation_id": "12709688", "url": "https://genius.com/12709688", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "God, the way that she servin’ that cake, I gotta sit back and digest (Straight up)", "vault_lines": ["God, the way that she servin' that cake, I gotta sit back and digest (Straight up)"], "meaning": "“Cake” can work two ways in the line: the first is slang that refers to a big butt. The second, though unlikely, can be taken literally, as cake is eaten and digested.", "annotation_id": "12709657", "url": "https://genius.com/12709657", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Bring us up, I never take us down But if you bring me up, then they might take you down", "vault_lines": ["Bring us up, I never take us down", "But if you bring me up, then they might take you down"], "meaning": "Loyalty to his squad has always been very important to Drake. He’s implying that he helps his squad to get to the top, and won’t do anything to take them down.\n\nIn the song “Headlines” from his classic album Take Care, he states:\n\n‘Cause one of us goes in, and we all go through it\n\nSimilarly to this line, if you mess with one of them, then you mess with all of them. If you bring up drake’s name, his rap family won’t hesitate to take you down.", "annotation_id": "12528176", "url": "https://genius.com/12528176", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Never listen to the class rules (No) Switch it up, they bit the last move (Let’s go!)", "vault_lines": ["Never listen to the class rules (No)", "Switch it up, they bit the last move (Let's go!)"], "meaning": "Quavo solidifies himself as a leader in the rap industry, claiming that he is a trend setter and other people take his style.\n\nHis claims prove to be true as he, and the  Migos, have created worldwide trends including “Pipe It Up,” the “Dab” and their hit song “Bad and Boujee.”\n\nQuavo talked about their style during an interview with Paper Magazine in 2015:\n\nWe rap about our fashion so much in our music, and how we dress and how we set the trends, and how we came up. We came up talking about our swag anyways. That’s what blew us up.", "annotation_id": "11723170", "url": "https://genius.com/11723170", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Murda on the beat so it’s not nice", "vault_lines": ["Murda on the beat so it's not nice"], "meaning": "This is one of Canadian producer Murda Beatz' production tags. This tag also appears on track two of More Life, “No Long Talk,” which features Giggs. Both artists have collaborated with Murda before.\n\nThe voice tag first appeared on fellow OVO crew member Baka’s song “AKA,” which Murda produced in 2016.", "annotation_id": "11541607", "url": "https://genius.com/11541607", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "I mix, double the cup when I stress", "vault_lines": ["I mix, double the cup when I stress"], "meaning": "“Double cup” refers to lean, a mixture of promethazine with codeine (cough syrup), Sprite, and Jolly Ranchers. Using two cups keeps the drink cold, though it is more of a fad now and not solely used for lean.", "annotation_id": "11536661", "url": "https://genius.com/11536661", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Don’t come around thinkin’ you gettin’ saved", "vault_lines": ["Don't come around thinkin' you gettin' saved"], "meaning": "“Getting' saved” means taking someone out of a tough life/financial situation and giving them a better life through a romantic relationship.\n\nDrake warns his side girl that he’s here for a good time and nothing more.", "annotation_id": "11535329", "url": "https://genius.com/11535329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Bitin’ everybody, which is ironic ’Cause your next album probably won’t ever see the light of day Had fans, but you let ’em down But I guess that’s how you niggas gettin’ down", "vault_lines": ["Had fans, but you let 'em down", "But I guess that's how you niggas gettin' down", "Bitin' everybody, which is ironic", "'Cause your next album probably won't ever see the light of day"], "meaning": "This is probably a shot at Tory Lanez whose first name is Daystar. Drake has been subbing Tory as far back as January 2016, notably on his track, “Summer Sixteen.”\n\nTory responded to Drake on Sway Freestyle Pt.2:\n\nAnd this shit is ironic that the outcome and look the album is this summer sixteen\n\nTory released his album I Told You in August 2016, but Drake suggests it will be his only one.\n\nThere’s also an underlying play on vampires here—they are known to bite their enemies and stay hidden in the dark since their skins burn in daylight.", "annotation_id": "11534349", "url": "https://genius.com/11534349", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Free Max B up out the bracelets (Yeah, yeah) Don’t hit us askin’ what the wave is", "vault_lines": ["Free Max B up out the bracelets (Yeah, yeah)", "Don't hit us askin' what the wave is"], "meaning": "“The bracelets” refers to handcuffs—Max B is currently in prison.\n\nMax B is best known for pioneering “wavy” hip-hop, which is the root cause of Wiz Khalifa taking shots at Kanye for naming his album Waves without Max B’s blessing.", "annotation_id": "11534316", "url": "https://genius.com/11534316", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Yeah, my side girl got a 5S with the screen cracked Still hit me back right away, better not never hesitate", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, my side girl got a 5S with the screen cracked", "Still hit me back right away, better not never hesitate"], "meaning": "At the time of More Life’s release, the iPhone 5S was about 3.5 years old. Drake points out that despite the age of his side girl’s phone and its state of disrepair (which presumably makes communication difficult) she still texts him back immediately due to her fervent loyalty.\n\n\n\nHe also boasts that he doesn’t purchase nice things for her, but still texts him back right away for whatever he needs.", "annotation_id": "11534111", "url": "https://genius.com/11534111", "state": "accepted", "votes": 241, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Throwin’ Dead Prez in my livin’ room, yeah (In my room)", "vault_lines": ["Throwin' Dead Prez in my livin' room, yeah (In my room)"], "meaning": "American bills have deceased former U.S. Presidents on the front.", "annotation_id": "11534104", "url": "https://genius.com/11534104", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "I got the keys to the streets (Keys)", "vault_lines": ["I got the keys to the streets (Keys)", "I got the keys to the warzone (Brrapt)"], "meaning": "This is a nod to a song Quavo worked on with fellow Atlanta rapper YFN Lucci, “Key to the Streets.”", "annotation_id": "11534067", "url": "https://genius.com/11534067", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Ike Turner with the left hand (Ike)", "vault_lines": ["Ike Turner with the left hand (Ike)"], "meaning": "Ike Turner was a famous R&B artist and husband of Tina Turner, while Gangland is a modern television show exploring some of America’s most notorious street gangs, from the heroin kingpins in 1970s Harlem to today’s most dangerous gang, MS-13.\n\n“Left hand” and “right hand” are probably references to whipping crack cocaine. Like Ike Turner notoriously abused his wife Tina with his fists, Quavo whips up crack with just as much force.", "annotation_id": "11533045", "url": "https://genius.com/11533045", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Quavo, young nigga, I invented you (Ugh)", "vault_lines": ["Quavo, young nigga, I invented you (Ugh)"], "meaning": "Quavo’s group, Migos, has laid claim to re-popularizing the “Versace flow” or triplet flow, as heard in hits such as “Cross the Country”.\n\nThis, along with a multitude of other aspects of popular culture impacted by Migos, has led many to name Quavo a major influence on the rap game.", "annotation_id": "11533004", "url": "https://genius.com/11533004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Park the Benz just to ride the Wraith Skrrt skrrt Ten million dollars, gotta hide the safe [Post-Chorus: Quavo] Skrrt-skrrt, oh", "vault_lines": ["Park the Benz just to ride the Wraith", "Ten million dollars, gotta hide the safe"], "meaning": "“Benz” refers to Mercedes-Benz, a high end automobile company, whose ultra-luxurious variants Drake himself has namedropped before.\n\n“Wraith” refers to the Rolls-Royce Wraith, which retails at around $300,000 and is an English car owned by a  German company.", "annotation_id": "11532924", "url": "https://genius.com/11532924", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Hell nah Never let these niggas ride your wave Nope, no way, nah Never let these niggas ride your wave Nope, no way, nah", "vault_lines": ["Never let these niggas ride your wave", "Nope, no way, nah"], "meaning": "“Riding the wave” refers to copying or following whatever style is currently popular in the moment. This has plagued Quavo and his group Migos from the beginning of their careers, especially in the case of them re-popularizing the triplet flow and other pop culture phenomenons like the dab.\n\nMany fans have found the irony in this hook since both Travis Scott and Drake have been accused of being culture vultures and wave riders.", "annotation_id": "11532891", "url": "https://genius.com/11532891", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Yeah It’s a Habibis ting, ya?", "vault_lines": ["It's a Habibis ting, ya?"], "meaning": "Habibi is a term of endearment in the Arabic language. It means “my love.” He is saying it’s a “family thing” and calling Quavo and Travis his brothers.\n\nHabibi (@habibizcafe) is also a very close friend of Drake who runs Habibi’s Hookah Lounge in Toronto, Canada.", "annotation_id": "11532696", "url": "https://genius.com/11532696", "state": "accepted", "votes": 187, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "I could never have a kid, then be out here still kiddin’ round Boys playin’ around, where you really wanna take it now?", "vault_lines": ["I could never have a kid, then be out here still kiddin' round", "Boys playin' around, where you really wanna take it now?"], "meaning": "This may be a subliminal aimed at JAY-Z—Drake and Hov have been in a cold-war of sorts for the last few years.\n\nHov had his daughter in 2012, but Beyonce suspected he was cheating and devoted her 2016 album Lemonade to the topic.\n\nDrake collaborated with Jay early in his career on “Light Up,” “Pound Cake,” and on “Off That,” but since then, the pair have done nothing but thrown subliminals.\n\nJay is 47 and rose to wealth and fame in his mid thirties and forties. Drake, on the other hand, is only 30 and plans on out-doing Jay by becoming richer at a younger age.", "annotation_id": "11532295", "url": "https://genius.com/11532295", "state": "accepted", "votes": 220, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Yeah, I might just go get the fade, buy the du-rag and just tie the waves", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I might just go get the fade, buy the du-rag and just tie the waves"], "meaning": "A “fade” is a type of haircut. The most popular one being a “ball fade”, which Travis had in his younger years.\n\n\n\n“Du-rags” are fiber-like materials usually worn around the head.  Travis has been wearing various du-rags recently, most notably on the front covers of Paper Magazine. Du-rags are generally used for “waves\"—they help maintain the waves in place.", "annotation_id": "11532173", "url": "https://genius.com/11532173", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Yeah, it’s lit at the crib, you could stay, me and Drake kick like Kid N’ Play (Kid N’ Play)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, it's lit at the crib, you could stay, me and Drake kick like Kid N' Play (Kid N' Play)"], "meaning": "Kid ‘n Play is a hip-hop duo consisting of  Christopher Reid (Kid) and Christopher Martin (Play). Chris Reid released a solo title “Why Don’t You Stay”. Travis Scott compares Drake and himself with the hip-hop duo.", "annotation_id": "11531996", "url": "https://genius.com/11531996", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Gettin’ loose (Yeah), yeah Off the Henny, ’Roc and a hint of Goose (Yeah), yeah", "vault_lines": ["Off the Henny, 'Roc and a hint of Goose (Yeah), yeah"], "meaning": "Travis is mixing Grey Goose vodka, Ciroc vodka (“the Roc”), and Hennessy. Travis also had a similar line on his song “The Hooch”:\n\nWrist, wrist, mix up the hooch\nMix up the hooch\nNo this not Goose, this the hooch\nYeah, off the hooch, umm yeah\nFeelin' kinda loose off the hooch\nYeah, loose off the hooch\nMix, mix, wrist, mix, mix, wrist up the hooch\nMix up the hooch\n\nGoose Hollow is also a well-known nightlife district in Downtown Portland, and is known as the place in Portland for black market drugs.", "annotation_id": "11531976", "url": "https://genius.com/11531976", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "No, I did not sign with Jay but I still send a Tidal wave", "vault_lines": ["No, I did not sign with Jay but I still send a Tidal wave"], "meaning": "Travis was not part of the co-ownership deal for Jay Z’s streaming service, Tidal, that his mentor, Kanye West was involved in. La Flame also didn’t sign to Jay’s Roc Nation label, instead choosing to sign as a rapper with TI’s Grand Hustle & L.A. Reid’s Epic and with Kanye’s Very G.O.O.D. Beats as a producer.\n\nWhen releasing his second album Birds In The Trap Sing McKnight, Scott made a deal with Apple Music, one of Tidal’s main competitors. Despite not signing with Tidal his album was still very successful.", "annotation_id": "11531939", "url": "https://genius.com/11531939", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Undertaker with the tattoos (Tats)", "vault_lines": ["Undertaker with the tattoos (Tats)"], "meaning": "The Undertaker is a famous professional wrestler for WWE. He’s known for having large amounts of tattoos, something he shares with Quavo.\n\n\n\n\n\nIt’s worth noting that in the lines preceding this one about a fighter, Quavo refers to his left and right hand.", "annotation_id": "11531900", "url": "https://genius.com/11531900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Michael Phelps with the swim moves Skrrt-skrrt, oh Michael Jordan with the tennis shoes", "vault_lines": ["Michael Phelps with the swim moves", "Michael Jordan with the tennis shoes"], "meaning": "In these lines, Quavo compares his rap prowess to that of sports legends that are the best of their respective events.\n\nMichael Phelps is an American former competitive swimmer and the most decorated Olympian of all time with a total of 28 medals.\n\nMichael Jordan is a basketball legend and six time championship holder, who is often referred to as the greatest player of all time.\n\n\n\nIn addition, Jordan has dominated the basketball scene off the court, with his line of Air Jordan basketball shoes spawning a multi-billion dollar industry. The brand is a product of Nike, Inc., whose headquarters is located in Beaverton, Oregon, near Portland.\n\nSome fans have noted that the flow of these lines could be a nod to Drake’s “Summer Sixteen”:\n\n Chris Breezy with the dance moves\nMo-G with the dance moves\nAve Boy with the dance moves", "annotation_id": "11531657", "url": "https://genius.com/11531657", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Got my mama out the basement (Mama) That’s a different type of face lift", "vault_lines": ["Got my mama out the basement (Mama)", "That's a different type of face lift"], "meaning": "Rather than a surgical procedure to improve signs of ageing, Travis' face lift was getting his family a new house for Christmas.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/trvisXX/status/813068716065910784?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\nhttps://twitter.com/trvisXX/status/813076316123779072?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\nThe purpose of this purchase was obviously to make his mother happy; and note that when you smile, your face lifts literally.\n\nOn 2015’s “Oh My Dis Side,” Travis explained how his mother could no longer kick him out, now that he has purchased her a house.\n\nGot my momma that new house now, oh my\nNow she cannot kick me out now, oh my\nI’ve been up for a long time, oh my", "annotation_id": "11531569", "url": "https://genius.com/11531569", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Griselda Blanco with the trap moves (Trappin’)", "vault_lines": ["Griselda Blanco with the trap moves (Trappin')"], "meaning": "Griselda Blanco aka ‘The Black Widow’ was a member of the Medellín Cartel. Despite being arrested and convicted in the United States, she continued to call shots from within prison before avoiding murder charges and being released from prison and deported to Colombia in 2004.", "annotation_id": "11531362", "url": "https://genius.com/11531362", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Takin’ shots, feelin’ bulletproof", "vault_lines": ["Takin' shots, feelin' bulletproof"], "meaning": "Travis takes a play on the words “shots” and “bulletproof.” Travis is taking so many shots (alcohol) that he feels unbeatable. This is a common effect of alcohol, as many people feel invincible when they are drinking. It also could be a reference to Bulleit Bourbon.\n\nThere are also similarities here to Kanye’s bar off “Glow”:\n\nMan the glow got me feelin' like I’m gunproof.\n\n\n\nIn keeping with the firearms theme, ‘taking shots’ could refer to both throwing shade at people, or literally firing a gun.", "annotation_id": "11531326", "url": "https://genius.com/11531326", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "line": "Flood my Rollie, told my bitch: “Let’s go snorkelin’!” (Yeah) Out in Portland, tryna get in her organs (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Flood my Rollie, told my bitch: \"Let's go snorkelin'!\" (Yeah)", "Out in Portland, tryna get in her organs (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Just a day before the release of Drake’s More Life, Travis tweeted that Portland, Oregon was his favorite place in the U.S. to find peace.\n\n“Getting in her organs” is both a play on “Oregon” and a sexual innuendo.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/trvisXX/status/842766234676613120", "annotation_id": "11530900", "url": "https://genius.com/11530900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Portland", "status": "done", "targets": 33, "clicked": 33, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-portland-lyrics", "genius_title": "Portland"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "Baby girl gon’ suck it till her jaws locked (Suck it up)", "vault_lines": ["Baby girl gon' suck it till her jaws locked (Suck it up)"], "meaning": "According to University Health News, lockjaw is a painful medical condition that causes the temporomandibular joint, the joint connecting the jaw to the skull, to swell, hurt and not perform as expected, like it’s “locked”. Causes can include too much chewing and opening your jaw to wide, which could be similar motions to performing oral sex as 21  implies.\n\n\n\nMan suffering from lockjaw", "annotation_id": "16890433", "url": "https://genius.com/16890433", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "We got London on da Track", "vault_lines": ["We got London on da Track"], "meaning": "“Sneakin'” was produced by London on da Track, and this is his signature producer tag. London sat down with Genius and described making the beat for the first episode of the “Deconstructed” series.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BLhGk9zQxpQ", "annotation_id": "11792689", "url": "https://genius.com/11792689", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "We the ones poppin’, we the ones shoppin’ (21)", "vault_lines": ["We the ones poppin', we the ones shoppin' (21)"], "meaning": "The mere fact he’s appearing on a track with Drake is proof Savage’s career is “poppin'.” He also has his own merch collaboration with Metro Boomin, and his very own Timbaland boot.", "annotation_id": "10754511", "url": "https://genius.com/10754511", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "We the one droppin’ niggas, we the ones (We the ones)", "vault_lines": ["We the one droppin' niggas, we the ones (We the ones)"], "meaning": "“Droppin' niggas” simultaneously refers to killing people (dropping bodies), and releasing new music (dropping songs).", "annotation_id": "10738686", "url": "https://genius.com/10738686", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "Got my Spanish ting convinced that I know Spanish Really, when she get to talkin’, I don’t understand it", "vault_lines": ["Got my Spanish ting convinced that I know Spanish", "Really, when she get to talkin', I don't understand it"], "meaning": "This line is reminiscent of the opening skit in French Montana’s “No Shopping” music video, where El Draké shows off his hilarious impersonation of a Dominican accent.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/mDU-5jwPKmQ?t=15s\nIt appears that Drake has become fluent enough with his Spanglish to convince his Latina friend that he can actually comprende.", "annotation_id": "10715975", "url": "https://genius.com/10715975", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "I get the hits like somebody pitchin’ underhanded", "vault_lines": ["I get the hits like somebody pitchin' underhanded"], "meaning": "Drake gets hits on the Billboard charts like he’s playing softball, a sport where the pitcher throws the ball underhanded, a style of pitch that’s, traditionally, much easier to hit.\n\nIn 2015, Drake actually participated in a celebrity softball game.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/3ARlAKyH0l/", "annotation_id": "10713554", "url": "https://genius.com/10713554", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "Mom’s stressed, there’s a lot of things we need Chain smokin’ in the house, it’s hard to breathe I used to have to hit my Ts with Febreze Jordan pull up in the TL, now I’m cheese", "vault_lines": ["Mom's stressed, there's a lot of things we need", "Chain smokin' in the house, it's hard to breathe", "I used to have to hit my Ts with Febreze", "Jordan pull up in the TL, now I'm cheese"], "meaning": "Another reference to close friend and OVO member Jordan. Drake’s reminiscing back to when he wasn’t a superstar and had to use Febreze, an odour eliminator (to remove the tobacco), and drive around in Acura TL’s.\n\n“Cheesed” is Toronto slang for upset—Drake’s upset Jordan isn’t driving a better car and is determined to become successful so they can upgrade, something he goes into more detail about in the lines to follow.", "annotation_id": "10711559", "url": "https://genius.com/10711559", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "I’ma turn this TSX into a Benz soon I’ma see a lot of blessin’s for my friends soon Hunnid-fifty-inch screen in my bedroom 6'1\", man, a nigga need some leg room", "vault_lines": ["I'ma turn this TSX into a Benz soon", "I'ma see a lot of blessin's for my friends soon", "Hunnid-fifty-inch screen in my bedroom", "6'1\", man, a nigga need some leg room"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to the Acura TSX. He has rapped about his experience driving Acuras many times throughout his career.\n\nIn 2015, he claimed to have copped a Mercedes Benz Pullman, a car that can easily accommodate his 6 foot frame. He’s evidently moved on, and now plans to share that success (blessings) amongst his friends and crew.\n\n\n\nCollaborator Starrah tweeted these lines during Ep. 30 of OVO Sound, starting speculation that she ghost-wrote Drake’s parts.\n\n\n\nDrake also bragged about having a 100\" TV before on “Versace.”\n\nHundred inch TV at my house\nI sit back, like, “Damn, I look great on it.”", "annotation_id": "10710400", "url": "https://genius.com/10710400", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "’Bout to take over your city and you can’t stand it My accountant say you sinkin’ like the Titanic David Blaine last summer, man, you had to vanish", "vault_lines": ["'Bout to take over your city and you can't stand it", "My accountant say you sinkin' like the Titanic", "David Blaine last summer, man, you had to vanish"], "meaning": "This could be a shot at Drake’s rival Meek Mill. When Drake performed in Meek’s hometown of Philadelphia, the crowd went wild when Drake played diss songs aimed at Meek. Drake also implies that Meek’s financial situation is as dire at the fate of the Titanic, a passenger steamship that hit an iceberg and sank in 1912, killing 1514 people.\n\nDrake uses illusionist David Blane, who is adept at making things disappear, to point out that he was so prominent last summer that everybody else (including Meek) seemed to have vanish like one of Blaine’s magic tricks. Drake also appeared in a David Blaine special alongside Stephen Curry and Dave Chapelle, who made fun of Meek in front of Drake. https://youtu.be/g0fylxoxC_o?t=7", "annotation_id": "10710268", "url": "https://genius.com/10710268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "I don’t pay no mind to no sneak diss (Nope)", "vault_lines": ["I don't pay no mind to no sneak diss (Nope)"], "meaning": "In “No Heart” Savage made his thoughts clear on sneak dissing, and how he handles it:\n\nPussy niggas love sneak dissing ‘til I pull up on 'em, slap 'em out with the fire", "annotation_id": "10710263", "url": "https://genius.com/10710263", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "Shit ain’t been the same like before I still love it but I used to love it more", "vault_lines": ["Shit ain't been the same like before", "I still love it but I used to love it more"], "meaning": "Drake’s career has left its honeymoon period. He now faces multiple beefs, label problems, and critics claiming he’s lost his inspiration in their appraisal of his 2016 album Views. He claimed rap was “something I do on the side” on 2016’s “Big Amount,” and he seems to insinuate he’s lost some of his passion for hip-hop, or music in general. Maybe he won’t wait till 35 to retire?\n\nThe line is also likely a reference to the title of his third album, Nothing Was the Same, and this would be the sense in which things are no longer the same, “like before”.", "annotation_id": "10710226", "url": "https://genius.com/10710226", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "I been tryna change life around for everyone I know ’Cause if I don’t share the wealth Then how the fuck we supposed to grow?", "vault_lines": ["I been tryna change life around for everyone I know", "'Cause if I don't share the wealth", "Then how the fuck we supposed to grow?"], "meaning": "These lines are likely referring to all the beef Drake has been handling, most of which wasn’t instigated by him. The implication is he empowers those around him, rather than aiming diss records at them.\n\nHe’s done this via his OVO music label, which has helped the careers of PARTYNEXTDOOR, Majid Jordan, Roy Woods, ​dvsn, ILoveMakonnen, and brought more fame and prosperity to producers Noah “40” Shebib and Boi-1da.\n\nHe’s also reached out to the Grime community, and, either way you look at it, helped Dancehall return to the charts.", "annotation_id": "10710027", "url": "https://genius.com/10710027", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "FaceTime with my shawty on tour And she textin’ purple hearts ’cause she know that we at war, yuh", "vault_lines": ["FaceTime with my shawty on tour", "And she textin' purple hearts 'cause she know that we at war, yuh"], "meaning": "The “Purple Heart” is a medal given to American soldiers wounded in combat. Drake’s war is being fought against rival rappers while he is on tour, similar to the military definition of “tour of duty.” Drake’s girl is sending him purple hearts for the valor he is displaying.\n\n\n\nAfter the release of this song, fans have taken to posting purple hearts underneath Rihanna’s Instagram photos.", "annotation_id": "10710019", "url": "https://genius.com/10710019", "state": "accepted", "votes": 206, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "And I’m sellin’ out arenas but you puttin’ on a show", "vault_lines": ["And I'm sellin' out arenas but you puttin' on a show"], "meaning": "Drake’s Summer Sixteen Tour sold out venues like Madison Square Garden four nights in a row, and when he performed in Meek Mill’s home town of Philadelphia, he sold it out as well.\n\nMeanwhile, it was claimed that Meek tried to influence the concert with a “blockade,” but was unsuccessful. While Drake is selling out the Wells Fargo Arena, Meek’s crew was busy putting on a show on Instagram.", "annotation_id": "10710007", "url": "https://genius.com/10710007", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "21 Savage a.k.a. The Reaper, huh (21) Pussy niggas gettin’ shot for all that reachin’, huh (Ya dig?)", "vault_lines": ["21 Savage a.k.a. The Reaper, huh (21)", "Pussy niggas gettin' shot for all that reachin', huh (Ya dig?)"], "meaning": "21 Savage is sometimes referred to as the “Atlanta Grim Reaper.” The Grim Reaper is a personification of death, as either the bringer or collector of.\n\n\n\nIn the hook Drake uses the term “reaching” to explain how his enemies are using irrelevant (to him, anyway) evidence or information to take him down. 21 explains that anyone employing this same tactic on him will be killed. Reaching for a weapon, either physically or metaphorically, would trigger the “Atlanta Grim Reaper” to shoot without hesitation.", "annotation_id": "10710006", "url": "https://genius.com/10710006", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "Used to sleep inside a den, I got acres now (Mansion) I was fightin’ niggas then but I’m sprayin’ now (Blastin’)", "vault_lines": ["Used to sleep inside a den, I got acres now (Mansion)", "I was fightin' niggas then but I'm sprayin' now (Blastin')"], "meaning": "21 Savage had a difficult youth, being expelled from all schools in his area for carrying a gun, getting shot six times, and losing his friend to the streets. During an interview with Fader, he took them to an apartment complex where “Within minutes, prescription opiates are changing hands.”\n\nClearly, his success has afforded him the luxury of a house on a large piece of property.", "annotation_id": "10709982", "url": "https://genius.com/10709982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "This is all God’s doin’, man, you can’t plan it But if the devil’s in the details, then I’m Satanic", "vault_lines": ["This is all God's doin', man, you can't plan it", "But if the devil's in the details, then I'm Satanic"], "meaning": "Drake claims his wild success is “God’s doing,” a catch-all for results that can only be bestowed by fate. Then again, Drake calls himself the “6 God,” so maybe he’s asserting that he orchestrated his own success.\n\n“The devil is in the details” is an idiom that implies failure awaits those who don’t pay attention to nuance. Drake’s career was incredibly strategic in 2016, a success which he attributes to his ability to focus on the details.", "annotation_id": "10709970", "url": "https://genius.com/10709970", "state": "accepted", "votes": 150, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "Baby, I’m a savage, I ain’t romantic When I come around, these rapper niggas start to panic And they pockets goin’ under like the Titanic", "vault_lines": ["Baby, I'm a savage, I ain't romantic", "When I come around, these rapper niggas start to panic", "And they pockets goin' under like the Titanic"], "meaning": "Savage uses the same reference Drake did in the first verse, implying his own success is taking money out of the pockets of his competitors. Their stock is sinking like the Titanic.", "annotation_id": "10709968", "url": "https://genius.com/10709968", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "You ain’t own it right away, you had to wait on niggas Man, I’m only twenty-nine, have some patience with us Plus I never met nobody from my label, nigga I just pop up with the music then they pay a nigga (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["You ain't own it right away, you had to wait on niggas", "Man, I'm only twenty-nine, have some patience with us", "Plus I never met nobody from my label, nigga", "I just pop up with the music then they pay a nigga (Yeah)"], "meaning": "This couplet might be a response to Jay Z. On “Summer Sixteen,” Drake rapped:\n\nI used to wanna be on Roc-A-Fella then I turned into Jay\n\nWhich may have prompted these lines from Jay on DJ Khaled’s “I Got The Keys”:\n\nTill you own your own you can’t be free\nTill you’re on your own you can’t be me\n\nDrake’s argument is that he’s only 29 and, like Jay, will eventually own the direction of his career—his own label and master rights included. Jay formed his own record label, Roc-A-Fella, with Dame Dash and Kareem Burke in 1995 (he was 26), and won ownership of his masters in 2015 when he was 45. Jay and Drake’s masters tit-for-tat dates back to Jay’s “We Made It (Freestyle)”:\n\nI own my own masters\nYou know I ain’t missin' no royalty statements\n\nWhile Drake is still signed to Cash Money/Young Money, he rarely collaborates with his fellow  signees outside of Nicki Minaj and Lil Wayne, and it’s reported that More Life will be his last on the imprint.\n\nAlthough this song was recorded when Drake was 29, it debuted on OVO Sound Radio the day before Drake’s 30th birthday.", "annotation_id": "10709963", "url": "https://genius.com/10709963", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "I don’t need love, I’m the GOAT, I just hit the beat and float", "vault_lines": ["I don't need love, I'm the GOAT, I just hit the beat and float"], "meaning": "Drake claims to be the GOAT—“Greatest of All Time.” He’s constantly shattering records, whether it is releasing the most streamed song on Spotify of all time, or his album hitting triple platinum in less than 6 months, Drake is a crucial player in the music industry.\n\nHe’s content with these achievements, and doesn’t need universal acclaim or extra “love” to confirm his status.\n\n\n\nLL Cool J also called himself “The G.O.A.T.” and has a track entitled “I Need Love.” The greatest boxer of all time Muhammad Ali also “floated.”", "annotation_id": "10709941", "url": "https://genius.com/10709941", "state": "accepted", "votes": 122, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "line": "Niggas keep reachin’ and dissin’ Can’t afford mistakes, not on this end Make me call my bros for assistance You gon’ make me call my bros for assistance Niggas keep reachin’ and dissin’ Put me in a fucked-up position (Yah) You gon’ make me call my bros for assistance (21) Make me call my bro (21, uh)", "vault_lines": ["Niggas keep reachin' and dissin'", "Can't afford mistakes, not on this end", "Make me call my bros for assistance", "You gon' make me call my bros for assistance", "Put me in a fucked-up position (Yah)", "Make me call my bro (21, uh)", "She gon' make me put my bitch on call block (Fuckin' up)", "Make me call my bro, dawg", "Make me call my bro, don't"], "meaning": "Meek Mill, Joe Budden, and Kid Cudi have all initiated their beefs with Drake. Since Drake has been the most successful rapper of the last 2 years, he’s arguably got the most to lose in these conflicts if he slips up.\n\nLuckily, he has his his entire OVO set to call if things go awry. It seems Jas Prince already assisted him in Philly.", "annotation_id": "10709887", "url": "https://genius.com/10709887", "state": "accepted", "votes": 152, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sneakin’", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 21, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-sneakin-lyrics", "genius_title": "Sneakin’"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "I’ll tell ’em link up at the valet at the Hazy", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The “Hazy” is the nickname for The Hazleton Hotel, a five-star hotel in Toronto’s Yorkdale neighborhood.", "annotation_id": "41369155", "url": "https://genius.com/41369155", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Jewels look like I found a motherfuckin’ treasure map And ain’t told no one where the fuck it’s at", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The money and riches Drake has is been accumulated liked the jewels. He is keeping the success secret, like not telling anyone where he found the treasure map.", "annotation_id": "40571820", "url": "https://genius.com/40571820", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "[Verse 3: Lil Wayne]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The Wayne verse on this version has a different mix than the OG with the released cut using noticeably less Auto-Tune.", "annotation_id": "39439666", "url": "https://genius.com/39439666", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Fuck my nigga Terry for a new Blackberry", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "terry seems too small to be worth an iphone 6, but a blackberry will do", "annotation_id": "29706146", "url": "https://genius.com/29706146", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Way more gully gully than buddy buddy Never needed your acceptance, never needed nothin’", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This diss could be a possible jab at famous hip-hop enthusiast, Joe Budden. Drake has gone after Budden multiple times including in his songs  4pm In Calabasas  &  No Shopping  with French Montana where Drake says\n\nPump, pump, pump it up (Skrrt)\nShe got a good head on her, but I pump it up (Skrrt)\nI’m not a one hit wonder, they know all my stuff (Haan)\nYou let me turn into the nigga that you almost was”\n\nBudden fully explained the beef to Hot 97’s Ebro Darden back in 2016.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/5G_EwfZ85Wk", "annotation_id": "25290424", "url": "https://genius.com/25290424", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Make sure the plane got a phone now So when we ’bout to land I can call to tell the woes I’m home now", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In an interview with Noisey, Toronto rapper and OVO Sound affiliate Devontee defines the meaning behind his and Drake’s ‘woes’:\n\n“Woe is my crew. It stands for ‘working on excellence.’ It’s just my whole brand and my whole movement and my way of life for everyone. I want everyone to work on excellence. So, all my friends are my Woes and I feel anybody working on excellence in life is a Woe in life as well.”\n\nWhen Drake returns to his home in the six (Toronto), he’s able to call his crew to get together.", "annotation_id": "20086446", "url": "https://genius.com/20086446", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "[Produced by WondaGurl]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In an episode of The Creator Class' ORIGINALS series, WondaGurl unveiled the sample (Marcello Giombini’s “Synthotango”) and how she sent this beat to Drake:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/J7unBlyNGeg?t=137", "annotation_id": "8338874", "url": "https://genius.com/8338874", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Y’all don’t understand, I’m the one they want to hear from Y’all don’t understand that it’s me or nothin’", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a reference to the beef between Drake and Diddy. Originally, Diddy wanted to use the beat that Boi-1da had sent him. However, Drake quickly hopped on the beat, and released 0 to 100/The Catch Up, which infuriated Diddy, causing him to punch Drake.\n\n\n\nDrake responds here by saying that the people would rather hear from him than anyone else, being that he is a legend.\n\nAnd just like every single other thing in my life, you can have her when I’m finished", "annotation_id": "4920497", "url": "https://genius.com/4920497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Shaq postin’ up on niggas that I used to have posters of", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This line might refer to the retired basketball player Shaquille O'Neal, who was known for his dominant plays in the post.\n\nDrake is a big fan of basketball and is currently working as the Global Ambassador of his hometown team in the NBA: Toronto Raptors.\n\n(e.g., Shaq with some post moves)\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MxZQw-dEydU\n\nHe could also be referring to the fact that the same artists that have influenced his drive for music (e.g., Kanye West and Jay-Z) are currently the same individuals he is up against in Hip-Hop, and possibly will overcome towards the latter of his career.", "annotation_id": "4898112", "url": "https://genius.com/4898112", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Real quick, man, you couldn’t have hated that Let’s be real, nigga, you couldn’t have made it that, woo!", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "It is rumored that Diddy smacked Drake over the song “0 to 100.” In the chorus he says, “0 to 100 nigga real quick,” because Diddy gave the instrumental to Drake to write something for him to rap over, but instead he put it out for himself.\n\nHere, Drake refers to the controversy saying that Diddy couldn’t have hated the move he made because he couldn’t have made such an amazing record on his own.", "annotation_id": "4891897", "url": "https://genius.com/4891897", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Yeah, I’m fuckin’ glowin’ up", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is not only glowin' up in the rap industry, he’s outshining everyone else around him. Recently, Drake has been dominating the game and with the recent release of If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, Drake is further emphasizing his dominance.\n\nThis is also more than likely a reference to the Glo Up Challenge on Twitter where you post pictures of your past and compare them to the current.\n\nYeah, you ain’t really fuck with me way back then, girl but How Bout Now?\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to Chief Keef and his Glo Gang.", "annotation_id": "4888712", "url": "https://genius.com/4888712", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Although this song appeared on both Lil Wayne’s Sorry 4 The Wait 2 and Drake’s If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, this verse was only featured on the latter.", "annotation_id": "4888426", "url": "https://genius.com/4888426", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "I be with the bands Like a nigga went to Jackson State or Grambling Young Nick Cannon with the snare drum, dancin’ Watch the way I handle it, uh", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "We were not really feeling this line…AT ALL", "annotation_id": "4768262", "url": "https://genius.com/4768262", "state": "verified", "votes": -854, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Trap pumpin’ all night like Chevron", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Chevron is an energy corporation that owns gas stations. The “trap” can refer to Wayne’s trap music, or his trap house. Regardless, the trap is pumping like the gas pumped at a Chevron gas station – 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.", "annotation_id": "4767972", "url": "https://genius.com/4767972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Boy, do I smell beef? Mmmm, pheromones", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Beef is the common term for a fight or feud and a lot of animals are attracted to each other by chemical releases called pheromones. Weezy is saying that rather than run from it, he loves a good fight; anyone who wants to beef with him had better beware.", "annotation_id": "4761331", "url": "https://genius.com/4761331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Promethazine over pinot", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Wayne prefers promethazine, an ingredient in lean, which he was strongly addicted to, rather than wine – pinot is a grape used to make wine.", "annotation_id": "4761277", "url": "https://genius.com/4761277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Goin’ at a nigga throat like a herringbone", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Herringbone is a weaving pattern in fabric, typically seen in neckties (or chains). Blood would splatter all over the herringbone tie if the wearer’s throat was slashed. Manners, Wayne.", "annotation_id": "4761061", "url": "https://genius.com/4761061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "I got, mind control over Deebo", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A reference to the classic film, Friday.\n\nhttp://m.youtube.com/watch?v=Kw31fE_AWsI", "annotation_id": "4760645", "url": "https://genius.com/4760645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Shout out to the G’s from the ends We don’t love no girls from the ends", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In a nod to Skepta’s 2014 banger “That’s Not Me”, Drake informs the listener that even neighborhood girls don’t get love from him or his crew.\n\n“Ends” is a London slang term for “neighborhood”, as referenced in Skepta’s track. This can also apply to Drake because in Toronto they refer to different parts of the city as the “East End and West End” of the city.\n\nDrake credits Skepta as an inspiration in the album notes for If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, and has praised the artist in a series of recent Instagram posts.", "annotation_id": "4760266", "url": "https://genius.com/4760266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "When you get to where the fuck I’m at You gotta remind ’em about where you been About all of the money that done came and went About the two cents I ain’t never spent When they say you’re too famous to pack a gat I gotta remind ’em about where I’m from", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is from Hollygrove, New Orleans, where the crime rate is high. Due to the high crime rate, it’s a no-brainer that he knows how to handle a gat (slang for gun).\n\nNow, Wayne is sitting on $140 million in net worth. He looks at his current state of living, and compares it to when he was growing up.\n\n\n\nThe idiom two cents is used to describe what someone has to put in with their wisdom. Wayne uses the idiom to say that he has never told somebody how to do their job, so they should not tell him how to do his! This could be in reference to the Cash Money beef.", "annotation_id": "4760032", "url": "https://genius.com/4760032", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "They gon’ say your name on them airwaves They gon’ hit you up right after like it’s only rap", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This could be a shot at Kendrick Lamar, who called out Drake, along with many other rappers,  in his blown up “Control” verse. In the track, Kendrick mentioned that in the end, it’s all just rap.\n\nCould also  be a subliminal at Chris Brown who said during an interview with Sway:\n\nIt’s rap man, it’s hip-hop. I say whatever I want, I’m not a rapper, so you gonna be stupid rapping back to a singer, but I'ma keep coming\n\nhttps://youtu.be/vQdL6XROV90?t=1m5s\n\nDrake and Chris Brown have been beefing for a while.", "annotation_id": "4759707", "url": "https://genius.com/4759707", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Only see the truth when I’m staring in the mirror Lookin’ at myself like, there it is there Yeah, like there it is there man, whoo!", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drizzy alludes to the popular song named “Whoomp! (There It Is)” by Tag Team.\n\nLooking for the truth in a mirror is a waste of time with Drake; it’s standing there right in front of him!", "annotation_id": "4759149", "url": "https://genius.com/4759149", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "And when my bloods start shootin’ that’s B-roll, bitch", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "B-roll is a production term used in film theatres hence the reference to “shooting” both in the sense of making a movie (or making it big) or defending your clique by firing back.\n\nB is also a blood type.", "annotation_id": "4758844", "url": "https://genius.com/4758844", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "I ain’t tryna chance it,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "As Drake said in “Draft Day”:\n\nAnd if I left this shit to chance I would’ve picked a name like Chance the Rapper", "annotation_id": "4758824", "url": "https://genius.com/4758824", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Yeah—sound, sound, sound Yeah, real 6 side shit Sickos, ah man", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drizzy sets the tone by shouting out his label OVO Sound and his home town, Toronto (416).\n\nComing from the 6 side", "annotation_id": "4758795", "url": "https://genius.com/4758795", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "I was only five, but still remember the drought in ’87", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In 1987 the United States suffered a terrible drought.\n\nHowever Lil Wayne is probably referring a series of mix tapes he released earlier during his career called Da Drought. Although he was only five in 1987 he had already seen things that left an impression in his life forever and he incorporated these memories in his music. He has always been a young visionary!", "annotation_id": "4756799", "url": "https://genius.com/4756799", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "Suck a nigga dick for a iPhone 6", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A reference to the classic line in DJ Jimi’s “Where They At”\n\nI said BITCH! stop talking that shit\nAnd suck a nigga dick for an outfit\n\nWeezy previously (and famously) referred to this line on French Montana’s “Pop That”\n\nBitch! Stop talkin' that shit\nAnd suck a nigga dick for some Trukfit", "annotation_id": "4756536", "url": "https://genius.com/4756536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "You can get buried for a ounce of Katy Perry", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Is Wayne really talking about RiFF RAFF’s girl, when he is on the intro and outro?\n\nAnyway, an ounce of Katy Perry refers to an ounce of cocaine, as Katy Perry is a white girl, and “white” is a commonly used term when talking about cocaine.", "annotation_id": "4756420", "url": "https://genius.com/4756420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "I ain’t felt the pressure in a little while It’s gonna take some getting used to (What you say?) Floatin’ all through the city with the windows down Puttin’ on like I used to (What you say?) They never told me when you get the crown It’s gon’ take some getting used to (What you say?) New friends all in their old feelings now They don’t love you like they used to, man", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Unlike The Throne, Drake feels like no one’s pressuring him musically. Since every song really is Drake featuring Drake – he can afford to forget about Jay Z & Kendrick Lamar’s lyrical jabs.\n\nKeeping the same “No New Friends” policy keeps Drake fleek – those “do you remember me?” acquaintances just get shrugged off.\n\nShe says they miss the old Drake, girl don’t tempt me", "annotation_id": "4756367", "url": "https://genius.com/4756367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "I’m gon hit ’em with the wham once again I’ma always end up as a man in the end, dog It’s just apparent every year", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "To wham someone is to verbally assault them. Drizzy’s coming for the number one spot in 2015, just like he did in 2014, and 2013.\n\nDuring an interview with GQ Magazine, Kanye West had this to say about Drake’s success in 2013:\n\n“[Drake]. He got last summer. And I’d never given it up till last summer.” Now he’s thinking about taking it back. “It’s a real question for me. Do I want to?”\n\nAdditionally, “gon hi'em with the wham once again” could allude to Drake deploying the vegetarian ham replacement ‘Wham\" as a weapon. This effectively captures Drake’s defining characteristics (can go hard but is obviously a softy deep down, hence the meat replacement..)", "annotation_id": "4756352", "url": "https://genius.com/4756352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "line": "I be with the bands Like a nigga went to Jackson State or Grambling Young Nick Cannon with the snare drum, dancin’ Watch the way I handle it, uh", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "On “Believe Me”, Drake made a similar connection between his money and rock bands Nirvana and Coldplay. In this line he takes a new approach: college marching bands.\n\nJackson State, Grambling, Clark-Atlanta and Hampton University are all HBCUs with renowned marching bands. Jackson State even used Drake’s “Trophies” in one of their performances.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L8Db1WJ5-58\n\nTopping off this set of lines is another college band reference: the 2002 film, Drumline, where Nick Cannon plays the role of a talented drummer from Harlem who was recruited to play for the fictional Atlanta A&T University.", "annotation_id": "4756244", "url": "https://genius.com/4756244", "state": "accepted", "votes": 206, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Us", "status": "done", "targets": 31, "clicked": 31, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-used-to-lyrics", "genius_title": "Used To"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "Niggas make me nauseous, I had enough of broke boys I can’t talk to broke boys, yeah, I can’t talk to broke boys I can’t talk to broke boys, I can’t talk to broke boys, wait, yeah I can’t talk to broke boys, I can’t talk to broke boys, wait", "vault_lines": ["Niggas make me nauseous, I had enough of broke boys", "I can't talk to broke boys, yeah, I can't talk to broke boys", "I can't talk to broke boys, I can't talk to broke boys, wait, yeah", "I can't talk to broke boys, I can't talk to broke boys, wait", "I can't talk to broke boys, I can't talk to broke boys", "I can't talk to broke boys", "I can't talk to broke boy, I can't talk to broke boys, I can't talk to broke boys", "I can't talk to broke boys, nah, nah, I can't talk to broke boys, I can't talk to—"], "meaning": "It has circulated widely within various online communities and social media platforms that the renowned Canadian rapper Drake has allegedly appropriated lyrics from the popular streamer FlightReacts, specifically from this track titled “Broken Broke Boi.” However, this claim has been thoroughly investigated and debunked on multiple occasions, demonstrating that there is no substantial evidence to support such allegations.", "annotation_id": "32540710", "url": "https://genius.com/32540710", "state": "pending", "votes": -6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "You boys gettin’ ready to diss, but don’t even mention the O", "vault_lines": ["You boys gettin' ready to diss, but don't even mention the O"], "meaning": "Potential reference to October’s Very Own, Drake’s record label.\n\nUnlikely but could be a reference to OFB in North London, UK. Drake collaborated with Headie One, member of OFB, a collective that goes by the name “the O”, also signalled by the hand sign by bringing the thumb to the index finger in an O shape", "annotation_id": "28902806", "url": "https://genius.com/28902806", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "I’m on a Jet like my last name Li", "vault_lines": ["I'm on a Jet like my last name Li"], "meaning": "21 plays on the homonym “jet”, saying he is on a private jet while also referencing actor Jet Li.", "annotation_id": "27475861", "url": "https://genius.com/27475861", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "Baby, please, make me nervous, stop reachin’ in your purse", "vault_lines": ["Baby, please, make me nervous, stop reachin' in your purse"], "meaning": "‘make me nervous’ as in “you make me nervous'.\n\nHe just says it in very mumbled speech like you might when you’re trying to be low key about what you’re saying to someone.\n\nBeing in Drake’s position has him a little paranoid and untrustworthy, so, when he’s hanging with a woman and she keeps reaching in her purse for things, he gets nervous cause he’s not sure what she might pull out.\n\nReminiscent of previous sentiments about his distrust of the women he deals with.", "annotation_id": "27146581", "url": "https://genius.com/27146581", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "Two sticks in my hand like I’m playin’ the Wii", "vault_lines": ["Two sticks in my hand like I'm playin' the Wii"], "meaning": "In 21 Savage’s case, “sticks” alludes to an automatic firearm. He also refers to a nostalgic 2006 console, Wii and its remotes, referring them as sticks.\n\n\n\nPictured: Wii Remotes.\n\nThis simile implies that he views gang violence as a game, given that the Wii is a gaming console. Additionally, he perhaps sees himself as having perfected aiming when murdering his opponents.", "annotation_id": "27097993", "url": "https://genius.com/27097993", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "My brother would give up his life to the O He told me he gon’ be a martyr for me", "vault_lines": ["My brother would give up his life to the O", "He told me he gon' be a martyr for me"], "meaning": "The “O” is likely in reference to one of two things. Either Drake’s label “OVO” or his hometown Toronto, Ontario.\n\nDrake is ultimately saying his brother would sacrifice his own life or put anything on the line for the “O”.", "annotation_id": "27081541", "url": "https://genius.com/27081541", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "Snakes in the grass so they harder to see", "vault_lines": ["Snakes in the grass so they harder to see"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the idiom “snakes in the grass” meaning a secretly unfaithful friend. Drake says these people are harder to notice than overt haters, as they show love straight up to his face, but are plotting against him behind his back.\n\nHe uses that line to prepare for the next one, which says that he has real friends who would give up their life for OVO.", "annotation_id": "27073224", "url": "https://genius.com/27073224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "Nigga went and bought a house when he coulda bought a verse Niggas dumb as fuck, imagine if I did that stupid shit when I was comin’ up", "vault_lines": ["Nigga went and bought a house when he coulda bought a verse", "Niggas dumb as fuck, imagine if I did that stupid shit when I was comin' up"], "meaning": "Drake encourages upcoming artists to invest money into their career rather than real estate. From Trey Songz on “Replacement Girl” to Lil Wayne on “Man of the Year,” Drake’s feature list on his 2007 mixtape Comeback Season shows that he practices what he preaches by investing in notable features. Driving home this point, Drake only bought his first house in 2012.\n\nThese lines from Drake dovetail with the sentiments he expressed in the chorus of “Privileged Rappers”:\n\nI hate a privileged rapper who don’t even know what it take…\nI hate a privileged rapper that ain’t had a hit since he signed", "annotation_id": "27068241", "url": "https://genius.com/27068241", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "I’m ridin’ around in Atlanta with Sav ‘Cause that nigga been goin’ harder than me", "vault_lines": ["'Cause that nigga been goin' harder than me", "I'm ridin' around in Atlanta with Sav"], "meaning": "21 Savage was born in Newham, England, and raised on the Eastside of Atlanta, Georgia.\n\nPeople say Drake has “fallen off” with his recent albums, Certified Lover Boy, and Honestly, Nevermind. Some say he “fell off” after If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late.\n\n21 Savage, on the other hand, has been “goin' hard” recently, with albums such as i am > i was, Issa Album, Without Warning and Savage Mode II..\n\nDrake is also pointing that walking with 21 out in Atlanta could be a way of protecting himself, since Savage has a lot more gang connections than Drake.", "annotation_id": "27062353", "url": "https://genius.com/27062353", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "We rough niggas up, now they beggin’ for peace (G Block Babies)", "vault_lines": ["We rough niggas up, now they beggin' for peace (G Block Babies)"], "meaning": "This is a reference to “Galloway Road,” also known as G-Block, a hood in Toronto with some of the country’s highest public housing density.", "annotation_id": "27061609", "url": "https://genius.com/27061609", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Broke Boys", "line": "You must be Coach K ’cause you ain’t P", "vault_lines": ["You must be Coach K 'cause you ain't P"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION:\n\n21 plays on the previous reference to QC. Quality Control Music was founded by Kevin “Coach K” Lee and Pierre “P” Thomas and have signed the Migos, Lil Yachty, Lil Baby, and others.", "annotation_id": "27052157", "url": "https://genius.com/27052157", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
