{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "Too early, maybe later you can show me things", "vault_lines": ["Too early, maybe later you can show me things"], "meaning": "In this line he is guessing that he went to fast, and asked the girl too early and she has not have forgotten her previous relationship. He is saying maybe if he was more patient he could have a chance with here.", "annotation_id": "33873013", "url": "https://genius.com/33873013", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "Elevator up to the room", "vault_lines": ["Elevator up to the room"], "meaning": "This lyric is a genius storytelling line from Drake. As he states that he elevators up to his destination room. This links back to Drake’s history of bragging about staying in fancy hotels. With the history of Drake bragging about these hotels in mind we can deduce that Drake is within one of said Hotels and Drake is a man of wealth and fanciness so he does not take the stairs like a peasant. He takes the elevator instead. It’s a lot more fancy. And why an elevator specifically?\nBecause they take you up and down building floors. Hence why he said Elevator up to the room because that’s what elevators are for.", "annotation_id": "33071242", "url": "https://genius.com/33071242", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "Ayy, truck to the plane to the truck Truck to the hotel lobby Me, I go through underground garages", "vault_lines": ["Show to the shower to the truck", "Ayy, truck to the plane to the truck", "Truck to the hotel lobby", "Me, I go through underground garages"], "meaning": "Here Drake states the following:\n“Ayy, truck to the plane to the truck\nTruck to the hotel lobby\nMe, I go through underground garages”\n\nThis lyric is actually genius because here Drake says he goes from truck to plane then back to truck This could be a genius doubleentendre. His first line might signify that Drake travels with trucks because he’s that amazing to plane because he can fly people out and he himself out to places far away then travel by truck again. His second line says that he goes to hotels in his truck. Drake has infamousely bragged about Hotels in plenty of songs before such as in Trophies so it means he goes to grand hotels with a grand entrance too. He also goes through underground garages as he is quite the silly sneaky goose. Drake does that because he moves around in silence without anyone knowing when he’ll strike. Drake says that he can be sneaky and he can strike with a flash of glamour and the second meaning is that Drake probably really likes Trucks. Because that’s what’s unique about Drake. Other rappers pull up in Benzes, Mercedes, Lambos and Drake pulls up with a fucking truck to accommodate this massive penis energy. And I agree. Trucks are fucking awesome. Thanks for shouting out Trucks Drake :D", "annotation_id": "33071191", "url": "https://genius.com/33071191", "state": "pending", "votes": -8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "She said, “Babe, I think there was a typo” Nah, girl, I got it, that’s light though I could show you bigger things than that", "vault_lines": ["She said, \"Babe, I think there was a typo\"", "Nah, girl, I got it, that's light though", "I could show you bigger things than that"], "meaning": "Here  Drake basically says that the girl he was with couldn’t pay for the bill but Drake can because he’s rich. Typical Drake stuff. However because of more recent events the second part might have actually been a double entendre. The first meaning is Drake’s wealth as in Big Whips, Big Cribs etc. And the second is his big you know what. To quote Family Matters “There’s only Big D and there’s Video Proof”. So it seems like Drake was referring to 2 types of big things he could show to this girl.", "annotation_id": "32870071", "url": "https://genius.com/32870071", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "Lot of people tryna take us viral I’m just tryin’ to have a nice night though", "vault_lines": ["Lot of people tryna take us viral", "I'm just tryin' to have a nice night though"], "meaning": "People are always trying to peep who the next relationship a celebrity has, and many try to get the picture to make it go viral, showing the world who they’re with now.", "annotation_id": "24869215", "url": "https://genius.com/24869215", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "Hit another one, it goes green Must’ve changed phones on the team ’Member when you let me in between That was 2017", "vault_lines": ["Hit another one, it goes green", "Must've changed phones on the team", "'Member when you let me in between"], "meaning": "Drake uses an iPhone, any message sent from iPhone to a non-apple device will show up as green, instead of the iPhone to iPhone blue. If it was blue, now its green, this person probably either switched numbers or upgraded phones, thus ‘changed phone on the team’.\n\n\n\nSimultaneously, Drake could be referencing his 2017 song “Free Smoke,” where he said:\n\nI drunk text J-Lo\nOld number, so it bounce back", "annotation_id": "19392926", "url": "https://genius.com/19392926", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "Shower up and then we hit the club", "vault_lines": ["Shower up and then we hit the club"], "meaning": "In his 2011 Complex cover story, Drake told the interviewer that he would have liked to do the interview at his apartment but he was getting installed the most luxurious addition to his bathroom: “an experience shower”.\n\nIt’s a shower that’s lit by all LED lights. It has 10 jets, an overhead, and it sprays out lavender or whatever scents you want. It’s something I’ve always wanted.\n\nHis Toronto mansion, designed by Ferris Rafauli, has a huge tub with a skylight above, along with two rooftop terrace areas with an outdoor hot tub that offers guests to soak their feet in.\n\n\n\nDrake's 4,000 lbs marble bathtub (est. worth around $30,000-$50,000).", "annotation_id": "19190766", "url": "https://genius.com/19190766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "All good, Chubbs will look around Find one that seem my type That my dawg and he know what I like He done found me plenty in my life", "vault_lines": ["All good, Chubbs will look around", "Find one that seem my type", "That my dawg and he know what I like", "He done found me plenty in my life"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his close friend and head of security Nessel Beezer, who mainly goes by the alias “Chubbs” or “OVO Chubbs”.\n\nIn these few lines, Drake explains how Chubbs has been able to come across plenty of women that Drake finds attractive.", "annotation_id": "19185287", "url": "https://genius.com/19185287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "Problem is, I meet a girl tonight Then I go and treat her too nice Galleria credit card swipes I don’t even know if she a wife", "vault_lines": ["Problem is, I meet a girl tonight", "Then I go and treat her too nice", "Galleria credit card swipes", "I don't even know if she a wife"], "meaning": "Galleria is an upscale mall in Houston, TX, a city where Drake frequently raps about and visits.\n\nDrake is saying he is too nice to women who may just be using him for his fame and wealth. Hence why they are using his credit card at malls to shop even though he doesn’t know if they could ever be his wife or if they’re a wife to someone else.", "annotation_id": "19179974", "url": "https://genius.com/19179974", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "Pistol on J for survival Lot of people gunnin’ for the title", "vault_lines": ["Pistol on J for survival", "Lot of people gunnin' for the title"], "meaning": "In these lines, Drake first states and refers to his person security carrying a gun for his protection.\n\nIn the next line, he uses the previous to execute his punchline about other rappers who are out overtake him in the music industry. There are many people who are out to get Drake knocked off his perch at the top of the music industry, and potentially also want him dead. We know that Drake his people who want him dead from the line in his song “Mob Ties” from his 2018 Scorpion Album:\n\nThey want me gone but dont know why\n\nAlso from his lyrics in “I’m Upset”. a single from the album Scorpion, where Drake, in different parts of the song raps these lyrics:\n\nFifty thousand on my head, its disrespect\nHunnid thousand on my head, its disrespect and also\nHalf a million on my head I can accept", "annotation_id": "19176406", "url": "https://genius.com/19176406", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "AMEX had a limit in the past I had plenty women in the past I done plenty sinnin’ in the past You don’t need to know, but you can ask", "vault_lines": ["AMEX had a limit in the past", "I had plenty women in the past", "I done plenty sinnin' in the past", "You don't need to know, but you can ask"], "meaning": "Reminiscing about previous times, Drake reflects on changes in affection and financial circumstances. Addressing his current love interest, he highlights plenty of former relationships despite spending limits on his AMEX or American Express credit card.\n\nFor most consumers, credit card companies impose limits on the amount that one can spend each month. As a product of his established wealth, Drake has since moved on to the infamous American Express Centurion “black card,” an invitation-only charge card with no spending limits.\n\nMoving to the present, Drake finds perspective in examining past financial challenges but does not seek to detail misdeeds from lost love.", "annotation_id": "19176146", "url": "https://genius.com/19176146", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "But I do know one thing though Women, they come they go Saturday through Sunday, Monday Monday through Sunday, yo Maybe I’ll love you one day Maybe we’ll someday grow ’Til then, I’ll sit my drunk ass on that runway On this one way", "vault_lines": ["But I do know one thing though", "Women, they come they go", "Saturday through Sunday, Monday", "Monday through Sunday, yo", "Maybe I'll love you one day", "Maybe we'll someday grow", "'Til then, I'll sit my drunk ass on that runway", "On this one way"], "meaning": "Drake pays tribute to Eminem by interpolating his May 2002 song “Superman” but with slightly changed lyrics:\n\nBut I do know one thing though\nBitches, they come, they go\nSaturday through Sunday, Monday\nMonday through Sunday, yo\nMaybe I’ll love you one day\nMaybe we’ll someday grow\n‘Til then just sit your drunk ass on that fuckin’ runway, ho\n\nIn Eminem’s song, he takes a very aggressive tone. “Superman” was written in the wake of Eminem’s divorce and his falling out with Mariah Carey, with whom he allegedly had a brief relationship. In the chorus, Eminem is speaking to his admirer, who is likely Mariah, and he reminds the woman that she is dispensable. If she acts out, there are plenty of women in line that are ready to take her place.\n\nIn contrast, Drake’s interpretation of the lyrics is melancholic. He has grown tired of quick relationships and is looking for something more serious, but his current lifestyle makes that impossible. In Drake’s version, the “runway” is an airport runway, where he has boarded a one-way flight to his next show.", "annotation_id": "19173768", "url": "https://genius.com/19173768", "state": "accepted", "votes": 586, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "line": "Two-thirty, baby, won’t you meet me by The Bean?", "vault_lines": ["Two-thirty, baby, won't you meet me by The Bean?"], "meaning": "In accordance with the track’s title, Giveon drops multiple iconic characteristics of the track’s titular city.\n\nFirstly, Cloud Gate, known by its popular name “The Chicago Bean,” is a public art sculpture designed by Indian-British sculptor Anish Kapoor that was first unveiled in 2004 in Millennium Park, Chicago. The sculpture has become an iconic piece of Chicago and is one of the most popular tourist locations in the city.\n\n\n\nSecondly, Chicago’s nickname is “The Windy City.” While this nickname is usually attributed to Chicago’s freezing cold temperatures due to the winds coming from Lake Michigan, the name is thought to have been  actually derived in the 1800s with politicians in the city blowing “hot air,” which is a saying for spewing lots of words without any actual substance.", "annotation_id": "19173734", "url": "https://genius.com/19173734", "state": "accepted", "votes": 239, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Chicago Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-giveon-chicago-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Chicago Freestyle"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "Don’t link me", "vault_lines": ["Don't hit me when you hear this and tell me your favorite song", "Don't tell me how you knew it would be like this all along"], "meaning": "“Don’t link me” is slang meaning: don’t try to connect or interact with me. Drake wants to be left alone.", "annotation_id": "26250499", "url": "https://genius.com/26250499", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "The people I look up to are goin’ from bad to worse Their actions out of character even when they rehearse", "vault_lines": ["The people I look up to are goin' from bad to worse", "Their actions out of character even when they rehearse"], "meaning": "In late spring of 2018, Drake flew out to Wyoming to help one of his idols, Kanye West with his 5-album project. He even has a writing credit on “Yikes”, the second track on West’s album, ye. Despite this, he believes Ye turned on him during Drizzy’s beef with Pusha T in which Ye sided with the latter rapper.\n\nDrake feels as if he’s being used by his idols who he thought had mutual respect for him but their actions say otherwise.", "annotation_id": "14919891", "url": "https://genius.com/14919891", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "Leavin’ me to ask what’s their motive in makin’ peace", "vault_lines": ["Leavin' me to ask what's their motive in makin' peace"], "meaning": "Drake is most likely questioning Kanye West with this line. Since Kanye supossedly ended the beef between himself, Drake and Pusha T when he tweeted this:https://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1002784133888688128?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E1002784133888688128&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.highsnobiety.com%2Fp%2Fkanye-west-drake-pusha-t-beef-over%2F\nPusha T also confirmed the beef is over.\n\nHowever Drake is now questioning Kanyes/Pushas true motives with ending this beef. When speaking on the un-released diss track that Drake’s camp claims to have, J. Prince did say:\n\nI know for a fact that it would have been bad for Kanye and my man, it just wouldn’t be good. It’s beyond music at that point. It’s going to affect the livelihood. It’s going to interfere with his whole lifestyle from that moment.”\n\n Maybe in reality Kanye doesn’t want the smoke he believes Drake might be able to bring? Perhaps Kanye wants Drake to continue helping him write songs.  Either way, in the end Drake is stuck in the aftermath wondering what his enemies true intentions are.", "annotation_id": "14919397", "url": "https://genius.com/14919397", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "Good-hearted people are takin’ it to extremes", "vault_lines": ["Good-hearted people are takin' it to extremes"], "meaning": "“Good-hearted people” is probably toying with the fact that Pusha T is the president of G.O.O.D. Music.\n\nBefore the release of Scorpion, Pusha released a potent diss track towards Drake called “The Story of Adidon,” which Drake believes took the beef a little too far. At one time Drake definitely thought Pusha T was a good-hearted person since he owned a microphone signed by him.", "annotation_id": "14918106", "url": "https://genius.com/14918106", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "Missin’ out on my days Scrollin’ through life and fishin’ for praise Opinions from total strangers take me out of my ways I try and see who’s there on the other end of the shade Most times it’s just somebody that’s underaged That’s probably just alone and afraid And lashin’ out so that someone else can feel they pain", "vault_lines": ["Missin' out on my years", "Missin' out on my days", "Scrollin' through life and fishin' for praise", "Opinions from total strangers take me out of my ways", "I try and see who's there on the other end of the shade", "Most times it's just somebody that's underaged", "That's probably just alone and afraid", "And lashin' out so that someone else can feel they pain"], "meaning": "As echoed throughout the song, Drake conveys his problems with modern internet culture. He voices that social media is wasting his time, causing him to seek unnecessary praise and overreact to criticism.\n\nOn a grander scale, this segment addresses online bullying and speaks to the nature of anonymity on the internet.", "annotation_id": "14908672", "url": "https://genius.com/14908672", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "Breakin’ news in my life, I don’t run to the blogs The only ones I wanna tell are the ones I can call", "vault_lines": ["Breakin' news in my life, I don't run to the blogs", "The only ones I wanna tell are the ones I can call"], "meaning": "Drake’s son Adonis was born on October 11th, 2017. Although they share the same birth month, they don’t share the Scorpio zodiac sign that was Drake’s inspiration for this album.\n\nThe public was largely unaware that Drake had a son until May 29, 2018 when Pusha T released “The Story of Adidon,” a diss track targeting Drake.\n\nDrake’s reinforcing that he doesn’t need to let media know about the intimate details of his life, which he also addresses earlier in this song, but that only the people who are really close to him should know about his personal life events.", "annotation_id": "14893143", "url": "https://genius.com/14893143", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "Workin’ in the land of the free, the home of the brave I gotta bring my brothers or else I feel out of place", "vault_lines": ["Workin' in the land of the free, the home of the brave", "I gotta bring my brothers or else I feel out of place"], "meaning": "Even though Drake is from Toronto, he records many of his songs in the U.S. at Sandra Gale Studios inside his Yolo Estate in Hidden Hills, California. In the “Star Spangled Banner,” the United States' National Anthem, each verse ends with the line:\n\nO'er the land of the free and the home of the brave", "annotation_id": "14890971", "url": "https://genius.com/14890971", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "Missin’ out on my years There’s times when I wish I was where I was Back when I used to wish I was here", "vault_lines": ["Missin' out on my years", "There's times when I wish I was where I was", "Back when I used to wish I was here", "Missin' out on my days"], "meaning": "Drake’s grappling with getting older, a feeling he expresses on other Scorpion cuts like “Ratchet Happy Birthday,” where he sings “Seems like time’s out of our control,” or on “Do Not Disturb,” where he raps:\n\nSeasons go by like I’m binge-watchin\n\nMoreover, for Drake it is unsettling and confusing to think that he has achieved many of his goals but still feels unfulfilled. Success comes with sacrifices, and you cannot have the cake and eat it too. By phrasing these lines in a way that is intentionally confusing, Drake conveys the confusion he is feeling to the listener.", "annotation_id": "14888298", "url": "https://genius.com/14888298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "I can’t even capture the feeling I had at first Meetin’ all my heroes like seein’ how magic works", "vault_lines": ["Meetin' all my heroes like seein' how magic works", "I can't even capture the feeling I had at first"], "meaning": "One of Drake’s heroes was Kanye West—in 2009, he told MTV how much of an influence ‘Ye had on him:\n\nHe knows how to recognize great music that’s not his. He knows how to utilize great sounds and great music. So before I met him, I had the utmost respect for Kanye West. I’d even go as far as to say he’s the most influential person as far as a musician that I’d ever had in my life.\n\nIn 2018, Drake’s relationship with Kanye has transformed, mostly due to his beef with G.O.O.D. Music artist Pusha-T—another rapper Drake had looked up to (he even owned one of Pusha’s signed microphones).\n\n“Seeing how magic works” is a phrase describing how it is disillusioning and disappointing to discover the truth behind something one idolized. Magic is cool and mysterious but loses its appeal when one knows the tricks used to pull off the illusions. Drake uses this phrase to compare his feelings with discovering the flaws in his idols upon entering the rap game.\n\nPast idols that Drake has developed strained relationships with include Kanye West, Pusha-T, and JAY-Z.", "annotation_id": "14885404", "url": "https://genius.com/14885404", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "Iconic duos rip and split at the seams", "vault_lines": ["Iconic duos rip and split at the seams"], "meaning": "On this track, Drake seems to be talking about the consequences of being part of the rap game. There are a lot of examples of two great friends coming together to make music, and over time they turn to bitter enemies. An impactful example of this is the 2011 album Watch The Throne created by longtime friends and rap giants JAY-Z and Kanye West.\n\n\n\nClipse, formed by brothers Pusha-T and No Malice,  is another example of an iconic duo that didn’t last; they split in 2014. The relationship between Lil Wayne and Birdman was also perilous, after they started having issues that same year, that ended in financial turmoil.", "annotation_id": "14884036", "url": "https://genius.com/14884036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "All these followers but who gon’ follow me to the end? I guess I’ll make it to the end and I’ma find out then", "vault_lines": ["All these followers but who gon' follow me to the end?", "I guess I'll make it to the end and I'ma find out then"], "meaning": "As of June 2018, Drake’s Instagram account, @champagnepapi, has 42.8 million followers on Instagram, making him the 38th most followed person on the platform. However, social media ties are seldom dependable or truly valuable, and Drizzy wonders who really will “follow” him and stay by his side in the end. He wants to have real friends around him that will support him through thick and thin, an idea that he hopes to find out before “the end” approaches.", "annotation_id": "14883391", "url": "https://genius.com/14883391", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "I know the truth is you won’t love me until I’m gone", "vault_lines": ["I know the truth is you won't love me until I'm gone"], "meaning": "When an artist dies, the news circulates and often leads to greater exposure to their art. For example, Lil Peep’s Instagram follower count went from around seventy-five thousand to over one million after his unfortunate death from an accidental overdose of the dangerous opiate, Fentanyl.\n\nSimilarly, after rapper XXXTentacion was shot dead in Florida the week before Scorpion dropped, his streaming numbers skyrocketed, bringing his song “SAD!” to the number 1 spot on the Billboard chart.\n\nDrake expressed a similar sentiment to this lyric in the first verse of “God’s Plan”:\n\nI don’t wanna die for them to miss me", "annotation_id": "14879462", "url": "https://genius.com/14879462", "state": "accepted", "votes": 144, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "You’ve got me feeling emotions Ayy, higher Ah You’ve got me fe– You, oh", "vault_lines": ["You've got me feeling emotions", "You've got me fe–"], "meaning": "This is sampled from the 12\" Club Mix of Mariah Carey’s 1991 song “Emotions”:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nSN--av2DYA", "annotation_id": "14877196", "url": "https://genius.com/14877196", "state": "accepted", "votes": 153, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "Breakin’ speed records on roads that these niggas paved And they don’t like that, it’s written all on they face", "vault_lines": ["Breakin' speed records on roads that these niggas paved", "And they don't like that, it's written all on they face"], "meaning": "Drake used a similar “pave the way” metaphor on his 2013 song “Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2,” which featured JAY-Z:\n\nAnd do it major like the niggas who paved the way for us\n\nNow, instead of being just like his predecessors, he’s claiming to have surpassed them—after all, Drake has broken numerous records throughout his musical career, despite its brevity compared to some of his contemporaries like JAY-Z and Kanye West. Among other successes, he was on the Billboard Hot 100 for 431 consecutive weeks, has had the most simultaneously charting songs of all time, and the highest single-day streaming record on both Spotify and Apple Music with “God’s Plan.”", "annotation_id": "14877157", "url": "https://genius.com/14877157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "They always ask, “Why let the story run if it’s false?” You know a wise man once said nothin’ at all", "vault_lines": ["They always ask, \"Why let the story run if it's false?\"", "You know a wise man once said nothin' at all"], "meaning": "After Pusha-T’s diss track, “The Story of Adidon,” many critics were wondering why Drake had not made a response to Pusha’s accusations of him having a child, and why Drake wouldn’t at least deny the claims brought up by Pusha-T if they were false.\n\n“A wise man once said nothing” is a proverb of uncertain origins, but it’s exemplified in a passage from the Bible in Mark 14: 60-61:\n\nThen the high priest stood up before them and asked Jesus, ‘Are you not going to answer? What is this testimony that these men are bringing against you?’ But Jesus remained silent and gave no answer.\n\nRap-a-Lot Records CEO, J. Prince, may share this same philosophy since he told Drake to stand down from continuing the lyrical exchange.", "annotation_id": "14877073", "url": "https://genius.com/14877073", "state": "accepted", "votes": 180, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "I know a girl whose one goal was to visit Rome Then she finally got to Rome And all she did was post pictures for people at home ‘Cause all that mattered was impressin’ everybody she’s known I know another girl that’s cryin’ out for help But her latest caption is “Leave me alone” I know a girl happily married ’til she puts down her phone I know a girl that saves pictures from places she’s flown To post later and make it look like she still on the go Look at the way we live", "vault_lines": ["I know a girl whose one goal was to visit Rome", "Then she finally got to Rome", "And all she did was post pictures for people at home", "'Cause all that mattered was impressin' everybody she's known", "I know another girl that's cryin' out for help", "But her latest caption is \"Leave me alone\"", "I know a girl happily married 'til she puts down her phone", "I know a girl that saves pictures from places she's flown", "To post later and make it look like she still on the go", "Look at the way we live", "I guess I'll make it to the end and I'ma find out then"], "meaning": "Drake articulates how certain people use social media in order to seek validation from others, rather than living in the moment.\n\nAlthough this lyric isn’t about Drake’s mom, his 2011 song, “Look What You’ve Done,” expressed how visiting Rome was something she had always hoped:\n\nAnd I finally send you to Rome\nI get to make good on my promise\n\n\n\nThe Trevi Fountain in Rome, Italy", "annotation_id": "14876632", "url": "https://genius.com/14876632", "state": "accepted", "votes": 174, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "line": "I wasn’t hidin’ my kid from the world I was hidin’ the world from my kid From empty souls who just wake up and look to debate Until you starin’ at your seed, you can never relate", "vault_lines": ["I wasn't hidin' my kid from the world", "I was hidin' the world from my kid", "From empty souls who just wake up and look to debate", "Until you starin' at your seed, you can never relate"], "meaning": "This is a direct response to Pusha-T, who exposed Drake for hiding his child with Sophie Brussaux, an artist and former porn star.\n\nOn “The Story of Adidon,” Pusha rapped:\n\nSophie knows better as your baby mother\nCleaned her up for IG, but the stench is on her\nA baby’s involved, it’s deeper than rap\nWe talkin' character, let me keep with the facts\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\n\nDrake responds by claiming that the action of hiding the child was a deliberate form of protection from his high-profile lifestyle—which may have been good foresight considering the controversy and questioning Drake has received since the Pusha diss.\n\nA Desus and Mero parody of the Verzuz Instagram series references this lyric (see Instagram comments at lower left mid-way through the video):\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xCQ0Yay98UE", "annotation_id": "14876492", "url": "https://genius.com/14876492", "state": "accepted", "votes": 403, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emotionless", "status": "done", "targets": 17, "clicked": 17, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-emotionless-lyrics", "genius_title": "Emotionless"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "EVERY CHANCE I GET", "status": "missing", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "He might be at the trap and order some ones But ain’t throwin’ more than me", "vault_lines": ["But, baby, not more than me", "He might be at the trap and order some ones", "But ain't throwin' more than me"], "meaning": "By “the Trap,” Drake is likely referring to Booby Trap on the River, a popular strip club in Miami that he’s been known to frequent for years. He’s basically saying nobody can outspend him there, flexing both his wealth and his status at a spot where he’s become somewhat of a local legend.", "annotation_id": "40103125", "url": "https://genius.com/40103125", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "How can you keep it a buck if you ain’t got no bucks? How can you keep it a hundred if you ain’t got hundreds around ‘cause you stackin’ ’em up?", "vault_lines": ["How can you keep it a buck if you ain't got no bucks?", "How can you keep it a hundred if you ain't got hundreds around 'cause you stackin' 'em up?"], "meaning": "Drake combines the use of several metaphors to drive home that others are both poor and dishonest.\n“Keep it a buck” and “keep it a hundred” are both phrases that mean someone is being honest, and derived originally from references to currency. Drake says this person is unable to “keep it a buck” because they don’t have any bucks (slang for dollars), and can’t keep it a hundred, because they don’t have hundreds (referring to the $100 denomination) around. Drake pokes fun that this person must be dishonest because of their lack of wealth.", "annotation_id": "37526124", "url": "https://genius.com/37526124", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "Let’s keep it frank, I just got dessert", "vault_lines": ["Let's keep it frank, I just got dessert"], "meaning": "Drake’s pronunciation of this line leads to two different meanings.\n\n“I just got dessert” sounds very similar to “I just got to Zurich.” Zurich is Switzerland’s largest city, a global hub for finance and banking where the currency is the Swiss franc. This ties into the first part of the line. Drake also visited the city and performed at Hallenstadion for two shows on August 11 and 12, 2025 as part of his “Some Special Shows 4 U” tour.", "annotation_id": "37526097", "url": "https://genius.com/37526097", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "Yeah, that man, he still with you, he can’t leave you Y’all go on vacation, I bet it’s Antigua", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, that man, he still with you, he can't leave you", "Y'all go on vacation, I bet it's Antigua"], "meaning": "Yet another diss at A$AP Rocky and Rihanna. Drake is saying that Rocky isn’t going anywhere, having children with Rihanna. This could also be referencing Riri’s networth, being far higher than Rocky’s. Drake is saying they go vacation in Antigua, an island in the Carribean because Rihanna is from Barbados, essentially saying that she calls the shots in the relationship.", "annotation_id": "37504995", "url": "https://genius.com/37504995", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "I never met a bitch that wasn’t for it Don’t pay for pussy, I tip for the service", "vault_lines": ["I never met a bitch that wasn't for it", "Don't pay for pussy, I tip for the service"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION:\n\nService is a wordplay on “Cervix” which is the female part of the body which forms the lower end of the uterus. Instilling that he doesn’t pay for pussy but rather a part of it.", "annotation_id": "29742381", "url": "https://genius.com/29742381", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "The chain on her neck is a A", "vault_lines": ["The chain on her neck is a A"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "36860478", "url": "https://genius.com/36860478", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "He might have some hits But, baby, not more than me", "vault_lines": ["But, baby, not more than me", "But ain't throwin' more than me"], "meaning": "Drake could be talking about virtually anyone here, because he is the biggest hit-maker in all of hiphop. But it’s possible that he could be singling out Rihanna’s new man, A$AP Rocky. While Rocky has had some big songs like Praise da Lord and others, these pale in comparison to the mega hits Drake has released in the last decade.", "annotation_id": "36739791", "url": "https://genius.com/36739791", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "You niggas some pussy, for real You niggas some sissy, for real", "vault_lines": ["You niggas some pussy, for real", "You niggas some sissy, for real"], "meaning": "This is a sneak diss at rapper, singer and producer Pharell Williams, who is from Virginia Beach. The way Drake says ‘for real’ sounds awfully similar to ‘Pharell’.\n\nThis was mentioned by whatsthedirt in his break-down of the album’s disses on YouTube.", "annotation_id": "36739765", "url": "https://genius.com/36739765", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "And she got a lot that she need So she gotta drop to her knees Then she can go shoppin’ for free", "vault_lines": ["And she got a lot that she need", "So she gotta drop to her knees", "Then she can go shoppin' for free"], "meaning": "This girl has luxurious spending habits, and many items she wants to purchase. Drake says that if she performs oral sex by “dropping to her knees”, he will pay for her to go shopping.", "annotation_id": "36388242", "url": "https://genius.com/36388242", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "I know you a cat, but can your pussy do the dog?", "vault_lines": ["I know you a cat, but can your pussy do thе dog?", "I know you a cat, but can your pussy do the dog?"], "meaning": "Drake interpolates The Cramps‘ 1985 song “Can Your Pussy Do the Dog?,” which features the following chorus:\n\nHey, can your pussy do the dog?\nCan your pussy do the dog?", "annotation_id": "29730338", "url": "https://genius.com/29730338", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "Got in my feelings, I had to record it", "vault_lines": ["Got in my feelings, I had to record it"], "meaning": "This is a wordplay about one of his most famous songs, “In My Feelings” and the necessity of Drake creating music that reflects his feelings.", "annotation_id": "29728958", "url": "https://genius.com/29728958", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "Virginia, I pull up and chill You know you can’t come where I stay", "vault_lines": ["Virginia, I pull up and chill", "You know you can't come where I stay"], "meaning": "Drake calls out his long-term adversary, Pusha T, who currently resides in Norfolk, Virginia. Drake claims he would be safe and unbothered traveling in Pusha’s city, while Pusha is scared to set foot in his.\n\nThis bar also includes a double entendre as Pusha T’s wife is named Virginia Williams, and has once before been mentioned in Drake’s “Duppy Freestyle”:\n\nI told you keep playin' with my name\nAnd I'ma let it ring on you like Virginia Williams", "annotation_id": "29727477", "url": "https://genius.com/29727477", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "[Verse]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This could be interpreted as a Rihanna diss. Throughout much of the first decade of his career, Drake was never shy to express his fondness of the Bajan songstress. This was perhaps most loudly proclaimed when he presented her with the Vanguard Award at the 2016 MTV Video Music Awards, saying:\n\nShe’s someone I’ve been in love with since I was 22 years old. She’s one of my best friends in the world. All my adult life I’ve looked up to her, even though she’s younger than me.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HGcc3LyANtA\n\nAfter years of rumoured dates, flings, and trysts, Drake and Rihanna finally had their romantic moment in the sun in 2016, and dated for several months. They seemed not to have left things amicably, as Rihanna was quoted saying the following about the status of their relationship in a 2018 Vogue interview:\n\nWe don’t have a friendship now, but we’re not enemies either. It is what it is.\n\nSince the dissolution of their relationship in 2017, Drake has been dogged with trolls alleging that he still has feelings for Rihanna and will never get over the woman that many fans are still convinced is the love of his life.\n\nClues that this is indeed a dig at Rih include his use of “gyal,” a derivation from “girl” that comes from the English-based creole languages spoken in the Carribean, notably including Jamaican Patois and Bajan Creole, Rihanna’s native dialect. Famously, Rihanna’s Instagram handle is “badgalriri,” where “gal” would likely be pronounced as “gyal” in her accent.\n\nAdditionally, Drake repeats the word “anti” in both of its pronunciations, and uses it to form double entendres with “auntie”, its homophone. ANTI is still Rihanna’s latest album at the time of this song’s release, and has been followed by a musical hiatus of seven years and counting as Rih has focussed on other ventures.\n\nIf this is what it seems like, Drake’s relationship with A$AP Rocky, Rihanna’s partner and the father of their children, has likely soured beyond repair as well.", "annotation_id": "29726363", "url": "https://genius.com/29726363", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "Let’s go pound for pound, I’m in Europe", "vault_lines": ["Let's go pound for pound, I'm in Europe"], "meaning": "Pound (also referred to as GBP and symbolized as £) is Great Britain’s currency and also one of the most popular currencies in the rest of Europe.", "annotation_id": "29725578", "url": "https://genius.com/29725578", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "Out the country, I link with OZ", "vault_lines": ["Out the country, I link with OZ"], "meaning": "Drake takes the time when out of the country to link with artists he works with across the globe, such as OZ. OZ is a Swiss-Turkish producer who has made many songs with Drake, including hits like “SICKO MODE” with Travis Scott and “Toosie Slide,” as well as 3 tracks off For All The Dogs.", "annotation_id": "29724855", "url": "https://genius.com/29724855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "Chrome Heart Culli’, I had to import it", "vault_lines": ["Chrome Heart Culli', I had to import it"], "meaning": "In 2021, Drake and the clothing line Chrome Hearts teamed up to design a one-of-one Rolls-Royce Cullinan. Fitted with custom quilted seats, the Chrome Heart logo stitched all throughout the car, and custom rims, it was ultimately unveiled at the Institute of Contemporary Art in Miami.\n\nInside Drake’s and Chrome Hearts' one-of-one Rolls-Royce\n\nWith the reveal of the Cullinan, Drake and Chrome Hearts went on to drop a collaborated merch line, Chrome Hearts x Drake because of Drake’s likeness in wearing Chrome Hearts throughout the years.\n\nDrake and Chrome Hearts hoodie collaboration", "annotation_id": "29724808", "url": "https://genius.com/29724808", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "My niggas is crazy, Wallahi", "vault_lines": ["My niggas is crazy, Wallahi"], "meaning": "“Wallahi” is an Arabic word, commonly used by Muslims to swear, translating to “By God” in English. The word is frequently used in Canadian and British slang vernacular.", "annotation_id": "29724769", "url": "https://genius.com/29724769", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "Girl, you make me wanna cuff you like the law, huh", "vault_lines": ["Girl, you make me wanna cuff you like the law, huh", "Girl, you makе me wanna work you like the job, ayy", "Girl, you make me wanna work you like the job, ayy"], "meaning": "“Cuffing” someone is known as pulling them in close, in hopes for a relationship. This also refers to handcuffs, which are usually handled by law enforcement.", "annotation_id": "29724719", "url": "https://genius.com/29724719", "state": "pending", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "line": "I’m in the G-Wagon Maybach Just like some currency, baby, they had to convert it", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the G-Wagon Maybach", "Just like some currency, baby, they had to convert it"], "meaning": "Drake mentions his Mercedes-Maybach G 650 Landaulet. He likens his car to converting real-world currencies as the Landaulet is a convertible make of the Mercedes-Maybach G-Wagon.", "annotation_id": "29724610", "url": "https://genius.com/29724610", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fear Of Heights", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 19, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-fear-of-heights-lyrics", "genius_title": "Fear Of Heights"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "Now all of a sudden, these gossip rags want to cover me And you making it seem like it happened that way because of me", "vault_lines": ["Now all of a sudden, these gossip rags want to cover me", "And you making it seem like it happened that way because of me"], "meaning": "Drake captures the strain that public attention puts on a personal relationship. The sudden arrival of paparazzi and tabloid headlines creates friction between him and his partner, who accuses him of inviting the chaos.\n\n\n\nDrake pushes back on this guilt trip, pointing out the hypocrisy and unfairness of being blamed for an industry byproduct he couldn’t control, showing how fame destroys private love life from the outside in.", "annotation_id": "40986387", "url": "https://genius.com/40986387", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "I’m just such a gentleman, you should give it up for me Look at how I’m placing all my napkins and my cutlery", "vault_lines": ["I'm just such a gentleman, you should give it up for me", "Look at how I'm placing all my napkins and my cutlery"], "meaning": "This is an early sarcastic jab at hip-hop critics who often labeled Drake as too soft, emotional, or gentlemanly compared to the genre’s tougher personas. Rather than distancing himself from that image, Drake embraced it and made it a core part of his appeal.", "annotation_id": "40114029", "url": "https://genius.com/40114029", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "October’s Own but it’s looking like July 4", "vault_lines": ["October's Own but it's looking like July 4"], "meaning": "This line references Drake’s transition from All Things Fresh (ATF) to OVO (October’s Very Own), the brand and collective he co-founded with Oliver El-Khatib. While “October” has become central to Drake’s identity and career, he says his life feels like the Fourth of July—a holiday synonymous with fireworks and celebration.\n\nThe comparison suggests that he’s living in a constant state of success and excitement. No matter what month it is, there’s always something worth celebrating. The idea of fireworks also ties directly into the song’s title.", "annotation_id": "40113956", "url": "https://genius.com/40113956", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "My dad called me up knowing that I still listen", "vault_lines": ["My dad called me up knowing that I still listen"], "meaning": "Perhaps in the next line refers to Drake’s Dad complaining about his mom and how things ended. He knows Drake will listen to him and let him vent showing they have a good relationship.", "annotation_id": "38569080", "url": "https://genius.com/38569080", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "It’s been years though, I just learn to deal with it For real, me and my realtor, we built up a better rapport Got my mother in a place with some better decor She searched the entire city, I let her explore And now she’s saying she more lonely than ever before", "vault_lines": ["It's been years though, I just learn to deal with it", "For real, me and my realtor, we built up a better rapport", "Got my mother in a place with some better decor", "She searched the entire city, I let her explore", "And now she's saying she more lonely than ever before"], "meaning": "Life tests you constantly. With time he has been able to handle the pain. He figures he can purchase property for his mom in another city in hopes to make her happier. Unfortunately the plan back fires and his mom feels lonelier than ever in a new place.", "annotation_id": "38569056", "url": "https://genius.com/38569056", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "I’ll never forget it, baby, what an experience You could’ve been the one but it wasn’t that serious There was smoke in the air before, that was me clearing it That felt good, all in all, I learned a lesson from it though", "vault_lines": ["But I was curious", "And I'll never forget it, baby, what an experience", "You could've been the one but it wasn't that serious", "There was smoke in the air before, that was me clearing it", "That felt good, all in all, I learned a lesson from it though"], "meaning": "drake-fireworks. ts so real", "annotation_id": "37903761", "url": "https://genius.com/37903761", "state": "pending", "votes": -13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "Looking down from the top and it’s crowded below", "vault_lines": ["Looking down from the top and it's crowded below"], "meaning": "It could mean Drake is on top of the “game” with the the future crowded below.", "annotation_id": "36760734", "url": "https://genius.com/36760734", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "Everything the same but it feels different", "vault_lines": ["Everything the same but it feels different"], "meaning": "The sentiment here that life only feels different, and has not really changed is one that wouldn’t last. Only 3 years after the release of the, “Thank Me Later” album, we would get an album by the title of, “Nothing Was The Same.”\nThis sentiment is common throughout Drake works of this era with him even mentioning on the track “Fear” which has also been referenced in this song that people believe he’s changed even though it’s only been his appeal that’s changed and grown.", "annotation_id": "35067883", "url": "https://genius.com/35067883", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "Yeah, and my dreams are who I’m racing with", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and my dreams are who I'm racing with"], "meaning": "Drake is likely mentioning how ambitious he is. His dreams of starhood and wonderful tracks are hard to keep up with. Hence, he is racing with them.\n\nA more nuanced interpretation goes as follows and utilizes a metaphor. One wherein he likens the rap game to a race. He’s racing with his dreams, being his idols and fellow rappers. On another track on the “Thank Me Later” album, being “Thank Me Now” where Drake states, “that’s around the time that your idols become your rivals”. He may be expressing a similar sentiment here.", "annotation_id": "35059553", "url": "https://genius.com/35059553", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "Hate is so familiar to me, I’m slowly embracing it", "vault_lines": ["Hate is so familiar to me, I'm slowly embracing it"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "33301628", "url": "https://genius.com/33301628", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "You never see it coming, you just get to see it go", "vault_lines": ["You never see it coming, you just get to see it go"], "meaning": "Love, a fickle emotion, often eludes our conscious grasp until it fades away. Its absence unveils the depths of its hold on our hearts, leaving us longing for what we once took for granted. The veil lifts, revealing a profound revelation: true love was there all along, yet we failed to recognize its significance until it slipped away, forever imprinting the lesson on our souls.", "annotation_id": "28962286", "url": "https://genius.com/28962286", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "How many of our parents’ marriages lasted?", "vault_lines": ["How many of our parents' marriages lasted?"], "meaning": "Drake’s parents got divorced, and it has become a recurring theme in his life, Their marriage didn’t last very long, and it’s been a recurring theme in his music.", "annotation_id": "27489104", "url": "https://genius.com/27489104", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "Yeah, but I guess I gotta find it first", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, but I guess I gotta find it first"], "meaning": "This is most likely a reference to Drake’s hit single “Find Your Love” which revolves around the topic of Drake wanting to find the love that the right woman for him has.\n\nAlso connects with the last line “I want to witness love, I never seen it close”", "annotation_id": "24036772", "url": "https://genius.com/24036772", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "I’ve missed them before But won’t miss them again", "vault_lines": ["I've missed them before", "But won't miss them again"], "meaning": "Drake is saying here that he had some chances during his childhood to become more successful in his life but couldn’t take advantage of them because of all of his recent struggles. However, because of where he is now and how he made a big name for himself, he won’t miss those opportunities again", "annotation_id": "24035815", "url": "https://genius.com/24035815", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "And he’s still got his foot out, guilt tripping", "vault_lines": ["And he's still got his foot out, guilt tripping"], "meaning": "Drake’s father at this time was still blaming Drake for everything that happened between him and his Mom and for the things Drake said about him in some of his songs. “Guilt tripping” essentially means to make someone feel guilty", "annotation_id": "24035755", "url": "https://genius.com/24035755", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "My fifteen minutes started an hour ago", "vault_lines": ["My fifteen minutes started an hour ago"], "meaning": "Consider the idea of “fifteen minutes of fame” which suggests an artist lacking longevity, unable to withstand the course of a full career.\n\nDrake is saying that this “fifteen minutes of fame” sentiment doesn’t apply to him. Refer to his sustained time in the spotlight as a well-known television star prior to this rap debut.\n\nStopping critics before they start, Drake’s preemptively muting those willing to assume this debut album is representative of his first foray into the spotlight or otherwise minute one of his fifteen minutes of fame—it isn’t.\n\nAn hour being sixty minutes, is multiple times longer than fifteen minutes, this suggests Drake’s confidence in his own ability to continue to thrive.", "annotation_id": "22576567", "url": "https://genius.com/22576567", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "Money just changed everything", "vault_lines": ["Money just changed everything"], "meaning": "This line serves as the transition from “So Far Gone” to “Thank Me Later”. Drake mentioned in an interview with MTV that “the last line of the third verse in the song \"Fear” is gonna be the first line on my album [Thank Me Later]“. The last line of that track is: \"It’s funny how money can change everything”.", "annotation_id": "21378500", "url": "https://genius.com/21378500", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "I keep having the same dream And I think that I just realized what it means", "vault_lines": ["I keep having the same dream", "And I think that I just realized what it means"], "meaning": "Drake explains this dream in an interview with Billboard:\n\n“I kept having this reoccurring dream that I was standing in this field by where my uncle used to play golf and coincidentally, where my uncle played golf and where my grandfather is buried are right beside each other. I kept having this dream that I was standing in the middle of that field and watching these fireworks go off. For a really long time I was trying to figure out what it all meant, and I guess the conclusion that I came to was, I’m at a very triumphant point in my life. I think I can make a lot of things happen for not only myself but a lot of people around me.”", "annotation_id": "3396609", "url": "https://genius.com/3396609", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "Yeah, I should’ve looked up in the sky at first Now I can see it in her eyes, fireworks", "vault_lines": ["All I see is fireworks", "Yeah, I should've looked up in the sky at first", "Now I can see it in her eyes, fireworks"], "meaning": "Going toward the point of walking blindly, Drake believes before things went downhill, he should have “looked up” for answers first. Now when he looks in his girl’s eyes, he can see the buildup of “fireworks” or strong emotions (whether rage &/or happiness)", "annotation_id": "1417527", "url": "https://genius.com/1417527", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "All I see is fireworks All I see is fireworks Every night it’s fireworks Every night it’s fireworks All I see is fireworks All I see is fireworks Taking off like fireworks Taking off like—Oh", "vault_lines": ["All I see is fireworks", "Every night it's fireworks", "Taking off like fireworks"], "meaning": "Drake interprets fireworks to be a symbol of rapid success. Seeing as this song came after Drake dropped a string of successful mixtapes, EPs, and singles, it really must have felt like he was surrounded by fireworks.", "annotation_id": "1417508", "url": "https://genius.com/1417508", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "I just wish they’d let you try it first This time I’m really going off, fireworks", "vault_lines": ["I just wish they'd let you try it first", "This time I'm really going off, fireworks", "That's why I'm really going off, fireworks"], "meaning": "Drake wishes that he could “test out” fame before becoming a superstar so that you could adapt yourself to this new lifestyle without being consumed by it.\n\nDrake is using fireworks as a metaphor for his seemingly explosive rise to fame, as he essentially became an overnight celebrity after the release of his So Far Gone mixtape, led by lead single “Best I Ever Had”.", "annotation_id": "440614", "url": "https://genius.com/440614", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "Wayne put me right here, that’s who I get the paper with I hope that my success never alters our relationship", "vault_lines": ["Wayne put me right here, that's who I get the paper with", "I hope that my success never alters our relationship"], "meaning": "Drake’s career really took off when he was co-signed by Lil Wayne and signed to Wayne’s Young Money Entertainment label. While things are good between them now, Drake knows that success can drive even the greatest of groups apart.\n\nDrake said in an interview that this verse addresses:\n\n…the sort of fear that me and Wayne will be driven apart by the fact that I actively did gain success as an artist, and I always worry that sometimes that might drive a wedge between us, which I pray it never will and which I say in the song.", "annotation_id": "440608", "url": "https://genius.com/440608", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "I’m flying back home for the Heritage Classic", "vault_lines": ["I'm flying back home for the Heritage Classic"], "meaning": "Heritage Classic is an outdoor NHL Hockey Game held once in 2003 and and once in 2011 in the Alberta province of Canada, Drake’s homeland.", "annotation_id": "19760", "url": "https://genius.com/19760", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "I could tell it wasn’t love, I just thought you’d fuck with me Who could’ve predicted Lucky Strike would have you stuck with me? Damn, I kept my wits about me luckily What happened between us that night? It always seems to trouble me", "vault_lines": ["I could tell it wasn't love, I just thought you'd fuck with me", "Who could've predicted Lucky Strike would have you stuck with me?", "Damn, I kept my wits about me luckily", "What happened between us that night?", "It always seems to trouble me"], "meaning": "Lucky Strike Lanes & Lounge is a popular bowling ally/nightclub in New York – it is also the first place Drake and Rihanna were ever spotted together.\n\n\n\nDrake has expressed in interviews that he’s glad he didn’t allow himself to truly fall for her, because he got the feeling that the relationship didn’t mean as much to her as it did to him.", "annotation_id": "19754", "url": "https://genius.com/19754", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "line": "From the concrete who knew that a flower would grow", "vault_lines": ["From the concrete who knew that a flower would grow"], "meaning": "A reference to the Tupac poem “The Rose That Grew From Concrete”.", "annotation_id": "19744", "url": "https://genius.com/19744", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fireworks", "status": "done", "targets": 25, "clicked": 25, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-fireworks-lyrics", "genius_title": "Fireworks"}
