{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "I do better with the rider in my system", "vault_lines": ["I do better with the rider in my system"], "meaning": "Additionally, alcohol has been frequently referred to by authors as the true “writer”. Drake is using this play on words to refer to the fact that he writes best when he’s been drinking. Alcohol inhibits judgement, so his lyrics tend to be the most honest with the “writer in his system”", "annotation_id": "4981752", "url": "https://genius.com/4981752", "state": "verified", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Used to flip apps, now that old plug murked Ain’t a damn thing changed, you can still get the work, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Used to flip apps, now that old plug murked", "Ain't a damn thing changed, you can still get the work, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake is a great lyricist and he makes a very creative use of Apple’s App store and Steve (Jobs') last name. Even though his plug (Steve Jobs) who created the apps market is ‘merked’, we can still get the (Work). Apps like the Capcom system for Drake’s last tour are expensive and require high profile promotional budgets. Thankfully we can get music/work by going to itunes.  If only he were indie… (Cash Money Owns this his fourth and final album under the label contract.)", "annotation_id": "4971790", "url": "https://genius.com/4971790", "state": "verified", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Brand new Beretta, can’t wait to let it go Walk up in my label like, “Where the check, though?” Yeah, I said it Wouldn’t dap you with the left, ho,", "vault_lines": ["Wouldn't dap you with the left, ho,", "Brand new Beretta, can't wait to let it go", "Walk up in my label like, \"Where the check, though?\""], "meaning": "Here, Aubrey references how he’s obtained a “brand new Beretta” and “can’t wait to let it go,” ultimately showing his very well-known sensitive side and his struggle to desperately git rid of the unwanted firearm.", "annotation_id": "4971375", "url": "https://genius.com/4971375", "state": "verified", "votes": -63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Brand new Beretta, can’t wait to let it go Walk up in my label like, “Where the check, though?” Yeah, I said it Wouldn’t dap you with the left, ho,", "vault_lines": ["Wouldn't dap you with the left, ho,", "Brand new Beretta, can't wait to let it go", "Walk up in my label like, \"Where the check, though?\""], "meaning": "Brand New Beretta is a reference to Beretta Firearms, they manufacture popular handguns.It also fits in with the Lil Wayne intro reference to clips (ammo)", "annotation_id": "4957276", "url": "https://genius.com/4957276", "state": "verified", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Line blowin’ up, workin’ on the phones Now we in the basement, and we workin’ on the phones But I just couldn’t do it, had to leave that shit alone, man", "vault_lines": ["Now we in the basement, and we workin' on the phones", "Now we in the basement, and we workin' on the phones, man", "Line blowin' up, workin' on the phones", "But I just couldn't do it, had to leave that shit alone, man", "All I had to do was just go and get it", "And now we blowin' up"], "meaning": "If you notice the word “my” is said very close to the word “main”. Which could be interpreted that Drake is saying the “main” niggas need the whole thing, profit, credit, and notoriety from Drake’s musical accomplishments (ie.Birdman).", "annotation_id": "4933731", "url": "https://genius.com/4933731", "state": "verified", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Brand new Beretta, can’t wait to let it go Walk up in my label like, “Where the check, though?” Yeah, I said it Wouldn’t dap you with the left, ho,", "vault_lines": ["Wouldn't dap you with the left, ho,", "Brand new Beretta, can't wait to let it go", "Walk up in my label like, \"Where the check, though?\""], "meaning": "Since this verse begins and ends with specific references to Birdman, it’s possible this whole verse is Drake chronicling a day the two squared off and expressing his feelings on them parting ways.", "annotation_id": "4930908", "url": "https://genius.com/4930908", "state": "verified", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Used to flip apps, now that old plug murked Ain’t a damn thing changed, you can still get the work, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Used to flip apps, now that old plug murked", "Ain't a damn thing changed, you can still get the work, ayy"], "meaning": "This is also likely a reference to Baby’s seminal work on the Big Tymers album “I Got That Work,” certified platinum in 2000. The album’s title itself was borrowed from a phrase used both to refer the drug trade (“I have product to deal”) and the music biz (“I just dropped a Platinum album”). “You can get the work,” may also be a diss, telling Baby he can “get the business,” (get fucked) a la Lil Wayne on Every Girl", "annotation_id": "4930818", "url": "https://genius.com/4930818", "state": "verified", "votes": -4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Line blowin’ up, workin’ on the phones Now we in the basement, and we workin’ on the phones But I just couldn’t do it, had to leave that shit alone, man", "vault_lines": ["Now we in the basement, and we workin' on the phones", "Now we in the basement, and we workin' on the phones, man", "Line blowin' up, workin' on the phones", "But I just couldn't do it, had to leave that shit alone, man", "All I had to do was just go and get it", "And now we blowin' up"], "meaning": "idk how much money there is in phone scams, but it unlikely rivals the illigil drug industry. Getting “put on” usually refers getting a drug supply. This corresponds with being on the phone alot because he is making alot of deals. This bar also makes sense when he says on Tuesday “squad going up, nobody flippin packs now.”", "annotation_id": "4929117", "url": "https://genius.com/4929117", "state": "verified", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Line blowin’ up, workin’ on the phones Now we in the basement, and we workin’ on the phones But I just couldn’t do it, had to leave that shit alone, man", "vault_lines": ["Now we in the basement, and we workin' on the phones", "Now we in the basement, and we workin' on the phones, man", "Line blowin' up, workin' on the phones", "But I just couldn't do it, had to leave that shit alone, man", "All I had to do was just go and get it", "And now we blowin' up"], "meaning": "When a phone line is “blowing up” it is in constant use. Drake and his crew are putting in work on their phone scam, because they genuinely need the money, especially if they are going to be supporting a $10k cocaine habit.\n\nTo blow up is also, of course, to make it big in the industry in a short period of time. Drake’s expenses were more than he could cover at $50k/year, so he really does need to blow up as a rapper to continue his current lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "4901483", "url": "https://genius.com/4901483", "state": "pending", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "I do better with the rider in my system", "vault_lines": ["I do better with the rider in my system"], "meaning": "A “rider” is a list of items that an entertainer request be available to them before, after and during a live show. Drake’s rider includes a large amount of alcohol, which seems to confirm his “Ignant Shit” line.", "annotation_id": "4898406", "url": "https://genius.com/4898406", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Used to flip apps, now that old plug murked Ain’t a damn thing changed, you can still get the work, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Used to flip apps, now that old plug murked", "Ain't a damn thing changed, you can still get the work, ayy"], "meaning": "Birdman was Drake’s old plug and supplied Drake with the resources he needed to make music. Their relationship has severed, but Drake assures the fans that they’ll still receive top quality product.", "annotation_id": "4896277", "url": "https://genius.com/4896277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Hear what? Yo hear what, take time (Ha-ha-ha-ha) Ahh, no shit Yo hear what? Wha gwan, fry skull Him fried, him fried and a sleep awhile ago inna di van, you know Drizzy (Ha-ha-ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["Yo hear what, take time", "Wha gwan, fry skull", "Him fried, him fried and a sleep awhile ago inna di van, you know Drizzy"], "meaning": "Sound clip from a call between Drake and his Unruly crew in Jamaica. The sampled part begins at 20:44.\n\nhttps://vimeo.com/116727998#t=1244", "annotation_id": "4891776", "url": "https://genius.com/4891776", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "[Verse 1]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The beat switches up for the rest of the song, creating a less aggressive atmosphere and provoking a more thoughtful mindset. He looks back at his previous life here and juxtaposes it with where he is now, always making sure to let us know that he has put in a lot of hard work to get where he is.", "annotation_id": "4889716", "url": "https://genius.com/4889716", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "I am not a man, I can’t do this on my own So I started askin’ them if they would put me on And they did put me on, yeah, they did put me on Now we in the basement, and we workin’ on the phones Now we in the basement, and we workin’ on the phones, man", "vault_lines": ["I am not a man, I can't do this on my own", "So I started askin' them if they would put me on", "And they did put me on, yeah, they did put me on", "Now we in the basement, and we workin' on the phones", "Now we in the basement, and we workin' on the phones, man", "And now we blowin' up"], "meaning": "Drake struggled with providing for his mother and himself growing up as a teenager. He realized he couldn’t do it on his own, and needed assistance from his friends. Haffa and Jordan put him on a phone scam they were doing in Niko’s basement, also briefly mentioned in “0 to 100/The Catch Up.”", "annotation_id": "4889636", "url": "https://genius.com/4889636", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "I remember I had went to Louis V with Haf’ Watched them spread ten thousand dollars on the glass I never, ever thought I’d see that in my life Now I’m in the East ‘cause them boys are gettin’ right, man", "vault_lines": ["Now I'm in the Rolls with illuminated angel", "I remember I had went to Louis V with Haf'", "Watched them spread ten thousand dollars on the glass", "I never, ever thought I'd see that in my life", "Now I'm in the East 'cause them boys are gettin' right, man"], "meaning": "Haf' is short for Haffa, one of Drake’s childhood friends. He’s namedropped on “Lord Knows.”\n\nDrake is in nostalgic mode, recalling his younger days when the people he ran with would spread $10k. That was pre-fame Drake when he wasn’t used to being around so much money.\n\nThe East refers to Scarborough — Toronto’s East End — an area known for its high crime rate. This is where his buddies would get money through various fraud schemes.", "annotation_id": "4889617", "url": "https://genius.com/4889617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Got my foot on the ’Cedes Benz pedal Doin’ ninety on the bridge, like, “Nigga, you already know”", "vault_lines": ["Got my foot on the 'Cedes Benz pedal", "Doin' ninety on the bridge, like, \"Nigga, you already know\""], "meaning": "Drake picked up a 2015 Mercedes BRABUS S-Coupe that is currently the only one of its kind in North America.\n\nHis vehicle is white like cocaine, but he doesn’t need a scale. He made his money legally and not from drug dealing.", "annotation_id": "4888715", "url": "https://genius.com/4888715", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "And if you don’t know, then now you know, now you know", "vault_lines": ["And if you don't know, then now you know, now you know"], "meaning": "Shout out to Biggie’s “Juicy” and the original Rap Genius slogan:", "annotation_id": "4888495", "url": "https://genius.com/4888495", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Switchin’ up the angles Now I’m in the Rolls with illuminated angel", "vault_lines": ["Switchin' up the angles", "Now I'm in the Rolls with illuminated angel"], "meaning": "Drake is pulling up in a Rolls Royce, one of the most luxurious cars ever made. He switches up the angles by repositioning himself from the Mercedes, to the Rolls Royce, which is a car you are generally chauffeured in.\n\nThe emblem on the front of a Rolls is an angel known as The Spirit of Ecstasy. The emblem is made out of an illuminated crystal that definitely attracts attention when it pulls up to the scene!", "annotation_id": "4888452", "url": "https://genius.com/4888452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "[Intro: Lil Wayne] That’s what they doin’, Cam’ron They actin’ like these singers, man ”I ain’t—I ain’t goin’ to the studio ’til I got a situation A subject, I need a beat, I need the producer Who—who gon’ be on the hook?” Man, what is you doin’? Go in the studio with fuckin’ clips, clips, ammo", "vault_lines": ["That's what they doin', Cam'ron", "They actin' like these singers, man", "\"I ain't—I ain't goin' to the studio 'til I got a situation", "A subject, I need a beat, I need the producer", "Who—who gon' be on the hook?\" Man, what is you doin'?", "Go in the studio with fuckin' clips, clips, ammo"], "meaning": "This is taken from footage of Lil Wayne from 2007 when he was expressing his frustration with the rap game. “That’s what they doin, Cam”, is Weezy venting to companion and fellow rapper, Cam'ron.\n\nhttp://youtu.be/vU_y_JRJHo8\n\nLil Wayne mocks modern rap artists' workflow. These artists are too picky and, foremost, worrisome; they need very specific circumstances in order to make a track that they will be content with. They worry about who might be featured, or who might produce the track. Wayne urges these people to wake up, go in the studio with confidence, and hammer out whatever comes to mind. This is the rap game, after all. Don’t act like you’re some country singer that takes 3 years to release a 15-track album.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eQVhAN7-IAw", "annotation_id": "4888403", "url": "https://genius.com/4888403", "state": "accepted", "votes": 293, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Like Zoe mama, I go hippy Peace sign in the air like I’m Nixon, I’m mixin’", "vault_lines": ["Like Zoe mama, I go hippy", "Peace sign in the air like I'm Nixon, I'm mixin'"], "meaning": "Both of Zoë Kravitz’s parents, Lenny Kravitz and Lisa Bonet, are hippies. They are free, wild, and loving spirits. Hippies also normally promote peace. When resigning from the office, President Nixon waved goodbye in the form of a peace sign.", "annotation_id": "4888313", "url": "https://genius.com/4888313", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "I was on TV, makin’ fifty racks a year After helpin’ Mama out, the shit would disappear", "vault_lines": ["I was on TV, makin' fifty racks a year", "After helpin' Mama out, the shit would disappear"], "meaning": "Drake earned $50,000 a year for his role as Jimmy Brooks on the television show Degrassi: The Next Generation.\n\nToronto is a very expensive city. With Drake and his mother living as tenants in a house situated in Forest Hill, one of the wealthier areas in the city, it can be very difficult to support two people on 50k.\n\nDrake’s mother also had a lot of health issues as learned from the song “Look What You’ve Done.”", "annotation_id": "4888292", "url": "https://genius.com/4888292", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Ooh yeah, I’m on deck, when you call me, I’ll listen I listen unless I been mixin’ You know when I’m mixin’, you know when I’m mixin’ I smoke when I drink, it’s tradition", "vault_lines": ["Ooh yeah, I'm on deck, when you call me, I'll listen", "I listen unless I been mixin'", "You know when I'm mixin', you know when I'm mixin'", "I smoke when I drink, it's tradition"], "meaning": "“On deck” simply means to be around. Considering Drake doesn’t reply to texts from centerfolds, this is a strong endorsement. However, Drake only has time for this girl when he’s not busy mixing.\n\nWhen Drake is drinking, possibly some of that Henny or D'usse mentioned earlier on the album, it’s guaranteed that he’ll mix it with a blunt. It is so common for him to be mixin' that it has developed into a tradition.", "annotation_id": "4888227", "url": "https://genius.com/4888227", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "I am not Esco, but it was written, I knew when they didn’t I been had these visions of the life I’m livin’ since I was Jimmy All I had to do was just go and get it", "vault_lines": ["I am not Esco, but it was written, I knew when they didn't", "I been had these visions of the life I'm livin' since I was Jimmy", "All I had to do was just go and get it"], "meaning": "Jimmy was the name of Drake’s character on Degrassi.\n\n“Esco” is one of Nas' nicknames, and It Was Written is the name of his second album.  Even though people hated on him, Drake always knew it was his destiny to become one of the top rappers. It was written in stone, like Nas' album title.\n\nIt’s worth noting Nas referred to Drake’s Nothing Was The Same as a classic album in his Reddit AMA. Drake and Nas also hosted a concert together the night after the mixtape was released.", "annotation_id": "4888122", "url": "https://genius.com/4888122", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Shut the fuck up Text from a centerfold, I ain’t reply, let her know I read it though Voicemail say she ready though (Yeah) Niggas know I’m credible, ain’t no pussy on a pedestal", "vault_lines": ["shut the fuck up", "Text from a centerfold, I ain't reply, let her know I read it though", "Voicemail say she ready though (Yeah)", "Niggas know I'm credible, ain't no pussy on a pedestal"], "meaning": "Even though Drake’s texting with a supermodel, he’s not starstruck. He’ll ignore her messages (and voicemails) begging to hook up, and he’ll even enable his read receipts so she’s 100% certain he’s not interested.\n\nThat’s just cold, Drizzy.", "annotation_id": "4888102", "url": "https://genius.com/4888102", "state": "accepted", "votes": 182, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Just hold on one moment and someone will be right with you (We’re sorry, you have reached a number that has been disconnected or is no longer in service)", "vault_lines": ["Just hold on one moment and someone will be right with you"], "meaning": "When calling a number that isn’t in service you hear this familiar tone. If you dial *67 it makes your cell number come up as unknown. This is a metaphor for Drake forgetting the fake people in his life. If they try to call him, they won’t reach him.", "annotation_id": "4888062", "url": "https://genius.com/4888062", "state": "accepted", "votes": 170, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "line": "Brand new Beretta, can’t wait to let it go Walk up in my label like, “Where the check, though?” Yeah, I said it Wouldn’t dap you with the left, ho,", "vault_lines": ["Wouldn't dap you with the left, ho,", "Brand new Beretta, can't wait to let it go", "Walk up in my label like, \"Where the check, though?\""], "meaning": "There’s been some tension within the YMCMB label, as Birdman refuses to release Weezy’s highly anticipated album Tha Carter V, leading Wayne to sue Birdman for 51 million dollars. It has also been reported that Birdman doesn’t pay anyone at all, which would anger the employees.\n\nThe mention of the “brand new Beretta” picks up from Wayne’s mention of “ammo” in the intro, referring to Drake’s new music. He’s ready with new material, but is unsure if he’ll get paid for it.\nThis could also a be a reference to the pistol he mentioned in the song 4pm in Calabasas, released shortly after IYRTITL.\n\n“Dapping” is a form of greeting where both parties touch fists. Handshakes are usually done with the right hand to show respect, and Drake doesn’t have enough respect to dap you with his left.", "annotation_id": "4887937", "url": "https://genius.com/4887937", "state": "accepted", "votes": 163, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Star67", "status": "done", "targets": 34, "clicked": 34, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-star67-lyrics", "genius_title": "Star67"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "To do what you couldn’t do", "vault_lines": ["To do what you couldn't do"], "meaning": "The line is a continuation of the chorus “Looking for revenge” and a prequel to what Drake has planned for his competition. Throughout the song Drake addresses rivals who tried to end his reign as one of the most influential hip hop artists of this era. These attempts fell short and now it is Drake’s turn to do to his rivals what they couldn’t do to him.", "annotation_id": "8636649", "url": "https://genius.com/8636649", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Now I got a house in LA, now I got a bigger pool than Ye And look man, Ye’s pool is nice, mine’s just bigger’s what I’m saying I’m that nigga’s what I’m saying", "vault_lines": ["Now I got a house in LA, now I got a bigger pool than Ye", "And look man, Ye’s pool is nice, mine's just bigger's what I’m saying", "I’m that nigga’s what I’m saying"], "meaning": "Drake’s LA house is massive, and at around 7,500 square feet it should put in perspective everything he’s done in his career. However, the thing he turns to as signal of success is the size of the pool. In an interview with Rolling Stone he said:\n\nI’m obsessed with, like, residential pools. One of my goals in life is to have the biggest residential pool on the planet.\n\nAlong with the Jay Z reference above, this playful jab is Drizzy’s way of comparing himself to hip hop legends. Kanye himself has at least one braggadocios line about his pool.\n\n\n\nDrake’s pool on the left, Kanye’s on the right\n\nOn Big Boy’s “The Neighborhood” radio show, Kanye was asked if Drake’s pool is really bigger than his. Unable to deny it, Ye defended himself stating he has 3 pools! However, three weeks after this song was released, TMZ reported Kanye had dug up his current pool and had to decided to build a “man made lake” on his Hidden Hills property.", "annotation_id": "8610312", "url": "https://genius.com/8610312", "state": "accepted", "votes": 210, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "You was never gang, gang, gang, gang, you was never one of us Had us fooled for a minute there, now we done all grown up But I’m better off anyway, y’all never gon’ finish Drake", "vault_lines": ["You was never gang, gang, gang, gang, you was never one of us", "Had us fooled for a minute there, now we done all grown up", "But I’m better off anyway, y’all never gon’ finish Drake"], "meaning": "Drake uses the Scarborough slang “Gang, gang, gang, gang,” which is chanted in repetition to depict how close knit of a crew you are, while  borrowing Tory Lanez’s flow of repeating words in his verses to add emphasis. He does this on his single “B.L.O.W.:”\n\nHate to be the reason\nWhy your dream bitches never get to see ya\nNever, ever, ever, ever, ever\nMe forever, ever, ever, ever, ever?\n\nIn an interview with HipHopDX back in 2014, Tory spoke about how Drake helped to put Toronto on the map and the influence he had on the growth of his music:\n\nI feel like in Toronto, we’ve always tried to have a particular sound, and I feel like Drake happened to be one of the dudes that paved that sound. […] I feel like [Drake has] helped me piece my sound together, and I feel like subconsciously, as an artist, you’re gonna take certain things from what you hear on a day-to-day basis.\n\nHowever, once Drake got bigger and started separating himself from his Toronto peers, artists like Tory started to show dislike. Drake noticed this and realized Tory’s true feelings towards him. It goes to show that Drake will stick to his “No New Friends” policy and focus on his business.\n\nTory seemingly addressed the line with a similar repetitive flow in his collaboration with A$AP Ferg, “Line Up The Flex”:\n\nI was never ever gang, gang, gang, gang\nI was One Umbrella Mob\n\nAssociate turned enemy Quentin Miller claimed this line was directed at him, responding his own track “unHoly:”\n\nI was never Gang Gang\nI was 1317 the whole time", "annotation_id": "8608545", "url": "https://genius.com/8608545", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Say you seeing ‘bout it when you see me man y’all never home anyway Thought of things that you shoulda said, said things that you shouldn’t say", "vault_lines": ["Say you seeing ‘bout it when you see me man y’all never home anyway", "Thought of things that you shoulda said, said things that you shouldn’t say"], "meaning": "This is a shot at the Toronto native Tory Lanez, who infamously went against the OVO regime, saying that calling Toronto the “6” is “not cool” in this tweet:\n\n\n\nDrake discredits Lanez’s argument, claiming he’s never even home in Toronto (look at the location of the tweet).\n\nTaking a look at Lanez’s Instagram, he also spends a lot of time in LA.", "annotation_id": "8608220", "url": "https://genius.com/8608220", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Getting things done around here How you let me run it down here? I’m not even from around here", "vault_lines": ["Getting things done around here", "How you let me run it down here?", "I’m not even from around here"], "meaning": "On his worst behaviour, Drake taunts the American competition, rhetorically asking how they let a Canadian “run” the rap game.\n\nAlthough Drake reps Toronto, he also has a home in L.A. He lives in L.A. for most of the year.\n\nThis may also be another thinly veiled shot at Canadian rapper Tory Lanez who raps with similar lyrics and flow on his song “Round Here”:\n\nDon’t you bring none of that ‘round here\nDon’t you bring none of that 'round here\nFuck nigga, don’t come around here\nFuck nigga, don’t come around here", "annotation_id": "8607748", "url": "https://genius.com/8607748", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Looking like a damn football team All in the same thing All repping one thing Looking for revenge", "vault_lines": ["Looking like a damn football team", "All in the same thing", "All repping one thing"], "meaning": "Drake compares his OVO Crew to an NFL squad—football rosters can boast up to 53 players; they all wear uniforms and all work towards a common goal. In Drake’s case, his goal is to own the summer of 2016.", "annotation_id": "8605374", "url": "https://genius.com/8605374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 198, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "[Beat switch]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "As verse 2 begins, the beat switches. Drake & Noah “40” Shebib have employed this on other songs like “0-100/The Catch Up.”\n\nThis part of the song samples “Glass Tubes” by Brain Bennett.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SPR7LVjnJ80\n\nClick here for additional production information.", "annotation_id": "8605260", "url": "https://genius.com/8605260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "We even gave y’all the whole money play and y’all broke to this day “Oh it’s your time now” yeah, that’s what everybody say", "vault_lines": ["We even gave y'all the whole money play and y'all broke to this day", "“Oh it’s your time now” yeah, that’s what everybody say"], "meaning": "Drake tried to give up and coming Toronto artists advice on how to make it in the industry. He may have also shared some of his $75 million dollar fortune with them. If this is in fact the case, it’s only natural he wants to cut ties when they go dissing his movement like Tory Lanez did.\n\nThis line is reminiscent of Kanye’s on “Gorgeous”\n\nYou blowin' up, that’s good, fantastic\nThat y’all, it’s like that y'all\nI don’t really give a fuck about it at all\n\n\n\nIt’s yet another shot at Torey Lanez, who has referred to this as being “histime” repeatedly in songs and interviews.", "annotation_id": "8604499", "url": "https://genius.com/8604499", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "I might get a key to the city and give it to Wayne", "vault_lines": ["I might get a key to the city and give it to Wayne"], "meaning": "In a rather prophetic manner, Drake saw this line come true less than a month after making the boast. On February 12, John Tory awarded Drake with the municipal honor.\n\nWe can only hope Drake will keep good on his promise and pass it on to mentor Lil Wayne. In Drake’s eyes, giving him the keys to the city would be superfluous, he knows he’s a big part of the city and doesn’t need a symbolic gesture to prove it.\n\nAlso, note the double entendre: Key Wane is a producer who has worked with both Drake and his beef partner Meek Mill on songs like “Amen” and “All Me.”", "annotation_id": "8604414", "url": "https://genius.com/8604414", "state": "accepted", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "I’m just a sicko, a real sicko when you get to know me, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I’m just a sicko, a real sicko when you get to know me, nigga"], "meaning": "Although his public image might portray him as a soft, innocent rapper in touch with his feelings, Drake claims to be a tough guy. He mentioned this in his song “No Tellin':”\n\nTrue say, I been goin' hard, but then again\nThey think I’m soft, think I’m innocent\n\n“6ickos” is the nickname of Drake’s friends and acquaintances. For example, Kevin Durant tweeted the following after Drake released “Charged Up”:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kdtrey5/status/625095671595401217", "annotation_id": "8604311", "url": "https://genius.com/8604311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Six, six, six, six, six, soon as I’m back in the city they throw a parade", "vault_lines": ["Six, six, six, six, six, soon as I’m back in the city they throw a parade"], "meaning": "The “Six” is short for the area codes of Toronto, Canada, Drake’s hometown. This line, then, picks up where the preceding one left off (“I’m not even from around here”).\n\nDrizzy has named numerous songs after the six and gave himself the “6 God” title. His accomplishments, such as album sales, affiliation with the Toronto Raptors, and deal with Apple Music alone are enough to make his city proud and celebrate when he returns home. Norm Kelly, Toronto politician and Drake advocate would most likely agree.\n\nOn September 11th, 2015, Drake and Future surprised Ryerson University students with a parade and concert. Norm Kelly made a guest appearance as well. This was only days before the artists' collaboration What a Time To be Alive was released.", "annotation_id": "8604301", "url": "https://genius.com/8604301", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Golden State running practice at my house", "vault_lines": ["Golden State running practice at my house"], "meaning": "Drake’s house has a basketball court. In an interview with CSN Bay Area, Drake explained the story behind the line, stating:\n\nSteph comes and hoops at the house, he plays Jazz a lot. That’s kind of where the line came from. And then I was like “man you know, we gotta ball this summer, we gotta actually play, you should bring the whole squad” and he’s [Steph] like “alright we’re gonna come, we might really come out and run a little scrimmage.” So I was like “okay, well I might have Golden State practicing at my house.”\n\nSteph further clarified the line in April 2016, stating that he was the only member of the team present:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cMvRnxVXnu4\n\nDrake mentioned Steph before on 2014’s “0 to 100/ The Catch Up”:\n\nI been Steph Curry with the shot\nBeen cookin' with the sauce, Chef Curry with the pot, boy\n360 with the wrist, boy", "annotation_id": "8604264", "url": "https://genius.com/8604264", "state": "accepted", "votes": 155, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Nigga, what am I about? You gon’ really feel it now I’m out here…", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, what am I about?", "You gon’ really feel it now"], "meaning": "While he used to complain about being so busy to not have enough time to relax in the summer, Drake is back on the grind and ready to body anyone who tries to get in his way this summer. Just like fellow rapper Kanye West said on “Otis:”\n\nNow I’m about to make them tuck their whole summer in\n\nBe prepared to feel the wrath of the Almighty 6 God.", "annotation_id": "8604253", "url": "https://genius.com/8604253", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "You would love it if I went away or didn’t say nothing else", "vault_lines": ["You would love it if I went away or didn’t say nothing else"], "meaning": "Drake addresses all his enemies and reminds them who’s keeping it real. While they respond on social media and behind his back, Drake shares his grievances on the public record.", "annotation_id": "8604242", "url": "https://genius.com/8604242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "[Produced by Noah “40” Shebib, Boi-1da & Cubeatz]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song contains a sample from the outro of “The Question Is” by The Winans and a slowed down sample from Brian Bennett’s song, “Glass Tubes.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XTGaZXsRgvo", "annotation_id": "8604220", "url": "https://genius.com/8604220", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Tell Obama that my verses are just like the whips that he in They bulletproof", "vault_lines": ["Tell Obama that my verses are just like the whips that he in"], "meaning": "US presidents have been issued their own presidential state cars since the late 1930s. President Obama’s Cadillac is bulletproof and bombproof, just like Drake’s verses.\n\nWhen asked about who’d win in a rap battle between Drake and Kendrick Lamar, Obama went with Kendrick. Drizzy wants to assure him that his verses are A1.", "annotation_id": "8604195", "url": "https://genius.com/8604195", "state": "accepted", "votes": 382, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Minus twenty we in Pitfield That Kai’s kitchen in a Canada Goose Famous as fuck but I’m still in the cut when they round up the troops", "vault_lines": ["Minus twenty we in Pitfield", "That Kai's kitchen in a Canada Goose", "Famous as fuck but I’m still in the cut when they round up the troops"], "meaning": "Drake juxtaposes a historically poor, more “hood” side of Toronto with the swanky Fashion District to prove that he can mingle with everyone from shooters to chefs.\n\nPitfield Road is in Scarborough, Toronto. The borough is well known for its West Indian community, something Drake has rapped about before. Drake made a cameo in P. Reign’s “In My Hood” video on Pitfield road in 2010.\n\n“Kai’s kitchen,” more commonly known as Fring’s, is a restaurant located in the wealthy Fashion District. Drake launched the restaurant alongside Toronto-based chef and Masterchef Asia judge Susur Lee in 2015. As reported by Fashion Magazine, Drake is a longtime friend of Lee’s sons, Kai and Levi.\n\nIn 2015, Drake and Canada Goose teamed up for an OVO x Canada Goose collection. Prices start at $895.", "annotation_id": "8604177", "url": "https://genius.com/8604177", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Looking, looking, looking Looking, looking, looking Looking, looking Looking Looking", "vault_lines": ["Looking like a damn football team"], "meaning": "Drake is “looking for revenge” on his new album, Views (released later on April 29, 2016). You can see an actual eye in the artwork of the song, which appears to be a hand signal.", "annotation_id": "8604143", "url": "https://genius.com/8604143", "state": "accepted", "votes": 186, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Chris Breezy with the dance moves", "vault_lines": ["Chris Breezy with the dance moves", "Mo-G with the dance moves", "Ave Boy with the dance moves"], "meaning": "Although Drake and Chris Brown have a checkered past, this isn’t a shot at the R&B singer.\n\nDrake’s comparing his dance moves in the “Hotline Bling” music video to Chris Brown. While Breezy is famous for his footwork, Drake is too, just in a different sense:", "annotation_id": "8604122", "url": "https://genius.com/8604122", "state": "accepted", "votes": 231, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Why would I put on a vest? I expect you to aim for the head", "vault_lines": ["Why would I put on a vest? I expect you to aim for the head"], "meaning": "A bulletproof vest protects your torso from stab wounds and gunfire. In combat, a “head shot” is considered the better option since the victim won’t be protected. Drake also expects opposition to “aim for the head” because without a brain he would not be able to write more lyrics or produce songs. When Drake released two diss tracks—“Charged Up” and “Back to Back”—he expected more from Meek than the missed opportunity that is “Wanna Know.”\n\nWhile it seems he considered Meek’s last diss track a total waste of time, “War Pain” is a different story. Meek released the song 15 minutes after Drake released “Summer Sixteen.” Meek responds to all his disses, a feat that would have been impossible without hearing the song first. Meek claims Drake’s “ghost writer” leaked the song.\n\nCould also be a reference to this Jay Z line from H.A.M., which keeps in line with other references to “The Throne” in this song.", "annotation_id": "8604118", "url": "https://genius.com/8604118", "state": "accepted", "votes": 157, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Looking for revenge All summer sixteen All summer sixteen Playing dirty not clean Out in front of Four Seasons", "vault_lines": ["Playing dirty not clean", "Out in front of Four Seasons"], "meaning": "The first half of this hook alludes to the ongoing rivalry between Drake and Meek Mill. The beef first started in July 2015 when Meek took shots at Drake for ghostwriting. Drake quickly fired back with two diss tracks—“Charged Up” and “Back to Back”—and Meek responded with “Wanna Know.” This all happened in the span of one month. Drake sent some shots at Meek in late 2015 with his Future collab album What A Time To Be Alive, but it wasn’t until earlier in 2016 that Meek rekindled the beef by attacking Drake on his 4/4 EP.\n\nWhen Meek came to Toronto as part of the Pinkprint Tour, he stayed at the Four Seasons. Drake booked the room right on top of Meek’s and played “Back to Back” just to mock him all night. He addresses this in the first verse:\n\nI let the diss record drop, you was staying right below me, nigga", "annotation_id": "8604103", "url": "https://genius.com/8604103", "state": "accepted", "votes": 383, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Yeah, and I could really dish it out Come and get it from the source Fuck with all the word of mouth", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and I could really dish it out", "Come and get it from the source", "Fuck with all the word of mouth"], "meaning": "Drake has shown time and time again that he is not the man to mess with. His massive fanbase and lyrical prowess makes him unstoppable, and when he comes for you he will put all of his energy into dishing out a loss.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623700698509758464\n\nIn Meek’s frustration, he put out a tweet explaining that the reason Drake wouldn’t tweet his album out was because Atlanta rapper Quentin Miller wrote his verse for him. This is a petty thing to say publicly when it could have been addressed in person.\n\n“By word of mouth” is a common idiom, meaning something that is spread orally rather than through writing; this type of communication is generally to be implied as less official and more prone to rumors than truths. All the rumors about Drake have him confused; it makes more sense to go to him for the official word, rather than paying attention to rumors.", "annotation_id": "8604064", "url": "https://genius.com/8604064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Mo-G with the dance moves Ave Boy with the dance moves", "vault_lines": ["Chris Breezy with the dance moves", "Mo-G with the dance moves", "Ave Boy with the dance moves"], "meaning": "Mo-G is a Canadian rapper from the rap group Halal Gang. His latest project is titled Ave Boy, which OVO helped finance the recording for.\n\nMo-G’s line “Switch it up with the left like Ginobili” in the song “Still” inspired a dance that Drake performs in the video for “Energy.” This is the same dance move he referred to on “Jumpman”:\n\nI hit that Ginobili with my left hand up, like woo!\"", "annotation_id": "8604063", "url": "https://genius.com/8604063", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "It’s nothing personal I would have done it to anyone And I blame where I came from", "vault_lines": ["It’s nothing personal I would have done it to anyone", "And I blame where I came from"], "meaning": "Regardless of who would diss him, Drake’s insults and behaviour would be justifiable in any situation.  This claim is nice in sentiment, but many people have dissed Drake and never heard a reply from him.\n\nFor Drake, the frigid Canadian climate only made him a tougher, more emotionally cold person. Drake rapped about this on “6 Man”:\n\nI’m here to fuck with nigga’s souls, my heart is cold\nIt’s prolly cause I’m from the snow, with all my woes", "annotation_id": "8604053", "url": "https://genius.com/8604053", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Then I hit ‘em with the Hotline", "vault_lines": ["Then I hit ‘em with the Hotline"], "meaning": "Drake released “Hotline Bling” on OVO Sound Radio the same day he dropped his Meek Mill diss “Charged Up.” This showcases Drake’s versatility — he can switch between a hard diss track and a hit single with ease. After the initial chatter about the beef and “Charged Up” died down, “Hotline Bling” went on to peak at No. 2 on the Billboard 100.", "annotation_id": "8604051", "url": "https://genius.com/8604051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Or give it to one of the young boys to carry the wave Yeah, so trust me, they’ll be out here…", "vault_lines": ["Or give it to one of the young boys to carry the wave", "Yeah, so trust me, they’ll be out here…"], "meaning": "Perhaps Roy Woods? It’s well known that the two are friendly, Drake even premiered the young Torontonian’s remix of Kodak Black’s Skrt on the same OVO Sound Radio Episode we first heard “Summer Sixteen.”\n\n\n\nIn December 2015, Drake made an Instagram post of himself and PARTYNEXTDOOR. The caption read: “borrow our waves, but we need that back.”", "annotation_id": "8604029", "url": "https://genius.com/8604029", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "And I blame all my day ones You know Chubbs like Draymond You better off not saying nothing Them boys they a handful", "vault_lines": ["And I blame all my day ones", "You know Chubbs like Draymond", "You better off not saying nothing", "Them boys they a handful"], "meaning": "Drake compares his relationship with his right hand man and bodyguard “Chubbs” to Stephen Curry and Draymond Green of the Golden State Warriors.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BByUi9eQj-K/?taken-by=chubbsview\n\n Draymond Green with Chubbs at NBA All-Star Weekend 2016 in Toronto \n\nDraymond is 6'7\", 230 pounds and is considered Steph Curry’s “muscle” on the court. In January 2015, Green had to hold Curry back from fighting with Trevor Ariza of the Houston Rockets. Chubbs may have done the same for Drizzy once or twice.\n\nhttps://vine.co/v/OIrOpWAPHnT", "annotation_id": "8604009", "url": "https://genius.com/8604009", "state": "accepted", "votes": 169, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "I let the diss record drop, you was staying right below me, nigga We must have played it a hundred times, you was going to bed", "vault_lines": ["I let the diss record drop, you was staying right below me, nigga", "We must have played it a hundred times, you was going to bed"], "meaning": "Meek Mill accompanied Nicki Minaj on her The Pinkprint tour, which eventually rolled around to Toronto. When it did (right after “Charged Up” dropped), they stayed in a Four Seasons Hotel. Drake checked into the same hotel and played “Back to Back” on repeat in a room directly above him.\n\nMeek responded to these claims in the intro of his response track, “War Pain”:\n\nLocation: Toronto; Status: five star hotel, Four Seasons. Them chumps right upstairs, they know not to come down here playin' no real niggas. Mood: I’m still up countin' five hundred thousand cash. Nicki in the bedroom sleep, life is good", "annotation_id": "8603996", "url": "https://genius.com/8603996", "state": "accepted", "votes": 255, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "Jimi Hendrix with the solo Those are strings that you can’t pull", "vault_lines": ["Jimi Hendrix with the solo", "Those are strings that you can’t pull"], "meaning": "Drake compares himself to legendary guitarist Jimi Hendrix. Other artists can’t pull the same strings he can because they aren’t on the same level.\n\nDrake has referred to Hendrix before in 2011 on “Free Spirit,” and even left  a note on the great guitarist’s grave.\n\nWho else could pull off 14 simultaneous hot 100 songs and create a joint album with Future in the same year?\n\nNo one, quite literally, could pull the same guitar strings as the legendary Jimi Hendrix. Since he was left-handed, he played a right-handed guitar upside down.\n\n\n\nOl' Dirty Bastard spit a similar line 21 years earlier, on “Damage.”", "annotation_id": "8603989", "url": "https://genius.com/8603989", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "I coulda killed you the first time", "vault_lines": ["I coulda killed you the first time"], "meaning": "In summer of 2015, Drake released both “Charged Up” and  “Back to Back”—aimed at Meek Mill.  Drake claims that, even though he went back to back, he could have dropped “Back to Back” as the first track and ruined Meek regardless. Drake is saying he could’ve went harder on Charged Up and avoided releasing “Back to Back”", "annotation_id": "8603986", "url": "https://genius.com/8603986", "state": "accepted", "votes": 142, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "They don’t want us to have a bigger pool than Kanye", "vault_lines": ["They don’t want us to have a bigger pool than Kanye"], "meaning": "In late 2015/early 2016, frequent Drake collaborator and friend DJ Khaled became a worldwide sensation for his Snapchat stories. Khaled often uses Snapchat to share his “keys to success,” and frequently uses the phrase “they don’t want you to…”\n\nIn one of his stories, Khaled proclaimed “They don’t want you to have a bigger pool than Kanye West,” in order to prove to his followers that you can overcome any limit society puts on you.\n\n\n\nDrake has a pretty big pool himself! In the summer of 2015, he  threw a massive pool party that Kanye attended.", "annotation_id": "8603985", "url": "https://genius.com/8603985", "state": "accepted", "votes": 481, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "You don’t have to try and say it louder, nigga Trust, we heard you the first time", "vault_lines": ["You don’t have to try and say it louder, nigga", "Trust, we heard you the first time"], "meaning": "Meek Mill is often criticized for yelling while he raps. Drake makes it clear that “Wanna Know” was enough of a failed diss record and Meek did not need to waste his time with the additional disses on his 4/4 EP.", "annotation_id": "8603984", "url": "https://genius.com/8603984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 191, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "All you boys in the new Toronto want to be me a little", "vault_lines": ["All you boys in the new Toronto want to be me a little", "All in the same thing"], "meaning": "In 2009, Drake exploded onto the U.S rap scene and became one of the first urban artists from Toronto to truly breakthrough into the mainstream. Since then, he’s founded the OVO empire and become one of the most recognized artists of a generation.\n\nFellow Toronto rapper, Tory Lanez, had thrown subliminals Drake’s way before this song was released. He recently dropped a new mixtape titled The New Toronto.\n\nOn “War Pain”, Meek Mill’s response to Summer Sixteen (which came out only 15 minutes after Drake premiered Summer Sixteen), Meek refutes this line and defends Tory Lanez, questioning how Drake could attack a young person from his own city trying to come up in the game. Tory collaborated with Meek on Meek’s song, “Lord Knows.”\n\nTory was recently questioned about this line and you can see his response, as well as a history of the “beef”, in the video.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UUxh_M7ZWp0\n\nTory went on to release a cover of  Drake’s “Controlla” from his album Views. On 20 July 2016, on the opening night of his “Summer Sixteen Tour” with Future, Drake altered this particular lyric to address Tory Lanez’s cover. He changed it to:\n\nAll you boys doing fake Controllas want to be me a little\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aGPi1InmeSw", "annotation_id": "8603982", "url": "https://genius.com/8603982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 165, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "All your exes know I like my O’s with a V in the middle", "vault_lines": ["All your exes know I like my O’s with a V in the middle", "All in the same thing"], "meaning": "Drake cleverly blends the phrase X’s and O’s while referencing his record label and ex girlfriends. “O’s with a V in the middle” spells out the letters of the label he holds very dear to his heart: OVO Sound.\n\n\n\nDrake and The Weeknd’s two labels make up OVOXO.", "annotation_id": "8603972", "url": "https://genius.com/8603972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 191, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Summer Sixteen", "line": "I used to wanna be on Roc-A-Fella then I turned into Jay", "vault_lines": ["I used to wanna be on Roc-A-Fella then I turned into Jay"], "meaning": "Although Jay Z and Drizzy have had a somewhat turbulent history, there’s no denying Jigga is one of the most skilled and successful MCs of all time. Although he’s served as a role model, and was in talks to sign Drake early on, Drake started taking some of Shawn’s records years ago, as well as perhaps making OVO the new Roc-A-Fella, which was enough to earn him the nickname of “This Generation’s Jay Z.”\n\nMeek Mill fired back at Drake minutes after this song was released with “And you claimin' you HOV now? Why you state that shit?”", "annotation_id": "8603968", "url": "https://genius.com/8603968", "state": "accepted", "votes": 175, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
