{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I’m greater than everybody like some shredded cheese", "vault_lines": ["I'm greater than everybody like some shredded cheese"], "meaning": "Shredded cheese is a common topping for foods. With shredded cheese drake is implying he’s on top of everything, like you would see on i.e a pizza. Like he’s above the rest.", "annotation_id": "40134874", "url": "https://genius.com/40134874", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I’m better off independent, they should let him leave, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I'm better off independent, they should let him leave, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake’s tension with his label, Universal Music Group, stems from a major legal dispute he initiated with the label post Kendrick Lamar’s diss track, “Not Like Us”. Drake alleged that UMG promoted the song using illegal streaming and broadcasting tactics to damage his brand.\n\nDrake has come to the realisation that he no longer requires the support of UMG for his promotion, as his name is solidified in the industry.", "annotation_id": "39925757", "url": "https://genius.com/39925757", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "To let you know that everything that’s said to me will forever be stored in my memory", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the rap feud between Drake and Kendrick Lamar in 2024, Kendrick released “Euphoria” where he disses Drake several times.\n\nDementia must run in his family, but let it get shaky\nI’ll park his son\n\nAlthough the mentioning of dementia was just a set up for the punchline, Drake is referencing this bar, debunking the claim. He responds to “Euphoria” in the lyrics prior.", "annotation_id": "39925693", "url": "https://genius.com/39925693", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Turnin’ on my read receipts", "vault_lines": ["And that's why I'm turnin' on my read receipts"], "meaning": "If you use iMessage, you can turn on “read receipts,” which means the other person will  see “Read” whenever you’ve read his/her message. If you have them off, the other person will only see “Delivered” once you receive the message but has no way of knowing whether you’ve seen it.\n\nEveryone knows that if you a real one who keeps it 100, you keep read receipts turned on.", "annotation_id": "39843711", "url": "https://genius.com/39843711", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Free Free (Yeah) Free", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The track is built over a sample of Deniece Williams‘ 1976 soul hit, “Free.”\n\nhttps://youtu.be/OlLrn6AVV2s?si=ZEIm15NfC5Ff1wK_&t=123\n\nDrake uses it as a plea for release, whether it’s from pressure, expectations, conflict, or the weight of his own reputation. However, he could also be talking about his contract with Universal Music Group—pleading that he wants to get out of his current deal with them, amidst his dismissed defamation lawsuit with the label.\n\nDrake also asked to be “freed” by UMG on the ICEMAN track, “Janice STFU”:\n\nSwear my label gotta free me, baby", "annotation_id": "39801671", "url": "https://genius.com/39801671", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I was in a dream hotel before I was in the Loop like a carabiner, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I was in Hazelton at the One before I was in the Loop like a set of keys", "I was in a dream hotel before I was in the Loop like a carabiner, yeah"], "meaning": "“Dream Hotel” most likely refers to the luxury hotel chain, while “The Loop” refers to being inside elite industry circles, social networks, or high-status environments. Drake is saying he experienced luxury and aspirational living before he was fully accepted into those circles.\n\nThe “carabiner” line is a double entendre. A carabiner is a metal clip that loops onto things like belts, ropes, or keychains. Drake compares himself being “in the Loop” to how a carabiner physically loops into place.\n\nThe line also continues on recurring themes of success before acceptance, being an outsider despite fame and moving through luxury spaces before industry validation\n\nIt also mirrors a line later in the song:\n\nI was in Hazelton at the One before I was in the Loop like a set of keys\n\nAdditionally, “the Loop” refers to the central business district in downtown Chicago, named for the elevated ‘L’ train tracks that loop around it.", "annotation_id": "39814347", "url": "https://genius.com/39814347", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Damn, who is this guy for real? I guess a magician Hundred million streams vanished, no one got questions for niggas", "vault_lines": ["Damn, who is this guy for real? I guess a magician", "Hundred million streams vanished, no one got questions for niggas"], "meaning": "Through his company Frozen Moments, Drake accused both Spotify and UMG of artificially inflating streaming numbers for “Not Like Us” as part of a campaign to turn the diss track into a hit.\n\nAfter Kendrick’s Super Bowl performance, a Twitter/X parody account called PooTingz claimed that Spotify had removed approximately 235 million streams from Kendrick and SZA’s “Luther” as part of its bot purge. Spotify has never confirmed the figures or named any affected artists, so the exact scale remains unverified. Aside from the now-deleted post from PooTings and a separate post from DJ Akademiks, which used Twitter/X’s generative AI bot Grok as a source, there’s been no substantial proof of any of Kendrick’s streams actually being removed.\n\nThe narrative that Kendrick bots his streams ties back to Drake jabs from “THE HEART PART 6”:\n\nStop buyin' views and bot comments, you may as well keep the paper\nShit you ‘bout to need for later\nI give a fuck about your streamin’ data\n\nIt also ties into more subtle lines on “Family Matters,” where he insinuated a bigger scheme was at play here when it came to fake streams linked to a company named “Nowhere to Hide” connected to Kojo Asamoah:\n\nAnthony set up the plays, Kojo be chargin' you double for nothin'\n…\nThere’s nowhere to hide, there’s nowhere to hide, you know what I mean", "annotation_id": "39811597", "url": "https://genius.com/39811597", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I got problems with people who I would’ve never dreamed", "vault_lines": ["I got problems with people who I would've never dreamed"], "meaning": "Following the theme of drake referencing his beef with Virginia natives & legends Pharrell & Pusha t\nHe acknowledges that before he became the big star he is he never fathomed that these people who was once his idols would eventually become his rivals later in his career as he got bigger pause.\n\nIn his early interviews, such as in the 2000s, Drake frequently cited Pharrell and The Neptunes as the primary influence that made him want to become a rapper\nHowever, their relationship has grown tense in recent years with Drake taking jabs at the superproducer over jewelry and fashion to the petty point of even buying his jewelry collection & flexing with it\nDrake flaunted the pieces in his “Jumbotron Shit Poppin” music video and has later used his ownership of Pharrell’s legacy as a way to send shots just like he did previously in the lyrics of this song\n\nDrake also bought an autographed microphone allegedly used by Pusha T on eBay in 2010 for $200. He bought it because he was a massive fan of Pusha T’s rap group, Clipse. He used it to perform their songs in his basement, even rubbing the signature off with excessive use.Drake first shared this story publicly in 2010 on MTV’s When I Was 17. Years later, during their 2018 rap feud, Drake weaponized this fan moment against Pusha T on his diss track “Duppy Freestyle”. In the song, he rapped about how the faded autograph mirrored how their relationship had deteriorated.", "annotation_id": "39798336", "url": "https://genius.com/39798336", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "These niggas measurin’ they dick size in millimeters", "vault_lines": ["These niggas measurin' they dick size in millimeters"], "meaning": "Drake uses penis length as a metaphor to show the difference between him and the other rappers in the game. His dick is bigger than theirs cause hes just on a whole other level above them. In 2024, an explicit video of Drake leaked and many memes circulated joking about how big Drake’s genitals were. His dick is bigger than his competitors both metaphorically and literally.", "annotation_id": "39791467", "url": "https://genius.com/39791467", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "But let me find out that Amex the only ones that credit me for makin’ y’all everything you could never be", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "An Amex is an American credit card known to have high or even non-existent spending limits due to the strict requirements to obtain the card. Drake uses this as an analogy to state that while his peers seem to care when it benefits them financially and use his credit (card), they are unable to give him actual credit when it comes to congratulating and supporting him.\n\nDrake also under UMG has been the biggest artist under their portfolio. His alleged exit of UMG would see their companies value decrease. UMG’s portfolio and borrowing of capital is affected by this is exit. Artist under UMG would’ve benefited from Drake simply being signed to UMG, but his exit lowers UMG borrowing power.", "annotation_id": "39789499", "url": "https://genius.com/39789499", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Well, maybe I’m best as the villain ’Cause I got real numbers on niggas who say they got billions", "vault_lines": ["Well, maybe I'm best as the villain", "'Cause I got real numbers on niggas who say they got billions"], "meaning": "This could be a dig at Kanye West. In 2025, Kanye claimed he was a billionaire again, estimating his net worth to be $2.77 billion. However, as of May 2026, Forbes still had him listed at $400 million.\n\nIn 2022, Adidas terminated their partnership with Kanye after he made antisemitic comments, which resulted in his estimated net worth plummeting from $2 billion down to $400 million.", "annotation_id": "39789487", "url": "https://genius.com/39789487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "And apparently niggas’ souls is for sale like the seven seas", "vault_lines": ["And apparently niggas' souls is for sale like the seven seas"], "meaning": "References the common rap idea of artists “selling their soul,” with Drake playing on the homonym of “sailing” through the seven seas.", "annotation_id": "39779838", "url": "https://genius.com/39779838", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "But I realize it’s gotta get a little deeper Other than me talking ‘bout the way I’m flippin’ dough like Little Caesars", "vault_lines": ["But I realize it's gotta get a little deeper", "Other than me talking 'bout the way I'm flippin' dough like Little Caesars"], "meaning": "“Dough” refers to Drake’s cash flow, with an estimated net worth of $400M.\n\nThis is also a reference to the fast-food pizza restaurant Little Caesars, which is said to use pan-based methods when making pizza, rather than flipping pizza dough.", "annotation_id": "39779746", "url": "https://genius.com/39779746", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I remember lobster ravioli up at Serafina", "vault_lines": ["I remember lobster ravioli up at Serafina"], "meaning": "Serafina is a popular Italian restaurant chain in New York City, known for pasta dishes like lobster ravioli.", "annotation_id": "39779736", "url": "https://genius.com/39779736", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "And shout to all the sweet tings from Wilhelmina Who tell me at the white party, “Drake, I love the album, man, well, I mean, you’re kinda genius” And like Dipset, they really mean it, yeah I need compliments ’cause lately, it’s just falling-outs and disagreements, industry is really evil", "vault_lines": ["And shout to all the sweet tings from Wilhelmina", "Who tell me at the white party, \"Drake, I love the album, man, well, I mean, you're kinda gеnius\"", "And like Dipset, they rеally mean it, yeah"], "meaning": "Wilhelmina Models is a popular modeling agency based in New York City with satellite offices in places like Los Angeles, Miami, and London. Their models are often hot comities at billionaire Michael Rubin and his celebrity-filled “White Parties.” In fact, Drake attended one of his parties in 2024.\n\nDrake also nods to The Diplomats—also known as Dipset—and their March 2003 classic, “I Really Mean It.” He notes his appreciation for positive feedback, following a turbulent 2024 with his Kendrick Lamar beef—resulting in intense public scrutiny from critics and a legal fallout with his record label.", "annotation_id": "39779080", "url": "https://genius.com/39779080", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I done got my shit inside arenas like Gilbert Arenas", "vault_lines": ["I done got my shit inside arenas like Gilbert Arenas"], "meaning": "In December 2009, three-time NBA All-Star Gilbert Arenas brought unloaded firearms into the Washington Wizards' locker room during a dispute with teammate Javaris Crittenton over a gambling debt from a card game.\n\nArenas responded to the callout on his show.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/gT8uy1x1-ME", "annotation_id": "39779063", "url": "https://genius.com/39779063", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "My sixth sense is kickin’ in ’cause all I hear is people that are dead to me", "vault_lines": ["My sixth sense is kickin' in 'cause all I hear is people that are dead to me"], "meaning": "This line references the 1999 horror film The Sixth Sense, which explores the story of Cole Sear (portrayed by Haley Joel Osment), a troubled child, who is gifted with a unique power to communicate with the dead.\n\nThis calls back to Kendrick Lamar’s bars from “euphoria”:\n\nAnd my sixth sense tellin' me to off him", "annotation_id": "39778811", "url": "https://genius.com/39778811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Now the penthouse in Bahamas is right next to Jordan like I’m Lebanese", "vault_lines": ["Now the penthouse in Bahamas is right next to Jordan like I'm Lebanese"], "meaning": "Michael Jordan owns a private, ultra-luxury estate in the Bahamas. While not sharing a border, Lebanon and Jordan are neighboring countries in the Levant.", "annotation_id": "39778762", "url": "https://genius.com/39778762", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Shout out to Cherokee D and Skyy Black ‘Cause they was at my birthday when I was searchin’ big-booty ebonies, jeez", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to Cherokee D and Skyy Black", "'Cause they was at my birthday when I was searchin' big-booty ebonies, jeez"], "meaning": "Drake is known for having a long history of referencing strip club culture, adult entertainers, escorts and internet sex symbols in his music.\nHere, he shouts out two professional adult actresses, Cherokee Dee and Skyy Black.\n\nConnections can be made to his 38th birthday in 2024, where he celebrated in Houston with a combination of a family dinner and a wild night at a strip club, in which he may have possibly flown two of his favourite escorts out.\n\nSearchin’ big-booty ebonies\n\nMeans he was specifically looking for Black women with curvier body types. “Ebony” or “ebonie” is common slang in adult entertainment and hip hop culture referring to Black women.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, Cherokee D’Ass took to social media about the bar, saying, “Rapping about shit  that happened over 20 years ago lol.”", "annotation_id": "39778693", "url": "https://genius.com/39778693", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Now you gotta plan a meeting with me just like the Russian leader and shake my fuckin’ hand in front of other people", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Vladimir Putin is a Russian intelligence officer who has served as the country’s president since 2012. He’s considered one of the most powerful leaders of the 21st century.\n\nWhen meeting with other world leaders, Putin usually shake their hands—for example, he shook President Donald Trump’s hand after the two met in Alaska in 2025.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9Cqx_goXHv4", "annotation_id": "39778351", "url": "https://genius.com/39778351", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "And ain’t no passes for you Crip walkin’, all of that diss talkin’, kiddo, beat it I’m supposed to be my brother’s keeper, so don’t make us hit another buzzer beater And don’t be suddenly eager to hit me up and have no discussions either", "vault_lines": ["And ain't no passes for you Crip walkin', all of that diss talkin', kiddo, beat it", "I'm supposed to be my brother's keeper, so don't make us hit another buzzer beater", "And don't be suddenly eager to hit me up and have no discussions either"], "meaning": "“Am I my brother’s keeper?” is a phrase that originated from the Biblical story of Cain and Abel—specifically Genesis 4:9—where Cain responds to God after murdering his brother Abel:\n\nThen the Lord said to Cain, “Where is Abel your brother?” He said, “I do not know; am I my brother’s keeper?”\n\nThis could be a reference to LeBron James—he was seen dancing to Kendrick’s “Not Like Us” at a day party in France in July 2025. The “crip walk” is a signature dance created by Crip gang members but Drake’s likely using it as a nod to James' dancing to the song.\n\nThe “buzzer beater” line calls back to Kawhi Leonard’s buzzer beater over the Philadelphia 76ers in the second round of the 2019 NBA playoffs. Philadelphia is also known as the “City of Brotherly Love,” which plays into the “my brother’s keeper” line.", "annotation_id": "39778304", "url": "https://genius.com/39778304", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I rack up a tab in Chanel ’cause I do buy everything like I’m Middle Eastern", "vault_lines": ["I rack up a tab in Chanel 'cause I do buy everything like I'm Middle Eastern"], "meaning": "Chanel is an expensive French fashion house. Drake plays on his extravagant spending habits by using “do buy” as a homonym for Dubai, a city known for its luxury and wealth. “I rack” also sounds like Iraq, another Middle Eastern country.\n\nDrake’s also insinuating that he spends like a stereotypically wealthy Middle Eastern figure who never says no to buying expensive things, furthering this wordplay.\n\nDrake similar used Iraq wordplay on Travis Scott’s July 2023 track, “MELTDOWN” reading:\n\nI told her I don’t got no cash and she said she could wait on a rack, on some Arabic shit", "annotation_id": "39778235", "url": "https://genius.com/39778235", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I’m tryna catch one of you boys stargazin’ in the field like little leaguers, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I'm tryna catch one of you boys stargazin' in the field like little leaguers, yeah"], "meaning": "In little league baseball, kids tend to easily get distracted in the outfield—“stargazing”—which tends to lead to a missed opportunity for a play to be made. Similarly, Drake is waiting for his enemies to drop their guard and strike them.\n\nHowever, this could also be a shot at Travis Scott—he has a song titled “STARGAZING,” which is the intro to his August 2018 album ASTROWORLD. In March 2024, Travis notably asked Metro Boomin and Future to play their “Like That” record at Rolling Loud, the song that ignited the Drake and Kendrick beef.", "annotation_id": "39777088", "url": "https://genius.com/39777088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Yeah, look Albany Marina, they done served us a hundred subpoenas", "vault_lines": ["Albany Marina, they done served us a hundred subpoenas"], "meaning": "Located on the southwestern coast of The Bahamas' New Providence Island, Albany Marina is a premier mega-yacht facility. Drake uses the Bahamian paradise of the Albany Marina to show that extreme wealth does not buy peace of mind. Even while surrounded by the ultimate luxury, the reality of his industry status means he and his circle are still plagued by a constant stream of legal conflicts.\n\n\n\n\nAlbany Marina in the Bahamas", "annotation_id": "39776678", "url": "https://genius.com/39776678", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "My niggas run a phone soon as you check into prison Your niggas run and talk to Hov for a second opinion Me, I stood ten T’s and accepted the mission ’Cause I’d much rather death than submission How can you press the ignition and let some memories of the past affect your decision? I love you ‘cause of the history, but if we bein’ real, I could never forgive you And you never called me back, but destiny’s written, for real", "vault_lines": ["My niggas run a phone soon as you check into prison", "Your niggas run and talk to Hov for a second opinion", "Me, I stood ten T's and accepted the mission", "'Cause I'd much rather death than submission", "How can you press the ignition and let some memories of the past affect your decision?", "I love you 'cause of the history, but if we bein' real, I could never forgive you", "And you never called me back, but destiny's written, for real"], "meaning": "These lines are directed at J. Cole—after Kendrick took shots at him and Drake on “Like That,” Cole responded with “7 Minute Drill” before publicly apologizing 48 hours later for the track and called it one of the “lamest shit I ever did in my fucking life.”\n\nHov is JAY-Z, who is Cole’s mentor. Similar to Cole, Hov was involved in a beef with Nas in 2001. After Nas released the scathing “Ether,” Hov would respond with “Super Ugly”—a record that caused him to apologize after his mother said he went “too far.” Therefore, Drake could be a drawing a direct parallel to both rappers apologizing in their respective beefs.", "annotation_id": "39776517", "url": "https://genius.com/39776517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I was in Hazelton at the One before I was in the Loop like a set of keys", "vault_lines": ["I was in Hazelton at the One before I was in the Loop like a set of keys", "I was in a dream hotel before I was in the Loop like a carabiner, yeah"], "meaning": "The Hazelton Hotel is a five-star luxury hotel located in the prestigious Yorkville neighborhood of Toronto, Canada and ONE Restaurant is a fine dining establishment located inside the hotel. Drake has been spotted at the hotel as far back as 2010.\n\n\nThe One Restaurant", "annotation_id": "39776433", "url": "https://genius.com/39776433", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Dog, I was aidin’ Ross with streams before Adin Ross had ever streamed", "vault_lines": ["Dog, I was aidin' Ross with streams before Adin Ross had ever streamed"], "meaning": "Drake takes a shot at rapper Rick Ross over their fallout during the 2024 feud involving Kendrick Lamar. Ross previously released the diss track “Champagne Moments,” aimed at Drake, despite the two having a long history of collaborations together.\n\nDrake plays on the phrase “aidin’ Ross,” implying he helped boost Rick Ross’ career by collaborating with him frequently, driving streams and attention. He then flips the wording into a reference to popular live streamer Adin Ross. The bar is a clever word play, comparing Drake “aiding Ross” before Adin Ross had even started streaming.\n\n\n\nTheir first collaboration dates back to 2010, with the partnership producing classics like “Aston Martin Music,” “Stay Schemin',” “Lord Knows”  and “Lemon Pepper Freestyle.” Drake was even responsible for giving Rick Ross his first ever billion-streamed song on Spotify with “Money in the Grave.” Adin Ross began streaming back in 2019.", "annotation_id": "39776012", "url": "https://genius.com/39776012", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Yeah, fuck the chatter Since 2009, as I climb the ladder They envy me like Nevada, but it never mattered", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, fuck the chatter", "Since 2009, as I climb the ladder", "They envy me like Nevada, but it never mattered"], "meaning": "In 2009, Drake broke out with the release of his mixtape, So Far Gone. Its' lead single, “Best I Ever Had,” was a commercial success, peaking at #2 on the Billboard Hot 100 charts.\n\n“Climb the ladder” refers to Drake’s journey to the top of the rap industry—he started at the bottom of the ladder in 2009 but fast forward to today, he’s reached the top. “Envy” sounds like “NV,” the abbreviation for Nevada—not only taking shots at haters but also creating a double on words.\n\nThis also calls back to Kendrick Lamar’s bar on his April 2024 diss track, “Not Like Us”:\n\nSince 2009, I’ve had this bitch jumping\n\nTherefore, Drake could also be calling out Kendrick’s jealousy and how it never impeded his rise to the top.", "annotation_id": "39775993", "url": "https://genius.com/39775993", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "I got all the chains that they ever repped in Virginia I got niggas’ prized possessions, I get possessive on niggas", "vault_lines": ["I got all the chains that they ever repped in Virginia", "I got niggas' prized possessions, I get possessive on niggas"], "meaning": "Sneak diss to Pharrell Williams, who’s jewelry was bought by Drake, who is saying he has Pharrell’s prized possessions.\n\nhttps://www.vanityfair.com/style/2023/01/drake-bought-pharrell-williams-nerd-chaing-jewelry-auction-jumbotron-shit-poppin-music-video\n\n“I got all the chains that they ever repped in Virginia” could reference Chains & Whips by Clipse, a Virginia duo, consisting of Pusha T and Malice. Clipse are known for their longstanding partnership with Pharrell Williams. Drake repeats the word “chains” while specifically mentioning “Virginia,” connecting the line to the title and artists of the song.\n\nThe inclusion of Virginia can also be a subtle diss at Pusha T, who’s wife’s name is Virgina.", "annotation_id": "39775987", "url": "https://genius.com/39775987", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "And, Khaled, you know what I mean The beef was fully live, you went halal and got on your deen And your people are still waitin’ for a free Palestine But apparently everything isn’t black and white and red and green, damn I’m seein’ everyone’s true colors, for real, I’m sensin’ a theme", "vault_lines": ["And, Khaled, you know what I mean", "The beef was fully live, you went halal and got on your deen", "And your people are still waitin' for a free Palestine", "But apparently everything isn't black and white and red and green, damn", "I'm seein' everyone's true colors, for real, I'm sensin' a theme"], "meaning": "Drake takes a shot at DJ Khaled, who preferred to stay uninvolved in Drake’s 2024 feud with Kendrick Lamar. Playing on “beef,” Drake uses Islamic terminology such as halal, meaning foods and practices permissible under Islamic law, and deen, meaning faith, to suggest Khaled stayed clean and restrained during the feud.\n\nDJ Khaled was born to Muslim Palestinian immigrant parents. Despite this, he’s faced massive criticism due to his silence regarding the conflicts in Gaza. Drake adds to the backlash, for Khaled is yet to make a statement calling to “Free Palestine,” which has become the main slogan of the Palestinian cause.  Notably, there is also a clip of a streamer calling to Khaled to say “Free Palestine” on camera, in where he dodges the request and replies with “Peace and love to everybody.”\n\nDrake suggests this situation isn’t “black and white,” adding red and green to complete the colors of the Palestinian flag and contrast simplicity with complexity.", "annotation_id": "39775953", "url": "https://genius.com/39775953", "state": "accepted", "votes": 197, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "And I faced the way they paint me, but it hurts just like the Philly Eagles", "vault_lines": ["And I faced the way they paint me, but it hurts just like the Philly Eagles"], "meaning": "Drake compares his ability to take hits from the public and industry and stay strong to Philadelphia Eagles quarterback Jalen Hurts’s ability to do the same. While playing his sport, Hurts characteristically wears large amounts of eye black, or “face paint,” that covers both his cheeks:\n\n\n\nHurts on the field before a game against the Washington Commanders on Sunday, Oct. 1, 2023, in Philadelphia.\n\nHurts’s performance is often criticized, like other dual-threat quarterbacks, that his propensity to run as well as pass makes him not a true “quarterback.” Additionally, Hurts is often ranked by pundits below quarterbacks who have accomplished less in the NFL than him, despite consistently solid play and well-performing Eagles teams. Similarly, Drake’s ability to make a diverse set of music, including R&B and pop songs, as well as controversies that have come up throughout his career, brings him ridicule about not being a true “rapper.”\n\nDespite the criticisms that both performers face, they are both at the top of their game. Hurts signed a 5 year/$255 million contract in April 2023, has made it to two Super Bowls, winning one of them, as well as taking home the MVP of the game. Drake boasts similar accomplishments, with numerous #1 hits in recent history, with his most recent solo album charting #1 on the Billboard 200 as well.", "annotation_id": "39775912", "url": "https://genius.com/39775912", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Fuck a big three anyway, there was too many chefs in the kitchen, it was a mess to begin with, yeah And now they got a new GOAT and we gotta test the position", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, fuck the chatter", "And now they got a new GOAT and we gotta test the position"], "meaning": "The “Big Three” was a title the hip-hop culture celebrated for the better part of a decade crowning Drake, Kendrick Lamar and J. Cole as the reigning trio in hip-hop.\n\nCole first planted the flag on October 2023’s “First Person Shooter”:\n\nIs it K-Dot? Is it Aubrey? Or me?\nWe the big three like we started a league\n\nKendrick wasted no time dismantling it on March 2024’s “Like That”:\n\nMotherfuck the big three, nigga, it’s just big me \n\nWith Cole withdrawing from the beef and Kendrick standing on the other side, the “big three” concept became more of a liability than a legacy for Drake that he felt he has to dissolve. Now, he reframes that arrangement was never as clean as the culture made it seem.\n\nThe “new GOAT” refers to Kendrick. Crowned as one by outlets like Complex, Drake challenges him to uphold the standard that the “GOAT” title comes with.", "annotation_id": "39775863", "url": "https://genius.com/39775863", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "line": "Don’t be surprised, you see the whole YM in CA just like The Village People", "vault_lines": ["Don't be surprised, you see the whole YM in CA just like The Village People"], "meaning": "The Village People is the band that created the song YMCA", "annotation_id": "39775852", "url": "https://genius.com/39775852", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make Them Pay", "status": "done", "targets": 33, "clicked": 33, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-make-them-pay-lyrics", "genius_title": "Make Them Pay"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "I can’t forget the looks on they faces", "vault_lines": ["I can't forget the looks on they faces"], "meaning": "Drake likes burgers", "annotation_id": "39817606", "url": "https://genius.com/39817606", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "And fairy tales are saved for the bedtime stories I tell you now", "vault_lines": ["And fairy tales are saved for the bedtime stories I tell you now"], "meaning": "In Drakes early music, he spoke about love from the perspective of a hopeless romantic who wanted a rom-com movie type of love. Gaining the reputation of saving strippers and pursuing relationships with unavailable women.\nIn Deceiving, 2009 he said “Mr Edward’s, I tried to save your daughter but she wasn’t satisfied with none of the things that I bought her”. As Drake has gotten older and wiser he’s become less engulfed by the idea of a romantic love as he rapped on CLB/The Remorse, 2021 “Can’t picture being a hubby finger too stubby to fit a ring on, unless Kawhi wanna run it back. Other than that the strings will be unattached.”\nHere he is alluding to that fairytale type of love being unrealistic and saving fictional stories for the ones he tells Adonis before going to sleep.", "annotation_id": "29881105", "url": "https://genius.com/29881105", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "This the first positive DNA we ever celebrated", "vault_lines": ["This the first positive DNA we ever celebrated"], "meaning": "In terms of a crime scene, if DNA from a suspect is found positive, the suspect will usually be found guilty. However, Drake refers to a paternity test used to confirm that Adonis is legitimately his son after many false accusations of fatherhood. This test has been referenced previously on Scorpion through a sample used in “Final Fantasy”.\n\nThis line could also be alluding to Drake’s “30 for 30 Freestyle” in which he raps about his DNA being tested before:\n\nPaternity testing with women I never slept with\nI’m legally obligated if they request it", "annotation_id": "14900663", "url": "https://genius.com/14900663", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "You haven’t met your grandfather yet, that nigga a trip He probably could’a did stand-up Yeah, but at the same time, he’s a stand up And that’s how you gon’ be when it’s time to man up", "vault_lines": ["But man, you know", "You haven't met your grandfather yet, that nigga a trip", "He probably could'a did stand-up", "Yeah, but at the same time, he's a stand up", "And that's how you gon' be when it's time to man up"], "meaning": "Drake talks about his father, Dennis Graham who is of course, Drake’s son, Adonis' paternal grandfather. He has mentioned his father in many songs and even appears on the cover of More Life.\n\n\n\nDrake has said in the past that his love of music came from his father, spending time with him in Memphis, Tennessee away from his hometown in Toronto as his parents divorced during his childhood.\n\nDrake’s father is a musician and a performer, as such, he could have done stand-up comedy. “Stand up” also serves as a double entendre that can be used to describe not only stand-up comedy but also a stand-up person—a person who is well respected and reliable.", "annotation_id": "14896965", "url": "https://genius.com/14896965", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "But this Champagne toast is short-lived I got an empty crib in my empty crib", "vault_lines": ["But this Champagne toast is short-lived", "I got an empty crib in my empty crib"], "meaning": "Drake reminisces about his celebration of the birth of his first child – a champagne toast – which quickly turns into disappointment upon realizes that he and the mother will be living separately as the child is in her custody.\n\nDrake uses wordplay with “empty crib” to symbolize how his baby’s bed will be physically empty and that his own residence will be vacated of celebrations and father-son moments as a result.", "annotation_id": "14884050", "url": "https://genius.com/14884050", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "Sandi used to tell me all it takes is one time, and all it took was one time Shit, we only met two times, two times And both times were nothin’ like the new times", "vault_lines": ["Sandi used to tell me all it takes is one time, and all it took was one time", "Shit, we only met two times, two times", "And both times were nothin' like the new times"], "meaning": "Sandi is Drake’s mother, whom he looks up to and respects highly. He dedicated “You & The 6” to her and has talked about his adoration and loyalty to her in several of his songs, most recently on God’s Plan\":\n\nShe said, “Do you love me?” I tell her, “Only partly”\nI only love my bed and my momma, I’m sorry\n\nMothers often warn their children about unwanted pregnancies and how quickly it can happen. In this situation, it seems as though his mother was right, now that his son is in the picture. It’s also very likely that they did only meet two times since it has been rumored that Drake was beginning his Boy Meets World tour when Adonis was supposedly conceived in late January 2017.\n\nThere is also a reference to a character named Jimmy Two Times from Martin Scorsese’s Mob epic Goodfellas, a character who is known to repeat words twice.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aXUgLqsdZvI", "annotation_id": "14879966", "url": "https://genius.com/14879966", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "Now it’s rough times, I’m out here on front lines Just tryin’ to make sure that I see him sometimes", "vault_lines": ["Just tryin' to make sure that I see him sometimes", "Now it's rough times, I'm out here on front lines"], "meaning": "Drake is one of the most popular musical figures of the past decade, boasting streaming records and a vast variety of individual accolades as well as a large and loyal fanbase.\n\n\n\nHowever, with this fame comes the media keeping him under the microscope. Since Drake is such a popular and in-demand person, he has many commitments such as tours and appearances at award shows and such. He mentions this on his recent Lil' Baby collaboration “Yes Indeed”:\n\nBooked for three years, man you can’t even book me\n\nThis makes it difficult for Drake to raise a son since he’s always on the road. Although, he is trying to support his son the best he can and see him when he can.", "annotation_id": "14879650", "url": "https://genius.com/14879650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "Hopefully by the time you hear this Me and your mother will have come around Instead of always cuttin’ each other down", "vault_lines": ["Hopefully by the time you hear this", "Me and your mother will have come around", "Instead of always cuttin' each other down"], "meaning": "At the time of the release of “March 14”, Drake was not on good terms with the mother, Sophie Brussaux, regarding his newborn child, Adonis. It had been speculated that she and Drake hooked up while he was on tour in Rotterdam, Amsterdam and consequently conceived Adonis. When she claimed that he was the father of her child, he denied the allegations despite Brussaux claiming “she had the text messages to prove it.”\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/Ba9YR2Tjuyx/?hl=en&taken-by=sophieknowsbetter\n\nDrake is saying that he wants all the fighting between him and Sophie to cease for the sake of their son. It’s also worth noting that according to a report from TMZ, it seems that Drake is doing his best to make amends by financially supporting Sophie from before Adonis' birth.", "annotation_id": "14878495", "url": "https://genius.com/14878495", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "Got the news in Miami that now we all got ones that we raisin’ Tell Gelo bring some Mod Rosé and the Baccarats out For our cheers to the next generation", "vault_lines": ["Got the news in Miami that now we all got ones that we raisin'", "Tell Gelo bring some Mod Rosé and the Baccarats out", "For our cheers to the next generation"], "meaning": "Drake references OVO crew member Angelo “Gelo” Ferraro, who became a business partner and restaurant associate after serving as the rapper’s favorite waiter at Toronto’s Sotto Sotto Trattoria.\n\nTo celebrate the ‘next generation’ of children that he and his partners are now “raisin'”, Drake requests Mod Rosé. Rosé is a light pink wine which comes in a variety of sweetness levels. He also asks for Baccarat crystal wine glasses. This could also be a reference to  the revealed mother of Drake’s child, Sophie Brussaux, an artist and former porn star whose stage name was Rosee Divine (pronounced Rosé).\n\nDrake has previously referenced celebratory toasts on songs such as his 2017 More Life cut,  “Free Smoke”. He also called upon Gelo in the past on iLoveMakonnen’s “Tuesday”:\n\nTell Gelo, “Bring the juice, we about to get lit”\nFill the room up with some tings, one night off and this is it", "annotation_id": "14878312", "url": "https://genius.com/14878312", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "I only met you one time, introduced you to Saint Nick I think he must’ve brought you like twenty gifts", "vault_lines": ["I only met you one time, introduced you to Saint Nick", "I think he must've brought you like twenty gifts"], "meaning": "In June 2018, TMZ reported that Drake flew out Sophie Brussaux, his baby’s mother, and his son Adonis to be with him for Christmas. On Christmas Eve, Brussaux posted a photo boarding a private jet. In these lines, Drake confirms the rumor, stating that he had someone dress up as Saint Nick (more commonly known as Santa Claus) to present Adonis with gifts.", "annotation_id": "14876833", "url": "https://genius.com/14876833", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "I’m changing from boy to a man No one to guide me, I’m all alone No one to cry on I need shelter from the rain, to ease the pain Of changing from boy to a man No one to guide me, I’m all alone No one to cry on I need shelter from the rain, to ease the pain Of changing from boy to a man I’m all alone No one to cry on I need shelter from the rain, to ease the pain Of changing from boy to a man Here we go again", "vault_lines": ["I'm changing from boy to a man", "No one to guide me, I'm all alone", "No one to cry on", "I need shelter from the rain, to ease the pain", "Of changing from boy to a man", "Here we go again"], "meaning": "After finally responding to Pusha-T’s claims of an unexpected child with Sophie Brussaux, an artist and former porn star, on “The Story of Adidon,” Drake feels rather exposed and vulnerable. Due to the fact that he sits at the top of an industry, he feels very isolated and alone in his struggles and emotions. On “God’s Plan,” he reminds listeners that he is a regular person who experiences the ups and downs of life:\n\nI feel good, sometimes I don’t, ayy, don’t\n\nThe mood of this outro also supports Drake’s astrological sign, Scorpio, which is known to be particularly emotional.\n\nHe interpolates Boyz II Men’s “Khalil Interlude”.\n\nEspecially since the release of his Nothing Was the Same, Drake has frequently referred to himself as “The Boy”, so much so that he named his 2017 tour The Boy Meets World. This outro seems to imply that with the birth of his son, Drake is accepting maturity and literally developing into a responsible man and father figure, making the interpolation symbolic.", "annotation_id": "14876645", "url": "https://genius.com/14876645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 193, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "It’s breakin’ my spirit Single father, I hate when I hear it I used to challenge my parents on every album Now I’m embarrassed to tell ’em I ended up as a co-parent Always promised the family unit I wanted it to be different because I’ve been through it But this is the harsh truth now", "vault_lines": ["It's breakin' my spirit", "Single father, I hate when I hear it", "I used to challenge my parents on every album", "Now I'm embarrassed to tell 'em I ended up as a co-parent", "Always promised the family unit", "I wanted it to be different because I've been through it", "But this is the harsh truth now"], "meaning": "These lines echo as a response to the accusations made by Pusha T against Drake on his diss track “The Story of Adidon”:\n\nYour father walked away at five—hell of a dad thing\nMarriage is somethin' that Sandi never had, Drake\nHow you a winner but she keep comin' in last place\n…\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\nDeadbeat mothafucka playin' border patrol\n\nDrake is acknowledging here that based on his previous experiences of living in a single-parent household, he has tried to avoid this occurring for any of his future children although it ended up happening.\n\nDespite his openness with his parents in the past on tracks like “Look What You’ve Done” and “From Time” he’s unable to tell them about this. Some of the common themes of Scorpion include being emotionally exhausted by beef, backstabbing peers, celebrity life, meaningless sex, and constantly being picked at by the internet. “March 14” dives deeper into his psyche with the emotional realization that he, in fact, is the father of Adonis, and Drizzy is now going through a similar situation that his parents were once in.", "annotation_id": "14876598", "url": "https://genius.com/14876598", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "October baby for irony’s sake, of course I got this 11 tatted for somebody, now it’s yours", "vault_lines": ["October baby for irony's sake, of course", "I got this 11 tatted for somebody, now it's yours"], "meaning": "Drake confirms his child’s date of birth–October 11, 2017–which is ironically in the same month as his. Although his son shares the same birth month with him, Adonis is a Libra and doesn’t share the Scorpio astrology sign with his father which is thought to be the inspiration for the album title. Adding on the irony, Drake’s brand and label name is October’s Very Own which is commonly shortened to OVO.\n\nThe 11 that’s tatted for “somebody” is believed to be for Jorja Smith who was born on June 11th. Her debut November 2016 EP is titled “Project 11”. Since Drake and Jorja broke up, he dedicates the 11 to his son born on October 11th.", "annotation_id": "14876568", "url": "https://genius.com/14876568", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "March 14", "line": "Yesterday morning was crazy I had to come to terms with the fact that it’s not a maybe That shit is in stone, sealed and signed She not my lover like ”Billie Jean”, but the kid is mine", "vault_lines": ["Yesterday morning was crazy", "I had to come to terms with the fact that it's not a maybe", "That shit is in stone, sealed and signed", "She not my lover like \"Billie Jean\", but the kid is mine"], "meaning": "During Drake and Pusha T’s feud in mid 2018, Push released a song called “The Story of Adidon” where he alluded to Drake having a son with former pornstar, Sophie Brussaux:\n\nSophie knows better as your baby mother\nCleaned her up for IG, but the stench is on her\nA baby’s involved, it’s deeper than rap\nWe talkin' character, let me keep with the facts\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\nDeadbeat mothafucka playin' border patrol, ooh\nAdonis is your son\n\nIt wasn’t a baseless claim. TMZ published an article establishing the rumor a year prior in May 2017:\n\nA woman who was hanging out with Drake after his split with Jennifer Lopez claims she’s pregnant with his baby and she says she has text messages to prove it. She claims she’s 3 ½ months pregnant and pins down conception to either January 20 or 21.\n\nInterestingly, Drake started off his 2017 song, “Sacrifices” with a line that established one of the two dates that Brussaux claimed when he impregnated her:\n\nWrote this shit, January 21\n\nAdditionally, Drake uses lines from two popular songs: Stevie Wonder’s 1970 song “Signed, Sealed, Delivered,” where he sings:\n\nI am, signed, sealed, delivered, I’m yours\n\nAnd Michael Jackson’s 1983 hit “Billie Jean,” a song about groupies pretending to be MJ’s lovers:\n\nBillie Jean is not my lover\nShe’s just a girl who claims that I am the one\nBut the kid is not my son\nShe says I am the one, but the kid is not my son\n\nThis plays into the aforementioned TMZ article because Drake originally dismissed the claims.", "annotation_id": "14876191", "url": "https://genius.com/14876191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
