{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Two cars in the back, two in the front, you know what I’m sayin’?", "vault_lines": ["You know what I'm sayin', yeah", "Two cars in the back, two in the front, you know what I'm sayin'?"], "meaning": "On the surface, Drake appears to be boasting about having two cars parked in the front driveway and two in the back. However, the line may also reference the formation of a security convoy. High-profile individuals are often transported between multiple escort vehicles, with two cars leading and two cars following for protection while traveling through populated areas.", "annotation_id": "39967660", "url": "https://genius.com/39967660", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "They so down for me, gotta punt, you understand?", "vault_lines": ["They so down for me, gotta punt, you understand?"], "meaning": "In American Football, a team has four downs per possession. Most of the time if a team gets to fourth down, they will punt the ball. Drake is saying “they so down for/four me, gotta punt” as a double entendre that his team are so down for him and will ride with him through whatever. And they are so “down 4” (fourth down) that the ball must be punted.", "annotation_id": "39967653", "url": "https://genius.com/39967653", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Two cars in the back, two in the front, you know what I’m sayin’?", "vault_lines": ["You know what I'm sayin', yeah", "Two cars in the back, two in the front, you know what I'm sayin'?"], "meaning": "On the surface, Drake appears to be boasting about having two cars parked in the front driveway and two in the back. However, the line may also reference the formation of a security convoy. High-profile individuals are often transported between multiple escort vehicles, with two cars leading and two cars following for protection while traveling through populated areas.", "annotation_id": "39942746", "url": "https://genius.com/39942746", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Know what I’m sayin’?", "vault_lines": ["You know what I'm sayin', yeah", "Outside is back to what it was, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Atlanta'll tell you 'bout my run, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Ask Pluto, Bank, or 21, you know what I'm sayin'?", "I done put up half, what's done is done, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Two cars in the back, two in the front, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Can't even tell you what day it is, I'm on so many pills, I don't know how I made it here (Yeah)", "Fuck boy, rrt, know what I'm sayin'? Ski", "Stood on that business, girl, you know what I'm sayin'? Yeah", "Atlanta nigga, twin, know what I'm sayin'? Yeah"], "meaning": "The repetitive use of the phrase “know what I’m sayin?” is a dual reference.\n\nIt pays homage to Three Six Mafia’s “Whatcha know”.\n\nThis is a direct callback to Talk Up, where Drake repetitively used the phrase. Talk Up was co-produced by DJ Paul, a member of Three Six Mafia. Talk Up also features Jay-Z, who has been the target of several high-tension bars in “Whisper my Name”, “Make them pay,” and “Janice STFU.”", "annotation_id": "39936554", "url": "https://genius.com/39936554", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Niggas standin’ ten toes on quicksand", "vault_lines": ["Niggas standin' ten toes on quicksand"], "meaning": "Drake, especially during the beef with Kendrick Lamar, dealt with hatred from many people, including other artists which banded against him and even people he used to call friends.\n\nDrake calls out those same people who switched up during the beef and says they’re standing ten toes on quicksand.\n\nTo stand ten toes means to be committed fully to something – in this case, those people who went against Drake are standing ten toes on that same idea of hating him.\n\nWhat they don’t realize is that they’re standing on quicksand – meaning that idea which they stubbornly defend will be their downfall", "annotation_id": "39927575", "url": "https://genius.com/39927575", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "They so down for me, gotta punt, you understand?", "vault_lines": ["They so down for me, gotta punt, you understand?"], "meaning": "In American Football, a team has four downs per possession. Most of the time if a team gets to fourth down, they will punt the ball. Drake is saying “they so down for/four me, gotta punt” as a double entendre that his team are so down for him and will ride with him through whatever. And they are so “down 4” (fourth down) that the ball must be punted.", "annotation_id": "39853912", "url": "https://genius.com/39853912", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Atlanta’ll tell you ’bout my run, you know what I’m sayin’? Ask Pluto, Bank, or 21, you know what I’m sayin’?", "vault_lines": ["You know what I'm sayin', yeah", "Atlanta'll tell you 'bout my run, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Ask Pluto, Bank, or 21, you know what I'm sayin'?"], "meaning": "Here, Drake references his influence and “run” across states, highlighting his incredible discography and how he is able to transcend genre-bound stereotypes of certain albums and create multifaceted music— RNB, trap, pop, the list goes on.\n\nTo further back this, he mentions his praise from various other artists such as Bank, or Big Bank, a Atlanta figure from Edgewood in Zone 6 East Atlanta and creator of Duct Tape Entertainment, Pluto, one of the nicknames for Future, and 21, an abbreviation of 21 savage, another well-known friend and collaborator of Drake. All of them are culturally influenced by Atlanta, a place of major rap influence.", "annotation_id": "39850987", "url": "https://genius.com/39850987", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Bring all the freaks to France and skrrt in the Sprinter, they wanna play Molly Santana (They do)", "vault_lines": ["Bring all the freaks to France and skrrt in the Sprinter, they wanna play Molly Santana (They do)"], "meaning": "Molly reacted to this bar in a Tweet:\n\nhttps://x.com/mollysantana00/status/2055351632150294840", "annotation_id": "39847705", "url": "https://genius.com/39847705", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Atlanta’ll tell you ’bout my run, you know what I’m sayin’?", "vault_lines": ["You know what I'm sayin', yeah", "Atlanta'll tell you 'bout my run, you know what I'm sayin'?"], "meaning": "This is a response to Kendrick Lamar’s single Not Like Us, specifically when he says:\n\nYou run to Atlanta when you need a few dollars\nNo you not a colleague, you a fucking colonizer\n\nIn the verse above, Kendrick claims that Drake visits Atlanta and collaborates with artists from Atlanta with the intention to make more money and increase his street credibility, which is the reason why people online call him a culture vulture. Drake responds by saying that he had a phenomenal run in Atlanta, and they can back him up on this.\n\nIn the upcoming verse, he also mentions Future (also known as Pluto), Big Bank Black (founder of DTE) and 21 Savage as part of the people that can back him up on this.", "annotation_id": "39843770", "url": "https://genius.com/39843770", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Got a three-ticket Richie on my hand (Okay)", "vault_lines": ["Got a three-ticket Richie on my hand (Okay)"], "meaning": "“Ticket” in money slang refers to a sum of $1 million. For his 35th birthday Drake treated himself to a Richard Mille RM 056 Sapphire Split-Seconds Chronograph Tourbillon, one of only five ever made, valued at $5.5 million.", "annotation_id": "39839329", "url": "https://genius.com/39839329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "I just fucked Chanelly up on some light shit (What else?)", "vault_lines": ["I just fucked a milli' off on some fine shit (What else?)", "I just ran a milli' up on some slime shit (What else?)", "I just fucked they feelings up on some pride shit (What else?)", "I just fucked Chanelly up on some light shit (What else?)"], "meaning": "Molly borrows Oregon rapper Yeat’s signature playful twist of adding suffixes like “-y” and “-ly” to regular words, applying it on the designer brand “Chanel”.\n\nThis is not the first time we see the twist happen to the brand’s name, as Yeat has a song named the same way, released on 2021.\n\nYeah, we in Chanelly, double C (Double C)\nAnd the bitch want Gucci, double G (Double G)\n\nEver since the release of that track, several artists have used “Chanelly” in their bars as well.", "annotation_id": "39823373", "url": "https://genius.com/39823373", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "All you pussy boys losin’ it and you need to take meds, hey", "vault_lines": ["All you pussy boys losin' it and you need to take meds, hey"], "meaning": "Drake may be referencing Ye here. Although the two publicly reconciled in December 2021 by appearing together at the “Free Larry Hoover” concert, tensions between them resurfaced in later years.\n\nIn early 2025, Ye went on a series of erratic social media outbursts directed at multiple artists, including Drake. His posts alternated between hostility and admiration: at times insulting Drake with homophobic slurs, while elsewhere praising him and even saying Drake should speak at his funeral. The contradictory nature of the posts led many fans to interpret them as evidence of Ye’s ongoing fixation and conflicted feelings toward Drake.\n\nOn January 26, 2026, Ye later issued a public apology through a full-page ad in The Wall Street Journal, where he attributed much of his erratic behavior to Bipolar Type-1 disorder and said he was committed to treatment after “los[ing] touch with reality.”", "annotation_id": "39797673", "url": "https://genius.com/39797673", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "When I tell you dip ’cause it’s Ice time, bitch, it ain’t the fake feds, hey", "vault_lines": ["When I tell you dip 'cause it's Ice time, bitch, it ain't the fake feds, hey"], "meaning": "Drake is telling everyone to clear out because it’s ICEMAN – his new alter ego– time. But ICE also stands for U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, the federal agency that has been at the center of some of the most Controversial immigration enforcement practices in recent American history.\n\nICE has long been a target of criticism has been subject to widespread scrutiny for having a lower caliber of training and expertise relative to other federal law enforcement agencies.", "annotation_id": "39793913", "url": "https://genius.com/39793913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Used to cook Hannah Montan’", "vault_lines": ["Used to cook Hannah Montan'"], "meaning": "Refers to cocaine. Rappers often use the names of white celebrities as humorous euphemisms for cocaine due to its “white” color.\n\nAlso a reference to Molly Santana who compares herself to Hannah Montana in the next verse.", "annotation_id": "39789460", "url": "https://genius.com/39789460", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Embarrassment the only thing I’m gettin’ secondhand (Hahaha)", "vault_lines": ["Embarrassment the only thing I'm gettin' secondhand (Hahaha)"], "meaning": "Drake uses a double entendre to flex his wealth while sonning his competition.\n\nOn one level, he’s bragging that he doesn’t buy anything used or “secondhand\"—everything he owns is custom, deadstock, or retail.\n\nOn a deeper level, he’s talking about vicarious embarrassment. He’s suggesting that his opps are acting so “cringe” or desperate that the only thing he feels when he looks at them is embarrassment on their behalf.", "annotation_id": "39787681", "url": "https://genius.com/39787681", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "We sharin’ women that I already fucked at the height of my success, ayy", "vault_lines": ["We sharin' women that I already fucked at the height of my success, ayy"], "meaning": "Lyric used as a diss for A$AP Rocky referencing his current partner Rihanna", "annotation_id": "39779606", "url": "https://genius.com/39779606", "state": "pending", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "She said, “Astaghfirullah,” because I’m back with Taliban (Uh-huh)", "vault_lines": ["She said, \"Astaghfirullah,\" because I'm back with Taliban (Uh-huh)"], "meaning": "“Astaghfirullah” (استغفر الله) is a common Arabic phrase that translates to “I seek forgiveness in God.” It’s frequently used by Muslims as an exclamation of shock, disapproval, or repentance when witnessing something sinful or morally wrong.\n\nThe Taliban is a militant organization that currently rules Afghanistan. Drake, however, uses the name to refer to Future, a self-identified member of the “Freeband Taliban.” Drake also used the nickname on his September 2015 hit collaboration with Future, “Jumpman”:\n\nHalloween\nTaliban, Taliban", "annotation_id": "39779044", "url": "https://genius.com/39779044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Ask Pluto, Bank, or 21, you know what I’m sayin’?", "vault_lines": ["You know what I'm sayin', yeah", "Ask Pluto, Bank, or 21, you know what I'm sayin'?"], "meaning": "Reference to Big Bank, a legendary Atlanta street figure from Edgewood in Zone 6 East Atlanta and founder of Duct Tape Entertainment. He is also the host of the wildly popular new podcast ‘Perspektives’ with Bank and one third of the ‘Big Facts’ podcast with DJ Scream and Baby Jade.", "annotation_id": "39778889", "url": "https://genius.com/39778889", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
