{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "I went Laurie Lynn Stark on my pants", "vault_lines": ["I went Laurie Lynn Stark on my pants"], "meaning": "Drake references Laurie Lynn Stark, the co-owner and creative force behind the luxury label Chrome Hearts. Alongside Richard Stark, she played a major role in shaping the brand’s distinctive aesthetic, which is known for its leatherwork, silver jewellery, and heavy use of cross motifs.\n\nWhen he says he “went Laurie Lynn Stark” on his pants, he’s basically implying that he’s wearing heavily customised or high-end Chrome Hearts pieces. Rather than mentioning the brand itself, Drake references one of its founders. It’s an insider fashion flex that shows he’s familiar with luxury culture beyond just recognising the big-name brands.", "annotation_id": "39777914", "url": "https://genius.com/39777914", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Molly Santana new Hannah Montana Molly Santana new Hannah Montana (Yeah) Molly Santana new Hannah Montana Molly Santana new Hannah Montana", "vault_lines": ["Molly Santana new Hannah Montana"], "meaning": "Hannah Montana was a popular Disney Channel teen sitcom that aired for four seasons from March 24, 2006, to January 16, 2011. The series follows Miley Stewart—played by Miley Cyrus—who is an average teenager that secretly leads a double life as the world-famous pop star, Hannah Montana.\n\n At the time of this song’s release, Molly Santana started to experience a major mainstream breakout, slowly building up her underground fanbase after releasing her 2024 self-titled debut and her 2025 album Molly and Her Week of Wonders. She also went viral a few days prior for bringing out North West during her Rolling Loud set. By comparing herself to Hannah Montana, she nods to her crossing over from an underground cult hero to a superstar pop-culture figure.", "annotation_id": "39777838", "url": "https://genius.com/39777838", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "I went St. John the Baptist on the Lam’ (Uh-huh)", "vault_lines": ["I went St. John the Baptist on the Lam' (Uh-huh)"], "meaning": "St. John the Baptist was a major religious figure in the 1st century AD, revered as a prophet in Christianity, Islam, the Bahá'í Faith, and Mandaeism. He is best known in biblical history as the predecessor and messenger who prepared the way for Jesus Christ. He was executed by beheadment after he openly criticized King Herod Antipas for unlawfully marrying his brother’s wife, Herodias—whose daughter, Salome—asked for the head of John on a platter.\n\n“Lamb” is short for a Lamborghini. Drake uses wordplay by comparing John’s beheadment by taking the “head” off or top off his Lamb, making it into a convertible. It should also be noted that a lamb is a significant symbol used throughout the Bible. In the Bible, the lamb symbolizes innocence, sacrifice, and Jesus Christ himself (“the Lamb of God”), so pairing it with the John the Baptist reference adds another layer of religious symbolism to the bar.", "annotation_id": "39776818", "url": "https://genius.com/39776818", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "Me and Hendrix back by popular demand (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Me and Hendrix back by popular demand (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Hendrix is Future’s nickname. Future was one of the central figures that instigated the 2024 beef, executive producing both We Don’t Trust You and We Still Don’t Trust You with Metro Boomin, the projects that launched Kendrick’s opening shot and alot diss tracks aimed at Drake.\n\nYet of all the opponents and former allies Drake fell out with during the beef, Future appears to be the only one he’s genuinely made peace with. This aligns with his bar on “Family Matters” where he felt that Future was roped into the beef by Metro:\n\nPluto shit make me sick to my stomach, we ain’t never really been through it\nLeland Wayne, he a fuckin' lame, so I know he had to be an influence\n\nThis marks their first collaboration since the beef. The two have built one of the most celebrated duo catalogs in modern hip-hop, from What a Time to Be Alive to “Life Is Good,” so  their reunion is definitely deserving of high demand.", "annotation_id": "39776736", "url": "https://genius.com/39776736", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ran To Atlanta", "line": "You know what I’m sayin’, yeah Freebandz turn shit up again", "vault_lines": ["You know what I'm sayin', yeah", "Freebandz turn shit up again", "Atlanta nigga, twin, know what I'm sayin'? Yeah"], "meaning": "Freebandz is a record label based in Atlanta, Georgia. It was founded by Future after he signed a major-label deal with Epic Records in September 2011.\n\nNotably, Drake’s and Future’s friendship started the same month when they collaborated on “Tony Montana” and have gone to collab on numerous occasions since.", "annotation_id": "39776654", "url": "https://genius.com/39776654", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
