{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "line": "See where we from, the 50's are bloody And the 100 dollar bill is dirty brown, they love me", "vault_lines": ["See where we from, the 50's are bloody", "And the 100 dollar bill is dirty brown, they love me"], "meaning": "In Drake and Saukrates’s hometown of Toronto, Ontario, Canada the currency is CAD —$50 bills are red and $100 bills are brown. This is also where they receive the most support.", "annotation_id": "1135939", "url": "https://genius.com/1135939", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "line": "Tryin’ to prove rappers are intelligent black men", "vault_lines": ["Tryin' to prove rappers are intelligent black men"], "meaning": "Drake is trying to fight the stereotypes of hip hop present in society and promote a more conscious and intelligent sound along the lines of artists like Talib Kweli, and Sage Francis.", "annotation_id": "491130", "url": "https://genius.com/491130", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "line": "Nowadays I serve backhands with backspin", "vault_lines": ["Nowadays I serve backhands with backspin"], "meaning": "Tying in the pimping allusion from previous lines, Drake creates a double entendre with “backhand.” Pimps often discipline their workers with back handed slaps to the face. Backhand swings are used in tennis and table tennis, often creating a backspin on the ball.\n\nThe wordplay is simply a declaration of Drizzy’s prolific rap skills.", "annotation_id": "491109", "url": "https://genius.com/491109", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "line": "My city full of players they just needed a captain In need of him right now in need of him back then But back then I was actin’", "vault_lines": ["My city full of players they just needed a captain", "In need of him right now in need of him back then", "But back then I was actin'"], "meaning": "Before Drake the rap scene in Toronto was unable to crossover into the larger American hip-hop scene (apart from a few exceptions). With artists such as K'naan and Kardinal Offishall making noise locally, the city was seemingly just on the cusp of breaking out.\n\nInsert Drake, a guy who believes he can lead his city into international fame. He postures that he might have done so sooner if he wasn’t playing the role of Jimmy on the hit teen sitcom Degrassi: The Next Generation.", "annotation_id": "491107", "url": "https://genius.com/491107", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "line": "And yeah we get our dumb on like San Frisco Bay With 40 and Mac Dre", "vault_lines": ["And yeah we get our dumb on like San Frisco Bay", "With 40 and Mac Dre", "And keep on, on, on, on, on"], "meaning": "“Going dumb” is slang used in the popular hyphy movement—which originated in the Bay Area—meaning dance or rap with a lot of energy and swagger. The Bay Area includes San Francisco and Oakland.\n\nTwo rappers known for cultivating the Bay Area hip-hop scene are E-40 and Mac Dre.", "annotation_id": "491091", "url": "https://genius.com/491091", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "line": "But the allure of this year’s Scream Tour has been cuttin’ all them Jawns that be hurtin’ the game", "vault_lines": ["But the allure of this year's Scream Tour has been cuttin' all them Jawns that be hurtin' the game"], "meaning": "Scream Tour is a concert series, started in the early 2000s, that brings hot teenage acts together for summer performances across the nation. In it’s early years it featured the likes of Bow Wow, B2K, Chris Brown, Ciara, and Trey Songz.\n\nThe success of the tours often boosted artists into new realm of success. The prospect of being on this tour and gaining more fame entices Drake to consider more mainstream music, even if it’s detrimental to the culture.", "annotation_id": "490916", "url": "https://genius.com/490916", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "line": "They tellin’ me Trey sound It’s too mainstream if I plan on me stickin’ around", "vault_lines": ["It's too mainstream if I plan on me stickin' around", "I keep on, on, on, on, on", "They tellin' me Trey sound"], "meaning": "After dropping So Far Gone and establishing himself as a force in hip-hop, Drake became the center of a bidding war between labels trying to sign him.\n\nWhile meeting with executives in Atlanta he was told that his rapping-singing combo, something his friend and collaborator Trey Songz was trying to do at the time, wasn’t a style that could last.", "annotation_id": "490902", "url": "https://genius.com/490902", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "line": "If this was you, I wonder what you’d do in that position Try to make a profit or try to make a difference Try to make ’em play your shit or try to make ’em listen", "vault_lines": ["If this was you, I wonder what you'd do in that position", "Try to make a profit or try to make a difference", "Try to make 'em play your shit or try to make 'em listen"], "meaning": "Drake here discusses the common issue of selling out in the music industry—artists struggle to strike a balance between covering meaningful topics and garnering mass appeal.", "annotation_id": "490856", "url": "https://genius.com/490856", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "line": "Hip hop’s newest and youngest acquisition", "vault_lines": ["Hip hop's newest and youngest acquisition"], "meaning": "This song was released just months after Drake’s breakout mixtape So Far Gone was released in 2009. At the time he was just 23 years old and causing a lot of buzz.", "annotation_id": "490849", "url": "https://genius.com/490849", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Search", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 9, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-the-search-lyrics", "genius_title": "The Search"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Up", "line": "In the ‘rari doing donuts like my name was J Dilla", "vault_lines": ["In the ‘rari doing donuts like my name was J Dilla"], "meaning": "Bun’s going around and around in circles, showing off his new car and making a donut shape\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gTRTiOZji6g\n\nAlso a reference to producer J Dilla whose last album was a collection of beats called Donuts, released on his 32nd birthday, 3 days before he died", "annotation_id": "2061489", "url": "https://genius.com/2061489", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Up", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 1, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-what-up-lyrics", "genius_title": "What Up"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "AM 2 PM", "line": "Purple drink", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Codeine/promethazine cough syrup mixed in with some sprite. serve it up in a white styrafoam cup with some ice. Popular in the Southern Hip-Hop community. It’s an illegal beverage however, and has no alcohol. Apparently a favorite of Drake’s..", "annotation_id": "286995", "url": "https://genius.com/286995", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "AM 2 PM", "line": "To the tenth degree, Tennekey, T-Dot to the country y’all Orange Mound and Whitehaven, and back up north to Montreal", "vault_lines": ["To the tenth degree, Tennekey, T-Dot to the country y'all", "Orange Mound and Whitehaven, and back up north to Montreal"], "meaning": "Shout out to Toronto, Canada – Drake’s hometown.\n\n“Orange Mound” and “Whitehaven” are two historically black neighborhoods in Memphis", "annotation_id": "151286", "url": "https://genius.com/151286", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "AM 2 PM", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 2, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-am-2-pm-lyrics", "genius_title": "AM 2 PM"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "Thank you, thanks, Drake, thanks baby Motherfuckers drunk and tired Tired, I’m tired, boss", "vault_lines": ["Thank you, thanks, Drake, thanks baby", "Motherfuckers drunk and tired", "Tired, I'm tired, boss"], "meaning": "This sample comes from an Instagram post by DJ Eric, resident DJ of Drake’s favorite Houston strip club V-Live.", "annotation_id": "9087785", "url": "https://genius.com/9087785", "state": "accepted", "votes": 101, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "Pack a bag, you gon’ end up staying where I’m at Took you from the hood and I could never give you back", "vault_lines": ["Pack a bag, you gon' end up staying where I'm at", "Took you from the hood and I could never give you back"], "meaning": "Drizzy confesses that he wants this girl by his side rather than in the the hood where she grew up. This contradicts what he said in the first verse:\n\nDon’t make me give you back to the hood\nDon’t make me give you back\n\nHe could be talking about the same girl from the “Jungle”:\n\nTrust me girl, this shit is everything to me\nShe from the jungle, she from the jungle\nI take somebody else’s car, drive there undercover\nThis shit is everything to me, this shit is everything\n\n“Jungle” is a slang term for Lawrence Heights, a neighborhood in Toronto known for recurrent gang violence.", "annotation_id": "9077477", "url": "https://genius.com/9077477", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "You wildin’, you super childish, you go to CVS for Kotex In my Bugatti, I took the key and tried to hide it So you can’t drive it, and put on mileage Then you find it, awkward silence", "vault_lines": ["You wildin', you super childish, you go to CVS for Kotex", "In my Bugatti, I took the key and tried to hide it", "So you can't drive it, and put on mileage", "Then you find it, awkward silence"], "meaning": "CVS is a popular chain of American pharmacies, while Kotex is brand of pads/tampons. The woman involved took Drizzy’s most expensive car to get pads for herself, seemingly pissing him off.\n\n\n\nHe hid the keys so she couldn’t do it again, and upon her finding them and realizing he hid them from her, things probably only got worse between them.", "annotation_id": "9076276", "url": "https://genius.com/9076276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 124, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "Whippin’ on this shit, I’m getting ready, where you at?", "vault_lines": ["Whippin' on this shit, I'm getting ready, where you at?"], "meaning": "While Drake hid his Bugatti keys from his girl in the first verse, it seems now he’s getting ready to pick her up and take her out. He realizes he’s in love with her.\n\nThis line mirrors the first lines of “Feel No Ways”:\n\nI should be downtown, whipping on the way to you\nYou got something that belongs to me", "annotation_id": "9074676", "url": "https://genius.com/9074676", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "You don’t wanna fall in love, I know I’m not someone you should trust, I know", "vault_lines": ["You don't wanna fall in love, I know", "I'm not someone you should trust, I know", "I just wanna let you know that someone love you back"], "meaning": "Drake’s had trust issues before. In August 2015, TMZ reported Serena Williams' friends had warned her that Drake isn’t someone she should trust.\n\nFortunately for Drake’s sake, Young Metro would disagree.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/699696503674302465", "annotation_id": "9073940", "url": "https://genius.com/9073940", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "Breaking news, my niggas If your girlfriend has went to any Season opener basketball game Best believe, she’s fucking some niggas on that team She’s fucking somebody on the basketball team If she’s at the game", "vault_lines": ["Breaking news, my niggas", "If your girlfriend has went to any", "Season opener basketball game", "Best believe, she's fucking some niggas on that team", "She's fucking somebody on the basketball team", "If she's at the game"], "meaning": "This is a soundbite from DJ Eric, resident DJ at Drake’s favorite Houston strip club, V-Live:\n\nhttps://streamable.com/ai73\n\nNBA players are given three complimentary tickets for home games to give to friends and family. Otherwise, season opener tickets are too expensive for the average person to purchase. Eric assumes that women with good seats at these games had to have sex with a player to obtain the tickets.\n\nThis connects into the body of the song, where Drake reminds his significant other that he’s funding her luxury lifestyle and has the power to put her back in poverty:\n\nDon’t make me give you back to the hood\nDon’t make me give you back", "annotation_id": "9072723", "url": "https://genius.com/9072723", "state": "accepted", "votes": 266, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "My past checkered like the Louis you just got", "vault_lines": ["My past checkered like the Louis you just got"], "meaning": "Drake is talking about his rough past probably with other women and other relationships. Thus his past has something in common with Louis Vuitton bags—they are both “checkered.” By comparing his past to “the Louis you just got,”  Drake is reminding this girl to consider what’s more important – getting hung up on his past, or enjoying the benefits of being involved with a platinum rapper?", "annotation_id": "9072487", "url": "https://genius.com/9072487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "How many girls have slept in this bed? Say a different number than the one that’s in my head Lying to protect you, I be doing that a lot", "vault_lines": ["How many girls have slept in this bed?", "Say a different number than the one that's in my head", "Lying to protect you, I be doing that a lot"], "meaning": "Drake’s girl questions his character, but he still lies to her. While in his eyes a white lie is better than revealing the truth, she might not feel the same way.\n\nThis dichotomy was explored on “Redemption,” where Drake raps:\n\nSay to you\nBut 80 percent of it was the truth, yeah\n\nDrake has sung frequently about sleeping with multiple women, and the challenges that arise when one of them wants to be exclusive, dedicating an entire song to this topic on 2011’s “Hate Sleeping Alone.”", "annotation_id": "9072368", "url": "https://genius.com/9072368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "I give Chanel out like a hug, I know I knew a couple of your friends way before", "vault_lines": ["I give Chanel out like a hug, I know", "I knew a couple of your friends way before"], "meaning": "Chanel is a luxury French fashion house famous for their hand bags. Drake gifts so many handbags, he feels like it’s as common as hugging someone.\n\nDrake’s face every time he gives away a $4,500 Chanel bag.", "annotation_id": "9072308", "url": "https://genius.com/9072308", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "Yeah, rode that dick like a soldier She rode it like a soldier She rode it like a jungle soldier She rode it like a, yeah, yeah I got it, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, rode that dick like a soldier", "She rode it like a soldier", "She rode it like a jungle soldier", "She rode it like a, yeah, yeah", "She rode that dick like a soldier", "She rode it like a Melpomene soldier", "Rode that dick like a soldier"], "meaning": "Drake’s in house producer 40 samples one of New Orleans' greatest bounce hits, Ha-Sizzle’s “Rode Dat Dick Like A Soldier.”\n\nhttps://youtu.be/wHpQOhxltXE\n\nThe term Melpomene comes from the New Orleans housing project known as the Guste Apartments, one of the few projects to survive hurricane Katrina in 2005. Several of the apartments were destroyed for not meeting the guidelines for the New Orleans Housing Authority in 2004.", "annotation_id": "9070721", "url": "https://genius.com/9070721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "Why you gotta fight with me at Cheesecake? You know I love to go there", "vault_lines": ["Why you gotta fight with me at Cheesecake?", "You know I love to go there"], "meaning": "“Cheesecake” is a reference to the popular chain restaurant, The Cheesecake Factory.\n\nWhen this song was produced, there was no Cheesecake Factory in Toronto, Drake’s hometown. However, one was recently announced and coming to Toronto in the fall of 2017. He may have been referencing the branch in Calabasas, where Drake’s second home is located, or the branch near Disneyland in Anaheim, hence the Disneyland reference in lines to follow.\n\nHe also may have been referencing the branch at the Houston Galleria. In 2013, he signed a menu for two fans that were eating there:\n\n\n\nDrake has been spotted at The Cheesecake Factory on more than one occasion. In fact, Genius wrote an article on it.\n\nThis reference to The Cheesecake Factory became an Internet sensation – a fact Drake would eventually pay homage to in his official music video for the song.", "annotation_id": "9070705", "url": "https://genius.com/9070705", "state": "accepted", "votes": 154, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "My city love me like a college running back", "vault_lines": ["My city love me like a college running back"], "meaning": "Drake compares himself to a college running back. College athletes, especially football players, often become popular in their state/city.\n\nAlthough Drake dropped out of high school and never went to college, Toronto still loves him.\n\n\n\nFormer Alabama star running back, Trent Richardson.", "annotation_id": "9070311", "url": "https://genius.com/9070311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "I say bounce that shit like woah Yeah, bounce that shit like woah This is not a fairytale, I already know how you like it Take you to the mall and get you a new outfit Girl, that’s just some child’s play Bounce that shit like woah, woah, woah Woah, woah, heard all of the stories about you I already know and I like it Take you to the mall and get you a new outfit Girl, that’s just some child’s play Bounce that shit like woah, yeah Bounce that shit like woah", "vault_lines": ["This is not a fairytale, I already know how you like it", "Take you to the mall and get you a new outfit", "Girl, that's just some child's play", "Bounce that shit like woah, woah, woah", "Woah, woah, heard all of the stories about you", "I already know and I like it", "Bounce that shit like woah, yeah", "Bounce that shit like woah", "She rode it like a, yeah, yeah", "I say bounce that shit like woah", "Yeah, bounce that shit like woah"], "meaning": "Buying a woman a new outfit is nothing to Drake. With a net worth of over 70 million dollars, he can probably buy her the entire mall. It is considered child’s play to him, or extremely easy to do.\n\nHere, he acknowledges that the woman he is seeing is after him for his money and status (“this is not a fairytale, I know how you like it,” “heard all of the stories about you”), and he’s saying that’s no big thing. He likes that she has expensive taste, and likes being able to indulge her. As long as she keeps fulfilling him sexually (bouncing that shit on him), he sees no trouble in rewarding her with the material things she likes.", "annotation_id": "9070280", "url": "https://genius.com/9070280", "state": "accepted", "votes": 231, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "I just wanna let you know that someone love you back All the Cadillac like I’m Teddy Pendergrass", "vault_lines": ["You don't wanna fall in love, I know", "I'm not someone you should trust, I know", "I just wanna let you know that someone love you back", "All the Cadillac like I'm Teddy Pendergrass"], "meaning": "Teddy Pendergrass was a R&B & soul singer in the 1970s and 1980s. Before he started his singing career, he was the drummer for the band The Cadillacs.\n\nTeddy Pendergrass has a song titled “When Somebody Loves You Back.”", "annotation_id": "9070260", "url": "https://genius.com/9070260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "This a place for families that drive Camrys and go to Disney They don’t need to know all of our business", "vault_lines": ["This a place for families that drive Camrys and go to Disney", "They don't need to know all of our business"], "meaning": "Vacationing at Disneyland and driving Toyota Camrys are both clichés which evoke the suburban, white-picket-fence, 2.5 children lifestyle of the average Cheesecake Factory patron. Yelling and screaming in areas where young kids congregate is frowned upon.", "annotation_id": "9070174", "url": "https://genius.com/9070174", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "Married in our twenties, now, where the fun in that?", "vault_lines": ["Married in our twenties, now, where the fun in that?"], "meaning": "At the time this song was released, Drake was 29, and spent the majority of his twenties as an eligible bachelor. It seems that was by choice.\n\nIronically, on “Miss Me,” Drake made this claim:\n\nI love Nicki Minaj, I told her I’d admit it\nI hope one day we get married just to say we fucking did it\nAnd girl I’m fucking serious, I’m with it if you with it", "annotation_id": "9070123", "url": "https://genius.com/9070123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "Mama is a saint, yes, she raised me real good All because of her I don’t do you like I should Don’t make me give you back to the hood Don’t make me give you back", "vault_lines": ["Mama is a saint, yes, she raised me real good", "All because of her I don't do you like I should", "Don't make me give you back to the hood", "Don't make me give you back"], "meaning": "He’s tempted to drop the girl, leave her flat, and he thinks she deserves it for the way she’s been acting. He only doesn’t because his mother raised him to feel a sense of responsibility.\n\nThese lyrics come off as classist and misogynstic, and Drake has been called out for his problematic portrayal of women in the past. Still, Drake insists that he has a great respect for women which he attributes to his mother’s influence.\n\n\n\nIt’s hard to imagine his momma would approve of these bars though, as she probably didn’t raise him to issue passive-aggressive ultimatums to his significant other, saying he’s responsible for elevating her from her hood origins into the high life and he can take that life away from her if she doesn’t clean up her act.", "annotation_id": "9070089", "url": "https://genius.com/9070089", "state": "accepted", "votes": 189, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "line": "Say I’m actin’ lightskin, I can’t take you nowhere", "vault_lines": ["Say I'm actin' lightskin, I can't take you nowhere"], "meaning": "Drake has a white mother, and thus has lighter skin than the average black man. In the black community, “acting light skin” means you’re acting stuck up or arrogant, like you’re better than others—especially darker skin people. This colorism, especially in North America, finds its roots in the days of slavery wherein light skin slaves were treated better than dark skin slaves because of their assumed white ancestry, and were given less menial jobs such as work in the house rather than in the fields.\n\nActing “lightskinned” can also be typified by acting affectedly polished, emotional, or “soft”—traits which Drake, with his collection of dad sweaters, has been known to embody from time to time. In fact, Drake in his ubiquity has become something of a reference point for the light-skin stereotype.\n\n\n\nThe girl in question believes that Drake is acting “lightskin” due to his frustration at her for picking fights with him in public.", "annotation_id": "9070048", "url": "https://genius.com/9070048", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Child’s Play", "status": "done", "targets": 18, "clicked": 18, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-childs-play-lyrics", "genius_title": "Child’s Play"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "I never did the VMAs, I’m not in need of praise", "vault_lines": ["I never did the VMAs, I'm not in need of praise"], "meaning": "The MTV Video Music Awards (VMAs) is a major award show by MTV that honor achievements across multiple categories in the music video industry, including Best Hip-Hop video, Best Collaboration, and Artist of the Year.\n\n\n\nMTV VMAs 2024 highlights\n\n\n\n\nDespite being nominated seven times, Cole has never won. This bar highlights his attitude of not needing industry accolades to feel accomplished.", "annotation_id": "35041945", "url": "https://genius.com/35041945", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "At the top playin’ keep-away with the crown", "vault_lines": ["At the top playin' keep-away with the crown"], "meaning": "Refers to the informal children’s ball game keep-away. J. Cole is saying he and Drake are way at the top of the rap game, just the two of them, fighting over rap’s crown by themselves.", "annotation_id": "31674541", "url": "https://genius.com/31674541", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Shit is 360 like the label deal you signed to get your people paid", "vault_lines": ["Shit is 360 like the label deal you signed to get your people paid"], "meaning": "A 360 is an exclusive contract between a record label and a band or artist in which the record company not only takes a share of the artist’s music sales, but also percentages of their revenue from other ventures outside of what the label is directly involved with.\n\nSo, not just record and digital music sales, but also things like merchandise and concerts — which have traditionally been the main way artists actually earn money. But labels don’t stop there. They also want a piece of your songwriting and publishing revenues, licensing and placement royalties, and pretty much any other kind of income stream they can think of.                                 (https://www.hypebot.com/hypebot/2023/06/what-is-a-360-record-deal.html)", "annotation_id": "30922470", "url": "https://genius.com/30922470", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "AKA me and Drizzy Drake, we the wave", "vault_lines": ["AKA me and Drizzy Drake, we the wave", "Uh, yeah, we the wave"], "meaning": "This bar is in connection with the previous one, where Cole says “…when they see their faves”, meaning that people refer to Drake and him as their “faves”, and is an implicit name two refer to the duo.", "annotation_id": "30922421", "url": "https://genius.com/30922421", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Scary Hours undefeated, y’all should be afraid Yeah, y’all should be afraid", "vault_lines": ["Scary Hours undefeated, y'all should be afraid", "Yeah, y'all should be afraid"], "meaning": "J. Cole is saying how Drake has never missed on a Scary Hours project.\n\nNot either Scary hours with the songs “Diplomatic” and the mega hit “Gods plan” or Scary hours 2 with the songs “What’s next”, “Wants and needs” and “Lemon pepper freestyle”\n\n“Y’all should be afraid” is some wordplay used by J. Cole. The line prior is talking about the scary hours series. And when something is scary, people get afraid.", "annotation_id": "30131963", "url": "https://genius.com/30131963", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Hard to blame ’em, Lord knows this game can be depraved", "vault_lines": ["Hard to blame 'em, Lord knows this game can be depraved"], "meaning": "Cole doesn’t fault Drake for being jaded when the rap game as a whole is the corrupted one.\n\nAlso a Cole reference to Drake’s song, Lord Knows, on Take Care. Similar to Drake referencing Cole’s album, Born Sinner, in the few lines within the song.", "annotation_id": "30124173", "url": "https://genius.com/30124173", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Young angel goin’ through his demon phase", "vault_lines": ["Young angel goin' through his demon phase"], "meaning": "Drake has been in the rap game for far too long that he’s bound to be jaded by it.\n\nAlso Cole referencing the recent news of now former Braves coach Eric Young going to the Angels.\n\nhttps://www.si.com/mlb/braves/news/breaking-eric-young-sr-to-leave-braves-and-join-ron-washington-with-angels", "annotation_id": "30124156", "url": "https://genius.com/30124156", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Time is speedin’ now, I see the greys pokin’ out this beard, but it’s weird ’cause I feel like I ain’t even age (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Time tends to feel like it moves faster as you get older, and grey hairs are a visible sign of that aging. Despite this, Cole says he still feels young both physically and emotionally.\n\nThe line also reflects his confidence as a rapper: despite getting older, he believes his lyrical ability hasn’t declined and remains just as sharp as when he first entered the game. Over a decade into his career, Cole is still at the top of rap’s hierarchy, showing that age hasn’t dulled his skills or relevance.", "annotation_id": "30113176", "url": "https://genius.com/30113176", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "I conquered hell, I walked the ’Ville and set my feet ablaze", "vault_lines": ["I conquered hell, I walkеd the 'Ville and set my feet ablazе"], "meaning": "By saying he “conquered hell,” Cole speaks to his journey out of hardship. “Hell” here likely represents the struggles he faced growing up in Fayetteville, North Carolina—a place he often describes as rough. Conquering it suggests he not only survived but thrived despite those challenges.\n\nCole setting his feet ablaze and walking back through the ‘Ville symbolizes shedding his old self through fire—allowing him to be reborn as a respected rapper. Fire often represents both destruction and transformation, and in this case, it marks his evolution from struggle to success. He walked through the flames of adversity and emerged stronger, a testament to where he came from and how it shaped him.\n\nThis could also be an ode to Method Man’s bars on Protect Ya Neck (The Jump Off):\n\nPut on my gasoline boots and walk through Hell", "annotation_id": "30113148", "url": "https://genius.com/30113148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Me, I got tickets like a meter maid", "vault_lines": ["Me, I got tickets like a meter maid"], "meaning": "A meter maid is a parking enforcement officer who issues tickets for parking violations. Drake plays on the word, stating that he has tickets himself, meaning a large sum of money, specifically millions of dollars which can be referred to as “mill tickets.”", "annotation_id": "30112395", "url": "https://genius.com/30112395", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Beefin’ with a block that’s five hundred feet away Wheel of Fortune shit the way they say they need a K", "vault_lines": ["Beefin' with a block that's five hundred feet away", "Wheel of Fortune shit the way they say they need a K"], "meaning": "“Wheel of Fortune” is a game show where contestants guess letters to complete phrases. Drake plays on the fact that people on the show often say, “I’d like to buy a K,” when guessing letters. But in this context, “K” is also  slang for an AK-47,  meaning his people in Toronto are saying they “need a K” in a much more serious way, likely to resolve street conflict with a rival block that’s only 500 feet away.", "annotation_id": "30112352", "url": "https://genius.com/30112352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Coke got they nose bleedin’ like the seats where you can’t see the stage High up in arenas where they see their faves", "vault_lines": ["Coke got they nose bleedin' like the seats where you can't see the stage", "High up in arenas where they see their faves"], "meaning": "Cole packs multiple layers of wordplay into this bar. He links cocaine use, which can cause nosebleeds, with “nosebleed seats,” the cheap spots high up in arenas where fans are so far away they can barely see the stage. He then flips “high” itself into a double entendre, those fans are physically high up in the venue, just like drug users are high from cocaine.", "annotation_id": "30111997", "url": "https://genius.com/30111997", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Diss me and just may see us on your block like the street parade", "vault_lines": ["Diss me and just may see us on your block like the street parade"], "meaning": "Drake uses wordplay with “may see” sounding like Macy’s, referencing the Macy’s Thanksgiving Day Parade, a famous New York City event featuring large floats and marching bands. He warns his opps that dissing him might lead to seeing him and his boys on their block with the same overwhelming numbers and intensity as a parade marching down the street.\n\nMacy’s Thanksgiving Parade", "annotation_id": "30111965", "url": "https://genius.com/30111965", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "I’m with Drizzy in Atlanta, so many hitters with that nigga, they could be the Braves", "vault_lines": ["I'm with Drizzy in Atlanta, so many hitters with that nigga, they could be the Braves"], "meaning": "This bar is a double entendre. Cole calls Drake’s security staff “hitters”, which is slang for a killer.\n\nHe then says they could be the Braves, referencing the 2023 Atlanta Braves, who had one of the best offensive seasons ever, with hitters in the lineup from 1 to 9.", "annotation_id": "30111056", "url": "https://genius.com/30111056", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Next time I get in ROSALÍA face I hope she tell her people that we need some space", "vault_lines": ["Next time I get in ROSALÍA face", "I hope she tell her people that we need some space"], "meaning": "Drake and ROSALÍA were seated next to each other during the Nike Fashion Week Presentation in 2020. They appear to be within close proximity to each other, and so Drake is saying next time they meet they will need more space to converse and flirt more comfortably.", "annotation_id": "30105543", "url": "https://genius.com/30105543", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Bought this nigga jewels, these shits is light, let’s get it reappraised", "vault_lines": ["Bought this nigga jewels, these shits is light, let's get it reappraised"], "meaning": "Drake bought Pusha T’s boss Pharrell’s jewelry and is now saying they weren’t as heavy with diamonds or worth as much as claimed.", "annotation_id": "30105520", "url": "https://genius.com/30105520", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Nah, uh, I only need a raise And a safe to stash these Frito Lays", "vault_lines": ["Nah, uh, I only need a raise", "And a safe to stash these Frito Lays"], "meaning": "“Frito Lays” is a play on words; referring to the popular brand of chips but also doubling as a slang reference to money (“chips”). J. Cole is basically saying he only needs a raise and a secure spot to stash his earnings.", "annotation_id": "30105306", "url": "https://genius.com/30105306", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "And 21 my nigga like he celebratin’ legal age", "vault_lines": ["And 21 my nigga like he celebratin' legal age"], "meaning": "6 God shouts out his frequent collaborator and Her Loss partner-in-rhyme, 21 Savage. In addition to being a commonly used ad-lib by Savage, 21 is the legal age for buying and drinking alcohol in the USA.", "annotation_id": "30105156", "url": "https://genius.com/30105156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "And broski didn’t do it, he like a piece of art, judge, he was framed", "vault_lines": ["And broski didn't do it, he like a piece of art, judge, he was framed"], "meaning": "Being “framed” or setup is the act of falsely implicating someone in a crime by issuing fabricated evidence or testimony. On the other side, art works are surrounded by a frame to protect the piece inside a border.\n\nFans speculate that this reference is about Tory Lanez whom Drake has showed public support to before. Piece of art could in that case also allude to his music discography which is a form of art.", "annotation_id": "30105096", "url": "https://genius.com/30105096", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Zopiclone baby, I can’t rest without the sleepin’ aids", "vault_lines": ["Zopiclone baby, I can't rest without the sleepin' aids"], "meaning": "Zopiclone is a non-benzodiazepine drug used to treat difficulty sleeping.", "annotation_id": "30105075", "url": "https://genius.com/30105075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Wisdom comin’ out my mouth like some teethin’ pain", "vault_lines": ["Wisdom comin' out my mouth like some teethin' pain"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the homonym “wisdom,” comparing the wisdom he shares in his raps to wisdom teeth, both of which come out of his mouth. Just as wisdom teeth cause intense discomfort when removed, Drake suggests the truths he speaks can be just as sharp and hard to swallow.", "annotation_id": "30104977", "url": "https://genius.com/30104977", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "You niggas ain’t gettin’ no bread, you in a keto phase", "vault_lines": ["You niggas ain't gettin' no bread, you in a keto phase"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the homonym “bread.” He says his opps aren’t making any bread, slang for money, comparing them to someone on a keto diet, which cuts out the food bread and other carbs entirely.", "annotation_id": "30104946", "url": "https://genius.com/30104946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "And now my paper folded like when teachers don’t want classmates to see your grade", "vault_lines": ["And now my paper folded like when teachers don't want classmates to see your grade"], "meaning": "Cole uses a witty simile, playing on the homonym “paper.” He’s saying his paper, slang for money, is folded and stacked because of the large amount he’s earned over his lucrative career. He compares this to teachers handing out test papers folded down to hide a bad score, shielding students from embarrassment and ridicule.\n\nhttps://pbs.twimg.com/media/By9uNHICMAAPbTo.jpg:large", "annotation_id": "30104715", "url": "https://genius.com/30104715", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Yeah, and we linkin’ like we freed the slaves", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and we linkin' like we freed the slaves", "Uh, yeah, we the wave"], "meaning": "The Emancipation Proclamation issued by former president Abraham Lincoln declared that “that all persons held as slaves […] are, and henceforward shall be free.”\n\nThe oronym of “Lincoln” and “linkin'” is a reference to this track being Cole and Drake’s fourth collaboration. “Linkin” could also be a reference to Lincoln cars, following Drake’s mention of a Volkswagen Beetle in the previous bar, keeping with the theme of car brands.", "annotation_id": "30101872", "url": "https://genius.com/30101872", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Drake pulled a white bitch that’s goin’ both ways, she like the queen of spades", "vault_lines": ["Drake pulled a white bitch that's goin' both ways, she like the queen of spades"], "meaning": "Cole references Drake pulling a white woman who is bisexual (“goin’ both ways”), comparing her to the Queen of Spades playing card. The card is symmetrical, showing the queen upright on both the top and bottom, mirroring the idea of “going both ways.”\n\n\n\nThe Queen of Spades also carries a cultural connotation, often used in subcultures as a symbol for white women attracted to Black men. Since Drake is black and the woman in question is white, this adds another layer to the bar.", "annotation_id": "30101653", "url": "https://genius.com/30101653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Times was hard, I watched my mama robbin’ Paul just to get Peter paid", "vault_lines": ["Times was hard, I watched my mama robbin' Paul just to get Peter paid"], "meaning": "To “rob Peter to pay Paul” is a phrase which means to take something from a person in order to cover one’s debts to somebody else. Often, the phrase describes a situation in which, after one debt is paid off, a new debt to the party who is “robbed” is incurred.", "annotation_id": "30101625", "url": "https://genius.com/30101625", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "My heart hardens every year like sneakers that Adidas made", "vault_lines": ["My heart hardens every year like sneakers that Adidas made"], "meaning": "Cole suggests he’s becoming emotionally tougher over time. At the same time, it’s a reference to NBA superstar James Harden’s Adidas sneaker line, since Harden’s name sounds like “hardens.”\n\n\n\nHarden showcasing his new collection, the Harden Vol. 4", "annotation_id": "30101395", "url": "https://genius.com/30101395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Uh, yeah, we the wave Like Christian Combs with a brush in his hand once the grease is laid", "vault_lines": ["Uh, yeah, we the wave", "Like Christian Combs with a brush in his hand once the grease is laid"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the homonym “wave.” He proclaims him and Cole are “the wave”, slang for a major trend or movement, comparing it to the waves in Christian Combs’ hair. Combs, son of Diddy, is known for his exceptionally wavy hair and is often seen brushing it to keep his 360 waves tight and defined, which requires using hair grease.\n\nThe reference goes a step further, as Christian also created his own soda flavor with Exotic Pop called, “Wave Juice.”", "annotation_id": "30101346", "url": "https://genius.com/30101346", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Volkswagen shit the way I’m runnin’ Beatles plays", "vault_lines": ["Volkswagen shit the way I'm runnin' Beatles plays"], "meaning": "Drake could be referring here to the iconic cover of The Beatles‘ 1969 album, Abbey Road, which also—and erroneously—features a Volkswagen Beetle.\n\n\n\nThe Beatles', Abbey Road artwork\n\nAlthough the appearance of this car was accidental, it gave rise to many sales of those types of cars, but at the same time generated many theories, which one of the most popular was that the license plate of the car showed a possible hint of the alleged death of Paul McCartney, ex-member of the band.\n\nWhen he refers to the fact that he’s “runnin' Beatles' plays,” he compares himself on how he has the same success as the band, achieving hits, reproductions and also breaking records, such as breaking The Beatles' 29 Top 5 songs, which they maintained for more than 55 years.", "annotation_id": "30101310", "url": "https://genius.com/30101310", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "My whip used to have the seat displays where S.M.A.C.K. DVD would play", "vault_lines": ["My whip used to have the seat displays where S.M.A.C.K. DVD would play"], "meaning": "Drake makes a shout out to the Ultimate Rap League or “URL,” formerly known as “S.M.A.C.K” rap league. During the 2000’s the company would release their video content on discs known as “SMACK DVD’s,” they would become a staple in Hip-hop and rap battle culture forever.", "annotation_id": "30101301", "url": "https://genius.com/30101301", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Man, I’m livin’ out Carlito’s way", "vault_lines": ["Man, I'm livin' out Carlito's way"], "meaning": "Carlito’s Way is a 1993 film where Carlito Brigante, a former criminal, attempts to leave his past behind but ultimately gets pulled back into it, leading to his downfall.\n\nSimilarly, Drake announced after releasing For All The Dogs on October 6, 2023, that he would take a hiatus to focus on his health. Yet, less than two months later, he dropped For All The Dogs: Scary Hours Edition, featuring this song. The line compares him to Carlito: just as Carlito couldn’t escape his past, Drake can’t step away from rap, even when he says he will.", "annotation_id": "30101255", "url": "https://genius.com/30101255", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "My brothers runnin’ through the 6ix like the Green Berets", "vault_lines": ["My brothers runnin' through the 6ix like the Green Berets"], "meaning": "Drake likens his crew in Toronto (“the 6ix”) to the Green Berets, the elite U.S. Special Forces known for for executing the military’s most sensitive and unconventional missions.", "annotation_id": "30101251", "url": "https://genius.com/30101251", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "For thinkin’ it’s a game, if it’s a game, these streets would be Bushido’s Blade", "vault_lines": ["For thinkin' it's a game, if it's a game, these streets would be Bushido's Blade"], "meaning": "Cole compares the violence of the streets to that of 1997 video game Bushido’s Blade. The Square Enix title is known for its gory, sword-based combat and (at the time) innovative 3D graphics and platforming:\n\n\n\nGameplay from Bushido’s Blade.", "annotation_id": "30101208", "url": "https://genius.com/30101208", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Oh, baby, be honest with yourself", "vault_lines": ["Yeah (Oh, baby, be honest with yourself", "Oh, baby, be honest with yourself"], "meaning": "The vocal samples appearing throughout the track were taken from The Family Circle’s “Change,” which appeared on their self-titled 1973 album.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/OfobeyDi9PQ?si=gNzJu4bx0e1y702O&t=21\n\nFeatured artist J. Cole’s sample for his 2014 hit “Wet Dreamz” was taken from “Mariya,” another track on the same album.", "annotation_id": "30101192", "url": "https://genius.com/30101192", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "Niggas think I won’t get ’em hit, but like an Indian marriage, it could be arranged", "vault_lines": ["Niggas think I won't get 'em hit, but like an Indian marriage, it could be arranged"], "meaning": "According to NPR, more than 90% of Indian people are in arranged marriages. This is due primarily to a longstanding cultural tradition of prioritizing exclusively Hindu marriages.\n\nIn the same way that said marriages are arranged, Drake has the clientele to arrange someone being “hit” or killed by goons if necessary.", "annotation_id": "30101179", "url": "https://genius.com/30101179", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "line": "All praise to the born sinners Jesus saves", "vault_lines": ["All praise to the born sinners Jesus saves"], "meaning": "Drake references Born Sinner, the 2013 sophomore album by featured artist J. Cole.\n\n\n\nThe project is coated in religious themes and imagery, which Cole himself invokes later on when he writes:\n\nYoung angel goin' through his demon phase\n\nSpeaking literally, the Christian Bible states that all people are literally born as sinners who are saved by the salvation of Jesus Christ.\n\nThe line also plays on the previous bar, where J. Cole dismisses the importance of the VMAs, saying he doesn’t seek industry validation. Drake follows up with a nod to Cole’s 2013 album title Born Sinner, reinforcing the idea that accolades don’t define Cole’s worth. At the same time, Drake’s line feels like a gesture of respect, as if he’s tipping his hat to Cole.", "annotation_id": "30101151", "url": "https://genius.com/30101151", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Evil Ways", "status": "done", "targets": 36, "clicked": 36, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-evil-ways-lyrics", "genius_title": "Evil Ways"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I was on top when that shit meant a lot Still on top like I’m scared of the drop Still on top and these niggas wanna swap", "vault_lines": ["I was on top when that shit meant a lot", "Still on top like I'm scared of the drop", "Still on top and these niggas wanna swap"], "meaning": "These lines are subliminal disses at Kendrick. Drake often used the name of Kendricks label at the time “Top Dawg Entertainment” to sub Kendrick. He says he still on “Top” & “they wanna swap”. As in, trade or spar. Swap also could be a reference to the now closed “Compton Swap Meet” a once famous flea market in Compton. Then he references Slauson & Watts in the next line, which are two famous neighborhoods in Los Angeles County, such as Compton. Drake also say states “Still on top like I’m scared of the drop”. Which was one of Drake’s pokes at Kendrick during their beef. Telling him to “drop something”, see Push Ups & Taylor Made Freestyle. Not to mention, this beat also has a West Coast feel to it, which can be another dig.", "annotation_id": "39881645", "url": "https://genius.com/39881645", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "When you niggas thinkin’ small in the mall with a rat (Rat)", "vault_lines": ["When you niggas thinkin' small in the mall with a rat (Rat)"], "meaning": "Ross is saying that his doubters and enemies aren’t even close to being on his level here. By “rat” he means someone who snitches, but this line is also a play on words with the term mallrat, used to describe someone who loiters around the mall all day. The phrase was popularized by the 1995 Kevin Smith cult classic film “Mallrats,” starring Jason Lee, Ben Affleck, and Shannen Doherty.", "annotation_id": "17523087", "url": "https://genius.com/17523087", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Bury my motherfuckin’ Chase Bank, time to bounce (Bounce)", "vault_lines": ["Bury my motherfuckin' Chase Bank, time to bounce (Bounce)"], "meaning": "Making a play on the track’s theme of burying Drake and Rick Ross with their money to take it to the afterlife, Ross expands the metaphor, claiming that he wants his whole bank account to be buried with him. Chase Bank is a national bank that deals with credit cards, mortgages, retirement planning and much more.", "annotation_id": "17396611", "url": "https://genius.com/17396611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Rickey Smiley’s in Decatur with the yé Lil’ nigga, just another state case", "vault_lines": ["Rickey Smiley's in Decatur with the yé", "Lil' nigga, just another state case"], "meaning": "Ricky Smiley is a stand-up comedian and actor, who’s known for his prank phone calls. Rick Ross has featured on Smiley’s TV programme The Rickey Smiley Morning Show, and when Ross was hospitalized in March 2018, there was a segment in the Hip Hop Spot category of the show that addressed rumours of how he faced the health scare.\n\nDecatur is a city in Georgia; in January 2017, Ross opened a new Wingstop in the city. The store was the company’s 1000th branch opened and Ross has often mentioned his love for Wingstop; for example, in “The Devil is a Lie”, he rapped:\n\nWingstop, fat boy need a 10 piece\n\n“Yé” is a slang term meaning cocaine—Ross is talking about small-time jokers (Ricky Smileys) selling coke in Decatur who are about to be state cases because of the foolish moves they make.", "annotation_id": "17396506", "url": "https://genius.com/17396506", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I really gotta put a couple niggas in they place Really just lapped every nigga in the race", "vault_lines": ["I really gotta put a couple niggas in they place", "Really just lapped every nigga in the race"], "meaning": "This could be a dig at former rappers that Drizzy’s had beef with, most recently being Pusha T.\n\nDrake’s compared the rap game to a race before on “Sacrifices”:\n\nIt’s a marathon, not a sprint, but I still gotta win the race, yeah\n\nBy saying he lapped other rappers in the race that is the rap game, Drake is indicating that what is new for other rappers, he has already accomplished, as he is that far ahead.", "annotation_id": "17396448", "url": "https://genius.com/17396448", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I really might tat “Realest Nigga” on my face", "vault_lines": ["I really might tat \"Realest Nigga\" on my face"], "meaning": "Over the years, Drake has faced criticism for being fake, especially when he was accused of having a ghostwriter in the form of Quentin Miller, who threatened to expose him. The allegations were first made by Philadelphia rapper Meek Mill which sparked the two’s now dead beef. Funkmaster Flex even contributed to the scandal, airing a whole series of “reference tracks” that Drake later used. However, Drake defended himself:\n\nMusic can be a collaborative process. I need, sometimes, individuals to spark an idea so that I can take off running. […] I’m not ashamed.\n\nThat said, Drake here says that he’s the most real rapper in the scene, and this self-given title could be made even more credible with his honesty in the ghostwriting scandal.", "annotation_id": "17396418", "url": "https://genius.com/17396418", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Rich niggas and I’m really bein’ modest", "vault_lines": ["Rich niggas and I'm really bein' modest"], "meaning": "As of 2019, Rick Ross' net worth was estimated to be around $35 million. He’s made a lot of this money from his label Maybach Music Group, as well as making classic hits like “The Devil is a Lie” and “Stay Scheming”. However, Ross ironically claims that he’s actually being modest, and that he has a lot more to boast about.", "annotation_id": "17396401", "url": "https://genius.com/17396401", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "The homies quiet, but we all smoke the loudest (Loudest)", "vault_lines": ["The homies quiet, but we all smoke the loudest (Loudest)"], "meaning": "Here, Rick Ross juxtaposes the slang term of “smoking loud”, which means smoking high-quality marijuana, with his homies keeping their cool.", "annotation_id": "17396391", "url": "https://genius.com/17396391", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got two phones, one need a charge", "vault_lines": ["I got two phones, one need a charge"], "meaning": "Drake references Kevin Gates’s November 2015 song “2 Phones”:\n\nI got two phones, one for the plug and one for the load\nI got two phones, one for the bitches and one for the dough\nThink I need two more, line bumpin’ I’m ring, ring, ringin’\n\nThe line also references Drake’s July 2015 track, “Charged Up,” which features a iPhone charging screen on it’s cover art. On the diss track aimed at Meek Mill, Drake rapped:\n\nI’m charged up\nOh no we eatin', niggas ain’t about to starve us\nNah, niggas ain’t about to starve us\nI’m charged up\n\nDrake often juggles work and his personal relationships, and has a phone for each part of his life. Just like a phone in need of a charge, when Drake focuses too much of his time on one aspect of his life, the other suffers.", "annotation_id": "17396360", "url": "https://genius.com/17396360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Thought my raps wasn’t facts ’til they sat with the bars", "vault_lines": ["Thought my raps wasn't facts 'til they sat with the bars"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to Meek Mill’s July 2015 Twitter rant in which he infamously accused Drake of working with a ghostwriter and not writing his own raps. He quickly responded with two diss tracks against Meek, “Charged Up” and “Back to Back,” the latter of which received a Grammy nomination. Drizzy is widely considered to have won the unexpected beef with Meek and re-established himself as a true lyricist despite the accusations.\n\nIn late 2018, Drake and Meek officially ended their beef. However, the ghostwriting accusations were still a topic of concern for others, such as Pusha-T, who dissed him on his May 2018 track, “Infrared”:\n\nThe game’s fucked up\nNiggas' beats is bangin', nigga, ya hooks did it\nThe lyric pennin' equal the Trumps winnin'\nThe bigger question is how the Russians did it\nIt was written like Nas, but it came from Quentin\n\nOn the same day as the song’s release, Drake replied with “Duppy Freestyle”:\n\nWhoever supposedly makin' me hits\nBut then got no hits sound like they need me\nMy hooks did it, my lyrics did it\nMy spirit did it, I’m fearless with it, yeah\n\nOthers came to the defense of Drake’s writing ability, such as Boi-1da, who, in an April 2018 interview, said:\n\nI think Drake is literally one of the greatest songwriters of all time, and he writes his own songs. And for someone to try and discredit somebody who I’ve literally seen write songs like he’s writing an essay, it bothers me because I’m close to it. I know he writes his own songs.", "annotation_id": "17396345", "url": "https://genius.com/17396345", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Thinkin’ back to the fact that they dead", "vault_lines": ["Thinkin' back to the fact that they dead"], "meaning": "In September 2017, OVO affiliate, Anthony Soares, was gunned down in Toronto. In honor of his late friend, Drake had a custom jewelry piece made by Seattle Gold, with the inscription “Rest In Piece Anthony Soares.”\n\n\n\nDrake shared a message on the loss of his friend through a September 2017 Instagram post:\n\nRIP to one of our family members…our brother… I still can’t even believe this morning was real. It was a honor to have shared years together and I will always keep your memory alive", "annotation_id": "17396306", "url": "https://genius.com/17396306", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Yeah, they twins, I could tell they ass apart", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, they twins, I could tell they ass apart"], "meaning": "Identical twins are two people who share a womb and originate from a single zygote that split. They typically share the same physical characteristics and are indistinguishable to most people.\n\nWhile figuring out the identity of a twin is considered difficult for some, Drake’s expertise with women allows him to accurately decide. He also boasts that he had sexual intercourse with each of the sisters, allowing him to distinguish them.\n\nDrake previously rapped about identical twins during his feature on Rihanna’s January 2016 track, “Work”:\n\nIf you had a twin, I would still choose you\n\nDrizzy also likely references the Lejonhjärta twins, Elizabeth and Victoria. They are identical twin sisters from Sweden who have been affiliated with Drake and modeled for OVO for years. They even appeared in the “Nice for What” music video.", "annotation_id": "17396287", "url": "https://genius.com/17396287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "In the next life, I’m tryna stay paid When I die, put my money in the grave", "vault_lines": ["In the next life, I'm tryna stay paid", "When I die, put my money in the grave", "Tell 'em when I die, put my money in the grave"], "meaning": "Drake revives the ancient notion that you can bring belongings with you beyond death, and claims that he wants to be buried with his money to take it to the afterlife.\n\nDrake rapped contrary lyrics on “Pop That” (which also featured Rick Ross):\n\nMoney coming, money going, ain’t like you can take it with you\n\nHe also discussed his prerogative about money on his The Weeknd assisted November 2011 track, “Crew Love”:\n\nTell them I'ma need reservations for 20\nI’ve never really been one for the preservation of money, nah\nMuch rather spend it all while I’m breathing", "annotation_id": "17396245", "url": "https://genius.com/17396245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got the hookup and there’s really no limit (Limit)", "vault_lines": ["I got the hookup and there's really no limit (Limit)"], "meaning": "Ross fires off a quick reference to the 1998 movie I Got The Hook Up, which was written by and starred Master P.\n\nI Got the Hook Up was just one contributing factor in the legendary run that Master P’s No Limit Records had in the year 1998.", "annotation_id": "17396221", "url": "https://genius.com/17396221", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "You could DM my accountant My per diem six figures and I’m countin’", "vault_lines": ["You could DM my accountant", "My per diem six figures and I'm countin'"], "meaning": "“Per Diem” (Latin for per day or for each day) refers to the amount of money an organisation gives its employees to cover their daily expenses; i.e. lodging, meals & incidental expenses.\n\n\n\nRick Ross is boasting of a wealthy lifestyle in which he spends 6 figures ($100,000 or more) daily for the previously outlined expenses.", "annotation_id": "17396183", "url": "https://genius.com/17396183", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got Lil Max with me, he the wave", "vault_lines": ["I got Lil Max with me, he the wave"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake kills two birds with one stone by simultaneously shouting out his young OVO affiliate Max and the incarcerated New York City rapper Max B, the latter of whom is known for coining the terms “wave” and “wavy” in hip-hop lexicon.\n\nMax B’s status as the originator of the term wave became a hotly debated topic after it sparked a Twitter feud between Kanye West and Wiz Khalifa and eventually led to West including Max B on West’s Life of Pablo track “Siiiiiiiiilver Surffffeeeeer Intermission.”\n\nDrake has previously shouted out Max, notably on 2018’s “Elevate”:\n\nI see the future when I get wavy (Wavy God!)", "annotation_id": "17396121", "url": "https://genius.com/17396121", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Ross got it (Maybach Music)", "vault_lines": ["Ross got it (Maybach Music)"], "meaning": "Maybach Music Group (MMG) is a record label imprint of Atlantic Records founded by featured artist Rick Ross. Originally, MMG was an imprint of Def Jam Recordings until 2011 when it moved to Warner Music Group and finally Atlantic in 2013. Alongside Ross, MMG is home to Wale and Meek Mill, the latter of which featured Drake on November 2018’s “Going Bad.”\n\nRick Ross and Drake have collaborated many times before, most notably on Ross' August 2012 track, “Diced Pineapples,” April 2012’s “Stay Schemin',” with Wale, and Drake’s November 2011 track “Lord Knows.” The duo also notably appeared alongside Lil Wayne on DJ Khaled’s May 2011 release “I’m On One,” April 2013 track “No New Friends” and French Montana’s June 2012 collaboration “Pop That.”", "annotation_id": "17396113", "url": "https://genius.com/17396113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Nine figures was the goal ’til I hit it", "vault_lines": ["Nine figures was the goal 'til I hit it"], "meaning": "Rick Ross’s net worth is currently estimated to be approximately $35 million, indicating that Ross still needs a significant amount of money in order to achieve his prior goal of $100 million.\n\nHe previously rapped about his net worth on his June 2017 track “Glory of War,” when his net worth was in the hundred thousands:\n\n8 figure nigga, tell me how those numbers sound\nHit the Bay and let Berner know that I want a pound", "annotation_id": "17396109", "url": "https://genius.com/17396109", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Niggas wanna swap like it’s Slauson or Watts", "vault_lines": ["Niggas wanna swap like it's Slauson or Watts"], "meaning": "The Slauson Super Mall, also known as the Slauson Swap Meet, is a famous shopping center located at the intersection of Slauson and Western Avenues in Los Angeles.  Drake references the “swap meet” to make a play on words as he boasts that his adversaries would like to “swap” places with him.", "annotation_id": "17396102", "url": "https://genius.com/17396102", "state": "pending", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Got the trap jumpin’ like Zion when I rebound it Then I’m out (Then I’m out)", "vault_lines": ["Got the trap jumpin' like Zion when I rebound it", "Then I'm out (Then I'm out)"], "meaning": "Zion Williamson is a former one-and-done Duke basketball star and rising basketball sensation. As a freshman in college, Zion averaged 8.9 total rebounds per game, an impressive accomplishment in collegiate athletics.\n\nHe is also considered one of the most athletic players to enter the draft in years and has a max vertical jump of approximately 40 inches, which set a new record for Duke.", "annotation_id": "17396090", "url": "https://genius.com/17396090", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Mob ties, so I’m always good where I’m at Word to Junior, Jazzy, Baby J", "vault_lines": ["Mob ties, so I'm always good where I'm at", "Word to Junior, Jazzy, Baby J"], "meaning": "Drake probably references his June 2018 track “Mob Ties,” where he severed ties with everyone that may have been disingenuous towards him. Drake implies that he’d rather stay where he is than change for someone else.\n\n“Mob” is also short for mafia: that could be a nod to Drake’s other song on the EP, “Omertà”, which takes its name from the Law of Omertà, a code of silence used by the mafia when dealing with the government and often imposed to common people, so they won’t denounce some committed crimes in order not to being persecuted or murdered.\n\nAlso, “Junior (J. Prince Jr.),” “Jazzy (Jas Prince),” and “Baby J” are the nicknames of the sons of respected Houston rap executive James Prince (aka J. Prince).  According to Vice:\n\nJas Prince discovered Drake from the days of the Toronto rapper releasing tracks on MySpace. […] Jas brought Drake not only to Lil Wayne, but also to his father. That’s why J. Prince’s name is included as executive producer for Thank Me Later & Nothing Was The Same.\n\nJ. Prince is a known defender and protector of Drake and other artists. For instance, in March 2019, after YBN Almighty Jay, whose crew YBN has been put under J. Prince’s protection as well, was beaten and robbed in New York City, J. Prince–in a lengthy Instagram post–called upon his “mob ties” to get the chain back.\n\n\n\nAccording to a subsequent Instagram post by the older Prince, he and his son Jas did ultimately get YBN Almighty Jay’s chain back:\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BvMqzZZFdGD/", "annotation_id": "17396019", "url": "https://genius.com/17396019", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got my niggas ‘cross the street livin’ large", "vault_lines": ["I got my niggas 'cross the street livin' large"], "meaning": "Drake lives on a secluded street in Hidden Hills, a small town near Calabasas, California. His neighborhood is home to a wide variety of stars and celebrities, since the houses are lavish and expensive.\n\nDrizzy previously mentioned sharing success with those who supported him on his June 2018 track, “Elevate”:\n\nSlowly, my mouth is goin' off, I don’t know patience\nI got a chance then my niggas got it too like it’s contagious\n\nMoreover, this could allude to famous rapper Kanye West, who also lives in Hidden Hills. The two were engaged in a back-and-forth rap beef in 2018, which included multiple subliminal disses and Tweets.\n\nDrake previously rapped about his close proximity on his January 2016 track, “Summer Sixteen”:\n\nNow I got a house in LA, now I got a bigger pool than Ye\nAnd look man, Ye’s pool is nice, mine’s just bigger’s what I’m saying\nI’m that nigga’s what I’m saying", "annotation_id": "17394564", "url": "https://genius.com/17394564", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I used to save hoes with a mask and a cape Now I’m like, “Nah, love, I’m good, go away”", "vault_lines": ["I used to save hoes with a mask and a cape", "Now I'm like, \"Nah, love, I'm good, go away\""], "meaning": "Drake compares himself to a superhero in these lines as vigilantes typically wear disguises to conceal their identity in comics.\n\nDrake echoes the sentiments of E-40’s Classic 1993 track “Captain Save A Hoe”:\n\nLook up in the sky it’s a bird it’s a plane\nWhat’s that nigga’s name?\nCaptain save a ho, mayne!\nMore faster than a speeding bullet\nTo put on his cape his card\n\nDrake also potentially alludes to the consistent themes of female empowerment and self love that is present throughout his discography. In April 2018, Drake released “Nice For What” which reached critical acclaim for its inspiring themes and positive depiction of women. The accompanying visuals were also praised for showing strong, independent women that were able to rise to fame and fortune on their own.\n\nIn addition, he juxtaposes the way he used to prefer the company of needy women to his newfound interest in independent females. He rapped a similar line on his 2016 track “Redemption”:\n\nWhy do I want an independent woman\nTo feel like she needs me?—I lost my way\n\nDrake also references Future’s famous 2018 text interaction with an Instagram model who was attempting to play him. The woman, Shamartess Monique Whisett, claimed that he invited her to his house for sexual intercourse but when she refused, he wouldn’t purchase plane tickets for her to return home. She called him out in texts, to which he replied:\n\nI’m good luv. Enjoy", "annotation_id": "17393851", "url": "https://genius.com/17393851", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Yeah, okay Lil CC on the beat, mmm Yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Lil CC on the beat, mmm"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out American model Cydney Christine, who serves as the main producer on the track. She confirmed her status as the primary producer after the initial release was teased.\n\nThis track marks the first collaboration between Drake and Christine, as well as the first time she has been credited as a producer.", "annotation_id": "17393717", "url": "https://genius.com/17393717", "state": "accepted", "votes": 179, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got hoes that I’m keepin’ in the dark", "vault_lines": ["I got hoes that I'm keepin' in the dark"], "meaning": "This line could be a reference to the controversy surrounding Sophie Brussaux, a pornstar that Drake secretly had a child with. Pusha T infamously made the world aware of this fact on his May 2018 diss track “The Story Of Adidon”:\n\nSince you name-dropped my fiancée\nLet ‘em know who you chose as your Beyoncé\nSophie knows better as your baby mother\nCleaned her up for IG, but the stench is on her\nA baby’s involved, it’s deeper than rap\nWe talkin’ character, let me keep with the facts\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\nDeadbeat mothafucka playin' border patrol, ooh\n\nDrake then responded to these allegations on his June 2018 track “Emotionless”:\n\nI wasn’t hidin' my kid from the world\nI was hidin' the world from my kid\nFrom empty souls who just wake up and look to debate\nUntil you starin' at your seed, you can never relate", "annotation_id": "17392949", "url": "https://genius.com/17392949", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
