{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "[Verse 2]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s flow in this verse was later interpolated by Baby Keem on track “The Hillbillies”:\n\nMessi, get them girls off the stage, ‘cause somebody’s gonna get taken\nSomebody’s gonna invade on a one-on-one conversation\n\nKeem even called the track “Baby Keem & Kendrick Lamar – The Hillbillies (Sticky Dub)” via Twitter, acknowledging the interpolation.", "annotation_id": "30627678", "url": "https://genius.com/30627678", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Back in the ocean you go, it’s a—", "vault_lines": ["Back in the ocean you go, it's a—"], "meaning": "Drake uses the age-old idiom ‘There’s plenty of fish in the sea’, meaning a single person still has lots of options out there for future partners.\nApparently, Drake isn’t happy with his current girl so he’s throwing her back into the sea.\n\nClassic Drake.", "annotation_id": "28661781", "url": "https://genius.com/28661781", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "If it’s an escort, it’s a police one", "vault_lines": ["If it's an escort, it's a police one", "It's a deep one"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t have to pay for women to be around him- if there’s any sort of escort with the rapper, it’s only law enforcement there to protect him.", "annotation_id": "27554991", "url": "https://genius.com/27554991", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Every song that I made is Ringin’ like I got engaged (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Every song that I made is", "Ringin' like I got engaged (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Drake’s never had a fiancé, but his songs are playing in the club- “ringing,” like an engagement ring.\n\nHonestly, Nevermind has been praised as a good album to listen to in the club, and on the night it was released, many nightclubs celebrated Virgil Abloh’s life by playing it.", "annotation_id": "27554972", "url": "https://genius.com/27554972", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Ayo Eric, bring them girls to the stage ’Cause somebody’s getting paid and", "vault_lines": ["Ayo Eric, bring them girls to the stage", "'Cause somebody's getting paid and"], "meaning": "Yet another reference to his friend and  DJ from Houston, Eric the car retriever. If nights like this are any indication, there is always plenty to go around indeed when the honorary resident of UGK town makes an appearance.", "annotation_id": "26456972", "url": "https://genius.com/26456972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "My brother named his ting Nadal Let’s stop all that back and forth over the ’net", "vault_lines": ["My brother named his ting Nadal", "Let's stop all that back and forth over the 'net"], "meaning": "Rafael Nadal is a tennis legend, winning 22 Grand Slam titles over the course of his ongoing career. Commonly referred to as one of the all-time greats of the sport, his achievements are world-renowned.\n\nDrake employs a double entendre in these lines, playing off of tennis’ over the net play style. The lines start with the rapper claiming his brother named his “ting,” which is often used as slang for a gun, after Rafael Nadal. He then ties the lines together, with a reference to back and forth over the net. This can be taken as comments over the internet or a tennis ball being hit from one side to another.\n\nThe Toronto native has made references to the game of tennis before, notably on his September 2021 collaboration with 21 Savage and Project Pat, “Knife Talk,” where he raps:\n\nJacob charged me four-fifty for a tennis chain\nUS Open, had it on us at the tennis game\nTell the coach don’t take me out, I like to finish games", "annotation_id": "26113398", "url": "https://genius.com/26113398", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "My mama wish I woulda went corporate She wish I woulda went exec’ I still turn to a CEO so the lifestyle she respect", "vault_lines": ["My mama wish I woulda went corporate", "She wish I woulda went exec'", "I still turn to a CEO so the lifestyle she respect"], "meaning": "Despite Drake’s principal career as a rapper, he hasn’t strayed too far from his mother’s corporate ambitions.\n\nDrake founded his own record label, OVO Sound, in 2012. His entrepreneurial endeavors, however, extend to everything from e-sports organization 100 Thieves and film production company DreamCrew.", "annotation_id": "26085537", "url": "https://genius.com/26085537", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "You know I gotta bring the G-Block", "vault_lines": ["You know I gotta bring the set", "You know I gotta bring the G-Block", "You know I gotta bring the D-Block"], "meaning": "This is a reference to “Galloway Road,” also known as G-Way or G-Block, a neighborhood in Toronto with one of Canada’s highest concentration of public and social housing units.\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake has referenced the east-end neighborhood in his music–he previously gave the hood a shoutout, along with G-Way affiliate Gilla in “No Long Talk”:\n\nIf Gilla call shots, no questions\nG-Way til I’m restin'\nBut we still got love for the West End\n\nDrake also has referenced Galloway music group Reps Up numerous times, as well as cosigning Galloway artist YTN Paco via a story post following his incarceration.", "annotation_id": "26081633", "url": "https://genius.com/26081633", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "You know I gotta bring the D-Block", "vault_lines": ["You know I gotta bring the set", "You know I gotta bring the G-Block", "You know I gotta bring the D-Block"], "meaning": "Drake references Toronto’s Danzig neighborhood, referred to as D-Block. The neighborhood aligns itself closely with Galloway, and has made the news for a number of tragic shootings and gun violence deaths.\n\n\n\nThis could also be a reference to the The LOX, a hip-hop crew from Yonkers, New York, who also go by the name D-Block. Drake previously mentioned the group on 2010’s “All of the Lights (Remix),” rapping:\n\nYoung Money, power, respect in this bitch\nYou would think we got the LOX in this muthafucka", "annotation_id": "26077277", "url": "https://genius.com/26077277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Like, we weren’t supposed to come up with something this clean Like something happened", "vault_lines": ["Like, we weren't supposed to come up with something this clean"], "meaning": "This is taken from the 2017 “Everything in Quotes” lecture conducted by Virgil Abloh at Columbia University GSAPP in which he explains how the design for the cover of Kanye West’s 2013 album Yeezus was made.\n\n\n\nPictured: Virgil Abloh during the “Everything in Quotes” lecture at Columbia GSAPP.", "annotation_id": "26077038", "url": "https://genius.com/26077038", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Gordo got me on the wave (Ayy)", "vault_lines": ["Gordo got me on the wave (Ayy)", "Ant got me on the wave (Ayy)"], "meaning": "Drake references one of the song’s producers, Carnage, who also goes by the name Gordo.\n\nAfter years of making hip-hop music, Carnage debuted “Gordo,” his new stage name and persona dedicated to crafting dance and house music, a sound which Drake implemented on Honestly, Nevermind.\n\nCarnage and Drake have been friends for many years and the duo worked closely on the album.", "annotation_id": "26076745", "url": "https://genius.com/26076745", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "They only givin’ niggas plus ones, so I never pull up to the Met", "vault_lines": ["They only givin' niggas plus ones, so I never pull up to the Met"], "meaning": "The Met Gala is an annual fundraising event for the Metropolitan Museum of Art’s Costume Institute in New York City. It is a highly regarded fashion event where many luxury designers invite celebrities to model their clothes.\n\nNotably, while many of Drake’s contemporaries have attended the prestigious event, he has never been. He explains that this is because he is only allowed to bring one guest along, thus preventing him from bringing more members of his extensive entourage.\n\n\n\nPictured: Drake’s “Fuckin' Problems” collaborator A$AP Rocky and his ex-partner Rihanna at the Met Gala in 2021.", "annotation_id": "26076738", "url": "https://genius.com/26076738", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Ayy, two sprinters to Quebec Chérie, où est mon bec?", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, two sprinters to Quebec", "Chérie, où est mon bec?"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to the Canadian province of Quebec, where French is the official language, hence why he says “Chérie, où est mon bec?,” which translates to “Honey, where’s my kiss?” in French.\n\n\n\nIt’s worth noting that the word “bec”–meaning “kiss”–is used in Québécois French, however, in the more traditional version of French from France, the equivalent term would be “bisou.”", "annotation_id": "26076737", "url": "https://genius.com/26076737", "state": "accepted", "votes": 166, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Homer hanging on my neck The bracelet matches the set", "vault_lines": ["Homer hanging on my neck", "The bracelet matches the set"], "meaning": "Drake references Frank Ocean’s independent luxury company, Homer.\n\nIn March 2022, Drake sat courtside at an NBA game wearing Homer’s 127.5-carat “Sphere Legs” diamond necklace, which has a retail price of $1,898,000, which he paired with a matching 41.23-carat “Sphere Legs” bracelet, worth $271,500.\n\n\n\nPictured: Drake wearing Homer’s “Sphere Legs” collection at the Lakers vs Raptors game on March 18, 2022.", "annotation_id": "26076728", "url": "https://genius.com/26076728", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Off-road Maybach, Pyrex trap Virgil came back through the boy, damn", "vault_lines": ["Off-road Maybach, Pyrex trap", "Virgil came back through the boy, damn"], "meaning": "Drake, referring to himself as “The Boy,” states that he is going to keep the late designer Virgil Abloh’s spirit alive after his passing.\n\nPrior to his passing, Abloh collaborated with Mercedes-Benz to create a one-of-one Mercedes-Maybach off-road electric vehicle, which Drake references here. In the song’s accompanying music video, Drake is seen sitting in the vehicle when rapping these lyrics.\n\n\n\nDrake also mentions streetwear brand Pyrex Vision, which Abloh launched in 2012. The brand which was the precursor to his high-end fashion house Off-White.\n\nIn the music video, Drake is seen posing next to Virgil’s collaboration vehicle with Mercedes:", "annotation_id": "26076706", "url": "https://genius.com/26076706", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Free Big Slime out the cage and", "vault_lines": ["Free Big Slime out the cage and"], "meaning": "Drake references his friend and frequent collaborator Young Thug, who also goes by the alias “Big Slime.”\n\nOn May 9, 2022, Thug was arrested in Atlanta, Georgia on gang-related charges along with 28 people associated with YSL.", "annotation_id": "26076635", "url": "https://genius.com/26076635", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sticky", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-sticky-lyrics", "genius_title": "Sticky"}
