{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "That’s fuckin’ crazy That’s crazy", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is still in shock from the beef of last year. He can’t believe how old  friends like Rick Ross, Future, The Weeknd and Lebron James turned on him.", "annotation_id": "35840685", "url": "https://genius.com/35840685", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "But next time, bury me in gold, my blood is Egyptian", "vault_lines": ["But next time, bury me in gold, my blood is Egyptian"], "meaning": "Bury me in gold means that if they try to “kill” or cancel Drake, that he should be burried in gold like an Egyptian king. This could mean that he will die like a king and therefore be impossible to be canceled\n\n\n\n\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: he was dating baby m at the time who is egyptian", "annotation_id": "34295675", "url": "https://genius.com/34295675", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "And we all crashin’ out together, my brothers complicit", "vault_lines": ["And we all crashin' out together, my brothers complicit"], "meaning": "Despite Drake’s reputation taking a hit after losing his feud with Kendrick Lamar, Drake hasn’t hesitated to show gratitude to those who stood by him throughout the fallout.\n\nOn “It’s Up,” Drizzy raps:\n\nI love all my friends\nThinkin' about this life I provide for them\n…\nI get knocked down, they help me rise again", "annotation_id": "34166220", "url": "https://genius.com/34166220", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "All that brother talk as if I really discovered a sibling", "vault_lines": ["All that brother talk as if I really discovered a sibling"], "meaning": "Drake critiques Bron speaking of brotherhood, suggesting their connection isn’t genuine.\n\nLater in the song Drake raps,\n\n“My dad has another child, I told him that wasn’t my sister”\n\nRevealing the depths of Drake’s willingness  to cut anyone off. Even if they are blood.", "annotation_id": "34165412", "url": "https://genius.com/34165412", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Show me how different we are, there’s blessings in juxtaposition", "vault_lines": ["Show me how different we are, there's blessings in juxtaposition"], "meaning": "Drake’s line is likely sarcastic.\n\nMocking the idea that their differences are a “blessing.” He’s throwing shade, masking bitterness about betrayal with fake reflection.", "annotation_id": "34165356", "url": "https://genius.com/34165356", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "The Dennis James come out when the vodka is floodin’ my system", "vault_lines": ["The Dennis James come out when the vodka is floodin' my system"], "meaning": "Drake in the past has been vocal with his Dad’s drinking problem going all the way back to “From Time” feat Jhene Aiko. In that song Drake raps,\n\n“When he put the bottle down, girl, that ****’s amazin”\n\nDrake throughout his career has indicated inherited tendencies he has from his parents. This time around Drake is embracing the good and bad of his dad.", "annotation_id": "34165098", "url": "https://genius.com/34165098", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "My dad has another child, I told him that wasn’t my sister", "vault_lines": ["My dad has another child, I told him that wasn't my sister"], "meaning": "Drake’s father, Dennis James Graham, has at least two other children besides Drake. Per his interview with Office Magazine in 2016:\n\nInterviewer Marc Palatucci: “Was he [Drake] your only child?\"\n\nDennis Graham: “I have more kids, one by a girl I went to high school with, and I had a daughter after my wife and I split up. So he’s got two half-siblings.”\n\nhttps://officemagazine.net/interview/godfather\n\nhttps://imgur.com/a/WCV6Qrw", "annotation_id": "34129022", "url": "https://genius.com/34129022", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Niggas started fuckin’ with my family, I’m goin’ to prison", "vault_lines": ["Niggas started fuckin' with my family, I'm goin' to prison"], "meaning": "Since Kendrick’s “Meet the Graham” track references almost every Drake family member, Drake now seems to be very aggressive in responding to the allegations. He is ready to do anything to defend his family.", "annotation_id": "34158865", "url": "https://genius.com/34158865", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Cold shoulders I gave in the Hamptons, it come with the distance Figured we was always gon’ be close, like ovens and kitchens I was sadly mistaken, the loyalty wasn’t a given", "vault_lines": ["Cold shoulders I gave in the Hamptons, it come with the distance", "Figured we was always gon' be close, like ovens and kitchens", "I was sadly mistaken, the loyalty wasn't a given"], "meaning": "Drake references his time at the annual Fourth of July All-White party held by Michael Rubin in 2024 at his home located in the Hamptons where many of his peers such as Travis Scott, Camila Cabello, GloRilla, Lil Wayne and others were in attendance and talks about giving someone he was once close with the ‘cold shoulder’ at the party last year. The subject matter of this record leads most to believe he further referencing his relationship with LeBron James who has attended the All-White party before but was not last year. This has led to the theory that Drake was speaking to his relationship with Travis Scott or Lil Wayne in these particular lines, both artists he’s had very successful collaborations in the past. Drake had photos with some guests like GloRilla, Yeat, Odell Beckham Jr., Camila Cabello, and even the Host Rubin himself but was never pictured with Travis or Wayne who both had photos with guests from that night and Wayne even said after the party “Shout out to Glo, GloRilla the only person that came up to me and really hollered at me\". Drake also connects the lines “it come with the distance, Figured we was always gon' be close, like ovens and kitchens” and juxtaposes the words “distance” and “close”, displaying his lyrical talent by using those terms to literally and figuratively describe his perspective of the relationship struggles.\n\n\n\n\n\nhttps://www.xxlmag.com/lil-wayne-glorilla-michael-rubin-all-white-party/", "annotation_id": "34153655", "url": "https://genius.com/34153655", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "I said a prayer that morning for Sean, and it wasn’t religious", "vault_lines": ["I said a prayer that morning for Sean, and it wasn't religious"], "meaning": "Drake has generally maintained a neutral relationship with Big Sean, a G.O.O.D Music signee. However, in August 2024, reports surfaced that Sean joked about Drake’s “duck face” on Instagram Live, implying Sean is no longer on Drake’s side.\n\nNotably, G.O.O.D Music is owned by rapper-producer Kanye West, a long-time rival of Drake after their once-close friendship fell apart—mirroring Drake’s fallout with others over the years.", "annotation_id": "34148954", "url": "https://genius.com/34148954", "state": "accepted", "votes": -5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Or when I get my fifth Maybach ’cause the color is different", "vault_lines": ["Or when I get my fifth Maybach 'cause the color is different"], "meaning": "This is a respond towards Rick Ross' Champagne Moments, because in that song Rick Ross is portraying Drake as a white boy and accusing him of disrespect towards his black ancestory. Rick Ross also has his famous maybach music-adlib, which seems to clarify this was aimed at him.\n\nThis line means that people will never be happy for him when he achieves more success, because he feels that everyone would rather hate him than stand on his side.", "annotation_id": "34146782", "url": "https://genius.com/34146782", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "What are we celebratin’? I’ll rent out the club at Edition", "vault_lines": ["What are we celebratin'? I'll rent out the club at Edition"], "meaning": "Edition Hotels is a luxury lifestyle hotel brand created by Ian Schrager, a pioneer of boutique hotels, in partnership with Marriott International. As of January 2025, Edition Hotels operates 19 properties worldwide. In North America, the brand features locations in New York, Florida, California and Mexico.\n\n\n\n\"The Miami Beach\" Edition Hotel", "annotation_id": "34134180", "url": "https://genius.com/34134180", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Rappin’ was over for now, it came like a sudden decision", "vault_lines": ["Rappin' was over for now, it came like a sudden decision"], "meaning": "Drake was inevitably forced to take a pause from rapping after the shooting incident at his home in Toronto.", "annotation_id": "34132937", "url": "https://genius.com/34132937", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "God forbid we ever got to tarnish your public image", "vault_lines": ["God forbid we ever got to tarnish your public image"], "meaning": "As LeBron James has been referenced throughout this track, this line may also be directed at him. LeBron, widely regarded as the NBA’s biggest star for over two decades, is renowned for maintaining an almost spotless public image. This is a rarity for someone of his stature, as immense fame and success often invite heightened scrutiny and fabricated rumors, driven by envy, public fascination, or the desire to bring down celebrated figures.\n\nDrake hints at the monumental weight of tarnishing such an untouchable reputation, suggesting he may possess knowledge of secrets or controversies that could challenge it.\n\nDrake & LeBron", "annotation_id": "34132504", "url": "https://genius.com/34132504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "I don’t have a drinkin’ problem, I got a subtle addiction", "vault_lines": ["I don't have a drinkin' problem, I got a subtle addiction"], "meaning": "Please edit the line above – it should be Dennis James as in his Dad, not a random German bodybuilder?! Drake’s dad’s full name is Dennis James Graham. A genealogist did a deep dive on his ancestry: https://youtu.be/qwjf8NobOSY?si=KXpyg4gX5OKp17RC", "annotation_id": "34132355", "url": "https://genius.com/34132355", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "6 Degrees, my memory’s retrievin’ our humble beginnings", "vault_lines": ["6 Degrees, my memory's retrievin' our humble beginnings"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to 6 Degrees, a well-known nightclub in Toronto, where he held the release party for his 3rd mixtape So Far Gone in 2009. At the time, Drake was still primarily a local Toronto artist, but his buzz was undeniable.\n\nLebron James, who was at the peak of his NBA dominance, and as known rap enthusiast, would attend the event, to show some love and support for Drake. This would mark the first time Drake met LeBron James.\n\n\n\nDrake meeting LeBron for the first time at his So Far Gone release party at the 6 Degrees nightclub in Toronto\nThe phrase “six degrees” might also be referencing the idea of six degrees of separation — the concept that everyone is connected through a small chain of acquaintances. When Drake linked up with LeBron for the first time, it was a significant moment that could be viewed as the fruition of this concept.\n\nThe nostalgic nod to their early days, juxtapose their modest/humble starts with their current positions as the biggest names in music and sports respectively.", "annotation_id": "34132071", "url": "https://genius.com/34132071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "The summer is over, I missed it, barrel was smoking, I kissed it", "vault_lines": ["The summer is over, I missed it, barrel was smoking, I kissed it"], "meaning": "A smoking gun implies a shot was fired, and kissing the gun after firing it signifies that the target was hit and eliminated. In this case, Drake is referring to the many diss records he released over the summer of 2024 in his battle with Kendrick Lamar. For Drake, this behavior means he is celebrating his victory over the opposition and is ready for his next confrontation.\n\nThis line also recalls a similar one from “The Language” off of Nothing Was the Same, a song that was seen as response to Lamar’s verse from “Control”:\n\nBeen workin' so hard on the album, I missed the whole summer", "annotation_id": "34119508", "url": "https://genius.com/34119508", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "(Conductor)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the sampled producer tag for Kansas City hip-hop producer Conductor Williams, who has worked with Drizzy on multiple tracks.", "annotation_id": "34131150", "url": "https://genius.com/34131150", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "It came from a place of desperation and utter submission", "vault_lines": ["It came from a place of desperation and utter submission"], "meaning": "Taken together with the line above, the saying reflects a deeply human moment of crisis where religious or ideological frameworks don’t offer solace or answers. Instead, he speaks from a place of being stripped bare by the intensity of the struggles—completely overwhelmed and forced into a position of surrender. This may not be a serene or graceful submission but rather one born of exhaustion, where there is no longer any defiance or hope for control.\n\nIt’s a moment of pure, vulnerable humanity, where he expresses his emotional state without the filters of religious duty or the hope of a higher power intervening. Instead, it’s simply the raw reality of someone confronting their personal limits and yielding to a harsh, uncontrollable situation.", "annotation_id": "34126831", "url": "https://genius.com/34126831", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "I don’t give a fuck about you snakes or these other musicians", "vault_lines": ["I don't give a fuck about you snakes or these other musicians"], "meaning": "Drake once again reinforces the reality of him assisting many artists such as A$AP Rocky, The Weeknd, Future, Rick Ross, Kendrick Lamar etc. and feeling like they stabbed him in the back despite genuine love he had displayed in their careers.\n\nHis various successful features with Rick Ross and Future or Verses/Tracks he gave away such as ‘Fuckin Problems’ – A$AP Rocky ft. 2Chainz, Drake, and Kendrick Lamar or even him posting artists’ early songs like Tyler The Creator’s breakout ‘Yonkers’ or Kendrick Lamar’s ‘Cut You Off’ music video off the mixtape ‘Overly Dedicated’ released in September 2010 on his OVO blog back on February 11, 2011(before Section 80. buzz and Buried Alive Interlude later that year) speak to the sentiment of Drake feeling betrayed and disrespected by many of these musicians he showed authentic love to throughout the years.\n\nhttps://octobersveryown.blogspot.com/2011/02/", "annotation_id": "34124907", "url": "https://genius.com/34124907", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Grew up with dozens of Jews, churches with dozens of Christians Also, developed a deep respect for the Muslim religion", "vault_lines": ["Grew up with dozens of Jews, churches with dozens of Christians", "Also, developed a deep respect for the Muslim religion"], "meaning": "Going all the way back to the Kendrick Lamar feud, Drake directly addresses Lamar’s claims from the “meet the grahams” diss track, where Kendrick accused him of lying about his religious views:\n\nWhy believe you? You never gave us nothin' to believe in\n‘Cause you lied about religious views\n\nDrake clarifies that while he was raised Jewish—his mother, Sandi, being Jewish—his upbringing exposed him to people from various religious backgrounds, including Christianity and Islam. Through these experiences, he developed a bigger understanding and a strong respect for these other religions.", "annotation_id": "34122987", "url": "https://genius.com/34122987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "’Member we traded watches? I gave you that Arabic dial, you gave me a numbered edition You would even check up on my son like a pediatrician", "vault_lines": ["'Member we traded watches?", "I gave you that Arabic dial, you gave me a numbered edition", "You would even check up on my son like a pediatrician"], "meaning": "These couple of lines seems to specifically address LeBron James, a figure Drake has openly celebrated and respected in the past.\n\nBoth Drake and LeBron are known  luxury watch-enthusiast. An Arabic dial watch is a coveted piece among collectors, It features Arabic numerals (١,٢,٣ etc.) on the dial, as opposed to Roman numerals and many luxury brands like Rolex produce them in small quantities. It wouldn’t be surprising if the two had exchanged such high-end timepieces as a gesture of their friendship.\n\n\n\nBoth LeBron and Drake rocking a platinum Rolex  with ice blue dial, Arabic numerals, these models' market price is around $350,000 USD\n\n\nDrake previously opened up about his son, Adonis, on LeBron’s show The Shop. This marked the first time Drake revealed what his son looked like to LeBron in particular out of all the people on the show, symbolizing the trust in their relationship. Drake’s line about LeBron checking up on his son “like a pediatrician” further emphasizes the strength of the bond they must have had and how deeply involved and caring LeBron seemed to be—regularly inquiring about Adonis’s well-being, much like a doctor specialized in healthcare of children.", "annotation_id": "34122590", "url": "https://genius.com/34122590", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "The world fell in love with the gimmicks, even my brothers got tickets Seemed like they loved every minute Just know this shit is personal to us, and it wasn’t just business", "vault_lines": ["The world fell in love with the gimmicks, even my brothers got tickets", "Seemed like they loved every minute", "Just know this shit is personal to us, and it wasn't just business"], "meaning": "Drake addresses the overwhelming support Kendrick Lamar received during their feud, particularly from celebrities like LeBron James and DeMar DeRozan —these were individuals he once considered close allies, which he dismisses as infatuation with “gimmicks,” implying that Kendrick relied on attention-grabbing tactics or controversy to gain public support, rather than purely relying on skill or artistic merit.\n\n\n\nDrake’s tattoo of LeBron James\n\nIn the third verse, Drake would double-down on his critique of the “gimmicks” he perceives Kendrick Lamar to have used during their feud:\n\nThis nigga pulled a rabbit out the hat, and y'all love a magician\nIt’s funny to see you all rejoicin' and huggin' and kissin'\n\nLeBron, once seen as a close ally, was spotted vibing to “Not Like Us”—a diss track targeting Drake with harsh accusations, including the insinuation of predatory behavior. To make matters worse, LeBron was also seen enthusiastically rapping lines from Kendrick’s latest album, GNX.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/w7pYyE13oY0\n\nDeMar DeRozan would even go up to make a surprising appearance in the music video for Kendrick’s “Not Like Us”, a move Drake likely viewed as a direct insult. This tension reportedly came to a head during a Raptors game where Drake and DeRozan had a notably unpleasant interaction. Drake would also throw shade at DeRozan during the in-game commentary as well.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GJ5hCQFPWOo&pp=ygUTZHJha2UgZGVtYXIgcmFwdG9ycw%3D%3D Both LeBron and DeRozan attended Kendrick’s Pop Out concert, a post-feud victory lap for Lamar. Kendrick performed several diss tracks from the feud, with DeRozan even jumping on stage to dance to tracks that targeted Drake.https://youtu.be/LrMCYJuGAzQ                For Drake, these moments symbolized profound disloyalty, as both LeBron and DeRozan’s actions appeared to align them with Kendrick during a particularly vulnerable period. Drake further states that this feud was deeply personal, far beyond a mere competitive rivalry, making their alignment with his opposition feel nothing short of betrayal.", "annotation_id": "34122327", "url": "https://genius.com/34122327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "And niggas cried the blues for you, sayin’ it wasn’t malicious Talkin’ ’bout we family, well, I’m not the cousin to visit", "vault_lines": ["And niggas cried the blues for you, sayin' it wasn't malicious", "Talkin' 'bout we family, well, I'm not the cousin to visit"], "meaning": "Possibly a reference to DeMar Derozan who is Crip-affiliated, and known for having his bouts with depression and anxiety.\nHe tells the story of when he was traded to the Spurs from the Raptors in 2018, he went to Drake’s house to calm down.\n\nhttps://m.youtube.com/watch?v=QEzRl4nC8AU\n\nLater Demar is mentioned in “Not Like Us”, went to the Popout show, and appeared in the video.", "annotation_id": "34122183", "url": "https://genius.com/34122183", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "That was a body double, and we’ve done dozens of switches", "vault_lines": ["That was a body double, and we've done dozens of switches"], "meaning": "A body double is a person who substitutes for another individual, typically in film, television, or theater, during specific scenes where the primary actor cannot or does not want to perform.\n\nThis could be referenced to Drake allegedly leaking out false information about himself having a hidden daughter to Kendrick, which Kendrick rapped about in MTG but turned out to be false.\n\nThe word play also links well with the double entendre in the next line, the dozens of switches being referred may be Drake insinuating that he has shooters that have shot up dozens of switches in his name compared to the shots fired at his house that shot one of his security guards.", "annotation_id": "34119757", "url": "https://genius.com/34119757", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Analyzin’ behavioral patterns, is someone suspicious?", "vault_lines": ["Analyzin' behavioral patterns, is someone suspicious?"], "meaning": "This line references Kendrick Lamar’s “Meet the Grahams”, where Lamar meticulously analyzes Drake’s actions. By describing this kind of scrutiny as “somewhat suspicious,” Drake subtly flips the script on Lamar’s commentary, suggesting Lamar is projecting onto others and that he is the one hiding secrets, not Drake, as well as implying that Lamar might be overly fixated on him, which could stem from feeling overshadowed by Drake’s unparalleled commercial success.\n\nThese comments reflect a broader tension that exists between the two artists, with Drake framing Lamar’s behavior as obsessive rather than insightful.", "annotation_id": "34119743", "url": "https://genius.com/34119743", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "I guess now you boys gotta abandon your summer tradition", "vault_lines": ["I guess now you boys gotta abandon your summer tradition"], "meaning": "This line continues Drake’s commentary at LeBron James, as James has a summer tradition of attending Caribana and OVO Fest for over a decade in Toronto.\n\nThis line applies to more than just LeBron. He said “you boys” (plural). Drake has a hit every summer. Usually that hit has a feature or is Drake featuring on someone else’s song. He’s saying their summer tradition is coming to him for hits, and they’re going to have to abandon that said tradition.", "annotation_id": "34119446", "url": "https://genius.com/34119446", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Cover my body in dirt, but that wasn’t me That wasn’t me", "vault_lines": ["Cover my body in dirt, but that wasn't me"], "meaning": "“Cover my body in dirt” here has two meanings. The first and most immediately obvious is a reference to a funeral burial, where Drake seems to be openly acknowledging for the first time the fact that he was “buried” during the Kendrick Lamar beef last year. This could also be a reference to ‘Buried Alive Interlude’, one of Kendrick and Drake’s only tracks together.\n\nSimultaneously, in conjunction with his claim that “that wasn’t me,” it’s also a reference to the idiom “put dirt on my name,” i.e., to publicly say false or harmful things about someone to damage their reputation. It appears that he is acknowledging the public’s widely-held perception of him having lost, but deflecting by claiming that the “dirt” used to “bury” him was not factually accurate, therefore “wasn’t him” that was actually being buried in the process. This is further reiterated with the “body double” line following this bar.", "annotation_id": "34119400", "url": "https://genius.com/34119400", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Ferris showed us the renders for Texas, it’s unrealistic Cut ‘em the check ’cause I hate reviewin’ the budgets and limits Just pay the man", "vault_lines": ["Ferris showed us the renders for Texas, it's unrealistic", "Cut 'em the check 'cause I hate reviewin' the budgets and limits", "Just pay the man"], "meaning": "His trusted designer, Ferris Rafauli, is well-known for creating iconic, high-end spaces—most notably Drake’s Toronto mansion, The Embassy. It’s no surprise that Drake would enlist him to reimagine his $15 million, 313-acre ranch in Brenham, Texas.\n\n\nDrake announced the purchase during the Houston leg of his It’s All a Blur tour in late 2023, expressing excitement about finally securing a property in a city so integral to his career and connections. In his words, “I finally, finally after all these years found me a place in Houston, Texas.”  https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EgHC3a7Va00\n\nDespite Ferris’s renders for the ranch being labeled as “unrealistic” by Drake, the rapper seems content to give Ferris complete creative freedom. He dislikes reviewing expenses but is willing to spare no cost, confident in Ferris’s unparalleled design expertise.", "annotation_id": "34119068", "url": "https://genius.com/34119068", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "I’m tryna blow the doors off that bitch when you touch the ignition But then I gotta remember who made this shit come to fruition", "vault_lines": ["I'm tryna blow the doors off that bitch when you touch the ignition", "But then I gotta remember who made this shit come to fruition"], "meaning": "Drake wants to retaliate against his enemies but is well aware of his position as a rapper, artist, and public figure and knows it’s not worth to lose everything over a small thing.\n\nHe’s also mentioned this conudrum previously in “4PM in Calabasas”\n\nI got a lot to lose cause in every situation. I’m the bigger artist, always gotta play it smarter.", "annotation_id": "34119042", "url": "https://genius.com/34119042", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Who put a pipe bomb in your trunk? It was an exhibit", "vault_lines": ["Who put a pipe bomb in your trunk? It was an exhibit"], "meaning": "The real lyric is “It was an exhibit”. It’s a double entendre, and a reference to Xzibit’s “Concrete”:\n\nI’m the mechanic with a pipe bomb that fits your car\nKa-boom, now I assume your homies wanna retalliate\n\nWhen Drake retaliates against his opps, he makes it public, “an exhibit”.\n\nIn the example of the “pipe bomb in the trunk” it turns into “an exhibit”, be it of art when it explodes, creating a scene, or police evidence (as in “exhibit A of a crime”), given the previous lines talking about him and his brothers “crashing out together”.\n\nIt’s also a unspoken nod to Xzibit’s MTV show “Pimp My Ride”. Pimp My Ride was an American television series produced by MTV and hosted by rapper Xzibit, which ran for six seasons on MTV from 2004 to 2007. In each episode, a car in poor condition is both restored and customized.\n\nDrake is “restoring” his opp’s car by putting a pipe bomb in the trunk and installing a igniton bomb that would be powerful enough to make the doors blow off.", "annotation_id": "34119023", "url": "https://genius.com/34119023", "state": "pending", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Who’s got the most to lose, and who’s in that other position? Signal conversations, well, I hope they got double encryption I’d hate to see the empire crumble on judge’s convictions", "vault_lines": ["Who's got the most to lose, and who's in that other position?", "Signal conversations, well, I hope they got double encryption", "I'd hate to see the empire crumble on judge's convictions"], "meaning": "Drake talks about the uneven stakes in his feud. As one of the most successful artists in the industry, he has a lot to protect—his career and empire. On the other hand, his rivals, like Kendrick Lamar, might have less to lose. This makes the risk of escalating the conflict much greater for him.\n\nDrake also explains how careful he has to be in dangerous situations. He mentions using Signal, a private messaging app with strong encryption, to keep his conversations secure.\n\nAdditionally, Drake ties it all together, showing his fear of losing everything to legal trouble. If reckless actions, like the violence mentioned earlier, lead to a court case, it could destroy his career and empire.", "annotation_id": "34118992", "url": "https://genius.com/34118992", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "I’m starin’ at my daddy while y’all tell me I wasn’t that nigga", "vault_lines": ["I'm starin' at my daddy while y'all tell me I wasn't that nigga"], "meaning": "Throughout Drake’s 2024 feud with Kendrick Lamar, Kendrick questioned Drake’s connection to Black culture and authenticity, often criticizing his suburban Canadian upbringing and Jewish heritage. On his April 2024 diss track “euphoria,” Kendrick delivered a pointed critique:\n\nI even hate when you say the word “nigga,” but that’s just me, I guess\nSome shit just cringeworthy, it ain’t even gotta be deep, I guess\n\nDrake fires back by pointing out the obvious—his father is a Black man from Memphis. Staring at his dad while hearing these critiques emphasizes Drake’s disbelief and frustration, as he challenges the validity of Kendrick’s accusations, asserting that his racial identity and roots are indisputable.\n\nDrake and his dad, Dennis Graham\n\nThis is the second time Drake has intentionally used the N-word to dismiss Lamar, as he previously did in “Family Matters”:\n\nMaybe in this song, you shouldn’t start by saying\nNigga, I said it, I know that you mad\n\nThis line also stands in direct juxtaposition to Lamar’s commentary on Dennis Graham in “Meet the Grahams”:\n\nDear Dennis, you gave birth to a master manipulator\nEven usin' you to prove who he is is a huge favor", "annotation_id": "34118956", "url": "https://genius.com/34118956", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Niggas tried to light the front gate up, it wasn’t for Christmas", "vault_lines": ["Niggas tried to light the front gate up, it wasn't for Christmas"], "meaning": "On the night of May 7, 2024 around 2 AM EDT (6 AM GMT), Drake’s home was shot and a security guard got hit and had to be taken to the hospital:\n\nThe guard, whose identity was not revealed, was allegedly “shot while standing outside the gates in front of” Drake’s home and hospitalized with “life-threatening injuries,” police said in a release published Tuesday.\n\nThis happened three days after the feud with Kendrick Lamar reached a new level of tension when Lamar uploaded “Not Like Us” and Drake then uploaded a rebuttal the following day, “The Heart Part 6.” Since this incident, Drake’s home has been broken into twice:\n\n\nOn Wednesday, police returned to Drake’s home in response to a report of an attempted break-in. Police detained the suspect on the scene for an evaluation under Canada’s Mental Health Act, a spokesperson confirmed to The Times. As of Thursday, the alleged trespasser had not been charged.\n\nThe three incidents come amid Drake’s highly public feud with rapper Kendrick Lamar. For weeks, the Grammy-winning musicians have traded diss tracks filled with scathing allegations, including domestic violence and child sex abuse. For the cover art on his “Not Like Us” diss, Lamar used a Google Earth image of Drake’s home pinned with multiple red “sex offender markers,” according to online fans.", "annotation_id": "34118912", "url": "https://genius.com/34118912", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Nothing was the same with all you bitches and nothing’s forgiven", "vault_lines": ["Nothing was the same with all you bitches and nothing's forgiven"], "meaning": "Drake is making a wordplay using the name of his third studio album, “Nothing was the same”.\n\nDrake is taking a shot at all the people who sided with Kendrick against him during the battle. Drake claims these people are fake and doesn’t want any interest in them.\n\nThis could be Drake’s response to the famous tweet by Young Thug which called for peace among Drake, Future and Metro Boomin' after the battle was over. Future has shared the tweet by Thug but Drake has never responded publicly.\n\nThere were some rumors that Drake and Future had got on the phone and squashed their beef, however, according to other sources this isn’t true.\n\nhttps://tribune.com.pk/story/2504084/fans-react-after-future-likes-young-thugs-post-about-ending-beef-with-drake", "annotation_id": "34118836", "url": "https://genius.com/34118836", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "This nigga pulled a rabbit out the hat, and y’all love a magician It’s funny to see you all rejoicin’ and huggin’ and kissin’", "vault_lines": ["This nigga pulled a rabbit out the hat, and y'all love a magician", "It's funny to see you all rejoicin' and huggin' and kissin'"], "meaning": "Drake may be using the metaphor of “pulling a rabbit out of a hat”—a classic magic trick—to suggest that Kendrick Lamar’s accusations during their 2024 feud, including allegations of pedophilia, sex trafficking, and having a secret daughter, were dramatic but ultimately fabricated, like an illusion. By likening Kendrick’s moves to magic, Drake implies that these claims were crafted to captivate and manipulate the public rather than reveal any genuine truths.\n\n“Huggin' and kissin'” also refers to a line from “euphoria”, where the phrase is used to refer to Drake and J. Cole:\n\nIt’s three GOATs left, and I seen two of them kissin' and huggin' on stage\n\nDrizzy further mocks the public’s reaction, portraying their support for Kendrick as overly enthusiastic and misplaced. This line reflects Drake’s strategy of discrediting Kendrick’s victories, framing them as clever illusions designed to distract rather than meaningful blows.", "annotation_id": "34118817", "url": "https://genius.com/34118817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fighting Irish Freestyle", "line": "Niggas was never happy for me when I’d run up the digits Or when I’m breakin’ world records still, as I guzzle a Guinness", "vault_lines": ["Niggas was never happy for me when I'd run up the digits", "Or when I'm breakin' world records still, as I guzzle a Guinness"], "meaning": "Drake cleverly references the brand Guinness in both its modern forms, comparing his frequent breaking of Guinness World Records to guzzling Guinness beer. The imagery portrays him shattering records as effortlessly as someone drinking a pint, emphasizing his dominance in the music industry.\n\nAt the same time, the reference to drinking Guinness may suggest using alcohol to numb the pain of feeling unappreciated, highlighting the bittersweet truth that even monumental achievements can fail to provide genuine support or fulfillment.", "annotation_id": "34118581", "url": "https://genius.com/34118581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
