{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "Look like she used to play volleyball", "vault_lines": ["Look like she used to play volleyball"], "meaning": "21 Savage might be referencing Ice Spice, as she played volleyball and in the following lines he mentions a Finsta, something Ice Spice got her name from.\n\nhttps://hiphopdx.com/news/ice-spice-volleyball-high-school-photo", "annotation_id": "28417127", "url": "https://genius.com/28417127", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "One wrong call from your ex nigga sayin’ dumb shit’ll get him sent home One call and my niggas ten toes Down to go wherever I say go Even if we gotta travel ’cross the globe", "vault_lines": ["One wrong call from your ex nigga sayin' dumb shit'll get him sent home", "One call and my niggas ten toes", "Down to go wherever I say go", "Even if we gotta travel 'cross the globe"], "meaning": "“Ten toes” is slang for being extremely devoted to something or someone. Basically, Drake has loyal affiliates quick to act upon his request, even in cases requiring violence. What Drizzy says, goes.\n\nDrake has previously discussed being connected with violent criminals who are “ten toes down” for him. For example, on “Mob Ties,” he raps:\n\nSick of these niggas (Sick, sick)\nHire some help (Help), get rid of these niggas (Grr)\n[…] I fuck with the mob and I got ties (Lotta ties, lotta ties)\nKnock you off to pay their tithes (Do doo)\n\nGoing outside of his music, Drake’s good friend and bodyguard “Chubbs” violently assaulted record producer Detail.", "annotation_id": "27475176", "url": "https://genius.com/27475176", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "Wipe him like he snotty for you, baby girl", "vault_lines": ["Wipe him like he snotty for you, baby girl"], "meaning": "Drake delivers this line so that it can be heard/interpreted two ways:\n\n1) Wipe him like he’s snotty for you, meaning to get rid of him as you would do with snot on your nose.\n\n2) Wipe him likes he’s not it for you, meaning to wipe (another word for murder) him because he’s not the one for her and he’s in the way.", "annotation_id": "27409206", "url": "https://genius.com/27409206", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "Lovin’ tonight (Ooh) Give me the feelin’, so right (You gotta motherfuckin’ feel this shit, boy) Give me your lovin’ (BanBwoi, I want your love) Give me your lovin’ (Woah, give me your lovin’)", "vault_lines": ["Give me the feelin', so right (You gotta motherfuckin' feel this shit, boy)", "Give me your lovin' (BanBwoi, I want your love)", "So I gotta respect ya", "Want ya, I want-want ya", "Oh, your lovin' so deep (Feelin' so deep)"], "meaning": "The outro features a prominent sample from the chorus of the 1995 song “Give Me Your Lov-N,” the breakout hit of the R&B group B.G.O.T.I.:\n\nGive me your lovin' tonight (Ooh)\nGive me the feelin', so right\nGive me your lovin' (I want your love)\nGive me your lovin' (Give me your love, babe)\nBaby, I want you right here (I want you right here)\nYou know your love is so deep (Feelin' so deep)\nGive me your lovin'\nGive me your lovin'", "annotation_id": "27207110", "url": "https://genius.com/27207110", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "I’m a made man, tied in", "vault_lines": ["I gotta protect ya, I'm a made man, tied in, all the way, baby"], "meaning": "Drake mentions he is a “made man”, meaning a fully initiated member of the Mafia.", "annotation_id": "27142041", "url": "https://genius.com/27142041", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "She wanna hit Highlight Room and show it off", "vault_lines": ["She wanna hit Highlight Room and show it off"], "meaning": "Savage references The Highlight Room, a high-end roof-top bar in Los Angeles, California.", "annotation_id": "27088228", "url": "https://genius.com/27088228", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "Down to take you to the end of the road, for real", "vault_lines": ["Down to take you to the end of the road, for real"], "meaning": "Drake references “End of the Road,” probably the most popular Boyz II Men track. That makes two nods to ‘90s R&B groups in one verse!", "annotation_id": "27072489", "url": "https://genius.com/27072489", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "Wipe him like he snotty for you", "vault_lines": ["Wipe him like he snotty for you, baby girl"], "meaning": "To wipe somebody refers to murdering them.\n\nDrake mentioned a similar word play on What’s Next:\n\nHe thought he was sick, now he wipin' his nose", "annotation_id": "27070824", "url": "https://genius.com/27070824", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "Niggas put hands on you in the past, insecure because your body is precious", "vault_lines": ["Niggas put hands on you in the past, insecure because your body is precious"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his girl’s abusive past, stating that she is now insecure about her body as it was the reason she was being abused by previous boyfriends as it was “precious” and they did not want her to leave them.", "annotation_id": "27061953", "url": "https://genius.com/27061953", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "G-Wag’", "vault_lines": ["She got a new G-Wag'"], "meaning": "The Mercedes-Benz G-Class, sometimes colloquially called the G-Wagen [G-Wag] (as an abbreviation of Geländewagen) is a four-wheel drive automobile sold by Mercedes-Benz. Originally developed as a military off-roader, later more luxurious models were added to the line, leading to its prominence in rappers' lyrics as a status item.\n\n\n\nThe G-Wagon is characterised by its boxy styling and body-on-frame construction. It uses three fully locking differentials, one of the few passenger car vehicles to have such a feature.", "annotation_id": "27063300", "url": "https://genius.com/27063300", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "American Express, you can have it all", "vault_lines": ["American Express, you can have it all", "Code to the safe, you can have it all"], "meaning": "This might be a referral to the famous American Express black card. This card isn’t just given to anyone. According to Investopedia, it’s a charge card that’s offered to high-net-worth individuals (HNWIs) through an invitation.\nYou most likely have to be a high spender on this card to be invited to own it. With this line, 21 is alluding to having this card and giving a girl the luxury and prestige of having this card because of his feelings for her.", "annotation_id": "27062605", "url": "https://genius.com/27062605", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "Damn, just turned on the news and seen that men who never got pussy in school Are makin’ laws about what women can do I gotta protect ya,", "vault_lines": ["Damn, just turned on the news and seen that men who never got pussy in school", "Are makin' laws about what women can do", "So I gotta respect ya", "Want ya, I want-want ya"], "meaning": "Contrasting the album’s title, Her Loss—which advocates for the loss of women being the gain of a man, further shown in the Apple Music bio—Drake stands up for the female community by calling out government officials for attempting to hold control over women’s bodies.\n\nBeginning his verse, Drake notes that the ones who attempt to take control over the opposite sex’s body rights are resentful and bitter due to them never “[getting] pussy in school.”\n\nIn June 2022, after the Supreme court overturned Roe v. Wade regarding abortion rights, it was made illegal to have an abortion in fifteen states in the US. Following the ban, a statement from Jonathan Mitchell caused extreme outrage as it was seen as controlling and disrespectful — relating directly to Drake’s lyric:\n\nWomen can ‘control their reproductive lives' without access to abortion; they can do so by refraining from sexual intercourse", "annotation_id": "27053225", "url": "https://genius.com/27053225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "And I know what I said ‘bout bein’ in Vogue But just like that R&B group from the ’90s Girl, one call, I’ll get you in Vogue One call, you in runway shows One call, I’m sittin’ front row", "vault_lines": ["And I know what I said 'bout bein' in Vogue", "But just like that R&B group from the '90s", "Girl, one call, I'll get you in Vogue", "One call, you in runway shows", "One call, I'm sittin' front row"], "meaning": "Drake references his viral bar on his song “Jimmy Cooks” :\n\nBitch, don’t tell me that you model if you ain’t been in Vogue\n\nHe next plays on the name of ‘90s R&B group En Vogue, a homophone for “in Vogue.”\n\n\nHe goes on to flex his power and influence by emphasizing how easy it is to get someone a spot in Vogue Magazine, a runway gig, or front row seats to an event.", "annotation_id": "27052896", "url": "https://genius.com/27052896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "You know how it goes when they can’t get a reservation up in Carbone", "vault_lines": ["You know how it goes when they can't get a reservation up in Carbone"], "meaning": "Last time Drake referenced Carbone was on the track Do No Distub! The line was “Last table left in Carbone, callin’ plays on a rotary phone.”", "annotation_id": "27052636", "url": "https://genius.com/27052636", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "I got feelings for you Hope you ain’t lovin’ the crew", "vault_lines": ["I got feelings for you", "Hope you ain't lovin' the crew"], "meaning": "21 references Her Loss-collaborator Drake’s 2012 collaboration with The Weeknd, “Crew Love.” On the chorus, Abel sings:\n\nWhy? ‘Cause they loving the crew\nThey loving the crew\nThey loving the crew\nOh, they loving the crew\nOh, they loving the crew\nThey loving the crew\nThey loving the crew\nThey loving the crew, woah\n\nHere, 21 expresses his affection for a girl. However, unlike The Weeknd, he’d prefer to keep her to himself.", "annotation_id": "27052617", "url": "https://genius.com/27052617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Spin Bout U", "line": "Fuck your main page, what’s your Finsta? I wanna know the real you", "vault_lines": ["Fuck your main pagе, what's your Finsta? I wanna know the real you"], "meaning": "Some Instagram users have what’s called a Finsta, short for “Fake Insta.” It serves as a more private alternate account for close friends.\n\n21 would prefer to see her true self portrayed on her Finsta, rather than the unrealistic lifestyle often glamorized on one’s main page. It’s ironic that the “real you” is found on a fake Instagram.", "annotation_id": "27052403", "url": "https://genius.com/27052403", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
