{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Think Good Thoughts", "line": "Well-read like sunburn", "vault_lines": ["Peeped, 'cause I ain't dumb, shit, I'm well-read like sunburn"], "meaning": "Well-read individuals may also be referred to as “well-red,” a term that is commonly used to describe the appearance of sunburned skin.", "annotation_id": "32281126", "url": "https://genius.com/32281126", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Think Good Thoughts", "line": "First name Phonte, I’d be pleased to date ya, college educated, got degrees and papers", "vault_lines": ["First name Phonte, I'd be pleased to date ya, college educated, got degrees and papers"], "meaning": "Via Complex\n\nPhonte met Big Pooh and 9th Wonder at North Carolina Central University, where the trio formed Little Brother. Phonte, who majored in English, was the only one to graduate.\n\n\n\n“Degrees in papers” may be a play on English papers with a pun for money.", "annotation_id": "1277715", "url": "https://genius.com/1277715", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Think Good Thoughts", "line": "They don’t be saying that I’m humble, or I’m hella nice or that I’ve been approached to sign to Roc-A-Fella twice", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Instead of talking about how nice Drizzy is, or that he’s been in talks with the Roc twice before, they’d rather keep the rumor mill running.\n\nDon’t think Drake is “humble” or “hella nice?”\nPeep some of his interviews..", "annotation_id": "406412", "url": "https://genius.com/406412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Think Good Thoughts", "line": "They just tell you all the rumors they heard and we laugh soon as you come and “Tell Me,” like Amel and Bryce My groove theory", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Chicks run to Drake talking about the things they’ve heard about him and he just laughs it off.\n\nAmel Larrieux & Bryce Wilson were in a group called ‘Groove Theory’ and they had a hit single called “Tell Me”\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dmNMbee42s0", "annotation_id": "353928", "url": "https://genius.com/353928", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Think Good Thoughts", "line": "That’s why I am Little Nicky to these devils in rap", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Little Nicky was always the favorite of his father, Satan, even though his brothers were more powerful and had higher status being older. Drake may also be hinting at the fact other rappers in the game are growing unhappy with his increasing popularity.", "annotation_id": "316009", "url": "https://genius.com/316009", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Think Good Thoughts", "line": "You need to get real and check out my real life I ain’t a tip-drill, don’t care what it smell like But if you feel like putting all your notions aside And talking to a real nigga, I’d gladly oblige", "vault_lines": ["You need to get real and check out my real life", "I ain't a tip-drill, don't care what it smell like", "But if you feel like putting all your notions aside", "And talking to a real nigga, I'd gladly oblige"], "meaning": "If she was willing to put her assumptions aside, Phonte would be willing to show her that he’s actually not a bad guy.\n\nHe’s also dropping names of popular videos that use to play on UNCUT:\n\n\nNelly – “Tip Drill”\nBlack Jesus – “What That Thing Smell Like”", "annotation_id": "302777", "url": "https://genius.com/302777", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Think Good Thoughts", "line": "You probably think I walk around with my gun tucked And swang dick to these hoes like nunchucks Well, you can think what you want, but I think you’ve been watching too much ‘BET: Uncut’", "vault_lines": ["You probably think I walk around with my gun tucked", "And swang dick to these hoes like nunchucks", "Well, you can think what you want, but", "I think you've been watching too much 'BET: Uncut'"], "meaning": "Phonte’s saying that just because he raps, you probably think he walks around with a gun tucked somewhere on him at all times, and that he has sex with any random girl he meets.\n\nHe then says you can have whatever judgmental views you want about him and other rappers in general, but if you’re thinking that stereotypically, you should stop watching BET: Uncut and absorbing the wrong ideas from it. (BET: Uncut was a television show that featured Rap/Hip Hop artists' music videos that tended to be chock-full of very sexual and/or violent scenes and content in general.)", "annotation_id": "279936", "url": "https://genius.com/279936", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Think Good Thoughts", "status": "done", "targets": 8, "clicked": 8, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-think-good-thoughts-lyrics", "genius_title": "Think Good Thoughts"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Can I walk a straight line? Am I on a tightrope?", "vault_lines": ["Can I walk a straight line? Am I on a tightrope?"], "meaning": "This captures the duality of many of Drake’s relationship verses, where he’s not sure if he’s ready to settle down and leave his bachelor lifestyle (walk the straight line), or if he’s on dangerous footing in the relationship as the other person is unpredictable or does not reciprocate his level of commitment (walking on a tightrope).", "annotation_id": "41619522", "url": "https://genius.com/41619522", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "I feel accomplished, is six months a milestone?", "vault_lines": ["I feel accomplished, is six months a milestone?"], "meaning": "This echos other sentiments in the verse of uncertainty in the relationship, as Drake infamously has had many short term flings that have fallen apart with time. The line is ambiguous, as it could mean Drake isn’t sure if other people would celebrate 6 months together (but its long for him), or that he’s surprised and proud that he could make it work for 6 months with this person that he’s unsure about.", "annotation_id": "41619497", "url": "https://genius.com/41619497", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "White dress with wine stains", "vault_lines": ["White dress with wine stains, farm house with Mai Tais"], "meaning": "A white wedding dress is a symbol of purity, and Drake foreshadows that everything might not be as stable as it seems in the relationship with this image.  It also alludes to his partner not being able to leave the party life behind as the source of the turbulence.", "annotation_id": "41619447", "url": "https://genius.com/41619447", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Farm house with Mai Tais", "vault_lines": ["White dress with wine stains, farm house with Mai Tais"], "meaning": "I’d say this means “farmhouse with my taste” rather than “Mai Tais.”\n\nWhile “Mai Tais” could also make sense, since a Mai Tai is a cocktail and would fit Drake-type imagery, I still think he means “my taste.”", "annotation_id": "40799791", "url": "https://genius.com/40799791", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Anchor you in my love, do I need a lifeboat? Do we need a lighthouse?", "vault_lines": ["Anchor you in my love, do I need a lifeboat?", "Do we need a lighthouse?"], "meaning": "Drake continues to question his relationship with his girl while playing on the words anchor, lifeboat and lighthouse.\n\n-An anchor is used to stablize a ships position at sea/ocean\n-A lifeboat is used in emergency situations to escape sinking ships etc.\n-A lighthouse is used to help ships navigate the waters, they’re usually placed near rocky shores /harbors.\n\nDrake is basically saying if I make you my main girl (anchor) will this relationship fail meaning will I need a lifeboat to escape (emergency) and do we need guidance/help to navigate our relationship safely (lighthouse)", "annotation_id": "40114583", "url": "https://genius.com/40114583", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Worked me to the white bone", "vault_lines": ["You worked me too hard, G, worked me to the white bone"], "meaning": "This line, and the name sake of the song come from the the idiom “work to the bone” (often phrased as “work your fingers to the bone”). The phrase originated in the mid-1800’s and means to work extremely hard, intensely, and for a long period of time.", "annotation_id": "39942921", "url": "https://genius.com/39942921", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Ridin’ a Clydesdale", "vault_lines": ["Still in design phase, ridin' a Clydesdale"], "meaning": "The Clydesdale is a breed of draught horse which originated in the seventeenth century, and takes its name from the Clydesdale district of Scotland. Iconic from its usage in the Budweiser advertisements, it is also a popular choice for wedding festivities to pull elegant carriages or provide ride-ins for the bride and groom.", "annotation_id": "39942894", "url": "https://genius.com/39942894", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Dubai doesn’t need another Pilates studio", "vault_lines": ["Dubai doesn't need another Pilates studio"], "meaning": "This is a jab at one of Drake’s exes, Baby M, who opened Kure Pilates in Dubai.\n\nBaby M responded by posting a photo with the caption:\n\n@kurepilates is the studio Dubai didn’t know it needed", "annotation_id": "39780600", "url": "https://genius.com/39780600", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Giddy up, cowgirl, Las Vegas timezone What is this, a zoo trip? Spearmint Rhinos", "vault_lines": ["Giddy up, cowgirl, Las Vegas timezone", "What is this, a zoo trip? Spearmint Rhinos"], "meaning": "Drake continues his pattern of referencing strip clubs and nightlife culture, this time name-dropping the adult entertainment chain Spearmint Rhino.\n\nThe line compares the women surrounding him to a chaotic group outing, framing the environment as excessive and crowded. “Zoo trip” works as a dismissive jab, suggesting disorder, spectacle, and people gathering to stare.\n\nSpearmint Rhino is a gentlemen’s club in Las Vegas, tying back into the previous line.", "annotation_id": "39887393", "url": "https://genius.com/39887393", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "I’m the only don you know other than Julio", "vault_lines": ["I'm the only don you know othеr than Julio"], "meaning": "The word Don means as much as Sir. Don Julio is a tequila brand.", "annotation_id": "39792536", "url": "https://genius.com/39792536", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Are you tired of me yet? Tired like Firestone", "vault_lines": ["Are you tired of me yet? Tired like Firestone"], "meaning": "This is a play on words here, as Firestone is a tire company.", "annotation_id": "39791123", "url": "https://genius.com/39791123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Fisher Island does sound a lot better than Sulphur Springs, bro, but", "vault_lines": ["Fisher Island does sound a lot better than Sulphur Springs, bro, but"], "meaning": "Drake contrasts Fisher Island, one of the wealthiest private island communities in the U.S., with Sulphur Springs, a historically lower-income neighborhood in Tampa, Florida.\n\nThis most likely refers to Drake’s ex, Georgia Montour, whom he was rumored to be engaged to. Her father, Jerry Montour, is a Canadian billionaire and longtime CEO and shareholder of Grand River Enterprises, one of Canada’s largest independent Indigenous-owned tobacco manufacturers. He also owns property on the ultra-exclusive Fisher Island, where Drake likely spent time while dating his daughter.\n\nDrake uses the comparison to suggest that his life has moved far beyond humble or rough beginnings, setting extreme wealth against a place associated with poverty and hardship. The trailing “but” hints that, despite the rise, there is still some emotional tension left unresolved.", "annotation_id": "39779812", "url": "https://genius.com/39779812", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "I’m Mr. Told You So I’m not a people-pleaser, bro, I’m the CEO What I charge for a show, they charge for Van Gogh And some people fucked me over, I can’t let it go Problems, they follow me wherever I go", "vault_lines": ["I'm so close, so close", "I'm Mr. Told You So", "I'm not a people-pleaser, bro, I'm the CEO", "What I charge for a show, they charge for Van Gogh", "And some people fucked me over, I can't let it go", "Problems, they follow me wherever I go"], "meaning": "Drake presents himself as someone who no longer seeks approval from others, emphasizing power and control by calling himself the “CEO.”\n\nDrake also is referring to the cost of buying one of Van Gogh’s paintings, and likely talking about how much he would charge for a private show.\n\nAt the same time, the admission that he “can’t let it go” reveals Drakes tendency to dwell on personal conflicts and broken relationships despite his success. The final line adds a darker tone, implying that fame, money, and status have not freed him from emotional struggles or constant problems.", "annotation_id": "39776785", "url": "https://genius.com/39776785", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "White Bone", "line": "Goin’ to see Morgan, cowboy boots, rhinestones", "vault_lines": ["Goin' to see Morgan, cowboy boots, rhinestones"], "meaning": "Referring to Morgan Wallen, a popular country-pop singer in the U.S. who is often associated with a cowboy image.\n\nRhinestones are diamond simulants originally made from rock crystal. Artist Michael Jackson’s glove was covered in Swarovski crystals, a premium type of rhinestone. It also appears on the cover of Drake’s album ICEMAN.", "annotation_id": "39776335", "url": "https://genius.com/39776335", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
