{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Same niggas beakin’, always duckin’ my release dates", "vault_lines": ["Same niggas beakin', always duckin' my release dates"], "meaning": "A play off the bird connection between the toronto slang term “beaking”, which is to talk shit about someone, and ducking.", "annotation_id": "28070990", "url": "https://genius.com/28070990", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "That’s when the phone starts ringin’, like, “Are we straight?”", "vault_lines": ["That's when the phone starts ringin', like, \"Are we straight?\""], "meaning": "Drake is likely referring to when Rick Ross arranged a meeting for the beef between Meek and Drake to be squashed. This is known because in 2016, N.O.R.E. revealed that Drake and Rick Ross met to discuss squashing the beef. This clearly didn’t work because the feud continued later in the later months of the year Drake sent several shots at Meek Mill on More Life. He also expressed his disinterest in being friends with Meek Mill in an interview with DJ Semtex. It seems further attempts to repair the relationship between Meek and Drake were made because in May 2017, Rick Ross went on an interview and said that he arranged a meeting to squash the beef. It appears some kind of agreement was finally able to be made because the feud didn’t continue after that. Drake showed his public support for Meek Mill to be freed from prison by saying “Free Meek Mill…” on his Boy Meets The World Tour. And in December 2017, on Lil Wayne’s remix of Family Feud, he expressed that the beef between him and Meek is over.", "annotation_id": "20892966", "url": "https://genius.com/20892966", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "It’s The Boy, but I’m still the man, come and get your mans", "vault_lines": ["It's The Boy, but I'm still the man, come and get your mans"], "meaning": "Drake is also known as “The Boy”. He had a tour called “The Boy Meets World Tour” back in 2017.\n\n\n\nDrake also has a “The Boy On Tour” Stone Island chain made by Ben Baller.", "annotation_id": "16143904", "url": "https://genius.com/16143904", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Aww, man, fresh up out the sand, February tan", "vault_lines": ["Aww, man, fresh up out the sand, February tan"], "meaning": "Drake is known for his lavish tropical vacations where, even in the colder months, he’s able to maintain a healthy glow.", "annotation_id": "13923842", "url": "https://genius.com/13923842", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Want a lot, can’t have everything, can’t have everything Want a lot, can’t have everything, but I want everything", "vault_lines": ["Want a lot, can't have everything, can't have everything", "Want a lot, can't have everything, but I want everything", "Can't have everything, can't have everything"], "meaning": "Drizzy often ponders about this statement, previously addressing his lust for success on “No Tellin'” from his 2016 mixtape, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late:\n\nYeah, I stay up late at night, thinkin' ‘bout my life\nWant a lot, will I get it all? Ain’t no tellin’\n\nAs well as later within this playlist, on the Thugger-assisted “Ice Melts,” declaring:\n\nThought I had everything, somehow\n\nOn the contrary, Drake has also expressed that he has “everything” as heard on “All Me” featured on his 2013 album, Nothing Was The Same:\n\nGot everything, I got everything\nI cannot complain, I cannot", "annotation_id": "12931042", "url": "https://genius.com/12931042", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Tell your big homie I’m all for goin’ there again He ain’t even die, and I ball with his inheritance All that’s in my account at the Bank of America", "vault_lines": ["Tell your big homie I'm all for goin' there again", "He ain't even die, and I ball with his inheritance", "All that's in my account at the Bank of America"], "meaning": "This is actually a reference to Rick Ross, who sent subliminal shots at Drake on his 2015 track, “Color Money”, which references foreign currency, in this case Canadian money, which comes in multiple colors. So Drake is telling Meek Mill, to tell Ross that he is willing to go to America despite the difference in currency. Drake’s not afraid because he already has an account at the Bank of America, which is one of the largest banks in America. He mentions Meek Mill not dying, but losing his inheritance, since after his famous “Back to Back” diss, it appears that Meek Mill has been less successful.", "annotation_id": "12632617", "url": "https://genius.com/12632617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Tryna cool it all down, who’s callin’ my name? Who’s involved now? Tell me who I gotta down I’ll do a song now, man", "vault_lines": ["Tryna cool it all down, who's callin' my name?", "Who's involved now? Tell me who I gotta down", "I'll do a song now, man"], "meaning": "On “Can’t Have Everything,” Drake reflects on the highly publicized beefs that he has been caught up in in the past.  The tone of the track is that Drake does not enjoy the beefs, but partakes because he sees it as an obligation.\n\nWhen Meek Mill called Drake out for using ghostwriters, Drake responded with two consecutive diss tracks: “Charged Up” and the hit “Back to Back”.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623700698509758464", "annotation_id": "11792783", "url": "https://genius.com/11792783", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Damn, I must be coachin’, ‘cause I’m not playin’ with them Can you not see the difference?", "vault_lines": ["Damn, I must be coachin', 'cause I'm not playin' with them", "Can you not see the difference?"], "meaning": "A coach calls the plays off the court without participating in the game. Similarly, Drake takes a back seat to the rap game, orchestrating and planning events while others fight for their success.\n\nIn 2016, Drake was a celebrity basketball coach during the NBA All-Star Weekend in Toronto, Canada.", "annotation_id": "11599238", "url": "https://genius.com/11599238", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "I don’t stop, man, I’m stuck on go, always hug the road", "vault_lines": ["I don't stop, man, I'm stuck on go, always hug the road"], "meaning": "To “hug the road” means to stay firmly on the road without going off track. Drakes always on the road. Even after Drake finished his North American Summer Sixteen tour, he immediately followed it up with the European Boy Meets World tour months later.", "annotation_id": "11598923", "url": "https://genius.com/11598923", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "All that Drake hysteria 6 side, East side, all that for my area", "vault_lines": ["All that Drake hysteria", "6 side, East side, all that for my area"], "meaning": "The 6 is what Drake calls Toronto because of its area code, 416. In an interview with Jimmy Fallon in 2016, Drake explains that the area he lives in (Toronto) “was once broken up into 6 sections.”\n\nDrake continues to provide for Toronto, shown by the Key to the City he received in February 2016.", "annotation_id": "11572376", "url": "https://genius.com/11572376", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Two-faced niggas back around with the three-face, damn Ol’ triple-double-Russ face, watch-with-the bust face Never-met-the-plug-but-I-rap-about-the-plug face Never-met-myself, I-don’t-remember-who-I-was face", "vault_lines": ["Two-faced niggas back around with the three-face, damn", "Ol' triple-double-Russ face, watch-with-the bust face"], "meaning": "These lines all end with “face.” It’s a shorter version of the phrase, “face ass nigga,” which is a common way to end a sentence when making fun of someone:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/urbanengiish/status/316686662774173697?lang=en\n\nThis is likely a shot at Pusha T. Drake went at him in a similar way on “Two Birds, One Stone.” The beef started when Push dissed Drake on H.G.T.V. Freestyle for using ghostwriters.\n\nDrizzy is once again accusing Pusha T of being a fake drug dealer whose content is limited to cocaine and jewelry. He says his watch has a “bust face,” which is short for “bust down,” or diamond studded. A “plug,” is a person who produces large amounts of drugs. Think Sosa from Scarface. Drake thinks Pusha has fabricated so many stories that he doesn’t know himself anymore.\n\nHe uses the Kevin Durant and Russell Westbrook situation to draw a comparison.  Drake is a Golden State Warriors fan, so he sides with Durant, who chose to leave Oklahoma City Thunder and go play with Warriors. Westbrook is on a rampage against Durant, nearly scoring a triple double when they play against each other but still losing the game.", "annotation_id": "11571421", "url": "https://genius.com/11571421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Started out doin’ college shows, Calipari flow Then I popped like you never seen", "vault_lines": ["Started out doin' college shows, Calipari flow", "Then I popped like you never seen"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out to of the all-time great basketball coaches to describe his come up on college tours to becoming a professional icon. First, he references his good friend, John Calipari, the current head coach at the University of Kentucky, then makes a more subtle reference to San Antonio Spurs head coach Greg Popovich.\n\nDrake’s first headlining concert tour the “Away from Home Tour” took him to various colleges across the United States.\n\nDrake stuck with colleges on his second headlining concert tour, the “Club Paradise Tour,” explaining to Vibe that he “really made this album for the same people that supported…[him]…from day one.‘”\n\nSimilar to Drake, Coach Cal started off his coaching career in college basketball, where he now is a part of the Basketball Hall of Fame and has led Kentucky to 4 Final Fours and a NCAA National Championship in 2012. Drake and Coach Cal have a close relationship—Drake told ESPN First Take that Coach Cal came to his high school graduation when he decided to finish.\n\n\n\nNow, Drake is on the level of Popovich and playing to sold out arenas.", "annotation_id": "11540060", "url": "https://genius.com/11540060", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "I mean, I keep the fuckin’ lights on in the buildin’ Man, my record deal should be five-houndred million, goddamn", "vault_lines": ["I mean, I keep the fuckin' lights on in the buildin'", "Man, my record deal should be five-houndred million, goddamn"], "meaning": "Views, Drake’s 2016 project, was the first album ever to hit a billion streams on Apple Music. When More Life dropped, it was streamed just shy of 90 million times in 24 hours on Apple Music, breaking the single-day streaming record for all music services.\n\nApple reportedly paid $20 million for Views, but Drizzy’s not satisfied.", "annotation_id": "11537628", "url": "https://genius.com/11537628", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "I don’t know, first, you caught the hands, then you took the stand It’s a joke, but you say you real, I don’t understand", "vault_lines": ["I don't know, first, you caught the hands, then you took the stand", "It's a joke, but you say you real, I don't understand"], "meaning": "This is a reference to producer Noel Fisher a.k.a. Detail. Fisher worked with Drake on his 2013 album Nothing Was The Same. He produced “305 to My City” and “Own It,” and also has a writing credit on “Worst Behavior.”\n\nIn June of 2016, Detail sued Drake. He claimed Drake’s right-hand man Chubbs assaulted him and broke his jaw. Allegedly, Drake was retaliating after Detail refused an offer to be an exclusive producer for Drake in 2014.", "annotation_id": "11537446", "url": "https://genius.com/11537446", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "We with everything I went off in the ’16, give me ’17", "vault_lines": [", we with everything", "I went off in the '16, give me '17"], "meaning": "2016 was arguably Drake’s most successful year with countless accomplishments followed by his very successful album. In 2016, Drake earned his first No. 1 record as lead artist with “One Dance,” released Views which went quadruple platinum in less than 11 months, toured across the country for his “Summer Sixteen” tour, and much more.\n\nDrake dropped this line on his Instagram months before More Life.\n\n https://www.instagram.com/p/BOtfVMYDKps/?hl=en", "annotation_id": "11534026", "url": "https://genius.com/11534026", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Y’all fuckin’ hilarious Y’all really think y’all niggas teamin’ up is scarin’ us Y’all niggas is arrogant, y’all sleep at the Sheraton All that shit embarrassin’", "vault_lines": ["Y'all really think y'all niggas teamin' up is scarin' us", "Y'all niggas is arrogant, y'all sleep at the Sheraton", "All that shit embarrassin'"], "meaning": "Drake’s position as one of the highest charting musicians has placed a target on his back, as people who decide to step up and challenge him are thrust into the spotlight.\n\nIndeed, Drake has had numerous altercations with rappers including Meek Mill, Tory Lanez, Joe Budden, Pusha T, and Jay Z.\n\nHowever, Drake has benefited from most of these confrontations, with his beef with Meek Mill resulting in one of the most famous diss tracks of all time and a Grammy Nomination.\n\nSheraton Hotels are a widespread chain of affordable hotels. Drake is saying that despite these rappers attempting to take him down, he’s only doing better, while they are living a lower class life.", "annotation_id": "11532822", "url": "https://genius.com/11532822", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "We evolved, used to think vacation meant Niagara Falls Swear to God, shout to Buffalo, never duckin’ low", "vault_lines": ["We evolved, used to think vacation meant Niagara Falls", "Swear to God, shout to Buffalo, never duckin' low"], "meaning": "With his success, Drake’s worldview has astronomically increased due to the experiences that he has had after achieving fame.\n\nBeing from Toronto, a popular destination for vacations in his area would be Niagara Falls: three large waterfalls on the Canadian-American border, between the province of Ontario and the state of New York.\n\nBuffalo is a city in western New York and is the first metropolis on the American side of the Niagara Falls.", "annotation_id": "11532612", "url": "https://genius.com/11532612", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "When others go low, we go high", "vault_lines": ["When others go low, we go high"], "meaning": "Drake’s mom concludes by using a quote by Michelle Obama to tell him that if Meek Mill, or any other rapper low blows Drake in a song (or tweet), he should not go lower (as he did by dropping “Back to Back”), he should handle the situation with class and be the better man.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623700698509758464\n\nThe words “go low” also act as a smooth transition to the next track.", "annotation_id": "11531761", "url": "https://genius.com/11531761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "[Outro: Sandi Graham] You know, hun, I’m a bit concerned about this negative tone that I’m hearing in your voice these days And I, I can appreciate where your uncertainty stems from And you have reason to question your anxieties and how disillusioned you feel As well as feeling skeptical about who you believe you can trust But that attitude will just hold you back in this life And you’re going to continue to feel alienated Give some thought to this, because I’m confident in you And I know that you can reach your desired destination And accomplish your goals much more quickly without this confrontation that I’m hearing in your tone these days", "vault_lines": ["And I, I can appreciate where your uncertainty stems from", "And you have reason to question your anxieties and how disillusioned you feel", "As well as feeling skeptical about who you believe you can trust", "But that attitude will just hold you back in this life", "And you're going to continue to feel alienated", "Give some thought to this, because I'm confident in you", "And I know that you can reach your desired destination"], "meaning": "Sandi Graham (Drake’s mother) sends a voicemail to Drake saying she is concerned about Drake’s “negative tone” on diss tracks towards Meek Mill, probably referring to his ruthless lyrics on “Back to Back.” Drake is dealing with many trust issues, especially with women, as he says in “Passionfruit.” Sandi talks about how proud she is of Drake in this interview at the Grammys.\n\n\n\nThis is also the perfect segway for the song with Kanye, it speaks on his disappointment with Kanye’s rants at his last Saint Pablo Tour show. She said “when others go low we go high” which Drake did by putting this song on the album even though Kanye took shots.", "annotation_id": "11531688", "url": "https://genius.com/11531688", "state": "accepted", "votes": 242, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "line": "Finally got my mind in a free state Niggas tried to serve me up a cheesesteak I gave them back a clean plate", "vault_lines": ["Finally got my mind in a free state", "Niggas tried to serve me up a cheesesteak", "I gave them back a clean plate"], "meaning": "Drake’s reflecting on his beef with Meek Mill. A “Philly cheesesteak” is a popular beef and cheese sandwich which, as its name implies, originated in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. Meek is from Philly. “Finally got my mind in a free state” also refers to Philadelphia because Pennsylvania was the first state to pass a law to abolish slavery.\n\nIn a February 2017 interview with Dj Semtex, Drake addressed his conflict with Meek:\n\nYou know how good I am at writing music. But you really tried to spin the entire narrative of my career and end my life, and take food from my family and really try to like end it all. You didn’t even do it through music. You just talked or tweeted. That was like sickening to me. I had to really get revenge.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1K8P2iNyvng&feature=youtu.be&t=1m32s", "annotation_id": "11531234", "url": "https://genius.com/11531234", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Have Everything", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 20, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-cant-have-everything-lyrics", "genius_title": "Can’t Have Everything"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Touch down in NY, tear the Mercer up (One more—)", "vault_lines": ["Touch down in NY, tear the Mercer up (One more—)"], "meaning": "The Mercer is a prominent boutique hotel in the SoHo neighborhood of Lower Manhattan, New York City. The upscale hotel is known to be frequented by many celebrities including Drake.", "annotation_id": "27160368", "url": "https://genius.com/27160368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "SF90 rims red like a poker chip (Pussy)", "vault_lines": ["SF90 rims red like a poker chip (Pussy)"], "meaning": "“SF90” refers to sports car model Ferrari SF90 Stradale. 21 states he got the rims (the outer edge of wheels) red like a red poker chip, which are found in all Poker sets.\n\nSF90’s can cost anywhere from $375,000 and above, so 21 is flexing his wealth to be able to afford such luxury.\n\n21 has also previously mentioned the SF90 earlier in the album on “Major Distribution” and on the song “thought i was playing”.\n\nSF90 cost a M, dumped this Glock like Ye did Kim", "annotation_id": "27072637", "url": "https://genius.com/27072637", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Savage got a new stick he wanna dirty up", "vault_lines": ["Savage got a new stick he wanna dirty up"], "meaning": "“Stick” is slang for a gun, especially an automatic rifle. “Dirty up” means use for the first time, as using something for the first time will inevitably cause it to wear and get dirt on it.\n\n Drizzy is saying 21 has a new gun he wants to use for the first time.", "annotation_id": "27072539", "url": "https://genius.com/27072539", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Ayy, known the girl for six months, dinner up at my place But I got these diamonds on my neck, so it’s a blind date", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, known the girl for six months, dinner up at my place", "But I got these diamonds on my neck, so it's a blind date"], "meaning": "Drake’s talking about the clarity of his diamond chain. A high quality cut diamond acts like a mirror, as it reflects light back out. Poor cuts stones “leak” light through the bottom, which makes them look dark or dull.\n\nA “blind date” is a romantic meeting between two complete strangers but Drake is punning on the fact that his chain is so bright, it would make his dinner date go “blind” instead. Moreso, he could also be paying to JAŸ-Z’s line on his October 2002 song, “Bonnie & Clyde”:\n\nThis necklace the reason all my dates been blind dates", "annotation_id": "27068157", "url": "https://genius.com/27068157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Shorty say she graduated, she ain’t learn enough Play your album, track one, ’kay, I heard enough", "vault_lines": ["Shorty say she graduated, she ain't learn еnough", "Play your album, track one, 'kay, I heard enough"], "meaning": "This line—as well as the one earlier in the verse—were speculated to be aimed at Houston rapper Megan Thee Stallion, as she graduated from Texas Southern University with a bachelors degree in health administration in 2021.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/theestallion/status/1452754183124307975?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E1452754183124307975%7Ctwgr%5E8208d15f51770fa509eaffec4070b68b5cc71d06%7Ctwcon%5Es1_&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.edsurge.com%2Fnews%2F2022-01-25-now-one-degree-hotter-megan-thee-stallion-s-graduation-inspires-interest-in-her-hbcu\n\nIn November 2020, Megan released her debut album, Good News. On the opening track, “Shots Fired,” Megan discussed the shooting incident that involved her and Tory Lanez:\n\nImagine niggas lyin' ‘bout shootin’ a real bitch (Huh?)\nJust to save face for rapper niggas you chill with\n\nDrake could be seemingly denouncing Megan’s claims here by saying he “heard enough” but Lil Yachty—a credited writer on Her Loss—explained on an Instagram live that Drake’s verse had no references to the shooting.", "annotation_id": "27060028", "url": "https://genius.com/27060028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Got a fur on in Tampa, got me burnin’ up", "vault_lines": ["Got a fur on in Tampa, got me burnin' up"], "meaning": "Tampa is a city located in Florida. The city has a warm and temperate climate, with daily high temperatures averaging above 86°F.\n\nDrake has visited Tampa before—for example, he toured there with Future in 2016.", "annotation_id": "27059753", "url": "https://genius.com/27059753", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "I just put ’em on a jet, now they all Italian Way I’m dressin’ tell I been to a thousand islands", "vault_lines": ["I just put 'em on a jet, now they all Italian", "Way I'm dressin' tell I been to a thousand islands"], "meaning": "Drake owns a private $185-million Boeing 767 Jet, which he nicknamed “Air Drake.” He was given the jet as a gift from Canadian airline Cargojet in 2019 in exchange for publicity from his followers.\n\n\n\nWith owning the jet, it’s not far-fetched to say Drake travels quite a bit. He also plays on words “dressing” and “thousand islands” as Thousand Island dressing is a salad dressing made with ketchup and mayonnaise.", "annotation_id": "27059559", "url": "https://genius.com/27059559", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "I been fuckin’ on a French bitch, c’est la vie", "vault_lines": ["I been fuckin' on a French bitch, c'est la vie"], "meaning": "“C'est la vie” is French for “that’s life.” Drake is no stranger to being with French women; the mother of Drake’s son, Adonis, is Sophie Brussaux, who was born in Bordeaux, France.\n\nThis isn’t Drake’s first time using French—he also used it on DJ Khaled’s July 2020 song, “GREECE”:\n\nFull speed, je suis ton génie\n\nHailing from Ontario, the French language is considered home to them. Drake has referred to Quebec, which is known for their heavy French language in Canada. On his June 2022 track “Sticky,” he raps:\n\nAyy, two sprinters to Quebec\nChérie, où est mon bec?", "annotation_id": "27057786", "url": "https://genius.com/27057786", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Went from Angell Town Estate to a big estate Prolly woulda had a zombie on me if I woulda stayed (21) Still a caught a case if I woulda stayed (Facts) I been thuggin’ all my life, that’s just how I play it (Facts)", "vault_lines": ["Went from Angell Town Estate to a big estate", "Prolly woulda had a zombie on me if I woulda stayed (21)", "Still a caught a case if I woulda stayed (Facts)", "I been thuggin' all my life, that's just how I play it (Facts)"], "meaning": "Angell Town Estate is a housing estate located in the Brixton area of South West London. Though Savage was born in Newham, East London, he moved to South West London. Legendary London MC Sneakbo hails from the Estate and has popularized the area. Drake also labelled him as an inspiration in the early 2010s.\n\n\n\n21 still has family in the estate, such as his brother Terrell Davis, known in music as TM1way, who was unfortunately stabbed to death in November 2020 near the area.\n\nThe estate is also known to have some of the highest gang violence rates in the city. A “zombie” alludes to a “zombie knife” or a “zombie killer,” a type of knife which have sharp serrated edges and words etched onto them inspired by zombie apocalypse movies. They are vastly popular within London gang violence and has since been banned from being sold and imported.\n\nDue to this “thug” demeanor, even if 21 stayed in London and didn’t move over to Atlanta, he knew he would have gotten caught up in gang violence in the area, much like his late brother did.", "annotation_id": "27054819", "url": "https://genius.com/27054819", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Still gotta see the Gunners win Premier League (21)", "vault_lines": ["Still gotta see the Gunners win Premier League (21)"], "meaning": "21 savage being born in London, supported Arsenal growing up. The English club adopted the name “The Gunners” because of the large cannon on the club badge and the armaments manufacturing roots of the club.\n\n\n\nAt the time of this recording, Arsenal sat 2 points top of the Premier League Table after their best ever start to a season with odds of 6/1 to win the competition", "annotation_id": "27053223", "url": "https://genius.com/27053223", "state": "accepted", "votes": 180, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "This bitch lie ‘bout gettin’ shots, but she still a stallion She don’t even get the joke, but she still smilin’", "vault_lines": ["This bitch lie 'bout gettin' shots, but she still a stallion", "She don't even get the joke, but she still smilin'"], "meaning": "This line said was deemed rather controversial across the internet, as it seemed like Drake was making an apparent implication that Megan Thee Stallion lied about being shot by Tory Lanez on July 12, 2020 through the way of a promiscuous analogy. In actuality, the Toronto rapper is complimenting a woman on her physique, comparing her beauty to that of a stallion. On the other hand, he is confident that her body is fake, implying that she received injections to achieve her ideal look.\n\nMegan Thee Stallion was not happy about this supposed diss. When “Circo Loco” released, she made several tweets clarifying her stance on Drake’s comments, the first of which reading:\n\nStop using my shooting for clout bitch ass Niggas! Since when tf is it cool to joke abt women getting shot ! You niggas especially RAP NIGGAS ARE LAME! Ready to boycott bout shoes and clothes but dog pile on a black woman when she say one of y’all homeboys abused her\n\nLil Yachty, who contributed to numerous tracks on Her Loss clarified on behalf of Drake during an Instagram live on the day of the album’s release, explaining how the bar had nothing to do with the criminal complaint between rappers Tory and Meg and was merely a one-dimensional line about women lying about butt injections. For context, prosecutors alleged that Tory (whose real name is Daystar Peterson) got out of an SUV following an argument with Megan Thee Stallion in Hollywood Hills on July 12, 2020 and inflicted bodily injury on her. Lanez was officially charged with one count of carrying a semiautomatic firearm with another count of carrying a loaded, concealed firearm in a vehicle. According to a video made by Megan Thee Stallion on August 20, 2020, a month after the alleged incident, Tory was not only the perpetrator who had assaulted her, but he had shot her twice in the foot.\n\n\n\nMegan Thee Stallion and Tory Lanez\n\nYachty also commented during the same live video on how he was confident that Drake himself was not going to clear anything up about the line despite it having nothing to do with Megan Thee Stallion.", "annotation_id": "27053071", "url": "https://genius.com/27053071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 162, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Every night late night like I’m Jimmy Fallon Cro shoot from anywhere like he Ray Allen Crodie, turn me up Crodie, turn me up Crodie, turn me up", "vault_lines": ["Crodie, turn me up", "Ayy, Crodie, turn me", "Every night late night like I'm Jimmy Fallon", "Cro shoot from anywhere like he Ray Allen"], "meaning": "Drake likens himself to Jimmy Fallon, the host of the NBC late-night talk show The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon where Drake was a guest back in 2016.\n\n\n\n“Cro” and “crodie” are crip alterations of the term “bro” and “brodie”, respectively, originating from Toronto’s Northside Jane and Finch neighborhood. Drizzy namedrops two-time NBA champion and Hall of Famer Ray Allen, who once held the NBA record for most three-point field goals made.\n\nThis is not the first time that Drake uses the term—on his May 2020 Playboi Carti-assisted “Pain 1993,” he raps:\n\nI love my crodie to death (Codeine)\n\nMoreover, on his December 2019 track “War,” he spits the following line:\n\nWassa, wassa, just like the crodie Pressa, I don’t do no passa", "annotation_id": "27052294", "url": "https://genius.com/27052294", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Feel like seventeen, two Percs, frog eyes", "vault_lines": ["Feel like seventeen, two Percs, frog eyes"], "meaning": "Percocet is a powerful and addictive painkiller. It contains a combination of acetaminophen and oxycodone. One of its many side effects include having wide pupils, which Drake refers to as “frog eyes.”\n\nThis is also a nod to Toronto rapper, 17Duvy—hence “seventeen”—and his January 2021 song, “Southway,” where he made a similar reference:\n\nOff two Percs so I had frog eyes (Had frog eyes)\n\nDrake gave the rapper his co-sign as he followed Duvy on social media a day after the released the song.", "annotation_id": "27052184", "url": "https://genius.com/27052184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "Linking with the opps, bitch, I did that shit for J Prince Bitch, I did it for the mob ties", "vault_lines": ["Linking with the opps, bitch, I did that shit for J Prince", "Bitch, I did it for the mob ties"], "meaning": "On November 16, 2021, Drake and Kanye West met in Toronto to reconcile their relationship after a three-year feud filled with constant shots at each other through music and social media. The meeting was set up by J Prince, the American music executive, mostly known as a co-founder of Rap-A-Lot Records, with the intention of reuniting both on stage to free Larry Hoover, co-founder of Chicago’s Gangster Disciples, who is currently serving six life sentences.\n\nFurther, J Prince was the target of an investigation in 1988 by DEA agent Ernest Howard for involvement in drug distribution, which concluded suspiciously after twelve years. Drake rapped about it on June 2018’s “Mob Ties,” where he also dissed Ye and Pusha T.\n\nYe would respond to these lines via Twitter the morning the album was released, in a confused tone after he talked positively about Drake on his controversial October 16 Drink Champs interview and even promoted Her Loss via Parler with the caption “Gods.”\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1588521039554052096?s=20&t=ii6yAusmfpX0cXS_YfJQ-Q", "annotation_id": "27052170", "url": "https://genius.com/27052170", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Circo Loco", "line": "One more—", "vault_lines": ["Touch down in NY, tear the Mercer up (One more—)", "Me, I'd rather hit 'em up one more time", "Prolly won't see him for some years, when I do, though (One more—)", "All the opps get a bullet on some Oprah shit (One more—)", "One more time, you gotta run the fades"], "meaning": "On the chorus, Drake interpolates lines from Daft Punk’s November 2000 classic, “One More Time.” “Circo Loco” also samples the same song:\n\nOne more time\nWe’re gonna celebrate\nOh yeah, all right\nDon’t stop the dancing\n\n“Head” is slang for oral sex. Notably, as part of the Her Loss promo, Drake shared in a fake interview with Howard Stern that his favorite genre of pornography is oral sex:\n\n[I watch the] highest tier of top givers… Those are the real superstars of the world to me.", "annotation_id": "27052084", "url": "https://genius.com/27052084", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
