{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Spirit", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A hypnotic and alluring chorus alluding to the fact said woman will always be Drakes whether he’s around or not but most importantly even when they’re broken up!", "annotation_id": "28892832", "url": "https://genius.com/28892832", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Spirit", "line": "Puffing purple hash, welcome to my power circle", "vault_lines": ["Puffing purple hash, welcome to my power circle"], "meaning": "Purple hash, also known as purple haze, is a hybrid flavored cannabis strain that has a purple color.\n\nRoss' “power circle” is MMG or Maybach Music Group, his record label, who consists of notable artists such as Wale, Meek Mill, Gunplay and Ross himself. It would also the title of a popular song by Ross released the following year in June 2012.", "annotation_id": "18559865", "url": "https://genius.com/18559865", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Spirit", "line": "My momma still praying for her Rubberband Man", "vault_lines": ["My momma still praying for her Rubberband Man"], "meaning": "A “rubberband man” is someone who can get money with ease. They’re given this name as rubber bands are used to hold large wads of cash together. Ross may also be alluding to T.I’s December 2003 hit, “Rubber Band Man.” Both are good friends and have collaborated many times together.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s5bjt4ySI7M", "annotation_id": "18559785", "url": "https://genius.com/18559785", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Spirit", "line": "I fondle the money, fornicate with a fortune", "vault_lines": ["I fondle the money, fornicate with a fortune"], "meaning": "Fornication is the act of two unmarried people who have sexual intercourse. On Drake’s November 2011 track, “Lord Knows,” Ross also mentions fornication:\n\nMink coats make your woman wanna fornicate (fornicate)", "annotation_id": "18559765", "url": "https://genius.com/18559765", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Spirit", "line": "When them wheels land, Travis Barker, drums playing Tap-dance to my drum roll", "vault_lines": ["When them wheels land, Travis Barker, drums playing", "Tap-dance to my drum roll"], "meaning": "Rozay wants a real-life drum roll played by Blink-182 drummer Travis Barker.  Lest you think Ross is secretly headbanging to “What’s My Age Again”, Barker also works with tons of rappers, and in fact toured with Ross in 2011.  However, the “wheels land” language here is rather thoughtless, as Barker was involved in a 2008 plane crash that left him with serious burns, and killed four others", "annotation_id": "359762", "url": "https://genius.com/359762", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Spirit", "line": "Pimp flows and Screw flows, my shit is sounding like grey tapes Now, let me gon’ hit that cup, one time ’fore a nigga hit that road These days keep going by too fast, so give me anything to make shit go slow", "vault_lines": ["Pimp flows and Screw flows, my shit is sounding like grey tapes", "Now, let me gon' hit that cup, one time 'fore a nigga hit that road", "These days keep going by too fast, so give me anything to make shit go slow"], "meaning": "This section serves as a tribute to deceased Texas rap figures.  Chad “Pimp C” Butler was a member of the legendary Texas rap group UGK.  The group’s remaining member, Bernard “Bun B” Freeman, has taken to championing Drake\n\n\n\nRobert “DJ Screw” Davis, Jr. was another seminal Texas rap figure, associated with the Houston scene.  The DJ’s claim to fame was making mixtapes where he slowed down the music, using a technique that would come to bear his name.  Screw made almost 250 mixtapes before his 2000 passing, and some of the earlier ones were referred to as grey tapes because they were recorded on a Maxell tape similar to the one shown below:\n\n\n\nThe “chopped and screwed” sound, and most of the Houston rap scene, would come to be associated with purple drank, a drink containing codeine and promethazine, whose woozy buzz perfectly matched the slowed-down feel of Screw’s tapes.  The final two lines mention the drank and its buzz as a means Drake has taken to slow down his fast-paced life.", "annotation_id": "359753", "url": "https://genius.com/359753", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Spirit", "line": "Fuck with my young niggas, XO Gang", "vault_lines": ["Fuck with my young niggas, XO Gang"], "meaning": "Drake shouting out the XO team, The Weeknd.", "annotation_id": "358045", "url": "https://genius.com/358045", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Spirit", "line": "Voodoo Child to my new hoes", "vault_lines": ["Voodoo Child to my new hoes"], "meaning": "Drake has been, by his own admission, obsessed with Jimi Hendrix in recent years, even leaving a note on the great guitarist’s grave – his pilgrimage there is pictured below. “Voodoo Chile” and, especially, its reprise “Voodoo Child (Slight Return)”, are well-known Hendrix songs.\n\n\n\nDrake visiting Jimi Hendrix’s gravesite circa September 2010 via October Very Own’s blogspot", "annotation_id": "357985", "url": "https://genius.com/357985", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Free Spirit", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 22, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-free-spirit-lyrics", "genius_title": "Free Spirit"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "It’s Drizzy Thank Me Later coming soon, yeah Just let me live man It’s all I ask, ah Here I go", "vault_lines": ["Thank Me Later coming soon, yeah", "Just let me live man", "It's all I ask, ah"], "meaning": "This song was officially released in 2010 (two years after it appeared on DJ Wristpect’s mixtape), after the massive success of So Far Gone and while he was recording his debut album Thank Me Later. He was just having the best time of his life, period.", "annotation_id": "4128087", "url": "https://genius.com/4128087", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "Little Brother Pooh, ’Te Big doe I see you homie, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Big doe I see you homie, yeah"], "meaning": "Little Brother was an American hip hop group that consisted of rappers Phonte and Big Pooh.\n\nDrake has said a couple times in the past how the group and especially Phonte have been a big influence on his career.\n\nThey collaborated on Don’t You Have a Man from his second mixtape.", "annotation_id": "4128086", "url": "https://genius.com/4128086", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "And Drake was at the club and he balled like no hair", "vault_lines": ["And Drake was at the club and he balled like no hair"], "meaning": "He makes a play on the homophones bald and balled. Drake used a very similar line later on Forever:\n\nIf I was at the club you know I balled, chemo.", "annotation_id": "4128082", "url": "https://genius.com/4128082", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "Oliver’s here and you know me ho", "vault_lines": ["Oliver's here and you know me ho"], "meaning": "Oliver El-Khatib is one of OVO Sound' founders alongside Drake and 40, and is also Drake’s Manager, Creative Director and producer (he’s credited in The Resistance).", "annotation_id": "4128076", "url": "https://genius.com/4128076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "I’m the cash cow front man of OVO", "vault_lines": ["I'm the cash cow front man of OVO"], "meaning": "At the inception of his record label, O.V.O. Sound, Drake was the premier artist—later additions such as PartyNextDoor and P Reign helped carry the load. “Cash cow” is a phrase used to describe anyone or anything that attracts all the revenue for any one entity.", "annotation_id": "2791872", "url": "https://genius.com/2791872", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "His name 40 and his name Future", "vault_lines": ["His name 40 and his name Future"], "meaning": "Drake is shouting out his homeboys. Noah “40” Shebib, the man who has been with Drake since the beginning. Like Drizzy, he’s from Toronto. He produces most of Drake’s instrumentals.\nFuture’s Drake close friend, official DJ and manager.", "annotation_id": "2791784", "url": "https://genius.com/2791784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "But don’t mind him girl, that’s just Freako Please don’t cuss girl, that don’t suit ya", "vault_lines": ["But that's just me though", "But don't mind him girl, that's just Freako", "Please don't cuss girl, that don't suit ya"], "meaning": "Known for always keeping his friends close, Drake is begging a girl’s pardon because his long-time friend, Oliver El-Khatib, is getting a little out of hand. He referred to Oliver as Freako again on the track “Still Fly.”", "annotation_id": "2529056", "url": "https://genius.com/2529056", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "Uh, shout out to DJ Wristpect", "vault_lines": ["Uh, shout out to DJ Wristpect"], "meaning": "This track was originally recorded for DJ Wristpect’s  Bridging the Gap: Vol. 3 Toronto to North Carolina mixtape.", "annotation_id": "429867", "url": "https://genius.com/429867", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "Damn shame am I over-expressing the game Should I hold back on addressing the lame? Cause they becoming obsessed with my name I’m trying to clean up my mess and explain", "vault_lines": ["Damn shame am I over-expressing the game", "Should I hold back on addressing the lame?", "Cause they becoming obsessed with my name", "I'm trying to clean up my mess and explain", "I got the city on my back, get over it"], "meaning": "Feeding off his previous lines, Drake is contemplating his role in this storm of hate he’s begun to receive. He wonders if the jealously is a result of his overbearing presence in the rap game.\n\nMaybe it would be more practical for Drake to refrain from addressing his detractors and take a step back, otherwise he might fuel even more hostility.", "annotation_id": "429836", "url": "https://genius.com/429836", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "And I can see the heart on your sleeve And I know this shit is hard to believe You gone have to get over it", "vault_lines": ["And I can see the heart on your sleeve", "And I know this shit is hard to believe", "You gone have to get over it", "And all you women that I fucked, get over it", "You know you got to hear my records, get over it", "I got the city on my back, get over it"], "meaning": "“Wear your heart on your sleeve,” is a phrase that means to show your true emotions. Drake is likely speaking of an ex lover or friend who is no longer in his life. Now that he is rich and famous people from his past are noticeably regretful about ending their relationship.\n\nDrake is unwilling to amend those old ties and advises he exes to “get over it.”", "annotation_id": "429828", "url": "https://genius.com/429828", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "Living life from a whole different angle Only see the road through the wings of an angel", "vault_lines": ["Living life from a whole different angle", "Only see the road through the wings of an angel"], "meaning": "These lines can be interpreted several ways. One, it could be taken as a reference to The Rolls Royce Phantom, which has a very specific figurine on the front of it (the “spirit of ecstasy”), that looks very similar to an angel, with arms back and wings open.\n\n\n\nDrake blogged (on a now deleted webpage) saying that this is one of the few times he made a recording mistake, and states that he intended to say “Only see the world through the wings of an angel.”\n\nWe’re led to believe he meant that he’s seeing life through a more optimistic, worldly perspective that was previously dismissed. Also, his self-declared nickname is “Young Angel” in other songs.", "annotation_id": "429811", "url": "https://genius.com/429811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "And ever since I’ve been messing with Wayne People getting jealous I can see they expressions have changed They seemed to love me when this shit was going nowhere And now they got a bunch of feelings that they won’t share They just give me the fuck it I’m broke stare", "vault_lines": ["And ever since I've been messing with Wayne", "People getting jealous I can see they expressions have changed", "They seemed to love me when this shit was going nowhere", "And now they got a bunch of feelings that they won't share", "They just give me the fuck it I'm broke stare"], "meaning": "One of Drake’s biggest criticism’s is his affiliation with Young Money boss Lil Wayne, which ironically plays a huge role in his success. Ever since he met up with Wayne (through Jas Prince in 2008) and starting recording tracks, the people who once endeared him are now jealous of his opportunities.", "annotation_id": "429805", "url": "https://genius.com/429805", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "And boy you know my progression insane", "vault_lines": ["And boy you know my progression insane"], "meaning": "In 2006 Drake dropped Room For Improvement and followed up with Comeback Season. in 2007, and So Far Gone in 2008. The second featured his first hit single, “Replacement Girl (Ft. Trey Songz),” and the third is now considered a modern classic. Many would consider this two year span his leap from local Toronto artist to international rap star. Not many can advance so far in such short time.", "annotation_id": "429791", "url": "https://genius.com/429791", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "Tay you know, you know I told you, many times you my favorite rapper right? So I’mma start this off like you would do it", "vault_lines": ["Tay you know, you know I told you, many times you my favorite rapper right?", "So I'mma start this off like you would do it"], "meaning": "Here Drake shouts out his friend Phonte of the R&B group Foreign Exchange,, claiming this is his favorite rapper even though he previously mentioned Andre 3K and Kanye as idols.", "annotation_id": "429739", "url": "https://genius.com/429739", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "But, hah, ask Apple how I get it though I’ll be on the phone with a bitch that you will never know", "vault_lines": ["But, hah, ask Apple how I get it though", "I'll be on the phone with a bitch that you will never know"], "meaning": "Drake makes a play on Apple’s iconic stylization of their product names — iPod, iMac, iPhone  etc.\n\nAs top selling artist on Apple’s music platform iTunes, the company knows first hand how big Drake is doing it, writing him big royalty checks seemingly every month.\n\nThis line would prove to be somewhat prophetic, as Drake and Apple would go on to create a partnership, for which Drake was reportedly paid $19 million.", "annotation_id": "163260", "url": "https://genius.com/163260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "I am not doing features now, no means no And the hate starts building the more we grow", "vault_lines": ["I am not doing features now, no means no", "And the hate starts building the more we grow"], "meaning": "As Drake’s rises to fame he is becoming more selective with who he works with. Very reminiscent of his line in “Closer”:\n\nSame rappers that’s all in ya face sayin' Drake is wack\n Are checkin' my availability just to make a track\n\nThose who previously criticized him now want his help to make hits. When he turns them down they become angry and spiteful.", "annotation_id": "112945", "url": "https://genius.com/112945", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "I don’t get bitter, I just get better", "vault_lines": ["I don't get bitter, I just get better"], "meaning": "He does not waste time hating anyone or the industry. Instead he just improves (hence his first mixtape is entitled Room For Improvement)", "annotation_id": "81682", "url": "https://genius.com/81682", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Get Over It", "line": "My engineer is the best in the game", "vault_lines": ["My engineer is the best in the game"], "meaning": "40, part of OVO, and Drake’s main engineer/producer. 40 has a large production history with alot of hit songs.", "annotation_id": "72437", "url": "https://genius.com/72437", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
