{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "But girl, you ain’t the only one that’s tryna be the only one, uh At least I admit that, if you get that, and you with that Then, fuck, let’s get it then", "vault_lines": ["Well, fuck, let's get it then", "But girl, you ain't the only one that's tryna be the only one, uh", "At least I admit that, if you get that, and you with that", "Then, fuck, let's get it then"], "meaning": "he is saying that all these woman want to be exclusive with drizzy and they should be thankful that he is atleast telling them the truth that ‘they aint the only one’. following this if they are okay that there are other woman then he doesnt mind if they have ‘fun’", "annotation_id": "39173131", "url": "https://genius.com/39173131", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Women give it to me like they owe me one", "vault_lines": ["These days, women give it to me like they owe me one"], "meaning": "suggesting that woman are always have sex with him like they are in debt to him –> possibly alluding to his high status", "annotation_id": "39173112", "url": "https://genius.com/39173112", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Because it’s hard to say no (Say no) Yeah, it gets hard to say no", "vault_lines": ["Because it's hard to say no (Say no)", "Yeah, it gets hard to say no"], "meaning": "Since Drake has become rich, he has found it increasingly difficult to say no to the people around him. Whether it be people asking him for money or asking him to try new drugs, he’s always finding himself giving the same answer: YES!", "annotation_id": "36139914", "url": "https://genius.com/36139914", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Believe that", "vault_lines": [", nigga (Believe that"], "meaning": "Around this time, “believe that” was one of Birdman’s most frequently-used catchphrases, appearing in songs such as Why U Mad.", "annotation_id": "35138422", "url": "https://genius.com/35138422", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Drizzy, your turn, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Drizzy, your turn, nigga"], "meaning": "Birdman, who stood to directly profit from Drake’s success as a Lil Wayne signing, frequently hyped Drake up as the next big thing in hip hop- a prediction that would come correct.", "annotation_id": "35138412", "url": "https://genius.com/35138412", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "YMCMB", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, that YMCMB shit, nigga"], "meaning": "Young Money Cash Money Billionaires", "annotation_id": "33061771", "url": "https://genius.com/33061771", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Should we make this one a double? You ain’t even gotta ask, ah", "vault_lines": ["Should we make this one a double? You ain't even gotta ask, ah"], "meaning": "“Make this one a double” refers to stacking two Styrofoam cups to hold the drank, or “double-cupping” it. Drake has admitted to sipping lean on occasion.", "annotation_id": "32160649", "url": "https://genius.com/32160649", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Should we make this one a double? You ain’t even gotta ask, ah", "vault_lines": ["Should we make this one a double? You ain't even gotta ask, ah"], "meaning": "“Make this one a double” refers to stacking two Styrofoam cups to hold the drank, or “double-cupping” it. Drake has admitted to sipping lean on occasion.", "annotation_id": "32160631", "url": "https://genius.com/32160631", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "And the line around the corner, it’s my motherfuckin’ time", "vault_lines": ["And the line around the corner, it's my motherfuckin' time"], "meaning": "Drake is in high demand, so the line for his show stretches around the street corner of the venue. Take Care was promoted with the “Club Paradise” tour, which grossed $42,600,000, and was the highest-grossing hip-hop tour of the year, so it is safe to say that the line preceding the show would definitely stretch around the corner.\n\nDrake’s 2016 “Summer Sixteen Tour” grossed $84,300,000 and became the highest-grossing hip-hop tour of all time.", "annotation_id": "11791912", "url": "https://genius.com/11791912", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "In the presidential, do you like your new room?", "vault_lines": ["In the presidential, do you like your new room?"], "meaning": "A hotel’s “Presidential suite” has been the meme resting place for rappers at least since Jay-Z’s 1996 nod to his “residential for the weekend”.", "annotation_id": "439365", "url": "https://genius.com/439365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "She says, “You’re such a dog,” I say, “You’re such a bone”", "vault_lines": ["She says, \"You're such a dog,\" I say, \"You're such a bone\""], "meaning": "She is calling him a ‘dog,’ for those lovely bitches, dogs have negative connotations associated with them. In this context she is calling him a womanizer who tries to work his way into some ladypants.\n\nBut he’s calling her a ‘bone,’ which alludes to the phrase “give a dog a bone.” In this situation he is saying to give him a break, the only reason he seems like a dog is because she is so god damn sexy.\n\nHis response “you’re such a bone” might be a play on how dogs love bones. He’s embracing the dog title and telling her as a response to being called such by calling her a bone, so he still wants to get down.\n\n“Boning” is a euphemism for sex so he might be  asking for sex", "annotation_id": "436195", "url": "https://genius.com/436195", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Always presidential and tonight’s no blue moon", "vault_lines": ["Always presidential and tonight's no blue moon"], "meaning": "Drake is always on top of his game, it’s not just a once in a blue moon occurrence. According to Google, once in a blue moon = 1.16699016 × 10-8 hertz\n\nbtw, presidential rooms are usually the nicest suite in the hotel. the more you know.", "annotation_id": "435380", "url": "https://genius.com/435380", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Heard Nicki just bought a brand new crib Goddamn, man, she’s beauty and the beast, Lord", "vault_lines": ["Heard Nicki just bought a brand new crib", "Goddamn, man, she's beauty and the beast, Lord"], "meaning": "Nicki Minaj is known for her over the top fashion sense and multiple personalities\n\n\n\nDrake compliments Nicki on her beauty while at the same time respecting her ‘beast mode’ in the way she’s become rich enough to buy a new crib. This line can also be perceived as “she’s booty and the beast.” Nicki Minaj is known for having a rather plump backside.\n\nSee Nicki go into beast mode here.", "annotation_id": "434314", "url": "https://genius.com/434314", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Since I saw Aaliyah’s precious life go too soon She deserve the credit for how I’m about to get it", "vault_lines": ["Since I saw Aaliyah's precious life go too soon", "She deserve the credit for how I'm about to get it"], "meaning": "Aaliyah was a popular R&B singer who died in 2001 as a result of a plane crash.\n\n\n\nDrake frequently lists Aaliyah as one of his influences, and wrote a posthumous mash note to her\n\nAaliyah serves as a reminder of that YOLO. (you only live once)\nHe also has a tattoo of her on his back.", "annotation_id": "434309", "url": "https://genius.com/434309", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "line": "Never thoughts of suicide, I’m too alive", "vault_lines": ["Never thoughts of suicide, I'm too alive"], "meaning": "Drizzy is too high on his fame and fortune to want to end his own life.\n\n\n\nIt questions the depression that many other rappers discuss (we are looking at you OFWGKTA etc.) Drake is on top of the world, how can he kill himself?\n\nA possible specific allusion to Biggie’s “Suicidal Thoughts”, seeing as the next line contains “do or die” (Bedford-Stuyvesent, the birthplace of B.I.G., is known as “Do Or Die Bed-Sty”, or just the “Do Or Die”).", "annotation_id": "434302", "url": "https://genius.com/434302", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "We’ll Be Fine", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 20, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-well-be-fine-lyrics", "genius_title": "We’ll Be Fine"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "Oh, the idea is fun (Which one?) Oh, the idea is fun (Which one?) Oh, the idea is so fun every time", "vault_lines": ["Oh, the idea is so fun every time", "Oh, the idea is fun (Which one?)"], "meaning": "Building off the last couple lines. It is clear they both love the idea of true romance but Drake and her both know that deep down it’ll never work", "annotation_id": "40959231", "url": "https://genius.com/40959231", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "Every conversation starts with, “This time will be different?” Oh", "vault_lines": ["How every conversation starts with, \"This time will be different?\" Oh"], "meaning": "toxic relation, they come back to eachtoher everytime but always get the same ending and try to convince their self by saying “this time will be different”", "annotation_id": "36086071", "url": "https://genius.com/36086071", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "How you talk all this shit and we still lack communication?", "vault_lines": ["How you talk all this shit and we still lack communication?"], "meaning": "the girl that he’s talking about is basically arguing all the time whit him so they talk but its still a lack of communication because they don’t talk things out\n\nor\n\nthe girl talks behind his back and barely speaks to him", "annotation_id": "36085992", "url": "https://genius.com/36085992", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "I’ll be there, just swangin’ I’ll be there, just swangin’", "vault_lines": ["I'll be here, just swangin'", "I'll be there, just swangin'"], "meaning": "Swanging refers to swerving from lane to lane slowly while listening to load music.", "annotation_id": "34624787", "url": "https://genius.com/34624787", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "I’m on the road right now, swangin’, girl", "vault_lines": ["I'm on the road right now, swangin', girl"], "meaning": "This is a reference to a popular driving style in Houston, where the driver swerves from lane to lane on a highway: hailed ‘Swanging’.", "annotation_id": "34005798", "url": "https://genius.com/34005798", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "Wish you would learn to love people and use things And not the other way around", "vault_lines": ["Wish you would learn to love people and use things", "And not the other way around"], "meaning": "Drake believes and wishes this woman would prioritise caring for and connecting with others rather than accumulating and placing importance on objects. When people “love things and use people,” they might exploit or neglect relationships in pursuit of material gain, leading to a less fulfilling and potentially harmful life.\n\nThis woman also may simply be using Drake (a person) and his wealth as a tool to gratify her love for material desires (things), playing on the classic gold digger narrative.\n\nIn an interview with Vibe Magazine, Drake revealed he wrote this bar with Kenza Samir, who has had multiple writing credits throughout Drizzy’s discography:\n\nWho helped me write on ‘Connect’ is this girl Kenza. She’s a great girl and a phenomenal poetry writer. We just sit together and come up with the best way to say things. Actually, me and her did [the lyric] ‘love people and use things and not the other way around.’ It’s cool to get another creative mind in there, just someone who’s thinking solely about the words and not the melodies and placement. It’s nice to read her poetry sometimes, I’ll take from that.\n\nKenza, Nebby and Drake out in Toronto in 2015.", "annotation_id": "31725577", "url": "https://genius.com/31725577", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "Same city, same friends, if you’re lookin’ for me", "vault_lines": ["Same city, same friends, if you're lookin' for me", "Same city, same friends, if you're lookin'"], "meaning": "Aubrey “Drake” Graham was born in Toronto, Ontario, Canada. He’s been very clear in the past that he keeps his circle tight.\n\n\n\nDrake is well known for having multiple residences in the city over the years and spending much of his free time there.\n\nIn 2019, he finished building one of the most expensive estates in Canada on the Bridle Path, an affluent neighbourhood in Toronto. He refers to this mansion as “The Embassy.”\n\nAdditionally, many of Drake’s closest associates from his early days in Toronto are now official business partners on his music and fashion label, October’s Very Own, aka OVO.", "annotation_id": "25210659", "url": "https://genius.com/25210659", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Swangin' is Houston slang for veering slowly in a car – usually a SLAB (slow low and bangin') to be specific. Drizzy frequently shows love to H-Town (e.g. see “Houstatlantavegas”), even calling it his second home.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=94niVnjatq0", "annotation_id": "2288350", "url": "https://genius.com/2288350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "There’s three balls and a strike Here’s the pitch It’s belted deep to center Revere goes back, jumps at the wall, and it’s gone!", "vault_lines": ["There's three balls and a strike", "It's belted deep to center", "Revere goes back, jumps at the wall, and it's gone!"], "meaning": "This intro sets up the baseball metaphors throughout the song. Drake explained in an interview with the self-proclaimed GOAT of Hip Hop journalism Elliott Wilson how the intro came about:\n\nI had a sample in the beginning of it and I had to bring this baseball announcer in to re-cut the sample for me.\n\nThis is a call from a Toronto Blue Jays game, Toronto being Drake’s hometown.", "annotation_id": "2244864", "url": "https://genius.com/2244864", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "At least we try for home run every time", "vault_lines": ["At least we try for home run every time"], "meaning": "Every time Drake and this girl see each other, they try to conduct the full sexual act.\n\nThe term “home run” is a famous baseball metaphor for sex, just like:\n\n\n\nFirst base – Mouth-to-mouth kiss; French kissing\n\nSecond base – Kissing and touching erogenous zones\n\n\nStrikeout – Failure to engage in any form of sexual activity\n\n\n\n…etc\n\n\n\nBut, it’s more than that. This isn’t one of Drake’s songs so much about sex, as it is about love. Drake is saying that even though they can never make the relationship work, they at least try for the best every time. This leads to the hook, where swangin' is a metaphor for them constantly “swinging away,” trying for the home run of a perfect relationship but striking out every time.\n\nThis line also ties in with the Shawn Lawrence sample at the beginning of the song about a home run. He uses the “home run” to conclude the first verse, meanwhile matching it with the interlude, for consistency.", "annotation_id": "2234190", "url": "https://genius.com/2234190", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Throughout the second verse and much of the rest of the song, the following sample from “Swang” by Trae and Fat Pat may be heard in the background:\n\nSwang and I swang and I swang to the left\nPop-pop my trunk and dip dip dip", "annotation_id": "2218316", "url": "https://genius.com/2218316", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "Summer is comin’, know you could feel it", "vault_lines": ["Summer is comin', know you could feel it"], "meaning": "Summer is coming and Drake can feel the heat since there’s more crime in the warm months. (Double entendre since it’s literally hot in the summer)\n\nThis could also be a reference to Summer from “From Time”", "annotation_id": "2217809", "url": "https://genius.com/2217809", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "Twenty on pump, whatever, I ain’t got enough to fill it", "vault_lines": ["Twenty on pump, whatever, I ain't got enough to fill it"], "meaning": "As a highschooler driving his uncle’s car, Drake’s not exactly in the position to drop a bunch of cash filling the tank.\n\nEver since the gas prices began to skyrocket, finally exceeding $1.00 per litre in 2005 (around Toronto), it became common practice to only drop $20 on gas at time – always hoping the prices would eventually go down…\n\n\n\nSpoiler alert: they never did.\n\nThis line is interestingly similar to one from ‘Closer’ off Drake’s mixtape Comeback Season, back when his schedule really was as flexible as she is.", "annotation_id": "2214428", "url": "https://genius.com/2214428", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "I take Eglinton to 401 East and Exit at Markham Road in the East End Where all the pretty girls are sleepin’", "vault_lines": ["I take Eglinton to 401 East and", "Exit at Markham Road in the East End", "Where all the pretty girls are sleepin'"], "meaning": "Drake spent part of his adolescence living in Forest Hill, a wealthy neighbourhood in Toronto intersecting Eglinton and Bathurst.\n\n\n\nHe’s describing the route he would take, heading east to Scarborough where he’d meet up with his girl.\n\nDrake also mentions in “The Motion” that most of his friends are in the East side of the city; it’s where he would spend most of his time.", "annotation_id": "2214144", "url": "https://genius.com/2214144", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "Swangin’, eyes closed, just swangin’", "vault_lines": ["Swangin', eyes closed, just swangin'"], "meaning": "Drake is making a baseball reference, “eyes closed just swangin” as a desperate attempt to make the relationship work. This can be drawn from the baseball game commentary soundbite in the background as the song begins.\n\n\n\nSwanging is also a reference to the popular style of driving in Houston, a city we all know Drake is quite fond of.", "annotation_id": "2213561", "url": "https://genius.com/2213561", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "line": "My uncle say you drive my whip like it’s yours", "vault_lines": ["My uncle say you drive my whip like it's yours"], "meaning": "Drake used to drive around his uncle’s car before he could afford to dance around in the snow next to fancy 2014 Bentley GTC. He discusses this memory often:\n\nAnd my uncle calling me like “Where ya at?\nI gave you the keys told ya bring it right back”\n-“Started From The Bottom”\n\nHe discusses the same memory in “Underdog” as well.\n\nAnother one of the many references to the “It’s yours” theme.", "annotation_id": "2213343", "url": "https://genius.com/2213343", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Connect", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-connect-lyrics", "genius_title": "Connect"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "[Produced by The Fam; co-produced by Boi-1da]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The sample was one of my favorite songs to begin with! I  always loved that particular part in the song when Lauryn broke it down! So grabbing that was easy! I slowed the sample down but pitched it up a bit. That was my first track! Next is what I feel took the track to the next level! The Synth Bass. I put it down 1st take! Looked back at Duck like did you hear that shit? He agreed it was stupid! The way they sung over the synth bass just immediately grabbed us! So next Ducko goes through some effects on the sample track and sets it off with a sidechain sounding effect! I came back and threw some vocal chants in! Theres a special combo of effects i like to use that give it the filtered grungy feel! Feel like those were key to! Duck found this percussion loop and threw it on the next track! I came back and changed the pitch to match the key of the sample! and threw in the open hat on the 2 and 4! we stepped back and just listened for a few hours! I immediately sent it to Boi1da and you have to understand the genius of this guy as well! originally the track was a little slower but he sped it up and threw those crazy ass drums on there! The swing of the drums were key in this beat too I feel! and thats how this beat came together! He sent it back to us and we knew it was going to be major!", "annotation_id": "3030387", "url": "https://genius.com/3030387", "state": "verified", "votes": 107, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Too worried ‘bout bitches and fashion, they go missin’ in action", "vault_lines": ["Too worried 'bout bitches and fashion, they go missin' in action"], "meaning": "Drake sees other rappers who were trying to be the man BUT now they are M.I.A because they seem too caught up in chasing women and partaking in the fashion industry!\n\nAlthough many people seem to assume that this line is referring to Drake’s idol, Kanye West, or even A$AP Rocky, there aren’t enough context clues to really point out one specific target of this line. In an interview with The Breakfast Club, though, Kanye admitted he felt the line was directed at him.", "annotation_id": "3023576", "url": "https://genius.com/3023576", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Get us donuts and coffee, we’ll wait for him in the lobby", "vault_lines": ["Get us donuts and coffee, we'll wait for him in the lobby"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that the Somalis are telling him to get them some donuts and coffee as they wait for a while. Basically, telling Drake, they got his back and all they need is some donuts and coffee as they wait in the lobby for the person(s) that they are plotting on.", "annotation_id": "3016750", "url": "https://genius.com/3016750", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "SZN", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "TOPSZN is OB O'Brien and OVORyan’s regime; it’s name originating from a strain of weed. This regime consists of the two, Drake, Oliver, Niko, 40, and as of recent, Johnny Manziel.\n\n\n\nCheck out the OVO tattoo on his wrist", "annotation_id": "3016461", "url": "https://genius.com/3016461", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "That boy know that they shittin’ on you, and they can’t get past you You should’ve followed all my moves, you won’t realize ’til after", "vault_lines": ["That boy know that they shittin' on you, and they can't get past you", "You should've followed all my moves, you won't realize 'til after"], "meaning": "Drizzy seems to be referring to himself as “that boy” at this point in the song. He’s speaking on how he’s shitting on his competition right now and no one can get past him\n\nAs Drake’s current frenemy is fond of saying, “Don’t follow me, young'un, follow my moves”", "annotation_id": "3015607", "url": "https://genius.com/3015607", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "You know it’s real when your niggas’ll take the fall for you", "vault_lines": ["You know it's real when your niggas'll take the fall for you"], "meaning": "Drizzy’s got friends that’ll do time for crimes for him, or otherwise “take the blame” for him. Drake has made the line, “so I know it’s real” very popular through his song, Free Spirit with Rick Ross where he said:\n\nTat my name on you so I know it’s real\nTat my fuckin name on you so I know it’s real\n\nThis time Drizzy doesn’t need evidence to know it’s real. Cause it’s his motherfuckin' boys!\n\n\n\nThis line also could tie into the next two lines about the eggs. When police entered the house of Justin Bieber (who Drake has written songs for, as well as collaborated with on Bieber’s Right Here – Bieber also remixed Drizzy’s Trust Issues) after he had allegedly egged some houses, they found what they believed to be cocaine and later Ecstasy and Xanax. Rapper Lil Za was arrested for these and many believe he was covering up or taking the blame for Bieber.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7aXeoR7677Q", "annotation_id": "3015599", "url": "https://genius.com/3015599", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "You know I had to do it for ya", "vault_lines": ["Man, you know I had to—", "Aww man, you know I had to do it for you", "I mean, for real, girl you know I had to do it for ya", "You know I had to do it for ya", "Man, I had to do it for ya"], "meaning": "Many of Drake’s fans were sure expecting a shot back at Jay-Z after an abrupt exchange of shots.  It was often believed Drake would never respond to disses, but this is definitely one for the books!", "annotation_id": "3015491", "url": "https://genius.com/3015491", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "[Outro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s close friend and fellow OVO and SZN member, OB O'Brien, supplies the outro with a remix and mash of comments made by Mike Gundy and Dan Hawkins. This outro is only on the non-streaming version of the song.\n\nThat’s why I don’t read the news, it’s garbage\nAnd the editor that wrote the thing, he’s garbage\nGot guys on my back about schedulin', saying it’s too tough\nWell, guess what?\nIt’s Division-1 football! It’s the SEC!\nHeck, you pledged, didn’t ya?\nGo play intramurals, brother, go play intramurals\n\nThe 1st 2 lines are from this video https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aoMmbUmKN0E\n\nThis could be a critique on the way Johnny Manziel’s every move gets covered. For example, when Manziel was sent home from the Manning Passing Camp where he was participating as a counselor. Ordinarily, this would barely qualify as news but the sports media made a big deal about it simply because it was Johnny Manziel. So this Gundy interpretation could be saying, “these Manziel stories aren’t even news, it’s just garbage…and the people who are covering these sensationalized stories are garbage”\n\nThe rest is a voiceover of OB O'Brien impersonating Dan Hawkins (which starts at 0:44) referring to Texas A&M, the university that Johnny Manziel was quarterback for.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_detailpage&v=je-gpy6Y_UA#t=44\n\nNotice that A&M is in the SEC compared to the BIG-12.\n\nIn relation to Drake’s beef/drama, this is a big metaphor about how Jay Z, fans, and members of the media are blowing his remarks (particularly in his recent Rolling Stone article) out of proportion. He doesn’t pay attention to it and in essence calls out rappers/musicians to stick their true calling, music.\n\nAlso, Drake had beef with Rolling Stone because the main editor took out Drake as the cover and printed things that weren’t supposed to be included in the issue. Read more about that here.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/statuses/433971541622001664", "annotation_id": "3015439", "url": "https://genius.com/3015439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "See whatever I say goes, I play like I’m on ’roids, no Canseco", "vault_lines": ["See whatever I say goes, I play like I'm on 'roids, no Canseco"], "meaning": "Jose Canseco is a former MLB player who was busted for steroid use. Jose Canseco also suffered from roid rage, note his legal issues. Canseco also ran for Mayor of Toronto, which is interesting since Drake is from Toronto.\n\nDrake utilizes a simile in this line, comparing his music to an athlete who is using steroids. An athlete using steroids can be a lethal force, and their performance is usually higher than the average player not on any kind of substance. Obviously, Drake gets the point across that he isn’t average in any way, his music is great. Also, Drake uses the steroid reference to go along with the sports theme of the song.\n\nNotice how Drake says “No Canseco” at the end of the line. This alludes to the fact that Drake is destroying people with his natural talent alone, he doesn’t need to cheat or use outside help.\n\n\n\nWonder how Jose feels about this one?\n\nhttps://twitter.com/JoseCanseco/statuses/451477401449418752\nhttps://twitter.com/ModelLeila/statuses/451431657019420672", "annotation_id": "3015419", "url": "https://genius.com/3015419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Sometimes I laugh with God about how you can’t stop me I’m his darkest angel probably but he still got me", "vault_lines": ["Sometimes I laugh with God about how you can't stop me", "I'm his darkest angel probably but he still got me"], "meaning": "Reference to the story of the angel, Lucifer. In The Bible, Lucifer fell and became Satan, the darkest angel. Drake also refers to himself as Young Angel from time to time.\n\n\n\nA possible reference to Jay Z, as he’s known as Hov (originated from Jehovah, the Hebrew name for God).\n\nDrizzy let’s Jay know that he can’t stop his strive for greatness with a little jab, he then adds on that he’s a product of Jay but definitely Hov’s biggest competition at the same time. The final part of the line depicts that Jay’s still maintaining a friendship with Drake despite the back and forth mockery as of late.", "annotation_id": "3015411", "url": "https://genius.com/3015411", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "And then you never notice they missin’", "vault_lines": ["And then you never notice they missin'"], "meaning": "Drake could care less about the competition and whether or not they are missing (or becoming irrelevant) because he already LEADS the rap game, so he doesn’t care to look behind to see if anybody following his path is MIA.", "annotation_id": "3015391", "url": "https://genius.com/3015391", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "You know I had to do it for ya You know I had to do it for ya Aww man, aww man, aww man Man, you know I had to—", "vault_lines": ["Man, you know I had to—", "Aww man, you know I had to do it for you", "I mean, for real, girl you know I had to do it for ya", "You know I had to do it for ya", "Man, I had to do it for ya", "Aww man, aww man, aww man"], "meaning": "If you couldn’t tell from the beginning of the hook, Drake is tight with Johnny Manziel and Andrew Wiggins.\n\n\n\nWith the NBA and NFL drafts coming up in early May, it’s only right that Drake drops this banger for Manziel/Wiggins and builds up the already-ridiculous hype both have acquired through their amazing college careers.", "annotation_id": "3015383", "url": "https://genius.com/3015383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "I think I’m on my eighth flow, just watch me paint flow", "vault_lines": ["I think I'm on my eighth flow, just watch me paint flow"], "meaning": "He’s literally on his eighth flow since he’s known for switching it up a lot to remain versatile.\n\n\n\nHe then leads in his next line by telling everyone to watch him paint flow. He’s a music artist, but he’s comparing himself to a painter in the way that he carefully constructs each line to compliment each other and craft the perfect track. As if that weren’t enough, he does just that by speaking on art in the next line, which was a diss aimed at Jay-Z for his line on the “We Made It” Freestyle:\n\nSorry Mrs. Drizzy for so much art talk\nSilly me rappin' ‘bout shit that I really bought", "annotation_id": "3015379", "url": "https://genius.com/3015379", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Signin’ off on more deals than a lawyer with a heavy case load", "vault_lines": ["Signin' off on more deals than a lawyer with a heavy case load"], "meaning": "Drake signed with Young Money Entertainment in 2009. In 2010 Drake signed to Birdman’s, Cash Money Records, which is under Republic Records.\n\n\n\nIn addition!\n\nDrake also has his own label, OVO Sound whose releases are distributed by Warner Bros. Records.\n\nDrake does indeed sign more deals than a lawyer with a heavy case load…\n\n\n\nThis was also addressed by Pusha T as a diss towards drake on Exodus 23:1 :\n\nYou signed to one nigga that signed to another nigga\nThat’s signed to three niggas, now that’s bad luck\n\n\n\nAlso, likely a reference to his many endorsement deals which include:\n\nSprite, Virgin America, and most recently the Toronto Raptors\n\nhttp://youtu.be/YPxEiukH7Aw\n\nAnd..\n\nNike/Jordan\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Jumpman23/statuses/408176928982433792\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAND\n\nCalvin Klein…", "annotation_id": "3015372", "url": "https://genius.com/3015372", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "And I gotta tell him chill,", "vault_lines": ["And I gotta tell him chill,"], "meaning": "He has to tell the goons not to kill this man, he’s too high profile to be involved in a murder case.", "annotation_id": "3015367", "url": "https://genius.com/3015367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Dog, what the fuck happen to so-and-so, where did they go?", "vault_lines": ["Dog, what the fuck happen to so-and-so, where did they go?"], "meaning": "The rap game is constantly moving from and embracing rapper to rapper and right now it’s Drake’s turn. Many rappers have taken the top spot where they were the head of the rap game and now they aren’t selling, and no one is paying attention to them, but this won’t happen to Drizzy.\n\nThis could also be a subtle jab at Kendrick Lamar. Drake addresses Kendrick’s Control verse similarly here:\n\nhttp://youtu.be/L7BCxCjxtgg?t=21m5s", "annotation_id": "3015360", "url": "https://genius.com/3015360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Know some Somalis that say we got it Wallahi", "vault_lines": ["Know some Somalis that say we got it Wallahi"], "meaning": "There is a large Somali population in Toronto, so Drake probably has some Somali friends. And Wallahi is a term Somalis are known to overuse meaning “I swear to God (Allah)”. Drake’s Somali friends are swearing to him they will assassinate his competition for him, but as he articulates in later lines, he doesn’t want them to do it.\n\n\n\nCould be a reference to his Somali-Canadian DJ Future The Prince.", "annotation_id": "3015359", "url": "https://genius.com/3015359", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "How the game turn into the Drake show?", "vault_lines": ["How the game turn into the Drake show?"], "meaning": "The rap game is all about Drake right now. He is saying there isn’t much competition for him now and all eyes are on him.\n\n\n\nThis could also be a reference to Eminem’s album The Eminem Show which was released around the time when Eminem was running the game.", "annotation_id": "3015358", "url": "https://genius.com/3015358", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Man that boy growin’ up quick, that boy know he the shit", "vault_lines": ["Man that boy growin' up quick, that boy know he the shit"], "meaning": "Manziel is only 21 years old and is projected to be a first round draft pick in the NFL. To do this successfully, he’ll have to grow up quick. Much like Drake, who had to grow up quick due to his parent’s divorcing and his father being put in jail for two bids that lasted nearly 5 years.\n\nDespite growing up quick, both Drake and Johnny Football are very confident with their abilities.", "annotation_id": "3015351", "url": "https://genius.com/3015351", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "We all do it for the art so I could never hate though", "vault_lines": ["We all do it for the art so I could never hate though"], "meaning": "The “art” talk here continues the subliminals to Hov. Drake is implying that he and Jay both do it for “art” but in different ways: Jay Z raps so he can have the money to buy the art he is constantly rapping about; Drake on the other hand raps for the art & the culture.\n\n\n\nDrake at the Philadelphia Museum of Art – see, he doesn’t hate art after all!", "annotation_id": "3015350", "url": "https://genius.com/3015350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "That boy singin’ on every song when he know he can spit That boy manifested it, that boy knew it was written That boy did it on purpose", "vault_lines": ["That boy singin' on every song when he know he can spit", "That boy manifested it, that boy knew it was written", "That boy did it on purpose"], "meaning": "One of the biggest criticisms levied against Drake is that despite the fact it is evident that he is one of the most talented rappers in the game, he is always trying to make R&B songs that consist of him mostly singing.\n\nDrake acknowledges these criticisms here, but, he doesn’t care about these opinions, he will continue to do his thing and get paid, even if that involves making pop songs instead of rap. Drake knows that what he is doing is innovative and unique, but people won’t realize that until years from now.\n\n\n\nThe use of “It was written” may be a sly reference to Jay Z’s former antagonist and fellow rap legend, Nas.\n\nSure enough, just a couple days after “Draft Day” dropped, Drake followed up with “Days In The East”, filled with his familiar crooning, just as promised.", "annotation_id": "3015344", "url": "https://genius.com/3015344", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "But when I’m here, not a sound, that’ll make me snap, jot it down", "vault_lines": ["But when I'm here, not a sound, that'll make me snap, jot it down"], "meaning": "Note: The majority of listeners that criticize drake and rappers that take shots at Drake usually aren’t there to say to him on a one-on-one type of thing.  Drake knows they’re all talk and he’ll be ready to confront or get aggravated at somebody who speaks negatively about him.\n\nACCEPTED COMMENT: When Jay-Z’s “We Made It” freestyle came out Drake was in Europe\n\n\n\nOr maybe Drake’s one of them kids people like to make fun of behind his back, but don’t value their time worth it to fight with him when he’s there, who knows? See Chris Brown and Drake Beef for further evidence.\n\n\n\nEverybody knows Drake doesn’t freestyle, so he has to “jot” down his lyrics. So he’s saying they can’t make a sound, or he’s gonna “jot” it down, make a note of it. & “jot” it down, put it in a verse, and body somebody.\n\nIn addition, this is strangely similar to a Meek line from “My Life.”", "annotation_id": "3015341", "url": "https://genius.com/3015341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "No Oakland A’s though, shout out the Bay though", "vault_lines": ["No Oakland A's though, shout out the Bay though"], "meaning": "Canseco played for the Oakland Athletics (A’s for short) a team that’s had its share of steroid users, including Canseco, Mark McGwire, Jason and Jeremy Giambi, and Miguel Tejada.\n\nThe A’s play in the Bay Area of California and Drizzy likes the area – he filmed the music video for “The Motto” there and also gave it a shout out in the song:\n\nI’m in the building and I’m feeling myself\nRest in peace, Mac Dre, I’m-a do it for the Bay", "annotation_id": "3015339", "url": "https://genius.com/3015339", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Let that man live, they say, “Okay, if you say so”", "vault_lines": ["Let that man live, they say, \"Okay, if you say so\""], "meaning": "Let that man live\n\nIs a reference to Jay Z’s “Can I Live” record.\n\nContinuing on from the previous lines, Drake insists he’s got guys willing to catch a body for him. He’s basically saying he’s allowing Jay to survive in today’s hip hop game. Additionally, he doesn’t want to ruin his career in a beef – hence the Sprite shoutout.", "annotation_id": "3015332", "url": "https://genius.com/3015332", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "All loyal so you keep eggin’ me on And we gonna have to crack a shell just to prove to you, you ain’t hard-boiled, mm Last night I tried some raw oysters", "vault_lines": ["All loyal so you keep eggin' me on", "And we gonna have to crack a shell just to prove to you, you ain't hard-boiled, mm", "Last night I tried some raw oysters"], "meaning": "Drizzy engages in some food-related wordplay here.  The “eggin' me on” idiom sets up a line where the rapper talks that breakfast talk to show his unnamed opponent that, if you mess with Drake, your hard exterior won’t hold up and your soft side will definitely show.  Note the implication of shotgun shells here as well\n\n\n\n“Hardboiled” is also a genre of crime books and movies known for their strong violence. The “hardboiled” character is one who considers himself particularly tough, and behaves like he’s immune to violence and danger (like a hard-boiled egg which, if dropped, won’t break). Hardboiled stories almost always involve organised crime, which fits Drake’s assertion in the previous line that he has a group of friends willing to take any fall for him.\n\nThe oysters in the next line continue the “crack a shell” theme, but also serve as a measure of his wealth (they’re hella expensive!) and romantic prowess (oysters are a well-known aphrodisiac)", "annotation_id": "3015329", "url": "https://genius.com/3015329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Go in the booth and lay a body down", "vault_lines": ["Go in the booth and lay a body down"], "meaning": "Drizzy is killin it in his tracks, he has the most number ones ever for a reason\n\n\n\nYou gone hype me up and make me catch a body like that\n\nACCEPTED COMMENT: In addition…\nHe clearly transitions form the previous line – pointing out how haters always hate from far away because they are aware drake might just go to the studio and murder shit when it comes down to actual rapping.", "annotation_id": "3015320", "url": "https://genius.com/3015320", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "You know I had to— oh, wait, sidebar, left some beat at the end So that all of you fuck niggas could loop it and get your lies off", "vault_lines": ["Man, you know I had to—", "Aww man, you know I had to do it for you", "You know I had to— oh, wait, sidebar, left some beat at the end", "So that all of you fuck niggas could loop it and get your lies off", "You know I had to do it for ya"], "meaning": "Despite possibly taking subliminal shots at Jay Z throughout the song, they both know that a lot of rappers are lying about their lifestyles:\n\nSilly me rappin' ‘bout shit that I really bought\nWhile these rappers rap about guns they ain’t shot\nAnd a bunch of other silly shit that they ain’t got\n\nVery kind of Drizzy to let them rap lie on this instrumental instead of cheap remakes.", "annotation_id": "3015317", "url": "https://genius.com/3015317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "[Produced by The Fam; co-produced by Boi-1da]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Boi-1da teams up with The Fam (production duo SykSense & Ducko Mcfli) who hail from Nashville, TN – read more about them here. The beat samples Lauryn Hill’s “Doo Wop (That Thing),” though having slowed down the tempo makes “Draft Day” sound a lot more laid back.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_embedded&v=T6QKqFPRZSA#t=2m43s", "annotation_id": "3015304", "url": "https://genius.com/3015304", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Brunch with some Qatar royals and my cup is all oil", "vault_lines": ["Brunch with some Qatar royals and my cup is all oil"], "meaning": "Qatar is currently the world’s richest nation. Although Qatar’s wealth technically owes to its large natural gas reserves, not oil, like its neighbouring countries in the Arab Peninsula it is associated in the public imagination with massive oil wealth. It’s also easier to rhyme than “United Arab Emirates.”\n\nOil is a main reason why Arab nations are so wealthy and Drizzy is saying that he has plenty of oil – he has the qualities needed to get rich and become a major player in the rap game!\n\n\n\nSaying his cup is “all oil” is him drinking codeine syrup. In Texas, codeine is called “mud” or “oil” so although Qatar is the world’s richest nation, he is also saying his cup contains a healthy amount of codeine.\n\nCould also relate to the first line about God. Psalm 23:5 states, “You prepare a table before me in the presence of my enemies; you anoint my head with oil; my cup overflows.”", "annotation_id": "3015298", "url": "https://genius.com/3015298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "And if I left this shit to chance I would’ve picked a name like Chance the Rapper", "vault_lines": ["And if I left this shit to chance I would've picked a name like Chance the Rapper"], "meaning": "Sorry Drizzy! That name has been taken.\n\nA friendly jab at young and up-and-coming artist, Chance The Rapper, that generates some buzz for him, thanks Drake! This could possibly be responding to a line said by Chano on his song Family from his debut tape, #10Day.\n\nSing all you want, just don’t Drake on they ass", "annotation_id": "3015296", "url": "https://genius.com/3015296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 295, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Sprite got me on payroll", "vault_lines": ["Sprite got me on payroll"], "meaning": "Drake is sponsored by Sprite, and has been in many Sprite commercials, and his current deal with them is worth an estimated 850,000 dollars.\n\n\n\nThis line linked with the previous line were Drake tells his Somali friends not to catch a body. Saying he has a Sprite deal reminds many listeners of Rick Ross’s situation where a silly situation can cost you a sponsorship. Drake is too smart to let this happen to him.", "annotation_id": "3015293", "url": "https://genius.com/3015293", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "I’m focused on making records and gettin’ bigger Just hits, no misses, that’s for the married folk Tell ‘em fix my suite up ’cause I’m comin’ home I heard they talking crazy, I was out of town You know they love to pop all that shit when I’m not around", "vault_lines": ["Man, you know I had to—", "I'm focused on making records and gettin' bigger", "Just hits, no misses, that's for the married folk", "Tell 'em fix my suite up 'cause I'm comin' home", "I heard they talking crazy, I was out of town", "You know they love to pop all that shit when I'm not around"], "meaning": "Rather than worrying about petty and immature shots, Drake has been busy with business ventures, artistically progressing (same can’t be said about Jay!), building OVO into an empire, and growing his fanbase with quality music.\n\nLikely a response to Jay Z’s lines from “We Made It”. He plays on Mrs./misses – Jay is married to Beyonce. It’s safe to say Hov is past his prime quality and popularity wise. You can also attribute the failure of Roc Nation to him being disloyal to his signees and poor marketing. Artists like Jay Electronica and J Cole have been held back from reaching their true potential, thus being misses.\n\nHe also used a similar wordplay on his “A Little Favour” GQ freestyle, refering to marriage as well:\n\nThis shit is all about hits and misses\nThe ones that you fuck and the ones you wife up\nJust don’t let them fuck your life up", "annotation_id": "3015289", "url": "https://genius.com/3015289", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Draft day, A. Wiggins Fuck that other side, bitch, we stay winnin’", "vault_lines": ["Draft day, A. Wiggins", "Fuck that other side, bitch, we stay winnin'"], "meaning": "Following the theme of the last line, Drake compares his position in the rap game to the current position of NBA prospect and Kansas Jayhawk, Andrew Wiggins. The reference may be a little out of the blue until you remember Wiggins was raised in Toronto. People are calling Andrew Wiggins the next LeBron James, similar to how Drake’s fans see him becoming a future legend in the rap game.\n\nInterestingly enough, Andrew Wiggins is said to be currently deciding between Roc Nation Sports and another agency right around the time him and Jay Z have something escalating. Does this make the sidebitch none other than Jay, hence starting the possible stabs at him throughout the track?\n\nFun Fact: Andrew Wiggins was on the front cover of a Slam issue with Jabari Parker titled “Young Money: The Game’s Next Superstars Have Arrived”.\n\n\n\n\n\nAnother interesting piece of information is that after this song was released, Andrew Wiggins as well as Johnny Manziel were both drafted by Cleveland sports teams (Wiggens to the Cavaliers and Manziel to the Browns).\n\nAlthough Andrew Wiggens was drafted by a Cleveland-based team, he has since be traded to the Minnesota Timberwolves", "annotation_id": "3015283", "url": "https://genius.com/3015283", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "On some Hunger Games shit, I would die for my district Jennifer Lawrence, you can really get it, yeah I mean, for real, girl you know I had to do it for ya", "vault_lines": ["Man, you know I had to—", "On some Hunger Games shit, I would die for my district", "Aww man, you know I had to do it for you", "Jennifer Lawrence, you can really get it, yeah", "I mean, for real, girl you know I had to do it for ya", "You know I had to do it for ya", "Man, I had to do it for ya"], "meaning": "Reference to the the Hunger Games trilogy of books – later turned into movies – set in the country of Panem, where there are 13 districts. As a result of some kind of uprising, the Capitol (they run shit) picks a boy and a girl from each district to fight to the death on TV with everyone watching. Similarly, Drake would willingly “volunteer as tribute” to die for his “district” of Toronto.\n\nThe beautiful Jennifer Lawrence stars as the main character Katniss Everdeen in the movies. Drizzy is just one of the many who would give it up to her.\n\n\n\nIt’s also interesting to note, Jay Z shouted out Lupita Nyong'o in similar fashion. Both were nominated for Best Supporting Actress at the 2014 Oscars. Nyong'o took home the hardware, though Lawrence won Best Actress the year before.", "annotation_id": "3015282", "url": "https://genius.com/3015282", "state": "accepted", "votes": 144, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Draft day, Johnny Manziel Five years later, how am I the man still?", "vault_lines": ["Draft day, Johnny Manziel", "Five years later, how am I the man still?"], "meaning": "When Drake first got big in the rap game about five years ago with his hit EP/Mixtape, So Far Gone, he was in the same position as college football star, Johnny Manziel is in right now, about to be drafted into a label (or in Manziel’s case a team) so he could hit the big time.\n\n Now five years later, Drake is still a huge success in the rap game and it looks like he will continue to be a star for years to come, just like Manziel hopes to one day be a legendary NFL Quarterback.\n\n\n\nAlso, note that Drake has a very strong friendship with Manziel outside of music, going so far as to call them a duo. At one point, Drake even wore a shirt with Manziel’s face on it.\n\n\n\nManziel eventually got picked by the Cleveland Browns with the #22 pick in the NFL draft weeks after the release of this track, surprising observers who expected him to be a top five pick.", "annotation_id": "3015266", "url": "https://genius.com/3015266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Yeah, no offense ’cause I don’t know that nigga", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, no offense 'cause I don't know that nigga"], "meaning": "Drake is clarifying that he isn’t trying to diss Chance because he doesn’t know much about him and doesn’t know him in person. But, Chance definitely knows all about Drake.\nhttps://twitter.com/chancetherapper/status/367036878358196224\nhttps://twitter.com/chancetherapper/status/394995271090053120\nhttps://twitter.com/chancetherapper/status/369107540799279104", "annotation_id": "3015264", "url": "https://genius.com/3015264", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "line": "Aww man, you know I had to do it for you You know I had to do it for ya Yeah, suits and ties yellin’ out, “Pay the guys” Man, I had to do it for ya", "vault_lines": ["Man, you know I had to—", "Aww man, you know I had to do it for you", "I mean, for real, girl you know I had to do it for ya", "You know I had to do it for ya", "Yeah, suits and ties yellin' out, \"Pay the guys\"", "Man, I had to do it for ya", "Aww man, aww man, aww man"], "meaning": "Doubles down as the theme of the track, referring to agents on  “Draft Day”, as well as Jay Z. Hov recently became a sports agent himself and collaborated with Justin Timberlake on last years smash hit “Suit & Tie”.\n\nIs Drake implying he had to “diss” Hov in that Rolling Stone Interview for Jigga to come up with a decent verse on “We Made It”? After-all, Kanye West did admit it was Drake who pushed them to make Watch The Throne\n\n\n\nAlso, there is an ongoing debate about whether collegiate athletes should get paid like professionals.", "annotation_id": "3015262", "url": "https://genius.com/3015262", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Draft Day", "status": "done", "targets": 37, "clicked": 37, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-draft-day-lyrics", "genius_title": "Draft Day"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "305 to My City", "line": "Tonight was your night Go get you some lobsters and shrimp", "vault_lines": ["Tonight was your night", "Go get you some lobsters and shrimp"], "meaning": "After a hard night’s work the girl can allow herself an expensive meal. Go get it, girl. This is what you are doing it for, isn’t it?\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to a 1998 Timbaland single featuring Jay Z.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pN9cRI9GIVk", "annotation_id": "2214609", "url": "https://genius.com/2214609", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "305 to My City", "line": "Your girl got a DUI? I’ll make the calls to get y’all through customs Tell your best friend, “Girl, get your paperwork right”", "vault_lines": ["Your best friend is bartending", "Your girl got a DUI?", "I'll make the calls to get y'all through customs", "Tell your best friend, \"Girl, get your paperwork right\""], "meaning": "The girl Drake’s fuckin' is trying to bring her friend along, but she has a DUI, a criminal offence, and can’t get past the tolls to Canada to get them back to his hometown, Toronto, to party at his crib. But he will make some calls to get her through, anyways. The things you can do if you’re famous, eh?\n\nThis is similar to a line in Dreams Money Can Buy, a little more confident this time around though.", "annotation_id": "2213411", "url": "https://genius.com/2213411", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "305 to My City", "line": "281 to my city Heard you had trouble at customs", "vault_lines": ["281 to my city", "Heard you had trouble at customs"], "meaning": "281 is an area code serving Houston, Texas, one of Drake’s favorite cities. Drake has invited girls from Houston to visit him in Toronto, but the stripper’s friend is having difficulty getting through Canadian customs. Good thing Drake’s going to make some calls and sort everything out.", "annotation_id": "2213323", "url": "https://genius.com/2213323", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "305 to My City", "line": "Oooh, I got the 305 to my city I get it, I get it", "vault_lines": ["I get it, I get it, I swear that I get it", "I get it, you did it, you did it", "Oooh, I got the 305 to my city", "281 to my city", "I get it, I get it", "I get it, get it, get it", "I swear that I get it, I get it, I get it"], "meaning": "305 is the area code to Drake’s known city hotspot, Miami, FL.\n\nDrake will fly the girl from Miami (305) to Toronto (his city). But as the verses show Drake doesn’t care where she is to begin with, he will still fly her to his city.\n\nIf I like her, I just fly her to the city I’m in\n\nSide note: This song is 4 minutes and 16 seconds long, can’t be coincidental that the song length is the area code of his city Toronto.", "annotation_id": "2212904", "url": "https://genius.com/2212904", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "305 to My City", "line": "They don’t work hard as you Damn, that’s so crazy At the end of the night when you count Numbers don’t lie to my baby", "vault_lines": ["They don't work hard as you", "Damn, that's so crazy", "At the end of the night when you count", "Numbers don't lie to my baby"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to a stripper, (that may also prostitute), and explaining how what she does is nothing to be ashamed of and to ignore people who look down on it.\n\nHe justifies it because at the end of the night when the the money that the strippers make is counted, it should reveal that Drake’s girl made the most, hence is the best. “Numbers don’t lie” could be a reference to Jay Z’s “men lie, women lie, numbers don’t” line that distinguishes him from other rappers who don’t do the same numbers (shows, album sales, capital venture revenue) as he does.", "annotation_id": "2212729", "url": "https://genius.com/2212729", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "305 to My City", "line": "Your momma used to live at the church on Sunday You just go to LIV after church on Sunday, oh Lord Oh Lord, we’re not in Kansas anymore We’re not in Kansas anymore (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Your momma used to live at the church on Sunday", "You just go to LIV after church on Sunday, oh Lord", "Oh Lord, we're not in Kansas anymore", "We're not in Kansas anymore (Yeah)"], "meaning": "A little wordplay on “live at church”/“LIV after church”:\n\nThe girl Drake is talking to likes to have fun, but her family is very religious. She might attend church, but after hours she would rather party at exclusive Miami clubs.\n\nIn which there happens to be a nightclub in Miami, “305” by the name of LIV that she perhaps attends to after church on Sundays.\n\n\n\nHe then plays on the phrase “we’re not in Kansas anymore” from The Wizard of Oz. Dorothy said it when she landed in Oz and literally wasn’t in Kansas. Everything was very different and much more dangerous.\n\nThis stripper definitely isn’t in Kansas (or church) anymore.\n\n\n\nSame reference was used by André 3000 on “Sixteen”.", "annotation_id": "2211798", "url": "https://genius.com/2211798", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "305 to My City", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 6, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-305-to-my-city-lyrics", "genius_title": "305 to My City"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "They say “holy” when they see the chain, that’s not a cross", "vault_lines": ["They say \"holy\" when they see the chain, that's not a cross", "They say \"holy\" when they see my chain, it's not a cross"], "meaning": "Drake’s chain is luxurious and lavish, people’s first reaction when they see it is to say, “Holy shit”, a common phrase said by people when they tend to be shocked or in disbelief.\n\nDrake is likening the word “holy” from the aforementioned phrase to the “holiness” of a cross, used to symbolize Jesus in the Christian faith.  However, Drake is not wearing a cross on the chain, yet is most likely wearing the matching OVO Owl chain that he and Future have.\n\nThe word “holy” may also reference Drake’s self-named 6 God.", "annotation_id": "19485178", "url": "https://genius.com/19485178", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "All-All I did was boss up the city, that’s my fault", "vault_lines": ["All-All I did was boss up the city, that's my fault"], "meaning": "Future is a proud representative of his hometown of Atlanta, Georgia, becoming one of the most popular rappers from the area. Despite his superstar lifestyle, Future continues to give back to his community and has been a very generous philanthropist. He even created multiple different organizations to help the Atlanta community, such as the FreeWishes Foundation.\n\nFuture’s care for the community was apparent in December 2017, when Future attended Atlanta’s Winter Wishland Extravaganza. At the event, he spoke to the children and raffled away expensive items on their behalf. After the event, Future’s mother spoke on their behalf, emphasizing their love for giving back:\n\nWe are truly humbled to be able to give back to so many deserving families. Future and I are completely filled with joy when we see so many smiling faces from the mothers, fathers and children\n\nThis line could also refer to how Future’s music is empowering for street individuals and the way his music influences its listeners in Atlanta to “boss up” or step it up. Future’s music features heavy themes of hedonism and gang violence, which could inspire its listeners to pursue a similar lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "18709868", "url": "https://genius.com/18709868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "I got slimes with me, they got sticks too", "vault_lines": ["I got slimes with me, they got sticks too"], "meaning": "“Slime” is an acronym for “Street Life Intelligence and Money is Everything” and is a frequently used term in hip hop. The term was popularized in the Atlanta rap scene by rappers such as Young Thug, Gunna, and Lil Baby. “Stick” is common slang referring to a gun.\n\nIt’s important to note that Future and frequent collaborator Young Thug teamed up for an October 2017 joint mixtape, SUPER SLIMEY, suggesting Thug may be the aforementioned “slime.”", "annotation_id": "18684924", "url": "https://genius.com/18684924", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "I get Boosie in the Lamborghini, wipe me off", "vault_lines": ["I get Boosie in the Lamborghini, wipe me off"], "meaning": "Boosie Badazz is a Louisiana rapper who previously rapped under the moniker “Lil Boosie.” He has collaborated with Future three times prior: on Trae tha Truth’s July 2015 track, “Tricken Every Car I Get,” Juicy J’s September 2015 track, “Film Remix,” and DJ Khaled’s October 2015 track “I Ride.” In March 2007, Boosie released his hit track “Wipe Me Down,” in which featured artist Foxx-a-million rapped:\n\n‘Cause I’m on (Wipe me down)\n\nLamborghini is a luxury Italian sports car brand. Future is known for his taste in cars and has a diverse collection, including multiple Lamborghini models.", "annotation_id": "18684839", "url": "https://genius.com/18684839", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Fuckin’ with my gang and get murdered Shout out to my city, they murder", "vault_lines": ["Fuckin' with my gang and get murdered", "Shout out to my city, they murder"], "meaning": "In 2019, Forbes listed Atlanta as the sixth most dangerous city in the United States due to its crime rate of roughly 1.433% and large amount of gangs. A 2018 CBS study revealed that the murder rate in Atlanta was roughly 18%, making it the twenty third most deadly city in the country.\n\nFuture’s “gang” likely refers to his Freebandz Music Label, which is home to primarily Atlanta artists like Young Scooter and DJ Esco.", "annotation_id": "18681830", "url": "https://genius.com/18681830", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Cashville, I got bands still like a boss", "vault_lines": ["Cashville, I got bands still like a boss"], "meaning": "Cashville Records is a famous record label in Tennessee that was created by Young Buck. Drake’s father, Dennis Graham is a Memphis native, who revealed that Tennessee actually helped mold Drizzy’s career in an interview with WREG:\n\nHe definitely got it honestly. He was introduced to everything. He said, ‘That’s what I want to do dad. I want to do exactly what you do.\n\nBoth Drake and Future are wealthy individuals, having a net worth of $150 million and $40 million respectively.", "annotation_id": "18681697", "url": "https://genius.com/18681697", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Brand new Phantom cost me ten Rollies", "vault_lines": ["Brand new Phantom cost me ten Rollies"], "meaning": "A Rolls Royce Phantom is a famous luxury car model that was developed in 1925 and is now worth approximately $450,000 on average. In comparison to ten Rolex watches, which typically settle for under 10 thousand dollars, Rolls Royce’s are worth significantly more, indicating that Future is factually incorrect.\n\nFuture previously name dropped the luxury automobile before, on his January 2013’s “Chosen One”:\n\nBought a Phantom cause I always wanted one", "annotation_id": "18410953", "url": "https://genius.com/18410953", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Exotic up the driveway, big mood", "vault_lines": ["Exotic up the driveway, big mood", "Double up the contract, big mood", "Bossin' up the gang, that's a big mood", "Bossin' up the city, that's a big mood", "Sparkle up the wrist, now that's a big mood"], "meaning": "Drake has a very impressive car collection which he has been building for years. He owns many luxury cars, and has spoken on them in much of his past music. On his 2016 track “Child’s Play,” he rapped about owning a Bugatti, a French luxury automobile:\n\nYou wildin', you super childish, you go to CVS for Kotex\nIn my Bugatti, I took the key and tried to hide it\nSo you can’t drive it, and put on mileage\nThen you find it, awkward silence\n\nDrake does actually own one of the cars, as seen here:", "annotation_id": "18364141", "url": "https://genius.com/18364141", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "We put yo BM in rotation Shit, niggas woulda did the same thing if it was me", "vault_lines": ["We put yo BM in rotation", "Shit, niggas woulda did the same thing if it was me"], "meaning": "A “BM” is a slang abbreviation for a baby mama or a woman who has a man’s child without being officially eloped. Future warns his opposition that he can steal their women at any point he desires.\n\nFuture’s issues associated with these women have been well documented, as he has at least six known children with six different women. His most recent “BM” is an Instagram model named Eliza Reign, who revealed her pregnancy with the Atlanta rapper on her social media.\n\nFuture famously boasted about these types of women on his December 2015 track, “Low Life”:\n\nGot my baby momma and my side bitch kissin'\nI turn the Ritz into a lean house", "annotation_id": "18027885", "url": "https://genius.com/18027885", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Wisdom tooth, I’m pimpin’ like Goldie", "vault_lines": ["Wisdom tooth, I'm pimpin' like Goldie"], "meaning": "Future interpolates Drake’s flow and lyrics from the opening line of the track:\n\nWisdom tooth, got Perc' in my system\n\n“Goldie” is the name of the infamous protagonist from the 1973 movie “The Mack,” where the Max Julien portrayed character is released from prison and rejoins the illicit, underground pimping business.\n\nHarlem rapper A$AP Rocky later used a similar reference on his April 2012 track, “Goldie,” where he also likened himself to a “pimp.”\n\nThis could also reference his own golden grill, which cost him approximately thirty thousand dollars. The customized accessory is frequently worn by Future.", "annotation_id": "17972923", "url": "https://genius.com/17972923", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Fuck Trump, that’s for my woadies (Gang, gang)", "vault_lines": ["Fuck Trump, that's for my woadies (Gang, gang)"], "meaning": "Future sends shots at United States president Donald Trump. Trump’s been criticized in the past for a variety of issues but most notably his racially charged Tweets and his inconsiderate handling of immigration.\n\nWhile he typically tends to avoid politics in his music, Future did send shots at Trump on his June 2019 track, “Government Official,” as he attacked the infamous rumors that Trump colluded with Russia to win the 2016 Presidential Election:\n\nFuck the government official, we plugged with Putin (Yeah, yeah)\n\nA “woadie” is a slang term utilized to describe a childhood friend who also grew up in a difficult neighborhood. This could also reference the famous line from Drake’s February 2015 track, “Know Yourself,” where he used a variation of the word:\n\nI was runnin' through the 6 with my woes\nYou know how that should go\nYou know how that should go", "annotation_id": "17939309", "url": "https://genius.com/17939309", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "You haven’t found another ’cause there ain’t another", "vault_lines": ["You haven't found another 'cause there ain't another"], "meaning": "Since the release of his 2009 mixtape So Far Gone, Drake’s unprecedented run has included record breaking statistics and previously impossible accomplishments, such as six BillBoard number one hits and a diamond track. As one of the most influential artists of all time, Drake attracts a large number of detractors who unsuccessfully attempt to find his successor.\n\nDrake previously boasted about his irreplaceable status in hip hop on his June 2019 track, “Money in the Grave”:\n\nI was on top when that shit meant a lot\nStill on top like I’m scared of the drop\nStill on top and these niggas wanna swap", "annotation_id": "17887380", "url": "https://genius.com/17887380", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Bossin’ up the city, that’s a big mood", "vault_lines": ["Exotic up the driveway, big mood", "Double up the contract, big mood", "Bossin' up the gang, that's a big mood", "Bossin' up the city, that's a big mood", "Sparkle up the wrist, now that's a big mood"], "meaning": "Drake is famously proud of his hometown of Toronto, Canada. Throughout his career, Drake has attempted to give back to his community through philanthropy and publicity. In September 2018, a BillBoard article reported that Drake’s contributions have led to an increase of $440 million in revenue or approximately 5% of the total economy.\n\nThis could also allude to the way he cosigns Canadian rappers and attempts to put them on in the rap game. In an attempt to help the burgeoning Toronto music scene, Drake has assisted mutliple different rappers, such as The Weeknd, Roy Woods and SAFE.\n\nDrake rapped about his influence on the city during his September 2013 track, “Wutang Forever”:\n\nStadium packed, just glad to see the city on the map\nI just gave the city life, it ain’t about who did it first\nIt’s ‘bout who did it right, niggas looking like “Preach”\n\nDrizzy’s hometown pride is present throughout his discography. He even proclaimed himself the “Six God” which stems from the area codes  of Toronto: either 647 or 416. He first gave himself this title on the eponymous October 2014 track:\n\nI give a fuck about old, boy (Man, that boy old)\nI give a fuck about his squad, boy (Man, that thing old)\nI give a fuck about your mob, boy (Oh)\nI’m the real 6 God, boy (Whoa)", "annotation_id": "17856453", "url": "https://genius.com/17856453", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Niggas we don’t even fuck with gettin’ Ms too But just know we could cut that off again too", "vault_lines": ["Niggas we don't fuck with get them bags too", "Niggas we don't even fuck with gettin' Ms too", "But just know we could cut that off again too"], "meaning": "Drake likely alludes to his beef with Pusha T, which reached its peak in mid 2018 when they released back and forth diss tracks: Drake’s “Duppy Freestyle” and Pusha’s “The Story of Adidon.”\n\nOn “Duppy Freestyle,” Drake famously boasted about the increase in sales that his response would lead to for Pusha:\n\nTell ‘Ye we got a invoice comin’ to you\nConsiderin' that we just sold another 20 for you\n\nIn order to further the brutality of the diss, Drake sent Kanye West and his G.O.O.D. Music label an official invoice for one hundred thousand dollars, which he boasted about on Instagram. Drake’s claim that he brought Pusha back to relevance through responding, is partially true, as Pusha T’s album, DAYTONA was his highest charting to date and sold approximately 77,000 album units.", "annotation_id": "17851430", "url": "https://genius.com/17851430", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Right now, not the season for givin’", "vault_lines": ["Right now, not the season for givin'"], "meaning": "Drake is famously known for giving cosigns and helping put other artists on through features. Since entering into the rap game, he’s helped a plethora of rapper rise to fame such as The Weeknd, Migos, and even Kendrick Lamar. In a famous article, Hip Hop DX broke down the power of Drake’s assistance and mentioned multiple times where he put rappers on.\n\nIn a July 2018 interview, rising rapper 21 Savage pointed out the work that Drake does for other rappers and credited him for furthering the rap culture:\n\nDrake just a genuine-ass good-hearted person. He did a lot of shit he didn’t have to do. He do a lot of shit for up-and-coming rappers, period. I don’t think he get his respect for that. Every fuckin’ year, he pull a new artist up. Ain’t no other artist on his level do that shit.\n\nDrake also rapped about this tendency on his March 2013 track, “5AM in Toronto”:\n\nGive these niggas the look, the verse, and even the hook\nThat’s why every song sound like Drake featurin' Drake", "annotation_id": "17851374", "url": "https://genius.com/17851374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Wisdom tooth got Mollies and Perkies", "vault_lines": ["Wisdom tooth got Mollies and Perkies"], "meaning": "Future frequently raps about his use of prescription medication and illicit substances in his music. His July 2015 album Dirty Sprite 2 explicitly dives into the consequences of his incessant drug usage and  his drug infused lifestyle.\n\nHe famously rapped about the two drugs in the chorus of his 2017 hit track, “Mask Off”:\n\nPercocets (Ya), molly, Percocets (Percocets)\nPercocets (Ya), molly, Percocets (Percocets)\n\nIn an interview with Watch Loud, Future admitted that he fabricates his excessive use of drugs:\n\nI’m not like super drugged out or [a] drug addict. My music may portray a certain kind of image and I know it’s some people that might be super drugged out and they listen to the music like, ‘Ay thank you, you speaking for me’ and then some people that’s not [super drugged out] that feel like, “Man I don’t have to do drugs, I can listen to Future and feel like I’m on something’ and don’t have to try [drugs]. I don’t do it for you to really have to live that type of life.", "annotation_id": "17733813", "url": "https://genius.com/17733813", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "I’ve been buyin’ Birkins in rhythm Save ’em for my wife when I get her", "vault_lines": ["I've been buyin' Birkins in rhythm", "Save 'em for my wife when I get her"], "meaning": "Birkins are designer bags that were designed in 1984 and typically made out of leather. Drake and Chris Brown previously rapped a similar line about the bag on their 2019 collaboration, “No Guidance”:\n\nLil' baby in her bag, in her Birkin\nNo nine to five, put the work in\n\nIn an interview with Hollywood Reporter, Drake claimed that “[he has] been collecting Birkin bags for years, a gift for the woman [he ends] up with.”\n\nAs of 2019, Drake has yet to get married, despite being involved in a variety of high profile relationships with women like Rihanna and Jennifer Lopez. He is also famously a single parent, which he expressed regret for on his August 2018 track, “Emotionless.”\n\nDrake recently discussed his perspective of marriage on his 2019 track, “Omertà”:\n\nKeepin' my connections strictly physical\nEveryone that’s married is miserable\nI know that that is not a lifestyle I can give into", "annotation_id": "17717047", "url": "https://genius.com/17717047", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Wisdom tooth, got Perc’ in my system", "vault_lines": ["Wisdom tooth, got Perc' in my system"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to Percocets, which are popular painkillers that are frequently referenced in hip hop. In 2012, Drake admitted to utilizing drugs in order to deal with his fame:\n\nHave I sipped codeine before? Yeah, of course. Have I smoked weed? Yes. Do I drink wine? Yes. But do I do it excessively? No. I’m not a reckless guy. I do it all within moderation. You get artists in this position who go crazy and don’t know how to handle it. There are people who have killed themselves. There’s the overwhelming stress, how tired you are, the weight on your shoulders of going out there and giving 18,000 people entertainment… it’s a lot of pressure.\n\nDrake famously alluded to the use of painkillers on Travis Scott’s August 2018 track, “SICKO MODE,” where he detailed an experience with Xanax.\n\nIt’s also important to note that Future commonly uses the illicit substance. He famously rapped about the drug in the chorus of his 2017 hit “Mask Off”:\n\nPercocets (Ya), molly, Percocets (Percocets)\nPercocets (Ya), molly, Percocets (Percocets)", "annotation_id": "17716740", "url": "https://genius.com/17716740", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "All they do is PR double-E me like I’m Dolph", "vault_lines": ["All they do is PR double-E me like I'm Dolph"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to the Memphis rap label Paper Route Empire, the group that Young Dolph founded and is currently a part of. He has alluded to the group throughout his music, perhaps most famously on “Paper Route Campaign”:\n\nYoung Dolph. Paper Route Entertainment\nPaper Route boys (yeah)\nSkrilla squad, what it do?\nSouth Memphis cash state, that’s my hood nigga\n\nThe assorted letters combine to spell the word “pree.” which is a Toronto slang term used for observing or watching something closely. Drake, a famous celebrity, attracts a lot of people that spend a significant amount of time watching his life and every move.\n\nDrake previously used the term on his March 2013 track, “5AM in Toronto”:\n\nStr8, Y pree? Why is it always me?\nGot us watchin' our words like it’s wiretaps on the team\n\nAs of 2019, neither Drake nor Future have collaborated with Dolph.", "annotation_id": "17716703", "url": "https://genius.com/17716703", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "I’ve been buyin’ rings like I’m Kobe (Pluto!)", "vault_lines": ["I've been buyin' rings like I'm Kobe (Pluto!)"], "meaning": "Kobe Bryant is a former NBA superstar who played for the Los Angeles Lakers. Throughout the course of his career, Bryant won 5 championships with the Lakers and became one of the most accomplished players in NBA history.\n\nFuture likens his vast collection of jewelry to Kobe’s rings. Future’s net worth is approximately $30 million, allowing him to purchase whatever items he desires.\n\nIn addition, Future references the What A Time To Be Alive hit track, “Big Rings,” where Drake rapped:\n\n‘Cause I got a really big team And they need some really big rings", "annotation_id": "17681063", "url": "https://genius.com/17681063", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Baka, he a star, but still around to burst a bubble", "vault_lines": ["Baka, he a star, but still around to burst a bubble"], "meaning": "Drake references his close friend and October’s Very Own affiliate Baka Not Nice, who is also from Canada. He reached mainstream fame in March 2017 due to the success of his hit track, “Live Up to My Name.”\n\nDrizzy alludes to Baka’s origins as his personal bodyguard and also his 13 year stint in jail. While Baka’s now in the public scope and a successful rapper, he will still defend Drake in anyway. Baka previously discussed his willingness to defend Drizzy on “Live Up To My Name”:\n\nGyah, gyah! Diss The Boy? Are you insane?\nI drink Henny when I shoot my pistol at the range\nThat way, when I’m tipsy, I still know I got my aim\nA.K.A. it’s Not Nice and it’s not a fuckin' game", "annotation_id": "17680933", "url": "https://genius.com/17680933", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Put the owl on it, you can never take it off", "vault_lines": ["Put the owl on it, you can never take it off"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to the owl, an important symbol in his music and the logo for his October’s Very Own symbol. He frequently references OVO and his owl throughout his discography, perhaps most famously on his June 2018 track, “Nonstop”:\n\nFuture took the business and ran it for me I let Ollie take the owl, told him brand it for me I get 2 million a pop and that’s standard for me\n\nDrizzy famously boasts multiple OVO jewelry and items. Among his immense collection, Drake owns a 2017 Ben Baller diamond owl chain and a 2018 October’s Very Own piece.\n\nIn addition, Drake alludes to his expansive OVO Sound roster, which features the likes of PARTYNEXTDOOR, Popcaan, Majid Jordan, and more. Drizzy confirms his tight knit relationship with his peers from the record label and describes the family aspect present.", "annotation_id": "17676846", "url": "https://genius.com/17676846", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "King Pluto, might as well nail me to the cross", "vault_lines": ["King Pluto, might as well nail me to the cross"], "meaning": "Future references his nickname “Pluto,” which originates from the name of a small dwarf planet in the solar system. Future discussed the word’s meaning to him in an April 2012 interview with Complex:\n\nThat’s why I say “astronaut status” because that’s what I make, timeless music. Astronauts; it’s space. How long does it take to get to space? Timeless! It’s timeless. It’s music that is going to be around forever. That’s the kind of music I make—astronaut music. People don’t understand what I’m saying. It’s big. It’s bigger than life.\n\nIt’s also a reference to Future’s debut album Pluto, which served as his introduction to mainstream rap. Future claims that his fans have stuck with him since the beginning and remember his very first studio album.\n\nIn the Bible, Jesus Christ was crucified and punished for his crimes, which ultimately led to his death.", "annotation_id": "17671829", "url": "https://genius.com/17671829", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Double up the contract, big mood", "vault_lines": ["Exotic up the driveway, big mood", "Double up the contract, big mood", "Bossin' up the gang, that's a big mood", "Bossin' up the city, that's a big mood", "Sparkle up the wrist, now that's a big mood"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to his maximum record label deal that he earns from rap and the second set of earnings he earns from endorsement deals. Drake’s net worth is approximately $150 million, through a combination of the two sets of income. Drake boasts endorsements from major companies like Jordan, Sprite, and Apple Music.\n\nAccording to a study from Variety, Drake could earn a record setting figure of approximately $20 million per album, when he finally signs a new deal. In 2019, his releases appeared under the Frozen Moments record label, indicating the possibility of a new contract.\n\nDrizzy previously boasted about his contract on his March 2017 track, “Gyalchester”:\n\nContract max, I gotta bring that shit back", "annotation_id": "17671605", "url": "https://genius.com/17671605", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Bitch, this not Fugazi, but my neck still changin’ color", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, this not Fugazi, but my neck still changin' color"], "meaning": "“Fugazi” is a slang term that is used to describe a “fake” item or objects. Drake refuses to own inauthentic material possessions and is rich enough to afford whatever he desires.\n\nFuture, the other artist who appears on the track, frequently utilizes the term throughout his discography, perhaps most famously on his July 2015 track, “Blow a Bag”:\n\nI woke up feeling like fucking up some paper I blow a bag today, I don’t do nothin' fugazi\n\nDrake also alludes to the American rock band Fugazi to boast about his own expensive necklaces and jewelry. Drake’s collection of lavish items features his “OVO ‘O’ chain,” a diamond necklace that he always wears around his neck.", "annotation_id": "17671533", "url": "https://genius.com/17671533", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Triple, double, Chubbs is hoodied up, I feel like Russell", "vault_lines": ["Triple, double, Chubbs is hoodied up, I feel like Russell"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to NBA superstar Russell Westbrook, a player who averaged a triple double for a record breaking three straight seasons. Drake compares his unprecedented success in the rap game to Westbrook’s accomplishments.\n\nChubbs is one of Drake’s closest friends and his personal bodyguard. Drake frequently references his friend in his music, perhaps most famously on his March 2017 track, “No Long Talk”:\n\nJust know man like Chubbs He’s a fixer if I ever gotta fix tings", "annotation_id": "17671437", "url": "https://genius.com/17671437", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "line": "Bitch, you crazy, don’t pull up at 3 AM to cuddle", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, you crazy, don't pull up at 3 AM to cuddle"], "meaning": "Drake interpolates his January 2018 track, “God’s Plan”:\n\nDon’t pull up at 6 AM to cuddle with me\nYou know how I like it when you lovin’ on me\n\nIn both instances, he reflects on an intimate relationship with a girl where he refuses to serve as a late night hookup.", "annotation_id": "17671433", "url": "https://genius.com/17671433", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Mood", "status": "done", "targets": 27, "clicked": 27, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-future-big-mood-lyrics", "genius_title": "Big Mood"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "Get down, never lay down", "vault_lines": ["Get down, never lay down"], "meaning": "This line is a reference to the 2002 film “State Property” where character “Beans” played by American rapper Beanie Sigel says the famous quote, “Nigga, you gon’ get down or you gon’ fuckin’ lay down”\n\nhttps://youtu.be/z3_vEFBCn6w?si=e5_0w4ZLXQjSF7OH", "annotation_id": "32795380", "url": "https://genius.com/32795380", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "[Part II]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The beat fades out almost entirely at this point, slightly rising once the bridge starts its 3rd line.\n\nOnce the bridge finishes, the new higher tempo beat kicks in for a more anthem-y feel with orchestral notes, bass, and soulful vocal work similar to “Lord Knows”.\n\nThis beat samples “Hold On, For We’re Going Home” by The Corinthian Temple Cogic Choir which was released in 1990.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hMrFo0eftt8", "annotation_id": "11886659", "url": "https://genius.com/11886659", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "[Produced by Noah “40” Shebib and Hagler / Co-produced by Jake One]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jake One is the main producer on this track. He did the body of the work while 40 mixed and mastered it, and One thought it was a nice addition to it. One did a short “interview” of sorts on the making of the track and it’s sampling issues.\n\nThe jazzy, soulful beat is driven by it’s drums and piano cords. Drake brings it together to make this track, and the laid back vibe makes it’s easy to jam to. You can hear the piano drive it along with various other  effects and hits to make it a little more modern.\n\nJake One is credited using his “old school ways” on why he doesn’t tell where anything is sampled from, but that’s where the choir part is from, (until 40 had a live one done in Toronto). Gene Brown originally found that sample, and he has worked to find samples with a number of countless producers. Jake had talked and sent beats to Drake for years, but Drake finally said listening to it, “This is the one”.\n\nThere was just something about the dynamic of that band, in that church\n\nDue to sampling issues, the track was then sent to G Koop who remade the parts with issues that night and resent it that morning. Then 40 added the finishing touches to bring it together.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R8rpv5ikov4", "annotation_id": "2712413", "url": "https://genius.com/2712413", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "And now you hate me, stop pretendin’, stop that frontin’, I can’t take it", "vault_lines": ["And now you hate me, stop pretendin', stop that frontin', I can't take it"], "meaning": "His insecurities are at a high with him rehashing that he knows that he’s a great lover and that if things went sour it couldn’t have been all his fault and so the girl needs to own some of the blame.\n\nHis boastful self-identity on care-giving is also present in “Wu-Tang Forever” and “Own It”", "annotation_id": "2218131", "url": "https://genius.com/2218131", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "Promise to break everybody off before I break down", "vault_lines": ["Promise to break everybody off before I break down"], "meaning": "Multiple Meanings\n\n\nDrake vows to “break off” a piece of his wealth for everybody he cares about before he breaks down a piece for himself. As the saying goes, “the cook is always the last to eat.” Drake’s been cooking up a storm, so there’s plenty of food to go around.\nHe also vows to himself that he’ll literally “break off” his relationships with other people before all of the pressures of having personal relationships when you’re famous “breaks him down”.\n\n\n\nThe first meaning is similar to feelings of guilt Jay showed on MCHG’s Nickels and Dimes\n\nEverybody ‘round me rich, or will be\nBaby boy I promise you this , or kill me", "annotation_id": "2217155", "url": "https://genius.com/2217155", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "Girl, don’t treat me like a stranger, girl, you know I seen you naked", "vault_lines": ["Girl, don't treat me like a stranger, girl, you know I seen you naked", "Girl, you know that I remember, don't be a pretender"], "meaning": "Seeing a partner naked is a sign of intimacy which most people would not be privy to, so even though their relationship has gotten complicated, Drake doesn’t want to lose everything they have together. He doesn’t want to just be somebody that she used to know.\n\n\n\nSeeing somebody naked also depicts their most vulnerable state. Drake doesn’t want the girl to treat him like he doesn’t know her even if he has seen her at the most vulnerable a person can be.", "annotation_id": "2215010", "url": "https://genius.com/2215010", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "The furthest thing from perfect, like everyone I know", "vault_lines": ["The furthest thing from perfect, like everyone I know"], "meaning": "Drake has mentioned his imperfections before:\n\n“Unstoppable”\n\nMy name is Drizzy, and I ain’t perfect\n\n“Shot for Me”\n\nAnd all your friends telling you stories that you often misinterpret\nAnd taint all your images of your “Mr. Perfect”\n\n\n\nThe last part is Drake connecting with the audience, as he notes that all of us are imperfect, making the song that much more relatable.", "annotation_id": "2214576", "url": "https://genius.com/2214576", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "People I believed in, they don’t even show they face now What they got to say now? Nothin’ they can say now", "vault_lines": ["What they got to say now? Nothin' they can say now", "What more can I say now? What more can I say now?", "People I believed in, they don't even show they face now"], "meaning": "Drake is finding it hard to trust the friends he came up with and some of that may be due to his busy lifestyle. He expresses similar sentiments at several other points in the album:\n\n“Started From The Bottom”\n\nFuck a fake friend, where your real friends at?\n\n“Wu Tang Forever”\n\nPeople like Mazin who was a best friend to me\nStart to become a distant memory", "annotation_id": "2214475", "url": "https://genius.com/2214475", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "Everyone just wait now, so much on my plate now", "vault_lines": ["Everyone just wait now, so much on my plate now"], "meaning": "Drake has been BUSY:\n\n\nPrepping NWTS\nFeatures all over the place – “Fuckin' Problems”, “No Lie”, every song on the radio is starting to sound like Drake featuring Drake\nPromoting his own label, October’s Very Own or OVO, signing acts like PARTYNEXTDOOR and Majid Jordan\n\n\n\nHe needs to take some time out for himself, some time for introspection, and figure out the position he wants to be in. Much of this song, and the album, deals with these themes.", "annotation_id": "2214331", "url": "https://genius.com/2214331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "A nigga fillin’ up arenas, who the fuck could see us?", "vault_lines": ["A nigga fillin' up arenas, who the fuck could see us?"], "meaning": "Drake’s Club Paradise Tour in promotion for Take Care grossed over $42 million, becoming the most successful hip-hop tour of 2012.\n\nWith Nothing Was The Same being the most highly anticipated album of the year, his fall 2013 tour is sure to be equally (or even more) successful", "annotation_id": "2214110", "url": "https://genius.com/2214110", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "I got ’em worried, like make sure you save a slice for me I should have Spoons serve you up with a fork and knife for me", "vault_lines": ["I got 'em worried, like make sure you save a slice for me", "I should have Spoons serve you up with a fork and knife for me"], "meaning": "Drake has all the competition scared that when he’s done with the rap game, there won’t be anything left for them.\n\nHe plays off this food metaphor and his bodyguard’s name, Spoons, to threaten how he’ll eat up all his competition.", "annotation_id": "2214078", "url": "https://genius.com/2214078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "Y’all niggas party too much, man, I just chill and record", "vault_lines": ["Y'all niggas party too much, man, I just chill and record"], "meaning": "Drake is all about his business, he’ll make money and leave the partying up to the other rappers. He would rather make money recording in the studio then out at clubs partying. He told GQ in 2013:\n\nBy no means will I take a water break,“ he tells me. \"I feel guilty on vacation. I feel guilty right now, talking to you, guilty that I’m not working.\n\n\n\nDrake expressed a similar sentiment on “Light Up”, off Thank Me Later:\n\nCause while all of my closest friends out partying\nI’m just here making all the music that they party to", "annotation_id": "2213516", "url": "https://genius.com/2213516", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "Donate a million to some children, that’s just how I’m feelin’", "vault_lines": ["Donate a million to some children, that's just how I'm feelin'"], "meaning": "While he didn’t give quite that much, Drake did recently donate $30,000 to Jamaican schools.\n\n\n\nThough, factoring in the exchange rate, this does equate to more than 3 million Jamaican dollars.", "annotation_id": "2213498", "url": "https://genius.com/2213498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "I had to Derrick Rose the knee up before I got the re-up", "vault_lines": ["I had to Derrick Rose the knee up before I got the re-up"], "meaning": "Chicago Bull, Derrick Rose, tore his left ACL forcing him to miss the 2012 playoffs and entire 2012-2013 season. There was speculation he might come back in 2013, but he decided to take time to make sure his knee was totally recovered.\n\nDrake dropped Take Care in 2011, but he sat out 2012 (save for some features) to make sure this album was just right.\n\n\n\nDrake also tore his ACL during a show in New Jersey in 2009:\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PLSVTAbmKPM", "annotation_id": "2213222", "url": "https://genius.com/2213222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "line": "Somewhere between psychotic and iconic Somewhere between I want it and I got it Somewhere between I’m sober and I’m lifted Somewhere between a mistress and commitment", "vault_lines": ["Somewhere between psychotic and iconic", "Somewhere between I want it and I got it", "Somewhere between I'm sober and I'm lifted", "Somewhere between a mistress and commitment"], "meaning": "This is where Drake is currently at: he’s walking a fine line between crazy and genius. He’s successful, but not totally there yet. He’s high, but not too high. He’s got a main girl, but he’s also got someone on the side. (We already know how he feels about commitment)\n\nThere’s levels to this shit…\n\n\n\nDrake breaks down this first line in an interview with Vibe magazine:\n\nIt’s just forever hanging in the balance of knowing in the far-off distance there’s this vision of being as legendary as Hov, Pac, Bob Marley—who knows what the possibilities are? I’m not sure what I mean to this generation; I won’t understand until the story plays out. But there is that vision of the legacy. There’s also so many opportunities to plummet and not reach that. I keep pushing forward, but sometimes I glance back like, Damn, that shit’s crazy back there. Look at all those guys back there. That shit’s scary. That shit can drive you crazy. That’s what that line means to me. There are two directions you can go. It’s up to you.", "annotation_id": "2213213", "url": "https://genius.com/2213213", "state": "accepted", "votes": 168, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Furthest Thing", "status": "done", "targets": 15, "clicked": 15, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-furthest-thing-lyrics", "genius_title": "Furthest Thing"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "They tried to kill me once, but, darling, you just resurrected me", "vault_lines": ["They tried to kill me once, but, darling, you just resurrected me"], "meaning": "Those who opposed him in 2024’s Kendrick and Drake feud sought the downfall of Drake’s career. He implies that the beef itself brought him back to making music. Here, Drake continues that by returning with his comeback release of ICEMAN alongside HABIBTI and MAID OF HONOR. In that sense, his enemies are intended to realize that after his break, his resurrection will only continue to eclipse their past attempts to see his fall.\n\n Simultaneously, the ‘darling’ referenced may apply to Emiliana–a specific woman or a metaphor i.e. the media or his fans–saying that she in a way brought him and his motivation/career back to life.", "annotation_id": "40584812", "url": "https://genius.com/40584812", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Chi-town, poppin’ a pill, go on a drill, lookin’ for someone to kill", "vault_lines": ["Chi-town, poppin' a pill, go on a drill, lookin' for someone to kill"], "meaning": "“Chi-town” refers to Chicago, where Kendrick Lamar’s parents lived before relocating to Compton. Kendrick’s father, Kenny Duckworth, has spoken about being affiliated with the Gangster Disciples, a Chicago street gang, before leaving the city to escape the violence.\n\n\n\nHere, Drake accuses Kendrick of having conveniently “forgotten how it feels” to live with the violence and consequences of street life. The brutal, violent life Kendrick’s father once lived as a Gangster Disciple — “going on drills” and “looking for someone to kill” — contrasts with Kendrick’s conscious, morally reflective music. Drake suggests Kendrick is disconnected from that reality, now engaging in performative activism while living a life of wealth and luxury. Drake also links going on a drill to Chicago as Chicago is widely known as the birthplace of Drill music", "annotation_id": "39878589", "url": "https://genius.com/39878589", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "And I might just tell Kai get another Small price ’cause he kept it one hundred", "vault_lines": ["And I might just tell Kai get another", "Small price 'cause he kept it one hundred"], "meaning": "Kai Bent-Lee is a Canadian entrepreneur, influencer, and the son of legendary Toronto chef Susur Lee.\n\nHe and Drake are great friends and business partners. For instance, they opened a Toronto restaurant and lounge called Fring’s in 2015 before closing three years later in 2018.\n\nAdditionally, in 2022, Kai gifted Drake a massive, custom diamond ring named “Lucky Number 11.” It was crafted by celebrity jeweler Alex Moss.", "annotation_id": "39842424", "url": "https://genius.com/39842424", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "If I call up your shawty right now, she pickin’ up, yeah", "vault_lines": ["If I call up your shawty right now, shе pickin' up, yeah"], "meaning": "A plausible target is Kanye West. In 2018, Kanye took to Instagram in a series of videos addressing rumors that Drake had slept with Kim Kardashian, saying “The fact that it’s people making rumors or thinking that you fucked my wife and you not saying nothing and you carrying it like that — that don’t sit well with my spirit.”\n\n\n\nYe would bring up this dynamic again in 2025 when he shared a heated vent on his X account.\n\n\n\nThe Weeknd is another obvious target. Drake and The Weeknd’s relationship has been complicated for years, with long speculations that it all ties to their overlapping romantic histories; particularly over the model Bella Hadid. Drake referenced this on “Family Matters”:\n\nStill mad about that one ho, we ain’t even fuck, I just lip-locked with her\nI get active when it’s war time, I ain’t even really let my dick drop, nigga\n\nHowever the most current and sensical target is A$AP Rocky — a man Drake dedicates an entire song to on this very album. Rihanna, who Drake was publicly and emotionally entangled with for the better part of a decade, is now Rocky’s partner and the mother of his children.", "annotation_id": "39840900", "url": "https://genius.com/39840900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "These hoes know how I pop it for real These hoes know how I pop it for real, yeah", "vault_lines": ["These hoes know how I pop it for real", "These hoes know how I pop it for real, yeah"], "meaning": "“Pop it for real” sounds like “Pop at Pharrell”—in reference to Pharrell Williams. Drake first took shots at the producer on Travis Scott’s July 2023 track “MELTDOWN,” after Pharrell was made the men’s creative director for Louis Vuitton. He replaced the previous director and Drake’s close friend, the late Virgil Abloh:\n\nMan, fuck all that spinnin' the narrative shit\nI melt down the chains that I bought from yo' boss\nGive a fuck about all of that heritage shit\nSince V not around, the members done hung up the Louis, they not even wearing that shit\nDon’t come to the boy ‘bout repairing some shit\nDon’t come to the boy about sparing some shit\n\nKendrick Lamar—a friend of Pharrell—went to bat for him on April 2024’s “euphoria,” telling Drake to back off Pharrell:\n\nI don’t like you poppin' shit at Pharrell, for him, I inherit the beef\nYeah, fuck all that pushin' P, let me see you push a T", "annotation_id": "39840452", "url": "https://genius.com/39840452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "They say that karma could take an eternity, yours is droppin’ the same night as me Funny thing is that they ain’t gon’ compete, you gon’ get yours while I’m doin’ me You gon’ get yours while I’m doin’ me", "vault_lines": ["They say that karma could take an eternity, yours is droppin' the same night as me", "Funny thing is that they ain't gon' compete, you gon' get yours while I'm doin' me", "You gon' get yours while I'm doin' me"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the phrase “karma takes an eternity” by claiming he is the karma or rather, his new music is. He doesn’t have to wait for the universe to punish his enemies—his continued success and new releases will do it for him instead.\n\nDrake’s albums have always sold well upon release. For instance, his April 2016 album Views sold 1,040,000 copies in its first week, which was one of the biggest sales weeks in hip-hop history. By timing his drop to land on the exact same night as his target, he positions himself as the enforcer of that karma. He overshadows their moment with his album instead, knowing they wouldn’t stand a chance with his sales.", "annotation_id": "39838928", "url": "https://genius.com/39838928", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Yeah, yeah I made way too much in a week Must be two hundred and fifty at least", "vault_lines": ["I made way too much in a week", "Must be two hundred and fifty at least"], "meaning": "Drake is being deliberately nonchalant with it; tossing out $250,000 in a week as a casual estimate when the reality is that figure probably doesn’t survive contact with a single day of his actual earnings from music alone. The real easter egg though is in the timing as this bar lands exactly at the 2:50 mark of the track.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/SD4yRDY9mek?t=170", "annotation_id": "39838191", "url": "https://genius.com/39838191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Trickin’ it off on her, payin’ her bill That’s just how I do the sauce and the spill", "vault_lines": ["Trickin' it off on her, payin' her bill", "That's just how I do the sauce and the spill"], "meaning": "“Tricking” means to spend money lavishly on a woman in exchange for sexual intercourse. However, in this instance, he’s paying one of his opposition’s women to get information on them. This connects to the “sauce and the spill” line—he gets the secrets, then reveal them in his songs.\n\nAdditionally, this could be a reference to Houston-based rapper Sauce Walka—on June 1, 2026, a leaked phone call of Walka surfaced online where he threatened to have Drake killed for allegedly sleeping with his baby mother.", "annotation_id": "39834468", "url": "https://genius.com/39834468", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "We in Paris like two hundred deep, for real", "vault_lines": ["We in Paris like two hundred deep, for real"], "meaning": "A flex rooted in real events. In September 2025, Drake performed two sold-out nights in Paris at Accor Arena as part of his $ome $pecial $hows 4 EU tour, his first shows in France in six years. “Two hundred deep” refers to the size of his entourage the team, the crew, the whole operation that travels with him when he moves at that level.", "annotation_id": "39834324", "url": "https://genius.com/39834324", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "They say the truth will set you free, well, mine is gon’ stream while you watch in HD", "vault_lines": ["They say the truth will set you free, well, mine is gon' stream while you watch in HD"], "meaning": "“The truth will set you free” is a famous proverb  taken from John 8:31-32:\n\nSo Jesus said to the Jews who had believed him, “If you abide in my word, you are truly my disciples […] and you will know the truth, and the truth will set you free.\n\nThe “truth” Drake is talking about is the industry betrayals he has suffered and the 2024 rap beef. The HD detail ties directly into the ICEMAN rollout itself as Drake released four cinematic pre-album livestream episodes in the lead-up to ICEMAN.", "annotation_id": "39833899", "url": "https://genius.com/39833899", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "You say what my work means to me will one day be the death of me", "vault_lines": ["You say what my work means to me will one day be the death of me"], "meaning": "The perfectionism Drake documents across his catalog — nodded to as far back as “Tuscan Leather”— eventually caught up with him, as he works long hours and puts an enormous amount of effort into creating the sound that he and his fans desire, placing his health as a second priority. He famously decided to take a break from music at the end of 2023 to focus on his health issues, before being pulled back by the 2024 beef.", "annotation_id": "39833809", "url": "https://genius.com/39833809", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Blow on me just like some green tea, baby Ayy, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Blow on me just like some green tea, baby"], "meaning": "“Blow” is a sexual term for oral intercourse, making this a double entendre. High-quality green tea is brewed at 60°C / 140°F as the tea will be too bitter if the water temperature is too hot.", "annotation_id": "39833574", "url": "https://genius.com/39833574", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "How many more times is y’all gon’ keep callin’ it soft when it’s silk? Damn", "vault_lines": ["How many more times is y'all gon' keep callin' it soft when it's silk? Damn"], "meaning": "One of the most persistent criticisms of Drake’s career is that his music is too pop-adjacent for someone claiming a lane in rap. His rebuttal is that smooth isn’t the same thing as soft, it’s silk. Note the play on fabric types.", "annotation_id": "39830372", "url": "https://genius.com/39830372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Tired of all of y’all tellin’ me niggas is real, pussy, I know when it’s real You only come home to pose and pop off a look, but you forgot how it feel", "vault_lines": ["Tired of all of y'all tellin' me niggas is real, pussy, I know when it's real", "You only come home to pose and pop off a look, but you forgot how it feel", "These hoes know how I pop it for real", "These hoes know how I pop it for real, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake is questioning the weight and sincerity of Kendrick Lamar’s community engagement, basically saying that the distance is real when it comes to his connection with his hometown.\n\n\n\nDrake is filing Kendrick’s philanthropy under the same category as career politicians; showing up in the hood with cameras and goodwill that evaporates the moment the press does.\n\nThe turkeys reference is a nod to the iconic scene in New Jack City, where drug lord Nino Brown hands out Thanksgiving turkeys to his Harlem community, to maintain public image rather than out of any genuine care for the community.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FJ09gCCvDY\n\nDrake then demands actual receipts on what Kendrick’s big deals actually did for the people back home. This is an angle he first raised on “Family Matters”:\n\nDon’t even go back to your hood and plant no money trees", "annotation_id": "39816024", "url": "https://genius.com/39816024", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Thought they had me in a deep sleep, baby I’m still scorchin’ hot in these streets, baby", "vault_lines": ["Thought they had me in a deep sleep, baby", "I'm still scorchin' hot in these streets, baby"], "meaning": "After the 2024 beef concluded with Kendrick Lamar’s victory lap, the general consensus hardened that Drake was finished. This nods to the opening bar from verse one:\n\nAyy, buried alive, someone come dig me up\n\nHe also addressed this on “Make Them Remember”:\n\nThey tryna have me stuck in this position like rigor mortis\n\nAs of the release of ICEMAN on May 15, 2026, Drake still holds the all-time record for the most songs ever charted on the Billboard with 359 career Hot 100 entries.\n\nMoreover ahead of ICEMAN, reports also noted that multiple older Drake albums and songs were resurging on Billboard charts due to anticipation for the release.\n\nDrake remains the hottest artist in Hip Hop despite claims that he was dethroned due to his 2024 beef with Kendrick.", "annotation_id": "39793074", "url": "https://genius.com/39793074", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "You boys got big off my name, that’s big enough", "vault_lines": ["You boys got big off my name, that's big enough"], "meaning": "The “Drake Stimulus Package” is a well-known music industry phenomenon describing the massive career boost that artists receive when Drake features them or hop on their track. This is a reminder that several of his current opponents built their earliest mainstream foundations on his co-sign.\n\n\n\nDuring the 2012 Club Paradise Tour, Drake personally selected Kendrick Lamar and A$AP Rocky as opening acts; both have publicly credited the tour as a big moment that introduced them to wider audiences.\n\n\n\nDrake on stage with Kendrick Lamar and A$AP Rocky during the tour\n\nThe Weeknd is another case study. Drake was instrumental in platforming him during the Take Care era, co-signing his early mixtapes and bringing him into his orbit at a time when Abel was pretty much still an anonymous musician.\n\nBut the most relevant target this is alluding to is Kendrick who reached a career high during his 2024 beef with Drake. “Not Like Us” became his first number one single and the Super Bowl followed. Ironically, Drake who first gave Kendrick his earliest mainstream stage would end up being the vehicle for his greatest commercial peak.", "annotation_id": "39792805", "url": "https://genius.com/39792805", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "They tryna cover it up like a quilt", "vault_lines": ["They tryna cover it up like a quilt"], "meaning": "A direct shot at Kendrick Lamar and the narrative machine surrounding him. Drake is accusing the industry fans, media, and label of stitching together a flattering image of Kendrick out of mismatched scraps, just like a quilt: many pieces sewn together to cover something up.\n\nThe line ties directly into Drake’s long-running allegation that UMG conspired to artificially inflate “Not Like Us” on Spotify using bots and pay-to-play agreements to deceive consumers into believing the song was more popular than it actually was. To Drake, the quilt is the bot farm a manufactured cover story draped over the truth.\n\n\n\nIn the closing moments of ICEMAN Episode 4, Drake stood inside a room designed to resemble a bot farm filled with phones, one of which displayed the cover art for “Not Like Us.” Wearing Michael Jackson-inspired gloves, he then set the entire room ablaze. The quilt goes up in flames.", "annotation_id": "39792030", "url": "https://genius.com/39792030", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "[Verse 2]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is repurposing bars from his unreleased Pre-ICEMAN era track “That’s Just How I Feel.”\n\nThe repeated “Damn” throughout the verse is no accident. DAMN. is the title of Kendrick Lamar’s 2017 Pulitzer Prize-winning album. Drake uses it as a Interpolation throughout a verse loaded with Kendrick disses.\n\nhttps://i.scdn.co/image/ab67616d0000b2738b52c6b9bc4e43d873869699", "annotation_id": "39789640", "url": "https://genius.com/39789640", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Like the money just grew off a tree Like the money just came in for free, for real, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Like the money just grew off a tree", "Like the money just came in for free, for real, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake is flexing his spending habits, as if money is a renewable natural resource that replenishes itself endlessly.\n\n“Money Trees” happens to be the title of one of Kendrick Lamar’s biggest tracks. This ties back to Drake’s earlier questioning of whether Kendrick has actually converted his capital into anything tangible on the ground.\n\nThe subliminals carry on to Dave Free — Kendrick’s creative director and co-founder of pgLang; whose last name is embedded in the word “free.” The implication Drake’s insinuating here is that Dave is skimming Kendrick’s money behind his back. This mirrors Drake’s angle on “Push Ups”:\n\nExtortion baby, whole career, you been shook up\n‘Cause Top told you, “Drop and give me fifty,” like some push-ups, huh\n\n“For Free” also nods to Drake’s DJ Khaled collaboration of the same name, which in itself interpolates Kendrick’s “For Free?” from To Pimp A Butterfly.", "annotation_id": "39786228", "url": "https://genius.com/39786228", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "MRI machine scannin’ my knee Like how long you been runnin’ the streets? For real, yeah", "vault_lines": ["MRI machine scannin' my knee", "Like how long you been runnin' the streets? For real, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake has had multiple knee injuries, most recently in 2020 when he underwent surgery to repair a torn meniscus, which delayed his Certified Lover Boy project’s release until 2021.\n\nhttps://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcTLuqHJxF5xdYoZWeP3L_-RzTFgROTvjrcZDw&sDrake is wearing a compressive knee support brace on the same knee he tore his meniscus in\n\nHe ties this to a recurring theme that he has been running the game for more than a decade.", "annotation_id": "39786224", "url": "https://genius.com/39786224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "And I might book a flight out to London", "vault_lines": ["And I might book a flight out to London"], "meaning": "Drake has spent years building genuine ties to UK rap culture through collaborations with artists like Central Cee and Skepta, and the city has become one of his closest cultural homes outside North America.\n\nThat relationship was cemented when he headlined Wireless Festival 2025 for three consecutive nights, later describing it on Instagram as “the best 3 nights of my performance career.”", "annotation_id": "39784020", "url": "https://genius.com/39784020", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Reach me, baby Call my phone and say you need me, baby", "vault_lines": ["Call my phone and say you need me, baby"], "meaning": "Drake interpolates the melody and cadence of Lykke Li’s “I Follow Rivers” chorus:\n\nI-I follow, I follow you, deep sea baby\n\nWhile the original expresses obsessive devotion and emotional pursuit, Drake flips it into a more direct plea for connection and reassurance.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/K3JGxj2rvAs?t=75\n\nLi celebrated Drake’s interpolation of her 2011 hit:\n\nI think it’s potent. It has that raw, revenge, hip-hop energy — via Rolling Stones", "annotation_id": "39782267", "url": "https://genius.com/39782267", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Difference between niggas gettin’ you out of your deal and lettin’ you out of your deal, damn Against your will, how many new names do you got on your will? Damn", "vault_lines": ["Difference between niggas gettin' you out of your deal and lettin' you out of your deal, damn", "Against your will, how many new names do you got on your will? Damn"], "meaning": "These lines are aimed at Kendrick Lamar and his former Top Dawg Entertainment label boss, Anthony “Top Dawg” Tiffith. Lamar left TDE in 2022 to establish his own creative company, pgLang in a move which was positively framed publicly. Drake alleges that the situation may have been less clean than it appeared, and that Kendrick was forced to make massive financial sacrifices “against [his] will.” Asking how many names are on his “will” suggests Kendrick had to sign away a percentage of his future earnings or rights to his old label executives. Drake made similar claims on April 2024’s “Push Ups”:\n\nExtortion baby, whole career, you been shook up\n‘Cause Top told you, “Drop and give me fifty,” like some push-ups, huh\n\nThe use of “damn” as an ad-lib could be a reference to Kendrick’s April 2017 album, DAMN. Drake uses it to portray embarrassment for Kendrick and his alleged contract details.\n\nDrake definitely make a distinction with “getting-” and “letting- you out of your deal.” Getting out of your deal means you fought your way out. Drake is currently in the process of engineering his own exit from UMG as his contract is nearing fulfillment. The three albums release in one night wasn’t generosity. Parallels have been drawn to Frank Ocean’s 2016 departure from Def Jam.", "annotation_id": "39778834", "url": "https://genius.com/39778834", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "She just text me, like, “Oui, oui, oui, oui”", "vault_lines": ["She just text me, like, \"Oui, oui, oui, oui\""], "meaning": "“Oui, oui, oui, oui,” as yes in French, which also sounds like “We, we, we, we,” in English. Changes the meaning from her running the streets alone, to running the streets with Drake as well.", "annotation_id": "39778670", "url": "https://genius.com/39778670", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Swear my label gotta free me, baby", "vault_lines": ["Swear my label gotta free me, baby"], "meaning": "With the ongoing lawsuit, the alleged radio suppression, and the UMG-Spotify RICO allegations, Drake frames his position at his own label not as a business dispute but as captivity, a man being held against his will by an institution he believes actively worked and is still working against him.\n\nObservers noted that the triple album drop appears designed to fulfill his remaining contractual obligations and walk out the door.", "annotation_id": "39777896", "url": "https://genius.com/39777896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Pull up Maybach, beep-beep, baby And my shit came with the heat seats, baby", "vault_lines": ["And my shit came with the heat seats, baby", "Pull up Maybach, beep-beep, baby"], "meaning": "The line works as a double entendre.\n\nOn the surface, Drake is bragging about a Maybach with heated seats. Currently, he owns several, indluding the Mercedes-Maybach S-Class, a rare Mercedes-Maybach G 650 Landaulet, and a Mercedes-Maybach Pullman.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J6SsDBGmOfw&t\n\nAt the same time, “heat seats” sounds like “heat seeks” (heat-seeking missiles). Drake frames his Maybach like a spy car, likening himself to the iconic super spy James Bond, whose cars were outfitted with missiles and other weapons. Although Bond is more closely associated with Aston Martins, Drake updates this image through a more modern luxury vehicle, aligning himself with Bond’s charismatic, charming, wealthy, and womanizing persona.", "annotation_id": "39776805", "url": "https://genius.com/39776805", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "(Ayy, Janice, shut the fuck up)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Janice” is Joe Budden. The name traces back to a resurfaced clip from Budden’s own show where he asked a co-host for their “white woman name” and revealed his was Janice. At the end of the clip, Budden exclaims, “I'ma tell you, shut the fuck up”—the story behind the song’s title, “Janice STFU.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/0zudN9KKABM?feature=share\n\nJoe Budden has positioned himself as Drake’s top critic for many years long before the Kendrick feud made it popular. Here, Drake dismisses Budden’s decade of podcast commentary as unbearable noise that he can no longer stomach.\n\nThe audio itself may be taken from the series, The Sopranos with Tony Soprano snapping at his sister Janice to shut up mid-conversation. Prior to the album’s release, Drake posted pictures from the show on his burner Instagram account, likely as cryptic hints for ICEMAN’s rollout.", "annotation_id": "39776494", "url": "https://genius.com/39776494", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "I’m so green, you gotta teach me, baby From Vancouver, you a BC baby", "vault_lines": ["I'm so green, you gotta teach me, baby", "From Vancouver, you a BC baby"], "meaning": "Vancouver is a city in Drake’s home country, Canada. “BC” stands for British Columbia, the Canadian province where Vancouver is located.\n\n“Green”—derived from “greenhorn”—means to be inexperienced since it’s usually the color of immature and unripe fruit. Drake wants this woman to teach him some new sexual tricks.\n\nNotably, “green” is also slang for weed—British Columbia is well known for its legendary “BC Bud” strain, due to the region’s favorable growing climates and fertile land.", "annotation_id": "39776463", "url": "https://genius.com/39776463", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "How many more interviews y’all gonna do just to get Ice to chill? Damn", "vault_lines": ["How many more interviews y'all gonna do just to get Ice to chill? Damn"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is the most visible example. Fans were quick to clock that Ross had been bringing up Drake on practically every interview he appeared on, with one comment going viral: “This is like the 10th podcast he’s been on talking about Drake in the last week.” The appearances ranged from telling Bootleg Kev the beef “was never that deep” to telling another interviewer Drake “has a lot of issues he needs to address.”\n\n“Chill” means to calm down but ice is already cold by definition. Ice doesn’t chill. “Ice” is Drake’s alter ego for this ICEMAN era.", "annotation_id": "39776193", "url": "https://genius.com/39776193", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "’Rari go skrrt on a boy like a kilt", "vault_lines": ["'Rari go skrrt on a boy like a kilt"], "meaning": "A “kilt” is a Scottish garment for men, similar to a skirt for women, Drake compares it to “skrrting” (skirt) as in a Ferrari car drifting.", "annotation_id": "39775968", "url": "https://genius.com/39775968", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "And I went bought a whip for my brother Same body, but two different colors", "vault_lines": ["And I went bought a whip for my brother", "Same body, but two different colors"], "meaning": "In December 2025, Drake surprised streamer BenDaDonnn with a fully customized, blacked-out Mansory Rolls-Royce Spectre during a Stake livestream on Kick. It’s estimated to be valued at $800,000.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/iWVZOrTm_GM", "annotation_id": "39775925", "url": "https://genius.com/39775925", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "White kids listen to you ’cause they feel some guilt and that’s how your soul gets fulfilled", "vault_lines": ["White kids listen to you 'cause they feel some guilt and that's how your soul gets fulfilled"], "meaning": "The argument is one that has circulated in hip-hop forums long before this beef; that a significant portion of Kendrick’s white audience engages with his music not purely on artistic merit but as a form of white guilt absolution.\n\nKendrick’s soul, in Drake’s framing, gets filled not by genuine fan connection but by being the vessel through which white liberals abolish their guilt.", "annotation_id": "39775894", "url": "https://genius.com/39775894", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Kept it a hundred on paper like Wilt", "vault_lines": ["Kept it a hundred on paper like Wilt"], "meaning": "Wilt Chamberlain was an NBA player who set the scoring record with a 100-point game on March 2, 1962.\n\nChamberlain’s feat, which coincided with 25 rebounds, was not televised or widely recorded on video, thus limiting proof to the box score and a famous post game image of him holding the No. 100 on a piece of paper.", "annotation_id": "39775776", "url": "https://genius.com/39775776", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "We know how you OGs rockin’ already, my nigga, the jig is up", "vault_lines": ["We know how you OGs rockin' already, my nigga, the jig is up"], "meaning": "Hip-hop mogul JAY-Z, whose nickname is “Jigga” makes this land as both a punchline and an accusation. “The Jig is up” is an idiom meaning the deception is over, but Drake is directing it at someone whose behind-the-scenes maneuvering he’s done tolerating. “Rockin'” means how someone carries themselves but it’s also a nod to JAY’s label Roc-A-Fella Records.\n\nTheir history is complicated. Despite multiple collaborations, JAY’s fingerprints seem to appear every time Drake finds himself under serious fire.\n\n\n\nJAY’s Roc Nation was Meek Mill’s management home at the time he went public with his ghostwriting accusations against Drake, JAY notably never stepped in to cool things down.\n\n“The Story of Adidon”  which is Pusha T’s nuclear Drake diss that exposed his secret son Adonis. The beat itself sampled JAY’s track “The Story of O.J.”, a thing which he must have cleared.\n\nVia his company Roc Nation, JAY selected Kendrick Lamar to headline the Super Bowl LIX halftime show in New Orleans in February 2025 in the immediate aftermath of the beef, which was seen as a part of anti-Drake agenda.\n\n\n\nJAY has taken shots at Drake in interviews and on wax subliminally over the years, the kind of plausible-deniability style beef that Drake is now calling out directly.\n\nJust two weeks after ICEMAN dropped, Hov headlined the 2026 Roots Picnic in Philadelphia alongside The Roots and delivered a four-minute freestyle in response to this line directly:\n\nThe jig is up, nigga, I’m up 10, wrong chart champ\nNiggas looked up to Hov, I never looked up to them", "annotation_id": "39774966", "url": "https://genius.com/39774966", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Janice STFU", "line": "Ayy, buried alive, someone come dig me up", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, buried alive, somеone come dig me up"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing the widespread consensus that he was finished — artistically and culturally buried — in the wake of his 2024 beef with Kendrick Lamar, a framing that feels especially ironic considering Kendrick previously delivered the entirety of “Buried Alive Interlude” on Drake’s Take Care and then his diss track “Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2”.\n\nThe line plays on a string of high-profile write-offs that pushed this narrative. Joe Budden, never one to mince words, declared on his podcast: “I am not doing a back and forth with a corpse” — refusing to even engage with Drake as a relevant artist.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/OyF38ifk8Eo?feature=share\n\nAnother instance would be A$AP Rocky pilling on with “Ruby Rosary,”:\n\nLike, who dared me to die? (Uh)\nYou a dead man walkin' like you barely alive", "annotation_id": "39774824", "url": "https://genius.com/39774824", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
