{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Fuck, all you pussy, man, who could relate?", "vault_lines": ["Fuck, all you pussy, man, who could relate?"], "meaning": "WHO CAN RELATE, WOO!!", "annotation_id": "40856346", "url": "https://genius.com/40856346", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Damn, way niggas talkin’ ’bout Ice, must be a sale on microphone", "vault_lines": ["Damn, way niggas talkin' 'bout Ice, must be a sale on microphone"], "meaning": "“Ice” is Drake’s alter ego for the ICEMAN era. The punchline that follows is that his name became the easiest content play in hip-hop post the beef;  It’s like microphones went on sale overnight, everybody rushed to share their two cents on Drizzy.", "annotation_id": "39833134", "url": "https://genius.com/39833134", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "How you niggas go from askin’ for some tickets? Now you niggas tryna stop the show", "vault_lines": ["How you niggas go from askin' for some tickets? Now you niggas tryna stop the show"], "meaning": "People who were once in Drake’s DMs begging for tickets and fanboying him, are now trying to stop his momentum. The switch didn’t happen gradually; the beef flipped them overnight.\n\nThe most literal example of “stop the show” came with his own Anita Max Win Tour, when the remaining Australian and New Zealand dates were cancelled in July 2025 after stage equipment was shipped to Europe ahead of his Wireless Festival run, with Live Nation citing scheduling conflicts as the official reason. Kendrick Lamar fans immediately framed it as a victory lap, but the reality was rather logistical. Drake went on to headline all three nights of Wireless Festival in London, selling out in what became the fastest ticket sale in festival history.\n\nThe real irony is that when it comes to actually stopping shows, Drake himself wrote the playbook. ScHoolboy Q’s Toronto show was cancelled after Drake’s venue — History, which he co-owns, pulled out, with other venues in the city reportedly refusing to host TDE acts out of fear of retaliation.", "annotation_id": "39831147", "url": "https://genius.com/39831147", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Go to the hotel right after your game", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is another line aimed at professional basketball player DeMar DeRozan. DeRozan was playing for the Sacramento Kings at the time of this song’s release so with the Kings being in the Western conference and Drake’s team, the Toronto Raptors being in the Eastern conference, the Kings would only visit Toronto once for their lone road game against the Raptors.\n\nWith their falling out, Drake doesn’t want DeRozan staying around in his old hometown anymore longer than he needs to. Despite that, DeRozan still holds love and appreciation for Toronto—he interviewed with TSN in 2024 talking about it:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3mWvpQFou18", "annotation_id": "39812460", "url": "https://genius.com/39812460", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Alex Moss tryna bring me back a hundred years from now, he got my body froze", "vault_lines": ["Alex Moss tryna bring me back a hundred years from now, he got my body froze"], "meaning": "Alex Moss is a New York–based celebrity jeweler known for creating custom diamond pieces for popular rappers, including Drake. He has worked with Drake on numerous occasions—in October 2023, after the release of his album For All The Dogs, Moss made a one-of-a-kind chain for Drake called “The Crown Jewel of Toronto,” which showcased the city’s mascots and its professional sports teams. On May 16, 2026, Drake revealed custom, diamond-encrusted “Iceman” owl pendants, also made by Moss.\n\n“Got my body froze” means to be covered in “ice” or diamonds but Drake is also talking about cryonics, the practice of preserving legally a dead body at extremely low temperatures in liquid nitrogen, with the hope of reviving them later in time when medical advancements are developed.", "annotation_id": "39811582", "url": "https://genius.com/39811582", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "I would probably need a YouTube search just for me to do a laundry load", "vault_lines": ["I would probably need a YouTube search just for me to do a laundry load"], "meaning": "This is Drake’s way of saying how out of touch with normal, everyday life due to his wealthy lifestyle. As he points out on his 2009 rare cut “You Know, You Know,” he would rather have maids do his housework for him:\n\nYeah but what does it take to feel secure?\nA place with wooden floors and space to put awards\nA couple of parking spots and maids to do the chores", "annotation_id": "39811565", "url": "https://genius.com/39811565", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Ironic ’cause the Iceman was a nice man, now I’m hot and cold", "vault_lines": ["Ironic 'cause the Iceman was a nice man, now I'm hot and cold"], "meaning": "Drake has been known as a “nice guy” for many years but after an intense 2024 that saw the music industry turn against him, he’s now embracing a more cutthroat or “cold” persona. However, he’s still “hot” in terms of his musical relevancy—ICEMAN, alongside HABIBTI and MAID OF HONOUR debuted at number one, two, and three respectively. He became the first artist ever to do so, which showcases he’s still dominate on the Billboard charts.\n\nNotably, in the lead up to the album’s release, fans jokingly theorized that Drake would find a way to rhyme “Iceman” with “nice man.” As a result, ICEMAN AI-generated parody songs started to go viral on social platforms like TikTok. In fact, one of the parody songs went:\n\nI am the Iceman\nI’m a pretty nice man\nI’ll cook some rice, man", "annotation_id": "39787677", "url": "https://genius.com/39787677", "state": "accepted", "votes": 205, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Check signin’ is my kink, pushin’ out ink, I feel like a squid", "vault_lines": ["Check signin' is my kink, pushin' out ink, I feel like a squid"], "meaning": "Having accumulated a hefty sum of wealth from music and business ventures, Drake is flexing his riches and the fact that he is privileged enough to treat “check signing” as a kink.\n\nThe squid reference works on two levels. A squid can push out ink as a defense mechanism, which in a sense mirrors Drake’s post-beef posture after two years of people writing off his cultural relevance. “Pushing out ink” also means signing more checks.\n\nhttps://ocean.si.edu/sites/default/files/styles/full_width_large/public/2023-11/humboldt-squid-skerry.jpg.webp?itok=-ulgvSzV", "annotation_id": "39779201", "url": "https://genius.com/39779201", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Come do your show and get back on the plane But don’t stick around in the 6ix Don’t stick around in the 6ix", "vault_lines": ["Come do your show and get back on the plane", "But don't stick around in the 6ix", "Don't stick around in the 6ix"], "meaning": "In June 2025, Kendrick Lamar brought his Grand National Tour with SZA to Toronto for two sold-out shows at the Rogers Centre. After the first show date concluded, Kendrick was spotted leaving the ballpark with a heavy Toronto Police Service (TPS) and security escort.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nmm233I2Mt0\n\nDrake pokes fun at Kendrick for wanting to leave Toronto as soon as possible, likely fearful of a hostile situation or confrontation by Drake’s entourage as a result of the heightened tension of his presence in the city.\n\nIn addition, a video was posted online showing Toronto Police Service monitoring Drake’s house during Kendrick’s Toronto shows, suggesting TPS was concerned enough about a potential retaliation.", "annotation_id": "39776970", "url": "https://genius.com/39776970", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "All these niggas want a head tap, I did more damage off a body blow I can’t hit you off the iPhone shit, let me hit you off the lobby phone Niggas gotta be around, head on swivel ’cause we are not alone Niggas gotta be around Ice, on the same shit like we monotone Niggas gotta be around Ice with the sticks, I’m like a hockey coach Niggas better have a bag with a pipe like they came from a— What? Bag with a pipe like they came from a Scottish home", "vault_lines": ["All these niggas want a head tap, I did more damage off a body blow", "I can't hit you off the iPhone shit, let me hit you off the lobby phone", "Niggas gotta be around, head on swivel 'cause we are not alone", "Niggas gotta be around Ice, on the same shit like we monotone", "Niggas gotta be around Ice with the sticks, I'm like a hockey coach", "Niggas better have a bag with a pipe like they came from a—", "What? Bag with a pipe like they came from a Scottish home"], "meaning": "A “stick” and a “pipe” are both slang terms for guns. Drake makes a plays on both words—he’s directing his armed posse, much like how a hockey coach directs their players, who skate on ice with hockey sticks.\n\nSecondly, a bagpipe is a type of woodwind instrument that’s commonly associated with Scottish culture but Drake uses it to say his guys keep their guns concealed in bags.\n\n“Ice” is Drake’s alter ego for the ICEMAN era—he wants armed protection at all times.", "annotation_id": "39776209", "url": "https://genius.com/39776209", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "O— Okay O— Okay O— Okay O—", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the producer tag for Wraith9, a British recording artist and producer, known for working with UK artists like EsDeeKid, Fimiguerrero and fakemink. This song marks the first time Drake and Wraith9 have worked together.\n\nThe “ok” sample actually has deep roots in UK Grime and early 2000s dancehall, originating from a retro E-mu sound module, but Wraith flipped it into his own signature sound.", "annotation_id": "39776089", "url": "https://genius.com/39776089", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Out in the 6ix, I’m a national treasure The niggas that back me gon’ back me forever", "vault_lines": ["Out in the 6ix, I'm a national treasure", "The niggas that back me gon' back me forever"], "meaning": "“The 6ix” is a nickname for Toronto, which comes from the 416 and 647 area codes in the city. Also, at one point, Toronto was broken up into six areas, Old Toronto, Scarborough, East York, North York, Etobicoke, and York, giving the nickname more significance.\n\nDrake has done a lot for his hometown—he’s the global ambassador of the Toronto’s Raptors, put Toronto artists The Weeknd and PartyNextDoor on the big scene and has brought millions to Toronto’s economy. He shared a similar sentiment on his March 2020’s song, “When To Say When”:\n\nBrought a few Ws to the 6ix", "annotation_id": "36960704", "url": "https://genius.com/36960704", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Might just go take me a walk by the sea They diss on the boy ’til they blue in the face I put all the faces that’s blue in the safe", "vault_lines": ["Might just go take me a walk by the sea", "They diss on the boy 'til they blue in the face", "I put all the faces that's blue in the safe"], "meaning": "Drake uses wordplay to contrast his opposition’s' obsession with his own financial success.\n\n“Until you are blue in the face” is a common idiom that means to talk or complain about something until you are completely physically exhausted and out of breath. Meanwhile, “blue in the safe” are $100 bills. These bills specifically feature a light blue tint across Benjamin Franklin’s face—it entered circulation in October 2013.\n\nIn August 2025, Drake showed off a picture of his safe in an Instagram post:\n\n\n\nDrake is also nodding to his collection of luxury watches – the Rolex Submariner in its iconic blue colorway in particular.\n\nThis line also connects with the previous one. Sea is a homophone for the letter “C” which stands for Crip, the Los Angeles street gang alliance historically associated with the color blue. Like how the sea is blue, Like the sea, the Crips run blue.\n\nThe Crip Walk (or the C-Walk) is the intricate footwork dance that originated with the Crips in the 1970s. initially used as a form of street sign language to show loyalty and taunt rivals before evolving into a defining staple of West Coast hip-hop culture. This could also be read as a rebuttal to one of the most talked-about moments of the 2025 Super Bowl halftime show. During Kendrick Lamar’s performance of “Not Like Us,” the camera cut to Serena Williams — Drake’s reported ex — C-walking on an elevated platform as the diss track played to 127 million viewers.", "annotation_id": "36949637", "url": "https://genius.com/36949637", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "And my mind on the money, I’m rackin’ my brain And none of you pussies is actin’ the same", "vault_lines": ["And my mind on the money, I'm rackin' my brain", "And none of you pussies is actin' the same", "None of you pussies is actin' the same"], "meaning": "When someone is “racking their brain,” it means they’re trying hard to remember something or solve a problem—Drake is focused on making his money—but a “rack” is also slang for $1,000, creating a double entendre.\n\nDrake uses a similar lyric from his October 2023 song, “All The Parties”:\n\nMoney on my mind, I’m rackin' my brain\n\nHe also uses these lyrics on the following ICEMAN track, “B’s On The Table.”", "annotation_id": "36949575", "url": "https://genius.com/36949575", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "R.I.P. Kobe, but y’all niggas brokie That’s probably why y’all be shackin’ together", "vault_lines": ["R.I.P. Kobe, but y'all niggas brokie", "That's probably why y'all be shackin' together"], "meaning": "This is a homage to Kobe Bryant, the legendary NBA player who passed away in 2020.\n\n\n\nDrake contrasts Kobe’s status/success with others' he calls “brokie,” who have to come together or “shack up” to survive or get by.\n\nHowever, “shackin'” is a homophone to Shaquille O’Neal, another legendary NBA player who shared the Lakers' colors with Kobe at one point, and they had a complicated relationship. The reference basically means that Drake and these presumed “brokies” don’t see eye to eye.\n\n\n\n Additionally, Shaq used to break backboards with his dunks which gives the “brokie” label an extra layer of meaning.", "annotation_id": "36947564", "url": "https://genius.com/36947564", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Yeah For the city, though Yeah They gotta light the tower up for this Yeah Okay", "vault_lines": ["For the city, though", "They gotta light the tower up for this"], "meaning": "The “tower” Drake is talking about is the CN Tower in Toronto, one of Canada’s most iconic landmarks. The tower is often lit up in different colors to mark national events, holidays or major sports wins (for example, when the Raptors won the NBA championship, or for Canada Day).\n\n\n\nThe CN Tower was lit up appropriately with the red celebrating the Toronto Raptors NBA 2019 Championship win\n\nDrake is boasting, saying that dropping new music and being in his bag musically is so big and deserving to be treated like a historic national event. It also highlights his influence within Canada, befitting of the track title.\n\nDuring ICEMAN Episode 4, his fourth and final pre-release livestream, Drake “froze” the CN Tower using a massive projection-mapping stunt powered by 75 projectors.\n\nhttps://www.vmcdn.ca/f/files/shared/feeds/cp/2026/05/22ec0f67d7cf1a7cdae5d519d2dac18cbc588d6de41222bb0939491f7eeb8c61.jpg;w=1200;h=800;mode=crop", "annotation_id": "36947500", "url": "https://genius.com/36947500", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "I sent ’em a sack ’cause I wanted you shipped to the king When you was a part of the team We used to be plannin’ our Mexico trip in the spring We must’ve been dealin’ in spur of the moment ’Cause why did we think you could get us a ring? They braggin’ ’bout how you went home, the fuck are they on? Crodie, we threw ’em away G Pop sent us a real one from Daygo And next thing we knew, we was doin’ parades", "vault_lines": ["I sent 'еm a sack 'cause I wanted you shipped to the king", "When you was a part of the team", "We used to be plannin' our Mexico trip in the spring", "We must've been dealin' in spur of the moment", "'Cause why did we think you could get us a ring?", "They braggin' 'bout how you went home, the fuck are they on?", "Crodie, we threw 'em away", "G Pop sent us a real one from Daygo", "And next thing we knew, we was doin' parades"], "meaning": "Drake is taking shots at professional basketball player DeMar DeRozan, who was drafted by Drake’s hometown basketball team, Toronto Raptors in 2009 and played there for nine seasons. DeRozan was beloved by the Toronto fans, including Drake himself, but his teams consistently fell short in the NBA playoffs. When a team gets bounced in the NBA playoffs, it’s often joked that the losing team’s players are going to “Cancun”—located in Mexico—to start their early offseason vacations, which Drake nods to here.\n\nIn the 2018-19 NBA season, DeRozan was traded to the San Antonio Spurs—who was coached by hall of fame coach, Gregg Popovich or “G Pop” at the time. In exchange, the Raptors got Kawhi Leonard—who has strong ties to San Diego or “Daygo.” They would go on to win their first NBA championship in team history later that year and as such, celebrated with a parade.\n\nOn his May 2024 diss track, “Not Like Us,” Kendrick Lamar brought up DeRozan, saying he was “glad” he came home—as both are Compton natives—but Drake pushes back, noting that his team didn’t need him since they won right after he was traded. Additionally, DeRozan showed support for Kendrick by appearing at his “The Pop Out: Ken & Friends” concert and making a cameo in the “Not Like Us” music video.\n\nAt the time of this song’s release, DeRozan was playing for the Sacramento Kings, which Drake puns at with “sack” and “king.” He also intentionally makes “cause why” sounds like “Kawhi” and “cuh why,” likely as a nod to Demar’s affiliation with the LA street gang Crips.", "annotation_id": "36944877", "url": "https://genius.com/36944877", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "National Treasures", "line": "Capo and Lita got habits together Like couples vacation, they packin’ together Like rushin’ to finish, they’ll slap it together The charge is like COVID, they’ll catch it together They hate me so much, it’s a sickenin’ thing They must’ve heard wrong on the call", "vault_lines": ["The charge is like COVID, they'll catch it together", "They hate me so much, it's a sickenin' thing", "They must'vе heard wrong on the call", "Capo and Lita got habits together", "Like couples vacation, they packin' together", "Like rushin' to finish, they'll slap it together"], "meaning": "“Capo” is a nickname for Nessel “Chubbs” Beezer is one of Drake’s closest friends and his OVO head of security, while Lita is another unknown OVO affiliate and enforcer.\n\nDrake notes his guys would do anything in order to protect him, like staying strapped and catching murder charges if they have to. By mentioning the contagious virus COVID-19, Drake employs a double entendre on the word “catch” and tops it off with a cough at the end of the line—a symptom of the virus.\n\nAdditionally, “rushin' to finish” is a homophone to “Russian to Finnish.” Russia and Finland—home of the Finnish—share a land border, continuing Drake’s wordplay on “slap together.” It also could be a nod to the 1939 Soviet Union attack on Finland, which was also known as The Winter War—tying into the theme of the album’s title, ICEMAN.", "annotation_id": "36944445", "url": "https://genius.com/36944445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
