{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Come see us and get it fixed, boy (Hey)", "vault_lines": ["Come see us and get it fixed, boy (Hey)", "Get hurt, boy (Get hurt)"], "meaning": "Drake is challenging any opposition, daring them to approach him and his crew and get put in their place.", "annotation_id": "40043852", "url": "https://genius.com/40043852", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Yeah, so I don’t give a fuck about what anybody sayin’ right now", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, so I don't give a fuck about what anybody sayin' right now"], "meaning": "Because he’s at the top and controlling the scene, he doesn’t care about critics, haters, or what anyone else has to say about him.", "annotation_id": "34469960", "url": "https://genius.com/34469960", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Yeah, my sound got the whole city in your way right now", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, my sound got the whole city in your way right now"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that his music and influence are so strong that they affect everything happening in the city. It could also mean that his presence in the industry is so big that others feel blocked or overshadowed by him.", "annotation_id": "34469948", "url": "https://genius.com/34469948", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Aww, here go another muhfucker that don’t understand The concept of puttin’ money first, boy (First)", "vault_lines": ["Aww, here go another muhfucker that don't understand", "The concept of puttin' money first, boy (First)"], "meaning": "Most people don’t understand that you gotta care about money more than everything else.", "annotation_id": "31601539", "url": "https://genius.com/31601539", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Yeah, I know you heard things Nobody really likes us except for us Yeah, all I ever needed was the squad so that’s what’s up", "vault_lines": ["I know you heard things", "Bitch, I know you heard things", "Yeah, I know you heard things, I know you", "Yeah, I know you heard things", "Nobody really likes us except for us", "Yeah, all I ever needed was the squad so that's what's up"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "28518396", "url": "https://genius.com/28518396", "state": "pending", "votes": -5, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "(Worst)", "vault_lines": ["Ahh, that's the worst, boy (Worst)"], "meaning": "This ad-lib was pulled from Drake’s “Worst Behavior.” The song did fairly well, winning Best Hip Hop Video and People’s Champ Award at the 2014 BET Hip Hop Awards, as well as peaking at 89th on the Billboard Hot 100 chart.", "annotation_id": "12677775", "url": "https://genius.com/12677775", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "[Produced by Boi-1da and SykSense]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s long-time collaborator Boi-1da and up-and-coming Nashville-based producer SykSense pieced together this bass-heavy banger around a melody pulled from the 1995 SNES video game Donkey Kong Country 2: Diddy Kong’s Quest.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CFRsMztuM30\n\nSykSense is half of the production duo The Fam, who also produced Drizzy’s “Draft Day”. Read their incredible verified Producer Genius annotation here!", "annotation_id": "4255243", "url": "https://genius.com/4255243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "I’m ’bout to hit you with the work, boy (Work) I’m ’bout to hit you with the work, boy (Work)", "vault_lines": ["I'm 'bout to hit you with the work, boy (Work)"], "meaning": "Drake “hit” everyone with two singles on the date of October 25, 2014. He released this full length track along with “Heat of the Moment”, and “How Bout Now”.\n\nThat’s some work, boy.\n\n\n\nSo far in 2014, Drake has seriously been putting in a lot of work. Between helping out with two singles on Tha Carter V, going on tour, and working on his own projects, Drizzy is constantly raising his net worth, which is what we know he really wants to do.", "annotation_id": "4244184", "url": "https://genius.com/4244184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Rrrr ting (Brrt, brrt)", "vault_lines": ["Rrrr ting (Brrt, brrt)"], "meaning": "The song is introduced with a common Toronto slang term. Drake provided a pronunciation on Instagram six months earlier.\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/nOp_7BDQFo", "annotation_id": "4244061", "url": "https://genius.com/4244061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "I’m the real 6 God, boy (Woah)", "vault_lines": ["I'm the real 6 God, boy (Woah)"], "meaning": "6, short for 416 and 647, Toronto’s area codes, is often used by OVO Crew members in their songs.\n\nDrizzy is from Toronto. Other rappers might have claimed themselves the god of the Toronto rap scene but the throne already belongs to Drake.", "annotation_id": "4243920", "url": "https://genius.com/4243920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 120, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Watch your motherfuckin’ tone, boy (Watch that) Get hurt, boy (Get hurt)", "vault_lines": ["Watch your motherfuckin' tone, boy (Watch that)", "Get hurt, boy (Get hurt)"], "meaning": "Drake has refused to release diss tracks in response to other artists, but that hasn’t stopped him from sending countless subliminal warning shots in his records. It’s safe to say you don’t want to make Drake catch a body like that.\n\nThe usage of “boy” should be noted – it seems to be his ad-lib as of late (sorry Big Sean) and was used heavily on “0 to 100”:\n\n360 with the wrist, boy\nWho the fuck them niggas is, boy?\nOVO, man we really with the shits, boy\n\n\n\n * Disclaimer: While this was released just after YMCMB label-mate Tyga went after him in a Vibe interview, a snippet of this track was leaked way back in August, two months before this feud started.", "annotation_id": "4243916", "url": "https://genius.com/4243916", "state": "pending", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Black Benz on the road, boy (On the road) Already had a Rolls Royce (On the road) Sold a couple Bentley last week Them were my old toys (Man, this shit is old)", "vault_lines": ["I hear the talk on road is I'm the shit, boy", "Black Benz on the road, boy (On the road)", "Already had a Rolls Royce (On the road)", "Sold a couple Bentley last week", "Them were my old toys (Man, this shit is old)"], "meaning": "Needless to say, Drake owns a lot of cars. He’s been the proud owner of a Mercedes and Rolls Royce in the past, but those are nothing compared to his $3 million Bugatti.\n\nDrake also owns a brand new 2015 Mercedes S-Class coupe, tuned by Brabus. He’s the only person in North America to own this bad boy!", "annotation_id": "4243897", "url": "https://genius.com/4243897", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "It’s a OVO come through murk things I know you heard things Bitch, I know you heard things", "vault_lines": ["It's a OVO come through murk things", "I know you heard things", "Bitch, I know you heard things", "Yeah, I know you heard things, I know you", "Yeah, I know you heard things"], "meaning": "OVO Sound is Drake’s record label started in 2012. It was founded alongside good friend and producer Noah “40” Shebib, and was started to persue Drake’s ambitions to try more things as opposed to just music.\nDrake’s been active since 2006, making it eight years in the game from him. There’s no question he’s one of the few running the game right now. As a matter of fact, we all know he’s the only one that gets the job done.", "annotation_id": "4243891", "url": "https://genius.com/4243891", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "I’m not new to this", "vault_lines": ["I'm not new to this", "I'm not new to this, I'm not new, I'm not new"], "meaning": "I’m the rookie and the vet\n\nDrake dropped his first album in 2010, and his first mixtape in 2006. This means that as of the release of this song Drake has been in the game for eight years, much longer than many other new school stars. Drake has been in the entertainment industry even longer that, ever since his role on Degrassi.", "annotation_id": "4243838", "url": "https://genius.com/4243838", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "I hear the talk on road is I’m the shit, boy", "vault_lines": ["I hear the talk on road is I'm the shit, boy", "Black Benz on the road, boy (On the road)"], "meaning": "“Talk on road” is another way of saying “Word on the street.” Word On Road is also the name of his official fan site.\n\n\n\nWord on road is the clique about to blow", "annotation_id": "4243824", "url": "https://genius.com/4243824", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Coming from the motherfucking 6 side", "vault_lines": ["Coming from the motherfucking 6 side"], "meaning": "While the predominant hometowns of hip-hop legends are in the United States (places like New York, Chicago, LA, etc), it’s rare for an artist born in another country to make an impact as big as Drake has.\n\nThis could also be in reference to Toronto’s crime – only Drake could avoid getting caught in this lifestyle and end up becoming successful.", "annotation_id": "4243775", "url": "https://genius.com/4243775", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "You was poppin’ back when Usher wore a U-chain Goddamn, you changed", "vault_lines": ["You was poppin' back when Usher wore a U-chain"], "meaning": "Usher used to be famous for his U-chain back in his prime during the 2000’s.\n\n\n\nDuring this period, Chris Brown released the track “Poppin”. Brown and Drake had a public spat in 2014, that continued well in to 2015.", "annotation_id": "4243772", "url": "https://genius.com/4243772", "state": "pending", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Rolling swishers, hittin’ swishes Got me feelin’ like a ball hog I don’t pass ’em when I get it (Naw)", "vault_lines": ["Rolling swishers, hittin' swishes", "Got me feelin' like a ball hog", "I don't pass 'em when I get it (Naw)"], "meaning": "Drake smokes some blunts until he has the warm smell of colitas rising up through the air.  While it is good sportsmanship to pass the blunt to your neighbor, like sharing the basketball with the rest of your team, Drake prefers to keep it all to himself when he gets it because it’s just that good.\n\nSwisher Sweets are a popular brand of cigar that you can freak (twist all the tobacco out) and refill with that sweet marijuana.\n\nHe compares the act of puffing and passing to basketball because he “hits swishes” or drains shots on the court rather than passing the ball. The double entendre is quite ironic due to his recent airball when playing with the Kentucky Wildcats.", "annotation_id": "4243768", "url": "https://genius.com/4243768", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "line": "Phone call back home, shit is hot up in the 6, boy (Hot up in the 6) Shit hot up in the 6 right now Shit hot up in the 6, boy (Hot up in the 6) Shit is hot up in the 6 right now (Ga, ga, ga)", "vault_lines": ["Phone call back home, shit is hot up in the 6, boy (Hot up in the 6)", "Shit hot up in the 6 right now", "Shit hot up in the 6, boy (Hot up in the 6)", "Shit is hot up in the 6 right now (Ga, ga, ga)"], "meaning": "Drake just got a phone call saying that something is going down in Toronto right now, something like a party. Drake is about to head over and turn up in his hometown, Drake is always down with his city and even claims to run it:\n\nAll these old heads keep they minds on one track\nAnd me, I run Toronto, holla at me when you done laps \n\nHe is so down with his city that he was even made an executive of the Toronto Raptors basketball team.", "annotation_id": "4243761", "url": "https://genius.com/4243761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "6 God", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 19, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-6-god-lyrics", "genius_title": "6 God"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Don’t push me when I’m in album mode", "vault_lines": ["Don't push me when I'm in album mode"], "meaning": "Drake rapped something similar in his Meek Mill diss track “Back to Back,” months before he released his fourth studio album, Views:\n\nThey gon' ask if I can play this shit back to back\nI took a break from Views, now it’s back to that, nigga\n\nOn April 16th, Drizzy took to social media to announce Scorpion, an upcoming project that may be released in June. As one would assume, that’s what the majority of his focus has been during the months leading towards the release. “Don’t push me” is also a play on Pusha-T’s moniker.", "annotation_id": "14646555", "url": "https://genius.com/14646555", "state": "accepted", "votes": 123, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Yeah, who gassed you to play with me? Man, you made this shit as easy as ABCs", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, who gassed you to play with me?", "Man, you made this shit as easy as ABCs"], "meaning": "Drake insinuates that Push’s confidence has been overinflated by his peers and that he’s been set up for failure.\n\nThe second line may be reference to the track “Chinese New Year” by Clipse where Push says:\n\nADT’s ain’t stop me, simple like ABC’s\nSnip, cut, yank just as easy as 1-2-3,", "annotation_id": "14646537", "url": "https://genius.com/14646537", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "I told Weezy and Baby, “I’ma done him for you”", "vault_lines": ["I told Weezy and Baby, \"I'ma done him for you\""], "meaning": "Drake gets back at Push for bringing up the Lil Wayne and Birdman feud which started back in 2006 when Lil Wayne allegedly bit Clipse’s style by wearing BAPE clothing in his “Hustler Musik” music video and on the front cover of Vibe Magazine, but persisted into the ‘10s and Pusha’s track “Exodus 23:1.” Drake signed to Young Money in 2009.", "annotation_id": "14646498", "url": "https://genius.com/14646498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "You send shots, well I got to challenge those But I bring Calicos to the Alamo", "vault_lines": ["You send shots, well I got to challenge those", "But I bring Calicos to the Alamo"], "meaning": "While promoting his DAYTONA album, Pusha stopped by various media outlets and spoke on his disses aimed at Drake, saying he felt like he had to challenge Drake’s ghostwriting rumors since Drake had challenged his street credibility on “Two Birds One Stone.”\n\nA Calico, or Calico M-900, is a powerful automatic gun. “The Alamo” was a battle between Mexico and the Republic of Texas in 1836. Drake uses guns and warfare as a metaphor to suggest that when his enemies attack him lyrically—“send shots”—he will respond with much more advanced weaponry.\n\nReferencing a Mexican–American battle is likely a nod to Pusha T’s lyric on “Numbers On The Board,” where he compares himself to Mexican cartel leaders:\n\nI got money with the best of ‘em\nGo blow for blow with any Mexican", "annotation_id": "14646435", "url": "https://genius.com/14646435", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Tired of sittin’ quiet and helpin’ my enemies eat Keep gettin’ temperature checks They know that my head overheats", "vault_lines": ["Tired of sittin' quiet and helpin' my enemies eat", "Keep gettin' temperature checks", "They know that my head overheats"], "meaning": "Prior to his beef with Meek Mill in 2016, Drake had feuds with many other rappers who checked his temperature (lyrical ability) but never released full diss tracks until he responded (overheated) to Meek Mill with “Charged Up” and “Back to Back.”\n\nIn a 2017 interview with DJ Semtex, Drake discussed not exposing artists who used ghostwriters, stating:\n\nIf I was like an evil spirit, if I had like a different agenda, I could sit here and tell you how the shit really works. I could sit here and tell you 10, 20 people that are worse than me…that literally take everything and it’s just a verbatim process. But I’m not like that.\n\nDrake seems to have taken particular offense to Pusha’s latest disses on DAYTONA, and reneged on his interview stance, because the people who are dissing him—G.O.O.D Music—are the same people asking for his help to write for Kanye.", "annotation_id": "14646328", "url": "https://genius.com/14646328", "state": "accepted", "votes": 193, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Don’t know why the fuck you niggas listen to Denim or Steve", "vault_lines": ["Don't know why the fuck you niggas listen to Denim or Steve"], "meaning": "This line refers to Denim Tears and Steven Victor.\n\nDenim Tears is a fashion figure who dabbles in music and has been seen working with Kanye at his new Yeezy company.\n\nSteven Victor is the Executive Vice President at Universal Music Group and Head of A&R at Def Jam. He also is the manager and close friend of Pusha-T.", "annotation_id": "14646279", "url": "https://genius.com/14646279", "state": "accepted", "votes": 140, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "To be honest Duppy", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The term “duppy” is another name for an evil spirit, demon or the ghost of a dead person, originating from Jamaican folklore. Drake feels like Pusha’s a ghost of the past and isn’t relevant in 2018.\n\nBoi-1da, the Jamaican-born producer of the freestyle and longtime Drake collaborator confirmed the ghost reference on Twitter:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Boi1da/status/1000141177822248961\n\nWhilst the term originated from Jamaica, it’s often used as Toronto and UK street slang, meaning to destroy or annihilate someone. The word gets used a lot when grime artists beef with each other. The original “Duppy Freestyle” was made by Skepta when he dissed fellow UK rapper Megaman in 2006.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5VJnngsZl0g", "annotation_id": "14646278", "url": "https://genius.com/14646278", "state": "accepted", "votes": 170, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Please believe your demise will be televised, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Please believe your demise will be televised, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake references to Gill Scott-Heron’s song “The Revolution Will Not Be Televised,” and flips it against Pusha.\n\nPusha previously referenced Gill Scott’s song on 2015’s “M.F.T.R,” rapping:\n\nThe illusion of money we don’t believe in\nYou ask me, Tyga looking like a genius\nI’m Kim Jong of the crack song\nGil Scott-Heron to the black poem\nWoo, the revolution will be televised\n‘Cause we done see all and they telling lies\n\nDrake may have interpreted these lyrics as a shot at him given Tyga was previously signed to Cash Money Records before joining G.O.O.D Music Management.", "annotation_id": "14646273", "url": "https://genius.com/14646273", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Look, holla at me when you multi-million", "vault_lines": ["Look, holla at me when you multi-million"], "meaning": "This is a direct response to Pusha’s Lil Wayne diss on “Infrared”:\n\nAnother multi-platinum rapper trapped and can’t retire\n\nDrake’s discography boasts 6 platinum albums and 1 gold mixtape as of May 25, 2018:\n\n\n\nNothing Was The Same 4x platinum\n\nViews 6x platinum\n\nWhat A Time To Be Alive (Collab album w/ Future) 1x platinum\n\nTake Care 4x platinum\n\nIf You’re Reading This It’s Too Late 2x platinum\n\nThank Me Later 1x platinum\n\nSo Far Gone 1x gold\n\n\n\nDespite having a head start on Drake, Pusha has yet to release a solo project that has gone as much as gold. His only certified records to date are Clipse’s 2002 album Lord Willin’ and a feature on Future’s “Move That Dope.”", "annotation_id": "14646260", "url": "https://genius.com/14646260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "I had a microphone of yours, but then the signature faded I think that pretty much resembles what’s been happenin’ lately", "vault_lines": ["I had a microphone of yours, but then the signature faded", "I think that pretty much resembles what's been happenin' lately"], "meaning": "Drake once owned a signed Clipse era Pusha-T microphone that he bought off eBay for $200. In a WorldStar video, Drake spoke of how he used to pretend to do interviews on the red carpet and perform Clipse songs with the microphone.\n\nLike a signature would naturally fade over time, Drake feels like Pusha’s career has been declining too.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/HipHopShoops/status/677999021865066496", "annotation_id": "14646254", "url": "https://genius.com/14646254", "state": "accepted", "votes": 205, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "I could never have a Virgil in my circle And hold him back ’cause he makes me nervous I wanna see my brothers flourish to they higher purpose You niggas leeches and serpents I think it’s good that now the teachers are learnin’, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I could never have a Virgil in my circle", "And hold him back 'cause he makes me nervous", "I wanna see my brothers flourish to they higher purpose", "You niggas leeches and serpents", "I think it's good that now the teachers are learnin', yeah"], "meaning": "Virgil Abloh is a fashion designer and, as of 2018, head menswear designer at Louis Vuitton. He is most known for his streetwear brand Off White and was previously head of Kanye’s creative brand DONDA. He’s assisted Kanye with everything from album covers (as early 808s & Heartbreak) to stage design for tours like Yeezus.\n\nThis lyric is a reference to Kanye’s interviews with Axel Vervoordt and Charlamagne Tha God where Kanye said that Virgil becoming the director of Louis Vuitton made him feel a little nervous and seemed envious of his friends’ success, specifically Virgil. Kanye previously had a deal with Nike, but had a falling out with the footwear company and signed with Adidas. In 2017, Virgil Abloh collaborated with the fashion juggernaut.\n\nDrake has already expressed his will to see people around him succeed, which is one of the reasons for the creation of OVO Sound. In an old interview, he stated:\n\nAll I ever wanna do is just see this city get the recognition and the love it deserves, see people from this city shine. Y’know, I put a lot of people in positions to do great things. That’s all I wanna keep doing.\n\n“Virgil in my circle” could also be a reference to Dante’s poem “Inferno,” where the poet Virgil guides Dante through the 9 circles of hell.", "annotation_id": "14646196", "url": "https://genius.com/14646196", "state": "accepted", "votes": 259, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Tell ‘Ye we got a invoice comin’ to you Considerin’ that we just sold another 20 for you", "vault_lines": ["Tell 'Ye we got a invoice comin' to you", "Considerin' that we just sold another 20 for you"], "meaning": "Drake estimates that the extra attention Pusha’s album DAYTONA is going to get from this diss track will result in an additional 20,000 units sold.\n\nPush responded to this line on Twitter minutes after the song dropped:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/PUSHA_T/status/1000145724506550272\n\nDrake followed up by posting an invoice addressed to G.O.O.D Music on his Instagram for $100,000.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BjOL4WcHt-E/\n\nThis is a similar move to what Drake promised to do on his Meek Mill diss, “Back 2 Back,” with respects to bestowing gifts on his enemies. He sent Charlamagne 6 bottles of Dom Pérignon.", "annotation_id": "14646177", "url": "https://genius.com/14646177", "state": "accepted", "votes": 472, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Your brother said it was your cousin, then him, then you So, you don’t rap what you did, you just rap what you knew Don’t be ashamed, it’s plenty niggas that do what you do There’s no malice in your heart, you’re an approachable dude", "vault_lines": ["Your brother said it was your cousin, then him, then you", "So, you don't rap what you did, you just rap what you knew", "Don't be ashamed, it's plenty niggas that do what you do", "There's no malice in your heart, you're an approachable dude"], "meaning": "Drake’s referring to a 2017 VLAD interview where No Malice talked about how he and Push got involved in the drug game:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/MkvXVjpbFGI?t=201\n\nPrior to being a solo act, Pusha-T performed with his biological brother “Malice” as the duo Clipse. The theme of their music was centered around their past selling cocaine. After three albums, Malice decided that he was no longer comfortable with the image nor the message he was promoting and changed his rap name to “No Malice” and retired from coke rap.\n\nJAY-Z spit a similar line in his Nas “Takeover” diss, which lends itself to Drake’s plea that there are others who do the same thing:\n\nNigga, you ain’t live it, you witnessed it from your folks' pad\nYou scribbled it in your notepad and created your life", "annotation_id": "14646090", "url": "https://genius.com/14646090", "state": "accepted", "votes": 207, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "I’m too resilient, get out your feelings It’s gonna be a cruel summer for you", "vault_lines": ["I'm too resilient, get out your feelings", "It's gonna be a cruel summer for you"], "meaning": "Cruel Summer is a collaborative album spearheaded by Kanye featuring members and affiliates of G.O.O.D Music. Drake is planning to release his album Scorpion in June 2018.\n\nDrake seems to be suggesting that Pusha is sabotaging his own summer by challenging him.  However, Pusha was quick to reciprocate this threat on “The Story of Adidon,” promising to subject Drake to a “surgical summer” of lyrical assaults.", "annotation_id": "14646079", "url": "https://genius.com/14646079", "state": "accepted", "votes": 139, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "I just left from over by y’all puttin’ pen to the sheets", "vault_lines": ["I just left from over by y'all puttin' pen to the sheets"], "meaning": "Drake seemingly confirms the rumors that he was writing for Kanye while he was in Wyoming.\n\nPrior to the release of this freestyle, Kanye had been spending time at a secret location in Wyoming working on music. Drake was photographed in the same location on March 15th, leading to speculation that he was contributing to the album.\n\nAfter the release of the album Paul Rosenberg confirmed on Hot97 that Drake wrote the hook for “Yikes”, but was left uncredited:\n\n\nIt’s now a confirmed thing that Drake wrote the hook for ‘Yikes,‘” said Rosenberg. “It may be the best melody on the album when you go listen to it. You’ll hear it now and go, ‘Oh, that does sound really good. I get that.\n\nThey left him off. He doesn’t have a writing credit. Interesting timing to forget the writing credit.", "annotation_id": "14646076", "url": "https://genius.com/14646076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 253, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "I told you keep playin’ with my name And I’ma let it ring on you like Virginia Williams", "vault_lines": ["I told you keep playin' with my name", "And I'ma let it ring on you like Virginia Williams"], "meaning": "Drake mimics Pusha’s flow when he delivers this line to imply he could replace him. In terms of the rap game and in his personal life—in 2017, Pusha became engaged to his longtime girlfriend Virginia Williams.\n\n\n\nDrake’s delivery is also similar to when he checked Tory Lanez on “Do Not Disturb.” “Ring on you” is Toronto slang that means “shoot at you.”\n\nPush would answer back to Drake for naming his fiance by bringing up Drake’s alleged baby mama  Sophie Brussaux in his brutal diss track “The Story of Adidon”.", "annotation_id": "14646075", "url": "https://genius.com/14646075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 245, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "I really shouldn’t have given you none of my time ‘Cause you older than the nigga you runnin’ behind", "vault_lines": ["I really shouldn't have given you none of my time", "'Cause you older than the nigga you runnin' behind"], "meaning": "Drake feels that Pusha isn’t a big enough deal to warrant a response. Push is almost one month older than Kanye West, making Pusha T older than his label head.\n\nIt’s also worth noting that Push titled his album DAYTONA because he felt like “he had the luxury of time.” Drake is now giving Push his time.\n\nThis line is similar to Drake’s line from his Meek Mill diss “Back to Back”:\n\nWhen I look back, I might be mad that I gave this attention", "annotation_id": "14646074", "url": "https://genius.com/14646074", "state": "accepted", "votes": 205, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "My hooks did it, my lyrics did it My spirit did it, I’m fearless with it, yeah", "vault_lines": ["My hooks did it, my lyrics did it", "My spirit did it, I'm fearless with it, yeah"], "meaning": "Unlike Pusha, Drake believes that he didn’t need anyone else to become as large and as successful as he is now—his song writing and overall spirit are largely responsible for his fame.\n\nThis is also a nod to JAY-Z’s 2006 track, “The Prelude”:\n\nNigga’s beats is bangin', nigga your hooks did it\nYour lyrics didn’t, your gangsta look did it\nSo I would write it if y'all could get it\n\nDrake actually interpolates the flow and lyrics from Pusha T’s opening lines on “Infrared”:\n\nThe game’s fucked up\nNigga’s beats is bangin', nigga, ya hooks did it\nThe lyric pennin'", "annotation_id": "14646064", "url": "https://genius.com/14646064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Must’ve had your Infrared wrong, now your head in the beam Y’all are the spittin’ image of whatever jealousy breeds", "vault_lines": ["Must've had your Infrared wrong, now your head in the beam", "Y'all are the spittin' image of whatever jealousy breeds"], "meaning": "“Infrared” is the final track on Pusha T’s album DAYTONA, where he disses Drake for not writing his own lyrics. “Infrared” is also a play on info read—as in the information Pusha’s getting about Drake (ghost writing rumors) is wrong.\n\nInfrared beams, or red lasers are used on guns to help shooters aim. Drake makes a play on the song title by comparing Pusha’s diss to a missed shot. Now Drizzy is replying with a diss of his own, effectively making Pusha a target who’s head is “in the beam”.\n\nDrake also appears to be dissing Kanye as well since “head in the beam” may reference Ye’s “Ultralight Beam” from his 2016 album The Life of Pablo.", "annotation_id": "14646053", "url": "https://genius.com/14646053", "state": "accepted", "votes": 216, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "Man, you might’ve sold to college kids for Nike and Mercedes But you act like you sold drugs for Escobar in the ’80s", "vault_lines": ["Man, you might've sold to college kids for Nike and Mercedes", "But you act like you sold drugs for Escobar in the '80s"], "meaning": "Drake is possibly alluding to one of Pusha’s lines in “Infrared”:\n\nLet’s cram numbers, easily\nThe only rapper sold more dope than me was Eazy-E\n\nDrake isn’t saying Pusha never sold drugs, it was just never as extravagant as his lyrics make it seem.\n\nPablo Escobar was a famous drug lord in the 1980’s who controlled over 80% of the global cocaine market and made over $30 billion from selling cocaine.", "annotation_id": "14646037", "url": "https://genius.com/14646037", "state": "accepted", "votes": 249, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "You’re not even top 5 as far as your label talent goes", "vault_lines": ["You're not even top 5 as far as your label talent goes"], "meaning": "This lyric is a direct response to the first line of Pusha T’s verse on “What Would Meek Do?“:\n\nI’m top five and all of them Dylan\n\nPusha’s label—G.O.O.D Music—is home to artists like Kanye West, Big Sean, and Desiigner. Although Pusha is considered by many to be a top tier artist, Drake feels like he isn’t even in the top 5 of his own label, let alone the whole rap game.\n\nThese are the current G.O.O.D. Music signees:\n\n\nKanye West\nBig Sean\nPusha-T\nCyHi The Prynce\nTeyana Taylor\nKacy Hill\nHXLT\nDesiigner\n070 Shake\nFrancis and the Lights\nValee\nSheck Wes", "annotation_id": "14646022", "url": "https://genius.com/14646022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 229, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "And as for Q, man, I changed his life a couple times Nigga was at Kroger workin’ double time Y’all actin’ like he made the boy when I was tryna help the guy", "vault_lines": ["And as for Q, man, I changed his life a couple times", "Nigga was at Kroger workin' double time", "Y'all actin' like he made the boy when I was tryna help the guy"], "meaning": "Pusha T threw shots at Drake on “Infrared,” bringing back a three-year-old accusation that Quentin Miller ghostwrote a handful of songs on Drake’s If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late in 2015.\n\nDrake fires back by saying that he was trying to help out Quentin by giving him a job in the music industry — including giving Quentin a number of songwriting credits — so that he could quit working long hours for minimum wage at a retail store. Quentin acknowledged this line shortly after the release of the song, clarifying that it was actually a Publix, not a Kroger.", "annotation_id": "14646021", "url": "https://genius.com/14646021", "state": "accepted", "votes": 302, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "I’m in shock The nerve, the audacity Okay", "vault_lines": ["The nerve, the audacity"], "meaning": "“Duppy Freestyle” is a response to Pusha T’s “Infrared,” the last track on DAYTONA, where Push questioned Drake’s penmanship and his relationship to songwriter Quentin Miller.\n\nThe game’s fucked up\nNiggas beats is bangin', nigga, ya hooks did it\nThe lyric pennin' equal the Trumps winnin'\nThe bigger question is how the Russians did it\nIt was written like Nas but it came from Quentin\n\nPush ends the song and the album with another shot at Drizzy:\n\nHow could you ever right these wrongs\nWhen you don’t even write your songs?\nBut let us all play along\nWe all know what niggas for real been waitin' on, Push\n\nPush started his 2011 Drake diss “Don’t Fuck With Me” with “The nerve of you / He’ll sit and clip at your lines like he ain’t heard of you.”", "annotation_id": "14646004", "url": "https://genius.com/14646004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 205, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Duppy Freestyle", "line": "So if you rebuke me for workin’ with someone else on a couple of Vs What do you really think of the nigga that’s makin’ your beats? I’ve done things for him I thought that he never would need Father had to stretch his hands out and get it from me I pop style for 30 hours then let him repeat Now you poppin’ up with the jokes, I’m dead, I’m asleep", "vault_lines": ["So if you rebuke me for workin' with someone else on a couple of Vs", "What do you really think of the nigga that's makin' your beats?", "I've done things for him I thought that he never would need", "I told Weezy and Baby, \"I'ma done him for you\"", "Father had to stretch his hands out and get it from me", "I pop style for 30 hours then let him repeat", "Now you poppin' up with the jokes, I'm dead, I'm asleep"], "meaning": "Pusha-T’s 2018 album DAYTONA was entirely produced by Kanye West and contained multiple shots at Drake.\n\nDrake questions Pusha’s disses on the final track “Infrared” that called him out for using writers on a couple of Vs (verses) when Kanye also uses writers, including Drake. Leading up to Kanye’s 2016 album The Life of Pablo, he thanked Drake for helping with “Father Stretch My Hands, Pt. 1” and “30 Hours.” Drake may also be calling back to when Kanye asked for donations to help pay off his $53 million debt around the time when he released TLOP.\n\n\n\nWhen Drake originally dropped “Pop Style,” it featured Kanye West himself, along with JAY-Z but both were cut from the album version.", "annotation_id": "14645993", "url": "https://genius.com/14645993", "state": "accepted", "votes": 442, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
