{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 61 Tracklist", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-ovo-sound-radio-episode-61-tracklist-lyrics", "genius_title": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 61 Tracklist"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "I got some real demons across the border fence And made a note of the mistakes we can’t afford again", "vault_lines": ["I got some real demons across the border fence", "And made a note of the mistakes we can't afford again"], "meaning": "Drake is reflecting on the problems and experiences from his past that still follow him, even though he has created some distance from them. The “demons across the border fence” can represent people, conflicts, regrets, or difficult situations that he knows are still out there. He’s aware of what those experiences have cost him, which is why he’s keeping track of the mistakes he and the people around him have made. The line is ultimately about learning from the past and making sure he doesn’t put himself in a position to repeat mistakes that could have serious consequences.", "annotation_id": "41086780", "url": "https://genius.com/41086780", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Niggas want to hang but I’m too busy doin’ dirty work", "vault_lines": ["Niggas want to hang but I'm too busy doin' dirty work"], "meaning": "he’s doing criminal work", "annotation_id": "40007105", "url": "https://genius.com/40007105", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "I’m standin’ at the top of where you niggas are climbin’ to reach", "vault_lines": ["I'm standin' at the top of where you niggas are climbin' to reach"], "meaning": "Drake uses this line to assert that he’s already at the pinnacle others are still struggling to reach, highlighting the gap between his reality and the aspirations of his peers, and more specifically to the context of this verse, his critic’s. In a genre like hip-hop, which is defined by proving yourself, Drake flips the script—he doesn’t need to prove anything because he is the proof, the measuring stick others use to define success.", "annotation_id": "36286280", "url": "https://genius.com/36286280", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "I even got my very own initials inscribed on my sheets Subtle reminders are key, jeez!", "vault_lines": ["I even got my very own initials inscribed on my sheets", "Subtle reminders are key, jeez!"], "meaning": "Here Drake makes a pun. In the line top line he states that he has his initials in his bed sheets. Drake government name is Aubery Graham. His initial are A.G. He links this to money also known as G’s.", "annotation_id": "35876741", "url": "https://genius.com/35876741", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Niggas scared to come towards us, gotta run from us", "vault_lines": ["Niggas scared to come towards us, gotta run from us"], "meaning": "Drake is simply saying that too many people that talk badly behind his back when they see him in real life they are scared and they don’t want to talk with him.", "annotation_id": "32830129", "url": "https://genius.com/32830129", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "[Chorus: Maneesh & Drake] Yeah, no more, no more Baby, baby, baby, baby, baby, baby, baby Yeah, no more, no more Baby, baby, baby, baby, baby, baby, baby Ooh, ooh (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, no more, no more", "Baby, baby, baby, baby, baby, baby, baby", "Baby, baby, baby, baby, baby, baby, baby, yeah"], "meaning": "As DJ Premier told DJBooth:\n\nThe sample is done by his guy Maneesh. I knew of Maneesh back when he was known as DJ M-Rock. Me and Marco Polo was just talking about him the other day. When 40 said he was the one who created the sample, he literally created it. All the voices you hear in the background, he makes it sound like a sample you would get from a record. Drake wanted me to flip it.", "annotation_id": "31056954", "url": "https://genius.com/31056954", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "I’m the chosen one, flowers never pick themselves", "vault_lines": ["I'm the chosen one, flowers never pick themselves"], "meaning": "In this bar Drake is claiming to be hand picked by God, as “flowers never pick themselves”. Thus this path that he has been on has been predestined and has been chosen by God and not Drake himself.", "annotation_id": "30509871", "url": "https://genius.com/30509871", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "I got to up the ante", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, fuck that, I got to up the ante"], "meaning": "The phrase “up the ante” suggests a desire or need to increase the level of something, usually in the context of competition or performance.\n\nIn this context, it means Drake feels the need to raise his game and take things to a higher level.", "annotation_id": "30034495", "url": "https://genius.com/30034495", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Type of hood where bandanas make niggas a family", "vault_lines": ["Type of hood where bandanas make niggas a family"], "meaning": "This is a simple reference to how gangs are a family-like group that often wear bandanas of the same color to show their allegiance.", "annotation_id": "22132919", "url": "https://genius.com/22132919", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Price tags on makin’ the world feel some", "vault_lines": ["Price tags on makin' the world feel some"], "meaning": "Following up on the first line in this song. Drake answers the question of “who did i steal from” by claiming he has made the world “feel some(thing)” through his music and that is what made him his money, not by stealing.", "annotation_id": "21985093", "url": "https://genius.com/21985093", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "When I say that they cursin’ me, it ain’t dirty words", "vault_lines": ["When I say that they cursin' me, it ain't dirty words"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that the people he makes angry aren’t swearing at him (to curse means to swear). They are cursing him by wishing him death.", "annotation_id": "21210998", "url": "https://genius.com/21210998", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Niggas see the crib and ask who did I steal from", "vault_lines": ["Niggas see the crib and ask who did I steal from"], "meaning": "Niggas see the crib and ask who did I steal from", "annotation_id": "20863869", "url": "https://genius.com/20863869", "state": "pending", "votes": -62, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Like I said, can of worms and I’m the early bird", "vault_lines": ["Like I said, can of worms and I'm the early bird"], "meaning": "“The early bird gets the worm” is an English proverb that suggests that those who arrive first have the best chance of success.\n\nDrake calls back to this line in the first verse:\n\nStack of c-notes get all you niggas scratched like Preemo\nWorms, I just opened up a can of those", "annotation_id": "15159184", "url": "https://genius.com/15159184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Backstabbed so many times I started walkin’ backwards", "vault_lines": ["Backstabbed so many times I started walkin' backwards"], "meaning": "To be backstabbed means to have a friend do something “behind their back” that would harm them in any way.\n\nThroughout the years, Drake has had many bonds with people which resulted in people going against him. A prime example of this would be in the recent feud he has had with Kanye West.\n\nIn May of 2018, Pusha T dropped an album called Daytona in which the last song, Infrared, included shots at the 6 God himself. Drake swiftly responded with a track called Duppy Freestyle in which he fired back at Pusha T and ultimately threw jabs at Yeezy. In this song, Drake notes that it was Kanye reaching out for help to write songs, notably Father Stretch My Hands Pt. 1 even though Pusha T brought up old claims of Drake’s use of a ghost writer.\n\nThe ultimate backstab comes from the possibility the fuel added to Pusha T’s ultimate response (and the last of Drake vs. Pusha T beef) in The Story of Adidon about Drake hiding a child from the world came from Kanye West. Drake’s last song on his most recent album Scorpion, March 14 was recorded before Pusha T’s bombshell of a diss track. There is reason to believe Drake may have actually played this track for Kanye West during the notorious Wyoming recording session for his album, Ye.\n\nThis backstab put Drake’s morality and personality under severe scrutiny, so in response 6 God is now walking backwards as in to not allow anyone to go behind him in this fashion.", "annotation_id": "15138737", "url": "https://genius.com/15138737", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "I find it funny how I keep on talkin’ and commas increase", "vault_lines": ["I find it funny how I keep on talkin' and commas increase"], "meaning": "Drake makes a comparison between his creative consistency and his steadily increasing salary. Every splash he makes in the music industry allows him to rake in more “commas”—money in large amounts.\n\nDrake placed 4th in the Forbes Magazine 10 Highest Paid Entertainers list of 2017, earning a total of $94 Million from June 2016-June 2017.", "annotation_id": "14908571", "url": "https://genius.com/14908571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Niggas want a classic, that’s just ten of these", "vault_lines": ["Niggas want a classic, that's just ten of these"], "meaning": "Common criticism that Drake recieves regarding his discography is that he does not have a “classic album.” Arguably, this means he doesn’t have a project that separates itself from others in an extremely unique fashion. He addresses this by insinuating that his entire music catalog is a “classic,” as Scorpion is Drake’s 10th project release since his debut mixtape in 2006.", "annotation_id": "14895438", "url": "https://genius.com/14895438", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "I walk in godly form amongst the mortal men", "vault_lines": ["I walk in godly form amongst the mortal men"], "meaning": "Drake has often compared himself to that of an immortal being and has given himself the nickname “6 God”. He has often sprinkled in religious imagery into his songs such as in “Gods Plan” or “Jungle”; tracks that reference his marked soul, betrayal, and death.\n\nThis line could be interpreted as a possible jab at fellow rapper Kendrick Lamar who concluded his 2015 album, To Pimp A Butterfly with a track entitled Mortal Man. Drake and Kendrick have taken subliminal shots at each other in the past, but as of yet there has been no full out beef.", "annotation_id": "14893153", "url": "https://genius.com/14893153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "My uncle tryna change my energy with stones and crystals But it’s gon’ take more than that for me to control my issues", "vault_lines": ["My uncle tryna change my energy with stones and crystals", "But it's gon' take more than that for me to control my issues"], "meaning": "Some believe that certain stones and crystals, such as Rose Quartz, can get rid of or protect you from negative energy.\n\nDrake’s Uncle, Larry Graham Jr., is a bass guitar player for the psychedelic band, Sly and the Family Stone.", "annotation_id": "14891515", "url": "https://genius.com/14891515", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Spoiler alert: the second act is tragic", "vault_lines": ["Spoiler alert: the second act is tragic"], "meaning": "Drake compares the “A” and “B” sides of Scorpion to that of a play; the “B” side of the album contains mostly R&B material. He is preparing the listener for the second part which is going to be emotional.", "annotation_id": "14891460", "url": "https://genius.com/14891460", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "I don’t know who’s protectin’ me but we hit it off", "vault_lines": ["I don't know who's protectin' me but we hit it off"], "meaning": "This is a common refrain for Drake. He is hyper-aware of his own success as well the certain degree of luck—or support from God—that is required for anyone to achieve as much as he has.\n\nHere, Drake is echoing that sentiment once again. By saying that he “hit it off” with whoever is protecting him, Drake is suggesting that he is blessed and well-liked by God.\n\nAs mentioned above, this is not the first time Drake has rapped in this vein. In “God’s Plan”, Drake raps:\n\nI can’t do this on my own, ayy, no, ayy\nSomeone watchin' this shit close, yep, close\n\nOn “Sacrifices”, he raps:\n\nWe seein' so many blessings, shit don’t make no sense\nSomeone watchin' over us, so shout goes out to Him\nYeah, I’m convinced\n\nAll together, these lines point to Drake’s strong belief that he is not only talented, but in fact blessed with luck and the support of God.", "annotation_id": "14888114", "url": "https://genius.com/14888114", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Sandra knows I pulled us out of a living hell", "vault_lines": ["Sandra knows I pulled us out of a living hell"], "meaning": "It sounds as if he is referring to the times before he became famous; when he was on Canadian drama series, Degrassi: The Next Generation. He also talks about this in his 2015 track, “Star67”;\n\nI was on TV makin' fifty racks a year\nAfter helpin' mama out, the shit would disappear\nI am not a man, I can’t do this on my own\n\nEssentially, he worked hard, made connections to come up and pull his mom out of poverty and health issues; as spoken on “Look What You’ve Done.”", "annotation_id": "14883697", "url": "https://genius.com/14883697", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Stack of C-notes, get all you niggas scratched like Preemo Worms, I just opened up a can of those", "vault_lines": ["Stack of C-notes, get all you niggas scratched like Preemo", "Worms, I just opened up a can of those"], "meaning": "A “c-note” equals $100, so a stack of hundreds could be paid to have his enemies scratched/beaten up which would “open up a can of worms” meaning to possibly cause more harm to himself than others.\n\nThis song is produced by legendary producer DJ Premier (also known as “Preemo”), whose signature sound consists of scratching a record usually at the chorus or at the end of a song.\n\n\n\nThis song marks the first time the two have collaborated.", "annotation_id": "14883291", "url": "https://genius.com/14883291", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "They don’t have enough to satisfy a real one Maverick Carter couldn’t even get the deal done", "vault_lines": ["They don't have enough to satisfy a real one", "Maverick Carter couldn't even get the deal done"], "meaning": "Maverick Carter is the business man responsible for engineering LeBron James' television special “The Decision” in which he announced his choice to take his talents to Miami in free agency.\n\nHe has worked as LeBron’s manager as well as Johnny Manziel’s, so it is fair to say he has experience closing deals.\n\nDrake is a known huge basketball fan, and continues to bring in basketball metaphors later in this verse.", "annotation_id": "14880313", "url": "https://genius.com/14880313", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Hit ’em back and say we’ll link up on the 33rd", "vault_lines": ["Hit 'em back and say we'll link up on the 33rd"], "meaning": "Champagne Papi says he’ll link on the 33rd day of the month. Since no month is longer than 31 days, this is a funny way of saying that he’ll never link up with these guys.\n\nThis is similar to Kanye saying that his haters have Kim and him wary on the “35th of nevuary.”", "annotation_id": "14878083", "url": "https://genius.com/14878083", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Bury me and I’ll be born again", "vault_lines": ["Bury me and I'll be born again"], "meaning": "Drake is essentially saying that if he gets “bodied” by an opponent, he’ll come back and be “reborn” in a sense. For instance, this was seen after “The Story of Adidon” when many thought he was defeated by Pusha T with the release of this diss track. However, he carried on and released Scorpion.\n\nThis could reference a line from “Gyalchester” of his 2017 project More Life, in which he raps:\n\nBury me now and I only get bigger\nThat’s word to my nigga", "annotation_id": "14879742", "url": "https://genius.com/14879742", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "My mother had a flower shop, but I was Sandra’s rose", "vault_lines": ["My mother had a flower shop, but I was Sandra's rose"], "meaning": "Sandra is Drake’s mother, and he’s calling himself her rose. Drake’s mom was a florist. He rapped about it on his 2010 song “The Resistance”:\n\nIt’s ironic ‘cause my mother was a florist\nAnd that’s how she met my pops\nAnd now my garden is enormous\n\nDrake is fond of mentioning his mom in his songs, he even dedicated a song to her, titled “You & The 6.”\n\n\n\nDrake famously referenced his mother on his 2018 hit “God’s Plan,” where he rapped:\n\nShe said, “Do you love me?” I tell her, “Only partly”\nI only love my bed and my momma, I’m sorry", "annotation_id": "14877908", "url": "https://genius.com/14877908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Two girls that I rope like Indiana Jones", "vault_lines": ["Two girls that I rope like Indiana Jones"], "meaning": "Drizzy uses a simple yet effective simile to compare his ability to whip girls (i.e. completely control them) to the way in which Indiana Jones uses his rope and whip.\n\nIndiana Jones is a famous film series who’s titular protagonist is distinguished by his iconic whip, fedora and leather jacket.\n\n\n\nWhen Drizzy hosted SNL he appeared in a skit where he played a ride operator at Disney Land on the Indiana Jones ride.", "annotation_id": "14877332", "url": "https://genius.com/14877332", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "My house is full of supermodels just like Mohamed Hadid", "vault_lines": ["My house is full of supermodels just like Mohamed Hadid"], "meaning": "Real estate developer Mohamed Hadid has been surrounded by models his entire life. He is the father of supermodels Bella Hadid and Gigi Hadid who he raised with his supermodel ex-wife Yolanda Hadid. His current partner Shiva Safai is also a model.", "annotation_id": "14877072", "url": "https://genius.com/14877072", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Had me all in Nipsey hood to go link up with Sammy", "vault_lines": ["Had me all in Nipsey hood to go link up with Sammy"], "meaning": "This line references the rap artist Nipsey Hussle, who hails from Los Angeles, California.\n\nDrake speaks about visiting LA, and “linking up” or associating with Sammy. Nipsey Hussle has a sister named Sammy who 1: Drake could be visiting her, or 2: this is metaphorical for a sister-like associate who he sees there.", "annotation_id": "14876943", "url": "https://genius.com/14876943", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Church of Pentecost, Holy Spirit synagogue", "vault_lines": ["Church of Pentecost, Holy Spirit synagogue"], "meaning": "Drake separates his respective religious beliefs – as a proud Jewish person, he believes in visiting the Synagogue, the building where a Jewish assembly or congregation meets for religious worship and instruction. However, he also gives mention to the Church of Pentecost, a Ghanian Protestant Church.\n\nDrake can be found discussing his religion more here:\n\n“On October 24th 2011 Aubrey “Drake” Graham chose to get re-bar mitzvah’d as a re-commitment to the Jewish religion.” The entire video is a parody of a bar mitzvah, with rappers drinking and dancing like they are at a nightclub. Drake was born to a Catholic father and a Jewish mother, and he attended Jewish school and had a Bar Mitzvah. In regards to religion in his childhood he stated in an interview “I went to a Jewish school, where nobody understood what it was like to be black and Jewish.”", "annotation_id": "14876920", "url": "https://genius.com/14876920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Crime family like the Genovese, you don’t want drama, capisc’?", "vault_lines": ["Crime family like the Genovese, you don't want drama, capisc'?"], "meaning": "The Genovese family, located in New York and New Jersey, is one of the biggest and most powerful organized crime syndicates associated with the Mafia.\n\nDrake keeps his family close (specifically his labels Young Money and OVO), similarly to how the intense bonds of the criminals of the Genovese kept them together. Drake further alludes to this in tracks like “Mob Ties.”\n\n“Capisc' ” (short for “capisci” and pronounced /cah-PEESH/) is the present tense second-person singular of the Italian verb “capire”. In its interrogative form it translates to “you understand?”, and it’s a common expression among mafia affiliates.", "annotation_id": "14876785", "url": "https://genius.com/14876785", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Head on a swivel, I could shoot but I could never dribble", "vault_lines": ["Head on a swivel, I could shoot but I could never dribble"], "meaning": "It’s a quite common tradition that basketball coaches everywhere tell their players to keep their head up when moving the ball up the court as to look for open teammates. Drake uses this as a comparison along with his usage of “head on a swivel” to convey how he always walks with his head up to keep an eye out for any danger that may present itself to him.\n\nAdditionally, basketball is a sport where players must dribble a ball up court and then shoot it into the basket. While Drake may not have the dexterity to move a basketball, he alludes the line to shooting a gun. The latter part of this bar could also mean that he only has time to take a couple shots on the court but not enough to actually dribble and become comfortable.\n\nIn reality, however, a video of Drake airballing a jumper during March Madness went viral in 2014.", "annotation_id": "14876720", "url": "https://genius.com/14876720", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Life too short, I gotta get it ’fore they blow the whistle", "vault_lines": ["Life too short, I gotta get it 'fore they blow the whistle"], "meaning": "Drake references Bay Area rap legend Too $hort and his hit singles “Life is Too Short”,“Gettin' It”, and “Blow The Whistle”.\n\n\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake has paid homage to the West Coast MC – he interpolated a few bars “Blow The Whistle” the latter single in his 2016 DJ Khaled collaboration “For Free”.\n\nI go on and on\nCan’t understand how I last so long\nI must have the superpowers\nLast 223 thousand hours", "annotation_id": "14876625", "url": "https://genius.com/14876625", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "California girls sweeter than pieces of candy", "vault_lines": ["California girls sweeter than pieces of candy"], "meaning": "A reference to Katy Perry’s hit song “California Gurls”, featuring Snoop Dogg, where the virtues of California girls are similarly lauded in confectionary terms.", "annotation_id": "14876471", "url": "https://genius.com/14876471", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "I make them hoes walk together like I’m Amber Rose", "vault_lines": ["I make them hoes walk together like I'm Amber Rose"], "meaning": "Amber Rose, model, actress and former sex worker, is known for promoting “SlutWalk”, starting her own annual festival, campaigning for an end to rape culture, specifically slut-shaming, and victim-blaming.\n\nDrake groups his “hoes” like they’re on the SlutWalk.\n\nAmber Rose is also well known in part to her high-profile relationships, with Kanye West, 21 Savage, and Wiz Khalifa, many of whom Drake has collaborated with.\n\n21 Savage even attended one of the walks, while close friend and label-mate of Drake, Nicki Minaj, has donated to the movement.", "annotation_id": "14876460", "url": "https://genius.com/14876460", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Louisville hush money for my young gunners Rick Pitino, I take ’em to strip clubs and casinos", "vault_lines": ["Louisville hush money for my young gunners", "Rick Pitino, I take 'em to strip clubs and casinos"], "meaning": "In late 2015, the University of Louisville was put under investigation by the NCAA for allegedly hiring strippers during recruiting visits in order to attract players to their school. Later, in 2017, the University was once again put under investigation; this time for secretly paying a recruit an unknown amount of money that was believed to be upwards of $100,000.  The head coach of the basketball team during this time was Rick Pitino.\n\nJust as money was secretly provided to a recruit under Pitino’s watch, Drake secretly provides money to his shooters — a play on basketball, although Drake is referring to shooters with guns. Moreover, playing even more off the aforementioned scandals, Drake furthers the reference by stating that he also takes his shooters out to strip clubs.\n\nIt’s worth noting that the University of Louisville is a major rival to the University of Kentucky — a school that Drake has shown his love for on numerous occasions. He has been seen at Kentucky basketball games and even mentioned them on the track “Scholarships”:\n\nAnd I rock Kentucky blue on these hoes\nDrafted, I’m gettin' choose by these hoes", "annotation_id": "14876343", "url": "https://genius.com/14876343", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Like Charlamagne, I see the light and see the darkest patches", "vault_lines": ["Like Charlamagne, I see the light and see the darkest patches"], "meaning": "Charlamagne tha God a radio/tv show personality most well known for his work on the New York radio show The Breakfast Club, has often been accused of undergoing a skin-lightening procedure through chemical peeling and various other skin treatments which was due to his complexion having dark patches as seen here:\n\n\n\nThe line pokes fun at Charlamagne by comparing his light and dark patches (positive and negative experiences) to Charlamagne’s literal light and dark patches. Drake has mentioned Charlamagne in his raps before. On his Meek Mill diss Back 2 Back Drake rapped:\n\nYou gon' make me step out of my fuckin' frame\nYou gon' make me buy bottles for Charlamagne\n\nDrake made good on the promise, sending Charlamagne the champagne in question.\n\nMany also believe that Charlamagne and other similar radio hosts thrive on negativity and rumors when there’s something positive being displayed or conveyed. These “darkest patches” could be the hints of pessimism that he displays in his character.", "annotation_id": "14876332", "url": "https://genius.com/14876332", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sandra’s Rose", "line": "Every title doin’ numbers like I’m Miss Adele", "vault_lines": ["Every title doin' numbers like I'm Miss Adele"], "meaning": "Drake makes a double entendre with a reference to world renowned pop singer Adele. All of Adele’s studio albums are named (or ‘titled’) after the age she was when they were recorded, namely 19, 21 and finally 25.\n\nAdele has also managed to put up extremely impressive numbers in terms of sales. She managed to sell over 50 million units on the back of 21 and 25 alone. She has, as of 2018, also accumulated 18 Grammy nominations and 15 wins, solidifying her as one of the most successful artists of her generation.\n\nAfter breaking a single day streaming record, going to new heights by dethroning himself at No. 1 on the Billboard Hot 100, and earning five consecutive No. 1 albums, Drizzy feels his colossal success can be compared to the likes of Adele.\n\nAt the 2017 Billboard Music Awards, Drake one-upped Adele’s record of 12 wins in a single night by taking home 13 for the music on Views.", "annotation_id": "14876287", "url": "https://genius.com/14876287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
