{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "[Intro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake samples SWV for the first part of the track, a noteworthy choice given Kendrick Lamar’s use of the group’s music on “heart pt. 6” off GNX. In the context of the bridge, the sample can be seen as Drake showcasing his creative superiority.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/m-PO1_fzxVM", "annotation_id": "40128654", "url": "https://genius.com/40128654", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Woah, woah, livin’ large, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake might be making fun and mockingly interpolating Kendrick Lamar’s viral line\n\nWop, wop, wop, wop, wop, Dot, fuck ‘em up\nWop, wop, wop, wop, wop, I'ma do my stuff\n\nBy copying Kendrick’s exact flow and putting it over a West Coast-style beat he supports the idea of him making better west coast sound than California’s own.", "annotation_id": "40023118", "url": "https://genius.com/40023118", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Yeah, I’m fuckin’ power couples up, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm fuckin' power couples up, yeah"], "meaning": "Direct diss at Jay Z and Beyoncé as a power couple.\n\nAt mark 2:21 in the official video he does the Roc sign, and mark 2:22, he raises the middle finger saying I am fucking power couples up.", "annotation_id": "39969149", "url": "https://genius.com/39969149", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Pipe Down", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, it's Mr. Make Her Pipe Down"], "meaning": "Pipe Down is a track in Drake’s 2021 album “Certified Lover Boy”", "annotation_id": "39946644", "url": "https://genius.com/39946644", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Ayy, first off, I make real town smacks", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, first off, I make real town smacks"], "meaning": "“Real town smacks” is a nod to Oakland, The Town or Real Town being one of the city’s nicknames. Drake’s Bay Area connection runs older than most people realize. His homage dates back to “The Motto,” the music video featured cameos from E-40, Berner, Mistah F.A.B., and Mac Dre’s mother Mac Wanda, alongside shots of the town’s landmarks. The track itself rightfully qualifies as a “smack” (a Bay Area slang for a song that hits hard, synonymous with “slaps.”)\n\nHe continued the thread with “Who Do You Love?” alongside YG in 2014, produced by Mustard, and collaborated with Oakland producer Ovrkast. on “Red Button” and “The Shoe Fits” from For All The Dogs.\n\nThe track also serves as a direct rebuttal to Kendrick Lamar’s “Not Like Us” claim that Drake can’t come to Oakland.\n\nIt is also worth noting that Bay Area producers P-lo and Karri are among the producers on the track.", "annotation_id": "39925934", "url": "https://genius.com/39925934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Shout my lil’ cousin, Mr. Havin’ None of It Tryna campaign, yeah, Drizzy back runnin’ shit", "vault_lines": ["Shout my lil' cousin, Mr. Havin' None of It", "Tryna campaign, yeah, Drizzy back runnin' shit"], "meaning": "Shout my lil’ cousin, Mr. Havin’ None of It / Tryna campaign, yeah, Drizzy back runnin’ shit” is Drake talking his talk again after a quieter period in rap. “Campaign” and “runnin’ shit” frame his return like a political comeback or re-election run, implying he’s reclaiming his spot at the top.\n\n“Mr. Havin’ None of It” sounds like a nickname for someone in his circle who doesn’t tolerate disrespect or nonsense, fitting the aggressive, confident energy of the verse.", "annotation_id": "39818181", "url": "https://genius.com/39818181", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Damn, you should try and get back to that", "vault_lines": ["Damn, you should try and get back to that"], "meaning": "reference to Euphoria where Kendrick Lamar said\n\n“Back To Back, I like that record. Imma get back to that for the record”\n\ni dont think its a stretch, hes dissing kendrick all over this album and directly referencing kendrick’s bars. of course he would do it again on the Bay Area inspired song", "annotation_id": "39817000", "url": "https://genius.com/39817000", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "You see the backpack to the front, you gettin’ spanked", "vault_lines": ["You see the backpack to the front, you gettin' spanked"], "meaning": "Wearing a backpack on your front-side (instead of on your back) gives you the ability to access the contents of the bag while wearing it.\n\nThis line refers to a common scenario where somebody who intends to commit gun violence wears their backpack on “the front” to conceal their firearm while making it easily accessible.", "annotation_id": "39807010", "url": "https://genius.com/39807010", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "When I lost big Nadia, I took it hard", "vault_lines": ["When I lost big Nadia, I took it hard"], "meaning": "Drake spoke about Nadia’s death on For All The Dogs Scary Hours Edition track, “Wickman”.\n\nNadia Ntuli was a former UK model and friend of Drake who passed away in January 2021. Although there are scarce reports on her death, she is thought to have passed away in a motorcycle accident in Dubai.\n\nLeading up to the release of CLB, Drake posted an Instagram story where he dedicated the album to Ntuli and Mercedes Morr.", "annotation_id": "39804267", "url": "https://genius.com/39804267", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Facts, nine hundred million for the tracks", "vault_lines": ["Facts, nine hundred million for the tracks"], "meaning": "Drake implies his albums contract payout was $900 million.", "annotation_id": "39801611", "url": "https://genius.com/39801611", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "You boys need to worry ’bout a jury in your life, ayy", "vault_lines": ["You boys need to worry 'bout a jury in your life, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake warns Jay-Z and Pusha T that the island rumors could put them in front of a jury. At the same time, Drake is also predicting that Kendrick’s wife will eventually press charges for domestic abuse.", "annotation_id": "39801455", "url": "https://genius.com/39801455", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Actin’ like you love marryin’ your wife, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Actin' like you love marryin' your wife, ayy"], "meaning": "Possibly a shot at Kendrick Lamar. Drake has mentioned before, specifically in his 2024 diss-track ‘Family Matters’ about Kendrick and Whitney’s relationship (2015 proposal).\n\nHe may be bringing this back up on the West Coast beat, reversing everything K-Dot did with ‘Not Like Us’, and ‘GNX’.", "annotation_id": "39801143", "url": "https://genius.com/39801143", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "And a nigga doin’ laps", "vault_lines": ["And a nigga doin' laps"], "meaning": "Considering the Cali vibe this could be a reference to Nipsey Hussle who Drake collabed with on The song “Killer” before his untimely passing. Kendrick referenced Nipsey multiple times during the beef and Nipseys brand was The Marathon often talking about running laps.", "annotation_id": "39797843", "url": "https://genius.com/39797843", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "[Bridge] Hey, hey What? Yeah, what? I’ma show you what to do, lil’ nigga, ayy I’ma show you what to do, lil’ boy, ayy I’ma show you what—", "vault_lines": ["I'ma show you what to do, lil' nigga, ayy", "I'ma show you what to do, lil' boy, ayy", "I'ma show you what—"], "meaning": "At this part in the song, the production shifts to a West Coast bounce similar to the kind of sound LA rapper Kendrick Lamar leaned on during his feud with Drake on “Not Like Us” and throughout GNX.\n\nDrake is telling Kendrick that not only can he make West Coast records, he can also make them better. This is also a continuation of the short jokes he peppers throughout the album.", "annotation_id": "39796516", "url": "https://genius.com/39796516", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "When I lost big Nadia, I took it hard", "vault_lines": ["When I lost big Nadia, I took it hard"], "meaning": "Drake references his close friend Nadia Ntuli, who died in an ATV accident in 2021. They had been friends for years, and he dedicated Certified Lover Boy to her when it was released.\n\n\n\nDrake also mentioned Nadia’s death on “Wick Man”:\n\nNadia died in Dubai, I waited on a spirit to come by for like seventeen months\nThat shit didn’t visit me once, damn", "annotation_id": "39795847", "url": "https://genius.com/39795847", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "I really blow M’s for the fun of it New owl, blue diamonds on the front of it", "vault_lines": ["I really blow M's for the fun of it", "New owl, blue diamonds on the front of it"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing a custom piece made by jeweler Alex Moss featuring an iced-out blue OVO owl pendant. The “owl blue diamonds” line ties into the aesthetic and branding surrounding ICEMAN, with Drake flexing that he spends millions (“blow M’s”) casually, treating luxury jewelry like it’s nothing.", "annotation_id": "39792345", "url": "https://genius.com/39792345", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Woah, woah, livin’ large, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Flips the midgets “WOP” while showing him how to make a song, a WEST coast song at that", "annotation_id": "39790962", "url": "https://genius.com/39790962", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "This new shit, you could’ve kept it on the lap’, fact", "vault_lines": ["This new shit, you could've kept it on the lap', fact"], "meaning": "“Laugh Fact'” references the Laugh Factory comedy club and claims another artist’s new release is comedy, aka trash.", "annotation_id": "39788987", "url": "https://genius.com/39788987", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Yeah, it’s Mr. Popstar, that’s the way it go", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, it's Mr. Popstar, that's the way it go"], "meaning": "“Mr. Popstar” is Drake embracing the level of fame he reached over the years. The line connects to his 2020 hit “Popstar” by Drake with DJ Khaled, where he talks about living under constant attention and pressure. Here, he accepts that his image became bigger than just rap he’s a global celebrity whose lifestyle, relationships, and every move are watched by the public. Saying “that’s the way it go” makes it sound inevitable, like fame changed his identity whether he wanted it or not.", "annotation_id": "39788583", "url": "https://genius.com/39788583", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "My young boys from the Yoc goin’ crazy I’m on the bridge with a tank of unleaded I pull up early to that bitch, that bitch was yankin’ already, I said yank In the city and I really got rank", "vault_lines": ["My young boys from the Yoc goin' crazy", "I'm on the bridge with a tank of unleaded", "I pull up early to that bitch, that bitch was yankin' already, I said yank", "In the city and I really got rank", "And I put that on citas and the boulevard"], "meaning": "“The Yoc” is Bay Area slang for Antioch, California — a city in Contra Costa County, east of Oakland. Rapper Woodie helped popularize “The Yoc,” “Yoc Life” and “Mob Livin’” for his hometown in the late ‘90s and early 2000s. Drake shouting out the young boys from The Yoc signals he’s got love and connections deep in the Bay, not just the metro areas.\n\nDrake mentions being on the bridge, which could suggest he’s in San Francisco a.k.a. “The City” after flying into SFO. He gets treated like royalty before his vehicle with expensive California gas crosses the Bay Bridge into “The Town” of Oakland en route to a poppin' party in The Yoc.", "annotation_id": "39781395", "url": "https://genius.com/39781395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Boy, you know the motto, gotta push it to the mack", "vault_lines": ["Boy, you know the motto, gotta push it to the mack"], "meaning": "“The Motto” music video was filmed throughout the Bay Area, opening with Mac Dre’s mother Wanda Salvatto (known as “Mac Wanda”) paying tribute to her son and affirming that his legacy lives on through the culture. Drake made the homage explicit in the song itself:\n\nI’m in the building and I’m feeling myself, rest in peace Mac Dre, I'ma do it for the Bay\n\n“Push it to the mack” completes the Mac Dre reference, Mac Dre being the late Vallejo rapper whose influence runs from the title of the track downward.", "annotation_id": "39778858", "url": "https://genius.com/39778858", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Yeah, it’s Mr. Popstar, that’s the way it go", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, it's Mr. Popstar, that's the way it go"], "meaning": "A nod to “POPSTAR,” Drake’s 2020 collaboration with DJ Khaled.", "annotation_id": "39778829", "url": "https://genius.com/39778829", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Back when they was askin’ ’bout where Davidson was at Now everybody got a blue thirty on they back", "vault_lines": ["Back when they was askin' 'bout where Davidson was at", "Now everybody got a blue thirty on they back"], "meaning": "Drake suggests that his friendship with Golden State Warriors superstar Stephen Curry goes back before his Elite Eight run in 2008 at the small school Davidson College in Davidson, North Carolina. It’s possible they may have crossed paths as kids while Steph’s father, Dell Curry, played for Drake’s hometown Toronto Raptors from 1999 to 2002.\n\nMultiple championships later, Steph has had the top-selling jersey in the NBA several times with a lot of blue 30s on the backs of many fans.", "annotation_id": "39778825", "url": "https://genius.com/39778825", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Now I’m too hard for the fuckin’ radio Too hard for the fuckin’ radio Yeah, too hard for the fuckin’ radio Too hard for the fuckin’ radio", "vault_lines": ["Now I'm too hard for the fuckin' radio", "Too hard for the fuckin' radio", "Yeah, too hard for the fuckin' radio"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing rapper Mac Dre’s “2 Hard 4 the Fuckin’ Radio,” which inspired the track’s title.\n\nHe could also be alluding to his 2025 single “What Did I Miss?” debuting at No. 2 after a Billboard rule change gave more weight to radio play in chart rankings. The song got just 2% of radio play and lost the No. 1 spot to “Ordinary,” which had 40%.", "annotation_id": "39778812", "url": "https://genius.com/39778812", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "[Verse 2]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake using the same type of flow that Kendrick was using on Not Like Us in this verse.", "annotation_id": "39778809", "url": "https://genius.com/39778809", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "And I put that on citas and the boulevard", "vault_lines": ["And I put that on citas and the boulеvard", "And I put that on citas and the boulevard"], "meaning": "“On citas” is Bay Area slang for “on mamas,” and putting something “on citas/on mamas” or “the boulevard” is a way of saying that you’re telling the truth about something and stand on it. “On citas” has been used in popular Bay Area songs such as Keak da Sneak’s “Super Hyphy” and Mistah F.A.B.’s “N.E.W. Oakland.”", "annotation_id": "39778797", "url": "https://genius.com/39778797", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "“Rack City,” bitch, we remember that", "vault_lines": ["\"Rack City,\" bitch, we remember that"], "meaning": "“Rack City” is the 2011 hit single by Drake’s former Young Money labelmate Tyga produced by DJ Mustard and Mike Free. Despite Drake’s issues with Mustard’s alliance with Kendrick Lamar, Drake will still give Mustard his props when they’re due. Drake has shown praise for the track in the past as he notably played it for Bobbi Althoff during their interview in 2023:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/-MlDq-BgY7s?si=YlSpUNLLp5xZF2rk&t=1849", "annotation_id": "39778753", "url": "https://genius.com/39778753", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Pipe Down", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, it's Mr. Make Her Pipe Down"], "meaning": "Reference to “Pipe Down” from Certified Lover Boy", "annotation_id": "39778601", "url": "https://genius.com/39778601", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Got an Oakland show tonight, baby", "vault_lines": ["Got an Oakland show tonight, baby"], "meaning": "This is a response to Kendrick Lamar’s “Not Like Us,” where Kendrick says:\n\nI think that Oakland show gon' be your last stop, nigga\n\nDrake is basically saying he can still perform there.", "annotation_id": "39778577", "url": "https://genius.com/39778577", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Listen up, it’s about to be smoke Ain’t nothin’ but some shit I wrote About a rich-ass nigga that’s deep in the game They call me Drizzy Drake and I’m keepin’ the name I sport Nike shoes, I got a mic to use To talk bad about you pussies, I don’t like you fools", "vault_lines": ["In the city and I really got rank", "Listen up, it's about to be smoke", "Ain't nothin' but some shit I wrote", "And a nigga doin' laps", "About a rich-ass nigga that's deep in the game", "They call me Drizzy Drake and I'm keepin' the name", "I sport Nike shoes, I got a mic to use", "To talk bad about you pussies, I don't like you fools"], "meaning": "These bars and the track title interpolate and pay homage to late Bay Area legend Mac Dre, who originally released “Too Hard for the Fuckin' Radio” in 1989:\n\nListen up, I’m about to get dope\nIt ain’t nothin' but some shit I wrote\nAbout a young brotha deep in the game\nThey call me Mac Dre and I’m keepin' the name\nI sport Nike shoes, I got a mic to use\nTo talk bad about suckers, I don’t like the fools\n\nDrake paid respect to Mac Dre twice on his 2011 album, Take Care:\n\n\nMy city love me like Mac Dre in the Bay\n– “Over My Dead Body”\n\nI’m in the building and I’m feelin' myself\nRest in peace Mac Dre, I'ma do it for the Bay, okay\n– “The Motto”\n\n\nDrake is an ambassador for the shoe brand Nike, with collaborations dating back to 2013. In December 2020, Drake launched a shoe and apparel sub-label with Nike called NOCTA.", "annotation_id": "39778210", "url": "https://genius.com/39778210", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Need to call up a Stark for some Chrome Hearts", "vault_lines": ["Need to call up a Stark for some Chrome Hеarts", "Need to call up a Stark for some Chrome Hearts"], "meaning": "Drake is flexing here, claiming that he’ll purchase luxury Chrome Hearts items straight from the co-founder himself, Richard Stark.\n\n\n\nThis could also be a double entendre for the fictional Marvel character Tony Starks, also known as the superhero Iron Man. Starks has an arc reactor power source attached to his chest to power his Iron Man armor and prevent shrapnel from killing him.", "annotation_id": "39776691", "url": "https://genius.com/39776691", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Damn, y’all was really island hoppin’ back then Huh, now y’all names got redacted Yeah, now a nigga gotta fact check, what?", "vault_lines": ["Damn, y'all was really island hoppin' back then", "Huh, now y'all names got redacted", "Yeah, now a nigga gotta fact check, what?"], "meaning": "The island in question is the Little St. James island (infamously nicknamed the Epstein Island,) the center of the Jeffrey Epstein’s trafficking operation.\n\n\n\nThe DOJ released three million documents from the Epstein files in January 2026, with the latest batch including mentions of JAŸ-Z and Pusha T. Both rappers were named in an anonymous tip to the FBI. The tip itself does not imply that they were ever investigated, nor that they are guilty, and that’s exactly Drake’s point. The same media outlets and people who treated Kendrick’s predatory allegations against Drake as facts went quiet and extended the benefit of the doubt when it came to others hit with the same allegations.", "annotation_id": "39776596", "url": "https://genius.com/39776596", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Mustard heard about us, gotta catch up to the slaps You ain’t had one since me and YG rapped", "vault_lines": ["Mustard heard about us, gotta catch up to the slaps", "You ain't had one since me and YG rapped"], "meaning": "This is a diss aimed at producer Mustard. He was involved in the Kendrick Lamar and Drake feud, producing the notable “Not Like Us.”\n\nDrake is claiming Mustard has not made a hit since “Who Do You Love?”, the 2014 collaboration between Drake and YG that Mustard produced.\n\nThis is a double entendre using Mustard (the producer) and “catch up,” which sounds like “ketchup,” continuing the condiment theme.", "annotation_id": "39776468", "url": "https://genius.com/39776468", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "line": "Yeah, and I’m on the west like the Pac-10", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and I'm on the west like the Pac-10"], "meaning": "Drake ties him rapping on a west coast beat with the Pacific-10, an NCAA Power Five conference on the west coast of the United States that stood from 1978-2011 before expanding to the Pac-12.\n\nConference realignment resulted in 10 of the 12 Pac-12 teams exiting the conference in 2024. A new-age Pac-12 is being reborn in 2026.", "annotation_id": "39776216", "url": "https://genius.com/39776216", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "2 Hard 4 The Radio", "status": "done", "targets": 36, "clicked": 36, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-2-hard-4-the-radio-lyrics", "genius_title": "2 Hard 4 The Radio"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "I, I, I, I, I", "vault_lines": ["Sold out, I see you", "I am the only rapper taking you there, all night baby", "I got a lot of respect", "I ain't tripping on you but I'ma show you later", "I'ma turn this back and show ya how I keep coming back", "And I can give it to you all night", "Oh, I want what I want", "Oh, I don't give up", "I stunt, so step it up, baby", "I stunt, so step it up", "I got my own ish, I ain't impressed B", "I'm hopping out the whip then I stagger to my home", "Get a A-plus sticker for the way that I stick it, stick it", "I ball hard and Toronto please acknowledge that", "So even if she getting stuck I stick it the best, oh", "'Cause I don't want you confused, it's always better knowing", "And so it's only right I make you aware"], "meaning": "“I,I,I,I,I” kinda rhymes with “yiy yiy yiy…” calling back to his “yiy” which changed in the first line", "annotation_id": "39581020", "url": "https://genius.com/39581020", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The hook in “Controlla” sounds very similar to the the hook of Erykah Badu’s song “Out My Mind, Just In Time.” For instance:\n\nAnd I’d lie for you\nI’d cry for you…\n…Do what you want me to\n\nListen to it below:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/78-E1NnGxQo?t=37", "annotation_id": "9109599", "url": "https://genius.com/9109599", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "[Break: Beenie Man]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Beenie Man is a Jamaican dancehall legend—he’s often referred to as  “the King of Dancehall.”\n\nHe told Billboard that he’s protective of dancehall’s local roots, especially now that its sound has entered the mainstream—Rihanna and Drake’s dancehall-infused collaboration “Work” hit #1.\n\nDancehall is in the mainstream now…Dancehall is a culture that everyone wants to be a part of. Nobody wants to be part of a culture with no meaning in it. Right now, everybody tries to kick it with the dancehall. It’s our music. And it’s not going nowhere. We make dancehall so we can feel good about ourselves….I have to protect the culture. Even when I go on tour, I don’t do other songs, just dancehall music. I introduce the world to what dancehall is ‘cause I’m the king of it, and I have to work to keep it up. Jamaica needs trendsetters.", "annotation_id": "9107073", "url": "https://genius.com/9107073", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "My yiy just changed (Just changed) You just buzzed the front gate, I Thank God you came (You came)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“My yiy just changed” is an expression which stems from a 2012 collaborative mixtape by Popcaan and DJ Creep Chromatic. ‘Yiy change’ can refer to the physical change of one’s eyes getting red from smoking, or a more metaphorical change of mindset.\n\nPhysically, the pupils in the eye can dilate a lot if you take drugs, become sexually aroused, or basically do anything that gets your adrenaline pumping. Drake (and his libido) perks up at the sound the woman ringing the front gate. He also used the term on his “Sweeterman (Remix).”\n\nMetaphorically, Drake usually doesn’t get emotionally attached, but he’s drawn to this “angel,” and feels their connection growing stronger. Perhaps this is how he felt as he grew closer to Rihanna as they collaborated for their hit single “Work.”\n\n\n\nWhile there is an aura of buzz around the two, Rihanna spoke with Ellen telling her that she wouldn’t have the time to commit to one person.\n\nI’m single right now and it’s not that I’m like, ‘Oh, pro-single, yes, I hate men!’ I just think I have so much on my plate that I can’t even find the time to entertain a steady relationship or anything serious. Or even a text.", "annotation_id": "9093665", "url": "https://genius.com/9093665", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "And that’s why I need All the energy that you bring to me My last girl would tear me apart But she’d never wanna split a ting with me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake has revealed in past songs that he has a lot of enemies and they drain him. On “Energy,” Drake raps:\n\nI got enemies, got a lot of enemies\nGot a lot of people tryna drain me of my energy\n\nA successful man needs a strong woman behind him, and Drake needs the love of a good woman to help him restore this lost energy.\n\nDrake’s last girl would split him apart, but she wouldn’t split a “ting” with him. The word carries a couple different slang meanings: Ting is a very popular Jamaican soda. Not wanting to share a simple thing such as a drink exemplifies the ex girl’s selfishness.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oCjeDvJJYjI\n\nOther slang meanings suggest that perhaps she wouldn’t share a joint with him, or split her stash, which is so not chill. Or else she refused to split a ‘bad girl’ with him (see ‘ting’ usage later in this song), i.e. have a threesome, which is quite popular among rappers these days.\n\nWhatever the ting was, the overall meaning shows through: Drake’s last girl wasn’t as generous with herself materially, emotionally, or sexually as his new flame.", "annotation_id": "9088584", "url": "https://genius.com/9088584", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "They don’t wanna see me Smiling back when they pree", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Pree” is Jamaican Patois for taking special notice of someone or something, to the point of lurking.\n\nIn one way, these lines speak to the fact that Drake is always being chased by women everywhere he goes. Even though they’re supposedly ‘obsessed with him,’ they don’t want to see him smiling back at some other woman, and don’t respond well when they find out he’s in a committed relationship.\n\nHowever, in another way, Drake is also calling these jealous girls out. Drake knows that they get catty and root against the women he dates. He could be saying they don’t want to see him smiling back at them, continuing to be happy in the face of their disapproval.\n\n“They don’t wanna see me” seems to be inspired by DJ Khaled, who characterizes “they” as people who try to bring negative energy into your life when it’s going well (i.e. haters):\n\nhttps://youtu.be/zHqujeBeCDA?t=52\n\n“Jheeze!” is London slang, started by rapper Giggs. It means to be happy about something. He has used it in various songs like “Talkin' da Hardest”:\n\nIf you’re talking the hardest\nGiggs better pop up in your thoughts as an artist (Jheeze)", "annotation_id": "9072970", "url": "https://genius.com/9072970", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "But you can’t just diss and come tell man sorry You can’t listen to me talk and go tell my story, nah (Nah) It don’t work like that when you love somebody", "vault_lines": ["When you put me in check and tell me to my face"], "meaning": "Drake uses the Jamaican slang “tell man sorry” to express that although he’d do anything to please this girl, he’ll not tolerate disloyalty.\n\nIf she really wants to be with him, she won’t sell him out, gossiping about him to others/the public and then crawling back saying sorry, the fame went to her head. The vulnerability and secrets that people confide in their significant others is a sacred thing, a trust issue, and Drake has been burned by women betraying him on this front before.", "annotation_id": "9070961", "url": "https://genius.com/9070961", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "Music a drop! Gyal a whine up dem bumpa Dis is di summa summa controlla Drake from Canada, Beenie Man from Jamaica Da one yah a murda—, Zagga!", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "While shouting out Drake and himself, Beenie describes what happens in Jamaica when a song drops in a club: the girls start whining.\n\nWhining is a dance of Caribbean descent in which one slowly winds their waist in a circular motion. The pace of rotation varies, depending on the music and the beats per minute.\n\n\n\nFinally, he points out that the people want to hear “murda.” “Murda” is a slang in Jamaican/Caribbean dialect for killing a track. Zagga is a casual greeting like Hello.", "annotation_id": "9070852", "url": "https://genius.com/9070852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "Gyal a tear off mi garments An’ a bawl fi come inna mi apartment (Woy!) Di gyal dem waan di length an’ strength Action speak louder dan argument, well", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This a sample from Beenie Man’s 1995 song “Tear Off Mi Garment.”\n\nIn these lyrics, Beenie describes a situation where a woman wants to get right into sex. He uses the saying “actions speak louder than words” to express how much this girl is ready to go. She’s not just saying it,  she starts to get physical fast and tears off his clothes.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GAHyaJ0eIA8", "annotation_id": "9070684", "url": "https://genius.com/9070684", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "Them girls, they just wanna take my money They don’t want me to give you nothing They don’t want you to have nothing They don’t wanna see me find your lovin’", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Them girls” seem to be the “waste ting shorty” types from above who aren’t honest when they’re with him. He sees through them. They would never support Drake when he’s with another girl, even if she obviously makes him happy, because that means they can’t get their chance with him. The final line is probably a reference to Drake’s single, “Find Your Love.”\n\nDrake defies them girls who want to be the object of his affection for mercenary reasons. He already has this girl, and he will take care of her because they have something special together, and not because she flatters him to get a piece of his fame.\n\nDrake highlights the abusive nature of “them girls,” portraying them as jealous gold diggers. You don’t want a gold digger—just ask Kanye. However, according to CNN, some girls see logic in marrying for money:\n\nBank accounts—and debts—do matter. And acknowledging that doesn’t make me a gold digger akin to Anna Nicole Smith — it makes me smart.\n\nAccording to Forbes, they may not have the worst thought process:\n\nIt’s time to start treating your life like reality, not a fairy tale. We live in a world that runs on dollars and good sense. The GDI can prevent a future that no smart girl believes will happen to her. Were not saying become Anna Nicole and go after some old codger just for his wallet (unless that’s what you’re into). We’re saying find a man to enjoy, to have fun with, be intimate with. Just remember to take economics into account. It’s a move toward financial security.", "annotation_id": "9070679", "url": "https://genius.com/9070679", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "I think I’d lie for you (Woop) I think I’d die for you Jodeci “Cry for You” Do things when you want me to Like controlla, controlla (Woop), yeah Like controlla, controlla (Woop), yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In line with the Jamaican dialect which appears throughout the track, ‘controller’ becomes ‘controlla.’ Think of ‘controlla’ like a game controller. You use the controller and what ever is desired is done. Basically, Drake’s giving this woman full autonomy in their relationship—she can pick the time and the place, and he’ll satisfy all her requests.\n\nAlternatively, ‘controlla’ could be meant to sound like control her, suggesting what Drake talks about in the second verse—this woman wants him to take the dominant role, both in bed and in other aspects of their partnership.\n\nAt first glance, these sentiments could appear to be purely sexual, however, Drizzy emphasizes his long term commitment to this relationship by paying homage to Jodeci’s passion-filled song “Cry For You.” The track itself paints the vivid image of a man laying out every emotion and devoting his whole life to attain a woman’s heart.\n\n\n\nAside from sampling them on his track “How About Now,” Drake’s shown love for Jodeci on many occasions. His father, Dennis Graham, spoke on how influential their music was during the early stages of his son’s life in Drizzy’s track “Jodeci Freestyle” featuring J. Cole:\n\nThe music that you and I used to listen to ahh.. was just..\nAbsolutely phenomenal, because we went through it all\nWe went through errythang..\nI remember you loved Jodeci\nI mean like studied, you even made me a CD…", "annotation_id": "9070665", "url": "https://genius.com/9070665", "state": "accepted", "votes": 168, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "Okay (o—), you like it (You like it), when I get (When I get) Aggressive (Aggressive), tell you to (Tell you to), yeah “Go slower (Slower), go faster (Faster)” Like controlla, controlla, yeah Like controlla, controlla, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This girl likes to be dominated in the bed. Drake talks as if he has a controller where he “pushes her buttons” and his girl acts on his command. “Waste ting” is Patois for a useless thing. Drake isn’t going to get funky in the sheets with a girl unless she’s honest about what she wants, and tells him just how to please her. Obviously, it’s something he’s into as well, so it all works out!\n\nThis once again could be about Rihanna, who has stated publicly that she likes a dominant guy in the bedroom. In an interview with Elle, she said, “Any man that tells me what to do is sexy!” and in Rolling Stone:\n\nBeing submissive in the bedroom is really fun. You get to be a little lady, to have somebody be macho and in charge of your shit. That’s fun to me…I like to be spanked. Being tied up is fun. I like to keep it spontaneous. Sometimes whips and chains can be overly planned – you gotta stop, get the whip from the drawer downstairs. I’d rather have him use his hands.\n\nOn May 18, 2016, Drake appeared on The Ellen Show and was asked about his relationship with Rihanna. Ellen confirmed that RiRi and Drizzy were “just friends, only friends” to which Drake unconvincingly responded, “yes.”\n\nIn the below video, Ellen brings up a photo of Drake and Rihanna grinding onstage, to which Drake coyly responds, “that’s friendship at its finest.”\n\nhttp://ellentube.com/videos/0-nw6hexsb/", "annotation_id": "9070645", "url": "https://genius.com/9070645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Control", "line": "My old flex is my new flex now, and we’re workin’ on it, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Flex in this case is slang for a girl. His old girl is now his new girl because he’s back with her trying to work on their relationship to fix the problem that ended it in the first place.\n\nThis line is probably about Rihanna, as “we’re workin' on it” could easily be a reference to the Rihanna’s song “Work,” on which Drake is featured.", "annotation_id": "9070560", "url": "https://genius.com/9070560", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
