{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "(Over and over and over and over and over again)", "vault_lines": ["And now you're coming over", "Over and over again, over and over again", "And you want me to show ya, uh (I'm gonna show you again)"], "meaning": "This use of repetition suggests the love and sex is amazing, making his girl desire him enough to come back, time and time again", "annotation_id": "33035902", "url": "https://genius.com/33035902", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "How far we go always seems to get better with time", "vault_lines": ["How far we go always seems to get better with time"], "meaning": "Daniel Daley is saying he enjoys time with his girl more over time and the intimacy grows deeper with repetition", "annotation_id": "33035878", "url": "https://genius.com/33035878", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "Niggas like to play games ’Cause they feel like they know me (They know me) You don’t know me, nigga, I done changed (I done changed) So don’t be trying to be putting on shit off in the game (Game)", "vault_lines": ["So don't be trying to be putting on shit off in the game (Game)", "I know you been", "Niggas like to play games", "'Cause they feel like they know me (They know me)", "You don't know me, nigga, I done changed (I done changed)"], "meaning": "Pimp C plays on “game\"—playing games refers to not being up front or truthful for ones own benefit, while \"the game” refers to the rap game—Pimp doesn’t want any fake rappers succeeding.", "annotation_id": "8969037", "url": "https://genius.com/8969037", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "On my way from the studio, so get undressed Let’s do the things that we say on text (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["On my way from the studio, so get undressed", "Let's do the things that we say on text (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Sexting is a form of instant messaging ranging from sexually explicit messages to nude photographs.\n\nNo stranger to sexting (although his attempts have been labelled “cringeworthy, incredibly cringeworthy”) and the occasional kinky activity, Drake wants to bring their virtual fantasies to the bedroom.\n\n\n\nThis girl seems more receptive to his advances. Maybe he was utilizing this app?", "annotation_id": "8924111", "url": "https://genius.com/8924111", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "I want to turn you out like pitch black Want to watch you do work while I sit back (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I want to turn you out like pitch black", "Want to watch you do work while I sit back (Yeah)"], "meaning": "“Turn you out” is to introduce a shy or timid person to a new realm of sexual fantasy. The hook to “A Night Off” explains the “pitch black.”\n\nGo ahead and turn the lights off\nBut keep your heels on\nThey’re fittin' for what I’m about to do\n\nThe lyrics are also reminiscent of Rihanna’s song “Work” which Drake featured on.", "annotation_id": "8920317", "url": "https://genius.com/8920317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "You hit me like, “I know you’re there with someone else” (Yeah) That pussy knows me better than I know myself", "vault_lines": ["You expensive, you know that?", "You hit me like, \"I know you’re there with someone else\" (Yeah)", "That pussy knows me better than I know myself", "I know you been"], "meaning": "Drake never cheats, unless you count the girls he “cheats on.”\n\nEven on a track entitled “Faithful” about commitment to a hard working independent girl, Drake debases her slightly, implying her intellect is housed in her vagina. He has shown growth from this sentiment on 2009’s So Far Gone.\n\nShe sent me one back, but I ain’t never read it\nCause pussy’s only pussy and I get it when I need it\n\nHe’s returning text messages now, which is progress.", "annotation_id": "8920306", "url": "https://genius.com/8920306", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "You would think it’s all mine the way I took it (Yeah) You would think it broke down the way I push it (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["You would think it's all mine the way I took it (Yeah)", "You would think it broke down the way I push it (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Unable to pursue a relationship with this girl, Drake is forced to wait on the sideline. ‘Mine’ implies ownership or exclusivity, as he expressed on Beyoncé’s “Mine”:\n\nI just wanna say, you’re mine, you’re mine\nI just wanna say, you’re mine, you’re mine\nFuck what you heard, you’re mine, you’re mine\nAll I’m really asking for is you\nYou’re mine, you’re mine\n\n‘Push it’ refers to the intensity of Drake’s feelings for this girl. It also demonstrates that he’s willing to put in work when it’s necessary, like pushing a car that’s out of gas. He ‘pushes’ their relationship so hard it’s like he’s fighting to win her back.", "annotation_id": "8920287", "url": "https://genius.com/8920287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "You expensive, you know that? ”I’m high maintenance a little bit, but not in a Not in a negative way I just like extremely expensive things”", "vault_lines": ["You expensive, you know that?", "\"I'm high maintenance a little bit, but not in a", "I know you been", "Not in a negative way", "I just like extremely expensive things\""], "meaning": "The female voice heard in the intro is Amber Rose, a 32-year-old model who dated Kanye West and has a child with Wiz Khalifa.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/_Cv5o4kq73/?taken-by=amberrose&hl=en\n\nIn 2015, a gossip site published extracts from an interview with Amber, in which she said men she meets find her intimidating and “high maintenance.”\n\nHer expensive taste is much easier to document, charging fans up to $80 to simply meet her—money she seems to funnel into her lavish car collection.", "annotation_id": "8920276", "url": "https://genius.com/8920276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "You hate it when I coat things all in sugar You want to hear the real talk, well, girl, who wouldn’t? (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["You hate it when I coat things all in sugar", "You want to hear the real talk, well, girl, who wouldn't? (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Sugar coating the truth wraps an alternate narrative around the facts. As one of the realest out, Drake prefers to keep things 100.\n\nShe think I’m the realest out\nAnd I say damn that makes two of us\n\nThe pre-hook has even more similarities with “Shot for Me,” on which Drake rapped:\n\nOk, look, I’m honest. Girl, I can’t lie, I miss you\nYou and the music were the only things that I’d commit to", "annotation_id": "8920052", "url": "https://genius.com/8920052", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "Check, one-two, one-two, bitch", "vault_lines": ["Check, one-two, one-two, bitch"], "meaning": "“Check 1-2” is a popular trope, describing someone checking a microphone to see if it’s working correctly.  Pimp C has used this intro before on “Watch the Reaction” and “Like That (Remix)”.", "annotation_id": "8918944", "url": "https://genius.com/8918944", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "This ain’t no motherfucking ’91 We out here rapping for money, you niggas rapping for fun", "vault_lines": ["This ain't no motherfucking '91", "We out here rapping for money, you niggas rapping for fun"], "meaning": "Pimp C gives an introspective look on how business in the rap game has changed since 1991. UGK released their first EP, The Southern Way, and their first album, Too Hard To Swallow, in 1992—for them rap was a full-time job, not a hobby.\n\nRapping hasn’t always been so lucrative. Only after the turn of the millennium have we seen a surge in rappers that are worth tens of millions.\n\nPimp C criticizes rappers who are stuck in the established conventions of hip hop—their unprofessional attitude is going to keep them from hitting the mainstream.", "annotation_id": "8917322", "url": "https://genius.com/8917322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "‘Foot off in their ass’ is what Ken told me", "vault_lines": ["'Foot off in their ass' is what Ken told me"], "meaning": "“Ken” refers to Pimpin' Ken. He was a former pimp, and a semi-successful author, writing the critically acclaimed The Art of Human Chess: A Study Guide to Winning.\n\nPimp C and Pimpin' Ken were close friends and collaborators. He was notably featured on “Two Type Of Bitches,” a standout track from UGK’s Underground Kingz.\n\n\n\n“Foot in your ass” is an assertion of dominance or control.", "annotation_id": "8917196", "url": "https://genius.com/8917196", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "[Verse 1: Pimp C]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake takes the first part of Pimp C’s verse on “Tom Ford (Remix),” which was released on Pimp C’s 44th birthday back in 2014.\n\nDrake has shown love for Pimp C’s duo UGK, before on “Under Ground Kings.” He also spoke about the rapper in an interview with MTV:\n\nI’m a huge Pimp [C] fan. I feel like I’m extended UGK family. Bun and Pimp are like uncles, dads to me…. It’s just seeing a bit of them in myself…", "annotation_id": "8917128", "url": "https://genius.com/8917128", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "’Cause you talk like you got what I need (Yeah) You talk like you got the juice and the squeeze (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you talk like you got what I need (Yeah)", "You talk like you got the juice and the squeeze (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Drake’s girl is something special, and she knows it. Assuming Drake needs it is quite braggadocios—she has “the juice and the squeeze\"—explicit euphemisms for ‘wet’ and ‘tight’.\n\nThis may also be a callback to Eve’s 2001 song “Got What You Need.”", "annotation_id": "8917092", "url": "https://genius.com/8917092", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "Working, working, working, working, ain’t ya? You don’t have no time to lay up You just tryna be somebody ’Fore you say you need somebody Get all your affairs in order I won’t have affairs, I’m yours, girl Faithful, faithful, faithful, faithful", "vault_lines": ["Working, working, working, working, ain't ya?", "You don't have no time to lay up", "You just tryna be somebody", "'Fore you say you need somebody", "Get all your affairs in order", "I won't have affairs, I'm yours, girl", "Faithful, faithful, faithful, faithful", "Won't ya, outsmart me, no"], "meaning": "Similar to the predicament portrayed in his “Tell Your Friends (Remix),” this woman is continuously working on improving her career, meaning she has very little time to commit to a relationship. Furthermore, she doubts if Drake’s serious, as his reputation of loving and leaving women has preceded him.\n\nDespite her apprehensions, Drake uses subtle wordplay around “affairs,” to both reassure her that he’s willing to wait for her and give her the sufficient space to get her life in order; whilst also letting her know that he’ll stay monogamous.\n\nThe repetition of “Working” and Drake’s eagerness for this woman to finish at work is reminiscent of his and Rihanna’s 2016 collaboration, “Work”:\n\nYou need to get done, done, done, done at work, come over", "annotation_id": "8917086", "url": "https://genius.com/8917086", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "I don’t fuck with nobody in this shit but Bun (In this shit but Bun)", "vault_lines": ["I don't fuck with nobody in this shit but Bun (In this shit but Bun)"], "meaning": "In the late 1980’s, Pimp C began a rap duo with Bun B called UGK. The two were very close friends as they released several albums charting on Billboard 200. Sadly, Pimp C passed away in 2007 from accidental cough medicine overdose.", "annotation_id": "8917036", "url": "https://genius.com/8917036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "line": "I want to get straight to the climax (Straight, straight to the) Yeah, have you coming all summer like a season pass (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I want to get straight to the climax (Straight, straight to the)", "Yeah, have you coming all summer like a season pass (Yeah)"], "meaning": "A season pass holder has access to every home game a team plays during the regular season. It can also be for an amusement park, allowing entry and use for an entire year.\n\n“Come” is interchangeable with homophone “cum”, meaning to orgasm, or climax. Drake’s feature on Trey Songz’s “I Invented Sex” is a strong endorsement for his ability in the bedroom.", "annotation_id": "8917008", "url": "https://genius.com/8917008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faithful", "status": "done", "targets": 17, "clicked": 17, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-faithful-lyrics", "genius_title": "Faithful"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fucking Fans", "line": "You was at the crib reading stories that they sent you Most of that was bullshit but some of it I did do", "vault_lines": ["You was at the crib reading stories that they sent you", "Most of that was bullshit but some of it I did do"], "meaning": "Drake relives old memories when describing such an evocative and graphic scenario. Due to Drake’s fame and wealth, a lot of girls, especially lustful and one-night colleens, search for a taste of Drake’s lifestyle. And even if Drake sees behind the lies and fake emotions, he still accepts them, even if it’ll prejudice him in the future.", "annotation_id": "35651914", "url": "https://genius.com/35651914", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fucking Fans", "line": "Nights when I just needed to hold somebody Feeling overwhelmed, should’ve told somebody", "vault_lines": ["Nights when I just needed to hold somebody", "Feeling overwhelmed, should've told somebody"], "meaning": "Drake paints a vivid picture of nights when he felt alone and excluded, showing us how lonely it can get at his level of wealth and fame.\n\nDrake is known to put much thought into his craft, often overwhelming himself with various thoughts and emotions, leading to unhealthy brainstorming. So, to cope, Drake claims he should’ve told somebody how he felt to clear his mind. It’s unclear what consequences came from not doing so, but it’s likely that it led to sleepless nights and unhealthy habits.", "annotation_id": "35651864", "url": "https://genius.com/35651864", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fucking Fans", "line": "Went six digits on the lock screen code You crack into that ho and it’s gon’ be a lot of smoke", "vault_lines": ["Went six digits on the lock screen code", "You crack into that ho and it's gon' be a lot of smoke"], "meaning": "On an iPhone you have the option to unlock your phone using FaceID or a regular numerical passcode. Most people use a 4-digit code but Drake took it one step further and set up a 6-digit code, which ideally should provide more security.\n\nIf his girl successfully breaks into his phone than he’s gonna be really pissed because a 6-digit code takes a lot of effort to break into.", "annotation_id": "24032164", "url": "https://genius.com/24032164", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fucking Fans", "line": "Cover up a lot, can’t cover up your soul", "vault_lines": ["Cover up a lot, can't cover up your soul"], "meaning": "These lines may be about Drake’s ex-girlfriend Rihanna. It was reported that both artists had gotten matching shark tattoos during a visit to Ripley’s Aquarium Of Canada in Toronto back in 2016.\n\nIn 2021 amid her relationship with rapper A$AP Rocky, Rihanna covered up this tattoo.", "annotation_id": "23887329", "url": "https://genius.com/23887329", "state": "pending", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fucking Fans", "line": "If she got a watch then you know it’s not a stainless", "vault_lines": ["If she got a watch then you know it's not a stainless"], "meaning": "Stainless steel is a common, but cheap, material for watches.  If Drake gifted a girl a watch, it won’t be stainless steel but instead something more expensive like gold or platinum.", "annotation_id": "23877839", "url": "https://genius.com/23877839", "state": "pending", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fucking Fans", "line": "Especially when the whole entire world wished they had you", "vault_lines": ["Especially when the whole entire world wished they had you"], "meaning": "Drake may potentially be pointing toward the idea that he is talking about Rihanna – being the worldwide star she is.\n\nDrake and Rihanna have notably had a complicated on and off relationship over the years. Publically, they even shared the stage at the 2016 VMA’s together.", "annotation_id": "23874650", "url": "https://genius.com/23874650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fucking Fans", "line": "Calendar must say two, zero, one, four", "vault_lines": ["Calendar must say two, zero, one, four"], "meaning": "Could Drake be referencing that the girl is acting the same as she did back in 2014?", "annotation_id": "23874541", "url": "https://genius.com/23874541", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fucking Fans", "line": "Then I had a kid even though I never planned to", "vault_lines": ["Then I had a kid even though I never planned to"], "meaning": "In 2017, Drake had an unplanned child–Adonis Graham–with French retired adult film star Sophie Brussaux. Brussaux alleged that, upon discovering of her pregnancy, Drake sent her text messages requesting her to have an abortion, however, Drake has since come to appreciate Brussaux and Adonis.\n\nDrake discussed the situation in-depth on his 2018 track “March 14.”", "annotation_id": "23874206", "url": "https://genius.com/23874206", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fucking Fans", "status": "done", "targets": 8, "clicked": 8, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-fucking-fans-lyrics", "genius_title": "Fucking Fans"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "(Freebandz)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Future founded his record label Freebandz in December of 2012. His signed artists are collectively known as Freeband Gang.\n\n\n\nFreebandz logo", "annotation_id": "29587865", "url": "https://genius.com/29587865", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Reporting live from the gutter I will buy this motherfucker, it’s not even a discussion, woo And I got my niggas with me, yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Reporting live from the gutter", "I will buy this motherfucker, it's not even a discussion, woo", "And I got my niggas with me, yeah, yeah", "Live from the gutter, dawg, yeah, yeah", "Reportin' live from the damn gutter", "Reportin' live from the gutter"], "meaning": "Echoing the sentiment from Drake’s ubiquitous “Started From The Bottom,” his income allows him to buy just about anything he requires.\n\nI’ll buy the neighbors house if they complain about the noise\n\nNo mean feat when you consider where he lives.", "annotation_id": "7857413", "url": "https://genius.com/7857413", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Known to pop a bottle on a model, fuck her like I’m on my last damn dollar", "vault_lines": ["Known to pop a bottle on a model, fuck her like I'm on my last damn dollar"], "meaning": "Generosity, propelled by survivor’s guilt, keeps Future popping bottles past his means.\n\nWhen you consider his net worth is just $4 million, and he is spending $60k on a Monday, not to mention Rollies and Phantom’s, it’s not inconceivable that he will be on his last damn dollar in the not too distant future.\n\nExpensive taste…", "annotation_id": "7857406", "url": "https://genius.com/7857406", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Known for getting that guala out in Europe, but I’m still hood", "vault_lines": ["Known for getting that guala out in Europe, but I'm still hood"], "meaning": "Future toured on the back of Honest in 2014, taking in the UK, France, Italy, and Germany.\n\nI did 56 broads on the European tour and they was all crazy\n\nHow many trappers tour around Europe every other year? How many do it, and can still come back? Future has direct ties to the trap, and it keeps his hood pass valid no matter what luxury he indulges in.", "annotation_id": "7857400", "url": "https://genius.com/7857400", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Wake up in the crib, pool sitting on the hill now I just need some niggas with me that’s gon’ keep it real now", "vault_lines": ["Wake up in the crib, pool sitting on the hill now", "I just need some niggas with me that's gon' keep it real now"], "meaning": "Future’s move from Atlanta to L.A. was the ultimate “move out of the trap” moment, Atlanta being the home of trap music right now, and L.A. being home to the Hollywood elite.\n\n“The Hills” is an exclusive neighbourhood in L.A., populated by the rich and famous. Future needs to reach back and pull some of his day one people with him, lest the money and fame go to his head.", "annotation_id": "7856747", "url": "https://genius.com/7856747", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "A fiend for that lean, I ain’t start drinking beer yet They bust the trap, I live there Came out clean, I ain’t clean, my niggas still there", "vault_lines": ["A fiend for that lean, I ain't start drinking beer yet", "They bust the trap, I live there", "Came out clean, I ain't clean, my niggas still there"], "meaning": "Future’s lean addiction is real. He’s named two albums after the cocktail and the number of references in his lyrics extends past triple figures. While lean a daily requirement, alcohol (and marijuana) are saved for after dark.\n\n\n\nA “trap” is an area where drugs are made and or sold. Unlike some rappers who just use trap music’s sound to make hit records, Future claims to still live that lifestyle. He’s managed to avoid the penitentiary, hence he “came out clean”, but unlike some he remains tethered by his “hobby”, and the friends he left behind.\n\nNoisey’s series Atlanta gives an insight into what that world is really like.", "annotation_id": "7842398", "url": "https://genius.com/7842398", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "I see hell everywhere, I get mail everywhere Walked inside the booth and came out in a Learjet", "vault_lines": ["I see girls everywhere, I see scales everywhere", "I see hell everywhere, I get mail everywhere", "Walked inside the booth and came out in a Learjet"], "meaning": "A reference to Superman’s Clark Kent. Clark would go into a phone booth as Clark and come out as Superman. In Future’s case he walks into his “booth” or studio, and comes out in a Learjet – a very expensive private jet.\n\nThe skills he displays in music have afforded him the luxury of private airline travel. Email privileges come standard.", "annotation_id": "7841396", "url": "https://genius.com/7841396", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "This for my niggas on that bullshit and that nonsense", "vault_lines": ["This for my niggas on that bullshit and that nonsense"], "meaning": "In 2015 Drake has proven himself a savage when it comes to rap beef, a title he’d never courted nor had bestowed upon him before.\n\nThrough “Charged Up” and “Back To Back”, he effectively silenced Meek Mill with ruthless effectiveness.\n\nHe has recently come out and spoken on this feelings towards the Meek beef.\n\n“This is a discussion about music, and no one’s putting forth any music?… Nobody told you that this was a bad idea, to engage in this and not have something?…It was weighing heavy on me. I didn’t get it. I didn’t get how there was no strategy on the opposite end. I just didn’t understand. I didn’t understand it because that’s just not how we operate.”\n\nWhere there is smoke, there is fire, and Meek has effectively shut himself out of the beef since dropping his critically panned “Wanna Know” diss track. It seems he hasn’t reached out to Drake either, offering to “smoke” with him and squash the feud. He appears to have washed his hands clean of the beef, yet is still copping heavy subliminal shots.", "annotation_id": "7841227", "url": "https://genius.com/7841227", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Wake up in the house, I look up, I see bales everywhere I see girls everywhere, I see scales everywhere", "vault_lines": ["Wake up in the house, I look up, I see bales everywhere", "I see girls everywhere, I see scales everywhere"], "meaning": "Bales are compressed bricks of marijuana, and scales are as common in a trap house as naked women.\n\nFuture is the plug and the boss, so women aren’t just accessories to trafficking, they’re seduced by the money and power.\n\nHeron is short for heroin.", "annotation_id": "7841047", "url": "https://genius.com/7841047", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Talking ten mil’ just to get our assets I know them tears still fallin’ now on my last bitch", "vault_lines": ["I know them tears still fallin' now on my last bitch", "Talking ten mil' just to get our assets"], "meaning": "Future is playing with big fish here. Drake made a staggering $39.5 million in the past 12 months, whilst Future’s net worth is quite a bit lower than that…\n\nLower even than his ex-girlfriend Ciara, who is referenced here. She cried in an interview after their break-up, and although she has moved on there is clearly some ill feeling left behind.", "annotation_id": "7840866", "url": "https://genius.com/7840866", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Reportin’ live from the fuckin’ gutter, bitch I ain’t talkin’ but some big money shit I ain’t talkin’ nothin’ but big money shit I ain’t talkin’ nothin’ but big money, bitch", "vault_lines": ["Reportin' live from the fuckin' gutter, bitch", "I ain't talkin' but some big money shit", "I ain't talkin' nothin' but big money shit", "Reporting live from the gutter", "Live from the gutter, dawg, yeah, yeah", "I ain't talkin' nothin' but big money, bitch", "Reportin' live from the damn gutter", "Reportin' live from the gutter"], "meaning": "Future’s early life was rough—he grew up in the Kirkwood neighborhood of Atlanta, a city that was hit hard by the 1980’s crack epidemic. The murder rate nearly doubled by the time he turned 7, and he visited his uncle twice a month in prison.\n\nIn an interview with Rolling Stone, Future explained why he talks about money:\n\nWhen I was in the streets, I ain’t never think about that, man, fuck tomorrow, nigga, let’s do this shit today. I’m a fucking rock star, dogg. I’m enjoying this shit, I ain’t trying to remember yesterday.", "annotation_id": "7837514", "url": "https://genius.com/7837514", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "She gon’ end up dippin’ with me, yeah, yeah And I got her trippin’ off the yah-yah Pillow talking, dishing out on all y’all, yeah In one ear and out the other Shut your mouth and take what’s coming", "vault_lines": ["And I got my niggas with me, yeah, yeah", "She gon' end up dippin' with me, yeah, yeah", "And I got her trippin' off the yah-yah", "Pillow talking, dishing out on all y'all, yeah", "In one ear and out the other", "Shut your mouth and take what's coming"], "meaning": "“Dipping” means to leave suddenly. Drake was with his boys, partying music video-style when he left with this new girl.\n\n“Yay yay” is slang for cocaine. Drake picks up a lovely young girl, invites her back to his bachelor pad, and they indulge in some cocaine. All of a sudden, she’s spilling secrets on the competition. Nothing of note though, or at least nothing worth delaying sex for.\n\nThe way he says “yeah, yeah” is similar to how OG Maco says it on “FUCKEMX3.”", "annotation_id": "7837442", "url": "https://genius.com/7837442", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Just imagine you was livin’ lavish and they still there", "vault_lines": ["Just imagine you was livin' lavish and they still there"], "meaning": "Future references a line from his hook off of “March Madness”, this time around flipping the boastful bar into a reflective line based around survivors guilt. How would you feel if you were dropping $60k at Magic City on a Monday whilst your closest friends remain in the ghetto, selling drugs?", "annotation_id": "7836798", "url": "https://genius.com/7836798", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "This money make me hungry, I’m a savage I seen the stars lining up, you couldn’t imagine", "vault_lines": ["This money make me hungry, I'm a savage", "I seen the stars lining up, you couldn't imagine"], "meaning": "Drake has evidently been inducted into Future’s Savage Crew, and he’s a worthy asset. He’s achieved cash cow status in hip hop. Future’s own star has only risen in the past 3 years. He’s now part of the elite hip-hop new school.\n\nAs in 2011’s Watch The Throne, two of the biggest stars of the moment have aligned to produce an epic collaboration.", "annotation_id": "7836721", "url": "https://genius.com/7836721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Ah, Cuban links hanging on my wrist, I was on welfare", "vault_lines": ["Ah, Cuban links hanging on my wrist, I was on welfare"], "meaning": "Now, it’s nothing for Future to drop some stacks on a Cuban Link chain, even for his girl.\n\nThe Cuban links on my ho\nTen whips in a row, who would think we were poor?\nWho would think we was poor?\n\nRemember though, he started from the gutter. When  Raekwon’s Only Built 4 Cuban Linx was released in 1995, Future was just 12, and an aspiring basketball player who relied on welfare.", "annotation_id": "7835762", "url": "https://genius.com/7835762", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "I watched my broad give up on me like I’m average I went back inside the attic, counted up and started laughing, ah I went back inside the attic, counted up and started laughing, ah", "vault_lines": ["I watched my broad give up on me like I'm average", "I went back inside the attic, counted up and started laughing, ah"], "meaning": "Ciara gave up on Future in the summer of 2014, just three months after their son, Future Zahir Wilburn, was born. Celebrity gossip magazines like US Weekly said Ciara broke off their engagement when she heard Future was cheating on her with his wardrobe consultant.\n\nCiara probably did think Future was acting average. Future, though, denied any infidelity. “I feel like 95% of relationships end with cheating, but me and her relationship had nothing to do with cheating. We grew apart…sometimes the lady don’t see what you see.”\n\nCheating on your significant other is something the average guy might do, but Future knows he’s far from that. But since he’s single now, he can take all the money he’d spend on her and keep it for himself.", "annotation_id": "7835725", "url": "https://genius.com/7835725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Straight up out the gutter, never had shit Now we got 90210 on our address", "vault_lines": ["Straight up out the gutter, never had shit", "Now we got 90210 on our address"], "meaning": "Future’s teenage years were lost to drug dealing. Coming from a poor background, from seventeen to twenty-four he was hustling hard, and it wasn’t until as late as 2010 that he really devoted himself to music.\n\nThe payoff is clear: he moved from Atlanta to L.A. and can call Beverly Hills, California’s famous zip code 90210 his own.", "annotation_id": "7835496", "url": "https://genius.com/7835496", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "On the Billboards, all we do is pop shit Soon as night fall, that’s when we lock in", "vault_lines": ["On the Billboards, all we do is pop shit", "Soon as night fall, that's when we lock in"], "meaning": "Drake is the owner of some pretty prestigious Billboard records—all time most #1 Rap/R&B singles and most simultaneous Hot 100 songs—so it makes sense or him to pop bottles in celebration.\n\n\n\nSince Drake is talking about the popularity of his songs, it’s possible he’s also talking about his presence on the Billboard Pop Charts—he’s a true crossover success.\n\nWhile his evening play-time may have expanded some since 2010, he’s still the self-proclaimed hardest worker in the game.\n\nThey don’t work hard as you, damn that’s so crazy\nAt the end of the night when you count, numbers don’t lie to my baby", "annotation_id": "7835474", "url": "https://genius.com/7835474", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "This for my dawgs that go Karrueche with the chopsticks, woo", "vault_lines": ["This for my dawgs that go Karrueche with the chopsticks, woo"], "meaning": "Drake is both throwing subliminal shots at his eternal rival Chris Brown and paying homage to Future’s DJ, Esco. Esco was rumored to be dating Karrueche Tran, an ex-girlfriend Chris Brown who is part Vietnamese (hence the chopsticks reference). However, Karrueche shot the rumors down.\n\nHours following the release of this record, Chris Brown responded on Instagram with the caption:\n\nI’m too blessed for the bullshit. People who have had to sit a watch me for years do me and watch their females actually love me always show their insecurities. I embrace anything God has given me. It’s bigger than this music shit. I’m content with being Chris Brown. A lot of people are unhappy and a lot of these artist lie to the fans or rely on hype to gain substance. You niggas bore me. Ima keep being who God made me to be!  #IAMMUSIC #othersbarelyholdanote #thesehandsworkgreat", "annotation_id": "7835463", "url": "https://genius.com/7835463", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "line": "Hendrix Yeah Aqua Ah (I woke up like this)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Future’s nickname “Future Hendrix” comes from Jimi Hendrix, one of the best guitarists of all time. He uses this nickname quite often in his music, and it’s also the name of his Instagram profile.\n\n“I woke up like this” is a reference to Beyoncé’s song “***Flawless”:\n\nI woke up like this, I woke up like this\nWe flawless, ladies tell ‘em\nSay “I look so good tonight”", "annotation_id": "7835372", "url": "https://genius.com/7835372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live from the Gutter", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 20, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-future-live-from-the-gutter-lyrics", "genius_title": "Live from the Gutter"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Love Me", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-would-you-love-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "Would You Love Me"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Lighty lookin’ healthy and she gets smiles", "vault_lines": ["Lighty lookin' healthy and she gets smiles"], "meaning": "Lighty is British slang for a light-skinned girl, usually of mixed race, while healthy is British slang for good looking. He’s talking about a light-skinned good looking girl who attracts smiles from people who look at her.", "annotation_id": "12848354", "url": "https://genius.com/12848354", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Man gets wrappin’ when we’re suckin’ somethin’ (Straight)", "vault_lines": ["Man gets wrappin' when we're suckin' somethin' (Straight)"], "meaning": "Using the metaphor of a lollipop, Giggs explains that while his team obtains a sweet lolly, his opposition simply gets the useless wrapping surrounding the lollipop.", "annotation_id": "12718236", "url": "https://genius.com/12718236", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Saw the monster, it’s like a truck or somethin’ (Jheeze)", "vault_lines": ["Stuck or somethin', like a nigga stuck or somethin' (Stuck or somethin')", "Saw the monster, it's like a truck or somethin' (Jheeze)"], "meaning": "Here Giggs likens himself to a monster truck, a type of large pick-up vehicle known to be used in events that include racing and style jumps. Giggs also describes himself as the “Monsta Man”, which is kind of his alter ego in the bedroom.", "annotation_id": "12278925", "url": "https://genius.com/12278925", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Man catch suckers, then we’re touchin’ somethin’ (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Man catch suckers, then we’re touchin' somethin' (Yeah)"], "meaning": "To ‘touch’ someone is a phrase commonly used in UK grime music, and it means attack, beat up, or generally harm. This is a term Giggs has used before in Bus Commercial\n\nBring it to the battlefield I’ll touch his mate\n\nIf Giggs or any of his associates find someone who has wronged them in any way, they are likely to face the consequences.", "annotation_id": "11841355", "url": "https://genius.com/11841355", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Now you man are on a diss ting", "vault_lines": ["Now you man are on a diss ting"], "meaning": "Here Drake alludes to the various artists that have come at him with diss tracks or general beef. Be it his highly publicized feud with Meek Mill, or other tracks that are up for interpretation such as Jay-Z on DJ Khaled’s ‘Shining’, or even Kanye West’s Twitter rants, due to Drizzy’s popularity he will always be someone’s target.", "annotation_id": "11841317", "url": "https://genius.com/11841317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Niggas wanna talk splits, now we need the whole thing", "vault_lines": ["Niggas wanna talk splits, now we need the whole thing"], "meaning": "A theme on “No Long Talk” is that Drake will retaliate at anyone who insults him, which is evident with lines like:\n\n\nIf it’s a chit-chat ting, better talk nice\n\nYouts talk down, then they get ran\n\n\nEven from his colleagues, Drake doesn’t want splitting profits to come up as he is insulted by it and will take the whole pot if it happens.\n\nThis could also be a nod to the hook of “Star67” on his 2015 mixtape, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late.\n\nBlowin' up, line blowin' up, they need the whole thing\nBlowin' up, my niggas really need the whole thing", "annotation_id": "11792712", "url": "https://genius.com/11792712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "(Switch it)", "vault_lines": ["In the whip, on the left side, on a death ride (Switch it)", "Sexy, and I rate that and her sex style (Switch it)"], "meaning": "This is a signature Giggs' adlib which he uses to indicate he is about to change his flow during a verse.", "annotation_id": "11639811", "url": "https://genius.com/11639811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Skull gets hot, then I’m not nice", "vault_lines": ["Skull gets hot, then I'm not nice"], "meaning": "“Hot skull” is Jamaican slang for a thug/gangsta.\n\nDrake has a “hot skull” tattoo with “unruly” written below in reference to Jamaican artist and friend, Popcaan.", "annotation_id": "11601802", "url": "https://genius.com/11601802", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "I’m on the best side, got the TEC lined In the whip, on the left side, on a death ride (Switch it)", "vault_lines": ["I'm on the best side, got the TEC lined", "In the whip, on the left side, on a death ride (Switch it)"], "meaning": "In the UK the driver is on the right hand side. Giggs, sitting in the shotgun position of the car on a death ride with his TEC-9 lined up to fire, implies that him and Drake are about to commit a drive by.", "annotation_id": "11566011", "url": "https://genius.com/11566011", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "You dun know, it’s Hollowman Giggs", "vault_lines": ["You dun know, it's Hollowman Giggs"], "meaning": "You dun know = You’ve ‘done already known’ about something. In other words, you should know, or you do know.\n\nHollowman is Giggs' nickname which he explained in a BBC interview  that he got from his time in prison:\n\nHollowman came from when i was in jail. People can’t see me when I’m coming – if you get what i mean\n\nGiggs is borrowing from Hollowman, a movie about a scientist who turns himself invisible.", "annotation_id": "11546351", "url": "https://genius.com/11546351", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "MAC-10 and the spesh flies, and the TEC slide Nutty shit like somethin’ just climbed out the X-Files (Ooh)", "vault_lines": ["MAC-10 and the spesh flies, and the TEC slide", "Nutty shit like somethin' just climbed out the X-Files (Ooh)"], "meaning": "The MAC-10 and the TEC-9 are both automatic firearms, commonly used by the military but are also heavily available through the black market. The “spesh” refers to the .38 Smith & Wesson Special, a cartridge commonly fired out of a revolver.\n\nGiggs is threatening enemies with both these weapons and noting that they look so dangerous, they would have been something the agents from the sci-fi drama, The X-Files, would have used, which ran from 1993 to 2002. The show centered around two FBI agents who investigated paranormal crimes.", "annotation_id": "11543495", "url": "https://genius.com/11543495", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "(Wheel it!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Wheel it” means to transition between beats or to “reload” the beat and play the track again. Drake’s had his DJ do this before while performing live.", "annotation_id": "11539678", "url": "https://genius.com/11539678", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Just know man like Fif, he’s a sickaz You get tanned, he don’t miss things", "vault_lines": ["Just know man like Chubbs", "Just know man like Fif, he's a sickaz", "You get tanned, he don’t miss things", "Just know man like me, I’m a Sixer"], "meaning": "Anthony “Fif” Soares is a member of the OVO affiliate group, Reps Up.\n\nTan and/or tanning is Jamaican/UK slang for shooting someone. Drake claims that Fif is a sharpshooter and he will not miss his target.\n\nFif reportedly passed away on Sept 14th, 2017. According to a local news report, the victim was shot multiple times as he was entering the lobby of a building in Scarborough, Toronto.\n\nDrake posted about the death of his childhood friend on his Instagram, followed with the caption:\n\nRIP to one of our family members… our brother… I still can’t even believe this morning was real. It was a honor to have shared years together and I will always keep your memory alive Forever Fif\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/BZCdvvHD8R_/\n\nTo further celebrate the life of Fif, Drake was revealed to of gained a tattoo featuring the phrase “Forever Fif” with a portrait for his face.", "annotation_id": "11537359", "url": "https://genius.com/11537359", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Preme got the err tucked, can’t see the whole thing", "vault_lines": ["Preme got the err tucked, can't see the whole thing"], "meaning": "Preme, better known as P. Reign, is a Canadian hip-hop artist and member of Reps Up, which is an affiliate label of OVO. He is also Drake’s close friend.\n\n“Err” is a slang term for a gun; P. Reign’s always prepared with a firearm. Since it’s tucked away on his person, not all of it is visible.", "annotation_id": "11537297", "url": "https://genius.com/11537297", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Man gone Hollywood, it’s like I’m Buck or somethin’ (Buck)", "vault_lines": ["Man gone Hollywood, it's like I'm Buck or somethin' (Buck)"], "meaning": "Giggs' manager, Michael ‘Buck’ Maris, has also had a prosperous career in the film industry as an actor, writer and producer.\n\nHis credits include Anuvahood (2011), the roadman comedy parodying Kidulthood, and Anti-Social (2015), which starred Skepta and Devlin. Giggs himself even appeared in the 2011 urban action film Victim, which Buck co-wrote.\n\nNow that Drake has put Giggs' music onto an American audience, he looks forward to the dizzying heights of international stardom which may well come alongside these guest verses. In this sense, he’s “gone Hollywood”.", "annotation_id": "11533895", "url": "https://genius.com/11533895", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "OVO and SN1 to the world, brap", "vault_lines": ["OVO and SN1 to the world, brap"], "meaning": "“OvO” (Octobers Very Own) is Drake’s own record label and “SN1” (Spare No 1) is Giggs' record label. Giggs is making their partnership official.", "annotation_id": "11533540", "url": "https://genius.com/11533540", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Yeah (Six!) Yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“The Six” is a nickname for Drake’s hometown of Toronto. He popularized the term, which is based on the number six in both of Toronto’s area codes, 416 and 647.\n\nDrake has mentioned The Six numerous times in his music. He included “6” in the titles of multiple tracks on his 2015 mixtape If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, and promoted his 2016 studio album as Views From the 6 before changing it to just Views at the last minute.", "annotation_id": "11533516", "url": "https://genius.com/11533516", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Murda on tha beat so it’s not nice", "vault_lines": ["Murda on tha beat so it's not nice"], "meaning": "This is one of Canadian producer Murda Beatz' production tags. This tag also appears on track 11 of More Life, “Portland,” which features Travis Scott and Quavo. Both artists have collaborated with Murda before.\n\nThe voice tag first appeared on fellow OVO crew member Baka’s song “AKA,” which Murda produced in 2016.", "annotation_id": "11531849", "url": "https://genius.com/11531849", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "If man child play it, then we Chucky somethin’ (Straight)", "vault_lines": ["If man child play it, then we Chucky somethin' (Straight)"], "meaning": "This line references Drake’s song “Child’s Play” off his fourth studio album, Views (2016). It also refers to the movie that the Chucky doll first appeared in, also titled Child’s Play.\n\nGiggs is implying that if someone comes to him with the bullshit then a fate similar to that of Chucky’s victims may befall them.", "annotation_id": "11531749", "url": "https://genius.com/11531749", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Just know man like me, I’m a Sixer And I oversee the whole thing Yeah, I pree the whole thing (SN1) Yeah, I pree the whole thing", "vault_lines": ["Just know man like Chubbs", "Just know man like Fif, he's a sickaz", "Just know man like me, I’m a Sixer", "And I oversee the whole thing", "Yeah, I pree the whole thing (SN1)"], "meaning": "“The Six” is a nickname for Toronto, which derives from the city’s main area codes, 416 and 647. Drake’s a “Sixer” because he’s from Toronto. One of his pseudonyms is “6 God”—in 2015, Toronto Life magazine named him the most influential person in Toronto.\n\n“Pree” is a Jamaican Patois expression that means “watch or stare at.” Drake sees everything that’s going on in the Toronto music scene.", "annotation_id": "11531387", "url": "https://genius.com/11531387", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Man gets duppied when we touch a button (Touch a button)", "vault_lines": ["Man gets duppied when we touch a button (Touch a button)"], "meaning": "“Duppied” is Jamaican slang for getting knocked out in a physical altercation. Giggs says that when his crew gets things rolling, you will get left down and out. To touch a button (recent reference Vybz Kartel – “Touch A Button”) is also slang in Jamaica and a wider part of the Caribbean meaning to perform an action/make a move.\n\n“Duppy” is also a Jamaican term for “ghost”. Enemies of Giggs cease to exist when he presses the button. Drake referred to making Meek Mill a ghost in the first track on this playlist “Free Smoke”:\n\nHow you let the kid fightin'\nGhost-writin' rumors turn you to a ghost?", "annotation_id": "11531358", "url": "https://genius.com/11531358", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "If Gilla call shots, no questions G-Way til I’m restin’ But we still got love for the West End", "vault_lines": ["If Gilla call shots, no questions", "G-Way til I'm restin'", "But we still got love for the West End"], "meaning": "Gilla is an artist signed to Reps Up, an affiliate label of OVO. “G-Way” is short for Kingston-Galloway, a neighborhood in the East End of Toronto. Galloway is known for its high crime rate and gang activity. Gilla reps G-Way; the music video for his song “Wrapped Up” was shot there:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9L7WP4hX3g", "annotation_id": "11530772", "url": "https://genius.com/11530772", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "That’s Baka, he’s a no-long-talker Quick to let the motherfuckin’ TEC slam", "vault_lines": ["That’s Baka, he's a no-long-talker", "Quick to let the motherfuckin' TEC slam"], "meaning": "Baka, or Baka Not Nice, is a member of the OVO crew and a long-time friend of Drake. He voiced the outro of the previous song, “Free Smoke.” “No long talk'” is a West Indian Patois phrase commonly used by Jamaicans to express frustration with someone who talks too much.\n\n“TEC” is short for “TEC-9,” a semi-automatic pistol. It is popular in gangs because it can be easy converted to fully automatic.\n\nSince Baka was released from jail in 2015, he has started a music career with Drake’s help. He performed next to Drake on his “Boy Meets World” tour in Europe.", "annotation_id": "11530767", "url": "https://genius.com/11530767", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "Just know man like Chubbs He’s a fixer if I ever gotta fix tings", "vault_lines": ["Just know man like Chubbs", "He's a fixer if I ever gotta fix tings", "Just know man like Fif, he's a sickaz", "Just know man like me, I’m a Sixer"], "meaning": "Chubbs is Drake’s bodyguard and close friend who he’s frequently mentioned on popular songs like “The Motion”, “Summer Sixteen”, and “4PM In Calabasas.”", "annotation_id": "11457581", "url": "https://genius.com/11457581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "line": "We don’t need to hear about a next man Yutes talk down, then they get ran Left them, get dipped from the whole ends", "vault_lines": ["We don’t need to hear about a next man", "Yutes talk down, then they get ran", "Left them, get dipped from the whole ends"], "meaning": "“Yout” or “Yute” is the Jamaican Patois pronunciation of the word “youth,” meaning a group of young people. Use of this term to reference others is common within Toronto and the UK’s street culture.\n\nTo “dip” is to leave, exit or depart.\n\n“Ends” is common Toronto and UK slang, rooted in Jamaican Patois, referring to your house or neighborhood.\n\n“Getting dipped from the whole ends” means getting chased out of your neighborhood and not being able to return.", "annotation_id": "11372792", "url": "https://genius.com/11372792", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Long Talk", "status": "done", "targets": 25, "clicked": 25, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-no-long-talk-lyrics", "genius_title": "No Long Talk"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "Yeah, on Benedict with the top down", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, on Benedict with the top down"], "meaning": "Benedict Road is a residential street in the Princess-Rosethorn neighborhood of Etobicoke (Toronto), Ontario, primarily featuring high-end detached houses. It is located near Princess Anne Park and close to amenities on Islington Avenue, such as Thorncrest Plaza and Richview Square.", "annotation_id": "39613503", "url": "https://genius.com/39613503", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "Woah, I’m runnin’ out of opps They’re runnin’ out of options Put ’em up for adoption Sign my name on the dot, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Woah, I'm runnin' out of opps", "They're runnin' out of options", "Put 'em up for adoption", "Sign my name on the dot, ayy"], "meaning": "Keeping with the theme of comparing his opps to his children, Drake agrees to take over custody as if they were kids being put up for adoption.\n\nAlso, the first of a few subliminal shots at Kendrick in the song. Drake’s foes are running out of options, and he’s seizing the opportunity to write his name overtop of theirs to claim the number one spot. He signs his name over “DOTS”", "annotation_id": "39613422", "url": "https://genius.com/39613422", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "Mentioned havin’ us a kid, ’42 hit, I tend to kid around", "vault_lines": ["Mentioned havin' us a kid, '42 hit, I tend to kid around"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing Don Julio 1942, which is a premium tequila.", "annotation_id": "39456630", "url": "https://genius.com/39456630", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "At this point, I’ma start collectin’ child support the opposite way", "vault_lines": ["At this point, I'ma start collectin' child support the opposite way"], "meaning": "Nicki is referencing to her well-known catch phrase “all these bitches is my sons”. Almost ten years ago she said that she didn’t want custody. But now, she got so many “sons” that she might collecting child support.", "annotation_id": "27987329", "url": "https://genius.com/27987329", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "Big owl, big birds forever", "vault_lines": ["Big owl, big birds forever"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to ‘Octobers Very Own’, better known as OVO. Octobers Very Own is a Toronto record label founded by Drake back in 2012. OVO’s logo consists of an Owl. In this line, Drake is simply shouting out his record label.", "annotation_id": "24548320", "url": "https://genius.com/24548320", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "City coming off a lockdown", "vault_lines": ["City coming off a lockdown"], "meaning": "The lockdown in question is an obvious reference to the COVID-19 restrictions lifting in a certain city, possibly Toronto since that is Drakes home town.\n\nhttps://www.cbc.ca/amp/1.6209162", "annotation_id": "24301023", "url": "https://genius.com/24301023", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "This is not luck, baby, this no four-leaf, no horseshoe", "vault_lines": ["This is not luck, baby, this no four-leaf, no horseshoe"], "meaning": "This line refers to the superstitions surrounding the finding of a “four-leaf” clover, and the way in which one holds a horseshoe.\n\nCommonly known, finding a “Four-leaf” clover is considered to be special as there are approximately 10,000 three-leaf clovers for every “lucky” four-leaf and thus finding one is considered to bring luck upon the finder.\n\nFor the “horseshoe”, the superstition involves the ends of a horse’s shoe pointing down, meaning that the good luck is able to flow out and surround the holder.", "annotation_id": "24122720", "url": "https://genius.com/24122720", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "I know that I hurt you", "vault_lines": ["I know that I hurt you", "I'm standing at the top, that's how I know you never seen the top", "Hear the talks when I walk by like, \"You know it's over when he drop\"", "I remember that I told you I miss you, that was kinda like a mass text", "I remember that I told you I loved you, that was really in the past tense", "I remember that I told you I get you, truth is that I get around", "I apologize for my absence, I know I left you without a name to drop", "I don't know how I expected you to get your clout up and get your money up, but"], "meaning": "This song samples Montell Jordan’s Daddy’s Home (1996) a song about a father who abandoned his son, in similar fashion to Drake, who abandoned his “sons” in the rap game. Having dropped a mixtape (Dark Lane Demo Tapes, 2020) and his 2021 EP, (Scary Hours 2) but no full length studio album since his 2018’s Scorpion.\n\nDrake can also be seen mentioning his absence from music on his feature on Bloccboy JB’s 2018 hit, Look Alive.\n\nI’ve been gone since, like, July, niggas actin' like I died", "annotation_id": "23960762", "url": "https://genius.com/23960762", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "Rap niggas doing weak features for a pop artist ’cause they popped down", "vault_lines": ["Rap niggas doing weak features for a pop artist 'cause they popped down"], "meaning": "Keeping in line with the braggadocious themes of the song, Drake disses his hip-hop competition that looks to collaborate with popular pop artists that make it much easier to gain streaming success. Drake has been known to be able to carry songs by himself and also launch lesser known artists to chart success with successful features.\n\nThis may also provide an inkling on why Drake doesn’t tend to collaborate with big pop artists such as Ariana Grande, Dua Lipa, Selena Gomez, etc. The exception is Rihanna in which he regularly collaborated with when he was in a romantic relationship with her. He generally wants people to know that he was the star of the song.", "annotation_id": "23880233", "url": "https://genius.com/23880233", "state": "pending", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "Sierra Canyon parking lot lookin’ like Magic City parking lot", "vault_lines": ["Sierra Canyon parking lot lookin' like Magic City parking lot"], "meaning": "Here drake does not say “Sierra king,” here drake says: “Sierra canyon parking lot lookin' like Magic City parking lot.” Referring to the basketball team of the famous high school “Sierra canyon” in which he has famously attended games with Micheal B Jordan.\n\n\n\n\n\nAnd which the crowds never disappoint, which would lead to a packed parking lot like a magic city parking lot. Which they have famously sold out an arena for a state championship and even at their own home court at Sierra canyon high school.\n\nOn the other hand, magic city  is a famous club in Atlanta Georgia which is very known to be a magnet for athletes & rappers; in addition, drake has also partied in this club.\n\nhttps://media.gettyimages.com/photos/rapper-future-chubbie-baby-and-drake-attend-futures-future-city-at-picture-id1070135024?s=612x612\n\nAnd the most infamous moments for magic city, is the lemon pepper lou’ Williams. Which is being compared to how packed the Sierra canyon parking lot looks like for their games.\n\nhttps://media.gettyimages.com/photos/general-view-of-at-magic-city-on-april-22-2013-in-atlanta-georgia-picture-id167271095?s=612x612", "annotation_id": "23876762", "url": "https://genius.com/23876762", "state": "pending", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "YM forever", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "‘YM’ stands for ‘Young Money’ which is the name of the record label founded by rapper Lil Wayne. The label also includes Drake and Nicki Minaj.", "annotation_id": "23876182", "url": "https://genius.com/23876182", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "I’m in New York goin’ Jon Chetrit", "vault_lines": ["I'm in New York goin' Jon Chetrit"], "meaning": "Jonathan Chetrit is the son of Joseph Chetrit, an American real estate investor and founder of the Chetrit Group who is known for purchasing a number of high profile properties, particularly in New York.\n\n\n\nJonathan Chetrit", "annotation_id": "23876129", "url": "https://genius.com/23876129", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "I was a child trying to be a man", "vault_lines": ["I was a child trying to be a man"], "meaning": "Despite the fact of this just being a sample, I’d like to point out that this is possibly a reference to “March 14” from the five times platinum album, Scorpion:\n\nI’m changing from boy to a man", "annotation_id": "23875323", "url": "https://genius.com/23875323", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "Supermodels and they all sexy Lock the door to the bathroom ‘cause they doin’ something that is not Pepsi", "vault_lines": ["Supermodels and they all sexy", "Lock the door to the bathroom 'cause they doin' something that is not Pepsi"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake alludes to women in the bathroom snorting cocaine. The common slang term for cocaine, coke, bears resemblance to the soft drink Coca-Cola, a major rival of Pepsi. Cocaine is often taken in the bathroom in order to be discreet, as not all social situations and venues are amenable to the behavior.\n\nhttps://th.bing.com/th/id/OIP.YdjWRjBYXRbKB2jZCmnwUQHaFc?w=245&h=180&c=7&r=0&o=5&pid=1.7\n\nCocaine is a highly addictive psychoactive drug, often inhaled nasally in powdered form. Use of the substance has many potential dangerous side effects, both physical and mental.", "annotation_id": "23874412", "url": "https://genius.com/23874412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Papi’s Home", "line": "Used to wanna throw the Roc up, niggas know how I rock now", "vault_lines": ["Used to wanna throw the Roc up, niggas know how I rock now"], "meaning": "Hov and Drizzy have a long history with each other. From collaboratively working together as far back on Jay-Z’s, The Blueprint 3, to subtly exchanging praises and jabs with each other over the years on different tracks.\n\nAt one point, Jay-Z’s record label, Roc-A-Fella, was the label to be signed to. Having signed notable acts over the years such as Kanye West, Cam'ron, Juelz Santana, The Diplomats and many more. It’s no surprise to hear Aubrey discuss how he used to want to throw the rock up, a sign commonly thrown up by Jay-Z, as it’s the logo to the company he founded, Roc Nation. And this isn’t the first time we’ve heard from Drake that this was his dream label to be signed to. On his track Summer Sixteen, he states:\n\nI used to wanna be on Roc-A-Fella then I turned into Jay\n\nDrake is letting it known that there was a point he wanted to be signed to Roc-A-Fella. But make no mistake, those days are long gone as he’s now releasing music under his own label, OVO. Letting it be known that he is a legend in his own respect and now he’s the person that rising artists hope to be blessed with a co-sign by.", "annotation_id": "23873941", "url": "https://genius.com/23873941", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
