{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "101010", "line": "And my delivery just got me buzzin’ like the pizza man", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "‘Buzz’ is essentially hype around one’s name. Drake’s delivery has brought lots of hype to his name in the hiphop industry, his ability to rap his verses like Lil Wayne and sing hooks like Trey Songz.\n\nDouble entendre as well because a pizza man’s job is to deliver pizza boxes to customers. Once he arrives, he will press the doorbell which then makes a buzzing nose, letting the customer know that he’s come.", "annotation_id": "36159841", "url": "https://genius.com/36159841", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "101010", "line": "The game needs change and I’m the mofucking cashier Nickels for my thoughts, dimes in my bed Quarters of the kush, shape the lines in my head", "vault_lines": ["The game needs change and I'm the mofucking cashier", "Nickels for my thoughts, dimes in my bed"], "meaning": "Drake uses a lot of ‘coin’ wordplay in this section of the verse. First, he proclaims that the rap industry needs change. Drake compares himself to a cashier, who’ll give you change if you give him more money than how much the goods you’re purchasing cost.\n\nWhile a thought is often to be said to be ‘two cents’, Drake says his are a ‘nickel’- five whole cents! Double that and you get a dime, which means 10 cents and Drake has plenty of 10/10 women in his bed.\n\nDrake then chops his weed up into fourths, ‘quarters’ (25 cents). He shapes these lines in his head, which is also a double entendre with the second meaning being that he freestyles the lyrics in his songs!", "annotation_id": "36159790", "url": "https://genius.com/36159790", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
