{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "line": "We stayed up, Christmas lights in the middle of summer", "vault_lines": ["We stayed up, Christmas lights in the middle of summer"], "meaning": "Drake uses a play on words with staying up referring to Birdman’s, Lil Wayne’s and his own status in the rap game as well as referring to Christmas lights physically staying up", "annotation_id": "40792946", "url": "https://genius.com/40792946", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "line": "That manual Ferrari Italia’s some fly shit", "vault_lines": ["That manual Ferrari Italia's some fly shit"], "meaning": "Referring to the Ferrari 458 Italia.", "annotation_id": "37096010", "url": "https://genius.com/37096010", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "line": "And they tryna bring us down, me, Weezy, and Stunna", "vault_lines": ["And they tryna bring us down, me, Weezy, and Stunna"], "meaning": "Drake name drops Cash Money labelmate Lil Wayne, also known as Weezy and Cash Money co-founder Birdman, also known as Stunna.\n\nThis bar might have been the beginning of Drake’s feud with Pusha T. The dispute between Clipse and Cash Money started when Clipse dissed Wayne for wearing BAPE clothing on their October 2006 track, “Mr. Me Too”:\n\nNiggas bite the style from the shoes to the watches\n\nDrake eventually joined in with some subliminal disses, supporting his mentor Wayne in the feud, with the two lines above. After this song was dropped, Pusha T released “Don’t Fuck With Me,” a diss track over this very beat:\n\nRappers on their sophomores, actin' like they boss lords\nFame such a funny thing for sure\n\nThis is a clear shot at Drake, fresh off the release of his sophomore studio album, Take Care.", "annotation_id": "17735603", "url": "https://genius.com/17735603", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "line": "YMCMB These niggas make it so hard to be friendly When I know part of it’s envy", "vault_lines": ["These niggas make it so hard to be friendly", "When I know part of it's envy"], "meaning": "YMCMB is an acronym for Young Money Cash Money Billionaires, a portmanteau for the two record labels, Young Money Entertainment and Cash Money Records. Young Money began as a subsidiary of Cash Money, but is now owned entirely by its founder, Lil Wayne, though both labels remain part of Republic Records. Young Money’s emancipation from Cash Money occurred after a public feud between Lil Wayne and Cash Money co-founder Birdman over payment discrepancies.\n\nThe Young Money/Cash Money roster includes heavy hitters like Weezy, Nicki Minaj, Young Thug and more.\n\n\n\nDrizzy signed a joint venture with Young Money and Cash Money in 2009, and despite hinting that he has completed his contractual obligations on 2018’s “Is There More,” as of this song’s release, it is unclear whether he remains on the label.", "annotation_id": "17728145", "url": "https://genius.com/17728145", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "line": "Don’t fuck with me, don’t fuck with me Don’t fuck with me, don’t fuck with me Don’t fuck with me, don’t fuck with me (Don’t)", "vault_lines": ["Don't fuck with me, don't fuck with me"], "meaning": "The chorus samples Jai Paul’s April 2011 track, “BTSTU”. The song was the first to ever surface on the internet from the mysterious UK singer and producer and it has been sampled throughout the industry. For example, on Beyoncé’s April 2012 track, “End of Time.”", "annotation_id": "207494", "url": "https://genius.com/207494", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "line": "Everybody yelled ”Surprise!”, I wasn’t surprised", "vault_lines": ["Everybody yelled \"Surprise!\", I wasn't surprised"], "meaning": "Drake quoted this line in a May 2011 tweet. Like Granny at her “surprise” birthday party, Drake knew his success was coming before it happened.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/#!/drakkardnoir/status/71545660821864448", "annotation_id": "207117", "url": "https://genius.com/207117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "line": "And I feel like lately, it went from top five to remaining five My favorite rappers either lost it or they ain’t alive", "vault_lines": ["And I feel like lately, it went from top five to remaining five", "My favorite rappers either lost it or they ain't alive"], "meaning": "When this track released, Drake was a big fan of the Clipse. However, Pusha T took offense to this line and in September 2011, he dissed Drizzy on this same beat.", "annotation_id": "207053", "url": "https://genius.com/207053", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "line": "Food from India, she in Charlotte Olympias", "vault_lines": ["Food from India, she in Charlotte Olympias"], "meaning": "Charlotte Olympia is a high-end British shoe brand founded in 2008. Their shoes can cost upwards of $1000.\n\n\n\nThe Silva 85, which retails for $1195", "annotation_id": "206955", "url": "https://genius.com/206955", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "line": "I want Saudi money, I want art money", "vault_lines": ["I want Saudi money, I want art money"], "meaning": "The House of Saud is the royal ruling family of Saudi Arabia. Its net worth is estimated in the trillions.\n\nSoon after the release of this track, Drake purchased a print of characters from the 1963 children’s story, Where the Wild Things Are.\n\n\n\nThe most expensive piece of art is Leonardo da Vinci’s Salvator Mundi, which sold for $450 million.", "annotation_id": "206884", "url": "https://genius.com/206884", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Dreams Money Can Buy", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 9, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-dreams-money-can-buy-lyrics", "genius_title": "Dreams Money Can Buy"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "[Verse 1]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The first verse samples “Sing” from the variety show The TERREL Show, Where the words “When we make a joyful song, I know where I belong” are looped, sped- and pitched-up:  https://youtu.be/y_una87dVxg?t=158", "annotation_id": "34680821", "url": "https://genius.com/34680821", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Them Nike tights is huggin’ on that ass like they missed it", "vault_lines": ["Them Nike tights is huggin' on that ass like they missed it"], "meaning": "Here Drake references Nike brand tights conveying his admiration for the way the tights fit.  “huggin’ on that ass like they missed it” ties with the name of the song, “Gimme a Hug”.", "annotation_id": "34590266", "url": "https://genius.com/34590266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Guilt trips, not Turks trips when it’s cold out here in February", "vault_lines": ["Guilt trips, not Turks trips when it's cold out here in February"], "meaning": "Guilt tripping someone means trying to make them feel unreasonably guilty for something they didn’t do or that wasn’t as intense as the shame they get after. The Turks and Caicos islands are a popular luxury vacation spot in Central America, known for their fancy resorts and gorgeous beaches.\n\n\n\nThis line could be continuing from the previous line, implying that Kendrick Lamar is handing out guilt trips and bringing the mood down while Drake is handing out good times. It may also tie into the line after it, as “trip” is often used to refer to the period of time someone spends on a psychedelic drug like MDMA/molly.", "annotation_id": "34569761", "url": "https://genius.com/34569761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Walk in the strip club, damn, I missed you hoes, give me a hug Give me a hug, give me a hug, give me a hug, give me a hug", "vault_lines": ["Walk in the strip club, damn, I missed you hoes, give me a hug", "Give me a hug, give me a hug, give me a hug, give me a hug", "Yeah, give me a hug", "I miss you hoes, give me a hug", "Give me a hug, give me a hug", "You, you, you, you, you"], "meaning": "Drake paints a comical picture of his return to one of his favorite spots—strip clubs, specifically Houston’s Area 29—following a hiatus amid his beef with Kendrick Lamar and others.\n\nHe frames the club as more than just a place of indulgence; for Drake, it’s a homecoming where he is treated like royalty, even if it bends workplace rules. He then name-drops of specific dancers—likely strippers he knows personally—adding authenticity to the whole narrative.\n\nBeyond the literal setting, this could also serve as a metaphor for his current musical approach, signaling a return to his signature club-friendly, pop-leaning sound, a sound that is pretty palpable on this very album. Drake has long been known for his love of strip clubs, where he frequently spends lavishly.\n\n\n\nDrake pulled up to an Atlanta strip club with $250k in a large bin", "annotation_id": "34572360", "url": "https://genius.com/34572360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Fuck that, make the beat switch, turn the hoes up, give a million to ’em", "vault_lines": ["Fuck that, make the beat switch, turn the hoes up, give a million to 'em"], "meaning": "On cue for a beat switch, Drake is calling for a change in vibe, similar to the one he created when he left around $1 million on the floor of Area 29, a strip club in Houston, where he definitely made the dancers go wild that night.\n\n\n\nDrake’s pile of cash after his private party at the Area 29 club", "annotation_id": "34572179", "url": "https://genius.com/34572179", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "’Cause right now, shorty parachuting molly like she flying for the military", "vault_lines": ["'Cause right now, shorty parachuting molly like she flying for the military"], "meaning": "“Parachuting”, AKA bombing, is a slang term for a method of drug ingestion where a pill is crushed, wrapped in an edible tissue, and swallowed, allowing for a more concentrated release of the drug into the system once the tissue dissolves.\n\nDrake also plays on the military reference, as “parachuting” relates to soldiers deploying from planes.", "annotation_id": "34569614", "url": "https://genius.com/34569614", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Right now, I got so many villas booked, I’m like a villain to ’em", "vault_lines": ["Right now, I got so many villas booked, I'm like a villain to 'em"], "meaning": "Here, Drake uses wordplay to show off his wealth. He spends so much time in rented villas, it’s like he lives there. Like someone who lives in Canada is called a Canadian, someone who lives in a villa might be called a villain.\n\nThis can link with the last line about blowing money in strip clubs so Drake can further show off how much money he has. He has the financial means to reserve many luxurious homes and properties, and implies that he is so accustomed to this lifestyle that it feels almost routine. As if he’s simply charging these expenses to his account without a second thought.\n\n\n\nDrake’s $100M mansion in Toronto, CA", "annotation_id": "34568708", "url": "https://genius.com/34568708", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "What’s up? Niggas is hating the boy for sure, the women in love", "vault_lines": ["Niggas is hating the boy for sure, the women in love"], "meaning": "The lines highlight a divide between haters and fans. Drake’s male detractors, with whom he has had some of his famous public feuds, include Kendrick Lamar, Kanye West, Chris Brown, and Meek Mill.\n\nSince his rise to fame, Drake has been romantically linked to numerous high-profile female stars such as Nicki Minaj, Rihanna, SZA, and Jennifer Lopez. This is indicative of the appreciation that women have had towards the rapper.\n\nAs Drake gets ready to leave grievances behind, he can’t help but notice the men are advocating against him. However, the continued attacks from his male counterparts don’t seem to distract the Canadian rapper from acknowledging he still has the female vote. Women continue to support and show their affection to him even in nightclubs and entertainment venues.\n\nDrake is back to receive love in “GIMME A HUG,” and the lyrics contemplate his desire to move on from the conflict with Lamar.\n\nDrake smiling alongside his female fans", "annotation_id": "34565481", "url": "https://genius.com/34565481", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "This girl face so pretty, I can only think of fuckin’ missionary", "vault_lines": ["This girl face so pretty, I can only think of fuckin' missionary"], "meaning": "Missionary is a sex position where the man faces the woman, allowing him to see her face. Drake emphasizes that the woman’s face is so attractive that he can only imagine being intimate with her in this position.", "annotation_id": "34564093", "url": "https://genius.com/34564093", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "[Verse 3]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is echoing the vocals from Aaron Hall’s 1993 hit, “I Miss You,” which is sampled throughout both the third verse and outro.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkEATnHMYIs", "annotation_id": "34563807", "url": "https://genius.com/34563807", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Have the girls up at 29 on stage twerkin’ with a dictionary?", "vault_lines": ["Have the girls up at 29 on stage twerkin' with a dictionary?"], "meaning": "This is in essence a joke about the way Kendrick Lamar’s complex lyricism might not fit the typical club or strip club scene.\n\nDrake is implying that if he was no longer around, Kendrick’s lyric-heavy, conscious rap would be a difficult fit for mainstream party settings where the club-goers (like the dancers at Area 29) would need a dictionary to keep up with Kendrick’s dense wordplay. Area 29 is a popular strip club in Houston that Drake frequents often.", "annotation_id": "34562846", "url": "https://genius.com/34562846", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Funny how it’s only bitch niggas that are waiting on the boy’s obituary ’Cause if I die, it’s these niggas that become the sole beneficiary And what the fuck are they gon’ do with it?", "vault_lines": ["Funny how it's only bitch niggas that are waiting on the boy's obituary", "'Cause if I die, it's these niggas that become the sole beneficiary", "And what the fuck are they gon' do with it?"], "meaning": "Partly throwing shade at his adversaries who are eager to see his downfall, Drake implies that they’re only waiting for his “obituary” (his career’s end) because they hope to take his place. This is most likely referring to Kendrick Lamar who instigated a vicious beef with Drake, fighting for the title of the top guy in hip-hop. However, Drake questions their ability to truly fill his shoes, suggesting that even if he were gone, they wouldn’t know what to do with the throne.", "annotation_id": "34562384", "url": "https://genius.com/34562384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Bulletproof doors so heavy, got me rollin’ ’round like a dignitary", "vault_lines": ["Bulletproof doors so heavy, got me rollin' 'round like a dignitary"], "meaning": "Dignitary is another word for someone in a high ranking office, like a president or an ambassador. Any vehicle they ride in has bulletproof doors and windows to protect against assassination attempts. These bulletproof doors being “heavy” could be a reference to the responsibility Drake feels to his fans or the weight of having people coming at him.\n\nThroughout 2024, several rappers put out disses on Drake, figuratively taking shots at him. While this was happening, one of his bodyguards really was shot, though there’s never been any confirmation that it had anything to do with the feuds.", "annotation_id": "34562358", "url": "https://genius.com/34562358", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Only reason I ain’t got a girl and I ain’t gettin’ married ’Cause I’m really out here, filling up the itinerary ’til I’m six feet in a cemetery", "vault_lines": ["Only reason I ain't got a girl and I ain't gettin' marriеd"], "meaning": "Drake expresses his reluctance to pursue emotional commitments, seeing them as distractions that could delay his destiny to greatness – which he’s committed to until his death.\n\nIn an interview with The Really Good Podcast with Bobbi Althoff, When asked why he hasn’t been married, Drake said:\n\nI don’t know. It seems like a thing of, like, ancient times or something. I think I will eventually. … I don’t know, I don’t think I can offer somebody what they’d be looking for. Just consistency. I think my life, my work is my priority\n                                                                                     https://youtu.be/-MlDq-BgY7s?si=8ajGQYJ2PodCWh4S&t=1513", "annotation_id": "34562315", "url": "https://genius.com/34562315", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Damn They be droppin’ shit, but we be droppin’ harder shit (Droppin’ harder shit)", "vault_lines": ["They be droppin' shit, but we be droppin' harder shit (Droppin' harder shit)"], "meaning": "This could be a sneak diss to Kendrick Lamar. DAMN. is the name of Kendrick’s 4th studio album, released in 2017.\n\nOn November 22, 2024, Kendrick GNX, in which he referenced his feud with Drake multiple times. He also performed his hit diss track, “Not Like Us” during the Super Bowl LIX Halftime Show along with short segments with completely new lyrics.\n\nDrake claims that he doesn’t care about Kendrick releasing new material, because his music hits harder. This connection is also reinforced by the fact that $ome $exy $ongs 4 U was released less than three months after GNX.", "annotation_id": "34561553", "url": "https://genius.com/34561553", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "I appreciate the fans rocking with me, this is really just a small token Really fuckin’ with a visionary", "vault_lines": ["Really fuckin' with a visionary", "I appreciate the fans rocking with me, this is really just a small token"], "meaning": "Despite going through many rap beefs and controversies, Drake has maintained a cult-like fan base. Here, he expresses his appreciation for his die-hard supporters, referring to this album release as merely a small token of gratitude. By calling himself a visionary—someone with great foresight and the ability to shape the future—Drake could be hinting at much more to come, possibly future releases to further reward his loyal fanbase.", "annotation_id": "34561540", "url": "https://genius.com/34561540", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Thousand niggas rappin’ murder shit and like ten niggas that’ll really do it", "vault_lines": ["Thousand niggas rappin' murder shit and like ten niggas that'll really do it"], "meaning": "Here, Drake shifts the focus to calling out rappers who constantly rap about murder but don’t actually live that life, claiming that out of a thousand who talk about it, only about ten are really about it. This could be a broad critique of the rap industry’s obsession with violence, but given the context of his recent feud with Kendrick Lamar, it might also be a direct shot at him. During their lyrical sparing, Kendrick portrayed himself as ruthless, but Drake is implying that Kendrick is all talk.\n\nAdditionally, the line gains further weight when considering that Lil Durk—who was mentioned in the previous lyric—is currently incarcerated pending trial for murder-for-hire.", "annotation_id": "34561529", "url": "https://genius.com/34561529", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Durk’s boy told me “Marvin’s Room” was his favorite song, he was drilling to it", "vault_lines": ["Durk's boy told me \"Marvin's Room\" was his favorite song, he was drilling to it"], "meaning": "The lead single for Take Care (2011), “Marvin’s Room,” is an introspective track that sees Drake, drunk, venting his feelings of loneliness and regret over the phone to an ex-girlfriend.\n\nDrake claims that one of Lil Durk’s associates listens to “Marvin’s Room” while “drilling,” a slang term for carrying out shootings or other violent acts, commonly associated with drill music culture. The irony lies in the fact that “Marvin’s Room” is far from being a typical “drill” music—given the song’s soft, vulnerable nature, it shouldn’t really classify as being a drive-by music.\n\nThe mention of Lil Durk is significant because Durk himself is a well-known Chicago drill rapper, who has collaborated with Drake multiple times, including on songs like “Laugh Now Cry Later” and “In the Bible.”", "annotation_id": "34561462", "url": "https://genius.com/34561462", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Take a minute, take a deep breath, have a little bit of patience ”Drizzy, you amazin’, you the inspiration You set the bar for the next generation”", "vault_lines": ["Take a minute, take a deep breath, have a little bit of patience", "\"Drizzy, you amazin', you the inspiration", "You set the bar for the next generation\"", "You, you, you, you, you"], "meaning": "Drake is hailed as one of the most decorated and best-selling artists in music history. Beyond commercial success, his influence on hip-hop and pop culture is undeniable. Many up-and-coming artists cite him as their inspiration, seeing him as a benchmark for success.\n\n\n\nDrake accepting Billboard’s Artist of the Decade award at 2021 Billboard Music Awards\n\n\nThese bars come off as Drake speaking to himself in a self-reassuring manner, likely reflecting his mindset in the aftermath of the Kendrick beef.", "annotation_id": "34561441", "url": "https://genius.com/34561441", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Melyssa Ford, you a legend from the 6ix, hate to see you with a dick-sucker", "vault_lines": ["Melyssa Ford, you a legend from the 6ix, hate to see you with a dick-sucker", "You, you, you, you, you"], "meaning": "Melyssa Ford is a former video vixen and a co-host of The Joe Budden Podcast. Her and Drake were romantically linked years ago.\n\n\n\nDrake is calling Joe Budden a “dick-sucker” due to their long-standing history of beef. When Budden was an active rapper, he had a feud with Drake and since retiring and becoming a podcaster, Budden has been one of Drake’s most vocal critics.\n\nJust a couple of days before the release of this album, Drake took to his Instagram to troll Joe Budden by posting an unflattering video of him, mocking his physique. In response, Budden dismissed the jab, saying, “I’m not doing a back-and-forth with a corpse.” Apparently, this remark didn’t sit well with Drake, as he fired back on the album with another shot at Budden.\n\nIt so happens that the guys at The Joe Budden Podcast reacted to this bar on their show. When discussing the track, Melyssa Ford admitted she was still “processing” the mention and shared her thoughts: “I mean, it’s great. It’s awesome. I mean, it’s not the first time, though. It’s not even the second, it’s not even the third. But thank you. I don’t know what he is trying to do, I am not in that man’s mind, I do not know. I will let you guys opine on that.” The other co-hosts joked that Drake was strategically trying to “divide and conquer” by putting Ford on a pedestal while taking shots at her co-host. Joe Budden responded simply: “I was happy [with] that. I was happy.”", "annotation_id": "34561310", "url": "https://genius.com/34561310", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Shit about to be a lit summer, tell Tony leave the motor runnin’", "vault_lines": ["Shit about to be a lit summer, tell Tony leave the motor runnin'"], "meaning": "Tony Bet is Drake’s car dealer who has supplied him with many exotic and custom cars. This line implies that Drake’s about to get a new car, or maybe a few, to have fun with and drive around this summer.\n\n\n\nDrake’s custom 900HP Braubus Crawler, supplied by Tony Bet", "annotation_id": "34561259", "url": "https://genius.com/34561259", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Using you for promotion, truer words had never been spoken", "vault_lines": ["You, you, you, you, you", "Using you for promotion, truer words had never been spoken"], "meaning": "Drake is saying other rappers used him or his name for to get themselves attention. Because of “Not Like Us”, a track released during their 2024 beef, Kendrick Lamar received 5 Grammys and broke Spotify records which includes highest monthly listeners by a rapper. Before their feud, the two had done some shows together when Drake was a more established artist. Another rapper that dissed Drake in 2024, A$AP Rocky, also did some shows with Drake when Rocky was a smaller artist.\n\nWe Don’t Trust You by Future and Metro Boomin, which featured a Drake diss on “Like That”, topped the Billboard 200 and several of its singles also made it onto the Billboard Hot 100. Before this, Drake had worked with Future and Metro.\n\nWith this line, Drake suggests that none of these other rappers ever actually respected him or wanted to work with him. In his mind, they only ever wanted the press that comes with being associated with him, instead of just having a change of heart somewhere down the line.\n\nDrake has already touched on this exact point, particularly calling out Kendrick on “Family Matters”:\n\nOut here beggin' for attention, nigga, say please\n\nand on “THE HEART PT.6”:\n\nAlbum droppin' soon, no wonder you turn to a clout chaser ‘stead of doin’ hard labor", "annotation_id": "34561126", "url": "https://genius.com/34561126", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Niggas want to see RIP me on a t-shirt like I’m Hulk Hogan", "vault_lines": ["Niggas want to see RIP me on a t-shirt like I'm Hulk Hogan"], "meaning": "In this double entendre, Drake points out that people wish for his downfall, hoping to see him commemorated like a fallen celebrity with his face and “RIP”, short for rest in peace, on a t-shirt. Hulk Hogan is famous wrestler known for ripping his shirt off before matches.\n\n\n\nDuring the debut of WWE on Netflix in January 2025, Hulk Hogan was booed offstage during his speech after decades of receiving mostly cheers from the WWE live audiences. Drake could be relating himself to Hulk with the way that he spent many years being cheered on by fans, only to feel jeered and mocked during his rap beef with Kendrick Lamar.\n\nDrake could also be hitting at his enemies not only hating him themselves, but trying to get a mob of non-rappers or even his own fans to turn on him publicly. In the year before the release of $ome $exy $ongs 4 U, he got involved in an intense beef with Kendrick Lamar. On top of Kendrick, some other rappers and even the producer Metro Boomin got involved. Metro specifically released a free-to-use beat for anyone to put their own disses over. Not only were artists getting their hands dirty, but some were trying to make a massive show of other people doing the same and taking shots at Drake, similar to if they had matching T-shirts with “RIP Drake” on them.\n\nIt so happens that ‘RIP Drake 1986-2024’ shirts surfaced following Drake’s beef with Kendrick.", "annotation_id": "34561100", "url": "https://genius.com/34561100", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Tell Mike that the ceiling’s too low in Booby, how we supposed to throw a hundred?", "vault_lines": ["Tell Mike that the ceiling's too low in Booby, how we supposed to throw a hundred?"], "meaning": "Michael Marhefka, is the Director of Security at Booby Trap, a Miami strip club. Drake mentioned the club before on “Drew A Picasso”:\n\nIn Booby Trap, throwin' more bills than your baby daddy trap\n\nBoth lines mention throwing money, which is common in strip clubs. Saying that the ceiling is too low here could literally mean that the bills Drake throws hit the ceiling when he doesn’t mean to because it isn’t high enough. It could also mean that he’s going to “raise the roof” or get a party going with his music, because the energy is too low for him.", "annotation_id": "34561033", "url": "https://genius.com/34561033", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Fuck a rap beef, I’m tryna get the party lit Tryna get the party lit for the bitches", "vault_lines": ["Fuck a rap beef, I'm tryna get the party lit", "Tryna get the party lit for the bitches"], "meaning": "Drake’s shift to party music signals a desire to move past the 2024 rap beef he participated in with Kendrick Lamar and instead embrace a more carefree vibe. He is now mostly focused on having fun and connecting with women through upbeat tracks instead of male drama. Ironically, this line seems to echo something Kendrick said about him in “euphoria”:\n\nI like Drake with the melodies, I don’t like Drake when he act tough\n\nThis line connects to the feud in another way. It sounds incredibly similar to lyrics from an earlier Kendrick diss, “meet the grahams”, which says:\n\nFuck a rap battle, this a long life battle with yourself\n\nThis could be Drake’s attempt to take the power away from that line. The battle with himself turns into a party, instead of any internal struggle Kendrick alleged, he’s just turning up and having fun.\n\n“Tryna get the party lit” is also a contrast to the title of Kendrick’s track “The Day The Party Died,” which is Kendrick’s first release after their widely publicized beef.", "annotation_id": "34561028", "url": "https://genius.com/34561028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Savage, you the only nigga checkin’ on me when we really in some shit, brother", "vault_lines": ["Savage, you the only nigga checkin' on me when we really in some shit, brother", "You, you, you, you, you"], "meaning": "Throughout the recent years it seems that 21 Savage is a close friend of Drake’s and has stuck by his side through a lot of things. Especially after their 2022 album Her Loss and songs like “Jimmy Cooks”.\n\n\n\nThis line may also be another way for Drake to shoot back at rappers he’s had beef with before the release of $ome $exy $ongs 4 U. When Kendrick Lamar started taking public shots at Drake, several other artists that he’d worked with before like A$AP Rocky and Metro Boomin also started putting out disses in one form or another. Instead of  turning on him, 21 Savage never put out anything negative about Drake, even though he was mentioned on Kendrick’s “Not Like Us”.", "annotation_id": "34560787", "url": "https://genius.com/34560787", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "You Neo in the matrix, these niggas just Nemo in the ocean Small fish, making kids feel emotion", "vault_lines": ["You Neo in the matrix, these niggas just Nemo in the ocean", "You, you, you, you, you", "Small fish, making kids feel emotion"], "meaning": "Drake pulls out two movie references here, comparing himself to the character Neo in The Matrix franchise and his competition to Nemo in 2003’s Finding Nemo.\n\nThroughout the track Drake brags about escaping the death / “elimination” that his rap competition sought out. This resembles the iconic Matrix scene where Neo bends backward in slow motion to evade incoming bullets, showcasing his superhuman ability to evade death. Drake made a similar statement a few weeks before this track’s release, when he wore a bullet hole filled hoodie to a show, showcasing that he was shot and still standing.\n\n\n\n\n\nIn comparison, Nemo is an animated clownfish lacking a sense of direction. In Finding Nemo, he gets taken by a human diver, making his father and another fish he meets have to come rescue him. Drake has name dropped the character previously on 2009’s “Forever”:\n\nSwimmin' in the money, come and find me, Nemo\n\nBeing a Disney movie, Finding Nemo has a lot of child fans and it’s heartfelt story would have an emotional impact on them. In the context of other rappers, Drake’s implying that they don’t make real meaningful music that impacts adults. Instead, they just write shallow or inflammatory things that would only really affect kids—basically shadowing their fanbases for being immature. Them being “small fish” also refers to how little effect Drake feels that they have in the rap game, as opposed to the “big fish”, himself and his collaborators. Additionally, Neo from The Matrix is  also known as ‘The One,’ so Drake is, in a way, positioning himself as the real deal.\n\nThe phrase “small fish” may also be a reference to Kendrick Lamar specifically. Throughout their beef, Drake has made several jokes about the other rapper’s height, somewhere around 5’ 5”. One of these jokes can be seen in “Family Matters” where he raps:\n\nYour man a lil' K, we call that shit a mini Drac'\nHe always said I overlooked him, I was starin' straight\nThese bars go over Kenny head no matter what I say", "annotation_id": "34560784", "url": "https://genius.com/34560784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "line": "Yeah, Drake elimination, fake intimidation", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, Drake elimination, fake intimidation"], "meaning": "“Drake elimination” references Kanye West’s comments about wanting to see Drake “eliminated” from his position as the guy at top of the music business. In an interview with The Download podcast, Kanye said that he and his collaborators were ecstatic about the idea of stripping Drake of his spot. This statement came in the context of his involvement in “LIKE THAT REMIX,” a diss track aimed at Drake.\n\nhttps://www.facebook.com/watch/?v=941734634163387", "annotation_id": "34560730", "url": "https://genius.com/34560730", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GIMME A HUG", "status": "done", "targets": 28, "clicked": 28, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-gimme-a-hug-lyrics", "genius_title": "GIMME A HUG"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going in for Life", "line": "If Hov is Jordan, I guess I’m cool with Pippen ’Til I mention that I wanna play a new position", "vault_lines": ["If Hov is Jordan, I guess I'm cool with Pippen", "'Til I mention that I wanna play a new position"], "meaning": "At the time of this song’s release in 2007, Jay-Z (also known as Hov) was undoubtedly one of the biggest rappers and moguls in the hip-hop scene. Kingdom Come, Hov’s November 2006 comeback album following 2003’s smash hit The Black Album, was a commercial success. It debuted at number one on the US Billboard 200, selling 680,000 copies in its first week and earning him a nomination for Best Rap Album at the 50th Annual Grammy Awards.\n\nBuilding on such accomplishments, Drake compares Hov to Michael Jordan, widely regarded as the greatest basketball player of all time. Similarly, Scottie Pippen is considered one of the greatest small forwards in basketball history. Pippen played a crucial role in transforming the Chicago Bulls into a championship dynasty and popularizing the NBA worldwide during the 1990s. While Pippen never managed to surpass Jordan’s on-court or off-court success and popularity, Drake expresses contentment with being second to Hov, accepting the role of the “Scottie Pippen” to Jay-Z’s “Michael Jordan” in the rap game.\n\nHowever, as he predicted in these bars, Drake ultimately eclipsed Jay-Z in popularity. Following the release of his third mixtape So Far Gone in February 2009, Drake dominated the music industry in the late 2000s and well into the 2010s, solidifying his place as one of the greatest artists of his era.\n\nNotably, less than three years after the release of this track, Drake collaborated with his Jay-Z on the 2010 song “Light Up” off his debut studio album, Thank Me Later.\n\nCoincidentally, both Drake and Hov were embroiled in scandals for their courting of young women.", "annotation_id": "33914205", "url": "https://genius.com/33914205", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going in for Life", "line": "No team-playing, no screen-setting “Because I wanna win games, Coach, I’m through assisting”", "vault_lines": ["No team-playing, no screen-setting", "\"Because I wanna win games, Coach, I'm through assisting\""], "meaning": "He wants to be the main guy on the team. Play so that everything goes thru him. Where he doesn’t set screens or pass up a shot to anyone else.\n\nAlso during the 1994 playoffs Pippen famously sat out the last 1.8 seconds of a game, because he wouldn’t get the last shot. So it nicely ties it together.", "annotation_id": "31463074", "url": "https://genius.com/31463074", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going in for Life", "line": "The takeover, the break’s over, nigga", "vault_lines": ["The takeover, the break's over, nigga"], "meaning": "He’s saying that he’s about to turn up, takeover and be the GOAT. Which is dope because he just mentioned being second to Jay-Z (Hov). This line is also a reference to the first line in the first verse of the song “Takeover” by Jay-Z", "annotation_id": "31056164", "url": "https://genius.com/31056164", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going in for Life", "line": "I got a Jewish lawyer as my lead defender A Menschkeit, as they say; he’s a legal bender", "vault_lines": ["I got a Jewish lawyer as my lead defender", "A Menschkeit, as they say; he’s a legal bender"], "meaning": "b", "annotation_id": "27768973", "url": "https://genius.com/27768973", "state": "pending", "votes": -5, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going in for Life", "line": "Now, all she wanna do is shop for Christian Dior clothes", "vault_lines": ["Now, all she wanna do is shop for Christian Dior clothes"], "meaning": "Drake turned a good Christian girl bad, now all she wants to do is buy expensive materialistic things. i.e. Christian Dior", "annotation_id": "148919", "url": "https://genius.com/148919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going in for Life", "line": "She used to be a Christian before", "vault_lines": ["She used to be a Christian before"], "meaning": "She was more concerned about becoming spiritually saved and a higher self as oppose to worldly materialistic things", "annotation_id": "103052", "url": "https://genius.com/103052", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going in for Life", "line": "My foot is just solely fit for this shoe it fits in", "vault_lines": ["My foot is just solely fit for this shoe it fits in"], "meaning": "A play on the old adage, “don’t judge someone until you’ve walked a mile in someone else’s shoes.” Drake is saying how he is too busy living his life to worry about what others are doing or saying about him.", "annotation_id": "93814", "url": "https://genius.com/93814", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going in for Life", "line": "Where I am about to be home again like New Edition", "vault_lines": ["Where I am about to be home again like New Edition"], "meaning": "Referencing the New Edition album entitled Home Again. Drake is presumably talking about his pending court date back in Toronto.", "annotation_id": "93813", "url": "https://genius.com/93813", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going in for Life", "status": "done", "targets": 8, "clicked": 8, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-going-in-for-life-lyrics", "genius_title": "Going in for Life"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "Any time people want to start problems It’s like “for real, are you dumb?” You know who mans are", "vault_lines": ["Any time people want to start problems", "It's like “for real, are you dumb?”", "You know who mans are"], "meaning": "Drake has said it before, and now he has someone else say it for him: Don’t mess with him.  The OVO crew is always there to back him up, and Drake is not one for holding back, look what happened to Meek Mill.\n\nYeah, trigger fingers turn to twitter fingers\nYeah, you gettin' bodied by a singin' nigga\n I’m not the type of nigga that’ll type to niggas\n\nAnd physical altercations? This isn’t the kind of person you want to be fighting with.", "annotation_id": "9073168", "url": "https://genius.com/9073168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "With my dad out in Tennessee is where I belong Out here in L.A., I don’t know what’s going on (Yeah) I don’t know what’s going on", "vault_lines": ["With my dad out in Tennessee is where I belong", "Out here in L.A., I don't know what's going on (Yeah)", "I don't know what's going on"], "meaning": "Drake’s got quite a few relatives in Memphis, including his father; he paid tribute to them and the city in the music video for “Worst Behavior,” in which his father actually appears.\n\nDespite living lavishly in L.A., Drake feels out of place in this city of untrustworthy people who, despite being predictable, Drake simply cannot comprehend. He wishes he could be in Memphis with his father, leading a simpler life. Tragically, Drake has explained in the past that he feels incapable of returning to Memphis:\n\nI have a lot of family that I love very deeply [in Memphis]. But if anybody knows anything about the South and how it is, it’s tough to go around people who could use your help and you can’t help everybody. It’s like a guilt or a burden that comes over me, so I hadn’t gone to Memphis in a long time.\n\nFortunately, after shooting the “Worst Behavior” music video, Drake felt more at ease about the city, which means a respite there is not out of the question.", "annotation_id": "9071963", "url": "https://genius.com/9071963", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "You sit and you pray hoping that the stars align My luck is a sure thing ’cause I’m living right When I needed you, you couldn’t give me any advice But you always had something to say every other time", "vault_lines": ["You sit and you pray hoping that the stars align", "My luck is a sure thing 'cause I'm living right", "When I needed you, you couldn't give me any advice", "But you always had something to say every other time"], "meaning": "The alignment of the stars or planets refers to an event that’s out of your hands. The term originated from the fact that planets can’t actually align perfectly. Therefore, someone who prays for the stars to align is leaving their luck to fate. Drizzy, on the other hand, has the resources to accomplish anything he wants to put his mind to.\n\nAdditionally, this person was only interested in Drake every time other than being there for him when he actually needed them. Here’s where we find out that this person wasn’t really Drake’s “friend” and they were waiting for fate to intervene instead of working hard to earn what they were craving for.", "annotation_id": "9071270", "url": "https://genius.com/9071270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "I’m not afraid no gyal heart man And I’m not afraid of no cyattie And I’m not afraid of no waste yute neither So anybody who want it can get it Seh feh", "vault_lines": ["I'm not afraid no gyal heart man", "And I'm not afraid of no cyattie", "And I'm not afraid of no waste yute neither", "So anybody who want it can get it"], "meaning": "Drake’s on the phone with a girl who plays the persona of a tough female.\n\n“Gyal heart man,” as in “girl-hearted man,” is patois for a man who acts like a female. She’s not afraid of a soft man, potentially a “singing nigga” like Drake.\n\n“No cyattie” is also patois and means “no woman”; specifically, any woman who wants to try and get up in her face. She’s not afraid of “no waste yute,” or a youth who’s a waste of time and space.\n\n“Anybody who want it can get it” means to fight someone and “seh feh” is patois for “say something if you dare.”\n\nThe girl’s voice on the outro is 416Jamz. She is on the Gyal Cast Podcast.", "annotation_id": "9071082", "url": "https://genius.com/9071082", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "And it’s all because you chose a side You’re supposed to put your pride aside and ride for me Guess it wasn’t time (Yeah) And of course you went and chose a side that wasn’t mine", "vault_lines": ["And it's all because you chose a side", "You're supposed to put your pride aside and ride for me", "Guess it wasn't time (Yeah)", "And of course you went and chose a side that wasn't mine"], "meaning": "Drake revisits a running theme—questioning the loyalty of his friends, who don’t always stand by his side in times of need. Someone chose sides against him, and he feels betrayed.\n\nTwo possible targets: Nicki Minaj—who’s been a friend and/or a love interest, and Quentin Miller, his former collaborator who has generally tried to stay out of a fight with Drake.\n\nDrake’s beef with Meek Mill in 2015 strained his relationship with Nicki, who was dating Meek at the time. Nicki told MTV:\n\nYou don’t ever want to choose sides between people you love.\n\nBut it seems that bridges were burned anyway. Drake told Zane Lowe:\n\nI don’t really talk to Nicki, and you know, again, another person I have a lot of love for, a lot of love and a lot of respect. Not only for our past and how much work and time we put in, but in even just the way she dealt with the situation at hand.\nI understand what love is, and I understand a personal situation. You know, she dealt with me how I would expect her to, which was with respect…there was points in time when I was sitting here waiting, like “how deep does your love go?”", "annotation_id": "9070919", "url": "https://genius.com/9070919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 124, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "I always saw you for what you could’ve been And even when it’s business with you it’s personal again I knew you before you made ends meet And now we’re meeting our end", "vault_lines": ["I always saw you for what you could've been", "And even when it's business with you it's personal again", "I knew you before you made ends meet", "And now we're meeting our end", "Always saw you for what you could've been"], "meaning": "“It’s not personal — it’s just business,” was reportedly coined by Otto Berman. Its popularity increased when it was featured in a scene from The Godfather II:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bo7zkd0kRS4\n\nDrake met this woman when they first started out and were struggling to survive and get recognized for their craft. As the years went by, they shared history and many memories together.\n\nHowever, now the adage doesn’t hold true for Drake’s relationship with this woman. Their lives are too intertwined for such thing as a professional relationship to exist between them. He has reached a point where he has to say goodbye to her and go their separate ways.", "annotation_id": "9070720", "url": "https://genius.com/9070720", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "How do you not check on me when things go wrong", "vault_lines": ["How do you not check on me when things go wrong"], "meaning": "Drake questions why people are around when things are going well, but always seem to disappear when they go wrong. They’re not around for all the seasons.\n\n2015 was a successful yet controversial year for Drake. Views was pushed back, he had contractual issues with Cash Money, and was involved in an ongoing rap beef between himself and Meek Mill.\n\nThis leaves him wondering why his close “friends” aren’t checking on him to make sure he’s straight.", "annotation_id": "9070696", "url": "https://genius.com/9070696", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "Forgiveness for your ways If I ever loved ya, I’ll always love ya; that’s how I was raised Same way I’m right here still feeling the way Same way I’m realizing on a day to day that", "vault_lines": ["Forgiveness for your ways", "If I ever loved ya, I'll always love ya; that's how I was raised", "Same way I'm right here still feeling the way", "Same way I'm realizing on a day to day that"], "meaning": "Drake distinguishes between the people that he used to love and the ones he never did. From his point of view, it’s nonsense to develop feelings for someone’s personality unless it was meant for a lifetime. For example, if Rihanna and Drake had beef, he would still love her. That might apply to Drake’s relationship to Nicki as well—he already mentioned in “Tuscan Leather”:\n\nNot even talkin' to Nicki, communication is breakin'\nI dropped the ball on some personal shit, I need to embrace it\nI’m honest, I make mistakes, I’d be the second to admit it\nThink that’s why I need her in my life, to check me when I’m trippin'\n\nDrake also mentioned he “wasn’t speaking to Nicki” as much in his Views interview with Zane Lowe.\n\n“feeling a way” means to be in your emotions about something or someone. (i.e. someones actions made you feel a way) Drake has built a career out of “feeling a way” about his romantic history. Drake uses the last line to re-iterate the theme of the song which is his realization that he can’t rely on his exes to actually care about him/hit him up to see how he’s doing.", "annotation_id": "9070639", "url": "https://genius.com/9070639", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "All of my “let’s just be friends” are friends I don’t have anymore", "vault_lines": ["All of my \"let's just be friends\" are friends I don't have anymore"], "meaning": "“Let’s just be friends” is a cliché breakup line. Drizzy points out the irony—when people give each other this line, they rarely follow through and stay friends.\n\nIt seems that Drake’s having relationship troubles—again. You can get out of Drake’s life, but it’s harder to get out of his lyrics.\n\nThis line may be directed at Nicki Minaj. The collaborators were rumoured to have an on-and-off thing together, until Nicki said they were just friends. Now, with the Meek Mill-Drake beef, Nicki and Drake have stopped talking.", "annotation_id": "9070609", "url": "https://genius.com/9070609", "state": "accepted", "votes": 200, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "Guess I should’ve tried to keep my family closer Much closer", "vault_lines": ["Guess I should've tried to keep my family closer"], "meaning": "Drake can’t rely on his exes to be his support system—he wishes he’d trusted in his woes instead—as well as connecting with his actual family.\n\nIn the second verse, Drake says “with my dad out in Tennessee is where I belong.” So “closer” refers to literal distance, as well as emotional connection.\n\nDrake has a strained relationship with his father, who left his mom when he was five. But at this album’s launch, he told Zane Lowe:\n\nMy father is a huge inspiration for me…I’ve grown to just have an appreciation for the man that he is.\n\nDrake is closer with his mother,  whom he called “the most important person in my life.”", "annotation_id": "9070536", "url": "https://genius.com/9070536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "It’s a little chilly out there How you can stand there is beyond me", "vault_lines": ["It's a little chilly out there", "How you can stand there is beyond me"], "meaning": "In his pre-album release interview with Beats 1’s Zane Lowe, Drake said that VIEWS is based around the change of the seasons in Toronto—from winter to summer, back to winter.\n\nAs the opening track, “Keep The Family Close” is based in the dead of winter—hence the woman (voiced by Sabrina Galmo) mentioning the cold.\n\nDrake is shown sitting atop the CN Tower on the album cover, wearing a  winter coat, with the storm approaching—her dialogue fits that moment, asking him how he can stand it out there.\n\nAs Drake told Zane Lowe:\n\nWe also make our winters work, and they’re harsh.\n\nThe first two sound effects are a TTC streetcar, and a TTC subway entering a platform. Both staple sound effects of the Toronto landscape.", "annotation_id": "9070426", "url": "https://genius.com/9070426", "state": "accepted", "votes": 151, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "You’re so predictable I hate people like you Kennedy Road taught me not to trust people like you", "vault_lines": ["You're so predictable I hate people like you", "Kennedy Road taught me not to trust people like you"], "meaning": "Kennedy Road is a street in Scarborough, ON; Drake has given it a shoutout before on “Energy”:\n\nI got real ones livin’ past Kennedy Road\nI got real ones wit' me everywhere that I go\n\nDrake’s got both friends and girls in Scarborough, which also houses Drake’s beloved Habibiz hookah restaurant and cafe. In fact, Drake was so enamored of Habibiz that he included a replica of it on his 27th birthday cake.\n\n\n\nSuffice it to say, Drake’s got ties in Scarborough, which means he’s probably aware of the high levels of crime the district is notorious for. Spending time there may have made him more wary; he’s begun to watch his back in all situations. Or perhaps some of his many friends from Scarborough have only stayed close to him in order to bask in his wealth and glory; Drake therefore finds this person’s behavior “predictable.”", "annotation_id": "9070383", "url": "https://genius.com/9070383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "Always saw you for what you could’ve been Ever since you met me Like when Chrysler made that one car That looked just like the Bentley", "vault_lines": ["Ever since you met me", "Like when Chrysler made that one car", "That looked just like the Bentley", "I always saw you for what you could've been", "Always saw you for what you could've been"], "meaning": "This person had so much potential in Drake’s eyes. He envisioned them as being great and saw what they could have been, but instead they let him down. This brings to mind the lyrics of his song “Madonna,” where Drake sees a girl that could reach the same heights as the Queen of Pop:\n\nYou could be big as Madonna\nJust get in the car and then let’s touch the road\nDon’t make other plans\n\nThis is similar to the Chrysler 300, which looks almost like a Bentley, but falls short. Bentleys are known for being luxurious and high-end vehicles. At first glance, a Chrysler could be mistaken for a Bentley, but once you really take a good look, the differences are obvious.", "annotation_id": "9070255", "url": "https://genius.com/9070255", "state": "accepted", "votes": 170, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep the Family Close", "line": "Everybody that I met on the way tries to get in the way I’m fooding and serving them all Like I’m working minimum wage Someone up there must just love testing my patience Someone up there must be in need of some entertainment", "vault_lines": ["That looked just like the Bentley", "Everybody that I met on the way tries to get in the way", "I'm fooding and serving them all", "Like I'm working minimum wage", "Someone up there must just love testing my patience", "Someone up there must be in need of some entertainment"], "meaning": "Drake turns to his enemies, describing how people get in his pathway towards success. He feels like God is testing his patience.\n\nDespite his tribulations, Drake found a way to succeed. Like a waiter/chef, he’s delivering and feeding the people. Both are average jobs with low wages. Instead of plates and dishes, though, he’s delivering and feeding the world with top-notch music.\n\nIn a span of just 16 months, Drake’s dropped three full projects: the surprise release of If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, What a Time to Be Alive (with Future), and the highly anticipated VIEWS.", "annotation_id": "9070222", "url": "https://genius.com/9070222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
