{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "The police still arrest the homies No statement, no testimony", "vault_lines": ["The police still arrest the homies", "No statement, no testimony"], "meaning": "Here Skepta is airing his grievances with the London Metropolitan police, with the issue also applying to how the police treat African Americans in the USA.\n\nThe view is that black men are being targeted by the police, and when they are brought into the station they plead the fifth and refuse to respond to questioning.\n\nThis relates to a similar theme explored in Skepta’s Crime Riddim, in that police are too eager to reprimand young black men due to racial stereotyping.", "annotation_id": "11765877", "url": "https://genius.com/11765877", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "But it’s greatness only for the love of Britain", "vault_lines": ["But it's greatness only for the love of Britain"], "meaning": "‘Greatness Only’ is the name of a documentary about Skepta that charts his life in music as well as being one of his catchphrases that regularly features on his social media accounts.", "annotation_id": "11720945", "url": "https://genius.com/11720945", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Sound like me, but they ain’t the same (Greaze!) See them online tryna fake the fame", "vault_lines": ["Sound like me, but they ain't the same (Greaze!)", "See them online tryna fake the fame"], "meaning": "Lots of people try to mimic Skepta’s style and flow, but they are not as good as him. He also criticises their online behaviour, which could be an allusion to the line “Block that account, it’s a catfish” in the hook.", "annotation_id": "11720898", "url": "https://genius.com/11720898", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "(Dubplate special)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A dubplate special is a rare, adapted version of a song with tailored lyrics that shout out a particular DJ or organisation for their personal use. They often only exist in the form of a single copy, owned by the DJ they’re addressed to. They could be subtle variations on a hit record or could use a different instrumental and new lyrics entirely.\n\nThe term originated in reggae and dancehall music but has since been applied to grime and drum and bass among other genres.\n\nWhile this song isn’t technically a dubplate—it received a full digital release through a record label—it pays homage to the culture, and shouts out OVO numerous times in the same way that an authentic dubplate would.\n\nSkeppy is no stranger to these; his “That’s Not Me” dubplate made for Virgil Abloh and Benji B in 2014 was eventually released on SoundCloud. Kyla, the guest singer on Drake’s global smash “One Dance”, has also created dubplates of their hit for Red Bull Culture Clash where she’s adapted the lyrics to big up particular soundsystems.\n\nhttps://soundcloud.com/flat_white/skepta-thats-not-me-flat-white-x-benji-b-dubplate", "annotation_id": "11622878", "url": "https://genius.com/11622878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Block that account, it’s a catfish (Yeah) Block that account, it’s a catfish", "vault_lines": ["Block that account, it's a catfish (Yeah)", "Block that account, that's a catfish"], "meaning": "Elizabeth Wood sent me a text the day More Life dropped with a freeze frame of the Genius lyric page and like a million emojis of fire and trophies. If this was all we ever gave to world I would die happy.\n\nIt’s an honor to be part of someone else’s art. The new definition of “catfish” was inducted into Webster’s dictionary a couple years back, but somehow making it onto Drake’s new album feels more significant. Also this guy Skepta who actually uses the word in the song is fire.\n\nIt sucks that there are so many people insecure and lonely enough to catfish, but I’m happy to offer a single word that describes this phenomenon. Otherwise it would take a lot of words.", "annotation_id": "11621053", "url": "https://genius.com/11621053", "state": "verified", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "OVO, man’s so unruly (Bo!)", "vault_lines": ["OVO, man's so unruly (Bo!)"], "meaning": "OVO Unruly is a fusion of Drake’s OVO crew and Jamaican singer-songwriter Popcaan’s Unruly Gang.", "annotation_id": "11548119", "url": "https://genius.com/11548119", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "A young G, I had the weapon on me Slice up work like pepperoni (Shing)", "vault_lines": ["A young G, I had the weapon on me", "Slice up work like pepperoni (Shing)"], "meaning": "Whilst growing up on the streets of London, Skepta was involved in several gang crimes such as carrying fire arms and selling cocaine. He details his adolescent hardships on the song “Straight Up Remix,” where he raps:\n\n2005 when I first shot that 9, I didn’t see where the bullet went, but all I know it made my arm go flyin'\nLooked at Solo, this guy’s shooting and laughing at the same time\nI said Solo fam, I won’t lie\nWe ain’t gonna buss in this ting if 24/7 us man are on crime\n\n“Slice up work” is slang for dividing up drugs to sell; pepperoni is a deli meat which is literally sliced up.", "annotation_id": "11545147", "url": "https://genius.com/11545147", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Just came back from the embassy Check my crime sheet, check my legacy And now I’m back on tour, so what you tellin’ me? (Bo-bo)", "vault_lines": ["Just came back from the embassy", "Check my crime sheet, check my legacy", "And now I'm back on tour, so what you tellin' me? (Bo-bo)"], "meaning": "Skepta was announced as part of the official line-up of the 2016 Coachella Music Festival, but he wasn’t able to attend because the US embassy denied him a working visa. He commented on their refusal in an Instagram post:\n\nLove to everybody that wanted to see me on tour this month, my U.S visa has been denied and I am not allowed in America right now. Still taking forward steps and forever working with God. Brb.\n\nOn March 19, 2017, Skepta announced his “Banned From America Tour,” the title of which was presumably inspired by his clash with the US embassy.", "annotation_id": "11540755", "url": "https://genius.com/11540755", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Yeah, that’s that boy from the magazine", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, that's that boy from the magazine"], "meaning": "Skepta has been featured on the cover of numerous magazines, most notably on FADER’s June/July 2015 issue as well as NME’s December 2015 issue. Skepta uses this bar to accentuate his status within the UK grime scene and more recently the global music scene in general.", "annotation_id": "11539132", "url": "https://genius.com/11539132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "It’s a madness Check my account, it’s a madness (Mm-mm)", "vault_lines": ["Check my account, it's a madness (Mm-mm)", "Check my account, that's a madness"], "meaning": "“Madness” is a UK slang term for something incredible—in this case, Skepta’s wealth. According to Celebrity Net Worth, Skepta’s worth approximately $2 million.\n\nSkepta could also be referring to his Instagram account hitting one million followers, making him the first grime artist to ever reach this milestone, followed only by his fellow grime MC, Stormzy.", "annotation_id": "11539054", "url": "https://genius.com/11539054", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "The most solid gang that you will ever see (Get me)", "vault_lines": ["The most solid gang that you will ever see (Get me)"], "meaning": "Here, Skeppy nods to Boy Better Know, the collective he founded alongside his brother Jme over a decade ago. Its roster boasts grime veterans such as Wiley and Frisco, and Drake himself claimed in 2016 to have become “the first Canadian signed to BBK.” The brand has branched out into selling merchandise and even launching a pay-as-you-go mobile network over the years.\n\nIn making the claim that they’re “the most solid gang”, Skepta implies that they’ve surpassed legendary garage crew So Solid Crew, who are widely regarded to have paved the way for grime as a genre before it existed. BBK have tangibly influenced the scene a huge amount; they were the first to provide heavyweights such as Tinchy Stryder and President T with a platform to release their music to a wider audience, and tracks like Skepta’s “Shutdown” are sure-fire hits in British nightclubs to this date.", "annotation_id": "11534052", "url": "https://genius.com/11534052", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "I might switch it up and direct a movie (Mm)", "vault_lines": ["I might switch it up and direct a movie (Mm)"], "meaning": "In late 2016, Skepta announced he was planning to direct a movie about his collective and record label Boy Better Know, which he co-founded with his brother Jamie Adenuga (Jme).", "annotation_id": "11533850", "url": "https://genius.com/11533850", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "On cloud nine, a man’s extra cozy (Bo! Greaze!)", "vault_lines": ["On cloud nine, a man's extra cozy (Bo! Greaze!)"], "meaning": "“Cloud 9” is a slang term for feelings of extreme happiness or euphoria, as though you’re floating on a cloud.\n\n“Cozy” is a reference to deceased rapper A$AP Yams, since “Cozy Boys” is a movement dedicated to him. Following Yams' death, his name become synonymous with being insanely high, which A$AP Mob acknowledged on their Yams tribute track “Yamborghini High,” the intro to their 2016 posse mixtape Cozy Tapes Vol. 1: Friends.\n\nSkepta previously worked with the mob, including A$AP Nast’s feature on his song “Ladies Hit Squad” and his feature on Rocky’s “Put That On My Set.”", "annotation_id": "11533691", "url": "https://genius.com/11533691", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Don’t phone me, send a text to Julie", "vault_lines": ["Don't phone me, send a text to Julie"], "meaning": "Julie Adenuga is Skepta’s sister and the host of Beats 1 Radio.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/JulieAdenuga/status/810932286149509124", "annotation_id": "11533579", "url": "https://genius.com/11533579", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Front row, jacket tailor-made", "vault_lines": ["Front row, jacket tailor-made"], "meaning": "Skepta was sitting front row for the Chanel show which was part of the Paris Fashion Week for Fall/Winter.\n\n\n\nHe is sitting next to Pharrell Williams who he has collaborated with on “Numbers” from his album Konnichiwa.", "annotation_id": "11533302", "url": "https://genius.com/11533302", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Crackhead swag with the razor blade (Sex)", "vault_lines": ["Crackhead swag with the razor blade (Sex)"], "meaning": "In early 2017, Skepta engaged in an Instagram Live stream with fans, with many calling him a “crackhead” due to his recent weight loss and change in attire, ditching the typical clothing of British culture and opting for a more left-field appearance. Skepta spoke about embracing the negative terms that people call you – thus he labelled his new look “crackhead swag”.\n\n\n\nSkepta is also using multiple meanings saying his crackhead swag is sharp (on point), like a razor blade. He is also referring to his razor blade chain and alluding to the use of razor blades to cut crack.", "annotation_id": "11533258", "url": "https://genius.com/11533258", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Like Martin Luther, man, I had a dream Now it’s my world, you can have the scene", "vault_lines": ["Like Martin Luther, man, I had a dream", "Now it's my world, you can have the scene"], "meaning": "Skepta likens his humble aspirations to be a successful artist to Martin Luther King Jr.’s definitive, “I Have a Dream” speech, in which he envisions an America free from racism and vast opportunities for everyone, regardless of skin colour.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/I47Y6VHc3Ms?t=12m12s\n\nFor much of Skepta’s lengthy career, he has enjoyed success as a prominent grime artist, however, his popularity was restricted to the UK. In 2015, coming back from a short hiatus in which he would completely reinvent himself, Skepta released his internationally recognised album Konnichiwa. The album would spawn accolades such as BET award’s Best International Act.", "annotation_id": "11533179", "url": "https://genius.com/11533179", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Stand up tall, right next to Kobe (mm-mm)", "vault_lines": ["Stand up tall, right next to Kobe (mm-mm)"], "meaning": "Kobe Bryant was a well-known retired American basketball player who played for the Los Angeles Lakers.\n\nTo “stand tall” is to be brave and proud—Kobe was 1.98m tall (or 6'6\"), so standing next to him (and figuratively being the same height) is quite a feat. Skepta is estimated to be 6'1\", fives inches shorter than Kobe.\n\n“Stand Up Tall” is also a 2004 song by Skepta’s fellow UK MC, Dizzee Rascal.", "annotation_id": "11533138", "url": "https://genius.com/11533138", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Real life greaze when I touch the riddim", "vault_lines": ["Real life greaze when I touch the riddim"], "meaning": "To be “with the Greaze” is to be down and dirty, i.e. you’ve seen some shit and done some stuff. This is a common Grime trope. Skepta is saying here that when he’s on the track (‘Riddim’) you can always trust he’s bringing this street mentality to the music.", "annotation_id": "11532702", "url": "https://genius.com/11532702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Spit in your face with extra bogey", "vault_lines": ["Spit in your face with extra bogey"], "meaning": "“Bogey” is British slang for snot, usually used by young children. In other words, he’s spitting in your face with extra phlegm.", "annotation_id": "11532225", "url": "https://genius.com/11532225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "(OVO, BBK, greaze!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Skepta ad-libs the names of both his and Drake’s respective record label/crews, Drake’s October’s Very Own and Skepta’s Boy Better Know.\n\nIn 2016 Drake, signed to BBK, and even got a BBK tattoo. Skepta got an OVO tat in return.", "annotation_id": "11532200", "url": "https://genius.com/11532200", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "They wanna bust jokes, they think I’m Damon Wayans (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["They wanna bust jokes, they think I'm Damon Wayans (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Damon Wayans is a well known comedian and actor, and a member of the well known Wayans family. He is best known for his work on In Living Colour.\n\nSkepta is saying that some people don’t take him seriously, treating him as they would treat Damon Wayans.", "annotation_id": "11532188", "url": "https://genius.com/11532188", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Still the same guy when I touched a million Record labels wanted us to listen", "vault_lines": ["Still the same guy when I touched a million", "Record labels wanted us to listen"], "meaning": "In the early 2010s, Skepta was signed to  Universal Music, whose commercial ethics often came at the compromise of artistic liberty. Under the label Skepta would release his commercial sophomore LP, Doin' It Again, only to leave the label. In 2015 Skepta would return with his internationally recognised album, Konnichiwa, under his own independent label Boy Better Know on which he forgoes euphoric pop-riffs in favour of a full on grime sound.\n\nIn interview with The FADER, Skepta gave his thoughts on record labels:\n\nSomewhere in my heart I feel like I’m way past a record deal now […] It’s pointless to me. I believe in my talent so much, it’s crazy. It’s not about a record label to me.", "annotation_id": "11532090", "url": "https://genius.com/11532090", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "It’s my time, I don’t flex a Rollie", "vault_lines": ["It's my time, I don't flex a Rollie"], "meaning": "Skepta has a long-standing aversion to high fashion brands like Rolex and has publicly spoken out against them several times. In a 2015 interview with Dazed, he said:\n\nI stopped wearing all that a little while ago. I just think that nowadays in the streets, loads of people are robbing each other and killing each other to keep up with rappers and everybody that’s got money. They think that buying Gucci and Louis and standing on their council estate with like £2 in their pocket and a £300 Gucci bag means that they are on a level. But when you look at the Gucci and Louis campaigns, they never pull no street guys, when it’s probably street guys and lap dancers that buy the most Louis and Gucci anyway! They’re keeping Gucci going, but they just ignore our culture and act like we don’t exist, so I threw that out there to say fuck them.\n\nOn JME’s 2015 song “Don’t @ Me,” he specifically called out Rolex:\n\nSold my Rolex, no time on my hand", "annotation_id": "11531999", "url": "https://genius.com/11531999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Fish-eye lens on my tunnel vision (Straight)", "vault_lines": ["Fish-eye lens on my tunnel vision (Straight)"], "meaning": "Skepta restates a line from No Security A fisheye lens is a camera lens that has an ultra-wide viewing angle. Tunnel vision is a defective sight that has a close field of view.\n\nSkepta applies the fisheye lens to his current narrow tunnel vision to see the bigger picture.", "annotation_id": "11531942", "url": "https://genius.com/11531942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "South by, ride out, no Suzuki Got the Austin Powers, a man’s extra groovy (Greaze!)", "vault_lines": ["South by, ride out, no Suzuki", "Got the Austin Powers, a man's extra groovy (Greaze!)"], "meaning": "Skepta performed in Austin, Texas music festival South by Southwest in 2015 and 2016. He suggests he doesn’t leave the venue in a budget vehicle like a Suzuki but is rather escorted in a higher end car.\n\nAustin Powers is a fictional British spy created and portrayed by Mike Myers, and the protagonist of the Austin Powers film series. He is characterized as a hard partying, 1960s-era London swinger.", "annotation_id": "11531901", "url": "https://genius.com/11531901", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "(Greaze!) Taliban, Halloween (Icy)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The term Taliban represents the trap movement and was coined and popularized by The Diplomats. In 2012,  Metro Boomin would revitalize the term after a Twitter post of a lyric from T.I.’s “Rubber Band Man:”\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MetroBoomin/status/192823085345415168?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\nHalloween being in October, is a play on October’s Very Own.\n\nThe phrase has most notably appeared on Drake and Future’s “Jumpman.” However it has not been without controversy. With obvious connotations to the Taliban, it doesn’t appear in the censored version of “Jumpman” and was omitted from an Apple ad.\n\nSkepta has also made a Jumpman remix called ‘Roadman’ which was a Halloween Mix and was debuted on OVO Sound Radio on Halloween 2015.", "annotation_id": "11531549", "url": "https://genius.com/11531549", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Red umbrella when I make it rain (Sex)", "vault_lines": ["Red umbrella when I make it rain (Sex)"], "meaning": "Skepta dances with a red umbrella in shallow water in the music video for his February 2017 song “No Security”:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/f6suaDGWmOo?t=103\n\nThis could also refer to women with red umbrellas on European streets, which indicates they are strippers looking to get customers—Skepta is “making it rain,” or spending money in strip clubs.", "annotation_id": "11531436", "url": "https://genius.com/11531436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "line": "Died and came back as Fela Kuti", "vault_lines": ["Died and came back as Fela Kuti"], "meaning": "Fela Kuti was one of Africa’s most influential musicians, who was also known for his passionate political and human rights activism. In early 2017, Skepta visited Kuti’s New Afrikan Shrine in Nigeria.\n\nSkepta uses death as a metaphor to describe his transformation into a musician who isn’t afraid to offer his opinion. This is also a play on the Yoruba meaning of Fela Kuti’s middle name, Anikulapo, which means “He who carries death in his pouch”).", "annotation_id": "11531398", "url": "https://genius.com/11531398", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Skepta Interlude", "status": "done", "targets": 29, "clicked": 29, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-skepta-interlude-lyrics", "genius_title": "Skepta Interlude"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Street Cred", "line": "Man, I am so far gone, bitch, I’m outta here", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Reference to his 2009 mixtape, So Far Gone.", "annotation_id": "39260847", "url": "https://genius.com/39260847", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Street Cred", "line": "Дей, гиди, пушка-бойл—", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song samples “Pilentse Pee” by The London Bulgarian Choir.\nThe original line from the intro is\n\nДей, гиди, пушка-бойлия?\n\nWhen translated, it reads as\n\nWhere is the long rifle?", "annotation_id": "31757242", "url": "https://genius.com/31757242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Street Cred", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 2, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-king-leon-street-cred-lyrics", "genius_title": "King Leon (Street Cred)"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Talking all them good things, that’s all I’m really good for", "vault_lines": ["Talking all them good things, that's all I'm really good for"], "meaning": "reflects on reputation, Drake wins a lot and obtains lots of success but also suggests a subtle emptiness not finding deeper fulfilment", "annotation_id": "36173042", "url": "https://genius.com/36173042", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "The living proof that you ain’t gotta die to get to heaven", "vault_lines": ["The living proof that you ain't gotta die to get to heaven"], "meaning": "Drake is living a decadent lifestyle during his earthly stay, one that many can only hope to experience after death.", "annotation_id": "25015298", "url": "https://genius.com/25015298", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "That was back in the days, Acura days", "vault_lines": ["That was back in the days, Acura days"], "meaning": "Drake’s first car was an Acura TSX, and he drove it around the time he was on the teen TeenNick TV show Degrassi: The Next Generation, which would have been around the mid to late 2000’s.", "annotation_id": "17478887", "url": "https://genius.com/17478887", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "[Verse 2]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse was inspired by Drake’s visit to Memphis, Tennessee at 15. Drake credits this trip with inspiring him to become a rapper. This is when Yo Gotti was starting to blow locally, Drake was taken to a club by Gotti and E-Mack (who at the time managed Gotti, and was also Drake’s cousins boyfriend). Drake had never seen so many cars and jewelry, this is when Drake realized rap really pays. Drake speaks on it at 18:28 of his 2016 Nardwuar Interview.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DOEDtqdbWAg", "annotation_id": "9137247", "url": "https://genius.com/9137247", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "[Intro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The liner notes credit three samples: “Neck of the Woods” by Birdman, “Duffle Bag Boy” by Playaz Circle, and Jah Cure’s “Farmer’s Pleasure”.\n\nT-Minus performed all the instrumental parts except keys, which were done by 40.", "annotation_id": "6607856", "url": "https://genius.com/6607856", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Probably shouldn’t be driving, it just got so much harder Can’t even see it straight, I can’t even see it straight, ah", "vault_lines": ["Probably shouldn't be driving, it just got so much harder", "Can't even see it straight, I can't even see it straight, ah"], "meaning": "Drake knows that he is too drunk/high to drive but continues to drive. Since he is drunk/high, his vision is impaired so his vision probably looks something like this:\n\n\n\nPerhaps also an allusion to the fact that feels me may also be on a path that he can no longer control. After such an explosive rise to fame, he feels he’s not qualified to live this life of fame and fortune. At least, maybe not this soon.", "annotation_id": "1250419", "url": "https://genius.com/1250419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Live a little, ’cause niggas die a lot And lie a lot, but I’m the truth, that’s right, I fuckin’ said it", "vault_lines": ["Live a little, 'cause niggas die a lot", "And lie a lot, but I'm the truth, that's right, I fuckin' said it"], "meaning": "Drizzy and his crew  take advantage of being young, black, and famous. He is lucky though. Very few get to live the life he has, especially at such a young age. He realizes that while he is able to enjoy his lifestyle, there are still people struggling and losing their lives as a result of violence. So in a way, Drake lives his life for the black youth that couldn’t make it to the spot he is at.\n\nAt the same time, Drizzy can be pompous about it. While he can rightfully brag, there are plenty of guys who act like they are the shit, when the truth is they don’t have shit.\n\n\n\nAbove: Drake with someone who does have shit. A lot of it.", "annotation_id": "667818", "url": "https://genius.com/667818", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "It’s been two years since somebody asked me who I was", "vault_lines": ["It's been two years since somebody asked me who I was"], "meaning": "Drake has become so popular in current society that he has become a house-hold name. He’s not just that dude from Degrassi anymore.\n\nNext he’ll admit that he is in fact, “the greatest” immediately following, illustrating that no matter how much time he spends staring at himself in the mirror (two years?), Drake is still capable of being sure of who he is and what he wants. He won’t listen to the haters.", "annotation_id": "657619", "url": "https://genius.com/657619", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "You, girl, you right there, you look like you like this shit How’d I know? How’d I know? That’s me on some psychic shit", "vault_lines": ["You, girl, you right there, you look like you like this shit", "How'd I know? How'd I know? That's me on some psychic shit"], "meaning": "Imagine someone looked at you and knew if you liked them or not, specifically their music. That, right there, is psychic.\n\nHere, Drake is breaking the fourth wall by speaking directly to the listener, who he guesses is most likely a girl who’s a big fan of his, and will, consequently, like the song. Of course, he could be wrong if he’s speaking to a guy fan, or even just a hater.", "annotation_id": "559032", "url": "https://genius.com/559032", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Rap and bitches, rap and bitches, bitches and rappin’ Rappin’ and bitches until all of it switches, I swear", "vault_lines": ["Rap and bitches, rap and bitches, bitches and rappin'", "Rappin' and bitches until all of it switches, I swear"], "meaning": "He only cares about rap music, women and women rappers (Nicki Minaj).\n\nHe’s saying that currently he only cares about rap and just a little bit about women, at least until it switches – possibly at a time such as marriage or when he finds the right lady.\n\nSimple honesty from Drizzy. He’s focused on gettin bitches and makin' music; wouldn’t you do the same if you were in his place?\n\nBut as we know, this isn’t really all he thinks about, because he has an emotional side that he often expresses.\n\nDrake says “rap” and “bitches” both four times, implying he may actually care about them equally.", "annotation_id": "497326", "url": "https://genius.com/497326", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Making major changes to the life I’m livin’ I had no choice, I had to prove I made the right decision", "vault_lines": ["Making major changes to the life I'm livin'", "I had no choice, I had to prove I made the right decision"], "meaning": "He is referring to the decision he made prior to being signed where he chose to leave television and enter music. This is alluded to in “Look What You’ve Done”, as well. In that song he talks about speaking with his uncle over the decision to take music seriously and drop television.\n\nHe made such a huge life changing decision that he had no choice but to succeed to prove to everyone including himself that he made the right decision to pursue his dream in music.", "annotation_id": "457547", "url": "https://genius.com/457547", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Bridge over troubled water, ice in my muddy water", "vault_lines": ["Bridge over troubled water, ice in my muddy water"], "meaning": "Muddy is slang for Lean/Sizzurp due to the thickness of the syurp The Weeknd also makes use of this in the  song “Gone”.\n\n\n\nLean originated in Houston, which is Drake’s “musical home” in the states. He refers a lot to Houston. That being said, he is clearly saying he prefers his Leans ice cold.\n\nGiven that this song is about Drake’s Southern and Canadian roots, he’s also likely referring to the Blues artist Muddy Waters, who shared Drake’s North vs. South cross-culture. Muddy was born and raised in Mississippi before becoming the father of Chicago blues. Drake was born in Toronto but signed his first record deal with Young Money Entertainment, the New Orleans based label founded by none other than Dwayne Michael Carter, Jr.", "annotation_id": "453117", "url": "https://genius.com/453117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Fuck with me, I buy the shots", "vault_lines": ["Fuck with me, I buy the shots"], "meaning": "In this line, “fuck” has more than one meaning. Drake could mean:\n\n\nThat he wants women to literally fuck him\nHe wants girls to talk to him\n\n\n\nAnd for those of you who are still doubting that Drake isn’t a soft rapper, here is an interpretation for you: if you fuck with Drake (mess or screw around with him), he will “buy the shots”, i.e hire a hitman to Take Care of you. Which is why niggas die a lot.", "annotation_id": "448006", "url": "https://genius.com/448006", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Sometimes I need that stripper that’s gon’ tell me that she don’t dance", "vault_lines": ["Sometimes I need that romance, sometimes I need that pole dance", "Sometimes I need that stripper that's gon' tell me that she don't dance"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t mind the occasional reminder that he must remain humble and realize that he is not entitled to anything he wants just because he is rich and famous.\n\nHe could be referring to being in the V.I.P. room with a stripper. When a stripper says she doesn’t dance, she means that she is down to fuck. Relationships are rough and sometimes a one and done is exactly what you need.\n\nOr he’s alternately referring to both perspectives on this line. He’s referring to a girl that will be a stripper for him in private (romance) but not in the public (“don’t dance” referring to pole dancing at a club in the previous line).\n\nDrake could also be saying that he is fine with a stripper blatantly lying to him (ie. a stripper that does not dance) all in an effort to massage his ego. It’s like someone saying “ I usually don’t do this, but you are special”.\n\nTogether, it suggests that sometimes he wants to kick back and have a romantic time, sometimes he wants to just fuck, and sometimes he wants a girl that will give him the best of both worlds.", "annotation_id": "441317", "url": "https://genius.com/441317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "And these niggas got them diamonds glowin’ in they mouth And they rockin’ furs like it’s snowin’ in the South And every pretty girl tell me that’s the shit that she like So why am I in class if this is who I’m tryna be like?", "vault_lines": ["And these niggas got them diamonds glowin' in they mouth", "And they rockin' furs like it's snowin' in the South", "And every pretty girl tell me that's the shit that she like", "So why am I in class if this is who I'm tryna be like?"], "meaning": "As a youth in Memphis, Drake sees that rappers' style – in this case their diamond grills:\n\n\n\nand propensity to wear pimp-like fur coats (UGK’s Chad “Pimp C” Butler, below) gets them attention from ladies.  Drake, no dummy, realizes that copping their steez is a much more efficient way to get laid than going to school, getting good grades, etc.\n\n\n\n#BONUS\n\n\n\nNotice Drake’s emphasis on some of the words in the final line in this explanation: “Y… M… C… M… B”", "annotation_id": "439306", "url": "https://genius.com/439306", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "With fame on my mind, my girl on my nerves I was pushing myself to get something that I deserve [Pre-Chorus] That was back in the days, Acura days", "vault_lines": ["With fame on my mind, my girl on my nerves", "I was pushing myself to get something that I deserve", "That was back in the days, Acura days"], "meaning": "A quick capsule description of his life and mindstate before signing with Wayne – a hunger for fame, hangups about girls.\n\n\n\nDrake’s first car was in fact an Acura TSX.\n\nAn a-typical Drake talks about his decision to drive an Acura. Those other cars are just too “pretentious”. But hey, if you have the money, then feel free! http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8zjLZPpRpWg", "annotation_id": "439285", "url": "https://genius.com/439285", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Take my crown to the grave, I’m an underground king", "vault_lines": ["Take my crown to the grave, I'm an underground king"], "meaning": "Drake feels he’s a king of the rap game. Along with being a king comes a crown. Drizzy has no plans to let go of his crown, and will hold on to it even when he is dead and buried – literally making him an “underground king”.\n\nAlso a reference to Pimp C, half of UGK, who was an Underground King (in the figurative sense) until the day he died, and now he’s dead and buried, is quite literally an “underground king”.", "annotation_id": "438789", "url": "https://genius.com/438789", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "And I do it for the city ’cause you know the city love a nigga Do it for the city ’cause you know the city love a nigga Do it for the city ’cause you know the city love a nigga", "vault_lines": ["And I do it for the city 'cause you know the city love a nigga", "Do it for the city 'cause you know the city love a nigga"], "meaning": "All the hard work Drake does is for his city – Toronto.  This feeling, and the people of Toronto’s sometimes loving, sometimes ungrateful response, is a big theme of this album.", "annotation_id": "436733", "url": "https://genius.com/436733", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "I’m the greatest, man, I said that before I knew I was", "vault_lines": ["I'm the greatest, man, I said that before I knew I was"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Muhammad Ali’s famous quote\n\nI am the greatest, I said that even before I knew I was.\n\nY'all remember lesser know Drizzy calling himself the greatest?", "annotation_id": "436150", "url": "https://genius.com/436150", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "I was a cold dude, I’m gettin’ back to my ways", "vault_lines": ["I was a cold dude, I'm gettin' back to my ways"], "meaning": "He was working pretty god damn hard to get famous, he had to become one cold ass nigga to get there. Now that he has got that money he can go back to how he was before then.\n\nDrake was cold rapper back in his early days (think “9am in Dallas”) and he’s now getting back two the boundary-pushing, creative shit and away from the pop cookie cutter music of some of his recent songs.\n\nDrake also began his rapping in Toronto, which is considerably colder than New Orleans, where his record label was located.", "annotation_id": "435854", "url": "https://genius.com/435854", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "When me and my crew was all about this rapper from New Orleans", "vault_lines": ["When me and my crew was all about this rapper from New Orleans"], "meaning": "This part of the song talks about the person who gave Drake his real introduction into the rap game, Lil Wayne, who signed Drake to his Young Money label in 2009", "annotation_id": "435831", "url": "https://genius.com/435831", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Memphis, Tennessee know, see, I start to go deep back In Ridge Crest with my seat back with Yo Gotti and E-Mack", "vault_lines": ["Memphis, Tennessee know, see, I start to go deep back", "In Ridge Crest with my seat back with Yo Gotti and E-Mack"], "meaning": "Drake’s father is Memphis drummer Dennis Graham, and a young Drizzy spent summers in that city with pops:\n\n\nI’d never fly to Memphis; my dad would always pick me up in Toronto and we’d drive, and it was like 21 hours,“ says Drake. \"On that drive he would just play me the greatest music, and then bring me to Beale Street and drive around the city, and I started to understand what it was about Memphis\n\nIt’s really the first place that I discovered what I love about music, which was the soul, the melody and the message in the lyrics\n\n\nRidge Crest is a Memphis housing project where rapper Yo Gotti grew up.  Gotti’s official bio talks about the area:\n\nRidge Crest housing projects area was not always an easy life style, after all this area is well known for it’s [sic] crime and more so the gangster ridden neighbour [sic] hoods\n\nThe “E-Mack” here may refer to Memphis rapper Mac E, though we can’t confirm that", "annotation_id": "435771", "url": "https://genius.com/435771", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Do it for the city (UGK, fuck these other niggas)", "vault_lines": ["Do it for the city (UGK, fuck these other niggas)"], "meaning": "UGK (which stands for “Underground Kingz”) was the legendary Texas rap duo consisting of Bernard “Bun B” Freeman (with whom Drake is pictured in the song info section) and the late Chad “Pimp C” Butler\n\nThe slowed down (screwed) voice on this line is a reference to Pimp C’s love for Houston’s chopped-and-screwed music, and for its associated drug, the codeine-laced purple drank that would eventually kill him\n\nDrake is a big UGK fan, and the group’s surviving member has been welcoming, recording with Drizzy and publicly singing the Canadian’s praises.  Drake himself has said that he views Butler and Freeman as “like uncles…to me”", "annotation_id": "435326", "url": "https://genius.com/435326", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "And if I don’t do nothing, I’ma ball I’m countin’ all day like the clock on the wall, yeah, I need that", "vault_lines": ["And if I don't do nothing, I'ma ball", "I'm countin' all day like the clock on the wall, yeah, I need that"], "meaning": "Drake quotes Lil' Wayne’s line from the song “Duffle Bag Boy”, again showing off his pretty good impression of his boss, though it’s not as good as Affion Crockett’s (middle, below):", "annotation_id": "435065", "url": "https://genius.com/435065", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Tell me lies, make it sound good, make it sound good Do me like the women from my town would", "vault_lines": ["Tell me lies, make it sound good, make it sound good", "Do me like the women from my town would"], "meaning": "The pole dancer is making up for the fact that Drake is far away from his usual bitches. He misses those girls, and that makes Drake sad.\n\n\n\nWhen Drake was on Degrassi, he made a song called “Tell Me Lies” which made girls in his city go crazy over him, exposing his skills in rapping and opening himself to the underground.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dAU1pAIU1nc", "annotation_id": "435028", "url": "https://genius.com/435028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "So I drop out, lessons I was taught are quick to fade Soon as I realize that turnin’ papers in won’t get me paid", "vault_lines": ["So I drop out, lessons I was taught are quick to fade", "Soon as I realize that turnin' papers in won't get me paid"], "meaning": "Drake really did drop out of high school against his mother’s wishes. After dropping out, he slowly forgets everything that he has learned.\n\nThe wordplay is on “papers” since paper is another way of saying money. He needs to make paper to be paid, not turn them in (as you would do a school paper).\n\nDrake eventually got his high school diploma in 2012.", "annotation_id": "435016", "url": "https://genius.com/435016", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Singing, ”Walking like a man, finger on the trigger I got money in my pocket, I’m a uptown nigga,” ah", "vault_lines": ["Singing, \"Walking like a man, finger on the trigger", "I got money in my pocket, I'm a uptown nigga,\" ah"], "meaning": "A quote (done in a not-so-bad Weezy impression) from Lil Wayne’s hook on “Neck of the Woods” with Birdman.", "annotation_id": "434893", "url": "https://genius.com/434893", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Under Ground Kings", "line": "Rich off a mixtape, got rich off a mixtape", "vault_lines": ["Rich off a mixtape, got rich off a mixtape"], "meaning": "Drake’s mixtape So Far Gone was very popular in 2009. It was well-received by the general public and its single “Best I Ever Had” got nominated for two Grammies. Drake once said the mixtape sounded like an album.", "annotation_id": "434512", "url": "https://genius.com/434512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
