{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "Kappa Alpha, me and my gang, we do all the steppin’", "vault_lines": ["Kappa Alpha, me and my gang, we do all the steppin'"], "meaning": "21 savage gives us a double entendre (French for double meaning) a technique used by a lot of rappers inadvertently to describe the fact his gang are ‘ Steppers’ but also to describe his gang’s way of stepping is like the college fraternity “Kappa Alpha Psi” who rhythmically “step” in a synchronized dance which has elements originating in both from traditional African dance and the rhythmic dance traditions of African Americans. “Kappa Alpha Psi” is historically an African American fraternity. The fraternity was founded on January 05, 1911 on the campus of Indiana University at Bloomington.", "annotation_id": "30863287", "url": "https://genius.com/30863287", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "I come straight up out the 6, and we don’t spare sissies", "vault_lines": ["I come straight up out the 6, and we don't spare sissies"], "meaning": "21 Savage is from a certain police zone in Atlanta, referred to as ‘Zone 6’.\nhttps://images.app.goo.gl/9MBRKLxvw3D9VjPE7", "annotation_id": "29934206", "url": "https://genius.com/29934206", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "I’ma drop this shit and have these pussies droppin’ like some motherfuckin’ flies", "vault_lines": ["I'ma drop this shit and have these pussies droppin' like some motherfuckin' flies"], "meaning": "Drake implies that whenever he drops an album it is going to top the charts, causing the rest of the albums that are charting to drop extremely low due to Drake’s success. He compares the other charting albums dropping to flies dropping dead (most likely because of a fogger or some other deterrent). By calling them “pussies” he is most likely suggesting that their albums are bad, or very subpar compared to his.\n\n\n\n A fogger, used to kill flies and other pests\n\nThis lyric can be interpreted in another way too:\nDrake could be saying that after he drops his album other rappers will imitate him and drop music, hoping to have as much success as him. Again, by calling them “pussies” he is alluding that their music is bad, and it will never be anywhere near as successful as his own.", "annotation_id": "27901640", "url": "https://genius.com/27901640", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "Flipper, so much bread, I’m a gymnast", "vault_lines": ["Flipper, so much bread, I'm a gymnast"], "meaning": "He has the spirit of a hustler, so he feels like he can sell anything around him to make some quick cash, colloquially known as “bread”. This practice is widely known as “Flipping” — hence referring to himself as a “Flipper”. He further emphasizes this by making a metaphor alluding to him being a gymnast.", "annotation_id": "27196387", "url": "https://genius.com/27196387", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "Metro", "vault_lines": ["If Young Metro don't trust you I'm gon' shoot you"], "meaning": "This tag is performed by Young Thug and it was taken from his 2016 track with Metro Boomin “Hercules” off their scrapped project “Metro Thuggin”.", "annotation_id": "26941542", "url": "https://genius.com/26941542", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "Jacob charged me four-fifty for a tennis chain US Open, had it on us at the tennis game", "vault_lines": ["Jacob charged me four-fifty for a tennis chain", "US Open, had it on us at the tennis game"], "meaning": "Drake mentions celebrity jeweler Yakov “Jacob” Arabo, stating that he purchased a tennis chain from Arobo for $450,000. As Drake owns a vast jewelry collection, this claim is likely to be accurate.\n\n\n\nDrake and Arobo in February 2020.\n\nSpecifically, Drake’s rocking his “tennis chain,” a type of necklace containing small diamonds connected by a thin, precious metal chain, to a tennis match at the US Open, one of four major “Grand Slam” tournaments held each year.\n\n\n\nDrake sporting a tennis chain (circa 2018).\n\nNotably, Drake has been seen at the US Open on several occasions, most of which whilst he was romantically linked to tennis star Serena Williams.\n\n\n\nDrake watching Williams play at the US Open.\n\nAdditionally, in keeping with the song’s violent undertones, having “it” on him is likely a double entendre, referring not only to wearing an expensive necklace, but to members of his crew keeping firearms with them for protection.", "annotation_id": "23972700", "url": "https://genius.com/23972700", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "I hit up err, tell him do the err, for sure", "vault_lines": ["I hit up err, tell him do the err, for sure"], "meaning": "This bar is a shout out to rapper Bizz Loc, who is a prominent Toronto rapper from Eglinton West. He is known for saying “ERRR” in his songs, and even has a song called “Errr God”.\nhttps://youtu.be/Ln8k5-4y6so?t=19\nDrake is known for shouting out rappers from his city, as he’s done in the past with Toronto rappers such as Top 5, Chromazz, Smiley and more.", "annotation_id": "23906277", "url": "https://genius.com/23906277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "Let the chopper bang on you like a Blood or a Cripper (Gang)", "vault_lines": ["Let it bang, bang, let it bang, bang", "Let the chopper bang on you like a Blood or a Cripper (Gang)"], "meaning": "The Bloods and the Crips' are main rivals. The Bloods were initially formed to provide members protection from the Crips.\n\n\n\nThe rivalry started in the 1960s when Washington and other Crip members attacked Sylvester Scott and Benson Owens, two students at Centennial High School. After the incident, Scott formed the Pirus, while Owens established the West Piru gang.\n\nMembers of the Bloods and Crips occasionally fight each other and are responsible for a significant portion of gang-related murders in Los Angeles.\n\nProject Pat uses this simile to emphasize how blood it’s going to be when he shooting this individual", "annotation_id": "23883290", "url": "https://genius.com/23883290", "state": "pending", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "No capper, street nigga, not a rapper", "vault_lines": ["No capper, street nigga, not a rapper"], "meaning": "21 has frequently stated in the past that he is not a rapper and never wanted to be a rapper. On Snoop Dogg’s show “GGN News,” 21 stated that he still doesn’t consider himself a rapper, or even famous at all and that he only started rapping cause his contemporaries around him asked him to.", "annotation_id": "23876946", "url": "https://genius.com/23876946", "state": "pending", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "If Young Metro don’t trust you I’m gon’ shoot you", "vault_lines": ["If Young Metro don't trust you I'm gon' shoot you"], "meaning": "This is the tag of Metro Boomin – a multi-platinum rap producer hailing from Atlanta, Georgia. The tag is voiced by Future, who first used it on his August 2015 Uncle Murda collaboration, “Right Now.”\n\nIn a since-deleted DJ Drama interview, Metro talks more about the tag’s origin:\n\n“I’ve been using that as a tag in beats since about August. ‘Jumpman,’ before the song was recorded, that beat already said that. We had did this one with Uncle Murda one time, and one of the lines that Future said in there was, ‘If young Metro don’t trust you, I’m gon’ shoot you.’ When he said that, it had a different tone, but I remember when we were in the studio he had another tone, which is the one that’s the tag that was just like muted. I just made sure to go back on the session and just get it and just go HAM with it.”", "annotation_id": "23876919", "url": "https://genius.com/23876919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "I heard Papi outside And he got the double-R droppy outside", "vault_lines": ["I heard Papi outside", "And he got the double-R droppy outside"], "meaning": "Drake, referring to himself by a shortened version of his Instagram moniker “Champagne Papi,” is referring to his Rolls Royce Dawn.\n\nThe Dawn, which is a 2-door convertible, is the only “drop-top” car currently manufactured by Rolls Royce following the discontinuation of the Phantom Drophead Coupe in 2016.\n\nDrake, who has a vast collection of Rolls Royces, purchased a white Dawn in 2016.\n\n\n\n\n\nAlternatively, this may be a reference to Drake’s friend and OVO Sound signee Popcaan, shortened to Poppy, to whom Drake gifted a Range Rover Evoque Convertible–a double-R droppy–in 2016.", "annotation_id": "23876493", "url": "https://genius.com/23876493", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "I’m mister body catcher (Gang), Slaughter Gang soul snatcher", "vault_lines": ["I'm mister body catcher (Gang), Slaughter Gang soul snatcher"], "meaning": "Throughout the first verse, 21 Savage employs Project Pat’s quintessential flow by stressing the penultimate syllable and then drawing out the final syllable of each clause. In the opening line of the first verse, 21 melds this with SG Tip’s flow from the 2017 song “Mister.” Tip is a close affiliate of 21 Savage and has been a member of Slaughter Gang since he was a teenager.\n\n\n\nPictured: G Herbo, SG Tip, & 21 Savage at a costume party in 2020.", "annotation_id": "23874250", "url": "https://genius.com/23874250", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "line": "[Intro: Project Pat & 21 Savage]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The introduction samples the Project Pat verse from Juicy J’s 2017 song, “Feed The Streets,” which also features A$AP Rocky. “Feed The Streets” is produced by Metro Boomin as well, similar to this song.", "annotation_id": "23873855", "url": "https://genius.com/23873855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Knife Talk", "status": "done", "targets": 17, "clicked": 17, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-knife-talk-lyrics", "genius_title": "Knife Talk"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money (Remix)", "line": "You know a girl is special if I stick with her honey", "vault_lines": ["You know a girl is special if I stick with her honey"], "meaning": "Every time I think of sticking with her, she bring that friend around and make a nigga reconsider\n\nIf you can get Drizzy Drake to actually stick with you, you’re a special girl. Sticking with ‘her honey’ is metaphor, as it’s actually sex talk and honey is sticky.", "annotation_id": "3138189", "url": "https://genius.com/3138189", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money (Remix)", "line": "Now-a-days you sayin’ that I should go Jermaine, I should go Pharrell", "vault_lines": ["Now-a-days you sayin' that I should go Jermaine, I should go Pharrell"], "meaning": "Drake is going out and getting his money. Ever since he started getting paid, people started telling him to get bigger and change his style. Pharrell is a producer, actor, and singer; an all around entertainer who gets money in all different ways. People are telling Drake to become a mogul and prioritize that money.", "annotation_id": "3138176", "url": "https://genius.com/3138176", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Money (Remix)", "line": "So you could get checks cut like Benedict in Ocean’s Twelve", "vault_lines": ["So you could get checks cut like Benedict in Ocean's Twelve"], "meaning": "In the movie “Ocean’s Twelve”, Terry Benedict is the casino owner who the main cast store a lot of money from in the previous film.  The main cast has to “cut” him a really big check (they owed him $198M).", "annotation_id": "244626", "url": "https://genius.com/244626", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
