{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "More M’s", "line": "6 God lurkin’", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“6 God” is a nickname that Drake adopted after being considered the best artist out of his hometown of Toronto, Canada, popularly known as “The 6ix” or “The 6”, since the city’s official area code is 416. He also has a song named “6 God”, that is featured on his fourth mixtape, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, released on February 13, 2015.\n\nhttps://i.scdn.co/image/ab67616d0000b2737eee4ab077c3604845596ab1", "annotation_id": "27052859", "url": "https://genius.com/27052859", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "More M’s", "line": "Used to be in ’Sauga out at Sega City Playdium", "vault_lines": ["Used to be in 'Sauga out at Sega City Playdium"], "meaning": "Sauga is short for Mississauga, a city next to/part of the Toronto metropolitan area.\n\nSega City is what Playdium Park was originally known as when it opened in 1996. It was an indoor/outdoor entertainment & arcade complex (similar to Dave & Buster’s).", "annotation_id": "27052375", "url": "https://genius.com/27052375", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "More M’s", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 20, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-21-savage-more-ms-lyrics", "genius_title": "More M’s"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "See, I was Young Angel", "vault_lines": ["See, I was Young Angel"], "meaning": "Drake has used the nickname “Young Angel” since 2008, most often on his collaborations with Lil Wayne who would refer to himself as “Young Lion” (due to the look/length of his dreadlocks at the time). He’s mentioned this nickname previously on Stuntin', Ignant Shit, Family Feud remix, Seeing Green, etc.", "annotation_id": "23922773", "url": "https://genius.com/23922773", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "And all them tweets and all them posts", "vault_lines": ["And all them tweets and all them posts"], "meaning": "Drake is directly referencing Kanye’s 2018 rant on Twitter. Kanye refused to take accountability for the diss tracks released by Pusha T at the time, but rather blamed Drake for sneak disses on other artists' tracks. It may be important to mention a Kanye Tweet saying:\n\nIt’s all love bro bro, but don’t play with me\nYou stay too close to be playing all these industry games bro\n\nDrake doesn’t feel the need to indulge him. He’s got more important matters to attend to, rather than argue with his enemies on social media.", "annotation_id": "23899897", "url": "https://genius.com/23899897", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "Niggas love to start the beef, don’t wanna keep it rap Yeah, you hit us up and now we owe you somethin’ back", "vault_lines": ["Niggas love to start the beef, don't wanna keep it rap", "Yeah, you hit us up and now we owe you somethin' back"], "meaning": "Drake is possibly referencing Pusha T, who famously announced the existence of Drake’s son on his “Story of Adidon”:\n\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\nDeadbeat mothafucka playin' border patrol, ooh\nAdonis is your son\n\nBy doing so, Pusha made the beef bigger than a rap battle, invading Drake’s privacy and personal life.\n\nFrustrated with the sleazy tactics Pusha used during their infamous feud during 2018, Drake assures Pusha—and his enemies—that he’s not done, and there is still a score to settle.", "annotation_id": "23884399", "url": "https://genius.com/23884399", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "I’m really down to die behind these verses in my notes", "vault_lines": ["I'm really down to die behind these verses in my notes"], "meaning": "Deeply passionate and committed to his music and career, Drake is ready to sacrifice his life for it. For that, he will not allow anyone to intervene with it, no matter the price.\n\nThese sentiments of sacrifice and passion are similar to ones he showed to his hometown Toronto on his 2016 song “9” from his album Views:\n\nAnd I turn the six upside down, it’s a nine now\nI made a decision last night that I would die for it\"", "annotation_id": "23884271", "url": "https://genius.com/23884271", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "Fuck debatin’ who the GOAT Bet not make this shit about the numbers all I know Bet not make this shit about the summer all I know Better find you someone else to hit with all that smoke, nigga, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I know I, hey, hey", "Ayy, what? Fuck debatin' who the GOAT", "Bet not make this shit about the numbers all I know", "Bet not make this shit about the summer all I know", "Better find you someone else to hit with all that smoke, nigga, yeah"], "meaning": "On September 4th, 2021, Drake leaked a Kanye West track, called “Life Of The Party” on his Apple Music radio station “Sound 42.” On the song, Kanye disses Drake, referencing the alleged ghostwriting rumors surrounding Drake and Kanye’s confidence in outselling Drake this summer during their album rollout:\n\nTold Drake don’t play with me on GD\nAnd he sent that message to everybody\nSo if I hit you with a “WYD”\nYou better hit me with, “Yes sir, I’m writing everything you need”\nI told you I was gonna take the summer back\nSo any of the cap, won’t take none of that\n\nDrake could also be replying to Kendrick Lamar on “family ties.” Through his comeback verse, Kendrick challenged all rappers by announcing his coming for the rap throne, declaring his readiness to “smoke” anyone’s top five.\n\nSure of outselling anyone, Drake makes it obvious that he’s not worried about sales and numbers. To him, the debate about him being the G.O.A.T. is already concluded in his favor. Streaming wise, Drake holds the majority of the top 10 biggest debuts in US Spotify history with songs from both Certified Lover Boy and 2018’s Scorpion. At the time of its release, Certified Lover Boy has broken the all-time record for the biggest single-day streams in Spotify history, earning over 175 million streams on the first day of its release. Soon after its release, Drake became the most streamed artist in a single day on Apple Music for the year 2021, breaking the record set by Kanye a few days before the release of CLB.", "annotation_id": "23883804", "url": "https://genius.com/23883804", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "(North Memphis, South Memphis, Westwood, Orange Mound)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The song samples ‘Niggaz Ain’t Barin’ Dat’ by Three 6 Mafia, which was sampled from Project Pat’s ‘This Ain’t No Game’", "annotation_id": "23874952", "url": "https://genius.com/23874952", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "I was known for snappin’ when I chat before the app", "vault_lines": ["I was known for snappin' when I chat before the app"], "meaning": "In this clever line Drake says he was “snapping” (which is slang for “Opposite of flopped; when someone comes thru.”) before Snapchat even existed.\n\nDrake is correct, Snapchat’s initial release date was July 8, 2011.\nOn 2011 Drake was already known for snapping as he was well into his carrer, dropping his sophomore studio album Take Care that year.", "annotation_id": "23874882", "url": "https://genius.com/23874882", "state": "pending", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "Why they always act like we can fix it with a meetin’?", "vault_lines": ["Why they always act like we can fix it with a meetin'?"], "meaning": "Kanye West went at Drake on Twitter in December 2018, accusing the Canadian rapper of threatening him and his family. During his tantrum, he tweeted the following:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1073320449088675841\n\nDrake just reacted with the following Instagram story:\n\n\n\nPrior this, Kanye West apologized to Drake for everything he did in September 2018\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1037248508116398080\n\nBut that’s not all, just last year, in September 14th, 2020, Kanye West went on another usual Twitter tirade, where he demanded a public apology from Drake and J. Cole.\n\n\n\nAt the same time, he asked for a meeting with HOV, while misspelling his government name.\n\n\n\nThe day after that, he also tweeted about his desire to meet up with Drake, J. Cole and Kendrick Lamar.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1305968545667317760\n\nDrake and J. Cole never fulfilled Kanye’s apology request.", "annotation_id": "23874743", "url": "https://genius.com/23874743", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "Ain’t got the type of time to be playin’ with you folk I had a Richard prior to these niggas, that’s the joke", "vault_lines": ["Ain't got the type of time to be playin' with you folk", "I had a Richard prior to these niggas, that's the joke"], "meaning": "In these lines, Drake deploys some typical wordplay, as expected from the Toronto rapper. A “Richard” refers to a Richard Mille watch, which is an expensive designer brand of wristwatches, one of which Drake claims to have owned before beefing with anyone in the music industry. It’s a common accusation to say that rappers feud with each other for the publicity as this can often be advantageous for the careers of both parties. However, Drake claims he didn’t need his infamous tiffs with the likes of Meek Mill, Pusha T, and Kanye West to kickstart his rise to stardom, instead boasting his already established wealth before said events even began. The mention of a Richard Mille wristwatch also links to the mention of “time” in the previous line.\n\nDrake also plays on the homophonous pronunciation between the word “prior” and the surname “Pryor” belonging to multi GRAMMY award-winning comedian, Richard Pryor. The use of Pryor’s name also links to the end of the punchline, “that’s the joke,” as a comedian’s job is literally to tell jokes. The joke to Drake, however, is that people think he needed to beef with the aforementioned rappers to garner his fame and success.", "annotation_id": "23874567", "url": "https://genius.com/23874567", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "And I’m like Sha’Carri, smoke ’em on and off the track, ayy", "vault_lines": ["And I'm like Sha'Carri, smoke 'em on and off the track, ayy"], "meaning": "Sha'Carri Richardson is an American track and field athlete who won the 2021 United States Olympic Trials in 100 meters but she was later disqualified due to her positive test for marijuana use. Although her one-month suspension ended before the relay events, Sha'Carri was not selected for the team.\n\nDrizzy likens himself to the Dallas sprinter and plays on “smoke” and “track,” suggesting that he can beat his opponents in music and in real life.", "annotation_id": "23874204", "url": "https://genius.com/23874204", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Friends In The Industry", "line": "You get Drizzy on a track, he’ll put you on the map", "vault_lines": ["You get Drizzy on a track, he'll put you on the map"], "meaning": "Drake has consistently introduced numerous up and coming artists to the world by hopping on one of their tracks.\n\nSome of the most recent and memorable cases of drake putting other artists on the map include:\nBlocBoy JB – Look Alive which peaked at #5 on the Billboard Hot 100.\n\n\n\nYung Bleu – You’re Mines Still (Remix) which peaked at #18  on the Billboard Hot 100.\n\n\n\nSmiley – Over The Top which peaked at #57 on the Billboard Hot 100.\n\n\n\nA Drake feature is the easiest way for a new artist to turn into a household name, because Drake will deliver a hit every single time.", "annotation_id": "23874089", "url": "https://genius.com/23874089", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
