{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 68 Tracklist", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-ovo-sound-radio-episode-68-tracklist-lyrics", "genius_title": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 68 Tracklist"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "I sit back and laugh ’cause these niggas traitors (Hahaha) Mentally, none of this shit can faze me (Mentally) Mentally, none of this shit can faze me (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I sit back and laugh 'cause these niggas traitors (Hahaha)", "Mentally, none of this shit can faze me (Mentally)"], "meaning": "From Future’s perspective, the lyrics reflect a sense of resilience and detachment. When he mentions sitting back and laughing at traitors, it puts him in a position of superiority with an awareness of betrayal that around him. Despite this, he remains unfazed by it. His laughter indicates his confidence in the ability he has to rise above those who have wronged him.\n\nThe repetition of the line “Mentally, none of this shit can faze me” reinforces his mental toughness. Future is expressing that despite all of the challenges, betrayals, and negativity that has come his way, his mental state remains strong and cannot be shaken. This mindset is a common theme in his music, where he often portrays himself as resilient and unbreakable, navigating the complexities of his life and career with a hardened outlook.\n\nThese lyrics showcase a blend of contempt for disloyalty and a proud affirmation of his mental fortitude, elements that are frequently explored in Future’s work, illustrating his survival through both personal and professional adversities.", "annotation_id": "31822078", "url": "https://genius.com/31822078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "(Freebandz)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Future founded his record label Freebandz in December of 2012. His signed artists are collectively known as Freeband Gang.\n\n\n\nFreebandz logo", "annotation_id": "29587802", "url": "https://genius.com/29587802", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "We got that purple rain for the pain My niggas, we ain’t change, we ain’t change, yeah", "vault_lines": ["We got that purple rain for the pain", "My niggas, we ain't change, we ain't change, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake is commenting that he intends to move on from these hoes and other romantic relationships with women by indulging in “purple rain” or lean.\n\nThis habit of drug consumption during times of distress has been ongoing for other people as well as Drake says “we ain’t change”.", "annotation_id": "28385184", "url": "https://genius.com/28385184", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "Shoot out the carbine, no lovey-dovey (Facts) These bitches, they lookin’ for hubby-hubby (Hubby-hubby) I’m lookin’ for paper in any city (I swear) I got a bitch located in every city Gotta have them sticks on us in every city (I swear)", "vault_lines": ["Shoot out the carbine, no lovey-dovey (Facts)", "These bitches, they lookin' for hubby-hubby (Hubby-hubby)", "I'm lookin' for paper in any city (I swear)", "I got a bitch located in every city", "Gotta have them sticks on us in every city (I swear)"], "meaning": "Back on the road, and Future’s track record is impeccable. The trap house he opened in Europe is proof that his desire for money knows no international bounds.\n\nI did 56 broads on the European tour and they was all crazy\nI did 56 broads on the European tour and they was all crazy\nI took 56 bars all in one month n— and I’m still dranking\n\n“Sticks” refer to guns, meaning Future is so well connected he has shooters in every city.\n\nThere’s a complete disconnect between Future and the women pursuing him. They’re looking for a committed relationship, and his focus is on money and drugs. If he walked away from a marriage with Ciara, what chance does anyone else have?", "annotation_id": "7869260", "url": "https://genius.com/7869260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "I wake up on a daily basis I count this money up, I’m already rich", "vault_lines": ["I wake up on a daily basis", "I count this money up, I'm already rich", "I count the money, man, I'm already rich"], "meaning": "Future reminds us that waking up on a daily basis is essential to staying alive.\n\nHis lyrics are littered with references to wealth; it’s his second most prolific theme. His most prolific theme, and the one he has built his career around, is drugs. “Dodie” is marijuana, and “rollie” is a Rolex watch.\n\nI got some dope in my system, man I can’t give it up", "annotation_id": "7869020", "url": "https://genius.com/7869020", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "Take the private ‘cause lately, out here, we gettin’ it (Yeah) Spent like seven-hundred thousand on both the Bentleys (Skrrt)", "vault_lines": ["Take the private 'cause lately, out here, we gettin' it (Yeah)", "Spent like seven-hundred thousand on both the Bentleys (Skrrt)"], "meaning": "Private jets and luxury cars are the epitome of Future’s lavish lifestyle. Before his infamous split with singer Ciara, Future dropped about $700k on matching black Bentley’s. While the media usually frowns upon matching couples items, they seemed to love this cute and grand gift of Future’s.\n\nFuture hit up an Atlanta dealership and got hold of one of the 2014 Bentley Flying Spurs to match Ciara’s recent purchase of a Bentley Continental. Reports claims that Future’s purchase is the first 2014 Bentley sold at the Atlanta dealership, and is said to be valued at $210,000.", "annotation_id": "7856661", "url": "https://genius.com/7856661", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "I turned on the gas with a nickel-plated (I did)", "vault_lines": ["I turned on the gas with a nickel-plated (I did)"], "meaning": "“Gas” is a strain of weed, Sour Diesel. Sparking it up with an expensive nickel plated Zippo lighter creates a triple meaning in the line.\n\n\n\n“Gas” refers to the gas on a stove, used to cook up dope in the trap house.\n\nThis also refers to starting up a vehicle. Since he’s smoking marijuana, and good quality at that, he will be taking a “trip.” His propensity for expensive cars means he will have no need to turn a key; a starter button will be present. His weapon is nickel plated, so he pokes the starter button with his gun and he sparks the engine.", "annotation_id": "7850206", "url": "https://genius.com/7850206", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "I came out the trenches, I really made it (I really did) I came out the gutter, I really made it (Sure I did)", "vault_lines": ["I came out the trenches, I really made it (I really did)", "I came out the gutter, I really made it (Sure I did)"], "meaning": "Future had a rough childhood in a Kirkwood neighborhood in Atlanta, Georgia. Kirkwood is not the best place to live if you are trying to avoid crime. Future used to visit his uncle in prison twice a month.\n\nHe came from the “trenches/gutter” “Made It” to a net worth of 4 million dollars. This echoes the theme in Drake’s mega hit, “Started from the Bottom.”", "annotation_id": "7842659", "url": "https://genius.com/7842659", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "And me, I just stick and move on these hoes", "vault_lines": ["And I rock Kentucky blue on these hoes", "And me, I just stick and move on these hoes"], "meaning": "Drake has a propensity to not settle for hoe-ish women. “Stick and move” is a boxing move used to quickly land a hit and back away before you’re struck. Like a boxer in the ring, Drake dodges women he doesn’t want to see with the stick and move.\n\nDrake echoes a similar sentiment in “I’m The Plug.”", "annotation_id": "7838338", "url": "https://genius.com/7838338", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "Usually, they just leave when we done I don’t wan’ share no room with these hoes", "vault_lines": ["Usually, they just leave when we done", "I don't wan' share no room with these hoes"], "meaning": "Drake insists his groupies leave after sex. This causes less problems down the road, since he expects most of these girls to fall for him at some point.\n\nThis actually happened with Houston stripper Jhonni Blaze, who after a weekend of hooking up with Drake, expected more from him. Jhonni posted text messages where she questioned his involvement with her coworker Lira Galore (Rick Ross‘ new fiancé) after seeing a picture of her in his bathroom (allegedly) and Drake responded that he  didn’t owe her an explanation after only hanging out for a week.", "annotation_id": "7836327", "url": "https://genius.com/7836327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "I wake up, pray every morning (Pray) These demons, they callin’ my soul (Hold up) I said fuck all of you hoes (Hold up) I’m ballin’ outta control (I’m ballin’) I’m ballin’ outta control (Let’s ball)", "vault_lines": ["I'm ballin' outta control (I'm ballin')", "Wake up and pray every morning", "Demons, they callin' my soul (Hold up)", "I wake up, pray every morning (Pray)", "These demons, they callin' my soul (Hold up)", "I said fuck all of you hoes (Hold up)"], "meaning": "A fiend for that lean I ain’t start drinking beer yet\nThey bust the trap, I live there\nCame out clean, I ain’t clean my n— still there\n\nFuture paints an image of ultimate luxury and wealth throughout much of his discography. However on Honest, he showed a different side of his personality, one that is affected by the trappings of fame and haunted by the ghosts of the past. His demons come in the form of failed relationships, severe drug addiction, and a past that still haunts his mind.\n\nDown five Xanax and I pray I wake up and forget", "annotation_id": "7836296", "url": "https://genius.com/7836296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "I stack up the butter, I gotta save it", "vault_lines": ["I stack up the butter, I gotta save it", "Stack up the cheddar, you gotta save it (Gang)"], "meaning": "Butter and Cheddar both refer to money in this instance, but vastly different amounts. “Butter” is immediately more luxurious, seductive, and attractive. “Cheddar” has that Government cheese aura around it, and the tag line of poverty attached to it.\n\nFuture’s is stacking up mills, completing an epic transition from the gutter to the top of the game. The competition is stacking cheese, just trying to survive, a situation he is all too familiar with.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/7qA9bZkoDP/?taken-by=future", "annotation_id": "7836247", "url": "https://genius.com/7836247", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "If I could give everything back to you All this passion I got, all I ever need (All that) For me to move on and succeed (All that) For me to move on and succeed (All that) Jealousy, envy, and greed (Hah) Too much of this shit, I don’t need it", "vault_lines": ["If I could give everything back to you", "All this passion I got, all I ever need (All that)", "For me to move on and succeed (All that)", "Jealousy, envy, and greed (Hah)", "Too much of this shit, I don't need it", "I need acknowledgement (Need it)", "If I got it, then tell me I got it then (Tell me)"], "meaning": "Future and Ciara’s break up was relatively calm until the introduction of Russell Wilson. Since, it’s been a case of both parties sniping at each other, with jealousy and envy both playing a part in an unhappy circumstance.\n\nFuture has remained relatively quiet on wax about the break up. Ciara hasn’t…\n\nSo you bought me a car, he can buy that too\nI can take care of myself and I can find someone to do it too, baby\nYou actin' like you upgraded me, I upgraded you\nYou and me, Fashion Week at Paris, I put you on to that new\n\nhttps://twitter.com/ciara/status/620745666474700800?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw", "annotation_id": "7836060", "url": "https://genius.com/7836060", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "Mayor comin’ to the house (Woo) Nigga, please, watch your mouth (Watch it) I’m the one without a doubt (Watch it), yeah", "vault_lines": ["Mayor comin' to the house (Woo)", "Nigga, please, watch your mouth (Watch it)", "I'm the one without a doubt (Watch it), yeah"], "meaning": "While Drake played a fictionalized version of him in the “Energy” video, it seems Drizzy may have invited Toronto Mayor, Rob Ford to his home. Other rappers should stay woke and realize betting against Drake is only going to end up in a loss.\n\nThis line is reminiscent of Kanye West’s on “Bound 2,” where he raps,\n\nJerome’s in the house, watch your mouth", "annotation_id": "7835869", "url": "https://genius.com/7835869", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "And I rock Kentucky blue on these hoes Drafted, I’m gettin’ choosed by these hoes", "vault_lines": ["And I rock Kentucky blue on these hoes", "Drafted, I'm gettin' choosed by these hoes"], "meaning": "Drake made an appearance at the University of Kentucky’s Big Blue Madness and even had a chance to warm up with the players… although that didn’t go so well.\n\n\n\nKentucky’s basketball team has had a remarkable number of players drafted by the NBA. Similarly, Drake gets chosen by women the way Kentucky players are selected by NBA teams.\n\nA few months after releasing the track, Drake premiered a pair of new “Kentucky Blue” OVO Jordans on his instagram page, which  could mean that the sneaker was already on development around the time the song was recorded :\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/BBvZGQIDQF_/", "annotation_id": "7835678", "url": "https://genius.com/7835678", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "Metro gon’ make it boom on these hoes", "vault_lines": ["Metro gon' make it boom on these hoes"], "meaning": "A shout out to producer Metro Boomin, who produced several tracks on What A Time to Be Alive, including this one.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MetroBoomin/status/632962004752445440", "annotation_id": "7835660", "url": "https://genius.com/7835660", "state": "pending", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "I’m ballin’ outta control (Ballin’) Keep on receiving the scholarships (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["I'm ballin' outta control (I'm ballin')", "Keep on receiving the scholarships (Woo)"], "meaning": "Quite literally, when one is ‘ballin’ outta control' in their younger years, college basketball coaches will offer players scholarships.\n\nJuicy J took the strip club money-as-scholarships meme literally in 2014 when he offered a $50,000 scholarship for twerking. Ironically, the winner of the competition didn’t even twerk – “she read the complete rules of the competition and realized that there was no twerking required.”\n\nDrake is no stranger to charity scholarships either…\n\nI’m up in Stadium in D.C. still tipping man\nShe coulda paid tuition five times, still stripping\nI just throw a couple bills, and she’ll have a pair of heels", "annotation_id": "7835321", "url": "https://genius.com/7835321", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "line": "I need acknowledgement (Need it) If I got it, then tell me I got it then (Tell me)", "vault_lines": ["I need acknowledgement (Need it)", "If I got it, then tell me I got it then (Tell me)"], "meaning": "Drake has a similar philosophy to fellow Toronto native The Weeknd. If he has it, he wants people to acknowledge it and compliment him. He’s been known to “be strung out on compliments.”\n\nHe also needs reassurance, that his prolific feelings are returned in earnest, and that the girl he wants exclusively is in fact exclusively his.", "annotation_id": "7835281", "url": "https://genius.com/7835281", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Scholarships", "status": "done", "targets": 18, "clicked": 18, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-future-scholarships-lyrics", "genius_title": "Scholarships"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What a Time to be a Slime", "line": "London been treatin’ me very well Whole lotta chocolate and caramel", "vault_lines": ["London been treatin' me very well", "Whole lotta chocolate and caramel"], "meaning": "Drake is grateful of London’s hospitality and choice of women, chocolate being referred to dark-skinned women and caramel being mixed-women", "annotation_id": "34123068", "url": "https://genius.com/34123068", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What a Time to be a Slime", "line": "Summer, all I did was rest New Year’s, all I did was stretch Valentine’s Day, I had sex We’ll see what’s ’bout to happen next", "vault_lines": ["Summer, all I did was rest", "New Year's, all I did was stretch", "Valentine's Day, I had sex"], "meaning": "Here, Drake raps the same lines as in the chorus to his March 2021 song, ‘'What’s Next’'.\n\nWell, summer, all I did was rest, okay?\nAnd New Year’s, all I did was stretch, okay?\nAnd Valentine’s Day, I had sex, okay?\nWe’ll see what’s ‘bout to happen next", "annotation_id": "23118217", "url": "https://genius.com/23118217", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What a Time to be a Slime", "line": "In Vegas, I get two million a night From the Wynn, just to run through the hits", "vault_lines": ["In Vegas, I get two million a night", "From the Wynn, just to run through the hits"], "meaning": "Drake makes reference to his multi-year residency with the Wynn Las Vegas. Drake made $2 million for a performance at the Wynn Las Vegas in 2019 prior to signing his multi-year residency to perform at the XS Nightclub at the Wynn Las Vegas. Drake also posted a photo of the venue on January 12, 2019 and captioned it “new home”.", "annotation_id": "22439918", "url": "https://genius.com/22439918", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What a Time to be a Slime", "line": "Bet on fifteen ’cause of Nip My pocket get blue like a Crip, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Bet on fifteen 'cause of Nip", "My pocket get blue like a Crip, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake mentions late rapper Nipsey Hussle who he previously collaborated with in 2009. Nipsey had also hopped on a remix of The Motto (alongside YG and Snoop Dogg) and collaborated with Drake’s close friend and labelmate PARTYNEXTDOOR. In a commemorative Instagram post to Nipsey following his murder, Drake mentioned having just linked up with him and that the two were supposed to collaborate again.\n\nDrake mentions ‘blue like a Crip’ in the following line because the color blue is heavily associated with the Crips street gang, of which Nipsey was a member of the Rollin' 60s Neighborhood Crips.", "annotation_id": "21653994", "url": "https://genius.com/21653994", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What a Time to be a Slime", "line": "Might end up sleepin’ in Annabel’s", "vault_lines": ["Might end up sleepin' in Annabel's"], "meaning": "Annabel’s is an elegant private club in London with a starlit dance floor for the famous, the dressed-up and the well-heeled. Drake might end up crashing at the club given the wild nights he’s been enjoying while in the city.", "annotation_id": "21653938", "url": "https://genius.com/21653938", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What a Time to be a Slime", "status": "done", "targets": 5, "clicked": 5, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-what-a-time-to-be-a-slime-lyrics", "genius_title": "What a Time to be a Slime"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yikes", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-yikes-lyrics", "genius_title": "Yikes*"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "And your weave look good with the Indian tracks", "vault_lines": ["And your weave look good with the Indian tracks"], "meaning": "Kendrick doubles down on the concept of “bad bitch”, complimenting the girls weave, because those are the groupies and the type of girls that are falling for rappers especially when they are about to blow up. A real woman would not wear weaves, therefore a real woman would not entertain a rapper on the come-up. Kendrick is making the difference clear between what type of attraction comes with fame and what is the actual attraction that people need, which is organic and comes along with being authentic.", "annotation_id": "39631139", "url": "https://genius.com/39631139", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "My vice is similar Women love when you’re my type", "vault_lines": ["My vice is similar", "Women love when you're my type"], "meaning": "Vice is described as immoral behavior, and often involves sexual deviancy. Kendrick saying his vice is similar, it is actually a little different\n\n\n\nWomen love when they have access to a famous rapper, and his access to these women is feeding his vice.", "annotation_id": "38914939", "url": "https://genius.com/38914939", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "I tell a bad bitch your ass too fat, capitalize that", "vault_lines": ["I tell a bad bitch your ass too fat, capitalize that"], "meaning": "This could be a reference to Drake’s former crew prior to OVO, All Things Fresh, or A.T.F. It included Jas Prince and Karla Moy, who ran Drake’s website which has since been shut down.", "annotation_id": "24804066", "url": "https://genius.com/24804066", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "We talk casually about the industry And how the women be the tastemakers for the shit we makin’", "vault_lines": ["We talk casually about the industry", "And how the women be the tastemakers for the shit we makin'"], "meaning": "To put this line into context, Kendrick Lamar had just come out with his album Section.80 which has a large focus on women with songs like “No Make-Up (Her Vice)”, Tammy’s Song (Her Evils)“, and \"Keisha’s Song (Her Pain).”", "annotation_id": "24416187", "url": "https://genius.com/24416187", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "That said last year that she slept with a Canadian That gave him an addiction that’d keep him in Mercedes Benz", "vault_lines": ["That said last year that she slept with a Canadian", "That gave him an addiction that'd keep him in Mercedes Benz"], "meaning": "These lines heavily connect to the themes of Kendrick’s 2015 album, To Pimp A Butterfly. He says in “Alright,”\n\nWhat you want you, a house⁠? You, a car?\n40 acres and a mule? A piano, a guitar?\nAnything, see my name is Lucy, I’m your dog\n\nHere, Lucy refers to the devil. In “Buried Alive Interlude,” the alien is revealed to be a “devil in a dress” a few lines later. Becoming famous led to Drake being introduced to this devil, leading to his desire for luxuries and expensive things.", "annotation_id": "21755438", "url": "https://genius.com/21755438", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Bright lights and Rihanna as a lady friend", "vault_lines": ["Bright lights and Rihanna as a lady friend"], "meaning": "Drake has been speculated to have had a romantic relationship with Rihanna throughout his career. Drake and Rihanna is one for the books.\n\n\n\nThe bright lights refer to the attention and paparazzi that follow Drake. It could also refer to Rihanna’s appearance on Kanye West’s 2010 hit single “All of the Lights”.", "annotation_id": "16136704", "url": "https://genius.com/16136704", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "[Verse: Kendrick Lamar]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Kendrick talked about the making of this verse with Hot New Hip Hop:\n\nHe reached out, I wanna say, after I dropped Section80. He was actually the first person to hear Section80. I gave it to him that night. He was catching a flight somewhere, and I sent it to him through e-mail, and he was just rocking out with it for a minute. Really bigging me up on the project, telling me to keep doing what I’m doing, that it’s amazing. Probably a week after [it dropped], he said, I wanna get you on the album. The first time he told me, he said he wanted to get me on a song with The Weeknd. But I don’t know if that song actually made the album. And then after that, a few more conversations, and he finally sent me an instrumental and told me to do what I want on it.", "annotation_id": "2038347", "url": "https://genius.com/2038347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 163, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "I’m in the matter of man, arm wrestlin’ hands I was dealt When I said the music business was all I needed", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the matter of man, arm wrestlin' hands I was dealt", "When I said the music business was all I needed"], "meaning": "A record deal and fame was all he ever wanted. But when he finally signed his contract the A&R’s/the music business' hand shake felt strong and painful like a match of arm wrestling.\n\n\n\nKendrick talks about the hands he was dealt. Simply put, he is fighting the odds and the fact that he was born in somewhat poverty to reach success.", "annotation_id": "450997", "url": "https://genius.com/450997", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Then he said that he was the same age as Myself, and it didn’t help ’cause it made me even more rude and impatient", "vault_lines": ["Then he said that he was the same age as", "Myself, and it didn't help 'cause it made me even more rude and impatient"], "meaning": "Kendrick and Drake almost share the same age (June 17, 1987 and October 24, 1986).\nWhen Kendrick sees everything that comes with the fame he wants to become famous, too. ASAP!\nHe gets impatient when Drake tells him that his time is yet to come and that he probably should focus more on a healthy growth as an artist than to rush to his 15 minutes of fame.\n\nKendrick is amazed and jealous a person nearly the same age as him could have achieved so much. Kendrick seems slightly intimidated that Drake is so much further ahead in terms of popularity, when they’ve both had the same amount of time as rappers to establish themselves.", "annotation_id": "450954", "url": "https://genius.com/450954", "state": "accepted", "votes": 119, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Devil in a dress,", "vault_lines": ["Devil in a dress,"], "meaning": "The alien woman/the fame is a devil in a dress. She is dangerous and diabolic but you won’t recognize this danger due to her seductive appearance. Like you won’t recognize all the negative aspects that fame brings with it.\n\nAlso probably a reference to Kanye West’s track “Devil in a New Dress”.", "annotation_id": "450931", "url": "https://genius.com/450931", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Felt like the initiation, a reality livin’ in the matrix", "vault_lines": ["Felt like the initiation, a reality livin' in the matrix"], "meaning": "In the movie The Matrix, Neo is initiated into the real world by choosing the red pill. Kendrick sees Drake and learns about living with fame from the encounter.\n\nHe felt like Drake was bringing him into the game. Into the higher part of the game and bringing him out of the underground. Now, his dreams are coming true so he is no longer living reality, he is in the matrix of life.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=te6qG4yn-Ps", "annotation_id": "442841", "url": "https://genius.com/442841", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Lookin’ in the mirror, I’m embarrassed I’m feelin’ like a suicidal terrorist React like an infant whenever you are mentioned", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you are the—", "Lookin' in the mirror, I'm embarrassed", "I'm feelin' like a suicidal terrorist", "React like an infant whenever you are mentioned"], "meaning": "Now he feels like a suicidal terrorist because he’s killing the person he used to be for hopes of a better life. (Similar to the afterlife that terrorists are often promised for the sacrifice)\n\nHe is embarrassed because when he looks in the mirror he realizes he is becoming a mainstream rapper and is on the verge of blowing up.\n\nBy “suicidal terrorist”, Kendrick also means that he would actually KILL himself due to all of the anxieties and issues that come along with fame itself. Read it in one of his recent interviews.\n\nKendrick also means he feels as though he is about to blow up.", "annotation_id": "439040", "url": "https://genius.com/439040", "state": "accepted", "votes": 229, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Then I died", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I’m feeling like a suicidal terrorist\n\nAlthough Drake tried to warn him that by choosing this life, he’s essentially giving his life away and despite all that Kendrick still decides that he wants to do it.\n\nKendrick Lamar was quoted on these last lines in a XXL Magazine interview\n\nAt the end of that verse, that’s him, in the conversation, him telling me that you have to accept this lifestyle, because all of that will come, and it’s up to you how far you’re gonna go with it, or how much you’re gonna let in. Me acknowledging that, when I say, ‘Then I died,’ it leaves it hanging on the audience trying to figure out where would I go with it? Will I let it taint me or destroy me? Or will I know how to deal with it?", "annotation_id": "439035", "url": "https://genius.com/439035", "state": "accepted", "votes": 178, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "The reason why the highlight was when he said You belong to the people when you outside", "vault_lines": ["The reason why the highlight was when he said", "You belong to the people when you outside"], "meaning": "The reason why he got rude and impatient was particularly the following:\n\n\nPeople make you famous, and you have to keep feeding your fame whenever you step into the public. As an artist, you belong to the fans, who tell you to do this and that, collaborate with this person, do a song with that person, etc.\nYou’re becoming more a waiter than an artist.\n\nThe reason why he needs to let her go is because he has to focus on fame and not his female friend. The two could never successfully coexist in his life. Drake said:\n\nThe girl or the world?", "annotation_id": "438886", "url": "https://genius.com/438886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "The reason why my best friend say she love me more than life But I live a double life and need to let her go", "vault_lines": ["The reason why my best friend say she love me more than life", "But I live a double life and need to let her go"], "meaning": "Though he has someone that loves him, he can’t be with her because the newfound demands of fame and career will tear them apart.\n\nIt could also be the other way around and the reason why his girlfriend/bestfriend loves him more than life is because of all the vanity/material possessions he has. Kendrick is aware of this, (lives a double life), and knows that her love isn’t real and needs to let her go.\n\nKendrick is also suggesting that his best friend, most likely a personification of truth, or being true to his art, loves him – but he is flirting with fame and is going to have to let truth go to become famous.", "annotation_id": "438721", "url": "https://genius.com/438721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "So dig a shovel full of money, full of power, full of pussy Full of fame and bury yourself alive,", "vault_lines": ["So dig a shovel full of money, full of power, full of pussy", "Full of fame and bury yourself alive,"], "meaning": "A powerful metaphor for the trappings of fame – you may be surrounded by wonderful things, but you are in essence buried underground – kept away from other people and meaningful interactions the same lifestyle that provides you with said things\n\nWith this metaphor of digging his own grave he closes the circle to the very first metaphor in the song, the pine box coffin:\n\nIf you was in a pine box\nI would surely break the lock", "annotation_id": "437458", "url": "https://genius.com/437458", "state": "accepted", "votes": 142, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "No doors, all that nigga was missin’ was Aaliyah", "vault_lines": ["No doors, all that nigga was missin' was Aaliyah"], "meaning": "Kendrick is saying that Drake has almost everything he wants (evident from the previous bars), but he’s missing Aaliyah (a famous R&B singer who tragically died in a 2001 plane crash), a play on the emotional and possessive definitions of the word “missing”.\n\nThis is supported by the letter to Aaliyah which Drake wrote a while back, along with his track “Unforgettable” from his debut, Thank Me Later, featuring a sample of Aaliyah’s “At Your Best (You Are Love)”, and “We’ll Be Fine” off Take Care.\n\nKendrick is also literally referring to the black convertible Aaliyah was singing on top of in her “One in a Million” video\n\n\n\nKendrick’s song “Blow My High (Members Only)” is also about Aaliyah.", "annotation_id": "435686", "url": "https://genius.com/435686", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Mind over matter never worked for my nemesis", "vault_lines": ["Mind over matter never worked for my nemesis"], "meaning": "Mind over matter is a term used to describe the belief that the mind is more powerful than the body. A nemesis is defined as a persons weakness or the thing that will ruin them. So he’s saying the fundamentals of mind over matter never worked when it comes to his nemesis (which I assume is the fame, money, and vanity talked about through the song).", "annotation_id": "435681", "url": "https://genius.com/435681", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "A black Maybach, 40 pulled up Jeep", "vault_lines": ["A black Maybach, 40 pulled up Jeep"], "meaning": "Drake pulled up with a Maybach and Noah “40” Shebib in a Jeep.\n\n\n\nThe section that follows was based on a real incident.  Kendrick explains:\n\n\n Drake called me and said he had a beat that 40 did and wanted me on it, told me to do what I feel on the record. The first thing that came to mind was my first time meeting dude. Me being a fan and following his music, I kind of had an idea of where he was at in his career. He’s in a space where vanity can be a definite crutch if you’re in denial\n\n But [Drake’s] honest, he acknowledges his vices and faults. I wanted to speak from a standpoint of being a new artist watching everything that’s killing him and wanting to die. We all want a taste of vanity. I seen a taste of it the night I met homie; black Maybach, white waitress, private dinner with crew, 40 pulling up, jeep with no doors. All he needed was Aaliyah [laughs]. All of this was in the homie’s possession that night, and in that moment I wanted it. The conversation that night was real. ‘Am I ready to accept this lifestyle…’ is where I end the verse", "annotation_id": "435659", "url": "https://genius.com/435659", "state": "accepted", "votes": 111, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Trackin’ device on your used 5 Series I don’t call back, just blame it on your Canadian", "vault_lines": ["Trackin' device on your used 5 Series", "I don't call back, just blame it on your Canadian"], "meaning": "It’s common for urban car dealers to use GPS trackers in cars that the buyer gets on financing. They do this so they can find and repo the car if the buyer stops making their payments.\n\nThe girl Kendrick is referring to bought her 5 Series BMW on credit. Kendrick does not call her back because due to his newfound fame and pursuit of more of it, he would not go after a woman with bad credit. He says to blame it on Drake for showing him the high life.", "annotation_id": "435647", "url": "https://genius.com/435647", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "The same day we say we in the area cruisin’ in Toronto Hit me on the cellular, thought he was gonna sell me a false word like the rappers I know Sat down with a few drinks, located where you can’t see us A white waitress on standby when we need her", "vault_lines": ["The same day we say we in the area cruisin' in Toronto", "Hit me on the cellular, thought he was gonna sell me a false word like the rappers I know", "Sat down with a few drinks, located where you can't see us", "A white waitress on standby when we need her", "The, the death of me, you, you, the death of me"], "meaning": "Speaking about meeting Drake when he was on tour in Toronto. He thought Drake would just say they’ll work together but not follow through with it, but Kendrick Lamar was proven wrong and got put on the album. This can also mean that he thought Drake was gonna be just as fake as all the other Hip-Hop artists.\n\nHe explains exactly this situation on a recent interview with MTV.\n\nHere’s what Drake might have been trying to recreate:\n\n\n\nThere’s also the shift in society Kendrick is trying to highlight, by having 2 black men (him and Drizzy) be served “when we need her” by a white waitress.", "annotation_id": "435641", "url": "https://genius.com/435641", "state": "accepted", "votes": 122, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Platinum Chanel", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Platinum Égoïste by Chanel is a cologne. Kendrick is wearing this to attract women, a measure of his newfound vanity\n\négoïste = egoistic (From French)", "annotation_id": "435630", "url": "https://genius.com/435630", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "So instead of a verse bein’ read I’ma go and get some head off the strength of my music", "vault_lines": ["So instead of a verse bein' read", "I'ma go and get some head off the strength of my music"], "meaning": "Instead of reading a verse from the Bible, he goes to get head from a woman that is probably only interested because of the fame his music brought him\n\nAh, the famous “Blow Job vs Bible” dichotomy… (which side are you on??) He addresses this infamous subject in his song “Money Trees” off of his second album:\n\nIt goes Halle Berry or Hallelujah\n\nKendrick is also stating that instead of keeping his lyrics as intelligent as they once were, which requires people to do a double take and read his verses, he’ll put out shit that will make girls want to be all over him.", "annotation_id": "435619", "url": "https://genius.com/435619", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Put her in the Palms Hotel, sin city", "vault_lines": ["Put her in the Palms Hotel, sin city"], "meaning": "A continuation of the previous mentioning of the word, palms, this time referring to the Palms Hotel, a popular vacation spot in Las Vegas.\n\n\n\nLas Vegas is often referred to as Sin City due to the gambling fast life it is commonly assosciated with.", "annotation_id": "435614", "url": "https://genius.com/435614", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "And you’re winnin’ from everything that your palm write", "vault_lines": ["And you're winnin' from everything that your palm write"], "meaning": "They especially love it when you got the fame and the money from the songs that you write. For a true artiste like Kendrick, it is impossible to avoid groupie attention\n\nCould be also referencing back to Drake, saying he’s winning because of all the verses he writes on his Blackberry. Which Drake is known for.", "annotation_id": "435605", "url": "https://genius.com/435605", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "When I got it, I was greeted by an alien", "vault_lines": ["When I got it, I was greeted by an alien"], "meaning": "The term “alien” is Kendrick Lamar’s way of describing a beautiful woman. He shares this with us in his track “Alien Girl (Today With Her)” off the (O)verly (D)edicated EP.\n\nWhen he finally got his sound right, he met Fame.  On this track, Kendrick (like several rappers in the past) personifies Fame and gives her a somewhat poisonous personality.\n\nThe alien he talks about can also be the music industry here. When he has always been fighting to get into it, once there, he felt it cold and impersonal.\n\nMacklemore and Kendrick’s fellow Black Hippy, Ab-Soul, later dedicated a whole song to facing the reality of the music industry: “Jimmy Iovine.”", "annotation_id": "435592", "url": "https://genius.com/435592", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "Box I would surely break the lock I’d jump right in and, and fall asleep, I, I fall asleep ’Cause, ’cause, ’cause you, ’cause you are the death of me ’Cause you are the— If you was in a pine box, box I would surely break the lock I’d jump right in and, and fall asleep, I, I fall asleep ’Cause, ’cause, ’cause you, ’cause you are the death of me ’Cause you are the— The, the death of me, you, you, the death of me", "vault_lines": ["I would surely break the lock", "I'd jump right in and, and fall asleep, I, I fall asleep", "'Cause, 'cause, 'cause you, 'cause you are the death of me", "'Cause you are the—", "If you was in a pine box, box", "The, the death of me, you, you, the death of me"], "meaning": "This might seem like a straightforward message of him talking about how much he cares about someone, but really it’s nothing like that at all. He’s using death as symbolism for becoming mainstream or “selling out”.\n\nWhat’s inside the box is his true self, the one before the fame, the millions. He would rescue his former self if one day “he’s buried alive,” meaning he’ll never let the fame change him or his creativity.", "annotation_id": "435591", "url": "https://genius.com/435591", "state": "accepted", "votes": 418, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "line": "So blame it on Mr. OVOXO The reason why I’m breathin’ all the vanity I know", "vault_lines": ["So blame it on Mr. OVOXO", "The reason why I'm breathin' all the vanity I know"], "meaning": "So blame it on Drake, he’s highlighting and talking about the emotional consequences of a life of vanity and the life of ‘the party’ that Kendrick Lamar also raps about.\n\nDrake crew: OVO (October’s Very Own)\nThe Weeknd crew: XO (Ecstasy Oxycodone)\n\nThe Weeknd and Drake are very close and combine their crew names to make OVOXO.", "annotation_id": "435437", "url": "https://genius.com/435437", "state": "accepted", "votes": 192, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude", "status": "done", "targets": 28, "clicked": 28, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-kendrick-lamar-buried-alive-interlude-lyrics", "genius_title": "Buried Alive Interlude"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Hide from Love Freestyle", "line": "This the new version fresh prince and andreana miller", "vault_lines": ["This the new version fresh prince and andreana miller"], "meaning": "When Drake first started rapping back in the day, he used to call himself as ‘The New Version of Fresh Prince’, referring to the 90’s show ‘ The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air’.\n\nAudreena Miller was the ‘first lady of the ATF’ (Drake’s crew back then), as she is called in one of Drake’s verses on the song ‘'Closer to My Dreams’‘, in which she is also featured. So probably one of Drake’s girlfriends.", "annotation_id": "1213069", "url": "https://genius.com/1213069", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Hide from Love Freestyle", "line": "We keep it on and poppin like the dance scene in Thriller", "vault_lines": ["We keep it on and poppin like the dance scene in Thriller"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to the phenomenal dance scene in Michael Jackson’s iconic “Thriller” music video.", "annotation_id": "1030402", "url": "https://genius.com/1030402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Hide from Love Freestyle", "line": "Wanna know if I actually flow just ask V-O", "vault_lines": ["Watchu mean do I actually flow, wanna know if I actually flow just ask V-O"], "meaning": "Reference to Voyce Alexander, the R&B artist, who was close to Drake at one point.", "annotation_id": "1014753", "url": "https://genius.com/1014753", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Hide from Love Freestyle", "line": "Carry the city on my back like a Jansport", "vault_lines": ["Uh, ?, carry the city on my back like a Jansport"], "meaning": "Drake always stays reppin' the 416 – Toronto’s area code. Jansport is a durable backpack brand, so one could metaphorically carry a city in it.", "annotation_id": "994156", "url": "https://genius.com/994156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Can’t Hide from Love Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 4, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-cant-hide-from-love-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Can’t Hide from Love Freestyle"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Run", "line": "Caribana, soca dancer, Machel Montano", "vault_lines": ["Soca dancer, Machel Montano"], "meaning": "“Caribana” is the local and annual Caribbean carnival held in Toronto.\n“Soca” is a caribbean music genre\nand during the carnival, soca dancing is very popular.\n\n\n\nMachel Montano is a Soca singer\nthat (in 2016) performed during the Caribana/OVO Fest weekend.", "annotation_id": "14120049", "url": "https://genius.com/14120049", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Run", "line": "She wanna come to the 6 side She know that’s the best side", "vault_lines": ["She said she wanna come to Toronto", "She wanna come to the 6ix side", "She know that's the best side, Caribana"], "meaning": "The recurrent term “six” (6) in Drake´s songs is referring to his hometown Toronto, where the area code for the Canadian city is 416. In an interview Drake said:\n\nWe were debating on The Four, but I went tail-end on them and went 6. And at one point Toronto was broken up into six areas, so it’s all clicking man.”", "annotation_id": "14119954", "url": "https://genius.com/14119954", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
