{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "(Over and over and over and over and over again)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This use of repetition suggests the love and sex is amazing, making his girl desire him enough to come back, time and time again", "annotation_id": "33035902", "url": "https://genius.com/33035902", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "How far we go always seems to get better with time", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Daniel Daley is saying he enjoys time with his girl more over time and the intimacy grows deeper with repetition", "annotation_id": "33035878", "url": "https://genius.com/33035878", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "Niggas like to play games ’Cause they feel like they know me (They know me) You don’t know me, nigga, I done changed (I done changed) So don’t be trying to be putting on shit off in the game (Game)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Pimp C plays on “game\"—playing games refers to not being up front or truthful for ones own benefit, while \"the game” refers to the rap game—Pimp doesn’t want any fake rappers succeeding.", "annotation_id": "8969037", "url": "https://genius.com/8969037", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "On my way from the studio, so get undressed Let’s do the things that we say on text (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Sexting is a form of instant messaging ranging from sexually explicit messages to nude photographs.\n\nNo stranger to sexting (although his attempts have been labelled “cringeworthy, incredibly cringeworthy”) and the occasional kinky activity, Drake wants to bring their virtual fantasies to the bedroom.\n\n\n\nThis girl seems more receptive to his advances. Maybe he was utilizing this app?", "annotation_id": "8924111", "url": "https://genius.com/8924111", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "I want to turn you out like pitch black Want to watch you do work while I sit back (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Turn you out” is to introduce a shy or timid person to a new realm of sexual fantasy. The hook to “A Night Off” explains the “pitch black.”\n\nGo ahead and turn the lights off\nBut keep your heels on\nThey’re fittin' for what I’m about to do\n\nThe lyrics are also reminiscent of Rihanna’s song “Work” which Drake featured on.", "annotation_id": "8920317", "url": "https://genius.com/8920317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "You hit me like, “I know you’re there with someone else” (Yeah) That pussy knows me better than I know myself", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake never cheats, unless you count the girls he “cheats on.”\n\nEven on a track entitled “Faithful” about commitment to a hard working independent girl, Drake debases her slightly, implying her intellect is housed in her vagina. He has shown growth from this sentiment on 2009’s So Far Gone.\n\nShe sent me one back, but I ain’t never read it\nCause pussy’s only pussy and I get it when I need it\n\nHe’s returning text messages now, which is progress.", "annotation_id": "8920306", "url": "https://genius.com/8920306", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "You would think it’s all mine the way I took it (Yeah) You would think it broke down the way I push it (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Unable to pursue a relationship with this girl, Drake is forced to wait on the sideline. ‘Mine’ implies ownership or exclusivity, as he expressed on Beyoncé’s “Mine”:\n\nI just wanna say, you’re mine, you’re mine\nI just wanna say, you’re mine, you’re mine\nFuck what you heard, you’re mine, you’re mine\nAll I’m really asking for is you\nYou’re mine, you’re mine\n\n‘Push it’ refers to the intensity of Drake’s feelings for this girl. It also demonstrates that he’s willing to put in work when it’s necessary, like pushing a car that’s out of gas. He ‘pushes’ their relationship so hard it’s like he’s fighting to win her back.", "annotation_id": "8920287", "url": "https://genius.com/8920287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "You expensive, you know that? ”I’m high maintenance a little bit, but not in a Not in a negative way I just like extremely expensive things”", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The female voice heard in the intro is Amber Rose, a 32-year-old model who dated Kanye West and has a child with Wiz Khalifa.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/_Cv5o4kq73/?taken-by=amberrose&hl=en\n\nIn 2015, a gossip site published extracts from an interview with Amber, in which she said men she meets find her intimidating and “high maintenance.”\n\nHer expensive taste is much easier to document, charging fans up to $80 to simply meet her—money she seems to funnel into her lavish car collection.", "annotation_id": "8920276", "url": "https://genius.com/8920276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "You hate it when I coat things all in sugar You want to hear the real talk, well, girl, who wouldn’t? (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Sugar coating the truth wraps an alternate narrative around the facts. As one of the realest out, Drake prefers to keep things 100.\n\nShe think I’m the realest out\nAnd I say damn that makes two of us\n\nThe pre-hook has even more similarities with “Shot for Me,” on which Drake rapped:\n\nOk, look, I’m honest. Girl, I can’t lie, I miss you\nYou and the music were the only things that I’d commit to", "annotation_id": "8920052", "url": "https://genius.com/8920052", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "Check, one-two, one-two, bitch", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Check 1-2” is a popular trope, describing someone checking a microphone to see if it’s working correctly.  Pimp C has used this intro before on “Watch the Reaction” and “Like That (Remix)”.", "annotation_id": "8918944", "url": "https://genius.com/8918944", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "This ain’t no motherfucking ’91 We out here rapping for money, you niggas rapping for fun", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Pimp C gives an introspective look on how business in the rap game has changed since 1991. UGK released their first EP, The Southern Way, and their first album, Too Hard To Swallow, in 1992—for them rap was a full-time job, not a hobby.\n\nRapping hasn’t always been so lucrative. Only after the turn of the millennium have we seen a surge in rappers that are worth tens of millions.\n\nPimp C criticizes rappers who are stuck in the established conventions of hip hop—their unprofessional attitude is going to keep them from hitting the mainstream.", "annotation_id": "8917322", "url": "https://genius.com/8917322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "‘Foot off in their ass’ is what Ken told me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Ken” refers to Pimpin' Ken. He was a former pimp, and a semi-successful author, writing the critically acclaimed The Art of Human Chess: A Study Guide to Winning.\n\nPimp C and Pimpin' Ken were close friends and collaborators. He was notably featured on “Two Type Of Bitches,” a standout track from UGK’s Underground Kingz.\n\n\n\n“Foot in your ass” is an assertion of dominance or control.", "annotation_id": "8917196", "url": "https://genius.com/8917196", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "[Verse 1: Pimp C]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake takes the first part of Pimp C’s verse on “Tom Ford (Remix),” which was released on Pimp C’s 44th birthday back in 2014.\n\nDrake has shown love for Pimp C’s duo UGK, before on “Under Ground Kings.” He also spoke about the rapper in an interview with MTV:\n\nI’m a huge Pimp [C] fan. I feel like I’m extended UGK family. Bun and Pimp are like uncles, dads to me…. It’s just seeing a bit of them in myself…", "annotation_id": "8917128", "url": "https://genius.com/8917128", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "’Cause you talk like you got what I need (Yeah) You talk like you got the juice and the squeeze (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s girl is something special, and she knows it. Assuming Drake needs it is quite braggadocios—she has “the juice and the squeeze\"—explicit euphemisms for ‘wet’ and ‘tight’.\n\nThis may also be a callback to Eve’s 2001 song “Got What You Need.”", "annotation_id": "8917092", "url": "https://genius.com/8917092", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "Working, working, working, working, ain’t ya? You don’t have no time to lay up You just tryna be somebody ’Fore you say you need somebody Get all your affairs in order I won’t have affairs, I’m yours, girl Faithful, faithful, faithful, faithful", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Similar to the predicament portrayed in his “Tell Your Friends (Remix),” this woman is continuously working on improving her career, meaning she has very little time to commit to a relationship. Furthermore, she doubts if Drake’s serious, as his reputation of loving and leaving women has preceded him.\n\nDespite her apprehensions, Drake uses subtle wordplay around “affairs,” to both reassure her that he’s willing to wait for her and give her the sufficient space to get her life in order; whilst also letting her know that he’ll stay monogamous.\n\nThe repetition of “Working” and Drake’s eagerness for this woman to finish at work is reminiscent of his and Rihanna’s 2016 collaboration, “Work”:\n\nYou need to get done, done, done, done at work, come over", "annotation_id": "8917086", "url": "https://genius.com/8917086", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "I don’t fuck with nobody in this shit but Bun (In this shit but Bun)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the late 1980’s, Pimp C began a rap duo with Bun B called UGK. The two were very close friends as they released several albums charting on Billboard 200. Sadly, Pimp C passed away in 2007 from accidental cough medicine overdose.", "annotation_id": "8917036", "url": "https://genius.com/8917036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "line": "I want to get straight to the climax (Straight, straight to the) Yeah, have you coming all summer like a season pass (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A season pass holder has access to every home game a team plays during the regular season. It can also be for an amusement park, allowing entry and use for an entire year.\n\n“Come” is interchangeable with homophone “cum”, meaning to orgasm, or climax. Drake’s feature on Trey Songz’s “I Invented Sex” is a strong endorsement for his ability in the bedroom.", "annotation_id": "8917008", "url": "https://genius.com/8917008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith", "status": "done", "targets": 17, "clicked": 17, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-faithful-lyrics", "genius_title": "Faithful"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "Judge me how you judge me, take them bitches out to trial", "vault_lines": ["Judge me how you judge me, take them bitches out to trial"], "meaning": "Because of Drake’s sexual history, the woman judges Drake and his choices, just as he does of hers in the next line. Drake facetiously suggests that because she is judging him, she put his previous lovers on trial, much like a Judge would do in a legal proceeding to determine their guilt, further tying in with the woman “judging” him.", "annotation_id": "37258035", "url": "https://genius.com/37258035", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "Countin’ up my bodies, damn, the thing just start to pile", "vault_lines": ["Countin' up my bodies, damn, the thing just start to pile"], "meaning": "Drake reflects on his sexual history and past sexual partners, and makes the realization that his “bodies” (sexual partners, derived from the term “body count”) he has accumulated have started to significantly add up. This forms the fundamental tension between him and the woman in the chorus, as they both have had many sexual partners and judge each other as a result.", "annotation_id": "37257976", "url": "https://genius.com/37257976", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "You don’t know love, you don’t love me like my child", "vault_lines": ["You don't know love, you don't love me like my child", "You love, you trust, you trust"], "meaning": "Drake questions this woman’s love and loyalty to him, and makes the comparison—however unfair—that she does not love him as much as his son Adonis does.\nDrake often mentions his love for his son, who he featured on his song “8am in Charlotte” from the For All the Dogs album.", "annotation_id": "37257951", "url": "https://genius.com/37257951", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "Fucked a hundred niggas, how them niggas love you now?", "vault_lines": ["Fucked a hundred niggas, how them niggas love you now?", "You love, you trust, you trust"], "meaning": "Despite this woman having sex with so many men, Drake points out that she has very little to show for it. While they may have loved her in the moment, very few still pay attention or maintain relationships with her. Drake questions her lifestyle and approach, while admitting in the previous line that he maintained a similar approach “piling” up bodies.", "annotation_id": "37257941", "url": "https://genius.com/37257941", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "[Verse 2: Lil Durk]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "It seems Drake gave Durk more space for his verse on this song due to how short his verse was on their last collaboration “Laugh Now Cry Later” which was released a year-prior\n\nHowever Durk did stated the reasons for his verse being short was due to the 2020-pandemic and the studios closing early in his Interview on FULL SEND PODCAST", "annotation_id": "32779656", "url": "https://genius.com/32779656", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "Turn up every day, girl, it don’t say that in the Bible", "vault_lines": ["Turn up every day, girl, it don't say that in the Bible"], "meaning": "“Turn up” is essentially another word for party. In mentioning the Bible, Drake is referring to how the hedonistic nature of partying is often regarded as ungodly and sinful. Drake’s religious beliefs are not known as he was born to a Catholic father, raised by a Jewish mother and has spoken Islamic words on his 2018 track, “Diplomatic Immunity”:\n\nInsha'Allah, I hope the mission keeps on gettin' clearer\n\nHowever, he has referred to God on numerous songs such as “God’s Plan” and “Jungle”, in which he raps:\n\nThese days, I’m lettin' God handle all things above me\n\nSimilarly, on “Sneakin'”, he raps:\n\nThis is all God’s doin', man, you can’t plan it", "annotation_id": "24465873", "url": "https://genius.com/24465873", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "I did my dance one time on TikTok and went viral with it Mm-mm (Yeah, yeah, yeah) Mm, mm-mm-mm (Yeah, yeah, yeah) Mm, mm-mm-mm (Yeah, yeah, yeah) Mm, mm-mm-mm (Yeah, yeah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I did my dance one time on TikTok and went viral with it"], "meaning": "This line likely refers to a short video of the dance Lil Durk and the late King Von did at the end of Durk’s “Chiraqimony” video that went viral on the video-sharing platform TikTok:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G7G2_jyqOjo\n\nThe dance is especially poignant now, showing a special moment between the two friends while King Von was still alive.\n\nKing Von and Lil Durk were extremely close. They grew up together on O'Block, they faced attempted murder charges together, and have even collaborated with each other on several occasions while Von was also signed to Durk’s label, Only The Family.\n\nThis part of the song also went viral on TikTok, helping it become the second most popular track from Certified Lover Boy on the app with over 500,000 videos, just after “Way 2 Sexy” featuring Young Thug and Future.\n\nThese lyrics have been used in a dance challenge and also by Memphis native rapper NLE Choppa, who Lil Durk has collaborated with before on Only The Family’s “High Tolerance”.", "annotation_id": "24465795", "url": "https://genius.com/24465795", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "Take her to Sono Bello and get her some ass and titties", "vault_lines": ["Take her to Sono Bello and get her some ass and titties"], "meaning": "Sono Bello is a popular plastic surgery clinic in New York City specializing in liposuction and body sculpting.\n\nDurk mimics Lil Baby’s sentiment earlier on the album, when he mentions paying for plastic surgery for his two girlfriends on “Girls Want Girls”.\n\nIf gym don’t work, get surgery\nI’ll pay for that, my courtesy", "annotation_id": "24032043", "url": "https://genius.com/24032043", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "She hittin’ up Virgil for Louis Vuitton", "vault_lines": ["She hittin' up Virgil for Louis Vuitton"], "meaning": "Durk is talking about Virgil Abloh, fashion icon who is the menswear Artistic Director for Louis Vuitton, the first African-American man to hold that position.\n\nHe is also the CEO/Founder of popular streetwear brand Off-White and was previously Kanye West’s friend/protege/assistant.", "annotation_id": "24031991", "url": "https://genius.com/24031991", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "India Royale cosmetic, I’m just promotin’ my bitch Drake song do a billion streams for sure, I’m just promotin’ her shit", "vault_lines": ["India Royale cosmetic, I'm just promotin' my bitch", "Drake song do a billion streams for sure, I'm just promotin' her shit"], "meaning": "India Royale Beauty was the cosmetics company of Durk’s then-girlfiend India Royale.\n\n“Billion streams” is a bit hyperbolic, but several Drake songs have over a billion streams on Spotify alone, and as of 2024, “In The Bible” has over 150 million.\n\nWhile the shoutout did generate some buzz, the brand appears to have been short lived. The website seems to have stopped working properly some time in 2022.", "annotation_id": "23880913", "url": "https://genius.com/23880913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "Told the girls to meet at Tao", "vault_lines": ["Told the girls to meet at Tao"], "meaning": "Tao is an upscale Asian restaurant which doubles as a nightclub afterhours. Drake is a longtime fan, celebrating his 25th birthday at Tao.", "annotation_id": "23877250", "url": "https://genius.com/23877250", "state": "pending", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "Think I’m Tiger Woods, the way I’m teed off the 17", "vault_lines": ["Think I'm Tiger Woods, the way I'm teed off thе 17"], "meaning": "In golf, a player “tees off” when they strikes the golf ball off the tee to begin a round or hole. Drake likens himself to the Americian professional golfer Tiger Woods, who delivered the iconic “better than most” 17th hole at TPC Sawgrass in 2001.\n\nDrizzy also forms a double entendre by referencing Rémy Martin’s 1738 Accord Royal Cognac and suggests that he’s feeling invincible after the drink just like Woods at the golf course.\n\n\n\nTiger Woods' tee shot", "annotation_id": "23875477", "url": "https://genius.com/23875477", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "The way I be smilin’ and holdin’ my gun She think I be mimickin’ Von, Grandson", "vault_lines": ["The way I be smilin' and holdin' my gun", "She think I be mimickin' Von, Grandson"], "meaning": "King Von was a Chicago drill rapper and signee of Durk’s Only The Family label. He began his rap career in 2018 after beating a murder charge and soon found success, which was later bolstered by Durk, resulting in numerous collaborations between the two.\n\nHailing from dangerous areas on Chicago’s South Side, both artists have been known to lead violent lifestyles, specifically with firearms—Von is from Parkway Gardens while Durk is from Englewood. Sadly, gun violence lead to Von’s untimely death outside an Atlanta nightclub in November 2020, just one week after the release of his studio album Welcome to O'Block.\n\n\n\nKing Von as depicted by Durk\n\n“Grandson” is Von’s primary nickname, which most notably serves as the title to his September 2019 mixtape. When asked the story behind the moniker, Von explained:\n\nWhen I was in jail, I got the name ‘Grandson’ because I reminded people of [Chicago gangster David Barksdale, AKA] King David […] The older guys in jail, guys who were around when David was around, would call me that, ‘cause I carried myself like him […] King David was a legendary figure, and a guy that everybody looked up to. He ran the streets with his own morals, and his own code, and that’s the code I live by too. That’s why people call me Grandson. Everybody wanted to follow me, and be around me.", "annotation_id": "23874437", "url": "https://genius.com/23874437", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In The Bible", "line": "It’s not in the Bible to wife off one girl I’m Muslim, I go by Quran", "vault_lines": ["It's not in the Bible to wife off one girl", "I'm Muslim, I go by Quran"], "meaning": "Lil Durk is a Muslim. He’s mentioned it numerous times. He has also mentioned it in plenty of interviews including one with DJ Vlad. Durk does a play on words here because the track is called “In The Bible” but Muslims don’t follow the Bible, they follow the Quran.", "annotation_id": "23873860", "url": "https://genius.com/23873860", "state": "pending", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
