{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "I used to want a GMC, when Woe was doing B&E", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake shares his earlier ambitions and environment when he was younger:\n\n\n‘GMC’ refers to the car brand he wanted to cop.\n‘Woe’ is slang for crew. It is derived from ‘Woadie,’ a New Orleans slang term for someone from the same ward or neighborhood. Drake had previously used the term on “Know Yourself.”\n‘B&E’ (breaking and entering) implies that his homies were involved in criminal activity.", "annotation_id": "39068274", "url": "https://genius.com/39068274", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "(6ix)", "vault_lines": ["Get your ass mushed, smooshed (6ix)"], "meaning": "6ix means that Drake is the Six God", "annotation_id": "27199144", "url": "https://genius.com/27199144", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "All the dawgs eatin’ off a Baccarat plate", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Baccarat is a luxury crystal manufacturer, known for its one-of-a-kind quality of crystal since the 18th century that has been purchased by historical figures and celebrities since.\n\nBaccarat nowadays produces crystal barware, home decor, tableware, lighting pieces, jewelry and even fragrance. Due to its high quality and craftsmanship, Baccarat pieces are considered a luxury. Baccarat plates in particular can cost up to $4-600 per piece.\n\nDrake is expressing how all his “dawgs” or friends are served with the luxurious Baccarat tableware.", "annotation_id": "30205743", "url": "https://genius.com/30205743", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Why my opps be posting guns and only use they feet? (21)", "vault_lines": ["Why my opps be posting guns and only use they feet? (21)"], "meaning": "What 21 Savage means in this line by only use they feet is running. In a situation where 21 Savage were to encounter one of his opps, instead of using the guns they posted they’d instead use their feet and run away from him.", "annotation_id": "29514124", "url": "https://genius.com/29514124", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Go buy a zip of weed, hit the club Pay for ’bout ten niggas to get in We crunk, lit, in this bitch, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Go buy a zip of weed, hit the club", "Pay for 'bout ten niggas to get in", "We crunk, lit, in this bitch, yeah"], "meaning": "For the intro and outro of “Rich Flex,” voice clips from Young Nudy’s Genius Verified video for his March 2018 song “Since When” featuring 21 Savage are used.\n\nIn the video, the segment in the outro can be found at 3 minutes and 15 seconds and the segment in the intro can be found at 3 minutes and 28 seconds.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-0QHPc69DcU", "annotation_id": "29082374", "url": "https://genius.com/29082374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Nan’ nigga seein’ me, I’m Young Money CMB", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake used to be part of the rap label Young Money Cash Money Billionaires, alongside his mentor Lil Wayne and labelmate Nicki Minaj. Although he is now independent and the owner of his current label – OVO Sound, he still references YMCMB often in his music and has kept his relationships with Wayne and Nicki intact.", "annotation_id": "27672757", "url": "https://genius.com/27672757", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Swear this shit is gettin’ ate, I’m on ten for the cake Get a lot of love from twelve, but I don’t reciprocate", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "These bars tie in with the previous two lines as Drake drops some brilliant numerical wordplay, counting up to 12 in increments of two.\n\n“Livin every 24 like Kobe did” Drizzy starts off with 2 and 4, then “shoutout to the 6ix, R.I.P to 8”. Drake also plays on the homophone ate / eight to potentially reiterate the 8; “Swear this shit is gettin’ ate (8), I’m on ten (10) for the cake.” Drake then finishes it off by saying “get a lot of love from twelve (12), but I don’t reciprocate”.\n\n12 is slang for police, so in the last line Drizzy is likely stating that despite getting a lot of love from the police, likely due to his celebrity status, he does not respect them. This is likely due to the numerous cases of police brutality, particularly to members of the black community, which Drake is a part of.\n\nHowever, this may also have to do with Drake’s run in with the feds himself, as Drizzy was detained by Swedish Police in July 2022 over an alleged drugs charge.\n\nDrake’s letter from the Swedish Police", "annotation_id": "27227400", "url": "https://genius.com/27227400", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Put a nigga in the chicken wing", "vault_lines": ["Put a nigga in the chicken wing, pussy"], "meaning": "21 includes a few meanings about his treatment of his opposition in this line for his listeners to discover.\n\nFirstly, he implies that his enemies and haters are too “chicken” (or cowardly) to act upon their words, and they just diss him from behind a screen without wanting real BEEF (as chicken is usually a softer, cheaper meat than a tough piece of beef). This builds upon the idea presented in Mr. Right Now, which was 21’s 2020 collab with Drake:\n\nEverybody with an @ before they name tough\n\nBoth 21 and Drake are ridiculing people who act as haters on the internet from the safety of their home, but don’t have the guts to actually back up their words.\n\nThis line is also likely a reference to WWE hall of famer Bob Backlund’s finisher, the “Cross Face Chicken Wing”, which can be interpreted to mean that 21 is implying that he can put his opponents in a headlock (as shown below):\n\nhttps://www.google.com/url?sa=t&rct=j&q=&esrc=s&source=web&cd=&cad=rja&uact=8&ved=2ahUKEwj27c3cttD7AhWElIkEHVOLB_8QFnoECAsQAQ&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.youtube.com%2Fwatch%3Fv%3Duz8S8f0VpU8&usg=AOvVaw0CgCuoD4Zh0HoM_BqP9nvD\n\nAdditionally, 21 conveys the idea that he can “cook” his opposition, as a “chicken wing” is a popular dish that’s usually fried/marinated at restaurants like Wing Stop. He implies that he can kill, cook, and eat/destroy his enemies like humans do to chicken wings, because they’re too weak or cowardly to defend their words.\n\n\nIn his 2017 collab Ghostface Killers with Offset, Metro Boomin, and Travis Scott, 21 presented this exact same idea:\n\nYou broke ass rappers food, it’s a Po' Boy\n\nHe continues with this theme of making food of his opposition here, but this time compares his enemies to chicken wings instead of Po' Boy sandwiches.\n\nLastly, 21 also implies that he’ll send his opposition to the hospital, as hospitals are usually divided into “wings” that specialize in different elements of healthcare (like psychiatry, cancer care, etc). In this line, 21 Savage is saying that he’ll damage his opposition so badly that they’ll need to go to a special part of the hospital designated specifically for chickens/cowards like themselves.", "annotation_id": "27226635", "url": "https://genius.com/27226635", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "It’s a Slaughter Gang CEO I got dick for you if I’m not workin’, girl If I’m busy then fuck no You need to find you someone else to call When your bank account get low, you need to find you someone", "vault_lines": ["It's a Slaughter Gang CEO", "I got dick for you if I'm not workin', girl", "If I'm busy then fuck no", "You need to find you someone else to call", "When your bank account get low, you need to find you someone—"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake alludes to his co-star 21 Savage in a couple different ways.\n\nFirstly, he references the Slaughter Gang, which is the label that 21 created in 2017, thus making him the “Slaughter Gang CEO”. Additionally, the CEO or leader of a group/organization could also be considered the King of this group, which connects to 21 Savage’s hit 2015 album Slaughter King; if he’s the CEO of the Slaughter Gang, you can just call him the Slaughter King.\n\nThe next few lines have a bit of a play on words. Drake explains that he’s down for sex whenever he’s not busy at work, but if he’s working then the answer is “fuck no”. This phrase can be flipped around to emphasize what Drake’s trying to say: if he’s busy, then there’s going to be “No fuck(ing)”.\n\nLastly, Drake once again alludes to 21 Savage when he tells the girl he’s speaking to that she needs to find someone when her “bank account get(s) low”. 21’s most streamed song is his immensely popular hit Bank Account from his 2017 album Issa Album (shown below), so Drake is clearly referring to 21 Savage as the “someone” that this girl needs to find to help put\n\n1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, M’s in (her) Bank Account", "annotation_id": "27216716", "url": "https://genius.com/27216716", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "21, can you do somethin’ for me? (21) Can you hit a lil’ rich flex for me? (21) And 21, can you do somethin’ for me? (21, 21) Drop some bars to my pussy ex for me Then 21 (21), can you do somethin’ for me? (Yeah) Can you talk to the opps’ necks for me? (Okay) 21, do your thing 21, do your thing (21) Do your thing, 21, do your thing", "vault_lines": ["Can you hit a lil' rich flex for me? (21)", "And 21, can you do somethin' for me? (21, 21)", "Drop some bars to my pussy ex for me", "Then 21 (21), can you do somethin' for me? (Yeah)", "Can you talk to the opps' necks for me? (Okay)", "21, do your thing 21, do your thing (21)", "Do your thing, 21, do your thing", "Yeah, 21, the biggest", "21, can you do somethin' for me? (21)"], "meaning": "Drake opens the album by asking 21 Savage to add his signature flare to the track. It’s clear that Drake trusts 21 to deliver, as the two have been frequent collaborators since their collaboration on 2016’s “Sneakin'.” On the song, we got a taste of the “rich flex” or financial braggadocio Drake requests:\n\nUsed to sleep inside a den, I got acres now (Mansion)\n[…]\nWe the ones poppin', we the ones shoppin' (21)\n\nSince then, the two have earned a title for being a dynamic duo. On other collaborations like 2020’s “Mr. Right Now,” Savage details his dismissive attitude towards women:\n\nShe want me to fuck her to Beyoncé (Straight up)\nBut I don’t treat her like she my fiancé (On God)\n\nWhile these may be the bars Drake wants 21 to rap “to [his] pussy ex” or aggravating ex-lover, that line may also be in reference to 21’s iconic “pussy” ad-lib. Drake directly invokes the ad-lib on their 2022 collab “Jimmy Cooks” where Drake segues into 21’s verse by rapping:\n\nIf I let my nigga 21 tell it, you a\nPussy\n\nFinally, 21’s verses are often marked by constant threats to his “opps” or opposition. Drake likely wants 21 to “talk to [their] opps' neck” by holding a knife to them, as it’d be a subtle reference to their 2021 collab “Knife Talk.”\n\nThis chorus notably went viral after the album released with many making memes about how Drake seemed to be flirting with Savage. 21 himself notably reacted to a couple of the memes while streaming with YouTuber Kai Cenat:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/cJmyV27mUqg?t=1260", "annotation_id": "27081571", "url": "https://genius.com/27081571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 162, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "I used to roll with CMG, the house is not a BNB", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "CMG stands for the label Memphis rapper and mogul Yo Gotti founded Collective Music Group (previously known as Cocaine Muzik Group).\n\nDrake has previously alluded to his ties with Yo Gotti on his 2011 song Under Ground Kings:\n\nMemphis, Tennessee know, see, I start to go deep back\nIn Ridge Crest with my seat back with Yo Gotti and E-Mack\n\nAs well as this, Drake has mentioned his relationship with Gotti in interviews", "annotation_id": "27080666", "url": "https://genius.com/27080666", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Ayy, I’m livin’ every twenty-four like Kobe did", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is referencing the late, great Kobe Bryant, who wore Number 24 from 2006 up until his retirement in 2016. Drake suggests he is going to live his best life the full 24 hours every day, much like Kobe’s legacy and inspiration will live on in every NBA player who will wear Number 24, as the number is synonymous with Kobe. This could also be reference to Kobe only getting 3-4 hours sleep per night in large parts of his career, almost being awake for 24 hours a day, which Drake intends to replicate so he can live his life to the max and make the most of every hour.\n\nDrake was a huge fan of Kobe, and even has Kobe’s trademark Number 8 and 24 near the custom scoreboard in his home basketball court to honour the man. RIP, Black Mamba.\n\n\n\n\n\nThis verse can also be a reference to Kanye’s track “24”, further complementing his last punchline. Drake has performed the track on their colaborative concert Free Larry Hoover:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hQmrM1tpmu0", "annotation_id": "27067854", "url": "https://genius.com/27067854", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Never send a bitch your dot, that’s how you get shot", "vault_lines": ["Never send a bitch your dot, that's how you get shot"], "meaning": "By dot, 21 means your location. He’s saying never share your location with a woman or you could end up being shot.", "annotation_id": "27061787", "url": "https://genius.com/27061787", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "I DM in Vanish Mode, I do that shit a lot", "vault_lines": ["I DM in Vanish Mode, I do that shit a lot"], "meaning": "Vanish mode is simply a way to DM someone on Instagram and the messages disappear immediately when the chat is closed.", "annotation_id": "27060310", "url": "https://genius.com/27060310", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "I told her ass to kiss me in the club, fuck a TMZ", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake simply implies that he is unconcerned about what the “girl” will do to him — mostly sexually while in public — as he mentions one of the well-known websites known to host celebrity news, TMZ; whose acronym was derived from Thirty Mile Zone. Considering that Drake is a well-known artist, the website has frequently updated news about him, such as his 36th celebration party, and many more.\n\nDrake of course has had much to say about such websites, bloggers or comments given by people about him, which can be seen in a July 2010 interview that saw him give his remarks on them:\n\nI definitely haven’t said this before, but I stopped going on a computer. I have a problem where if I go read a hundred positive things about me and there’s one guy in there who says ‘I hate Drake’ that’s the one I pay attention to. I think that’s a common problem. Negativity hurts us more than positivity helps us. I asked about 10 or 20 people around me, ‘When’s the last time you went on a website and commented on something, like a song dropped and you went on and said, ‘That song is hot’ or ‘That song is terrible?’ And everyone I asked around me, whose opinions I respect, the people I love, were like, ‘I’ve never done that before.’ And these are all level-headed, intelligent people whose opinion I respect, so I just started saying to myself, ‘It takes a certain type of individual to really participate in a group discussion about someone else, especially if they’re going super hard with consistent hate.", "annotation_id": "27057156", "url": "https://genius.com/27057156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Fifty-one division stay patrollin’ when it’s late", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake references TPS’s 51st division, located within the Regent Park neighborhood – home to Halal Gang.\n\nDrake has close ties to the neighborhood through both OVO’s affiliation with Halal Gang and his personal relationships with its most notable members: Smoke Dawg, Puffy Lz and Mustafa the Poet.", "annotation_id": "27057088", "url": "https://genius.com/27057088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "21 my addy, so the knife is on the gate", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The address of Drake’s Toronto mansion is 21 Park Circle Lane Drake uses this as wordplay with 21 Savage’s name and references the knife tattoo on his forehead.", "annotation_id": "27056447", "url": "https://genius.com/27056447", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Niggas see Drake and they underestimate Take it from a vet’, that’s a rookie-ass mistake, ayy", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is a hip-hop veteran. At the release of this song, he would be sixteen years into his career, with his first project, Room for Improvement being released back in 2006.\n\nDrake has reflected on his veteran status throughout the years; for instance, on his June 2014 song, “0 to 100 / The Catch Up,” he raps:\n\nOh, Lord, I’m the rookie and the vet", "annotation_id": "27054518", "url": "https://genius.com/27054518", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "We revvin’ up and goin’ on a run like we DMC", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Revvin” is a direct reference to Joseph Simmons aka Rev. Run of 1980s HipHop group Run-DMC. They had hit songs such as “It’s Tricky”“King of Rock” and “Walk This Way\"featuring Rock band Aerosmith. He regained popularity with his 2005 MTV show also called \"Run’s House”\n\n\n\nDMC also stands for “DeLorean Motor Company” a car (that Revs up) company that produced one of the most famous cars ever, but just for two years before going out of business due to a recession in the early 80s. It is known as the biggest fails in automotive history. A modified version appeared in the “Back To The Future Movie” and Will.i.Am owned one and customized it.", "annotation_id": "27054094", "url": "https://genius.com/27054094", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Shoutout to the 6ix, R.I.P. to 8", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The 6ix refers to the 6 boroughs of Toronto as well as – 416 647 – the commonly known Toronto area codes. Drake famously references the 6 in much of his work – for example, in many of the If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late tracks like, 6 God and You & The 6\n\nDrake pays homage to the late, great icon, Kobe Bryant through his use of the number 8 – Kobe’s first jersey number for the Los Angeles Lakers. In addition, 6 is also a double entendre, playing on the jersey number of Lebron James – widely seen as the greatest player of the last generation. Drake references Kobe on previous tracks such as his line on From Florida with Love :\n\nWeezy played that shit for me and Kobe on the bus", "annotation_id": "27053579", "url": "https://genius.com/27053579", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "The bad bitches waitin’ on a nigga like I’m PND", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "PND is an abbreviation for Drake’s partner in crime and fellow OVO artist PARTYNEXTDOOR. PND is known for his long hiatus' between dropping projects, adding to the mysterious and subtle personality of his brand.\n\nFor example, PND’s last studio album was PARTYMOBILE released in 2020.\nThe project before that was his Seven Days EP in 2017.", "annotation_id": "27053544", "url": "https://genius.com/27053544", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "I’m steady pushin’ P, you niggas pushing PTSD", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake references “pushin P,” the second track off DS4EVER by Gunna. The phrase essentially means to “keep it real.”\n\nHe contrasts this with his opponents “pushing PTSD” or Post-traumatic stress disorder; they’re stressed while Drake is relaxed.", "annotation_id": "27052393", "url": "https://genius.com/27052393", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Took her panties off and this bitch thicker than the plot", "vault_lines": ["Took her panties off and this bitch thicker than the plot"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the quote “The plot thickens.” 21 Savage is saying that when this girl undressed, her ass was bigger/thicker than what the “plot” would’ve been.", "annotation_id": "27052130", "url": "https://genius.com/27052130", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Ayy, ayy, ayy, ayy, ayy I’m on that Slaughter Gang shit, ayy, murder gang shit Ayy, Slaughter Gang shit, ayy, murder gang shit, ayy", "vault_lines": ["I'm on that Slaughter Gang shit, ayy, murder gang shit", "Ayy, Slaughter Gang shit, ayy, murder gang shit, ayy", "Slaughter Gang shit, ayy, murder gang shit", "Ayy, Slaughter Gang shit, ayy, murder gang shit"], "meaning": "Drake calls back to 21 Savage’s July 2015 platinum-certified track “Red Opps,” where 21 originally rapped within the chorus:\n\nI’m on that Slaughter Gang shit, (Shit) Murder Gang shit (Shit)\nSlaughter Gang shit, (Shit) Murder Gang shit (Shit)\nI’m on that Slaughter Gang shit, (Shit) Murder Gang shit (Shit)\nSlaughter Gang shit, (Shit) Murder Gang shit (Shit)", "annotation_id": "27052043", "url": "https://genius.com/27052043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 121, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "You rappers love askin’ if I fucked, when you know we did When you know we did She came in heels but she left out on her cozy shit", "vault_lines": ["You rappers love askin' if I fucked, when you know we did"], "meaning": "This verse could probably be an answer to the now deleted Ye’s interview on Drink Champs, where the artist says:\n\n“Drake is like \"I never smashed Kim”, but I was like, ‘You acted as you did. You got bars like Hidden Hills, give your wife the chills.’ It’s like someone throwing a marble and hiding their hand or something like that. It’s disrespectful. I actually had this conversation with him. It reached a point where Kim said that she doesn’t listen to Drake anymore. He was acting like he smashed. We never really had that conversation, ‘Oh, I never smashed Kim’. But what about, Did you DM her? Or everything else around it. Cause it ain’t just about the actual act of it.\n\nDrake, until June 2022, had a mansion in Hidden Hills, a city next to Calabasas, where Kim lives (and where Ye used to live). With the double entendre “she came in Hills/heels”, Drake drops more hints about his supposed relations with Kanye’s ex-wife.\n\nDrake has previously dropped indirect clues of him sleeping with a certain woman most probably, Kim Kardashian—Kanye’s Ex-wife on various songs, such as, Travis Scott’s August 2018 song, “Sicko Mode”:\n\nI crept down the block (Down the block)\nMade a right (Yeah, right)\nCut the lights (Yeah, what?), paid the price (Yeah)\n\nHe also admitted to doing the same on his 2021 song, “Wants And Needs”:\n\nYeah, I probably go link to Yeezy\nI need me some Jesus\nBut as soon as I start confessin’ my sins, he wouldn’t believe us\n\nThese conclusions of many, that Drake and Kardashian had slept together, only fuelled Drake’s feud with Kanye—who also revealed recently that Drake had slept with Kris Jenner, Kim’s mom.\n\nIt’s worth noting that Drake has always been associated with ladies who are in a relationship, to an extent of naming himself Heartbreak Drake in 2009 and certified lover boy —from his 2021 album. This act can be proved by several occurrences, such as, when he claimed to have slept with Summer Walker—DJ Drama’s ex-fiancée, on his 2015 song “I’m The Plug”\n\nAnd It really ain’t no thang, I got summer P on this thang", "annotation_id": "27052041", "url": "https://genius.com/27052041", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "I’m a savage (21) Smack her booty in Magic (21, 21) I’ll slap a pussy nigga with a ratchet (Pussy)", "vault_lines": ["I'm a savage (21)", "Smack her booty in Magic (21, 21)", "I'll slap a pussy nigga with a ratchet (Pussy)"], "meaning": "As the beat pauses, with a similar cadence, 21 Savage interpolates the chorus from Houston rapper Megan Thee Stallion’s March 2020 smash hit “Savage”:\n\nI’m a savage (Yeah)\nClassy, bougie, ratchet (Yeah)\nSassy, moody, nasty (Hey, hey, yeah)", "annotation_id": "27052012", "url": "https://genius.com/27052012", "state": "accepted", "votes": 196, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "line": "Sticks and stones, chrome on chrome That’s just what a nigga on Internet clones, got ‘em kissin’ through the phone", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake invokes the age-old adage “Sticks and stones may break my bones, but words won’t do a thing,” essentially meaning that verbal insults don’t phase him.\n\n“Chrome on chrome” likely refers to a chrome-plated pistol, as “stick” is slang for a gun. It may also refer to Drake’s longstanding relationship with the Chrome Hearts brand. He frequently wears a lot of their clothing:\n\n\n\n Drake wearing a Chrome Hearts T-shirt in the music video for “Laugh Now Cry Later.”\n\n\nHe even has a “one-of-one” Chrome Hearts designed Rolls-Royce:\n\n\n\nWhile these lines reference “Kiss Me Thru the Phone” by Soulja Boy from his 2008 album iSouljaBoyTellem, the flow of these three lines is heavily reminiscent of “24s” by T.I. from his 2003 album Trap Muzik.", "annotation_id": "27052011", "url": "https://genius.com/27052011", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Rich Flex", "status": "done", "targets": 27, "clicked": 27, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-21-savage-rich-flex-lyrics", "genius_title": "Rich Flex"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "But I know it get better than that I know it get better than that I know it get better than that", "vault_lines": ["Gotta aim better than that", "But I know it get better than that", "I know it get better than that"], "meaning": "The artist is saying that even though his life is easy and exciting, it still gets better. He is speculating that he is not yet in his prime and the best has yet to come for him.", "annotation_id": "40172752", "url": "https://genius.com/40172752", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "What’s up with life? It’s lit", "vault_lines": ["What's up with life? It's lit"], "meaning": "The artist is saying that his life is easy and careless, but at the same time exciting and fun.\nThis may also be a reference to Travis Scott and how in most of his songs, he says, “It’s lit!” sort of like a catchphrase.", "annotation_id": "40172734", "url": "https://genius.com/40172734", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Yeah, Ice", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake uses the word “Ice” to signify that he is figuratively “ice cold”\nThis is also a play on words to the album name, ICEMAN.", "annotation_id": "40172684", "url": "https://genius.com/40172684", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Who should I take? His bitch", "vault_lines": ["Who should I take? His bitch"], "meaning": "Drake is talking about Playboi Carti’s ex, Giovanna Ramos.\n\nPlayboi Carti had Kendrick Lamar executive produce his album MUSIC and removed Drake’s feature. Following these course of actions, Drake dissed Carti on Whisper My Name.\n\nBaby boy, please\nI heard what you said to lil' bro about me\nYeah, and when you run into the Iceman, what you gon' do except freeze?\nYou not ‘bout to squeeze, you not in the streets\n\nGio has been seen posting using tracks from ICEMAN and reposting stories highlighting Drake’s unique successes in a flirtatious manner.\n\n\n\n\n\nGio also posted a video on her story to this song, and during this exact line, she mouths the lyrics and points towards herself.", "annotation_id": "40143084", "url": "https://genius.com/40143084", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Where is the beef? It’s cooked Where is the boy? He’s booked (Skrrt, skrrt)", "vault_lines": ["Where is the beef? It's cooked", "Where is the boy? He's booked (Skrrt, skrrt)"], "meaning": "Drake addresses (again) the beef with Kendrick Lamar that happened in 2024 saying “it’s over, it’s done.” Many fans believe that Kendrick won the beef.\n\nDrake has referred to himself as “The Boy” for years, it is a very well known nickname of his. Drake is saying even after the rap beef, he’s still booked & busy.", "annotation_id": "40130485", "url": "https://genius.com/40130485", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "What’s in my raps? It’s facts", "vault_lines": ["What's in my raps? It's facts"], "meaning": "A  callback to the main argument Drake made on “The Heart PART 6,” that everything Kendrick Lamar deployed during the beef was fiction  while Drake’s raps, in his telling, deal strictly in facts:\n\nYou know, but\nJust let me know when we’re gettin' to the facts\nEverything in my shit is facts (Prove it)\nI’m waitin' on you to return the favor, like", "annotation_id": "39867784", "url": "https://genius.com/39867784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Don’t even recognize none of these names at the top of the charts, I looked (Huh?) Am I upset? A bit", "vault_lines": ["Don't even recognize none of thеse names at the top of thе charts, I looked (Huh?)", "Am I upset? A bit"], "meaning": "His “What Did I Miss?” debuted at No. 2 on the Billboard Hot 100 in July 2025, blocked from the top spot by Alex Warren’s “Ordinary.”\n\nBy October 2025, for the first time since 1990, no rap songs appeared in the Billboard Hot 100’s top 40, ending a streak that had lasted over 35 years. The last hip-hop track to crack the top 10 was Drake’s own “What Did I Miss?” — so for nine straight months after that the genre went quiet at the upper end of the chart.", "annotation_id": "39867706", "url": "https://genius.com/39867706", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "She said she miss me, all caps Gotta aim better than that", "vault_lines": ["She said she miss me, all caps", "Gotta aim better than that"], "meaning": "On his June 2022 track “Texts Go Green,” Drake dismisses an ex of his in similar fashion to what he does here:\n\nTexts go green, it hits a little different, don’t it?\nKnow you miss the days when I was grippin' on it\nKnow you’re in a house tonight just thinkin' on it\nI moved on so long ago", "annotation_id": "39856081", "url": "https://genius.com/39856081", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "All of my opps, they dead Why do they gas me up? Where does it go? My head (Mama)", "vault_lines": ["All of my opps, they dead", "All my opps, they dess", "Why do they gas me up?", "All of my opps, they dess", "Where does it go? My head (Mama)"], "meaning": "Drake has shared similar sentiments about handling his enemies with violence all the way back to his August 2011 track, “Headlines”:\n\nYou gon' hype me up and make me catch a body like that\n\nAdditionally, on October 2023’s “Daylight,” Drake mentioned an associate of his got arrested for shooting someone on his call:\n\nMy doggy got booked for actin' like Book\nHe shot him in…\n[…]\nShot him in daylight", "annotation_id": "39855431", "url": "https://genius.com/39855431", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Where is the GOAT? They need one The mirror’s right here, I see one", "vault_lines": ["Where is the GOAT? They need one", "The mirror's right here, I see one"], "meaning": "“GOAT” is an acronym for the “Greatest Of All Time”—something Drake has called himself on different occasions. For instance, on the ICEMAN track “Whisper My Name,” he acknowledge fans who had him in GOAT discussions but switched up on him after the 2024 beef:\n\nAm I your GOAT? Well, probably not\nI probably was, but you probably forgot", "annotation_id": "39855307", "url": "https://genius.com/39855307", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Don’t leave again, I won’t Thinkin’ of tryin’ me, do like Bryson, pussy-ass nigga, then don’t", "vault_lines": ["Thinkin' of tryin' me, do like Bryson, pussy-ass nigga, then—", "Don't leave again, I won't", "Thinkin' of tryin' me, do like Bryson, pussy-ass nigga, then don't"], "meaning": "Another song reference—this time, it’s R&B artist Bryson Tiller and his May 2015 hit single, “Don’t.” On the song, Tiller talks about a girl who’s in a poor relationship and how he wishes to be with her. Tillers says “don’t” on the song’s chorus in a similar style to Drake here.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/d7cVLE4SaN0?si=Y6J3hwIjsuiwdd6x&t=91", "annotation_id": "39784920", "url": "https://genius.com/39784920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "40, what’s up? Shabang (Uh)", "vault_lines": ["40, what's up? Shabang (Uh)"], "meaning": "40 refers to Noah “40” Shebib, a primary in-house producer for OVO who has collaborated with Drizzy all the way since 2008.\nHe helped produced some huge Drake songs like Headlines, Marvin’s Room, One Dance, God’s Plan and more.", "annotation_id": "39779713", "url": "https://genius.com/39779713", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "All my opps, they dess What am I now? Possessed How do I feel, though? Refreshed (Woo) Secrets I know got you stressed Will I take those to my death? (What?) Haven’t decided just yet, ayy, yeah", "vault_lines": ["All of my opps, they dead", "All my opps, they dess", "What am I now? Possessed", "How do I feel, though? Refreshed (Woo)", "Secrets I know got you stressed", "Will I take those to my death? (What?)", "Haven't decided just yet, ayy, yeah", "All of my opps, they dess"], "meaning": "In popular Toronto slang, “dess” usually means undesirable, unattractive, embarrassing, lame, or low quality depending on context.\n\nIt comes from “desperate” or sometimes is used like a shortened form of calling something “sad” or “wack.”", "annotation_id": "39779052", "url": "https://genius.com/39779052", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Maneesh on the beat, shabang (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["Maneesh on the beat, shabang (Woo)"], "meaning": "Maneesh Bidaye is a Canadian record producer, songwriter, and multi-instrumentalist from Toronto. He has worked with Drake on numerous tracks—he produced the ICEMAN intro “Make Them Cry” and hits like July 2015’s “Charged Up” and April 2016’s “Too Good.”", "annotation_id": "39779022", "url": "https://genius.com/39779022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "My nigga don’t know how to pay me back, so he said next hat he catch is a free one (Grrah) What kinda clip? Extendo What kinda switch? Not Nintendo Y’all hangin’ out with friends, though They hangin’ out the window (Baow)", "vault_lines": ["My nigga don't know how to pay me back, so he said next hat he catch is a free one (Grrah)", "What kinda clip? Extendo", "What kinda switch? Not Nintendo", "Y'all hangin' out with friends, though", "They hangin' out the window (Baow)", "Please don't put me to the test (What?)"], "meaning": "“Catch a hat” means to murder somebody, an “extendo” is an extended magazine—an attachment that allows for more rounds than a standard magazine and a “switch” is a gun.\n\nA Switch is also a handheld video game console developed by Nintendo, which Drake clarifies he’s not talking about.", "annotation_id": "39778525", "url": "https://genius.com/39778525", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Shorty keep askin’ the date She tryna finesse Polymarket for bread (Bando)", "vault_lines": ["Shorty keep askin' the date", "She tryna finesse Polymarket for bread (Bando)"], "meaning": "Polymarket is a prediction market platform where users trade on the outcomes of real-world events. Making money on Polymarket comes down to buying ‘Yes’ or ‘No’ shares on event outcomes, then either selling them early as odds shift or holding until the final resolution to redeem winning shares.\n\nDrake implies this woman tried to get information on ICEMAN’s release date so she could profit off it through Polymarket bets. Notably, he name-dropped Polymarket’s CEO, Shayne Coplan on the ICEMAN track, “B’s On The Table”:\n\nBetter treat me like Shayne Coplan", "annotation_id": "39778345", "url": "https://genius.com/39778345", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Tennis lessons with a Nike skirt on and her ass poppin’ out, you’re sick (She bad)", "vault_lines": ["Tennis lessons with a Nike skirt on and her ass poppin' out, you're sick (She bad)"], "meaning": "In November 2025, Drake and Sexyy Red went viral after sharing a tennis-themed photoshoot on Instagram that sparked collaboration rumors and social media controversy.", "annotation_id": "39777702", "url": "https://genius.com/39777702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "How much I got? A lot", "vault_lines": ["How much I got? A lot"], "meaning": "This is a nod to 21 Savage and his December 2018 song, “a lot.” On the chorus, he raps:\n\nHow much money you got? (A lot)\nHow many problems you got? (A lot)\n\nhttps://youtu.be/DmWWqogr_r8?si=sYNMDsyS2w9K8L2C&t=26\n\nThe bar notably starts on the 21st second of the track, giving a subtle shoutout to the rapper.", "annotation_id": "39776222", "url": "https://genius.com/39776222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "line": "Last one you dropped was shit I did press play on that ho Mid, mid, mid, skip, skip", "vault_lines": ["Last one you dropped was shit", "I did press play on that ho", "Mid, mid, mid, skip, skip"], "meaning": "Drake is taking shots at two albums—Kendrick Lamar’s GNX and A$AP Rocky’s Don’t Be Dumb.\n\nGNX dropped as a surprise in November 2024 and moved units, but divided listeners who expected something more definitive from Kendrick fresh off the beef win. By most accounts, GNX wouldn’t crack Kendrick’s own top five.\n\nDon’t Be Dumb had it worse — eight years in the making, with a fanbase that had been waiting since Testing for Rocky to deliver something spectacular. Critics described it as “overstuffed,” with songs that felt “phoned-in lyrically.”", "annotation_id": "39776054", "url": "https://genius.com/39776054", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Shabang", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 19, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-shabang-lyrics", "genius_title": "Shabang"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Underdog", "line": "You should take a trip and view it", "vault_lines": ["Said I got my city buzzin', you should take a trip and view it"], "meaning": "Toronto is renowned for its various tourist attractions. It is acclaimed for being home to the CN Towner, one of the tallest buildings in the world. The tower has a spinning restaurant which gives someone the ability to view the entire city over the period of a meal.\n\nThis may have been an inspiration for what is believed to be Drake’s biggest project yet!", "annotation_id": "7641520", "url": "https://genius.com/7641520", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Underdog", "line": "I ain’t never been nothin’ but a underdog And I ain’t gonna never let a nigga hold me under dog", "vault_lines": ["Scared I ain't never been, yeah, I ain't never been", "Nothing but a underdog, but I'm never under y'all", "I ain't never been nothin' but a underdog", "And I ain't gonna never let a nigga hold me under dog"], "meaning": "Drake and Trey came up from the bottom of the music industry. They worked very hard to get to where they are currently, and now that they are at the top, they won’t let anyone bring them down.\n\nAs Drake preached on his hit single “Started from The Bottom”:\n\nStarted from the bottom, now we’re here\nStarted from the bottom, now my whole team fucking here\nStarted from the bottom, now we’re here\nStarted from the bottom, now the whole team here, nigga", "annotation_id": "7641511", "url": "https://genius.com/7641511", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Underdog", "line": "This here’s my uncle’s car, I drop it and the top is gone", "vault_lines": ["This here's my uncle's car, I drop it and the top is gone"], "meaning": "A reference to Drake’s uncle’s drop top Lexus which his uncle let him borrow from time to time. Drake also does this again later in his career in the song “Look What You’ve Done” where he raps:\n\nYeah, just a young kid in a drop top\nLexus hopin' that I don’t get arrested", "annotation_id": "477582", "url": "https://genius.com/477582", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Underdog", "line": "Still in the hood yeah, I ain’t talking outerwear", "vault_lines": ["Still in the hood yeah, I ain't talking outerwear"], "meaning": "Trey grew up in the hoods of Petersburg, VA which he still visits whenever he can so when he say’s he’s in the hood he doesn’t mean a beanie, or any other head covering garment.", "annotation_id": "477394", "url": "https://genius.com/477394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Underdog", "line": "Shout out to Mr. Rogers ’cause he’s supposed to chop and screw it", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to Mr. Rogers 'cause he's supposed to chop and screw it"], "meaning": "This is a shout out to DJ Mr Rogers who does music production, mix tape hosting and he also chops and screws music\n\n\n\nIt could also be a reference to Mr. Rogers, of the classic TV show Mr. Rogers' Neighborhood, who shares Drake’s affinity for sweaters.", "annotation_id": "305262", "url": "https://genius.com/305262", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
