{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "Cause if you let me Here’s what I’ll do I’ll take care of you", "vault_lines": ["'Cause if you let me", "If you let me, here's what I'll do", "Here's what I'll do", "I'll take care of you (I'll take, I'll take)"], "meaning": "Cause if you let me\nHere’s what I’ll do\nI’ll take care of you", "annotation_id": "33958607", "url": "https://genius.com/33958607", "state": "pending", "votes": -12, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "I’ll be there for you, I would care for you I keep thinking you just don’t know Tryna run from that, say you done with that", "vault_lines": ["I'll be there for you, I would care for you", "I keep thinking you just don't know", "Tryna run from that, say you done with that", "I'll take care of you (I'll take, I'll take)"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "33958531", "url": "https://genius.com/33958531", "state": "pending", "votes": -6, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "I’ve had mine, you’ve had yours, we both know, we know", "vault_lines": ["I've had mine, you've had yours, we both know, we know"], "meaning": "Drake and this girl have both lived their lives and done things. Drake says that they can both relate to that and it’s also backed up by the next line:\n\n“They won’t get you like I will”", "annotation_id": "28065448", "url": "https://genius.com/28065448", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "What’s a life with no fun? Please don’t be so ashamed", "vault_lines": ["What's a life with no fun? Please don't be so ashamed"], "meaning": "Whatever he heard about the girl had to have been possibly sexual experiences or experiences in general that she had fun but shouldn’t be known for certain reasons. People may look down on what she did but Drake doesn’t. He still accepts her even if she had a controversial past.", "annotation_id": "28065441", "url": "https://genius.com/28065441", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "But my mind didn’t change and I still feel the same", "vault_lines": ["But my mind didn't change and I still feel the same"], "meaning": "The things that he heard about the girl he likes may not be great news but he still is interested in her.", "annotation_id": "28065437", "url": "https://genius.com/28065437", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "I’ve asked about you and they told me things", "vault_lines": ["I've asked about you and they told me things"], "meaning": "Drake asked the girl’s friends or his own friends about the girl and they told him stories about her. Most likely, he didn’t like what he heard about her.", "annotation_id": "28065435", "url": "https://genius.com/28065435", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "Can’t deny that I want you, but I’ll lie if I have to ’Cause you don’t say you love me To your friends when they ask you Even though we both know that you do, you do", "vault_lines": ["'Cause if you let me", "If you let me, here's what I'll do", "Can't deny that I want you, but I'll lie if I have to", "'Cause you don't say you love me", "To your friends when they ask you", "Even though we both know that you do, you do", "To save you, instead, they say", "Don't, tell me, I don't care if you hurt"], "meaning": "Drake is torn between desire for his lover and staying aloof from this to protect himself from hurt because she isn’t as committed as he wants her to be.\n\nShe doesn’t tell her friends that she loves him, which a denial of her true feelings about the relationship (at least according for him). A partner not being willing/able to express affection in a relationship can be hurtful and indicates either a lack of comfort (as may be suggested by the past hurts alluded to in the rest of the song) or indicates that the relationship may not really be as important as the partner says it is in private.", "annotation_id": "26598906", "url": "https://genius.com/26598906", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "You hate bein’ alone, well, you ain’t the only one You hate the fact that you bought the dream and they sold you one You love your friends, but somebody should’ve told you somethin’ To save you, instead, they say [Interlude: Gil Scott-Heron] Don’t, tell me, I don’t care if you hurt I don’t, tell you, you don’t care if you’re true Don’t, tell me, I don’t care if you hurt I don’t, tell you, you don’t care if you’re true", "vault_lines": ["'Cause if you let me", "'Cause you don't say you love me", "To your friends when they ask you", "You hate bein' alone, well, you ain't the only one", "You hate the fact that you bought the dream and they sold you one", "You love your friends, but somebody should've told you somethin'", "To save you, instead, they say", "Don't, tell me, I don't care if you hurt", "I don't, tell you, you don't care if you're true"], "meaning": "Drake’s lover’s friends abandon her in the end, despite being overly supportive for a destructive partner at the beginning.\nTransitioning into the interlude, Drake’s woeful expression to his love interest (“To save you, instead they say”) seamlessly answers itself using Gil Scott-Heron’s sample:\n\nDon’t, tell me, I don’t care if you hurt\nI don’t, tell you, you don’t care if you’re true\nDon’t, tell me, I don’t care if you hurt\nI don’t, tell you, you don’t care if you’re true", "annotation_id": "25015267", "url": "https://genius.com/25015267", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "They won’t get you like I will, my only wish is I die real", "vault_lines": ["They won't get you like I will, my only wish is I die real"], "meaning": "Nobody can understand this girl as well as Drake can. They have shared secrets about themselves and their past which nobody else knows about.\nIf Rihanna is the subject of this song, then this line becomes even more significant as only another artist, like Drake, could comprehend the ordeals which come with celebrity.\n\nAlthough there are cases where Drake does struggle to read his woman, such as in “Too Good”:\n\nLook…I don’t know how to talk to you\nI don’t know how to ask you if you’re okay\n\nInterestingly enough, “Too Good” also features Rihanna in it… coincidence?", "annotation_id": "9112818", "url": "https://genius.com/9112818", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "’Cause that truth hurts and those lies heal And you can’t sleep thinkin’ that he lies still", "vault_lines": ["'Cause that truth hurts and those lies heal", "And you can't sleep thinkin' that he lies still"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t want to lie to this girl, but if he tells her the truth it will kill her. Because of his lies, Drake can’t sleep.\n\nHere, Drake plays on the words “lies still”, serving as a double entendre:\n\nFirstly, and most obvious, Drake admits that he (the person he’s talking about) still is lying, making this girl unable to sleep.\n\nSecondly, she can’t sleep as she’s restless, while he is lying still, because he’s sound asleep.\n\nFurthermore, it’s also a play on the previous line. He wants to die real because if he tells her the truth, it’ll hurt her, whereas if he lies, it won’t make her feel as bad.", "annotation_id": "452652", "url": "https://genius.com/452652", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "Dealin’ with a heart that I didn’t break", "vault_lines": ["Dealin' with a heart that I didn't break"], "meaning": "This girl had her heart broken in a previous relationship; in the vein of the opening line, “I know you’ve been hurt by someone else.”\n\nIn this song, Drizzy is trying to capture how difficult it is to be in a relationship with someone with baggage.\n\nThis could be a reference to Chris Brown. Drake and Brown’s feud has not been a secret to the public, and it was arguably caused by a dispute over Ri-Ri. And also everybody knows about how Brown mistreated and abused Rihanna during their relationship, that had to do some damage to Rihanna’s emotions. So when Drizzy and Rihanna were romantically involved after that, he might’ve had to deal with some of the aftermath of Brown and Ri-Ri’s relationship.", "annotation_id": "442403", "url": "https://genius.com/442403", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "I know you’ve been hurt by someone else I can tell by the way you carry yourself If you let me, here’s what I’ll do I’ll take care of you (I’ll take, I’ll take) I’ve loved and I’ve lost", "vault_lines": ["I know you've been hurt by someone else", "I can tell by the way you carry yourself", "'Cause if you let me", "If you let me, here's what I'll do", "Here's what I'll do", "I'll take care of you (I'll take, I'll take)", "Don't, tell me, I don't care if you hurt", "I don't, tell you, you don't care if you're true", "I've loved and I've lost"], "meaning": "As mentioned in the song notes, this hook comes from the song “I’ll Take Care of You” by Brook Benton.  Here’s Bobby “Blue” Bland singing it in likely its earliest recorded version, from 1959:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RaaSKY1VYIs\n\n“I’ve loved and I’ve lost” is a twist on the well known saying: “it’s better to have loved and have lost than to have never loved at all”.", "annotation_id": "438343", "url": "https://genius.com/438343", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "So you cry still, tears all in the pillowcase Big girls all get a little taste, aw", "vault_lines": ["So you cry still, tears all in the pillowcase", "Big girls all get a little taste, aw"], "meaning": "A slight allusion to The Four Seasons' “Big Girls Don’t Cry” – Drake knows that even big girls seek refuge in the tears of childhood when their hearts are broken.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YVBvy2cc0Io\n\nFergie also has a song called “Big Girls Don’t Cry”.", "annotation_id": "435829", "url": "https://genius.com/435829", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "line": "It’s my birthday, I’ll get high if I want to", "vault_lines": ["It's my birthday, I'll get high if I want to"], "meaning": "Drake interpolates Lesley Gore’s 1963 track “It’s My Party,” where she says:\n\nIt’s my party, I’ll cry if I want to\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XsYJyVEUaC4\n\nIf you listen to the chorus of “It’s My Party,” you’ll see that Drake mimics its tone in this line and the next few lines.\n\nIt would be clever if he was taking this line from back in the day and using it as an excuse to blaze. However, he’s actually interpolating Fabolous’s line in “This is My Party” from his Street Dreams album (2003).\n\n\n\nTake Care (the album) was originally going to be released on Drake’s birthday (October 24th).", "annotation_id": "434917", "url": "https://genius.com/434917", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Take Care", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-take-care-lyrics", "genius_title": "Take Care"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "You got somebody other than me Don’t play the victim when you’re with me Free time is costing me more than it seems Sacrificing things", "vault_lines": ["You got somebody other than me", "Don't play the victim when you're with me", "Free time is costing me more than it seems"], "meaning": "Drake knows this girl has another man she’s seeing behind his back, but she still plays it off like Drake’s treating her badly and that she is the “victim” in the relationship.\n\n“Free time” is time to do whatever you want. In Drake’s case, it is time where he could be making music or performing, (therefore making money), but instead he’s dealing with her partner playing victim at something she doesn’t have the rights to.\n\nDrake’s time is incredibly valuable, especially considering people are willing to pay $1,000 to meet him during his Summer Sixteen tour.", "annotation_id": "9077367", "url": "https://genius.com/9077367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "Years go by too fast I can’t keep track How long did we last? I feel bad for asking It can’t end like this We gotta take time with this", "vault_lines": ["Years go by too fast", "I can't keep track", "How long did we last?", "I feel bad for asking", "It can't end like this", "We gotta take time with this"], "meaning": "This couple has been together for a while now; years apparently. However, not keeping track of the years they’ve been together shows the lack of care someone has for their relationship.\n\nAlternatively, in terms of music, Drake and Rihanna released their first collaboration, “What’s My Name?” in October 2010. It was after coming together on “Take Care” (November 2011) that the pair collaborated again on Rihanna’s “Work”, and now on this song, which marks their fourth collaboration.\n\nIt was a while between “Take Care” and “Work”, but hopefully Drake & Rih as collaborators can have a happy ending. The couple in the song might not, though.", "annotation_id": "9075536", "url": "https://genius.com/9075536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "And I wanna tell you my intentions I wanna do the things that I mention I wanna benefit from the friendship I wanna get the late night message from you, from you", "vault_lines": ["And I wanna tell you my intentions", "I wanna do the things that I mention", "I wanna benefit from the friendship", "I wanna get the late night message from you, from you"], "meaning": "The things Drake wants from the relationship are primarily sexual—he wants to “benefit from the friendship,” he wants her to hit him up for a late night booty call.\n\n(This might be why she feels she’s not getting across to him…)\n\n\n\nThe late night phone message is familiar territory for Drake—he’s often put phone calls (or texts) together with relationships in his music. A late night call was the centerpiece of his hit, “Hotline Bling.”", "annotation_id": "9074174", "url": "https://genius.com/9074174", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "Look, I don’t know how to talk to you I don’t know how to ask you if you’re okay", "vault_lines": ["Look, I don't know how to talk to you", "I don't know how to talk to you", "I don't know how to ask you if you're okay", "These days, I don't know how to talk to you"], "meaning": "Unaware on how to connect with his girl emotionally, Drake’s nervous to talk to her as he’s unsure what’s wrong.\n\nAs an artist that has tours frequently, it’s likely he feels this way because he’s not really there for her when she needs it.\n\nThis line itself is a way of asking if she’s okay—a device known as apophasis.\n\n He expressed similar sentiments on “Tuscan Leather,” where he spoke about Nicki Minaj:\n\nNot even talkin' to Nicki, communication is breakin'\nI dropped the ball on some personal shit, I need to embrace it", "annotation_id": "9072549", "url": "https://genius.com/9072549", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "Cock up yuh bumper, sit down pon it Let me see if this is something I can fix (Six)", "vault_lines": ["Cock up yuh bumper, sit down pon it", "Let me see if this is something I can fix (Six)", "Baby, cock up yuh bumper, sit down pon it"], "meaning": "This line is taken from Popcaan’s March 2014 song, “Love Yuh Bad”:\n\nBaby, cock up yuh bumper, sit down pon it\nGyal yo pum pum good and yuh fit\n\n“Cock up” is Jamaican Patois for propping up or sticking out something and “bumper” is Bajan slang for buttocks.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/nCaeioeKduE?t=36\n\nNotably, this song’s outro samples the chorus of “Love Yuh Bad.”", "annotation_id": "9072093", "url": "https://genius.com/9072093", "state": "accepted", "votes": 160, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "Lately, you just make me work too hard for you", "vault_lines": ["Lately, you just make me work too hard for you"], "meaning": "In their contentious relationship, Rihanna’s character feels like she’s putting in way too much work—and she’s getting sick of it.\n\nIt also might play into Rihanna and Drake’s previous duet—“Work”:\n\nWork, work, work, work, work, work\nHe said me haffi\nWork, work, work, work, work, work\n\nIn both songs, he’s making her put in work (although the meaning is more sexually charged in “Work,” and more relationship-focused here).", "annotation_id": "9071727", "url": "https://genius.com/9071727", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "Got me on flights overseas, and I still can’t get across to you", "vault_lines": ["Got me on flights overseas, and I still can't get across to you"], "meaning": "Even though her lover is flying her out to see him (taking him across the ocean), she can’t get across to him emotionally.\n\nThis plays into the theme of both lovers thinking they’re being too good to each other. He’s paying for overseas flights, which he feels is generous.\nShe’s spending all her time flying out there, while not getting enough of a connection in return.\n\nRihanna followed Drake around during the European leg of ‘Would You Like a Tour’ in 2014. She was spotted at about a dozen shows. Here she’s seen wearing Drake’s jacket at the airport after leaving him in London:\n\n\n\nThis also plays into a line from the Popcaan original, “Luv Yuh Bad,” that is sampled in the outro:\n\nPay fi yuh visa meck yo fly out regular\n\nHe’s paying for her visa (generous) but making her fly out to see him (putting a burden on her).", "annotation_id": "9071436", "url": "https://genius.com/9071436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "Gyal a you mi waan Pay fi yuh visa meck yo fly out regular Baby, cock up yuh bumper, sit down pon it Gyal yo pum pum good and yuh fit Mi wi give you everything weh deh in my wallet And in my pocket Cock up yuh bumper, sit down pon it Gyal yo pum pum good and yuh fit Mi wi give you everything weh deh in my wallet And in my pocket", "vault_lines": ["Cock up yuh bumper, sit down pon it", "Gyal a you mi waan", "Pay fi yuh visa meck yo fly out regular", "Baby, cock up yuh bumper, sit down pon it", "Gyal yo pum pum good and yuh fit", "Mi wi give you everything weh deh in my wallet", "And in my pocket"], "meaning": "Popcaan is speaking in Jamaican Patois. The English translation would be:\n\nGirl you are who I want\nPay for your visa, make you fly out regular\nBaby, cock up your bumper, sit down on it\nGirl your pum pum good and you’re fit\nI will give you everything that’s in my wallet\nAnd in my pocket\n\nPopcaan is the continuation of Drake’s character in this song. “Bumper” is slang for booty, while “pum pum” translates to vagina and “cock up” refers to doggystyle sex.  He’s willing to pay for her flights and please her with everything she desires, just so he can have sex with her.\n\nHowever, the generous offer isn’t enough. She wants something deeper than just sex, but it seems to be a lost cause for both of them.", "annotation_id": "9071260", "url": "https://genius.com/9071260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "[Outro: Popcaan]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a pitched-up sample from Popcaan’s ”Luv Yuh Bad”—a track that Drake also interpolates during his third verse.\n\n\n\nPopcaan is a Jamaican dancehall artist with growing crossover appeal. On the leaked version of “Controlla,” Popcaan was featured, but his verses were removed from the album version.", "annotation_id": "9070500", "url": "https://genius.com/9070500", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "line": "I’m too good to you I’m way too good to you You take my love for granted I just don’t understand it No, I’m too good to you I’m way too good to you You take my love for granted I just don’t understand it", "vault_lines": ["I'm too good to you", "I'm way too good to you", "You take my love for granted", "I just don't understand it", "No, I'm too good to you"], "meaning": "On “Diamonds Dancing,” Drake’s expressed similar sentiments:\n\nI’m too good for you, too good for you\n\nIn “Too Good” and “Diamonds Dancing,” Drake thinks he’s giving too much to an ungrateful lover—though this time he’s “too good to” her, instead of “too good for” her.", "annotation_id": "9070386", "url": "https://genius.com/9070386", "state": "accepted", "votes": 149, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Too Good", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 10, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-too-good-lyrics", "genius_title": "Too Good"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "The closest you gon’ get to this is a Pac t-shirt", "vault_lines": ["The closest you gon' get to this is a Pac t-shirt"], "meaning": "The closest the other person is gonna get to fame is by wearing a Pac or Tupac t-shirt. Which is a t-shirt with his face on it.", "annotation_id": "38430404", "url": "https://genius.com/38430404", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "When I say “For life,” y’all say “Forever” (Ever)", "vault_lines": ["When I say \"For life,\" y'all say \"Forever\" (Ever)"], "meaning": "This line references Drakes song “Forever”which is a posse cut with Kanye West lil Wanye and Eminem displaying one of their best performances, released a year prior.", "annotation_id": "32479270", "url": "https://genius.com/32479270", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Like for y’all to meet the love of my life, her name’s the game", "vault_lines": ["Like for y'all to meet the love of my life, her name's the game"], "meaning": "Staying true to the hustle Jeezy states he has no physical lover, and all his energy is focused on the “game”. Which refers to the game of life, his success story.", "annotation_id": "31350627", "url": "https://genius.com/31350627", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "I just really hope that (I just really hope that you’ll think of me) I just really hope that (You’ll think of me) I just really hope that (You’ll think of me) I’m doing my thang, I’m doing my thang, I’m doing my thang (’Cause I’m tryna be unforgettable)", "vault_lines": ["I just really hope that (I just really hope that you'll think of me)", "I'm doing my thang, I'm doing my thang, I'm doing my thang ('Cause I'm tryna be unforgettable)"], "meaning": "In a bittersweet tale, Drizzy loses a relationship but becomes increasingly famous. He copes with this feeling of sadness by trying to replace his love with success, praying the girl won’t forget about him- as that is when he truly loses her. He ultimately decides to pursue his career over his ex lover.", "annotation_id": "31350605", "url": "https://genius.com/31350605", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Never forgettin’ from where I came And no matter where I’m headed, I promise to stay the same, I’m just", "vault_lines": ["Never forgettin' from where I came", "And no matter where I'm headed, I promise to stay the same, I'm just"], "meaning": "Drizzy promises that the fame won’t change him, and that he will always rep Toronto. He in fact kept true to his promise, and continues to put Toronto on the map years later, mentioning it many times within his newest album “For all the Dogs” (2024)", "annotation_id": "31350583", "url": "https://genius.com/31350583", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "This might just be my realest flow ever", "vault_lines": ["This might just be my realest flow ever"], "meaning": "This is a nod to Drake who earlier in the song said\n\n“𝘛𝘩𝘪𝘴 𝘪𝘴 𝘳𝘦𝘢𝘭𝘭𝘺 𝘰𝘯𝘦 𝘰𝘧 𝘮𝘺 𝘥𝘶𝘮𝘣𝘦𝘴𝘵 𝘧𝘭𝘰𝘸𝘴 𝘦𝘷𝘦𝘳”", "annotation_id": "31143734", "url": "https://genius.com/31143734", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "I just really hope that (I just really hope that you’ll think of me) I just really hope that (You’ll think of me) I just really hope that (You’ll think of me) I’m doing my thang, I’m doing my thang, I’m doing my thang (’Cause I’m tryna be unforgettable)", "vault_lines": ["I just really hope that (I just really hope that you'll think of me)", "I'm doing my thang, I'm doing my thang, I'm doing my thang ('Cause I'm tryna be unforgettable)"], "meaning": "In regards of when it comes to who did their thing within a time period and beyond that Jeezy hopes they don’t forget who paved the way and so on. As well as drake!", "annotation_id": "29804648", "url": "https://genius.com/29804648", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "So I’m single", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Alluding to Lil Wayne’s 2010 song I’m Single on which Drake has a writing credit.", "annotation_id": "27654794", "url": "https://genius.com/27654794", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Pain hurts like a cut from a beautiful knife", "vault_lines": ["Pain hurts like a cut from a beautiful knife"], "meaning": "“Pain hurts like a cut from a beautiful knife”\n\nFrom this line alone we can understand that Drizzy may be talking about a failed relationship.  The use of “beautiful” in describing a knife, which cut him, could be an insinuation to a previous lover.  Previous lovers are beautiful, but like a knife- no matter how pretty something appears on the outside, it can always do damage.", "annotation_id": "26820034", "url": "https://genius.com/26820034", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Damn right, so fly, I need feathers (Woah)", "vault_lines": ["Damn right, so fly, I need feathers (Woah)"], "meaning": "“Fly” is slang for “cool”. Jeezy takes it literally by saying he needs feathers (found on most birds) because he is so fly.", "annotation_id": "18974466", "url": "https://genius.com/18974466", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Now my insides looking like a Snickers bar Yeah, I’m talkin’ caramel leathers", "vault_lines": ["Now my insides looking like a Snickers bar", "Yeah, I'm talkin' caramel leathers"], "meaning": "The inside of a snickers bar contains caramel, peanuts, and nougat.", "annotation_id": "18974447", "url": "https://genius.com/18974447", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "I mean paparazzi on me, tryin’ not to get caught out They always seem to catch me with the woman that I brought out", "vault_lines": ["They always seem to catch me with the woman that I brought out", "I mean paparazzi on me, tryin' not to get caught out"], "meaning": "The paparazzi is trying to get a picture of Drake with his girl.", "annotation_id": "18974438", "url": "https://genius.com/18974438", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "But I ain’t Santa, I got somethin’ for these bad bitches too", "vault_lines": ["But I ain't Santa, I got somethin' for these bad bitches too"], "meaning": "As the fairytale goes, Santa brings toys through the chimney on christmas for all the good children who stayed off the naughty list.\n\nHowever, you don’t need to be good to get with Drake. You just gotta be “bad” (sexy, beautiful, etc.)", "annotation_id": "16382448", "url": "https://genius.com/16382448", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "The good die young, so I’ma need a thug wife", "vault_lines": ["The good die young, so I'ma need a thug wife"], "meaning": "This is reminiscent of Aaliyah and the coincidence that Drake sampled Aaliyah’s vocals from At Your Best. Well, obviously Drake has made it clear that he has a thing for her, he mentioned that she has influenced most of his music.\n\nIn the article “Aaliyah: Angel So Fly” published in Fader, Missy Elliott said “She was always sweet and caring and compassionate. Just a good person.”\n\nAnyhow, Aaliyah died at a very young age, as in verse 2, “the good die young” and therefore he needs someone thug that won’t be sent to heaven too early. Aaliyah was a good person and many think of her as an angel.", "annotation_id": "13624095", "url": "https://genius.com/13624095", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Just know she right here on my hip, my beautiful wife", "vault_lines": ["Just know she right here on my hip, my beautiful wife"], "meaning": "Jeezy always stays strapped with his gun on his hip.\n\nThis line is reminiscent of 2Pac’s song, “Me and My Girlfriend” where Pac referred to his gun as his girlfriend throughout the track.", "annotation_id": "3222571", "url": "https://genius.com/3222571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Let me know, let me know Ah, let me know Let me know, let me know", "vault_lines": ["Let me know, let me know", "Ah, let me know"], "meaning": "Aaliyah’s “At Your Best” can be heard in the background at the start of the song.\n\nDrake has always had a thing for Aaliyah, as she influenced his content and lifestyle. He’s even went as far as to make a posthumous collaboration with her.", "annotation_id": "2730994", "url": "https://genius.com/2730994", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Know our jewelry probably louder than a car alarm (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Know our jewelry probably louder than a car alarm (Yeah)"], "meaning": "This lyric is reminiscent of a line he said on “24-23”\n\nSee I’m sitting here chilling\nWith this Roley on my arm\nWhat I paid for it, should’ve came with an alarm\n\nHowever, this time around it doesn’t need a car alarm since it’s louder (another term for brighter/flashier.)", "annotation_id": "1134548", "url": "https://genius.com/1134548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "And all the girls that played me, eat your motherfuckin’ heart out", "vault_lines": ["And all the girls that played me, eat your motherfuckin' heart out"], "meaning": "This line is to all of the girls that played (here are several definitions of what that means)  him. He doesn’t think that they deserve to love because of the things they did to him. Drake is still bitter because he had very strong feelings about these girls and they used him, didn’t care for him, etc.", "annotation_id": "911644", "url": "https://genius.com/911644", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "It’s Drizzy, always gone but never hard to find", "vault_lines": ["It's Drizzy, always gone but never hard to find"], "meaning": "Drake is always physically gone from piles of kush or cups of lean–or even both.\n\nHowever, he’s blown up so much recently that it’s not hard to find him playing shows near you or on a TV in your house, especially thanks to his Degrassi days.", "annotation_id": "520614", "url": "https://genius.com/520614", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "And since you can’t escape me, do I ever cross your mind?", "vault_lines": ["And since you can't escape me, do I ever cross your mind?"], "meaning": "Drake wants to know if this girl still thinks about him from time-to-time because he’s all over the radio, television, magazines etc. so its like he’s impossible to get away from.\n\nAlso a reference to Brian McKnight’s song Anytime", "annotation_id": "166148", "url": "https://genius.com/166148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "But I’m good, I know that niggas would kill for this lifestyle I’m lookin’ forward to the memories of right now", "vault_lines": ["But I'm good, I know that niggas would kill for this lifestyle", "I'm lookin' forward to the memories of right now"], "meaning": "Drake isn’t entirely satisfied with fame, but he tries not to complain because a) he knows there are people struggling that would kill to be as rich/famous as him, and b) he knows that in time the bad parts of fame will fade and he’ll only be left with the good memories.", "annotation_id": "57913", "url": "https://genius.com/57913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Because all this shit is new to me, I’m learnin’ to behave And still spendin’ every dollar they encourage me to save", "vault_lines": ["Because all this shit is new to me, I'm learnin' to behave", "And still spendin' every dollar they encourage me to save"], "meaning": "Fame isn’t entirely new to Drake – he experienced it to some degree while he was on Degrassi. But this level of fame is something Drake is struggling to adapt to, and something that threatens to bankrupt him a la MC Hammer if he doesn’t get his act together.", "annotation_id": "57521", "url": "https://genius.com/57521", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "I haven’t slept in days and me and my latest girl agreed to go our separate ways", "vault_lines": ["I haven't slept in days and me and my latest girl agreed to go our separate ways"], "meaning": "We all know Drake is a thoughtful dude, so it should come as no surprise that he loses sleep over a bad break-up.", "annotation_id": "57519", "url": "https://genius.com/57519", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "line": "Same shit in them streets, so what’s the dilly? Young done everything in the A but fuck Chilli (Haha)", "vault_lines": ["Same shit in them streets, so what's the dilly?", "Young done everything in the A but fuck Chilli (Haha)"], "meaning": "Jeezy has done everything there is to do in Atlanta except have sex with Chilli–of the popular 90’s girl band TLC\n\nThings To Do in Atlanta:", "annotation_id": "17619", "url": "https://genius.com/17619", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Unforgettable", "status": "done", "targets": 24, "clicked": 24, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-unforgettable-lyrics", "genius_title": "Unforgettable"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "[Bridge] Matter fact, everybody in this bitch scream out, “Fuck my ex” Yeah, fuck my ex Fuck my ex (Fuck my ex) Fuck my ex (Fuck my ex) Fuck my ex (Middle fingers up) Fuck my ex (’Boutta turn this bitch up) Fuck my ex Fuck my ex (I should drop a name) Fuck my ex (Drop a couple names) Fuck my ex (Since you want some fame) Fuck my ex (Yeah, pussy niggas that you turnin’ up with) Fuck my ex (In a whip, clownin’ on me, yeah) Fuck my ex (Fuck my ex) Fuck my ex (Yeah, fuck my ex) Fuck my ex (Fuck my ex) Fuck my ex (Woah, woah, woah) Fuck my ex (I can’t let it go) Fuck my ex (Payback a bitch) It’s fuck my ex (If you ain’t did this shit) Fuck my ex (You would’ve popped off, you would’ve said shit) Fuck my ex (You would’ve posted shit, I know your ass too well) Fuck my ex (Fuck my ex)", "vault_lines": ["Matter fact, everybody in this bitch scream out, \"Fuck my ex\"", "Yeah, fuck my ex", "Fuck my ex (Fuck my ex)", "It's fuck my ex (If you ain't did this shit)", "Fuck my ex, ayy, ayy, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah", "Fuck my ex, yeah, right, right, right"], "meaning": "The bridge begins like a crowd chant against an ex, but gradually turns into Drake emotionally spiraling in real time. The repeated “fuck my ex” shifts from confidence into obsession, especially with lines like “I can’t let it go,” which reveal he is still deeply affected by the breakup.\n\n“I should drop a name” and “Since you want some fame” suggest Drake feels his ex is using his status or attention for clout.\n\nAt the same time, “You would’ve posted shit, I know your ass too well” shows paranoia and familiarity, implying he expects public retaliation or online drama.\n\nThe repetition mirrors intoxication, fixation, and unresolved anger rather than genuine closure.", "annotation_id": "39785215", "url": "https://genius.com/39785215", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "Down bad boys, sad boys, I been representin’ since birth", "vault_lines": ["Down bad boys, sad boys, I been representin' since birth"], "meaning": "Drake embraces the emotional persona that has defined much of his music. “Sad boys” references his reputation for heartbreak, vulnerability, and introspective songwriting, while “down bad” ties into modern slang surrounding emotional struggle and desperation in relationships.\n\n“I been representin’ since birth” humorously suggests these traits have always been part of his identity.", "annotation_id": "39785198", "url": "https://genius.com/39785198", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "Screenshots solved plenty problems", "vault_lines": ["Screenshots solved plenty problems"], "meaning": "Drake references using screenshots as proof during conflicts, whether in relationships, friendships, or industry situations. In celebrity culture, screenshots often expose lies, protect reputations, or settle disputes.\n\nThe line suggests Drake keeps receipts and evidence strategically, preventing people from speaking negatively on his name or rewriting narratives about him publicly.", "annotation_id": "39785165", "url": "https://genius.com/39785165", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "Got more tracks than when a hair salon is busy", "vault_lines": ["Got more tracks than when a hair salon is busy"], "meaning": "Drake uses a simple double meaning here. “Tracks” refers to both songs and hair tracks, the extensions and weave pieces commonly installed in hair salons.\n\nBy saying he has “more tracks than when a hair salon is busy,” Drake boasts about the sheer volume of music he makes and releases. The comparison exaggerates his output by likening it to a packed salon full of hair extensions being installed all day.\n\nThe bar also fits the context of the verse, where Drake stacks flex after flex about dominance and consistency:\n\nMy tour route’ll leave a lazy nigga dizzy\nGot more tracks than when a hair salon is busy", "annotation_id": "39785136", "url": "https://genius.com/39785136", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "My tour rider leave a lazy nigga dizzy", "vault_lines": ["My tour rider leave a lazy nigga dizzy"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to his hectic previous world tour “ It’s all a blur” with 21 savage which continually will see the duo perform from July 5th 2023 to March 27th 2024.\n\nThis is Drakes first tour in over five years which consists over 70 shows across America and Canada.", "annotation_id": "30563609", "url": "https://genius.com/30563609", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "She got a Jung body", "vault_lines": ["And lil' baby bad, she got a Jung body"], "meaning": "He says she got a Jung body in reference to Dr. Calvin Jung who is a renowned BBL specialist.", "annotation_id": "30168362", "url": "https://genius.com/30168362", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "Huh? Yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "OVO signee Smiley’s signature ad-libs.", "annotation_id": "30113454", "url": "https://genius.com/30113454", "state": "pending", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "Freaky nigga like I sing with Pretty Ricky", "vault_lines": ["Freaky nigga like I sing with Pretty Ricky"], "meaning": "Pretty Ricky is an American R&B / hip hop group originating from Miami, Florida. The group consists of members Baby Blue, Slick ‘Em and Pleasure P.\n\nTheir songs lyrical contents are often filled with vulgar sexual references towards women, hence Drake’s line “Freaky n***a like I sing with Pretty Ricky”", "annotation_id": "30113415", "url": "https://genius.com/30113415", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "I put careers on shelves like Amazon distribution center help", "vault_lines": ["I put careers on shelves like Amazon distribution center help"], "meaning": "Drake writes on how he’s helped to put many artists into the rap scene’s spotlight by featuring them / being featured on their songs.\n\nHe compares this to an Amazon employs that stacks goods onto shelves and says he’s put rappers into a higher position then they could’ve put themself.", "annotation_id": "30113294", "url": "https://genius.com/30113294", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "My notepad caught many bodies", "vault_lines": ["My notepad caught many bodies"], "meaning": "To catch a body is to kill or have killed someone. Whether that be physically with a weapon or to kill someone’s career by outrapping them so badly, Drake has caught bodies.\n\nAdditionally, Drake wrote a line mentioning notepads on his second single, 8am in Charlotte, leading up to his album For All The Dogs, that wrote:\n\nShania Twain, notepad, I’m makin' it line-dance\n\nThis ties back into an additional idea of people often naming their sexual partners (bodies) in their notes app or on a notepad to keep track of their sexual history.", "annotation_id": "30113194", "url": "https://genius.com/30113194", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "Oh, baby, don’t go Don’t go", "vault_lines": ["Oh, baby, don't go"], "meaning": "These sampled vocals come from a live version of Major Harris’s “I Got Over Love,” which was famously sampled by Just Blaze for 2003 track “I Really Meant It” from The Diplomats' album Diplomatic Immunity.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/AbH6cd-iAgg?si=UodTFMpZI2IMmj4J\n\nDrake evoked the Diplomats on the original Scary Hours in 2018 with the track “Diplomatic Immunity.”", "annotation_id": "30111606", "url": "https://genius.com/30111606", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "People love to diss me, I hear Every single thing, man, I’m all ears like I took the fam to Disney", "vault_lines": ["People love to diss me, I hear", "Every single thing, man, I'm all ears like I took the fam to Disney"], "meaning": "One of the most popular pieces of Disney merchandise is the classic ear headband based on the iconic shape of Mickey and Minnie Mouse’s ears. Headbands are frequently purchased by tourists as a souvenir of their trip to Disney World. As of November 2023, Disney had released over eighty different designs for their ear headbands in 2023 alone.\n\nA Disney ear headband based on the design of Minnie Mouse\n\nThe saying “I’m all ears” means that one is listening closely and carefully, eager to pick up on every detail they hear. Drake describes himself as all ears to the various disses he receives, essentially saying that while he may not reply to each one, he is keen to take note of those who speak ill of him and is unlikely to forget.", "annotation_id": "30105523", "url": "https://genius.com/30105523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "Got the ad-libs from Smiggy", "vault_lines": ["Got the ad-libs from Smiggy"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out his friend and track feature, Smiley. It was revealed in a Bobbi Althoff interview that Drake also calls him Smiggs or Smiggy.\n\nSmiley signed with Drake’s label OVO in 2021. They’ve since collaborated on tracks such as “Over the Top”", "annotation_id": "30101201", "url": "https://genius.com/30101201", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "line": "Pushed to the edge like Vert", "vault_lines": ["Pushed to the edge like Vert"], "meaning": "In conveying negative sentiments regarding his relationship with an unnamed ex-girlfriend, Drake references Philadelphia rapper Lil Uzi Vert’s February 2017 hit song “XO Tour Llif3,” during the song’s chorus, Uzi raps:\n\nPush me to the edge\nAll my friends are dead\n\nThis line conveys their solitary mindset and expresses that a large level of emotional distance exists between them and their social circle, while also stating that the only thing that makes them satisfied is the money, or “dead presidents,” which they have accumulated throughout their rap career.\n\nIn Drake’s case, however, the usage of this line takes on a different meaning. In being “pushed to the edge”, Drake implies that he tried to continue loving his ex-girlfriend and made an attempt to make their relationship work despite her actions, but the inclusion of the previous two lines signifies that Drake’s efforts could not preserve the relationship and that his commitment only led to heartbreak.\n\nUzi (left) & Drake (right)", "annotation_id": "30101056", "url": "https://genius.com/30101056", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You Broke My Heart", "status": "done", "targets": 14, "clicked": 14, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-you-broke-my-heart-lyrics", "genius_title": "You Broke My Heart"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "line": "You ask how many girls I been with in my life I tell ya, “I stopped keeping count when I was 25” I ask you the same thing, you tell me, “Seven guys” But we both know that’s a lie", "vault_lines": ["You ask how many girls I been with in my life", "I tell ya, \"I stopped keeping count when I was 25\"", "I ask you the same thing, you tell me, \"Seven guys\"", "But we both know that's a lie"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "28288307", "url": "https://genius.com/28288307", "state": "pending", "votes": -5, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "line": "I like when it’s you and I Plus I always feel like if somebody outperforms you That could jeopardize your pride and I’m not into choosin’ sides Unless you ‘bout to sleep and you askin’ me what’s my side It’s the right at home, but this a hotel so you can decide Lessons that I learned before hittin’ my prime Confessions that I blame on the zodiac sign Things you need to hear ’fore we say our goodbyes", "vault_lines": ["I like when it's you and I", "Plus I always feel like if somebody outperforms you", "That could jeopardize your pride and I'm not into choosin' sides", "Unless you 'bout to sleep and you askin' me what's my side", "It's the right at home, but this a hotel so you can decide", "Lessons that I learned before hittin' my prime", "Confessions that I blame on the zodiac sign", "Things you need to hear 'fore we say our goodbyes", "Like you want me to lie"], "meaning": "The lyrics above explore the idea of rivalry and competition, specifically in the context of relationships. Drake observes that when someone outperforms another person, this can lead to feelings of jealousy and insecurity. He also notes that this can create a rift between people, as one person may choose to support the individual who is outperforming them, while the other person may feel protective of their pride. Ultimately, Drake suggests that it is better to avoid choosing sides in these situations and allow each person to make their own decisions.\n\nThe lyrics also touch on the idea of personal growth and development. Drake reflects on how they have learned from their past experiences and grown as a result. He also suggest that this growth process is not linear and often involves setbacks and struggles. Finally, the songwriter hints at an upcoming transformation or rebirth, symbolized by the Scorpio rising.", "annotation_id": "26476474", "url": "https://genius.com/26476474", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "line": "Had a lot of backs that I would never talk behind I’m not really into threesomes, I like one thing at a time", "vault_lines": ["I had a lot of backs that I would never talk behind", "I'm not really into threesomes, I like one thing at a time"], "meaning": "it’s dope", "annotation_id": "22337970", "url": "https://genius.com/22337970", "state": "pending", "votes": -8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "line": "Marcus prolly had to bring me straps like thirty times", "vault_lines": ["Marcus prolly had to bring me straps like thirty times"], "meaning": "For anyone wondering Drizzy is not talking about guns in this line, the meaning of “straps” in this context is actually condoms, as in to strap up for sex.\n\nIn the line Drake is saying that his personal assistant Mark Robinson A.K.A Marcus, has had to bring him condoms bout thirty times while he has been working for him since 2016.", "annotation_id": "21541810", "url": "https://genius.com/21541810", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "line": "Free myself of it all, watch a Scorpio rise", "vault_lines": ["Free myself of it all, watch a Scorpio rise"], "meaning": "Drake was born on October 24th, 1986. The zodiac sign of those born on or between October 22nd and November 21st is Scorpio. Drake’s 2018 album, Scoprion, also pays homage to his Zodiac Sign.", "annotation_id": "21182701", "url": "https://genius.com/21182701", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "line": "’Cause then I switch up and I blame it on my Gemini", "vault_lines": ["'Cause then I switch up and I blame it on my Gemini"], "meaning": "The zodiac sign of those born on or between May 20th and June 20th is Gemini. Jessie Reyez was born on June 12, 1991.\n\nGemini are known to be “two-faced” in astrology, based on their symbol which depicts twins. Because of their “two sides,” Geminis can seem like they have different sides to them, as they often switch their personality based on the context and situation they are in.", "annotation_id": "21182680", "url": "https://genius.com/21182680", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "line": "And lately, papi, you been acting tonto", "vault_lines": ["And lately, papi, you been acting tonto"], "meaning": "Reyez’s use of “papi” is probably a reference to Drake’s Instagram tag, “@champangepapi.” Additionally,  “Tonto” is Spanish for “crazy” or “stupid.”", "annotation_id": "21182634", "url": "https://genius.com/21182634", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "line": "He took over like four years ago when Courtnee had resigned", "vault_lines": ["He took over like four years ago when Courtnee had resigned"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his previous personal assistant Courtne Smith. She cited the reasons for her resignation as follows:\n\n“After many years of observation, I felt confident enough to take the first step towards launching my own venture.”", "annotation_id": "19363152", "url": "https://genius.com/19363152", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "line": "And if it’s LOV and you’re my MVP Then why we talkin’ about practice?", "vault_lines": ["And if it's LOV and you're my MVP", "Then why we talkin' about practice?"], "meaning": "Jessie is referring to Allen Iverson, a former NBA player for the Philadelphia 76ers. Iverson won the league’s MVP (Most Valuable Player) award in 2001. A frustrated Iverson went on a famous 2002 press conference rant, where he exclaimed “we talking about practice” twenty times.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/tknXRyUEJtU", "annotation_id": "19354703", "url": "https://genius.com/19354703", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zodiac Sign", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 9, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-zodiac-sign-lyrics", "genius_title": "Zodiac Sign"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "I got a whole ’nother level that I can tap into", "vault_lines": ["I got a whole 'nother level that I can tap into"], "meaning": "This line encapsulates Drake’s confidence in his untapped potential and his strategic restraint. This might be particularly referencing Drake’s decision to hold back on releasing an explosive diss track against Pusha T and Kanye West in response to the former’s “story of addidon,” a move influenced by J. Prince’s advice to avoid unnecessary escalation to the feud.\n\nJ. Prince, the CEO of Rap-A-Lot Records who has often been associated with having strong connections to the street and underground networks, is significant mentor figure to Drake.\n\nIn a Interview with Rap Radar, Drake would speak about the beef with Pusha T and would make a mention of the unreleased diss track.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/Xcta-ROw8JM?si=Z44kNfycptbajgZv&t=90\n\nFurthermore, J. Prince would speak up on the track in interviews describing it as “career-ending” and praising Drake for exercising restraint and not escalating the feud to a point of no return. In his book The Art & Science of Respect, J. Prince would elaborate more on advising Drake against releasing the track.\n\nDrake would subtly reference the contemplation regarding shelfing the diss record on his track “Survival”:\n\nYeah, I was about to— Man, I thought about it\nIt’s unsettlin’ to talk about it\n\nand on “Omertà”:\n\n Man, truth be told, I think about it often", "annotation_id": "32403553", "url": "https://genius.com/32403553", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "They been keepin’ tabs on me like I’m payin’ next time I think I sense a little fear from the other side", "vault_lines": ["They been keepin' tabs on me like I'm payin' next time", "I think I sense a little fear from the other side"], "meaning": "Drake is basically saying he feels like he’s always under a microscope, with people just waiting for him to mess up or somehow owe them. You know how if you’re at a restaurant and someone covers the bill, but they’re keeping track of everything you order because they know next time it’ll be their turn.\n\nWhen Drake says this, he’s likely throwing shade at Kanye West and Pusha T. These lines  in the second bar would come to fruition as back in December 2018, Kanye went off on Twitter, accusing Drake of threatening his family, which was months after Drake’s album Scorpion dropped.", "annotation_id": "32403467", "url": "https://genius.com/32403467", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Miss makin’ ’em pay", "vault_lines": ["Miss makin' 'em pay"], "meaning": "In terms of his beef with Pusha T, Drake has opted to become the bigger person and let the beef go as the response would have been considered “more fuel for the fire.”\n\nBecause Drake has elected to let it go, he reminisces about his old attitude which involves responding to disses in a quite ruthless manner much like he did with his Meek Mill beef on the 2015 diss tracks: “Charged Up” and “Back To Back”.", "annotation_id": "14952607", "url": "https://genius.com/14952607", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "As luck would have it, I’ve settled into my role as the good guy I guess luck is on your side I guess luck is on your side", "vault_lines": ["As luck would have it, I've settled into my role as the good guy", "I guess luck is on your side"], "meaning": "Since the release of “Infared”, “Duppy Freestyle”, and “The Story of Adidon”, fans and others have been avidly awaiting a response track from Drake. In an interview with Sway in the Morning, J. Prince confirmed that Drake is apparently sitting on a career ending Pusha T and Kanye diss track. Rather than release the track, however, Drake has opted to be the bigger person and let the beef go as the response would have been more fuel for the fire. In the interview, Prince stated:\n\nAfter speaking with Kanye, I spoke with the brother, and he didn’t want this. I saw this going to a place that I feel would have ended his career if Drake would’ve put out this song that he had on him. And definitely would’ve hurt families and we’re not in it for that. That’s not Drake’s character to tear a man down to that extent. It just wouldn’t be good. It’s beyond music at that point. It’s going to affect the livelihood. It’s going to interfere with his whole lifestyle from that moment.", "annotation_id": "14912354", "url": "https://genius.com/14912354", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Kiss my mom on the forehead then kiss your ass goodbye", "vault_lines": ["Kiss my son on the forehead then kiss your ass goodbye", "Kiss my mom on the forehead then kiss your ass goodbye"], "meaning": "Drake has a very close relationship with his mom, Sandi Graham. In the past, he’s referred to himself as a “momma’s boy.” Drake frequently mentions his mom in his songs, and even dedicated his 2015 track “You & The 6” to her.\n\nThis line is very reminiscent of a famous couplet from “God’s Plan”, where Drake expresses his ultimate care and love for his mom over anyone else:\n\nShe said, “Do you love me?” I tell her, “Only partly”\nI only love my bed and my mama, I’m sorry", "annotation_id": "14912326", "url": "https://genius.com/14912326", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Drizzy ’bout to drop, the game is in disarray I’d tell you hear me out but we both know end of the day", "vault_lines": ["Drizzy 'bout to drop, the game is in disarray", "I'd tell you hear me out but we both know end of the day"], "meaning": "While countless quarrels erupt in the rap community, most famously between Drake and Pusha-T along with Kanye, Drake opts to keep his cool and focus on the release of his highly anticipated album Scorpion. Since the release of “Infared”, “Duppy Freestyle”, and “The Story of Adidon”, rather than publish a direct response track Drake chose to integrate lines throughout the album.\n\nDrizzy is able to effectively respond in this way because of his influential authority in music. Since 2009, Drake has had a song in the Billboard Top 100 every week. On top of that, just 24 hours after its release, Scorpion had more than 170 million streams worldwide on Apple Music–Pusha-T is not nearly as popular.\n\nDrake also addresses his versatility as an artist, being both a rapper and singer, and the wide variety of listeners he is able to influence. In a 2012 interview with NME, he stated boastfully:\n\nI’m the first person to successfully rap and sing", "annotation_id": "14912258", "url": "https://genius.com/14912258", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "I gotta breathe real deep when I catch an attitude", "vault_lines": ["I gotta breathe real deep when I catch an attitude"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing the beginning of his response track to Pusha T’s, “Infrared”, where Pusha raps:\n\nHow could you ever right these wrongs\nWhen you don’t even write your songs?\n\nIn this response track titled, “Duppy Freestyle”, he starts the track off by breathing deep and sighing.", "annotation_id": "14912194", "url": "https://genius.com/14912194", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "1da sent this in, I’m goin’ up all the way", "vault_lines": ["1da sent this in, I'm goin' up all the way"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out the producer of this song, Boi-1da. He has made beats for Drake on many hits like “God’s Plan,” “Know Yourself,” “Energy,” Rihanna’s “Work,” and much more.", "annotation_id": "14891111", "url": "https://genius.com/14891111", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Three Forum shows but I played Staples today", "vault_lines": ["Three Forum shows but I played Staples today"], "meaning": "The Forum is an indoor arena in Inglewood, California which is approximately twelve miles from Los Angeles, California. Staples Center is located in Los Angeles, California and is home to the likes of the NBA’s Los Angeles Lakers and Los Angeles Clippers along with the NHL’s Los Angeles Kings.\n\nDrake has three shows scheduled at The Forum in 2018 as part of the “Aubrey and The Three Migos Tour” between October 16th and the 19th. Prior to his three shows at The Forum, he has three shows at Staples Center back to back between October 12th and the 14th.", "annotation_id": "14889033", "url": "https://genius.com/14889033", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Max said they only blessed when they attached to you", "vault_lines": ["Max said they only blessed when they attached to you"], "meaning": "Drake references his friend and Hawaiian UFC Featherweight champion Max “Blessed” Holloway in this line, before his July 7, 2018 fight against Brian Ortega at UFC 226.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/blessedmma/status/1012803818478657536?s=21\n\nAs part of the build up for his fight, Holloway released a mock-up of Scorpion’s album cover featuring his face, signature and later a “tracklist” of the fight card for that night.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/ufc/status/1013082263754608640?s=21\n\nHolloway earned the UFC Interim Featherweight Title in Drake’s hometown, Toronto while coming out to Drake’s music. He later defended his title in Detroit coming out to the Big Sean’s collaboration with Drake, “Blessings”. He routinely reps Toronto and quotes Drake on Twitter and Instagram like in this tweet;\n\nhttps://twitter.com/blessedmma/status/1012806767711932417?s=21", "annotation_id": "14885555", "url": "https://genius.com/14885555", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "If shit was at a eight, we like to, we like to, we like to— Yeah, if shit was at a eight, we like to make it ten", "vault_lines": ["If shit was at a eight, we like to, we like to, we like to—", "Yeah, if shit was at a eight, we like to make it ten"], "meaning": "Drake asserts early on that he would typically respond to heat with the highest intensity. This can be seen in the manner in which he fired back in response to Pusha T’s diss-track “Infrared” with “Duppy Freestyle”, in which he intensifies the beef, by not only going after Kanye West, who produced the beat, but introducing personal attacks involving Pusha T’s family members.\n\nAfter Pusha T retaliated with an even harder-hitting track, “The Story of Adidon”, Drake showed no indication of releasing another diss-track. However J. Prince revealed to Ebro that Drake was preparing a diss-track, that in J. Prince’s opinion would have potentially hurt Kanye West’s life and ended his career, before being advised not to follow through with it.\n\nBy naming the song “8 Out of 10”, Drake is implying that he could have easily made the song a diss-track to Pusha T to the highest degree, but intentionally dialled the heat back down, so that the beef could come to an end.", "annotation_id": "14882599", "url": "https://genius.com/14882599", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "I don’t like to talk when there’s nothin’ else left to say (True)", "vault_lines": ["I don't like to talk when there's nothin' else left to say (True)"], "meaning": "Drake is giving his response to the public’s expectations for him to retaliate in response to Pusha T’s diss-track, “The Story of Adidon”, the last release in the series of diss-tracks between the two rappers. Drake is asserting his decision not to retaliate since he has already addressed his initial beef with Pusha. Given the personal backlash of the diss-tracks by both artists, it is reasonable to assume a retaliation would do more worse than good.", "annotation_id": "14881651", "url": "https://genius.com/14881651", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Who grips the mic and likes to kill they friends?", "vault_lines": ["Who grips the mic and likes to kill they friends?"], "meaning": "Drake criticizes Kanye’s tendency of ranting about fellow musicians onstage. Most notably, during his Sacramento Show on the 2016 Saint Pablo Tour, Kanye went on a full-blown 15-minute rant about Jay-Z, Beyonce, DJ Khaled and a list of others. He began the rant by criticizing radio stations for taking money in exchange for overplaying the same songs before he withdraws and says,\n\nBut we love Drake, Drake is great.\n\nAt the time, Drake and Kanye were on good terms and rumored to be working on a joint album together.", "annotation_id": "14881223", "url": "https://genius.com/14881223", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Your sister is pressin’ play, your trainer is pressin’ play Your wifey, your wifey, your wifey, your wifey— (True)", "vault_lines": ["Your sister is pressin' play, your trainer is pressin' play", "Your wifey, your wifey, your wifey, your wifey— (True)", "Your niggas is pressin' play, your nanny is pressin' play"], "meaning": "In contrast to many other rappers, he has an ongoing popularity with female listeners. The chorus may be reminiscent of “Best I Ever Had”:\n\nWhen my album drop, bitches’ll buy it for the picture\nAnd niggas’ll buy it too and claim they got it for they sister\n\nThe vocal pattern of the repeated “your wifey” may also recall that of the repeated vocal sample from Kanye’s “Runaway”–on the track, he speaks openly about his poor treatment of women and his troubled personality. Pusha-T also features on the track, and in a conflicting message with that of the song, speaks defiantly on the power his wealth and fame give him to act as he pleases with women.\n\nIn light of the negative treatment of women espoused and confessed by two of his rivals, Drake takes a shot at both of them by emphasizing his continued popularity amongst women.", "annotation_id": "14881174", "url": "https://genius.com/14881174", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Too rich for who? Y’all just got rich again", "vault_lines": ["Too rich for who? Y'all just got rich again"], "meaning": "On “No Mistakes,” Kanye rapped:\n\nToo close to snipe you, truth told, I like you\nToo bold to type you, too rich to fight you\nCalm down, you light skin!\n\nIn response, Drake references Kanye’s own admission that he was upwards of $53 million in debt.\n\nOnly now after the continued success of Kanye’s Adidas lines, help from Kim, and the release of multiple projects is Kanye rich enough to check Drake on his wealth.", "annotation_id": "14879576", "url": "https://genius.com/14879576", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "And the product is still the best though It’s only good in my city because I said so Just to fit the estate man, I had to Copperfield like presto Voilà, tada (True)", "vault_lines": ["And the product is still the best though", "It's only good in my city because I said so", "Just to fit the estate man, I had to Copperfield like presto"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the connection with real estate and “to cop a field” (buying more property or land to fit his estate on) by namedropping the famous, high-grossing magician David Copperfield.\n“Presto”, “voila” and “tada” are all expressions used in magic to indicate the sudden appearance or occurrence of something. Presto is also the name of the public transit pass in Toronto, Drizzy’s hometown.\n\nDrake suggests that by repping his hometown he improves the value of real estate. Furthermore, to fit with the previous lines, Drake fires a shot at “G.O.O.D.” Music label owner Kanye West and signed-rapper Pusha-T, following the release of “The Story of Adidon”.\n\nAdditionally, it could also be a reference to the 40,000-square-foot estate Drake is building in Toronto, of which the property alone was already worth 6.7 million dollars.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BRy8kzolj_1/?utm_source=ig_embed", "annotation_id": "14877477", "url": "https://genius.com/14877477", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Helipad from Will Smith crib straight to the stage", "vault_lines": ["Helipad from Will Smith crib straight to the stage"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Drake taking a helicopter from famous actor and rapper, Will Smith’s property to perform on stage at The Forum and the Staples Center, both stadiums in California.\n\nThis could call back to a line made by Drake’s rival, Pusha T on “Infrared” which Drizzy flips to make a humble brag about his connection to the now legendary actor.\n\nShit, remember Will Smith won the first Grammy?", "annotation_id": "14877467", "url": "https://genius.com/14877467", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "Kiss my son on the forehead then kiss your ass goodbye As luck would have it, I’ve settled into my role as the good guy", "vault_lines": ["Kiss my son on the forehead then kiss your ass goodbye", "As luck would have it, I've settled into my role as the good guy", "Kiss my mom on the forehead then kiss your ass goodbye"], "meaning": "This line refers to Drake’s supposedly abandoned son, as alleged by Pusha T in “The Story of Adidon”:\n\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\nAdonis is your son\nAnd he deserves more than an Adidas press run, that’s real\nLove that baby, respect that girl\n\nPusha T accused Drake of being the father of an abandoned child with former porn-star Sophie Brussaux, and that Drake should reconnect with his son and be the father his son deserves.\n\nIn Drake’s response here he claims that actually, he does take care of his son, and is involved in his life; a sentiment he also delivers in “Emotionless” and “March 14th.”\n\nThe second part of the lyric references the supposed truce between Pusha T and Drake, pushed by both J Prince and Kanye as mediators on Twitter.\n\nPusha is lucky that Drake is sticking to his nice guy reputation and avoids going into the ‘mud’ with Pusha by responding to him on a personal level.\n\nThese lines are also likely a reference to “Long Kiss Goodnight”, the highly-regarded diss track by The Notorious B.I.G against rival 2Pac. Drake credited the former’s album Life after Death – the album on which “Long Kiss Goodnight” appears – as being an inspiration for Scorpion.", "annotation_id": "14877387", "url": "https://genius.com/14877387", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "[Outro: Plies] Hahaha, I can’t argue with you, no! You mad! Look at you, you mad! You big mad! Haheheh I’m happy! Leave me alone! I just want some money, a lot of money I don’t get paid to argue with you No! Who is you? You ain’t nobody! You mad", "vault_lines": ["Hahaha, I can't argue with you, no! You mad!", "Look at you, you mad! You big mad! Haheheh", "I'm happy! Leave me alone!", "I just want some money, a lot of money", "I don't get paid to argue with you", "No! Who is you? You ain't nobody!"], "meaning": "This outro is a sample of a video by Plies from November of 2017 that became a meme. It was made in reference to a woman that tried to argue with him.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/plies/status/928307576957276160", "annotation_id": "14877252", "url": "https://genius.com/14877252", "state": "accepted", "votes": 175, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "I been on top for three sets of three years", "vault_lines": ["I been on top for three sets of three years"], "meaning": "As far as Drake himself is concerned, his run started in 2009, the year he released So Far Gone.\n\nHe referenced being on top of the game since 2009 on “Pop Style” as well:\n\nMVP, MVP, oh-nine all the way to sixteen\nEven next season looking like a breeze\nLot of y'all ain’t built for the league, yeah\n\nThis is almost certainly a reference to Ice Cube’s statement about only getting three years at the top of the rap game. Drake claims to have been on top for three times that time period.\n\nScorpion is also Drake’s third album in the last 3 years, following Views in 2016 and More Life in 2017.", "annotation_id": "14877081", "url": "https://genius.com/14877081", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "I guess luck is on your side I guess luck is on your side All sevens, no sixes, rest easy, get some shut eye", "vault_lines": ["I guess luck is on your side", "All sevens, no sixes, rest easy, get some shut eye"], "meaning": "Drake is mentioning the Surgical Summer streak of albums that Kanye West has had his hand in producing:\n\n\nPusha T – Daytona\nKanye West – Ye\nKIDS SEE GHOSTS – KIDS SEE GHOSTS\nNas – Nasir\n\n\n\nAll of the aforementioned albums had tracklists consisting of seven songs. Additionally, seven is considered a lucky number by many cultures around the world, which ties into the lines “luck is on your side.”\n\nDrake is sometimes referred to as “6 God”, based off a song from If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late.\n\nIt’s also possible these lines are alluding to the seven deadly sins. The seventh sin is pride and the sixth is wrath, a sense of being vindictive. Kanye West tweeted a screenshot of the seven sins prior to the release of Scorpion.", "annotation_id": "14876307", "url": "https://genius.com/14876307", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "8 Out of 10", "line": "The only deadbeats is whatever beats I been rappin’ to (True)", "vault_lines": ["The only deadbeats is whatever beats I been rappin' to (True)"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Pusha T’s diss track aimed at Drake, “The Story of Adidon,” where Pusha called Drake a deadbeat father:\n\nDeadbeat mothafucka playin' border patrol, ooh\n\nDrake is saying he’s not a deadbeat father, instead, saying that he is killing every beat that he raps on.\n\nAlso, on Pusha T’s diss track, Pusha comes at Drakes producer, Noah “40” Shebib:\n\nOvO 40 hunched over like he 80, tick tick tick\nHow much time is left, that man is sick sick sick", "annotation_id": "14876242", "url": "https://genius.com/14876242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
