{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bad Meaning Good", "line": "Streets are seedy, I call ’em sesame, it’s Ernie and Bert’s town", "vault_lines": ["Streets are seedy, I call 'em sesame, it's Ernie and Bert's town"], "meaning": "Drake puns on the meaning of “seedy” (shady/clandestine) by referencing Sesame Street, the kid’s TV show which contains the characters Ernie and Bert.", "annotation_id": "3467790", "url": "https://genius.com/3467790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bad Meaning Good", "line": "Not bad meaning bad, but bad meaning good, good, good", "vault_lines": ["Not bad meaning bad, but bad meaning good, good, good", "Not bad meaning bad, but bad meaning good (Uh, uh, uh, uh)"], "meaning": "This is taken from Run DMC’s classic track “Peter Piper”. “Peter Piper” was one of the first songs that started the trend of referring to things that are awesome as “bad”.", "annotation_id": "3467784", "url": "https://genius.com/3467784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bad Meaning Good", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 2, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-bad-meaning-good-lyrics", "genius_title": "Bad Meaning Good"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Straps over territory", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Should be “scraps over territory,” i.e. fights over territory. This page has been locked by  Genius Staff which usually means the lyrics will never be fixed.", "annotation_id": "39790958", "url": "https://genius.com/39790958", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Summertime heatwaves, I used to just cut sleeves", "vault_lines": ["Summertime heatwaves, I used to just cut sleeves"], "meaning": "Drake used to cut the sleeves off of his shirts to make tanktops or shortsleeve tees as opposed to wearing longsleeves", "annotation_id": "32415706", "url": "https://genius.com/32415706", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Yeah, then we rollin’, then we rollin’ Deep pockets on a nigga, I can’t find my phone in Now we rollin’ Yeah, then we rollin’, then we rollin’ Deep pockets on a nigga, I can’t find my phone in Now we rollin’", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, then we rollin', then we rollin'", "Deep pockets on a nigga, I can't find my phone in", "Then we rollin', then we rollin'"], "meaning": "“Deep Pockets” originally was titled “Rollin” due to the repetition of “rollin” throughout the chorus, but the term “deep pockets” (which refers to someone having a huge amount of money) was likely decided to fit this track better as a title as it describes Drake’s progression from being a broke youth to a millionaire songwriterr", "annotation_id": "32266342", "url": "https://genius.com/32266342", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "My shorty really love me, man, she want me to cut keys", "vault_lines": ["My shorty really love me, man, she want me to cut keys"], "meaning": "To cut keys either means to A: distribute kilos of cocaine or B: to make copies of a house key. It’s likely here Drake means B, his girl loves him so she wants a copy of the house key he has", "annotation_id": "32266320", "url": "https://genius.com/32266320", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Back when Jill Scott was the apple of my afrocentric eye", "vault_lines": ["Back when Jill Scott was the apple of my afrocentric eye"], "meaning": "Jill Scott was a Black female singer and a symbol of Black women’s voices in the early 2000’s\n\nhttps://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcQ7V6xy1R2y4WD8gNwSvet9UyzSMRaxRxp03w&s", "annotation_id": "32266297", "url": "https://genius.com/32266297", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Inspired by a few, but my mind really drives itself like Tesla", "vault_lines": ["Inspired by a few, but my mind really drives itself like Tesla"], "meaning": "Drake has never been shy to credit those who came before him, and has mentioned several artists who have influenced him, such as rappers Jay-Z and Lil Wayne, and R&B artists such as Usher and Aaliyah. However, Drake has stated on multiple occasions that now rival Kanye West has had the “biggest influence” on his sound.\n\nDespite the influences, Drake says it’s his creativity and own mind that has really driven himself to the heights of his success, likening this to Tesla’s Autopilot cars, which also drive themselves.", "annotation_id": "29859668", "url": "https://genius.com/29859668", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "And the car could get from A to B, but won’t impress ya", "vault_lines": ["And the car could get from A to B, but won't impress ya"], "meaning": "Here “impress ya” sounds like a reference to the Subaru Impreza which is a budget hatchback.\nHe also mentioned a Subaru Hatchback back in 2015 on the song “Know Yourself” in the line “That was when Ethan was pushin' a Subaru hatchback”", "annotation_id": "25594867", "url": "https://genius.com/25594867", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "My soundtrack is the second Carter, dreamin’ of acceptin’ offers", "vault_lines": ["My soundtrack is the second Carter, dreamin' of acceptin' offers"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the Lil Wayne album Tha Carter II.", "annotation_id": "23722234", "url": "https://genius.com/23722234", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "I can’t do that normal type of life, is it just me", "vault_lines": ["I can't do that normal type of life, is it just me"], "meaning": "Drake has been thrust into the spotlight for the majority of his life, making something such as eccentric as “cutting keys” i.e. being a cocaine dealer, seem “normal” to him or something much more simpler such as getting copies of his girlfriends house keys.", "annotation_id": "23223814", "url": "https://genius.com/23223814", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Back when Big Apple sold dreams, I stuck to my own thing", "vault_lines": ["Back when Big Apple sold dreams, I stuck to my own thing"], "meaning": "Big Apple was a Toronto area store owner and record promoter. He was originally from NYC thus earning him the name Big Apple, he made a lot of promises to Toronto area rappers Drake talks about him selling dreams to them.\n\nDrake previously makes mention of Big Apple on Views standout Weston Road Flows\n\n“Big Apple had the white Hummer parked right in front of Fluid”", "annotation_id": "19842942", "url": "https://genius.com/19842942", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Runnin’ round from Laurier to MacKenzie when the city’s empty, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Runnin' round from Laurier to MacKenzie when the city's empty, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake is talking about travelling between the east end of Toronto and the West end of Toronto late at night.\n\n“Laurier” and “MacKenzie” are two high schools in Toronto. Sir Wilfrid Laurier Collegiate Institute is in Scarborough at the east end of the city while William Lyon Mackenzie Collegiate Institute is in North York, more west end of the city. Back in 2011, Drake shot his music video for “Headlines” in the Guild Estate which is located beside Laurier.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/guildinnestate/status/725730215834447872?lang=en\n\nDrake moved into his 50,000 square feet mansion back in 2019 which is located in North York which could end his “round” at his house.\n\n\n\nToronto is a very densely populated city with bad traffic that congested during the day but empty late at night.\n\nIt is also possible that Drake is making a nod to Wilfrid Laurier University, a university located in Waterloo, Ontario. Laurier has been a big fan of Drake and the OVO Crew, with Drake featuring in their History of Rock Music course available for first-year students.\n\nIn 2018, labelmate NAV performed at Laurier’s O-Week Concert Night.", "annotation_id": "19734806", "url": "https://genius.com/19734806", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Smokin’ on crushed leaves", "vault_lines": ["Smokin' on crushed leaves"], "meaning": "Drake says he smokes marijuana, crushed leaves is a reference to the dried and cured leaves of the cannabis.\n\nCannabis needs to be dried before smoking to increase potency, making it hard and easy to crush.\n\n\n\n Cannabis being dried and cured", "annotation_id": "19721531", "url": "https://genius.com/19721531", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Pyramid schemes just like the Egyptians", "vault_lines": ["Pyramid schemes just like the Egyptians"], "meaning": "A bit of wordplay from Drake—he references the namesake Great Pyramids of Egypt in connection to illegal pyramid scheme scams, which are usually conducted over the phone.\n\nDrake himself used to do phone scams when he was struggling to provide for himself and his mother, which he talks about briefly in the song “Star67”:\n\nNow we in the basement and we workin' on the phone\nNow we in the basement and we workin' on the phone, man", "annotation_id": "19716613", "url": "https://genius.com/19716613", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Back when Corey was our sole investor", "vault_lines": ["Back when Corey was our sole investor"], "meaning": "Corey Skeet is a Canadian entrepreneur living in Cancun, Mexico. He is the owner of The Surfin Burrito and Padrino’s Pizza Express, and also has some other rental businesses as well.\n\nDrake shouted him out on an Instagram Story when Dark Lane Demo Tapes was released.", "annotation_id": "19721125", "url": "https://genius.com/19721125", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "For my nigga Hush Yeah, look", "vault_lines": ["For my nigga Hush"], "meaning": "Anthony “Hush” Palman is a member of OVO. He has writing credits for Drake stretching back to his second studio album, November 2011’s Take Care. Notably, he was caught up in some controversy in 2015 after he was accused of ghostwriting for Drake.\n\nDrake previously shouted out Hush along with other OVO members on his June 2010 song, “Miss Me”\n\nNeeks got the weed, Hush got a gun\nCJ got my credit cards and a lot of ones", "annotation_id": "19717184", "url": "https://genius.com/19717184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Get home and white Air Forces get brushed clean", "vault_lines": ["Get home and white Air Forces get brushed clean"], "meaning": "Drake cleans his white Air Forces, a very popular colorway that’s very hard to keep pristine, right when he arrives home.\n\nCreated by designer Bruce Kilgore in 1982, the Nike Air Force One, now known simply as the Air Force 1, takes its name from Air Force One, the plane that carries the President of the United States.", "annotation_id": "19716984", "url": "https://genius.com/19716984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Early twenties, but I want fifties, hundreds, not pennies I need plenty Got me ready to flip the F out like Fendi", "vault_lines": ["Early twenties, but I want fifties, hundreds, not pennies", "Got me ready to flip the F out like Fendi"], "meaning": "Due to the success Drake had early in his music career with the song “Forever” during his twenties, he expected to be paid large amounts of money, in preferably bills higher than $50.\n\nTo “flip the ‘F’ out” means to be angered or go crazy due to something you hear or see. Drake might flip out if he isn’t paid the correct amount of money he should get.\n\nDrake relates flipping the “F” out to fashion brand Fendi and it’s logo which consist of a right up F and inverted F.", "annotation_id": "19716664", "url": "https://genius.com/19716664", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Back when the house that I own now was my home screen", "vault_lines": ["Back when the house that I own now was my home screen"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing the YOLO Estate, his mansion in Hidden Hills, California. In an interview with Rolling Stone Magazine, Drake reveals that at one point this was the desktop image to his computer, years before he bought it:\n\nThis house was the desktop image on my computer years before I bought it…I was like, ‘What are the world’s craziest residential pools?’ and when I searched online, this came up.\n\nIn 2007, while on a trip in L.A. he tried, and failed, to find this place. In 2009, the home hit the market and Drizzy was able to pick it up at a bargain, for $7.7 million, which was down from the original asking price of $27 million.", "annotation_id": "19716659", "url": "https://genius.com/19716659", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Dressed in fatigues, I rep the East with my trustees", "vault_lines": ["Dressed in fatigues, I rep the East with my trustees"], "meaning": "Drake, who is originally from Toronto, has mentioned “the East” in much of his music most notably in the track “Days in the East”. This is a term used by residents of Scarborough, a borough in the east end of Toronto that’s known for its diversity and has a reputation for being ghetto. Scarborough is where many of Drake’s friends are from, including Preme, Baka, and Chubbz.\n\nDrake and his crew (a.k.a. his “trustees”) used to wear military fatigues back in the day. He mentions this on “Moment 4 Life” where he raps:\n\nShout out to the OVO, red wings and fatigues.", "annotation_id": "19716552", "url": "https://genius.com/19716552", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Keepin’ family out of East Detention And out of Pine Hill Funeral Center Spots we got no business enterin’", "vault_lines": ["Keepin' family out of East Detention", "And out of Pine Hill Funeral Center", "Spots we got no business enterin'"], "meaning": "“East Detention” is short for Toronto East Detention Centre, a prison in the Scarborough district of the city. Pine Hills Funeral Centre is a funeral home also located in Scarborough. Drake is saying he’s trying keep his family from ending up in jail or being buried, because he feels there is no reason they should be ending up in those situations.\n\n\n\n\n\nAdditionally this is likely a reference to those Drake considers family. Anthony “Fif” Soares (who’s photo serves as the back cover of the album) was gunned down on September 14, 2017 and later had his funeral in a Scarborough funeral centre.  Baka Not Nice was charged with human trafficking in June 2014 and sentenced to  6 months for assault.", "annotation_id": "19567700", "url": "https://genius.com/19567700", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "line": "Back when hotlines were still flippin’ Now I’m seein’ money off of hotlines blingin’ but it feels different", "vault_lines": ["Back when hotlines were still flippin'", "Now I'm seein' money off of hotlines blingin' but it feels different"], "meaning": "Before his rise to fame, Drake used to make money through shady phone scams and flipping apps in Niko’s basement. He talks about this June 2014 track “0 to 100”:\n\nWasn’t payin' me enough, I needed somethin' quicker\nSo now I’m all in Niko basement puttin' work in on the phones\n\nThis is also nod to Drake’s July 2015 summer hit, “Hotline Bling.” The song’s title refers to the ringing of cell phones. As one of the biggest musicians in the world, Drake certainly gets lots of money and a good portion of it comes from hit songs like “Hotline Bling.”", "annotation_id": "19529110", "url": "https://genius.com/19529110", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Deep Pockets", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 21, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-deep-pockets-lyrics", "genius_title": "Deep Pockets"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "[Outro: Drake] You doing me dirty (Dirty, dirty) You doing me dirty Doing me dirty You doing me dirty", "vault_lines": ["You doing me dirty (Dirty, dirty)", "Doing me dirty, you doing me dirty"], "meaning": "There’s a lot of speculation over whom this last part is about.\n\nThough some people think he’s referencing Rihanna and her new fling with Travi$ Scott, the leading theory is that Drake addresses Nicki Minaj directly here. Nicki and Drake used to be really close – they came up together under Lil Wayne, collaborated with each other multiple times, and even joked about getting married in their verses. Despite the rumors, Nicki tells us she never fucked Drake, and he corroborates.\n\nNowadays Nicki is dating Meek Mill. The Drake vs Meek feud generated some of the hottest verses of 2015, but we haven’t heard from Nicki about the bad blood between her boyfriend and her friend.\n\nOf course, we can’t know for sure whom this is about. But Drake fans sure seem to think this is about Nicki – her instagram comments are littered with diamonds, a reference to this mixtape’s cover.\n\n\n\nDrizzy sets the tone of this outro by repeating his four-word accusation four times. In the rest of this outro Drake tells this girl how he really feels.", "annotation_id": "7843301", "url": "https://genius.com/7843301", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "I’m barely paying attention, baby, I need substance I know you spent some time puttin’ on your makeup and your outfit, but", "vault_lines": ["And you know what I need", "I'm barely paying attention, baby, I need substance", "I know you spent some time puttin' on your makeup and your outfit, but"], "meaning": "Future needs substances (drugs) to help him pay attention to what’s going on. His drugs of choice are molly, lean, weed, Percocet, and Xanax. Future drug obsession has people wondering whether he would still be great without drugs.\n\n\n\nHe knows she’s spending a lot of time working on her appearance, but unless she has some substance beyond her superficial looks, she can’t get Future hooked.", "annotation_id": "7842582", "url": "https://genius.com/7842582", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "I’m able to, uh The stage in my life where I’m Where I feel like I can conquer anything and everything", "vault_lines": ["I'm able to, uh", "The stage in my life where I'm", "Where I feel like I can conquer anything and everything"], "meaning": "Future feels powerful and rightfully so. He started 2015 off with 3 projects:\n\n\n\nBeast Mode with Zaytoven (Jan. 15)\n\n56 Nights with DJ Esco & 808 Mafia (Mar. 21)\n\nDirty Sprite 2 (DS2) (Jul. 17)\n\n\n\nWhat a Time To Be Alive marks his fourth release this year, but there’s even more in store. Future has two mixtapes ready to be released soon: Beast Mode 2 with Zaytoven and Ape Shit with Mike WiLL Made It.\n\nHe’s also drawn attention with bangers like “Fuck Up Some Commas” and “Where Ya At”, a track he did with Drake. After all this, who wouldn’t feel omnipotent?", "annotation_id": "7839254", "url": "https://genius.com/7839254", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing", "vault_lines": ["Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing"], "meaning": "Their obsession with diamonds may have been what led to them choosing the album cover.", "annotation_id": "7835977", "url": "https://genius.com/7835977", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "Bring that Ace by the case, my niggas with me", "vault_lines": ["Bring that Ace by the case, my niggas with me"], "meaning": "Ace refers to Armand de Brignac Brut Gold, colloquially called “Ace of Spades” because of their logo. The company is currently owned by Shawn Carter, aka Jay Z. A single bottle runs in the $300 neighborhood, but special singular bottles have been purchased for more than $150,000 USD.\n\nBecause Future is with his friends, he needs a full case of Armand de Brignac.", "annotation_id": "7835741", "url": "https://genius.com/7835741", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "You should go back to him, perfect match for you, unstable (Hah)", "vault_lines": ["You should go back to him, perfect match for you, unstable (Hah)"], "meaning": "How you interpret this line depends on which theory you believe.\n\nIf you believe this outro is about RiRi, then the line is about Chris Brown. Breezy was diagnosed with bipolar disorder, so he was a perfect match to an unstable girl like Rihanna. Nowadays, Rihanna is making out with the controversial rapper Travi$ Scott, who was featured one track on Drake’s mixtape.\n\nBut if you think this whole thing is about Nicki, then this line is talking about Safaree Samuels. Known as SB, he was notably unstable and reportedly suicidal following the breakup with Nicki Minaj.\n\nNicki’s current boyfriend, Meek Mill, is also known for being volatile, often going on rambling twitter rants, one of which started his beef with Drake.\n\nRegardless of whom it’s about, this is a two-for-one insult: he’s calling this girl crazy and putting her on the same level as her ex. Harsh!", "annotation_id": "7835693", "url": "https://genius.com/7835693", "state": "accepted", "votes": 127, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "Sippin’ on Dom Pérignon for no reason Poppin’ tags, upper echelon for no reason", "vault_lines": ["Sippin' on Dom Pérignon for no reason", "Poppin' tags, upper echelon for no reason"], "meaning": "If money is an issue, you generally want to justify your purchases. But Future is rich enough to spend without a worry: he’s pouring his favorite brand of vintage champagne and copping all new clothes just ‘cause.\n\nThe upper echelon is the top of the top, an exclusive level nobody else is on. This isn’t the first time Future mentions this. He is also paying homage to Travis Scott’s 2013 single titled, “Upper Echelon”", "annotation_id": "7835579", "url": "https://genius.com/7835579", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "I’m familiar with this cash flow And if you juggin’, you can vouch for me I did it my way, you can vouch for me I put the cocaine and the powder in the couch, homie", "vault_lines": ["I'm familiar with this cash flow", "And if you juggin', you can vouch for me", "I did it my way, you can vouch for me", "I put the cocaine and the powder in the couch, homie"], "meaning": "“Jugging” is all about making money by whatever means necessary, usually involving cheating, scamming, and any sort of illegal activity. People jugging can recognize how much money Future’s making.\n\nFetty Wap is probably one of those people: he has songs called “Jugg” and “My Way.” Coincidence?\n\nFuture’s a trapper, so it’s not surprising he would hide crack with baby powder in the couch.", "annotation_id": "7835509", "url": "https://genius.com/7835509", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing", "vault_lines": ["Diamond, diamond, diamond, diamonds on me dancing"], "meaning": "As Future’s chains swing around his neck, the diamonds appear to “dance” on him as they’re exposed to light at certain angles. Future also personifies the diamonds, conjuring the image of jewelry-clad girls dancing around him.\n\nIf he’s really talking about girls, you know they’re all dimes, perfect 10s. And they’re iced out because diamonds are a girl’s best friend. Drake’s no stranger to the dancers at the King of Diamonds strip club: he mentions them in “Miss Me” and “All Me.”", "annotation_id": "7835492", "url": "https://genius.com/7835492", "state": "accepted", "votes": 146, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "I don’t want no liquor ‘cause I been drinkin’ that dirty", "vault_lines": ["I don't want no liquor 'cause I been drinkin' that dirty"], "meaning": "Future doesn’t want any liquor because he’s been consuming a different drug of choice: Dirty Sprite or codeine.\n\nSimilar to his line on “Live from the Gutter”:\n\nA fiend for that lean I ain’t even started drinking beer yet", "annotation_id": "7835402", "url": "https://genius.com/7835402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diamonds Dancing", "line": "Ballin’ in the middle of the club, no jersey", "vault_lines": ["Ballin' in the middle of the club, no jersey"], "meaning": "Future is balling hard in the club—not balling on the court, like an NBA player with a jersey.\n\n\n\n\n\nAnd he’s not wearing a jersey to the club.\n\nThe line plays on Jersey club music—a popular genre in clubs all over the country.\n\nAlso, “Jersey” is the title of this mixtape’s 10th track.", "annotation_id": "7834848", "url": "https://genius.com/7834848", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
