{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "Ayy, how many hoes in this club? Is it just me and you, my love?", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, how many hoes in this club?", "Is it just me and you, my love?", "How many hoes in this club?", "Is it really just me, my love?"], "meaning": "Some fans interpret this as Drake cheekily referencing Kendrick Lamar’s “two bad bitches” jab from “Not Like Us.” By asking if it’s “just me and you” among the “hoes in this club,” Drake could be jokingly placing himself in the same category, turning a diss into a self-aware punchline. This remains speculative and has not been confirmed by Drake.", "annotation_id": "40320729", "url": "https://genius.com/40320729", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "So if you just playin’ around, you need to tell your girl, “Take your fine ass home,” and that’s real", "vault_lines": ["So if you just playin' around, you need to tell your girl, \"Take your fine ass home,\" and that's real"], "meaning": "Drakes basically saying take your girl home or else he’ll take her.", "annotation_id": "39253948", "url": "https://genius.com/39253948", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who’s callin’ that shit?) Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who’s callin’ that shit?) Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who’s callin’ that shit?)", "vault_lines": ["Who's callin' my phone? (Who's callin' that shit?)", "Is it Stacy? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Who's callin' my— (Ayy)", "Is it Becky? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Is it Keisha? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Is it Ashley? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Was it Dani? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Is it PARTY? (Who's callin' my phone?)"], "meaning": "when Drake asks, “Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who’s callin’ that shit?)”, he’s likely expressing a mix of suspicion and emotional detachment. The line gives off a sense that he’s either being interrupted by someone he’s not eager to hear from or that he’s grown cautious about who gets access to him. Considering the title “Nokia” references an old-school phone, the lyric might carry a nostalgic tone—hinting at people from the past suddenly trying to reconnect. It could be old flames, fake friends, or opportunists who now want something from him.\n\nThe repetition of the question adds a layer of annoyance or skepticism, as if he’s questioning the motives behind the call. It speaks to the isolation that often comes with success, where people only seem to reach out when they need something. In this context, the phone becomes more than just a way to communicate—it’s a symbol of how fame brings unwanted attention and blurred boundaries. Drake isn’t just asking who’s calling; he’s indirectly asking, “What do you want now?”", "annotation_id": "36923612", "url": "https://genius.com/36923612", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who’s callin’ that shit?) Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who’s callin’ that shit?) Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who’s callin’ that shit?) Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who’s callin’ that shit?)", "vault_lines": ["Who's callin' my phone? (Who's callin' that shit?)", "Is it Stacy? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Who's callin' my— (Ayy)", "Is it Becky? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Is it Keisha? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Is it Ashley? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Was it Dani? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Is it PARTY? (Who's callin' my phone?)"], "meaning": "when Drake asks, “Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who’s callin’ that shit?)”, he’s likely expressing a mix of suspicion and emotional detachment. The line gives off a sense that he’s either being interrupted by someone he’s not eager to hear from or that he’s grown cautious about who gets access to him. Considering the title “Nokia” references an old-school phone, the lyric might carry a nostalgic tone—hinting at people from the past suddenly trying to reconnect. It could be old flames, fake friends, or opportunists who now want something from him.\n\nThe repetition of the question adds a layer of annoyance or skepticism, as if he’s questioning the motives behind the call. It speaks to the isolation that often comes with success, where people only seem to reach out when they need something. In this context, the phone becomes more than just a way to communicate—it’s a symbol of how fame brings unwanted attention and blurred boundaries. Drake isn’t just asking who’s calling; he’s indirectly asking, “What do you want now?”", "annotation_id": "36923608", "url": "https://genius.com/36923608", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "The way I feel right now, I feel like we need to be all alone", "vault_lines": ["The way I feel right now, I feel like we need to be all alone"], "meaning": "Kanye West – ‘Paranoid’", "annotation_id": "36288148", "url": "https://genius.com/36288148", "state": "pending", "votes": -4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "We had a lock on the game, but it’s more to come", "vault_lines": ["We had a lock on the game, but it's more to come"], "meaning": "In the context of sports or games, “we had a lock in the game” means the team or players were in a dominant position, with a high probability of winning. They had a strong lead, a tight defense, or were otherwise in control, and it seemed very likely they would not lose.\n\nDrake uses metaphors to describe his crew, OVO, and how they dominate the game—the rap game. So, the “players” would mean Drake’s crew, and the “game” would mean the rap game; the spot for the best.\n\nDominant Position: In this context, “lock” refers to a strong, virtually assured position.\n\nHigh Probability of Winning: The team or players were in a situation where it was highly likely they would win, or at least not lose.\n\nControl and Stability: They had a good lead, a strong defense, or were otherwise in a position of power. This could be due to a combination of factors, such as confidence and brotherhood between Drake and his crew, a big lead compared to Drake’s competitors (e.g., Kendrick Lamar), and they’re otherwise in a position of control.\n\nIn simpler terms, it means the competitors' beliefs were essentially over, Drake was winning, and it seemed very unlikely any other artist could come back and take the lead.", "annotation_id": "35708714", "url": "https://genius.com/35708714", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "Leavin’ me, we got sticks in the club illegally", "vault_lines": ["Leavin' me, we got sticks in the club illegally"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to having “guns” while in the club illegally", "annotation_id": "35586169", "url": "https://genius.com/35586169", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "You could see right through my love, ayy", "vault_lines": ["You could see right through my love, ayy"], "meaning": "This line speaks to the transparency—or perhaps the insincerity—of his affection. Drake admits that the person he’s addressing could tell his love wasn’t as deep or genuine as it may have seemed.\n\nThe phrasing “see right through” suggests vulnerability, but also guilt—like he knows he couldn’t hide how empty or performative the love was, no matter how much he tried to convince her otherwise.\n\nTo sum it all up, He admits that his attempts at expressing love were insincere, and the person he’s addressing could easily perceive the lack of depth in his emotions.", "annotation_id": "35278087", "url": "https://genius.com/35278087", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "I could change your life so easily", "vault_lines": ["I could change your life so easily"], "meaning": "NOTE:  Please double check all of the information I have stated below.\n\nThe lyrics “I could change your life so easily” can be viewed as a statement backed by Drake’s history. Hinting how his influence, fame and platform can immediately elevate someone’s carrer or their life as a whole.\n\nWith Drake proving this over and over with artists such as the:\n\nThe Weeknd – Before becoming a global superstar that we all know him as, he began his career as an underground rapper in Toronto. That was until Drake heard his music, shared it and featured him on Take Care. With The Weeknd contributing on the album, with his songwriting, production and a featured artist role in “Crew Love.” Which eventually became his road to success.\n\nWith the next artists being A$AP Rocky – Drake was one of the first major artists to bring Rocky on tour – during his Club Paradise tour in 2012.  At the time, Rocky was an emerging artist gaining attention with his mixtape Live.Love.A$AP. This tour, which also featured Kendrick Lamar, provided Rocky the much needed exposure to broader audiences across North America and Europe. Moreover Rocky has also stated in an interview that he owes his entire life to Drake, which can be seen here.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/T1sirD8M0dE?si=gtMKL9BShmhGHZfi\n\nKendrick Lamar – Surprisingly, yes is true that Drake had a role in Kendrick’s success.  Kendrick has already publicly acknowledged Drake’s impact on his career. In various interviews over the years, Kendrick has mentioned that Drake gave him a massive opportunity later on. When Kendrick dropped Section.80 in 2011, Drake was among the first to listen to it and give it recognition. In many interviews, Kendrick expressed deep respect for Drake, thanking him for helping boost his career by providing a platform and recognition. Moreover, Kendrick expressed more gratitude for Drake’s early co-sign and how their collaboration on tracks like “Poetic Justice” from Kendrick’s Good Kid, M.A.A.D City was significant in boosting his career.\n\nThe interviews can be seen here: (Note: This is probably not all, but I can’t find anymore. They have been either buried to the ground or deleted.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/1L7CW-p4-MI?si=EwdvfwCRwp0v-Bu3\n\nhttps://youtu.be/IZg8BBHiFmw?si=BbL1hV5roESQaWCm", "annotation_id": "35250605", "url": "https://genius.com/35250605", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "[Verse: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "For the first half of this verse, the producer Elkan added a reference to the original Nokia Tune from 1994 into the instrumental.\n\nThis adds some syncopation to the rhythm of the song while also being a clever nod to the instantly recognizable ringtone.\n\nThis reference can be heard easier in this video – https://www.youtube.com/shorts/BrCux7mQP1E", "annotation_id": "35180871", "url": "https://genius.com/35180871", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "How many hoes in this club? Is it really just me, my love?", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, how many hoes in this club?", "Is it just me and you, my love?", "How many hoes in this club?", "Is it really just me, my love?"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that despite being surrounded by what we can only assume are sex workers, he is the only actual “hoe” in the club, being well known for allegedly having a lot of straight sex with various cis-gendered women in the absence of long term or meaningful relationships with any of them.", "annotation_id": "35140638", "url": "https://genius.com/35140638", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "You got some bass in the trunk, my love, damn", "vault_lines": ["You got some bass in the trunk, my love, damn"], "meaning": "Drake is actually talking about someone’s ass being big.", "annotation_id": "35037294", "url": "https://genius.com/35037294", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "Baby girl Baby girl Baby girl", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "It is unclear whether this is the natural voice of Elkan (the track’s producer) or if vocal effects were used, but the tone bears a strong resemblance to Kendrick Lamar’s voice. This has led to speculation that Drake is mocking Kendrick, particularly in reference to his diss track “meet the grahams.” In that track, Kendrick opens the third verse with the line, “Dear baby girl, I’m sorry that your father not active inside your world,” accusing Drake of having an unclaimed daughter—an allegation that Drake has firmly denied.\n\nGiven the comedic tone, the phrase serves as a way of reminding the listeners of Kendrick’s unverified accusations.", "annotation_id": "34745229", "url": "https://genius.com/34745229", "state": "accepted", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "You know my dad ‘nem pimpin’ for real You ever been down to Memphis, Tennessee, my love? Uh", "vault_lines": ["You know my dad 'nem pimpin' for real", "You ever been down to Memphis, Tennessee, my love? Uh"], "meaning": "Drake’s father is from Memphis, Tennessee.", "annotation_id": "34744449", "url": "https://genius.com/34744449", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who? Who?) Who’s callin’ my phone? (Who? Who?) Who’s callin’ my phone? (Yeah, yeah) Who’s callin’ my phone? (OVO Sound, 2025)", "vault_lines": ["Who's callin' my phone? (Who's callin' that shit?)", "Who's callin' my— (Ayy)"], "meaning": "Drake questions who is calling his phone. He says Who twice to sound like an owl. An owl commonly represents OVO his label. Which he then shouts out.", "annotation_id": "34630921", "url": "https://genius.com/34630921", "state": "pending", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "Take a pic for the ’Gram, show the whole world (Show the whole world), yeah", "vault_lines": ["Take a pic for the 'Gram, show the whole world (Show the whole world), yeah"], "meaning": "Elkan is playing on the word “‘Gram”; referring to the social media platform Instagram, but it’s also a nod to his Drake’s last name, Graham.", "annotation_id": "34630803", "url": "https://genius.com/34630803", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "Is it Stacy? (Who’s callin’ my phone?) Is it Becky? (Who’s callin’ my phone?) Is it Keisha? (Who’s callin’ my phone?) Is it Ashley? (Who’s callin’ my phone?) Was it Dani? (Who’s callin’ my phone?) Is it PARTY? (Who’s callin’ my phone?) Where’s the function? (Where the fuck the function?) Send the addy (Where the fuck the function?)", "vault_lines": ["Who's callin' my phone? (Who's callin' that shit?)", "Is it Stacy? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Who's callin' my— (Ayy)", "Is it Becky? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Is it Keisha? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Is it Ashley? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Was it Dani? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Is it PARTY? (Who's callin' my phone?)", "Where's the function? (Where the fuck the function?)", "Send the addy (Where the fuck the function?)"], "meaning": "Drake bragged about the women in his contacts list in similar fashion on “First Person Shooter” from his 2023 album For All The Dogs where he rapped:\n\nI search one name, and end up seein' twenty tings\nNadine, Christine, Justine, Kathleen, Charlene, Pauline, Claudine\nMan, I pack ‘em in this phone like some sardines\n\nJust as it did then, this may interpolate the iconic second verse of “What These Bitches Want” by DMX from his 1999 classic album …And Then There Was X. In the verse, DMX lists out his romantic history by first names:\n\nThere was Brenda, Latisha, Linda, Felicia\nDawn, LeShaun, Ines and Alicia\nTeresa, Monica, Sharon, Nicki\nLisa, Veronica, Karen, Vicky\n\nThis may also be an interpolation of the 2007 track “Shawty Is Da Shit” by The-Dream where featured artist Fabolous similarly asks:\n\nIs it Keisha? Is it Tisha?\nMaybe Lisa? Or Teresa?\nIt could be Tia, maybe Aaliyah\n\n“Is it PARTY?” jokingly refers to the album’s other featured artist PARTYNEXTDOOR.", "annotation_id": "34570956", "url": "https://genius.com/34570956", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "I’ma at you like bless me", "vault_lines": ["I'ma at you like bless me"], "meaning": "“at you” sounds like “Achoo” which is commonly known as the sound of a sneeze. When someone sneezes, many people commonly say “bless you” as a polite gesture.\n\nThe line could also refer to the previous line about posting on Instagram. To “at” someone on social media is to tag them or mention them. Drake says that in exchange for giving them clout by mentioning them on his Insta, he wants them to do him a favor.", "annotation_id": "34561581", "url": "https://genius.com/34561581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "Got the whole 6ix side, they believe in me We got members east of the DVP We got members west on the 401", "vault_lines": ["Got the whole 6ix side, they believe in me", "We got members east of the DVP", "We got members west on the 401"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to different areas of Toronto, Canada, in these lyrics. DVP (Don Valley Parkway): A major highway running north-south through Toronto. “East of the DVP” refers to neighborhoods like Scarborough. 401 (Highway 401): One of the busiest highways in North America, running east-west across Toronto. “West, on 401” likely refers to areas in the western part of the city, such as Etobicoke or Mississauga. Essentially, Drake is emphasizing that he has connections and influence across the entire city, both in the east (Scarborough) and the west (Etobicoke, Mississauga).", "annotation_id": "34561116", "url": "https://genius.com/34561116", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "NOKIA", "line": "I’ma ice both of y’all like Gretzky", "vault_lines": ["I'ma ice both of y'all like Gretzky"], "meaning": "“Ice” means to buy expensive jewelry, which Drake claims he will do for both this girl and her bestie, flexing his wealth and generosity.\n\nDrizzy then uses a simile to liken this to Wayne Gretzky, widely regarded as the greatest ice hockey player of all time. This creates clever wordplay on the homonym “ice”, not only referencing diamonds but also subtly flexing that their jewelry will be as elite as Gretzky was on the ice.\n\nGretzky in Drake’s OVO Merch", "annotation_id": "34560969", "url": "https://genius.com/34560969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
