{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "I know way too many people here right now That I didn’t know last year, who the fuck are y’all? I swear it feels like the last few nights We been everywhere and back But I just can’t remember it all What am I doin’? What am I doin’? Oh, yeah, that’s right, I’m doin’ me, I’m doin’ me I’m livin’ life right now, man And this what I’ma do ’til it’s over, ’til it’s over But it’s far from over", "vault_lines": ["I know way too many people here right now", "But it's far from over", "That I didn't know last year—who the fuck are y'all?", "\"Y'all know them? Well, fuck it, me either\"", "I swear it feels like the last few nights", "We been everywhere and back", "Yeah, that's right—I'm doin' me, I'm doin' me", "But it's far from over, uh", "But I just can't remember it all", "What am I doin'? What am I doin'?", "Oh yeah, that's right—I'm doin' me, I'm doin' me", "I'm livin' life right now, man", "And this what I'ma do 'til it's over, 'til it's over"], "meaning": "In 2009—the year before this song’s release—Drake released his third and debut commercial mixtape, So Far Gone. The mixtape was essential in launching Drake’s career as it received universal acclaim from many critics and hip-hop fans alike.\n\nAdditionally, his June 2009 single “Best I Ever Had” was Drake’s real first taste of success as a solo artist as it peaked at number two on the Billboard Hot 100 and was nominated for two Grammy Awards: Best Rap Solo Performance and Best Rap Song.\n\nThese achievements essentially set the tone for the rest of Drake’s career in the following years as he continues to release successful projects after projects—both commercially and critically—with ease and shows no sign of falling off anytime soon.", "annotation_id": "18479181", "url": "https://genius.com/18479181", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "Never drop the ball, fuck are y’all thinkin’?", "vault_lines": ["Never drop the ball, fuck are y'all thinkin'?"], "meaning": "To “drop the ball” means to make an error or miss an opportunity. This was a shot at a radio host named Star, who said Drake had “dropped the ball.” He responded in an August 2010 segment of his radio show.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/TxJ37-Luakg?t=300", "annotation_id": "18435502", "url": "https://genius.com/18435502", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "About to set it off in this bitch, Jada Pinkett", "vault_lines": ["About to set it off in this bitch, Jada Pinkett"], "meaning": "Set It Off is a 1996 critically acclaimed heist film, which is about four best friends who decide to rob a bank so they can provide better for themselves and their families. The movie stars Jada Pinkett as Lida “Stony” Newsom, one of the four friends.", "annotation_id": "18435440", "url": "https://genius.com/18435440", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "Making sure the Young Money ship is never sinking", "vault_lines": ["Making sure the Young Money ship is never sinking"], "meaning": "Young Money Entertainment is Lil Wayne’s record label that he found in 2005 and Drake signed to the label in June 2009.\n\nDrake not letting the “Young Money ship sinking” means he’s not going to let the label be forgotten and will continue to put out hits. On June 2010’s “Miss Me,” YM CEO Lil Wayne shared a similar sentiment about Drake:\n\nHa ha, damn! I’ll be gone ‘til November\nBut fuck it, I ain’t trippin’, I know Drizzy gon' kill ‘em", "annotation_id": "18435372", "url": "https://genius.com/18435372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "Young Money", "vault_lines": ["Making sure the Young Money ship is never sinking"], "meaning": "Young Money is the record label Drake is signed to. Around this time many members of Young Money (mainly Lil Wayne) would scream Young Money over intros, hooks and outros to songs.", "annotation_id": "13043174", "url": "https://genius.com/13043174", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "I did it overnight, it couldn’t happen any quicker", "vault_lines": ["I did it overnight, it couldn't happen any quicker"], "meaning": "Drake was getting a lot of popularity after releasing his mixtape “So Far Gone”, and working with fellow rapper Lil Wayne. This escalated Drake and he gained popularity rapidly, which is why he says it couldn’t’ve happened any quicker.", "annotation_id": "12330273", "url": "https://genius.com/12330273", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "Alright, bottles on me, long as someone drink it", "vault_lines": ["Alright, bottles on me, long as someone drink it"], "meaning": "Drake later expresses a similar sentiment on his August 2011 song, “Headlines,” where he raps about drinking to the success he has achieved in his rap career:\n\nDrinkin' every night because we drink to my accomplishments\nFaded way too long, I’m floatin' in and out of consciousness", "annotation_id": "12330242", "url": "https://genius.com/12330242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "It wouldn’t be the first time I’d done it, throwin’ hundreds When I should be throwin’ ones, bitch, I run it, ah", "vault_lines": ["It wouldn't be the first time I'd done it, throwin' hundreds", "When I should be throwin' ones, bitch, I run it, ah"], "meaning": "Since So Far Gone, Drake has made his affinity for strippers and strip clubs well-known through his music. He even went as far as to promote his second album Take Care with a single called “Club Paradise,” named after his favorite strip club in Toronto.\n\nIn this line, Drake indicates his wealth by saying that he can afford to make it rain $100 bills in strip clubs instead of the customary $1 bills.", "annotation_id": "11791622", "url": "https://genius.com/11791622", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "But I really can’t complain, everything is kosher", "vault_lines": ["But I really can't complain, everything is kosher"], "meaning": "The word kosher is in reference to the Jewish dietary code, which prohibits the consumption or pork and shellfish among other things. Drake himself was raised Jewish by his mother, having attended a Jewish sunday school and had a Bar Mitzvah. To this day, Drake seems to be proud of his heritage, particularly that eventful night that was his Bar Mitzvah. He recreated it on SNL and later themed his 31st birthday party around it.", "annotation_id": "11578753", "url": "https://genius.com/11578753", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "Man, nobody’s ever done it like I did it, uh", "vault_lines": ["Man, nobody's ever done it like I did it, uh"], "meaning": "Drake’s come-up consisted of an early acting career, three mixtapes, two key music videos and one tour with Lil Wayne before signing to Young Money.\n\nHis 2007 music video Replacement Girl got him featured on BET and gained the attention of Lil Wayne who invited him on Tha Carter III Tour.\n\nSince music videos and touring are standard means of promotion, he’s likely boasting in his achievement of fame in spite of no major label assistance. In that sense, Drake’s success coincided with a wave of rappers who came up through independent internet distributers such as Youtube and Datpiff. One parallel artist would be Tyler the Creator whose debut mixtape Bastard was released the same year as Drake’s breakout So Far Gone.\n\nhttps://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/S1LI5bh0_default/index.html?videoId=5214872600001\n\nProbably the closest of Drake’s contemporaries to “do it like he did it” would be Childish Gambino who also began his showmanship as an actor, released his first mixtape in 2008, and released his first major label debut three years later in 2011 which was also promoted through a music video (Freaks and Geeks).", "annotation_id": "11491612", "url": "https://genius.com/11491612", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "I swear this life is like the sweetest thing I’ve ever known", "vault_lines": ["I swear this life is like the sweetest thing I've ever known"], "meaning": "Call out to Lauryn Hill’s “The Sweetest Thing?”\n\nLauryn’s “Sweetest Thing” was love, but Drake’s is fame and influence.", "annotation_id": "8907078", "url": "https://genius.com/8907078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "[Produced by Boi-1da and Nick Brongers]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Boi-1da, one of Drake’s go-to producers, shows how he created this beat in FL Studio:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N15Y78qWxvY", "annotation_id": "3279744", "url": "https://genius.com/3279744", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "Man, they treat me like a legend, am I really this cold? I’m really too young to be feelin’ this old", "vault_lines": ["Man, they treat me like a legend, am I really this cold?", "I'm really too young to be feelin' this old"], "meaning": "Drake was only 23 when this song dropped, but he had the money/respect of someone that had been in the game for years. Drizzy often plays on this idea in his raps, as he has described himself as “the rookie and the vet” and “young as an intern, but money like I built this shit”.", "annotation_id": "12523", "url": "https://genius.com/12523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "Uh, one thing ’bout music, when it hits, you feel no pain And I swear I got this shit that make these bitches go insane So they tell me that they love me, I know better than that, it’s just game It’s just what comes with the fame, and I’m ready for that, I’m just sayin’", "vault_lines": ["Uh, one thing 'bout music, when it hits, you feel no pain", "And I swear I got this shit that make these bitches go insane", "So they tell me that they love me, I know better than that, it's just game", "It's just what comes with the fame, and I'm ready for that, I'm just sayin'"], "meaning": "The first few bars of this verse echo the beginning of “Hip-Hop” by Dead Prez, which in turn references “Trenchtown Rock” by Bob Marley.\n\nOne thing about music:\nWhen it hit, you feel no pain\nWhite folks says it controls your brain\nI know better than that. That’s game\nAnd we ready for that…", "annotation_id": "12481", "url": "https://genius.com/12481", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "So I’m ridin’ through the city with my high beams on Can you see me? Can you see me? Get your Visine on", "vault_lines": ["So I'm ridin' through the city with my high beams on", "Can you see me? Can you see me? Get your Visine on"], "meaning": "Drake wants to remain as visible as possible, so he flashes the high beams on his car whenever he drives by someone and suggests his competition put in some eye drops so that they can properly “see” him.", "annotation_id": "12454", "url": "https://genius.com/12454", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "Two thumbs up, Ebert and Roeper", "vault_lines": ["Two thumbs up, Ebert and Roeper"], "meaning": "When someone asks you “How are you doing?” You may answer nonverbally with a thumbs up. In this case, shit is going so great for Mr. Do-Right-And-Kill-Everything that it deserves two thumbs up.\n\nSiskel and Ebert are film critics who pioneered the “thumbs up/thumbs down” rating system. After Gene Siskel died of a brain tumor, Richard Roeper replaced him as Roger Ebert’s thumb buddy.", "annotation_id": "12452", "url": "https://genius.com/12452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "Super good smidoke, a package of the Swisher’s", "vault_lines": ["Super good smidoke, a package of the Swisher's"], "meaning": "Drake packs high-quality smoke, pronounced with some “izzle-wizzle” playfulness à la Snoop Dogg, into a Swisher blunt wrap.", "annotation_id": "12440", "url": "https://genius.com/12440", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "’Bout to go thriller, Mike Jackson on these niggas All I need’s a fuckin’ red jacket with some zippers", "vault_lines": ["About to go thriller, Mike Jackson on these niggas", "All I need's a fuckin' red jacket with some zippers"], "meaning": "Drake is about to thrill you, as did a young Michael Jackson in the classic music video, “Thriller”, in which he wears a red jacket with superfluous zippers.\n\nFunnily enough, Drake portrayed the King of Pop in an episode of Degrassi.\n\n\n\nIn 2010, Canadian interviewer Nardwuar the Human Serviette interviewed Drake and “40”. Known for his extroverted style and seemingly inane qualities, he often surprises people with profound gifts and knowledge of his interview subjects. He presented Drake with ‘a fucking red jacket with some zippers’ during his interview. Drake:\n\n“This is my first time ever wearing an actual red jacket with the zippers–a fucking red jacket with the zippers.”\n\nDrake stated that his interview with Nardwuar was the “best that I’ve ever done in my entire life.”", "annotation_id": "12434", "url": "https://genius.com/12434", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over", "line": "I could teach you how to speak my language, Rosetta Stone", "vault_lines": ["I could teach you how to speak my language, Rosetta Stone"], "meaning": "Rosetta Stone™ is the new breakthrough software used for learning new languages! Learning a new language can be easy and fun with what CNN calls “the best in language education software”\n\n(note: not a paid advertisement for Rosetta Stone)\n\nThe software is named after the Rosetta Stone – an ancient artifact that was instrumental to Egyptologists' understanding of Ancient Egyptian hieroglyphic writing", "annotation_id": "12430", "url": "https://genius.com/12430", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
