{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "[Bridge]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The bridge uses smilar lyrics to Ramriddlz’s chorus on “P2P (iii)”:\n\nPussy too pink\nDrink too strong\nEyes too chink\nI’m too sprung\nPussy too pink\nDrink too strong\nEyes too chink\nI’m too gone\"\n\nDrake also draws inspiration from “P2P (iii)” by using “I, I, I” throughout the song.", "annotation_id": "7380743", "url": "https://genius.com/7380743", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "And if he wasn’t with it, then why were you with him? Maybe you just had to realize That knowing the difference can make all the difference", "vault_lines": ["And if he wasn't with it, then why were you with him?", "Maybe you just had to realize", "That knowing the difference can make all the difference"], "meaning": "One of Drake’s favourite topics is taking girls from their man. In particular, he is fond of direct comparisons between himself and her less than ideal partner:\n\nDon’t I treat you better than your ooo-old man\nHe couldn’t have done it this sho' nuff\".\n\nThe difference between a top-tier man like Drake, and the lesser specimens that the world throws at you, can change your life. Or, at least increase your bank balance.", "annotation_id": "7331836", "url": "https://genius.com/7331836", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "’Cause the pussy too good, drink too strong", "vault_lines": ["'Cause the pussy too good, drink too strong", "The pussy too good, drink too strong"], "meaning": "Mimicking the hallucinogenic nature of the original song, Drake describes an altered state of consciousness brought about by potent alcohol mixed with potent beauty. He’s “sprung” in the sense that he believes he’s falling for the girl, but in reality he’s just under the influence.\n\nAs he rapped in “Star67,” Drake is not a pussy on a pedestal kind of guy:\n\nShut the fuck up, text from a centerfold, I ain’t reply/Let her know I read it though/Voice mail say she ready though/n—s know I’m credible, ain’t no pussy on a pedestal", "annotation_id": "7273266", "url": "https://genius.com/7273266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "She say I’m a sweeterman But she say that she don’t need a man I say why don’t we make an agreement then We can just fuck while we’re being friends", "vault_lines": ["She say I'm a sweeterman", "But she say that she don't need a man", "I say why don't we make an agreement then", "We can just fuck while we're being friends", "What if we make an agreement then"], "meaning": "Drake’s dichotomy as an artist is sometimes forgotten. Whilst he has been know to drop less savoury verses where women are treated as “bitches & hoes,” he’s known by the wider public as a sensitive soul – a rarity in this genre. His subject matter has projected his music to a much larger audience who view him as a breath of fresh air in an era of misogyny.\n\nThis dichotomy is present even in these lines. The girl making eyes at Drake is independent, and isn’t looking for a long term relationship. And from his dating history, we can tell he isn’t one too concerned with official titles (despite the content of some of his songs), so friends with benefits seems like a natural fit!", "annotation_id": "7273185", "url": "https://genius.com/7273185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "Yiy too change", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Referring to the Jamacian Drake affiliate; Popcaan’s mixtape Yiy Change.\n\nDancehall and Reggae have emerged from the underground and are becoming increasingly popular. Along with the success of Grime artists such as Skepta in the UK, hip-hop is tapping into different influences as artists try to break away from the traditional “trap” sound that has dominated radio for the past few years.\n\nDrake for instance, has been dropping Jamaican slang on his records as early as 2013.", "annotation_id": "7269533", "url": "https://genius.com/7269533", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "So she wanna give me the adunana-ne, ohh", "vault_lines": ["And she wanna give me the adunana-ne, ohh", "So she wanna give me the adunana-ne, ohh"], "meaning": "As a hat tip to Ramriddlz, Drake borrows “adunana-ne,” slang for vagina, from the original version of “Sweeterman.”", "annotation_id": "7267068", "url": "https://genius.com/7267068", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "She heard the ting bend like banana", "vault_lines": ["She heard the ting bend like banana"], "meaning": "In an allusion to the Vybz Kartel song “It Bend like Banana,” Drake compares his penis to a banana. Ting is Jamaican slang and according to RapStats, has spiked in usage as of 2015.", "annotation_id": "7266649", "url": "https://genius.com/7266649", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "She’s a spice, ride pipe like bike, like Yokohama", "vault_lines": ["Ride the pipe like a bike, oh", "Ride the pipe like a bike, make sure you're going the distance", "She's a spice, ride pipe like bike, like Yokohama"], "meaning": "Even though “spices” (like Saffron) are exotic and add heat to food, Spice is also a dancehall artist known for her raunchy lyrics. Drake applies a reggae-like flow at this point and pays homage to Vybz Kartel, who has a song called “Bicycle.”\n\nIt should also be noted Yokohama don’t actually make motorcycles, they make tires – their full name being Yokohama Rubber Company. Was Drake aware of this? We’re not sure, but either way, it’s good to see him practising safe sex!", "annotation_id": "7266471", "url": "https://genius.com/7266471", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "This is a blessing, mashallah, wallahi", "vault_lines": ["This is a blessing, mashallah, wallahi"], "meaning": "Following the word blessing with an Arabic word that has a similar meaning, in being thankful to god – mashallah; and then reinforcing his statement by saying “I swear to God” – wallahi, kind of like what he did here.", "annotation_id": "7266332", "url": "https://genius.com/7266332", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "line": "Hotter than the 6 in the summer, deadass, girl I wanna", "vault_lines": ["Girl, I know you wanna, you know I wanna", "Hotter than the 6 in the summer, deadass, girl I wanna", "Girl, I know you wanna, I, I, I", "Girl, I know you wanna"], "meaning": "The summer is the well-known hot season.  Drizzy’s heart might be cold, but in the summer on the six, he passes the message that he wants to do hot things with that hot girl.\n\nKeep in mind that Toronto can reach temperatures of 95 degrees Fahrenheit, so this girl is definitely hot!", "annotation_id": "7266244", "url": "https://genius.com/7266244", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Sweeterman (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 10, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-sweeterman-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Sweeterman (Remix)"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "They had it set up for real, but they didn’t come with the slaps When I heard the shit I was skippin’ through that", "vault_lines": ["They had it set up for real, but they didn't come with the slaps", "When I heard the shit I was skippin' through that"], "meaning": "In an interview with LeBron James, Drake delves into how he felt played by Kanye during early 2018 for revealing his album release date and talking about his son. Drake recalls after he told Kanye the release date, GOOD Music began announcing projects around this release date as a “set up” to take the spotlight.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/s4iff/status/1051048659901665280?lang=en\n\nBy the time Kanye’s album ye finally came out, it was received with mixed reviews. Drake acknowledges these reviews and claims the album was weak and full of skips.", "annotation_id": "20225777", "url": "https://genius.com/20225777", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Based off of that, they gotta run me the max They gotta run me the max, they gotta double the racks", "vault_lines": ["Based off of that, they gotta run me the max", "They gotta run me the max, they gotta double the racks"], "meaning": "Drake boasts about his maximum record label deal and the second set of earnings he gains from endorsement deals. Drake’s net worth is approximately $150 million, through a combination of the two sets of income. Drake has endorsements from major companies like Jordan, Sprite, and Apple Music.\n\nAccording to a study from Variety, Drake could earn a record setting figure of approximately $20 million per album, when he finally signs a new deal. Later in 2019, his releases appeared under the Frozen Moments record label, indicating the possibility of a new contract.\n\nDrizzy previously boasted about his contract on his March 2017 track, “Gyalchester”:\n\nContract max, I gotta bring that shit back", "annotation_id": "18376435", "url": "https://genius.com/18376435", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Ten of us, we movin’ as one", "vault_lines": ["Ten of us, we movin' as one"], "meaning": "This is likely a reference to the members of OVO SOUND, which is a record label founded by Drake & long-time friends Noah “40” Shebib & Oliver El-Khatib. In addition to its creators, the label’s current roster is comprised of seven artists—PARTYNEXTDOOR, Majid Jordan, Roy Woods, Ob O'brien, dvsn, PLAZA, & Baka Not Nice—combining to equal the “ten of us” Drake is referring to “movin' as one” record label.", "annotation_id": "15086366", "url": "https://genius.com/15086366", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "I’m so proud of who I’ve become", "vault_lines": ["I'm so proud of who I've become"], "meaning": "Ever since Drake entered rap in 2006 with his debut mixtape, Room For Improvement, he’s made some massive strides.  He went from the bottom to becoming one of the most colossal stars in the music industry. One of his biggest feats includes charting for eight straight years on the Billboard Hot 100.", "annotation_id": "14937523", "url": "https://genius.com/14937523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Woo! You know what I’m sayin’? Woo! Ayy, ayy, yeah, you know what I’m sayin’? (Gyeah, gyeah, gyeah, gyeah!)", "vault_lines": ["Woo! You know what I'm sayin'?", "Woo! Ayy, ayy, yeah, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Yeah, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Ayy, ayy, yeah, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Heh, you know what I'm sayin'?"], "meaning": "This line is a homage to Three 6 Mafia’s, “Whatcha Know”. DJ Paul, the producer on this track, is a member of the group.\n\nThe “gyeah!” heard in this line was also heard on Drake’s More Life track, “Gyalchester”.", "annotation_id": "14905000", "url": "https://genius.com/14905000", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "You might think I’ve taken some lumps Only if we talkin’ ’bout sums (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["You might think I've taken some lumps", "Only if we talkin' 'bout sums (Yeah)"], "meaning": "The phrase, “taken some lumps” refers to someone receiving punishment or a loss for one’s actions. Drake, however suggests that the only “lumps” he takes are those of money.\n\nThis may be a response to people suggesting that Drake lost his 2018 Pusha-T beef.", "annotation_id": "14894756", "url": "https://genius.com/14894756", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "My money is young, my problems are old", "vault_lines": ["My money is young, my problems are old"], "meaning": "Drake uses a double entendre to reference his growing sources of new wealth in addition to his record label, Young Money Entertainment. Three weeks before the release of Scorpion, Drake’s mentor, Lil Wayne, reached a settlement with label imprint Cash Money Records after years of legal battles over withheld payments.\n\nSince signing to the label in 2009, Drake has had his fair share of label problems as seen on tracks such as “Star67.” Many continue to speculate about Drake’s departure from the label as detailed in another Scorpion cut, “Is There More”:\n\nYeah, soon as this album drop I’m out of the deal\n\nIn addition to label complications, Drake continues to face business and personal situations which outlast his wealth, as explained on “Weston Road Flows” off of his 2016 album Views:\n\n40 the only one that know how I deal with the pressure\nA couple nights where it started to feel like the feelings fadin'\nA lot of problems that can’t be fixed with a conversation", "annotation_id": "14887034", "url": "https://genius.com/14887034", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "(Dopeman!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song heavily samples the instrumental and chorus of NWA’s infamous 1987 song “Dopeman”.\n\nIn 2018 Ice Cube did an interview in the aftermath of Drake and Pusha’s beef, where he predicted that Drake’s reign is over. However he later clarified that he wasn’t speaking on Drake specifically, but about the game in general:\n\nYou usually have a three, maybe five-year reign at the tip-top before the industry is looking for something new\n\nThis whole song might be an acknowledgement of Cube and his statements via the Dopeman sample.", "annotation_id": "14885749", "url": "https://genius.com/14885749", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Heh, you know what I’m sayin’? We in the buildin’, we came for a billion, ain’t nobody playin’", "vault_lines": ["Woo! You know what I'm sayin'?", "Woo! Ayy, ayy, yeah, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Yeah, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Ayy, ayy, yeah, you know what I'm sayin'?", "Heh, you know what I'm sayin'?", "We in the buildin', we came for a billion, ain't nobody playin'"], "meaning": "According to multiple sources, such as Forbes  and Time Magazine, JAY-Z and Beyoncé have a combined net worth of about 1.6 billion.", "annotation_id": "14882994", "url": "https://genius.com/14882994", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Live every word that I’m rappin’, say I lost ninety bricks and it happened You probably wouldn’t believe everything that you seein’ right now if it wasn’t live action", "vault_lines": ["Live every word that I'm rappin', say I lost ninety bricks and it happened", "You probably wouldn't believe everything that you seein' right now if it wasn't live action"], "meaning": "JAY-Z stays true to the stories that he details in his rhymes. For instance, on the September 2001 track “Never Change” he says:\n\nLost 92 bricks, had to fall back\nKnocked a nigga off his feet, but I crawled back\nHad A-1 credit, got more crack\nFrom the first to the fifth, gave it all back\n\nHere, he juxtaposes real life occurrences with the term “live action”—meaning live action cinematography. He used similar imagery on December 1999’s “There’s Been a Murder”:\n\nBack to live action, I’m packin, I’m still in the mix\nLike new hits, I think I’m goin' over your head a lil' bit\n\nIt’s possible that this is a subtle nod to the upcoming live action Hamilton film, which JAY-Z is helping to produce alongside Lin-Manuel Miranda.", "annotation_id": "14882506", "url": "https://genius.com/14882506", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "First Rollie flooded out, I ain’t see no time, woah", "vault_lines": ["First Rollie flooded out, I ain't see no time, woah"], "meaning": "In a classic Hov double entendre, here time refers to both the fact he never served any time in prison for any of the illegal activities he conducted in his occupation before rapping; and to the occupation of the Rolex he is wearing. Jay can’t tell the time on his Rollie because the watch is encrusted with hundreds of diamonds. It’s hard to see the hands when the entire watch face is shining.\n\nAs a watch aficionado, Jay has owned many Rolex timepieces throughout his life. He famously wore a Rolex “Day-Date” on the cover of his 1997 sophomore album In My Lifetime, Vol. 1; and in 1999 he was the first person in the United States to own the “Day-Date I Masterpiece Platinum.” The rare platinum timepiece came embellished with 115 diamonds.", "annotation_id": "14880371", "url": "https://genius.com/14880371", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "I promise I’m bridgin’ the gap", "vault_lines": ["I promise I'm bridgin' the gap"], "meaning": "A play on his prior juxtaposition of young money, old problems. But also a double-entendre with a nod to Jay-Z’s presence on the song.\n\nHov is regarded as the Michael Jordan of rap to many, but to younger listeners he’s Jordan the team owner, not Jordan 6-time champion on the Chicago Bulls. Jay’s subject matter and attitude on this guest verse feels closer to his younger days than some of his recent work, and his prior collaborations with Drake.\n\nDrizzy is showcasing vintage Jigga to some of the youth that might be unaware.", "annotation_id": "14879539", "url": "https://genius.com/14879539", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "My mentions are jokes, but they never give me the facts This isn’t that, can’t be ignorin’ the stats", "vault_lines": ["This isn't that, can't be ignorin' the stats", "My mentions are jokes, but they never give me the facts"], "meaning": "Twitter mentions comprise a section of Twitter where you can see all the tweets you’ve been mentioned in. Drake’s mentions consist of many memes, something that Drake openly embraced during his beef with Meek Mill, as well as during the aftermath of his “Hotline Bling” video, which was one of the most parodied music videos of all time.\n\nHowever, this line could also mean that a lot of people in his mentions don’t take him seriously, ignoring his career successes—like how is two previous albums, Views and More Life, sold 1.04 million and 505,000 copies within their first weeks of availability—and instead focusing on drama, like his ghostwriting scandal.", "annotation_id": "14878224", "url": "https://genius.com/14878224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "If Alpo didn’t snitch, niggas’d be like Young", "vault_lines": ["If Alpo didn't snitch, niggas'd be like Young"], "meaning": "Alberto Geddis Martinez, a.k.a, “Alpo” was a notorious drug dealer and gangster from Harlem, New York. Alpo reigned over New York in the 80s when crack/cocaine was more popular. Eventually Alpo’s fast life caught up to him and he was arrested in DC on November 6, 1991. Shortly being arrested Alpo turned into a government informant, a.k.a. a snitch. According to Don Diva Magazine, he was released from federal prison in 2015 and is now waiting in the Witness Protection Program.", "annotation_id": "14878137", "url": "https://genius.com/14878137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "I’m what Meech shoulda been", "vault_lines": ["I'm what Meech shoulda been"], "meaning": "Demetrius Flenory, otherwise known as “Big Meech,” and his brother Terry “Southwest T” were the ringleaders of Black Mafia Family (BMF); a drug trafficking ring. Atlanta was the hub of the distribution.\n\nHowever, they also ventured into the hip hop scene through their company BMF Entertainment.\n\nMeech was eventually locked up, and his involvement in rap stalled. This never happened to Jay. He sold drugs and advanced in rap game.\n\nJay and Meech have something of a history and Jay has mentioned BMF before on “D.O.A”.", "annotation_id": "14878025", "url": "https://genius.com/14878025", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Lot of sixs in here tonight", "vault_lines": ["Lot of sixs in here tonight"], "meaning": "DJ Paul, one of the founders of seminal hardcore hip-hop group Three 6 Mafia co-produced this song—while Drake is a representative of his home city Toronto, Canada, a.k.a. “The 6.”", "annotation_id": "14878021", "url": "https://genius.com/14878021", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Yo, get close enough to Hov, smell like a kilo still (Still) First album at twenty six, I ain’t need no deal (Real)", "vault_lines": ["Yo, get close enough to Hov, smell like a kilo still (Still)", "First album at twenty six, I ain't need no deal (Real)"], "meaning": "JAY-Z opens with a reference to his drug dealing days in the late ‘80s and early '90s. A “kilo” refers to a brick of cocaine. Jigga sold so much product that you can still smell it on his clothes—even after being 24 years removed from the occupation.\n\nDope boy still smelling like cocaína\n\nThis hustlers spirit lead to JAY-Z and his associates setting up their own record label, Roc-A-Fella Records, in 1994. However, it’s important to note that Jay, Dame and “Biggs” did not use illegal money to fund Roc-A-Fella:\n\nWe didn’t need dirty money to start Roc-A-Fella, because we had Payday’s money and just so you know, I didn’t want to ask Priority for shit. They would have kicked in some money [for overhead expenses], but I wanted to do this on my own so the profit was mine, free and clear. At a certain point if you ain’t livin’ right you want to do things legally. You want to leave your past behind and start looking for the future.\n\nOnce the record label was established, Jay released his debut album Reasonable Doubt (1996) at age 26.", "annotation_id": "14877438", "url": "https://genius.com/14877438", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Stand-up niggas, we only duckin’ indictments", "vault_lines": ["Stand-up niggas, we only duckin' indictments"], "meaning": "To be a “stand-up” guy connotes a “high degree of personal integrity or loyalty.” It can also be interpreted as a call for audience members to rise to their feet.\n\nThe succeeding line refers to JAY-Z maneuvering around possible lawsuits being levied against him. Ideally, he ‘ducks indictments’ by not being in contravention of the law to begin with.\n\nHowever, in 2018, JAY-Z was subpoenaed to appear in court by the SEC, in connection with the sale of his Rocawear clothing company. He has also allegedly avoided a paternity test concerning a supposed love child named “Rymir Satterthwaite.”\n\nThis lyric recalls a stanza from his featured verse on DJ Khaled’s “I Got the Keys”:\n\nI got a bag for lawyers, like, ‘Fuck your charges!’\nHop out the courtroom, like, ‘What charges?’\nBig pimpin' on your court steps\nCase y'all ain’t notice, I ain’t lost yet…", "annotation_id": "14877365", "url": "https://genius.com/14877365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "“He’s at the top,” and “he’s at the top,” but nobody stayin’", "vault_lines": ["\"He's at the top,\" and \"he's at the top,\" but nobody stayin'"], "meaning": "It is possible that these lines reference comments made by Ice Cube in the aftermath of Drake and Pusha’s beef. Ice Cube predicted that Drake’s reign has come to an end. The background sample of Dopeman might suggest this as well.\n\nHowever Ice Cube later clarified that he wasn’t speaking on Drake specifically, but about the game in general:\n\nYou usually have a three, maybe five-year reign at the tip-top before the industry is looking for something new\n\nDrake might be acknowledging this comment but flipping it, using “he” to refer both to himself and to others that are currently hot. While trends come and go alongside other rappers, none hold the longevity that he does.", "annotation_id": "14877027", "url": "https://genius.com/14877027", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Dope boys, Off-White, lookin’ like soft white on ’em", "vault_lines": ["Dope boys, Off-White, lookin' like soft white on 'em"], "meaning": "Hov feels that the current generation of “dope boys”, AKA drug dealers, are “soft” in comparison to Hov and his generation. Hov expresses his discontentment with his successors whilst referencing the immensely popular clothing brand Off-White, which Hov has been seen wearing numerous times.", "annotation_id": "14876725", "url": "https://genius.com/14876725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "You know where I’m at, I put the six on the map", "vault_lines": ["You know where I'm at, I put the six on the map"], "meaning": "“The 6” is the famous Drake-forged nickname of Toronto. It is in honor of the six municipalities making up the Greater Toronto Area and the merging of the city’s area codes, 416 and 647.\n\nDrake is one of the first rappers from Toronto to gain worldwide fame and recognition. Since his early career, Drizzy has been repping his city and proudly putting on local artists. Subsequently, Toronto has become a cultural hip-hop hub and has produced numerous successful artists who have been inspired by, or collaborated with, Drake such as The Weeknd, Preme and Tory Lanez.\n\nOn a world map, only the larger cities or capitals have labels (aside from countries, continents, oceans, etc). Drake says he put his hometown “on the map”—an idiom meaning to make something famous.", "annotation_id": "14876694", "url": "https://genius.com/14876694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "I ain’t on the ’Gram, they record who I am", "vault_lines": ["I ain't on the 'Gram, they record who I am"], "meaning": "Jay-Z drops a triple entendre (don’t even ask me how):\n\nHe isn’t on the ‘gram as in the photo-sharing site Instagram. His wife, however, is one of the more popular posters on the site, so he probably doesn’t feel the need to have his own account since Beyoncé or other associates like AintNoJigga could post pictures of him. Also, wherever he goes, paparazzi follows and they post videos or pictures of him on the Internet for the public to see—even pics of him and Bey when they’re trying to be low-key. Jay also doesn’t need Instagram since engineers such as Young Guru record who he is through the visuals created in the music.\n\nThe man who claimed he lost 92 bricks also stays off the gram as in (kilo)grams of cocaine, whether consuming them himself or going back to selling them. Jay wants to keep his mind sharp on his legitimate business since, again, the paparazzi—and possibly the police—are watching his every move and would be looking for any reason to destroy his legacy in order to make a name for themselves.\n\nAs far as his musical legacy is concerned, Jay-Z doesn’t want to be recorded taking L’s amongst his peers in a widely televised event, so this line could also mean that he is going back to boycotting the Grammys. After last attending in 2004 to support Beyoncé’s Dangerously in Love album, Jay decided to attend the 2018 ceremony in February due to his album, 4:44, having eight nominations. Four months later, on the single “APESHIT” from his joint album with Beyoncé, Everything is Love, Jay sent a message to the Recording Academy regarding 4:44’s lack of wins:\n\nTell the Grammys ‘fuck that 0-for-8 shit’", "annotation_id": "14876512", "url": "https://genius.com/14876512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 107, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "I’m what Supreme didn’t become", "vault_lines": [", I'm what Supreme didn't become"], "meaning": "Here JAY-Z is referencing Queens drug lord Kenneth “Supreme” McGriff. The drug trafficker came to prominence in the early-1980s when he formed the “Supreme Team,” a large crack cocaine distribution ring.\n\nThe Supreme Team became one of the most polarizing crews to feature in the golden age of hip-hop. From their ‘80s crack-era heyday to Supreme’s close affiliation with Irv Gotti’s Murder Inc. Records in the '90s and early-'00s, The Supreme Team name has stayed relevant in the rap world. Many of their crimes were linked to rap too: its been alleged that Supreme had Jam Master Jay from Run-D.M.C. murdered and had 50 Cent shot.\n\nIn 2007 Supreme was found guilty of murder conspiracy and drug trafficking, and was imprisoned. He is currently incarcerated in Virginia.\n\n\n\nHov met Supreme in the ‘90s, and according to Irv Gotti the drug trafficker played a crucial role in getting “Ain’t No Nigga” delivered to a wider audience.\n\nEarly early Roc-A-Fella days. Jay had released ‘Dead Presidents.’ Wasn’t getting any real movement. This was also around the time I was chilling wit Preme and BJ everyday. Hov had just made ‘Ain’t No Nigga’ with Foxy. Preme would get in the car and say ‘Play that Jay joint with Foxy.’ He absolutely loves that record. He loved the line ‘Weapons concealed what the fuck y’all feel, when y’all niggas play sick we can all get ill.’ He would be like, ‘Jay is VICIOUS man!’ Through Supreme’s energy for the record I went to my Roc-A-Fella brothers. Begged them to put this record out. But ‘Dead Presidents’ is what they was already going with. Shot a video to it and everything. But through me pressing them, they said Fuck it' and made it the B-Side of ‘Dead Presidents.’ But that’s all I needed to run with it … “ANN” makes me think of Supreme! He championed that record with me. ‘Play that Jay shit man, he a vicious motherfucker.‘ All we would play. Riding around Jamaica, Queens. Harlem. Brooklyn. Everywhere.\n\nIn 2005 it was revealed that Supreme was planning to ask Hov for money to fund his defense against multiple racketeering, narcotics and murder charges.", "annotation_id": "14876447", "url": "https://genius.com/14876447", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "I got your President tweetin’, I won’t even meet with him", "vault_lines": ["I got your President tweetin', I won't even meet with him"], "meaning": "In January of 2018, the president of the United States, Donald Trump controversially referred to African countries as what he called, “shithole countries.” This caused much criticism towards the president and provoked JAY-Z to discuss the remarks on a CNN Saturday Night segment with Van Jones.\n\nYou treat people like human beings. That’s the main point. It goes back to the whole thing—‘Treat me really bad and pay me well.’ It’s not going to lead to happiness, it’s going to lead to, again, the same thing. Everyone’s going to be sick.\n\nThe next morning, Trump took it to his Twitter account to ‘fire back’ at Hov.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/realDonaldTrump/status/957603800579297280?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDue to Trump’s high power and presidency, Jay uses the tweet to brag that he gets recognition from him.\n\nThis line could also be taken as a shot at Kanye West who has become famously known to be a Trump supporter. In August 2017, Ye met with the president himself in Trump Tower. In this line, Jay is simply calling Kanye a hypocrite for supporting the president and meeting him—even though he made a racist remark.\n\nJay also rapped about Trump’s tweets on “SALUD!”:\n\nYour president tweeting about Hov like he knows us", "annotation_id": "14876370", "url": "https://genius.com/14876370", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "line": "Y’all killed X and let Zimmerman live, shhh, s-streets is done", "vault_lines": ["Y'all killed X and let Zimmerman live, shhh, s-streets is done"], "meaning": "JAY-Z mentions the late Floridian rapper XXXTENTACION, who was murdered at a motorsport shop in Deerfield Beach, Florida on June 18th, 2018.\n\nHe compares the murder of the young rapper to the case of George Zimmermann, a Florida man who became infamous for the killing of unarmed black teen, Trayvon Martin, in which he claimed self-defense based on Florida’s ‘Stand your ground’ law. He was found not guilty of second degree murder in a trial by jury. The Black Panthers put a $10,000 bounty on his head, but he remains alive today, allegedly boasting about killing Trayvon.\n\nWith these bars, Jay is voicing his frustration with both the state and citizens of Florida: they let a young rapper die a premature death, but couldn’t put an end to a man who murdered an unarmed black teen.\n\nSince the murder of Martin, Jay has connected with his family and lent them both financial and emotional support. In July 2018 Hov’s latest docuseries production, Rest in Power: The Trayvon Martin Story will premiere on the Paramount Network.", "annotation_id": "14876268", "url": "https://genius.com/14876268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 544, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Talk Up", "status": "done", "targets": 25, "clicked": 25, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-talk-up-lyrics", "genius_title": "Talk Up"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Trust Issues", "line": "Drizzy Drake, check me out Coming live from the motherfuckin’ North Side Kick game, run game, run it real good But never ever have my bitches sitting courtside Same nigga that you knew way back when You acting like it’s somebody you don’t know Tell me, how the fuck we ’posed to stay friends When you got a bunch of feelings that you don’t show?", "vault_lines": ["Drizzy Drake, check me out", "Coming live from the motherfuckin' North Side", "Kick game, run game, run it real good", "But never ever have my bitches sitting courtside", "Same nigga that you knew way back when", "You acting like it's somebody you don't know", "Tell me, how the fuck we 'posed to stay friends", "When you got a bunch of feelings that you don't show?"], "meaning": "Idk what this means", "annotation_id": "39532931", "url": "https://genius.com/39532931", "state": "pending", "votes": -9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
