{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Press this red button, dog, and everything forever changes", "vault_lines": ["Press this red button, dog, and everything forever changes"], "meaning": "Referring to Forever by drake feat Kanye west", "annotation_id": "38199311", "url": "https://genius.com/38199311", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Even when you pad the stats, period, I never hated", "vault_lines": ["Even when you pad the stats, period, I never hated"], "meaning": "Drake is calling out artists who inflate their numbers, whether that be through fake streams, marketing bundles, or label tactics, to make themselves look more successful than they really are. He points out that even when people do this, he doesn’t hate or speak out about it. He sees through the industry games, but he stays above it, confident in his own position.\n\nDrake also plays on the fact that period refers to menstruation, which one could use menstrual pads for.", "annotation_id": "36287856", "url": "https://genius.com/36287856", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "The most decorated, competition decimated", "vault_lines": ["The most decorated, competition decimated"], "meaning": "Drake has been officially named Billboard’s Artist of the Decade for the 2010s, an honor recognizing his immense success throughout the decade. He holds the record for the most Billboard Hot 100 hits and the most Billboard Music Awards overall.", "annotation_id": "32955385", "url": "https://genius.com/32955385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Even when you stab me in the back, the vest is metal-plated", "vault_lines": ["Even when you stab me in the back, the vest is metal-plated"], "meaning": "Drake is telling us that no matter what people try to do to him, he’s always ready or has something up his sleeve.\n\nIf you try to stab him in the back, he will have something up his sleeve, in this case, he wears a metal plated vest.", "annotation_id": "30131932", "url": "https://genius.com/30131932", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "My drive is dedicated, your drive is designated Niggas got you to the spot and then you separated", "vault_lines": ["My drive is dedicated, your drive is designated", "Niggas got you to the spot and then you separated"], "meaning": "Drake uses “drive” alluding to both his motivation, him being dedicated, as well as in reference to a chauffer.\nHis drive(driver) is dedicated(is formally employed )and has a long-term commitment to being Aubrey’s driver, while most people would have to have a designated driver(a person who abstains from alcohol at a social gathering so as to be fit to drive others home) and has a short-term commitment to whoever they’re supposed to be driving and often separate after that commitment has been fulfilled. Separating himself from the regular person.", "annotation_id": "30112819", "url": "https://genius.com/30112819", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "And then we’ll see who’s really crazy", "vault_lines": ["And then we'll see who's really crazy"], "meaning": "The song title “Red Button” and various lyrics throughout, along with the multiple references to Ye, suggest a callback to Ye’s 2018 single “Ghost Town”, widely considered to be one of his best songs ever from his self-titled eighth studio solo album.\n\nIn “Ghost Town”, PND (and in the SNL and various other live versions, Ye) sings:\n\n“Some days, I’m not gonna please everybody”\n“Some day, I’m gonna hit that red dot on everybody”\nhttps://youtu.be/qAsHVwl-MU4?si=TnV502v98tSq4J8n\n\nDrake is suggesting he will hit the metaphorical “red dot” or Red Button and go “nuclear” on everyone before Ye does. The “red button” is a symbolic term referring to the mechanism or authority to launch a nuclear strike, typically associated with the President of the United States or other world leaders who have nuclear arsenals.\n\n\n\nEDIT 1: Almost 6 months later, on May 3, 2024, Drake’s red dot was finally pressed in the form of the video for “Family Matters”, which was almost immediately obliterated by Kendrick Lamar’s “meet the grahams” and subsequent global smash hit “Not Like Us”. Turns out, Drake was bluffing the whole time.\n\n\n\nEDIT 2: On February 9, 2025, Kendrick lampooned the irony of Drake’s ultimately anticlimactic red button by displaying a gigantic circular red icon with the Apple Music logo at the beginning of his halftime performance at Super Bowl LIX. With this performance, Kendrick became the first solo rapper ever to headline a Super Bowl halftime show, the most viewed event in television history, surpassing the Apollo 11 Moon landing in U.S. television viewership, garnering an average of 133.5 million viewers across all platforms.", "annotation_id": "30112241", "url": "https://genius.com/30112241", "state": "pending", "votes": -21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "I will fuckin’ double-cross you niggas like it’s meditation", "vault_lines": ["I will fuckin' double-cross you niggas like it's meditation"], "meaning": "To double-cross someone comes from the world of espionage and refers to betraying one’s own side or organization to betray another.\n\nMeditation is a practice of mindfulness, seen in many traditions. Common poses in meditation require one to cross their legs.", "annotation_id": "30110304", "url": "https://genius.com/30110304", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Word to M-Dolla, she the only one could maybe save it Should’ve hit you first, but, sis, you know about the shit I’ve taken", "vault_lines": ["Word to M-Dolla, she the only one could maybe save it", "Should've hit you first, but, sis, you know about the shit I've taken"], "meaning": "Drake refers to one of Rihanna’s closest friends, Melissa Forde—also known as M-Dolla. Noting the gals' everlasting friendship, Drake claims she could be the gateway to peace between him and Rihanna, who he’d taken shots at on the original For All The Dogs; most notably on “Fear of Heights,” where the song opened with:\n\nAyy, ayy, look\nWhy they make it sound like I’m still hung up on you?\nThat could never be\nGyal can’t run me\nBetter him than me\nBetter it’s not me\nI’m anti, I’m anti\nYeah, and the sex was average with you\nYeah, I’m anti ‘cause I had it with you\nOkay, I’m auntie like your daddy’s sister\nAuntie like a family picture\nAnd I had way badder bitches than you, TBH\nYeah, that man, he still with you, he can’t leave you\nY'all go on vacation, I bet it’s Antigua", "annotation_id": "30103471", "url": "https://genius.com/30103471", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "I’ll give you a hard pill to swallow, this your medication I will fuckin’ pop up on your ass like a revelation I could tell you better than I show you, this a demonstration I will fuckin’ leave you in the dirt like some vegetation Chemicals is mixin’ in my brain and killin’ hesitation I will fuckin’ force a few shots like a vaccination", "vault_lines": ["I'll give you a hard pill to swallow, this your medication", "I will fuckin' pop up on your ass like a revelation", "I could tell you better than I show you, this a demonstration", "I will fuckin' leave you in the dirt like some vegetation", "Chemicals is mixin' in my brain and killin' hesitation", "I will fuckin' force a few shots like a vaccination", "I'll fuckin'—, I'll fuckin'—"], "meaning": "Drake expresses his vitriol for Kanye, while referencing characteristics that are commonly associated to Kanye; Such as Kanye’s mental health issues, anger, & strong faith in God.\n\nPhrases like “hard pill to swalllow”, “pop up” “medication”, “chemicals mixing in my brain” & “forcing shots like vaccination”, all play off of the infamous debate between Kanye and his mental health. It is no secret that Kanye refuses to take prescribed medication to help treat his bipolar disorder, which is a mental disorder associated mood swings & manic episodes, with the disorder being linked to a chemical imbalance in the brain. Kanye’s countless controversies over the years have left many to label him “crazy”, and Drizzy plays off this idea of being crazy with bars that not only threatens great bodily harm towards Kanye with dark imagery, but with references mentioned earlier about chemical imbalance and describing actions that could only be carried out by an unstable person if done in real life. It captures the magnitude of animosity Drake has towards Ye. Drake  also uses biblial references, saying he will appear in front of Kanye like a “revelation”, which is a word commonly associated with the bible, which plays on Kanye’s strong faith in God.\n\n“Leave you in the dirt” references Yeezy’s own hatred Pete Davidson, after the comedian was depicted in the dirt in Ye’s music video “Eazy”, which was criticized for displaying violent imagery toward’s Pete over his then relationship with Kanye’s ex wife Kim Kardashian.", "annotation_id": "30102956", "url": "https://genius.com/30102956", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Niggas think it’s sweet, but I am not a diabetic patient No", "vault_lines": ["Niggas think it's sweet, but I am not a diabetic patient"], "meaning": "Diabetes is a chronic disease that occurs either when the pancreas does not produce enough insulin or when the body cannot effectively use the insulin it produces. Sweet, sugary substances are linked to be the no. 1 cause of diabetes in patients.\n\nTo refer to something being sweet means that a person thinks it’s easier to do something than it really is. In this case, Drake jokes that being as successful a rapper as he is isn’t something that people should think is ‘sweet’.", "annotation_id": "30102432", "url": "https://genius.com/30102432", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Man, all this luggage in the lobby like I’m gettin’ traded", "vault_lines": ["Man, all this luggage in the lobby like I'm gettin' traded"], "meaning": "The NBA,  a sporting organisation Drake is renowned for showing a great love towards, frequently sees players being traded from one team to another. Trades occur between cities and states, thus requiring a player to pack their belongings and luggage to move.\n\nAdditionally, he compares this to himself, where he raps on how he too is carrying luggage in the form of personal issues. These personal issues may potentially be a result of previous beefs or relationships, like the content he raps about on the album’s final track, You Broke My Heart.", "annotation_id": "30102394", "url": "https://genius.com/30102394", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Radio is king again, Billboard got me regulated Wanna make it fair for you, numbers that I generated", "vault_lines": ["Radio is king again, Billboard got me regulated", "Wanna makе it fair for you, numbers that I generatеd"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing random-number generators which are meant to produce numbers randomly and fairly to avoid bias.\n\nIn recent years, Drake has gained a reputation for being able to produce high charting billboard songs, that are also majoritively radio friendly.\n\nDrake references the change Billboard made in how it counts it’s streaming numbers. He also speaks on how radio plays count for more towards Billboard chart placements.\n\nDrake currently has 13 Billboard Number 1 songs and is tied in first with Michael Jackson all time. As stated in First Person Shooter, he needed 1 more for the tie, and that same song went number 1. Both of these artists are well known for making radio hits.\n\nDrake is saying he’s being blackballed and radioplay is being used against him. This can be observed on the US radio charts – Drake receives a fraction of the radio popstars do, despite his songs receiving far more streams.", "annotation_id": "30102367", "url": "https://genius.com/30102367", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Niggas fuckin’ call me up to cap, this not a graduation", "vault_lines": ["Niggas fuckin' call me up to cap, this not a graduation"], "meaning": "This line could be interpreted as a reference to the long running rivalry between Kanye West and Drake. The mention of “call me up to cap” suggests that any attempt to subtle their beef was disingenuous. The phrase “this not a graduation” may allude to Kanye West’s album “Graduation” and could imply that the situation is not a celebration or positive event but rather the conflict hasn’t been settled.", "annotation_id": "30102299", "url": "https://genius.com/30102299", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "I’ll fuckin’—, I’ll fuckin’—", "vault_lines": ["I'll fuckin'—, I'll fuckin'—", "I'll fuckin' find out wherever y'all are celebratin'"], "meaning": "the same way that Eminem did on Get You Mad Drake like he stated prior is digging deep for anything to show the petty ways he’ll violate and belittle you.", "annotation_id": "30101881", "url": "https://genius.com/30101881", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Tryna see a B inside my circle like I’m gettin’ graded", "vault_lines": ["Tryna see a B inside my circle like I'm gettin' graded"], "meaning": "Drake wants to see a net worth of a billion, or a B, within his crew under his OVO company. The B or billion inside his circle is compared to the way letter grades at the top of graded assignments in school are denoted by teachers.\n\n“See a B”sounds like ‘C’, ‘A’, ‘B’, these letters are also used to grade scores in schools.", "annotation_id": "30101727", "url": "https://genius.com/30101727", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Rarely celebrated, grade eleven educated", "vault_lines": ["Rarely celebrated, grade eleven educated"], "meaning": "Drake was a high school drop out, he dropped out of high school for various reasons. At the age of 15, Drake made the drastic decision to drop out of school. One reason being to help support his ailing, some other factors being bullied in school for his racial and religious background. After believing his class schedule was inauspicious and detrimental, he decide to put his high school ventures on hiatus.\n\n*Note: He did get his diploma in 2012, a decade after dropping out.", "annotation_id": "30101721", "url": "https://genius.com/30101721", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Everyone was good with me, then everyone expression faded", "vault_lines": ["Everyone was good with me, then everyone expression faded"], "meaning": "Drake has never tried to hide his appreciation for Clipse, the infamous rap-duo that consisted of Pusha-T and Pharell. In the 2018 Rap Radar interview, when asked about the Pusha-T autographed microphone that Drake purchased many years ago,  Drake clarifies that his initial infatuation with Pusha-T was prior to before he knew the drug-dealing lyrics and gangster-fueled lyrics were all fictious stories.", "annotation_id": "30101634", "url": "https://genius.com/30101634", "state": "pending", "votes": -14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Leave your label devastated", "vault_lines": ["Leave your label devastated"], "meaning": "After Kanye West went on a string of antisemitic rants in late 2022, his label GOOD Music began to fall apart. Def Jam cut ties and Pusha T stepped down as president of the label.", "annotation_id": "30101598", "url": "https://genius.com/30101598", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "I will fuckin’ put your ass on pause like I’m Pastor Mason", "vault_lines": ["I will fuckin' pop up on your ass like a revelation", "I will fuckin' put your ass on pause like I'm Pastor Mason"], "meaning": "Pastor Mason A.K.A. rapper Ma$e is a co-host of the sports podcast It Is What It Is with Cam'ron.\n\nThe podcast sees them frequently use the word “pause” to negate any homosexual connotations, in a similar fashion to the more popular slang “no homo.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rWN46OSFyvM&ab_channel=REPAUSEORDER", "annotation_id": "30101567", "url": "https://genius.com/30101567", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Pull up, park my Phantom on the curb like I’m Larry David", "vault_lines": ["Pull up, park my Phantom on the curb like I'm Larry David"], "meaning": "Larry David plays himself in his sitcom “Curb your Enthusiasm” often referred to as “Curb”", "annotation_id": "30101540", "url": "https://genius.com/30101540", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "I will start blackin’ over here like it’s segregation", "vault_lines": ["I will start blackin' over here like it's segregation"], "meaning": "The term ‘blackin’ is slang for ‘going off’ or exerting one’s self to the fullest.\n\nDrake alludes to Jim Crow laws, which were state and local laws introduced in the Southern United States in the late 19th and early 20th centuries that enforced racial segregation historical racial segregation in US, in which Black and white people were separated.", "annotation_id": "30101508", "url": "https://genius.com/30101508", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Thinkin’ we gon’ finally peace it up and get to levitatin’", "vault_lines": ["Thinkin' we gon' finally peace it up and get to levitatin'"], "meaning": "In an episode of The Shop: with Lebron James, Drake sheds light on and further emphasizes Ye’s impure intentions throughout the attempt of hashing out their problems. While Drake was genuinely ready to collaborate upon being requested in Wyoming where Ye was working on “ye”, Ye was “…trolling me!”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BkHSfgOOg6k\n\nDrake uses the word levitating as a simile to allude towards the ‘Lift Yourself’ incident, where Ye asked Drake to fly out to Wyoming and help work on a song for his then-new album titled “ye”, only for the song they collaborated on to have the CDQ version released full of senseless words such as ‘poopity scoop’.", "annotation_id": "30101294", "url": "https://genius.com/30101294", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Every time you need me for a boost, I never hesitated", "vault_lines": ["Every time you need me for a boost, I never hesitated"], "meaning": "Likely alluding to Ye’s infamous ‘Yeezy Boost’ sneaker collection, Drake sheds light on his tarnished long-friendship with Ye, sharing that he never hesitated when Ye needed him for anything. Aubrey uses these preliminary bars to cultivate a foundation for further expression on the diminish of their relationship.", "annotation_id": "30101214", "url": "https://genius.com/30101214", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Tickin’ time bomb and they beggin’ me to detonate it If I press this red button, dog, everybody Heaven Gated", "vault_lines": ["Tickin' time bomb and they beggin' me to detonate it", "If I press this red button, dog, everybody Heaven Gated", "Press this red button, dog, and everything forever changes"], "meaning": "The “red button” is a metaphor for a detonation device and the illuminated button on studio recording equipment, indicating that a recording is in progress or a broadcast is underway. Drake believes his words have incendiary potential, and his opposition is at the mercy of his microphone; activating the “red button,” or initiating a recording, will ‘blow up’ his competition.\n\nDrake may be taking a shot at Ye, Kendrick Lamar, or another celebrity, hinting that he holds information capable of terminating their career with just the ‘push of a button.’ In a previous track on For All The Dogs, titled “8am in Charlotte,” Drake claims he possesses video footage that may severely impact the reputation of an unnamed individual. Similarly, in the 2018 track “Behind Barz,” he references having access to private information that he does not reveal.\n\nHeaven’s Gate was an American new-age religious group, led by Marshall Applewhite, which gained notoriety for committing mass suicide in 1997. The San Diego-based group believed an alien spacecraft trailed the Hale-Bopp comet and that mass suicide would facilitate their transportation to the spaceship when the comet’s orbit approached Earth. Photos of the suicide ritual’s aftermath were widely circulated in the press, showing members of the group in matching uniforms, lying in their beds, deceased—imagery which Drake evokes here.\n\n\n\n\nOn the night of May 3, 2024, after a slew of back-and-forth jabs between Drake & Kendrick Lamar in the preceding weeks, Drake posted a photo on his Instagram story of a cartoon owl looking at a red button, a reference to the song & lyrics of “Red Button.” Moments later, Drake released “Family Matters,” his third diss track aimed at Lamar after “Push Ups” & “Taylor Made Freestyle,” where he directly responds to Lamar’s “euphoria” & “6:16 in LA” with a flurry of allegations against Lamar and his family & friends, including his fiancée Whitney Alford, cousin Baby Keem, record executive Dave Free, rapper Rick Ross, singer The Weeknd, and others; Lamar immediately responded with the song “​meet the grahams,” released the same night as “Family Matters.”", "annotation_id": "30101168", "url": "https://genius.com/30101168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Do right and kill everything, people knew that death awaited", "vault_lines": ["Do right and kill everything, people knew that death awaited"], "meaning": "Drake uses the letters of his middle name to create the phrase, “Do Right And Kill Everything,” he also made the same play on words on the track “Miss Me”:\n\nAnd Drake just stand for Do Right and Kill Everything\n\nHe purposely references the line in order to remind you that he informed us beforehand that “death awaited” and he would indeed, kill everything.", "annotation_id": "30101053", "url": "https://genius.com/30101053", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Taylor Swift the only nigga that I ever rated Only one could make me drop the album just a little later Rest of y’all, I treat you like you never made it", "vault_lines": ["Taylor Swift the only nigga that I ever rated", "Only one could make me drop the album just a little later", "Rest of y'all, I treat you like you never made it"], "meaning": "To avoid missing out on sales and higher spots on Billboard top 100, artists would delay their music on the week of new Drake music. However, in Drake’s case, Taylor Swift is the one that has that effect on him. From his standpoint, she is the only one that “made it.”\n\nThis was evident during the release week of Drake’s joint album with 21 Savage, Her Loss. The album came 2 weeks after Taylor’s Midnights, which achieved huge success. Her lead single, “Anti-Hero,” dominated the Billboard Top 100, ranking over all of Drake’s new drops. At the time, Drake shared a story on Instagram congratulating 21 Savage for ranking on the top 10, while putting emojis over Taylor’s top 1 position. Many fans considered Drake’s story to be a jab at Taylor, especially with Vinylz’s tweet at the time. Vinylz is a producer that frequently works with Drake, with their latest project being Her Loss.\n\nNonetheless, Drake and Taylor share a long history. They were presumed to be close friends at a certain point, with speculations of them dropping music together. These rumors came around during Drake’s 30th birthday party, when he shared pictures of him with Taylor at the party.\n\n\n\nIn this verse, Drake pays homage to Taylor and gives her his respect (referring to her as “the only nigga” he would ever rate, in any gender). Although both ruling over their own genres, Drake and Taylor remain the biggest names in music. In a previous Instagram photo dump post, Drake again shared a photo (shown below) with Taylor. The caption on that photo read “They too soft to understand the meaning of hard work.” Despite her taking number 1 spots from him, in the end, game recognizes game.\n\n\n\nTaylor Swift is considered one of the biggest artists in history. Examples of her latest accomplishments include:\n\n\nMost weeks at number 1 on Billboard Artist 100 Chart.\nMost consecutive number 1 studio albums on the US Billboard 200.\nMost streamed female act on Spotify.\nMost American Music Awards won.\nIn 2022 on Spotify, she had the most streamed act in 24 hours, most streamed album in 24 hours, and most streamed album in one week.\n\nIn 2023 on Spotify, she broke her own record with 1989 (Taylor’s Version), becoming the most streamed artist in a single day in Spotify history. Her album was also the most streamed album in one day as November 2023.", "annotation_id": "30100998", "url": "https://genius.com/30100998", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "I’ll get to you ten years from now like procrastination", "vault_lines": ["I'll get to you ten years from now like procrastination"], "meaning": "A continuation from a bar of his on his intro to Nothing Was the Same, Tuscan Leather.\n\nJust give it time, we’ll see who’s still around a decade from now\n\nDrake’s longevity is so extensive that it will cover another decade at least.\n\nHe also uses a simile to explain this same meaning, by comparing his duration at the top to the time it takes for a procrastinator to get started on work.\n\nThis bar, combined with the ones that follow, also suggests that he does not forget or forgive and is willing to pull up on his enemies even 10 years after the fact, ready to escalate and spoil their party.", "annotation_id": "30100984", "url": "https://genius.com/30100984", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "line": "Every time that Yeezy called a truce, he had my head inflated", "vault_lines": ["Every time that Yeezy called a truce, he had my head inflated"], "meaning": "In this instance, Drake is saying when Kanye West calls a truce — such as the Free Larry Hoover Concert — that his own ego grows and he feels flattered.\n\nAfter the release of each artists' respective albums in late 2021, Kanye called out Drake to perform side-by-side with him in a once-in-a-lifetime event with the goal of getting Larry Hoover Sr. out of prison. In 1972, Hoover was convicted of murder, conspiracy and extortion and sentenced to six life sentences.", "annotation_id": "30100979", "url": "https://genius.com/30100979", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Red Button", "status": "done", "targets": 28, "clicked": 28, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-red-button-lyrics", "genius_title": "Red Button"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "I feel like I’m always on thin ice with a thick bitch", "vault_lines": ["I feel like I'm always on thin ice with a thick bitch"], "meaning": "To be on thin ice means to have to be extremely careful or always at risk, very similar to the idiom “walking on eggshells.”\n\nDrake is juxtaposing the two opposites (thick and thin) in order to say that he always feels like he has to be careful when he’s with a thick bitch (“thick” being slang for a curvy body / big ass). He’s suggesting that when women are “thick,” they act more entitled or have more sass toward their men.", "annotation_id": "31830711", "url": "https://genius.com/31830711", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "I really hate the fact I make this shit sound effortless I put so much thought into the messages It’s borderline obsessiveness, remember who you messin’ with", "vault_lines": ["I really hate the fact I make this shit sound effortless", "I put so much thought into the messages", "It's borderline obsessiveness, remember who you messin' with"], "meaning": "Drake has stated before that “anybody that knows me knows that my strongest talent is writing”. Given how smooth his lyricism and delivery is, it’s easy to forget or ignore the amount of thought and effort he puts into his work. Papi finds this irritating.", "annotation_id": "31716591", "url": "https://genius.com/31716591", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "The last message she sent to my phone’s ’bout keeping in touch", "vault_lines": ["The last message she sent to my phone's 'bout keeping in touch"], "meaning": "This ties into the lines above:\n\nNadia died in Dubai\nI waited on a spirit to come by for like seventeen months\n\nAs well as the line directly below.\n\nDrizzy is telling a sad story here. His last contact with his friend Nadia Ntuli was by text, with her talking about staying in touch. After she died, Drake waited for 17 months hoping that her spirit would come visit him so they could make good on this commitment, but it never came. This has him questioning God and losing faith.", "annotation_id": "31716446", "url": "https://genius.com/31716446", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "And they don’t stay in they lane, it’s like niggas hit the median", "vault_lines": ["And they don't stay in they lane, it's like niggas hit the median"], "meaning": "Rappers nowadays try to venture into different “lanes” by making songs they shouldn’t be making. “Stay in your lane” means to mind your own business and stick to what you’re good at.\n\nA median is a physical barrier that separates two-way lanes on the road.\n\nhttps://images.app.goo.gl/L99aFxTSfRUE6mPx8", "annotation_id": "31324085", "url": "https://genius.com/31324085", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "So many shots fired, I need me a clip switch", "vault_lines": ["So many shots fired, I need me a clip switch"], "meaning": "“Shots fired” in rap songs means dissing people.\n\nClips is a row of bullets you put into a weapon so that you can fire bullets from the weapon.\n\nDrake is saying that he has so many shots fired at people (so many disses towards people) that he needs to switch clips. (The shots in the previous clip had ran out)", "annotation_id": "30132059", "url": "https://genius.com/30132059", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "Every day breakin’ records, shit is just happening fast That shit was a quick switch, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Every day breakin' records, shit is just happening fast", "That shit was a quick switch, yeah"], "meaning": "Throughout Drake’s lengthy career, he has broken multiple records. For example, in 2017, Drake beat Adele’s record at the Billboard Music Awards after winning thirteen awards in one night. In 2018, following the release of “God’s Plan,” Drake beat Taylor Swift’s Spotify streaming record alongside Apple Music’s first-day streaming record, not to mention countless other records broken throughout his career.\n\nIn August 2023, Drake passed The Beatles‘ Billboard Hot 100 record for the most top five hits on the chart. However, in October 2023, Drake tied Michael Jackson’s Billboard Hot 100 record for the most #1 hits on the chart, placing him at the joint fourth position on the list. On the J. Cole-accompanied “First Person Shooter,” Drake boasted about his to-come accomplishment—before it debuted at the #1 position—as he rapped:\n\nNiggas talkin' ‘bout when this gon’ be repeated\nWhat the fuck, bro? I’m one away from Michael\nNigga, beat it, nigga, beat it, what?\n\nFollowing “First Person Shooter” tying Michael’s record, Drake had taken to his Instagram to share an image of himself wearing Michael’s iconic sparkling glove.", "annotation_id": "30117991", "url": "https://genius.com/30117991", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "I feel like John Wick, empty clips Yeah, empty clips Yeah, empty clips", "vault_lines": ["I feel like John Wick, empty clips"], "meaning": "Drake references the world-renowned, fictional assassin and ex-hitman, John Wick (portrayed by Keanu Reeves), from the eponymous John Wick movie series. Throughout the series, John Wick can be seen making full use of a vast variety of weapons in a tactile and swift manner to eliminate opposing threats.\n\nHomophonically, Drake uses “clips” and “clipse” to allude to the hip-hop duo, Clipse, which is formed by Drake’s biggest rival, Pusha T, and his older brother, No Malice.\n\nLastly, Drake makes a double entendre with the line. Mocking the duo, he references Clipse’s disbandment in 2010, and alludes to him metaphorically firing at them.", "annotation_id": "30113634", "url": "https://genius.com/30113634", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "So pardon me questioning God and not believing in much", "vault_lines": ["So pardon me questioning God and not believing in much"], "meaning": "The pronounciation of “pardon me” sounds as if Drake is saying “part of me”, referencing part of himself questioning his loyalty to God while the other part pledges its allegiance.", "annotation_id": "30113093", "url": "https://genius.com/30113093", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "They gon’ have to talk to Ouija boards like heebie jeebian Like the large was too big for you, you’ll need a medium Just to send a message to somebody you loved previous", "vault_lines": ["They gon' have to talk to Ouija boards like heebie jeebian", "Like the large was too big for you, you'll need a medium", "Just to send a message to somebody you loved previous"], "meaning": "In these lines, Drake evokes imagery of supernatural communication with the deceased. A medium is a someone who claims to contact the dead, while Ouija boards are instruments for communicating with deceased.", "annotation_id": "30111578", "url": "https://genius.com/30111578", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "Livin’ for right now ’cause I really think heaven’s a front Nadia died in Dubai I waited on a spirit to come by for like seventeen months That shit didn’t visit me once, damn", "vault_lines": ["Livin' for right now 'cause I really think heaven's a front", "Nadia died in Dubai", "I waited on a spirit to come by for like seventeen months", "That shit didn't visit me once, damn"], "meaning": "This refers to the late Nadia Ntuli, a former UK model and friend of Drake who passed away in January 2021. Although there are scarce reports on her death, she is thought to have passed away in a motorcycle accident in Dubai.\n\nLeading up to the release of CLB, Drake posted an Instagram story where he dedicated the album to Ntuli and Mercedes Morr.", "annotation_id": "30104445", "url": "https://genius.com/30104445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "John Cena wouldn’t know emotions I wrestle with", "vault_lines": ["John Cena wouldn't know emotions I wrеstle with"], "meaning": "John Cena is an American actor and professional wrestler, currently signed to WWE. He is heavily linked with one of his catchphrases “You Can’t See Me”, which also was the name of his debut, and currently only, rap album.\n\n\n\nWhile Cena wrestles people, Drake is wrestling emotions that are giving him a tough time.", "annotation_id": "30104336", "url": "https://genius.com/30104336", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "That nigga a munch", "vault_lines": ["Her ex would eat away at her soul, that nigga a munch"], "meaning": "This line is a nod to Bronx rapper Ice Spice’s “Munch (Feelin' U)”.\n\nDuring the song’s chorus, Ice raps “That nigga a munch / Nigga a eater, he ate it for lunch”. During an October 2022 interview with Genius, Ice clarifies her personal interpretation on the term “Munch”:\n\nI would define “munch” as, like, just an eater, or somebody that’s, like, obsessed with you, or it could be a hater. Either way, they still obsessed with you.", "annotation_id": "30104302", "url": "https://genius.com/30104302", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "Boat say he the recipe, I must be the key ingredient", "vault_lines": ["Boat say he the recipe, I must be the key ingredient"], "meaning": "Drake references his close friendship with fellow artist and one of the producers of his latest album For All The Dogs – Lil Yachty and pays homage to the Boat’s recent track “The Secret Recipe” with J. Cole.\n\n\n\nThe line not only underscores their personal bond but also hints at Yachty’s crucial role in the making of the FATD album.", "annotation_id": "30103839", "url": "https://genius.com/30103839", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "Black America love to remind me what my mama look like As if I’d ever fuckin’ forget I’m never enough", "vault_lines": ["Black America love to remind me what my mama look like", "As if I'd ever fuckin' forget"], "meaning": "Drake’s mother, Sandi Graham, is a White Jewish woman.\n\n\n\nDrake and his mother.\n\nWhile Drake considers himself Black, some African Americans refuse to accept him as part of the Black community due to his mother’s complexion. This inability to “fit in” with Black America is something Drake touched on in “You & The 6” from his 2015 album If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late when he rapped:\n\nI used to get teased for being Black\nAnd now I’m here and I’m not Black enough\n\nPusha T, dissed earlier in the song, notably teased Drake about this exactly in his 2018 diss track “The Story of Adidon” when he rapped:\n\nConfused, always felt you weren’t Black enough\nAfraid to grow it ‘cause your 'fro wouldn’t nap enough", "annotation_id": "30101636", "url": "https://genius.com/30101636", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "Capo a wicked nigga, I pray it don’t hit the media", "vault_lines": ["Capo a wicked nigga, I pray it don't hit the media"], "meaning": "Chubbs is a long-time friend of Drake since the earliest days of his career, now ultimately his personal bodyguard. Drake often boasts how safe he is being that Chubbs is always posted somewhere in the background, notably with a strap.\n\nThis is just one of many references to the fact that Chubbs is willing to protect Drake and OVO – recently shedding light on this within his verse on ‘On The Radar’ with Central Cee, “…if Chubbs has on the Chanel side bag, you don’t wanna see him reach inside”.\n\nDrake also previously acknowledged Chubbs' rawness on Daylight, “…Capo the type to show up to the wake” – hypothetically saying that, even if Chubbs were to kill somebody, he would still show up to their viewing or burial.", "annotation_id": "30101428", "url": "https://genius.com/30101428", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wick Man", "line": "Man, I remember niggas was jokin’ ’bout some tick, tick And now that rapper broke as fuck, the boy a statistic", "vault_lines": ["Man, I remember niggas was jokin' 'bout some tick, tick", "And now that rapper broke as fuck, the boy a statistic"], "meaning": "Drake shrugs off an infamous line from “Story of Adidon,” a 2018 diss track aimed at him by Pusha T where Pusha rapped:\n\nOVO 40, hunched over like he 80—tick, tick, tick\nHow much time he got? That man is sick, sick, sick\n\nThe line was a jab at Noah “40” Shebib, one of Drake’s favorite producers and a founding member of OVO Sound, who was diagnosed with multiple sclerosis in 2005. While there does seem to be a consensus that Pusha won the battle, Drake sees himself as the long-term winner. Though Pusha’s $14 million dollar net worth suggests he’s far from broke, many detractors have argued that his career largely plateaued after the feud in 2018.\n\nCalling someone “a statistic” is shorthand for saying they’ve been killed, which Drake believes this diss has done to Pusha.\n\n“Empty clips” may also be a homonymic play on Clipse, the rap duo that Pusha T is part of with his brother No Malice.", "annotation_id": "30101302", "url": "https://genius.com/30101302", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
