{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Well, tell ’em to search my house I bet they find you face down", "vault_lines": ["Well, tell 'em to search my house", "I bet they find you face down"], "meaning": "Wolf ends the intro with a defiant and lethal warning. After establishing herself as someone who was exploited, she now acts as the bait or the “gatekeeper” for the Dog House.\n\n\nThe Invitation: “Tell ‘em to search my house” is a direct challenge to her detractors. It implies that she is no longer hiding and is confident in the protection surrounding her.\nThe Double Entendre of “Face Down”: This phrase carries a heavy weight in rap noir. It suggests that anyone who tries to enter her private space with bad intentions will end up defeated—either figuratively (in a position of submission) or literally (as a victim of the “Big Dog’s” retaliation).\n\n\n\nThis creates a perfect transition into the chorus, shifting the mood from Julia’s vulnerable storytelling to Drake’s aggressive and boastful lifestyle. The victim is gone; the trap is set.", "annotation_id": "39501580", "url": "https://genius.com/39501580", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Big dog loves a crowd", "vault_lines": ["Big dog loves a crowd"], "meaning": "The “Big Dog” is a direct moniker for Drake, echoing the themes from his For All The Dogs era. In this context, the “crowd” represents his massive influence, his OVO entourage, and his status as a global superstar who thrives in the spotlight.\n\nBy introducing this line after describing her own exploitation, Julia Wolf signals a shift in power dynamics. While she was previously alone and “chewed up,” she is now associated with the “Big Dog” who commands attention and leads a pack. It establishes that while the intro started with vulnerability, there is a formidable force backing her up—one that isn’t afraid of being seen or making a scene.", "annotation_id": "39501565", "url": "https://genius.com/39501565", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "They’ve taken everything from me Chew me up and spit me out", "vault_lines": ["They've taken everything from me", "Chew me up and spit me out"], "meaning": "This couplet is a raw depiction of exploitation and disposal. The imagery of being “chewed up and spit out” is a common idiom used to describe the ruthless nature of the music industry or a predatory relationship. It suggests that the subject was treated as a consumable resource—once they were “drained” of their value, energy, or talent, they were discarded like waste.\n\nBy starting the song with this sense of total loss, Wolf establishes a “nothing left to lose” mentality. This sets the stage for the transition into the “Dog House,” where those who were once victims find a more aggressive form of protection or power under the “Big Dog.”", "annotation_id": "39501555", "url": "https://genius.com/39501555", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "When they’re all searching for my body I don’t know who they’ll find", "vault_lines": ["When they're all searching for my body", "I don't know who they'll find"], "meaning": "Wolf uses these lines to explore the theme of identity erasure. The search for her “body” represents people looking for the person she used to be or the “victim” they expect her to be. However, her uncertainty about “who they’ll find” suggests that her experiences—whether in the industry or a personal struggle—have fundamentally altered her soul.\n\nIt functions as a noir-style metaphor: while the world expects to find a corpse (someone defeated), they might instead encounter a version of her that is unrecognizable, cold, or even predatory. It sets a haunting tone, suggesting that the “old Julia” is gone, and something new has taken her place.", "annotation_id": "39501531", "url": "https://genius.com/39501531", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Fuck a money tree, I’m a cash cow (I-I-I—)", "vault_lines": ["Fuck a money tree, I'm a cash cow (I-I-I—)"], "meaning": "This might be a nod to Kendrick Lamar’s October 2012 song, “Money Trees”—Yeat flips Kendrick’s concept, denouncing his “money trees” to boast about his substanial income.\n\nDrake also included a reference to Kendrick’s classic in his May 2024 diss track “Family Matters,” where he said:\n\nDon’t even go back to your hood and plant no money trees", "annotation_id": "39479371", "url": "https://genius.com/39479371", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "She in Hidden Hills in a glass house", "vault_lines": ["She in Hidden Hills in a glass house"], "meaning": "Hidden Hills is a city and wealthy gated community in the Santa Monica Mountains region of Los Angeles County, California, known for its rolling hills and luxury mansions. This line further contributes to the likelihood that this song refers to Kim Kardashian, as Kim’s $60 million mansion is located in this neighborhood.\n\nLuxury mansions often contain  expansive glass interiors and exteriors. However, this line likely also refers to the common idiom “People who live in glass houses shouldn’t throw stones” which means you should not criticize others for faults you also possess, as you are equally vulnerable.\nThe connection here is that Drake has had a longstanding on and off again beef with Kim’s ex-husband Kanye West, which makes it likely that this glass house line refers to the idiom, and Drake is saying that Kanye and possibly Kim are vulnerable to much of the criticism Drake receives.", "annotation_id": "39008379", "url": "https://genius.com/39008379", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Lil’ girls trip, lil’ photo dump, yeah, what?", "vault_lines": ["Lil' girls trip, lil' photo dump, yeah, what?"], "meaning": "Drake describes a group of women that went on a girls trip without their partners, and afterwards posted a photo dump, which is a collection of pictures from the trip, on their Instagram.\n\nThis contrasts with the type of girls' trips Drake has rapped about in previous songs, like on “The Shoe Fits”:\n\nI mean, it was girls there, but it wasn’t a girls trip\n\nIn that context, the women likely cheated on their partners during their ‘girls’ trip.", "annotation_id": "39008312", "url": "https://genius.com/39008312", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Got that lil’ employee mad ’cause he fanned out (One sided)", "vault_lines": ["Got that lil' employee mad 'cause he fanned out (One sided)"], "meaning": "Despite being friends and collaborators in the past, Yeat could be talking about Ken Carson. In June 2025, while performing at Summer Smash Festival, Carson took shots at Yeat and his good friend, Don Toliver for headlining together as a duo at the festival:\n\nFuck you talking about, ain’t no duos\n\nToliver later responded on his Instagram, calling Carson “lame,” to which he replied and bought up Toliver’s baby mother and—at the time—girlfriend, singer Kali Uchis. Yeat stayed quiet during the drama, although this could now be his way of calling Carson out.", "annotation_id": "36908275", "url": "https://genius.com/36908275", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "All my bitches wet for me, I’m suited up, they scuba’d up (Oh yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Mm (Oh, oh, oh, yeah)", "All my bitches wet for me, I'm suited up, they scuba'd up (Oh yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat is making “his bitches wet” , a vulgar way to mention how sexually excited women get around him.\n\nHe states they must have scuba suits on due to an hyperbolic amount of “water” he seems to make them produce from his sheer presence, so much that if they don’t wear a scuba suit they could drown.\n\nThis is another classic line in a song full of references to both Yeat’s and Drake’s natural talent not only getting, but satisfying women.", "annotation_id": "36882755", "url": "https://genius.com/36882755", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Just like Nicki said, money make her cum, what? What?", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, yeah, molly make her, what?", "Just like Nicki said, money make her cum, what? What?"], "meaning": "Reference to rapper Nicki Minaj, who’s known for her bold, sexy lyrics and confident energy.\n\nWhile Nicki has never said “Money makes [me] cum” outright – it was rather Megan Thee Stallion that said “Money makes me cum” in her song “Freak Nasty\" – Drake is clearly channeling that provocative and confident energy Nicki’s known for.\n\nDrizzy and Nicki have had quite the history together. Both originally came up under Young Money/Cash Money Records, and have collaborated on several tracks including “Moment 4 Life” (2010), “Up All Night” and “Make Me Proud” (2011), “Only” and “Truffle Butter” (2014), and most recently, “Needle” and “No Frauds” (2017).\n\nNicki giving Drake a lap dance in the music video for her 2014 song “Anaconda”.", "annotation_id": "36879639", "url": "https://genius.com/36879639", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "I like what I saw and so I cashed out Ya bad, ayy, Cindy, ya bad, ya bad, uh I like what I saw and so I cashed out", "vault_lines": ["I like what I saw and so I cashed out", "Ya bad, ayy, Cindy, ya bad, ya bad, uh"], "meaning": "Drake is shouting out Cindy Jorgji, a Toronto-based photographer with heavy OVO ties. She confirmed the name-drop on her Instagram page by uploading a video of Drake “coming up with dog house on the spot” as she notes in the caption.\n\n\n\n“Cindy, ya bad” also sounds like “sending you a bag,” which aligns with the surrounding lines—Drake likes how this girl looks so he’ll willing to spend money or “cash out” on her.", "annotation_id": "36878131", "url": "https://genius.com/36878131", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Like twizzy said, I cracked the seal then passed out (Hey)", "vault_lines": ["Like twizzy said, I cracked the seal then passed out (Hey)"], "meaning": "Yeat uses a near identical lyric from his unreleased collaboration with Lil Uzi Vert called “Crashëd dat bitch”:\n\nFirst, I crack the seal and then I pass out", "annotation_id": "36871375", "url": "https://genius.com/36871375", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "I got Bentley truck, so fuck a Jag’, I got two cougars up (Oh yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Mm (Oh, oh, oh, yeah)", "I got Bentley truck, so fuck a Jag', I got two cougars up (Oh yeah)"], "meaning": "Bentley and Jaguar are both British luxury car manufacturers. As he notes here, Yeat is more of a Bentley fan and often mentions it in his music—for example, on September 2021’s “Monëy so big,” he raps:\n\nI just pulled up in that Bentley, that bitch got them frog eyes\n\nA cougar is a large, predatory cat that lives in mountains and deserts but it’s also a term for older women pursuing younger men. A jaguar is also another kind of large cat. Yeat uses a double entendre by saying he doesn’t want a Jaguar car because he already has a Bentley and wants “two cougars”—or two older women—with him instead.", "annotation_id": "36866838", "url": "https://genius.com/36866838", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "My money started at a penny, but now it’s a lot It’s hard to swallow the truth when, baby, you swallow me a lot", "vault_lines": ["My money started at a penny, but now it's a lot", "It's hard to swallow the truth when, baby, you swallow me a lot"], "meaning": "“The truth is hard to swallow” is an idiom that means reality is difficult to accept or deal with emotionally.\n\nYeat puts a spin on the phrase—he’s asking this woman to accept or “swallow” his new reality of being rich and successful after being starting out poor but notes it’s gonna be hard to do so as she already “swallows” him while performing oral sex.", "annotation_id": "36855124", "url": "https://genius.com/36855124", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "VAVA on the jacket, baby, Jesse Jo my pants, baby", "vault_lines": ["VAVA on the jacket, baby, Jesse Jo my pants, baby"], "meaning": "VAVA (also known as Gruppo Vava) is Drake’s elusive clothing label, the name standing for Virgil Abloh Vision Always. The brand has not yet been released to the public but surfaced when Drake appeared in VAVA’s “Virgil” Immortal Varsity Jacket in the music video for his 2023 track “8am in Charlotte.”\n\nJesse Jo refers to Jesse Jo Stark, daughter of Richard Stark, the founder of Chrome Hearts. Drake is well known for wearing custom Chrome Hearts pieces, and his frequent collaborations with the brand have become a key part of his style.", "annotation_id": "36841415", "url": "https://genius.com/36841415", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Ayy, ayy, ayy, yeah Daddy Mac’ll make her, Kris Kross’ll make her jump out to Jamaica", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, yeah, molly make her, what?", "Daddy Mac'll make hеr, Kris Kross'll make her jump out to Jamaica"], "meaning": "Reference to the 1992 single “Jump” by Kris Kross:\n\nThe Mac Dad’ll make ya (Jump, jump)\nThe Daddy Mac’ll make ya (Jump, jump)\nKris Kross’ll make ya (Jump, jump)", "annotation_id": "36841387", "url": "https://genius.com/36841387", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Yeah, yeah, molly make her, what? Molly make her dance with her ass out, damn", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, yeah, molly make her, what?", "Molly make her dancе with her ass out, damn", "Molly make her dance with her ass out, yeah, what?", "Molly make her dance with her ass out", "Ayy, yeah, molly make her dance, what?", "Molly make her dance with her ass out, damn (Yeah)"], "meaning": "“Molly” is slang for MDMA—better known as ecstasy. It’s a psychoactive drug that produces feelings of euphoria, heightened energy, and increased sensitivity to touch and music. Under its influence, people often feel more uninhibited and physically expressive. Here, Drake describes how it makes a woman dance provocatively without worrying about how she’s perceived.\n\nThe line’s repetition throughout the track mirrors the hypnotic, looping energy of a party high, while also evoking the compulsive nature of drug use itself.", "annotation_id": "36831802", "url": "https://genius.com/36831802", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Yeah, jump up out the bed, she ’bout to jump, ayy, jump, where? Jump up on the flight, she ’bout to jump, ’bout to jump, yeah Jump like Ja Morant, she ’bout to jump, ’bout to jump", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, jump up out the bed, she 'bout to jump, ayy, jump, where?", "Jump up on the flight, she 'bout to jump, 'bout to jump, yeah", "Jump like Ja Morant, she 'bout to jump, 'bout to jump"], "meaning": "Drake likens a woman’s leap (whether onto him sexually or onto a flight with him) to NBA star Ja Morant. Widely regarded as one of the league’s most athletic players, Ja has become a household name for his explosive hops and highlight-reel dunks. Watch his best dunks here:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/HVjrYjoH6jE?feature=shared", "annotation_id": "36831646", "url": "https://genius.com/36831646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "My money ’bout to faint, it get passed out, ayy, what?", "vault_lines": ["My money 'bout to faint, it get passed out, ayy, what?"], "meaning": "Drake can be generous with his money—in the music video for his January 2018’s hit “God’s Plan,” he gifted $996,631.90 to various families, schools and charities throughout the Miami community.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xpVfcZ0ZcFM\n\nWhen someone “faints,” they momentarily lose consciousness or “pass out,” hence Drake’s reference of his money fainting.", "annotation_id": "36827645", "url": "https://genius.com/36827645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "[Intro: Julia Wolf & Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s inclusion of Queens native Julia Wolf follows on the heels of her third album, PRESSURE, led by the TikTok-viral “In My Room,” now topping 40 million streams and drawing Drake’s attention. More than four years later, she shared the backstory of the pair’s uniting to Rolling Stone:\n\nYeah, so the story goes that he was with a couple friends playing music, and I guess this girl who was a friend of a friend, he didn’t really know her, came up to him and requested my song, “In My Room,” which shout-out the girls for always having my back. He said he heard it and then immediately just reached out. So then I was being woken up at six in the morning the next day being like, oh, my God, Drake followed you. And then he reached out, and we just started working together.\n\nDrake would then go on to make a surprise appearance at Julia’s sold-out Toronto show earlier in 2025, eventually joining her on stage. The pair were spotted posing for photos together, with Drake even tying her stage name to Wolfie’s Deli, a Toronto staple, signaling hometown love and co-signing her artistry.\n\nA longtime fan of Drake, when asked to name her biggest musical inspiration in a 2021 interview, Julia shared, “I have to put Drake at the top for his incredible wordplay and storytelling skills.” She went into more detail in her recent Rolling Stone interview, sharing:\n\nOh, my gosh. There are SoundCloud covers of me, which I probably shouldn’t even say because I don’t want people to find them. I’ve deleted them all. But yeah, I used to do covers and flips of Drake. And then he brought us out to his Wireless Fest show in London, and I knew every song. I was like, what is happening? How do I know all this? So yes, definitely have always been a Drake fan.\n\nMore than four years later, the two teamed up after Drake asked Julia to share a few demos and was instantly drawn to the lyrics of what would become the intro, which he felt resonated with his own life.\n\nDrake and Julia Wolf after her 2025 Toronto show", "annotation_id": "36826695", "url": "https://genius.com/36826695", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "Just like Biggie said, I just need one chance, baby Just like Drizzy said, I just need one dance, baby", "vault_lines": ["Just like Biggie said, I just need one chance, baby", "Just like Drizzy said, I just need one dance, baby"], "meaning": "Drake makes a nod to The Notorious B.I.G.’s September 1994 track “One More Chance,” but its 1995 remix is the more well-known version, as it’s one of Biggie’s most popular hits. On both songs, Biggie brags about the grandness of his love life.\n\nDrake also calls back to his April 2016 chart-topping hit, “One Dance”:\n\nThat’s why I need a one dance\nGot a Hennessy in my hand\n\nEssentially, Drake just needs a moment with this girl and he’ll convince her to be with him.", "annotation_id": "36820280", "url": "https://genius.com/36820280", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DOG HOUSE", "line": "If I ain’t give a fuck then, I don’t give a fuck now (I-I-I—, woo)", "vault_lines": ["If I ain't give a fuck then, I don't give a fuck now (I-I-I—, woo)"], "meaning": "Yeat makes a nod back to Drake’s October 2023 song, “IDGAF,” in which he was featured on. On the chorus, Yeat raps:\n\nYeah, I don’t be givin' no fucks (Not one), uh (Just pull up, pull up, woah)\nI don’t know if you caught on (Caught on)\nI don’t be givin' no fuck (Huh)", "annotation_id": "36820246", "url": "https://genius.com/36820246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
