{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Texts Go Green", "line": "Texts go green, it hits a little different, don’t it?", "vault_lines": ["Texts go green, it hits a little different, don't it?"], "meaning": "On an iPhone, sending a text to a number that’s blocked yours will result in your text bubble appearing green rather than blue.", "annotation_id": "27554932", "url": "https://genius.com/27554932", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Texts Go Green", "line": "You’re dealing with me rough I know you like it rough", "vault_lines": ["You're dealing with me rough", "I know you like it rough", "Been dealing with me rough", "You're dealing with me"], "meaning": "Drake juxtaposes two different kinds of “rough” here. “You like it rough” is an allusion to her preferences in the bedroom. Drake says she’s treating him “roughly”, unfairly, outside of the bedroom as well, in the broader context of the relationship, which might be too much for him.", "annotation_id": "26085942", "url": "https://genius.com/26085942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Texts Go Green", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 2, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-texts-go-green-lyrics", "genius_title": "Texts Go Green"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "Soon as you give ’em your soul You blow up and they say you’re sellin’ your soul, okay", "vault_lines": ["Soon as you give 'em your soul", "You blow up and they say you're sеllin' your soul, okay"], "meaning": "Drake here gives his take on the infamous proposition of celebrities selling their souls. Here he says that artists like himself give everything they have (their soul) to the craft, just for online media and fans to come out and say they sold their soul after they’ve blown up.", "annotation_id": "36019216", "url": "https://genius.com/36019216", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "I heard you was givin’ your chain away That’s kinda like givin’ your fame away What’s wrong with you?", "vault_lines": ["I heard you was givin' your chain away", "That's kinda like givin' your fame away", "What's wrong with you?"], "meaning": "While this track dropped in early 2021, well before it was public knowledge, Pharrell Williams would later auction off pieces from his iconic jewelry collection. Drake eventually got his hands on some of those chains and flexed them hard in the “Jumbotron Shit Poppin” music video. It’s possible Drake had insider info or caught wind of the sale ahead of time.\n\n\n\n Drake showing off his newly acquired $2.2M diamond pendent chain that once belonged to Pharrell Williams, in the “Jumbotron Shit Poppin” music video  \n\nDrake is basically clowning Pharrell, implying that selling off his chains is synonymous with falling off or losing relevance. Also giving away something as symbolic as a chain, especially in the hip-hop culture, equates to giving up status.", "annotation_id": "35100104", "url": "https://genius.com/35100104", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "Man, how many times have I shown improvement?", "vault_lines": ["Man, how many times have I shown improvement?", "Man, how many times have I told you the truth?"], "meaning": "Drake makes reference to his debut mixtape Room for Improvement which released in 2006. At the time of this release, Drake was nineteen-years-old and was simultaneously acting on the Canadian tv series Degrassi: The Next Generation. Drake’s catalog of albums can be seen here and his catalog of mixtapes can be seen here. Since releasing Room for Improvement, Drake has indeed shown quite the improvement in regard to sales, streams, awards and more. A number of Drake’s accolades can be seen here.", "annotation_id": "22362577", "url": "https://genius.com/22362577", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "I’m on the hot one hundo, numero uno This one ain’t come with a bundle", "vault_lines": ["I'm on the hot one hundo, numero uno", "This one ain't come with a bundle"], "meaning": "Drake broke the record last year for most Billboard Hot 100 entries ever with 208. He also became the first male artist with three number one arrivals on the Hot 100 charts with his quarantine-inspired single “Toosie Slide”. Needless to say, Drake is no stranger to being number one.\n\nHe also sends a slick shot at artists who achieve these same feats through the use of merchandise and album bundle sales, a practice that has since been banned by Billboard. Relevant artists including Travis Scott, Nicki Minaj, and Tyler, The Creator, gave out free album downloads in exchange for the purchase of a merch item. This catapulted the success of Travis Scott’s ASTROWORLD and Tyler’s IGOR, allowing their albums to reach No. 1 on Billboard, beating out Nicki Minaj and DJ Khaled respectively.\n\nDrake’s recent mixtape Dark Lane Demo Tapes was also beat out for a No. 1 spot by Kenny Chesney’s Here and Now due to merch and album bundle sales.\n\nSurely enough, this song debuted at #1 on the Billboard Hot 100.\nhttps://twitter.com/chartdata/status/1371516088148705281", "annotation_id": "22362040", "url": "https://genius.com/22362040", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "He thought he was sick, now he wipin’ his nose, okay", "vault_lines": ["He thought he was sick, now he wipin' his nose, okay"], "meaning": "Simply put, this line is just an image of someone literally sick with an illness like the flu or a cold, who wipes their nose because of the nasal discharges.\n\nBut in the context of this release, this is probably a  subliminal diss targeting Kid Cudi who has been publicly open about struggling with mental illness and, as rumored and reported over time, issues involving substance abuse, including cocaine (which might involve “wiping the nose” due to snorting cocaine).\n\nhttps://youtu.be/-IiFh-WxPy4?t=541\n\nThe whole beef between Drake and Kid Cudi erupted after Kid Cudi went on a Twitter rant accusing both Drake and Kanye West of using ghostwriters for their music:\n\nEveryone thinks they’re soooo great. Talking top 5 and be having 30 people write songs for them\n\nDrake would retaliate by releasing “Two Birds, One Stone,” a diss track released in 2016 aimed at both Pusha T and Kid Cudi, the later who at a time was in rehab for depression and suicidal thoughts, Something that was seen as a low blow by many.\n\nDespite the initial hostility, the two eventually reconciled a few years later, and would even collaborated musically – notably on Drake’s 2021 song “IMY2.”", "annotation_id": "22361508", "url": "https://genius.com/22361508", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "A boss is a role that I’ve grown into", "vault_lines": ["A boss is a role that I've grown into"], "meaning": "Along with his come-up in the rap game, Drake has increasingly become a boss of the music industry as well as other business ventures.\n\nIn 2018, Drake referenced his boss standing on Meek Mill’s “Going Bad,” where he rapped:\n\nYou asked to see the boss, so they sent me, dawg (Sent me, dawg)", "annotation_id": "22360447", "url": "https://genius.com/22360447", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "I sit in a box where the owners do", "vault_lines": ["I sit in a box where the owners do"], "meaning": "A luxury box is the most exclusive place to sit at a sports arena. This is likely a reference to his close relationship with the Toronto Raptors, the NBA team in his home city. Although he does not have an ownership stake, he has been the team’s official Global Ambassador since 2013.", "annotation_id": "22360206", "url": "https://genius.com/22360206", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "I’m in the Wynn, a million in chocolate chips And that’s just how my cookie crumble", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the Wynn, a million in chocolate chips", "And that's just how my cookie crumble"], "meaning": "Wynn is a brand of high-end hotels, based in  Paradise, Nevada. On September 12, 2019, Drake partnered with its Las Vegas location by opening an OVO pop-up boutique there. The collaboration coincided with Drake’s performance at the hotel two days later on the 14th.\n\n“A million in chocolate chips” refers to money and “that’s just how my cookie crumble” is a play on the phrase “that’s the way the cookie crumbles,” which means that one should accept the way things are. Essentially, Drake is saying he has a lot of money and that it should be accepted.", "annotation_id": "22359729", "url": "https://genius.com/22359729", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "Weezy had handed it off, I still got no fumbles", "vault_lines": ["Weezy had handed it off, I still got no fumbles"], "meaning": "In American Football, a “fumble” is when an offensive player loses possession of a ball before scoring, going out of bounds, or being tackled.\n\n“Weezy” is Lil Wayne’s nickname. He signed Drake to his label Young Money Records back in 2009, following the release of breakout commercial mixtape, So Far Gone.\n\n Since then, Drake has released countless hits and has gone on to be one of the most successful artists of all time. Drake uses the “fumble” football metaphor to note that he hasn’t flopped once in his music career since Wayne signed him.", "annotation_id": "22359513", "url": "https://genius.com/22359513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "Well, summer, all I did was rest, okay? And New Year’s, all I did was stretch, okay? And Valentine’s Day, I had sex, okay? We’ll see what’s ’bout to happen next Okay? Okay? Okay? We’ll see what’s ’bout to happen next Okay? Okay? Okay? We’ll see what’s ’bout to happen, ayy, ayy We’ll see what’s ’bout to happen, ayy We’ll see what’s ’bout to happen", "vault_lines": ["Well, summer, all I did was rest, okay?", "And New Year's, all I did was stretch, okay?", "And Valentine's Day, I had sex, okay?", "We'll see what's 'bout to happen next", "We'll see what's 'bout to happen, ayy, ayy", "We'll see what's 'bout to happen, ayy", "We'll see what's 'bout to happen"], "meaning": "Drake answers the people who are too invested in his life in a sarcastic fashion, similar to how he did with interview questions on his November 2011 song, “HYFR”:\n\nAnd we say hell yeah, hell yeah, hell yeah\nFuckin' right, fuckin' right, alright\n\nThe chorus notably takes lyrics from the end of Drake’s verse on the leaked and unfinished track, “What A Time To Be A Slime.” This may indicate that this song is the finished version of the said track.", "annotation_id": "22359464", "url": "https://genius.com/22359464", "state": "accepted", "votes": 141, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "Yeah, I got one, Virgil got one and that there is the only two", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I got one, Virgil got one and that there is the only two", "I got one, Laurie got one and that there is the only two"], "meaning": "Virgil Abloh is the founder of the fashion brand, Off-White and is one of Drake’s closest friends.\n\nIn April 2019, Off-White collaborated with the luxury watch brand, Patek Philippe, for a one-of-a-kind version of the Nautilus design.\n\nOnly Drake and Virgil own a pair of the exclusive watch. They debuted it on their Instagram pages back in January 2020.", "annotation_id": "22359409", "url": "https://genius.com/22359409", "state": "accepted", "votes": 107, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "I put a skirt on a whip and a crown on the six But there’s no need to dress up the numbers", "vault_lines": ["I put a skirt on a whip and a crown on the six", "But there's no need to dress up the numbers"], "meaning": "Side skirts on cars are used to reduce the amount of high pressure area on the side of the car to go under the car from the sides.\n\n\n\nDrake has also metaphorically crowned “The Six” by continuing to be “numero uno” on the Billboard charts in America while repping for his hometown in Toronto, Canada.\n\nHe’s using wordplay by mentioning clothing accessories such as a “skirt” and a “crown”, and saying that there’s no need to “dress up the numbers” like artists who use merch and album bundle sales.", "annotation_id": "22320988", "url": "https://genius.com/22320988", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "line": "I’m posted in Stockholm It’s me, the owls and the twins, it’s only the real", "vault_lines": ["I'm posted in Stockholm", "It's me, the owls and the twins, it's only the real"], "meaning": "“The twins” are Elizabeth and Victoria Lejonhjärta, Swedish twins who have frequently collaborated with Drake such as modeling for his OVO clothing line and starring in the “Nice For What” music video. Stockholm is the capital of Sweden.\n\n\n\nOwls might refer to OVO Mark and OVO Noel, also known as Night Owl Sound. Mark is one of Drake’s DJ’s and Noel is a frequent producer/engineer.", "annotation_id": "22312069", "url": "https://genius.com/22312069", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s Next", "status": "done", "targets": 13, "clicked": 13, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-whats-next-lyrics", "genius_title": "What’s Next"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "Hand me the tools like I’m pickin’ a lock", "vault_lines": ["Hand me the tools like I'm pickin' a lock"], "meaning": "Hand me the tools = Beats for music. Drake is saying that he is gonna keep working even though the whole industry seems to have turned on him. Drake is speaking of making the best even when no one seems to be on his side. Picking the lock is a play on he is gonna break through the door of the industry since they closed it on him. He is implying that he is gonna keep doing him and make his style of music and let it speak for him.", "annotation_id": "40935323", "url": "https://genius.com/40935323", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "You whisper my name ’cause the weight that it carry, you can’t even speak", "vault_lines": ["You whisper my name 'cause the weight that it carry, you can't even speak", "Yeah, you can't even speak", "You gotta whisper my name", "Okay, you gotta whisper my name"], "meaning": "Drake is allegedly directing this bar at Hova. There has been a lot of beef between drake and hov since drake decided to go with apple and not tidal. Drake is saying Hov whispers his name because saying it out loud could alarm Drake so he whispers it so drake wont hear. Hov is said to be behind the push of Kendrick Lamar being that Drake signed with apple and not tidal. Drake could be implying Hova is salty behind this.", "annotation_id": "40935296", "url": "https://genius.com/40935296", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "My brother got no fuck to give, he poppin’ at everyone", "vault_lines": ["My brother got no fuck to give, he poppin' at everyone"], "meaning": "Self Explanatory. Drake is saying if you attempt to harm him that his brother is willing to defend him and let loose on your whole Crew. Drake is saying he isnt a pus and is willing to defend himself when necessary.", "annotation_id": "40706569", "url": "https://genius.com/40706569", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "Nowadays, y’all don’t wan’ put in no work Y’all just wan’ jump to the front of the line and hurry and run out the nine", "vault_lines": ["Nowadays, y'all don't wan' put in no work", "Y'all just wan' jump to the front of the line and hurry and run out the ninе"], "meaning": "This appears to be a shot at fellow Toronto artist The Weeknd. Drake seems to be questioning how much credit and hometown love someone should get when they aren’t really around or involved with the city like that anymore.\n\nThis is reminiscent of Drake’s stance on “Family Matters,” where he implied that  The Weeknd has become more of a representation of Toronto than an actual presence in it:\n\nYou don’t even be at home, dog, you a souvenir-out-the-gift-shop nigga\n\nThere’s also a football double entendre at play. A 9-yard run is a solid gain, but it’s still one yard short of a first down. In that sense, Drake is saying that no matter how successful the person is, they’re still falling short in an area that actually matters to him.", "annotation_id": "40141082", "url": "https://genius.com/40141082", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "You know how I fly ’cause I land at the beach, when you land, you can see me", "vault_lines": ["You know how I fly 'cause I land at the beach, when you land, you can see me"], "meaning": "Referencing the previous line, he reminds the audience that he certainly does not fly economy. His customized Boeing 767-200ER is hard to miss, and once famously flew over dozens of onlookers in a low-altitude beachside landing.", "annotation_id": "39911476", "url": "https://genius.com/39911476", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "Yeah, my brother said he want a blue Bim’, I’ma get him one", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, my brother said he want a blue Bim', I'ma get him one"], "meaning": "“Bimmer” (pronounced BIM-er) is a slang for a BMW automobile. Drake’s generosity toward his inner circle is almost nonsensical. Most recently, he gifted streamer Adin Ross a fully loaded $330,000 Maybach. He then surprised fellow streamer BenDaDonnn with an $800,000 Rolls-Royce Spectre Mansory during a Christmas livestream. A blue BMW for the homie barely registers on that scale.", "annotation_id": "39830017", "url": "https://genius.com/39830017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "This ain’t fixable smoke when you see us, stop sittin’ with Farrakhan, nigga This shit like the bag that you bring on the plane, it’s gon’ carry on", "vault_lines": ["This ain't fixable smoke when you see us, stop sittin' with Farrakhan, nigga", "This shit like the bag that you bring on the plane, it's gon' carry on"], "meaning": "Louis Farrakhan is a prominent American religious leader, activist, and the longtime head of the Nation of Islam (NOI), a Black nationalist and religious organization. Farrakhan has a long history of stepping in to broker peace treaties and squash feuds between hip-hop artists but Drake notes his beef will never be squashed with his enemies.\n\nDrake follows this sentiment into the next line—a suitcase that you bring with you into the cabin of an airplane is called a “carry-on” bag but Drake uses this to say the beef is going to literally “carry on” forever.", "annotation_id": "39828225", "url": "https://genius.com/39828225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "You niggas be hittin’ the ’net for some love, you a tennis ball", "vault_lines": ["You niggas be hittin' the 'net for some love, you a tennis ball"], "meaning": "In the sport of tennis, a score of zero is called “love.” When you hit a tennis ball into the net, the play stops and you don’t score any points.\n\nDrake uses some wordplay—he calls his opps  “tennis balls” because they “hit the net” or go on the internet to look for validation and attention, but just like a bad shot in tennis, they end up with zero “love”—nobody cares about them.", "annotation_id": "39827210", "url": "https://genius.com/39827210", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "Me and white bitches been kissin’ a lot", "vault_lines": ["Me and white bitches been kissin' a lot"], "meaning": "Drake also rapped about being with white women on his November 2011 classic “Over My Dead Body,” where he made a reference to R&B singer Seal and his wife at the time, German supermodel Heidi Klum:\n\nI mean you ain’t the only real nigga\nThey got me on these white women like Seal, nigga\nSlave to the pussy, but I’m just playin' the field, nigga, yeah", "annotation_id": "39823781", "url": "https://genius.com/39823781", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "Am I your GOAT? Well, probably not I probably was, but you probably forgot", "vault_lines": ["Am I your GOAT? Well, probably not", "I probably was, but you probably forgot"], "meaning": "“GOAT” is an acronym for the “Greatest Of All Time.” Drake is calling out those who turned on him after the 2024 rap civil war against Kendrick Lamar and the intense industry backlash that followed, which shifted the public heavily against him.\n\nDrake also brought up the “GOAT” discussion on his October 2023 song, “First Person Shooter”:\n\nI love a dinner with some fine women when they start debatin' about who the GOAT\nI’m like go'n ‘head, say it then, who the GOAT?\nWho the GOAT? Who the GOAT? Who the GOAT?\nWho you bitches really rootin’ for?", "annotation_id": "39823307", "url": "https://genius.com/39823307", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "My honor comes from the shit that I do for the ones that I love, it’s between ’em and I Your honor comes to the court and he offers a plea deal so that you could cut off some time", "vault_lines": ["My honor comes from the shit that I do for the ones that I love, it's between 'em and I", "Your honor comes to the court and he offers a plea deal so that you could cut off some time"], "meaning": "Drake’s “honor” is personal and private, it comes from the things he does behind closed doors that never make it to the public’s eyes.\n\nHis opponent’s “honor” on the other hand is Your Honor — the formal courtroom address for a judge. The only time these people show honor is when they’re facing jail-time or negotiating a plea deal to shave time off a sentence, Basically insinuating that they are spineless and have no honor whatsoever.", "annotation_id": "39815566", "url": "https://genius.com/39815566", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "If you my nigga, then say that, my nigga Like, “What’s up, my crodie? Come hug me right now” Come up to me and get one of them out ‘Cause you know all these niggas been countin’ me out", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you gotta come here and you know I pop out", "If you my nigga, then say that, my nigga", "Like, \"What's up, my crodie? Come hug me right now\"", "Come up to me and get one of them out", "'Cause you know all these niggas been countin' me out"], "meaning": "“Crodie” is Crip slang for brother as it alters the word “Brodie,” replacing the B for a C. It’s a widely used term in Drake’s hometown of Toronto. Drake first used it on his December 2019 UK-drill-inspired track, “War,” where he gave a shoutout to Toronto artist, Pressa:\n\nWassa, wassa, just like the crodie Pressa, I don’t do no passa\n\nNotably, Kendrick Lamar mocked Drake’s Toronto background using the term on his April 2024 diss track “euphoria,” where he rapped these lines in a Toronto accent:\n\nDon’t speak on the family, crodie\nIt can get deep in the family, crodie\nTalk about me and my family, crodie?\nSomeone gon' bleed in your family, crodie", "annotation_id": "39809389", "url": "https://genius.com/39809389", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "I’m doing my big one, you doing a little one What kinda man are you? A middle one", "vault_lines": ["I'm doing my big one, you doing a little one", "What kinda man are you? A middle one"], "meaning": "A “middle man” is someone who negotiates between two parties instead of standing firmly on one side.\n\nThis is a possible subliminal jab at J. Cole and his decision to publicly back out of the 2024 feud between Drake and Kendrick Lamar—instead expressing love and respect for both artists, rather than choosing a side. During a March 2026 interview with Apple Music, Cole said he admired both rappers:\n\nThose are dudes that I admire, I got genuine love for these dudes, I hate to see the world shit on either one, in defence of the other.\n\nAlso, Cole has a single titled “MIDDLE CHILD” that he released in January 2019.", "annotation_id": "39796101", "url": "https://genius.com/39796101", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "My white label had to go white label shit when he stepped out his body and finally got hot", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Universal Music Group (a label led by white executives) had to “go white label” on Kendrick Lamar, rebranding him as a hotter, marketable star during the 2024 beef. In this context, “white label” describes how UMG re‑launched Kendrick’s image and sound so heavily that his newfound heat feels manufactured rather than organic.\n\nDrake filed a defamation lawsuit against UMG, alleging the label failed to prevent the release of “Not Like Us” despite its allegedly false and damaging accusations, and that UMG actively allowed and promoted the song to his detriment. He followed up with a second petition alleging a “pay-to-play” scheme between UMG and iHeartRadio, claiming radio airplay itself was being rigged in Kendrick’s favor. Most tellingly, Drake accused UMG and Spotify of violating the RICO Act by using illegal tactics to boost streams for “Not Like Us.”\n\nUMG also happened to lift copyright restrictions on Kendrick diss tracks  so that streamers and reactor channels could freely engage with them without getting flagged. Another maneuver that could be interpreted as a trying to sway the public.", "annotation_id": "39785853", "url": "https://genius.com/39785853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "If I’m lyin’, I’m flyin’ economy, give me a motherfuckin’ seat If I’m lyin’, I’m flyin’ economy, I bet I know who I see", "vault_lines": ["If I'm lyin', I'm flyin' economy, give me a motherfuckin' seat", "If I'm lyin', I'm flyin' economy, I bet I know who I see"], "meaning": "In reality, Drake is almost certainly not flying economy, dude owns a customized Boeing 767-300F private jet, known as “Air Drake,” valued at roughly $185 million. So the line definitely reads as a tongue‑in‑cheek lie. It’s also a play on the slang phrase “If I’m lying, I’m flying,” used to guarantee that a statement is 100% true.\n\n\n\nDrake’s private jet named “Air Drake”\n\nBack in June 2024, Kanye West and his wife, Bianca Censori, were spotted flying economy on a commercial flight from Italy to Japan, which sparked widespread social‑media attention. Given their past tensions, this is likely who Drake is betting he’ll see, subtly shading him.", "annotation_id": "39785044", "url": "https://genius.com/39785044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "Ashes to ashes, dust to dust The dustiest niggas been fuckin’ with us", "vault_lines": ["Ashes to ashes, dust to dust", "The dustiest niggas been fuckin' with us"], "meaning": "“Ashes to ashes, dust to dust” is a common phrase spoken at funerals that reminds mourners that the body returns to the earth it came from.\n\nDrake is invoking it in the context of ICEMAN, an album framed entirely around resurrection. The burial imagery has followed him since the beef, with people like Joe Budden declaring him a corpse and the culture writing him off as irrelevant.\n\n“Dusty” in slang means broke, or someone with no shine. Drake is saying the people who came at him hardest were the least qualified to do so.\n\n“Dust” is the title of the previous track,  serving as a reprise.", "annotation_id": "39783817", "url": "https://genius.com/39783817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "We turnt up Coachella, had boys backin’ down And we might do the same if we touch Rolling Loud", "vault_lines": ["We turnt up Coachella, had boys backin' down", "And we might do the same if we touch Rolling Loud", "We turned up Coachella, had boys backin' down"], "meaning": "Drake notably headlined the Coachella Valley Music & Arts Festival in April 2015, featuring a surprise appearance and viral kiss from Madonna, as well as a subsequent cameo from Nicki Minaj during his set. He later returned to headline again during Weekend 2.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KMnicRhvkvA\n\nEven with Drake’s massive success, he has shockingly never performed at Rolling Loud, despite the festival’s co-founder, Tariq Cherif, revealing they offer him their “max offer” of millions of dollars every year to headline.", "annotation_id": "39781277", "url": "https://genius.com/39781277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "I’ll take 500K, not the dinner, I never could learn shit from none of y’all", "vault_lines": ["I'll take 500K, not the dinner, I never could learn shit from none of y'all"], "meaning": "Reference to the popular debate “Dinner with JAY-Z or $500,000,” where a dinner with him is seen as potentially offering life-changing advice worth more than the money. For context, the average U.S. citizen would need about 8–10 years of earnings to reach $500K, assuming an annual income of roughly $60–$62K.\n\nIn a 2023 interview with CBS Mornings, JAY-Z himself said it’s better to take the money, arguing that everything he could teach is already in his music:\n\nYou gotta take the money. What I'ma say, man, everything that I'ma teach you […] you got all that in the music for $10.99. [Taking the dinner] is a bad deal. I wouldn’t tell you to take a bad deal. Take the $500k, go buy some albums, and listen to the albums, and it’s all there. If you piece it together and really listen to the music for the words, for what it is, it’s all in there.\n\nDrake has long cited JAY-Z as a major influence in his career, and despite periods of tension in their relationship, he has drawn much of his inspiration from JAY-Z’s work.", "annotation_id": "39778514", "url": "https://genius.com/39778514", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "My new ting don’t know how I live, she shoppin’ at Erewhon", "vault_lines": ["My new ting don't know how I live, she shoppin' at Erewhon"], "meaning": "Erewhon is a Los Angeles based luxury grocery store chain that’s known for their expensive wellness products. What gives the store’s products their high price tag is the store’s strict organic standards and small-batch specialty items.", "annotation_id": "39777004", "url": "https://genius.com/39777004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "Whisper my name and don’t say it too loud ’Cause you gotta come here and you know I pop out", "vault_lines": ["Whisper my name and don't say it too loud", "'Cause you gotta come here and you know I pop out", "You gotta whisper my name", "Okay, you gotta whisper my name"], "meaning": "During the peak of their 2024 feud, Kendrick Lamar hosted a massive Juneteenth concert in Los Angeles called Pop Out: Ken & Friends concert.\n\nThe event was widely seen as a historic victory lap against Drake, featuring artists from all over the West Coast coming together to perform diss tracks live on stage. The name came from a line Kendrick said on his May 2024 diss track “Not Like Us”:\n\nSometimes you gotta pop out and show niggas\n\nDrake’s using Kendrick’s big moment and flipping it into a threat, warning he shouldn’t talk recklessly on his name.", "annotation_id": "39776316", "url": "https://genius.com/39776316", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "Baby boy, please I heard what you said to lil’ bro about me Yeah, and when you run into the Iceman, what you gon’ do except freeze? You not ’bout to squeeze, you not in the streets I’m cuttin’ niggas off at the knees, there ain’t no standin’ on business with me", "vault_lines": ["I heard what you said to lil' bro about mе", "Yeah, and when you run into the Iceman, what you gon' do except freeze?", "You not 'bout to squeeze, you not in the streets", "I'm cuttin' niggas off at the knees, there ain't no standin' on business with me"], "meaning": "These lines are likely directed at Playboi Carti, who goes by the nickname “Baby Boi.” While Carti didn’t speak on the 2024 beef, he did quietly aligned himself with Kendrick Lamar’s camp, notably refusing to clear his guest feature on Drake’s August 2024 track “No Face,” which caused Drake to release it solo. He also collaborated with Kendrick twice on his March 2025 album, MUSIC.\n\n“Lil bro” is The Weeknd—in a 2016 Beats 1 interview with Zane Lowe, he called Drake his big brother and how supportive he was of him:\n\nHe came in as my big bro and showed the world what I could do […] From then on, he’s always been supporting me and I’ve always been supporting him.\n\nThe Weeknd and Playboi Carti are close—in 2025, Playboi Carti spent months on the road with The Weeknd, serving as the opening act for the After Hours Til Dawn stadium tour and have collaborated together on numerous occasions. Drake hints that Carti talks about him behind his back to The Weeknd.", "annotation_id": "39776245", "url": "https://genius.com/39776245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "These boys dress up and think that they somebody, shit is like Comic-Con", "vault_lines": ["These boys dress up and think that they somebody, shit is like Comic-Con"], "meaning": "Comic-Con is a massive annual convention where fans gather and participate in cosplay. They dress up in elaborate costumes to pretend they are superheroes, villains, or fictional characters. Drake’s opps act in similar fashion—they put on fake personas.\n\n\n\nA Comic-Con event", "annotation_id": "39776095", "url": "https://genius.com/39776095", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whisper My Name", "line": "My YGs are fastin’ and prayin’, you lucky it’s Ramadan Otherwise, they would be posted outside like the Pentagon", "vault_lines": ["My YGs are fastin' and prayin', you lucky it's Ramadan", "Otherwise, they would be posted outside like the Pentagon"], "meaning": "“YGs” is slang for Young Gangsters. Drake is saying his people are on a leash right now, and the only reason is God. His young crew members are deep in Ramadan, the holiest month in Islam, locked in on fasting and prayer. or otherwise they would be posted outside akin to the Pentagon Force Protection Agency (PFPA.)\n\nDrake is clearly surrounded by a significant number of Muslim members in his circle.", "annotation_id": "39775767", "url": "https://genius.com/39775767", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
