{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Tried to kill me but I am very much alive on the six side Yeah, I said I am very much alive on the six side", "vault_lines": ["Tried to kill me but they still want the views", "Tried to kill me but I am very much alive on the six side", "Yeah, I said I am very much alive on the six side"], "meaning": "Drake says that people try to ‘kill’ him, but he cannot ‘die’ as the spirit of the six side (Toronto) keeps him ‘alive’.", "annotation_id": "35671216", "url": "https://genius.com/35671216", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "And then when I saw the news Make me feel afraid of how I’d been to you Trust me when I say I thought it all through Trust me when I say that shit is old moves", "vault_lines": ["And then when I saw the news", "Make me feel afraid of how I’d been to you", "Trust me when I say I thought it all through", "Trust me when I say that shit is old moves"], "meaning": "Revealed via a twitter thread on April 7th, 2025, it is now brought to people’s attention that this outro is an unfinished unreleased track featuring Rihanna titled, “INVINCIBLE”:\n\nhttps://x.com/dianasalam13/status/1909407255289905189?s=46&t=E8Io5aav0O7Z37C-1XdPkw\n\nBy following the twitter link embedded above, you can see that the song was discovered in a discord server where Rihanna is heard singing a part of these lyrics. Knowing the terms that Drake and Rihanna are on now, that track will likely never see the light of day.", "annotation_id": "35150069", "url": "https://genius.com/35150069", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Everybody wanna know how shit gets like this, man Man, the whole city Shit’s crazy right now, and shit ain’t gonna stop", "vault_lines": ["Everybody wanna know how shit gets like this, man", "Man, the whole city", "Shit's crazy right now, and shit ain't gonna stop"], "meaning": "This is quoted from Toronto local legend Jelleestone. These lines appear on Rexdale rapper’s intro track “2 Sides” on his 2005 album “The Hood Is Here”. It is fair to assume that Drake grew up listening to Jellee, shouting him out on “Weston Road Flows” and bringing him out to preform at Toronto History 2022.", "annotation_id": "29923982", "url": "https://genius.com/29923982", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "God made himself a way from me to you", "vault_lines": ["God made himself a way from me to you"], "meaning": "Drake is talking about a girl he’s been seeing and feels that fate brought them together.\n\nGod “making a way” to this girl, is in the same vein as Moses parting the Red Sea. Although destiny called upon Moses to lead the people to safety, according to the Exodus account, it was actually God who parted the Red Sea.", "annotation_id": "9167841", "url": "https://genius.com/9167841", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "[Outro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The outro seems to reinforce the whole winter scheme connected to this section of the album as we can hear the harsh winds & what seems to be an unreleased Drake song playing through the speakers of a car.", "annotation_id": "9095830", "url": "https://genius.com/9095830", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Know shit gonna get a lot more worse before it get any better", "vault_lines": ["Know shit gonna get a lot more worse before it get any better"], "meaning": "There is no doubt that Drizzy has been through some very difficult situations in his years. He had to be the man of the house for his childhood and teenage years,  and had everyday struggles.\n\n\n\nBeing the 6 God is an extremely difficult job, and Drake has a big image that he needs to keep up in order for his fanbase to know that he’s still the best. Even though some days may be harder than others, Drizzy knows that in the end it will all be worth it.", "annotation_id": "9092084", "url": "https://genius.com/9092084", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "All these handouts, man it’s getting outta hand I’ma start telling niggas “Get it how you can”", "vault_lines": ["All these handouts, man it's getting outta hand", "I'ma start telling niggas \"Get it how you can\""], "meaning": "Drake uses wordplay to express the fact that he feels like he’s giving out too many hand outs to the people around him—whether it’s financial, or a career-boosting feature.\n\nThis could be a reference to Toronto rapper Mo-G. He dissed Drake in March 2016 for failing to pay him for studio work. XXL claim that the reason Mo-G was able to release his debut EP Ave Boy was because OVO helped fund it:\n\nOliver passed the music over to Drizzy and moves were made from there. Mo-G won over the “Hotline Bling” rapper and OVO helped fund Mo-G’s debut EP, Ave Boy.", "annotation_id": "9080809", "url": "https://genius.com/9080809", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "They give me loyalty and I don’t gotta pay for it Same way, breads gotta break for it Keeping people fed is my only peace of mind now", "vault_lines": ["They give me loyalty and I don't gotta pay for it", "Same way, breads gotta break for it", "Keeping people fed is my only peace of mind now"], "meaning": "Drake has day-one friends around him. His crew consists of people like El Khatib, part of the family in 2008. His cousin Ryan Silverstein is regularly seen partying with Drake, and “Chubbs” provides muscle for the superstar. All of them have been with the rapper since the beginning of his career.\n\nTo “break bread” is to share a meal with friends, often as a gesture of loyalty or exercise of trust. Breaking off some bread can also mean giving someone a cut (money, assets). Loyalty comes from true friendship or money, and in both senses, Drake’s keeping his entourage fed.", "annotation_id": "9075741", "url": "https://genius.com/9075741", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "If they don’t have a story these days, they’ll make one", "vault_lines": ["If they don't have a story these days, they'll make one"], "meaning": "Being in the public eye can be especially difficult for any celebrity – Drake included. Drake could be referring to the media whom often write stories that aren’t true, including ones that claim that he’s in relationships he’s not, and how they’ll do anything to write an article about him.\n\nDuring Drake’s sit down interview with Zane Lowe, he mentioned the “72 hour rule” that his mother taught him; he controls his emotions for the first 72 hours after something potentially damaging happens, until:\n\nSomeone else will have done something stupid enough that everyone will forget.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BEpl8QKjQLU/?taken-by=champagnepapi", "annotation_id": "9074815", "url": "https://genius.com/9074815", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Now they wanna act like I do no good", "vault_lines": ["Now they wanna act like I do no good"], "meaning": "Drake’s had a history of closely mimicking people’s styles, even being labelled a “culture vulture” for the way he uses styles for his benefit.\n\nDrake has done features with or borrowed slang from rappers and singers, like TT, who have stated the co-sign really only helped Drake.\n\nToronto rapper Mo-G was the latest to make similar criticisms of Drake. Mo G claimed Drake’s manager Oliver El-Khatib brought him to the studio and asked him for new hooks, flows, & bars to use on Views from the 6 but only offered to compensate him $500.\n\nDrake essentially put the Toronto music scene on the map, and with recent allegations, people tend to forget that. Drake wants us to remember the good he’s done for the city.", "annotation_id": "9074637", "url": "https://genius.com/9074637", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "(Dying, woo, yeah, yeah, gangstas, gangstas, gangstas) (Dying, woo, yeah, yeah, gangstas, gangstas, gangstas) (Dying, woo, yeah, yeah, gangstas, gangstas, gangstas) (Dying, woo, yeah, yeah, gangstas, gangstas, gangstas)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The chorus is sampled from Jamaican reggae artist Mavado’s song “Dying”—but with the sound of dying altered to sound more like “Nine,” in keeping with the song’s theme of changing “the six” to a ”nine.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?time_continue=6&v=vSo0M1sL6UE\n\nMavado appeared previously in the music video for Drake’s ”Find Your Love”.\n\nThis sample also appeared on Drake’s 2007 mixtape Comeback Season on the outro of “Missin You”.\nhttps://youtu.be/5hG369PbH3o?t=4m7s", "annotation_id": "9073481", "url": "https://genius.com/9073481", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "And I can’t sleep these days unless I take one", "vault_lines": ["And I can't sleep these days unless I take one"], "meaning": "At first glance, it seems like Drake’s referring to using sleeping pills or anti-anxiety medication to fall asleep.\n\nHowever, in his Zane Lowe interview about VIEWS, Drake says he doesn’t use drugs (other than occasional weed smoking and drinking):\n\nMy mother taught me a lot about being able to deal with stuff the way that I do and the poise that I can possess…My mother is the reason why I don’t really like ever do drugs…That’s never really been my thing.\n\nDrake’s talking about taking shots and not pills, continuing the theme about contentious position within the rap game from the previous line. Drake discussed his beef with Meek Mill in the FADER. He recorded his first diss track (“Charged Up”) the night their feud began. He couldn’t sleep until he took one.", "annotation_id": "9073014", "url": "https://genius.com/9073014", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "I made a decision last night that I would die for it", "vault_lines": ["I made a decision last night that I would die for it", "I made a decision last night, I'd die for it", "And I made a decision last night that I would die for it"], "meaning": "This line, though referring to a single decision that Drake will stand by with his life, signifies his confidence in the choices he’s made throughout his life. From moving on from acting to moving on from a woman, Drake is not one to second guess himself often.", "annotation_id": "9070810", "url": "https://genius.com/9070810", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Yeah, for the dogs dem, you know", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, for the dogs dem, you know"], "meaning": "“9” follows the intro track, ”Keep the Family Close,” a softer, melodic track.\n\nNow he’s letting everyone know that this song will change gears and wake his dogs up (his more loyal rap audience).\n\n”Dem” (”them”) is a way to pluralize words in Jamaican patois (technically, he could just say “dog dem” rather than “dogs dem”)—and Drake has adopted a Jamaican sound for this album.", "annotation_id": "9070557", "url": "https://genius.com/9070557", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Life is always on, man, I never get a break from it Doesn’t matter where I go, I can never get away from it", "vault_lines": ["Life is always on, man, I never get a break from it", "Doesn't matter where I go, I can never get away from it"], "meaning": "Drake’s life as a superstar is inescapable—no matter where he goes.\n\nHe told FADER in his cover story:\n\nI know everything. I know everything that’s being said about you. I know everything that’s being said about me. I’m very in tune with this life. Much like, I assume, most of my listeners are.\n\nThat’s part of his recipe for superstardom—always being tuned in to the conversation around him, and always adjusting. It’s effective, but it can also be burdening.\n\nDrake starred in a “Noisey Raps” episode where he spoke about sacrificing a normal life and understands that there’s people whose job is to try to expose him.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/_nFKC7IwJYc?t=3m33s", "annotation_id": "9070485", "url": "https://genius.com/9070485", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Funny ’cause I really did more than I should I made a decision last night that I would die for it Just to show the city what it takes to be alive for it", "vault_lines": ["I made a decision last night that I would die for it", "Just to show the city what it takes to be alive for it", "I made a decision last night, I'd die for it", "And I made a decision last night that I would die for it", "Funny 'cause I really did more than I should"], "meaning": "Drake might be starting to feel like Jay Z:\n\nDark Knight feeling, die and be a hero\nOr live long enough to see yourself become a villain\n\nWhile he’s done so much for the city of Toronto (they gave him the key to the city in February 2016), he’s still struggling to gain the support of the up and coming rappers from the city.\n\nDrake isn’t getting enough credit as he really should from Toronto. He’s willing to die for the city just to make people realize that the things he’s doing for it is not an easy job.", "annotation_id": "9070296", "url": "https://genius.com/9070296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Keychain go jang-a-lang, I wanna do major things", "vault_lines": ["Keychain go jang-a-lang, I wanna do major things"], "meaning": "Drizzy continues to flex about his car collection—“jangalang“ is the sound of jingling keys, and the more keys you have, the more noise they make. He’s got so many luxury whips that his keychain probably looks a little like this:", "annotation_id": "9070266", "url": "https://genius.com/9070266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Can’t get me on the line so they hang me out to dry for it", "vault_lines": ["Can't get me on the line so they hang me out to dry for it"], "meaning": "People can’t get in touch with Drake (“get him on the line”) because he’s gotten so big—so they get bitter and abandon him (“hang him out to dry”.)\n\n\n\nIt’s a double entendre—if you literally are hanging clothes out to dry, you put them on a clothesline (“get them on the line”).", "annotation_id": "9070191", "url": "https://genius.com/9070191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Look, mama hit my phone and said rap’s no good Better than her telling me the check’s no good", "vault_lines": ["Look, mama hit my phone and said rap's no good", "Better than her telling me the check's no good"], "meaning": "Drizzy‘s mom didn’t approve of rap as a career choice, but he kept at it, because it could help her pay the bills. He’d rather do something she didn’t like than let the family go broke.\n\n\n\nDrake has often rapped about his mother, Sandi Graham. He told E!:\n\nShe’s the most important person in my life.\n\nDrake also spoke about the disputes he had with his mother in “Started From Bottom,” where his mother is in the video outside of his childhood home at 0:36.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RubBzkZzpUA#t=0m36s", "annotation_id": "9070126", "url": "https://genius.com/9070126", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "Like Mannie, like Stunna, man, shit is big time now", "vault_lines": ["Like Mannie, like Stunna, man, shit is big time now"], "meaning": "Drake compares himself to Mannie Fresh and Stunna (also known as Birdman or Baby), the two southern rap pioneers who formed the duo Big Tymers in the 1990s.\n\n\n\nTheir name became synonymous with a flashy, “big time” image—with lots of bling and cars.\n\nHe’s also paying homage to his record label, Cash Money. Birdman is a co-founder, and  Mannie was the label’s most prolific producer in the early days.", "annotation_id": "9070092", "url": "https://genius.com/9070092", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "I only drove it five times, paid 1.5 for it, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I only drove it five times, paid 1.5 for it, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake brags that he bought a $1.5 million car and only drove it five times—a true sign of extreme, throwaway wealth.\n\nAuto Evolution ran an article about Drake selling his Bugatti Veyron Sang Noir—the only one of that model sold in Canada—after only a few months, with only 3,000 miles on it. The list price on the car was $3.4 million, but nobody knows exactly what he paid for it.\n\nAnother bit of numerology: 6 times 1.5 is 9. Coincidence? Probably, but maybe not.\n\nDrake also posted this Instagram photo, with the caption “Drove it 5 times”:\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BJPg5jEjaSK/", "annotation_id": "9070076", "url": "https://genius.com/9070076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "You know 40 wants peace but I’m down to cut ties for it", "vault_lines": ["You know 40 wants peace but I'm down to cut ties for it"], "meaning": "This line has a double meaning pivoting on “peace.” Firstly, it’s a reference to Drake’s beefs with other rappers from Toronto like Tory Lanez and Mo-G. Noah “40” Shebib, his producer (who in many ways is responsible for Drake’s idiosyncratic sound) wants peace but Drake is willing to end friendships to be the best.\n\nHe’s done it before over the Meek Mill beef—he’s no longer speaking to Nicki Minaj and Rick Ross.\n\n40 needs peace and quiet, since he’s been diagnosed with multiple sclerosis. Drake has previously rapped about how he has to make sure “40” doesn’t overwork himself on his 2014 single “0 to 100/The Catch Up.”\n\nNevertheless, Drake’s willing to make some noise in his quest to be the best in the game.", "annotation_id": "9070070", "url": "https://genius.com/9070070", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "MJ in every way, I just don’t fade away", "vault_lines": ["MJ in every way, I just don't fade away"], "meaning": "Drake feels like MJ—it could be Michael Jackson, the King of Pop (and Drake has been racking up the #1 hits), or Michael Jordan, the GOAT (and the Jumpman). Drake replaced Kanye West as the spokesman for Nike’s Air Jordan brand in 2013, so he is like MJ, in that they’re both making money off Jordan’s name.\n\nBoth MJs stayed on top of their game for a long time—and didn’t fade away. Drake’s also making reference to Michael Jordan’s signature fadeaway jumper.", "annotation_id": "9070066", "url": "https://genius.com/9070066", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "line": "And I turn the six upside down, it’s a nine now", "vault_lines": ["And I turn the six upside down, it's a nine now", "Six upside down, it's a nine now"], "meaning": "The six is a slang popularized by Drake in reference to his hometown of Toronto. The number is used because of Toronto’s main area codes: 416 and 647. As an ode to the city, Drake also set the songtime to be 4:16 long.\n\nWith his superstardom, selling upwards of 500,000 records in the first 3 days of If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, Drake has figuratively flipped the city of Toronto upside-down, making the city synonymous with his name. He shows this through metaphor by flipping the 6 to a 9.", "annotation_id": "9070061", "url": "https://genius.com/9070061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 320, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "9", "status": "done", "targets": 27, "clicked": 27, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-9-lyrics", "genius_title": "9"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Empire", "line": "The deal got signed and my split’s all good", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s verse recycles lyrics used back in 2009, around the same time he signed to Young Money Records.\n\nI am very happy in my situation now, which is signed to Cortez [Bryant] and Gee Roberson at Young Money and management through ‘Hip Hop Since 1978.’ The most important thing for me is being around my team – they are stronger than any label.", "annotation_id": "8576174", "url": "https://genius.com/8576174", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Empire", "line": "And don’t listen to anybody that knew me Cause to have known me would mean that there’s a new me And if you think I changed in the slightest could’ve fooled me Yeah, and to my city I’m the two-three Drug dealers live vicariously through me", "vault_lines": ["And if you think I changed in the slightest could've fooled me", "Yeah, and to my city I'm the two-three"], "meaning": "These lyrics also appear on Drake’s “Say What’s Real,” off his mixtape So Far Gone, which was released back in 2009. Although the lyrics are identical, there’s a distinct difference in the way the bars are delivered. In this track, Drake adopts a more laid back cadence opposed to the aggressive and emotive flow that was featured on the previous cut.", "annotation_id": "8575402", "url": "https://genius.com/8575402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Empire", "line": "And these other lames is hardly mentioned I bet that with this pen I could draw attention But you can tell by my strut I’m just chillin’ in the cut, nigga what, and I’m feelin’ like", "vault_lines": ["I'ma tell you like this, nigga", "And these other lames is hardly mentioned", "I bet that with this pen I could draw attention", "But you can tell by my strut", "I'm just chillin' in the cut, nigga what, and I'm feelin' like"], "meaning": "Drake apparently can’t draw in the traditional sense, but he sure knows how to draw a crowd. This was written in 2011, around the time he was prepping a November release for his sophomore, Take Care, which to date is his best-selling album with 2,260,000+ copies sold. Since then, he has only made a bigger name for himself, becoming hip hop’s biggest player, and breaking into the pop-o-sphere. He pretty much predicted his success, and did it with ease.", "annotation_id": "8575003", "url": "https://genius.com/8575003", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Empire", "line": "[Intro: Rick Ross]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Rick reuses his verse and the hook from “Addicted,” from his collab album with Birdman.", "annotation_id": "8574702", "url": "https://genius.com/8574702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Empire", "line": "Olympic pools of money, I’m just glad I learned to swim", "vault_lines": ["I'm addicted to money, addicted to chips", "Olympic pools of money, I'm just glad I learned to swim"], "meaning": "The volume of an Olympic-size swimming pool is 2,500 cubic meters, or 88,000 cubic feet. WolframAlpha places the volume of a dollar bill at 0.06943 cubic inches. Therefore, to fill an Olympic-sized swimming pool with American bills, you would need 2,190,200,000 such bills of any denomination to do so. You could use only $1s and still have a cool couple of billion, or upgrade to $100s and nearly triple the wealth of the world’s richest man.", "annotation_id": "8574649", "url": "https://genius.com/8574649", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Empire", "line": "No bench time, nickname Starter Drake Franchise player, I don’t even get a water break", "vault_lines": ["No bench time, nickname Starter Drake", "Franchise player, I don't even get a water break"], "meaning": "Reminiscent of a line from “Tuscan Leather”:\n\nBench players talkin' like starters, I hate it\n\nA franchise player is a team’s most talented, recognizable player—think LeBron James or JJ Watt. Drake views himself as the rap game’s franchise player, and it’s a full-time job.\n\nIf he doesn’t work on his long-range game, he might see some bench time.", "annotation_id": "8574646", "url": "https://genius.com/8574646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Empire", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 6, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-empire-lyrics", "genius_title": "Empire"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "I swear if somebody put hands on you, then he is getting erased (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I swear if somebody put hands on you, then he is getting erased (Yeah)"], "meaning": "This could be a play-on to the previous bar.\n\nIn the Marvel Universe, the main supervillain Thanos uses a gadget named the Infinity Glove. With the usage of this glove, he was able to erase half the universe with a single snap. Drake says that he could erase whoever puts his hands on the person in question as fast as Thanos did.", "annotation_id": "40206867", "url": "https://genius.com/40206867", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "My hands, they never get dirty, I’m not even leaving a trace Not even leaving a trace, yeah", "vault_lines": ["My hands, they never get dirty, I'm not even leaving a trace", "Not even leaving a trace, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake boasts about his ability to orchestrate hits without leaving a paper trail for the media or law enforcement. He also alludes to this idea in “DUST,” rapping,\n\nI heard your brother got flipped, did it sloppy, so that wasn’t our work Definitely gon' know if it’s us if we got him, just wait ‘til it’s our turn, ayy\n\nBy contrasting his precision with the sloppy execution of others, he reinforces the idea that his crew operates with unmatched discretion.", "annotation_id": "40022770", "url": "https://genius.com/40022770", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "Your friends, they be gatherin’ evidence on me to take you away", "vault_lines": ["Your friends, they be gatherin' evidence on me to take you away"], "meaning": "This line introduces a theme of criminal acts which plays along the ex-men/marvel starting with\n\ngathering evidence\n\nher friends are gathering evidence to see if Drake is cheating either out of spite of Drake or may stem from the idea that ‘single girls keep girls single’.", "annotation_id": "39848904", "url": "https://genius.com/39848904", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "It’s a jumbo honey bun for an eight-bar feature (You know I’m feeling you)", "vault_lines": ["You know I'm feeling you, I'm wanting you", "You know I'm feeling you", "It's a jumbo honey bun for an eight-bar feature (You know I'm feeling you)"], "meaning": "A jumbo honey bun in slang refers to $100,000", "annotation_id": "39816522", "url": "https://genius.com/39816522", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "Girl, I’m feeling you, I’m wanting you (Yeah) You know I’m feeling you, I’m wanting you You know I’m feeling you", "vault_lines": ["Girl, I'm feeling you, I'm wanting you (Yeah)", "You know I'm feeling you, I'm wanting you", "You know I'm feeling you"], "meaning": "The bridge interpolates the chorus from “U Know What’s Up,” a late 90s R&B classic from Grammy-winning artist Donell Jones:\n\nI’m diggin' you (Baby), I’m feelin' you\nAnd you know what’s up (You gotta know what’s up, baby)\nSaid I’m big on you and I’m wantin' you\nSo tell me, what’s up? (So tell me, what’s up, baby?)\n\nhttps://youtu.be/W2mEjkBmDQo?t=74", "annotation_id": "39801637", "url": "https://genius.com/39801637", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "Mixed signs, wish I was able to read your mind", "vault_lines": ["Mixed signs, wish I was able to read your mind"], "meaning": "Drakes talk about how the partner/SO in question is giving him mixed signals (when their actions and words don’t go together, like being inconsistent with texts, feelings and ultimately create confusion).\n\nAs a result, Drake states that he wishes he had the ability to read this person’s mind, in order to free himself of the sticky situation", "annotation_id": "39797013", "url": "https://genius.com/39797013", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "Your BM ain’t got no munyun, come get with a keeper (Your man)", "vault_lines": ["Your BM ain't got no munyun, come get with a keeper (Your man)"], "meaning": "A BM often means a Baby momma, who is a woman who has a child with a man ,but is not married to him or in a committed relationship with him.", "annotation_id": "39796978", "url": "https://genius.com/39796978", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "It’s Sexyy Red (Yeah), it’s nice to meet ya (Hey)", "vault_lines": ["It's Sexyy Red (Yeah), it's nice to meet ya (Hey)"], "meaning": "Sexyy Red starts her verse in a similar format to the song “Illegal” by PinkPantheress. (“My name is Pink and I’m really glad to meet you”)", "annotation_id": "39796973", "url": "https://genius.com/39796973", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "You flew out a", "vault_lines": ["You flew out a"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to Baby M and his ongoing love triangle with her and Dillon Brooks an NBA player for the Phoenix Suns. Shai Gilgeous-Alexander who plays for the OKC Thunder dropped 30 points on Brooks when Baby M came out to watch her boyfriend Brooks play.", "annotation_id": "39796127", "url": "https://genius.com/39796127", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "Got ex-men issues like Marvel, but them niggas know I don’t play (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Got ex-men issues like Marvel, but them niggas know I don't play (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Drake references X-Men, making use of “ex-men” as a play on words for former boyfriends and relationship issues. He follows the line with “them niggas know I don’t play,” implying the men connected to the women he dates know not to challenge or disrespect him.\n\nX-Men is an American superhero film series based on the X-Men, a group of characters created by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby for comic books published by Marvel Comics. The character, “Iceman”, is also an X-Men.", "annotation_id": "39779697", "url": "https://genius.com/39779697", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "line": "Shai gave your boyfriend a thirty-piece", "vault_lines": ["nd Shai gave your boyfriend a thirty-piece"], "meaning": "Should be “Shai gave your boyfriend a 30-piece” in reference to Toronto native and Drake affiliate Shai Gilgeous Alexander of the Oklahoma City Thunder.", "annotation_id": "39778965", "url": "https://genius.com/39778965", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 11, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-sexyy-red-hurrr-nor-thurrr-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hurrr Nor Thurrr"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "Gotta be real with it, ayy, yup", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the Music video, this line is restated as “I just gotta be real with it” As it was purposely cut off in streaming platforms.", "annotation_id": "38493242", "url": "https://genius.com/38493242", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, he bad", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a sample from Magnolia Shorty’s 2010 track, “Smoking Gun,” which in turn sampled Jadakiss' 2009 track of the same name. Magnolia’s version exemplifies an authentic New Orleans sound, known as Bounce, and the sample here also serves as homage to Magnolia Shorty, Drake’s former labelmate at Cash Money Records, who was murdered in 2010.\n\n\n\nMagnolia, right, standing with Cash Money founder and fellow New Orleans rapper, Birdman.\n\n“Trade” is New Orleans slang for men who’re handsome and have money.", "annotation_id": "15207694", "url": "https://genius.com/15207694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "Look, the new me is really still the real me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake may be repudiating critics' claims that he has “sold out”–both as an artist and as a father/partner–in his pursuit of commercial success.\n\nFollowing the controversial revelations elucidated in Pusha-T’s “The Story of Adidon,” Drake utilizes this first line to affirm his belief that his decisions are true to himself.", "annotation_id": "15085692", "url": "https://genius.com/15085692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "Skate and Smoke and Rap Now let me see you", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A sample from Lil Wayne’s 2012 “S.A.S.A.R.A.F.,” this lyric was initially not included in “In My Feelings” when Scorpion dropped. Previously, there was four seconds of silence between the chorus and the breakdown, before being updated on streaming services.\n\nProducer BlaqNmilD added this sample after Drake mentioned that it would’ve been nice to have gotten Wayne on the song.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/mAr9bX_38ms?t=100", "annotation_id": "15034493", "url": "https://genius.com/15034493", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "Two bad bitches and we kissin’ in the Wraith Kissin’-kissin’ in the Wraith, kiss-kissin’ in the Wraith", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A Wraith is a British luxury vehicle manufactured by Rolls-Royce Motor Cars. It starts at $320,000.\n\nSince the 2013 model released, many big-name rappers have been seen driving Wraiths, including Drake, who owns one in black.\nhttp://thenewswheel.com/wp-content/themes/patterns/timthumb.php?src=http://thenewswheel.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/12/Drakes-Rolls-Royce-Wraith.png&q=90&w=660&zc=1\n\nIn 2016, Birdman, Drake’s former boss, released a pointed video calling out rappers who buy Wraiths—saying the car is for ladies, in response to Rick Ross' claims of being the first with one.", "annotation_id": "14998712", "url": "https://genius.com/14998712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "I need that black card and the code to the safe Code to the safe, code-code to the safe-safe", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The American Express Centurion Card, informally known as the Black Card, is an invite only charge card.\n\n\n\n American Express only issues these cards to members after they have met a variety of criteria. The $7,500 initiation fee has been enough to make the Black Card a symbol of wealth and prosperity, as it offers members features such as 24/7 concierge service and an unlimited spend limit.", "annotation_id": "14981411", "url": "https://genius.com/14981411", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "(BlaqNmilD, you a genius, you diggin’ me?)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the producer tag for BlaqNmilD, who co-produced this song with TrapMoneyBenny. He also produced “Nice for What,” the other song on the Scorpion album influenced by New Orleans bounce—which is BlaqNmilD’s speciality.", "annotation_id": "14890642", "url": "https://genius.com/14890642", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "Kiki, do you love me? Are you riding? Say you’ll never ever leave from beside me ’Cause I want ya, and I need ya And I’m down for you always KB, do you love me? Are you riding? Say you’ll never ever leave from beside me ’Cause I want ya, and I need ya And I’m down for you always", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "While some fans speculated that “Kiki” was singer Keshia Chanté, a credible source told Genius “Kiki” is actually model and social media personality K'yanna Barber (“KB”).\n\nDrake would go on to confirm this in July 2023, in a podcast with Bobbi Altoff:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/YrdQI3nqh7A?t=1987\n\nAlthough Barber has not directly acknowledged that she’s the Kiki in these lyrics, she tweeted about them, liked a tweet where a fan speculated they were referring to her, and even quoted the “KB” lyric in a tweet:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/4daloveofKJ/status/1016160146676781056", "annotation_id": "14882003", "url": "https://genius.com/14882003", "state": "accepted", "votes": 662, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "Bring that ass, bring that ass, bring that ass back B-bring that ass, bring that ass, bring that ass back Shawty say the nigga that she with can’t hit But shawty, I’ma hit it, hit it like I can’t miss", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a sample of Lil Wayne’s 2008 hit “Lollipop.” It was his most commercially successful song, winning him a Grammy, and it went 4x Platinum.\n\nDrake sampled another of his label mates, Nicki Minaj, on “That’s How You Feel,” which appears two songs prior to this one on the Scorpion tracklist.", "annotation_id": "14881972", "url": "https://genius.com/14881972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "I buy you Champagne but you love some Henny", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Hennessy, or Henny, is a cognac from France. It’s very popular in the African American community and countless rappers have mentioned it in their music (Drake rapped about it in 2016’s “One Dance” and 2012’s “HYFR”). Drake offers champagne, which is a symbol of sophistication and class, but Kiki and KB are more down to earth and from the block, so they prefer Henny.\n\n\n\nDrake also goes under the alias of “champagne papi,” as per his Instagram handle. Though he gives her himself (the champagne) she still refuses and prefers something else.", "annotation_id": "14877723", "url": "https://genius.com/14877723", "state": "accepted", "votes": 100, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "Trap, TrapMoneyBenny", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "TrapMoneyBenny is a producer from Cleveland, Ohio who has worked with artists like Chief Keef, 21 Savage, and Gucci Mane. This track marks Drake and Benny’s first collaboration.\n\nIn an interview with The Fader published six days after “In My Feelings” dropped, Benny spoke about working on the track:\n\nI did kind of like the “meat and potatoes” of the beat: the melody, and most of the drums. 40 and BlaqNmilD are the other producers on it, and did a hell of a job with it. They really kind of flipped it and went crazy. They did the samples and gave it that super New Orleans bounce feel.", "annotation_id": "14877418", "url": "https://genius.com/14877418", "state": "accepted", "votes": 695, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "’Resha, do you love me? Are you riding? Say you’ll never ever leave from beside me ’Cause I want ya, and I need ya And I’m down for you always JT, do you love me? Are you riding? Say you’ll never ever leave from beside me ’Cause I want ya, and I need ya And I’m down for you always", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "JT and Yung Miami, whose real name is Caresha, make up the Quality Control-signed duo City Girls. They’re featured in the segue following this chorus.", "annotation_id": "14876695", "url": "https://genius.com/14876695", "state": "accepted", "votes": 312, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "[Skit: Zazie Beetz] You know what, I don’t even care, I need a photo with Drake Because my Instagram is weak as fuck I’m just being real, my shit, look—", "vault_lines": ["You choose, i don't even care why"], "meaning": "This is a scene from Atlanta Season 2, Episode 7: “Champagne Papi” (named after Drake’s Instagram handle).\n\nIn the episode, Zazie Beetz’s character Van and her friends attend a New Years Eve party at Drake’s house. Though Van is hoping to get a picture with Drake (potentially to make Earn jealous), Drake ends up being out of town and on tour.\n\nSoon after the episode aired on FX, Drake posted to his Instagram story making it clear that he had watched and enjoyed the episode.\n\nhttps://pbs.twimg.com/media/Da_J_HUVwAADn_W.jpg:large", "annotation_id": "14876588", "url": "https://genius.com/14876588", "state": "accepted", "votes": 393, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "i", "line": "From the block like you Jenny", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Jenny from the Block” is Jennifer Lopez‘ nickname, and also the title of a song she dropped in 2002 featuring The Lox. The song is about Lopez never forgetting her roots in the Bronx despite her fame and fortune. In the same way Drake states that his girl has been 100 with him since day one, and has not let his fame change her.\n\nIn late 2016, after Drake and J-Lo posted some snuggly photos online, it was rumored that they were more than friendly. While Drake never publicly addressed the rumor, he seemed to imply they’d fallen out of favor on his 2017 track, “Free Smoke”:\n\nI drunk text J-Lo\nOld number, so it bounce back\n\nAnd his January 2018 single “Diplomatic Immunity”:\n\n2017 I lost a J. Lo\n\nDrake also sampled J-Lo on “Teenage Fever” from his 2017 “playlist” More Life.", "annotation_id": "14876322", "url": "https://genius.com/14876322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
