{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 18 Tracklist", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 4, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-ovo-sound-radio-episode-18-tracklist-lyrics", "genius_title": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 18 Tracklist"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Got so many chains, they call me Chaining Tatum (They do, they do)", "vault_lines": ["Got so many chains, they call me Chaining Tatum (They do, they do)"], "meaning": "Drake creates a portmanteau with Channing Tatum and Chaining Tatum. The actor is a symbol of success, as is a thick chain.\n\n\n\nYou won’t catch Drake chain-less, he made that clear on “Started From The Bottom”:\n\nNigga, just as a reminder to myself\nI wear every single chain, even when I’m in the house cause we…\n\nWhen a rapper is affiliated with a crew, or record label, they are bestowed a chain. Over Drake’s lifetime,he’s seen Jay Z create a dynasty with Roc-A-Fella and 50 Cent do the same with G-Unit, now it’s Drake’s turn with OVO.\n\nChanning Tatum’s wife, Jenna Dewan-Tatum, playfully responded to this line in an Instagram post.\n\nThis line was included in XXL’s, “20 Most Disappointing Hip Hop Lyrics of 2016 So Far.”", "annotation_id": "9086716", "url": "https://genius.com/9086716", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "This sound like some forty-three-oh-one shit (One shit)", "vault_lines": ["This sound like some forty-three-oh-one shit (One shit)"], "meaning": "4301 Kingston Rd. is a housing complex in Toronto, Canada. Members of Reps Up records (P. Reign’s crew) reside in Galloway, just down the street from 4301 .\n\nhttps://twitter.com/WordOnRd/status/342090475945205760\n\n“40-301” could also be the numerical representation of Noah ‘40’ Shebib and Boi-1da. Shebib is synonymous with the number “40”, whilst the first three letters of Boi-1da could signify “301”. Both producers worked on this track, and are frequent collaborators of Drake.", "annotation_id": "9075702", "url": "https://genius.com/9075702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "[Verse 2]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a different version of the song than the one released prior to Views. The single featured Jay Z and Kanye West (credited as “The Throne”).\n\nOn this version, Drake replaced them with a verse of his own.", "annotation_id": "9071007", "url": "https://genius.com/9071007", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "All I do is hang with the young and heartless", "vault_lines": ["All I do is hang with the young and heartless"], "meaning": "Drake’s OVO crew is considerably younger than Kanye and Jay Z (Drake is 29, Kanye is 38 and Jay is 46). Young people are often associated with reckless decisions that make them seem heartless.\n\n\n\nFans have also pointed out “young” and “heartless” may allude to Jay Z (Young Hova) and Kanye (see: “Heartless”).", "annotation_id": "8989932", "url": "https://genius.com/8989932", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Problems hit the gym, they all work out", "vault_lines": ["Problems hit the gym, they all work out"], "meaning": "Like every celebrity who suddenly discovers the weight room, Drake has gained a passion for working out.\n\nHe “problem solves” his problems in the gym,  similar to what he portrays in the hook of  “Faithful”:\n\nWorking, working, working, working, ain’t ya?\nYou don’t have no time to lay up\nYou just trying to be somebody\n\n\n\nSince the speculations of Drake and Serena Williams being in a relationship flooded social media, Drake has posted multiple gym pictures of him flexing via Instagram.", "annotation_id": "8967699", "url": "https://genius.com/8967699", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Everybody looking out for themself But they still got they eyes on me", "vault_lines": ["Everybody looking out for themself", "But they still got they eyes on me"], "meaning": "Drake warns everyone to watch out for themselves as he did on “Summer Sixteen,” while throwing a subtle jab at  Meek Mill once more with a reference to Meek’s collaboration with his partner, Nicki Minaj, on “All Eyes on You.” Meek’s track is inspired by 2Pac’s song “All Eyez on Me,” which this lyric may also be referring to.\n\nDrizzy points out that, although everyone in the rap game needs to focus on their business, everyone will still watches his moves since he’s the biggest name in the game right now.", "annotation_id": "8967688", "url": "https://genius.com/8967688", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "They still out to get me, I don’t get it I can not be got, and that’s a given", "vault_lines": ["They still out to get me 'cause they never got me", "They still out to get me, I don't get it", "I can not be got, and that's a given"], "meaning": "Drake recites the two lines Jay Z wrote on on the original “Pop Style” featuring The Throne, applying it to his own situation.\n\nDrake explained to Zane Lowe why he didn’t keep The Throne version for Views:\n\nJay didn’t really do a verse. Really I was just trying to get ‘Ye on it at first, and ‘Ye just sent it to me like that, like, ‘Yo, Jay just kind of did my first two lines for me. He was just here, and that’s how we flexed it.’ It was cool, he was all excited about, ‘Yo, put The Throne, it’ll be a crazy moment!’\n\nTo get “got” is an expression used primarily in the mob/gang scene when referencing someone being killed, or in this case being de-throned.\n\nWith Drake’s enviable position in the rap game, many people would choose him as their next target. However, Drake refutes this, claiming the people who want to de-throne him don’t deserve his position. This is something Jay Z has done many times.\n\nOn “Summer Sixteen,” Drake labelled himself the next Jay Z:\n\nI used to wanna be on Roc-A-Fella then I turned into Jay", "annotation_id": "8967602", "url": "https://genius.com/8967602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "I can’t trust no fuckin’ body They still out to get me ’cause they never got me", "vault_lines": ["I can't trust no fuckin' body", "They still out to get me 'cause they never got me", "They still out to get me, I don't get it"], "meaning": "Drake’s trust issues stem from disloyalty within his close circle. From 2014-early 2016, copious Drake songs have made way to the public in part to leaks. Upwards of 15 songs have leaked with connection to his forthcoming Views From the 6, as well as a handful of songs being leaked before 2015’s surprise album, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late.\n\nMore recently, Meek Mill responded Drizzy’s “Summer Sixteen” with “War Pain” in a matter of just 15 minutes. The song was littered with references to “Summer Sixteen,” something that only could’ve happened if it was leaked, which Meek claimed Drake’s ghostwriter leaked him the lyrics.\n\nEven with the quick response, Drake sees himself as the winner of the beef because no diss record of Meek has ever had as much publicity as Drake’s grammy nominated “Back to Back.”", "annotation_id": "8967191", "url": "https://genius.com/8967191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Girl, let me rock, rock, rock, rock, rock your body Justin Timberlake and then I hit the highway", "vault_lines": ["Girl, let me rock, rock, rock, rock, rock your body", "Justin Timberlake and then I hit the highway"], "meaning": "A reference to the classic Justin Timberlake song, “Rock Your Body.”\n\n\n\nThe song details Justin and his cravings for his girl. Similarly, Drake rocks his girl’s body—as soon as he’s done, he’s on his worst behavior, leaving straight after sex.", "annotation_id": "8967190", "url": "https://genius.com/8967190", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "I feel like they wanna see me learn the hard way But you know I always handle that one my way", "vault_lines": ["I feel like they wanna see me learn the hard way", "But you know I always handle that one my way"], "meaning": "As of 2016, Drake’s on top of the rap game. 9 top 10 charting singles, what was regarded by most as a success beef against Meek Mill and high sales numbers (his surprise album If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late sold 535,000 units in the first week), among many other accomplishments.\n\nFor Drake’s haters (Meek Mill, Mo-G among others), they want to see him fall from the top. However, Drake started from the bottom, so don’t expect him to go out without a fight.", "annotation_id": "8967188", "url": "https://genius.com/8967188", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "All this for my family, man, I try my hardest It’s all I ever did and look where it got him", "vault_lines": ["All this for my family, man, I try my hardest", "It's all I ever did and look where it got him"], "meaning": "Despite his copious accolades, Drake’s motivation in the rap game stems from his desire to take care of those that he’s close with. Drake famously explored this idea on the chorus to his February 2014 hit track, “Trophies,” Drake boasted:\n\nWhat’s the move? Can I tell the truth?\nIf I was doing this for you then I’d have nothing left to prove\nNah, this for me though\nI’m just tryna stay alive and take care of my people\nAnd they don’t have no award for that", "annotation_id": "8967187", "url": "https://genius.com/8967187", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "And I like to finish what you think you started Man, you boys just got to Hollywood, you boys just started You don’t know what you just started", "vault_lines": ["And you don't know what you started", "And I like to finish what you think you started", "Man, you boys just got to Hollywood, you boys just started", "You don't know what you just started"], "meaning": "Drake replies to all the new school rappers who have sent shots at him, specifically Tory Lanez, Meek Mill and Mo-G.\n\nIn each of the three scenarios, Drake was attacked first. Tory took a shot at Drake in a freestyle, Meek Mill infamously tweeted about Drake’s alleged use of a ghostwriter,, and Mo-G accused Drake of stealing a line from him.\n\nDespite leaving things unsettled with Meek after “Back to Back”, the public has already announced Drake as the winner.\n\nDrake’s early acting career prepared him for drama and adequately equipped him with the tools to defeat his jealous contemporaries.", "annotation_id": "8967184", "url": "https://genius.com/8967184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Tell my mom I love her if I do not make it (Do not make it)", "vault_lines": ["Tell my mom I love her if I do not make it (Do not make it)"], "meaning": "Drake and his mom have always had a strong relationship. In “Look What You’ve Done,” Drake speaks to his mom and emphasizes that no matter what fights that they’ve been through, he would still be there for her and vice-versa. “You & The 6,” Drake says his mom (and the 6) “raised me right.” In an interview with E!, he called his mom the most important person in his life:\n\nMy mother is an incredible woman. I talk about her a lot on this album [referring to Nothing Was The Same], she’s brought me to this point single-handedly…She’s the most important person in my life.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/0oKrQSDQFA/\nRap is competitive and dangerous, as collaborator Quentin Miller found out. Meek Mill took violent shots at him on “I’m Da Plug (Freestyle)” and reportedly beat him in an L.A Nike store.", "annotation_id": "8967182", "url": "https://genius.com/8967182", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "All my niggas wanna do is pop style (Pop style)", "vault_lines": ["All my niggas wanna do is pop style (Pop style)"], "meaning": "“Pop Style” is a Jamaican term meaning “ballin’” or “showing off.” The term was famously used on Althea and Donna’s “Uptown Top Ranking,\" in 1977:\n\nNo pop no style\nI strictly roots\nNo pop no style\nI strictly roots\n\nDrake and his OVO crew flex their expensive items, as mentioned on “Crew Love”:\n\nThere’s times when I might blow like 50k on a vacation\nFor all my soldiers just to see the looks on all they faces", "annotation_id": "8967175", "url": "https://genius.com/8967175", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "My friend’s chain so big that he look like the artist", "vault_lines": ["My friend's chain so big that he look like the artist"], "meaning": "Drake’s non-musical crew is large and well taken care of. One such member is Chubbs, Drake’s body guard. He rocks a giant Maple Leaf chain.\n\n\n\nThere’s also Ryan Silverstein, an associate who has his own OVO chain. Drake uses this line to show “Started From The Bottom” wasn’t just posturing for Instagram, his friends chains are just as big as mortal rap artists.", "annotation_id": "8966934", "url": "https://genius.com/8966934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "See me putting in the hard work now, ayy Momma doesn’t have to call work now, ayy I decide when I start work now, ayy", "vault_lines": ["See me putting in the hard work now, ayy", "Momma doesn't have to call work now, ayy", "I decide when I start work now, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake has become successful enough where his mother Sandi doesn’t have to worry about money. His love for his mom has never been a secret.\n\nAdditionally, he works so he can have easy hours. Being your own boss has its perks, like flexible hours and flexible pay. After all, he is a perfectionist:\n\nBorn a perfectionist, guess that makes me a bit obsessive", "annotation_id": "8966894", "url": "https://genius.com/8966894", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "They been out here tryna slide on me They been out here telling lies on me", "vault_lines": ["They been out here tryna slide on me", "They been out here telling lies on me"], "meaning": "Artists like Meek Mill and Mo-G have been accusing Drake of having ghost writers and stealing styles and lyrics from other artists. Drake is setting the record straight—the allegations are false.", "annotation_id": "8966811", "url": "https://genius.com/8966811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Trade you off the team while you in your sleep, yeah Y’all showed me that nothing’s guaranteed And you don’t know what you started", "vault_lines": ["Trade you off the team while you in your sleep, yeah", "Y'all showed me that nothing's guaranteed", "And you don't know what you started", "You don't know what you just started"], "meaning": "A possible shot at Mo-G, fellow Canadian rapper and former OVO affiliate. In the weeks leading up to the songs release, Mo made numerous posts on Instagram alleging Drake and his manager Oliver El-Khatib failed to pay him after he helped write songs.\n\nHe also accused Drake of swagger jacking his “switch it up with the left like Ginobili” dance. Drake shouted Mo and his dance out on “Summer Sixteen.”\n\nJust days after these posts were deleted, Mo posted a picture of himself on Instagram in hospital with the caption: “When you speak from the heart this is what happens…#Dirtysociety #Dirtyindustry.”\n\n\n\nSeems OVO didn’t like what they saw and “swapped” Mo out.\n\nIt could also refer to the initial version of this song, that featured “The Throne,” aka Kanye West and Jay Z. In his Zane Lowe interview Drake explained the situation “didn’t play out” as he’d hoped, and he removed their parts on the track for the album version.\n\nAlthough Drake has expressed his admiration for Kanye, he has a torturous relationship with Jay. This could be another footnote in the saga.", "annotation_id": "8966800", "url": "https://genius.com/8966800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Cannot be me I’m a one-of-one Schooling your ass like 101", "vault_lines": ["Cannot be me I'm a one-of-one", "Schooling your ass like 101"], "meaning": "Drake can’t be copied. Although many have tried to imitate his style, no one can truly match Drake at his own game.\n\n“Schooling someone” means to beat someone  by such a large margin the game is considered more of a lesson (teacher and student) than anything competitive. “101” are the introductory classes to a subject in school. Drake’s so far ahead of his competition, that he has to teach them how to rap properly in the first place.\n\nFellow artist Kanye West rapped a similar line in “We Major”:\n\nSo they ask me – why you call it Late Registration ‘Ye?\nCause we takin these muh'fuckers back to school\n\nAlso worth noting, by this line Drake has said the number “1” 6 times in this verse! Intentional?", "annotation_id": "8966792", "url": "https://genius.com/8966792", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Turn my birthday into a lifestyle (Lifestyle)", "vault_lines": ["Turn my birthday into a lifestyle (Lifestyle)"], "meaning": "Drake’s birthday is October 24th and the inspiration for his brand OVO (October’s Very Own), a lifestyle that fuses music, culture and fashion.\n\n\n\nAs Drake said on “Headlines”:\n\nDrinking every night because we drink to my accomplishments", "annotation_id": "8966764", "url": "https://genius.com/8966764", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "MVP, MVP, oh-nine all the way to sixteen Even next season looking like a breeze Lot of y’all ain’t built for the league, yeah", "vault_lines": ["MVP, MVP, oh-nine all the way to sixteen", "Even next season looking like a breeze", "Lot of y'all ain't built for the league, yeah"], "meaning": "From 2009’s So Far Gone to 2016’s Views, Drake believes he’s been atop of the music industry for the last seven years. Drizzy’s dominance has him feeling like the most valuable player, or MVP.\n\nJudging by his past success, Drake is confident his “Summer Sixteen” success will permeate the seasons to come. Those who try to discredit him are mere amateurs—he’s a major leaguer.", "annotation_id": "8966758", "url": "https://genius.com/8966758", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Dropped outta school now we dumb rich (Dumb rich)", "vault_lines": ["Dropped outta school now we dumb rich (Dumb rich)"], "meaning": "Drake flips “dumb” from an insult for someone who’s not educated into a braggadocio rhyme—he’s a millionaire without education.\n\nDrizzy attended Forest Hill Collegiate Institute, and later dropped out of Vaughan Road Academy. In his mid 20s, Drake finally graduated.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/258731694687920130\n\nEven with school history, Drake became “dumb rich.” He was ranked 3rd on 2015’s Forbes Cash Kings list.", "annotation_id": "8966748", "url": "https://genius.com/8966748", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "line": "Give a real nigga the number one Even though I got like twenty-one", "vault_lines": ["Give a real nigga the number one", "Even though I got like twenty-one"], "meaning": "At the time of this song’s release, Drake hadn’t yet reached #1 on the Billboard Hot 100 as a primary artist. He has featured on two #1 records—“What’s My Name” and “Work” with Rihanna. Drake’s highest charting singles are 2009’s “Best I Ever Had” and 2015’s “Hotline Bling” which peaked at #2.\n\nHowever, Drake finally got his first Hot 100 #1 with the song “One Dance”, also on Views\n\nDrake also holds records for the most #1 songs on both Billboard’s R&B/Hip-Hop chart and Rap chart with 31 entries combined.\n\nHe has also surpassed The Beatles with the most entries  on the Billboard Hot 100.", "annotation_id": "8966702", "url": "https://genius.com/8966702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pop Style", "status": "done", "targets": 23, "clicked": 23, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-pop-style-lyrics", "genius_title": "Pop Style"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say Something", "line": "This shit was all I knew You and me only I did it all for you Still you were lonely", "vault_lines": ["This shit was all I knew, you and me only", "I did it all for you, still you were lonely"], "meaning": "Drake was enamoured with his now ex-girlfriend, willing to do anything for her but she didn’t seem to care", "annotation_id": "13422309", "url": "https://genius.com/13422309", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Say Something", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 1, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-say-something-lyrics", "genius_title": "Say Something"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "line": "I live for the nights that I can’t remember With the people that I won’t forget", "vault_lines": ["I live for the nights that I can't remember", "With the people that I won't forget"], "meaning": "Drake is talking about celebrating those wild, carefree nights where the details fade but the people you’re with leave a lasting mark. This line captures the idea that unforgettable bonds matter more than remembering exactly what went down.", "annotation_id": "37163883", "url": "https://genius.com/37163883", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "line": "Call me overrated or created or too jaded Because any way you put it, bitch, I made it—Yup!", "vault_lines": ["Call me overrated or created or too jaded", "Because any way you put it, bitch, I made it—Yup!"], "meaning": "Drake has had a good amount of relationships turn sour. In his song From Time Drake reminisce on a conversation with an old fling telling him he will never be as big as Trey Songz.\n\n“Then we got to talking ’bout something we disagreed on Then she start telling me how I’ll never be as big as Trey Songz Boy, was she wrong”\n\nCould drake be using Trey Songz’s ad lib. in this line to subliminally call her out for doubting him.\n\nI don’t doubt this, Drake is the self proclaimed petty king\n\nhttps://genius.com/Drake-omerta-lyrics#note-17400441", "annotation_id": "23301694", "url": "https://genius.com/23301694", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "line": "I came up in the underground, though So I’mma spend another $10,000 for Dilla", "vault_lines": ["I came up in the underground, though", "So I'mma spend another $10,000 for Dilla"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to the record producer J Dilla, who came up in the underground hip-hop scene in the 1990’s. Him and Drake have a collaboration called “Where to Now” from Drake’s second mixtape, Comeback Season, released in 2007. However, a year earlier, J Dilla had passed away due to a rare blood disease. According to his obituary on NPR, J Dilla was one of the most influential hip-hop artists.\n\n\n\nA photo of J Dilla", "annotation_id": "16542112", "url": "https://genius.com/16542112", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "line": "Can’t get me right and not do Wayne Cash Money, Young Money, pop champagne", "vault_lines": ["Can't get me right and not do Wayne", "Cash Money, Young Money, pop champagne"], "meaning": "Drake and Lil Wayne have known each other since 2006, when they met through a mutual friend named Jas Prince, and later Drake officially signed to Wayne’s label, Young Money, in 2009. It was formerly under Birdman’s label, Cash Money, before they separated in 2018.", "annotation_id": "16541989", "url": "https://genius.com/16541989", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "line": "Presidential suite, girl: Barack Hussein", "vault_lines": ["Presidential suite, girl: Barack Hussein"], "meaning": "Barack Hussein Obama\n\n\n\nIn these case: Drake is refering to the finest room: The Presidential? (It’s not the first time he’s made this claim).", "annotation_id": "2730914", "url": "https://genius.com/2730914", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "line": "I’m the Osiris to this shit right now", "vault_lines": ["I'm the Osiris to this shit right now"], "meaning": "Quoting Old Dirty Bastard (RIP) of Wu-Tang – who used it as one of his many nicknames – Drake compares himself to Osiris, the green-skinned Ancient Egyptian God of the Underworld… get it? God of the underworld? Underground King?", "annotation_id": "19774", "url": "https://genius.com/19774", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "line": "People really hate when a backpack rapper Get rich and start living that life, dawg Feels like when you get into that paper, hip hop hates you", "vault_lines": ["People really hate when a backpack rapper", "Get rich and start living that life, dawg", "Feels like when you get into that paper, hip hop hates you"], "meaning": "A “backpack rapper” is like an “indie rocker” – anti-mainstream and sometimes viewed as snobby/elitist. Drake claims that he used to be a backpack rapper (debatable), and now people hate him because he’s making money and living the life.", "annotation_id": "19771", "url": "https://genius.com/19771", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "line": "What we doing right now, this our dream Wu-Tang Clan, niggas want that cream", "vault_lines": ["What we doing right now, this our dream", "Wu-Tang Clan, niggas want that cream"], "meaning": "Drake wants “Cream” (dairy slang for “cash”) because Wu-Tang Clan taught him as a young man: “Cash Rules Everything Around [him]”\n\n..aaaand two albums later, Drake drops “Wu-Tang Forever”.", "annotation_id": "19764", "url": "https://genius.com/19764", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "line": "Tell me can we kick it like Ali Shaheed and Phife Dawg?", "vault_lines": ["Tell me can we kick it like Ali Shaheed and Phife Dawg?"], "meaning": "Refers to the A Tribe Called Quest feel-good song “Can I Kick It”, in which Ali and Phife (members of A Tribe Called Quest) ask the ladies (and fellas, too) one question: “Can I Kick It?”", "annotation_id": "19763", "url": "https://genius.com/19763", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Show Me a Good Time", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 9, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-show-me-a-good-time-lyrics", "genius_title": "Show Me a Good Time"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "She want the fit off the mannequin, she want a reason to get out of Canada", "vault_lines": ["She want the fit off the mannequin, she want a reason to get out of Canada"], "meaning": "Drake puts a double meaning into this bar: “she want the fit off the mannequin” can also mean she want the Fits (a cheap Sri Lankan airline, FitsAir) off to Dominica, a Caribbean country. In the following line he claims the cheap prices of the airline as the reason to get out of Canada.", "annotation_id": "40062406", "url": "https://genius.com/40062406", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "There ain’t no point in pullin’ out my record and trackin’ it, preach what you practicin’", "vault_lines": ["There ain't no point in pullin' out my record and trackin' it, preach what you practicin'"], "meaning": "Drake is pointing out the girl’s hypocrisy. She does not need to give receipts of Drake’s sexual escapades when she herself was doing the same thing, hence why Drake is saying if she is just the same as Drake then she should shut up.\n\nIf she was innocent she would have a reason to call Drake out, however, they both doing the same thing but she gets to see only Drake flaws and not hers? Ultimately.", "annotation_id": "39911891", "url": "https://genius.com/39911891", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "You went to Queen’s University, now you a graduate, shout out your family them", "vault_lines": ["You went to Queen's University, now you a graduate, shout out your family them"], "meaning": "Queen’s University is a varsity in Kingston, Ontario, Canada. The girl Drake is talking to, is not a simple girl he met by chance. He added this to show she is a scholar. She graduated in College and he even gives a shout out to her family who probably paid for her tuition which can cost between CAD $7,000 – $20,000 annually.\n\nDrake has highlighted this girl’s vices but he adds this line to give perspective that this is not just an ordinary girl, she is learned and usually a learned person you expect to have a particular perspective in life compared to some who aren’t.", "annotation_id": "39911599", "url": "https://genius.com/39911599", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "Yeah, most girls in the city will come to my house and they know where the bathroom is", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, most girls in the city will come to my house and they know where the bathroom is"], "meaning": "Drake has been with so many women in Toronto that most of them would know where the bathroom is in his house without asking.", "annotation_id": "39842198", "url": "https://genius.com/39842198", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "Better find love ‘fore we slap the whole city in and there ain’t nothin’ left", "vault_lines": ["Better find love 'fore we slap the whole city in and there ain't nothin' left"], "meaning": "Drake and his friends know they need to find love soon or they’re going to run the risk of hooking up with every girl in the city (“slap the city up”) and running through all of their viable options of a girl “worthy” of long term relationship. Ironically being in his position of fame he understands it’s probably impossible to find love because he’ll never know if a woman really loves him for him or just because he’s Drake. Thus the endless loop of meaningless sex while true love remains elusive.", "annotation_id": "39842128", "url": "https://genius.com/39842128", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "Two hundred girls filled the crib, who summoned ’em? Spade or Astral, it must be one of them", "vault_lines": ["Two hundred girls filled the crib, who summoned 'em?", "Spade or Astral, it must be one of them"], "meaning": "DJ Spade (Spade Muzik) is known simply as “Spade,” Drake’s official touring DJ and close personal friend. The two have collaborated closely on events, and Spade frequently hosts Drake’s exclusive parties.\n\nPicture of Spademuzik in New York\n\nAstral (Niko) who is also recognized widely as “@photobyastral,” he is Drake’s creative director and photographer.", "annotation_id": "39838803", "url": "https://genius.com/39838803", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "Your waist so nice to grip, didn’t cost a grip", "vault_lines": ["Your waist so nice to grip, didn't cost a grip"], "meaning": "“Grip” is used two ways in the same couplet. The first means a handful, the second is slang for a lot of money. Drake is saying her waist is nice to hold and her body didn’t cost much, meaning it’s natural rather than surgically done.\nThe compliment sets up the verse’s running theme of separating what’s real from what’s bought, which becomes his whole argument by verse three.", "annotation_id": "39804979", "url": "https://genius.com/39804979", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "You expect men you fuckin’ to buy you nice things, but you wouldn’t call it transactional", "vault_lines": ["You expect men you fuckin' to buy you nice things, but you wouldn't call it transactional"], "meaning": "Drake is basically calling out what he sees as a contradiction.\n\nA “transactional” relationship means affection, attention, sex, or companionship are exchanged for money, gifts, status, or benefits rather than genuine connection.\n\nSo when he says:\n\n“You expect men you fuckin’ to buy you nice things, but you wouldn’t call it transactional”\n\nhe’s saying:\n\n\nThe woman expects expensive gifts or financial treatment from the men she’s involved with.\nBut she doesn’t want to label the relationship as being based on exchange or material benefit.\nDrake is pointing out that even if nobody openly says “pay me for this,” the expectations still make the dynamic feel transactional to him.\n\n\n\nThe lyric reflects a common theme in his music:\n\n\nblurred lines between love and luxury,\nmodern dating expectations,\nand questioning whether affection is genuine or tied to lifestyle/status.", "annotation_id": "39803037", "url": "https://genius.com/39803037", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "Yeah, you treat Clase Azul like it’s apple juice", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, you treat Clase Azul like it's apple juice"], "meaning": "Clase Azul Tequila is a luxury tequila commonly found in upscale social settings, but in her world it’s just part of the usual scene.\n\nDrake saying she treats it like “apple juice” shows how casually she consumes luxury,  what feels high-end to others is just routine to her.", "annotation_id": "39798665", "url": "https://genius.com/39798665", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "Big crib, but I feel like no one’s home", "vault_lines": ["Big crib, but I feel like no one's home"], "meaning": "Drake refers to his mansion, “The Embassy” which is located in the ultra-luxurious Bridle Path Neighborhood in Toronto.\n\nHe states that in his massive estate, he feels completely alone. This can either mean the sheer size of the house, or the fact that he is currently not in a relationship, meaning there is no one home for him to come back to, except his son.", "annotation_id": "39797111", "url": "https://genius.com/39797111", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "Don’t try and tell your man good things come in no small-ass packages, you need a daggerin’", "vault_lines": ["Don't try and tell your man good things come in no small-ass packages, you need a daggerin'"], "meaning": "“Good things come in small packages” is an idiom that’s usually used humorously to mean small things/people are of great worth. It’s sometimes used to “gas up” people who are short or of small stature. It normally carries no sexual connotation at all; however, based on the context of the line and considering “package” is a euphemism for penis, the listener is supposed to imagine a situation where this girl’s man is feeling insecure about his small dick and she’s telling him, “Don’t worry, good things come in small packages!” Drake’s telling her not to bother consoling her man; she can come to Drake and he’ll “dagger her” himself so she can be satisfied.", "annotation_id": "39796688", "url": "https://genius.com/39796688", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "Or just get loose, fly you to wherever, even to the moon", "vault_lines": ["Or just get loose, fly you to wherever, even to the moon"], "meaning": "This line references Fly Me to the Moon, a song originally written by Bart Howard in 1954, however commonly associated to Frank Sinatra’s cover.\nThis is not the first time Drake’s referenced Frank, as he previously had in his 2019 compilation Care Package in 5AM in Toronto.", "annotation_id": "39787511", "url": "https://genius.com/39787511", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "I’m finally ready to see you, I promise I’m burning my candles and flippin’ my mattresses", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Here, “burning my candles” and “flippin’ my mattresses” suggest a restless, almost ritual-like state of preparation. The speaker is not just casually getting ready to meet someone — he sounds emotionally overwhelmed, anxious, and desperate for clarity.\n\n“Burning my candles” can imply staying up late, creating a spiritual or intimate atmosphere, or trying to manifest/control the moment. “Flipping my mattresses” feels like an image of sleeplessness and mental chaos: tossing, turning, searching, and physically disturbing his space because his mind is unsettled.\n\nTogether, the line shows that seeing this person is not simple for him. It has built up into something intense, personal, and almost obsessive.\n\nThis could also be referencing the Sia’s music video for “Chandelier”, in which Maddie Ziegler is seen in a room surrounded by lights and mattresses.", "annotation_id": "39780440", "url": "https://genius.com/39780440", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "And before that, we’ve been movin’ like dogs, I can’t even lie", "vault_lines": ["And before that, we've been movin' like dogs, I can't even lie"], "meaning": "Before this album he dropped For All the Dogs. That’s why he is saying that previously he’s been moving like a dog. Moving like a dog also means acting dirty, disloyal or shameful which in this case could mean that they’ve both been sleeping around or behaving promiscuously.", "annotation_id": "39780221", "url": "https://genius.com/39780221", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "You say you downloaded Hinge, but no one’s compatible, nothin’ was magical There’s no one you matchin’ with", "vault_lines": ["You say you downloaded Hinge, but no one's compatible, nothin' was magical", "There's no one you matchin' with"], "meaning": "On the dating app Hinge, “Most Compatible” is an algorithmically-selected recommendation highlighted at the top of your Discover feed. It pairs you with someone the app calculates you are highly likely to match with based on mutual dealbreakers and recent user activity.", "annotation_id": "39779436", "url": "https://genius.com/39779436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Slap The City", "line": "Should I call you ”Twizz”? Should I call you ”Twin”?", "vault_lines": ["Didn't say your name, should I call you \"Miss\"?", "Should I call you \"Twizz\"? Should I call you \"Twin\"?"], "meaning": "Drake says, “Should I call you Twizz? Should I call you Twin?”\n\nThe word “Twizz” is a slang term popularized by American rapper Yeat and others in the music scene. It’s generally used to refer to a close friend, similar to calling someone your twin or brother.\n\nInstagram Story by @yeat", "annotation_id": "39777676", "url": "https://genius.com/39777676", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
