{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "This life got too deep for you, baby", "vault_lines": ["This life got too deep for you, baby"], "meaning": "This line suggests that the lifestyle Drake leads (likely referencing fame, danger, loyalty, and street life) became overwhelming or too intense for the person he’s addressing, likely his romantic partner.\n\nHe’s acknowledging that the emotional, psychological, or possibly even physical demands of his world were more than they could handle, leading to distance or a falling out. It’s a mix of resignation and reflection – recognizing the gap between his reality and what others can cope with", "annotation_id": "35461742", "url": "https://genius.com/35461742", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Got so many people that I love out of troubled spots", "vault_lines": ["Got so many people that I love out of troubled spots"], "meaning": "Throughout his career, Drake has used his influence and fame to help out people in difficult situations.\n\nIn a 2019 interview with RapUP TV, OVO Sound label-mate Baka Not Nice credits Drizzy with changing his life after he was released from prison. Baka opened up on the interview saying,\n\nHe gave me a chance… he gave me, ya know, a shot… to turn my life around… to do something positive… and that’s what I’m doing right now.\n\nIn 2008, when Canadian rapper Preme (formerly known as P. Reign) was charged with nine counts of weapons possession, Drake helped pay for the legal fees that led to Preme getting acquitted of all charges.\n\nDrake has also been an advocate for other musicians, including when he brought UK rapper JHus on stage immediately following his release from prison. In 2018, Drake’s cosign helped City Girls rapper J.T. to delay her prison check-in after her attorney name-dropped Champagne Papi in court.", "annotation_id": "19466282", "url": "https://genius.com/19466282", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Toosie slide, then I hit it double-time Then I hit a spin ’cause we spun their block a couple times If it’s not the right time, there’ll always be another time I’m not even trippin’, we’ll just see ’em in the summertime, woah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Toosie slide, then I hit it double-time", "Then I hit a spin 'cause we spun their block a couple times", "If it's not the right time, there'll always be another time", "I'm not even trippin', we'll just see 'em in the summertime, woah, yeah"], "meaning": "These lines suggest an alternate interpretation of the chorus—\"sliding\" and “spinning the block” are both terms referring to a drive-by hit. This concept loosely echoes Drake’s lines from Travis Scott’s August 2018 release, “SICKO MODE”:\n\nI crept down the block (Down the block)\nMade a right (Yeah, right)\nCut the lights (Yeah, what?), paid the price (Yeah)\nNiggas think it’s sweet (Nah, never), it’s on sight (Yeah, what?)\n\n“Double time” is a musical term referring to the pace of a song—specifically doubling the song’s tempo. In both verses, Drake’s cadence speeds up from the slower-paced pre-chorus, chorus and post-chorus.", "annotation_id": "19459934", "url": "https://genius.com/19459934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Two thousand shorties wanna tie the knot, ayy, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Two thousand shorties wanna tie the knot, ayy, yeah"], "meaning": "“Tie the knot” means to get married. Drake has shown his disinterest in marriage on his June 2019 song, “Omertà”:\n\nEveryone that’s married is miserable\nI know that that is not a lifestyle I can give into\n\nHowever, a source close to Drake told Hollywood Life in 2017 that he plans to marry when the time is right:\n\nDrake does want to settle down and have kids one day, and he’s confident that the right woman will come along at the right time. Until then, Drake’s not forcing anything, and he’s making the most of the lifestyle that comes along with being Drake.", "annotation_id": "19459929", "url": "https://genius.com/19459929", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Got so many opps, I be mistakin’ opps for other opps", "vault_lines": ["Got so many opps, I be mistakin' opps for other opps"], "meaning": "One of Drake’s “many opps” is Pusha T, who Drake’s been beefing with since 2011 when Push took at Drake on “Don’t Fuck With Me”—a remix of Drake’s May 2011 song “Dreams Money Can Buy.” After that, the two continued to throw shots at each other over the following years.\n\nThe beef reached a peak when Pusha released May 2018’s “The Story of Adidon,” where he exposed Drake for hiding a child he had with former pornstar, Sophie Brussaux. The track was, in turn, a response to Drake’s diss track “Duppy Freestyle” which itself was a response to Pusha’s subs on his song, “Infrared”—both tracks were released on the same day.\n\nDrake’s other “opps” include Joe Budden, who he started beefing with after Budden criticized him for sounding “uninspired” on his fourth studio album, April 2016’s Views and Kanye West, who he started beefing with after he threw shots at Ye on “Duppy Freestyle” and that Ye supposedly told Pusha T about his son, which would have then led to “The Story of Adidon.”", "annotation_id": "19449953", "url": "https://genius.com/19449953", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Solo niggas, only YOLO, for real", "vault_lines": ["Solo niggas, only YOLO, for real"], "meaning": "The term “YOLO” stands for “you only live once.” Drake popularized the term when he used it on the November 2011 Take Care bonus track, “The Motto”:\n\nYou only live once: that’s the motto, nigga, YOLO\n\nNotably, in 2012, Drake wanted royalties for the use of “YOLO” due to the growth of merchandise that showed the word and lyrics from “The Motto” but he didn’t own a trademark on it so he couldn’t earn anything.", "annotation_id": "19449694", "url": "https://genius.com/19449694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "If I’m movin’ shaky, Chubbs’ll do this shit himself, yeah", "vault_lines": ["If they movin' shaky, we'll just do this shit ourselves, woah", "If I'm movin' shaky, Chubbs'll do this shit himself, yeah"], "meaning": "Nessel “Chubbs” Beezer is one of Drake’s closest friends and his OVO head of security. Drake frequently name-drops him in his music—for example, on his June 2016 song, “4PM in Calabasas,” he raps:\n\nCameras pointing every time I look up\nThat’s why I gotta duck behind Chubb’s shoulder just to hit the kush up", "annotation_id": "19449641", "url": "https://genius.com/19449641", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Petal off a rose like I love her not, maybe not", "vault_lines": ["Petal off a rose like I love her not, maybe not"], "meaning": "“She Loves Me, She Loves Me Not” is a game in which one uses a flower to determine if their affection is reciprocated. To play, one alternates between “she loves me” and “she loves me not” while picking off one flower petal each time. The phrase spoken for the final petal is believed to represent the truth.\n\n\n\nFlowers have played an important role in Drake’s life. As he mentioned on his June 2018 track, “Sandra’s Rose,” his mother Sandra Graham was a florist:\n\nMy mother had a flower shop, but I was Sandra’s rose", "annotation_id": "19449525", "url": "https://genius.com/19449525", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Got a dance, but it’s really on some street shit I’ma show you how to get it", "vault_lines": ["Got a dance, but it's really on some street shit", "I'ma show you how", "I'ma show you how to get it"], "meaning": "These are the instructions for the “Toosie Slide,” which was created and named after famed Atlanta choreographer Toosie. On March 29—five days before the song’s release—he, along with other dancers Hiii Key, Ayo and Teo, posted a video of themselves doing the dance, which later went viral. In an interview with GQ a day before the song was released, Toosie spoke on how the dance came about:\n\n[Drake] had a record idea and needed me to come up with a dance to it […] Everybody was contributing little moves, and slowly but surely, we started piecing it together.\n\nOn his Instagram Live stream on April 7, 2020, Drake revealed that he originally wrote these lyrics about Michael Jackson’s iconic “Moonwalk” dance and not a new specific dance made by Toosie:\n\nShoutout to Toosie, ‘cause when I first made that song I was only talking about[…] the “Moonwalk.” That’s why I was like “right foot up, left foot, slide,” but Toosie made the dance to that shit so it’s poppin’ now. I didn’t know it was gonna be a dance song like that but it is what it is.\n\nFollowing his instructions for the song’s dance, Drake plays on the word “slide,” where he talks about the directionality of the dance, with the performer moving both left and right when executing it and secondly saying he can’t let something “slide” or allow to happen.\n\nThe “who’s bad” ad-lib is a nod to Michael Jackson’s September 1987 hit “Bad”—Drake compares his dancing skills to Jackson’s on the song’s post-chorus.", "annotation_id": "19437091", "url": "https://genius.com/19437091", "state": "accepted", "votes": 171, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Nike crossbody, got a piece in it", "vault_lines": ["Nike crossbody, got a piece in it"], "meaning": "A “cross-body” bag is a bag that is worn across a person’s torso. They are sold by a variety of brands, including Nike, a brand that Drake has frequently collaborated with. “Got a piece in it” could mean Drake has a weapon in the bag or his stake in the Nike brand.\n\n\n\nNike’s Tech Cross-Body Bag\n\nDrake has teamed up with Nike since 2014 on their Air Jordan’s line and gave them a shoutout on Travis Scott’s August 2018 song “SICKO MODE” where he mentioned Nike’s swoosh logo:\n\nChecks over stripes (Yeah)\nThat’s what I like (Yeah), that’s what we like (Yeah)", "annotation_id": "19430658", "url": "https://genius.com/19430658", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Black leather glove, no sequins", "vault_lines": ["Black leather glove, no sequins", "Black leather gloves, no sequins, yeah"], "meaning": "A “sequin” is a small, disk-shaped, plastic bead that’s sewn onto clothes. It comes in a variety of colors. Drake implies here that he rocks leather gloves instead of sequin gloves.\n\nThis is an interesting line as Michael Jackson wore sequined gloves throughout his career, one being his iconic white sequined glove that made its television debut in 1983 during Jackson’s performance of “Billie Jean” at Motown’s 25th anniversary TV special. Drake later compares himself to Jackson in the song’s post-chorus.", "annotation_id": "19430373", "url": "https://genius.com/19430373", "state": "accepted", "votes": 123, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Don’t you wanna dance with me? No? I could dance like Michael Jackson I could give you thug passion It’s a Thriller in the trap where we from Baby, don’t you wanna dance with me? No? I could dance like Michael Jackson I could give you satisfaction And you know we out here every day with it I’ma show you how to get it", "vault_lines": ["Don't you wanna dance with me? No?", "I could dance like Michael Jackson", "I could give you thug passion", "It's a Thriller in the trap where we from", "Baby, don't you wanna dance with me? No?", "I could give you satisfaction", "And you know we out here every day with it", "I'ma show you how", "I'ma show you how to get it"], "meaning": "Michael Jackson was a singer known for his prominent dance moves. Among his famous dancing catalog is his world-renowned, signature dance move, the moonwalk.\n\n“Thriller” is the popular funky 1984 single from Jackson’s album of the same name. Its iconic music video has made a lasting impact on popular culture due to Jackson’s “zombie dance” and his famous red jacket. It’s also ranked as the “greatest music video of all-time” by VH1.\n\n\n\nNotably, in the episode titled “Girls Just Wanna Have Fun” on the Canadian television show, Degrassi: The Next Generation, Drake’s character Jimmy Brooks dresses up as Jackson and dances like him.\n\n“Thug Passion” is a famous drink created by 2Pac that he rapped about on his February 1996 song of the same name. Drake previously name-dropped Michael Jackson and Pac on his June 2016 song “4PM in Calabasas” and collaborated with Jackson’s estate on his June 2018 Scorpion cut “Don’t Matter to Me.”", "annotation_id": "19429910", "url": "https://genius.com/19429910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "line": "Buckles on the jacket, it’s Alyx shit", "vault_lines": ["Buckles on the jacket, it's Alyx shit"], "meaning": "1017 ALYX 9SM is a fashion brand based in New York City. It was founded by Californian Matthew Williams in 2015. The name was initially just ALYX until it was changed to its current name in May 2018.\n\nThe brand is known for its signature rollercoaster buckles on its products, which ranges from belts to shoes to jackets. On December 21, 2019, Drake posted a picture of him wearing a Nike Hat & Alyx Collab Black Hoodie and was seen wearing a 6 Moncler 1017 ALYX 9SM jacket in the music video for his December 2019 song, “War.”\n\n\n\nConsidering all the Michael Jackson references, “buckles on the jacket” may also refer to the famous buckle jacket MJ wore on his Bad album. Drake also shouts-out this album with his “Who’s bad” ad-lib in the last chorus.", "annotation_id": "19429771", "url": "https://genius.com/19429771", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Toosie Slide", "status": "done", "targets": 13, "clicked": 13, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-toosie-slide-lyrics", "genius_title": "Toosie Slide"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yamaha Mama", "line": "And I’ma ride for you too, as soon as it turn green", "vault_lines": ["And I'ma ride for you too, as soon as it turn green"], "meaning": "This plays off the Red Light line previous and also possibly of the name of the song (“Yamaha Mama”) But Drake’s trying to impress this girl and show her he’s different when he gets the chance to go.", "annotation_id": "800665", "url": "https://genius.com/800665", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yamaha Mama", "line": "Ayy, ayy, she my My Yamaha mama Ayy, ayy, be my My Yamaha mama Ayy, ayy, she my My Yamaha mama Ayy, ayy, be my", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A “Yamaha Mama”, in this context, can be defined as a “ride-or-die chick” – a girl that will defend her other half through thick or thin and be there for them to the very end.\n\n\n\nNotable (and possibly infamous) ride-or-die chicks:\n\n\n\nKarrueche Tran (on/off partner of Chris Brown)\n\nShante Broadus (partner of Snoop Dogg)\n\nCoco Austin (partner of Ice-T)", "annotation_id": "800241", "url": "https://genius.com/800241", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yamaha Mama", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 2, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-yamaha-mama-lyrics", "genius_title": "Yamaha Mama"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "After Dark", "line": "You like, “Fuck these niggas,” rather keep it to yourself", "vault_lines": ["You like, \"Fuck these niggas,\" rather keep it to yourself"], "meaning": "Possibly a reference to The-Dream’s “I Luv Your Girl”:\n\n…but she like, (Fuck! That nigga!)\n\nBoth are sung in falsetto and have a slight pause after “fuck.”", "annotation_id": "15280247", "url": "https://genius.com/15280247", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "After Dark", "line": "[Intro: Static Major] Haha, haha Yeah, ayy (After dark)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This vocal cut comes courtesy from the late R&B musician, Static Major, whom is famous for his production and songwriting credits with major hip-hop artists such as Aaliyah, Lil Wayne, Timbaland and Ginuwine.  In years past, Drake has paid homage to Static Major through numerous credits in his music including interpolations on “Unforgettable”, and “Legend” and chopped vocals and piano notes on “Look What You’ve Done.”", "annotation_id": "15073317", "url": "https://genius.com/15073317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "After Dark", "line": "93.7, WBLK, tied your knot at the Quiet Storm", "vault_lines": ["93.7, WBLK, tied your knot at the Quiet Storm"], "meaning": "After Scorpion’s release, 93.7 WBLK responded to Drake’s inclusion of one of their “Quiet Storm” air-checks on their official website,\n\nEverytime […] we do an event in Toronto, the people there treat us like royalty. Well Drake, we love you, bro, and appreciate the mention (which is an understatement) on your new album.\"\n\nThe mention led to their professional phone line, 716-644-9393, to be called hundreds upon thousands of times within the first few hours alone. In the same public statement WBLK claims,\n\n“[They] have been ringing nonstop, off the hook!!! We’ve gotten calls from people just trying the number out, thinking it was just a made-up station and phone number. […] We are now the station-of-choice based on calls we’re getting from Australia, Germany, Italy, etc, and of course the numerous calls from just about every State in America.”\n\nThe Buffalo-based station also sent a personal statement to Drake to which the Toronto-raised musician replied on his Instagram Story saying, “This station raised us.”", "annotation_id": "14878917", "url": "https://genius.com/14878917", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "After Dark", "line": "Late night like Left Eye, I’m creepin’ Assuming the worst ’cause I haven’t heard from you all weekend", "vault_lines": ["Late night like Left Eye, I'm creepin'", "Assuming the worst 'cause I haven't heard from you all weekend"], "meaning": "“Creep” was a song by girl group TLC, of which Lisa “Left Eye” Lopes was a member before her untimely death.\n\nDrake is keeping an eye on his love interest as he fears she might be drifting from him.", "annotation_id": "14876551", "url": "https://genius.com/14876551", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
