{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "line": "Why are we rushing and forcing it?—this isn’t making sense", "vault_lines": ["Why are we rushing and forcing it?—this isn't making sense"], "meaning": "Drake asks the girl why are they rushing their relationship and not staying as friends for a longer period of time.", "annotation_id": "38238475", "url": "https://genius.com/38238475", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "line": "Me and you are playing house Start thinking that I’m losing touch Got a tight grip on you now Maybe I should loosen up", "vault_lines": ["Me and you are playing house", "Start thinking that I'm losing touch", "Got a tight grip on you now", "Maybe I should loosen up"], "meaning": "Drake being drake has always been open regarding the amount of pressure the public inflicts on him. He also expresses paranoia as an artist on a day to day basis at times. All of this however can interfere with his personal relationships. He could feel as if he isn’t all there; quite possibly feeling disassociated from the intimate moments shared with a significant other.", "annotation_id": "29721335", "url": "https://genius.com/29721335", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "line": "Why are we wasting our relationship on a relationship?", "vault_lines": ["Why are we wasting our relationship on a relationship?", "Why are we focused on things"], "meaning": "Drake has concerns that being in a ‘couple’ relationship is jeopardizing their relationship as friends / casual dating friends", "annotation_id": "26458388", "url": "https://genius.com/26458388", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "line": "You go up higher than me some days You know what I think we could be some day", "vault_lines": ["You go up higher than me some days", "You know what I think we could be some day"], "meaning": "There are a rare few times when Drake’s girl is bigger than him in terms of media. This implies that she lives a similar, high-class lifestyle to his. This could also suggest she gets conceited, even more so than Drake and his semi-large ego, Drake Jr.\n\n\n\nHe is most likely referring to the former, which goes to show just how great a power-couple they could be. This could add to the conspiracy that the mystery girl this song was made for is Rihanna…", "annotation_id": "5213120", "url": "https://genius.com/5213120", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "line": "I empower girls that don’t deserve it I don’t mean to do that shit on purpose", "vault_lines": ["I empower girls that don't deserve it", "I don't mean to do that shit on purpose"], "meaning": "I saw potential in you from the go, you know that I did\nI don’t know if you know, but I know who you are\nYou could be big as Madonna\nJust get in the car and then let’s touch the road\n– Drake on “Madonna”\n\nAs Drake has previously mentioned, he has the ability to turn one of his girls into a superstar solely because of all the publicity he gets.\n\nDrake doesn’t necessarily get to know all of his girls for their true selves, so if he slips up and gets caught by the media hanging out with a girl, he may unintentionally create a monster. Maybe some of the girls end up abusing the power, or maybe some of them just aren’t ready for it. Publicity is a tough thing to handle and it can even go so far as to destroy a person.", "annotation_id": "5212793", "url": "https://genius.com/5212793", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "line": "Yeah, that’s about it This shit sound like what being rich feel like, fo real", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, that's about it", "This shit sound like what being rich feel like, fo real"], "meaning": "Boi-1da & Drake  are known for making smash hits that consequently produce a lot of income. As soon as the beat kicks in, Drizzy knows the track is destined for great things.\n\nThe “dub” effect gives the beat a hectic-fast feel of time passing by. This may symbolise the rapid growth of Drake’s career and bank account.", "annotation_id": "5212711", "url": "https://genius.com/5212711", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "line": "Why are we focused on things That don’t even mean nothing, girl? Why can we not find the time And sit down and discuss it, girl? Let me at least tell you my side of things, man Aw, man, aw, man, aw, man", "vault_lines": ["Why are we focused on things", "That don't even mean nothing, girl?", "Why can we not find the time", "And sit down and discuss it, girl?", "Let me at least tell you my side of things, man", "Aw, man, aw, man, aw, man", "Me and you are playing house", "Let me at least show you my side of things, man"], "meaning": "Drake and his girl are over the “honeymoon phase.” They’re arguing way more than they used to and don’t listen to each other instead of acting like they’re in a mature, adult relationship.\n\nThe “aw man’s” at the end of the verse may symbolize the guilt over hurting her sinking in. This connects how he takes advantage of the distance between the two. Is this turning out to be a make up song?", "annotation_id": "5212673", "url": "https://genius.com/5212673", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "line": "Why you never come to where I stay? Always hiding out in your New York condo", "vault_lines": ["Always hiding out in your New York condo", "And you don't ever come to where I stay at", "You just rather stay up in your New York condo", "You don't ever come to where I stay at", "Why you never come to where I stay?"], "meaning": "Drake questions his love interest’s decision not to visit him at the places in which he stays – either his safe house in Calabasas, or his condo in Toronto – choosing instead to hide out in her condo in New York.\n\n\n\nIt is rumored that Drake is referring to Rihanna.\n\nOn the first single off PND’s upcoming album Drake talks again on the status of his relationship with the girl.", "annotation_id": "5212647", "url": "https://genius.com/5212647", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "line": "Too much, too much, too much Too much, too much, too much", "vault_lines": ["Too much going on", "Too much, too much, too much"], "meaning": "A reference to Drake’s song “Too Much” off of his third album, Nothing Was the Same. Both detail the hectic rush of living a fast-paced life.", "annotation_id": "5212635", "url": "https://genius.com/5212635", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "My Side", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 9, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-my-side-lyrics", "genius_title": "My Side"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Not You Too", "line": "Trust Trust who? Watchin’ my back even when I’m in the booth Oh, trust who? Doin’ my thing, but I’m down to come clean, not like you I wanna make you feel the weight’s off your shoulders too I’ll take it Just tell me it can’t be that crazy And even if it is that crazy, baby I’ll take it I’ll mold you, I know you’re not an angel Not you Oh, just save it Don’t waste your time ‘Cause I’m wavy and I’m sayin’ things That I don’t mean, but I do mean And I don’t want you to miss me ’Cause there’s no way that you’re sorry And you’re crying and you’re sayin’ things That I do believe, but I don’t even Want you to get in my mind I’m supposed to be in your mind (Oh woah) For the first time in a long time (For the first time in a long) For the first time in the longest time", "vault_lines": ["First time in a long time", "Just tell me it can't be that crazy", "And even if it is that crazy, baby", "I'll mold you, I know you're not an angel", "Oh, just save it", "Don't waste your time", "'Cause I'm wavy and I'm sayin' things", "That I don't mean, but I do mean", "And I don't want you to miss me", "'Cause there's no way that you're sorry", "And you're crying and you're sayin' things", "That I do believe, but I don't even", "Want you to get in my mind", "I'm supposed to be in your mind (Oh woah)", "For the first time in a long time", "For the first time in the longest time", "Trust me and I can set you free", "When I scooped you in the Maybach", "When you know that we was meant to be", "Watchin' my back even when I'm in the booth", "Doin' my thing, but I'm down to come clean, not like you", "I wanna make you feel the weight's off your shoulders too"], "meaning": "These lyrics navigate the emotional place of a relationship caught between vulnerability and self protection, a dynamic that’s as intimate as it is unstable. At its core, “Not You Too” explores what happens when two people fail to fully trust each other, even though there’s a deep longing for connection. Drake is torn, not just between another person and himself, but between what he feels and what he’s willing to admit.\n                                                                           This song isn’t just about another person. It’s also a reflection of Drake’s own inner conflict, how he deals with love, betrayal, confusion, vulnerability, and the inability to let go. He both wants and rejects closeness. He’s honest and dishonest. He’s willing to forgive but unwilling to be hurt again. The entire relationship seems like a mirror of his own contradictions.\n\nAnd in many ways, this speaks to real world modern relationships, where trust is rare, communication is broken, and people wrestle between opening up and protecting themselves. It’s a song that doesn’t offer resolution, but instead gives voice to the messy, unresolved spaces between love and loss, connection and withdrawal.", "annotation_id": "35593155", "url": "https://genius.com/35593155", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Not You Too", "line": "Houston, we got a problem", "vault_lines": ["Houston, we got a problem"], "meaning": "This line is a direct nod to the famous phrase from NASA’s Apollo 13 mission in April 1970. After an oxygen tank explosion jeopardized the mission, astronaut Jack Swigert radioed, “Okay, Houston, we’ve had a problem here,” followed by Commander Jim Lovell saying, “Ah, Houston, we’ve had a problem.” The line was later popularized — in slightly altered form — as “Houston, we have a problem” in the 1995 film Apollo 13, becoming a cultural shorthand for serious, unexpected trouble.\n\nDrake reworks that iconic phrase to reflect the emotional breakdown in a relationship. Just like the original quote signaled a critical moment during a life-threatening mission, here Drake uses it to imply that something has gone wrong — subtly but deeply — between him and someone close. The tone is understated but heavy with meaning, fitting the song’s melancholic vibe.\n\nThere’s also a second layer: “Houston” isn’t just a stand-in for crisis — it’s a city that means a lot to Drake. He’s frequently shown love to Houston throughout his career, citing it as a major influence on his sound and culture. The city has been the backdrop for key relationships and come-ups in his journey. So saying “Houston, we got a problem” might also suggest conflict tied to that part of his life or people associated with it.\n\nAs always, Drake blends cultural references and emotional weight with layered meaning — turning a historic phrase into something intimate and personal.", "annotation_id": "35526344", "url": "https://genius.com/35526344", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Not You Too", "line": "Why you keep that little shit from me? Left me down on bended knee", "vault_lines": ["Why you keep that little shit from me?", "Left me down on bended knee", "Why you do that little shit to me, shawty?"], "meaning": "In a classic double entendre, Drake says that he was down on his knees for this girl- whether it was planning or dreaming about asking her to marry him, or whether he was down bad for her, she left him on the floor, waiting.", "annotation_id": "32975860", "url": "https://genius.com/32975860", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Not You Too", "line": "When I scooped you in the Maybach The only time a nigga ever made you take a backseat", "vault_lines": ["First time in a long time", "When I scooped you in the Maybach", "The only time a nigga ever made you take a backseat"], "meaning": "Drakes girl always rides in the front seat with him, regardless of who else is in the car with them.\n\nThis line has a hidden meaning, at first glance it may seem Drake makes his girl sit in the back seat of the Maybach as it’s typically the most luxurious seat of the car, similarly to Rolls Royce, the Maybach is made for the passenger, not the driver.\n\nHowever, the main message behind this line is the double meaning behind ‘back’ and ‘Bach’. In the Maybach, she’s in the ‘Bach’ seat, meaning she’s not specifically in the backseat, but any seat in the Maybach she chooses.\n\n\n\nIn the ‘Laugh now cry later’ music video, Drake can be seen dancing around a Mercedes Maybach unreleased concept car", "annotation_id": "23432038", "url": "https://genius.com/23432038", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Not You Too", "line": "You the real MVP, my love", "vault_lines": ["You the real MVP, my love"], "meaning": "In 2014, NBA Forward Kevin Durant was named the NBA’s Most Valuable Player. In his acceptance speech, Durant referred to his mother as being the real MVP for her contributions in raising him to be the man he became. Drake and Durant are good friends and Drake has a tattoo which pays homage to Durant. The tattoo can be seen here.", "annotation_id": "19720619", "url": "https://genius.com/19720619", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Not You Too", "line": "Ride dirty like Pimp C", "vault_lines": ["Ride dirty like Pimp C"], "meaning": "Pimp C was a rapper and producer from Texas who alongside Bun B, was a founding member of the iconic rap group UGK. Riding Dirty was UGK’s third studio album, released in 1996.\n\nIn 2007 Pimp C died of a suspected overdose from codeine-based cough syrup. Drake included a posthumous feature from Pimp C on the Views track, “Faithful”.", "annotation_id": "19716683", "url": "https://genius.com/19716683", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
