{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 8 Tracklist", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-ovo-sound-radio-episode-8-tracklist-lyrics", "genius_title": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 8 Tracklist"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "I put some ice on her hand I let her take an advance Shit wasn’t goin’ as planned I put that shit in the van She gotta move with her friend Heard she went back to her man Give a fuck, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Shit wasn't goin' as planned", "I put that shit in the van", "She gotta move with her friend", "Heard she went back to her man", "I put that top left", "Could give a fuck, ayy, yeah, yeah", "I put some ice on her hand", "I let her take an advance"], "meaning": "Drake flexes his wealthy by recounting a failed relationship, comparing it to a common music industry practice.\n\nUpon signing an artist, record labels often give them an advance—a sum of money which must be recouped at a later date subject to the agreement (often through record sales and/or royalties).\n\nIn the early stages of a relationship, Drake, who is known for having many relationships, gave his partner money to treat herself (presumably designer products and/or cosmetic surgeries) much like how a label would give an artist an advance.\n\nOnce signs of incompatibility became apparent , Drake ended the relationship. Unlike a record label, Drake didn’t seek to recoup his losses. Rather, given his status as one of hip-hop’s most successful stars, he allowed her to keep the gifts, rather than reclaiming them as many less wealthy men would.", "annotation_id": "28917468", "url": "https://genius.com/28917468", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Niggas ain’t gotta respect Niggas just gotta accept I put that top left", "vault_lines": ["Niggas ain't gotta respect", "Niggas just gotta accept", "I put that top left"], "meaning": "“Top left” is Toronto slang for telling the truth. On Dark Lane Demo Tapes‘ eleventh track “Losses,” Drake proclaims his lyrics are “cold facts”:\n\nI’m not tryna make no song, these are cold facts", "annotation_id": "20040742", "url": "https://genius.com/20040742", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Police in my city, man, I keep they pockets greased up (Okay)", "vault_lines": ["Police in my city, man, I keep they pockets greased up (Okay)"], "meaning": "“I keep they pockets greased up” means Drake has dirty cops working for him under his payroll. While he doesn’t mind cops working for him, Drake wouldn’t work with them—on September 2013’s “Worst Behavior,” he rapped about not telling the police anything whenever they’d asked him questions:\n\nBut your shit is like the police askin' us questions\nNigga, we don’t know shit\n\nA similar sentiment followed on his February 2015 song, “No Tellin’”:\n\nYeah, police comin' ‘round lookin’ for some help\nOn a case they gotta solve, we never help ‘em", "annotation_id": "20040658", "url": "https://genius.com/20040658", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Used to be an antisocial nigga, now I’m makin’ friends", "vault_lines": ["Used to be an antisocial nigga, now I’m makin' friends"], "meaning": "When someone is antisocial, they’re not sociable or unable to associate themselves with other people.\n\nEarlier in his career, Drake often rapped about not making friends and avoiding fake friends—for example, on his February 2013 song “Started From The Bottom,” he raps:\n\nNo new niggas, nigga, we don’t feel that\nFuck a fake friend, where your real friends at?\nWe don’t like to do too much explainin'\nStory stayed the same, I never changed it", "annotation_id": "19754003", "url": "https://genius.com/19754003", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Diamonds all in my teeth, I called up the dentist, yeah (Okay)", "vault_lines": ["Diamonds all in my teeth, I called up the dentist, yeah (Okay)"], "meaning": "Playboi Carti has a custom 14 karat white gold top and bottom grill set, which was made by custom jewelers, IF & Co. He mentioned his grills before on his May 2018 song, “Right Now”:\n\nAll these diamonds in my teeth, I had to smile (Cash, cash)", "annotation_id": "19716982", "url": "https://genius.com/19716982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Every time I’m home, you know I whip the Bentley (Yeah, yeah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Every time I'm home, you know I whip the Bentley (Yeah, yeah, yeah)"], "meaning": "Bentley is a luxury British car manufacturer that’s known for its high-quality SUVs. Carti rapped about a Bentley before on his March 2017 song, ​“wokeuplikethis*”:\n\nFuck me, Bentley coupe I’m ridin' with a thottie, fuck\n\nHe also rapped about one on his April 2017 song, “Location”:\n\nYou cannot hop in my car (Yeah, yeah)\nBentley coupe ridin' with stars (Yeah, yeah)", "annotation_id": "19716693", "url": "https://genius.com/19716693", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Got a Goyard bag, I threw them racks in it (My racks, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Got a Goyard bag, I threw them racks in it (My racks, yeah)"], "meaning": "Goyard is an expensive French brand that sells luxury travel trunks and leather goods, such as bags and briefcases. Carti’s a big fan of the brand—he rapped about it on his April 2017 song “Had 2”\n\nGoyard ‘round my waist and it hold the Glock, ooh\n\nHe also rapped about it on his unreleased cut “Goku”:\n\nGoyard, I done did it", "annotation_id": "19716677", "url": "https://genius.com/19716677", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Now every day I rock Christian Dior (Okay, Dior, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Now every day I rock Christian Dior (Okay, Dior, yeah)"], "meaning": "Christian Dior is a French luxury goods company with distinct designs. Carti has previously shouted out the luxury brand before, like on his leaked track “Butterfly Doors”, where he raps:\n\nRockin' that Christian Dior\nI had to mop up the floors (What?)", "annotation_id": "19716641", "url": "https://genius.com/19716641", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Racks all in my jeans and they Raf Simons (Racks in my jeans, okay)", "vault_lines": ["Racks all in my jeans and they Raf Simons (Racks in my jeans, okay)"], "meaning": "Raf Simons is a luxury menswear label that was founded in 1995 by Belgian fashion designer Raf Simons. Notably, Carti featured on the May 2017 A$AP Mob posse-cut “RAF,” a song dedicated to the label.\n\n\n\nCarti pictured with Gucci Mane wearing Raf Simons in 2017", "annotation_id": "19716561", "url": "https://genius.com/19716561", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "That Lam’ truck got numbers on the board (Okay, get it)", "vault_lines": ["That Lam' truck got numbers on the board (Okay, get it)"], "meaning": "Carti is referring to the Lamborghini Urus, a large model vehicle offered from the Italian car manufacturer. At high speed, the car’s odometer shows high numbers in the front.\n\nThis is the first time Carti has mentioned the Italian brand since he was pulled over in the vehicle for an expired tag, which led to his arrest.\n\nInterestingly, Carti has openly mocked Lamb trucks, as he posted this photo in 2018, showing off the Lamborghini coupe that girlfriend Iggy Azalea bought him for Christmas.", "annotation_id": "19716547", "url": "https://genius.com/19716547", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Me and Capo tryna leave our mark and peace the East up Ain’t nobody makin’ too much money off the beef stuff (Okay)", "vault_lines": ["Me and Capo tryna leave our mark and peace the East up", "Ain't nobody makin' too much money off the beef stuff (Okay)"], "meaning": "Drake has given a shout-out to “Capo” several times, most notably on “Look Alive”:\n\nThey won’t be expectin' shit when Capo go to slide (Hah)\n\nCapo could refer to a close friend and associate of Halal Gang: a Toronto hip-hop collective comprised of rappers Puffy L’z,  Mo-G, SAFE, and the now-deceased Smoke Dawg. Drake has some history with the group, cosigning them in 2015 by posting the track “Still” – famous for popularizing the “Ginobili dance”. While Drake was later involved in a minor beef with the group, the line about patching up beef may hint at it being squashed.\n\nIt is also possible that Capo actually refers to Chubbs, Drakes head of security, right handman, and “fixer”. Drake has directly referred to him as Capo in the past. In Mafia terminology, a capo is often the head of a specialized crew, who gets his orders directly from the family’s (under)boss(es).", "annotation_id": "19716120", "url": "https://genius.com/19716120", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Now every day I gotta pour me a four (Lean, lean, lean, lean) Now every day I gotta pour me a four (Lean, lean, lean, lean, okay)", "vault_lines": ["Now every day I gotta pour me a four (Lean, lean, lean, lean)"], "meaning": "Playboi Carti often raps about the street drug lean, to the extent he has a song named after it, “Lean 4 Real” where throughout the chorus he raps:\n\nI’m on the lean for real (What? Yeah, yeah)\"\n\nOn his song “Pull Up” he raps even more blatantly:\n\nLean, ooh, lean, lean, lean, lean, lean, lean, uh\n\nAdditionally, in April 2020, Carti was arrested and police found codeine cough syrup in his car, a primary ingredient in lean.", "annotation_id": "19715921", "url": "https://genius.com/19715921", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "I love my crodie to death (Codeine)", "vault_lines": ["I love my crodie to death (Codeine)"], "meaning": "“Crodie” is Crip slang for ‘brother’ as it alters the word “Brodie,” replacing the B for a C. Drake first used the term on his December 2019 UK-drill-inspired track, “War,” where he shouted out Toronto artist, Pressa:\n\nWassa, wassa, just like the crodie Pressa, I don’t do no passa\n\nHe later used it again on Toronto artist Smiley_61st’s March 2020 track “Organization,” where he shouted out his close friend and OVO affiliate, Baka Not Nice:\n\nBaka, my crodie, his songs are amazing\nBut I knew that nigga when he wasn’t makin’ ’em, yeah\n\nCodeine is an addictive opioid pain medication, which is often used to make the drug, lean—a mixture of codeine and other ingredients, like soda and candy.", "annotation_id": "19502020", "url": "https://genius.com/19502020", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "When I shoot my shot it’s the Kawhi way, it’s goin’ in", "vault_lines": ["When I shoot my shot it's the Kawhi way, it's goin' in"], "meaning": "Kawhi Leonard is a professional NBA basketball player and former player of the Toronto Raptors, Drake’s hometown team.\n\nLeonard’s 2019 NBA playoffs run is often described as one of the greatest postseason performances in NBA history due to how efficient his field-goal scoring was. Amongst his run was his tie-breaking Game 7 buzzer-beater win in the Eastern Semifinals against the Philadelphia 76ers where Kawhi shot the ball at the last second and made it with the ball bouncing on the rim four times.\n\n\n\nAfter beating the Milwaukee Bucks in six games in the next round, he led the Raptors to their first championship title in franchise history versus the Golden State Warriors at the 2019 NBA Finals.\n\nDrake and Kawhi are good friends. For example, they took a picture together in December 2018 and Drake allowed Kawhi to stay at his Hidden Hills mansion in 2019 when Kawhi was flying back and forth between Toronto and L.A. for free agency meetings after his contract with the Raptors was finished.", "annotation_id": "19501450", "url": "https://genius.com/19501450", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "I just put a Wagen in the driveway, you know I did", "vault_lines": ["I just put a Wagen in the driveway, you know I did"], "meaning": "The G-Wagon is a sport utility vehicle of the German automobile brand Mercedes-Benz and its G-Class of vehicles. All of the vehicles are very expensive, with 2020 models of the car running over $100,000.\n\nDrake has an even nicer rendition of the luxury car as he purchased a Mercedes-Maybach G 650 Landaulet in February 2019, a model of which only ninety-nine vehicles were produced. When announcing the purchase for Drake, his associate Tony Bet said the price tag was “$$$$$$$.”\n\nDrake previously mentioned the car model in his featured verse on PARTYNEXTDOOR’s June 2017 track “Freak In You (Remix)”:\n\nI got a triple king bed and I don’t mind sharing\nI got a big G-Wagon, I don’t mind sharing", "annotation_id": "19501444", "url": "https://genius.com/19501444", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "I just got a mansion out in Turks and it’s a beachfront (Okay)", "vault_lines": ["I just got a mansion out in Turks and it’s a beachfront (Okay)"], "meaning": "“Turks” is short for Turks and Caicos, a group of islands in the Atlantic Ocean off the coast of the United States.\n\nDrake has been seen out in the islands many times. His private jet, known as the Air Drake, was seen at a private airport on the island and Drake posted pictures of himself at a villa on the beach on the islands in December of 2018.", "annotation_id": "19501332", "url": "https://genius.com/19501332", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Hype Williams, Lil’ X If you don’t say it direct", "vault_lines": ["Hype Williams, Lil' X", "If you don’t say it direct"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to two music video directors with prolific careers in the rap industry, Hype Williams of Queens, New York, and Director X from Brampton, Canada – just outside of Drake’s hometown of Toronto, Canada.\n\nWilliams is known for his innovative and eye-catching videos, including popular tracks like JAY-Z’s October 2009 “Empire State of Mind” and Beyoncé’s December 2013 smash “Drunk in Love,” although he is most recognized amongst the new school of hip-hop for his critically acclaimed artistic video for Travis Scott’s “90210” in September 2015 and Kanye West’s notorious seizure-inducing “All of the Lights” in November 2010.\n\nAlthough Drake has never had a video directed by Hype, his relationship with Director X is an extensive one. The two have collaborated eight times, beginning with Drake’s “HYFR” (April 2012), 2 Chainz’s “No Lie” (May 2012), “Started from the Bottom” (February 2013), “Worst Behavior” (September 2013), “Hotline Bling” (July 2015), Rihanna’s “Work” (January 2016), “God’s Plan” (January 2018), and Future’s “Life Is Good” (January 2020). Future even shouted out X in the video for “Life Is Good,” proclaiming:\n\nBigger than Lil X? […] You see how his videos be poppin'\n\n\n\nX and Drizzy on set for “Hotline Bling”", "annotation_id": "19501251", "url": "https://genius.com/19501251", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "But I beef a rapper ’cause I’m never with the sweet stuff", "vault_lines": ["But I beef a rapper 'cause I'm never with the sweet stuff"], "meaning": "Drake has shown many times before that he is not hesitant to call somebody out even if they are a famous person or someone he has worked with before. The best example of this was shown in his Rap Radar interview released on December 25, 2019. When he was asked about his issues with recording artist Kanye West, he commented:\n\nYeah, if he turns up on me again I’ll turn up on him again… I’m not just gonna sit by while somebody’s, you know, just talking loose about me or whatever I have going on.\n\nSince 2012, he has also had beef with Chris Brown, Common, Tyga, Meek Mill, and Pusha T. However, since 2018 and 2019, respectively, he was on friendly terms with Meek Mill and Chris Brown.", "annotation_id": "19501043", "url": "https://genius.com/19501043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "Me and lil’ Sicko sittin’ sideways, breakin’ tens", "vault_lines": ["Me and lil' Sicko sittin' sideways, breakin’ tens"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out fashion designer Ian Connor, the creator of clothing brand Born from Pain / Sickö. “Born from Pain 1993” is Ian’s motto, the namesake of this song.\n\nDrake was first seen with Ian Connor in the early 2010s and later in November 2018.\n\n\n\nDrake & Ian\n\n\n“Sicko” or “6icko”—where the number 6 is used as a code for Drake’s hometown, Toronto—is also a term used by Drake to indicate his friends. It was popularized by basketball star Kevin Durant in 2015 when he posted it in a tweet in reaction to Drake’s June 2015 diss track “Charged Up.” Drake also used the term himself on his January 2016 song “Summer Sixteen” and on Travis Scott’s August 2018 hit “SICKO MODE.”", "annotation_id": "19500191", "url": "https://genius.com/19500191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Pain 1993", "line": "(Yo, Pi’erre, yo, Pi’erre)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is one of the many tags of producer and rapper Pi'erre Bourne. As a producer, he is well-known for working with Playboi Carti on iconic tracks like 2017’s “Magnolia” but has also worked with other popular rappers such as Kanye West and Travis Scott.\n\nThis tag comes from a video of Pi'erre’s fans recorded during Carti’s Summer 2017 US tour. It was first used on Pi'erre’s 2018 single “Planet Namek.”", "annotation_id": "18843791", "url": "https://genius.com/18843791", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
