{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tim Westwood Freestyle", "line": "I’m too fly, keep it hip-hop like Afu Ra", "vault_lines": ["I'm too fly, keep it hip-hop like Afu Ra"], "meaning": "Aaron Phillip, also known as Afu-Ra, is an American rapper from New York City.\n\nAfu-Ra made a song called “Hip Hop” and Drake is referring to that song.", "annotation_id": "22533464", "url": "https://genius.com/22533464", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tim Westwood Freestyle", "line": "I’m what Lebron was to St. Vincent-St. Mary’s", "vault_lines": ["I'm what Lebron was to St. Vincent-St. Mary's"], "meaning": "Legendary basketball player LeBron James who is recognized as one of the greatest players in the NBA, used to study at the St. Vincent-St. Mary High School which is located in Ohio.\n\nIn 2017, LeBron donated a million dollars to his old school as a thank you to the school for his childhood.", "annotation_id": "22533457", "url": "https://genius.com/22533457", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tim Westwood Freestyle", "line": "I could kick a punt and turn that shit into a field goal", "vault_lines": ["I could kick a punt and turn that shit into a field goal"], "meaning": "In football a punt is characterized by dropping the ball and kicking it before it hits the ground. It is used to switch field position and consequently is worth 0 points.\n\n\n\nDrizzy is so revolutionary at what he does that his punts are more like field goals. In other words he is worth more (field goals are worth 3 points) than his counterparts", "annotation_id": "1602520", "url": "https://genius.com/1602520", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tim Westwood Freestyle", "line": "I got a lot of things, I mention those barely", "vault_lines": ["I got a lot of things, I mention those barely"], "meaning": "Drake is saying he has alot of things he doesnt brag about .", "annotation_id": "108616", "url": "https://genius.com/108616", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tim Westwood Freestyle", "line": "I got a couple cars, I never get to lose", "vault_lines": ["I got a couple cars, I never get to lose"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t lose his cars cause he doesn’t have to drive, his chauffeur does. Or it can mean he bets on his cars. But he never loses the bets so he doesn’t lose his cars.", "annotation_id": "95774", "url": "https://genius.com/95774", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tim Westwood Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 5, "clicked": 5, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-tim-westwood-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Tim Westwood Freestyle"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "That’s not love you’re in, it’s more like compromisin’", "vault_lines": ["That's not love you're in, it's more like compromisin'"], "meaning": "Drake is pointing out that the relationship isn’t based on true love, but rather on compromises. He’s suggesting that the connection might be more about settling or adjusting to each other’s needs and expectations, rather than a pure, passionate love. It implies that both people might be compromising on things, possibly at the expense of their own desires or happiness, rather than experiencing a deep, genuine connection.", "annotation_id": "35070508", "url": "https://genius.com/35070508", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Lean into me, lean into me Lean in, lean into me", "vault_lines": ["Lean into me, lean into me", "Yeah, lean in, lean into me", "Lean in, lean into me"], "meaning": "Drake is asking the other person to get closer, both physically and emotionally. By repeating it, he emphasizes a desire for the person to trust him, open up, and fully engage with him in the moment. It’s about creating intimacy and closeness, inviting them to rely on him or be vulnerable together.", "annotation_id": "35070506", "url": "https://genius.com/35070506", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "[Break: Drake] Yeah, yeah Yeah Grr, grr Yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The repeated “Yeah, yeah / Yeah / Grr, grr / Yeah” are ad-libs Drake uses to add energy and emphasis to the track. The “grr, grr” sound is a signature vocal expression in his music, adding intensity and a sense of confidence or aggression. It’s meant to amplify the mood and vibe of the song, creating a more powerful, hyped-up feel.", "annotation_id": "35070505", "url": "https://genius.com/35070505", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "‘Cause you been goin’ off on my ass, then I tell your ass where I’m at", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you been goin' off on my ass, then I tell your ass where I'm at"], "meaning": "Drake is describing a situation where the other person has been upset or angry with him (“goin' off on my ass”), and instead of letting it slide, he responds by telling them exactly where he is or what he’s doing.\n\nIt reflects a back-and-forth dynamic where the person is acting out emotionally, and Drake, rather than avoiding confrontation, directly lets them know where he stands or where he physically is. This might indicate a tense relationship where both sides are expressing their feelings, but in a confrontational or unresolved way.", "annotation_id": "35070503", "url": "https://genius.com/35070503", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "And you act like I’m not supposed to mention that", "vault_lines": ["And you act like I'm not supposed to mention that"], "meaning": "Drake is expressing frustration or confusion that the other person is acting as if certain things should be ignored or not talked about.\n\nIt suggests that there’s something significant (likely a past action or truth) that he feels needs to be addressed, but the other person is pretending it’s not important or that it should be left unsaid. Drake seems to be calling out this behavior, implying that pretending certain things don’t exist or shouldn’t be acknowledged is unfair or unrealistic.", "annotation_id": "35070501", "url": "https://genius.com/35070501", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "I introduce you to my friends’ girlfriends, you ain’t interested in makin’ friends", "vault_lines": ["I introduce you to my friends' girlfriends, you ain't interested in makin' friends"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that he tried to bring the person into his social circle, but they weren’t interested in forming connections with others.\n\nIt suggests that the person he’s talking about may have been more focused on the relationship with him than on building other relationships, like with his friends or their partners. It could point to a more possessive or exclusive vibe in the relationship, where the person wasn’t as interested in expanding their social network, only focusing on the bond with Drake.", "annotation_id": "35070498", "url": "https://genius.com/35070498", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "We ain’t even gotta mention that, any nigga try and trouble you", "vault_lines": ["We ain't even gotta mention that, any nigga try and trouble you"], "meaning": "Drake is likely expressing that there’s no need to dwell on certain issues, but he’s making it clear that if anyone tries to cause trouble for the person he’s speaking to, he’ll handle it.\n\nIt shows a protective side, where he’s willing to stand up for them without having to talk about it or bring up past drama. It’s about being there for someone when it counts, without needing to over-explain or relive past situations.", "annotation_id": "35070495", "url": "https://genius.com/35070495", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Yeah, ask me if I coulda treated you better, but no Not at all, not at all", "vault_lines": ["Asked me if I coulda treated you better, but no", "Yeah, ask me if I coulda treated you better, but no", "You say I coulda treated you better or whatever, but", "Not at all, not at all", "I coulda treated you better, that's crazy"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that while someone might ask if he could have treated them better, he quickly rejects that idea, implying he doesn’t feel any regret or think he should’ve done things differently. It’s a mix of acknowledging the question but standing firm in his choices.", "annotation_id": "35070493", "url": "https://genius.com/35070493", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "I got to know a whole other you, but let’s not forget how I discovered you", "vault_lines": ["I know what you say", "I got to know a whole other you, but let's not forget how I discovered you", "And you know how you get"], "meaning": "The line “I got to know a whole other you, but let’s not forget how I discovered you” could be Drake reflecting on the complexities of a relationship. He’s acknowledging that he’s learned more about this person, perhaps discovering a side of them he hadn’t seen before, but he also doesn’t want to forget the initial impression or the way they first came into his life.\n\nIn the context of Drake’s usual themes—relationships, trust, and personal growth—this lyric suggests that while he’s evolved in his understanding of the person, he’s also aware of how things started. It might reflect a sense of caution or recognition that things weren’t always as they seem or that the person’s true nature took time to reveal itself.\n\nThe “discovery” could also hint at a change in the relationship dynamic, where what once felt one way has shifted, but the past still holds some significance.", "annotation_id": "35070491", "url": "https://genius.com/35070491", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Asked me if I coulda treated you better, but no", "vault_lines": ["Asked me if I coulda treated you better, but no", "Yeah, ask me if I coulda treated you better, but no", "You say I coulda treated you better or whatever, but", "I coulda treated you better, that's crazy"], "meaning": "Drake is acknowledging that someone asked him whether he could have treated them better in the past. His response, “but no,” suggests that he doesn’t believe he could have done anything differently or that the relationship, as it was, couldn’t have been improved. It may also imply a sense of finality, as if he has come to terms with how things unfolded and doesn’t regret his actions.", "annotation_id": "35070483", "url": "https://genius.com/35070483", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "That nigga spent his last check on your car and you got it and drove it to me", "vault_lines": ["That nigga spent his last check on your car and you got it and drove it to me"], "meaning": "Drake is describing a situation where someone else (a man) invested heavily in buying a car for the person, possibly out of love or to impress them. However, instead of appreciating that gesture, the person drove the car to Drake, implying that they may have been more interested in him or emotionally connected to him, despite the sacrifices made by the other person. It suggests a sense of betrayal or unreciprocated effort from the person who made the financial sacrifice.", "annotation_id": "35070482", "url": "https://genius.com/35070482", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "I wanna get back to the days that you loved me for nothin’ but what you could see", "vault_lines": ["I wanna get back to the days that you loved me for nothin' but what you could see", "I know what you say"], "meaning": "Drake is expressing a longing for a simpler time in the relationship when love was unconditional and based purely on who he was, rather than external factors or expectations. He misses when the love was pure, without complications or outside influences, and when he was appreciated just for being himself.", "annotation_id": "35070481", "url": "https://genius.com/35070481", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Yeah, lean in, lean into me", "vault_lines": ["Lean into me, lean into me", "Yeah, lean in, lean into me", "Lean in, lean into me"], "meaning": "Drake is encouraging someone to trust him and get closer emotionally or physically. “Lean in” suggests being vulnerable, letting your guard down, and allowing for a deeper connection or intimacy. It’s an invitation to open up and rely on him, whether it’s for support, comfort, or closeness.", "annotation_id": "35070480", "url": "https://genius.com/35070480", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "That’s not love you’re in, it’s more like compromisin’", "vault_lines": ["That's not love you're in, it's more like compromisin'"], "meaning": "Drake is suggesting that the feelings are not genuine love but rather a situation where both parties are simply settling or making concessions, sacrificing their desires or needs, rather than experiencing a deep, fulfilling connection.", "annotation_id": "35070478", "url": "https://genius.com/35070478", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Put a baby in you, you a hot mom", "vault_lines": [", put a baby in you, you a hot mom"], "meaning": "The line “put a baby in you, you a hot mom” cleverly ties back to the preceding references to ‘www’ and ‘dot-com’. The phrase ‘hot moms’ is a common trope found in online searches and adult content, where these terms are often linked to internet fantasies and provocative imagery.\n\nThe other side of this entendre is seen in a literal context. The girl that Drake is talking about would in his opinion be a hot mom if she had a baby/was pregnant.\n\nAdditionally, “on site like dot-com” references his clothing collection, hotmoms.com, including a For All The Dogs Scary Hours hoodie, and an unreleased Iceman hoodie he has been seen wearing on an Adin Ross stream.", "annotation_id": "34296073", "url": "https://genius.com/34296073", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Birkin is croc like the rubber shoe, I swear that I’m not tryna smother you", "vault_lines": ["Birkin is croc like the rubber shoe, I swear that I'm not tryna smother you"], "meaning": "A wordplay comparing a luxurious crocodile skin Hermès Birkin bag to a simple rubber croc shoe, indicating he bought her a handbag but doesn’t intend to be overbearing.", "annotation_id": "29813464", "url": "https://genius.com/29813464", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Pussy, it makin’ me tweak, ended up stayin’ a week", "vault_lines": ["Pussy, it makin' me tweak, ended up stayin' a week"], "meaning": "Tweaking is a term used usually when referring to drug use. Drake says that the pussy is making him crazy and making him tweak, like a drug making him stay up a week. This is a play on words implying drakes addiction to the person he is talking about", "annotation_id": "29788131", "url": "https://genius.com/29788131", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Lean into me, lean into me", "vault_lines": ["Lean into me, lean into me", "Yeah, lean in, lean into me", "Lean in, lean into me"], "meaning": "Drake uses a double entendre with the word lean, meaning both the drug and also meaning literally to lean into something. He is asking someone to “lean in” to him meaning to get closer", "annotation_id": "29788081", "url": "https://genius.com/29788081", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "You put some pain in me, I wanna get back to the major league", "vault_lines": ["You put some pain in me, I wanna get back to the major league"], "meaning": "This line suggests that Drake experienced heartache caused by someone. The desire to “get back to the major league” likely signifies a desire to return to a higher level of emotional well-being or a happier state of being. This could imply when Drake felt happier, more content, or in control of their emotions, contrasting with the pain he currently feels – left over from the relationship.", "annotation_id": "29737968", "url": "https://genius.com/29737968", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "She wanna grab on the throttle, I wanna get back in the driver’s seat", "vault_lines": ["She wanna grab on the throttle, I wanna get back in the driver's seat"], "meaning": "We can assume this specific line is referring to a relationship dynamic rather than quite literally “get back in the driver’s seat.”\n\nThe lyrics convey a relationship dynamic where Drake and his partner are seeking control. His partner wants to take charge (“grab on the throttle”), while Drake wants the control back (“get back in the driver’s seat”). This symbolizes a desire for more influence in the relationship.", "annotation_id": "29737907", "url": "https://genius.com/29737907", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Talkin’ ’bout you wanna cook for a nigga in the kitchen, can you make amends? Least we know you got the cakes on you, girl, you should prolly stick to bakin’ then", "vault_lines": ["Talkin' 'bout you wanna cook for a nigga in the kitchen, can you make amends?", "Least we know you got the cakes on you, girl, you should prolly stick to bakin' then"], "meaning": "In these lines, Drake uses some clever cooking wordplay. During cooking you make a meal, but instead Drake sarcastically asks his girl if she can make amends for her past mistakes via her cooking. Drake is likely implying his girl’s offer to cook is an attempt to make amends.\n\nIn the next line, Drake plays on the homonym “cake”. Drizzy jokes that since his girl already has cake, which is slang for a nice ass, she should not cook but instead stick to baking cakes, the food, as she is already an expert in that field.", "annotation_id": "29729381", "url": "https://genius.com/29729381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "You keep talkin’ ’bout some, “Period,” but where you ’bout to end the sentence at?", "vault_lines": ["You keep talkin' 'bout some, \"Period,\" but where you 'bout to end the sentence at?"], "meaning": "In these line “Period” mean both an end in a conversation and a woman’s menstrual cycle. He makes reference at the emotional ups and downs women face their menstrual cycle. By asking “where you ‘bout to end the sentence at?”, he tries to seek clarity in these emotional moments.", "annotation_id": "29728842", "url": "https://genius.com/29728842", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "She pretty but ghetto, pretty but rough, just like Virginia Beach", "vault_lines": ["She pretty but ghetto, pretty but rough, just like Virginia Beach"], "meaning": "Here, Drake compares a woman to the city of Virginia Beach, Virginia. This place is renowned by tourists for its scenic beauty, yet known by locals as an impoverished and ‘rough’ area disguised by the disproportionately wealthy. This comparison suggests that both possess a blend of soft and hard characteristics, implying the woman to be good-looking but tough.\n\nAlso Drake performed in Virginia Beach in 2014.\n\nVirginia Beach is also where Pusha T is from; Drake and Pusha have long standing beef", "annotation_id": "29728776", "url": "https://genius.com/29728776", "state": "pending", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "I move mountains for you, fuck that social climbin’", "vault_lines": ["I move mountains for you, fuck that social climbin'"], "meaning": "“Social Climbing” is the act of trying to improve your social position by being very friendly to people from a higher social class.\n\nDrake, by using a metaphor of moving mountains so she doesn’t have to climb them, says that the girl doesn’t need to act super-friendly with other people from a higher social class and that he’ll take care of her, thanks to his very high social class.", "annotation_id": "29727487", "url": "https://genius.com/29727487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Lean in, lean in soda, Fanta, fantasizin’", "vault_lines": ["Lean in, lean in soda, Fanta, fantasizin'", "Lean in soda, Fanta, fantasizin'"], "meaning": "Lean is a recreational drug beverage, prepared by mixing prescription strength cough or cold syrup containing codeine or promethazine with a soft drink.\nLean is well known in the rap culture and it is being referenced a lot.\n\nDrake makes a double entendre about leaning into the fantasy (of them together, that hopefully will lead to her leaning into him) and mixing syrup with Fanta, which is a brand of sodas that is very popular to make lean.", "annotation_id": "29727165", "url": "https://genius.com/29727165", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "[Intro: Drake & Frank Ocean]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake samples Frank Ocean’s track “Wise Man,” posted to Ocean’s Tumblr in  2012.\n\nIn the lead-up to For All The Dogs, Drake shared a picture on Instagram of Ocean’s 2022 vinyl reissue of Blonde, causing fans to speculate Ocean’s involvement on the upcoming album.\n\nNotably, Drake had worn a one-of-one, 127.5-carat diamond necklace from Frank’s luxury jewelry company, Homer, and even sang about it on two of his tracks, “Sticky” from his 2022 album Honestly, Nevermind and “Privileged Rappers” from his and 21 Savage’s 2022 album Her Loss:\n\n\nHomer hanging on my neck\nThe bracelet matches the set\n- “Sticky”\n\nThe diamonds, they hit like a rainbow, that’s ‘cause the necklace a Frank (Purr)\n- “Privileged Rappers”", "annotation_id": "29725949", "url": "https://genius.com/29725949", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "You bust down the Jubilee, I swapped it and made you go factory, wait", "vault_lines": ["You bust down the Jubilee, I swapped it and made you go factory, wait"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing Rolex’s “Jubilee” bracelet, which is featured on the brand’s cheaper models, such as the Datejust and GMT, both of which are offered in stainless steel. Many people take these watches to jewellers who drill holes in the watches and install poor-quality diamonds. As opposed to this, Drake encourages worthwhile investment, hence purchasing factory gem-set pieces.\n\n\n\nPictured: A “bust-down” Rolex Datejust 41 on a Jubilee bracelet.\n\nDrake himself is known for having quite the watch collection, as a quick Google search will evidence.\n\nThis line demonstrates the calibre of wealth in which Drake resides, as factory gem-set pieces are incredibly rare and expensive.", "annotation_id": "29725931", "url": "https://genius.com/29725931", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "He gon’ find out that it’s on-site like W-W-W On site like dot-com", "vault_lines": ["He gon' find out that it's on-site like W-W-W", "On site like dot-com"], "meaning": "By engaging the term “on-site” (or else on sight) and www, Drake is referring to how the action will happen when they see each other and also the World Wide Web (abbreviated as www), which refers to all the public websites or pages that users can access on their local computers and other devices through the internet. These pages and documents are interconnected by means of hyperlinks that users click on for information.\n\n.com is short for “commercial.” A site doesn’t necessarily have to have a commercial purpose to use it (though most commercial sites do use this domain). Instead, “.com” is a bit of a catch-all, since this domain name is available to anyone.", "annotation_id": "29724930", "url": "https://genius.com/29724930", "state": "pending", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "I know what you say You say I coulda treated you better or whatever, but I don’t know, I think I did alright Know what I’m sayin’? And you know how you get Drawin’ conclusions like you got a Parsons degree or somethin’ I coulda treated you better, that’s crazy Nope", "vault_lines": ["Asked me if I coulda treated you better, but no", "I know what you say", "You say I coulda treated you better or whatever, but", "I don't know, I think I did alright", "Know what I'm sayin'?", "And you know how you get", "Drawin' conclusions like you got a Parsons degree or somethin'", "I coulda treated you better, that's crazy"], "meaning": "Drake chooses to open FAtD by addressing his former on-and-off partner, Rihanna.\n\nDrake does this by referencing NYC’s Parsons School of Design, one of America’s oldest and most esteemed design and art schools. He utilizes the idiom “draw a conclusion,” which means to consider things and make a judgment, to play on the double meaning of drawing, as Parsons art students literally draw. But how is this related to Rihanna? Well, she accepted an honorary Parsons degree in 2017.\n\n\n\nParsons School of Design honored Rihanna for her pioneering moves in the fashion industry [on May 22, 2017.] #ParsonsBenefit\n—Rihanna’s Facebook", "annotation_id": "29724751", "url": "https://genius.com/29724751", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "line": "Our texts feelin’ like a fencin’ match, your temper shorter and I’m sensin’ that", "vault_lines": ["Our texts feelin' like a fencin' match, your temper shorter and I'm sensin' that"], "meaning": "Drake compares going back and forth over text messages with his woman to a “fencing match”, a 1-on-1 sword-fighting sport based around scoring points against your opponent.", "annotation_id": "29724560", "url": "https://genius.com/29724560", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Virginia Beach", "status": "done", "targets": 30, "clicked": 30, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-virginia-beach-lyrics", "genius_title": "Virginia Beach"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Pasha Sasha pissed,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I think Pasha Sasha is another one Drake’s homie.\n\nBut I have interesting and quite absurd version. Pasha and Sasha can be russian soccer players – Pasha (Pavel) Mamaev and Sasha (Aleksandr) Kokorin. These players are known for their strong friendship and their scandals. After the Zenit – Krasnodar match (Kokorin played for Zenit, and Mamaev for Krasnodar), they went to Moscow and took part in two fights. In one of them, they pissed and hit an official with a chair, and in the other, the driver of an employee of a federal TV channel was injured. They were sentenced to 1.5 years in prison, but after 5 months they were released on parole.\n\n\n\nI think this version has a place to be, since Drake is interested in sports and particular in soccer, he even has photos with famous soccer players.\n\n\n\nDrake with Chelsea legend Frank Lampard", "annotation_id": "25998118", "url": "https://genius.com/25998118", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Steppin’ outta line, get outlined, and you know I’m tied up stateside", "vault_lines": ["Steppin' outta line, get outlined, and you know I'm tied up stateside"], "meaning": "If you step out of line (disrespect Drake and his gang in some way), you’ll get outlined—like a dead body at a crime scene.\n\n\n\nWhat Drake is referencing here is actually more of a myth than reality; the police don’t do this and aren’t supposed to. But it’s a common trope in pop culture and you’ll see it all the time in cop shows, hence Drake refers to it here to convey to his opps that they can get murdered for stepping to him.", "annotation_id": "25002363", "url": "https://genius.com/25002363", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Prince, I carry that last name, that shit stays on me like brand names", "vault_lines": ["Prince, I carry that last name, that shit stays on me like brand names"], "meaning": "Drake’s affiliation with the Prince family headed by  powerful music executive James “J.” Prince is well-known throughout hip-hop culture. Drake has name-dropped the family and its members in several of his songs.", "annotation_id": "20443469", "url": "https://genius.com/20443469", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Jas just lined up brand new hand tings and nice guy on a madting", "vault_lines": ["Jas just lined up brand new hand tings and nice guy on a madting"], "meaning": "“Jas”, or Jas Prince, is the son of James “J” Prince, the owner of Rap-A-Lot Records, and is credited as the person who discovered Drake on MySpace and brought him to Cash Money Records to meet Lil Wayne.\n\n\n\nDrake hints in the line that Jas Prince has equipped Drake and his crew with brand-new handguns for protection.", "annotation_id": "20443424", "url": "https://genius.com/20443424", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Back when Ricky was doin’ up Teesdale, I was doin’ dinner with Teezy", "vault_lines": ["Back when Ricky was doin' up Teesdale, I was doin' dinner with Teezy"], "meaning": "This line is in reference to a classic Toronto hood DVD that was called The Real Toronto. A Toronto rap fan who went by the name Madd Russian had put together a compilation-documentary in which he would showcase various hoods around the city, interview rivaling gang members, and in some instances even document crimes.\n\nUpon the films release in 2005, it had spread like wildfire through word of mouth. DVDs had been sold by children in their schools, and some teachers had even purchased copies to play in their classrooms. It did not take long until the media and law enforcement got a hold of the DVD, which led to various raids and dozens of arrests.\n\nIn the DVD, Ricky shows us around the Teesdale neighbourhood in Scarborough. He talks about previous issues with members of the Sri Lankan neighborhood Crescent Town, a snitch in their complex who is too scared to set foot out of his own apartment, and at some point in the recording an associate shows off and shoots a loaded shotgun.\n\nTo bring things full circle, Ricky now works with OVO Sound and Teesdale is actually the neighbourhood that The Weeknd grew up in. Check out the clip here:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/SCoRTkH4nV8?t=1407", "annotation_id": "20200561", "url": "https://genius.com/20200561", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "The woman I do end up with has to be a bad gyal just like Munchie Can’t have no pretentious ting when I know this road gets bumpy", "vault_lines": ["The woman I do end up with has to be a bad gyal just like Munchie", "Can't have no pretentious ting when I know this road gets bumpy"], "meaning": "Though fans thought Drake was talking about his old flame Rihanna, he’s about talking about Munchie, a woman that has OVO ties. Not much is known about her as her social media accounts are private.\n\n\n\nMunchie posing with Chris Brown\n\n\nIn a December 2019 interview with Rap Radar, Drake described the kind of woman he would get in a committed relationship with:\n\nI love my space, I love my work and I love my routine. And for me to break that for somebody, it would just have to be a really special person that fits into that puzzle and that is supportive of the things I’m doing. Have to be somebody that has taste in music. It’d have to be somebody that, you know, that I, that I get along with so much to the point that when we’re separate, I’m feeling like I can’t function properly without their presence.", "annotation_id": "18786899", "url": "https://genius.com/18786899", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Bring ten shots to the poolside, never seen none of these grown men outside", "vault_lines": ["Bring ten shots to the poolside, never seen none of these grown men outside"], "meaning": "Drake is most likely referring to an incident that took place during Joe Budden’s annual pool party in 2019.\nJoe went live on his Instagram during the party and Drake was quick to join and leave a few comments, telling Joe to play his diss tracks or ring his shots, to see what reaction it would bring.\n\nhttps://pbs.twimg.com/media/ECxls_1XUAEK6G7?format=jpg&name=medium\n\nThe beef started in May of 2016 when Joe released a podcast with his negative opinion on Drake’s March 2016 record, Views. This led to Drake making a short response on his June 2016 track “4PM In Calabasas”:\n\nLook at me now, they look at me like the golden child\n\nThe line prompted Joe to release the June 2016 track “Making A Murder Pt. 1” and July 2016 track “Wake,” which both took shots at the Toronto emcee, who in turn responded in July on French Montana’s “No Shopping”:\n\nPump, pump, pump it up (Skrrt)\nShe got a good head on her, but I pump it up (Skrrt)\nI’m not a one hit wonder, they know all my stuff\nYou let me turn into the nigga that you almost was\n\nThis verse led to Joe’s final diss track “Afraid,” released just a few hours after the aforementioned French Montana track. These are the shots that Joe should have rang at his poolside party filled with grown men that Drake wouldn’t see outside of Joe’s life.", "annotation_id": "18733033", "url": "https://genius.com/18733033", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Wassa, wassa, just like the crodie Pressa, I don’t do no passa", "vault_lines": ["Wassa, wassa, just like the crodie Pressa, I don't do no passa"], "meaning": "Drake gives a shoutout to fellow Toronto artist Pressa. “Wassa” is a term used by Pressa’s gang, Wass Gang—which includes other artists Houdini, Why G, and Robin Banks—to refer to members of the gang. The name comes from one of Pressa’s close friends, Kwasi “Wassi” Skene-Peters, who was shot to death by police in 2015 while on the run for a double murder. Pressa also has a November 2015 song named after the group to honor Wassi.\n\nWass Gang is known to be a Crip set, and members often use the term “crodie”— a Crip variation of the slang term “brodie.” “Passa” is Jamaican Patois for gossip.", "annotation_id": "18712556", "url": "https://genius.com/18712556", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Link with Skull and Cuz and Jamie ah yard and we deh pon Gaza", "vault_lines": ["Link with Skull and Cuz and Jamie ah yard and we deh pon Gaza"], "meaning": "Drake is giving a shoutout to Jamaican artist and OVO signee Popcaan, whose nicknames include Pop Skull and Hot Skull, though he could also be shouting out Dre Skull, the founder of Mixpak Records label, which Popcaan signed to in 2012.\n\nUnruly Cuz is a friend of Popcaan’s and has been mentioned in his music before, such as his May 2015 song, “Unruly Prayer”:\n\nCuz haffi give thanks, yeah\n\nLastly, Jamie YVP is a producer for Mixpak Records.\n\nTo “link up” means to meet up and “ah yard'—also written as “ah yaad”—is Jamaican patois for “at home.”\n\n“Gaza” is Jamaican slang for Waterford, a district located in Portmore, which is a large coastal town located in southern Jamaica. The slang is largely associated with popular Jamaican dancehall artist and former musical mentor of Popcaan, Vybz Kartel as he grew up in Waterford and is largely responsible for popularizing it.", "annotation_id": "18712214", "url": "https://genius.com/18712214", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Pick one man with slaps that’s rappin’, I bet he don’t want no clashings", "vault_lines": ["Pick one man with slaps that's rappin', I bet he don't want no clashings"], "meaning": "‘Clashing’ is a term derivative of Jamaican sound system culture that describes a battle between two or more MC’s.\n\nThis has had a heavy influence on British genres such as grime. Lord of the Mics is a prominent grime platform dedicated to clashing between MC’s. Drake has publicly demonstrated his appreciation of it. In particular the famous clash between Devilman and frequent collaborator Skepta:\n\n\n\nDiss tracks also heavily feature in UK drill music, of which “War” draws its inspiration. Drake is asserting that no MC would want to clash with Drake.", "annotation_id": "18711357", "url": "https://genius.com/18711357", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Niko never move neeky, sweatsuit Nike, sweatsuit DG", "vault_lines": ["Niko never move neeky, sweatsuit Nike, sweatsuit DG"], "meaning": "Niko, otherwise known by the nickname Neeks, is one of Drake’s longtime friends from Toronto. He is noted by Drake as his “closest confidant” on the Thank Me Later album credits.\n\n“Neeky” is slang popularized in the United Kingdom which means geeky or out of fashion. Drake underscores that Neeks has never fit that description, instead wearing Nike and Dolce & Gabanna (DG) sweatsuits.\n\nDrake rapped a similar line connecting Niko to designer clothing on the 2016 track, “Views”:\n\nYou don’t worry ‘bout fittin’ in when you custom made\nMe and Niko used to plot on how to make a change", "annotation_id": "18709949", "url": "https://genius.com/18709949", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Woi-oi Ayy", "vault_lines": ["Woi-oi, man got flown like private jets, for way, way less"], "meaning": "“Woi-oi” is an ad-lib that’s often heard in UK drill music but has Jamaican roots dating back to the ‘80s with reggae artists using it in some form.\n\nThough the ad-lib has been around for years, it gained popularity after Jamaican artist and OVO signee Popcaan used it in a number of his songs, which include his December 2015 song, “Wicked Man Ting,” his December 2016 song, “Christmas Gift” and his April 2017 song, “Stronger Now.”", "annotation_id": "18709521", "url": "https://genius.com/18709521", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "4301, won’t spare no one, niggas gotta know about Southside", "vault_lines": ["4301, won't spare no one, niggas gotta know about Southside"], "meaning": "Drake could be nodding to 4301 Kingston Road, a housing complex in Scarborough, Toronto.\n\n\n\nDrizzy also referred to the area in “Pop Style” from his album Views:\n\nThis sound like some forty-three-oh-one shit (One shit)\n\nThis may also be a reference to SN1 (Spare No-1), a record label founded by British rapper Giggs and affiliated artists in South London. Giggs appears in the music video for “War”. In 2017, Drake had a custom OVO-SN1 ring made.", "annotation_id": "18708297", "url": "https://genius.com/18708297", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "The LV pouch on chest is just for fashion, niggas just actin’", "vault_lines": ["The LV pouch on chest is just for fashion, niggas just actin'"], "meaning": "Here, Drake is stating that people pretend to be concealing weapons in their Louis Vuitton bag(s), but in reality, it is just a facade.\n\nThis line is a nod to Baka Not Nice’s 2017 single “Live Up to My Name”. Baka is a long-time friend of Drake and is signed to his OVO Sound record label.\n\n\n\nDrake and Baka sitting courtside for game 2 of the Raptors vs. Wizards series in Toronto in 2018\n\nOn the opening line of the track, Baka famously rapped:\n\nLouis pouch across my body, this is not for fashion\n\n“Live Up to My Name” was Baka’s first release on the label and went on to peak at number 77 on the Canadian Hot 100. Additionally, it was revealed that Drake had ghostwritten the song when a reference track leaked in 2017.", "annotation_id": "18708291", "url": "https://genius.com/18708291", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Three waps in the whip and we beat that case, where man live way too comfy I don’t do well with people makin’ disses or makin’ threats Woi-oi, man got flown like private jets, for way, way less", "vault_lines": ["Three waps in the whip and we beat that case, where man live way too comfy", "I don't do well with people makin' disses or makin' threats", "Woi-oi, man got flown like private jets, for way, way less"], "meaning": "Private jets aren’t cheap, costing around $3 million at the cheapest. Drake himself owns a private jet; his customised Boeing 767 “Air Drake” is worth $185 million.\n\n\n\n“Waps” is a slang term for “guns”, while “beating a case” refers to winning a court case.\n\nDrake also emphasises here that he doesn’t deal with anyone taking shots at him, alluding that he’ll shoot them and make them “fly” into the air.\n\nThis could also be an allusion to Preme’s court case which Drake helped win. After Preme was pulled over with three guns in a car, Drake paid legal representatives to assist Preme in beating the case.", "annotation_id": "18708288", "url": "https://genius.com/18708288", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "AXL (AXL), AXL (AXL), AXL (AXL)", "vault_lines": ["AXL (AXL), AXL (AXL), AXL (AXL)"], "meaning": "This is the producer tag of UK producer AXL Beats, who is well-known for helping globalize the UK drill sound. He has worked with American artists like Fivio Foreign, Pop Smoke, and Sheff G, but this is his first time working with Drake.", "annotation_id": "18708283", "url": "https://genius.com/18708283", "state": "accepted", "votes": 151, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Are you dumb or stupid? The wheels on the Rolls is Chromazz, headshot domazz", "vault_lines": ["Are you dumb or stupid? The wheels on the Rolls is Chromazz, headshot domazz"], "meaning": "Chromazz is a Toronto rapper. Drake plays on her name to represent the chrome wheels on his Rolls Royce.\n\n\n\n“Are you dumb or stupid” is a nod to Toronto slang that has Jamaican origins. Calling someone “stupid” is usually a response to their actions or comments, but in Toronto circles, the slang is an initial claim, often used at the start of a monologue.", "annotation_id": "18708039", "url": "https://genius.com/18708039", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Before I was ever around Kendall’s, I was at ends though, dreamin’ up Enzos", "vault_lines": ["Before I was ever around Kendall's, I was at ends though, dreamin' up Enzos"], "meaning": "Kendall Jenner is a well-known supermodel from Los Angeles, California. She has been seen together with Drake frequently since their high profile dinner in 2015. The two remain good friends, despite Drake’s highly publicized rap beef with Kendall’s brother-in-law Kanye West.\n\nFerrari Enzos are exclusive models of cars that are highly coveted—a car Drake used to dream about but owns a Yellow Ferrari LaFerrari, the successor model to the Enzo.", "annotation_id": "18708032", "url": "https://genius.com/18708032", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Feds wanna tap up man and wire up man like Chubbs did Detail", "vault_lines": ["Feds wanna tap up man and wire up man like Chubbs did Detail"], "meaning": "Chubbs is one of Drake’s closest friends and his OVO bodyguard. In June 2016, producer Detail filed a lawsuit against Drake, claiming that back in 2014 Chubbs broke his jaw and sent him to the hospital. It was later reported that the judge threw away the case because Detail didn’t appear at the final status conference.", "annotation_id": "18708004", "url": "https://genius.com/18708004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 107, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Everyone I know has codenames, anyone I’m beefin’ with is a no-name", "vault_lines": ["Everyone I know has codenames, anyone I'm beefin' with is a no-name"], "meaning": "Throughout the course of his career, Drake has been engaged in multiple high-profile rap beefs; however, this line likely addresses his battle with bitter rival Pusha T. The two released back-to-back diss tracks in 2018- Drake’s “Duppy Freestyle” and Pusha T’s “The Story of Adidon”- before quieting down in 2019.\n\n“Duppy Freestyle” famously called out Pusha for needing his help to sell records and claimed that the diss track actually helped Pusha increase his sales, which he confirmed by sending an actual invoice:\n\nTell ‘Ye we got a invoice comin’ to you\nConsiderin' that we just sold another 20 for you\n\nDrake echoed this sentiment on his June 2019 track, “Omertà”:\n\nLast year, niggas really feel like they rode on me\nLast year, niggas got hot ‘cause they told on me", "annotation_id": "18707980", "url": "https://genius.com/18707980", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Niggas can’t even win home games, they just gotta fall in line like Soul Train", "vault_lines": ["Niggas can't even win home games, they just gotta fall in line like Soul Train"], "meaning": "Soul Train is an African American-focused TV program famous for its line dance, where the dancers formed two lines with a space in the middle for dancers to strut down and dance in consecutive order. Drake paid homage to the television program’s dance alongside Migos on the music video for “Walk It Talk It.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W8YSxo_-HlU", "annotation_id": "18707976", "url": "https://genius.com/18707976", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Bringin’ man down when they take sides, tryna get saved by the bell like Bayside", "vault_lines": ["Bringin' man down when they take sides, tryna get saved by the bell like Bayside"], "meaning": "Saved by the Bell is a 1990s sitcom surrounding the students of Bayside High School in California. The title is in reference to the idiom “saved by the bell,” which involves narrowly escaping danger due to unexpected intervention.", "annotation_id": "18707959", "url": "https://genius.com/18707959", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "Used to look up to a man from certain ends with tune on repeat Thought he was a bad boy, then ’til man got pinched and man went PC Man went PC just like Dell and Windows, some man been those", "vault_lines": ["Man went PC just like Dell and Windows, some man been those", "Used to look up to a man from certain ends with tune on repeat", "Thought he was a bad boy, then 'til man got pinched and man went PC"], "meaning": "Drake’s likely talking about Toronto rapper Mayhem Morearty, who was popular in the late ’90s to late 2000s. He was a gangster rapper who hailed from a project housing within Toronto known as “The Jungle” or formally, Lawrence Heights.\n\nMayhem became infamous for aiding a Toronto serial killer Mark Moore, who was also a rapper that went by the name of Presidenteh. In the early 2010s, Moore went a killing spree, where he ended up murdering four people to prove he was a serious rapper to Mayhem. After Moore’s fourth victim, Mayhem became an informant for the police and confessed Moore’s actions.\n\nIn 2014, Moore and Mayhem were both sentenced to 12 years in prison for a jewelry robbery they committed years prior. It was also where Moore committed his first murder. However, the following year in 2015, Moore was given four additional life sentences for each murder he committed, following Mayhem’s testimony against him. In response for his cooperation with the police, Mayhem received a reduced sentence of 10 years.\n\nDrake plays on the abbreviation “PC” as it means a personal computer, which Drake alludes to by mentioning Dell computers and the Windows operating system and protective custody, which Mayhem needed after he got “pinched” and snitched on Moore.", "annotation_id": "18707946", "url": "https://genius.com/18707946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "line": "OVOXO link up, mandem drink up, me and the drillers Hawk and Sticks and Cash and Baka, Gucci, P, and Gilla And the boy that sound like he sang on Thriller, you know that’s been my nigga Yeah, we just had to fix things, family, 6ix tings, we can’t split up", "vault_lines": ["OVOXO link up, mandem drink up, me and the drillers", "And the boy that sound like he sang on Thriller, you know that's been my nigga", "Yeah, we just had to fix things, family, 6ix tings, we can't split up"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to his feud with Canadian R&B artist The Weeknd, as they have been engaged in an on-and-off conflict since 2011. The Weeknd’s high octave vocal range is frequently compared to pop legend Michael Jackson, who released the classic track “Thriller” in January 1984.\n\nDrake’s ex-bodyguard Baka Not Nice is an OVO affiliate, along with Canadian rappers Reps Up Gilla, TV Gucci and Preme. Hawk Marley and Sticks are two of Abel’s best friends and members of his XO crew, and Amir “Cash” Esmailian is Abel’s manager and a cofounder of XO. Drake gave a similar shout out on his March 2017 track, “Lose You”:\n\nIf we not on the charts, my XO niggas eatin'\nFifty-two consecutive weekends, shout out to Weeknd\n\nIn January 2019, The Weeknd sent shots at Drake on DJ Gesaffelstein’s track, “Lost in The Fire,” which attacked him for keeping his son a secret:\n\nAnd I just want a baby with the right one\n‘Cause I could never be the one to hide one\n\nIn November 2019, Drake re-followed Abel on Instagram, seemingly confirming that the two have since reconciled.", "annotation_id": "18707922", "url": "https://genius.com/18707922", "state": "accepted", "votes": 318, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "War", "status": "done", "targets": 24, "clicked": 24, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-war-lyrics", "genius_title": "War"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Where Were You", "line": "I hate that I looked desperate You gone is like me naked", "vault_lines": ["I hate that I looked desperate", "You gone is like me naked"], "meaning": "That moment in a breakup where you have nothing to loose. You do everything and anything to try to fix it even if it makes you look desperate.\n\nWhen you got out of a relationship you can feel almost naked or exposed because you are so use to the persons presents in your life", "annotation_id": "23655662", "url": "https://genius.com/23655662", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Where Were You", "line": "I have to watch the road I swear that every car that passes has you in it I can’t believe we’re finished", "vault_lines": ["I have to watch the road", "I swear I heard you call", "I swear that every car that passes has you in it", "I can't go to bed", "I can’t believe we’re finished"], "meaning": "This is a moment when you are so distracted by the hurt/ pain/ breakup that you have to remind yourself to pay attention. Your looking out hoping you’ll cross their path and maybe they’ll change their mind about the relationship", "annotation_id": "23655654", "url": "https://genius.com/23655654", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Where Were You", "line": "That’s why I’m askin’ this question", "vault_lines": ["That's why I'm askin' this question"], "meaning": "She’s really confused with the end of the relationship, and apparently wants him back… So, she wonders were he is.", "annotation_id": "1072203", "url": "https://genius.com/1072203", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Where Were You", "status": "done", "targets": 3, "clicked": 3, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-where-were-you-lyrics", "genius_title": "Where Were You"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "I’m just game droppin’", "vault_lines": ["I'm just game droppin'"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing how The Game is known for name dropping. The Game has dropped names 1,788 times over his first 8 studio albums making him the artist to name drop the most times in hiphop history. This VICE article explains more: https://www.vice.com/en/article/we-now-have-hard-proof-the-game-cant-keep-peoples-names-out-of-his-mouth/", "annotation_id": "38210314", "url": "https://genius.com/38210314", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "Would you love Ed like you love Drake?", "vault_lines": ["Would you love Ed like you love Drake?", "Love Fred like you love Drake?", "Love Greg like you love Drake?", "Love Ced like you love Drake?", "Better take your ass home tonight and love that man like you say you love Drake"], "meaning": "This line refers to Edgar Stepanyan who is friend of Aubrey Drake", "annotation_id": "37200647", "url": "https://genius.com/37200647", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "Name droppin’,", "vault_lines": ["Nah, I ain't name droppin',"], "meaning": "In this line Drake could be referring to how people have called him a ‘sneak-disser’, which this line practically confirms this statement.", "annotation_id": "32983639", "url": "https://genius.com/32983639", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "BARK Radio", "vault_lines": ["And you're listening to BARK Radio"], "meaning": "‘BARK Radio’ known as a Radio Station.", "annotation_id": "32983624", "url": "https://genius.com/32983624", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "Now see ****, that girl thick", "vault_lines": ["Now see ****, that girl thick"], "meaning": "Given the rhyme scheme and 7/8 syllable count for each line in this section of the verse (plus the features list off the album), willing to bet “SZA” is what got censored.", "annotation_id": "30464509", "url": "https://genius.com/30464509", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "Can I feel it? Can I feel it? Can I feel it? Can I feel one? Can I feel it? Can I feel it? Can I feel?", "vault_lines": ["Can I feel it? Can I feel it? Can I feel it? Can I feel one?", "Can I feel it? Can I feel it? Can I feel?"], "meaning": "Drake is expressing his deep desire to grab some ass", "annotation_id": "29749233", "url": "https://genius.com/29749233", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "Dressed up to the nines", "vault_lines": ["Dressed up to the nines"], "meaning": "“Dressed up to the nines” means the girls are wearing fashionable or formal clothes for a special occasion.\n\nShe goes all out when she’s going to a drake show.", "annotation_id": "29731287", "url": "https://genius.com/29731287", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "Sometimes, I think to myself, “What if I was somebody else?” Would your ass still be here? What if I had a CDL leavin’ outta STL On the way to ATL in a big eighteen-wheel Would your ass still be here? Would your ass still be here?", "vault_lines": ["Sometimes, I think to myself, \"What if I was somebody else?\"", "Would your ass still be here?", "What if I had a CDL leavin' outta STL", "On the way to ATL in a big eighteen-wheel"], "meaning": "Drake ponders if he wasn’t himself and was someone less famous, if this woman would be with him still.\n\nHe wonders how different it would be if he were a trucker with his CDL, Commercial Driver’s License,  driving an 18-wheeler from state to state. He uses abbreviations referencing both St. Louis and Atlanta.", "annotation_id": "29726024", "url": "https://genius.com/29726024", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "I’m a Saint, alright? I’ll Franklin Saint a bitch", "vault_lines": ["I'm a Saint, alright? I'll Franklin Saint a bitch"], "meaning": "Drake references Franklin Saint, the main protagonist  from the TV series “Snowfall”.", "annotation_id": "29725466", "url": "https://genius.com/29725466", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "I’m as well as well-known gets with a long list Of some ho-ho-hoes still waitin’ on a gift Guess them ho-ho-hoes still believe in Saint Nick, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I'm a well-known player, I'm a well-known trick", "I'm as well as well-known gets with a long list", "Of some ho-ho-hoes still waitin' on a gift", "Guess them ho-ho-hoes still believe in Saint Nick, yeah"], "meaning": "The wordplay in “ho-ho-hoes” combines the slang term “ho” with the festive “ho-ho-ho” associated with Santa Claus.\n\nThis line playfully suggests that despite his fame, there are still individuals eagerly awaiting something from him, drawing a parallel to the anticipation of receiving gifts during the holiday season.\n\nImplying they must still believe in Santa Claus (“Saint Nick”), he is as nonexistent as anything these women may think he owes them.", "annotation_id": "29725346", "url": "https://genius.com/29725346", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "They say love’s like a BBL, you won’t know if it’s real until you feel one", "vault_lines": ["They say love's like a BBL, you won't know if it's real until you feel one"], "meaning": "Drake uses a clever metaphor to describe the nature of love. He compares it to the popular Brazilian Butt Lift cosmetic surgery, abbreviated “BBL”.\n\nThe essence of the metaphor lies in the idea that you can’t truly know if love is genuine until you experience it firsthand, just like you can’t know the result of a BBL until you actually have or feel one.\n\nDrake’s reference to a BBL is meant to convey the idea that love can be deceptive and elusive.", "annotation_id": "29725224", "url": "https://genius.com/29725224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "line": "[Outro: Sade Adu] This is Sade Adu", "vault_lines": ["This is Sade Adu"], "meaning": "Sade Adu is a Nigerian-born British singer, known as the lead vocalist of her band Sade. She is also recognized for being an ambassador for black women in England.\n\n\n\nPictured: Drake and Sade Adu in 2017\n\nDrake has publicly showcased his admiration for Sade and even has a tattoo of her face on his side abdominals.\n\n\n\nPictured: Drake’s tattoo of Sade Adu\n\nFor Drake’s 30th birthday, Ms. Adu sent him over a special gift – a portrait of the singer and a handwritten note from her that read:\n\nThe 24th of October 1986 was a very good day. Shine on :-) Love, Sade.", "annotation_id": "29725217", "url": "https://genius.com/29725217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "BBL Love (Interlude)", "status": "done", "targets": 12, "clicked": 12, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-bbl-love-interlude-lyrics", "genius_title": "BBL Love (Interlude)"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "Yeah I know we just signed a deal, but I need my advance on the next one too, they know I’ma be around Yeah I need it", "vault_lines": ["I know we just signed a deal, but", "I need my advance on the next one too, they know I'ma be around"], "meaning": "Drake refers to the Apple deal he was supposedly offered for $20 million dollars. He’s good enough to receive more cash upfront, because unlike many other rappers, Drake has longevity.", "annotation_id": "7836709", "url": "https://genius.com/7836709", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "These niggas so jealous, jealous Man, these niggas get scared, they tellin’, tellin’ I fuck a check up on everything I bought some ice when the feds came", "vault_lines": ["These niggas so jealous, jealous", "Man, these niggas get scared, they tellin', tellin'", "I fuck a check up on everything", "I bought some ice when the feds came"], "meaning": "Drake already told y’all there’s nooo tellin’ – there’s very few things that’s worse than being labeled a “snitch” in the streets as it goes against the G code.\n\nFuture’s success is so strong that he has become a threat to the competition, making them attempt to overthrow his throne by any means. Little do they know he’s so confident he stunts even in the presence of feds. Furthermore, jewelry is often pawned as a way to get money to post bail on an arrest. Police can’t confiscate jewelry, but all drug money gets immediately seized.\n\nAlternatively, this might be Future’s take on the Meek x Drake feud, claiming that Meek only tweeted about Drake’s supposed ghostwriting because he was jealous of Drake’s success. Meek has a track titled “Check” on his latest album.", "annotation_id": "7834834", "url": "https://genius.com/7834834", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "All of my rings Aquafina, my bitch Aquafina", "vault_lines": ["All of my rings Aquafina, my bitch Aquafina"], "meaning": "Aquafina is a brand of purified water bottles. The value of diamonds are usually determined by its clarity; the diamonds in Future’s rings are so clear that they are as transparent as Aquafina water. Additionally, his girl is shaped like a bottle (hourglass figure) – or perhaps she’s just wet.", "annotation_id": "7834828", "url": "https://genius.com/7834828", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "I bought that bitch new Celine, I got rings, I got racks like Serena", "vault_lines": ["I bought that bitch new Celine, I got rings, I got racks like Serena"], "meaning": "Céline, a French luxury brand that’s owned by LVMH, doesn’t have rings in its online Fall 2015 collection, but what it does sell it prices well into the hundreds or even thousands of dollars. With money like Serena Williams, who’s made over $74 million from tennis alone, those prices would be easily within budget. “Racks” could also function as a play on “rackets.\"", "annotation_id": "7834823", "url": "https://genius.com/7834823", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "Look at the smile on me, look at the owl on me I do not chase girls, but they run a mile for me", "vault_lines": ["Look at the smile on me, look at the owl on me", "I do not chase girls, but they run a mile for me"], "meaning": "Drake’s charismatic smile, money, and affinity for OVO owl sweaters attract the ladies. They flock to him for shallow reasons and will do anything to be the best he’s ever had, in hopes of becoming Mrs. Aubrey Drake Graham.\n\nIt’s also worth mentioning that Lolo Jones, a famous track star (who literally runs miles), was thirsting after Drake on Twitter.", "annotation_id": "7834814", "url": "https://genius.com/7834814", "state": "pending", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "We take a Mello Yello, then we fill it with red forever-ever", "vault_lines": ["We take a Mello Yello, then we fill it with red forever-ever"], "meaning": "As the intro lines to the verse suggests, Future is big into lean; this time, instead of Sprite, Future mixes the cough syrup with the Coca-Cola beverage Mellow Yellow. “Forever Eva” is also the title of another Future track.", "annotation_id": "7834810", "url": "https://genius.com/7834810", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "Man, what a time to be alive I’m drinkin’ lean, they thought I died", "vault_lines": ["Man, what a time to be alive", "I'm drinkin' lean, they thought I died", "Man, what a time"], "meaning": "Future often brags of his love for lean, the infamous mixture of Sprite soda and codeine cough syrup. Codeine is an opiate, like heroin or morphine. Overdoses of the drug can be fatal.\n\nRumors of Future’s death spread in July 2015 after fake articles alleged that Future died of a codeine overdose. Multiple rappers, such as Pimp C, DJ Screw and A$AP Yams, have sadly overdosed on lean, so Future assures his fans he’s still alive and kicking despite his heavy intake of the drug.", "annotation_id": "7834800", "url": "https://genius.com/7834800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "Say she gon’ ride for me, I’ll buy the tires for you", "vault_lines": ["Say she gon' ride for me, I'll buy the tires for you"], "meaning": "Drake has a good track record when it comes to tire purchases. He rewards loyalty with financial and emotional support. Interestingly enough, Serena Williams will star in the 2016 Pirelli calendar, Pirelli being a tire manufacturer.\n\n\n\nIt’s also a sexual reference, with girls riding him and him supplying the rubbers (tires).", "annotation_id": "7834798", "url": "https://genius.com/7834798", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "Pluto and Jupiter, I go to Venus", "vault_lines": ["Pluto and Jupiter, I go to Venus"], "meaning": "Future’s a big fan of space exploration – he’s already been to Pluto and Mars. Curiously, he traveled to the furthest (Pluto) first, and has stayed closer to Earth for his latest travels.\n\n\n\nSince Future just shouted out Drake’s rumored girlfriend Serena Williams in the previous lines, this is surely in reference to her sister Venus.", "annotation_id": "7834785", "url": "https://genius.com/7834785", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "This game is different, you only get one shot when niggas go foul on you", "vault_lines": ["This game is different, you only get one shot when niggas go foul on you"], "meaning": "Drake took his side of the Meek Mill beef seriously, releasing “Charged Up” and “Back to Back” before Meek even stepped in the booth. Even though Meek hit Drake with a personal foul mid-shot, he was still able to sink that three ball.\n\nA shooting foul in the NBA would normally result in 2 free-throws, but in the rap game, you’re only allowed 1 shot.", "annotation_id": "7834775", "url": "https://genius.com/7834775", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "You just a battle rapper, I’m an official trapper Niggas be droppin’ subliminals, nigga, they just some jibber-jabber", "vault_lines": ["You just a battle rapper, I'm an official trapper", "Niggas be droppin' subliminals, nigga, they just some jibber-jabber"], "meaning": "Future claims he is a real criminal (a trapper, someone who deals drugs) rather than a mere rapper.\n\nThe battle rap aesthetic has not been a leading force in commercial hip hop for some time. Calling someone a “battle rapper” suggests they are both soft and out-of-date.\n\nThe rap battle circuit and the trap rap arenas are vastly different worlds; one focuses on putting others down, and the latter is simply about getting your dollars up. Future isn’t too concerned about whoever may be hating, he’s over here doing his thing.\n\nA “subliminal” in rap is a diss that doesn’t name its target. It’s considered a somewhat cowardly move, but is widely engaged in, even by rappers who declaim the practice elsewhere. Case in point: Future might be dropping a subliminal here.\n\n\n\nConsidering Meek Mill’s past as a battle rapper, many initially interpreted this as a shot at Meek, following his comments against Future at his Made In America set. However, in a cover story for XXL, Future expressed his love and respect for Meek.", "annotation_id": "7834749", "url": "https://genius.com/7834749", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "line": "’Cause I got a really big team And they need some really big rings They need some really nice things Better be comin’ with no strings Better be comin’ with no strings We need some really nice things We need some really big rings I got a really big team I got a really big team They need some really big rings They need some really nice things Better be comin’ with no strings Better be comin’ with no strings We need some really nice things We need some really big rings I got a really big team", "vault_lines": ["We need some really big rings", "I got a really big team", "They need some really big rings", "And I got a really big team", "'Cause I got a really big team", "And they need some really big rings", "They need some really nice things", "Better be comin' with no strings", "We need some really nice things"], "meaning": "Drake’s squad is extremely loyal, whether they’re catching bodies for him or taking jail time for him, they’ve always had him covered. He’s thanking them now by getting them all cheques without the woo-hah – it’s important to show love to the crew.\n\nDrake’s growing wealth allows him to treat his team to a multitude of luxuries, ranging from spontaneous holidays to expensive jewelry – they’re now living like underground kings. It should also be noted that Drake is no stranger to big rings.\n\nDrake compares his team to basketball teams, and he feels like his team has won the championship, making them worthy of all the best basketball rings.\n\nFuture’s favorite jeweler Elliot Avianne actually designed some pretty big rings to celebrate the collaboration between the two artists.", "annotation_id": "7834747", "url": "https://genius.com/7834747", "state": "accepted", "votes": 143, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Rings", "status": "done", "targets": 12, "clicked": 12, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-future-big-rings-lyrics", "genius_title": "Big Rings"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "Take it to the head, baby, penetration Money not a problem, you can finger trace it Drinking on the syrup, taking medications", "vault_lines": ["Take it to the head, baby, penetration", "Money not a problem, you can finger trace it", "Drinking on the syrup, taking medications"], "meaning": "Future’s encouraging this girl to drink her alcohol straight, no hesitation. But that’s not the only thing going in her mouth, hence penetration.\n\nFinger tracing is an activity used to transition kids into writing. Whatever dollar amount she can write with her hands, Future’s got it.\n\nAs usual, Future’s sticking to his drink of choice, “Dirty Sprite,” to wash down his Adderall and Percocet.", "annotation_id": "7843311", "url": "https://genius.com/7843311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "Roxx got a case, but they get off again", "vault_lines": ["Roxx got a case, but they get off again"], "meaning": "Drake has paid for lawyers and paid people off to keep his crew members and friends out of prison.\n\nOVO member Jonny Roxx, the personal trainer who’s been helping Drake bulk up, may have caught a case recently, but OVO came to his aid.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/7c812fj7Ii/?taken-by=ovojonnyroxx", "annotation_id": "7836433", "url": "https://genius.com/7836433", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "Reps Up, you know there’s a hundred of them", "vault_lines": ["Reps Up, you know there's a hundred of them"], "meaning": "“Reps Up” is the clique name of Drake’s signee P. Reign. He tagged along with Drake across Canada on the Away From Home tour.\n\nP. Reign had his own run in with the law, and Drake paid his lawyer fees just like he got Roxx off.\n\nThey hired me one of the top lawyers in North America and he represented me. We went to trial and we won, thank God. I really owe it to him. It was definitely a large lump sum of money that he put up for me to take care of me.", "annotation_id": "7836045", "url": "https://genius.com/7836045", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "Yeah You know how nights like this go (OVOFBG)", "vault_lines": ["You know how nights like this go"], "meaning": "October’s Very Own (OVO) and Freebandz Gang (FBG) are Drake and Future’s respective record labels. What a Time to Be Alive marks the convergence of these two.", "annotation_id": "7835868", "url": "https://genius.com/7835868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "I peel them hundreds on hundreds I get that money on money I get that guala on guala I get them dollars on dollars Then throw out them dollars, them bitches gon’ holler I got more style than the stylists I like that money is piling I see that money just piling I see it’s piling and piling and piling and piling and piling and piling", "vault_lines": ["I peel them hundreds on hundreds", "I get that money on money", "I get that guala on guala", "I get them dollars on dollars", "Then throw out them dollars, them bitches gon' holler", "I got more style than the stylists", "I like that money is piling", "I see that money just piling", "I see it's piling and piling and piling and piling and piling and piling"], "meaning": "After dropping DS2 in July to critical acclaim, Future’s only getting bigger and bigger and the same goes for his money.\n\nMoney’s literally piling and piling right in front of Future’s eyes. With the release of What A Time To Be Alive, he can expect to see a lot more money too. The mixtape is predicted to sell 500k copies it’s first week!", "annotation_id": "7835770", "url": "https://genius.com/7835770", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "Six summers straight and I run it again", "vault_lines": ["Six summers straight and I run it again"], "meaning": "Drake’s been a public figure in the game since 2009, when he dropped his mixtape So Far Gone and was on everyone’s summer playlist. Since then, the 6 God hasn’t slowed down, and even when he doesn’t drop an album, he’s still the talk of the summer with singles like “0-100/The Catch Up”.", "annotation_id": "7835648", "url": "https://genius.com/7835648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "This Adderall got me to the ceiling (I’m to the ceiling and I) I think them Percocets had me in my feelings (In my feelings and I)", "vault_lines": ["I think them Percocets had me in my feelings (In my feelings and I)", "This Adderall got me to the ceiling (I'm to the ceiling and I)"], "meaning": "Adderall is an upper derived from amphetamine, while Percocet is a downer and an opioid. Future’s pill popping has him in between highs and lows, and it’d be tempting to take one to balance out the effects of the other.", "annotation_id": "7835465", "url": "https://genius.com/7835465", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "Sixty naked bitches, no exaggeration We bought all the bottles, had to change locations Smashing all the models, now we hit the waitress When you gettin’ a lot of money, you can’t help but save it", "vault_lines": ["Sixty naked bitches, no exaggeration", "We bought all the bottles, had to change locations", "Smashing all the models, now we hit the waitress", "When you gettin' a lot of money, you can't help but save it"], "meaning": "Drake and Future bought all the bottles in the club and a ton of strippers to go with them (60 to be exact) and they’re going to club hop to get more.\n\nDrake alone has a net worth of $76 million dollars, while Future has a net worth of $4 million dollars. It’s safe to say that the pair can afford to party like this.\n\nThey’re “smashing” the strippers in the club and are going to do the same to the waitresses just to go one step further.\n\nThis isn’t the first time Future and Drake have gone crazy in a club, either.", "annotation_id": "7835356", "url": "https://genius.com/7835356", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "Fuck all the opps and the shots that they send I let off first, then I let off again You will not hear from them ever again", "vault_lines": ["Fuck all the opps and the shots that they send", "I let off first, then I let off again", "You will not hear from them ever again"], "meaning": "Drake calls out his opposition and the most recent “opp,” Meek Mill.\n\nDrake “let off” his first diss track “Charged Up” and then “let off” again with “Back to Back.” Meek Mill responded with “Wanna Know” but has been silent since “Back to Back.”\n\nMany criticized Meek because of this beef and it isn’t helping his career right now.", "annotation_id": "7835341", "url": "https://genius.com/7835341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Change Locations", "line": "But tonight, me and my friends, we got money to spend Me and my friends, we got money to spend Me and my friends, me and my friends, we got money to spend Me and my friends, we got money to spend Me and my friends, me and my friends, we got money to spend", "vault_lines": ["But tonight, me and my friends, we got money to spend", "Me and my friends, we got money to spend", "Me and my friends, me and my friends, we got money to spend"], "meaning": "Drizzy has the money, so Drizzy’s gonna pay it.\n\nGoing to “Dreams with a suitcase” or any other strip club, Drake’s going to spend a lot of money on strippers and alcohol.\n\nDrake’s been known to make it rain, even throwing $50k at one club. He’s also thrown money away out of anger, particularly when his crew member was attacked in a club.", "annotation_id": "7835045", "url": "https://genius.com/7835045", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
