{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Practice", "line": "[Chorus]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The Weeknd is responsible for crafting the hook of this song.  He explains this in an interview with Complex Magazine:\n\nThat whole hook was me. That’s probably the only song I wrote for Take Care. The rest of it was just shit I was going to have for [House of Balloons]. He really wanted to incorporate my sound, which was inspired by his sound.", "annotation_id": "1998661", "url": "https://genius.com/1998661", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Practice", "line": "After you back it up, then stop (Back it up, baby) After you back it up, then stop (Uh, hey, babygirl) After you back it up, then stop (Yeah) After you back it up, then stop", "vault_lines": ["After you back it up, then stop (Back it up, baby)"], "meaning": "Drizzy’s ode to Lil' Wayne.\n\nAlso an ode to Lil Wayne in “Back That Azz Up”.", "annotation_id": "446458", "url": "https://genius.com/446458", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Practice", "line": "And my girls from the 504 need to drop it right now, uh Need to drop it right now", "vault_lines": ["And my girls from the 504 need to drop it right now, uh", "Need to drop it right now", "All my girls from the 504 need to drop it right now", "Need to drop it right now, oh"], "meaning": "504 is the area code of New Orleans, LA, so Drake is telling  all of the New Orleans girls to dance. This also continues the allusion to Cash Money Records, since the label was founded in New Orleans", "annotation_id": "442800", "url": "https://genius.com/442800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Practice", "line": "I’m a Big Tymer nigga, yeah, money flipper, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I'm a Big Tymer nigga, yeah, money flipper, yeah"], "meaning": "He’s comparing himself to the Big Tymers duo, which consisted of Birdman and Mannie Fresh.  The group would often brag about having platinum football fields and other such feats of incredible ballerness\n\n\n\nIt’s another reference to “Back That Azz Up” – “I’m a big Tymer n-gga, pull the trigger, yeah”", "annotation_id": "435531", "url": "https://genius.com/435531", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Practice", "line": "Girl, you look good, won’t you back that ass up? You a fine motherfucker, won’t you back that ass up? Call me big daddy, won’t you back that ass up?", "vault_lines": ["You a fine motherfucker, won't you back that ass up?", "Call me big daddy, won't you back that ass up?", "Girl, who is you playing with? Back that ass up", "Girl, you look good, won't you back that ass up?"], "meaning": "Drake’s complimenting the said stripper on how good she looks and how well she performs for him\n\nThis is also a clever interpolation of former Cash Money rapper Juvenile’s classic record, Back That Azz Up, one that Juvie himself was okay with", "annotation_id": "435513", "url": "https://genius.com/435513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
