{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Belly Shit (Alicia)", "line": "I’m so fly, I glide Clyde Drexler", "vault_lines": ["I'm so fly, I glide Clyde Drexler"], "meaning": "Clyde Drexler is a former basketball player and one of the best guards of all time. Belly refers to Clyde’s nickname “The Glide” which was coined due to his high-flying yet effortless dunks.", "annotation_id": "22533394", "url": "https://genius.com/22533394", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Belly Shit (Alicia)", "line": "But Belly told me to do me And don’t listen to anybody that knew me Cause to have known me Would mean that there’s a new me And if you think I changed in the slightest could’ve fooled me And tell my city I’m the two-three Drug dealers live vicariously through me", "vault_lines": ["But Belly told me to do me", "Cause to have known me", "Would mean that there's a new me", "And tell my city I'm the two-three", "Tell them rappers I'm the man in the city that I run", "I'm tryna tell you I do this"], "meaning": "These lyrics were officially used on Drake’s track “Say What’s Real,” off his critically acclaimed mixtape So Far Gone, which was released in 2009.\n\nIn comparison to the original cut, Drake’s cadence on this track is less aggressive and instead of  the featured Belly, he references long term producer and friend, Noah ‘40’ Shebib.\n\n\n\nBelly and Drake collaborated on the track “Make It Go,” which was officially released  at the end of 2009, rendering “Belly Shit” a throwaway—hence why Drizzy took these lyrics for himself.", "annotation_id": "9146749", "url": "https://genius.com/9146749", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Belly Shit (Alicia)", "line": "No bench time, nickname started Drake Franchise player, I don’t even get a water break", "vault_lines": ["No bench time, nickname started Drake"], "meaning": "Reminiscent of a line from “Tuscan Leather”:\n\nBench players talkin' like starters, I hate it\n\nA franchise player is a team’s most talented, recognizable player—think LeBron James or JJ Watt. Drake views himself as the rap game’s franchise player, and it’s a full-time job.\n\nIf he doesn’t work on his long-range game, he might see some bench time.", "annotation_id": "9146643", "url": "https://genius.com/9146643", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Belly Shit (Alicia)", "line": "And these other niggas is hardly mentioned I bet that with this pen I could draw attention But you can tell by my strut I’m just chilling in the cut, nigga what And I’m feeling like", "vault_lines": ["And tell my city I'm the two-three", "And these other niggas is hardly mentioned", "I'm just chilling in the cut, nigga what", "And I'm feeling like", "I'm tryna tell you I do this"], "meaning": "Drake apparently can’t draw in the traditional sense, but he sure knows how to draw a crowd. This was written in 2011, around the time he was prepping a November release for his sophomore, Take Care, which to date is his best-selling album with 2,260,000+ copies sold. Since then, he has only made a bigger name for himself, becoming hip hop’s biggest player, and breaking into the pop-o-sphere. He pretty much predicted his success, and did it with ease.", "annotation_id": "9144756", "url": "https://genius.com/9144756", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Belly Shit (Alicia)", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 4, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-belly-shit-alicia-lyrics", "genius_title": "Belly Shit (Alicia)"}
