{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "I got a show out in London, when?", "vault_lines": ["I got a show out in London, when?"], "meaning": "Drake hasn’t touched down in London since 2019 and is alluding to a future show. Drake has shown love to the UK on several instances, such as featuring multiple UK artists on More Life and his surprise appearance at Wireless Festival 2018", "annotation_id": "32647642", "url": "https://genius.com/32647642", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Yeah, how many summers did I run? Like ten", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, how many summers did I run? Like ten", "How many summers did I run? Like ten"], "meaning": "Drake is casually noting how long his reign at the top has been and it is not an exaggeration. From 2014 to 2024, Drake had several charting songs on the Billboard Hot 100 each summer.\n\nAlso this play on the fact that “Not Like Us” producer Mustard had an album named 10 Summers which came out back in August 2014. 10 Summers is also the name of a record label founded by DJ Mustard.", "annotation_id": "32647602", "url": "https://genius.com/32647602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Please write my mother and tell her, tell her the shape that I’m in", "vault_lines": ["Please write my mother and tell her, tell her the shape that I'm in"], "meaning": "Similar to how on “Meet The Grahams”, by Kendrick Lamar, Kendrick was talking to Drake’s mother about her son’s “misogynistic antics”.\n\n\n\nNow Drake is telling the world (in a sense) to contact his mother again and let her know how his life is in the aftermath of the rap battle with Kendrick Lamar.", "annotation_id": "32622267", "url": "https://genius.com/32622267", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "I got away from the city, I’m on the outskirts of town", "vault_lines": ["I got away from the city, I'm on the outskirts of town"], "meaning": "Drake posted a picture of himself, his dad and his son at his Texas Ranch House post the beef with Kendrick Lamar. The Ranch house is a 313-acre Inn at Dos Brisas property, which was at one time an iconic resort that offered a country refuge for city slickers from Austin or Houston. It’s about 70 miles northwest of Houston midway between Navasota and Brenham.", "annotation_id": "32621620", "url": "https://genius.com/32621620", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Might put some dollar ’pon head Or just turn up the place instead", "vault_lines": ["Might put some dollar 'pon head", "Or just turn up the place instead"], "meaning": "To “put money on someone’s head” means to offer a reward or bounty for someone to be harmed or killed. It typically implies that someone is willing to pay a sum of money to have another person injured or eliminated.\n\nDrake here is contemplating putting out a hit on his enemies post the beef or just continuing to make hit records like he has always done in his career and becoming more successful than them, thus killing them with success instead.", "annotation_id": "32621545", "url": "https://genius.com/32621545", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "I have a watch face just like dem But me nah watch face like dem", "vault_lines": ["I have a watch face just like dem", "But me nah watch face like dem"], "meaning": "Drake here appears to be alluding that many of his so called friends have “many faces” just like some of his expensive watch collections.\n\nHaving many faces is often a metaphorical expression that means a person has different sides to their personality or behaviour. This can refer to someone who adapts or changes their demeanour depending on the situation, or it could imply that they are not fully transparent, perhaps being deceptive or hiding their true self.\n\nThis may also be a jab at Future as even on Push Ups Drake had this bar:\n“Backstage in my city it was friendzone” pointing out that some of the people now going at him, supposedly Future, had been recently in Toronto claiming to be close friends with Drake while performing there, but once they were back across the border, they are starting to diss him.\n\nAlso on More M’s Drake rapped:\n“Niggas ain’t got love for the boy, so they fake it\nCrack a couple jokes to some bitches on some snake shit\nBut if I send a verse to they ass, then they’ll take it\nShoot a video, arm around me like we aces\nOr pop out at my shows, jump around with me on stages’’\nWhich also appears to be Drake taking shots at Future for the songs they did together in 2022 but beefing with him in private before the release of “We don’t trust you”.", "annotation_id": "32621522", "url": "https://genius.com/32621522", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Run treadmill dey inna di GoodLife? You wanna work up a sweat in a Life Time?", "vault_lines": ["Run treadmill dey inna di GoodLife?", "You wanna work up a sweat in a Life Time?"], "meaning": "Drake references GoodLife and LifeTime, two popular gym chains. GoodLife being a large Canadian chain, gives a nod to the Ethiopian girls in Toronto.", "annotation_id": "32619962", "url": "https://genius.com/32619962", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Might put some dollar ’pon head Or just turn up the place instead", "vault_lines": ["Might put some dollar 'pon head", "Or just turn up the place instead"], "meaning": "“Might put some dollar ‘pon head” insinuating that he might put a bounty on someone’s head for someone to collect by killing the person.\n\n“Or just turn up the place instead”\nOr he might go kill the person who would’ve gotten the bounty placed on him himself, from a perspective to maybe save money or his hatred is so fueled that he wants to do it himself and not get someone else to do it.", "annotation_id": "32619431", "url": "https://genius.com/32619431", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Oromo girls, Tigray girls, all of my Amhara girls Habesha girl, you’re my kind, my kind", "vault_lines": ["Oromo girls, Tigray girls, all of my Amhara girls", "Habesha girl, you're my kind, my kind"], "meaning": "Drake has consistently expressed admiration for Ethiopian women in his music, and his lyrics in “Blue Green Red” continue that tradition. By referencing Ethiopia’s three major ethnic groups—Oromo, Tigray, and Amhara—Drake acknowledges the country’s cultural diversity and the beauty of women from these communities. The term “Habesha” specifically refers to people from Ethiopia’s northern highlands, and by mentioning “Habesha girls,” Drake pays homage to the rich cultural heritage of the region.\n\nDrake’s appreciation for Ethiopian women is not new; it has been evident in his music for years. On his second mixtape, Comeback Season, in the track “Where to Now”, he raps, “Ethiopian girl, Ethiopian girl with yo long curly hair and yo big ass bootay / Reading all them pro black, pro female books / Just let ‘em know that you ain’t no groupie.” Here, Drake celebrates not only the physical beauty but also the intellectual depth of Ethiopian women.\n\nIn “Over Here”, Drake continues  this theme with the lyrics, “It can’t be that hard to find us / Go to Jungle, pick up my Ethiopian goddess / That’s when words spread, a Bugatti is in the projects,” further emphasising his high regard for Ethiopian women, portraying them as rare and precious.\n\nSimilarly, in “Poetic Justice” featuring Kendrick Lamar, Drake references a “young East African girl,” rapping, “I was trying to put you on game, put you on a plane, take you and your momma to the motherland; I could do it,” highlighting his deep connection to East African culture, particularly Ethiopian heritage.\n\nDrake’s consistent references to Ethiopian women across these tracks underscore his genuine admiration for their beauty, intellect, and cultural pride.", "annotation_id": "32619310", "url": "https://genius.com/32619310", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "I got a show out in London, when?", "vault_lines": ["I got a show out in London, when?"], "meaning": "Drake might be suggesting that he will be performing in London later in 2024 along with Europe as he announced in his Stake livestream in December 2023.\nHe seemed to have been teasing the European Tour on his finsta, plottttwistttttt, named “NO ONE LIKES US”", "annotation_id": "32611182", "url": "https://genius.com/32611182", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Badmind gyal can’t friend up with me, friend up with me, no", "vault_lines": ["Badmind gyal can't friend up with me, no", "Badmind gyal can't friend up with me, friend up with me, no"], "meaning": "This line resonates strongly with Vybz Kartel’s song “Badmind” Both artists are addressing the issue of envy and fake friendships, specifically rejecting those who harbor negative feelings like jealousy and bad intentions, commonly referred to as “badmind” in Jamaican Patois. Drake is saying that regardless of physical attraction or chemistry, he refuses to engage with a woman who has malicious “badmind” intentions.", "annotation_id": "32606095", "url": "https://genius.com/32606095", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Badmind gyal can’t friend up with me, no", "vault_lines": ["Badmind gyal can't friend up with me, no", "Badmind gyal can't friend up with me, friend up with me, no"], "meaning": "This line resonates strongly with Vybz Kartel’s song “Badmind” Both artists are addressing the issue of envy and fake friendships, specifically rejecting those who harbor negative feelings like jealousy and bad intentions, commonly referred to as “badmind” in Jamaican Patois. Drake is saying that regardless of physical attraction or chemistry, he refuses to engage with a woman who has malicious “badmind” intentions.", "annotation_id": "32606027", "url": "https://genius.com/32606027", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Badmind gyal can’t friend up with me, no", "vault_lines": ["Badmind gyal can't friend up with me, no", "Badmind gyal can't friend up with me, friend up with me, no"], "meaning": "This line resonates strongly with Vybz Kartel’s song “Badmind” Both artists are addressing the issue of envy and fake friendships, specifically rejecting those who harbor negative feelings like jealousy and bad intentions, commonly referred to as “badmind” in Jamaican Patois. Drake is saying that regardless of physical attraction or chemistry, he refuses to engage with a woman who has malicious “badmind” intentions.", "annotation_id": "32605991", "url": "https://genius.com/32605991", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "How many summers did I run? Like ten", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, how many summers did I run? Like ten", "How many summers did I run? Like ten"], "meaning": "Drake has been releasing songs that control the summer for many years now, such as Controlla (2016) and What’s Next (2021).\n\nThis can also be seen as a shot at DJ Mustard, who owns a record label called 10 Summers, which is also the namesake of his first studio album. Mustard went on a Twitter rant in early August 2024 mentioning Drake after his album, Faith of a Mustard Seed, sold 18,000 copies first week, calling Drake the “Malcom X of white people” and referring to his fans as bots.", "annotation_id": "32601363", "url": "https://genius.com/32601363", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Yeah, four gunshot, mi nuh dead", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, four gunshot, mi nuh dead"], "meaning": "A potential reference to the 4 solo diss tracks launched at Drake by Kendrick Lamar during their Feud ( “Euphoria”,“6:16 in LA”,“Meet the Grahams”,“Not Like Us”) and how despite the perceived and speculated damage, Drake’s career is far from over.", "annotation_id": "32591814", "url": "https://genius.com/32591814", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Yeah, gotta lay down when you make bed Gotta let some tears just shed Since we blew up and got green I gotta leave some of y’all on read", "vault_lines": ["I gotta leave some of y'all on read", "Gotta lay down when you make bed", "Yeah, gotta lay down when you make bed", "Gotta let some tears just shed", "Since we blew up and got green"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing the idiom “you’ve made your bed, now lie in it”. This idiom means someone must accept the consequences of their actions.\n\nThis could also be a reference to his 8am in Charlotte, where he says,\n\nWe all gotta lay in the bed we make, but that couldn’t be Drake\n\nThe title of the track is also explained here: (blue) up, got green, left on (red).\n\nThose who do Drake wrong must accept their consequences. Since he got green (money), he is able to cut these people off (leaving them on read).", "annotation_id": "32589817", "url": "https://genius.com/32589817", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Please write my father and tell him I found some fortune and fame They’re gonna try and behead me and speak on the family name", "vault_lines": ["Please write my father and tell him I found some fortune and fame", "They're gonna try and behead me and speak on the family name"], "meaning": "Drake is just trying to make good music. His father already knows he’s a gold mine but telling us to write his father and tell him, seems he’s making reference to a diss track by Kendrick  in which kendrick called out Drakes family.\nIn the first verse, he started by saying, Please ring my mother and tell her and then in the second verse he said, Please write my father and tell him.\nThe next line They’re gonna try and behead me can be likened to what Jay-Z said in lights up, in which he warns Drake about how his opps will come for his head with silly rap feuds, and try to distract him from his goals.\nThe final piece that connects this verse to the diss by kendrick is the next line, which reads And speak on the family name. Drakes family name is Graham and one of the diss track by Kendrick was titled: Meet the Grahams", "annotation_id": "32583839", "url": "https://genius.com/32583839", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "They make lyrics for who? Men We make gyal come, then come again", "vault_lines": ["They make lyrics for who? Men", "We make gyal come, then come again"], "meaning": "In this section of the record, Drake uses a flow inspired by Ranking Tiger’s 1991 dancehall classic “When.”", "annotation_id": "32583797", "url": "https://genius.com/32583797", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Fi the mandem, fi the gyaldem", "vault_lines": ["Fi the mandem, fi the gyaldem"], "meaning": "Drake uses a london accent here. He asked the questions where the mandem? Where the gyaldem?\nMandem refers to men, guys, male homies.\nGyaldem refers to ladies, women, female homies", "annotation_id": "32583739", "url": "https://genius.com/32583739", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "What the clock inna London? Yeah, Big Ben", "vault_lines": ["What the clock inna London? Yeah, Big Ben"], "meaning": "The Big Ben is a large clock tower located in London, England. It is one of England’s most famous tourist attractions.\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake has mentioned a British landmark, in fact, 2017’s More Life is filled with references to British culture.\n\nDrake has also went to London a few times before, example being to perfom at the 02 Arena in London.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dizcts_P7vE", "annotation_id": "32583358", "url": "https://genius.com/32583358", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Fi the 6 side, fi the 6 side, fi the 6 side", "vault_lines": ["Fi the 6 side, fi the 6 side, fi the 6 side"], "meaning": "“Six” (6) refers to Drake’s hometown of Toronto, as “6” is short for Toronto area codes “416” and “647.”\n\nIt also refers to Toronto’s six boroughs, being East York, Etobicoke, North York, Scarborough, Toronto, and York.", "annotation_id": "32583208", "url": "https://genius.com/32583208", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blue Green Red", "line": "Thirty-six in the chest, okay Twenty-eight in the waist, okay Forty-six in the hips, come swing my way", "vault_lines": ["Thirty-six in the chest, okay", "Twenty-eight in the waist, okay", "Forty-six in the hips, come swing my way"], "meaning": "Drake’s mention of measurements here nods to the curvaceous figures often celebrated in Ethiopian women, who are admired for their distinctive and voluptuous shapes. This appreciation is further highlighted by the previous lyrics, “Oromo girl, Tigray girl, all of my Amhara girls,” where Drake acknowledges the beauty and diversity of Ethiopian women from various ethnic groups, reflecting a broader admiration for their physical attributes and cultural heritage.", "annotation_id": "32582833", "url": "https://genius.com/32582833", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
