{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faded", "line": "I’m feelin’ like I’m faded-faded-f-faded-f-faded-faded-f-faded-f-faded faded-f-faded-f-faded I’m feelin’ like I’m faded-faded-f-faded-f-faded-faded-f-faded-f-faded faded-f-faded-f-faded", "vault_lines": ["Maybe I'm hearing things 'cause I'm feelin' like I'm faded", "I'm feelin' like I'm—"], "meaning": "“Faded” is a word used for when one who is high/stoned but also used when simply under the intoxicated of any drug.\n\nDrake feels as though he is at the point beyond comprehension similar to that euphoria, sort of high on life. He uses the stuttering f’s as a wordplay on actually being faded.", "annotation_id": "151678", "url": "https://genius.com/151678", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faded", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 1, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-faded-lyrics", "genius_title": "Faded"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IMY2", "line": "Real niggas in life survive (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Real niggas in life survive (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Kid Cudi has talked about depression and suicidal tendencies throughout his entire discography. This line is quoted from his song “Don’t Play This Song”:\n\n“Reckless and young, and my, my mom’s calling,\nThink I should hit decline\nI’m numb faced while I’m thinking about suicide”\n\nWhen he is saying “Real niggas in life survive”, he is stating that there are better times ahead for those that continue to push forward through the terrible.", "annotation_id": "24235667", "url": "https://genius.com/24235667", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IMY2", "line": "No stress is on my brain, no worries here, I’m chosen (Yeah, yeah) Made another way, my nigga, watch me go shine", "vault_lines": ["No stress is on my brain, no worries here, I'm chosen (Yeah, yeah)", "Made another way, my nigga, watch me go shine"], "meaning": "Ever since the 2018 release of “Reborn” on breakthrough album kids see ghost, kid Cudi’s music has expressed themes of rejuvenation, finding “another way”, and having “no stress” as he has learned to deal with the demons of his past in a positive way.\n\nCudi has also expressed the theme of being “chosen” many times throughout his discography, including his newest album titled “man on the moon 3: the chosen.” He knows his purpose here on earth is greater than himself, he is chosen for a bigger cause.\n\nEven though he may miss his past girl, he has found peace moving forward in his life", "annotation_id": "23878867", "url": "https://genius.com/23878867", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IMY2", "line": "I think that’s what life is about Truly findin’ yourself And then closin’ your eyes and dyin’ in your sleep", "vault_lines": ["I think that's what life is about", "And then closin' your eyes and dyin' in your sleep"], "meaning": "The song starts with posthumous vocals from the late rapper Juice WRLD. They were taken from an August 2019 interview Juice had with hip-hop interviewer, Montreality:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/8-BLWt_GdE8?t=670\n\nAlthough Juice and Drake never had much public interaction, Drake posted a tribute to him on Instagram, following the rapper’s death in December 2019. He posted a picture of Juice with the caption:\n\nI would like to see all the younger talent live longer and I hate waking up hearing another story filled with blessings was cut short 😢\n\nJuice also regarded himself as “one of Drake’s biggest fans you could ever find,” which therefore likely explains his appearance on this song.", "annotation_id": "23874521", "url": "https://genius.com/23874521", "state": "accepted", "votes": 334, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IMY2", "line": "Undisclosed amounts straight from Universal", "vault_lines": ["Undisclosed amounts straight from Universal"], "meaning": "Drake is signed to one of the largest record labels in the world, Universal Records. It was previously rumored by executives at the label that Drake has an “unlimited budget” for his projects. Here, Drake seems to touch on that.", "annotation_id": "23874520", "url": "https://genius.com/23874520", "state": "pending", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IMY2", "line": "Bold niggas gettin’ real italics ’round me", "vault_lines": ["Bold niggas gettin' real italics 'round me"], "meaning": "Drake here is referring to how some people, otherwise known to be tough, show themselves to actually be quite the opposite while around him. He draws (no pun intended) an analogy here to bold and italic typeface, as in a word processor, in order to make his point.", "annotation_id": "23874508", "url": "https://genius.com/23874508", "state": "pending", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "IMY2", "status": "done", "targets": 5, "clicked": 5, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-imy2-lyrics", "genius_title": "IMY2"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mention Me", "line": "My yiy’s too fried", "vault_lines": ["My yiy's too fried"], "meaning": "Yiy is Patois for “eye.” It’s slang for getting some sleep/rest. especially after a big party or function.\n\nHere Drake could be suggesting that he needs rest, or his eyes are literally fried from the drugs he has been taking.", "annotation_id": "27027293", "url": "https://genius.com/27027293", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mention Me", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 1, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-mention-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "Mention Me"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "I do not forget a thing, I’m patient, it’s a gift (yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I do not forget a thing, I'm patient, it's a gift (yeah)"], "meaning": "This line underscores Drake’s meticulous nature when it comes to handling subliminal disses thrown his way. He views his patience and his awareness of the rap landscape as gifts, allowing him to retaliate at the opportune moment.\n\nEven Kanye West, who he had a long-running feud with, spoke on these antics in an interview with Drink Champs:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/PHneOjmOB1c?feature=share\n\nDrake’s strategic approach to dealing with subliminal disses is evident in various tracks throughout his career:\n\nI heard the lil' lil' homie talking reckless in Vibe / That’s quite a platform you chose, you should’ve kept it inside “6PM in New York”\n\nContext: Drake calls out Tyga for his comments in an interview with VIBE magazine where Tyga shared his dislike towards Drake: “I don’t like Drake as a person. He’s just fake to me.”\n\nDrake is really fond of this gift of his and recurrently references it:\n\nI’ve been hearing all the things you say through the grapevine “Do Not Disturb”", "annotation_id": "32364942", "url": "https://genius.com/32364942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "Heard you hit up so and so, that name don’t ring a bell, nah", "vault_lines": ["Heard you hit up so and so, that name don't ring a bell, nah"], "meaning": "While Drake doesn’t name who he is referring to, He is likely referring to Pusha T or Kanye West. This line suggests that the person in question tried to reach out to an individual, possibly in an attempt to gather information, form alliances, or create discord.\n\nBy dismissing the significance of that certain someone that the other camp is trying to associate themselves with, Drake reinforces the notion that his rivals and their allies are inconsequential in the grand scheme of things. In the context of “Mob Ties”, he could saying that his adversaries are not as connected as him.\n\n\n\n  Picture of (from the left) Drake, his father, J. Prince Jr. and J. Prince; J. Prince is a prominent music mogul, and  mob-figure in Houston", "annotation_id": "32185297", "url": "https://genius.com/32185297", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "[Verse 1]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "On 28 May 2024, a reference track for “Mob Ties” performed by Vory emerged. The cadence and flow is near identical and features very similar lyrics to those used in the first verse, Pre-Chorus and Chorus:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/Zd9CD5XQJbc?si=uuVinv8jk2ros0Hy\n\nAlthough Vory is a credited writer on “Mob Ties”, the large extent of his contributions may be surprising. Prior to the reference track, DJ Akademiks claimed that Vory “wrote nothing” for this track, which is now proven to be false.\n\nDrake using writers has been an ongoing critique of his pedigree as an emcee ever since 2015, when a few reference tracks from a ghostwriter, Quentin Miller, recorded for Drake on songs like “10 Bands” and his verse on Meek Mill’s “R.I.C.O.” were released by Funk Flex during an ongoing beef between Drake and Meek.\n\nAdditionally, in April 2024 a reference track for “Jumbotron Shit Poppin” performed by Lil Yatchy emerged.", "annotation_id": "31893702", "url": "https://genius.com/31893702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "In a rocket and that bitch ain’t got no tags (Skrr, skrr)", "vault_lines": ["In a rocket and that bitch ain't got no tags (Skrr, skrr)"], "meaning": "Following the lines about Mercedes-Benz models, he is now likely mentioning the tuned version of the Mercedes S-Class by tuning company Brabus, the Rocket 900.", "annotation_id": "15613566", "url": "https://genius.com/15613566", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "They want me gone but don’t know why It’s too late for all that lovey-dovey shit I’m your brother shit, all that other shit It’s too late for all that It’s too late for all that, ayy It’s too late for all that lovey-dovey shit I’m your brother shit, all that other shit It’s too late for all that, ayy It’s too late for all that", "vault_lines": ["They want me gone but don't know why", "It's too late for all that lovey-dovey shit", "I'm your brother shit, all that other shit", "It's too late for all that", "It's too late for all that, ayy"], "meaning": "This is another shot at Kanye West and the GOOD Music label. Drake originally had respect for him and another signee, Pusha T before the beef and now he believes it’s too late to start over with their mutual relationship. Perhaps this could reference a tweet made by Kanye that supposedly called off the feud between him, Push, and Drake:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1002784133888688128?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E1002784133888688128&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.highsnobiety.com%2Fp%2Fkanye-west-drake-pusha-t-beef-over%2F\n\nSimilarly, he felt the same way about Meek Mill, and stated as such in his diss song “Back 2 Back”:\n\nI’m not sure what it was that really made y'all mad\nBut I guess this is what I gotta do to make y'all rap", "annotation_id": "14929317", "url": "https://genius.com/14929317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "I fuck with the mob and I got ties (Lotta ties, lotta ties) Knock you off to pay their tithes (Do doo)", "vault_lines": ["I fuck with the mob and I got ties (Lotta ties, lotta ties)", "Knock you off to pay their tithes (Do doo)"], "meaning": "Despite not having come from the street life like many other rappers, Drake still has connections to gangsters who can get their hands dirty for him if need be.\n\nDrake’s close affiliate, J. Prince, the boss off Rap-a-Lot Records, has been both feared and respected in the streets as well as the rap game for decades.", "annotation_id": "14916815", "url": "https://genius.com/14916815", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "I’m runnin’ a blitz (Ayy)", "vault_lines": ["I'm runnin' a blitz (Ayy)"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the American football play called the “Blitz”. “Blitzing” is used by the defensive line and involves a greater than normal amount of defending players attacking the offending quarterback. The objective of the play is to quickly take him down, or overwhelm the offensive line and force the quarterback into making a potentially inaccurate pass.\n\n\n\nDrake could also be referring “blitz” to a military context, as it also means an intensive or sudden military attack. Either way, Drake insinuates that he’s taking down the competition, and pressuring them to rush their next move.", "annotation_id": "14901329", "url": "https://genius.com/14901329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "My bitch in Chanel now Your bitch in Escada", "vault_lines": ["My bitch in Chanel now", "Your bitch in Escada"], "meaning": "Chanel is a French company founded in 1910, while Escada was founded 68 years later in Germany.\n\nBoth are luxury fashion brands which sell women clothing, shoes, and other accessories. However, Chanel is known to be more expensive and more unique than Escada, as they offer haute couture services.\n\nDrake uses the two brands as a status symbol in this lyric. He implies that because his woman is wearing a more exclusive brand, that he is better at providing for his romantic partners than others.", "annotation_id": "14901321", "url": "https://genius.com/14901321", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "S Class, G Class, lotta class (Sss, sss)", "vault_lines": ["S Class, G Class, lotta class (Sss, sss)"], "meaning": "“S-Class” refers to those models that fall under the S-Class banner for German automaker Mercedes-Benz. S-Class models include the S450 Sedan and the AMG S65 Sedan.\n\n“G-Class” references the G-Class model of Mercedes-Benz. G-Class models include the G550 4x4 SUV and the AMG G 65 SUV.\n\nDrake’s speaking to the fact that he is classy and has plenty of models of Mercedes-Benz from the S and G-Class which are depicted as classy.", "annotation_id": "14889116", "url": "https://genius.com/14889116", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "GLE, ‘cause that Lambo movin’ fast", "vault_lines": ["GLE, 'cause that Lambo movin' fast"], "meaning": "GLE is an elegant 2018 Mercedes coupé. Drizzy tells us that he drives a GLE when he doesn’t feel the need to drive a supercar, such as those made by Lamborghini.", "annotation_id": "14886120", "url": "https://genius.com/14886120", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "Louis bags in exchange for body bags, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Louis bags in exchange for body bags, yeah"], "meaning": "Louis Vuitton is a French fashion house known for a variety of luxury items—Drake is saying that he gives valuable things to people who are willing to do his dirty work.\n\nDrake has collaborated with Louis Vuitton before. His song “Signs” was made to be featured at the brand’s 2018 Spring/Summer Collection.\n\nThis could also be a reference to late Floridian artist XXXTENTACION, who was shot and murdered in June 2018. Reports claimed that his killers fled the scene with a designer Louis Vuitton bag.", "annotation_id": "14877427", "url": "https://genius.com/14877427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 263, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "Sick of this shit, move to the Ritz", "vault_lines": ["I'm sick of this shit (Sick, sick)", "Sick of this shit, move to the Ritz"], "meaning": "The Ritz-Carlton is a brand of luxury hotels and resorts with global presence.\n\nThe Ritz also provides residences in the form of condos and penthouses for sale. While waiting for his home to be built, Drake moved into a condo at the Ritz in downtown Toronto.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?time_continue=6&v=oceD6MjoRK8", "annotation_id": "14877383", "url": "https://genius.com/14877383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "I’m not with the ra-ra I am a Dada", "vault_lines": ["I'm not with the ra-ra"], "meaning": "Da-da is short for “Don Dada,” which is patois for “crime lord,” whereas “ra-ra” means trash talk. Drake implies that he does not indulge in trash talk and is a man of action—similar to a Don.\n\nDrake has been known to use a lot of patois in his music, noticeably on his March 2017 project More Life, where numerous songs use Jamaican slang—tracks such as “Madiba Riddim” and “Blem” are prime examples. In 2017, Vox broke down Drake’s use of patois:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?time_continue=88&v=a3-GJB2htE4", "annotation_id": "14877222", "url": "https://genius.com/14877222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 124, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "Lead the league in scorin’, man, but look at my assists (Shh)", "vault_lines": ["Lead the league in scorin', man, but look at my assists (Shh)"], "meaning": "Drake is no stranger to sports metaphors. Here, he uses a basketball metaphor to show his dominance in the game. Drake is a known fan of the sport, even serving as the global ambassador of the Toronto Raptors, his hometown NBA.\n\nHere, Drizzy discusses how he is simultaneously putting up huge numbers for himself, evident from the success of singles like “God’s Plan” and “Nice For What,” while also supporting up-and-comers like BlocBoy JB (“Look Alive”) and Lil Baby (“Yes Indeed”).", "annotation_id": "14876456", "url": "https://genius.com/14876456", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "I just gave ’em two for forty million like Chappelle (Two)", "vault_lines": ["I just gave 'em two for forty million like Chappelle (Two)"], "meaning": "Dave Chappelle famously walked away from a 50 million dollar deal with Comedy Central for the extension of Chappelle’s Show in 2004.\n\nHe returned to stand up comedy specials after 12 years away in 2017, inking a deal reportedly worth $60 million dollars with Netflix for three comedy specials.\n\nDrake may be stating that he gave them (either us the general public, or the record label) two or a double album for the price of $40 million, similar to Chapelle’s deal with Netflix.", "annotation_id": "14876452", "url": "https://genius.com/14876452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 100, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "Standin’ over coffin with a hammer and a nail (Two)", "vault_lines": ["Standin' over coffin with a hammer and a nail (Two)"], "meaning": "A hammer and nails are traditionally the tools used to seal a coffin once the corpse has been placed in it.\n\nThis bar is very similar to another one of Drake’s from “No Tellin'”:\n\nYou pick the casket, I’ll put the nail through it", "annotation_id": "14876438", "url": "https://genius.com/14876438", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "Yes I be with Future but I like to reminisce (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Yes I be with Future but I like to reminisce (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Drake is close with fellow rapper Future. The duo has collaborated numerous times, perhaps most notably on their 2015 mixtape, What A Time to Be Alive. Additionally, they worked together on this album, Scorpion, on the track “Blue Tint.”\n\nAlternatively, Drake could be referring to his long term friend, manager, and tour DJ, Future the Prince. Drake has mentioned Future the Prince before in his music, notably on his 2015 track “6PM in New York,” rapping:\n\nIf me and Future hadn’t made it with this rappin'\nWe prolly be out in Silicon tryna get our billions on\n\nDrake’s wordplay here refers to the contrast between Future’s stage name and reminiscing, which is the act of fondly thinking about the past.", "annotation_id": "14876401", "url": "https://genius.com/14876401", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "line": "Twenty-nine is comin’, they on edge when I cook (Cook)", "vault_lines": ["Twenty-nine is comin', they on edge when I cook (Cook)"], "meaning": "Scorpion was released on June 29, 2018. Drake claims that his peers and opposition in the rap game were apprehensive as the date approached. Though he announced the release month months in advance, the release date was announced with an Instagram post of the album cover on June 14.\n\nInterestingly, Drake’s previous album Views also released on the 29th of the month, specifically on April 29, 2016.", "annotation_id": "14876368", "url": "https://genius.com/14876368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Mob Ties", "status": "done", "targets": 18, "clicked": 18, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-mob-ties-lyrics", "genius_title": "Mob Ties"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 2 Tracklist", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-ovo-sound-radio-episode-2-tracklist-lyrics", "genius_title": "OVO Sound Radio Episode 2 Tracklist"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "[Intro]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the clean version, for some reason, Drake adds two “Yeah” ad-libs: one before his ad-lib “Ayy”, and one after it.", "annotation_id": "41020880", "url": "https://genius.com/41020880", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "If my emotions are showin’, it’s only because I’m ahead of what they got goin’", "vault_lines": ["If my emotions are showin', it's only because I'm ahead of what they got goin'"], "meaning": "Drake thinks of himself as nonchalant and if in any manner he ends up showing emotion it’s because he sees ahead and probably knows the damage it will do in the future.", "annotation_id": "39825214", "url": "https://genius.com/39825214", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "The judge tryna move so racist, we gotta go beat some cases before we show faces And all I do is shut down stages, my face never ages, I must be in God’s good graces", "vault_lines": ["The judge tryna move so racist, we gotta go beat some cases before we show faces", "And all I do is shut down stages, my face never ages, I must be in God's good graces"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION:\n\nDrake is directly addressing the viral photos from Michael Rubin’s annual all-white 4th of July party in the Hamptons (July 2024). In the images, he appeared noticeably tired and aged, which sparked widespread memes claiming the Kendrick beef had “aged him” dramatically.", "annotation_id": "39796921", "url": "https://genius.com/39796921", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "I’m tryna stay laid back ‘cause you know the hot heads with me and they wanna get the payback goin’", "vault_lines": ["I'm tryna stay laid back 'cause you know the hot heads with me and they wanna get the payback goin'"], "meaning": "Drake appears to be referring to the fact that he has had to distance himself from certain members of his camp, most notably Top5, because they seemed more willing to get active against his rivals than he was.\n\nIncidents such as Top5 reportedly being around Kendrick’s hotel, along with other OVO affiliates making their presence felt in Los Angeles during the height of the feud, give this line additional context. Drake touches on a similar idea in “Make Them Know” when he tells TV Gucci, “Ayo, Gucci, get back in the ride ‘cause that’s definitely not the guy. Mistaken identity mixed with adrenaline, almost just kissed you goodbye.”\n\nThe irony is that despite being the central figure in the beef, Drake often portrays himself as the one telling his associates to “lowe it” rather than escalate things.", "annotation_id": "40129371", "url": "https://genius.com/40129371", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "We can fly downtown just us two", "vault_lines": ["we can fly downtown just us two"], "meaning": "Drake borrows from 2Pac’s approach on “Me and My Girlfriend,” which personifies a firearm as a romantic partner. The line “we can fly downtown just us two” reads like a romantic outing. The subtext, however, is that Drake implies he doesn’t need security because he’s armed — “us two” meaning him and his gun. That double meaning is reinforced by the video, which shows him with a firearm at the corresponding timestamp.", "annotation_id": "40102349", "url": "https://genius.com/40102349", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "I haven’t seen bro in a while, but that nigga wild, I’m not in a rush to", "vault_lines": ["I haven't seen bro in a while, but that nigga wild, I'm not in a rush to"], "meaning": "“Haven’t seen bro in a while”:\nDrake is talking about his long-time friend and fellow Toronto rapper, Top 5.\n\n“But that nigga wild”:\nAnyone who follows Top 5 knows this is an understatement. The guy has a reputation for being an absolute loose cannon—beating a murder case, running into street beef, and generally acting like he has nothing to lose.\n\n“I’m not in a rush to”:\nThis is the punchline. Drake is basically saying he loves the guy, but he’s not exactly booking a flight to go kick it with him. It’s a respectful nod, but with a healthy dose of “you’re too hot to touch right now.”\n\nDrake and Top 5 got a real bond. You don’t pay for someone’s lawyer if you don’t mess with them. But even Drake knows you gotta keep the wild boy at arm’s length sometimes.", "annotation_id": "39944375", "url": "https://genius.com/39944375", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "My young boys don’t do it for clout, if they do it, they cover they head and they mouth", "vault_lines": ["My young boys don't do it for clout, if they do it, they cover they head and they mouth"], "meaning": "In an era where street credibility is performed online, where everything gets documented and posted on socials for clout, Drake is saying his circle operates by a completely different and older code. when they handle business, they handle it quietly with no trail.\n\n“Cover they head and they mouth” serves two meanings; a practical description of discretion as in using ski masks but covering your mouth also means not talking/snitching.\n\n\n\n The cover photo for Drake’s Dark Lane Demo Tapes shows him standing in the dark, wearing a black ski mask", "annotation_id": "39832809", "url": "https://genius.com/39832809", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "My killies My youngins put locks on the city, so niggas be shook to come out when it’s litty T, that’s the crodie, so free him,", "vault_lines": ["My youngins put locks on the city, so niggas be shook to come out when it's litty", "T, that's the crodie, so free him,"], "meaning": "In Drake’s telling, his young crew had the city so locked down that people were afraid to come outside when things got dangerous. “Litty” here is slang for of hot/not safe, but it is also the title of a Meek Mill record that Tory Lanez absolutely scorched on.\n\nDrake shouts out Tory again within the span of just a couple of bars, twice petitioning for the release of the man serving ten years.", "annotation_id": "39818008", "url": "https://genius.com/39818008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "I don’t need security to ride ’round town,", "vault_lines": ["I don't need security to ride 'round town,"], "meaning": "After Kendrick Lamar’s concert in Toronto, reports and viral videos showed him leaving the venue with an unusually large police and security convoy (about 25 to 30 vehicles) and rushing straight to the airport to fly back to New York.", "annotation_id": "39815113", "url": "https://genius.com/39815113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "I’ll beat a producer like, “What’s the BPM?” I cannot de-escalate things if we see ’em", "vault_lines": ["I'll beat a producer like, \"What's the BPM?\" I cannot de-escalate things if we see 'em"], "meaning": "“BPM” stands for beats per minute, the tempo measurement every producer works with. Drake is saying he’d rough up a producer mid-session asking about the technical specs of a beat.\n\nThis is certainly a reference to Metro Boomin – once a frequent Drake collaborator who became one of the main instigators of the 2024 beef,  executive producing We Don’t Trust You and We Still Don’t Trust You with Future. Both projects included diss tracks aimed at Drake, most prominently Kendrick Lamar’s opening shot “Like That”. And we can’t forget about “BBL Drizzy BPM 150,” a taunting instrumental whose title ties into the BPM joke.\n\n\n\nDrake is making it clear that If their paths cross, it is gonna get ugly.", "annotation_id": "39811784", "url": "https://genius.com/39811784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "All I know is the mandem are pisst (Pisst)", "vault_lines": ["All I know is the mandem are pisst (Pisst)"], "meaning": "In Toronto, “mandem” is used to refer to a group of men, usually one’s friends. The term comes from Jamaican Patois and is widley used across Toronto and the UK, both of which have strong Caribbean linguistic influence.\n\n“Pissed” and “pisst” also carry distinct meanings in Toronto. While sometimes used interchangably, “pisst” is often used to describe someone who is fired up, amped up, or moving with intense energy rather than anger or frustration.", "annotation_id": "39808070", "url": "https://genius.com/39808070", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "Say a whole lot, but we saw you and we had you beggin’ it out, ’member that shit?", "vault_lines": ["Say a whole lot, but we saw you and we had you beggin' it out, 'member that shit?"], "meaning": "A “Metro Boomin / Future” Camp Affiliate: Some fans point to the track’s broader targets. Because Drake uses the song to address internet chatter and people “talking a whole lot” on forums, it is also viewed as a blanket shot toward the extended circle of artists who teamed up against him, suggesting one of them folded when confronted in person.", "annotation_id": "39806783", "url": "https://genius.com/39806783", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "Pullin’ out the cash on camera, waterproof iPhone case, bro splash on camera", "vault_lines": ["Pullin' out the cash on camera, waterproof iPhone case, bro splash on camera"], "meaning": "“splash” = throwing money, luxury movement, or big impact energy\n“on camera” ties it to public visibility again\n\nSo “splash” can mean:\n\nliterally splashing water (luxury setting like pool/yacht scenes as pictured in his music video “Sticky”)\n\nfiguratively flexing his wealth (money “splashes”)\n\nstreet slang overlap with “drip” culture\n\nmake a splash: To become suddenly successful, famous, or to attract a lot of attention.\n\nsplash of color: A small area of bright, contrasting color in an environment or outfit.\n\nfront-page splash: A highly prominent, attention-grabbing news story in a newspaper or broadcast.\n\nMeaning:\n\nmaking a noticeable, flashy impact while being filmed and constantly under media attention—turning lifestyle into content", "annotation_id": "39796854", "url": "https://genius.com/39796854", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "Unless you know the G-way handshake when we go to shake hands, man can’t trust you", "vault_lines": ["Unless you know the G-way handshake when we go to shake hands, man can't trust you"], "meaning": "A reference to the Galloway neighborhood in Scarborough, Ontario, known locally as G-Way to the people who grew up there. His Reps Up affiliates – including Chubbs, his longtime head of security – are from the area.\n\nYou are only one of his if you know the G-Way handshake.\n\n\n\nIn the music video, Drake demonstrates the handshake himself as the bar plays out", "annotation_id": "39796187", "url": "https://genius.com/39796187", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "Swear there’s some shit I’m forgettin’ about, 03 up the slime, when they lettin’ him out? (They did)", "vault_lines": ["Swear there's some shit I'm forgettin' about, 03 up the slime, when they lettin' him out? (They did)"], "meaning": "Drake often uses “three” in place of “free” when talking about freeing someone from prison. He’s done this before on “All the Parties” with lines like:\n\nThree Baby M out the shackle and chain\n\nand\n\nIt’s still three the guys ’til they free men\n\nYoung Thug (aka “Slime”) was a central figure in the high‑profile  YSL RICO case  after his 2022 arrest on gang‑related charges. He spent more than two years in jail before accepting a plea deal and being released in late 2024 (“They did” — an ad‑lib by Young Thug — is the icing on the cake.)\n\n\n\n“03” is a subtle nod to 03 Greedo, an LA rapper Drake has supported in the past. Greedo served several years in prison and was released in 2023.", "annotation_id": "39790816", "url": "https://genius.com/39790816", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "I don’t recognize none of that shit that he’s throwin’, but maybe where he’s from, they know it", "vault_lines": ["I don't recognize none of that shit that he's throwin', but maybe where he's from, they know it"], "meaning": "This can be interpreted as a subliminal jab toward a West Coast gang member associated with Kendrick Lamar. Many West Coast gangs are known for representing their affiliations through hand signs or “throwing up” their set. Drake dismisses the gesture by implying it carries no status, influence, or significance in his world, even if it may hold weight where the other person is from.", "annotation_id": "39787914", "url": "https://genius.com/39787914", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "I be with them guys from the 6ix that you niggas stay talkin’ on Reddit about", "vault_lines": ["I be with them guys from the 6ix that you niggas stay talkin' on Reddit about"], "meaning": "Drake’s Toronto street affiliations are well documented across the internet. For example, Torontology is a whole subreddit dedicated to breaking down the Toronto gang and music scene, where the people Drake is referencing are discussed and catalogued by people who will never actually be in those rooms.\n\nThe “Six” (or “6”) is a common nickname for Drake’s hometown of Toronto, Ontario.", "annotation_id": "39786154", "url": "https://genius.com/39786154", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "These niggas a joke and I wouldn’t wanna be ’em", "vault_lines": ["these niggas a joke and I wouldn't wanna be 'em"], "meaning": "This rebuttal is aimed especially at Kendrick , given that the title of his main diss track is “Not Like Us.” This line is also reminiscent of Drake’s bars from the leaked track “Spicy Hot”:\n\nI don’t look nothing like y'all, you right about that, it’s just like you said (What?)\nThat shit a compliment, boy, you actin' like we want to be you instead (What?)\nThat’s when I’d rather be dead (Hey)", "annotation_id": "39784507", "url": "https://genius.com/39784507", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "I can’t talk on bro case ’cause it’s sticky, free T and free Greezy,", "vault_lines": ["I can't talk on bro case 'cause it's sticky, free T and free Greezy,"], "meaning": "“T” refers to fellow Canadian rapper Tory Lanez who is currently serving a ten-year sentence for shooting Megan Thee Stallion.\n\n\n\nDrake has beenpublicly and repeatedly vocal in Tory’s support, even facing backlash for it. He previously referred to him as “T” and “T Lanez” during a stream with Adin Ross:\n\nFree up T. Free up T I know they don’t wanna hear me say that, but free up T Lanez\n\nThe “sticky” caution is about the legal specifics of the case itself being too sensitive to address publicly in detail.\n\n“Greezy Money” was the leader of the Galloway Boys, a street gang originating in the Kingston-Galloway area of Scarborough, Toronto. Greezy was convicted of first-degree murder for the benefit of a criminal organization and is currently serving a life sentence.", "annotation_id": "39779061", "url": "https://genius.com/39779061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "You pussy boy better be ready and able, I’m comin’", "vault_lines": ["You pussy boy better be ready and able, I'm comin'"], "meaning": "“Able” is a homonym for “Abel,” the first name of R&B artist The Weeknd, who was once heavily endorsed by Drake when he was breaking into the music business. Like many others, The Weeknd later chose to side against Drake during the 2024 rap civil war.", "annotation_id": "39779038", "url": "https://genius.com/39779038", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "She wanna pop ass on camera, sweetie, you can film, just don’t put— on camera", "vault_lines": ["She wanna pop ass on camera, sweetie, you can film, just don't put— on camera"], "meaning": "The “–” likely stands in for something like “my dick,” referencing the February 2024 leak of a video showing Drake’s genitals, which went viral and he clearly doesn’t want to repeat.", "annotation_id": "39778993", "url": "https://genius.com/39778993", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "People keep tellin’ me smile, but bro is on trial, there’s shit to adjust to", "vault_lines": ["People keep tellin' me smile, but bro is on trial, there's shit to adjust to"], "meaning": "This refers to the time when close friend and frequent collaborator, Young Thug was on trial, charged with allegedly being part of a criminal street gang responsible for murders, drug dealing, and illegal gun possession under the guise of his record label, YSL. Drake couldn’t express joy since Thug was undergoing trial.\n\n“Smile” is also an anagram for the word “slime” — one of Thug’s signature words.\n\nDrake expressed a similar sentiment on the 2024 song It’s Up, where he spits the following line:\n\nI can’t sleep at night with Thug at Cobb County Corrections\nI think he did enough reflectin', I think my brother learned his lesson", "annotation_id": "39778969", "url": "https://genius.com/39778969", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "Twist, twistin’ it Yeah, he’s twistin’ his fingers and lookin’ at me Twistin’ some shit up that I never seen Twistin’ it up, I don’t know what that means, twist I’m twistin’ it back ‘cause he twistin’ at me Twistin’ that 6ix in his face, twistin’ that R in his face like Styles and P Twistin’ that M and the O and the B Twistin’ the O and the C and the T and the O and the B and the E and the R and the G and the A and the N and the G Twist, ayy Yeah, twistin’ it Ayy, twistin’ it I’m twistin’ it up ‘cause he twistin’ at me", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, he's twistin' his fingers and lookin' at me", "Twistin' some shit up that I never seen", "Twistin' it up, I don't know what that means, twist", "I'm twistin' it back 'cause he twistin' at me", "Twistin' that 6ix in his face, twistin' that R in his face like Styles and P", "Twistin' that M and the O and the B", "I'm twistin' it up 'cause he twistin' at me", "Fuck that shit, man, twist, twistin' it"], "meaning": "Drake is spelling out “MOB OCTOBER GANG” letter by letter. But the delivery is unique, he’s twisting his fingers into gang signs, gesturing every affiliation he has in succession. The whole sequence is a direct response to someone who provoked him, so Drake is letting him know who he’s dealing with sign by sign.\n\nThe “6ix” is Drake’s shorthand for the city of Toronto while the “R” references the Reps Ups street and music crew formed by his day one affiliates Preme (aka P-Reign), Styles, and Chubbs.\n\n\n\nPreme (aka P-Reign) on the left and Styles on the right\n\n\n\n\nNotice how that the same hand gesture can be read as either a “6” or an upside “R.”\n\n\n\nMOB references the founder of Rap-A-Lot records, J. Prince  who is a feared Houston figure operating a mafia-style hip-hop enterprise.\n\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/f9d6c27658e2617c189070f016f255b01c08f3496be0cffc53bb3e18c18990af%2Fwfuk424f9phDrake and J. Prince Jr. throwing the Mob Ties hand sign\n\nOctober Gang is OVO (October’s Very Own) whose symbol is the owl, a shape Drake has a specific hand gesture for that his whole camp throws.", "annotation_id": "39778878", "url": "https://genius.com/39778878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "October’s Own is the label, seven-figure deal was a fable Eight-figure bought out the neighbors, nine figures turnin’ the tables", "vault_lines": ["October's Own is the label, seven-figure deal was a fable", "Eight-figure bought out the neighbors, nine figures turnin' the tables"], "meaning": "When an artist is on the come-up, a “seven-figure deal” can sound too good to be true.\n\nBack in 2016, as deals grew, he bought the $2.85 million house next door to his $7.7 million Hidden Hills “YOLO Estate.”\n\n\n\nDrake's party compound, the 'Yolo Estate\n\n\n\n\nwhat once would have been myth is now modest next to his last real-life contract with UMG, reported at roughly $400 million (a whooping nine‑figure deal.)", "annotation_id": "39778855", "url": "https://genius.com/39778855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Plot Twist", "line": "Is that Drake’s necklace? (It’s mine) Oh, you’re, you’re part of his, uh, best friends group? One of— yeah, this is our gang Aw, what is it called?", "vault_lines": ["Is that Drake's necklace? (It's mine)", "Oh, you're, you're part of his, uh, best friends group?", "One of— yeah, this is our gang", "Aw, what is it called?"], "meaning": "Drake samples a clip of his close friend Ben, professionally known as BenDaDonnn, and fellow streamer, Yonna Jay.\n\nYonna compares Ben’s necklace, gifted by Drake, to that of a child’s friendship bracelet. She teases the two grown men’s friendship, as Ben is 34 and Drake is 39.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/zLaeXcDqMZA", "annotation_id": "39775815", "url": "https://genius.com/39775815", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
