{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "It’s funny when you niggas thought it was luck", "vault_lines": ["It's funny when you niggas thought it was luck"], "meaning": "Here, Drake adresses the people thinking his success comes from luck, rather than talent.\n\nDrake previously  adressed this in his trailer for the Billboard Music Awards of 2021 where Drake won the award for Artist of the Decade.", "annotation_id": "23118278", "url": "https://genius.com/23118278", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "Pull up smooth, A.I. with the cross (Haha)", "vault_lines": ["Pull up smooth, A.I. with the cross (Haha)"], "meaning": "Allen “A.I.” Iverson was an NBA player who played in the league for 14 years and was known for his killer crossover move. Roddy is comparing his life to ball and by the looks of it he’s doin well.", "annotation_id": "22829764", "url": "https://genius.com/22829764", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "I buy diamonds when I’m under pressure (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I buy diamonds when I’m under pressure (Yeah)"], "meaning": "In order to make diamonds carbon needs to be subjected to two conditions; extreme heat and extreme pressure.\n\nSimilarly, Drake takes the immense pressure he’s placed under in the industry and consistently performs, amassing his riches and expanding his diamond collection.", "annotation_id": "22623150", "url": "https://genius.com/22623150", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "Cruisin’ down Biscayne I was in Miami, she gave me top with the top off Cartier wrist gang, got her a Audemar", "vault_lines": ["When I was down, I was down", "I was in Miami, she gave me top with the top off", "Cartier wrist gang, got her a Audemar"], "meaning": "Roddy name drops a series of luxurious nouns here. First, he’s cruising around Biscayne Bay, a beautiful piece of sea off the shore of Downtown Miami. He, and the girl with him, are rocking Cartier on the wrist, probably one of the jewelry company’s watches or bracelets. To top it off, Roddy gifts his cruising companion an Audemar watch, another flashy item.", "annotation_id": "21570558", "url": "https://genius.com/21570558", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "I might pull up in the Six with Drizzy", "vault_lines": ["I might pull up in the Six with Drizzy"], "meaning": "“The Six” is Drake’s name for his hometown of Toronto, Canada. A Toronto Sun article from 2016 quotes him explaining that the name is based on the third digit of Toronto’s area code, which is 416.\n\n“Drizzy” is one of Drake’s nicknames. Its exact origin is unknown, but it is thought that he gained the nickname while collaborating with his mentor Lil Wayne. Drake uses it on his Twitter account.", "annotation_id": "21169934", "url": "https://genius.com/21169934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "Thugger got the YSL, huh", "vault_lines": ["Thugger got the YSL, huh"], "meaning": "Thugger is a nickname for Atlanta rapper Young Thug. He owns a label named YSL Records.\n\nYSL stands for “Young Stoner Life,” or “Young Slime Life.”\n\n\n\nYSL also stands for Yves Saint Laurent, the full name of the Saint Laurent fashion designer company. This double meaning would make sense considering the Chanel name-drop in the previous bar.", "annotation_id": "21169890", "url": "https://genius.com/21169890", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "I was just in Houston, fuckin’ with the mob ties", "vault_lines": ["I was just in Houston, fuckin’ with the mob ties"], "meaning": "Houston is a very familiar city for both rappers, especially Drake, who has made his love of the city clear in the past.\n\n\n\nAt the end of the line, Roddy could be making a reference to Drake’s song “Mob Ties” from his 2018 album Scorpion.\n\nRoddy might also be referencing his song “Gods Eyes”, in which he raps:\n\nI got gang gang (Gang gang), I got mob ties (Mob ties)", "annotation_id": "20699931", "url": "https://genius.com/20699931", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "Look, I’m in the cut, The Boy in the cut", "vault_lines": ["Still in the cut, The Boy in the cut", "Still in the cut, The Boy in the cut, yeah", "Look, I'm in the cut, The Boy in the cut"], "meaning": "“In the cut” is slang for a secluded area, somewhere that’s hidden from open view. Drake has rapped about being “in the cut” few times before—for example, on January 2016’s “Summer Sixteen,” he raps:\n\nFamous as fuck but I’m still in the cut when they round up the troops\n\n“The Boy” is one of Drake’s many nicknames. He used it on March 2017’s “Can’t Have Everything”:\n\nIt’s The Boy but I’m still the man, come and get your mans", "annotation_id": "20288417", "url": "https://genius.com/20288417", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "Trendsetter, Ben Frank’ getter (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Trendsetter, Ben Frank' getter (Yeah)"], "meaning": "“Ben Frank” is short for Benjamin Franklin—one of the Founding Fathers of the United States. He’s featured on the $100 dollar bill.\n\nDrake’s net worth as of 2020 is estimated to be $170 million, leaving him with a lot of $100 dollar bills.", "annotation_id": "20288384", "url": "https://genius.com/20288384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "line": "Your foreign is used, I looked up the VIN", "vault_lines": ["Your foreign is used, I looked up the VIN"], "meaning": "A VIN or Vehicle Identification Number is a 17-digit code that uniquely identifies a motor vehicle. Located at the bottom right of the driver’s side dashboard, the number series provides a variety of information, including the manufacturer and production year. A VIN search can also link to past owner history, indicating whether a car is in fact used or new.\n\nHere, Drake uses VIN information to call out those who flex in discount foreign cars with prior owners.", "annotation_id": "20270738", "url": "https://genius.com/20270738", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In the Cut", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 10, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-in-the-cut-lyrics", "genius_title": "In the Cut"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Perkies for the bitch in Magic, Don Juan", "vault_lines": ["Perkies for the bitch in Magic, Don Juan"], "meaning": "Future is providing Percocets for the women and even more for himself (he’s already sung the praises of the “percies” and strippers combo on Dirty Sprite 2). Bishop Don “Magic” Juan is a former pimp turned preacher and the creator and organizer of “The Player’s Club Ball,” an annual celebration of pimpdom.\n\n\n\nThere’s also a strip club called Magic City in Atlanta. Future has a rightful claim to Magic City—his buzz first began in the club. DJ Esco hosts the infamous Magic City Mondays and also happens to be Future’s DJ.", "annotation_id": "7856715", "url": "https://genius.com/7856715", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Standing ovation and an encore And I keep a check, Nike Concord", "vault_lines": ["Standing ovation and an encore", "And I keep a check, Nike Concord"], "meaning": "Concords are a specific type of Nike sneaker. Future links the check in the Nike logo to the check he receives regularly. He’s getting paid and he’s got classic shoes.", "annotation_id": "7847114", "url": "https://genius.com/7847114", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Who really think they can get in the way? Nah, nah, niggas is bitter, they hurtin’", "vault_lines": ["Who really think they can get in the way?", "Nah, nah, niggas is bitter, they hurtin'"], "meaning": "Another potential shot at Meek Mill being bitter from all the controversy going on with Drake not writing his own music (“R.I.C.O.” verse, Quentin Miller, IYRTITL Album). Meek’s career has arguably taken a significant blow from the Drake-Meek beef.", "annotation_id": "7842260", "url": "https://genius.com/7842260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "You gon’ think we printin’ money ’cause the way we make it, uh", "vault_lines": ["You gon' think we printin' money 'cause the way we make it, uh"], "meaning": "With a net worth of 4 million, and 75 million respectively – Future and Drake have big bucks. They’re balling so hard people think their money must be fake.", "annotation_id": "7842023", "url": "https://genius.com/7842023", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Got a gang of broads riding and they on drugs with me", "vault_lines": ["Got a gang of broads riding and they on drugs with me"], "meaning": "Future’s riding around with a group of women and likely hooking up with a few of them. They all popped pills or took some kind of drug, perhaps to intensify the fun of the ride.\n\nLike he said on “The Percocet & Stripper Joint”\n\nI just need a whole lot of drugs in my system", "annotation_id": "7838440", "url": "https://genius.com/7838440", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Draped in all designer, draped in all designer Draped in all designer, draped in all designer Yeah", "vault_lines": ["Draped in all designer, draped in all designer"], "meaning": "Future is very enthusiastic about his fashion. He can always be spotted wearing Chanel Vintage, Jimmy Choo, Gucci, Burberry, Bathing Ape and Maison Margiela or other expensive brands.", "annotation_id": "7838004", "url": "https://genius.com/7838004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Got a gang of cash on me like I brought the plug with me Niggas looking for the plug, nigga, I’m the plug really, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Really, I'm the plug, really, I'm the plug", "Got a gang of cash on me like I brought the plug with me", "Niggas looking for the plug, nigga, I'm the plug really, ayy"], "meaning": "The “plug” is the drug dealer, the supplier. Usually loaded with both cash and substances, the plug is a key player in the recreational life of some rappers. When a rapper in need hits a new city, he has to find a new plug.\n\nBut not Future. His last record Dirty Sprite 2 was full of drug references, and now he has become the plug, really.", "annotation_id": "7837538", "url": "https://genius.com/7837538", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "And it really ain’t no thang, I got Summer P in this thang", "vault_lines": ["And it really ain't no thang, I got Summer P in this thang"], "meaning": "Summer P. is DJ Drama’s ex wife Summer P. Walker. She didn’t seem too upset about the namecheck—she tweeted it out herself.\n\n\n\nIt’s possible that Drake and Summer P. once had a thing of their own and that she’s the Summer he “got a Michael Kors with his momma’s credit” in his song “From Time.”\n\nIt’s likely the shout out here is more of a shot at her ex-husband Drama for possibly leaking the Quentin Miller reference tracks Meek infamously tweeted about. Drake brags that the Meek Mill beef had no negative effect on him.", "annotation_id": "7836946", "url": "https://genius.com/7836946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "This that OVO Halloween gang, you know how I’m rocking already", "vault_lines": ["This that OVO Halloween gang, you know how I'm rocking already"], "meaning": "Drake’s record label is called OVO Sound; OVO stands for October’s very own.\n\nHalloween is of course also in October. Some took the Halloween references throughout the album as a suggestion that the long-awaited View from the 6 would release in October 2015, but it was not to be. Others think it’s yet another reference to the ghost-writing scandal—Drake’s not afraid.", "annotation_id": "7836720", "url": "https://genius.com/7836720", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "I got all exotic bitches, you gon’ think I’m racist, yeah I just called a gang of bitches out of immigration, uh", "vault_lines": ["I got all exotic bitches, you gon' think I'm racist, yeah", "I just called a gang of bitches out of immigration, uh"], "meaning": "He already mentioned, that he got a ‘gang of bad bitches’ on his track Honest. He’s so determined to get with women from all over that he’ll even endure the bureaucracy of our immigration system to bring them into the country.\n\nReferring to a woman as “exotic” implies that she is of mixed race or from a foreign country. Colorism—particularly discrimination against darker skinned women—is a popular topic of debate within the black community. Future sees sleeping with light-skinned “exotic” women as a status symbol, and here he’s mocking those who might call his choice of women “racist.”", "annotation_id": "7836144", "url": "https://genius.com/7836144", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Franchise tag on me, Bron Bron", "vault_lines": ["Franchise tag on me, Bron Bron"], "meaning": "In the NFL, a team can apply a franchise tag to their star player when he’s an unrestricted free agent to keep him on the team for one more year. The NBA doesn’t have a franchise tag though, so LeBron James needn’t worry. Future welcomes the tag, since he feels he’s a star in the industry.", "annotation_id": "7835521", "url": "https://genius.com/7835521", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "If you come here tryna take somethin’, there’s a lot more where that came from", "vault_lines": ["If you come here tryna take somethin', there's a lot more where that came from"], "meaning": "When Meek Mill came after Drake, Drake was able to come back quick and then keep the tracks coming. In their whole back and forth, Meek released one track (but a lot of tweets). Drake’s output is just too much for Meek or any others to keep up with.", "annotation_id": "7835487", "url": "https://genius.com/7835487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "I dropped a few whoop-da-whoops in my cup", "vault_lines": ["I dropped a few whoop-da-whoops in my cup"], "meaning": "Future adds a couple of pills to his cup of purple drank (promethazine and codeine).", "annotation_id": "7835454", "url": "https://genius.com/7835454", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Look at the business mergers", "vault_lines": ["Look at the business mergers"], "meaning": "Drake and Future’s whole collaboration is a business merger of a kind. Drake owns his own label, OVO, and Future owns FBG. FBGOVO was also a popular twitter hashtag leading up to the project—it’s the two label abbreviations combined.", "annotation_id": "7835340", "url": "https://genius.com/7835340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Steph Curry wrist, nigga, Oakland", "vault_lines": ["Steph Curry wrist, nigga, Oakland"], "meaning": "Sharpshooter Stephen Curry plays for the Golden State Warriors, based in Oakland. Future’s wrist watch game reps the Bay: his Rolex is black, white, and silver – the colors of the Oakland Raiders.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/522QMxkoLb/?taken-by=future", "annotation_id": "7835302", "url": "https://genius.com/7835302", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Pink slip sitting in the dashboard with me", "vault_lines": ["Pink slip sitting in the dashboard with me"], "meaning": "A “pink slip,” in this context, is a certification of title (ownership) for a vehicle that establishes the title holder as its legal owner. If someone were to doubt you owned the nice car you were driving, a pink slip would show them what’s up.\n\n\n\nFuture boasts that he owns quite a few cars.", "annotation_id": "7835225", "url": "https://genius.com/7835225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Yeah, yeah, up and away Y’all should have seen this shit comin’ in May", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, yeah, up and away", "Y'all should have seen this shit comin' in May"], "meaning": "Drake feels as if he’s flying and, according to the previous line, largely due to effects of Red Bull and ecstasy.\n\nDrake and Future launched a largely successful tour, titled Jungle Tour, which ran from May 24, 2015 to June 5, 2015. The spectacle began at the Toyota Center (Houston) and concluded at the Governors Ball.", "annotation_id": "7835143", "url": "https://genius.com/7835143", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "We doing three hundred records a day", "vault_lines": ["We doing three hundred records a day"], "meaning": "Drake and Future have both been releasing a lot of music lately. This is Drake’s second mixtape this year after If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late. Future already released his album DS2 and two mixtapes in 2015, plus another near the end of 2014.\n\nDrake talked about Future’s work ethic on Beats 1 Radio before streaming the album for the first time,\n\n“I went to Atlanta for six days a couple weeks ago with the hopes of doing some songs with Future, and when you get around Future, it’s like a vortex, that guy can outwork anybody right now,” Drake said. “It’s tough to see someone do four, five songs in one night and not try to match it.”", "annotation_id": "7835122", "url": "https://genius.com/7835122", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "I just dropped a jig in my Red Bull and it really gave me wings", "vault_lines": ["I just dropped a jig in my Red Bull and it really gave me wings"], "meaning": "Red Bull is an energy drink—its slogan is “Red Bull gives you wings!” Jig is an ecstasy pill, and Drake’s mixing them together. The high he gets from the combo is as good as having actual wings and flying.", "annotation_id": "7835067", "url": "https://genius.com/7835067", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "And she was actin’ like a virgin (Nah) I gave it to her, then I curved her", "vault_lines": ["I was like, \"Nah, nigga, let's get it\"", "And she was actin' like a virgin (Nah)", "I gave it to her, then I curved her"], "meaning": "Drake plays with Madonna’s iconic line. Drake’s girl was acting like a virgin, but he knew better—instead of pursuing a serious relationship, he had sex with her then kicked her out right after. It’s the bitches be like “it’s my first time, I swear” meme come to life.\n\nOf course, it may also be a reference to Drake’s less than enthusiastic response to a kiss from Queen Madge.", "annotation_id": "7835052", "url": "https://genius.com/7835052", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "Whole time, I was ready They was like, “Hold up, wait a minute” I was like, “Nah, nigga, let’s get it”", "vault_lines": ["Whole time, I was ready", "They was like, \"Hold up, wait a minute\"", "I was like, \"Nah, nigga, let's get it\""], "meaning": "Another clever shot at Meek Mill. Meek is known for his lyric “hold up wait a minute” on the opening track “Dreams and Nightmares” from his first album, after which he switches his flow.\n\nDrizzy plays on this phrase, saying that if Meek really thought that Drizzy would stop throwing shots, he was wrong—Drake is ready for war.\n\nMeek Mill talked about doing a joint album or project with Future, but Drake took action and made it instead of waiting.", "annotation_id": "7835015", "url": "https://genius.com/7835015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "line": "I got all these fuckin’ whips and ain’t got no payments", "vault_lines": ["I got all these fuckin' whips and ain't got no payments"], "meaning": "With an estimated net worth of $4 million, you can imagine how many nice “whips” Future can afford – no rentals or leases involved.", "annotation_id": "7834994", "url": "https://genius.com/7834994", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m the Plug", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 22, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-future-im-the-plug-lyrics", "genius_title": "I’m the Plug"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "I might have to make her paint a 6 on her pinky toe", "vault_lines": ["I might have to make her paint a 6 on her pinky toe"], "meaning": "Throughout Drake’s career he has been associated with the number 6 in songs like 6 Man, 6 God, You & The 6, 9 and more. Another thing is the fact the number 6 is even in the cover for hes 2015 mixtape If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late", "annotation_id": "38713570", "url": "https://genius.com/38713570", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Fuck a pigeonhole, I’m a night owl, this a different mode", "vault_lines": ["Fuck a pigeonhole, I'm a night owl, this a different mode"], "meaning": "a night owl is the logo of Drake’s label OVO (October’s Very Own)", "annotation_id": "38713558", "url": "https://genius.com/38713558", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Bitch, don’t tell me that you model if you ain’t been in Vogue", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, don't tell me that you model if you ain't been in Vogue"], "meaning": "Vogue is universally regarded as the most prestigious fashion magazine, representing the pinnacle of professional modelling. Drake makes it clear that he’s only interested in models who have reached this elite level, emphasizing his high standards and ability to attract the best.\n\nHowever, just five months later on “Spin Bout U,” Drizzy humorously retracts this statement. He flexes his power and influence by claiming he could easily get the girl featured in Vogue, showing a more playful side to his earlier standard:\n\nAnd I know what I said ‘bout bein’ in Vogue\nBut just like that R&B group from the ’90s\nGirl, one call, I’ll get you in Vogue\nOne call, you in runway shows\nOne call, I’m sittin’ front row", "annotation_id": "33229343", "url": "https://genius.com/33229343", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "The chopper like to feel on all the opps, it’s a molester (21)", "vault_lines": ["The chopper like to feel on all the opps, it's a molester (21)"], "meaning": "A “chopper” is a term used to describe assault rifles and a few other types of firearms, while one’s “opps” are their opposition/enemies. He’s conveying that the AR likes to “feel” (as a molester may do to others), hence calling it a molester.\n\n21 Savage is basically saying that he unloads his rifle on his opposition so ruthlessly that it’s basically assault (hence the mention of an assault rifle), which is a synonym for “molest”.\n\nHowever, the word “molest” is generally used to mean “sexual assault (especially on children)”, so 21 might want to rethink this one.", "annotation_id": "26856728", "url": "https://genius.com/26856728", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Heard you with a shooting guard, just let a nigga know I would have you courtside, not the middle row", "vault_lines": ["I would have you courtside, not the middle row", "Heard you with a shooting guard, just let a nigga know"], "meaning": "Athletes with a sneaky link don’t want public attention on the woman they’re with. So they give her middle row seats, which can be quality but not enough to draw attention. Drake is saying that he’d claim her and get the courtside seats that would put cameras on her.", "annotation_id": "26550560", "url": "https://genius.com/26550560", "state": "pending", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Shot his ass twenty times, damn, this nigga lucky (Damn, that nigga lucky)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Numerical wordplay based on what is inferred more than what is directly stated. While being shot twenty times would kill most people, the guy 21 Savage shot didn’t die because the implication is that 21 “killed it” on this verse. The conflation between lyrical skill and literal violence is a common trend in hip hop. Here, however, 21 Savage tells his listeners he killed it without directly telling them he killed it.", "annotation_id": "26091386", "url": "https://genius.com/26091386", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "[Verse: 21 Savage & Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the second half of this verse, 21 interpolates and uses the same cadence of Three 6 Mafia’s “Poppin' My Collar.”\n\n21 previously interpolated this flow on his last collaboration with Drake, “Knife Talk” alongside Project Pat, a year earlier.", "annotation_id": "26083431", "url": "https://genius.com/26083431", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Bro think he John Wayne, I bought him yellow stones", "vault_lines": ["Bro think he John Wayne, I bought him yellow stones"], "meaning": "Yellowstone is an American neo-Western drama television series starring Kevin Costner that follows the conflicts along the shared borders of a large cattle ranch, an Indian reservation, and land developers.\n\nJohn Wayne was an actor who became a popular icon through his starring roles in films made during Hollywood’s Golden Age, especially in Western and war movies.\n\nMany believe there never be another Hollywood cowboy quite like John Wayne, however, Yellowstone fans think Kevin Costner is the only one to come close. Hence, Drake is saying wearing jewelry encrusted with yellow gemstones will make you feel legendary and invincible.\n\nDrake is a fan of Yellowstone, and was spotted at a bar wearing the shows' merchandise in a viral Twitter video on June 2nd, 2022, just two weeks prior to the release of the album:\nhttps://twitter.com/SHYSNOE/status/1532527121578467349?s=20&t=Ru8ZRmN0cMV8GFi6NDbGhA", "annotation_id": "26081444", "url": "https://genius.com/26081444", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Smack the backside of his head like he Bart (Pussy)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "21 references Bart Simpson, a mischievous and rebellious character from the animated series The Simpsons, who is often punished by his father, Homer, for misbehaving. Comparing himself to Homer, 21 disciplines his enemies as if they were his children.\n\n\n\n21 Simpson.", "annotation_id": "26077381", "url": "https://genius.com/26077381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Hoes say I’m suave, but I can’t get RICO’d", "vault_lines": ["Hoes say I'm suave, but I can't get RICO'd"], "meaning": "This line is a play on words on Gerardo’s hit single “Rico Suave.” The federal law known as the RICO Act provides for extended criminal penalties and a civil cause of action for acts performed as part of an ongoing criminal organization.\n\nThis is an allusion to the current legal troubles that has put fellow rappers Young Thug and Gunna behind bars in the state of Georgia. Fellow rappers have condemned the use of lyrics to build the case against the YSL artists as they feel that their art is for entertainment purposes only and should not be used as a tool to depict their characters in a court of law.\n\nThe recent New York “Rap Music on Trial” bill engages similar issues:\n\nIn January 2022, major news outlets like The Guardian reported that a group of high profile names from the world of music—led by the rapper JAY-Z as well as Killer Mike, Fat Joe, Kelly Rowland, and Meek Mill—had signed an open letter demanding that New York legislators pass a new reform bill limiting prosecutors' use of rap lyrics as evidence in criminal trials. Titled “Rap Music on Trial,” the bill would require prosecutors to present “clear and convincing evidence” that lyrics from songs reflect real life; that they are “literal, rather than figurative or fictional,” according to Rolling Stone.\n\n\n\n“Suave” could be a reference to the previous line, as it’s the name of a popular personal care brand that makes body wash and shampoo, among other toiletries: “bubbles and suds.”", "annotation_id": "26077853", "url": "https://genius.com/26077853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Just awaken shaken once again, so you know it’s on Just awaken shaken once again, ho, you know it’s on Just awaken shaken once again, so you know it’s on Just awaken shaken once again, ho, you know it’s on", "vault_lines": ["Just awaken shaken once again, so you know it's on", "Just awaken shaken once again, ho, you know it's on"], "meaning": "This is a direct sample of the chorus in Playa Fly’s iconic song, “Just Awaken Shaken”—which he originally released in 1995 on cassette tape for his debut studio album, Just Gettin' It On:\n\nJust awaken shaken once again, so you know it’s on\nJust awaken shaken once again, ho, you know it’s on\n\nhttps://youtu.be/1mLZBWaZhgk?is=vwsDO-nq6KDuO70n&t=161s", "annotation_id": "26077757", "url": "https://genius.com/26077757", "state": "accepted", "votes": 118, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Spin a block twice like it ain’t nowhere to park (21)", "vault_lines": ["Spin a block twice like it ain't nowhere to park (21)"], "meaning": "A man of honour, 21 will stop at nothing to ensure that his enemies are vanquished, repeatedly circling his opponent in his car until he is able to shoot and murder him in cold blood.\n\nNo stranger to gun violence, 21 previously discussed this kind of sadistic torture on his menacing track “Brand New Draco,” evoking relentless, villainous imagery, rapping:\n\nSpin that shit every day, every night\nBack to back to back to back to back", "annotation_id": "26077613", "url": "https://genius.com/26077613", "state": "pending", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "I got mad love for the boy, yeah, that’s my twizzin (21)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "21 affirms his respect for Drake, referring to him by one of his well-known aliases, The Boy. Drake and 21 have a storied friendship, having previously collaborated on the songs “Knife Talk,” “Mr. Right Now,” and “Sneakin'.”\n\n\n\n\n\n21’s epenthetic cadence is reminiscent of Project Pat’s on the aforementioned track “Knife Talk,” rapping:\n\nTo stay ahead in this bitch-ard (Gang), drank syrup like it’s liquor\nStreet life’ll have you catchin' up to God quicker (Yeah, gang)\nSticker, AK-40 to your liver\nLet the chopper bang on you like a Blood or a Cripper (Gang)", "annotation_id": "26077479", "url": "https://genius.com/26077479", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "She wanna hear some Afrobeats ’cause she just popped a Tesla", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Though Tesla is the name of the car manufacturer owned by Elon Musk, 21 Savage uses the brand name in this line to refer to ecstasy pills which are often pressed using a mould of the Tesla logo.\n\n\n\nPictured: A typical blue Tesla pill\n\nMDMA is a stimulant and is often associated with parties, therefore a genre like Afrobeats, or other dance genres, are typically perfect musical accompaniments for the high. Furthermore, the album this song belongs to, Honestly, Nevermind, draws greatly upon inspiration from Afrobeats, as has often been the case throughout Drake’s discography.", "annotation_id": "26077324", "url": "https://genius.com/26077324", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Rest in peace to Lil Keed", "vault_lines": ["Rest in peace to Drama King, we was straight stuntin'", "Rest in peace to Lil Keed"], "meaning": "Lil Keed was a rapper from Atlanta, Georgia, who passed away on May 13, 2022, due to liver and kidney failure. He was signed to Young Thug’s YSL Records.\n\nAlthough Drake and Keed did not release a collaboration during Keed’s life, the two artists showed mutual respect for each other. Notably, Drake showed up to one of Keed’s concerts as a surprise guest.\n\nAfter his death, Drake posted a picture of Keed on his Instagram story to pay tribute to him.", "annotation_id": "26077228", "url": "https://genius.com/26077228", "state": "accepted", "votes": 208, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Last nigga played with me got turned duppy", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The word “Duppy” stems from the Jamaican folklore and refers to the ghost of a dead person or, more generally, to an evil spirit. Thus, 21 is implying that he had his last opponent killed.\n\nThis is also in reference to Drake’s diss track “Duppy Freestyle,” which was aimed at Kanye West and Pusha T as part of their 2018 beef, which culminated in the release of Pusha’s counter diss track “The Story of Adidon”.\n\n21 previously used a similar line on KSI’s 2021 track “Number 2,” rapping:\n\nLast opp played with us, we turned his ass duppy (Whack)", "annotation_id": "26077075", "url": "https://genius.com/26077075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "OVO 4L, we come out when it get dark (21, 21)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "21 references the unity between Drake’s OVO collective and his “4L” gang, which he founded with his cousin Young Nudy.\n\nNotably, in 2020, following the release of the duo’s hit song “Mr. Right Now,” 21 and Drake gifted each other 4L and OVO chains respectively, cementing the bond between the two artists.\n\nFurthermore, 21 asserts that his gang partakes in illegal activities after hours, drawing comparisons with OVO’s owl mascot, which is a nocturnal species.", "annotation_id": "26076989", "url": "https://genius.com/26076989", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Askin’ all these questions, bitch, you must think you Nadeska", "vault_lines": ["Askin' all these questions, bitch, you must think you Nadeska"], "meaning": "Nadeska Alexis is a music journalist best known for her hip-hop reporting. She has interviewed numerous rappers including A$AP Rocky, Pusha T, and ScHoolboy Q. She is also the executive producer and moderator of Complex’s Everyday Struggle, as well as the host of Apple Music’s The Nadeska Show.\n\n\n\nNadeska and Drake pictured together.", "annotation_id": "26076964", "url": "https://genius.com/26076964", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "I be with my gun like Rozay be with lemon pepper", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "21 Savage likens himself to Rick Ross, nicknamed “Rozay,” who is known for his love for wings. The Miami rapper is the owner of multiple Wingstop locations, and he helped launch Thighstop during the chicken wing shortage in 2021.\n\n\n\nThe line might also be a nod to Rick Ross and Drake’s March 2021 collaboration “Lemon Pepper Freestyle” from Drake’s EP, Scary Hours 2.", "annotation_id": "26076906", "url": "https://genius.com/26076906", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Rest in peace to Drama King, we was straight stuntin’", "vault_lines": ["Rest in peace to Drama King, we was straight stuntin'", "Rest in peace to Lil Keed"], "meaning": "“Drama King” was the nickname given to the late DJ Kay Slay, who passed away in April of 2022 due to COVID-related complications. The New York artist was also the owner of the Straight Stuntin Magazine, which Drake alludes to in his verse, a Playboy-like magazine focusing on interviews and models. The link between Drake and Kay Slay indicates their shared style and ability to show off.", "annotation_id": "26076835", "url": "https://genius.com/26076835", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "Dread talkin’ to you niggas like I’m J. Cole", "vault_lines": ["Dread talkin' to you niggas like I'm J. Cole"], "meaning": "Drake plays on both meanings of “dread(s),” as the word means to anticipate begrudgingly and is also shorthand for dreadlocks, a popular Black hairstyle that fellow rapper J. Cole is renowned for:\n\n\n\nCole’s voice can be heard at the end of the verse saying “Get out my face, nigga.”\n\nCole and Drake notably collaborated a while back on their 2013 track “Jodeci Freestyle.” Drake also featured on “In the Morning” from Cole’s 2010 mixtape Friday Night Lights. The two have a strong history of being great friends and supporters of each other.", "annotation_id": "26076623", "url": "https://genius.com/26076623", "state": "accepted", "votes": 265, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "line": "This Glock 4-5 came with a switch (21) If I was Will Smith, I would’ve slapped him with a stick", "vault_lines": ["This Glock 4-5 came with a switch (21)"], "meaning": "21 refers to the infamous incident of Will Smith slapping Chris Rock at the 94th Academy Awards. Rock, while hosting the event, made a joke about Smith’s wife, Jada Pinkett Smith. This prompted Smith to walk onstage and slap Rock:\n\n\n\n“Stick” is a popular slang for a firearm; 21 is likely referring to pistol-whipping. The “switch” that Savage mentions likely refers to a Glock switch, a metal piece that can be added to the back of a handgun—such as a Glock 45—to convert it from a semi-automatic to fully automatic weapon.\n\nIt’s worth noting that “switch” is also slang for a rod (or literal stick) used to hit someone.", "annotation_id": "26076597", "url": "https://genius.com/26076597", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Jimmy Cooks", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 22, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-jimmy-cooks-lyrics", "genius_title": "Jimmy Cooks"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "I’ve got bitches in the merky, swervin’, lookin’ all curvy", "vault_lines": ["I've got bitches in the merky, swervin', lookin' all curvy"], "meaning": "“Merky” is a slang term for the car manufacturer Mercedes and “swervin” is slang for driving recklessly. Giggs has a bunch of girls in his Benz.", "annotation_id": "11579968", "url": "https://genius.com/11579968", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "You know dem ones? Hahaha", "vault_lines": ["You know dem ones? Hahaha"], "meaning": "“You know dem ones?” is a common rhetorical slang question used in the UK, particularly in London. It is used when referring to a situation which the person being addressed is familiar with.\n\nGiggs says this straight after Drake ends his verse with: “I heard your new shit and I’m kissing my teeth,” indicating that he’s familiar with hearing music and disapproving of its quality.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/officialgiggs/status/590445276269301761", "annotation_id": "11548102", "url": "https://genius.com/11548102", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "I’m a black man (Black man), government earner (Earner)", "vault_lines": ["I'm a black man (Black man), government earner (Earner)"], "meaning": "Numerous online sources have estimated Giggs' net worth to be approximately $3 million. For over a decade Giggs has been independently releasing music, so the fact he compares his net worth to a privileged member of Parliament is ironic. On the other hand, “Government earner,” could be Giggs' way of pointing out that a lot of black people are on welfare.", "annotation_id": "11548072", "url": "https://genius.com/11548072", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Dust a man down with the pen, it’s a sweep", "vault_lines": ["Dust a man down with the pen, it's a sweep"], "meaning": "Drake plays on “dust” and “sweep” to describe how good his raps are. “Sweep” is a term used in sports (mostly basketball and baseball) when one team wins every game in a series, while “dust,” is slang meaning “to kill” someone.\n\nDuring his 2015 beef with Meek Mill, many of fans would consider Drake’s one-two-punch of “Charged Up” and “Back to Back” as a sweep.", "annotation_id": "11541321", "url": "https://genius.com/11541321", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "October Firm", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "October Firm consists of Drake and OVO co-founder Oliver El-Khatib. During Episode 38 of their OVO Sound Radio, Drizzy explained:\n\nWe’re a firm, like an old English firm.", "annotation_id": "11540263", "url": "https://genius.com/11540263", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "I was pushin’ that dark shit, pushin’ that charcoal", "vault_lines": ["I was pushin' that dark shit, pushin' that charcoal"], "meaning": "Black tar Heroin is a very potent and addictive type of Heroin. Charcoal is black in color.", "annotation_id": "11537942", "url": "https://genius.com/11537942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Long as they pockets is greaze (Greaze!)", "vault_lines": ["Long as they pockets is greaze (Greaze!)"], "meaning": "“Greaze” is a UK slang popularized by Skepta, whose ad-lib can be heard following this line.\n\nThe term emerged in West London as a way of expressing personal appreciation and became Skepta’s signature ad-lib during the Konnichiwa album era. In context, Drake has a certain liking for his “pockets” or his money.\n\nThe same ad-lib is used again on “Skepta Interlude.”", "annotation_id": "11536635", "url": "https://genius.com/11536635", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Shoutout to T, he did three And he brought it in ’cause of me", "vault_lines": ["Shoutout to T, he did three", "And he brought it in 'cause of me"], "meaning": "This is a shout out to rapper Tee Grizzley, who was sentenced to 3 years in jail due to home invasion charges. Tee shouted out Drake in an Instagram Live video a week after More Life was released.\n\nThis could also be a reference to producer T-Minus, who has three production credits on Drake’s sophomore album Take Care: “The Motto,” “HYFR,” and “Make Me Proud.”", "annotation_id": "11536466", "url": "https://genius.com/11536466", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Spen jumped out the Ghost in a suit, lookin’ all churchy", "vault_lines": ["Spen jumped out the Ghost in a suit, lookin' all churchy"], "meaning": "Giggs' brother Spen (aka Spender) is getting out of a Rolls Royce Ghost wearing a suit. It’s common to wear your “Sunday best” (suit) to Church.\n\nGiggs may also be referring to “Churches” shoes, a high-end traditional English shoe maker. Churches are also referenced by Kano in his Fire In The Booth Freestyle.", "annotation_id": "11535948", "url": "https://genius.com/11535948", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "I gave my bro an advance", "vault_lines": ["I gave my bro an advance"], "meaning": "Within the music world, someone will get an advance when they sign a label or before they start a new album. However, in this case, Drake’s probably referring to lending Baka money because he just got out of jail.", "annotation_id": "11535099", "url": "https://genius.com/11535099", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Mizzy with the quick extension, ringin’ off thirty", "vault_lines": ["Mizzy with the quick extension, ringin' off thirty"], "meaning": "Mizzy is slang for a “MAC\"—a sub compact machine pistol. Giggs even has an extended magazine on it that holds thirty bullets.", "annotation_id": "11534878", "url": "https://genius.com/11534878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Bringin’ that dirty-dirty, bringin’ that certy (Certy)", "vault_lines": ["Bringin' that dirty-dirty, bringin' that certy (Certy)"], "meaning": "“Certy” is UK slang for “certified,” meaning cool/well respected. It can be used to describe almost anything from a song/songs (like in this context), to a person, to a car, or anything else that is deserving of praise. In this sense, Giggs is reaffirming the fact that he has been dropping great music for years now.\n\nThis line by Giggs was immortalised on the reverse side of Drake’s new Stone Island chain, which was regarded by many as the final step in Drake’s transition into a London roadman.", "annotation_id": "11534295", "url": "https://genius.com/11534295", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "And you already know I love them breasts, lookin’ all perky", "vault_lines": ["And you already know I love them breasts, lookin' all perky"], "meaning": "Giggs has made it known in the past that he prefers breasts, namely this line from “Look What the Cat Dragged In”:\n\nStraight nasty, I ain’t glancin'\nI’m a breast man, but I rate arses", "annotation_id": "11533107", "url": "https://genius.com/11533107", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Demon just got out the can", "vault_lines": ["Demon just got out the can"], "meaning": "Demon is an old school grime MC who hasn’t been active since he released his mixtape Demon’s World back in 2006, so it would make sense that he just came out of prison.\n\nBefore More Life was released, Giggs posted this photo on Instagram using this lyric as the caption:\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BRdQbPQDFkR/", "annotation_id": "11532815", "url": "https://genius.com/11532815", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "(Ness on the beat)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Ness is a producer from Rochester, New York.  He is signed to High Bridge The Label (HBTL). He uses a sample from the 2006 video game “Sonic the Hedgehog.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vRe3h1iQ1Os", "annotation_id": "11532181", "url": "https://genius.com/11532181", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Taller in person, you’ll see when we meet", "vault_lines": ["Taller in person, you'll see when we meet"], "meaning": "Drake’s 6'1 (~185 cm), but in some photos looks shorter than he actually is.", "annotation_id": "11532145", "url": "https://genius.com/11532145", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "I’m in the penthouse but still, nothin’ is sweet", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the penthouse but still, nothin' is sweet"], "meaning": "The homophones “sweet” and “suite” refer to the comfortable penthouse suite Drake’s currently in, but he makes it clear that he has to work to maintain what he has—nothing comes easy.", "annotation_id": "11492088", "url": "https://genius.com/11492088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "How long I been on this streak?", "vault_lines": ["How long I been on this streak?"], "meaning": "Drake’s musical output has been unmatchable over the past three years. He began in 2015 with the surprise mixtape If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late and the collaborative mixtape What a Time to Be Alive with Future, before breaking records with 2016’s Views, and  has since re-broken those records with More Life.\n\nWith consecutive hits like “Jumpman,” “Sneakin',” “Know Yourself,” and “0 to 100/The Catch Up,” Drake’s got his hand firmly on the streets.", "annotation_id": "11258670", "url": "https://genius.com/11258670", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "I heard your new shit and I’m kissin’ my teeth", "vault_lines": ["I heard your new shit and I'm kissin' my teeth"], "meaning": "The act of kissing your teeth derives from the Jamaican slang expression “Kiss mi teeth” (often abbreviated to KMT) and is a mouth gesture used to show annoyance, disapproval or strong dislike.  Drake has heard your recent music and disapproves of it.\n\nAs Jamaican slang is widely used in both Toronto and London (Giggs' hometown), it is likely they both use the term.", "annotation_id": "11253170", "url": "https://genius.com/11253170", "state": "accepted", "votes": 186, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Whippin’ that white girl (Yeah?), cookin’ that Cersei", "vault_lines": ["Whippin' that white girl (Yeah?), cookin' that Cersei"], "meaning": "“White girl” is slang for cocaine, while “Cersei” is a reference to the Game of Thrones character, Cersei Lannister.\n\n\n\nGiggs uses Cersei’s name as a synonym for cocaine, as seen with other popular, white characters and celebrities, eg. “Hannah Montana” and Christina Aguilera.\n\nGiggs is known for his love of fantasy television shows and films, frequently name-dropping Star Wars and Marvel characters in his lyrics. He also made a Game of Thrones reference in 2015 on “Dirty Bastard”:\n\nNow man’s Game of Thrones just like Arya\nBodied the game and then I went away\nCame back, bodied again, I’ll get you bolognesed\n\n‘Cersei’ is phonetically similar to ‘Cerasee’, a Jamaican medicinal herb that requires ‘cooking’ to be drank as a tea.", "annotation_id": "11251491", "url": "https://genius.com/11251491", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Jheeze!", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Jheeze” is slang for happiness or excitement.\n\nDrake has previously used it on “Controlla” and “Grammys.”", "annotation_id": "11250650", "url": "https://genius.com/11250650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Man are gettin’ bread now, and this is that hard dough", "vault_lines": ["Man are gettin' bread now, and this is that hard dough"], "meaning": "Hard dough bread is a Jamaican bread that’s tough and sweet. Ard Doe is also the name of a classic mixtape by UK rapper Dubz. Giggs featured on the track “Riding Season”. The project was a follow-up to their joint mixtape Ard Bodied.\n\nBread and dough are both slang terms for money. These terms originated as cockney rhyming slang in Victorian era London.\n\nGiggs released his fourth album, Landlord, in 2016. It soared in popularity in the UK peaking at second place on the album chart, making it a huge commercial success.", "annotation_id": "11250313", "url": "https://genius.com/11250313", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Now this is that big bad, this is Gustavo", "vault_lines": ["Now this is that big bad, this is Gustavo"], "meaning": "Giggs often refers to himself as the “Rap Gustavo,” and even has a track built around the same name, name dropping Breaking Bad’s Gustavo Fring:\n\nShit’s already Breaking Bad\nMan, I’m the rap Gustavo\n\nJust as Gustavo Fring ran the meth business in AMC’s Breaking Bad, by titling himself “Gustavo,” Giggs confirms he’s the kingpin of the U.K rap scene.\n\nThis is also a play on the “big bad wolf” phrase, with Giggs replacing “wolf” with “Gustavo.”", "annotation_id": "11250061", "url": "https://genius.com/11250061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Love is just not in my plans Not even takin’ a chance", "vault_lines": ["Love is just not in my plans", "Not even takin' a chance"], "meaning": "Despite being romantically linked to numerous women in the media, Drake dismisses any possibility of him currently being in a relationship.\n\nBefore More Life was released, it was rumoured he was dating Jennifer Lopez. Drake more or less confirmed on “Free Smoke” they’re not dating as of the album’s release.", "annotation_id": "11248575", "url": "https://genius.com/11248575", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Studio right in my yaad I’m doin’ ten in a week", "vault_lines": ["Studio right in my yaad", "I'm doin' ten in a week"], "meaning": "Although Drake’s pronunciation makes the lyric sound like “yacht,” it’s actually “yaad.” “Yaad” is a Jamaican slang for a person’s home. Drake makes ten songs at his home per week.\n\nIn an interview with Genius, Allen Ritter, a close collaborator with Drake, stated that “Work” was made at Drake’s house:\n\nDrake had this pool party the day before we were all out there. […] He told us we can stay there to make music at his house. Sevn pulled the “Work” beat, but nobody knew where to take it. As soon as he pulled that up, I played the keys on it. I honestly didn’t even think the beat was done. But it’s big now.\n\nThe YOLO Estate doesn’t have a confirmed studio within the house or yard, however, at an estimated price tag of $20 million with 12,500 square feet, it’s likely Drake has one set up.", "annotation_id": "11248498", "url": "https://genius.com/11248498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "line": "Could’ve just slapped man, but he wanted it further (Why?) Batman, da-na-na-da-na", "vault_lines": ["Could've just slapped man, but he wanted it further (Why?)"], "meaning": "Giggs also uses this batman reference in his verse on Jme’s “Man Don’t Care”:\n\nLike Batman, da-na-da-na-da-na\n\nBoth lines are references to the Batman franchise, albeit from different mediums.\n\nA frame from the November 1965 comic World’s Finest #153 was turned into a meme in the late 2000’s. It features Batman slapping Robin, with superimposed text often giving different context to the action.\n\n\n\nThe Adventures of Batman was a 1968 television cartoon series whose intro music Giggs references:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/1jgE-lrfZ3k?t=35s\n\nGiggs has a history of Batman lines, including “Man Don’t Care” and “Ner Ner.”", "annotation_id": "11248410", "url": "https://genius.com/11248410", "state": "accepted", "votes": 190, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "KMT", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 26, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-kmt-lyrics", "genius_title": "KMT"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "I was not kiddin’, don’t know why they playin’ with him", "vault_lines": ["I was not kiddin', don't know why they playin' with him"], "meaning": "Drake delivers a double entendre here, blending seriousness with a subtle nod to hip-hop history. On the surface, he’s emphasizing that he was dead serious — not joking (not kiddin’) — and confused why people still underestimate or “play” with him. But there’s also a slick reference to Kid ‘n Play, the iconic late ‘80s/early ‘90s rap duo known for their playful style and classic House Party films.\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake has nodded at Kid ‘n Play. On “Free Smoke” from More Life (2017), he rapped:\n\n“House party up the road, yeah / I’m not Kid ‘n Play / This kid doesn’t play about the flow, yeah”\n\nThat bar was a direct homage to the duo while drawing a clear contrast — Drake positions himself as a serious rapper who doesn’t mess around with his craft. The “House party” line was a clever setup referencing the duo’s movie, while “this kid doesn’t play” extended the pun, again emphasizing his commitment to rap and lyrical dominance.\n\nAltogether, these lines show how Drake skillfully weaves wordplay and cultural references into his verses — paying respect while asserting his own legacy.", "annotation_id": "35242008", "url": "https://genius.com/35242008", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "Pushin’ five Cadillacs like a politician", "vault_lines": ["Pushin' five Cadillacs like a politician"], "meaning": "Many famous politicians, including the President of the United States, are often surrounded by security driving Cadillac Escalades. Drake compares his security to someone like the President.\n\nhttps://cdn.motor1.com/images/mgl/xMmYG/s1/cadillac-escalade-by-addarmor-lead-image.webp", "annotation_id": "32185292", "url": "https://genius.com/32185292", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "Stick that motherfucker at the Clearport and park that", "vault_lines": ["Stick that motherfucker at the Clearport and park that"], "meaning": "Drake talks about landing his jet Air Drake at the Clearport, another name for the Teterboro Airport in New York that is often shouted out by rappers.", "annotation_id": "31885397", "url": "https://genius.com/31885397", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "And I bought that girl a shovel ’cause she gold-diggin’, yeah", "vault_lines": ["And I bought that girl a shovel 'cause she gold-diggin', yeah"], "meaning": "A gold digger is slang for one who is only fond of an individual for his/her wealth and/or material possessions. Drake is providing a female with a shovel so she can dig her own grave which would prevent her from mooching off of him and gold digging.\n\nDrake has rapped about trust issues in the past. On Trust Issues Drake rapped:\n\nYou know what I’m sipping, I’ll teach you how to mix it\nBut you’re the only one ‘cause I don’t trust these bitches\nI don’t, I don’t trust these bitches, they might catch me slipping\"\n\nDrake rapped about how a female had set him up in a 2009 robbery on From Florida with Love. More on the robbery can be seen here.", "annotation_id": "19723909", "url": "https://genius.com/19723909", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "And I bought my dawg a Rollie ’cause he rolled with it, yeah", "vault_lines": ["And I bought my dawg a Rollie 'cause he rolled with it, yeah"], "meaning": "This is a possible reference to a Rolex purchase that Drake made for J. Cole. On January 23, 2019, J. Cole dropped the track “Middle Child'' in which he rapped:\n\nJust put the Rollie right back on my wrist\nThis watch came from Drizzy, he gave me a gift\nBack when the rap game was prayin' I’d diss\nThey act like two legends cannot coexist\"\n\nMore on the lines and Drake and J. Cole’s relationship can be seen here.", "annotation_id": "19722381", "url": "https://genius.com/19722381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "Ayy, ayy, I done dosed off", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, ayy, I done dosed off", "I done got a different number 'cause I'm closed off"], "meaning": "Drake could be saying he took a pill that he made him fall asleep. He rapped about taking Xanax to help him sleep on a flight on Travis Scott’s August 2018 smash hit, “SICKO MODE”:\n\nI did half a Xan, 13 hours till I land\nHad me out like a light (Ay, yuh)\nLike a light (Ay, yuh)\nLike a light (Ay)\nSlept through the flight (Ay)", "annotation_id": "19716873", "url": "https://genius.com/19716873", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "Boardin’ Air Drake, then we takin’ off in it, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Boardin' Air Drake, then we takin' off in it, yeah"], "meaning": "Air Drake is the name for Drake’s private jet, a Boeing 767 worth $185 million dollars. He received the plane for free from Cargojet, the Canadian manufacturer, since they decided it would pay for itself off of publicity.\n\nAir Drake was redesigned in 2020 by Virgil Abloh, the American fashion designer who is the artistic director of Louis Vuitton’s men’s wear collection, along with being the founder of Off-White.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/B-TKln2lKMC/", "annotation_id": "19716822", "url": "https://genius.com/19716822", "state": "pending", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "Patek doin’ backflips, showin’ off", "vault_lines": ["Patek doin' backflips, showin' off"], "meaning": "Drake’s likely making an allusion to his bar on Future’s January 2020 song “Life Is Good” where he talked about an exclusive version of a Patek Philippe watch, designed by Virgil Abloh’s fashion brand, Off-White:\n\nVirgil got the Patek on my wrist doin' front flips", "annotation_id": "19716784", "url": "https://genius.com/19716784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "I be in and out arenas like I’m Scottie Pippen", "vault_lines": ["I be in and out arenas like I'm Scottie Pippen"], "meaning": "Drake likens himself to former NBA Small Forward Scottie Pippen who played in the NBA from 1987 until 2004. During his NBA tenure, Pippen played for the Chicago Bulls for two stints, Houston Rockets and Portland Trail Blazers. Pippen played a total of 1,178 games during his NBA career. Drake likens his many performances in arenas to the large amount of basketball games Pippen has played in arenas.", "annotation_id": "19601060", "url": "https://genius.com/19601060", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "line": "Wrote this with a Cartier pen, do I sound different? Yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Wrote this with a Cartier pen, do I sound different?", "Wrote this with a Cartier pen, do I sound different? Yeah, yeah"], "meaning": "Cartier’s “Writing Instruments” range in price from $385 to $234,000 CDN.\n\n\n\nOf course, Drake sounds no different while singing the line that he wrote with a Cartier pen. It is a rhetorical question, asking not if he truly sounds different, but whether or not the Cartier pen is worth the mass amount of money that he paid for it. The answer does not matter: what matters is Drake’s financial ability to purchase a pen that expensive.\n\nOn April 14th, 2020, Drake posted a Cartier pen on his Instagram Story using the caption, “Wrote this with the…,” a direct reference to this line of the song.", "annotation_id": "19527647", "url": "https://genius.com/19527647", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Landed", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 10, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-landed-lyrics", "genius_title": "Landed"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "I used to fuck you slow, I used to leave it in", "vault_lines": ["I used to fuck you slow, I used to leave it in"], "meaning": "He used to have sex slowly, and do creampie.\n(intern ejac)", "annotation_id": "41437535", "url": "https://genius.com/41437535", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "Until your new man cop them tickets, you won’t see me ’gain", "vault_lines": ["Until your new man cop them tickets, you won't see me 'gain"], "meaning": "To ‘cop tickets’, it means to either secure, obtain, or possess tickets to a concert, in this sense, a Drake concert, or event.\n\nThis line may also mean that Drake is distancing himself and refusing to help or spend time with the person now that they’re with someone else, in this cause ‘Lauryn’.", "annotation_id": "40369587", "url": "https://genius.com/40369587", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "2009, I’m lit, I told them, “Hurry up and book me”", "vault_lines": ["2009, I'm lit, I told them, \"Hurry up and book me\""], "meaning": "Drake’s 2009 breakout hit single “Best I’ve Ever Had” debuted at #2 on the US Billboard Hot 100—causing commotion between him and Labels including Atlantic Records and Motown Records, but decided to choose Lil Wayne’s Young Money Entertainment.\n\nDrake took advantage of the hype generated from his time as a up-and-coming hot artist. In modern days to book him, it would cost “a fortune”, between $1,500,000 and $6,000,000 million in baseline performance fees.", "annotation_id": "40102807", "url": "https://genius.com/40102807", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "Flirting with the thirty on me", "vault_lines": ["I'm back outside in here, flirting with the thirty on me"], "meaning": "This is a double entendre being Ayesha Curry flirting whilst being with Steph Curry and Drake being in the club with a 30 round weapon of some sort. “Flirting with the 30 on me”, the 30 being Steph Curry which is also his jersey number and Drake flirting/brandishing his weapon.", "annotation_id": "40047793", "url": "https://genius.com/40047793", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "I don’t like goin’ to Vegas, I start missin’ Virgil He’d be sick if he was witnessing this shit in person", "vault_lines": ["I don't like goin' to Vegas, I start missin' Virgil", "He'd be sick if he was witnessing this shit in person"], "meaning": "Virgil Abloh was an American fashion designer known for working alongside luxury brands like Louis Vuitton and his very own, Off-White. Abloh was born in Chicago; however, he had a residency at Wynn Las Vegas, where he performed DJ sets and other work for Off-White.\n\nDrake and Virgil were also very close friends and even tributed him on his Instagram following Virgil’s passing:\n\nMy plan is to touch the sky 1000 more times for you…love you eternally brother 🕊thank you for everything\n\n\n\nDrake and Virgil", "annotation_id": "39845273", "url": "https://genius.com/39845273", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "All these pussy niggas actin’ like they Mr. Murder", "vault_lines": ["All these pussy niggas trippin' like they clipped the hurdle", "All these pussy niggas actin' like they Mr. Murder"], "meaning": "Mr. Murder is a 1993 thriller novel about a successful writer named Martin Stillwater who is hunted by a genetically engineered clone. The clone is a relentless killer who tries to murder the original Martin and steal his life, family, and success.\n\nBy Drake making this comparison, he is calling his rivals clones—they try to replicate his success, but they will never be the original.", "annotation_id": "39844856", "url": "https://genius.com/39844856", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "(D-D-DJ Frisco954, yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "DJ Frisco954 is a DJ from Broward County, Florida. He is best known for making fast-paced remix versions of rap songs and helping popularize South Florida’s “fast music” sound online.\n\nHe is also credited on “Rusty Intro,” the opening track for HABIBTI.", "annotation_id": "39828529", "url": "https://genius.com/39828529", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "Ayy, ayy Ayy, what? Yeah, his-and-hers it", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, what? Yeah, his-and-hers it"], "meaning": "Alex Moss is a New York–based celebrity jeweler known for creating custom diamond pieces for popular rappers, including Drake. He has worked with Drake on numerous occasions—in October 2023, after the release of his album For All The Dogs, Moss made a one-of-a-kind chain for Drake called “The Crown Jewel of Toronto,” which showcased the city’s mascots and its professional sports teams. On May 16, 2026, Drake revealed custom, diamond-encrusted “Iceman” owl pendants, also made by Moss.\n\n“His-and-hers” refers to buying matching versions of a luxury item meant to match with a spouse or partner.", "annotation_id": "39796305", "url": "https://genius.com/39796305", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "All these Arab women treat me like the Prince of Persia", "vault_lines": ["All these Arab women treat me like the Prince of Persia"], "meaning": "Persia is the historic name for Iran. Although Arabs are a small minority in the country, Persians actually make up about 60% of the population.\n\nPrince of Persia is also a mythical action-adventure video game franchise that dates back to 1989. It’s about a royal hero that fights to undo magical catastrophes.", "annotation_id": "39793781", "url": "https://genius.com/39793781", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "I put CC’s on that ho just like a titty surgeon", "vault_lines": ["I put CC's on that ho just like a titty surgeon"], "meaning": "Drake uses “CC’s” as a double entendre. On one side, he’s talking about Chanel, an expensive French designer brand. Their logo consists of two interlocking C’s, which pays homage to the fashion house’s founder, Gabrielle “Coco” Chanel.\n\n\n\nOn the other, breast implants are measured by volume in cubic centimeters or “cc.” Therefore, a cosmetic surgeon would literally be putting “cc’s” into someone.", "annotation_id": "39793009", "url": "https://genius.com/39793009", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "All these pussy niggas trippin’ like they clipped the hurdle", "vault_lines": ["All these pussy niggas trippin' like they clipped the hurdle", "All these pussy niggas actin' like they Mr. Murder"], "meaning": "Hurdling is a track and field event where runners must clear a set of raised obstacles known as hurdles. Clipping a hurdle would result in a loss of balance, meaning you would fall or “trip” to the ground. However, “trip” also means to act in an irrational matter.", "annotation_id": "39778448", "url": "https://genius.com/39778448", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "Speak on me and gain a lot, just like the dish detergent", "vault_lines": ["Speak on me and gain a lot, just like the dish detergent"], "meaning": "Gain is a popular laundry and dish detergent but Drake uses the word to talk about the clout that is “gained” from talking about him. For instance, Kendrick Lamar’s April 2024 song “Not Like Us” is his biggest hit ever—a track aimed at Drake. It broke several records and was very successful commercially.", "annotation_id": "39778411", "url": "https://genius.com/39778411", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Little Birdie", "line": "Lauryn, why you actin’ like I’m not important to you? You was there in ’24 when niggas shot the door in", "vault_lines": ["Lauryn, why you actin' like I'm not important to you?", "You was there in '24 when niggas shot the door in"], "meaning": "On May 7, 2024, shortly after two in the morning, a security guard was seriously injured in a drive-by shooting outside Drake’s Toronto mansion in the Bridle Path area. Police reviewed the camera footage but could not identify the suspects.\n\nThe incident happened during the height of the Drake and Kendrick Lamar feud but there was no connection found between the two.", "annotation_id": "39776564", "url": "https://genius.com/39776564", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
