{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "And I be riding rims if my tires any thinner", "vault_lines": ["And I be riding rims if my tires any thinner"], "meaning": "Drake upgraded his car’s wheels to a size so big that his tires are barely there.\n\n\n\nHe should take care on the road, though. The additional weight of massive rims can put extra strain on a vehicle’s suspension and brakes. Plus, thin tires don’t absorb as much impact as standard sized tires.\n\nIn the words of Houston rapper Paul Wall: Drive slow, homie.", "annotation_id": "8310245", "url": "https://genius.com/8310245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "Ayy, yeah I swear like everytime we find ourself in this situation, you know I just get that feeling like I’m in Houston Candy paint switching colors in the light It’s about like 11 PM and you just rolling through the city Bumping that Screw, Big Moe, UGK, Lil’ Keke It feel like, like everything’s just moving slow Let’s take my time, I pace it baby, yeah, I’m gone", "vault_lines": ["And you gon' get undressed and we gon' get it on", "This is something I know, I know, I know", "I swear like everytime we find ourself in this situation, you know", "I just get that feeling like I'm in Houston", "Candy paint switching colors in the light", "It's about like 11 PM and you just rolling through the city", "Bumping that Screw, Big Moe, UGK, Lil' Keke", "It feel like, like everything's just moving slow", "Let's take my time, I pace it baby, yeah, I'm gone"], "meaning": "Drake punctuates this song with a final salute to Houston, shouting out hometown rap heroes and cultural trademarks, like iridescent, candy-painted cars.\n\n\n\nAs he sings earlier, Drake compares his interactions with a woman to the lethargic speed of this Texas city that’s characterized by chopped-and-screwed music and lean’s sluggish euphoria.", "annotation_id": "8309950", "url": "https://genius.com/8309950", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "I say my name is Drizzy and ain’t nobody realer", "vault_lines": ["I say my name is Drizzy and ain't nobody realer"], "meaning": "On “Congratulations,” Drake raps about the origins of his Drizzy moniker:\n\nDad named me Drake, Footz named me Drizzy\n\nMuch of Drizzy’s appeal as an artist is tied to how real he gets in his lyrics. Fabolous once told Sway that Drake’s “Resistance” is “one of the realest” songs he’s ever heard:\n\n[H]e said he just heard his grandma got moved to a nursing home and he be acting like he don’t know how to work a phone, but if you look through his phone, you see that he just called some chick he met at the mall that he barely knows at all. That hit me so hard. I said ‘Man, when was the last time I called my grandmoms?’ I had to stop the song and call my grand-moms.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/mM4yVbwckPU?t=8m49s", "annotation_id": "8300928", "url": "https://genius.com/8300928", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "Just met a girl, say she from the H-Town", "vault_lines": ["Just met a girl, say she from the H-Town"], "meaning": "Drake is probably referring to Maliah Michel, a stripper from Houston, or H-Town. They were in a brief relationship before breaking up over Twitter.", "annotation_id": "1688978", "url": "https://genius.com/1688978", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "Draped up, dripped out, know what I’m talking ’bout 3 in the morning, get it popping in the parking lot", "vault_lines": ["Draped up, dripped out, know what I'm talking 'bout", "3 in the morning, get it popping in the parking lot"], "meaning": "This is a play on “Pimp the Pen,” a popular Lil Keke’s track on DJ Screw’s 3 N Tha Mornin' Part ll tape.\n\n\n\n“Pimp the Pen” begins with, “Draped up dripped out know what I’m talking bout/Three in the morning, get the gat out the stash spot.”\n\nSome more Houston slang: “Draped up” means his car’s windows have tints so opaque that they resemble drapes.\n\n“Dripped out” alludes to the car’s candy paint—it’s so fresh that it’s virtually dripping off the frame.", "annotation_id": "710096", "url": "https://genius.com/710096", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "One time for the homie DJ Screw Already, I’m feeling throwed in this bitch", "vault_lines": ["One time for the homie DJ Screw", "Already, I'm feeling throwed in this bitch"], "meaning": "DJ Screw, the “chopped-and-screwed” genre’s namesake, has had a substantial influence on Houston hip-hop, even after his death from a codeine overdose in 2000.\n\n“Throwed” is Houston slang for being drunk—Paul Wall famously touched on the subject in “I’m Throwed.”", "annotation_id": "558239", "url": "https://genius.com/558239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "[Sample: Notorious B.I.G] It’s the ones that smoke blunts with ya, see ya picture Now they wanna grab the guns and come and get ya It’s the ones that smoke blunts with ya, see ya picture Now they wanna grab the guns and come and get ya", "vault_lines": ["It's the ones that smoke blunts with ya, see ya picture", "Now they wanna grab the guns and come and get ya"], "meaning": "This beat was from a famous freestyle on DJ Screw’s June 27 tape: a chopped-and-screwed version of Kris Kross‘ “Da Streets Ain’t Right.”\n\nJune 27 was arguably DJ Screw’s most famous tape, namely for the half-hour long freestyle over this instrumental. The tape was recorded and named in celebration of his homie D-Moe’s birthday.\n\nThese lyrics originally appear in The Notorious B.I.G.’s 1994 song “Warning.”", "annotation_id": "558238", "url": "https://genius.com/558238", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "I sent ya girl a message, said I’ll see ya when I can She sent me one back, but I ain’t never read it ’Cause pussy’s only pussy and I get it when I need it", "vault_lines": ["I sent ya girl a message, said I'll see ya when I can", "She sent me one back, but I ain't never read it", "'Cause pussy's only pussy and I get it when I need it"], "meaning": "Drake has maintained his dismissiveness over the years. On 2015’s “Star67,” he rhymes about brushing off supermodels.\n\nText from a centerfold, I ain’t reply/Let her know I read it though", "annotation_id": "386338", "url": "https://genius.com/386338", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "Don’t know why it happens, everytime we alone But here we are again and I swear I’m in my zone So I’ma sip this drank until that muhfucka gone And you gon’ get undressed and we gon’ get it on", "vault_lines": ["Don't know why it happens, everytime we alone", "But here we are again and I swear I'm in my zone", "So I'ma sip this drank until that muhfucka gone", "And you gon' get undressed and we gon' get it on", "This is something I know, I know, I know", "I swear like everytime we find ourself in this situation, you know"], "meaning": "There’s always sexual tension between Drake and this woman when they are alone together. As a result, Drake plans to get in the right mind state, or “zone,” (which would probably be classified as fucked up) so that they can “get it on.”", "annotation_id": "83016", "url": "https://genius.com/83016", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "And I’m telling you, I’m as cold as windows down in the winter", "vault_lines": ["And I'm telling you, I'm as cold as windows down in the winter"], "meaning": "Drake compares his flippant demeanor to the biting conditions of winter. The average January low temperature in Drake’s native Toronto is 19 °F—you’d definitely keep your windows shut tight.\n\n\n\nHe could also be saying he’s as cold as an actual window—glass—in the winter.", "annotation_id": "83008", "url": "https://genius.com/83008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "Got these boppas going crazy, nigga I’m, the man", "vault_lines": ["Got these boppas going crazy, nigga I'm, the man"], "meaning": "In Texas, the term “boppa” is slang for a promiscuous woman.\n\nThus, Drizzy has the hoes going crazy for him because of his fame, good looks, and luxurious lifestyle, all of which would classify any man as the man.", "annotation_id": "82993", "url": "https://genius.com/82993", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "Airport stunting, flying charters overseas Full of Dom Perignon and the water for the D’s", "vault_lines": ["Airport stunting, flying charters overseas", "Full of Dom Perignon and the water for the D's"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the UGK song “It’s Supposed to Bubble,” on which Bun B raps:\n\nYeah bitch, I got ya ho, but ho I’ll break ya knees\nFull of Dom Perignon, and water for the Gs\n\n\n\nLike 2 Chainz, Drake indulges in champagne on airplanes while the D's—or drivers—drink water.\n\nDrake has given glimpses of his private jet lifestyle on social media.", "annotation_id": "74365", "url": "https://genius.com/74365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "Up so high even when I’m coming down", "vault_lines": ["Up so high even when I'm coming down"], "meaning": "Drake plays with language here, juxtaposing getting “high” on marijuana with “coming down\"—Houston slang for pulling up in a mean slab.\n\n\n\nThis lyric could also mean that Drake remains high—on top of the rap game—even as he comes down from his drug-induced euphoria.", "annotation_id": "73071", "url": "https://genius.com/73071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "line": "Cup inside a cup, smoking Ghostface Killah", "vault_lines": ["Cup inside a cup, smoking Ghostface Killah"], "meaning": "Ghostface Killah—a nod to the storied Wu-Tang Clan rapper—is a name for a type of weed.\n\nDouble cupping is a common way that lean drinkers conceal the syrup as they sip—it also insulates the drink like a thermos.", "annotation_id": "70134", "url": "https://genius.com/70134", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "November 18th", "status": "done", "targets": 14, "clicked": 14, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-november-18th-lyrics", "genius_title": "November 18th"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "I’ve been doing this wrong, I’ve been here for too long", "vault_lines": ["I've been doing this wrong, I've been here for too long"], "meaning": "Drake’s career is taking off, and he realizes that he’s doing the label side of the business wrong. While boosting his career in the beginning, it’s time for Drake to move away from Cash Money and search for a better record deal.", "annotation_id": "41369115", "url": "https://genius.com/41369115", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "No more, no more, no more No more, no more, no more No more, no more, no more No more, no more", "vault_lines": ["No more, no more, no more", "No more, no more", "I can't stay here no more, and I can't sleep on the floor", "I ain't looking back no more, no, no, no", "No more, no more, no more, no more, no more"], "meaning": "Future background vocals?", "annotation_id": "37982604", "url": "https://genius.com/37982604", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "[Produced by Jimmy Johnson & Eric Dingus]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Eric Dingus of Austin, TX and Jimmy Johnson from Toronto – Teams up once more, this time to deliver a great beat, that is in fact a remake of the Eric Dingus remix of the Trap House 3 track by Gucci Mane and Rick Ross.\n\n\n\nThe vocal sample comes from Gayatri Mantra – Namasté.", "annotation_id": "4991730", "url": "https://genius.com/4991730", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "I just been out and bad from way back when", "vault_lines": ["I just been out and bad from way back when"], "meaning": "The phrase “out and bad” is an expression Jamaicans use when excited about something that’s “awesome” or “wicked”. It was made popular by the late dance legend Bogle).\n\nIn this case, Drake is saying he’s been “awesome” and “wicked” even before he became a star.", "annotation_id": "4892383", "url": "https://genius.com/4892383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "I can’t be out here on no laid back shit I can’t be out here on no laid back shit", "vault_lines": ["I can't be out here on no laid back shit"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t feel like he can have a laid back approach to things, probably due in part to his tense situation with Cash Money at the moment.\n\nNevertheless, The Boy has always had a strong work ethic, which he mentions in a lot of his music, including on this album. He wants every season to be OVO Season.", "annotation_id": "4889875", "url": "https://genius.com/4889875", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "I gotta go out and get it, want you to know what I did Want you to know how it went That’s why I keep telling you over and over again Just let me go Just let me go, let me bring it home to you Just let me go, let me bring it home to you Now and forever, I’ll bring it home to you Just let me go, let me bring it home Let me go, let me bring it home to you Let me go, let me bring it home to you Let me go, let me bring it home Now and forever, I’ll bring it home to you Let me go, let me bring it home to you Let me go, let me bring it home to you Now and forever, I’ll bring it home Now and forever, I’ll bring it home", "vault_lines": ["I just gotta know the road", "I gotta go out and get it, want you to know what I did", "Want you to know how it went", "That's why I keep telling you over and over again", "Just let me go", "Just let me go, let me bring it home to you", "Now and forever, I'll bring it home to you", "Just let me go, let me bring it home", "Let me go, let me bring it home to you", "Let me go, let me bring it home", "Now and forever, I'll bring it home"], "meaning": "These 5 bars blur the line between reality and fiction, and point to the fact that Drake is not outwardly dissing his label or Birdman. He describes a situation where a girlfriend or partner is holding Drake back, stopping him from exploring the world and creating his own legacy. He says, as a way of diffusing the situation, if you let me go, I will bring the spoils of victory back for you.\n\nWe know Drake is a sensitive soul, so his more concilatory tone is understandable.", "annotation_id": "4888647", "url": "https://genius.com/4888647", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "I just gotta hit the road, I just gotta know the road I just gotta hit the road, I just gotta know the road I just gotta know the road", "vault_lines": ["I just gotta hit the road, I just gotta know the road", "I just gotta know the road", "I gotta get on the road, I gotta get on the road"], "meaning": "To “hit the road” is an expression meaning to split, usually related to a marriage or relationship, and made famous by Ray Charles' song “Hit The Road Jack”.", "annotation_id": "4888620", "url": "https://genius.com/4888620", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "I’m afraid that I’ma die before I get where I’m going I know I’ma be alone, I know I’m out on my own", "vault_lines": ["I'm afraid that I'ma die before I get where I'm going", "I know I'ma be alone, I know I'm out on my own"], "meaning": "Tyga has expressed his desire to become fully independent, and Lil Wayne seems to be moving in a similar direction. However, Drake’s situation is vastly different. He has his own record label: “OVO Sound”, which is not affiliated with Cash Money Records, although Drake, as a solo artist, is signed primarily to Cash Money Records.\n\nIf he did decide to split, he would be doing so with an established label already at his disposal.", "annotation_id": "4888617", "url": "https://genius.com/4888617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "I don’t wanna miss the boat, I don’t wanna sit in coach I don’t wanna sit at home, I gotta get where I’m going", "vault_lines": ["I don't wanna miss the boat, I don't wanna sit in coach", "I don't wanna sit at home, I gotta get where I'm going", "I gotta get on the road, I gotta get on the road"], "meaning": "Is Drake feeling as though he is being held back by his label and his record contract?\n\nSince signing to Cash Money in 2009, he has sold over 5 million records, is considered one of the most influential rappers of the new school, and has 8 BET awards and a Grammy to his name.\n\n“Sitting in coach” refers to economy class on an airplane or bus, which ties in with the current monetary problems that Cash Money seem to be having.\n\nStill, I don’t think Drake has flown coach in a while..", "annotation_id": "4888611", "url": "https://genius.com/4888611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "Had to knock down the wall Yeah, I swear to God that I’m gone, I’m leaving, I’m leaving No looking back when I’m gone, no looking back when I’m gone No more, no more, no more, no more, no more", "vault_lines": ["It's over, it's over, yeah, I'm leaving, I'm gone", "No more, no more, no more", "No more, no more", "Yeah, I'm leaving, I'm leaving, I'm leaving, I'm gone", "I ain't looking back no more, no, no, no", "Yeah, I'm leaving, yeah, I'm leaving, yeah, I'm leaving, I'm gone", "I'm leaving, I'm gone", ", had to knock down the wall", "Yeah, I swear to God that I'm gone, I'm leaving, I'm leaving", "No looking back when I'm gone, no looking back when I'm gone", "No more, no more, no more, no more, no more"], "meaning": "Ever since the Lil Wayne vs Birdman story dropped, people have been curious as to the status of Drake, and whether he would leave Cash Money if Wayne left.\n\nWayne addressed this (partially) on the shuddering first track to his mixtape Sorry 4 The Wait 2, on the first track “CoCo”.\n\nThis is the closest Drake has come to taking sides on the matter.", "annotation_id": "4888595", "url": "https://genius.com/4888595", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "Man, I’m leaving, I’m leaving, you know I got my reasons Yeah, I’m leaving, yeah, I’m leaving, yeah, I’m leaving, I’m gone I’m leaving, I’m gone", "vault_lines": ["It's over, it's over, yeah, I'm leaving, I'm gone", "I'm leaving, I'm leaving, you know I got my reasons", "Yeah, I'm leaving, I'm leaving, I'm leaving, I'm gone", "Man, I'm leaving, I'm leaving, you know I got my reasons", "Yeah, I'm leaving, yeah, I'm leaving, yeah, I'm leaving, I'm gone", "I'm leaving, I'm gone"], "meaning": "While Lil Wayne left mainly because of the delay of his upcoming album, and the fact that there were contractual obligations that Cash Money didn’t adhere to, Drake explains on this track that he too has reasons to feel slighted by the label that birthed him.\n\nDrake has had label issues dating back to 2012, and numerous artists, including Turk have come out and said that Birdman and Cash Money do not pay their artists money they are owed.\n\nDrake has not outwardly expressed that he is leaving Cash Money, neither has Nicki Minaj, but their labelmate Tyga has already attempted to split from the label.", "annotation_id": "4888337", "url": "https://genius.com/4888337", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "I can’t stay here no more, and I can’t sleep on the floor", "vault_lines": ["No more, no more, no more", "No more, no more", "I can't stay here no more, and I can't sleep on the floor", "No more, no more, no more, no more, no more"], "meaning": "Due to the unfair deals that Birdman and YMCMB have been known to give, Drake has made a lot less money than he could have. Although Drake and (his estimated 55 million dollar net worth) is not actually sleeping on the floor, he is making a lot less money than he could be.", "annotation_id": "4888206", "url": "https://genius.com/4888206", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Now & Forever", "line": "It’s over, it’s over, yeah, I’m leaving, I’m gone", "vault_lines": ["It's over, it's over, yeah, I'm leaving, I'm gone", "Yeah, I'm leaving, I'm leaving, I'm leaving, I'm gone", "Yeah, I'm leaving, yeah, I'm leaving, yeah, I'm leaving, I'm gone", "I'm leaving, I'm gone", "Yeah, I swear to God that I'm gone, I'm leaving, I'm leaving"], "meaning": "Similar to his mentor Lil Wayne, Drizzy is leaving Cash Money, and rebelling against Birdman. Although he is a little less direct than Weezy was, he still gets the point across.", "annotation_id": "4888168", "url": "https://genius.com/4888168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
