{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stay Down", "line": "Let me get a quickie, speed stick, no deodorant To cover up the smell ’cause your parents are the nosiest", "vault_lines": ["Let me get a quickie, speed stick, no deodorant", "To cover up the smell 'cause your parents are the nosiest"], "meaning": "Drake wants to have sex in a short amount of time as he mentions quickie followed by a play on word with “speed stick” which is a deodorant and also a metaphor for dick and also plays on the word “nosiest” as it relates back to the word “Smell” as the parents of the woman dealing would suspicion due to smell of after sex.", "annotation_id": "20138685", "url": "https://genius.com/20138685", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Stay Down", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 1, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-stay-down-lyrics", "genius_title": "Stay Down"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "I ain’t even have to cut the tie, it severed itself", "vault_lines": ["I ain't even have to cut the tie, it severed itself"], "meaning": "Drake is likely referring to his ties with Kanye West here\nPreviously affiliated with Kanye West, even having collaborated with the artist on his hit track, “Forever” Drake is stating that cutting ties with him was not required, but instead happened on it’s own with time.\n\nAs for why Drake would’ve wanted to cut ties with Kanye West, this may be due to his claims of writing for Kanye and helping him out. This was referenced by Drake on his track, “Duppy Freestyle” where he mentions writing for Kanye West’s, “Father Stretch My Hands Pt. 1”. This is confirmed by looking at the song’s writing credits.", "annotation_id": "35059472", "url": "https://genius.com/35059472", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "Yeah, I was about to— Man, I thought about it It’s unsettlin’ to talk about it", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I was about to— Man, I thought about it", "It's unsettlin' to talk about it"], "meaning": "While Drake leaves this line up to interpretation, he may be referring to his alleged “career ending diss track” in response to Pusha T’s scathing Drake diss “The Story of Adidon”. Drake discusses this in his 2019 Rap Radar interview:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/mmsasEoupO4?si=JO8kjzw0wFmwqYWd&t=1h09m25s\n\nJ Prince corroborated Drake’s claim, alleging he told Drake not to release the diss due to the personal content matter. This may be why it is “unsettling” for Drake to talk about it. J said this in an interview with DLTR radio:\n\nAfter speaking with Kanye, I spoke with the brother…and he didn’t want this. I saw this going to a place that I feel would have ended his career if Drake would’ve put out this song that he had on him. It definitely would’ve hurt families and we’re not in it for that. That’s not Drake’s character to tear a man down to that extent.\n[…]\nOh no doubt about it… the ingredients were overwhelming. I know for a fact that it would’ve been bad for Kanye and my man [Pusha T]. It wouldn’t have been good, it’s beyond music at that point. It’s gonna affect the livelihood, it’s gonna interfere with his entire lifestyle from that day forward.\n\nJ Prince, Drake and Kanye", "annotation_id": "32227677", "url": "https://genius.com/32227677", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "Free all of my niggas that they caught with it", "vault_lines": ["Free all of my niggas that they caught with it", "RIP my niggas that they caught without it"], "meaning": "Reference to the iconic phrase “rather be caught with it than without it” meaning its better to be in jail cause the police caught you possessing a deadly weapon than to be dead because the opps ran up on you when you didn’t have a gun.", "annotation_id": "29802694", "url": "https://genius.com/29802694", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "Takin’ shots with the GOAT and talked about shots that we sendin’", "vault_lines": ["Takin' shots with the GOAT and talked about shots that we sendin'"], "meaning": "Drake talks about sitting down with JAY-Z, who Drizzy and many others refer to as the GOAT of rap, to discuss the subliminal shots they’ve been taking at each others over the years.\n\nIn Drake’s 2019 Rap Radar interview, he confirmed he and Hov genuinely did have a sit down to discuss the shots they’ve been taking at each other:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/mmsasEoupO4?feature=shared&t=1h19m45s", "annotation_id": "20309743", "url": "https://genius.com/20309743", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "Who do you really love? Well, that’s sure to be in question", "vault_lines": ["Who do you really love? Well, that's sure to be in question"], "meaning": "This line could be Drake addressing how he allegedly appeared to use a hefty amount of Rappin' 4-Tay’s lyrics from the 1992 song “Playaz Club” on his verse for YG’s 2014 hit “Who Do You Love.” He reportedly payed out $100k for this back in 2014, and has been sent another invoice for it in June of 2018 by 4-Tay (in a joking manner) after 4-Tay saw Drake send Pusha T an invoice for services rendered after “Duppy Freestyle.”", "annotation_id": "14900164", "url": "https://genius.com/14900164", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "Always got a ace up my sleeve for whatever was dealt", "vault_lines": ["Always got a ace up my sleeve for whatever was dealt"], "meaning": "An “ace up your sleeve'' refers to one secretly knowing something that provides him/her with an advantage that the opposition is unaware of.\n\nRegardless of the situation Drake finds himself in, he always has ammo for those that throw shots at him.\n\nWhen Meek Mill alleged that Drake had a ghostwriter after having featured Drake on his track R.I.C.O., Drake quickly fired back with the tracks “Charged Up'' and “Back to Back''.\n\nWhen Pusha T took numerous shots at Drake on his album Daytona, Drake fired back in less than twenty-four hours with his track “Duppy”.\n\nNo matter the situation, Drake will be prepared to deal with the opposition and his responses to Meek Mill and Pusha T serve as evidence.", "annotation_id": "14893190", "url": "https://genius.com/14893190", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "Who’s givin’ out this much return on investment? After my run, man, how is that even a question? After this summer, man, how is that even a—", "vault_lines": ["Who's givin' out this much return on investment?", "After my run, man, how is that even a question?", "After this summer, man, how is that even a—"], "meaning": "Drake was signed to Young Money, Lil Wayne’s label, back in 2009. After a almost a decade, he’s one of the most successful artists of his generation, and has also broken several records with his music. So we can say that he definitely did not disappoint those who had faith in him and invested in his career.", "annotation_id": "14884405", "url": "https://genius.com/14884405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "I have tea with the stars and I swim with the sharks And I see in the dark, wasn’t this cold at the start", "vault_lines": ["I have tea with the stars and I swim with the sharks", "And I see in the dark, wasn't this cold at the start"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake is trying to draw a sharp contrast between two very distinct parts of his life.\n\nDrake could be referring to a moment he mentioned on “Days In The East” where he rapped:\n\nRemember one night I went to Erykah Badu house, she made tea for me\nWe talked about love and what life could really be for me\nShe said, “when that shit is real, you just know”\n\nThis describes the calm and relaxing part of celebrity, where stars can simply share experiences. But, Drake continues by saying he needs to watch over his shoulders, because many people in the industry don’t want the best for him and are ready to start a beef.\n\nSharks can see even in dark or murky water, which means, that if rappers want to bring it, he’s ready, but he feels like the environment was warmer when he started out almost a decade ago.", "annotation_id": "14878027", "url": "https://genius.com/14878027", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "All of this disorder, no addressin’ The crown is broken in pieces, but there’s more in my possession There’s a whole lot in my possession", "vault_lines": ["All of this disorder, no addressin'", "The crown is broken in pieces, but there's more in my possession", "There's a whole lot in my possession"], "meaning": "This “disorder” that Drake certainly most likely refers to his beef with Pusha-T that would take place between May-June 2018. The latter rapper sent subliminals towards Drake in “Infrared”, however he responded hours later with “Duppy Freestyle”. Pusha then responded with “The Story of Adidon” where he revealed some of Drake’s controversial family and personal problems including hiding a son.\n\nDrake didn’t respond to Pusha-T’s diss but instead waited for Scorpion to drop to address the rumors such as in “Emotionless”:\n\nI wasn’t hidin' my kid from the world\nI was hidin' the world from my kid\n\nDrake claims that he is still on top of the game, even though the crown is broken. But he also acknowledges that it’s not just about hip-hop now; there is more at stake. He has a son and is a major influence for millions of people, and wants to take responsibility for what he does.", "annotation_id": "14878024", "url": "https://genius.com/14878024", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "I am a cream-of-the-crop nigga", "vault_lines": ["I am a cream-of-the-crop nigga"], "meaning": "Cream is the most concentrated, and desirable part in a batch of milk. The term has been used to mean “the best” since the 16th century. So in this case, the idiom “cream-of-the-crop” means that Drake refers to himself as the best rapper today.", "annotation_id": "14878018", "url": "https://genius.com/14878018", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "RIP my niggas that they caught without it", "vault_lines": ["Free all of my niggas that they caught with it", "RIP my niggas that they caught without it"], "meaning": "Drake’s friend and OVO associate Anthony “Fif” Soares was savagely gunned down in Toronto in late 2017 as he was returning home to his apartment.\n\nThe two assailants sprayed Soares with bullets as he was defenseless and even shot more after he was seemingly unresponsive at the end.", "annotation_id": "14877420", "url": "https://genius.com/14877420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "I’m pretty sure we got a label, I’m still independent", "vault_lines": ["I'm pretty sure we got a label, I'm still independent"], "meaning": "Drake founded OVO Sound in 2012 along with Noah “40” Shebib. Having his own personal label grants him a massive amount of creative freedom.\n\nHowever, he is also still signed to Young Money and still releases music through Warner Bros Records.", "annotation_id": "14877156", "url": "https://genius.com/14877156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "I fell back a hundred times when I don’t get the credit Seen this movie a hundred times, I know where it’s headed Realize someone gotta die when no one’ll dead it Niggas gamblin’ with they life for some content That’s the type of lottery that could get your top picked", "vault_lines": ["I fell back a hundred times when I don't get the credit", "Seen this movie a hundred times, I know where it's headed", "Realize someone gotta die when no one'll dead it", "Niggas gamblin' with they life for some content", "That's the type of lottery that could get your top picked", "Free all of my niggas that they caught with it"], "meaning": "Drake often spoke about the fact that people don’t give him the credit he feels he deserves, because he’s not considered black enough.\n\nBlack excellence, but I guess when it comes to me it’s not the same though, all goodie\n\nWhen Pusha-T re-ignited the accusation that Drake has ghostwriters, he already knew what he had to do, because he’s been dealing with it for his whole career. That’s why after Pusha-T released The Story Of Adidon, Drake felt the line had been crossed, and someone needed to put an end to it before it got out of hand.\n\nThis was a job left to founder of Rap-a-Lot records J-Prince, who took a press run announcing that Drake wasn’t going to release a response because it would have “career ending consequences” for Kanye West. A truce was called instead.\n\nThe lottery is part of the annual NBA Draft, a process that brings a high-level selection of collegiate basketball players into the professional league.\n\nIn this scenery, rappers are gambling their careers when they diss Drake to get some views, because he’s the top rapper right now, and they know he could easily end their careers.", "annotation_id": "14876984", "url": "https://genius.com/14876984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "You niggas pop mollies, my ’Malis pop niggas", "vault_lines": ["You niggas pop mollies, my 'Malis pop niggas"], "meaning": "Molly is a name for the capsule or powder form of MDMA. To “pop” molly is to ingest the drug.\n\nDrake refers to “'Malis” or Somali people, and uses the word “pop” to express the act of shooting.\n\nDrake has talked about his allegiances with Somali natives in the past, as mentioned on “Draft Day”:\n\nKnow some Somalis that say we got it Wallahi\n\nHe could also refer to Toronto, his hometown, as more than 50 percent of the city’s residents are foreign born. – Among them, Somali Canadians are the largest African immigrant population in the city.", "annotation_id": "14876646", "url": "https://genius.com/14876646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "House on both coasts, but I live on the charts", "vault_lines": ["House on both coasts, but I live on the charts"], "meaning": "Drake is the most popular rapper in the game as of Scorpion’s release, with numerous songs in the Billboard 100.\n\nThis line can be taken very literally. Drake spent a total of 431 consecutive weeks on the Hot 100, a run that dated back to 2009 and ended only in August 2017.\n\nFurthermore, Drizzy has multiple cribs in the US as well as Canada. He owns a sprawling $10-million-dollar mansion in Hidden Hills, California as well as a mansion in Toronto, Canada, his hometown.", "annotation_id": "14876563", "url": "https://genius.com/14876563", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "I’ve had scuffles with bad boys that wasn’t pretendin’", "vault_lines": ["I've had scuffles with bad boys that wasn't pretendin'"], "meaning": "Drake references his beef with Puff Daddy, that was unveiled on “4PM in Calabasas”:\n\nEven had the OG’s tryna press me, ha-ha-ha, ha\nNo way out ‘cause I’m already in it\n\nIn 2014, Drake and Diddy were feuding over “0-100/ The Catch Up.” Drake supposedly was going to include Diddy, but after waiting for nearly eight months, he released the track on his own. Diddy didn’t appreciate the success Drake received for it and was caught on camera taking a swing at Drake while the two were at LIV Nightclub in Miami.\n\n“Bad boys” is a reference to P. Diddy’s label Bad Boy Records, and possibly also a reference to a track released in 2001 under his alias “Puff Daddy” titled “Bad Boy For Life.”", "annotation_id": "14876352", "url": "https://genius.com/14876352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "This is God’s plan, young man, you said it yourself", "vault_lines": ["This is God's plan, young man, you said it yourself"], "meaning": "In conjunction with the last line, Drake discusses the success of his future and the rest of the album with this line. It is also a clear reference to his longest leading Billboard Hot 100 track “God’s Plan”, where he raps about his position in the industry and the fact that people want him to fail; however, God simply won’t let that happen.\n\nDrake has let God choose his path in the past–on the If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late cut “Jungle,” he raps:\n\nThese days, I’m lettin' God handle all things above me", "annotation_id": "14876250", "url": "https://genius.com/14876250", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "Daddy got suits like Bernie Mac, he dresses himself I stopped askin’ myself and I started feelin’ myself", "vault_lines": ["Daddy got suits like Bernie Mac, he dresses himself", "I stopped askin' myself and I started feelin' myself"], "meaning": "Drake makes a reference to his father Dennis Graham, who has made various appearances throughout Drake’s catalog (including album covers). Dennis has his own suit line featuring many formidable outfits.\n\n\n\nHere Drizzy compares Dennis to deceased stand up comedian Bernie Mac, also notorious for his ambitious wardrobe.\n\nThis could be a response to Pusha T’s lines in “Story Of Adidon”, which instead compared him to Steve Harvey:\n\nMonkey-suit Dennis, you parade him\nA Steve Harvey-suit nigga made him\n\nLastly, Drake makes a reference to “Feelin' Myself,” the 2004 hit by Mac Dre, who gets a shout out even on “Nonstop.”", "annotation_id": "14876194", "url": "https://genius.com/14876194", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "My Mount Rushmore is me with four different expressions", "vault_lines": ["My Mount Rushmore is me with four different expressions"], "meaning": "Mount Rushmore is a mountain sculpture in South Dakota. The carving depicts George Washington, Thomas Jefferson, Theodore Roosevelt, and Abraham Lincoln. All of the people depicted are widely regarded as the top four presidents of all time.\nhttps://imgur.com/KrU2tuz\n\nThe phrase “Mount Rushmore” has also been used to describe someone’s top four in a particular subject. For example, ESPN correspondent Stephen A. Smith has previously stated that LeBron James is on the “Mount Rushmore of Basketball.”\n\nDrake’s Mount Rushmore could also be his album covers: Thank Me Later, Take Care, Nothing Was The Same, and Scorpion all have his face on them, each having a different expression which represents the tone of such album.", "annotation_id": "14876175", "url": "https://genius.com/14876175", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "This just the intro, let me not get ahead of myself", "vault_lines": ["This just the intro, let me not get ahead of myself"], "meaning": "Ever since So Far Gone catapulted him to stardom in 2009, each of Drake’s projects has used the intro as a moment for introspection–songs like “Fireworks”, “Over My Dead Body”, and “Legend” revealed where Drake believed he was in his terms of his career. On “Tuscan Leather,” the opening track off 2013’s Nothing Was the Same, Drake even asks, “How much time this nigga spending on the intro?” He takes his own rhetoric on board this time, exchanging a 6-minute intro for a succinct 2-minute one.\n\nIn a 2009 interview with Complex, Drake outlines the intro strategy he’d eventually use time and time again, stating:\n\nIt starts with this monologue, ‘Lust for Life,’ of me crying out in my head the things that I never say. The things that I was just thinking, that was my mindset. Not only was I confused about my career, I was also in a very destructive, sort of exhausting relationship with a female and it was just a bad headspace for me to be in. So that’s where the tape starts.\n\nOn this particular intro, Drake raps as though he is secure in his status and aims to create even more space between him and supposed counterparts. However, he knows his intros are hard, so he reminds himself to slow down.", "annotation_id": "14876169", "url": "https://genius.com/14876169", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "line": "I’ve had real Philly niggas try to write my endin’", "vault_lines": ["I've had real Philly niggas try to write my endin'"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Drake’s now infamous beef with Meek Mill, a Philly native.\n\nThe beef started when Meek leveled accusations of ghostwriting at Drake in 2015 – specifically for his contribution to the song R.I.C.O. Reference tracks surfaced backing up Meeks claim, and the ghostwriter in question – Quintin Miller – was not credited on the album.\n\nEssentially, artists like Meek tried to ‘write’ the ending of Drake’s reign with accusations of ghost-‘writing’ which he’s been battling since. Drake refutes the ghostwriting allegations by making it clear he is going to write his own ending, specifically to his career, firmly stating he controls his own fate and no allegations can tear him down.\n\nMeek and Drake squashed their beef in 2018. As such, this is less a diss at Meek, and more a dispassionate reflection on what happened, as Drake credits Meek for his “real” street ties.\n\nDuring their beef Drake also turned up to Philly, where several goons attempted to press him, i.e. actually end him.", "annotation_id": "14876126", "url": "https://genius.com/14876126", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Survival", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 21, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-survival-lyrics", "genius_title": "Survival"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Zone", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-zone-lyrics", "genius_title": "Zone"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "And here’s another hit, Barry Bonds (Barry Bonds)", "vault_lines": ["And here's another hit, Barry Bonds (Barry Bonds)"], "meaning": "Barry bonds is arguably one of the greatest batters in MLB history if not the greatest. With the highest home runs hit in a season ever.\n\nIn this verse, Drake likens himself to Barry Bonds. Claiming that he makes hit music just like Barry bonds gets home runs", "annotation_id": "35669902", "url": "https://genius.com/35669902", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "Nigga, keep your two cents, I ain’t tryna leave you broke", "vault_lines": ["Well, I'm thinkin' I should leave out on this note, nigga, keep your two cents, I ain't tryna leave you broke"], "meaning": "The phrase “let me give my 2 cents” is used when people are referring to giving you advice in a situation.\n\nIn this verse Drake talks about this both in the literal and metaphorical sense. Keeping your two cents meaning keeping your opinion and keeping your two cents as that’s all they have left.", "annotation_id": "35669833", "url": "https://genius.com/35669833", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "And me, I’m rolling with some brokers, like, damn, could you niggas get any broker?", "vault_lines": ["And me, I'm rolling with some brokers, like, damn, could you niggas get any broker?"], "meaning": "Usually brokers are in the bank taking care of money/stock however drake uses this also as an insult in the second verse to criticise how little money his opposition has.", "annotation_id": "35669821", "url": "https://genius.com/35669821", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "Nowadays, rappin’ is a children’s hobby", "vault_lines": ["Nowadays, rappin' is a children's hobby"], "meaning": "Drake says that now rapping is a children’s hobby saying that the new generation of rappers are emerging.\n\nIronically nowadays, touching kids is drakes hobby showing how he uses his status against the kids", "annotation_id": "32847651", "url": "https://genius.com/32847651", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "Millionaire shades, fade with the waves, I smirk at a nigga if he still rockin’ braids That just let’s me know that we ain’t on the same page and that goes out to every nigga, except Trey", "vault_lines": ["Millionaire shades, fade with the waves, I smirk at a nigga if he still rockin' braids"], "meaning": "Here Drake takes a clear shot at Pusha T. Pusha has been rocking his signature braids for almost two decades, and Drake is envious of them because they look amazing and Drake’s hair won’t grow like that.\n\nIronically Drake, the perennial culture vulture, would go on to start wearing cornrows himself in 2022. Many speculate that this isn’t even Drake‘s real hair and that he wears a weave. The other irony in this lyric is that Drake compliments Trey Songz alone for rocking braids; meanwhile, Trey turned out to be a serial sex offender and Drake has been accused of inappropriate behavior with minors.\n\nDrake has been going at Pusha T since the very beginning of his rap career, mainly due an early snub from Pusha, who Drake idolized. The Clipse dropped out of Drake’s debut tour “Lights Dreams and Nightmares” in the summer of 2010, before it had even gotten started. Drake’s team then had to scramble to replace them, adding the mostly unknown Tyga as the opening act (Tyga at the time was signed to Young Money, although not for long).\n\nDrake and Pusha were initially on good terms, with Drake having been a huge Clipse fan since 2002 and even hopped on a track with Malice in 2007. However, they soon went separate ways as Drake chose to sign with Birdman, Lil Wayne, and Cash Money, with whom both The Clipse and Pharrell of the Neptunes had bad business dealings years prior (20 years later, Birdman aka Baby has yet to pay Pharrell or The Clipse for the beat or lyrics that led to the huge commercial success of “What Happened to That Boy”). Pusha T went on to sign as a solo artist with Kanye’s much more successful and prolific G.O.O.D. Music label, of which he was soon appointed President. Meanwhile, Drake ended up stuck and trapped in a ten album contract with YMCMB, had to sue them in court, still has to pay J. Prince, Birdman, and Lil Wayne more than 30% of his profits, and still doesn’t own his masters. Kanye helped Pusha get his masters and he has the freedom to release whenever he so chooses with the full backing and promotion of his label.\n\nWise choice on the braids Push! Bad move by Drake here with this lyric – yikes!", "annotation_id": "27224767", "url": "https://genius.com/27224767", "state": "pending", "votes": -4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "He just mad ’cause his girl at the house with her tongue sticking out like a Michael Jordan rookie card", "vault_lines": ["He just mad 'cause his girl at the house with her tongue sticking out like a Michael Jordan rookie card"], "meaning": "Michael Jordan, the greatest basketball player of all time, was known to play with his tongue hanging out. He did this especially in big moments, usually when he was focusing very hard, and seeing Jordan with his tongue out became a well known sign of greatness and put fear in his opponents.\n\nIronically, Jordan actually does have his tongue hanging out in his rookie card.\n\n\n\nIn 2011, a perfect condition Michael Jordan rookie card sold for $100,000.", "annotation_id": "20438195", "url": "https://genius.com/20438195", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "But I’m tryna have you on that trip with me, sliding through Henri Bendel, like it’s slippery", "vault_lines": ["But I'm tryna have you on that trip with me, sliding through Henri Bendel, like it's slippery"], "meaning": "Drake wants to take his girl on a trip to NYC to slide through the Henri Bendel flagship store. Henri Bendel was a very high end fashion brand, mostly known for their handbags.\n\nIn January 2019, after 123 years in business, Henri Bendel closed all its store and shut down its website.", "annotation_id": "20438186", "url": "https://genius.com/20438186", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "Might cut the phone and disappear like Michie Mee", "vault_lines": [", might cut the phone and disappear like Michie Mee"], "meaning": "Michie Mee is known for being the first prominent female Canadian rapper, who started her career by being thrown on stage and introduced to the Toronto crowd (by KRS One) at 15 years old.\n\nAfter moderate success in the late 80s and early 90s, Michie basically ended her music career to transition to acting, causing her to theoretically “disappear” to the public.", "annotation_id": "20438151", "url": "https://genius.com/20438151", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "A lot of ice, a lot of cream like Dickie Dee", "vault_lines": ["A lot of ice, a lot of cream like Dickie Dee"], "meaning": "Dickie Dee is a Canadian brand of ice cream that Drake is probably very familiar with from his childhood since he grew up in Canada.\n\nIce refers to jewelry and cream refers to money. Drake has a lot of both, just like Dickie Dee’s.", "annotation_id": "20438128", "url": "https://genius.com/20438128", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "You and your whole crew are like a deck of fifty-four, so it’s obvious, y’all gon’ steady be rolling with some jokers", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Most people consider a deck of cards to be 52 cards, but a lot of decks come with 2 extra Joker cards that aren’t used in most games, bringing the total to 54.\n\nDrake is saying your crew has some “jokers”, or lames  in it, just like a deck of 54 cards.", "annotation_id": "20438111", "url": "https://genius.com/20438111", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "And you can probably find him walking out of a Macy’s", "vault_lines": ["And you can probably find him walking out of a Macy's"], "meaning": "Macy’s is a mid-range department store. Here,  Drake suggests that his competition, especially those pursuing the same women as him, can’t afford to shop at luxury department stores such as Bloomingdale’s and Saks.\n\nCommon brands carried by Macy’s include Polo Ralph Lauren, Tommy Hilfinger, and Nautica.", "annotation_id": "10093407", "url": "https://genius.com/10093407", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "You took that ’Ye beat and you put that shit in a choke", "vault_lines": ["Exit with a joke, leave these niggas some hope, you took that 'Ye beat and you put that shit in a choke"], "meaning": "Barry Bonds is a song off Kanye’s Graduation album. The beat was co-produced by Nottz and Kanye West.\n\nDrake is absolutely dominating the beat like he has it in a choke hold.", "annotation_id": "90705", "url": "https://genius.com/90705", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "It’s what y’all all been waiting fo’, ain’t it?", "vault_lines": ["It's what y'all all been waiting fo', ain't it?"], "meaning": "Drake is saying he’s the latest in entertainment, in a music industry that craves new blood.\nAlso, this same line starts Kanye West’s song of the same name", "annotation_id": "82395", "url": "https://genius.com/82395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "Just because I got a buzz like a building lobby", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Wordplay on “buzz”\n\n\nThe bell that can be rung to enter the building makes a buzz.\nBuzz as in lots of people talking about you\nBeing high on a drug gives a pleasant buzzing throughout your body.", "annotation_id": "80901", "url": "https://genius.com/80901", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "So I keep it rocking, for Pete’s sakes", "vault_lines": ["So I keep it rocking, for Pete's sakes"], "meaning": "Play on words with reference to a old school rapper Pete Rock.\n\nThe “hidden” reference to Pete Rock is appropriate, since Drake is rapping about how he has to keep his street/underground side hidden to sell records and please his label.", "annotation_id": "79153", "url": "https://genius.com/79153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "I tried to sell weed, give me cash for the zip", "vault_lines": ["And ain't a song that yo' ass finna skip, I tried to sell weed, give me cash for the zip"], "meaning": "People put weed in ziploc bags so the smell doesn’t get in the air.  This makes it harder for drug dogs to catch them, but sometimes, they are still caught..\n\nA zip is also drug slang for an ounce.", "annotation_id": "78310", "url": "https://genius.com/78310", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "line": "Since we getting Seinfeld on some Jerry and Elaine shit", "vault_lines": ["Since we getting Seinfeld on some Jerry and Elaine shit"], "meaning": "On the tv show Seinfeld, Jerry and Elaine had dated (you could tell from the comic energy that they’d previously fiznucked)\n\n\n\nThis line also sounds similar to “gettin' signed failed.” This would allude to how Drake was unsigned, but still ripping apart beats. Refer to this line from “Forever” for more details.", "annotation_id": "76487", "url": "https://genius.com/76487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)", "status": "done", "targets": 17, "clicked": 17, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-barry-bonds-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Barry Bonds (Freestyle)"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Lord, forgive me (Lord, forgive me), it’s my turn", "vault_lines": ["Lord, forgive me (Lord, forgive me), it's my turn", "Lord, forgive me (Lord, forgive me)"], "meaning": "Here Drake highlights a shift from trying to keep the peace to seeking retaliation and burning ties.", "annotation_id": "40836551", "url": "https://genius.com/40836551", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Capo got so many watches, he might clock a nigga", "vault_lines": ["Capo got so many watches, he might clock a nigga", "Capo got so many watches, he need supervision"], "meaning": "This bar plays on two different timepieces: watches and clocks. Drake says Chubbs (Capo)  has so many watches that he might “clock” somebody. While “clock” refers to another way of telling time, it’s also slang for hitting someone. The result is a layered punchline that turns a luxury watch flex into a threat at the same time.\n\nThe threat carries extra weight because Chubbs has a reputation for actually “clocking” people. Having an altercation with producer Detail, as well as long-standing rumors surrounding an incident involving D.R.A.M.", "annotation_id": "40129276", "url": "https://genius.com/40129276", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Now they drop the top so much, they got a drop addiction", "vault_lines": ["Now they drop the top so much, they got a drop addiction"], "meaning": "Following the previous line, Drake reflects on the financial glow-up of the people around him, going from not having a pot to piss in to riding around in drop-top vehicles. He ties in the addiction theme through a play on the word “drop,” which can also be slang for a pill, suggesting that the luxury lifestyle has become addictive.", "annotation_id": "40129184", "url": "https://genius.com/40129184", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "I’d rather take the long way", "vault_lines": ["I'd rather take the long way"], "meaning": "Drake is saying he ain’t worried about losing industry favors, connections, or the perks that come with being in everybody’s good graces. After burning a few bridges, he’s cool with taking the long way around if that’s what it takes. Instead of playing politics or depending on people behind the scenes, he’d rather bet on himself and grind it out alone. The line carries a “me against the world” mentality, with Drake making it clear that he’d rather move on his own terms than fake relationships just to keep doors open.", "annotation_id": "40129152", "url": "https://genius.com/40129152", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Part II", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I have think the slowed down tempo of this part might be poking fun at ASAP Rocky’s style of rapping", "annotation_id": "40098789", "url": "https://genius.com/40098789", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Burning, burning", "vault_lines": ["Burning bridges is okay"], "meaning": "This alludes to ASAP Rocky’s Don’t be dumb Album song “Whiskey” where Rocky says i don’t burn to bridges or friendship and more subliminal disses towards Drake.", "annotation_id": "40016281", "url": "https://genius.com/40016281", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Tables turn, tables turn, bridges burn, bridges burn", "vault_lines": ["Tables turn, tables turn, bridges burn, bridges burn"], "meaning": "“Tables turn, bridges burn, you live and learn” is a famous quote from Drake’s 2013 hit Pound Cake.", "annotation_id": "39895253", "url": "https://genius.com/39895253", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Capo got so many watches, he need supervision", "vault_lines": ["Capo got so many watches, he might clock a nigga", "Capo got so many watches, he need supervision"], "meaning": "A play on words where Drake mentions that Chubbs needs to be observed because of the number of watches he has, and at the same time it could mean that Chubbs needs a supervisory position because he sees everything.", "annotation_id": "39894644", "url": "https://genius.com/39894644", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "My niggas didn’t have a pot to piss in", "vault_lines": ["My niggas didn't have a pot to piss in"], "meaning": "Might be refering to a lyric in A$AP Rocky’s song, Long Live A$AP\n\n“Ain’t had no pot to piss in, now my kitchen full of dishes”\n\nhttps://genius.com/1314158", "annotation_id": "39894231", "url": "https://genius.com/39894231", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "But I still got love for your missus and her sister", "vault_lines": ["But I still got love for your missus and her sister"], "meaning": "Drake is signaling that his history with Rihanna and , her half-sister, Samantha is the only reason A$AP Rocky is walking away from this with just a diss record.\n\nIn 2014, Samantha was spotted partying with Drake in Berlin while he was linked to Rihanna, so his relationship with the sister probably runs just as deep.", "annotation_id": "39873040", "url": "https://genius.com/39873040", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Look in the mirror, see where talkin’ get you, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Look in the mirror, see where talkin' get you, yeah"], "meaning": "Of everyone Drake targets on ICEMAN, A$AP Rocky earned his place by coming at Drake on three separate tracks across Don’t Be Dumb.\n\nRocky bears a facial scar stretching from the corner of his mouth to the jawline. The origin of which he has been vague about. Drake is telling Rocky to look at it as if it was karma for him speaking ill of his name.\n\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/7cb8238b9a4df85ab0601bb9857c4b865bbb4914e88fb05a28c2656fce97d02a%2Fq808p637ngp\n\nThis callback to Drake’s bars from “Family Matters” during the beef:\n\nAsk Fring if this a good idea the next time you cuddled in that bed again\nShe’ll even tell you leave the boy alone ‘fore you get your head split again", "annotation_id": "39871618", "url": "https://genius.com/39871618", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "I swear I could cry when I see your face I’m way too in love just to let you walk away I know it’s your dream to start a family in LA They love me too much here, I cannot relocate I just can’t move away I can’t leave my brothers in them trenches, gotta lead the way, yeah I cannot relocate Just even the thought to turn my back on them is not okay I cannot relocate We just gotta post up here, I promise things will be okay ’Cause I love you, bae Damn, but they love me too much here, I cannot—", "vault_lines": ["Just even the thought to turn my back on them is not okay", "We just gotta post up here, I promise things will be okay", "'Cause I love you, bae", "Damn, but they love me too much here, I cannot—", "Lord, forgive me (Lord, forgive me), it's my turn", "I swear I could cry when I see your face", "I'm way too in love just to let you walk away", "I know it's your dream to start a family in LA", "They love me too much here, I cannot relocate", "I just can't move away", "I can't leave my brothers in them trenches, gotta lead the way, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake is spilling the tea on why things never fully worked out between him and Rihanna, they were romantically linked on and off for the better part of a decade.\n\n\n\nRihanna wanted to start a family and build a life in Los Angeles. The 6ix god couldn’t do it. Not because he didn’t love her, but because he felt that Toronto was never just a home address, it was a responsibility. His brothers in the trenches, the city that made him, the people who were there before the fame and would still be there if it all went away. Leaving felt like a betrayal he couldn’t live with, even for the person he loved most.\n\nRihanna did eventually start that family in LA, which is pretty telling. She and A$AP Rocky are currently living in Beverly Hills, California with their children. The dream she described to Drake got built. He just wasn’t in it.\n\n\n\nRihanna’s $13.8 million Beverly Hills mansion", "annotation_id": "39871610", "url": "https://genius.com/39871610", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Why you walkin’ ’round the facts like you’re superstitious?", "vault_lines": ["Why you walkin' 'round the facts like you're superstitious?"], "meaning": "Drake is making a reference to the Fak family in the TV Show the Bear with the Facts/Faks double entendre\n\nOne of the main traits in the show is their superstition so much so that it’s kinda contagious for the people around them", "annotation_id": "39829504", "url": "https://genius.com/39829504", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "We G-block babies, we are not musicians", "vault_lines": ["We G-block babies, we are not musicians"], "meaning": "G-Block is a reference to the Galloway corridor in Scarborough. That is another nod to the street network Drake has been threading throughout the album:\n\nUnless you know the G-way handshake when we go to shake hands, man can’t trust you   “Plot Twist”\n\n Drake is saying they came from a world that predates the music, that exists independently of it, and that would still be standing even if it ceases to exist.", "annotation_id": "39828568", "url": "https://genius.com/39828568", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "I’d rather take the long way", "vault_lines": ["I'd rather take the long way"], "meaning": "This is both a continuance of the burning bridges metaphor with ASAP Rocky but also may be a play on Drake being willing to wait on Rihanna and Rocky’s relationship to end in order to rekindle a relationship with Rihanna.", "annotation_id": "39813242", "url": "https://genius.com/39813242", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Burning bridges is okay", "vault_lines": ["Burning bridges is okay"], "meaning": "Rihanna and ASAP Rocky are both Bajan. Rihanna was born and raised in Bridgetown, Barbados. “Burning Bridges” may be an allusion to  the hometown connection between Rihanna and Rocky, with Drake starting the song alluding to having no problem burning bridges with ASAP Rocky and no problem waiting for the Bridgetown Bajan connection to burn as well.", "annotation_id": "39813103", "url": "https://genius.com/39813103", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "You should be swimmin’ with the fuckin’ fishes", "vault_lines": ["You should be swimmin' with the fuckin' fishes"], "meaning": "“sleeping with the fishes” is a Mafia euphemism for a murder victim dumped into a body of water, weighted down and left to sink, popularized by Francis Ford Coppola’s 1972 The Godfather when Luca Brasi’s death is announced via a dead fish wrapped in his bulletproof vest.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HsRvgCsLXWw\n\nThis is a reciprocation of what A$AP Rocky started on  “Ruby Rosary”:\n\nLike, who dared me to die? (Uh)\nYou a dead man walkin' like you barely alive\nCut everything but the family ties (That’s right)", "annotation_id": "39793769", "url": "https://genius.com/39793769", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "Your baby mama ain’t even post your single, damn Where she at? Yeah, where she at? Your baby mama ain’t even post your single, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Your baby mama ain't even post your single, damn", "Where she at? Yeah, where she at?", "Your baby mama ain't even post your single, yeah", "Yeah, your baby mama ain't even post your single, damn"], "meaning": "Drake and Rihanna’s relationship spanned nearly a decade of on-again, off-again romantic entanglement and collaborations. At the 2016 MTV VMAs, Drake stood on stage to present Rihanna with the Video Vanguard Award and declared, “She’s someone I’ve been in love with since I was 22 years old.”\n\n\n\nA$AP Rocky and Drake were once super tight and frequent collaborators — Drake brought Rocky as an opening act on the 2012 Club Paradise Tour  when he was relatively unknown, and the two appeared together on “Fuckin' Problems.” But once Rocky locked in with Rihanna, the friendship curdled. During the 2024 Kendrick beef, Rocky chose his side — appearing on Future and Metro Boomin’s We Still Don’t Trust You on the track “Show of Hands,” where he dissed Drake:\n\nI smashed before you birthed son,\n Flacko hit it first, son\n\nRocky then brought that energy to his early 2026 album Don’t Be Dumb, where he dissed Drake on three tracks off the project.\n\nDrake’s trying to weaponize the perception that Rihanna was absent from Rocky’s promotional rollout but in reality she attended the album release party in New York City, and celebrated the number one debut on X with “Just me here to let yall know my baby daddy got the NUMBER 1 ALBUM!!!” But at the end of the day. she never posted a single, which is the angle Drake is exploiting.\n\nEven though the album debuted at number one on the billboard, it only had 123,000 equivalent album units and was met with mixed reviews.", "annotation_id": "39778759", "url": "https://genius.com/39778759", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "I’m off a Quay, it make my body tingle", "vault_lines": ["I'm off a Quay, it make my body tingle (Yeah)"], "meaning": "“Quay” short for a quaalude is a synthetic, sedative-like central nervous system depressant known for producing intense relaxation, euphoria, and a “warm, dreamy” state.", "annotation_id": "39778200", "url": "https://genius.com/39778200", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "So I told Capo we can’t burn them bridges, yeah", "vault_lines": ["So I told Capo we can't burn them bridges, yeah"], "meaning": "Capo is the nickname of Nessel Chubbs\" Beezer, Drake’s best friend, head of security, and an OVO member.\n\n“Burning bridges” means damaging or ending a relationship in a way that cannot be undone.", "annotation_id": "39777763", "url": "https://genius.com/39777763", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "I’m off a Quay, it make my body tingle (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I'm off a Quay, it make my body tingle (Yeah)"], "meaning": "“Quay” is a street name/slang term for Quaaludes (methaqualone,) a sedative-hypnotic medication that commonly produce euphoria and relaxation. Tingling/numbness can also occur during withdrawal.", "annotation_id": "39777687", "url": "https://genius.com/39777687", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "I’m with Noel like I’m Kris Kringle, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I'm with Noel like I'm Kris Kringle, yeah"], "meaning": "Noel Cadastre is Drake’s friend and an OVO sound engineer. Noël is the French word for Christmas, Kris Kringle is an alternate name for Santa Claus in many countries including Canada.", "annotation_id": "39777619", "url": "https://genius.com/39777619", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "I put you niggas through the wringer, yeah You gettin’ bodied by a singin’ nigga, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I put you niggas through the wringer, yeah", "You gettin' bodied by a singin' nigga, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake calls back to his iconic line on his July 2015 Meek Mill diss track “Back to Back,” which was in response to Meek accusing Drake of using ghostwriters on Twitter:\n\nYeah, trigger fingers turn to Twitter fingers\nYeah, you gettin' bodied by a singin' nigga\nI’m not the type of nigga that’ll type to niggas\n\nChris Brown said similar lines on his April 2024 Quavo diss track, “Weakest Link”:\n\nI’ll run your ass through the wringer, nigga\nYou just got bodied by a singin' nigga, bitch", "annotation_id": "39776253", "url": "https://genius.com/39776253", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "line": "You saw my brother, you was tryna fix it Now you drop your album and you back dissin’, yeah", "vault_lines": ["You saw my brother, you was tryna fix it", "Now you drop your album and you back dissin', yeah"], "meaning": "This likely refers to A$AP Rocky. In his interview with Akademiks, he briefly talks about how things became tense between him and Drake.\n\nThat tension seems to carry into A$AP Rocky’s album Don’t Be Dumb, released on January 16, 2026. On “STOLE YA FLOW”, he appears to diss Drake with the line:\n\nFirst, you stole my flow, so I stole yo' bitch", "annotation_id": "39776212", "url": "https://genius.com/39776212", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Burning Bridges", "status": "done", "targets": 24, "clicked": 24, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-burning-bridges-lyrics", "genius_title": "Burning Bridges"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Cheetah Print", "line": "[Verse 3: Sexyy Red]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This guest verse by Sexxy Redd is an interpolation heavily inspired by DJ Casper’s 2000 Hit Cha Cha Slide.", "annotation_id": "40209252", "url": "https://genius.com/40209252", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Cheetah Print", "line": "Smack that ass, smack that ass until it’s cheetah print", "vault_lines": ["I'll smack that ass, smack that ass until I leave a print", "Smack that ass, smack that ass until it's cheetah print", "I'll smack that ass, smack that ass until it's cheetah print, damn", "And then smack that ass, smack that ass until I lеave a print", "I'll smack that ass, smack that ass until it's cheetah print"], "meaning": "Drake suggests that he has “smacked that ass” so many times that it leaves a visible pattern on the woman’s buttox. He compares these marks to cheetah print (hence the name of the song), to give a visual image to the listener and emphasize the pattern left.\n\nHowever, zebra print may have been a more fitting comparison, as handprints leave more long linear marks similar to zebra print.", "annotation_id": "40114550", "url": "https://genius.com/40114550", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Cheetah Print", "line": "Hands on your knees, hands on your knees (Ayy, ayy) Hands on your knees, hands on your knees (Ayy, ayy) Hands on your knees, hands on your knees (Ayy, ayy)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A reference to a lyrics from Sexyy Red’s verse on the song “Rich Baby Daddy”:\n\n“Shake that ass, bitch, hands on your knees”", "annotation_id": "39786277", "url": "https://genius.com/39786277", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Cheetah Print", "line": "Girl, it’s your birthday, you can cry if you want to You can walk in the mall, you can buy what you want to Get high if you want to, get fly if you want to Sent you a drunk text, reply if you want to Play shy if you want to Told you I, told you I want you, told you I", "vault_lines": ["Girl, it's your birthday, you can cry if you want to", "You can walk in the mall, you can buy what you want to", "Get high if you want to, get fly if you want to", "Sent you a drunk text, reply if you want to", "Play shy if you want to", "Told you I, told you I want you, told you I"], "meaning": "Drake interpolates Lesley Gore’s 1963 hit “It’s My Party”, previously interpolated in 2011’s Song “Take Care”\n\nIt’s my party and I’ll cry if I want to\n\nDrake transforms Lesley Gore’s emotional birthday anthem into a lavish, intoxicated celebration of romance and excess.", "annotation_id": "39783836", "url": "https://genius.com/39783836", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Cheetah Print", "line": "Tell me what you want and I’ll tell you what I really want Like I’m Scary Spice, girl, I’ma bring you to a balcony out in paradise", "vault_lines": ["Tell me what you want and I'll tell you what I really want", "Likе I'm Scary Spice, girl, I'ma bring you to a balcony out in paradise", "Told you I, told you I want you, told you I"], "meaning": "This line is a reference to the 1996 Hit Wannabe by the pop group Spice Girls, where Mel B, known in the group as Scary Spice, notably sings\n\nYo, I’ll tell you what I want, what I really, really want\nSo tell me what you want, what you really, really want\n\nShe is also well-known for wearing cheetah print, linking to the title of the song:", "annotation_id": "39779459", "url": "https://genius.com/39779459", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Cheetah Print", "line": "Clap with no hands Aight, we gon’ start with the basic step, ladies, nice and slow To the left, to the right, throw it back now, y’all One cheek this time, two cheeks this time Slow it down now, y’all Now turn around Aight, now it’s time to go up some notches To the left, to the right, take it back now, y’all One smack this time, one smack this time Left cheek, two pumps, right cheek, two pumps", "vault_lines": ["Clap with no hands", "Aight, we gon' start with the basic step, ladies, nice and slow", "To the left, to the right, throw it back now, y'all", "One cheek this time, two cheeks this time", "Slow it down now, y'all", "Aight, now it's time to go up some notches", "To the left, to the right, take it back now, y'all", "One smack this time, one smack this time", "Left cheek, two pumps, right cheek, two pumps"], "meaning": "Sexyy Red is interpolating hit song Cha Cha Slide by MrC The Slide Man\n\n“To the left.\nTake it back now, y'all.\nOne hop this time.\nRight foot, let’s stomp.\nLeft foot, let’s stomp.\nCha cha real smooth.\nTurn it out.”", "annotation_id": "39776989", "url": "https://genius.com/39776989", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
