{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I Stay Fly", "line": "Europeako jeans bleeding on them white air max", "vault_lines": ["Europeako jeans bleeding on them white air max"], "meaning": "The correct spelling is Yoropiko Jeans – a fancy Japanese jean company\n\nDrake wears them to match his white Air Max Nikes as opposed to the more preppy stile of tucking the jeans into the shoes", "annotation_id": "22662", "url": "https://genius.com/22662", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I Stay Fly", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 1, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-i-stay-fly-lyrics", "genius_title": "I Stay Fly"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Knocked that boy off and I don’t want no credit", "vault_lines": ["Knocked that boy off and I don't want no credit"], "meaning": "Drake could be referring to the 2018 murder of rapper XXXTentacion. XXXTentacion’s death has been widely speculated across the internet to have been a contract kill masked as a robbery gone wrong.\n\nIt would be relevant for Drake to rap about this since he has been named the main suspect by the internet conjectured to have been the one, who ordered the hit. None of these claims have been proven true.\nXXXTentacion had been dissing Drake and his label OVO for the past months before his death.\n\nThe line “Knocked that boy off and I don’t want no credit” would in this matter make sense, since Drake hasn’t claimed the body, implying not wanting to be credited for it.\nDrake has even denied his involvement to the case on some of his later verses.\n\nDrake has kept throwing subliminal shots/disses at X after his death on many of his other songs too. Here’s a few examples:\n\nDrake on On BS:\n\nDamn, maybe I should do a twenty, maybe I should break that twenty, do a ten\nMaybe I should break that ten, do a five, then if it gets live, do a five again\nIf he held his tongue on that live, he’d be alive again, damn\n\nDrake on Daylight:\n\nShot him in daylight\nShot him in daylight\nShot him in daylight\n(Shot him, he shot him, what? Shot him, what?)\nShot him in daylight\nShot him in daylight\nShot him in daylight (Broad day)", "annotation_id": "34635075", "url": "https://genius.com/34635075", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "I know that they at the crib goin’ crazy, down bad What they had didn’t last, damn, baby", "vault_lines": ["I know that they at the crib goin' crazy, down bad", "What they had didn't last, damn, baby"], "meaning": "Drake could be referencing his ongoing fued with Kanye West, alluding to his infamous Twitter rants, some of which have even targeted Drake himself.\n\nI know that they at the crib goin' crazy, down bad\n\nAt the time, Kanye had been experiencing a very public mental breakdown, and receiving backlash in the media for his outlandish public statements.\n\nIt wouldn’t be the first time Drake has targeted a rappers mental health issues. Drake went after Kid Cudi, signed to Kanye’s Good Music, on Two Birds, One Stone stating:\n\nYou were the man on the moon\nNow you just go through your phases\nLife of the angry and famous\nYou stay xann’d and perk’d up\nSo when reality set in, you don’t gotta face it", "annotation_id": "23607673", "url": "https://genius.com/23607673", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Anytime that I ran into somebody, it must be a victory lap, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Anytime that I ran into somebody, it must be a victory lap, ayy"], "meaning": "This line is a sly double-entendre that referencing a track runner. Drake’s already finished the race and is so far ahead of the other runners he’s lapped them, coming up behind them and “ran” into them.\n\nThis could also be a nod to Drake’s close friend, late rapper Nipsey Hussle. Nipsey’s debut album, released in 2018, was titled Victory Lap. After Hussle’s passing in 2019, Drake paid tribute to him at a London concert.", "annotation_id": "20852556", "url": "https://genius.com/20852556", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "When he tell the story, that’s not how it went Know they be lyin’, a hundred percent", "vault_lines": ["When he tell the story, that's not how it went", "Know they be lyin', a hundred percent"], "meaning": "With these lines, Drake could be nodding to two things. For one, he could be talking about the allegations that he has ghostwriters. Although the allegations first date back to 2015 when Drake had beef with Meek Mill, Pusha T revived them when he took shots at Drake on May 2018’s “Infrared,“ saying that he uses ghostwriters:\n\nIt was written like Nas, but it came from Quentin\n\nQuentin Miller, Drake’s alleged ghostwriter, denied the rumors back in 2015 where he said, “I am not and never will be a ‘ghostwriter’ for Drake.”\n\n Drake could also be talking about the fact that he was hiding a secret child, Adonis, which Pusha T exposed on his diss track, “The Story of Adidon.\" Drake responded with his Scorpion track, “Emotionless,“ where Drake said he was just protecting his son from his high-profile lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "20623766", "url": "https://genius.com/20623766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Been wakin’ up in the crib and sometimes I don’t even know where I’m at", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the trenches, relax", "And I never been embraced", "Been wakin' up in the crib and sometimes I don't even know where I'm at"], "meaning": "Drake has an extravagant $100 million dollar mansion that’s located in his hometown of Toronto, Canada.\n\nDubbed “The Embassy,” the 50,000-square-foot property houses an indoor swimming pool, an NBA regulation-size basketball court, a recording studio, among other things. Drake’s mansion can be seen in the jointed-music video for “When To Say When” and “Chicago Freestyle,” as well as the music video for April 2020’s “Toosie Slide.”\n\nDrake jokes around, saying that he sometimes feels lost in his own house, due to how large it is.\n\n\n\nDrake’s mansion, “The Embassy”", "annotation_id": "20608375", "url": "https://genius.com/20608375", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Pillow talk with ‘em, she spillin’ the tea And then shawty came back and said she didn’t mean it It’s hard to believe it", "vault_lines": ["And the money's hard to make", "Pillow talk with 'em, she spillin' the tea", "And then shawty came back and said she didn't mean it", "It's hard to believe it"], "meaning": "When Drake appeared on Lebron James’s HBO series The Shop in October 2018, he explained that Kanye West is the one that told Pusha T about Drake’s child, giving Pusha T the ammunition for “The Story of Adidon,” which was released a few months prior.\n\nHowever, Pusha T countered. When speaking with Joe Budden on The Joe Budden Podcast, he revealed he actually got the information from a woman that 40, Drake’s trusted engineer and producer, was pillow-talking with.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/zN26h4s-gS8?t=1026\n\nWith these lines, it seems that Drake finds Pusha T’s story “hard to believe” and instead continues to believe that Kanye West told Pusha T about the existence of his son.", "annotation_id": "20584117", "url": "https://genius.com/20584117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Please don’t play that nigga songs in this party, I can’t even listen to that", "vault_lines": ["Please don't play that nigga songs in this party, I can't even listen to that"], "meaning": "In October 2019, Drake celebrated his 33rd birthday by throwing a huge party where he invited many of his close friends. At one point during the party, Drake’s DJ started playing the G.O.O.D. Music’s remix of Chief Keef’s “Don’t Like,” which features his rivals Pusha T and Kanye West.\n\nThe song was played for a few seconds before Mark Robinson, one of Drake’s oldest and closest friends, told the DJ to cut the song off.", "annotation_id": "20583942", "url": "https://genius.com/20583942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 149, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Ooh-oh Bring Drake to the hood, surround Drake around Dracs Even though I got a case, I’ma do what it take", "vault_lines": ["Bring Drake to the hood, surround Drake around Dracs", "Even though I got a case, I'ma do what it take"], "meaning": "A “Draco” or more specifically a Romanian Draco AK-47 Pistol is a type of AK-47 assault rifle with a shortened back. Durk frequently mentions the weapon in his music, rapping on March 2020’s “Chiraq Demons” for example:\n\nLil' boy, he better graduate before this Draco cap and gown him\n\nThe first syllable in “Draco” also matches the sound of Drake which is the reasoning for his shoutout here, along with the fact that Durk’s featured on his song.", "annotation_id": "20580951", "url": "https://genius.com/20580951", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "We in Atlanta, I buy her a wig, she tellin’ me Tae is the best", "vault_lines": ["We in Atlanta, I buy her a wig, she tellin' me Tae is the best"], "meaning": "Dionte Gray, also known as Arrogant Tae, is a popular hairstylist and wig expert from Chicago—where Durk is from—but resides in Atlanta, Georgia. He has worked with many famous celebrities, some of them being Nicki Minaj, Teyana Taylor, Dream Doll and more.\n\nShortly after the song’s release, Tae posted a reaction to his shoutout via Instagram, thanking Lil Durk for the name-drop.", "annotation_id": "20580860", "url": "https://genius.com/20580860", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Sometimes we laugh and sometimes we cry, but I guess you know now, baby", "vault_lines": ["Sometimes we laugh and sometimes we cry, but I guess you know now, baby"], "meaning": "Drake’s use of the phrase “laugh now, cry later” is likely inspired by 2Pac, who had the phrase “smile now, cry later” tattoed on his back, together with a pair of comedy and tragedy masks:\n\n\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake and his OVO imprint have taken inspiration from 2Pac’s body art—OVO released a T-shirt with the masks prominently featured on the back, and Drake added the smiling mask to the wings of his private jet, Air Drake.\n\nTwo weeks before the release of this track, Drake also purchased matching chains that depicted 2Pac with a crown of thorns—the pieces are worth $600,000.", "annotation_id": "20580814", "url": "https://genius.com/20580814", "state": "accepted", "votes": 146, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Tired of beefin’ you bums, you can’t even pay me enough to react", "vault_lines": ["Tired of beefin' you bums, you can't even pay me enough to react"], "meaning": "Drake’s likely talking about Pusha T and Kanye West, who he continuously throws shots at throughout the song.\n\nDrake and Pusha have been beefing since 2011 when Pusha dissed Drake on “Don’t Fuck With Me”—a remix of Drake’s May 2011 song “Dreams Money Can Buy.” Drake and Kanye, on the other hand, have had years of issues between them dating back to 2010.\n\nThe beef between Drake and Pusha T was revived in May 2018 following Pusha subs on his Kanye-produced song, “Infrared” and Drake responded with “Duppy Freestyle” within 24 hours where he took shots at both Pusha and Kanye.\n\n“You can’t even pay me enough to react” might be a nod to Drake sarcastically sending Push and his label G.O.O.D. Music an invoice for $100,000 for “promotional assistance and career reviving,” as he promised he would on “Duppy Freestyle”:\n\nTell ‘Ye we got a invoice comin’ to you\nConsiderin' that we just sold another 20 for you", "annotation_id": "20580650", "url": "https://genius.com/20580650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "We took a trip, now we on your block and it’s like a ghost town, baby Where do these niggas be at when they say they doin’ all this and all that?", "vault_lines": ["We took a trip, now we on your block and it's like a ghost town, baby", "Where do these niggas be at when they say they doin' all this and all that?"], "meaning": "In these lines, Drake takes aim at Kanye West, with whom he has had tumultuous relationship, specifically referencing West’s June 2018 track “Ghost Town” and Drizzy’s infamous shots at Ye from his verse on Travis Scott’s August 2018 track “SICKO MODE,” on which he raps:\n\nI crept down the block (Down the block)\nMade a right (Yeah, right)\nCut the lights (Yeah, what?), paid the price (Yeah)\n\nThe two artists began as friends, with Drake citing Ye as one of his biggest influences in a May 2009 interview, and the duo collaborating on Drake’s 2009 hit “Forever,” however, the relationship soured over the years, culminating in the release of Drake’s 2018 track “Duppy Freestyle,” a response to Pusha T’s May 2018 Kanye-produced track “Infrared”. Pusha responded with the scathing “The Story of Adidon,” which Drake called “[not] about battle rap, the information was too shocking.” Kanye later voiced his frustration with Drake via multiple Twitter rants.\n\nDrake continued to pay no mind to Ye’s episodic rants, and the two continued to have a frosty relationship until November 2021 when the duo reunited for a benefit concert to free Larry Hoover.", "annotation_id": "20580309", "url": "https://genius.com/20580309", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Backyard, it look like the Garden of Eden", "vault_lines": ["Backyard, it look like the Garden of Eden"], "meaning": "The Garden of Eden is a Biblical paradise in the Eden created by God, intended to be inhabited by the first human creations. The landscape portrays a flamboyant scene with its rich nature and horticulture, while representing perfection and harmony to Christians.\n\nAs written in Genesis 2, God creates the earthly paradise in an attempt to serve Adam and Eve—the first two humans on earth according to the Bible:\n\n 8 The Lord God planted a garden in Eden, in the east, and there he placed the man he had formed. 9 The Lord God caused to grow out of the ground every tree pleasing in appearance and good for food, including the tree of life in the middle of the garden, as well as the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. 10 A river went out from Eden to water the garden.\n\n\n\nGarden of Eden depiction\n\n The Embassy is Drake’s $100 million Toronto mansion. His backyard is equally luxurious, as seen through this video he posted on Instagram stories in June 2020.", "annotation_id": "20580245", "url": "https://genius.com/20580245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Moved out the Ritz and forgot ‘bout the Bent’ Valet just called me to tell me come get it", "vault_lines": ["Moved out the Ritz and forgot 'bout the Bent'", "Valet just called me to tell me come get it"], "meaning": "Prior to his massive mansion compound in Toronto being finished, Drake planted his home base at the Ritz-Carlton in Toronto. The massive apartment had floor-to-ceiling windows with views right next to the famous CN Tower.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/oceD6MjoRK8\n\nDrake officially made his move in 2020 and gave a nod to his massive car collection, which includes a Bentley Continental that was famously used in his “Started from the Bottom” music video.", "annotation_id": "20580175", "url": "https://genius.com/20580175", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Can you not play that lil’ boy in the club? ’Cause we do not listen to rats", "vault_lines": ["Can you not play that lil' boy in the club? 'Cause we do not listen to rats"], "meaning": "Lil Durk’s likely talking about New York rapper, Tekashi 6ix9ine and the “lil boy” mention likely stems from a May 2020 Instagram Live Tekashi had where he said if other New York rappers didn’t have the same watch as him, they were a “little boy” to him.\n\nTekashi was named a “rat” by the hip-hop industry after his infamous September 2019 testimony in the case of United States vs. Nine Trey Bloods where he testified against the Nine Trey gang members for a lighter prison sentence. For his cooperation, he was sentenced to only two years in prison instead of the much-heavier 47 years sentence for his crimes.\n\nFollowing his testimony, Lil Durk wrote a now-deleted tweet “Fuck 69 snitch” and talked about the situation in an interview with Rob Markman during a September 2019 For The Record Genius episode:\n\nA rat is a rat. You know what you did, like, if you’re not like that man, just stay away from the streets. Portraying the image that’s really not you, you know what I’m saying? […] Ain’t nobody deserve to go to jail at the end of the day, but a rat is a rat. I’m not surprised he told.\n\nAdditionally, three days after this song’s release, during an interview with POWER 106, Durk was asked about the lyric, to which he responded “whoever make music and [is a] rat,” fueling further that the line may be about Tekashi.\n\nHowever, in 2018, before his arrest, 6ix9ine and Durk were friendly with each other. Durk, at the time, was even trying to mediate the beef between Tekashi and fellow Chicago rapper, Chief Keef to keep things from escalating further.", "annotation_id": "20580159", "url": "https://genius.com/20580159", "state": "accepted", "votes": 224, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "I took a half and she took the whole thing, slow down, baby", "vault_lines": ["I took a half and she took the whole thing, slow down, baby"], "meaning": "On Travis Scott’s August 2018 song “SICKO MODE,” Drake similarly raps about taking half of a pill—here xanax—to sleep on a flight:\n\nI did half a Xan, 13 hours ‘til I land\nHad me out like a light, ayy, yeah", "annotation_id": "20580150", "url": "https://genius.com/20580150", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Distance between us is not like a store, this isn’t a closeable gap, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Distance between us is not like a store, this isn't a closeable gap, ayy"], "meaning": "Gap is an American clothing store brand, most known for being a home of comfort clothing. It was founded in 1969 and owns well-known sub-brands such as Banana Republic and Old Navy among others.\n\nThis line could also be a diss towards rapper Kanye West, who signed a deal in June 2020 to bring his Yeezy clothing brand to Gap.\n\nWhile Ye is teaming up with an average clothing brand, Drake has been a large figure for Nike throughout his career – with this track’s music video being shot at Nike’s headquarters in Beaverton, Oregon.", "annotation_id": "20580126", "url": "https://genius.com/20580126", "state": "accepted", "votes": 111, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Laugh Now Cry Later", "line": "Point at the nigga who act like a killer, but you only one from the ’net I’m like DaBaby, I’m not just a rapper, you play with me, you gon’ get stretched", "vault_lines": ["Point at the nigga who act like a killer, but you only one from the 'net", "I'm like DaBaby, I'm not just a rapper, you play with me, you gon' get stretched"], "meaning": "DaBaby is a rapper from Charlotte, North Carolina with an extensive criminal history.\n\nOf all his charges, his most serious one was carrying a concealed gun, which stemmed from a deadly shooting that occurred on November 5, 2018. While shopping with his family at a Walmart, DaBaby fatally shot nineteen-year-old Jaylin Domonique Craig in self-defense after he approached him in an attempt to rob him. The charges were later dropped.\n\nDaBaby later touched on the incident in his April 2020 number one hit, “ROCKSTAR,” where he mentioned he would take another man’s life to his protect his daughter:\n\nShe know what I do, she know ‘fore I run from a nigga, I'ma pull it out and shoot (Boom)\nPTSD, I’m always waking up in cold sweats like I got the flu\nMy daughter a G, she saw me kill a nigga in front of her before the age of two\nAnd I’ll kill another nigga too\n'Fore I let another nigga do somethin’ to you\nLong as you know that, don’t let nobody tell you different\nDaddy love you (Yeah, yeah)", "annotation_id": "20580044", "url": "https://genius.com/20580044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 211, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
