{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "Jump up out the bed like I’m possessed", "vault_lines": ["Jump up out the bed like I'm possessed"], "meaning": "This is a nod to the 1973 American supernatural horror film, The Exorcist, where the main character, 12-year-old Regan, becomes possessed by a demon spirit.\n\nThe movie has an iconic scene where Regan’s body jerks completely off of the bed as her inner demon becomes enraged.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bWyi3ShHxzM", "annotation_id": "14820323", "url": "https://genius.com/14820323", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "I still got like seven years of doin’ what I want", "vault_lines": ["I still got like seven years of doin' what I want"], "meaning": "It is Drake’s belief that he should enjoy his youth by staying single and avoid being prematurely tied down. On “Child’s Play” from his previous album, Drake rapped:\n\nMarried in our twenties, now, where the fun in that?\n\nDrake has been tied to several women in his career, but a large portion of this has been out of spotlight and speculative.", "annotation_id": "14655293", "url": "https://genius.com/14655293", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "Hate me, never met me in the flesh (Flesh)", "vault_lines": ["Hate me, never met me in the flesh (Flesh)"], "meaning": "Many rappers have expressed hatred and attempted to feud with Drake for a variety of reasons such as damaged reputations and the ending of their careers. This includes artists such as Common,  Puff Daddy and XXXTENTACION. Ironically, the people that he is trading bars and negative energy with are people he’s never even met in person. This may also be interpreted as these rappers never even got a chance to talk to Drake about their relationship which is why they feel negatively towards him.\n\nThis may also be a jab towards Pusha T, a rapper that Drake has been beefing with in the days leading up to the release of “I’m Upset.” The former rapper dissed Drizzy in his song, “Infrared” from his 2018 album, DAYTONA, prompting Drake to respond with “Duppy Freestyle” and send an invoice to Pusha’s label G.O.O.D. Music for “promotional assistance” and “career reviving”.", "annotation_id": "14654825", "url": "https://genius.com/14654825", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "Said she’s got some things she gotta come here and collect That shit is in a box to the left, to the left (Left, left, left)", "vault_lines": ["Said she's got some things she gotta come here and collect", "That shit is in a box to the left, to the left (Left, left, left)"], "meaning": "Drizzy’s ex has left some of her belongings at his crib to which he has discarded them in a box. He does so by referencing Beyoncé’s 2006 song “Irreplaceable” on which she sings:\n\nTo the left, to the left\nEverything you own in the box to the left\n\nDrake is a known Beyoncé fan and released a song in 2013 titled “Girls Love Beyoncé” which was an ode to the Houston singer. Additionally, he went on to collaborate with Bey that same year on her track “Mine.”\n\n\n\n(From left to right) Beyoncé, JAY Z & Drake at the 2013 Grammys\n\nFurthermore, in March 2018 Beyoncé referenced Drake’s track “Gyalchester” on DJ Khaled’s song “Top Off” with the line:\n\nI break the internet, top two and I ain’t number two", "annotation_id": "14654243", "url": "https://genius.com/14654243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "Word to Flacko Jodye, he done seen us put it down Niggas askin’ if I’m cool", "vault_lines": ["Word to Flacko Jodye, he done seen us put it down", "Niggas askin' if I'm cool"], "meaning": "Drizzy shouts out his “Fuckin' Problems” collaborator A$AP Rocky, stating that anyone who is questioning whether he “puts it down” should ask him.\n\nTo “put it down” means to do what needs to be done, at any given moment; to do the right thing at the right time. The phrase can also be used to describe turning up and getting lit.\n\nDrake cosigned Rocky early on in his career by inviting him to open for him on the North American leg of his Club Paradise tour in 2012. There’s no doubt Flacko has some wild stories from their time on the road.\n\n\n\n\n\nDrake and Rocky also have a series of shared lovers, who could (presumably) attest that the two men can “put it down.” Both men have reportedly dated Sophie Brussaux, the mother of Drake’s child, with Rocky still seeing Brussaux  whilst she was pregnant with Drake’s child. Furthermore, Drake and Rocky have both dated Rihanna.", "annotation_id": "14654156", "url": "https://genius.com/14654156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 243, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "Made me wanna buy a vest and a TEC (Skrr)", "vault_lines": ["Made me wanna buy a vest and a TEC (Skrr)"], "meaning": "Due to the threat of the aforementioned bounty mentioned in previous lines, Drizzy feels the need to purchase a bulletproof vest and a gun to protect himself.\n\nTEC stands for an Intratec TEC-9, a semi-automatic pistol. It is high in popularity due to its cheap price and easy customization, but it was banned in many states in the United States of America due to its association with organized crime and mass shootings.", "annotation_id": "14654146", "url": "https://genius.com/14654146", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "Why you keep on shootin’ if you know that nigga dead? (Skrr) That’s the only kind of shit that gets you some respect", "vault_lines": ["Why you keep on shootin' if you know that nigga dead? (Skrr)", "That's the only kind of shit that gets you some respect"], "meaning": "Drake approaches his competition in a very fierce method—kill your competition even if they’re already dead.\n\nHe notably used this tactic in his beef with Meek Mill when he attempted to expose Quentin Miller, a  co-writer and alleged ghostwriter that Drake claims he employed as an act of kindness. Following this, Drake unleashed “Charged Up” and “Back To Back” before Meek Mill could have a chance to quickly respond; Drake performed the latter diss track for several months in an act of spite even after hype around the beef had died down.", "annotation_id": "14654129", "url": "https://genius.com/14654129", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "Got a lot of blood and it’s cold They keep tryna get me for my soul", "vault_lines": ["Got a lot of blood and it's cold", "They keep tryna get me for my soul"], "meaning": "A “cold-blooded person” describes someone lacking emotion or pity, and is deliberately cruel. This line could reference his recent track “Duppy Freestyle”, in which he responded to Pusha T’s song “Infrared”. Being cold-blooded and reserved is a trait often associated with people born under the Scorpio zodiac sign (Drake himself), and “Scorpion” is the official title of his upcoming album.\n\nDrake feels that, because of his cold-blooded soul, many people are out to get him and take his life. Lyrics similar to these can be found later in the song:\n\nHalf a million on my head I can’t accept, yeah\n‘least it makes me feel someone tried their best, yeah\nWant to waste a half a million be my guest\nMade me wanna buy a vest, and a tech (skrr)\n\nDrake has a history of showing his ruthless side when he released the track “Back to Back” in July 2015 when Meek Mill accused Drake of ghostwriting.", "annotation_id": "14654042", "url": "https://genius.com/14654042", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "My dad still got child support from 1991 (One, one, one)", "vault_lines": ["My dad still got child support from 1991 (One, one, one)"], "meaning": "Drake’s father, Dennis Graham, wasn’t around for much of Drake’s childhood but they have since mended their relationship. Drake was only 5 years old when Dennis left his mother in 1991.\n\nOn the 2011 track “Look What You’ve Done”, Drake explained why his dad couldn’t visit him in Canada:\n\nAnd my father living in Memphis now, he can’t come this way\nOver some minor charges and child support\nThat just wasn’t paid, damn", "annotation_id": "14654031", "url": "https://genius.com/14654031", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "Thankful for the women that I know Can’t go fifty-fifty with no ho Every month I’m supposed to pay her bills and get her what she want", "vault_lines": ["Can't go fifty-fifty with no ho", "I'm supposed to pay her bills and get her what she want", "Thankful for the women that I know"], "meaning": "Drake is not shy about praising the strong women in his life. He’s shown support for Serena Williams, Rihanna, Nicki Minaj, and other high-profile women. He also has meaningful relationships with his mother, his Aunt Doe, and his grandmother. In April of 2018, he released “Nice For What,” and worked with dozens of influential women—some of whom he has a long-standing relationship with. All of these women have had a positive  influence on Drizzy.\n\nTraditionally, a man is expected to provide for his partner. In the event of a divorce, he has to pay alimony and sacrifice up to half of his income. Drake isn’t interested in financing anyone else’s lifestyle.\n\nGoing “fifty-fifty” with a woman might also mean having a child, as a reference to the popular phrase “let’s go half on a baby.” In 2017, there were rumors that Drake got a porn star named Sophie Brussaux pregnant, which he confirmed on the Scorpion track “March 14.”", "annotation_id": "14653972", "url": "https://genius.com/14653972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 129, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "I’m working on dying", "vault_lines": ["I'm working on dying"], "meaning": "This is the producer tag for the Philadelphia-based collective Working On Dying. This track’s producer, Oogie Mane, is one of the group’s best known members, having produced songs for Lil Uzi Vert, Bladee, and CHXPO.", "annotation_id": "14653969", "url": "https://genius.com/14653969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "I go out on tour and I say I’m drinkin’ less End up gettin’ loose and gettin’ pictures from my ex (Ex, ex, ex, ex) SMS, triple X That’s the only time I ever shoot below the neck (Skrr)", "vault_lines": ["I go out on tour and I say I'm drinkin' less", "End up gettin' loose and gettin' pictures from my ex (Ex, ex, ex, ex)", "That's the only time I ever shoot below the neck (Skrr)"], "meaning": "Shooting ‘below the neck’ refers to Drake excluding his face from any nude (or XXX) pictures he sends to women via text message and is the only time he aims for anything other than a headshot.\n\nUnfortunately, some artists have learned the hard way, like Kanye West, who suffered embarrassment when his nudes were leaked. Additionally, this may also refer to his pull-out game, as most men actively avoid getting women pregnant.", "annotation_id": "14653957", "url": "https://genius.com/14653957", "state": "accepted", "votes": 293, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m Upset", "line": "You tryna check? This is real life, niggas think we playin’ chess So what’s next?", "vault_lines": ["This is real life, niggas think we playin' chess"], "meaning": "Chess is a board game which requires both intellectual stamina and strategy. Drake is asking his haters if they are trying to “check” him. The word check is a chess term used when a player’s king is under threat of being captured in the following turn. By saying that people are trying to check him, Drake is implying that he himself is a king.\n\nHowever, Drake is reassuring his opponents that the rap game isn’t like a game of chess, it’s real life and, as such, comes with consequences. Additionally, Drake implies that like chess, people can be under threat from other people due to their actions.\n\nDrake has made references to the rap game and chess previously on his 2013 song “Pound Cake / Paris Morton Music 2:\n\nTook a while, got the jokers out of the deck now\nI’m holdin' all the cards and niggas wanna play chess now", "annotation_id": "14653856", "url": "https://genius.com/14653856", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
