{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "Baby, you’re out of state (My, my)", "vault_lines": ["Baby, you're out of state (My, my)"], "meaning": "Double entendre the person Drake is referring to could be literally out of state as the title “WNBA” considers a WNBA player would be moving across states busy travelling for games.\n\nAlternate meaning that the girl is out of state and no longer is picking up Drake’s calls and that she is “out of state” unlike her usual self, could also mean she is unavailable.", "annotation_id": "39837208", "url": "https://genius.com/39837208", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "Used to be just one call away (Just one)", "vault_lines": ["Used to be just one call away (Just one)"], "meaning": "An idiom for someone being readily available to talk or be with", "annotation_id": "39837195", "url": "https://genius.com/39837195", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "You keep goin’ away, Bahamas, MIA", "vault_lines": ["You keep goin' away, Bahamas, MIA"], "meaning": "“MIA” is a double meaning:\n\nMiami / the airport lifestyle\n“Missing in action,” meaning unavailable or disappearing\n\nThe Bahamas + Miami references also fit Drake’s real-life luxury-travel circle and yacht culture.", "annotation_id": "39802575", "url": "https://genius.com/39802575", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "And split the filet and chardonnay, okay", "vault_lines": ["And split the filet and chardonnay, okay"], "meaning": "Drake’s just promised her if she comes to him, he’ll front her costs, but then agrees to “go Dutch” on the meal, showing his respect for her independence.  He may also be mixing a metaphor for how they are great individually, like filet mignon and a fine chardonnay would be, but they aren’t really in sync with each other; steak traditionally pairs with full-bodied red wine, not a white chardonnay.  Then again, Drake has famously skewed toward white wines, including a line in The Motto that “We got Santa Margherita by the liter” referencing a particular white wine by name, so a chardonnay may be the wine Drake would pair with his steak even if experts would normally choose a red wine, such as a Cab or Bordeaux.", "annotation_id": "39797832", "url": "https://genius.com/39797832", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "You dip out the league, I’ll make you the main", "vault_lines": ["You dip out the league, I'll make you the main"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that if the woman in question “dip(s) out the league,” then he’ll “make (her) the main,” (aka “main chick,”). A “main” is commonly referred to as a woman who’s the primary partner for a man (or only partner).\n\nIn this case, “the league,” is her talking to other men, playing with Drake’s emotions, and not taking their relationship seriously. Drake implies that he feels a connection with her that’s deeper than usual, and is pleading with her to stop “playing,” and settle down with him.", "annotation_id": "39795693", "url": "https://genius.com/39795693", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "Home or away, this shit ain’t a game", "vault_lines": ["Home or away, this shit ain't a game"], "meaning": "In most sports, including WNBA, teams are either “home,” or “away,” depending on the venue the game is being held at. Drake is saying whether the woman in question is at home or away on a trip, their relationship and how her actions affect him “ain’t a game,” (implying Drake’s emotions aren’t something to be played around with).", "annotation_id": "39795682", "url": "https://genius.com/39795682", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "I know that you ball, I know that you play like girls in the WNBA", "vault_lines": ["I know that you ball, I know that you play like girls in the WNBA"], "meaning": "The WNBA serves as an extended metaphor for the woman’s lifestyle throughout the song. Drake uses the athletic world to describe someone who is competitive, constantly traveling, and operating at a high level.\n\n“You ball” is a compliment she’s elite, dedicated, always on the move. But Drake immediately parenthetically adds “I’m just playin'”, keeping it playful rather than literal. The basketball framework then runs through the whole verse: going “home or away,” staying in your lane, the idea that “this shit ain’t a game” all sports language applied to romantic stakes.", "annotation_id": "39792170", "url": "https://genius.com/39792170", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "Girl, we never gon’ fade, I know how it be when you be in LA​​", "vault_lines": ["Girl, we never gon' fade, I know how it be when you be in LA​​"], "meaning": "Drake says “when you be in LA” and then proceeds to say “or go MIA or hit JFK.” This line has two meanings. The phrase “MIA” can mean “missing in action,” a military term used for soldiers whose status is unknown after war. Drake compares this idea to his relationship with the woman, implying she disappears or becomes emotionally distant. At the same time, the lyric is also a play on airport names and travel culture. MIA is the airport code for Miami and JFK is the airport code for New York. This reflects the constant travel lifestyle common among influencers, artists, and wealthy individuals. The line also connects back to Drake’s earlier lyric, “girl we never goin’ fade, I know how it be when you be in LA,” with “LA” subtly referencing LAX airport as well. Drake ties the emotional distance in the relationship directly to movement and travel culture, showing how fame and constantly living between cities can cause people to “go MIA” both emotionally and physically.", "annotation_id": "39790531", "url": "https://genius.com/39790531", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "You keep goin’ away, Bahamas, MIA", "vault_lines": ["You keep goin' away, Bahamas, MIA"], "meaning": "Another double entendre implying the woman is constantly “goin' away,” to the Bahamas. “MIA,” in this case means both “missing in action,” and “Miami.” There are few direct flights to the Bahamas due to the small size of their airport and short length of their runway. Miami is a common layover for people going to the Bahamas, because it is one of the only major airports close enough to have direct flights.", "annotation_id": "39780470", "url": "https://genius.com/39780470", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "Retire your number, go out with a bang, damn What she gon’ do for a ring? Damn", "vault_lines": ["Retire your number, go out with a bang, damn", "What she gon' do for a ring? Damn"], "meaning": "Drake is telling this woman to retire her number (or phone number), aka stop speaking to men and being promiscuous. Retiring a deemed legendary athlete’s jersey number (so nobody else in the league can ever play with it, preserving their legacy) is also present in sports leagues such as the WNBA and NBA.\n\nIn the line “What she gon' do for a ring?”, the word “ring” can either allude to a championship ring, given to the members of the WNBA (or NBA) season finals winning team, or a wedding ring (asking what will she do to “earn,” a potential marriage).", "annotation_id": "39775864", "url": "https://genius.com/39775864", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "Ayy, how do you manage to Cartier Crash But stay in your lane?", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, how do you manage to Cartier Crash", "But stay in your lane?"], "meaning": "The Cartier Crash is a uniquely shaped asymmetrical Cartier wristwatch.\n\n\n\nThis is also a double entendre. He’s questioning how it’s possible to crash when you stay in your lane, as wordplay on driving.", "annotation_id": "39775854", "url": "https://genius.com/39775854", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WNBA", "line": "Or go MIA or hit JFK, stressin’ you out, should give you a raise", "vault_lines": ["Or go MIA or hit JFK, stressin' you out, should give you a raise"], "meaning": "Go Missing In Action, and hit John F. Kennedy [Airport].\n\nThere’s also a double meaning: In addition to meaning “missing in action”, MIA is also the airport code for Miami International Airport – which is among the ten biggest airports in the United States, alongside the aforementioned LA(X) and JFK.", "annotation_id": "39775845", "url": "https://genius.com/39775845", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
