{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "Face, no face, no face No face, no face, no face No face, no face, no face No face, no face, no", "vault_lines": ["I can't be showin' no stress in my face, what? Yeah", "No face, no face", "No face, no face, no face", "No face, no face, no", "I can't be showin' no stress in my face, what?"], "meaning": "Well Clearly another Diss to Exhu Yasuke for wearing a mask the first diss was on push-ups saying Yasuke has not been to Japan so now is he prepping Japan, and as you know Yasuke responded be speaking to his Japanese fans in Japanese the backlash drake received was brutal", "annotation_id": "36178347", "url": "https://genius.com/36178347", "state": "pending", "votes": -7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "I can’t be showin’ no stress in my face, what?", "vault_lines": ["I can't be showin' no stress in my face, what? Yeah", "No face, no face", "No face, no face, no face", "No face, no face, no", "I can't be showin' no stress in my face, what?"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "33490320", "url": "https://genius.com/33490320", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "No face, no face, no face No face, no face,", "vault_lines": ["I can't be showin' no stress in my face, what? Yeah", "No face, no face", "No face, no face, no face", "No face, no face, no", "I can't be showin' no stress in my face, what?"], "meaning": "Drake is saying how he can not be showing stress or no expression on his face, or possibly in a balaclava to hide his face… Unlikely but a possibility\n\nThe expression could reference the feud between him and Kendrick Lamar, which many people believe Drake lost. However, this is unlikely due to the timing of the “100 Gigs” album dropping, which has lots of shots at the Compton rapper. This song has shot after shot towards Kendrick.", "annotation_id": "33293705", "url": "https://genius.com/33293705", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "All the shit they don’t address in my face", "vault_lines": ["All the shit they don't address in my face"], "meaning": "Drake felt deeply disrespected by Kendrick’s “Control” verse, interpreting it as a serious threat. When he saw Kendrick five days later, he was uncertain if things would escalate. At the VMAs, Kendrick greeted Drake casually without mentioning the verse, which likely made Drake feel even more slighted, as he perceived Kendrick as a friend who had betrayed him and then acted as if everything was fine.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N1q0ycGataI\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dbqiIqJpavo", "annotation_id": "32881302", "url": "https://genius.com/32881302", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "Me, I’m expressionless Niggas be talkin’ ’bout don’t shoot the messenger Fah-fah-fah, you get shot for way less in here None of you pussies is blockin’ my blessings here, what?", "vault_lines": ["Niggas be talkin' 'bout don't shoot the messenger", "Fah-fah-fah, you get shot for way less in here", "None of you pussies is blockin' my blessings here, what?"], "meaning": "Drake appears to be addressing people in the industry offering their input on his recent beef with Kendrick Lamar and other rappers, questioning whether he lost or can make a comeback, all while under the guise of being a “messenger” of public opinion.\n\nHe suggests he is cutting ties with anyone speaking negatively on him, asserting that they will not be contributing to his downfall as his career is far from finished and has significant plans on the horizon.", "annotation_id": "32792502", "url": "https://genius.com/32792502", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "All these years throwin’ success in your face All of these baddies obsessed with my face", "vault_lines": ["All these years throwin' success in your face", "All of these baddies obsessed with my face"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his competitors in the rap game being bitter about how he’s been flaunting his success over the last 15 years and taking their women.\n\nIt reached a breaking point in Spring 2024 where many artists teamed up to go against Drake with the intention of “eliminating him”, including but not limited to; Metro Boomin, The Weeknd, Rick Ross, Future, ASAP Rocky, Kanye West, and Kendrick Lamar.\n\nIt’s alleged that one of the reasons the artists teamed up against Drake was over women. Drake is openly a ladies man and is rumoured to have had relations with women who are tied to these artists.\n\nOn Push Ups he mentions\n\n“I might take your latest girl and cuff her like I’m Ricky”\n\na line aimed at Rick Ross which suggests Drake got involved with a woman who belonged to Ross.\n\nOn Family Matters, Drake says\n\n“I mean it’s true a nigga slimed me for my AP, just like how Metro nigga slimed him for his main squeeze”\n\nwhich is another line stating that a woman who was with one of these artists (Metro Boomin) was taken from them. It’s speculated whether Drake is actually referring to himself in the third person in this line.", "annotation_id": "32792193", "url": "https://genius.com/32792193", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "All these years throwin’ success in your face All of these baddies obsessed with my face", "vault_lines": ["All these years throwin' success in your face", "All of these baddies obsessed with my face"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his competitors in the rap game being bitter about how he’s been flaunting his success over the last 15 years and taking their women.\n\nIt reached a breaking point in Spring 2024 where many artists teamed up to go against Drake with the intention of “eliminating him”, including but not limited to; Metro Boomin, The Weeknd, Rick Ross, Future, ASAP Rocky, Kanye West, and Kendrick Lamar.\n\nIt’s alleged that one of the reasons the artists teamed up against Drake was over women. Drake is openly a ladies man and is rumoured to have had relations with women who are tied to these artists.\n\nOn Push Ups he mentions\n\n“I might take your latest girl and cuff her like I’m Ricky”\n\na line aimed at Rick Ross which suggests Drake got involved with a woman who belonged to Ross.\n\nOn Family Matters, Drake says\n\n“I mean it’s true a nigga slimed me for my AP, just like how Metro nigga slimed him for his main squeeze”\n\nwhich is another line stating that a woman who was with one of these artists (Metro Boomin) was taken from them. It’s speculated whether Drake is actually referring to himself in the third person in this line.", "annotation_id": "32792118", "url": "https://genius.com/32792118", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "I don’t get sleepy off no melatonin My therapist put in a thirty-day notice", "vault_lines": ["I don't get sleepy off no melatonin", "My therapist put in a thirty-day notice"], "meaning": "Melatonin is a hormone often used as a sleep aid in insomniacs. Drake’s mention of it suggests he’s not losing sleep over his beef with Kendrick Lamar. This lack of distress on Drake’s part was already highlighted on “Family Matters”:\n\nAnd, nigga, Cole losin' sleep on this, it ain’t me\n\nThe second part could be a rebuttal to Kendrick Lamar’s claims on “Meet the Grahams,” where Kendrick listed a series of self-destructive behaviors that Drake has indulged himself in, and in turn suggested therapy as a solution:\n\nYou got gamblin' problems, drinkin' problems, pill-poppin' and spendin' problems\nBad with money, whorehouse\nSolicitin' women problems, therapy’s a lovely start\n\nThrough the verse, Drake highlights that his intense focus, primarily on managing personal struggles and addressing envy from others, is so consuming that his therapist decided to end their professional relationship. These issues in question juxtaposes with Kendrick’s portrayal of Drake’s broader set of personal issues.", "annotation_id": "32778105", "url": "https://genius.com/32778105", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "I’m spreadin’ my wings, I hop out, cocoon I’m studio trappin’, I’m locked in the room, what?", "vault_lines": ["I'm spreadin' my wings, I hop out, cocoon", "I'm studio trappin', I'm locked in the room, what?"], "meaning": "Drake likens his new phase of creativity to an insect’s metamorphosis, suggesting that he has been going through a period of artistic development preparing for a significant release. This also serves as a subtle warning to his adversaries that he is not done yet and is coming back stronger despite the aftermath of his beef with Kendrick Lamar.\n\n“trappin\" serves as a double entendre highlighting his long recording sessions in the studio as if he is actually trapped in the studio, It also nods to the popular Atlanta rap style known as trap music, which that in itself could be Drake’s way of ascertaining that he got more tracks, with rappers from Atlanta, in the making. Lamar made several references to Atlanta and it’s rappers in “Not Like Us,” accusing Drake of interacting with Atlanta and its rappers to validate his Blackness and profit from Black people:\n\nYou run to Atlanta when you need a check balance\n\nDrake and PARTYNEXTDOOR are set to release a collaborative studio album in the fall of 2024. The news was confirmed by Drake himself after an appearance at his OVO signees' show in Toronto.\n\nOn behalf of me and Party, we’ve been working on something for y’all\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/Q7EW-GWV6Xg?feature=share\n\nIt was also rumored that Drake might be releasing an album solely produced by Conductor Williams some time this year.", "annotation_id": "32776581", "url": "https://genius.com/32776581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "This is the shit that my daddy had raised me on", "vault_lines": ["This is the shit that my daddy had raised me on"], "meaning": "With the recent beef in the summer of 2024, People have been questioning Drakes roots like if he wasn’t raised in Memphis with his dad growing up.", "annotation_id": "32774837", "url": "https://genius.com/32774837", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "It’s so many people we turnin’ the tables on Capo got plans, but we’ll save that for later on", "vault_lines": ["It's so many people we turnin' the tables on", "Capo got plans, but we'll save that for later on"], "meaning": "During the Kendrick feud, a number of former Drake celebrity friends pledged their support for the West Coast rapper, something to which he alludes to in the second verse. A famous example would be fellow Compton Native DeMar DeRozan, who was close with Drake during his stint with the Toronto Raptors. DeRozan famously popped out at Kendrick’s “Juneteenth concert” and was featured on the Not Like Us video.\n\nChubbs, Drake’s long-time friend and head of security is ready to do them just like he did Detail, but Drake’s pacing himself on the retaliation.", "annotation_id": "32756078", "url": "https://genius.com/32756078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "Hush’ll keep sayin’ they know that they played it wrong", "vault_lines": ["Hush'll keep sayin' they know that they played it wrong"], "meaning": "OVO Hush formerly know as Young Tony is a close friend of Drake and has contributed to his music before, most notably getting writing credits on both “Take Care” and “Nothing Was the Same” albums.\n\nAs a skilled MC himself – Drake has referred to him as one of his favourites rappers –  Hush stands by his man’s ability to still come out of this war the winner.", "annotation_id": "32755541", "url": "https://genius.com/32755541", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "O’ keep on sendin’ me slaps to go crazy on 40 keep sayin’ it’s time to be patient", "vault_lines": ["O' keep on sendin' me slaps to go crazy on", "40 keep sayin' it's time to be patient"], "meaning": "Noah “40” Shebib and Oliver El-Khatib are long time friends and associates of Drake who have been there for the majority of his career. With 40 being Drake’s longtime producer and engineer and both being co-founders of October’s Very Own and OVO SOUND.\n\nOliver, who Drake calls “The Elusive One”, has been responsible for putting in on to various new sounds and artists, famously showing him The Weeknd’s music for the first time. He also refers to him as his Consigliere and while at war, O would be the one advising him on which moves to make next.\n\n40 on the other hand, seems to be Drake’s good conscience.", "annotation_id": "32755512", "url": "https://genius.com/32755512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "This is the moment I know they been prayin’ on What? Ayy, what? Ayy Try knock The Boy off, but fuck it, I’m stayin’ on", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, what? What? What?", "I gotta know, I gotta know", "This is the moment I know they been prayin' on", "Try knock The Boy off, but fuck it, I'm stayin' on"], "meaning": "Drake had a busy couple of months dishing out and taking disses from other artists including Kendrick Lamar, Future, Weeknd, Metro Boomin, Kanye West, ASAP Rocky, and Rick Ross.\n\nEven with the Kendrick Lamar diss “Not Like Us” doing well on the charts and breaking streaming records, Drake is holding his stance  firm saying he isn’t out just yet.", "annotation_id": "32755377", "url": "https://genius.com/32755377", "state": "pending", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "‘Cause I keep on talkin’ ’bout beefin’ and business and money and women, there’s no diagnosis They emptied the clip (Pfft) quick Swapped that shit out and I came back reloaded I’m just so happy that niggas who envied and held that shit in got to finally show it I’m over the moon, yeah, we’ll see you boys soon", "vault_lines": ["They emptied the clip (Pfft) quick", "Swapped that shit out and I came back reloaded", "I'm just so happy that niggas who envied and held that shit in got to finally show it", "I'm over the moon, yeah, we'll see you boys soon"], "meaning": "Throughout 2024, Drake found himself at the center of rap’s civil war, standing as the lone wolf against a plethora of adversaries. His decade-long feud with Kendrick Lamar escalated dramatically, pulling in figures like Metro Boomin, Future, A$AP Rocky, and The Weeknd, all of whom took shots at Drake alongside Kendrick on Future and Metro’s 2024 WE DON’T TRUST YOU duology. Adding to the beef, Rick Ross and Kanye West also dropped diss tracks aimed at Drake, with Kanye even stating in an interview that this was a coordinated effort:\n\nEverybody was very, very energized about the elimination of Drake\n\nDrake, who was undoubtedly the biggest rapper of the 2010s, suggests that the attacks stem from envy and jealousy, and these rappers are now finally showing their true colors. Drake’s reference to being “over the Moon” and his promise that “we’ll see you boys soon” signals that he’s not backing down and is ready to confront these challenges head-on, possibly hinting at future diss tracks.", "annotation_id": "32755375", "url": "https://genius.com/32755375", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "KD just text, “What the fuck are we waitin’ on?” Damn", "vault_lines": ["KD just text, \"What the fuck are we waitin' on?\" Damn"], "meaning": "Kevin Durant is a long time friend of Drake and OVO affiliate who was even listed as an A&R for Drake’s album For All The Dogs. While also being an executive producer on For All The Dogs: Scary Hours Edition. He has also name dropped the famous hoop star on Views’ “Weston Road Flows” and More Life intro “Free Smoke”.", "annotation_id": "32755283", "url": "https://genius.com/32755283", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Face", "line": "Niggas got lit off the features I skated on I gotta know, I gotta know How you get lit off the nigga you hatin’ on? Numbers untouchable, they got the data wrong", "vault_lines": ["Niggas got lit off the features I skated on", "I gotta know, I gotta know", "How you get lit off the nigga you hatin' on?", "Numbers untouchable, they got the data wrong"], "meaning": "The “Drake Stimulus Package” is a well-documented phenomenon where a feature from Drake can skyrocket an artist’s commercial success. The facts speak for themselves:\n\nThe effects of a Drake feature\n\nHowever throughout 2024, Drake found himself at the center of rap’s civil war, standing as the lone wolf against a plethora of adversaries. His decade-long feud with Kendrick Lamar escalated dramatically, pulling in figures like Metro Boomin, Future, A$AP Rocky, and The Weeknd, all of whom took shots at Drake alongside Kendrick on Future and Metro’s 2024 WE DON’T TRUST YOU duology. Adding to the beef, Rick Ross and Kanye West also dropped diss tracks aimed at Drake.\n\nDrake questions how these artists can harbor hate towards him when he’s the one who propelled many of them to greater commercial heights, either through collaborations or by exposing them to a broader audience.\n\nDrake’s point is well-founded: both Future and Rick Ross have achieved some of their biggest hits through collaborations with him, such as “WAIT FOR U” and “Aston Martin Music” respectively. Beyond features, Drake significantly boosted the careers of Kendrick Lamar and A$AP Rocky by taking them on his 2012 Club Paradise Tour when they were still relatively unknown. Additionally, Drake was one of the earliest and most vocal supporters of The Weeknd, featuring him extensively on his 2011 sophomore album Take Care at a critical point in The Weeknd’s career.", "annotation_id": "32755184", "url": "https://genius.com/32755184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
