{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "That’s How You Feel", "line": "Time, it done left you with a broken heart Hardly excuses how you play with mine Mindin’ my business and you show up Up to no good, I should’ve seen the signs Signin’ me up to do your dirty work Workin’ to try and get you off my mind", "vault_lines": ["Time, it done left you with a broken heart", "Hardly excuses how you play with mine", "Mindin' my business and you show up", "Up to no good, I should've seen the signs", "Signin' me up to do your dirty work", "Workin' to try and get you off my mind", "Sidetrackin' me when I was good to you"], "meaning": "He continues his wordplay from the first verse.", "annotation_id": "34189490", "url": "https://genius.com/34189490", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "That’s How You Feel", "line": "Time gets to tickin’ and it takes a toll Told you I couldn’t really draw the line Line ringin’, sayin’ that you told him things Things that you could’ve kept inside Sidetrackin’ me when I was good to you You were somebody I would stand behind", "vault_lines": ["Time gets to tickin' and it takes a toll", "Told you I couldn't really draw the line", "Line ringin', sayin' that you told him things", "Things that you could've kept inside", "Sidetrackin' me when I was good to you", "You were somebody I would stand behind"], "meaning": "In this song, every word at the end of the line is repeated in some way in the next verse. (Toll -> Tol(d), Line -> Line). First, he explains he cant draw the line between them. His phone rings explaining what she’s done, the things she could have kept inside. He also explains how he felt he could trust or “stand behind” her, but she sidetracked him.", "annotation_id": "34189461", "url": "https://genius.com/34189461", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "That’s How You Feel", "line": "Your friends say they miss ya But they don’t really miss ya", "vault_lines": ["Your friends say they miss ya", "But they don't really miss ya"], "meaning": "Drizzy is referring to the toxicity of social media. He’s talking about the aspect of social media in which people comment positive but over-exaggerated remarks that are often much nicer than the truth. The 6 God gives an example of this when expressing to a partner that her friends “say” that they miss her, but in actuality they’re simply embellishing their comments.", "annotation_id": "26464970", "url": "https://genius.com/26464970", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "That’s How You Feel", "line": "[Verse 1: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In both verses Drake uses a stylistic device called anadiplosis, where the last word of a line is used to begin the next one.\n\n\n\n\nVerse 1:\n\nI’m just in the cut sittin' sideways\nWay that you lookin' all the time\nTime gets to tickin' and it takes a toll\nTold you I couldn’t really draw the line\nLine ringin', sayin' that you told him things\nThings that you could’ve kept inside\nSidetrackin' me when I was good to you\nYou were somebody I would stand behind\n\nVerse 2:\n\nI’m just in the cut sittin' sideways\nWay that you lookin' all the time\nTime,it done left you with a broken heart\nHardly excuses how you play with mine\nMindin' my business and you show up\nUp to no good, I should’ve seen the signs\nSignin' me up to do your dirty work\nWorkin' to try to get you off my mind", "annotation_id": "14894725", "url": "https://genius.com/14894725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "That’s How You Feel", "line": "I’m just in the cut sittin’ sideways Way that you lookin’ all the time", "vault_lines": ["I'm just in the cut sittin' sideways", "Way that you lookin' all the time"], "meaning": "Drake uses wordplay on sitting and looking sideways when he’s watching his lover. “Sitting sideways” could be an ode to Houston legend and rapper Paul Wall’s single “Sittin Sideways.” The phrase ultimately means to flip the switch on the hydraulics in your car, so it literally sits sideways.\n\n\n\nDrake uses a familiar line in his 2016 track, “Too Good”:\n\nIt feels like the only time you see me\nIs when you turn your head to the side and look at me differently", "annotation_id": "14891323", "url": "https://genius.com/14891323", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "That’s How You Feel", "line": "I know you wanna Vacay to a place where you could Take pictures, post on Insta", "vault_lines": ["Vacay to a place where you could", "Take pictures, post on Insta"], "meaning": "This line relates to another verse from “Emotionless”, the fourth track of Scorpion:\n\nI know a girl whose one goal was to visit Rome\nThen she finally got to Rome\nAnd all she did was post pictures for people at home\n‘Cause all that mattered was impressin’ everybody she’s known\nI know another girl that’s cryin' out for help\nBut her latest caption is “Leave me alone”\n\nBoth lines tie into Drake’s views on social media. One of the central themes on Scorpion is his struggle with social media; this instance is succumbing to his lover’s sojourn to a certain location to impress her social media followers.", "annotation_id": "14883940", "url": "https://genius.com/14883940", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "That’s How You Feel", "line": "I know you like to drink ’til the sun up Grind ’til you come up Work all winter, shine all summer", "vault_lines": ["I know you like to drink 'til the sun up", "Grind 'til you come up", "Work all winter, shine all summer"], "meaning": "This is an homage to DJ Khaled’s 2012 song “Take It to the Head,” which featured Drake’s label mates Lil Wayne and Nicki Minaj:\n\nWorking all winter\nShining all summer\n\nThe lines and cadence also call back to those from Take Care opener “Shot for Me” on which Drake raps:\n\nI could tell that you been crying all night, drinking all summer\nPrayin' for your happiness, hope that you recover", "annotation_id": "14877667", "url": "https://genius.com/14877667", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "That’s How You Feel", "line": "[Refrain: Nicki Minaj & DJ Boof] Rule number one, to be a boss-ass bitch (Uh-huh) Never let a clown nigga try to play you (Okay) If he play you, then rule number two (Okay) Is fuck his best friends and make ’em yes men (Okay) And– (Wow, that’s how you feel?)", "vault_lines": ["Rule number one, to be a boss-ass bitch (Uh-huh)", "Never let a clown nigga try to play you (Okay)", "If he play you, then rule number two (Okay)", "Is fuck his best friends and make 'em yes men (Okay)", "And– (Wow, that's how you feel?)"], "meaning": "This sample is from Nicki Minaj performing her remix of PTAF’s “Boss Ass Bitch” live at Power 106’s Powerhouse 2014. This sample could have been significant in terms of Drake’s beef with Meek Mill and the latter rapper’s lyrics on his “What’s Beef” remix.\n\nNicki and Drake are longtime friends and collaborators, as they have come together on for example Nicki’s “No Frauds” and Drake’s “Make Me Proud”. Nicki also commented on the song on her Instagram:", "annotation_id": "14876260", "url": "https://genius.com/14876260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 214, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "That’s How You Feel", "status": "done", "targets": 8, "clicked": 8, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-thats-how-you-feel-lyrics", "genius_title": "That’s How You Feel"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Time Flies", "line": "Heart’s still on javelin, baby", "vault_lines": ["Heart's still on javelin, baby"], "meaning": "He’s still dealing with the piercing pain of heartbreak.", "annotation_id": "34741997", "url": "https://genius.com/34741997", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Time Flies", "line": "I could be your man but maybe down the road", "vault_lines": ["I could be your man but maybe down the road"], "meaning": "He wants to be in a relationship with this woman, but he just has absolutely no time for that now. That’s why he wishes all feelings of desire would disappear inside him, which is what it means when he says “rather see my heart turn to chrome, rather see my heart turn crystal.” He wants his heart to be stone-cold now so that he can deal with relationship stuff later.", "annotation_id": "34741939", "url": "https://genius.com/34741939", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Time Flies", "line": "Right now I would rather see my heart turn to chrome", "vault_lines": ["Right now I would rather see my heart turn to chrome", "Rather see my heart turn crystal"], "meaning": "Drake references the luxury clothing brand Chrome Hearts, popular for their jeans with leather crosses embroidered all over them.\n\nFrom denim to hoodies to custom Timberland boots to having the interior of his Rolls-Royce decorated by the brand, Drake has been seen rocking just about everything Chrome Hearts, and in May 2021 announced a collaboration with them, available for a limited time at the Chrome Hearts flagship store in Miami.", "annotation_id": "22917817", "url": "https://genius.com/22917817", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Time Flies", "line": "You believe in angles more than angels", "vault_lines": ["You believe in angles more than angels"], "meaning": "Drake uses an anagram with “angles” and “angels” and reminds us of his 2018 hit single “Nice For What”:\n\nWith your phone out, gotta hit them angles\nWith your phone out, snappin' like you Fabo\n\nAs many girls put in a lot of effort to get their followers and likes up on social media, Drake thinks it’s more likely this girl believes in getting the perfect pic for the gram with her best angle and great lighting than the angels themselves.\n\nAt the end of the day, for all of humanity, humans have questioned, “Do Angels Exist? As that answer is still debated, IG models, however, have proved how to work their magic with the angles.", "annotation_id": "19846658", "url": "https://genius.com/19846658", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Time Flies", "line": "I just caught a shawty off a finsta", "vault_lines": ["I just caught a shawty off a finsta"], "meaning": "A “finsta” is a fake Instagram account or an account not publicly linked to a user’s primary one.\n\nFinstas are particularly useful for celebrities who wish to remain anonymous online while retaining an official page. Some celebrities have admitted to using them, like Justin Bieber and Bella Throne for example.\n\nDrake himself previously hinted at the existence of a finsta, writing in a now-deleted post on his real Instagram:\n\nme when I find a shordee that didn’t slash anyone famous yet… is this my finsta? shit it’s not.", "annotation_id": "19736400", "url": "https://genius.com/19736400", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Time Flies", "line": "Yeah, I’m outside in an AMG Right outside, TT", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm outside in an AMG", "I'm outside in a—", "And-and-and-and I'm outside in a—", "I'm outside in an AMG, yeah"], "meaning": "Mercedes-AMG is the luxury subsidiary of Daimler AG known for custom high-performance vehicles. All AMG engines come from the factory with twin turbos, hence the line “right outside, TT.” This is also a double entendre for the phrase “Too Turnt,” coined by Toronto rapper Robin Banks. Drake has previously used the abbreviation in his Instagram caption to nod his head to Banks:\n\n\n\nDrake later posted his Mercedes-AMG G63 on his Instagram story:", "annotation_id": "19718447", "url": "https://genius.com/19718447", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Time Flies", "line": "Just threw on a hoodie, it’s a Crenshaw", "vault_lines": ["Just threw on a hoodie, it's a Crenshaw"], "meaning": "Drake pays homage to the late rapper and community activist, Nipsey Hussle, who he collaborated with on the song “Killer”.  Nipsey’s clothing imprint, The Marathon Clothing, sold sweatsuits and other items that bore a Crenshaw logo.\n\n\n\nRap legend Snoop Dogg (left) posing with Nipsey Hussle in Marathon Clothing merch.\n\nNipsey came from the Crenshaw neighborhood of South Central Los Angeles.  He has said that versions of the Crenshaw logo were inspired by a similar logo worn by the character Tre in Boyz n the Hood, which was filmed in the same neighborhood.\n\nIn March 2019, Nipsey Hussle was killed outside of his Marathon Clothing store, located on Crenshaw and Slauson.", "annotation_id": "19717797", "url": "https://genius.com/19717797", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Time Flies", "status": "done", "targets": 7, "clicked": 7, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-time-flies-lyrics", "genius_title": "Time Flies"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "[Chorus]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The opening scene of the music video, which features Drake surrounded by an array of firearms in a backyard setting, is inspired by National Geographic photographer Gabriele Galimberti’s The Ameriguns photo series.\n\n\n\nThe Italian photographer would end up suing Drake in November 2025, alleging the rapper’s music video copied imagery from his book The Ameriguns.The suit was reportedly dropped in January 2026.", "annotation_id": "39090638", "url": "https://genius.com/39090638", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "[Bridge]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "German artist Evîn Kücükali contributed the background vocals heard echoing throughout the second beat of the track.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/l2bCce1SyZ4?feature=share", "annotation_id": "36152185", "url": "https://genius.com/36152185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "Her ass is all natty like Florida Gator, let’s go", "vault_lines": ["Hеr ass is all natty like Florida Gator, let's go", "Ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, let's go, ayy, let's go", "Yeah, let's go, let's go", "Let's go, let's go"], "meaning": "The Florida Gators are the athletic teams representing the University of Florida. The word “natty” is commonly used as slang for winning a national championship in college sports. The Florida Gators men’s basketball team won the 2025 national championship.\n\n\n\nIn this context, Drake is complimenting a woman’s natural figure.", "annotation_id": "36127864", "url": "https://genius.com/36127864", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "I done made plenty shit right out of two wrongs, shit, let’s go", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, let's go, ayy, let's go", "I done made plenty shit right out of two wrongs, shit, let's go", "Yeah, let's go, let's go", "Let's go, let's go"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the well-known saying “Two wrongs don’t make a right,” where he flips it, saying that despite starting with mistakes or negative situations (“two wrongs”), he has managed to turn things around and create something positive (“made plenty shit right”). This speaks of his confidence that he will ultimately come out on top despite the public perception.\n\nThis is reminiscent of his situation after the Pusha T beef, where, despite the controversy, he stood his ground and released his album Scorpion, solidifying his status as one of the hottest artists alive. Similarly, Drake is signaling that he will emerge from the difficult aftermath of his beef with Kendrick Lamar as the dominant figure.", "annotation_id": "36127382", "url": "https://genius.com/36127382", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "Iceman, Tiffany blue stones", "vault_lines": ["Iceman, Tiffany blue stones"], "meaning": "Drake mentions the title of his upcoming album and the new persona or alter-ego he’s built for himself over the last 11 months, ICEMAN. He also refers to Tiffany & Co, the American luxury jewelry brand, which is very popular in hiphop culture.\n\n And also just the obvious color scheme connection, Tiffany & Co’s famous color is very similar to the blue color featured in the ICEMAN promotional materials, including the cover artwork for this single", "annotation_id": "36126616", "url": "https://genius.com/36126616", "state": "pending", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "I’m back in your city tonight, walkin’ around with my head high", "vault_lines": ["I'm back in your city tonight, walkin' around with my head high"], "meaning": "Possibly a shot at Kendrick Lamar, Drake’s arch-nemesis. Gang affiliation was a recurring theme in last year’s beef.\n\nAfter Kendrick recently performed in Toronto and had to be escorted out by over a dozen police cars just to leave the venue, Drake here ridicules him for it. He’s in Kendrick’s city with his chest out more confident and comfortable than ever just like when he was in Compton for the Game’s 100 video shoot.", "annotation_id": "36126576", "url": "https://genius.com/36126576", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "She might decide to say no to me now, but say yes to me later, let’s go", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, let's go, ayy, let's go", "Yeah, let's go, let's go", "Let's go, let's go", "She might decide to say no to me now, but say yеs to me later, let's go"], "meaning": "In the context of Drake’s recent controversies, some people—whether women in a romantic sense or the general audience—might currently reject him, either personally or professionally.\n\nHowever, Drake expresses confidence that this rejection is not permanent. He believes that his upcoming album, Iceman, will be so compelling and successful that those who turned away will come back to support him.", "annotation_id": "36126545", "url": "https://genius.com/36126545", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "And nobody cares until they in front of your tombstone Y’all been on that type of timing for too long", "vault_lines": ["And nobody cares until they in front of your tombstone", "Y'all been on that type of timing for too long"], "meaning": "Aubrey referrs to the phenomenon that people only get the recognition they deserve once they left the world, drakes though process might be “right now im here and im popping new music all the time so they take me for granted, but once im gone they will recognize me as the legend who dropped classic projects”\n\nHe also adds on how he sees the people around him as fake when they switched up when the timing is in early-mid 2024.\n\nA long time collabarator of Drake, producer Conductor Williams leakd a track named “Fighting Irish Freestyle In which he delves deeper into people he belive stratigeclly conspired to hurt him like LeBron James, Future, The Weeknd, Rocky, and obviously Kendrick Lamar.", "annotation_id": "36124921", "url": "https://genius.com/36124921", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "It feels like nobody’s there until you start givin’ out two-tones", "vault_lines": ["It feels like nobody's there until you start givin' out two-tones"], "meaning": "Drake says he only gets affection when he is gifting people things like two-tone luxury watches. He has previously used this expression in the song 7 AM on Bridle Path in the line\n\nSee the watch collection and assume I got time\n‘Cause of the ruby rose two-tone\n\nThis meaning is reinforced by one of the following lines\n\nY'all been on that type of timing for too long\n\nDrake is also alluding to Virgil, who released a highly sought after limited series of two-toned Mercedes‑Maybach’s in 2022 a year after his untimely passing from cancer in 2021.\n\nAnd nobody cares until they in front of your tombstone\n\nDrake is making reference to Virgil’s passing and to many of his peers (like Kanye) who weren’t around for until Virgil his passing, but were clamoring to get one of the Maybach’s.", "annotation_id": "36124682", "url": "https://genius.com/36124682", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "You niggas just better not ask for no favors, let’s go, let’s go, let’s go It’s love for my brothers and death to a traitor, let’s go", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, let's go, ayy, let's go", "Yeah, let's go, let's go", "Let's go, let's go", "You niggas just better not ask for no favors, let's go, let's go, let's go", "It's love for my brothers and death to a traitor, let's go"], "meaning": "This comes on the heels of Drake’s historic beef with Kendrick Lamar, which culminated in a heated exchange of diss tracks throughout 2024 and subliminal shots traded throughout 2025. Essentially, Drake announced he is no longer doing features as he once did. Before the beef, Drake was known for collaborating and showing love to other rappers, both established and emerging, including Kendrick, whom Drake worked with early on before Kendrick’s mainstream breakthrough. However, many of Drake’s former collaborators turned their backs on him, siding with Lamar. As a result, Drake is no longer doing features or favours for artists who have not shown him loyalty in recent times.", "annotation_id": "36123746", "url": "https://genius.com/36123746", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "I’m whippin’ around on like six hundred acres, let’s go, let’s go, let’s go", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, let's go, ayy, let's go", "Yeah, let's go, let's go", "Let's go, let's go", "I'm whippin' around on like six hundred acres, let's go, let's go, let's go"], "meaning": "Drake references the Inn at Dos Brisas, a 313-acre luxury ranch located in Brenham, Texas, which he purchased in October 2024. The ranch serves as a private retreat where he often secluded himself to get away and deal with a tough year.\n\n\n\nDrake with his son Adonis and dad Dennis Graham at his Texas ranch\n\nNotably, the first part of the track’s music video was filmed on the backyard of the Texas ranch.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/weU76DGHKU0?t=11", "annotation_id": "36123727", "url": "https://genius.com/36123727", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "Yeah, niggas get punched in the face on some TLC shit, on the dead guys Some TLC shit ‘cause, my nigga, you gon’ need a chilly ice pack for your left eye", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, niggas get punched in the face on some TLC shit, on the dead guys", "Some TLC shit 'cause, my nigga, you gon' need a chilly ice pack for your left eye"], "meaning": "During a performance at the Ignite Music Festival in Vancouver, Canada,  Rick Ross played Kendrick Lamar’s diss track “Not Like Us” amid the height of the Drake vs. Kendrick Lamar feud. This provoked a backstage brawl, and footage circulated online showing Ross apparently being punched in the face.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/dU1Z7KRmWjc\n\nThe man who punched Rick Ross, known as Icarus, is reportedly affiliated with the Hells Angels, a notorious biker gang which is indirectly connected to Drake.\n\nTLC commonly means “tender, love, and care,” something one would need after getting punched. Drake also references the iconic R&B group TLC whose members include Chilli and Left Eye:\n\n\n“Chilly ice pack”: Refers both to the need for a cold compress after a fight and to the group member “Chilli.”\n“Left eye”: Refers to Lisa “Left Eye” Lopes, and also to the literal left eye, which might need an ice pack after being punched. (Ironically, Ross was punched on the left side of his face during the incident.)\n\n\n\nAdditionally, TLC could be a subtle nod to WWE’s “Tables, Ladders, and Chairs” matches, known for their intense physicality and brutality.", "annotation_id": "36123140", "url": "https://genius.com/36123140", "state": "accepted", "votes": 107, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "What did I miss? What did I miss? What did I miss? What did I miss?", "vault_lines": ["Making sure wires were hit, man, what did I miss?", "You always felt like this, man, what did I miss?"], "meaning": "Drake reflects on the support he gave to artists who’ve since turned against him, questioning how things soured despite all he contributed. Lines like “giving out verses and bookings to y’all” point to his history of helping others rise: from taking Kendrick Lamar and A$AP Rocky on his 2012 Club Paradise Tour, to being one of the first to co-sign The Weeknd and featuring him heavily on his 2011 sophomore album Take Care, back when Abel was still on the come-up.\n\nThe rapper also collaborated extensively with Future and Rick Ross, relationships that helped produce some of their biggest hits, such as “WAIT FOR U” and “Aston Martin Music” respectively.\n\nThe repeated question, “What did I miss?,” captures Drake’s disbelief and hurt. He’s trying to understand how people who once stood beside him ended up cliquing up against him in 2024’s rap civil war, which Kanye West dubbed “the elimination of Drake.” Whether out of envy, convenience, or long-buried resentment, Drake sees it as more than just rap politics, it’s betrayal from people he once considered close.", "annotation_id": "36122782", "url": "https://genius.com/36122782", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "Askin’ me, “How did it feel?” Can’t say it didn’t surprise me Last time I looked to my right, you niggas was standing beside me How can some people I love hang around pussies who try me? Let’s go", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, let's go, ayy, let's go", "Askin' me, \"How did it feel?\" Can't say it didn't surprise me", "Yeah, let's go, let's go", "Last time I looked to my right, you niggas was standing beside me", "How can some people I love hang around pussies who try me? Let's go", "Let's go, let's go"], "meaning": "Drake expresses disbelief and disappointment over how many of his former collaborators and close associates turned against him during the 2024 rap civil war sparked by Kendrick Lamar’s verse on “Like That.”\n\nThe diss came on a song by Future, an artist Drake has a long, successful history with, making the attack sting even more. The fallout widened, with artists like Rick Ross, A$AP Rocky, and The Weeknd also taking shots at Drake, despite all having benefited from early co-signs or major collaborations with him.\n\nThe line may also extend beyond the rap world. Drake may be addressing LeBron James, who was seen dancing at Kendrick’s The Pop Out: Ken & Friends concert in June 2024 —a show widely viewed as a victory lap following Kendrick’s dominant performance in the feud. The concert’s climax featured five encores of “Not Like Us,” a track that directly targeted Drake with accusations of pedophilia. Given their long-standing friendship, Drake questions how someone he once considered family could be so at ease supporting those publicly tearing him down.\n\nDrake ultimately and painfully questions how people he once helped, or even loved, are now aligning with his enemies.", "annotation_id": "36122726", "url": "https://genius.com/36122726", "state": "accepted", "votes": 141, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "You switched on the guys and supported a hater, let’s go What’s the get-back for niggas? It’s TBD I look at this shit like a BTC Could be down this week, then I’m up next week", "vault_lines": ["You switched on the guys and supported a hater, let's go", "What's the get-back for niggas? It's TBD", "I look at this shit like a BTC", "Could be down this week, then I'm up next week", "Ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, ayy, let's go, let's go", "Ayy, let's go, ayy, let's go", "Yeah, let's go, let's go", "Let's go, let's go"], "meaning": "Drake speaks on possible revenge for all the friends who switched up on him during his high-profile feud with Kendrick Lamar. Some notable names that make the list include Lebron James, Demar Derozan, and Rick Ross.\n\nThe timeline of his plans is like Bitcoin (BTC); they could change at anytime. Bitcoin itself is famously volatile, with its value changing by the minute based on current events and regulatory factors.", "annotation_id": "36122722", "url": "https://genius.com/36122722", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "line": "I saw bro went to Pop Out with them, but been dick riding gang since “Headlines”", "vault_lines": ["I saw bro went to Pop Out with them, but been dick riding gang since \"Headlines\""], "meaning": "“Pop Out” refers to The Pop Out: Ken & Friends, a June 2024 concert by Kendrick Lamar.\n\nhttps://pbs.twimg.com/media/GQfhF9qbcAAnU_7.jpg:large Kendrick Lamar amongst various artists and gang members during his The Pop Out: Ken & Friends performance.\n\nHeld in Inglewood, California, the concert was not only a celebration of West Coast hip hop culture featuring various artists of the area but essentially a victory lap following Lamar’s unanimously declared win over Drake in their high profile beef with the May 2024 hit diss track “Not Like Us.” The concert’s name evokes one of many iconic lines from the track:\n\nSometimes you gotta pop out and show niggas\n\nWhile it isn’t explicitly clear who Drake is referring to in these lines, he calls out a longtime supporter of his, someone who’s apparently been a fan since his 2011 hit “Headlines,” for their betrayal by attending the concert in solidarity with Lamar.\n\nMany have speculated this line was a jab at NBA player LeBron James, who fittingly attended said Pop Out as a Los Angeles Lakers legend:\n\n\n\n LeBron James at The Pop Out.\n\n\nDrake and James have had a public friendship dating back to the release party for Drake’s 2009 mixtape So Far Gone where James made an appearance. However, the relationship has seemingly soured since the Pop Out with fans speculating that these lines from Drake’s January 2025 “Fighting Irish” freestyle were aimed at James for the very same reason of betrayal:\n\nThe world fell in love with the gimmicks, even my brothers got tickets\nSeemed like they loved every minute\nJust know this shit is personal to us, and it wasn’t just business\n[…]\nGod forbid we ever got to tarnish your public image\nAll that brother talk as if I really discovered a sibling\n6 Degrees, my memory’s retrievin' our humble beginnings", "annotation_id": "36122718", "url": "https://genius.com/36122718", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What Did I Miss?", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 16, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-what-did-i-miss-lyrics", "genius_title": "What Did I Miss?"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "24", "line": "V’s not finished", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, know you're alive and God's not finished", "Know you're alive and God's not finished", "I know you're alive, God's not finished", "God's not finished, God's not—", "The Devil's a lie and God's not finished", "God's not finished, God's not finished", "LA's not finished, Ye's not finished", "Drake's not finished, V's not finished"], "meaning": "The V in question is the late Virgil Abloh, who unfortunately passed away 11 days prior to the Free Larry Hoover concert.", "annotation_id": "24939616", "url": "https://genius.com/24939616", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
