{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Me and my broski are twins, but we don’t look alike", "vault_lines": ["Me and my broski are twins, but we don't look alike"], "meaning": "Drake has many notable friends in his various circles from his OVO crew to Young Money, but as the first line of the verse mentions October Firm, a collaboration between Drake and OVO brand manager Oliver El-Khatib, this line is likely a reference to the friendship between Drake and Oliver.\n\n\n\nOliver was born on October 1, 1986, and Drake was born on October 24, 1986, so there are not far from being twins with a mere 23 days between them.", "annotation_id": "11903966", "url": "https://genius.com/11903966", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "They want me gone, out of the picture (Yah)", "vault_lines": ["They want me gone, out of the picture (Yah)", "They want me gone, wait for the kicker"], "meaning": "Drake has been constantly referring to an on-going conspiracy against his life in his lyrics. The pessimistic imagery of planned assassination started popping up on his songs back in 2015. Such references were all over If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late.\n\nDrake continuously suggests that “higher-ups” are after him. This idea of people with power wanting to end his run is not too far-fetched, as Drake has been dominating the music industry and grown beyond simply being a hip-hop artist.", "annotation_id": "11617077", "url": "https://genius.com/11617077", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Contract max, I gotta bring that shit back (Blah)", "vault_lines": ["Contract max, I gotta bring that shit back (Blah)"], "meaning": "A max contract is salary agreement usually reserved for the best sports players and is the highest amount you can pay someone. For example, the maximum an NBA player could earn in the 2016-17 season was $30,963,450, and the only player to earn that much was Lebron James.\n\nDrake’s going to bring his earnings back to Toronto. He rapped a similar bar on his 2015 track “Charged Up,” where he compared himself to a basketball player and spoke of bringing his winnings home:\n\nI get a ring and I bring it home like I’m Corey Joe", "annotation_id": "11585500", "url": "https://genius.com/11585500", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Muscle relax (Ha, ha, ha) That and the spliff put me right on my back, I gotta unpack (Yah)", "vault_lines": ["Muscle relax (Ha, ha, ha)", "That and the spliff put me right on my back, I gotta unpack (Yah)"], "meaning": "A “spliff” is slang for a rolled joint of marijuana and tobacco. Drake jokes that the sedative effect of combining a muscle relaxant with cannabis is knocking him out.\n\nHe’s right about the effects of the drugs. Marijuana, specifically indica strains, have well-documented muscle relaxant properties. When combined with a prescription muscle relaxer, the drugs' synergistic effect causes a level of sedation much more pronounced than could be achieved by taking either substance alone.", "annotation_id": "11564397", "url": "https://genius.com/11564397", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Bury me now and I only get bigger That’s word to my nigga (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["That's word to my, word to my—", "Bury me now and I only get bigger", "That's word to my nigga (Woo)"], "meaning": "Historically, artists in all mediums have become more famous after their deaths—Drake claims he’d be no exception. In music, posthumous acclaim has propelled a number of artists to truly legendary status, like The Notorious B.I.G, and 2Pac.\n\nDrizzy previously mentioned this concept on his track “Legend” from his 2015 mixtape If You’re Reading This, It’s Too Late:\n\nIf I die, all I know is I’m a mothafuckin' legend", "annotation_id": "11551243", "url": "https://genius.com/11551243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Tat on my ribs like I do not know what permanent is (Yah)", "vault_lines": ["Tat on my ribs like I do not know what permanent is (Yah)"], "meaning": "Drake has a tattoo on each of his sides. On his right side is the number “416\"—a Toronto area code. On his left side is a portrait of singer Sade, with the caption, \"With love, Sade.” This tattoo was unveiled a few days after the release of More Life.", "annotation_id": "11550705", "url": "https://genius.com/11550705", "state": "accepted", "votes": 144, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "I’m so hot, yeah, I’m so right now (Right now)", "vault_lines": ["I'm so hot, yeah, I'm so right now (Right now)"], "meaning": "Drake is one of the hottest artists of the decade, and from 2009 to the present day, his music has consistently hit the Billboard charts and garnered award nominations and wins.  Not content to rest on his laurels, he emphasizes that he’s as hot in this moment as he has been in the past.  He shattered Spotify and Apple Music streaming records on the day More Life released; the album was streamed 61,302,082 times.", "annotation_id": "11550267", "url": "https://genius.com/11550267", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Stay at the top like I’m stuck, that’s just how I’m givin’ it up", "vault_lines": ["Stay at the top like I'm stuck, that's just how I'm givin' it up"], "meaning": "Drake’s 2016 album, Views, topped charts for 13 total weeks, and also broke the record for most streamed album of all time. Fast forward a single year and Drake has yet again destroyed the same streaming record on both Spotify and Apple Music with the release of More Life. He hasn’t left the charts since 2009’s So Far Gone.", "annotation_id": "11539432", "url": "https://genius.com/11539432", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "She wanna get married tonight But I can’t take a knee, ‘cause I’m wearin’ all white", "vault_lines": ["But I can't take a knee, 'cause I'm wearin' all white", "She wanna get married tonight"], "meaning": "This could be a reference to Gucci Mane proposing to his girlfriend, Keyshia Ka'oir, at an Atlanta Hawks game. Guwop proposed on Kiss Cam but didn’t get down on one knee, presumably because he was wearing all white.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=koRl3nT3H6o", "annotation_id": "11537498", "url": "https://genius.com/11537498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 119, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "I’m never washed, but I’m not new", "vault_lines": ["I'm never washed, but I'm not new"], "meaning": "Washed is a slang term derived from the term “washed up.” It’s used to describe someone old, tired and past their prime. Here Drake is saying that he isn’t past his peak as an artist, but he also isn’t a rookie and has much more to give to the game.\n\nThe term “washed” was actually popularized by hip-hop influencer and OVO affiliate It’s Bizkit. Biz has his own line of Washed brand clothing that he sells on his website.", "annotation_id": "11536061", "url": "https://genius.com/11536061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "I don’t take naps Me and the money are way too attached to go and do that", "vault_lines": ["I don't take naps", "Me and the money are way too attached to go and do that"], "meaning": "Drake has often talked about sacrificing sleep for work. On October 24, 2016, at his 30th birthday party, he mentioned his lack of sleep while giving a toast:\n\nYou know, I don’t get much sleep, and it’s not because I’m troubled, it’s because I’m blessed.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/_P1AL-zu6Y8?t=2m29s\n\nOn his 2016 song “9,” he rapped:\n\nAnd I can’t sleep these days unless I take one\n\nAnd on More Life’s closing track “Do Not Disturb,” he also broaches the subject:\n\nScary whenever I close my eyes at night", "annotation_id": "11535743", "url": "https://genius.com/11535743", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "I got a new mansion, ’cause I outgrew the condo", "vault_lines": ["I got a new mansion, 'cause I outgrew the condo"], "meaning": "Drake’s been constructing a 45,000 square foot mansion in Toronto since 2016.\n\nHe also mentions the mansion earlier in the More Life tracklist, on the second verse of “Blem”:\n\nI’m buildin' up a house where they raised me", "annotation_id": "11533741", "url": "https://genius.com/11533741", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "I switch flow like I switch time zone", "vault_lines": ["I switch flow like I switch time zone"], "meaning": "Throughout his career, Drake has been known for his stylistic versatility. He’s capable of dropping melodic, rap-free tracks like the intro cut from Views, “Keep the Family Close”, but he can also drop bars like he did on his AM-PM song series.\n\nShortly before More Life dropped, Drake was accused of copying XXXTENTACION’s flow from “Look At Me!” when he previewed “KMT” while touring in Europe.\n\nA big theme on More Life is globalizing the OVO brand, and this simile allusion to travelling is consistent with that.", "annotation_id": "11531962", "url": "https://genius.com/11531962", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Keep hearin’ clicks when I’m talkin’ on the iPhone Feds in the city hate to see us on a high note (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["Keep hearin' clicks when I'm talkin' on the iPhone", "Feds in the city hate to see us on a high note (Woo)"], "meaning": "Clicking sounds during phone conversations can occur when phones have been tapped. Telephone tapping or wiretapping is the monitoring of phone conversations executed by a third party.\n\nIn Drake’s case, he may legitimately believe his enemies want to access his otherwise private information. He’s been suspicious of wiretapping for a while; on his 2015 song “Preach,” he rapped:\n\nI am convinced that my calls are being recorded\n\nAdditionally, he might be paranoid about a police investigation into his illicit actions. A “high note” is a successful moment, but “high” also refers to the euphoria experienced when taking drugs, namely smoking marijuana.", "annotation_id": "11531887", "url": "https://genius.com/11531887", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Can’t get Nobu, but you can get Milestone (Baka)", "vault_lines": ["Can't get Nobu, but you can get Milestone (Baka)"], "meaning": "Milestones is a Canadian grill and bar restaurant, while Nobu is a luxurious and exclusive chain of Japanese restaurants founded by Chef Nobu Matsuhisa.", "annotation_id": "11531653", "url": "https://genius.com/11531653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Hermès link, ice-blue mink (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["Hermès link, ice-blue mink (Woo)"], "meaning": "Hermès is a French high fashion brand. Drake has been spotted wearing their clothes and owns a Hermes chain with an OVO owl pendant, designed by Ben Baller.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BSckhUlj-lb/?taken-by=champagnepapi&hl=en\n\n2014 images of Drake’s former flame Rihanna wearing a blue mink coat re-circulated on social media after this song was released, but it’s unlikely that he’s referring to her with this lyric.", "annotation_id": "11531395", "url": "https://genius.com/11531395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 258, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Yeah, October Firm in the cut (Yah)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, October Firm in the cut (Yah)"], "meaning": "“October Firm” is another name for October’s Very Own (OVO), Drake’s record label. October Firm consists of Drake and OVO co-founder Oliver El-Khatib.\n\nDuring Episode 38 of their OVO Sound Radio, Drizzy explained:\n\nWe’re a firm, like an old English firm.\n\n“In the cut” refers to a location hard to find, or far away from public areas.", "annotation_id": "11531370", "url": "https://genius.com/11531370", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Gotta do Maybach, she wanna fuck on the drive home Yeah, met her once and I got through (Yah)", "vault_lines": ["Gotta do Maybach, she wanna fuck on the drive home", "Yeah, met her once and I got through (Yah)"], "meaning": "In 2015, Drake bought a Mercedes-Maybach Pullman, a luxury limousine that retails for upwards of $566,000. The car, like all Maybach models, comes equipped with rear-seat curtains to ensure privacy.\n\n\n\nHe previously rapped about his new car on his “30 for 30 Freestyle” on his 2015 collaboration album with Future What a Time to Be Alive, which was released around the same time as his purchase:\n\nI just got me the Mercedes Pullman\nYou niggas never heard of it, you gotta hit up Google", "annotation_id": "11531172", "url": "https://genius.com/11531172", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "Virginia Black (Doo-doo) I could go make enough money off that and not even rap What’s that? Facts (Facts, nigga, yah)", "vault_lines": ["I could go make enough money off that and not even rap", "What's that? Facts (Facts, nigga, yah)"], "meaning": "Virginia Black whiskey is a collaboration between Brent Hocking and Drake. The decadent whiskey sold 1,779 bottles on its September 30 launch. In the same week, 4,650 bottles were sold, totaling $186,000 CAN.\n\nAlthough $200,000 is very small compared to Drizzy’s estimated net-worth of $60 million, he could make a lot of money if all of his efforts were put into selling whiskey.\n\nDrake has also mentioned Virginia Black on his track “Blem”:\n\nWho keeps bringing more? I’ve had too many\nThis Virginia done me off already\n\n\n\nAlso, this is probably a response to Jay Z’s “rap facts and fiction” lyric from DJ Khaled’s “I Got The Keys”:\n\nY'all know the difference right\nFrom rap facts and fiction, right?\nReal life I’m like HOV, real life I’m life goals\nIn real life they’re like me? In real life I’m like, “No.”\n\nDrake’s letting Jay know that he too, is a rap mogul.", "annotation_id": "11531148", "url": "https://genius.com/11531148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gyalchester", "line": "I know I said top five, but I’m top two And I’m not two and I got one Thought you had one, but it’s not one, nigga, nah", "vault_lines": ["I know I said top five, but I'm top two", "And I'm not two and I got one", "Thought you had one, but it's not one, nigga, nah"], "meaning": "Drake has repeatedly made reference to being among the “top five” greatest rappers. On his 2016 song “Grammys,” he spits:\n\nWhole city goin' crazy, top five no debating\nTop five, top five, top five and the whole city rate me\n\nBy the numbers, Drake’s certainly a leader—he claimed a spot on Forbes wealthiest artists of 2016 list with five songs on the Billboard Hot 100. Kanye West even called Drake the “most popular man in rap” in 2015.\n\n“Thought you had one, but it’s not one” is a double entendre, both referring to Drake being the number one rapper and meaning whoever his target is thought they had a number one hit, but it flopped.\n\nThis may be a specific shot at Kendrick Lamar, who replied to this line supposedly in ‘ELEMENT’ where he says:\n\nMr. One through Five, that’s the only logic\nFake my death, go to Cuba, that’s the only option", "annotation_id": "11531095", "url": "https://genius.com/11531095", "state": "accepted", "votes": 225, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
