{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "I feel alive, no thanks to you What is this I’m waking up to, waking up to? I don’t need you breaking my news, I see it too I don’t have as much patience as you", "vault_lines": ["I feel alive, no thanks to you", "What is this I'm waking up to, waking up to?", "I don't need you breaking my news, I see it too", "I don't have as much patience as you"], "meaning": "It’s commentary on his daily life post-beef. He’s managed to come out the other side alive and still relevant in the culture. He wakes up and checks his phone and sees the latest “anti-drake” headlines, and he get’s frustrated when his girl starts giving him updates about the stuff he’s already reading.", "annotation_id": "41692469", "url": "https://genius.com/41692469", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "You know that I’m drinkin’, smokin’, thinkin’ (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["You know that I'm drinkin', smokin', thinkin' (Yeah)"], "meaning": "This can be a callback to Drake’s very popular Nothing Was The Same track, Furthest Thing. On this track, Drake says:\n\n“You might feel like nothing was the same\nI still been drinking on the low,\nSmoking on the low\nFucking on the low\nMobbin' on the low”\n\n…\n\nThe furthest thing from perfect,\nLike everyone I know\"", "annotation_id": "40438462", "url": "https://genius.com/40438462", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "When do they look up to you? Guess that isn’t up to you (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["When do they look up to you? Guess that isn't up to you (Yeah)"], "meaning": "There are numerous examples on Iceman of Drake referencing things he’s said earlier in his career. Here he echos a line from Thank Me Now:\n\nHow long they choose to love you will never be your decision\n\nJust like with the original bar, in this verse Drake is using a significant other as a metaphor for his relationship with the general public and more specifically his fans.", "annotation_id": "40243534", "url": "https://genius.com/40243534", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "We still up, it’s bedtime on the Westside", "vault_lines": ["We still up, it's bedtime on the Westside"], "meaning": "This could be a line referring the timezone difference of Toronto (where Drake is from and part of the “Eastside” of North America) and the state of California, which is referred to the West side.\n\nDespite California being 3 hours behind the EST timezone, this bar may be talking about how it is bedtime for someone in LA while it is the midnight hours on the East coast. (11PM on the PST is 2AM in EST) This line is used to portray those on the west similar to kids who have a curfew, while those on the east run the night.\n\nAdditionally, the lyrics could imply that the Westside has been put to sleep.", "annotation_id": "40116938", "url": "https://genius.com/40116938", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "We still up, it’s bedtime on the Westside", "vault_lines": ["We still up, it's bedtime on the Westside"], "meaning": "This could be a line referring the timezone difference of Toronto (where Drake is from and part of the “Eastside” of North America) and the state of California, which is referred to the West side.\n\nDespite California being 3 hours behind the EST timezone, this bar may be talking about how it is bedtime for someone in LA while it is the midnight hours on the East coast. (11PM on the PST is 2AM in EST) This line is used to portray those on the west similar to kids who have a curfew, while those on the east run the night.\n\nAdditionally, the lyrics could imply that the Westside has been put to sleep.", "annotation_id": "40116664", "url": "https://genius.com/40116664", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "Eastside, Westside, Westside, Eastside (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Eastside, Westside, Westside, Eastside (Yeah)"], "meaning": "This to me sounds like Drake is mocking the whole coastal beef thing that Kendrick and his fans like to bring up, especially how he says Eastside for the second time 😂😂", "annotation_id": "40076553", "url": "https://genius.com/40076553", "state": "pending", "votes": -4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "If he trippin’ with the gang, then I just wanna see a boy struggle with the payments If he trippin’ with the gang, then I just might get that boy hit for entertainment If he trippin’ with the gang, then", "vault_lines": ["I just wanna see a boy beg on the pavement", "If he trippin' with the gang, then", "I just wanna see a boy struggle with the payments", "I just might get that boy hit for entertainment", "I just wanna see a boy working on the day shift", "I just wanna see that boy struggle with the phone bill"], "meaning": "The other notes on the refrains are wildly incorrect. This is clearly Drake leaning into a colder, more personal form of revenge than the usual “get back” narratives in rap. Instead of focusing solely on violence or flashy success, Drake is praying on this persons downfall and watching them lose stability and dignity in everyday life. References to phone bills, light bills, double shifts, and “begging on the pavement” paint a picture of someone being reduced from confidence to survival mode. The repeated phrase “If he trippin’ with the gang, then” acts like both a warning and a justification, reinforcing loyalty and consequences. What really makes the verse hit is how grounded the imagery is. Instead of expressing rage as killing/taking women etc, Drake describes ordinary struggles most people understand.", "annotation_id": "39891892", "url": "https://genius.com/39891892", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "I just wanna see that boy struggle with the phone bill And the light bill (That’s how I feel)", "vault_lines": ["I just wanna see a boy beg on the pavement", "I just wanna see a boy struggle with the payments", "And the night shift (That's how I feel)", "I just wanna see that boy struggle with the phone bill", "And the light bill (That's how I feel)"], "meaning": "The other notes on the refrains are wildly incorrect. This is clearly Drake leaning into a colder, more personal form of revenge than the usual “get back” narratives in rap. Instead of focusing solely on violence or flashy success, Drake is praying on this persons downfall and watching them lose stability and dignity in everyday life. References to phone bills, light bills, double shifts, and “begging on the pavement” paint a picture of someone being reduced from confidence to survival mode. The repeated phrase “If he trippin’ with the gang, then” acts like both a warning and a justification, reinforcing loyalty and consequences. What really makes the verse hit is how grounded the imagery is. Instead of expressing rage as killing/taking women etc, Drake describes ordinary struggles most people understand.", "annotation_id": "39891878", "url": "https://genius.com/39891878", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "I just wanna see a boy beg on the pavement", "vault_lines": ["I just wanna see a boy beg on the pavement", "I just wanna see a boy struggle with the payments", "I just wanna see a boy working on the day shift", "I just wanna see that boy struggle with the phone bill"], "meaning": "Drake evokes the image of a homeless beggar, implying he wants anyone who disses him to go back to being broke.", "annotation_id": "39872223", "url": "https://genius.com/39872223", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "Please stop askin’ me, “When are we linkin’?” It’s Iceman season", "vault_lines": ["Please stop askin' me, \"When are we linkin'?\" It's Iceman season"], "meaning": "May 15th Canadian rapper Drake (Aubrey Graham) released his 9th studio album under OVO creating ICEMAN season being May 2026.\n\nIceman season\n\nis probably in reference to Drake coming back from 2024 rap feud (Its his comeback).", "annotation_id": "39836811", "url": "https://genius.com/39836811", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Worry", "line": "I just wanna see a boy working on the day shift And the night shift (That’s how I feel)", "vault_lines": ["I just wanna see a boy beg on the pavement", "I just wanna see a boy struggle with the payments", "I just wanna see a boy working on the day shift", "And the night shift (That's how I feel)", "And the light bill (That's how I feel)"], "meaning": "Drake wants to see his enemies go broke and work all day just to pay the bills. It’s probably also a flipped callback to “6 Man” where he brags about working at night:\n\nYeah, work the night shift\nYoung, but I’m makin' millions to work the night shift", "annotation_id": "39835846", "url": "https://genius.com/39835846", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
