{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "And I apologize", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I am not sure if this is Drake saying “I’m still a good man, and I apologize.” or “And I apologize” is her response to Drake saying, “I’m still a good man.”\n\nI lean towards the latter.", "annotation_id": "33568020", "url": "https://genius.com/33568020", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "Look at my life though, show me some courtesy Promise you heard of me, promise you heard of me I’m still a good man", "vault_lines": ["I still, I'm still a really good man", "Look at my life though, show me some courtesy", "Promise you heard of me, promise you heard of me", "I'm still a good man", "I'm still capable of being a really good man to you"], "meaning": "This woman is broken and realizes the context of the reality she is living with Drake. She is now pleading with Drake and using Drake’s earlier tactic of showing pain and that she is human. Sharing that Drake is hurting her emotionally, to which Drake responds with, “I’m still a good man.”", "annotation_id": "33567988", "url": "https://genius.com/33567988", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "You want some certainty You want eternity You want paternity, maternity and nursery", "vault_lines": ["You want some certainty", "You want paternity, maternity and nursery"], "meaning": "This is an interesting bit in the verse.\n\nThe woman realizes she does not have the upper hand in this discussion, thus, she reverts to a simple personal retort of “You want some certainty.” It is known that “masculine” behavior is associated with order which is associated with certainty rather than chaos or uncertainty.\n\nDrake then responds, balancing out the equation by saying “You want eternity.”\n\nHer retort is her trying to show Drake that the world is chaotic and is impossible to control or play god. Drake responds to this by telling her that we are humans and we, like all other things, die out.\n\nThis leads into Drake empathizing with both the man and the woman’s perspective whilst also demonstrating we want the same things and are both human, but we have two different perspectives or interpretations. Men want to be fathers (paternity), women want to be mothers (maternity), we both want to be caregivers (nursery).", "annotation_id": "33567895", "url": "https://genius.com/33567895", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "Girl, aren’t you proud of me? If not, then what does that mеan? I don’t think you’re mad at me I think it’s other things Things like insecurity, girls that got surgery", "vault_lines": [", girl, arеn't you proud of me?", "If not, then what does that mеan?", "I don't think you'rе mad at me", "I think it's other things", "Things like insecurity, girls that got surgery", "Promise you heard of me, promise you heard of me"], "meaning": "He has done so much for himself and others, he feels she doesn’t show gratitude to what he brought to her and the world through his career. If not, he questions if maybe SHE is using him. Not just for pleasure, but for the extrinsic aspects and status that come with being with Drake.\n\nSeeing that Drake is now projecting how she made him feel earlier from her previous statement in the verse, he comes to the realization that she’s not actually mad at him for his behavior. That it is actually a complex web rooted in comparison and insecurity with the other woman who previously, currently, or who could potentially be in his life.\n\nHe concludes this by reminding her that their relationship doesn’t have a healthy or any foundation what so ever as he explains: “We were so drunk when we met, we got no anniversary.” He then concludes this rant stating, implicitly, that she is like a prostitute as he “bought” her in an “emergency” signifying that he was desperate in that moment, and needed her quickly. Like how a Porsche is a fast expensive (to most) car.", "annotation_id": "33567806", "url": "https://genius.com/33567806", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "Aren’t you", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a cue for Drake to step back in as he begins to unearth some more deeper, darker emotions.\n\nThis is signified by the change of tone in his voice.", "annotation_id": "33567798", "url": "https://genius.com/33567798", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "Touch and go with me Had your mama reach out to me Like you ain’t come out of her and then come straight to me Why do you play with me? Please, don’t be vague with me", "vault_lines": ["Touch and go with me", "Had your mama reach out to me", "Like you ain't come out of her and then come straight to me", "Why do you play with me?", "Please, don't be vague with me", "Promise you heard of me, promise you heard of me"], "meaning": "The woman is complaining about Drake using her for pleasure and avoiding his feelings. She pleads with him to be direct with her rather than dancing around the subject, leaving her to question who he is and what he is doing when she is not around. Seeing as though she is in touch with Sandra, it is clear Drake values this woman, plus being around his mother, she is able to really understand the side of Drake that he hides from her.\n\nAnother indicator that we are now hearing from the woman’s perspective is the previous lines “Following me, unfollowing me.  Still got no sense of direction, I see.” Drake is known to follow and unfollow various celebrities and accounts on social media. Plus, his music doesn’t have one specific style or “direction.”", "annotation_id": "33567723", "url": "https://genius.com/33567723", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "And I apologize", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the cue for the woman to step in as all she heard and processed was “And I apologize.”", "annotation_id": "33567713", "url": "https://genius.com/33567713", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "Still feel a lot of things I still feel pain I still see pain I still sleep pain, eat pain Heart on my sleeve, they should call me T-Pain I still, I’m still a really good man I’m still", "vault_lines": ["I still feel pain", "I still see pain", "I still sleep pain, eat pain", "Heart on my sleeve, they should call me T-Pain", "I still, I'm still a really good man", "I'm still a good man", "I'm still capable of being a really good man to you", "Still feel a lot of things"], "meaning": "Trying to show he is human, he tries to show that he has feelings and isn’t as cold hearted as his behavior may lead on. He is trying to emphasize to this woman that underneath his mistakes, he is still a good man. The “I still” rather than “I’m still” is a demonstration of Drake beginning a rebirthing process with this woman. “I still” sounds like something a crying baby would say, not to label Drake a “cry baby.” For this specific instance, the “I still,” into “I’m still a really good man” (mixed with influx of his vocals on the “man”) indicates someone crying who is trying to catch their breath and recuperate. Concluding with “I’m still,” Drake realizes he has gotten his message across and stops his train of thought. This allows the woman to respond and express her emotions.", "annotation_id": "33567653", "url": "https://genius.com/33567653", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "[Chorus] I’m still a good man I’m still a good man I’m still capable of being a really good man to you I swear it’s true", "vault_lines": ["I still, I'm still a really good man", "I'm still a good man", "I'm still capable of being a really good man to you", "I swear it's true"], "meaning": "Continuing on from his plea in his last line in verse 1. The chorus is Drake trying to convince this woman that he can change. Not only himself, but himself for her.", "annotation_id": "33567640", "url": "https://genius.com/33567640", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "Out the back door, can’t get caught on camera", "vault_lines": ["Out the back door, can't get caught on camera"], "meaning": "Assuming the “girl” is curious, “Out the back door, can’t get caught on camera” may be Drake’s confession to her question about HOW he snuck out without getting caught. Seeing that he has a guilty conscience and his feelings for this woman are strong, he decides she deserves to know the truth. This then triggers the emotions and feelings that he truly has for this woman, as he follows up with “I can change, I put that shit on Sandra.” A show of emotion to help sway the situation in his favor.", "annotation_id": "33567606", "url": "https://genius.com/33567606", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "I’m still fucked up from me living in Atlanta", "vault_lines": ["I'm still fucked up from me living in Atlanta"], "meaning": "I interpret this song as Drake’s version of ‘We Cry Together’ by Kendrick Lamar. Rather than Kendrick’s approach of utilizing a man and a woman’s voice individually, Drake speaks through both lenses. This is also in connection with Drake’s ability to be a rapper and a singer. The other difference is in the emotion of the song. Kendrick and Taylour Paige are more direct and personal with their retorts. Where as Drake takes a holistic approach, rather than an individualistic approach. In essence, the feeling from this discussion is the one in which both parties share together in this moment, collectively.\n\nTo begin, Drake starts out through the man or the masculine perspective (can also be seen symbolically as the destructive perspective). He tries gaining some sympathy from their partner by bringing up what appears to be a life changing experience in Atlanta.", "annotation_id": "33567520", "url": "https://genius.com/33567520", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "Following me, unfollowing me Still got no sense of direction, I see", "vault_lines": ["I still see pain", "Following me, unfollowing me", "Still got no sense of direction, I see"], "meaning": ".", "annotation_id": "32537913", "url": "https://genius.com/32537913", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "We were so drunk when we met, we got no anniversary", "vault_lines": ["We were so drunk when we met, we got no anniversary"], "meaning": "When Drake and his girl met, they were both so drunk, that they don’t remember when it even happened, hence why they have no anniversary.", "annotation_id": "32211448", "url": "https://genius.com/32211448", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "I can change, I put that shit on Sandra", "vault_lines": ["I can change, I put that shit on Sandra"], "meaning": "Drake loves this girl so much, that he swears on Sandra “Sandi” Graham, his mother, that he can change.\n\nDrake has expressed his infinite love for his mother numerous times before, even dedicating her a song on his 2015 mixtape, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, “You & The 6”\n\n\nDrake and his mother, Sandi, in 2011", "annotation_id": "32211342", "url": "https://genius.com/32211342", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "Three hoes ain’t enough, girl, I ain’t Santa", "vault_lines": ["Three hoes ain't enough, girl, I ain't Santa"], "meaning": "“Three hoes” is referencing the iconic line used by Santa Claus; “Ho, ho, ho”. Drake is saying that, unlike Santa, he isn’t satisfied with just three “hoes”, or women.", "annotation_id": "32211325", "url": "https://genius.com/32211325", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I’m The Problem (Still A Good Man)", "line": "Porsche 911 I bought your ass, like an emergency", "vault_lines": ["Porsche 911 I bought your ass, like an emergency"], "meaning": "Porsche 911  is a two-door, high-performance, luxury sports car manufactured by German automobile manufacturer Porsche—with its 2024 model starting at $116,050.\n\n\n\n Pictured: 2023 Porsche 911  \n\n\nThrough the usage of puns, Drake additionally alludes to the world-renowned emergency number 9-1-1 (or “911”), which is used for law enforcement, emergency services, and fire departments.\n\nDrake describes his lavish annual income of $70,000,000 as a desperate need of spending, hence his “emergency” of buying costly luxury sports cars.", "annotation_id": "29901346", "url": "https://genius.com/29901346", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
