{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Wow, all praise to the most high up...", "vault_lines": ["Wow, all praise to the most high up..."], "meaning": "As seen in earlier lines, Drake is clearly thankful to God for his success, whom he refers to as “the most high.”\n\nAlternatively, Drake could be referring to himself as the 6 God, and being “the most high up” in the rap game; also literally, on the Views album cover he sits on the top of the CN Tower, which is the tallest freestanding building in the Western Hemisphere (and was the tallest in the world from 1976 to 2010)", "annotation_id": "9089523", "url": "https://genius.com/9089523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Oh, and I’m back, dog Oh, you just went and turned your back, dog I thought that we were family You showed me that we can’t be", "vault_lines": ["Oh, and I'm back, dog", "Oh, you just went and turned your back, dog", "I thought that we were family", "You showed me that we can't be"], "meaning": "Just as Kanye did on “Real Friends,” Drake underlines the importance of family and loyalty. This could be a reference to multiple artists who have fallen out with Drake recently.\n\nMo-G has called Drake’s manager a culture vulture, turning on the OVO camp and claiming Drake has stolen his style.\n\nIt’s possible he’s referring to Tory Lanez, although Lanez publicly said in February 2016 that he would never diss Drake and that “he’s a fan.”\n\nAll of these artists were around Drake’s team at one point and have fallen out. Meek Mill can be counted in that group, with the two collaborating on “R.I.C.O.” before their epic falling out.", "annotation_id": "9071332", "url": "https://genius.com/9071332", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Me and all my niggas doin’ well, doin’ well, dog You not from the city, I could tell, I could tell, dog Did it, did it, did it by myself, by myself, dog Blew up and I’m in the city still, I’m still here, dog", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, me and all my", "Oh, and I'm back, dog", "Blew up and I'm in the city still, I'm still—", "Me and all my niggas doin' well, doin' well, dog", "You not from the city, I could tell, I could tell, dog", "Did it, did it, did it by myself, by myself, dog", "Blew up and I'm in the city still, I'm still here, dog"], "meaning": "This is a common message from Drake, exemplified by his hit track “Started From The Bottom”:\n\nStarted from the bottom, now we’re here\nStarted from the bottom, now my whole team fucking here\n\nHis “team” might be described as his OVO Sound crew, incorporating ​dvsn, Noah Shebib and PARTYNEXTDOOR. All of them incredibly successful in their own right.\n\nMost of those Drake surrounds himself with are from Canada. Everyone signed to his OVO Sound label (except Mike Zombie) is from his home country. His story is one of individual brilliance, self-releasing his first three mixtapes and building his own buzz. BY the time So Far Gone came out in 2009, he had attracted the eye of Lil Wayne, and the rest is history.\n\nDespite all the fame and fortune, he’s more committed to his home town than ever, using the CN Tower in Toronto to cover arguably the biggest record of his career.", "annotation_id": "9071305", "url": "https://genius.com/9071305", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "And I see your girl like all the time, all the time though I can’t tell you if she’s yours or mine but I do know", "vault_lines": ["And I see your girl like all the time, all the time though", "I can't tell you if she's yours or mine but I do know"], "meaning": "Is this girl paying Drake for his time? It may be a reversal of roles, or part of the epiphany he had during “Hype”:\n\nSwear I just had an epiphany\nIt cost me 50 at Tiffany’s\nShout out to Tiffany, Stephanie\nThey used to always come check for me\n\nInstead of buying women expensive goods, he’s asking what they can do for him before he commits, returning his the fancy girls from his first record.\n\nDrake has already gone on wax claiming “I Can Take Your Girl,” with the lyrics:\n\nI’m nothing like your man, but I can’t love you neither\n\nIt seems like this girl doesn’t belong to Drake or her real man.", "annotation_id": "9071289", "url": "https://genius.com/9071289", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "I make people pay me for my time, yeah, I need that", "vault_lines": ["I make people pay me for my time, yeah, I need that", "Yeah, me and all my"], "meaning": "During “You Love Me, You Hate Me” Lil Wayne expressed this phenomenon of being paid for your time:\n\n\nThe club; that’s my job\nThe mall; that’s my job\nYour neighborhood; that’s my job\nThis studio; this is my life\n\nYou get money and you get crazy\nI get crazy and I get money\n\n\nIn 2014 Complex claimed a Drake appearance fee could set you back anywhere between $350,000 and $600,000. After his world beating 2015, it’s safe to say this fee has gone up even further.", "annotation_id": "9071279", "url": "https://genius.com/9071279", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "I don’t need no pill to speak my mind, I don’t need that", "vault_lines": ["I don't need no pill to speak my mind, I don't need that"], "meaning": "Just as he explained on “Hype,” he’s not averse to being open about his mindset. The current drug culture in hip hop has focused much more on prescription medications. It’s a stark contrast to the message collaborator Future has been preaching on his recent releases, relying heavily on medications to create the atmosphere he desires.\n\nAlthough Drake is regularly spotted in clubs and at fancy parties, he doesn’t mention his own consumption often, preferring to allow his women to divulge secrets under the influence.\n\nBut you get real on a pill and I like it", "annotation_id": "9071262", "url": "https://genius.com/9071262", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "All you niggas fightin’ over crumbs, where the bread at?", "vault_lines": ["All you niggas fightin' over crumbs, where the bread at?"], "meaning": "Drake’s net worth sits at a staggering $78 million, comfortably in the top 10 earners in hip hop.\n\nWhile other rappers fight for the crumbs, Drake’s busy baking $19 million dollar Apple deals.", "annotation_id": "9071247", "url": "https://genius.com/9071247", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Nothing mutual about my funds, ’bout my funds though", "vault_lines": ["Nothing mutual about my funds, 'bout my funds though"], "meaning": "As the founder of his own label, and the sole recipient of his mammoth Apple deal, Drake doesn’t share much of his earnings with anyone. However, he is still signed to Young Money/Cash Money records. Lil Wayne and Birdman both collect cheques.\n\nSome may question this line, given his business relationship with Birdman, which has been speculated to include earnings from his major projects. During the Lil Wayne/Birdman lawsuit from early 2015, something called the “2009 Drake Letter Agreement” was uncovered, stating that Cash Money (Birdman’s label) had to release all of Drake’s monthly earnings to Young Money (Lil Wayne’s CM imprint). It was claimed this never happened, but the fact that it needs to happen points to Drake’s earnings being overseen by Cash Money.", "annotation_id": "9071218", "url": "https://genius.com/9071218", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "I can’t fuck with you no more ‘cause you be actin’ extra Do your favorite rapper like my son, like my son though", "vault_lines": ["I can't fuck with you no more 'cause you be actin' extra", "Do your favorite rapper like my son, like my son though"], "meaning": "“Actin' extra” also means this person is hanging around, making a nuisance of themselves when Drake is in crew mode. His message on “No New Friends” must be heeded.\n\nAs if to stamp his authority on the situation, he compares himself to this person’s favourite rapper, exclaiming that he’s so well respected as a rapper he’s able to “boss up” on anyone’s favourite rapper and treat them like his son. Dropping Kanye and Jay Z from “Pop Style” is one such example.\n\n\n\nAdding to the wordplay, an “extra” is someone paid to play a tiny role in a TV show or movie. Drake played a major role in Degrassi, he was certainly no extra.", "annotation_id": "9071203", "url": "https://genius.com/9071203", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Girls all in my bed and they don’t trip off first impressions Girls all in your bed and they just ask a hundred questions", "vault_lines": ["Girls all in your bed and they just ask a hundred questions", "Yeah, me and all my", "Girls all in my bed and they don't trip off first impressions"], "meaning": "Regardless of the state Drake is in when he meets these girls (drunk or high), they’re unperturbed. He might get so drunk he forgets their name, but they’re not concerned, convinced they know him from his sensitive songs.\n\nOn the contrary, his competition, or regular men, are accosted with questions, just as Lil Wayne was on “HYFR”:\n\nDo you love this shit? Are you high right now?\nDo you ever get nervous?\nAre you single? I heard you fucked your girl, is it true?\nYou getting money?\nYou think them n—s you with is with you?", "annotation_id": "9071166", "url": "https://genius.com/9071166", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Lion of the Judah", "vault_lines": ["Lion of the Judah"], "meaning": "Lion of Judah is a symbol of the Jewish tribe. Drake continues to pay homage to the religion he was raised under. It’s also a prominent symbol in Rasta culture, along with the 6 pointed star of David which is mentioned in this line. Drake has incorporated Jamaican influence into his music for some time, and aligned himself with popular Rasta musicians.", "annotation_id": "9071113", "url": "https://genius.com/9071113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "All my niggas ain’t off rockin’ Gucci, one do it, then we all gotta do it", "vault_lines": ["All my niggas ain't off rockin' Gucci, one do it, then we all gotta do it"], "meaning": "In this line Drake might be referring to UK grime legend Skepta’s track “That’s Not Me”:\n\nYeah, I used to wear Gucci\nI put it all in the bin cause that’s not me\nTrue, I used to look like you\nBut dressing like a mess? Nah, that’s not me\n\nAlthough Drake was recently seen wearing a Gucci scarf and jacket.\n\n\n\nThis line might be stating that his “niggas” in general, do not follow trends, hence the link to Skepta, who has repeatedly distanced himself from all the big brands he used to wear.\n\nIf Drake chooses not to wear Gucci, his OVO crew must follow suit, as prophesied on “Headlines”:\n\nCause one of us goes in, and we all go through it", "annotation_id": "9070563", "url": "https://genius.com/9070563", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Got the key, now the door’s open, and we all goin’ through it Whole city at your head for the boy, I ain’t even gotta call no one for it", "vault_lines": ["Got the key, now the door's open, and we all goin' through it", "Whole city at your head for the boy, I ain't even gotta call no one for it"], "meaning": "In February 2016, Drake was awarded the key to the city of Toronto by the mayor, just before the celebrity all-star game.\n\nVia his OVO Sound record label he’s now reached back and helped those around him, most notably Noah “40” Shebib, who started working with Drake in 2005. Every artist bar one that is signed to the label is Canadian.\n\nDuring the height of the Meek Mill beef, Toronto was booing the Philly rapper on behalf of Drake. Even Canadian politicians were joing Drake’s side against Meek.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/norm/status/623828839131648000?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw", "annotation_id": "9070460", "url": "https://genius.com/9070460", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Hitting like that 30 on my jersey, man, I’m gifted", "vault_lines": ["Hitting like that 30 on my jersey, man, I'm gifted"], "meaning": "Drake compares his talent to Golden State Warriors point guard Stephen Curry, whose jersey number is 30. Although, Drake needs to work on hitting three’s like Steph does:\n\n\n\nThis isn’t the first time Drake has brought up Curry. On “0 to 100 / The Catch Up” he raps,\n\nThis shit a mothafuckin' lay up\nI been Steph Curry with the shot\nBeen cookin' with the sauce, Chef Curry with the pot, boy", "annotation_id": "9070324", "url": "https://genius.com/9070324", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Six-point star,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A 6-point star is the Star of David, the symbol of Judaism. Drake was raised Jewish.\n\nThe Hexagram can also be used in Occult rituals, just as Drake’s “6” talk could be mistaken for devil worship. He’s clarified in the lines before though he isn’t practising anything impure.", "annotation_id": "9070262", "url": "https://genius.com/9070262", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "I gotta talk to God even though He isn’t near me Based on what I got, it’s hard to think that He don’t hear me", "vault_lines": ["I gotta talk to God even though He isn't near me", "Based on what I got, it's hard to think that He don't hear me"], "meaning": "Although Drake hasn’t achieved the levels of self-aggrandising Kanye has, he does refer to himself as the “6 God”, and these lyrics do mimic Kanye’s from “Clique”:\n\nBut I been talking to God for so long\nThat if you look at my life I guess he’s talking back\n\nDrake’s vast success in hip hop is proof to him he’s been blessed by the Lord, despite their apparent distance.\n\nI cannot see Heaven being much better than this, yeah\nBlessings on blessings for me and my niggas from the 6\nLook at what we did, yeah", "annotation_id": "9070219", "url": "https://genius.com/9070219", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Doin’ well, dog Yeah, me and all my Yeah, me—yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, me and all my", "Me and all my niggas doin' well, doin' well, dog"], "meaning": "Views is an album ostensibly about Drake’s home city, and references of a close-knit circle of associates litter the album. During his Zane Lowe interview (39 minute mark) he said:\n\nNow it’s kind of like if you don’t claim Toronto, what are you on? You’re a waste.\n\nPrior to this, Drake claimed that Toronto was now the “in” thing in hip-hop, on par with London and the Grime scene he has aligned himself with.\n\nAs the leader of this movement, he can spot fakes. His tight-knit crew continue to see success, and the establishment of his OVO Sound record label has distributed the wealth and success among his closest associates.", "annotation_id": "9070200", "url": "https://genius.com/9070200", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "How did I finesse all of this shit from Jane and Weston?", "vault_lines": ["How did I finesse all of this shit from Jane and Weston?"], "meaning": "Jane St and Weston Road (referred to on “Weston Road Flows”) is an intersection in the northwest part of Toronto, generally a blacker and less wealthy area of the city. Drake grew up in the area until his mom moved them to the affluent neighbourhood of Forest Hill.\n\n“Finesse” means to convince someone to part with their belongings, in comparison to “hitting a lick,” which uses violence or stealth to directly rob a person. The success Drake has achieved, coming from an area considered quite dangerous in Toronto, boggles his mind.", "annotation_id": "9070145", "url": "https://genius.com/9070145", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "line": "Whole lot of sixes, but I’m still like Hallelujah, Hallelujah, Hallelujah", "vault_lines": ["Whole lot of sixes, but I'm still like"], "meaning": "The number 6 is often associated with evil and the Devil, however, Drake has made the number synonymous with the city of Toronto. Now that he’s rich and successful, the 6 no longer holds a sinister meaning for him. For this miraculous turn-around, Drake praises God by shouting “Hallelujah.”\n\nHallelujah is said also during the Jewish new year to welcome in new blessings. It is connected to the next line “6 point star, lion of the Judah” which speaks to Rastafarian influences throughout his music. Rastafarians and Jews are very much linked in that they have overlapping celebrations and share all Jewish blessings and songs.", "annotation_id": "9070130", "url": "https://genius.com/9070130", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Still Here", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 19, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-still-here-lyrics", "genius_title": "Still Here"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Lies", "line": "They like, ”Please, please tell us your reality, J! We can’t fathom how it feel to be forever confined.”", "vault_lines": ["They like, \"Please, please tell us your reality, J!", "We can't fathom how it feel to be forever confined.\"", "And now they like, \"Please, please tell us your reality, J!"], "meaning": "Jimmy was permanently paralyzed from the waist down due to the school shooting that occurs in the two-part season finale of Season 3. He had to live his entire life in the confinement of a wheelchair.\n\n\n\nBecause of him being put into such a predicament and being popular amongst the school, many people often asked him how it felt to be stuck in a wheelchair.", "annotation_id": "12928491", "url": "https://genius.com/12928491", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Lies", "line": "Look, me and the mic, we coexist My flow is a glass box with no exits And you can observe while you trapped in it", "vault_lines": ["Look, me and the mic, we coexist", "My flow is a glass box with no exits", "And you can observe while you trapped in it"], "meaning": "Drizzy and the the microphone are connected in a perfected sense; they coexist. He proves this by stating that his flow when rapping is as solid as a glass box with no exits.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn relation to Degrassi:  \n\nThis song premiers in Episode 4 of Season 7: “It’s Tricky.” During the time, former high school basketball star Jimmy Brooks was still suffering from the slight depression of being disabled due to the school shooting incident that occurred in season 3 (which, in a sense, is what the entire song is about.) He was permanently paralyzed from the waist down, preventing him from doing a lot of the hobbies he loved.\n\nA glass box is obviously transparent, so you can observe what’s happening while trapped inside it, however you can’t escape it. Jimmy compares this to how it feels to be stuck in a wheelchair—he feels isolated, watching all his friends do the things he wish he could.\n\n\n\nHis flow is so poignant that he can get the listeners to relate to or “observe” the situation from his point of view.", "annotation_id": "12928051", "url": "https://genius.com/12928051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Lies", "line": "Took my lady’s track and I sprinkled some rap in it", "vault_lines": ["Took my lady's track and I sprinkled some rap in it"], "meaning": "Drake happens to have worked with a lot of the women he’d eventually date, two of the most notable being Nicki Minaj and Rihanna. One of Drake’s 2011 hit songs “Make Me Proud” featured Nicki while Rihanna sang the hook for the title song of his 2011 album Take Care. Romantic or not, he would continue to make songs with both artists.\n\n\n\n\nIn relation to Degrassi:  \n\nIn the show, Jimmy has an on-and-off relationship with Ashley Kerwin.\n\nhttps://vignette2.wikia.nocookie.net/degrassi/images/5/5e/Season_six_06.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20130422002603\n\nSpoiler Alert: In the episode, Ashley, with the help of Jimmy, decides to start up her music career again. She writes the original version of “Tell Me Lies” and performs it at a talent contest where she’s initially boo’d off stage until Jimmy comes in, raps over it, and gets a standing ovation from the crowd (which would later start conflict between the two.)", "annotation_id": "636788", "url": "https://genius.com/636788", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Lies", "line": "And every time I say that I can do it myself In the same breath, I wonder where my accomplice at", "vault_lines": ["And every time I say that I can do it myself", "In the same breath, I wonder where my accomplice at"], "meaning": "Drake’s confident in his ability to make it on his own, but he’s always wondering where his true friends and supporters are in the back of his mind. In the appropriately titled “Fear”, he raps\n\nPeople think I’ve changed just cause my appeal has grown\nAnd now security follow me everywhere\nSo I never actually am alone, I just always feel alone\n\nHe then follows it up with the contradiction of saying “Cause the fabrication (or in simpler words, lies) would help settle that.” Those who show enough ‘fake love’ to him may not have good intentions, but he admits that him believing them would ease his paranoia of having no one to turn to.\n\n\n\n\nIn relation to Degrassi:  \n\nThis could be in relation to a number of incidents, as Jimmy Brooks is betrayed and left behind multiple times around the events of this episode. The most notable of which would be Spinner Mason when he inadvertently sets Jimmy up to get shot by Rick Murray. In Episode 16 of Season 4, Spinner, guilt-ridden, confesses this to Jimmy which prompts him to end their friendship.\n\nIt could also be in reference to Ashley Kerwin who has an on-and-off relationship with Jimmy.\n\nUltimately though, Jimmy’s just be saying he doesn’t know who he can turn to anymore for genuine assistance.", "annotation_id": "636785", "url": "https://genius.com/636785", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Lies", "line": "It’s like", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is once again in desire of not wanting to hear the truth and preferring to be told false statements, as they help comfort him and mitigate the stress in his mind. He has a similar mentality in “Under Ground Kings” when he raps\n\nTell me lies, make it sound good, make it sound good\n\n\n\n\nIn relation to Degrassi:  \n\nJimmy is ultimately in a predicament of confusion. He doesn’t know where to go or who he can turn to—which evolved into a bigger issue in Episode 6 of Season 5: “I Still Haven’t Found What I’m Looking For.” Therefore, he’d rather just be told lies and “fabrications” because he can’t bring himself to face the truth of him being permanently disabled.", "annotation_id": "636770", "url": "https://genius.com/636770", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Lies", "line": "My mother always told me what to do with my best foot So to this day, you know that it is never behind", "vault_lines": ["My mother always told me what to do with my best foot", "So to this day, you know that it is never behind"], "meaning": "To “put one’s best foot forward” is an idiom that means to try as hard as you can; to put forth your best effort at something. Drake credits his mother for teaching him such a profound lesson.\n\n\n\n\nIn relation to Degrassi:  \n\nAfter finding out he was disabled, Jimmy’s parents only motivated him despite his situation—which would become full on issue in Episode 6 of Season 5: “I Still Haven’t Found What I’m Looking For” where Jimmy is confused between what he wants to do for the rest of his high school career.\n\nNevertheless though, Jimmy put his full effort into making the most out of his situation.", "annotation_id": "636761", "url": "https://genius.com/636761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Lies", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 6, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-tell-me-lies-lyrics", "genius_title": "Tell Me Lies"}
