{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Matter to Me", "line": "Ayy, wild things you’re doin’ at night Trips to wherever feels right Doin’ it all just to feel things Drinking’s enough of a vice Drugs just aren’t suiting you right You were just fine as a real thing, thing", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, wild things you're doin' at night", "Trips to wherever feels right", "Doin' it all just to feel things", "Drinking's enough of a vice", "Drugs just aren't suiting you right", "You were just fine as a real thing, thing"], "meaning": "On The Weeknd’s August 2011 song, “The Zone,” Drake also expressed his concern for his girl doing drugs:\n\nI thought taking drugs just ain’t you, be you\nYeah girl, just be you", "annotation_id": "18761776", "url": "https://genius.com/18761776", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Matter to Me", "line": "All of a sudden you say you don’t want me no more All of a sudden you say that I closed the door", "vault_lines": ["All of a sudden you say you don't want me no more", "All of a sudden you say that I closed the door"], "meaning": "In 1983, Paul Anka and Michael Jackson linked up for a recording session for an ultimately shelved collaboration. Jackson’s vocals from the session remained unused until shortly after his death, when their song “This is It” appeared as the title track on Jackson’s first posthumous album. The pop star’s second posthumous album Xscape also featured a recording from this session: “Love Never Felt So Good.”\n\nPaul Anka also has writing credits on “Don’t Matter to Me,” and he confirmed that Jackson’s “Don’t Matter to Me” vocals came from this same session.", "annotation_id": "14878508", "url": "https://genius.com/14878508", "state": "accepted", "votes": 564, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Don’t Matter to Me", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 2, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-michael-jackson-dont-matter-to-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "Don’t Matter to Me"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "[Verse 1]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The purpose of this verse is to show that things can change quite drastically (kids not reading at school and wanting to be something they’re not) and how some things never change (cops doing nothing about crime). This is Drake using those examples as a comparison to his own life; will the end of his career spell a change in him or will he be the same old Drizzy. This obviously scares him as he leads into the line in the chorus, ‘I don’t wanna think about that right now’ because, as the first verse shows, he has already.", "annotation_id": "4498377", "url": "https://genius.com/4498377", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "Can’t think straight in the heat of the moment, heat of the moment", "vault_lines": ["Heat of the moment, girl, heat of the moment", "Can't think straight in the heat of the moment, heat of the moment"], "meaning": "It was so easy seeing things the first time round when his girl asked him the same questions since he’s caught feelings for her. Because of this, mostly everything she says will sound sweet to him.\n\n\n\nWithout his girl asking the questions, the pressure has finally hit him in reality and recognizes how much of a struggle it is to deal with everything that is going on around him.", "annotation_id": "4246983", "url": "https://genius.com/4246983", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "And all the cops are still hangin’ out at the doughnut shops Talkin’ ‘bout how the weather’s changin’ The ice is meltin’ as if the world is endin’", "vault_lines": ["And all the cops are still hangin' out at the doughnut shops", "Talkin' 'bout how the weather's changin'", "The ice is meltin' as if the world is endin'"], "meaning": "Drizzy’s taking the most stereotypical conversation starter – “how about this weather?” – and adding an extremely pessimistic spin regarding global warming. Manmade pollution is causing the planet to heat up, and ice in the Arctic is melting 12% every decade.\n\nThis doomsday scenario was the plot of the 2004 movie The Day After Tomorrow.", "annotation_id": "4244821", "url": "https://genius.com/4244821", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "The last man fucked up, I’ll take it since he doesn’t want it", "vault_lines": ["The last man fucked up, I'll take it since he doesn't want it"], "meaning": "Drizzy is possibly referencing the infamous Chris Brown incident where he “fucked up” his picture-perfect relationship with Rihanna. After that rough period in her life, Drizzy swooped in as the gentleman he is and saved her from her abusive relationship and treated her properly.\n\nHis insecurities are the fact that … at one point in his life the woman that he loves fell into my lap. I did what a real nigga would do and treated her with respect.", "annotation_id": "4244493", "url": "https://genius.com/4244493", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "Yes Lord, yes Lord, yes Lord I’ve seen some things I wish we had met when I was in my teens Yes Lord, yes Lord, yes Lord ’Cause these days I only know conditional love I’m not used to settlin’ down I’m too used to switchin’ it up, oh", "vault_lines": ["Yes Lord, yes Lord, yes Lord", "I've seen some things", "I wish we had met when I was in my teens", "'Cause these days I only know conditional love", "I'm not used to settlin' down", "I'm too used to switchin' it up, oh"], "meaning": "Drake fears girls are only interested in him because of his fame and that notion makes it difficult for him to get romantically involved with anyone. At this point in his life girls come and go and he is constantly on the road because of his hectic schedule. If he had met this girl prior to all of the glitz and glamour he would’ve been more sure that it was all for real.", "annotation_id": "4243924", "url": "https://genius.com/4243924", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "[Outro: Dennis Graham & Drake] All of this, it’s alcohol, it’s drugs, weed, it’s everything that you could ever imagine. Man, our sisters, our sisters, and our brothers, our brothers, need to stop this shit. They need to take a step up. Young girls are hot, and they will fuck anybody, anytime, anyway. Because they are so hot and young guys are, they are going for it. This shit has to stop, man. This shit has to stop. These people need to uh, uh, uh, uh educate themselves. Women are too fucking hot. Yeah, you’re right. You’re right. Alright man, well, shit, I just wanted… They know they’re too hot. They don’t give a fuck. It’s the heat of the moment. It’s the heat of the moment and that’s all it is. It’s the heat of the moment. I agree This shit, that’s all it is. It’s the heat of the moment. Be a 100% woman, or try and be a 100% man, and use a fucking condom Haaa", "vault_lines": ["Heat of the moment, girl, heat of the moment", "And I say \"Well, goddamn\"", "Yeah, you're right. You're right. Alright man, well, shit, I just wanted…"], "meaning": "Here you can hear Drake’s father (Dennis Graham just schooling his son on some life advice and what it’s really like to be in the heat of the moment. He is a past musician and knows a lot about that lifestyle. The two are talking about how these girls are young, naive and gorgeous but they tend to make poor decisions in the heat of the moment. The two are ultimately shaking their heads at the majority of females these days and wishing for them too change.\n\n\n\nRead what Drizzy has to say about his pops hopping on this track here.", "annotation_id": "4243922", "url": "https://genius.com/4243922", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "Then ask myself Who am I gonna be when it’s all over? When it’s all over? Who am I gonna be when it’s all over? When it’s all over? I don’t wanna think about that right now", "vault_lines": ["\"Who are you gonna be when it's all over? When it's all over?\"", "I don't wanna think about that right now", "Who am I gonna be when it's all over? When it's all over?"], "meaning": "As opposed to the first hook, where his girl asks these questions, Drake is now evaluating his own career instead. He is no stranger when it comes to looking forward in his career. Take the song “Tuscan Leather” when he raps :\n\nJust give it time, we’ll see who’s still around a decade from now that’s real\n\nBut It’s Far From Over…", "annotation_id": "4243903", "url": "https://genius.com/4243903", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "What are we even doing?", "vault_lines": ["What are we even doing?"], "meaning": "Like on “How About Now”, Drizzy incorporates recordings of females to further the narrative of the track.\n\nThe hook sees Drake exploiting the indecency of this girl’s previous partner, but throughout the hook and up to this point, the girl is questioning Drake. She functions as a conscience (albeit with a rather seductive tone), inquiring about his future, reputation, and current choices, whereas Drake displays his disregard for all of those things.\n\nThis all embodies the song’s title, “Heat of the Moment”, which essentially equates to Drizzy living in the present over the past or future.", "annotation_id": "4243867", "url": "https://genius.com/4243867", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "Heat of the moment Heat of the moment, girl, heat of the moment", "vault_lines": ["Heat of the moment, girl, heat of the moment", "Can't think straight in the heat of the moment, heat of the moment"], "meaning": "Drake is caught up in all of the things he is doing right now. Whether it is working on his new album, touring with Weezy, or appearing in Nicki’s music video.\n\n\n\nDrake is always grinding. He is too busy to think about the future, he has to make important decisions in a split second, but that really doesn’t phase him because he goes 0 to 100 real quick.", "annotation_id": "4243863", "url": "https://genius.com/4243863", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "And she says, “You don’t how good it is to be you ’cause you’re him”", "vault_lines": ["And she says, \"You don't how good it is to be you 'cause you're him\""], "meaning": "This girl is trying to give Drake an outside perspective on his life. She is telling him that he is verging on greatness and is living the dream life but he doesn’t recognize this because he is too caught up in the rush of fame and fortune to look back and realize what he has achieved.\n\nHe had a similar conversation in “The Catch Up”:\n\nImagine how it feel to watch another nigga at the top\nYou know that if you wasn’t you, you would be dissin' you, dawg.\n\nDrake seems to gain a lot of insight through a female’s view of him.", "annotation_id": "4243845", "url": "https://genius.com/4243845", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "Ridin’ clean With you on my mind and I’m thinkin’ dirty things", "vault_lines": ["With you on my mind and I'm thinkin' dirty things"], "meaning": "Riding clean means to drive under no influence. With his mind clear of all drugs and alcohol, he is completely focused on this one girl he’s going home to enjoy. It’s also worth noting the contrast with clean and dirty here.\n\nDrake actually had a photo captioned with this on his Instagram two years ago.", "annotation_id": "4243842", "url": "https://genius.com/4243842", "state": "pending", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "I ain’t even got a strap, if you think we really need one, I’m on it", "vault_lines": ["I ain't even got a strap, if you think we really need one, I'm on it"], "meaning": "Here a strap refers to a condom. Drake doesn’t see the need for one, but even in the heat of the moment Drake is perfectly fine with strapping on if his girl insists on it. He is about to smash, after all.", "annotation_id": "4243826", "url": "https://genius.com/4243826", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "All the school kids are so sick of books and learnin’ They don’t read anymore, they don’t even read anymore They just wanna be like all the rappers that I can’t stand Niggas we don’t need anymore All the niggas we don’t need anymore", "vault_lines": ["All the school kids are so sick of books and learnin'", "What are we even doing?", "They don't read anymore, they don't even read anymore", "They just wanna be like all the rappers that I can't stand", "Niggas we don't need anymore", "All the niggas we don't need anymore"], "meaning": "Drake sees the hip hop game full of artists just echoing one another. There’s a pervasive lack of originality. Kids would rather emulate all of this to be “cool” instead of educating themselves.\n\nDrake does not approve. Education is clearly important to Drizzy. Though he dropped out in his first round of schooling he made a point of going back and is dedicated to educational causes.\n\nObviously Drake is the rapper you should look up to!\n\nhttps://twitter.com/drake/status/258731694687920130", "annotation_id": "4243756", "url": "https://genius.com/4243756", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Heat of the Moment", "line": "She asked me “Who are you gonna be when it’s all over? When it’s all over?” She asked me “Who are you gonna be when it’s all over? When it’s all over?” I don’t wanna think about that right now", "vault_lines": ["\"Who are you gonna be when it's all over? When it's all over?\"", "I don't wanna think about that right now", "Who am I gonna be when it's all over? When it's all over?"], "meaning": "Drake is asked what type of person he’s going to be at the end of his career, but he’s still a young rapper and that outcome is too far away to think about. He has a vision of his career being long lasting and maybe building an empire like Jay Z’s.", "annotation_id": "2412248", "url": "https://genius.com/2412248", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
