{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "I wanna know, how come we can never slash and stay friends?", "vault_lines": ["I wanna know, how come we can never slash and stay friends?"], "meaning": "To Drake, “slash” directly translates to “fuck”. This can be seen on his clean live performance of “Gyalchester” at the 2017 Billboard Awards where he replaced the word “fuck” for “slash”.\n\nGirls often get emotionally attached to Drake after they get intimate, which is why they cannot stay friends with him to avoid the emotional pain. While Drake has no time for relationships, he would still like to be friends with those women.", "annotation_id": "12238252", "url": "https://genius.com/12238252", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "I know we can’t keep it together forever ’Cause you’re crazy sometimes And I only see you sometimes", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I know what I like", "I know we can't keep it together forever", "'Cause you're crazy sometimes", "And I only see you sometimes"], "meaning": "Drake has had his fair share of crazy women as can be seen in the skit of his “Child’s Play” video, and he can no longer deal with it.\n\nAnother reason for Drake’s failed relationships is the fact that he’s too busy to see a lot of these women, as he raps on “Do Not Disturb”:\n\nI gotta start sleepin' at the studio\nI don’t have no time to be no Romeo\n\n\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: “Together forever” is probably a call out to Lionel Ritchie’s “All Night Long.”", "annotation_id": "12192202", "url": "https://genius.com/12192202", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "Good morning, good afternoon, good night", "vault_lines": ["Good morning, good afternoon, good night"], "meaning": "Wayne greets a world-wide audience of listeners all tuning in at the same time while in different time zones in order to listen to the premiere of More Life on episode 39 of OVO Sound Radio before it was released on iTunes/Apple Music, Spotify, Tidal and other streaming platforms.", "annotation_id": "11824075", "url": "https://genius.com/11824075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "Don’t switch on me, I got big plans We need to forward to the islands And get you gold, no spray tans", "vault_lines": ["I don't need no advice", "And get you gold, no spray tan", "Don't switch on me, I got big plans", "We need to forward to the islands", "And get you gold, no spray tans"], "meaning": "Drake wants a getaway with the lady he’s addressing, despite the fact that he is not seeking a longterm relationship with her.\nThere is evidence that this verse is referring to dance-pop superstar Rihanna. They had an on again, off again relationship from 2011 to 2016.  And the reference to the “islands” is significant,\nsince Rihanna hails from the island country of Barbados.\n\n\n\nWhile the “no spray tans” phrase raises the possibility that that the object of his affections is white and therefore not Rihanna, it is also possible Drake threw the line in to protect her identity.\n\nGo forwards may be a play on the Jamaican patois term “go forwards,” meaning sandals with no heel strap, the type of footwear you might wear to the beach.", "annotation_id": "11564720", "url": "https://genius.com/11564720", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "Don’t switch on me, I got big plans We need to forward to the islands And get you gold, no spray tan", "vault_lines": ["I don't need no advice", "And get you gold, no spray tan", "Don't switch on me, I got big plans", "We need to forward to the islands", "And get you gold, no spray tans"], "meaning": "Drake wants a getaway with the lady he’s addressing, despite the fact that he is not seeking a longterm relationship with her.\nThere is evidence that this verse is referring to dance-pop superstar Rihanna. They had an on again, off again relationship from 2011 to 2016.  And the reference to the “islands” is significant,\nsince Rihanna hails from the island country of Barbados.\n\n\n\nWhile the “no spray tans” phrase raises the possibility that that the object of his affections is white and therefore not Rihanna, it is also possible Drake threw the line in to protect her identity.", "annotation_id": "11564719", "url": "https://genius.com/11564719", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "Don’t switch on me, I got big plans We need to forward to the islands And get you gold, no spray tans", "vault_lines": ["I don't need no advice", "And get you gold, no spray tan", "Don't switch on me, I got big plans", "We need to forward to the islands", "And get you gold, no spray tans"], "meaning": "Drake wants a getaway with the lady he’s addressing, despite the fact that he is not seeking a long-term relationship with her.\nThere is evidence that this verse is referring to dance-pop superstar Rihanna. They had an on again, off again relationship from 2011 to 2016.  And the reference to the “islands” is significant since Rihanna hails from the island country of Barbados.\n\n\n\nWhile the “no spray tans” phrase raises the possibility that the object of his affections is white and therefore not Rihanna, it is also possible Drake threw the line in to protect her identity.\n\nThe word “forward” is Jamaican Patois slang, means to come here; to come on let’s go, or a form of saying “I’m coming”. In this case, Drake is saying we need to go to da islands.", "annotation_id": "11564692", "url": "https://genius.com/11564692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "So, move from me when you’re extra Move from me with the passa", "vault_lines": ["So, move from me when you're extra", "Move from me with the passa", "Move from me when you're extra"], "meaning": "Despite his obvious passion for the woman who is the subject of this track, he can’t live with her because her melodramatic attitude is too much for him.  The slang term “extra” means “over the top; excessive” and the Jamaican patois term “Passa,” means “gossip.”\n\nAppearing on a Beats 1 interview episode with host Zane Lowe back on May 3rd, 2016 – just under a year prior to the release of More Life, Drake provided some insight into the meaning behind ‘the passa’, saying:\n\nShe’s (my mom) always down with the – we call it the passa passa, that’s what we call it\nThe passa, you know – that’s the talk, so my moms always – she gets all the passa straight to her phone.", "annotation_id": "11548614", "url": "https://genius.com/11548614", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "Unruly, unruly, maddas", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Unruly is Jamaican dancehall star Popcaan’s crew.  Drake has a close affiliation with Popcaan,  who often uses “Unruly” as a signature ad-lib. On the song “No Telling” Popcaan has an ad-lib in the middle of the song:\n\nOVO, Unruly\nOne shot to make it in a life\nFrom the six to the fucking 876\n\nMaddas is a patois word, which when translated literally, means “madder”. When used in patois, it is used to describe a person who is “not right in their head” aka a crazy person. Maddas, coupled with the use of unruly, Drake is telling you he’s not the one to fuck with, as he does what he wants.\n\n\n\nDizzy actually tattooed the word “unruly” along with a flaming skull in November of 2016, 4 months prior to the release of this song. Many believe that it was a way of reciprocating his appreciation to Popcaan after he tattooed the OVO owl on his forearm only a week prior.", "annotation_id": "11533867", "url": "https://genius.com/11533867", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "[Outro: Lil Wayne & Lionel Richie] Together forever", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The brief vocal in the outro is sung by Lionel Richie. Drake posted a photo on Instagram with the caption “Thanks for the outro vocal on Blem dad.”", "annotation_id": "11532821", "url": "https://genius.com/11532821", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "I need you to stop runnin’ back to your ex, he’s a wasteman", "vault_lines": ["I need you to stop runnin' back to your ex, he's a wasteman"], "meaning": "“Wasteman” is Jamaican slang for a worthless person, used in Toronto as a common insult, as well as in the UK.\n\nIn this line, Drake is stating that the girl’s ex-boyfriend is good for nothing and she needs to stop running back to him when things go south. He’s trying to persuade her to move on from him instead.\n\n\n\nThis could also be a reference to Nicki Minaj and a diss to Meek Mill, whom she recently broke up with.", "annotation_id": "11532119", "url": "https://genius.com/11532119", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "I’m blem for real, I might just say how I feel I’m blem for real, I might just say how I feel", "vault_lines": ["I'm blem for real, I might just say how I feel"], "meaning": "“Blem” is a term adopted into Toronto slang (originally derived from Jamaican slang) to express the state of being high on mind altering substances—the drugs make Drake feel uninhibited to speak his mind. “Blem” can also mean to be blunt or honest.", "annotation_id": "11531929", "url": "https://genius.com/11531929", "state": "accepted", "votes": 209, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "Who keeps bringing more? I’ve had too many This Virginia done me off already", "vault_lines": ["Who keeps bringing more? I've had too many", "This Virginia done me off already"], "meaning": "Drake owns a whiskey brand called Virginia Black. He launched the beverage in 2016 alongside Brent Hocking, a mortgage loan merchant who previously founded DeLeón tequila and sold it to Diddy in 2013.\n\n\n\nDrake has aslo promoted Virginia Black on his track “Gyalchester”, rapping:\n\nVirginia Black\nI could go make enough money off that and not even rap\nWhat’s that? Facts", "annotation_id": "11531144", "url": "https://genius.com/11531144", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blem", "line": "I’m buildin’ up a house where they raised me You move with me, I’ll go crazy", "vault_lines": ["I'm buildin' up a house where they raised me", "You move with me, I'll go crazy", "You move with me, I'll go—, look, just"], "meaning": "Drake currently lives in Hidden Hills, CA. He bought the house for 7.7 million in 2012.\n\nNow it seems that he wants to move back to the 6 (Toronto), his hometown. This is evident as he has begun remodeling a house he bought there in 2015.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BRy8kzolj_1/", "annotation_id": "11531106", "url": "https://genius.com/11531106", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
