{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "You been hatin’ hard for a very long time", "vault_lines": ["You been hatin' hard for a very long time"], "meaning": "Drake calls back to the long history of the ongoing feud between him and Kendrick, which started with Kendrick’s controversial verse on Control back in 2013.\n\nDrake also seems to use the word “hate” to purposefully call back to Kendrick’s track euphoria:\n\nIt’s always been about love and hate, now let me say I’m the biggest hater\nI hate the way that you walk, the way that you talk, I hate the way that you dress\nI hate the way that you sneak diss, if I catch flight, it’s gon' be direct\nWe hate the bitches you fuck ‘cause they confuse themself with real women", "annotation_id": "33347445", "url": "https://genius.com/33347445", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "Streets out here talkin’ like, “At least a nigga tried”", "vault_lines": ["Streets out here talkin' like, \"At least a nigga tried\""], "meaning": "Pretty straightforward. Drake is saying that “the streets” are giving Kendrick props for at the very least trying to go at Drake in a rap battle.", "annotation_id": "32403332", "url": "https://genius.com/32403332", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "Now you sayin’ two pussy boys gettin’ outta line", "vault_lines": ["Now you sayin' two pussy boys gettin' outta line"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to Kendrick Lamar mentioning Drake and J Cole on euphoria\n\nIt’s three GOATs left, and I seen two of them kissin' and huggin' on stage\n\nThe implication is that the “kissing and hugging on stage” makes Drake and J Cole “pussy boys”", "annotation_id": "32286552", "url": "https://genius.com/32286552", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "It’s how you felt in 2011, why we wastin’ time? Dreams come true, crodie, this is where you die", "vault_lines": ["It's how you felt in 2011, why we wastin' time?", "Dreams come true, crodie, this is where you die"], "meaning": "A clear callback to Kendrick’s Buried Alive Interlude from Drake’s 2011 album, Take Care, and the final two lines in particular:\n\nSo dig a shovel full of money, full of power, full of pussy\nFull of fame and bury yourself alive, then I died\n\nThe interlude serves as a meditation on the nature of fame, with Kendrick relaying a meeting he had with Drake in which the latter educated him on the sacrifices that come with stardom, and its impact on personal identity. Kendrick also mentions his jealousy upon realizing how much more Drake had achieved than him at that point, despite them being the same age, and closes the track by accepting the price of stardom: being buried alive by money, power, pussy, and fame, with his old self metaphorically “dying” in the process.", "annotation_id": "31838640", "url": "https://genius.com/31838640", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "You always said how you wanna bury me alive", "vault_lines": ["You always said how you wanna bury me alive"], "meaning": "This is a play on the song title “Buried Alive Interlude” and likely a reference to Kendick Lamar’s verse on Big Sean’s “Control” (2013):\n\n“I’m usually homeboys with the same niggas I’m rhymin' with\nBut this is hip-hop, and them niggas should know what time it is\nAnd that goes for Jermaine Cole, Big K.R.I.T., Wale\nPusha T, Meek Millz, A$AP Rocky, Drake\nBig Sean, Jay Electron', Tyler, Mac Miller\nI got love for you all, but I’m tryna murder you niggas”", "annotation_id": "31809995", "url": "https://genius.com/31809995", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "React like an infant whenever I am mentioned", "vault_lines": ["React like an infant whenever I am mentioned"], "meaning": "This line comes from Kendrick Lamar’s Buried Alive verse but “you” is changed to “I” in this instance\nhttps://genius.com/439040/Drake-buried-alive-interlude/Lookin-in-the-mirror-im-embarrassed-im-feelin-like-a-suicidal-terrorist-react-like-an-infant-whenever-you-are-mentioned", "annotation_id": "31682255", "url": "https://genius.com/31682255", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "Stop talkin’ how you gon’ spare us", "vault_lines": ["Stop talkin' how you gon' spare us"], "meaning": "In Kendrick’s dedicated diss, “euphoria,” he alludes several times to having more material on Drake which he’s honorably refraining from using:\n\n\nI make music that electrify ‘em, you make music that pacify 'em\nI can double down on that line, but spare you this time, that’s random acts of kindness\n\nWe ain’t gotta get personal, this a friendly fade, you should keep it that way\nI know some shit about niggas that make Gunna Wunna look like a saint\n\nIf you take it there, I’m takin' it further\nPsst, that’s something you don’t wanna do\n\n\nMany fans speculated the use of “pacify” was a reference to a baby pacifier, a subtle allusion to years-long rumors of Drake being an ephebophile. In following disses “meet the grahams” and “Not Like Us,” Kendrick confirmed this as being one of the angles up his sleeve, accusing Drake of being a pedophile and sex trafficker; he also accused Drake of being a drug addict and deadbeat father to a hidden daughter.", "annotation_id": "31681201", "url": "https://genius.com/31681201", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "Lookin’ in the mirror, still embarrassed", "vault_lines": ["Lookin' in the mirror, still embarrassed"], "meaning": "The first lyric of the verse on Buried Alive Interlude is “Lookin' in the mirror I’m embarrassed.”\n\nDrake is saying that even after 13 years of the song being out, Kendrick is still embarrassed of himself because he’s not at the level that Drake is.", "annotation_id": "31671534", "url": "https://genius.com/31671534", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "Jealousy disguised as yo’ motherfuckin’ pride", "vault_lines": ["Jealousy disguised as yo' motherfuckin' pride"], "meaning": "Drake says that Kendrick is truly just jealous of Drake’s fame and success and is saying that he is better than Drake, but really isn’t in that position.", "annotation_id": "31671519", "url": "https://genius.com/31671519", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "It’s like you need tension to always get attention", "vault_lines": ["It's like you need tension to always get attention"], "meaning": "Drake says that Kendrick always needs some sort of conflict to gain fame or attention. His previous albums have referenced social issues regarding mental health or the structural oppression of African Americans. Now he is using this beef to regain his fame, as J. Cole mentioned on the now deleted “7 Minute Drill,”\n“If he wasn’t dissin', then we wouldn’t be discussin' him.”", "annotation_id": "31671487", "url": "https://genius.com/31671487", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "[Verse]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse is delivered with the same cadence as Kendrick’s verse on “Buried Alive Interlude.”", "annotation_id": "31671484", "url": "https://genius.com/31671484", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "It feel like Twitter ghostwritin’ your reply", "vault_lines": ["It feel like Twitter ghostwritin’ your reply"], "meaning": "This line is supposed to be comedic as Kendrick has previously criticized Drake for having ghostwriters or people writing his songs for him.\n\nDrake suggests that Kendrick makes music that is very cryptic and unexplained by him, and people especially on Twitter overanalyze his lyrics and give them meaning that Kendrick did not intend.", "annotation_id": "31663863", "url": "https://genius.com/31663863", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "If you in a pine box, box I would shovel dirt on top, top I’d play this record on repeat, ’peat You always been a bitch to me If you was in a pine box, box I would still be in my spot, spot For you to make it to the peak, peak It’d have to be the death of me, the death of me", "vault_lines": ["If you in a pine box, box", "I was headline, you was standin' on the sidе", "I would shovel dirt on top, top", "I'd play this record on repeat, 'peat", "You always been a bitch to me", "I would still be in my spot, spot", "For you to make it to the peak, peak", "It'd have to be the death of me, the death of me"], "meaning": "Drake reinterprets and twists Kendrick Lamar’s original intro from “Buried Alive Interlude” to amplify his resentment towards Lamar, insinuating that even in death (even if he is in a casket “pine box”,) he wouldn’t let go of his bitterness (by still shoveling dirt on his casket.)\n\nThe line “You always been a bitch to me” could reflect Drake mocking Kendrick’s emotional vulnerability, which was particularly evident on his most recent album, Mr. Morale & The Big Steppers. It might also suggest that Drake views the animosity he perceives from Kendrick as stemming from jealousy—a trait he implies is feminine in nature.\n\nDrake then positions himself as the obstacle Kendrick must overcome to claim the top spot in hip-hop, implying that Kendrick’s success and ascension are contingent upon his (Drake’s) absence (“death”.)", "annotation_id": "31644515", "url": "https://genius.com/31644515", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "line": "Took you on your first tour with us, tryna catch a vibe I was headline, you was standin’ on the side Brought you and that other ho along for the ride First time people lined up for your ass", "vault_lines": ["Took you on your first tour with us, tryna catch a vibе", "I was headline, you was standin' on the sidе", "Brought you and that other ho along for the ride", "First time people lined up for your ass"], "meaning": "The Club Paradise Tour was Drake’s second headlining gig. It came right after he dropped his album Take Care. And it got so popular that tickets sold out super fast. In the end, the tour grossed approximately a whopping $42.6 million worldwide and was one the biggest hip-hop tours of 2012.\n\n\n\nKendrick Lamar and A$AP Rocky were opening acts on most of the dates. For Kendrick and Rocky, being on the Club Paradise Tour was a huge deal, especially since they were still newcomers at that time. So sharing the stage with Drake, really helped boost their profiles and introduce them to a wider audience.\n\nKendrick even talked about this in an interview, saying how it was the first time he performed in front of such huge crowds:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1L7CW-p4-MI&t=16s&pp=ygUla2VuZHJpayBvbiBkcmFrZSBicmluZ2luZyBoaW0gb24gdG91cg%3D%3D", "annotation_id": "31634312", "url": "https://genius.com/31634312", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2", "status": "done", "targets": 14, "clicked": 14, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-buried-alive-interlude-pt-2-lyrics", "genius_title": "Buried Alive Interlude, Pt. 2"}
