{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "The seats for these Heat games?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "At the time the song was recorded, the Miami Heat were among the best teams in the NBA, winning back-to-back championships in 2012 and 2013", "annotation_id": "26369486", "url": "https://genius.com/26369486", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "And I got them wedding ring flows, that engaging", "vault_lines": ["And I got them wedding ring flows, that engaging"], "meaning": "Drizzy plays with the topic of engagement/weddings, noting how his style of delivery engages millions of people worldwide to listen to his music", "annotation_id": "26369476", "url": "https://genius.com/26369476", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "I took some sense and made a nickel of it", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Pun on coins, “I took some sense / cents and made a nickel of it”.\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: He took some advice from his mum and made great off it from his endeavors", "annotation_id": "23643597", "url": "https://genius.com/23643597", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "Would’ve came back for you, I just needed time To do what I had to do, caught in the life I can’t let it go Whether that’s right, I will never know, oh-oh-oh", "vault_lines": ["Would've came back for you, I just needed time", "To do what I had to do, caught in the life", "I can't let it go", "Whether that's right, I will never know, oh-oh-oh", "But I would've came back, but I would've came back for you"], "meaning": "Drake is implying to the woman he is with or has been with in the past that he would’ve came back to her but most likely got caught up in the fame and fortune and it has consumed him and he cant  seem to change his ways because he has got so adjusted to a certain lifestyle   “Caught up in the life I cant let it go”.", "annotation_id": "19711717", "url": "https://genius.com/19711717", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "La Familia", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Gang started in brooklyn NY\nLa Familia Michoacana, La Familia, or LFM is a Mexican drug cartel and a organized crime syndicate based in the Mexican state of Michoacán. Formerly allied to the Gulf Cartel—as part of Los Zetas—it split off in 2006. The cartel was founded by Carlos Rosales Mendoza a close associate of Osiel Cárdenas.", "annotation_id": "18943070", "url": "https://genius.com/18943070", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "I got that Courtney Love for you, that crazy shit", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake plays on the name of American singer-songwriter Courtney Love, who was Kurt Cobain’s wife. Vanity Fair called Love a “trainwreck personality” in 1992, as her relationship with Cobain was extremely high-profile.", "annotation_id": "17735551", "url": "https://genius.com/17735551", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "I’m on some Marvin Gaye shit, a bunch of distant lovers", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drizzy references Marvin Gaye’s 1973 track “Distant Lover” from the classic album Let’s Get It On. A live version of the track peaked at #28 on the Billboard Hot 100 in 1974, while the original was sampled in Kanye West’s 2004 track “Spaceship.”", "annotation_id": "17728268", "url": "https://genius.com/17728268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "To be far from hood, but to understand the streets", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake has been criticized for coming off like he was raised in a rough neighborhood when in reality Drake grew up in a nice Toronto suburb. \n\nHis song “Started From The Bottom” really upset some listeners, claiming that Drake doesn’t have any idea of what it really is to be at the bottom…\n\nEither way, Drake claims here that he isn’t a “hood” rapper but he is street smart and still understands what goes on in the hood.\n\nThis line also pertains to Drake’s appeal and his target audience. In his music, he tries to be accessible to both those in rough neighborhoods and children in prep school.\n\nThis line is similar to his line on the track, “Ignant Shit”, where he raps:\n\nAnd me doin' the shows gettin' everyone nervous\n‘Cause them hipsters gon’ have to get along with them hood niggas", "annotation_id": "1485812", "url": "https://genius.com/1485812", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "23 and going through a midlife crisis", "vault_lines": ["23 and going through a midlife crisis"], "meaning": "Drake, at 23, due to his fame, is feeling the amount of pressure in his life that men in their mid 40’s and such do, which is appropriately titled a mid-life crisis.\n\nWhen men hit their mid life crisis as lot of impulse or over excessive spending tend to occur. Therefore, Drake feels that at 23 he is spending boat load of money which is the equivalent of a man going through his mid life.\n\nHe later raps a similar line on the track, “Over”, where he also contemplates his wisdom beyond his years. On the track, he raps:\n\nMan they treat me like a legend, am I really this cold?\nI’m really too young to be feeling this old", "annotation_id": "30529", "url": "https://genius.com/30529", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "I miss Memphis, Tennessee, my cousins, my dad The simplistic beauty that all of them Southerners have", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "As a child, Drake spent his Summers with his (black) father in Memphis and the rest of the year with his (white) mother in Canada\n\nNice color-coding..\n\nSaying that Southerners have ‘simplistic beauty’ seems like a backhanded compliment at best.\n\n\n\nThis guy has the simplest of beauty.", "annotation_id": "30527", "url": "https://genius.com/30527", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "I never threw away that paper with my Grammy speech Because I haven’t hit the pinnacles I plan to reach", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake was nominated for 2 Grammys in 2010 for “Best I Ever Had” – but lost to Jay-Z’s “D.O.A.” in both categories\n\nBut he kept his victory speech saved! (On Google Docs) – because surely he’ll win some award sooner or later…\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q-ml0fdqBlk#t=282\n\nNevertheless, although Drake has achieved, he feel like he’s not done.", "annotation_id": "27952", "url": "https://genius.com/27952", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "I really think that you stare at yourself and you see things", "vault_lines": ["I really think that you stare at yourself and you see things"], "meaning": "When the girl stares at herself in the mirror, she notices flaws that he doesn’t see, a smooth way of Drake telling the girl she’s fine.\n\nHe could also be noting that people look in the mirror and they want to become something they aren’t. Either fake people who think they’ve made it or all the way to young girls trying to look like models", "annotation_id": "27088", "url": "https://genius.com/27088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "line": "Feeling like all four members of Color Me Badd", "vault_lines": ["Feeling like all four members of Color Me Badd"], "meaning": "Color Me Badd was a R&B group in the early 90s; Drake is as famous as all 4 of them combined, and his various shopping bags exhibit similar color variety to Color Me Badd’s suits", "annotation_id": "25906", "url": "https://genius.com/25906", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Paris Morton Music", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 22, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-paris-morton-music-lyrics", "genius_title": "Paris Morton Music"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Passionfruit", "line": "Um, trying to think of the right thing to say", "vault_lines": ["Um, trying to think of the right thing to say"], "meaning": "These are the words of Zoë Kravitz, an American actress, singer, model and one of Drake’s best friends. She has been mentioned before by Drake on “Star67,”  from his 2015 mixtape If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late.\n\nIn the past there have been rumors of the two dating, however Zoë cleared these up on an interview with Complex News in April of 2015, saying:\n\nI’m very flirtatious, but he’s family to me. He’s a really, really awesome dude. We inspire each other. We play each other music.", "annotation_id": "11755735", "url": "https://genius.com/11755735", "state": "accepted", "votes": 260, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Passionfruit", "line": "Listen Seein’ you got ritualistic Cleansin’ my soul of addiction for now ‘Cause I’m fallin’ apart, yeah", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I'm fallin' apart, yeah", "’Cause we're fallin’ apart", "Seein' you got ritualistic", "Cleansin' my soul of addiction for now"], "meaning": "In the past, Drake visited this girl ‘religiously,’ or so routinely that it began to feel like he developed a dependence on her. Although she may want him to come and see her, he needs some time to focus on taking care of himself.\n\nDrake shares similar feelings on “Good Ones Go” from his 2011 album Take Care:\n\nI’m spending time just taking care of me right now\nI’m getting money just taking care of me, girl\n‘cause you’d be tired of taking care of me by now\n\nWhether by design or coincidence, the religious imagery in these lines bears a connection to the song’s title, “Passionfruit.” The fruit’s name is derived from Christianity’s “Passion of the Christ.” The striking appearance of the passion flower was thought by 16th century missionaries to be symbolic of Christ’s crucifixion.", "annotation_id": "11566698", "url": "https://genius.com/11566698", "state": "accepted", "votes": 124, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Passionfruit", "line": "Listen Harder buildin’ trust from a distance I think we should rule out commitment for now ‘Cause we’re fallin’ apart", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I'm fallin' apart, yeah", "’Cause we're fallin’ apart", "Harder buildin' trust from a distance", "I think we should rule out commitment for now"], "meaning": "Drake is frequently away from Toronto, whether he’s on tour or spending time at his Calabasas estate near Los Angeles.\n\nLosing touch in long distance relationships is nothing new for Drake. Even back in 2011 when he released the single “Club Paradise,” it was clear that his absence put strain on his relationships:\n\nIt’s crazy all the emotions forgot in a year\nShe like, ‘Why you even give a fuck, you not even here?’\nWell, out there there ain’t nothing for me and I think I need to come home", "annotation_id": "11554553", "url": "https://genius.com/11554553", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Passionfruit", "line": "Leavin’ You’re just doing that to get even Don’t pick up the pieces, just leave it for now They keep fallin’ apart", "vault_lines": ["You're just doing that to get even", "Don't pick up the pieces, just leave it for now", "They keep fallin' apart"], "meaning": "Drake’s perspective on “picking up the pieces” has changed over the years. On “Connect,” a song from Drake’s 2013 album Nothing Was The Same, he sang:\n\nBut when it falls apart, I’m always still down\nTo pick a million tiny little pieces off the ground\nWish you would learn to love people and use things\nAnd not the other way around\n\nAt that time, Drake seemed willing to put effort into fixing his relationships. More recently, on his 2016 track “Redemption” from Views, he expressed his frustration about the same kind of situation:\n\nWhy do I settle for women that force me to pick up the pieces?\n\nIt’s clear that he’s grown tired of trying to fix his dysfunctional relationships.", "annotation_id": "11554534", "url": "https://genius.com/11554534", "state": "accepted", "votes": 124, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Passionfruit", "line": "Tension Between us just like picket fences You got issues that I won’t mention for now ‘Cause we’re fallin’ apart", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I'm fallin' apart, yeah", "Between us just like picket fences", "You got issues that I won’t mention for now", "’Cause we're fallin’ apart"], "meaning": "Picket fences are symbols of domesticity and the quintessential suburban life, but they’re also an unavoidable boundary or enclosure. Sometimes domestic life is not so ideal, and the fence represents what divides a couple.\n\n\n\nThe adaptation of the play Fences by August Wilson was released a few months (December 2016) prior to More Life. Drake may be playing off the sexual tension between the script’s main characters, Troy and Rose Maxson.", "annotation_id": "11551983", "url": "https://genius.com/11551983", "state": "accepted", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Passionfruit", "line": "Passionate from miles away Passive with the things you say Passin’ up on my old ways I can’t blame you, no, no Passionate from miles away Passive with the things you say Passin’ up on my old ways I can’t blame you, no, no", "vault_lines": ["Passionate from miles away", "Passive with the things you say", "Passin' up on my old ways", "I can't blame you, no, no"], "meaning": "“Passion” and “passive” share Old French origins—“passion” meant “physical suffering,” specifically that of Jesus Christ, and “passif” meant “undergoing hardship.” “Passionfruit” is a species of “Passiflora,” the Latin genus name for all varieties of passion flower. Missionaries in Brazil originally called it “the flower of five wounds” as a reference to Jesus’ crucifixion when they were trying to convert the natives to Christianity.\n\nDrake used flower emojis in promotional social media posts for More Life, and even got a flower tattoo resembling a passion flower around the time of its release. Even though Drake probably didn’t know the etymological connection when he decided to alliterate the words and get the tattoo, the pain he describes aligns with their historical meaning.", "annotation_id": "11550947", "url": "https://genius.com/11550947", "state": "accepted", "votes": 509, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Passionfruit", "line": "Hold on, hold on, fuck that Fuck that shit Hold on, I got to start this motherfuckin’ record over again, wait a minute Fuck that shit Still on this motherfuckin’ record I’ma play this motherfucker for y’all Ayy, y’all get some more drinks goin’ on I’ll sound a whole lot better", "vault_lines": ["Hold on, hold on, fuck that", "Hold on, I got to start this motherfuckin' record over again, wait a minute", "Still on this motherfuckin’ record", "I'ma play this motherfucker for y'all", "Ayy, y’all get some more drinks goin' on", "I'll sound a whole lot better"], "meaning": "This is a sample from a live set by Detroit House producer and DJ Moodymann (a.k.a. Kenny Dixon Jr.) titled “Live at Cutloose 2nd Birthday Party, Manchester, UK 17.09.10.”\n\nMore Life was originally scheduled for release in late 2016, but didn’t drop until March 2017. The sample in the context of More Life could suggest that Drake was running into issues while making the playlist, and started drinking for inspiration, making this song.", "annotation_id": "11531554", "url": "https://genius.com/11531554", "state": "accepted", "votes": 577, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
