{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "[Part II]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The song sampled here is a 1980 instrumental called “Tinted Glass” by Network. The group was one of many formed by prolific Dutch stock-music composer Jan Stoeckart, who recorded over 1300 songs for commercial libraries.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=knR8rsGW0iU", "annotation_id": "7473969", "url": "https://genius.com/7473969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 133, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "...Shell out, all if yuh eye nuh big Always haffi look out, yuh no know if yuh head book out", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This line means you always have to be vigilant cause you’ll never know if a hit is on your head or someone is trying to kill you", "annotation_id": "6566376", "url": "https://genius.com/6566376", "state": "verified", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "[Outro: Popcaan & Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Popcaan is a popular Jamaican dancehall artist and leader of Unruly Gang, a brand that’s similar to Drake’s OVO. Drake has been a fan of Popcaan for a while, in 2012 he tweeted lyrics to Popcaan’s breakout hit “Only Man She Wants.”\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/167086100236599296", "annotation_id": "6345572", "url": "https://genius.com/6345572", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Man, that was when Ethan was pushin’ a Subaru hatchback", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "These last 8 lines of the first verse is Drake reminiscing on a time long ago, before he blew up and became a face in the rap industry. To convey how different it was, Drake is referring to when his friend Ethan was driving around in a Subaru hatchback, a lame-ass car compared to the whips he has now. Not to say Drake himself didn’t have humble beginnings", "annotation_id": "5428225", "url": "https://genius.com/5428225", "state": "verified", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Had a job sellin’ Girbaud jeans", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Girbraud jeans” -> “Girbaud jeans”", "annotation_id": "4988296", "url": "https://genius.com/4988296", "state": "verified", "votes": 12, "second_sense": false, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I’m turnin’ into a nigga that thinks about money and women Like 24/7, that’s where my life took me That’s just how shit happened to go And you know Runnin’ through the 6 with my, with my", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Conventional grammar structures usually prescribe the use of “that” in relation to objects, not people. Drake’s choice of the word “that” instead of the word “who” could indicate that he considers his constant preoccupation with “money and women” a negative trait, perhaps even something subhuman.", "annotation_id": "4981981", "url": "https://genius.com/4981981", "state": "verified", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Yuh can jus suck yuh mada!! With no apology,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "‘'Suck yuh mada/mother’‘ is a highly offensive curse phrase in Jamaica. It suggests you perform oral sex on your own mother. Since it mentions the person being dissed and their mother, it’s essentially like two insults in one.\n\nSuck yuh madda is commonly used in Jamaica and can accurately ‘translate’ to “fuck off” in English slang. In this context, Popcaan’s saying that if you don’t appreciate OVO/Chromatic, you can go fuck yourself.", "annotation_id": "4964946", "url": "https://genius.com/4964946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "This is that nasty flow Top boy in this shit, I’m so international", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/Drake/status/520066319773745153", "annotation_id": "4944172", "url": "https://genius.com/4944172", "state": "verified", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "And if a boy nuh like OVO or Chromatic", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Chromatic is a 5-man crew of Jamaican Sound System DJs. They have a tight connection with Drake and the OVO crew; the audio at the beginning of this song is from a trip the OVO crew took to Jamaica to hang out with Chromatic, Popcaan, and others. Chromatic is also mentioned in Drake’s thank you letter for the album.", "annotation_id": "4939229", "url": "https://genius.com/4939229", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Man, that was when Ethan was pushin’ a Subaru hatchback", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Subaru Hatchback is likely referring to the ever popular WRX wagon. The impreza WRX, having hit the U.S. and Canada Market by storm in 2002, was offered in a sedan and hatchback model. The vehicles were capable of 200 + horsepower stock on a flat-four boxer engine. Being rather affordable and offering substantial “bang for your buck”, Ethan clearly made a very wise choice for a whip…", "annotation_id": "4937349", "url": "https://genius.com/4937349", "state": "verified", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I’m turnin’ into a nigga that thinks about money and women Like 24/7, that’s where my life took me That’s just how shit happened to go And you know Runnin’ through the 6 with my, with my", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake has talked upon the topics of money and women ever since his So Far Gone days. He even had hearts and dollar bills used on the cover of the mixtape.", "annotation_id": "4936666", "url": "https://genius.com/4936666", "state": "verified", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Man, that was when Ethan was pushin’ a Subaru hatchback", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Homeboy was driving a beater compared to what they can afford now which is a reoccurring theme of referencing how he started from the bottom because most think drizzy started out rich before fame.", "annotation_id": "4936082", "url": "https://genius.com/4936082", "state": "verified", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Man, that was when Ethan was pushin’ a Subaru hatchback", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Ethan is a friend who’s been with Drake since he started from the bottom.\n\nSubaru is a Japanese automobile brand. In Canada, Subaru’s are the 14th most popular car brand; the Impreza, which can come as a hatchback, is its most popular model. Drizzy can afford better cars now.", "annotation_id": "4936081", "url": "https://genius.com/4936081", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "With my woes", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I’m gonna offer a rebuttal to this lyric. So far those who I’ve discussed the song with believe that “woes” is his New Orleans homage to whoadie, but I believe it’s less a reference to Weezy as it is simply his woes. Drake has always been known for his emotional output on his songs, wearing his heart on his sleeve, his trouble with current bitches and past relationships, so he’s running thoo the 6 with his troubles, the blues that illuminate his life. It’s a handsomely ambiguous lyric.", "annotation_id": "4935379", "url": "https://genius.com/4935379", "state": "verified", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "With my woes", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/OVO40/status/566096453186363393\n\nDrake was really out in his city grinding with his crew before he made it to the level of stardom he is at now.\n\nIn this case “woes” translates to his friends. It is short for whoadie, a Southern slang term for “friend”. Fellow Toronto rapper Devontée has said that “woe” also stands for “working on excellence”.\n\nDrake and his squad knew exactly what their goals in life were, and aimed to achieve them. Obviously they achieved a great level of success since Drake’s newest project sold a little over 500k in a week.", "annotation_id": "4935378", "url": "https://genius.com/4935378", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes Countin’ money, you know how it goes", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "According to Noisey Interview of Devontée (OVO Sound affiliate) WOES= working on excellence\n\n\n\nExcerpt from Interview: http://noisey.vice.com/en_ca/blog/devontee-woe-interview-2014\n\nWhat is Woe?\nWoe is my crew. It stands for “working on excellence.” It’s just my whole brand and my whole movement and my way of life for everyone. I want everyone to work on excellence. So, all my friends are my Woes and I feel anybody working on excellence in life is a Woe in life as well.", "annotation_id": "4932510", "url": "https://genius.com/4932510", "state": "verified", "votes": 143, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I ain’t rockin’ my jewelry, that’s on purpose", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a reference to when Drake was robbed at gunpoint in Toronto in 2009.  He references this incident on “The Resistance” on his debut album ‘Thank Me Later.’ https://genius.com/18187/Drake-the-resistance/Maybe-it-was-the-fast-paced-switch-up-or-the-two-guns-in-my-face-durin-the-stick-up-maybe-cause-a-girl-i-thought-i-trusted-was-who-set-the-whole-shit-up\n\nDrake has confirmed that it is a true story in a radio interview. http://youtu.be/0dnRRScx-Gw?t=2m56s", "annotation_id": "4923131", "url": "https://genius.com/4923131", "state": "verified", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I’ve always been me, I guess I know myself Shakiness, man, I don’t have no time for that", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Quintessential aphorism, Drake has always maintained his strength of Character despite his rise to fame.\n\n“Shakiness” is modern Jamaican slang used to refer to instability or uncertainty in a situation. In this context he just doesn’t have time for fuckery.", "annotation_id": "4917407", "url": "https://genius.com/4917407", "state": "verified", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I know a nigga named Johnny Bling He put me on to the finer things", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Most likely a reference to Johnny Dang, aka TV Johnny, aka “The King of Bling,” a legendary jeweler from Houston, Texas. Here is a picture of Drake and Johnny. Also pictured is another famous Johnny…", "annotation_id": "4917099", "url": "https://genius.com/4917099", "state": "verified", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I’m way up, I stay up, I’m two up, I’m three up I had to get back to you, woah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I’m two up should be “I’m too up”. He then says “three up” to refer to the double meaning too/two, saying he’s even more than “too up”.", "annotation_id": "4912112", "url": "https://genius.com/4912112", "state": "verified", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I know a nigga named Johnny Bling He put me on to the finer things", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Most likely referring to the famous jeweler TV Johnny. Well know for having many famous clients. http://www.tvjohnny.net", "annotation_id": "4905029", "url": "https://genius.com/4905029", "state": "verified", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Man, I’m talkin’ way before hashtags I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes (Yeah!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This isn’t the first time Drake is saying he’s been at it before hashtags and Twitter names https://genius.com/3271781/Drake-0-to-100-the-catch-up/", "annotation_id": "4904731", "url": "https://genius.com/4904731", "state": "verified", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "https://www.thefader.com/2015/02/13/what-does-drake-really-mean-when-he-talks-about-his-woe\n\nDrake talks about what his woes mean here.", "annotation_id": "4904157", "url": "https://genius.com/4904157", "state": "verified", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "My city too turned up, I’ll take the fine for that This been where you find me at That’s been where you find me at", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "He’s also saying that he’ll take the credit for the hype surrounding his city.", "annotation_id": "4899681", "url": "https://genius.com/4899681", "state": "verified", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "it supposed to be “no sleepin in the streets” niko said this to drake in the “jungle” video\nhttp://youtu.be/0lKH5dMNcq0?t=13m1s", "annotation_id": "4899261", "url": "https://genius.com/4899261", "state": "verified", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The hook does not say “You know how that shit go” He is saying “You know how that should go” Listen to it carefully. I also have the clean version of the Album it does not get edited out because he’s not saying “shit”. Also for more clarification Noah “40” Shebib tweets the Line on his Twitter https://twitter.com/OVO40/status/566096453186363393\nOnly In the 5th Line in the Verse 2 he says “You know how that shit go”\n\nAlso Woes DOES NOT! mean working on Excellence its a south slang word Woadie Meaning “my Friend” “My Nigga” for example. So he took a short form of it and said woes. Running through the 6 with my working on excellence makes 0 sense.", "annotation_id": "4898338", "url": "https://genius.com/4898338", "state": "verified", "votes": 644, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Sleeping in this case refers to not knowing what is going on, not actual sleeping. He’s saying that you don’t really know what goes on in these streets.", "annotation_id": "4897823", "url": "https://genius.com/4897823", "state": "verified", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Man, I’m talkin’ way before hashtags I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes (Yeah!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is really conscious of his life before stardom and everything that he went through to get to where he is now. Unlike other artists who try to rewrite their own history (being classy mafia leaders in the NY projects), Drake accepts and relishes all that he did to get where he is today. A lot of fans really like the honest approach to life that is unique in Drake’s music", "annotation_id": "4896884", "url": "https://genius.com/4896884", "state": "verified", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I’ve always been me, I guess I know myself Shakiness, man, I don’t have no time for that", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake hasn’t change who he is for anyone, he’s always maintained his strength of character despite his rise to fame. As he said back on “Say What’s Real”:\n\nDon’t listen to anybody that knew me because to have known me would mean there’s a new me and if you think I changed in the slightest could’ve fooled me\n\n“Shakiness” is modern Jamaican slang that refers to instability or uncertainty in a situation.", "annotation_id": "4895453", "url": "https://genius.com/4895453", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "*Machine gun shots*", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The gunshots refer to the intro of the albums previous song “Energy,” where the DJ tells us gunshots mean the song will be rewound. This is why we hear the song’s hook when these shots finish.\n\nThis also relates to Popcaan’s earlier statement in the outro that “if you diss you will get execute”. Shots will pop off if you speak ill of the OVO crew.", "annotation_id": "4895362", "url": "https://genius.com/4895362", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Hol’ it yute, hol’ it, hol’ it, hol’ it, hol’ it, hol’ it No sleepin’ in the streets! Shaky warrior", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This part of the intro is taken from a clip of the OVO crew in Jamaica. The person is speaking in Patois. “Hol it” means pay attention. He grabs the attention of a man passed out on the corner and tells him he can’t sleep in the streets. Check out the 3:38 mark:\n\nhttps://vimeo.com/116727998\n\n“Shaky Warrior” means someone is acting suspect. It’s also a song by Jamaican artist Popcaan, who appears on the outro of this track.", "annotation_id": "4894270", "url": "https://genius.com/4894270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 207, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "This is that nasty flow Top boy in this shit, I’m so international", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Top Boy was a British television drama that aired from 2011 to 2013. Drake, who was once on a TV Drama himself, is a pretty big fan of the show.\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/520066319773745153\n\nThe TV series was shown not only in Britain but also the United States, making it international like Drake, who is Canadian, but is quite popular all over the world.", "annotation_id": "4892756", "url": "https://genius.com/4892756", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Had a job sellin’ Girbaud jeans", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake reminisces about the days when his hustle was a more traditional job; selling jeans made by Marithé + François Girbaud, an international clothing company based in France that was very popular throughout the 1990’s.", "annotation_id": "4892742", "url": "https://genius.com/4892742", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Boi-1da, what’s poppin’? Yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A quick shoutout to Toronto-bred producer Boi-1da who is Drake’s good friend and OVO label-mate. He also produced this track along with SkySense & Vinylz.\n\n\n\nBoi-1da is pictured left.", "annotation_id": "4892670", "url": "https://genius.com/4892670", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "My nigga Jibba, he whip it, I ride in the passenger", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Much like his other homie Ethan, Drake and Jibba (shown in the Jungle premier video) drive or whip it around Toronto until the brakes fall off.", "annotation_id": "4890971", "url": "https://genius.com/4890971", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "...Shell out, all if yuh eye nuh big Always haffi look out, yuh no know if yuh head book out", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Popcaan’s singing the intro to his song “Ghetto” off his debut album Where We Come From:\n\nAlways haffi look out\nYuh no know fi yuh head book out\n\nPopcaan is advising you to keep your head on a swivel. “Book out” can mean to that your head is sticking out in the light due to your popularity or that people have put money on your head like they would put money on the books or records.", "annotation_id": "4889471", "url": "https://genius.com/4889471", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Pray the real live forever, man Pray the fakes get exposed", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake expresses a similar sentiment to A$AP Rocky’s banger “Multiply” with a strike at the many rappers who have lied about their past experiences. Drake explains on “You & The 6”:\n\nCause I’m not acting tough\nOr making stories up ‘bout where I’m actually from\n\n\n\nThis lyric became rather ironic when Quentin Miller’s reference track was leaked during the Meek Mill vs Drake beef.", "annotation_id": "4889377", "url": "https://genius.com/4889377", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I know a nigga named Johnny Bling He put me on to the finer things", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A reference to a childhood friend, Jonny Bling. His love of gold, diamonds, etc. rubbed off on Drake at an early age.\n\nSome have suggested that this is a reference to Johnny “Bling” Dang, aka TV Johnny, a legendary jeweler from Houston, Texas, although Dang has never been known as “Johnny Bling.”\n\n\n\n Dang is in the middle, with another famous Johnny and OVO affiliate on the right.", "annotation_id": "4889239", "url": "https://genius.com/4889239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Man, I’m talkin’ way before hashtags I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes (Yeah!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "All the events Drake discusses take place in an era before the world was consumed by smartphones and hashtags (pre-2007). He made a similar reference on “0 to 100/The Catch Up”:\n\nMe and Noel been at it before Twitter names", "annotation_id": "4889039", "url": "https://genius.com/4889039", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes Countin’ money, you know how it goes", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "416 and 647 are two of three area codes for the city of Toronto. Referring to Toronto as “the 6” is something Drake invented and was not a widely used metonym before he tried to popularize it. Torontonians did not react positively.\n\n“Woes” is an abbreviation of the word “Woadie,” which is New Orleans slang for people from the same ward (which sounds like “WHOA-dee” when pronounced with a New Orleans accent). Drake’s home city of Toronto is divided into 44 wards, each with its own city councillor so even though the slang is southern, it still makes sense for the 6.\n\nFellow Toronto rapper and OVO affiliate Devontée has said that “woe” also stands for “working on excellence,” although Drake has never confirmed it.", "annotation_id": "4888996", "url": "https://genius.com/4888996", "state": "accepted", "votes": 460, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "My city too turned up, I’ll take the fine for that This been where you find me at That’s been where you find me at", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake cracks a joke about the the NBA fining him $25,000 for allegedly recruiting Kevin Durant to the Toronto Raptors. During the 2014 OVO Fest, Drake, the Raptors' “Global Ambassador,” suggested Durant come play for his hometown team. The team had the option of forcing Drake to step down from his Ambassador position or take the fine, so the team took the fine.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ECk4v7pSsZs", "annotation_id": "4888846", "url": "https://genius.com/4888846", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Niggas want my spot and don’t deserve it I don’t like how serious they take themselves", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "After dropping hit after hit, Drake’s solidified his position as king of the rap game. He knows people are coming to replace him but he feels that none of them are worthy of his spot.\n\n\nIf I ain't the greatest then I'm headed for it― “0 to 100/The Catch Up” by Drake", "annotation_id": "4888823", "url": "https://genius.com/4888823", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I’m way up, I stay up, I’m two up, I’m three up I had to get back to you, woah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "While people often try to get “one up” on others, Drake is three up on his competition. This could be referring to the number of studio albums Drake had released to that date.\n\n\n\nThe phrase “way up, stay up” may come from a Chi Ching Ching song by that title. Popcaan, who is on this song’s outro, is featured on that track.", "annotation_id": "4888709", "url": "https://genius.com/4888709", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Yeah, this that Oliver, 40, Niko shit man 15 Fort York shit, y’know?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "15 Fort York Blvd is the condo in Toronto where Drake’s longtime friend and producer 40 used to live. It’s also where Drake’s first projects were put together.\n\n\n\nOliver El-Khatib is the creative director and brand manager of OVO, while Niko is Drake’s right hand man and responsible for OVO’s merchandise. Niko and Drake have been friends since Drake was on Degrassi. They both helped Drake find OVO.", "annotation_id": "4888303", "url": "https://genius.com/4888303", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I might declare it a holiday as soon as Baka get back on the road Yeah, but you know how that shit go They so irrational, they don’t wanna patch it up They wanna mash it up, woah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Baka is Drake’s friend who reps Scarborough, Toronto’s eastern region. He was charged for Human Trafficking in June 2014 and served an 11 month sentence.\n\nBaka tweeted “#knowyourself” in May 2014, so this song may be paying homage to him, or Drake may have been cooking this tape since that time.\nhttps://twitter.com/BakaAKAnotnice/status/472184374822060033\n\nIn April 2015, Baka was released from jail. While there was no indication that Drake actually did declare that day a holiday, there were 11 prayer hand emojis in the Instagram post announcing his friend’s return.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/1MTDldjQPk/?taken-by=champagnepapi", "annotation_id": "4888232", "url": "https://genius.com/4888232", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I ain’t rockin’ my jewelry, that’s on purpose", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake isn’t showing off his jewelry because his wealth speaks for itself. He doesn’t need to brag, but does anyway – note the pun on “rockin'” (jewelry is diamond-encrusted), that’s on purpose.\n\nHe’s grown considerably since he “Started From the Bottom”:\n\nI wear every single chain, even when I’m in the house cause we…\n\nIt should also be noted Drake was once robbed at gunpoint in Toronto in 2009. He refers to the incident on “The Resistance,” from his debut album Thank Me Later.", "annotation_id": "4888203", "url": "https://genius.com/4888203", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Don’t fuck with them niggas, they too irrational, woah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This lyric, and culminating hook is about Drake and his long time friends, particularly those from Scarborough. He only messes with his close friends, sticking to his “no new friends” policy from 2013.", "annotation_id": "4888187", "url": "https://genius.com/4888187", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I’ve always been me, I guess I know myself Shakiness, man, I don’t have no time for that", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake hasn’t change who he is for anyone. This can be referenced back to his “Say What’s Real” line “don’t listen to anybody that knew me because to have known me would mean there’s a new me and if you think I changed in the slightest could’ve fooled me”", "annotation_id": "4888185", "url": "https://genius.com/4888185", "state": "verified", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "Reps up is in here Got P Reign and Chubby and TJ and Winnie and woah Yeah, and you know how that shit go", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Reps Up Entertainment is the Toronto record label Drake’s homeboy and OVO affiliate P Reign is signed.\n\nChubby or Chubbs is Drake’s bodyguard. He literally plays Drake’s right hand man in the “Hold On We’re Going Home” video as he is mostly Drake’s right side for the video.\n\nWinnie is a member of Reps Up Records and is a close affiliate of Drake’s. He appears in the “Energy” music video alongside Drake as he raps the line:\n\nY’all don’t wanna see Win Win, 50 or Whoa\n\n\n\nPictured left", "annotation_id": "4888162", "url": "https://genius.com/4888162", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I’m turnin’ into a nigga that thinks about money and women Like 24/7, that’s where my life took me That’s just how shit happened to go And you know Runnin’ through the 6 with my, with my", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Its interesting that Drake is only just now admitting to thinking about money and women, since both have been subjects in Drake’s music since his So Far Gone days. He even had hearts and dollar bills on the cover of the mixtape.", "annotation_id": "4887973", "url": "https://genius.com/4887973", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I was runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go Runnin’ through the 6 with my woes You know how that shit go You know how that shit go You know how that shit go", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "https://twitter.com/OVO40/status/566096453186363393\n\nDrake was really out in his city (Toronto’s area codes are 416 and 647) grinding with his crew before he made it to the level of stardom he is at now. Toronto is also divided into six boroughs (like New York City) and include Old Toronto, York, North York, East York, Etobicoke and Scarborough.\n\n“Woes” is an abbreviation of the word “Woadie,” which is New Orleans slang for people from the same ward (which sounds like “WHOA-dee” when pronounced with a New Orleans accent). Drake’s home city of Toronto is divided into 44 wards, each with its own city councillor so even though the slang is southern, it still makes sense for the 6.\n\nFellow Toronto rapper and OVO affiliate Devontée has said that “woe” also stands for “working on excellence,” although Drake has never confirmed it.", "annotation_id": "4887951", "url": "https://genius.com/4887951", "state": "accepted", "votes": 431, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I want that Ferrari, then I swerve I want that Bugatti, just to hurt", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake wants to buy a Ferrari and Bugatti just make an impression on his haters. Since Drizzy’s worth about $75 million, he has more than enough money to blow on these lavish vehicles, which cost up to $3 million and $2.7 million respectively.", "annotation_id": "4887895", "url": "https://genius.com/4887895", "state": "accepted", "votes": 178, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "k", "line": "I had a yellow TechnoMarine Then Kanye dropped, it was polos and backpacks", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "TechnoMarine is a brand of watch that was popular with rappers in the early 2000s. Kanye refers to them as outdated on “Last Call.”\n\nKanye went onto popularize the Ralph Lauren Polo and backpack “prep-rapper” look when he (and The College Dropout) blew up in 2004.\n\nOn his Chicago tour stop of the Aubrey & the Three Migos Tour promoting his fifth album Scorpion, Drake performed this lyric as “Kanye flopped” rather than “Kanye dropped.” Kanye and Drake both released albums in June 2018: ye and Scorpion respectively. Both albums debuted at number 1 on the Billboard 200, but Kanye bowed with a paltry 208,000 album-equivalent units compared to Drake’s 732,000. Prior to this, Drake had been taking shots at Kanye over what he perceived to be a betrayal since immediately after the release of Pusha-T’s DAYTONA, notably on “Duppy Freestyle” and “Behind Barz.”\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EdvqKuzc4r8", "annotation_id": "4887766", "url": "https://genius.com/4887766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 167, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
