{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tommorow’s Today", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-tommorows-today-lyrics", "genius_title": "Tommorow’s Today"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4422", "line": "I’m runnin’, but can’t run away You say you changed But you never change up", "vault_lines": ["I'm runnin', but can't run away", "You say you changed", "But you never change up"], "meaning": "“I’m runnin', but can’t run away”\nTo breakdown what Sampha refers to here is that he cannot escape the person he tries to escape because he tries to leave but remembers the good times and contemplates whether he made a mistake leaving them or not. We can see that he is conflicted between right and left; good and bad. He has to make the decision of whether he wants to go back to this person.\n\n“You say you changed. But you never change up.”\nHere we can infer that Sampha has been told by this person that they have changed but he implies that they contradict themselves because they, quote on quote, ‘never change up’ therefore leaving Sampha to believe that he is being tricked into this as the person claims they never switch up or backstab. This also leaves Sampha in the zone of balance. He does not know if this person truly changes or stays the same therefore he can run, but he cannot run away.\n\nThis metaphor is used to describe a situation where he cannot depict whether this person really changes or not, it leaves him in a place of ambiguity. The reason this is a metaphor is that Sampha is comparing the running but not running away to the person changing although the person claims they never change up.\n\nThis tells us that the person could be lying to Sampha and could be betraying Sampha once again.", "annotation_id": "22650755", "url": "https://genius.com/22650755", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4422", "line": "I’m runnin’, but can’t run away You say you changed But you never change up", "vault_lines": ["I'm runnin', but can't run away", "You say you changed", "But you never change up"], "meaning": "“I’m runnin', but can’t run away”\nTo breakdown what Sampha refers to here is that he cannot escape the person he tries to escape because he tries to leave but remembers the good times and contemplates whether he made a mistake leaving them or not. We can see that he is conflicted between right and left; good and bad. He has to make the decision of whether he wants to go back to this person.\n\n“You say you changed. But you never change up.”\nHere we can infer that Sampha has been told by this person that they have changed but he implies that they contradict themselves because they, quote on quote, ‘never change up’ therefore leaving Sampha to believe that he is being tricked into this as the person claims they never switch up or backstab. This also leaves Sampha in the zone of balance. He does not know if this person truly changes or stays the same therefore he can run, but he cannot run away.\n\nThis metaphor is used to describe a situation where he cannot depict whether this person really changes or not, it leaves him in a place of ambiguity. The reason this is a metaphor is that Sampha is comparing the running but not running away to the person changing although the person claims they never change up.\n\nThis tells us that the person could be lying to Sampha and could be betraying Sampha once again.", "annotation_id": "22650706", "url": "https://genius.com/22650706", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4422", "line": "I know I fear trust I know I fear fear too much On my plate, lookin’ up Outer space, Pearly Gates", "vault_lines": ["Outer space, Pearly Gates", "I know I fear trust", "I know I fear fear too much", "On my plate, lookin' up"], "meaning": "Sampha lost his dad to lung cancer and his mother to stomach cancer. His brother had a stroke right before his mother was diagnosed. He had a lot on his plate while trying to maintain his music career.\n\nSampha has also been studying Tibetan Buddhism. Its message of accepting death resonates with him the most. He contrasts “outer space” with “Pearly Gates,” which signifies that he accepts death but is unsure whether the life thereafter is a meaningless void or a physical place of bliss where he’ll see his loved ones again.", "annotation_id": "11551230", "url": "https://genius.com/11551230", "state": "accepted", "votes": 142, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4422", "line": "44, 22 You build it up to break it halfway through Just make the call, 22 But you’re just the same as I ever knew 44, 22 You build it up to break it halfway through Just make the call, 22 But you’re just the same as I ever knew 44, 22 You build it up to break it halfway through Just make the call, 22 But you’re just the same as I ever knew 44, 22 You build it up to break it halfway through Just make the call, 22 But you’re just the same as I ever knew 44, 22 You build it up to break it halfway through Just make the call, 22 But you’re just the same as I ever knew", "vault_lines": ["But you never change up", "You build it up to break it halfway through", "Just make the call, 22", "But you're just the same as I ever knew"], "meaning": "“44” is the country code for the United Kingdom, where Sampha was born, and “22” is the area code for Freetown, Sierra Leone.  Sampha’s family is originally from Sierra Leone.\n\nSampha travels from the United Kingdom (44) to Freetown (22) in a short film for his album Process. He promoted it in a tweet:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/sampha/status/827292457599717376\n\nThese lines are about commitment issues. It may be a long distance relationship with someone in Freetown. He could call, but his fear of trust holds him back.\n\nOther theories about these lyrics include that 22 is simply half of 44, and that this symbolizes the halfway marker of the relationship, or that the numbers are a reference to Isaiah 44:22, which speaks on forgiveness. If the person he’s addressing has the commitment issue, then he forgives them.", "annotation_id": "11533023", "url": "https://genius.com/11533023", "state": "accepted", "votes": 413, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "4422", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 4, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-and-sampha-4422-lyrics", "genius_title": "4422"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bullets Don’t Tickle*", "line": "I ain’t playin’ my nigga, go ahead and giggle But bullets don’t tickle If you think you playin’ with Elmo, hell no", "vault_lines": ["But bullets don't tickle", "If you think you playin' with Elmo, hell no", "I ain't playin' my nigga, go ahead and giggle"], "meaning": "Drake references the character Elmo from the television show Sesame Street. In particular, Drake references the Tickle Me Elmo doll which is most commonly known for its giggle.\n\nDrake makes it known that he has bullets for the opposition and his threats are not a laughing matter.", "annotation_id": "19601113", "url": "https://genius.com/19601113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
