{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "Ohhh, ohhh", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "PND sample or backing vocals?", "annotation_id": "19705625", "url": "https://genius.com/19705625", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "Yeah, remember you was livin’ at The London for a month Service elevator up to 4201 We was still a secret, couldn’t come in through the front", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The London is a luxury hotel in New York City. Drake has spent time in the city before, even has titled a song about it, so it’s likely that he has stayed there for a night or two.\n\nDrake might take the service elevator, opposed to the main elevator, up to his girl’s floor to avoid being seen. The London has 54 floors so 4201 is likely referring to a room 01 on the 42nd floor.\n\n\n\nAround 2014 Drake was rumoured to be in a relationship with Rihanna. It’s possible he’s reminiscing about the times they had together.\n\nThe lyrics have been slightly altered from the leaked version.", "annotation_id": "9072951", "url": "https://genius.com/9072951", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "And on my way to make this dough A lot of niggas cut the check so they can take this flow A lot of niggas cut the check so they can take this flow", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Of the entire song, Drake chooses to put his greatest emphasis on these lines indicating their importance.\n\nA 6ix God feature costs a lot. In fact, it’s rumoured to be upwards of $200,000.\n\nHe’s stated how he’s “done doing favours for people, cause it ain’t like I need the money I make off a feature,” and he’s certainly taking it to heart. In 2015 and 2016, the only time you’d see Drake on a feature was for a friend, like for Future, Rihanna and Big Sean.\n\nAdditionally, cutting a check to take someone’s flow refers to ghostwriting. Drake has ghostwritten for Dr. Dre, Diddy, and Alicia Keys, among others. This is likely a response to the ghostwriting allegations, saying he in fact does write his own stuff, and even writes for other people.\n\nLate last year there was a leak of this same song where the song cuts off at the 3rd verse. The snippet was named “Views from the 6” and started off the same way this song starts off. Drake knew about the leak so he changed it. “Take the flow” could be referring to this incident, and possible a reason he changed the song around.", "annotation_id": "9070860", "url": "https://genius.com/9070860", "state": "accepted", "votes": 286, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "I wanna know how much time you spent on them paragraphs Where you get at me All that grey in our conversation history, you Playin’ mind games, when you sayin’ things Playin’ mime games, we both doin’ the same thing", "vault_lines": ["You love me and I love you"], "meaning": "In the iPhone’s iMessage app, grey text bubbles display the messages you’ve received. This girl is clearly bombarding Drake with well-crafted messages to play “mind games” with him but isn’t getting many responses in return, hence all of the grey on Drake’s screen. “Grey in our conversation” also refers to the state of their relationship. It’s not black & white (straightforward), it’s a grey area (uncertain).\n\nThe “mime games” line is a pun. Drake refers to the mime’s trick of imitating exactly what the other subject does. They’re both playing mind games with each other.", "annotation_id": "9070842", "url": "https://genius.com/9070842", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "That’s for sure though, I made a career off reminiscin’ Time got a funny way of tickin’, things are so much different I’ll admit it, I’ve admitted to a lot of things", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In his albums Take Care and Nothing Was The Same, and especially in songs like “Look What You’ve Done,” “From Time,” and “Furthest Thing,” Drake reminisces on past relationships and experiences.\n\nHis constant honesty throughout his career has made him admit various things and earned him a reputation of being soft. Among the things Drake has admitted, is his love for labelmate Nicki Minaj. As he said on his song “Miss Me”:\n\nI love Nicki Minaj, I told her I’d admit it\nI hope one day we get married just to say we fucking did it\nAnd girl I’m fucking serious, I’m with it if you with it\n\nNow Drake’s moved on to reminiscing about reminiscing.\n\nThis line is similar to the one he delivers in “6 God” where he constantly repeats:\n\nI’ll admit it, I’ll admit it\n\nDrake’s now become completely self aware to be able to admit all of his issues.", "annotation_id": "9070839", "url": "https://genius.com/9070839", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "LOLOL, I’m glad you find this shit amusin’", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Lol” is a common phrase used to express laughter. It seems the girl Drake’s sending DM’s to isn’t taking him seriously. Drizzy sarcastically states that he’s glad she’s finding his efforts to rekindle their love amusing.\n\nThe way Drake delivers “LOLOL” also sounds like “Lol, oh well.”", "annotation_id": "9070821", "url": "https://genius.com/9070821", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "And my house is the definition Of alcohol and weed addiction", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s house is never without marijuana or alcohol. This becomes a double entendre as they could be at his house to entertain his guests or help Drake deal with his inner issues. As he said in “You & The 6”:\n\nNever get sloppy drunk, but alcohol is problem solving", "annotation_id": "9070748", "url": "https://genius.com/9070748", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "Act like you know it, fuck them stories, fuck the shade they throwin’ Understand I got responsibilities to people that I need", "vault_lines": ["I say: \"Fuck you, I hate you"], "meaning": "Drake’s telling this girl to not believe in the false allegations they say about him.\n\nHe faced a lot of controversy after Meek Mill accused him of not writing his own lyrics. After all the beef served in 2015, Drake was considered the winner. Drake’s girl doesn’t need to worry because it doesn’t matter what they say or what they do, nobody is on Drake’s level.", "annotation_id": "9070724", "url": "https://genius.com/9070724", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "We both slide on a late night Do things in our off time We both, yeah Made some mistakes, pon road Yeah, how’s that for real?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake admits that both he and this girl “slide” a.k.a pursue other people when they’re not seeing each other, or in their “off time.” Drake even admits this may have been a mistake.\n\n“Pon” means “on” in Patois, meaning they both made mistakes on the road.", "annotation_id": "9070397", "url": "https://genius.com/9070397", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "I tell ’em they belong to me, that goes on for forever And I think we just get closer when we not together", "vault_lines": ["You love me and I love you", "And tell me that you're sorry"], "meaning": "Playing on the saying “distance makes the heart grow fonder,” Drake is more comfortable with his exes when they’re distanced from him—Drizzy’s always felt possessive of all his past significant others.\n\nSince he’s attached to these girls, he tells them that it’s better to keep distance given that he’d mess up and lose them again if they got back together.", "annotation_id": "9070338", "url": "https://genius.com/9070338", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "Tryna give your ass the world You runnin’ your fingers through my curls You knew me when the kid had waves But that’s enough of that You could never say I came up and forgot about your ass And that’s some real shit", "vault_lines": ["You love me and I love you", "I say: \"Fuck you, I hate you"], "meaning": "Drake shows his frustration with the girl he’s addressing. It seems like she’s blind to what he’s done for her. He’s always been giving her all she wants but he’s never had anything in return.\n\nShe seems to have known Drizzy for a while now since he mentions the waves he had on the cover of his 2007 mixtape Comeback Season, all the way to the hairstyle of curls he had between 2012 and 2013.\n\n\n\nDrake’s had enough of all the friendly flirting and wants a serious relationship. And he might have a real shot since even after all the success and fame he has received, he still hasn’t forgotten about her.\n\nThese lines were originally written by PARTYNEXTDOOR with intentions of appearing on Drake’s “Legend,” as heard on PND’s “Legend (Demo).”", "annotation_id": "9070282", "url": "https://genius.com/9070282", "state": "accepted", "votes": 162, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "Cuts too deep for a band-aid solution We too deep in this thing, you’ll never lose me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Playing on “deep,” Drake describes the magnitude of emotions he’s feeling.\n\nHis pain is heartfelt and stinging and can’t simply be healed by a band-aid. Yet, he feels too invested in the relationship to leave now. It’s a push and pull in the dance of lovers.\n\nCuts was an American sitcom that featured Marques Houston, mentioned in the previous line.", "annotation_id": "9070263", "url": "https://genius.com/9070263", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "You got a different vision You wanna walk around naked in the kitchen Without runnin’ into one of my niggas That’s not the way we livin’ Too much goin’ on, it’s just not realistic", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "As we found out in the promo clip of Drake receiving his Grammy for Take Care, Drake’s YOLO Estate has enough rooms to accommodate all his entourage, so he does in fact keep his family close.\n\nThis girl wants an intimate relationship with Drake, to the point where the two of them can live alone together and she can be as comfortable as she likes. However, this isn’t possible because of Drake’s lifestyle. This portrays the tension between his music life and romantic life.\n\nDrake rapped something similar on his single “Trophies”:\n\n“She like ‘I heard all your niggas stay where you stay’ House so big I haven’t seen them boys in 2 days”\n\nDrizzy is also alluding to a statement by NY Giants receiver Odell Beckham, a close friend and house guest of Drake’s during the album creation:\n\n“He needs to chill out with that house he’s got out there, it’s just too much going on”", "annotation_id": "9070137", "url": "https://genius.com/9070137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "It’s like a lot of games bein’ played How’s it goin’ down? If it’s on ’til we gone, then I gots to know now Is you wit’ me or what? Yeah It’s like a lot of games are bein’ played How’s it goin’ down? If it’s on ’til we gone, then I gots to know now Is you wit’ me or what? Yeah", "vault_lines": ["You love me and I love you"], "meaning": "This hook is taken directly from DMX’s hook on “How’s It Goin' Down” and it’s delivered in a similar flow. “How’s it Goin' Down” is also about an affair, so Drake may be alluding to some type of infidelity by the girl.\n\nDrake and his girl are having serious trust issues—he doesn’t see how their relationship is progressing and is doubtful his girl is as committed to the relationship as she should be. He needs to know if his girl will ride for him or if they should end the relationship without wasting anymore of each other’s time.\n\nDrake’s unsure where his relationship is going, he is falling out of touch with the girl and believes that their love is fading.\n\nLike he says in his song “Jungle”:\n\nAre you down? Are you down? Are you down?\nAre you down for the cause?\nAre you down? Are you down? Are you down?\nAre you down for the cause?\nYou still down? You still down? You still down?", "annotation_id": "9070090", "url": "https://genius.com/9070090", "state": "accepted", "votes": 200, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "[Verse 3: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Using the same format as “Summer Sixteen” and “0 to 100 / The Catch Up,” Noah “40” Shebib switches the beat once again.\n\nThe lyrics in verse 3 are eerily similar to those that were leaked on August of 2014, originally thought to be for a song titled “Views From the 6.” While there are distinct differences in the lyrics, the flow and subject matter is very similar.", "annotation_id": "9070083", "url": "https://genius.com/9070083", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "Slide on a late night You like to slide on a late night You sent a “Are you here?” text without an invite That’s that shit that I don’t like", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a reference to Chief Keef’s song “I Don’t Like.”\n\n\n\nDrake plays with the texting theme in the song, while pointing out that this woman only likes to meet with him after hours. She’s sliding into his messages late at night in hopes of sliding into his bed.", "annotation_id": "9070030", "url": "https://genius.com/9070030", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "Girl, I had your back when all you used to do was front", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake plays on “front” and “back” to explain how he remained completely loyal and faithful to his girl; however, she only played games and maintained a dishonest relationship.\n\nHe also plays off “couldn’t come in through the front” from the previous line.", "annotation_id": "9070027", "url": "https://genius.com/9070027", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "I know you heard that my girl is sponsored by Audemars That’s why she always correctin’ me when my time is off", "vault_lines": ["You love me and I love you"], "meaning": "This is an implicit reference to the rumors about Drake and Serena Williams, who is sponsored by Audemars Piguet, a manufacturer of luxury watches.\n\n\n\nDrake plays with Serena’s sponsorship, saying that she’s always correcting him when his timing is off. We’re sure he does the same when her timing is off on the court.\n\nDrake also mentioned Serena in his 2013 hit “Worst Behavior”:\n\nI’m with my whole set, tennis matches at the crib\nI swear I could beat Serena when she playin' with her left\n\nDrizzy could be innocently talking about playing tennis with the legend, or “tennis match” could be a euphemism for the sexy-time that also happens “at [his] crib.”", "annotation_id": "9070022", "url": "https://genius.com/9070022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "On some DMX shit I group DM my exes", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Aubrey shouts out the collaborator whom he has sampled twice on VIEWS. The line, paired with DMX’s, may be a  reference to J. Cole’s “She Knows”:\n\nWhat these bitches want from a nigga?\nOn some DMX shit.\n\nNote the pairing of “DMX” and “DM my eXes.”\n\nMore over Drizzy is group DM-ing his exes can be an explanation of how busy he is with life and not finding the time to text each one individually.\n\nAlso, by grouping them he is sending them all the same message “I tell ‘em they belong to me”, which indicates he does not prefer any of them over the other and he loved them all the same way and equally.\n\nAn interesting choice for a name drop and sample subject in light of DMX showing his dislike for Drake on The Breakfast Club.\n\nIn 2016, DMX said he approves of Drake’s shout-out.", "annotation_id": "9070017", "url": "https://genius.com/9070017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 229, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "What these bitches want from a nigga?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This line is a sample of the late rapper DMX’s song “What These Bitches Want,” from DMX’s 1999 album, …And Then There Was X.\n\nIn 2012, DMX called out Drake on a Power 105 interview, saying:\n\nI don’t like anything about Drake. I don’t like his fucking voice. I don’t like what he talks about. I don’t like his face. I don’t like the way he walks. Nothing…\n\nThe sample clearance and use may indicate that the beef between the two rappers has faded, and that Drake is giving props to another one of the hip hop legends. N.O.R.E. claimed he’s the reason Drake got in touch with DMX.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/noreaga/status/725929485841747969", "annotation_id": "9070015", "url": "https://genius.com/9070015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 218, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "Heard a lot about me ’fore we started off I know you heard that my pool parties like Mardi Gras", "vault_lines": ["You love me and I love you"], "meaning": "Mardi Gras, also known as Fat Tuesday, is the day before the Christian holiday Ash Wednesday which signifies the begin of the Season of Lent. In Lent, Christians repent, and in doing so, usually cut down on partying, junk-food, etc. For this reason, Mardi Gras is one of the biggest days of partying of the year.\n\n\n\nDrake’s pool parties have always been the talk of the town. He hosted a foam pool party on Summer 2015 and the likes of Kanye West, The Game, and even Amber Rose were in attendance.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/37XCFVDQP2/", "annotation_id": "9069991", "url": "https://genius.com/9069991", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "3 dots, you thinkin’ of a reaction still While you’re typin’, make sure you tell me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "When typing an iMessage on an iPhone, three gray dots enclosed within an oval appear in the bottom corner of the conversation log, signifying that the other person is typing a message.\n\nDrake’s girl is taking too long to decide how to react, perhaps revealing a deceitful side to her.", "annotation_id": "9069984", "url": "https://genius.com/9069984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 140, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "You tell me that I’m confusin’ More immature than Marques Houston", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "One of Drake’s ex’s compares his immaturity to Marques Houston, founding member of the R&B group “Immature,” later known as IMX.  Marques also played Roger in the popular 90’s show Sister, Sister.  Roger was always immature, as he constantly attempted to make either Tia & Tamara (twins), his girlfriend.\n\nThis girl could be alluding to Drake’s immaturity in the sense of him not being committed the way Roger was.  In the season four episode “When a Man Loves Two Women” in particular, he asks out both Tia and Tamera, along with several other girls.", "annotation_id": "9069973", "url": "https://genius.com/9069973", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "u.", "line": "You toyin’ with it like Happy Meal", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "McDonald’s Happy Meal’s contain small, inexpensive toys for kids to play with. This girl is playing with Drake’s heart as if it were a cheap toy.\n\nIt’s clear that Drizzy doesn’t like being taken advantage of in the relationship, and he wants her to take him more seriously.", "annotation_id": "9069962", "url": "https://genius.com/9069962", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": true, "click_status": "open", "strength": "accepted"}
