{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started From The Bottom Remix", "line": "Versace shoulder shrug", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Very similar to this line. Jody may have an actual Versace scarf or shrug on, but the reason he chooses to describe a “shoulder shrug” is because of its double meaning – a “shoulder shrug” can also refer a gesture of indifference. The purpose of the line is to convey that he doesn’t give a fuck.", "annotation_id": "2187693", "url": "https://genius.com/2187693", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started From The Bottom Remix", "line": "Back in high school had a chip in front of my shoulder But it’s 2k13", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "When he was school-aged, Riff had a problem with everything and everyone – a “chip on his shoulder”. He talks a little about that time here.\n\nNow, in 2013, things are different…\n\nhttps://twitter.com/JODYHiGHROLLER/statuses/219657901671849984\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nhttps://twitter.com/JODYHiGHROLLER/statuses/244371628157444096", "annotation_id": "2187668", "url": "https://genius.com/2187668", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started From The Bottom Remix", "line": "’sace on my eye", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Riff’s got a lot of glasses. This time around, they’re Versace.", "annotation_id": "2187628", "url": "https://genius.com/2187628", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started From The Bottom Remix", "line": "Graduated hardwood degree, a martial artist", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "When Riff uses the phrase “hardwood degree”, he’s primarily referring to the metaphorical school of hard knocks that many rappers lay claim to. However, he gives the phrase another meaning when he claims he’s a martial artist; he is referencing the fact that some forms of karate teach the practitioner to chop through wooden boards with their bare hands.", "annotation_id": "2187602", "url": "https://genius.com/2187602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Started From The Bottom Remix", "line": "[Hook 2: Drake] Started from the bottom now we’re here Started from the bottom now my whole team fucking here Started from the bottom now we’re here Started from the bottom now the whole team here, nigga Started from the bottom now we’re here Started from the bottom now my whole team here, nigga Started from the bottom now we’re here Started from the bottom now the whole team fucking here", "vault_lines": ["Started from the bottom now we here", "Started from the bottom now my whole team freaking here", "Started from the bottom aha", "Started from the bottom now we here, yeah", "Started from the bottom now my whole team freaking here, trick, yeah", "Started from the bottom now my team here", "Started from the bottom", "Started from the bottom now we headin'"], "meaning": "See original version.", "annotation_id": "2187596", "url": "https://genius.com/2187596", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "status": "done", "targets": 35, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Jack-harlow-churchill-downs-lyrics", "genius_title": "Churchill Downs", "genius_id": 7902082, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Lucky me, people that don’t fuck with me Are linkin’ up with people that don’t fuck with me to fuck with me This shit is gettin’ ugly", "vault_lines": ["Lucky me, people that don't fuck with me", "Are linkin' up with people that don't fuck with me to fuck with me", "This shit is gettin' ugly"], "meaning": "This line has aged very well. Whereas many artists—including Future, Metro Boomin, Kendrick Lamar, A$AP Rocky, Rick Ross, and The Weeknd—have all worked together on Metro and Future’s collaborative albums, whereas all of these artists dissed Drake in one way or another.\n\nDrake later responded by dissing everyone in the song “Family Matters,” continuing the feud between the artists.", "annotation_id": "38237510", "url": "https://genius.com/38237510", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Seven-fifty for the round canaries, and they glitterin’", "vault_lines": ["Seven-fifty for the round canaries, and they glitterin'"], "meaning": "“Round canaries” are a type of gem. Drake is saying he spent $750,000 on them.\n\nThere used to be an annotation here that explained this that had a lot of upvotes, but some “Genius Editor” deleted it for literally no reason, so I’m adding it back.\n\nShout out to the guy who wrote the original annotation (@GabeSil)", "annotation_id": "37372152", "url": "https://genius.com/37372152", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "I’m gettin’ so rich, my music’s not even relatable", "vault_lines": ["I'm gettin' so rich, my music's not even relatable"], "meaning": "Drake’s net worth in 2022 was estimated to be around 503 million dollars. In 2019, Forbes named Drake as the fifth richest hip-hop artist in the world with the majority of Drake’s wealth coming from YouTube views, music streams, and promotions and the remainder from his real estate investments, his Virginia Black whiskey brand, touring proceeds, and his residency at the Vegas XS Nightclub.\n\nDrake’s music has changed with his success, with early songs like “Successful” and “The Resistance” focusing on the grind and the pressure of making it:\n\nThe game needed life, I put my heart in it\nI blew myself up, I’m on some martyr shit\nCarry the weight for my city like a cargo ship\nUh, I’m 23 with a money tree\nGrowing more too, I just planted a hundred seeds\n\nThemes of wealth, luxury, and the emotional highs and lows of living large are especially prominent in tracks like “Tuscan Leather” and “Nonstop.” In his track “Rich Flex,” Drake fully embraces the lavish lifestyle that once seemed so distant:\n\nSticks and stones, chrome on chrome\nThat’s just what a nigga on\nInternet clones, got ‘em kissin’ through the phone\n\nIn “Over,” Drake talks about being prepared for success and fame, knowing that it’s coming. He also admits that his life is starting to feel different from his listeners and that they might not relate to him the same way anymore:\n\nUh, one thing ‘bout music, when it hits, you feel no pain\nAnd I swear I got this shit that make these bitches go insane\nSo they tell me that they love me, I know better than that, it’s just game\nIt’s just what comes with the fame, and I’m ready for that, I’m just sayin’", "annotation_id": "36573792", "url": "https://genius.com/36573792", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "I know my grandpa would have a heart attack if I pulled a hunnid grand out So I’m not gonna pull a hunnid grand out", "vault_lines": ["I know my grandpa would have a heart attack if I pulled a hunnid grand out", "So I'm not gonna pull a hunnid grand out"], "meaning": "People doubt Jack Harlow’s lyrical ability, but this lyric proves to the doubters that Jack truly is the Shakespeare of our time. In fact, many have mistaken this bar for a Sigmund Freud quote.\n\n‘Hunnid grand out’ is a perfect rhyme with ‘Hunnid grand out’, with both phrases sharing the same vowels, consonants and rhythm.\n\nFew would attempt to craft a sentence with such perfect lyrical symmetry, but Jack has the audacity, and genius, to  pull it off. He really is the rapper of all time.", "annotation_id": "32342309", "url": "https://genius.com/32342309", "state": "accepted", "votes": 119, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "All I hear is plug talk comin’ from middlemen", "vault_lines": ["All I hear is plug talk comin' from middlemen", "All I hear is tall tales comin' from little men"], "meaning": "Likely a shot at Pusha T. “Plug” in this context means drug dealer, and Drake is saying that middlemen (people who get drugs from a plug and then deal it to someone else) are rapping like they’re actually the plugs, and pretending they’re more into the drug lifestyle than they actually are.\n\nThis could be a shot at many different rappers in the industry, but this would definitely include Pusha T because of Drake and Push’s previous beef, and the fact that Pusha T’s whole career has been built upon his persona as a crack dealer.\n\nIn fact, back in 2018, Drake accused Pusha T of the same thing in Duppy Freestyle, his diss track that  was the precursor to Pusha T’s infamous The Story of Adidon. At that time, Drake claimed his cousin was the plug and Push was a middleman:\n\nYour brother said it was your cousin, then him, then you\nSo you don’t rap what you did, you just rap what you knew\nDon’t be ashamed, it’s plenty niggas that do what you do", "annotation_id": "31723836", "url": "https://genius.com/31723836", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "How can I address you when you don’t own property?", "vault_lines": ["How can I address you when you don't own property?"], "meaning": "The first sense: Drake can’t be bothered to speak to / associate with people who aren’t wealthy.\n\nThe second sense: Drake says if you don’t have property of your own, you literally don’t have an address, so how can he address you? (Of course, technically you still have an address when you’re renting.)\n\nAlso, this ties in to the previous line, because Monopoly is a game all about owning different properties on the board.", "annotation_id": "31723702", "url": "https://genius.com/31723702", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "To be honest, y’all financial situation’s my biggest motivation And how you should take that statement is based on what you makin’", "vault_lines": ["To be honest, y'all financial situation's my biggest motivation", "And how you should take that statement is based on what you makin'"], "meaning": "Drake’s sending two different messages to two subsets of his competition with the same bar—if you hear this and you’re richer than Drake, then he’s motivated to surpass you. If you hear this and you’re poorer then he is, then he’s motivated by a desire to never be at your level.", "annotation_id": "31648320", "url": "https://genius.com/31648320", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Prayin’ on my downfall don’t make you religious, man", "vault_lines": ["Prayin' on my downfall don't make you religious, man"], "meaning": "This could be a shot at Kanye West, who was still in his “Donda” era at this time and was identifying strongly with religion. However, it should be noted that Drake and Ye supposedly made up in late 2021 at the Free Larry Hoover concert, so it’s not clear whether Drake would have re-dissed him just months after that.", "annotation_id": "31648190", "url": "https://genius.com/31648190", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "When I say “Bitch,” I’m very rarely referrin’ to women Most of the bitches I know are niggas, they not even women I know that sounds like I’m bein’ funny, I’m not even kidding Same ones that say they run the game when they not even in it", "vault_lines": ["When I say \"Bitch,\" I'm very rarely referrin' to women", "Most of the bitches I know are niggas, they not even women", "I know that sounds like I'm bein' funny, I'm not even kidding", "Same ones that say they run the game when they not even in it"], "meaning": "It’s possible that Drake is referring to his overall use of the word “bitch,” or perhaps just his use of the word in his personal life. In fact, in a 2023 interview with Caleb Pressley, Drake explained in detail how he doesn’t like referring to women as bitches. But how true is this claim he makes when it comes to his lyrics?\n\nIf we look at the rapper’s discography up until the release of “Churchill Downs,” we find that Drake used the word, “Bitch” a total of 103 times across his commercial albums, mixtapes, and EPs. His usage of the word varies, sometimes referring to a location (i.e., “I’m in this bitch”), or in reference to a person of undetermined gender. However, approximately 62 of the aforementioned uses (the most abundant of the variations) are pretty clear allusions to a female or females. Drake says “bitch” 18 times—the most in his discography—on his 2019 compilation album, Care Package, which includes the 2010 track, “Paris Morton Music.” On that song, Drake raps:\n\nBitches, what’s up with ya?\nI hate calling the women “bitches,” but the bitches love it\n\nSimilarly to this line on “Churchill Downs,” Drake doesn’t feel comfortable with the negative connotation that the curse word carries, as it is disrespectful towards women. A similar sentiment was echoed on a 2007 song, ironically titled, “Bitch Is Crazy,” where Drizzy sings:\n\nI respect a woman\nBut this bitch is crazy\n\nIt seems that despite his claim, Drake does refer to women as bitches quite frequently. However, with this subliminal diss, he uses the definition of “Bitch” that means cowardly. Drake finds it ironic how so many men in the rap game would be better described as bitches despite the word’s association with women.", "annotation_id": "30690048", "url": "https://genius.com/30690048", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Fully automatic with the jams and they don’t jam", "vault_lines": ["Fully automatic with the jams and they don't jam"], "meaning": "Jack is constantly, relentlessly (“fully automatic”) creating hits (“jams”) without any interruption, equivalent to a weapon that never jams.", "annotation_id": "30475862", "url": "https://genius.com/30475862", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "But schools closed in Kentucky, so I like when it snowed", "vault_lines": ["But schools closed in Kentucky, so I like when it snowed"], "meaning": "Kentucky schools are notorious for closing at any threat of snow due to the hilly topography preventing snow removal and increasing the danger of driving. For Harlow’s hometown of Louisville, KY, the snow removal team is comprised of 300 employees responsible for clearing 1,362 miles of road", "annotation_id": "29948764", "url": "https://genius.com/29948764", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Whips and chains like a dominatrix, wap, pssh", "vault_lines": ["Whips and chains like a dominatrix, wap, pssh"], "meaning": "Drake brags about all his whips (cars) and chains.\n\nDominatrices are sexually dominant women who are known for committing violence or inflicting pain in a pleasing way. A dominatrix may have an actual whip for whipping, or chains used to restrain somebody.\n\nFurthermore, there exists an unreleased Kendrick Lamar and Pusha T song, allegedly titled “Whips and Chains.” That title could be linked with this bar. This interpretation is further supported by the next line, which is a clear reference to Pusha T.", "annotation_id": "29233597", "url": "https://genius.com/29233597", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "I know in Toronto they got salt for the roads", "vault_lines": ["I mean the world's in denial, but they all know what I'm headed for", "I know in Toronto they got salt for the roads"], "meaning": "Salt is used to melt ice and snow on the roads so people can still drive.\n\n\n\nJack references Drake’s hometown of Toronto, Canada. Later in Drake’s verse, he references the Kentucky Derby, which takes place in Churchill Downs in Jack’s hometown of Louisville, KY.", "annotation_id": "29177518", "url": "https://genius.com/29177518", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Ain’t too many cons when you playin’ with the pros ’Cept for how your life get exposed, but I make that sacrifice for the life that I chose", "vault_lines": ["Ain't too many cons when you playin' with the pros", "'Cept for how your life get exposed, but I make that sacrifice for the life that I chose"], "meaning": "Jack is sharing that the many pros of being wealthy and famous outweigh the cons. He knew that if he wanted to have a career as a rapper, he would have to make certain sacrifices and deal with the common issues that come with being a celebrity, such as negative or invasive media attention.\n\nI didn’t write this part below:\n\nThis is also a double entendre, where pros can also mean prose, which is the art of written or spoken language. Jack is also trying to express that he is exposing his life through the act of writing and performing lyrical content.", "annotation_id": "29177425", "url": "https://genius.com/29177425", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "The kids carry chopsticks not for rice in the bowl School counselors all know how they life is at home", "vault_lines": ["The kids carry chopsticks not for rice in the bowl", "School counselors all know how they life is at home"], "meaning": "Jack uses some clever wordplay with ‘Chopsticks’ in this context used as a euphemism for guns that kids carry with them going to school in neighborhoods with lots of violence, while also referring to the utensil used in many Asian cultures", "annotation_id": "27075218", "url": "https://genius.com/27075218", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "I got enough pull to make the city start throwin’ games", "vault_lines": ["I got enough pull to make the city start throwin' games"], "meaning": "Drake refers to the Toronto Raptors, the NBA team from his hometown. He is notably a massive fan of the team and is known to be close with many of their past and present players.\n\nAt the time of this song’s release, Toronto had just been knocked out of the NBA Playoffs in the first round by the Philadelphia 76ers.", "annotation_id": "25787774", "url": "https://genius.com/25787774", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "How much water can I fit under the bridge before it overflows?", "vault_lines": ["How much water can I fit under the bridge before it overflows?"], "meaning": "The phrase “water under the bridge” basically refers to something that happened in the past and is no longer important or worth talking about.\n\nDrake has had so many things happen to him, most recently his beef with Pusha T, that he asks himself how much can he keep bottled up before he snaps?", "annotation_id": "25782666", "url": "https://genius.com/25782666", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Chubb’s got the magazine cover like Rolling Stone ‘Cause we already know how they rock, they throwin’ stones", "vault_lines": ["Chubb's got the magazine cover like Rolling Stone", "'Cause we already know how they rock, they throwin' stones"], "meaning": "Here, Drake shouts out his close friend and head of security Nessel “Chubbs” Beezer while basing his clever wordplay on both the American magazine Rolling Stone—which mainly covers music, politics, and popular culture—and the English band The Rolling Stones, which has inspired several brands.\n\nPrimarily, the line suggests that Chubbs has his weapon (with magazine referring to a gun magazine) covered and concealed on his waistline. This interpretation is reinforced by Drake’s gesture in the music video, where he tucks his hand below his shirt and into his waistline, mimicking the motion of securing a firearm.\n\nDrake also alludes to his bitter history with Rolling Stone, having called them out back in 2014 for misinterpreting his comments about Kanye West and Jay-Z. He implies that Rolling Stone operates by creating false narratives that incite controversy and ultimately damage relationships.\n\nDrake previously used similar wordplay on his 2015 track “You & the 6:”:\n\nBut I just roll with it, momma, rolling stone with it, momma\nGotta be careful around Rolling Stone\nOr anyone that’s tryna throw stones at me, momma", "annotation_id": "25780755", "url": "https://genius.com/25780755", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "My urges for revenge are uncontrollable I know we’re gettin’ older though, yeah But I gotta get a nigga back for that, it’s non-negotiable It’s not even debatable", "vault_lines": ["My urges for revenge are uncontrollable", "I know we're gettin' older though, yeah", "But I gotta get a nigga back for that, it's non-negotiable", "It's not even debatable"], "meaning": "Drake references his feud with Pusha T and says “I know we’re gettin' older” to hint at how long-standing it is. He acknowledges that he cannot control his urges to get revenge on Pusha regardless despite the advice he’s received. Their beef was heavily publicized after Pusha exposed Drake’s son in the the song “The Story of Adidon”. The title is a portmanteau of Adonis, the name of Drake’s son, and Adidas, the brand Drake was rumored to be joining in 2018.\n\nIn the verse of the song Pusha namedrops Drake’s baby mama—Sophie Brussaux, an artist and former pornstar, and even accuses Drake of being an absent father.\n\nSophie knows better as your baby mother\nCleaned her up for IG, but the stench is on her\nA baby’s involved, it’s deeper than rap\n\n\n\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\nDeadbeat mothafucka playin' border patrol, ooh\nAdonis is your son\nAnd he deserves more than an Adidas press run; that’s real", "annotation_id": "25771606", "url": "https://genius.com/25771606", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "The G.O.A.T.s call me to the side like, “Can we have a word?”", "vault_lines": ["The G.O.A.T.s call me to the side like, \"Can we have a word?\""], "meaning": "G.O.A.T. is a common acronym for “Greatest Of All Time,” a possible allusion Jack Harlow uses towards some of rap’s most respected and admired artists, such as Kanye West, Eminem, and the feature on this track, Drake.\n\nIn September 2021, Jack explained how “Killer (Remix),”—a track released that same year—came together with Eminem to Billboard:\n\nWe didn’t get to meet, but we had a phone call that meant the world to me, I haven’t shared it with the world yet, but I can’t wait until the world hears it. He gave me a lot of props that any artist would love to get. Sometimes, the best gem is just somebody you admire, letting you know who you think you are. He let me know, “You’re that, you’re dope.” I’ve waited a decade to hear that, so it was special.\n\nJack could as well be referring to himself being praised by the notorious Kanye West, who he previously collaborated with on “LOUIE BAGS” in 2022, Kanye said this in a now-deleted Instagram post:\n\nThis n—a can raaaaaaap bro, And I’m saying n—a as a compliment Top 5 out right now.\n\nIt is clear Jack has achieved a new sense of enlightenment when recognized by his idols. Additionally, Jack’s feelings were reaffirmed by Drake’s support his career, now, appearing alongside Jack on this song.", "annotation_id": "25762828", "url": "https://genius.com/25762828", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "My son’s gotta learn that forgiveness is a lonely road The cribs on his will like motor homes", "vault_lines": ["My son's gotta learn that forgiveness is a lonely road", "The cribs on his will like motor homes"], "meaning": "Drake uses a play on words with the homophone of will/wheel. Drakes home is on his son’s will for him to inherit, and a motor home is on wheels.", "annotation_id": "25762594", "url": "https://genius.com/25762594", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "We ’bout to feed these youngins to the metaverse Meanwhile I’m over here just tryna pen a verse", "vault_lines": ["We 'bout to feed these youngins to the metaverse", "Meanwhile I'm over here just tryna pen a verse"], "meaning": "A Metaverse is a network of 3D virtual worlds focused on social connection. In futurism and science fiction, it is often described as a hypothetical iteration of the Internet as a single, universal virtual world that is facilitated by the use of virtual reality (VR) and augmented reality (AR) headsets. The Metaverse craze fired up with the recent transformation of Facebook to Meta. Younger people are drawn towards the Metaverse because it has a lot of positive implications for gaming and entertainment, fields that are growing in popularity in the younger generations.", "annotation_id": "25761841", "url": "https://genius.com/25761841", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Cold hearts and heated floors No parental guidance, I just see divorce Therapy sessions, I’m in the waiting room, readin’ Forbes Abandonment issues I’m gettin’ treated for", "vault_lines": ["Cold hearts and heated floors", "No parental guidance, I just see divorce", "Therapy sessions, I'm in the waiting room, readin' Forbes", "Abandonment issues I'm gettin' treated for"], "meaning": "Here, Drake speaks on his upbringing, especially alluding to the divorce of his parents Sandi and Dennis Graham which seemingly had a massive impact on him.\n\nIn “Champagne Poetry”, he speaks on all of the trauma that he carries around even after arguably becoming one of the biggest rappers of all time:\n\nMy parents' divorce is on me\nMy therapist’s voice is making the choices for me\nAnd I always censor myself ‘cause no matter what, they reporting on me\n\nTo have a “cold heart” is to have a lack of empathy or an inability to show emotions, usually stemming from PTSD from unresolved problems. This juxtaposes the heated floors in Drake’s mansion, which is a major signifier of his wealth and status. Also shown in the next line as Drake reads Forbes, in which he was included in the 2021 Rich List after making $49million whilst he waits to get therapy – proving that despite his riches, his negative feelings aren’t as easily resolved.\n\nHaving ‘no parental guidance’ is meant in a literal sense as his parents were not together, and his father allegedly abandoned him when he was young.\n\nHe can also be speaking on the Parental Advisory sticker which is added to music as a warning label that signifies there is explicit content. His parents also possibly didn’t monitor the music he was listening to to which Drake claimed was 2Pac, 50 Cent and Lil Wayne. Alternatively, having no parental guidance made him vent in his music, leading for there to be a ‘parental guidance’ on his music.", "annotation_id": "25760987", "url": "https://genius.com/25760987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Kentucky derby races, my presence in the spot is so abrasive Box at the Churchill Downs, that’s motivation, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Kentucky derby races, my presence in the spot is so abrasive", "Box at the Churchill Downs, that's motivation, yeah"], "meaning": "Churchill Downs is a famous racehorse track in Louisville, Kentucky, where Jack Harlow comes from.\n\nThe weekend of the album’s release, Drake and Jack were both seen at the Kentucky Derby, watching the event from a special box, so they really brought the bar to life.", "annotation_id": "25760326", "url": "https://genius.com/25760326", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "If I see ya, I spit in ya faces, ha-tu", "vault_lines": ["If I see ya, I spit in ya faces, ha-tu"], "meaning": "This goes hand in hand with the next line. Snot is generally the colour green. Pusha T made an album called “Daytona”. If Drake spits in his face it will be “Daytona with green faces”. Jealousy is also associated with green face. Drake is also flexing his wealth with the amount of watches he has.", "annotation_id": "25759563", "url": "https://genius.com/25759563", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "I got my realtor out here playin’ Monopoly", "vault_lines": ["I got my realtor out here playin' Monopoly"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to purchasing his neighbor’s Hidden Hills, California home in 2016. He purchased a third home in Hidden Hills in October 2018. As of March 2022, Drake had put his “Hidden Hills Trio'' of homes up for sale. Drake also made reference to the purchase of his neighbor’s home on the track “Where Ya At'' by Future when he rapped,\n\nI’ll buy the neighbor’s house if they complain about the noise", "annotation_id": "25759390", "url": "https://genius.com/25759390", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "And shorty like, “You know that boy Jack is goin’ places”", "vault_lines": ["And shorty like, \"You know that boy Jack is goin' places\""], "meaning": "Drake is one of Jack’s biggest influences. Ever since he has started to gain mainstream attention, this has been a note of his music, with many comparing the approach to that of Drake’s early career.\n\nHere Drake is acknowledging the success that Jack has attained and also winking to this notion as well, as the word is so large of his ascension that plenty of girls he runs with let him know.\n\nhttps://hiphop24x7.com/interview/jack-harlow-names-drake-eminem-more-as-his-rap-influences/", "annotation_id": "25758693", "url": "https://genius.com/25758693", "state": "pending", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "All that time in the kitchen finally panned out, uh I put some flavor in a pot and took the bland out", "vault_lines": ["All that time in the kitchen finally panned out, uh", "I put some flavor in a pot and took the bland out"], "meaning": "Along with Jack’s use of “kitchen” as a metaphor for a studio, he also used to work at KFC, creating a double entendre. Jack also uses wordplay in “panned out”; as a pan is a standard piece of equipment found in the kitchen.\n\nFurthermore, the wordplay in “put some flavor in a pot and took the bland out” refers to the gradual improvement in Jack’s rap skills, resulting in his music being more interesting, or having more “flavor” over time.", "annotation_id": "25757233", "url": "https://genius.com/25757233", "state": "pending", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Cold like the Minnesota Vikings at home", "vault_lines": ["Cold like the Minnesota Vikings at home"], "meaning": "The Minnesota Vikings are an NFL team out of Minneapolis, Minnesota, one of the coldest regions in the country.\n\nSince 1982, the Vikings have mostly played home games in domes to combat late fall/early winter cold weather, but in 2014-15 they were forced to play at then-TCF Bank Stadium during stadium construction.\n\nOn January 10, 2016, the Vikings hosted an NFL Wild Card game vs Seattle that ranked as one of the coldest home games in league history.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/ian_leonard/status/684496785885147136", "annotation_id": "25756057", "url": "https://genius.com/25756057", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "The profit is sickenin’, the stones is shimmerin’ Came from the north, but I got hot as fuck, so ain’t no shiverin’, yeah", "vault_lines": ["The profit is sickenin', the stones is shimmerin'", "Came from the north, but", "I got hot as fuck, so ain't no shiverin', yeah"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his diamond encrusted OVO pendant made by Kilani Jewellery in his hometown of Toronto, Canada.", "annotation_id": "25593213", "url": "https://genius.com/25593213", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "All I hear is tall tales comin’ from little men", "vault_lines": ["All I hear is plug talk comin' from middlemen", "All I hear is tall tales comin' from little men"], "meaning": "This is the second time that Drake has used the tall tales idiom to describe someone he is beefing with. The first time was on the song Good Riddance where he dissed a local Canadian rapper in the line “But, I don’t admire you, short niggas tell tall tales\nGood riddance Aristo I just retired you!”\n\nThis line is likely a subliminal shot to Kendrick Lamar, who is 5'5'‘. Following Kendrick’s direct diss against Kendrick in the song Like That, Drake released the track Push Ups, which made further jokes at the expense of Kendrick’s height, calling him a pipsquek and a midget, and also saying:\n\nHow the fuck you big steppin' with a size-seven men’s on?", "annotation_id": "25589529", "url": "https://genius.com/25589529", "state": "pending", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Everybody know Jack, but they don’t know Jack, man They just know I got the flows and the hoes and the packed out shows", "vault_lines": ["Everybody know Jack, but they don't know Jack, man", "They just know I got the flows and the hoes and the packed out shows"], "meaning": "The bar here is a play on the phrase ‘you don’t know jack’, a colloquial meaning ‘to not know anything’. Here Jack uses a double entendre to assert that ‘everybody’ doesn’t know ‘jack’, but they also don’t know ‘Jack’ himself.\n\nIt is also worth noting that the lyrics ‘Jack, man’ is also a play on his government name ‘Jackman’. In evoking his extended name in the line about not knowing ‘Jack’, Harlow further extrapolates upon the established idea that ‘everybody’ does not know him on such a personal level.", "annotation_id": "25586683", "url": "https://genius.com/25586683", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Baby blue G class, I feel like a kid again", "vault_lines": ["Baby blue G class, I feel like a kid again"], "meaning": "In a recent Instagram post, Drake showed off a baby blue colored Mercedes-Benz G-Wagon.", "annotation_id": "25586355", "url": "https://genius.com/25586355", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "Daytonas with the green faces", "vault_lines": ["Daytonas with the green faces"], "meaning": "A green dial Rolex Daytona chronograph watch is a highly-popular, 6-figure timepiece.\n\nAt the same time this line can be interpreted as a shot at Pusha T as Pusha T had an album called Daytona and green face is associated with envy. Meaning Pusha T is envious of Drake.\n\nhttps://www.luxuryofwatches.com/rolex-cosmograph-daytona-green-116508/?gclid=CjwKCAjw6dmSBhBkEiwA_W-EoCyLKsLCwMvRN2F30UH3ES2GHTRmx8mLmcT2HAuUtPTy6kUWQzgu5RoCbVkQAvD_BwE", "annotation_id": "25586318", "url": "https://genius.com/25586318", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Churchill Downs", "line": "The label used to wonder how I’m supposed to stand next to Vert Prolly never thought that I would get these legs to work", "vault_lines": ["The label used to wonder how I'm supposed to stand next to Vert", "Prolly never thought that I would get these legs to work"], "meaning": "Jack Harlow alludes to his off-and-on feud with Philadelphia rapper Lil Uzi Vert involving their label Generation Now, stating how they would question if his popularity would stand up to Uzi’s. This could also be taken literally as Lil Uzi Vert stands at 5'4\" while Jack Harlow is 6'3\" — a whopping eleven-inch difference in height.\n\nUzi is known for his troubles with the label ever since 2016, going back and forth with Generation Now owners DJ Drama and Don Cannon for not allowing him to release music on time, prioritizing other artists, a part of the label, over him.\n\nThe beef initiated in March 2019 when Lil Uzi Vert sent shots at Jack Harlow through his Instagram story, uploading a photo of Jack with a clown emoji covering his face. The photo stemmed from a picture of Jack with their label-head DJ Drama during his birthday in Las Vegas.\n\n\n\nLil Uzi Vert’s Instagram story\n\n\nSince Lil Uzi Vert’s commercial success has simmered down over the years, Jack Harlow has since seen massive success after releasing his hit single “WHATS POPPIN,” being featured on the number one Billboard single “INDUSTRY BABY,” and even appearing on Kanye West’s album DONDA 2.", "annotation_id": "25585879", "url": "https://genius.com/25585879", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
