{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "status": "done", "targets": 43, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Nicki-minaj-drake-and-lil-wayne-seeing-green-lyrics", "genius_title": "Seeing Green", "genius_id": 6809866, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Propaganda ’bout to blow out the candle", "vault_lines": ["Propaganda 'bout to blow out the candle"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne cleverly splits the word “propaganda” into “props” for a TV screen to contrast his authentic wealth and Gucci gear with rappers who use fake items for the camera. By blowing out the candle, he signifies that the weak hype and narratives of his competition are about to be completely extinguished by his real success.", "annotation_id": "41460469", "url": "https://genius.com/41460469", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "You niggas can’t trouble me from the Doubletree Step your suites up,", "vault_lines": ["Fuck with me, you niggas can't trouble me from the Doubletree", "Step your suites up,"], "meaning": "The DoubleTree by Hilton is a hotel chain. If you stay here, which isn’t inexpensive to the common person, Drake doesn’t feel like you can give him any problems.\n\nStep your suites/sweets up is a clever double entendre. He’s telling you to level up and stay at a higher quality hotel than the DoubleTree. At the same time, the DoubleTree is known for their signature chocolate chip cookies, which were originally made in the early 1980s for VIPs but now given to all guests", "annotation_id": "38834980", "url": "https://genius.com/38834980", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "But I been on my feet since Cinderella lost her glass shoe", "vault_lines": ["But I been on my feet since Cinderella lost her glass shoe"], "meaning": "The way Wayne pronounces ‘feet’ also sounds like ‘beast’ which plays into the Disney theme.", "annotation_id": "38769882", "url": "https://genius.com/38769882", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Yeah, yeah, yeah (Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah) Yeah, yeah, yeah (Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I'm smokin' tarantula (Yeah)", "You dig? Yeah, hold up, hm"], "meaning": "These vocals, which are pitched up and run throughout the track, are sampled from Heather Headley’s “In My Mind”:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/9dfZyfckKPk?feature=shared&t=216", "annotation_id": "36976622", "url": "https://genius.com/36976622", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "I should go cop a new jersey, that’s word to Camden", "vault_lines": ["I should go cop a new jersey, that's word to Camden"], "meaning": "A reference to The City in New Jersey, Camden. Minaj has been known to where Jersey’s in the past events, notably at Hoops Mania", "annotation_id": "33427083", "url": "https://genius.com/33427083", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "These bitches thirsty, I can see why they alcoholics", "vault_lines": ["These bitches thirsty, I can see why they alcoholics"], "meaning": "This is the first subliminal diss directed at Houston rapper Megan Thee Stallion. Fans speculated for months whether or not the two were at odds after Megan teamed up with Nicki’s rival Cardi B for their August 2020 super smash hit WAP. In November 2020 after WAPs release, Megan stated that her and Nicki’s relationship hasn’t changed since collabing with Cardi B.\n\n\n\nHowever in January 2021, Nicki Minaj unfollowed Megan Thee Stallion on Instagram. Finally, dissing her in this line marking the beginning of the rap beef in May 2021. The two rappers have been dissing each other ever since and finally going head to head in 2024 with Megan Thee Stallion’s HISS and Nicki Minaj’s Big Foot.\n\n\n\n\n\nAlso, the way Nicki says “see why” sounds like the acronym CIWA which stands for the Clinical Institute Withdrawal Assessment for Alcohol. Which is a medical tool used to assess and determine the severity of alcohol withdrawal.", "annotation_id": "33410259", "url": "https://genius.com/33410259", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "None of these shits is a Dodge, none of this shit’s a facade None of this shit’s a mirage", "vault_lines": ["None of these shits is a Dodge, none of this shit's a facade", "None of this shit's a mirage"], "meaning": "Drake has filled his garage with expensive cars and no regular cars like the Dodge Charger, Volkswagen Passat (sounds like Facade), or the Mitsubishi Mirage.", "annotation_id": "33046213", "url": "https://genius.com/33046213", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "I’m already known as the GOAT", "vault_lines": ["I'm already close to the mob, I'm already known as the GOAT"], "meaning": "Drake is usually put in conversations about the best rapper of all time due to his great success and impact, as well as some of his releases in the early 2010’s, being considered as some of the best projects of the decade.", "annotation_id": "31084212", "url": "https://genius.com/31084212", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Thought I was seein’ things when I was seein’ green", "vault_lines": ["Thought I was seein' things when I was seein' green"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to his beginnings in rap and how he thought he might be dreaming/hallucinating when he started generating significant amounts of money.\n\n“Seeing green” can be interpreted as “seeing money”.", "annotation_id": "31084198", "url": "https://genius.com/31084198", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Ayy, dios mío, the ice is frío on the Jesus, it’s bringin’ me closer to God", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, dios mío, the ice is frío on the Jesus, it's bringin' me closer to God"], "meaning": "“Ay Dios mio” means “Oh My God” in spanish, and “frío” means “cold”. The first part of the sentence can be translated as “Oh My God, the ice is cold on the Jesus”.\n\nDrake is implying that his Jesus piece (a type of jewelry that depicts Jesus Christ, typically in the form of a pendant or medal) is going to help him to have a deeper connection to his spiritual side and God.", "annotation_id": "31084147", "url": "https://genius.com/31084147", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Even with them floor seats, they couldn’t see the team The K-I-N-G, the GOAT, plus me, the Queen", "vault_lines": ["Even with them floor seats, they couldn't see the team", "The K-I-N-G, the GOAT, plus me, the Queen"], "meaning": "In Basketball, floor seats, better known as courtside seats are the ones closest to the basketball court. They are very expensive, since you get the best view of the game.\n\nIt’s literally impossible to oversee the team when you have floor seats, so she’s obviously not talking about somebody’s eyesight. She means they couldn’t see the team in the sense of, they aren’t on their level. Their team, consisting of the K-I-N-G who is Drake, the G.O.A.T. who is Wayne and Nicki as the Queen, is untouchable and stays unmatched.", "annotation_id": "24515970", "url": "https://genius.com/24515970", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "I know bae trust my vision but don’t want me to see prison Said, ”I can’t make no promises, either leave or come visit But leave the keys to your heart next to your soul and your spirit”", "vault_lines": ["I know bae trust my vision but don't want me to see prison", "Said, \"I can't make no promises, either leave or come visit", "But leave the keys to your heart next to your soul and your spirit\""], "meaning": "Wayne is almost certainly referring to Denise Bidot, who he recently is rumored to have married in a secret ceremony.", "annotation_id": "23172762", "url": "https://genius.com/23172762", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "I wore a Gat’ before a tattoo, I had to As a matter of fact, I had two", "vault_lines": ["I wore a Gat' before a tattoo, I had to", "As a matter of fact, I had two"], "meaning": "Wayne gets autobiographical here and talks about having and handling guns before he could even legally get a tattoo.\n\nThe most infamous story related to him with a firearm at a young age is when he shot himself in the chest with a gun he found amongst his mother’s things at the age of 12. Originally (and for years) Wayne claimed that the shooting was an accident, but after some hints in various songs, he finally revealed that the shooting was a suicide attempt.", "annotation_id": "23172716", "url": "https://genius.com/23172716", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Cita told me to stunt my ass off, that’s all", "vault_lines": ["Cita told me to stunt my ass off, that's all"], "meaning": "Cita is Lil Wayne’s mother, real name Jacinda Carter. Weezy has always been close this his mother, and often references her. In this instance he is alleging that any deeds he does moving forward are because she told him to.", "annotation_id": "23172697", "url": "https://genius.com/23172697", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Life is a beach house, don’t let the sand in I ball in any arena, go let the fans in", "vault_lines": ["Life is a beach house, don't let the sand in", "I ball in any arena, go let the fans in"], "meaning": "Nicki uses a “beach house” methaphor as a way of saying she’s no longer letting haters get inside her head.  She’s now feeding only from positives energies like her fans'.\n\nAlso, “arena” is Spanish for “sand,” which connects the two lines.", "annotation_id": "23162000", "url": "https://genius.com/23162000", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "That’s why I’m private like the airports I land in", "vault_lines": ["That's why I'm private like the airports I land in"], "meaning": "Nicki often travels in her own G5 plane, as seen in many of her Instagram stories such as the one which started the viral #NickiChallenge on 2017.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BVTZb6EB7vf/?utm_source=ig_embed\n\nThe singer has, also, talked about her plane in other songs such as “Majesty,” from her 2018 Queen album.\n\nMmm, uh, yo, I got the money and the power now\nThe G5’ll get me out there in an hour now", "annotation_id": "23161575", "url": "https://genius.com/23161575", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Ayo, I am the star in any room that I stand in I am the standout, you just my stand-in", "vault_lines": ["Ayo, I am the star in any room that I stand in", "I am the standout, you just my stand-in"], "meaning": "Whether it is because of her fame, her top-tier designer clothes or even her body, Nicki Minaj knows well she outshines everyone else whenever she appears in an event.\n\nAs she said on Meghan Trainor’s “Nice to Meet Ya,”\n\nBoom, I turn every head when I walk in the room\nPretty in real life, hit my pics with the zoom\"", "annotation_id": "23161496", "url": "https://genius.com/23161496", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "I used to live next door to Drake and Momma Dee And Lisa Dee on Eagle Street", "vault_lines": ["I used to live next door to Drake and Momma Dee", "And Lisa Dee on Eagle Street"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne grew up on the corner of Apple St. & Eagle St, in the Holly Grove neighborhood, which is located located in New Orleans’s 17th Ward.\n\n\n\nHowever, Cash Money Records was based in Miami by the time Drake was signed to their sub-label Young Money, since the whole clique had to move there after Hurricane Katrina displaced many residents.", "annotation_id": "23100747", "url": "https://genius.com/23100747", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Gucci flannel, Gucci socks, Gucci sandals Gucci teddy bears, and pandas, not fake", "vault_lines": ["Gucci flannel, Gucci socks, Gucci sandals", "Gucci teddy bears, and pandas, not fake"], "meaning": "These bars are a nod to Fake/Not,  a Gucci capsule collection of an ironic homage to the institutional code of the brand. The narrative began with a print inspired by a retro appropriation of the Gucci logo featuring the bicolor green and red stripe accompanied by ‘NOT FAKE'—a playful commentary on the idea of imitation. Wayne donned the Fake/Not collection for the music video of his November 2020 track “Something Different.”", "annotation_id": "23050857", "url": "https://genius.com/23050857", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Brand new vanilla Maserati, I been Häagen-Dazsin’", "vault_lines": ["Brand new vanilla Maserati, I been Häagen-Dazsin'"], "meaning": "In this line, the word vanilla has two meanings. Nicki uses vanilla to describe the color of her brand new car, with vanilla being “[an] rich off-white tint, as well as a medium pale shade of yellow.” Nicki also uses vanilla to describe the flavor of Häagen-Dazs ice cream.\n\n\n\nVanilla could also be used to describe the fact that the car does not have any flashy upgrades like her Pink Lamborghini, thus rendering it more discrete.", "annotation_id": "23047853", "url": "https://genius.com/23047853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "I D&G the wallet, my money tall and brolic", "vault_lines": ["I D&G the wallet, my money tall and brolic"], "meaning": "D&G is short for Dolce & Gabbana, which is an Italian luxury brand. This isn’t the first time Nicki Minaj has mentioned D&G in her lyrics—in her 2018 track “LLC,” she spits a line about the highly opulent brand:\n\nDolce Gabbana, DG, pretty gang rated PG (Woah)\n\n\n\nPhoto: Nicki wearing the brand for an appearance on the LIVE with Kelly and Michael show in 2014.\n\n\n\n\n“Brolic” can mean strong, powerful, muscular—an obscure New York slang word. Essentially, Minaj is saying she has a lot of money, and it is well-stacked as in “money tall.”", "annotation_id": "23044606", "url": "https://genius.com/23044606", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "I put you six feet deep, I’m bein’ socially distant", "vault_lines": ["I put you six feet deep, I'm bein' socially distant"], "meaning": "This line is a wordplay from Wayne considering the context of COVID-19 which requires keeping a distance of 6 feet between one another so as to avoid being exposed to the virus.\n\nWeezy flaunts being ‘socially distant’ by putting someone “6 feet deep” instead, which alludes to killing whoever messes with him, as burials of the bodies happen 6 feet under the soil.", "annotation_id": "23020385", "url": "https://genius.com/23020385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "My bitch a vacuum I told her, “Keep me clean,” the scene serene", "vault_lines": ["My bitch a vacuum", "I told her, \"Keep me clean,\" the scene serene"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is referring to his girl being a vacuum in the sense that she gives him high quality blowjobs.\n\nHe mentioned something similar on the 2015 record “Truffle Butter,” which was also a collab with Drake and Nicki Minaj.\n\nShe became a vacuum, put it on my dick like carpet''", "annotation_id": "23020355", "url": "https://genius.com/23020355", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Big-ass backyards, just so Papa Bear can frolic", "vault_lines": ["Big-ass backyards, just so Papa Bear can frolic"], "meaning": "Papa Bear is the nickname Nicki gave to her son. Via Instagram, Minaj posted pictures of her baby coupled with a wholesome caption:\n\n[#]PapaBear thank you so very much for choosing me to be your mama 🎀🦄🙏 Wishing you guys a happy & prosperous New Year. Thank you for your love & support throughout this journey. It’s meant so much to me. Becoming a mom is by far the most fulfilling job I’ve ever taken on. Sending love to all the superhero mothers out there. Big hugs to all the women who have been pregnant during this challenging time.", "annotation_id": "23020334", "url": "https://genius.com/23020334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "The cash blue, but I’m still seein’ green I’m in the bathroom, and I’m peein’ lean", "vault_lines": ["The cash blue, but I'm still seein' green", "I'm in the bathroom, and I'm peein' lean", "Ain't no C in green, but I'm seein' green"], "meaning": "This line entails a triple entendre :\n\n‘Blue’ is a homophone in this line as it sounds like ‘blew’ which denotes that Wayne blew the money he had at the moment he still has a lot more.\n\n‘Seeing green’ may also refer to being high on lean, which is mentioned in the next line.  When one is intoxicated with lean, they tend to trip and see things which is why Yeezy is seeing blue money instead of green.\n\nThe cash being blue could also be referring to the blue undertone of $100 bills which reiterates his wealthy side as he only keeps $100 bills.", "annotation_id": "23020333", "url": "https://genius.com/23020333", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "6 G-O-D, CMB, yes sir", "vault_lines": ["6 G-O-D, CMB, yes sir"], "meaning": "“The 6” is a nickname Drake is known to have given his home city, Toronto, in Ontario, Canada; it is derived from the city’s area codes 416 (Toronto) and 647 (an overlay which serves for the same city). He even has it tatted on him:\n\n\n\n Drake on the left, with a “416” tattoo inked down his right rib cage, pictured with a tattoo artist on the right.\n\nDrake has referred to himself as the “6 God” multiple times in the past, even naming one of his songs after it on his fourth mixtape, If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, released in February 2015.\n\n“CMB,” of course, would allude to the previously rapped “YMCMB,” in this instance meaning Cash Money Billionaires in reference to the collaborative label Cash Money Records, formerly home to the 3 artists on the track, but always home in spirit.", "annotation_id": "23020260", "url": "https://genius.com/23020260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "These bitches still my sons, who could ever deny it?", "vault_lines": ["These bitches still my sons, who could ever deny it?"], "meaning": "Once again, Minaj reminds her competition that they are mere children of which she is in control of, thus running the rap game. After all, one may even say that she is The Pinkprint, taking after her inspiration Jay Z’s The Blueprint, considering the extent at which other artists try to imitate the two.\n\nMinaj first claimed that her rap rivals in the industry were her “sons” on April 2011’s “Did It On ‘Em,” and has used the postpartum diss numerous times ever since.\n\nMoreover, who could challenge the sheer success of her influential career? Since beginning her journey in 2004, Minaj has stayed prominent within the industry, often in discussions being referred to as the “Queen of Rap.” After receiving multiple accolades and performing well in charts thanks to her millions of global fans, in 2016 Minaj had the honour of being listed in Time’s “100 most influential artists and entertainers in the world.” Then, in 2019, Complex ranked her eighth on their list of “best rappers of the 2010s” – she was the only female to be listed, highlighting her power in the game.", "annotation_id": "23020133", "url": "https://genius.com/23020133", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "I play forty-eight minutes on a torn meniscus, who’s subbin’?", "vault_lines": ["I play forty-eight minutes on a torn meniscus, who's subbin'?"], "meaning": "This is confirmation that Drake’s most recent knee injury was from playing basketball, but it’s also a double (almost a triple really) entendre: someone may need to “sub” in after an injury (not Drake though), but it’s also a warning not to “sub” Drake in a song.\n\nBecause it comes after five bars of subliminal shots at Kanye, it’s also Drake playing mock innocence (“Who, me? Subbing someone?”). He is technically “subbing” Kanye here, but the implication is he’s just doing it for the sport since he’s established enough to go directly at anyone’s head now.", "annotation_id": "23018513", "url": "https://genius.com/23018513", "state": "pending", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "This ain’t gon’ be the first time that I do numbers on two crutches", "vault_lines": ["This ain't gon' be the first time that I do numbers on two crutches"], "meaning": "Drake raps a sly triple entendre about his undeniable success, even while on literal and figurative crutches.\n\nThe line’s first meaning is very literal; in late October 2020, Drake underwent surgery after injuring himself playing basketball. This song was recorded since his surgery, so Drake is actively on two crutches as he recovers. His surgery and recovery are part of why Drake’s album, Certified Lover Boy, was delayed.\n\n\n\nHowever, this is not the first time that Drake injured himself during his career. He previously tore his ACL in June of 2009, but he was still “doing numbers” at that time with the success of his breakout mixtape, So Far Gone, and his huge single, “Best I Ever Had.”\n\nThe second meaning is a reference to Drake’s history as an actor on Degrassi: The Next Generation. In the show, Drake’s character, Jimmy, is shot, which leaves him paralyzed from the waist down. Before dawning the famous wheelchair, Jimmy unsuccessfully uses crutches. Although his character was handicapped, Drake helped boost the show’s popularity, therefore “doing numbers.”\n\n\n\nThe line’s third meaning is that Drake’s counterparts, Wayne and Nicki, function as his crutches, because they always mutually support each other and know that their collective success is guaranteed when the trio is united. Even if Drake were to disappoint or underperform, he would still do numbers alongside the Young Money crew.", "annotation_id": "23018458", "url": "https://genius.com/23018458", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "No one bitch could be my opp, that shit offends me It’s corporate giants and machines that went against me", "vault_lines": ["No one bitch could be my opp, that shit offends me", "It's corporate giants and machines that went against me"], "meaning": "Nicki acknowledges that her beef is not with any single female artist, since they cannot compete with her lyrically.\n\nThe word “opp” is Chicago slang for competition, and Nicki’s real battle is against the “corporate giants” who tried to blackball her from radio, fund the careers of new females, and attempt to “replace” Nicki in the hip-hop industry.\n\nThe industry events surrounding the “Nicki Minaj hate-train” that began in 2017 have been well-documented by fans.", "annotation_id": "23015853", "url": "https://genius.com/23015853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "These bitches copy my homework, that’s what they hand in", "vault_lines": ["These bitches copy my homework, that's what they hand in"], "meaning": "Nicki acknowledges that all the emerging women in hip hop have been heavily influenced by her career, and that this “copying” shows up in the new girls' music, or “homework.” Nicki also suggests that she is the “teacher” of all the new females in rap, since they have to “hand in” their homework to her.\n\nNicki continues this metaphor in “Fractions,” when she raps:\n\nI graded your homework, bitch, it’s incomplete.", "annotation_id": "23015822", "url": "https://genius.com/23015822", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Known her eight years and still fuck her like a new husband", "vault_lines": ["Known her eight years and still fuck her like a new husband"], "meaning": "These bars could be a sub towards Jamie Sun who claimed Drake broke up his engagement with his fiancé, Naomi Sharon, who Sun has been with for “8 years”. It’s rumored Drake slept with Sun’s fiancé.\n\nJamie posted on Instagram ““Imagine having the most beautiful, supportive, and faithful relationship 8 years long with an engagement and wedding planned. A beautiful woman on your side as ride or die. Then this major opportunity comes and a world star (DRAKE) calls your fiancé to sign a record deal. Flies you both over and than out of nowhere all the trust is vanished with knifes in your back and in your heart,”. He later posted, “I am no longer with the person people think I am together with. I am no longer with the 8 years I thought I was together with.”\n\nDrake allegedly flew Naomi Sharon and Jamie Sun out to work on his forthcoming album Certified Lover Boy. Allegedly there were rumors of signing Naomi to OVO.", "annotation_id": "23015727", "url": "https://genius.com/23015727", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Don’t put no K after that B-Boy, “Bad kall,” that’s flag talk", "vault_lines": ["Don't put no K after that B-Boy, \"Bad kall,\" that's flag talk"], "meaning": "Wayne delivers a double entendre in this line.\n\nFirst interpretation:\n\nDon’t put no K after that B, boy, “bad kall,” that’s flag talk\n\nPutting a K after a B gives BK, meaning Blood Killer in gang culture. It is also the initials of Wayne’s next two words, “Bad Kall.” He purposefully swaps out the C in “call” for a K in opposition to The Crips, as he reps the Blood gang. Saying BK is saying you are against Wayne.\n\nSecond interpretation:\n\nDon’t put no K after that B-Boy, bad call, that’s flag talk\n\nWayne says not to go after a B-Boy, a breakdancer, with a K, a gun, because they’re not gang-affiliated, just innocent bystanders. Killing an innocent bystander would be a bad call to make by the person killing, like a foul in sports. In some sports, a foul like that would make the referee throw a flag, hence, “flag talk.”", "annotation_id": "23015723", "url": "https://genius.com/23015723", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "My lil’ ho out Atlanta, got a ho out Atlanta With a ho out Atlanta, she a ho out Atlanta We get it, it’s the same in each city", "vault_lines": ["My lil' ho out Atlanta, got a ho out Atlanta", "With a ho out Atlanta, she a ho out Atlanta", "We get it, it's the same in each city"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne dates a girl who has a girlfriend, and her girlfriend has a girlfriend, all of whom are in Atlanta—but he has a similar group sex network established in virtually every city in America.\n\nIn the spirit of being nostalgic and self-referential, this may be a call back to “This is The Carter” where he said more or less the same thing in an ad-lib:\n\nSquad! My hoes got hoes, for real", "annotation_id": "23014047", "url": "https://genius.com/23014047", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Let me take this Balenciaga mask off to ask y’all, “Who asked y’all?”", "vault_lines": ["Let me take this Balenciaga mask off to ask y'all, \"Who asked y'all?\""], "meaning": "In the first line of Wayne’s verse, he delivers his line through a face mask. His voice sounds muffled, as if he is talking through a barrier or his voice is somewhat blocked, which does occur when a face mask is worn.\n\nOne of the most controversial effects of the 2019-2021 COVID-19 pandemic, Wayne’s first line refers to mask wearing, but is also a literal action that he performed while recording his verse.\n\nBalenciaga does indeed make face masks, marketing the mask as “Balenciaga Care Mask.” The mask is an astounding $130 and is stylistically similar to their Balenciaga Speed Trainer sneaker:", "annotation_id": "23013865", "url": "https://genius.com/23013865", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "And ever since the pandemic They don’t wanna let Drake out of Canada I been screamin’, “Free Drizzy”", "vault_lines": ["And ever since the pandemic", "They don't wanna let Drake out of Canada", "I been screamin', \"Free Drizzy\""], "meaning": "As a result of the COVID-19 pandemic, there have been travel restrictions and Drizzy hasn’t been able to fly out of Canada into the U.S. to make music and drop new material, which therefore keeps the “Young Money” label from making more money.\n\nWeezy is very aware that Drake is currently his top-selling artist and likely can’t wait for him to drop another album.\n\n\n\nAlso, this may be Tunechi’s way of telling that the tables have turned now, since Drake actively campaigned for “Free Weezy” when the rapper was in prison for gun-posession in 2010.", "annotation_id": "23013783", "url": "https://genius.com/23013783", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Tryna run a country like Putin one day, but who’s rushin’, who’s bluffin’? For real", "vault_lines": ["Tryna run a country like Putin one day, but who's rushin', who's bluffin'? For real"], "meaning": "Vladimir Putin is the president of Russia, an authoritarian leader who has been in power for more than two decades.\n\nNotice the wordplay – “rushin'” is a homophone to “Russian”, linking it with Putin’s nationality while also letting us know that Drake is taking his time with achieving his long-term goals.", "annotation_id": "23013043", "url": "https://genius.com/23013043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Your girl was better in the mornin’ like a slice of pizza", "vault_lines": ["Your girl was better in the mornin' like a slice of pizza"], "meaning": "Drizzy says he enjoyed a good time with this girl more in the morning, when he was was more than likely not intoxicated, like a cold pizza. People tend to find that cold pizza has a more savored taste compared to when it’s hot.\n\nThis is also a nod to his mentor, Weezy. On his ‘Racks’ freestyle of his 2011 mixtape Sorry 4 the Wait, Wayne raps.\n\nWake up early in the mornin', eat that pussy like cold pizza", "annotation_id": "23009533", "url": "https://genius.com/23009533", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Nina Ross on the hip, the .44 is my mistress", "vault_lines": ["Nina Ross on the hip, the .44 is my mistress"], "meaning": "This isn’t the first time Lil Wayne personifies his guns, as he stated in 2008’s “Put Some Keys On That:”\n\nAnd these hoes starting to get like fleas on my shit\nHave you seen her? Who? Nina, she’s on my hip\n\nAlso, the nickname “Nina” comes from his gun being a nine millimeter.", "annotation_id": "23009380", "url": "https://genius.com/23009380", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "You now tuned in to the biggest ever YMCMB, Tunechi Lee, Young Angel, Young Lion, man Y’all boys get on a track with Onika Maraj, and y’all still dyin’ Man, what’s up? We in this bitch", "vault_lines": ["And ever since the pandemic", "You now tuned in to the biggest ever", "YMCMB, Tunechi Lee, Young Angel, Young Lion, man", "Y'all boys get on a track with Onika Maraj, and y'all still dyin'", "Man, what's up? We in this bitch"], "meaning": "By recalling the names associated with them, Drake is hyping up that both, he and Lil Wayne, are on the track with Nicki Minaj, whose real name is Onika Tanya Maraj.\n\nYoung Money Cash Money Billionaires (YMCMB) is the collaborative name for Cash Money Records—a record label founded by Birdman in 1991, and signed Lil Wayne as a teenager in 1998—and its subsidiary, Young Money Entertainment created by Lil Wayne in 2005. In 2009, both, Nicki Minaj and Drake, signed-up under Young Money Entertainment.\n\nTunechi (Lil Tunechi/Tunechi Li/Tunechi Lee) is one of Wayne’s nicknames. In the 2010, he shared with MTV News the origin of it:\n\nMy nickname is Lil Tune, my grandmother named me that. She’s passed. My grandmother, her name, was Mercedes Carter. […] She named me Tune, Lil Tune. We just threw the ‘chi’ on there. Like Gucci.\n\nYoung Angel and Young Lion are nicknames Drake and Wayne are using for each other when they collaborate together. Drake calls himself Young Angel, while Lil Wayne refers to himself as Young Lion. The names appeared on “Stuntin'” (2008), “Ignant Shit” (2009), and “Family Feud” (2017).\n\nIt is also most likely a reference to events related to Nicki’s features on Kanye West’s albums. In 2010, as a fresh rapper, Nicki—alongside JAY-Z, Bon Iver, and Rick Ross—featured on “Monster,” the stand-out track from Kanye’s My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy. Kanye almost cut-off the Nicki verse because he thought it was too good and would outshine the whole album, but Nicki convinced him to let the track and her verse stay on the album.\n\nA similar situation happened with “New Body,” a song recorded in 2018 by Kanye, with Nicki Minaj and Ty Dolla $ign as a feature. The demo version leaked for the first time in 2018, as it was originally expected to be a part of West’s Yandhi, but the album was scrapped. In following years, another versions of the song were teased. A year later, Kanye asked Nicki to re-write her verse and song was supposed to be released on Jesus Is King, but didn’t make the cut. In 2020, Kanye announced a new album, and released—and quickly deleted—the album track list which included “New Body.” Kanye’s Donda was finally released in late 2021, but again without that track.", "annotation_id": "23009064", "url": "https://genius.com/23009064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "Who you huggin’, who you wanna be lovin’?", "vault_lines": ["Who you huggin', who you wanna be lovin'?"], "meaning": "Drake calls back to the second verse of Lil' Kim’s 1996 song “Queen Bitch:”\n\nUhh, who you loving? Who you wanna be hugging?\nRoll with niggas that be thugging, bugging", "annotation_id": "23008886", "url": "https://genius.com/23008886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "These bitches’ time tick-tockin’, better stick to dancin’", "vault_lines": ["These bitches' time tick-tockin', better stick to dancin'"], "meaning": "Nicki derides her competition by expressing the plummet in their declining careers, prompting for them to change occupations to save them the trouble of contesting her. The “tick-tockin'” trope alludes to the idea of their careers soon coming to an end—Nicki used similar lyrics on her 2017 hit feature, “Swalla,” in response to her then-rival:\n\nI gave these bitches two years, now your time’s up\nBless her heart, she throwing shots but every line sucks\n\nWhile doing so, she constructs a double-entendre with “tick-tockin',” referencing the famous Chinese social media app TikTok and its medley of users who attempt viral dances on the daily. With the former and latter being nearly homonymous words, the perfect wordplay is set up.\n\nThough, this isn’t her first time including famous social media platforms in her raps to harshly diss her adversaries. On her duo track, “Big Daddy,” off her 2014 album The Pinkprint, she spits:\n\nYour time is ticking, you bitches will be around shorter than Vine vids", "annotation_id": "23008621", "url": "https://genius.com/23008621", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Seeing Green", "line": "I just need a queen that ain’t scared to fuck a Stephen King", "vault_lines": ["I just need a queen that ain't scared to fuck a Stephen King"], "meaning": "Stephen King is a famous author known for his horror novels. He is one of the bestselling writers of all time, so this line could also mirror Wayne’s success in his respective field.", "annotation_id": "23008379", "url": "https://genius.com/23008379", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "status": "done", "targets": 53, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-family-feud-lyrics", "genius_title": "Family Feud", "genius_id": 3402324, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "All this motherfuckin’ beef, I need some rice on this plate", "vault_lines": ["All this motherfuckin' beef, I need some rice on this plate"], "meaning": "“Beef” is another term that is used to describe an altercation or fight. There have been many recorded rap beefs throughout history such as: Drake vs. Meek, and 50 Cent vs. Kanye West.\n\n\n\nWayne uses some wordplay to say that he is tired of so much beef in the rap game he wants something new.", "annotation_id": "13394387", "url": "https://genius.com/13394387", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Here come the leaves, get the rake, and we ain’t leavin’ the rake", "vault_lines": ["Here come the leaves, get the rake, and we ain't leavin' the rake"], "meaning": "The rake is the commission a casino takes out of the pot in a poker game. Weezy is comparing this to the money he is owed by Birdman and Universal Music Group, implying he isn’t leaving without it.", "annotation_id": "13393183", "url": "https://genius.com/13393183", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Yeah, Dedication 6, of course", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, Dedication 6, of course"], "meaning": "This is the first song released off of Dedication 6: Reloaded. Wayne says that he “ended up doing a whole bunch of songs so they wanna split it up” into a two-part Dedication 6 Mixtape.", "annotation_id": "13392112", "url": "https://genius.com/13392112", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "And you know life is a movie, you niggas ratin’ yourself", "vault_lines": ["And you know life is a movie, you niggas ratin' yourself", "Yeah, you know life is a movie, you niggas playin' yourselves, damn"], "meaning": "Wayne repeats the Shakespearean “life’s a stage” theme here to insinuate that other rappers are always explaining their music and hoping it’ll be understood. Wayne on the other hand, is comfortable being misunderstood and as he raps on Deep believes that one should just rap and hope fans of real music will understand even if critics cannot relate to what you’re saying.\nIn fact, this is a common theme among neoplatonic art in which artists find their own idea of beauty and their uniqueness contributes to a sense of beauty.  This idea was captured in Mannerism; an individualistic-style of visual art following the Renaissance period.\nWayne is comfortable being a visionary and\nevolving his own style rather than trying to copy the music surrounding him; which contributes to his vast influence", "annotation_id": "13392035", "url": "https://genius.com/13392035", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I make you pay attention then rip your receipt in your face", "vault_lines": ["I make you pay attention then rip your receipt in your face"], "meaning": "On top of the classic Weezy wordplay of taking a common phrase and using it literally to change its meaning, this line could have several potential meanings:\n\nOn the track “Receipt” from The Carter II, Wayne metaphorically refers to a receipt as the memories and experiences shared during a relationship. Here, Wayne could be reminding Birdman of all the blessings they shared together while also addressing the contempt currently felt by the two.\n\nSince he feels he is owed money from Birdman, Weezy may be hinting that he cares more about the principle of the debt rather than the financial gain. ‘Making you pay’ only to disrespect Birdman by ‘ripping your receipt in your face.’\n\nOn a previous Tunechi/Drake track, “She Will”, featured on Tha Carter IV, Wayne says:\n\nI tried to pay attention but attention paid me\n\nKnown for his clever wordplay, Weezy creates the dual meaning of payment to attention/paying attention and getting paid by/from attention. Once again drawing on this reference, Wayne is saying he will make you pay attention to what he’s doing (perhaps charging you for it) then ripping up the receipt deleting all evidence of the transaction. No refunds.", "annotation_id": "13391995", "url": "https://genius.com/13391995", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "You see blue face hundreds ’til you white in the face", "vault_lines": ["You see blue face hundreds 'til you white in the face"], "meaning": "In this bar, Wayne plays off a color motif with “blue” and “white”. Someone who is dying might have a blueish tint to their face versus a corpse which has a pale-white complexion. Blue-faced 100s could refer to the new $100 bills that have a blue security ribbon; but it also could have subtext calling out Birdman, (who this portion of the verse is directed at), for his greed.", "annotation_id": "13391970", "url": "https://genius.com/13391970", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Talked to your wifey today, she feelin’ triflin’ she say", "vault_lines": ["Talked to your wifey today, she feelin' triflin' she say"], "meaning": "Wayne is referring to Birdman’s potential wife Toni Braxton. Though “triflin” can sometimes mean cheating, it can also refer to how she told Wayne that Birdman and him are still cool.\n\n\n\nIn the line, Weezy is saying that Braxton is in the mood to cheat on Birdman with him. He’s telling Birdman that Lil Wayne is superior and is able to take his woman at any time he desires.", "annotation_id": "13391937", "url": "https://genius.com/13391937", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "These boys Hot Girls too", "vault_lines": [", these boys Hot Girls too"], "meaning": "The Hot Boyz were a hip-hop group composed of  Lil Wayne, Juvenile, B.G. and Turk. Some singles like “Back That Azz Up” were highly influential and their album Guerilla Warfare reached No. 1 on the Billboard Top 100 R&B and Hip Hop Chart in 1999.", "annotation_id": "13391908", "url": "https://genius.com/13391908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Don’t get my credit declined, give all the credit to Moms", "vault_lines": ["Don't get my credit declined, give all the credit to Moms"], "meaning": "Jacida Carter, commonly referred to as Cita or Ms. Cita, is Lil Wayne’s mother. He’s referenced her in songs like “Playing With Fire”. She birthed Wayne at 19. On the Free Weezy Album Lil Wayne pays respect to his mother; thanking her for not getting an abortion and for taking care of Wayne even though it came with many obstacles as a young, single mother. This theme of giving back to his mother is also mentioned on No Ceilings 2 when he raps “I don’t know my daaad, but my momma is rich”.", "annotation_id": "13391858", "url": "https://genius.com/13391858", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "If I ever see Trump, he better salute niggas Much as we do, nigga, for real", "vault_lines": ["If I ever see Trump, he better salute niggas", "Much as we do, nigga, for real"], "meaning": "On November 7, 2015, Donald Trump appeared on SNL and danced to Drake’s hit song “Hotline Bling”. The segment went viral and quickly became one of the most unforgettable moments of the presidential campaign.\n\nDonald Trump used the popularity of Drake’s song and dance moves to gain exposure and make people laugh.  Trump owes Drake credit therefore if the president sees the rapper he better salute him.", "annotation_id": "13390033", "url": "https://genius.com/13390033", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "So tell your squad I say “BLAOW” like Spice 1 would say", "vault_lines": ["So tell your squad I say \"BLAOW\" like Spice 1 would say"], "meaning": "Wayne shouts out the criminally underatted West Coast MC Spice1 who was well known in the 90’s and early 2000’s, especially for his frequent collaborations with other West Coast MC’s like E-40, Too $hort, MC Eiht, Kurupt, Rappin' 4-Tay, and indeed 2Pac.\n\nWeezy specifically refers here to Spice1 and MC Eiht’s 2006 collaborative effort Let it Blow. The chorus of the song included a distinctive gunshot ad-lib\n\nBLAOW\n\nWhich Weezy imitates here, presumably aimed at those trying to finesse him.", "annotation_id": "13389673", "url": "https://genius.com/13389673", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "You playin’ with fire today", "vault_lines": ["You playin' with the right one today", "You playin' with fire today"], "meaning": "To “play with fire” is to do something dangerous that may cause you harm and trouble.\n\nOnce again, Weezy might be talking about Birdman, who refuses to pay him for his music.\n\nLater in this verse, Tune hints at talking to snipers to aim at Baby. Birdman better hurry up and pay him, because Wayne knows all about playing with fire!", "annotation_id": "13389647", "url": "https://genius.com/13389647", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Talked to the sniper, he say he aimin’ at diapers today", "vault_lines": ["Talked to the sniper, he say he aimin' at diapers today"], "meaning": "This is potentially another Birdman diss. The Cash Money Records boss goes by the nickname Baby, and since babies wear diapers, Weezy could be referring to Birdman considering most of his verse is shots at him. Tune might be setting up a sniper to shoot him!", "annotation_id": "13389620", "url": "https://genius.com/13389620", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Oh my God, it feel like I done life in this place But swear to God, I got money doin’ life in my safe", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, alright, I got it, yeah", "Oh my God, lightning strikin' twice in this place", "Oh my God, it feel like I done life in this place", "But swear to God, I got money doin' life in my safe"], "meaning": "This could have 2 meanings:\n\n\nWeezy has been in the rap game for so long that he feels like it has been a lifetime. He started rapping when he was only 8 years old, he is, at the release of this song, 35 years old.\nWeezy has revealed that Cash Money Records are not paying him for his music nor letting him leave the label. He stated that he is a prisoner to them, and since it has been so long, he feels like he is doing a life sentence just like real prisoners.\n\n\n\nEither way, Weezy got enough money in his safe that would last a lifetime, so he got no worries.", "annotation_id": "13389578", "url": "https://genius.com/13389578", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Inspirin’ to the youth", "vault_lines": ["Inspirin' to the youth"], "meaning": "Drake is rightly credited alongside Kanye and Kid Cudi as the artist(s) that brought melody based rap with deeply introspective lyrics to the forefront of\nhip-hop. Which in turn inspired a generation of “youth” to make music in that style. Hence the new wave of artists who are young and making that same music.\n\n\n\nLook no further then 6Lack, Tory Lanez and even many of the “mumble rappers” all of which make music in the same vein and often cite Drake and Kanye West as an influence", "annotation_id": "13389540", "url": "https://genius.com/13389540", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "You wearin’ em tight ones today", "vault_lines": ["You wearin' em tight ones today"], "meaning": "Weezy has already talked about how he influenced other rappers on this song, so he could be making another reference here.\n\nAt the 2011 MTV awards, Wayne performed two hits from Tha Carter IV while wearing tight pants. This has caught the audience’s attention. Now rappers who are heavily influenced by Wayne wear them as well, such as Young Thug and Rich Homie Quan.\n\nSkinny pants and some Vans\nCall me Triple A, get my advance in advance, amen", "annotation_id": "13389288", "url": "https://genius.com/13389288", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "You call her Stephanie, I call her Steph ’round this bitch", "vault_lines": ["You call her Stephanie, I call her Steph 'round this bitch"], "meaning": "Weezy refers to his Young Money artist Stephanie Acevado, who was signed with the label in January 2015. They collaborated on “Crystal Ball” which appeared on No Ceilings 2. On that song, Weezy stated that she walks around his house with nothing but a thong on, so they must be pretty close.\n\n\n\nThis could also be a reference to Young Ma’s most popular song to date, “OOOUUU”:\n\nYou call her Stephanie? I call her Headphanie\n\nThis could also be a shoutout to Nicki Minaj, who is really close friends with Weezy. In her verse on the song Side to Side, she rapped a similar line:\n\nCurry with the shot, just tell ‘em to call me Stephanie", "annotation_id": "13389224", "url": "https://genius.com/13389224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Hey, what’s for dinner? Popeyes", "vault_lines": ["hey, what's for dinner? Popeyes"], "meaning": "This line is a reference to Popeye’s,  a fast food restaurant specializing in fried chicken. It was first founded in 1972 in New Orleans, Louisiana.\n\nLil Wayne is known to be a fan of Popeyes' food. During his 33rd birthday party he indulged in a massive amount of their fried chicken.\n\n\n\nThis line may be a subliminal diss to Birdman: “Popeye’s” could be double entendre for “Pop eyes.” He could be saying that his father-figure’s eye balls are on the menu.", "annotation_id": "13388651", "url": "https://genius.com/13388651", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Oh my God, lightning strikin’ twice in this place Should we make a wish or should we make it right in this place?", "vault_lines": ["Oh my God, lightning strikin' twice in this place", "Should we make a wish or should we make it right in this place?"], "meaning": "There is an old saying that “Lightning never strikes the same place twice.” Essentially, it is used to mean that the odds of something happening twice are astronomically low.\n\nHere, Wayne could be referring to the previous line. The odds of having two greats linking up are extremely slim in the same way that the odds of lighting striking the same place twice would be.\n\n\n\nAnother way of looking at it, is that Birdman referred to Wayne as a son, then proceeded to not pay him, and then reconciled with him only to do it again.", "annotation_id": "13388458", "url": "https://genius.com/13388458", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Young Angel, Young Lion", "vault_lines": ["Young Angel, Young Lion", "The Angel and Lion are greats"], "meaning": "Young Angel and Young Lion are old nicknames that Drake and Lil Wayne had around 2008. These names appear on some of the songs Lil Wayne and Drake are on such as Stuntin- Lil wayne from the Dedication 3 mixtape, and Ignant Shit from So Far Gone.\n\n\n\nLil Wayne was called “young lion,” due to the length of his dreadlocks.", "annotation_id": "13388381", "url": "https://genius.com/13388381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I’m Hall of Fame in the booth, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I'm Hall of Fame in the booth, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake has frequently declared himself as one of the best to ever do it.\n\n\n\nThis appeared very prominent on “Forever” where Drake rapped:\n\nLast name ever, first name greatest\n\nOn “Grammy’s” he declared himself “top 5”.\n\nReferencing his above claim, Drake recanted it on “Gyalchester”, where he said:\n\nI know I said top five, but I’m top two\n\n“Grindin'” saw Drake asking rhetorically “you could ask anyone out here, who running the game? And they’ll tell you the truth”.\nHis wordplay on “Weston Road Flows” at first somewhat modestly proposed that he is “the most successful rapper 35” but then quickly suggested that actually, he is the most successful rapper alive because he’s “assumin' everybody’s 35 and under”\n\n\n\nThese bars continue this trend.\n\nThe line could also possibly be a response to Kendrick Lamar’s jab on “The Heart Part 4” where he likely chastised Drake for comparing himself to Jay-Z on “Summer Sixteen” and on “Sneakin”\n\nHo, JAY-Z Hall of Fame, sit yo' punk-ass down!", "annotation_id": "13388293", "url": "https://genius.com/13388293", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I want Arabian wealth", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, yeah, yeah, I want Arabian wealth", "I want Arabian wealth"], "meaning": "Fortune made a list of the top 10 riches countries in the world, and 3 of them were Middle Eastern countries with Qatar being at #1, Kuwait at #7 and UAE at #8.\n\nOn Forbes’s list of the richest men in the Middle East, Saudi Prince Alwaleed took the #1 spot with a net worth of $17.3 billion. Compared to Wayne’s net worth of $150 million, there is a huge gap. But money is always on Weezy’s mind!\n\n\n\nIn 2008, Lil Wayne appeared on the remix of Busta Rhymes “Arab Money.”", "annotation_id": "13388237", "url": "https://genius.com/13388237", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Yeah, you know life is a movie, you niggas playin’ yourselves, damn", "vault_lines": ["And you know life is a movie, you niggas ratin' yourself", "Yeah, you know life is a movie, you niggas playin' yourselves, damn"], "meaning": "Here Tunechi uses an effective double entendre. He puts his own spin on Shakespeare’s “All the world’s a stage” line, by calling life a film. If that’s the case then everyone is performing as- or “playing themselves”.\n\n   At the same time he is insinuating that the snakes from the previously line are “playing themselves”- essentially screwing themselves over- if they continue with their dishonest ways.", "annotation_id": "13388193", "url": "https://genius.com/13388193", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I could care less ‘bout your squad, I don’t connect when I’m flyin’ I’m on a jet and it’s mine, I just hope the weather is fine I took the jet to Dubai, I took the jet to Milan", "vault_lines": ["I could care less 'bout your squad, I don't connect when I'm flyin'", "I'm on a jet and it's mine, I just hope the weather is fine", "I took the jet to Dubai, I took the jet to Milan"], "meaning": "In 2016, Weezy visited both Milan, Italy and for the first time Dubai, United Arab Emirates. He performed some of his greatest hits there such as “Lollipop” and “No Worries”.\n\nHe even revealed what his favorite thing about Dubai was:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lygfnzRw3GY", "annotation_id": "13388172", "url": "https://genius.com/13388172", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "All you niggas some bitches, I feel like Hef ’round this bitch", "vault_lines": ["All you niggas some bitches, I feel like Hef 'round this bitch"], "meaning": "Hugh Hefner, the founder and face of Playboy, was frequently photographed surrounded by women, especially his Playboy bunnies. In fact, for many years, his Playboy Mansion residence served as an ever-changing, rotating harem of women in his employment.", "annotation_id": "13388078", "url": "https://genius.com/13388078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Your flag is a white one today", "vault_lines": ["Your flag is a white one today"], "meaning": "Raising a white flag means to admit defeat. The ‘White flag’ is thus synonymous for “surrender”.\n\n\n\nLil Wayne is saying that he’s coming for Birdman and that he’ll be victorious.\n\nFurthermore, Wayne is a known ‘red flag representative’, as is Birdman.", "annotation_id": "13388076", "url": "https://genius.com/13388076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "That’s truth, no boost, nigga", "vault_lines": ["That's truth, no boost, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake really made it. His net worth is estimated to be around 90 million dollars around the time of the release of this song.\n\nDrake might also be furthering his previous bars. As he keeps on winning, he’s expanding pools, pools that were already bigger than Ye’s.\n\nIn this context the word “boost” might be referring to Kanye’s “Yeezy Boosts”. As such, Drake is using this bar to say that his pool is expanding, and that his enormous pool isn’t anything like Kanye’s “tiny” pool.\n\n\n\nDrake’s pool on the left, Kanye’s on the right\n\nFurthermore, as Drake is boasting about his success, it might also be a reference to his sneaker collaboration with Jordan, saying that he isn’t with boost and that he is representing Jordan.\n\nLastly, “to boost” also means to lie as well. Drake would thus be saying that he really made all the above mentioned adjustments to his house.", "annotation_id": "13388064", "url": "https://genius.com/13388064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Now back to whippin’ the baby on some step mama shit", "vault_lines": ["Now back to whippin' the baby on some step mama shit"], "meaning": "On the majority of this verse, Weezy is dissing his former “father figure”, mentor and label boss Birdman, who goes by the nickname Baby.\n\nFor a few lines, he took a break from dissing him, now he went back to it just like a step mother would beat her husband’s baby. It is not her real baby, so she won’t care for it as much as she cares for her babies.", "annotation_id": "13388059", "url": "https://genius.com/13388059", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Fuck is the point in all the beefin’ when we really blood?", "vault_lines": ["Fuck is the point in all the beefin' when we really blood?"], "meaning": "Drake continues from the previous line, claiming that the beef between him and Meek is over. The two have collaborated on tracks before, most notably “Amen” from Meek’s Dreams and Nightmares and Dreamchasers 2, as well as “R.I.C.O” from Meek’s album Dreams Worth More Than Money.\n\nDrake is being the bigger person and is trying to figure out why they are beefing when they are basically “blood” brothers. Drake may also be alluding to Meek’s hometown mentioned in the prior line, Philadelphia a.k.a. the City of Brotherly Love.\n\nThe line is also likely a reference to the Birdman-Wayne situation. Since Cash Money presents itself as a family, Drake is saying that there is no need for them to be beefing to the extent that they are. The family is more successful together than apart.", "annotation_id": "13387977", "url": "https://genius.com/13387977", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "The Angel and Lion are greats", "vault_lines": ["Young Angel, Young Lion", "The Angel and Lion are greats"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne and Drake have referred to themselves as Young Lion and Young Angel respectively since at least 2008, on tracks like Stuntin and on posse cuts like Fuck da Bullshit.\n\nDrake also tweeted out this moniker in 2009\n\nWayne is called “Young Lion,” on account of his long dreadlocks, whereas “Young Angel,” likely refers to Drake’s softer, more sensitive image (at least back in 2008). Note that the “Young” has been dropped here, likely because neither Drake (31) nor Weezy (35) are actually that young any more.\n\nWayne uses the line to suggest that the duo are one of the greatest in hip-hop to date.", "annotation_id": "13387974", "url": "https://genius.com/13387974", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I come direct with my shit, I come correct with my shit A blank check on your face, put some respek on my shit", "vault_lines": ["I come direct with my shit, I come correct with my shit", "A blank check on your face, put some respek on my shit"], "meaning": "In 2016, Birdman sat with the Breakfast Club for an interview, after a small argument he started telling them to put respect on his name.\n\nAin’t no issue, if there was a issue, you’ll feel me. I just come here to let y'all know to put some respect on my name. Are y'all finished or are y'all done? I ain’t got no more talkin'\n\nIn 2014, Weezy revealed that Birdman was not paying him for his music (Tha Carter V, specifically). Now he asks him to respect his hustle and pay him for his hard work.\n\n\n\nA “blank check on your face” could also insinuate that there’s a limitless bounty on your head. “Your” being Birdman’s head.", "annotation_id": "13387951", "url": "https://genius.com/13387951", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I took the jet way to Africa, shout out my nigga Akon", "vault_lines": ["I took the jet to Dubai, I took the jet to Milan", "I took the jet way to Africa, shout out my nigga Akon"], "meaning": "Akon is an American rapper and singer who spent his childhood in Senegal, West-Africa, where his family is originally from.\n\nAkon has done a lot for the continent, starting the “Akon Lighting Africa” initiative in 2014. Since then, over 80 million people have been provided with electricity.\n\n\n\nLil Wayne worked together with Akon on his 2008 hit “I’m So Paid”.", "annotation_id": "13387930", "url": "https://genius.com/13387930", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "But this isn’t all about callin’ truce I’m still dishin’ out verbal abuse That shit could get re-introduced If somebody got somethin’ they urgin’ to prove, nigga", "vault_lines": ["But this isn't all about callin' truce", "I'm still dishin' out verbal abuse", "That shit could get re-introduced", "If somebody got somethin' they urgin' to prove, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake insinuates that even though he’s attempting to mend his relationship with Meek Mill (as the previous lines show), other rappers should be weary of dissing Drake as he will respond aggressively.\n\nIn 2015, Drake and Meek engaged in a feud that saw the two drop numerous diss tracks and subliminal lines at one another. Drake has previously fired his “verbal abuse” at Meek in “Back To Back”, at Kid Cudi and Pusha T on “Two Birds, One Stone”, and at Common on “Stay Schemin’”.", "annotation_id": "13387924", "url": "https://genius.com/13387924", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Somebody get Larry Jackson on the phone I need some ownership if we pressin’ go ‘Cause business is boomin’ on behalf of me I need a bite outta the Apple like Adam and Eve", "vault_lines": ["Somebody get Larry Jackson on the phone", "I need some ownership if we pressin' go", "'Cause business is boomin' on behalf of me", "I need a bite outta the Apple like Adam and Eve"], "meaning": "Apple Music has made it their goal to dominate the streaming market by acquiring exclusive rights to music made by big name artists. In 2015, Drake inked a partnership with Apple Music for $19 million dollars. Drake is frequently listed among the most streamed artists, so it was indeed a blockbuster move on behalf of Apple.\n\nLarry Jackson is the head of original content at Apple Music. Drake feels that he needs to be on his side to successfully re-negotiate his contract.\n\nThe line “I need a bite outta the apple” is both a spin on the idiom “slice of the pie” which means to get your share of a profit and a reference to the Garden of Eden from Genesis 3:6 where Eve ate from the forbidden fruit of knowledge. It just so happens that Apple’s logo is of an apple with a missing chunk:\n\n\n\nFurthermore, “business is booming” could be a reference to Benjamin Kicks, who uses the phrase frequently.", "annotation_id": "13387909", "url": "https://genius.com/13387909", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "This for Cita and Nae, this for my people and bae I got three sons of a gun, them niggas keep me on safe", "vault_lines": ["This for Cita and Nae, this for my people and bae", "I got three sons of a gun, them niggas keep me on safe"], "meaning": "Weezy shouts out his family; His mother Jacida Carter who he calls “Ms. Cita,” his daughter Reginae Carter who goes by the nickname “Nae,” and his 3 sons Dwayne Michael Carter III, Cameron Carter, and Neal Carter.\n\n\n\nWayne also refers to himself here as a gun, meaning he would naturally go off and cause some violence. However, having children to take care of has kept him from doing anything that would put him in prison and not being able to see his children.", "annotation_id": "13387900", "url": "https://genius.com/13387900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "These chickens got bird flu, and they chirp, chirp too Always sung, never flew", "vault_lines": ["These chickens got bird flu, and they chirp, chirp too", "Always sung, never flew"], "meaning": "This is a subliminal diss to Birdman, Wayne is insinuating that there is something wrong with him. This is done in a very clever way using “bird flu,” as some sort of disease metaphor. He goes on to say that Birdman is all bark no bite in a clever way; “Always sung, never flew.”\n\n\n\nThis also correlates to the previous line due to Popeye’s being a fried chicken place. Fun fact: Popeye’s is a store founded in NO, Louisianna, Which is also the place Lil wayne is from.", "annotation_id": "13387778", "url": "https://genius.com/13387778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Put on the gloves like MJ, go from OG to OJ", "vault_lines": ["Put on the gloves like MJ, go from OG to OJ"], "meaning": "“MJ,” short for Michael Jackson, famously rocked a single white glove during shows. Throughout his career he became an icon, considered to be the “King of Pop”, and the person who popularized the dance move Moonwalk.\n\n\n\n“OJ” refers to O.J. Simpson, who was accused, but found not guilty, of the murder of his ex-wife, despite how the evidence stacked against him. Arguably the most pivotal moment of the case was when O.J. tried on the gloves he was accused of wearing during the murder but struggled to put them on, pushing the jury to find him not guilty.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=16KaoVmVTPE", "annotation_id": "13387766", "url": "https://genius.com/13387766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Branches startin’ to shake, here comes the leaves, get the rake I want my piece of the cake, it should be sweeter than cake", "vault_lines": ["Branches startin' to shake, here comes the leaves, get the rake", "I want my piece of the cake, it should be sweeter than cake"], "meaning": "Wayne uses the term “leaves” to refer to “money” on other songs in Dedication 6 such as: “XO Tour Life”. He’s going to make so much money he’ll need a garden tool to “rake it in.”", "annotation_id": "13387676", "url": "https://genius.com/13387676", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Angel hair at 2AM for Bey and Hov Just to show ’em how I treat the city like my humble abode", "vault_lines": ["I flipped the bird at the Bird –", "Angel hair at 2AM for Bey and Hov", "Just to show 'em how I treat the city like my humble abode"], "meaning": "A reference to capellini pasta—known colloquially as “angel hair\"—a thin, delicate noodle. Drake may be hinting at the fact he took JAY-Z and Beyoncé out to dinner in Toronto after he had attended Hov’s “4:44 Tour” show on November 24, 2017. To get the \"Family Feud” instrumental from Young Guru he would have needed clearance from Hov—what better time to ask than over a pasta dinner?\n\n“Hair” may also be a near-homophone of “here”. As far back as Lil Wayne’s Dedication 3 from 2008, Drake has been referring to himself as “Young Angel” and Lil Wayne as “Young Lion” on some of their collaborative tracks. One of Jay’s lyrical techniques is the use of near-homophones to create extra meaning.\n\nDrake also draws comparisons between the way JAY-Z (Hov) speaks about Brooklyn and Marcy Projects being his home (notably on “Marcy Me” off 4:44, the record this beat came from) and the way Drake interacts with his own hometown, Toronto. This was a prevalent theme on Drake’s 2016 album Views.\n\nDrake, Lil Wayne, and fellow Young Money artist Nicki Minaj have all compared themselves to JAY-Z on numerous occasions during their careers.", "annotation_id": "13387650", "url": "https://genius.com/13387650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "If money grow on trees, I climb and rest in that bitch Build a treehouse and knock the bird nest out that bitch Stuck my neck out and shit, then got the heck out that shit And when I did, I took the special effect out of that shit Tunechi F ’round this bitch, the best thing left ’round this bitch", "vault_lines": ["I flipped the bird at the Bird –", "If money grow on trees, I climb and rest in that bitch", "Build a treehouse and knock the bird nest out that bitch", "Stuck my neck out and shit, then got the heck out that shit", "And when I did, I took the special effect out of that shit", "Tunechi F 'round this bitch, the best thing left 'round this bitch"], "meaning": "Wayne suggests that he wants to build a home (or a record label) in the “money tree” as well as to knock out others who might be nesting there, specifically birds, i.e. Birdman, the CEO of Cash-Money with whom Wayne remains locked in a bitter legal battle with over royalties.\n\nWayne goes on to refer to how hard he has had to work for Cash Money Records, even though he was not properly remunerated, perhaps to the tune of some $51 Million and because of this, and the stress it has caused him, he had to leave the label.\n\nLike previously mentioned on “Sorry 4 The Wait 2”:\n\nCash Money is an army I’m a one man army\n\n\n\nHe feels as though because of his departure he removed the one thing that held Cash Money Records together for so long, namely him. Moreover, his departure took the ‘special effect’ out of the money tree. Cash-Money is nothing without him, in other words.", "annotation_id": "13387625", "url": "https://genius.com/13387625", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Pick you off like pickin’ peppers, Peter Piper today", "vault_lines": ["Pick you off like pickin' peppers, Peter Piper today"], "meaning": "“Peter Piper” is a nursery rhyme filled with “p” alliteration. The tongue twister is generally said as fast as one can possibly say it, as the alliteration makes the rhyme hard to complete. Though the first written form of Peter Piper dates back to 1813 in Peter Piper’s Practical Principles of Plain and Perfect Pronunciation by John Harris, many suspect the rhyme was said earlier than that.\n\nIt is believed that “Peter Piper” refers to Pierre Poivre. A French horticulturist, Pierre was responsible for growing many plants from foreign, including spices such as cloves.\n\nThe tongue-twister reads as the following:\n\nPeter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers.\nA peck of pickled peppers Peter Piper picked.\nIf Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers,\nWhere’s the peck of pickled peppers Peter Piper picked?", "annotation_id": "13387579", "url": "https://genius.com/13387579", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "More Life, no dyin’", "vault_lines": ["More Life, no dyin'"], "meaning": "Drake is shamelessly plugging his early 2017 project called More Life which he described as “a playlist with all original music”.\n\nAlso, “more life” is a Jamaican greeting used to wish somebody (more) success.", "annotation_id": "13387569", "url": "https://genius.com/13387569", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Shout out Guru for sendin’ me the beat, too", "vault_lines": ["Shout out Guru for sendin' me the beat, too"], "meaning": "This song is a remix of JAY-Z’s standout 4:44 track “Family Feud.” Lil Wayne and Drake had to ask Young Guru, Hov’s personal engineer and keeper of his hard drives, for the instrumental.\n\nHov name-drops Guru on the intro of his version too:\n\nTurn my vocal up, Guru!", "annotation_id": "13387550", "url": "https://genius.com/13387550", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "It’s Mortal Kombat tonight, it’s Street Fighter today I’m Major Bison, Drizzy lit like Raiden light up the place", "vault_lines": ["It's Mortal Kombat tonight, it's Street Fighter today", "I'm Major Bison, Drizzy lit like Raiden light up the place"], "meaning": "Mortal Kombat and Street Fighter are iconic fighting game franchises that were released on multiple consoles starting as early as the SNES and Sega Genesis.\n\nWhile both games involve opponents fighting each other until knocked out, Mortal Kombat is known for additionally letting the winner execute his opponent in gruesome, gory ways in a move called “Fatality”.\n\n\n\nWayne likens himself and Drake to two popular characters from these games. M. Bison is a Street Fighter character wearing a military outfit. In Mortal Kombat, Raiden is a sort of god who can control lightning (therefore “lighting up” the place).\n\nWayne referencing these two games is meant to state that he isn’t playing around and that anyone opposing him and Drake will get beaten up or killed.\n\nThis also references their co-headlining tour “Drake vs. Lil Wayne”, where both artists competed against each other to determine who “is the best rapper in the world.” The tour poster used the same art style as Street Fighter games and was officially supported by Capcom, the game’s developer.", "annotation_id": "13387517", "url": "https://genius.com/13387517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I make the crib, expandin’ pools and expandin’ rooms Addin’ Hammam Spas with tannin’ booths, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I make the crib, expandin' pools and expandin' rooms", "Addin' Hammam Spas with tannin' booths, nigga"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his house that is currently under development in his hometown, Toronto. The house is being built from the ground up, and will be 40,00 square feet when it is completed.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BRy8kzolj_1/\n\nDrake also refers again to his swimming pools, after boasting about the size of his swimming pool in Summer Sixteen:\n\nNow I got a house in LA, Now I got a bigger pool than Ye\nAnd look man, Ye’s pool is nice, mine’s just bigger’s what I’m saying\nI’m that nigga’s what I’m saying", "annotation_id": "13387508", "url": "https://genius.com/13387508", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I taught these boys how to lean", "vault_lines": ["I taught these hoes how to love,", "I taught these boys how to lean"], "meaning": "Lean is a purple drank that requires promethazine and original sprite soda to make. Its side effects include dizziness, blurred vision, nausea & memory problems.\n\nIn 2007, Wayne was arguably the hottest rapper out, and he used to rap about lean a lot. He carried a double cup with him everywhere he went. He even made a song about it called “Me & My Drank”.\n\nNowadays, a lot of new rappers talk about it, including Lil Pump, Young Thug, Kodak Black, Rich The Kid, who also post pictures/videos of them on social media drinking it. Even Future shouted out Weezy for it on one of his most popular songs:\n\nDouble cups like Tunechi, yeah\nBust it down with these goonies, yeah", "annotation_id": "13387505", "url": "https://genius.com/13387505", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I taught these hoes how to love,", "vault_lines": ["I taught these hoes how to love,", "I taught these boys how to lean"], "meaning": "In 2011, Wayne released a song that was called “How To Love”, which was the third single from his multi-platinum album Tha Carter IV. Wayne has said this song was for the women:\n\nThe song is about women who don’t know how to love because there’s deep reasons for them not knowing how to love…And what I mean by deep reasons is deep and dark reasons.\n\nThis song peaked at #5 on the Billboard Hot 100 and it reached over a hundred million views on YouTube.", "annotation_id": "13387504", "url": "https://genius.com/13387504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "I flipped the bird at the Bird –", "vault_lines": ["I flipped the bird at the Bird –"], "meaning": "“Flipping the bird” means giving someone the middle finger. Here, Lil Wayne is speaking about his feud with Birdman, his old mentor and founder of Cash Money Records. Though Birdman was highly regarded as a “father figure” to Wayne, the two hit a rough patch in 2014 when Wayne called out Birdman over the delay in releasing his album, Tha Carter V.", "annotation_id": "13387494", "url": "https://genius.com/13387494", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "New Years Eve, lookin’ like a royal flush Wait, we all in the same suit", "vault_lines": ["New Years Eve, lookin' like a royal flush", "Wait, we all in the same suit"], "meaning": "Drake references the royal flush, which is a hand in Poker where you have a straight with face cards and they are all the same suit. Drake and his squad show up to New Years all wearing the same suit, hence, looking like a royal flush.", "annotation_id": "13387484", "url": "https://genius.com/13387484", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "You snakes come make me a belt", "vault_lines": ["You snakes come make me a belt"], "meaning": "Some high-end belts are made from snakeskin.  Wayne may be saying he is going to metaphorically kill any snakes that come after him and use their skin as his victory belt.\n\nWeezy also may be referring to belts sold by the high end clothing brand Gucci; these belts sport a prominent snake logo.", "annotation_id": "13387445", "url": "https://genius.com/13387445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "We gon’ have to break the billi’ curse I need my paper long like “A Milli” verse Or too long like a sentence from a Philly judge", "vault_lines": ["We gon' have to break the billi' curse", "I need my paper long like \"A Milli\" verse", "Or too long like a sentence from a Philly judge"], "meaning": "The “billi' curse”, short for billionaire curse, is the curse that no rapper has been able to attain one billion dollars net worth. In recent years, rappers Jay-Z and Diddy were racing to see who could become the first, however neither has been able to.\n\nDrake is saying his goal is to break the curse, being the first to earn a billion dollars net worth. “A Milli” is attributed to being one of Lil Wayne’s most popular songs. He actually raps for over three minutes, with verses that are essentially not even separated, making it a “long verse”. The phrase A Milli was popularized by Senator Everett Dirksen. Dirksen famously stated, “A million here, a million there, and pretty soon you’re talking real money!”\n\nDrake compares the amount of money he’d like to the amount of time fellow rapper, Philadelphia native Meek Mill who previously had beef with Drake, received from his judge. This controversial sentencing of 2 to 4 years in prison has sparked an outrage from the rap community, including Jay-Z, Rick Ross and even Drake to come by his side. A Milli also sounds like Mill, thus making the two a homophone. ‘’ A milli Verse\" could also be in reference to a Meek Mill Verse, (as meek normally refers to himself as Meek Milly) and he mostly does 16 bars or more in his verses.", "annotation_id": "13387396", "url": "https://genius.com/13387396", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Ayy, tell me if TD Bank is approvin’ loans I’m thinkin’ of payin’ Wayne what Universal owes My nigga spent a lifetime goin’ platinum and gold He should own half of the label, shit outta control", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, tell me if TD Bank is approvin' loans", "I'm thinkin' of payin' Wayne what Universal owes", "My nigga spent a lifetime goin' platinum and gold", "He should own half of the label, shit outta control"], "meaning": "Here Drake references Lil Wayne’s feud with Universal Music Group.\n\nBack in early 2016, Lil Wayne filed a lawsuit against UMG, citing millions of dollars he is said to be owed for discovering artists such as Drake, Nicki Minaj, and Tyga. Wayne’s attorneys released a statement saying:\n\nUniversal should be grateful and respectful to Lil Wayne for the millions of dollars in distribution fees and profits they have earned on the artists he brought to the company, instead of seizing all of his profits on those artists in a desperate attempt to recoup the tens of millions of dollars they are owed by Cash Money Records,\n\nDrake obviously isn’t fond of this situation and is hoping that Toronto Dominion Bank will give him a loan to pay Wayne the nearly $100 million that he believes is being withheld from him.\n\nHe also cites Lil Wayne’s lifetime of success in addition to the successes of the talent he helped discover and nurture, believing that UMG might not be where they are without Wayne’s help.", "annotation_id": "13387395", "url": "https://genius.com/13387395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Family Feud", "line": "Super Bowl goals, I’m at the crib with Puff He got Kaepernick on the phone, he in a whole different mode", "vault_lines": ["Super Bowl goals, I'm at the crib with Puff", "He got Kaepernick on the phone, he in a whole different mode"], "meaning": "Drake reworks the lines said by Jay-Z on the original version of this song from 4:44:\n\nSuper Bowl goals\nMy wife in the crib feedin' the kids liquid gold\nWe in a whole different mode\n\nSean “Puffy” Combs recently revealed his Super Bowl goals and caused an uproar on social media when he tweeted that he was heavily interested in buying the Carolina Panthers and signing Colin Kaepernick—the free-agent quarterback known for protesting the national anthem by kneeling—to diversify the ownership of the NFL.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Diddy/status/942572662311841797\n\nIt’s interesting that Drake mentions hanging with Puffy considering the conflicts they’ve had in the past. Around Christmas of 2017, Drake spent time with Diddy at his mansion in Holmby Hills, so it’s safe to say that both men have squashed their feud.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BcjmAKljEQB/?hl=en&taken-by=champagnepapi", "annotation_id": "13387394", "url": "https://genius.com/13387394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 39, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Headie-one-and-drake-only-you-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Only You Freestyle", "genius_id": 5611509, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "No rap cap, we just speak what the truth is (Told me turn, uh)", "vault_lines": ["No rap cap, we just speak what the truth is (Told me turn, uh)"], "meaning": "Headie switches his flow at this line.", "annotation_id": "31969916", "url": "https://genius.com/31969916", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Don’t make me have to rise my rifle", "vault_lines": ["Don't make me have to rise my rifle", "Man try send some young boys for me, don't make me have to ride by high school", "Shit you man been droppin' lately, don't make me have to fly my iTunes", "So much people buy into my hype, don't make me have to buy my hype too", "Dealt with the big homie already, don't make me have to side-by-side you"], "meaning": "Starting from this line, Drake switches his flow making it untimed until near the end of his verse.", "annotation_id": "31901987", "url": "https://genius.com/31901987", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Yeah, gyal just came to the booth and asked for a wheel She never heard drill in Atlanta", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, gyal just came to the booth and asked for a wheel", "She never heard drill in Atlanta"], "meaning": "The girl Drake is with is asking him for a wheel, the type that’s used for buffing, grinding, and cleaning. She’s thinking about the wrong kind of drill. While he’s talking about drill music, she’s thinking about the drill tool.\n\nShe might have never heard of drill music in Atlanta because Atlanta is more of a trap music place which revolves around drugs and drug dealing. Drill is more about violence and street activity, and is a more popular music choice in certain cities such as Chicago, Brooklyn/NYC, or the UK.", "annotation_id": "28617670", "url": "https://genius.com/28617670", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Lord forgive them, they don’t know what they’re doing (Told me turn)", "vault_lines": ["Lord forgive them, they don't know what they're doing (Told me turn)"], "meaning": "Headie One uses a Biblical reference from Luke 23:34 to add emphasis on how his oppositions are underestimating Headie and his crew:\n\nThen Jesus said, “Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they are doing”", "annotation_id": "23830305", "url": "https://genius.com/23830305", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Bentley, Range, then Bentley (One)", "vault_lines": ["Bentley, Range, then Bentley (One)"], "meaning": "He now has enough money to choose between the finest automobiles. In April 2020 he left prison in a hellicopter and jumped straight into his Bentley:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Ace_QK/status/1245689382566322179\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eRZ0qyfjZVk&t=1s", "annotation_id": "21036893", "url": "https://genius.com/21036893", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Them times it was M way (What?) I mean M way, T house, M way (Huh?) Now it’s penty, uptown penty (Huh?)", "vault_lines": ["Them times it was M way (What?)", "I mean M way, T house, M way (Huh?)", "Now it's penty, uptown penty (Huh?)"], "meaning": "M-way refers to the motorway in the UK, the equivalent of the highway in America.\n\n\n\nT-house refers to the traphouse, a derelict house where illigal drugs are manufactured, packaged or sold.\n\n\n\nHeadie One is reminiscing the early days of travelling down the m-way to go to the t-house in the countryside. This is in constrast to his current lifestyle where he frequents uptown in the city. The penty is his penthouse.", "annotation_id": "21036704", "url": "https://genius.com/21036704", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "CC bag look nice, but the Birkin bag look way more heavy", "vault_lines": ["CC bag look nice, but the Birkin bag look way more heavy"], "meaning": "Drakes taunts all those who put too much value on ‘CC’ (Chanel) bags explaining that even though they are considered a luxury they are still pretty low-end compared to what he gets for his girl which is the famed Hermes Birken Bag a bag that usually costs around $40,000 to $500,000 for a single bag.", "annotation_id": "20438445", "url": "https://genius.com/20438445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Yeah, can’t backchat to the Prezi’", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, can't backchat to the Prezi'"], "meaning": "“Prezi,” short for President, is a Jamaican slang term for a leader in certain inner-city communities. The most famous “Prezi” was perhaps the notorious Christopher ‘Dudus’ Coke, the feared drug lord and ex-leader for the community of Tivoli Gardens in Kingston, Jamaica. He was eventually arrested in 2010 and sentenced to 23 years in federal prison shortly after.\n\nDrakes calls himself a “Prezi” because of his leadership role on many fronts; amongst his peers, in the music scene, and in other regards.", "annotation_id": "20438413", "url": "https://genius.com/20438413", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Used to do lean and Wock’", "vault_lines": ["Used to do lean and Wock'"], "meaning": "Drake looks back at the time when he used to drink lean (also known as Purple Drank and Sizzurp, which is a mixture of codeine cough syrup, soda, and hard candy.) using his preferred brand Wockhardt aka ‘Wock’ a name popularized by hip hop artists for the pharmaceutical manufacturer of the codeine-based syrup.", "annotation_id": "20438371", "url": "https://genius.com/20438371", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Niggas want peace like Cassava But we let bridge dem burn like grabba", "vault_lines": ["Niggas want peace like Cassava", "But we let bridge dem burn like grabba"], "meaning": "Drake makes a play on dancehall artist Mavado’s hometown of Cassava Piece in Kingston, Jamaica. Many believe these bars are directed at Mavado who once shared a friendship with Drake, even co-starring in the music video for his May 2010 song, “Find Your Love,” until their relationship turned sour possibly due to a failed mutual endeavour. Furthermore, OVO signee Popcaan, whom Drake gave a shoutout in the previous line, has beef with Mavado, which is another likely reason for Drake’s alleged shot at him here.\n\n“Grabba” is a natural tobacco leaf that’s commonly used in Jamaica as a cigar blunt wrapper or filler and is oftentimes mixed and smoked with marijuana. Drake draws a comparison between the burning of Grabba and the burning of a bridge, as “burn bridges” means to cut or sever ties with someone. It is implied that Mavado and his associates want peace, but Drake has no interest in mending friendships, as he’s willing to burn the bridge between them.\n\nThree days after the song’s release, Mavado seemingly replied to Drake in an Instagram post with the caption, “From you a look clout, a copper to you mouth.” And six days after that, Mavado released “Enemy Line,” which is presumedly his response to Drake’s sneak diss.", "annotation_id": "20432367", "url": "https://genius.com/20432367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "She give headie, headie My tracksuit Louis, now covered in Fenty", "vault_lines": ["She give headie, headie", "My tracksuit Louis, now covered in Fenty"], "meaning": "Headie plays on his own name as “head” is slang for oral sex.\n\nLouis Vuitton is a French luxury clothing brand while Fenty Beauty is Rihanna’s cosmetics brand. As a result of receiving oral sex, Headie’s tracksuit is stained with his girl’s Fenty makeup.", "annotation_id": "20401994", "url": "https://genius.com/20401994", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Bro-bro chinged up an opp Boy, I swear he got chinged already (They told me turn, uh)", "vault_lines": ["Bro-bro chinged up an opp", "Boy, I swear he got chinged already (They told me turn, uh)", "Then he got shot, Nathan Tettey (They told me turn)"], "meaning": "“Chinged” is British slang for getting stabbed, while an “opp” boy is an enemy, rival, or opposing gang member. Headie’s mocking one of his opps for not only being stabbed once but being stabbed again following the first time.", "annotation_id": "20390381", "url": "https://genius.com/20390381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Comin’ for the jungle, you know where the zoo is (They told me turn, uh)", "vault_lines": ["Comin' for the jungle, you know where the zoo is (They told me turn, uh)"], "meaning": "“Zoo” and “jungle” are both names for a wild and dangerous area. However, Headie may also be making a nod to South London’s drill crew Zone 2 and their December 2017 mixtape Known Zoo. The title was referring to the crew’s area, Peckham, which is considered a violent area of London.\n\nHeadie’s drill crew, OFB, and Zone 2 have collaborated on several songs, including May 2017’s “Things on Things.”  and August 2019’s “Times Three.”", "annotation_id": "20389772", "url": "https://genius.com/20389772", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Got this cutie, she half-Cuban Probably why she like playin’ with the Cuban (Turn)", "vault_lines": ["Got this cutie, she half-Cuban", "Probably why she like playin' with the Cuban (Turn)"], "meaning": "“The Cuban” Headie’s girl likes playing with is the Miami Cuban Link, a gold chain. It’s a popular style of jewellery amongst rappers, originating in the 1970s.", "annotation_id": "20389712", "url": "https://genius.com/20389712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "CPS murder in the dock like Rupert (Suh, suh, suh, suh)", "vault_lines": ["CPS murder in the dock like Rupert (Suh, suh, suh, suh)"], "meaning": "Here Headie talks about murder and its subsequent consequences, segueing neatly from the previous line about Boogie’s prosecution.\n\nIn a clever double entendre based around the words ‘murder’ and ‘dock’ (the box a criminal stands in in court), he namedrops Rupert Murdoch, a US media mogul who founded News Corp and Fox News channels.\n\n\n\n An old-fashioned court dock.\n“CPS” refers to the UK’s Crown Prosecution Service, the government authority that deals with crime and justice, and would put people ‘in the dock’.", "annotation_id": "20389231", "url": "https://genius.com/20389231", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Raemi just turned mommy, and that’s my sis, so I just turned abti", "vault_lines": ["Raemi just turned mommy, and that's my sis, so I just turned abti"], "meaning": "Raemiah Julianna is a woman that’s associated with Drake. It has been reported that Rae is actually Drake’s half-sister, although neither has confirmed it. Additionally, Drake called Rae his sister numerous times on social media.\n\n\n\nRaemiah and Drake\n\n“Abti” is Somali for uncle from your mom’s side. If Rae had a child, that would make Drake an abti or an uncle. Drake’s use of Somali on the track is likely influenced by his upbringing in Toronto.", "annotation_id": "20389169", "url": "https://genius.com/20389169", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "My next one from yard love reggae (Shawty)", "vault_lines": ["My next one from yard love reggae (Shawty)"], "meaning": "‘Yard’ is Jamaican patois and London slang for your home, or slang for Jamaica itself. Hence he is likely referring to a woman from Jamaica or one of Jamaican descent. Reggae is a Jamaican music genre, which Headie asserts she loves. ‘Shawty’ is slang used to address women, which provides further clarity.", "annotation_id": "20389131", "url": "https://genius.com/20389131", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "I done run through so much Scottish notes", "vault_lines": ["I done run through so much Scottish notes"], "meaning": "Headie One (back when he was known as Headz) was arrested in Aberdeen, Scotland, and was sentenced to do 30 months after carrying £21,600 worth of cocaine as well as £16,320 worth of heroin.\n\nScotland, being a part of the United Kingdom, uses Great British Pounds (GBP, or £) as its currency. However, some Scottish banks issue their own banknotes that’re distinct to the ones issued in the rest of the UK (Northern Ireland, another part of the UK, also has its own notes).", "annotation_id": "20389020", "url": "https://genius.com/20389020", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Say she like chillin’ with the ooters, booters", "vault_lines": ["Say she like chillin' with the ooters, booters"], "meaning": "An “ooter” and “booter” are both slang terms for a shooter, or more generally speaking, a gang member.", "annotation_id": "20389004", "url": "https://genius.com/20389004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Put a dark in the blue, then we put it in the red, Balotelli", "vault_lines": ["Put a dark in the blue, then we put it in the red, Balotelli"], "meaning": "Mario Ballotelli is an Italian professional footballer, who played for Inter Milan from 2007 to 2010 and Manchester City from 2010 to 2013. Both teams' home kit is blue. He then went to play for AC Milan from 2013 to 2014 and Liverpool from 2014 to 2016. Both teams' home kit is red.\n\n“Dark” is slang for heroin. Headie likens the act of moving “dark” to Mario moving football teams. Mario also has a notable dark complexion, furthering Headie’s wordplay.", "annotation_id": "20388987", "url": "https://genius.com/20388987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "I never even drive no whips I own, so they all look way too dusty", "vault_lines": ["I never even drive no whips I own, so they all look way too dusty"], "meaning": "Drake has quite the car collection, most of which rarely makes a public appearance likely due to Drake never driving them. A notable car of his that’s rarely seen in public is his 2013 Bentley Continental GTC V8, which costed him just under $200,000.\n\nThe car made an appearance in the music video for Drake’s February 2013 song “Started from the Bottom” and hasn’t been seen since.", "annotation_id": "20388891", "url": "https://genius.com/20388891", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Didn’t ever have to rise that dots It was just under my bed and ready (Turn)", "vault_lines": ["Didn't ever have to rise that dots", "It was just under my bed and ready (Turn)"], "meaning": "“Dot” is UK slang for a shotgun. Headie often uses the term in his music, like his July 2019 song “Back to Basics” for example:\n\nBring out the dots, get back to basics", "annotation_id": "20388797", "url": "https://genius.com/20388797", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Oh, how we hate leggies (Suh-suh) How much points have the gang got? Many (One)", "vault_lines": ["Oh, how we hate leggies (Suh-suh)", "How much points have the gang got? Many (One)"], "meaning": "A “leggie” is UK slang for a leg stab wound. Headie hate leggies because he would rather use more lethal force than stabbing someone. This ties into the next line where he mentions “points,” which is another word for a murder.\n\nHeadie One is from Tottenham, an area in North London known for its violence due to the amount of beefs between gangs there. Also, OFB’s January 2018 song “Know Better,” in which Headie is a featured artist, was said to have “caused a wave of killings” between rival gangs in North London.\n\nThe “points” line also echo a similar line from his January 2020 Fred again.. collaboration, “Charades”:\n\nHow many man have the gangdem got?", "annotation_id": "20388711", "url": "https://genius.com/20388711", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Six litres flying through Epsom (Turn, turn, turn) I swear I’ve seen life from both perspectives (They told me turn, uh)", "vault_lines": ["Six litres flying through Epsom (Turn, turn, turn)", "I swear I've seen life from both perspectives (They told me turn, uh)"], "meaning": "Epsom is a town in Surrey, South-East England.\n\nHeadie One comes from Broadwater Estate in North London, an area notorious for its long history of gang activity. London gangs are known to operate so called county line drug networks, wherein they travel to neighbouring areas outside of London and big cities to transport and sell drugs—hence, travelling to Epsom.\n\nWhile travelling, Headie has driven through wealthier, and more upper-class towns in expensive cars with six litre engines, which vastly contrast his own upbringing in the gritty blocks of his North London estate.", "annotation_id": "20388639", "url": "https://genius.com/20388639", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Man said they would do this and that, but the man wasn’t really that wassi, was he?", "vault_lines": ["Man said they would do this and that, but the man wasn't really that wassi, was he?", "Nah, nah, nah, the man wasn't really that what?"], "meaning": "The word “wassy” is patois for crazy. It’s used both in Toronto, where Drake is from, and in the UK. Drake has previously used the term on tracks like his July 2015 Meek Mill diss track “Back to Back”:\n\nPlease, somebody stop me\nI’m talkin' boasy and gwanin wassy\n\nThis is also a reference to Kwasi Skene-Peters aka Wassi, who was a well-known member of the Driftwood Crips in Toronto. He was killed on July 25th, 2015 in a shootout with police while on the run for a double homicide. Wassi was the best friend of rapper Pressa, who has been shouted out by Drake multiple times, including on his 2019 single “War”:\n\nWassa, wassa, just like the crodie Pressa, I don’t do no passa\n\nPressa and his clique named themselves Wass Gang in honor of their fallen friend. Members of this crew are referred to as Wassas.", "annotation_id": "20388626", "url": "https://genius.com/20388626", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "If me and Gallest go St. Michael, the gyal come way too fussy, crushy", "vault_lines": ["If me and Gallest go St. Michael, the gyal come way too fussy, crushy"], "meaning": "St. Michael is a parish located in Barbados. Prior to the release of “Only You Freestyle,” Drake was spotted in Barbados on vacation, where he visited the childhood home of his ex-girlfriend, Rihanna and met up with her brother, Rorrey Fenty.\n\n‘Gallest’ is Rorrey’s Instagram username. After the song’s release, he posted a picture of him, Drake, and Popcaan with the caption “Somewhere In St. Michael.”\n\n“Fussy” is Bajan slang for being happy or excited, meaning when Drake goes out, girls get excited and crush on him. This may be a subtle shoutout to Rihanna’s cosmetics brand Fenty Beauty, where she has “Fu$$y” as the name of one of her lip gloss shades.", "annotation_id": "20388518", "url": "https://genius.com/20388518", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Suh-suh-suh (OFB, OFB)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "OFB, or Original Farm Boys, is a gang and UK drill group based in Tottenham, North London. Headie One is a member of OFB, hence the shoutout.\n\nOFB has generally done quite well within the drill scene. In 2019, they released their first group project entitled Frontstreet which was dubbed “one of the best British debuts of the decade” by Dazed Digital. Other individual members of OFB, such as RV, SJ, or Bandokay, are all fairly well known within the drill scene.", "annotation_id": "20388513", "url": "https://genius.com/20388513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "With Pop Skull in Gaza, but not that Gaza, but still it’s a mazza", "vault_lines": ["With Pop Skull in Gaza, but not that Gaza, but still it's a mazza"], "meaning": "Drake is giving a shoutout to Jamaican artist and OVO signee Popcaan, whose nicknames include Pop Skull and Hot Skull.\n\nA ‘mazza’ is (British slang for) something that’s ‘crazy’ or ‘sick’. “Gaza” is Jamaican slang for Waterford, a district located in Portmore, which is a large coastal town located in southern Jamaica. The slang is largely associated with popular Jamaican dancehall artist and former musical mentor of Popcaan, Vybz Kartel, as he grew up in Waterford and is largely responsible for popularizing it. The name, ‘Gaza,’ derives from the Gaza Strip, which is a Palestinian territory located in the Middle East. Drake is clarifying that he is not referring to the Gaza Strip, but Jamaican Gaza.\n\nThis particular song is UK drill, featuring a UK drill artist. This line quite similar to and may be referencing a line from one of Drake’s other UK drill songs, “War”:\n\nLink with Skull and Cuz and Jamie ah yard and we deh pon Gaza", "annotation_id": "20388467", "url": "https://genius.com/20388467", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Shit you man been droppin’ lately, don’t make me have to fly my iTunes So much people buy into my hype, don’t make me have to buy my hype too Dealt with the big homie already, don’t make me have to side-by-side you Nuff times he tried to hide behind you Amnesia but when I remind you I’m touchin’ road and I can’t find you Word to the M-O-B I’m tied to", "vault_lines": ["Don't make me have to rise my rifle", "Shit you man been droppin' lately, don't make me have to fly my iTunes", "So much people buy into my hype, don't make me have to buy my hype too", "Dealt with the big homie already, don't make me have to side-by-side you", "Nuff times he tried to hide behind you", "Amnesia but when I remind you", "I'm touchin' road and I can't find you", "Word to the M-O-B I'm tied to"], "meaning": "These lines are likely aimed at Pusha T and Kanye West. The two had been collaborating more frequently since Drake and Push’s revived beef in 2018, with Kanye producing Push’s May 2018 album DAYTONA and Push being featured on Kanye’s October 2019 track “Use This Gospel,” the tenth track from Ye’s album JESUS IS KING.\n\n“So much people buy into my hype” could mean that the beef Drake’s had with both Ye and Push helped increase their respective album sales. Drake made a similar claim on his May 2018 diss track “Duppy Freestyle” where he said Push’s new album at the time, DAYTONA would sell an additional 20,000 units due to the amount of attention the diss track got:\n\nTell ‘Ye we got a invoice comin’ to you\nConsiderin' that we just sold another 20 for you\n\n“Dealt with the big homie already” is a shot at Kanye as Drake dissed him as well on “Duppy Freestyle.” Kanye never responded to the track—he only posted on social media that he’s “all about love.” Therefore, Drake pokes fun at him, saying he’s “hiding behind” Push, and can’t seem to find him when he goes looking for him.\n\n“Word to the M-O-B I’m tied to” might be a nod to Drake’s June 2018 song “Mob Ties,” where he took shots at Kanye in the chorus.\n\nThese shots notably come in light of Pusha T’s apparent shots at Drake on an unreleased version of Pop Smoke’s “Paranoia,” which leaked a few weeks prior to this song’s release.\n\nA few hours after this song’s release, Kanye acknowledged the shots taken at him here during a Twitter rant, all of which have been since deleted:\n\n\n\nFurthermore, Headie One is a member of OFB, a derivative for TMD (Tottenham Mandem), otherwise known as The Tottenham Mob.", "annotation_id": "20388454", "url": "https://genius.com/20388454", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Chubbs might jam this yute for a chain And give it to a sweet one, called that Maya Jama", "vault_lines": ["Chubbs might jam this yute for a chain", "And give it to a sweet one, called that Maya Jama"], "meaning": "Nessel “Chubbs” Beezer is one of Drake’s closest friends and head of his security.\n\n“Sweet one” is UK slang for an attractive person and Maya Jama is a British television and radio presenter. Drake reportedly reached out to her privately back in 2018 but she ignored him due to her being in a relationship with UK rapper Stormzy at the time. Since they broke up in 2019, after four years of being together, Drake doesn’t mind making his advances towards Maya public.\n\nA “jammer” is a robber. Drake plays on the word to make it sound like Maya’s last name, Jama.\n\n\n\nMaya Jama pictured at the 2020 Brit Awards", "annotation_id": "20388412", "url": "https://genius.com/20388412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 122, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Woi Four in the cliz and one in the headie Hand no shake, man, hold that steady You man love pose with the ting for the picture You man shoulda buss that ting already You man love hezi’, hezi’", "vault_lines": ["Four in the cliz and one in the headie", "Hand no shake, man, hold that steady", "You man love pose with the ting for the picture", "You man shoulda buss that ting already", "You man love hezi', hezi'"], "meaning": "These lines may be directed at dancehall artist Mavado as Drake allegedly takes shots at him in the previous lines.\n\n“Cliz” is slang for “clip” and “one in the headie” is another way of saying “one in the chamber”—the chamber being the part of a firearm where ammunition is fired from. The mention of “one” and “headie” also doubles as a reference to Headie One.\n\nDespite having four bullets in the clip, Drake boasts he only needs one for a headshot. His hand not shaking also shows he isn’t reluctant to shoot his weapon. In the following lines, Drake ridicules someone—possibly Mavado—for taking pictures with a gun but is hesitant to pull the trigger himself.", "annotation_id": "20388399", "url": "https://genius.com/20388399", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "I’m just tryna take this live ting to my juvies, juvies (Suh-suh-suh) So the beef is fryin’ They ain’t tryna stop slidin’, Toosie, Toosie", "vault_lines": ["I'm just tryna take this live ting to my juvies, juvies (Suh-suh-suh)", "So the beef is fryin'", "They ain't tryna stop slidin', Toosie, Toosie"], "meaning": "“Juvies” are the younger members in a gang and “live tings” is slang for an active firearm. Headie plays on the word “frying” as it also means that the beef with their opposition continues to become more intense as his people continue to make moves on them.\n\n“Sliding” is slang for performing a drive-by. Headie plays on “sliding” as he also nods to Drake’s April 2020 hit “Toosie Slide” and its accompanying dance of the same name.", "annotation_id": "20388379", "url": "https://genius.com/20388379", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "I still get one jail call from Boogie Judge gave him twenty years straight, it moved me", "vault_lines": ["I still get one jail call from Boogie", "Judge gave him twenty years straight, it moved me"], "meaning": "Boogie Bando and Headie One are both members of OFB, a gang based in Tottenham, North London, and one of the most popular UK drill groups in that area.\n\nIn February 2019, Boogie was sentenced to life with a minimum term of 20 years for a fatal stabbing during a birthday party. He has been shouted out on many other OFB songs, like on SJ’s June 2019 song “Youngest In Charge” for example:\n\nFree Boogie Bando, he got birded off", "annotation_id": "20388347", "url": "https://genius.com/20388347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "(M1OnTheBeat)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the producer tag for M1OnTheBeat, a prominent producer from London, England. He worked extensively with Headie One on his  December 2017’s mixtape The One.", "annotation_id": "20388328", "url": "https://genius.com/20388328", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "That next one thought he was boss Then he got shot, Nathan Tettey (They told me turn)", "vault_lines": ["That next one thought he was boss", "Then he got shot, Nathan Tettey (They told me turn)"], "meaning": "Headie shouts out Nathan Tettey, the director of the music video for this song. He’s from the UK and has worked with Headie One a few times before, particularly on “18HUNNA”, as well as his chart-topping hit “Both.” Furthermore, Headie also compares the act of filming a video clip, commonly known as ‘shooting’ a video, to setting off a firearm\n\n“That next one” mainly refers to the murder of Kelvin Odunyi, otherwise known as Lampz or DipDat, who was a member of the Woodgreen MOB. Odunyi was shot and killed on March 9, 2018, in retaliation for the murder of Kwabena Nelson on February 3, 2018. Headie references Nelson to mock his opposition as he is also from the Broadwater Farm Estate — as is Headie.", "annotation_id": "20388308", "url": "https://genius.com/20388308", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Then we in France balling out, Trezeguet (One)", "vault_lines": ["Then we in France balling out, Trezeguet (One)"], "meaning": "Headie refers to David Trezeguet, a former French professional football player.", "annotation_id": "20388228", "url": "https://genius.com/20388228", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Arabic ting told me that I look like Youssef, look like Hamza Habibti please, ana akeed, inti wa ana ahla", "vault_lines": ["Habibti please, ana akeed, inti wa ana ahla", "Arabic ting told me that I look like Youssef, look like Hamza"], "meaning": "“Youssef” and “Hamza” are both common male Arab names. Drake’s saying how an Arab girl told him that he looks Arab, due to him having similar Arab features—his full dark beard, tan skin, and dark brown eyes, all being examples. He previously recognized his Arab features in a 2015 Instagram post from his visit to Dubai, where he admitted to bearing a resemblance to the son of the ruler of Dubai, HH Sheikh Mansoor Bin Mohammed Al-Maktoum, and joked that he was his “long lost brother.”\n\nThe mention of Hamza may also be a sly reference to the Moroccan-Belgian recording artist of Arab origin, whom Drake cosigned by bringing him out during a Paris show on his 2019 Drake’s Assassination Tour.\n\n“Habibti” is Arabic for “My love” and أنا أكيد، إنت وأنا أحلى—pronounced “ana akeed, inti wa ana ahla”—is Arabic for “I’m certain you and I look better together.” Drake previously used Arabic terms on both his March 2017 song “Portland” where he says “Habibi”—similarly to how he uses it here—and on his January 2018 song “Diplomatic Immunity” where he says “Insha'Allah,” which is Arabic for “if God wills it.”", "annotation_id": "20388225", "url": "https://genius.com/20388225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 408, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "Closet filled up with designer, comin’ like Panda, Panda", "vault_lines": ["Closet filled up with designer, comin' like Panda, Panda"], "meaning": "This is an allusion to New York rapper Desiigner’s December 2015 song “Panda.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E5ONTXHS2mM\n\nDrake plays on Desiigner’s name by saying that his closet is full of designer clothing, boasting his ability to buy expensive things for himself.", "annotation_id": "20388207", "url": "https://genius.com/20388207", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Only You Freestyle", "line": "One-nine-four-two hits my system, man get way too frosty, buzzy", "vault_lines": ["One-nine-four-two hits my system, man get way too frosty, buzzy"], "meaning": "1942 or Don Julio 1942 is an expensive brand of tequila. It’s known for its refined taste because of its aging process. “Frosty” and “buzzy” are both slang for getting drunk.", "annotation_id": "20388205", "url": "https://genius.com/20388205", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
