{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Ain’t been on Earth (No) ever since I copped that rocket chain and took a trip outer space", "vault_lines": ["Ain't been on Earth (No) ever since I copped that rocket chain and took a trip outer space"], "meaning": "Takeoff is talking about his space shuttle pendant where the NASA logo was replaced with “YRN”😂😂. Piece was designed by Elliot avianne.\n\n\n(Takeoff seen wearing chain)", "annotation_id": "35111441", "url": "https://genius.com/35111441", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Shane", "vault_lines": ["I ride in Mercedes with Shane"], "meaning": "Drake shout outs his bodyguard Shane Knox who is rumoured to be affiliated with the Hells Angels (shown below wearing their clothing). This ties in with the previous line where Drake references 81.", "annotation_id": "27128305", "url": "https://genius.com/27128305", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Load up the Drac’", "vault_lines": ["Load up the Drac'", "Load up the bases, now I feel like Cal Ripken (Ayy)"], "meaning": "“Drac’” is slang for a Draco, a compact AK-47 style firearm. It’s like saying he’s ready and armed. But the word “Drac'” phonetically is similar to “Drake,” creating a double entendre where “load up the Drac'” means “load up Drake” as in, play his music or prepare for his musical output.\n\n\n\nOn top of that, it might even be a nod to his Drake’s custom Boeing 767 branded “Air Drake,” meaning he’s getting ready to take off in style.\n\n\n\n Drake's 185 million dollars private jet", "annotation_id": "23653490", "url": "https://genius.com/23653490", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "And unlike that clock on the wall at your mama house I do not have time to hang", "vault_lines": ["And unlike that clock on the wall at your mama house", "I do not have time to hang"], "meaning": "The word “hang” is a homonym because it has two different meanings in this bar. Drake does not have time to hang around and chill, he is too busy working and making money. He compares this to hanging a clock (time) on the wall, like the ones a lot of mothers have in their house.", "annotation_id": "23653456", "url": "https://genius.com/23653456", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Ain’t gotta run ’cause this not a race", "vault_lines": ["Ain't gotta run 'cause this not a race"], "meaning": "It has been 3 and a half years since Migos released their previous album Culture II, but Takeoff rather takes his time with making new music instead of rushing it. He prefers quality over quantity.", "annotation_id": "23653416", "url": "https://genius.com/23653416", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Big ’81, but I don’t own a Harley", "vault_lines": ["Big '81, but I don't own a Harley"], "meaning": "The number 81 is a common metonym linked to the Hell’s Angels biker gang, representing the 8th and 1st letters of the alphabet—H and A—which stand for Hell’s Angels. The gang’s presence is quite noticeable in Drake’s home country, Canada.\n\nWhile Drake doesn’t own a Harley Davidson motorcycle (the brand exclusively ridden by the gang), he has shown support for them. He previously referenced “81” in his song “God’s Plan” and faced criticism in 2018 for wearing Hell’s Angels merchandise.\n\n\n\nDrake pictured wearing a Hell’s Angel shirt featuring the “81” metonym", "annotation_id": "23468623", "url": "https://genius.com/23468623", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "We done did so many pull-ups on niggas, no wonder we gettin’ in shape", "vault_lines": ["We done did so many pull-ups on niggas, no wonder we gettin' in shape"], "meaning": "A “pull-up” is a bodyweight exercise where you suspend your body by a bar and pull yourself upward, but to “pull up” on someone also means to confront them about an issue. It’s all comes together with the “No wonder we getting in shape”, which connects it to the exercising part of the “pull up” bar.", "annotation_id": "23288914", "url": "https://genius.com/23288914", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "I was seventeen on a song with Drake", "vault_lines": ["I was seventeen on a song with Drake"], "meaning": "When Drake remixed the Migos viral 2013 song “Versace”, Takeoff, the youngest member of the group, was just 17 years old. At that time, Drake had already established himself as one of the biggest names in the rap game, so of course it was a big moment for Takeoff, as it would have been for any teenager.", "annotation_id": "23272790", "url": "https://genius.com/23272790", "state": "pending", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Told myself that I would get through this verse, and I’m not gonna mention the plane But look at the plane", "vault_lines": ["Told myself that I would get through this verse, and I'm not gonna mention the plane", "But look at the plane"], "meaning": "Drake boasts about his personal private jet, which is appropriately named Air Drake. He first unveiled the plane in May of 2019.\n\nOn March 28, 2020, Drake and Virgil Abloh revealed the plane’s redesign, courtesy of the famous artist.\n\n\n\nThe plane notably appears at the beginning of the music video for “What’s Next,” amongst other instances where Drake has made its existence known.", "annotation_id": "23269978", "url": "https://genius.com/23269978", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Go put all my cleats on, walkin’, ain’t slippin’ (Walk)", "vault_lines": ["Go put all my cleats on, walkin', ain't slippin' (Walk)"], "meaning": "Quavo makes reference to his days on the football field in which players sport cleats. Quavo had a rather successful tenure in high-school serving as the Quarterback for Berkmar High School in Lilburn, Georgia. More on Quavo’s high-school football career can be seen here. Quavo then subtly name drops a former Migos and Drake track, “Walk It, Talk It'', which appeared on Culture II along with name dropping their track “Slippery'' ft. Gucci Mane off of Culture. Quavo isn’t slipping, but is rather headed in an upward trajectory and is able to “talk the talk and walk the walk”.", "annotation_id": "23258526", "url": "https://genius.com/23258526", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "She had it her way, now she out of a Bentley (Skrrt) (Skrrt-skrrt, get out, ooh)", "vault_lines": ["She had it her way, now she out of a Bentley (Skrrt)"], "meaning": "Quavo alludes to his high-profile breakup with  Saweetie in March 2021. The couple had a viral back-and-forth on Twitter where Saweetie confirmed the end of the relationship and claimed that Quavo had been unfaithful.\n\nAs a Christmas gift in 2020, Quavo gave Saweetie an “icy” light-blue Bentley Continental GT worth $350,000. He also followed up the gift with endearing social media posts about his love for her.\n\n\n\nPictured: Saweetie and her Bentley Continental GT\n\n\nOn March 22, 2021, reports came out that Quavo had repossessed the Bentley following their breakup as the contract was under his name. News site TMZ later reported that Quavo did not repo the Bentley and that it was a rumor. In April 2021, Quavo shared a snippet on Instagram, confirming that he did repossess:\n\nShe slimy, she sneaky (Yeah, sneaky)\nSkrr, skrr, takin' back the Bentley", "annotation_id": "23253092", "url": "https://genius.com/23253092", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "I’ve been too solid to ever have stripes on my sneak’s, you get what I mean?", "vault_lines": ["I've been too solid to ever have stripes on my sneak's, you get what I mean?"], "meaning": "Adidas apparel is branded with three parallel stripes. Drake is signed to Nike. In 2018, he was allegedly in negotiations with Adidas before they fell through: a timeline can be seen here.\n\nDrake took shots at Adidas in August 2018 when he featured on Travis Scott’s “SICKO MODE”:\n\nJesus Christ (Yeah), checks over stripes (Yeah)\nThat’s what I like (Yeah), that’s what we like (Yeah)\nLost my respect, you not a threat\n\nAlso, in September 2018, Drake featured on French Montana’s “No Stylist,” on which he rapped:\n\nYeah, keepin' it G, I told her, ‘Don’t wear no 350s 'round me’\n\nBeyond sneakers, this line contributes to Drake’s eight-ball pool reference from the surrounding bars, since the game features solid and striped balls.", "annotation_id": "23250893", "url": "https://genius.com/23250893", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "I’m playin’ pool and the pool just connects to the beach", "vault_lines": ["I'm playin' pool and the pool just connects to the beach"], "meaning": "Drake references eight-ball pool, a game where the final objective is to sink the eight-ball into a pocket of your choice.\n\nDrake employs wordplay related to the popular table game: an eight-ball is slang for 1/8th of an ounce of cocaine, which his friends dealt straight into coke addicts' pant pockets.\nhttps://thumbor.forbes.com/thumbor/960x0/https%3A%2F%2Fspecials-images.forbesimg.com%2Fdam%2Fimageserve%2F1138911929%2F960x0.jpg%3Ffit%3Dscale", "annotation_id": "23250662", "url": "https://genius.com/23250662", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Please don’t reach out to me Think watchin’ too much of Stephen done got to me Verses too eas’, I’m servin’ them up by the threes", "vault_lines": ["Please don't reach out to me", "Think watchin' too much of Stephen done got to me", "Verses too eas', I'm servin' them up by the threes"], "meaning": "Drake has a strong relationship with Golden State Warriors guard Stephen Curry. Despite being a Toronto native and Raptors fan, Drizzy has never been shy of his respect towards Curry, going as far as getting a tattoo of his jersey number 30 with the word “Gifted” underneath on his left arm.\n\nAs of this track’s release, Curry sits at No. 7 in NBA history with a 43.3% clip from three-point range, but with a volume of 8.6 attempts and 3.7 makes per game, Steph has become one of the best distance shooters in history.\n\nDrake even referenced his bond with the Warriors on 2017’s “Free Smoke”:\n\nI know the question rhetorical\nI took the team plane from Oracle", "annotation_id": "23248418", "url": "https://genius.com/23248418", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Blue or the red Bugatti, this the Matrix", "vault_lines": ["Blue or the red Bugatti, this the Matrix"], "meaning": "Offset compares picking which color Bugatti he wants to drive to the popular 1999 science fiction film, The Matrix.\n\nIn the film, the main character, Neo, is given the option of taking a red pill or a blue pill — a choice that would alter his future:\n\nYou take the blue pill—the story ends, you wake up in your bed and believe whatever you want to believe. You take the red pill—you stay in Wonderland, and I show you how deep the rabbit hole goes. Remember: all I’m offering is the truth. Nothing more.", "annotation_id": "23248385", "url": "https://genius.com/23248385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Shit done changed, billionaires talk to me different when they see my paystub from Lucian Grainge", "vault_lines": ["Shit done changed, billionaires talk to me different when they see my paystub from Lucian Grainge"], "meaning": "Lucian Grainge is the Chairman and CEO of Universal Music Group. Drake name-dropped him on Rick Ross‘ April 2012 song, “Stay Schemin’”:\n\nTell Lucian I said ‘fuck it,’ I’m tearin' holes in my budget\n\nWhen asked about the line at the Universal Grammy afterparty in January 2020, Lucian said:\n\nIf ever Drake or whenever Drake calls up and says he needs more money for his project, I give it to him […] because he’s the greatest", "annotation_id": "23248329", "url": "https://genius.com/23248329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Load up the bases, now I feel like Cal Ripken (Ayy)", "vault_lines": ["Load up the Drac'", "Load up the bases, now I feel like Cal Ripken (Ayy)"], "meaning": "Quavo name drops Baltimore Oriole legend Cal Ripken, Jr., who is known as “The Iron Man” of professional baseball.\n\nIn his 21 seasons with the Orioles, Ripken had 3,184 hits, 431 HR and 1,695 RBI which led to 19 All-Star appearances, two league MVP awards and an induction into the Hall of Fame. He also set the Major League record for consecutive games played at 2,632, surpassing Lou Gehrig’s seemingly unbreakable streak of 2,130 games that stood for 56 years.\n\nIn the context of loading the bases that Quavo poses, hitting a grand slam would result in scoring four runs and clearing the bases, something Cal Ripken, Jr. did eight times in his professional career.", "annotation_id": "23248275", "url": "https://genius.com/23248275", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Having Our Way", "line": "Okay, great, I’m in the backroom of Wally’s, I spent thirty thousand on somebody’s grapes", "vault_lines": ["Okay, great, I'm in the backroom of Wally's, I spent thirty thousand on somebody's grapes"], "meaning": "Wally’s is an upscale wine shop and restaurant in Beverly Hills, California that allows for private gatherings by request.\n\n\n\nDrake notes that he dropped $30,000 on wine, which is an alcohol derived from grapes. This isn’t the first time Drizzy’s mentioned Wally’s; he also shouted out the establishment on 2018’s “Diplomatic Immunity”:\n\nBooked a private room at Wally’s, waiter twistin' the cork", "annotation_id": "23248266", "url": "https://genius.com/23248266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "status": "done", "targets": 28, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-baby-gunna-and-drake-never-recover-lyrics", "genius_title": "Never Recover", "genius_id": 3995556, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Ring, ring, ring, we drip too hard, ayy", "vault_lines": ["Ring, ring, ring, we drip too hard, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to Baby and Gunna’s September 2018 single, “Drip Too Hard”, the lead single from Drip Harder, in which Baby raps:\n\nDrip too hard, don’t stand too close", "annotation_id": "19483765", "url": "https://genius.com/19483765", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Don’t gotta drip, I can leave it to Gunna", "vault_lines": ["Don't gotta drip, I can leave it to Gunna"], "meaning": "By this line, Baby is saying that Gunna is the influencer of drip, which means wearing expensive clothing or jewelry. Gunna even has a mixtape series named Drip Season, in which he released three sets.\n\nEssentially, Baby is saying he can leave the decisions on “drip” up to Gunna to decide.", "annotation_id": "19483643", "url": "https://genius.com/19483643", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "I told the judge, ”Fuck it”, I’m goin’ on the run You one of my dawgs, I’ll look out for the bond", "vault_lines": ["I told the judge, \"Fuck it\", I'm goin' on the run", "You one of my dawgs, I'll look out for the bond"], "meaning": "In June 2018, Gunna and his DJ were arrested for possession of a controlled substance. The next day, the two were released on bond. It is still unclear who the bond was paid by, but according to this song, it could most likely be Gunna’s friends. Going “on the run” could mean being free or avoiding the police.", "annotation_id": "19482051", "url": "https://genius.com/19482051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "The kid was surprised that my trunk in the front", "vault_lines": ["The kid was surprised that my trunk in the front"], "meaning": "Many cars that are either electric, mid-engined or rear-engined have a trunk in the front.\n\nExamples of these types of cars are the mid-engined Porsche Boxster, the electric Tesla Model X and the rear-engined Porsche 911.\n\nThe Porsche 911 is one of the most expensive of the front-trunked cars starting at $113,300.\n\n\nThe Porsche 911 with it's front trunk opened\n\n\nGunna could be referring to the Porsche 911 as it’s an expensive luxury car fitting the description mentioned.", "annotation_id": "16789286", "url": "https://genius.com/16789286", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "You told a story like shorty was feelin’ you She told a story like she split the bill with you", "vault_lines": ["You told a story like shorty was feelin' you", "She told a story like she split the bill with you"], "meaning": "The guy Drake describes in this bar brags about a woman being interested in him when he made her pay half the bill(presumably dinner) & that’s not what bosses do.", "annotation_id": "15861899", "url": "https://genius.com/15861899", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "I bring up money, they change up the topic", "vault_lines": ["I bring up hits and they change up the topic", "I bring up money, they change up the topic"], "meaning": "This can be taken as a jab towards all of Drake’s haters and competition. This could also be taken as a direct line towards Pusha T and the enduring and high-profile beef these two have had for a long time.\n\nDrake released Duppy Freestyle in response to Pusha T’s Infrared in which he replies to Pusha T’s criticisms by comparing their commercial and financial successes. In Pusha T’s infamous response track, The Story of Adidon, Pusha never counters the comments Drake made about the comparison of their success financially and or commercially. He’s implying that since Pusha T has nothing to say against Drake’s claim of more success, he has to change the topic altogether and resort to low-blows against him as a person.", "annotation_id": "15538667", "url": "https://genius.com/15538667", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "One hell of a year and a nigga still droppin’ They wanted to stop it, but they couldn’t stop it", "vault_lines": ["One hell of a year and a nigga still droppin'", "They wanted to stop it, but they couldn't stop it"], "meaning": "While Drake had a turbulent 2018, he’s still putting out material.\n\nIn January he dropped “God’s Plan” which went Diamond. He followed this up with his 5th studio album Scorpion, which spawned two number 1 singles with “Nice for What” and “In My Feelings”.\n\nHowever, Drakes album roll-out was interrupted by an escalation of the long simmering beef with Pusha T, which also roped in Kanye West – who Drake appears to also mention in this verse.\n\nWhile Pusha undoubtedly pressured Drake, Scorpion was still a commercial success, hitting number 1 and being certified platinum as of late 2018.\n\nThe reference to “writing” might also be a nod to the well documented ghost-writing accusations that once again came up in the beef with Pusha. Drake is likely suggesting that while his success story is unbelievable (“no one could write it”), it’s unlikely that he could’ve gotten as far as he did relying only on writers.", "annotation_id": "15523830", "url": "https://genius.com/15523830", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Tay Keith, this too hard", "vault_lines": ["Tay Keith, this too hard"], "meaning": "This could be Tay Keith’s first use of Drake as his producer tag following up his popular tag said by Memphis rapper, Lil Juice:\n\nTay Keith, fuck these niggas up\n\nThe tag is heard on all of Tay Keith’s most popular songs Drake’s “Nonstop”, Travis Scott’s “SICKO MODE”, BlocBoy JB’s “Look Alive”, and also this one.\n\nOn October 5, 2018, Tay Keith took to Twitter talking about using Drake’s lines here as a new producer tag:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/1taykeith/status/1048342933659955200?s=21\n\nhttps://twitter.com/1taykeith/status/1048343009002246145?s=21", "annotation_id": "15522611", "url": "https://genius.com/15522611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "If she tell me “No” once, I won’t even touch it", "vault_lines": ["If she tell me \"No\" once, I won't even touch it"], "meaning": "Lil Baby is no stranger to respecting women in his music. In his previous hit track, “Freestyle”, from his 2017 mixtape, Too Hard, Lil Baby states:\n\nI been gettin' faded I’m sippin' on maple\nIf she won’t fuck I won’t make her\n\nUnfortunately, Drip Harder is not free from anti-consent sentiments. On the intro track, “Off White VLONE”, featured rapper, Lil Durk, persists:\n\nGotta suck dick on your period\nYou can’t say, “No,” I ain’t hearin' it", "annotation_id": "15522387", "url": "https://genius.com/15522387", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Don’t got what I got ’cause I shop in Milan", "vault_lines": ["Don't got what I got 'cause I shop in Milan"], "meaning": "Gunna has expressed his love for European countries and cities on multiple songs in the past as well as on other songs from the Drip Harder album. He primarily refers to Europe as the place where he purchases his clothing, emphasizing his extravagant lifestyle.\n\nGunna also refers to Milan on the chorus of Off White VLONE by him and Lil Baby feat. Nav and Lil Durk, and says,\n\nAlexander, Laurent (yah), private flight to Milan (‘Lan)", "annotation_id": "15518562", "url": "https://genius.com/15518562", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "In black and white Chanel, I look like a nun", "vault_lines": ["In black and white Chanel, I look like a nun"], "meaning": "Gunna is a fan of the luxury fashion house Chanel, which has some designs that resemble the black and white veils that nuns wear.\n\n\n\nHe previously had a track with Young Thug named Chanel (Go Get It), praising his love for the brand.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BnWiDN7BIlb/?utm_source=ig_web_button_share_sheet\nGunna in Chanel’s sneaker", "annotation_id": "15516879", "url": "https://genius.com/15516879", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "I bring up hits and they change up the topic", "vault_lines": ["I bring up hits and they change up the topic", "I bring up money, they change up the topic"], "meaning": "Drake is the king of hits on the Billboard charts, as his most recent album at the time of Drip Harder’s release, Scorpion which became the first set to have its' singles spend a new-record of 29 cumulative weeks atop the chart, with songs like “God’s Plan”, “Nice For What”, and “In My Feelings” all spending over 8 weeks as the top song on the Billboard charts. He’s also the only male rapper to have had over 30 top 10 singles.", "annotation_id": "15516843", "url": "https://genius.com/15516843", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Treat every club like a Magic on Monday", "vault_lines": ["Treat every club like a Magic on Monday"], "meaning": "This is referencing Magic City, a notorious strip club in Atlanta, whose Mondays are its most exciting days due to special events where celebrities and influencers tend to appear. GQ has an article that considers Magic City a cultural phenomenon.\n\nBaby has used similar bars in the past. On “Life,” he rapped:\n\nPaying tithes Sunday, we gon' blow up one day\nMagic on a Monday, shout out to the strippers\n\nDrake, who is featured on this song, also referenced Magic City on “Plastic Bag,” a 2015 collaboration with Future where he rapped:\n\nOh yeah, Magic city on a Monday\nEsco treat that shit like church on Sunday", "annotation_id": "15516760", "url": "https://genius.com/15516760", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "(Run that back, Turbo)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the producer tag of Atlanta producer and mixing engineer, Turbo. He is known best for his work with Gunna and Lil Baby and produced 9 out of the 13 tracks on Drip Harder.\n\nHe’s also worked with Travis Scott on his  track, “YOSEMITE”, for ASTROWORLD. The track features Gunna and NAV who is also featured on Drip Harder. He posted the RIAA Gold certification for ASTROWORLD on his Instagram with the caption:\n\nTHANK GOD THANK ASTRO #YOSEMITE", "annotation_id": "15516585", "url": "https://genius.com/15516585", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Hit Eliantte and left with a puddle", "vault_lines": ["Hit Eliantte and left with a puddle"], "meaning": "Elliante is a jeweler known for his prowess in creating diamond pieces for rappers including Lil Pump, Rich the Kid, Offset and Young Scooter.\n\n\n\nGunna frequently refers to his drip, and this line is an extension of that metaphor. His three main projects (Drip Season 1-3) illustrate his affinity with the term drip.\n\nDripping is being dressed down in designer and looking good which usually includes jewelry as it is sometimes referred to as “water” or “ice” which also drips. Gunna’s line, in this case, is stating that he will have so much jewelry that his drip will make a puddle. He is also known for using the phrase “Drip or Drown”, even going as far to name his song the same name, “Drip or Drown”.", "annotation_id": "15515947", "url": "https://genius.com/15515947", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "I got a 19 and it fold in my pocket", "vault_lines": ["I got a 19 and it fold in my pocket"], "meaning": "The Glock 19 is a lightweight pistol, which can be adapted to fold. Once folded, the weapon is pocket-sized and can be concealed much easier than keeping it in a holster that can bulge on your side or in your waistband.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r6bA5uYkNA0\n\nDrake is bragging about keeping his gun clutched close to him, for protection.", "annotation_id": "15515913", "url": "https://genius.com/15515913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Clean that shit up and I give it right back to him If I don’t fuck with him, then I can’t rap with him I wanna be there when somebody catches him I want a witness to see that shit", "vault_lines": ["I wanna be there when somebody catches him", "I want a witness to see that shit", "Clean that shit up and I give it right back to him", "If I don't fuck with him, then I can't rap with him"], "meaning": "If this is the same person that Drake is hunting for on Travis Scott’s song, “SICKO MODE”, and the subject of Drake’s other subliminal shots around the time of this release, then this could be referring to Kanye West. On his verse in the final part of “SICKO MODE”, also produced by Tay Keith, Drake raps:\n\nI crept down the block (down the block)\nMade a right (yeah, right)\nCut the lights, (yeah, what) paid the price (yeah)\n\nDrake previously subbed Ye on his collaborative track with French Montana, “No Stylist”:\n\nYeah, keepin' it G, I told her “don’t wear no 350s ‘round me”\n\nKanye made several tweets trying to make peace with Drake by sending out messages to him and apologizing:\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1037248508116398080?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E1037248508116398080&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.highsnobiety.com%2Fp%2Fkanye-west-drake-apology-twitter%2F\n\nThese lines seem to be Drake’s response to Kanye’s attempts of rebuilding burnt bridges between them.", "annotation_id": "15514709", "url": "https://genius.com/15514709", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Spend a whole hundred on a new wristwatch", "vault_lines": ["Spend a whole hundred on a new wristwatch"], "meaning": "In an interview with GQ for their series, On the Rocks, Lil Baby showed off his entire jewelry collection. Although he never mentioned spending anywhere near $100,000 on a watch, he did claim that the jewelry he had on him was around half of a million dollars.", "annotation_id": "15512130", "url": "https://genius.com/15512130", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Drizzy hit me up like he got another one", "vault_lines": ["Drizzy hit me up like he got another one"], "meaning": "Lil Baby claims Drake hit him up with another track (this track, “Never Recover”) following the release of their collaborative track, “Yes Indeed”, which went certified RIAA platinum in late June of 2018.", "annotation_id": "15512108", "url": "https://genius.com/15512108", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Cuban link look like a box of crayons", "vault_lines": ["Cuban link look like a box of crayons"], "meaning": "Gunna loves his Cuban links. He raps about them extremely often, most notably, on his track, “Oh Okay”, featuring Lil Baby and Young Thug, where he raps:\n\nSimon says do what I said, Cuban link chokin' my neck\n\nCuban’s looking like a box of crayons is referring to the multiple colors his diamonds produce when the light hits them.\n\nGunna can be seen wearing two Cuban links on the cover of his project, Drip Season 3.", "annotation_id": "15512076", "url": "https://genius.com/15512076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Virgil just chef’d me a whole different colorway", "vault_lines": ["Virgil just chef'd me a whole different colorway"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out fashion designer, DJ, and good friend, Virgil Abloh. He became well-known for founding the Italian, luxury streetwear brand, Off-White, which is also mentioned in the title of the album’s first track, “Off White VLONE.” He is currently working as artistic director for menswear at Louis Vuitton.\n\n\n\nDrake is almost guaranteed to regularly receive unreleased and new collections from Abloh. The Off-White x Nike sneaker is one example of an item that he has received.\n\n“Off-white” is also a yellowish white color. Drake plays on Virgil’s change of career from streetwear to more formal clothing by saying that he now operates with a “different colorway.”\n\nDrake has previously mentioned Virgil Abloh on his track, “Duppy’s Freestyle,” criticizing Kanye West.", "annotation_id": "15511829", "url": "https://genius.com/15511829", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Sound like it could be Drip Season or somethin’", "vault_lines": ["Sound like it could be Drip Season or somethin'"], "meaning": "Gunna refers to his mixtape series, Drip Season, which he first released in 2016.\n\nSince his first installment, he’s released two others; his most recent project at the time of this release, Drip Season 3, landed on the Billboard 200 with features from Baby, Young Thug, NAV, and many more.", "annotation_id": "15511229", "url": "https://genius.com/15511229", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "I might just YSL my shit, the Thugger way", "vault_lines": ["I might just YSL my shit, the Thugger way"], "meaning": "YSL (Young Stoner Life) is the record label Gunna is signed to under created by label owner and mentor, Young Thug, also known as Thugger.\n\nDrake and Thug have worked together before on “Ice Melts” and “Sacrifices”, both from Drake’s 2017 playlist, More Life.\n\nYSL is also short for Yves Saint Laurent, a high-end designer clothing company where Drake is saying he will purchase his outfit from.", "annotation_id": "15511117", "url": "https://genius.com/15511117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Runnin’ this shit, only been out two summers", "vault_lines": ["Runnin' this shit, only been out two summers"], "meaning": "Lil Baby was released from prison in 2017 after doing 2 years for a probation violation. He talks more about this on an interview with VladTV.\n\nSince his release from prison, he released his debut mixtape, Perfect Timing, Harder Than Hard, Too Hard, Harder Than Ever, and now, Drip Harder.", "annotation_id": "15511060", "url": "https://genius.com/15511060", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Breadwinner like I’m Kevin Gates And I swear to God, I don’t get tired", "vault_lines": ["Breadwinner like I'm Kevin Gates", "And I swear to God, I don't get tired"], "meaning": "Kevin Gates' record label and brand is Bread Winners' Association.\n\nThis line also references his hit song “I Don’t Get Tired” from Luca Brasi 2. “I Don’t Get Tired” and its corresponding acronym IDGT became Kevin Gates' catchphrase after the song. He even has it tattooed on his hands.", "annotation_id": "15510994", "url": "https://genius.com/15510994", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Tay Keith, fuck these niggas up", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Tay Keith is a producer from Memphis, Tennessee who’s mostly known for his work with BlocBoy JB. His tag is voiced by Lil Juice who is also from Tennessee. Keith has worked with rappers like  Travis Scott, Eminem, Moneybagg Yo, and many more.\n\nHe’s worked with Lil Baby in the past on “Exotic”, featuring Starlito, but hasn’t worked on any song of Gunna’s before. This track also marks his fourth collaboration with Drake which comes right after the release of Travis Scott’s, “SICKO MODE”.", "annotation_id": "15510952", "url": "https://genius.com/15510952", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Young Gunna Gunna, I make this shit double", "vault_lines": ["Young Gunna Gunna, I make this shit double"], "meaning": "Gunna likes to call himself “Gunna Gunna” on songs all the time.\n\nHe’s done this on too many songs to list them off, but most notably, on his track, “Oh Okay”, featuring Young Thug and Lil Baby where he spits:\n\nYeen my slime, yeen my slime\nYoung Gunna Gunna, I’m in my prime", "annotation_id": "15510448", "url": "https://genius.com/15510448", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Never Recover", "line": "Next time I’m in Dallas, I’ll look for another", "vault_lines": ["Next time I'm in Dallas, I'll look for another"], "meaning": "Drake is no stranger to Dallas. On his track, “9AM in Dallas”, he speaks on various topics, but none referencing anything in Dallas itself. It’s titled after where (and when) Drake wrote that track.\n\nDrake’s titled multiple songs after where he wrote them, for example, “6PM in New York”, “4PM in Calabasas”.", "annotation_id": "15510414", "url": "https://genius.com/15510414", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-aston-martin-music-lyrics", "genius_title": "Aston Martin Music", "genius_id": 765, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "Fuck maintain, boy: I gotta keep ballin", "vault_lines": ["Fuck maintain, boy: I gotta keep ballin"], "meaning": "Instead of keeping a consistent balance of money Rick Ross decided to keep increasing his money.", "annotation_id": "21236602", "url": "https://genius.com/21236602", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "Yeah, you gotta own it if you want it Kisses all on her body, she tells me live in the moment", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, you gotta own it if you want it", "Kisses all on her body, she tells me live in the moment"], "meaning": "He’s reflecting on the times he had with this girl (specifically Paris) when they were together getting intimate.\n\n\n\nAs he serenaded her body in kisses: Drake questioned whether or not he should go as far as sexual intercourse, and she told him to forget everything else, and to just live in the moment.\n\nParis Morton, who Drake dedicated the title of a song to twice; Part 2 being on Nothing Was The Same in the form of the second half of “Pound Cake/ Paris Morton Music”, actually has a “Live In The Moment” tattoo.", "annotation_id": "13443801", "url": "https://genius.com/13443801", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "Listening to the Aston Martin Music, Music", "vault_lines": ["Listening to the Aston Martin Music, Music", "Aston Martin Music, Music"], "meaning": "Aston Martin is a British manufacturer of luxury cars, perhaps most famously the DBS that appeared in Casino Royale.\n\nThe idea is that you would listen to this song in your Aston Martin two seater with your girl and just ride out. Similarly to how Maybach Music is about stunting hard with your crew, this one is about that Bonnie and Clyde feel.\n\nIt could also be referring to the beautiful ‘music’ the Aston Martin’s engine produces.", "annotation_id": "264404", "url": "https://genius.com/264404", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "I don’t drink every bottle I own, I be agin’ shit", "vault_lines": ["I don't drink every bottle I own, I be agin' shit"], "meaning": "On 2016’s “You Know You Know,” Drake described his extensive wine collection:\n\nWhy has every woman never dined here before?\nAm I the only 23 year old wine connoisseur?\nThe Rothschild’s, the Krug’s, the Goya’s and the Opus’s\nIt’s always nice when you’re out with someone that notices\n\n\n\nThis also implicates a double meaning; Drake’s saving bottles of alcohol but is also saving songs or verses up to hit you all at once with a great combination.", "annotation_id": "73451", "url": "https://genius.com/73451", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "I’m on some Marvin Gaye shit, a bunch of distant lovers", "vault_lines": ["I'm on some Marvin Gaye shit, a bunch of distant lovers"], "meaning": "“Distant Lover” is the 1973 song by R&B legend Marvin Gaye. The song desrcibes a rocky relationship with Marvin and his woman.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U9BSjRCN0cQ\n\nThis may also be a foreshadowing of Drake’s 2011 hit single “Marvin’s Room”, which was named after Marvin Gaye’s Studio.", "annotation_id": "73440", "url": "https://genius.com/73440", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "I’m urgin’ all daughters to kiss they mothers With those lips that all that lipstick covers You’re never too grown up to miss and hug her", "vault_lines": ["I'm urgin' all daughters to kiss they mothers", "With those lips that all that lipstick covers", "You're never too grown up to miss and hug her"], "meaning": "Many mothers don’t know how to teach their daughters how to be a lady – but Drake wants them to be gal pals anyways\n\nDrake probably got the idea to include a parenting-related Public Service Announcement from RZA\n\nAfter all, showing respect to mothers is a recurring theme in hip hop, ranging from Rozay’s “Smile, Mama, Smile” and Ye’s “Hey Mama”, to Pac’s iconic “Dear Mama”. With all these figures and role models paying tribute to those that birthed them, Drake believes all women should take part.", "annotation_id": "73439", "url": "https://genius.com/73439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "I never threw away that paper with my Grammy speech Because I haven’t hit the pinnacles I plan to reach", "vault_lines": ["I never threw away that paper with my Grammy speech", "Because I haven't hit the pinnacles I plan to reach"], "meaning": "Drake was nominated for 2 Grammys in 2010 for “Best I Ever Had” – but lost to Jay-Z’s “D.O.A.” in both categories\n\nBut he kept his victory speech saved! (On Google Docs) – because surely he’ll win some award sooner or later", "annotation_id": "69305", "url": "https://genius.com/69305", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "A lot of quiet time, pink bottles of rose", "vault_lines": ["A lot of quiet time, pink bottles of rose"], "meaning": "Rosé wine, pronounced “rozay” not “rose” as Ross does here, is “a type of wine that has some of the color typical of a red wine, but only enough to turn it pink”\n\n\n\nRoss has fancied calling himself “Rozay” lately after his penchant for the variety", "annotation_id": "45873", "url": "https://genius.com/45873", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "VVS’s in the watch", "vault_lines": ["Mr. KFC, VVS's in the watch"], "meaning": "VVS diamonds are “very very slightly included” and have minute inclusions that only a jeweler can see with a microscope at 10x; the highest quality diamonds\n\n\n\nAlternately, Ross could’ve said “DBS is in the watch”, a reference to the exclusive Aston Martin watch that you can use as a remote key to lock / unlock.", "annotation_id": "28377", "url": "https://genius.com/28377", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "Pull up on the block in a drop-top chicken box", "vault_lines": ["Pull up on the block in a drop-top chicken box"], "meaning": "A coop is an enclosure or ‘box’ used to contain poultry\n\n“Chickenheads” are girls who like to give head because their heads make an up-and-down motion during the act (like a chicken). See definition #2 here.\n\nSo “chicken box” means his drop-top is used to transport chickenheads, not literal chickens or poultry.", "annotation_id": "28371", "url": "https://genius.com/28371", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Aston Martin Music", "line": "When I’m alone in my room, sometime I stare at the wall", "vault_lines": ["When I'm alone in my room, sometime I stare at the wall"], "meaning": "Ricky recycled LL Cool J’s line from his 1987 hit “I Need Love”, which was also recycled by Common in “Sum Shit I Wrote”", "annotation_id": "24376", "url": "https://genius.com/24376", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "status": "done", "targets": 27, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Ob-obrien-schemin-up-lyrics", "genius_title": "Schemin’ Up", "genius_id": 633642, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "And like you just got traded, girl, you don’t know who you’re playin’ with", "vault_lines": ["And like you just got traded, girl, you don't know who you're playin' with", "You know, you know, you know"], "meaning": "Getting traded in sports means joining a new team and not yet knowing your teammates. Here, it’s a metaphor for a girl not realizing the status or power of the person she’s now with.", "annotation_id": "36566855", "url": "https://genius.com/36566855", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Bitches gettin’ dugout like where all the fuckin’ players sit", "vault_lines": ["Bitches gettin' dugout like where all the fuckin' players sit"], "meaning": "In baseball, the dugout is where players sit during the game. In slang, “getting dug out” can mean having sex, so the line flips between a sports reference and a sexual one.", "annotation_id": "36566826", "url": "https://genius.com/36566826", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "All my tings are mixed (Mix!) and I make ’em mix (Mix!) That means I make ’em kiss, I make ’em pop, I make ’em strip", "vault_lines": ["Mix, all I do is mix (Mix!)", "All my tings are mixed (Mix!) and I make 'em mix (Mix!)", "That means I make 'em kiss, I make 'em pop, I make 'em strip"], "meaning": "All his girls are mixed race. Presumably mixed with white and black features. Which is the most common mixed race. And also constantly seen as very attractive.\nHe makes his mixed race women entertain him by making out with each other and performing other sexual acts", "annotation_id": "21838926", "url": "https://genius.com/21838926", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "I’ve been down, but I’m up right now", "vault_lines": ["I've been down, but I'm up right now"], "meaning": "Drake’s had moments away from the spotlight, but he’s firmly entrenched in it now. He recycles this line in the hook of “How About Now”:\n\nBut how ‘bout now?\nCause I’m up right now, and you stuck right now", "annotation_id": "7665560", "url": "https://genius.com/7665560", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "’Bout to go drown in the cup right now, holla at me", "vault_lines": ["'Bout to go drown in the cup right now, holla at me"], "meaning": "Since he reps Houston frequently, it’s reasonable to assume Drake’s drinking lean, the city’s famous beverage, which is typically consumed in a styrofoam cup.", "annotation_id": "7665555", "url": "https://genius.com/7665555", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Nigga been backstrokin’ and breaststrokin’ Through that lane that you left open, man, I don’t give a fuck right now Let a nigga swim in the pussy on the low", "vault_lines": ["Nigga been backstrokin' and breaststrokin'", "Through that lane that you left open, man, I don't give a fuck right now", "Let a nigga swim in the pussy on the low"], "meaning": "Backstroke and breaststroke are two of the four swimming strokes, and swimmers stay in separate lanes during competition.\n\n\n\nThis girl has allowed many men access between her legs, but Drake ultimately doesn’t mind – sex is sex.", "annotation_id": "7665553", "url": "https://genius.com/7665553", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Aw yeah, aw yeah, and my sound is what up right now", "vault_lines": ["Aw yeah, aw yeah, and my sound is what up right now"], "meaning": "As Drake will constantly remind us, he’s the leader of the new school and the hit-maker of this rap generation. He stays killin' it on Billboard and has a smash mixtape to go along with three successful mainstream albums.", "annotation_id": "7665552", "url": "https://genius.com/7665552", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Then I say: this that fuckin’ mob shit, this that fuckin’ player shit This that shake a bottle up and bust the top and spray the shit", "vault_lines": ["Then I say: this that fuckin' mob shit, this that fuckin' player shit", "This that shake a bottle up and bust the top and spray the shit"], "meaning": "These lines are reminiscent of “Trophies”:\n\nThis a “fuck-a-stripper-on-the-mink-rug” song\nThis a “fuck-them-boys-forever, hold-a-grudge” song\n“Pop-some-fucking-champagne-in-the-tub” song\n\nDrake can party whenever and however he wants because he’s loaded after dropping banger after banger for the fans. That’s definitely a cause for celebration.", "annotation_id": "7665541", "url": "https://genius.com/7665541", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "And free Greezy one time", "vault_lines": ["and free Greezy one time", "Shout out Sammy and Peezy one time, you know what it is"], "meaning": "Free Greezy though, let all of my dogs out\n\nGreezy is a currently jailed GS9 affiliate, first referenced in the 2014 viral song “Hot Nigga.” Greezy and Drake don’t personally know each other; however, Drizzy, ever the one to stay up-to-date, references such a current event to stay relevant.", "annotation_id": "4987653", "url": "https://genius.com/4987653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "I get a chicken, one night about it", "vault_lines": ["I get a chicken, one night about it"], "meaning": "Chicken is a stand in for cocaine, which P Reign is cooking up to make money. The word play here is that it also sounds like he is literally just cooking up a chicken to feed people. These lines also are in reference to the meme of “eatin'” as meaning that he is making money.", "annotation_id": "4801928", "url": "https://genius.com/4801928", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "I been going Hamilton for so long, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I been going Hamilton for so long, yeah"], "meaning": "In a nod to Kanye and Jay Z’s “H.A.M.”, Drake shouts out the city Hamilton, Ontario – where OB is from.\n\nIt’s interesting to note that Lewis Hamilton, a Formula One racer, appears to be affiliated with OVO.", "annotation_id": "4683984", "url": "https://genius.com/4683984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Still, tell them bring the rider, get my roll on", "vault_lines": ["Still, tell them bring the rider, get my roll on"], "meaning": "A rider is a list of a performer’s requests for the venue prior to a concert. OB’s rider presumably contains “get-high-supplies” – swishers, lighters, etc.\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/qylYiAnzYc/?modal=true", "annotation_id": "4597317", "url": "https://genius.com/4597317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "I been cookin’ up to feed everybody", "vault_lines": ["Flight risk on my right wrist, I been cookin' up to feed everybody"], "meaning": "Much like Drake, P. Reign takes care of his crew. Cookin' up references how he has been making bank, in turn to supply funds to everyone he rolls with.", "annotation_id": "4586861", "url": "https://genius.com/4586861", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Reps Up, OVO, you already", "vault_lines": ["Reps Up, OVO, you already", "Reps Up tell anybody, OVO, we ain't just anybody"], "meaning": "Reps Up is the record that P. Reign is signed under. OVO stands for Octobers Very Own and is Drake’s label/clique.", "annotation_id": "4575871", "url": "https://genius.com/4575871", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Someone tell Flex drop a bomb", "vault_lines": ["Someone tell Flex drop a bomb"], "meaning": "Funkmaster Flex has been a DJ on New York City’s popular hiphop radio station, hot97, since ‘92. He is notorious for overusing a soundclip of a bomb dropping during his sets and before/during/after songs he plays. You know you’ve made it when Flex is dropping bombs on your songs.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lAFZnvTfFAs", "annotation_id": "4575383", "url": "https://genius.com/4575383", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Shout out Sammy and Peezy one time, you know what it is", "vault_lines": ["and free Greezy one time", "Shout out Sammy and Peezy one time, you know what it is", "You know, you know, you know"], "meaning": "Sammy and Peezy are OB’s best friends back in Hamilton. Both are TOPSZN reps.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/wTpUMmHzWf/?modal=true", "annotation_id": "4569920", "url": "https://genius.com/4569920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "She’s like “TOPSZN, is that a bad thing?” I’m like “I don’t know, are you a bad ting?”", "vault_lines": ["She's like \"TOPSZN, is that a bad thing?\"", "I'm like \"I don't know, are you a bad ting?\"", "You know, you know, you know"], "meaning": "Many people question exactly what TOPSZN is. There’s even been speculation that TOPSZN is a strain of weed or a sexual gesture. While both interpretations could be correct, it’s most likely just a movement.\n\nBy not revealing the true meaning of TOPSZN to this girl – who’s clearly interested in the crew – OB maintains the mystique.\n\nIf you have to ask, you’ll never know.", "annotation_id": "4566331", "url": "https://genius.com/4566331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Bitches tell me that I look like 40 everyday (For sho) That’s okay, I’ll go and fuck them forty minutes straight (Hey!) Hey, showin’ up to 40 forty minutes late (Alright, alright) Just send forty to The H and put forty in the safe (Alright, alright) Damn, I got forty on my neck and wrist (Right!) OVO Soundboy, got 40 on the mix (Sound, mix!)", "vault_lines": ["Bitches tell me that I look like 40 everyday (For sho)", "That's okay, I'll go and fuck them forty minutes straight (Hey!)", "Hey, showin' up to 40 forty minutes late (Alright, alright)", "Just send forty to The H and put forty in the safe (Alright, alright)", "Damn, I got forty on my neck and wrist (Right!)", "OVO Soundboy, got 40 on the mix (Sound, mix!)", "OVO Sound, crank it up"], "meaning": "OB reinvents wordplay, weaving different uses of 40 through these lines.\n\nEven though he’s 30 years old, women tend to think he’s in his 40s because of his facial features. Their errors are no big deal, though, since he’ll show off his youthful stamina by correcting their mistakes in the bedroom.\n\nNoah “40” Shebib is affiliated with OVO Sound as Drake’s main engineer, so it’s not surprising that OB works frequently with him. Since blowing up on the music scene, OB has raked in the dough – he’s purchased expensive jewelry and sent other funds to the bank and his hometown of Hamilton.", "annotation_id": "4564394", "url": "https://genius.com/4564394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "No Money Manziel, the game is at a stand still How am I the man still? How am I the man still?", "vault_lines": ["No Money Manziel, the game is at a stand still", "How am I the man still? How am I the man still?"], "meaning": "Draft day, Johnny Manziel\nFive years later how am I the man still\n\nAs per the previous line, TOPSZN is not the “money sign” the NFL thinks it is.\n\nDrake dominates the rap game, which first round draft pick Johnny Manziel aims to do as he starts his career with the Cleveland Browns.", "annotation_id": "4564057", "url": "https://genius.com/4564057", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "I’m at the Hazey in the lobby with my robe on", "vault_lines": ["I'm at the Hazey in the lobby with my robe on"], "meaning": "The “Hazey” is The Hazelton Hotel, a luxury hotel in Yorkville. Drake, OB, and other OVO members stay here when in town.\n\nIn the Hazey’s lobby, Drake once drunkenly claimed to be Harry Styles.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cPSeEXwp9_g", "annotation_id": "4562525", "url": "https://genius.com/4562525", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "Bring you to Habibiz, get your smoke on", "vault_lines": ["Bring you to Habibiz, get your smoke on"], "meaning": "Habibiz is a café in Toronto where Drizzy and his squad often hang to smoke hookah. He’s not opposed to bringing his women there, either.", "annotation_id": "4562277", "url": "https://genius.com/4562277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "I’ll ride with the 45 until the day we run the 6", "vault_lines": ["I'll ride with the 45 until the day we run the 6"], "meaning": "Preme is going to keep his .45 caliber handgun around until the day him and the OVO crew are running Toronto and won’t need it anymore.\n\nThis is also a play on the sequence of numbers 4, 5, and 6.", "annotation_id": "4562078", "url": "https://genius.com/4562078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "And it kills me to yell out free Baka again But free Baka again,", "vault_lines": ["And it kills me to yell out free Baka again", "But free Baka again,"], "meaning": "Travis Savoury aka Baka is one of Drake’s closest friends and can be heard introducing himself at the end of “From Time”.\n\nBaka was out of jail from a gun charge with some help from Drake, but it seems like now he is back behind bars. While it hurts Drake to have to say ‘Free Baka’ again after going through the legal process with him for so long, Drizzy does it because he wants to see his brother out of jail.", "annotation_id": "4559777", "url": "https://genius.com/4559777", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "I’m halfway through the race and I’m already celebrating I’m on that Y shit, I’m back on my shit I ran the game so long, I should be wearing Dri-FIT", "vault_lines": ["I'm halfway through the race and I'm already celebrating", "I'm on that Y shit, I'm back on my shit", "I ran the game so long, I should be wearing Dri-FIT"], "meaning": "From dropping one of the 2014’s hottest songs, “0 to 100/The Catch Up,” to featuring on tracks such as “Believe Me” and “Only,” Drizzy had a HUGE year that helped boost both of his crews, OVO and YMCMB. Accomplishing all of this only 5 years after the release of his breakout mixtape is a good reason to celebrate!\n\n\n\nSince he’s been an avid endorser of Nike (collaborating with Jordan, wearing Nike gloves in his “Headlines” video, etc), it makes sense for him to wear their Dri-FIT line while running the rap game.\n\nAlthough he’s speaking metaphorically, he’s no stranger to running… into clubs… with his hands full of money…", "annotation_id": "4559546", "url": "https://genius.com/4559546", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "TOPSZN, I just sprinkle with my fingertips", "vault_lines": ["(Alright, alright!) TOPSZN, I just sprinkle with my fingertips"], "meaning": "The TOPSZN crew’s signature move is rubbing their fingertips together – see Johnny Manziel. It’s often referred to as the “money sign”.", "annotation_id": "4559174", "url": "https://genius.com/4559174", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "[Verse 1: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse was originally leaked in Rich Gang’s “Tell Me Why” – a cut featuring Drake and Rich Homie Quan.\n\nCheck out the original annotations right here!", "annotation_id": "4559076", "url": "https://genius.com/4559076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Schemin’ Up", "line": "I used to drive the Acura to school Now I don’t touch the wheel unless it’s new", "vault_lines": ["I used to drive the Acura to school", "Now I don't touch the wheel unless it's new"], "meaning": "That was back in the days, Acura days\n\nDrake used to be all about that Acura life, but he’s not the one driving these days. In fact, the only time you’ll ever catch Drizzy behind the wheel is if he’s whipping a new toy.", "annotation_id": "4558962", "url": "https://genius.com/4558962", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "status": "done", "targets": 40, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Big-sean-blessings-lyrics", "genius_title": "Blessings", "genius_id": 693878, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Always down for the cause, never down for the count", "vault_lines": ["Always down for the cause, never down for the count"], "meaning": "“Down for the count” is a boxing term. Once knocked down, boxers have until the referee counts to ten to stand back up, otherwise they lose the fight.\n\n\n\nSean is never in that situation. He’s always down to help. Whether it’s giving away Turkey’s in Detroit on Thanksgiving or previewing his album for disadvantaged fans.", "annotation_id": "8012937", "url": "https://genius.com/8012937", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "[Verse 4: Kanye West]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Editor’s Note: This verse, which appeared on the music video, was omitted from the album version.\n\nThe verses of “Blessings” are unique metrically, in that they feature a regular use of trisyllables (stress patterns involving three, rather that two syllables – see the annotations on Verse 1 for a solid breakdown).\n\nKanye’s verse, interestingly, is in some ways the most unique; nearly all his lines end on a spondee (two stressed syllables in a row), balanced by a regular use of anapests (short, short, long) and tribrachs earlier in the line (tribrachs are three unstressed syllables in a row – this is cool because you almost NEVER see them). This makes total sense for Kanye – he loves to hit the rhyme hard, and drag it out:\n\nThey WANT me by the ROAD holding up CARDBOARD\nso i GO extra HARD on the HARD FLOOR\nright NOW I’m calling YOU from my HOME GYM\nright after THAT nigga I’m GON' SWIM\n\nThe first two lines in the selection above are particularly ingenious. The internal (slant) rhyme on “road” and “hold,” the heavy “D” sounds, and the way the stresses fall repeatedly on the word “hard” gives the lines an incredible heaviness, underscoring the “hardness” of the both the lifestyle – and the floor – in question.", "annotation_id": "4854845", "url": "https://genius.com/4854845", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "[Verse 1: Big Sean]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This and the other verses are unique in that several lines fade in and out of trisyllabic meter– the syllables are pronounced in patterns of three rather than the patterns of two that are standard in rap.\n\nSome lines are dactyllic (long, short, short), others are anapestic (short, short, long), and still others are amphibrachic (short, long, short). Another well known example of trisyllabic meter in rap is the “The Way I Am” by Eminem.\n\nUsually the verses of “Blessings” begin dactylically, and then shift into variable trisyllabic hybrids, many of which are individually characteristic of each rapper. Here, Big Sean stays mostly dactylic, but has a fairly unique tendency to shift into pretty clean amphibrachs mid-verse.\n\nHere are some examples from the opening rhymes (note the emphases are marked according to his performance, not necessarily how they would read on paper):\n\nI lead a LIFE i deSERVE.\nFUCK a vaCAY I feel BETter at WORK\n\nThis scheme is dactylic, with an extra stressed syllable on the rhyme.\n\nBLEssings on BLEssings on BLEssings\nLOOK at my LIFE man thats LESsons on LESsons on LESsons\n\nThis scheme is dactylic.\n\nThe DRINKS are the COLDest, the FUTURE the BRIGHTest\nThe FEAT not diVIded, the LOVE is diVIded\n\nThis scheme is amphibrachic.", "annotation_id": "4840471", "url": "https://genius.com/4840471", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "The fee not divided, the love is divided And I just gotta thank God that we got it Blessed I don’t know what I would do without it", "vault_lines": ["The fee not divided, the love is divided", "And I just gotta thank God that we got it", "I don't know what I would do without it"], "meaning": "Sean isn’t concerned with “feats”, or achievements, rather positivity and love. As he told Complex Magazine:\n\nI’ve played the big guy and the little guy before, and I put my ego aside. I believe in karma, in giving and never being stingy. I believe in spreading love and being positive.", "annotation_id": "4815653", "url": "https://genius.com/4815653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "I don’t need them favors that you ask me for", "vault_lines": ["I don't need them favors that you ask me for"], "meaning": "Drake seems to think he didn’t need any favors to succeed, although he might owe Lil Wayne, at the very least, a “thank you,” as Wayne implies in his version of “Coco”.\n\nSome of his friends have taken a different approach, apparently.\n\nMaybe I keep movin' forward and they’re just stagnant, they ain’t movin' at all\nBut when they need a favor, who do they call?", "annotation_id": "4815368", "url": "https://genius.com/4815368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Try and blow my cake, just know that’s a death wish", "vault_lines": ["Try and blow my cake, just know that's a death wish"], "meaning": "When blowing out the candles on a birthday cake, it is a tradition to wish for something. Sean uses the double meaning of cake, and blow to add depth to his lyric – he’s saying that if you go after his money or any of his wealth, it will be a fatal mistake.", "annotation_id": "4815296", "url": "https://genius.com/4815296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Look at my life, man, that’s lessons on lessons on lessons", "vault_lines": ["Look at my life, man, that's lessons on lessons on lessons"], "meaning": "As he explains in “Nothing is Stopping You”, his career started with an opportunity to rap only sixteen bars — in front of Kanye West when the two ended up at the same radio station at the same time. Lesson learned, and a teachable moment for other rappers.", "annotation_id": "4813807", "url": "https://genius.com/4813807", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "And my daughter right there gettin’ homeschooled, I’m blessed And I was thinking ’bout starting up my own school, a Montessori", "vault_lines": ["Way up, I feel blessed", "I'm way up, I feel blessed", "And my daughter right there gettin' homeschooled, I'm blessed", "And I was thinking 'bout starting up my own school, a Montessori"], "meaning": "Neither Kanye or Kim were home schooled, but it seems with their ever increasing spotlight, it makes sense to give Nori some privacy.\n\nIn typical Kanye fashion, he wouldn’t mind creating his own school, one that encompasses a montessori education. This education focuses more on providing freedom for expression, along with child & social development. Ye' would probably make a good teacher!", "annotation_id": "4812695", "url": "https://genius.com/4812695", "state": "accepted", "votes": 111, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Right now, I’m callin’ you from my home gym Right after that, nigga, I’m gon’ swim Just did a couple laps in my home pool", "vault_lines": ["Right now, I'm callin' you from my home gym", "Right after that, nigga, I'm gon' swim", "Just did a couple laps in my home pool"], "meaning": "Kanye & Kim recently purchased a $20,000,000 Hidden Hills mansion.\n\n\n\n Maybe he makes summer workout tapes for Kim too?\n\n“Right now I’m calling you from my home gym”: Kanye’s way of telling us this verse is phoned in?", "annotation_id": "4812694", "url": "https://genius.com/4812694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Who flip like confetti And then when you back, they back to call you “dog,” that shit get-get petty", "vault_lines": ["Who flip like confetti", "And then when you back, they back to call you \"dog,\" that shit get-get petty"], "meaning": "Confetti is seen in motion, falling, turning over, perpetually flipping until it hits the ground. Sean personifies his friends as confetti: only around for the money, and if it didn’t come, they turned.\n\nSince Sean’s comeback, those friends who flipped have flipped yet again, and now thing Sean is their “dog,” though they’re likely just sniffing around for cash.", "annotation_id": "4812421", "url": "https://genius.com/4812421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "And the hallway lookin’ like a monastery, oh, yes I’m way up, I feel blessed", "vault_lines": ["Way up, I feel blessed", "I'm way up, I feel blessed", "And the hallway lookin' like a monastery, oh, yes"], "meaning": "A typical Monastery has hallways that are elegantly built and well decorated with beautiful art. Kanye’s homes are no different. His house in the Hidden Hills is fitting of the religious undertones (and blatant overtones) that Kanye litters his music with.\n\nIt seems he’s come a long way from jewelry, Yeezus cops homes now:\n\nI had a dream I could buy my way to heaven\nWhen I awoke, I spent that on a necklace", "annotation_id": "4812410", "url": "https://genius.com/4812410", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Never goin’ under, even with anesthetics At the top of the rap game and progressin’", "vault_lines": ["The family never goin' anorexic", "Never goin' under, even with anesthetics", "At the top of the rap game and progressin'"], "meaning": "This could be a dig at rappers prone to drug use, thought it’s likely just a play on anesthesia and Sean’s belief that he will never “go under” and be ranked low as a rapper.", "annotation_id": "4812405", "url": "https://genius.com/4812405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "So I’m going over time on the overtime", "vault_lines": ["So I'm going over time on the overtime"], "meaning": "Part of Sean’s work ethic came from his mentor Kanye West:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WBGHllSPhZc\n\nThis line ties back to his first verse:\n\nThis is that late night workin' after three\n\nSean works past the hours considered “overtime,” which might be technically impossible but, hey, poetry.", "annotation_id": "4812395", "url": "https://genius.com/4812395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Crew look like we robbed a bank, but all we make is deposits Young prophet with profit, oh God", "vault_lines": ["Crew look like we robbed a bank, but all we make is deposits", "Young prophet with profit, oh God"], "meaning": "Sean’s crew knows how to complete an outfit.\n\nWe hope Sean did his due diligence and got himself the highest possible interest rate for his checking account. This would also be a good time for him to diversify his portfolio.\n\n\n\nThe last line could be a reference to some bars from Kanye’s “Monster,” although it’s hard to tell with homophones like prophet/profit. Kanye’s verse in “Monster” doesn’t make any overtly religious references. (Later in “Monster,” Kanye does call himself a “pharaoh”.)", "annotation_id": "4812379", "url": "https://genius.com/4812379", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "For my niggas who gon’ go to Hell and back for me, I’ma give ’em Heaven on Earth Or a hell of a check, yeah, whichever come first", "vault_lines": ["For my niggas who gon' go to Hell and back for me, I'ma give 'em Heaven on Earth", "Or a hell of a check, yeah, whichever come first"], "meaning": "A play on “heaven” and “hell.” Sean will spend a hell of a lot of money for his friends to feel like they’re in heaven, or at least “Paradise.”\n\nIf that doesn’t work out, he’ll at least share the wealth.", "annotation_id": "4812311", "url": "https://genius.com/4812311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "They want me by the road holdin’ up cardboard So I go extra hard on the hard floor", "vault_lines": ["They want me by the road holdin' up cardboard", "So I go extra hard on the hard floor"], "meaning": "Haters want Kanye to fall from the mountain, and end up on the road, begging, with a cardboard sign.\n\n\n\nInstead Kanye promises to “go extra hard on the hard floor,” perhaps practicing in his private gym.\n\nHe delivered a similar rhyme in DJ Khaled’s song “Go Hard”:\n\nI think it’s time for me to go as hard as the hood I’m from", "annotation_id": "4812279", "url": "https://genius.com/4812279", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Man, the clique is the tightest, the pussy the tightest The drinks are the coldest, the future the brightest", "vault_lines": ["Man, the clique is the tightest, the pussy the tightest", "The drinks are the coldest, the future the brightest"], "meaning": "Sean has a crew deeper than Wu-Tang, a new partner he seems to appreciate, and he is organized enough to refrigerate all of his various bottles, before popping.\n\nThese things help Big Sean to remember why he does what he does.", "annotation_id": "4812261", "url": "https://genius.com/4812261", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "I guess when your stars align, you do like the solar system and plan it out", "vault_lines": ["I guess when your stars align, you do like the solar system and plan it out"], "meaning": "Big Sean uses the homophonic relationship between “plan it” and “planet” in order to talk about his work ethic. As with the solar system, Sean respects order.\n\n\n\n(Lil Wayne used a very similar line on his remix to “Levels”.)\n\nIt’s also important to remember that our solar system kicked a “planet out.” All “plan it/planet” puns from here on out are officially at Pluto’s expense. Poor Pluto.", "annotation_id": "4812236", "url": "https://genius.com/4812236", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Since the truth keep niggas traumatized They tryna compromise my condom size So I Snapchat that whole shit Tryna see titties, tryna show dick And I swear to God, I hope they post it, I’m blessed", "vault_lines": ["Since the truth keep niggas traumatized", "They tryna compromise my condom size", "So I Snapchat that whole shit", "Tryna see titties, tryna show dick", "And I swear to God, I hope they post it, I'm blessed"], "meaning": "I always had a PhD: a pretty huge dick\n\nKanye is confident about his size – so confident that he didn’t care when his nudes were leaked in 2010.\n\n\n\nNOTE: Kanye previously called himself a dick, so we felt this would be the only suitable way to censor the image.", "annotation_id": "4812106", "url": "https://genius.com/4812106", "state": "accepted", "votes": 307, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Even though I get slammed with lawsuits like car doors See three POs like Star Wars", "vault_lines": ["Even though I get slammed with lawsuits like car doors", "See three POs like Star Wars"], "meaning": "Ye is no stranger to run-ins with the law, Bound 2 sample disputes, and an assault against a paparazzo are just two of many on his resume.\n\nDue to his trouble with the law, Kanye sees three POs (parole officers). When pronounced, this sounds like C-3PO, a character from Star Wars. Big Sean used a line similar line on “Celebrity”, as has Method Man.", "annotation_id": "4812105", "url": "https://genius.com/4812105", "state": "accepted", "votes": 173, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Shit feel like Shaq and Penny got back together You tore the game apart, who put it back together? I", "vault_lines": ["Shit feel like Shaq and Penny got back together", "You tore the game apart, who put it back together?"], "meaning": "The NBA duo of Shaquille O'Neal and Anfernee “Penny” Hardaway is widely considered to be one of the better duos in NBA history. Playing together from 1993-1996 for the Orlando Magic, they led the team to their first 50-win season, and appearances in both the Eastern Conference Finals and the NBA Finals.\n\nWhat’s even more amazing is they actually got back together. The pair reunited and played with the Miami Heat during the 2007 NBA season.\n\n\n\nIn the music video, Sean is pointing towards himself and Drake when saying these lines.", "annotation_id": "4812066", "url": "https://genius.com/4812066", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "[Intro: Big Sean & Drake] Look I feel blessed Way up, I feel blessed Way up, I feel blessed (Uh-huh) Straight up, look", "vault_lines": ["Way up, I feel blessed", "I'm way up, I feel blessed"], "meaning": "Although Big Sean, Drake and Kanye all express gratitude for their good fortune, this isn’t the first time Big Sean has talked about his “blessings.”\n\nHere, Sean also uses his signature style of repeating, or “stacking,” words – as heard before here, here, and here.", "annotation_id": "4811911", "url": "https://genius.com/4811911", "state": "accepted", "votes": 205, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Stop worryin’ ’bout whoever’s next I am just worried ‘bout my mama worryin’ less", "vault_lines": ["Stop worryin' 'bout whoever's next", "I am just worried 'bout my mama worryin' less"], "meaning": "Like Drake raps on “All Me”:\n\nY'all keep talking ‘bout who next\nBut I’m about as big as it gets, I swear y'all just wasting y'all breath\n\nHe’s more concerned about making sure his mother is wealthy, rather than stressing about who the king of the rap game is.", "annotation_id": "4811904", "url": "https://genius.com/4811904", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "I’m here for a good time, not a long time, you know I I haven’t had a good time in a long time, you know I I’m way up, I feel blessed Way up, I feel blessed (Straight up) I’m way up, I feel blessed (Straight up, straight up) Way up, I feel blessed", "vault_lines": ["Way up, I feel blessed", "I'm here for a good time, not a long time, you know I", "I haven't had a good time in a long time, you know I", "I'm way up, I feel blessed"], "meaning": "Drake borrows “I’m here for a good time, not a long time” from a George Strait song (reversing the order of Strait’s original phrases). This is not the first time Drake has tipped his hat to the King of Country: Drizzy begins the first verse of “HYFR” with the line:  “All my exes live in Texas like I’m George Strait”.\n\nStrait’s song was released in 2011, over thirty years after one of Canada’s favorite classic-rock anthems, Trooper’s 1977 hit “We’re Here For A Good Time (Not a Long Time)”.\n\nThen Drake reiterates Sean’s intro: the three artists are thankful for their lives, and don’t take anything for granted.", "annotation_id": "4811883", "url": "https://genius.com/4811883", "state": "accepted", "votes": 182, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Bitch, don’t give no dap to me, nigga Funny thing about talking behind my back is that it just keep comin’ back to me, nigga Was off for a sec, now it’s back to me, nigga, you mad at me This ain’t what I want, man, this what it had to be", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, don't give no dap to me, nigga", "Funny thing about talking behind my back is that it just keep comin' back to me, nigga", "Was off for a sec, now it's back to me, nigga, you mad at me", "This ain't what I want, man, this what it had to be", "Man, this is why my old girl was mad at me", "I don't need them favors that you ask me for", "It is what it is, trust me, you get what you give, yeah"], "meaning": "When fake friends call Sean “dog,” he keeps it canine, calling them “bitch” in return. Getting “petty” runs along the same lines.\n\n\n\nThis isn’t the first time Sean has addressed “fake friends”. Drake also used a similar line on “The Language”:\n\nTalking that shit with your back to me just know it always gets back to me\n\nSean temporarily drops the triplet flow he uses throughout most of the verse, switches the style up (i.e. “was off for a sec”), and then returns to his original flow.", "annotation_id": "4811875", "url": "https://genius.com/4811875", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "No mistakes in life ever, it’s only lessons", "vault_lines": ["No mistakes in life ever, it's only lessons"], "meaning": "Sean claims he’s never made a mistake in his life, but has learned from his experiences. For instance on “4th Quarter”, he raps about learning from his relationships.\n\nSometimes to win the war man you got to lose a battle. But if I lose a girl just know my next one gonna be badder.", "annotation_id": "4811872", "url": "https://genius.com/4811872", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "So every morning I’m up ’cause I can’t let them down, down", "vault_lines": ["So every morning I'm up 'cause I can't let them down, down"], "meaning": "Hurtin' with my Mama looking at cribs we can’t purchase. Houses bigger than the Palace. But she a queen, so she deserve it\n\nSean’s repeated references to work are probably rooted in his experience growing up on the west side of Detroit — “really in the hood,” as he told the Huffington Post in 2011 — and then managing to bring his family up and out.", "annotation_id": "4811837", "url": "https://genius.com/4811837", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "I mean, whatever it’s worth, I’ll give whatever I’m worth", "vault_lines": ["I mean, whatever it's worth, I'll give whatever I'm worth"], "meaning": "Sean told us what he thinks worth on “Sanctified”: “100 million dollars and a bad bitch.” To achieve this, Sean will likely stay at work from “10 to 10”, as he’s told us before.", "annotation_id": "4811832", "url": "https://genius.com/4811832", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "If we’re back from somethin’, it’s some checks you owe us I expect that payment, nothing less or over", "vault_lines": ["‘Cause that implies that we're back from somethin'", "If we're back from somethin', it's some checks you owe us", "I expect that payment, nothing less or over"], "meaning": "Reminiscent of what Drake said in “Worst Behavior”:\n\nBitch you better have my money\nWhen I come for that shit like O.D.B\n\nThese bars may be a shot at Birdman, since the Young Money and Cash Money feud has escalated to a full-on law suit.", "annotation_id": "4811724", "url": "https://genius.com/4811724", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "I live the life I deserve, blessed Fuck a vacay, I feel better at work", "vault_lines": ["Way up, I feel blessed", "I live the life I deserve, blessed", "Fuck a vacay, I feel better at work"], "meaning": "Sean seems to have changed his mind about vacations. On the 2012 song “Mercy,” he rapped:\n\nFinally got paid, now I need shade and a vacay\n\nThen again, Sean’s work is making music – rapping is what makes him feel best.\n\nSean felt he deserved everything he had come to achieve, at the time having a reported net worth of $12 Million and dating Ariana Grande.", "annotation_id": "4811713", "url": "https://genius.com/4811713", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "I treat the beat like it’s a reverend I tell the truth like, “Father, forgive me, these are all my confessions”", "vault_lines": ["I treat the beat like it's a reverend", "I tell the truth like, \"Father, forgive me, these are all my confessions\""], "meaning": "Just as you’d never lie while confessing in church, Sean promises to never lie in his bars.\n\n\n\nAs he discussed in his interview with Complex Magazine, Sean’s bars on “4th Quarter” were a plainspoken tonic, the expression of a simple but brutal truth:\n\nIn the middle of recordin' we got caught up in the news. About what happened in St. Louis. We midwest kids that shit coulda happened to us", "annotation_id": "4811709", "url": "https://genius.com/4811709", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Blessings on blessings for me and my niggas from the 6, look at what we did, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Blessings on blessings for me and my niggas from the 6, look at what we did, yeah", "Blessings on blessings on blessings"], "meaning": "Drake is a consistent champion of talent from Toronto as well as across America. Thanks to Drake’s co-sign or direct business involvement, we’ve gotten to hear PARTYNEXTDOOR, Majid Jordan, ILoveMakonnen, OB O'Brien, and talented in-house producers like Boi-1da, Mike Zombie and Noah “40” Shebib, not to mention OVO’s close affiliates The Weeknd and P. Reign.", "annotation_id": "4811688", "url": "https://genius.com/4811688", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Them OVO boys the business, man It is what it is, trust me, you get what you give, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Them OVO boys the business, man", "It is what it is, trust me, you get what you give, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t really need the business advice of Birdman, frankly. Considering all the deals he has, like the OVO stores, and his job as the Toronto Raptors ambassador, Drake seems to have become a business, man!\n\n\n\nAdditionally, this could serve as a continuation of the receiving/giving of Grammys that Drake mentions in previous lines. Drake gets what he gives, which, in this specific case, are Grammys.", "annotation_id": "4811680", "url": "https://genius.com/4811680", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "This is that late night working after 3 Man, this is why my old girl was mad at me This why I’m your majesty", "vault_lines": ["This is that late night working after 3", "Man, this is why my old girl was mad at me", "This why I'm your majesty"], "meaning": "Sean alludes to his high school sweetheart, Ashley French. She inspired the song “Ashley” from his sophomore album, Hall of Fame. They ended up breaking up simply because Sean was finally becoming famous and would hardly be there when she needed him.", "annotation_id": "4811657", "url": "https://genius.com/4811657", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Look, I ain’t gon’ say that we back or nothin’ ‘Cause that implies that we’re back from somethin’", "vault_lines": ["Look, I ain't gon' say that we back or nothin'", "‘Cause that implies that we're back from somethin'"], "meaning": "As he recently proclaimed on “Used To,” Drake & his team are at the top of the rap game. He makes it clear they’re not on a “come back,” as this would imply they weren’t number one already.\n\n\n\nAs he rapped on “Headlines”:\n\nAnd they want to see me pick back up, well, where’d I leave it at?", "annotation_id": "4811653", "url": "https://genius.com/4811653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "My grandma just died, I’m the man of the house", "vault_lines": ["My grandma just died, I'm the man of the house"], "meaning": "Big Sean’s grandmother Mildred V. Leonard passed away on December 19, 2014, at the age of 93. Sean was raised by his grandmother, along with his mother, so there’s a subtle play here on gender, with Sean implying that his grandmother was the original “man of the house.”\n\nMy grandma passed away a couple days ago. I haven’t said anything to anyone because honestly I feel like she’s still here. I definitely can feel her consciousness. Im having a hard time accepting her death, but I must acknowledge my grandma’s life, a true hero. – Read more.", "annotation_id": "4811650", "url": "https://genius.com/4811650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "I think I’m famous enough, I don’t need any more press", "vault_lines": ["I think I'm famous enough, I don't need any more press"], "meaning": "It’s a well known that Drake is one of the most publicized rappers in the game. At this point, he’s made enough headlines to not need any more press.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/433971541622001664", "annotation_id": "4811648", "url": "https://genius.com/4811648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "You gotta come to my side and see how we live, yeah I cannot see Heaven being much better than this, yeah", "vault_lines": ["You gotta come to my side and see how we live, yeah", "I cannot see Heaven being much better than this, yeah"], "meaning": "“My side” is the “6” — Drake’s hometown of Toronto. According to Drake’s twitter, Toronto has already become his “Paradise”:\n\n\n\nDrake’s fourth studio album was also titled Views From The 6.", "annotation_id": "4811645", "url": "https://genius.com/4811645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "Man, this wasn’t luck, it was destined I done lost homies who been with me since Ed, Edd n Eddy", "vault_lines": ["Man, this wasn't luck, it was destined", "I done lost homies who been with me since Ed, Edd n Eddy"], "meaning": "Sean has lost his share of friends, ones that have been with him since day one – or, in this case, since Ed, Edd, and Eddy began in 1999.\n\n\n\nHis success wasn’t luck, it was always meant to happen. He addressed this subject on his song “My Last”:\n\nKnew that I’d be here so if you asked me how I feel\nI'mma just tell you, its everything that I expected", "annotation_id": "4811624", "url": "https://genius.com/4811624", "state": "accepted", "votes": 192, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Blessings", "line": "I could give two fucks ’bout where the Grammys go I just gave out Grammys on my Instagram", "vault_lines": ["I could give two fucks 'bout where the Grammys go", "I just gave out Grammys on my Instagram"], "meaning": "Drake is already a Grammy winner, and has been nominated for more this year without even releasing an album in 2014. Like most of the hip hop community, Drake doesn’t rely on the Grammys to tell him what the best music is.\n\nDrake conducted his own “Hood Grammys\" on Instagram, giving out his own personal awards to:\n\n\n\nBobby Shmurda for Vocal Performance of the Year\n\nOG Maco for best all around turn up\n\nRae Sremmurd and Rich Gang for best performance by a group or duo\n\nAnd many more.\n\n\n\nDrake echoed a similar sentiment during his 2019 Grammys acceptance speech:\n\nYou’ve already won if you have people singing your songs word for word…if there’s people who have regular jobs who are coming out in the rain, in the snow, spending their hard-earned money to buy tickets to come to your shows, you don’t need this right here [a Grammy], I promise you, you already won.", "annotation_id": "4811621", "url": "https://genius.com/4811621", "state": "accepted", "votes": 124, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "status": "done", "targets": 42, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-esco-100it-racks-lyrics", "genius_title": "100it Racks", "genius_id": 2688225, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "I ain’t gonna take no shots (Shots) Turn my dog to a boss (Boss) We ain’t gon’ take no loss (Nah)", "vault_lines": ["I ain't gonna take no shots (Shots)", "Turn my dog to a boss (Boss)", "We ain't gon' take no loss (Nah)"], "meaning": "A “short” is when someone pays you less than you’re owed. Future doesn’t use a scale, so he can tell if you’ve given him too little cash or drugs simply by sight.\n\nVia his aforementioned Freebandz label he’s providing an opportunity for his friends (dogs) to create their own entrepreneurial situations. Trusted collaborator Metro Boomin is well on the way to Boss status.", "annotation_id": "9918558", "url": "https://genius.com/9918558", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "You can go check these stones (Stones) Everything on me flooded (Flooded)", "vault_lines": ["You can go check these stones (Stones)", "Everything on me flooded (Flooded)"], "meaning": "The “stones” are diamonds that adorn all of Future’s jewellery. “Flooded” is to modify an existing piece of jewellery (a watch for example) by adding diamonds or expensive jewels (stones).", "annotation_id": "9917135", "url": "https://genius.com/9917135", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "I don’t really talk to y’all rappers (Mmm)", "vault_lines": ["I don't really talk to y'all rappers (Mmm)"], "meaning": "Chainz has a tight circle of friends, and despite a massive list of collaborations, he implies he’s really only close with a few of his contemporaries. It mimics Drake’s line from the first verse.", "annotation_id": "9912529", "url": "https://genius.com/9912529", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "I don’t talk to hoes about business", "vault_lines": ["I don't talk to hoes about business"], "meaning": "“Hoes” refers to both women, and his vanquished competition. On “Light Up” he explained this concept in more detail:\n\nAnd if you pillow talking with the women that are screwing you\nJust know that she gonna tell another nigga when she through with you\n\nHis successful response to Meek Mill’s accusations of ghostwriting (dropping “Back to Back” so soon after “Charged Up,”) suggest he keeps his plans to himself, blindsiding opponents who have no rumours or “pillow talk” to help them prepare.", "annotation_id": "9910398", "url": "https://genius.com/9910398", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Fuck y’all boys, y’all finished You’re dead!", "vault_lines": ["Fuck y'all boys, y'all finished"], "meaning": "Possible reference to Drake’s beef with Meek Mill, which currently stands in Drake’s favour. Another expression of confidence from the 6 God.", "annotation_id": "9910143", "url": "https://genius.com/9910143", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Diamond in my tooth from the dentist", "vault_lines": ["Diamond in my tooth from the dentist"], "meaning": "Johnny Dang, a popular jeweller and grill maker, created something for Drake involving diamonds in 2012.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/tvjohnny/status/185341515084742657", "annotation_id": "9891603", "url": "https://genius.com/9891603", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "All y’all niggas don’t matter", "vault_lines": ["All y'all niggas don't matter"], "meaning": "This line precedes an illustrated example of Drake’s supreme confidence. He hit’s threes despite defensive pressure, he crosses over anyone in his way to the hoop, he’s savage enough to steal anyone’s girl, he flips criticism into a positive, he shouts out the drug Xanax to reduce the mental anguish from any jabs, he drinks from Future’s Lean cup with no concern, and despite the attempts of the higher ups, he continues to thrive.", "annotation_id": "9882330", "url": "https://genius.com/9882330", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Hundred racks sittin’ in the plastic (Plastic) I ain’t gon’ even fuckin’ touch it (Touch it)", "vault_lines": ["Hundred racks sittin' in the plastic (Plastic)", "I ain't gon' even fuckin' touch it (Touch it)"], "meaning": "“Racks” of money are usually packaged in plastic, so large amounts can be transported at once. This is especially true of strip clubs, who usually deal in “singles” or dollar bills, so patrons need large quantities of cash to “make it rain.”\n\n\n\nFuture may also be talking about drugs. A “rack” is usually a line of cocaine, and that’s one drug Future sells but doesn’t use.", "annotation_id": "9859439", "url": "https://genius.com/9859439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "’Member when my phone was on minutes", "vault_lines": ["'Member when my phone was on minutes"], "meaning": "Prepaid phone plans, where you pay as you go, often operate on a total number of minutes per recharge. Although Drake was an actor on Degrassi High, he was only earning $50k a year, enough to “help mama out” but not enough to land an unlimited phone plan.", "annotation_id": "9858752", "url": "https://genius.com/9858752", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Know you tryna steal that sauce (Sauce)", "vault_lines": ["Know you tryna steal that sauce (Sauce)"], "meaning": "Due to his success in popularizing Southern “trap-rap,” Future feels like other rappers are trying to emulate him for their own selfish benefits.\n\nOne of the most frequently accused imitators of Future’s sound is the rapper Desiigner, who was propelled into fame due to his hit song “Panda.” The smash hit has a very strong “trap-rap” influence despite Desiigner being from New York. This lyric could be Future’s way of acknowledging the public rabble about their similarity.", "annotation_id": "9858169", "url": "https://genius.com/9858169", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "All of you niggas that took the swag (Ugh) I’ma have to get residuals (Ugh)", "vault_lines": ["All of you niggas that took the swag (Ugh)", "I'ma have to get residuals (Ugh)"], "meaning": "A “residual” in entertainment is a regular payment for a past idea or release. 2 Chainz is known for his wild style choices, and those that try to emulate him are free to do so, as long as they pay. He’s no stranger to making money off fashion.", "annotation_id": "9857118", "url": "https://genius.com/9857118", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Oh, shit, guess it don’t matter Talk down on me, I’m flattered", "vault_lines": ["Oh, shit, guess it don't matter", "Talk down on me, I'm flattered", "Wrong cup, guess it don't matter"], "meaning": "Drake has a lot of enemies, but their diss tracks and slander in interviews don’t bother him, and accusations that his success is “staged” only serves to flatter him.\n\nPublicity from these disputes adds to Drake’s popularity. He was nominated for a Grammy during his dispute with Meek Mill beef, for “Back to Back.”", "annotation_id": "9812892", "url": "https://genius.com/9812892", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "(Yeah!)", "vault_lines": ["Talkin' duffle talk, yeah", "We ain't gotta use no scale (Yeah)", "It's all there, you ain't gotta count it (Yeah)", "I'm 'bout to run up a bag (Yeah)", "Got a whole city on my back (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Both of 2 Chainz' most prevalent and popular adlibs. Chainz explained both his Tru adlib and his new name:\n\n\nTRU is an acronym for The Real University, it’s a thought that I came up with. It’s about being an individual, being comfortable in your own skin, doing things in the community, giving back, it’s just about being true, true to yourself.\n\nPeople ask me where the 2 Chainz came from I tell them I always talk about jewelry and I always been saying 2 Chainz in a lot of my songs. But lately like the last year or so I’ve been saying it in introducing my records like ‘2 Chainz!’ and kinda making it family friendly. However you wanna say it. You know like 2 Chainz, second chance. So I thought that that would have a little more substance.", "annotation_id": "9809939", "url": "https://genius.com/9809939", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Put another hundred in the rifle (Rifle) Everybody better be quiet, uh (Shh) Everybody put your hands higher (Higher)", "vault_lines": ["Put another hundred in the rifle (Rifle)", "Everybody better be quiet, uh (Shh)", "Everybody put your hands higher (Higher)"], "meaning": "Packing 100 rounds into a rifle is rare but doable. Chainz is no stranger to large weapons; in 2013 he and several members of his posse were arrested for weapon and drug possession.", "annotation_id": "9809886", "url": "https://genius.com/9809886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "I ain’t really worried ’bout a image", "vault_lines": ["I ain't really worried 'bout a image"], "meaning": "Drake lets us know he doesn’t care about what people think of him. Shrugging off haters and rumours is his method of preserving his integrity.", "annotation_id": "9802389", "url": "https://genius.com/9802389", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "All of my bitches bi-ah They buyin’ extra clothes, I mean They bisexual, I mean", "vault_lines": ["All of my bitches bi-ah", "They buyin' extra clothes, I mean", "They bisexual, I mean"], "meaning": "2 Chainz plays with the homophone buy/bi. All his women happily spend his money and go shopping for designer clothes, and they also have lesbian affairs. Chainz echoes this sentiment on “Where U Been”:\n\nI just hit my girlfriend and asked her where her wife at", "annotation_id": "9795946", "url": "https://genius.com/9795946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Got a new bank account alert (’Lert)", "vault_lines": ["Got a new bank account alert ('Lert)"], "meaning": "To stay under the radar, Future opened a brand new account. He also had an incredible 2015, in which his net worth increased by $1 million. he needs somewhere to store that tour money.", "annotation_id": "9765807", "url": "https://genius.com/9765807", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "All of my 16s fire, ooh", "vault_lines": ["All of my 16s fire, ooh"], "meaning": "Typically, guest features on rap songs are composed of 16 bar verses, with each verse containing four beats.\n\n2 Chainz claims every one of his guest verses—like this one—are “fire,” a well known slang term meaning hot or high quality.\n\n\n\nThere’s also between 15 and 17 bullets in a standard handgun clip. A little less than the 100 he threatened, but still deadly.", "annotation_id": "9763450", "url": "https://genius.com/9763450", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Whole Freebandz on Xanax Y’all can’t do no damage", "vault_lines": ["Whole Freebandz on Xanax", "Y'all can't do no damage", "Whole Freebandz on mud (Mud)", "Whole Freebandz on go"], "meaning": "Future and the other Freebandz artists often rap about taking Xanax, an anti-anxiety drug.\n\n\n\nThe drug is important in treating panic disorders, and can help the user overcome social anxiety, compulsive tendencies and irrational fear of nonthreatening situations. Taken by someone like Drake, it robs threats and barbs of their potential harm.", "annotation_id": "9763174", "url": "https://genius.com/9763174", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Crocodile Hermès bag (Bag) Stuffed all down my pockets (Whoa)", "vault_lines": ["Crocodile Hermès bag (Bag)", "Stuffed all down my pockets (Whoa)"], "meaning": "Hermès' crocodile bags are the famous Birkin Bags which run buyers tens of thousands of dollars a piece. In 2015, the bags were subject to controversy over animal rights in the production of the bags.\n\nThe Hermès Birkin bags actually came about because of Jane Birkin’s desire for a purse with pockets:\n\n\nIn the 1980s, so the tale goes, Birkin had been upgraded on an Air France flight and was fiddling with the contents that had fallen out of a mundane handbag, two days after her then-husband, Jacques Doillon, had reversed his car over the cherished basket she used to carry as well, ‘crushing it on purpose’.\n\nWhen the passenger sat next to her suggested she needed a bag with pockets, she said: ‘The day Hermès makes one with pockets I will have that.’ He turned out to be the Hermès chief executive and they came up with a design together on the back of a sick bag, in exchange for the use of her name.", "annotation_id": "9763166", "url": "https://genius.com/9763166", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Got a whole city on my back (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Got a whole city on my back (Yeah)"], "meaning": "To put the city on your back is to take responsibility for their success or failure, to lead them (hopefully) to victory. Future is from Atlanta, a city that helps run hip hop, and despite multiple high-quality artists emerging from the area, Future is arguably at the top of the tree.", "annotation_id": "9762953", "url": "https://genius.com/9762953", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "You can go ask L.A. (Lay) I don’t go to work on budgets (Ugh)", "vault_lines": ["You can go ask L.A. (Lay)", "I don't go to work on budgets (Ugh)"], "meaning": "Not be confused with the city of Los Angeles, Future is talking about L.A. Reid, CEO of Epic Records. Reid signed Future in the same year he took over the chair. Chief CFO Lynn Hazan is in charge of the budget, or in Future’s example, plastic wrapped “racks” of cash.", "annotation_id": "9762881", "url": "https://genius.com/9762881", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "And I gotta work three phones (Phones)", "vault_lines": ["And I gotta work three phones (Phones)"], "meaning": "Future is a busy man and busy men have a lot of contacts. He’s the plug after all. The multiple phones are to make it harder for the law to keep tabs on him.\n\nThis may also be a reference to Kevin Gates’s smash hit “2 Phones,” with Fewtch one-upping Kevin’s phone count.", "annotation_id": "9762724", "url": "https://genius.com/9762724", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "I’m a different individual (Ugh) Got my hand on my genitals (Ugh)", "vault_lines": ["I'm a different individual (Ugh)", "Got my hand on my genitals (Ugh)"], "meaning": "2 Chainz is 6 feet 5 inches tall, earned his college degree with a 4.0 GPA, and is worth about $6 million. He isn’t an average rapper (despite shouting out the standard rapper pose), and clearly isn’t an average person.\n\n\n\nThis line also serves as a reference to 2 Chainz’s hit-song, “I’m Different,” in which he also embraces his uniqueness.", "annotation_id": "9762591", "url": "https://genius.com/9762591", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "This is the crazy flow I got a straitjacket in the booth (Ugh)", "vault_lines": ["I, I got a... (DJ Esco)", "This is the crazy flow", "I got a straitjacket in the booth (Ugh)"], "meaning": "Straightjackets are used to restrict the movement of the criminally insane who exhibit violent tendencies so they can’t harm people around them.\n\nIn a similar fashion, 2 Chainz' flow is so “crazy” that he needs a straightjacket in the booth to contain him.", "annotation_id": "9762340", "url": "https://genius.com/9762340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Roll up the Texaco, I mean", "vault_lines": ["Roll up the Texaco, I mean"], "meaning": "Texaco is an American oil company owned by the Chevron Corporation, servicing gas stations across the country.\n\n“Gas” is slang for high quality weed strains. 2 Chainz takes the term even further, attributing the quality of his weed to an entire petroleum manufacturing organization.", "annotation_id": "9762210", "url": "https://genius.com/9762210", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Hundred thousand dollars for a walkthrough (Hundred) I’ma need mine in cash (Hundred)", "vault_lines": ["Hundred thousand dollars for a walkthrough (Hundred)", "I'ma need mine in cash (Hundred)"], "meaning": "A walkthrough is the bread and butter for a rapper, getting paid to simply appear at a club. On “Where Ya At,” another Drake and Future collab, Future proclaimed:\n\n60 for a walk through, bitch don’t panic\n\nNow, a year later and more famous than last year, Future has increased his walkthrough price to $100K. In cash, so he can spend it faster.", "annotation_id": "9762165", "url": "https://genius.com/9762165", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Whole Freebandz on mud (Mud) Taliban gang on Percs (Percs)", "vault_lines": ["Whole Freebandz on Xanax", "Whole Freebandz on mud (Mud)", "Taliban gang on Percs (Percs)", "Whole Freebandz on go"], "meaning": "Future name-drops his label, Freebandz—All of the labels members are prominent lean drinkers, the drink is also known as “mud” because of it’s dark composition and texture. “Perc” is short for Percocet, an opioid pain pill.\n\nIn “Jumpman,” Drake and Future confused many by shouting out “Taliban” in the intro, a name most known as an Afghanistan-based militant group. On Freebandz'  collaborative mixtape, FreeBand Gang: The Movie, Future and Stuey Rock had a song called “Freeband Taliban.”\n\nAlthough the actual origin of Future’s “Taliban” is unclear, many speculate it is a callback to producer Metro Boomin, who started using #Taliban in his tweets after he tweeted some lyrics to T.I.’s “Rubber Band Man”:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MetroBoomin/status/192823085345415168\n\nIn Atlanta, where Future grew up, there was a Grove Street (a street immortalized in Waka Flacka Flame’s “Grove St. Party”) based gang called Hit Squad Taliban. Future was never a part of the Hit Squad Taliban gang, and as far as we can tell, the gang no longer exists, but it’s more feasible than Future referencing a militant group.\n\n\n\n\nSome have speculated that “Taliban” refers to DJ Esco, Future’s DJ and the artist of this mixtape, and his 56 night stint in a Dubai prison (which you can read about here). According to Esco, inside the jail he met a captive Taliban soldier. This is not a feasible explanation, because Future has been using this phrase long before Esco was arrested.", "annotation_id": "9762025", "url": "https://genius.com/9762025", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Then I chop the top like, “Hi-yah,” woo!", "vault_lines": ["Then I chop the top like, \"Hi-yah,\" woo!"], "meaning": "“Hiyah” is a sound made when performing Karate, and the “Karate Chop” is a famous martial arts move.\n\n“Hiyah” can also be read as “Hiyah!”, a greeting. The removal of Chainz' roof brings him closer to the people.", "annotation_id": "9762002", "url": "https://genius.com/9762002", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Got a hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Got a hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper And I, I got a... Got a hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Got a hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Got a hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Got a hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Got a hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Got a hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper Got a hunnit racks bustin’ out the wrapper", "vault_lines": ["I, I got a... (DJ Esco)", "Hunnit racks bustin' out the wrapper", "Got a hunnit racks bustin' out the wrapper"], "meaning": "Drake and Future continue their support of strippers once again by throwing 100,000 in dollar bills at the strip club. In 2015, they dedicated “Plastic Bag” to strippers who pick up all the money on the floor when the clubs close.\n\nThe day before this song’s release, Drake and 2 Chainz allegedly spent $2 million at the strip club Vlive Atlanta. Drake also reportedly made it rain $50,000  in 2013 at a North Carolina strip club.", "annotation_id": "9761982", "url": "https://genius.com/9761982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Y’all still treat it like a scrimmage", "vault_lines": ["Y'all still treat it like a scrimmage"], "meaning": "A scrimmage is as close to a practice game as possible. It’s often still competitive, but usually doesn’t count towards either team’s wins or loses. Essentially, it’s meaningless in the grand scheme of things.\n\nDrake calls out all the other rappers. They treat everything they do like a scrimmage, while Drake treats it with the utmost seriousness.", "annotation_id": "9761941", "url": "https://genius.com/9761941", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Talkin’ duffle talk, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Talkin' duffle talk, yeah"], "meaning": "“Duffle Talk” is another way of saying “big money talk”—Future is bragging about his wealth. Large sums of money are often carried in duffle bags because of their volume.", "annotation_id": "9761709", "url": "https://genius.com/9761709", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Summer ’16 Yeah Freebandz, OVO DJ Esco", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The “Summer Sixteen Tour” is Drake and Future’s summer tour. The name is derived from Drake’s hit single, “Summer Sixteen,” which is about Drake owning the summer of 2016.\n\n\n\nDJ Esco shouts out Future (Freebandz is Future’s label), Drake (OVO is Drake’s label), and of course himself—he claims they will be the three artists who will dominate summer 2016.", "annotation_id": "9761586", "url": "https://genius.com/9761586", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Put a nigga name in the Guinness", "vault_lines": ["Put a nigga name in the Guinness"], "meaning": "Though Drake doesn’t have any Guinness World Records, he has his fair share of music records: Views charted a record eighteen songs on the Billboard Hot 100 at once, while his 2015 run of two number-one albums in one year tied a record.\n\nOne record relevant to his dismissive bars is having the diss track “Back to Back” nominated for a Grammy.", "annotation_id": "9761474", "url": "https://genius.com/9761474", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "I’m just blessed, full talent For the whole 6, I’m wildin’", "vault_lines": ["I'm just blessed, full talent", "For the whole 6, I'm wildin'"], "meaning": "Drake is one of the most successful rappers in the game, and he claims it is due to his musical talents. “Blessed” is also a callback to Big Sean’s “Blessings,” which he was featured on.\n\n“Wylin'” is slang for “bugging out” or “going crazy.” Not one to shy away from his love for Toronto, Drake is doing everything he can for his city.", "annotation_id": "9761446", "url": "https://genius.com/9761446", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Hundreds on hundreds on hundreds (Hundreds) I done ran up me a bag (Bag) We got a tour this summer (Summer) I’m ’bout to run up a bag (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I, I got a... (DJ Esco)", "Hundreds on hundreds on hundreds (Hundreds)", "I done ran up me a bag (Bag)", "We got a tour this summer (Summer)", "I'm 'bout to run up a bag (Yeah)"], "meaning": "In the summer of 2016, Drake and Future will be touring together as part of the Summer Sixteen Tour.\n\n“A bag” is slang for money. Considering the amount of money you can make from a tour, and the fact that Drake and Future are two of rap’s biggest stars, it’s safe to assume they’ll have plenty of bags after summer 2016.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/724554075866574848/photo/1?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw", "annotation_id": "9761405", "url": "https://genius.com/9761405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Name another nigga gettin’ cloned (Cloned) Put a hoe nigga on a shirt (Shirt)", "vault_lines": ["Put codeine on a salad (Salad)", "Name another nigga gettin' cloned (Cloned)", "Put a hoe nigga on a shirt (Shirt)"], "meaning": "Future has inspired several rappers and singers, especially when it comes to vocals and the use of autotune. This could be a subtle shot at Desiigner – who has scored a Billboard topping hit with “Panda.”\n\nMany people have pointed out the vocally similarities of both and it became quickly a meme in the internet.\n\n\n\nFuture might threaten to get him killed and to put him on a RIP t-shirt.\n\n“Clone” may also refer to his three phone’s. Cloning a phone involves transferring secure data from one device to another, to be disseminated later.", "annotation_id": "9761328", "url": "https://genius.com/9761328", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "I ain’t dead yet like I’m Bruce Willis", "vault_lines": ["I ain't dead yet like I'm Bruce Willis"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out the action movie series Die Hard.\n\nIn the movie series, the protagonist, Bruce Willis, is seemingly impervious to death, despite constantly being in harm’s way. Funnily enough, at the end of The Sixth Sense, Willis' character is revealed to have been dead all along.\n\n\n\nLike Bruce, Drake hasn’t died yet, despite multiple rappers coming at his head. Meek Mill, Pusha T, and Jay Z are among the many people that have targeted Drake, and yet, he’s still here.", "annotation_id": "9761305", "url": "https://genius.com/9761305", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "I switch hands like Allen", "vault_lines": ["I switch hands like Allen"], "meaning": "After the basketball reference in the previous line, Drake continues by referencing Allen Iverson’s penchant for crossing people over via switching the hand the basketball is in and creating misdirection.", "annotation_id": "9761303", "url": "https://genius.com/9761303", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "You think she your baby girl She text us like Dallas", "vault_lines": ["You think she your baby girl", "She text us like Dallas"], "meaning": "“Text us” sounds like “Texas”, and Dallas is a major city in the state of Texas.\n\n\n\n“Baby girl” is a common way to refer to a girlfriend or a daughter. Whoever he’s addressing here, Drizzy is saying that the girl most important in their lives is actually texting him and his crew.", "annotation_id": "9761299", "url": "https://genius.com/9761299", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "I hit threes off-balance", "vault_lines": ["I hit threes off-balance"], "meaning": "A three pointer in basketball is already an impressive feat, but hitting a three pointer off balance is one for the highlight reel.\n\nThis metaphor shows just how impressive Drizzy is: he’s able to get out of tough situations, like his beef with Meek Mill where he came out victorious. Drake’s actual jumper may need a little work, however.\n\n\n\nDrake often employs basketball metaphors in his raps. In “Still Here,” he compared himself to Stephen Curry:\n\n“Hittin' like that 30 on my jersey man I’m gifted”\n\nHe also plays with the next line and the number three, which was also the uniform number of Allen Iverson who is referenced in the next line.", "annotation_id": "9761287", "url": "https://genius.com/9761287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "100it Racks", "line": "Wrong cup, guess it don’t matter This one tastes like candy This one must be Hendrix", "vault_lines": ["Oh, shit, guess it don't matter", "Wrong cup, guess it don't matter", "This one tastes like candy", "This one must be Hendrix"], "meaning": "“Future Hendrix” is one of Future’s alternate names, born of his self-comparison to Jimi Hendrix.\n\nFuture is well known for his Lean usage, naming his 2011 mixtape Dirty Sprite in honour of the concoction.\n\nDrake has several songs inspired by lean, although he’s not a regular drinker.\n\n\n\nSome Lean sippers add candy to their concoction to colour it a distinct shade, with Jolly Ranchers a popular choice.", "annotation_id": "9761243", "url": "https://genius.com/9761243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "status": "done", "targets": 34, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/2-chainz-big-amount-lyrics", "genius_title": "Big Amount", "genius_id": 2831905, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Got the money and I never show it Let a nigga try to play heroic", "vault_lines": ["Got the money and I never show it", "Let a nigga try to play heroic"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION:\n\nThis is nothing other than another Drake line that he raps, but doesn’t actually mean. And the nerve of this dude to call anybody a fraud/fake.\n\nhttps://hypebeast.com/2017/7/drake-responds-to-jayz-money-phone", "annotation_id": "10199680", "url": "https://genius.com/10199680", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Tryna avoid all the pat-downs", "vault_lines": ["Tryna avoid all the pat-downs", "Tryna avoid all the lame hoes"], "meaning": "A pat down is officially known as a Terry Frisk.  This Supreme Court decision allows a police officer to “frisk” or “pat down” the outside layer of a suspects clothing for weapons.  The Court found this did not violate the suspects Bill of Rights because it has the goal of officer safety in mind.", "annotation_id": "11069773", "url": "https://genius.com/11069773", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "You know what they say Me and my safe, got a friendship Ten on me, thin weight, flip weight, gettin’ paid", "vault_lines": ["You know what they say", "Me and my safe, got a friendship", "Ten on me, thin weight, flip weight, gettin' paid"], "meaning": "Chainz has a relationship with his safe—the place he keeps all his money.\n\n10 bands is equal to $10,000, but that’s still light money to him because we all know he gets paid big money.", "annotation_id": "10211818", "url": "https://genius.com/10211818", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Fuck niggas on your payroll And you let ’em know the safe code And you knew him for a month though But you call them niggas big bro", "vault_lines": ["Fuck y'all niggas on, man?", "Fuck niggas on your payroll", "And you let 'em know the safe code", "And you knew him for a month though", "But you call them niggas big bro"], "meaning": "Possible shot at Chris Brown. Brown had 4 “friends” break into his house on July 15, 2016 in which they stole 50k dollars from his safe.", "annotation_id": "10205956", "url": "https://genius.com/10205956", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "I’ma tell you, I’ma tell you this right now If you, if you woke up this mornin’, nigga you winnin’ for real", "vault_lines": ["I'ma tell you, I'ma tell you this right now", "If you, if you woke up this mornin', nigga you winnin' for real"], "meaning": "It’s likely he is prompted by the amount of Black men being shot and killed by police, a sickening statistic making even trips to the store dangerous.", "annotation_id": "10204477", "url": "https://genius.com/10204477", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Got a tool with the screw missin’ Two girls in the pool kissin’", "vault_lines": ["Got a tool with the screw missin'", "Two girls in the pool kissin'"], "meaning": "A “tool” is slang for a firearm and having a “screw missing” means it has no safety, and is likely to go off at the slightest provocation. To “have a screw missing” is also to be slightly unhinged. 2 Chainz uses this play on words to tell us that he’s erratic with his weapons.", "annotation_id": "10201914", "url": "https://genius.com/10201914", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Last night was a blur to me This mornin’ I got two with me I don’t know what to do with me Goin’ ape like the zoo with me", "vault_lines": ["Last night was a blur to me", "This mornin' I got two with me", "I don't know what to do with me", "Goin' ape like the zoo with me"], "meaning": "Going ape occurs when you drink or smoke so much that you can’t even walk or speak properly, much less remember what you did the next morning. It is the extreme version of going bananas.", "annotation_id": "10201893", "url": "https://genius.com/10201893", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "I bought the dress that she in Red bottoms in his and hers Got a Rollie in his and hers Got a car in his and hers Walk in the zoo and say, “Pick a fur”", "vault_lines": ["I bought the dress that she in", "Red bottoms in his and hers", "Got a Rollie in his and hers", "Got a car in his and hers", "Walk in the zoo and say, \"Pick a fur\""], "meaning": "2 Chainz has such a big amount that he can afford to buy the same item twice, one for himself and the other one for his girl.\n\nUsually his and hers items aren’t very valuable, but 2 Chainz buys…\n\n\n\nRed bottoms or Christian Louboutins, a luxury footwear brand which has a signature of red sole.\n\nRollies or Rolexes, a famous brand and manufacturer of watches.\n\nCars. While what kind is unclear, on “Believe Me,” another song featuring Drake, Lil Wayne has his and hers Ferraris.", "annotation_id": "10201651", "url": "https://genius.com/10201651", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Gettin’ laid Bitch made, took off sick days 6 trey, 6-4 bounce, bitch, bounce", "vault_lines": ["Bitch made, took off sick days", "6 trey, 6-4 bounce, bitch, bounce"], "meaning": "“6 Trey” &  “6 4” are terms  used to describe 1963  and 1964 model Chevrolet Impala Lowrider Vehicles.  Hydraulics are usually installed in these type of cars to create a “bounce” effect, which draws reference to the girl’s ass bouncing. Culturally, lowriders are often associated with West Coast Rap.", "annotation_id": "10200953", "url": "https://genius.com/10200953", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Everywhere I go, the rod with me ‘Cause these niggas actin’ too fishy", "vault_lines": ["Everywhere I go, the rod with me", "'Cause these niggas actin' too fishy", "I'm respected everywhere I go"], "meaning": "Fishing rods catch fish. The rod Chainz is referring to may also be a gun, which he has for when people start acting different around him.", "annotation_id": "10200926", "url": "https://genius.com/10200926", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "And I’ve never seen the inside of a Marriott or a Westin Five stars, nothin’ less than", "vault_lines": ["And I've never seen the inside of a Marriott or a Westin", "Five stars, nothin' less than"], "meaning": "Both the Marriott and the Westin are “chain” hotels, and their quality isn’t at the level Drake’s fame and wealth demands.\n\nDrake’s claim is somewhat dubious, considering he’s been placed in both a Marriott and a Westin hotel before.\n\nhttps://genius.com/a/fact-check-has-drake-never-seen-the-inside-of-a-marriott-hotel", "annotation_id": "10200213", "url": "https://genius.com/10200213", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Lit and you can’t even put it out", "vault_lines": ["Lit and you can't even put it out"], "meaning": "Likely a comment on Drake’s current popularity, which has extended to stratospheric heights with the release of 2016’s Views. In the past 18 months he’s seen off high-profile beef with both Meek Mill and Joe Budden, neither of which has dented his success.\n\nThis line represents progression after 2015’s “30 For 30 Freestyle” where Drake expressed paranoia that industry forces were conspiring against him.\n\nIt may also be a subtle jab at Meek Mill, who was on house arrest at the time and unable to release music. When he came off house arrest, he released the track “Litty,” with bars aimed at Drake. It’s possible Drake knew about this song ahead of time, and is referencing it here.", "annotation_id": "10200119", "url": "https://genius.com/10200119", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Yeah, rock double-cross on these bitches, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, rock double-cross on these bitches, yeah"], "meaning": "2 Chainz wears Ksubi jeans, which feature a double cross stitched into them. It’s also a direct reference to his name, he rocks two Jesus pieces.", "annotation_id": "10200062", "url": "https://genius.com/10200062", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Just left V Live", "vault_lines": ["Just left V Live"], "meaning": "V Live is a nightclub/stripclub located in Atlanta. Drake and 2 Chainz were spotted there at the birthday party for Drake’s bodyguard Chubbs’s. Later that night the two took a photo together in the studio, presumably while recording this song.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U4xsdPT3O8o\nDrake previously referenced V Live in his verse on Lil Wayne’s “Believe Me”:\n\nV Live just took me for a cheque\n\nand in “No Tellin'”:\n\nV-Live, I order that Alfredo pasta\nthen eat in the kitchen like I’m in the mafia", "annotation_id": "10199734", "url": "https://genius.com/10199734", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Got the sand-colored FN", "vault_lines": ["Got the sand-colored FN"], "meaning": "FN is short for Fabrique Nationale d'Herstal, a Belgian gun manufacturer. Drake has one of their weapons with a desert or sand colored paint job.\n\n\n\nIt’s possible Drake could also be referring to Effen vodka, which is sponsored by 50 Cent. Their salted caramel flavor features a sand-colored design on the bottle.", "annotation_id": "10199733", "url": "https://genius.com/10199733", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Got hits and I ain’t even put ’em out", "vault_lines": ["Got hits and I ain't even put 'em out"], "meaning": "During an interview with Zane Lowe where he introduced Views to the world, Drake revealed he has “some other songs in the stash.” He also said:\n\nI’m gonna have an arsenal of songs that have nothing to do with Views.\n\nIn August 2016, during OVO Fest, he revealed a collaborative album with Kanye West was also on the way.\n\nThis line could also refer to the success of any song he’s featured on.", "annotation_id": "10199667", "url": "https://genius.com/10199667", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "I got a big amount, I took a different route I am the pick of the litter", "vault_lines": ["I got a big amount, I took a different route", "I am the pick of the litter", "Look, I got a big amount"], "meaning": "Chainz is something special—despite growing up in the hood, he made it out through the unsuspecting route of music.\n\n“Pick of the litter” is an idiom generally meaning the best out of a group. Ironically, 2 Chainz was an only child.", "annotation_id": "10199544", "url": "https://genius.com/10199544", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Fuck y’all niggas on, man? Duffle bag forever", "vault_lines": ["Fuck y'all niggas on, man?", "Fuck niggas on your payroll"], "meaning": "Before 2 Chainz had a solo career, he went by the name Tity Boi and was one half of the duo Playaz Circle. Drake is referencing the group’s biggest hit,  2007’s “Duffle Bag Boy,” featuring Lil Wayne.", "annotation_id": "10199529", "url": "https://genius.com/10199529", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Shawty ride like an equestrian", "vault_lines": ["Shawty ride like an equestrian"], "meaning": "An equestrian is a horseback rider. Chainz complements her skills in bed by comparing her to someone who “rides” professionally.\n\n\n\nEquestrian horses are bred to not only by strong, but beautiful too.", "annotation_id": "10199524", "url": "https://genius.com/10199524", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Hood nigga, favorite spot was the Waffle House Patty melt with the hash browns", "vault_lines": ["Hood nigga, favorite spot was the Waffle House", "Patty melt with the hash browns"], "meaning": "The Waffle House is a 24-hour restaurant which is famous for serving breakfast all day. Chainz really digged it, because he’s rapped about it on “Extremely Blessed” and “24 Hours.”\n\n\n\nThe patty melt is a grilled sandwich made with a burger patty and melted cheese (hence the name).", "annotation_id": "10199501", "url": "https://genius.com/10199501", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "You could move in the Hidden Hills And we still don’t live by the same code", "vault_lines": ["You could move in the Hidden Hills", "And we still don't live by the same code"], "meaning": "Hidden Hills is a gated community in Los Angeles County, California. Drake has a house there.\n\n\n\nThe “code” is a double entendre, referring to the zip code of Hidden Hills (91302) and the collection of rules that every man should live by.", "annotation_id": "10199497", "url": "https://genius.com/10199497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Yeah, I am a boss on these bitches Yeah, I pour some Voss on these bitches Yeah, I have no thoughts on these bitches", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I am a boss on these bitches", "Yeah, I pour some Voss on these bitches", "Yeah, I have no thoughts on these bitches"], "meaning": "Chainz is ready to let his hair down on hoes.\n\nVOSS water is essentially the Cadillac of water—to pour it out is to showcase his wealth.  He might be pouring it on women to metaphorically satisfy their “thirst.”", "annotation_id": "10199429", "url": "https://genius.com/10199429", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "I’m a J. Prince investment", "vault_lines": ["I'm a J. Prince investment"], "meaning": "In 2006, Jas Prince (son of Rap-a-Lot Records CEO James Prince) found Drake via his Myspace page, and eventually presented him to Lil Wayne. Wayne almost passed on the rapper, but with Jas' determination he eventually signed Drake to Young Money, and the rest is history.\n\nIn October 2015, Prince settled out of court with Cash Money Records over “unpaid royalties.”", "annotation_id": "10199408", "url": "https://genius.com/10199408", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Michael Jackson talkin’ to me in my dreams And he say, “You bad and you know it” Better shamone with my check then", "vault_lines": ["You know what they say", "Michael Jackson talkin' to me in my dreams", "And he say, \"You bad and you know it\"", "Better shamone with my check then"], "meaning": "Legendary singer Michael Jackson also crossed over in terms of popularity, and with his untimely death in 2009. His 1987 track “Bad” and its iconic hook is used as a description of Drake. “Shamone” is used on that hook, and is an intentional mispronunciation of “come on.”\n\nIn July 2016, Drake also tied with Jackson’s 1983 chart record, having the dual honours of number 1 song and album (“One Dance” and Views) for 7 weeks straight.", "annotation_id": "10199394", "url": "https://genius.com/10199394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "I’m respected everywhere I go Nigga, long live Bankroll", "vault_lines": ["I'm respected everywhere I go", "Nigga, long live Bankroll"], "meaning": "Drake is paying homage to Trentavious White, also known as Bankroll Fresh—an Atlanta rapper and an associate of 2 Chainz. He was shot and killed inside an Atlanta recording studio March 4th 2016, at only 28 years old.", "annotation_id": "10199391", "url": "https://genius.com/10199391", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "I want it easy, please do not tease me I wore my Yeezys to dinner This is the season, I got the seasonin’ Don’t make me sprinkle you niggas", "vault_lines": ["Don't make me sprinkle you niggas", "I got my reasons, you wanna please me", "I don't know what to do with me", "I want it easy, please do not tease me", "I wore my Yeezys to dinner", "This is the season, I got the seasonin'"], "meaning": "Tity is a seasoned rap veteran with information to “sprinkle” (drop) on younger artist. He may also be threatening to sprinkle his haters with bullets, or season them with his dope music.\n\nYeezys refers to the Yeezy Boosts sneakers, released by Kanye West. “Yeezy Season” is as phrase that has become synonymous with Kanye’s clothing line, fashion shows, and album releases.\n\n\n\nThis might also be a reference to TOPSZN (pronounced ‘top season’), the name of Drake’s personal friend group. Their gesture/greeting is simulating sprinkling seasoning.", "annotation_id": "10199388", "url": "https://genius.com/10199388", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Atlanta could never die as long as Tit alive Yeah", "vault_lines": ["Atlanta could never die as long as Tit alive"], "meaning": "Atlanta is a city in Georgia. It is known as one of the most popular rap locations and is home to many successful rappers during the past decade.\n\n2 Chainz, formally know as Tity Boi, was born in College Park, Georgia and helped contribute Georgia’s rap hype as being the most successful artist to date, and as long as he is alive, Atlanta will never ‘die’.", "annotation_id": "10199385", "url": "https://genius.com/10199385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Niggas love to talk reckless Then see me like best friends", "vault_lines": ["Niggas love to talk reckless", "Then see me like best friends"], "meaning": "Drake has a similar line on 2015’s “Used To,” and although it’s unclear which of his friends have been talking reckless behind his back, this could be a subliminal directed at ILoveMakonnen or The Weeknd.\n\nAfter ILoveMakonnen left Drake’s label OVO, he started sending shots at Drake, saying, in regard to Drake’s Views:\n\nWhat’s that dumb-ass album with the sky shit?\n\nHis history with The Weeknd is more complex, although it blew up after he explained he wrote some of Drake’s second album Take Care:\n\nHouse of Balloons was actually supposed to have more songs than it does. I had so many records left, and then Take Care came through. “Crew Love,” “Shot for Me,” and “The Ride” were supposed to be on House of Balloons. I wanted to come out with like 14 records.\n\nIn 2013 The Weeknd told Complex:\n\nI don’t like to spoon-feed people. I don’t like to be like, “You know what? I’m going to let the world know that we’re cool. We’re going to take a picture together. Everyone’s cool.” It’s all about the mystery, and people like it. Shit’s WWE, man. It’s wrestling, you know what I mean?\n\nIt’s possible Drake doesn’t quite feel the same way.", "annotation_id": "10199329", "url": "https://genius.com/10199329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Look, I got a big amount I think I’m the biggest out", "vault_lines": ["I got a big amount, I took a different route", "Look, I got a big amount", "I think I'm the biggest out"], "meaning": "This isn’t a debate, Drake is one of the most popular artists in the world in 2016. He tied with The Beatles in 2015 with 14 Billboard hits at once, “One Dance” spent 10 weeks atop the Billboard 100, and his record Views has, as of August 2016, spent 12 weeks at the top of the Billboard 200.\n\nThe “large amount” could be his mammoth net worth, or Views‘ double triple platinum status.", "annotation_id": "10199320", "url": "https://genius.com/10199320", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "I was in juvy, they gave me community I had to pick up some litter", "vault_lines": ["I was in juvy, they gave me community", "I had to pick up some litter"], "meaning": "When Chainz was 15 years old, he was incarcerated over a cocaine bust. Originally, his mom was arrested for his crime:\n\nThe first time was when I was fifteen, over cocaine. The house I was staying in had gotten busted and they locked my mom up instead of me, thinking it was hers. I was staying with my friend, trying to figure out how I was gonna get my mom out… But I was still going to school, and then the police came to my classroom to get me. They whole thing caused a scene. And the cycle started from there.\n\nHe was likely entered into the Juvenile Justice System, being under 18 and on his first offence. Part of rehabilitation involves community service, which can mean picking up litter.", "annotation_id": "10199305", "url": "https://genius.com/10199305", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Got the Billboard melodies Rap is somethin’ I do on the side Crossed over to the other side And I didn’t even have to die", "vault_lines": ["Got the Billboard melodies", "Rap is somethin' I do on the side", "Crossed over to the other side", "And I didn't even have to die"], "meaning": "Initially he was regarded as more of a rapper (though he’s always sung too), but here Drake suggests that he’s less focused on rap and more focused on crossover pop hits. Though he’s considered one of the best rappers of his generation, a lot of Drake’s success is due to his penchant for singing and providing melodies on his songs.\n\nHe’s a giant on the Billboard charts, with his first four records and both of his commercial mixtapes debuting at number 1 on the album charts.\n\nThe notion that he didn’t even have to die comes from the belief that deceased artists reach new levels of popularity once they’re gone. To “cross over” also means to bridge the gap between pop and hip-hop, appealing to both audiences, something Drake has achieved.\n\nDrizzy touched on a similar theme on 2015’s “Legend,” when he boasted that if he were to die, he’s already reached a legendary status in music.", "annotation_id": "10199304", "url": "https://genius.com/10199304", "state": "accepted", "votes": 103, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Dearly departed, gather today On some Prince shit", "vault_lines": ["Dearly departed, gather today", "On some Prince shit"], "meaning": "Here Chainz pays homage to Prince, who passed away on April 21, 2016. Chainz nods to one of Prince’s most famous tracks, his 1984 hit “Let’s Go Crazy,” which began with the monologue:\n\nDearly beloved,\nWe are gathered here today\nTo get through this thing called “Life”", "annotation_id": "10199272", "url": "https://genius.com/10199272", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Skip the rainbow, funny style shit And it’s straight to the pot of gold", "vault_lines": ["Skip the rainbow, funny style shit", "And it's straight to the pot of gold"], "meaning": "In Irish folklore, leprechauns hide a pot of gold at the end of a rainbow. Attaining this gold is an impossibility due to the visual trick a rainbow plays, so Drake eschews that narrative and goes straight for the gold himself.", "annotation_id": "10199259", "url": "https://genius.com/10199259", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Big Amount", "line": "Somebody, everybody know 6 God with the god flow Yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["6 God with the god flow"], "meaning": "“6 God” is a reference to Drake. “The 6” is a nickname for the city of Toronto coined by Toronto rapper Jimmy Prime. It is taken from the city’s main area codes of 416 and 647. Drake considers himself the god of Toronto, hence his nickname.", "annotation_id": "10199258", "url": "https://genius.com/10199258", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Not New To This", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/K-deep-not-new-to-this-lyrics", "genius_title": "Not New To This", "genius_id": 2332057, "source": "api"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "status": "done", "targets": 56, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-believe-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "Believe Me", "genius_id": 419570, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "When they jumped off the porch, I was stumblin’ up the steps", "vault_lines": ["When they jumped off the porch, I was stumblin' up the steps"], "meaning": "“Jumping off the porch” is a euphemism describing a person’s transition from innocent youth (and living with family) to hustling in the streets.  At the same time that people older than him (or his idols) started hustling, Wayne was “stumbling up the steps.”\n\nWayne’s “stumbling” suggests that he was a misguided adolescent, and he refers to “the steps” in both literal and metaphorical form.\n\nLiteral Steps:  Wayne is working his way to reach the same point as his predecessors, from where they jumped off the porch (started hustling), so that he can emulate them.  In other words, Wayne stumbled up the literal steps to the metaphorical porch that his predecessors “jumped off” of.\n\nMetaphorical Steps:  While others started hustling or living the street life (“When they jumped off the porch”), Wayne was aiming for something different and greater and, thus, took the route to reach that greater point (“I was stumbling up the steps”).  In other words, Wayne stumbled up the metaphorical steps to reach a great accomplishment.", "annotation_id": "37732984", "url": "https://genius.com/37732984", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Yayo get her ass whipped, whip that ass like horses racin’", "vault_lines": ["Yayo get her ass whipped, whip that ass like horses racin'"], "meaning": "“Yayo” is a slang term for cocaine. In this context, it refers to someone involved in illegal activities or a risky lifestyle. The phrase “get her ass whipped” suggests that this person is facing the consequences or effects of cocaine.\n\n“whip that ass like horses racin'”:\nLil Wayne uses vivid imagery to describe his sexual acts, comparing it to the act of whipping a horse during a race.", "annotation_id": "30914521", "url": "https://genius.com/30914521", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "They think I’m associated, I’m the one that orchestrated", "vault_lines": ["They think I'm associated, I'm the one that orchestrated"], "meaning": "This line underscores Lil Wayne’s position as a strategic thinker and leader within his endeavors, emphasizing his ability to orchestrate and oversee various projects. It reflects his confidence in his abilities and his willingness to take credit for his role in driving success.", "annotation_id": "30914501", "url": "https://genius.com/30914501", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Drop dead gorgeous but the bitch ain’t dyin’ for a nigga", "vault_lines": ["Drop dead gorgeous but the bitch ain't dyin' for a nigga"], "meaning": "“Drop dead gorgeous”: \nThis phrase describes the woman’s stunning physical appearance, suggesting that she is exceptionally beautiful and attractive.\n\n“but the bitch ain’t dyin' for a nigga”:\nDespite her beauty, the woman is not willing to devote herself entirely to a man or become emotionally dependent on him.", "annotation_id": "30914462", "url": "https://genius.com/30914462", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "All I gotta say is “sayonara, nigga”", "vault_lines": ["All I gotta say is \"sayonara, nigga\""], "meaning": "Lil Wayne exhibits a sense of assertiveness and self-assurance by using the Japanese term “sayonara,” which means “goodbye,”", "annotation_id": "30914433", "url": "https://genius.com/30914433", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Out of duct tape so when he prayin’ I ignore the nigga", "vault_lines": ["Out of duct tape so when he prayin' I ignore the nigga"], "meaning": "Duct tape is commonly used to bind or gag individuals, suggesting that Lil Wayne has run out of means to silence or ignore the person attempting to communicate with him.\n\nLil Wayne describes ignoring the person’s prayers or pleas for attention or assistance.", "annotation_id": "30914408", "url": "https://genius.com/30914408", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m the one they come to see because they all Believe me", "vault_lines": ["Believe me, believe me", "I'm the one they come to see because they all"], "meaning": "Drake asserts himself as the focal point, the main attraction, or the primary reason why people attend events or engage with his music. He suggests that the audience’s belief in him, his authenticity, and his talent is what draws them to him.", "annotation_id": "30914378", "url": "https://genius.com/30914378", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m the one they come to see because they all Believe me", "vault_lines": ["Believe me, believe me", "I'm the one they come to see because they all"], "meaning": "Drake asserts himself as the focal point, the main attraction, or the primary reason why people attend events or engage with his music. He suggests that the audience’s belief in him, his authenticity, and his talent is what draws them to him.", "annotation_id": "30914377", "url": "https://genius.com/30914377", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m not tryna find nobody else to beat", "vault_lines": ["I'm not tryna find nobody else to beat"], "meaning": "Drake implies that he’s not interested in looking for individuals to compete against or outdo.\n\nOverall, this line conveys a sense of contentment and confidence in Drake’s position. He’s not concerned with trying to prove himself or assert dominance over others. Instead, he seems focused on his own path and endeavors, rather than engaging in unnecessary competition.", "annotation_id": "30914375", "url": "https://genius.com/30914375", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m not tryna find nobody else to beat", "vault_lines": ["I'm not tryna find nobody else to beat"], "meaning": "Drake implies that he’s not interested in looking for individuals to compete against or outdo.\n\nOverall, this line conveys a sense of contentment and confidence in Drake’s position. He’s not concerned with trying to prove himself or assert dominance over others. Instead, he seems focused on his own path and endeavors, rather than engaging in unnecessary competition.", "annotation_id": "30914369", "url": "https://genius.com/30914369", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m the only one that get the job done I don’t know a nigga that could cover for me", "vault_lines": ["I'm the only one that get the job done", "I don't know a nigga that could cover for me", "One for me, one for my daughter, nigga", "I don't know a nigga that can cover for me"], "meaning": "Drake asserts himself as the primary force behind his success. He is confident in his abilities to accomplish tasks and achieve his goals.\n\nDrake suggests that there is no one else who can match his capabilities or step in to fulfill his responsibilities.", "annotation_id": "30914363", "url": "https://genius.com/30914363", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m that nigga, boy they love me in the streets", "vault_lines": ["I'm that nigga, boy they love me in the streets", "I'm the nigga, see me skatin' in the streets"], "meaning": "Drake acknowledges the admiration and support he receives from people in urban neighborhoods or street culture. This line suggests that his music resonates with a diverse audience, particularly those who identify with the urban lifestyle and its associated values.", "annotation_id": "30914360", "url": "https://genius.com/30914360", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m that nigga, boy they love me in the streets", "vault_lines": ["I'm that nigga, boy they love me in the streets", "I'm the nigga, see me skatin' in the streets"], "meaning": "Drake acknowledges the admiration and support he receives from people in urban neighborhoods or street culture. This line suggests that his music resonates with a diverse audience, particularly those who identify with the urban lifestyle and its associated values.", "annotation_id": "30914359", "url": "https://genius.com/30914359", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m the only one that get the job done I don’t know a nigga that could cover for me", "vault_lines": ["I'm the only one that get the job done", "I don't know a nigga that could cover for me", "One for me, one for my daughter, nigga", "I don't know a nigga that can cover for me"], "meaning": "Drake asserts himself as the primary force behind his success. He is confident in his abilities to accomplish tasks and achieve his goals.\n\nDrake suggests that there is no one else who can match his capabilities or step in to fulfill his responsibilities.", "annotation_id": "30914356", "url": "https://genius.com/30914356", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Doesn’t matter, could be winter or the summer", "vault_lines": ["Doesn't matter, could be winter or the summer"], "meaning": "Drake emphasizes that the time of year or external factors do not affect his ability to thrive in the music industry.", "annotation_id": "30914346", "url": "https://genius.com/30914346", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "If a nigga fuck with him, I’ll put him down quick", "vault_lines": ["If a nigga fuck with him, I'll put him down quick"], "meaning": "Drake expresses his willingness to defend Lil Wayne against any threats or adversaries. He asserts that he will act swiftly and decisively to protect Lil Wayne from harm or disrespect, demonstrating his loyalty and commitment to his friend.", "annotation_id": "30914327", "url": "https://genius.com/30914327", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Got a verse for anybody wanna talk about the clique", "vault_lines": ["Got a verse for anybody wanna talk about the clique"], "meaning": "The “clique” refers to Drake’s close circle of friends, collaborators, or associates within the music industry.\n\nDrake indicates that he has prepared lyrics or rhymes ready to address anyone who wishes to discuss or criticize his group, crew, or associates.", "annotation_id": "30914301", "url": "https://genius.com/30914301", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m the one they come to see because they all Believe me", "vault_lines": ["Believe me, believe me", "I'm the one they come to see because they all"], "meaning": "Drake asserts himself as the focal point, the main attraction, or the primary reason why people attend events or engage with his music. He suggests that the audience’s belief in him, his authenticity, and his talent is what draws them to him.", "annotation_id": "30914280", "url": "https://genius.com/30914280", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m not tryna find nobody else to beat", "vault_lines": ["I'm not tryna find nobody else to beat"], "meaning": "Drake implies that he’s not interested in looking for individuals to compete against or outdo.\n\nOverall, this line conveys a sense of contentment and confidence in Drake’s position. He’s not concerned with trying to prove himself or assert dominance over others. Instead, he seems focused on his own path and endeavors, rather than engaging in unnecessary competition.", "annotation_id": "30914255", "url": "https://genius.com/30914255", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m that nigga, boy they love me in the streets", "vault_lines": ["I'm that nigga, boy they love me in the streets", "I'm the nigga, see me skatin' in the streets"], "meaning": "Drake acknowledges the admiration and support he receives from people in urban neighborhoods or street culture. This line suggests that his music resonates with a diverse audience, particularly those who identify with the urban lifestyle and its associated values.", "annotation_id": "30914223", "url": "https://genius.com/30914223", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m the only one that get the job done I don’t know a nigga that could cover for me", "vault_lines": ["I'm the only one that get the job done", "I don't know a nigga that could cover for me", "One for me, one for my daughter, nigga", "I don't know a nigga that can cover for me"], "meaning": "Drake asserts himself as the primary force behind his success. He is confident in his abilities to accomplish tasks and achieve his goals.\n\nDrake suggests that there is no one else who can match his capabilities or step in to fulfill his responsibilities.", "annotation_id": "30914186", "url": "https://genius.com/30914186", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Believe me All you gotta do is pay me every week I had these bitches havin’ babies every week", "vault_lines": ["Believe me, believe me", "All you gotta do is pay me every week", "I had these bitches havin' babies every week"], "meaning": "Wayne continues with the previous lines by saying he will have women smuggle cocaine taped to their stomachs by pretending to be pregnant (“having babies”) every week as long as it is profitable (“all you gotta do is pay me every week)", "annotation_id": "21784367", "url": "https://genius.com/21784367", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Find out where you stay, and act like we found some oil, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Find out where you stay, and act like we found some oil, nigga"], "meaning": "When people find oil, they are very happy because of the wealth the oil will bring, so Wayne is saying he and his goons will celebrate when they find out where you live. Wayne also plays off the previous line about shell cases: SHELL is an oil company.", "annotation_id": "21784355", "url": "https://genius.com/21784355", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Rip rip, nigga", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A re-iteration of the first lines of each of Drake’s verses in this song\n\nIt’s been me and Young Tune off the rip\n\n­\n\nHad to get it poppin' off the rip\n\nSince this is the closing line to the song, though, it may have another meaning – R.I.P. to other rappers, especially those who talk about his clique.", "annotation_id": "3174011", "url": "https://genius.com/3174011", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m the only one that get the job done I don’t know a nigga that can cover for me", "vault_lines": ["I'm the only one that get the job done", "I don't know a nigga that could cover for me", "One for me, one for my daughter, nigga", "I don't know a nigga that can cover for me"], "meaning": "Wayne is the repeating the hook but the same goes for him.\n\nWayne has:\n- Won 4 Grammy’s\n- Over 15 million album sales.\n- Been signed to Cash Money since 12.\n- Worked with everyone from Biggie to Madonna.\n\nAnd so the list goes on…\n\n#ALL HAIL LIL TUNECHI", "annotation_id": "3156220", "url": "https://genius.com/3156220", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "[Produced by Boi-1da and Vinylz]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Boi-1da and Vinylz tell how they crafted the production to Lil Wayne’s “Believe Me”:\n\nCarrying a very minimalist sound compared to Wayne’s past works, “Believe Me” finds both Tunechi and Drizzy trading fiery lines. The song’s a testament to the streak the two artists garnered through the years, because of their dominant past collaborations.\n\n­\n\n“A lot of people complain about rappers these days sounding the same and using the same flows, but the producers are also responsible for that too because they’re making the same type of beats. So, it ends up making the rapper just wanna do the same sh-t. So every time we work, we just try something brand new,”", "annotation_id": "3155171", "url": "https://genius.com/3155171", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "It’d be a cold day in Hell, icicles made of sweat", "vault_lines": ["It'd be a cold day in Hell, icicles made of sweat"], "meaning": "Hell must be a pretty hot place since it is always envisioned with flames everywhere, so if you were down there you would probably be sweating. If there was a cold day in Hell however, all that sweat would freeze and form icicles.", "annotation_id": "3153551", "url": "https://genius.com/3153551", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Tape a couple kilos to the bitch stomach She look like she got a bun in the oven from me Preheat,", "vault_lines": ["Tape a couple kilos to the bitch stomach", "She look like she got a bun in the oven from me"], "meaning": "He uses play on words; using preheat because the bun in her oven (cocaine), is already made.\n\n“Bun in the oven” is a phrase used to describe a pregnant woman. This could refer to Wayne using his bitch to smuggle cocaine. Customs might not check pregnant women. Just last year, a Canadian woman was caught trying to smuggle 2 kg of cocaine from Columbia in a fake pregnancy suit – peep the video below or read more about it here.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HM-XSexZULY", "annotation_id": "3153426", "url": "https://genius.com/3153426", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Come on, fight these shell cases like a lawyer, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Come on, fight these shell cases like a lawyer, nigga"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne uses cases two ways, first in the literal sense shell cases. As referenced in the previous line, Wayne is holding a rifle.  Wayne wants you to accept his challenge and test him, ultimately you’ll be taking his bullets, trying to survive from your injuries.\n\n\n\nThe second meaning is a lawyer fights a court case. A lawyer’s job is to basically defend and fight for a person’s case in court. Lil Wayne is also alluding to a lawyer fighting specifically a gun charge, so you would be trying to pull through from your gun wounds the same way a lawyer would be trying to beat a gun possession charge.", "annotation_id": "3153363", "url": "https://genius.com/3153363", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Lord knows I’ll murk one of these niggas, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Lord knows I'll murk one of these niggas, yeah"], "meaning": "Wayne spit a very similar line in a verse on Rick Ross‘ song, “Thug Cry”:\n\nCan’t trust none of these niggas, I murk one of these niggas\nThen bury one of these niggas, still got dirt under my fingers…\n\nWe all know Weezy doesn’t play around with anyone fucking up his money or disrespecting him. ‘Murk’ is slang for killing.\n\n\n\nFun Fact:\n\n“Lord Knows” is actually a song by fellow Young Money artist, and feature guest on this track: Drake. “Lord Knows” also features the big homie mentioned earlier, Rick Ross.", "annotation_id": "3153243", "url": "https://genius.com/3153243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Mothafuck the rest", "vault_lines": ["Mothafuck award shows and mothafuck the press like that"], "meaning": "Wayne has already stated that he only rides for his niggas.\nAnd doesn’t care for the rest.\n\n\n\nIt could also be a reference to 2Pac’s second full verse after Snoop Dogg in 2 Of Amerikaz Most Wanted, like how this is the second verse after Drake, where Pac says:\n\nNow follow as we ride\nMotherfuck the rest, two of the best of the west side", "annotation_id": "3153236", "url": "https://genius.com/3153236", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Goin’ out to Houston, spendin’ all of my advance V Live just took me for a cheque “Drake you know I love you, you just took me outta debt”", "vault_lines": ["Goin' out to Houston, spendin' all of my advance", "V Live just took me for a cheque", "\"Drake you know I love you, you just took me outta debt\""], "meaning": "Drake has spent a lot of money having a good time in Houston, he’s been seen at V Live, a strip club in Houston, spending up to $100,000 in a night with his babe Rihanna, A$AP Rocky, Bun B, and more.\n\n\n\nThe stripper is thanking him for throwing around so much cash on her, enough to take away any debts she had.", "annotation_id": "3153232", "url": "https://genius.com/3153232", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m the one they tell yah been reppin’ in the 6", "vault_lines": ["I'm the one they tell yah been reppin' in the 6"], "meaning": "Toronto\n\nIf you ask anyone who’s been repping Toronto, the unanimous answer would be Drake.\n\n“The 6” is a popular nickname given to Toronto by Jimmy Johnson, a Toronto native. Toronto’s two main area codes are 416 and 647 hence the name “The 6”.", "annotation_id": "3153216", "url": "https://genius.com/3153216", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "He said she might say she love me She don’t love me like she say she love me Believe me, believe me", "vault_lines": ["He said she might say she love me", "She don't love me like she say she love me", "Believe me, believe me"], "meaning": "This may be a reference to another song that Weezy and Drizzy collaborated on, “Love Me”, even though Drake’s feelings about this girl are completely different than his feelings about this girl in “Love Me”.\n\nDrake’s girl may try to act like she is in love with him, but in reality, she is only in a relationship with him for Drake’s riches, or for sex. She might not be that good of a girl for him. Drizzy brought pretty much the same thing up in another track.\n\nYou ain’t even hit me when that nigga had threats for me, girl\nYou don’t love me, you just say that shit to get to me, girl\n\nTo sum it up, she’s a gold digger! (and she ain’t fuckin with no broke….)", "annotation_id": "3153210", "url": "https://genius.com/3153210", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Need a horn and a drumroll", "vault_lines": ["Need a horn and a drumroll"], "meaning": "Tunechi may be a humble king, but he needs his introduction like every other king!", "annotation_id": "3153208", "url": "https://genius.com/3153208", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Nirvana, Coldplay, nigga got bands", "vault_lines": ["Nirvana, Coldplay, nigga got bands"], "meaning": "Drake slays wordplay here: bands is slang for $1000 (also called racks or stacks). Bands also refers to a group of musicians – two notable ones being Nirvana and Coldplay.\n\nNotice how Drake grinds his voice a little bit at the beginning of the bar to reference the singing style of the late Kurt Cobain, Nirvana’s lead vocalist.", "annotation_id": "3153196", "url": "https://genius.com/3153196", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Yeah, got some game from my dad", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, got some game from my dad"], "meaning": "Damn, where I get it from? These niggas always wonder who\nThen they meet my pops and tell him, “Drake is just a younger you”\n\nOver the course of Drake’s upbringing, he’s gained a strong reputation with the ladies. His old man was the same way.\n\n\n\nNote: This line does NOT end with “days.” Listen to Lil Wayne rapping the hook during Floyd Mayweather’s ring intro for the Mayweather-Maidana fight.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uiIrN24c2Xg#t=24", "annotation_id": "3153192", "url": "https://genius.com/3153192", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "And I’ll been rockin’ with the team ’til Carter IX", "vault_lines": ["And I'll been rockin' with the team 'til Carter IX"], "meaning": "In this lyric, Drake is referring to his signing to Lil Wayne’s Young Money Entertainent (“the team”), which not so coincidentally took place in 2009.\n\nThis lyric also plays off the “Tha Carter” album series, as all of the album in the series are followed by a number (with the exception of “Tha Carter”).\n\nDrake is saying he will remain loyal to YMCMB until Carter IX drops and if Wayne sticks by his word, the Carter IX will never drop. AKA Drake will never leave his team.", "annotation_id": "3153164", "url": "https://genius.com/3153164", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "We can shoot it out and see who live to tell the story later", "vault_lines": ["We can shoot it out and see who live to tell the story later"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is daring you to a one-on-one shoot out, and the winner tells his war story to others because the other man died.\n\nContinuing on the previous line about the dangerous streets of Queens, this is also a reference to a story told in Prodigy’s book, where he details an incident in which N.O.R.E. was shooting at him and his crew.", "annotation_id": "3153159", "url": "https://genius.com/3153159", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Collect call from the boss like where we at though I was like, “Hah, it’s our time, nigga”", "vault_lines": ["Collect call from the boss like where we at though", "I was like, \"Hah, it's our time, nigga\""], "meaning": "While Lil' Wayne was locked up at Rikers Island, Drake had released his first studio album Thank Me Later which was a big success selling roughly 450k copies in its first week and had successful singles like Over, Miss Me and Find Your Love which kept Young Money in the mainstream, while Lil Wayne had also released I Am Not A Human Being during his 8 month sentence which had the chart topping single Right Above It featuring Drake", "annotation_id": "3153157", "url": "https://genius.com/3153157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Nigga I’ll fire this nina like it’s her first day On the job and the bitch overslept", "vault_lines": ["Nigga I'll fire this nina like it's her first day", "On the job and the bitch overslept"], "meaning": "Wayne “fires” his “nina” (slang for a 9-mm gun) just like someone would get “fired” from their job for being late on their first day if they “overslept”.", "annotation_id": "3153133", "url": "https://genius.com/3153133", "state": "accepted", "votes": 149, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Yeah, Stunna and Mack know When Wayne was gone for eight months, we put this thing up on our back And I was snappin’ off on every single track though", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, Stunna and Mack know", "When Wayne was gone for eight months, we put this thing up on our back", "And I was snappin' off on every single track though"], "meaning": "In 2010, Lil Wayne had an 8 month stint in jail. And during that time, Drake took over YMCMB, and he made sure to drop a hot verse on every single song during that time, in order to hold down YM and make sure they stayed relevant.\n\nStunna is Birdman, co-founder of Cash Money Records, and Mack is Mack Maine, president of Young Money Entertainment.\n\n\n\n\nDamn, I’ll be gone ‘til November, but fuck it I ain’t trippin’, I know Drizzy gonna kill ‘em", "annotation_id": "3153123", "url": "https://genius.com/3153123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’ve been takin’ shit light, you don’t wanna hear me trip God damn, do y’all really know who y’all fuckin’ with? Yeah, I mean you can’t blame me for wonderin’", "vault_lines": ["I've been takin' shit light, you don't wanna hear me trip", "God damn, do y'all really know who y'all fuckin' with?", "Yeah, I mean you can't blame me for wonderin'"], "meaning": "This is Drake responding to the disses and snarky comments he’s received as of late, most recently from Jay-Z. He hasn’t really taken great offense to the disses, but everyone has been begging for a serious Drake response. Drake says that they better hope he doesn’t respond because he’s the greatest, he’ll murder them on a track.\n\nDrake’s Sh!t (Remix) verse is suppose to be addressed to Kendrick Lamar, but only in why he hasn’t responded. It was taken by some as a diss track and Top Dawg tweeted saying if that’s all Drake’s got he feels sorry for him.\n\nThis is also a play on words. When people travel, some like to travel with small things (takin'shit light). He’s saying that he’s not really packing his verses as much, so he’s not exactly “tripping”. You haven’t heard him speak his mind yet, only the small (light) responses, but you’re not ready to hear him trip. (Full Response)", "annotation_id": "3153112", "url": "https://genius.com/3153112", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Waitin’ for someone to test me like a Harvard nigga", "vault_lines": ["Waitin' for someone to test me like a Harvard nigga"], "meaning": "Weezy is pissed off and is waiting for someone to “test” his anger.\n\nBeing the king of similes that he is, he puts a clever spin on the line by comparing that testing him is akin to challenging a Harvard student; both are confident in their abilities should they be.", "annotation_id": "3153110", "url": "https://genius.com/3153110", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Where the real queens at? Shout out Capone and Noreaga", "vault_lines": ["Where the real queens at? Shout out Capone and Noreaga"], "meaning": "Essentially – Wayne is looking for a top notch girl who’ll love him. The “Queen” in this instance serves a dual role – referring to a girl as well as Queens, NY.\n\n\n\nThe shouted out artists, Capone N Noreaga, made a song about Queens, their hometown, titled “Queens Finest.”\n\nWhile Capone and Weezy have never collaborated, N.O.R.E. and Weezy have – on songs such as “Finito”, “Play That Shit” and “She Tried.”", "annotation_id": "3153105", "url": "https://genius.com/3153105", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Diamonds in my Rollie face cannot be exfoliated", "vault_lines": ["Diamonds in my Rollie face cannot be exfoliated"], "meaning": "The diamonds are embedded into the face of the watch. The face of a watch is the part behind the dial. Exfoliating is a process where you remove the dead skin. A lot of people exfoliate their facial skin to clean their pores and get an glow-like effect. The diamonds can’t be removed from his Rolex.", "annotation_id": "3153100", "url": "https://genius.com/3153100", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "His and hers Ferraris, nigga One for me, one for my daughter, nigga", "vault_lines": ["His and hers Ferraris, nigga", "One for me, one for my daughter, nigga"], "meaning": "Reginae stuntin' like her daddy!\n\nWayne is playing off the phrase “his and hers”, which is generally used by couples to show co-possession over minor material goods or refers to items that they have bought twice, one for each partner…\n\n\n\nBut Wayne raises the bar on this one, literally. He’s talking about “Ferraris”, and before you think he’s talking about gifting it to one of his girl, he states that it’s for his 16-year old daughter!\n\nBy doing this, Lil Wayne exerts his morals for family over bitches.\n\n\n\nImagine having a Ferrari for a first car! Now that’s a “Super Sweet 16”.\n\n This is also an allusion to the former Young Money artist Curren$y’s track, “His & Her Ferraris”.", "annotation_id": "3153094", "url": "https://genius.com/3153094", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Yeah, rip, rip rip It’s been me and Young Tune off the rip", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, rip, rip rip", "It's been me and Young Tune off the rip"], "meaning": "Young Tune, one of many nicknames of the man more commonly known as Lil Wayne has been Drake’s mentor, like Birdman has been to Wayne. Wayne co-signed Drake before anyone knew who he was and was able to keep up the hype surrounding Drizzy.", "annotation_id": "3153092", "url": "https://genius.com/3153092", "state": "accepted", "votes": 95, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Come into the city and yah niggas get to trippin’ We’ll take yah to the Scarborough Bluff and drop you off a cliff", "vault_lines": ["Come into the city and yah niggas get to trippin'", "We'll take yah to the Scarborough Bluff and drop you off a cliff"], "meaning": "The Scarborough Bluffs, also known as The Bluffs, are an escarpment in the Scarborough district of Toronto, Ontario, Canada where Drake’s friend Chubbs was born.\n\n\n\nCoincidentally, there have been many deaths in this area.", "annotation_id": "3153091", "url": "https://genius.com/3153091", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "That’s the man that put me in this shit", "vault_lines": ["That's the man that put me in this shit"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne invited Drake out to Houston in 2008 and aided him with his breakthrough mixtape So Far Gone in early 2009. Since then Wayne signed him to his Young Money record label and Drake has gone on to release 3 successful albums under it, all of which have gone platinum. Not to mention Drake now has the most No. 1 rap songs of all time in his short career. Weezy definitely made a good decision putting him in this shit", "annotation_id": "3153084", "url": "https://genius.com/3153084", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "Slim Thug flow but you know I like ’em thick", "vault_lines": ["Slim Thug flow but you know I like 'em thick"], "meaning": "His flow is similar to Houston’s own Slim Thug, who was known for making a million before releasing an album. Thugga himself posted a video of this lyric on his Instagram.\n\nNote the contrast between slim and thick.", "annotation_id": "3153082", "url": "https://genius.com/3153082", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "And we YMCMB, waitin’ on somebody to try us, nigga Yeah", "vault_lines": ["And we YMCMB, waitin' on somebody to try us, nigga"], "meaning": "YMCMB obviously has some of the biggest names in the Hip Hop industry right now (primarily Wayne, Drake, Nicki and Tyga). They’re always on top of their game, both lyrically and commercially, and are simply waiting for some competition to come challenge their reign.\n\n\n\nNote the play on the last letter in YMCMB – dualing as “B” and “be”", "annotation_id": "3153075", "url": "https://genius.com/3153075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "He left Rikers in a Phantom, that’s my nigga", "vault_lines": ["He left Rikers in a Phantom, that's my nigga"], "meaning": "After Wayne was discharged from Riker’s Island in December 2010, he left the prison in a Rolls-Royce Phantom and headed straight to meet up with the rest of Cash Money.\n\nEven in their worst of times, YMCMB is always riding in style.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hKpnW6t_e-g", "annotation_id": "3153071", "url": "https://genius.com/3153071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "On the road, I do One Direction numbers, I don’t fuckin’ miss", "vault_lines": ["On the road, I do One Direction numbers, I don't fuckin' miss", "I don't step on one rose"], "meaning": "Drake was also an international teen star.\n\nWhile Drake’s concerts have been highly successful, none have made numbers quite near those from the tween sensation pop boy-band, One Direction. The group scored $52,582,225 for their 2013 Take Me Home Tour, while Drake made 42.6 million with his Club Paradise tour in 2012.\n\n\n\nHowever, the overall total means little because One Direction are probably performing higher numbers of shows than Drake. As you can see here. Drake actually gets more per show than One Direction, because he is doing it by himself.\n\nNote that there is some wordplay with One Direction. Drake’s career and numbers are going towards one direction – up.\n\nIt’s reminiscent of a lyric in “Worst Behaviour”:\n\nWho else making rap albums, doing numbers like it’s pop?", "annotation_id": "3153064", "url": "https://genius.com/3153064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 101, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I tote a 223, two Michael Jordans, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I tote a 223, two Michael Jordans, nigga"], "meaning": "Truly genius punchline here. Weezy always carries weapons on him, not just handguns, but assault rifles. A .223 is actually referring to the size of ammunition a rifle takes, not a specific rifle.\n\n\n\nOf course being the sports fan that Tunechi is, he relates this to basketball, playing on legendary shooting (get it?!) guard Michael Jordan’s jersey number: 23.", "annotation_id": "3153060", "url": "https://genius.com/3153060", "state": "accepted", "votes": 101, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Believe Me", "line": "I’m the nigga, see me skatin’ in the streets", "vault_lines": ["I'm that nigga, boy they love me in the streets", "I'm the nigga, see me skatin' in the streets"], "meaning": "We see you, Wayne.\n\n\n\nAll jokes aside, Wayne is passionate about skateboarding—he’s frequently seen skating and his clothing line, TRUKFIT is inspired by skate culture.", "annotation_id": "3153057", "url": "https://genius.com/3153057", "state": "accepted", "votes": 335, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-diced-pineapples-lyrics", "genius_title": "Diced Pineapples", "genius_id": 80557, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Tonight you shall reach a height that the sky won’t catch you The highest form of my admiration", "vault_lines": ["Tonight you shall reach a height that the sky won't catch you", "The highest form of my admiration"], "meaning": "It’s a poetic way of saying your feelings are extremely deep and uplifting—like your admiration will push someone to their highest potential or emotional “peak,” beyond limits or expectations.", "annotation_id": "39474127", "url": "https://genius.com/39474127", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Rushing you out your drawers,", "vault_lines": ["Rushing you out your drawers,"], "meaning": "It means someone is feeling intense physical attraction and things are moving fast between them, to the point where passion is overwhelming and impulsive.", "annotation_id": "39474118", "url": "https://genius.com/39474118", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "May your love come down so my mind might have you", "vault_lines": ["May your love come down so my mind might have you"], "meaning": "Wale cleverly implores his lover to perform oral sex on him with a pun on the head of his penis. If she brings her love down to his “head,” his “mind” will enjoy her presence.\n\nSimultaneously, this also reads as a heartfelt desire on Wale’s part to get to know the object of his affections.", "annotation_id": "38762589", "url": "https://genius.com/38762589", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Baby listen, this position is a blessing And with your permission, hopefully you’ll learn a lesson (ugh)", "vault_lines": ["Baby listen, this position is a blessing", "And with your permission, hopefully you'll learn a lesson (ugh)"], "meaning": "Rick Ross expresses an intention to show his lover the high life and to please her sexually–but only with her consent.\n\nA surprising and refreshing sentiment from Ricky Rozay, particularly considering his infamous promotion of drugging and raping women on the track “UOENO:”\n\n\nPut Molly all in her champagne, she ain’t even know it I took her home and I enjoyed that, she ain't even know it― Rocko (Ft. Future & Rick Ross) – U.O.E.N.O", "annotation_id": "38762455", "url": "https://genius.com/38762455", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Sex all night, couple shots of CÎROC", "vault_lines": ["Sex all night, couple shots of CÎROC"], "meaning": "Rick Ross engages in some forthright product placement for CÎROC, a premium vodka brand formerly associated with Sean “Diddy” Combs of later notoriety.\n\nThe music video prominently centers a bottle of peach CÎROC in the shot when Ross raps this lyric.", "annotation_id": "38762337", "url": "https://genius.com/38762337", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Like to go deep, but I hate to get too deeply involved", "vault_lines": ["Like to go deep, but I hate to get too deeply involved"], "meaning": "Wale is not here for the long haul. He is only here to please his girl and leave–or, more charitably, to be solely a source of pleasure for her as opposed to emotionally challenging.", "annotation_id": "38718797", "url": "https://genius.com/38718797", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "To get on a higher tree, gonna have to climb a sequoia", "vault_lines": ["To get on a higher tree, gonna have to climb a sequoia"], "meaning": "Wale’s clever double entendre alludes to both his ability to provide high-grade weed for his lover and his sexual prowess.\n\nHe can provide a “higher tree–” that is, better weed–but his lover will have to “climb a sequoia” to earn this. Sequoias are some of the largest and tallest trees around, and Wale is likening his penis to one.", "annotation_id": "38718767", "url": "https://genius.com/38718767", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "She so fine, she ain’t even gotta talk", "vault_lines": ["She so fine, she ain't even gotta talk"], "meaning": "Ross' lover is so attractive that she need not have any personality or way with words to win him over. Her presence and attractiveness are enough to enshrine her as his “diced pineapple” that gets him through the day.", "annotation_id": "38718761", "url": "https://genius.com/38718761", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Diced pineapples, I just bought my girl a set", "vault_lines": ["Diced pineapples, I just bought my girl a set"], "meaning": "Not only is Ross articulating the track’s central conceit–a lover as “diced pineapples,” a daily pleasure who supports him through hard times–but he is also making a pun on the appearance of diced pineapples. Yellow diamonds are also cubic in shape, and he’s just bought his girl some of them.", "annotation_id": "38718752", "url": "https://genius.com/38718752", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Then we fuck all night ’til things get right Then we fuck all night ’til things get right, aww yeah", "vault_lines": ["Then we fuck all night 'til things get right", "Then we fuck all night 'til things get right, aww yeah"], "meaning": "Drake and his lover have makeup sex after a heated argument. While an understandable urge due to passions running high–positive and negative–this is probably not healthy for the relationship. Drake hints at this through the refrain, generally.", "annotation_id": "38718743", "url": "https://genius.com/38718743", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Bitch so bad, got me wishing I could sing", "vault_lines": ["Bitch so bad, got me wishing I could sing"], "meaning": "Rick Ross' lover is so appealing that he wishes he was not a rapper, but a singer. He is likely alluding to the popular image of R&B singers as more sexually daring and explicit, or at least more forthright in signalling interest in women, than rappers in their lyrics.", "annotation_id": "38718732", "url": "https://genius.com/38718732", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "I love to make her toes curl as I’m lickin’ on her flesh (ugh)", "vault_lines": ["I love to make her toes curl as I'm lickin' on her flesh (ugh)"], "meaning": "B", "annotation_id": "29204361", "url": "https://genius.com/29204361", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "She never wrote a song, but I know that she’s a star", "vault_lines": ["She never wrote a song, but I know that she's a star"], "meaning": "Alluding to people with fame in a different area i.e. sex workers.\n\nThe woman he’s talking is a pornstar, hence “she’s a star\"", "annotation_id": "23894480", "url": "https://genius.com/23894480", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Her uniform Isabel Marant, when you on the team", "vault_lines": ["Her uniform Isabel Marant, when you on the team"], "meaning": "Isabel Marant is a women’s designer brand made by French designer, Isabel Marant. Ross alludes to the fact that whoever is with him, will dress in the best clothes that money can buy, as a uniform is an outfit someone is obliged to wear when they are part of a group.\n\n\n\nPictured: Isabel Marant", "annotation_id": "22366294", "url": "https://genius.com/22366294", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Double MG", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Maybach Music Group, or its initials MMG (Double M G), is the music label Rick Ross founded.", "annotation_id": "19774835", "url": "https://genius.com/19774835", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Pop bottles, make love, Thug Passion", "vault_lines": ["Pop bottles, make love, Thug Passion"], "meaning": "Thug Passion is a mixture of Alize and Cristal. The drink was popularized by the 1996 song “Thug Passion” by West Coast legend 2Pac.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mcjL8w7p1Xc", "annotation_id": "1703562", "url": "https://genius.com/1703562", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Hate when they be too anxious though, hate when they be too dull", "vault_lines": ["Hate when they be too anxious though, hate when they be too dull"], "meaning": "Wale wants a balanced passionate lover—not one that is too dull & lazy, but not a sex crazy slut either.\n\nAlso, note the brilliant use of wordplay: Wale was continuing with the pineapple references by [possibly] saying “hate when they be too Dole” which sounds like dull.\n\nDole Food Company Inc is an American-based agricultural multinational corporation headquartered in Westlake Village, California. Dole markets such food items as bananas, pineapples (what they are perhaps most famous for), grapes, strawberries, salads, and other fresh and frozen fruits and juices.\n\nThis interpretation would make sense because there is indeed better diced pineapples out there then those from Dole.", "annotation_id": "1120353", "url": "https://genius.com/1120353", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "When I peek into your nature And I promise you, my goals will exceed any physical pleasure", "vault_lines": ["When I peek into your nature", "And I promise you, my goals will exceed any physical pleasure"], "meaning": "“Peek” is a double entendre as its playing off the previous line “I can make you reach things unobtainable.” Wale is saying he can elevate her to the peak of her nature (i.e highest point her life) thus allowing to attain certain things she once deemed unreachable.\n\nFurthermore peek is also referring to sex—“peek into her nature.”  Wale is saying that when he gets inside her he is going to make her feel an emotional or spiritual connection.", "annotation_id": "1042193", "url": "https://genius.com/1042193", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Diced pineapples, talking diamonds by the jar", "vault_lines": ["Diced pineapples, talking diamonds by the jar"], "meaning": "JAR is a very high-end French jeweler.\n\nAlso could be Ross calling his yellow diamonds, diced pineapples.", "annotation_id": "1042145", "url": "https://genius.com/1042145", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Diced pineapple", "vault_lines": ["Shorty get it together, hah, diced pineapple", "Diced pineapples, damn, my baby tastes the best", "Diced pineapples, talking diamonds by the jar", "Diced pineapples, I just bought my girl a set"], "meaning": "When I got out of the hospital — you know, I had a seizure last year — when I was leaving, the doctor told me, ‘You gotta eat some more fruit, drink you some water, eat fruit and just relax for a little while.’ My fruit of choice was pineapples.\n\nFor the next three weeks, I woke up every morning and ate diced pineapples, and I put the concept together,“ he continued. \"Drizzy came in, as well as Wale, and it’s kinda like, ‘She could be my diced pineapple. This special lady, she could be what I wake up to every morning and help me get by every day.\n\n\n-Rick Ross", "annotation_id": "1004944", "url": "https://genius.com/1004944", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Hol’ up, showing off some Agent Provocateur", "vault_lines": ["Hol' up, showing off some Agent Provocateur"], "meaning": "Agent provocateur could either refer to a:\n\n\nBritish lingerie retailer, or;\nSpecial kind of agent that is an agent employed by the police or other entity to act undercover to entice or provoke another person to commit an illegal act.", "annotation_id": "940505", "url": "https://genius.com/940505", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Let me redefine foreplay ’til you need five and", "vault_lines": ["Let me redefine foreplay 'til you need five and"], "meaning": "Wale’s so good at foreplay (“four”-play), she’s gonna need a five minute break because she can’t handle his sex game. .\n\nYou can also take it as “five” meaning, one more level higher than four (“four”-play). He is gonna make foreplay so good that she is going to want “fiveplay.” .", "annotation_id": "935217", "url": "https://genius.com/935217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "I ain’t no connoisseur but I’m, kinda sure you will admire my taste", "vault_lines": ["I ain't no connoisseur but I'm, kinda sure you will admire my taste"], "meaning": "Connoisseurs are, so to speak, professional tasters. Wale may not be a professional taster but he is sure that she will like his taste in cars, clothes, jewelry, fine dining, etc. Could also refer to the fact she would love it when Wale eats her.\n\nThe sexual pun here is pretty obvious—Wale’s “taste”, i.e. the taste of his semen.", "annotation_id": "934735", "url": "https://genius.com/934735", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "And I won’t ever rest, we meet at the peak of your mountain", "vault_lines": ["And I won't ever rest, we meet at the peak of your mountain"], "meaning": "Wale puns here on the name of perhaps the world’s most famous mountain, Mount Everest.\n\nWale is talking about him embarking tirelessly on the task of ensuring that they both reach their climax together.", "annotation_id": "933933", "url": "https://genius.com/933933", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "Crib on the water, got LeBron up the block", "vault_lines": ["Crib on the water, got LeBron up the block"], "meaning": "This line shows how wealthy Rick Ross is, as he lived near LeBron since they both lived in Miami. It continues the basketball metaphor with “shots” from the previous line, because block could also be referring to the basketball statistic:\n\n\n\nLeBron & Ross are friends.", "annotation_id": "931573", "url": "https://genius.com/931573", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Diced Pineapples", "line": "I’ma trip, go to Cannes, France to catch a flick", "vault_lines": ["I'ma trip, go to Cannes, France to catch a flick"], "meaning": "Cannes, France is known for its incredibly prestigious film festival. The decadence of flying all the way to the Riviera just to take in a single film speaks to both Rick’s immense wealth and his familiarity with the highbrow world of art cinema.", "annotation_id": "931327", "url": "https://genius.com/931327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 24, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/The-weeknd-tell-your-friends-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "genius_id": 2297763, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "They told me not to fall in love, that shit is pointless Yeah, that shit is pointless They told me not to fall in love, that shit is pointless Yeah, that shit is pointless It’s only meant to fall in love That shit is pointless", "vault_lines": ["They told me not to fall in love, that shit is pointless", "Yeah, that shit is pointless", "It's only meant to fall in love", "That shit is pointless"], "meaning": "In the past, Abel has had to warn girls not to fall in love with him, and halted those who’ve rushed him into sex. He’s now being warned about the very same thing, as he won’t find love, not even in the sky; especially if he continues to cheat on girls he claims to love and sleep with girls that are already in relationships. Considering his inability to stay committed, he’s better off being acquaintances instead of partners with women.\n\nThis reluctant pursuit of romantic companionship is a prominent theme throughout this project. The initial lines of the album has Abel admitting he wasn’t made for love. His mother even chimes in giving detail to why he’ll never find long-term happiness with a woman.", "annotation_id": "7831600", "url": "https://genius.com/7831600", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "Used to hate attention, now I pull up in that wagon I was broken, I was broken, I was so broke I used to roam around the town when I was homeless", "vault_lines": ["Used to hate attention, now I pull up in that wagon", "I was broken, I was broken, I was so broke", "I used to roam around the town when I was homeless"], "meaning": "In 2010-2011, Abel was only known as ‘The Weeknd’. There was no article, Wikipedia page, Twitter, Instagram, iTunes section, or anything that could identify this mysterious singer further than his stage name; Abel liked it that way. As his 3 YouTube-released tracks became bigger and more popular, his bio finally broke to the Internet.\n\nAbel shares a similar message in the song “In Vein” where he sings: “Can’t nobody stop me, used to be homeless / Now that penthouse at the Ritz where my home is.” Abel left home to pursue music and convinced XO member La Mar C. Taylor to do the same.", "annotation_id": "7831595", "url": "https://genius.com/7831595", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "Used to roam on Queen, now I sing Queen street anthems", "vault_lines": ["Used to roam on Queen, now I sing Queen street anthems"], "meaning": "The Weeknd used to roam Queen Street in Toronto when he had no home to go to. Yet now he sings about his life for us all to consume, and always makes sure we know that he cares about the town that raised him.", "annotation_id": "7831594", "url": "https://genius.com/7831594", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "My cousin said I made it big and it’s unusual She tried to take a selfie at my Grandma’s funeral", "vault_lines": ["My cousin said I made it big and it's unusual", "She tried to take a selfie at my Grandma's funeral"], "meaning": "Abel started off releasing free mixtapes such as House of Balloons, which received acclaim from media critics across the web—now he’s making tracks with Kanye West—he’s come a long way.\n\nNow that Abel is famous, he’s the center of attention, especially back home in Toronto. His fame even changes the dynamic at his own grandmother’s funeral – someone he was very close to; she helped raise him. Friends and family are so intrigued and overwhelmed by his fame, they’re inadvertently becoming impolite in delicate situations, just to capture their celebrity encounter.", "annotation_id": "7831593", "url": "https://genius.com/7831593", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "Driving by the streets I used to walk through When I had no crib I guess you call that shit a miracle", "vault_lines": ["Driving by the streets I used to walk through", "When I had no crib I guess you call that shit a miracle"], "meaning": "Similar to Abel’s line in “King of the Fall”:\n\nDriving by the streets we used to walk through like a triumph.\n\nThe Weeknd compares his old days of walking with his current success. Abel is originally from the East End of Toronto, but the West End is where the hip bars, clubs, and restaurants are. It’s also where Abel lived when he was making his first three mixtapes.", "annotation_id": "7831591", "url": "https://genius.com/7831591", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "And I miss my city man it’s been a minute M.I.A. a habit, Cali was the mission Cruise through the west-end in my new Benz I’m just tryna live life through a new lens", "vault_lines": ["I'm a villain in my city, I just made another killing", "And I miss my city man it's been a minute", "M.I.A. a habit, Cali was the mission", "Cruise through the west-end in my new Benz", "I'm just tryna live life through a new lens"], "meaning": "Abel is originally from Toronto’s Scarborough district, a place nothing like California. Back on House of Balloons, Abel said “Cali is the mission,” now he’s made it and the mission is past tense. He’s made a habit of spending time in Miami too. M.I.A. is of course also a military term that stands for “missing in action\"—there are always mixed feelings with our Abel!\n\nWhile The Weeknd first intended to become famous out West, he’s now determined to go worldwide. Since Abel’s made it, he can take a ride in his expensive G-Class with the potential to be the world’s biggest Pop/R&B star.", "annotation_id": "7831590", "url": "https://genius.com/7831590", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "I’mma spend it all on bitches And everybody fuckin, everybody fucking Pussy on the house, everybody fucking", "vault_lines": ["I'mma spend it all on bitches", "And everybody fuckin, everybody fucking", "Pussy on the house, everybody fucking"], "meaning": "The Weeknd keeps playing with his persona, hitting every note we’ve come to love him for. He insists he is going to spend every single dollar on his self-destructive lifestyle, especially drugs and women. As he’s said before on “The Fall”:\n\nI got show money baby\nI wanna show you how I blow money baby\nI wanna show you how I throw money baby\nI’m a kid, so it’s hard for me to hold money, baby", "annotation_id": "7831589", "url": "https://genius.com/7831589", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "I’m a villain in my city, I just made another killing", "vault_lines": ["I'm a villain in my city, I just made another killing"], "meaning": "The Weeknd likens the release of Beauty Behind the Madness to a villain making his latest kill.\n\nHe knows his past projects Trilogy and Kiss Land were killers too, and reminds everyone that this isn’t his first piece of action.\n\nThis could also be taken from a different perspective. Both the 6 God, Drake and Abel hail from Toronto. Currently Drake reigns in his kingdom of the 6 and his OVO crew is taking the city over. By not taking up on Drake’s offer to sign to OVO, The Weeknd appears as a villain to his city.", "annotation_id": "7831588", "url": "https://genius.com/7831588", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "I think these hoes deserve another fixing I’m talking about the ones from the beginning Don’t believe the rumours bitch, I’m still a user I’m still rocking camo and still roll with shooters", "vault_lines": ["I think these hoes deserve another fixing", "I'm talking about the ones from the beginning", "Don't believe the rumours bitch, I'm still a user", "I'm still rocking camo and still roll with shooters"], "meaning": "Abel gives his girls a “fix” of what they want—he assures us that even with his evolving sound, he can still deliver for the fans who’ve been addicted to his music since Trilogy.\n\nIn connection to the hook, The Weeknd explains that his fashion sense and lifestyle haven’t changed as much as we would expect of a rising star. He still does drugs, and he’s still the mysterious figure who likes camouflage print.", "annotation_id": "7831587", "url": "https://genius.com/7831587", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "Last year I did all the politicin’ This year I’m all focused on the vision", "vault_lines": ["Last year I did all the politicin'", "This year I'm all focused on the vision"], "meaning": "Last year consisted of very little material from The Weeknd, with exceptions such as Ariana Grande’s “Love Me Harder” which helped bring him to mainstream listeners. This year using all the fame he generated through the “politics” from 2014, Abel pushes forth his vision, the third chapter to his saga, Beauty Behind the Madness.", "annotation_id": "7831584", "url": "https://genius.com/7831584", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "Go tell your friends about it (About it) Go tell your friends about it (About it) Go tell them what you know, what you seen How I roll, how I did it on the low Go tell your friends about it (About it) Go tell your friends about it (About it) Go tell them what you know, what you seen How I roll, how I be off that coke Go tell your friends about it (About it) Go tell your friends about it I’m that nigga with the hair Singin’ ‘bout poppin’ pills, fuckin’ bitches, livin’ life so trill", "vault_lines": ["Tell her friends about it", "Go tell your friends about it (About it)", "Go tell them what you know, what you seen", "How I roll, how I did it on the low", "How I roll, how I be off that coke", "I'm that nigga with the hair", "Singin' 'bout poppin' pills, fuckin' bitches, livin' life so trill", "How I roll, how I be on the low", "Singing 'bout popping pills, fucking bitches, living life so trill"], "meaning": "The Weeknd calls on his listeners to spread the word about him—he wants us to tell our friends about the drugs, the sex, the hair and of course, the music. In the past, Abel was one to keep things on the low-low, but his quest to become the biggest star in the world means giving up some of the mystery. What’s more, he’s making apostles of us all.", "annotation_id": "7831575", "url": "https://genius.com/7831575", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "And some dope dimes on some coke lines Give me head all night, cum four times Baby girl just wanna smoke a pound Do an ounce, get some dick Tell her friends about it", "vault_lines": ["And some dope dimes on some coke lines", "Give me head all night, cum four times", "Baby girl just wanna smoke a pound", "Do an ounce, get some dick", "Tell her friends about it", "Go tell your friends about it (About it)"], "meaning": "The Weeknd ends thrilling nights by doing lines of coke, having sex and smoking weed. These activities are all par for the course with Abel, and now he’s got a girl who’s to get down with him (and tell her friends).\n\nAll my hoes are trained, I make all of them swallow", "annotation_id": "7831568", "url": "https://genius.com/7831568", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "I don’t drink my liquor with a chasin’ And money is the only thing I’m chasin’", "vault_lines": ["I don't drink my liquor with a chasin'", "And money is the only thing I'm chasin'"], "meaning": "Even back on Trilogy, Abel was always team no chaser—he chases after the money instead.\n\n\n\nA “chaser” is usually a non-alcoholic drink that’s consumed directly after drinking hard liquor.", "annotation_id": "7831567", "url": "https://genius.com/7831567", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "And everybody around you is so basic I’m never rocking white, I’m like a racist", "vault_lines": ["And everybody around you is so basic", "I'm never rocking white, I'm like a racist"], "meaning": "The Weeknd is known for “rocking” clothes with darker colors and camo prints – never white. His disclusion of that color is similar to way a racist would segregate a certain color.\n\n A few weeks before releasing this song, he was interviewed by GQ Magazine and commented on his aesthetic,\n\nThe camo, the army look, the black boots, the nonchalant kind of vagrant look.", "annotation_id": "7831561", "url": "https://genius.com/7831561", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "XO niggas ain’t nothing to mess with Nobody stopping us, oh no we’re destined", "vault_lines": ["XO niggas ain't nothing to mess with", "Nobody stopping us, oh no we're destined"], "meaning": "XO is Abel’s label and crew, many of whom are still trapping and committing crimes. While he was arrested for knocking down a cop in Las Vegas earlier in 2015, he usually lets his crew handle those situations. As he sung on “Might Not”,\n\nNot really the type to let a nigga talk back but\nI'mma let it slide cause my niggas too violent", "annotation_id": "7831540", "url": "https://genius.com/7831540", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "These niggas they been doing too much flexing And they’re about to call the wrong attention And I ain’t got no patience, no more testing I do shit how I want, don’t need no blessing", "vault_lines": ["These niggas they been doing too much flexing", "And they're about to call the wrong attention", "And I ain't got no patience, no more testing", "I do shit how I want, don't need no blessing"], "meaning": "While his peers hide their emptiness (or lack of talent) behind big watches and flashy jewelry, Abel is ready to call his own shots. After hearing this project, they’ll realize just how different he is.\n\nSome listeners thought this may be a shot at Drake, since he “blessed” him with a start in 2011, but this seems unlikely. Even though the pair traded jabs with tracks like “5am In Toronto” – The Weeknd was quoted in Complex Magazine in 2013 stating the two never had beef:\n\nNo, that’s not true. Definitely not true. But it makes sense. The thing about Drake is I told him what my decisions were going to be. And he was down with it from the beginning.\n\nTo put a nail in the coffin of those rumors, Drake dropped a remix to this very song – possible OVOXO revival?", "annotation_id": "7831536", "url": "https://genius.com/7831536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "We are not the same I am too reckless I’m not tryna go in that direction", "vault_lines": ["We are not the same I am too reckless", "I'm not tryna go in that direction"], "meaning": "Abel borrows a line from Lil Wayne, who famously said “We are not the same I am a Martian.” Like Wayne, The Weeknd sets himself apart from everyone else around—he isn’t from another planet, but he takes real risks on this one. The second line is more ambiguous—is Abel scared of where his own recklessness might take him, or of taking the safe path like everyone else?", "annotation_id": "7831535", "url": "https://genius.com/7831535", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "Been thinking ’bout home actually Where Roxx has Kim and Mark has Ashley and Niks has Ezee And I, I don’t even know what I have anymore Evolving Evolving Evolving Evolving", "vault_lines": ["Been thinking 'bout home actually", "Where Roxx has Kim and Mark has Ashley and Niks has Ezee", "And I, I don’t even know what I have anymore"], "meaning": "In similar fashion to The Weeknd’s line off the original where he sings:\n\nAnd I miss my city man, it’s been a minute\n\nThe 6 God reminisces about missing his kingdom, the 6 itself, Toronto.\n\nHe mentions how his OVO boys Jonny Roxx and Mark both have girls (whom he calls out by name) that they can fall back to, and Niko has his son – but Drake isn’t sure of who he has to trust, he’s still evolving and coming to grips with life ever since he left the city.", "annotation_id": "7749075", "url": "https://genius.com/7749075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "Yeah, the ball is in your court No defense, nobody’s keeping score", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, the ball is in your court", "No defense, nobody's keeping score"], "meaning": "Drake and tennis star Serena Williams have had an extensive history, dating back to 2011. Mere days before this song was released, the couple were seen making out in a Cincinnati restaurant.\n\nDrizzy incorporates a tennis reference to convey that he wants to be with Serena, however, now it is her turn to reveal her feelings – he wants to know if she loves him, or if he’s losing.", "annotation_id": "7748592", "url": "https://genius.com/7748592", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "But you’re too famous to buy you purses That barely scratches the surface", "vault_lines": ["But you're too famous to buy you purses", "That barely scratches the surface"], "meaning": "The go-to method for seducing a woman in hip hop is to lace her with expensive goods.\n\nBut what happens when the girl you’re dating is more famous than you? Serena Williams is worth a frankly staggering $140 million, almost double Drake’s worth. She should be buying him purses…\n\n\n\nThis is merely the top layer of a complex dynamic that will now play out between the two. Both of them are in the stratospheric heights of stratospheric fields, so their relationship will be something to behold.", "annotation_id": "7748572", "url": "https://genius.com/7748572", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "This time is different, you give me purpose Tell me you taking it serious, I gotta ask how serious? ’Cause your serious has just been not that serious Got me on tour acting like I’m not that curious Ignoring all the pussy that’s thrown at me", "vault_lines": ["This time is different, you give me purpose", "Tell me you taking it serious, I gotta ask how serious?", "'Cause your serious has just been not that serious", "Got me on tour acting like I'm not that curious", "Ignoring all the pussy that's thrown at me", "How I roll, how I did it on the low", "This year I'm all focused on the vision"], "meaning": "Girls have often questioned Drake’s ability in taking relationships seriously, as his music often contains a lot of references to different women.\n\nEven as early as So Far Gone, Drizzy’s had a lot of women in his life that have even shared their story with the world. However, this woman has attained his full attention and he no longer desires these one off engagements.\n\nDrake wonders whether this girl will properly commit to something meaningful with him. He’s looking for a definitive answer, he doesn’t want to be her trophy.", "annotation_id": "7748523", "url": "https://genius.com/7748523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "No offense but I’ve played this before Maybe you can and you just don’t care anymore Evolving Evolving Evolving Yeah And I’m right back here when I said I wouldn’t You were never a good girl but you’re a better woman now Evolving Evolving Evolving, evolving", "vault_lines": ["You were never a good girl but you're a better woman now", "No offense but I've played this before", "Maybe you can and you just don't care anymore", "And I’m right back here when I said I wouldn't"], "meaning": "Drake’s a veteran when it comes to “playing the field,” yet even this girl (likely to be Serena Williams) has him questioning their relationship. Is she seeing other people? Maybe she can be with him, but she just doesn’t want to? Drake wants a “woman” as successful as he is, rather than any girl he can get on tour.\n\nThe rumours of their relationship started swirling as far back as 2011, when Drake tweeted:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/98932269770747906\n\nIn 2012, Drake was involved in a feud with fellow rapper Common when he dissed Drake on “Sweet” & “Stay Schemin' (Remix).” Common and Serena had previously dated. Drizzy then went on to name drop her on his 2012 hit, “Worst Behaviour.”\n\nAfter some back and fourth, it seems the pair have “evolved” and are back on in 2015. They were spotted having dinner together just weeks before this song was released.", "annotation_id": "7748398", "url": "https://genius.com/7748398", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "Yeah, live from the Waldorf in Berlin Coming at you once again With stories of truth and stories of sin", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, live from the Waldorf in Berlin", "Coming at you once again", "With stories of truth and stories of sin"], "meaning": "Waldorf Astoria is part of the famous Hilton hotel group and is one of Germany’s most prominent luxury hotels. It’s a go-to destination in Berlin for anyone who’s wealthy.\n\n\n\nDrake was last in Germany during his 2014 tour. At the time he may have been seeing Rihanna, so some fans have indicated the verse was originally penned for her. Looking at the lyrics and timing though, it seems clear the song is in reference to Serena Williams.", "annotation_id": "7748378", "url": "https://genius.com/7748378", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Your Friends (Remix)", "line": "Look, you remind me of Jane Birkin, your body insane in person Wanna do special things for you, you deserve it", "vault_lines": ["Look, you remind me of Jane Birkin, your body insane in person", "Wanna do special things for you, you deserve it"], "meaning": "Jane Birkin  is an English actress and singer from London, England who was well known for being the wife of the French singer songwriter, Serge Gainsbourg. Many saw her as an icon for redefining fashion in the 70s by showcasing her style in many photoshoots. Birkin was often described as stunning, beautiful, and perfect like the woman Drizzy croons about.", "annotation_id": "7748370", "url": "https://genius.com/7748370", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 33, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-bb-king-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "B.B. King Freestyle", "genius_id": 6209991, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "I fuck your bitch and she die in my arms", "vault_lines": ["Higher than yours, I fuck your bitch and she die in my arms"], "meaning": "“She die in my arms” means that she had an orgasm. It is actually a translation of a French idiom, according to the band Cutting Crew, who famously used this phrase in their ‘80s hit “I Just Died in Your Arms Tonight.”", "annotation_id": "38438502", "url": "https://genius.com/38438502", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "First private plane I ever rode was a eight-seater Before that, I got the bedroom hot with a space heater On top of that, I didn’t have shit to my name either, that’s real", "vault_lines": ["First private plane I ever rode was a eight-seater", "Before that, I got the bedroom hot with a space heater", "On top of that, I didn't have shit to my name either, that's real", "Got a two-seater problem"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his custom Boeing 767 private jet, nicknamed “Air Drake,” which was gifted to him by Cargojet in 2019.\n\nClearly he came a long way in life to own a private jet worth $185-200 million, because before he owned a private jet, he came up from being very financially unstable to a point where he had to use space heaters to keep himself warm since he wasn’t financially stable enough to afford the heating bills, neither did he have anything else to begin with.", "annotation_id": "38388196", "url": "https://genius.com/38388196", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Tommy gun on the counter, I call it Mr. Thomas That’ll keep niggas honest", "vault_lines": ["Tommy gun on the counter, I call it Mr. Thomas", "That'll keep niggas honest"], "meaning": "He has a Tommy Gun that he uses for interrogation called Mr. Thoma", "annotation_id": "36755454", "url": "https://genius.com/36755454", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Okay, February came around, I used to get paid shovelin’ Sold clothes, walked dogs, trust me, I stay hustlin’", "vault_lines": ["Okay, February came around, I used to get paid shovelin'", "Sold clothes, walked dogs, trust me, I stay hustlin'"], "meaning": "Drake uses this line as a call-back to his humble beginnings in Forest Hill-a neighbourhood in his hometown Toronto, Canada-where he used to pick up odd jobs during the winter to make ends meet.", "annotation_id": "35056826", "url": "https://genius.com/35056826", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "I could feed a country with the tax that I pay governments Whatever they’re doin’ with my cash is very troublin’", "vault_lines": ["I could feed a country with the tax that I pay governments", "Whatеver they're doin' with my cash is vеry troublin'"], "meaning": "Drake highlights the immense wealth he generates and expresses concern about how his tax money is being used.\n\nHe emphasizes the sheer volume of his tax payments, claiming he could “feed a country” with the amount he contributes to governments.\n\nThis hyperbole underscores his financial success and the significant role he plays in the economy. However, he also expresses frustration and unease about the lack of transparency and accountability in how his tax dollars are spent.\n\nThis sentiment reflects a common concern among high-income earners about the effectiveness and ethical implications of government spending.\n\nIt’s worth noting that he probably is paying an incredible amount in taxes – as of February 2025, his net worth hit $250 million.", "annotation_id": "34814492", "url": "https://genius.com/34814492", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Throw a purple tint on a two-liter", "vault_lines": ["Pete O. used to throw a purple tint on a two-liter"], "meaning": "This refers to making the recreational drug known as lean, or drank. It is prepared by mixing codeine cough syrup with soft drinks, like Sprite. The resulting drink often has a purple colour, but that could vary depending on the type of cough syrup", "annotation_id": "34219700", "url": "https://genius.com/34219700", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Peachy", "vault_lines": ["Everyone talkin' lemons when everything is peachy"], "meaning": "“Peachy” is an American slang meaning “something is fine” or “excellent”", "annotation_id": "33645939", "url": "https://genius.com/33645939", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "All them jokes about Aubrey, they got me messed up, for real", "vault_lines": ["All them jokes about Aubrey, they got me messed up, for real"], "meaning": "Could be talking about the “Drake the type of guy…” jokes.", "annotation_id": "33645921", "url": "https://genius.com/33645921", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "I come with a lot of complications inside me It’s always people misleadin’ me that are tryna guide me", "vault_lines": ["I come with a lot of complications inside me", "It's always people misleadin' me that are tryna guide me"], "meaning": "Drake alluded to the idea that the like of HipHop OG’s like Budden’s music career didn’t reach the same level of success as his own.\n\nBut they never fail to give an opinion on what he does\n\nBudden’s career and the like had notable moments, but it didn’t achieve the mainstream success and longevity that Drake’s has.", "annotation_id": "32485430", "url": "https://genius.com/32485430", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "I’ma need more, niggas is shifty like Honda Accords", "vault_lines": ["I'ma need more, niggas is shifty like Honda Accords"], "meaning": "A Honda Accord is a type of car. “Shifting” in the context of cars refers to shifting the car from one gear to another.", "annotation_id": "31677353", "url": "https://genius.com/31677353", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "They say I’m trippin’, I guess they wishin’ I finally fall", "vault_lines": ["They say I'm trippin', I guess they wishin' I finally fall"], "meaning": "The colloquial term “trippin'” is a multifaceted choice of words. Commonly used in informal speech, “trippin'” often refers to someone acting in an irrational, unreasonable, or unusual manner. It’s a phrase that encapsulates a deviation from the norm, suggesting a loss of the usual composure or rationality.\n\nHowever, in the clever hands of Lil Wayne, “trippin'” does more than describe erratic behavior. It becomes a bridge to the imagery of physically stumbling or falling. This use of “trippin'” carries an implicit anticipation of a mistake or failure, aligning with the literal notion of a fall. The term thus serves a dual purpose: it reflects the accusations of his critics while also setting the stage for the metaphorical fall they anticipate.", "annotation_id": "30613629", "url": "https://genius.com/30613629", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "All I gotta be is all that I can possibly", "vault_lines": ["All I gotta be is all that I can possibly"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne employs a nuanced and inventive rhyming technique that showcases his unique lyrical prowess. Rather than opting for a more conventional phrase like “all I gotta be is all that I can possibly be” or “all I gotta be is all that I can be,” Wayne cleverly merges these ideas into a singular, impactful expression. He does this by placing a distinctive emphasis on the syllable “bly” in “possibly,” creating an auditory illusion where “possibly” simultaneously evokes the sound of “possibly be.” This intricate play on words and sounds demonstrates Wayne’s skill in manipulating language and rhythm, creating a layered and sophisticated rhyme that may not be immediately apparent in text but becomes strikingly evident when heard.", "annotation_id": "30613574", "url": "https://genius.com/30613574", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Choppin’ up a lil’ cash cow, that’s steak I’m eatin’", "vault_lines": ["Choppin' up a lil' cash cow, that's steak I'm eatin'"], "meaning": "The phrase “cash cow,” a common metaphor for a consistent source of profit, is ingeniously juxtaposed with the literal concept of “steak,” a product derived from a cow. This juxtaposition is not merely a play on words but a layered representation of success and wealth. By “chopping up” the metaphorical cash cow, the artist symbolically enjoys the fruits of his labor, represented by the “steak.” The pun relies on the word “steak” serving as both a literal reference to a cut of beef and a homophone for “stake,” meaning a share or investment in a venture. By using “steak,” Wayne cleverly alludes to both the tangible rewards of his success (eating a literal steak) and the financial gains from his investments (having a stake in a “cash cow” business).", "annotation_id": "30613535", "url": "https://genius.com/30613535", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Every nigga that stare me down just came to see me", "vault_lines": ["Every nigga that stare me down just came to see me"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne subverts the conventional interpretation of a ‘stare down’ from aggression to admiration. This deft linguistic twist transforms a typically confrontational gesture into a symbol of recognition, showcasing his linguistic ingenuity and inviting a deeper exploration of the complexities inherent in language and perception.", "annotation_id": "30613517", "url": "https://genius.com/30613517", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Ayy, the blues is now kickin’, and dinner is three Michelin", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, the blues is now kickin', and dinner is three Michelin"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing that, because of who he is, he only eats at the best of the best, aka 3 Michelin stars restaurants. 3 Michelin star restaurants are widely regarded as the very best places to eat in the world. At the current time of this writing, there are only 14 3 Michelin star restaurants in the U.S., representing the best of the best.\nDrake has always referenced his taste for fine dining as we’ve seen on “The Ride” where he mentions “The French Laundry” in Nappa Valley, consistently ranked as one of the best places to eat in the world/", "annotation_id": "23523500", "url": "https://genius.com/23523500", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Classics, I keep scribblin’, lights in the Universal building just keep flickerin’", "vault_lines": ["Classics, I keep scribblin', lights in the Universal building just keep flickerin'"], "meaning": "Drake is suggesting that, like rumors have said, he has outgrown his deal with Universal and he is the only reason Universal Music Group is making money, the owner of Republic Records, which distributes Drake’s music under his imprint OVO Sound.\n\nIt was reported that in June 2020, Lil Wayne apparently sold Young Money to Universal for $100 million. A deal which, if true, would include all of Drake’s masters prior to and including his 2018 Scorpion album.", "annotation_id": "21808701", "url": "https://genius.com/21808701", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Everyone wants to try me, but no one wants to buy me", "vault_lines": ["Everyone wants to try me, but no one wants to buy me", "Everyone wants to meet me, but no one wants to keep me"], "meaning": "Like a nice but expensive shoe or dress at the store, which everyone will like to TRY on but probably not BUY or KEEP, Drake compares he’s position in the rap game to this but in a different context, basically saying everyone wants to start a beef but not keep it, in Drake’s about 10 year long rap career he’s had various disses/beefs with he’s rapper colleagues but most of them don’t tend to want to keep beefing with the 6 God, in 2015 Drake and Meek started their famous feud when Meek alleged that the 6 God doesn’t write his own lyrics, Meek and Drake officially squashed their beef on September 8, 2018, when Drake brought Meek on stage during the Boston show of the Aubrey & The Three Migos Tour. However, in an interview with BigBoyTV, Meek revealed that he and Drake had been in contact for about a year before their public reconciliation", "annotation_id": "21575354", "url": "https://genius.com/21575354", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Money just keep comin’ in, you would think I’m Irish the way that it stays doublin’", "vault_lines": ["Money just keep comin' in, you would think I'm Irish the way that it stays doublin'"], "meaning": "Drake uses clever wordplay here with the word ‘doublin’, a homophone of ‘Dublin’, the capital and largest city in Ireland.\n\nDublin evidently has a lot of strong Drake fans as reported by the Irish Post, including glamour model Ava Van Rose who Drake once invited on tour.", "annotation_id": "21556789", "url": "https://genius.com/21556789", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "I’m eye to eye with niggas, I can’t help but to see beyond ’em", "vault_lines": ["I'm eye to eye with niggas, I can't help but to see beyond 'em"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is always looking ahead, even when other rappers are trying to beef with him, as he is too focused on money to be distracted, alluding to Drake’s earlier line:\n\nI don’t eat red meat, but still got beef sizzlin'\nKnow that I need discipline\n\nThis shows that Wayne’s attitude towards their work has been transferred to Drake through his mentorship.\n\n“To see beyond them” sounds like 2CB in the way that Weezy delivers the line, relating to a party drug that provides effects that are similar to those of both MDMA and LSD dependant on the dosage.\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2C-B\nShowing that Lil Wayne is in a safe position where he can be intoxicated on different drugs as well as his well-known love for codeine and marijuana.", "annotation_id": "21497629", "url": "https://genius.com/21497629", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Man, if you saw what I flew here, you’d be like, “He’s sickenin’”", "vault_lines": ["Man, if you saw what I flew here, you'd be like, \"He's sickenin'\""], "meaning": "Drake cleverly employs a double entendre by playing on the homophones, “flew” and “flu”. When one has the flu, they are sick and unwell.\n\nIn 2019, Drake was gifted a Boeing 767, courtesy of Cargojet because they believe it will pay for itself in publicity. The planes exterior has been customized by popular fashion designer, Virgil Abloh.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g3bVgKwXzGc&ab_channel=EsquireExplores\n\n\n\nAlternatively, when haters see his massive 30 seater plane, they become sick with envy.\n\nDrake has also in the past alluded to himself as a “Sicko” or “6icko” (the 6 referring to Drake’s hometown Toronto), and is derived from Drake’s vernacular.", "annotation_id": "21492162", "url": "https://genius.com/21492162", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "The sex platonic, I talk intelligent, text Ebonics", "vault_lines": ["The sex platonic, I talk intelligent, text Ebonics"], "meaning": "Ebonics is an outdated term for African-American Vernacular English. It is a variety or patois of English predominately spoken by working and middle-class African Americans.\n\n“I talk intelligent” could be an ironic statement from Wayne, as it is grammatically incorrect in standard English. However, if we understand “Ebonics” as a patois of standard English, “I talk intelligent” could be considered correct or at least perfectly acceptable, just as “incorrect” grammar and spelling is considered perfectly acceptable when text messaging.", "annotation_id": "21491165", "url": "https://genius.com/21491165", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "[Verse 1: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The first verse’s repetition & Khaled’s DJ drops were removed from the song when it landed on streaming services on Monday, November 30, 2020.", "annotation_id": "21484376", "url": "https://genius.com/21484376", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "The electronic guitars whinin’, that’s just Nirvana", "vault_lines": ["The electronic guitars whinin', that's just Nirvana"], "meaning": "Nirvana was a grunge band based in Aberdeen, Washington, whose style was characterized by catchy electric guitar riffs, which often included distortion and several other effects: some key examples of that include “Come As You Are,” “All Apologies” and their biggest hit, the 1991 song “Smells Like Teen Spirit.”\n\n\n\nGuitar Fingering Pattern of “Smells Like Teen Spirit” By Nirvana\n\nIn reference to Blues music, particularly in the style that B.B. King played, the lead guitar can often be described as “whining” or “crying”. Blues guitarists are known for submitting themselves to the guitar in order to achieve such sounds and convey deep emotions: therefore, in a way, they are achieving a state of “nirvana.”", "annotation_id": "21472910", "url": "https://genius.com/21472910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "You fuckin’ with my math, you better know some trigonometry ’Cause I’ma be bustin’, leave your lima bean at your mama feet", "vault_lines": ["You fuckin' with my math, you better know some trigonometry", "'Cause I'ma be bustin', leave your lima bean at your mama feet"], "meaning": "Trigonometry is a branch of Math that studies relationships between side lengths and angles of triangles. Weezy is relating angles here to getting shot, math in Weezy’s case refers to money. Weezy has referred to the numerical aspect before in his track, “No haters” off of his 2015 album Sorry 4 the wait 2 where Weezy raps:\n\nMoney and drugs, I’m talking Math and Science\n\nWeezy also sounds like he’s saying “mag” instead of “math” and “triggernometry” instead of “trigonometry” but this is intentional, maintaining his title as the pun genius.\n\nA mag is an abbreviation for “magazine”. A magazine is an ammunition storage and feeding device for a repeating firearm, which is used with a trigger, thus “triggernometry”. Weezy confirms this in the following line with “I'ma be bustin'”, referring back to “shooting” using the firearm.\n\nWeezy has done similar wordplay before; one such occasion is in “Abortion” off of his 2011 album Tha Carter IV:\n\nA blessing in disguise is right before our eyes\nBut since it is in disguise, we don’t know that it’s there\n\nThe last phrase is a reference to a line in Weezy’s 2011 hit “6 foot 7 foot” sung by Cory Gunz:\n\nWord to my mama, I’m out of my lima bean", "annotation_id": "21472404", "url": "https://genius.com/21472404", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Got no time for her, but give Richard Mille to her", "vault_lines": ["Got no time for her, but give Richard Mille to her"], "meaning": "Richard Mille is a Swiss watch manufacturer. Although Drizzy is unwilling to devote time to a woman, he would rather gift her a luxury timepiece.", "annotation_id": "21467285", "url": "https://genius.com/21467285", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "My dawgs love sticks and drums like they Questlove", "vault_lines": ["My dawgs love sticks and drums like they Questlove"], "meaning": "Sticks and drums are slang for automatic rifles and their ammunition clips, respectively.\n\nQuestlove is the ever-famous and extremely talented drummer for the hip-hop group, The Roots.\n\nDrake’s boys love guns and ammo as much as Questlove loves his drums. Better watch out.", "annotation_id": "21465335", "url": "https://genius.com/21465335", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Play that “Mask Off” when they find the real cure", "vault_lines": ["Play that \"Mask Off\" when they find the real cure"], "meaning": "Drake is speaking of the COVID-19 pandemic and how it’s been recommended by the CDC and other reputable health officials to wear a mask in order to protect the aerial cavities (nose and mouth), thus preventing the spread of the virus. In 2017, the Atlanta-based rapper Future released the track “Mask Off,” which eventually became a smash hit of that year and, in a more recent scenario, could also be a fitting song to be played once a definitive cure for the virus and/or some vaccines to prevent it have been found.\n\nThroughout the years, Drake and Future have had a pretty prolific series of collaborations, culminating in their 2015 collaborative mixtape What a Time To Be Alive: also, at the time of the release of this song, they have crossed their respective paths twice more, on the songs “Life Is Good” and “Desires.”", "annotation_id": "21464888", "url": "https://genius.com/21464888", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "Shawty make a scene in the house, she get locked out of it Yeah, you not ‘bout to be chillin’ in this bitch with kicked feet up I’ma make you bounce out this ho like Big Freedia", "vault_lines": ["Shawty make a scene in the house, she get locked out of it", "Yeah, you not 'bout to be chillin' in this bitch with kicked feet up", "I'ma make you bounce out this ho like Big Freedia"], "meaning": "Drake says he’ll kick his girl out — or bounce her out – of his house if she decides to act up.\n\nBig Freedia is an American musician and drag queen, mainly known for her huge contributions to the success of bounce music, a genre that first became popular in New Orleans during the ‘90s and which is mainly known for its hyper-energetic instrumentals (which fuse elements of dance, house and hip-hop), its frequently raw and explicit lyrics, and the habit of its vocalists to acknowledge the city’s different urban areas.\n\n\n\nThroughout the years, elements of bounce music have been incorporated in the works of many different artists, including Wayne and Drake themselves; the former’s contributions have helped turn several bounce-inspired songs (such as “A Milli” and Juvenile’s “Back That A** Up”) into nationwide hits, while the latter paid tribute to the genre on his 2019 song “Nice For What,” which even features a short introduction by Big Freedia herself.\n\nFinally, it’s worthwhile to mention that the pair of artists previously worked together on another seemingly bounce-inspired track: Nicki Minaj’s 2015 hit single “Truffle Butter”.", "annotation_id": "21461881", "url": "https://genius.com/21461881", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "I don’t eat red meat, but still got beef sizzlin’ Know that I need discipline", "vault_lines": ["I don't eat red meat, but still got beef sizzlin'", "Know that I need discipline"], "meaning": "In March 2018, Drake revealed he was a vegetarian during a live stream with Twitch streamer Ninja. He alluded to going vegan in an Instagram post the same month as well. On January 2020’s “Desires,” he rapped:\n\nHow you goin' vegan, but still beefin' with me again?\n\nDrake’s use of “beef” doubles as a reference to the rappers he’s feuded with. One of Drizzy’s most popular quarrels was with Meek Mill in 2015, who he dissed on “Charged Up” and “Back To Back,” respectively. They reconciled in September 2018.\n\nMeanwhile, Drake has feuded with Pusha T since 2011—he took shots at Push on “Dreams Money Can Buy,” and subliminally dissed him through the years. Their dispute reached new heights in May 2018 after trading blows on “Infrared,” “Duppy Freestyle,” and “The Story of Adidon.”\n\nDuring his feud with Push, Drake also picked up beef with Kanye West, who affiliates with Pusha through G.O.O.D. Music—their label.", "annotation_id": "21460724", "url": "https://genius.com/21460724", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "I flip the economy like Dominique Dawes", "vault_lines": ["I flip the economy like Dominique Dawes"], "meaning": "Dominique Dawes is one of the most famous gymnasts of all time. She was the first black woman to win an individual Olympic medal in artistic gymnastics, and the first black person of any sex or nationality to win an Olympic gold medal.\n\n\n\nLil Wayne wrote a song when he was 8 years old, although the song was never released. He did drop an album titled True Story as a member of the B.G.‘z group in ‘95 when he was just 12 years old. He released his debut solo album, Tha Block is Hot in '99 at 16. The album debuted at #3 on the Hot 100 on its released and sold 229K copies in week 1. The album also went platinum a month after its release. As of 2012, the album has sold 2.1M copies in the US and 2.4M worldwide. Wayne compares how he flipped the rap game to Dawes when she won the Olympics.", "annotation_id": "21459787", "url": "https://genius.com/21459787", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "When it came to school, there’s no way that I’m A-plus-in’ it So I just dropped out of it, trust me, I’m not proud of it", "vault_lines": ["When it came to school, there's no way that I'm A-plus-in' it", "So I just dropped out of it, trust me, I'm not proud of it"], "meaning": "Although he was considered an ambitious and capable student, Drake dropped out of high school at 17 to pursue his acting career on the TV show Degrassi and financially support his mother.\n\nDrake mentioned dropping out of school in his April 2016 song, “Views”:\n\nI dropped out right before I graduate\nSix credits left, my mama had the saddest day\n‘It’s only up from here, I promise you, you just got to wait’\nAnd she took my word for it, that’s all I had to say\n\nEventually, in 2012 he reportedly took the GED tests and officially became a high school graduate.", "annotation_id": "21459667", "url": "https://genius.com/21459667", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "I used to fuck Gucci models, I’m fuckin’ Gucci designers I keep it too G regardless, I’m gettin’ too deep for divers", "vault_lines": ["I used to fuck Gucci models, I'm fuckin' Gucci designers", "I keep it too G regardless, I'm gettin' too deep for divers"], "meaning": "Clever wordplay by Wayne..\n“2-G” has two meanings in this case:\n1. Gucci’s logo is two Gs joined together\n 2. Too G as in Wayne always keep its real like a G.\n\nThis was my first Genius annotation.. Hope you guys enjoy!!", "annotation_id": "21459613", "url": "https://genius.com/21459613", "state": "pending", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "B.B. King Freestyle", "line": "But now I’m givin’ house tours ’til it’s back to world tours", "vault_lines": ["But now I'm givin' house tours 'til it's back to world tours"], "meaning": "Drake is likely referring to the music video for his April 2020 single, “Toosie Slide,” wherein he shows various areas of his mansion, known as “The Embassy,” situated in Toronto, Ontario.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/xWggTb45brM\n\nThe song released in the midst of the COVID-19 worldwide pandemic, when concert tours were temporarily not allowed as one of the many efforts to reduce the impact of the outbreak.", "annotation_id": "21459584", "url": "https://genius.com/21459584", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
