{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-fed-up-lyrics", "genius_title": "Fed Up", "genius_id": 2687, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "I am absolutely, positively on my grizzy", "vault_lines": ["I am absolutely, positively on my grizzy"], "meaning": "“Grizzy” is slang for Grind", "annotation_id": "24324661", "url": "https://genius.com/24324661", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "And when they love you to death, just never die on ’em”", "vault_lines": ["And when they give ya they shoulders, never cry on 'em", "And when they love you to death, just never die on 'em\""], "meaning": "Clever word play, when someone loves you “to death,” it means that they are really in love with you. So Drake says to not “die on them” or let them down.", "annotation_id": "410906", "url": "https://genius.com/410906", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "[Verse 4: Lil Wayne]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse is not on the single version, due to Wayne not being able to make the music video shoot. This verse only appears on the album version of the record.", "annotation_id": "105529", "url": "https://genius.com/105529", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "I keep a red flag, in case I get penalized", "vault_lines": ["I keep a red flag, in case I get penalized"], "meaning": "In American football, a yellow flag is thrown if there is a penalty. A red flag is thrown to challenge a referee’s decision. He uses a red flag instead of a yellow one as a reference to his gang affiliation, blood. Blood in, blood out.\n\nAlso, if one’s team is called for a penalty, the coach can challenge the call by throwing his red flag. Weezy is saying that he won’t back down to those who are attacking him.", "annotation_id": "105077", "url": "https://genius.com/105077", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "Got a chest full of heart, no Tin Man", "vault_lines": ["Got a chest full of heart, no Tin Man"], "meaning": "Wayne actually portrayed L.Frank Baum’s Tin Man in a school rendition of The Wizard of Oz when he was 12.", "annotation_id": "97178", "url": "https://genius.com/97178", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "It was all a dream, now we the Dream Team", "vault_lines": ["It was all a dream, now we the Dream Team"], "meaning": "Hiphop has gone from the “fad” it was supposed to be with people claiming it would be over in a few years like disco to now where people like Wayne are among the most successful and acclaimed artists in all of music.\nWe’ve gone from the era of “Juicy” to the era where Young Money and Lil Wayne are at the top of the charts.\nYoung Money’s succes was something Wayne dreamt about and it’s come to fruition.", "annotation_id": "97172", "url": "https://genius.com/97172", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "That’s why I’m higher than the Star Trek Enterprise", "vault_lines": ["That’s why I’m higher than the Star Trek Enterprise"], "meaning": "The flagship of Starfleet.  Star Trek: Enterprise the show, however, was supposed to serve as a prequel series for the franchise but it was met with middling reviews and lukewarm fan reaction and was canceled after only five seasons making second shortest of the live-action television series after the original.  Thus Wayne’s line works on two levels: First, he’s high as fuck and in “outer space” mentally. Secondly all the hatred has fueled his monumental success so far which has given him a longer run than the bedraggled show.", "annotation_id": "97164", "url": "https://genius.com/97164", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "I’m so sick and tired of being sick and tired", "vault_lines": ["I'm so sick and tired of being sick and tired", "I'm sick and tired of you suckers, so now I'm fed up", "I’m tired of these haters"], "meaning": "This line comes from American voting rights activist and civil rights leader Fannie Lou Hamer. She ran for Congress in 1964 and 1965, showing people that a Black woman can run for office.\n\nIn a speech, she states, “All my life I’ve been sick and tired. Now I’m sick and tired of being sick and tired.”\n\n\n\nAnthony Hamilton’s chorus on the Nappy Roots‘ “Sick and Tired” also quotes this line.", "annotation_id": "97152", "url": "https://genius.com/97152", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "Stop playin’, I hit Drizzy with the give-and-go", "vault_lines": ["Stop playin', I hit Drizzy with the give-and-go"], "meaning": "A give-and-go is a fundamental maneuver in many team sports which involves two players passing the ball or puck back and forth. The player who has the ball or puck passes to a teammate and then repositions in order to receive a return pass and a scoring opportunity.", "annotation_id": "90183", "url": "https://genius.com/90183", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "That’s why I go hard like A.C. Slater", "vault_lines": ["That’s why I go hard like A.C. Slater"], "meaning": "A character from Saved By The Bell (an American television sitcom that aired between 1989 and 1993), played by Mario Lopez. He’s a ‘jock’, usually sporting the most up to date fashion items and greasey mullet to boot.", "annotation_id": "90178", "url": "https://genius.com/90178", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fed Up", "line": "And when they give ya they shoulders, never cry on ’em", "vault_lines": ["And when they give ya they shoulders, never cry on 'em", "And when they love you to death, just never die on 'em\""], "meaning": "This is a double entendre. It refers to:\nA shoulder shrug (meaning that if people feel “iffy” about what you do), don’t “cry” or feel negative…\nAnd to the common phrase “a shoulder to cry on”. When someone gives a person their shoulder, they’re expressing sympathy for the person.\n\nEither way, Drake is saying to stay strong and independent…", "annotation_id": "79011", "url": "https://genius.com/79011", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Emperor (Feat. Drake) - Caesars Edition", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Josh-cortes-emperor-feat-drake-caesars-edition-lyrics", "genius_title": "Emperor (Feat. Drake) - Caesars Edition", "genius_id": 9868093, "source": "api"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "status": "done", "targets": 15, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Jay-z-off-that-lyrics", "genius_title": "Off That", "genius_id": 674, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "And you know this shit", "vault_lines": ["And you know this shit"], "meaning": "Once again, Jay-Z refers to his notorious “diarrhea incident” that he described in “Why I Love You”.", "annotation_id": "40316432", "url": "https://genius.com/40316432", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "“Blueprint’s” in my white iPod", "vault_lines": ["\"Blueprint's\" in my white iPod"], "meaning": "Hov further delves into his contrast of black and white by highlighting how his Blueprint albums, which were created by a black man, are stored in his white iPod.", "annotation_id": "31039672", "url": "https://genius.com/31039672", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "Black diamonds in my Jesus piece, my God", "vault_lines": ["Black diamonds in my Jesus piece, my God"], "meaning": "As a black man, Hov draws emphasis to the fact that Jesus is still his God with this clever wordplay that ties the exclamation of “my God” to the black diamonds in his Jesus piece.", "annotation_id": "31039664", "url": "https://genius.com/31039664", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "And my best white mate, say what’s up to Chris", "vault_lines": ["And my best white mate, say what's up to Chris"], "meaning": "This is a shout out to Jay-Z’s long time friend and collaborator Chris Martin from Coldplay.", "annotation_id": "22478006", "url": "https://genius.com/22478006", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "And we don’t really care what you used to say Unless that affects your future pay", "vault_lines": ["You used to move, what you used to do", "That you used to screw, what your future do", "And we don't really care what you used to say", "Unless that affects your future pay"], "meaning": "A helpful warning to the aspiring Hovs of the world:\n\nWatch what you say, because it can come back to haunt you and your career later – an example would be Rick Ross, who was a former corrections officer and stole the name of a freewayman for his stage name. This shit always finds it’s way back around.\n\nInteresting that Jay says this given the the hilarity in hindsight of Drake saying this line on the chorus of this very same song.", "annotation_id": "86097", "url": "https://genius.com/86097", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "This a Benetton ad,", "vault_lines": ["This a Benetton ad,"], "meaning": "The reason Jay-Z referenced Benetton ads is their propensity to have ads with multi-racial models.  This falls right in line with all of the color references Jay-Z made.", "annotation_id": "66487", "url": "https://genius.com/66487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "And I don’t give a fidduck abiddout the bitches That you used to screw, what your future do", "vault_lines": ["I don't give a fidduck abiddout the widdate", "You used to move, what you used to do", "And I don't give a fidduck abiddout the bitches", "That you used to screw, what your future do", "And we don't really care what you used to say", "So I don't give a fidduck abiddout the shit", "And we don't give a fidduck, nigga we off that"], "meaning": "Believing that it doesn’t matter what has gone down in the past, Jay is living in the moment, and thinking ahead to his future life.\n\nThe illusion of not caring about previous sexual encounters here could be a sign of Jay maturing before fatherhood – Blue Ivy was born a couple of years later and fatherhood may have been something floating around in Jigga’s mind.", "annotation_id": "47230", "url": "https://genius.com/47230", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "The only time I deal in past tense ’Cause I’m past rims, and I’m past tints", "vault_lines": ["The only time I deal in past tense", "'Cause I'm past rims, and I'm past tints"], "meaning": "Another classic homophone from Hov (“tense” and “tints”). Here, Jay returns to a recurring boast: that he has moved beyond his former means of exhibiting wealth and prestige, namely, superficial automobile modifications\n\nThe couplet recalls 2006’s “30 Something”:\n\nI’m young enough to know the right car to buy\nYet grown enough not to put rims on it.\nI got that six-duce with curtains so you can’t see me\nand I didn’t even have to put tints on it", "annotation_id": "39606", "url": "https://genius.com/39606", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "TriBeCa loft", "vault_lines": ["Uhh, in my TriBeCa loft"], "meaning": "TriBeCa is the upscale neighborhood of New York City where Jay lives.\n\nYeah, I’m out that Brooklyn\nNow I’m down in Tribeca, right next to DeNiro\nBut I’ll be hood forever", "annotation_id": "37291", "url": "https://genius.com/37291", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "I’m on a practice field runnin’ Two-A-Days So I don’t drop the ball when it’s threw my way", "vault_lines": ["I'm on a practice field runnin' Two-A-Days", "So I don't drop the ball when it's threw my way"], "meaning": "American football metaphors, Jay’s practising what he does twice a day – one in the morning and one in the afternoon, like the famous NFL “Two-A-Day” regime.\n\nOf course, even if he does practice his rhyming, he can’t help dropping the ball and forgetting his words every once in a while…", "annotation_id": "35253", "url": "https://genius.com/35253", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "With my high brow art", "vault_lines": ["With my high brow art"], "meaning": "Hov has touted his Basquiat and Warhol pieces throughout the years and has even used one of Warhol’s rorschach pieces for Jay’s Decoded book cover\n\n\n\n The video for Jay-Z’s “Blue Magic” features a few pieces in his art collection, including a painting by Damien Hirst.", "annotation_id": "24954", "url": "https://genius.com/24954", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "This ain’t black vs. white, my nigga we off that Please tell Bill O’Reilly to fall back Tell Rush Limbaugh to get off my balls It’s 2010, not 1864", "vault_lines": ["Timbs we off that, rims we off that", "This ain't black vs. white, my nigga we off that", "Please tell Bill O'Reilly to fall back", "Tell Rush Limbaugh to get off my balls", "Uhh, we ain't tripping off that"], "meaning": "Bill O'Reilly and Rush Limbaugh are conservative figures in the media who often come under fire for racism. Jay Z is reminding them that we don’t live in slavery times, in 2010 we should be over racism.\n\nSpecifically, 1864 is the year before the Civil War ended as well as slavery through the passage of the 13th Amendment (outlawing slavery nationwide), both of which occurred in 1865.", "annotation_id": "24307", "url": "https://genius.com/24307", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "Oversized clothes and chains we off that (Hey, hey) Niggas still making it rain and we off that (Hey, hey) ’Cause you can’t bring the future back, back (Hey) Tell them haters get off me, the Cris’ we off that (Hey, hey) Timbs we off that, rims we off that", "vault_lines": ["You about to tell her you love her we off that (Hey, hey)", "But you can't bring the future back, back (Hey, hey)", "Oversized clothes and chains we off that (Hey, hey)", "Niggas still making it rain and we off that (Hey, hey)", "'Cause you can't bring the future back, back (Hey)", "Tell them haters get off me, the Cris' we off that (Hey, hey)", "Timbs we off that, rims we off that", "Yeah we off that, is you still on that? (Hey, hey)", "And we still makin money 'cause we still on that (Hey, err)", "Hey, hey, hey, hey"], "meaning": "With the whole “gangsta rap” scene, oversized clothes and chains, throwing money in the air at strip-clubs, drinking Crystal, wearing Timberland boots and putting huge rims on their cars were all common rap stereotypes at the time of the album’s release.\n\nBecause they’re ahead of the game, Drake and Jay Z are on to creating new stereotypes and fashions for the rap scene -they’re on to the next one!\n\nDrake has been ticking those items off his list in ensuing years; all that’s left for him to backtrack on his rims as well as timbs.\n\n\n\nDrake can be seen wearing timbs as recent as 2015. This song came out in 2009.", "annotation_id": "22763", "url": "https://genius.com/22763", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "Got a black president, got green presidents", "vault_lines": ["Got a black president, got green presidents"], "meaning": "My President is black, my Maybach too\nAnd I’ll be God damned if my diamonds ain’t blue\n\nContrasting the first black President in the White House with the green faces of white dead presidents, Jay Z acknowledges how far black people have come, and it is due to this that he is in a position to show off his cash.", "annotation_id": "22590", "url": "https://genius.com/22590", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Off That", "line": "I’m so tomorrow the Audemar says yesterday", "vault_lines": ["I'm so tomorrow the Audemar says yesterday"], "meaning": "Jay is so ahead of his competitors that even his hella expensive Audemars Piguet watch can’t keep up!\n\nAlso, wearing something can be a statement. So it might be about the competition imitating Jay, wearing an expensive watch, which is by now gone out of style: so yesterday.\n\nThe double entendre here could be time zones: Jay flies all over the world in his private jet. His mechanical Audemars tracks dates, but because he flies back and forth in time zones, the watch got ‘confused’.\n\nThis little gem will set you back $1 million. In addition to confirming that you are ahead of your time, it can also mow the lawn, fix a garbage disposal and keep moles out of your prize-winning petunia garden", "annotation_id": "22589", "url": "https://genius.com/22589", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
