{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "Yeah, we in this bitch, like tampons", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, we in this bitch, like tampons"], "meaning": "Wayne is relating a girl using a tampon to him having sex with them. Both are rod like things that go into the vagina, so this was a simile waiting to happen.", "annotation_id": "23885514", "url": "https://genius.com/23885514", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "I am not a human Shout to all my moon men", "vault_lines": ["I am not a human", "Shout to all my moon men"], "meaning": "Sticking with the theme and title of this album, “I Am Not a Human Being”, Lil Wayne is indicating that he is so out of this world and so unique as an artist that he is not a basic human being. The first verse of this intro song to the album establishes the fact that Wayne is taking on the persona of a Martian from another planet. Similar to previous verses of his such as in the songs “Phone Home” and “Forever”.\nThe term “moon men” is a term for a carbon life form not from this planet.", "annotation_id": "21133683", "url": "https://genius.com/21133683", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "Yes, I’m with Young Money, tell the magazines stop asking me I be with the dread with the tattoos on his head And a flag the color red like a fucking low battery", "vault_lines": ["Yes, I'm with Young Money, tell the magazines stop asking me", "I be with the dread with the tattoos on his head", "And a flag the color red like a fucking low battery"], "meaning": "Drake confirms that he is signed to Young Money Entertainment and says that he is with someone who sounds eerily similar to Lil Wayne (dreads, face tattoos, Blood red flag).", "annotation_id": "20729816", "url": "https://genius.com/20729816", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "Bal’ like Solange, India Arie, Britney Spears", "vault_lines": ["Bal' like Solange, India Arie, Britney Spears"], "meaning": "Wayne is saying that he “balls”, however the way he pronounces it sounds like “bald” and he finishes off the simile with a list of bald headed female celebrities.\n\n\n\nSolange\n\n\n\nIndia Arie\n\n\n\nBritney Spears", "annotation_id": "20729786", "url": "https://genius.com/20729786", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "Smoking on that Headband, call that shit that Paul Pierce", "vault_lines": ["Smoking on that Headband, call that shit that Paul Pierce"], "meaning": "Headband is a super fire strain of weed that is a cross between OG Kush and Sour Diesel, two of the best and most popular strains.\n\nPaul Pierce is a former NBA player who was a 10x All Star with the Boston Celtics and a 1x NBA Champion. Paul Pierce wore a headband in literally almost every single game he played in and was rarely seen without it.", "annotation_id": "20729758", "url": "https://genius.com/20729758", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "Man, I ball hard, even with five fouls", "vault_lines": ["Man, I ball hard, even with five fouls"], "meaning": "Even If Wayne fouls out of the game, he is still going to BALL on you!  “A player who commits five personal fouls over the course of a 40-minute game, or six in a 48-minute game, fouls out and is disqualified for the remainder of the game.”", "annotation_id": "20273553", "url": "https://genius.com/20273553", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "Like welfare I’m Saint Elsewhere", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In the medical field, institutions that serve unfortunate people turned away by prestigious hospitals are often referred to as “St. Elsewhere.”\n\nLike welfare and St. Elsewhere type hospitals, Lil Wayne serves the underprivileged with these fuego bars.\n\nSt. Elsewhere is also an ‘80s medical show about about an urban teaching hospital in Boston.", "annotation_id": "14259247", "url": "https://genius.com/14259247", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "That’s more tear drops, call me Crybaby", "vault_lines": ["That's more tear drops, call me Crybaby"], "meaning": "Teardrop tattoos on the face are usually to acknowledge you have killed someone; the more teardrops, the more victims\n\n\n\nBut the four teardrops tattooed on Lil Wayne’s face signify the deaths of two close friends; Weezy hasn’t murdered anyone, as he verifies on the track “Hustla Musik”:\n\nI ain’t never killed nobody, I promise!\n\nCall me cry baby could also refer to the 1990 movie “Cry Baby” starring Johnny Depp.", "annotation_id": "39362", "url": "https://genius.com/39362", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "I’ll let my goons rush ya, like Moscow", "vault_lines": ["I'll let my goons rush ya, like Moscow"], "meaning": "Operation: Barbarossa a.k.a. the beginning of The Battle of Moscow, was the largest military offensive/rush/attack of all times (literally). About 1.25 million Germans rushed Moscow, Russia.\n\nSO Wayne’s comparing his goons to an entire army that will rush your whole city and leave it all fucked up.\n\n\n\nAlso a nod to Weezy’s affiliation with the Bloods (same color as the Bloody Red Communists)\n\nNote the “Rush ya / Russia” pun", "annotation_id": "39340", "url": "https://genius.com/39340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "You’s a bitch like Zeta Phi Beta, motherfucker", "vault_lines": ["You's a bitch like Zeta Phi Beta, motherfucker"], "meaning": "Zeta Phi Beta is the country’s 3rd-oldest black sorority, and Wayne, being a blood, isn’t too fond of their choice in color scheme.", "annotation_id": "39335", "url": "https://genius.com/39335", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Gonorrhea", "line": "Rock-a-bye-baby; homicide, baby", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A reference to a scene in New Jack City, where a girl in Nino Brown’s crew CMB (Cash Money Brothers) kills a competing drug dealer – she shoots him in the head and says “rock-a-bye baby”\n\nBirdman’s “Cash Money Records” is in reference to this movie, too", "annotation_id": "39315", "url": "https://genius.com/39315", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Migos-walk-it-talk-it-lyrics", "genius_title": "Walk It Talk It", "genius_id": 3433244, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Ballin’, somethin’ we do often (Ball)", "vault_lines": ["Ballin', somethin' we do often (Ball)"], "meaning": "“Ballin'”, in this context, is a basketball analogy signifying how Takeoff and the other 2 Migos are enjoying financial success in their lives (due to their immensely popular music careers).\n\nThroughout his verse, Takeoff makes several basketball allusions (“Buck, no Milwaukee”, “I’m splashin' up”, “I play the coach so I gotta call it”, “I get a rebound, I ain’t talkin' ‘bout Spalding”), so he sticks with the theme in this line and explicitly mentions “Ballin’” to his listeners as one more basketball reference.\n\nTakeoff compares himself to a professional basketball player here because high-level athletes are consistently paid extremely well, with the minimum NBA salary being $1,018,000. This fits in perfectly with the main idea of the song, as “Walk It Talk It” centers around the theme of the Migos' status as rich elites who don’t fake their success.", "annotation_id": "27177298", "url": "https://genius.com/27177298", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Yeah, yeah (Deko) Woah, hold on (OG Parker)", "vault_lines": ["Woah, hold on (OG Parker)"], "meaning": "Deko and OG Parker are the producers of “Walk It Talk It”.\n\nOG Parker has worked with the 3 rappers on other songs, such as “Slippery” on their “Culture” album.\n\nDeko, meanwhile, has been working with the band for quite some time. Deko produced “Hit Em”, “Found Out”, and “One Time”, all of which were released in 2015 or earlier.", "annotation_id": "27177278", "url": "https://genius.com/27177278", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Takeoff, rocket, keep ’em in pocket", "vault_lines": ["Takeoff, rocket, keep 'em in pocket"], "meaning": "Offset gives a shoutout to Takeoff, one of the other two members of Migos. Here, Offset is also making a play on words with “takeoff” and “rocket,” with rocket ships primarily being known for literally taking off into Earth’s orbit.\n\nAdditionally, Takeoff later named his late-2018 debut solo album The Last Rocket, which also references his own stage name.\n\n\n\nTo keep someone “in pocket” is to keep them under control or on the right track.", "annotation_id": "17918299", "url": "https://genius.com/17918299", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "I take the nine and go buy me a faucet (Nine)", "vault_lines": ["I take the nine and go buy me a faucet (Nine)"], "meaning": "“The nine” refers to a 9mm pistol (probably a glock). When Takeoff says  “buy me a faucet”, it’s because he’s going to “rain” bullets on whoever crosses him.", "annotation_id": "15905289", "url": "https://genius.com/15905289", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "By the pair, I got karats that choke (By the pair) By the pair, I got karats each lobe (By the pair)", "vault_lines": ["By the pair, I got karats that choke (By the pair)", "By the pair, I got karats each lobe (By the pair)"], "meaning": "Karats are a unit of measurement used to measure the fineness of precious metals. It is most commonly used to measure gold while carats are used to measure diamonds. 24 Karat gold is 100% gold. 18 karat gold is 75% gold.", "annotation_id": "15033785", "url": "https://genius.com/15033785", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "I get a rebound, I ain’t talkin’ ’bout Spalding (Ball)", "vault_lines": ["I get a rebound, I ain't talkin' 'bout Spalding (Ball)"], "meaning": "Spalding is a sporting goods company best known for its production of basketballs.\n\n\n\nWhile he and the rest of the Migos can often be found sitting courtside at a basketball game, Takeoff here refers to a rebound relationship, in which one enters a new relationship shortly following a break-up.", "annotation_id": "14723483", "url": "https://genius.com/14723483", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Watch it buck, no Milwaukee (Hunnid)", "vault_lines": ["Watch it buck, no Milwaukee (Hunnid)"], "meaning": "This line is referring to the Milwaukee Bucks, a basketball team based in Milwaukee, Wisconsin.\n\nTakeoff also uses a good play on words to tell someone to watch what they’re doing around him. Buck is slang for a person who is young.", "annotation_id": "14238333", "url": "https://genius.com/14238333", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Say that we been beefin’, dawg, but you on your own", "vault_lines": ["Say that we been beefin', dawg, but you on your own"], "meaning": "This could be a shot at Joe Budden who has dissed Drake multiple times with no response back. Ultimately, this was the best move for Drake as it made Budden look desperate for attention. The “you on your own” line is particularly scathing since Joe has admitted to being frustrated with the one-sided exchange.\n\nAnother explication for this lyric could be the tension between Drake and XXXTENTACION since Drake’s single “KMT” sounds similar to X’s breakout track “Look at Me”. X has also been feuding with the Migos in which the group allegedly jumped him at a Los Angeles hotel.", "annotation_id": "13822166", "url": "https://genius.com/13822166", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "I can’t be beefin’ with no wack nigga, got no backbone", "vault_lines": ["I can't be beefin' with no wack nigga, got no backbone"], "meaning": "The term “backbone” can refer to someone who is a foundational contributor of a team, event, or project. Conversely, when someone has “no backbone”, they are being described as a coward who backs out of things easily.\n\nThis is probably a reference to the ongoing beef between Drake and Joe Budden. On Drake’s Scary Hours track, “Diplomatic Immunity”, he similarly calls out Budden for being uninvolved and cowardly:\n\nShit is complex like short niggas ‘round tall ladies\nI gotta watch who I’m talkin’ to like it’s all-ages\nI’ve seen buddin' careers turn to sit around and talk about other careers, judgin' their peers\nKnowledge from niggas who did not contribute to none of this here\n\nThis is possibly also a reference to Joe Budden having scoliosis.", "annotation_id": "13714384", "url": "https://genius.com/13714384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "She just bought a new ass but got the same boobs (Same boobs)", "vault_lines": ["She just bought a new ass but got the same boobs (Same boobs)"], "meaning": "Breast and buttock surgical augmentations and augmentations have drastically increased over the past decade. Here are the ridiculous percentage growths in both, according to the American Society of Plastic Surgeons:\n\nButtock lifts, up 252 percent (4,767 in 2015, up from 1,356 in 2000)\nBreast lifts, up 89 percent (99,614 in 2015, up from 52,836 in 2000)\n\nQuavo is referring to one of his girls, probably a sidechick. The woman surgically augmented her ass in order to try to appear more attractive to Huncho.", "annotation_id": "13677118", "url": "https://genius.com/13677118", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "[Intro: Quavo]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This intro intentionally features static noise that sounds similar to the noises you hear when you use a walkie talkie.\n\nThe addition of these sounds was likely inspired by both the chorus and this line in Takeoff’s verse:\n\nThat’s my bro, he know the lingo (lingo), ain’t no walkie-talkie (no)", "annotation_id": "13638531", "url": "https://genius.com/13638531", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "That’s my bro, he know the lingo (Lingo) Ain’t no walkie-talkie (no)", "vault_lines": ["That's my bro, he know the lingo (Lingo)", "Ain't no walkie-talkie (no)"], "meaning": "“Lingo” is a term that refers to regional vocabulary. A comprehensive list of Atlanta-based lingo can be found here.\n\nThis line was likely inspired by Takeoff’s younger brother, YRN Lingo.\n\n\n\nHis brother knows the lingo, so it’s not just coming out of his mouth like he’s a walkie-talkie.", "annotation_id": "13635420", "url": "https://genius.com/13635420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Niggas pocket watchin’ (Watchin’)", "vault_lines": ["Niggas pocket watchin' (Watchin')"], "meaning": "“Pocket watching” is the act of paying attention to another person’s financial business in attempt to find out how much money they have.\n\nTakeoff used the same line in Migos' 2017 release, “T-Shirt”:\n\nHad to cop the Audi, then the top, I had to chop it (skrt skrt)\nNiggas pocket watchin', so I gotta keep the rocket (grrah)", "annotation_id": "13628397", "url": "https://genius.com/13628397", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "I hit her, gave her back to the city, she home (She at home now!) That was that, so", "vault_lines": ["I hit her, gave her back to the city, she home", "(She at home now!) That was that, so"], "meaning": "Reminiscing to Drake’s line in “Child’s Play.”\n\nDon’t make me give you back to the hood\n\n“Savage Drake” has been known to fly out his groupies to where he’s staying and drop them back in the city, referring to Toronto.", "annotation_id": "13626971", "url": "https://genius.com/13626971", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Smoke the cookie, get the coughin’", "vault_lines": ["Smoke the cookie, get the coughin'"], "meaning": "Takeoff is smoking GSC (formerly known as Girl Scout Cookies,) a hybrid of OG Kush and Durban Poison.\n\n\n\nThis strain was most notably referenced to by Offset in Migos' hit, “Bad and Boujee”:\n\nRaindrop, drop top (drop top)\nSmokin' on cookie in the hotbox (cookie)", "annotation_id": "13625268", "url": "https://genius.com/13625268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Heard you signed your life for that brand new chain (I heard)", "vault_lines": ["Heard you signed your life for that brand new chain (I heard)"], "meaning": "Quavo is refering to hip-hop record label’s crew chains. From Jay-Z’s Roc-A-Fella to Birdman’s Cash Money Records the practice of giving your artist a chain to rock is a staple in the hip-hop community.\n\nQuavo also interlopes one of his previous verses on Big Sean’s “Sacrifices” where he says:\n\nHeard you sacrificed your life for that chain\n\nBut even deeper, he’s talking about how artists get screwed over by labels, and sign away their whole life.\n\nWe’ve seen it happen to some of the greatest artists of our generation. From Prince’s dispute with Warner Bros, to Lil Wayne’s feud with Cash Money Records over delaying Tha Carter V.\n\nLuckily, in today’s age artists have the power to self-release their music digitally on their own terms, or start their own record labels to prevent them from not getting their fair share.", "annotation_id": "13624409", "url": "https://genius.com/13624409", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Whippin’ up dope, scientist (Whip it up, whip it up, cook it up, cook it up, skrr, skrr)", "vault_lines": ["Whippin' up dope, scientist"], "meaning": "“Whipping it up” and “cookin' dope” are slang terms  used to describe the process of the making and production of narcotics.\n\n\n\nIn this line, Quavo connects scientists using chemistry and science to make chemicals to drug dealers and drug-lords, who also use chemistry to “cook up dope.”\n\nThe Migos are fond of using this reference, as they did many times on their 2014 mixtape No Label II, for example on the song “New Atlanta”:\n\nI’m a young rich nigga, whippin' up chemicals", "annotation_id": "13623395", "url": "https://genius.com/13623395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Take my shoes and walk a mile Somethin’ that you can’t do (Woo, hey)", "vault_lines": ["Take my shoes and walk a mile", "Somethin' that you can't do (Woo, hey)"], "meaning": "Quavo references the famous expression: “Walk a mile in someone’s shoes”. The phrase means to understand a person’s experiences and be empathetic towards them before making a judgment.\n\nHowever, Quavo claims that this isn’t possible, having grown up in Gwinnett County, a low-income and dangerous area that seems almost unimaginable. He discussed growing up there in a June 2015 interview with Rolling Stone.\n\nI ain’t going to sit here like, “My neighborhood was hard, and I had to get out there and grind.” We made it hard for ourselves. We chose to stay on the streets.", "annotation_id": "13622656", "url": "https://genius.com/13622656", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Walk it like I talk it (Walk it) Walk it like I talk it Walk it, walk it like I talk it (Woo) Walk it like I talk it (Yeah) Walk it like I talk it (Walk it) Walk it like I talk it Walk it, walk it like I talk it (Woo) Walk it like I talk it (Hey) Walk it like I talk it (Walk it) Walk it like I talk it (Walk it) Walk it like I talk it Walk it, walk it like I talk it (Woo) Walk it like I talk it (Talk it) Walk it like I talk it (Ayy) Walk it like I talk it (Woo) Walk it, walk it like I talk it (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Walk it like I talk it (Walk it), walk it like I talk it (Yeah)", "Walk it, walk it like I talk it (Talk it), walk it like I talk it (Woo)", "Walk it like I talk it (Walk it)", "Walk it like I talk it, walk it, walk it like I talk it (Hey)", "Walk it, walk it like I talk it (Woo)", "I walk like I walk (Hey) talk like I talk (Woah)"], "meaning": "The idiom “talk the talk, walk the walk” means to support your words through actions. A close derivative is “talk the talk, but don’t walk the walk”, a phrase used to describe when one’s actions do not agree with their verbal claims. Quavo uses classic repetition and parallelism for this chorus.\n\nThrough music and social media, Quavo often talks about his success and dominance in the music industry. He even goes by the nickname “Huncho”, an informal term that was coined in the U.S. in the 1940s meaning “officer in charge”. An example of his boastful nickname can be heard in his track “Hellcat”.\n\nDo anybody know what Huncho mean?\nLeader of the team, brrrt\nWhen the Huncho come in town\nWe run all the lights\nYeah\nHuncho\n\nIn terms of walking the walk, Quavo has contributed to break out projects like Billboard Hot 100 hit “Bad and Boujee” and Huncho Jack, Jack Huncho which has reached No. 1 on Billboard’s Top R&B/Hip-Hop Albums and Rap Albums.\n\nAdditionally, this hook uses the same staccato flow that Takeoff used in his verse on “T-Shirt”.", "annotation_id": "13622015", "url": "https://genius.com/13622015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 159, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "I bought a Plain Jane Rollie", "vault_lines": ["I bought a Plain Jane Rollie"], "meaning": "A ‘Plain Jane’ Rolex is an unaltered Rolex, a trend that was popularized in hip-hop by A$AP Ferg on his hit record “Plain Jane.” In his Verified episode for the track, he explains that flooding the face of a watch with diamonds or encrusting it lowers its value.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eISsaQNGnAU", "annotation_id": "13620813", "url": "https://genius.com/13620813", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Heard you livin’ in a mansion in all your raps, though But your shit look like the trap on this Google Maps, though", "vault_lines": ["Heard you livin' in a mansion in all your raps, though", "But your shit look like the trap on this Google Maps, though"], "meaning": "A “trap,” or more commonly “trap house,” is a slang term that’s used to describe a house where drugs are stored in and distributed from. The word “trap” is used since drug addicts are supplied with shelter and drugs, so they usually don’t leave trap houses, making themselves “trapped” in them.\n\nTrap houses are often abandoned houses in lower-class neighborhoods, they’re defined by their low and poor living conditions.\n\n\n\nGoogle Maps is a web mapping service developed by Google. The line alludes to the service’s Street View feature, which lets the user explore a 360° panoramic view of streets. Drake is calling out rappers who lie about where they live on their songs in order to make themselves look rich.", "annotation_id": "13620662", "url": "https://genius.com/13620662", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Ayy, I gotta stay in my zone", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, I gotta stay in my zone"], "meaning": "Drake has a collection of beefs. That’s why he believes he has to stay in his zone, because as soon as he does something, someone wants a problem with him.\n\nThat’s a constant subject for Drake these last months, he recently talked about it in his song, “God’s Plan”:\n\nI been movin' calm, don’t start no trouble with me\nTryna keep it peaceful is a struggle for me\n\nIn 2011, Drake featured on the Weeknd’s “The Zone”.", "annotation_id": "13620301", "url": "https://genius.com/13620301", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Elliot got me rocky", "vault_lines": ["Elliot got me rocky"], "meaning": "Elliot Avianne, co-owner of Avianne & Co., is a well-known jeweler in the hip-hop scene. He has created pieces for artists like Future and Young Thug, but he’s even more known for his over-the-top Migos pieces.\n\nHis pieces for Migos members include Ratatoille, Crash Bandicoot and Yoda-themed pendants for Quavo, an alien astronaut for Takeoff, and a raindrop pendant for Offset.\n\nElliot recently posted a clip to Instagram from the “Walk It Talk It” Music Video featuring Offset’s line.", "annotation_id": "13619154", "url": "https://genius.com/13619154", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "I think my back got scoliosis ’cause I swerve the lane (Skrr)", "vault_lines": ["I think my back got scoliosis 'cause I swerve the lane (Skrr)"], "meaning": "Quavo makes use of the homophones “my back” and “Maybach” to introduce the double entendre.\n\nScoliosis is a back condition that causes a sideways curvature of the spine. Quavo compares the form of the spinal curve with the act of constantly switching driving lanes while driving in a luxury Maybach.", "annotation_id": "13619062", "url": "https://genius.com/13619062", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "We been brothers since Versace bando, woah Name ringin’ like a Migo trap phone, woah", "vault_lines": ["We been brothers since Versace bando, woah", "Name ringin' like a Migo trap phone, woah"], "meaning": "“Versace” was Migos' breakout hit off of their album Young Rich Niggas. When the song blew up, Drake knew that Migos was special and he rapped a verse over it, creating a remix that made the song soar up the charts. Drake also settles prior disputes between him and Migos–in 2015, Migos called out Drake for biting their signature ‘flow’ with the release of his track “The Language”. Quavo spoke on the matter in a Billboard interview with John Kennedy:\n\nC’mon, Drizzy know he bit the flow! Drizzy got on ‘Versace,’ right? Then when I came on there I did ‘Versace/ Versace/ Medusa head on me like I’m illuminati.’ My boy Drizzy Drake got them bars from me.\n\nDrake also states that people are always calling his name, which parallels Migos' trap phone ringing because people want to buy drugs. A trap phone is often a disposable phone utilized to complete illicit actions.\n\nA bando is an abandon house, often used to traffic drugs, a theme in many of Migos' songs.  “Bando” is also a reference to one of Migos' very first successful songs.", "annotation_id": "13618627", "url": "https://genius.com/13618627", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Walk It Talk It", "line": "Used to be with Vashtie at Santo’s That’s on Tommy Campos, we live like Sopranos and I—", "vault_lines": ["Used to be with Vashtie at Santo's", "That's on Tommy Campos, we live like Sopranos and I—"], "meaning": "Va$hti Kola is a DJ, musician, and film-maker from New York, known for hosting her “1992” parties at Santos Party House. P Diddy has also hosted parties at Santos, and the likes of A$AP Rocky and Odd Future have performed there.\n\nIn 2015, Kola directed an alternate version of Drizzy’s “Hotline Bling” video and DJ’d for him at his Curated Sotheby’s art Show in New York.\n\n\n\nTommy Campos is also a New York-based OVO affiliate, who Drizzy has shouted out before, most notably on “5AM in Toronto.” Drizzy goes on to liken his OVO family to the notorious Soprano crime family of the eponymous TV series. Following the release of this song, Campos posted a picture of the Sopranos on Instagram and tagged Drake as Tony Soprano (the head of the family).\n\nDrake also finishes his verse abruptly, almost exactly how The Sopranos ended.", "annotation_id": "13618591", "url": "https://genius.com/13618591", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Metro-boomin-no-complaints-lyrics", "genius_title": "No Complaints", "genius_id": 3131793, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Y’all niggas, man, know what I’m sayin’?", "vault_lines": ["Fuck what y'all niggas doin'", "Yeah, know what I'm sayin'?", "Y'all niggas, man, know what I'm sayin'?"], "meaning": "dr", "annotation_id": "37601301", "url": "https://genius.com/37601301", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": false, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Robbin’ the bank (Bank)", "vault_lines": ["Robbin' the bank (Bank)", "Racks in the bank (Racks)"], "meaning": "Offset’s bank account has so much money that when he withdraws money from his account he feels like he is robbing a bank", "annotation_id": "28531355", "url": "https://genius.com/28531355", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "My bitch Amazon (Bad)", "vault_lines": ["My bitch Amazon (Bad)"], "meaning": "A frequent comparison made by Offset, he compares his girls to people living in the Amazon rainforest. The Amazon is wild, mysterious and unpredictable which are the same traits that his “bitch” exhibits.\n\nAn Amazonian is a member of a Greek mythical tribe of women that are superior to a normal human woman. When Offset says his bitch is “bad” she’s superior to any other basic woman around unlike one from the Amazon.", "annotation_id": "23035165", "url": "https://genius.com/23035165", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "My bitch Amazon (Bad)", "vault_lines": ["My bitch Amazon (Bad)"], "meaning": "A frequent comparison made by Offset, he compares his girls to people living in the Amazon rainforest. The Amazon is wild, mysterious and unpredictable which are the same traits that his “bitch” exhibits.\n\nAn Amazonian is a member of a Greek mythical tribe of women that are superior to a normal human woman.  When Offset says his bitch is “bad” she’s superior to any other basic woman around unlike one from the Amazon.", "annotation_id": "12472775", "url": "https://genius.com/12472775", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "I’ve been on Percocets for the last week (Perc) Sippin’ on lean, I’m sleep, geeked (Geeked) Move out the way, beep, beep (Beep)", "vault_lines": ["I've been on Percocets for the last week (Perc)", "Sippin' on lean, I'm sleep, geeked (Geeked)", "Move out the way, beep, beep (Beep)"], "meaning": "Offset is implying that he’s been high off of a very famous drug, percocets, for the last week.  “Percocet” is the brand name of a narcotic pain reliever containing a combination of oxycodone and acetaminophen, it is a narcotic painkiller that is highly addictive.\n\nOffset also says that he is sipping on lean, saying he’s “geeked”.  “Lean” is a mix of codeine-based prescription cough syrup, Sprite and, often, Jolly Ranchers candy. The drug is meant to give a slowed-down, tired feeling, hence the word “sleep” and “geeked”.\n\nIn the last line, Offset is saying to get out of the way of his dangerous driving, most likely because he is high off of these two drugs.", "annotation_id": "12362548", "url": "https://genius.com/12362548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "(If Young Metro don’t trust you, I’m gon’ shoot you)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Metro Boomin typically announces his presence on a song by using one of his many production tags. His past tags include: “This beat is so, so, Metro”, “Metro Boomin wants some more, nigga” and “Young Metro, Young Metro, Young Metro.”\n\nThe line itself comes from the bridge of Uncle Murda’s “Right Now” and is delivered by Future, Metro Boomin’s longtime collaborator. It gained prominence after appearing on Kanye West’s hit song, “Father Stretch My Hands Pt. 1” and on Drake and Future’s hit collaboration “Jumpman,” and even found itself in the political sphere, making its way into a Complex interview of former presidential candidate Ben Carson and protests of Donald Trump in Chicago.", "annotation_id": "12297218", "url": "https://genius.com/12297218", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Fuck a delay (Yeah) They gotta pay (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["They gotta pay (Yeah)"], "meaning": "This is a shot at Cash Money’s label head Birdman and a nod to the infamous delay of Lil Wayne’s long-awaited album, The Carter V.\n\nCarter V was initially due for a release in 2013 but was delayed multiple times by Birdman because Lil Wayne wanted to leave Cash Money for not getting paid for the album, hence Drake’s line “they gotta pay.”\n\nAfter Wayne sued Birdman in 2015 for violating the album’s terms—not getting paid for it—he scored a settlement of $10 million in June 2018 and C5 was released three months later in September 2018.", "annotation_id": "12276046", "url": "https://genius.com/12276046", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Pull out that stick and I’m sweepin’ the street (Bow, bow)", "vault_lines": ["Pull out the banger (Raahhh)", "Pull out that stick and I'm sweepin' the street (Bow, bow)"], "meaning": "“Stick” refers to a type of gun, probably automatic, which Offset boasts about shooting to “clean the streets” with, like a street-sweeping truck.", "annotation_id": "12246470", "url": "https://genius.com/12246470", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Quit ridin’ the waves, the sea deep (Woah)", "vault_lines": ["Quit ridin' the waves, the sea deep (Woah)"], "meaning": "“Riding the wave” is copying the “in” style (in this case, rapping) to gain popularity. This also was heavily mentioned on Drake’s March 2017 track, “Portland” where Quavo—who’s also from Migos—raps:\n\nHell nah\nNever let these niggas ride your wave\n\n“The sea deep” means that there are other styles one can choose from, just not Offset’s.", "annotation_id": "12234963", "url": "https://genius.com/12234963", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Me, I’m just based (Based)", "vault_lines": ["Me, I'm just based (Based)"], "meaning": "“Based” is a term coined by bay area rapper Lil B. The term describes someone who is free spirited, being positive, and not letting anyone else change themselves. Drake refers to himself as such since, as heard in the previous bars, he’s simply proud of how far he’s come in his career.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/196123342619684864", "annotation_id": "12225510", "url": "https://genius.com/12225510", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "(Metro Boomin want some more, nigga)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Metro Boomin’s secondary producer tag aside from the one sang by Future in his collaboration with Uncle Murda, “Right Now”. The Young Thug version comes from Thugger’s song “Some More”.", "annotation_id": "12224107", "url": "https://genius.com/12224107", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Know niggas didn’t do that shit like that boy Meech (Nah)", "vault_lines": ["Know niggas didn't do that shit like that boy Meech (Nah)"], "meaning": "Demetrius Flenory, otherwise known as “Big Meech,” and his brother Terry “Southwest T” were the ringleaders of Black Mafia Family (BMF); a drug trafficking ring. Atlanta was the hub of the distribution. However, they also ventured into the hip hop scene through their company BMF Entertainment. Owing to their presence in Atlanta, Meech has often been shouted out by those connected with the Atlanta scene, especially Jeezy, notably by 2 Chainz on his eponymous song “Big Meech Era”, and by Rick Ross on one of his biggest hit “B.M.F.”\n\nOffset continues this legacy here, pointing out that many falsely claim to have accomplished what Meech did.", "annotation_id": "12221493", "url": "https://genius.com/12221493", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "At 17, I wanted everything that was in store At 23, I bought it all just to make sure, yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["At 17, I wanted everything that was in store", "At 23, I bought it all just to make sure, yeah, yeah", "Everything just in case"], "meaning": "Drake experienced a meteoric rise in popularity when he was only 23 due to his signing with Young Money Entertainment and the release of his 3rd mixtape, So Far Gone.\n\nHis sudden rise in the rap game brought him from wanting to buy everything to being able to purchase whatever he desires and then some.", "annotation_id": "12219412", "url": "https://genius.com/12219412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 145, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "You do the math, nigga On 88 dates (Cash) The take-home was crazy I take home your baby, yeah!", "vault_lines": ["You take the bait (Bait)", "You do the math, nigga", "On 88 dates (Cash)", "The take-home was crazy", "I take home your baby, yeah!"], "meaning": "In 2016, Drake and Future went on the Summer Sixteen Tour, where they participated in a 66 show in 82 days tour across North America. The tour grossed $84 million on 56 shows (due to 10 cancelled stops), making it the highest grossing rap tour of all time.\n\n\n\nWhen he says on ‘88’ dates he’s combining the shows of Summer Sixteen & Boy Meets World, another tour promoting his more recent work, and thus combining the profits, which were clearly enormous.", "annotation_id": "12217618", "url": "https://genius.com/12217618", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "I’m a huncho, I’m the boss My set takin’ off", "vault_lines": ["I'm a huncho, I'm the boss", "My set takin' off"], "meaning": "Being the leader & co-founder of OVO and one of the leaders of the new school, Drake’s claim as a boss isn’t far fetched.\n\nThese lines also reference the rap group Migos, which consists of Quavo, Takeoff, and Offset, who is featured alongside Drake on this song.\n\n“Huncho” is one of Quavo’s nicknames, while “my set takin' off” references both Takeoff and Offset. Drake has worked with Migos as a whole on “Versace” and “Trophies (Remix)”, and with Quavo individually on “Portland.”\n\nhttp://img2-ak.lst.fm/i/u/arO/1e6da2451b7f4e60bccde76b9c7d40d4", "annotation_id": "12215101", "url": "https://genius.com/12215101", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Draco by me ’cause I keep it in reach", "vault_lines": ["Draco by me 'cause I keep it in reach"], "meaning": "A “Draco” is a smaller version of the AK-47 assualt rifle.\n\n\n\nThis weapon is often talked about in hip-hop by artists like Soulja Boy on his song “Beef,” and Future on his song “Draco.”", "annotation_id": "12215082", "url": "https://genius.com/12215082", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Billboard where I play They welcome me, I overstay", "vault_lines": ["Billboard where I play", "They welcome me, I overstay"], "meaning": "The Billboard charts became a playground for Drake; from his Hot 100 debut in May 2009, Drizzy remained in the charts for a record  eight uninterrupted years, until he dropped out in the fourth week of August 2017. He came back with a debut number 1 single, “God’s Plan”, which was replaced by another debut single (a Billboard first) “Nice for What” in 2018.", "annotation_id": "12215063", "url": "https://genius.com/12215063", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Young Metro on bass (Bass)", "vault_lines": ["Young Metro on bass (Bass)"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out the track’s producer, Metro Boomin. Metro has been a long time collaborator of Drake’s and is the main producer of Drizzy’s close friend and affiliate, Future. He produced most of the tracks on their collaborated album, What A Time To Be Alive.\n\nDrake has previously shouted out Metro Boomin, along with DJ Esco, on the track “Digital Dash” which Metro also produced, rapping:\n\nEsco and Boomin, they got it on smash", "annotation_id": "12215044", "url": "https://genius.com/12215044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "Polo used to be Lacoste Water used to be the tap, nigga, not the Voss Damn, things changed (Changed)", "vault_lines": ["Polo used to be Lacoste", "Water used to be the tap, nigga, not the Voss", "Damn, things changed (Changed)"], "meaning": "Lacoste is a upper-middle class clothing and accessory brand. In 2011’s “Amen” video, Drake can be seen wearing a Lacoste polo, but at the 2017 Billboard Music Awards, he wore a TOM FORD polo to the show.\n\n\n\nSimilarly, Drake now drinks VOSS Water instead of water from the tap. While VOSS isn’t extraordinarily expensive, drinking it regularly instead of tap water is a signifier that Drake’s lifestyle has changed from his humble beginnings.", "annotation_id": "12215043", "url": "https://genius.com/12215043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Complaints", "line": "I wanna be like Muhammad Ali (Hey) Hang on the ropes and I beat you to sleep (Hey)", "vault_lines": ["I wanna be like Muhammad Ali (Hey)", "Hang on the ropes and I beat you to sleep (Hey)"], "meaning": "Muhammad Ali was a professional boxer and social activist. He is widely considered one of the greatest boxers of all time.\n\nOffset is referencing Ali’s most famous fight— Rumble in the Jungle. During the fight, Ali used a technique called “Rope-A-Dope,” where he stayed on the ropes and let his opponent, George Foreman, punch him and get tired before knocking him out.", "annotation_id": "12215029", "url": "https://genius.com/12215029", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Poppin Bottles", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Ti-poppin-bottles-lyrics", "genius_title": "Poppin Bottles", "genius_id": 2274, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Poppin Bottles", "line": "Man we start with straight shots, then get the bottle popping", "vault_lines": ["Man we start with straight shots, then get the bottle popping"], "meaning": "Drake references the hook of his mentors Birdman & Lil Wayne’s song Pop Bottles, which itself is a sample of Jadakiss’s Put Ya Hands Up", "annotation_id": "38476605", "url": "https://genius.com/38476605", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Poppin Bottles", "line": "[Verse 3: T.I.]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse is a reuse of T.I.’s 2nd Verse from his unreleased song “At It All Night” made back in 2009 with the last bar changed from “Double that” to “Triple that”.", "annotation_id": "32757163", "url": "https://genius.com/32757163", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Poppin Bottles", "line": "You with a lot of dudes, that’s that Elton John shit", "vault_lines": ["You with a lot of dudes, that’s that Elton John shit"], "meaning": "Drake refers to celebrity and Music Icon Elton John in this line. John announced he was bisexual in 1976 and has been openly gay since 1988.\n\nBy being with a lot of dudes, Drake is suspecting that the male he is referring to is gay just like Elton John. This is further reinforced in the next line where drake mentions “to each his own”, which is an idiom used to say that other people are free to like different things", "annotation_id": "24873085", "url": "https://genius.com/24873085", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Poppin Bottles", "line": "We be working all night, telethon shit", "vault_lines": ["We be working all night, telethon shit"], "meaning": "A telethon’s a fundraising event that lasts many hours or even days. Drake here’s expressing that not only does he ball hard, he works hard too.", "annotation_id": "68507", "url": "https://genius.com/68507", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Poppin Bottles", "line": "Me and Weezy run this shit so bring me one soda and vodka And a Fiji for my nigga cause the police probably watching Man, probation is a bitch but going back is not an option", "vault_lines": ["Me and Weezy run this shit so bring me one soda and vodka", "And a Fiji for my nigga cause the police probably watching", "Man, probation is a bitch but going back is not an option"], "meaning": "The vodka and soda is for Drake and the Fiji water is for Lil Wayne who was just released from prison and could not have any alcohol as a requirement of his three-year probation.", "annotation_id": "63711", "url": "https://genius.com/63711", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Poppin Bottles", "line": "The tag team back bitch, boom shakalaka", "vault_lines": ["The tag team back bitch, boom shakalaka"], "meaning": "A reference to Rap group Tag Team whose 90’s hit “Whoomp! (There It Is)” featured the chant “boom shakalaka” in its lyrics.\n\nDrake’s “tag team” partner is Lil Wayne. Their collaborative tracks have been highly praised, and Wayne had just been released from prison at the time.\n\nACCEPTED SUGGESTION: It could also reference the video game NBA Jam because its a 2 on 2 basketball, the same number of people required in a tag team. Also, the video game is notorious for the “boom shakalaka” phrase.", "annotation_id": "63709", "url": "https://genius.com/63709", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "status": "done", "targets": 28, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Nicki-minaj-drake-and-lil-wayne-no-frauds-lyrics", "genius_title": "No Frauds", "genius_id": 3017997, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Ayo, throw your wrist-es up, all my bitches up These niggas is pussier than what all them dicks is up", "vault_lines": ["Ayo, throw your wrist-es up, all my bitches up", "These niggas is pussier than what all them dicks is up"], "meaning": "The correct wording is, “These niggas is pussier than what all them dicks is up,” without the question mark.\n\nAfter “ShETHER” released, Nicki posted videos with Future, riding in a Rolls Royce Dawn and throwing up her wrist to floss an expensive watch.\n\n“All my bitches up” means that all of her female friends are bosses and financially successful. Nicki is stressing that numbers matter.", "annotation_id": "11509695", "url": "https://genius.com/11509695", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "M-M-M-Murda", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Canadian producer and OVO collaborator Murda Beatz produced this song with the assistance of German production duo CuBeatz.\n\nTo make his presence known, Murda’s producer tag “M-M-Murda” starts off the track letting the world know who’s behind the boards.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/murdabeatz_/status/839244983249092608", "annotation_id": "11491039", "url": "https://genius.com/11491039", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "I don’t need no drama, I just need some nana", "vault_lines": ["I don't need no fraud (I don't need no)", "I don't need no (I don't need no) drama when you call (Call my line)", "I don't need no fake", "'Cause I don't need no fraud", "I don't need no drama, I just need some nana", "I don't need no lies"], "meaning": "“Nana” is a modification of the word “punani,” which is slang for vagina. Weezy isn’t interested in a relationship or the drama that comes with it; he just wants sex.", "annotation_id": "11489561", "url": "https://genius.com/11489561", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "I really gotta ease off the wine Which is funny, because I just saw Nick’ the other day And she keeps getting finer over time", "vault_lines": ["I really gotta ease off the wine", "Which is funny, because I just saw Nick’ the other day", "And she keeps getting finer over time"], "meaning": "Wine gets better as it ages because tannins—groups of molecules that come from grape stems and seeds—chemically react with the wine as it matures. Mature tannins cause the taste to linger longer in your mouth. Drake thinks Nicki becomes more attractive as she gets older, just like wine.", "annotation_id": "11488670", "url": "https://genius.com/11488670", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "That’s why Jay ain’t clear his verse for your album", "vault_lines": ["That's why Jay ain't clear his verse for your album"], "meaning": "Here, Nicki refers to Jay-Z’s “All the Way Up (Remix)” which did not appear on Remy Ma and Fat Joe’s album Plata O Plomo because Jay didn’t do the necessary paperwork to allow it.\n\nNicki concludes that Hov is only willing to do a single with her because he knows it will do well, but he doesn’t want to be on her album since he knows it won’t sell as much as the single.", "annotation_id": "11487904", "url": "https://genius.com/11487904", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Drake a headliner, and Nicki, she get finer, word", "vault_lines": ["Drake a headliner, and Nicki, she get finer, word"], "meaning": "Since Drake’s first hit single in 2009, “Best I Ever Had,” he’s grown to become one of the biggest artists in the world and has been a headliner of many shows since. This may also be a reference to his 2011 song “Headlines.”\n\nHe also calls back to Drake’s earlier line about Nicki looking better with age like a fine wine.", "annotation_id": "11487214", "url": "https://genius.com/11487214", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "I got before and after pictures of your surgery Rah took you to her doc, but you don’t look like Rah Left the operating table, still look like ”nah”, eh", "vault_lines": ["I got before and after pictures of your surgery", "Rah took you to her doc, but you don't look like Rah", "Left the operating table, still look like \"nah\", eh"], "meaning": "On “ShETHER,” Remy claimed Nicki had plastic surgery to enlarge her butt:\n\nBefore the butt job, you was a Spongebob\n\nAfter releasing her second Nicki diss track “Another One,” Remy posted a racy picture of a young Nicki Minaj on her Instagram, again accusing her of getting cosmetic surgery.\n\nSince then, many have provided photos which clearly show a change in Remy’s breast size. Nicki claps back by claiming she also has incriminating pictures of Remy.\n\nRashidah “Rah” Ali is Remy Ma’s longtime friend who appeared on season six of Love & Hip Hop with her. In late 2016, rumors surfaced that Rah removed two of her ribs to make her waist smaller.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/6smBLlwVsY/\n\nThe last line references Gucci Mane’s track “Make Love” which Nicki was featured on. On the song, Nicki raps:\n\nI’m a yes and these bitches is a bunch of nahs\n\nThis seemingly confirms that it was indeed Remy Ma who Nicki was throwing subliminal shots at before.", "annotation_id": "11485630", "url": "https://genius.com/11485630", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Uh, I am not a fraud, YM, I’m the god They don’t make ’em like me no more, man, I’m a dinosaur", "vault_lines": ["I don't need no fraud (I don't need no)", "I don't need no fake", "Uh, I am not a fraud, YM, I'm the god", "They don't make 'em like me no more, man, I'm a dinosaur", "'Cause I don't need no fraud", "I don't need no lies"], "meaning": "Since 2015, Lil Wayne and Birdman, his former best friend and father figure, have been in an ongoing lawsuit. At first, Wayne was furious about the delay of The Carter V, which caused him to publicly blame Birdman and Cash Money, Birdman’s label, on Twitter. Then in January 15 of that same year, Wayne filed a $51 million lawsuit again Cash Money over royalties.\n\nSince the lawsuit, Lil Wayne hasn’t classified himself as a member of Cash Money, but instead as a member of Young Money, a label he started. As the founder of Young Money, he helped sign Drake and Nicki Minaj. He also refers to himself as “the god” since he “created” the brand just like God created the universe.\n\n\n\nReferring back to the god verse, he claims God doesn’t make people like him anymore (best rapper alive); just like when God stopped “creating” the dinosaurs. In reality, dinosaurs were most likely extinct due to a meteor, climate change, and lack of food.", "annotation_id": "11485310", "url": "https://genius.com/11485310", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "I took the price (Yeah) and lift that bitch up I took the ice (Yeah), let me lift my wrist up I took the price and lift that bitch up I took the ice (Yeah), let me lift my wrist up (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I took the ice (Yeah), let me lift my wrist up", "I-I-I took the price and lift that bitch up", "I took the price (Yeah) and lift that bitch up"], "meaning": "To “take the price” means to raise someone in an offer. By responding to Remy’s beef, Nicki believes that she will only bring Remy Ma more into the spotlight, elevating her by association.\n\nSimilarly, “…and lift my wrist up” might mean that Nicki is taking the price and raising it. Or to put it in context, Nicki is responding to the beef with something even better than Remy’s verses. Upping the anti.\n\n“Ice” is slang for diamond-encrusted jewelry.", "annotation_id": "11485299", "url": "https://genius.com/11485299", "state": "accepted", "votes": 151, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Yeah, yeah, never been no fraud Why do niggas that are not involved love to get involved? Why do niggas claim the streets and act just like the law?", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, yeah, never been no fraud", "Why do niggas that are not involved love to get involved?", "Why do niggas claim the streets and act just like the law?"], "meaning": "In 2015, Meek Mill accused Drake of not writing his own raps.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623700698509758464?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\nFollowing the beef, up-and-coming Canadian rapper Mo-G claimed Drake didn’t pay him for his help writing verses. Mo-G also tried to “expose” Drake for covering “These Days,” which went on to become “Things I Forgot To Do.”\n\nDrake has also fought off multiple accusations that he bites from other artists. Whether it be jumping on remixes like “Wanna Know (Remix),” “Versace (Remix),” and “My Way (Remix),” helping give a new sound some time in the spotlight like dancehall and grime, or even helping new artists like Roy Woods and OB O'Brien make hits, critics continue to claim he’s a “wave rider.”", "annotation_id": "11484785", "url": "https://genius.com/11484785", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Feelin’ like Tune-y Montana, Weezy, he get honored", "vault_lines": ["Feelin' like Tune-y Montana, Weezy, he get honored"], "meaning": "Tony Montana is the protagonist of the 1983 movie Scarface. “Lil' Tunechi” is one of Lil Wayne’s pseudonyms; he blends his name into Montana’s to compare himself to the fictional drug lord.", "annotation_id": "11484348", "url": "https://genius.com/11484348", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "What the fuck is this bitch inhalin’?", "vault_lines": ["What the fuck is this bitch inhalin'?"], "meaning": "Nicki thinks Remy must be on drugs to believe she’s a better rapper than Nicki. This is likely a reply to Remy’s similar drug diss on “shETHER”:\n\nThey gassin' you up, but you been on E, though\n“Pills and Potions,” yep, you been on E, hoe", "annotation_id": "11483874", "url": "https://genius.com/11483874", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Lil Boogie down basic bitch thinkin’ she back", "vault_lines": ["Lil Boogie down basic bitch thinkin' she back"], "meaning": "Boogie Down Bronx is a nickname for The Bronx, New York City’s northernmost borough. It’s called that because it’s thought to be the birthplace of hip-hop. Remy Ma hails from the Castle Hill projects in South Bronx.\n\nThis is likely a response to Remy’s Boogie Down Bronx mention on “ShETHER”:\n\nBoogie Down Bronx, I come out of the Boogie", "annotation_id": "11483493", "url": "https://genius.com/11483493", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "They say numbers don’t matter, but when they discussin’ the kings They turn around and say LeBron ain’t got six rings", "vault_lines": ["They say numbers don't matter, but when they discussin' the kings", "They turn around and say LeBron ain't got six rings"], "meaning": "On “shETHER,” Remy urged Nicki to “stop talkin' numbers.” Nicki responded by mocking the abysmal performance of Remy’s collaboration album with Fat Joe, Plata O Plomo, on Instagram:\n\n\n\nThe Breakfast Club considered this response wack and made the argument that numbers don’t matter in a rap battle and that this beef has nothing to do with album sales. Nicki thinks numbers do matter, and that she’s the better artist because she’s been far more successful; Nicki has 25 times more Hot 100 solo placements, 21 times more RIAA certifications, and five times more Grammy nominations than Remy.\n\nOne of the most debated topics in NBA history is whether Michael Jordan or LeBron James is the greatest player of all time. Nicki claims Jordan’s the better player because he has six championship rings (1991-1993, 1996-1998) that he won with the Chicago Bulls, which is double LeBron’s three championship rings (2012, 2013, 2016). She likens Jordan’s by-the-numbers superiority to James to her by-the-numbers superiority to Remy.", "annotation_id": "11483459", "url": "https://genius.com/11483459", "state": "accepted", "votes": 188, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "I am the generous queen, ask Ms. Ellen (Uh)", "vault_lines": ["I am the generous queen, ask Ms. Ellen (Uh)"], "meaning": "“The generous” is a play on Ellen DeGeneres' last name. Ellen is a huge fan of Nicki and even dressed up like her for Halloween once. Nicki has appeared on her show eight times since her first appearance on January 10, 2011.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=na5yOfZJmPQ", "annotation_id": "11483427", "url": "https://genius.com/11483427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 158, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "I woulda helped you out that pit you fell in", "vault_lines": ["I woulda helped you out that pit you fell in"], "meaning": "In 2008, Remy was tried and convicted for shooting her ex-friend the previous year. She spent six years in jail.\n\nNicki might have been Remy’s friend and helped her out during the hard times she faced, but any chance of that happening was gone after Remy released “ShETHER” and “Another One.” “Fell in” is a homophone of “felon.”", "annotation_id": "11483404", "url": "https://genius.com/11483404", "state": "accepted", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Man, I know you niggas saw this one comin’", "vault_lines": ["Man, I know you niggas saw this one comin'"], "meaning": "This ‘YMCMB reunion’ with Drake, Lil Wayne and Nicki Minaj has presumably been in the works since the trio recently met up overseas.\n\nWith the fanfare leading up to this collaboration and the hype behind this long-awaited track, it’s safe to say that the “rap game” saw a track of this proportion coming from a mile away.\n\n\n\nFurthermore, this line could also serve to add onto the several jabs at Meek Mill taken throughout the song:\nIn the previous line, Drake talks about how the Young Money crew discussed the 2015 beef with Meek during their overseas meet-up. A foreboding Drake + Nicki track was inevitable after the couple’s breakup.", "annotation_id": "11483394", "url": "https://genius.com/11483394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Sometimes I ask God, “Man, why him?” Like the team that I would never leave behind", "vault_lines": ["Sometimes I ask God, \"Man, why him?\"", "Like the team that I would never leave behind"], "meaning": "Drake is most likely referencing Meek Mill here. He was friends with Meek prior to their popularized 2015 beef, working together on “Amen” and “R.I.C.O.”, and he wonders why it had to be Meek, Nicki’s boyfriend at the time, that came for him.\n\nHe also pronounces “why him” as if he’s saying “YM”, an acronym for his record label Young Money that he pledges loyalty to.", "annotation_id": "11483357", "url": "https://genius.com/11483357", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "All my real niggas down to ride, throw your GSXRs up", "vault_lines": ["All my real niggas down to ride, throw your GSXRs up"], "meaning": "Suzuki’s GSX-R series of sports bikes was introduced in 1984 and remains popular today. While the bike brand is phonetically pronounced by calling out each letter individually (G-S-X-R), Nicki uses a popular slang term when she says “Jix-er.”\n\nNicki’s fellow Queens, NY rapper 50 Cent also shouted out the line of bikes on “Ghetto Qu'ran” when he rapped:\n\nRumors in the hood was Gus was snitching\nI ain’t believe that, pa, he helped me cop my first GSXR\n\nThe line might also be a reference to Nicki’s former boyfriend Meek Mill, who is a known bike enthusiast.", "annotation_id": "11483350", "url": "https://genius.com/11483350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Sheneneh, you a fraud committin’ perjury", "vault_lines": ["Sheneneh, you a fraud committin' perjury"], "meaning": "Sheneneh was a character played by Martin Lawrence in his classic 1990s sitcom Martin.\n\nThough Lawrence wore a wig and make-up while playing Sheneneh, the character still looked manly. Comparing Remy to Sheneneh is no compliment.\n\nCoincidentally, there’s a 1997 episode of the show titled Power to the People’s Court where Sheneneh sues Tommy, lies about being injured, and is granted only $100 after Tommy bumps into her car.", "annotation_id": "11483343", "url": "https://genius.com/11483343", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "My net worth sound like grrr But they don’t pay in cash", "vault_lines": ["My net worth sound like grrr", "But they don't pay in cash"], "meaning": "Drake used a similar grrrrrr vocal sound when he featured on DJ Khaled’s anthem “No New Friends”:\n\nMoney counter go brrrr when you sellin' out the Garden\n\nDrake may be suggesting that his net worth sounds like a money counter working overtime.\n\nHis most prominent business deal is a $19 million contract with Apple.", "annotation_id": "11483308", "url": "https://genius.com/11483308", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Niggas see me like, “What up, Killa?” Man, please stop bringin’ up my past I’d really like to leave that behind", "vault_lines": ["Niggas see me like, \"What up, Killa?\"", "Man, please stop bringin' up my past", "I'd really like to leave that behind"], "meaning": "This is an interpolation of Lil Wayne’s 2008 track “Mr. Carter”:\n\nThey say, ‘What up, Killa?’\nMan, stop bringin' up my past!\n\nDrake’s also referencing his newfound “killer” persona. Early in his career, Drake rapped “Diss me and you’ll never hear a reply for it,” but on “Back to Back,” “Two Birds One Stone,” and “4PM in Calabasas” he responded to the disses Meek Mill, Pusha T, and Joe Budden sent at him.\n\nIn Drake’s recent DJ Semtex interview, he expressed similar thoughts about moving on from his past beefs.", "annotation_id": "11483255", "url": "https://genius.com/11483255", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "I never signed a 360, bitch, you wild dumb", "vault_lines": ["I never signed a 360, bitch, you wild dumb"], "meaning": "Remy claimed that Nicki signed a 360 deal on “shETHER”:\n\nAnd stop talkin' numbers, you signed a 360 deal\nThrough Young Money, through Cash Money, through Republic\nWhich means your money go through five niggas before you touch it\n\nA 360 deal occurs when a company agrees to provide support for an artist in exchange for a portion of the artist’s earnings. When Nicki signed to Young Money, however, her deal ensured she retained and owned all of her 360 rights. Her money isn’t an issue anyways—she pulled in $20.5 million in 2016.", "annotation_id": "11483202", "url": "https://genius.com/11483202", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "I ain’t in the projects, but all my bricks is up (Rrr) You can’t beat Pablo if your work ain’t sellin’", "vault_lines": ["I ain't in the projects, but all my bricks is up (Rrr)", "You can't beat Pablo if your work ain't sellin'"], "meaning": "Pablo Escobar was an infamous and influential Colombian drug lord. The juxtaposition of “Pablo” with “my bricks is up” suggests Escobar over other famous Pablos, since “brick” is slang for a kilogram of cocaine, which Escobar traded.\n\nNas often called himself “Escobar,” and his song “Ether” was on Columbia Records; the real Pablo was Colombian. Remy Ma fashioned “ShETHER” after “Ether,” and also referenced Escobar multiple times on her collaborative album with Fat Joe Plata O Plomo, which was inspired by the drug kingpin. “Work” is slang for drugs, but Nicki’s using it as a metaphor for music; she claims Remy can’t be like Nas because her music just isn’t selling.", "annotation_id": "11483156", "url": "https://genius.com/11483156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 181, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Tried to drop ”Another One”, you was itchin’ to scrap You exposed your ghostwriter, now you wish it was scrapped", "vault_lines": ["Tried to drop \"Another One\", you was itchin' to scrap", "You exposed your ghostwriter, now you wish it was scrapped"], "meaning": "“Another One” is Remy’s second Nicki diss track, which she released a week after “ShETHER”. “Scrap” means “fight,” but it can also mean “discard.” Since Remy released “Another One” without waiting for Nicki to respond to “ShETHER,” Nicki claims Remy was desperate to fight with her, but she should regret ever making the tracks in the first place.\n\nOn “ShEther”, Remy accused Nicki of ghostwriting:\n\nGot your ghostwriters back, so you think you lit\n\nIt has been speculated that Papoose ghostwrote “ShETHER,” since Remy couldn’t remember the lyrics during a live performance. Nicki points out the irony of Remy calling her out for ghostwriting when she may have used a ghostwriter herself.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BQ-_junjs3P/?taken-by=ryandaviscomedy&hl=en", "annotation_id": "11483146", "url": "https://genius.com/11483146", "state": "accepted", "votes": 194, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Heard your pussy on yuck, I guess you needed a Pap", "vault_lines": ["Heard your pussy on yuck, I guess you needed a Pap"], "meaning": "A “Pap” stands for “Pap smear,” which is a screening procedure for cervical cancer. The test itself involves collection of a sample of cells from a woman’s cervix.\nIf her vagina has serious issues like that, it’s what Wayne said on “Yuck”\n\n Let’s fuck\nShe said she on her period, I said yuck\n\nShe made fun of Remy having brought home “prison pussy”, and not being medically cleared – inferring she was someone’s “bitch” while imprisoned. It’s a response to this line:\n\nTo let you know, real bitches never lie on their pussy\n\n“Pap” is short for Papoose, Remy husband, while “Poose” sounds like “Puss” in pussy, also can be heard as “Poo”.\nHe therefore is one himself, for writing her stuff, and intervening in a woman’s fight.\nShe’s insinuating Pap, smeared his signature to fix Remy’s “shitty” bars on her pad.\nEither way, Remy is hiding someone’s D.N.A. behind bars so nobody notices.", "annotation_id": "11483091", "url": "https://genius.com/11483091", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "Back to back, oh, you mean back to wack? ”Back to Back”? Me and Drizzy laughed at that (Haha)", "vault_lines": ["Back to back, oh, you mean back to wack?", "\"Back to Back\"? Me and Drizzy laughed at that (Haha)"], "meaning": "Drake released the Meek Mill diss track “Back to Back” in the summer of 2015. On “Another One,” Remy purposely emulated “Back to Back” by mirroring its themes and interpolating some of its lyrics. Drizzy can also be heard laughing in the background during this line.\n\nRemy Ma released “ShETHER”“ and “Another One” back to back; \"Another One” came out just a week after “ShETHER.”", "annotation_id": "11483087", "url": "https://genius.com/11483087", "state": "accepted", "votes": 173, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Frauds", "line": "What type of bum bitch shoot a friend over a rack? What type of mother leave her one son over a stack?", "vault_lines": ["What type of bum bitch shoot a friend over a rack?", "What type of mother leave her one son over a stack?"], "meaning": "In 2007, Remy Ma shot her ex-friend because she believed she had stolen $3,000 from her. After being found guilty on two charges of first-degree assault in March 2008, Remy served six years in prison.\n\nWhile in jail, she missed out on raising her son Jayson, which she mentioned on “shETHER”:\n\nSnatched me from my son, braids to the back, state green", "annotation_id": "11483081", "url": "https://genius.com/11483081", "state": "accepted", "votes": 195, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Put It Down", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Bun-b-put-it-down-lyrics", "genius_title": "Put It Down", "genius_id": 1111, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Put It Down", "line": "Thank Me Later, man, a million copies shipped and gone", "vault_lines": ["Thank Me Later, man, a million copies shipped and gone"], "meaning": "Drake’s debut album was a massive success, selling 447,000 copies and going #1 on Billboard in the 1st week alone", "annotation_id": "26369505", "url": "https://genius.com/26369505", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Put It Down", "line": "Drizzy Drake, mane—Young Sweet Jones", "vault_lines": ["Drizzy Drake, mane—Young Sweet Jones"], "meaning": "Young Sweet Jones is one of Drake’s earliest releases, a mixtape put out in 2010", "annotation_id": "26369499", "url": "https://genius.com/26369499", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Put It Down", "line": "Can’t take your eyes off me (Off me) Like when you watch The Wire (Wire)", "vault_lines": ["Can't take your eyes off me (Off me)", "Like when you watch The Wire (Wire)"], "meaning": "The Wire is widely (and rightly) regarded as one of the greatest shows of all time, so Bun’s got that right, you’re not going to be doing much blinking if you’re watching this modern day masterpiece.\n\n\n\nOn another level though, the titular Wire is a reference to telephone tapping; in the show Freamon and Prez (among others at various times) have to keep their eye on the tap at all times to make sure they don’t miss anything pertinent.\n\n\n\nAs a side note, Chamillionaire’s Picture Perfect, featuring Bun B was featured in episode nine of season four, Know Your Place.", "annotation_id": "1618750", "url": "https://genius.com/1618750", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Put It Down", "line": "And when it come to bein’ trill, I’m the litmus test", "vault_lines": ["And when it come to bein' trill, I'm the litmus test"], "meaning": "Bun B is the measuring stick used to determine how trill (true and real) someone is, which means that he is the trillest.\n\nA litmus test is also used to determine pH labels (acid and alkaline materials), and while phat isn’t exactly a term widely used anymore – it’s still pretty clever.\n\nAlso, the ends of the pH scale are red (acidic) and blue (alkaline). Red and blue are also the colours of North and South Houston, where Bun B is from.", "annotation_id": "228855", "url": "https://genius.com/228855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
