{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Spider and Jack", "vault_lines": ["In lace, I know you lovin' Spider and Jack"], "meaning": "“King Spider” is a name attributed to Young Thug, while “Cactus Jack” is a name associated with Travis Scott.", "annotation_id": "28634613", "url": "https://genius.com/28634613", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "The SVR still livin’ deranged (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["The SVR still livin' deranged (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Referring to the Range Rover Sport SVR (Special Vehicle Rating), although there is also a Jaguar F-PACE SVR.\n\n\n\nAlso a play on words: ‘Deranged’ relating to ‘Range.’", "annotation_id": "26609494", "url": "https://genius.com/26609494", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Pull up too deep like a fuckin’ parade", "vault_lines": ["Pull up too deep like a fuckin' parade"], "meaning": "“Deep” means that you have a bunch of people with you.\nSo when thug pulls up he comes with a gang/large group of people. He compares the amount of people he has with him, to the amount of people at a parade.", "annotation_id": "24260841", "url": "https://genius.com/24260841", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "I got a ten-year-old mink still ain’t fuzzy", "vault_lines": ["I got a ten-year-old mink still ain't fuzzy"], "meaning": "Thugger has always had a penchant for wearing extravagant outfits, specifically an obsession with huge, mink fur coats, as evidenced in the music video for 2018’s “Mink Flow,” a collaboration with Future.\n\nThere’s no doubt that Thug has an unfuzzed, 10-year-old mink laying around somewhere, protected amongst his extensive selection of exotic clothing.", "annotation_id": "24203579", "url": "https://genius.com/24203579", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Texas ranch so big, that shit is perfect for the dippin’", "vault_lines": ["Texas ranch so big, that shit is perfect for the dippin'"], "meaning": "Drake is known to be a big fan of Houston, a city in Texas, and even mentions it repeatedly in his music, most recently on “N 2 Deep” from his 2021 album Certified Lover Boy. He frequents the city almost every year to meet up with friends, most notably after his Billboard historical achievement of landing 3 songs in the Top 3 in 2021. It’s also important to note that Travis Scott, one of Drake’s frequent collaborators and a feature on this song, is from Houston.\n\nDrake uses “Ranch” as a double entendre to boast getaways to his estate found in Texas, which includes a swimming pool, similar to how people “dip” their food in Ranch. “Ranch” is a popular condiment used most commonly for salad dressings and “dipping” fries in.", "annotation_id": "24185119", "url": "https://genius.com/24185119", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Ridin’ in the Porsche, rockin’ Tom Ford I ball at Barneys, new Christian Dior I took the jet to the New York store", "vault_lines": ["Ride in the Porsche, rockin' Tom Ford", "Ridin' in the Porsche, rockin' Tom Ford", "I ball at Barneys, new Christian Dior", "I took the jet to the New York store"], "meaning": "In these lines, Thug asserts his fondness for the finer things in life, namely luxury automobile manufacturer Porsche, who Thug rapped about on his 2018 track “Chanel (Go Get It),” rapping:\n\nThe Porsche Cayenne, she gettin' it\nHop out the coupe, she get it\n\n\n\nFurther, Thug mentions Barneys New York, a chain of luxury department stores founded in New York. Known for stocking a variety of designer fashion brands, including Dior and Tom Ford, Barneys filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy in August 2019, ceasing operations in February 2020. Thug has rapped about shopping at Barneys several times, most recently on “Love You More,” rapping:\n\nWalking around with a shopping cart, we tearing down Barneys\n\n\n\nBarneys' now-closed flagship store on 60th Street in Manhattan, New York.", "annotation_id": "24181891", "url": "https://genius.com/24181891", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Wheezy outta here", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is one of the producer tags used by Wesley Tyler Glass, popularly known as Wheezy. Wheezy has produced for many artists such as Lil Baby, Meek Mill, Young Thug, and many other artists.\n\nYoung Thug and Wheezy have many officially released collaborations such as “Hot,” “Constantly Hating,” and “Feel it.”\n\n\n\nWheezy and Young Thug pictured together", "annotation_id": "24180797", "url": "https://genius.com/24180797", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Ride in the Porsche, rockin’ Tom Ford", "vault_lines": ["Ride in the Porsche, rockin' Tom Ford", "Ridin' in the Porsche, rockin' Tom Ford"], "meaning": "Drake harkens back to Thug’s lyrics from earlier in the track, asserting his fondness for the finer things in life.\n\nDrake has a penchant for luxury brand Tom Ford, notably naming his 2013 song “Tuscan Leather” after the brand’s cologne of the same name and wearing Tom Ford clothes on numerous occasions. In return, Tom Ford released a line of lipstick inspired by Drake in 2015.\n\n\n\nDrake in the Tom Ford dark green Cashmere Suede Western Jacket at his 31st birthday celebration in Los Angeles.", "annotation_id": "24176830", "url": "https://genius.com/24176830", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "My career ’bout to blast like bubbly", "vault_lines": ["My career 'bout to blast like bubbly"], "meaning": "Despite having spent 10 years in the rap game, bursting onto the scene with his I Came from Nothing mixtape series in the early 2010s, achieving critical and commercial success, and influencing a plethora of artists along the way, Thug still feels that he is yet to reach the pinnacle of his career.\n\nThug uses a simplistic yet effective simile, comparing his inbound success to a bubbly beverage, such as champagne or soda, which will “blast” towards the ceiling upon being shaken.\n\n\n\nThug rapped a similar line on his 2017 song “Do U Love Me,” rapping:\n\nDrankin' bubbly, we get thuggish and rugged", "annotation_id": "24176799", "url": "https://genius.com/24176799", "state": "pending", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "I took the jet to the New York store Park at the regular airport", "vault_lines": ["Park at the regular airport", "I took the jet to the New York store"], "meaning": "Drake flies in style as he owns a heavily modified Boeing 767-300F commercial airliner, dubbed “Air Drake,” complete with multiple bedrooms, a private office, and a living room. Drake acquired the jet through his partnership with Cargojet, Canada’s largest cargo airline.\n\n\n\nDrake with Ajay Virmani, the president and CEO of Cargojet, circa 2019.\n\nWorth an estimated $242 million, the plane is complete with a custom exterior designed by Louis Vuitton’s creative director Virgil Abloh.", "annotation_id": "24176767", "url": "https://genius.com/24176767", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Doin’ this shit just to show we get down in the 6, man, it’s bigger than me", "vault_lines": ["Doin' this shit just to show we get down in the 6, man, it's bigger than me"], "meaning": "Drake asserts that his efforts to put his hometown of Toronto—dubbed “the 6ix” by Drake—“on the map” were not only for his own benefit but for the benefit of up and coming artists from the city, giving credibility to its hip-hop scene.\n\nSince blowing up and starting his OVO Sound record label, Drake has shown love to several artists from the city, including, but not limited to, PartyNextDoor, Baka Not Nice, and Smiley amongst others.", "annotation_id": "24176746", "url": "https://genius.com/24176746", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "I’m a young boy too T’d", "vault_lines": ["I'm a young boy too T'd"], "meaning": "Drake, who feels that he is still a “young boy” despite being 34 years old as of this song’s release, is “T’d up,” meaning he is “turnt up” or intoxicated, likely under the influence of drugs and/or alcohol.\n\nThis phrase is popular within hip-hop and was popularised by Rae Sremmurd on their 2018 track “T’d Up.\"", "annotation_id": "24176679", "url": "https://genius.com/24176679", "state": "pending", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Eric came through with a little entertainment (It’s lit)", "vault_lines": ["Eric came through with a little entertainment (It's lit)"], "meaning": "Travis Scott could be referring to Houston DJ and radio personality DJ Eric, who’s the host of The Big Tho & DJ Eric show on KBXX 97.9 The Box. This is not the first time that the Houston rapper name-drops Eric—on the March 2016 remix of MadeinTYO’s breakout single “Uber Everywhere,” Travis raps:\n\nWhen we hit V Live, I got that bag (Straight up)\nBig Tho, DJ Eric got that bitch on smash, yeah, yeah, yeah\nPlay that shit that make ‘em dance, yeah", "annotation_id": "24175051", "url": "https://genius.com/24175051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Area 29, got it lookin’ like space Houston, it’s a problem way we bustin’ up faces and shit", "vault_lines": ["Area 29, got it lookin' like space", "Houston, it's a problem way we bustin' up faces and shit"], "meaning": "In this line Travis Scott references the famous saying “Houston, we have a problem” although the famous quote is incorrect, the actual sentence that was said is “Houston, we’ve had a problem here”.\n\nThe saying originated from Apollo 13’s space expedition in 1970 when Apollo 13 astronaut Jack Swigert contacted the NASA Mission Control Center when they discovered the explosion that crippled their spacecraft.", "annotation_id": "24175038", "url": "https://genius.com/24175038", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "I came with the snakes, no squids (Straight up) I broke out the game with a 456", "vault_lines": ["I came with the snakes, no squids (Straight up)", "I broke out the game with a 456"], "meaning": "Travis mentions YSL members Young Thug and Gunna, who often describe themselves as “snakes” in their music. He does so whilst referencing the viral sensation that is South Korean mini-series Squid Game, in which 456 players compete in a series of challenges for a chance to win a ₩45.6 billion prize. In September 2021, Travis shared his thoughts on the series via a cryptic Instagram Story.\n\nWhile the challenges in Squid Game require a lot of luck to successfully complete, Travis has not achieved his success through luck. Rather, he has done so by surrounding himself with like-minded individuals, such as Gunna and Thug, who he compares to the show’s lead character, Seong Gi-Hun; contestant number 456. Travis tackled the difficulties of fake friends on Drake’s 2021 song “Fair Trade,” rapping:\n\nWorry, sick, I’m sick of worryin' (Sick, sick)\nIt’s just the world we in, I’d rather bury them\nI’m talkin' fake friends and skeletons (Yeah)\n\nFurther, in mentioning “squids,” Travis may also be referencing Atlanta rapper SahBabii, who is synonymous with the term and uses it frequently in his music. A prominent figure in Atlanta’s underground rap scene, Sah notably collaborated with Thugger on the remix of his breakout hit “Pull Up wit ah Stick” and was co-signed by Drake early in his career.", "annotation_id": "24175005", "url": "https://genius.com/24175005", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Bubbly", "line": "Been on the charts for five hundred weeks, so somebody love when I speak", "vault_lines": ["Been on the charts for five hundred weeks, so somebody love when I speak"], "meaning": "Drizzy boasts of his immense success on the Billboard Hot 100 chart. He holds the record for the most consecutive weeks on the chart with 431 weeks from May 2009 to August 2017, which also made him the first artist to ever stay on the Hot 100 for eight straight years.  The Canadian rapper also holds the following Hot 100 records:\n\n\n\nthe most entries (since March 2020)\n\nthe most top 10 entries (since July 2020)\n\nthe most top 10 debuts in a week (in September 2021)\n\n\n\n\n\nDrake at the 2017 Billboard Music Awards", "annotation_id": "24174681", "url": "https://genius.com/24174681", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
