{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Guns turn you boys into pussies, sex change", "vault_lines": ["Guns turn you boys into pussies, sex change"], "meaning": "Wayne brings some wordplay here:\n“Guns turn you boys into pussies” means the boys are afraid of the guns or turn into wusses. This is similar to the boys turning into girls (literally), hence “sex change.”", "annotation_id": "39658965", "url": "https://genius.com/39658965", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I hit the strip club, and all them bitches find a pole", "vault_lines": ["I hit the strip club, and all them bitches find a pole"], "meaning": "It seems when he comes to the club the women start dancing to attract him.", "annotation_id": "39658942", "url": "https://genius.com/39658942", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Don’t like my women single, I like my chicks in two’s", "vault_lines": ["Don't like my women single, I like my chicks in two's"], "meaning": "Another example of paraprosdokian: the first part of the line suggests Drake likes taken/married women; however, the second part changes the meaning with him not liking one woman alone (he prefers women in twos).", "annotation_id": "39658927", "url": "https://genius.com/39658927", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "We walk the same path, but got on different shoes Live in the same building, but we got different views", "vault_lines": ["Kane is in the building", "We walk the same path, but got on different shoes", "Live in the same building, but we got different views"], "meaning": "Drake suggests that while he exists in the same reality, he can [presumably] afford better kicks and housing (maybe he’s got the penthouse).", "annotation_id": "39658906", "url": "https://genius.com/39658906", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I got a couple cars, I never get to use", "vault_lines": ["I got a couple cars, I never get to use"], "meaning": "Drake employs paraprosdokian, where the start of the line gives the listener the impression that Drake is bragging about having a couple cars. The end of the line flexes much harder, suggesting that Drake’s got a fleet so large that he has cars he doesn’t even get to use.", "annotation_id": "39658870", "url": "https://genius.com/39658870", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Damn where you stumbled at? From where they make Gumbo at", "vault_lines": ["Damn where you stumbled at? From where they make Gumbo at"], "meaning": "Where did Lil Wayne come from? Well, he is from New Orleans, Louisiana, the official hometown of Gumbo, a popular cajun soup dish.", "annotation_id": "20729872", "url": "https://genius.com/20729872", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Motherfuckin’ Birdman Junior, 11th Grade", "vault_lines": ["Motherfuckin' Birdman Junior, 11th Grade"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne used to frequently go by the nickname Birdman Jr, paying homage to his Cash Money label boss and father figure Birdman.\n\nWhen you’re in 11th grade you are called a “junior”", "annotation_id": "20729855", "url": "https://genius.com/20729855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Skinny pants and some Vans", "vault_lines": ["Skinny pants and some Vans"], "meaning": "This was Wayne’s go-to fit in these days, with this album being released in 2010. During this time he was transitioning his persona from gangster to a more rockstar skateboarder type.\n\nSkinny ass pants, fresh pair of Vans\n\nYou can even see him rocking skinny jeans and Vans sneakers on the cover of this album.", "annotation_id": "20729847", "url": "https://genius.com/20729847", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Yeah, and I ain’t a killer but don’t push me", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and I ain't a killer but don't push me"], "meaning": "Likely a reference to the first line in the first verse of Tupac’s “Hail Mary”, lauded as one of the best lines in hip hop history.", "annotation_id": "18664292", "url": "https://genius.com/18664292", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "We on, it’s Young Money motherfucker", "vault_lines": ["We on, it's Young Money motherfucker"], "meaning": "Young Money Entertainment is an American record label founded by Lil Wayne. As of this song’s release, Wayne and Drake were both signed to the label.", "annotation_id": "13203792", "url": "https://genius.com/13203792", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Uh, wake up and smell the pussy", "vault_lines": ["Uh, wake up and smell the pussy"], "meaning": "A play on the phrase “wake up and smell the coffee” which means to become aware of the realities of a situation, however unpleasant. This ties in to the previous line where he plays on the phrase “Life is a bitch”.\n\nOftentimes, the first thing people smell in the morning is coffee. Since Wayne has sex first thing in the morning, the first thing he smells is a girl’s pussy.", "annotation_id": "12970859", "url": "https://genius.com/12970859", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "[Produced by Kane Beatz] [Intro] Kane is in the building", "vault_lines": ["Kane is in the building"], "meaning": "Watch Kane Beatz recreate the trumpets here:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/0smX2t4fojU?t=150", "annotation_id": "10074798", "url": "https://genius.com/10074798", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Yes I’m in the building, you just on the list of guest names", "vault_lines": ["Kane is in the building", "Yes I'm in the building, you just on the list of guest names"], "meaning": "Like Elvis: known for causing panics when he entered public places (hence the catchphrase “Elvis has left the building!”)\n\n\n\nAlso referring to the producer, Kane Beatz, who’s label is called “the building”, at the beginning of a song done by Kane you’ll hear his tag: “Kane is in the building nigga” (or something of the sort).\n\nThe term “in the building” is slang that means that a person has arrived or has achieved. It came from the older slang term “in the house” and progressed to “in the building”", "annotation_id": "63040", "url": "https://genius.com/63040", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "[Verse 1: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse is recycled from the beginning of his Tim Westwood “freestyle,” with a few changes (and a few mishaps).\n\nIn 2015, most of Drake’s verse was used in the opening theme to the HBO series Ballers.", "annotation_id": "44828", "url": "https://genius.com/44828", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I’m with Marley G’s bro", "vault_lines": ["I'm with Marley G's bro"], "meaning": "He’s using ‘Marley G’s Bro\" as a euphemism for Lil’ Wayne.\n\nMarley G is Lil' Wayne’s right hand man (and/or goon) – he does Weezy’s dirty work, Lil Wayne calms Marley on several songs, saying “Marley don’t shoot ‘em”\n\nDrake is asserting that he can ‘fuck with Hollywood Cole’ since he’s with Wayne and Marley G. Hear that, J. Cole?", "annotation_id": "31182", "url": "https://genius.com/31182", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "And you niggas know I rep my gang like Jessie James", "vault_lines": ["And you niggas know I rep my gang like Jessie James"], "meaning": "The outlaw, not the car-customizer", "annotation_id": "31148", "url": "https://genius.com/31148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "President Carter, Young Money Democrat", "vault_lines": ["President Carter, Young Money Democrat"], "meaning": "Wayne’s real name is Dwayne Carter and he’s the President/founder of Young Money Records. Wayne stretches this idea out to a whole song with “President Carter”\n\nPolitically-speaking, he’s a Democrat (just like the REAL “President Carter”)\n\n\n\nDisclaimer: It seems that Wayne is no longer actually President but, as Young Money’s CEO, has appointed Mack Maine to fill this position.", "annotation_id": "30512", "url": "https://genius.com/30512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Fake friends write the wrong answers on the mirror for me", "vault_lines": ["Fake friends write the wrong answers on the mirror for me"], "meaning": "In Slumdog Millionaire, the game show host tries to trick the hero by writing an incorrect answer to the question the hero faces on a bathroom mirror\n\nDrake is saying that he lost touch with his real friends and is surrounded by backstabbers who are trying to cause his downfall (just like the game show host)\n\n\n\nDouble meaning:\nWhen you look in the mirror you see you. So his fake friends told him to do the wrong things to himself.", "annotation_id": "29656", "url": "https://genius.com/29656", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Who else is really trying to fuck with Hollywood Cole", "vault_lines": ["Who else is really trying to fuck with Hollywood Cole"], "meaning": "Many people interpreted this as a shot at J. Cole, but it is not. Both Cole and Drake are using this line from “SpottieOttieDopealiscous”.\n\nThe only reason Cole used it is because the pronunciation of Hollywood COURTS by André 3000 sounded like Hollywood Cole.\n\nDrake is known to idolize 3 Stacks; perhaps this is an homage.", "annotation_id": "29648", "url": "https://genius.com/29648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Flying Holly Grove chicks to my Hollywood shows", "vault_lines": ["Flying Holly Grove chicks to my Hollywood shows"], "meaning": "Drake’s rich enough to fly multiple girls from Lil Wayne’s neighborhood all the way out to Hollywood. Lil Wayne would later play on Hollygrove / Hollywood on 2015’s “HollyWeezy”:\n\nToo Hollygrove to go Hollywood\n\nIn the freestyle version, Drake says “treating Birmingham just like my Hollywood shows” instead.", "annotation_id": "29513", "url": "https://genius.com/29513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "This that Slum Dog Millionaire Bollywood flow and uh", "vault_lines": ["This that Slum Dog Millionaire Bollywood flow and uh"], "meaning": "Bollywood is the “Hollywood” of India—they make commercial movies for a mass audience\n\n\n\nThe hit movie Slumdog Millionare, which was set in India, tells the tale of rags-to-riches Indian ballers (similar to Drake’s overnight rise to stardom). Interestingly enough, Slumdog Millionaire is not technically a Bollywood film. It was directed by Danny Boyle, a British director.", "annotation_id": "29134", "url": "https://genius.com/29134", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Limping off tour, ’cause I made more off my second leg", "vault_lines": ["Limping off tour, 'cause I made more off my second leg"], "meaning": "The second “leg” (half) of his concert made more money: if you load more weight on one leg than the other, you develop a limp\n\nWeezy and Drake have taken some tumbles on stage, so there might be a bit of play here as well", "annotation_id": "27681", "url": "https://genius.com/27681", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Hip-Hop, I’m the heart of that", "vault_lines": ["Hip-Hop, I'm the heart of that"], "meaning": "In homage to Huey Lewis' awful song “The Heart of Rock & Roll”.\n\nWayne personifies hip-hop and claims that he’s the beating heart of the organism; the center of and the leader of hip-hop as a genre and also what’s keeping it alive.\n\n\n\nIf Rap was a human or living being, Wayne would be the heart pumping blood or, for the sake of the personification, music through the veins or minds of the listeners.", "annotation_id": "27664", "url": "https://genius.com/27664", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I can hand it to Drake or do a quarterback draw Wildcat offense, check the paw prints", "vault_lines": ["I can hand it to Drake or do a quarterback draw", "Wildcat offense, check the paw prints"], "meaning": "In football’s Wildcat offense, the player who takes the snap normally has a few options: run the ball himself, hand it off, or throw a pass. Either Wayne or Drake can handle the beat and put together some nice rhymes.\n\nDrake has also labeled himself the “Young Money running back,” so this is good continuity from Weezy. He later compared Drake to a famous athlete: remember Drake Griffin?", "annotation_id": "27658", "url": "https://genius.com/27658", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I been flyin’ so long I fell asleep on the fucking plane", "vault_lines": ["I been flyin' so long I fell asleep on the fucking plane"], "meaning": "To be fly is to have swagger and style. At the same time he uses it to make people see that he’s been “up there” for so long that to “fly” on a plane would be boring, making him fall asleep while other would be greatfull to be “Fly” or be “flying”.\n\nWayne loves to talk about being bored of being the best: on the “Can’t Tell Me Nothing” freestyle he says “I’m so tired of balling excuse me if I yawn”", "annotation_id": "27617", "url": "https://genius.com/27617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-no-new-friends-lyrics", "genius_title": "No New Friends", "genius_id": 140752, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "[Outro: Future]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Future’s vocals are only on the SoundCloud version of the song. His vocals were later removed from the single/album version.", "annotation_id": "17747831", "url": "https://genius.com/17747831", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Ever since YouTube, niggas been callin’ me the leader of the new school", "vault_lines": ["Ever since YouTube, niggas been callin' me the leader of the new school"], "meaning": "There’s been immense hype over Drake ever since he first began releasing music on the internet (his first mixtape, Room for Improvement, was dropped in 2006). Following his 2009 self-release of So Far Gone, there was a fierce bidding war between labels to sign him.\n\nMore recently, Drake has been integral in assisting the launch of the careers of other new school musicians, such as fellow Torontonian The Weeknd, and ASAP Rocky and Kendrick Lamar, who headlined his 2012 Club Paradise Tour\n\nThis line may be a reference to lyrics from “The Calm” on So Far Gone:\n\nYou can see it in my face or even read it on my palm\nLeader of the new school, it’s proven and it’s known\n\n\n\nLeaders of the New School is also the name of the hip hop group that Busta Rhymes started out in. Drake often praises Native Tongues Posse rappers and their affiliates, so it is possible he is referring to this group.\n\nThis line has also been used by K.dot on Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe Remix\n\nLeader of the new school, on my toes like a ballerina. Who knew I’d be Black Swan", "annotation_id": "1681742", "url": "https://genius.com/1681742", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Remember sip slow, live fast", "vault_lines": ["Remember sip slow, live fast"], "meaning": "Following the culture of the South and the rappers that sipped lean before Wayne, he stresses taking your time with the drink and enjoy the effects that it has on you. Sipping lean to fast makes you drowsy and will knock you out, which is not an enjoyable experience and there is the possibility of overdosing from it.\n\nWayne also sees the dangers of the drug that is so glorified in the rap industry. With the death of his dear friend Pimp C and many others, along with his recent run in with death (which was rumored to be caused by his lean addiction), he wants people that sip lean to be careful when doing so.\n\nHe isn’t telling those who sip to stop, just to watch yourself when doing so and be reckless in different areas, such as being reckless on a verse. Weezy discusses sipping lean below.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ye_N7pBLsfQ#t=6m45s", "annotation_id": "1681515", "url": "https://genius.com/1681515", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "(Fuck a fake friend! Where your real friends at? Started!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This specific line was sampled from Drake’s “Started From the Bottom” song.", "annotation_id": "1681054", "url": "https://genius.com/1681054", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "And if we ball, catch us", "vault_lines": ["And if we ball, catch us"], "meaning": "A play on the saying that was administered by Magic and C-Murder in the song Ball ‘Till We Fall where you ball (or make money) until you die. Wayne could be asking his homies that if he is about to die from this money making schemes, please “catch” him before he dies.\n\nWeezy also made this reference in his hook for Duffle Bag Boy.\n\nContinuing from the Goodfellas line, at the climax of the movie, the majority of the characters are either caught by the police or killed by the mafia. Perhaps Wayne is insinuating that if Wayne and his homies are to be in the same boat as the Goodfellas, then Wayne will need some “catching” before he is sent to jail or dies.\n\nAnother meaning is for spectators or average people to make sure to catch Wayne & friends when they ball. This is also a play on the actual physical meaning of the word “ball”, and to “catch” it being one of the most common activities with a ball.", "annotation_id": "1681043", "url": "https://genius.com/1681043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Dope boy, that’s my dress code", "vault_lines": ["Dope boy, that's my dress code"], "meaning": "Dress the way you live… because Ross makes hella money from dope, he then spends on expensive clothes such as YSL and Hermes.\n\n\n\nAlso, Ross' girlfriend Shateria Moragne-El has her own fashion line, Front Row in which Ross frequently models for.", "annotation_id": "1681017", "url": "https://genius.com/1681017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Fuck all y’all niggas except my niggas Fuck them other niggas ’Cause I’m down for my niggas", "vault_lines": ["Fuck all y'all niggas except my niggas", "Fuck them other niggas", "'Cause I'm down for my niggas"], "meaning": "Future borrows from C-Murder’s hook on Snoop Dogg’s “Down 4 My Niggas” to express that he’s only concerned with his small circle of friends and people he can trust.\n\nFuck them other niggas ‘cause I’m down for my niggas\nFuck them other niggas 'cause I’m down for my niggas\nFuck them other niggas 'cause I ride for my niggas\nI die for my niggas (What), fuck them other niggas, niggas", "annotation_id": "1680972", "url": "https://genius.com/1680972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Strip club like e’ry night", "vault_lines": ["Strip club like e'ry night"], "meaning": "Another strip club reference by Rozay.\n\nIn 2011, Rick Ross claimed to have spent a million dollars at King of Diamonds on his birthday (even though Blac Chyna denies it).", "annotation_id": "1680409", "url": "https://genius.com/1680409", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Bitch, we good-fellas Boy, all them niggas with you They just pall bearers", "vault_lines": ["Boy, all them niggas with you", "They just pall bearers"], "meaning": "A reference to a Goodfellas a famous movie that was centered around gangsters and killers, it was directed by Martin Scorcese.\n\nWayne and his band of brothers will kill you, so that pall-bearer’s will have to carry you to your grave.", "annotation_id": "1680390", "url": "https://genius.com/1680390", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Young Money, stay young I been Cash Money since day one", "vault_lines": ["Young Money, stay young", "I been Cash Money since day one"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne has exclusively been on Cash Money Records since he started recording the age of 14.\n\nBack Then:\n\n\n\nNow:", "annotation_id": "1680366", "url": "https://genius.com/1680366", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Down in Turks and Caicos", "vault_lines": ["Down in Turks and Caicos"], "meaning": "The Turks and Caicos Islands are a British Overseas Territory consisting of the larger Caicos Islands and smaller Turks Islands. They are known primarily for their tourism.\n\n\n\nThis is also a reference to Drake’s opening lyrics on Rozay’s remix of “Us”.", "annotation_id": "1680323", "url": "https://genius.com/1680323", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Money counter go *brrrr* when you sellin’ out the Garden", "vault_lines": ["Money counter go *brrrr* when you sellin' out the Garden"], "meaning": "Ex: Money Counter\nhttp://youtu.be/s9faz0R7ag0\n\nDrake has performed at Madison Square Garden in New York City but he has never has headline a sold out concert at MSG.\n\nIn recent memory, Jay-Z, Run DMC, Bow Wow, and Eminem are the only rappers that have sold out MSG.\n\n\n\nConsidering that the previous line talked about James Harden, this could be a reference to his Houston Rockets teammate Jeremy Lin. When the Knicks were struggling in the middle of the season and Carmelo was injured, Lin emerged and brought some energy to the Garden. The excitement of Linsanity brought people to Madison Square Garden to watch the New York Knicks when there was little to be excited about. As a result of Lin’s breakout season, he was able to move on to the Houston and get a significant pay raise.\n\n\n\nSidenote: Drake could be talking about the MGM Grand Garden Arena in Las Vegas. He went there and sold out during his Club Paradise Tour last May.", "annotation_id": "1680297", "url": "https://genius.com/1680297", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Day-one niggas, man, y’all stuck with me, dawg", "vault_lines": ["Day-one niggas, man, y'all stuck with me, dawg", "Fuck with me, dawg, yeah"], "meaning": "Noah “40” Shebib, Niko, Oliver, OB and Ryan (From the SFTB video) are common names in his songs, and he regards them as his closest friends because of the fact that they’ve been there since before the money and the fame.\n\nEvery nigga who knew him from day one is always gonna be his dawg cause they stuck with him on the roller coaster. He’s also that type of person, a real nigga who never forgets his brothers. Only severe betrayal without apology/remorse (everyone makes mistakes) might make Drizzy drop your company.\n\nAlso, Drake could be referring to how the rap game has been “stuck” with him from day 1, either because he is the best.", "annotation_id": "1680294", "url": "https://genius.com/1680294", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Man, this shit so ill that we had to restart it", "vault_lines": ["Man, this shit so ill that we had to restart it"], "meaning": "This was originally a remix for “Started From The Bottom,” as stated by Drake in an interview with Rap Radar, but was then turned into Khaled’s first single.\n\nNot only have they “restarted” “Started From the Bottom”, Drake is also implying that this song is so good that you’ll also have to keep restarting this one to hear it again.\n\n\n\nThis line and the next few use the same flow as Make Me Proud.\n\nAnother possible explanation would be restarting what these 4 created with the hit “I’m On One.” The forumla for I’m On One and No New Friends is pretty much the same. A DJ Khaled song where….\n\n\nDrake does the hook and raps the first verse\nRick Ross gets Verse 2\nLil Wayne does the 3rd verse\nDJ Khaled does his trademark adlibs", "annotation_id": "1680292", "url": "https://genius.com/1680292", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "All my bitches love me;", "vault_lines": ["All my bitches love me;"], "meaning": "A reference to Lil Wayne’s song, Bitches Love Me in which Drizzy is prominently featured.", "annotation_id": "1680201", "url": "https://genius.com/1680201", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Khaled, that’s my flesh, ho", "vault_lines": ["Khaled, that's my flesh, ho"], "meaning": "DJ Khaled and Rick Ross are like brothers. Khaled was an instrumental part of Ross' initial success in Miami, which led to his major record label deal with Def Jam. At least a quarter of the songs on every Khaled album has a Ross feature on it, including most of the singles such as We Taking Over, I’m So Hood, Out Here Gridin', Fed Up, All I Do Is Win, I’m On One, Take It To The Head, etc.\n\n\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rickyrozay/status/273088802791256064\nhttps://twitter.com/djkhaled/status/264597068113391617\n\nCould also be a reference to the 1985 movie, Flesh and Blood, as Ross had mentioned blood in the previous line.", "annotation_id": "1680176", "url": "https://genius.com/1680176", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "Birdman go *brrrr* ’cause he know this shit retarded", "vault_lines": ["Birdman go *brrrr* 'cause he know this shit retarded"], "meaning": "Birdman gives one of his signature sounds to show his approval. Check some more out on Rap Board third line down.\n\n\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k_NsXAoApxY&t=1m43s\n\nThis line also comes back from the money counter line, saying that Birdman’s adlib is similar to the sound a money counter makes.\n\nAlso, this “brrr” is said in a little higher tone than the one above, making it sound more like a Bird(man)", "annotation_id": "1680173", "url": "https://genius.com/1680173", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "No new friends, no new friends No new friends, no, no new (DJ Khaled) Still here with my day-one niggas So you hear me say no new friends No new friends, no new friends, no, no new I still ride with my day-one niggas I don’t really need no new friends No new friends, no new friends, no, no new I stay down from day one, so I say", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I stay down with my day-one niggas", "I stay down from day one, so I say", "Fuck all y'all niggas, stay down from day one, so I say", "No new friends, no new friends", "No new friends, no, no new (DJ Khaled)", "Ugh, I'm here with my niggas", "Still here with my day-one niggas", "So you hear me say no new friends", "No new friends, no new friends, no, no new", "I still ride with my day-one niggas", "I don't really need no new friends"], "meaning": "As he noted in his platinum certified single, Started From The Bottom, Drake still fucks with his friends that were there from day one and doesn’t plan on making any more friends.\n\n\n\nRealistically, the only new friend Drake has made was The Weeknd but they have since had some issues. This song would make sense to target The Weeknd by featuring everyone close to Drake other than him. However, Drake said that he still talks to Abel and that they didn’t exactly have a “parting of ways”, so this might not be a shot at him at all.\n\nBut this mantra is also popular in Chicago, where Fredo Santana doesn’t have time for anyone outside of his familia.", "annotation_id": "1680159", "url": "https://genius.com/1680159", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "That’s what yo’ ass really call started from the bottom", "vault_lines": ["That's what yo' ass really call started from the bottom"], "meaning": "DRIZZY TRIPLE ENTENDRE!\n\n\nDrake switched positions to him givin' her ass shots, so he’s starting from HER bottom.\nIt could be taken as the sex starting from the bottom (the floor) then moving up to the bed.\nClearly a reference to his hit Started From the Bottom, as this song was originally deemed the remix.\n\n\n\n\n\nAlso referring to Chris Brown using “Started From The Bottom” as a euphemism to imply Drake was “comin' out the closet” on the former’s verse on Young Jeezy’s R.I.P. (Remix). Drake responds to Chris in this by implying the girl he “started from the bottom” with is Rihanna.", "annotation_id": "1680149", "url": "https://genius.com/1680149", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No New Friends", "line": "H-town my second home like I’m James Harden", "vault_lines": ["H-town my second home like I'm James Harden"], "meaning": "Drake has always had ties to Houston – it’s where he first met Lil Wayne – and claims he reps it till the day he dies.\n\nNBA star James Harden was drafted by the Oklahoma City Thunder in 2009 but was then traded to the Houston Rockets, making the team his second home.", "annotation_id": "1680124", "url": "https://genius.com/1680124", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
