{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "status": "done", "targets": 15, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Smiley-over-the-top-lyrics", "genius_title": "Over the Top", "genius_id": 7032239, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "Prada and Gucci, it don’t go together Louis and Dior, I swear it go better", "vault_lines": ["Prada and Gucci, it don't go together", "Louis and Dior, I swear it go better"], "meaning": "Smiley is saying that the luxury brands, Prada and Gucci, don’t go well together and that just matching Prada with Prada or Gucci with Gucci would make a way better outfit than matching the two. Smiley states this because he can afford buying a full Prada outfit and a full Gucci outfit.\n\n\n\n\nSmiley also refers to the luxury clothing brand, Louis Vuitton, and the expensive cologne brand, Dior. Since one is a clothing brand, and the other is a fragrance brand, they match well together, unlike Prada and Gucci.", "annotation_id": "33539022", "url": "https://genius.com/33539022", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "(Tay Keith, fuck these niggas up)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Brytavious Keith Chambers, better known by his stage name Tay Keith, is a record producer from Memphis, Tennessee.\n\nSmiley introduces Tay Keith with his signature producer line here, which is vocalized by Tennessee rapper Lil Juice:\n\nTay Keith, fuck these n***** up!", "annotation_id": "27216641", "url": "https://genius.com/27216641", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "I didn’t even know ’bout Goyard (I didn’t)", "vault_lines": ["I didn't even know 'bout Goyard (I didn't)"], "meaning": "Goyard is a luxury French trunk and leather goods maker. Popular amongst the wealthy, Smiley refers to his modest life before fame when he did not even know expensive brands like Goyard.", "annotation_id": "23647008", "url": "https://genius.com/23647008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "Might make this glizzy go pop (Bang)", "vault_lines": ["Might make this glizzy go pop (Bang)"], "meaning": "Glizzy is a slang for a Glock type semi-automatic gun. The line refers to Smiley might end up firing a Glock type gun.", "annotation_id": "23646918", "url": "https://genius.com/23646918", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "I know that I came with a slide from left to right, but now I don’t wanna dance", "vault_lines": ["I know that I came with a slide from left to right, but now I don't wanna dance"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing his #1 Hot 100 2x Platinum hit, “Toosie Slide” where Drake sings, “It go right foot up, left foot, slide Left foot up, right foot, slide” however now he raps that he has no time to dance and mess around.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/xWggTb45brM", "annotation_id": "23638273", "url": "https://genius.com/23638273", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "The bezel is Tiffany stamped", "vault_lines": ["The bezel is Tiffany stamped"], "meaning": "Drake is talking about rare Rolex collaborations with jewelry company  Tiffany and Co.  These are usually vintage or neo-vintage watches. More information can be found on https://www.bobswatches.com/rolex-blog/resources/history-rolex-tiffany-dials.html\n\nelshjz :\n\nDrake is referring to Patek Phillipe models sold by Tiffany and Co. Since Tiffany sells them, they also are allowed to stamp them with the Tiffany logo as part of the collaboration. These models are extremely rare and the Tiffany stamp can easily double the price of the watch. Tiffany will only sell these watches to clients who have spent a hefty amount at their jewelry stores. Drake following “The level is just too advanced” with this bar just solidifies his wealth, status, and connections as such Pateks are hard to come by. In the past, Tiffany had such a partnership with Rolex, but Drake hasn’t been known to be a vintage watch collector and references Pateks more often in his recent lyrics. Also, the partnership is currently active with Patek Phillipe only, as seen on Tiffany’s website https://www.tiffany.com/watches/patek-philippe-partnership/", "annotation_id": "23580697", "url": "https://genius.com/23580697", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "I thought about letting her go (I did) But I wasn’t over the top", "vault_lines": ["I thought about letting her go (I did)", "But I wasn't over the top"], "meaning": "Smiley raps about how he’s living the lavish life with money, women, cars, and clothes. He’s considering letting his current fling go; maybe by replacing her in the roster or perchance by ghosting this woman.\n\nHowever, this woman seems to be quite skilled at fellatio. So good in fact that it is causing Smiley to really reconsider whether or not he truly wants to replace this woman, since he “[isnt] over the top”. It seems he hasn’t had his full because he does want her to continue topping him off.\n\nThis bar is a play on the title of the song “Over the Top”, as well as the previous bars’ usage of the phrase “over the top” which refers to something being excessive or exaggerated like a luxury home or vehicle.", "annotation_id": "23576931", "url": "https://genius.com/23576931", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "BM text me, throw ten at her like Messi", "vault_lines": ["BM text me, throw ten at her like Messi"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Argentinian football (soccer) player Leo Messi. Smiley says his Baby mama texted him asking for money, then he throws 10k at her. Messi uses the No. 10 shirt at both FC Barcelona and Argentina’s national team", "annotation_id": "23573217", "url": "https://genius.com/23573217", "state": "pending", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "Cannot depend on the man I slide her some bread in a jam, that’s just who I am", "vault_lines": ["Cannot depend on the man", "I slide her some bread in a jam, that's just who I am"], "meaning": "Drake presents a juxtaposition of a girl who portrays herself as independent and self reliant to the world but when times get hard he supports but doesn’t sell her out.\n\nThis could be a reference to his new girlfriend Johanna Leia who raised highschool basketball play Amari Bailey as a single mum.\n\nFurther more this is a clever word play on the condiment jam that is usually placed on bread. But is used to represent “money” – bread and “problems” – jam.", "annotation_id": "23568801", "url": "https://genius.com/23568801", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "Anyone else would retire, but I’m not content", "vault_lines": ["Anyone else would retire, but I'm not content"], "meaning": "Most people with Drake’s amount of success will be thinking about retiring. In fact, the two top rappers – J. Cole and Kendrick Lamar, who Drake has been paired with all his career, they have teased retirement while Drake has shown no signs of slowing down.", "annotation_id": "23568637", "url": "https://genius.com/23568637", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "Customs just wavin’ at us from the window They don’t even come on the plane when we land", "vault_lines": ["Customs just wavin' at us from the window", "They don't even come on the plane when we land"], "meaning": "Drake flies private on his Air Drake cargo jet and Passengers on all international private charter flights usually have to stop at customs but with Drake’s “advanced level” they don’t get checked by customs and they know it is him because the jet is unmistakable.\n\nBaka has once said he was able to travel the USA while he still had felonies because he used to fly with Drake and nobody asks any question.\n\nFact check.", "annotation_id": "23568612", "url": "https://genius.com/23568612", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "You know how October get hot (You know)", "vault_lines": ["You know how October get hot (You know)"], "meaning": "Drake’s Brand is known as October’s Very Own, and here Smiley sends a shoutout to Drake’s birth month as well as the brand", "annotation_id": "23567878", "url": "https://genius.com/23567878", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "Got a verse from the boy and it leaked (Fuck) He sent me a new one, said use it instead (Use it)", "vault_lines": ["Got a verse from the boy and it leaked (Fuck)", "He sent me a new one, said use it instead (Use it)", "The bridge got burnt and it shattered (Fuck)"], "meaning": "In 2020 a song unofficial known as “Organization”  leaked and was another Drake and Smiley collaboration.\n\nGiven Drake features oftentimes do a lot for artist’s careers, Smiley is discussing how Drake didn’t mind making a new song and now the new joint has been officially released.", "annotation_id": "23567863", "url": "https://genius.com/23567863", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "Nike don’t pay me to tell you, “Just do it” They pay me to show you I’ll do it again", "vault_lines": ["Nike don't pay me to tell you, \"Just do it\"", "They pay me to show you I'll do it again"], "meaning": "Since early 2015 Drake has had multiple collaborations with Nike especially in terms of shoes.\n\nIn Mid-2020 It was rumored and then later confirmed that Drake’s upcoming Album Certified Lover Boy would be in partnership with Nike.\n\nSince then, he released the Summer 2020 Hit song Laugh Now Cry Later (feat. Lil Durk) which had a lot of ties to the world famous sports brand.\n\nIn fact the music video for the song was filmed at Nike’s Headquarters in Oregon.\n\nIn December, Drake released another collaboration with Nike known as NOCTA.\n\nIt is no secret Drake loves repping Nike and in this line he shows us with superior word play using Nike’s slogan to show us that he has no signs of slowing down most likely referring to one of the most anticipated albums of 2021, Certified Lover Boy rumored to be released in September 2021", "annotation_id": "23567816", "url": "https://genius.com/23567816", "state": "pending", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Over the Top", "line": "Kilani gang, I swear he go wetter", "vault_lines": ["Kilani gang, I swear he go wetter"], "meaning": "Kilani Jewellery is one of the biggest and most talented jewelers coming from Toronto, Canada. Working with big names like Drake, DJ Khaled, Stephen Curry, etc. and a bunch of Toronto artists and influencers, including making custom chains for rappers competing on Toronto-based battle rap league King of the Dot.\n\nWhile Smiley mentions Kilani’s work, he makes a wordplay with the title of the June 2005 hit song “Grillz”, by American artist Nelly.", "annotation_id": "23567687", "url": "https://genius.com/23567687", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "status": "done", "targets": 18, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-its-good-lyrics", "genius_title": "It’s Good", "genius_id": 49157, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "I’ll take you out, that’s a date, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I'll take you out, that's a date, nigga"], "meaning": "Wayne uses wordplay in this line. “taking someone out” can refer to murder (first context Wayne uses), and it can also refer to literally taking someone out on a date (the second context).\n“That’s a date” is also a homophone for “That’s sedate”, and to sedate someone is to make one sleep by administering a sedative drug.\nIn addition to “taking someone out” referring to murder, Wayne could also be saying that he’ll take you out (put you to sleep) with a sedative drug.", "annotation_id": "19740937", "url": "https://genius.com/19740937", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "I just touched down, kick the motherfuckin’ field goal", "vault_lines": ["I just touched down, kick the motherfuckin' field goal"], "meaning": "In American football, a touchdown occurs when a team scores behind the other team’s goal line while a field goal occurs when a team kicks the ball over the other team’s uprights during a regular offensive play.\n\nWhat Wayne’s talking about, however, is conversion, which occurs when the team who scored a touchdown is allowed to try for an extra point by kicking the ball through the other team’s uprights—similarily to a field goal.\n\nThe difference between a conversion and a field goal is that a conversion only offers one extra point, in addition to the six points earned from a touchdown while a field goal is three points.", "annotation_id": "19400711", "url": "https://genius.com/19400711", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Give me three wishes, I wish, I wish, I wish you would, bitch", "vault_lines": ["Give me three wishes, I wish, I wish, I wish you would, bitch"], "meaning": "Wayne expressed a similar sentiment on his December 2000 song, “Wish You Would”:\n\nDon’t play with me, I wish you would\nI'ma hit your hood, then split your hood", "annotation_id": "19399988", "url": "https://genius.com/19399988", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Lil’ Weezyana, the boot nigga, steel toe", "vault_lines": ["Lil' Weezyana, the boot nigga, steel toe"], "meaning": "“Lil Weezyana” is a conflation of Weezy and his home state, Louisiana. It’s also the name of a festival Lil Wayne holds annually in New Orleans, which is also located in Louisiana.\n\n Louisiana is also known as “The Boot” state as it’s shaped like one.", "annotation_id": "19390817", "url": "https://genius.com/19390817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Two Glock .40's, nigga, you got eighty problems", "vault_lines": ["Two Glock .40's, nigga, you got eighty problems"], "meaning": "A Glock .40 is a handgun that’s popular among many law enforcement and government agencies. Wayne plays on having two Glock .40’s as 40 times two equals 80, hence “80 problems.”\n\nAdditionally, this line might be an allusion to Jay-Z’s November 2003 song, “99 Problems”—Wayne takes shots at Jay later in his verse.", "annotation_id": "19390113", "url": "https://genius.com/19390113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "This is Wayne’s world, and y’all are just some tourists", "vault_lines": ["This is Wayne's world, and y'all are just some tourists"], "meaning": "Wayne’s World is a 1992 American comedy film about Wayne Campbell and Garth Algar, two rock fans, who start a public-access cable show.\n\nThe film stars Mike Myers as Wayne and  Dana Carvey as Garth. It was a hit at the box office, grossing over $183 million worldwide.", "annotation_id": "19389919", "url": "https://genius.com/19389919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Uh, stop playin’, I ain’t with that bullshit Niggas act like bitches, Sheneneh, oh my goodness", "vault_lines": ["Uh, stop playin', I ain't with that bullshit", "Niggas act like bitches, Sheneneh, oh my goodness", "My niggas hungry, my bitches greedy"], "meaning": "Sheneneh Jenkins is a character played by Martin Lawrence in his 90s sitcom, Martin.  The “act like bitches” line connects with the fact that Sheneneh—who is a female on the show—is played by a male, Lawrence. Additionally, Sheneneh’s catchphrase is “Oh, my goodness.”", "annotation_id": "19388815", "url": "https://genius.com/19388815", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "They tellin’ lies about me, aw yeah, I must’ve made it", "vault_lines": ["They tellin' lies about me, aw yeah, I must've made it"], "meaning": "Drake later rapped about someone telling lies about him on his November 2011 song, “Cameras”:\n\nDon’t listen to the lies, I swear they all lies", "annotation_id": "19373927", "url": "https://genius.com/19373927", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Three visits later, I went and did it major", "vault_lines": ["Three visits later, I went and did it major"], "meaning": "Drake may be talking about his debut studio album, Thank Me Later, which saw commercial and critical success. It topped the Billboard charts and was certified platinum by the RIAA.\n\nInterestingly, Thank Me Later was released in June 2010, three months after Wayne was arrested in March 2010 so the “three visits later” line might be a connection to that.", "annotation_id": "19373794", "url": "https://genius.com/19373794", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Rikers Island on this flow, eight months for that pistol But at least they had some bad bitches workin’ in that shit hole, ah!", "vault_lines": ["Rikers Island on this flow, eight months for that pistol", "But at least they had some bad bitches workin' in that shit hole, ah!"], "meaning": "On October 22, 2009, Lil Wayne pleaded guilty to attempted criminal possession of a weapon, which stems from a 2007 incident when police found .40 caliber pistol in his possession.\n\n After two postponements, on March 8, 2010, Wayne was sentenced to one year in prison, which he served at Rikers Island. On November 4, 2010, after serving eight months of his one-year sentence, Wayne was released.", "annotation_id": "19373784", "url": "https://genius.com/19373784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Faded, fuckin’ faded, aw yeah, I’m fuckin’ faded", "vault_lines": ["Faded, fuckin' faded, aw yeah, I'm fuckin' faded"], "meaning": "“Faded” means to be intoxicated by drugs. Drake also used the term on his September 2007 song, “Faded.”\n\nThis also connects with the “munchies” line above as smoking cannabis makes you hungry—it affects the receptors in our brain’s olfactory bulb, which is how we smell and taste and sense food.", "annotation_id": "19373586", "url": "https://genius.com/19373586", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "‘Cause we still fuckin’ hungry, Young Money got the munchies", "vault_lines": ["'Cause we still fuckin' hungry, Young Money got the munchies"], "meaning": "Young Money Entertainment is a record label that was founded by Lil Wayne in 2005. The label was an imprint of Cash Money Records until September 2018 when Wayne reached a settlement with Birdman and Universal Music Group after he filed a lawsuit against them over unpaid advances and rights to his catalog.", "annotation_id": "19373548", "url": "https://genius.com/19373548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Mind in one place, heart in another Please pardon my brother, he’s just angry at you niggas Who don’t have your heart in your rap shit and got too fuckin’ comfy", "vault_lines": ["Mind in one place, heart in another", "Please pardon my brother, he's just angry at you niggas", "Who don't have your heart in your rap shit and got too fuckin' comfy"], "meaning": "This is a nod to the Jay-Z diss in Wayne’s following verse. Wayne took shots at Jay for his Birdman diss on his Kanye West-assisted “H·A·M” where he made fun of Birdman’s net worth, calling it “baby money”:\n\nI’m like, “Really: half a billi nigga, really?” You got baby money\nKeep it real with niggas, niggas ain’t got my lady’s money", "annotation_id": "19372950", "url": "https://genius.com/19372950", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "You who are rich and whose troubles are few May come around to see my point of view What price the crown of a king on his throne When you’re chained in the dark all alone?", "vault_lines": ["You who are rich and whose troubles are few", "May come around to see my point of view", "What price the crown of a king on his throne", "When you're chained in the dark all alone?"], "meaning": "The intro is sampled from The Alan Parsons Project’s May 1976 song, “The Cask of Amontillado.” The lead vocalist who sang these lyrics is British musician John Miles.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/nrrIm4ozbUc?t=128", "annotation_id": "19372810", "url": "https://genius.com/19372810", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Brand new pussy, pussy good as baby powder", "vault_lines": ["Brand new pussy, pussy good as baby powder"], "meaning": "Baby powder is talc or cornstarch-based powder that’s used to prevent diaper rash for babies but it can also be used as a dry shampoo and a freshener.\n\nWayne implies here that his girl’s genitalia is as fresh as if it had baby powder on it.", "annotation_id": "1566841", "url": "https://genius.com/1566841", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Feds got my man, shit is real son ’Cause my godson just became my real son", "vault_lines": ["'Cause my godson just became my real son", "Feds got my man, shit is real son"], "meaning": "Reference to the police raid of his house, where his dear friend, Alberto Lajara, was arrested and charged with fifth degree criminal possession of marijuana and disorderly conduct. and because he had to go away, he couldn’t take care of his son (Jada’s godson) so he is now, Jada’s son..", "annotation_id": "336445", "url": "https://genius.com/336445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "I ain’t workin’ with a full deck but I deal, ho", "vault_lines": ["I ain't workin' with a full deck but I deal, ho"], "meaning": "“52 cards come out when I’m through dealing now”\n\nWayne has some dirt on his shoulder, and he’s dealing with that business in this song. He doesn’t have his head on straight, but he’ll still drop knowledge, or he’s quick to mess someone up.\n\n“Not working with a full deck” is common slang that draw roots back to the 90’s. Party Arty used the line in Big L’s “Da Graveyard”. Coincidentally, this was one of Jay-Z first features. .", "annotation_id": "335875", "url": "https://genius.com/335875", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "It’s Good", "line": "Talkin ’bout baby money? I got your baby money Kidnap your bitch, get that “How much you love your lady?” money", "vault_lines": ["Talkin 'bout baby money? I got your baby money", "Kidnap your bitch, get that \"How much you love your lady?\" money"], "meaning": "After Hov was declared to be the “Hottest MC” by MTV in 2009, Birdman replied:\n\nI don’t think [Jay-Z] is the number one emcee in no kind of way. Wayne’s the best. He do the most and he make the most money. I don’t think no nigga in the business make more money than us. How can you be the best if you don’t make the most money? And you don’t do the most? Lyrically, come on man, be for real, can’t nobody fuck with Wayne… If you number one and you ain’t getting no money it don’t mean nothing.\n\nIn January Hov had reminded Baby and Wayne on “H·A·M” that they couldn’t touch his 2011 $500 million net worth, or his and wife Beyoncé’s $1 billion combined worth.\n\nHere Wayne would defend his Cash Money mentor and fired back, threatening to kidnap Beyoncé. The “how much you love your lady” money is a ransom threat, forcing Jay to choose between B and his money. This line prompted a comical Hov response two years later on “La Familia.” In an interview with “The Breakfast Club” on July 10 of that year Hov discussed Wayne’s bars:\n\nIt was alright. It’s part of the sport and the competition. I didn’t take it serious. I didn’t view it as a threat. I thought it was just a flip of a line that I said, because I’d said ‘you ain’t got my wifey money.’ I wasn’t really speaking to him but I guess he’s holding the flag, so he did the right thing for his camp. But I didn’t take it as a real threat, I took it as a flip of a line.\n\nTheir semi-beef is a bit ironic nowadays considering Wayne began suing Birdman in 2015, and in 2017 was in talks to sign to Roc Nation.", "annotation_id": "335794", "url": "https://genius.com/335794", "state": "accepted", "votes": 233, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 23, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Wizkid-ojuelegba-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "genius_id": 2173932, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "This thing got me thanking God for life", "vault_lines": ["This thing got me thanking God for life"], "meaning": "While WizKid might be thanking God for where he’s at in life currently, Drake is thanking God for the girl he’s with or the substance he’s high off of.", "annotation_id": "23344757", "url": "https://genius.com/23344757", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "But everyday is like another episode of Everybody Hates Chris", "vault_lines": ["But everyday is like another episode of Everybody Hates Chris"], "meaning": "‘Everybody Hates  Chris’, was an American period sitcom that was created by comedian, Chris Rock. Skepta, recounts his childhood where his voice was silenced due to being discriminated against. In a 2019 interview with Apple Music, Skepta and his sister (Julie Adenuga) discussed the poverty they endured during childhood, and the journey they had taken in life to achieve their ‘nirvana’.\nThe allusion to ‘Everybody Hates Chris’ is used as Tyler James Williams' character, Chris is usually comically blamed despite his innocence.\nComparing his experiences to ‘another episode’ implies the consistency of the unjust treatment, Skepta received as a youth although he did not deserve it.", "annotation_id": "18608005", "url": "https://genius.com/18608005", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Are you feeling good tonight?", "vault_lines": ["I am feeling good tonight", "Are you feeling good tonight?"], "meaning": "In contrast to how Wizkid says ‘I am feeling good tonight’ Drake is thinking about how the other person/people is feeling right now. Drake is known to be very thoughtful of other people and their feelings, he has even expressed this within some of his songs an example of this is from his 2016 song ‘Grammys’ from his album Views\n\nTell me how you really feel\nTell me how you really feel", "annotation_id": "18429399", "url": "https://genius.com/18429399", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "I never go anywhere, they never see me", "vault_lines": ["I never go anywhere, they never see me"], "meaning": "Drake is open about the fact that he often prefers to stay at home in order to focus on writing music. For instance, in “10 Bands,” he raps\n\nHaven’t left the condo for a week now\n\nAt the time of this song Drake was living in a condo in downtown Toronto until construction on his 21,000 square foot Bridle Path mansion is completed. He shows off the lavish condo here:\nhttps://youtu.be/oceD6MjoRK8", "annotation_id": "13276189", "url": "https://genius.com/13276189", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Skeppy, Wiz and Drake, it’s a ting right now", "vault_lines": ["Skeppy, Wiz and Drake, it's a ting right now"], "meaning": "Drake and Skepta had been going back and forth on social media and had talked about working together, and they finally linked up for the first time and performed Skepta’s single “Shutdown” at a concert in London.\n\nOne week later, this remix is released, showing that the duo wasted no time getting in the booth together.", "annotation_id": "12205960", "url": "https://genius.com/12205960", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake plays with patois over a dancehall beat in this verse. Drake also remixed Ramriddlez’s “Sweeterman” this same month (July 2015) and added a similar Jamaican flair.\n\nHe went on to release a plethora of songs inspired by dancehall and Jamaican culture in his  fourth studio album Views which he released the next year.\n\nAfter the release of several singles from Views, Drake’s infatuation with dancehall was seen as thievery by some artists who believed that Drake was stealing their music. This controversy was addressed by Rob Kenner in his article Is Drake’s Dancehall Obsession Homage or Exploitation?\n\nTwitter users went on to make fun of his attempt as incorporating patois into his music.", "annotation_id": "10330977", "url": "https://genius.com/10330977", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Yeah, I love the sun but I respect the rain", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I love the sun but I respect the rain"], "meaning": "Skepta understands that there will inevitably be good times and bad times, and while you enjoy the good you have to accept and work through the bad.\n\nMight be a continuation of the previous line,(due to the climates of Nigeria and Uk) he loves the sun (he loves Africa), but he respects the rain (he respects the UK).", "annotation_id": "7334417", "url": "https://genius.com/7334417", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Look forward to good times, can’t forget the pain I was the kid in school with the £10 shoes White socks, jack-ups and the pepper grains", "vault_lines": ["Look forward to good times, can't forget the pain", "I was the kid in school with the £10 shoes", "White socks, jack-ups and the pepper grains"], "meaning": "Skepta is reminiscing upon times prior fame when his family couldn’t afford a lot.\n\nIn the UK, it was embarrassing to wear jack-ups or trousers too short for your legs. White socks would only add to that problem making your fashion faux-pas more obvious.\n\n\n\n Similarly, nappy hair (“pepper grains”) also are laughed at.\n\nSkepta addresses this line on twitter:\nhttps://twitter.com/Skepta/status/269223320292044800", "annotation_id": "7275140", "url": "https://genius.com/7275140", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "I had to tell my story cause they’d rather show you black kids with flies on their faces on the television", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "On television, Africa, and African children are mostly portrayed as dirty, unsanitary and have an overall poor way of life. Many commercials showcase impoverished neighborhoods and starving children. Skepta is not going to let it slide and, would rather, educate you on how he himself grew up being African and now has this now glamorous lifestyle. He has achieved this lifestyle through the hardships of being African in a country that is not educated on African culture.", "annotation_id": "7269378", "url": "https://genius.com/7269378", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Rest in peace my niggas that are missing", "vault_lines": ["Rest in peace my niggas that are missing"], "meaning": "Skepta’s close friend and fellow rapper Lukey Maxwell was stabbed and killed around a month before this song was released. Skepta has already dedicated a whole song to Lukey, and here he remembers Lukey once more, along with any of his other friends who are dead or missing for whatever reason.", "annotation_id": "7269216", "url": "https://genius.com/7269216", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Kilo dale, aiye o le to yen o Aiye o ni double, adura lo le se o Call on daddy, baba God Adura a gba o Tisu eni ba dele A fowo bo n je", "vault_lines": ["Kilo dale, aiye o le to yen o", "Aiye o ni double, adura lo le se o", "Call on daddy, baba God", "Tisu eni ba dele", "A fowo bo n je"], "meaning": "Wizkid asks with a relaxed tone why everyone  takes. He reiterates that life is easier than we all make it appear to be and recommends prayer to God as a guaranteed coping mechanism to get by the weight of living.\n\nHe goes further to quote a Yoruba proverb that says “If your yam is cooked and ready to eat, you cover the plate with your hands and eat it secretly”\n\nYam in African culture and literature like Chinua Achebe’s “Things Fall Apart” is a symbol of wealth, power and success. As such Wiz suggests that when you become powerful and successful, it is advisable you humble yourself by not over flaunting your success. In many African societies, the logic behind this wise saying is to essentially lowkey life regardless of success to prevent haters, enemies and jealous people from trying to bring you down.", "annotation_id": "7268920", "url": "https://genius.com/7268920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Ni ojuelegba o, my people dey there My people suffer, dem dey pray for blessing eh Ni ojuelegba o, my people dey there Dem dey pray for blessing, for better living eh eh", "vault_lines": ["Dem dey pray for blessing, for better living eh eh", "Ni ojuelegba o, my people dey there", "My people suffer, dem dey pray for blessing eh"], "meaning": "Wizkid is telling us that in Ojuelegba, he has many friends who’s also shared his suffering and his hustle. As evident, Ojuelegba is a busy area in Lagos, Nigeria where everyone there is striving for a means to survive. Everyone there is hopeful and always praying for more blessings\n\nThis is a reference to the lyrical style/content of Nigerian afrobeat musician, Fela Kuti. See “Coffin for Head of State” for example.", "annotation_id": "7268908", "url": "https://genius.com/7268908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "From Mo’Dogg studio I be hustle to work, eh", "vault_lines": ["I be hustle to work, eh", "We been hustle to work, eh"], "meaning": "Mo' Dogg is the name of a Nigerian rapper who had a studio in Surulere, Lagos where Wizkid used to record and hang out before he achieved success and fame years later.", "annotation_id": "7268905", "url": "https://genius.com/7268905", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Dami loun oluwa Wizzy", "vault_lines": ["Dami loun oluwa Wizzy"], "meaning": "Wizkid is saying “Answer me now, In Ojuelegba,\n\nOjuelegba is a community in Lagos state, Nigeria. This is happens to be were Wizkid was brought up.\n\nThe song was recorded at significant time in Wizkid’s career were he felt it was his time to shine, just before he dropped his album “Ayo'.\n\nEveryone in ojuelegba know his hustle from grass to grace which started from the  streets of ojuelegba", "annotation_id": "7268902", "url": "https://genius.com/7268902", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Now I got the peng tings in the front row Saying, “Skeppy come home, baby come home!”", "vault_lines": ["Now I got the peng tings in the front row", "Saying, \"Skeppy come home, baby come home!\""], "meaning": "He has a lot of beautiful women in front row at his shows (peng is British slang for beautiful).\n\n“Skeppy come home,” may be African girls telling Skepta to return to Africa his true home, alluding to his international success.", "annotation_id": "7268792", "url": "https://genius.com/7268792", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Just spoke to the boy, said he’s flying in with a ting", "vault_lines": ["Just spoke to the boy, said he's flying in with a ting"], "meaning": "Drake (“The Boy”) told Skepta he’s coming to the UK to meet up. “Ting” is a West Indian/Caribbean word for thing – and in this case it can mean he’s flying in with a girl or a project proposal/idea.\n\nAt the time he was in the UK supporting his alleged new fling, Serena Williams in her Wimbledon match.", "annotation_id": "7268766", "url": "https://genius.com/7268766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Ever since mum said, “Son you are a king” I feel like Floyd when I’m stepping into the ring", "vault_lines": ["Ever since mum said, \"Son you are a king\"", "I feel like Floyd when I'm stepping into the ring"], "meaning": "Mum you know you’re my number one\nAnd I know actions speak louder than words too but I’m telling you now I love you mum\n– Skepta - “Sweet Mother”\n\nSkepta has always had a close relationship with his mother, perhaps never as pronounced as his ode to her above, so it’s no surprise she is one of those who encouraged him to get to where he is today – the best in his class, so it’s only natural he’s got a can’t lose attitude like the undefeated Floyd Mayweather.", "annotation_id": "7268132", "url": "https://genius.com/7268132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Why am I repping these ends? Man, I don’t know The government played roulette with my postcode", "vault_lines": ["Why am I repping these ends? Man, I don't know", "The government played roulette with my postcode"], "meaning": "‘Ends’ is London slang for ‘neighbourhood’. Skepta is from Tottenham (N17 postcode) in North London. The Tottenham area is notorious for crime and poverty, with its issues not tackled by the government.\n\nThe 2011 London riots were triggered by the police shooting a man dead in Tottenham. The riots left Tottenham burning, and scarred the community.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9Qmks0QVI40\n\n“The Government played roulette with my postcode” to me means that when you’re living in public housing the government randomly chooses which project you’ll be living in and can move you very easily; that’s why he doesn’t understand how he ended up in Tottenham.", "annotation_id": "7268014", "url": "https://genius.com/7268014", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Pree me, dem a pree me", "vault_lines": ["Pree me, dem a pree me"], "meaning": "To ‘pree’(origin: Jamaica) is to watch or pay close attention to. Other artists as well as the paparazzi are keeping a close eye on Drake, scheming to take his spot or embarrass him.", "annotation_id": "7266978", "url": "https://genius.com/7266978", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Only thing I fear is a headshot or a screenshot", "vault_lines": ["Only thing I fear is a headshot or a screenshot"], "meaning": "Drake states that he only fears two things: getting shot in the head or someone having a screenshot that depicts his infidelity or something compromising he has done.\n\nGetting shot in the head would end his life but an explicit screenshot could ruin his music career, which Drake might be saying is as important as life to him.", "annotation_id": "7266453", "url": "https://genius.com/7266453", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "All I ever needed was a chance to get the team hot", "vault_lines": ["All I ever needed was a chance to get the team hot"], "meaning": "Drake’s label, OVO Sound see’s him carrying on a tradition in hip hop of cultivating your own talent once you have achieved success yourself.\n\nWith the OVO Fest and the new OVO Sound Radio show on Beats 1, Drake is ensuring that the artists he is trying to bring up are going to get as much exposure as possible.", "annotation_id": "7266446", "url": "https://genius.com/7266446", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "Look, it’s gon’ be a long long time ’fore we stop Boy better know, they better know who make the scene pop", "vault_lines": ["Look, it's gon' be a long long time 'fore we stop", "Boy better know, they better know who make the scene pop"], "meaning": "Drake is now six years strong at the top of the rap game and it doesn’t look like he will be falling off any time soon. As he said on “Draft Day”:\n\nFive years later how am I the man still\n\nNote the play on words with Boy Better Know. Not only is Drake ensuring others are aware that he is responsible for the current surge in musicians from Canada, but he is also giving a shout out to Skepta’s label and collective Boy Better Know and their similar influence in the Grime scene.", "annotation_id": "7266390", "url": "https://genius.com/7266390", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ojuelegba (Remix)", "line": "When I was in school, being African was a diss Sounds like you need help saying my surname, Miss", "vault_lines": ["When I was in school, being African was a diss", "Sounds like you need help saying my surname, Miss"], "meaning": "During Skepta’s time in school, it was much cooler to be of Jamaican heritage than it was to be of African heritage. This is no longer the case, with British youth embracing their African identity via Afrobeats and Afroswing.\n\nSkepta’s real name is Joseph Junior Adenuga. In schools it is often the case that teachers mis-pronounce non-English sounding names.\n\nWith Skepta being of Nigerian heritage, his name was difficult to pronounce in the classroom and would also be the butt of everyone’s jokes on the playground. It’s therefore no surprise that he “left school with one GCSE”.", "annotation_id": "7266328", "url": "https://genius.com/7266328", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
