{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Metro Boomin want some more, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Metro Boomin want some more, nigga"], "meaning": "This is the producer tag of St. Louis-made veteran producer Metro Boomin, who is one of the most popular modern hip-hop producers in the world. He’s known for working with 21 Savage, Drake, Kodak Black, Big Sean, Future, and more popular artists. You may have also heard his other producer tag “If young Metro don’t trust you I’m ‘gon shoot you.”", "annotation_id": "22661097", "url": "https://genius.com/22661097", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "It ain’t no way, no how I made it on my own I made my own style I don’t think that I should stay You know I gotta go Been moving too fast Don’t wanna take it slow", "vault_lines": ["I don't think that I should dance", "You know my molly pink", "You know my gas stink", "It ain't no way, no how", "I made it on my own", "I made my own style", "I don't think that I should stay", "You know I gotta go", "Been moving too fast", "Don't wanna take it slow"], "meaning": "When I came up, I had the support of other musicians on the scene. Father and Key!, Awful Records, with the help of Sony Digital and 808 Mafia—damn, so many people. DJ Spinz and Hoodrich.\n\nI wrote the whole song in the house of P Nasty, a producer with Mike Will’s production company. Rae Sremmurd was there too. They’d just come to Atlanta. They weren’t even signed. Everyone was busted. It was raining. Nobody had a haircut. I didn’t. We were just all young artists who wanted to make music. That was the place to go make it.", "annotation_id": "8261021", "url": "https://genius.com/8261021", "state": "verified", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "I got the loudest of the loud You know my gas stink", "vault_lines": ["You know my molly pink", "I got the loudest of the loud", "You know my gas stink", "You know I gotta go"], "meaning": "Yeah – it’s the gas, loud weed smell thing.", "annotation_id": "8260998", "url": "https://genius.com/8260998", "state": "verified", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "I don’t think that I should dance I’m just gon’ have another drink I’m doin’ my stance You know my molly pink", "vault_lines": ["I'm just gon' have another drink", "I don't think that I should dance", "I'm doin' my stance", "You know my molly pink", "You know my gas stink", "I don't think that I should stay", "You know I gotta go"], "meaning": "I was at a friend’s house freestyling in his room. He had pink molly on the dresser. I was like “I’m gonna have another drink,” which was water, because I don’t drink alcohol.", "annotation_id": "8260980", "url": "https://genius.com/8260980", "state": "verified", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "I’ve been flippin’ in the house Makin’ juggs on the highway I’ve been ridin’ out of state Makin’ money like my way", "vault_lines": ["On the corner flippin' hard", "I've been flippin' in the house", "Makin' juggs on the highway", "I've been ridin' out of state", "Makin' money like my way"], "meaning": "I’ve driven from Atlanta to New York. And from New York back to Atlanta. In my friend’s BMW—it wasn’t mine. And a Range Rover. It’s varied.", "annotation_id": "8260961", "url": "https://genius.com/8260961", "state": "verified", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Made at least three thousand On the boulevard", "vault_lines": ["Made at least three thousand"], "meaning": "This is a reference to boulevards period. All over the world. I’m sure tonight someone is going to make $3,000 on a boulevard.", "annotation_id": "8260946", "url": "https://genius.com/8260946", "state": "verified", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Workin’ Monday night On the corner flippin’ hard", "vault_lines": ["On the corner flippin' hard"], "meaning": "You’re flipping — that’s what you’re selling. You’re doing it hard. In New York, we saw a little boy selling sticks outside. He was like “Buy a stick, buy a stick.” He was flipping sticks. It was so inspiring. It was cold out there and everything. It’s not about what you’re selling, it’s how you sell it.", "annotation_id": "8260916", "url": "https://genius.com/8260916", "state": "verified", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Got your girl in the cut and she choosey", "vault_lines": ["Got your girl in the cut and she choosey"], "meaning": "I’ve never heard of that place in North Druid Hills. “In the cut” is when someone’s in the club—with who they came with, but at the same time sneaky, looking at you. Not all out in the open. In the cut.", "annotation_id": "8260910", "url": "https://genius.com/8260910", "state": "verified", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Got the club goin’ up on a Tuesday", "vault_lines": ["Got the club goin' up on a Tuesday", "Goin' up on a Tuesday", "Club goin' up on a Tuesday", "Got the club goin' up"], "meaning": "Makonnen doesn’t have time to party on the weekend, so he has the club turnt on Tuesday. This was confirmed by him in this episode of Complex’s Fashion Bros.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TVsYVXictUc", "annotation_id": "8240927", "url": "https://genius.com/8240927", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "My P.O. think I’m in the house Don’t give a damn ’bout what she think", "vault_lines": ["My P.O. think I'm in the house", "Don't give a damn 'bout what she think"], "meaning": "This line likely refers to the fact that Makonnen was blamed for his friend’s accidental death. He ended up going to jail for 4 weeks and had to serve 2 years house arrest. He admitted that he was mostly on the internet during the time, but it’s likely that he snuck out to go party (on Tuesday, an inconspicuous night.)\n\nHis P.O. is slacking.", "annotation_id": "8043359", "url": "https://genius.com/8043359", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "I’ve been workin’ graveyard Shifts every other weekend Ain’t got no fuckin’ time To party on the weekend", "vault_lines": ["Ain't got no motherfuckin' time", "To party on the weekend", "I've been workin' graveyard", "Shifts every other weekend", "Ain't got no fuckin' time"], "meaning": "I’ve been workin' this graveshift and I ain’t made shit\n\nContrary to popular beliefs, trappers work more than the average 9-5er. Since his weekends are just as, if not more busy than the business week, he’s forced to make the club go up on Tuesdays.", "annotation_id": "8043347", "url": "https://genius.com/8043347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "[Verse 1: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake called up Sonny Digital to get the beat. Sony Digital in turn called iLoveMakonnen to see if he approved, and he agreed.\n\nA few days later when Drake dropped the song via Instagram with his verse iLoveMakonnen actually cried, because he had been “trippin' off shrooms”:\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/rlqjZtvD7L\n\nYou can see iLoveMakonnen’s full story here.", "annotation_id": "3956566", "url": "https://genius.com/3956566", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Tell Gelo bring the juice We about to get lit Fill the room up with some tings One night off and this is it", "vault_lines": ["Tell Gelo bring the juice", "We about to get lit", "Fill the room up with some tings", "One night off and this is it"], "meaning": "Angelo Ferraro, aka Gelo, is performing standard crew duties — he’s on substance gathering patrol.\n\nGelo was Drake’s favourite waiter at Sotto Sotto, and has since joined the crew.\n\n\n\nCould be a reference to tourmate A$AP Rocky’s “Get Lit” off of Live.Love.Asap.\n\nDrake’s influence is felt everywhere. “Get lit” and “tings” have both increased in usage since Drake began using them.", "annotation_id": "3904302", "url": "https://genius.com/3904302", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "I think we gettin’ too deep Shit I’m talkin’ might be too true", "vault_lines": ["I think we gettin' too deep", "Shit I'm talkin' might be too true"], "meaning": "Drizzy’s known for not lying on his tracks, as his extensive experience in the rap game has made his word as good as gold:\n\nEven though I used to act, man, this shit is not a act, nigga. I have never lied on a track – only facts, nigga.\n\n\n\nSecond, we all know Drake isn’t one for commitment, or at least avoids it:\n\nI’ve been avoiding commitment. That’s why I’m in this position.\n\nHowever, with this lover (most probably a stripper or an OVO groupie), he thinks that they might be getting a little too close for comfort in terms of their relationship, especially since the drunken romantic things he is saying to her aren’t lies anymore – they’re personal and from the heart.", "annotation_id": "3904299", "url": "https://genius.com/3904299", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Upstairs I got Xans in an Advil bottle I don’t take them shits but you do So I got ’em for you I don’t need the pills I’m just gon’ have another drink", "vault_lines": ["Upstairs I got Xans in an Advil bottle", "I don't take them shits but you do", "So I got 'em for you", "I don't need the pills", "I'm just gon' have another drink"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t take Xanax but still comes in clutch for his chick who takes them. The dude is a good host.\n\nThere has been speculation of Drake and Rihanna’s romantic entanglement, and while no concrete evidence exists of Rihanna taking Xanax, it could be a coy reference to a scathing concert review that hit the internet a week before this song’s release.\n\nThis could also be a reference to his song “Days In The East”\nhttp://rap.genius.com/3020922", "annotation_id": "3904046", "url": "https://genius.com/3904046", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Always workin’ OT Overtime and out of town", "vault_lines": ["Overtime and out of town"], "meaning": "Drake is always OT, ‘overtime’ or ‘out of town’ but in his case he is always both working a lot out of time. This can be applied to his tours and making hit songs.\n\n\n\nHate to leave the city, but I’ve got to do the overtime", "annotation_id": "3904039", "url": "https://genius.com/3904039", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "I just did three in a row Them shows is back-to-back to back now", "vault_lines": ["I just did three in a row", "Them shows is back-to-back to back now"], "meaning": "When Drake dropped this song, he was 3 shows into his Drake vs. Lil Wayne tour. Having performed in Buffalo, Indianapolis, & Chicago on August 8, 9, & 10 respectively, he really was going back-to-back-to-back.", "annotation_id": "3904006", "url": "https://genius.com/3904006", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Shit is crazy back home It kills me that I’m not around", "vault_lines": ["Shit is crazy back home", "It kills me that I'm not around"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that back in his home of Toronto it’s wild, people die, crimes happen. He wishes he could be home but he can’t due to his career.\n\nYea, sure this ain’t Compton but this ain’t a fairytale land neither\nWe all lost mad people, the city wild", "annotation_id": "3903989", "url": "https://genius.com/3903989", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "And when I’m puttin’ work in on a weekend I’ll look back on this and think How we had the club going up", "vault_lines": ["To party on the weekend", "And when I'm puttin' work in on a weekend", "Got the club goin' up on a Tuesday", "I'll look back on this and think", "How we had the club going up", "Goin' up on a Tuesday", "Club goin' up on a Tuesday", "Got the club goin' up"], "meaning": "Whether at the studio or on tour, Drake will always remember how he and his team would get the club poppin' every time (especially with some of their hit tracks)!", "annotation_id": "3903955", "url": "https://genius.com/3903955", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Squad goin’ up Nobody flippin’ packs now", "vault_lines": ["Nobody flippin' packs now"], "meaning": "Drake’s crew no longer needs to deal drugs to make a living, but fame comes with a price. Drake has admitted to taking drugs in response to becoming famous. Flipped, indeed.", "annotation_id": "3903937", "url": "https://genius.com/3903937", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tuesday", "line": "Put the world on our sound You know PARTY and The Weeknd Ain’t got no motherfuckin’ time To party on the weekend", "vault_lines": ["Put the world on our sound", "You know PARTY and The Weeknd", "Ain't got no motherfuckin' time", "To party on the weekend", "Ain't got no fuckin' time"], "meaning": "A clever flip of the meme line from the original.\n\nDrake has practically made PARTYNEXTDOOR & The Weeknd big. He first discovered The Weeknd after hearing about his performance in Toronto that got him recognized locally, so he brought him to his second annual OVO Fest and then got The Weeknd to be a heavy asset on Take Care with Abel letting Drizzy have some of his House of Balloons tracks including his album feature, “Crew Love”. This, among other things, got The Weeknd a lot of buzz.\n\n\n\nDrake discovered PARTY through local buzz about his electronic dance music-infused R&B. Drake decided to sign him to his label, OVO Sound and pretty much announced it by releasing “Make a Mil” on the OVO blog. Then Drake was featured on PARTY’s self-titled mixtape on the song “Over Here”. This got PARTY a lot of buzz as well.\n\n\n\nDrake has gotten these two artists big to the point where they can’t party without hearing wordplay on their own names!", "annotation_id": "3903905", "url": "https://genius.com/3903905", "state": "accepted", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Imma Take Yo Girl", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Kidd-kidd-imma-take-yo-girl-lyrics", "genius_title": "Imma Take Yo Girl", "genius_id": 3027, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Imma Take Yo Girl", "line": "And I can tell you, she wants me She wants me, she wants me, she wants me And yes I want her too I’ma take your girl", "vault_lines": ["And she wants me, she wants me yeah", "And I want her too, yeah! I want her too", "But I'ma take your girl", "I'ma take your girl", "And I can tell you, she wants me", "She wants me, she wants me, she wants me", "And yes I want her too"], "meaning": "Drake and this girl have a mutual interest in each other, so much so that he believes she will leave her man for him.\n\nThis behaviour from women isn’t unusual for Drake, as he stated in “BedRock”:\n\nThat’s when we disappear, you need GPS to find her. Oh, that was your girl? I thought I recognized her.\n\nHe was also accused by Chris Brown for dating his girlfriend, Karrueche Tran, whilst he was in prison. This lead Drake to be dubbed “the real Mr Steal Yo Girl” by Twitter users.", "annotation_id": "10063175", "url": "https://genius.com/10063175", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Imma Take Yo Girl", "line": "And shawty gots a husband, and everyone knows bout him But, Fuck him", "vault_lines": ["And shawty gots a husband, and everyone knows bout him"], "meaning": "This line is about Nivea, she was married to The Dream, and everybody knows how Wayne felt about her, he said in an interview that he loved “everything about her”.", "annotation_id": "444940", "url": "https://genius.com/444940", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-for-free-lyrics", "genius_title": "For Free", "genius_id": 2486863, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "And, yeah, I need it all right now Last year I had drama, girl, not right now", "vault_lines": ["And, yeah, I need it all right now", "Last year I had drama, girl, not right now", "Yeah, I go on and on"], "meaning": "Drake said this during the height of his back and forth with Meek. I caught some stray bullets too, this probably being one of ‘em. There was always speculation that this was a double entendre aimed at me since the narrative was that I was involved in the Meek/Drake beef because of my ex. I still sing along to this line when it comes on in the club!", "annotation_id": "10994962", "url": "https://genius.com/10994962", "state": "verified", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a real story: I was shooting my album cover, and I had a real lion on the album cover. I was sitting on the throne, and the lion was right here. Drake texts me with the second verse done. Mind you, I’m shooting my album cover, the lion is in front of me, and I’m on the throne. I swear on everything this is a true story. I even snapped and said, “The Drake vocals came in!” And the lion roars. This is all real. I’m not lying! You can go document and go find this.\n\nI thought that was so powerful and spiritual and amazing. I couldn’t sleep at night until it was done. Because I knew I had the Jay Z record done. I just gotta finish this Drake record. I come with the Drake. Boom! I come with the Jay Z. Boom! And it’s gonna be like “Who does that?” My first two singles was Drake and Jay Z and Future! It’s unbelievable.", "annotation_id": "10713856", "url": "https://genius.com/10713856", "state": "verified", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "Yeah, I go on and on Can’t understand how I last so long I must have the superpowers Last two-hundred twenty-three thousand hours", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I go on and on", "Can't understand how I last so long", "I must have the superpowers", "Last two-hundred twenty-three thousand hours"], "meaning": "First and foremost, it’s a refreshing spin on the music. You feel the energy on there and had he not said those first few lines to let you know where the vibe came from, I would’ve heard “Blow The Whistle” in there like the inspiration. It has that rhythm.\n\nI take it as him paying homage. I take it as—loud and clear—\"I know your music, I’m a fan.“ I don’t see it any different in the studio with my producers trying to catch a vibe off a Motown song or George Clinton. It’s the music that you grew up on.\n\nI’m talking about rapping, he’s talking about fucking for 223 thousand hours. I can’t tell you how many times I sat in the studio listening to George Clinton & Parliament Funkadelics trying to figure out how to make a song similar to what they did. Drake doesn’t go around flipping Too $hort songs, it’s just a moment they had in the studio. “Blow The Whistle” has been played a lot over the last 10 years.", "annotation_id": "9302190", "url": "https://genius.com/9302190", "state": "verified", "votes": 408, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "Bless up", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Bless up” has become one of DJ Khaled’s most popular and recognizable ad-libs.  In an article by Pop Sugar, they attribute the phrase to something similar to #blessed, or being thankful for one’s circumstances.\n\nDJ Khaled has capitalized upon this ubiquitous phrase by creating “bless up” shirts, shoes and more.", "annotation_id": "9277375", "url": "https://genius.com/9277375", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "Don’t ever play yourself (Haha, haha)", "vault_lines": ["Don't ever play yourself (Haha, haha)"], "meaning": "Yet another one!  “Don’t play yourself” is one of DJ Khaled’s ad-libs, which are superfluously incorporated in this song.\n\nPopularized via Snapchat, the phrase conveys a message of self-respect and confidence.\n\n\n\nMore information on Khaled’s ad-libs and rise on Snapchat can be found here.", "annotation_id": "9277352", "url": "https://genius.com/9277352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "One time for Future the Prince One time for 40 One time for Drake", "vault_lines": ["One time for Future the Prince", "One time for Drake"], "meaning": "DJ Khaled shouts out some of the key people on the OVO crew.\n\nFuture The Prince is Drake’s DJ and was featured on the booklet for Views, while Noah “40” Shebib is Drake’s go-to producer and long-time friend.\n\n\n\n40, Drake, Oliver El-Khatib and Future The Prince", "annotation_id": "9277109", "url": "https://genius.com/9277109", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "Another one (Another one)", "vault_lines": ["Another one (Another one, another one)"], "meaning": "“Another One” is the one of the catch phrases DJ Khaled has frequently used throughout his career on many of his hit records.\n\nIn the music video for “How Many Times” Khaled asks a woman to kiss him, and requests more by saying “another one.”", "annotation_id": "9274252", "url": "https://genius.com/9274252", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "Even though you in the hood, I’m still pullin’ up Dip, dip, straight to your doorstep", "vault_lines": ["Even though you in the hood, I'm still pullin' up", "Dip, dip, straight to your doorstep"], "meaning": "Drake is going to pull up at her house to take her out, even though she may be ratchet or from the hood. He has gained a reputation for “saving these hoes.”\n\nHowever, she has to be careful not to make a mistake while she’s with him, because he can return her to where she belongs real fast. Like Drizzy said on “Child’s Play”:\n\nMomma is a saint, yes she raised me real good\nAll because of her I don’t do you like I should\nDon’t make me give you back to the hood\nDon’t make me give you back", "annotation_id": "9273517", "url": "https://genius.com/9273517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "You got attitude on na-na And your pussy on agua And your stomach on flat-flat And your ass on “What’s that?”", "vault_lines": ["You got attitude on na-na", "And your pussy on agua", "And your stomach on flat-flat", "And your ass on \"What's that?\""], "meaning": "Drake is describing the mindset that girls have on the summer. “Na na” could mean that this girl have an “I don’t care” or, more appropriately, a YOLO attitude because she’s on vacation.\n\nSince she’s there to have a good time, she’s going to show off her flat abdomen and her big butt.\n\n“Agua” is the Spanish and Portuguese word for water. She has a wet pussy, so she’s turned on and ready to have sex.", "annotation_id": "9272912", "url": "https://genius.com/9272912", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "This the real thing, can you feel the force yet?", "vault_lines": ["This the real thing, can you feel the force yet?"], "meaning": "This is a reference to The Real Thing’s “Can You Feel The Force”, released in 1979.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ifMe1s43eHw\n\nDrake could be referring to how their relationship is true love, or the chemistry they share with each other while they have sex. In both cases, it doesn’t feel forced because what they have is real.", "annotation_id": "9272771", "url": "https://genius.com/9272771", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "It’s We The Best OVO", "vault_lines": ["We the Best Music! (Music, music) Major Key! (Major Key)", "It's We The Best OVO"], "meaning": "We The Best is DJ Khaled’s record label and, arguably, his most well known catchphrase. His personal brand is plastered on shirts, hats and everything you can be imagine; it is synonymous with DJ Khaled and his fans.\n\nOVO Sound is Drake’s music label. It stands for “October’s Very Own”, a reference to Drake’s October 24th birthday.\n\nAs usual, this line is DJ Khaled giving a shout out to Drake as the featured artist on the track, his fans, and himself.", "annotation_id": "9271702", "url": "https://genius.com/9271702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "I would never go and chat what we talkin’ about You the only one I know could fit it all in her— man", "vault_lines": ["I would never go and chat what we talkin' about", "You the only one I know could fit it all in her— man", "You know I never put the pressure on you", "Yeah, I go on and on"], "meaning": "Drake usually wouldn’t be the type to discuss his sexual desires with this girl, but it seems like he has decided to be up front and let her know that he’s not messing around.\n\nThis girl is actually so good that she can put all of Drizzy in her mouth, and like he said on A$AP Rocky’s “Fuckin' Problems”:\n\nI don’t really say this often\nBut this long dick nigga ain’t for the long talkin'", "annotation_id": "9271413", "url": "https://genius.com/9271413", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "I got girls that I shoulda made pay for it Got girls that I shoulda made wait for it", "vault_lines": ["I got girls that I shoulda made pay for it", "Got girls that I shoulda made wait for it"], "meaning": "Drake conveys a complete role reversal in the dating world.  Usually women are the ones in control of the level of sexuality in a relationship.  With regard to women in the strip club, on an expensive date, they expect the man to supply the funds.\n\nHowever, with the lethal combination of Drake’s iconic status and skills in bed, he believes he is the one who should be in control.  Thus, women should be paying or waiting for him.\n\nThis lyric is reminiscent of an Aziz Ansari excerpt from the movie Funny People which he used on “All Me”:\n\nI’m really stepping up my game\nThese bitches gotta start paying me for this\nCan’t get no more free Randy", "annotation_id": "9271412", "url": "https://genius.com/9271412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "I got girls that’ll cancel a flight back home Stay another day for it", "vault_lines": ["I got girls that'll cancel a flight back home", "Stay another day for it"], "meaning": "Women are so obsessed with Drake that they will second guess their decision to leave him the next day, just to return to him.\n\nThe role reversal conveyed throughout the song is evident when compared to Drake’s old music about flights.  In “5AM in Toronto,” he had to fly women to him, but now they’re canceling their own flights to be with him.\n\nIf I like her, I just fly her to the city I’m in", "annotation_id": "9271331", "url": "https://genius.com/9271331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "Would you fuck me for free?", "vault_lines": ["Would you fuck me for free?"], "meaning": "This is a direct sample taken from the ‘90s hip hop artist Akinyele’s track, “Fuck Me For Free.” The sample is taken from the chorus of the original, and is played at a lower pitch.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/QFy_EtbwjvY?t=14", "annotation_id": "9271175", "url": "https://genius.com/9271175", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "And it’s ’cause I’m off of CC And I’m off the Hennessy", "vault_lines": ["And I'm off the Hennessy", "And it's 'cause I'm off of CC"], "meaning": "“CC” is short for Canadian Club, a popular Canadian whiskey, while Hennessy is a cognac from France. Hennessy seems to be becoming one of Drake' favorite drink choices. In his 2016 #1 hit “One Dance,” he sings:\n\nThat’s why I need a one dance\nGot a Hennessy in my hand\n\nBecause Drake is poppin' that CC and Hennessy, he can last forever in the sack.", "annotation_id": "9271019", "url": "https://genius.com/9271019", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "Now you gotta run it up I be out of words, tryna sum it up Girl, you throw it back like one love", "vault_lines": ["Now you gotta run it up", "I be out of words, tryna sum it up", "Girl, you throw it back like one love"], "meaning": "This girl’s butt is so big that Drake doesn’t have any word to describe it. However, that isn’t a problem for him since he just wants her to “throw it back,” which refers to a woman continously grinding back and forth.\n\nDrake may be referring to one, or several, throwback songs titled “One Love.” Bob Marley, Nas, and E-40 all have songs with that title. But considering that this song samples Too $hort and has a Bay Area inspired sound, it’s most likely he’s referring to E-40’s version.", "annotation_id": "9271018", "url": "https://genius.com/9271018", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "They don’t want me to have another anthem So I made sure I got another anthem (Another anthem)", "vault_lines": ["They don't want me to have another anthem", "So I made sure I got another anthem (Another anthem)"], "meaning": "DJ Khaled has a roster of big hits including “All I Do Is Win,” “I’m On One,” “I’m So Hood” and “No New Friends.”\n\nLeading up to the release of “For Free,” DJ Khaled was hyping it up as the song of the summer and another anthem for the fans.\n\n“They don’t want you to _____” is one of Khaled’s many catchphrases that come from his Snapchat.", "annotation_id": "9270996", "url": "https://genius.com/9270996", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "Another one (Another one, another one) We the Best Music! (Music, music) Major Key! (Major Key) DJ Khaled!", "vault_lines": ["Another one (Another one, another one)", "We the Best Music! (Music, music) Major Key! (Major Key)", "It's We The Best OVO"], "meaning": "These ad-libs are all ubiquitous with DJ Khaled. While Khaled’s been shouting “We the Best Music”, “DJ Khaled!”, and “Another One” on tracks since the early 2000s, the “major key” catchphrase was created in 2015.\n\nIn the music video for “How Many Times,” Khaled asks a woman to kiss him, and requests more by saying “another one.”\n\nKhaled joined snapchat on the advice of a friend in 2015 and his off-the-cuff snaps on “the keys to success” have since taken on a life of their own. The catchphrase “major key” refers to the things in life Khaled feels are important.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0VK7zbZsuLk", "annotation_id": "9270949", "url": "https://genius.com/9270949", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "For Free", "line": "And like your boy from Compton said You know this dick ain’t free", "vault_lines": ["And like your boy from Compton said", "You know this dick ain't free"], "meaning": "Drake references Compton native Kendrick Lamar’s lyric, first heard on “Buy the World” by Mike Will Made-It, and later on his interlude “For Free?”.\n\nDrake and Kendrick have a long history, collaborating together several times. However, their relationship soured after Drake took offense to Kendrick calling him out on Control in 2013.", "annotation_id": "9270938", "url": "https://genius.com/9270938", "state": "accepted", "votes": 401, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Nerd-and-rihanna-lemon-drake-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "genius_id": 3588100, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "Free the dawgs doin’ B.I.Ds", "vault_lines": ["Free the dawgs doin' B.I.Ds"], "meaning": "B.I.D. stands for “bis in die” which is Latin for “twice a day.” It’s a reference to the frequency of taking prescription drugs, similar to how “Q.D.” means “once a day.”\n\n“Doing a bid” is also slang for going to prison.\n\nDrake is commenting on the fact that many prisoners are convicted for harmless drug charges, and argues they should be set free. Drake had a similar line on his 2018 intro track “Survival:”\n\nFree all of my niggas that they caught with it", "annotation_id": "19476314", "url": "https://genius.com/19476314", "state": "accepted", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "Woo! This beat tastes like lunch", "vault_lines": ["Woo! This beat tastes like lunch"], "meaning": "This N.E.R.D. beat is so good Rihanna could eat it for lunch. Note the double entendre with beat, as she could be alluding to the vegetable as well. Rihanna also makes a play on the “eat that for breakfast” boastful idiom, saying that she could eat it for lunch as in easily rap a great verse.", "annotation_id": "14617698", "url": "https://genius.com/14617698", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "But it’s runnin’ from veneers and it’s runnin’ from the fronts", "vault_lines": ["But it's runnin' from veneers and it's runnin' from the fronts"], "meaning": "The play on words continues through Rih’s verse as the beat mentioned is the track “Lemon” is known to being acidic and thus, detrimental to the healthy state of teeth and veneers.", "annotation_id": "14617696", "url": "https://genius.com/14617696", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "But everyday, hey, wasn’t lemonade", "vault_lines": ["But everyday, hey, wasn't lemonade"], "meaning": "Rihanna and N.E.R.D recorded this song in 2015 as proven by the following Instagram pic.  Lyrics from this song “Tell da paparazzi get the lens right” are seen in the caption of Rihanna’s Instagram post.  She shared the pic on January 3, 2015.\n\n\n\nRihanna is referring to the saying “when life gives you lemon make lemonade”. By saying every day “wasn’t lemonade” she is expressing how some days it was a struggle to make the best out of what she had.", "annotation_id": "14617692", "url": "https://genius.com/14617692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "I was afraid, once a nigga graduate Would I be okay? So I prayed and I played", "vault_lines": ["I was afraid, once a nigga graduate", "Would I be okay?", "So I prayed and I played"], "meaning": "Rihanna, fearing the outcomes success could bring, learned to ‘play the game’ and roll with it.\n\nIn this line, she uses the term ‘graduation’ as a genteelism. Despite the fact she didn’t graduate high-school—confirmed in a 2009 interview with GQ—she deviated from education to become a bona fide artist in her era.\n\nBy the way, did you ever graduate high school?\n—No, I wanted to. I just got really busy.", "annotation_id": "14617690", "url": "https://genius.com/14617690", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "It’s Rihanna, nigga My constellation in space Warp speed, Doctor Spock couldn’t chase, nigga", "vault_lines": ["My constellation in space", "Warp speed, Doctor Spock couldn't chase, nigga"], "meaning": "Spock is a character from the sci-fi series Star Trek. Originally played by Leonard Nimoy, and later by Zachary Quinto, Spock was the scientific officer aboard the USS enterprise as they explored outer space.\n\n\n\nRihanna is so far beyond the competition and listeners' comprehension that not even Spock  travelling faster than the speed of light (“warp speed”) would be able to catch her.\n\nFurthermore, this may be a reference to Rihanna’s “Sledgehammer” contribution to the 2016 film Star Trek Beyond.", "annotation_id": "14617687", "url": "https://genius.com/14617687", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "Just waitin’ for my thumb like The Fonz", "vault_lines": ["Just waitin' for my thumb like The Fonz"], "meaning": "The Fonz is a character from the 1970s TV series Happy Days played by Henry Winkler. He was known as the epitome of ‘cool’ at the time. One of his trademarks was sticking his thumbs up and acting nonchalant: “Ayyyy.”\n\n\n\nThis connects to the “truck got arms” line. Her shooters are waiting for her go-ahead signal, indicated by a thumbs up.", "annotation_id": "14617685", "url": "https://genius.com/14617685", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "And the truck behind me got arms Yeah, longer than LeBron", "vault_lines": ["And the truck behind me got arms", "Yeah, longer than LeBron"], "meaning": "Rihanna is a huge fan of LeBron James, a basketball player, so she compares the “arms” of a truck to Lebron’s arms, as he was measured with a wingspan of over seven feet.\n\n\n\nRihanna has previously shown her support to him in songs and on her Instagram. She even showed up at the NBA final games to cheer him on.\n\nRih also referenced LeBron’s stature on her 2015 song “Bitch Better Have My Money”:\n\nBallin' bigger than LeBron\nBitch, give me your money\n\nNote that ‘arms’ is also a wordplay meaning ‘guns’; i.e. Rihanna has shooters ready to do her whim.", "annotation_id": "14617683", "url": "https://genius.com/14617683", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "You can catch me, Rih, in the new La Ferrar’", "vault_lines": ["You can catch me, Rih, in the new La Ferrar'", "You can catch me..."], "meaning": "Wordplay by Rihanna, who cuts off the Ferrari’s LaFerrari name to shout herself out by implying a “Rih” at the end.\n\nWhile this model usually retails at $1.5 million, Ferrari auctioned its 500th LaFerrari for a record price of $7 million, making it the most expensive 21st century car ever auctioned. All the money raised was donated to reconstruction efforts in central Italy, which suffered severe earthquake damage.", "annotation_id": "14617681", "url": "https://genius.com/14617681", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "Nigga the Veyron glide", "vault_lines": ["Nigga the Veyron glide"], "meaning": "The Veyron is a luxury sports car by Bugatti, a subsidiary of Volkswagen. They go for around $1.5 million and have been called the most expensive car in the world.", "annotation_id": "14617679", "url": "https://genius.com/14617679", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "Wait, wait, wait a minute Shout out to them people, people Wait, wait a minute P-p-p-people, people, people Wait, wait a minute Mad ethnic right now", "vault_lines": ["Wait, wait, wait a minute", "Shout out to them people, people", "Wait, wait a minute", "Mad ethnic right now"], "meaning": "The sample of the man yelling “wait a minute” is from former United States Senator, Arlen Specter, at a 2009 Pennsylvania town hall meeting.\nhttps://youtu.be/BppB_X_jlkw?t=14\n\n“Shout out to them people” and “mad ethnic right now” are phrases sampled from the 2015 viral Twitter video recorded by rap artist Retch, which was also used in Pharrell and Kirk Franklin’s “123 Victory (Remix).”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xu6VnVjHziU\n\nThe scream sample before the last line: “Mad ethnic right now” is from a video of a girl’s reaction to crossing her opponent:\n\n https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AZz4hv8CAnI", "annotation_id": "14617672", "url": "https://genius.com/14617672", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "I get it how I live it I live it how I get it Count the mothafuckin’ digits", "vault_lines": ["I live it how I get it", "Count the mothafuckin' digits", "I get it how I live it", "I live it how I get it, y'all don't really get it"], "meaning": "“Get it how I live” is an expression that means you get money using the means available to you—it’s typically used as a euphemism for selling drugs, robbery, stripping, and other unsavory hustles.\n\nThe expression was first popularized by Cash Money Records supergroup Hot Boys (featuring a then 15-year-old Lil Wayne). Their debut album Get It How U Live!! became an independent success in 1997.\n\n\n\nThe phrase was frequently used by New Orleans rappers in the late ’90s, and became commonplace in mainstream hip-hop by the mid-2000s.", "annotation_id": "14527361", "url": "https://genius.com/14527361", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "Bath salt, bitin’ speakers in the face Bath salt, bitin’ speakers in the face Bath, bath salt, bitin’ speakers in the face Bath, bath salt, bitin’ speakers in the face Bath, bath salt, bitin’ speakers in the face Bath, bath salt, bitin’ speakers in the face Bath, bath salt, bitin’ speakers in the face Bath, bath salt, bitin’ speakers in the face Bath, bath salt, bitin’ speakers in the face", "vault_lines": ["Bath salt, bitin' speakers in the face", "Bath, bath salt, bitin' speakers in the face"], "meaning": "On May 26th, 2012, there were reports about cannibalism in Miami. Under the influence of a street drug known as bath salts, Rudy Eugene(left), chewed flesh off of the face of Ronald Poppo (right).\n\n\n\nPharrell is saying that this song is so hype, it’s biting your speakers in the face.\n\nIn the aftermath of the attack, no trace of a substance other than marijuana was found in his body, suggesting the attack was perhaps the product of a mental crisis.", "annotation_id": "14527253", "url": "https://genius.com/14527253", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "Money 3D printin’", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“A license to print money” is slang for having an idea/product that’s incredibly profitable, implying that the resulting cash flow is so certain that you might as well skip the middle stuff and just print your own money.\n\nDrake says he’s got money “3D printing” to imply that he’s “for real” printing money (3D: Reality, 2D: Screens) and also to play on the popularity and futuristic steez of additive manufacturing.\n\nColloquially called “3D printing”, additive manufacturing is a family of processes where geometry is produced by building a structure up, e.g. from a powder or resin rather than by the traditional (subtractive) methods of cutting material off of a blank which is larger than the final size of the desired piece. It is possible to manufacture geometries that, traditionally, would have been impossible to produce or which would have required assembly steps after machining.", "annotation_id": "14243823", "url": "https://genius.com/14243823", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "Maybe I can get some 50s for your birthday”", "vault_lines": ["Maybe I can get some 50s for your birthday\""], "meaning": "Drake could be referencing sneakers, as in Yeezy 350s, and buying them for his girl’s birthday, or possibly buying them for N.E.R.D. or Rihanna since he remixed their track. This could be supported by the following lyric.\n\nDrake could also be alluding to the rumor that he is moving away from Nike and Jordans and joining Adidas.", "annotation_id": "14107347", "url": "https://genius.com/14107347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Lemon (Drake Remix)", "line": "I get it how I live it I live it how I get it, y’all don’t really get it I pull up in a lemon, blocks get to spinnin’", "vault_lines": ["I live it how I get it", "I pull up with a lemon", "I get it how I live it", "I live it how I get it, y'all don't really get it", "I pull up in a lemon, blocks get to spinnin'"], "meaning": "Here Drake interpolates Rihanna’s opening bars from her verse on the original track as well as mimicking her flow and cadence.\n\nLike in the original, Drake is likely playing around with the meaning of the term ‘Lemon’ which usually refers to a defective car. In this case however, ‘Lemon’ likely refers to a car with a bright yellow paint-job, which causes peoples heads (“blocks”) to spin at the dazzling sight.\n\nIn late February 2018 Drizzy was spotted driving such a car, specifically a Yellow ‘Ferrari LaFerrari.'", "annotation_id": "14113866", "url": "https://genius.com/14113866", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Number 15", "status": "done", "targets": 18, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Nickelus-f-number-15-lyrics", "genius_title": "Number 15", "genius_id": 111263, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Number 15", "line": "We ride box Chevys but we built Ford tough", "vault_lines": ["We ride box Chevys but we built Ford tough"], "meaning": "The line appears to showcase a lighthearted juxtaposition between two different car brands and their respective qualities. It could convey a sense of individuality and pride in their preferences, emphasizing that Nickelus F may choose to ride Chevys, but he still possess the resilience and toughness often associated with Ford vehicles. (Ford has a slogan this is “Built Ford Tough”)\n\nIt might also reflect a broader theme of embracing uniqueness and not conforming to stereotypes or expectations.\n\nOverall, the line seems to celebrate a distinct sense of identity and humorously plays with the perceptions and stereotypes associated with different car brands.", "annotation_id": "29101095", "url": "https://genius.com/29101095", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Number 15", "line": "Champagne", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is knew for the his nickname as ChampagnePapi.", "annotation_id": "18968825", "url": "https://genius.com/18968825", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Number 15", "line": "Now I’m in it for the hoes and the checks", "vault_lines": ["Now I'm in it for the hoes and the checks"], "meaning": "In relation to the previous lines, he and his team are in the rap game for money and for the girls, not to impress others. In fact, Drake has been about money and hoes since the start, as Trey Songz sang on the hook of his second single :\n\nI want the money, money and the cars\nCars and the clothes, the hoes, I suppose", "annotation_id": "4119394", "url": "https://genius.com/4119394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Number 15", "line": "Lay back fucking with FIFA", "vault_lines": ["lay back fucking with FIFA"], "meaning": "FIFA is like Modern Warfare of the non-US world, and Drake loves them. He even opened for them at the introduction of the latest FIFA.\n\nDrake loves FIFA, he now has an endorsement. He presented the game to E3, the biggest video game event conference. He even did a commercial.", "annotation_id": "2049548", "url": "https://genius.com/2049548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Number 15", "line": "I be talking arithmetic in the club, re-negotiations", "vault_lines": ["I be talking arithmetic in the club, re-negotiations"], "meaning": "It could be a reference to his math skills.\n\nAlso Drake means that he discusses business such as record deals and prices for a verse etc.", "annotation_id": "2049477", "url": "https://genius.com/2049477", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Number 15", "line": "But I just quit my job, but got some pussy cause I’m balling", "vault_lines": ["But I just quit my job, but got some pussy cause I'm balling"], "meaning": "In A.M. 2 P.M. they talked about what they did in the beginning of the morning all the way 2 the end. In the morning  Nickelus had a job.\n\n Yo I wake up every morning, shower, gather my belongings\nHead to works, I get some breakfast cause, still a nigga yawning\n\nIn Number 15 he almost says the same verse, but this time he doesn’t have a job because he quit. He still gets pussy though – cause he balling\n\n“I wake up every mornin' shower, gather my belongings\nBut I just quit my job, but got some pussy cause I’m balling”", "annotation_id": "1958618", "url": "https://genius.com/1958618", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Number 15", "line": "Puffin shisha,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In Arabic – معسل‎  Syruppy tobacco mix with molasses and vegetable glycerol smoked in a hookah.\n\nShisha (شيشة), from the Persian word shīshe (شیشه), meaning glass, is the common term for the hookah itself in Egypt, Sudan and the Arab countries the Persian Gulf (including Kuwait, Bahrain, Qatar, Oman, UAE, and Saudi Arabia), and in Algeria, Morocco, Tunisia, Somalia and Yemen. Initial traces of hookah smoking have been found in the North Western provinces of India in the states of Rajasthan and Gujarat.\nTypical flavors of mu‘assel include apple, grape, guava, lemon, mint, as well as many other fruit based mixes. Unusual flavors, including white gummy bear, blueberry muffin, and Powerbull flavor (similar to the flavor of a Red Bull energy drink), have been introduced in recent years.\n\nDrizzy enjoys puffing on Shisha to the point where he even has a personal hookah in the studio with him at times, so he imbides in between takes of raps or crooning. It could be seen as a vice, even though it was originally seen as social facet rather than a recreational one.", "annotation_id": "1636749", "url": "https://genius.com/1636749", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Number 15", "line": "The pigs try to lock us down But we broke through cuffs", "vault_lines": ["The pigs try to lock us down", "But we broke through cuffs"], "meaning": "The pigs a.k.a. the police have been trying to arrest and lock Nick F up, but he broke out of the chains\n\n\n\nAlso could be a reference to breaking out of the bonds of slavery.  Nickelus F is, after all, from Richmond, the capital of the confederate states of America during the Civil War", "annotation_id": "1463331", "url": "https://genius.com/1463331", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
