{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yg-who-do-you-love-lyrics", "genius_title": "Who Do You Love?", "genius_id": 324980, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "Did ”The Motto”, took a flight to the golden state", "vault_lines": ["Did \"The Motto\", took a flight to the golden state"], "meaning": "Drake calls back to his legendary 2011 Lil Wayne-collaboration “The Motto”.\n\n  Pictured: Lil Wayne and Drake \n\nCalifornia is also known as “the Golden State”—in reference to its history of gold mining in the 19th century. Drake suggests that he flew out to see Compton-rapper YG in CA after recording or releasing the aforementioned track, perhaps to work on this song.", "annotation_id": "31664239", "url": "https://genius.com/31664239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "I call my homies, not 911", "vault_lines": ["I call my homies, not 911"], "meaning": "911 is the emergency number for North America. The number is intended to be used in the event of an emergency where police or other public services need to be dispatched.\n\nWhen a conflict or issue arises, YG does not call for police or ambulance. Instead, he calls his homies directly to help deal with the situation.", "annotation_id": "20466328", "url": "https://genius.com/20466328", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "Separatin’ the real from the fake The fake from the real", "vault_lines": ["Separatin' the real from the fake", "The fake from the real"], "meaning": "Now that YG is famous he has to separate who his genuine friends are from the people who are just with him because of his rising success.\n\nThe line might also be an interpolation of Havoc’s bars on “Seperated”\n\nSeparate the real from the fake\nThe fake from the real", "annotation_id": "2971888", "url": "https://genius.com/2971888", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "Bitch, who do you love? Bitch, who do you love? Bitch, who do you love? Bitch, who do you love? (Champagne) Bitch, who do you love?", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, who do you love?"], "meaning": "I got the idea of this song from a Lil Boosie record", "annotation_id": "2963590", "url": "https://genius.com/2963590", "state": "verified", "votes": 202, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "I’m the nigga with the juice But I’ll never do my nigga like Pac did Q", "vault_lines": ["I'm that nigga with the plugs", "I'm the nigga with the juice", "But I'll never do my nigga like Pac did Q", "I'm that nigga, I'm that nigga"], "meaning": "If you aint seen this movie, YOU NEED TO WATCH IT A$AP.\n2pac played a role people need to beware of.\n\nThis 1 of my favorite movies tho -400", "annotation_id": "2963570", "url": "https://genius.com/2963570", "state": "verified", "votes": 236, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "They got money that they jumpin’ on the pole to make", "vault_lines": ["They got money that they jumpin' on the pole to make"], "meaning": "Referring to the last lines metaphor, these rappers ‘dance’ or just entertain and aren’t really doing anything in the game.\n\nThey simply just put on an act for money and shit.\n\nDrake loves strip clubs.\n\nWe hit the strip club, and all them girls find a pole\n\nHere’s Drizzy at an acutal strip club:", "annotation_id": "2962974", "url": "https://genius.com/2962974", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "[Bridge: YG] I’m that nigga, I’m that nigga Bank of America account got six figures I’m that nigga on the block With the fat heat; run like athletes I’m that nigga, I’m that nigga Bank of America account got six figures I’m that nigga on the block", "vault_lines": ["I'm that nigga with the plugs", "I'm the nigga with the juice", "I'm the nigga on the back street", "Bank of America account got six figures", "With the fat heat; run like athletes", "With the fat heat, niggas better run like athletes", "I'm that nigga, I'm that nigga", "My Bank of America account got six figures", "I'm that nigga on the block", "You that nigga on the low-low"], "meaning": "This bridge incorporates scratched vocals from YG’s verse on this track. Scroll up to check the annotations for these lines!", "annotation_id": "2741602", "url": "https://genius.com/2741602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "We turnt up in the studio late night That’s why the songs that you hear are comin’ real tight", "vault_lines": ["We turnt up in the studio late night", "That's why the songs that you hear are comin' real tight"], "meaning": "I’m literally sleeping in the studio\n\nDrake dropped many hits in 2014, topping charts and breaking records. He’s only continued his historic success, moving into third place for the most top 10 hits of all time in 2018.\n\n\n\nJust like the previous lines, Drake’s verse closely follows Rappin' 4-Tay’s “Playaz Club”, prompting 4-Tay to call him out before they would finally settle the situation via $100,000 payment.\nhttps://twitter.com/rappin_4tay/status/417852679683993600", "annotation_id": "2621925", "url": "https://genius.com/2621925", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s flow and lyrics are partially derived from Rappin' 4-Tay’s “Playaz Club”:\n\nI got a ho named Reel-To-Reel\nShe got a buddy named SP-12, now you know the deal\nWe gets freaky in the studio late-night\nThat’s why the beats that you hear are comin' real tight\n\n\n\nBeef escalated between him and Rappin' 4-Tay, and it was settled by Drake’s record label paying him $100,000 for “borrowing” the lines from his song. 4-Tay says he is no longer offended by this verse, not surprisingly. You can see more here.", "annotation_id": "2613473", "url": "https://genius.com/2613473", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "I got a shorty name Texas Syn She got a buddy named Young JB and now you know the deal", "vault_lines": ["I got a shorty name Texas Syn", "She got a buddy named Young JB and now you know the deal", "Bitch, you know the game!"], "meaning": "Texas Syn is a dancer/model acquaintance of Drake’s from Houston. The two met on the set of one of his music videos.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SMXgP3nBPI0\n\n“Young JB” is referring to Just Brittany, a local Houston artist that worked with Drake as a video vixen in Bun B’s “Put It Down” music video & was once a label mate at Cash Money Records.\n\nDrizzy ended up paying $100K to “borrow” this line from Rappin' 4-Tay’s 1994 song, “Playaz Club” after being called out for biting.\nhttps://twitter.com/rappin_4tay/status/417852679683993600", "annotation_id": "2613461", "url": "https://genius.com/2613461", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "I’m that nigga with the plugs I’m the nigga who got homies that be sellin’ drugs", "vault_lines": ["I'm that nigga with the plugs", "I'm the nigga with the juice", "I'm the nigga who got homies that be sellin' drugs", "I'm that nigga, I'm that nigga", "I'm that nigga on the block"], "meaning": "A plug is a source who ships and/or grows drugs – see this Cam'ron line for perhaps the most amusing hip-hop use of the term.", "annotation_id": "2611764", "url": "https://genius.com/2611764", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "But I’m big on the West like I’m big in the South So we gon’ pay some people off, we gon’ figure it out", "vault_lines": ["But I'm big on the West like I'm big in the South", "So we gon' pay some people off, we gon' figure it out"], "meaning": "Drake is known out in Cali, just as well as he is in the South: like he says in “Versace”\n\nI feel like Atlanta adopted us\n\nBecause he’s so well known, he should be able to do something under the table to help his man out with the charges and get him free.", "annotation_id": "2611631", "url": "https://genius.com/2611631", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "You that nigga on the low-low You’re the nigga, you’re the one that be talkin’ to the po-pos", "vault_lines": ["You're the nigga, you're the one that be talkin' to the po-pos", "I'm that nigga, I'm that nigga", "I'm that nigga on the block", "You that nigga on the low-low"], "meaning": "“Low low” as in a low key, shady type of dude. He’s always taking observations and willing to inform the cops – I guess the G-Code isn’t embedded in his blood.", "annotation_id": "2611548", "url": "https://genius.com/2611548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "My Bank of America account got six figures", "vault_lines": ["Bank of America account got six figures", "My Bank of America account got six figures"], "meaning": "While BOA is definitely a safe choice for a bank, since it’s firmly in the too-big-to-fail category, it’s also corrupt as hell\n\n\n\nThe alternative (in terms of size) would be America’s largest bank, Chase, but the blue logo and “C” in their name might be a problem for someone reppin' Bloods as heavy as YG.", "annotation_id": "2611538", "url": "https://genius.com/2611538", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "OVO crew, nigga, thought I told you", "vault_lines": ["OVO crew, nigga, thought I told you"], "meaning": "Drake reps OVO (October’s Very Own), the crew and record label that Drake started with producer OVO 40 back in Toronto, beginning with Drake’s first tape Room For Improvement in 2006. Drake started “All Things Fresh” as an unsigned artist, which would officially become the OVO Sound record label in 2012. The crew is named after Drake’s birth month of October, label-mates Boi-1da & Majid Al Maskati of Majid Jordan also share the same birth month.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/252896672156356609", "annotation_id": "2611513", "url": "https://genius.com/2611513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "A lot of fools puttin’ salt in the game Until these women get the notion that they runnin’ the game", "vault_lines": ["A lot of fools puttin' salt in the game", "Until these women get the notion that they runnin' the game"], "meaning": "Drizzy creates the metaphor that these “other artists” are simply strippers on a pole, acting out for attention and a quick buck.\n\nHe pays homage to Rappin 4 Tay’s – “Playaz Club”\n\nA lot of fools put salt in the game\nTill these women get the notion that they running the game\n\nThe culinary reference (salt) in first line also contrasts with the previous lines' description of Drake’s baller lifestyle: he’s eating at Nobu, while other rappers are just salty dudes making stripper money.", "annotation_id": "2611440", "url": "https://genius.com/2611440", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "I’m the general, just makin’ sure my soldiers straight Had to leave my nigga, homie got an open case", "vault_lines": ["I'm the nigga with the juice", "I'm the general, just makin' sure my soldiers straight", "Had to leave my nigga, homie got an open case", "I'm that nigga, I'm that nigga"], "meaning": "Could be referring to P. Reign who still deals with old cases from his hustling days, and isn’t allowed to cross the border into the USA, so Drake has to leave him home every time he is out there.\n\nBut also to Baka from the outro on “From Time,” who he also mentioned in “We Made It Freestyle”:\n\nWhen Baka came home for the holidays\nI was like “Nigga we made it!”\nAnd all of those lawyer fees, nigga I paid it\n\nSeems like he’s back on the road now, though:", "annotation_id": "2611430", "url": "https://genius.com/2611430", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "And my name too big, and my gang too big Young Money shit, me and Lil Wayne too big", "vault_lines": ["And my name too big, and my gang too big", "Young Money shit, me and Lil Wayne too big"], "meaning": "Drake must be talking about popularity, because his fellow YMCMB label mate Lil Wayne is only 5'6\"", "annotation_id": "2611425", "url": "https://genius.com/2611425", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "I’ma crush that ass even if it ain’t too big", "vault_lines": ["I'ma crush that ass even if it ain't too big"], "meaning": "In an interview with Nessa, Selena Gomez said this about Drake:\n\nI mean this in the nicest way possible, but I don’t think my butt is big enough for Drake.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=POdT8Qp-Ipc&t=2m7s", "annotation_id": "2611421", "url": "https://genius.com/2611421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "And man shout my nigga Game he just rolled through Eatin’ crab out in Malibu at Nobu", "vault_lines": ["And man shout my nigga Game he just rolled through", "Eatin' crab out in Malibu at Nobu"], "meaning": "Game isn’t actually signed to Cash Money (just yet). He and Birdman both sent tweets to hint at it but no actual contracts have been signed so far. Game and Cash Money (Drake included, of course) have been cool for a minute though. Game also had a verse on the unofficial version of this song.\n\nDrizzy and his potential label mate Game hit Malibu up for a night out. Malibu’s an exclusive sea side suburb just outside of Los Angeles, known for it’s quality seafood.\n\nNobu is a famous restaurant located in many big cities around the world.  Drake seems to be an avid fan of the place.", "annotation_id": "2611419", "url": "https://genius.com/2611419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "I would pinky swear but my pinky ring too big", "vault_lines": ["I would pinky swear but my pinky ring too big"], "meaning": "In the video for Drake’s track, “Headlines”, he wears a pretty large pinky ring.\n\n\n\nThis ring is big, which prevents him from pinky swearing to keep his promise, even though we know Drake doesn’t lie.", "annotation_id": "2611399", "url": "https://genius.com/2611399", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Do You Love?", "line": "Porsche sittin’ on Forgi’s Niggas can’t afford these The Panamera shittin’ on the 9-11", "vault_lines": ["Porsche sittin' on Forgi's", "Niggas can't afford these", "The Panamera shittin' on the 9-11"], "meaning": "Porsche is an automobile company that is known for their stylish, luxiourious sportscars. Both the Panamera and 911 models will set you back at least $80K each.\n\nNot only does YG have a Panamera, but he put it on Forgiato rims. These are highly popular and run around $5K.\n\n\n\nPictured is a Porsche Panamera GTS with F2.15 Forgiato Wheels", "annotation_id": "2611386", "url": "https://genius.com/2611386", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep The Faith", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dvsn-keep-the-faith-lyrics", "genius_title": "Keep The Faith", "genius_id": 2875124, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep The Faith", "line": "[Bridge: dvsn]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "New to the extended version, verses 4, 5 and the bridge from dvsn are brand new, while the duet hook between Drake and dvsn is also a new addition.", "annotation_id": "13886518", "url": "https://genius.com/13886518", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Keep The Faith", "line": "And I know that you been working, working, working, working, ain’t ya? You don’t have no time to lay up You just trying to be somebody ’Fore you say you need somebody Get all your affairs in order I won’t have affairs, I’m yours, girl Faithful, faithful, faithful, faithful", "vault_lines": ["And you know that I ain't gonna go", "And I know that you been working, working, working, working, ain't ya?", "You just trying to be somebody", "It ain't about me no more (need you to know)", "Girl you know you, know you gotta get comfortable", "And I fuck with you", "Get as freaky as you wanna be, girl it's up to you", "I'll get to know you again", "You know that I'm gon' make sure (need you to know)", "You know I gotta make sure, gotta make sure (need you to know)"], "meaning": "Similar to the predicament portrayed in his “Tell Your Friends (Remix),” this woman is continuously working on improving her career, meaning she has very little time to commit to a relationship. Furthermore, she doubts if Drake’s serious, as his reputation of loving and leaving women has preceded him.\n\nDespite her apprehensions, Drake uses subtle wordplay around “affairs,” to both reassure her that he’s willing to wait for her and give her the sufficient space to get her life in order; whilst also letting her know that he’ll stay monogamous.\n\nThe repetition of “Working” and Drake’s eagerness for this woman to finish at work is reminiscent of his and Rihanna’s 2016 collaboration, “Work”:\n\nYou need to get done, done, done, done at work, come over", "annotation_id": "11062196", "url": "https://genius.com/11062196", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Love You More Than Always", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-kloud-love-you-more-than-always-lyrics", "genius_title": "Love You More Than Always", "genius_id": 8309181, "source": "api"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Swishin’", "status": "done", "targets": 5, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Partynextdoor-swishin-lyrics", "genius_title": "Swishin’", "genius_id": 2329183, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Swishin’", "line": "She popped her some pills now that bitch is just rolling Roll up another one we just keep going I hope that she ride me the same in the morning", "vault_lines": ["Roll up another one", "Pull me another one", "I want another one", "She popped her some pills now that bitch is just rolling", "Roll up another one we just keep going", "I hope that she ride me the same in the morning"], "meaning": "PARTY is almost always consuming drugs with the girl he spends then night with, because of the drowsy and relaxing effect they have.\nAt this point he underlines this feeling of this situation with his dynamic wording – just like the beat.\n\n\n\nProbably this girl is just a rider, with whom he actually shares no feelings.", "annotation_id": "8423223", "url": "https://genius.com/8423223", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Swishin’", "line": "Pull up like Jordan I’m ballin’, whenever I shoot it goes swish", "vault_lines": ["Pull up like Jordan", "I'm ballin', whenever I shoot it goes swish"], "meaning": "PARTY is comparing himself with the former basketball player Michael Jordan, which is widely considered as a legend. According to the fact that he’s always swishin', PARTY’s technique is on point – whatever he does.\n\nA ‘swish’ is a shot, whereby you score without hitting nothing but the net.\n\n\n\nDrake referred to Jordan in a similar way in his track “Jumpman”, which feautures Future.", "annotation_id": "8421214", "url": "https://genius.com/8421214", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Swishin’", "line": "Roll up another one Pull me another one I want another one Every time that I take a shot I swear I never miss I love it, I’m winnin’, I’m feenin the game Ain’t got no assists", "vault_lines": ["Roll up another one", "Pull me another one", "I want another one", "Every time that I take a shot I swear I never miss", "I love it, I’m winnin', I’m feenin the game", "Ain’t got no assists"], "meaning": "Just like Micheal Jordan, he already mentioned in the hook, PARTY is able to run the game on his own and keep on winning. Since he’s a hustler, his desire for more won’t run out that easily. The repitition of ‘another one’ is a good example for this. (Low key shoot-out to DJ Khaled)\n\n\n\nMaybe PARTY also means that he wants another women to spend time with.", "annotation_id": "8421001", "url": "https://genius.com/8421001", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Swishin’", "line": "I couldn’t resist (I couldn’t resist) I mean look at that bitch She looked at my wrist and she couldn’t resist Back to back one, pop a bottle how could you resist", "vault_lines": ["I couldn't resist (I couldn't resist)", "I mean look at that bitch", "She looked at my wrist and she couldn’t resist", "Back to back one, pop a bottle how could you resist"], "meaning": "PARTY is explaining that he couldn’t resist the affection of a good looking women. But obviously he isn’t the only one to feel this way. Since PARTY  is having an expensive lifestyle, this women also couldn’t resist him and his status.\n\nFurthermore “Back To Back” is a shoot-out to the grammy-nominated single by Drake.", "annotation_id": "8420844", "url": "https://genius.com/8420844", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Swishin’", "line": "Pull up like Jordan I’m ballin’, whenever I shoot it goes swish", "vault_lines": ["Pull up like Jordan", "I'm ballin', whenever I shoot it goes swish"], "meaning": "PARTYNEXTDOOR is referring to former Professional Basketball player Michael Jordan. He is one of the most known, if not the most known, basketball player in the NBA. He also owns the line of sneakers Jordans, which OVO has collabed with to make their own line.", "annotation_id": "8000585", "url": "https://genius.com/8000585", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DnF", "status": "done", "targets": 7, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Preme-dnf-lyrics", "genius_title": "DnF", "genius_id": 505089, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DnF", "line": "It’s funny how she shows up late, but always cums before me", "vault_lines": ["It's funny how she shows up late, but always cums before me"], "meaning": "Drake showcases his lyrical ability with this sexual double entendre. Drizzy describes how his lady shows up late to events while ironically she “comes before” him in relation to their intercourse.", "annotation_id": "26118950", "url": "https://genius.com/26118950", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DnF", "line": "Don’t fuck with them or hollow tips’ll fly", "vault_lines": ["Don't fuck with them or hollow tips'll fly"], "meaning": "P. Reign was charged with nine counts of weapons possession charges after Toronto Police found three loaded handguns in the backseat of his leased Cadillac. Also, Drake says in Live For: “Chubbs might hold that thing, If he get caught, he’ll be home by spring” Chubbs is a Reps Up member and founder with P. Reign.\n\n\n\nChubbs also said to Complex once:\n\n“Everybody wants to poke and jab at Drake because they don’t feel like he will throw back, But nobody around here is going to let something happen to him at any time, especially me. I’m not ever going to let nothing happen to him. If it’s going to happen it’s going to happen to me first.", "annotation_id": "3964864", "url": "https://genius.com/3964864", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DnF", "line": "And lots of liquor Tell her, “Fuck me like you hate me” “You love me?” I said, “Maybe”", "vault_lines": ["And lots of liquor", "Tell her, \"Fuck me like you hate me\"", "\"You love me?\" I said, \"Maybe\"", "Drunk and fuck, drunk and fuck", "Drunk and fuck, drunk"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Drake’s opening lines at “Hate Sleeping Alone”.\n\n“She says \"kiss me like you miss me, fuck me like you hate me”\n\nOnly this time P is the one telling this to the girl he’s with and not the other way around.", "annotation_id": "3961778", "url": "https://genius.com/3961778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DnF", "line": "You say that you’d rather smoke? Well, let me call up OB’s room and tell him, “Roll up a cone” I know he probably got that extra special (Yup) I never second-guess it", "vault_lines": ["You say that you'd rather smoke?", "Well, let me call up OB's room and tell him, \"Roll up a cone\"", "I know he probably got that extra special (Yup)", "I never second-guess it"], "meaning": "OB O'Brien is a member of Drake’s crew and is also signed to his label — OVO Sound.\n\nHe gained major attention after doing a skit on the video for Started From the Bottom with fellow TopSZN member OVO Ryan.\n\n\n\nOB’s always got that extra special weed so Drizzy calls him up and tells him to roll up a “cone,” which is a type of marijuana joint.\n\n\n\nFamous rapper and marijuana user Wiz Khalifa referenced the line on Twitter, where he poked fun at Drake for the line.", "annotation_id": "3961767", "url": "https://genius.com/3961767", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DnF", "line": "“How’s Villanova? What classes you taking next semester?” (Ayy) Man, she like, ”Fuck the small talk I’m not here to discuss extra credits or my professors” No time for funny stories", "vault_lines": ["\"How's Villanova? What classes you taking next semester?\" (Ayy)", "Man, she like, \"Fuck the small talk", "I'm not here to discuss extra credits or my professors\"", "No time for funny stories"], "meaning": "Villanova is a prestigious university in Pennsylvania.\n\nIn an interview with E!, Drake mentioned how he wanted to be in a relationship with a girl who went to college and had goals:\n\nI need somebody funny, I need somebody driven. I like unique goals. You know, you talk to a lot of girls and they’re like ‘oh modeling, acting.’ I like to hear about school, I like to hear about wanting to be a business owner…in my world that’s unique.\n\nThis line most likely refers to Drake’s close friend Gigi to whom he met through work relations with members of her family. On his birthday this year, Drake posted an instagram picture of a birthday card he received from the Villanova grad.", "annotation_id": "3961761", "url": "https://genius.com/3961761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DnF", "line": "OVO, I ride (Ride), OMO, I ride (Ride) Reps Up, them my niggas from the Scarborough side", "vault_lines": ["OVO, I ride (Ride), OMO, I ride (Ride)", "Reps Up, them my niggas from the Scarborough side"], "meaning": "Drake is known for representing multiple teams:\n\n\n\nOVO (October’s Very Own) – Drake’s label\n\nOMO (On My Own) – PARTYNEXTDOOR’s movement\n\nReps Up – the label P. Reign is signed to\n\n\n\n\n\nBoth Drake and P.Reign rep Scarborough, a rough suburb on the East side of Toronto, though only the latter actually grew up there. Infamously, Drake was raised in posh Toronto neighbourhood Forest Hill.", "annotation_id": "3961701", "url": "https://genius.com/3961701", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DnF", "line": "You just turned twenty-one like Adele, that’s good with me (That’s good with me)", "vault_lines": ["You just turned twenty-one like Adele, that's good with me (That's good with me)"], "meaning": "Singer Adele is widely regarded for her breakout sophomore album, 21.", "annotation_id": "3961650", "url": "https://genius.com/3961650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yg-why-you-always-hatin-lyrics", "genius_title": "Why You Always Hatin?", "genius_id": 2472621, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Just copped the Benzo with the tint You should know cause in every song I said this shit Yeah, in every song I said this shit I’m just, I’m just proud of my accomplishments", "vault_lines": ["Just copped the Benzo with the tint", "You should know cause in every song I said this shit", "Yeah, in every song I said this shit", "I'm just, I'm just proud of my accomplishments", "This some other shit, yeah", "Yeah, yeah, this some other shit"], "meaning": "I got the Maybach. I just had the S600 or some shit. Then after I got shot, we was driving to the hospital, got in a car accident, totaled that joint. It was brand new. So after that joint, I got the Maybach. I’m like “Fuck it. I’m about to get the big boy.”\n\nIt ain’t too many young niggas with Maybachs sliding around, period. It’s only 2015 and 2016 Maybachs. It’s a lot of people sliding around in Ghosts and other whips, because you can get a 2010, 11, or 12 for the low. You can’t get this one for the low. [Laughs]\n\nI’m just poppin' shit, you know. I don’t really do that a lot, though. I ain’t that type of dude. I ain’t a shit talker.", "annotation_id": "9664397", "url": "https://genius.com/9664397", "state": "verified", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Gotta ask YG if he hit it already, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Gotta ask YG if he hit it already, yeah"], "meaning": "That’s real life. Homies gonna do that. “Aye, you know this one? She’s from your area. What’s happenin'?” And it’s either ‘yeah’ or it’s ‘nah’. Or the homie did that. I ain’t do it. That’s just regular nigga shit.", "annotation_id": "9664389", "url": "https://genius.com/9664389", "state": "verified", "votes": 121, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "If you ain’t know me, bet you know now Drizzy came through with the verse for me both times", "vault_lines": ["If you ain't know me, bet you know now", "Drizzy came through with the verse for me both times"], "meaning": "He fucks with my music. He knows I fuck with his. We all peoples. Everybody in his camp. Everybody in my camp. It’s regular. I said that, because niggas wouldn’t say that. Niggas wasn’t like “Yeah. I got two verses from the homie. First album and the second album.” They ain’t gonna wanna talk about that. That’s why I said it.", "annotation_id": "9664387", "url": "https://genius.com/9664387", "state": "verified", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I hit him and told him I had a record I wanted him to hear and see if he fucked with. He was like, “Send it to me.” I sent it to him and he hit me like, “I fuck with this shit. This is an anthem. I got you.” He was supposed to be turning his album in, so he really wasn’t supposed to be doing nothing. It was at the last minute. But he got the verse out the way, just like that. It’s just like a respect thing. Bro fucks with what I do. He knows I got my side on lock.", "annotation_id": "9664384", "url": "https://genius.com/9664384", "state": "verified", "votes": 121, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Heard you talkin’ ’bout I’m real, I’m fraud Your kids and broad, they straight, you shouldn’t care at all", "vault_lines": ["Heard you talkin' 'bout I'm real, I'm fraud", "Your kids and broad, they straight, you shouldn't care at all"], "meaning": "YG knows people will talk smack about him, but he doesn’t care—he knows he’s a “real one.” He feels like people should be more concerned about feeding their families than questioning his authenticity.\n\nThis is a callback to his hit “I’m A Real 1”:\n\n Nigga, I’m a real one\nYep, all my niggas bangers\nWatch how I twist my fingers\nNigga, I’m a real one", "annotation_id": "9204934", "url": "https://genius.com/9204934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "I’ma spend the summer gettin’ to the cake And I’ma wish a nigga would on every candle I’m like", "vault_lines": ["I'm a star like Moesha's nigga", "I'ma spend the summer gettin' to the cake", "And I'ma wish a nigga would on every candle"], "meaning": "Getting to the “cake” is a reference to making money; Drake plans to spend the summer making more!\n\nTraditionally, birthday cakes have candles on top, in which a wish is made when they’re blown out. He is also saying he “wish a nigga would” try  him.", "annotation_id": "9203629", "url": "https://genius.com/9203629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "I’m a 4Hunnid nigga, I don’t care at all Have her suck dick, lick the head and all", "vault_lines": ["I'm a 4Hunnid nigga, I don't care at all", "Have her suck dick, lick the head and all"], "meaning": "YG reps his block so much he titled one of his mixtapes 4Hunnid Degrees and trademarked his brands 4 Hunnid, 4 Hunnid Records and terms “Bompton” and “Bicken Back Bein' Bool.”\n\nLook, 4Hunnid records, 4Hunnid necklace\n\n\n\nSeems he also enjoys fellatio, too.", "annotation_id": "9197249", "url": "https://genius.com/9197249", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Just admit it already You got plans to do it, but we did it already", "vault_lines": ["Just admit it already", "You got plans to do it, but we did it already"], "meaning": "Drizzy has done virtually everything in the rap game and received the accolades for it, including recently gaining his first number one for his single in “One Dance.” Younger rappers who are new to the mainstream scene are planning to reach the heights that Drake has.", "annotation_id": "9197168", "url": "https://genius.com/9197168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Why you hatin’ on the progress?", "vault_lines": ["Why you hatin' on the progress?"], "meaning": "YG is progressing as an artist, businessman, and philanthropist. He’s due to drop his long awaited sophomore album in June, has a net worth of close to $3 million, and  enjoys giving back to his community. In 2013, he took 60 kids to go shopping for the holidays.", "annotation_id": "9196339", "url": "https://genius.com/9196339", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "But I got it all handled They try to box me in, I got my own angles, yeah", "vault_lines": ["But I got it all handled", "They try to box me in, I got my own angles, yeah"], "meaning": "Drake throws shots at haters in this line, saying they’re trying to put boxing him in terms of categorizing his music. Drake doesn’t want to just rap, he wants to do whatever he wants.\n\nWith the success of dancehall-inspired tracks like “Controlla” and “Work,” as well as the afrobeat number one hit “One Dance,” Drake keeps expanding his musical boundaries and proving that he’s more than just a rapper.", "annotation_id": "9196218", "url": "https://genius.com/9196218", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Slaps on deck for the Oaktown", "vault_lines": ["Slaps on deck for the Oaktown"], "meaning": "A “slapper” can be another name for an “anthem” or a song that is known to bump throughout the city and clubs. Drake and YG are both known to have a few songs that meet the criteria of slapper, most notably their last collaboration.\n\n\n\n“Oaktown” refers to Oakland, home of Kamaiyah, the 20 year old female MC who performs the hook on this song.", "annotation_id": "9195990", "url": "https://genius.com/9195990", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "I got the bity back brackin’, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I got the bity back brackin', nigga"], "meaning": "YG says “the City Back Crackin'” but won’t pronounce “C” because he is a Blood gang member.\n\nThis line is similar to the title of one his songs, “Bicken Back Bein Bool.”", "annotation_id": "9195899", "url": "https://genius.com/9195899", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Hate from a distance, please don’t try me ’Cause we’ll turn it up on anybody I see why they don’t like me Yellin’ out 4Hunnid, they go too hyphy", "vault_lines": ["Hate from a distance, please don't try me", "'Cause we'll turn it up on anybody", "I see why they don't like me", "Yellin' out 4Hunnid, they go too hyphy"], "meaning": "Bay Area legend Mac Dre coined the term “hyphy.” It’s Bay Area slang for “going crazy.”\n\nYG reps his block, 400 Spruce Street in Compton, his nickname and adlib is YG400!\n\nIce on my neck got ice in my ears\nHit the club niggas looking at me hella weird\nIt’s YG 400, bitch, get it right", "annotation_id": "9195655", "url": "https://genius.com/9195655", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Tryna get back close, hit ’em with the stiff Gears I shift, make sure they feel the drift", "vault_lines": ["Tryna get back close, hit 'em with the stiff", "Gears I shift, make sure they feel the drift"], "meaning": "Once you’ve crossed YG’s path once, he doesn’t give you a second chance. Like an NFL player, he’ll give you a “stiff arm” and watch you slowly drift away out of his life—he wants nothing to do with you.\n\n\n\nAlso note the wordplay on “shift” and “drift,” two actions associated with cars. Shifting up a gear increases speed and engine RPM, while drifting oversteers the car, applying too much power intentionally so that the wheels lose traction and the car spins out. The move is typically done by street racers.\n\n\n\nYG’s is no stranger to nice cars. He owns a Porsche Panamera and a drop-top classic Chevrolet Impala. He also flexes his car muscles on Instagram.", "annotation_id": "9195489", "url": "https://genius.com/9195489", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "In Bomp-town that’s an automatic, hold down What’s that? Ask no questions, just hold it down", "vault_lines": ["In Bomp-town that's an automatic, hold down", "What's that? Ask no questions, just hold it down"], "meaning": "To hold something down is to represent it. So, if YG says he is “holding down Bompton,” he is repping his home city, Compton, California. He doesn’t pronounce the C because he is a Blood gang member.\n\n\n\nYG is not the first rapper to rep Compton as his home town. From the likes Dr. Dre and N.W.A to Kendrick Lamar, Compton has knack for producing quality rappers.", "annotation_id": "9195381", "url": "https://genius.com/9195381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "I’m a star like Moesha’s nigga", "vault_lines": ["I'm a star like Moesha's nigga"], "meaning": "In the self titled 90’s sitcom “Moesha,” played by Brandy Norwood, she has a relationship with “Q” who’s played by Fredro Starr. Drake is saying he’s a Starr/Star like Moesha’s nigga.\n\n\n\nBrandy Norwood is also sampled on the song “Fire & Desire”  from Drake’s album Views.", "annotation_id": "9195344", "url": "https://genius.com/9195344", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Yeah, I be slidin’, I be creepin’, nigga Girls these days, they just don’t know how to keep a nigga", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I be slidin', I be creepin', nigga", "Girls these days, they just don't know how to keep a nigga"], "meaning": "Once again referring to DMs (direct messages), Drake claims it’s the girls in his life (not him) who are messing up their relationships with him.\n\nThis lyric could be a general statement or allude to the likes of Serena Williams or Rihanna, who Drizzy has recently been rumoured to have hooked up with.", "annotation_id": "9195340", "url": "https://genius.com/9195340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "I’m a humble nigga, I don’t even pop shit What I do? I just drive the Maybach through the projects And Still Brazy ain’t even dropped yet (Whoop-whoop)", "vault_lines": ["I'm a humble nigga, I don't even pop shit", "I just drive the Maybach through the projects", "And Still Brazy ain't even dropped yet (Whoop-whoop)"], "meaning": "YG keeps to himself and does his thing, driving through his old neighbourhood in his Mercedes Maybach, even though his album hasn’t dropped yet! “Popping off” means to start shit with someone.\n\nDrake said something similar on “Days In The East”:\n\nI’m about to ride through the old hood right now\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/9HFya_oB_X/?hl=en", "annotation_id": "9195330", "url": "https://genius.com/9195330", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "I’m with the posse back at it, nigga (Whoop) That mean Kamaiyah goin’ platinum, nigga (Whoop)", "vault_lines": ["I'm with the posse back at it, nigga (Whoop)", "That mean Kamaiyah goin' platinum, nigga (Whoop)"], "meaning": "YG implies that he and Drake are back with another banger and Kamaiyah will score her first platinum song.\n\nYG’s hit song “Who Do You Love” from his debut album My Krazy Life, (which also featured an a verse from Drizzy Drake) went platinum on November 25, 2014.", "annotation_id": "9195329", "url": "https://genius.com/9195329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "All up in my face, you not from the clique Give me space, we might rob your shit Most hated, baby they don’t love the clique I thought they love you when you make it This some other shit, yeah Yeah, yeah, this some other shit", "vault_lines": ["All up in my face, you not from the clique", "Yeah, in every song I said this shit", "Give me space, we might rob your shit", "Most hated, baby they don't love the clique", "I thought they love you when you make it", "This some other shit, yeah", "Yeah, yeah, this some other shit"], "meaning": "After the success of his debut album My Krazy Life, YG thought everyone from family to friends to fans, would wish him nothing but happiness. Unfortunately for YG, he’s had to learn the hard way.\n\nIn 2015, YG was shot in a studio in L.A.. In an interview with Billboard, he commented on the shooting and how it’s affected his relationships:\n\nI have trust issues. For reasons, though, plenty of reasons. Like, I don’t know who shot me. I trust nobody across the board.  If I ain’t with you almost every day, if you ain’t pulling up, if I ain’t calling you, I don’t trust you, especially now. I know a lot of people be mad, jealous or whatever it is, some people feel like I’m not touchable but I’m still out here, and that make it worse.", "annotation_id": "9195317", "url": "https://genius.com/9195317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Please, please tell me why you always hatin’ Why you hatin’? Please tell me why you always hatin’ Please, please tell me why you always hatin’ Why you hatin’? Please tell me why you always hatin’ Please, please tell me why you always hatin’ Why you hatin’? Please tell me why you always hatin’ Please, please tell me why you always hatin’ Why you hatin’? Please tell me why you always hatin’", "vault_lines": ["Please, please tell me why you always hatin'", "Please tell me why you always hatin'"], "meaning": "A hater is a person that can’t be happy for another person’s success. Instead of people being happy for him, haters keep talking down on YG.\n\nYG’s endured his fair share of haters throughout his life. In 2015, he was shot in the hip while recording in an L.A studio, and in 2014, local police departments in Southern California cancelled his shows because they considered him to be “dangerous.” He commented:\n\nThat’s just bullshit. They just hating. All my shows, they have been smooth. There ain’t nothing going on. They canceled my other shows, and I know it’s somebody behind the scenes and they feel some type of way about me. I’m out here doing nothing to nobody. I don’t know what they seeing and hearing, but I’m still crazy. People trying to blackball me, and I got shot? Studios, they don’t want to book me. They need extra security, what?", "annotation_id": "9195240", "url": "https://genius.com/9195240", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Hunnid 4 times how it go down", "vault_lines": ["Hunnid 4 times how it go down"], "meaning": "Drake opens his verse by alluding to the common phrase YG uses, “YG 4 hunnid!”, which is a reference to the 400 block of Spruce Street where YG grew up.\n\nIn 2016, YG also launched a clothing brand, titled 4 Hunnid.", "annotation_id": "9195229", "url": "https://genius.com/9195229", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Runnin’ up the numbers like Ayesha’s nigga", "vault_lines": ["Runnin' up the numbers like Ayesha's nigga"], "meaning": "Drake references Ayesha Curry, wife of NBA all-star, and arguably the best shooter of all time, Stephen Curry.\n\nJust as Curry is setting records and “runnin' up numbers”—such as his single season 3pt record or most points scored in an overtime period—Drake’s also moving numbers. His last album sold 852,000 units, 1.07 million with streams, and became the all-time most played artist on Spotify.", "annotation_id": "9195191", "url": "https://genius.com/9195191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "On mamas, man I did it with my own sound And I got my own city that I hold down", "vault_lines": ["On mamas, man I did it with my own sound", "And I got my own city that I hold down"], "meaning": "“On mamas” is popular Bay Area (especially Oakland) slang. It means “on everything” as in “I’m hella serious” or “this is not a lie”\n\nFrom dabbling in simp-style music, to the island-fused sound, or even trap-inspired, Drake’s crafted a sound that mixes all of them into one, as evident on his latest album, Views.\n\nWhile in the process of creating his own sound, he always supported his city. Songs like “Know Yourself” and “Weston Road Flows” show his more-recent affection to his city, while “5AM in Toronto” and “305 to My City” show he’s been passionate about his city for awhile.", "annotation_id": "9195164", "url": "https://genius.com/9195164", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Got a couple DMs I done slid in already", "vault_lines": ["Got a couple DMs I done slid in already"], "meaning": "Drake has been caught for sliding into celebrity DMs many times in the past. In a January 2015  interview with CBS Miami 560 WQAM, adult actress Mia Khalifa not-so-subtly hinted at the rapper’s advances:\n\n\nWhat famous person has tried to contact you?\nOh I would never reveal that.\n\nYou can blow ‘em up, don’t worry.\nNo way, I would never reveal that, that’s rude.\n\nGive me a name it rhymes with?\nOh my God, it rhymes with “Rake.”\n\n\nDrake previously alluded to ‘DMing’ in “U With Me?,” a track from his 4th studio album, Views.\n\nOn some DMX shit\nI group DM my exes", "annotation_id": "9195162", "url": "https://genius.com/9195162", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Why You Always Hatin?", "line": "Friends and business don’t mix, so I’m brotherless", "vault_lines": ["Friends and business don't mix, so I'm brotherless"], "meaning": "YG knows that involving friends with his business isn’t a wise move. In 2015, he and close friend/collaborator DJ Mustard got into it on social media after Mustard claimed he wasn’t paid for producing most of his debut album, My Krazy Life. Both parties have since worked out their differences.\n\nThis is also reminiscent of something Biggie said on “Ten Crack Commandments”:\n\n7: this rule is so underrated\nKeep your family and business completely separated\nMoney and blood don’t mix…", "annotation_id": "9195160", "url": "https://genius.com/9195160", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I Want This Forever", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-i-want-this-forever-lyrics", "genius_title": "I Want This Forever", "genius_id": 1547, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I Want This Forever", "line": "I’m on them high heels and I am no Bootsy", "vault_lines": ["I'm on them high heels and I am no Bootsy"], "meaning": "P-Funk bassist Boosty Collins has been known to rock his share of platform shoes. Note the “Collins” that continues in the next line", "annotation_id": "61143", "url": "https://genius.com/61143", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I Want This Forever", "line": "I swear I ain’t lyin’ like this is my cubs", "vault_lines": ["I swear I ain't lyin' like this is my cubs"], "meaning": "This is a play on word with “lion” A baby lion is a cub. The word “lyin” is pronounced the same as “lion”", "annotation_id": "51225", "url": "https://genius.com/51225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "status": "done", "targets": 30, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Meek-mill-rico-lyrics", "genius_title": "R.I.C.O.", "genius_id": 2024226, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "We might just get hit with the R.I.C.O", "vault_lines": ["we might just get hit with the R.I.C.O"], "meaning": "Vinylz and CuBeatz made the beat. So I heard the beat already with Drake on it. Vinylz sent it to me and said, “You need to add a little breakdown.” What I added was supposed to be for a chorus, but they used it as an outro. Once I sent that, I heard Meek Mill on it when it came out. I’m not even sure whose song it was first. But I heard Drake on it first. This happened probably the end of 2014, early 2015.", "annotation_id": "8715034", "url": "https://genius.com/8715034", "state": "verified", "votes": 185, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "[Verse 1: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Meek made headlines on Twitter when he claimed Drake’s raps are ghostwritten. According to Meek, he found out that Drake didn’t write this verse after the album was released and said he would’ve removed Drake’s verse from the album had he known.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623700698509758464\n\nSo, who did write the verse? According to Meek, Quentin Miller did, but Quentin refuted this claim in a blog post.", "annotation_id": "7416527", "url": "https://genius.com/7416527", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I can hear echoes from feds on this beat from informants I think they recording Shhh, listen, you hear that?", "vault_lines": ["I can hear echoes from feds on this beat from informants", "I think they recording", "Shhh, listen, you hear that?"], "meaning": "When a phone conversation is tapped, the listeners can usually hear some background noise like echoes, static, or clicking. These noises are produced when two conductors, like a wiretap on a phone line, are connected.\n\nWhile creating this song in the studio, Meek heard some suspicious background noise on the beat which made him assume that the police were listening in and recording their session. Although this seems ironic as they are already recording themselves, Meek already took precaution to not self-incriminate himself.", "annotation_id": "7249243", "url": "https://genius.com/7249243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "All theses choppers poppin’, niggas wildin’, violence Why we even got to take it here? Why we even got to play these games? Run up on me, catch a facial there It’s the Chasers, what you thought?", "vault_lines": ["All theses choppers poppin', niggas wildin', violence", "Why we even got to take it here?", "Why we even got to play these games?", "Run up on me, catch a facial there", "It's the Chasers, what you thought?"], "meaning": "Statistically, Meek’s hometown of Philadelphia is one of the most violent in the nation:\n\n\n\nIn his mind, his Dreamchasers crew is no exception if given the right circumstances.", "annotation_id": "7247306", "url": "https://genius.com/7247306", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "How he hate me when I never met the man, woo!", "vault_lines": ["How he hate me when I never met the man, woo!"], "meaning": "The underlying subject of this sneak diss is possibly Charlamagne Tha God, a notorious critic of Drake despite never having formally met him.\n\nWhy so much hate? Scholars suggest that we envy the perfect lives of celebrities and “take pleasure in their downfall” even though we don’t personally know them.", "annotation_id": "7246251", "url": "https://genius.com/7246251", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I had to drop this to make it clear That I got it locked like Jamaican hair", "vault_lines": ["I had to drop this to make it clear", "That I got it locked like Jamaican hair"], "meaning": "It is common for Jamaican people to style their hair into dreadlocks, or matted coils of hair, due to the country’s heavy Rastafarian influence.\n\n\n\nMeek plays on the dual meaning of “lock” to say that he has the rap game secure and under control. He used the same simile in “Middle of the Summer”:\n\nNarcs rushin', cops lock you like Jamaican hair'", "annotation_id": "7192976", "url": "https://genius.com/7192976", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I come through my block like I’m Rico Poppin’ a wheelie, no squealie, can’t talk to the cops, that’s illegal", "vault_lines": ["I come through my block like I'm Rico", "Poppin' a wheelie, no squealie, can't talk to the cops, that's illegal"], "meaning": "Meek compares himself to Rico, a Harlem drug kingpin played by Cam'ron in the movie Paid in Full.\n\nRico was a bit reckless on his motorcycle, and Meek’s popped a few wheelies of his own, but he’s also taking a shot at Rico because he turned out to be an informant.\n\n\n\n“Rico” also means “rich” in Spanish and Portuguese.", "annotation_id": "7192399", "url": "https://genius.com/7192399", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "The girl of your dreams to me is probably not a challenge", "vault_lines": ["The girl of your dreams to me is probably not a challenge"], "meaning": "It isn’t just Drake’s wealth and fame that helps him effortlessly attract beautiful women; his music is also very female friendly. Whether he’s pouring his heart out on ballads like “Doing It Wrong” or showing vulnerability on “Marvin’s Room”, women seem to connect with it.\n\nMy reality is brighter than your dreams are\nI gotcha dream girl ridin in ya dream car\n\nWhen Meek first heard the line, he misunderstood Drake’s intentions a bit:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/meekmill/status/623710557191802880", "annotation_id": "7189525", "url": "https://genius.com/7189525", "state": "accepted", "votes": 142, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "We came up from nothing We started on list’s most wanted but now it’s the Forbes list We really was doing shit", "vault_lines": ["We came up from nothing", "We started on list's most wanted but now it's the Forbes list", "We really was doing shit"], "meaning": "Meek Mill was arrested on drug and gun charges in 2008 and spent eight months in jail before being put on a five-year probation.\n\nHowever, he has since eschewed his criminal ways, joined Maybach Music Group, and made Forbes' List of “Hip-Hop Cash Princes” in 2014.\n\nIn an interview with HipHopDX he stated that his jail sentence helped him:\n\nBefore I went to jail, I used to be a slouch. I used to smoke weed; things like that. Get high, hang on corners, play with guns – do a lot of dumb stuff. It took me to sit down in jail one time and use my brain and think. That’s the best thing jail did for me.\n\nAlso interesting to note that Meek appeared frequently on the Philly’s Most Wanted compilation series.", "annotation_id": "7187765", "url": "https://genius.com/7187765", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "For my teachers that said I wouldn’t make it here I spend a day what you make a year", "vault_lines": ["For my teachers that said I wouldn't make it here", "I spend a day what you make a year"], "meaning": "Though Meek stretched the truth on his girlfriend’s earnings, it’s safe to say he’s spent more than $45,000 – the average annual salary of a teacher – in one day. However, when on dates with Nicki, if the bill gets upwards of $50,000 he tends to split it with her.\n\nBiggie had a similar chip on his shoulder in “Juicy”:\n\nYeah, this album is dedicated\nTo all the teachers that told me I’d never amount to nothing", "annotation_id": "7187646", "url": "https://genius.com/7187646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I was like more a Rich Porter, no double-crossing on my peoples I take an M to the table and split it with my niggas equal I’m back on that hood shit, yeah that wish you would shit", "vault_lines": ["I was like more a Rich Porter, no double-crossing on my peoples", "I take an M to the table and split it with my niggas equal", "I'm back on that hood shit, yeah that wish you would shit", "To the people that think that I owe you shit", "Payback's a bitch and you know that shit"], "meaning": "It must be great to be friends with Drake or with Meek!\n\nRich Porter was a Harlem drug dealer in the mid 80’s known for his extravagant lifestyle. He would later be murdered by his friend and drug dealing partner. The movie Paid In Full, referenced in the previous lines, was based on his life.\n\nMeek finds himself in a similar situation, minus the untrustworthy friends. He has an entire track about Rich Porter and has referenced him several times in other songs:\n\nWhen I step up in the club I’m feelin’ like Rich, Porter that is\n\n\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/605181212030726145", "annotation_id": "7187611", "url": "https://genius.com/7187611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "Today I woke up with my dream girl, she rich as a Beatle", "vault_lines": ["Today I woke up with my dream girl, she rich as a Beatle"], "meaning": "Meek has been thirsting over his girlfriend Nicki Minaj for a long time, and has called her his dream girl before.\n\nHowever, like many rappers, Meek stretches the truth in terms of earnings: Nicki’s net worth is estimated at about $70 million dollars, while all four of The Beatles are estimated over $200 million each.\n\nhttps://instagram.com/p/4NmMLLikEc/?taken-by=meekmill", "annotation_id": "7187558", "url": "https://genius.com/7187558", "state": "pending", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "They gon’ go Tony Montana and cop them some Shaq at the free throws", "vault_lines": ["They gon' go Tony Montana and cop them some Shaq at the free throws"], "meaning": "Tony Montana is the oft-referenced main character from the movie Scarface – a cocaine kingpin revered by many gangster rappers.\n\nHis large quantity of the drug, which is often packaged in “bricks,” can be compared to the number of bricks in former NBA star Shaquille O'Neal’s career at the free throw line. Shaq shot just 52.7% from the line during his career, and missed a whopping total of 5,317 over the twenty year span.\n\n\n\nMontana’s involvement in organized crime also ties into the R.I.C.O. theme of the song.", "annotation_id": "7187487", "url": "https://genius.com/7187487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 127, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "But they’re from the way fam, there’s not much to say fam", "vault_lines": ["But they're from the way fam, there's not much to say fam"], "meaning": "Galloway is an area in the West Hill section of Scarborough, Toronto that P. Reign and much of his crew grew up in.", "annotation_id": "7187484", "url": "https://genius.com/7187484", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I’m talking that cross you that fade you fourth quarter Like Jordan, we back on that bullshit", "vault_lines": ["I'm talking that cross you that fade you fourth quarter", "Like Jordan, we back on that bullshit"], "meaning": "Michael Jordan’s signature crossover and “fadeaway” shot were deadly moves in the Hall of Famer’s arsenal. He was also known for his late game heroics – specifically, in game six of the 1998 NBA finals vs. the Utah Jazz, when Jordan hit the game winner on a crossover fadeaway. It was the last shot of his final game with the Bulls, hence the “bullshit” pun.\n\n\n\nJordan also famously left the NBA and the Chicago Bulls, before coming out of retirement to rejoin the Bulls and win another three championships; “we back on that bull”.", "annotation_id": "7187471", "url": "https://genius.com/7187471", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "Meek Milly! ’Cause we in the field with them birds like we play for the Eagles", "vault_lines": ["'Cause we in the field with them birds like we play for the Eagles"], "meaning": "Meek is a big fan of his hometown football team, the Philadelphia Eagles, often called “The Birds” locally. “Bird” is also slang for bricks of cocaine. The’s also the wordplay of “the field” and “the Phil.”\n\n\n\nMeek previously remixed Wiz Khalifa’s “Black and Yellow” in a green and white homage to his own team.", "annotation_id": "7187434", "url": "https://genius.com/7187434", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I’ve been up for way too many days", "vault_lines": ["I've been up for way too many days"], "meaning": "At the time this verse dropped, Drake was in the middle of recording his second “album” of 2015, Views From The 6, and we’ve seen Drake’s work ethic in the past:\n\n\nI've been up for four days, getting money both ways, Dirty and clean,― Drake (Ft. Jay Z) – Light Up\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHowever, his penchant for turning up on weekdays may have something to do with this as well.", "annotation_id": "7187405", "url": "https://genius.com/7187405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "Madonna’s a ting I know and I’m the king of pop", "vault_lines": ["Madonna’s a ting I know and I’m the king of pop"], "meaning": "Madonna has long been known as the “Queen of Pop.” After titling a song after her, and receiving an awkward surprise kiss from the Queen at Coachella Music Festival, Drake’s ready to extrapolate this relationship into another claim for his musical crown.", "annotation_id": "7187395", "url": "https://genius.com/7187395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "Everyone home for the summer, so let’s not do nothing illegal", "vault_lines": ["Everyone home for the summer, so let's not do nothing illegal"], "meaning": "A good number of Drake’s people have been locked up over the past few years, from fellow Toronto rapper P. Reign to Drake’s homie Baka, and even Meek Mill. Drake wants to make sure that everybody stays out of trouble so they can all go in this summer.", "annotation_id": "7187389", "url": "https://genius.com/7187389", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I go make 50 million then I give some millions to my people", "vault_lines": ["I go make 50 million then I give some millions to my people"], "meaning": "We know Drake has respect for his friends and makes sure they’re making money too, whether that be his women, family, or OVO crew.\n\nDrake could also be hinting at his past ‘Club Paradise Tour’ which was rumoured to make approximately $47 million.\n\nI should run a clinic for niggas that think that they’re winning\nOn some coach shit, 50Ms for a three month road trip", "annotation_id": "7187371", "url": "https://genius.com/7187371", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "OVO, East End, Reps Up,", "vault_lines": ["OVO, East End, Reps Up,"], "meaning": "It’s not a Drake verse without shoutouts to his crew, town, or affiliated artists. Here he has all three, nodding to his OVO crew, the East End of Toronto, and close friend P. Reign’s label/squad Reps Up.\n\n\n\nDrake and P. Reign", "annotation_id": "7187366", "url": "https://genius.com/7187366", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "My dad is from Memphis, and I am the king I should probably just move into Graceland", "vault_lines": ["My dad is from Memphis, and I am the king", "I should probably just move into Graceland"], "meaning": "The “King of Rock and Roll” Elvis Presley used to live in a Memphis mansion dubbed Graceland. Elvis died there in 1977, and the estate was declared a National Historic Landmark in 2006. It is now a popular tourist attraction.\n\n\n\nSince his dad, Dennis Graham, is a former musician from Memphis, Drake feels he has a rightful claim to the throne.", "annotation_id": "7187360", "url": "https://genius.com/7187360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "They told me to tell you you mans are some wastemans And stay in your place fam", "vault_lines": ["They told me to tell you you mans are some wastemans", "And stay in your place fam"], "meaning": "“Waste[man]” is Jamaican slang for an individual who wastes their own and others' time/space; worthless. In other words, someone pathetic and without ambition.\n\nTerms like “fam” and “wasteman” have been largely adopted in Toronto and the UK, the latter of which Drake seems to have really become a fan of since his recent co-sign of UK grime artist Skepta.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/GRMDAILY/status/614186379250171904", "annotation_id": "7187359", "url": "https://genius.com/7187359", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "Old ways, new women, gotta keep a balance", "vault_lines": ["Old ways, new women, gotta keep a balance"], "meaning": "When Drizzy released his debut album Thank Me Later, one lyric in particular got everyone talking: when he professed his love for Nicki Minaj, now Meek’s girlfriend. Drake has moved on to new women, but continues to deny any new friends.", "annotation_id": "7187355", "url": "https://genius.com/7187355", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "Y’all niggas gettin’ too old for this Please don’t think nobody notices", "vault_lines": ["Please don't think nobody notices", "Y'all niggas gettin' too old for this"], "meaning": "The leader of new school has long thought that there are some rappers overstaying their welcome. Back in 2011, he quipped on “Dreams Money Can Buy”:\n\nAnd I feel like lately it went from top 5 to remaining 5 / My favorite rappers either lost it or ain’t alive\n\nThough he has had various beefs with many older rappers – namely Pusha T, Jay Z, Diddy, and Birdman – this line is indicative of the rap game in general, as so many of Drake’s lyrics are.", "annotation_id": "7187353", "url": "https://genius.com/7187353", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I’m on probation so let me not talk what’s illegal (Switch it up)", "vault_lines": ["I'm on probation so let me not talk what's illegal (Switch it up)"], "meaning": "Meek Mill was recently released from prison, and is still facing a probationary period. So, he doesn’t want to inform anyone that he is still up to his street ways and give his DA a reason to lock him up again.", "annotation_id": "7187352", "url": "https://genius.com/7187352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "Yeah, views, views, views Yeah, yeah, alright", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, views, views, views"], "meaning": "A reference to Drake’s highly anticipated fourth album, Views, which was finally released on April 29th, 2016.", "annotation_id": "7187334", "url": "https://genius.com/7187334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 319, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I’m building Never-Never Land", "vault_lines": ["I’m building Never-Never Land"], "meaning": "Michael Jackson famously named his house “Never Land Ranch.“ As the newly self-proclaimed “King of Pop,” he notes that it’s his turn to do the same.\n\nDrake actually lives in a 7.7 million dollar complex he bought in Hidden Hills, CA, that he has named the “Yolo Estate.” He’s also building a world-class mansion in Toronto.", "annotation_id": "7187333", "url": "https://genius.com/7187333", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "I’ve been counted out so many times, I couldn’t count it Funny how now my accountant is havin’ trouble tryna count it", "vault_lines": ["I've been counted out so many times, I couldn't count it", "Funny how now my accountant is havin' trouble tryna count it"], "meaning": "This “Started from the Bottom”-esque braggadocio uses counting to show how he overcame marginalization to make so much money that it gives his accountant fits trying to keep track of it.\n\n\n\nIn music accountant news around the time of this song’s release, Rihanna and her former financial managers just settled on $10 million for mismanaging her money. She later threw shade in their direction in her “Bitch Better Have My Money” music video.", "annotation_id": "7187291", "url": "https://genius.com/7187291", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "R.I.C.O.", "line": "To the people that think that I owe you shit Payback’s a bitch and you know that shit", "vault_lines": ["To the people that think that I owe you shit", "Payback's a bitch and you know that shit"], "meaning": "As far as things-that-are-bitches go, payback falls slightly behind life and karma, but you should still be careful what you wish for.\n\n\n\nThe use of payback is also a play on the fact that he doesn’t “owe you shit,” hence not getting paid back.", "annotation_id": "6737597", "url": "https://genius.com/6737597", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
