{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To The Floor", "line": "Shit just changed forever first I get a lot of dough Then I end up with your woman that’s just how it’s gotta go", "vault_lines": ["Shit just changed forever first I get a lot of dough", "Then I end up with your woman that's just how it's gotta go"], "meaning": "His life changed forever with this fame and money and he says first i got a lot of money then he’ll end up with with your girl cause he knows he’s the hottest dude out", "annotation_id": "115610", "url": "https://genius.com/115610", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Black Magic", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Jd-era-black-magic-lyrics", "genius_title": "Black Magic", "genius_id": 72141, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Black Magic", "line": "On my Flo-Rida shit tell’in everyone to get low", "vault_lines": ["On my Flo-Rida shit tell'in everyone to get low"], "meaning": "Reference to the infamous Flo Rida song Low\n\n\n\nIn JD Era’s case he is telling everyone to get low to avoid the metaphorical bullets that he is about to spray with his lyrics.", "annotation_id": "2894137", "url": "https://genius.com/2894137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Black Magic", "line": "It must be an omen The curse so tragic Til they give me a Grammy Then poof, black magic", "vault_lines": ["It must be an omen", "The curse so tragic", "Til they give me a Grammy", "Then poof, black magic"], "meaning": "The people in his city are constantly saying that Toronto doesn’t get enough love in the music scene (to the point where their music scene “must be cursed”).\n\n\n\nNice foreshadowing in this verse as Drake eventually received a Grammy for Best Rap Album in 2012 for Take Care\n\nHowever, when someone from their city finally reaches the pinnacles they planned to, their fans will turn on them and say they sold themselves out. This “selling themselves out” often involves, in fans eyes, the artist giving themselves up to dark forces such as the infamous “Illuminati” (an accusation which Drake has been accused of a few times).\n\n\n\nBlack magic is defined as the use of supernatural powers for evil and selfish purposes according to the Encyclopedia of Occultism and Parapsychology, so Drake plays with this fallacious idea, in a tongue in cheek way, to tie his verse in with the title of the song.", "annotation_id": "2881629", "url": "https://genius.com/2881629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "status": "done", "targets": 12, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/J-hus-who-told-you-lyrics", "genius_title": "Who Told You", "genius_id": 9203250, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "Had a mad life, I could write a journal But I can’t lie, I love the journey", "vault_lines": ["Had a mad life, I could write a journal", "But I can't lie, I love the journey"], "meaning": "Had a mad life, I could write a journal\n\nJ Hus is describing how he had a difficult upbringing and how life wasn’t easy for him. He lived a chaotic life which was full of danger.\n\nBut I can’t lie, I love the journey\n\nJ Hus flips the tone here. Instead of letting the previous chaos and danger get to his head, he’s reflecting on it. J Hus is explaining how even though his life was difficult, he grew from it. His difficult upbringing helped to develop his character.", "annotation_id": "38731138", "url": "https://genius.com/38731138", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "Block’s so hot like inferno", "vault_lines": ["Block's so hot like inferno"], "meaning": "Could be a reference to towering inferno, a film where a big building catches on fire, a block of flats traditionally look similarly to the building in the film", "annotation_id": "35766728", "url": "https://genius.com/35766728", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "Had some issues that were internal", "vault_lines": ["Had some issues that were internal"], "meaning": "Hus says he’s been battling mental challenges lately, and expresses a similar sentiment in his deeply introspective track “Deeper Than Rap” off his chart-topping Big Conspiracy album:\n\n“ I was fresh from a war but it was internal”", "annotation_id": "28828059", "url": "https://genius.com/28828059", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "Comme ci, comme ça", "vault_lines": ["I want the best, not comme ci, comme ça"], "meaning": "In this context “not comme ci, comme ça” translates from French to English as\"not like this, like that\".", "annotation_id": "28784163", "url": "https://genius.com/28784163", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "You feel like ecstasy", "vault_lines": ["You feel like ecstasy"], "meaning": "Ecstasy is defined as “an overwhelming feeling of great happiness or joyful excitement”.\n\n3,4-Methylenedioxymethamphetamine (MDMA), is a drug also commonly known as ecstasy. The beneficial pharmacological effects include altered sensations, increased energy, empathy, and pleasure. When taken by mouth, effects begin in 30 to 45 minutes and last three to six hours.", "annotation_id": "28784134", "url": "https://genius.com/28784134", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "You feel like ecstasy", "vault_lines": ["You feel like ecstasy"], "meaning": "Ecstasy is defined as “an overwhelming feeling of great happiness or joyful excitement”.\n\n3,4-Methylenedioxymethamphetamine (MDMA), is a drug also commonly known as ecstasy. The beneficial pharmacological effects include altered sensations, increased energy, empathy, and pleasure. When taken by mouth, effects begin in 30 to 45 minutes and last three to six hours.", "annotation_id": "28782485", "url": "https://genius.com/28782485", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "You got a fat pum-pum, I got a long Johnson", "vault_lines": ["You got a fat pum-pum, I got a long Johnson"], "meaning": "A ‘fat pum pum’ is referring to a specific desirable shape/design/feel of a woman’s vagina – often also referred to as a ‘pussy’ (cat)… and “Long Johnson” is the phonetic translation of a noise produced by a cat, popularised by the episode “Faith Hilling” from South Park. Johnson' is also a slang term for a penis. J Hus often uses double meanings and idioms within his lyrics.\n\n.jpg)", "annotation_id": "28767690", "url": "https://genius.com/28767690", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "Touch my forehead, chest, left shoulder Then right side, pray my brothers are good outside", "vault_lines": ["Touch my forehead, chest, left shoulder", "Then right side, pray my brothers are good outside"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to the “Sign of the Cross,“ a ritual blessing made by members of some branches of Christianity. In this case, Drake prays his friends are staying out of trouble. This is extra surprising given Drake is Jewish.", "annotation_id": "28760291", "url": "https://genius.com/28760291", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "Let me hold your controlla, I am not one of these controlling guys I want you to touch road with the girls-dem, and socialize Enjoy your life, your backside is so fit, it opens eyes I know the vibes, I know the vibes", "vault_lines": ["I know the vibes, I know the vibes", "Let me hold your controlla, I am not one of these controlling guys", "I want you to touch road with the girls-dem, and socialize", "Enjoy your life, your backside is so fit, it opens eyes"], "meaning": "Drake references his 2016 smash-hit “Controlla” which was one of the hits-of-the-summer, much like this is set to be.\n\nMuch like video game controller, Drake speaks on how he is able to control this girls movements whilst dancing with her. However, despite being able to control her physically, he ensures he won’t be the type to control her mentally and not have fun with her friends.\n\nBut Drake being a veteran of the game knows that women have to let loose sometimes which he endorses. On the single “Nice For What” he raps:\n\nWith your phone out, gotta hit them angles\nWith your phone out, snappin' like you Fabo\nAnd you showin' off, but it’s alright\nAnd you showin' off, but it’s alright (Alright!)\nIt’s a short life, yeah", "annotation_id": "28753098", "url": "https://genius.com/28753098", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "All the ratchet gyals wan’ pattern up All the posh gyals get ratchet for me If you love me, you’re clappin’ for me If you throw it, I catch it, trust me", "vault_lines": ["All the ratchet gyals wan' pattern up", "All the posh gyals get ratchet for me", "If you love me, you're clappin' for me", "If you throw it, I catch it, trust me"], "meaning": "Being from the hood and making it out, now being a wealthy rapper, Hus understands that the hood-girls – ratchet alluding to somewhat of a “ghetto diva” – want to use him to get their money up also.\n\nHus has previously explores the topic of getting a higher calibre of women since he became a famous rapper – notoriously, posh girls. In this case, to catch his attention, posh girls become “ghetto divas”. From his Common Sense album, the cut “Sweet Cheeks” dedicates an entire song to the matter:\n\nTold my nigga put down his balaclava\nWould you not rather\nFuck with a posh tata from Upminster?\nHer pops a minister, yeah, she’s cute but she’s so sinister\nWhen she give you that look, saying go finish her\nBlud, you’re moist if you don’t finish her\n\nClapping for someone means to support them in their successes after witnessing their hardwork, which Hus would want his girl to do for him. He would also like them to “clap their ass” and twerk for him. This could also be heard as “clap it for me”. To “clap someone/something” means to let off your firearm. If Hus' friends shoot their opps for him, he’ll know that’s love.\n\n“Throwing it back” alludes to moving into a position where a girl backs up into a guy to start whining on him. To “catch the throw” means to accept the whine and dance with them. Hus knows he could get anyone in the party therefore rejects a lot of advances, but ensures he won’t reject this one.", "annotation_id": "28753009", "url": "https://genius.com/28753009", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "So Lilah Pi, don’t make my eye cry", "vault_lines": ["So Lilah Pi, don't make my eye cry"], "meaning": "Lilah Pi, known musically by her first name, is a British multimedia artist and seemingly one of Drake’s confidants.\n\nFirst, the UK singer was featured as the helmet-wearing female on the cover of Drake’s April 2023 single “Search & Rescue.” Next, he name drops her on “Who Told You” and gives her a shoutout on his Instagram story minutes later, posting a picture of her with the caption:\n\nMore life to the gyal that’s impossible to duplicate. My inspo my confidant my best mate my heart @lilahpi 🥹💘happy birthday\n\nDrake also shouted her out his June 2022 song on “Flight’s Booked”:\n\nLilah\nI know it’s getting late\nAnd I’m struggling to let go\nAlthough there’s distance between us\nThere’s no place I’d rather be\n\nInterestingly, “Who Told You” was released on Lilah’s birthday, which is June 8, 1998.", "annotation_id": "28753001", "url": "https://genius.com/28753001", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Who Told You", "line": "Who told you bad man don’t dance? Who told you gangsters don’t dance? Even with a wap on my hip, I dance Bad man, take another sip and dance Two left feet, don’t trip and dance", "vault_lines": ["Who told you bad man don't dance?", "Who told you gangsters don't dance?", "Even with a wap on my hip, I dance", "Bad man, take another sip and dance", "Two left feet, don't trip and dance"], "meaning": "The stereotypical demeanour of a “gangsta” would be stone-faced, serious and unable to show emotions. The UK synonym to this would be “roadman”, who are known to refuse to dance at parties. Big Shaq’s satirical single “Man Don’t Dance” explores this well:\n\nShe wanted to vibe, man don’t dance\nLooked in my eyes, man don’t dance\nOne, two step, man don’t dance\nFlex on your ex, man don’t dance\nShe stepped on my creps, man don’t dance\nTouch my chest (‘Low that), man don’t dance\nOne, two step, man don’t dance\nMan don’t dance, man don’t dance\n\nHowever, Hus has flipped the script and has proven to be a thug with an exuberant personality. The same way he tells his fellow thugs it’s okay to let loose, he warns people that this doesn’t make him soft so come close enough and he’ll get violent. This is backed up by him having a weapon on him and by warning his enemies not to “trip”.\n\nHus himself has endorsed gangsters letting loose. In “Dark Vader”, he said:\n\nTell all the gangsta dem to loosen up\n\nAlternatively, no matter who you are, Hus’ riddims bring out that dance in you, whether you like it or not. In “Lean & Bop”, he said:\n\nYo my dance so cool even roadman do it\nMake a young boy and an old man do it", "annotation_id": "28738813", "url": "https://genius.com/28738813", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Girls Need Love (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Summer-walker-and-drake-girls-need-love-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Girls Need Love (Remix)", "genius_id": 4348897, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Girls Need Love (Remix)", "line": "White wine, weed smoke", "vault_lines": ["White wine, weed smoke"], "meaning": "This line seems to be a callback to Drake’s verse on Rihanna’s October 2010 track, “What’s My Name?,” which he was featured on. There, he sings:\n\nThe square root of sixty-nine is eight somethin', right?\n‘Cause I’ve been tryna work it out, oh\nGood weed, white wine, uh\nI come alive in the night time, yeah", "annotation_id": "20554800", "url": "https://genius.com/20554800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
