{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Chris-brown-no-guidance-lyrics", "genius_title": "No Guidance", "genius_id": 4533406, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "line": "Way they hittin’ you, the DM lookin’ violent", "vault_lines": ["Way they hittin' you, the DM lookin' violent"], "meaning": "The phrase to “hit on” is slang for talking with someone you’re sexually attracted to. Some start these conversations with potential partners by “DMing” them on an app called Instagram. More commonly, however, “hit” describes the simple act of striking someone.\n\nIn this bar, Drake refers to the sexual and standard definition of the word. Since so many people are sexually “hittin'” on this girl, he remarks that the her Instagram “DMs” must be filled with physical “violence” from all the slaps.", "annotation_id": "17695666", "url": "https://genius.com/17695666", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "line": "You a lil’ hot girl, you a lil’ sweetie (No, sweet) Sweet like Pearland, sweet like Peachtree (Like that)", "vault_lines": ["You a lil' hot girl, you a lil' sweetie (No, sweet)", "Sweet like Pearland, sweet like Peachtree (Like that)"], "meaning": "Here Drake makes a reference to rapper Megan Thee Stallion who also goes by “Hot Girl Meg.” In the following line, Drake mentions Pearland, which is a city in the Houston area where Megan grew up. Megan also graduated from Pearland high school in 2013. On “No New Friends,” Drake refers to Houston as a “second home” which suggests that he is accustomed to the area:\n\nH-town my second home like I’m James Harden\n\nPeachtree is a location within Atlanta, somewhere he is all too familiar with according to his track “From Time” from his 2013 album Nothing Was the Same:\n\nThe one that I needed was Courtney from Hooters on Peachtree\nI’ve always been feeling like she was the piece to complete me\n\nPeachtree is also a well-known iced tea, famous for its rich taste. When he says, “you a lil' sweetie,” it also intentionally sounds like “you a lil' sweet tea.” Drake makes a subtle reference by telling the girl that just like the drink, he thinks that she is also very “sweet.”\n\nDrake is known for dropping locations in various different songs, even including many locations within the song title, such as “9AM in Dallas,” “4PM in Calabasas,” and “6PM in New York.”", "annotation_id": "17383010", "url": "https://genius.com/17383010", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "line": "Flew the coop at seventeen, no guidance", "vault_lines": ["Flew the coop at seventeen, no guidance"], "meaning": "“Flew the coop” means to escape a place that limits freedom. It’s often used to describe children moving out of their parent’s home.\n\nDrake and Chris Brown both dated Rihanna, which caused a mutual resentment between them. Interestingly, RiRi, born February 20, 1988, left her native Barbados to move to the States after she signed with Def Jam in 2005, when she was 17.\n\nDrake captioned an instagram post with this lyric, which may indicate he drove fancy cars at a young age.", "annotation_id": "17347439", "url": "https://genius.com/17347439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "line": "Pretty lil’ thing, you got a bag and now you wildin’", "vault_lines": ["Pretty lil' thing, you got a bag and now you wildin'"], "meaning": "Drake rapped a similar line on his March 2013 track “5AM in Toronto”:\n\nI mean, where you think she at when she ain’t with ya?\nWildin', doin' shit that’s way out of your budget", "annotation_id": "17346542", "url": "https://genius.com/17346542", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "line": "You just took it off the lot, no mileage", "vault_lines": ["You just took it off the lot, no mileage"], "meaning": "Drake seems to imply that his romantic interest lacks sexual experience—“mileage” is slang for sexual encounters.\n\nOn his June 2010 track, “Thank Me Now,” Drake reflected on his sexual encounters:\n\nThat make me wish I had a little less mileage on me", "annotation_id": "17346504", "url": "https://genius.com/17346504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "line": "Six God talk but I ain’t tryna get preachy (No, no-no)", "vault_lines": ["Six God talk but I ain't tryna get preachy (No, no-no)"], "meaning": "Drake’s nickname, “Six God,” stems from the area code of his hometown of Toronto, Canada, which can be either 647 or 416. He first gave himself this title on the eponymous October 2014 track:\n\nI give a fuck about old, boy (Man, that boy old)\nI give a fuck about his squad, boy (Man, that thing old)\nI give a fuck about your mob, boy (Oh)\nI’m the real 6 God, boy (Whoa)\n\nThis line also contradicts a line from Drake’s February 2015 track, “Preach,” where PARTYNEXTDOOR encourages Drake to preach:\n\nYoung nigga preach\nPreach, preach, preach, preach, preach, preach", "annotation_id": "17338130", "url": "https://genius.com/17338130", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "line": "Lil’ baby in her bag, in her Birkin (Ayy)", "vault_lines": ["Lil' baby in her bag, in her Birkin (Ayy)"], "meaning": "The Birkin bag was created in 1983 by Jean-Louis Dumas, who at the time, was serving as chief-executive for high-end fashion boutique, Hermès. The bags are named after actress and singer, Jane Birkin, and are handcrafted from calf leather. They cost anywhere between $12,000 and $300,000.\n\n\n\nThis could also be a reference to rapper Gunna’s February 2019 track “Baby Birkin”:\n\nI have a daughter, I’m buying her baby Birkin\n\nGunna also collaborates frequently with rapper Lil Baby.", "annotation_id": "17338073", "url": "https://genius.com/17338073", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "line": "Before I die, I’m tryna fuck you, baby Hopefully, we don’t have no babies I don’t even wanna go back home Hopefully, I don’t leave you on your own", "vault_lines": ["Before I die, I'm tryna fuck you, baby", "Hopefully, we don't have no babies", "I don't wanna play no games, play no games", "I don't even wanna go back home", "Hopefully, I don't leave you on your own"], "meaning": "The intro and post-chorus samples Che Ecru’s March 2018 song, “Before I Die.” The sample can be heard throughout the song.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/P5wR-EfA0AA?t=12", "annotation_id": "17338012", "url": "https://genius.com/17338012", "state": "accepted", "votes": 222, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guidance", "line": "I don’t wanna play no games, play no games", "vault_lines": ["I don't wanna play no games, play no games", "Play no games (No)", "'Cause I don't wanna (No) play no games, play no games"], "meaning": "This is a nod to Breezy’s feature on Big Sean’s September 2015 track “Play No Games”:\n\nI ain’t like them other niggas (Other niggas)\nAnd I ain’t ‘bout to play no games with you (Play no games)", "annotation_id": "17320311", "url": "https://genius.com/17320311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
