{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "status": "done", "targets": 12, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Snowd4y-wah-gwan-delilah-lyrics", "genius_title": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "genius_id": 10488353, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "I’m two thousand kilometres away", "vault_lines": ["I'm two thousand kilometres away"], "meaning": "The original lyric is “I’m two thousand miles away”, but the imperial unit “miles” is replaced with “kilometres” because Canada uses the metric system.", "annotation_id": "32192682", "url": "https://genius.com/32192682", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "Sorry, he’s wearin’ a shiesty, he’s not beat It’s just too smokey in these streets", "vault_lines": ["Sorry, he's wearin' a shiesty, he's not beat", "It's just too smokey in these streets"], "meaning": "‘beat’ in Toronto slang means ugly. Drake says that his friend isn’t wearing a shiesty (specific type of ski masks worn almost exclusively by gangsters) to hide any ugliness, but rather to hide his face in public.\nThis is because its ‘too smokey’, which means that there is a lot of danger (smoke) for his friend, who might either be a fugitive or involved in gang wars.", "annotation_id": "35315858", "url": "https://genius.com/35315858", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "But girl, tonight, you look so bad, oh, yes, you do", "vault_lines": ["But girl, tonight, you look so bad, oh, yes, you do"], "meaning": "Although as the former line says he is 2000 thousand kilometres away, he is still willing to meet this girl as she looks so “bad” (good)", "annotation_id": "31979525", "url": "https://genius.com/31979525", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "Dundas Square don’t shine as bright as you", "vault_lines": ["Square One don't shine as bright as you", "Dundas Square don't shine as bright as you"], "meaning": "Dundas Square is Toronto’s equivalent of Times Square in NYC. It is full of bright displays that are for advertisements, and can get quite bright at night. Snowd4day is saying that Dundas Square is not as bright as her, complimenting his girl", "annotation_id": "31970930", "url": "https://genius.com/31970930", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "Wah gwan, Delilah, what’s it like in Toronto City?", "vault_lines": ["Yo, wah gwan, Delilah", "Wah gwan, Delilah, what's it like in Toronto City?", "Wah gwan, Sabrina, what's it like in Mississauga?"], "meaning": "Wah gwan, or wagwan, is a phrase in Jamaican Patois meaning “What’s going on?”. Slang in Ontario, Canada, especially Toronto, has been majorly influenced by an influx in immigration from the Caribbean from the 1960s to the 1990s, so this immediately conveys the song’s Toronto theme. This lyric is a parody of the well-known first line of “Hey There Delilah” by the Plain White T’s:\n\nHey there, Delilah, what’s it like in New York City?", "annotation_id": "31970041", "url": "https://genius.com/31970041", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "I just showed my dog your ’Gram He said he knows a man that slapped it, I’m so cheesed Your mademoiselle nights are geeked, I’m bent, low-key", "vault_lines": ["I just showed my dog your 'Gram", "He said he knows a man that slapped it, I'm so cheesed", "Your mademoiselle nights are geeked, I'm bent, low-key"], "meaning": "After introducing an acquaintance to his partner’s Instagram, Drake uncovers that a mutual friend has already engaged in intimate relations with his prospective partner. Drake conveys his disappointment upon realizing that the woman he had regarded as a person of sophistication—a “mademoiselle,” if you will—turns out to be far more promiscuous than he had originally perceived.\n\nThe bar itself carries a triple entendre. “Mademoiselle” is not only a term of endearment but also the name of a renowned fine-dining establishment located in downtown Toronto. Drake subtly references his partner’s behavior following a night out at this bar.\n\n\n\nFurthermore, the pronunciation of “mademoiselle” sounds like “mandem wozzle,” a colloquial expression implying that she has previously engaged in fellatio with many of Drake’s friends and acquaintances during her evenings out.", "annotation_id": "31961590", "url": "https://genius.com/31961590", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "Miyute, don’t be movin’ tough ’cause I will call your Scarborough bluff", "vault_lines": ["Miyute, don't be movin' tough 'cause I will call your Scarborough bluff"], "meaning": "“Miyute” is Toronto slang for a child or someone younger than the speaker. It is often used in a derogatory manner to belittle or undermine the receiver\n\n“I’ll call your Scarborough bluff” is a double entendre, the Scarborough Bluffs is an escarpment in the Scarborough district of Toronto.  While bluffs additionally refer to lies that people tell. To call someone’s bluff refers to catching a person in a lie.", "annotation_id": "31964273", "url": "https://genius.com/31964273", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "Top left, top left", "vault_lines": ["Top left, top left"], "meaning": "Toronto slang for a sincere version of “its true, its true”, as seen in the ouro “I swear it’s, I swear it’s true”.", "annotation_id": "31963276", "url": "https://genius.com/31963276", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "I scan my Presto wit’ no funds Kick me off, you get brunched", "vault_lines": ["I scan my Presto wit' no funds", "Kick me off, you get brunched"], "meaning": "Snowd4y reiterates his animosity towards TTC transit enforcement, explicitly threatening physical violence against any fare inspector he encounters.", "annotation_id": "31961684", "url": "https://genius.com/31961684", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "I spend that CERB, why would I save?", "vault_lines": ["I spend that CERB, why would I save?"], "meaning": "During the COVID-19 pandemic, Snowd4y was among the numerous recipients of Canada’s Canada Emergency Response Benefit, receiving a total of $16,000 in government subsidies. Following a consultation with a financial advisor, he allocated these emergency funds towards purchasing Nike Air Jordans and acquiring a “Jane Baby” diamond-encrusted pendant.", "annotation_id": "31961648", "url": "https://genius.com/31961648", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "Like Tarzan, I am always on the Jane (Always on the Jane)", "vault_lines": ["Like Tarzan, I am always on the Jane (Always on the Jane)"], "meaning": "Snowd4y draws a parallel between himself and the iconic character Tarzan, emphasizing his frequent presence in Toronto’s notoriously dangerous Jane and Finch neighborhood.\n\n\n\nThe bar is also a double entendre, as Snowd4y is always smoking “Mary Jane.”", "annotation_id": "31961622", "url": "https://genius.com/31961622", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wah Gwan Delilah", "line": "And oh, if you’re tryna hit a lick on the TD, RBC, Scotiabank, R.B.C, C.I.B.C, P.C Financial Holler at me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In typical Toronto man fashion, Snowd4y articulates his desire to exploit his partner’s banking details to perpetrate wire fraud across a multitude of Canadian financial institutions.", "annotation_id": "31961608", "url": "https://genius.com/31961608", "state": "pending", "votes": 157, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/The-weeknd-the-zone-lyrics", "genius_title": "The Zone", "genius_id": 54471, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "Got some good things ahead of me when these bad bitches let go", "vault_lines": ["Got some good things ahead of me when these bad bitches let go"], "meaning": "Once these women in Drake’s life leave, he knows a lot of good things will come his way. He believes they’re holding him back from reaching his full potential. Note the juxtaposition between good in ‘'good things’‘ and bad in ’‘bad bitches’'", "annotation_id": "36259721", "url": "https://genius.com/36259721", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "I’ll be making love to her through you So let me keep my eyes closed", "vault_lines": ["So let me keep my eyes closed", "I'll be making love to her through you"], "meaning": "It’s theorized that the Thursday girl (aka Valerie) is a girl Weeknd only sees once a week, and is with another girl every other day of the week. So when Weeknd hooks up with Valerie, he’s not there emotionally, because he’s thinking of his other lover while they’re having sex.", "annotation_id": "25094600", "url": "https://genius.com/25094600", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "[Directed by Abel Tesfaye]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The video contains allusions to the 1997 French-American film Lost Highway during the opening and ending scenes when a car is driving down a dark road with it’s lights on. This doubles as Abel inviting the audience into his world, or the zone. Abel uses this allusion again in the “Tell Your Friends” video.\n\nBefore her journey into music, artist Elissa Mielke starred in the video as Valerie, which she reflects on in an instagram post from 2015. Throughout most of the video, Valerie is in an all white room with a bunch of balloons alone, dressed in light-colored lingerie. In stark contrast, The Weeknd is positioned in a dark industrial setting with a projection of Valerie’s space cast behind him; even in the one small clip when they’re together, looking in the mirror with him taking photos, Abel is able to stand outside in his primary setting and watch the interaction from the projection. This gives him a “god-like” omniscience, watching the angel Valerie from afar.\n\nValerie’s “zone” is portrayed as almost pure and innocent, with all white books on her table, white and gold furniture, and white lighting that give the room a dimensionless, heaven-like appearance. The red objects symbolize bridges to Abel’s “zone:” the red makeup, dress, and alcohol.\n\nBefore Drake’s verse, an open gateway to light or possibly to Valerie’s space is shown behind Abel, who looks to hooded-mentor Drizzy instead. Abel’s “zone,” the true “House of Balloons,” appears when Drake begins rapping: dark, drunk, and messy. Thus, Drake’s verse in this song proves to be the catalyst for Valerie’s destruction, who ends up sitting in her vice-filled red dress crying in solitude. If “The Zone” is Valerie’s vision of her relationship with Abel after “Thursday,” then the end of this video reveals that she has a harsh reality to face.\n\nLastly, Valerie’s eyes are open to Abel’s world and vision at the beginning of the video. However, they close at the end; although Valerie cannot close her heart to The Weeknd, she can’t compete with anyone else, leading to the ongoing battle between the two for the remainder of the project.\n\nA clip of Valerie crying is used in the second Trilogy teaser with “Valerie” as the audio.", "annotation_id": "8470145", "url": "https://genius.com/8470145", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "And I won’t see a damn thing I can’t feel a damn thing But I’ma touch you right I won’t see a damn thing I can’t feel a damn thing But I’ma touch you right (Oh yeah)", "vault_lines": ["And I won't see a damn thing", "I can't feel a damn thing", "But I'ma touch you right", "I won't see a damn thing", "I'ma touch you right (Ooh yeah)", "And I don't give a damn", "So, I won't say a damn thing", "Oh, I can't see a damn thing", "Ooh, I'ma touch you right"], "meaning": "While Abel’s all drugged up, he physically can’t see that much since he’s in his “zone,” leaving his vision distorted. He also keeps his eyes closed so he can vicariously love the woman he wishes were there, the “Lonely Star.”\n\n\n\nAbel also can’t feel the pleasure in having sex with this girl likely due to a numbness from his intoxication as well as his lack of affection for this mistress. He also can’t feel any emotional or physical pain because codeine is a strong painkiller.\n\nBut even though he’s so “fucked up”- he is still going to pleasure the girl in the right way.", "annotation_id": "705116", "url": "https://genius.com/705116", "state": "accepted", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "I left it all behind, baby I didn’t need no one But I’m in need tonight baby I’ve been alone for too long Ohh, oh yeah", "vault_lines": ["I left it all behind, baby", "I didn't need no one", "But I'm in need tonight baby", "I've been alone for too long"], "meaning": "When Abel was 17, he “left one weekend and never came back home” as he mentions in this interview. However, once he got evicted from the place he moved into, he ended up having to bed hop with various girls for a place to stay.\n\nThis night, some time after Thursday with the Lonely Star, could be Abel fiending for sex and for shelter.", "annotation_id": "334973", "url": "https://genius.com/334973", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "And I’ma lean, ’til I fall, ooh yeah And I don’t give a damn I felt the ground before", "vault_lines": ["And I'ma lean, 'til I fall, ooh yeah", "And I don't give a damn", "I felt the ground before"], "meaning": "He’s on that sizzurp, so he’s leanin', and he’ll do it until he passes out, and isn’t worried because he frequently is on codeine.\n\n\n\n“Lean until I fall” a double entendre: He also refers to pushing the limits in his life.  His ultra-rapid rise to stardom has literally made him a wealthy, jet-setting sensation overnight – sitting courtside at basketball games.\n\nThis rapid increase in status and wealth took place so quickly that he can still remember what it was like to be a poor, struggling artist, and it wasn’t that bad.  So he is prepared to push it all to the limit, go too far, or too hard, even at the risk of losing it all, because he’s been there before.\n\nThe Weeknd also refers to this line on “The Fall”.", "annotation_id": "334158", "url": "https://genius.com/334158", "state": "accepted", "votes": 80, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "I’ma touch you right (Ooh yeah) (Just let me sip this slow) I’ma touch you right (Let me get inside my zone) I’ma touch you right (Oh yeah) (Just let me) Ohh ahh, ahh Ooh yeah", "vault_lines": ["But I'ma touch you right", "I'ma touch you right (Ooh yeah)", "Ooh, I'ma touch you right", "Yeah girl, just be you", "So lemme sip this slow", "Just let me get in my zone"], "meaning": "Abel is talking about drinking lean which would make him feel much better about himself. Hence, “inside my zone”.\n\nAlso, Abel is depressed about the girl he is truly in love with but being male he is willing to have sex with this current girl he just has to “get in the zone” but when he does he’s going to touch her in all the right places.", "annotation_id": "330630", "url": "https://genius.com/330630", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "She in love with my crew", "vault_lines": ["She in love with my crew"], "meaning": "This is the t-shirt she wears when she’s done dancing:\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to their song “Crew Love” which was meant to be on House of Balloons (along with “Shot for Me” and “The Ride”), but The Weeknd told Drake to put it on Take Care.", "annotation_id": "328758", "url": "https://genius.com/328758", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "Lips so French, ass so Spanish", "vault_lines": ["Lips so French, ass so Spanish"], "meaning": "The term “French Kissing” was originally synonymous with oral sex and not just tongue kissing; Drake is praising her ability to fellate. “Ass so Spanish” is a reference to a common belief that Spanish women have great asses, and also completes the parallel structure by replacing French with Spanish, two closely associated languages.\n\nIn 2016’s “Party Monster”, the Weeknd sings:\n\nAngelina, lips like Angelina\nLike Selena, ass shaped like Selena\n\nAngeline Jolie owned a vineyard, Château Miraval, in France, and Selena Quintanilla-Pérez was a legendary Hispanic recording artist.", "annotation_id": "328739", "url": "https://genius.com/328739", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "Woah, all these broken hearts on that pole Man, if pole dancing’s an art, you know how many fuckin’ artists I know?", "vault_lines": ["Woah, all these broken hearts on that pole", "Man, if pole dancing's an art, you know how many fuckin' artists I know?"], "meaning": "Whether it be a father who was never around, or a boy who broke their heart, exotic dancers often portray their hearts being broken before choosing their profession.\n\nDancing, in general, is widely regarded as art—even being classified as “performing arts”—but when the dancing is as provocative as pole dancing and stripping, “art” is not a term society would use.\n\nDrake acknowledges that it’s not technically art, by societies standards. As he likes to hangout at strip clubs, he’d know a ton of artists if pole dancing was considered art.", "annotation_id": "328721", "url": "https://genius.com/328721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 129, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "The Zone", "line": "And I do this shit for my hometown, it been going down, it ain’t new That’s that north north, that up top That OVO and that XO", "vault_lines": ["And I do this shit for my hometown, it been going down, it ain't new", "That's that north north, that up top", "That OVO and that XO"], "meaning": "OVO is Drake’s label and XO is The Weeknd’s. When they collaborate, OVOXO is in full swing.\n\n“North North, that up top” refers to Toronto, Canada, since it’s high up in the Northern Hemisphere. Both label heads hail from the city.\n\n“North north” could also refer to where Drake’s father lived in Memphis, where popular rappers and brothers Juicy J and Project Pat commonly say “north north” to refer to their neighborhood in North Memphis.\n\nThe Weeknd sings this line at the end of Echoes of Silence standout “Initiation”.", "annotation_id": "328719", "url": "https://genius.com/328719", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
