{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "I could probably make some stepsisters fuck each other, whew", "vault_lines": ["I could probably make some stepsisters fuck each other, whew"], "meaning": "Even though Step siblings are not directly related, they tend to think of each other as brother and sister. Drake is saying that he has so much swag and game, that he could make them forget the fact that they would other wise be uncomfortable fucking each other. But since Drake asked them too, they will.", "annotation_id": "4047057", "url": "https://genius.com/4047057", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "In L.A, in L.A, in L.A (in L.A) We in the mansion, all day MIA, all the girls with it Late night, they say “Don’t stop, get it, get it” 8 mil’ for the new home", "vault_lines": ["In L.A, in L.A, in L.A (in L.A)", "We in the mansion, all day", "MIA, all the girls with it", "Late night, they say \"Don't stop, get it, get it\"", "8 mil' for the new home"], "meaning": "Double meaning here :\n\n\nHe is at his $6.5 million mansion on Calabasas, L.A and has some freak women from Miami that are down for everything.\nTyga picked up a new crib with a $8 million price tag on Miami and is partying with girls at the famous Mansion Nightclub on South Beach.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe “Don’t stop get it, get it” is a reference to former 2 Live Crew Member who is also from Miami, Uncle Luke’s classic song Doo Doo Brow.", "annotation_id": "4036634", "url": "https://genius.com/4036634", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "See the gold car, she like Raw home", "vault_lines": ["See the gold car, she like Raw home"], "meaning": "Tyga is referring to either his Lamborghini Aventador, or Audi R8 which are wrapped in chrome gold. T-Raw is another nickname of Tyga.", "annotation_id": "4029303", "url": "https://genius.com/4029303", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "New flow, I got a dozen of em", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lately, Drake has become a little more experimental with his flows and creating more of a unique sound.\n\nExamples of this:\n\n\nHis verses on “Believe Me”\n\n“Draft Day”\n“0 to 100”\n“We Made It (Freestyle)”", "annotation_id": "4015384", "url": "https://genius.com/4015384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "Fresh sheets and towels, man she gotta love it", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "These are common necessities, Drake, nothing to make a girl go crazy…", "annotation_id": "4015336", "url": "https://genius.com/4015336", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "[Produced by Nineteen85 and Maya Jane Coles]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Nineteen85 slowed down Mary Jane Cole’s “What They Say”, an electronic track, for the backing of this beat.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMYZI4lK9co#t=92", "annotation_id": "4015033", "url": "https://genius.com/4015033", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "Wonder how I keep comin’ with the Bay number", "vault_lines": ["Wonder how I keep comin' with the Bay number"], "meaning": "Reference to the 187 slang code, which is the California Penal Code that defines the crime of murder, and as most of us know Tyga has been repping 187. He even has a mixtape entitled 187.", "annotation_id": "4008046", "url": "https://genius.com/4008046", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "Niggas see me rolling and their mood change", "vault_lines": ["Niggas see me rolling and their mood change"], "meaning": "Drizzy has delivered four distinct projects in the span of five years, each collection of songs showing a clear progression and growth while still spawning chart-topping hits, and boundary-pushing collaborations. As of February 2014, Drake has released three studio albums that have sold a combined 5 million copies. He has won a Grammy, two Juno Awards for Rap Recording of the Year as well as Best New Artist in 2010 and a BET award. He also has a net worth of $45 million dollars.\n\nAs he previously mentioned in Timbaland’s Say Something:\n\nIt’s funny how someone else’s success brings pain", "annotation_id": "4006889", "url": "https://genius.com/4006889", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "I’ll call you Billie Jean, but what would Mike say?", "vault_lines": ["I'll call you Billie Jean, but what would Mike say?"], "meaning": "Reference to Michael Jackson’s second single from his sixth solo album Thriller. Billie Jean is a composite of all the groupies that would come around and claim to be pregnant with some of the Jackson’s children.\n\nBillie Jean is also a 70’s and 80’s term for women who feverishly stalk male celebrities.", "annotation_id": "4003548", "url": "https://genius.com/4003548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "Talkin’ filets with the truffle butter", "vault_lines": ["Talkin' filets with the truffle butter"], "meaning": "Filets mignons paired with truffle butter are the ultimate choice for a special occasion. This recipe calls for truffle salt, which has visible flakes of the edible fungi. In its simplicity and richness, this is a fantastic dish.", "annotation_id": "4003526", "url": "https://genius.com/4003526", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "Hard to make a song ‘bout somethin’ other than the money", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake has always been a big name in Hip Hop since he began rapping, but in the last year or so, he has really exploded to superstardom. So to quote Wiz Khalifa…\n\nAnd they say all I rap about is bitches and champagne, you would too if every night you seen the same thing\n\nYou might think Drake raps about money too much, but he raps about what he likes and what he sees a lot of…and you better believe both of those things are consisting of money.", "annotation_id": "4002997", "url": "https://genius.com/4002997", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Whatcha Say", "line": "All white like I move things", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is no stranger to wearing all white – most notably in the video for “Started From the Bottom”\n\n\n\nSince the color white is normally associated with cocaine, Drake finishes the line with “move things” – comparing his attire to drug dealers moving coke.\n\n\n\nFun fact: 8 months prior to the release of this song, many thought that Drake was releasing a song titled “All White” due to a live performance of these lyrics.\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/j_58iNyjhF/", "annotation_id": "4001661", "url": "https://genius.com/4001661", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "20 Hoes (Remix)*", "status": "done", "targets": 3, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Future-20-hoes-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "20 Hoes (Remix)*", "genius_id": 3558196, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "20 Hoes (Remix)*", "line": "Real shit, Kylie Jenner, that’s a side piece (Woo) Yeah, I got twenty mothafuckin’ Kylies (Woo) Nigga, I got twenty damn Kylies (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["Real shit, Kylie Jenner, that's a side piece (Woo)", "Yeah, I got twenty mothafuckin' Kylies (Woo)", "Nigga, I got twenty damn Kylies (Woo)", "Yeah, I got twenty damn Kendalls (Twenty, ayy, I got twenty damn, ayy)", "Yeah, I got twenty fuckin' Gigis", "I got twenty mothafuckin' Kylies", "I got twenty, twenty damn Kylies (Woo)", "I got, I got twenty big bodies (Woo)"], "meaning": "Kendall Jenner is a well known supermodel from Los Angeles, California. She’s the older sibling of self made billionaire Kylie Jenner, who was dating Travis Scott upon this track’s release.\n\nDrake previously rapped about Kendall on his March 2017 track, “Sacrifices”:\n\nKendall turned 21, was up the street with 21\nThey could see me online, but they won’t see me on the ones\n\nBoth Kendall and Kylie are friends with Drake, as they are seen together frequently, since their high profile dinner in 2015. The three remain close, despite Drake’s highly publicized rap beef with  the girl’s cousin-in-law Kanye West.\n\nThese lines drew ire from Kanye who viewed it as a personal diss towards both himself and Kylie’s ex Travis Scott:\n\nWhen you played that record for me in the studio that said, ‘Yo, we got some Kylie’s, we got some Kendalls,’ I told you, ‘Yo, Travis is your man. Don’t make no record like that. That man just had a baby with her, that’s gonna be offensive to her. I hit Trav about the ‘XTCY’ record and made sure that we was all good, that we family. So I did tell you not to do that. So period, don’t speak on nobody from my family, nothing that can be even mentioned with my wife, period.", "annotation_id": "18374141", "url": "https://genius.com/18374141", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "20 Hoes (Remix)*", "line": "Yeah, I got twenty fuckin’ Gigis", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I got twenty mothafuckin' Kylies (Woo)", "Yeah, I got twenty damn Kendalls (Twenty, ayy, I got twenty damn, ayy)", "Yeah, I got twenty fuckin' Gigis", "I got twenty mothafuckin' Kylies"], "meaning": "Drake references supermodel and close friend of Kylie and Kendall, Gigi Hadid.", "annotation_id": "14654181", "url": "https://genius.com/14654181", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "20 Hoes (Remix)*", "line": "Young slim baddies and they en vogue (Twenty, twenty, I got)", "vault_lines": ["Young slim baddies and they en vogue (Twenty, twenty, I got)", "I got twenty mothafuckin' Kylies"], "meaning": "“En vogue” is a French term that translates to “fashionable”, or “in fashion”.\n\nEn Vogue is also the name of a popular 90’s R&B girl group (now trio). In 2015, Billboard ranked them as the ninth-best girl group of all time, and two of their singles (“Hold On” and “Don’t Let Go (Love)”) sold over one million copies.\n\n\n\nFurthermore, “en” is both a homophone and the French word for “in”.  This line could therefore refer to Vogue Magazine, which covers multiple topics regarding beauty and fashion. The magazine’s front cover typically has a female celebrity pictured on it. Notable examples include Kendall Jenner and Gigi Hadid, both of whom are referred to in the song.", "annotation_id": "14342104", "url": "https://genius.com/14342104", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rihanna-whats-my-name-lyrics", "genius_title": "What’s My Name?", "genius_id": 1812, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "line": "I heard you good with them soft lips Yeah, you know, word of mouth", "vault_lines": ["I heard you good with them soft lips", "Yeah, you know, word of mouth"], "meaning": "Drake is saying he heard that this girl knows how to work her lips, and has heard it from others' mouths. This could be wordplay based on lips from one’s mouth or her pussy lips downstairs…", "annotation_id": "33052518", "url": "https://genius.com/33052518", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "line": "Hey, boy, I really wanna see if you Can go downtown with a girl like me Hey, boy, I really wanna be with you ’Cause you just my type Ooh, na, na, na, na I need a boy to take it over Lookin’ for a guy to put in work Oh woah, oh woah Hey, boy, I really wanna see if you Can go downtown with a girl like me Hey, boy, I really wanna be with you ’Cause you just my type Ooh, na, na, na, na I need a boy to take it over Lookin’ for a guy to put in work Oh woah, oh woah", "vault_lines": ["Oh na-na, what's my name?", "Hey, boy, I really wanna see if you", "Can go downtown with a girl like me", "Hey, boy, I really wanna be with you", "'Cause you just my type", "Ooh, na, na, na, na", "I need a boy to take it over", "Lookin' for a guy to put in work", "I will let you in", "That's why you take me", "You 'bout to break me"], "meaning": "“Go downtown” means cunnilingus. Rihanna has found the perfect man for her but before she pursues a relationship with him, she needs to be sure he can satisfy her needs.\n\nRih tops it off with a subtle reference to her 2006 sophomore album A Girl Like Me.", "annotation_id": "15404977", "url": "https://genius.com/15404977", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "line": "It’s gettin’ hot, crack a window, air it out I can get you through a mighty long day Soon as you go, the text that I write is gon’ say", "vault_lines": ["It's gettin' hot, crack a window, air it out", "I can get you through a mighty long day", "Soon as you go, the text that I write is gon' say"], "meaning": "Past studies conducted using thermal imaging  showed that face and body temperature increase during long periods of sexual arousing.\n\nAfter a long, stressful day, Drake plans to relieve his girl with cunnilingus and sex.", "annotation_id": "15380328", "url": "https://genius.com/15380328", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "line": "Say you gotta leave, but I know you wanna stay You just waitin’ on the traffic jam to finish, girl The things that we could do in twenty minutes, girl Say my name, say my name, wear it out", "vault_lines": ["Say you gotta leave, but I know you wanna stay", "You just waitin' on the traffic jam to finish, girl", "The things that we could do in twenty minutes, girl", "Say my name, say my name, wear it out", "But, boy, you stay up on it", "'Cause you just my type", "I will let you in"], "meaning": "When Drake’s stuck in traffic with his girl, he has sexual plans in mind to past the time.\n\n“Say my name” is a reference to the 1999 Destiny’s Child song of the same name. Drake has showed his love for the song many times, covering it at shows and releasing a remix—titled “Girls Love Beyoncé”—in 2013.", "annotation_id": "15380302", "url": "https://genius.com/15380302", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "line": "Good weed, white wine, uh I come alive in the night time, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Good weed, white wine, uh", "I come alive in the night time, yeah"], "meaning": "Weed and alcohol is a subsequent theme in Drake’s music—on A$AP Rocky’s 2013 song “Fuckin' Problems,” he raps:\n\nAin’t a fuckin' sing-along ‘less you brought the weed along\n\nAnd on Gucci Mane’s 2016 song “Both”:\n\nI don’t usually do this unless I’m drunk or I’m high\nBut I’m both right now, got me talking about my life\n\n“I come alive in the night time” means to be under the effects of those substances but may serve as a double entendre for owls. Drake’s brand October’s Very Own uses an owl as its symbol and owls are active during the night as they’re nocturnal animals.", "annotation_id": "15380153", "url": "https://genius.com/15380153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "line": "[Verse 1: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is the hottest rapper out right now, and we’ve always been trying to work together. He’s the only person I thought could really understand the melody of the song, and the minute he heard it he said, “I know exactly what I’m going to do. I love it.” And he did it like three days later.", "annotation_id": "10241031", "url": "https://genius.com/10241031", "state": "verified", "votes": 274, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "line": "You’re so amazing You took the time to figure me out That’s why you take me Way past the point of turnin’ me on You ’bout to break me I swear you got me losin’ my mind", "vault_lines": ["Baby, you got me", "You took the time to figure me out", "That's why you take me", "Way past the point of turnin' me on", "You 'bout to break me", "I swear you got me losin' my mind"], "meaning": "Rih feels emotionally connected to this man. Her mind goes wild whenever she’s around him. She expressed a similar sentiment on 2007’s “Say It”:\n\nEmotions running wild\nI could feel it when I’m next to you", "annotation_id": "68883", "url": "https://genius.com/68883", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "line": "Okay, away we go Only thing we have on is the radio Let it play", "vault_lines": ["Only thing we have on is the radio", "Okay, away we go"], "meaning": "This line reworks a famous line that Marilyn Monroe said during a 1953 interview with Esquire Magazine. She was asked if it was true that she “had nothing on”—meaning she was naked—during a photoshoot for a calendar and she responded:\n\nIt’s not quite true I had nothing on: the radio was on.", "annotation_id": "55379", "url": "https://genius.com/55379", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "What’s My Name?", "line": "The square root of sixty-nine is eight somethin’, right? ’Cause I’ve been tryna work it out, oh", "vault_lines": ["The square root of sixty-nine is eight somethin', right?", "'Cause I've been tryna work it out, oh"], "meaning": "“69” is a sex position involving simultaneous fellatio and cunnilingus. The participants are inverted like the number 6 and 9 in 69, hence the name.\n\n“8 (ate) something” refers to cunnilingus, and is also a continuation of Drake’s “word of mouth” wordplay. Also, the square root of 69 is 8.30662386 so Drake is mathematically correct.\n\n“Work it out” means he’s talking about solving the aforementioned square root problem but he’s also trying to “work out” a relationship with this girl.", "annotation_id": "52654", "url": "https://genius.com/52654", "state": "accepted", "votes": 426, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
