{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dave-wanna-know-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "genius_id": 2890565, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "All my man are running to the money We be running like ah, ah, ah, ah And all my man are running from them hoes on the roads like woah, woah, woah, woah", "vault_lines": ["We be running like ah, ah, ah, ah", "And all my man are running from them hoes on the roads like woah, woah, woah, woah", "And all my man, all my man are bros", "All my man are running to the money", "Like +44 on the code", "'Cause all my man are running to the money"], "meaning": "In a nutshell me and my brothers are focussed on success and avoiding gold diggers. When I wrote this I was in college and for the first time I had discovered attention because of the designer clothes I was known for wearing, this led me to go out and make the hook and say that we are running to the money and from the hoes, because at these times money etc brought the girls and the attention, but the latter didn’t bring the former.", "annotation_id": "10768212", "url": "https://genius.com/10768212", "state": "verified", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "’Cause all my man are running to the money We be running like ah, ah, ah, ah And all my man are running from them hoes on the roads like woah, woah, woah, woah", "vault_lines": ["We be running like ah, ah, ah, ah", "And all my man are running from them hoes on the roads like woah, woah, woah, woah", "And all my man, all my man are bros", "All my man are running to the money", "Like +44 on the code", "'Cause all my man are running to the money"], "meaning": "In a nutshell me and my brothers are focussed on success and avoiding gold diggers. When I wrote this I was in college and for the first time I had discovered attention because of the designer clothes I was known for wearing, this led me to go out and make the hook and say that we are running to the money and from the hoes, because at these times money etc brought the girls and the attention, but the latter didn’t bring the former.", "annotation_id": "10768179", "url": "https://genius.com/10768179", "state": "verified", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "All these man dem, they applaud me on the road", "vault_lines": ["All these man dem, they applaud me on the road"], "meaning": "http://man-dem.urbanup.com/3845052\n\n“mandem or man dem” is a Jamaican/Patois term, which has become established in the UK streets mostly used for acquaintances and friends but also any men\n\nDave is widely respected and acclaimed in the streets", "annotation_id": "10755645", "url": "https://genius.com/10755645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "Jizzle never won no wrestling, but he’s on a big bell metal ting", "vault_lines": ["Jizzle never won no wrestling, but he's on a big bell metal ting"], "meaning": "T-Jizzle is a homie of Drake’s, and was the DJ for the  Summer Sixteen tour.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BKL5Rr0gKNw/?taken-by=djtjizzle\n\nIn the UK, “big bells” refer to shotgun shells, so Drake is implying that he has people willing to shoot for him.", "annotation_id": "10748368", "url": "https://genius.com/10748368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "Yeah, like woah, woah, woah, woah Like woah, years ago, they didn’t wanna know But now I’m seeing dough", "vault_lines": ["Like woah, a month ago you didn't wanna know", "Like woah, years ago, they didn't wanna know", "But now I'm seeing dough", "A month ago you didn't wanna know, but now I'm seeing dough"], "meaning": "Drake reiterates part of Dave’s hook, but it still applies to his own life. He’s come a long way from his days as Jimmy on Degrassi, and all the people that doubted him get to see him reap in the profits of his successful rap career.", "annotation_id": "10726697", "url": "https://genius.com/10726697", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "Free smoke Anybody want it, they can get it, no joke Call up Landlord, nigga, that’s bro", "vault_lines": ["Anybody want it, they can get it, no joke", "Call up Landlord, nigga, that's bro"], "meaning": "“Smoke” refers to killing with a gun. Drake has had numerous altercations with rappers including Meek Mill, Tory Lanez, Joe Budden, Pusha T, and Jay Z. Drake is sending a warning shot to all of his enemies, and letting them know that no one is safe.\n\n“Landlord” is a reference to Giggs, a legendary pillar to the UK rap scene who recently dropped his 4th studio album Landlord.", "annotation_id": "10722758", "url": "https://genius.com/10722758", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "Like woah, a month ago you didn’t wanna know But now I’m seeing dough, these catties they be calling off my phone All these man dem, they applaud me on the road", "vault_lines": ["Like woah, a month ago you didn't wanna know", "But now I'm seeing dough, these catties they be calling off my phone", "All these man dem, they applaud me on the road", "It's funny how these catties wanna know, but", "Like woah, years ago, they didn't wanna know", "But now I'm seeing dough", "Catties, they be calling up my phone", "Like +44 on the code", "A month ago you didn't wanna know, but now I'm seeing dough", "These catties they be calling up my phone"], "meaning": "A “catty” or “cyattie” is a Jamaican patois term for “woman”. Jamaican patois is a major influence in London slang, due to its large Jamaican diaspora.\n\nJust a month ago, Dave had no money and people didn’t want anything to do with him. Now that he’s getting money, women (catties) are calling his phone wanting something from him.", "annotation_id": "10715987", "url": "https://genius.com/10715987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "I put corn in for my bro Man dem keep on talkin’ on a low Man dem keep on talkin’ on a low, but all my man are bros And all my man, all my man are bros", "vault_lines": ["I put corn in for my bro", "Man dem keep on talkin' on a low", "Man dem keep on talkin' on a low, but all my man are bros", "And all my man, all my man are bros", "Man dem keep on talking on a low", "Man dem keep talkin' on a low, low, low"], "meaning": "“Corn” is London slang for “bullets” and “Man dem” is Jamaican patois slang meaning “those men over there.” This term originated from the Carribean, but found its way to the streets of the UK where it is widely used to refer to a group of friends or homies. These “man dem” are talking down on Dave “on a low,” or in secret.", "annotation_id": "10715956", "url": "https://genius.com/10715956", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "Streets unruly, more life for my brother’s tryna better things, ’cause", "vault_lines": ["Streets unruly, more life for my brother's tryna better things, 'cause"], "meaning": "Drake wishes “more life,” or blessings, to his friend/collaborator Popcaan, a Jamaican dancehall artist who’s runs the label Unruly.  “More life” was tied into Drake’s future 2017 album/playlist, More Life.", "annotation_id": "10714748", "url": "https://genius.com/10714748", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "My man dem will have your mandem on the ropes I’ll have man dem on your road Man dem in a batch or on they own with a bat or with a pole I got man dem that will slap a man with both", "vault_lines": ["All these man dem, they applaud me on the road", "My man dem will have your mandem on the ropes", "I'll have man dem on your road", "Man dem in a batch or on they own with a bat or with a pole", "I got man dem that will slap a man with both"], "meaning": "In boxing it is often a bad sign to have your back up against the ropes, as it puts you in a state of vulnerability. It allows your opponent to attack you in a variety of places, while you have very little space to maneuver and get out.\n\nSimilarly, Dave and his mandem (group of men) can put people in the same position when on roads, or in your neighborhood. Much like being up against the ropes, if you were to come across Dave and his boys in a dark alley you’d be vulnerable to attack.", "annotation_id": "10712388", "url": "https://genius.com/10712388", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "Nike jacket on my back, I’m looking cold", "vault_lines": ["Nike jacket on my back, I'm looking cold"], "meaning": "Nike, Adidas, and many other sportswear brands are popular amongst the U.K grime culture. Cold is slang for looking good, but in the U.K it can literally get quite cold as well.\n\nDave is a Nike sponsored artist, which is why you will mostly see him wearing Nike apparel.\n\nIf you scroll through Dave’s Instagram, you can see most of the clothes he’s wearing are Nike.\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BGMyDLUCEpA/?taken-by=santandave", "annotation_id": "10712350", "url": "https://genius.com/10712350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "I fall back just to see how it unfolds", "vault_lines": ["I fall back just to see how it unfolds"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t need the attention or the annoyance of placing his input. He just sits back and patiently watches everything happen, a possible play on his persona as the “6 God”.\n\nIn “Charged Up,” Drake’s first Meek Mill diss track, he says something similar:\n\nI stay silent ‘cause we at war and I’m very patient\nSix God is watching, I just hope you’re prepared to face him\"", "annotation_id": "10710511", "url": "https://genius.com/10710511", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "I only link with the real ones, Lord knows", "vault_lines": ["I only link with the real ones, Lord knows"], "meaning": "It’s possible Drake is dissing his rival Meek Mill here, implying that Meek is fake and a higher power (or maybe the 6 God himself) has knowledge of this.\n\n“Lord Knows” is also a track off of Meek Mill’s successful album Dreams Worth More Than Money. The song feature’s Tory Lanez, a fellow Toronto rapper who has a history with Drake as well. Drake also has a track called “Lord Knows,” and Meek has a Drake diss named “Wanna Know.”", "annotation_id": "10710475", "url": "https://genius.com/10710475", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "I don’t run to Twitter with the things I know", "vault_lines": ["I don't run to Twitter with the things I know"], "meaning": "In the past year, Drake has had to deal with reckless Twitter rants aimed at him. In the summer of 2015, Meek Mill infamously went to Twitter to voice his opinion about Drake’s writing habits.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/623700698509758464\n\n\n \n\nSoon after, Drake responded with two diss tracks and played on Meek’s social media activity in the latter diss track.\n\nMore recently, Kid Cudi went on a Twitter rant throwing shots at Drake and Kanye West, calling them “clowns” who don’t write their own music. Drake abstains from airing out his beefs on social media, and doesn’t seek to expose secret information about other artists.", "annotation_id": "10710432", "url": "https://genius.com/10710432", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "Catties, they be calling up my phone Trying to get tickets to a show Yeah, I got U.K. tings on the WhatsApp now Like +44 on the code", "vault_lines": ["Catties, they be calling up my phone", "Trying to get tickets to a show", "Yeah, I got U.K. tings on the WhatsApp now", "Like +44 on the code", "These catties they be calling up my phone"], "meaning": "Drake has got girls from the UK hitting up his phone on WhatsApp, an instant messaging app used to avoid expensive international calls. “+44” is the calling code for the United Kingdom.", "annotation_id": "10710388", "url": "https://genius.com/10710388", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Wanna Know (Remix)", "line": "Yeah, they try to send for me Envy and jealousy is everything I wish upon my enemies All that for them, right back at them What my mind keeps telling me, mind keeps telling me I hear everything, I don’t ask if it’s all there ’cause it better be Trust me, it better be, no long talking or settling", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, they try to send for me", "Envy and jealousy is everything I wish upon my enemies", "All that for them, right back at them", "What my mind keeps telling me, mind keeps telling me", "I hear everything, I don't ask if it's all there 'cause it better be", "Trust me, it better be, no long talking or settling"], "meaning": "Drake borrows these lines from the original version of the song. He doesn’t wish success or happiness upon his enemies. Instead, he only wishes for them to keep hating on him and becoming more and more jealous and envious.\n\nThis is in contrast to his kind words on “One Dance” aimed at his friends:\n\nStrength and guidance\nAll that I’m wishing for my friends", "annotation_id": "10710376", "url": "https://genius.com/10710376", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Make It Go", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Belly-make-it-go-lyrics", "genius_title": "Make It Go", "genius_id": 2972, "source": "api"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Meek-mill-going-bad-lyrics", "genius_title": "Going Bad", "genius_id": 4125712, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "I just... what? I just... uh, put a Richard on the card", "vault_lines": ["I just... what? I just... uh, put a Richard on the card"], "meaning": "Richard Mille is an expensive Swiss luxury watch brand that was founded in 1999. The watches can range from $100,000 to  $1.7 million. Drake implies that he uses a charge card to purchase, which has no spending limit.\n\nIn October 2018, Drake posted on Instagram flexing a Richard Millie RM 69 Erotic Tourbillon–retailing at approximately $750k.", "annotation_id": "34451317", "url": "https://genius.com/34451317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Jeez, jeez", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jeez, or “jheeze,” is an interjection in London slang used to express excitement or happiness. This expression was originally popularized by UK rapper Giggs, who first used it in 2007 on his song “Talkin' da Hardest”:\n\nIf you’re talkin' the hardest\nGiggs better pop up in your thoughts as an artist (Jheeze)\n\nDrake also notably uses it on his 2016 tracks “Controlla” and “Grammys”.", "annotation_id": "21474927", "url": "https://genius.com/21474927", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Foreign shit runnin’ on diesel, dawg", "vault_lines": ["Foreign shit runnin' on diesel, dawg"], "meaning": "Drake is saying that he has cars made in other countries that he drives. These cars apparently are not electric, and are old-fashiondly powered by diesel, the fuel that is burned by the engine. Some of the foreign cars in Drake’s collection are a Bugatti Veyron Sang Noir (from France), a Lamborghini Aventador Roadster (from Italy), and a Mercedes-Maybach S 600 Pullman (from Germany).\n\n\n\nHere’s what the Bugatti basically looks like.\n\nIt’s possible that Drake is literally referring to a car powered by diesel (which is pretty much only for trucks in the US). A number of premium European cars are available with diesel engines, including Jaguars and Land Rovers.", "annotation_id": "17424694", "url": "https://genius.com/17424694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "I just broke her off with a 10-piece, dawg (10-piece, dawg) That ain’t nothin’, I’m just bein’ friendly, dawg", "vault_lines": ["I just broke her off with a 10-piece, dawg (10-piece, dawg)", "That ain't nothin', I'm just bein' friendly, dawg", "It's just a lil' 10-piece for her"], "meaning": "To “break someone off” is a slang term that means to give someone cash, but can also connote sex as it was used in The Roots 2002 love song “Break You Off.”\n\nA “10 Piece” is a slang term for 10 grand or $10,000, as used in the Wiz Khalifa & Curren$y song “10 Piece.”\n\nDrake implies that 10 grand “ain’t nothing” compared to the amount of money he has, while also suggesting that hooking up is just an ordinary occurrence for him.", "annotation_id": "17259782", "url": "https://genius.com/17259782", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Don’t just say you’re down, you gotta show me (What you gotta do?) Bring the clip back empty (It’s empty)", "vault_lines": ["Bring the clip back empty (It's empty)", "Don't just say you're down, you gotta show me (What you gotta do?)"], "meaning": "Drake extends the mob allusion that he began when he mentioned Don Corleone. This isn’t the first time Drake has romanticized or claimed to have mob ties. On August 2011’s “Headlines,” Drake raps:\n\nTuck my napkin in my shirt, ‘cause I’m just mobbin' like that\n\nThen, on February 2015’s “No Tellin'”:\n\nV-Live, I order that Alfredo pasta\nThen eat in the kitchen like I’m in the mafia\n\nFinally, in August 2018, Drake dedicated an entire track called “Mob Ties” to his connection to hip-hop and mob heavyweight J. Prince.", "annotation_id": "15911402", "url": "https://genius.com/15911402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "I could fit like 80 racks in my Amiris (80 racks)", "vault_lines": ["I could fit like 80 racks in my Amiris (80 racks)"], "meaning": "Amiri is a high-quality streetwear fashion brand created by designer Mike Amiri. The jeans are often skin tight, which makes it difficult to fit $80,000 in the pockets.\n\n\n\nMeek used a similar line on A$AP Ferg's August 2017 track, “Trap and a Dream”:\n\nIn the back like a spleen, hoo, hunnid racks in my jeans, aw yeah", "annotation_id": "15909999", "url": "https://genius.com/15909999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Trust me, at the top it isn’t lonely (Strapped) Everybody actin’ like they know me, dawg", "vault_lines": ["Trust me, at the top it isn't lonely (Strapped)", "Everybody actin' like they know me, dawg"], "meaning": "Drake flips the popular idiom “it’s lonely at the top,” which means the most successful people have the fewest friends. Drizzy counters by claiming when you’re as successful as him, a lot of fake people want to be around. He also discussed this on the 2011 song, “The Motto”:\n\nI’m the fuckin' man, y'all don’t get it, do ya?\nType of money, everybody acting like they knew ya", "annotation_id": "15909115", "url": "https://genius.com/15909115", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Known to keep the baddest bitches on commando (Salute)", "vault_lines": ["Known to keep the baddest bitches on commando (Salute)"], "meaning": "Meek keeps women “on command” and ready for sex or action, like a commando soldier.\n\nTo “go commando” also means not to wear underwear. The term developed from men who wore no underwear during wars to help with mobility and ventilation.", "annotation_id": "15907929", "url": "https://genius.com/15907929", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Richard Mille cost a Lambo (That’s a Lambo)", "vault_lines": ["Richard Mille cost a Lambo (That's a Lambo)"], "meaning": "Meek follows Drake’s line above and mentions Richard Millie, a Swiss luxury watch brand, founded in 1999. Meek has been spotting wearing a Richard Mille RM 11-03 Automatic Flyback Chronograph in Titanium and 18K Rose Gold which is priced around $265,000.\n\n\n\nA Lamborghini Huracan starts around $203,000.", "annotation_id": "15907636", "url": "https://genius.com/15907636", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Back home, smokin’ legal (Legal)", "vault_lines": ["Back home, smokin' legal (Legal)"], "meaning": "The Cannabis Act of Canada, which allows the legal use of marijuana, was passed on October 17th, 2018, making it legal for Drake to smoke in his hometown of Toronto.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/TorontoPolice/status/1052332487551127553?s=19", "annotation_id": "15906901", "url": "https://genius.com/15906901", "state": "accepted", "votes": 155, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "I just... what? I just... uh, put a Richard on the card", "vault_lines": ["I just... what? I just... uh, put a Richard on the card"], "meaning": "Richard Mille is an expensive Swiss luxury watch brand that was founded in 1999. The watches can range from $100,000 to  $1.7 million. Drake implies that he uses a charge card to purchase, which has no spending limit.\n\nIn October 2018, Drake posted on Instagram flexing a Richard Millie RM 69 Erotic Tourbillon–retailing at approximately $750k.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BotK2NiHnRO/?utm_source=ig_embed", "annotation_id": "15906894", "url": "https://genius.com/15906894", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Bitch, I graduated, call me “Big Fish” (Marlin)", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, I graduated, call me \"Big Fish\" (Marlin)"], "meaning": "Meek Mill started using the moniker “Lil Fish” on his 2017 song, “Left Hollywood”. He later clarified why on his 2018 song “1 Am” that:\n\nThey call me lil' fish ‘cause I be swimming in that pussy\n\nWith the heightening of his celebrity profile in 2018, he has levelled up to “Big Fish” status.\n\nThis is also a play on the collective noun, ‘school of fish.’ He has graduated into the big leagues.\n\n“Marlin” is a species of large fish that has an elongated body, a spear-like snout or bill, and a long, rigid dorsal fin which extends forward to form a crest.", "annotation_id": "15906826", "url": "https://genius.com/15906826", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "I got Lori Harvey on my wish list (That’s Lori)", "vault_lines": ["I got Lori Harvey on my wish list (That's Lori)"], "meaning": "Lori Harvey is an American model and the step-daughter of American comedian Steve Harvey.\n\n\n\nIn a December 2018 interview with The Breakfast Club, Meek confirmed that he meant Lori Harvey and not the actress, Laurie Harrier.", "annotation_id": "15906792", "url": "https://genius.com/15906792", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Playin’ with my name, that shit is lethal, dawg (Who you say you was?) Don Corleone", "vault_lines": ["Playin' with my name, that shit is lethal, dawg (Who you say you was?)"], "meaning": "Don Vito Corleone is a fictional mob boss and the main character in the classic 1972 crime film The Godfather.", "annotation_id": "15906749", "url": "https://genius.com/15906749", "state": "accepted", "votes": 145, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Put some bands all on your head like Jason Terry (Brrt, brrt, ooh)", "vault_lines": ["Put some bands all on your head like Jason Terry (Brrt, brrt, ooh)"], "meaning": "“Bands on your head” means to pay someone thousands of dollars to commit a murder. Meek also flips this and name drops NBA veteran Jason Terry, who was known for always wearing headbands.", "annotation_id": "15906705", "url": "https://genius.com/15906705", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "DC, OVO, we back again, we goin’ plat’ (Ooh, ooh)", "vault_lines": ["DC, OVO, we back again, we goin' plat' (Ooh, ooh)"], "meaning": "DC stands for Dreamchasers, Meek’s label created in 2012. AT the time of this songs release, artists like Omelly, Nick Papz, and YBS Skola are signed. Meek also has a mixtape series of the same name which was initially released in August 2011.\n\nOVO Sound is Drake’s record label which he also founded in 2012. OVO is the home of artists like PARTYNEXTDOOR, Boi-1da, Roy Woods, and more.\n\nMeek believes that his and Drake’s collaboration will go platinum, meaning it will sell at least a million records. He could also be referring to their chains being platinum, as both artists have chains with their label’s logo.", "annotation_id": "15906690", "url": "https://genius.com/15906690", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Girl, I left your love at Magic, now it’s all shade", "vault_lines": ["Girl, I left your love at Magic, now it's all shade"], "meaning": "Drake’s alluding to a girl he met at Magic City, an Atlanta strip club that hip-hop artists often frequent.\n\n\n\nIn November 2018, Drake unveiled a merch capsule collaboration with the strip club.", "annotation_id": "15906684", "url": "https://genius.com/15906684", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "(Wheezy outta here)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Wheezy is an Atlanta producer most known for his work with Young Thug, 21 Savage, Migos and more. His producer tag is said by Atlanta rapper, Future.\n\nWheezy and Drake’s first collaboration came on October 2017’s “Issa” by 21 Savage with Young Thug. The two then teamed up with Lil Baby to produce the platinum May 2018 track, “Yes Indeed.” They also worked together on Quavo’s October 2018 track, “FLIP THE SWITCH,” which was released over a month prior to “Going Bad.”\n\nWheezy has also collaborated with Meek Mill on Wins & Losses July 2017 cut, “We Ball,” featuring Young Thug.", "annotation_id": "15906607", "url": "https://genius.com/15906607", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Yeah, lot of Murakami in the hallway (What?)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, lot of Murakami in the hallway (What?)"], "meaning": "Takashi Murakami is a Japanese artist from Tokyo. He’s known for having designed the cover art for Kanye West’s September 2007 album, Graduation, as well as Kanye and Kid Cudi’s June 2018 collab album Kids See Ghosts. In November 2018, Drake and Murakami collaborated on an OVO x Murakami Varsity Jacket and later released an entire collection at OVO’s Vancouver flagship store. Interestingly, in February 2014, Drake criticized JAY-Z for rapping about art.", "annotation_id": "15906603", "url": "https://genius.com/15906603", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Every time I’m in my trap, I move like Rambo (Extended) Ain’t a neighborhood in Philly that I can’t go (That’s a Fendi)", "vault_lines": ["Every time I'm in my trap, I move like Rambo (Extended)", "Ain't a neighborhood in Philly that I can't go (That's a Fendi)"], "meaning": "John Rambo, played by Sylvester Stallone, is the main protagonist of the Rambo film series, which began in 1982, and was modeled by David Morell’s 1972 novel First Blood. Meek compares his status as a hometown hero to the highly respected former Vietnam veteran and highly skilled Green Beret, which is an Army special forces soldier. Stallone is also known for playing the iconic Philly fighter Robert “Rocky\" Balboa, in the Rocky series, which began in 1976, and Creed series where Stallone plays Rocky as a supporting character to Michael B. Jordan’s title character. Meek appeared on “Lord Knows” and “Check,” from the 2015 Creed (Original Motion Picture Soundtrack).\n\n“Fendi” is a slang term for fact, which Meek uses to further affirm that he’s good in Philly.", "annotation_id": "15906585", "url": "https://genius.com/15906585", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "I got more slaps than The Beatles (Beatles)", "vault_lines": ["I got more slaps than The Beatles (Beatles)"], "meaning": "On October 22, 2018, Drake scored 12 hits on Billboard Hot 100, beating The Beatles' 1964 record of 11, thanks to his Scary Hours EP, popular tracks from Scorpion, and features throughout the year.\n\nThe Beatles held the record with hits like “Can’t Buy Me Love,” “She Loves You,” and more.\n\n“Slaps” is slang used for a hit song or simply to describe a song that sounds good.\n\nIn the week of June 24, 2019, Drake’s single “Money in the Grave” debuted at No. 7 on the Hot 100, effectively giving him his 35th Top 10 and breaking another record previously held by The Beatles.", "annotation_id": "15906577", "url": "https://genius.com/15906577", "state": "accepted", "votes": 217, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Going Bad", "line": "Me and Drizzy back-to-back, it’s gettin’ scary (Back-to-back)", "vault_lines": ["Me and Drizzy back-to-back, it's gettin' scary (Back-to-back)"], "meaning": "“Going Bad” marks the first collaboration between Drake and Meek Mill since their June 2015 hit “R.I.C.O.” Nearly a month after that song’s release, Meek Mill criticized Drake on Twitter for using a ghostwriter, later found out to be Quentin Miller, to write his “R.I.C.O.” verse, which started a rap battle between the two.\n\n\n\nDrake released two diss tracks in a one-week period: “Charged Up” and “Back to Back.” Meek Mill responded with “Wanna Know,” but most consider Drake the winner of the battle. “Back to Back” would go on to achieve 2x Multi-Platinum status, peak at #21 on the Billboard Hot 100, and even be nominated for Best Rap Performance at the 2016 Grammys.\n\nWith this long-awaited collaboration, Meek claims that he and Drake have put their beef aside, which he declared publicly on May 10, 2018. Drake brought out Meek in September 2018 on his Aubrey & the Three Migos tour and even supported Meek in 2017 when Meek was facing prison time, saying “Free Meek Mill. You right.”", "annotation_id": "15906575", "url": "https://genius.com/15906575", "state": "accepted", "votes": 180, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "status": "done", "targets": 13, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-popstar-lyrics", "genius_title": "POPSTAR", "genius_id": 5774099, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "Busy at the crib, cookin’ salmon with the lobster", "vault_lines": ["Busy at the crib, cookin' salmon with the lobster"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake is suggesting that he’s living a luxurious and extravagant lifestyle, as he’s busy cooking both salmon and lobster, which are both high-end and expensive types of seafood. The use of these specific foods implies that Drake has the means and the resources to indulge in the finest things in life. The phrase “at the crib” means that he’s doing all of this in his own home, which further emphasizes his wealth and privilege.", "annotation_id": "28068786", "url": "https://genius.com/28068786", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "Say she rep a whole different block, so I blocked her", "vault_lines": ["Say she rep a whole different block, so I blocked her"], "meaning": "This line means that Drake heard the woman he was talking to claims to represent a different area or neighborhood, which he may perceive as a potential threat or source of drama. As a result, he decided to block her or cut off communication to avoid any potential conflicts or misunderstandings. The line may also imply that Drake is not interested in being associated with someone from a different neighborhood or community, possibly due to tensions between different blocks or areas.", "annotation_id": "28068773", "url": "https://genius.com/28068773", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "I want a long life, a legendary one (Yeah) I want a quick death (Yeah), and an easy one (Yeah) I want a pretty girl (Yeah), and an honest one (Yeah) I want this drink (Yeah), and another one, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I want a quick death (Yeah), and an easy one (Yeah)", "I want a pretty girl (Yeah), and an honest one (Yeah)", "I want this drink (Yeah), and another one, yeah", "I want a long life, a legendary one (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Drake cleverly borrows inspiration for his rap lyrics from the 2014 drama series, Outlander. In the hit show, a character proposes a toast in the scene, stating:\n\nHere’s to a long life, and a merry one\nA quick death, and a easy one\nA pretty girl and a honest one\nA stip whiskey and another one\n\nhttps://youtu.be/0K0_tZnLrBM?t=4\n\nExpanding on his lyricism, Drake expresses his honesty in his future, perhaps wanting to put his playboy life to rest—as well as saluting his success in life as drinking with one another is a sign of celebrating one’s success or an event.", "annotation_id": "26998218", "url": "https://genius.com/26998218", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "Shit don’t even usually get this big without a Bieber face", "vault_lines": ["Shit don't even usually get this big without a Bieber face"], "meaning": "Drake gives a shoutout to fellow Canadian artist, Justin Bieber. He acts as a stand-in for Drake in the song’s official music video, which was released almost two months after the single’s initial release.\n\nWith these lines, Drake’s likely saying it’s not easy to get to his level of popularity without a “Bieber” or a handsome face as handsome artists get a lot of attention, especially from their female fans. Also, Justin Bieber is a pop singer, which adds to the “popstar” theme of the song.\n\nIn the first verse, Drake mentions Bieber’s manager, Scooter Braun, who is in part responsible for Justin’s fame and success, marking a connection between the two lines.", "annotation_id": "20443038", "url": "https://genius.com/20443038", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "I’m a popstar, but this shit ain’t bubblegum, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I'm a popstar, but this shit ain't bubblegum, yeah", "I'm a popstar, not a doctor"], "meaning": "Bubblegum pop is generic, upbeat pop music that’s mainly marketed towards children or young adults. It originated in the late ‘60s and was popular through the early '70s before other genres like disco and punk rock emerged.\n\nDrake has been questioned on whether he’s a rapper or a pop singer throughout his career due to his frequent use of mixing melodic singing and rapping together, which leads many people to discredit his bars and status as a rapper. However, he is the top selling solo male artist of all time in America, which gives him the celebrity and status of a pop star.\n\nAlthough he may not have the gritty or trap style of other popular rappers, his pop-like songs—July 2015’s “Hotline Bling” for example—would not be considered bubblegum music as he points out here.", "annotation_id": "20363481", "url": "https://genius.com/20363481", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "Two, four, six, eight watches, factory, so they appreciate", "vault_lines": ["Two, four, six, eight watches, factory, so they appreciate"], "meaning": "This line is a play on the commonly known cheer, “2, 4, 6, 8… Who do we appreciate?” However, Drake’s playing on the word “appreciate” as it also denotes an increase in the value of an asset over time.\n\nDrake has an extensive watch collection that he buys in stock form directly from the factory. Each one appreciates in value, perhaps merely by association with the artist. A notable watch to join his collection is his one-of-a-kind Patek Philippe Nautilus, designed by Off-White founder and Louis Vuitton artistic director Virgil Abloh. In January 2020, Drake debuted the watch on his Instagram page.", "annotation_id": "20363436", "url": "https://genius.com/20363436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "Naw, naw, piece of cake, naw, naw, Turks and Caic’, yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Naw, naw, piece of cake, naw, naw, Turks and Caic', yeah, yeah"], "meaning": "Turks and Caicos is a group of 40 islands in the Atlantic Ocean, southeast of the Bahamas.\n\nDrake has been known to be a visitor to the islands; in May 2019, he debuted his custom Boeing 767 airplane, named Air Drake, at the island of Providenciales. In June 2019, Drake was seen on the islands in “album mode,” preparing for his sixth studio album.\n\n\n\nDrake also name-drops the islands on “Greece,” the other DJ Khaled collaboration released on July 17, 2020:\n\nGone out to Caicos, she can stay in my room", "annotation_id": "20362340", "url": "https://genius.com/20362340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "Yeah, last year, I kept it on the tuck, ayy 2020, I came to fuck it up, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, last year, I kept it on the tuck, ayy", "2020, I came to fuck it up, yeah"], "meaning": "In 2019, Drake had a low-key year, only releasing three new songs: June 2019’s “Money in the Grave” and “Omertà”—both released apart of his The Best in the World Pack EP—and December 2019’s “War.” While not a new track, in September 2019, Drake re-released “Behind Barz” on streaming platforms, a freestyle he originally performed in July 2018 for the British rap platform Link Up TV.\n\nMoving into 2020, Drake has already released a number one hit, April 2020’s “Toosie Slide” and a commercial mixtape, Dark Lane Demo Tapes. His sixth studio album was also due for a summer 2020 release but was later delayed indefinitely.", "annotation_id": "20359607", "url": "https://genius.com/20359607", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "We The Best Music Another one (Yeah) DJ Khaled", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "These are DJ Khaled’s trademark slogans that he often proclaims in the intro and outro of his songs. We the Best Music Group is a record label imprint of Epic Records that was founded by Khaled in 2008.\n\n“Another one” is Khaled’s signature way of announcing that he has another hit on his hands.", "annotation_id": "20359581", "url": "https://genius.com/20359581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "If we talkin’ joints, it’s just me and David Foster", "vault_lines": ["If we talkin' joints, it's just me and David Foster"], "meaning": "Drake pays an ode to fellow Canadian musician, songwriter and composer David Foster.\n\nFoster was first credited in 1972 and has continued to make music of his own and help others throughout multiple decades. He has worked with other talents such as Diana Ross, Céline Dion, Whitney Houston, Madonna, Michael Jackson, Lionel Richie, and more.\n\nOn top of having sixteen Grammy awards and a Golden Globe under his belt, Foster was inducted into the Canadian Music Hall of Fame in 2007 and has also been inducted into the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\n\nOne of Foster’s most famous production credits was Whitney Houston’s 1992 version of “I Will Always Love You” from “The Bodyguard” soundtrack.\n\nThe film starred Houston and Kevin Costner, who Drake deliberately references in the next two lines. Costner, a co-producer on the film, was the one that suggested to Foster that he rearrange the original Dolly Parton song for Houston to sing.", "annotation_id": "20359578", "url": "https://genius.com/20359578", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "Look, Ariana, Selena, my Visa It can take as many charges as it needs to, my girl That shit platinum just like all of my releases, my girl", "vault_lines": ["Look, Ariana, Selena, my Visa", "It can take as many charges as it needs to, my girl", "That shit platinum just like all of my releases, my girl"], "meaning": "Continuing with the braggadocios lyrics, Drake flexes that he has a Visa Platinum Card which has an exceedingly high spending limit and greater purchasing power. As of a 2018 Forbes article, Drake is the fourth wealthiest hip-hop artist with over $100 million net worth.\n\nDrake also links his Platinum card to his success in hip-hop, as each of his albums has garnered the Platinum certification or higher. Platinum certification means that an album has sold over 1,000,000 copies, which all his records have done besides his most recent May 2020 release Dark Lane Demo Tapes. As of June 2018, Drake has also become the highest certified digital singles artist, bringing in over 148 million equivalent units.\n\nDrake also gives a shout to female artists Ariana Grande and Selena Gomez respectively who are both successful artists, with each of them going platinum.", "annotation_id": "20359569", "url": "https://genius.com/20359569", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "Bodyguards don’t look like Kevin Costner, you tweakin’ Just pulled up to Whitney Houston, Texas for the evenin’", "vault_lines": ["Bodyguards don't look like Kevin Costner, you tweakin'", "Just pulled up to Whitney Houston, Texas for the evenin'"], "meaning": "This is a nod to the 1992 film The Bodyguard which stars Kevin Costner and Whitney Houston. It’s about a former Secret Service agent who goes on to protect a pop star who had been receiving threats.\n\nDrake plays on Whitney’s last name with the city of the same name, which is located in Texas. Houston holds a special place in Drake’s heart as it was where he flew to meet Lil Wayne back in 2008 to kickstart his career. He also dedicated his February 2009 song “November 18th” to the city and has “Houston” tatted on his body.", "annotation_id": "20359518", "url": "https://genius.com/20359518", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "POPSTAR", "line": "You would probably think my manager is Scooter Braun, yeah But my manager with twenty hoes in Buddakan, yeah, ayy", "vault_lines": ["You would probably think my manager is Scooter Braun, yeah", "But my manager with twenty hoes in Buddakan, yeah, ayy"], "meaning": "Scooter Braun is a well-known entrepreneur and manager to popular artists like fellow Canadian singer Justin Bieber—who appears along with Braun in the song’s official music video—and American singer Ariana Grande, the latter of whom is mentioned in the next line. Since Drake’s a popular rapper himself, he jokes that someone may think Braun is managing him since he manages many popular artists. However, Drake’s manager is his longtime friend and DJ, Future the Prince.\n\nInterestingly though, Drake and Scooter are affiliated together through a common business interest as in 2018, both became co-owners of popular gaming organization 100 Thieves.\n\nBuddakan is a restaurant chain that serves New American Asian cuisine. They have three locations across the US—one in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, New York City, New York, and Atlantic City, New Jersey.", "annotation_id": "20359457", "url": "https://genius.com/20359457", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
