{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "So if pretty don’t come with somethin’, well, then I dead it", "vault_lines": ["So if pretty don't come with somethin', well, then I dead it"], "meaning": "Drake makes a decision to end relationships that lack substance or depth, emphasizing the importance of meaningful connections over surface-level attractiveness.", "annotation_id": "31566412", "url": "https://genius.com/31566412", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "Pretty had me givin’ more than I was gettin’", "vault_lines": ["Pretty had me givin' more than I was gettin'"], "meaning": "here drake highlights the imbalance in the relationship, where he was investing more emotionally or materially than he was receiving in return.", "annotation_id": "31566406", "url": "https://genius.com/31566406", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "But pretty taught me ugly lessons", "vault_lines": ["But pretty taught me ugly lessons"], "meaning": "Drake implies that despite the initial attraction based on beauty, the experience with this person turned out to be negative or hurtful in some way, teaching him about the darker side of relationships based on superficiality.", "annotation_id": "31566394", "url": "https://genius.com/31566394", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "Pretty face, pretty tempted", "vault_lines": ["Pretty face, pretty tempted"], "meaning": "This line suggests being attracted to someone solely based on their physical appearance", "annotation_id": "31566390", "url": "https://genius.com/31566390", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "I wasn’t lookin’ for your secrets, they just came to me And they contradicted everything you claimed to be", "vault_lines": ["I wasn't lookin' for your secrets, they just came to me", "And they contradicted everything you claimed to be"], "meaning": "girls hide stuff from you, but once you find out they get mad at you for knowing who they really aree", "annotation_id": "30829001", "url": "https://genius.com/30829001", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "They say time heals", "vault_lines": ["They say time heals"], "meaning": "The idea that time heals all wounds is often given to people who are going through a difficult time in an effort to make them feel better. In some cases, over time, the feeling of being heartbroken is fixed by forgetting that person or relationship.\n\nTime also allows for reflection on the difficult experience in a way that promotes insight and the ability to move on.\n- Heather Z. Lyons, PHD", "annotation_id": "30288960", "url": "https://genius.com/30288960", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "They say time heals", "vault_lines": ["They say time heals"], "meaning": "The idea that time heals all wounds is often given to people who are going through a difficult time in an effort to make them feel better. In some cases, over time, the feeling of being heartbroken is fixed by forgetting that person or relationship.\n\nTime also allows for reflection on the difficult experience in a way that promotes insight and the ability to move on.\n- Heather Z. Lyons, PHD", "annotation_id": "30288954", "url": "https://genius.com/30288954", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "They say time heals", "vault_lines": ["They say time heals"], "meaning": "The idea that time heals all wounds is often given to people who are going through a difficult time in an effort to make them feel better. In some cases, over time, the feeling of being heartbroken is fixed by forgetting that person or relationship.\n\nTime also allows for reflection on the difficult experience in a way that promotes insight and the ability to move on.\n- Heather Z. Lyons, PHD", "annotation_id": "30288891", "url": "https://genius.com/30288891", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "And she don’t wanna go to sleep, she angry", "vault_lines": ["But she don't wanna go to sleep, she angry", "So don't go to sleep", "And she don't wanna go to sleep, she angry"], "meaning": "to the idiots that thumb down my sht fuck u losers ur poor", "annotation_id": "26086355", "url": "https://genius.com/26086355", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "Ashamed to tell my friends how much I’d do for you ’Cause they know that you would never do the same for me", "vault_lines": ["Ashamed to tell my friends how much I'd do for you", "'Cause they know that you would never do the same for me"], "meaning": "Drake Is Talking About How Females Back Stab", "annotation_id": "24140708", "url": "https://genius.com/24140708", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "I wish that we can change places Don’t want no new, new faces She got my heartbeat racing", "vault_lines": ["Don't want no new, new faces", "She got my heartbeat racing", "I wish that we can change places"], "meaning": "Yung Bleu turns his attention to the new boyfriend, he wishes to be in his shoes so as to be with the girl again, he too cannot bare to move on with someone else other this girl. Yung Bleu has in the past [https://genius.com/Yung-bleu-time-heals-lyrics] spoken of girl who has moved on from him and he sings asking if the new guy can replace him.", "annotation_id": "22243697", "url": "https://genius.com/22243697", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "If I ever made you angry, girl Just know that it get better with time (Time) They say time heals", "vault_lines": ["If I ever made you angry, girl", "Just know that it get better with time (Time)", "They say time heals"], "meaning": "In these starting lines Yung Bleu expresses regret in losing this particular girl. He, without apologizing, hopes that as time goes she will find it in her heart to forgive him.\n\nThese lines here have been interpolated from his song “Time heals, [https://genius.com/Yung-bleu-time-heals-lyrics].", "annotation_id": "22243530", "url": "https://genius.com/22243530", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "You’re Mines Still (Remix)", "line": "I took you to the club and you hugged on somebody that I know And I know them type of hugs Same shit I do to women when I know I used to fuck And I know they with they nigga, but they never brought it up", "vault_lines": ["'Cause they know that you would never do the same for me", "I took you to the club and you hugged on somebody that I know", "And I know them type of hugs", "Same shit I do to women when I know I used to fuck", "And I know they with they nigga, but they never brought it up"], "meaning": "Reflecting on unrequited love, Drake recognizes his own behavior in the way his ex greeted one’s of his friends. In this case, a longer and more intimate hug implies a relationship history far beyond acquaintance.\n\nOn the 2016 track “Child’s Play” off of Views, Drake similarly discussed past affection as a hugger:\n\nI give Chanel out like a hug, I know\nI knew a couple of your friends way before", "annotation_id": "21092676", "url": "https://genius.com/21092676", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith Pt. 2", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/K-os-faith-pt-2-lyrics", "genius_title": "Faith Pt. 2", "genius_id": 62153, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith Pt. 2", "line": "You can thank me later for the record", "vault_lines": ["You can thank me later for the record"], "meaning": "A reference to Drake’s first studio album “Thank Me Later”, stating that I don’t even need your thanks", "annotation_id": "38813328", "url": "https://genius.com/38813328", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith Pt. 2", "line": "I’ve been told I was next by a legendary that was on Death Row", "vault_lines": ["I’ve been told I was next by a legendary that was on Death Row"], "meaning": "POSSIBLY speaking about Snoop Dogg, who Drake has been seen with several times, who was signed to Death Row Records, but now owns it as of 2022. His voice is also heard multiple times on Drake’s album, “For All The Dogs”.", "annotation_id": "38103531", "url": "https://genius.com/38103531", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith Pt. 2", "line": "I-I-I swear, I know, they are in the boat I row E’rrybody in it gotta paddle, muthaf-cker won’t let go", "vault_lines": ["I-I-I swear, I know, they are in the boat I row", "E’rrybody in it gotta paddle, muthaf-cker won’t let go"], "meaning": "Drake using the metaphor of a person rowing a boat to him and the control he has over his road to success. It seems like everyone wants to put their two cents in (everybody has a paddle), but Drake is controlling his own path (the boat i row)", "annotation_id": "541326", "url": "https://genius.com/541326", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Faith Pt. 2", "line": "Let my money flow through the air, I can make it rain on your parade", "vault_lines": ["Let my money flow through the air, I can make it rain on your parade"], "meaning": "The expression “Make it rain on your parade” is a way of to say bringing someone down when they are up and positive. The hip hop expression to “make it rain” is of course a way to say throwing money up in the air, all over something.\n\nDrake cleverly is saying he has so much money, he would have no problem covering a parade.\n\nHe could also be saying since he has so much money, in comparison he could be making you feel bad about yourself (bringing you down)", "annotation_id": "541318", "url": "https://genius.com/541318", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "status": "done", "targets": 29, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-baby-and-drake-yes-indeed-lyrics", "genius_title": "Yes Indeed", "genius_id": 3702977, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Got M’s in the bank, like, “Yes, indeed”", "vault_lines": ["Got M's in the bank, like, \"Yes, indeed\""], "meaning": "Lil Baby is referring to the millions he has in the bank from his successful rapping career.", "annotation_id": "33539075", "url": "https://genius.com/33539075", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Did all the favors, they never repaid me", "vault_lines": ["Did all the favors, they never repaid me"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to the many favors he has given over the years to different rappers, but these rappers didn’t repay Drake with favors or money.", "annotation_id": "33539058", "url": "https://genius.com/33539058", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "I’m gettin’ money like I’m from the eighties", "vault_lines": ["I'm gettin' money like I'm from the eighties"], "meaning": "this could a reference to how much income the rappers of the 80’s such as N.W.A. received during the 80’s.\nOr it could be that in the 80’s many drug dealers had a large income based on a large client base and a constant need for supply. He could be saying that although not many people are needing crack and cocaine, he still makes bank off of selling what he sells.", "annotation_id": "14685650", "url": "https://genius.com/14685650", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Twenty-five hundred for a new pair of tennis shoes The same price, I could make them youngins come and finish you", "vault_lines": ["Twenty-five hundred for a new pair of tennis shoes", "The same price, I could make them youngins come and finish you"], "meaning": "Sarcastic and very aggressive line from Lil, he equals the price of some shoes with the life of his adversary.\n\nHe tells us that he buys a pair of shoes or orders to kill with the same ease and for the same price, while making fun of the rival.\n\nIn the Ganster slang, ”youngins”, means “Young ones”. In several Thug/Ganster Lifestyles, Young people are used to carry out criminal activities because they get better legal treatment, avoiding jail in some cases.", "annotation_id": "14663707", "url": "https://genius.com/14663707", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "When I started out, I just took what they gave me", "vault_lines": ["When I started out, I just took what they gave me"], "meaning": "This is most likely Drake reflecting on his early days in the music industry.\n\nWhen he first started in music, Drake was a signee to Birdman’s YMCMB, and though he still, in part, represents the label, fast forward a few years and Drake owns one of the most popular record labels and collectives in his time; OVO: Octobers Very Own. OVO has a hand in fashion, music, radio and even has it’s very own annual festival.\n\nSo at first, he took what he was given but now, he is the one giving out opportunities and making decisions for himself.", "annotation_id": "14641492", "url": "https://genius.com/14641492", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "I trusted my niggas, they never betrayed me", "vault_lines": ["I trusted my niggas, they never betrayed me"], "meaning": "Although Drake has had his fair share of beef over the years, his loyal friends and label mates which he had around from his early days in Toronto, Canada are still working with him till this day.\n\nMost notably Drake’s producer and engineer (and genius) ‘Noah “40” Shebib’ who has been working with Drake since the beginning of time.\n\n\n\nDrake has echoed similar lyrics about his loyalty to his friends over industry personnel in his “6PM in New York” freestyle in 2015 off his If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late album:\n\nI’m managed by my friends that I grew up with\nI’d rather give that 15% to people I fuck with", "annotation_id": "14641374", "url": "https://genius.com/14641374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Soon as I nut, you can gon’ leave", "vault_lines": ["Soon as I nut, you can gon' leave"], "meaning": "Lil Baby briefly explained this lyric in his Breakfast Club interview by saying that after he nuts, she needs to leave so he can sleep. However, he confirmed he was currently in a relationship and that this mindset was one of his “older life”.\n\nLil Baby’s Breakfast Club interview explaining this is available on Youtube.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9s0KmG1a5ds", "annotation_id": "14641281", "url": "https://genius.com/14641281", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "It’s me and Lil Baby, this shit goin’ crazy", "vault_lines": ["It's me and Lil Baby, this shit goin' crazy"], "meaning": "The song made it to Lil Baby’s debut album Harder Than Ever and with 71,000 first week sales (it was projected to reach anywhere between 65k-80k), there is no doubt that the collaboration with Drake was the catalyst for these impressive figures.\n\nSome people refer to this reaction as the ‘Drake Effect’. Where Drake’s mention, co-sign or feature increases the popularity (and sometimes sales) of the subject in question. Examples include the 146% increase in streams of the artist Giggs after his feature on Drake’s ‘More Life’ playlist in 2017.", "annotation_id": "14641160", "url": "https://genius.com/14641160", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Life is amazin’, it is what it should be", "vault_lines": ["Life is amazin', it is what it should be"], "meaning": "From acting to music, Drake has been grafting for a very long time and as a result; his life is amazing.\n\nFrom winning awards, to buying Italian and German cars, dominating billboard charts, breaking Spotify records and an estimated net worth of 90 million dollars, Drake’s quality of life is arguably above average.\n\nDrake is stating that it should be, because he put in the work for it; it’s well deserved.", "annotation_id": "14641044", "url": "https://genius.com/14641044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Wham-wham-wham, bitch, I’m the baby", "vault_lines": ["Wham-wham-wham, bitch, I'm the baby"], "meaning": "Lil Baby uses onomatopoeia here to make the noise of a young infant, or in other words, a “lil' baby.”\n\nHe makes fun of the childishness of his name, showing that he is self-aware. Being 23 at the time this song was recorded, he obviously is not an infant. He chose the name “Lil Baby” because he “ran around with older dudes.”\n\nLil Baby revealed in a 2018 interview that it was Offset who came up with this line while they were in the studio together.", "annotation_id": "14620596", "url": "https://genius.com/14620596", "state": "accepted", "votes": 215, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Presidential tints slide by and we don’t see you", "vault_lines": ["Presidential tints slide by and we don't see you"], "meaning": "The legal limit for window tints is usually 50% (of light allowed in) for the front window and 35% for all other windows. Dark tints are illegal because police prefer seeing the driver.\n\nPresidential tint likely refer to the windows on the presidential state car, AKA “The Beast”. Many of the specifications on the long car are for the safety of the passengers.", "annotation_id": "14589502", "url": "https://genius.com/14589502", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Me and my dog goin’ all the way", "vault_lines": ["Me and my dog goin' all the way"], "meaning": "Sure this makes sense as it is, as Lil Baby is referring to his friends as his “dawgs”, but he is also referencing his biggest hit, “My Dawg” where he raps and sings about his close friends;\n\nYeah that’s my dawg for sure\nYeah that’s my dawg\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eizDlMTanE4\n\nLil Baby ain’t a goofy, and isn’t easily trusting.\nHe uses this term to certify a real nigga who is a part of his circle and affiliates with proudly. He is very picky with who he classifies as a true friend, a dawg and his nigga (as referenced in ‘Freestyle’);\n\nNone of my niggas no hoe\nAll of my niggas want smoke\nAll of my niggas together we came from the bottom we used to wear each other clothes\nNone of my niggas gon' fold\nCouple pussy niggas told\nThey ain’t my niggas no mo'…\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MHryuYVyHhk", "annotation_id": "14581868", "url": "https://genius.com/14581868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Met all these niggas, they sweeter than Sadie", "vault_lines": ["Met all these niggas, they sweeter than Sadie"], "meaning": "Drake is making a reference to the 1974 track “Sadie” by The Spinners. The referenced lyrics to this song are:\n\nOh, Sadie\nDon’t you know we love you\nSweet Sadie\nPlace no one above you\n\n2Pac interpolated the lyric on his 1995 classic “Dear Mama.”", "annotation_id": "14571181", "url": "https://genius.com/14571181", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Me and Drake ‘bout to drop, man, this shit gon’ go crazy", "vault_lines": ["Me and Drake 'bout to drop, man, this shit gon' go crazy"], "meaning": "Drake has a history of launching artists' careers. In 2013, Drake remixed Atlanta rap group Migos' song “Versace.” His remix made the song go viral and peak at 99 on the US Billboard Hot 100 chart.\n\nMany other large artists can attribute where they are to a Drake co-sign. Throughout his career Drake has collaborated with artists that were on the rise, such as Kendrick Lamar, The Weeknd, PARTYNEXTDOOR, and most recently BlocBoy JB (with their collaboration “Look Alive” reaching #5 on the US Billboard Hot 100 chart).", "annotation_id": "14569132", "url": "https://genius.com/14569132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Tailor my clothes, no starch, please", "vault_lines": ["Tailor my clothes, no starch, please"], "meaning": "When dry cleaning clothes, the option to apply starch can help make the garments easier to clean, lengthen the time between cleanings, and stiffen clothes for a more presentable appearance.\n\nThere are a few reasons Lil Baby may not want his clothes pressed with starch. The first is that starching is typically used on more formal attire and his apparel is mostly relaxed street wear. A second reason could be that because of his wealth, he has no need to clean a garment twice and would prefer to just buy a new piece.", "annotation_id": "14568063", "url": "https://genius.com/14568063", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Booked for three years, man, you can’t even book me", "vault_lines": ["Booked for three years, man, you can't even book me"], "meaning": "Drake is arguably the most popular musician in the world as of May 2018. On streaming services, he has broken several records, including single-day streams. Also, he is the second most-listened-to artist on Spotify, with 50 million listeners. On top of that, Drake has had a total of 163 songs on Billboard charts, including 25 top ten hits and 5 songs that went number one.\n\nWith this popularity comes people wanting to book Drake for shows and private events. “You can’t even book me” may also mean that he’ll never be caught doing something illegal.", "annotation_id": "14565268", "url": "https://genius.com/14565268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Real dope boy, hundred thousand in Evisu", "vault_lines": ["Real dope boy, hundred thousand in Evisu"], "meaning": "Evisu, also known as Evisu Genes, is a Japanese designer clothing company that specializes in denim wear. It’s also a popular brand among rappers—for example, JAY-Z namedropped it on his 2001 song “Jigga That Nigga”:\n\nAnd so I breeze through, jeans is Evisu\n\nAnd T.I. shouted it out on 2005’s “ASAP”:\n\nSeen on the scene, white tees and Evisu", "annotation_id": "14565168", "url": "https://genius.com/14565168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Brand-new whip got no keys", "vault_lines": ["Brand-new whip got no keys"], "meaning": "The keyless ignition is a piece of technology that was introduced by Mercedes-Benz in 1998. In a typical ignition system, a key is inserted into the ignition to start the car. However, in the keyless system, the car recognizes when a key fob is in the car, allowing for the ignition to be activated at the press of a button.", "annotation_id": "14554350", "url": "https://genius.com/14554350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Young Money Records, the Army, the Navy", "vault_lines": ["Young Money Records, the Army, the Navy"], "meaning": "Drake offers his own spin of fellow Cash Money artist, BG’s “Cash Money Is An Army” which would later be interpolated by JAY-Z   on “Takeover” and even by Drake himself on French Montana’s “No Shopping”:\n\nCash Money is a army, nigga, a navy, nigga\n\nSince 2009, Drake has been signed to Young Money Entertainment, a popular label owned and curated by Lil Wayne. Other notable artists that are signed to Young Money include Nicki Minaj, Cory Gunz, and Mack Maine.\n\nAlthough Drake is a part of Young Money, he also owns his label: October’s Very Own, a subsidiary of Warner Bros. Records. Since Young Money associates with Atlantic Records, Drake has announced that his work would be released through YMCMB whereas members of OVO Sound have their records released with Warner Bros.", "annotation_id": "14553499", "url": "https://genius.com/14553499", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "(Wheezy outta here)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Wheezy is an Atlanta producer, best known for his work with Young Thug, 21 Savage, and a majority of rap artists that come from the ATL area. He previously worked with Drake on 21 Savage’s 2017 single “Issa”.\n\nThis is the first time Wheezy utilized the tag: “Wheezy outta here” – spoken by fellow ATL rapper Future as he had before used the tags, “Wheezy,” “My nigga Wheezy, know, what I’m sayin'?” and “Wheezy Beatz” throughout his discography.", "annotation_id": "14553420", "url": "https://genius.com/14553420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 157, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "They ran me ten thousand, I threw it like Brady", "vault_lines": ["They ran me ten thousand, I threw it like Brady"], "meaning": "Drake’s net worth, as of October 2017, is estimated at $90 million, so ten thousand to him is like “throwing money”—in this line, he is also revisiting the previous “snowin' in Tootsie’s.”\n\nDrake even compares his throwing ability to the one of Tom Brady, an American football quarterback for the New England Patriots, who had thrown for over 66,000 yards in his career as of this song’s release, and won five Super Bowls, thus making him the winningest player in the NFL history.", "annotation_id": "14551386", "url": "https://genius.com/14551386", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Cartier glasses, I won’t even peek at you", "vault_lines": ["Cartier glasses, I won't even peek at you"], "meaning": "Société Cartier is a French luxury jewelry brand, known for their eyewear. Their glasses and sunglasses are quite expensive and could be compared to a symbol of status and wealth.\n\n\n\n  Drake wearing a pair of vintage gold Cartier Sunglasses  \n\nLil Baby plays on the phonetic similarities between “peek-at-you” and the yellow Pokémon, Pikachu—a character he also references in the subsequent lyric.", "annotation_id": "14551271", "url": "https://genius.com/14551271", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "The foreign is yellow like Tracee and K.D.​", "vault_lines": ["The foreign is yellow like Tracee and K.D.​"], "meaning": "In 2018, Drake purchased a Ferrari LaFerrari, which has an estimated price of $1.5 million. He compares its yellow color to Tracee Ellis Ross and K.D. Aubert, who are light-skinned African-American women—or “yellow bones.”\n\nInterestingly, Tracee featured in Drake’s music video for his 2018 track, “Nice For What,” which dealt with female empowerment.", "annotation_id": "14550157", "url": "https://genius.com/14550157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "The dash, it’s digi’, the schedule busy", "vault_lines": ["The dash, it's digi', the schedule busy"], "meaning": "Most likely a reference to Drake and Future’s 2015 track “Digital Dash” from their What A Time To Be Alive collaboration.\n\nA “digital dash” is a modern dashboard in newer supercars that displays information on a digital screen.", "annotation_id": "14550877", "url": "https://genius.com/14550877", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "My cousins are crazy, my cousins like Boogie", "vault_lines": ["My cousins are crazy, my cousins like Boogie"], "meaning": "NBA superstar DeMarcus “Boogie” Cousins is known for having a short temper on the court and often argues with officials and players. At the time of this song’s release, he has amassed a total of 118 technical fouls in the NBA and 13 ejections.\n\n\n\nDrake and Boogie have a good relationship off the court, and Drake has mentioned Cousins before in his music on the 2015 song “6 Man”:\n\nI might go DeMarcus Cousins out in public", "annotation_id": "14550311", "url": "https://genius.com/14550311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 148, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Been here for ten, but I feel like a rookie", "vault_lines": ["Been here for ten, but I feel like a rookie"], "meaning": "Drake released his first EP which was also his third mixtape ‘So Far Gone’ in 2009. The EP included Best I Ever Had as the lead single later becoming Drake’s first top-ten single.\n\nEver since the release of ‘So Far Gone’, Drake has released 4 studio albums, along with 2 mixtapes, an EP, and a playlist. The albums being: ‘Thank Me Later’ which released in 2010, ‘Take Care’ releasing in 2011, ‘Nothing Was the Same’ in 2013 and his most recent being ‘Views’ which released in 2016. His mixtapes include: ‘If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late’ which released in 2015 and a collaboration mixtape with Future being ‘What a Time to Be Alive’ which released in 2015. And the playlist being ‘More Life’.\n\nThe EP titled ‘Scary Hours’ released in 2018 having God’s Plan as one of the two songs on the project.\n\nOn April 16, 2018, Drake posted a photo on instagram announcing his fifth album ‘Scorpion’ expected to release in June of 2018 with the post’s caption being\n\n 📀 JUNE 2018\n\nThis marks ten years since Drake’s career started.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BhpENZcnJMK/?taken-by=champagnepapi\n\nHe says he feels like rookie because he is still very popular to this day. Especially with God’s Plan and Nice For What, his two newest hits of 2018. Usually, artists are forgotten after so many years.", "annotation_id": "14550235", "url": "https://genius.com/14550235", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Wheezy produced it and Weezy F. made me", "vault_lines": ["Wheezy produced it and Weezy F. made me"], "meaning": "Wheezy is the Atlanta, GA, producer responsible for this collaboration’s instrumental. Wheezy has been credited on many of Young Thug’s tracks as well as the 21 Savage and Drake collab “Issa.”\n\nWeezy F. Baby—the F. meaning many different things—or Lil Wayne played a major role as a mentor for Drake by signing him to Cash Money Records in 2009, solidifying his status as a superstar.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WpdfOKK9Ou8&t=0s&index=43&list=PLlBigmhynF-VsYnnHChfiwdagGDrkJopk", "annotation_id": "14550122", "url": "https://genius.com/14550122", "state": "accepted", "votes": 158, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "I tell her, “Look up,” ‘cause it’s snowin’ in Tootsie’s", "vault_lines": ["I tell her, \"Look up,\" 'cause it's snowin' in Tootsie's"], "meaning": "Tootsie’s Cabaret is a strip club in Miami. The snow Drake is referring to is the bills he is throwing on the girls at the club.\n\nThis is most likely the same Tootsie’s he mentioned in “Back to Back:”\n\nSecond floor at Tootsies, gettin' shoulder rubs\n\nAdditionally, snow is a common street name for the drug cocaine, which is most often consumed by snorting through the nose. A common practice is to tilt one’s head back following a hit as it is thought to help absorption.", "annotation_id": "14549733", "url": "https://genius.com/14549733", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Yes Indeed", "line": "Yellow Ferrari like Pikachu", "vault_lines": ["Yellow Ferrari like Pikachu"], "meaning": "Pikachu is a yellow Pokémon character. Pikachu is the mascot for Pokémon, and can be seen in many video games, animes, and in the Pokémon trading card game.\n\nLil Baby is referring to Drake’s new $7 million LaFerrari, which is yellow, and saying that the color matches that of Pikachu.  This song was also originally leaked under the name of “Pikachu”.\n\n\n\nPokémon references in hip-hop is not new. In fact, Genius has produced a video that explains some of the most well-known:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PH5nkhmXcEI", "annotation_id": "14549692", "url": "https://genius.com/14549692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
