{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "She got ice in all her veins (All the ice)", "vault_lines": ["She got ice in all her veins (All the ice)"], "meaning": "Quavo is referencing NBA player D'Angelo Russell in this line, in which D'Angelo’s signature celebration is pointing to his wrist, meaning he is cold blooded (ice in his veins).\n\n\n\nQuavo is saying that this girl is cold blooded, meaning that she shows and/or has no emotions.", "annotation_id": "16831367", "url": "https://genius.com/16831367", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "Let’s call the boys up (BM) Lambos gon’ swing (Skrrt)", "vault_lines": ["Let's call the boys up (BM) Lambos gon' swing (Skrrt)"], "meaning": "When Quavo was on tour in Norway, he got friends with one of the best rap groups in the country (ÅBB). After his concert in Oslo, they drove around in lambos before the after party.\n\n\n\nQuavo also posted a video from when they drove around, on his instagram account.", "annotation_id": "15960696", "url": "https://genius.com/15960696", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "She wanna hang when the album drop, that’s timely", "vault_lines": ["She wanna hang when the album drop, that's timely"], "meaning": "This is a possible shot at Nicki Minaj. Drake and Nicki hadn’t been seen together in the public eye for much of 2018.\nHowever, Nicki posted this video of the two of them together in a studio just before her album dropped to likely increase anticipation.\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/Bl_i7tHAPrn/?taken-by=nickiminaj\n\nDrake makes it known that he finds it funny that just before her album is released is when she decides to hang out together", "annotation_id": "15563516", "url": "https://genius.com/15563516", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "Word to Smiley", "vault_lines": ["Ayy, got a present for my oppers, word to Smiley"], "meaning": "He is referring to the artist Smiley 61st, whom he previously mentioned on IG among others that he used for “inspiration” when he was working on Scorpion.", "annotation_id": "15562026", "url": "https://genius.com/15562026", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "You the one that’s tryna keep your business low-key, huh? (huh?) You the one that’s having your main nigga, can’t get no sleep, huh? (Sleep, hey) You the one who walk around on fleek, huh? (Fleek) You the one that started from the bottom but you’ve reached your peak, huh? (Peak, peak) You taught her how to swallow with no teeth, huh? Make them dollars out the street, huh? (Cash) Baddest bitch you ever seen, huh? (Bad) You the one who split the guap with your team, huh? (Split it) You the one who drink a lotta codeine, huh? (Drank) Ayy, ayy I like this shit, this shit take me back to the ’99, 2000", "vault_lines": ["You the one who drink a lotta codeine, huh? (Drank)", "Ayy, ayy I like this shit, this shit take me back to the '99, 2000", "Ayy, ayy, I got shorties out tryna find me, that look", "You the one that's tryna keep your business low-key, huh? (huh?)", "Got to move low-key with the gangsters", "You the one that's having your main nigga, can't get no sleep, huh? (Sleep, hey)", "You the one who walk around on fleek, huh? (Fleek)", "You the one that started from the bottom but you reached your peak, huh? (Peak, peak)", "You the one that started from the bottom but you've reached your peak, huh? (Peak, peak)", "You taught her how to swallow with no teeth, huh?", "Make them dollars out the street, huh? (Cash)", "Baddest bitch you ever seen, huh? (Bad)", "You the one who split the guap with your team, huh? (Split it)"], "meaning": "Quavo interpolates Juvenile’s flow from his iconic 1998 single “Ha.” On the song, Juvenile starts most bars with “you” and ends them with the eponymous “Ha.”\n\nThe last line is inspired by Juvenile’s breakthrough mainstream hit “Back That Azz Up”:\n\nCash Money Records taking over for the ‘99 & the 2000\n\nQuavo also borrowed Juvenile’s “Ha” flow for his verse on DJ Mustard’s September 2016 track, “Want Her.”", "annotation_id": "15561782", "url": "https://genius.com/15561782", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "I just order Phantoms off the website They ask how I want the shit, I say, “Surprise me”", "vault_lines": ["I just order Phantoms off the website", "They ask how I want the shit, I say, \"Surprise me\""], "meaning": "Drake’s time is too valuable at this point in his career to go to the Rolls Royce dealer and worry about the modifications on a new Phantom Celestial. In August 2013, Drake posted a video of his vehicle being pulled over by police in Toronto.  He was driving his custom-ordered Phantom Rolls Royce, rumored to have cost $800,000.00. The car created a lot of curiosity because it showcased a fiber optic starlight roof, which suggests he modified it (or was a surprise modification they made for Drizzy?) Drake’s a huge fan of the car, as shown by some of his many references to it throughout his career:\n\n\nPhantom slidin' like the shit just hit a puddle of grease― Drake – 6PM in New York\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nI feel like Juelz Santana Leg hangin' out the Phantom― Drake – Hype", "annotation_id": "15561626", "url": "https://genius.com/15561626", "state": "pending", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "Slipping through the cracks ’cause I’m slimy, I slipped", "vault_lines": ["Slipping through the cracks 'cause I'm slimy, I slipped"], "meaning": "Here, Drizzy gives a shout out to his collaborator and former tourmate Young Thug who often refers to himself as “Slime.” Slime is a motif that’s prevalent in Thugger’s music and persona, so much so that he titled the mixtape series Slime Season after the phrase. In an interview with SWAY’S UNIVERSE, Thug explained the various indeterminate meanings of the slang term:\nhttps://youtu.be/ip8dztJp840?t=25m\n\nDrake rapped a similar line on his 2018 track “Nonstop”:\n\nAnd you know it’s King Slime Drizzy damn (ooh yeah)\n\nHe may also be referencing the acronym form of the term–Street Life Intelligence and Money is Everything–which is mentioned by Lil Wayne on Drake’s track “Miss Me”:\n\nIt’s Blood gang, slime, but I parlay with Snoop", "annotation_id": "15561612", "url": "https://genius.com/15561612", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "Bad bitches and they lit (Woo) I just told ’em, “Flip the switch” (Flip it) Gang ’round, with the shits (Switch it) I just told ’em, “Flip the switch” (Hey) (Flip it, flip it, flip it, flip it) Switch it (Flip it, flip it, flip it, flip it) Flip that ho switch (Flip it!)", "vault_lines": ["Bad bitches and they lit (Woo)", "I just told 'em, \"Flip the switch\" (Flip it)", "Gang 'round, with the shits (Switch it)", "Flip that ho switch"], "meaning": "A reference to Drake’s line in his 2018 track, “Nonstop,” where he raps:\n\nLook, I just flipped a switch (flipped, flipped)\n\nDrake is also featured on this song while the producer of “Nonstop”, Tay Keith, also has credits for his production on “SHINE,” another track from QUAVO HUNCHO.\n\nIn this line, flipping the switch could refer to Quavo telling these women to start partying and engaging in sexual activity with him.", "annotation_id": "15561470", "url": "https://genius.com/15561470", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "Nigga, you was in your 20s in the ’90s, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, you was in your 20s in the '90s, yeah"], "meaning": "Kanye West was born in the year of 1977, which means he was 20 by 1997 making him a perfect candidate for this line. Another likely candidate is Pusha T, who was born in the same year as West.\n\nDrake has been going back and forth with Kanye and Pusha T since Pusha dissed him on the Kanye produced track, “Infrared”, where he rapped:\n\nHow could you ever right these wrongs\nWhen you don’t even write your songs?\n\nDrake then responded in less than 24 hours with his track, “Duppy Freestyle,” which was filled with shots at Ye and Pusha.\n\nPusha then released the final diss track, “The Story of Adidon,” where he then went on to expose Drake’s child with a pornstar:\n\nAdonis is your son\nAnd he deserves more than an Adidas press run, that’s real\nLove that baby, respect that girl\nForget she’s a pornstar, let her be your world, yuugh!\n\nSince then, Drake has taken subtle shots at Ye and Pusha throughout his most recent album, Scorpion, and in features such as “SICKO MODE” where Drake rapped:\n\nJesus Christ, (yeah) checks over stripes (yeah)\nThat’s what I like, (yeah) that’s what we like (yeah)\nLost my respect, you not a threat\n\nDrake could be dissing these two by making a point that they are no longer relevant in the rap game due to their older age at 41 years old at the time of the song’s release.", "annotation_id": "15561435", "url": "https://genius.com/15561435", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "FLIP THE SWITCH", "line": "Chain wetter than a river you could cry me Quick to Timberlake a bitch, now she despise me, I swear", "vault_lines": ["Chain wetter than a river you could cry me", "Quick to Timberlake a bitch, now she despise me, I swear"], "meaning": "This pair of lines is a metaphorical combination of the term, “dripping” and a throwback to the infamous song “Cry Me a River” by Justin Timberlake.\n\nThe slang word “drip” is used in reference to having expensive clothes/jewelry. Just like a faucet that drips, the result of the drip is a wet surface. In this case, Drake is stating that his chain is dripping wet so much that it creates a river.\n\n\n\nJustin Timberlake dropped his famous song, Cry Me A River, back in 2002 but it has been frequently referred to as one of the greatest break up songs of all time. Drake dropped in a clever reference here, but the song title wasn’t the only clever part to this reference. Drake is simultaneously alluding to the fact that he doesn’t care if women cry a river over him possibly hurting them, because he keeps it pushing regardless.", "annotation_id": "15561392", "url": "https://genius.com/15561392", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "status": "done", "targets": 13, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Partynextdoor-and-drake-come-and-see-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "Come and See Me", "genius_id": 2442052, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "Could be standin’ in a field, and he still ain’t in the field", "vault_lines": ["Could be standin' in a field, and he still ain't in the field"], "meaning": "This man may be there by her side at times, but although he is physically present, he is not mentally present.\n\nEven if Drake was standing in a field, his mind would be somewhere else because he may act like he cares about her, but he doesn’t really.\n\nYet another Drake sports reference for the trophy room.", "annotation_id": "8846263", "url": "https://genius.com/8846263", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "I hit you up like “Do you wanna hang right now?”", "vault_lines": ["I hit you up like \"Do you wanna hang right now?\""], "meaning": "Despite usually waiting for the girl to call him, Party asserts himself by trying to initiate a late-night meet up, similar to the encounters Drake detailed on “Hotline Bling.”\n\nHowever, this woman isn’t flattered by Party’s offer. She feels like he’s using her for convenient sex. She’s sarcastic as she criticizes him for making a late-night booty call.\n\nPerhaps she’d rather go shopping for a Rolex in daylight?", "annotation_id": "8846242", "url": "https://genius.com/8846242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "But either way you’re still invited and I can’t even lie to you", "vault_lines": ["But either way you're still invited and I can't even lie to you"], "meaning": "This line contains a certain level of irony, as the main complaint, portrayed in the hook, is that Party doesn’t make the effort to visit her, yet he seemingly disregards this and offers her an open invitation to hook up with him.\n\nThis ignorance is potentially deliberate, allowing the listener to sympathise with the female’s perspective, a rarity in male driven music. Alternatively, the lust he has towards this woman may’ve clouded his judgement, and he’s not using his brain to make decisions so to speak…\n\nThinkin' my heart don’t got nothing to do with my penis\nHe got a mind of his own and he just be seeing shit", "annotation_id": "8846100", "url": "https://genius.com/8846100", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "Things change, people change, feelings change too Never thought the circumstances woulda changed you You said you never traded, no (Yeah, you said that) And I believed you when they told me don’t (Yeah, I thought you meant that)", "vault_lines": ["Things change, people change, feelings change too", "Never thought the circumstances woulda changed you", "You said you never traded, no (Yeah, you said that)", "And I believed you when they told me don't"], "meaning": "There is danger of getting into a relationship while being in the limelight. A woman can get paranoid when their man goes on tour and is around a lot of adoring women. They may also become infatuated by the allure of money.\n\nDespite his friends' warning, he believed these factors wouldn’t change the way she acted towards him. Stylistically, the sentiments expressed here are similar to those in the track “Things & Such,” where Party reflects on his own public image, and the detrimental effects it had on his and Kehlani’s relationship:\n\nShe said “Who’s that nigga?”, he’s a R&B singer\n“Who’s that nigga, what’s his intention?”\nYou say “he’s a good nigga, he’s a good nigga, he’s a hood nigga”\n\n\n\nPND now avoids questions about Kehlani during interviews.", "annotation_id": "8845852", "url": "https://genius.com/8845852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "All she talkin’ bout is, ”Come and see me for once Come and see me for once You don’t ever come to me, you don’t ever come to me” All she ever say is, ”Come and see me for once Come and see me for once You don’t ever come to me, you don’t ever come to me”", "vault_lines": ["Come and see me for once", "You don't ever come to me, you don't ever come to me\"", "All she ever say is, \"Come and see me for once", "Swear these days all you say is, \"Come and see me for once", "All she hit me with is, \"Come and see me for once", "Come and see me for once, oh", "All she talkin' bout is, \"Come and see me for once"], "meaning": "PND and Drake garner a lot of attention from women due to the sensual vibe in their music. They usually don’t have to make much effort (despite some demanding requests) to get a girl to comply.\n\nThis specific woman is questioning their selfish mentality, which serves as a sobering reminder that men should be making an equal effort to visit their woman, instead of always expecting her to make the journey.\n\nOn “Things and Such,” Party called a taxi for himself just to go and satisfy his woman’s needs, while Drizzy has asked a former lover why she never visits him at his place on “My Side.”", "annotation_id": "8845776", "url": "https://genius.com/8845776", "state": "accepted", "votes": 189, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "I been up for two whole days thinkin’ what I did to keep you goin’ Thumbin’ through the voicemails that you left me tellin’ me where I went wrong", "vault_lines": ["I been up for two whole days thinkin' what I did to keep you goin'", "Thumbin' through the voicemails that you left me tellin' me where I went wrong"], "meaning": "On “Wednesday Night Interlude,” PND experienced a similar bout of introspection.\n\nBeen a minute since we’ve slept together\nGotta get myself together\nI’ve been thinkin' about everything\nI don’t know if it’s because I’m lonely\n\nThis relationship has died at his hand, and it’s a far cry from the message on “No Feelings.” He doesn’t yet feel responsible for the end, but he’s trying to make sense of it.", "annotation_id": "8845684", "url": "https://genius.com/8845684", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "Why you gotta start, girl, why you gotta start?", "vault_lines": ["Why you gotta start, girl, why you gotta start?"], "meaning": "Drake responds to the last line on the hook, insisting he isn’t in the mood for yet another argument.\n\nHe rapped about a similar situation earlier in his career on Chris Brown’s “Deuces (Remix)”:\n\nSeems like we’re arguing more and it’s getting less romantic", "annotation_id": "8845629", "url": "https://genius.com/8845629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "I don’t even know what things are looking like inside of your place Or how it’s decorated This thing is gettin’ one-sided, I can’t even lie to you right now Yeah", "vault_lines": ["I don't even know what things are looking like inside of your place", "Or how it's decorated", "This thing is gettin' one-sided, I can't even lie to you right now"], "meaning": "In the final couplet, Drake realizes that he’s the one messing things up—he can’t even describe what her place looks like.\n\nThis track reverses a situation that Drake raps about on “My Side”—this time it’s Drizzy who’s not going to her place:\n\nWhy you never come to where I stay at?\nAlways hiding out in your New York condo", "annotation_id": "8845611", "url": "https://genius.com/8845611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "If anything should change then I thought we had a deal Lately you keep questionin’ what you get out the deal Doin’ things to make me feel the way I make you feel", "vault_lines": ["If anything should change then I thought we had a deal", "Lately you keep questionin' what you get out the deal", "Doin' things to make me feel the way I make you feel"], "meaning": "It seems Drake signed this girl to the “Sweeterman” contract but she had second thoughts and started seeing someone else.\n\nAlthough Drake also sees other girls, this still makes him jealous. He expressed a similar sentiment on “Hotline Bling”:\n\nGirl you got me down, you got me stressed out\nCause ever since I left the city, you\nStarted wearing less and goin' out more", "annotation_id": "8845553", "url": "https://genius.com/8845553", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "How hard is it to let you know when I’m comin’ home? That way you can be prepared Maybe take a couple sick days and not miss pay", "vault_lines": ["How hard is it to let you know when I'm comin' home?", "That way you can be prepared", "Maybe take a couple sick days and not miss pay"], "meaning": "Drake’s always on the road. The night this song was released, he announced at SXSW that he’d be back in Austin later in the year for the presumed Views tour.\n\n\n\nDrake’s taking some responsibility in this relationship by trying to make time for his girl in advance, but he also requires some effort on her part. He wants this girl to take sick leave from work, so they can hang and she still gets paid.\n\nAssuming Drizzy’s girl is in Toronto, the only way for her to get paid sick days is if she works at a company with over 50 employees—paid sick days are not guaranteed for smaller firms under Ontario public health laws. Corporate gyal!", "annotation_id": "8845446", "url": "https://genius.com/8845446", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "I know you got another nigga tryna play the part Just ’cause he got a heart don’t mean he got heart", "vault_lines": ["I know you got another nigga tryna play the part", "Just 'cause he got a heart don't mean he got heart"], "meaning": "On “Moment 4 Life,” Nicki Minaj rapped:\n\nBut to live, doesn’t mean you’re alive\n\nDrake was featured on that track and applies a similar message here. For this girl’s “side piece” to be alive he must have a heart, but the organ Drake refers to is more than a pump. Just because this person has a heart, it doesn’t mean they’re using it to care for this girl.\n\nAnd you don’t even have to ask twice\nYou could have my heart or we could share it like the last slice", "annotation_id": "8845421", "url": "https://genius.com/8845421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "On the East Side and you know I’m with the gang right now", "vault_lines": ["On the East Side and you know I'm with the gang right now"], "meaning": "On “FWU,” Party sings:\n\nWorkin' in the East but you know we’re in the West End\n\nParty is actually from Mississauga, which is West Toronto—on occasion he refers to the city as West District.\n\nParty’s girl is presumably back in his hometown (West), while he’s touring and making music in the East. Consequently, it’d be easier for her to come and visit him instead of the other way round—he wants her to hold him down while he’s away.\n\nPND’s crew, OVO, has a number of artists that call the East Side of the Greater Toronto Area home. Drake and Majid Jordan both hail from Toronto and reference East Side neighbourhoods in their music:\n\nI got real ones livin’ past Kennedy Road", "annotation_id": "8845419", "url": "https://genius.com/8845419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Come and See Me", "line": "Doesn’t make sense now Shit just got real, things are gettin’ intense now I hear you talkin’ ’bout “We” a lot, oh, you speak French now? Giving me the signs so I gotta take a hint now", "vault_lines": ["Doesn't make sense now", "Shit just got real, things are gettin' intense now", "I hear you talkin' 'bout \"We\" a lot, oh, you speak French now?", "Doin' things to make me feel the way I make you feel", "Giving me the signs so I gotta take a hint now"], "meaning": "Party isn’t ready for a real relationship but his girl is. To him she’s just a booty call—this is why he never puts in the effort to go to her place.\n\n“Oui” is the French word for “yes.” Party knows that she’s hinting at a relationship but he deflects with a sarcastic joke. He doesn’t want to talk about their situation right now, he only wants her to say “oui” (French for yes) when he asks if he can come over.\n\nParty has a song titled “Speaking French” where he sings: “Do, do you wanna sleep with me?” in French:\n\nVoulez, Voulez-vous coucher avec moi?\nVoulez, Voulez-vous coucher avec moi?\nVoulez, Voulez-vous coucher avec moi?\nVoulez, Voulez-vous coucher avec moi, ce soir?\n\nThe question was famously first posed in Patti LaBelle’s 1975 soul/funk hit “Lady Marmalade.”", "annotation_id": "8845412", "url": "https://genius.com/8845412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 161, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
