{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ahí", "line": "How ya mean, how ya mean, how ya mean", "vault_lines": ["How ya mean, how ya mean, how ya mean"], "meaning": "Could be an interpolation of his own song “Cameras”?\n\nhttps://genius.com/Drake-cameras-lyrics", "annotation_id": "40980353", "url": "https://genius.com/40980353", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ahí", "line": "Well like, one specific human trait Like a woman can have What, what would you like in a woman? Kind, and, uh Latina", "vault_lines": ["Well like, one specific human trait", "Like a woman can have", "What, what would you like in a woman?"], "meaning": "The outro is from a viral “man on the street” style interview in which the interviewer, comedian Anthony Santino asks a man what he is looking for in a woman.", "annotation_id": "40979918", "url": "https://genius.com/40979918", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ahí", "line": "Bro just snuck in a place with a Nina", "vault_lines": ["Bro just snuck in a place with a Nina"], "meaning": "Nina deriving from nine is commonly used as slang for a 9mm handgun. This is also likely a double entendre, as niña (with a slightly different pronunciation due to the tilde) directly translated means “girl,” so Drake is saying his bro snuck in with a weapon and also was with a woman.", "annotation_id": "40977548", "url": "https://genius.com/40977548", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Ahí", "line": "I want a Latina, I want a Latina I want a Latina, I want a La— (Ah)", "vault_lines": ["I want a Latina, I want a Latina", "I want a Latina, I want a La— (Ah)", "I want a La— (Ah, papi)"], "meaning": "This verse interpolates his own on “New Bestie”, the tenth track on “MAID OF HONOUR”,  the dance and club-oriented album of the three suprise releases.\n\nDrake mentions Karol G in these lyrics.\n\nYeah, I want a Latina, I want a Latina\nI want a G like Karolina", "annotation_id": "40972135", "url": "https://genius.com/40972135", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/The-game-no-brakes-lyrics", "genius_title": "No Brakes", "genius_id": 14111746, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "What up, twin? Yeah, that’s my twinny-twin-twin", "vault_lines": ["What up, twin? Yeah, that's my twinny-twin-twin"], "meaning": "Game is boosting his solidarity and close kinship with Drake. This is also most likely a sneak diss aimed at Kendrick Lamar, making fun of his lyric on Playboi Carti’s “GOOD CREDIT”:\n\nCardo my evil twin, Carti my evil twin", "annotation_id": "41337562", "url": "https://genius.com/41337562", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "I’m back outside, sleep when I die", "vault_lines": ["I'm back outside, sleep when I die"], "meaning": "In this line Drake and The game are telling us something that has also been hinted in the title “No brakes”. So The game and Drake are saying in this line and in the title that they are going to live they’re life to the fullest and nobody is gonna stop them. In other words they are saying that “ They are gonna sleep when they die ” Which they don’t mean actually but say it as I’m not gonna do things in life that waste my time (sleeping) “I can sleep when I die ” And I’m gonna make the best of that time.", "annotation_id": "41220876", "url": "https://genius.com/41220876", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "You know I keep that motherfucker tucked", "vault_lines": ["You know I keep that motherfucker tucked"], "meaning": "Another shot at Kendrick actually. He is referencing the 2013 BET Hip Hop Awards – TDE Cypher, where Kendrick infamously said: “And tucked a sensitive rapper back in his pajama clothes”, which was subliminal to Drake and potentially all rappers that were not happy with his “Control” verse back in the days.", "annotation_id": "41182882", "url": "https://genius.com/41182882", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "Nigga said pick a side, then I’m pickin’ homicide", "vault_lines": ["Nigga said pick a side, then I'm pickin' homicide"], "meaning": "This is a shot at Kendrick Lamar who said on “wacced out murals”\n\n“Since you’re pandering to choose a side, let me do it for you”\n\nKendrick was saying that there is no friendship with him and Drake, you either align yourself with one or the other. Game says that due to his gangbanging history, the only “side” he knows is “homicide” or choosing violence.\n\nCould also be a homophone for “homies side”. Game has picked a side, its his homie Drakes.", "annotation_id": "41173029", "url": "https://genius.com/41173029", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "Nigga walk in this shit with a limp Nigga snuck a whole Drac’ in this bitch", "vault_lines": ["Nigga walk in this shit with a limp", "Nigga snuck a whole Drac' in this bitch"], "meaning": "Walking anywhere with a Drac' gun concealed would weigh your pants down and cause you to limp. Similarly this is a play on words for Drakes name, Game snuck a Drake feature into this album that is impossible to conceal because of Drakes size.", "annotation_id": "41169213", "url": "https://genius.com/41169213", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "Champagne shootin’ like it’s B-roll", "vault_lines": ["Champagne shootin' like it's B-roll"], "meaning": "Champagne is a reference to Drake, aka Champagnepapi. He is “shooting” on the chorus like B-roll, which is additional footage that intercuts the main shot (The Game verses)", "annotation_id": "41168036", "url": "https://genius.com/41168036", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "Boy, what? Didn’t I say I’m ridin’ with a Drac’? And if 12 get behind me, then I’m runnin’ the race, all gas", "vault_lines": ["Boy, what? Didn't I say I'm ridin' with a Drac'?", "And if 12 get behind me, then I'm runnin' the race, all gas"], "meaning": "“Drac'” refers to a Draco-style firearm, while “12” is slang for the police.\n\nThe Game describes fleeing once police get behind him, with “running the race” referring to the ensuing chase.\n\n“All gas” continues the song’s “no breaks” theme, suggesting both that he is accelerating without slowing down and that he is moving at full intensity.", "annotation_id": "41162217", "url": "https://genius.com/41162217", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "Run up on the six-four, get smoked like a pre-roll, gas", "vault_lines": ["Run up on the six-four, get smoked like a pre-roll, gas"], "meaning": "“The 6ix” is a nickname for Toronto, Drake’s hometown, which he notably helped popularize throughout his music.\n\nThe Game then uses “smoked” as a double entendre, “getting smoked” refers to being killed or defeated, while a pre-roll is a pre-rolled cannabis joint that is literally smoked.\n\nGiven The Game’s friendship and loyalty toward Drake, the line can also be interpreted as a warning toward anyone trying to challenge Drake or his home city.", "annotation_id": "41162157", "url": "https://genius.com/41162157", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "Leather Gucci jacket, I’m sexy and red", "vault_lines": ["Leather Gucci jacket, I'm sexy and red"], "meaning": "The Game appears to reference Sexyy Red through the phrase “sexy and red”, which closely mirrors her stage name.\n\nThe reference is especially fitting given Drake’s close friendship and collaborative history with Sexyy Red, including their work together on “Rich Baby Daddy” and “U My Everything”.\n\nThe word “red” may also carry a second meaning for The Game, who has frequently referenced Bloods associated imagery throughout his career.", "annotation_id": "41162119", "url": "https://genius.com/41162119", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "Like we in Future cup how this motherfucker lean", "vault_lines": ["Like we in Future cup how this motherfucker lean"], "meaning": "The Game uses Future as a double entendre, referencing Future, whose music is heavily associated with lean, a codeine based drink.\n\nA “future cup” implies a cup filled with lean, while “lean” also describes the cup physically tilting to one side.", "annotation_id": "41162061", "url": "https://genius.com/41162061", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Brakes", "line": "Enemigos, take off on a nigga like a Migo And my diamonds offset like a Migo And I’m sippin’ Quavo like a Migo Me, Drake, and a Drac’, nigga, we the three amigos", "vault_lines": ["And my diamonds offset like a Migo", "And I'm sippin' Quavo like a Migo", "Me, Drake, and a Drac', nigga, we the three amigos", "Enemigos, take off on a nigga like a Migo"], "meaning": "The Game references all three members of Migos through wordplay: Takeoff, Offset, and Quavo.\n\n“Takeoff” and “off set” directly play on the names of Takeoff and Offset, while “Quavo” likely doubles as Cuervo, referring to Jose Cuervo tequila.\n\nHe then extends the scheme with Drake and a Drac', short for a Draco firearm, basically calling the trio the “Three Amigos”, a phrase used to describe three close companions.", "annotation_id": "41161971", "url": "https://genius.com/41161971", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Trippie-redd-betrayal-lyrics", "genius_title": "Betrayal", "genius_id": 7097006, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "Finish (Finish) (​PinkGrillz)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "These are the producer tags for Dynox, (Finish), and PinkGrillz88, both producers signed to Internet Money. On September 5, 2021, Dynox and PinkGrillz uploaded a video to the Internet Money YouTube channel breaking down how the beat was made:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Aqf71X-6SZY", "annotation_id": "23998838", "url": "https://genius.com/23998838", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "Keep them choppers like Resident Evil, we dress like BloodRayne", "vault_lines": ["Keep them choppers like Resident Evil, we dress like BloodRaynе"], "meaning": "Trippie references the horror video-game series Resident Evil, in which the player must use a variety of firearms, or “choppers,” in order to survive against zombies.\n\nThe music video for Trippie’s 2017 song “Dark Knight Dummo” is believed to have been inspired by the Resident Evil series, with Trippie similarly fending off hordes of zombies.\n\nFurther, Trippie references the action video-game series BloodRanye. In the series, the main character, a vampire hunter named Rayne, dresses in gothic, black clothes, similar to Trippie’s current vampire-inspired style.", "annotation_id": "23797878", "url": "https://genius.com/23797878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "Brand new K, ballin’ like 2K", "vault_lines": ["Brand new K, ballin' like 2K"], "meaning": "Thanks to Trippie’s success in the music industry, he is “balling,” which is a phrase used to describe living a lavish lifestyle and playing basketball.\n\nTrippie uses a figure of speech, detailing his opulence by referencing basketball video-game series NBA 2K.\n\nTrippie used a similar basketball reference on his 2021 song “MP5,” rapping:\n\nKnow we crackheads, pass me the rock and I’ll be balling like Kobe\n\nFurther, Trippie’s song “UKA UKA” was featured on the NBA 2K20 soundtrack.", "annotation_id": "23790655", "url": "https://genius.com/23790655", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "Niggas prayin’ that I spare ’em like a bowling lane (Woo, woo) Yeah, like Mike (Like Mike) Corleone (Corleone), sporty flow (Sporty flow)", "vault_lines": ["Niggas prayin' that I spare 'em like a bowling lane (Woo, woo)", "Yeah, like Mike (Like Mike)", "Corleone (Corleone), sporty flow (Sporty flow)"], "meaning": "A spare is reference to when a player knocks down all of the remaining pins on the second ball of the frame in bowling. Drake juxtaposes a spare in bowling to Michael Jordan sparing his opponents in the NBA and himself sparing his counterparts in the music industry. Jordan first retired in 1993 and signed with the MLB’s Chicago White Sox despite having come off winning three straight NBA Championships from 1991-1993. Jordan returned in 1995 and proceeded to win three straight NBA Championships from 1996-1998  before retiring in 1999. Jordan would return for a third time playing for the Washington Wizards from 2001-2003.", "annotation_id": "23783285", "url": "https://genius.com/23783285", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "All these fools I’m beefin’ that I barely know", "vault_lines": ["All these fools I'm beefin' that I barely know"], "meaning": "Many rappers have attempted to feud (beef) with Drake for a variety of reasons such as damaged reputations and the ending of their careers. This includes artists such as Common, Puff Daddy and the late XXXTENTACION. Ironically, the people that he is/was trading bars and negative energy with are people who he barely knows. This is notable in Drake’s hit “I’m Upset”:\n\nHate me, never met me in the flesh\n\nThis may also be interpreted as these rappers never even got a chance to talk to Drake about their relationship which is why they feel negatively towards him.\n\nThis may also be a jab towards Ye and Pusha T—the latter rapper dissed Drizzy in his song, “Infrared” from his 2018 album, DAYTONA produced by Ye:\n\nHow could you ever right these wrongs\nWhen you don’t even write your songs?\n\nThese jabs at Ye are confirmed literally in the following lines:\n\nForty-five, forty-four (Burned out), let it go\nYe ain’t changin' shit for me, it’s set in stone", "annotation_id": "23781509", "url": "https://genius.com/23781509", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "Ices out at Johnny Dang", "vault_lines": ["Ices out at Johnny Dang"], "meaning": "Johnny Dang is a jeweler from Texas and is the owner of Johnny Dang & Co.\n\nDang is Trippie’s go-to jeweler, having purchased several bespoke pieces from Dang, including his $50k shark teeth grills.\n\nTrippie previously mentioned Dang on his 2019 track “RMP,” on which he rapped:\n\nEveryday a movie to me, uh, Johnny Cage (Cage)\nGot this ice around my neck like Johnny Dang\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6ycFoGvaS88&ab_channel=johnnydangandco", "annotation_id": "23781239", "url": "https://genius.com/23781239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "Beat shit, Liu Kang Flamin’ shit, Liu Kang", "vault_lines": [", beat shit, Liu Kang", "Flamin' shit, Liu Kang"], "meaning": "Trippie continues his theme of referencing video games throughout the album, and here he references the character Liu Kang from Mortal Kombat\n\n\n\nThis isn’t the only time Trippie references Liu Kang in the album, he also mentions him later on in his song, Matt Hardy 999, with Juice WRLD https://genius.com/20242082", "annotation_id": "23781137", "url": "https://genius.com/23781137", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "Twin Glocks, Max Payne, kickin’ shit, Johnny Cage", "vault_lines": ["Twin Glocks, Max Payne, kickin' shit, Johnny Cage"], "meaning": "Continuing the video-game references, Trippie uses a series of metaphors to describe his violent conduct.\n\nFirst, Trippie is toting two Glock handguns, which he compares to Max Payne, the namesake of the Max Payne videogame series, developed by Remedy Entertainment and Rockstar Studios. Throughout the series, the player has the ability to dual-wield a variety of firearms, including pistols.\n\nFurther, Trippie proceeds to reference Johnny Cage, another video-game character, this time from the Mortal Kombat fighting series. Trippie compares his fighting abilities to that of Johnny, who is an action movie star with martial arts abilities.\n\nTrippie previously referenced Johnny Cage on his 2019 track “RMP,” rapping:\n\nEveryday a movie to me, uh, Johnny Cage (Cage)\nGot this ice around my neck like Johnny Dang", "annotation_id": "23781048", "url": "https://genius.com/23781048", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "I done done it all, it’s like I’m Shawty Lo (Shawty)", "vault_lines": ["I done done it all, it's like I'm Shawty Lo (Shawty)"], "meaning": "Drake boasts his accomplishments while referencing the late Georgia rapper Shawty Lo’s 2007 song, “Dunn Dunn”:\n\nDone done it all, done done it all, I’ve done it all, I’ve done it all\nWho mentioned Bankhead? Didn’t acknowledge me\nWhat, you blind, need some glasses to see?\nDone done it all, done done it all, I’ve done it all, I’ve done it all\nWhat, you blind, need some glasses to see?\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IbcpHDNHd6I", "annotation_id": "23781014", "url": "https://genius.com/23781014", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "Forty-five, forty-four (Burned out), let it go Ye ain’t changin’ shit for me, it’s set in stone", "vault_lines": ["Forty-five, forty-four (Burned out), let it go", "Ye ain't changin' shit for me, it's set in stone"], "meaning": "This appears to be a shot aimed at both Kanye West and Pusha T. Drake has had issues with both rappers for years; he’s feuded with Pusha since 2011, and his friendship with Kanye deteriorated in 2018.\n\nKanye and Pusha are both forty-four years old at the time of this song’s release. Drizzy may be making fun of their age as he’s younger with more popularity, or he might be insinuating that Kanye and Pusha have to overwork themselves just to compete with him.\n\nMoreover, Drake seemingly addresses the rumor that Kanye planned to release his tenth studio album, Donda, around the same time as Drake’s Certified Lover Boy. Despite duelling rollouts, Drake claims that his album won’t be affected in terms of sales.\n\nShortly after this song was released, Kanye responded to the lines on his Instagram by posting a screenshot of a group text with eight people, including Pusha, and possibly Drake. In the chat, West had sent a photo of Joaquin Phoenix as the Joker and a warning that fans speculate is aimed at Drake.", "annotation_id": "23780403", "url": "https://genius.com/23780403", "state": "accepted", "votes": 266, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Betrayal", "line": "Say one thing to me, you Texas toast, no Raising Cane’s", "vault_lines": ["Say one thing to me, you Texas toast, no Raising Cane's"], "meaning": "Trippie Redd nods to the fast food restaurant, Raising Cane’s, a place that specializes in chicken fingers. The restaurant is known for serving their chicken meals with toast, a common practice in many southern restaurants. Trippie plays off the word toast, as it’s often used as an expression of being in trouble.\n\nThe Ohio native frequently mentions the fast food chain throughout his discography, including his November 2020 feature on Doe Boy & Southside’s “Yesterday,” where he raps,\n\nPussy, throw your shit up if you rep the damn gang (Gang, gang)\nI been gettin' to that chicken in the trenches, Raising Cane’s (Oh, really? Raising Cane)", "annotation_id": "23780298", "url": "https://genius.com/23780298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
