{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I Get Paper", "line": "Boi-1da", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Boi-1da is a Grammy award-winning/certified diamond producer from Toronto, a frequent collaborator of Drizzy’s. He’s also the man behind this beat.", "annotation_id": "611062", "url": "https://genius.com/611062", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I Get Paper", "line": "Just show me who’s the hottest I’mma knock ’em outta place Call me ”Homicide Drizzy”, I’m about to kill your ego I’m about my green, puffin’ goodie like I’m Cee-Lo", "vault_lines": ["I'mma show off, like I'm supposed to", "Just show me who's the hottest I'mma knock 'em outta place", "Call me \"Homicide Drizzy\", I'm about to kill your ego", "I'm about my green, puffin' goodie like I'm Cee-Lo"], "meaning": "Drake will exceed anyone who thinks they are the best, he will make the best feel like shit.\n\nThe weed that Drizzy smokes is “goodie,” or high quality. Cee-Lo is in the Atlanta-based hip-hop group Goodie Mob.", "annotation_id": "148226", "url": "https://genius.com/148226", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "I Get Paper", "line": "Chillin even if I’m in a goose down coat I can’t see you through a Mosley Tribes", "vault_lines": ["I can't see you through a Mosley Tribes"], "meaning": "Don’t be fooled – Canada-Goose down coats pack quite the heat, so either Drake is lying or he’s somewhere real cold.\n\nThat’s a literal interpretation however. Drake’s really saying he’s comfortable in his clothing and that the tint of his luxurious sunglasses makes it difficult to see a hater.\n\nLittle did you know…\n\nThat coat you see down there was made by Drake in collaboration with Canada Goose.", "annotation_id": "148193", "url": "https://genius.com/148193", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DIE TRYING", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Partynextdoor-drake-and-yebba-die-trying-lyrics", "genius_title": "DIE TRYING", "genius_id": 11473558, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DIE TRYING", "line": "I got no dog left in the fight The bark don’t match the bite", "vault_lines": ["I got no dog left in the fight", "The bark don't match the bite"], "meaning": "Drake uses two idioms here to describe his current mental state. Both involve dogs, which has been heavily featured in Drake’s music and persona since the release of his eight studio album “For All The Dogs”.\n\nWhen someone no longer has a dog in the fight, it means the person no longer has a personal stake or interest in the resolution of the situation.\n\nThe bark don’t match the bite is a reverse of the traditional idiom “the bark is worse than the bite”. Here, Drake uses this reversed idiom to signify he may talk a big game and appear to be threatening but he may lack the actual power to do so. These two sayings combine to paint a picture that Drake feels despondent and like he is giving up on this relationship, as a result of the the bleak picture painted in the previous lines.", "annotation_id": "37017037", "url": "https://genius.com/37017037", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DIE TRYING", "line": "I can’t vent to you through no text message and reply I need to see you face to face Gotta look you in the eye, I do", "vault_lines": ["I can't vent to you through no text message and reply", "I need to see you face to face", "Gotta look you in the eye, I do"], "meaning": "Drake is looking to be vulnerable and share his feelings and frustrations with his girl, or “vent” to her. However, he feels like he would be unable to do so through texts, and instead needs to do so in person, where he can feel more comfortable having this conversation as the two make eye contact. This possibly is because of the risk texts can be screenshotted and shared especially for someone with Drake’s fame, and also because deep conversations are more comfortably had in an in-person setting.\n\nDrake has frequently commented on his need for “face to face” interactions, especially in the context of the difficulties of long distance relationships. In I get lonely he says:\n\nFace to face, just you and me with no rules. Just like you, I get lonely too\n\nAnd in his verse in Work by Rihanna he says:\n\nWe just need a face-to-face. You could pick the time and the place.\nYou spent some time away", "annotation_id": "37017008", "url": "https://genius.com/37017008", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DIE TRYING", "line": "Me and my old man, we just get fucked up every night He said, “Son, these hoes just don’t love you” I said, “I’ll keep that in mind” Those ain’t words to live by, wouldn’t call that sound advice Our future doesn’t sound too bright But I just nod and say, “You’re right,” I do", "vault_lines": ["Me and my old man, we just get fucked up every night", "He said, \"Son, these hoes just don't love you\"", "I said, \"I'll keep that in mind\"", "Those ain't words to live by, wouldn't call that sound advice", "Our future doesn't sound too bright", "But I just nod and say, \"You're right,\" I do"], "meaning": "Drake rekindled his relationship with his father after years of estrangement around 2013. Here, he describes a relationship where they now get intoxicated together every night and talk.\nDrake receives advice from his father to be cautious around women, warning him that the woman may like Drake for his fortune and fame but ultimately do not care about him. Even though Drake does not think this advice is sound, he still nods and agrees with his father.\nDrake has previously described his father’s advice as less than helpful, like in 4PM in Calabasas when he says:\n\n“Mike never tried to rap like Pac, Pac never tried to sing like Mike”\nThose my dad’s words to me when I asked him how to make it in life\nAnd I always said my mother gave the greatest advice, yeah\n\nThere is also a thematic connection to Drake’s feature on Lil Wayne’s 2014 song Believe Me where he raps on the hook:\n\nYeah, got some game from my dad\nHe said she might say she love me\nShe don’t love me like she say she love me", "annotation_id": "36986963", "url": "https://genius.com/36986963", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DIE TRYING", "line": "Just to see the silver lines", "vault_lines": ["Just to see the silver lines"], "meaning": "This line alludes to the proverb “every cloud has a silver lining.”\n\nAfter reflecting on the loss of friends, Drake expresses a wish that they were still here to witness the brighter, more hopeful moments (“silver lines”) that life can eventually bring.", "annotation_id": "35463970", "url": "https://genius.com/35463970", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DIE TRYING", "line": "Girl, I miss my friends, you know too much of them have died I wish that they were still alive", "vault_lines": ["Girl, I miss my friends, you know too much of them have died", "I wish that they were still alive"], "meaning": "Drake has experienced the loss of several friends over the years. Notably:\n\n\nAnthony “Fif” Soares: A close associate of Drake and member of the OVO crew, Soares was tragically shot and killed in Toronto in 2017.\nDolla : The rapper, known for his single “Who the F* Is That?”, was fatally shot in Los Angeles in 2009. Drake paid tribute to him during a performance shortly after his passing.\nSmoke Dawg: A Toronto rapper and friend of Drake, Smoke Dawg was killed in a shooting in 2018. [Drake honored him on social media, expressing his condolences]\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/bf1385608e58030058075d38c6c1d0ff62ef9b9c3ec66bf3d586dc4f2be1c47f%2Ftbv0ekfemac\nAnthony “Hush” Palman: A longtime friend and collaborator, Palman passed away in 2020. He was instrumental in Drake’s early career, co-writing several songs.\n\n\n\nThese losses have profoundly impacted Drake, influencing his music and personal life.", "annotation_id": "34649224", "url": "https://genius.com/34649224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "DIE TRYING", "line": "This year, me and God, we wasn’t seein’ eye to eye I prayed to her from time to time She was busy on another vibe", "vault_lines": ["This year, me and God, we wasn't seein' eye to eye", "I prayed to her from time to time", "She was busy on another vibe"], "meaning": "Drake seems to envision God as a woman here, which could align with his constant romanticisation of love despite its challenges.\n\nIt is the third person in the song with whom Drake shares a mutual misunderstanding and wishes to rectify his relationship.\n\nThe passage also alludes to Drake’s attitude toward 2024, a year with tremendous implications for Drake’s image and popularity.", "annotation_id": "34567939", "url": "https://genius.com/34567939", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
