{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "I know what you really like (What you like)", "vault_lines": ["I know what you really like (What you like)"], "meaning": "The way he says “I know what you really like” in the second verse sounds like Party is teasing/taunting the girl with “what she really likes.”\n\nOffering or displaying something desirable just out of reach to entice, control, or tease someone.\n\n“I know what you really li-i-ke”", "annotation_id": "40887743", "url": "https://genius.com/40887743", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "Pour me a shotty, let it flow through my body Flow through my body, flow through my body", "vault_lines": ["Pour me a shotty, let it flow through my body", "Flow through my body, flow through my body"], "meaning": "Drake is saying he will drink all the problems away and “let it flow through my body”.", "annotation_id": "39157498", "url": "https://genius.com/39157498", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "Is this what you want? Is this what you want? Is this what you want? Is this what you want? Is this what you want?", "vault_lines": ["Is this what you want?", "What do you want? Tell it to me", "Is this what you want? Is this what you want?"], "meaning": "Drake is basically saying that “Are you really interested in me as a person, or do you just want the fame, lifestyle, and benefits that come with being with me?”", "annotation_id": "39157487", "url": "https://genius.com/39157487", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "Got you talking so tough while I’m calling your bluff", "vault_lines": ["Got you talking so tough while I'm calling your bluff"], "meaning": "The girl Drake is talking to is flirting by being a little mean and bossy towards him, but Drake knows she’s doing it in an effort to get his attention.", "annotation_id": "36860118", "url": "https://genius.com/36860118", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "Life is good between your thighs (Between your thighs)", "vault_lines": ["Life is good between your thighs (Between your thighs)"], "meaning": "“Life is Good” is also a 2020 Drake/Future song. Here, it contrasts domestic sexual satisfaction with chaotic public life.", "annotation_id": "35371190", "url": "https://genius.com/35371190", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "We’re not like them, baby, and they’re not like us, either one", "vault_lines": ["We'rе not like them, baby, and they're not like us, either one"], "meaning": "Kendrick’s chorus in “Not Like Us” goes “they not like us,” targeting Drake and his circle.\n\nDrake flips the phrase here, separating both himself and Kendrick from “them,” possibly to diminish Kendrick’s claim of authenticity or similarity — a strategic rhetorical inversion.\n\nThe delivery is slick — passive-aggressive and ambiguous enough to let listeners decide.", "annotation_id": "35371163", "url": "https://genius.com/35371163", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "The concept of you and I (I) Take it one night at a time (A time) Heart, body, and your mind (Oh, ooh) I love that we’re intertwined And I learned from mistakes I made with girls that made it worse", "vault_lines": ["The concept of you and I (I)", "Take it one night at a time (A time)", "Heart, body, and your mind (Oh, ooh)", "I love that we're intertwined", "And I learned from mistakes I made with girls that made it worse"], "meaning": "Partynextdoor’s past relationships with women have taught him that connections develop better without the pressure of a relationship looming.\n\nHe tells the woman he is sleeping or “intertwined” with to take their relationship one night at a time, or in other words, to rid herself of long-term expectations.", "annotation_id": "35056650", "url": "https://genius.com/35056650", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "Fuck, I guess it’s up and it’s stuck", "vault_lines": ["Fuck, I guess it's up and it's stuck"], "meaning": "“If it’s up, then it’s stuck” (or “up and stuck”) is a Southern U.S. slang phrase that means a situation has occurred and it is there to stay/won’t go away on it’s own.\n\nThis phrase was also recently popularized by the 2021 song “Up” by Cardi B.", "annotation_id": "34744468", "url": "https://genius.com/34744468", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "Raj keeps on bringing that bottle and topping you up (Showtime)", "vault_lines": ["Raj keeps on bringing that bottle and topping you up (Showtime)"], "meaning": "Raj, also known as ovorajuju on Instagram\nis Sureshkumar Subramaniam who is Drake’s assistant and housekeeper.", "annotation_id": "34567509", "url": "https://genius.com/34567509", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "CELIBACY", "line": "Audemus is all in our cup", "vault_lines": ["Audemus is all in our cup"], "meaning": "Audemus Spirits is a micro-distillery based in Cognac, France, renowned for its innovative and unconventional approach to spirit making. Founded by Miko Abouaf and Ian Spink in 2013, the distillery combines traditional French distillation techniques with a modern, avant-garde spirit.", "annotation_id": "34565053", "url": "https://genius.com/34565053", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "status": "done", "targets": 24, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Future-used-to-this-lyrics", "genius_title": "Used to This", "genius_id": 2859956, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "(Zaytoven)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "I was at home and that was just one beat amongst many. You know most of the day I sit down and make beats. I might make 10 at a time, 5 at a time, and that was just one of those beats and I was just making beats for Future since Future was calling me everyday like, “Zay I need some more beats, send me some beats, I’m going to the studio.” At this time it’s like every beat I’m making is strictly for Future, I ain’t send it to nobody else; these beats are only going to Future. So like I said that was just one of those. […] I went to the studio later and he played the song for me that night when I got there. You know how we are, we don’t think that much on one song, its just like “Oooh that song hard, that joints hard let’s just keep doing some more songs.” That’s how we work. We just record, record, record.\n\n– Hotnewhiphop", "annotation_id": "13724028", "url": "https://genius.com/13724028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "I give you my whole heart ‘til it ain’t nothin’ to give (Future)", "vault_lines": ["I give you my whole heart 'til it ain't nothin' to give (Future)"], "meaning": "Future may be referring to his Ex, Ciara, who is now with NFL Superstar Russell Wilson. In a lot of Future’s past songs, he refers to her an abundant amount of times.", "annotation_id": "11485702", "url": "https://genius.com/11485702", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "You know how far we came if you know where we been (Freebandz)", "vault_lines": ["You know how far we came if you know where we been"], "meaning": "Freebandz is Future’s record label which is distributed by Epic Records.", "annotation_id": "11298080", "url": "https://genius.com/11298080", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Stack of booty bitches, I got used to this", "vault_lines": ["Dirty, dirty money, I got used to this (Gang)", "Stack of booty bitches, I got used to this"], "meaning": "Future’s fame has led him to treat women like objects. Not only does he reduce women to their mere physical components—“booty,” in this case—he treats them as objects to be collected, as with the “stack” of women he has, reminiscent of a stack of dollar bills. This callous disregard clearly reflects his power and fame, but also the way in which he is actively dehumanizing others for his own pleasure.", "annotation_id": "10910266", "url": "https://genius.com/10910266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "I had to put my back against the wall (And what?)", "vault_lines": ["I had to put my back against the wall (And what?)", "Thought they had my back against the wall"], "meaning": "To have your “back against the wall” means to have the odds stacked against you. It implies difficult and unfavorable circumstances. To then choose to fight back means you will need to beat the odds to succeed.", "annotation_id": "10818920", "url": "https://genius.com/10818920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Eyes gettin’ low but I’m still observin’, I see you lurkin’", "vault_lines": ["Eyes gettin' low but I'm still observin', I see you lurkin'"], "meaning": "Drake’s hunger to stay on top of the game is evident in these lines. He refuses to be complacent, and while he may be “used to this,” he’s keeping his eyes on those coming up to try and take his title. As the “eyes getting low” image invokes, Drake may be tired, but he ain’t sleeping, so don’t sleep on Drake.", "annotation_id": "10810477", "url": "https://genius.com/10810477", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "No one looks surprised ’cause we used to this I’ma make sure that we get used to this", "vault_lines": ["No one looks surprised 'cause we used to this", "I'ma make sure that we get used to this"], "meaning": "Ever since the release of his Grammy-award winning album Take Care, Drake has continued to build on his accomplishments, being able to share a luxurious lifestyle with those around him.\n\nDrake knows that having a high standard of living is typical for his crew, something he doesn’t find astonishing.", "annotation_id": "10788479", "url": "https://genius.com/10788479", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Drop-top Porsches, I’m so used to this (Yeah) Smokin’ out the pound, I’m so used to this (I swear) I know where I’m from, but I got used to this (Ayy) Mansion in the hills, I got used to this (Future)", "vault_lines": ["Dirty, dirty money, I got used to this (Gang)", "Drop-top Porsches, I'm so used to this (Yeah)", "Smokin' out the pound, I'm so used to this (I swear)", "I know where I'm from, but I got used to this (Ayy)", "Mansion in the hills, I got used to this (Future)"], "meaning": "Future’s career is a rags to riches story, from his troubled childhood in the streets of Atlanta to a house in Los Angeles with then-fiancee Ciara. He’s since moved back to Atlanta, purchasing a $2 million home in a gated community.", "annotation_id": "10783592", "url": "https://genius.com/10783592", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Treat my brother’s kids like they one of my kids", "vault_lines": ["Treat my brother's kids like they one of my kids"], "meaning": "Drake is likely referring to the children of his close friend Niko. He called Niko his brother in the thank you note for Thank Me Later:\n\nTo my brother Niko, our friendship is what keeps me alive and focused. I always thought I was an only child but my mother lied!\n\nIn the music video for “Used to This,” Drake briefly reveals a tattoo of some names on his shoulder, most likely of Niko’s children. Although we cannot be certain these are the kids, if they are, it shows Drake is committed to them.\n\nhttps://giphy.com/gifs/72Zsox91oxQFG", "annotation_id": "10783520", "url": "https://genius.com/10783520", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Money on your head if you make a nigga nervous", "vault_lines": ["Money on your head if you make a nigga nervous"], "meaning": "“Money on your head” means there is a bounty on your life, so killers are incentivized to kill you to receive the money—Drake takes on the persona of a contractor who might put up such a bounty on the life of anyone who threatens him.", "annotation_id": "10782649", "url": "https://genius.com/10782649", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Never see me out in person, I’m always workin’", "vault_lines": ["Never see me out in person, I'm always workin'"], "meaning": "With four albums, two commerical mixtapes, two mixtapes, collaboration projects with Future and Kanye West, an EP with Gucci Mane, and the More Life playlist all releasing in the span of seven years, Drake doesn’t have time to be out in public.", "annotation_id": "10782605", "url": "https://genius.com/10782605", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Selling dope all my life, I can’t do minimum wage (Nah) Dirty, dirty money, I got used to this (Gang)", "vault_lines": ["This ice flooded my wrist, I done got used to this", "Selling dope all my life, I can't do minimum wage (Nah)", "Dirty, dirty money, I got used to this (Gang)"], "meaning": "“Dirty money” is money gained through illicit or criminal means. Future told VLADTV he “was born a hustler,” and his backpack was full of the proceeds from hustling. The dope he sells now is his music and the experience of his live shows.\n\nA June 1998 study (published in November 2016) estimated that low-level drug dealers earn less than the minimum wage in America, which is $7.25/hour.", "annotation_id": "10779910", "url": "https://genius.com/10779910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Be honest to yourself, don’t you never pretend (Tell ’em) Don’t ever play yourself, know when it all begins (You know)", "vault_lines": ["Be honest to yourself, don't you never pretend (Tell 'em)", "Don't ever play yourself, know when it all begins (You know)"], "meaning": "“Don’t play yourself” is one of DJ Khaled’s famous sayings, which saw a meteoric rise in popularity in late 2015 to 2016.\n\nSpending almost every waking hour at the studio ensures Future doesn’t play himself by taking his success for granted.", "annotation_id": "10778699", "url": "https://genius.com/10778699", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Beat the odds, do numbers and remain humble (Future)", "vault_lines": ["Beat the odds, do numbers and remain humble (Future)"], "meaning": "Future grew up “a hustler.” During an interview with Clique, he explained how grateful he is to beat the odds, to achieve fame and success despite a difficult upbringing.\n\nIn 2015, Future’s DS2 sold 151K copies in the first week, eventually being certified platinum. His collaborative mixtape with Drake, What A Time To Be Alive, also went platinum. He’s managed three chart-topping albums as well, further solidifying his numbers.", "annotation_id": "10778169", "url": "https://genius.com/10778169", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Lambo come alive, man, I’m used to this", "vault_lines": ["Lambo come alive, man, I'm used to this"], "meaning": "In early June 2016, Drake bought a new Grigio Titans 2016 Lamborghini Aventador LP 700-4 Roadster, which has a price tag of more than $500,000.\n\nWhile an expensive luxury vehicle is something normal people would stunt in, Drake isn’t normal; he’s only driven in it 5 times.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BFzxurbDQDv/", "annotation_id": "10778137", "url": "https://genius.com/10778137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Get the llama, I party with the real Madonna (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Get the llama, I party with the real Madonna (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Drake and Madonna have appeared on stage together in honor of Drake’s song of the same name. It’s not known if Future’s met the pop star, but he name-checked her in the third verse of his track “Codeine Crazy.”\n\n“Llama” is a slang term for a gun deriving from the Spanish arms company of the same name.\n\nFuture has one foot in the streets by carrying a gun, and the other in the world of the rich and famous.", "annotation_id": "10778123", "url": "https://genius.com/10778123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Never made a move out here unless I was certain", "vault_lines": ["Never made a move out here unless I was certain"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t let a track leave the studio unless he’s certain it’s the best he can do and knows it’ll make waves once it’s dropped. In his Views interview with Zane Lowe, he said:\n\n[Any song on Views] could’ve been the best song on the album, [but] if we don’t feel that way, we move on.\n\nIn the book The 48 Laws of Power, a popular book with celebrities that Drake previously mentioned on “What I’m Thinkin' Right Now,” Law 28 reads:\n\nIf you are unsure of a course of action, do not attempt it.", "annotation_id": "10778093", "url": "https://genius.com/10778093", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "This ice flooded my wrist, I done got used to this", "vault_lines": ["This ice flooded my wrist, I done got used to this", "Dirty, dirty money, I got used to this (Gang)"], "meaning": "“Icy” is a term rappers commonly use to compare the shine of their jewelry to the reflective sheen of ice. Future takes it a step further and brags that his jewelry gleams as much as an entire land of ice.\n\nIronically, Iceland may not have been the most accurate choice; though it’s implied by the name, Iceland isn’t the iciest country around. Iceland has cold winters, but it also has relatively warm summers, reaching temperatures as high as 26°C (78.8°F). Greenland is actually much icier.", "annotation_id": "10777894", "url": "https://genius.com/10777894", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Tatted on me, but this shit is deeper than the surface", "vault_lines": ["Tatted on me, but this shit is deeper than the surface"], "meaning": "While Drake has numerous tattoos, this line references the matching shark tattoos he got with Rihanna. There is a figurative story behind every tattoo, but “deeper than the surface” invokes this particular tattoo, as sharks literally swim below the surface of the ocean. Drake also gestured to the shark tattoo while rapping this line in the video.", "annotation_id": "10777606", "url": "https://genius.com/10777606", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "New persona, we movin’ on from Ferragamo (I’m movin’ on)", "vault_lines": ["New persona, we movin' on from Ferragamo (I'm movin' on)"], "meaning": "Salvatore Ferragamo is an Italian fashion brand. On 2013’s “Finessin,” Future claimed everything he wears in public is Ferragamo.\n\n\n\n Future (left) sporting a Ferragamo belt", "annotation_id": "10777535", "url": "https://genius.com/10777535", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Never lookin’ back on it, we did what we did Could never find the time for the people I miss Thought they had my back against the wall Tell me that I don’t deserve to ball Well, then tell me who deserve it", "vault_lines": ["I had to put my back against the wall (And what?)", "Tell me that I don't deserve to ball", "Never lookin' back on it, we did what we did", "Could never find the time for the people I miss", "Thought they had my back against the wall", "Well, then tell me who deserve it"], "meaning": "Drake’s claims his work ethic affords him the luxury of balling, and any friendships he lost due to his long hours in the studio were unavoidable.\n\nOne of these people might be Nicki Minaj, who Drake was once close but has since drifted away from, likely due to his dispute with Meek Mill.", "annotation_id": "10777449", "url": "https://genius.com/10777449", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "I’m with everyone that I was here with in the first place Makin’ sure that they all good before they close the curtain", "vault_lines": ["I'm with everyone that I was here with in the first place", "Makin' sure that they all good before they close the curtain"], "meaning": "Just like he proclaimed on “No New Friends,” Drake foregoes new relationships in favor of the people he’s always been closest to. Some of these people include Future, who first collaborated with Drake in 2011, Lil Wayne, who signed Drake to Young Money in 2009, and PARTYNEXTDOOR, who was the first artist signed to OVO.\n\nWith his estimated $60 million net worth, Drake can afford to help out the crew before he finishes rapping, or “closes the curtain.” After pondering who he’ll be “once it’s all over” on “Heat Of The Moment,” he finally has an answer.", "annotation_id": "10777421", "url": "https://genius.com/10777421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Mexicano, I’m workin’ like I’m Mexicano", "vault_lines": ["Mexicano, I'm workin' like I'm Mexicano"], "meaning": "Mexicans work longer hours than the citizens of any other country in the OECD. Future’s work ethic is comparable—as of this song’s release, he has dropped 14 projects since 2012. He also embarked on the Summer Sixteen Tour with Drake, performing 60 shows between June and November 2016.\n\nFuture also grew up in Lil Mexico, aka Kirkwood Atlanta. He’s been repping the neighborhood for a while now.", "annotation_id": "10777306", "url": "https://genius.com/10777306", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Used to This", "line": "Tropicana bitches, I got all kinda flavors", "vault_lines": ["Tropicana bitches, I got all kinda flavors"], "meaning": "Future compares the different races of the women he has relations with to Tropicana’s vast catalog of fruit juice flavors.", "annotation_id": "10777130", "url": "https://genius.com/10777130", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "status": "done", "targets": 35, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Bun-b-all-night-long-og-mix-lyrics", "genius_title": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "genius_id": 114010, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "You should come with me If you got some time this is where you wanna be We can go all night long This can last all night long Bet it’s goin’ down, we gon make it right This is what they mean when they talk about the life We can go all night long This can last all night long I’m with if you with it", "vault_lines": ["You should come with me", "If you got some time this is where you wanna be", "This can last all night", "We can go all night long", "This can last all night long", "Bet it's goin' down, we gon make it right", "This is what they mean when they talk about the life", "I'm with if you with it"], "meaning": "On “Miss Me,” the final version of the song, Drake used a different chorus:\n\nYeah, girl, it’s on\nYou know what it is when I finally make it home\nI just hope that you miss me a little when I’m gone\nYeah I just hope that you miss me a little when I’m gone\nAnd you just tell me what you down for, anything you down for\nI know things have changed, know I used to be around more\nBut you should miss me a little when I’m gone\nI just hope that you miss me a little when I’m gone, gone, gone", "annotation_id": "18533455", "url": "https://genius.com/18533455", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Soo-woo if you bangin’, motherfucker!", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Soo-woo” is a call used by members of the Bloods to express affiliation and to greet each other. It’s been claimed that Wayne had ties to the Bloods since he was a teenager, during his Hot Boyz days. While he hasn’t validated that claim, he has used the call on numerous songs, including June 2008’s “Lollipop (Remix)”:\n\nI do it for Bloods' sake, soo-woo, think it’s voodoo\n\nAnd June 2008’s “Mr Carter”:\n\nNow all my Bloods scream “Soo-woo” and “Da-da-doe”", "annotation_id": "18533386", "url": "https://genius.com/18533386", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Weezy F. Baby and the F is for Front Do’ ’Cause that’s where I brang it", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "‘Weezy F Baby’ is one of Wayne’s various monikers that he has coined throughout his rap career. He’s been using the name as far back as 2004—on “Da Drought,” he raps:\n\nWeezy F Baby, F ya ladies so quick so fast\n\nIn February 2018, Genius broke down the numerous times Wayne has used the nickname:\n\nhttps://www.dailymotion.com/video/x6f74nr", "annotation_id": "18533385", "url": "https://genius.com/18533385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "I’m stickin’ to the script like lint on denim", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lint is the tiny bits of fabric fibers that are usually stuck to clothing. Wayne plays on the word “sticking\" as “sticking to the script” means to be legit. This correlates with the last two lines above which talk about Wayne’s prison stint at Rikers Island.", "annotation_id": "18533379", "url": "https://genius.com/18533379", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Ha ha, damn! I’ll be gone ’til November But fuck it, I ain’t trippin’, I know Drizzy gon’ kill ’em", "vault_lines": ["Ha ha, damn! I'll be gone 'til November"], "meaning": "On October 22, 2009, Lil Wayne pleaded guilty to attempted criminal possession of a weapon, which stems from a 2007 incident when police found .40 caliber pistol in his possession. After two postponements, on March 8, 2010, Wayne was sentenced to one year in prison, which he served at Rikers Island. On November 4, 2010, after serving eight months of his one-year sentence, Wayne was released.\n\nIn April 2010, during Lil Wayne’s incarceration, his friends created a website called Weezy Thanx You, which published letters written by Wayne while he was in prison. The first letter was titled “Gone ‘til November,” in which Wayne was said he thought about his children every day, spent his time regularly working out regularly and read the Bible on a daily basis.", "annotation_id": "18533373", "url": "https://genius.com/18533373", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "And when I’m in the booth, bitch, the lion is loose", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Young Lion is one of Lil Wayne’s nicknames. He also called himself Young Lion on his June 2007 song, “Zoo”:\n\nSee I’m the young lion and he the young bull\n\nDrake also mentioned the nickname, as well as his own, Young Angel, on Wayne’s November 2008 song, “Stuntin”:\n\nYoung Angel, Young Lion, I’m done tryin'\n\nAnd again on his February 2009 song, “Ignant Shit”:\n\nYoung Angel and Young Lion, you know what it is\n\nThis line also concludes the “lying/lion” wordplay Wayne used in the last two lines.", "annotation_id": "18533371", "url": "https://genius.com/18533371", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Turn you to a vegetable like you lyin’ in soup", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "When someone in a “vegetable” state, they are no longer able to function physically or mentally. On Gudda Gudda’s 2009 song, “Demolition Freestyle Part 2,” Wayne similarily raps:\n\nTell your boyfriend I’ll turn him to a vegetable", "annotation_id": "18533365", "url": "https://genius.com/18533365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "I ain’t lyin’, I shoot, you don’t need signs for proof", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In October 2009, Lil Wayne pleaded guilty to attempted criminal possession of a weapon and in March 2010, he was sentenced to one year in prison, which he served at Rikers Island.", "annotation_id": "18533364", "url": "https://genius.com/18533364", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "It’s Blood gang, slime, but I parlay with Snoop", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Parlay” means to hang out—Lil Wayne is a Blood while Snoop Dogg is a Crip. Although the Bloods and Crips are rivals, Wayne hangs out with Snoop regardless. They have also collaborated on four different occasions: OutKast’s August 2006 song, “Hollywood Divorce,” The Game’s November 2006 song, “It’s Okay (One Blood) [Remix],” David Banner’s July 2007 song, “Speaker,” and Wayne’s September 2018 song, “Dope Niggaz.”\n\n“Slime” is an acronym for “street life intelligence, money [is] everything.” It originated from Harlem, New York.", "annotation_id": "18533360", "url": "https://genius.com/18533360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "It’s only me and her ’cause the Bugatti a coupe", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A “coupe” is a passenger car with a fixed roof and Bugatti is a French supercar manufacturer. In 2010, Lil Wayne became the first rapper to buy the Bugatti Veyron, which cost him $1.8 million.\n\nThe Veyron is one of the fastest cars in the world, clocking a top speed of 254.04 MPH and is one of the rarest with Wayne’s Bugatti being one of 450 units manufactured as of 2018.", "annotation_id": "18533358", "url": "https://genius.com/18533358", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Uh, I’m a love machine And I won’t work for nobody but you", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "These lines are taken from The Miracles‘ 1975 hit, “Love Machine” where the lead vocalist Billy Griffin said the same thing. The overall song is about a lover being compared to an electronic device.\n\nThe song is The Miracles' best selling single—it peaked at number one on the Billboard Hot 100, sold over 4.5 million copies, and generated over $15 million in revenue.", "annotation_id": "18533352", "url": "https://genius.com/18533352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "No, I’m not that thuggish, not that ruggish but I do pack Bone", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a reference to Bone Thugs-N-Harmony’s April 1994 song, “Thuggish Ruggish Bone.\" However, “bone,” in this context, refers to Wayne’s genitalia.\n\nThis may also be another line paying homage to New Orleans rapper Mystikal, who rapped a nearly identical line on his June 1994 song, “Y’all Ain’t Ready Yet”:\n\nI’m not that Thug and I’m not that Ruggish but I do pack Bone", "annotation_id": "18533349", "url": "https://genius.com/18533349", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Young Money’s Jerry Sloan, I turn every stone", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jerry Sloan was a long-time NBA coach for the Utah Jazz who, aside from being a Hall Of Fame player, is known for coaching one team longer than anyone in NBA history. He coached the Jazz for 23 seasons until he resigned in 2011. He was the fifth coach in NBA history to reach 1,000 wins and is one of two coaches in NBA history to earn 1,000 wins with one team.\n\nOn the other hand, Young Money is Lil Wayne’s record label that he found in 2005.", "annotation_id": "18533347", "url": "https://genius.com/18533347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Beat the pussy up, call me Larry Holmes", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Larry Holmes is a former Ameican professional heavyweight boxer. He is regarded as one of the greatest heavyweights of all time—he held the WBC heavyweight title from 1978 to 1983, the lineal heavyweight titles from 1980 to 1985, and the IBF heavyweight title from 1983 to 1985. He retired in 2002 with a record of 69 wins and 6 losses and was inducted into the International Boxing Hall of Fame in 2008.", "annotation_id": "18533343", "url": "https://genius.com/18533343", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Voice baritone, haters carry on", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A baritone voice is the most common male voice and is the second-lowest adult male singing voice. It falls in between the higher tenor and lower bass registers. An example of a male singer with a baritone voice is a male opera singer.", "annotation_id": "18533342", "url": "https://genius.com/18533342", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "James Bond cologne, honey I put on", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A February 2019 article by GQ Magazine broke down the type of fragrance fictional spy, James Bond wore since it’s never explicitly explained in the novels and movies. According to the article:\n\nBond never endorses a specific Floris fragrance, but he clearly approves of the brand. That’s no accident. [The author of James Bond novels, Ian Fleming] wore Floris No. 89 Eau de Toilette. This is arguably, therefore, the closest you will get to a ‘Bond fragrance’.", "annotation_id": "18533339", "url": "https://genius.com/18533339", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Yes, I am Weezy but I ain’t asthmatic", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Weezy” is a nickname Lil Wayne has been using for a long time and notes he’s not wheezing, a primary symptom of asthma. While it’s not known whether Wayne has asthma or not, a 2009 Rolling Stone article noted that Wayne used an asthma inhaler while recording a song.", "annotation_id": "18533338", "url": "https://genius.com/18533338", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Man, I swear, my bitches do it ’til they suck the brown off Ew, that’s nasty", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This may be a nod to the Family Guy and The Cleveland Show character, Cleveland Brown. “That’s nasty” is one of the signature phrases he says from time to time on the shows.", "annotation_id": "18533335", "url": "https://genius.com/18533335", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "’Bout to scoop ya girl up like a ground ball", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In baseball, a “ground-ball play” is when the ball is hit on the ground and rolls or bounces into the mitt of a member of the opposing team. Wayne uses this analogy figurately for taking someone’s girl.", "annotation_id": "18533333", "url": "https://genius.com/18533333", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Gettin’ money every day, I’m a groundhog", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is an allusion to the critically acclaimed 1993 American comedy film, Groundhog Day, which stars Bill Murray as Phil Connors. In the movie, Phil is caught in a time-loop, reliving the same day over and over repeatedly. Wayne uses the same concept for getting money repeatedly every day.\n\nNotably, there’s also a scene in the movie where Phil steals a bag of money from an unguarded armored car.", "annotation_id": "18533331", "url": "https://genius.com/18533331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Kickin’ bitches out the condo like Pam", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Pam is a character from the popular 90s sitcom, Martin. Pam was Gina’s (Martin’s girlfriend) best friend. She often visited their apartment and in some cases, got kicked out. In the same way, Wayne kick girls out of his house after sex.", "annotation_id": "18533330", "url": "https://genius.com/18533330", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Ooh, shit, motherfucker, God damn", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Wayne may be paying homage to New Orleans rapper Mystikal, who opened his verse on Snoop Dogg’s 1999’s song, “Ghetto Symphony,” with the same exact line.", "annotation_id": "18533327", "url": "https://genius.com/18533327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "I love Nicki Minaj, I told her I’d admit it I hope one day we get married just to say we fuckin’ did it And girl, I’m fuckin’ serious, I’m wit’ it if you wit’ it ‘Cause yo’ verses turn me on and yo’ pants are mighty fitted", "vault_lines": ["I'm with if you with it"], "meaning": "In August 2010, Drake and Nicki Minaj both announced on Twitter that they were married:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/nickiminaj/status/22298785237?lang=en\n\nWhile this was likely a publicity stunt, Drake revealed two months earlier in a June 2010 interview with MTV News that he always had a crush on her:\n\nI’ve always really, actually, really had a crush on her, always really loved her, and she’s always just looked at me as, like, her little brother.\n\nHe later rapped about marrying Nicki again on her December 2010 song, “Moment 4 Life”:\n\nFuck it, me and Nicki Nick gettin' married today", "annotation_id": "18533298", "url": "https://genius.com/18533298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "I’m just feelin’ sorry for whoever gotta sweep up Yeah, bills everywhere, trill everything", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake depicts the scene of a strip club after a big night with money everywhere on the floor.\n\nThe word “trill” is a blend of the words true and real. Bun B—who made an appearance in the song earlier—notably has five albums with the word “trill” in it: 2005’s Trill, 2008’s II Trill, 2010’s Trill OG, 2013’s Trill OG: The Epilogue and 2018’s Return of the Trill.", "annotation_id": "18533293", "url": "https://genius.com/18533293", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "That’s why me and Lil Jaz ’bout to spaz, can you keep up?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Lil Jaz” is Jas Prince, son of Houston rap executive James Prince. He was the one who introduced Lil Wayne to Drake—he told The Fader in 2015:\n\nI was really cool with Lil Wayne, and I brought Drake to his attention […] I put on ‘Replacement Girl’ and look over and see that he’s bopping his head, and I’m like Okay! […] I played Wayne another song called ‘Brand New,’ and I’m like, ‘He sings, too.’ Wayne is like, ‘Hold up, man, this dude is really fucking dope.’ He’s like, ‘Where is he at?’ I told him in Canada, and Wayne was like, ‘Let’s fly him here right now.’ We flew him into Houston. Drake and I had been talking a lot—I was really blowing him up, we was always texting—but that was my first time actually meeting him. When I brought him to Wayne, Wayne was nonchalant. […] Eventually Wayne was like, ‘I’m about to go to Atlanta tonight, why don’t y'all ride with me on the bus?’ […] When we got there, we went to the studio. That’s when Drake first recorded the original ‘Forever’ and ‘Stunt Hard.’ A couple of weeks later that got leaked, and that was the beginning of Drake.", "annotation_id": "18533289", "url": "https://genius.com/18533289", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "’Cause to her I’m just a rapper and soon she’ll have met another", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne would later reuse this line on Drake’s November 2011 song, “The Real Her.” The song, along with Wayne, notably features André 3000 and is on Drake’s follow-up album to Thank Me Later, Take Care.", "annotation_id": "18533280", "url": "https://genius.com/18533280", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Call up King of Diamonds and tell Chyna it’d be worth the flight I’ll be at my table, stackin’ dollars to the perfect height Work somethin’, twerk somethin’, basis", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "King of Diamonds is a popular strip club and Blac Chyna is a former stripper. She began stripping at the King of Diamonds in Miami in 2010 to help pay for her business classes at Johnson and Wales University before transiting to modeling shortly afterward.\n\n\n\nThe line “Work somethin', twerk somethin', basis” is taken from UGK’s November 2001 song, “Take It Off,” where Pimp C raps:\n\nIt’s onna, work somethin, twerk somethin basis", "annotation_id": "18533279", "url": "https://genius.com/18533279", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Someone tell Maliah I’m on fire, she should work tonight", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Maliah Michel is an American adult performer, hip-hop model, and stripper from Houston. At the time, she and Drake were dating.\n\nDrake first met her at a Houston-based strip club and flew her to Jamaica a few months later to star in the official video for “Find Your Love” as the lead dancer. “I’m on fire” comes from the fact that the URL of Maliah’s MySpace account is maliahsonfire.\n\nDrake also gave her a shoutout on his February 2009 song, “November 18th”:\n\nJust met a girl, say she from the H-Town", "annotation_id": "18533276", "url": "https://genius.com/18533276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Yeah, I’m in the city of the purple Sprite", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Purple Sprite” is lean—a mixture of promethazine codeine and soda. Lean originated in Houston in the 1960s when blues musicians would mix Robitussin with alcohol. Houston legends like DJ Screw and Pimp C all contributed to the popularity of lean, as well as Three 6 Mafia’s February 2000 hit “Sippin’ on Some Syrup,” which was a song dedicated to lean.\n\n“Miss Me” was also notably recorded in Houston. In a June 2010 interview with Complex, producer Noah “40” Shebib said:\n\nWe did it in January or February. We started working on that in Houston.", "annotation_id": "18533273", "url": "https://genius.com/18533273", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Neeks got the weed, Hush got a gun CJ got my credit cards and a lot of ones", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Neeks (Niko)—one of Drake’s longtime friends from high school—and Hush are both members of Drake’s OVO crew and CJ is his road manager. Drake gave Niko along with other OVO members a shoutout on his November 2011 song, “The Motto”:\n\nShout goes out to Niko, J and Chubbs, shouts to Gibbo", "annotation_id": "18533270", "url": "https://genius.com/18533270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "Drake, you got ’em, right? Yeah, I got ’em, Bun", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“All Night Long” would later become Drake’s June 2010 song “Miss Me.” In a June 2009 interview with Complex, Bun B explained that his vocals in this line were kept in the final version because it was already in Drake’s verse:\n\n[The part where I ask, ‘Drake, you got ’em right?’] was already in the song because we wrote it together for my album so that was already in his verse. He wrote it like that for me to say that line right there. Then, when he did the verse with Wayne, they just kept me in there.", "annotation_id": "18533247", "url": "https://genius.com/18533247", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "I will have a model wife, yo’ bitch is as hot as ice", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Models are a common topic in Drake’s music—on his September 2007 song, “Closer,” he raps:\n\nTo be the one and only nigga that you ain’t gon' see\nIn the club with a model spillin' drinks on me\n\nOn his November 2011 song, “Lord Knows,” he also raps:\n\nTalking strippers and models that try to gain attention\n\n“Hot” means attractive. Drake plays on “hot” by making a comparison to ice—which is cold—essentially meaning this other girl is unattractive.", "annotation_id": "18533244", "url": "https://genius.com/18533244", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "I’m ‘bout my paper like a motherfuckin’ scratch-and-win", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Scratch-and-win cards are cards that people can scratch off numbers and potentially win large amounts of money.\n\nDrake’s certainly focused on his money—as of 2019, he is worth about $150 million, according to Forbes. He also told them in 2013:\n\nEvery year, we just want to get more prepared and better at touring and better at things that make money.", "annotation_id": "18533239", "url": "https://genius.com/18533239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "I said, “Tell me what’s really goin’ on” Drizzy back up in this thing, I’m ready, what’s hannenin’?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is interpolating lyrics from the first verse of Soulja Boy’s August 2007 song, “What’s Hannenin'”:\n\nTell me what’s really going on\nSoulja Boy up in this thing, I’m ready\nWhat’s hannenin'?\n\nThese aren’t Soulja’s original lyrics however—he interpolated those lines from Shawty Lo’s chorus on D4L’s November 2005 song, “I’m Da Man”:\n\nI don’t know what y'all been told\nShawty Lo back on the map, what’s up\nThat’s what’s hannenin'\n\nDrake’s interpolation re-entered the public consciousness in January 2019 when Soulja Boy appeared on Power 105.1 FM’s The Breakfast Club and cited it as evidence of Drake copying his flow.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/Kp5A_1UAJog?t=2950", "annotation_id": "18533235", "url": "https://genius.com/18533235", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All Night Long (OG Mix)", "line": "When the head boy banging, it’s like the Funky Drummer drumming", "vault_lines": ["When the head boy banging, it's like the Funky Drummer drumming"], "meaning": "“Funky Drummer” is a legendary song in terms of hip-hop sampling.\n\nThe late James Brown’s track has what is considered the most popularly sampled drum beat.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8L4gITE3nUc", "annotation_id": "1472247", "url": "https://genius.com/1472247", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
