{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guns Allowed", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Snoop-lion-no-guns-allowed-lyrics", "genius_title": "No Guns Allowed", "genius_id": 131653, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guns Allowed", "line": "We all feel hurt,", "vault_lines": ["We all feel hurt,"], "meaning": "Senseless violence hurts all of us as a people and a nation due to the tragedies that occur.", "annotation_id": "1610517", "url": "https://genius.com/1610517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guns Allowed", "line": "I know that somebody died, somebody’s child", "vault_lines": ["I know that somebody died, somebody's child"], "meaning": "In the summer of 2012, two youths were fatally shot in the city of Toronto, Canada (Drake’s hometown).\n\n\n\nThe two deaths had a strong impact on the surrounding community, police, government forces and of course, the two victim’s parents.\nBelow is a photo of Shannon Longshaw, who organized the barbecue where the shooting took place. She was devastated that things took a turn for the worst, resulting in the fatalities.", "annotation_id": "1600237", "url": "https://genius.com/1600237", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guns Allowed", "line": "’Cause no guns are allowed in here tonight", "vault_lines": ["No guns are allowed in here tonight", "'Cause no guns are allowed in here tonight"], "meaning": "After having had a very weapon affiliated career, including songs like Gangbang Rookie and Murder Was The Case, Snoop has come to a point in his life where he must not only make choices for himself, but for those around him (his family).\n\n\n\nWith a song like “No Guns Allowed”, Snoop expresses his desire for gun-control in countries like the United States, as the set laws in play have been doing more sufficient damage to the world rather than helping protect people. Hear Snoop’s thoughts on gun-control below:\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CMLXiNiX-MU", "annotation_id": "1600189", "url": "https://genius.com/1600189", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guns Allowed", "line": "Two more young names to be carved out of stone", "vault_lines": ["Two more young names to be carved out of stone"], "meaning": "Drake’s verse is a dedication to Shyanne Charles and Joshua Yasay, two recent shooting victims in Drizzy’s hometown of Toronto; Drake’s referenced them before. Since their tragic deaths, their names will now have to be carved into tombstones.\n\nhttp://twitter.com/Drake/status/225358554138284032", "annotation_id": "1599929", "url": "https://genius.com/1599929", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guns Allowed", "line": "Got my dawg on the phone Cryin’ and sayin’ to leave him alone, but I’m not leavin’ his side", "vault_lines": ["Got my dawg on the phone", "Cryin' and sayin' to leave him alone, but I'm not leavin' his side"], "meaning": "The “dawg” here is likely P Reign, a rapper in Toronto affiliated with Drake.  Reign was so affected by the deaths that he made a song in tribute to them the next day", "annotation_id": "1599656", "url": "https://genius.com/1599656", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Guns Allowed", "line": "Dedicate this to Shyanne and Josh And pour sumthin’ out for the lives that they stole 416", "vault_lines": ["Dedicate this to Shyanne and Josh", "And pour sumthin' out for the lives that they stole"], "meaning": "Drake is pouring out something, for the lives they (the killers) stole. In this case the lives that were stolen were Shyanne Charles, 14, and Joshua Yasay, 23, who were killed after a shootout happened at a Scarborough block party on July 16th, 2012. Drake is close to the Yasay family and the shooting was one of the worst shootings in Toronto history with 23 others wounded\n\n\n\nPhoto credit: Anil H Photos\n\nToronto residents have come together in hopes of spreading a positive message to the community to stop violence (gun violence specifically). Another example is P. Reign’s “Angels” song and video http://youtu.be/66Pltlb_Ovw\n\n\nThe 416 at the bottom refers to Toronto’s main area code.", "annotation_id": "1599450", "url": "https://genius.com/1599450", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "status": "done", "targets": 15, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/The-weeknd-live-for-lyrics", "genius_title": "Live For", "genius_id": 206721, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "They tryna make me slow down Tryna tell me how to live, I’m ’bout to lose control (Lose control, oh) Well, they can watch me fuck it up all in one night", "vault_lines": ["They tryna make me slow down", "Tryna tell me how to live, I'm 'bout to lose control (Lose control, oh)", "Well, they can watch me fuck it up all in one night"], "meaning": "People are pushing The Weeknd to sober up, he takes this as people trying to forcibly change his lifestyle.\n\nHe can’t get sober and even though he tried (even one day was hard) he can’t last and he’s not going to let his progress continue. He states his intentions to go on a bender and ditch his newfound, short-lived sobriety.", "annotation_id": "26550241", "url": "https://genius.com/26550241", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "Me and my niggas, we ain’t never going broke", "vault_lines": ["Me and my niggas, we ain't never going broke"], "meaning": "A lot of this record features The Weeknd’s detachment from his beloved home city and his first lover, but this record channels something he’s quite proud of: the fact that he is still with his day one friends and the “XO Crew” that have been with him for the longest time.", "annotation_id": "24626655", "url": "https://genius.com/24626655", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "I’m in my city in the summer, camo’d out, leather booted", "vault_lines": ["I'm in my city in the summer, camo'd out, leather booted"], "meaning": "The Weeknd marches to the beat of his own drum; he has no problem sacrificing comfort for style, wearing winter clothes during Toronto’s warm-weather months. As informed by the previous lines, this decision is likely induced by his drug and alcohol consumption and a desire to stand out.\n\nThe Weeknd shared a similar sentiment on Future’s December 2015 track, “Low Life”:\n\nIf they try to stunt me, I go all out military\nI’m camo'ed all out, like I’m in the military\n\nThe Weeknd is notably a fan of A Bathing Ape, a famous Japanese clothing brand known for its camouflage print. In 2019, The Weeknd teamed up with the brand for an exclusive collaboration featuring multiple different camo items.", "annotation_id": "18606880", "url": "https://genius.com/18606880", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "How long do you think it’s gon’ take for y’all to fall? That’s a serious question, ’cause I seriously question all of y’all", "vault_lines": ["How long do you think it's gon' take for y'all to fall?", "That's a serious question, 'cause I seriously question all of y'all"], "meaning": "Since the release of his 2009 mixtape So Far Gone, Drake’s unprecedented run has included record breaking statistics and previously impossible accomplishments, such as six BillBoard number one hits and a diamond track. As one of the most influential artists of all time, Drake attracts a large number of detractors who unsuccessfully attempt to find his successor.\n\nIn comparison, most rappers experience unsustainable success and fall off after a short period of time. In a May 2016 article, Genius contributor Insanul Ahmed broke down the relatively short shelf live of rappers and the “five year windows” that most endure. Drake’s prime has long surpassed the typical number for his peers and he’s witnessed many rappers rise and fall from fame during this time.", "annotation_id": "18579408", "url": "https://genius.com/18579408", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "Yeah This the shit that I live for, this the shit that I’d die for", "vault_lines": ["This the shit that I live for, this the shit that I live for", "This the shit that I live for, with the people I'd die for", "This the shit that I live for, this the shit that I'd die for"], "meaning": "Drake interpolates The Weeknd’s chorus here:\n\nThis the shit that I live for, this the shit that I live for\nThis the shit that I live for, with the people I’d die for\n\nIn this instance, Drake also shows his devotion to the rap game and the important aspects of his life: sexual intercourse, monetary gains, and fame. Drake’s unwilling to give up his spot in hip hop and will risk dying over maintaining his fame and fortune.\n\nDrake would later rap a similar line on his April 2016 track, “9”:\n\nI made a decision last night, I’d die for it\nJust to show the city what it takes to be alive for it", "annotation_id": "18575582", "url": "https://genius.com/18575582", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "This the shit that I live for, this the shit that I live for This the shit that I live for, with the people I’d die for This the shit that I live for, this the shit that I live for This the shit that I live for, with the people I’d die for This the shit that I live for, this the shit that I live for This the shit that I live for, with the people I’d die for This the shit that I live for, this the shit that I live for This the shit that I live for, with the people I’d die for", "vault_lines": ["This the shit that I live for, this the shit that I live for", "This the shit that I live for, with the people I'd die for", "This the shit that I live for, this the shit that I'd die for"], "meaning": "The Weeknd affirms his devotion to the rap game and those who supported him along the way to the top. Abel would do anything for his XO crew, who remained loyal to him throughout his career. This also includes close friend Drake, who is featured on this track.\n\nHe later reemphasizes his passion for the life he lives on NAV’s March 2019 track, “Price on My Head”:\n\nI heard these niggas want to take my life\nI don’t apologize, I would die for this shit", "annotation_id": "18572632", "url": "https://genius.com/18572632", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": ", nigga, Champagne", "vault_lines": ["There's some shit I need to work on, but I know you see me working, nigga, Champagne"], "meaning": "One of Drake’s nicknames is Champagnepapi—it’s his Instagram name.\n\nChampagne symbolizes success and wealth. Drizzy has achieved worldwide fame and is making millions, so you know you’ll catch him popping bottles.\n\n\n\nWhen he says “I know you see me working” he is referring to the pictures he has been posting of him sleeping in the studio working on his third album, Nothing Was the Same. While some people may simply see him as someone who spends most of his time partying and drinking, don’t fall into that trap, you know?\n\nHe’s been dropping this coda a lot lately.", "annotation_id": "2116790", "url": "https://genius.com/2116790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "I’m not tryna stunt, I’m just tellin’ you the truth, I swear", "vault_lines": ["I'm not tryna stunt, I'm just tellin' you the truth, I swear"], "meaning": "Even if it’s something that he really shouldn’t share with the public (see the lines above) he’s still trying to be as honest as he can through his music.\n\nHe’s also saying that he’s not trying to put on a false persona that is often seen in hip-hop. But then again, this is coming from the same Heartbreak Drake that might catch a body.\n\n“Stunt” here is used as a play on words, saying he doesnt want to show off for others “for the sake of my career”, as stunts are usually considered very dangerous and involve much risk.\n\n\n\nSee how stunts are dangerous?", "annotation_id": "2116749", "url": "https://genius.com/2116749", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "She just offered a strip tease, but she don’t look like Demi Moore", "vault_lines": ["She just offered a strip tease, but she don't look like Demi Moore"], "meaning": "Thank God! Demi Moore is an old hag these days, and is certainly the opposite of Drake’s 45/24 requirements.\n\nThis line is a very dated play on the 1996 film Striptease, which actress Demi Moore starred in. You’d think Drake could’ve picked a more contemporary reference…\n\n\n\nIt’s likely the girl offering Drake a striptease is actually hotter than Demi Moore, because Drake often tells of his love for strippers and strip clubs.\n\nIn “Club Paradise” he says:\n\nNow I’m that guy that know them strippers by their real names\n\nIt is obvious that going to strip clubs is a hobby of his. The last time Drake was featured on a song by The Weeknd, he also spoke about strippers.\n\nHe’s also generous to the strippers, so they probably like him too:\n\nThrowing thousands in the strip club with Drizzy\nDifference is I’m throwin four, he throwing fifty", "annotation_id": "2116657", "url": "https://genius.com/2116657", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "I still got plaques hanging wall to wall, dawg", "vault_lines": ["I still got plaques hanging wall to wall, dawg"], "meaning": "Drake has been winning awards ranging from a Grammy for Best Rap Album (Take Care) to 4 Juno awards and 6 BET Awards. He also has albums and singles achieving platinum and gold status.", "annotation_id": "2116629", "url": "https://genius.com/2116629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "And you have to do it all Just to know where it gets you", "vault_lines": ["And you have to do it all", "Just to know where it gets you"], "meaning": "Here he could be talking to either other guys trying to imitate his image and lifestyle, or to groupies who want to ride with him.\n\nFor the groupies, they might have to take it off or meet his boys, guys might have to go through a different kind of initiation to prove that they actualliy live for the XO lifestyle, or he could be saying that in order to know where all paths lead in life you gotta do it all. Hence why he lives such a reckless lifestyle.\n\nIt also wouldn’t be surprising if he was talking about his own drug usage. This line could be saying that you need to take every drug in order to have the most amazing experiences (Abel is known to take a lot of drugs among other things).", "annotation_id": "2116623", "url": "https://genius.com/2116623", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "Kissing bitches in the club They wanna threesome (Threesome), then some (Then some)", "vault_lines": [", kissing bitches in the club", "They wanna threesome (Threesome), then some (Then some)"], "meaning": "The Weeknd is also known for his sexually active lifestyle, among other things, so it’s natural that he’d be getting groupies to have sex with him in a club or after a show.\n\nAlso, the tagline of “and then some” is both a play on the shared suffix of threesome and some but also to show that since he’s become famous women have been wanting more from him, much like Drake on Marvin’s Room. It can also show that he’s sleeping with more than three girls.", "annotation_id": "2116582", "url": "https://genius.com/2116582", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "Roll up in that thing, got hoes like Prince, but they know I’m king", "vault_lines": ["Roll up in that thing, got hoes like Prince, but they know I'm king"], "meaning": "This simile compares him to the legendary musician Prince and isn’t the first time Drake has done so. But he clarifies that if we’re talking about the hereditary title in the nobility of rap, he’s the king!\n\n\n\nPrince is an odd reference for Drake to make, since his influence is a lot more felt in alternative hip-hop, with artists like Andre 3000 and Janelle Monae taking considerable inspiration from him.", "annotation_id": "2116575", "url": "https://genius.com/2116575", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "Still scream, “XO!” when that Henny pour", "vault_lines": ["And it's all love in the city, still scream, \"XO!\" when that Henny pour"], "meaning": "Double entendre –\n\n1) Drake gives a shout out to The Weeknd’s label – eradicating the many rumors of a feud between OVO and XO.\n\n2) Hennessy is a popular cognac among rappers. XO is used to describe the aging of the cognac and stands for Extra Old (the most expensive).", "annotation_id": "2116571", "url": "https://genius.com/2116571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live For", "line": "Getting sober for a day got me feeling too low (Oh)", "vault_lines": ["Getting sober for a day got me feeling too low (Oh)"], "meaning": "The Weeknd is known for being high, or faded all the time, so much so that it’s started to affect his health and his doctor has warned him.\n\nWith low being the opposite of high and the Weeknd being a consistent drug user, or addict, he may be suffering from withdrawal symptoms, making him feel down, or low.\n\nHe can’t escape his daily problems if he is sober.\nHe also noted on “Love In the Sky” that he has self confidence problems; thus, being sober wouldn’t allow him to escape that reality.\n\nBut I’m always getting high\n‘Cause my confidence low", "annotation_id": "2116550", "url": "https://genius.com/2116550", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Outta Time", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Bryson-tiller-outta-time-lyrics", "genius_title": "Outta Time", "genius_id": 6010051, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Outta Time", "line": "Still play into your palm", "vault_lines": ["Still play into your palm"], "meaning": "Drake uses a variation of a popular idiom “Playing into someone’s hands”.\n\nTo play into someone’s hands means to do something that one does not realize will hurt oneself and help someone else.\n\nDefinition from https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/play%20into%20%28someone%27s%29%20hands#:~:text=idiom,oneself%20and%20help%20someone%20else", "annotation_id": "30866935", "url": "https://genius.com/30866935", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
