{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-to-the-max-lyrics", "genius_title": "To the Max", "genius_id": 3110340, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "If they pop somebody’s chain, then we gotta get it back No discussion to be had, we ain’t goin’ out like that Man, we lit", "vault_lines": ["If they pop somebody's chain, then we gotta get it back", "No discussion to be had, we ain't goin' out like that", "(Back it up, back it up", "Back it up, back it up, back it up)"], "meaning": "Drake owns a wide range of expensive chains. During his Summer Sixteen tour with Future, a thief by the name of Travion King allegedly stole a box of $3 million worth jewelry belonging to Drake’s DJ, Future the Prince. However, the police caught the thief the next day and managed to recover all the jewelry.", "annotation_id": "11970440", "url": "https://genius.com/11970440", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "My young niggas All of my young niggas, we lit My young niggas All of my young niggas, we lit My young niggas All of my young niggas, we lit My young niggas All of my young niggas, we lit", "vault_lines": ["All of my young niggas, we lit"], "meaning": "The chorus samples 1WayFrank’s “Lit,” which features J $tash. The song is sped up to keep up with the uptempo nature of “To The Max.”\n\nhttps://soundcloud.com/newerapromotions/1wayfrank-ft-j-tash-lit", "annotation_id": "11970250", "url": "https://genius.com/11970250", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "Made a knot in the line You can never break ties", "vault_lines": ["Made a knot in the line", "You can never break ties"], "meaning": "In this line ‘breaking ties’ is both literal and metaphorical. If you tie a knot that is strong enough, you wont be able to undo that knot. Also, breaking ties means to separate yourself from a person or a group, something that you cannot do if you are close to Drake.", "annotation_id": "11966613", "url": "https://genius.com/11966613", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "I talk gray, I don’t keep it white and black Only say “I love you” just so I can hear it back Sometimes, other times I’d love to have you back", "vault_lines": ["(Back it up, back it up", "Back it up, back it up, back it up)", "I talk gray, I don't keep it white and black", "Only say \"I love you\" just so I can hear it back", "Sometimes, other times I'd love to have you back"], "meaning": "“Black and white” represents keeping it simple and operating in absolutes, while “grey” represents flexibility. Drake understands that life is complicated and there isn’t always a definitive solution.\n\nDrizzy takes it a step further, though, conceding that he uses this grey area to be emotionally manipulative and self-serving. This isn’t anything new for Drake, though—he’s commented on selfishness in his lyrics from the start. On his 2011 song “Good Ones Go (Interlude),” he openly acknowledged he was egocentric:\n\nI shouldn’t be much longer but you shouldn’t have to wait\nCan’t lose you, can’t help it, I’m so sorry, I’m so selfish\n\nAnd on 2016’s “Child’s Play,” he admitted he lied to a woman he was seeing to keep her from getting upset:\n\nHow many girls have slept in this bed?\nSay a different number than the one that’s in my head\nLying to protect you, I be doing that a lot", "annotation_id": "11966359", "url": "https://genius.com/11966359", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "(Oh, ooh, get ’em right Get ’em right, get ’em right Get ’em right, get ’em right Get ’em right, get ’em right What you finna do, Gus?)", "vault_lines": ["(Oh, ooh, get 'em right", "Get 'em right, get 'em right", "What you finna do, Gus?)"], "meaning": "This is a sped up sample of Jay-O’s “Gus Get Em Right,” which appears on the intro, refrain, and outro. The song became an internet sensation during the Vine era, starting the “#fuckitup” challenge:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6AnkxgVkBCI\n\n“Gus Get Em Right” samples T2’s 2007 song “Heartbroken,” which can be heard later in this song during the bridge.", "annotation_id": "11966239", "url": "https://genius.com/11966239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "Now all of a sudden, people say we cousins I keep hearin’ all this “You my brother” talk, I can’t relate to nothin’", "vault_lines": ["Now all of a sudden, people say we cousins", "I keep hearin' all this \"You my brother\" talk, I can't relate to nothin'"], "meaning": "This appears to be a restatement of a similar complaint from Drake’s British friend and Boy Better Know label-mate, Skepta. On his 2016 single “Man,” Skepta rapped:\n\nI don’t know why man’s callin' me family all of a sudden\nLike hmm, my mum don’t know your mum\nStop telling man you’re my cousin\n\n“I can’t relate” has two meanings—he doesn’t understand this person’s perspective, and he doesn’t biologically “relate” to anybody claiming they’re his “brother” or “cousin.”", "annotation_id": "11966147", "url": "https://genius.com/11966147", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "Can’t think of a night that we ain’t turn up to the max To the maximum Like the album just went platinum, yeah Turnt up to the max", "vault_lines": ["Like the album just went platinum, yeah", "Turnt up to the max", "Can't think of a night that we ain't turn up to the max"], "meaning": "Drake and his OVO Crew are known for their parties. The OVO boss has  been known to make it rain in the club, and on 2016’s “U With Me?,” he bragged about his pool parties:\n\nI know you heard that my pool parties like Mardi Gras\n\n“To the max” means “the most possible.” Even though More Life wasn’t certified platinum by the RIAA as of this song’s release, Drake’s still partying like it has—and More Life reportedly reached platinum sales numbers in April 2017 (1,000,000 units sold). Platinum certification isn’t a foreign concept to Drake, though, since all of his other albums went platinum.", "annotation_id": "11965982", "url": "https://genius.com/11965982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "Got it on the dresser, I’ve been under pressure They wanna see me laid out on a stretcher Gotta keep my head up", "vault_lines": ["(Back it up, back it up", "Back it up, back it up, back it up)", "Got it on the dresser, I've been under pressure", "They wanna see me laid out on a stretcher", "Gotta keep my head up"], "meaning": "The first line is an homage to Nas' 1994 classic, “Represent.” In the first verse, Esco raps:\n\nPulling a TEC out the dresser; police got me under pressure\n\nMedical professionals use stretchers to carry injured people. Athletes are commonly carried off the field on stretchers from an injury sustained during the game. Similarly, Drake knows the rap game is coming for his head.\n\nDrake has rapped about being targeted by powerful people in the industry since 2015’s If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late. On “No Tellin,” Drizzy hinted at the possibility of a conspiracy against him, and on “30 for 30 Freestyle,” he rapped:\n\nWhen the higher-ups have all come together as a collective\nWith conspiracies to end my run and send me a message\n\nHe continued on “4PM in Calabasas” and 2017’s “Madiba Riddim,” which shares a similar sentiment to “30 for 30 Freestyle”:\n\nDevil’s working overtime\nVoodoo spells put on my life\nIt won’t work, they all have tried", "annotation_id": "11965554", "url": "https://genius.com/11965554", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "Oh, ’fessions to, I do, I, I do", "vault_lines": ["Oh, 'fessions to, I do, I, I do", "Oh, oh, 'fessions to, I do, I, I too"], "meaning": "This is sampled from the bridge of U.K. producer T2’s 2007 song “Heartbroken,” which features Jodie Aysha. Its use here is the another example of Drake’s appreciation for U.K. culture that was so prominent on his March 2017 album More Life.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WTsHWzvJWDQ", "annotation_id": "11965537", "url": "https://genius.com/11965537", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "More chune pon your head top", "vault_lines": ["More chune pon your head top"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Drake’s acceptance speech after winning “Favorite Artist Award” at the 2016 American Music Awards show.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/AMAs/status/800532136876871680\n\n“Chune” and “Headtop” are both Jamaican Patois for tunes and head, respectfully. The saying means pay respects so you don’t face the music after.\n\nThese lines were used on Drake’s “Free Smoke,” “Jorja Interlude,” and “Fake Love.”", "annotation_id": "11965523", "url": "https://genius.com/11965523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "To the Max", "line": "Another one", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Another One” is one of DJ Khaled’s signature slogans, which he commonly says in the intros to his songs. It appeared on two of his 2017 singles prior to “To The Max”—“I’m the One” and “Shining”—as well as multiple songs from Khaled’s ninth studio album, Major Key.\n\n“We The Best” is DJ Khaled’s record label.", "annotation_id": "11965514", "url": "https://genius.com/11965514", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
