{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OMW", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This entire verse is likely about Kim Kardashian, as @sashaalexey points out in a below suggestion. Their explanation in that suggestion is as follows:\n\nDrake’s verse contains multiple Kim Kardashian references: “Liz Taylor diamonds” nods to Kim’s famous jewelry collection and her purchase of Taylor’s pieces at auction. His Hidden Hills mansion sits minutes from Kim’s Calabasas home, making “Can you come home with me tonight” geographically significant. “I know you got the kids at the house” directly references her four children, while “Pay the nanny double and come roll with the owl” acknowledges her staff and invites her to Drake’s nearby OVO-branded residence. Their close proximity makes his promise “You’ll be back in time to make ‘em breakfast” entirely feasible, adding tension to this seemingly casual invitation.\n\nIn addition, there’s the line about ex turning his frown upside down that clearly is a jab at Kanye and his mental health struggles.", "annotation_id": "37540007", "url": "https://genius.com/37540007", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OMW", "line": "Your mind and your body is outta control", "vault_lines": ["Your mind and your body is outta control", "Hey, your mind and your body is outta control"], "meaning": "Presuming this song is indeed about Kim Kardashian:\nFor her brain, she completed her law office study program, and passed the “baby bar” (the first-year law students' exam) and the MPRE on her path to becoming a lawyer. She still needs to pass the California bar exam to become a licensed attorney.\nAs for her body, she has long been seen as one of the most attractive and desirable women in pop culture for several years.", "annotation_id": "37525875", "url": "https://genius.com/37525875", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OMW", "line": "If I brought your birthday, you turnin’ it up", "vault_lines": ["If I brought your birthday, you turnin' it up"], "meaning": "Party is throwing out the idea of buying a table/section for this woman for her birthday, potentially at LIV Miami.", "annotation_id": "36860268", "url": "https://genius.com/36860268", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OMW", "line": "Your ex needs to turn that frown upside down Smile and keep it pushin’ before it goes south", "vault_lines": ["Your ex needs to turn that frown upside down", "Smile and keep it pushin' before it goes south"], "meaning": "By advising, “Your ex needs to turn that frown upside down / Smile and keep it pushin’ before it goes south,” Drake suggests that it’s best to let go of past grievances and move forward to avoid further complications. This reflects a mature perspective on handling past relationships and the importance of not dwelling on what could have been.", "annotation_id": "35278168", "url": "https://genius.com/35278168", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OMW", "line": "My best friend, my shawty Oh, woah Hey, your mind and your body is outta control The fuck was they thinking? You on a roll You hit the splits and get naked, girl, you’re on a roll You payin’ the rent these days and the groceries, woah Luckily, I got rich after that Luckily, you still my bitch after that", "vault_lines": ["The fuck was they thinking? You on a roll", "You hit the splits and get naked, girl, you're on a roll", "You payin' the rent these days and the groceries, woah", "Luckily, I got rich after that", "Luckily, you still my bitch after that", "Your mind and your body is outta control", "The fuck was they thinking? You vulnerable, yeah (Okay)", "My best friend, my shawty", "Hey, your mind and your body is outta control"], "meaning": "Party’s girl played mad clutch when he was down in the trenches", "annotation_id": "35038696", "url": "https://genius.com/35038696", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OMW", "line": "Luckily, you went platinum like plaques", "vault_lines": ["Luckily, you went platinum like plaques"], "meaning": "Likely a metaphor comparing someone’s success or status to platinum music plaques (awards given when an album or song sells over a million copies).\n\nIn this context, it could mean:\n- The person became highly successful, like a platinum-selling artist.\n- The person gained value, prestige, or recognition in some way.\n-  It could also refer to jewelry (platinum being a precious metal), implying luxury or wealth.", "annotation_id": "34649304", "url": "https://genius.com/34649304", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OMW", "line": "Can you come home with me tonight, are you allowed? I know you got the kids at the house Pay the nanny double and come roll with the owl You’ll be back in time to make ’em breakfast, no doubt Just stay the night with me, get nasty", "vault_lines": ["You and I know, I know you deserve it", "Can you come home with me tonight, are you allowed?", "I know you got the kids at the house", "Pay the nanny double and come roll with the owl", "You'll be back in time to make 'em breakfast, no doubt", "Just stay the night with me, get nasty"], "meaning": "Drake serves up a sly double meaning here, layering personal intimacy with symbolic branding. On one level, the lyrics speak to how his sexual escapades involve staying up late and being nocturnal with Drake. But beneath this seduction lies another meaning, a deeper flex. Drake nods to the unmistakable symbol of OVO, suggesting that this nocturnal animal is crucial to Drake’s brand, empire, and mythology. The brilliance of the line is in its seamless ambiguity. It plays to the sensual—the promise of the night—and the mythic, the branding that extends far beyond the song.", "annotation_id": "34638936", "url": "https://genius.com/34638936", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "OMW", "line": "You got them Liz Taylor diamonds and a heart of gold", "vault_lines": ["You got them Liz Taylor diamonds and a heart of gold"], "meaning": "Elizabeth Rosemond Taylor (1932-2011) was a famous British actor who played in the famous movie Cleopatra (1963). She was also known for her expensive jewelry collection. One of these diamonds was the Krupp Diamond, which is now known as the Elizabeth Taylor Diamond. It is a 68 carat diamond with an estimated worth of over $9 million us dollars.\n\nKim K has previously bought Elizabeth Taylor jewelry at auction.", "annotation_id": "34564508", "url": "https://genius.com/34564508", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SOMEBODY LOVES ME", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Partynextdoor-and-drake-somebody-loves-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "SOMEBODY LOVES ME", "genius_id": 11469868, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SOMEBODY LOVES ME", "line": "She said her bestie wanna get you home and swap I said, “I’m shy, baby, and that’s doin’ a lot”", "vault_lines": ["She said her bestie wanna get you home and swap", "I said, \"I'm shy, baby, and that's doin' a lot\"", "And that's all I need"], "meaning": "Here, the woman lets Drake know that her best friend wants to get home and have sex with him, possibly the three of them together. Drake is hesitant to go along with the plan, saying it may be doing too much, and he is shy.\nInterestingly, in the Cash Cobain remixed part two version of this song, Drake’s response to this request is different when he says:\n\n“Well, this time, I might have to—”\nI said, “Well, this time, I might be a thot”\nI can’t tell y'all, “Stop”", "annotation_id": "37017138", "url": "https://genius.com/37017138", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SOMEBODY LOVES ME", "line": "I’m tryna do more than bust down your watch", "vault_lines": ["I'm tryna do more than bust down your watch"], "meaning": "A “bust down watch” is a luxury timepiece that has been modified with diamonds or other precious gemstones, which are drilled and set into the watch’s dial and bracelet. A bustdown is a customization process, and can be applied to any of the luxury watch brands such as Rolex, Audemars Piguet, or Patek Philippe. Drake has mentioned bust downs in several other songs, including most recently on the song “CRYING IN CHANEL” from this same album.\n\nI could tell you love your prized possessions, what’s your favorite?\nBustdown watches, Cuban links\nVan Cleef necklace, Carti' bracelets\n\nHere, Drake shares a desire to pursue romance with the woman,  rather than just spoil her with her lavish gifts (do more than bust down your watch).", "annotation_id": "37017108", "url": "https://genius.com/37017108", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SOMEBODY LOVES ME", "line": "We smokin’ broccoli, but she smellin’ Baccarat", "vault_lines": ["We smokin' broccoli, but she smellin' Baccarat"], "meaning": "Broccoli is slang for weed, or marijuana.\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/e2866a7e49a5dc59c636e493b00a0b9ca388f8f0a3dc296018ab877e7c518cf3%2Frb4j9lm94ha\n\nBaccarat Rouge 540 is a premium perfume .\nhttps://filepicker-images.genius.com/e2866a7e49a5dc59c636e493b00a0b9ca388f8f0a3dc296018ab877e7c518cf3%2F3vbpfpqj2kw", "annotation_id": "35439254", "url": "https://genius.com/35439254", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SOMEBODY LOVES ME", "line": "I’m holding y’all hands real, real, real, real tight when I say this ‘cause I don’t feel like fightin’ But please quit callin’ them lil’ one-week breaks celibacy You’re not celibate You’re not celibate You’re not celibate", "vault_lines": ["I'm holding y'all hands real, real, real, real tight when I say this 'cause I don't feel like fightin'", "But please quit callin' them lil' one-week breaks celibacy"], "meaning": "This outro is a transition into the next track of the album, “CELIBACY”.\n\nThe outro is voiced by Destiny Nowell.", "annotation_id": "34561835", "url": "https://genius.com/34561835", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WHEN HE’S GONE", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Partynextdoor-and-drake-when-hes-gone-lyrics", "genius_title": "WHEN HE’S GONE", "genius_id": 11473516, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WHEN HE’S GONE", "line": "I don’t wanna talk about money, it gets embarrassin’", "vault_lines": ["I don't wanna talk about money, it gets embarrassin'"], "meaning": "Drake’s signature tension between having everything and still feeling uncomfortable talking about it. It’s like he’s saying: “Yeah, I got it…but if I really laid it all out, it would sound ridiculous even to me.”", "annotation_id": "36151011", "url": "https://genius.com/36151011", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WHEN HE’S GONE", "line": "Hoes gon’ be hoes, that’s why I never had married ’em", "vault_lines": ["Hoes gon' be hoes, that's why I never had married 'em"], "meaning": "Drake appears to justify the fact that he is almost 40 (currently 38) and has not settled down yet. According to societal expectations, he should have long married and settled down. Instead, he is a single parent having had his son Adonis with french artist Sophie Brussaux. However, they are not together, nor is Drake in a long-term relationship with the prospect of settling down.\n\nHe has also addressed this on the song Lasers:\n\n“I’m getting older, pressure’s getting heavy”", "annotation_id": "35029090", "url": "https://genius.com/35029090", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WHEN HE’S GONE", "line": "How you supposed to stay with that nigga in this economy?", "vault_lines": ["How you supposed to stay with that nigga in this economy?"], "meaning": "Drake suggests that the girl cannot stay in a relationship with her current partner because he does not have sufficient financial means to provide for her in an economy that is plagued by inflation and high cost of living. Instead, the girl should turn to Drake, who has a net worth of roughly 250 million USD.\n\nhttps://www.celebritynetworth.com/richest-celebrities/richest-rappers/drake-net-worth/", "annotation_id": "35029032", "url": "https://genius.com/35029032", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WHEN HE’S GONE", "line": "Why you actin’ thirsty off anyone that’s American? (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Why you actin' thirsty off anyone that's American? (Yeah)"], "meaning": "This shade on “WHEN HE’S GONE” marks the third time on $ome $exy $ongs 4 U that Drake has urged women he’s courting—or simply knows—to rethink their love for men from the States.\n\nIn “RAINING IN HOUSTON,” he takes aim at how they speak to women:\n\nRather spend my energy boosting you up\nGassing you up, these American guys\nAre something different, they just talk looser than us\nYou say I talk proper, it suits me, my love\n\nAnd in “GIMME A HUG,” he advised Melyssa Ford to stop hanging with New Jersey native, Joe Budden:\n\nMelyssa Ford, you a legend from the 6ix, hate to see you with a dick-sucker\n\nFor what it’s worth, artists like A$AP Rocky, Future, and Kendrick Lamar have, at various points, embraced the red-white-and-blue iconography of American patriotism, which only reinforces Drake’s point.", "annotation_id": "34638962", "url": "https://genius.com/34638962", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Why", "status": "done", "targets": 7, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rich-homie-quan-tell-me-why-lyrics", "genius_title": "Tell Me Why", "genius_id": 622917, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Why", "line": "I’ve been down, but I’m up right now", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s had moments away from the spotlight, but he’s firmly entrenched in it now. He recycles this line in the hook of “How About Now”:\n\nBut how ‘bout now?\nCause I’m up right now, and you stuck right now", "annotation_id": "7665589", "url": "https://genius.com/7665589", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Why", "line": "Aww yeah, aww yeah, my sound is what up right now", "vault_lines": ["Aww yeah, aww yeah, my sound is what up right now"], "meaning": "As Drake will constantly remind us, he’s the leader of the new school and the hit-maker of this rap generation. He stays killin' it on Billboard and has a smash mixtape to go along with seven successful mainstream albums/mixtapes.", "annotation_id": "4556587", "url": "https://genius.com/4556587", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Why", "line": "Then I say: this that fuckin’ mob shit, this that fuckin’ player shit This that shake a bottle up and bust the top and spray the shit", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "As Drake spits these lines, the listener may find them reminiscent of “Trophies”:\n\nThis a “fuck-a-stripper-on-the-mink-rug” song/This a “fuck-them-boys, forever-hold-a-grudge” song/“Pop-some-fucking-champagne-in-the-tub” song/Nigga, “just-because” song\n\nDrake can rage whenever and however he wants because he’s loaded after dropping banger after banger for the fans. That’s definitely a cause for celebration.", "annotation_id": "4543378", "url": "https://genius.com/4543378", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Why", "line": "(Reps Up, OVO)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "OVO (October’s Very Own) is Drake’s label, while Reps Up Records is a record label in Toronto that has been associated with OVO and most specifically fellow Toronto rapper P Reign.", "annotation_id": "4539658", "url": "https://genius.com/4539658", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Why", "line": "’Bout to go drown in the cup right now, holla", "vault_lines": ["'Bout to go drown in the cup right now, holla"], "meaning": "Since he reps Houston frequently, it’s reasonable to assume Drake’s drinking lean, the city’s famous beverage, which is typically consumed in a styrofoam cup.", "annotation_id": "4537319", "url": "https://genius.com/4537319", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Why", "line": "Started from the bottom like Drake said", "vault_lines": ["Started from the bottom like Drake said"], "meaning": "A reference to the timeless Drake cut, “Started From The Bottom.”\n\nStarted from the bottom, now we here\n\nLike (or unlike?) Drake, Rich Homie started from the bottom. He’s grinded hard to get to this level, making hits such as “Lifestyle” and “Type Of Way.”", "annotation_id": "4537307", "url": "https://genius.com/4537307", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Tell Me Why", "line": "I don’t know what it was about you Like blue you left me without a clue I pictured the fate, is it true? ’Cause I tried to make this shit work But it seems like you was in your feelings You kept them hatin’ bitches all in our business But I just got one question, baby Tell me why?", "vault_lines": ["I pictured the fate, is it true?", "'Cause I tried to make this shit work", "But it seems like you was in your feelings", "You kept them hatin' bitches all in our business", "But I just got one question, baby", "And like you just got traded", "You don't know me, you must mistake me", "Question, baby, tell me why", "One question, baby, tell me why", "I don't know what it was about you", "Like blue you left me without a clue"], "meaning": "Although RHQ tried to make it work, the girl was the problem in the relationship. After letting her friends dictate the relationship, she suddenly left – no wonder why Quan is clueless.\n\nIn the second line, he plays off of Blue’s Clues, a popular children’s show that emphasized using clues to solve problems.", "annotation_id": "4536747", "url": "https://genius.com/4536747", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
