{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fuckin’ Problems (Original)", "line": "I love bad bitches, that’s my fuckin’ problem (Problem) And, yeah, I like to fuck, I got a fuckin’ problem (Problem) I love bad bitches, that’s my fuckin’ problem (Problem) And, yeah, I like to fuck, I got a fuckin’ problem (Problem) I love bad bitches, that’s my fuckin’ problem (Problem) And, yeah, I like to fuck, I got a fuckin’ problem (Problem) If finding somebody real is your fuckin’ problem (Yeah) Bring yo’ girls to the crib, maybe we could solve it", "vault_lines": ["Fuckin' problem, fuck your problems", "Bring yo' girls to the crib, maybe we could solve it, ayy", "If finding somebody real is your fuckin' problem (Yeah)", "Bring yo' girls to the crib, maybe we could solve it"], "meaning": "This version of the chorus features A$AP Rocky rapping the final line, whereas the official track has Drake performing the final two lines. The reasoning behind the change is unclear, although it seems like this was the original, unedited cut.\n\n\n\n2 Chainz utilizes a double entendre for the opening lyrics as the word “fucking” has multiple meanings. One interpretation is that he loves women which is his biggest “problem,” while another meaning is that having sexual intercourse itself is his major problem.\n\nIn an interview with XXL Magazine, Drake elaborated on the chorus:\n\n“Fuckin’ Problems” came from a song me and 2 Chainz had done. He said in his verse—it was really slow too—“I love bad bitches too that’s a fuckin’ problem.” I stopped his verse while we were listening to it, and I said, “Yo, that needs to be a hook. I don’t know how we’re going to explain this to him that we chopped a piece of his verse to do the hook.”", "annotation_id": "17854591", "url": "https://genius.com/17854591", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fuckin’ Problems (Original)", "line": "London, Tasha, Mya, Keisha Courtney, blue-eyed devils with no Visas", "vault_lines": ["London, Tasha, Mya, Keisha", "Courtney, blue-eyed devils with no Visas"], "meaning": "A “visa” is a form of documentation that permits people to get into and out of countries legally. Kendrick’s women are exotic foreigners who lack proper papers and are consequentially labeled as “illegal immigrants.”\n\nIn addition, Visa is a famous credit card company. Kendrick’s women are likely “gold diggers” or women that are focused on money and the pursuit of material possessions.The idea of these money-oriented women has been present in hip hop for years. It’s most notable mention was perhaps in Kanye West’s 2005 track, “Gold Digger.”\n\nKendrick Lamar also references his July 2011 track, “Keisha’s Song (Her Pain),” which was a deep track dedicated to his own sister. The song detailed an impoverished girl who didn’t value her own self worth and became a prostitute, which ultimately led to her own demise.", "annotation_id": "17854157", "url": "https://genius.com/17854157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fuckin’ Problems (Original)", "line": "I remember, being young, dumb Looking for the next thrill Angry adolescents Three things on our minds: Money, murder, or you", "vault_lines": ["I remember, being young, dumb", "Looking for the next thrill", "Three things on our minds: Money, murder, or you"], "meaning": "This opening ad-lib was recorded during the production of Kendrick’s first album, good kid, m.A.A.d. city–and this song was originally intended to be included.\n\nIt may seem like a simple introductory stanza to spark the listener’s attention leading up to the hook; but in fact, there is a much deeper meaning.\n\nFirstly, notice Kendrick is reminiscing about being “young and dumb” just as he did on “Sherane a.k.a. Master Splinter’s Daughter.”\n\nSecondly, he refers to simple-minded “angry adolescents”–which directly ties in to the “m.A.A.d.” concept. Kendrick explicitly informed listeners that this acronym had two meanings:\n\n\nMy Angels on Angel Dust\nMy Angry Adolescence Divided\n\n\n\nAnd thirdly, it is interesting to note (in hindsight) that the exact vices Kendrick warns us about here are mentioned in the outro for DNA.:\n\nSex, money, murder – these are the breaks…\nSex, money, murder – our DNA", "annotation_id": "13611045", "url": "https://genius.com/13611045", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fuckin’ Problems (Original)", "line": "Picture perfect, uh huh And I paint a perfect picture, my intention’s to get richer But I ain’t tryna split the profit with ya", "vault_lines": ["Picture perfect, uh huh", "And I paint a perfect picture, my intention's to get richer", "But I ain't tryna split the profit with ya"], "meaning": "This line is a reference to the opening verse of “2 of Amerikaz Most Wanted” by 2pac, featuring Snoop Dogg—but Kendrick’s interpolation is slightly different. The original goes:\n\nPicture-perfect, I paint a perfect picture\nBomb the hoochies with precision\nMy intention’s to get richer", "annotation_id": "13610919", "url": "https://genius.com/13610919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Fuckin’ Problems (Original)", "line": "Fuckin’ problem, fuck your problems I got my own but one ain’t a dollar", "vault_lines": ["Fuckin' problem, fuck your problems", "I got my own but one ain't a dollar"], "meaning": "A reference to one of the most famous JAY Z lines ever. Unlike Hov, Kendrick doesn’t care about your problems. While Jay never has to worry about pussy, Kendrick never has to worry about money.\n\nBut let’s be real, Jay never has to worry about money either.", "annotation_id": "4098921", "url": "https://genius.com/4098921", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
