{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In My Business", "status": "done", "targets": 7, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Gucci-mane-in-my-business-lyrics", "genius_title": "In My Business", "genius_id": 54906, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In My Business", "line": "I’m All-Star, team Jordan, small forward", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Michael Jordan is a former professional basketball player who played 15 seasons in the National Basketball Association (NBA), and is often regarded as the greatest player in the league’s history. He made fourteen appearances in the NBA All-Star Game.\n\n\n\nJordan with the Chicago Bulls\n\n\n\nIt’s worth noting that Jordan played as a shooting guard for most of his career; he only played as a small forward during his final two NBA seasons with the Washington Wizards.", "annotation_id": "38803859", "url": "https://genius.com/38803859", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In My Business", "line": "Girls are like buses, every fifteen min’, they run", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Gucci calls back to a famous line from his guest appearance on singer Mario’s April 2009 track “Break Up,” where he rapped:\n\nGirls are like buses—miss one, next fifteen, one comin' (Huh)\n\nGucci uses public transport buses as an analogy for women; if you miss a bus, there’s no need to worry, since another one will be coming not too long after.", "annotation_id": "38803849", "url": "https://genius.com/38803849", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In My Business", "line": "Classic line, bitches run like buses, Gucci said it", "vault_lines": ["Classic line, bitches run like buses, Gucci said it"], "meaning": "Garrett references a line that Gucci rapped on singer Mario’s April 2009 song “Break Up,” which Garrett also appeared on:\n\nGirls are like buses—miss one, next fifteen, one comin' (Huh)\n\n\n\nGucci also called back to that line with an earlier bar on this song, rapping:\n\nGirls are like buses, every fifteen min', they run", "annotation_id": "38791283", "url": "https://genius.com/38791283", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In My Business", "line": "I feel like I find a wife exactly when I need to", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake has been in no rush to settle down throughout his career, because he knows that he will have no trouble when the time comes. While this song was recorded near the beginning of his career, his mentality remained the same on “Child’s Play” from his fourth studio album Views, released in 2016 when Drake was 29.\n\nMarried in our twenties, now, where the fun in that?", "annotation_id": "11793500", "url": "https://genius.com/11793500", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In My Business", "line": "Shout out to your girlfriend, nigga, lately she distractin’ me Wonderin’ if I’m the best, well, I am, no Black Eyed Peas", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The girlfriend of the guy that Drake is talking to wonders if he’s the best, and he confirms her curiosity by saying, “Well I am.” will.i.am, legal name William James Adams, is a founding member of the Black Eyed Peas, a hip-hop trio and later quartet after the addition of Fergie to the group.\n\nDrake also uses the “your girlfriend is cheating on you with me” trope in,\n\n\n\n“Gonorrhea” by Lil Wayne:\n\nI be with your baby mama, you be with your child at home\n\n\n“Still Here”:\n\nAnd I see your girl like all the time, all the time though\nI can’t tell you if she’s yours or mine but I do know\n\n\n\n\nFor a list of these types of Drake lyrics, see here.", "annotation_id": "11742426", "url": "https://genius.com/11742426", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In My Business", "line": "Now they like potato chips, I just can’t have one", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This line is a reference to the Lays Potato Chips slogan “Bet you can’t eat just one!”, which is meant to communicate that the potato chips are so good that people who eat one chip will be unable to resist eating more. Gucci faces a similar situation with women; he can’t have just one girl, he needs several of them.", "annotation_id": "9283034", "url": "https://genius.com/9283034", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "In My Business", "line": "I got a lot of problems, gettin’ pussy just isn’t one", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This line about Gucci having no problem getting girls is also an allusion to Jay-Z’s “99 Problems”, the hook of which contains the iconic lines:\n\nIf you’re having girl problems, I feel bad for you, son\nI got 99 problems, but a bitch ain’t one", "annotation_id": "9283020", "url": "https://genius.com/9283020", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "No Disguise", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/En-derin-and-matt-u-johnson-no-disguise-lyrics", "genius_title": "No Disguise", "genius_id": 12978145, "source": "api"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-gods-plan-lyrics", "genius_title": "God’s Plan", "genius_id": 3315890, "source": "api", "album": "Scorpion"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "I feel good, sometimes, I don’t (Ayy, don’t)", "vault_lines": ["I feel good, sometimes I don't, ayy, (No)"], "meaning": "Drake, bouncing back from the last line discussing his beef, treats it with an indifference. He shows that his beef doesn’t affect his regular life, and that just like other humans, he feels good and sometimes he doesn’t.\n\nAlso, Drake is known for making songs that encapsulate a wide range of emotions from happy, to chill, to sad. He often transitions from happy to sad in one line, as he does in this line from “Karaoke” off of his 2010 project Thank Me Later:\n\nI know they say the first love is the sweetest, but that first cut is the deepest", "annotation_id": "14018517", "url": "https://genius.com/14018517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "I hold back, sometimes, I won’t, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I hold back, sometimes I won't, ayy (Yuh)"], "meaning": "Drake has had beef with many artists during the course of his career,  most notably with Philadelphia rapper Meek Mill.  Meek attacked Drake on Twitter for not writing his own music. Drake held back and never really responded to these allegations, but did state this to his friend Hitman Holla:\n\nI signed up for greatness, this comes with it.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/meekmill/status/623700698509758464?lang=en\n\nAnother recent example is the awfully one-sided beef between XXXTENTACION and Drizzy. X had accused Drake of stealing the flow from his hit single “Look At Me!” and continued to rant for months, but eventually stopped. Once again, Drake did not respond to any of his remarks.\n\nThough Drake has “held back” at times he has also responded to certain disses, like Pusha T’s “Exodus 23:1,” when he stated this at a concert:\n\nIf you was doing 16s when I was 16, and your shit still flopped, and you switched teams, don’t talk to me my nigga.", "annotation_id": "13797336", "url": "https://genius.com/13797336", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "Yes, I see the things that they wishin’ on me", "vault_lines": ["That they wishin' and wishin' and wishin' and wishin'", "They wishin' on me", "They wishin' on me, yeah, yeah", "They wishin' on me, yeah", "Yeah, I see the things that they wishin' on me"], "meaning": "Drake has a long history of conflicts. Chris Brown, who allegedly fought Drake in a bar, has publicly defended Meek Mill over legal issues. Meek Mill and Drake had been beefing since 2015, when Meek alleged that Drake didn’t write his own bars.\n\nThese lines are likely referencing the ongoing dispute between Drake and Joe Budden. On the other Scary Hours song, “Diplomatic Immunity,” Drake throws subliminal shots at Joe when he raps:\n\nShit is complex like short niggas ‘round tall ladies\nI gotta watch who I’m talkin’ to like it’s all-ages\nI’ve seen buddin' careers turn to sit around and talk about other careers, judgin' their peers\nKnowledge from niggas who did not contribute to none of this here\n\nThe above verse is explained in detail here.", "annotation_id": "13570953", "url": "https://genius.com/13570953", "state": "accepted", "votes": 413, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "She say, “Do you love me?” I tell her, ”Only partly I only love my bed and my mama, I’m sorry”", "vault_lines": ["She say, \"Do you love me?\"", "I tell her, \"Only partly, I only love my bed and my mama, I'm sorry\""], "meaning": "Drake has a close relationship with his mom, Sandi Graham. He frequently mentions her in his songs, and even dedicated his 2015 track, “You & The 6” to her.\n\nDrake previously mentioned not getting much sleep on 2016’s “9,” 2017’s “Gyalchester,” and “Do Not Disturb.\" He also spoke about his lack of rest in a toast during his 30th birthday. The limited rest time he gets makes him appreciate his bed even more.\n\nTwo days following the release of “God’s Plan,“ Drake posted a reference to the lyric on his Instagram. Ten days later, he posted the lyric again in an Instagram post dedicated to his mom on her 58th birthday.", "annotation_id": "13565245", "url": "https://genius.com/13565245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1227, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "I don’t wanna die for them to miss me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is referencing how the general public continuously praises artists after they pass away. Artists like Van Gogh were only appreciated as cultural icons posthumously, while successful musical artists such as Prince and David Bowie have garnered a ton of social media attention and memorial tributes after they died in 2016.\n\nDrake wants to earn respect and accolades while he’s alive, as he rapped on “Portland\":\n\nFuck being rich when I’m forty, man, I’m tryna make it now\n\nHe also talked about the phenomenon of posthumous fame in songs like “Gyalchester”:\n\nBury me now and I only get bigger\nThat’s word to my nigga\n\nand “Legend”:\n\nOh my God, oh my God\nIf I die, I’m a legend\nOh my God, oh my God\nIf I die, I’m a legend", "annotation_id": "13564877", "url": "https://genius.com/13564877", "state": "accepted", "votes": 150, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "Fifty Dub, I even got it tatted on me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Fifty Dub is short for Fifty World, where dub is slang for “w”. This is a reference to Drake’s friend and a member of his entourage, Fifty a.k.a Fif, who was murdered in September 2017 in Toronto. To commemorate his late friend, Drake tatted Fif’s face on himself in tribute.\n\nIn March of 2018, Drake showcased a chain dedicated to his late friend with the the caption, “Rest in Peace Fif…50 WORLD RWAY”", "annotation_id": "13561819", "url": "https://genius.com/13561819", "state": "accepted", "votes": 263, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "Hope I got some brothers that outlive me They gon’ tell the story, shit was different with me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake may be referring to his late OVO affiliate, Fif, who was gunned down in Toronto in September 2017. Drake later commemorated him with a tattoo of his face on his forearm a few days after his passing.\n\n\n\nThis may also refer to frequent collaborator, Lil Wayne, whom Drake considers a big brother. He has had a frequent history of almost deadly seizures heightened by his use of the narcotic, lean.\n\nSince God is a central theme of the song, Drake could also be comparing the 12 Apostles and Jesus Christ, to his OVO crew members and himself. As Jesus Christ’s most devout disciples, the 12 Apostles were tasked with spreading the gospel of Jesus after his death.\n\nDrake has not shied away from deifying himself using the alias ‘6 God’ in past tracks such as 6 God and 10 Bands:\n\nI’m the real 6 God, boy\n\nIf his OVO crew members outlive him, they can tell the story of his rise to fame, much like the Apostles did for Jesus Christ post-mortem. If he happens to die and somebody outlives him in his crew, they can tell you who Drake was behind closed doors, much better than what outside sources write up. That’s where “They gon' tell the story, shit was different with me” comes from.", "annotation_id": "13561781", "url": "https://genius.com/13561781", "state": "accepted", "votes": 144, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "I make sure that north-side eat", "vault_lines": ["I make sure that north-side eat, yuh, nah"], "meaning": "“North-side” is a reference to Toronto, Canada where Drake was born. Drake constantly shouts out the city in his music, an example being the 2016 album Views, which contains a great deal of references to Toronto. He may also be referencing the north-side of Toronto where​ he grew up near the Weston Road and Jane Street area.\n\nOn the other hand, Drake has also become the global ambassador for the Toronto Raptors, a basketball franchise, representing them at events, games and other festivities.\n\n\n\nDrake also recently opened a restaurant in Toronto called Pick6ix with his OVO partner Chubbs.", "annotation_id": "13560791", "url": "https://genius.com/13560791", "state": "accepted", "votes": 129, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "Eighty-one, they’ll bring the crashers to the party", "vault_lines": ["81, they'll bring the crashers to the party"], "meaning": "This line references Drake and OVO’s ties to the notorious Hells Angels Motorcycle Club (also referred to as A.H., HAMC or 81). The Hells Angels is considered an organized crime syndicate and one of North America’s largest criminal enterprises, with over 6,000 members involved in organized drug trafficking, extortion, prostitution and gang warfare.\n\nThe bar itself refers to the Hells Angels as 81, with 8 representing “H” and 1 representing “A” in the English alphabet. The “crashers” refer to HAMC enforcers, who commonly sport HAMC apparel with the term “Party Crasher” displayed.\n\n\n\nDrake’s connections to the HAMC stem from his security team, comprised of many notable Hells Angels' members within the Toronto area. Mike and Shane are the most recognizable examples.\n\n\n\nPictured: Drake (left) sitting in the Hells Angels T-shirt.", "annotation_id": "13560777", "url": "https://genius.com/13560777", "state": "accepted", "votes": 166, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "I been movin’ calm, don’t start no trouble with me Tryna keep it peaceful is a struggle for me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Although Drake squashed his major beef​, he’s no stranger to conflict.\n\nMost notably, he beefed with Philadelphia rapper Meek Mill in 2015 over claims that he didn’t write his own songs. Drake responded to these allegations with his diss tracks “Charged Up” and “Back to Back,” after which Meek Mill eventually responded with “Wanna Know.” After Drake’s OVO Fest, where he performed “Back to Back” and showed a slideshow of fan-made memes that dissed Meek Mill, Drake was widely considered the victor.\n\nDrake rapped a similar bar during his verse on Lil Wayne’s “Family Feud”:\n\nBut this isn’t all about a callin' truce\nI’m still dishin' out verbal abuse\nThat shit could get re-introduced\nIf somebody got somethin' they urgin' to prove, nigga", "annotation_id": "13560738", "url": "https://genius.com/13560738", "state": "accepted", "votes": 197, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "I can’t do this on my own, ayy (No, ayy) Someone watchin’ this shit close (Yep, close)", "vault_lines": ["I can't do this on my own, ayy, nah", "Someone watchin' this shit close, yep, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake knows that whatever God has planned for him, is for a greater good. As he crosses paths with other people and obstacles get put in his way, Drake can trust that the outcome will benefit him in the end.\n\nSometimes Drake seems to find God’s tests for him foolish:\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/180176189103673344\n\nThis concept is echoed in “Sacrifices,” featured on his 2017 release More Life, where he states in the chorus:\n\nSomeone watchin' over us, so shout goes out to Him", "annotation_id": "13560360", "url": "https://genius.com/13560360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "God’s plan, God’s plan", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake frequently calls himself “6 God” in his music;  “the 6” is a nickname for Toronto (and its six boroughs), where Drake is from, so calling himself “6 God” is his way of expressing his influence on the Toronto scene.\n\nHowever, this isn’t the first time Drake’s given it up to a religious God rather than himself. On the If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late cut “Jungle,” he raps:\n\nThese days, I’m lettin' God handle all things above me\n\nAnd on “Sneakin’,” his 2016 collaboration with 21 Savage, he raps an even more closely related bar:\n\nThis is all God’s doing, man, you can’t plan it\n\nThis line portrays the effects of Judaism in Drake’s life as he believes that God has had a plan for him from the beginning. Drake here implies that it was God’s Plan for him to be successful.", "annotation_id": "13560004", "url": "https://genius.com/13560004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 236, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "I’ve been me since Scarlett Road, ayy (Road, ayy)", "vault_lines": ["I've been me since Scarlett Road, ayy, nah"], "meaning": "Scarlett Road is a main road through Toronto, Ontario.\n\nhttps://www.google.co.uk/maps/place/Scarlett+Rd,+Toronto,+ON,+Canada/@43.683987,-79.5115265,13z/data=!4m2!3m1!1s0x882b36d65ac03c93:0x451f4be4eba4ddbb?sa=X&hl=en-GB\n\nDrake is saying that the fame never changed him as a person. He’s still the same person as he was when he lived on Weston Road.\n\nHe referenced Scarlett Road on his track “You & the 6”, saying:\n\nLet’s just call this shit even, we got some things to believe in\nDo you remember back to Weston Road, Scarlett Road?", "annotation_id": "13559852", "url": "https://genius.com/13559852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "I go hard on Southside G (Yeah, wait)", "vault_lines": ["I go hard on Southside G, yuh, wait"], "meaning": "Southside may be a reference to Memphis, Tennessee.\n\n\n\nShortly after this song’s release, Drizzy proceeded to feature on Memphis rapper BlocBoy JB’s hit song “Look Alive” where he represented Memphis with the opening line:\n\n901 Shelby Drive, look alive, look alive (‘live)\n\nDrake’s father’s side of the family is all based in Memphis. He even shot the “Worst Behavior” music video in Memphis, featuring his father and cousins.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/CccnAvfLPvE\n\nDrake may also be vaguely paying homage to G-Unit’s song “Straight Outta Southside”:\n\nYeah, nigga I’m straight outta Southside\n\nThis could also be a reference to the Atlanta producer, Southside. Drake tends to go hard on any Southside produced track, such as 2016’s “Grammys” or 2015’s “Digital Dash”.\n\nLastly, Drake could also be referring to the southside of the Scarborough neighborhood Galloway, which he references in a few of his songs.", "annotation_id": "13559527", "url": "https://genius.com/13559527", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "Might go down a G-O-D (Yeah, wait)", "vault_lines": ["Might go down as G.O.D. (Ayo), yeah, wait", "Might go down a G.O.D. (Ayo), yeah, ayy"], "meaning": "Drake seems fixated on the idea that death will immortalize him. He mentioned the same idea in the first verse of this song, but it’s clearly been on his mind for a while—he memorably rapped about it on 2015’s “Legend”:\n\nIf I die, I’m a legend", "annotation_id": "13559520", "url": "https://genius.com/13559520", "state": "accepted", "votes": 232, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "And you know me Turn the O2 into the O3, dog", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The O2 is a reference to an entertainment complex in southeast London, The O2, which has a capacity of 20,000. Behind Manchester Arena, The O2 is the second-largest indoor venue in the United Kingdom in terms of capacity.\n\nDrake performed at The O2 in late March 2017 during his ‘The Boy Meets World Tour’ and offered to refund the entire crowd’s ticket fee after Travis Scott fell off the stage, therefore turning it into the O3 (0-free).\n\nhttps://twitter.com/JmeBBK/status/965020068244574208\n\nDrake could also be saying that so many people come to the O2 to see him that it becomes the O3.\n\n\n\nIn preparation for doing 7 dates in London, the O2 Arena actually changed the 2 to 3\n\nhttps://twitter.com/OfficialGTHH/status/1112350093271814145\n\nDrake later confirmed that the O2 Arena sign changed to O3.", "annotation_id": "13559083", "url": "https://genius.com/13559083", "state": "accepted", "votes": 377, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "Without 40, Oli, there’d be no me ’Magine if I never met the broskies", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Noah “40” Shebib is Toronto native, OVO family member, and Drake’s main musical partner as a producer and engineer. 40’s production has become synonymous with a changing trend in hip-hop.\n\nOliver North is the nickname for Oliver El-Khatib, Drake’s manager, half of October Firm, and OVO co-founder.\n\nWithout them, he wouldn’t be the artist he is today. But, with his friends, ​Drake made an OVO Institution, or a “firm” as Drake preferred to call it during the More Life era.", "annotation_id": "13559017", "url": "https://genius.com/13559017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 313, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "I finessed down Weston Road (Ayy, ’nessed)", "vault_lines": ["I finessed down Weston Road, ayy, 'nessed, yeah"], "meaning": "Weston Road is a street in the Toronto area where Drake grew up. He even titled a song “Weston Road Flows” on his 2016 album Views, and on “Still Here\" he name-dropped the particular area where he grew up, the corner of Jane and Weston:\n\nHow did I finesse all of this shit from Jane and Weston?\n\nhttps://www.google.com/maps/place/1364-1356+Weston+Rd,+York,+ON+M6M+4R8,+Canada/@43.6906446,-79.501465,17z/data=!3m1!4b1!4m5!3m4!1s0x882b313377453079:0xec1e1d7901037616!8m2!3d43.6906407!4d-79.4992763", "annotation_id": "13558999", "url": "https://genius.com/13558999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "GODSPLAN", "line": "Don’t pull up at 6 a.m. to cuddle with me You know how I like it when you lovin’ on me", "vault_lines": ["Don't pull up at 6 AM to cuddle with me"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t want to be a booty call in the middle of the night—he’s worth the whole night.  Aubrey has echoed similar sentiments on his 2007 mixtape deep cut, “Don’t You Have A Man\":\n\nAll my other girls all up on that subtle shit\nThey don’t wanna cut it, they want a rapper to cuddle with\n\n“6” also has correlations to Toronto, Ontario, Canada, where Drake grew up and holds ties to. The nickname “The 6ix” comes from the six boroughs that make up Toronto as well as its area code, 416.", "annotation_id": "13549995", "url": "https://genius.com/13549995", "state": "accepted", "votes": 200, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Nothin’ but real niggas only, bad bitches only Rich niggas only, independent bitches only Boss niggas only, thick bitches only I got my real niggas here by my side (Yeah), on", "vault_lines": ["Nothin' but bad bitches in here tonight", "Nothin' but real niggas only, bad bitches only", "Rich niggas only, independent bitches only", "Boss niggas only, thick bitches only", "I got my real niggas here by my side (Yeah), on", "I got my real niggas here by my side, only"], "meaning": "showing that these particular people can only get in .", "annotation_id": "37254099", "url": "https://genius.com/37254099", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "For reals, if you mouth off, I blow your face off I mean “pop-pop-pop,” then I take off Nigga, now you see me, nigga, now you don’t Like Jamie Foxx actin’ like Ray Charles", "vault_lines": ["I mean brazy, my nigga", "For reals, if you mouth off, I blow your face off", "I mean \"pop-pop-pop,\" then I take off", "Nigga, now you see me, nigga, now you don't", "Like Jamie Foxx actin' like Ray Charles"], "meaning": "This is a clever play on the common magician’s line “Now you see it, now you don’t” and allusion to the 2004 film Ray, the cinematic biography of the legendary blind soul musician Ray Charles, played by Jamie Foxx in the film.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVHCQfcugdw\n\nBecause Ray was blind and Jamie is not, the switch between the character and actor is similar to Wayne’s gunplay, where you first “see [him]” and “now you don’t.”", "annotation_id": "20368351", "url": "https://genius.com/20368351", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Dinner with my man on a G5 Is my idea of a update", "vault_lines": ["Dinner with my man on a G5", "Is my idea of a update"], "meaning": "A Gulfstream V, colloquially known as a “G5,” is a luxury jet aircraft commonly owned by wealthy business owners. For example, billionaire Mark Cuban purchased such a plane in 1999 for a whopping $40 million!\n\nIn this lyric, Nicki also plays on the word “update.” Not only would dinner on a luxury jet be an upgrade from a regular meal, but since she would be flying in the air during this dinner, she would thus be on an up-date.", "annotation_id": "19721566", "url": "https://genius.com/19721566", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "(Young Mula, baby!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Young Mula is a nickname for Lil Wayne’s record label, Young Money Entertainment, a part of Republic Records. The label was founded in 2005 and has signed both Nicki Minaj and Drake.", "annotation_id": "15298540", "url": "https://genius.com/15298540", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "[Chorus: Chris Brown & Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "We wrote this chorus. I love Nicki Minaj. That’s a cool motherfucker. She embraced me and my brother. She came to the studio and she just let us rock. All she did was say, this is how I’m feeling, this is the vibe that I want. And she left the room. But she was very involved. She’d say, “I like it, but can you do this?” She knows exactly what she wants.", "annotation_id": "8172608", "url": "https://genius.com/8172608", "state": "verified", "votes": 241, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "I squirm and I shake, but I’m stuck in my ways", "vault_lines": ["I squirm and I shake, but I'm stuck in my ways"], "meaning": "Throughout his life Wayne has been battling with unexpected bouts of literal shaking caused by his lifelong struggle with epilepsy. An epileptic’s risk of seizures increases when they don’t get enough sleep, something Weezy is no stranger to because of his constant grinding. Regardless of the painful moments, he’s not showing any signs of slowing down anytime soon.", "annotation_id": "4293695", "url": "https://genius.com/4293695", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "The-These hoes couldn’t test me Even if their name was Pop Quiz", "vault_lines": ["The-These hoes couldn't test me", "Even if their name was Pop Quiz"], "meaning": "Every day, people jealous of Nicki’s success (especially in the media) try to throw tests at her, praying she’ll fail. The world loved watching her fight with Mariah Carey on American Idol, her wardrobe malfunction on national TV, and her physical struggle with SB.\n\nEven though she wasn’t expecting these curveballs in life, just as a student doesn’t expect pop quizzes, she always came out on top and aced the tests without flinching.\n\nTake bitches to school, then I Columbine these hoes", "annotation_id": "4274420", "url": "https://genius.com/4274420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Bad bitches who I fuck with Ma-Ma-Mad bitches we don’t fuck with", "vault_lines": ["Bad bitches who I fuck with", "Ma-Ma-Mad bitches we don't fuck with", "I don't fuck with them chickens"], "meaning": "Nicki always keeps a circle of bad bitches around her, including the friends who have been with her from the start, and the fans who ride or die for her. They repel any other bitter and jealous bitches like a magnet.\n\nYou see a bad bitch gettin' shine, you should love it\n\n\n\n\n\n To “test someone” (appropriately, in this case a woman) is to provoke one’s rage. Women are often stereotyped to ranting lunatics, or easily irritated (double pun for “bitchy”)\n\nShe and her friends don’t associate themselves, with crazy hos or bitches, neither do they have any, physical contact with them. (Note that when you handle raw chicken cutlets, you get can sick from not being careful with cleaning them). It’s another way of saying, she’ll never stoop down to their level.\n\nPeople this whole year, have been trying to “get under the skin” of Nicki and piss her off, however it’s not working, so she’s calling them fools.", "annotation_id": "4274419", "url": "https://genius.com/4274419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "And tell them bitches blow me Lance Stephenson", "vault_lines": ["And tell them bitches blow me"], "meaning": "NBA player and Brooklyn native Lance Stephenson infamously “taunted” rival LeBron James by literally blowing in his ear in the middle of a playoff game.\n\nNicki has a history of politely asking women beneath her in the game to suck her “cock” (another word for chicken). Not from her sons, though. That’d be gross.", "annotation_id": "4273175", "url": "https://genius.com/4273175", "state": "pending", "votes": 202, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "(Pinkprint, ayy)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In an interview with Radio, Nicki mentioned how Drake had heard the album a few months prior to its release. She quoted him saying, “Oh my God, you’re talking about new things, I’m so proud of you.”", "annotation_id": "4269010", "url": "https://genius.com/4269010", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "My man full, he just ate I don’t duck nobody but tape Yeah, that was a setup For a punchline on duct tape", "vault_lines": ["My man full, he just ate", "I don't duck nobody but tape", "Yeah, that was a setup", "For a punchline on duct tape"], "meaning": "Unlike Weezy and Drizzy, her lucky man got a taste of her cupcakes and didn’t stop eating until he was stuffed.\n\nNicki plays on “tape” and throws some shade towards Iggy Azalea, who was embroiled in an infamous sex tape scandal. Unlike Iggy, Nicki is smart enough to avoid that situation and “duck” from the cameras.\n\nIn typical Nicki fashion, she pauses for a moment to point out her clever wordplay to oblivious listeners. She’s also sarcastically implying that if you thought she was just making a joke about duct tape, you should rewind and listen a little more closely.\n\nIt sounds like Nicki should use her verified account!", "annotation_id": "4261154", "url": "https://genius.com/4261154", "state": "accepted", "votes": 163, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Oh, if you lame and you know it, be quiet", "vault_lines": ["Oh, if you lame and you know it, be quiet"], "meaning": "This plays on to the popular children’s song, “If You’re Happy and You Know It.” If you have nothing to your name then Chris has no interest in hearing from you as this is a place only for those who have made it.", "annotation_id": "4258844", "url": "https://genius.com/4258844", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "My story is how I went from “poor me” To “please pour me a drink and celebrate with me”", "vault_lines": ["My story is how I went from \"poor me\"", "To \"please pour me a drink and celebrate with me\""], "meaning": "Wayne embodies the essence of a rags-to-riches story, tirelessly working his way to the top of the rap game. As such he invites the listener to come over and celebrate his successes over some good wine.\n\nIn anticipation for Tha Carter V, he threw a pre-release party in his hometown of New Orleans.", "annotation_id": "4258721", "url": "https://genius.com/4258721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Wo-worried ’bout y’all niggas, us straight", "vault_lines": ["Wo-worried 'bout y'all niggas, us straight"], "meaning": "The week before this track dropped, Nicki and her Young Money labelmates went through some public controversies. This includes Tyga calling her and Drizzy “fake,” Tha Carter V and The Pinkprint both getting pushed back, and Drizzy being accused of assaulting a stripper.\n\nNicki assures us, though, that everyone is good, and that people prying into their business should spend more time worrying about their own problems.", "annotation_id": "4258563", "url": "https://genius.com/4258563", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "I piss greatness, like goldish yellow", "vault_lines": ["I piss greatness, like goldish yellow"], "meaning": "This marks where Wayne starts going off-topic. Only 6 bars in, and he’s already forgotten what the song is actually about.\n\n\n\n\nIt looks like Weezy’s moved on from punchlines about poop to a different bodily function. Gold represents value and high quality; everything about Wayne, down to his piss, is of pure excellence.\n\nWayne’s the rap game Ricky Bobby.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrvkB3pK8lg\n\nUrine that resembles a goldish yellow color indicates dehydration. Wayne has been working so hard over the years he doesn’t even have the time to keep himself hydrated.", "annotation_id": "4258489", "url": "https://genius.com/4258489", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Then I make you burp, boy, treat beef like sirloin I’m talkin’ ‘bout runnin’ in houses with army guns So think about your son and daughter rooms Got two hoes with me, masked up, they got smaller guns Ain’t thinkin’ ’bout your son and daughter rooms", "vault_lines": ["Then I make you burp, boy, treat beef like sirloin", "I'm talkin' 'bout runnin' in houses with army guns", "So think about your son and daughter rooms", "Got two hoes with me, masked up, they got smaller guns", "Ain't thinkin' 'bout your son and daughter rooms"], "meaning": "Good luck beefing with Wayne — he’ll serve you just like a fine dish of some grade A sirloin. Weezy, and his guns-blazing women companions, are crazy enough to shoot up your residence without concern for your sons and daughters rooms.\n\n\n\nWeezy made a whole song dedicated to his “Army Gunz” back in ‘06.", "annotation_id": "4258415", "url": "https://genius.com/4258415", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "I been peeped that you like me, you know? Who the fuck you really wanna be with besides me? I mean, it doesn’t take much for us to do this shit quietly", "vault_lines": ["Bad bitches who I fuck with", "I been peeped that you like me, you know?", "Who the fuck you really wanna be with besides me?", "I mean, it doesn't take much for us to do this shit quietly", "The NBA players fuck with you"], "meaning": "Drake has previously expressed doubts that he’s the only one his lady friends are interested in, a notable example being in his verse on PARTYNEXTDOOR’s “Recognize”:\n\nStories of you and your girls is all I hear\nWho do you fuck in the city when I’m not there?\n\nGiven that he’s mega famous, Drake prefers to keep his rendezvous on the down low to avoid media attention, especially from paparazzi. He does make exceptions, though…", "annotation_id": "4258322", "url": "https://genius.com/4258322", "state": "pending", "votes": 115, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "That money talk, I just rephrase it, my nigga", "vault_lines": ["I mean brazy, my nigga", "That money talk, I just rephrase it, my nigga"], "meaning": "Wayne is famous for his “money talks”—metaphors, a notable example being from his banger “Blunt Blowin'.” Your money “talking” lets your bank statement speak for you — the ultimate form of bragging — since you don’t even have to utter a word.", "annotation_id": "4258291", "url": "https://genius.com/4258291", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Hut-hut one, hut-hut two Big titties, big butt too", "vault_lines": ["Hut-hut one, hut-hut two", "Big titties, big butt too"], "meaning": "“Hut one, hut two” is football terminology; during a game, this is yelled by the quarterback before the center snaps the ball in an attempt to distract or fool the defense. Linebackers and tacklers seek out the quarterback for this reason.\n\nNicki repeats “hut” twice to create a balance in syllables for this line, i.e. ‘hut-hut one’ and ‘big tit-ties’. As the quarterback of this game, she’s being chased by men obsessed with her above-average assets. In “Roman’s Revenge” she claimed to be the Eli Manning of the game.", "annotation_id": "4258276", "url": "https://genius.com/4258276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "That’s another story, I’m no story-teller", "vault_lines": ["That's another story, I'm no story-teller"], "meaning": "Wayne seems to know the full story behind Nicki’s breakup, but doesn’t intend to publicly spill any details because that’s not his story to tell.\n\nHe’s also not particularly known for making storytelling songs, in the same vein as Nas' “Undying Love” or Eminem’s “Stan.” You can’t really blame him though — it can be hard to piece together a beginning, a middle, and an end when you don’t even write your lyrics down first\n\n\n\nIronically, Wanye ends this verse with a short story.", "annotation_id": "4258269", "url": "https://genius.com/4258269", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Blood gang, take the “B” off behavior, my nigga", "vault_lines": ["Blood gang, take the \"B\" off behavior, my nigga"], "meaning": "Wayne is affiliated with the Bloods, and like all gangs, they’re notorious for their violent and inappropriate behavior.\n\nBlood without the B is lood (lewd). Lewd behavior.\n\nThe wordplay is similar to the time he took the “star” out of starvation\".", "annotation_id": "4258220", "url": "https://genius.com/4258220", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Calendar say I got money for days", "vault_lines": ["Calendar say I got money for days"], "meaning": "Getting something for days illustrates the sheer quantity of it; Wayne can consistently spend money without running out. Calendars mark eventful days, and Weezy’s calendar consist of dollar signs only. He also used an almost identical line on Busta Rhyme’s “Arab Money Remix.”", "annotation_id": "4258161", "url": "https://genius.com/4258161", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "This shit is crazy, my nigga I mean brazy, my nigga", "vault_lines": ["This shit is crazy, my nigga", "I mean brazy, my nigga"], "meaning": "It sounds like Lil Wayne is saying, “I mean brazy, my nigga.” That would be a shout out to his blood affiliation; by replacing a “c” with the letter “b,” he makes “brazy” an anti-crip word.\n\nThe official lyric video, however, does say this line is, “I been praising my nigga.” He must’ve been praising the levels of his success.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BU769XX_dIQ&t=4m1s", "annotation_id": "4258145", "url": "https://genius.com/4258145", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "I never fucked Nick’ and that’s fucked up If I did fuck, she’d be fucked up Whoever is hittin’ ain’t hittin’ it right ’Cause she act like she need dick in her life", "vault_lines": ["I never fucked Nick' and that's fucked up", "If I did fuck, she'd be fucked up", "Whoever is hittin' ain't hittin' it right", "'Cause she act like she need dick in her life"], "meaning": "Nicki went through a high-profile breakup with her boyfriend and assistant (and possible fiancé?) of 14 years. Wayne wants Nicki to know he’s there for her in more ways than one, and ensures she won’t regret it (though Drizzy kinda called dibs like 30 seconds ago).\n\n\n\nEx-boyfriend Safaree Samuels pretty much confirmed the breakup rumors by covering up two tattoos of Nicki on his chest.", "annotation_id": "4258122", "url": "https://genius.com/4258122", "state": "pending", "votes": 195, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "The NBA players fuck with you", "vault_lines": ["Who the fuck you really wanna be with besides me?", "Oh! Yeah, you the man in the city when the mayor fuck with you", "The NBA players fuck with you", "The bad-ass bitches doin' makeup and hair fuck with you, oh"], "meaning": "In 2013, Drake became the ambassador for the Toronto Raptors, showing support and greatly boosting team morale. Pictures, however, tells us perhaps that Drake may adore the NBA players more than they like him.", "annotation_id": "4258096", "url": "https://genius.com/4258096", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Sixteen in a clip, one in the chamber 17 Ward bully with seventeen bullets", "vault_lines": ["Sixteen in a clip, one in the chamber", "17 Ward bully with seventeen bullets"], "meaning": "Cocking a gun places one round in the chamber. If you place a new, fully loaded magazine into the gun, you’ll have all the rounds in the magazine  plus one in the chamber to fire. Nate Dogg likes it the same way.\n\nWayne’s hometown, Hollygroove, is located in the 17th Ward of New Orleans. He has a tattoo dedicated to this area.", "annotation_id": "4258075", "url": "https://genius.com/4258075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Oh! Yeah, you the man in the city when the mayor fuck with you", "vault_lines": ["Oh! Yeah, you the man in the city when the mayor fuck with you", "The NBA players fuck with you"], "meaning": "Drake is now so successful that can hang out with the then-mayor of his hometown, Toronto.\n\nOVO that’s major shit, Toronto with me that’s mayor shit\n\nBelieve it or not, former Mayor Rob Ford was actually a far bigger partier than Drizzy. He had a well-known crack-cocaine problem and there have been videos leaked of his drunken rants.  Mayor Ford passed away in 2016 from cancer.\n\n\n\nAnnise Parker, the mayor of Houston (Drake’s second home), named June 10th as “Drake Day.”", "annotation_id": "4258071", "url": "https://genius.com/4258071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "My girlfriend will beat a bitch up if she wave They bet’ not fuck with her surfboard, surfboard", "vault_lines": ["My girlfriend will beat a bitch up if she wave", "They bet' not fuck with her surfboard, surfboard"], "meaning": "At the time of this recording, Wayne was dating Christina Milian. If anyone would get flirty with Weezy, she wouldn’t hesitate to put an end to it. Weezy was his girlfriend’s surfboard, and shedl protect him at all costs. Perhaps Christina was extra cautious since Wayne claimed he’d “surf” anyone who waves.\n\n\n\n“Surfboard” is bathtub-based sex act popularized in Beyoncé’s “Drunk In Love.” Wayne and Christina remixed the track.", "annotation_id": "4258057", "url": "https://genius.com/4258057", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Raise every bottle and cup in the sky Sparks in the air like the Fourth of July Nothin’ but bad bitches in here tonight", "vault_lines": ["Raise every bottle and cup in the sky", "Sparks in the air like the Fourth of July", "Nothin' but bad bitches in here tonight"], "meaning": "In many nightclubs, when ordering bottle service, the bottle girl will bring you what you ordered while waving sparkles in the air. The place will be lit up similar to how the sky’s filled with fireworks on the 4th of July, United States' Independence day. There might even be romantic sparks between Chris and his “bad bitches.”", "annotation_id": "4258051", "url": "https://genius.com/4258051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Yeah, so thick that everybody else in the room is so uncomfortable Ass on Houston, Texas, but the face look just like Clair Huxtable", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, so thick that everybody else in the room is so uncomfortable", "Ass on Houston, Texas, but the face look just like Clair Huxtable"], "meaning": "Drake’s professed love of large women makes some people uncomfortable—but he’s perfectly happy with it.\n\nDrake is particularly fond of women from Houston—at least three of his exes (Maliah Michel, Jhonni Blaze, and Lira Galore) hail from H-town and worked as strippers. His song “Houstatlantavegas” celebrates his love of a stripper, so he’s comparing his woman’s booty to that of a stripper.\n\nClair Huxtable is a character from The Cosby Show, played by actress Phylicia Rashad who happens to be from Houston. This comparison suggests she’s classy as well, with a nice face too.", "annotation_id": "4258000", "url": "https://genius.com/4258000", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "I mean, she say I’m obsessed with thick women and I agree Yeah, that’s right, I like my girls BBW Yeah, type that wanna suck you dry and then eat some lunch with you", "vault_lines": ["I mean, she say I'm obsessed with thick women and I agree", "Yeah, that's right, I like my girls BBW", "Yeah, type that wanna suck you dry and then eat some lunch with you", "I mean brazy, my nigga"], "meaning": "“BBW” stands for “big, beautiful woman,” sometimes also referred to “big, black women.”  The terms can be applied broadly, and despite Nicki’s certifiably huge behind, her model-thin waist suggests she’s not really a BBW.\n\nDrizzy’s girl blesses him with blowjob after blowjob, literally sucking him until he has nothing left to give her. Note that this is all happening before lunch, implying he slept with the girl the night before and woke up to the best alarm clock in the world. This also suggests BBW’s like to eat — in more ways than one. Sounds like a lovely afternoon.\n\nEven though Nicki might not fully fit the bill, we can tell there’s some sexual tension between Drake and Nicki. He appeared in the music video for “Anaconda” where he receives a lap dance from Nicki, a scene that displays his desire for thick women.", "annotation_id": "4257997", "url": "https://genius.com/4257997", "state": "accepted", "votes": 218, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "If I did, I’d ménage with ’em And let ’em eat my ass like a cupcake", "vault_lines": ["If I did, I'd ménage with 'em", "And let 'em eat my ass like a cupcake"], "meaning": "Nicki plays off her own name referencing a ménage à trois, the French term for a threesome. It’s no mistake that Nicki still holds the power in this sitch, allowing two of the biggest rappers to get a go at that world-famous ass.\n\nShe seems to have a thing for ass-eating and salad-tossing. Since she calls her vagina a “cookie,” it’s only appropriate that her ass is a cupcake.\n\n\n\nThere’s also an echo (maybe intentional, maybe not) of the famous phrase (attributed to Marie Antoinette) “let them eat cake”:\n\n\n\n“Let them eat cake”\n\n\n\n“Let them eat my ass like a cupcake”", "annotation_id": "4257992", "url": "https://genius.com/4257992", "state": "accepted", "votes": 235, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "All my goons so overzealous I’m from Hollygrove, the holy Mecca", "vault_lines": ["All my goons so overzealous", "I'm from Hollygrove, the holy Mecca"], "meaning": "Mecca (or “Makkah”) is a city in Saudi Arabia home to the Kaaba (pictured below), generally accepted as Islam’s holiest site and the gathering site for millions of Muslims each year.\n\nHollygrove is a neighborhood of New Orleans, where Wayne was born and raised. Just as Muslims hold Mecca in the highest regard and use it as the focus of their existence, so does Wayne of his hometown.\n\n\n\n“Zealousy” is an adjective often applied to religious attitudes. Weezy’s Hollygroove people are always down to ride or die for him, the same way he is for them. They shouldn’t forget who’s in charge though.", "annotation_id": "4257991", "url": "https://genius.com/4257991", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Had to show bitches where the top is Ri-Ring finger where the rock is", "vault_lines": ["Had to show bitches where the top is", "Ri-Ring finger where the rock is"], "meaning": "Nicki constantly breaks the glass ceiling for other women in rap, showing them that it’s possible to do whatever men are capable of and more.\n\nFor years, there have been rumors that she was engaged to ex-boyfriend Safaree Samuels (aka SB). They were caught wearing some diamond “rocks” on their ring fingers in Cabo.\n\n\n\nHowever, even though the engagement is now called off, Nicki will always be married to the rap game, and other jealous female rappers can talk to the ring.\n\n“The rock” could also be a nod to Jay Z’s Roc-A-Fella and Roc Nation brands. The Pinkprint is named in honor of Hov’s The Blueprint.", "annotation_id": "4257988", "url": "https://genius.com/4257988", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "And the other day in her Maybach I thought, “Goddamn, this is the perfect time” We had just come from that video You know, L.A. traffic, how the city slow She was sittin’ down on that big butt But I was still starin’ at the titties though Yeah, low-key or maybe high-key", "vault_lines": ["Big titties, big butt too", "And the other day in her Maybach", "I thought, \"Goddamn, this is the perfect time\"", "We had just come from that video", "You know, L.A. traffic, how the city slow", "She was sittin' down on that big butt", "But I was still starin' at the titties though", "Yeah, low-key or maybe high-key", "Yeah, that was a setup"], "meaning": "Drake admits it: even though he has yet to smash Nicki, he still checks her out.\n\nLos Angeles traffic is notorious for being congested, therefore giving one ample time in the car for other activities. During one such occasion, Drake found himself in the car with Nicki after a video shoot (presumably for “Anaconda,” in which him and Nicki share a sexually provocative scene) which he thought could serve as a great opportunity for intimacy.\n\nThe back of Nicki’s Maybach is also where she thought she had Meek Mill catching feelings.", "annotation_id": "4257977", "url": "https://genius.com/4257977", "state": "accepted", "votes": 143, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "When I walk in, sit up straight I don’t give a fuck if I was late", "vault_lines": ["When I walk in, sit up straight", "I don't give a fuck if I was late"], "meaning": "Nicki asserts her position at the top the rap game, comparing herself to the President or the Queen when she walks in a room on her own schedule.\n\nShe has been blasted by the media for being late to appointments, both in her E! Special, and when she was 13 minutes late to the first live American Idol taping.", "annotation_id": "4257971", "url": "https://genius.com/4257971", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Th-These girls are my sons Jo-Jon & Kate Plus Eight", "vault_lines": ["Th-These girls are my sons", "Jo-Jon & Kate Plus Eight"], "meaning": "This is Nicki’s thing — it’d be surprising if this song didn’t have a line cleverly dissing other girls by calling them Mother Nicki’s sons. Read more about all the other places she uses this trope.\n\nJon & Kate Plus 8 was a television show on TLC chronicling the lives of two parents raising sextuplets and a set of twins. Since the show first aired Jon has left Kate, leaving her to raise all the children herself. Sounds a lot like Nicki Minaj, bringing up all these other rappers dependent on her.", "annotation_id": "4257960", "url": "https://genius.com/4257960", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Wo-wo-wo-worried ’bout if my butt fake", "vault_lines": ["Wo-wo-wo-worried 'bout if my butt fake"], "meaning": "Minaj reacts to the on-going rumors claiming her ass is fake. She’s addressed them before with humor, but this image made its rounds after a 2014 performance, rekindling the rumors and bringing the topic back to the forefont with blogs and tabloids.", "annotation_id": "4257954", "url": "https://genius.com/4257954", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "Yo, I never fucked Wayne, I never fucked Drake On my life, man, fuck’s sake", "vault_lines": ["Yo, I never fucked Wayne, I never fucked Drake", "On my life, man, fuck's sake"], "meaning": "This line sets the tone for the whole song—both Wayne and Drake start their verses with “I never fucked Nicki,” completely disavowing the notion that she slept her way to her level of success.\n\nShe’s been addressing these kind of rumors in her lyrics since 2009. In a 2013 interview with MTV, Nicki spoke on “maintaining her dignity” whilst breaking into the rap world;\n\nWhen I started being around Young Money, Wayne thought I was shy, but it wasn’t that I was shy, I just didn’t want anybody to think I was easy… I didn’t want anyone in this game to ever be able to say ‘I had sex with her’ or ‘when she needed a deal, she had to…’ No.\n\n\n\nThe Pinkprint overall has a strong theme of Nicki using her sexuality on her own terms. For example, the previous single, “Anaconda”, is a sexual brag track playing off ‘90s tropes but with the stereotypical gender roles reversed.", "annotation_id": "4257946", "url": "https://genius.com/4257946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 460, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "I never fucked Nicki ’cause she got a man But when that’s over, then I’m first in line", "vault_lines": ["I never fucked Nicki 'cause she got a man", "But when that's over, then I'm first in line"], "meaning": "Nicki Minaj’s hypeman and all-around sidekick was also her decade-long boyfriend at the time, Safaree, also know as Scaff Beezy or SB. She shouts him out in “Stupid Hoe,” and he does the ad libs for “Did It On ‘Em,” but this is the first track where she acknowledged her relationship.\n\nThey later broke up, so theoretically Drizzy could’ve swooped in, but sadly it appears that Meek Mill took his spot.", "annotation_id": "4257943", "url": "https://genius.com/4257943", "state": "accepted", "votes": 238, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "line": "I don’t fuck with them chickens Unless they last name is Cutlet Let it soak in Like seasoning", "vault_lines": ["I don't fuck with them chickens", "Unless they last name is Cutlet", "Let it soak in"], "meaning": "“Chicken” is slang for girls. Nicki’s only favorable of literal chicken she’s eating, which comes in cutlet form, not any of these stupid broads.\n\nThe line is so layered that it might take a second to soak in, just like how seasoning takes a minute to soak into the food. Additionally, “seasoned” is commonly used as an adjective to describe someone who’s experienced in whatever field they’re in. By now, Nicki is certainly considered a heavy hitter in the rap game.\n\nYou not seasoned.", "annotation_id": "4257870", "url": "https://genius.com/4257870", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/2dc42155625a666c04d14a04a1b9e51e.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Only\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>OnlyNicki Minaj3.9M", "status": "done", "targets": 41, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Nicki-minaj-only-lyrics", "genius_title": "Only", "genius_id": 555359, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "At the gate outside, when they pull up, they get me loose", "vault_lines": ["At the gate outside, when they pull up, they get me loose"], "meaning": "This line refers to Travis' release from custody after a concert on May 13, 2017 in Rogers, Arkansas. His team met him when he was released.", "annotation_id": "41076564", "url": "https://genius.com/41076564", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "[Part III]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s verse on SICKO MODE was submitted at 2 a.m. on the day Astroworld was released — August 3, 2018. The track was already finalized, but his vocals arrived just hours before the album went live. Despite the late delivery, the team was able to mix and master the verse in time for the official release. Travis Scott first heard the completed version of the song, with Drake’s part included, on release day. His reaction, captured on video, shows his genuine surprise and excitement, confirming how last-minute the addition was.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f-0145hQ9r0", "annotation_id": "36040564", "url": "https://genius.com/36040564", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "[Part I]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The initial beat and melody of the first part of “Sicko Mode”, featuring Drake’s vocals, was initially conceived as a standalone, original song. Producer Rogét Chahayed created the organ chord progression and Hit-Boy added bass and drums, forming a complete beat that Travis Scott initially worked on in 2016. Later, when Scott was working on “Astroworld”, he decided to incorporate this beat into “Sicko Mode”, adding Drake’s vocals and transitioning into other parts of the song, according to Billboard.\n\nhttps://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/travis-scott-sicko-mode-number-one-hit-8488882/", "annotation_id": "36040502", "url": "https://genius.com/36040502", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "My dog would probably do it for a Louis belt That’s just all he know, he don’t know nothin’ else I tried to show ’em, yeah I tried to show ’em, yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["My dog would probably do it for a Louis belt", "That's just all he know, he don't know nothin' else", "I tried to show 'em, yeah", "I tried to show 'em, yeah, yeah"], "meaning": "Louis Vuitton is a luxury French fashion company. One Louis belt can cost up to $900.\n\nTravis commented these bars on a 2017 Instagram photo for Drake’s song, “Signs.” Drake recorded “Signs” for a Louis Vuitton runway show.\n\nNotably, Drake also has an American Bully XL named Winter, that made a cameo appearance in the “SICKO MODE” music video.", "annotation_id": "17948214", "url": "https://genius.com/17948214", "state": "accepted", "votes": 160, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "I crept down the block (Down the block) Made a right (Yeah, right) Cut the lights (Yeah, what?), paid the price (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I crept down the block (Down the block)", "Made a right (Yeah, right)", "Cut the lights (Yeah, what?), paid the price (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Many have perceived these lines to be a shot at Kanye West, who Drake’s beefing with after Ye supposedly told Pusha T about Drake’s son, Adonis—which would then lead to his May 2018 diss track “The Story of Adidon”—and that Drake originally wanted the “Lift Yourself” beat.\n\nSome evidence supporting this theory is explained in a popular Twitter thread, which compares the location of Drake’s house to Kanye’s house:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/tmorrison24/status/1035574650779181056?s=21\n\nLater in a now deleted December 2018 tweet, Kanye acknowledged Drake’s lines were allegedly about him.", "annotation_id": "15613424", "url": "https://genius.com/15613424", "state": "accepted", "votes": 230, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Shorty FaceTimed me out the blue", "vault_lines": ["Shorty FaceTimed me out the blue"], "meaning": "This line could be referring to Travis' relationship with Kylie Jenner. The couple went public in May 2017.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wgo_zH8kaMI\n\nEvidence of their use of FaceTime occurred during the NYC Ksubi party in October 2017. The event celebrated the launch of Travis Scott’s signature collection with the Australian brand.", "annotation_id": "15598583", "url": "https://genius.com/15598583", "state": "accepted", "votes": 119, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "I be spinnin’ Valley, circle blocks ’til I’m dizzy (Yeah, what?)", "vault_lines": ["I be spinnin' Valley, circle blocks 'til I'm dizzy (Yeah, what?)"], "meaning": "Valley Circle is a major street that runs through San Fernando Valley in Los Angeles. It connects the valley to the affluent Hidden Hills community in Calabasas where Drake, as well as many other celebrities, reside. Famous Calabasas residents include Kanye and Kim Kardashian-West, Justin Bieber, Jennifer Lopez, and the Rock, amongst others.", "annotation_id": "15188321", "url": "https://genius.com/15188321", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Some-some-some-someone said To win the retreat, we all in too deep Play-play-playin’ for keeps, don’t play us for weak (Someone said) To win the retreat, we all in too deep Play-play-playin’ for keeps, don’t play us for weak", "vault_lines": ["To win the retreat, we all in too deep", "Play-play-playin' for keeps, don't play us for weak (Someone said)", "Someone said, mother****, someone said", "Don't play us for weak"], "meaning": "Travis Scott is paying homage to Houston rapper, Big Hawk who was murdered in 2006.\n\nHawk was a member of the Screwed Up Click (S.U.C), a Houston rap group, which also featured DJ Screw, who is the subject of the song “R.I.P. SCREW.”\n\n\n\nIn an interview with Rolling Stone, Travis' longtime A&R/collaborator Sickamore mentioned that he felt like Travis wasn’t getting the credit he deserved as a rapper. Travis shared this sentiment, feeling as though people weren’t respecting him within the rap community.\n\nThis chorus is almost a direct shot back at those who doubt Travis. It speaks to his mentality regarding how he’s coming to attack the rap game. He’s “playing for keeps” and warning everyone not to “play him for weak.”", "annotation_id": "15181101", "url": "https://genius.com/15181101", "state": "accepted", "votes": 523, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "We ain’t even make it to the room She thought it was the ocean, it’s just the pool Now I got her open, it’s just the Goose", "vault_lines": ["We ain't even make it to the room", "She thought it was the ocean, it's just the pool", "Now I got her open, it's just the Goose"], "meaning": "Travis could be talking about an infinity edge pool, which is a custom-built pool that flows over an edge. This helps create the illusion of water flowing infinitely into the horizon, similar to how the ocean does.\n\nNotably, in 2020, Travis would go on to buy a $23.5 million three-story mansion in Los Angeles that included a 70-foot glass-bottom infinity pool.\n\n\n\nTravis' mansion and pool\n\n\nGrey Goose is a popular brand of vodka produced by Bacardi. After stopping by his pool, Travis' girl is ready to take the night further by loosing up with a drink.", "annotation_id": "15165340", "url": "https://genius.com/15165340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 129, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Hit my eses, I need the bootch", "vault_lines": ["Hit my eses, I need the bootch"], "meaning": "“Ese” is usually used to describe Mexican-American gang members. Over time people in America have used it to address anyone of Latin origin, in general.\n\nBootch is slang for cocaine. The term originated in Washington Heights, New York. Latinos are widely known for the production of cocaine in South America. Kanye West also mentions hitting bootch on his 2018 track “Fire”:\n\nSniffed a lil bootch off the nail", "annotation_id": "15150796", "url": "https://genius.com/15150796", "state": "accepted", "votes": 120, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Had to hit my old town to duck the news Two-four hour lockdown, we made no moves", "vault_lines": ["Had to hit my old town to duck the news", "Two-four hour lockdown, we made no moves"], "meaning": "This could mean that he went to his hometown (Missouri City, Texas) to dodge the press and paparazzi. As of 2016, the city has a small population of 74,561, which contrasts from Los Angeles' population of almost 4 million.\n\n“Two four hour lockdown” means “twenty four hour lockdown”—a phrase usually applied to criminals in prison. Travis uses “lockdown” to show that he had complete focus while finishing the album.", "annotation_id": "15149125", "url": "https://genius.com/15149125", "state": "accepted", "votes": 150, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "767, man, this shit got double bedroom, man", "vault_lines": ["767, man, this shit got double bedroom, man"], "meaning": "Drake uses a commercial plane as a private jet hire. The Boeing 767 typically seats upwards of 300 people, and this customized version apparently has two bedrooms.\n\nIn 2019, Drake was given a free 767 jet from Canadian cargo airline Cargojet worth around $185 million. Cargojet believes the publicity of having Drake attached to their company will help pay for the plane over time.", "annotation_id": "15145070", "url": "https://genius.com/15145070", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Who put this shit together? I’m the glue", "vault_lines": ["Who put this shit together? I'm the glue"], "meaning": "Travis is not only an artist, but also a curator in the sense that he plays a directorial role in his songs and projects. This is shown by the many featured artists that Travis utilises uniquely across his discography, most notably T.I.’s spoken word and narration on Rodeo.\n\nASTROWORLD is no exception—especially on “SICKO MODE.” Drake is used as an opener and a closer, Swae Lee’s vocals are cut off abruptly and the late Big Hawk is sampled throughout as a tribute to Travis' hometown of Houston.", "annotation_id": "15141556", "url": "https://genius.com/15141556", "state": "accepted", "votes": 160, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Had to slop the top off, it’s just a roof, uh", "vault_lines": ["Had to slop the top off, it's just a roof, uh"], "meaning": "The words “slop” and “top” usually make up the slang phrase “sloppy toppy,” meaning a blowjob. Travis plays on these words to convey that he’s receiving a blowjob while riding in a convertible vehicle with the top down. This may also be a reference to “Sloppy Toppy,” the 8th track on Travis' 2014 mixtape Days Before Rodeo.", "annotation_id": "15138470", "url": "https://genius.com/15138470", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Yeah, passed the dogs a celly Sendin’ texts, ain’t sendin’ kites, yeah He said, “Keep that on lock” I say, “You know this shit, it’s stife,” yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, passed the dogs a celly", "Sendin' texts, ain't sendin' kites, yeah", "He said, \"Keep that on lock\"", "I say, \"You know this shit, it's stife,\" yeah"], "meaning": "A “kite” in street culture is a letter sent to and from individuals caught up in the prison system. Phone usage is limited in prison so many turn to the postal service to keep in contact with the outside world, though the process can be slow.\n\nWhen something is “stife,” it’s safe and secured. Travis communicates here to his comrades by mobile not to snitch to the police, hence telling them to “keep that on lock.”", "annotation_id": "15134436", "url": "https://genius.com/15134436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Pass this to my daughter, I’ma show her what it took (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Pass this to my daughter, I'ma show her what it took (Yeah)"], "meaning": "On February 1st, 2018, Travis Scott and Kylie Jenner welcomed a daughter, Stormi, into the world. The pregnancy of Kylie attracted lots of media speculation as to whether Kylie was actually bearing his child, as the couple kept this matter private.\n\nIn July 2018, Kylie was named one of the richest celebrities in the world, being worth a whopping $900 million at 21-years old. Still, people have been debating the claim that she’s “self-made” like Forbes claims. Travis will instill the importance of hard work into Stormi, the same way he had to work to become one of the biggest names in hip-hop.", "annotation_id": "15126232", "url": "https://genius.com/15126232", "state": "accepted", "votes": 158, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Niggas think it’s sweet (Nah, never), it’s on sight (Yeah, what?)", "vault_lines": ["Niggas think it's sweet (Nah, never), it's on sight (Yeah, what?)"], "meaning": "A throwback to his brief beef with Common from 2011. Common had a 2011 song dissing Drake, called “Sweet,” which didn’t mention his name, but it was later confirmed in an interview with SwayInTheMorning that the lines were directed towards Drake.\n\nDrake responded on Rick Ross' 2012 song, “Stay Schemin,” where he raps:\n\nIt bothers me when the gods get to actin' like the broads\nGuess every team doesn’t come complete with niggas like ours\nThat’s why I see no need to compete with niggas like y’all\n\nCommon responded quickly on a remix of “Stay Schemin” and continued to take shots at Drake:\n\nYou so black and white, trying to live a nigga’s life\nI’m taking too long with this amateur guy\nYou ain’t wet nobody, nigga, you Canada dry\n\nThis line could also include a jab at Kanye who he has fired subliminal disses at previously throughout his most recent album at the time, Scorpion. “On Sight” is also the name of the first song off of Kanye’s album, Yeezus. Drake’s version of “on sight” has a different meaning than Kanye’s, which is to engage in violent activity on first glance of another person, while Kanye’s seems to be about police brutality, paparazzi, assumptions, and conclusions. It’s almost certain that a potential shot at Kanye definitely slipped passed Travis as he is dating the sister of Kanye’s wife, Kylie Jenner, who he refers to on the last line of his verse on this track. The Kanye diss could also have made it through considering that Drake’s verse was turned in the same day ASTROWORLD dropped.", "annotation_id": "15125113", "url": "https://genius.com/15125113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "And they chokin’, man, know the crackers wish it was a noose", "vault_lines": ["And they chokin', man, know the crackers wish it was a noose"], "meaning": "The chains Travis has on his neck are as tight as a choker, and he claims that “crackers”—a derogatory term for white people–would rather see him with a rope around his neck.\n\nA noose is a rope with a running knot, most commonly associated with capital punishment by hanging. In the late 19th and early 20th century, many African-Americans were lynched by mobs of white Americans.", "annotation_id": "15124935", "url": "https://genius.com/15124935", "state": "accepted", "votes": 239, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Wet like I’m Lizzie", "vault_lines": ["See the shots that I took (Ayy), wet like I'm Book (Ayy)", "Wet like I'm Lizzie"], "meaning": "After shouting out Devin Booker in the previous line, Drake then gives praises to WNBA star Liz Cambage–who, at the time, played for the Dallas Wings.\n\nAs the previous annotation explains, “wet” is used to describe players who make a lot of shots, and in 2018, Liz broke the WNBA record for most points in a game by scoring 53.\n\n\n\nLiz reacted to the shout-out via Twitter by posting retweet of the audio.", "annotation_id": "15124123", "url": "https://genius.com/15124123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 145, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Different colored chains, think my jeweler really sellin’ fruits", "vault_lines": ["Different colored chains, think my jeweler really sellin' fruits"], "meaning": "In 2017, Travis commisioned a jeweler to make him a colorful “Flame Boy” chain. He once compared his chain to rainbow colored Fruit Loops cereal, on his 2016 track, “sweet sweet”:\n\nShout my town, shout my troops (Shout, shout)\nShout my jeweler, made my chain look like Fruit Loops, yuh (It’s lit)\nCome get a taste, come get a scoop (Scoop)", "annotation_id": "15122666", "url": "https://genius.com/15122666", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Was off the Remy, had a Papoose", "vault_lines": ["Was off the Remy, had a Papoose"], "meaning": "Travis plays on the name “Remy,” which is short for Remy Martin cognac, and also short for Remy Ma, a well-known New York rapper and former member of Terror Squad.\n\nHe also plays on “papoose,” which means “child,” and implies that he conceived his child while intoxicated. On the other hand, Papoose is another New York rapper, who’s been married to Remy Ma since 2008.\n\nPapoose was involved in a short feud with 50 Cent who made flirtatious gestures towards Remy on one of her Instagram posts:\n\nDamn Remy you lost mad weight, hey slim.\n\nPapoose clapped back at 50 almost immediately:\n\nDamn, @DaphneJoy no wedding yet? If @50Cent doesn’t marry you… I’ll introduce you to my homeboy. He’s more mature.", "annotation_id": "15121028", "url": "https://genius.com/15121028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 204, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Ain’t by the book, yeah, this how it look, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Ain't by the book, yeah, this how it look, yeah"], "meaning": "“By the book” means to follow directions. This line ties back to an earlier line where Travis says he doesn’t follow suit. Travis sports eclectic fashion attire and has seemingly pioneered a unique aesthetic of punk-rock rawness blended with drug-fuelled trap music.", "annotation_id": "15119472", "url": "https://genius.com/15119472", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "(Tay Keith, fuck these niggas up)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Tay Keith is a producer from Memphis, Tennessee who’s mostly known for his work with BlocBoy JB. His tag is voiced by Lil Juice who is also from Tennessee. Keith has worked with rappers like Moneybagg Yo, Yo Gotti, Lil Baby, and many more.\n\n“SICKO MODE” marks his first collaboration with Travis Scott, but he has worked with Drake on a couple of occasions. These tracks include “Nonstop” from Drake’s Scorpion, and Keith’s first platinum record, “Look Alive” by BlocBoy JB featuring Drake.", "annotation_id": "15118820", "url": "https://genius.com/15118820", "state": "accepted", "votes": 511, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "’Bout a check, yeah (Check), just check the foots, yeah", "vault_lines": ["'Bout a check, yeah (Check), just check the foots, yeah"], "meaning": "Travis is playing on the Nike logo, which resembles a check. Drake also rapped a similar line earlier on this song:\n\nJesus Christ (Yeah), checks over stripes (Yeah)\n\n\n\nTravis wearing Nike Air Jordan 1 Fragments\n\n\nFurthermore, Travis has an “Air Force 1” collab with Nike that was released on the 10th of August, 2018—a week after the release of this song.", "annotation_id": "15118685", "url": "https://genius.com/15118685", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "(Woo) Made this here with all the ice on in the booth", "vault_lines": ["(Woo) Made this here with all the ice on in the booth"], "meaning": "“Ice” is hip-hop slang for jewelry encrusted with diamonds and gems. Travis is very fond of jewelry; he’s spent around $500,000 on chains for himself and his crew.\n\nAdditionally, he admitted to spending over $1,000,000 on the ‘Astronomia Tourbillon Baguette’, a watch made by Jacob & Co, which is known as one of the most expensive, and accurate watches in the world.", "annotation_id": "15117454", "url": "https://genius.com/15117454", "state": "accepted", "votes": 282, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "I still got scores to settle, man", "vault_lines": ["I still got scores to settle, man"], "meaning": "Drake has pending vendettas. Namely, his beef with Kanye and Pusha T that was reignited in May 2018.\n\nJ. Prince allegedly convinced Drake not to release a “career ending diss record” towards Pusha T. Instead, Drake has been dropping subliminal bars in many of the verses that have been released since Pusha T’s “Infrared” and “The Story of Adidon.” For example, on his track, “8 Out of 10” he raps:\n\nKiss my son on the forehead then kiss your ass goodbye\nAs luck would have it, I’ve settled into my role as the good guy", "annotation_id": "15117008", "url": "https://genius.com/15117008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "All of these O’s I made off records I produced I might take all my exes and put ’em all in a group", "vault_lines": ["All of these O's I made off records I produced", "I might take all my exes and put 'em all in a group"], "meaning": "This line could be about Rihanna. Travis had production and vocals on the 2016 song “Woo,” on her album ANTi, and had production on her hit single, “Bitch Better Have My Money,” which peaked at number 15 on the Billboard Hot 100. Travis and Rihanna dated in 2015.\n\nHe could also be making a general statement that his sound molded the current rap scene.", "annotation_id": "15116712", "url": "https://genius.com/15116712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 197, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "We back on the road, they jumpin’ off, no parachute, yeah Shorty in the back She said she workin’ on her glutes, yeah (Oh my God)", "vault_lines": ["We back on the road, they jumpin' off, no parachute, yeah", "Shorty in the back", "She said she workin' on her glutes, yeah (Oh my God)"], "meaning": "In 2017, Kylie Jenner joined Travis during The DAMN. Tour, after connecting at Coachella. As explained in their joint GQ interview:\n\nCoachella was one of the stops on his tour. So he said, ‘I’m going back on tour—what do we want to do about this?’ Because we obviously liked each other.\n\nApparently, Kylie had to stay in shape on the road and improve her butt through a glute workout.", "annotation_id": "15115871", "url": "https://genius.com/15115871", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "I just landed in, Chase B mixes pop like Jamba Juice", "vault_lines": ["I just landed in, Chase B mixes pop like Jamba Juice"], "meaning": "Chase B is a touring DJ and longtime friend of Travis Scott who has held residencies at nightclubs such as LIV, 1 OAK, and UP & Down.\n\nTravis compares his DJ’s ability to mix pop songs to the mixing involved at the smoothie retailer, Jamba Juice.\n\nIn 2016, Travis and Chase created their own radio show, .WAV Radio, on Apple Music’s Beats 1. Prior to the release of ASTROWORLD, they had not released a new episode since 2017. However, Travis confirmed the return of .WAV in an interview with Zane Lowe on Beats 1 leading up to the release of the album. Chase B also took to Twitter to confirm this.\n\nOn August 3rd, 2018, during a new .WAV Radio episode, Travis and Chase played three unreleased Travis songs that didn’t make it onto ASTROWORLD: “HOUDINI,” “PART TIME,” and “ZOOM.”\n\nFurthermore, three days after the release of his album, Travis Scott posted a picture of Chase B at Jamba Juice on his Instagram, alluding to this lyric.\n\nDuring his October 2018 appearance on Jimmy Fallon’s Tonight Show, Travis revealed that he actively enjoys drinking the juice, which he claims Kylie Jenner introduced him to. “Pop” in this context could also refer to lean, a mixture of  cough medicine with soda commonly referenced in rap. Houston, Texas,  Travis’s hometown, is commonly regarded as the city that popularized the widespread use of lean.", "annotation_id": "15115063", "url": "https://genius.com/15115063", "state": "accepted", "votes": 159, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Nothin’ nice (Yeah), baguettes in my ice (Aw, man)", "vault_lines": ["Nothin' nice (Yeah), baguettes in my ice (Aw, man)"], "meaning": "A baguette-cut, is a square-cut diamond which is thought to resemble the shape of a baguette—a type of French bread. Often used in a ring set or as accent stones for a larger piece, baguettes are cheaper than most other diamond cuts due to their more visible inclusions.", "annotation_id": "15114831", "url": "https://genius.com/15114831", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "She’s in love with who I am Back in high school, I used to bus it to the dance (Yeah) Now I hit the FBO with duffels in my hands", "vault_lines": ["She's in love with who I am", "Back in high school, I used to bus it to the dance (Yeah)", "Now I hit the FBO with duffels in my hands", "Shorty in the back"], "meaning": "An FBO or fixed base operator, is an airport building offering services to clients on private flights. One of the FBO perks is the ability to carry as many bags as possible when entering the plane, unlike most commercial airlines which only allow one complimentary carry on bag.\n\nDrake reflects on his humble high school days where he had to use public transportation to travel—now, he takes private flights whenever he likes.", "annotation_id": "15114583", "url": "https://genius.com/15114583", "state": "accepted", "votes": 469, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Now it’s 4 a.m. and I’m back up poppin’ with the crew", "vault_lines": ["Now it's 4 a.m. and I'm back up poppin' with the crew"], "meaning": "A nod to Travis' verse on 2 Chainz’s 2017 song “4 AM,” where Travis raps:\n\nOkay, you popped up on me by surprise (Yeah, yeah)\nYou see I never took you for the poppin' type (Straight up)\nDamn, it’s 4 a.m. so please believe the hype (It’s lit)", "annotation_id": "15114294", "url": "https://genius.com/15114294", "state": "accepted", "votes": 155, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Sun is down, freezin’ cold That’s how we already know, winter’s here", "vault_lines": ["Sun is down, freezin' cold", "That's how we already know, winter's here"], "meaning": "Drake’s hometown of Toronto, Ontario, Canada usually starts to reach winter temperatures in the middle of November. Around November 20, 2017, meteorological reports registered negative Celsius temperatures there, long before the official start of winter on December 21.\n\nAs winter hits, the sun starts to set earlier, and with less sunlight comes lower temperatures. On November 20, the sun in Toronto set around 4:45 PM/EST, and rose in the morning at 7:20 AM.", "annotation_id": "15114078", "url": "https://genius.com/15114078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 444, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "In the 305, bitches treat me like I’m Uncle Luke (Don’t stop, pop that pussy!)", "vault_lines": ["In the 305, bitches treat me like I'm Uncle Luke"], "meaning": "“305” is the area code for Miami, Florida, where “Uncle Luke,” a member of the rap group 2 Live Crew, resides. In 1992 Luke released “I Wanna Rock (Doo Doo Brown),” which happens to be sampled after this bar:\n\nDon’t stop pop that pussy\nLet me see you doo doo brown", "annotation_id": "15113996", "url": "https://genius.com/15113996", "state": "accepted", "votes": 389, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "It’s absolute, yeah (Yeah), I’m back, reboot (It’s lit) LaFerrari to Jamba Juice, yeah (Skrrt, skrrt)", "vault_lines": ["It's absolute, yeah (Yeah), I'm back, reboot (It's lit)", "LaFerrari to Jamba Juice, yeah (Skrrt, skrrt)"], "meaning": "A LaFerrari is a luxury car model, manufactured by Ferrari. In February 2018, Travis Scott gifted his girlfriend Kylie Jenner a rare $1.4 million model of this car, celebrating the birth of their baby, Stormi.\n\nHe also mentions the car on the fourteenth track of ASTROWORLD, “WHO? WHAT!”:\n\nBrand new LaFerrari (woo), my bitch ride iconic\n\nJamba Juice is a juice bar company, founded in 1990, that specializes in fruit bowls, baked goods and most prominently, smoothies. The same month Travis purchased the Ferrari, he and Kylie were photographed there. Travis extrapolated on his apparent love for Jamba Juice during his October 2018 appearance on Jimmy Fallon’s Tonight Show, which he claims Kylie Jenner introduced him to.", "annotation_id": "15113962", "url": "https://genius.com/15113962", "state": "accepted", "votes": 162, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "She said, “Where we goin’?” I said, “The moon”", "vault_lines": ["She said, \"Where we goin'?\" I said, \"The moon\""], "meaning": "Travis pays homage to Kid Cudi by alluding to his song “Dose of Dopeness.” In the song, Cudi asks a woman out to eat and when asked where they’re going he replies:\n\nThe moon…\n\nTravis and Kid Cudi collaborated on 2016’s “through the late night” and “way back.” Travis has said multiple times that Cudi was a large influence on him, going as far as to say:\n\nI idolize Kid Cudi a lot. That’s like, my favorite artist.\n\nTravis also has a 2013 song called “Dance on the Moon.”", "annotation_id": "15113913", "url": "https://genius.com/15113913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 177, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Yeah, yeah, yeah Goin’ on you with the pick and roll", "vault_lines": ["Goin' on you with the pick and roll"], "meaning": "“Pick and roll” is a basketball term used to describe the action of a teammate setting a screen (blocking the defender’s lane), and then “rolling” off the defender to receive a pass from his teammate.\n\n\n\nTravis metaphorically receives a pass from Drake, as the beat cuts out and Travis takes over.", "annotation_id": "15113868", "url": "https://genius.com/15113868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 297, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "See the shots that I took (Ayy), wet like I’m Book (Ayy)", "vault_lines": ["See the shots that I took (Ayy), wet like I'm Book (Ayy)", "Wet like I'm Lizzie"], "meaning": "Devin Booker is a young shooting guard for the Phoenix Suns, who won the 2018 NBA All-Star 3 point contest, and scored 70 points in a game versus the Celtics on March 24, 2017 – the first player to score 70+ points since Kobe’s 81 point game on January 22, 2006. Booker and Drake have shown each other mutual respect in the past: Drake wore Booker’s jersey to his Phoenix show, and Booker responding on Twitter, calling him his “brother.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LDbeAjbkp0Q\n\n“Wet” is a term for a player who makes a lot of shots. Drake’s “shots” are the bars he makes on his diss tracks. Whether it be against Meek Mill, JAY-Z, Pusha T, or anyone else Drake has gone against, he feels like he’s hit his shots enough to deem them “wet.”", "annotation_id": "15113854", "url": "https://genius.com/15113854", "state": "accepted", "votes": 202, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "I did half a Xan, thirteen hours ’til I land Had me out like a light, ayy, yeah Like a light, ayy, yeah", "vault_lines": ["I did half a Xan, thirteen hours 'til I land", "Had me out like a light, ayy, yeah", "Had me out like a light (Like a light)", "Like a light (Like a light)"], "meaning": "Xanax is a prescription drug that is used to treat anxiety. “Out like a light” is an old expression meaning to be in deep sleep.\n\nDrake has mentioned his use of Xanax on flights before, on the 2014 track “Days In The East”:\n\nTake a flight here, pop a xan for the boy\n\nDrake could be referring to his trip to Dubai in March 2018, which is just under 13 hours of flight time from his home city of Toronto. Drake’s love for Dubai is no secret.\n\n\n\nThe flow of the lines is adopted from Famous Dex’s hit song “JAPAN”:\n\nI just popped a xan, fifty thousand in Japan (fifty thousand in Japan)", "annotation_id": "15113838", "url": "https://genius.com/15113838", "state": "accepted", "votes": 464, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "’Bout to turn this function to Bonnaroo Told her, “Hop in, you comin’ too”", "vault_lines": ["'Bout to turn this function to Bonnaroo", "Told her, \"Hop in, you comin' too\""], "meaning": "The Bonnaroo Music and Arts Festival, is an annual four-day music festival that takes place in Manchester, Tennessee. Travis performed at the festival in 2017.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fpEhTa7A6rM", "annotation_id": "15113802", "url": "https://genius.com/15113802", "state": "accepted", "votes": 97, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "This shit way too big, when we pull up, give me the loot (Gimme the loot!)", "vault_lines": ["This shit way too big, when we pull up, give me the loot (Gimme the loot!)"], "meaning": "Travis quotes the chorus of The Notorious B.I.G.’s 1994 track, “Gimme the Loot.” The ad-lib on this line is a sample from the same song.\n\nGimme the loot, gimme the loot\n\n“Way too big” ties to The Notorious B.I.G.’s name.", "annotation_id": "15113781", "url": "https://genius.com/15113781", "state": "accepted", "votes": 513, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Young La Flame, he in sicko mode", "vault_lines": ["Young La Flame, he in sicko mode"], "meaning": "“La Flame” is a nickname for Travis Scott, derived from Travis' 2013 song, “Blocka La Flame.”\n\nDrake and Trav have collaborated prior to “SICKO MODE” on 2015’s “Company,” and more recently, on 2017’s “Portland.”\n\nDrake uses the term “sicko” (or 6icko) to describe his friends, but in this context, it’s referring to the rager mentality for which Travis is known.\n\nDrake previously referred to himself as a “sicko” on “Summer Sixteen”:\n\nI’m just a sicko, a real sicko when you get to know me, nigga", "annotation_id": "15113778", "url": "https://genius.com/15113778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 489, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Jesus Christ (Yeah), checks over stripes (Yeah) That’s what I like (Yeah), that’s what we like (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Jesus Christ (Yeah), checks over stripes (Yeah)", "That's what I like (Yeah), that's what we like (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Nike’s logo is a checkmark, while the Adidas logo features three parallel stripes. Drake and Travis Scott have both released Nike sneaker collaborations under the standard Nike and Jordan umbrellas.\n\n\n\nIn early 2018, it was suspected that Drake’s allegiances had switched from Nike to Adidas, and that he had an upcoming collection with Adidas that was yet to be unveiled. However, any plans for a collection that may have been in the works were dashed when Pusha-T dropped the diss track “The Story of Adidon” in May, exposing Drake’s intentions to publicly unveil his son Adonis with the “Adidon” collection. A month later, Drake was seen celebrating the release of Scorpion in a Nike tracksuit.\n\nPreferring checks over stripes could also allude to prioritizing the acquisition of wealth—i.e. checks—over earning street credibility, or “earning one’s stripes.” Thus, this may be considered a subliminal diss toward Pusha T, who discussed  “earning stripes” on “If You Know You Know”:\n\nWhere were you when Big Meech brought the tigers in?\n‘Cause I was busy earnin’ stripes like a tiger’s skin\n\nKanye West mentioned this line in a December 2018 Twitter rant about his relationship with Drake, perceiving it as a subliminal diss aimed at him. Drake went on to confirm this in his December 2019 Rap Radar interview:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/mmsasEoupO4?t=4590", "annotation_id": "15113702", "url": "https://genius.com/15113702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 364, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "When I shoot my shot, that shit wetty like I’m Sheck (Bitch)", "vault_lines": ["When I shoot my shot, that shit wetty like I'm Sheck (Bitch)"], "meaning": "Drake pays homage to Cactus Jack signee and Harlem rapper Sheck Wes. The young artist gained significant attention with the release of his single “Mo Bamba”, named after his friend, a basketballer who plays for the Orlando Magic. Sheck Wes himself is also known for being a very good basketball player. This explains Drakes use of “wetty” – a term derived from “wet” which is used to refer to a player who lands good shots (the ball “splashes” into the net).\n\nThis line features Sheck Wes' adlib, “bitch!” from the same track and is a response to Sheck Wes' shoutout to Drake on “Mo Bamba”:\n\nCall me Drake how a nigga controlla\n\nTravis brought Sheck Wes out at his inaugural Governors Ball set in July 2018 and the two performed “Mo Bamba” together.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gxL_qoIGTHI&t=1920s\n\nAdditionally, Sheck Wes is featured on the track “NO BYSTANDERS” along with Juice WRLD.", "annotation_id": "15113693", "url": "https://genius.com/15113693", "state": "accepted", "votes": 275, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Yeah, this shit way too formal, y’all know I don’t follow suit", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, this shit way too formal, y'all know I don't follow suit"], "meaning": "In their joint interview with GQ, Kylie described Travis' feelings toward the spotlight:\n\nHe was whispering to me the whole time. He just doesn’t like taking the photos.\n\nThe interview continued, saying Travis hates anything that “slows him down.”\n\nAlthough Travis claims to not like the celebrity life, he was spotted out with Kylie Jenner at such mega-events as the 2018 Met Gala.\n\n\n\nThis is also wordplay between the word “suit”—a type of formal attire—and the phrase “follow suit,” which means ‘to conform to society’s rules.’ An example of this was when he encouraged his fans, informally known as “ragers,” to bypass security and rush the stage during his set at the 2015 Lollapalooza music festival, leading to his subsequent arrest on charges of inciting a riot.", "annotation_id": "15113685", "url": "https://genius.com/15113685", "state": "accepted", "votes": 148, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Stacey Dash, most of these girls ain’t got a clue", "vault_lines": ["Stacey Dash, most of these girls ain't got a clue"], "meaning": "Actress Stacey Dash costars as “Dionne” alongside Alicia Silverstone in the 1995 film Clueless.\n\n\n\nDash is controversial within the black community for her conservative political stance, and Travis may consider her opinions to be naive.\n\nThis line may also play off of the previous one, as Stacey Dash’s character wore a suit in Clueless, but hers was less iconic than the yellow one that Alicia Silverstone wore.", "annotation_id": "15113668", "url": "https://genius.com/15113668", "state": "accepted", "votes": 194, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Baby mama cover Forbes, got these other bitches shook Yeah", "vault_lines": ["Baby mama cover Forbes, got these other bitches shook"], "meaning": "Kylie Jenner—the mother of Travis' daughter Stormi—was featured on the cover of the August 2018 edition of Forbes magazine. The magazine issue, which covers 60 of the richest self-made women notes:\n\nAt 21, [Kylie] is set to be the youngest ever self-made billionaire.\n\n\n\nKylie landed #27 on the Forbes list. Thanks to her make up company Kylie Cosmetics, Kylie’s brand has sold over 630 million products making her $330 million in 2017. In addition to that, Kylie makes millions from TV programs like Keeping Up with the Kardashians as well as endorsements from products such as Puma and Pacsun.", "annotation_id": "15113651", "url": "https://genius.com/15113651", "state": "accepted", "votes": 328, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "line": "Yeah, Jump Out boys, that’s Nike boys, hoppin’ out coupes", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, Jump Out boys, that's Nike boys, hoppin' out coupes"], "meaning": "“Jump Out boys” is a phrase usually attributed to the cops. It arose due to groups of police jumping out of vans to subdue suspects. Travis uses it to denote him and a group of friends hopping out of luxury vehicles, wearing fresh shoes and clothing.\n\nTravis is signed to the Nike Air Jordan brand. His first Jordan, which he designed to commemorate the Houston Oilers, released on June 9, 2018. The Jumpman logo appears on almost all Air Jordan silhouettes and could also be a reason for the phrase “Jump Out boys.”\n\n\n\nIt’s also worth noting Travis' use of the phrase “Nike boys”—he mentioned the brand in one of his older tweets and rapped a similar line on 2017’s  “Ghostface Killers”:\n\nNike boys, we don’t do three stripes (Yeah)", "annotation_id": "15113630", "url": "https://genius.com/15113630", "state": "accepted", "votes": 228, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/552ee994ceab813da08e85f101e64bc9.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"SICKO MODE\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>SICKO MODETravis Scott10.3M", "status": "done", "targets": 48, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Travis-scott-sicko-mode-lyrics", "genius_title": "SICKO MODE", "genius_id": 3876994, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 Hours", "line": "Expedition was Eddie Bauer edition", "vault_lines": ["Expedition was Eddie Bauer edition"], "meaning": "Referring to a special edition of the Ford Expedition, a large SUV produced by the Ford Motor Company. The Eddie Bauer edition was a trim level that featured unique styling cues, interior features, and sometimes additional options or packages. Eddie Bauer is a well-known American outdoor brand, and their collaboration with Ford resulted in specific design elements and branding being incorporated into the Expedition model. Typically, the Eddie Bauer edition of the Expedition offered a blend of ruggedness and luxury, with features and finishes inspired by the outdoor lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "29982144", "url": "https://genius.com/29982144", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 Hours", "line": "Checkin’ MediaTakeOut", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "MediaTakeOut is a popular online gossip and entertainment news outlet known for its coverage of celebrity news, rumors, and controversies.", "annotation_id": "29982127", "url": "https://genius.com/29982127", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "30 Hours", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Kanye-west-and-drake-30-hours-drake-reference-lyrics", "genius_title": "30 Hours (Drake Reference)", "genius_id": 6168379, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "When I see that thing move, I just wish we would fight less and we would talk more", "vault_lines": ["When I see that thing move, I just wish we would fight less and we would talk more"], "meaning": "While he’s suggesting that the sight of his woman’s beautiful body makes him want to stop fighting and communicate, ‘that thing’ could actually be a reference to Lauryn Hill’s smash single, “Doo-Wop (That Thing)”.\n\nOn that track, she raps:\n\n“The second verse is dedicated to the man,\nwho’s more concerned with his Timbs\nand his rims than his woman.”\n\nDrake rapping on the second verse of this track while mentioning this theme could be playing into Poetic Justice being a diss track towards him. The title of the album that featured this single was “The Mis-Education of Lauryn Hill,” and Kendrick did mention on The Heart pt 3, that his album was “the newer Miseducation.”", "annotation_id": "39632407", "url": "https://genius.com/39632407", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "You called up your girlfriends and y’all curled in that little bitty Range,", "vault_lines": ["You called up your girlfriends and y'all curled in that little bitty Range,"], "meaning": "After they’ve had a bit of champagne, it sounds like they’re ready for a Girls Night on the Town. Instead of just talking about her people, he’s suggesting they spend some time having fun together.", "annotation_id": "39632366", "url": "https://genius.com/39632366", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "I heard that", "vault_lines": ["If I told you that a flower bloomed in a dark room, would you trust it?", "I really wanna show you off, fuck that", "Every second, every minute, man, I swear that she can get it", "When I see that thing move, I just wish we would fight less and we would talk more", "Sex drive when you swerve, I want that interference, it's coherent, I can hear it, mhm", "Get out the car 'fore I snatch you out that motherfucker, homie"], "meaning": "Along with the use of fragrance and taste, this looks to be continuing a theme around the 5 senses.\n\nIt could also be a theme around the the Core Language Skills with speaking (told), writing, and reading (from the 3rd verse).", "annotation_id": "39632262", "url": "https://genius.com/39632262", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "That’s your heartbeat, it either caught me or it called me, mhm", "vault_lines": ["That's your heartbeat, it either caught me or it called me, mhm"], "meaning": "This sounds like Kendrick is referencing his Heart Series. At this time, he would have just released The Heart Pt 3 recently to help create more hype around the release of good kid, m.A.A.d. city.", "annotation_id": "39632212", "url": "https://genius.com/39632212", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Good God, what you doin’ that walk for?", "vault_lines": ["Good God, what you doin' that walk for?"], "meaning": "The walk she does always catches Drake’s eye", "annotation_id": "39554045", "url": "https://genius.com/39554045", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Maybe one day, when you figure out you’re gonna need someone", "vault_lines": ["Maybe one day, when you figure out you're gonna need someone"], "meaning": "Drake is saying maybe one day the girl he is rapping about will realize she will NEED one man in the future", "annotation_id": "39554025", "url": "https://genius.com/39554025", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "She wanna go and party, she wanna go and party", "vault_lines": ["She wanna go and party, she wanna go and party"], "meaning": "The girl Kendrick is rapping about wants to go to a party, as she enjoys them", "annotation_id": "39554019", "url": "https://genius.com/39554019", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "[Verse 1: Kendrick Lamar]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the first verse / segment of the track, and Kendrick Lamar is doing it", "annotation_id": "39554012", "url": "https://genius.com/39554012", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Big ass in that sundress, ooh", "vault_lines": ["Your natural hair and your soft skin and your big ass in that sundress, ooh"], "meaning": "ass mustve been huge", "annotation_id": "39043608", "url": "https://genius.com/39043608", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "(In the thundering rain)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This sample can be heard throughout the song, and is taken from Janet Jackson’s “Any Time, Any Place.”", "annotation_id": "38328782", "url": "https://genius.com/38328782", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "(In the thundering rain)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This sample can be heard throughout the song, and is taken from Janet Jackson’s “Any Time, Any Place.”", "annotation_id": "38328781", "url": "https://genius.com/38328781", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "You were so new to this life, but goddamn, you got adjusted", "vault_lines": ["You were so new to this life, but goddamn, you got adjusted"], "meaning": "???", "annotation_id": "38254794", "url": "https://genius.com/38254794", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": false, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Pour up plenty of champagne, cold nights when you curse this name", "vault_lines": ["Pour up plenty of champagne, cold nights when you curse this name"], "meaning": "The first part of this bar sets the mood for a fun evening. Pouring up “Champagne” is usually a sign of a good time, especially when you’re with a group of girlfriends.\n\nThe second half of this bar sends a couple of shots… the first subliminals on this song.\n\n“Cold Nights when you curse THIS name”\n\nCould be seen as sending shots to Drake, whose IG NAME is “Champagne” Papi… Kendrick could be describing the feeling women have after dealing with Drake to the point they want to curse his name.\n\nThis could also be a loose shot at J Cole (Cold), who has a popular mixtape titled Friday “Night” Lights.", "annotation_id": "38037752", "url": "https://genius.com/38037752", "state": "pending", "votes": -7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Read slow and you’ll find gold mines in these lines", "vault_lines": ["Read slow and you'll find gold mines in these lines"], "meaning": "As is the case with most of Kendrick’s music, if you pay enough attention and analyze what he writes, you’ll truly appreciate the quality of his lyrics. Such is the case on this record, where he deploys complex wordplay and metaphors.", "annotation_id": "36917786", "url": "https://genius.com/36917786", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "(In the thundering rain)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This sample can be heard throughout the song, and is taken from Janet Jackson’s “Any Time, Any Place”.", "annotation_id": "34927583", "url": "https://genius.com/34927583", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "And they say communication save relations, I can tell", "vault_lines": ["And they say communication save relations, I can tell"], "meaning": "This line connects to the line above from Verse 1: “They say conversation rule a nation.” There’s a double meaning here, one relating to the importance of communication in a romantic relationship and one relating to communication as antidote to gang warfare, which is depicted in the music video.", "annotation_id": "26670768", "url": "https://genius.com/26670768", "state": "pending", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "When you figure out it’s alright here in the city and you don’t run from where we come from", "vault_lines": ["When you figure out it's alright here in the city and you don't run from where we come from"], "meaning": "This line is used ironically in the music video, which shows people running from a shooter as this line is being delivered. In other words, it’s not “alright here in the city;” maybe there’s good reason to run.", "annotation_id": "26670759", "url": "https://genius.com/26670759", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "I really wanna show you off, fuck that", "vault_lines": ["I really wanna show you off, fuck that"], "meaning": "The way he pronounces this line is the same as when he says it in “A.D.H.D”, where it sounds like “fuck thought” or “fuck Dot” (as in K-Dot). In other words, he isn’t overthinking his relationship with Sherane; he might actually be underthinking it in a way that will have negative consequences for him later.", "annotation_id": "26670558", "url": "https://genius.com/26670558", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "And I know your taste is a little bit, mmm, high maintenance, uh", "vault_lines": ["And I know your taste is a little bit, mmm, high maintenance, uh"], "meaning": "Kendrick continues his description of Sherane using his senses here, but there is also a double meaning referring to Sherane’s expensive tastes. She can afford to be high maintenance because she is attractive and she knows it. Her indulgent tastes may also be a subtle nod to the previous track, “Money Trees”.", "annotation_id": "26669297", "url": "https://genius.com/26669297", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Say if you a bad bitch, put your hands up high Hands up high, hands up high Tell ’em dim the lights down right now, put me in the mood I’m talkin’ ’bout dark room, perfume Go, go", "vault_lines": ["Say if you a bad bitch, put your hands up high", "Hands up high, hands up high", "Tell 'em dim the lights down right now, put me in the mood", "I'm talkin' 'bout dark room, perfume"], "meaning": "These lines show Kendrick’s appreciation for attractive females and how he wants to be in his “mood” by drenching them in perfume and placing them in a darkroom. A darkroom is a room with zero normal light present and is commonly used as a popular photographic technique. The intro to “Poetic Justice” may also serve as a representation of his teenage desires as shown on “The Art of Peer Pressure”.\n\nBasketball shorts with the Gonzales Park odor\nWe on the mission for bad bitches and trouble\n\nThis may also depict his lust for Sherane as expressed throughout the majority of good kid, m.A.A.d city. Ultimately, one of the major thoughts that circle around K.Dot’s brain as a teenager is having intercourse with her as prominently expressed on the opening track of the album, mainly since he considers her to be a “hoodrat”.", "annotation_id": "12958899", "url": "https://genius.com/12958899", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Better yet, where your friends and ’em? I really wanna know you all", "vault_lines": ["Better yet, where your friends and 'em? I really wanna know you all"], "meaning": "By asking Sherane to introduce her friends to him, Kendrick adheres to the “be-friends-with-your-lover’s-friends” mentality. However, his intentions are likely more sinful, in hope of manipulating her to arrange some kind of ménage.", "annotation_id": "8680419", "url": "https://genius.com/8680419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 58, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "[Skit]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "According to the liner notes, the track was produced by Scoop DeVille with additional production by Sounwave. Scoop also produced the bonus track “The Recipe.”\n\nBelow is a video of Scoop DeVille breaking down his part of the production:\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_detailpage&v=kdZJhiuXZvs#t=207s\n\nThe beat is sampled from Janet Jackson’s “Any Time, Any Place,” and Scoop DeVille said in an interview with Complex magazine that the sample was “a special piece that I was saving for the right artist.” The sample can be heard below:\n\nhttp://youtu.be/RZ8a9wng3b0?t=48s\n\nFun Fact: Janet Jackson starred in the 1993 movie, Poetic Justice, alongside Tupac Shakur, a role model of Kendrick’s.", "annotation_id": "2351888", "url": "https://genius.com/2351888", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "[Intro: Kendrick Lamar]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "https://soundcloud.com/rapgenius/kendrick-on-poetic-justice\nI mean, that record considers itself as a radio record, or whatever you make it out to be, a hit record or whatever. But for the most part, that song was still in the concept of the first track on the album, which was Sherane. So it had its time right there but for the most part it covered everything as far as the concept of dealing with a promiscuous woman.", "annotation_id": "2339634", "url": "https://genius.com/2339634", "state": "verified", "votes": 512, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "If I told you that a flower bloomed in a dark room, would you trust it?", "vault_lines": ["If I told you that a flower bloomed in a dark room, would you trust it?"], "meaning": "https://soundcloud.com/rapgenius/kendrick-on-if-a-flower\nAm I the flower? No, I am thinking she is the flower the whole time because I have no idea about her background, just being oblivious to it. Being young and not knowing. She may look good on the outside, but this flower may be full of bees and no honey. You feel me?", "annotation_id": "2339630", "url": "https://genius.com/2339630", "state": "verified", "votes": 1146, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "You’re in the mood for empathy, it’s blood in my pen", "vault_lines": ["You're in the mood for empathy, it's blood in my pen"], "meaning": "https://soundcloud.com/rapgenius/kendrick-on-youre-in-the\nI don’t want it to be so one-sided. I want you to feel my perspective but at the same time know where she is coming from. She may come from a broken home where she wasn’t taught actual home training and dealing with men. I mean, not to actually say that in a blunt manner, but when I do a record like Poetic Justice, you see that she does have something about her that is attractive, and I really just want to show both sides of the fence. I never wanted to really completely slander her all the way down, so that is a good point. I am glad you caught that, and it is real tricky and that is something that I thought about writing that record, knowing the history that me and her had.", "annotation_id": "2339626", "url": "https://genius.com/2339626", "state": "verified", "votes": 587, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Kendrick told Complex about Drizzy:\n\nMe and Drake had been working for a while off the fact that I was on his tour and I was on Take Care. We were talking about what joints we wanted to do. When Scoop shot me the track, I immediately came up with the concept of doing that vibe and the first person I had in mind was Drake. Drake killed it and it came out crazy. I think that’s going to sit for a while.", "annotation_id": "2302454", "url": "https://genius.com/2302454", "state": "accepted", "votes": 208, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Every second, every minute, man, I swear that she can get it", "vault_lines": ["Every second, every minute, man, I swear that she can get it"], "meaning": "The screwed-up voice is saying his girl can get it any time, and is likely young Kendrick’s thoughts about how badly he wants Sherane.\n\nThere is a Janet Jackson sample, and during the intro it includes “Any time, any place”", "annotation_id": "1258100", "url": "https://genius.com/1258100", "state": "accepted", "votes": 136, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Sincerely, yours truly, and right before you go blind, PS", "vault_lines": ["Sincerely, yours truly, and right before you go blind, PS"], "meaning": "This may be an allusion to the common phrase, “love is blind.” Kendrick uses this statement to tie together the theme of love that is present in this verse.\n\nFurthermore, he’s referencing the idiom “justice is blind,” meaning that truth and justice will be meted out without bias.", "annotation_id": "1186401", "url": "https://genius.com/1186401", "state": "accepted", "votes": 112, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "A fatal attraction is common, and what we have common is pain", "vault_lines": ["A fatal attraction is common, and what we have common is pain"], "meaning": "According to Psychology Today:\n\nEssentially, fatal attraction occurs when the specific behavior/feature that drew you to another person is the same behavior/feature that causes the two of you to break-up.\n\nThere is some wordplay here as well: something “fatal” can be causative of death and, therefore, “pain.” Referring back to the Sherane narrative, this fatal attraction quite literally led to someone’s death, namely, Dave, during “Sing About Me, I’m Dying of Thirst.”\n\n\n\nThis could also refer to a 1987 thriller-romance called Fatal Attraction, where an insane woman returns to haunt her lover in scornful revenge.\n\nThis song’s visuals depict a fatal attraction between a Crips-affiliated woman and a Bloods-affiliated man, ending with the two being gunned down. In addition to being a modern-day Romeo & Juliet, it may be a broader statement about the fatal attraction of dangerous lifestyles.", "annotation_id": "1158876", "url": "https://genius.com/1158876", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "I mean, you need to hear this", "vault_lines": ["I mean, you need to hear this"], "meaning": "People need to realise love just doesn’t happen. Again with the emphasis on hearing possibly the key line in the song. Tell that to John Mayer.\n\nSee “Real” from good kid, m.A.A.d. city for an expounding on this repetitive lyric.\n\nLove is also a noun, not just a verb. Cheers, Professor K Dot.", "annotation_id": "1158588", "url": "https://genius.com/1158588", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Makin’ sure my punctuation curve, every letter here’s true Livin’ my life in the margin and that metaphor was proof", "vault_lines": ["Makin' sure my punctuation curve, every letter here's true", "Livin' my life in the margin and that metaphor was proof"], "meaning": "Kendrick poetically justifies why his writing is vital to hip-hop. To explain this, he sneaks 4 literary mechanisms into the inner-workings of his rhymes (punctuation, letter, margin, metaphor):\n\n\n“make sure my punctuation is curved”, i.e. a comma, which is curved, instead of a period, which would signify a full stop. i.e. Kendrick doesn’t stop!\nHis use of “letter” not only emphasizes that each and every character he writes is truthful, but it also refers to the fact that each verse of the song is structured as a letter, as evidenced by his sign-off and the postscripts.\nKendrick “lives his life in the margin”, which is literally the small blank space at the end of the lines he writes. Metaphorically he lives his life in the periphery outside the masses.\n“Proof,” as in proofread. One often writes in the margin of the page when he or she is proofreading. But also, a mathematical ‘proof’ to show the answer and conclusion to your equation. Looks like Kendrick’s equation here is sound…", "annotation_id": "1148769", "url": "https://genius.com/1148769", "state": "accepted", "votes": 242, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "That sound like poetic justice, poetic justice", "vault_lines": ["With poetic justice, poetic justice", "That sound like poetic justice, poetic justice"], "meaning": "Drake is using the title of the song, “Poetic Justice,” to describe the hypothetical idea that the woman he was pursuing would realize the good Drake is doing for her. If she is to ever come to this realization as well as figuring out what she really needs in life and remembering that it’s all in the city she grew up in, Drake would describe that as “justice”.\n\nThe woman is receiving poetic justice, or karma: getting what she deserves. She cheated on him with another man while he was planning expensive trips for her and her mother, but soon she’ll realize she needs him still.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/253556428680351744", "annotation_id": "1147670", "url": "https://genius.com/1147670", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "But I could never right my wrongs ’less I write it down for real, PS", "vault_lines": ["But I could never right my wrongs 'less I write it down for real, PS", "But I can never right my wrongs unless I write 'em down for real, PS"], "meaning": "The usage of this post script (P.S.) is to segue into the chorus by extending the play on the words write/right.\n\nThis is a play on words using the “right/write” homophones… very similar to what Kanye West used in the end of the second verse in “Touch the Sky”:\n\nI’m tryin' to right my wrongs\nIt’s funny, them same wrongs help me write the song\n\nThis shows the irony of being unable to right his wrongdoings and negative experiences of his youth unless he “writes them down for real”. Everything he spits is real or has happened and the only way to right his wrongs is by writing them.\n\nIt could be a play on the line before about him communicating with us through his lyrics and hip-hop, and sharing it with other people so we can all learn and relate. It’s up to our generation to save this nation and Kendrick does so by creating music for the masses.", "annotation_id": "1145862", "url": "https://genius.com/1145862", "state": "accepted", "votes": 174, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "They say conversation rule a nation, I can tell", "vault_lines": ["They say conversation rule a nation, I can tell"], "meaning": "Kendrick gives a nod to the Conscious Rapper school of thought – that rap (and words in general) have real power and ability to influence people, governments, nations, incite revolution, and create change. Pac and many of his followers understood his own calling in this way – leading to conspiracy theories after his death that the government got him offed to silence his philosophy. Kim’s wise words on the subject.\n\nThis is also a concept in the wider literary, philosophical world-but here Kendrick uses it on a smaller scale to illustrate how he can get a woman’s attention on him just by talking to her.\n\nIt’s also a pretty sly wordplay – “conversation rule a nation, I can tell", "annotation_id": "1144462", "url": "https://genius.com/1144462", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Love is not just a verb and I can see power steerin’ Sex drive when you swerve, I want that interference, it’s coherent, I can hear it, mhm", "vault_lines": ["And I know just, know just, know just, know just, know just what you want", "Love is not just a verb, it's you lookin' in the mirror", "Love is not just a verb and I can see power steerin'", "Sex drive when you swerve, I want that interference, it's coherent, I can hear it, mhm"], "meaning": "Power steering = troubles\nSex drive when she swerves = Horny girl when she drinks\nInterference = He wants to take care of the troubles\n\nThe girl is troubled and resorts to alcohol as a solution, but Kendrick believes he’s all she needs. It also could mean the way she rides the D, it’s like she’s power steering it. She “swerves” or i.e. she can ride a mean dick.\n\nHer interfering signals, (moans), are clear, which brings us to the Wave theory: Coherence is a special property of waves, (in this case sound waves), which makes it possible for the waves to interfere in a unique way with each other. In the lyrics, he hears her coherent moaning sounds interfere, (he perceives them louder than a ordinary moan).\n\nSex Drive is also a reference to fellow Black Hippy member Schoolboy Q’s track of that name which came out a few months before GKMC.", "annotation_id": "1144304", "url": "https://genius.com/1144304", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "I’m gon’ ask you one more time, homie Where is you from? Or it is a problem Ayy, ayy, ayy, ayy, ayy, ask him if he over here for Sherane Ayy, you over here for Sherane, homie? I don’t care who this nigga over here for If he don’t tell me where he from, it’s a wrap, I’m sorry Hold up, hold up, hold up, hold up, we gon’ do it like this, okay? I’ma tell you where I’m from, okay? You gon’ tell me where you from, okay? Or, or, or where your grandma stay Where your mama stay, or where your daddy stay, okay? Fuck all this talkin’ As a matter fact, get out the van, homie Get out the car ’fore I snatch you out that motherfucker, homie", "vault_lines": ["I'm gon' ask you one more time, homie", "Where is you from? Or it is a problem", "Ayy, ayy, ayy, ayy, ayy, ask him if he over here for Sherane", "Ayy, you over here for Sherane, homie?", "I don't care who this nigga over here for", "If he don't tell me where he from, it's a wrap, I'm sorry", "Hold up, hold up, hold up, hold up, we gon' do it like this, okay?", "I'ma tell you where I'm from, okay?", "You gon' tell me where you from, okay?", "Or, or, or where your grandma stay", "Where your mama stay, or where your daddy stay, okay?", "Fuck all this talkin'", "As a matter fact, get out the van, homie", "Get out the car 'fore I snatch you out that motherfucker, homie"], "meaning": "This skit links back to the final line of “Sherane a.k.a. Master Splinter’s Daughter,” where K.dot was on his way to Sherane’s house but got stopped by two guys in black hoodies. The ghostly vocal sample from “Sherane” plays over the opening of the skit, signifying the return to “present day,” and the end of the extended flashback that began with the skit at the end of “Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe.”\n\nThe conversation follows the two hooded guys interrogating Kendrick because they don’t like that he’s on their turf, and are definitely not a fan of his relationship with Sherane. Kendrick is obviously staying in the van and silent trying to avoid confrontation but it doesn’t go down like that…\n\nHe finds himself away from his hood, his desire of meeting with Sherane can’t be fulfilled because he’s blocked by these two guys who don’t recognize him from around there.\n\nThis skit appears to depict the chorus of the track “m.A.A.d city” in where ScHoolboy Q asks the same questions of “Where you from” and “Where you stay.” They both appear to be similar depictions of the same incident, however, the version in this song is much more realistic and sounds as if it was captured on a tape recorder.", "annotation_id": "1143559", "url": "https://genius.com/1143559", "state": "accepted", "votes": 577, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "I mean, you need to hear this Love is not just a verb, it’s you lookin’ in the mirror Love is not just a verb, it’s you lookin’ for it, maybe, call me crazy, we can both be insane", "vault_lines": ["Clearly a lot for me to take in,", "I mean, you need to hear this", "Love is not just a verb, it's you lookin' in the mirror", "Love is not just a verb, it's you lookin' for it, maybe, call me crazy, we can both be insane", "Love is not just a verb and I can see power steerin'"], "meaning": "Love is not just sex, and love is way more than an action or feeling, it is many things.\n\nKendrick uses 2 analogies to cut some of the ambiguity: It’s looking in the mirror, loving thyself, and it’s actively looking for it, rather than waiting for it to come.\n*In other words, you have to do some soul searching to find out what it means to you personally.\n\nAnother track from GKMC, “Real,” expounds in its entirety on this motif of what love is and is not, with the key connecting lyric being:\n\nBut what’s love got to do with it when you don’t love yourself\n\nLove is not just something that happens, in order to really love another person, you have to be willing to be honest about who you are, what you do, and why you do it. If you’re not willing to own what you do and who you are you cannot truly love a person fully, it’s selfish. Love doesn’t just happen, you have to be open to it.", "annotation_id": "1143480", "url": "https://genius.com/1143480", "state": "accepted", "votes": 144, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Every time I write these words, they become a taboo", "vault_lines": ["Every time I write these words, they become a taboo"], "meaning": "Kendrick’s beliefs are considered taboo to mainstream society. Songs like “Keisha’s Song,” “m.A.A.d City,” and “P&P” contain controversial themes\n\nIn another cultural sphere, him telling stories that actually have meaning is taboo to today’s rappers, since the majority of them just talk about money, cars, and other material things.\n\nExpressed sexual thoughts are often considered taboo in public.", "annotation_id": "1143475", "url": "https://genius.com/1143475", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "(An-an-an-an-anytime) You can get it, you can get it, you can get it, you can get it (And any place) And I know just, know just, know just, know just, know just what you want Poetic justice, put it in a song, alright (An-an-an-an-anytime) You can get it, you can get it, you can get it, you can get it (And any place) And I know just, know just, know just, know just, know just what you want Poetic justice, put it in a song, alright", "vault_lines": ["With poetic justice, poetic justice", "You can get it, you can get it, you can get it, you can get it (And any place)", "And I know just, know just, know just, know just, know just what you want", "Poetic justice, put it in a song, alright", "Poetic justice, put it in a song"], "meaning": "“Poetic justice is a literary device in which virtue is ultimately rewarded or vice punished, often in modern literature by an ironic twist of fate intimately related to the character’s own conduct.” He is also referring to the film  Poetic Justice, starring Tupac Shakur and Janet Jackson.\n\nPoetic Justice: a twist of fate.\nWithin the context of the short film, “poetic justice” is possibly following him going to see Sherane to get down, but instead he ends up getting jumped by two men in hoodies (see skit).\n\nAlso, in the video, these lyrics are juxtaposed with images of a drive by shooting, implying the meaning of “you can get it”, as being that, in the hood, you can become victim of senseless violence at anytime.", "annotation_id": "1142939", "url": "https://genius.com/1142939", "state": "accepted", "votes": 265, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "I mean, I write poems in these songs, dedicated to the fun sex", "vault_lines": ["I mean, I write poems in these songs dedicated to you when", "I mean, I write poems in these songs, dedicated to the fun sex"], "meaning": "A play on the phrase “fairer sex”, referring to women. Also to play on writing about sex in his songs/poems.\n\nIn an interview with Cosmo Magazine, he was asked:\n\nIs there anything that feels as good as sex does?\n\nHe replied:\n\nWhen I finish writing a rap verse. It’s a lot like sex: You start off slow with ideas, like foreplay, and then you put your all into it. When you end it with the perfect thought, it’s like that perfect last stroke.", "annotation_id": "1142181", "url": "https://genius.com/1142181", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "It don’t make sense", "vault_lines": ["it don't make sense"], "meaning": "There could be a stretching use of the quasi-homophones sense, cents and scents. If some of these Drake lines relate back to Kendrick’s first verse, then we can connect sense and scents, a la Kendrick’s recognizing fragrances, as a way to explain Drake’s longing for Neb. However, if we go with the classic “if it don’t make dollars, it don’t make sense/cents” homophone use, then it may correspond with her lavish, one-off vacations.\n\nBut we can probably rest most assure knowing that it is probably just another straightforward, dull Drake line, where he doesn’t understand what this flirty girl is trying to accomplish by sending him seductive photos.", "annotation_id": "1142161", "url": "https://genius.com/1142161", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "But I can never right my wrongs unless I write ’em down for real, PS", "vault_lines": ["But I could never right my wrongs 'less I write it down for real, PS", "But I can never right my wrongs unless I write 'em down for real, PS"], "meaning": "Identical with the end of the first verse.\n\nAgain, this is a play on words using “right”… very similar to what Kanye West used in the end of his second verse in “Touch the Sky”:\n\nI’m tryin' to right my wrongs\nIt’s funny, them same wrongs help me write this song\n\nNot to mention the transition to the hook by extending use of the term “P.S.” at the end.\n\nThis reiteration of the last line in the first verse is to re-emphasize the theme of poetic justice – making things right with finely crafted compositions.\n\nLamar’s verse was all about the responsibility he feels as an artist to utilize his negative personal experiences to help other people overcome the same problems and about how he believes the role of rap/words/art is powerful enough to fix a nation.\n\nDrake brings it down to another, personal level – he only cares about winning back his girl and hopes that writing a bunch of songs about her will fix all the mistakes he made in their relationship and get her to see that they should get back together", "annotation_id": "1142076", "url": "https://genius.com/1142076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 176, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Young East African girl, you too busy fuckin’ with your other man I was tryna put you on game, put you on a plane Take you and your mama to the motherland, I could do it", "vault_lines": ["Young East African girl, you too busy fuckin' with your other man", "I was tryna put you on game, put you on a plane", "Take you and your mama to the motherland, I could do it"], "meaning": "Drake says, “Young East African girl” in the previous line, but if he is still evoking Nebby, then his geography might be a bit off. Nebby (and her mother) are from Casablanca, Morocco.\n\n\n\nDrake has been known to express his interest in Ethiopian women, as is seen in the song “Where To Now”. The clique “XO” merged with OVO, and most of them were from Ethiopia, so this could explain where the attraction was fostered.\n\n\n\nConsidering the connotations Ethiopia/Abyssinia has as being the African “mother land”, referred to wistfully as such in songs like Bunny Wailer’s “Dream Land” and the Abyssinians' “Forward Unto Zion”, it is most likely that Drake is addressing an Ethiopian girl in this particular verse.\n\nThis is also a decent example of the smooth lover man image Drake enjoys projecting. He’s not only out to charm the girl in question but her mother as well.", "annotation_id": "1142063", "url": "https://genius.com/1142063", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "If I told you that a flower bloomed in a dark room, would you trust it?", "vault_lines": ["If I told you that a flower bloomed in a dark room, would you trust it?"], "meaning": "The “flower” is Kendrick and the dark room is his hometown, Compton. Plants (flowering or otherwise) require exposure to light to grow, as they use the energy from that light to transform carbon dioxide into sugars (that the plant feeds on) and oxygen.\n\nKendrick asks the girl if she trusts him and believes that he turned out to be a good and decent man despite the fact that he grew up in a hood riddled with violence and gang warfare, knowing it seems difficult to believe this is possible.\n\nThe reiteration of this line from the first verse suggests an emphasis on the figurative idea of trust in relation to the flower.\n\nThis could also be metaphoric to love between two people blooming in an unorthodox way; a one night stand or in a club, both physically and metaphorically darker places.\n\nAlso, during the flowering phase of marijuana or its blooming, it is said that one method is to give the plant only 11 hours of light through the day and leave in darkness for the rest.In the end this marijuana has many clinical and recreation uses. Kendrick could be refering to this and that just like this flower(marijuana) that grows in the dark, he did too in Compton.", "annotation_id": "1142045", "url": "https://genius.com/1142045", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "I really hope you play this ’cause, oh, girl, you test my patience With all these seductive photographs and all these one-off vacations you’ve been takin’ Clearly a lot for me to take in,", "vault_lines": ["I really hope you play this 'cause, oh, girl, you test my patience", "With all these seductive photographs and all these one-off vacations you've been takin'", "Clearly a lot for me to take in,"], "meaning": "Drake’s verse is towards Zineb (a.k.a Nebby), his ex-girlfriend from Toronto. He has been known to go back and forth with her with songs directing at her such as “Sooner Than Later”, “Best I Ever Had” and “Look What You’ve Done”.\n\nNebby is known to have a huge fan following on Twitter and Instagram, and is notorious for taking seductive pictures of her working out or showing off her assets.\n\n\n\nHowever, because Nebby is not from “East Africa,” we are prone to think he’s talking about Maliah. She’s Ethiopian. Ethiopia is in East Africa. She takes lots of sexy pictures being that she’s famous for “dat ass”. A “one off vacation” is when you go on vacation alone. It “doesn’t make sense” because she’s constantly sending him seductive photos and yet going places alone and she’s “too busy fucking with her other man” so he doesn’t get why she wouldn’t take a vacation with him when she clearly has the chance.", "annotation_id": "1142042", "url": "https://genius.com/1142042", "state": "accepted", "votes": 133, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Nigga, don’t approach her with that Atari Nigga, that ain’t good game, homie, sorry", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, don't approach her with that Atari", "Nigga, that ain't good game, homie, sorry"], "meaning": "The Atari 2600 was released in 1977 and ushered a shift in video games from arcades to homes. When compared with modern gaming consoles, it is extremely outdated.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, “Sorry” could be a connotative reference to the board game\n\n\n\nThe line is comparable to one in Cee-Lo Green’s August 2010 hit, “Fuck You,” although Kendrick plays on the use of the word “game\"—meaning the ability to flirt or seduce.", "annotation_id": "1135520", "url": "https://genius.com/1135520", "state": "accepted", "votes": 138, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "Everybody else basic, you live life on an everyday basis", "vault_lines": ["Everybody else basic, you live life on an everyday basis"], "meaning": "Alludes to the fact that this girl lives her life as if everyday counts.\n\n“Live not one’s life as though one had a thousand years, but live each day as the last.” – Marcus Aurelius\n\nEssentially a YOLO reference.\n\n\n\nOr even more appropriate to the Roman Emperor’s words and Kendrick Lamar is the Latin maxim, “carpe diem” which means “seize the day.” While YOLO can be used to vaguely justify acts, no matter what the consequence; in essence “seizing the day” can be an encouragement to live life the best way you can. Lamar’s original statement, when looked at this way, can be more connected to the next verse poetic justice, which is a just end to your actions good or bad.", "annotation_id": "1135503", "url": "https://genius.com/1135503", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "I mean, I write poems in these songs dedicated to you when", "vault_lines": ["I mean, I write poems in these songs dedicated to you when", "I mean, I write poems in these songs, dedicated to the fun sex"], "meaning": "The song itself seems to be an ode to Sherane asking for her trust and love.\n\n\n\nBut given Kendrick is now on a larger stage, “you” may very well refer to his audience. After all, Kendrick’s mom later encourages him to “tell [his] story for the little black and brown kids of Compton”.", "annotation_id": "1135502", "url": "https://genius.com/1135502", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "With poetic justice, poetic justice", "vault_lines": ["With poetic justice, poetic justice", "Poetic justice, put it in a song, alright", "That sound like poetic justice, poetic justice", "I'm talkin' poetic justice, poetic justice", "Poetic justice, put it in a song"], "meaning": "Interestingly enough, poetic justice is an actual literary notion, in which virtue is ultimately rewarded (or vice punished) often by an ironic twist of fate intimately related to the character’s own conduct.\n\nFor example, Wile E. Coyote always sets traps for Road Runner, but ends up in them.\n\nEssentially, it means to live life in adherence to karma, in a sense—that whatever happens to you is earned through the deeds you perform, whether positive or negative.\n\nAnother entendre:\n\nThere’s a movie called Poetic Justice, starring 2Pac and Janet Jackson (which is significant because Janet is sampled in the track and Kendrick feels he is carrying Pac’s legacy).\n\n\n\nAnother interesting fact: Maya Angelou acted in Poetic Justice for an important scene. Between scenes, she encountered a young 2Pac in an altercation with another man on set. She would have a profound influence on him, as she broke up the fight and took him away to speak with him privately—much as she does for Kendrick and his friends in “Sing About Me, I’m Dying of Thirst.”", "annotation_id": "1135498", "url": "https://genius.com/1135498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 192, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "line": "I recognize your fragrance, hol’ up, you ain’t never gotta say shit, uh", "vault_lines": ["I recognize your fragrance, hol' up, you ain't never gotta say shit, uh"], "meaning": "When Kendrick’s girl comes up to him from behind, she doesn’t have to talk. He knows it’s her just from her aroma.\n\nNotice the use of one of his notable phrases “Hol' up,” in a different context.\n\nHe also has the ability to pick out high class women based on simple surface observations such as her scent, touching on the studied fact that smell is our most evocative sense.", "annotation_id": "1135496", "url": "https://genius.com/1135496", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/b4d6d87f080c362200ce55ed35ec65bb.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Poetic Justice\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Poetic JusticeKendrick Lamar5M", "status": "done", "targets": 50, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Kendrick-lamar-poetic-justice-lyrics", "genius_title": "Poetic Justice", "genius_id": 92856, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "When I see potential, I just gotta see it through", "vault_lines": ["When I see potential, I just gotta see it through"], "meaning": "Drake emphasizes the importance of recognizing something great early on, rather than regretting the uncertainty of not knowing how things could have turned out.", "annotation_id": "29804291", "url": "https://genius.com/29804291", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "[Click here to learn more about the making of ”Work”]", "vault_lines": ["Work, work, work, work, work, work"], "meaning": "Drake had this pool party the day before we were all out there. It was me, Vinylz, Mike Zombie, Boi-1da, Sevn Thomas, and Syk Sense. Drake was going away; he was on tour or something. He told us we can stay there to make music at his house. Sevn pulled the “Work” beat, but nobody knew where to take it. As soon as he pulled that up, I played the keys on it. I honestly didn’t even think the beat was done. But it’s big now.\n\nI wasn’t in the studio with her when she recorded it. I think she sang it how she felt to sing it. We had no idea the song was for Rihanna. 1da sent it to PARTYNEXTDOOR, and PARTY wrote to it. That’s what made the track what it is. When you hear it without him, it seems empty.", "annotation_id": "8715004", "url": "https://genius.com/8715004", "state": "verified", "votes": 383, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "Nuh badda, text me in a crisis", "vault_lines": ["Nuh badda, text me in a crisis"], "meaning": "‘Nuh badda’ means “don’t bother.”\n\nIt’s unclear whether Rihanna is saying “don’t bother to text me when you’re in a crisis,” because she doesn’t care, or if she’s admitting loneliness here, wishing for someone who’d be responsive when she needs them most.\n\nEither way, Rihanna’s looking for a meaningful relationship, and she’s not finding it with this person.", "annotation_id": "8591548", "url": "https://genius.com/8591548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "[Produced by Boi-1da]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song samples various elements from Richie Stephens and Mikey 2000’s 1998 “Sail Away Riddim.” In addition, the synths you hear halfway throughout each verse originally came from Alexander O'Neal’s “If You Were Here Tonight.”\n\nIn May 2014, Boi-1da tweeted that he always wanted to work with Rihanna, and the collaboration finally came into fruition.\n\nFurthermore, contributing writer Sevn Thomas detailed how this song came together in an interview with Complex:\n\n\nSo basically, myself, Allen Ritter, 1da, Vinylz, Nineteen85, Illangelo, and Mike Zombie, we were all staying at Drake’s house in Los Angeles. We all stayed there for three or four days, and literally it was like a beat factory. Everyone was sitting there working and collaborating with each other. I had made this beat maybe a couple days before with a dancehall influence, and I played it for 1da and he loved it. So it kinda just awakened something within him because we’re both Jamaican-Canadian. It was just something in our DNA, so it woke him up, and we started remembering all these old dancehall songs from the ‘90s.\n\nHe came up with the idea of sampling an old school dancehall rhythm, and we just went with the chords, and everything sort of started coming through organically. We basically banged this track out in half an hour, and we were just jamming out because we could just feel that island vibe, and we knew that the sound of the industry is sort of shaking up its little island vibe, and we knew we were really authentic, we had the Jamaican culture, and we took it upon ourselves to hone in on that and make our new futuristic dancehall songs that we like to call the new wave. We put our heads together with that one, and then Allen Ritter killed the chords on it. Amazing process.", "annotation_id": "8589817", "url": "https://genius.com/8589817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "I just hope that it gets to you I hope that you see this through I hope that you see this true What can I say? Please recognize I’m tryin’, babe", "vault_lines": ["I just hope that it gets to you", "I hope that you see this through", "I hope that you see this true", "What can I say?", "Please recognize I'm tryin', babe", "When I see potential, I just gotta see it through"], "meaning": "In an interview with T Magazine from The New York Times, Rihanna explained how busy she was to be dating:\n\nGuys need attention. They need that nourishment, that little stroke of the ego that gets them by every now and then. I’ll give it to my family, I’ll give it to my work — but I will not give it to a man right now.\n\nRiRi’s partner is feeling neglected due to her busy schedule. However, she truly tries to make time for him as she genuinely has strong feelings for him and doesn’t want him to think otherwise.", "annotation_id": "8588537", "url": "https://genius.com/8588537", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "I don’t wanna rush into it if it’s too soon", "vault_lines": ["I don't wanna rush into it if it's too soon"], "meaning": "Drake doesn’t want to get caught up in the heat of the moment, potentially spoiling a healthy relationship by prematurely pursuing his sexual desires. PartyNextDoor, who helped co-write this song, also touched on the importance of taking things steady in his track “Wait For You.”\n\nIn certain situations, it’s unclear whether a relationship is worth further pursuing, a predicament that Drake’s faced on multiple counts:\n\nWhy are we wasting our relationship on a relationship?\nWhy are we rushing and forcing it? This isn’t making sense", "annotation_id": "8588148", "url": "https://genius.com/8588148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "You need to get done, done, done, done at work, come over", "vault_lines": ["Work, work, work, work, work, work", "You need to get done, done, done, done at work, come over", "But I know you need to get done, done, done, done", "If you come over", "Now you need to forward"], "meaning": "Drake painted a similar image, during his verse on J. Cole’s track, “In The Morning:”\n\nYeah, and if you gotta leave for work\nI’ll be right here in the same bed that you left me in\n\nDrizzy has always shown appreciation to girls who work all night, especially the ones working in strip clubs. Aside from his lustful entanglements, Drake’s shown genuine admiration for intelligent women, those who don’t rely on him for financial stability, and can instead support themselves. Drizzy urges his hardworking woman to come home and show him some love.", "annotation_id": "8586642", "url": "https://genius.com/8586642", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "Dry, me ah desert him Nuh time to have you lurking Him ah go act like he nuh like it You know I dealt with you the nicest", "vault_lines": ["Him ah go act like he nuh like it", "You know I dealt with you the nicest", "Dry, me ah desert him", "Nuh time to have you lurking"], "meaning": "“Dry!” is Jamaican Patois for boring or un-entertaining.\n\nAs a consequence, Rihanna says “Me ah desert him”—meaning it’s time to leave.\n\nThis is wordplay on the words “desert” and “dry.” A desert is the driest place on earth, so it’s a fitting place to leave him if he keeps being such a “dry” dude. She’s not aroused or excited to be around him—he’s not making her wet! She’s dry like a desert so now she’s got to desert him.\n\n“Nuh time to have you lurking” means she can’t have him sticking around doing nothing.\n\nThe man is going to be unhappy about it, but Rihanna insists she was good to him.\n\nRiRi’s rumoured to be dating Travis Scott, who is featured on ANTi’s “Woo”—but she more recently told Ellen DeGenres that she was “single,” and the lyrics don’t seem to be about Scott in particular.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rihanna/status/692402198634954754", "annotation_id": "8586285", "url": "https://genius.com/8586285", "state": "accepted", "votes": 193, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "But I wake up and act like nothing’s wrong", "vault_lines": ["But I wake up and act like nothing's wrong"], "meaning": "It seems like Rihanna was trying to lay the foundation of a strong relationship and all she wanted was for her man to reciprocate. He didn’t.\n\nRather than a true connection, this relationship hinges on sex, hence: “I wake up and act like nothing’s wrong / Just get ready for work, work, work.”\n\nEven further there’s a sense of loneliness and possible social rejection which is pivotal in the line ‘nuh budda text me in a crisis’. She feels she had not much human connection with him, just sex.", "annotation_id": "8586154", "url": "https://genius.com/8586154", "state": "accepted", "votes": 81, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "We just need a face-to-face You could pick the time and the place You spent some time away Now you need to forward", "vault_lines": ["We just need to slow the motion", "We just need a face-to-face", "You could pick the time and the place", "You spent some time away", "Now you need to forward"], "meaning": "Similar to sentiments expressed in tracks “Hotline Bling” and “Bria’s Interlude,” Drake comments on the detrimental effects that long distance has on his relationships. Due to his lack of physical presence, this woman has presumably lost sight of the emotional connection that they used to share. In attempts to rekindle their love, he proposes the idea of an intimate date. He’ll put the ball in her court, there’ll be no forced agenda, instead Drake will give her the autonomy to pick the environment that she feels most comfortable in.\n\nThis may also serve as a continuation to the narrative told in Drake and Rihanna’s previous collaborations. “What’s My Name?” touched on the lustful and mysterious first encounter, whereas “Take Care” took on a more serious stance, showing evidence of high level commitment and compassion. “Work” acts as a reflective piece, illustrating the transformation of both their sexual and platonic relationship.\n\nTake Me\nTo another place where I’ll be\nFace to face, just you and me\nWith no rules", "annotation_id": "8585983", "url": "https://genius.com/8585983", "state": "accepted", "votes": 94, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "You mistaken my love I brought for you for foundation All that I wanted from you was to give me Something that I never had Something that you’ve never seen Something that you’ve never been, mm", "vault_lines": ["You mistaken my love I brought for you for foundation", "All that I wanted from you was to give me", "Something that I never had", "Something that you've never seen", "Something that you've never been, mm"], "meaning": "Rihanna often sings about feeling lovelorn or despondent about a relationship that didn’t work out. She repeatedly feels duped or taken advantage of, as highlighted in “Love The Way You Lie (Part II),” and “FourFiveSeconds.”\n\nShe simply wants a man to give her the dedication she gives him.", "annotation_id": "8585971", "url": "https://genius.com/8585971", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "Rollin’, rollin’, rollin’, rollin’, rollin’ How many more shots until you’re rollin’?", "vault_lines": ["Rollin', rollin', rollin', rollin', rollin'", "How many more shots until you're rollin'?"], "meaning": "After spilling his emotions, Drake hints at being intoxicated (shots) and/or being high (rollin').\n\nHe wants to know how many more drinks it will take until she’s dancing/hip rolling on him.", "annotation_id": "8585845", "url": "https://genius.com/8585845", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "I believed all of your dreams, adoration You took my heart and my keys and my patience", "vault_lines": ["I believed all of your dreams, adoration", "You took my heart and my keys and my patience"], "meaning": "Within the song’s narrative, Rihanna reminds her partner of all the faith she put into him and his dreams. In return, Rihanna had everything taken from her; the love she gave him was cast aside and the patience she needed to keep their relationship strong through tough times was spent.\n\nKeys allow you to do things, they open doors and start cars. Rihanna’s suggestion that hers were taken implies that she enabled her companion’s lifestyle without receiving the benefits of the love she yearned for. Such one-sided relationships are also discussed in other ANTI songs such as “Love On The Brain.”\n\nThese lines could also be seen as showcasing Rihanna’s love for her fans. In 2015, RiRi and Samsung launched AntiDiaRy, a sort of dynamic experience where the “Keyholder”, a spooky Instagram account, released clues. These included references to  ANTI and the accompanying world tour being “the ultimate dream,” whilst fans tiring of the four-year wait for its release were told that “patience is the key.” Some fans were also given ornate golden keys with 5-digit codes inscribed on them, which unlocked two tickets to a Rihanna concert.", "annotation_id": "8585702", "url": "https://genius.com/8585702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "Work, work, work, work, work, work He said me haffi work, work, work, work, work, work He see me do mi dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt Ah so me put in work, work, work, work, work, work When you ah guh learn, learn, learn, learn, learn, learn? Me nuh cyar if him hurt, hurt, hurt, hurt, hurting", "vault_lines": ["Work, work, work, work, work, work", "He said me haffi work, work, work, work, work, work", "I haffi work, work, work, work, work, work", "So me put in work, work, work, work, work, work", "He see me do mi dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt", "Ah so me put in work, work, work, work, work, work", "When you ah guh learn, learn, learn, learn, learn, learn?", "Me nuh cyar if him hurt, hurt, hurt, hurt, hurting", "Dry, me ah desert him"], "meaning": "Never one to shy away from her sexual desires, Rihanna wants to get down in the bedroom.\n\nRihanna is speaking in Jamaican patois—\"Haffi\" means “have to” and “ah guh” means “is going to.” “Meh nuh cyar” means “I don’t care.”\n\nThe English translation is approximately:\n\nWork, work, work, work, work, work\nHe said I have to\nWork, work, work, work, work, work\nHe saw me doing my\nDirt, dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt, dirt\nSo I put in\nWork, work, work, work, work, work\nWhen are you going to\nLearn, learn, learn, learn, learn?\nI don’t care if he’s\nHurt, hurt, hurt, hurt, hurting\n\nBoi-1da, Sevyn Thomas, and PARTYNEXTDOOR, all Jamaican-Canadians, worked on the track. Rihanna is from Barbados, and her patois has a Bajan (Barbadian) accent.", "annotation_id": "8585632", "url": "https://genius.com/8585632", "state": "accepted", "votes": 749, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "You took my heart on my sleeve for decoration", "vault_lines": ["You took my heart on my sleeve for decoration"], "meaning": "To “wear your heart on your sleeve” means to be open and transparent about your emotions.\n\nThe phrase was first written in Shakespeare’s 17th century play, Othello. Iago speaks to Roderigo,\n\nFor when my outward action doth demonstrate\nThe native act and figure of my heart\nIn complement extern, ’tis not long after\nBut I will wear my heart upon my sleeve\nFor daws to peck at. I am not what I am.\n\nHere, Rihanna claims her lover took advantage of her openness and transparency.", "annotation_id": "8585611", "url": "https://genius.com/8585611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "I mean, who am I to hold your past against you?", "vault_lines": ["I mean, who am I to hold your past against you?"], "meaning": "Drake mentioned a similar topic on 2011’s Rihanna/Drake hit “Take Care:”\n\nWhat’s a life with no fun? Please, don’t be so ashamed\nI’ve had mine, you’ve had yours, we both know.. we know\n\nRegardless of what people have done in the past, it truly is irrelevant to the current state of a relationship. Luckily, Rihanna is aware of that and won’t hold her significant other to any of the mistakes in his past.", "annotation_id": "8585445", "url": "https://genius.com/8585445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "Beg you something, please Baby, don’t you leave Don’t leave me stuck here in the streets, uh-huh If I get another chance to I will never, no, never neglect you", "vault_lines": ["Something that I never had", "Beg you something, please", "Baby, don't you leave", "Don't leave me stuck here in the streets, uh-huh", "If I get another chance to", "I will never, no, never neglect you"], "meaning": "This collaboration brought up speculations of Drake and RiRi being back together, assuming they were ever a pair. Whoever Rihanna’s addressing, she’s fed up and wants to make their relationship more than just sex. He has other ideas.\n\nAnd if you think Rihanna could have a hard time in the streets, Charlamagne’s story might change your mind.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejRwLJcXjGQ", "annotation_id": "8585427", "url": "https://genius.com/8585427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "We just need to slow the motion", "vault_lines": ["We just need to slow the motion", "We just need a face-to-face"], "meaning": "Drake wants to slow down and live in the moment. If things are going good, why not take in the beauty. After all, we only live once and we should savour every moment. Drake had a similar theme in 2013’s “The Motion:”\n\nShe’ll probably come around\nSoon as I settle down, that’s the motion\nOh-oh, I guess that’s just the motion\n\nDrake’s fellow Cash Money rapper Juvenile released a song in 2004 titled “Slow Motion.”", "annotation_id": "8585415", "url": "https://genius.com/8585415", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "I spilled all my emotions tonight, I’m sorry", "vault_lines": ["I spilled all my emotions tonight, I'm sorry"], "meaning": "Drake’s gained a reputation for being ‘too soft’ as a rapper because of saying how he truly feels and admitting weakness.\n\nHe opened up to this girl in a moment of passion. It’s rare to find a rapper with such raw emotion and honesty. When you can relate to the people, sensitivity becomes a strength.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/OVO40/status/624026234184466432", "annotation_id": "8585267", "url": "https://genius.com/8585267", "state": "accepted", "votes": 85, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "Sorry if I’m way less friendly I got niggas tryna end me, oh (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Sorry if I'm way less friendly", "I got niggas tryna end me, oh (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Drake is aware that his recent commercial success makes him a target. Constantly fending off attacks might be making him a hard person to be around. Lyrics from “No Tellin'” similarly speak on his paranoia and new attitude:\n\nI gotta keep watchin' for oppers cause anything’s possible, yeah\nThere’s no code of ethics out here, anyone will take shots at you, yeah\nNiggas think they can come take what I got, let’s be logical, yeah\n\n\n\nDrake was in 2015’s hottest beef when Meek Mill tweeted that Drake didn’t write his own rhymes. This resulted in two diss tracks from Drake with “Charged Up” and “Back to Back,” and even more ghost-writing claims from Meek.", "annotation_id": "8585205", "url": "https://genius.com/8585205", "state": "accepted", "votes": 165, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "line": "If you had a twin, I would still choose you", "vault_lines": ["If you had a twin, I would still choose you"], "meaning": "While identical twins are very much alike in their appearances, they’re still different people with their own personal traits. Drake assures this special girl that he’s not pursuing her because of her looks, but because he’s genuinely into her.\n\nNotably, there’s actually a popular parody Instagram account that imagines what it would be like if Drake had a twin.", "annotation_id": "8585185", "url": "https://genius.com/8585185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/4a8010f3c0949e44ee30fb718f2445ec.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Work\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>WorkRihanna8.4M", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rihanna-work-lyrics", "genius_title": "Work", "genius_id": 2398213, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Kill ’em all, dead bodies in the hallway", "vault_lines": ["Kill 'em all, dead bodies in the hallway"], "meaning": "Kendrick pronounces “Kill ‘em all” in a way that sounds similar to “K. Lamar.” While Kendrick hadn’t called himself K. Lamar on a song when Fuckin’ Problems was released, he would go on to use it a couple of times, first on Mortal Man:\n\nWould you judge me a drug-head or see me as K. Lamar?\n\nAnd later on No More Parties in L.A.:\n\nShe said, “K. Lamar, you kinda dumb to be a poet”", "annotation_id": "24035750", "url": "https://genius.com/24035750", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "She rollin’ Swishers", "vault_lines": [", she rollin' Swishers"], "meaning": "A Swisher is a flavoured cigarette, however, they are notorious for the terrible quality of their tobacco and are commonly broken up down the middle, and refilled with marijuana, and then rolled back up.", "annotation_id": "15150944", "url": "https://genius.com/15150944", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "[Verse 3: Kendrick Lamar]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "EVERYBODY JUST DECIDED LET’S HIT EM WITH ONE. I LOVE WORKIN' WITH KENDRICK", "annotation_id": "1332378", "url": "https://genius.com/1332378", "state": "verified", "votes": 1738, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "[Produced by 40 & Drake]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s longtime collaborator 40 handles the production here, but Drake is also given a producer credit under his C. Papi moniker.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/OVO40/status/259037426360655872\n\nThe song’s beat interpolates a snippet of unreleased vocals from Drake’s girl Aaliyah, from a song called “Quit Hatin'.” The vocals run throughout, but can be heard most clearly in the song’s instrumental intro. (The sample can be heard at 2:19 in this video).\n\nDrake explained the Aaliyah connection:\n\nWe were working on the Aaliyah project [a never-released posthumous album from 2012] at the time, and we had this vocal sample that we were playing with, which we ended up having someone re-sing and in a different melody. We ended up with “Fuckin’ Problems.”", "annotation_id": "7621997", "url": "https://genius.com/7621997", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "[Verse 1: A$AP Rocky]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "It’s almost comical how profane this verse is. The censored version makes the verse sound very different, to the point where it’s pretty funny listening to it. Censored words:\n\n\n“bitches”\n“bitch”\n“pop”\n“semi”\n“nine”\n“raw”\n“motherfucker”\n“motherfuckers”\n“chrome”\n“nigga”\n“nigga”\n“nigga”\n“fuckin”\n“bitches”\n“rolling”\n“swishers”\n“bitches”\n“niggas”\n“lick it”\n“nigga”\n“niggerish”\n“nigga”\n“nigga”\n“fucking”\n“fucking”\n“dyke”\n“bitch”\nand last but not least, “fucking.”\n\n\n\nAbout 20% of the verse is changed (such as making “bitches” into “chickens”) or censored.", "annotation_id": "1880010", "url": "https://genius.com/1880010", "state": "accepted", "votes": 197, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "I be fuckin’ broads like I be fuckin’ bored", "vault_lines": ["I be fuckin' broads like I be fuckin' bored"], "meaning": "Like the hook, this line plays with different meanings of the word “fucking\"—in this case, as a verb and an intensifier.\n\nIn July 2015, he told NME:\n\nI’m a weirdo: I don’t want a virgin. I don’t. That’s boring. You’re gonna cheat on that girl, man. What about a girl that’s open-minded?", "annotation_id": "1618133", "url": "https://genius.com/1618133", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "If it’s comin’ from a nigga I don’t know, then I don’t trust it", "vault_lines": ["If it's comin' from a nigga I don't know, then I don't trust it"], "meaning": "Drake’s “no new friends” mentality is in full effect here. This possibly explains why Drake sent back a $2,000 bottle that Chris Brown bought him—a move that allegedly escalated their beef.", "annotation_id": "1356883", "url": "https://genius.com/1356883", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "But the chrome to your dome make you sweat like Keith", "vault_lines": ["But the chrome to your dome make you sweat like Keith"], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5237780515001", "annotation_id": "1332397", "url": "https://genius.com/1332397", "state": "verified", "votes": 554, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "I’m beast", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Each rapper finishes their verse with “beast” because they have a similar mentality to Marshawn Lynch:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BWKEJqkKLcs", "annotation_id": "1314848", "url": "https://genius.com/1314848", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "A.K.A. Benz is to me just a car (Mm)", "vault_lines": ["A.K.A. Benz is to me just a car (Mm)"], "meaning": "Kendrick’s giving himself a creative nickname, but it’s unclear if he’s using it to:\n\n\nBrag about the money he has. To regular people a Mercedes Benz is a huge investment, but for him it’s inconsequential.\nShow how anti-materialistic he is. Who cares about a Benz—it’s just a car!\n\n\n\n\n\nThe staccato delivery of this line (continued over the following two) is worth noting. He peppers the syllables in an almost child-like rhythm which is simultaneously ‘sing-song’ and very, very direct—almost machine-gun like.\n\nKendrick doesn’t do drugs either, so a Benz to him isn’t a benzodiazepine (benz, benzo) but a car.", "annotation_id": "1287433", "url": "https://genius.com/1287433", "state": "accepted", "votes": 170, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Turn a dyke bitch out, have her fuckin’ boys, beast", "vault_lines": ["Turn a dyke bitch out, have her fuckin' boys, beast"], "meaning": "He’s a beast* because he’s irresistible to all women—even to lesbians. This is an old hip-hop meme invoked by various artists, including The Notorious B.I.G. on “The What”.\n\nIf Rocky can really do what he claims, he should go to Hong Kong and catch a quick £40 million check:\n\n\n\n*It’s worth noting that every verse in this song ends with the word “beast.”", "annotation_id": "1215367", "url": "https://genius.com/1215367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 152, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Make that thing pop like a semi or a nine", "vault_lines": ["Make that thing pop like a semi or a nine"], "meaning": "Semi and nine refer to guns (a “semi-automatic” and a gun that uses 9mm caliber bullets). Like the “popping” that comes from a gun when it’s shot, A$AP wants this girl to pop it for pimp.\n\n\n\nThe choice between “a semi or a nine” is a confusing one, because 9mm guns don’t come in revolver style—they are exclusively semi-automatic.\n\nSince a “nine” is a “semi” by definition, saying “make it pop like a semi or a nine” makes as much sense as saying “driving a BMW or a car.”", "annotation_id": "1148186", "url": "https://genius.com/1148186", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Don’t get involved, listen what the crystal ball say", "vault_lines": ["Don't get involved, listen what the crystal ball say"], "meaning": "Crystal balls usually warn people of the dangers to come, much like Kendrick warns rappers not to mess with him or they may suffer the same fate as the those in the previous line.\n\n\n\nPretty fucking prescient of K-dot, really, a year before he shook the hip-hop world with similar words on his “Control” verse. Was he planning on shaking up the rap game way ahead of time?", "annotation_id": "1146408", "url": "https://genius.com/1146408", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "If you comin’ for my head, then motherfucker, get to bustin’", "vault_lines": ["If you comin' for my head, then motherfucker, get to bustin'"], "meaning": "Double Entendre:\n\n\nIf someone is coming at Drake, then they really need to do it instead of saying they will. Ironically,  Drake sure didn’t seem to appreciate it much when Common did this and instead resorted to having his father intervene to avoid the battle\nIf a girl is on his dick, particularly the head, then he’s gonna get to bustin'\n\n\n\n\n\nPeep the bust/ head wordplay\n\nAnd we all know the rumors about Drake “busting” at Chris Brown’s head", "annotation_id": "1146296", "url": "https://genius.com/1146296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "If findin’ somebody real is your fuckin’ problem", "vault_lines": ["If findin' somebody real is your fuckin' problem"], "meaning": "On the album version of the song, Drake performs both of these lines. However, in the music video, Drake performs the first line, and Rocky performs the second.", "annotation_id": "1146007", "url": "https://genius.com/1146007", "state": "accepted", "votes": 177, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Got your girl on my line, world on my line The irony, I fuck ’em at the same damn time", "vault_lines": ["Got your girl on my line, world on my line", "The irony, I fuck 'em at the same damn time"], "meaning": "Kendrick references Future’s 2011 single “Same Damn Time,” which spawned countless remixes. The irony is that “your girl” is also “your world” if she means everything to you.\n\n\n\nThis isn’t the first time Kendrick used this metaphor. For instance, on 2012’s “Backseat Freestyle” he said:\n\nI pray my dick get big as the Eiffel Tower\nSo I can fuck the world for seventy-two hours", "annotation_id": "1145995", "url": "https://genius.com/1145995", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "I don’t really say this often But this long-dick nigga ain’t for the long talkin’", "vault_lines": [", I don't really say this often", "But this long-dick nigga ain't for the long talkin'"], "meaning": "Drizzy channels his inner Wayne, who said on his 2012 single “No Worries”:\n\n“I would talk about my dick, but man that shit be a long story”\n\nMs. Cat Washington from Bad Girls Club on her relationship with Drake:\n\nHe is a phenomenal “peach eater” and he is black and Jewish and the black side is Mandingo", "annotation_id": "1145989", "url": "https://genius.com/1145989", "state": "accepted", "votes": 102, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "They say money make a nigga act niggarish (Uh) But least a nigga, nigga rich", "vault_lines": ["They say money make a nigga act niggarish (Uh)", "But least a nigga, nigga rich"], "meaning": "“Nigga Rich” is a derogatory term; it usually connotes the idea of the nouveau-riche but in relation to the successful African-Americans of the entertainment and sports industries (i.e. Basketball players & rappers).\n\nThis line emphasizes how Rocky isn’t bothered by haters looking down on him as tastelessly rich, because he enjoys the benefit of bountiful, liquid wealth. He may not have generational wealth, but at least he has the dough to blow on expensive things for the time being.\n\nIt’s worth noting that the unusual radio edit version of this line:\n\nThey say money make money act licorice\nAt least Hilfiger rich", "annotation_id": "1145784", "url": "https://genius.com/1145784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 137, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "‘Cause I’m the nigga, the nigga, nigga, like how you figure? (Yeah) Gettin’ figures and fuckin’ bitches", "vault_lines": ["‘Cause I'm the nigga, the nigga, nigga, like how you figure? (Yeah)", "Gettin' figures and fuckin' bitches"], "meaning": "“The nigga…the nigga nigga” is a iconic phrase in New Orleans rap that originated in the bounce music scene, as heard in TT Tucker’s “Where Dey At”. It later appeared in songs by Cash Money Records, including Mannie Fresh’s verse on the hit “Back That Ass Up”:\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_embedded&v=WL2txMU50CI#t=175s\n\nHe’s the “nigga\"—similar to saying he’s \"the man\"—because he gets money and women (both represented by the word \"figure”).", "annotation_id": "1145681", "url": "https://genius.com/1145681", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Vacayed in Atlanta, then she goin’ back to Cali (Mm)", "vault_lines": ["Vacayed in Atlanta, then she goin' back to Cali (Mm)"], "meaning": "The girl has been on holiday in Atlanta but is finally headed back home to Kendrick, who’s from Compton.\n\nThis line—and much of the verse—also works as a metaphor for the hip-hop game. Southern rap centered on Atlanta has been the dominant force in the rap scene for many years, but now that Kendrick’s on top, it’s coming back to the West Coast.\n\nThis is also a reference to the song “Going Back to Cali,” though unclear whether it’s the one by Biggie Smalls or LLCool J", "annotation_id": "1145658", "url": "https://genius.com/1145658", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "That mean your friends-es need be up to par See, my standards are pampered by threesomes tomorrow (Mm)", "vault_lines": ["That mean your friends-es need be up to par", "See, my standards are pampered by threesomes tomorrow (Mm)"], "meaning": "Kendrick is going to have threesomes with bad women in the future so the girl he’s with today better have friends that meet up to his standards.\n\nAlso a play on words with par and threesomes which are both golf terms.\n\nAdditionally, these two lines (along with the previous two) are rapped in a triplet meter as opposed to the duplet meter in the rest of the verse, creating a possible (unintentional) play on “three\"somes.\n\nThe reason these lines, starting with “AKA,” stand out is that Kendrick has switched over to a poetic meter known as “anapestic tetrameter,” or a repeated pattern of “short-short-LONG” syllables.\n\nThis is pretty rare in rap: another good example is Eminem on “The Way I Am.” Anapestic tetrameter occurs more commonly in comic poetry like Dr. Seuss or Twas the Night Before Christmas.", "annotation_id": "1145653", "url": "https://genius.com/1145653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Ain’t a fuckin’ sing-along ’less you brought the weed along", "vault_lines": ["Ain't a fuckin' sing-along 'less you brought the weed along"], "meaning": "A possible reference to Drake’s song “Crew Love”, which featured this line from The Weeknd:\n\nThis ain’t no fucking sing-along\nSo, girl, what you singing for?\n\n#CAUSE SHE LOVIN' THE CREEEEEEEEEEWWWWWWWWWWWW", "annotation_id": "1145610", "url": "https://genius.com/1145610", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Girl, I’m Kendrick Lamar (Mm)", "vault_lines": ["Girl, I'm Kendrick Lamar (Mm)"], "meaning": "Kendrick knows how famous he is, and you should too.\n\nThis line inspired a popular meme:", "annotation_id": "1145552", "url": "https://genius.com/1145552", "state": "accepted", "votes": 377, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Halle Berry, hallelujah Holler back, I’ll do ya, beast", "vault_lines": ["Holler back, I'll do ya, beast"], "meaning": "On his 2012 song “Money Trees,” Kendrick says:\n\nIt go Halle Berry or hallelujah\nPick your poison, tell me what you doing\n\nIt’s unclear which line was recorded first, since both songs were released around the same time. Kendrick explained the “Money Trees” line in a verified Genius annotation:\n\nYou can either go right, or you can go left and get crazy with us…Of course, hallelujah being the more good in me and Halle Berry being the actual vice of what’s going down – the lust for the money, the lust for this mission we are about to go on. Pick your poison, tell me what you’re doing. But when you tell me, tell me right now. We ain’t got time to wait.\n\nHere, Kendrick also seems to be using the line to praise Halle Berry’s beauty. He’d like her to “holla” at him so he can “do” her. Can you blame him?", "annotation_id": "1145550", "url": "https://genius.com/1145550", "state": "accepted", "votes": 344, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Say she from the hood, but she live inside in the valley now", "vault_lines": ["Say she from the hood, but she live inside in the valley now"], "meaning": "He’s referring to Southern California’s San Fernando Valley, which is known for being suburban, safe, and wealthy—at least compared to the “hood,” where Kendrick is also from.\n\nA “valley girl” is a popular term to describe a stereotypical woman from this area who is materialistic and arrogant. While Alicia Silverstone’s character in Clueless was actually from Beverly Hills, she represents the image of the Valley girl in popular culture.\n\n\n\nHe could also be implying that the girl is a porn star, since nearly 90% of all legally distributed pornographic films made in the United States are either filmed in or produced by studios based in the San Fernando Valley, according to the L.A. Times.\n\nThis line could also be a metaphor for the rap game, which started in the streets but has now become suburban.", "annotation_id": "1145547", "url": "https://genius.com/1145547", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Or we can stare up at the stars and put the Beatles on", "vault_lines": ["Or we can stare up at the stars and put the Beatles on"], "meaning": "While telling a girl to drop down and get her “eagle on” represents Drake’s wild side, don’t forget that he’s got a sensitive side too.\n\nIf Drizzy needs to pick a Beatles song to put on while star-gazing, he should consider “And I Love Her,” which includes this fitting verse:\n\nBright are the stars that shine\nDark is the sky\nI know this love of mine\nWill never die\nAnd I love her\n\nSimilar to Drake’s “sing-along” line a few bars before, the star-gazing here could possibly be to his song “Crew Love”, which features the line:\n\nSmoking weed under star projectors\nI guess we’ll never know what Harvard gets us\n\nAnd perhaps there’s even more connecting The Beatles to the previous line’s Nelly reference? “Flap Your Wings” could be a nod to Wings, the band started by Paul McCartney after he left the Beatles. The “get your eagle on” could also be some classic rock wordplay, referencing iconic ’70s band the Eagles.", "annotation_id": "1145538", "url": "https://genius.com/1145538", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "She eyein’ me like a nigga don’t exist Girl, I know you want this dick", "vault_lines": ["She eyein' me like a nigga don't exist", "Girl, I know you want this dick"], "meaning": "The way he says, “Girl, I know you want this dick” is a reference to Martin Lawrence’s standup movie You So Crazy.\n\n\n\nKendrick also wears a leather outfit similar to Martin’s in the song’s video.", "annotation_id": "1145519", "url": "https://genius.com/1145519", "state": "accepted", "votes": 396, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Uh, yeah, ho, this the finale", "vault_lines": ["Uh, yeah, ho, this the finale"], "meaning": "Kendrick Lamar takes the final verse.\n\nAs Jay Electronica learned on “Control,” it’s usually best to let Kendrick go last.", "annotation_id": "1145505", "url": "https://genius.com/1145505", "state": "accepted", "votes": 269, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "All that shit you talkin’ ’bout is not up for discussion I will pay to make it bigger, I don’t pay for no reduction", "vault_lines": ["All that shit you talkin' 'bout is not up for discussion", "I will pay to make it bigger, I don't pay for no reduction"], "meaning": "In an interview with Nardwuar, Drake said that\n\nI try and showcase the fact that you know, women with you know a full chest. It’s nothing to be ashamed of, and you shouldn’t go get them reduced. They’re beautiful. (emphasis ours)\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_detailpage&v=X_Tf3Tw5bk0#t=125\n\n2 Chainz definitely approves of this course of action…", "annotation_id": "1145497", "url": "https://genius.com/1145497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "A$AP (Yeah), get like me (Uh)", "vault_lines": ["A$AP (Yeah), get like me (Uh)"], "meaning": "Double entendre: at first glance, of course it’s a reference to his name, A$AP Rocky, but he’s also saying “get like me As Soon As Possible”\n\n“Get Like Me” was also a hit song by Mississippi-born rapper, producer, and entrepreneur David Banner.", "annotation_id": "1145484", "url": "https://genius.com/1145484", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Ain’t heard my album? Who you sleepin’ on? You should print the lyrics out and have a fuckin’ read-along", "vault_lines": ["Ain't heard my album? Who you sleepin' on?", "You should print the lyrics out and have a fuckin' read-along", "Ain't a fuckin' sing-along 'less you brought the weed along"], "meaning": "Drake released the hit album Take Care on November 15, 2011, giving rap fans approximately 11 months to peep it before “Fuckin' Problems” was released in October 2012. Funny enough, Drake’s mentor Lil Wayne was one of the few people who admitted that he hadn’t listened to Thank Me Later until months after it came out.\n\n\n\nWe hear Genius is a pretty good place to go if you want to print out Drake lyrics…", "annotation_id": "1145475", "url": "https://genius.com/1145475", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Make you think about all of the niggas you been leadin’ on Make me think about all of the rappers I’ve been feedin’ on Got a feelin’ that’s the same dudes that we speakin’ on, oh word?", "vault_lines": ["Make you think about all of the niggas you been leadin' on", "Make me think about all of the rappers I've been feedin' on", "Got a feelin' that's the same dudes that we speakin' on, oh word?"], "meaning": "Drake is claiming that the same dudes this girl has been leading on are the same rappers Drake has been feeding off of. He is out of their league in the rap game and she is too damn sexy for them so she rolls with Drake.\n\nOf course, this could be another Chris Brown reference, since the line is allegedly directed at Rihanna who (at the time of the song’s release) had an on-again/off-again relationship with/had been accused of leading on Chris Brown, with whom Drake is beefing.\n\nThis can also be seen as a Common reference. Earlier in 2012, these two rappers began beefing after Drake fired shots on the Ricky Rozay banger Stay Schemin', which Common responded to by releasing a remix of the same song, after Common was accused of referring to Drake on the song “Sweet,” which he later confirmed was true.", "annotation_id": "1145459", "url": "https://genius.com/1145459", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Then ju— okay, I got it Then just drop down and get your eagle on", "vault_lines": ["Then ju— okay, I got it", "Then just drop down and get your eagle on"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Nelly’s song “Flap your Wings.” “Getting yo eagle on” is a dance move, as seen in the video. Maybe instead of the dance, he wants her to get spread eagle for him.\n\nDrizzy holding out the “just” may insinuate that he began a sing-along, so someone must have brought the weed along.", "annotation_id": "1145453", "url": "https://genius.com/1145453", "state": "accepted", "votes": 193, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Never met a motherfucker fresh like me (Yeah) All these motherfuckers wanna dress like me (Uh)", "vault_lines": ["A$AP (Yeah), get like me (Uh)", "Never met a motherfucker fresh like me (Yeah)", "All these motherfuckers wanna dress like me (Uh)"], "meaning": "ASAP’s style and swag was very unique but now everyone is trying to copy it. This makes sense since he’s one of Complex’s 50 most stylish rappers of all time.\n\nHe may also be speaking about SpaceGhostPurrp who he has beef with and included in this beef, they both claim that the other stole their style.\n\nCoincidentally, Kendrick dresses similarly to Rocky in the song’s music video:", "annotation_id": "1145440", "url": "https://genius.com/1145440", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "My pep talk turn into a pep rally", "vault_lines": ["My pep talk turn into a pep rally"], "meaning": "His raps are good enough to have people hyped up as if they were at a pep rally—evidenced by his “pep rally” that shut the city down in 2012!", "annotation_id": "1145437", "url": "https://genius.com/1145437", "state": "accepted", "votes": 128, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Yes, Lord", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drizzy seems to have a new favorite ad-lib:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Drake/status/238185258900656128", "annotation_id": "1145421", "url": "https://genius.com/1145421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "I love bad bitches, that’s my fuckin’ problem (Problem) And yeah, I like to fuck, I got a fuckin’ problem (Problem) I love bad bitches, that’s my fuckin’ problem (Problem) And yeah, I like to fuck, I got a fuckin’ problem (Problem) I love bad bitches, that’s my fuckin’ problem (Problem) And yeah, I like to fuck, I got a fuckin’ problem (Problem)", "vault_lines": ["Gettin' figures and fuckin' bitches", "I love bad bitches, that's my fuckin' problem (Problem)", "And yeah, I like to fuck, I got a fuckin' problem (Problem)"], "meaning": "The hook is centered around a pun—Chainz both has a “fuckin' problem,” and a problem with fucking—i.e. he enjoys it so much that it has become a problem.\n\nDrake detailed the hook’s genesis:\n\n“Fuckin’ Problems” came from a song me and 2 Chainz had done. He said in his verse—it was really slow too—“I love bad bitches too that’s a fuckin’ problem.” I stopped his verse while we were listening to it, and I said, “Yo, that needs to be a hook. I don’t know how we’re going to explain this to him that we chopped a piece of his verse to do the hook.”\n\n2 Chainz echoed Drake’s account and provided his perspective during a November 2020 interview.", "annotation_id": "1145406", "url": "https://genius.com/1145406", "state": "accepted", "votes": 275, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Ooh, I know you love it when this beat is on", "vault_lines": ["Ooh, I know you love it when this beat is on"], "meaning": "Excuse Drake if he’s particularly proud of this beat—he co-produced it (using his alias C. Papi) with his partner 40.\n\nDrake has dabbled in co-production throughout his career, including his songs “Cameras,” “The Real Her,” “Practice,” and “Back To Back.”", "annotation_id": "1145392", "url": "https://genius.com/1145392", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "She love my licorice, I let her lick it (Alright)", "vault_lines": ["She love my licorice, I let her lick it (Alright)"], "meaning": "Licorice is a metaphor for ASAP’s dick.\n\n\n\nAlso possibly a reference to the previous line about liquor, because several liquors—including Ouzo, Sambucca, Pernod, Arak, and Jägermeister—are known for tasting like black licorice.\n\nDanny Brown had a similar line on his 2010 song “White Stripes”:\n\nHoes say I’m inconsiderate\nCause I make ‘em treat my dick like licorice\nBut they can’t get a pack of Twizzlers on me", "annotation_id": "1145297", "url": "https://genius.com/1145297", "state": "accepted", "votes": 65, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "line": "Ooh, baby like it raw with the shimmy shimmy ya, huh?", "vault_lines": ["Ooh, baby like it raw with the shimmy shimmy ya, huh?"], "meaning": "“Shimmy Shimmy Ya” is a song by Ol' Dirty Bastard off his debut album, Return to the 36 Chambers: The Dirty Version. In the song’s hook, ODB famously croons “ooh baby I like it raw”, and here Rocky flips the line to illustrate his girl’s love of raw—or unprotected—sex.\n\nA$AP has similar lines in his songs “Bass” and “Long.Live.A$AP”, and expressed his strong admiration for ODB in a Genius verified annotation.", "annotation_id": "1145267", "url": "https://genius.com/1145267", "state": "accepted", "votes": 111, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "<img src=\"https://images.genius.com/0e807f9e10b6b75fb4cce3664dfa87c6.300x300x1.png\" alt=\"Fuckin’ Problems\" class=\"SizedImage__NoScript-sc-d4d34081-2 kJPgLt\"/>Fuckin’ ProblemsA$AP Rocky8.1M", "status": "done", "targets": 40, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/A-ap-rocky-fuckin-problems-lyrics", "genius_title": "Fuckin’ Problems", "genius_id": 93539, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Splash Brothers", "line": "All the verses Taylor made for you to get offended", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Taylor Made Freestyle” Is He Talking About", "annotation_id": "35916908", "url": "https://genius.com/35916908", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Splash Brothers", "line": "Yeah, on double M G, I’ll fuck a rapper’s wife She ain’t even my type but I’ll make the sacrifice", "vault_lines": ["On double MG, I'll fuck a rapper's wife"], "meaning": "“Double M G” refers to Maybach Music Group (MMG), the label founded by Rick Ross and home to artists like French Montana. Drake is not directly associated with MMG, but he has collaborated with both Rick Ross and French Montana frequently, and by swearing on the label name, he stress on the seriousness of his statement. These bars highlights Drake’s approach when he tries to antagonize his enemies particularly by targeting that said enemy’s romantic partner, especially by hinting at or toying with the idea of perceived infidelity.\n\nHere are several instances where Drake has been rumored to involve himself with women connected to his rivals:\n\n\nSerena Williams (linked to Common): During Drake and Common’s feud, their tension was reportedly fueled by Drake’s connection with Serena Williams, whom Common had been romantically involved with.\nBlac Chyna (linked to Tyga): Drake seemingly targeted Tyga by flirting with Blac Chyna, Tyga’s former partner, and referencing her in subliminal shots during their beef.\nRihanna (linked to Chris Brown and A$AP Rocky): Drake’s relationship with Rihanna added fuel to his long-standing rivalry with Chris Brown and deintely lead to great tension in his relationship with Rocky.\nKim Kardashian (linked to Kanye West): The speculation surrounding “In My Feelings” being about Kim Kardashian deeply upset Kanye West. The tension escalated when fans speculated that the lyric “Kiki, do you love me?” referred to Kim, who has the nickname “Kiki.” Kanye’s reaction indicated the effectiveness of Drake’s tactic.  https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GYM9HP7fpLs", "annotation_id": "34235982", "url": "https://genius.com/34235982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Splash Brothers", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/French-montana-splash-brothers-original-lyrics", "genius_title": "Splash Brothers (Original)", "genius_id": 10633258, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "Posted like Ibaka, got your wifey runnin’ brains", "vault_lines": ["got your wifey runnin' brains"], "meaning": "Famous “post” basketball player, Serge Ibaka has recently joined the Toronto Raptors. He has also has been involved in an altercation with Robin Lopez, a player for the Bulls, during a game.\n\n\n\nAlso, notice the homophone between Ibaka and I’m Baka, after his own name.", "annotation_id": "14633623", "url": "https://genius.com/14633623", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "She say I look like Usher when I’m trappin’ in the rain Trap, trap, trappin’ in the rain She say I look like Usher when I’m trappin’ in the rain", "vault_lines": ["She say I look like Usher when I'm trappin' in the rain", "Trap, trap, trappin' in the rain"], "meaning": "Usher is an RnB superstar who has been known to record music videos in the rain, as seen as in his hit “U Got It Bad.”\n\nThis hook comes only days after Baka’s performance with Drake at the Billboard Music Awards, where the two performed “Gyalchester” in the famed Fountains of Bellagio at the Bellagio Las Vegas. Drake won 13 awards that night, surpassing Adele’s previous record set in 2012.", "annotation_id": "14633619", "url": "https://genius.com/14633619", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "You niggas move like the cops, don’t make me pepper your block", "vault_lines": ["You niggas move like the cops, don't make me pepper your block"], "meaning": "Baka means don’t let him make your block hot by shooting up the block which will attract police. Lil Wayne actually has an older song titled “ Tha Block is Hot”. Baka cleverly substitutes the actual term with pepper because pepper is “hot”.\n\n\n\n This is a term used in areas where crime is committed which in  fact means ruining the conducting of the illegal activities on the block that generates a flow of money.", "annotation_id": "14633617", "url": "https://genius.com/14633617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "I went to jail the day they released Men in Black When I came out, Timberlake was bringin’ Sexy Back", "vault_lines": ["I went to jail the day they released Men in Black", "When I came out, Timberlake was bringin' Sexy Back"], "meaning": "Men in Black was released on July 2, 1997, by Columbia Pictures, and grossed over $589.3 million worldwide against a $90 million budget, becoming the year’s third highest-grossing film, with an estimated 54,616,700 tickets sold in the US.\n\n\n\n“SexyBack” is a song recorded by American singer-songwriter Justin Timberlake for his second studio album, FutureSex/LoveSounds (2006). It was released on July 18, 2006, to US mainstream and rhythmic radio stations[3] by Jive Records as the lead single from the album.\n\nSo Baka was locked up from 1997 to 2006. He’s using pop culture references to give a time line.", "annotation_id": "14633614", "url": "https://genius.com/14633614", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "Niggas bringin’ talks to me, you better have a plan", "vault_lines": ["Niggas bringin' talks to me, you better have a plan"], "meaning": "Talks is Toronto slang which refers to talking shit, so he’s saying if you’re running your mouth to me you better have a plan otherwise you’re dead.", "annotation_id": "14633608", "url": "https://genius.com/14633608", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "Don’t make me turn this red light on your head like you Roxanne", "vault_lines": ["Don't make me turn this red light on your head like you Roxanne"], "meaning": "Drake threatens his potential victim by warning them of having to point his red laser beam at their head to assist with aim when shooting. The Roxanne reference is referring to Roxanne, a song by The Police. The song is about red light districts, which is infamous for a high concentration of prostitutes. Usually prostitutes come from many different backgrounds including human trafficking, which coincidentally the one who Drake wrote this for, Baka, was charged with but later dropped,in 2015.", "annotation_id": "14633598", "url": "https://genius.com/14633598", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "A.k.a. the man, a.k.a. I never ran", "vault_lines": ["A.K.A. the man, A.K.A. I never ran"], "meaning": "AKA is a common acronym that stands for ‘also known as’.\n\nDrake is saying he is also known as the man, and he doesn’t run from beef, alluding from he previous line, “This is not a playground, ain’t no fist fight bitch I’m blastin,” he won’t run from his problems instead he is the man, he confronts them upfront.", "annotation_id": "14633596", "url": "https://genius.com/14633596", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "I’ve been on so many flights, it’s like my record’s clear now", "vault_lines": ["I've been on so many flights, it's like my record's clear now"], "meaning": "Referring to Baka again, having a criminal record usually bars the holder from traveling internationally, unlike those who have never committed criminal offenses. Baka is stating that he’s been on so many flights that it’s almost almost as if he never committed any kind of crime and always had a clear criminal record.", "annotation_id": "14633591", "url": "https://genius.com/14633591", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "Bitch, I’m tryna fuck, I’ve been inside for thirteen years now", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, I'm tryna fuck, I've been inside for thirteen years now"], "meaning": "This track was written for Baka and he was imprisoned for involvement in a human trafficking case.\n\nIn prison, as a male, you will not find any females to engage in amorous activities with unless you get a conjugal visit. Therefore, being imprisoned for long periods of time often imply your involuntary abstinence. Drake implies that now that Baka is out, free, and enjoying life – he needs to let you his stance. He is trying to engage in coitus with you.", "annotation_id": "14633588", "url": "https://genius.com/14633588", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "If I tatted tears, I’d be cryin’ to my beard, man", "vault_lines": ["If I tatted tears, I'd be cryin' to my beard, man"], "meaning": "Tears as facial tattoos under one’s eyes are used to show that the person with the tattoos has killed someone, but this is not the only meaning. Each tear represents a victim, and Baka claims that he has killed enough people to have tear tattoos run from his eyes to his beard.\n\nA notable example of a tear tattoo in hip hop is Lil Wayne, but his tattoo represents the loss of a friend.", "annotation_id": "14633586", "url": "https://genius.com/14633586", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "line": "Louis pouch across my body, this is not for fashion", "vault_lines": ["Louis pouch across my body, this is not for fashion"], "meaning": "Drake is referring to his designer messenger bag  which has become a trend within the  Hip hop industry. Many artists came to endorse this choice of fashion, including 2 Chainz and A$AP Rocky.\n\n\n\nHowever, Drake is not wearing his Louis Vuitton pouch as a means of fashion, he probably uses this to carry around his gun, money or drugs.", "annotation_id": "14633583", "url": "https://genius.com/14633583", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Live Up to My Name", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Baka-not-nice-and-drake-live-up-to-my-name-drake-reference-lyrics", "genius_title": "Live Up to My Name (Drake Reference)", "genius_id": 3386807, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Yeah, we got the Roc in this muthafucka", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "JAY-Z’s Rocafella crew. Jigga started the Roc up himself back in 1996 so he could release his debut record, Reasonable Doubt, when major labels refused to sign him. The label (and ROC Nation since 2008) has since signed various artists such as Kanye West, Rihanna, J.Cole, Beanie Seagle, Memphis Bleek and others.\n\nFunnily enough, Drake would reveal he used to want be on Rocafella when he was younger on his 2016 single, Summer Sixteen.\n\n‘'I used to wanna be on Rocafella then I turned into JAY’'", "annotation_id": "36159912", "url": "https://genius.com/36159912", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Long knife, don’t like to ask twice You killin’ me baby, this the past life", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The lines “Long knife, don’t like to ask twice / You killing me baby, this the past life” convey a sense of urgency and intensity. Here’s a breakdown of the meaning:\n“Long knife, don’t like to ask twice” This suggests a person who is decisive and possibly ruthless, implying they take action without hesitation or needing to ask again.\n“You killing me baby, this the past life” This could mean that the speaker feels deeply hurt or affected by someone, possibly in a romantic context. “This the past life” might suggest that these feelings are tied to past experiences or relationships.\nOverall, the lines imply a combination of decisiveness and emotional intensity, with a nod to past experiences influencing the present.", "annotation_id": "32190492", "url": "https://genius.com/32190492", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Made five-thousand look like fifty", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A reference to banknote straps. $5,000 is the amount of a bank strap of $50 notes; 100 notes are in a strap. He’s saying that he can make all of that money just look like one 50 when he throws money at the strip club.", "annotation_id": "27277594", "url": "https://genius.com/27277594", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "I’m really about to go Spring Break now and start taking shots in this muthafucka And you didn’t have all that ass last month girl, what, you get some shots in this muthafucka?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake uses a triple-entendre on the word “shots.” In this case, Drake will insult his detractors (“taking shots”) the same way that college students are known to take shots of alcohol during Spring Break.\n\nTo  continue the analogy, Drake speculates that a girl he’s seeing got butt injections to enhance her figure.", "annotation_id": "23950718", "url": "https://genius.com/23950718", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Uh, Young Money, power, respect in this bitch, you would think we got the LOX in this muthafucka", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Released in 1998, “Money, Power & Respect” is the second single from The LOX’s debut album of the same name.\n\nThe phrase, which originated in the cult-classic gangster movie Scarface, is commonly associated with the key elements for success in both the music and drug industries.\n\nDrake alters the phrase by adding “young” to the beginning, a reference to Young Money, the record label Drake was signed at the time of this song’s release. Young Money’s co-founder Lil Wayne is also featured on the track.", "annotation_id": "23946960", "url": "https://genius.com/23946960", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "I spend all my time in Houston, smoke that kush, but I don’t do Whitney I don’t do Britney, I don’t do Lindsay", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake is known for his admiration for the city of Houston, Texas. Ever since his career began, the Toronto native has paid homage to Houston in his music; his 2009 track “November 18th” is influenced by the music of legendary Houston producer DJ Screw. His song “Houstonlantavegas” is also a tribute, in part, to the city.\n\nDrake makes wordplay out of “Houston” and references Whitney Houston, the iconic pop/R&B singer who became famous for hits such as “I Will Always Love You” and “I Wanna Dance With Somebody” before her untimely drug-related death on February 11, 2012.\n\nAdditionally, “Whitney” is a slang term for cocaine. Whitney Houston was known for her drug use, and it is believed that cocaine played a role in her death. Drake also name-checks pop singer Britney Spears and actress Lindsay Lohan, both of whom have been known in the past for their drug abuse, with both of their names serving as slang terms to cocaine.\n\n\n\nAlthough Drake has admitted to drug usage in the past, the rapper has primarily stayed away from drugs. In a 2019 Rap Radar interview, Drake claimed to not be doing any drugs.", "annotation_id": "19455420", "url": "https://genius.com/19455420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "(Lights on) And I’m on one, I got one", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake references his May 2011 feature on DJ Khaled’s hit track “I’m On One,” which also featured a guest verse from Lil Wayne:\n\nI’m on one, yeah, fuck it I’m on one\nYeah, I said “I’m on one”, fuck it I’m on one\n\nThe phrase “I’m on one” refers to an intoxicated person who’s feeling the effects of promethazine and codeine. As the end of the verse indicated, Drake further discusses his relationship with lean and his inebriated state.", "annotation_id": "17599946", "url": "https://genius.com/17599946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Bright lights, night lights, headlights, shh No lights but them infrared lights", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Wayne further interpolates Rihanna’s post-chorus from the original version of this track:\n\nFast cars, shooting stars\nAll of the lights, all of the lights\n\nThe use of lights represent a different part of Wayne’s professional and personal life. The “bright lights” refer to the constant media attention and press he receives, the “night lights” refer to the partying scene he participates in, the “headlights” refer to the exotic cars he drives, and “the infrared lights” refer to the laser sites on guns.\n\nWayne previously described infrared scopes on his June 2004 track, “BM J.R.”:\n\nDamn, and if the red dot spot ‘em then the hollow head got 'em", "annotation_id": "17599262", "url": "https://genius.com/17599262", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Now I’m spendin’ time, you in the blind", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne creates a double entendre in these lines. He further references the wordplay with time and money by describing the way he spends his time with a woman.\n\nIn addition, he describes the process of purchasing expensive watches that are encrusted with diamonds. Wayne uses hyperbole and claims that the luster of the metals he possesses are enough to “blind” the woman that he’s with.\n\nWayne previously referenced the diamonds on his watches on his May 2014 track, “Believe Me”:\n\nDiamonds in my Rollie face cannot be exfoliated", "annotation_id": "17599092", "url": "https://genius.com/17599092", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "If we could buy time, I’d spend my last money", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne references the famous quote from the famous historical figure Benjamin Franklin:\n\nRemember that Time is Money. He that can earn Ten Shillings a Day by his Labour, and goes abroad, or sits idle one half of that Day, tho’ he spends but Sixpence during his Diversion or Idleness, ought not to reckon that the only Expence; he has really spent or rather thrown away Five Shillings besides.\n\nWayne interpolates the saying to discuss his own life, as he’s unable to get the one thing he truly desires despite his net worth of $120 million.\n\nWayne later rapped a similar line on his September 2018 track, “Open Letter”:\n\n‘Cause if we could buy time every store would sell it\n\nHe also likely references his recent stint in prison in November 2010, where he was detained eight months for “attempted gun possession.” Wayne would rather spend some of his money and regain the time he missed in prison than to live an abbreviated life that frequently features time in jail.", "annotation_id": "17597266", "url": "https://genius.com/17597266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "In this “give-or-take” world, I got what it takes", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne responds to Rihanna’s post-chorus on the track’s original version:\n\nIf you want it, you can get it for the rest of your life\nIf you want it, you can get it for the rest of your life\n\nWayne describes his “go-getter” attitude and refusal to quit, which allows his to achieve all of his goals and aspirations. He also references the famous saying that it’s “a give or take” world, which means that there are people who make proactive actions and accomplish their dreams, while others aren’t as fast and lose out on opportunities. Wayne’s net worth and successful career are evidence of his approach to life.", "annotation_id": "17597013", "url": "https://genius.com/17597013", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "It’s cold as hell, I show and tell", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne utilizes an oxymoron in the first half of the line, as the spiritual underworld is widely considered to be hot. As a result, Wayne likely alludes to his recent stint in prison in November 2010, where he was detained eight months for “attempted gun possession.”\n\nThe second half of the line is a play on the classic writing technique “show don’t tell.” Wayne boasts about his vivid wordplay and clever lyricism, which allows him to do both.", "annotation_id": "17594643", "url": "https://genius.com/17594643", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Weezy home, our nigga out of jail", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne interpolates Kanye West’s first verse from the original track and mimics his delivery of the iconic lines:\n\nSomething wrong, I hold my head\nMJ gone, our nigga dead\n\nWayne also alludes to his lengthy history in prison and reflects on his release. In particular, Wayne references his premature release from prison in November 2010, where he was detained eight months for “attempted gun possession”.", "annotation_id": "17594481", "url": "https://genius.com/17594481", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "This little light of mine, I’ma let it shine", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne interpolates the 1939 song “This Little Light of Mine,” a classic gospel track that was popularized by Sam Cooke’s rendition:\n\nThis little light of mine\nI’m going to let it shine", "annotation_id": "17593529", "url": "https://genius.com/17593529", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Big slices, that’s how you cut the cake", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne plays on the dual meaning of the slang word “cake” to form a double entendre. One interpretation of the line is that Wayne is planning on equally dividing the money that his YMCMB record label brings in. The label, boasting big name artists like Drake and Nicki Minaj, is among the most successful in rap history and is predominantly owned by Wayne. As a result, he’s able to give a large allowance out to his family and his friends who supported him from the start.\n\nAdditionally, “cake” can also refer to a girl’s butt so Wayne discusses his desire for sexual intercourse with larger girls.\n\nWayne used a similar analogy on his September 2010 track, “I Don’t Like the Look of It”:\n\nI blow your candles out, now nigga cut the cake", "annotation_id": "17593156", "url": "https://genius.com/17593156", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "I see you dreaming, but you still awake", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne juxtaposes the two states of consciousness to form a double entendre. One potential interpretation is that he describes the girl’s daydreaming tendencies and inclination to want expensive items and possessions.\n\nIn addition, Wayne potentially alludes to a “lucid dream,” a realistic experience where a dream feels like reality. Weezy implies that he is able to make the women’s innermost thoughts and dreams a reality, a strong possible due to his net worth of approximately $120 million.", "annotation_id": "17593013", "url": "https://genius.com/17593013", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "This is history, your kids, kids, kids, kids, kids gon’ book report me, what?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Sean boasts about the superstar lifestyle he lives and boldly claims that he’s writing his own spot in the history books. At the time of this track’s release, Sean had only released three mixtapes and was in the process of making his debut album Finally Famous, but his goals and aspirations were much higher.\n\nHe later furthered these claims on his August 2013 album Hall of Fame, where he delved into his placement in hip hop and reflected on his legacy.", "annotation_id": "17592638", "url": "https://genius.com/17592638", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "I’m Young Money, I’m Cash Money", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Lil Wayne shows his allegiance to Cash Money record label and its subsidiary Young Money Entertainment, which is also home to featured artist Drake.\n\nLil Wayne frequently shouts out his label in his music, perhaps most notably on his May 2008 track, “Got Money”:\n\n“\"Young Money, Lil Wayne” then the music drop\nI make it snow, I make it flurry\nI make it all back tomorrow, don’t worry\nYeah, young Wayne on them hoes\nA.K.A Mr. Make-It-Rain-On-Them-Hoes\nYoung Money", "annotation_id": "17591946", "url": "https://genius.com/17591946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "I’m shitting on every statistic that said I’d be dead or fuckin’ broke", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Big Sean comes from Detroit, the second most dangerous city in America. At the time of this song’s release, the violent crime rate was roughly 22%.\n\n\n\nNot only that, but the poverty rate is almost 40%, unemployment rate is 12.4%, and Detroit has one of the lowest educational attainments. But despite the chances of Big Sean making it big were slim, he managed to become a successful rapper with a net worth of $12 million.", "annotation_id": "12903070", "url": "https://genius.com/12903070", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "She eat me up, she got expensive taste", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Weezy does some wordplay with the eating. His girl could be eating really high quality food that is expensive; but, he’s most likely referring to her giving him a blowjob. He is considers himself expensive, seeing as he made a song calling himself the best rapper alive.", "annotation_id": "12903052", "url": "https://genius.com/12903052", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "[Intro: Choir] (Lights, lights) All of the lights", "vault_lines": ["(All of the lights), lights", "Strobe lights, street lights (All of the lights, all of the lights)", "Rock life every night (All of the lights)", "Want you to see all of the lights", "All of the lights, all of the lights", "Until it's Vegas everywhere we are (All of the lights)"], "meaning": "By turning up the lights, Kanye and his celebrity pals want to enlighten us on how their lives really are. As the intro of this album says:\n\nYou might think you’ve peeped the scene\nYou haven’t, the real one’s far too mean.\n\n\n\nBy singing “turn up the lights in here, baby” and “want you to see everything”, the female vocalist (Rihanna) is inviting the listener into a private sphere. It can be viewed as a precursor to sex, sex with the lights on, or as Rihanna exposing all her secrets for our knowledge. Perhaps we are about to take a trip to the ghetto university ourselves?", "annotation_id": "8860189", "url": "https://genius.com/8860189", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "’Cause you spent all day with her spooning while I spent all night with her forking", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Playing on the fact that a spoon and fork are both cutlery.\n\nSpooning is when two people lay together whilst facing the same direction to resemble two spoons being stacked. The activity is more sentimental rather than sexual as Sean implies that this guy has been spending all day trying get to know this girl by acting as the nice guy whereas he just arrives at night and forks (fucks – has sex) with her.", "annotation_id": "4983609", "url": "https://genius.com/4983609", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "I take the purple and pour that all in the Sprite, all in the Sprite", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake shot a commercial for Sprite that ran during Superbowl XLIV.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R6Y8KioAaSg\n\nSprite is a common mixer used when making purple drink, a concoction consisting of promethazine-codeine cough syrup, a soft drink, and candy flavoring (typically Jolly Rancher.)", "annotation_id": "690437", "url": "https://genius.com/690437", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "To my surprise, nobody replacing me", "vault_lines": ["To my surprise, a nigga replacing me"], "meaning": "A play on Yeezy’s original line “To my surprise, a nigga replacing me”\nDrake’s claiming that he is not repleacable, could it be a sequel to the Drake/Kanye silent “feud” ?", "annotation_id": "143998", "url": "https://genius.com/143998", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "You thought niggas was going pop in this muthafucka?", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Thanks in no small part to Drake’s pop singles like “Find Your Love”, as well as the recent pop successes of Lil' Wayne and Nicki Minaj, a lot of people think that Young Money is toning down their hip-hop aspects in order to reach a mainstream audience.\n\nDrake’s counter-argument is that he’s going HAM on this shit, so anyone who thinks Young Money’s gone pop can kindly suck it.\n\nOR…\nThere were rumors that Drake and Wayne had beef with each other because drake wanted to make an album with Wayne first, but then Jay-Z and Kanye later said they were doing the same. These rumors were false, so Drake is saying “we have the ROC in the mutherfucker, you thought niggas was gon pop in this motherfucker?” because they are all cool with each other. Blogs thought otherwise.\n\nAND, it’s a continuation of the last line, pop rocks the candy..hence..got the ROC in this muthafucka, you thought niggas was gon POP in this muthafucka", "annotation_id": "143209", "url": "https://genius.com/143209", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Take Care and Carter IV, it won’t be long ’til they can see the lights", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s 2nd album Take Care was in the works; he was also advertising Weezy’s much-anticipated album Carter IV which was to be out soon (Weezy is notorious for releasing his albums later than planned – keeping them cooped up in the cave, as it were)\n\nTake Care and Carter IV have since been released to significant commercial success. Both seeing Grammy Nominations for Rap Album of The Year.", "annotation_id": "143000", "url": "https://genius.com/143000", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "I took your chick, now you want beef, and I’m just chuckling like I’m Porky", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Reference to Porky Pig\n\n\n\nListen to Porky Pig’s Uber Laugh Here\n\nIt should be noted that he actually manages to work three types of meat into these last two lines:\nChick – Poultry\nBeef\nPorky – Pork\n\nAnother thing this refers back to the “Allah” line, as in in Islam, you may not eat pork. When he says “I’m just chuckling like I’m porky” that also means that he’s unfuckwitable.", "annotation_id": "142982", "url": "https://genius.com/142982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Why every rapper named “Big” got bodied, huh? But every rapper named “Sean” got money? Ooh Well, I guess my chances are fifty-fifty But my vision is 20/20, so I be counting a hundred hundreds", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Notorious B.I.G., Big Pun, Big L…Dead.\n\nJay-Z (Shawn Carter), Diddy (Sean Combs)…Money.\n\nBig Sean is both a “Big” and a “Sean” though! So who knows what’s going to happen…?\n\nSimilar to his line from the 2010 G.O.O.D. Music B.E.T. Cypher\n\nThe last two lines have some wordplay with the numbers, which are all repeated twice. Even though his odds are “50/50”, with his perfect “20/20” vision he’s counting “a hundred 100’s” which is $10,000.", "annotation_id": "142938", "url": "https://genius.com/142938", "state": "accepted", "votes": 109, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "All of the Lights", "line": "Put you out your misery: Kathy Bates", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A “punchline rap” reference to the Stephen King novel – turned movie, Misery – in which Kathy Bates plays a fat girl who will slice you\n\n\n\nKanye has rapped a similar line", "annotation_id": "142898", "url": "https://genius.com/142898", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
