{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "WAIT FOR U (Mixed) [June 2022]", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Future-wait-for-u-mixed-june-2022-lyrics", "genius_title": "WAIT FOR U (Mixed) [June 2022]", "genius_id": 10504425, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "All of these hoes I made off records I produced I might take all my exes and put ’em all in a group (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["All of these hoes I made off records I produced"], "meaning": "This line could be about Rihanna. Travis had production and vocals on the 2016 song “Woo,” on her album ANTi, and had production on her hit single, “Bitch Better Have My Money,” which peaked at number 15 on the Billboard Hot 100. Travis and Rihanna dated in 2015.\n\nHe could also be making a general statement that his sound molded the current rap scene.\n\nThe way Travis Scott pronounces the word “hoes” also makes it sound like “O’s”, which could refer to all the zeros in his bank account that he made off records he produced. Interpreted this way, the “O’s” in the first line juxtaposes nicely with the “X’s” in the next line.  Taken together with the first-level meaning of these lines (a reference to his ex-girlfriends), this is a hidden “XO” message, signaling to those ex-girlfriends that he’s just playing around and still has affection for them.", "annotation_id": "23999029", "url": "https://genius.com/23999029", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "I be spinnin’ Valley, circle blocks ’til I’m dizzy (Yeah, what?)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Valley Circle is a major street that runs through San Fernando Valley in Los Angeles. It connects the valley to the affluent Hidden Hills community in Calabasas where Drake, as well as many other celebrities, reside. Famous Calabasas residents include Kanye and Kim Kardashian-West, Justin Bieber, Jennifer Lopez, and the Rock, amongst others.", "annotation_id": "15985415", "url": "https://genius.com/15985415", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "[Bridge: Big Hawk & Swae Lee] Some-Some-Some-Someone said To win the retreat, we all in too deep Pl-Pl-Playin’ for keeps, don’t play us for weak (Someone said) To win the retreat, we all in too deep Pl-Pl-Playin’ for keeps, don’t play us for weak", "vault_lines": ["Pl-Pl-Playin' for keeps, don't play us for weak (Someone said)"], "meaning": "Travis Scott is paying homage to Houston rapper, Big Hawk who was murdered in 2006.\n\nHawk was a member of the Screwed Up Click (S.U.C), a Houston rap group, which also featured DJ Screw who is the subject of the song “R.I.P. SCREW.”\n\n\n\nIn an interview with Rolling Stone, Travis' longtime A&R/collaborator Sickamore mentioned that he felt like Travis wasn’t getting the credit he deserved as a rapper. Travis shared this sentiment, feeling as though people weren’t respecting him within the rap community.\n\nThis chorus is almost a direct shot back at those who doubt Travis. It speaks to his mentality regarding how he’s coming to attack the rap game. He’s “playing for keeps” and warning everyone not to “play him for weak.”", "annotation_id": "15985406", "url": "https://genius.com/15985406", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Baby mama cover Forbes, got these other bitches shook Yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Kylie Jenner—the mother of Travis' daughter Stormi—was featured on the cover of the August 2018 edition of Forbes magazine. The magazine issue, which covers 60 of the richest self-made women notes:\n\nAt 21, [Kylie] is set to be the youngest ever self-made billionaire.\n\n\n\nKylie landed #27 on the Forbes list. Thanks to her make up company Kylie Cosmetics, Kylie’s brand has sold over 630 million products making her $330 million in 2017. In addition to that, Kylie makes millions from TV programs like  Keeping Up with the Kardashians as well as endorsements from products such as Puma and Pacsun.", "annotation_id": "15979487", "url": "https://genius.com/15979487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Pass this to my daughter, I’ma show her what it took (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "On February 1st, 2018, Travis Scott and Kylie Jenner welcomed a daughter, Stormi, into the world. The pregnancy of Kylie attracted lots of media speculation as to whether Kylie was actually bearing his child, as the couple kept this matter private.\n\nIn July 2018, Kylie was named one of the richest celebrities in the world, being worth a whopping $900 million at 21-years old. Still, people have been debating the claim that she’s “self-made” like Forbes claims. Travis will instill the importance of hard work into Stormi, the same way he had to work to become one of the biggest names in hip-hop.", "annotation_id": "15979485", "url": "https://genius.com/15979485", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "’Bout a check, yeah, (Check) just check the foots, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Travis is playing on the Nike logo, which resembles a check. Drake also rapped a similar line earlier on this song:\n\nJesus Christ (yeah) checks over stripes (yeah)\n\n\n\nTravis wearing Nike Air Jordan 1 Fragments\n\n\nFurthermore, Travis has an “Air Force 1” collab with Nike that was released on the 10th of August, 2018—a week after the release of this song.", "annotation_id": "15979483", "url": "https://genius.com/15979483", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Ain’t by the book, yeah, this how it look, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“By the book” means to follow directions. This line ties back to an earlier line where Travis says he doesn’t follow suit. Travis sports eclectic fashion attire and has seemingly pioneered a unique aesthetic of punk-rock rawness blended with drug-fuelled trap music.", "annotation_id": "15979482", "url": "https://genius.com/15979482", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "We back on the road, they jumpin’ off, no parachute, yeah Shawty in the back, she said she workin’ on her glutes, yeah (Oh my God)", "vault_lines": ["Shawty in the back, she said she workin' on her glutes, yeah (Oh my God)"], "meaning": "In 2017, Kylie Jenner joined Travis during The DAMN. Tour, after connecting at Coachella. As explained in their joint GQ interview:\n\nCoachella was one of the stops on his tour. So he said, ‘I’m going back on tour—what do we want to do about this?’ Because we obviously liked each other.\n\nApparently, Kylie had to stay in shape on the road and improve her butt through a glute workout.", "annotation_id": "15979468", "url": "https://genius.com/15979468", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "LaFerrari to Jamba Juice, yeah (Skrrt, skrrt)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A LaFerrari is a luxury car model, manufactured by Ferrari. In February 2018, Travis Scott gifted his girlfriend Kylie Jenner a rare $1.4 million model of this car, celebrating the birth of their baby, Stormi.\n\nHe also mentions the car on the fourteenth track of ASTROWORLD, “WHO? WHAT!”:\n\nBrand new LaFerrari (woo), my bitch ride iconic\n\nhttps://s.yimg.com/ny/api/res/1.2/vnsAi5FqtEaH3JpQzq8w2A--~A/YXBwaWQ9aGlnaGxhbmRlcjtzbT0xO3c9ODAw/http://media.zenfs.com/en-US/homerun/people_218/7dcbc4dc2fd61fb7b43dbad35904408c\n\nJamba Juice is a juice bar company, founded in 1990, that specializes in fruit bowls, baked goods and most prominently, smoothies. The same month Travis purchased the Ferrari, he and Kylie were photographed there. Travis extrapolated on his apparent love for Jamba Juice during his October 2018 appearance on Jimmy Fallon’s Tonight Show, which he claims Kylie Jenner introduced him to.", "annotation_id": "15979464", "url": "https://genius.com/15979464", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Yeah, passed the dawgs a celly Sendin’ texts, ain’t sendin’ kites, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, passed the dawgs a celly"], "meaning": "A “kite” in street culture is a letter sent to and from individuals caught up in the prison system. Phone usage is limited in prison so many turn to the postal service to keep in contact with the outside world, though the process can be slow.\n\nTravis gives mobile phones to his comrades in prison so they can communicate in real time. It could be risky if the prison prohibits such items.", "annotation_id": "15979435", "url": "https://genius.com/15979435", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Like where is he? (Yeah, what?) No one seen him (Yeah, yeah) I’m tryna clean ’em (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake allegedly wants to come after Kanye West as he is the reason why Pusha T found out about Drake’s son and the Adidas deal BEFORE Drake could make the news public himself.\n\nWe first hear about the news in Pusha T’s diss track called “The Story of Adidon” The Budden speculation goes that Drake came clean about it to Kanye West while they were working on Ye’s album together but then Kanye backbit Drake and told Pusha T about it so he could use it in his diss track and expose him.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/ernestbaker/status/1034828334570643456\n\nIronically, Travis Scott is a producer for G.O.O.D. Music, Kanye’s record label.", "annotation_id": "15979419", "url": "https://genius.com/15979419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Wet like I’m Lizzie", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "After shouting out Devin Booker in the previous line, Drake then gives praises to WNBA star Liz Cambage–who plays for the Dallas Wings.\n\nAs the previous annotation explains, “wet” is used to describe players who make a lot of shots, and in 2018, Liz broke the WNBA record for most points in a game by scoring 53.\n\n\n\nLiz reacted to the the shout-out via Twitter by posting retweet of the audio.", "annotation_id": "15979408", "url": "https://genius.com/15979408", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "See the shots that I took (Ayy), wet like I’m Book (Ayy)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Devin Booker is a young shooting guard for the Phoenix Suns, who won the 2018 NBA All-Star 3 point contest, and scored 70 points in a game versus the Celtics on March 24, 2017 – the first player to score 70+ points since Kobe’s 81 point game on January 22, 2006. Booker and Drake have shown each other mutual respect in the past: Drake wore Booker’s jersey to his Phoenix show, and Booker responding on Twitter, calling him his “brother.”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LDbeAjbkp0Q\n\n“Wet” is a term for a player who makes a lot of shots. Drake’s “shots” are the bars he makes on his diss tracks. Whether it be against Meek Mill, JAY-Z, Pusha T, or anyone else Drake has gone against, he feels like he’s hit his shots enough to deem them “wet.”", "annotation_id": "15979406", "url": "https://genius.com/15979406", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "When I shoot my shot, that shit wetty like I’m Sheck (Bitch!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake pays homage to Cactus Jack signee and Harlem rapper Sheck Wes. The young model, basketball player, and artist gained significant attention with the release of his single “Mo Bamba.” This line features Sheck Wes' adlib, “bitch!” from the same track.\n\nThis line is also a response to Sheck Wes' shoutout to Drake on “Mo Bamba”:\n\nCall me Drake how a nigga controlla\n\nTravis brought Sheck Wes out at his inaugural Governors Ball set in July 2018 and the two performed “Mo Bamba” together.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gxL_qoIGTHI&t=1920s\n\nAdditionally, Sheck Wes is featured on the track “NO BYSTANDERS” along with Juice WRLD.", "annotation_id": "15979404", "url": "https://genius.com/15979404", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Yeah, this-this shit way too formal, y’all know I don’t follow suit", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, this-this shit way too formal, y'all know I don’t follow suit"], "meaning": "In their joint interview with GQ, Kylie described Travis' feelings toward the spotlight:\n\nHe was whispering to me the whole time. He just doesn’t like taking the photos.\n\nThe interview continued, saying Travis hates anything that “slows him down.”\n\nAlthough Travis claims to not like the celebrity life, he was spotted out with Kylie Jenner at mega-events, like the 2018 Met Gala.\n\n\n\nThis is also a play of words in that a “suit” is a type of formal attire but he is also saying that he doesn’t “follow suit” or conform to society’s rules. An example of this is in 2015 at the Lollapalooza music festival when he encouraged his fans–a.k.a ragers–to bypass security and rush the stage during his set, leading to his subsequent arrest on charges of inciting a riot.", "annotation_id": "15979402", "url": "https://genius.com/15979402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Jesus Christ (Yeah), checks over stripes (Yeah) That’s what I like (Yeah), that’s what we like (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Nike’s logo is a checkmark, while Adidas’s logo and branding feature three parallel stripes. Drake and Travis Scott have both released Nike sneaker collaborations under the standard Nike and Jordan umbrellas.\n\n\n\nIn early 2018, it was suspected that Drake’s allegiances had switched from Nike to Adidas, and that he had an upcoming collection with Adidas that was yet to be unveiled.\n\nHowever, any plans for a collection that may have been in the works were dashed when Pusha-T dropped the diss track “The Story of Adidon” in May, exposing Drake’s intentions to publicly unveil his son Adonis with the “Adidon” collection. A month later, Drake was seen celebrating the release of Scorpion in a Nike tracksuit.\n\nPreferring checks over stripes could also allude to prioritizing the acquisition of wealth (checks) over earning street credibility (earning one’s stripes). Thus, this may be considered as a subliminal towards Pusha T, who discussed  “earning stripes” on “If You Know You Know”:\n\nWhere were you when Big Meech brought the tigers in?\n‘Cause I was busy earnin’ stripes like a tiger’s skin", "annotation_id": "15979397", "url": "https://genius.com/15979397", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Nothin’ nice (Yeah), baguettes in my ice (Aw, man)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A baguette-cut, is a square-cut diamond which is thought to resemble the shape of a baguette—a type of French bread. Often used in a ring set or as accent stones for a larger piece, baguettes are cheaper than most other diamond cuts due to their more visible inclusions.", "annotation_id": "15979395", "url": "https://genius.com/15979395", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Niggas think it’s sweet (Nah, never), it’s on sight (Yeah, what?)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A throwback to his brief beef with Common from 2011. Common had a 2011 song dissing Drake, called “Sweet,” which didn’t mention his name, but it was later confirmed in an interview with SwayInTheMorning that the lines were directed towards Drake.\n\nDrake responded on Rick Ross' 2012 song, “Stay Schemin,” where he raps:\n\nIt bothers me when the gods get to actin' like the broads\nGuess every team doesn’t come complete with niggas like ours\nThat’s why I see no need to compete with niggas like y’all\n\nCommon responded quickly on a remix of “Stay Schemin” and continued to take shots at Drake:\n\nYou so black and white, trying to live a nigga’s life\nI’m taking too long with this amateur guy\nYou ain’t wet nobody, nigga, you Canada dry\n\nThis line could also include a jab at Kanye who he has fired subliminal disses at previously throughout his most recent album at the time, Scorpion. “On Sight” is also the name of the first song off of Kanye’s album, Yeezus. Drake’s version of “on sight” has a different meaning than Kanye’s, which is to engage in violent activity on first glance of another person, while Kanye’s seems to be about police brutality, paparazzi, assumptions, and conclusions. It’s almost certain that a potential shot at Kanye definitely slipped passed Travis as he is dating the sister of Kanye’s wife, Kylie Jenner, who he refers to on the last line of his verse on this track. The Kanye diss could also have made it through considering that Drake’s verse was turned in the same day ASTROWORLD dropped.", "annotation_id": "15979392", "url": "https://genius.com/15979392", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "I crept down the block (Down the block) Made a right (Yeah, right) Cut the lights (Yeah, what?), paid the price (Yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In order to “settle his scores”, Drake goes down the block where his enemy was, “cut the lights” so he isn’t spotted and made his enemy “pay the price”. In this case, that enemy is Kanye West, who Drake had a beef with when he recorded his parts of the song. The “price paid” here is the loss of the alleged Adidas deal that was ruined by Kanye associate Pusha T’s diss track, “Story of Adidon”.\n\nFurther evidence supporting this theory is explained in this tweet, which compares the location of Drake’s house to Kanye’s house:\nhttps://twitter.com/tmorrison24/status/1035574650779181056\n\nDrake may also be talking metaphorically about his entry in the rap game and his very successful career while paying the price for everything that comes with success such as intimacy and family.", "annotation_id": "15979389", "url": "https://genius.com/15979389", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "I still got scores to settle, man", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake has pending vendettas. Namely, his beef with Kanye and Pusha T that was reignited in May 2018.\n\nJ. Prince allegedly convinced Drake not to release a “career ending diss record” towards Pusha T. Instead, Drake has been dropping subliminal bars in many of the verses that have been released since Pusha T’s “Infrared” and “The Story of Adidon.” For example, on his track, “8 Out of 10” he raps:\n\nKiss my son on the forehead then kiss your ass goodbye\nAs luck would have it, I’ve settled into my role as the good guy", "annotation_id": "15979384", "url": "https://genius.com/15979384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Knocked for the night, ayy 767, man, this shit got double bedroom, man", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake uses a commercial plane as a private jet hire. The Boeing 767 typically seats upwards of 300 people, and this customized version apparently has two bedrooms.\n\nInterestingly, Virgin America named one of their planes the “Air Drake” after him.", "annotation_id": "15979379", "url": "https://genius.com/15979379", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "I did half a Xan, thirteen hours ’til I land Had me out like a light, ayy, yeah Like a light, ayy, yeah", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Xanax is a prescription drug that is used to treat anxiety. “Out like a light” is an old expression meaning to be in deep sleep.\n\nDrake has mentioned his use of Xanax on flights before on the 2014 track, “Days In The East.”\n\nTake a flight here, pop a xan for the boy\n\nDrake could be referring to his trip to Dubai in March 2018, which from Toronto is just under 13 hours flight time. Drake’s love for Dubai is no secret.\n\n\n\nThe flow of the lines are interpolated from Famous Dex’s hit song, “JAPAN”:\n\nI just popped a xan, fifty thousand in Japan (fifty thousand in Japan)", "annotation_id": "15977907", "url": "https://genius.com/15977907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Back in high school, I used to bust it to the dance Now I hit the FBO with duffels in my hands Back in high school, I used to bust it to the dance", "vault_lines": ["Back in high school, I used to bust it to the dance"], "meaning": "An FBO or fixed base operator, is an airport building offering services to clients on private flights.\n\nOne of the FBO perks is the ability to carry as many bags as possible when entering the plane, unlike most commercial airlines which only allow one complimentary carry on bag.\n\nDrake juxtaposes this sign of wealth with the days where he had to use public transportation to get to after-school dances.\n\nThis is also a reference to an episode of Degrassi in which Drake’s character Jimmy literally had to take a bus to get to the dance.", "annotation_id": "15977904", "url": "https://genius.com/15977904", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Who put this shit together? I’m the glue", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Travis is not only an artist, but also a curator in the sense that he plays a directorial role in his songs and projects. This is shown by the many featured artists that Travis utilises uniquely across his discography, most notably T.I.’s spoken word and narration on Rodeo.\n\nASTROWORLD is no exception—especially on “SICKO MODE.” Drake is used as an opener and a closer, Swae Lee’s vocals are cut off before they finish and the late Big Hawk is sampled throughout as a tribute to Travis' hometown of Houston.", "annotation_id": "15977884", "url": "https://genius.com/15977884", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Now I got her open, it’s just the Goose", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Grey Goose is a well-known brand of vodka produced by Bacardi. His girl is open to his sexual advances.", "annotation_id": "15977882", "url": "https://genius.com/15977882", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "We ain’t even make it to the room She thought it was the ocean, it’s just the pool", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Travis and his amorous lady friend begin intercourse before reaching the bedroom. She thought his love ran deep like an ocean, but it was shallow like a pool.\n\nAlternatively, Travis could be referring to a literal swimming pool. Visual distortions are common side-effects from illicit drugs, such as cocaine. Macropsia, the neurological condition that causes small objects to appear large, is a common side-effect from using cocaine.", "annotation_id": "15977879", "url": "https://genius.com/15977879", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "She said, “Where we goin’?” I said, “The moon”", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Travis pays homage to Kid Cudi by alluding to his song “Dose of Dopeness.” In the song, Cudi asks a woman out to eat and when asked where they’re going he replies:\n\nThe moon…\n\nTravis and Kid Cudi collaborated on 2016’s “through the late night” and “way back.” Travis has said multiple times that Cudi was a large influence on him, going as far as to say:\n\nI idolize Kid Cudi a lot. That’s like, my favorite artist.\n\nTravis also has a 2013 song called “Dance on the Moon.”", "annotation_id": "15977878", "url": "https://genius.com/15977878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Had to slop the top off, it’s just a roof, uh", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The words “slop” and “top” usually make up the slang phrase “sloppy toppy,” meaning a blowjob. Travis plays on these words to convey that he’s receiving a blowjob while riding in a convertible vehicle with the top down. Interestingly, “Sloppy Toppy” is also the eighth track on Travis' 2014 mixtape Days Before Rodeo.", "annotation_id": "15977871", "url": "https://genius.com/15977871", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "In the 305, bitches treat me like I’m Uncle Luke (Don’t stop, pop that pussy!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“305” is the area code for Miami, Florida, where “Uncle Luke,” a member of the rap group 2 Live Crew, resides. He is best known for the 1992 hit “I Wanna Rock (Doo Doo Brown),” which happens to be sampled after this bar:\n\nDon’t stop pop that pussy\nLet me see you doo doo brown", "annotation_id": "15977850", "url": "https://genius.com/15977850", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "’Bout to turn this function to Bonnaroo (It’s lit) Told her, “Hop in, you comin’ too”", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The Bonnaroo Music and Arts Festival, is an annual four-day music festival that takes place in Manchester, Tennessee. Travis performed at the festival in 2017.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fpEhTa7A6rM", "annotation_id": "15977817", "url": "https://genius.com/15977817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Hit my eses, I need the bootch (yeah)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Ese” is usually used to describe Mexican-American gang members. Over time people in America have used it to address anyone of Latin origin, in general.\n\nBootch is slang for cocaine. The term originated in Washington Heights, New York. Latinos are widely known for the production of cocaine in South America. Kanye West also mentions hitting bootch on his 2018 track “Fire”:\n\nSniffed a lil bootch off the nail", "annotation_id": "15977593", "url": "https://genius.com/15977593", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Stacey Dash, most of these girls ain’t got a clue", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Actress Stacey Dash costars as Dionne alongside Alicia Silverstone in the 1995 cult classic film Clueless.\n\n\n\nDash is controversial within the black community for her conservative political opinions and Travis may consider her to be fittingly “clueless.”", "annotation_id": "15977590", "url": "https://genius.com/15977590", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "And they chokin’, man, know the crackers wish it was a noose", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The chains Travis has on his neck are as tight as a choker, and he claims that “crackers” – a derogatory term for white people that originated in the South during slavery – would rather see him with a noose around his neck.\n\n\n\nA noose is a rope with a running knot, most commonly associated with asphyxiation or hanging.  In the late 19th century and early 20th century, many African-Americans were lynched by mobs of white Americans.", "annotation_id": "15977259", "url": "https://genius.com/15977259", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Different colored chains, think my jeweler really sellin’ fruits", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In 2017, Travis commisioned a jeweler to make him a colorful “Flame Boy” chain. He once compared his chain to rainbow colored Fruit Loops cereal, on his 2016 track, “sweet sweet”:\n\nShout my town, shout my troops (shout, shout)\nShout my jeweler, made my chain look like Fruit Loops, yuh (it’s lit)\nCome get a taste, come get a scoop (scoop)", "annotation_id": "15977258", "url": "https://genius.com/15977258", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "I just landed in, Chase B mixes pop like Jamba Juice", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Chase B is a touring DJ and longtime friend of Travis Scott who has held residencies at nightclubs such as LIV, 1 OAK, and UP & Down.\n\nTravis compares his DJ’s ability to mix pop songs to the mixing involved at the smoothie retailer, Jamba Juice.\n\nIn 2016, Travis and Chase created their own radio show, .WAV Radio, on Apple Music’s Beats 1. Prior to the release of ASTROWORLD, they had not released a new episode since 2017. However, Travis confirmed the return of .WAV in an interview with Zane Lowe on Beats 1 leading up to the release of the album. Chase B also took to Twitter to confirm this.\n\nOn August 3rd, 2018, during a new .WAV Radio episode, Travis and Chase played three unreleased Travis songs that didn’t make it onto ASTROWORLD: “HOUDINI,” “PART TIME,” and “ZOOM.”\n\nFurthermore, three days after the release of his album, Travis Scott posted a picture of Chase B at Jamba Juice on his Instagram, alluding to this lyric.\n\nDuring his October 2018 appearance on Jimmy Fallon’s Tonight Show, Travis revealed that he actively enjoys drinking the juice, which he claims Kylie Jenner introduced him to.", "annotation_id": "15977251", "url": "https://genius.com/15977251", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Now it’s-it’s 4 A.M and I’m back up poppin’ with the crew", "vault_lines": ["Now it's-it's 4 A.M and I'm back up poppin' with the crew"], "meaning": "A nod to Travis' verse on 2 Chainz’s 2017 song “4 AM,” where Travis raps:\n\nOkay, you popped up on me by surprise (yeah, yeah)\nYou see I never took you for the poppin' type (straight up)\nDamn, it’s 4 AM so please believe the hype (it’s lit)", "annotation_id": "15977249", "url": "https://genius.com/15977249", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Had to hit my old town to duck the news Two four hour lockdown, we made no moves", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This could mean that he went to his hometown (Missouri City, Texas) to dodge the press and paparazzi. As of 2016, the city has a small population of 74,561, which contrasts from Los Angeles' population of almost 4 million.\n\n“Two four hour lockdown” means “twenty four hour lockdown”—a phrase usually applied to criminals in prison. Travis uses “lockdown” to show that he had complete focus while finishing the album.", "annotation_id": "15977243", "url": "https://genius.com/15977243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Was off the Remy, had a Papoose", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Travis plays on the name “Remy,” which is short for Remy Martin cognac, and also short for Remy Ma, a well-known New York rapper and former member of Terror Squad.\n\nHe also plays on “papoose,” which means “child,” and implies that he conceived his child while intoxicated. On the other hand, Papoose is another New York rapper, who’s been married to Remy Ma since 2008.\n\nPapoose was involved in a short feud with 50 Cent who made flirtatious gestures towards Remy on one of her Instagram posts:\n\nDamn Remy you lost mad weight, hey slim.\n\nPapoose clapped back at 50 almost immediately:\n\nDamn, @DaphneJoy no wedding yet? If @50Cent doesn’t marry you… I’ll introduce you to my homeboy. He’s more mature.", "annotation_id": "15977235", "url": "https://genius.com/15977235", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "This shit way too big, when we pull up, give me the loot (Gimme the loot!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Travis quotes the chorus of The Notorious B.I.G.’s 1994 track, “Gimme the Loot.” The ad-lib on this line is a sample from the same song.\n\nGimme the loot, gimme the loot\n\nHe starts the line with “way too big” as a reference Biggie’s name, as it follows up with the reference to his song.", "annotation_id": "15977186", "url": "https://genius.com/15977186", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Yeah, Jump Out boys, that’s Nike boys, hoppin’ out coupes", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Jump Out Boys” is a phrase usually attributed to the cops. It arose due to groups of police jumping out of vans to subdue suspects. Travis uses it to denote him and a group of friends hopping out of luxury vehicles, wearing fresh shoes and clothing.\n\nTravis is signed to the Nike Air Jordan brand. His first Jordan, which he designed to commemorate the Houston Oilers, released on June 9, 2018. The Jumpman logo appears on almost all Air Jordan silhouettes and could also be a reason for the phrase “Jump Out Boys.”\n\n\n\nIt’s also worth noting Travis' use of the phrase “Nike boys”—he mentioned the brand in one of his older tweets and rapped a similar line on 2017’s  “Ghostface Killers”:\n\nNike boys, we don’t do three stripes (yeah)", "annotation_id": "15977167", "url": "https://genius.com/15977167", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Woo, made this here with all the ice on in the booth", "vault_lines": ["Woo, made this here with all the ice on in the booth"], "meaning": "“Ice” is hip-hop slang for jewelry encrusted with diamonds and gems. Travis is very fond of jewelry; he’s spent around $500,000 on chains for himself and his crew.\n\nAdditionally, he admitted to spending over $1,000,000 on the ‘Astronomia Tourbillon Baguette’, a watch made by Jacob & Co, which is known as one of the most expensive, and accurate watches in the world.", "annotation_id": "15977165", "url": "https://genius.com/15977165", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Young La Flame, he in sicko mode", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“La Flame” is a nickname for Travis Scott, derived from Travis' 2013 song, “Blocka La Flame.”\n\nDrake and Trav have collaborated prior to “Sicko Mode” on 2015’s “Company,” and more recently, on 2017’s “Portland.”\n\nDrake uses the term “sicko” (or 6icko) to describe his friends, but in this context, it’s referring to the rager mentality for which Travis is known.\n\nDrake previously referred to himself as a “sicko” on “Summer Sixteen”:\n\nI’m just a sicko, a real sicko when you get to know me nigga", "annotation_id": "15977158", "url": "https://genius.com/15977158", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Yeah, yeah, yeah Goin’ on you with the pick and roll", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Pick and roll” is a basketball term used to describe the action of a teammate setting a screen (blocking the defender’s lane), and then “rolling” off the defender to receive a pass from his teammate.\n\n\n\nTravis metaphorically receives a pass from Drake, as the beat cuts out and Travis takes over.\n\nTravis' hometown team, the Houston Rockets, is especially known for excellent pick and roll plays.", "annotation_id": "15977148", "url": "https://genius.com/15977148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "My dawg would probably do it for a Louis belt That’s just all he know, he don’t know nothin’ else I tried to show ’em, yeah I tried to show ’em, yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["My dawg would probably do it for a Louis belt"], "meaning": "Louis Vuitton belts can cost upwards of $550. Drake could be saying he has a friend who’s too intrigued by material wealth, or may be willing to commit crimes for something as simple as a Louis belt.\n\nTravis commented these bars on a 2017 Instagram photo for the Drake song “Signs.” Drake made “Signs” for a Louis Vuitton runway show.\n\nhttps://pbs.twimg.com/media/DC26i1LXcAAbYvR.jpg:large\n\nAlso, in the official music video for “SICKO MODE”, Drake is seen walking his actual dog, whose name is Winter, which goes back to the previous line where Drake quips:\n\nSun is down, freezin' cold\nThat’s how we already know, winter’s here", "annotation_id": "15977138", "url": "https://genius.com/15977138", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "line": "Astro, yeah [Verse: Drake] Sun is down, freezin’ cold That’s how we already know, winter’s here", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Drake’s hometown of Toronto, Ontario, Canada usually starts to reach winter temperatures in the middle of November. Around November 20, 2017, meteorological reports registered negative Celsius temperatures there, long before the official start of winter on December 21.\n\nAs winter hits, the sun starts to set earlier, and with less sunlight comes lower temperatures. On November 20, the sun in Toronto set around 4:45 PM/EST, and rose in the morning at 7:20 AM.\n\nWhen the sun sets, temperatures fall for the night. Astroworld (along with most releases) come out at 12am EST, not only is it cold, but they’re dropping something cold— ‘winter’s here’\n\nAlso, Drake has a bulldog named Winter which he implies in the next line. This double entendre was seemingly confirmed when Winter appeared in the “SICKO MODE” music video.", "annotation_id": "15977133", "url": "https://genius.com/15977133", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Drake", "song": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 45, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Travis-scott-and-skrillex-sicko-mode-skrillex-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "SICKO MODE (Skrillex Remix)", "genius_id": 4039419, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Things I Forgot To Do", "line": "These days These days I seem to think about how I have failed to change all of my ways, ohh These days", "vault_lines": ["These days I seem to think about how I have failed to change all of my ways, ohh"], "meaning": "Drake reflects on how much he has changed  over time. No doubt, Drake’s evolved over the years, even sparking a meme about it;\n\n\n\nAlthough he has grown up and changed a lot, Drake still feels as if he hasn’t changed enough—as if he’s stuck in his ways. He’s still singing, he’s still making songs about old flames; not much has changed.", "annotation_id": "8899097", "url": "https://genius.com/8899097", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Things I Forgot To Do", "line": "I stopped my rambling Never been once a gambler, my town But for you, for you, for you, just once It might be fine these days These days", "vault_lines": ["I stopped my rambling", "Never been once a gambler, my town", "But for you, for you, for you, just once", "It might be fine these days"], "meaning": "Rambling is associated with journeying, with no specific destination in mind.  In love, this can take the form of multiple partners with little romantic or emotional connection.\n\nTo settle down with a single lover can be seen as a gamble by some.  The potential for pain may be too great.\n\nBabeo met a person that changed her from her rambling ways.  She gave up her past lifestyle by changing important parts of her personality for this person.  With her lover, she feels that regardless of what could go wrong in her gamble with love, she will be fine.", "annotation_id": "8897867", "url": "https://genius.com/8897867", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Things I Forgot To Do", "line": "Yeah, I had a lover Don’t think I’d risk another these days These days And if I seem to be afraid Just listen to the life I’ve made in songs These days", "vault_lines": ["Don't think I'd risk another these days"], "meaning": "In the original version of “These Days,” Jackson Browne sings,\n\nNow if I seem to be afraid to live the life that I have made in song\nWell it’s just that I’ve been losing\nFor so long\n\nRather than admitting defeat, Drake modifies the lyrics to call attention to his earlier songs. He urges the listener to look at at his previous work in order to understand why he can’t commit to a relationship now. In songs like  “Marvin’s Room,” Drake expresses timidity in finding a partner. In “Find Your Love,” he worries about choosing the right partner.\n\nIn a 2010 interview, Drake said,\n\nI look for love in the wrong people. It just always seem like the women I find are poison for me. I love women that can just walk into a room and stop the whole area. And I think that those initial qualities always end up getting me hurt, which is a crazy thing for a guy to say.“", "annotation_id": "8896249", "url": "https://genius.com/8896249", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Things I Forgot To Do", "line": "I’ve been out walking I don’t do too much talking these days These days These days I seem to think a lot About the things that I forgot to do These days", "vault_lines": ["I don't do too much scheming these days"], "meaning": "While much of the song is a slightly-edited interpolation of Jackson Browne’s  “These Days,” the first verse is exactly the same as the original.\n\nBrowne wrote the first iteration of the song when he was 16. A few years later he was working as a songwriter, pitching his lyrics to various recording artists. German model and singer Nico was the first artist to record a version of the song. The song would later be recorded by Browne himself and Gregg Allman.\n\nDrake has never shied away from singing/rapping about his real feelings. Browne’s original lyrics fit within Drake’s canon—he often references feeling lonely, isolated, and nostalgic.", "annotation_id": "8896198", "url": "https://genius.com/8896198", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Drake", "song": "Things I Forgot To Do", "line": "I sit inside a chauffeur car With windows down and count the stars", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "As much as Drake has embraced his fame and popularity, he’s still an introvert at heart. He rarely has a private life, always on the road, recording, being pursued by paparazzi etc, but for the few moments he does have to himself, he reflects on his life and has introspective thoughts (aka Drake feels)\n\nCould also refer to the fact that “these days” he rides on the backseat of his Rolls Royce, and “counts the stars”, referring to Rolls Royce’s famous Wraith Ceiling that lights up when dark making the small lights look like stars floating in space.", "annotation_id": "8896102", "url": "https://genius.com/8896102", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
